(window.KG_PARTS = window.KG_PARTS || []).push({"group":"ettuthogai_akam","chapters":{"ai:1":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":1,"title":"மருதம் decade 1","section":"மருதம்","theme":"The decade of wishes (வேட்கைப் பத்து, marutham, Orampokiyar): in each poem the mother wishes long life and prosperity for the king Aadan Avini and his land, and the girl's side answers with wishes for the man of the field town, rising from his welfare and his house to an open suitor's chariot, marriage, his kept oath, freedom from gossip and the bride taken to his town (1-10)."},"ai:2":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":2,"title":"மருதம் decade 2","section":"மருதம்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 11 to 20, the marutham reed decade of Orampokiyar: every poem opens on the reed (வேழம்) of the husband's ford-town, flowering like sugarcane, a horse's plume or a heron, serving as kohl tube or bathing float, and closes on the wife's body (shoulders, eyes, bangles, heart) bearing the cost of his other women."},"ai:3":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":3,"title":"மருதம் கள்வன் பத்து decade 3","section":"மருதம்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 21 to 30, the crab decade of the marutham hundred: ten four- and five-line poems in which the friend asks the mother, each time ending எவன்கொல் அன்னாய், why the girl pales and wastes for the man of the field town, while the field crab cuts lilies, creepers, sprouts and paddy, abandons its mate or hoards in its hole as the figure of what he does."},"ai:4":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":4,"title":"மருதம் தோழிக்கு உரைத்த பத்து decade 4","section":"மருதம்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 31 to 40, the marutham decade spoken to the friend: by our reading the other woman's voice throughout, holding the lord to an oath sworn at the bathing ford, mocking his wives who weep a week for one day's absence, watching her own eyes and skin turn the colour of the water lily as he forgets her, and hearing at last that he has settled back in his wives' town, where the leaping fish opens the closed lily."},"ai:5":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":5,"title":"மருதம் புலவிப் பத்து decade 5","section":"மருதம்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 41 to 50, the decade of sulking (புலவிப் பத்து) in Orampokiyar's marutham hundred: the wife and her friend refuse the husband who comes back from the courtesan, mocking his chest, his oaths and his bard, setting their pallor against his rich river town, and using the bard-women's barter of fish for grain and the tortoise and crocodile of the pond as images of his care and his cruelty."},"ai:6":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":6,"title":"மருதம் தோழி கூற்றுப் பத்து decade 6","section":"மருதம்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 51 to 60, the friend's speech decade of the marutham hundred: the friend faces the straying husband, calling him home to a pregnant wife, asking at whose door his chariot stands, laying the girl's sickness to his broken oath, measuring her fading beauty by the rich towns of the Pandya, the Chola and Viraan, mocking his rival woman, grieving that she who once cured his love has no cure for the wife, and in the last poem warning a night visitor of the father's spear."},"ai:7":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":7,"title":"மருதம் கிழத்தி கூற்றுப்பத்து decade 7","section":"மருதம்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 61 to 70, the wife's speeches of Orampokiyar's marutham: ten short retorts to a husband who goes to other women, built from the pond, the flood, the lily and the lotus, in which she mocks his new women as children and upstarts, counts the witnesses of his bathing, and turns her own body after childbirth into the ground of her refusal."},"ai:8":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":8,"title":"மருதம் புனலாட்டுப் பத்து decade 8","section":"மருதம்","theme":"The decade of water-play (புனலாட்டுப் பத்து, marutham, Orampokiyar): the husband bathes in the river floods with another woman; the wife and her friend meet the gossip with charges (71, 75, 76, 79, 80), the man praises the woman of the water-play (72-74), and the rival invites him to the flood and away from his house (77, 78)."},"ai:9":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":9,"title":"மருதம் புலவி விராய பத்து decade 9","section":"மருதம்","theme":"The decade of the other woman's sulking (புலவி விராய பத்து, marutham, Orampokiyar): the courtesan answers the husband, the bard and her own friend about the wife's anger, mocking the husband's straying, the wife's jealousy and the title of wife, and ending with the husband as a bee (81-90)."},"ai:10":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":10,"title":"மருதம் எருமைப் பத்து decade 10","section":"மருதம்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 91 to 100, the buffalo decade of the marutham hundred (எருமைப் பத்து, Orampokiyar): each short poem opens on a buffalo of the field town, trampling lilies, nursing its calf, resting with its mate, snapping its rope, bathing like a raft or digging up a bather's jewel, and turns to the girl, mostly as the man's praise of the field-lord's daughter, once as the friend's warning that her parents guard her, and once as the complaint of a woman the rope-breaking lord has left sick even by day."},"ai:11":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":11,"title":"நெய்தல் தாய்க்கு உரைத்த பத்து decade 11","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 101 to 110, Ammuvanar's neythal decade told to the mother (தாய்க்கு உரைத்த பத்து): every poem opens on the refrain அன்னை வாழி வேண்டு அன்னை and the friend lays the girl's secret love before her mother, first as cure (the chariot, its bell, the matched pair), then as symptom (gold brow, pale chest, sleepless nights), then as grievance (the shoulders he left) and a plea to fate."},"ai:12":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":12,"title":"நெய்தல் தோழிக்கு உரைத்த பத்து decade 12","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 111 to 120, Ammuvanar's neythal decade spoken by the heroine to her friend (தோழிக்கு உரைத்த பத்து): each poem opens அம்ம வாழி தோழி and sets one moment of the shore love, from the baited kayal and the fear of parting, through gossip, the mother's guard and the closing lilies of evening, to anger that relents and shoulders that bloom again when he comes."},"ai:13":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":13,"title":"நெய்தல் கிழவற்கு உரைத்த பத்து decade 13","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 121 to 128, the neythal decade spoken to the lord of the shore: by our reading the friend answering a husband who denies another woman, each poem opening 'did we not see your woman?' and showing her as a child at sea play, asking a heron for a lost jewel, filling the sea with sand to avenge a stolen doll, weeping red-eyed for it, and feeding a doll from a breast that has no milk."},"ai:14":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":14,"title":"நெய்தல் பாணற்கு உரைத்த பத்து decade 14","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 131 to 140, Ammoovanar's neythal decade spoken to the bard (பாணற்கு உரைத்த பத்து): the woman (once the man, once turning to the lord himself) uses the hero's bard as the ear for gossip, wasted shoulders and slipped bangles, rebukes him for carrying words instead of the man, and twice bids him look (காண்மதி பாண), once at beauty returned and once at bangles gone."},"ai:15":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":15,"title":"நெய்தல் ஞாழற் பத்து decade 15","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 141 to 150, Ammuvanar's neythal decade on the njaazhal of the sand dune: every poem opens on the dune's njaazhal tree (its scent, clusters, birds, tide and waves) and turns to the shore lord, through pallor, sleeplessness and wasting to his return, the bride price and the friend's warning not to leave."},"ai:16":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":16,"title":"நெய்தல் வெள்ளங் குருகுப் பத்து decade 16","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 151 to 160, Ammuvanar's neythal decade of the white heron: every poem opens on the simple-gaited stork that went to see what it took for the white heron's chick, and turns to the lord of the shore, with the woman refusing a melted heart, the friend reporting that he will marry, the woman complaining of the town's lies and of a sedge doll borne to a man who leaves with the tide, and the friend demanding back her beauty and begging him to embrace her."},"ai:17":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":17,"title":"நெய்தல் சிறுவெண் காக்கைப் பத்து decade 17","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 161 to 170, the neythal decade of the small white gull: each poem opens on the gull of the great sea's shore at rest, feeding, sleeping, nesting or taking fright, and by our reading the woman (twice her friend) weighs the shore lord's word against her wasting body: bangles that leave as he left, words that have changed, gossip bred by his good name, pallid eyes that do not obey her trust."},"ai:18":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":18,"title":"நெய்தல் தொண்டிப் பத்து decade 18","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 171 to 180, Ammuvanar's Thondi decade, a linked chain in which each poem opens with the last words of the one before and every poem likens the girl to the Chera port of Thondi: by our reading the man, struck by her, loses his heart and his sleep, is granted a tryst, pleads with her and her friend, and then the friend turns his own word, grace, back on him and asks for marriage."},"ai:19":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":19,"title":"நெய்தல் நெய்தற் பத்து decade 19","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 181 to 190, Ammuvanar's decade on the neythal flower: in every poem the blue lily of the shore appears, as the girls' eyes, a garland, a closing flower at evening, dawn bloom like a lover's eyes, or a flower cut down with the paddy, and the speakers move from the hope of marriage and the friend's disclosure to the heroine's grief and the eyes he heals or makes weep."},"ai:20":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":20,"title":"நெய்தல் வளைப் பத்து decade 20","section":"நெய்தல்","theme":"Ainkurunuru 191 to 200, Ammuvanar's neythal decade of the bangle: the conch shell of the shore sawn into bangles, which the man sees on the girl he cannot reach, the friend weighs as a lover's gift and as a reason to marry, and the woman reads as a gauge that loosens when the lord of the shore leaves and tightens when he returns."},"ai:21":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":21,"title":"குறிஞ்சி அன்னாய் வாழிப் பத்து decade 21","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"Ainkurunuru 201 to 210, Kapilar's kurinji decade to the mother (அன்னாய் வாழிப் பத்து): under the refrain அன்னாய் வாழி வேண்டு அன்னை the friend lays the girl's secret love before her mother through things of his hill, a leaf skirt from his slope, his tufted horses, the muddy water of his land, the gear he wears wet from the rains, and above all his hill itself, whose sight brings the girl tears when hidden and cure when seen."},"ai:22":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":22,"title":"குறிஞ்சி அன்னாய்ப் பத்து decade 22","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"The decade of 'mother' (அன்னாய்ப் பத்து, kurinji, Kapilar): every poem ends on அன்னாய், a girl and her friend speaking of the hill man of secret love, his wilting gift of leaves, his virtue, his coming and going, the omens of his return and the pallor his absence brings, each set against a scene of his hill (211-220)."},"ai:23":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":23,"title":"குறிஞ்சி அம்மவழிப் பத்து decade 23","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"Ainkurunuru 221 to 230, Kapilar's kurinji decade in which every poem opens அம்ம வாழி தோழி: the heroine and her friend talk of the secret lover's comings and goings measured on her body, her brow paling and shoulders slackening when he speaks of leaving or stays away, and her golden beauty returning when he comes by night or comes at last for the wedding."},"ai:24":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":24,"title":"குறிஞ்சி தெய்யோப் பத்து decade 24","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"Ainkurunuru 231 to 240, Kapilar's kurinji decade with the refrain தெய்யோ: by our reading the friend (once the girl) presses the hill lover in courtship, pointing to the girl's pallor and slipping bangles, the dream that shows him only asleep, the cold north wind, the ewe that waits and the tusker that embraces a stone, the scent of her hair on his chest, and at last proposing to go with him to his town now the mother knows."},"ai:25":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":25,"title":"குறிஞ்சி வெறிப்பத்து decade 25","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"Ainkurunuru 241 to 250, Kapilar's kurinji decade on the veri rite (வெறிப்பத்து): mother calls the diviner, who casts his beads and names Murugu as the cause of the girl's sickness, and the friend and the girl answer with irony, asking whether the diviner can know the hill man, whether Murugu is his name, and saying the cure is union, not offering."},"ai:26":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":26,"title":"குறிஞ்சி குன்றக் குறவன் பத்து decade 26","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"Ainkurunuru 251 to 260, Kapilar's kurinji decade on the hill man (குன்றக் குறவன் பத்து): every poem opens on the hill man, his shout, his hut, his sandal fire, his worship and his daughter; the decade moves from praise of the girl as a hill goddess, through the friend's pleas and the household's consent, to the ripened millet that ends the meetings."},"ai:27":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":27,"title":"குறிஞ்சி கேழற் பத்து decade 27","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"Ainkurunuru 261 to 270, Kapilar's kurinji decade on the boar: each poem opens on the boar of the hill (grazing the millet, keeping its mate, rearing motherless young, fighting the tiger, rooting the slope like a plough) and turns to the hill lord, through the heroine's doubts of his courage and love, her pallor and tears, his flattery and leaving, to his return at the harvest's end."},"ai:28":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":28,"title":"குறிஞ்சி 28 குரக்குப் பத்து decade 28","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"Ainkurunuru 271 to 280, Kapilar's kurinji decade of the monkey (குரக்குப் பத்து): each poem opens on a monkey of the hill lord's slope (the stuffed-cheeked female, the reckless young at the honey or the bamboo top, the male who carries his mate from the tiger or beats the rain bubbles) and turns it on the lord: his constancy, his night visits, his leaving, the beauty he took, the gossip he will raise, and the marriage the friend demands."},"ai:29":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":29,"title":"குறிஞ்சி கிள்ளைப் பத்து decade 29","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"Ainkurunuru 281 to 290, Kapilar's parrot decade (கிள்ளைப் பத்து) of kurinji: every poem turns on the parrots that raid the hill millet and the girl set to drive them off. By our reading the man blesses and thanks the birds whose raids put her in the field and envies them her glance, while the friend turns the same field against him: do not come by night, your promises are a net, how can you leave her, the reaped field still holds the parrots, and the elders may end her guarding, so marry her."},"ai:30":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":30,"title":"குறிஞ்சி 30 மஞ்ஞைப் பத்து decade 30","section":"குறிஞ்சி","theme":"Ainkurunuru 291 to 300, Kapilar's peacock decade (மஞ்ஞைப் பத்து) of kurinji: each poem sets the hill's peacock beside the hill girl, through the secret love (the man's longing, the friend's fear of his night visits, the girl's heart gone with him) to the wedding, and by the end the peacock is likened to the girl rather than she to it."},"ai:31":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":31,"title":"பாலை செலவு அழுங்குவித்த பத்து decade 31","section":"பாலை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 301 to 310, Othalanthaiyar's paalai decade செலவு அழுங்குவித்த பத்து, in which the friend tries to stop the man from crossing the waste for wealth: the road's flowers, its seized water, its starving elephant pair and its fires are set against the woman's grief, her slipping bangles and the smile of an unborn son."},"ai:32":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":32,"title":"பாலை செலவுப் பத்து decade 32","section":"பாலை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 311 to 320, Othalanthaiyar's paalai decade on the going (செலவுப் பத்து): the man, and in 313 the girl, crosses the wasteland of killing elephants, scorched trees and flaming silk-cotton, and those left behind measure the road by their own wasting, ask whether he will delay or forget, bless the hill that hid him, and find that the heart too has gone and not come back."},"ai:33":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":33,"title":"பாலை இடைச்சுரப் பத்து decade 33","section":"பாலை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 321 to 330, Othalanthaiyar's paalai decade on the middle of the waste (இடைச்சுரப் பத்து): in every poem the man, on the burning road away from the girl, sets the cruelty of the land against her nature (பண்பு, குணன்), which cools the heat, travels with him, takes his heart and at last turns him back."},"ai:34":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":34,"title":"பாலை தலைவி இரங்கு பத்து decade 34","section":"பாலை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 331 to 340, Othalanthaiyar's paalai decade of the wife's lament (தலைவி இரங்கு பத்து): every poem opens on அம்ம வாழி தோழி and the wife, her man gone across the waste for wealth, measures his leaving against the beasts and birds that should have stopped him, against stone, against the embrace, and against the season and the evening that should have brought him home."},"ai:35":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":35,"title":"பாலை இளவேனிற் பத்து decade 35","section":"பாலை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 341 to 350, Othalanthaiyar's paalai decade of early spring (இளவேனிற் பத்து): every poem opens on the same line, he does not come but the time has come, and closes on one sign of spring (the koel, drunk bees on the nunavam, the kongu bud, punku leaves on young breasts, the kadamba grove, the paathiri, the mango's flame-red shoots, the neem), until the last names it as the time his sweet words had promised."},"ai:36":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":36,"title":"பாலை வரவுரைத்த பத்து decade 36","section":"பாலை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 351 to 360, Othalanthaiyar's paalai decade on the return (வரவுரைத்த பத்து): the friend tells the wife that the men who crossed the waste have come back, and the man himself tells her that thinking of her he gave up the wealth-quest, and that the long burnt road grew short and easy on the way home."},"ai:37":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":37,"title":"பாலை முன்னிலைப் பத்து decade 37","section":"பாலை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 361 to 370, Othalanthaiyar's paalai decade of direct address (முன்னிலைப் பத்து): each poem speaks to someone face to face, the man to the girl whose beauty strikes him and to a mango shoot like her, the friend to the man who comes by night, who would hurry away, who has been gossiped about, and to the mother asking why the girl is pale, all set among the kongu, paathiri and silk-cotton of the dry season and the hunters of the waste."},"ai:38":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":38,"title":"பாலை மக்ட் போக்கிய வழித் தாயிரங்கு பத்து decade 38","section":"பாலை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 371 to 380, Othalanthaiyar's paalai decade of the mother's lament after her daughter's elopement (மகட்போக்கிய வழித் தாய் இரங்கு பத்து): the mother blesses the road, asks if she is remembered, curses the youth's mother and the fate that joined them, weeps over the doll and parrot and the toys left behind, grieves for the friend left alone, and ends saying she was mother only in name."},"ai:39":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":39,"title":"பாலை உடன்போக்கின் கண் இடைச் சுரத்து உரைத்த பத்து decade 39","section":"பாலை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 381 to 390, Othalanthaiyar's paalai decade of words spoken on the waste road during an elopement (உடன்போக்கின் கண் இடைச் சுரத்து உரைத்த பத்து): onlookers pity and admire the couple, the hamlet's mothers grieve, the youth delights in the girl's play, the girl sends word home to her companions and her mother, and travellers and brahmins answer the searching mother or foster mother."},"ai:40":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":40,"title":"பாலை மறுதரவுப் பத்து decade 40","section":"பாலை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 391 to 400, Othalanthaiyar's paalai decade of the return (மறுதரவுப் பத்து): the household bribes the crow to caw the eloped girl home, asks the mother whether her daughter has come, says that the loveless right has shown grace; the man brings the girl through the burnt wasteland to his hill country, decks her hair and enters his town with her as an evening guest; she sends word to her companions; the mother blesses the road, proposes to divide the rites with the youth's house, and the message comes that the couple arrive today."},"ai:41":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":41,"title":"முல்லை செவிலி கூற்றுப் பத்து decade 41","section":"முல்லை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 401 to 410, Peyanar's mullai decade of the foster mother's words (செவிலி கூற்றுப் பத்து): visiting the married daughter's house, the foster mother reports the couple lying with their son between them like a deer family, the father's embrace of mother and nursing child, the toddler named for his grandfather, the wife become the lamp of the house, and the evening on the courtyard cot that outdid the bard's lute."},"ai:42":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":42,"title":"முல்லை கிழவன் பருவம் பாராட்டுப் பத்து decade 42","section":"முல்லை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 411 to 420, Peyanar's mullai decade of the hero praising the season (கிழவன் பருவம் பாராட்டுப் பத்து): home again as the rains begin, the hero invites his wife to bathe and play in the flowering scrub, likens the peacocks and the beasts to the two of them, calls youth sweet when spent together, wonders whether she is a woman of the sky, and promises the scrub itself that they will come to see it."},"ai:43":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":43,"title":"முல்லை விரவுப் பத்து decade 43","section":"முல்லை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 421 to 430, Peyanar's mixed mullai decade (முல்லை விரவுப் பத்து): the man held from going by her shoulders and her tears, the friend dissuading him with the rains and the crying son, the hurried drive home past doves and mullai, and the choice between the king's war and the wife, renounced in one poem, made conditional in another, and ended by the rains in a third."},"ai:44":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":44,"title":"முல்லை புறவணிப் பத்து decade 44","section":"முல்லை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 431 to 440, Peyanar's mullai decade on the beauty of the forest (புறவணிப் பத்து): each poem opens 'good indeed is the road my lover took' and adds one more grace of the rainy season on it, peacocks, konrai-crowned men, rain-met forest, doe and fawn, neythal, konrai and kurunthu, mullai after hail, flowering bushes, herders' hamlets, thondri and thalavam, while the woman who says it waits at home."},"ai:45":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":45,"title":"முல்லை பாசறைப் பத்து decade 45","section":"முல்லை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 441 to 450, Peyanar's mullai decade of the war camp (பாசறைப் பத்து): the married man in the king's camp in the rains, longing for his wife, wishing she knew his pain, picturing her as hostess of guests and mother of his son, and making every return hang on one condition, if the king's work ends."},"ai:46":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":46,"title":"முல்லை பருவங்கண்டு கிழத்தி யுரைத்த பத்து decade 46","section":"முல்லை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 451 to 460, Peyanar's mullai decade of the wife who sees the season (பருவங்கண்டு கிழத்தி யுரைத்த பத்து): the rains have come and her husband, away with the king's army, has not, and she reads frogs, mullai, kondrai, pakanrai, herons and the north wind against her pale shoulders and wasted eyes."},"ai:47":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":47,"title":"முல்லை தோழி வற்புறுத்த பத்து decade 47","section":"முல்லை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 461 to 470, Peyanar's mullai decade of the friend's reassurance (தோழி வற்புறுத்த பத்து): with the rains beginning, the friend tells the waiting wife not to grieve, discredits the konrai that flowered too early, explains the delay by spoil from the enemy, cites the king's release and his own haste, calls her out to see the rightward-rising cloud, and finally rests the guarantee on her own beauty."},"ai:48":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":48,"title":"முல்லை பாணன் பத்து decade 48","section":"முல்லை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 471 to 480, Peyanar's mullai decade of the bard (பாணன் பத்து): the waiting wife reproaches the bard who comes from her absent husband, doubts his promises and calls him loveless; the husband, still away on campaign, posts the bard with her and asks him again and again for her words; and in the last poem the bard renounces his lord for ignoring her tears."},"ai:49":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":49,"title":"முல்லை தேர் வியங்கொண்ட பத்து decade 49","section":"முல்லை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 481 to 490, Peyanar's mullai decade of the man bidding his chariot home (தேர் வியங்கொண்ட பத்து): his work for the king done and the rains come, the returning husband urges his charioteer to goad the bird-swift horses and drive fast, so that his waiting wife's pining may end, her beauty return and her heart rejoice at the evening hour."},"ai:50":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":50,"title":"முல்லை வரவுச் சிரப்புரைத்த பத்து decade 50","section":"முல்லை","theme":"Ainkurunuru 491 to 500, Peyanar's mullai decade on the arrival (வரவுச் சிறப்புரைத்த பத்து): the man back from war or work tells the woman he hurried home with the rains, and the friend says the season and her beauty have returned together because he came."},"a:1":{"book":"akananuru","num":1,"title":"களிற்றியானைநிரை 0-20","section":"களிற்றியானைநிரை","theme":"The invocation to Shiva and Akananuru 1 to 20 in the anthology's fixed order of lands: paalai poems of men leaving or turning back for wealth and of mothers whose daughters eloped, Kapilar's hill courtships in which the friend presses for marriage, mullai homecomings at evening, neythal shores closed by gossip, and marutham quarrels over the husband's women. Chola, Chera, Pandya and lesser chiefs' names (Thithan's Uranthai, Thondi, Kodai, Avi's Pothini, Nannan's Pazhi) serve as measures of private beauty, guard and loss."},"a:2":{"book":"akananuru","num":2,"title":"களிற்றியானைநிரை 21-40","section":"களிற்றியானைநிரை","theme":"Akananuru 21 to 40, in the anthology's fixed order of lands: ten paalai poems of men on the road to wealth arguing with their hearts and women counting seasons, set beside hill courtships at the millet field, marutham quarrels settled by a child or sharpened by gossip, mullai camps and homecomings, and neythal evenings. Puram fame (Alankanam, Thithiyan, Kari's Pennai, Korkai, Kolli) supplies the similes for private feeling."},"a:3":{"book":"akananuru","num":3,"title":"களிற்றியானைநிரை 41-60","section":"களிற்றியானைநிரை","theme":"Akananuru 41 to 60, in the anthology's fixed order of lands: ten paalai poems of men away for wealth and women and mothers left behind, set beside hill courtships moving toward disclosure and marriage, marutham quarrels over the husband's other women, mullai homecomings and neythal pleas. Puram names (Venni, Musiri, Kurukkai, Pazhaiyan, Pannan) are used throughout as similes for private grief and joy."},"a:4":{"book":"akananuru","num":4,"title":"களிற்றியானைநிரை 61-80","section":"களிற்றியானைநிரை","theme":"Akananuru 61 to 80, in the anthology's order of lands: ten paalai poems of men leaving or refusing to leave for wealth, women and mothers left behind and an elopement, set beside hill trysts at night, mullai waiting and homecoming, a marutham husband held at home by his son and a courtesan's boast, and neythal pleas for open marriage. Puram fame (Pulli, Udhiyan Cheral, the Moriyar, Aay, Pittan, Paari with Kapilar, Rama at Kodi) is drawn on throughout as likeness."},"a:5":{"book":"akananuru","num":5,"title":"களிற்றியானைநிரை 81-100","section":"களிற்றியானைநிரை","theme":"Akananuru 81 to 100 in the anthology's fixed order of lands: ten paalai poems of men crossing the waste for wealth, of their return, and of a girl's elopement seen by her foster mother and by her lover; hill courtships under watch and a veriyattu rite; marutham marriage and infidelity, from a wedding night remembered to gossip loud as a Chola victory; mullai war camps and homecomings; and neythal confinement and pressure for marriage. Kings and towns of puram (Pulli's Venkatam, Kuttuvan's Kudanadu, Uraiyur, Koodal, Karuvur, Paruvur, Viyalur, Puranthai) serve as measures of distance, wealth and noise."},"a:6":{"book":"akananuru","num":6,"title":"களிற்றியானைநிரை 101-120","section":"களிற்றியானைநிரை","theme":"Akananuru 101 to 120, in the களிற்றியானைநிரை section: ten paalai poems of roads through raider country, a heroine doubting an old proverb, two mothers grieving for eloped daughters, set beside kurinji night trysts pressed toward marriage, marutham quarrels measured against Chezhiyan's wars, two mullai homecomings and two neythal scenes. Puram names (Ezhini, Evvi, Erumai, Ahthai and the Kosar, Vaanan, Chezhiyan at Koodal) serve as similes and settings for private grief."},"a:7":{"book":"akananuru","num":7,"title":"மணிமிடைபவளம் 121-140","section":"மணிமிடைபவளம்","theme":"Akananuru 121 to 140, from the மணிமிடைபவளம் section: paalai partings argued with the heart or comforted by the friend, kurinji night-tryst poems spoken for an overhearing lover (the sleepless town, the dangerous path, the mother's Murugu rite), three mullai returns in the rains, a neythal pair of men smitten by a girl's eyes, and a full wedding night recalled in marutham, with puram fame (Karikal at Vaakai, Anni and Thithiyan, Cheralathan, Kazhuvul's Kamur, Thithan's Uranthai) used as the scale of comparison."},"a:8":{"book":"akananuru","num":8,"title":"மணிமிடைபவளம் 141-160","section":"மணிமிடைபவளம்","theme":"Akananuru 141 to 160, in the anthology's fixed order of lands: ten paalai poems weigh wealth against parting from both sides (men who go, one who stays, women who consent, resolve to follow, or scorn the stay-at-home), two foster mothers mourn eloped girls, hill poems move secret meetings between day and night, and neythal and marutham poems press for marriage. Puram fame (Minjili and the fallen Eyinan, Kurukkai's punnai, Muziri's trade, Pittan, Nannan, Nalli, Aay) is used throughout as the measure of private feeling."},"a:9":{"book":"akananuru","num":9,"title":"மணிமிடைபவளம் 161-180","section":"மணிமிடைபவளம்","theme":"Manimidaipavalam 161-180: separation for wealth and war argued from both sides of the road, the friend's dissuasions and comforts, the marutham quarrel heard from wife and other woman, and the press toward marriage in the hills."},"a:10":{"book":"akananuru","num":10,"title":"மணிமிடைபவளம் 181-200","section":"மணிமிடைபவளம்","theme":"Akananuru 181 to 200 in the anthology's fixed cycle of lands: ten paalai poems in which men argue with their hearts about going for wealth, three of them giving up the journey, and mothers mourn eloped daughters; around them hill trysts ruled by the weather and the harvest, a marutham quarrel told by both courtesan and wife, waiting in the promised rains, and neythal disclosure and invitation. Puram names (Minjili and Eyinan, Pazhaiyan of Por, Thithiyan and Anni Minjili, Kannan Ezhini, Nannan and Narmudi Cheral) serve as measures for private feeling."},"a:11":{"book":"akananuru","num":11,"title":"மணிமிடைபவளம் 201-220","section":"மணிமிடைபவளம்","theme":"Manimidai pavalam 201-220: friends answer women left for the desert road, mothers of eloped daughters grieve, friends press lovers from night meetings toward marriage, and puram fame (Alankanam, Ori's Kolli, Eyinan's fall, Mathi's ford, Chellur) serves as the measure of gossip and beauty."},"a:12":{"book":"akananuru","num":12,"title":"மணிமிடைபவளம் 221-240","section":"மணிமிடைபவளம்","theme":"Akananuru 221 to 240, from the மணிமிடைபவளம் section: half the poems are paalai partings and comforts in which the friend argues from memory, glory or the lover's body that the man will return, set among kurinji night-tryst poems pressing for marriage, two mullai homecomings, two marutham reproaches and two neythal meetings, with puram fame (Kazhar, Uranthai, Unur, Uthiyan Cheral's feast, Nalli) used repeatedly as a scale of comparison."},"a:13":{"book":"akananuru","num":13,"title":"மணிமிடைபவளம் 241-260","section":"மணிமிடைபவளம்","theme":"Akananuru 241 to 260, in the மணிமிடைபவளம் section: paalai poems of women left for wealth and of friends who answer them with the man's memory of their bodies, two elopement poems, kurinji night trysts threatened by the mother's watch and the velan's rite, mullai homecomings by chariot, and marutham refusals in which the friend measures the husband's scandal by public shouts at Venni, Kudal and Kallur. Puram history runs through the roads: Karikal, the Pandyan, the Kosar, the Moriyar, Nanda's treasure, Nannan's Paazhi."},"a:14":{"book":"akananuru","num":14,"title":"மணிமிடைபவளம் 261-280","section":"மணிமிடைபவளம்","theme":"Akananuru 261 to 280, in the anthology's fixed order of lands: paalai roads seen from both ends of an elopement (the man recalling her shyness, two mothers at the empty house), wives counting seasons and refusing to blame the absent man, hill courtships threatened by a mother's Murugu worship and a midnight storm, neythal suitors who would marry by servitude, and marutham quarrels in which the wife or the courtesan speaks. Puram matter (Minili's vow, Evvi's victory feast, the Nandas' hoard, hero-stones, Kuttuvan's Kazhumalam) supplies the measures for private feeling."},"a:15":{"book":"akananuru","num":15,"title":"மணிமிடைபவளம் 281-300","section":"மணிமிடைபவளம்","theme":"Akananuru 281 to 300, in the anthology's fixed order of lands: ten paalai poems weigh the wealth-road against the woman left behind, mostly from the man's side as he speaks to his own heart on the way (her rites of waiting, her farewell, her offer to come), with two elopements urged and blessed by the friend; hill poems press for marriage through the father's consent, the shut-in girl and the dreaded veri rite; neythal and marutham poems use Thondi, Korkai and Koodal as measures of pallor and gossip, and the friend turns maxims and hospitality into ways of binding the lover."},"a:16":{"book":"akananuru","num":16,"title":"நித்திலக்கோவை 301-320","section":"நித்திலக்கோவை","theme":"Akananuru 301-320 (from the Nithilakkovai) alternate paalai poems of the man gone for wealth and the road he takes (bandits, carrion crows, a dove that will not leave a ruined hall) with kurinji and neythal poems in which the friend refuses or redirects the secret meetings and presses for marriage, two marutham poems on the courtesan quarrel, and two mullai poems of the rains and the return; wandering performers and the fame of Paari, Pulli, Vaanavan and the Pandya Vazhuthi supply the similes."},"a:17":{"book":"akananuru","num":17,"title":"நித்திலக்கோவை 321-340","section":"நித்திலக்கோவை","theme":"Akananuru 321 to 340 (Nithilakkovai): men on the wealth road arguing with their hearts by the desert's robbers and dying travellers, wives waiting through the promised rains, night trysts at the elephant's plantain slope, courtesans boasting by Chola victories and a mother following an eloped daughter in thought; the fame of Thithiyan, Pazhaiyan, Thiraiyan, Athiyan and the three crowned houses measures private feeling."},"a:18":{"book":"akananuru","num":18,"title":"நித்திலக்கோவை 341-360","section":"நித்திலக்கோவை","theme":"Akananuru 341 to 360, from the நித்திலக்கோவை section: paalai partings dominate, with heroines blessing or reproaching the man who left for wealth and the hero arguing with his heart on the road and at home, set among kurinji and neythal poems of secret meetings pressing toward marriage, two mullai homecomings and one marutham refusal; puram names (Cheralathan, Nannan, Anji, Pulli, Killi Valavan at Koodal) recur as measures of fame, noise and giving."},"a:19":{"book":"akananuru","num":19,"title":"நித்திலக்கோவை 361-380","section":"நித்திலக்கோவை","theme":"Akananuru 361 to 380, from the நித்திலக்கோவை section: nine paalai poems of men arguing with their hearts on the road to wealth and of wives and mothers left behind, set among kurinji night and day trysts pressing for marriage, two mullai rain poems, two marutham sulks and two neythal meetings, with Pazhi, Kaamur, Evvi, Uranthai and the Atthi legend used as scales of comparison."},"a:20":{"book":"akananuru","num":20,"title":"நித்திலக்கோவை 381-400","section":"நித்திலக்கோவை","theme":"The last twenty poems of the Akananuru (Nittilakkovai 381 to 400), in the fixed order of lands: ten paalai poems of men gone for wealth and fame and of daughters gone with lovers, as heard by wives, friends and mothers; hill poems of night meetings, the diviner's rite and the pleading lover; mullai returns and invitations; a salt-girl met on the shore and a wedding chariot; and two Paranar courtesans who measure a lover's broken oath against the puram legends of Eyinan, Aththi and Vanji."},"kl:1":{"book":"kalittokai","num":1,"title":"பாலைக்கலி 1-18 (1/2)","section":"பாலைக்கலி","theme":"The invocation to Shiva the dancer and the first eighteen poems of Palai Padiya Perunkadungo's paalaikkali: nearly every poem is a debate over leaving home for wealth, in which the friend or the wife sets love, youth and the household against the giving, conquest and gain that wealth promises, often with long argued refrains and moral similes drawn from kingship and impermanence; several end with the man turning back, and one (9) has ascetics defend an elopement as the way of virtue."},"kl:2":{"book":"kalittokai","num":2,"title":"பாலைக்கலி 19-36 (2/2)","section":"பாலைக்கலி","theme":"The second half of Perunkadunko's paalaikkali (19 to 36): seven poems in which the woman or her friend argues against the man's leaving for wealth (his false promises, wealth moving by former deeds, love as a loan repaid with another face, the deer and elephant that keep together on the waste), then eleven spring poems in which the season's flowers, bees and cuckoos torment the woman left at home in Madurai by the Vaigai, most ending with the friend announcing that the man has come back on the promised day; the similes are drawn heavily from kingship, friendship and virtue."},"kl:3":{"book":"kalittokai","num":3,"title":"குறிஞ்சிக்கலி 37-51 (1/2)","section":"குறிஞ்சிக்கலி","theme":"The first fifteen poems of Kapilar's kurinjikkali: secret love on the hill told as scenes, from the girl's staged fall from the swing (37) and the old playmate who seizes her wrist and is shielded by her lie (51) to the friend's disclosure and the kuravai wedding (39); a run of valli songs sung while pounding grain (40 to 43) alternates reproach of the absent lover with its own refutation until the hidden listener comes to marry; and the friend's speeches to the man measure the girl's wasting by the things of his own hill, refuse his night visits and praise his giving to shame him into marriage."},"kl:4":{"book":"kalittokai","num":4,"title":"குறிஞ்சிக்கலி 52-65 (2/2)","section":"குறிஞ்சிக்கலி","theme":"The second half of Kapilar's Kurinjikkali (52 to 65): the friend pressing a night visitor to marry, the girl recounting the lover's wooing and the night tryst, a run of kaikkilai addresses by a man to a girl too young to answer (56 to 59), and comic dialogues in which the suitor, the friend and the girl argue over grace, debt, virtue and the palm-stem horse."},"kl:5":{"book":"kalittokai","num":5,"title":"மருதக்கலி 66-83 (1/2)","section":"மருதக்கலி","theme":"The first eighteen poems of the maruthakkali (66 to 83), sung as the wife's sulk against the husband's courtesans: nearly every poem opens with a pond, field or bee scene addressed to the 'man of the town', runs three refrains that set his oaths and go-betweens (bard, charioteer, washerwoman, brahmin) against the marks other women left on him, and closes with a bitter or yielding turn; one poem (76) is a kalavu exchange about gossip, and the last four make the little son the go-between, whom the courtesans also claim."},"kl:6":{"book":"kalittokai","num":6,"title":"மருதக்கலி 84-100 (2/2)","section":"மருதக்கலி","theme":"The second half of the marutham-kali: almost all dialogues between a wife and her straying husband, in which each of his excuses (a dream of Koodal's grove, a stay with the gods, a quail fight, a horse ride, a newly caught elephant, a bath in the Vaigai flood) is taken up and turned into the figure of the other woman; three poems (84 to 86) bring in the little son, whom the courtesans adorn and whom the mother nurses and teaches against his father; one (94) is a comic courtship of a dwarf and a hunchbacked woman; and the last two have the friend measure the husband, addressed as king, by the parasol, sceptre and drum and by the praises of truth, giving and justice."},"kl:7":{"book":"kalittokai","num":7,"title":"முல்லைக்கலி 101-117","section":"முல்லைக்கலி","theme":"The first seventeen poems of Nalluruthiran's mullaikkali: six long songs of the herders' bull-catch (ஏறு கோள்), in which bulls coloured like the gods gore the young men in the pen, each feat is likened to an epic killing, and the herd girls, promised in advance to the man who takes a bull, sing in the kuravai that their shoulders are for no coward and praise the Pandya; then eleven dialogues of courtship among herd girls selling buttermilk and herders blocking their way, full of banter in the images of milking and churning, in which a chaplet falling into a girl's hair, a mother's silence or the town's gossip turns secret love into the kin's decision to marry."},"kl:8":{"book":"kalittokai","num":8,"title":"நெய்தற்கலி 118-134 (1/2)","section":"நெய்தற்கலி","theme":"The first seventeen poems of Nallanthuvanar's neythalkali: laments at the shore, most at evening, which the woman addresses as a cruel ruler, a hunter or Death, framed with political similes of just and weak kings; the friend reproaches the lord of the shore with the woman's pallor, slipping bangles and his broken assurances, and the woman speaks to her heart, to the sea, the anril and the flute, or tells of a dream; one poem lists the virtues to shame him, and one, a swing song, ends with his coming."},"kl:9":{"book":"kalittokai","num":9,"title":"நெய்தற்கலி 135-150 (2/2)","section":"நெய்தற்கலி","theme":"The second half of the neythal kali of Nallanthuvanar: two friend's pleas to a lord of the shore built on refrains and on the dice game (135, 136), a heroine's night lament (137), four madal poems in which the man rides the palm horse and sings to the town and its worthies (138 to 141), six poems of a woman whose love has unhinged her, singing to onlookers, sun, moon and sea until her lover comes back (142 to 147), the evening lament (148), a friend's argument for marriage from gratitude and oath (149) and a paalai consolation set in Shiva's images (150)."},"ku:1":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":1,"title":"கடவுள் வாழ்த்து 0-20","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 0 to 20: an invocation drawn wholly in Murugan's red, then short poems across the five lands; hill courtships praised and pressed toward marriage, shore laments of sleepless nights, marutham wives who hide their husbands' wrongs and the other woman who mocks him, and paalai women who would follow or who refuse to call the leavers wise; madal and puram names (Evvi, the Kosar, Katti) used as measures of private feeling."},"ku:2":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":2,"title":"kuruntokai 21-40","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Short speeches of secret love and separation: the woman refusing the rains' sign or the town's sleep, dreams and wasted beauty, the friend's comforts and disclosures (the diviner's song, the tusker on his road), the lover's madal and his vow, and the strangers whose hearts mingle like rain in red earth (21-40)."},"ku:3":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":3,"title":"kuruntokai 41-60","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Short poems of the five lands in many voices: the woman desolate when he goes (41, 43, 46, 50, 54), the friend testing or holding the man to his word (42, 47, 53, 55), the foster mother on the eloped girl's trail (44), vows that reach past death and birth (49, 57), the consent of kin and gossip (51), the lover's sickness his friends cannot cure (58), and the pleasure of merely seeing him (60)."},"ku:4":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":4,"title":"kuruntokai 61-80","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Short speeches of the five lands: women waiting through the rains and the cold for men who went far (64-68, 75-77, 79), men praising the girl's body and eyes (62, 70-72), the friend's stratagems and refusals (61, 66, 69, 73, 74), the companion on love's folly (78) and a courtesan's taunt (80)."},"ku:5":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":5,"title":"kuruntokai 81-100","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 81 to 100: short poems of hill courtship and its dangers (the night path, the town's talk, the friend testing and pressing the girl), set beside wives waiting through the rain season, a husband's go-between mocked, and a foster mother who reads her daughter's flight too late. Puram names (Aay's Pothiyil, Anji's raids, the Kolli of Porayan and of Ori) are used as measures for private feeling."},"ku:6":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":6,"title":"kuruntokai 101-120","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 101 to 120: short speeches of the love situation in every land. Men weigh earth and heaven against one day of embrace, or find a strong man undone by a slip of a girl; women burn whether they remember or try not to, curse the cock that ends a night beside the husband, hang between love and the town's talk, and face evenings and north winds that bring no one; the friend fixes trysts, mocks the mother's diviner and turns slipping bangles into a jibe."},"ku:7":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":7,"title":"kuruntokai 121-140","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 121 to 140: short poems in every landscape, most of them turning on one image set against one feeling; the cost of a missed tryst borne by the woman (121), evening that brings night with it (122), the laughing rainy season (126), the fish that fears the lotus after the heron (127), a king captured by a brow (129), love as a season and not a sickness (136), the maxim that work is men's life turned against going (135) and the oath that stakes a giver's honour on staying (137), with a run of gossip poems at the end (139, 140)."},"ku:8":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":8,"title":"kuruntokai 141-160","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 141-160: short speeches of kalavu and separation, most in the heroine's voice: night trysts refused and longed for (141, 150, 153), the lover's heart left behind (142, 156), the friend's comfort and the coming of marriage elders (143, 146, 159), the season and the dream that fail (147, 148, 155, 160), and three hens calling for absent mates (151, 154) beside the dawn, the storm and the elopement that part lovers (144, 157, 158)."},"ku:9":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":9,"title":"kuruntokai 161-180","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 161 to 180: short speeches across all five lands. A hill man stands in the night rain at a wakeful house; the jasmine laughs at a lonely man in the rains; a girl hears her own sleeplessness in the sea; a courtesan swears by the sea that she has no claim; a man rides the palmyra horse; a young wife cooks for her husband's one word of praise; a wife curses her own laughing teeth; and in the wasteland poems the woman and her friend weigh wealth against grace and ask whether the absent man has hardened."},"ku:10":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":10,"title":"kuruntokai 181-200","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 181 to 200: short poems mostly in the wife's voice waiting through the rains (the mullai budding, evening, thunder, the cold season as Death), with a threatened palm horse, a fisher girl's net of eyes, a husband's turned taste for the wife at home, and a lover who promises his pain will outlast this life."},"ku:11":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":11,"title":"kuruntokai 201-220","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 201-220: short speeches of the love situations, waiting under the promised rains, the friend's comfort drawn from beasts on the man's road, and love argued as feast and as pain."},"ku:12":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":12,"title":"kuruntokai 221-240","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 221 to 240: short akam poems of waiting and lost beauty, in which the mullai season arrives without the man (221, 234, 240), the friend demands back the beauty the man has consumed (236, 238), the woman's wasted body leaves no shame to keep (239), and onlookers credit fate with marrying childhood rivals (229)."},"ku:13":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":13,"title":"kuruntokai 241-260","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 241 to 260: short akam poems in which the body and the land are read as evidence: eyes that weep or block a chariot (241, 256), a forehead restored by a look at his hill (249), shoulders that swell as an omen (260), peacocks and stags that misread or mark the season (250, 251), and the mother's watch and the town's talk that hem the lovers in (244, 246, 248, 257)."},"ku:14":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":14,"title":"kuruntokai 261-280","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 261 to 280: short akam poems that weigh one day of love against a lifetime (271, 280) and the world's wealth against death (267), set the veri rite's slander against a blameless love (263), and show the returning man in the rain (270) and the friend listening for his chariot bell (275)."},"ku:15":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":15,"title":"kuruntokai 281-300","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Short speeches of the five lands: women waiting through the rains and the seasons for men gone to the waste (281-283, 285, 287, 289, 290), men remembering and reassuring the hill-girl (286, 291, 300), the friend's curses, counsel and pleas in secret love (284, 292, 294, 297, 298), and the wife's grief at the husband's other women (293, 295, 296), with two poems weighing love against heaven and the world (288, 300)."},"ku:16":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":16,"title":"kuruntokai 301-320","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 301-320, mostly shore poems: the heroine sleepless at midnight (301, 302), pale or wasting (303, 316), stung by gossip she did not earn (311, 320), holding the man to his oath and the knotted bond (304, 313, 318); waiting through the rains and evenings (314, 319, 310); the paalai crescent and tusker (307); the friend's neythal that reblooms where it was weeded (309); and Kapilar's girl who is a thief of two faces (312)."},"ku:17":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":17,"title":"kuruntokai 321-340","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 321-340: night visits and their dangers on hill and shore (321, 324, 336), the friend's push for disclosure and marriage (321, 332, 333, 339), waiting at evening and midnight while the man crosses the desert for wealth (329-331), his return with the stag at its doe (338), the man's own voice on the embrace and on a girl coming of age (323, 337), and the heroine's laments for a love begun in play or broken by her own word (325, 326, 334, 340)."},"ku:18":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":18,"title":"kuruntokai 341-360","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 341 to 360: short akam poems of waiting and leaving, in which the friend and the foster mother weigh the road (343, 347, 356), the mullai season and the evening return as signs (344, 352, 358), the veri rite and night visits endanger the secret love (355, 360), marriage silences the gossip (351), and a given beauty cannot be asked back (349)."},"ku:19":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":19,"title":"kuruntokai 361-380","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 361-380: the friend at work, mocking the diviner (362), dissuading departure (363), urging marriage through tears, gossip and a broken promise (365, 372, 379), and reporting the asking (374); girls who love beyond union (361, 371, 377); courtesans and a forbearing wife in the watered town (364, 368, 370); elopement seen by friend and foster mother (369, 378); waiting through the rains (367, 380); and the man's praise of a beloved warm in winter and cool in summer (376)."},"ku:20":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":20,"title":"kuruntokai 381-401","section":"குறுந்தொகை","theme":"Kuruntokai 381 to 401: the last short akam poems, where one laugh with the shore lord costs a girl her beauty (381) and is then forbidden her (401), the evening and the night become floods to swim (386, 387, 398), pallor parts and closes like well-moss under the lover's touch (399), the friend denies the season to save the man's word (382), and the foster mother reads too late the refused milk of a girl who has gone (396)."},"nt:1":{"book":"natrinai","num":1,"title":"கடவுள் வாழ்த்து 0-20","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 0 to 20: an invocation that builds the discus-bearer's body from the cosmos, then poems across the five lands: hill girls guarding millet and hiding a first love, elopements seen by the lover as spring groves and by onlookers as a tiger's road, men who weigh wealth against a wife's gaze and stay, shore friends who press for marriage with gossip and threats of death, and a marutham wife who answers a husband's denial with an ironic portrait of the other woman; Ori's forest, Poraiyan's Thondi and Pazhaiyan's spear lend puram fame to private love."},"nt:2":{"book":"natrinai","num":2,"title":"natrinai 21-40","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 21 to 40: short poems across the five lands, the friend's voice above all; she presses the man to marry, pleads his case to the girl, mocks the god called to the veri and the husband who denies his women, and reports the girl's tears; men going for wealth and returning, a mother whose daughter has gone with her lover, a courtesan jeering at the husband's midnight visit to his son, with Korkai, Maranthai, Koodal and Kaandavaayil as measures of beauty and home."},"nt:3":{"book":"natrinai","num":3,"title":"natrinai 41-60","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 41-60 across all five lands: men leaving for wealth and the friend's arguments for and against it (41, 43, 46, 48, 52), homecomings seen by the returning man (42, 59), the girl kept at home and the veri rite misreading her sickness (47, 51, 53, 57, 60), the friend's tricks and rebuffs (45, 50, 55, 58), and the woman alone with her heart, her evening and a heron (54, 56)."},"nt:4":{"book":"natrinai","num":4,"title":"natrinai 61-80","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 61 to 80: night-long longing hidden from the mother, girls shut in the house and gossip pressing on secret love; men turned away who call the girl their only medicine; friends who keep the man from leaving for wealth that does not last; an evening of mullai and a shore at sunset; a mother picturing her eloped daughter's eyes; and Malaiyan's raid made the measure of a lover's lost relief."},"nt:5":{"book":"natrinai","num":5,"title":"natrinai 81-100","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 81 to 100: returns and promised seasons kept or mistaken (the soldier racing home to a wife who longs to host guests, the friend who praises one man's punctuality and mocks the flowers that bloom for another's absence), hill trysts at night with owl, boar and elephant as the night's dangers, shore poems of memory and pallor pressing for marriage, a woman who resents that only men may speak their love, and marutham scenes of a poor girl at a festival swing and a courtesan laughing at a husband afraid of his wife."},"nt:6":{"book":"natrinai","num":6,"title":"natrinai 101-120","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 101 to 120: the hill tryst under threat of night paths, traps and confinement; the shore's evenings and returns; the man on the road for wealth held back by memory; and in two marutham and household poems the wife's hidden strength, in poverty and in the sulk a guest dissolves."},"nt:7":{"book":"natrinai","num":7,"title":"natrinai 121-140","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 121 to 140: secret loves of the hills kept on the clock of millet-guarding and pressed toward marriage, shore laments of gossip and lost play, heroes who argue their hearts out of journeys for wealth, and returns home through rain, with the friend speaking for, against and as the woman."},"nt:8":{"book":"natrinai","num":8,"title":"natrinai 141-160","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 141-160: the night path and its dangers, feared by the woman (144, 154, 158) and refused or redirected by the friend (151, 156); the house that learns, through a mother's scent of hair (143), a girl's guilty denial (147), the town's gossip and the cane (145, 149); the madal rider's public ride (146, 152); men held back or summoned home by a woman's hair and season (141, 142, 157); separation borne (148, 153); first wonder and its answer (155, 160); a courtesan's mockery (150); and the friend's demands for marriage (159)."},"nt:9":{"book":"natrinai","num":9,"title":"natrinai 161-180","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 161 to 180: men going home and men about to leave, the friend's refusals of bards, dancers and night visits, and the jealousies of the married house."},"nt:10":{"book":"natrinai","num":10,"title":"natrinai 181-200","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 181 to 200: shore and hill love in short scenes, a sparrow's evening and a moon called to witness, mothers left behind by eloping daughters, friends who press for marriage through confinement and harvest, and Kolli's god-made figure used to measure a girl's beauty as both danger and protection."},"nt:11":{"book":"natrinai","num":11,"title":"natrinai 201-220","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 201 to 220: hill courtships pressed toward marriage by millet harvests, a mother's look and a madal the children play at; shore girls who lament nightfall or refuse to blame the man they chose; paalai partings for wealth weighed against a wife's beauty and mocked by the promised rain; and marutham rebukes that define true wealth as fear of a dependant's distress."},"nt:12":{"book":"natrinai","num":12,"title":"natrinai 221-240","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 221-240: homecoming and departure set against the seasons (the son who calls his father, the clouds like a patron's elephants, the cloud that is not noble), the friend's pressure for marriage through gossip, seclusion and the night tryst, and the woman's maxims against wealth-seeking."},"nt:13":{"book":"natrinai","num":13,"title":"natrinai 241-260","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 241 to 260: the seasons and the night in the voices of the friend and the wife; the friend reads omens and denies the rains, presses the hill lord to marry because the millet is ripening and the girl is shut in, and plays the shore lord's suit, while the wife grieves wealth set above virtue, shies from her husband at their son's side, and men give up or hurry back from journeys with the stag and his doe as their emblem."},"nt:14":{"book":"natrinai","num":14,"title":"natrinai 261-280","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 261 to 280: the friend at the hill and the shore turning night trysts, veri rites and slander into pressure for marriage; male birds that feed their pregnant mates on the neythal shore; a hero torn between his wife and the shame of poverty; two mothers whose daughters have eloped; a heroine who curses a bee for not carrying word; and a wife who has no time to sulk because her house, like the Velir's Kunrur, is always full of guests."},"nt:15":{"book":"natrinai","num":15,"title":"natrinai 281-300","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 281 to 300: the friend's voice again leads, pressing for marriage with the hidden lover listening, fearing the veri rite and the mother's watch, and refusing the night visit; men are torn between work and the woman, a mother curses the mother of the youth who took her daughter, a courtesan mocks the husband as a bee, and puram fame (Malaiyan's cattle raids at Mullur, Thazhumban's Unur, Chezhiyan's Koodal, the Cholas' Kazhar) is turned into the measure of akam loss."},"nt:16":{"book":"natrinai","num":16,"title":"natrinai 301-320","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 301 to 320: the end of the millet field and the guarded midnight in kurinji and neythal; the false rain and the lying promise of the man gone for wealth; a man melted from his journey by her silence; and the courtesan's voice, mocking a go-between and a town of anxious wives, and grieving as an old boat laid aside."},"nt:17":{"book":"natrinai","num":17,"title":"natrinai 321-340","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 321 to 340: night meetings arranged by landmarks and dreaded for the tiger, bear and lightning on the hill path; velan rites mocked and mothers who begin to see; a wife's chest claimed as inheritance and a lover's hair weighed against wealth; the heron and the monkeys that keep their mates while men stay away; and Vaanan's Sirukudi made the measure of what an unfaithful husband has taken."},"nt:18":{"book":"natrinai","num":18,"title":"natrinai 341-360","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 341 to 360: a war-camp soldier and a road-weary traveller who picture the women they left, wives who refuse the husband back from courtesans, friends who use proverb, gossip and the mother's misreading to push the man to marry, a woman who prays to the oath-god for the lover who broke his word, and a sleepless woman who asks whether the whole rejoicing world is fighting her; with Kolli of Porayan, Iruppai of Viraan and Marungai of Valuthi as measures of beauty."},"nt:19":{"book":"natrinai","num":19,"title":"natrinai 361-380","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 361 to 380: returns read in mullai garlands, bells and herdsmen's flutes, with the wife eager to feast guests; the hill girl's millet field closing as mothers sense the secret and the friend warns or threatens publicity; a lover's madal and an eloper who will fight strangers but hide from her kin; shore laments at evening; and in marutham a husband's tender memory of his wife's lying-in set against the friend's use of the same motherhood to refuse his bard."},"nt:20":{"book":"natrinai","num":20,"title":"natrinai 381-400","section":"நற்றிணை","theme":"Natrinai 381-400, the close of the anthology: waiting through the rains (Avvaiyar's rain like Anji's gifts, the lightning of Alankanam), hiding grief for the man's good name, the friend's pressure for marriage (beauty demanded back, the night path's dangers, the girl's tears at sunset), creatures that guard and feed their mates as pledges of a husband's care, the eloping man's joy, the woman who fears a next birth only for the forgetting it would bring, and the courtesan's boasts and praise."}},"verses":{"ai:1":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":1,"t":["வாழி ஆதன் வாழி அவினி","நெற்பல பொலிக பொன்பெரிது சிறக்க","எனவேட் டோ ளே யாயே யாமே","நனைய காஞ்சிச் சினைய சிறுமீன்","யாணர் ஊரன் வாழ்க","பாணனும் வாழ்க எனவேட் டேமே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker reports that the mother wished long life to Aadan Avini, abundant paddy and great gold, while she and her companion wished that the man of the thriving town, where the kaanji is in bud and the small fish are full of roe, may live long, and his bard with him.","n":"The decade's frame is set here: one line of the mother's wishes for the king and the land, one line of 'we' wishing for the lover, so a private love is set inside a public prayer for the realm. Wishing the bard well too places the lover among the bards who carry his messages and his fame. The budding tree and the fish heavy with roe are read by some as a promise of a fruitful house, but the poem itself draws no match, so they stay scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vaazhthu","selvam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 1","lb":"AI1","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெற்பல","நெல்",2,"unavu","scene","paddy"],["காஞ்சிச்","காஞ்சி",4,"maram","scene","kaanji tree"],["சிறுமீன்","சிறு மீன்",4,"pira","scene","small fish"],["பாணனும்","பாணன்",6,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[["king","அவினி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:1"},"ai:2":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":2,"t":["வாழி ஆதன் வாழி அவினி","விளைக வயலே வருக இரவலர்","எனவேட் டோ ளே யாயே யாமே","பல் இதழ் நீலமொடு நெய்தல் நிகர்க்கும்","தண்துறை யூரன் கேண்மை","வழிவழிச் சிறக்க எனவேட் டேமே."],"p":"","r":"The mother wished long life to Aadan Avini, that the fields may yield and alms-seekers may come, while the speaker and her companion wished that their bond with the man of the cool ford, where the neythal rivals the many-petalled blue lily, may grow from generation to generation.","n":"The mother's wish that suppliants come is a king's virtue, giving; the girls' wish answers it with a bond that lasts 'line after line' (வழிவழி), a marriage that outlives the lovers. The two lilies matching each other at the ford sit beside the wish for a lasting bond as an image of a well-matched pair, though the poem does not say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","natpu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 2","lb":"AI2","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இரவலர்","இரவலர்",2,"seythi","scene","alms-seekers"],["நீலமொடு","நீலம்",4,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",4,"maram","scene","water lily"]],"en":[["king","அவினி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:1"},"ai:3":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":3,"t":["வாழி ஆதன் வாழி அவினி","பால்பல ஊறுக பகடுபல சிறக்க","எனவேட் டோ ளே யாயே யாமே","வித்திய உழவர் நெல்லோடு பெயரும்","பூக்கஞு லூரன் தன்மனை","வாழ்க்கை பொலிக என வேட்டேமே."],"p":"","r":"The mother wished long life to Aadan Avini, that milk may flow and the bulls thrive, while the speaker and her companion wished that the household of the man of the flower-crowded town, where the ploughmen who sowed go home with their paddy, may flourish.","n":"The wish is for his மனை வாழ்க்கை, the life of a house, which the girls can only share if he marries: so the blessing is also a claim. The ploughmen who sowed and now carry home the grain fit that claim (what was begun is brought home), but the poem leaves the match unspoken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","ilvaazhkkai","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 3","lb":"AI3","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பால்பல","பால்",2,"unavu","scene","milk"],["பகடுபல","பகடு",2,"maa","scene","bull"],["உழவர்","உழவர்",4,"seythi","scene","ploughmen"],["நெல்லோடு","நெல்",4,"unavu","scene","paddy"]],"en":[["king","அவினி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:1"},"ai:4":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":4,"t":["வாழி ஆதன் வாழி அவினி","பகைவர்புல் ஆர்க பார்ப்பார் ஓதுக","எனவேட் டோ ளே யாயே யாமே","பூத்த கரும்பிற்காய்த்த நெல்லிற்","கழனி யூரன் மார்பு","பழனம் ஆகற்க எனவேட் டேமே."],"p":"","r":"The mother wished long life to Aadan Avini, that his enemies be reduced to eating grass and the brahmins chant their learning, while the speaker and her companion wished that the chest of the man of the field town, where sugarcane flowers and paddy bears grain, may never become a common pond.","n":"பழனம் is the town's shared water and field, open to all; to wish that his chest not become one is to fear in advance the straying husband of the field country, whose embrace every woman of the town might use. The mother's wish that enemies be brought to grass and the girls' wish against a shared chest both ask that something stay in its rightful place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","parathai","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 4","lb":"AI4","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புல்","புல்",2,"maram","scene","grass"],["பார்ப்பார்","பார்ப்பார்",2,"seythi","scene","brahmins"],["கரும்பிற்","கரும்பு",4,"maram","scene","sugarcane"],["நெல்லிற்","நெல்",4,"maram","scene","paddy"]],"en":[["king","அவினி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:1"},"ai:5":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":5,"t":["வாழி ஆதன் வாழி அவினி","பசியில் ஆகுக பிணி சேண் நீங்குக","எனவேட் டோ ளே யாயே யாமே","முதலை போத்து முழுமீன் ஆரும்","தண்துறை யூரன் தேரேம்","முன்கடை நிற்க எனவேட் டேமே."],"p":"","r":"The mother wished long life to Aadan Avini, that there be no hunger and disease go far away, while the speaker and her companion wished that the chariot of the man of the cool ford, where the male crocodile swallows fish whole, may stand at their front gate.","n":"A chariot standing openly at the front gate is the sign of a man who comes as a suitor by day, not a secret lover by night, so the wish asks for marriage without naming it. The male crocodile gulping whole fish is sometimes read as a warning about a greedy lover, but nothing in the poem matches it to him, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 5","lb":"AI5","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முதலை","முதலை",4,"maa","scene","crocodile"],["போத்து","போத்து",4,"maa","scene","male"],["முழுமீன்","முழு மீன்",4,"pira","scene","whole fish"]],"en":[["king","அவினி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:1"},"ai:6":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":6,"t":["வாழி ஆதன் வாழி அவினி","வேந்துபகை தணிக யாண்டுபல நந்துக","எனவேட் டோ ளே யாயே யாமே","மலர்ந்த பொய்கை முகைந்த தாமரைத்","தண்துறை ஊரன் வரைக","எந்தையும் கொடுக்க எனவேட் டேமே."],"p":"","r":"The mother wished long life to Aadan Avini, that the king's wars cease and his years increase, while the speaker and her companion wished that the man of the cool ford, where lotus buds rise in a pond already in flower, may marry her and that their father may give her to him.","n":"This is the decade's plainest wish: வரைக, let him marry, and எந்தையும் கொடுக்க, let our father give, naming both halves of the rite. The 'our father' (எந்தை) shows the speaker speaks as one with the girl. The pond in flower with lotuses still in bud sets a scene of ripeness about to open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 6","lb":"AI6","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாமரைத்","தாமரை",4,"maram","scene","lotus"]],"en":[["king","அவினி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:1"},"ai:7":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":7,"t":["வாழி ஆதன் வாழி அவினி","அறநனி சிறக்க அல்லது கெடுக","என வேட்டோ ளே யாயே யாமே","உளைப் பூ மருதத்துக் கிளைக் குருகு இருக்கும்","தண்துறை யூரன் தன்னூர்க்","கொண்டனன் செல்க எனவேட் டேமே."],"p":"","r":"The mother wished long life to Aadan Avini, that virtue flourish greatly and all else perish, while the speaker and her companion wished that the man of the cool ford, where a heron sits with its kin in the tufted-flowered marutham tree, may take her and go to his own town.","n":"To be taken to his town is the bride's departure from her father's house; the heron settled among its kin (கிளை) in the tree of the field country shows what she is to have there, a place in his kindred.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","varaivu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 7","lb":"AI7","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மருதத்துக்","மருதம்",4,"maram","scene","marutham tree"],["குருகு","குருகு",4,"pul","iraicchi","heron"]],"en":[["king","அவினி",1]],"dv":[["iraicchi","குருகு","not_confirmed","0/1","as the heron lives among its own flock, the man should bring her to live among his kin in his own town"]],"ch":"ai:1"},"ai:8":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":8,"t":["வாழி ஆதன் வாழி அவினி","அரசுமுறை செய்க களவில் லாகுக","எனவேட் டோ ளே யாயே யாமே","அலங்குசினை மாஅத்து அணிமயில் இருக்கும்","பூக்கஞல் ஊரன் சூளிவண்","வாய்ப்ப தாக எனவேட்டோ மே."],"p":"","r":"The mother wished long life to Aadan Avini, that he rule justly and there be no theft, while the speaker and her companion wished that the oath sworn here by the man of the flower-crowded town, where a lovely peacock sits on the swaying bough of a mango, may come true.","n":"சூள் இவண் is 'the oath here': the vow was sworn in this place, to the girl, and the wish is that it 'prove true' (வாய்ப்பதாக), which admits it might not. The mother's wish against theft (களவு இல் ஆகுக) uses the same word as hidden love, களவு, so the decade's two wish-lines touch: let there be no stealing in the land, and let the lover's stolen union be made good by his word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","sool","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 8","lb":"AI8","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாஅத்து","மா",4,"maram","scene","mango"],["அணிமயில்","மயில்",4,"pul","scene","peacock"]],"en":[["king","அவினி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:1"},"ai:9":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":9,"t":["வாழி ஆதன்வாழி அவினி","நன்றுபெரிது சிறக்க தீதில் ஆகுக","என வேட் டோ ளே யாயே யாமே","கயலார் நாரை போர்வின் சேக்கும்","தண்துறை யூரன் கேண்மை","அம்பல் ஆகற்க எனவேட் டேமே."],"p":"","r":"The mother wished long life to Aadan Avini, that good increase greatly and harm vanish, while the speaker and her companion wished that their bond with the man of the cool ford, where the heron that has fed on kayal fish sleeps on the straw stacks, may not become the talk of the town.","n":"அம்பல் is the first low murmur of gossip, before it becomes open அலர்; the wish is that the secret bond be settled before the town can name it. The heron that has eaten its fill and sleeps on the stack in plain view of the field may hint at a love that should not be left lying in the open, but the poem draws no match, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 9","lb":"AI9","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கயலார்","கயல்",4,"pira","scene","carp"],["நாரை","நாரை",4,"pul","scene","heron"],["போர்வின்","போர்",4,"pira","scene","straw stack"]],"en":[["king","அவினி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:1"},"ai:10":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":10,"t":["வாழி ஆதன் வாழி அவினி","மாரி வாய்க்க வளநனி சிறக்க","எனவேட் டோ ளே யாயே யாமே","பூத்த மாஅத்துப் புலாலஞ் சிறுமீன்","தண்துறை யூரன் தன்னோடு","கொண்டனன் செல்க எனவேட் டேமே."],"p":"","r":"The mother wished long life to Aadan Avini, that the rains come in season and wealth greatly increase, while the speaker and her companion wished that the man of the cool ford, where small fish smelling of flesh lie under the flowering mango, may take her with him and go.","n":"The decade closes as poem 7 did, with the wish that he take her and go (கொண்டனன் செல்க), but here 'with him' (தன்னோடு) replaces 'to his town': the last wish is simply to be with him. The mother's wish for timely rain is the oldest of a king's blessings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhthu","varaivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 10","lb":"AI10","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாரி","மாரி",2,"pira","scene","rain"],["மாஅத்துப்","மா",4,"maram","scene","mango"],["சிறுமீன்","சிறு மீன்",4,"pira","scene","small fish"]],"en":[["king","அவினி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:1"},"ai:11":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":11,"t":["மனைநடு வயலை வேழஞ் சுற்றும்","துறைகேழ் ஊரன் கொடுமை நாணி","நல்லன் என்றும் யாமே","அல்லன் என்னுமென் தடமென் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The wife says that, ashamed of the cruelty of the lord of the ford-town where the vayalai of the house-yard and the reed twine together, she keeps saying he is good, but her broad soft shoulders say he is not.","n":"Her words and her body take opposite sides: shame (நாணி) makes her defend him, and the wasting shoulders give the verdict she will not. Line 1 can be read as the house creeper twining round the reed of the ford, the wife still holding to a man who belongs outside, but which plant clasps which is not fixed by the syntax, so it is left as scene, not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","melivu","naanam","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 11","lb":"AI11","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["வயலை","வயலை",1,"maram","scene","vayalai creeper"],["வேழஞ்","வேழம்",1,"maram","scene","reed"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:2"},"ai:12":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":12,"t":["கரைசேர் வேழம் கரும்பிற் பூக்கும்","துறைகேழ் ஊரன் கொடுமை நன்றும்","ஆற்றுக தில்ல யாமே","தோற்கதில்லஎன் தடமென் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The wife says: let the lord of the ford-town, where the reed on the bank flowers like sugarcane, keep up his cruelty as much as he likes; as for her, let her broad soft shoulders lose.","n":"The optatives (ஆற்றுக தில்ல, தோற்கதில்ல) give up the contest rather than make it: she grants him his cruelty and her shoulders their defeat. The traditional reading of the worthless reed that flowers like the sugarcane as the other woman passing for the wife has only one clear correspondence and no stated other woman, so it is not tagged as ullurai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","melivu","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 12","lb":"AI12","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வேழம்","வேழம்",1,"maram","simile","reed"],["கரும்பிற்","கரும்பு",1,"maram","simile","sugarcane"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:2"},"ai:13":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":13,"t":["பரியுடை நன்மான் பொங்குளை யன்ன","வடகரை வேழம் வெண்பூப் பகரும்","தண்துறை ஊரன் பெண்டிர்","துஞ்சூர் யாமத்துந் துயலறி யலரே"],"p":"","r":"Of the lord of the cool ford, where the reeds of the north bank put out white flowers like the frothing plume of a fine horse, the speaker says that his women know no rest even at midnight, when the town sleeps.","n":"The whole sense turns on துயல் in the last line: taken as துயில் (sleep) the lord's women lie awake while the town sleeps; taken as distress, they are untroubled, which would make the line sarcasm. The reed's white plume is set beside a war-horse's crest, a puram ornament on a field weed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","iravu","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 13","lb":"AI13","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நன்மான்","மா",1,"maa","simile","horse"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",2,"maram","simile","reed"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:2"},"ai:14":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":14,"t":["கொடிப்பூ வேழம் தீண்டி அயல","வடுக்கொண் மாஅத்து வண்தளிர் நுடங்கும்","மணித்துறை வீரன் மார்பே","பனித்துயில் செய்யும் இன்சா யற்றே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that the chest of the lord of the gem-clear ford, where the flag-flowered reed brushes the nearby mango with young fruit and its rich shoots sway, has a sweet softness that brings cool sleep.","n":"The only poem of the decade with no complaint. The reed touching the young mango's shoots (வடுக்கொண் மாஅத்து வண்தளிர்) could hint at a man drawn to a young girl, but nothing in the poem states it, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 14","lb":"AI14","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வேழம்","வேழம்",1,"maram","scene","reed"],["மாஅத்து","மா",2,"maram","scene","mango"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:2"},"ai:15":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":15,"t":["மணல் ஆடு மலிர்நிறை விரும்பிய ஒண்தழைப்","புனலாடு மகளிர்க்குப் புணர்துணை உதவும்","வேழ மூதூர் ஊரன்","ஊரன் ஆயினும் ஊரனல் லன்னே."],"p":"","r":"The wife says that the lord of the old reed town, whose reed serves as a float and partner to the leaf-skirted women who bathe in the swelling flood, is lord of a town but not lord of her.","n":"The pun ஊரன் ஆயினும் ஊரனல்லன் empties his title: he lords it over a town but is no husband. புனலாடு with a reed float is the marutham man's water-play with other women.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 15","lb":"AI15","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["ஒண்தழைப்","தழை",1,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["வேழ","வேழம்",3,"maram","iraicchi","reed"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வேழ","confirmed","1/2","as his town's reed partners the bathing women, so the man partners them and not his wife"],["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:2"},"ai:16":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":16,"t":["ஓங்குபூ வேழத்துத் தூம்புடைத் திரள்கால்","சிறுதொழு மகளிர் அஞ்சனம் பெய்யும்","பூக்கஞல் ஊரனை யுள்ளிப்","பூப்போல் உண்கண் பொன்போர்த் தனவே."],"p":"","r":"The wife says that thinking of the lord of the flower-crowded town, where little girls pour collyrium into the hollow round stems of the tall-flowered reed, her kohl-lined eyes, once like flowers, are coated over with gold-pale pallor.","n":"Two kohls meet: the girls fill the reed tubes with collyrium, and her own kohl eyes (உண்கண்) are covered over with gold, so the town's play with the reed is set against the ruin of her painted eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","ninaivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 16","lb":"AI16","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வேழத்துத்","வேழம்",1,"maram","scene","reed"],["அஞ்சனம்","அஞ்சனம்",2,"pira","scene","collyrium"],["பூப்போல்","பூ",4,"maram","simile","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:2"},"ai:17":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":17,"t":["புதன்மிசை நுடங்கும் வேழ வெண்பூ","விசும்பாடு குருகின் தோன்றும் ஊரன்","புதுவோர் மேவலன் ஆகலின்","வறிதா கின்றுஎன் மடங்கெழு நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The wife says that since the lord of the town, where the white reed flower swaying on the bush looks like a heron flying in the sky, now desires new women, her simple heart has become empty.","n":"The reed flower only looks like a flying heron, and the new women (புதுவோர்) are named in the next breath; the image of a showy look-alike fits them, but only one correspondence can be named, so it is a near miss for ullurai. மடங்கெழு, full of simplicity, makes her trust the cause of the emptiness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","nenju","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 17","lb":"AI17","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வேழ","வேழம்",1,"maram","simile","reed"],["குருகின்","குருகு",2,"pul","simile","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:2"},"ai:18":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":18,"t":["இருஞ்சா யன்ன செருந்தியொடு வேழம்","கரும்பின் அலமரும் கழனி ஊரன்","பொருந்து மல ரன்னஎன் கண்ணழப்","பிரிந்தனன் அல்லனோ பிரியலென் என்றே."],"p":"","r":"The wife says that the lord of the field-town, where the reed sways like sugarcane beside the serunthi that is like dark sedge, made her eyes, matched like paired flowers, weep when he left: did he not say he would never leave?","n":"She quotes his own promise (பிரியலென்) against him: the question ends the poem on the broken word rather than on her grief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vaaimai","kan","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 18","lb":"AI18","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["இருஞ்சா","சாய்",1,"maram","simile","sedge"],["செருந்தியொடு","செருந்தி",1,"maram","simile",""],["வேழம்","வேழம்",1,"maram","simile","reed"],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",2,"maram","simile","sugarcane"],["மல ரன்ன","மலர்",3,"maram","simile","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:2"},"ai:19":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":19,"t":["எக்கர் மாஅத்துப் புதுப்பூம் பெருஞ்சினை","புணர்ந்தோர் மெய்ம்மணங்க் கமழும் தண்பொழில்","வேழவெண்பூ வெள் உளை சீக்கும்","ஊரன் ஆகலின் கலங்கி","மாரி மலரின் கண்பனி யுகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that because he is lord of the town of cool groves, where the newly flowered great branch of the sandbank mango smells of lovers' bodies and the white reed's plume brushes there, her eyes, troubled, shed tears like flowers in the rain.","n":"The grove itself smells of lovers (புணர்ந்தோர் மெய்ம்மணம்), so the town's very air tells her where he spends himself; ஊரன் ஆகலின், 'because he is that town's lord', makes the place the charge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","kan","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 19","lb":"AI19","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மாஅத்துப்","மா",1,"maram","scene","mango"],["வேழவெண்பூ","வேழம்",3,"maram","scene","reed"],["மலரின்","மலர்",5,"maram","simile","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:2"},"ai:20":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":20,"t":["அறுசில் கால அஞ்சிறைத் தும்பி","நூற்றிதழ்த் தாமரைப் பூச்சினை சீக்கும்","காம்புகண் டன்ன தூம்புடை வேழத்துத்","துறைநணி யூரனை உள்ளியென்","இறையேர் எல்வளை நெகிழ்புஓ டும்மே."],"p":"","r":"The wife says that thinking of the lord of the town near the ford, where the six-legged fair-winged bee brushes the flowering of the hundred-petalled lotus and the hollow reed looks like bamboo, her bright bangles, once lovely on her wrist, loosen and slip away.","n":"The bee working the many-petalled lotus is the traditional figure of the man among many women, but the poem states no other woman, only her thought of him, so it is scene. The bamboo, a hill thing, enters only as the measure of the reed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 20","lb":"AI20","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தும்பி","தும்பி",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",2,"maram","scene","lotus"],["காம்புகண்","காம்பு",3,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["வேழத்துத்","வேழம்",3,"maram","simile","reed"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:2"},"ai:21":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":21,"t":["முள்ளி நீடிய முதுநீர் அடைகரைப்","புள்ளிக் களவன் ஆம்பல் அறுக்கும்","தண்டுறை ஊரன் தெளிப்பவும்","உண்கண் பசப்பது எவன்கொல் அன்னாய்"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the mother why, although the man of the cool-ford town, where the speckled crab cuts the water lily on the banks of old water overgrown with mulli, gives his assurances, the girl's darkened eyes still turn pale.","n":"The whole poem turns on the concession in தெளிப்பவும், 'even as he assures': the pallor answers his words and contradicts them. The crab cutting the lily can be felt as the ooran harming the girl, but the scene offers only that one correspondence, so it is left as scene (a near miss for ullurai).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","oodal","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 21","lb":"AI21","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["முள்ளி","முள்ளி",1,"maram","scene","water thorn-shrub"],["களவன்","களவன்",2,"pira","scene","crab"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",2,"maram","scene","water lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:3"},"ai:22":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":22,"t":["அள்ளல் ஆடிய புள்ளிக் களவன்","முள்ளி வேரளைச் செல்லும் ஊரன்","நல்லசொல்லி மணந்துஇனி","நீயேன் என்றது எவன்கொல் அன்னாய்"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the mother why the man of the town where the speckled crab, having wallowed in the mire, goes back into its hole at the mulli's roots, after speaking fair words and joining her, said that now he would never leave her.","n":"நீயேன் is read as the negative first person of நீ, 'I will not leave'; the question is then why he made a promise his conduct now belies. The crab's return to its own hole after the mud can be read as the man going back to his own house after his pleasures, but only that one match holds, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 22","lb":"AI22","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["களவன்","களவன்",1,"pira","scene","crab"],["முள்ளி","முள்ளி",2,"maram","scene","water thorn-shrub"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:3"},"ai:23":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":23,"t":["முள்ளி வேரளைக் களவன் ஆட்டிப்","பூக்குற்று எய்திய புனல் அணி யூரன்","தேற்றஞ் செய்துநப் புணர்ந்தினித்","தாக்கணங்கு ஆவ தெவன்கொல் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks why the man of the town adorned by floodwater, which stirred the crab out of its hole in the mulli roots and came on plucking flowers, having given his assurance and joined with us, should now become a goddess that strikes.","n":"தாக்கணங்கு, a deity that strikes those who approach it, is the word for what the lover has become: the one who should heal is now the cause of the sickness, which is why the friend speaks to the mother, who would otherwise look for the god. நப், 'us', joins the friend to the girl.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","vaaimai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 23","lb":"AI23","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["முள்ளி","முள்ளி",1,"maram","scene","water thorn-shrub"],["களவன்","களவன்",1,"pira","scene","crab"],["பூக்குற்று","பூ",2,"maram","scene","flowers"],["புனல்","புனல்",2,"pira","scene","flood water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:3"},"ai:24":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":24,"t":["தாய்சாப் பிறக்கும் புள்ளிக் களவனொடு","பிள்ளை தின்னும் முதலைத்து அவனூர்","எய்தினன் ஆகின்று கொல்லோ மகிழ்நன்","பொலந்தொடி தெளிர்ப்ப முயங்கியவர்","நலங்கொண்டு துறப்பது எவன்கொல் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether the husband has come to share the nature of his town, the town of the speckled crab whose birth kills its mother and of the crocodile that eats its own young, since he takes the beauty of the women he embraced till their gold bangles rang and then abandons them.","n":"The two creatures are both kin-destroyers, the young that kill the parent and the parent that eats the young, so the friend charges the man with destroying those who are his own. Because she states the link (அவனூர் எய்தினன் ஆகின்று கொல்லோ), it is an open juxtaposition, not ullurai.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","nalam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 24","lb":"AI24","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["களவனொடு","களவன்",1,"pira","scene","crab"],["முதலைத்து","முதலை",2,"pira","scene","crocodile"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:3"},"ai:25":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":25,"t":["அயல்புறந் தந்த புனிற்றுவளர் பைங்காய்","வயலைச் செங்கொடி களவன் அறுக்கும்","கழனி யூரன் மார்புபலர்க்கு","இழைநெகிழ் செல்லல் ஆகும் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that the chest of the man of the field town, where the crab cuts the red stem of the vayalai creeper, with its fresh young green pods, that has spread over from the neighbouring yard, brings bangle-loosening grief to many women.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 25","lb":"AI25","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வயலைச்","வயலை",2,"maram","ullurai","vayalai creeper"],["களவன்","களவன்",2,"pira","ullurai","crab"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","களவன் வயலைச்","confirmed","1/1","the ooran and the young women of other houses he spoils"]],"ch":"ai:3"},"ai:26":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":26,"t":["கரந்தைஅம் செறுவில் துணைதுறந்து களவன்","வள்ளை மென்கால் அறுக்கும் ஊரன்","எம்மும் பிறரும் அறியான்","இன்னன் ஆவது எவன்கொல் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the mother why the man of the town where, in a karanthai-grown field, the crab leaves its mate and cuts the soft stalks of the vallai, cares for neither us nor the other women, and has become such a man.","n":"The karanthai here is the field plant, not the puram thinai of the same name. The crab's abandoning its mate is also an act a person of the situation does, but it is the same carrier as the ullurai, so no iraicchi is tagged.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","orutalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 26","lb":"AI26","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கரந்தைஅம்","கரந்தை",1,"maram","scene","karanthai plant"],["களவன்","களவன்",1,"pira","ullurai","crab"],["வள்ளை","வள்ளை",2,"maram","ullurai","vallai creeper"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","களவன் வள்ளை","confirmed","1/1","the ooran who leaves his own woman and harms others"]],"ch":"ai:3"},"ai:27":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":27,"t":["செந்நெலம் செறுவில் கதிர் கொண்டு களவன்","தண்அக மண் அளைச் செல்லும் ஊரற்கு","எல் வளை நெகிழ சாஅய்","அல்லல் உழப்பது எவன்கொல் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks why, because of the man of the town where the crab carries off an ear of grain from the red-paddy field into its cool earthen hole, the girl, her bright bangles slipping and her body wasted, labours in grief.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","nalam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 27","lb":"AI27","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["செந்நெலம்","செந்நெல்",1,"maram","scene","red paddy"],["கதிர்","கதிர்",1,"maram","ullurai","ear of grain"],["களவன்","களவன்",1,"pira","ullurai","crab"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","களவன் கதிர்","not_confirmed","0/1","the ooran who takes the girl's beauty and keeps to his own house"],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:3"},"ai:28":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":28,"t":["உண்துறை அணங்கிவள் உறைநோய் ஆயின்","தண்சேறு களவன் வரிக்கும் ஊரற்கு","ஒண்டொடி நெகிழச் சாஅய்","மென் தோள் பசப்பது எவன்கொல் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that if this lingering sickness were the work of the goddess of the drinking ford it would be one thing, but asks why, for the man of the town where the crab scores lines in the cool mud, the girl's soft shoulders turn pale as her bright bangles slip and she wastes.","n":"The friend names the cure the mother would seek (the goddess of the ford) in order to set it aside: the cause is a man, and that is the disclosure the poem makes. The opening conditional is compressed and its construction is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","anangu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 28","lb":"AI28","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["அணங்கிவள்","அணங்கு",1,"deyvam","scene","goddess"],["களவன்","களவன்",2,"pira","scene","crab"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:3"},"ai:29":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":29,"t":["மாரி கடிகொளக் காவலர் கடுக","வித்திய வெண் முளை களவன் அறுக்கும்","கழனி ஊரன் மார்புற மரீஇத்","திதலை அல்குல் நின்மகள்","பசலை கொள்வது எவன்கொல் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the mother why your daughter, with the spotted loins, takes on pallor after she came close to the chest of the man of the field town, where the crab cuts the white sprouts that the watchmen sowed in haste when the rains set in.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kalaviyal","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 29","lb":"AI29","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மாரி","மாரி",1,"pira","scene","rains"],["காவலர்","காவலர்",1,"seythi","ullurai","field watchmen"],["வெண்","வெண் முளை",2,"maram","ullurai","white sprouts"],["களவன்","களவன்",2,"pira","ullurai","crab"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","களவன் வெண் காவலர்","confirmed","1/1","the girl guarded by her family and the ooran who reached her"]],"ch":"ai:3"},"ai:30":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":30,"t":["வேப்புநனை யன்ன நெடுங்கள் களவன்","தண்அக மண்அளை நிறைய நெல்லின்","இரும்பூ உறைக்கும் ஊரற்குஇவள்","பெருங்கவின் இழப்பது எவன்கொல் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks why, because of the man of the town where the crab with long eyes like neem buds fills its cool earthen hole with the large flowers of the paddy, this girl loses her great beauty.","n":"The crab's hole is filled while the girl is emptied of beauty; the contrast is felt but only the one match (his gain, her loss) holds, so it is scene, a near miss for ullurai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","melivu","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 30","lb":"AI30","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வேப்புநனை","வேம்பு நனை",1,"maram","simile","neem buds"],["களவன்","களவன்",1,"pira","simile","crab"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",2,"maram","scene","paddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:3"},"ai:31":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":31,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி மகிழ்நன்","கடனன்று என்னும் கொல்லோ","நம்மூர் முடமுதிர் மருதத்துப் பெருந்துறை","உடனாடு ஆயமோடு உற்ற சூளே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman asks her friend whether the lord will now say he owes nothing on the oath he swore at the great ford of their town, by the old bent marutham tree, in front of the band of women bathing together.","n":"The oath's only witnesses are the bathing companions (உடனாடு ஆயம்), not a god; her fear is not that he will break it but that he will call it no debt at all (கடனன்று), which a human witness cannot prevent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sool","parathai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 31","lb":"AI31","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மருதத்துப்","மருதம்",3,"maram","scene","marutham tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:4"},"ai:32":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":32,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி மகிழ்நன்","ஒருநாள் நம்மில் வந்ததற்கு எழுநாள்","அழுப என்பஅவன் பெண்டிர்","தீயுறு மெழுகின் ஞெகிழ்வனர் விரைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells her friend that, they say, for the one day the lord came to her house his women weep for seven, melting quickly like wax held to fire.","n":"The arithmetic (ஒருநாள் ... எழுநாள்) is the mockery: one visit costs the wives a week of tears, and the wax simile makes their grief look soft and helpless rather than wronged.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 32","lb":"AI32","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["மெழுகின்","மெழுகு",4,"pira","simile","wax"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:4"},"ai:33":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":33,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி மகிழ்நன்","மருதுயர்ந்து ஓங்கிய விரிபூம் பெருந்துறைப்","பெண்டிரோடு ஆடும் என்பதன்","தண்தார் அகலம் தலைத்தலைக் கொளவே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells her friend that, they say, the lord bathes with women at the great ford where the marutham trees stand tall among spread flowers, so that his cool-garlanded chest is taken by one after another.","n":"தலைத்தலைக் கொள: his chest passes from hand to hand like a shared float; she reports it as hearsay (என்ப), so the jealousy is hers but the evidence is the town's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","alar","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 33","lb":"AI33","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மருதுயர்ந்து","மருது",2,"maram","scene","marutham tree"],["தண்தார்","தார்",4,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:4"},"ai:34":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":34,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நம்மூர்ப்","பொய்கைப் பூத்த புழைக் கால் ஆம்பல்","தாதுஏர் வண்ணம் கொண்டன","ஏதிலாளர்க்குப் பசந்தஎன் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells her friend that her eyes, grown pale for a man who is a stranger to her, have taken the colour of the pollen of the hollow-stalked water lily in their town's pond.","n":"ஏதிலாளர் (a stranger, one with no tie) is bitter: the man she pales for has no bond to her that anyone would recognise, so even her pallor has no rightful claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kan","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 34","lb":"AI34","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",2,"maram","simile","water lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:4"},"ai:35":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":35,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நம்மூர்ப்","பொய்கை ஆம்பல் நார்உரி மென்கால்","நிறத்தினும் நிழற்றுதல் மன்னே","இனிப்பசந் தன்றுஎன் மாமைக் கவினே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells her friend that her dark beauty once glowed even beyond the colour of the soft, fibre-peeled stalk of the water lily in their town's pond, and now it has turned pale.","n":"மன்னே marks a state that is over: the comparison is of what she was, and the last line, which recurs in Ainkurunuru 144, closes it with the present pallor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","nalam","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 35","lb":"AI35","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",2,"maram","scene","water lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:4"},"ai:36":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":36,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி யூரன்","நம்மறந்து அமைகுவன் ஆயின் நாம்மறந்து","உள்ளாது அமைதலும் அமைகுவம் மன்னே","கயலெனக் கருதிய உண்கண்","பசலைக்கு ஒல்கா ஆகுதல் பெறினே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells her friend that if the man of the town can forget them and be content, they too could forget him and be content, if only her kohl-lined eyes, once taken for carp, did not give way to pallor.","n":"The will to forget is claimed in full (அமைகுவம்) and then taken back by the body: the condition she cannot meet is her own eyes, so the pride of the first lines is undone by the last.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pasalai","kan","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 36","lb":"AI36","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கயலெனக்","கயல்",4,"pira","simile","carp fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:4"},"ai:37":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":37,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி மகிழ்நன்","நயந்தோர் உண்கண் பசந்துபனி மல்க","வல்லன் வல்லன் பொய்த்தல்","தேற்றான் உற்ற சூள்வாய்த் தல்லே"],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells her friend that the lord, who makes the kohl-lined eyes of those who loved him pale and brim with tears, is skilled, truly skilled, at lying, and never shows the oath he swore to be true.","n":"வல்லன் வல்லன் is ironic praise: his one proven skill is deceit, and the paling, streaming eyes of those who trusted him are the evidence against his oath.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sool","vaaimai","kan","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 37","lb":"AI37","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:4"},"ai:38":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":38,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி மகிழ்நன்","தன்சொல் உணர்ந்தோர் அறியலன் என்றும்","தண் தளிர் வெளவும் மேனி","ஒள்தோடி முன்கை யாம்அழப் பிரிந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells her friend that the lord never cares for those who understood and trusted his word, for he left her weeping, she whose body outshines cool young leaves and whose forearm wears a bright bangle.","n":"Line 4's half-line (ஒள்தொடி முன்கை யாம் அழப் பிரிந்து) recurs in 40, where it leads to where he went; here it leads only to the charge that he forgets those who believed him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","sol","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 38","lb":"AI38","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தளிர்","தளிர்",3,"maram","simile","young leaves"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:4"},"ai:39":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":39,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி யூரன்","வெம்முலை யடைய முயங்கி நம்வயின்","திருந்திழைப் பணைத்தோள் ஞெகிழப்","பிரிந்தனன் ஆயினும் பிரியலன் மன்னே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells her friend that the man of the town, who once embraced her so close that her warm breasts pressed against him, has left so that her bamboo-smooth jewelled shoulders grow slack, though once he was a man who would never leave.","n":"The last line can be read two ways: 'though he has left, he was once one who never left' (மன்னே of a past state), or 'though he has left, he does not leave (my heart)'; the first is taken here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","pirivu","melivu","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 39","lb":"AI39","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:4"},"ai:40":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":40,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி மகிழ்நன்","ஒள்தொடி முன்கை யாம் அழப் பிரிந்துதன்","பெண்டிர் ஊர் இறை கொண்டனன் என்ப","கெண்டை பாய்தர அவிழ்ந்த","வண்டுபிணி ஆம்பல் நாடுகிழ வோனே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells her friend that, they say, the lord of the land where the leaping kendai fish opens the water lily that the bees had waited on has left her weeping and settled in the town of his wives.","n":"The closing epithet answers the news: as the fish's leap opens the closed lily, his coming home opens his wives, the same wives who in 32 melted for a week over one day's absence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","pirivu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 40","lb":"AI40","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கெண்டை","கெண்டை",4,"pira","ullurai","kendai fish"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",5,"maram","ullurai","water lily"],["வண்டுபிணி","வண்டு",5,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","கெண்டை ஆம்பல்","confirmed","2/2","the lord and the wives he has gone back to"]],"ch":"ai:4"},"ai:41":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":41,"t":["தன்பார்ப்புத் தின்னும் அன்புஇல் முதலையொடு","வெண்பூம் பொய்கைத்து அவனூர்என்ப அதனால்","தன்சொல் உணர்ந்தோர் மேனி","பொன்போல் செய்யும் ஊர்கிழ வோனே."],"p":"","r":"Speaking of the man in the third person, the woman says his town is said to have a pond of white flowers with a loveless crocodile that eats its own young, and that is why the lord of that town turns gold-pale the bodies of those who took his word as true.","n":"The crocodile that devours its own young is the hidden image a reader expects in marutham, but here அதனால் (therefore) states the inference outright: the town breeds such a lord. So it fails ullurai condition 2 and stands as a juxtaposition; the plural உணர்ந்தோர் makes her one of several women he has paled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","vanjam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 41","lb":"AI41","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முதலையொடு","முதலை",1,"pira","scene","crocodile"],["வெண்பூம்","பூ",2,"maram","scene",""],["பொய்கைத்து","பொய்கை",2,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:5"},"ai:42":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":42,"t":["மகிழ்மிகச் சிறப்ப மயங்கினள் கொலோ","யாணர் ஊரநின் மாண் இழை யரிவை","காவிரி மலிர்நிறை யன்னநின்","மார்புநனி விலக்கல் தொடங்கி யோளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man of the rich town whether his well-jewelled wife has lost her wits from too much joy, that she has begun to thrust away his chest, broad as the full flood of the Kaviri.","n":"The question is ironic: what the friend calls a joy-drunk delusion is the wife's deliberate sulk. The flood simile flatters his chest and at the same time marks it as something that sweeps over everything in its path.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 42","lb":"AI42","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[],"en":[["river","காவிரி",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:5"},"ai:43":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":43,"t":["அம்பணத் தன்ன யாமை யேறிச்","செம்பின் அன்ன பார்ப்புப் பலதுஞ்சும்","யாணர் ஊர நின்னினும்","பாணன் பொய்யன் பல்சூ ளினனே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the man of the rich town, where many copper-red young sleep on the back of a tortoise shaped like a measuring vessel, that his bard is an even greater liar than he is and swears many oaths.","n":"நின்னினும் (more than you) is the sting: in calling the bard the greater liar she calls the husband a liar too. The young asleep on the tortoise's back might suggest the household resting on the man, but that is one match of mood, not two pairs, so it stays scene and simile.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","vaaimai","sool"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 43","lb":"AI43","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["யாமை","யாமை",1,"pira","simile","tortoise"],["பார்ப்புப்","பார்ப்பு",2,"pira","simile","young ones"],["பாணன்","பாணன்",4,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:5"},"ai:44":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":44,"t":["தீம்பெரும் பொய்கை யாமை இளம்பார்ப்புத்","தாய்முகம் நோக்கி வளர்ந்திசின் ஆஅங்கு","அதுவே ஐயநின் மார்பே","அறிந்தனை ஒழுகுமதி அறனுமார் அதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that as the young of the tortoise in the sweet wide pond grow by looking at their mother's face, so is his chest to his wife; knowing this, he should act on it, for that is also virtue.","n":"The old belief that the tortoise feeds its young by its gaze makes the claim exact: she needs only his presence, not his provision. The last words turn a love complaint into a matter of அறன், duty as well as feeling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","ilvaazhkkai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 44","lb":"AI44","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யாமை","யாமை",1,"pira","simile","tortoise"],["இளம்பார்ப்புத்","பார்ப்பு",1,"pira","simile","young ones"],["பொய்கை","பொய்கை",1,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:5"},"ai:45":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":45,"t":["கூதிர் ஆயின் தண் கலிழ் தந்து","வேனில் ஆயின் மணிநிறங் கொள்ளும்","யாறுஅணிந் தன்றுநின் ஊரே","பசப்பு அணிந்தனவால் மகிழ்நஎன் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her husband that his town is adorned by a river which in the cold season brings cool muddy water and in summer takes the colour of sapphire, while her eyes are adorned with pallor.","n":"The one verb அணி serves both halves: the river's changing colours adorn his town in every season, her only ornament is the colour of grief. The river that changes with the seasons may also hint at a man who changes, but the poem leaves that unsaid.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kan","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 45","lb":"AI45","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["யாறு","யாறு",3,"pira","scene","river"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:5"},"ai:46":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":46,"t":["நினக்கே அன்றுஅஃது எமக்குமார் இனிதே","நின்மார்பு நயந்த நன்னுதல் அரிவை","வேண்டிய குறிப்பினை யாகி","ஈண்டுநீ அருளாது ஆண்டுறை தல்லே."],"p":"","r":"With bitter irony the woman tells the man that it is pleasant not only for him but for her too that he lives there, as the bright-browed woman who loves his chest wished, and shows no grace here.","n":"ஈண்டு (here) and ஆண்டு (there) carry the whole poem: two houses, and he has chosen the other. The praise of the rival as நன்னுதல் is part of the mockery.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 46","lb":"AI46","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:5"},"ai:47":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":47,"t":["முள்ளெயிற்றுப் பாண்மகள் இன்கெடிறு சொரிந்த","அகன்பெரு வட்டி நிறைய மனையோள்","அரிகால் பெரும்பயறு நிறைக்கும் ஊர","மாண் இழை ஆயம் அறியும்நின்","பாணன் போலப் பலபொய்த் தல்லே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the man of the town where his wife fills, with big striped-stalk beans, the wide basket in which the sharp-toothed bard-woman poured sweet ketiru fish, that her well-jewelled companions know he tells many lies, just like his bard.","n":"A near miss for ullurai: the bard-woman bringing her catch into the wife's house could stand for the bard's go-between work, but the poem names only one situation (lying) and the barter gives no second pair; record it as scene. The witnesses are the companions, so the lie is public.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 47","lb":"AI47","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பாண்மகள்","பாண்மகள்",1,"seythi","scene","bard-woman"],["இன்கெடிறு","கெடிறு",1,"pira","gift_food","fish"],["பெரும்பயறு","பயறு",3,"unavu","gift_food","pulse"],["பாணன்","பாணன்",5,"seythi","simile","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:5"},"ai:48":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":48,"t":["வலைவல் பாண்மகன் வாலெயிற்று மடமகள்","வராஅல் சொரிந்த வட்டியுள் மனையோள்","யாண்டுகழி வெண்ணெல் நிறைக்கும் ஊர","வேண்டேம் பெருமநின் பரத்தை","யாண்டுச் செய்குறியோடு ஈண்டுநீ வரலே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the man of the town where his wife fills, with white paddy kept for years, the basket in which the young daughter of the net-skilled bard poured varaal fish, that she does not want him coming here, lord, with the marks he made there with his other woman.","n":"The bard family that trades fish at the door is the same that carries the husband's messages to the courtesan, which is why the barter can carry the sense. யாண்டு in line 3 (years) and யாண்டு in line 5 (there) are a pun the poem builds on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 48","lb":"AI48","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாண்மகன்","பாண்மகன்",1,"seythi","scene","bard"],["வராஅல்","வரால்",2,"pira","ullurai","fish"],["வெண்ணெல்","வெண்ணெல்",3,"unavu","ullurai","paddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","வராஅல் வெண்ணெல்","confirmed","1/1","the husband who brings the other woman's marks into the wife's house and expects to be received"]],"ch":"ai:5"},"ai:49":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":49,"t":["அஞ்சில் ஓதி அசைநடைப் பாண்மகள்","சில்மீன் சொரிந்து பல்நெல் பெறூஉம்","யாணர் ஊரநின் பாண்மகன்","யார்நலம் சிதையப் பொய்க்குமோ இனியே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks the man of the rich town, where the soft-haired bard-woman of swaying gait pours out a few fish and gets much paddy, whose beauty his bard will now ruin with his lies.","n":"Near miss for ullurai: few fish for much paddy matches the bard who gets much for a few false words, but the poem gives only that one correspondence (condition 3). இனியே (now) implies her own beauty is already spent: he has no one left to deceive here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","vanjam","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 49","lb":"AI49","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பாண்மகள்","பாண்மகள்",1,"seythi","scene","bard-woman"],["சில்மீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","gift_food","fish"],["பல்நெல்","நெல்",2,"unavu","gift_food","paddy"],["பாண்மகன்","பாண்மகன்",3,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:5"},"ai:50":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":50,"t":["துணையோர் செல்வமும் யாமும் வருந்துதும்","வஞ்சி ஓங்கிய யாணர் ஊர","தஞ்சம் அருளாய் நீயேநின்","நெஞ்சம் பெற்ற இவளுமார் அழுமே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the rich town where the vanji tree stands tall that the wealth of his household and they themselves suffer, that he gives no refuge, and that this woman who won his heart weeps as well.","n":"Line 1 is hard: துணையோர் செல்வமும் may be the wealth of those bound to him, or the kin and their wealth together; either way the suffering is the household's, not the wife's alone, and இவளும் comes last as the climax.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","ilvaazhkkai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 50","lb":"AI50","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வஞ்சி","வஞ்சி",2,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:5"},"ai:51":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":51,"t":["நீருறை கோழி நீலச் சேவல்","கூருகிர்ப் பேடை வயாஅம் ஊர","புளிங்காய் வேட்கைத்து அன்றுநின்","மலர்ந்த மார்பிவள் வயாஅ நோய்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the town where the sharp-clawed hen of the blue water-cock has her pregnancy cravings that this girl's craving is not a longing for sour tamarind but a sickness that only his opened chest will cure.","n":"The town epithet already shows a pregnant bird with her mate; the friend then sets the woman's craving against the bird's and names the cure as the husband's embrace, so the pregnancy becomes her claim on a man who is elsewhere. The usual craving of pregnant women for tart fruit is denied in order to say what she really wants.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","punarchi","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 51","lb":"AI51","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோழி","கோழி",1,"pul","scene","water-fowl"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",1,"pul","scene","cock"],["பேடை","பேடை",2,"pul","scene","hen"],["புளிங்காய்","புளி",3,"maram","absent","tamarind"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:6"},"ai:52":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":52,"t":["வயலைச் செங்கொடிப் பிணையல் தைஇச்","செவ்விரல் சிவந்த சேயரி மழைக்கண்","செவ்வாய்க் குறுமகள் இனைய","எவ்வாய் முன்னின்று மகிழ்நநின் தேரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man, while the young girl with red mouth and red-lined rain-wet eyes grieves, her red fingers stained from weaving garlands of the red vayalai creeper, in front of which house his chariot now stands.","n":"The poem is built in red: the creeper, her fingers, her eyes reddened by weeping, her mouth; the one question at the end points to the houses of other women without naming them. The girl is still young enough to be playing with creeper garlands.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 52","lb":"AI52","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வயலைச்","வயலை",1,"maram","ornament","vayalai creeper"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:6"},"ai:53":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":53,"t":["துறைஎவன் அணங்கும் யாம்உற்ற நோயே","சிறையழி புதுப்புனல் பாய்ந்தெனக் கலங்கிக்","கழனித் தாமரை மலரும்","பழன ஊர நீயுற்ற சூளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the town of ponds, where the field lotus blooms though stirred muddy when the new flood breaks the dam and pours in, why anyone should think the ford's spirit afflicts them: the sickness they suffer is the oath he swore.","n":"The household's diagnosis is possession by the deity of the ford (துறை அணங்கும்); the friend replaces it with his broken oath, which was presumably sworn at that ford. The flood that breaks its bank and the lotus that blooms though muddied invite a hidden reading (his broken bound, her troubled faithfulness), but only one pair is clear, so it stays scene. Line 3 recurs in Ainkurunuru 94.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sool","anangu","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 53","lb":"AI53","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புதுப்புனல்","புதுப் புனல்",2,"pira","scene","new flood"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",3,"maram","scene","lotus"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:6"},"ai:54":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":54,"t":["திண்தேர்த் தென்னவன் நல்நாட்டு உள்ளதை","வேனில் ஆயினும் தண்புனல் ஒழுகும்","தேனூர் அன்ன இவள் தெரிவளை நெகிழ","ஊரின் ஊரனை நீதர வந்த","பஞ்சாய்க் கோதை மகளிர்க்கு","அஞ்சுவல் அம்ம அம்முறை வரினே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man, as this girl who is like Tenur in the Pandya's good land, where cool water flows even in summer, finds her chosen bangles slipping, that she fears for the girls in sedge garlands who came because he brought them, should their turn come too.","n":"The fear is not for the wife but for the new women: what has happened to her will happen to them in its order (அம்முறை). The Pandya's town that never runs dry is the measure of the beauty that has now run dry in her. The construction of lines 4 to 6 is uncertain (ஊரின் ஊரனை, and whom the man 'brought').","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","nalam","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 54","lb":"AI54","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தண்புனல்","தண் புனல்",2,"pira","simile","cool water"],["பஞ்சாய்க்","பஞ்சாய்",5,"maram","ornament","sedge"]],"en":[["king","தென்னவன்",1],["town","தேனூர்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:6"},"ai:55":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":55,"t":["கரும்பின் எந்திரம் களிறெதிர் பிளிற்றும்","தேர்வண் கோமான் தேனூர் அன்னஇவள்","நல்லணி நயந்துநீ துறத்தலின்","பல்லோர் அறியப் பசந்தன்று நுதலே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that because he desired this girl's fine beauty and then left her, she who is like Tenur of the chariot-giving lord, where the sugarcane press roars back at the elephants, now has a brow gone pale for many to see.","n":"பல்லோர் அறிய makes the pallor public: it is evidence against him in the town, not only her private suffering. The noisy cane-press answering the elephants gives Tenur the sound of plenty, set against the silent sign on her brow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","nalam","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 55","lb":"AI55","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",1,"maram","scene","sugarcane"],["எந்திரம்","கரும்பு எந்திரம்",1,"seythi","scene","cane press"],["களிறெதிர்","களிறு",1,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["town","தேனூர்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:6"},"ai:56":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":56,"t":["பகல்கொள் விளக்கோடு இராநாள் அறியா","வெல்போர்ச் சோழர் ஆமூர் அன்ன இவள்","நலம்பெறு சுடர்நுதல் தேம்ப","எவன்பயம் செய்யும்நீ தேற்றிய மொழியே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man what use his reassuring words are, now that the bright brow of this girl, who is like the war-winning Cholas' Amur, where lamps that hold off the day's end leave no night known, has faded.","n":"The town that never knows night because its lamps burn on is matched to a brow called சுடர்நுதல், a brow that gives light; its dimming (தேம்ப) is the one fact the man's words cannot argue away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","vaaimai","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 56","lb":"AI56","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["விளக்கோடு","விளக்கு",1,"pira","simile","lamp"]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",2],["town","ஆமூர்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:6"},"ai:57":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":57,"t":["பகலின் தோன்றும் பல்கதிர்த் தீயின்","ஆம்பல் அம் செறுவின் தேனூர் அன்ன","இவள் நலம் புலம்பப் பிரிய","அனைநலம் உடையளோ மகிழ்நநின் பெண்டே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man whether his woman has beauty to match this girl's, who is like Tenur with its fields of water lilies that glow in daylight like a many-rayed fire, that he leaves her beauty to languish alone.","n":"The question is mocking: the friend assumes no rival can equal her, so his leaving can only be folly. நின் பெண்டே turns the other woman into his, with the wife left without a word for herself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","nalam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 57","lb":"AI57","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",2,"maram","simile","water lily"]],"en":[["town","தேனூர்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:6"},"ai:58":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":58,"t":["விண்டு அன்ன வெண்ணெல் போர்வின்","கைவண் விராஅன் இருப்பை அன்ன","இவள் அணங்கு உற்றனை போறி","பிறர்க்கு மனையையால் வாழி நீயே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that he looks like one seized by this girl's beauty, she who is like Iruppai of generous-handed Viraan, where stacks of white paddy stand like mountains; yet he keeps house for others, long may he live.","n":"அணங்கு உற்றனை போறி is ironic: he acts the possessed lover here while his household is elsewhere (பிறர்க்கு மனையையால்); the closing blessing வாழி நீயே is said in scorn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","anangu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 58","lb":"AI58","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வெண்ணெல்","வெண் நெல்",1,"unavu","simile","white paddy"]],"en":[["patron","விராஅன்",2],["town","இருப்பை",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:6"},"ai:59":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":59,"t":["கேட்டிசின் வாழியோ மகிழ்ந ஆற்றுற","மையல் நெஞ்சிற்கு எவ்வம் தீர","நினக்குமருந் தாகிய யான்இனி","இவட்குமருந்து அன்மை நோம்என் நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man to listen: she who once became the medicine that ended the pain of his love-mad heart now grieves in her own heart that she can be no medicine for this girl.","n":"The friend recalls her old part as go-between in the secret love, when she cured him by bringing them together; now that he has strayed, the same friend has no cure for the wife, so her service in kalavu turns into helplessness in karpu.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","kaamanoy","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 59","lb":"AI59","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:6"},"ai:60":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":60,"t":["பழனக் கம்புள் பயிர்ப்பெடை அகவும்","கழனியுரநின் மொழிவல் என்றும்","துங்சுமனை நெடுநகர் வருதி","அஞ்சா யோஇவள் தந்தைகை வேலே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the field-town, where the water-hen in the pond calls its mate, that she will say it plainly: he comes again and again to the tall house while all are asleep; does he not fear the spear in this girl's father's hand?","n":"A marutham poem with a kurinji situation: the night visit to an unmarried girl's house and the father's spear are the stock pressures of kalavu toward marriage. The water-hen calling openly to its mate in the field is set against the lover who comes by stealth at night.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 60","lb":"AI60","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["கம்புள்","கம்புள்",1,"pul","iraicchi","water-hen"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கம்புள்","confirmed","1/1","the bird calls its mate in the open, while the man comes to her only by stealth at night; he should claim her openly"]],"ch":"ai:6"},"ai:61":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":61,"t":["நறுவடி மாஅத்து விளைந்துகு தீம் பழம்","நெடுநீர்ப் பொய்கைத் துடுமென விழூஉம்","கைவண்மத்தி கழாஅர் அன்ன","நல்லோர் நல்லோர் நாடி","வதுவை அயர விரும்புதி நீயே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her husband that he is set on seeking out good women, one fine woman after another, women as rich as Kazhar of open-handed Matti, where the sweet ripe mango falls with a plop into the deep pond, and on celebrating marriage with them.","n":"The doubled நல்லோர் நல்லோர் is the sting: not one new woman but a series. The falling ripe fruit belongs to Kazhar, not to the husband, so it is description of the town, not a hidden comparison; but Kuruntokai 8 opens with the same formula line, where the courtesan has the vaalai fish snatch the fruit, so a reader of the anthologies hears the fruit that drops into whatever water lies below as the man who falls to whoever is near (one pair only, a near miss on ullurai).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 61","lb":"AI61","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["மாஅத்து","மா",1,"maram","scene","mango"],["பழம்","பழம்",1,"maram","scene","ripe fruit"],["பொய்கைத்","பொய்கை",2,"pira","scene","pond"]],"en":[["patron","மத்தி",3],["town","கழாஅர்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:7"},"ai:62":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":62,"t":["இந்திர விழவின் பூவின் அன்ன","புன் தலைப் பேடை வரிநிழல் அகவும்","இவ்வூர் மங்கையர்த் தொகுத்துஇனி","எவ்வூர் நின்றன்று மகிழ்நநின் தேரே."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks her husband where his chariot now stands, having gathered up the young women of this town, while the soft-headed peahen, bright as the flowers of Indra's festival, calls in the dappled shade.","n":"The simile can be read two ways: the many-coloured flowers of Indra's festival describe the peahen's plumage, or, read across the line, the gathered women of the town, bright and many as festival flowers; either way the question 'in which town now?' implies this town's women are already used up.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 62","lb":"AI62","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பேடை","பேடை",2,"pul","simile","peahen"],["பூவின்","பூ",1,"maram","simile","festival flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:7"},"ai:63":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":63,"t":["பொய்கைப் பள்ளிப் புலவுநாறு நீர்நாய்","வாளை நாளிரை பெறூஉம் ஊர","எம்நலம் தொலைவ தாயினும்","துன்னலம் பெருமபிறர்த் தோய்ந்த மார்பே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the lord of the town where the flesh-smelling otter of the pond gets a vaalai fish for its daily food that even if her beauty wastes away she will not come near his chest, which has lain against other women.","n":"She accepts the loss of her own beauty (the usual sign of a wife's suffering) as the price of refusing him, so the refusal is chosen, not forced.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 63","lb":"AI63","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நீர்நாய்","நீர்நாய்",1,"maa","ullurai","otter"],["வாளை","வாளை",2,"pira","scene","vaalai fish"],["பொய்கைப்","பொய்கை",1,"pira","scene","pond"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","நீர்நாய்","confirmed","2/2","the husband who goes to other women"]],"ch":"ai:7"},"ai:64":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":64,"t":["அலமரல் ஆயமோடு அமர்துணை தழீஇ","நலமிகு புதுப்புனல் ஆடக் கண்டோர்","ஒருவரும் இருவரும் அல்லர்","பலரே தெய்யஎம் மறையா தீமே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her husband not to hide it from her: those who saw him bathing in the fine new flood with his laughing troupe, his beloved companion in his arms, were not one or two but many.","n":"The proof is a count of witnesses, not her own sight: she argues as at law, and 'many' turns the private wrong into public talk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 64","lb":"AI64","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["புதுப்புனல்","புதுப்புனல்",2,"pira","scene","new flood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:7"},"ai:65":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":65,"t":["கரும்புநடு பாத்தியில் கலித்த ஆம்பல்","சுரும்புபசி களையும் பெரும்புன லூர","புதல்வனை ஈன்றஎம் மேனி","முயங்கன்மோ தெய்யநின் மார்புசிதைப் பதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the lord of the town of great floods, where the water-lily that sprang up in the sugarcane bed relieves the hunger of the bees, not to embrace her body, which has borne him a son, since it would spoil his chest.","n":"'It would spoil your chest' is sarcasm: his chest is kept fine for other women, and her body after childbirth is offered as the excuse. The lily that grew unplanted in a bed meant for cane and feeds the bees invites a hidden reading (the other woman, not planted in the household, feeding his appetite), but the poem never states his straying, so it fails the anchor condition and stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","makkal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 65","lb":"AI65","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["கரும்புநடு","கரும்பு",1,"maram","scene","sugarcane"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","scene","water-lily"],["சுரும்புபசி","சுரும்பு",2,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:7"},"ai:66":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":66,"t":["உடலினேன் அல்லேன் பொய்யாது உரைமோ","யாரவள் மகிழ்ந தானே தேரொடு","தளர்நடைப் புதல்வனை யுள்ளிநின்","வளவமனை வருதலும் வௌவியோளே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her husband that she is not quarrelling and asks him to say truly who she is, the woman who snatched away even his coming home to his rich house when he was thinking of his toddling son with his little chariot.","n":"The rival has taken not only the husband but the visit owed to the child; the opening disclaimer 'I am not quarrelling' is itself the quarrel.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 66","lb":"AI66","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:7"},"ai:67":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":67,"t":["மடவள் அம்மநீ இனிக்கொண்டோ ளே","தன்னொடு நிகரா என்னொடு நிகரிப்","பெருநலம் தருக்கும் என்ப விரிமலர்த்","தாதுண் வண்டினும் பலரே","ஓதி ஒண்ணுதல் பசப்பித் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The wife says that the woman he has just taken is a simple thing, since, they say, she sets herself against one who is no match for her and is proud of her great beauty, when those who have made the wife's bright forehead pale are more than the bees that drink the pollen of open flowers.","n":"The new woman's pride is answered with a count: she is one of a crowd of rivals, so her claim to rank with the wife is the naivety the opening word மடவள் names.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 67","lb":"AI67","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","மடன்"]],"k":[["வண்டினும்","வண்டு",4,"pira","simile","bee"],["விரிமலர்த்","மலர்",3,"maram","scene","open flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:7"},"ai:68":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":68,"t":["கன்னி விடியல் கணக்கால் ஆம்பல்","தாமரை போல மலரும் ஊர","பேணா ளோநின் பெண்டே","யான் தன் அடக்கவும் தான்அடங் கலளே."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks the lord of the town where at first dawn the thick-stalked water-lily opens like the lotus whether his woman shows no regard: even while she herself holds back, the other will not hold herself in.","n":"The lily that opens like the lotus is the lesser flower behaving as the greater, which is the charge against the new woman.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 68","lb":"AI68","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","ullurai","water-lily"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",2,"maram","simile","lotus"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","ஆம்பல்","confirmed","1/1","the husband's new woman"]],"ch":"ai:7"},"ai:69":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":69,"t":["கண்டனெம் அல்லமோ மகிழ்நநின் பெண்டே","பலராடு பெருந்துறை மலரொடு வந்த","தண்புனல் வண்டல் உய்த்தென","உண்கண் சிவப்ப அழுதுநின் றோளே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her husband that she has indeed seen his woman: at the great ford where many bathe, when the cool flood that came down with flowers swept away her sand-play, she stood crying till her kohl-dark eyes went red.","n":"The rival is reduced to a child at play who cries over a ruined toy; the repeated line (Ainkurunuru 125) shows the jibe was a stock one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 69","lb":"AI69","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","இளமை"]],"k":[["தண்புனல்","புனல்",3,"pira","scene","flood"],["மலரொடு","மலர்",2,"maram","scene","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:7"},"ai:70":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":70,"t":["பழனப் பன்மீன் அருந்த நாரை","கழனி மருதின் சென்னிச் சேக்கும்","மாநீர்ப் பொய்கை யாணர் ஊர","தூயர் நறியர்நின் பெண்டிர்","பேஎய் அனையம்யாம் சேய்பயந் தனமே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the lord of the town of the great-watered, ever-yielding pond, where the heron, having eaten the many fish of the field pools, rests on the crown of the marutham tree in the field, that his women are clean and fragrant while she, who has borne a son, is like a ghoul.","n":"Self-mockery as accusation: the 'ghoul' is the mother whose body has served the household. The heron that feeds on many fish and then sits clean aloft is close to an ullurai (the many fish to his many women), but a second pair is not clear, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","makkal","parathai","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 70","lb":"AI70","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["நாரை","நாரை",1,"pul","scene","heron"],["பன்மீன்","மீன்",1,"pira","scene","fish"],["மருதின்","மருது",2,"maram","scene","marutham tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:7"},"ai:71":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":71,"t":["சூதார் குறுந்தொடிச் சூரமை நுடக்கத்து","நின்வெங் காதலி தழீஇ நெருநை","ஆடினை என்ப புனலே அலரே","மறைத்தல் ஒல்லுமோ மகிழ்ந","புதைத்தல் ஒல்லுமோ ஞாயிற்றது ஒளியே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the lord of revels that people say he embraced his beloved, the woman of small fine bangles and bewitching sway, and bathed with her in the flood yesterday; such gossip can no more be hidden than the light of the sun can be buried.","n":"The charge rests on what people say (என்ப), so the wife accuses him through the town's voice rather than her own sight; the sun image turns gossip into a force of nature that no one, least of all he, can stop. The two parallel questions (மறைத்தல் ஒல்லுமோ, புதைத்தல் ஒல்லுமோ) let the second answer the first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 71","lb":"AI71","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புனலே","புனல்",3,"pira","scene","flood water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:8"},"ai:72":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":72,"t":["வயல்மலர் ஆம்பல் கயில்அமை நுடங்கு தழைத்","திதலை அல்குல் துயல்வரும் கூந்தல்","குவளை உண்கண் ஏஎர் மெல்லியல்","மலரார் மலிர்நிறை வந்தெனப்","புனலாடு புணர்துணை ஆயினள் எமக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the soft, lovely woman, wearing a swaying leaf skirt of field water-lilies on her mottled waist, with swinging hair and kohl-lined eyes like the kuvalai, became his partner in the water-play when the flood came brimming with flowers.","n":"The portrait runs from waist to hair to eyes before the verb arrives in the last line, so the woman is all ornament until she becomes a 'joined partner' (புணர்துணை). The poem never says whether she is his wife or another woman; set in this decade of charges about his bathing, his praise reads as his answer to them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 72","lb":"AI72","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","ornament","water-lily"],["குவளை","குவளை",3,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["மலரார்","மலர்",4,"maram","scene","flowers"],["புனலாடு","புனல்",5,"pira","scene","flood water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:8"},"ai:73":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":73,"t":["வண்ண ஒந்தழை நுடங்க வாலிழை","ஒண்ணுதல் அரிவை பண்ணை பாய்ந்தெனக்","கள்நறுங் குவளை நாறித்","தண்ணென் றிசினே பெருந்துறைப் புனவே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that when the bright-browed young woman in fine jewels leapt into the water-play, her bright-coloured leaf skirt swaying, the water of the great ford became cool and smelt of honeyed, fragrant kuvalai.","n":"The hyperbole runs backwards from what one expects: the river does not cool or scent the woman; her leap gives the whole great ford her coolness and fragrance. The kuvalai is not said to grow there; the water simply smells of it once she is in it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 73","lb":"AI73","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["ஒந்தழை","தழை",1,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["குவளை","குவளை",3,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["புனவே","புனல்",4,"pira","scene","water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:8"},"ai:74":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":74,"t":["விசும்பிழி தோகைச் சீர்போன் றிசினே","பசும்பொன் அவிரிழை பைய நிழற்றக்","கரைசேர் மருதம் ஏறிப்","பண்ணை பாய்வோள் தண்ணறுங் கதுப்பே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the cool, fragrant hair of the woman who climbs the marutham tree on the bank and leaps into the water-play, her bright gold jewels glinting softly, is like the grace of a peacock gliding down from the sky.","n":"The whole poem is one sentence that ends on the hair (கதுப்பே): the leap from the branch is seen through the spread of her hair as she falls, which is why the vehicle is a peacock descending rather than one dancing. The peacock is a bird of the hills brought into a field poem only as a simile.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 74","lb":"AI74","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["தோகைச்","தோகை",1,"pul","simile","peacock"],["மருதம்","மருதம்",3,"maram","scene","marutham tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:8"},"ai:75":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":75,"t":["பலர் இவண் ஒவ்வாய் மகிழ்ந அதனால்","அலர்தொடங் கின்றால் ஊரே மலர","தொன்னிலை மருதத்துப் பெருந்துறை","நின்னோடு ஆடினள் தண்புனல் அதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of revels that because he is not one of the many here, the town has begun to gossip, its talk spreading wide: that she bathed with him in the cool water at the great ford of the ancient marutham tree.","n":"Line 1 can be taken as praise (no one here is your equal, so you are watched) or as reproach (you do not fit among us); either way his standing is what turns a bath into news. மலர puns on அலர் in the same line: the gossip blooms like the flowers of the ford.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 75","lb":"AI75","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மருதத்துப்","மருதம்",3,"maram","scene","marutham tree"],["தண்புனல்","புனல்",4,"pira","scene","cool water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:8"},"ai:76":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":76,"t":["பஞ்சாய்க் கூந்தல் பசுமலர்ச் சுணங்கின்","தண்புனல் ஆடித்தன் நலம்மேம் பட்டனள்","ஒள்தொடி மடவரல் நின்னோடு","அந்தர மகளிர்க்குத் தெய்வமும் போன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells the man that the girl of bright bangles, with hair soft as panjaay sedge and freckles like fresh flowers, bathed with him in the cool water and grew in beauty until she looked like a goddess even to the women of the heavens.","n":"The praise is the reproach: the other woman's beauty rose 'with you' (நின்னோடு), and the climb from earthly to heavenly comparison is pushed so far that it reads as mockery of his infatuation. பஞ்சாய் may also point to the sedge of which girls' dolls were made, so the hair would be a doll's hair.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","nalam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 76","lb":"AI76","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பஞ்சாய்க்","பஞ்சாய்",1,"maram","simile","sedge"],["பசுமலர்ச்","மலர்",1,"maram","simile","fresh flowers"],["தண்புனல்","புனல்",2,"pira","scene","cool water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:8"},"ai:77":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":77,"t":["அம்ம வாழியோ மகிழ்நநின் மொழிவல்","பேரூர் அலர்எழ நீரலைக் கலங்கி","நின்னொடு தண் புனல் ஆடுதும்","எம்மோடு சென்மோ செல்லல்நின் மனையே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the lord of revels she has something to say: so that gossip rises through the great town, let us bathe with you in the cool water, tossed by its waves; come with us, do not go to your house.","n":"Here the gossip that the wife and her friend dread in 71 and 75 is what the other woman wants: the public bath is meant to be seen. The last line sets 'with us' against 'your house' and makes the bath a choice between them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","alar","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 77","lb":"AI77","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["புனல்","புனல்",3,"pira","scene","cool water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:8"},"ai:78":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":78,"t":["கதிரிலை நெடுவேல் கடுமான் கிள்ளி","மதில்கொல் யானையின் கதழ்புநெறி வந்த","சிறையழி புதுப்புனல் ஆடுகம்","எம்மொடு கொண்மோஎம் தோள்புரை புணையே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks the man to bathe with her in the new flood that has burst its dam and come rushing down its course like the wall-breaking elephant of Killi, he of the leaf-bladed long spear and swift horses, and to take her shoulders as his raft.","n":"A king's war elephant battering a fort wall is the image of a flood breaking a dam (சிறையழி), and the broken restraint carries over to the invitation itself. The speaker may also be the wife calling him back to her own water-play; the colophon gives no thurai to decide it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 78","lb":"AI78","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["கடுமான்","மான்",1,"maa","scene","horse"],["யானையின்","யானை",2,"maa","simile","elephant"],["புதுப்புனல்","புனல்",3,"pira","scene","new flood"]],"en":[["king","கிள்ளி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:8"},"ai:79":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":79,"t":["புதுப்புனல் ஆடி அமர்த்த கண்ணள்","யார்மகள் இவளெனப் பற்றிய மகிழ்ந","யார்மகள் ஆயினும் அறியா","நீயார் மகனைஎம் பற்றியோயே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the lord of revels, who seized her asking whose daughter this was, the girl whose eyes had reddened from bathing in the new flood: whoever's daughter she may be, who is he, whose son, to seize her without knowing?","n":"She throws his own question back word for word (யார்மகள், யார் மகனை): a man who grabs women at the water without knowing their families has no family name worth asking about. If the speaker is the wife, the barb is sharper still: he did not know his own wife with her reddened eyes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 79","lb":"AI79","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புதுப்புனல்","புனல்",1,"pira","scene","new flood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:8"},"ai:80":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":80,"t":["புலக்குவேம் அல்லேம் பொய்யாது உரைமோ","நலத்தகு மகளிர்க்குத் தோள்துணை யாகித்","தலைப்பெயல் செம்புனல் ஆடித்","தவநனி சிவந்தன மகிழ்நநின் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the lord of revels that she is not sulking and asks him to say truly why, after becoming the shoulder-companion of fine women and bathing in the red flood of the first rains, his eyes have turned so very red.","n":"'We are not sulking' opens a sulk. She offers the red, muddy first-rain flood as the reason for his red eyes and leaves him to deny the other reason, the women whose shoulders he held.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 80","lb":"AI80","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["செம்புனல்","புனல்",3,"pira","scene","red flood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:8"},"ai:81":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":81,"t":["குருகு உடைத் தூண்ட வெள் அகட்டு யாமை","அரிப்பறை வினைஞர் அல்குமிசை கூட்டும்","மலரணி வாயில் பொய்கை ஊரநீ","என்னை நயந்தனென் என்றநின்","மனையோள் கேட்கின் வருந்துவள் பெரிதே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells the lord of the town, where field workers with their rattling drum add to their evening meal the white-bellied turtle a heron has cracked open, by a pond whose mouth is decked with flowers, that if his wife heard him say 'I desired you' to her, she would grieve greatly.","n":"Her concern for the wife is mock concern: she turns his declaration of love back on him as something he could not say at home, so the line that seems kind is a taunt. The heron that cracks the turtle and the workers who gather what is left can be read as the man who takes his pleasure and the women who live on his leavings, but the poem names no second match and draws no moral, so it fails condition 3 and stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","igal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 81","lb":"AI81","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["குருகு","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","heron"],["யாமை","யாமை",1,"pira","scene","turtle"],["அரிப்பறை","அரிப் பறை",2,"parai","scene","rattle drum"],["வினைஞர்","வினைஞர்",2,"seythi","scene","field workers"],["பொய்கை","பொய்கை",3,"pira","scene","pond"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:9"},"ai:82":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":82,"t":["வெகுண்டனள் என்ப பாணநின் தலைமகள்","மகிழ்நன் மார்பின் அவிழினர் நறுந்தார்த்","தாது உண் பறவை வந்துஎம்","போதார் கூந்தல் இருந்தன எனவே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells the bard that, they say, his mistress flew into a rage because the bees that fed on the pollen of the fragrant garland of open clusters on her husband's chest came and settled in her own flower-filled hair.","n":"The proof against him is so slight that it is comic: a few bees moving from his garland to her hair. She lets the bard carry back that the wife's anger is out of proportion, while admitting the closeness the bees reveal. The bees are not iraicchi: strike them and the wife's anger has no cause, so they are the poem's subject (the deletion test fails).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 82","lb":"AI82","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["நறுந்தார்த்","நறுந் தார்",2,"maram","ornament","garland"],["பறவை","பறவை",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["போதார்","போது",4,"maram","ornament","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:9"},"ai:83":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":83,"t":["மணந்தனை அருளாய் ஆயினும் பையத்","தணந்தனை யாகி உய்ம்மோ நும்மூர்","ஒண்தொடி முன்கை ஆயமும்","தண்துறை யூரன் பண்டெனப் படற்கே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells the man who joined with her and now shows no grace that he should at least draw away gently and let her survive, so that even the bright-bangled girls of his town may say of the lord of the cool ford that he was hers once.","n":"She does not ask him to stay, only to leave slowly, so that the break looks like a fading and not a dismissal; what she guards is how she will be spoken of among the town's girls, her standing rather than his love.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","pirivu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 83","lb":"AI83","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஒண்தொடி","ஒள் தொடி",3,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:9"},"ai:84":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":84,"t":["செவியிற் கேட்பினும் சொல்லிறந்து வெகுள்வோள்","கண்ணிற் காணின் எனா குவள்கொல்","நறுவீ ஐம்பால் மகளிர் ஆடும்","தைஇத் தண்கயம் போலப்","பலர்படிந்து உண்ணுநின் பரத்தை மார்பே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman says that the wife, who rages beyond words when she only hears of it, what will she become when she sees with her own eyes his philandering chest, which many plunge into and enjoy like the cool pond of the month of Thai where women with fragrant flowers in their braids bathe.","n":"She includes herself among the many who bathe in that pond, so the simile is a boast as well as a charge: the chest is common, and she has had her share. The Thai bath was a girls' vow for a good husband, which makes the pond an ironic image for a husband shared by all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","vekuli"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 84","lb":"AI84","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["நறுவீ","நறு வீ",3,"maram","ornament","flowers"],["தண்கயம்","தண் கயம்",4,"pira","simile","pond"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:9"},"ai:85":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":85,"t":["வெண்நுதல் கம்புள் அரிக்குரல் பேடை","தண்நறும் பழனத்துக் கிளையோடு ஆலும்","மறுவில் யாணர் மலி கேழ் ஊரநீ","சிறுவரின் இனைய செய்தி","நகாரோ பெருமநின் கண்டிசினோரே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells the lord of the town of flawless, overflowing new wealth, where the white-browed water-hen's rattling-voiced mate calls among her kin in the cool fragrant field-pond, that he does things like a little boy: won't those who see him laugh?","n":"The address swings from the grand (பெரும, lord of rich new wealth) to the belittling (like little boys), and the mockery lives in that gap. The hen calling among her kin could be read as a woman secure in her household, but it does nothing a person of the situation does, so it is scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 85","lb":"AI85","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["கம்புள்","கம்புள்",1,"pul","scene","water-hen"],["பேடை","பேடை",1,"pul","scene","female bird"],["பழனத்துக்","பழனம்",2,"pira","scene","field pond"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:9"},"ai:86":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":86,"t":["வெண்தலைக் குருகின் மென்பறை விளிக் குரல்","நீள்வயல் நண்ணி இமிழும் ஊர","எம் இவண் நல்குதல் அரிது","நும்மனை மடந்தையொடு தலைப்பெய் தீமே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells the lord of the town, where the soft-winged white-headed heron's calling voice echoes across the long fields, that it is hard for him to grace her here, so let him go and join the young woman of his house.","n":"Sending him home is her sulk: she calls the wife a மடந்தை, a young girl, which both concedes the wife's claim and belittles it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 86","lb":"AI86","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:9"},"ai:87":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":87,"t":["பகன்றைக் கண்ணிப் பல்ஆன் கோவலர்","கரும்பு குணிலா மாங்கனி யுதிர்க்கும்","யாணர் ஊரைநின் மனையோள்","யாரையும் புலக்கும் எம்மைமற் றெவனோ."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells the lord of the rich town, where cowherds of many cattle, wearing pakanrai chaplets, knock down mangoes using sugarcane as a stick, that his wife sulks at everyone, so why single her out?","n":"A town so rich that herdsmen use sugarcane to beat down mangoes is a town of careless plenty, and the wife who sulks at all comers is pictured as just as indiscriminate; a reader may see the cane-stick as the wife's anger spent on anyone, but only one match can be named, so it is a near miss on condition 3 and stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 87","lb":"AI87","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["parathai","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பகன்றைக்","பகன்றை",1,"maram","ornament","pakanrai flower"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",1,"seythi","scene","cowherds"],["பல்ஆன்","ஆன்",1,"maa","scene","cattle"],["கரும்பு","கரும்பு",2,"maram","scene","sugarcane"],["மாங்கனி","மாங்கனி",2,"maram","scene","mango fruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:9"},"ai:88":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":88,"t":["வண்டுறை நயவரும் வளமலர்ப் பொய்கைத்","தண்துறை யூரனை எவ்வை எம்வயின்","வருதல் வேண்டுதும் என்ப","தொல்லேம் போல்யாம் அதுவேண் டுதுமே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman says that they report our sister, his wife, wanting the lord of the cool ford of the rich flowered pond where the bees dwell to come to her; she too, as if she were one of his old ones, wants just that.","n":"Calling the wife எவ்வை, our sister, places the two women in one family of his women; the claim to want him 'like old ones' (தொல்லேம் போல்) mocks the wife's claim of seniority by borrowing it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 88","lb":"AI88","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வண்டுறை","வண்டு",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["வளமலர்ப்","மலர்",1,"maram","scene","flowers"],["பொய்கைத்","பொய்கை",1,"pira","scene","pond"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:9"},"ai:89":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":89,"t":["அம்மவாழி பாண எவ்வைக்கு","எவன் பெரி தளிக்கும் என்ப பழனத்து","வண்டு தாதூதும் ஊரன்","பெண்டென விரும்பின்று அவள்தன் பண்பே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman tells the bard that people ask what great grace the lord of the town, where bees sip pollen in the field-pond, shows our sister: it is her own nature that wished to be called his wife.","n":"The barb is that the wife has the name of wife (பெண்டு) and is content with it; the grace she is said to receive is her title, not his love. The bee sipping pollen in the epithet foreshadows the bee image of poem 90 but does nothing a person does here beyond the epithet, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 89","lb":"AI89","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பழனத்து","பழனம்",2,"pira","scene","field pond"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:9"},"ai:90":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":90,"t":["மகிழ்நன் மாண்குணம் வண்டுகொண் டனகொல்","வண்டின் மாண்குணம் மகிழ்நன்கொண் டான்கொல்","அன்ன தாகலும் அறியாள்","எம்மொடு புலக்கும்அவன் புதல்வன் தாயே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman wonders whether the bees took on the husband's fine nature or the husband took on the bees', and says that the mother of his son, not knowing he is so, sulks with her.","n":"The 'fine quality' (மாண்குணம்) shared by bee and husband is flitting from flower to flower, so the praise is the sharpest insult in the decade; and the wife is named only as the mother of his son, the one bond the other woman cannot share.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 90","lb":"AI90","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வண்டு","வண்டு",1,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:9"},"ai:91":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":91,"t":["நெறிமருப்பு எருமை நீலைரும் போத்து","வெறிமலர்ப் பொய்கை ஆம்பல் மயக்கும்","கழனியூரன் மகளிவள்","பழன வெதிரின் கொடிப்பிணை யலளே."],"p":"","r":"The man points out the girl: she is the daughter of the lord of the field town, where the dark curved-horned buffalo bull tramples and tangles the water lilies of the fragrant pond, and she wears a twined garland made from a creeper of the field.","n":"The closing word வெதிர் normally means bamboo, an odd plant for a marutham field and for a garland; what creeper of the field it names cannot be fixed from the line, so the garland's exact material is left open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 91","lb":"AI91","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",2,"maram","scene","water lily"],["கொடிப்பிணை","கொடி",4,"maram","ornament","creeper garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:10"},"ai:92":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":92,"t":["கருங்கோட்டு எருமைச் செங்கண் புனிற்றுஆக்","காதற் குழவிக்கு ஊறுமுலை மடுக்கும்","நுந்தை நும்மூர் வருதும்","ஒண்தொடி மடந்தை நின்னையாம் பெறினே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the bright-bangled girl that, if he may have her, he will come to her father's town, where the red-eyed cow of the black-horned buffalo, newly calved, gives her flowing udder to the calf she loves.","n":"'If we get you' (நின்னையாம் பெறினே) makes the visit a suitor's: to come to the father's town is to ask for her openly, and the nursing cow in that town is the home the asking would found.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 92","lb":"AI92","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எருமைச்","எருமை",1,"maa","iraicchi","buffalo cow"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","எருமைச்","not_confirmed","0/1","the nursing mother in her father's town is the household of wife and child the man promises if he wins her in marriage"]],"ch":"ai:10"},"ai:93":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":93,"t":["எருமைநல் ஏற்றினம் மேயல் அருந்தெனப்","பசுமோ ரோடமோடு ஆம்பல் ஒல்லா","செய்த இனைய மன்ற பல்பொழில்","தாது உண் வெறுக்கைய ஆகி இவள்","போதுஅவிழ் முச்சி யூதும் வண்டே."],"p":"","r":"Because the good buffalo bulls have grazed the fresh red flowers and water lilies bare, the bees, though they have fed their fill of pollen in many groves, now come humming to the crown of this girl's head, where buds are opening.","n":"The grazed field explains the bees' move only on the surface: a reader is meant to hear that the bees, sated on every grove, still choose her flower-dressed hair, the claim the man makes for her fragrance. Line 3 (செய்த இனைய மன்ற) is hard to construe, so how the grazing and the bees are joined is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 93","lb":"AI93","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எருமைநல்","எருமை",1,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["மோரோடமோடு","மோரோடம்",2,"maram","scene","red flower"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",2,"maram","scene","water lily"],["வண்டே","வண்டு",5,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:10"},"ai:94":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":94,"t":["மள்ளர் அன்ன தடங்கோட்டு எருமை","மகளிர் அன்ன துணையோடு வதியும்","நிழல்முதிர் இலஞ்சிப் பழனத் ததுவே","கழனித் தாமரை மலரும்","கவின்பெறு சுடர்நுதல் தந்தை ஊரே."],"p":"","r":"The man says her father's town, the town of the girl with the bright beautiful forehead where the field lotus blooms, is the one with the shaded pool in the fields where the broad-horned buffalo, like a warrior, rests with his mate, who is like a woman.","n":"The two similes (மள்ளர் அன்ன, மகளிர் அன்ன) make the buffalo pair a man and woman in all but name, so the sense the bull's resting carries lies almost on the surface.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 94","lb":"AI94","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","iraicchi","buffalo bull"],["மள்ளர்","மள்ளர்",1,"seythi","simile","field warriors"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",4,"maram","scene","lotus"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","எருமை","not_confirmed","0/1","as the bull keeps beside his mate in her father's fields, the man means to settle with her as husband"]],"ch":"ai:10"},"ai:95":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":95,"t":["கருங்கோட்டு எருமை கயிறுபரிந்து அசைஇ","நெடுங்கதிர் நெல்லின் நாள்மேயல் ஆரும்","புனல்முற் றூரன் பகலும்","படர்மலி அருநோய் செய்தனன் எமக்கே."],"p":"","r":"She says the lord of the water-ringed town, where the black-horned buffalo snaps its rope, ambles off and feeds through the morning on long-eared paddy, has given her, even by day, a hard sickness swollen with longing.","n":"Whether this is the courted girl pining or the wife whose husband has slipped his tether cannot be fixed from four lines; the rope-breaking buffalo, the house's stock image of the straying lord, tilts it toward the second. 'Even by day' (பகலும்) implies the nights were already given over to the pain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","oodal","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 95","lb":"AI95","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","ullurai","buffalo"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",2,"maram","ullurai","paddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","எருமை நெல்லின்","confirmed","1/1","the lord who has slipped the bond of his house and takes his pleasure where he likes"]],"ch":"ai:10"},"ai:96":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":96,"t":["அணிநடை எருமை ஆடிய அள்ளல்","மணிநிற நெய்தல் ஆம்பலொடு கலிக்கும்","கழனி ஊரன் மகளிவள்","பழன ஊரன் பாயல்இன் துணையே."],"p":"","r":"This girl, daughter of the lord of the field town, where gem-blue neythal flowers thrive with water lilies in the mire the graceful buffalo wallowed in, is the sweet bed companion of the lord of the pond town.","n":"Two lords are named: her father, the man of the field town (கழனி ஊரன்), and the man of the pond town (பழன ஊரன்) whose bed she shares; to call her his companion of the bed is to declare the union, which is why the speaker is likely the friend making it known to the household. The flowers rising thick from the buffalo's churned mud are scene, not a hidden comparison: they make only one match.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","punarchi","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 96","lb":"AI96","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","scene","blue water lily"],["ஆம்பலொடு","ஆம்பல்",2,"maram","scene","white water lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:10"},"ai:97":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":97,"t":["பகன்றை வான்மலர் மிடைந்த கோட்டைக்","கருந்தாள் எருமைக் கன்று வெரூஉம்","பொய்கை ஊரன் மகளிவள்","பொய்கைப் பூவினும் நறுந்தண் ணியளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says this girl, daughter of the lord of the pond town, where the calf of the black-legged buffalo shies at a heap thick with white pakanrai flowers, is more fragrant and cooler than the flowers of the pond itself.","n":"The frightened calf sets a small comic note before the praise; கோட்டை here is a heap or mass of the flowers, which the calf takes for something to fear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 97","lb":"AI97","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பகன்றை","பகன்றை",1,"maram","scene","pakanrai flower"],["எருமைக்","எருமை",2,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["பூவினும்","பூ",4,"maram","simile","pond flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:10"},"ai:98":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":98,"t":["தண் புனல் ஆடும் தடங்கோட்டு எருமை","திண்பிணி அம்பியின் தோன்றும் ஊர","ஒண்டொடி மடமகள் இவளினும்","நுந்தையும் யாயும் கடியரோ நின்னே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the town, where the broad-horned buffalo bathing in cool water looks like a tightly lashed raft, that this bright-bangled young girl's father and mother are stricter with him than she is.","n":"The line concedes that the girl herself would not keep him off; what bars him is her parents' guard, so the only way to her now runs through them, that is, through asking for her in marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 98","lb":"AI98","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","simile","buffalo"],["அம்பியின்","அம்பி",2,"seythi","simile","raft"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:10"},"ai:99":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":99,"t":["பழனப் பாகல் முயிறுமூசு குடம்பை","கழனி யெருமை கதிரொடு மயக்கும்","பூக்கஞல் ஊரன் மகளிவள்","நோய்க்குமருந் தாகிய பணைத்தோ ளோளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says this broad-shouldered girl, daughter of the lord of the flower-crowded town, where the field buffalo tangles the red-ant nest hanging on the field's bitter-gourd vine together with the ears of grain, has become the medicine for his sickness.","n":"ஆகிய (has become) puts the cure in the past: she is not hoped for as a remedy but has already been one, so the poem stands after their meeting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","marundhu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 99","lb":"AI99","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பாகல்","பாகல்",1,"maram","scene","bitter gourd"],["முயிறுமூசு","முயிறு",1,"pira","scene","red ant"],["யெருமை","எருமை",2,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["கதிரொடு","கதிர்",2,"maram","scene","grain ear"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:10"},"ai:100":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":100,"t":["புனலாடு மகளிர் இட்ட ஒள்ளிழை","மணலாடு சிமையத்து எருமை கிளைக்கும்","யாணர் ஊரன் மகளிவள்","பாணர் நரம்பினும் இன்கிள வியளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says this girl, daughter of the lord of the prosperous town, where the buffalo digs up on the sand ridge the bright jewel that women bathing in the stream had dropped, has speech sweeter than the strings of the bards' lute.","n":"The buffalo turning up a lost jewel could be read as the man finding her, but that is a single match and stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inchol","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 100","lb":"AI100","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",2,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["ஒள்ளிழை","இழை",1,"pira","scene","jewel"],["பாணர்","பாணர்",4,"seythi","simile","bards"],["நரம்பினும்","நரம்பு",4,"yaazh","simile","lute strings"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:10"},"ai:101":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":101,"t":["அன்னை வாழிவேண் டன்னை உதுக்காண்","ஏர்கொடிப் பாசடும்பு பரியஊர்பு இழிபு","நெய்தல் மயக்கி வந்தன்று நின்மகள்","பூப்போல் உண்கண் மரீஇய","நோய்க்குமருந் தாகிய கொண்கன் தேரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother to look: the shore lord's chariot has come, tearing through the green adumbu creepers, riding up and down and trampling the neythal, the chariot that is the medicine for the sickness her daughter's flower-like kohled eyes have caught.","n":"The poem names the cure before the illness is admitted: the friend does not tell the mother her daughter loves, she tells her the medicine is arriving, so the disclosure is made as a diagnosis. The chariot crushing the shore plants shows a man coming openly and in haste, not by stealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","marundhu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 101","lb":"AI101","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பாசடும்பு","அடும்பு",2,"maram","scene","adumbu creeper"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","scene","neythal flower"],["பூப்போல்","பூ",4,"maram","simile","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:11"},"ai:102":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":102,"t":["அன்னை வாழிவேண் டன்னை நம்மூர்","நீல்நிறப் பெருங்கடல் புள்ளின் ஆனாது","துன்புறு துயரம் நீங்க","இன்புற இசைக்கும் அவர் தேர்மணிக் குரலே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that in their town the bell of his chariot rings sweetly, so that the girl's suffering, unceasing as the cries of the birds of the dark-blue sea, may end.","n":"Line 2 can also be read as the bell sounding without pause like the sea birds; either way the sea birds' unending noise is set against one sound that ends the noise of grief. A bell heard openly in the town is the sign of a suitor, not a secret lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kaamanoy","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 102","lb":"AI102","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["புள்ளின்","புள்",2,"pul","simile","sea bird"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:11"},"ai:103":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":103,"t":["அன்னை வாழிவேண் டன்னை புன்னையொடு","ஞாழல் பூக்கும் தண்ணந் துறைவன்","இவட்குஅமைந் தனெனால் தானே","தனக்கு அமைந்த தன்றுஇவள் மாமைக் கவினே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that the lord of the cool ford, where punnai and njaazhal flower together, has become fit for this girl, and her dark beauty has become fit for him alone.","n":"The two trees flowering together look like a hidden image of the matched pair, but it is one correspondence (the pair matched), not two, so it fails condition 3 and is left as scene: a near-miss ullurai. The claim of mutual fitness (அமைந்தனன், அமைந்தன்று) is the friend's argument for marriage put to the mother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","azhagu","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 103","lb":"AI103","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புன்னையொடு","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene","punnai tree"],["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",2,"maram","scene","njaazhal tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:11"},"ai:104":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":104,"t":["அன்னை வழிவேண் டன்னை நம்மூர்ப்","பலர்மடி பொழுதின் நலம்மிகச் சாஅய்","நள்ளென வந்த இயல்தேர்ச்","செல்வக் கொண்கன் செல்வனஃ தூரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that in their town, at the hour when everyone sleeps, when the girl's beauty had wasted, the rich shore lord came in the dead of night in his well-made chariot, and that town of his is a rich one.","n":"The midnight hour of the kurinji poems is here told to the mother: the secret night visits are themselves part of the disclosure, offered as proof of the lord's devotion and wealth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 104","lb":"AI104","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:11"},"ai:105":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":105,"t":["அன்னை வாழிவேண் டன்னை முழங்குகடல்","திரைதரு முத்தம் வெண்மணல் இமைக்கும்","தண்ணம் துறைவன் வந்தெனப்","பொன்னினும் சிவந்தன்று கண்டிசின் நுதலே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that since the lord of the cool ford came, where the waves of the roaring sea bring pearls that glint on the white sand, the girl's brow, look, has turned more golden than gold.","n":"சிவந்தன்று can be read as the glow of joy at his coming or as the gold of pallor (பசலை is gold-coloured in the Akananuru and Kural); read as pallor, the friend names the man as the cause of the change the mother has seen, which is the point of a disclosure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 105","lb":"AI105","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முத்தம்","முத்து",2,"pira","scene","pearl"],["திரைதரு","திரை",2,"pira","scene","wave"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:11"},"ai:106":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":106,"t":["அன்னை வாழிவேண் டன்னை அவர்நாட்டுத்","துதிக்கால் அன்னம் துணைசெத்து மிதிக்கும்","தண் கடல் வளையினும் இலங்கும்இவள்","அம்கலிழ் ஆகம் கண்டிசின் நினைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother to look at the girl's lovely chest as she thinks of him: it gleams brighter than the conch of the cool sea in his land, which the swan with its trunk-like legs treads on, taking it for its mate.","n":"The swan that mistakes a white conch for its mate is a near-miss iraicchi: it acts as a lover would, but no single sense for the girl follows from it, so it stays scene. Whether the brightness of her chest is beauty or the whiteness of pallor is not fixed; நினைந்தே ties it to her thoughts of him either way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 106","lb":"AI106","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அன்னம்","அன்னம்",2,"pul","scene","swan"],["வளையினும்","வளை",3,"pira","simile","conch shell"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:11"},"ai:107":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":107,"t":["அன்னை வாழிவேண் டன்னைஎன் தோழி","சுடர்நுதல் பசப்பச் சாஅய்ப் படர்மெலிந்து","தண்கடல் படுதிரை கேட்டொறும்","துஞ்சாள் ஆகுதல் நோகோ யானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that her own friend's bright brow has paled, she has wasted and thinned with longing, and whenever she hears the waves breaking on the cool sea she does not sleep, and the friend grieves for it.","n":"The sea's waves, which in other neythal poems carry the lover's chariot, here only keep her awake; the friend reports the symptoms to the mother as her own pain (நோகோ யானே), so the disclosure is also an appeal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pasalai","kaamanoy","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 107","lb":"AI107","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["படுதிரை","திரை",3,"pira","scene","wave"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:11"},"ai:108":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":108,"t":["அன்னை வாழிவேண் டன்னை கழிய","முண்டக மலரும் தண்கடற் சேர்ப்பன்","எந்தோள் துறந்தனன் ஆயின்","எவன்கொல் மற்றவன் நயந்த தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that if the lord of the cool sea shore, where the mundakam blooms in the backwater, has abandoned our shoulders, then what are they worth, the shoulders he once desired?","n":"The same shoulders are counted twice, as the ones he has left and the ones he desired: the friend makes the mother see that what he wanted is now thrown away, so the family's honour as well as the girl's is at stake.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 108","lb":"AI108","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முண்டக","முண்டகம்",2,"maram","scene","mundakam"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:11"},"ai:109":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":109,"t":["அன்னை வாழிவேண் டன்னை நெய்தல்","நீர்படர் தூம்பின் பூக்கெழு துறைவன்","எந்தோள் துறந்த காலை எவன்கொல்","பன்னாள் வரும்அவன் அளித்த போழ்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that when the lord of the ford, where neythal with hollow stems spreading on the water is full of flowers, has abandoned our shoulders, what then of the many days he came and showed her his grace?","n":"Paired with 108: there the loss is measured against the shoulders he desired, here against the many days of his grace (அளித்த போழ்தே), so the abandonment is made a breach of a history the mother has not known of.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 109","lb":"AI109","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","scene","neythal flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:11"},"ai:110":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":110,"t":["அன்னை வாழிவேண் டன்னை புன்னை","பொன்னிறம் விரியும் பூக்கெழு துறைவனை","என்னை என்றும் யாமே இவ்வூர்","பிறதொன் றாகக் கூறும்","ஆங்கும் ஆக்குமோ வாழிய பாலே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that we always call the lord of the ford, where the punnai opens its gold-coloured flowers, our own, while this town says something else of him; she asks whether fate, long may it live, will make it so there too.","n":"பால் is the apportioning fate that joins lovers; the friend appeals past the town's talk to it, asking whether it will make the bond real there, in his house and before all, as it already is in their own words (என்னை). Line 4 does not say what the town says, only that it is other.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","oozh","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 110","lb":"AI110","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene","punnai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:11"},"ai:111":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":111,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி பாணன்","சூழ்கழி மருங்கின் நாண்இரை கொளீஇச்","சினைக்கயல் மாய்க்கும் துறைவன் கேண்மை","பிரிந்தும் வாழ்துமோ நாமே","அருந்தவம் முயறல் ஆற்றா தேமே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that she could not live parted from her bond with the lord of the ford where the fisherman fixes bait on a line at the edge of the winding backwater and kills the roe-heavy kayal, since she is not one who can carry out hard penance.","n":"Separation is measured against தவம்: to live apart from him would take an ascetic's power to bear pain, and she says she has none (ஆற்றாதேமே). பாணன் here is the fisher of the shore, not the bard; the fish killed is one that carries roe, so the catch destroys more than itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thavam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 111","lb":"AI111","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பாணன்","பாணன்",1,"seythi","ullurai","fisherman"],["நாண்இரை","நாண் இரை",2,"pira","ullurai","baited line"],["சினைக்கயல்","சினை கயல்",3,"pira","ullurai","roe-heavy carp"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","பாணன் சினைக்கயல்","confirmed","1/1","the lord of the ford and the woman caught in his bond"]],"ch":"ai:12"},"ai:112":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":112,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி பாசிலைச்","செருந்தி தாய இருங்கழிச் சேர்ப்பன்","தான்வரக் காண்குவம் நாமே","மறந்தோம் மன்ற நாணுடை நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that they will see the lord of the dark backwater, where green-leaved serunthi spreads, come in person, and tells her modest heart that they have surely forgotten (their shame).","n":"The heart is addressed as நாணுடை, the one that owns modesty, at the moment she tells it the modesty is gone: she will stand and watch him come. What was forgotten is not named, and the last line was repaired in our text, so the sense is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","nenju","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 112","lb":"AI112","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["செருந்தி","செருந்தி",2,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:12"},"ai:113":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":113,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நென்னல்","ஓங்குதிரை வெண்மணல் உடைக்கும் துறைவற்கு","ஊரார் பெண்டென மொழிய என்னை","அதுகேட் டன்னாய் என்றனள் அன்னை","பைபய வெம்மை என்றனென் யானே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that yesterday the townsfolk spoke of her as the woman of the lord of the ford where high waves break the white sand; hearing it, her mother called her, and she answered softly that it was only a fever.","n":"வெம்மை, heat or fever, is her cover: she gives the mother the love's bodily sign and keeps back its cause. The mother says only அன்னாய், and the girl's பைபய, slowly, is the whole art of the concealment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 113","lb":"AI113","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["ஓங்குதிரை","ஓங்கு திரை",2,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:12"},"ai:114":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":114,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி கொண்கன்","நேரேம் ஆயினும் செல்குவம் கொல்லோ","கடலின் நாரை இரற்றும்","மடலம் பெண்ணை அவனுடை நாட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether, even if the man of the shore does not consent, they should go to his country, where the sea stork cries on the frond-thick palmyra.","n":"Kalavu sends the man to the woman's shore; here the woman proposes crossing to his, unasked (நேரேம் ஆயினும்). All she pictures of his land is a bird crying on a palm, so it is already a place of her own longing; the stork only cries, so it is scene, not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kalaviyal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 114","lb":"AI114","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நாரை","நாரை",3,"pul","scene","stork"],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",4,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:12"},"ai:115":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":115,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி பன்மாண்","நுண்மணல் அடைகரை நம்மோடு ஆடிய","தண்ணந் துறைவன் மறைஇ","அன்னை அருங்கடி வந்துநின் றோனே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the lord of the cool ford, who played with them on the shore of fine sand, has come in hiding and stands at the hard guard their mother keeps.","n":"The day's open play on the sand (நம்மோடு ஆடிய) is set against the secret approach (மறைஇ); the mother's strict guard (அருங்கடி), meant to keep him out, has become the very place where he stands.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 115","lb":"AI115","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:12"},"ai:116":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":116,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நாம் அழ","நீல இருங்கழி நீலம் கூம்பு","மாலைவந் தன்று மன்ற","காலை யன்ன காலைமுந் துறுத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that while they weep, the evening in which the blue lilies of the dark backwater close has surely come, driving before it a morning that was like it.","n":"The closing lily marks the hour as the woman's own eyes are wet (நாம் அழ). Line 4 can be read as evening having pushed the morning before it, so that even the morning was evening for her; the repeated காலை is hard to fix, and the comparison rests on that reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 116","lb":"AI116","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நீலம்","நீலம்",2,"maram","scene","blue water-lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:12"},"ai:117":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":117,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நலனே","இன்ன தாகுதல் கொடிதே புன்னை","யணிமலர் துறைதொறும் வரிக்கும்","மணிநீர்ச் சேர்ப்பனை மறவா தோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that it is cruel that beauty should come to this in those who cannot forget the lord of the gem-clear shore, where the punnai's ornament flowers draw patterns at every ford.","n":"The punnai adorns every ford of his shore while her own beauty is spent. She calls the condition cruel (கொடிதே), not the man, and speaks of herself in the general plural, those who do not forget.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","melivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 117","lb":"AI117","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",2,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:12"},"ai:118":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":118,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி யான் இன்று","அறன்இ லாளன் கண்ட பொழுதில்","சினவுவென் தகைக்குவென் சென்றனென்","பின்நினைந்து இரங்கிப் பெயர்தந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that today, on seeing the man without justice, she went meaning to rage at him and hold him back, but thinking it over she relented and came away.","n":"அறனிலாளன், the man without aram, is her charge, and line 3 lists what she meant to do; line 4 undoes it. Whether she pitied him or herself (இரங்கி) is left open. The poem stands in a neythal decade, but its situation is the quarrel of the fields, so my thinai differs from the colophon.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","aram","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 118","lb":"AI118","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:12"},"ai:119":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":119,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நன்றும்","எய்யா மையின் ஏதில பற்றி","அன்பிலன் மன்ற பெரிதே","மென்புலக் கொண்கன் வாரா தோனே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the lord of the soft shore land who does not come is surely without love, and greatly so, having in his lack of understanding held to things foreign to them.","n":"The land is soft (மென்புலம்) and the man is hard; the verdict அன்பிலன் is sharpened with மன்ற and பெரிதே. Whose lack of understanding line 2 names, his or the women's in trusting him, is not fixed by the syntax.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 119","lb":"AI119","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:12"},"ai:120":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":120,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நலமிக","நல்ல ஆயின அளியமெல் தோளே","மல்லல் இருங்கழி நீரறல்விரியும்","மெல்லம் புலம்பன் வந்த மாறே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that her pitiable soft shoulders have grown beautiful again, very much so, because the lord of the soft shore, where the water's ripples spread over the rich dark backwater, has come.","n":"அளிய, pitiable, stays on the shoulders even as they bloom: their beauty is on loan from his presence, and the decade's run of wasting and fear closes on its reversal (வந்த மாறே, because he came).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 120","lb":"AI120","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["நீரறல்விரியும்","நீர் அறல்",3,"pira","scene","water ripple"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:12"},"ai:121":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":121,"t":["கண்டிகும் அல்லமோ கொண்கநின் கேளே","முண்டகக் கோதை நனையத்","தெண்டிரைப் பௌவம் பாய்ந்துநின் றோளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the shore whether they did not see his woman, the one who leapt into the clear-waved sea and stood there with her sea-holly garland soaking.","n":"The opening question is a rebuttal: கண்டிகும் அல்லமோ presumes he has denied her, and the friend proves the woman exists by describing her at play, so the evidence and the insult are the same picture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","ilamai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 121","lb":"AI121","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","இளமை"]],"k":[["முண்டகக்","முண்டகம்",2,"maram","ornament","sea holly"],["தெண்டிரைப்","தெண் திரை",3,"pira","scene","waves"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:13"},"ai:122":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":122,"t":["கண்டகும் அல்லமோ கொண்கநின் கேளே","ஒள்ளிழை உயர்மணல் வீழ்ந்தென","வெள்ளாங் குருகை வினைவு வோளே."],"p":"","r":"Did we not see his woman, the friend says, who, when her bright jewel fell on the high sand, went asking the white heron about it?","n":"Asking a heron after a lost jewel is the act of a child who thinks birds answer; the friend uses it to measure the rival's simplicity. The verb is read as வினவுவோள் (she who asks); the line's வினைவு does not give a sense as written.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","ilamai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 122","lb":"AI122","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["குருகை","குருகு",3,"pul","scene","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:13"},"ai:123":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":123,"t":["கண்டிகும் அல்லமோ கொண்கநின் கேளே","ஒண்ணுதல் ஆயம் ஆர்ப்பத்","தண்ணென் பெருங்கடல் திரைபாய் வோளே."],"p":"","r":"Did we not see his woman, says the friend, leaping into the waves of the cool great sea while her bright-browed playmates shouted?","n":"ஆயம், the troop of girl companions, belongs to an unmarried girl's world; placing the rival inside it says she is not yet a woman at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","ilamai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 123","lb":"AI123","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","இளமை"]],"k":[["திரைபாய்","திரை",3,"pira","scene","waves"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:13"},"ai:124":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":124,"t":["கண்டிகும் அல்லமோ கொண்கநின் கேளே","வண்டற் பாவை வெளவலின்","நுண்பொடி அளைஇக் கடல்தூர்ப் போளே."],"p":"","r":"Did we not see his woman, the friend says, who, because the wave took her sand doll, scoops up fine sand to fill in the sea?","n":"The wave's theft is implied, not stated (வெளவலின் has no subject in the line); 125 names it, தெண்டிரை. Filling the sea with sand to punish it is the folly the friend wants the husband to see in his choice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","ilamai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 124","lb":"AI124","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["நுண்பொடி","நுண் பொடி",3,"pira","scene","fine sand"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:13"},"ai:125":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":125,"t":["கண்டிகும் அல்லமோ கொண்கநின்","தெண்டிரை பாவை வெளவ","உண்கண் சிவப்ப அழுதுநின் றோளே."],"p":"","r":"Did we not see his woman, the friend says, who stood weeping till her kohl-lined eyes went red because the clear wave seized her doll?","n":"The red eyes from weeping are the sign the akam poems give a deserted wife; here they come from a lost toy, which turns the heroine's grief into a child's tantrum in the rival.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","ilamai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 125","lb":"AI125","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","இளமை"]],"k":[["தெண்டிரை","தெண் திரை",2,"pira","scene","wave"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:13"},"ai:126":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":126,"t":["கண்டிகும் அல்லமோ கொண்கநின் கேளே","யுண்கண் வண்டினம் மொய்ப்பத்","தெண்கடற் பெருந்திரை மூழ்கு வோளே."],"p":"","r":"Did we not see his woman, says the friend, who plunges into the great wave of the clear sea while swarms of bees crowd about her kohl eyes?","n":"Bees swarming to the eyes treat them as flowers, the one praise in the decade; even this is play, the girl ducking under the wave to escape them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","ilamai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 126","lb":"AI126","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வண்டினம்","வண்டு இனம்",2,"pira","scene","bees"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:13"},"ai:127":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":127,"t":["கண்டிகும் அல்லமோ கொண்கநின் கேளே","தும்பை மாலை இளமுலை","நுண்பூண் ஆகம் விலங்கு வோளே."],"p":"","r":"Did we not see his woman, the friend says, who wears a thumbai garland slung across her chest, its fine ornaments over breasts only just budding?","n":"The sense turns on விலங்குவோள், read here as wearing the garland crosswise; it could also be she who turns aside or bars the way with her chest. இளமுலை, a young breast, keeps the decade's point: the rival is barely grown.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","ilamai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 127","lb":"AI127","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","இளமை"]],"k":[["தும்பை","தும்பை",2,"maram","ornament","thumbai flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:13"},"ai:128":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":128,"t":["கண்டிகும் அல்லமோ கொண்கநின் கேளே","உறாஅ வறுமுலை மடாஅ","உண்ணாப் பாவையை ஊட்டு வோளே."],"p":"","r":"Did we not see his woman, says the friend, feeding a doll that cannot eat from her empty breast that no child has sucked and that has no milk?","n":"The decade's sharpest barb closes it: the girl playing mother with a dry breast is set against the wife who has borne his children, so the husband's rival is, in the friend's telling, still rehearsing the life the wife already lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","ilamai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 128","lb":"AI128","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","இளமை"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:13"},"ai:131":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":129,"t":["நன்றே பாண கொண்கனது நட்பே","தில்லை வேலி இவ்வூர்க்","கல்லென் கௌவை எழாஅக் காலே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the bard that the friendship of the shore lord is good indeed, so long as no loud gossip rises in this town hedged with thillai.","n":"The praise is hedged by its last line: the bond is good only at a time (காலே) when the town is quiet, so the opening நன்றே turns into a complaint that it cannot survive being known. The thillai hedge makes the town an enclosure that hears everything.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","alar","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 131","lb":"AI131","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["தில்லை","தில்லை",2,"maram","scene","thillai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:14"},"ai:132":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":130,"t":["அம்ம வாழி பாண புன்னை","அரும்புமலி கானல் இவ்வூர்","அலரா கின்றுஅவர் அருளு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the bard that this town of the seashore grove, thick with punnai buds, has turned the very way the man shows her grace into gossip.","n":"What the town talks about is not his neglect but his kindness (அருளும் ஆறே): his visits are the scandal. The punnai is only in bud (அரும்புமலி), and the gossip already blooms (அலர் also means a blossom).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","arul","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 132","lb":"AI132","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene","punnai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:14"},"ai:133":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":131,"t":["யானெவன் செய்கோ பாண ஆனாது","மெல்லம் புலம்பன் பிரிந்தெனப்","புல்லென் றனஎன் புரிவளைத் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks the bard what she can do, now that the lord of the gentle shore has left and her shoulders with their twisted bangles have grown dull without end.","n":"புலம்பன், the shore lord's title, also means the lonely one; the word that names him names her state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 133","lb":"AI133","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:14"},"ai:134":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":132,"t":["காண்மதி பாண இருங்கழிப் பாய்பரி","நெடுந்தேர்க் கொண்க னோடு","தான்வந் தன்றுஎன் மாமைக் கவினே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the bard to look: her dark-gold beauty has come back of its own accord together with the shore lord of the tall chariot whose leaping horses cross the wide backwater.","n":"The beauty is the subject of the verb (தான் வந்தன்று): it returns by itself, riding in with him, as if it had left in his chariot. காண்மதி makes the bard a witness to the cure as he is elsewhere a witness to the wasting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","nalam","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 134","lb":"AI134","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பரி","பரி",1,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:14"},"ai:135":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":133,"t":["பைதலம் அல்லேம் பாண பணைத்தோள்","ஐதுஅமைந்து அகன்ற அல்குல்","நெய்தலம் கண்ணியை நேர்தல்நாம் பெறினே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells the bard that we are not sorrowful, if only we may win the consent of her with bamboo-round shoulders, a broad finely shaped waist and a chaplet of neythal flowers.","n":"நேர்தல் may be her consent or a meeting; who speaks, and whether this is the man in kalavu or the friend vouching for the woman, the poem does not fix. Line 2 recurs as line 3 of Ainkurunuru 176.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 135","lb":"AI135","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paanan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெய்தலம்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","ornament","neythal flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:14"},"ai:136":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":134,"t":["நாணிலை மன்ற பாண நீயே","கோண்ஏர் இலங்குவளை நெகிழ்த்த","கானலம் துறைவற்குச் சொல்உகுப் போயே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the bard that he truly has no shame, pouring out words for the lord of the seashore ford who has made her bright, beautifully curved bangles slip.","n":"சொல் உகுப்போய், 'you who spill words', makes the bard's eloquence a waste, set against what the lord's absence has spilled: the bangle off her wrist.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","oodal","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 136","lb":"AI136","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:14"},"ai:137":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":135,"t":["நின்னொன்று வினவுவல் பாண நும்மூர்த்","திண்தேர்க் கொண்கனை நயந்தோர்","பண்டைத் தந்நலம் பெறுபவோ."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks the bard one thing: do the women who desired the strong-chariot shore lord of his town ever get back their former beauty?","n":"The plural நயந்தோர் counts her among many women who loved him, so the question is also a charge that he has made a habit of it. நும்மூர், 'your town', puts the bard on the lord's side.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","parathai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 137","lb":"AI137","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:14"},"ai:138":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":136,"t":["பண்பிலை மன்ற பாண இவ்வூர்","அன்பில கடிய கழறி","மென்புலக் கொண்கனைத் தாரா தோயே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the bard that he truly has no decency: he has spoken loveless, harsh words in this town and has not brought back the lord of the gentle shore.","n":"Whether the harsh words are the bard's own scolding of her people or the excuses he carries for the lord is not fixed by கழறி; either way he is blamed for bringing words instead of the man.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","oodal","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 138","lb":"AI138","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:14"},"ai:139":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":137,"t":["அம்ம வாழி கொண்க எம்வயின் மாண்நலம் மருட்டும் நின்னினும்","பாணன் நல்லோர் நலம்சிதைக் கும்மே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the shore lord that more than he, who beguiles her fine beauty, it is his bard who ruins the beauty of good women.","n":"The only poem of the decade not spoken to the bard: it turns to the lord and blames the bard, so that the man is let off lightly in words while the charge stands.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","parathai","thoothu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 139","lb":"AI139","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:14"},"ai:140":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":138,"t":["காண்மதி பாணநீ யுரைத்தற் குரியை","துறைகெழு கொண்கன் பிரிந்தென","விறைகேழ் எல்வளை நீங்கிய நிலையே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the bard to look, for he is the one fit to tell it: the state in which her bright bangles, with their shining wrist-band, have left her since the lord of the ford went away.","n":"The bard, rebuked in the decade for carrying the lord's words, is here asked to carry hers, and the message is not a speech but what he has seen. The decade closes on the same காண்மதி பாண as 134, with the opposite sight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 140","lb":"AI140","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:14"},"ai:141":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":139,"t":["எக்கர் ஞாழல் செருந்தியொடு கமழத்","துவலைத் தண்துளி வீசிப்","பயலை செய்தன பனிபடு துறையே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that the shore, where the njaazhal of the dune and the cherunthi spread their scent and the cold spray is flung in drops under the falling dew, has given her the pallor of longing.","n":"She blames the place, not the man: the scented, cold, wet shore is made the agent (செய்தன) of her pallor, so the lover is accused only through his landscape.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 141","lb":"AI141","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene","njaazhal tree"],["செருந்தியொடு","செருந்தி",1,"maram","scene","cherunthi"],["துவலைத்","துவலை",2,"pira","scene","sea spray"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:15"},"ai:142":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":140,"t":["எக்கர் ஞாழல் இறங்கு இணர்ப் படுசினைப்","புள்இறை கூரும் துறைவனை","உள்ளேன் தோழி படீஇயர்என் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend she will not think of the lord of the shore, where birds settle on the low branch of the dune's njaazhal with its drooping clusters, so that her eyes may sleep.","n":"The resolve is an admission: she must decide not to think of him precisely because thinking keeps her awake. The birds coming to roost on his shore while she cannot rest could be read as an implied contrast, but they do nothing a person of the situation does beyond resting, and the poem's sense does not need them, so they stay scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kan","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 142","lb":"AI142","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene","njaazhal tree"],["புள்இறை","புள்",2,"pul","scene","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:15"},"ai:143":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":141,"t":["எக்கர் ஞாழல் புள்ளிமிழ் அகன்துறை","இனிய செய்த நின்றுபின்","முனிவு செய்தஇவள் தடமெல் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that on the wide shore where birds cry by the dune's njaazhal, this girl's broad soft shoulders, which once gave delight, have afterwards come to give weariness.","n":"இனிய செய்த நின்று பின் முனிவு செய்த sets the same shoulders on both sides of his change: what pleased him once now wearies him, or (on the other reading) now wastes and pains her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["melivu","nalam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 143","lb":"AI143","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene","njaazhal tree"],["புள்ளிமிழ்","புள்",1,"pul","scene","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:15"},"ai:144":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":142,"t":["எக்கர் ஞாழல் இணர்படு பொதும்பர்த்","தனிக் குருகு உறங்கும் துறைவற்கு","இனிப்பசந் தன்றுஎன் மாமைக் கவினே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that for the lord of the shore, where a lone heron sleeps in the grove under the dune's njaazhal heavy with clusters, her dark gleaming beauty has now turned pale.","n":"இனி (now) marks a turn: the beauty was his once, and the pallor dates his neglect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","nalam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 144","lb":"AI144","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene","njaazhal tree"],["குருகு","குருகு",2,"pul","iraicchi","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","குருகு","confirmed","2/2","on his shore even a bird alone can sleep, while she, alone because of him, loses her colour"]],"ch":"ai:15"},"ai:145":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":143,"t":["எக்கர் ஞாழல் சிறியிலைப் பெருஞ்சினை","ஓதம் வாங்கும் துறைவன்","மாயோள் பசலை நீக்கினன் இனியே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says the lord of the shore, where the tide draws at the great branches of the small-leaved njaazhal on the dune, has now removed the pallor of the dark girl.","n":"Pallor is the one sign the man alone can cure; இனியே says the cure came only now, which the friend reports as his return to marry. The tide that bends the great branch has a single correspondence at most, so it is not ullurai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 145","lb":"AI145","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene","njaazhal tree"],["ஓதம்","ஓதம்",2,"pira","scene","tide"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:15"},"ai:146":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":144,"t":["எக்கர் ஞாழல் அரும்புமுதிர் அவிழிணர்","நறிய கமழும் துறைவற்கு","இனிய மன்றஎன் மாமைக் கவினே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that to the lord of the shore, where the dune's njaazhal opens its ripened buds into fragrant clusters, her dark gleaming beauty is surely sweet.","n":"The buds that ripen and open beside the beauty that pleases him make a single match of mood, so it stays scene; மன்ற (surely) makes the claim a confident one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 146","lb":"AI146","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene","njaazhal tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:15"},"ai:147":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":145,"t":["எக்கர் ஞாழல் மலரின் மகளிர்","ஒள்தழை அயரும் துறைவன்","தண்தழை விலையென நல்கினன் நாடே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says the lord of the shore, where women make bright leaf skirts with the flowers of the dune's njaazhal, has given his country as the price of the cool leaf skirt.","n":"The leaf skirt, the small courting gift of the shore, is answered with a whole country: the friend's hyperbole turns the gift of secret love into a bride price. மலரின் could also be read as a simile (women like flowers), but the instrumental sense fits அயரும்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 147","lb":"AI147","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","ornament","njaazhal tree"],["ஒள்தழை","தழை",2,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["தண்தழை","தழை",3,"maram","gift_food","leaf skirt"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:15"},"ai:148":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":146,"t":["எக்கர் ஞாழல் இகந்துபடு பெருஞ்சினை","வீஇனிது கமழும் துறைவனை","நீயினிது முயங்குமதி காத லோயே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine: embrace sweetly, you who love, the lord of the shore where the far-reaching great branch of the dune's njaazhal gives off its sweet-scented flowers.","n":"இனிது is used twice, once of the flowers' scent and once of the embrace, so the shore's sweetness passes over into the union the friend now permits.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 148","lb":"AI148","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene","njaazhal tree"],["வீஇனிது","வீ",2,"maram","scene","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:15"},"ai:149":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":147,"t":["எக்கர் ஞாழல் பூவின் அன்ன","சுணங்குவளர் இளமுலை மடந்தைக்கு","அணங்குவளர்த்து அகறல் வல்லா தீமோ."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man not to become one able to go away after he has nursed affliction in the young girl whose growing breasts bear spots like the flowers of the dune's njaazhal.","n":"அகறல் வல்லாதீமோ blames a capacity rather than an act: do not be the kind of man who can leave. The affliction (அணங்கு) is something he has grown (வளர்த்து) in her, as her breasts are growing (வளர்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 149","lb":"AI149","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","simile","njaazhal tree"],["பூவின்","பூ",1,"maram","simile","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:15"},"ai:150":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":148,"t":["எக்கர் ஞாழல் நறுமலர்ப் பெருஞ்சினைப்","புணரி திளைக்கும் துறைவன்","புணர்வின் இன்னான் அரும்புணர் வினனே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says the lord of the shore, where the sea waves sport about the great branch of the dune's njaazhal with its fragrant flowers, is bitter in union, a man hard to unite with.","n":"The poem puns on புணரி (the wave) and புணர்வு (union): the sea's constant embrace stands against his rare one, and his union is bitter because it is rare.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 150","lb":"AI150","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene","njaazhal tree"],["புணரி","புணரி",2,"pira","iraicchi","sea waves"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","புணரி","not_confirmed","0/1","the sea never leaves off embracing the tree on his shore, yet the man himself is rarely there to embrace"]],"ch":"ai:15"},"ai:151":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":149,"t":["வெள்ளாங் குருகின் பிள்ளை செத்தெனக்","காணிய சென்ற மடநடை நாரை","மிதிப்ப நக்க கண்போல் நெய்தல்","கள்கமழ்ந்து ஆனாத் துறைவற்கு","நெக்க நெஞ்சம் நேர்கல் லேனே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that for the lord of the shore, where the neythal that opened like an eye when the simple-gaited stork trod on it, going to see what it took for the white heron's chick, keeps on breathing out its honey scent, she will not give in to the heart that has melted.","n":"The decade's refrain turns on செத்தென, which can mean 'taking it for' or 'having died': either the stork mistook something for the heron's chick or went to see a chick that had died; this poem's reading does not depend on which. The flower that blooms under the foot that crushes it is the poem's whole argument: the hurt itself made her open to him, which is why she must refuse with her will what her heart has granted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 151","lb":"AI151","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","heron"],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","ullurai","stork"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","ullurai","blue lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","நாரை நெய்தல்","confirmed","1/1","the hero who has hurt her and the woman who still softens toward him"]],"ch":"ai:16"},"ai:152":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":150,"t":["வெள்ளாங் குருகின் பிள்ளை செத்தெனக்","காணிய சென்ற மடநடை நாரை","கையறுபு இரற்றும் கானலம் புலம்பம்","துறைவன் வரையும் என்ப","அறவன் போலும் அருளுமார் அதுவே."],"p":"","r":"Where the simple-gaited stork cries out helplessly in the lonely seaside grove, they say the lord of the shore will marry her; he seems a man of virtue, and that too is grace.","n":"போலும் here is the particle of seeming, not a comparison: the speaker will believe he is virtuous only when the marriage happens. The helpless cry of the stork in the lonely grove is the grief the news is meant to end, set beside it without comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","arul","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 152","lb":"AI152","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","heron"],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","scene","stork"],["கானலம்","கானல்",3,"maram","scene","seaside grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:16"},"ai:153":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":151,"t":["வெள்ளாங் குருகின் பிள்ளை செத்தெனக்","காணிய சென்ற மடநடை நாரை","உளர ஒழிந்த தூவி குலவுமணல்","போர்வின் பெறூஉம் துறைவன் கேண்மை","நன்னெடுங் கூந்தல் நாடுமோ மற்றே."],"p":"","r":"Will the intimacy of the lord of the shore, whose heaped sand gets a covering from the down shed by the shaking stork, seek out the girl of the fine long hair?","n":"A near miss for ullurai: the shore gains its covering from feathers the bird drops without meaning to, and one may hear the lord's affection as just as idle a gift, but only one pair maps, so condition 3 fails. The sense of போர்வின் பெறூஉம் is read as the sand getting a covering; the line is hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 153","lb":"AI153","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","heron"],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","scene","stork"],["குலவுமணல்","குலவு மணல்",3,"pira","scene","sand dune"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:16"},"ai:154":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":152,"t":["வெள்ளாங் குருகின் பிள்ளை செத்தெனக்","காணிய சென்ற மடநடை நாரை","கானற் சேக்கும் துறைவனோடு","யானெவன் செய்கோ பொய்க்கும் இவ்வூரே."],"p":"","r":"With the lord of the shore where the simple-gaited stork roosts in the seaside grove, what can I do? this town tells lies.","n":"Read as gossip: the town says what is not so, and she can neither stop it nor make it true. Another reading takes பொய்க்கும் as 'will prove false' of the man; the line will bear both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 154","lb":"AI154","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","heron"],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","scene","stork"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:16"},"ai:155":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":153,"t":["வெள்ளாங் குருகின் பிள்ளை செத்தெனக்","காணிய சென்ற மடநடை நாரை","பதைப்பத் ததைந்த நெய்தல் கழிய","ஓதமொடுபெயரும் துறைவற்குப்","பைஞ்சாய்ப் பாவை ஈன்றனென் யானே."],"p":"","r":"For the lord of the shore who goes away with the tide, where neythal flowers crushed as the stork flutters float down the backwater, I have borne a doll of green sedge, the woman says.","n":"The sedge doll is a girl's toy, so to say she has 'borne' one to him is to say their union has left her a child still, with no issue and no standing. A near miss for ullurai (the flowers the stork crushes and the tide carries off, the man who comes and goes with it) fails condition 3 on a second clear pair.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","ilamai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 155","lb":"AI155","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","heron"],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","scene","stork"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["ஓதமொடுபெயரும்","ஓதம்",4,"pira","scene","tide"],["பைஞ்சாய்ப்","பைஞ்சாய்",5,"maram","scene","sedge"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:16"},"ai:156":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":154,"t":["வெள்ளாங் குருகின் பிள்ளை செத்தெனக்","காணிய சென்ற மடநடை நாரை","பதைப்ப ஒழிந்த செம்மறுத் தூவி","தெள்கழிப் பரக்கும் துறைவன்","எனக்கோ காதலன் அனைக்கோ வேறே."],"p":"","r":"The lord of the shore, where the red-flecked down that the fluttering stork has shed spreads over the clear backwater, is to me a lover and to my mother someone else.","n":"One man, two names in one house: the poem states in a line the whole difficulty of secret love before it is disclosed, and so the need for marriage, without asking for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 156","lb":"AI156","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","heron"],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","scene","stork"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:16"},"ai:157":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":155,"t":["வெள்ளாங் குருகின் பிள்ளை செத்தெனக்","காணிய சென்ற மடநடை நாரை","காலை யிருந்து மாலைச் சேக்கும்","தெண்கடல் சேர்ப்பனொடு வாரான்","தான்வந் தனன்எம் காத லோனே."],"p":"","r":"Where the stork stays through the morning and roosts at evening by the clear sea, our dear one did not come with the lord of the shore; he came by himself.","n":"The bird that is there from morning to evening is set against a lord who is not, which favours the reading in which the man stays away and someone dear comes alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 157","lb":"AI157","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","heron"],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","scene","stork"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:16"},"ai:158":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":156,"t":["வெள்ளாங் குருகின் பிள்ளை செத்தெனக்","காணிய சென்ற மடநடை நாரை","கானலம் பெருந்துறைத் துணையொடு கொட்கும்","தண்ணந் துறைவன் கண்டிக்கும்","அம்மா மேனிஎம் தோழியது துயரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the lord of the cool shore, where the stork wheels about with its mate on the great grove-side shore, is the cause of the sorrow we see on her friend's lovely dark body.","n":"கண்டிக்கும் is read as the decade-neighbour's refrain கண்டிகும், 'we saw'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","melivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 158","lb":"AI158","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","heron"],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","iraicchi","stork"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","நாரை","not_confirmed","0/1","even the stork keeps its mate beside it, while the lord leaves her to a sorrow that shows on her body"]],"ch":"ai:16"},"ai:159":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":157,"t":["வெள்ளாங் குருகின் பிள்ளை செத்தெனக்","காணிய சென்ற மடநடை நாரை","பசிதின அல்கும் பனிநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப","நின்ஒன்று இரக்குவன் அல்லேன்","தந்தனை சென்மோ கொண்டஇவள் நலனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the cold-water shore, where the stork rests while hunger eats at it, that she is not begging anything of him: let him give back the beauty he took from her and go.","n":"'I am not begging' is the edge of the line: what she asks is not a gift but a return, so his refusal would be theft, not stinginess. The stork that waits hungry is a mood here, not an act the lovers share, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","varaivu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 159","lb":"AI159","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","heron"],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","scene","stork"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:16"},"ai:160":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":158,"t":["வெள்ளாங் குருகின் பிள்ளை செத்தெனக்","காணிய சென்ற மடநடை நாரை","நொந்ததன் தலையும் நோய்மிகும் துறைவ","பண்டையின் மிகப்பெரிது இனைஇ","முயங்குமதி பெரும மயங்கினள் பெரிதே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore that on top of her pain the sickness grows, and grieving far more than before she is badly bewildered: embrace her, lord.","n":"நொந்ததன் தலையும் may also be heard of the stork, which went to see a chick taken for dead and now grieves on top of grief, so the refrain's bird is the woman's double; the friend does not draw the match.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","punarchi","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 160","lb":"AI160","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","heron"],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","scene","stork"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:16"},"ai:161":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":159,"t":["பெருங்கடற் கரையது சிறுவெண் காக்கை","கருங்கோட்டுப் புன்னைத் தங்கும் துறைவற்குப்","பயந்தநுதல் அழியச் சாஅய்","நயந்த நெஞ்சம் நோய்ப்பாலஃதே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that for the lord of the ford where the small white gull of the great sea's shore rests on the black-branched punnai, her forehead, once spread with pallor, has wasted away, and the heart that desired him has gone over to sickness.","n":"The gull has a fixed perch on the punnai while her heart has none; the decade's refrain sets every poem against this bird that always has somewhere to rest. Read as scene: the gull only rests, it does nothing a person of the situation does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kaamanoy","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 161","lb":"AI161","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["காக்கை","சிறுவெண் காக்கை",1,"pul","scene","gull"],["பெருங்கடற்","பெருங்கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["புன்னைத்","புன்னை",2,"maram","scene","punnai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:17"},"ai:162":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":160,"t":["பெருங்கடற் கரையது சிறுவெண் காக்கை","நீத்துநீர் இருங்கழி இரைதேர்ந்து உண்டு","பூக்கமழ் பொதும்பர்ச் சேக்கும்","துறைவன் சொல்லோ பிறவா யினவே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that the words of the lord of the ford, where the small white gull picks its food from the flowing dark backwater and then sleeps in the fragrant grove, have become something else.","n":"Near miss for ullurai: the gull that feeds in one place and sleeps in another fragrant one can be read as the man who takes his pleasure elsewhere, but only one correspondence can be named, so condition 3 fails and the bird stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","vaaimai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 162","lb":"AI162","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["காக்கை","சிறுவெண் காக்கை",1,"pul","scene","gull"],["பெருங்கடற்","பெருங்கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["இருங்கழி","இருங்கழி",2,"pira","scene","backwater"],["பொதும்பர்ச்","பொதும்பர்",3,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:17"},"ai:163":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":161,"t":["பெருங்கடற் கரையது சிறுவெண் காக்கை","இருங்கழித் துவலை ஒலியில் துஞ்சும்","துறைவன் துறந்தெனத் துறந்துஎன்","இறையேர் முன்கை நீக்கிய வளையே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that because the lord of the ford, where the small white gull sleeps through the roar of the backwater's spray, has abandoned her, the bangles too have abandoned her shapely forearm.","n":"The verb துற is used twice, துறந்தென துறந்து: the bangles copy his act, so her wasting body becomes a second desertion, and the pun on துறைவன் (lord of the ford) makes his title name the deed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 163","lb":"AI163","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["காக்கை","சிறுவெண் காக்கை",1,"pul","iraicchi","gull"],["பெருங்கடற்","பெருங்கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["துவலை","துவலை",2,"pira","scene","spray"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","காக்கை","confirmed","1/1","the man who left sleeps as easily, untouched by the uproar his leaving has raised in her"]],"ch":"ai:17"},"ai:164":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":162,"t":["பெருங்கடற் கரையது சிறுவெண் காக்கை","இருங்கழி மருங்கின் அயிரை ஆரும்","தண்ணந் துறைவன் தகுதி","நம்மோடு அமையாது அலர்பயந் தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that the fitness of the cool lord of the ford, where the small white gull feeds on ayirai fish at the edge of the dark backwater, did not stay a matter between them but has bred gossip.","n":"தகுதி, his fitness or worth, is the thing that has gone public: the praise of the man is itself what the town now talks about, so the gossip is born of his good name, not his fault.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 164","lb":"AI164","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["காக்கை","சிறுவெண் காக்கை",1,"pul","scene","gull"],["பெருங்கடற்","பெருங்கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["அயிரை","அயிரை",2,"pira","scene","small fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:17"},"ai:165":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":163,"t":["பெருங்கடற் கரையது சிறுவெண் காக்கை","அறு கழிச் சிறுமீன் ஆர மாந்தும்","துறைவன் சொல்லிய சொல்என்","இறையோர் எல்வளை கொண்டுநின் றதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that the word spoken by the lord of the ford, where the small white gull gorges on the little fish of the drying backwater, has taken the bright bangles from her wrist.","n":"She blames the word, not the man: a promise unkept is the thief of the bangles, so the poem makes speech the agent of her wasting. The gull eating its fill of small fish from a drying pool is left as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","melivu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 165","lb":"AI165","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["காக்கை","சிறுவெண் காக்கை",1,"pul","scene","gull"],["பெருங்கடற்","பெருங்கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["சிறுமீன்","சிறு மீன்",2,"pira","scene","small fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:17"},"ai:166":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":164,"t":["பெருங்கடற் கரையது சிறுவெண் காக்கை","வரிவெண் தாலி வலைசெத்து வெரூஉம்","மெல்லம் புலம்பன் தேறி","நல்ல வாயின நல்லோள் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"Having come to trust the gentle lord of the shore, where the small white gull takes fright at the striped white thaali, thinking it a net, the good woman's eyes have become well again.","n":"What the gull fears is uncertain: வரிவெண் தாலி may be a striped white shell or a plant of the shore; either way the point is that it is harmless and only looks like a net.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","kan","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 166","lb":"AI166","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["காக்கை","சிறுவெண் காக்கை",1,"pul","iraicchi","gull"],["பெருங்கடற்","பெருங்கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["தாலி","தாலி",2,"pira","scene","striped white thing, a shell or plant"],["வலைசெத்து","வலை",2,"seythi","scene","net"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","காக்கை","not_confirmed","0/1","her earlier fear of the man was a fear of a snare that was never there, and his word has now shown it harmless"],["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:17"},"ai:167":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":165,"t":["பெருங்கடற் கரையது சிறுவெண் காக்கை","இருங்கழி இனக்கெடிறு ஆரும் துறைவன்","நல்குவன் போலக் கூறி","நல்கான் ஆயினும் தொல் கேளன்னே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that the lord of the ford, where the small white gull feeds on shoals of ketiru fish in the dark backwater, spoke as if he would show grace and did not, yet he is still an old companion.","n":"The final word turns the complaint: the broken promise is stated plainly, and then excused by the length of the bond, so the old tie survives the very failure she has just named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","kizhamai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 167","lb":"AI167","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காக்கை","சிறுவெண் காக்கை",1,"pul","scene","gull"],["பெருங்கடற்","பெருங்கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["இனக்கெடிறு","கெடிறு",2,"pira","scene","catfish shoal"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:17"},"ai:168":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":166,"t":["பெருங்கடற் கரையது சிறுவெண் காக்கை","துறைபடி யம்பி அகமணை ஈனும்","தண்ணந் துறைவன் நல்கின்","ஒண் நுதல் அரிவை பாலா ரும்மே."],"p":"","r":"If the cool lord of the ford, where the small white gull lays its eggs inside a boat moored at the landing, would show grace, the bright-browed girl would drink her milk again.","n":"Refusing milk is the sign of a girl wasting in secret love; the friend names the cure as the man's grace, which in kalavu means marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","kalaviyal","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 168","lb":"AI168","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["காக்கை","சிறுவெண் காக்கை",1,"pul","iraicchi","gull"],["பெருங்கடற்","பெருங்கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["யம்பி","அம்பி",2,"seythi","scene","boat"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","காக்கை","confirmed","1/1","even the gull settles its household at his landing, so he too should take her home and make a household with her"]],"ch":"ai:17"},"ai:169":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":167,"t":["பெருங்கடற் கரையது சிறுவெண் காக்கை","பொன்னிணார் ஞாழல் முனையில் பொதியவிழ்","புன்னையம் பூஞ்சினைச் சேக்கும் துறைவன்","நெஞ்சத்து உண்மை யறிந்தும்","என்செயப் பசக்கும் தோழியென் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend why, though she knows the truth in the heart of the lord of the ford, where the small white gull, tired of the golden-clustered nyazhal, rests on the flowering punnai bough with its bursting buds, her eyes still turn pale.","n":"Near miss for iraicchi: the gull that tires of one tree (முனையில்) and moves to another could carry a doubt of his constancy against her stated trust, but the act is not clearly one the man does or should do in the poem, so it stays scene. The poem's point is that knowledge does not govern the body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","nenju","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 169","lb":"AI169","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["காக்கை","சிறுவெண் காக்கை",1,"pul","scene","gull"],["பெருங்கடற்","பெருங்கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",2,"maram","scene","nyazhal tree"],["புன்னையம்","புன்னை",3,"maram","scene","punnai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:17"},"ai:170":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":168,"t":["பெருங்கடற் கரையது சிறுவெண் காக்கை","இருங்கழி நெய்தல் சிதைக்குந் துறைவன்","நல்லன் என்றி யாயின்","பல்லிதழ் உண்கண் பசத்தல்மற் றெவனோ."],"p":"","r":"If you say he is good, the woman asks her friend, then why do her many-petalled, kohl-lined eyes turn pale over the lord of the ford, where the small white gull tears the neythal flowers of the dark backwater?","n":"பல்லிதழ் (many-petalled) is the word for a flower, so her eyes are the neythal the gull tears; the friend's நல்லன் is answered by the image, not by argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kan","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 170","lb":"AI170","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["காக்கை","சிறுவெண் காக்கை",1,"pul","ullurai","gull"],["பெருங்கடற்","பெருங்கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","ullurai","water lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","காக்கை நெய்தல்","confirmed","1/1","the man the friend calls good, and the woman whose eyes he spoils"]],"ch":"ai:17"},"ai:171":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":169,"t":["திரைஇமிழ் இன்னிசை அளைஇ அயலது","முழவுஇமிழ் இன்னிசை மறுகுதொறு இசைக்கும்","தொண்டி அன்ன பணைத்தோள்","ஒள்தொடி அரிவைஎன் நெஞ்சுகொண் டோ ளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says the young woman with bright bangles and shoulders full as bamboo, lovely as Thondi where the sweet sound of the waves mingles with the sweet sound of drums beaten along every street, has carried off his heart.","n":"The town is heard before it is seen: the sea's music and the drums of the streets blend into one sound, and that is what the girl is likened to. The last words, the girl who took my heart, are taken up as the first words of 172, beginning the decade's linked chain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 171","lb":"AI171","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["திரைஇமிழ்","திரை",1,"pira","scene","waves"],["முழவுஇமிழ்","முழவு",2,"parai","scene","drum"]],"en":[["town","தொண்டி",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:18"},"ai:172":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":170,"t":["ஒள்தொடி அரிவை கொண்டனள் நெஞ்சே","வண்டிமிர் பனித்துறைத் தொண்டி ஆங்கண்","உரவுக் கடல்ஒலித் திரையென","இரவி னானும் துயிலறி யேனே."],"p":"","r":"The man says the bright-bangled girl has taken his heart, and like the waves of the strong sea that roar at the bee-loud cool ford of Thondi, he does not know sleep even at night.","n":"The sea is chosen because it never rests: what is a pleasant background roar in 171 has become, one poem later, the measure of a man who cannot lie still.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamanoy","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 172","lb":"AI172","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["வண்டிமிர்","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bees"],["திரையென","திரை",3,"pira","simile","waves"]],"en":[["town","தொண்டி",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:18"},"ai:173":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":171,"t":["இரவி னானும் இன் துயில் அறியாது","அரவுறு துயரம் எய்துப தொண்டித்","தண்நறு நெய்தல் நாறும்","பின்இருங் கூந்தல் அணங்குற் றோரே."],"p":"","r":"Those seized by the dark braided hair that smells of the cool fragrant neythal flowers of Thondi, the man says, do not know sweet sleep even at night and suffer a cutting pain.","n":"He speaks of himself in the third person plural, as if stating a law: whoever is struck by such hair suffers so. The hair is named as the அணங்கு, the afflicting power, so the girl is a danger as well as a beauty. அரவுறு துயரம் may be the pain of a file's cut or of a snake's bite; either way a bodily wound.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kaamanoy","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 173","lb":"AI173","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","ornament","water lily"]],"en":[["town","தொண்டி",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:18"},"ai:174":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":172,"t":["அணங்குடைப் பனித்துறைத் தொண்டி யன்ன","மணங்கமழ் பொழிற்குறி நல்கினள் நுணங்கு இழை","பொங்கரி பரந்த உண்கண்","அம் கலிழ் மேனி அசைஇய எமக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The man says the girl of fine jewels, with collyrium eyes where red veins spread and a lovely glowing body, has granted him in his weariness a tryst in a fragrant grove like Thondi of the cool, goddess-haunted ford.","n":"The haunted ford answers the அணங்கு of 173: the power that struck him now grants him a meeting. Line 4 is hard: அம் கலிழ் மேனி may be her glowing body or a body clouded with tears, and அசைஇய may describe him as worn out or her as at rest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 174","lb":"AI174","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["அணங்குடைப்","அணங்கு",1,"deyvam","scene","goddess"],["பொழிற்குறி","பொழில்",2,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[["town","தொண்டி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:18"},"ai:175":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":173,"t":["எமக்குநயந் தருளினை யாயின் பணைத்தோள்","நல்நுதல் அரிவையொடு மென்மெல இயலி","வந்திசின் வாழியோ மடந்தை","தொண்டி யன்னநின் பண்புல கொண்டே."],"p":"","r":"The man asks the young woman, if she has love and pity for him, to come walking slowly with her friend of the bamboo shoulders and bright brow, bringing with her the many qualities that make her like Thondi.","n":"Asking her to come with her friend, slowly, is asking for a meeting that looks like an ordinary walk. Line 4 பண்புல is read as பண்பு பல, the many qualities; 176 opens with பண்பும, which supports the word பண்பு.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","panpu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 175","lb":"AI175","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[["town","தொண்டி",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:18"},"ai:176":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":174,"t":["பண்பும் பாயலும் கொண்டனள் தொண்டித்","தண்கமழ் புதுமலர் நாறும் ஒண்டொடி","ஐதுஅமைந்து அகன்ற அல்குல்","கொய்தளிர் மேனி கூறுமதி தவறே."],"p":"","r":"The man asks the friend to tell the bright-bangled girl her fault: she, fragrant with the cool new flowers of Thondi, with finely made wide hips and a body like plucked tender shoots, has taken away both his good nature and his bed.","n":"The charge is theft: பண்பும் பாயலும் கொண்டனள், she has taken my nature and my bed, so the lover frames his sleeplessness as a wrong done to him that the friend must name to her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","panpu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 176","lb":"AI176","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["புதுமலர்","புது மலர்",2,"maram","ornament","flowers"],["கொய்தளிர்","கொய் தளிர்",4,"maram","scene","shoots"]],"en":[["town","தொண்டி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:18"},"ai:177":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":175,"t":["தவறிலர் ஆயினும் பனிப்ப மன்ற","இவறுதிரை திளைக்கும் இடுமணல் நெடுங்கோட்டு","முண்டக நறுமலர் கமழும்","தொண்டி அன்னோள் தோள்உற் றோரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have touched the shoulders of the woman like Thondi, the man says, where the fragrant sea-holly flowers scent the long ridge of heaped sand on which the greedy waves play, will surely tremble even if they have done no wrong.","n":"The trembling is not guilt, the poem insists, so it can only be the woman's power working on whoever has embraced her; the waves that endlessly play on the sand give that restlessness a shore.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","anangu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 177","lb":"AI177","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["இவறுதிரை","திரை",2,"pira","scene","waves"],["இடுமணல்","இடு மணல்",2,"pira","scene","sand"],["முண்டக","முண்டகம்",3,"maram","scene","sea holly"]],"en":[["town","தொண்டி",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:18"},"ai:178":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":176,"t":["தோளும் கூந்தலும் பலபா ராட்டி","வாழ்தல் ஒல்லுமோ மற்றே செங்கோல்","குட்டுவன் தொண்டி யன்ன","என்கண்டும் நயந்துநீ நல்காகக் காலே."],"p":"","r":"The man asks whether he can live merely by praising her shoulders and hair again and again, if she, who is like Thondi of Kuttuvan of the just sceptre, sees him and still does not love him and grant him grace.","n":"The just sceptre is not idle praise of the king: a town under a just ruler gives to all who come, and the lover who calls her such a town is asking for that same grace. Praise alone, he says, cannot keep him alive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","sengol","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 178","lb":"AI178","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[],"en":[["king","குட்டுவன்",3],["town","தொண்டி",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:18"},"ai:179":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":177,"t":["நல்குமதி வாழியோ நளிநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப","அலவன் தாக்கத் துறையிறாப் பிறழும்","இன்னொலித் தொண்டி அற்றே","நின்னலது இல்லா இவள்சிறு நுதலே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the shore of dense waters to be gracious, for the small brow of this girl, who has no one but him, is like sweet-sounding Thondi, where prawns leap at the ford when the crab strikes.","n":"The voice turns here from the man to the friend, and his own word நல்கு from 178 comes back at him as her demand. A near miss for ullurai: the crab's strike and the prawn's leap could stand for the man and the startled girl, but the poem gives no anchor for a second pair, so they are scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","nalam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 179","lb":"AI179","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அலவன்","அலவன்",2,"pira","scene","crab"],["துறையிறாப்","இறா",2,"pira","scene","prawn"]],"en":[["town","தொண்டி",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:18"},"ai:180":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":178,"t":["சிறுநனை வரைந்தனை கொண்மோ பெருநீர்","வலைவர் தந்த கொழுமீன் வல்சிப்","பறைதபு முதுகுருகு இருக்கும்","துறை கெழு தொண்டி அன்னஇவள் நலனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man to marry her and then take this small bud, her beauty, which is like Thondi of the ford where the old heron that can no longer fly lives on the rich fish the netters of the great waters bring.","n":"The heron that can no longer fly yet is fed by the netters is not tagged as iraicchi, since it acts only by being fed; but it sits beside a girl said in 179 to have no one but him, and a reader may hear in it the dependent who must be provided for. The order matters: வரைந்தனை கொண்மோ, marry first, then take her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","nalam","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 180","lb":"AI180","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வலைவர்","வலைவர்",2,"seythi","scene","netters"],["கொழுமீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","gift_food","fish"],["முதுகுருகு","குருகு",3,"pul","scene","heron"],["சிறுநனை","நனை",1,"maram","simile","bud"]],"en":[["town","தொண்டி",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:18"},"ai:181":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":179,"t":["நெய்தல் உண்கண் ஏர் இறைப் பணைத் தோள்","பொய்தல் ஆடிய பொய்யா மகளிர்","குப்பை வெண்மணல் குரவை நிறூஉந்","துறைகெழு கொண்கன் நல்கின்","உறைவுஇனிது அம்மஇவ் அழுங்கல் ஊரே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that if the lord of the shore, where unfailing girls with neythal-like eyes and lovely bamboo shoulders play sand games and set up the kuravai dance on heaps of white sand, would only grant his grace, living in this clamouring town would be sweet.","n":"அழுங்கல் ஊர் is a town full of noise, and the noise is double: the girls' kuravai on the sand and the talk about her; one condition, நல்கின், would turn both into the sound of a wedding town.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 181","lb":"AI181","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["பொய்தல்","பொய்தல்",2,"seythi","scene","sand play"],["வெண்மணல்","வெண் மணல்",3,"pira","scene","white sand"],["குரவை","குரவை",3,"yaazh","scene","round dance"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:19"},"ai:182":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":180,"t":["நெய்தல் நறுமலர் செருந்தியொடு விரைஇக்","கைபுனை நறுந்தார் கமழும் மார்பன்","அருந்திறல் கடவுள் அல்லன்","பெருந்துறைக் கண்டுஇவள் அணங்கி யோனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the one who struck this girl when she saw him at the great ford is no god of fearsome power but a man whose chest smells of a hand-woven garland of fragrant neythal mixed with cherunthi.","n":"The household reads her sickness as a god's seizure (அணங்கு); the friend keeps the word but gives it a human subject, அணங்கியோன், and the garland is her evidence: a god would not wear a shore man's hand-made wreath.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kalavu","veri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 182","lb":"AI182","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","ornament","blue lily"],["செருந்தியொடு","செருந்தி",1,"maram","ornament","cherunthi flower"],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",3,"deyvam","scene","god"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:19"},"ai:183":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":181,"t":["கணங்கொள் அருவிக் கான்கெழு நாடன்","குறும்பொறை நாடன் நல்வய லூரன்","தண்கடற் சேர்ப்பன் பிரிந்தெனப் பண்டையிற்","கடும்பகல் வருதி கையறு மாலை","கொடுங்கழி நெய்தலும் கூம்பக்","காலை வரினும் களைஞரோ இலரே."],"p":"","r":"Since the man who is lord of the land of falls and forests, of the small hills, of the town of good fields and of the cool sea has left her, the woman tells the helpless evening that it comes, unlike before, in the harsh daytime, as the neythal of the curved backwater closes, and that even when morning comes there is no one to take away her pain.","n":"She gives him the titles of all four lands in a row, so the man has no landscape that is not his, while she has only the closing hour of the shore; and the last line refuses the usual comfort that night's end brings relief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 183","lb":"AI183","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["அருவிக்","அருவி",1,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["நெய்தலும்","நெய்தல்",5,"maram","scene","blue lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:19"},"ai:184":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":182,"t":["நெய்தல் இருங்கழி நெய்தல் நீக்கி","மீன் உண் குருகினம் கானல் அல்கும்","கடல்அணிந் தன்றுஅவர் ஊரே","கடலினும் பெரிதுஎமக்கு அவருடை நட்பே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says his town, where flocks of fish-eating herons leave the neythal of the dark backwater to rest in the seaside grove, is adorned by the sea, but greater than the sea is our friendship with him.","n":"The sea is first his town's ornament, then the measure her love exceeds: the thing that most defines his place is the very thing she sets below their bond.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kalaviyal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 184","lb":"AI184","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["மீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","scene","fish"],["குருகினம்","குருகு",2,"pul","scene","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:19"},"ai:185":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":183,"t":["அலங்கிதழ் நெய்தல் கொற்கை முன்துறை","இலங்குமுத்து உறைக்கும் எயிறுகெழு துவர்வாய்","அரம்போழ் அவ்வளைக் குறுமகள்","நரம்புஆர்த் தன்ன தீம் கிளவியளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says the young girl at the front ford of Korkai of swaying-petalled neythal, with red lips set with teeth that shine like gleaming pearls and lovely saw-cut conch bangles, has speech as sweet as tuned lute strings.","n":"Every ornament is a Korkai product: pearl from its sea, conch cut by the saw into bangles; the girl is described in the town's own wares, and only her voice needs an image from outside the shore.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalaviyal","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 185","lb":"AI185","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["இலங்குமுத்து","முத்து",2,"pira","scene","pearl"],["அவ்வளைக்","வளை",3,"pira","ornament","conch bangle"],["நரம்புஆர்த்","நரம்பு",4,"yaazh","simile","lute string"]],"en":[["town","கொற்கை",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:19"},"ai:186":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":184,"t":["நாரை நல்லினம் கடுப்ப மகளிர்","நீர்வார் கூந்தல் உளரும் துறைவ","பொங்குழி நெய்தல் உறைப்ப இத்துறை","பல்கால் வரூஉம் தேரெனச்","செல்லாதீமோ என்றனள் யாயே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore, where women shake out their wet hair like a fine flock of herons, that their mother has said: a chariot comes many times to this ford where neythal drips on the swelling water, so do not go there.","n":"The mother's sentence is quoted whole, so the hero hears the prohibition from the mother's own mouth; the chariot that betrays him is his own, and the visits it repeats (பல்கால்) are what ended them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","alar","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 186","lb":"AI186","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["நாரை","நாரை",1,"pul","simile","heron"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","scene","blue lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:19"},"ai:187":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":185,"t":["நொதும லாளர் கொள்ளார் இவையே","எம்மொடு வந்து கடலாடு மகளிரும்","நெய்தலம் பகைத்தழைப் பாவை புனையார்","உடலகம் கொள்வோர் இன்மையின்","தொடலைக் குற்ற சிலபூ வினரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that strangers do not accept these leaves: even the girls who come with us to bathe in the sea do not make dolls of the green-mixed neythal leaves, and for want of anyone to wear them on the body they carry only a few flowers set in a garland.","n":"The 'these' of line 1 are best taken as the leaf garment the man offers as a courting gift; the friend refuses it by saying her company wears no stranger's leaves, so the refusal is also a reproach that he is still a நொதுமலாளர், a stranger, until he asks for her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","varaivu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 187","lb":"AI187","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெய்தலம்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","ornament","blue lily leaves"],["பகைத்தழைப்","பகைத்தழை",3,"maram","absent","leaf garment"],["சிலபூ","பூ",5,"maram","ornament","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:19"},"ai:188":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":186,"t":["இருங்கழிச் சேயிறா இனப் புள் ஆரும்","கொற்கைக் கோமான் கொற்கையம் பெருந்துறை","வைகறை மலரும்நெய்தல் போலத்","தகைபெரி துடை காதலி கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that his love's eyes have great beauty, like the neythal that opens at dawn at the great ford of Korkai of the lord of Korkai, where flocks of birds feed on red prawns of the dark backwater.","n":"The flower is the dawn neythal, newly opened, not the evening one that closes (183); the time word makes the eyes fresh as well as blue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 188","lb":"AI188","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["சேயிறா","சே இறா",1,"pira","scene","red prawn"],["புள்","புள்",1,"pul","scene","birds"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","simile","blue lily"]],"en":[["king","கொற்கைக் கோமான்",2],["town","கொற்கையம்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:19"},"ai:189":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":187,"t":["புன்னை நுண் தாது உறைத்தரு நெய்தல்","பொன்படு மணியில் பொற்பத் தோன்றும்","மெல்லம் புலம்பன் வந்தென","நல்லன வாயின தோழியென் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that since the lord of the gentle shore, where the neythal dusted with fine punnai pollen looks as lovely as a gem set in gold, has come, her eyes have become good again.","n":"The flower dusted with gold and the eyes made good are one image: her neythal-like eyes, grown pale in his absence, regain their colour as the blue flower takes on the punnai's gold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","varaivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 189","lb":"AI189","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",1,"maram","simile","punnai tree"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","simile","blue lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:19"},"ai:190":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":188,"t":["தண்ணறு நெய்தல் தளையவிழ் வான்பூ","வெண்ணெல் அரிநர் மாற்றினர் அறுக்கும்","மெல்லம் புலம்பன் மன்றஎம்","பல்லிதழ் உண்கண் பனிசெய் தோனே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that the lord of the gentle shore, where the reapers of white paddy cut down the fragrant cool neythal's opened white flower along with the grain, is surely the one who has made her many-petalled kohl eyes weep.","n":"The reapers do not aim at the neythal; it falls because it grows among the paddy they want, so the harm in the image is careless, not cruel, and the eyes it spoils are named with the flower's own word, பல்லிதழ்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 190","lb":"AI190","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","ullurai","blue lily"],["வெண்ணெல்","வெண் நெல்",2,"maram","scene","white paddy"],["அரிநர்","அரிநர்",2,"seythi","ullurai","reapers"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","நெய்தல் அரிநர்","confirmed","1/1","the man and the woman whose eyes he has made weep"]],"ch":"ai:19"},"ai:191":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":189,"t":["கடற்கோடு செறிந்த வளைவார் முன்கைக்","கழிப்பூத் தொடர்ந்த இரும் பல் கூந்தல்","கானல் ஞாழற் கவின்பெறு தழையள்","வரையர மகளிரின் அரியள்என்","நிறையரு நெஞ்சம் கொண்டொளித் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the girl with bangles of sea conch on her long forearms, backwater flowers strung through her dark heavy hair and a fine leaf skirt of the shore grove's njaazhal, harder to win than the nymphs of the mountain, has taken his heart that could not hold itself and hidden it away.","n":"Everything she wears is of the shore, conch at the wrist, backwater flowers in the hair, a skirt of njaazhal leaves, and only the measure of her is brought down from the mountain: she is a fisher girl, yet rarer than its goddesses. The theft runs the other way from the decade's usual loss: here it is the man's heart, நிறையரு (past holding), that is carried off and hidden.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","nenju","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 191","lb":"AI191","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கடற்கோடு","கடல் கோடு",1,"pira","ornament","conch shell"],["கழிப்பூத்","கழிப் பூ",2,"maram","ornament","backwater flower"],["ஞாழற்","ஞாழல்",3,"maram","ornament","njaazhal tree"],["வரையர","வரையர மகளிர்",4,"deyvam","simile","mountain nymphs"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:20"},"ai:192":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":190,"t":["கோடுபுலங் கொட்பக் கடலெழுந்து முழுங்கப்","பாடிமிழ் பனித்துறை யோடுகலம் உகைக்கும்","துறைவன் பிரிந்தென நெகிழ்ந்தன","வீங்கின மாதோ தோழிஎன் வளையே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that when the lord of the cold roaring ford, where the sea rises and thunders so the shores seem to whirl and running boats are driven out, went away, her bangles slipped loose, and now they have grown tight again.","n":"The two verbs stand side by side with no clause between them, நெகிழ்ந்தன (loosened) then வீங்கின (swelled, grew tight): the bangle is a gauge that reads both ways, and the second verb, ending in the exclamation மாதோ, reports his return without naming it. Whether வீங்கின means the arm has filled out again with his coming is the reading the sense turns on.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 192","lb":"AI192","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கடலெழுந்து","கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["யோடுகலம்","ஓடு கலம்",2,"seythi","scene","boat"],["வளையே","வளை",4,"pira","ornament","conch bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:20"},"ai:193":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":191,"t":["வலம்புரியுழுத வார்மணல் அடைகரை","இலங்குகதிர் முத்தம் இருள்கெட இமைக்கும்","துறை கெழு கொண்கநீ தந்த","அறைபுனல் வால்வளை நல்லவோ தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the shore, where the right-whorled conch ploughs the long sand of the bank and pearls with shining rays glitter so the darkness is gone, whether the white bangles from the dashing sea that he has given are good, then.","n":"The question நல்லவோ தாமே can be plain or barbed: on a shore where conch and pearl lie about lighting the dark, a gift of shell bangles is a small thing, and the friend may be asking whether such gifts can stand in for the marriage he delays. The sense turns on the tone, which four lines do not fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 193","lb":"AI193","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வலம்புரியுழுத","வலம்புரி",1,"pira","scene","right-whorled conch"],["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",2,"pira","scene","pearl"],["வால்வளை","வால் வளை",4,"pira","gift_food","conch bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:20"},"ai:194":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":192,"t":["கடற்கோ டறுத்த அரம்போழ் அவ்வளை","ஒள்தொடி மடவரல் கண்டிக்கும் கொண்க","நன்னுதல் இன்றுமால் செய்தெனக்","கொன்ஒன்று கடுத்தனள் அன்னையது நிலையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore that they have seen the young girl with bright bracelets, with lovely bangles sawn from sea conch, and because her fine brow is today clouded with longing, the mother has suspected some wrong cause, and that is how the mother stands.","n":"கொன் ஒன்று (some cause, in vain) is the whole of the mother's mistake in two words: she reads the girl's pallor as something other than love, the misreading that in longer poems brings the diviner and the veri rite, and the friend reports it to the man as the danger that only marriage will end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 194","lb":"AI194","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கடற்கோ","கடல் கோடு",1,"pira","ornament","conch shell"],["அரம்போழ்","அரம்",1,"seythi","scene","saw"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:20"},"ai:195":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":193,"t":["வளைபடு முத்தம் பரதவர் பகரும்","கடல்கெழு கொண்கன் காதல் மடமகள்","கெடலரும் துயரம் நல்கிப்","படலின் பாயல் வௌவியோளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the dear young daughter of the sea lord, on whose shore fishermen sell the pearls born of the conch, has given him a grief that will not pass and stolen his sleep on his bed.","n":"நல்கி, the verb of a patron's gift, is used for a sorrow, and the pearl that fishermen cry for sale sits beside the daughter no one may buy: a reader may hear in the pearl born of the conch the girl born of the chief, but the poem draws no second pair, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 195","lb":"AI195","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",1,"pira","scene","pearl"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",1,"seythi","scene","fishermen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:20"},"ai:196":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":194,"t":["கோடீர் எல்வளைக் கொழும்பல் கூந்தல்","ஆய்தொடி மடவரல் வேண்டுதி யாயின்","தெண் கழிச் சேயிறாப் படூஉம்","தண் கடல் சேர்ப்ப வரைந்தனை கொண்மோ."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the cool sea shore, where red prawns are caught in the clear backwater, that if he wants the girl with bright bangles cut from conch, rich heavy hair and fine bracelets, he should marry her and take her.","n":"The condition is put coolly, வேண்டுதி யாயின் (if you want her), so the demand to marry comes as the only terms on offer; the prawns caught in the clear backwater may suggest a catch to be landed properly, but a single image makes no ullurai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 196","lb":"AI196","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோடீர்","கோடு",1,"pira","ornament","conch shell"],["சேயிறாப்","சே இறா",3,"pira","scene","red prawn"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:20"},"ai:197":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":195,"t":["இலங்குவளை தெளிர்ப்ப அலவன் ஆட்டி","முகம்புதை கதுப்பினள் இறைஞ்சி நின்றோளே","புலம்புகொள் மாலை மறைய","நலம்கேழ் ஆகம் நல்குவள் எனக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the girl who stood bowed with her face hidden in her hair, her shining bangles jingling as she chased the crabs, will give him her lovely breast so that the lonely evening is gone.","n":"Two moments are set against each other: the girl caught at a child's game, then bowing and hiding her face once she is seen, and the man's certainty that the same girl will ease his evening. Her shame (முகம்புதை, இறைஞ்சி) is the sign he reads as consent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","maalai","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 197","lb":"AI197","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["இலங்குவளை","வளை",1,"pira","ornament","conch bangle"],["அலவன்","அலவன்",1,"pira","scene","crab"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:20"},"ai:198":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":196,"t":["வளையணி முன்கை வாலெயிற்று அமர்நகை","இளையர் ஆடும் தளைஅவிழ் கானல்","குறுந்துறை வினவி நின்ற","நெடுந்தோள் அண்ணல் கண்டிக்கும் யாமே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that they have seen the tall-shouldered noble man who stood asking about the small ford, in the shore grove where loose-budded flowers open and young girls with bangled forearms, white teeth and gentle smiles play.","n":"A man of tall shoulders stops to ask about a small ford: the question is the pretext of the first approach, and the friend's கண்டிக்கும் (we have seen) says she has seen through it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 198","lb":"AI198","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வளையணி","வளை",1,"pira","ornament","conch bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:20"},"ai:199":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":197,"t":["கானலம் பெருந்துறைக் கலிதிரை திளைக்கும்","வானுயர் நெடுமணல் ஏறி ஆனாது","காண்கம் வம்மோ தோழி","செறிவளை நெகிழ்த்தோன் எறிகடல் நாடே"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend to come with her and climb the sky-high long sand dune that the roaring waves beat at the great ford of the shore grove, and gaze without end at the land of the wave-dashed sea of him who loosened her close bangles.","n":"She names him only by what he has done to her, செறிவளை நெகிழ்த்தோன் (he who loosened the close bangles), and the remedy she proposes is not to hide the sign but to climb the highest dune in view of all and look at his country, ஆனாது (without stopping).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 199","lb":"AI199","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கலிதிரை","கலி திரை",1,"pira","scene","waves"],["செறிவளை","செறி வளை",4,"pira","ornament","close bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:20"},"ai:200":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":198,"t":["இலங்குவீங்கு எல்வளை ஆய்நுதல் கவினப்","பொலந்தேர்க் கொண்கன் வந்தனன் இனியே","விலங்குஅரி நெடுங்கண் ஞெகிழ்மதி","நலங்கவர் பசலையை நகுக நாமே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the lord with the golden chariot has now come, so that her shining bangles fit tight and her fine brow grows lovely; let her long eyes with their red lines ease, and let them laugh at the pallor that took her beauty.","n":"The decade ends by reversing its token: the bangle that slipped is வீங்கு (swelling, tight) again, and the pallor that ruled her is turned into something to mock. பொலந்தேர் (the golden chariot) suggests he comes openly, in state, not by stealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","punarchi","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 200","lb":"AI200","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["எல்வளை","வளை",1,"pira","ornament","conch bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:20"},"ai:201":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":199,"t":["அன்னாய் வாழிவேண் டன்னை என்னை","தானும் மலைந்தான் எமக்கும் தழையாயின","பொன்வீ மணியரும் பினவே","என்ன மரம்கொல்அவர் சாரல் அவ்வே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the mother, as if idly, what tree it can be on his slope whose golden flowers and gem-like buds my lord himself wore and which became leaf skirts for us as well.","n":"A leaf skirt given by a man is a token of courtship, so the friend's botanical question is a disclosure dressed as ignorance: the flowers belong to his slope and nowhere in theirs, and only the giver can explain them. Whether என்னை is the lover (my lord) or the girl's father decides how bold the hint is.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 201","lb":"AI201","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தழையாயின","தழை",2,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["பொன்வீ","பொன் வீ",3,"maram","ornament","flowers"],["மணியரும்","மணி அரும்பு",3,"maram","ornament","buds"],["மரம்கொல்அவர்","மரம்",4,"maram","scene","tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:21"},"ai:202":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":200,"t":["அன்னாய் வாழிவேண் டன்னை நம்மூர்ப்","பார்ப்பனக் குறுமகப் போலத் தாமும்","குடுமித் தலைய மன்ற","நெடுமலை நாடன் ஊர்ந்த மாவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that the horses the lord of the tall mountain rode here surely have tufted heads, just like the little Brahmin boy of our town.","n":"The horses are proof that the lord has come to their town (ஊர்ந்த, rode), and the friend lets the mother see them through a homely, laughable likeness, the tufted head of a boy she knows, so the evidence arrives without alarm. மன்ற (surely) marks the friend as having seen them herself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 202","lb":"AI202","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","இளமை"]],"k":[["பார்ப்பனக்","பார்ப்பனக் குறுமகன்",2,"seythi","simile","Brahmin boy"],["மாவே","மா",4,"maa","simile","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:21"},"ai:203":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":201,"t":["அன்னாய் வாழி வேண்டு அன்னை நம் படப்பை","தேன்மயங்கு பாலினும் இனிய அவர்நாட்டு","உவலை கூவற் கீழ","மானுண்டு எஞ்சிய கலிழி நீரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that the muddy water left over after deer have drunk, in a pit well under leaf litter in his land, is sweeter than the honey-mixed milk of our own garden.","n":"The water is the poorest a hill can offer, dregs that beasts have left in a hole under rotting leaves, and the home drink is the richest; the inversion is the disclosure, for only a heart already given to his land could rank them so. Speaking of tasting it implies she has been, or means to go, there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","anbu","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 203","lb":"AI203","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தேன்மயங்கு","தேன்",2,"unavu","simile","honey"],["பாலினும்","பால்",2,"unavu","simile","milk"],["உவலை","உவலை",3,"maram","scene","leaf litter"],["கூவற்","கூவல்",3,"pira","scene","pit well"],["மானுண்டு","மான்",4,"maa","scene","deer"],["கலிழி","கலிழி நீர்",4,"pira","simile","muddy water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:21"},"ai:204":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":202,"t":["அன்னாய் வாழிவேண் டன்னைஅஃது எவன்கொல்","வரையர மகளிரின் நிரையுடன் குழீஇப்","பெயர்வுழிப் பெயர் வழித் தவிராது நோக்கி","நல்லள் நல்லள் என்ப","தீயேன் தில்ல மலை கிழவோற்கே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the mother what it means that, wherever the girl moves among her companions gathered like the mountain nymphs, he gazes at her without pause and says 'she is good, she is good', while to the lord of the mountain I am, alas, the wicked one.","n":"The subject of நோக்கி and என்ப is not named; read with மலை கிழவோற்கு, it is the hill lord who watches and praises, and the friend tells the mother so by complaining that she is the obstacle. Another reading makes the townswomen the watchers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","azhagu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 204","lb":"AI204","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மகளிரின்","வரை அர மகளிர்",2,"deyvam","scene","mountain nymphs"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:21"},"ai:205":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":203,"t":["அன்னாய் வாழிவேண் டன்னையென் தோழி","நனி நாண் உடையள் நின்னும் அஞ்சும்","ஒலிவெள் ளருவி ஓங்குமலை நாடன்","மலர்ந்த மார்பின் பாயல்","துஞ்சிய வெய்யள் நோகோ யானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that her girl is very modest and fears even her, yet longs to sleep on the broad chest of the lord of the high mountain with its loud white waterfall, and the friend grieves for her.","n":"The friend explains why she, not the girl, is speaking: the girl's shame and her fear of her mother keep her silent, so the disclosure must come from a second mouth. The two halves, great shame and open desire, are set side by side without a 'but', as the girl holds both at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","naanam","kaamam","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 205","lb":"AI205","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["ளருவி","அருவி",3,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:21"},"ai:206":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":204,"t":["அன்னாய் வாழிவேண் டன்னை உவக்காண்","மாரிக் குன்றத்துக் காப்பாள் அன்னன்","தூவலின் நனைந்த தொடலை ஒள்வாள்","பாசி சூழ்ந்த பெருங்கழல்","தண்பனி வைகிய வரிக் கச்சினனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend bids the mother look there: he is like a watchman of the rainy hill, his bright sword wet in its strap from the spray, his great anklet ringed with moss, his striped belt beaded with cold dew.","n":"Every detail of his gear is a record of nights spent out in the wet hill: spray on the sword, moss grown on the anklet from wading, dew settled on the belt. The friend lets the mother read his constancy from his clothes, and the likeness to a field watchman makes the stranger look like one of their own hill people.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","iravu","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 206","lb":"AI206","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காப்பாள்","காப்பாள்",2,"seythi","simile","watchman"],["தூவலின்","தூவல்",3,"pira","scene","spray"],["பாசி","பாசி",4,"maram","scene","moss"],["தண்பனி","பனி",5,"pira","scene","dew"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:21"},"ai:207":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":205,"t":["அன்னாய் வாழிவேண் டன்னை நன்றும்","உணங்கல கொல்லோநின் தினையே உவக்காண்","நிணம்பொதி வழுக்கில் தோன்றும்","மழைத்தலை வைத்துஅவர் மணிநெடுங் குன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the mother whether her millet is not drying at all, and bids her look: his tall gem-dark hill shows with rain cloud on its head like a wrapping of caul fat.","n":"Whether உணங்கல means the millet will not dry because rain is coming from his hill, or that it is withering for want of rain that his hill's cloud promises, the friend turns the mother's eye from her crop to his hill; on the second reading the drying millet stands for the pining girl, as the Kalittokai says openly, but no stated situation anchors that pairing, so it is not tagged ullurai. வழுக்கு as the sense of the fat-wrapped likeness is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","ninaivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 207","lb":"AI207","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தினையே","தினை",2,"unavu","scene","millet"],["நிணம்பொதி","நிணம்",3,"pira","scene","caul fat"],["மழைத்தலை","மழை",4,"pira","scene","rain cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:21"},"ai:208":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":206,"t":["அன்னாய் வாழிவேண் டன்னை கானவர்","கிழங்ககழ் நெடுங்குழி மல்க வேங்கைப்","பொன்மலி புதுவீத் தாஅம் அவர் நாட்டு","மணிநிற மால்வரை மறைதொறு இவள்","அணிமலர் நெடுங்கண் ஆர்ந்தன பனியே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that whenever the gem-coloured great mountain of his land, where golden new vengai flowers fill the deep pits the hunters dug for tubers, is hidden from view, the girl's long flower-like eyes fill with tears.","n":"The vengai's golden bloom is the kurinji sign of the wedding season, and its flowers filling the hollows the hunters left can be felt as a promise that his land is ready; but no second correspondence or stated situation completes it, so it stays scene. The girl weeps not when he is away but when his hill is out of sight, so the sight of the land stands in for the man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","ninaivu","kan","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 208","lb":"AI208","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["கானவர்","கானவர்",1,"seythi","scene","hunters"],["கிழங்ககழ்","கிழங்கு",2,"unavu","scene","tubers"],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",2,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["அணிமலர்","மலர்",5,"maram","scene","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:21"},"ai:209":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":207,"t":["அன்னாய் வாழிவேண் டன்னை நீமற்று","யான்அவர் மறத்தல் வேண்டுதி யாயின்","கொண்டல் அவரைப் பூவின் அன்ன","வெண்டலை மாமழை சூடித்","தோன்றல் அனாதுஅவர் மணிநெடுங் குன்றே."],"p":"","r":"If you want me to forget him, the speaker tells her, his tall gem-dark hill never stops appearing, crowned with a white-headed dark cloud like the flower of the bean that blooms in the east wind.","n":"She does not refuse to forget; she says the landscape will not let her, putting the cause outside herself. The white-topped dark cloud is a bean flower in the cold east-wind season, so the image also tells the time of year, when the hill is most often cloud-crowned and so most often seen.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kalaviyal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 209","lb":"AI209","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொண்டல்","கொண்டல்",3,"pira","scene","east wind"],["அவரைப்","அவரை",3,"maram","simile","field bean"],["மாமழை","மழை",4,"pira","simile","dark cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:21"},"ai:210":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":208,"t":["அன்னாய் வாழிவேண் டன்னைநம் படப்பை","புலவுச்சேர் துறுகல் ஏறி அவர்நாட்டுப்","பூக்கெழு குன்றம் நோக்கி நின்று","மணிபுரை வயங்கு இழை நிலைபெறத்","தணிதற்கும் உரித்துஅவள் உற்ற நோயே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that the girl's sickness might abate, and her gem-like bright jewels stay in place, if she climbed the flesh-smelling boulder in our garden and stood looking at the flowering hill of his land.","n":"Jewels that stay in place mean a body that is no longer wasting, since slipping bangles are love-sickness's sign; the friend prescribes the cure while naming its source, his land, so the remedy is itself the disclosure. The boulder smells of flesh from the hunters' kills dried on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","marundhu","melivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 210","lb":"AI210","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["துறுகல்","துறுகல்",2,"pira","scene","boulder"],["பூக்கெழு","பூ",3,"maram","scene","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:21"},"ai:211":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":209,"t":["நெய்யொடு மயக்கிய உழுந்துநூற் றன்ன","வயலையஞ் சிலம்பின் தலையது","செயலையம் பகைத்தழி வாடும் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"On the hill where the vayalai creeper grows pale as black-gram flour kneaded with ghee, the lovely leaf-skirt of mixed asoka leaves from its top is fading, mother.","n":"The leaf-skirt (தழை) is the lover's gift of secret courtship; that it wilts is all the poem says, and the reader is left to set the withering gift beside a love that is not being renewed. The line has பகைத்தழி, read as பகைத்தழை, a skirt of contrasting leaves.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 211","lb":"AI211","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெய்யொடு","நெய்",1,"unavu","simile","ghee"],["உழுந்துநூற்","உழுந்து நூறு",1,"unavu","simile","black-gram flour"],["வயலையஞ்","வயலை",2,"maram","simile","vayalai creeper"],["செயலையம்","செயலை",3,"maram","ornament","asoka leaves"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:22"},"ai:212":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":210,"t":["சாந்த மரத்த பூழில் எழுபுகை","கூட்டுவிரை கமழும் நாடன்","அறவற்கு எவனோ நாம் அகல்வு அன்னாய்"],"p":"","r":"Why should we be parted from the man of the land where smoke rising from the fragrant wood among the sandal trees smells of blended perfume, a man of virtue, mother?","n":"The girl calls him அறவன், a man of virtue, and that word is the whole of her argument: the fragrance that rises even from the smoke of his land is offered as a sign that nothing ill comes from him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","kalaviyal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 212","lb":"AI212","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சாந்த","சாந்தம்",1,"maram","scene","sandal"],["பூழில்","பூழில்",1,"maram","scene","fragrant wood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:22"},"ai:213":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":211,"t":["நறுவடி மாஅத்து மூக்கிறுபு உதிர்த்த","ஈர்ந்தண் பெருவடுப் பாலையிற் குறவர்","உறைவீழ் ஆலியல் தொகுக்கும் சாரல்","மீமிசை நன்னாட் டவர்வரின்","யானுயிர் வாழ்தல் கூடும் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"If the man of the good land high on the slopes comes, where hill people gather the cool big young mangoes that the fragrant mango drops when their stalks break, as one gathers hail fallen with the rain, I can go on living, mother.","n":"The windfall the hill people only have to pick up is set against a life that hangs on his coming. The word பாலையிற் in line 2 is not clear as written and is left unread.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","kalaviyal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 213","lb":"AI213","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாஅத்து","மா",1,"maram","scene","mango"],["பெருவடுப்","வடு",2,"maram","simile","young mango"],["குறவர்","குறவர்",2,"seythi","scene","hill people"],["ஆலியல்","ஆலி",3,"pira","simile","hailstone"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:22"},"ai:214":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":212,"t":["சாரல் பலவின் கொழுந்துணர் நறும்பழம்","இருங் கல் விடரளை வீழ்ந்தென வெற்பில்","பெருந்தேன் இறாஅல் கீறும் நாடன்","பேரமர் மழைக்கண் கலிழ தன்","சீருடை நன்னாட்டுச் செல்லும் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"The man of the land where a ripe, fragrant jackfruit from its rich cluster on the slope falls into a cleft of the dark rock and tears open a great honeycomb on the hill goes off to his own fine country, making my wide, cool eyes run with tears, mother.","n":"The fruit does not mean to break the comb; it simply falls, and the honey runs. So the man does no deliberate wrong: he goes back to his own land, and her tears follow from his going as the honey does from the fall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kan","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 214","lb":"AI214","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பலவின்","பலா",1,"maram","ullurai","jackfruit"],["இறாஅல்","இறால்",3,"pira","ullurai","honeycomb"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","பலவின் இறாஅல்","confirmed","1/1","the man's going and the girl's tears"]],"ch":"ai:22"},"ai:215":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":213,"t":["கட்டளை யன்ன மணிநிறத் தும்பி","இட்டிய குயின்ற துறைவயின் செலீஇயர்","தட்டைத் தண்ணுமைப் பின்னர் இயவர்","தீங்குழல் ஆம்பலின் இனிய இமிரும்","புதன்மலர் மாலையும் பிரிவோர்","இதனினும் கொடிய செய்குவர் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"In the evening of bush flowers, when the gem-dark bee, black as a touchstone, goes to the narrow-cut ford and hums sweeter than the musicians' flute playing the ambal tune behind the clapper and drum, those who part from us now will do things crueller still, mother.","n":"The evening is not cruel in itself: every sound in it is sweet, the bee sweeter even than the flute. The cruelty is measured by the man who can leave at such an hour, and from that she reasons that he can do worse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 215","lb":"AI215","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தும்பி","தும்பி",1,"pira","simile","bee"],["தட்டைத்","தட்டை",3,"parai","scene","bamboo clapper"],["தண்ணுமைப்","தண்ணுமை",3,"parai","scene","drum"],["இயவர்","இயவர்",3,"seythi","scene","musicians"],["தீங்குழல்","குழல்",4,"yaazh","simile","flute"],["ஆம்பலின்","ஆம்பல்",4,"yaazh","simile","ambal tune"],["புதன்மலர்","புதல் மலர்",5,"maram","scene","bush flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:22"},"ai:216":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":214,"t":["குறுங்கை இரும்புலிக் கோள்வல் ஏற்றை","நெடும்புதல் கானத்து மடப்பிடி ஈன்ற","நடுங்குநடைக் குழவி கொளீஇய பலவின்","பழந்தாங்கு கொழுநிழல் ஒளிக்கும் நாடற்குக்","கொய்திடு தளிரின் வாடிநின்","மெய்பிறி தாதல் எவன்கொல் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"Why should your body change, wilting like a plucked shoot, for the man of the land where the strong male of the short-legged great tiger hides in the rich shade of a fruit-laden jackfruit to seize the trembling calf a young she-elephant has borne in the forest of tall bushes, mother?","n":"The friend's question half-answers itself: his land is one where a tiger lies in wait for a newborn calf, so the danger of his road, or of trusting such a man, is in the picture though never said. That reading is not tagged, since no second pair to the situation can be named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","kalaviyal","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 216","lb":"AI216","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இரும்புலிக்","புலி",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",2,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["குழவி","குழவி",3,"maa","scene","elephant calf"],["பலவின்","பலா",3,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["தளிரின்","தளிர்",5,"maram","simile","tender shoot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:22"},"ai:217":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":215,"t":["பெருவரை வேங்கைப் பொன்மருள் நறுவீ","மானினப் பெருங்கிளை மேயல் ஆரும்","கானக நாடன் வரவுமிவண்","மேனி பசப்பது எவன்கொல் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"Even though the man of the forest land comes here, where great herds of deer graze on the fragrant gold-like flowers of the vengai on the high hill, why does the body turn pale, mother?","n":"The puzzle is that the pallor comes while he is still coming: secret visits do not cure it. The vengai in flower is the season when marriages were made, which sharpens the question without answering it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kalaviyal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 217","lb":"AI217","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",1,"maram","simile","vengai tree"],["மானினப்","மான் இனம்",2,"maa","scene","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:22"},"ai:218":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":216,"t":["நுண்ணேர் புருவத்த கண்ணும் ஆடும்","மயிர்வார் முன்கை வளையும் செறூஉம்","களிறுகோள் பிழைத்த கதஞ்சிறந்து எழுபுலி","எழுதரு மழையின் குழுமும்","பெருங் கல் நாடன் வருங்கொல் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"My eyes under the fine brows throb and the bangles on my downy forearm grow tight: will the man of the great rocky land come, where a tiger that missed its seizure of a bull elephant rises in fury and roars like rising rain clouds, mother?","n":"Throbbing eyes and bangles that grow tight are good signs, the reverse of the slipping bangles of separation, and she reads them as news of his coming. Yet the land she names is one of a baffled, roaring tiger, so hope and the danger of his road arrive in the same breath.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","ninaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 218","lb":"AI218","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["களிறுகோள்","களிறு",3,"maa","scene","bull elephant"],["எழுபுலி","புலி",3,"maa","simile","tiger"],["மழையின்","மழை",4,"pira","simile","rain cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:22"},"ai:219":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":217,"t":["கருங்கால் வேங்கை மாத்தகட்டு ஒள்வீ","இருங் கல் வியலறை வரிப்பத் தாஅம்","நன்மலை நாடன் பிரிந்தென","ஒண்ணுதல் பசப்பது எவன்கொல் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"Since the man of the good hill went away, where the bright flowers of the black-stemmed vengai with its dark calyx fall and stripe the wide rock, why does that bright forehead turn pale, mother?","n":"The friend asks why, yet states the cause in the same sentence (பிரிந்தென, since he went), so the question is a gentle naming of the grief. The flowers striping the rock recall a tiger's coat, as the tree's name does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 219","lb":"AI219","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene","vengai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:22"},"ai:220":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":218,"t":["அலங்குமழை பொழிந்த அகன்கண் அருவி","ஆடுகழை அடுக்கத்து இழிதரு நாடன்","பெருவரை யன்ன திருவிறல் வியன்மார்பு","முயங்காது கழிந்த நாள்இவள்","மயங்கிதழ் மழைக்கண் கலிழும் அன்னாய்."],"p":"","r":"On the days she has not embraced the broad, mighty chest, like a great mountain, of the man of the land where a wide waterfall fed by swaying clouds comes down the slopes of waving bamboo, her cloud-cool eyes, their lids bewildered, run with tears, mother.","n":"Clouds pour the waterfall, and the girl's eyes, called cloud-eyes (மழைக்கண்), pour tears: the hill's water and her weeping are matched by the same word, with no marker joining them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","pirivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 220","lb":"AI220","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["அலங்குமழை","மழை",1,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["அருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["ஆடுகழை","கழை",2,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:22"},"ai:221":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":219,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி காதலர்","பாவை யன்னஎன் ஆய்கவின் தொலைய","நன்மா மேனி பசப்பச்","செல்வல் என்பதம் மலைகெழு நாடே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that her lover says he will go back to his hill country, and at that word her chosen beauty, fine as a doll's, begins to fade and her lovely dark body to pale.","n":"The harm is done by the announcement, not the journey: செல்வல் is a first-person future quoted, and the fading and paling are set against the saying itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","nalam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 221","lb":"AI221","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:23"},"ai:222":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":220,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நம்மூர்","நளிந்து வந்து உறையும் நறுந்தண் மார்பன்","இன் இனி வாரா மாறுகொல்","சின்னிரை ஓதிஎன் நுதல்பசப் பதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether it is because the man of the fragrant cool chest, who used to come close and stay in their town, no longer comes as sweetly now, that her brow under its small rows of hair is turning pale.","n":"She offers the cause as a question, as if the pallor were a riddle to be solved, though she names the answer herself; the question form lets her accuse him without saying it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kalaviyal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 222","lb":"AI222","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:23"},"ai:223":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":221,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நம்மலை","வரையாம் இழியக் கோடல் நீடக்","காதலர்ப் பிரிந்தோர் கையற நலியும்","தண்பனி வடந்தை அச்சிரம்","முந்துவந் தனர்நம் காத லோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that on their hill, as water runs down the peaks and the glory lily grows tall, their lover has come back ahead of the cold dew season and its north wind that torment those parted from their lovers.","n":"The season is named only to be beaten: the poem lists everything the cold time does to the parted, and the last line says he arrived before it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 223","lb":"AI223","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கோடல்","கோடல்",2,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["வடந்தை","வடந்தை",4,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:23"},"ai:224":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":222,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நம்மலை","மணிநிறங் கொண்ட மாமலை வெற்பில்","துணிநீர் அருவி நம்மோடு ஆடல்","எளிய மன்ஆல் அவர்க்கினி","அரிய ஆகுதல் மருண்டனென் யானே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that bathing with them in the clear falls of their sapphire-dark hill was once easy for him, and that it should now have grown hard for him leaves her bewildered.","n":"மன் marks the ease as something past and gone; the bewilderment is at the change in him, not at any obstacle she can see.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 224","lb":"AI224","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",3,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:23"},"ai:225":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":223,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி பைஞ்சுனைப்","பாசடை நிவந்த பனிமலர்க் குவளை","உள்ளகங் கமழும் கூந்தல் மெல்லியல்","ஏர்திகழ் ஒண்ணுதல் பசத்தல்","ஓரார் கொல்நம் காத லோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether their lover does not perceive that the bright lovely brow of the soft one, whose hair is fragrant within with the cool blue lily that rises above the green leaves of the hill pool, is turning pale.","n":"The heroine speaks of herself in the third person as மெல்லியல், setting her beauty out as if for him to see; the fragrance of the lily-filled hair is still there while the brow fails.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","nalam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 225","lb":"AI225","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பைஞ்சுனைப்","பைஞ் சுனை",1,"pira","scene","hill pool"],["குவளை","குவளை",2,"maram","ornament","blue lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:23"},"ai:226":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":224,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நம்மலை","நறுந்தண் சிலம்பின் நாறுகுலைக் காந்தள்","கொங்கு உண் வண்டின் பெயர்ந்துபுற மாறிநின்","வன்புடை விறற்கவின் கொண்ட","வன்பி லாளன் வந்தனன் இனியே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that the heartless man who took her strong, conquering beauty and then turned his back like the bee that drinks the pollen of the fragrant-clustered glory lily on the cool slope of their hill and leaves, has now come.","n":"The bee simile fixes what kind of lover he was, one who took and went; his arrival is reported without forgiveness, so the friend's news is also a charge. Because the tenor is stated, the bee is a simile, not ullurai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","nalam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 226","lb":"AI226","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",2,"maram","simile","glory lily"],["வண்டின்","வண்டு",3,"pira","simile","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:23"},"ai:227":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":225,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நாளும்","நன்னுதல் பசப்பவும் நறுந்தோள் நெகிழவும்","ஆற்றலம் யாம் என மதிப்பக் கூறி","நப்பிரிந்து உறைந்தோர் மன்றநீ","விட்டனை யோஅவர் உற்ற சூளே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether she has let go the oath he swore, since the man who won their trust by saying he could not bear to see her brow pale and her shoulders slacken day by day now lives apart from them.","n":"The oath was sworn to the friend as witness, so only the friend could release it; the question turns his absence into the friend's doing, which is reproach and irony at once.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sool","pirivu","pasalai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 227","lb":"AI227","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:23"},"ai:228":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":226,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நம்மூர்","நிரந்திலங்கு அருவிய நெடுமலை நாடன்","இரந்துகுறை யுறாஅன் பெயரின்","என்ஆ வதுகொல்நம் இன்னுயிர் நிலையே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend what will become of their dear life if the lord of the long hill with its falls spreading and shining goes away without pleading for their favour.","n":"இரந்து குறையுறல் is the lover's suit to the friend in kalavu; the heroine fears not his absence in general but his leaving with that suit refused or unspoken, so she presses the friend to receive it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","kalaviyal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 228","lb":"AI228","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["அருவிய","அருவி",2,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:23"},"ai:229":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":227,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நாம்அழப்","பன்னாள் பிரிந்த அறனி லாளன்","வந்தன னோமற்று இரவில்","பொன்போல் விறல்கவின் கொள்ளுநின் நுதலே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the woman whether the man without virtue, who stayed away many days while they wept, came to her in the night, since her brow has taken on a golden, conquering beauty.","n":"The friend reads the brow as evidence: the pallor's lifting betrays a night visit the heroine has not told of, so the question teases and also lets her know the secret is seen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","nalam","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 229","lb":"AI229","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:23"},"ai:230":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":228,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நம்மொடு","சிறுதினைக் காவலன் ஆகி பெரிதுநின்","மெல்தோள் நெகிழவும் திருநுதல் பசப்பவும்","பொன்போல் விறற்கவின் தொலைத்த","குன்ற நாடற்கு அயர்வர்நன் மணனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that for the lord of the hill country, who joined them as a guard of the little millet and then made her soft shoulders slacken, her lovely brow pale and her golden, conquering beauty fade, their people are now preparing the good wedding.","n":"The friend lists the harm he did at the very moment she announces the wedding, so the marriage is framed as the settling of a debt rather than as a gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","melivu","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 230","lb":"AI230","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["சிறுதினைக்","சிறு தினை",2,"unavu","scene","millet"],["காவலன்","காவலன்",2,"seythi","scene","field guard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:23"},"ai:231":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":229,"t":["யாங்குவல் லுநையோ ஓங்கல் வெற்ப","இரும்பல் கூந்தல் திருந்திழை அரிவை","திதலை மாமை தேயப்","பசலை பாயப் பிரிவு தெய்யோ."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the high mountain how he can bring himself to a parting that wears away the spotted gold of his girl's skin and spreads pallor over it.","n":"யாங்கு வல்லுநையோ asks how he is able: the strength he shows in staying away is set against the strength draining from her, so the question is a reproach that pushes him toward marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 231","lb":"AI231","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:24"},"ai:232":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":230,"t":["போதார் கூந்தல் இயலணி அழுங்க","ஏதி லாளனை நீபிரிந் ததற்கே","அழல் அவிர் மணிப்பூண் நனையப்","பெயலா னாஎன் கண்ணே தெய்யோ."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her lover that because he, a stranger to her still, has parted from her, the flower-filled hair loses its natural grace and her eyes rain without ceasing until the fire-bright gem jewel on her breast is soaked.","n":"ஏதிலாளனை calls him a stranger: in courtship he has no bond she can name, so his leaving is the act of an outsider, and the word itself presses for marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","varaivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 232","lb":"AI232","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["போதார்","போது",1,"maram","ornament","flower buds"],["மணிப்பூண்","மணி",3,"pira","ornament","gem"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:24"},"ai:233":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":231,"t":["வருவை யல்லை வாடைநனி கொடிதே","அருவரை மருங்கின் ஆய்மணி வரன்றி","ஒல்லென இழிதரும் அருவிநின்","கல்லுடை நாட்டுச் செல்லல் தெய்யோ."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hill lord that he does not come and the north wind is very cruel, so he should not go back to his rocky land where the waterfall rushes down the hard slope sweeping away choice gems.","n":"The waterfall that strips the mountain of its gems as it runs away may suggest the lover who takes the girl's beauty with him when he goes, but the image does no act of a person in the situation, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","varaivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 233","lb":"AI233","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆய்மணி","மணி",2,"pira","scene","gem"],["அருவிநின்","அருவி",3,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:24"},"ai:234":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":232,"t":["மின்னவிர் வயங்கிழை ஞெகிழச் சாஅய்","நன்னுதல் பசத்த லாவது துன்னிக்","கனவிற் காணும் இவளே","நனவிற் காணாள்நின் மார்பே தெய்யோ."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lover that the girl's bright bangles slip and her fair brow turns pale because she, who sees his chest close beside her in dreams, never sees it awake.","n":"The dream is not a comfort here but the measure of the lack: the closeness she has asleep (துன்னி) makes the waking absence the cause of the pallor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","pasalai","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 234","lb":"AI234","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:24"},"ai:235":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":233,"t":["கையுற வீழ்ந்த மையில் வானமொடு","அரிது காதலர்ப் பொழுதே அதனால்","தெரியிழை தெளிர்ப்ப முயங்கிப்","பிரியலம் என்கமோ எழுகமோ தெய்யோ."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that time for lovers is as hard to come by as a clear sky falling into one's hand, and asks whether, holding him so the chosen jewels jingle, they should say 'we will not part' or rise and go.","n":"Read with the next poem, எழுகமோ (shall we rise) is the first hint of going away with him; the two choices are to keep him here or to leave with him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","udanpokku","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 235","lb":"AI235","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:24"},"ai:236":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":234,"t":["அன்னையும் அறிந்தனள் அலரும் ஆயின்று","நன்மனை நெடுநகர் புலம்புகொள உறுதரும்","இன்னா வாடையும் மலையும்","நும்மூர்ச் செல்கம் எழுகமோ தெய்யோ."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lover that the mother has come to know, gossip has risen, and the cruel north wind assails the good house till its tall mansion is desolate, so they will go to his town: shall they set out?","n":"மலையும் is best taken as the verb (it assails) with the wind as subject; read as 'and the mountain', the line would add the hill to the wind among the things that make home unbearable.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","alar","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 236","lb":"AI236","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:24"},"ai:237":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":235,"t":["காமம் கடவ உள்ளம் இனைப்ப","யாம்வந்து காண்பதோர் பருவம் ஆயின்","ஓங்கித் தோன்றும் உயர்வரைக்கு","யாங்கெனப் படுவது நும்மூர் தெய்யோ."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lover where his town lies in relation to that high mountain rising tall, should a time come when desire drives them and the heart aches enough to come and see him.","n":"The innocent question about the way to his town is a threat and a plea at once: if he will not come, they may come to him, which the courting girl cannot do without disgrace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","varaivu","udanpokku"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 237","lb":"AI237","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:24"},"ai:238":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":236,"t":["வார் கோட்டு வயத்தகர் வாராது மாறினும்","குரூஉமயிர்ப் புருவை ஆசையின் அல்கும்","ஆஅல் அருவித் தண் பெருஞ் சிலம்ப","நீ இவண் வரூஉம் காலை","மேவரும் மாதோஇவள் நலனே தெய்யோ."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the cool great mountain of sounding falls, where the coloured-fleeced ewe stays on in longing even when the long-horned ram turns away and does not come, that her girl's beauty revives whenever he comes here.","n":"புருவை (ewe) and தகர் (ram) are rare in the akam lexicon; the ewe that stays put in desire is the girl who keeps faith while he stays away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","varaivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 238","lb":"AI238","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வயத்தகர்","தகர்",1,"maa","ullurai","ram"],["புருவை","புருவை",2,"maa","ullurai","ewe"],["அருவித்","அருவி",3,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","வயத்தகர் புருவை","not_confirmed","0/1","the lover who stays away and the girl who waits for him"],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:24"},"ai:239":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":237,"t":["சுரும்புணக் களித்த புகர்முக வேழம்","இரும்பிணர்த் துறுகல் பிடிசெத்துத் தழூநின்","குன்றுகெழு நன்னாட்டுச் சென்ற பின்றை","நேரிறைப் பணைத்தோள் ஞெகிழ","வாரா யாயின் வாழேம் தெய்யோ."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lover that since he went back to his good land of hills, where a spotted-faced elephant drunk with must, bees feeding on it, embraces a dark rough boulder taking it for his mate, they will not live if he does not come before the girl's shapely bamboo-smooth arms grow thin.","n":"The elephant's embrace of a stone is longing without its object: even the beast of his land cannot bear to be without its mate, and yet the man stays away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","varaivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 239","lb":"AI239","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["சுரும்புணக்","சுரும்பு",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",1,"maa","iraicchi","elephant"],["பணைத்தோள்","பணை",4,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வேழம்","confirmed","1/2","even the beast of his land cannot do without its mate, yet the man stays away from the girl"]],"ch":"ai:24"},"ai:240":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":238,"t":["அறியோம் அல்லேம் அறிந்தனம் மாதோ","பொறிவரிச் சிறைய வண்டினம் மொய்ப்பச்","சாந்தம் நாறும் நறியோள்","கூந்தல் நாறும்நின் மார்பே தெய்யோ."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lover that they are not ignorant but have come to know: his chest, where striped-winged bees swarm, smells of the hair of the fragrant girl who smells of sandal.","n":"The scent on his chest is the secret union made public to the friend; in a marutham poem the same words would accuse a husband of another woman, but in this kurinji decade the fragrant woman is the friend's own girl, and the friend's knowing is the step before pressing for marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","punarchi","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 240","lb":"AI240","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bees"],["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",3,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:24"},"ai:241":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":239,"t":["நம்முறு துயரம் நோக்கி அன்னை","வேலன் தந்தா ளாயின்அவ் வேலன்","வெறிகமழ் நாடன் கேண்மை","அறியுமோ தில்ல செறியெயிற் றோயே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that if mother, seeing the grief that has come on them, has brought the diviner, then that diviner can hardly know of her bond with the man of the fragrant hill country.","n":"வெறிகமழ் describes the man's land with the very word that names the mother's rite, so the land's fragrance and the diviner's veri share a name while the diviner, by the friend's question, cannot know which of them is at work.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 241","lb":"AI241","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வேலன்","வேலன்",2,"seythi","scene","diviner"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:25"},"ai:242":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":240,"t":["அறியா மையின் வெறியென மயங்கி","அன்னையும் அருந்துயர் உழந்தனள் அதனால்","எய்யாது விடுதலோ கொடிதே நிரையிதழ்","ஆய்மலர் உண்கண் பசப்பச்","சேய்மலை நாடன் செய்த நோயே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that mother, not knowing and confused into taking it for the god's seizure, has herself suffered sore grief, so it is cruel to leave her unknowing of the sickness the man of the far hill gave, which has paled her flower-like eyes.","n":"The argument for disclosure is made on the mother's behalf (அன்னையும் அருந்துயர் உழந்தனள்): the friend counts the mother's needless grief, not only the girl's, as the harm of silence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","pasalai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 242","lb":"AI242","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆய்மலர்","மலர்",4,"maram","scene","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:25"},"ai:243":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":241,"t":["கறிவளர் சிலம்பின் கடவுள் பேணி","அறியா வேலன் வெறியெனக் கூறும்","அதுமனம் கொள்குவை அனையிவள்","புதுமலர் மழைக்கண் புலம்பிய நோய்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells mother that she will take to heart what the ignorant diviner says after worshipping the god of the pepper-vine slope, that it is the god's seizure, as the cause of the sickness that has left her daughter's fresh-flower eyes tearful and forlorn.","n":"அறியா வேலன் puts the diviner's ignorance first and the mother's belief second (மனம் கொள்குவை), so the friend's words to the mother already turn her from the rite toward the cause she has not been told.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 243","lb":"AI243","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["கறிவளர்","கறி",1,"maram","scene","pepper vine"],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",1,"deyvam","scene","Murugu"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",2,"seythi","scene","diviner"],["புதுமலர்","மலர்",4,"maram","scene","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:25"},"ai:244":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":242,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி பன்மலர்","நறுந்தண் சோலை நாடுகெழ நெடுந்தகை","குன்றம் பாடான் ஆயின்","என்பயஞ் செய்யுமோ வேலற்குஅவ் வெறியே."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that unless the diviner sings the hill of the noble man of the land of fragrant cool groves full of flowers, his veri rite will do no good.","n":"She does not reject the rite but rewrites its song: the diviner's praise of the god's hill would cure her only if it were the man's hill, so the rite is useless exactly because it is aimed at the wrong lord.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","velan","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 244","lb":"AI244","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பன்மலர்","மலர்",1,"maram","scene","flowers"],["சோலை","சோலை",2,"maram","scene","grove"],["வேலற்கு","வேலன்",4,"seythi","scene","diviner"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:25"},"ai:245":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":243,"t":["பொய்யா மரபின் ஊர் முது வேலன்","கழங்குமெய்ப் படுத்துக் கன்னந் தூக்கி","முருகென மொழியும் ஆயின்","கெழுதகை கொல் இவள் அணங்கி யோற்கே."],"p":"","r":"If the old diviner of the town, of a tradition that does not lie, casts his beads, raises the offering and says it is Murugu, the friend asks, is that a kindness to the one who has afflicted her?","n":"பொய்யா மரபின் praises the diviner's lineage only to let it fail here: naming Murugu lets the real afflicter (அணங்கியோன், the man) go unnamed, so the error is a favour to him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["velan","anangu","veri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 245","lb":"AI245","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வேலன்","வேலன்",1,"seythi","scene","diviner"],["கழங்கு","கழங்கு",2,"pira","scene","molucca beans"],["முருகென","முருகு",3,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:25"},"ai:246":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":244,"t":["வெறிசெறித் தனனே வேலன் கறிய","கன்முகை வயப்புலி கழங்கு மெய்ப்படூஉ","புன்பலம் வித்திய புனவர் புணர்த்த","மெய்ம்மை யன்ன பெண்பாற் புணர்ந்து","மன்றில் பையுள் தீரும்","குன்ற நாடன் உறீஇய நோயே."],"p":"","r":"The diviner has crowded on his veri rite, but the sickness the man of the hill country gave is the kind that ends as the strong tiger of the pepper-hung rock cave ends its distress, by joining its mate in the open ground.","n":"Lines 2 to 4 are hard to construe: கழங்கு மெய்ப்படூஉ may belong to the diviner's casting or to the tiger scene, and the simile of the farmers' truth is obscure; the reading of the tiger's union as the cure is the plainer sense of மன்றில் பையுள் தீரும்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veri","kaamanoy","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 246","lb":"AI246","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேலன்","வேலன்",1,"seythi","scene","diviner"],["கறிய","கறி",1,"maram","scene","pepper vine"],["வயப்புலி","புலி",2,"maa","iraicchi","tiger"],["கழங்கு","கழங்கு",2,"pira","scene","molucca beans"],["புனவர்","புனவர்",3,"seythi","scene","hill farmers"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வயப்புலி","confirmed","1/1","her sickness, like the tiger's, ends only in union with the man who gave it, not in the diviner's rite"]],"ch":"ai:25"},"ai:247":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":245,"t":["அன்னை தந்தது ஆகுவது அறிவன்","பொன்னகர் வரைப்பின் கன்னம் தூக்கி","முருகென மொழியும் ஆயின்","அருவரை நாடன் பெயர்கொலோ அதுவே."],"p":"","r":"If the diviner mother brought, the one who is to know what will come, raises the offering within the bounds of the fine house and says it is Murugu, the speaker asks, is that the name of the man of the hard-to-reach hills?","n":"The question half grants the diviner's word: if the god's name is the man's name, then the diagnosis is right by accident, and the joke on the rite is also a disclosure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 247","lb":"AI247","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["அறிவன்","அறிவன்",1,"seythi","scene","diviner"],["முருகென","முருகு",3,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:25"},"ai:248":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":246,"t":["பெய்ம் மணல் முற்றம் கவின்பெற இயற்றி","மலைவான் கொண்ட சினைஇய வேலன்","கழங்கினால் அறிகுவது என்றால்","நன்றால் அம்ம நின்றஇவள் நலனே."],"p":"","r":"If the angry diviner, having made the sand-strewn courtyard beautiful, is to find the cause by his beads, then fine indeed, says the friend, will be the beauty that is left to her.","n":"நன்றால் அம்ம is ironic: the beauty is fading, and the friend means that the beads will leave it as ruined as it is.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 248","lb":"AI248","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["மணல்","மணல்",1,"pira","scene","sand"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",2,"seythi","scene","diviner"],["கழங்கினால்","கழங்கு",3,"pira","scene","molucca beans"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:25"},"ai:249":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":247,"t":["பெய்ம்மணல் வரைப்பின் கழங்குபடுத்து அன்னைக்கு","முருகென மொழியும் வேலன் மற்றவன்","வாழிய விலங்கு மருவிச்","சூர்மலை நாடனை அறியா தோனே."],"p":"","r":"The diviner who casts his beads in the sand-strewn yard and tells mother it is Murugu, may he live long, says the friend, for he does not know the man of the haunted hill with shining falls.","n":"The blessing (வாழிய) is double: mockery of a diviner who misses the cause, and real thanks, since his ignorance keeps the secret of the man of the hill.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 249","lb":"AI249","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பெய்ம்மணல்","மணல்",1,"pira","scene","sand"],["கழங்குபடுத்து","கழங்கு",1,"pira","scene","molucca beans"],["முருகென","முருகு",2,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",2,"seythi","scene","diviner"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:25"},"ai:250":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":248,"t":["பொய் படுபு அறியாக் கழங்கே மெய்யே","மணிவரைக் கட்சி மடமயில் ஆலும்நம்","மலர்ந்த வள்ளியம் கானம் கிழவோன்","ஆண்டகை விறல்வேள் அல்லன்இவள்","பூண்தாங்கு இளமுலை அணங்கியோனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the beads that never lie that truly the one who afflicted the girl's young ornamented breasts is not the manly victorious Vel, lord of our flowering valli forest where the young peahen dances in its haunt on the gem-bright hill.","n":"The god is described by his own emblems, the peacock and the valli, and then denied: the friend grants the beads their truth only to say the truth they show is not Murugu.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","veri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 250","lb":"AI250","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கழங்கே","கழங்கு",1,"pira","scene","molucca beans"],["மடமயில்","மயில்",2,"pul","scene","peahen"],["வள்ளியம்","வள்ளி",3,"maram","scene","valli creeper"],["விறல்வேள்","வேள்",4,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:25"},"ai:251":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":249,"t":["குன்றக் குறவன் ஆர்ப்பின் எழிலி","நுண் பல் அழிதுளி பொழியும் நாட","நெடுவரைப் படப்பை நும்மூர்க்","கடுவரல் அருவி காணினும் அழுமே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the land where the clouds, at the hill man's shout, pour fine and many drops, that the girl weeps even when she only sees the swift-falling cascade that comes down from his town on the tall hill.","n":"The waterfall has done nothing to her: it is only water that has passed his town, and that is enough to make her cry, so the poem measures her longing by the smallest trace of him. The cloud that pours as soon as a hill man calls stands against a lord who does not come though she weeps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 251","lb":"AI251","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குறவன்","குறவன்",1,"seythi","scene","hill man"],["எழிலி","எழிலி",1,"pira","iraicchi","cloud"],["அருவி","அருவி",4,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","எழிலி","not_confirmed","0/1","in his own land even a cloud answers a call, so the lord who leaves the girl's tears unanswered falls short of his hill's clouds"]],"ch":"ai:26"},"ai:252":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":250,"t":["குன்றக் குறவன் புல்வேய்க் குரம்பை","மன்றாடு இளமழை மறைக்கும் நாடன்","புரையோன் வாழி தோழி விரைபெயல்","அரும்பனி அளைஇய கூதிர்ப்","பெருந்தண் வாடையின் முந்துவந் தனனே."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that the lord of the land where young rain-clouds roving over the village common hide the hill man's grass-thatched hut is a noble man: he came back ahead of the great cold north wind of autumn, mixed with sharp dew and driving rain.","n":"He is praised for timing, not for gifts: he came before (முந்து) the cold wind that would have made the waiting unbearable, the kurinji season itself (கூதிர்) being the deadline he beat. The cloud that hides the hut on the common is the only cover the scene gives, and he arrived under it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","panpu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 252","lb":"AI252","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["குறவன்","குறவன்",1,"seythi","scene","hill man"],["குரம்பை","குரம்பை",1,"pira","scene","hut"],["இளமழை","இள மழை",2,"pira","scene","young cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:26"},"ai:253":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":251,"t":["குன்றக் குறவன் சாந்த நறும்புகை","தேம் கமழ் சிலம்பின் வரையகம் கமழும்","கானக நாடன் வரையின்","மன்றலும் உடையள்கொல் தோழி யாயே."],"p":"","r":"The girl asks her friend whether, if the lord of the forest land, where the fragrant smoke of sandal burnt by the hill man perfumes the whole slope, comes to marry her, their mother will consent to the wedding.","n":"வரையின் is both 'of the hill' and 'if he weds': the line can be read as the hill's wedding or as 'if he marries', and either way the question is about the mother, not the man. The smoke that fills the whole mountain from one hill man's fire is a scene, but it is the way a secret spreads.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 253","lb":"AI253","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குறவன்","குறவன்",1,"seythi","scene","hill man"],["சாந்த","சாந்தம்",1,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["தேம்","தேன்",2,"pira","scene","honey"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:26"},"ai:254":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":252,"t":["குன்றக் குறவன் ஆரம் அறுத்தென","நறும்புகை சூழ்ந்து காந்தள் நாறும்","வண்டு இமிர், சுடர்நுதல் குறுமகள்","கொண்டனர் செல்வர்தம் குன்றுகெழு நாட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that the young girl with the bright forehead, around whom bees hum, has been taken by the wealthy ones to their country of hills, a land where the kaanthal smells of the fragrant smoke rising when the hill man cuts the sandal.","n":"The kaanthal takes the scent of the sandal's smoke as the girl now takes the house of the man; the image has only that one correspondence, so it is left as scene. செல்வர் is plural, either honorific for the man or his people who came for her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 254","lb":"AI254","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குறவன்","குறவன்",1,"seythi","scene","hill man"],["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",1,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",2,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:26"},"ai:255":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":253,"t":["குன்றக் குறவன் காதல் மடமகள்","வரையர மகளிர்ப் புரையுஞ் சாயலள்","ஐயள் அரும்பிய முலையள்","செய்ய வாயினள் மார்பினள் சுணங்கே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the hill man's beloved young daughter has the grace of the goddesses of the mountain: she is delicate, her breasts have budded, her mouth is red and her chest is spotted with pale gold.","n":"The likeness to the hill goddesses is not flattery alone: in kurinji the அரமகளிர் are the beings who strike men who see them, and the catalogue that follows (budding breasts, the pale spots of a girl newly grown) says she has only just come of age.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 255","lb":"AI255","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குறவன்","குறவன்",1,"seythi","scene","hill man"],["வரையர","வரை அரமகளிர்",2,"deyvam","simile","mountain nymphs"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:26"},"ai:256":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":254,"t":["குன்றக் குறவன் காதல் மடமகள்","வண்டு படு கூந்தல் தண் தழைக் கொடிச்சி","வளையள் முளைவாள் எயிற்றள்","இளையள் ஆயினும் ஆர் அணங்கினளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the hill man's beloved young daughter, the hill girl with bee-haunted hair and a cool leaf skirt, bangled, with teeth bright as sprouts, is young indeed, yet she is a goddess who strikes hard.","n":"ஆயினும் carries the point: her youth (இளையள்) ought to make her harmless, and instead she is ஆர் அணங்கு, a power that afflicts. The fear is the man's, and it is the old fear of a hill goddess, not a figure of speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 256","lb":"AI256","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["குறவன்","குறவன்",1,"seythi","scene","hill man"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bee"],["தழைக்","தழை",2,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["முளைவாள்","முளை",3,"maram","scene","sprout"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:26"},"ai:257":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":255,"t":["குன்றக் குறவன் கடவுட் பேணி","இரந்தனன் பெற்ற எல் வளைக் குறுமகள்","ஆயரி நெடுங்கள் கலிழச்","சேயதால் தெய்யநீ பிரியும் நாடே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that this bright-bangled young girl, whom the hill man won by worshipping the god and begging, has her long, finely veined eyes flowing with tears, because the land to which he is leaving is far away.","n":"The first two lines set what is at stake: a daughter got by prayer from the god is the treasure the man would leave weeping, so the friend's argument is the father's long begging against the man's short leave-taking. The colophon's kurinji answers the decade's hill people; the situation is a parting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vazhipaadu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 257","lb":"AI257","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குறவன்","குறவன்",1,"seythi","scene","hill man"],["கடவுட்","கடவுள்",1,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:26"},"ai:258":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":256,"t":["குன்றக் குறவன் காதல் மடமகள்","அணிமயில் அன்ன அசைநடைக் கொடிச்சியைப்","பெருவரை நாடன் வரையும் ஆயின்","கொடுத்தனெம் ஆயினோம் நன்றே","இன்னும் ஆனாது நன்னுதல் துயரே."],"p":"","r":"Of the hill man's beloved young daughter, the hill girl whose swaying walk is like an adorned peacock's, the friend says that if the lord of the great hill comes to wed her they have given her already, and that is good; yet the girl's grief has not ceased.","n":"கொடுத்தனெம் ஆயினோம் is a past tense for a future act: the household counts her as already given, so nothing now stands between them but the man. The last line turns the good news back on him: consent has not ended her grief, because he has not come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 258","lb":"AI258","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["குறவன்","குறவன்",1,"seythi","scene","hill man"],["அணிமயில்","மயில்",2,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:26"},"ai:259":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":257,"t":["குன்றக் குறவன் காதல் மடமகள்","மன்ற வேங்கை மலர்சில கொண்டு","மலையுறை கடவுள் குலமுதல் வழுத்தித்","தேம்பலிச் செய்த ஈர்நறுங் கையள்","மலர்ந்த காந்தள் நாறிக்","கலிழ்ந்த கண்ணள்எம் அணங்கி யோளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the one who has stricken him is the hill man's beloved young daughter, who took a few vengai flowers from the village common, praised the mountain god who is the first of her clan, her hands wet and fragrant from making the honey offering, smelling of open kaanthal, her eyes brimming.","n":"The girl who has just worshipped the god of the hill becomes herself the power that strikes him (அணங்கியோள்): the rite and the affliction belong to one vocabulary. Why her eyes brim (கலிழ்ந்த) the poem does not say: it may be her own love she prays about.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","anangu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 259","lb":"AI259","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["குறவன்","குறவன்",1,"seythi","scene","hill man"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",2,"maram","scene",""],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",3,"deyvam","scene",""],["தேம்பலிச்","தேன் பலி",4,"unavu","gift_food","honey offering"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",5,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:26"},"ai:260":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":258,"t":["குன்றக் குறவன் காதல் மடமகள்","மென் தோள் கொடிச்சியைப் பெறற்கரிது தில்ல","பைம்புறப் படு கிளி ஒப்பலர்","புன்புல மயக்கத்து விளைந்தன தினையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that the soft-shouldered hill girl, the hill man's beloved daughter, will be hard to win now, since they no longer chase the green-backed parrots: the millet sown in the mixed poor ground has ripened.","n":"The harvest is the whole argument: the millet field was the only place the girl could be met, and with the grain ripe she will be kept at home. பெறற்கு அரிது says 'hard to obtain', the language of winning a bride rather than of meeting a lover.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 260","lb":"AI260","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குறவன்","குறவன்",1,"seythi","scene","hill man"],["கிளி","கிளி",3,"pul","scene","parrot"],["தினையே","தினை",4,"unavu","scene","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:26"},"ai:261":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":259,"t":["மென் தினை மேய்ந்த தறுகண் பன்றி","வன்கல் அடுக்கத்துத் துஞ்சும் நாடன்","எந்தை அறிதல் அஞ்சிக் கொல்","அதுவே மன்ற வாரா மையே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend whether the hill lord, of the land where the fierce-eyed boar that grazed the soft millet sleeps on the hard rock slope, stays away for fear that her father will find out, and concludes that this must be why he does not come.","n":"The excuse she finds for him is a reproach: the boar of his own land raids the guarded millet and then sleeps without fear, while he, she supposes, is afraid of her father.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","acham","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 261","lb":"AI261","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["தினை","தினை",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["பன்றி","பன்றி",1,"maa","iraicchi","boar"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பன்றி","confirmed","1/1","the beast of his own hill dares the guarded field and rests without fear, yet the man she loves stays away for fear of her father"]],"ch":"ai:27"},"ai:262":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":260,"t":["சிறுதினை மேய்ந்த தறுகண் பன்றி","துறுகல் அடுக்கத்துத் துணையொடு வதியும்","இலங்குமலை நாடன் வரூஉம்","மருந்தும் அறியும்கொல் தோழிஅவன் விருப்பே"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend whether the love of the lord of the shining mountain, where the fierce-eyed boar that grazed the small millet stays with its mate on the slope of piled rocks, knows that his coming is the one remedy for her.","n":"The whole sickness is turned into a question about his knowledge: his love may be real, but it does not seem to know the one medicine it could give, which is simply to come.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 262","lb":"AI262","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["சிறுதினை","சிறுதினை",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["பன்றி","பன்றி",1,"maa","iraicchi","boar"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பன்றி","confirmed","1/1","the boar keeps its mate's company, while the man stays away from the woman whose only cure is his coming"]],"ch":"ai:27"},"ai:263":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":261,"t":["நன்பொன் அன்ன புனிறுதீர் ஏனல்","கட்டளை அன்ன கேழல் மாந்தும்","குன்றுகெழு நாடன் தானும்","வந்தனன் வந்தன்று தோழிஎன் நலனே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that the lord of the hill land, where a boar dark as a touchstone eats the ripened millet golden as fine gold, has come himself, and with him her beauty has come back.","n":"The two similes belong together: gold and the black touchstone on which gold is tried, so the boar on the golden field looks like gold drawn across the testing stone. The doubled verb (வந்தனன் வந்தன்று) makes his arrival and the return of her beauty a single event. A hidden reading of the boar as the lover enjoying ripe beauty would rest on a single match of mood, so it stays simile.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","punarchi","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 263","lb":"AI263","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",1,"unavu","simile","millet"],["கேழல்","கேழல்",2,"maa","simile","boar"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:27"},"ai:264":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":262,"t":["இளம்பிறை யன்ன கோட்ட கேழல்","களங்கனி யன்ன பெண்பாற் புணரும்","அயந்திகழ் சிலம்ப கண்டிகும்","பயந்தன மாதோநீ நயந்தோள் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the slope bright with springs, where a boar with tusks like the young crescent unites with its sow dark as the kalam fruit, that she has seen it: the eyes of the woman he loves have turned pale.","n":"கண்டிகும் (we have seen) makes the friend a witness, so the pallor is reported as evidence, not complaint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kan","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 264","lb":"AI264","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கேழல்","கேழல்",1,"maa","iraicchi","boar"],["பெண்பாற்","பெண்பால்",2,"maa","iraicchi","sow"],["களங்கனி","களா",2,"maram","simile","kalam fruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கேழல்","confirmed","1/1","the beast of his hill unites with its own mate, while the man leaves the woman he loves to grow pale, so he should come and marry her"]],"ch":"ai:27"},"ai:265":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":263,"t":["புலிகொல் பெண்பால் பூவரிக் குருளை","வளைவெண் மருப்பின் கேழல் புரக்கும்","குன்றுகெழு நாடன் மறந்தனன்","பொன்போல் புதல்வனோடு என்நீத் தோனே"],"p":"","r":"The heroine says the lord of the hill land, where the boar with curved white tusks rears the striped young of the sow a tiger killed, has forgotten her, he who left her with their son bright as gold.","n":"The animal reverses the human case exactly: there the father rears young whose mother is dead; here the mother is left with a son whose father lives and has gone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","pirivu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 265","lb":"AI265","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["புலி","புலி",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["பெண்பால்","பெண்பால்",1,"maa","absent","sow"],["குருளை","குருளை",1,"maa","scene","young"],["கேழல்","கேழல்",2,"maa","iraicchi","boar"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கேழல்","confirmed","1/1","even the boar guards its motherless young, yet the man has forgotten and left his wife and son"]],"ch":"ai:27"},"ai:266":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":264,"t":["சிறுகண் பன்றிப் பெருஞ்சின ஒருத்தலொடு","குறுங்கை இரும்புலி பொரூஉம் நாட","நனிநாண் உடைமையம் மன்ற","பனிப்பயந் தனநீ நயந்தோள் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the land where the great-tempered small-eyed boar fights the short-armed great tiger that they are surely bound by great modesty, but the eyes of the woman he loves have filled with tears.","n":"The modesty is the reason they cannot speak or send for him, so the tears speak instead. The boar-and-tiger fight could be read as the dangers of his night path or as a clash he should take on, but the beasts do nothing a person of the situation does, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kan","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 266","lb":"AI266","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பன்றிப்","பன்றி",1,"maa","scene","boar"],["இரும்புலி","புலி",2,"maa","scene","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:27"},"ai:267":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":265,"t":["சிறுகண் பறிப் பெருஞ்சின ஒருத்தல்","துறுகல் அடுக்கத்து வில்லோர் மாற்றி","ஐவனம் கவரும் குன்ற நாடன்","வண்டுபடு கூந்தலைப் பேணிப்","பண்பில சொல்லும் தேறுதல் செத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that the lord of the hill land, where the great-tempered small-eyed boar drives off the bowmen on the boulder slope and carries off the mountain rice, fondles her bee-swarmed hair and speaks unworthy words, thinking she will believe them.","n":"தேறுதல் செத்து (thinking it reassurance) shows she sees through him: the sweet words are taken for a pledge only by him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","sol","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 267","lb":"AI267","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஒருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",1,"maa","ullurai","boar"],["வில்லோர்","வில்லோர்",2,"seythi","scene","bowmen"],["ஐவனம்","ஐவனம்",3,"unavu","scene","hill rice"],["வண்டுபடு","வண்டு",4,"pira","scene","bees"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","ஒருத்தல்","confirmed","1/1","the hill lord courting the heroine"]],"ch":"ai:27"},"ai:268":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":266,"t":["தாஅய் இழந்த தழுவரிக் குருளையொடு","வளமலைச் சிறுதினை உணீஇய, கானவர்","வரையோங்கு உயர்சிமைக் கேழல் உறங்கும்","நன்மலை நாடன் பிரிதல்","என்பயக்கும் மோநம் விட்டுத் துறந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks what the parting of the lord of the good hill can bring, now that he has left and abandoned them, the hill where the boar sleeps on the high peak with a striped young that has lost its mother, after eating the hunters' small millet.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 268","lb":"AI268","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["குருளையொடு","குருளை",1,"maa","scene","young"],["சிறுதினை","சிறுதினை",2,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",2,"seythi","scene","hunters"],["கேழல்","கேழல்",3,"maa","iraicchi","boar"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கேழல்","not_confirmed","0/1","even the boar does not leave the young that has lost its mother, so the man's leaving her can bring nothing good"]],"ch":"ai:27"},"ai:269":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":267,"t":["கேழல் உழுதெனக் கிளர்ந்த எருவை","விளைந்த செறுவில் தோன்றும் நாடன்","வாராது அவண்உறை நீடின் நேர்வளை","இணை ஈர் ஓதி நீயழத்","துணைநனி இழக்குவென் மடமை யானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine that if the lord of the land, where the reed sprung up from the boar's rooting shows like a crop in a ripened field, stays on there and does not come while she weeps, then by her own folly she will lose her companion.","n":"The friend blames her own மடமை: she helped the secret love along, and now fears losing the friend to grief. The reed that rises in the boar's furrow looks like a crop nobody sowed, as the love grew unplanned.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thunai","madam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 269","lb":"AI269","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கேழல்","கேழல்",1,"maa","scene","boar"],["எருவை","எருவை",1,"maram","scene","reed"],["நேர்வளை","வளை",3,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:27"},"ai:270":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":268,"t":["கிழங்ககழ் கேழல் உழுத சிலம்பில்","தலைவிளை கானவர் கொய்தனர் பெயரும்","புல்லென் குன்றத்துப் புலம்புகொள் நெடுவரை","காணினும் கலிழுநோய் செத்துத்","தாம்வந் தனர்நம் காத லோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine that, though the hunters have reaped the first crop from the slope the root-digging boar ploughed and gone, and the hill has grown bare and its high peak lonely, their lover, guessing their weeping sickness, has come himself.","n":"With the harvest in, the millet watch is over and the girl is kept at home, so the bare hill is the end of their meeting place; his coming unbidden answers the fear that the season's end would end the love.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kaamanoy","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 270","lb":"AI270","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கிழங்ககழ்","கிழங்கு",1,"unavu","scene","tubers"],["கேழல்","கேழல்",1,"maa","scene","boar"],["தலைவிளை","தலைவிளை",2,"unavu","scene","first crop"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",2,"seythi","scene","hunters"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:27"},"ai:271":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":269,"t":["அவரை அருந்த மந்தி பகர்வர்","பக்கின் தோன்றும் நாடன் வேண்டின்","பல்பசுப் பெண்டிரும் பெறுகுவன்","தொல்கேள் ஆகலின் நல்குமால் இவட்கே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the lord of the land where a female monkey that has eaten avarai beans looks like a trader's stuffed bag could have many fresh young women if he wanted them, but because he is her old kinsman he stays kind to this girl.","n":"The comic image of the greedy monkey with bulging cheeks stands beside the claim that the lord does not grab every woman he could have: what holds him is the old tie (தொல்கேள்), so the friend grounds his constancy in kinship rather than in passion.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","ali","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 271","lb":"AI271","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அவரை","அவரை",1,"maram","gift_food","bean"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",1,"maa","simile","female monkey"],["பகர்வர்","பகர்வர்",1,"seythi","simile","traders"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:28"},"ai:272":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":270,"t":["கருவிரல் மந்திக் கல்லா வன்பறழ்","அருவரைத் தீந்தேன் எடுப்பை அயலது","உருகெழு நெடுஞ்சினைப் பாயும் நாடன்","இரவின் வருதல் அறியான்","வரும் வரும் என்பள் தோழியாயே."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that the lord of the land where the untaught young of the black-fingered female monkey leaps onto the fearsome tall branch beside the sweet honey of the steep cliff does not know what coming by night means, and that her mother keeps saying 'he will come, he will come'.","n":"The untaught young monkey (கல்லா) that leaps at the dangerous branch beside the honey answers the lord who 'does not know' (அறியான்) the danger of the night path; that is a near-miss ullurai, left as scene because the closing lines cannot be fixed: whether the mother's 'he will come' means she has guessed the night visits or expects a suitor changes the situation the image would point to.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 272","lb":"AI272","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மந்திக்","மந்தி",1,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["வன்பறழ்","பறழ்",1,"maa","scene","young monkey"],["தீந்தேன்","தேன்",2,"unavu","scene","honey"],["நெடுஞ்சினைப்","சினை",3,"maram","scene","branch"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:28"},"ai:273":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":271,"t":["அத்தச் செயலைத் துப்புறழ் ஒள்தளிர்","புன் தலை மந்தி வன்பறழ் ஆரும்","நன்மலை நாட நீசெலின்","நின்நயத்து உறைவி என்னினும் கலிழ்மே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the good hill, where the strong young of the dull-headed female monkey feed on the coral-bright shoots of the asoka by the path, that if he goes away, she who lives on her love for him will weep even more than the friend herself.","n":"The friend measures the girl's grief against her own (என்னினும்): she admits she too will weep, so the plea is made from inside the shared household of the two girls, not as a go-between's tactic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kalaviyal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 273","lb":"AI273","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["செயலைத்","செயலை",1,"maram","scene","asoka"],["ஒள்தளிர்","தளிர்",1,"maram","gift_food","young shoots"],["துப்புறழ்","துப்பு",1,"pira","simile","coral"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",2,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["வன்பறழ்","பறழ்",2,"maa","scene","young monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:28"},"ai:274":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":272,"t":["மந்திக் கணவன் கல்லாக் கடுவன்","ஒண் கேழ் வயப்புலி குழுமலின் விரைந்து உடன்","குன்றுயர் அடுக்கம் கொள்ளும் நாடன்","சென்றனன் வாழி தோழியென்","மெல்தோள் கவினும் பாயலும் கொண்டே."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that the lord of the land where the untaught male, husband of the female monkey, hurries up the high slope together with her when the bright-striped tiger roars has gone away, taking with him the beauty of her soft shoulders and her sleep.","n":"கல்லா (untaught) is pointed: the creature that has learnt nothing still keeps his mate beside him, so the lord's leaving reads as a failure of what even a beast knows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nalam","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 274","lb":"AI274","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மந்திக்","மந்தி",1,"maa","iraicchi","female monkey"],["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",1,"maa","iraicchi","male monkey"],["வயப்புலி","புலி",2,"maa","scene","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கடுவன்","confirmed","1/1","even the untaught monkey takes his mate with him out of danger, while the lord went off alone and took only her beauty and her sleep"]],"ch":"ai:28"},"ai:275":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":273,"t":["குரங்கின் தலிஅவன் குருமயிர்க் கடுவன்","சூரலஞ்ச் சிறுகோல் கொண்டு வியலறை","மாரி மொக்குள் புடைக்கும் நாட","யாம்நின் நயந்தனம் எனினும்எம்","ஆய்நலம் வாடுமோ அருளுதி எனினே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the land where the red-furred male monkey, leader of the troop, beats the rain bubbles on the wide rock with a little cane stick that, though the girls desire him, their fine beauty would not fade if he would only be gracious.","n":"The monkey smashing the fragile rain bubbles for play sits close to the girl's beauty that fades while the lord delays, a near-miss ullurai: only one correspondence (careless play breaking something fragile) can be named, so it fails condition 3. The friend's question turns his grace (அருள்) into the cure: marriage, not desire, keeps beauty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","nalam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 275","lb":"AI275","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குரங்கின்","குரங்கு",1,"maa","scene","monkey troop"],["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",1,"maa","scene","male monkey"],["சூரலஞ்ச்","சூரல்",2,"maram","scene","cane"],["மொக்குள்","மொக்குள்",3,"pira","scene","rain bubbles"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:28"},"ai:276":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":274,"t":["மந்திக் காதலன் முறிமேய் கடுவன்","தண்கமழ் நறைக்கொடி கொண்டு வியலறைப்","பொங்கல் இளமழை புடைக்கும் நாட","நயவாய் ஆயினும் வரைந்தனை சென்மோ","கன்முகை வேங்கை மலரும்","நன்மலை நாடன் பெண்டெனப் படுத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the land where the leaf-eating male monkey, the female's lover, beats the swelling young clouds on the wide rock with a cool fragrant narai creeper: even if he does not love her, let him marry her before he goes, so that she is called the wife of the lord of the good hill where vengai blooms in the rock clefts.","n":"நயவாய் ஆயினும் is startling: the friend concedes he may not love her and still asks for marriage, so what she wants for the girl is the name (பெண்டு எனப் படுத்து), the public standing that protects her, more than his feeling. The vengai in bloom is the hill's marriage season.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","karpu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 276","lb":"AI276","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மந்திக்","மந்தி",1,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",1,"maa","scene","male monkey"],["நறைக்கொடி","நறைக்கொடி",2,"maram","scene","narai creeper"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",5,"maram","scene","vengai"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:28"},"ai:277":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":275,"t":["குறவர் முன்றில் மாதீண்டு துறுகல்","கல்லா மந்தி கடுவனோடு உகளும்","குன்ற நாடநின் மொழிவல் என்றும்","பயப்ப நீத்தல் என்இவள்","கயத்துவளர் குவளையின் அமர்த்த கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the hill, where the untaught female monkey frolics with her male on the boulder in the hunters' yard that the beasts rub against: let him never leave the girl so that pallor spreads over her eyes, which rival the blue lily growing in the pond.","n":"The monkeys play right beside human dwellings (குறவர் முன்றில்), so the pair is a sight the lord passes every time he comes: the friend's warning is set on his own doorstep.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 277","lb":"AI277","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குறவர்","குறவர்",1,"seythi","scene","hill hunters"],["மாதீண்டு","மா",1,"maa","scene","beasts"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",2,"maa","iraicchi","female monkey"],["கடுவனோடு","கடுவன்",2,"maa","iraicchi","male monkey"],["குவளையின்","குவளை",5,"maram","simile","blue water lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மந்தி","confirmed","1/1","the beasts of his own hill keep to their mates, so the lord should never leave the girl and let her eyes grow pale"]],"ch":"ai:28"},"ai:278":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":276,"t":["சிலம்பின் வெதிரத்துக் கண்விடு கழைக்கோல்","குரங்கின் வன் பறழ் பாய்ந்தன இலஞ்சி","மீனெறி தூண்டிலின் நிவக்கும் நாடன்","உற்றோர் மறவா நோய்தந்து","கண்டோர் தண்டா நலங்கொண் டனனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the lord of the land, where the jointed bamboo of the slope, once the strong young monkey has leapt off it, springs up like a fishing rod that jerks a fish out of the pool, has given the girl a sickness those who suffer it never forget and has taken the beauty onlookers never tire of.","n":"The fishing-rod simile is the key: the bamboo does not merely spring back, it springs back like a rod that has just landed a fish, so the image already contains the taking (கொண்டனன்) the closing line names.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nalam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 278","lb":"AI278","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வெதிரத்துக்","வெதிரம்",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["கழைக்கோல்","கழை",1,"maram","ullurai","bamboo stem"],["குரங்கின்","குரங்கு",2,"maa","ullurai","monkey"],["இலஞ்சி","இலஞ்சி",2,"pira","scene","pool"],["தூண்டிலின்","தூண்டில்",3,"seythi","simile","fishing rod"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","கழைக்கோல் குரங்கின்","confirmed","1/1","the lord who has had his joy of the girl and gone off unchanged"]],"ch":"ai:28"},"ai:279":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":277,"t":["கல் இவர் இற்றி புல்லுவன ஏறிக்","குளவி மேய்ந்த மந்தி துணையோடு","வரைமிசை உகளும் நாட நீவரின்","கல்லகத் ததுஎம் ஊரே","அம்பல் சேரி அலராம் கட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the land where the female monkey, having climbed the clinging itti tree that spreads over the rock and grazed on wild jasmine, frolics with her mate on the hillside, that if he comes, their village lies within the rocks and its whispering street will turn to open gossip.","n":"அம்பல் (the murmur) and அலர் (the spoken gossip) are two stages of the same talk: the friend warns that his next visit will turn the first into the second.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 279","lb":"AI279","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இற்றி","இற்றி",1,"maram","scene","rock fig"],["குளவி","குளவி",2,"maram","gift_food","wild jasmine"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",2,"maa","iraicchi","female monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மந்தி","confirmed","1/1","the beasts may be together in plain sight, while the lovers' meetings set the village whispering, so only marriage will let them be together openly"]],"ch":"ai:28"},"ai:280":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":278,"t":["கருவிரல் மந்திக் கல்லா வன்பார்ப்பு","இருவெதிர் ஈர்ங்கழை ஏறிச் சிறுகோல்","மதிபுடைப் பதுபோல தோன்றும் நாட","வரைந்தனை நீஎனக் கேட்டுயான்","உரைத்தனென் அல்லனோ அஃதென் யாய்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the land where the untaught strong young of the black-fingered female monkey, climbing the tall green bamboo, seems to beat the moon with a little stick: hearing that he would marry, did she not tell it to her mother?","n":"The monkey reaching for the moon is a joke about overreach, and the friend's question turns it on herself: she spoke to her mother on the strength of his word, so if he now delays she is the one left beating at the moon.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 280","lb":"AI280","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மந்திக்","மந்தி",1,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["வன்பார்ப்பு","பார்ப்பு",1,"maa","simile","young monkey"],["இருவெதிர்","வெதிர்",2,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["ஈர்ங்கழை","கழை",2,"maram","scene","bamboo stem"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:28"},"ai:281":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":279,"t":["வெள்ள வரம்பின் ஊழி போகியும்","கிள்ளை வாழிய பலவே ஒள்ளிழை","இரும்பல் கூந்தல் கொடிச்சி","பெருந்தோள் காவல் காட்டி யவ்வே."],"p":"","r":"The man blesses the parrots: may they live for ages beyond counting, for they brought about the guarding of the millet by the hill girl with bright jewels, thick dark hair and broad shoulders.","n":"The long-life blessing (வாழிய பலவே) that bards give kings is given to crop thieves. காவல் காட்டியவ்வே says the parrots 'showed' him her guarding: the raids that are the family's loss are his gain, since they set her alone in the field where he can see her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 281","lb":"AI281","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கிள்ளை","கிள்ளை",2,"pul","scene","parrot"],["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",3,"seythi","scene","hill girl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:29"},"ai:282":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":280,"t":["சாரல் புறத்த பெருங்குரல் சிறுதினைப்","பேரமர் மழைக்கண் கொடிச்சி கடியவும்","சோலைச் சிறுகிளி உன்னு நாட","அரிருள் பெருகின வாரல்","கோட்டுமா வழங்கும் காட்டக நெறியே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the land where the small parrot of the grove still waits on the big-eared millet on the slope even as the hill girl with large rain-cool eyes drives it off: do not come, the thick darkness has deepened on the forest path where tusked beasts roam.","n":"The parrot that keeps eyeing the millet while the girl chases it looks like the man who keeps coming though she is guarded, but no second pair or anchor ties it to him, so it stays scene (an ullurai near miss). The refusal is for the night only; the field by day is where she can be found.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kuri","acham","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 282","lb":"AI282","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["சிறுதினைப்","சிறு தினை",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",2,"seythi","scene","hill girl"],["சிறுகிளி","சிறு கிளி",3,"pul","scene","parrot"],["கோட்டுமா","கோட்டு மா",5,"maa","scene","tusked beast"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:29"},"ai:283":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":281,"t":["வன்கண் கானவன் மென்சொல் மடமகள்","புன்புல மயக்கத்து உழுத ஏனல்","பைம்புறச் சிறுகிளி கடியும் நாட","பெரிய கூறி நீப்பினும்","பொய்வலைப் படூஉம் பெண்டுதவப் பலவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the land where the soft-spoken simple daughter of a hard-eyed hunter drives the green-backed small parrots from the millet sown in mixed poor soil: even if you make great promises and leave, the women caught in your net of lies are very many.","n":"The opening sets the hard-eyed father against the soft-spoken daughter: she belongs to a house that can be fierce. The last line may be read as a taunt (you can leave her; you will find others in your net) or as a warning to the girl's side (many have been caught so); either way the friend names his promises as bait.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","vaaimai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 283","lb":"AI283","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["கானவன்","கானவன்",1,"seythi","scene","hunter"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",2,"unavu","scene","millet"],["சிறுகிளி","சிறு கிளி",3,"pul","scene","parrot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:29"},"ai:284":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":282,"t":["அளிய தாமே செவ்வாய்ப் பைங்கிளி","குன்றக் குறவர் கொய்தினைப் பைங்கால்","இருவை நீள்புனங் கண்டும்","பிரிதல் தேற்றாப் பேரன் பினவே."],"p":"","r":"Pitiable are the red-mouthed green parrots: even seeing the long field reduced to the green-stalked stubble of millet the hill hunters have cut, they have a great love that cannot learn to part from it.","n":"The whole poem is the parrots, so iraicchi fails the deletion test (strike the birds and nothing is left); the harvest that ends the girl's guard duty is what the parrots' love outlasts. Whom the pity is aimed at turns on the unstated speaker.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 284","lb":"AI284","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"kalavu",null],["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பைங்கிளி","பைங் கிளி",1,"pul","scene","parrot"],["குறவர்","குறவர்",2,"seythi","scene","hill people"],["கொய்தினைப்","கொய் தினை",2,"unavu","scene","cut millet"],["இருவை","இருவை",3,"maram","absent","stubble"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:29"},"ai:285":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":283,"t":["பின்னிருங் கூந்தல் நன்னுதல் குறமகள்","மெல்தினை நுவணை யுண்டு தட்டையின்","ஐவனச் சிறுகிளி கடியும் நாட","வீங்குவளை நெகிழப் பிரிதல்","யாங்குவல் லுநையோ ஈங்கிவள் துறந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man of the land where the hill girl with plaited dark hair and fine forehead eats soft millet flour and with her clapper drives small parrots from the hill rice: how can you bring yourself to part, leaving her here, so that her full bangles slip?","n":"The friend's picture of the girl is all small domestic ease, eating millet flour and rattling the clapper; the slipping bangle (வீங்குவளை நெகிழ) is the sign of wasting that his parting would bring, so the question யாங்கு வல்லுநையோ is a charge of hardness, not a request for reasons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 285","lb":"AI285","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குறமகள்","குறமகள்",1,"seythi","scene","hill girl"],["நுவணை","நுவணை",2,"unavu","gift_food","millet flour"],["தட்டையின்","தட்டை",2,"pira","scene","bamboo clapper"],["ஐவனச்","ஐவனம்",3,"unavu","scene","hill rice"],["சிறுகிளி","சிறு கிளி",3,"pul","scene","parrot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:29"},"ai:286":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":284,"t":["சிறுதினை கொய்த இருவை வெண்கால்","காய்த்த அவரைப் படுகிளி கடியும்","யாண ராகிய நன்மலை நாடன்","புகரின்று நயந்தனன் போலும்","கவரும் தோழிஎன் மாமைக் கவினே."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that the lord of the good, ever-yielding hill, where on the white stalks of the stubble left after the small millet was cut the bean has podded and the settling parrots are driven off, seems to have loved her without blemish, yet he carries off the dark glow of her beauty.","n":"His land is யாணர், yielding again even after the harvest, while her own glow is taken; போலும் (it seems) holds back from blaming him, and the loss is put on his love itself, which takes as it gives. The stubble bearing beans could be read as a hidden image of renewal, but no pair ties it to the lovers, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","nalam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 286","lb":"AI286","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["சிறுதினை","சிறு தினை",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["இருவை","இருவை",1,"maram","scene","stubble"],["அவரைப்","அவரை",2,"maram","scene","bean"],["படுகிளி","படு கிளி",2,"pul","scene","parrot"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:29"},"ai:287":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":285,"t":["நெடுவரை மிசையது குறுங்கால் வருடை","தினைபாய் கிள்ளை வெரூஉம் நாட","வல்லை மன்ற பொய்த்தல்","வல்லாய் மன்றநீ அல்லது செயலே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the land where the short-legged mountain goat on the high peak takes fright at the parrots leaping onto the millet: surely you are skilled at lying, and surely skilled at nothing else.","n":"The two lines turn on one word: வல்லை (you are able) at lying, வல்லாய் (you are not able) at anything else, a sarcasm that grants him skill only in deceit. The goat startled by small birds may hint at a strong man made timid, but it does no act of the love situation, so it is scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","vaaimai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 287","lb":"AI287","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வருடை","வருடை",1,"maa","scene","mountain goat"],["தினைபாய்","தினை",2,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கிள்ளை","கிள்ளை",2,"pul","scene","parrot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:29"},"ai:288":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":286,"t":["நன்றே செய்த உதவி நன்றுதெரிந்து","யாம் எவன் செய்குவம் நெஞ்சே காமர்","மெல்லியல் கொடிச்சி காப்பப்","பல்குரல் ஏனல் பாத்தரும் கிளியே."],"p":"","r":"The man asks his heart what they can do in return, knowing how good it was, for the help the parrots gave: while the gentle, lovely hill girl guards the many-eared millet, they come and share it out among themselves.","n":"The language of debt (செய்த உதவி, நன்று தெரிந்து, எவன் செய்குவம்) is the language of gratitude to a benefactor, put to birds who meant no help: their theft keeps her in the field. பாத்தரும் is read as share out and eat.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","nenju","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 288","lb":"AI288","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",3,"seythi","scene","hill girl"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",4,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கிளியே","கிளி",4,"pul","scene","parrot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:29"},"ai:289":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":287,"t":["கொடிச்சி இன்குரல் கிளிசெத் தடுக்கத்துப்","பைங்குரல் ஏனல் படர்தரும் கிளியெனக்","காவலும் கடியுநர் போல்வர்","மால்வரை நாட வரைந்தனை கொண்மோ."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the great hills that the elders, saying the parrots come to the green-eared millet on the slope because they take the hill girl's sweet voice for a parrot's, seem ready to stop even her guarding; so marry her and take her.","n":"The girl's charm turns against her: her voice is so like the birds' that it calls them instead of scaring them, and that gives the household reason to take the guard from her, ending the day meetings. The accham is the girl's side's fear of the elders' command.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 289","lb":"AI289","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",1,"seythi","scene","hill girl"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",2,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கிளியெனக்","கிளி",2,"pul","scene","parrot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:29"},"ai:290":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":288,"t":["அறம்புரி செங்கோல் மன்னனின் தாம்நனி","சிறந்தன போலும் கிள்ளை பிறங்கிய","பூக்கமழ் கூந்தல் கொடிச்சி","நோக்கவும் படும்அவள் ஒப்பவும் படுமே."],"p":"","r":"The man says the parrots seem far more fortunate than a king of the just red sceptre devoted to right: they are looked at by the hill girl with bright, flower-fragrant hair, and they are driven off by her.","n":"A comparison of excess (the ablative -இன், more than): the best a kingdom can offer is ranked below being glanced at and shooed away by her. ஒப்புதல் is the field word for driving off birds, so even her chasing is a favour he envies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 290","lb":"AI290","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["கிள்ளை","கிள்ளை",2,"pul","scene","parrot"],["பூக்கமழ்","பூ",3,"maram","ornament","flowers"],["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",3,"seythi","scene","hill girl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:29"},"ai:291":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":289,"t":["மயில்கள் ஆலக் குடிஞை இரட்டும்","துறுகல் அடுக்கத்து அதுவே பணைத்தோள்","ஆய்தழை நுடங்கும் அல்குல்","காதலி உறையும் நனிநல் லூரே."],"p":"","r":"The man points out the hill slope of piled boulders where peacocks dance and the owl calls back and forth, and says that is the fine town where his beloved lives, the girl with shoulders like bamboo and a waist where a skirt of chosen leaves sways.","n":"The poem names nothing but a place, yet the place is all lover's sight: the dancing peacocks and the calling owl are the sounds he hears around her town, and the leaf skirt on her waist is the one ornament of the hill girl.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kalaviyal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 291","lb":"AI291","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மயில்கள்","மயில்",1,"pul","scene","peacock"],["குடிஞை","குடிஞை",1,"pul","scene","owl"],["ஆய்தழை","தழை",3,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:30"},"ai:292":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":290,"t":["மயில்கள் ஆலப் பெருந்தேன் இமிரத்","தண்மழை தழீஇய மாமலை நாட","நின்னினும் சிறந்தனள் எமக்கே நீநயந்து","நன்மனை அருங்கடி அயர","எம்நலம் சிறப்பயாம் இனிப்பெற் றோளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the great hill embraced by cool rain clouds, where peacocks dance and big bees hum, that the girl is dearer to them than he is, for by holding in desire the strictly guarded wedding in the good house he has given them back their own well-being, and she is now theirs for good.","n":"The friend's praise of the bride is also a claim on her: the girl the man has married is more precious to her own circle than the man, and their own welfare (எம்நலம்) rose with the wedding, so the secret love's anxiety is over for the friend as much as for the girl.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","natpu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 292","lb":"AI292","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மயில்கள்","மயில்",1,"pul","scene","peacock"],["பெருந்தேன்","தேன்",1,"pira","scene","honeybee"],["தண்மழை","மழை",2,"pira","scene","rain cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:30"},"ai:293":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":291,"t":["சிலம்புகமழ் காந்தள் நறுங்குலை யன்ன","நலம்பெறு கையின்என் கண்புதைத் தோயே","பாயல் இன்துணை யாகிய பணைத்தோள்","தோகை மாட்சிய மடந்தை","நீயலது உளரோஎன் நெஞ்சமர்ந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The man asks the girl who covered his eyes with her lovely hand, like a fragrant cluster of kanthal that scents the hillside, the maiden of bamboo shoulders with the grace of a peacock who has become his sweet companion of the bed, whether anyone but her dwells in his heart.","n":"The game of covering his eyes from behind asks 'guess who'; his answer turns the game into a vow, since there is no one else it could be. The flower of the hand is the kanthal, the hill's own flower, so she is praised with things of her land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","nenju","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 293","lb":"AI293","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",1,"maram","simile","glory lily"],["தோகை","தோகை",4,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:30"},"ai:294":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":292,"t":["எரிமருள் வேங்கை இருந்த தோகை","இழையணி மடந்தையின் தோன்றும் நாட","இனிதுசெய் தனையால் நுந்தை வாழியர்","நன்மனை வதுவை அயர இவள்","பின்னருங் கூந்தல் மலர் அணிந்தோயே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the land where the peacock perched on the fire-bright vengai looks like a jewelled maiden that he has done a sweet thing, and blesses his father, for holding the wedding in the good house and setting flowers in the girl's thick braided hair.","n":"The simile already shows the wedding before the friend names it: a peacock dressed in the vengai's red-gold blossoms is a bride dressed in jewels, and the last line has the man himself dress her hair with flowers. The blessing on his father (நுந்தை வாழியர்) praises the man through his lineage, as one praises a good family's son. That the peacock and the bride make a hidden comparison is ruled out by the marker -இன், which states it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","karpu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 294","lb":"AI294","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["தோகை","தோகை",1,"pul","simile","peacock"],["மலர்","மலர்",5,"maram","ornament","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:30"},"ai:295":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":293,"t":["வருவது கொல்லோ தானே வாராது","அவணுறை மேவலின் அமைவது கொல்லோ","புனவர் கொள்ளியின் புகல்வரும் மஞ்ஞை","இருவி யிருந்த குருவி வருந்துறப்","பந்தாடு மகளிரின் படர்தரும்","குன்றுகெழு நாடனொடு சென்றஎன் நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The girl asks whether her heart, which went off with the man of the hill country, where the peacock, driven by the field-clearers' firebrands, rushes out like girls at ball play and harries the sparrows on the millet stubble, will come back, or will settle there because it wants to live with him.","n":"The question is put to no one: she cannot recall the heart and does not know its mind. The picture of the startled peacock scattering the sparrows like girls chasing a ball is lively play beside her stillness; reading it as a hidden image of her own unsettled heart would need more than one match, so it is left as scene. Line 3 (புனவர் கொள்ளியின் புகல்வரும்) may mean the peacock flees the firebrands or comes eagerly toward them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 295","lb":"AI295","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புனவர்","புனவர்",3,"seythi","scene","field-clearers"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",3,"pul","simile","peacock"],["இருவி","இருவி",4,"maram","scene","millet stubble"],["குருவி","குருவி",4,"pul","scene","sparrow"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:30"},"ai:296":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":294,"t":["கொடிச்சி காக்கும் பெருங்குரல் ஏனல்","அடுக்கல் மஞ்ஞை கவரு நாட","நடுநாள் கங்குலும் வருதி","கடுமா தாக்கின் அறியேன் யானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the land where the slope's peacock steals the heavy-eared millet the hill girl guards that he comes even in the dark of midnight, and that if a fierce beast should strike him she would not know what to do.","n":"The refusal is made as fear for him, not as blame. The opening scene does double work: the guarded millet stolen by the peacock is the guarded girl he takes in secret, and the millet field is also where the girls are by day, a hint of where he might meet her instead of at midnight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kalaviyal","acham","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 296","lb":"AI296","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",1,"seythi","ullurai","hill girl"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",1,"unavu","ullurai","millet"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",2,"pul","ullurai","peacock"],["கடுமா","மா",4,"maa","scene","fierce beast"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","கொடிச்சி ஏனல் மஞ்ஞை","not_confirmed","0/2","the guarded girl and the man who comes for her by night"],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:30"},"ai:297":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":295,"t":["விரிந்த வேங்கைப் பெருஞ்சினைத் தோகை","பூக்கொய் மகளிரின் தோன்றும் நாட","பிரியினும் பிரிவ தன்றே","நின்னொடு மேய மடந்தை நட்பே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the land where the peacock on the great bough of the spreading vengai looks like women plucking flowers that even if he goes away, the love of the girl who has joined with him will not go away.","n":"The play on பிரி (part) carries the poem: his parting is one thing and her bond's parting another, and the second will not follow the first. It is an assurance, but also a reminder that the bond holds her whether or not he returns.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pirivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 297","lb":"AI297","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["தோகை","தோகை",1,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:30"},"ai:298":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":296,"t":["மழைவரவு அறியா மஞ்ஞை ஆலும்","அடுக்கல் நல்லூர் அசைநடைக் கொடிச்சி","தான்எம் அருளாள் ஆயினும்","யாம்தன் உள்ளுபு மறந்தறியேமே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that though the slow-gaited hill girl of the good town on the slope, where peacocks dance without knowing whether rain will come, shows him no grace, he has never once forgotten her, thinking of her always.","n":"உள்ளுபு மறந்தறியேம், 'thinking of her, we have never known forgetting', is the same turn the Kural gives the man in 1125. The peacock that dances before any sign of rain is the man who keeps loving before any sign of her favour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kaamam","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 298","lb":"AI298","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",1,"pul","iraicchi","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மஞ்ஞை","confirmed","1/1","the man keeps the girl in his thoughts and loves on, though no sign of her grace has come"]],"ch":"ai:30"},"ai:299":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":297,"t":["குன்ற நாடன் குன்றத்துக் கவாஅன்","பைஞ்சுனைப் பூத்த பகுவாய்க் குவளையும்","அம்சில் ஓதி அசைநடைக் கொடிச்சி","கண்போல் மலர்தலும் அரிதுஇவள்","தன்போல் சாயல் மஞ்ஞைக்கும் அரிதே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the split-mouthed blue lilies blooming in the green spring on the hill-man's slope can hardly open like the eyes of the slow-gaited hill girl with her fine thin locks, and that even the peacock can hardly have her soft grace.","n":"The comparison is turned round: the lily and the peacock, the hill's usual likenesses for eyes and grace, are measured against her and fall short. Both come from her own hill (குன்ற நாடன் குன்றத்து), so it is her land's best that she outdoes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 299","lb":"AI299","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["பைஞ்சுனைப்","சுனை",2,"pira","scene","mountain spring"],["குவளையும்","குவளை",2,"maram","simile","blue water-lily"],["மஞ்ஞைக்கும்","மஞ்ஞை",5,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:30"},"ai:300":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":298,"t":["கொடிச்சி கூந்தல் போலத் தோகை","அம்சிறை விவரிக்கும் பெருங்கல் வெற்பன்","வந்தனன் எதிர்ந்தனர் கொடையே","அம்தீம் கிளவி பொலிகநின் சிறப்பே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl of sweet words that the lord of the great-rocked hill, where the peacock spreads its lovely plumes like a hill girl's hair, has come, and her kin have agreed to give her to him: may her good fortune flourish.","n":"The decade closes with the marriage settled. Here the peacock is likened to the girl, not she to it: by the end of the decade she is the standard the hill's birds are measured by.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 300","lb":"AI300","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["தோகை","தோகை",1,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:30"},"ai:301":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":299,"t":["மால்வெள் ளோத்திரத்து மையில் வாலிணர்","அருஞ்சுரம் செல்வோர் சென்னிக் கூட்டும்","அவ்வரை யிறக்குவை யாயின்","மைவரை நாட வருந்துவள் பெரிதே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the dark-hilled country that if he crosses that range, where travellers on the hard waste crown their heads with the white clusters of the great pale lodhra, the woman he leaves will suffer greatly.","n":"The only image of the road is a festive one: those who cross it wear its flowers on their heads. The friend grants the journey its attraction and sets against it a single plain clause about the one who stays; அவ்வரை, that range, is set against his own மைவரை, the dark hills he would leave.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 301","lb":"AI301","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மால்வெள் ளோத்திரத்து","வெள்ளோத்திரம்",1,"maram","ornament","white lodhra"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:31"},"ai:302":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":300,"t":["அரும்பெருள் செய்வினை தப்பற்கும் உரித்தே","பெருந்தோள் அரிவை தகைத்தற்கும் உரியள்","செல்லாய் ஆயினோ நன்றே","மல்லம் புலம்ப இவள்அழப் பிரிந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that his hard-won work for rare wealth has a right not to be failed, and the broad-shouldered woman has an equal right to hold him back; it would be good if he did not go, leaving her weeping and the rich place forlorn.","n":"The two claims are set in matching lines (உரித்தே, உரியள்): she does not call the wealth-work wrong, only equal, and தகைத்தல், holding back, is the very act the decade is named for. She asks for a choice, not a verdict.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 302","lb":"AI302","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:31"},"ai:303":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":301,"t":["புதுக்கலத் தன்ன கனிய ஆலம்","போகில்தனைத் தடுக்கும் வேனில் அருஞ்சுரம்","தண்ணிய இனிய வாக","எம்மொடுஞ் சென்மோ விடலை நீயே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the young man, since in summer's hard waste the banyan with fruit like new-fired pots holds back the போகில் bird, to take them with him so that the road may be cool and sweet.","n":"Near miss for iraicchi: the banyan detaining the passing bird does what the decade wants done to the man, but lines 1 and 2 also carry the waste that line 3's wish (தண்ணிய இனிய வாக) refers to, so the deletion test does not plainly pass; it stays scene. The plea changes ground: not 'do not go' but 'go with us'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thunai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 303","lb":"AI303","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆலம்","ஆலம்",1,"maram","scene","banyan"],["போகில்தனைத்","போகில்",2,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:31"},"ai:304":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":302,"t":["கல்லாக் கோவலர் கோலின் தோண்டிய","ஆன்நீர்ப் பத்தல் யானை வெளவும்","கல்லதர்க் கவலை செல்லின் மெல்லியல்","புயல்நெடுங் கூந்தல் புலம்பும்","வயமான் தோன்றல் வல்லா தீமே."],"p":"","r":"The friend warns the young lord of strong horses that if he goes by the stony forking path, where the elephant seizes the cattle's water from the trough the unlettered herdsmen dug with their sticks, the soft woman with long cloud-dark hair will grieve; let him not be capable of it.","n":"வல்லாதீமே asks him to be incapable: the strength his epithet praises (வயமான் தோன்றல்) is the strength the friend asks him to lack. The road's one water is dug by hand and still taken by force, so the waste is shown as a place where even what is made is lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasi","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 304","lb":"AI304","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["ஆன்நீர்ப்","ஆன்",2,"maa","scene","cattle"],["பத்தல்","பத்தல்",2,"pira","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","scene",""],["வயமான்","வய மா",5,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:31"},"ai:305":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":303,"t":["களிறு பிடிதழீஇப் பிறபுலம் படராது","பசிதின வருந்தும் பைதறு குன்றத்துச்","சுடர்தொடிக் குறுமகள் இனைய","எனப்பயஞ் செய்யுமோ விடலைநின் செலவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the young man what gain his going will bring, if it makes the girl of bright bangles grieve, on the parched hills where the tusker, holding his mate close, does not wander to other lands but suffers with her as hunger eats them.","n":"பிறபுலம் படராது is the exact negative of the hero's plan: the beast will not go to other lands even for food, the man will go to other lands for wealth. பயம், gain, is the hero's own measure turned against him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","payan","anbu","pasi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 305","lb":"AI305","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",1,"maa","iraicchi","bull elephant"],["பிடிதழீஇப்","பிடி",1,"maa","iraicchi","cow elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","களிறு","confirmed","1/1","even the beast in drought will not leave his mate for a richer land, so the man who would leave her for wealth falls below it"]],"ch":"ai:31"},"ai:306":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":304,"t":["வெல்போர்க் குருசில்நீ வியன்சுரம் இறப்பின்","பல்கழ் அல்குல் அவ்வரி வாடக்","குழலினும் இனைகுவள் பெரிதே","விழவொலி கூந்தல் மாஅ யோளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the warrior lord that if he crosses the wide waste, the dark woman with hair like a festival's abundance will grieve more than the flute laments, as the fine lines on her jewelled waist wither.","n":"Her grief is measured against the flute (குழலினும்), the instrument of the evening's mourning music, so her lament is pictured as heard, not only felt. The address வெல்போர்க் குருசில் praises his victories in war just as it asks him not to set out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 306","lb":"AI306","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குழலினும்","குழல்",3,"yaazh","scene","flute"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:31"},"ai:307":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":305,"t":["ஞெலி கழை முழங்கழல் வயமா வெரூஉம்","குன்றுடை அருஞ்சுரம் செலவயர்ந் தனையே","நன்றில் கொண்கநின் பொருளே","பாவை யன்னநின் துணைபிரிந்து வருமே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore that he has set his heart on the hill-bound hard waste where even the strong beast shies from the roaring flame of the fire-drill bamboo, and that his wealth is not good, since it comes by parting from his doll-like companion.","n":"The beast fears the fire and the man does not fear the road, which the friend does not praise. The wealth is the grammatical subject that comes (வருமே) by the parting, so the parting is its price, paid by her. கொண்க, the shore-lord's address, is unusual in a paalai poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 307","lb":"AI307","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கழை","கழை",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["வயமா","வய மா",1,"maa","scene","strong beast"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:31"},"ai:308":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":306,"t":["பல்லிருங் கூந்தல் மெல்லிய லோள்வயின்","பிரியாய் ஆயினும் நன்றே விரியிணர்க்","கால் எறுழ் ஒள்வீ தாஅய","முருகமர் மாமலை பிரிந்தெனப் பிரிமே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that it would be good not to part from the soft woman of thick dark hair; if he parts, they will part as one parts from the great hill where Murugan dwells and the bright blossoms of the eru tree lie strewn.","n":"பிரிமே is first person plural: the friend speaks of the women's own parting, and the comparison is to leaving the sacred home hill, so his going would uproot them too. Whether this means leaving home with him, or leaving life, the poem does not settle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 308","lb":"AI308","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["எறுழ்","எறுழ்",3,"maram","scene",""],["முருகமர்","முருகு",4,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:31"},"ai:309":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":307,"t":["வேனில் திங்கள் வெஞ்சுரம் இறந்து","செலவுஅயர்ந் தனையால் நீயே நன்று","நின்னயந்து உறைவி கடுஞ்சூல் சிறுவன்","முறுவல் காண்டலின் இனிதோ","இறுவரை நாடநீ இறந்துசெய் பொருளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the steep-hilled country that he is set on crossing the hot waste in the summer month, and asks whether the wealth he would make by going is sweeter than seeing the smile of the little son his loving wife carries in her heavy pregnancy.","n":"The son is not yet born (கடுஞ்சூல்), so the smile weighed against the wealth is one he has never seen; the friend sets made wealth (செய் பொருள்) against a good that needs only his presence. நன்று in line 2 is ironic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","makkal","selvam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 309","lb":"AI309","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:31"},"ai:310":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":308,"t":["பொலம்பசும் பாண்டில் காசுநிரை அல்குல்","இலங்குவளை மெல்தோள் இழைநிலை நெகிழப்","பிரிதல் வல்லுவை ஆயின்","அரிதே விடலை இவள் ஆய்நுதற் கவினே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the young man that if he is capable of parting, so that the bright bangles of her soft shoulders and her ornaments slip loose, the beauty of her fine brow will be hard to keep.","n":"Her body is described first by its ornaments (the gold girdle of coins, the bangles) and the harm is told as their loosening, the visible sign of wasting. வல்லுவை, 'if you are able', makes the departure a test of his hardness, not of his means.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nalam","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 310","lb":"AI310","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["இலங்குவளை","வளை",2,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:31"},"ai:311":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":309,"t":["வேங்கை கொய்யுநர் பஞ்சுரம் விளிப்பினும்","ஆரிடைச் செல்வோர் ஆறுநனி வெரூஉம்","காடுஇறந் தனரே காதலர்","நீடுவர் கொல்என நினையும்என் நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says her lover has crossed a forest whose road so frightens travellers that even the panchuram call of the girls plucking vengai flowers alarms them, and her heart keeps asking whether he will stay away long.","n":"The fear is put first in the travellers, not in her: a harmless sound from the hill, the flower-pluckers' cry, is enough to startle anyone on that road, so the danger he faces is measured by other people's nerves before she turns to her own worry about delay (நீடுவர் கொல்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 311","lb":"AI311","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["பஞ்சுரம்","பஞ்சுரம்",1,"yaazh","scene","paalai melody"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:32"},"ai:312":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":310,"t":["அறஞ்சா லியரோ அறஞ்சா லியரே","வறனுண் டாயினும் அறஞ்சா லியரோ","வாள்வனப் புற்ற அருவிக்","கோள்வல் என்னையை மறைத்த குன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The woman blesses, again and again, the hill with falls bright as a sword that has hidden her man, strong in the hunt, from her sight, praying it stay full of virtue even if drought comes upon it.","n":"She blesses rather than curses the hill that robbed her of the sight of him: the wish that it keep its virtue (and its water) even in drought is a wish for his safe passage over it. If என்னை is read as 'my father', the speaker would instead be the girl in flight looking back at the hill that hides her home, and the blessing would be for the cover it gives.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 312","lb":"AI312","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவிக்","அருவி",3,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:32"},"ai:313":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":311,"t":["தெறுவது அம்ம நும்மகள் விருப்பே","உறுதுயர் அவலமொடு உயிர்செலச் சாஅய்ப்","பாழ்படு நெஞ்சம் படர் அடக் கலங்க","நாடுஇடை விலங்கிய வைப்பின்","காடுஇறந் தனள்நம் காதலோளே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells the mother that her daughter's desire is a scorching thing: leaving them wasted with grief, life ebbing and hearts ruined, their beloved girl has crossed the forest of a tract that lies between lands.","n":"தெறுவது, 'that which burns', is said of the girl's love itself, not of the man or the road: the household's grief is laid at the door of her desire, the force that made the forest crossable for her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","idumbai","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 313","lb":"AI313","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:32"},"ai:314":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":312,"t":["அவிர்தொடி கொட்பக் கழுதுபுகவு அயரக்","கர்ங்கண் காக்கையொடு கழுகுவிசும்பு அகலச்","சிறுகண் யானை ஆள்வீழ்துத் திரிதரும்","நீளிடை அருஞ்சுரம் என்பநம்","தோளிடை முனிநர் சென்ற ஆறே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says they report that the road taken by the man who tired of lying on her shoulders is a long hard waste where ghouls feast with whirling bright bangles, dark-eyed crows and vultures fill the sky, and the small-eyed elephant roams after killing men.","n":"The scene is a battlefield's: ghouls, crows and vultures over men the elephant has killed. தோளிடை முனிநர், 'those who grew weary between our shoulders', turns his going into a rejection of her embrace, so the road of corpses is what he preferred to it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 314","lb":"AI314","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["கழுதுபுகவு","கழுது",1,"pira","scene","ghoul"],["காக்கையொடு","காக்கை",2,"pul","scene","crow"],["கழுகுவிசும்பு","கழுகு",2,"pul","scene","vulture"],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:32"},"ai:315":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":313,"t":["பாயல் கொண்ட பனிமலர் நெடுங்கண்","பூசல் கேளார் சேயர் என்ப","விழைநெகிழ் செல்லல் உறீஇக்","கழைமுதிர் சோலைக் காடுஇறந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says they report that he is far away and does not hear the outcry of her long eyes, cool as dewy flowers, that have taken to the bed, he who crossed the forest of groves where bamboo grows old, leaving her in a sorrow that undoes what she cherished.","n":"பூசல், an outcry or public clamour, is given to the eyes, so the weeping is imagined as a shout he is too far to hear: her grief is loud and the distance makes it silent.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kan","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 315","lb":"AI315","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கழைமுதிர்","கழை",4,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["சோலைக்","சோலை",4,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:32"},"ai:316":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":314,"t":["பொன்செய் பாண்டில் பொலங்கலம் நந்தத்","தேரகல் அல்குல் அவ்வரி வாட","இறந்தோர் மன்ற தாமே பிறங்கு மலைப்","புல்லரை ஓமை நீடிய","புலிவழங்கு அதர கானத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that, letting her golden ornaments and gold-wrought discs fall away and the fine lines of her chariot-broad waist fade, he has surely gone into the forest on the shining mountains where the thin-barked omai stands tall and tigers walk the paths.","n":"The two halves are set as cause and place: the loss of her gold and the fading of her waist-lines happen here, the tiger paths and omai trees are there, and மன்ற ('surely') makes her body the proof that he has gone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 316","lb":"AI316","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஓமை","ஓமை",4,"maram","scene","omai tree"],["புலிவழங்கு","புலி",5,"maa","scene","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:32"},"ai:317":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":315,"t":["சூழ்கம் வம்மோ தோழி பாழ்பட்டுப்","பைதற வெந்த பாலை வெங்காட்டு","அருஞ்சுரம் இறந்தோர் தேஎத்துச்","சென்ற நெஞ்சம் நீடிய பொருளே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend to come and think with her why her heart, which went to the country of the man who crossed the hard track of the hot paalai forest, burnt bare of all green, has stayed there so long.","n":"The man's delay is restated as the heart's: it is her heart that has gone to his land and lingers (சென்ற நெஞ்சம் நீடிய), so she asks the friend to deliberate about her own absent heart, the one part of her that did make the crossing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 317","lb":"AI317","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:32"},"ai:318":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":316,"t":["ஆய்நலம் பசப்ப அரும்படர் நலிய","வேய்மருள் பணைத்தோள் வில்லிலை நெகிழ","நசைநனி கொன்றோர் மன்ற இசைநிமிர்ந்து","ஓடெரி நடந்த வைப்பின்","கோடுயர் பிறங்கல் மலை இறந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that the men who crossed the high-peaked mountains of a tract where roaring fire ran have surely killed her longing outright, leaving her chosen beauty pale, hard distress tormenting her, and the bangles slipping from her broad shoulders like bamboo.","n":"நசைநனி கொன்றோர், 'those who utterly killed my longing', names the man as a killer: what dies is not her love but her hope, and the forest fire of line 4 runs through the land he chose over her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 318","lb":"AI318","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வேய்மருள்","வேய்",2,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["ஓடெரி","எரி",4,"pira","scene","forest fire"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:32"},"ai:319":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":317,"t":["கண்பொர விளங்கிய கதிர்தெறு வைப்பின்","மண்புரை பெருகிய மரம்முளி கானம்","இறந்தன ரோநம் காதலர்","மறந்தன ரோதில் மறவா நம்மே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks whether their lover has crossed the forest of withered trees and cracked earth in a tract scorched by rays that hurt the eyes, and whether he has forgotten them, who do not forget.","n":"The two questions run in parallel (இறந்தனரோ, மறந்தனரோ): crossing the forest and forgetting her are offered as the same act, and மறவா நம்மே sets her constancy against his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 319","lb":"AI319","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["மரம்முளி","மரம்",2,"maram","absent","trees"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:32"},"ai:320":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":318,"t":["முள்ளரை இலவத்து ஒள் இணர் வான்பூ","முழங்குஅழல் அசைவளி எடுப்ப வானத்து","உருமுப்படு கனலின் இருநிலத்து உறைக்கும்","கவலை அருஞ்சுரம் போயினர்","தவலில் அருநோய் தலைதந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says the man who brought her an unending hard sickness has gone by the forked hard track where the wind, stirred by roaring heat, lifts the bright clustered flowers of the thorny-trunked silk-cotton and scatters them on the earth like embers struck from the sky by thunder.","n":"The flowers do not simply glow; they fall like thunder-struck embers, so the beauty of the path is turned into a rain of fire, matching the sickness without end (தவலில் அருநோய்) he has left her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 320","lb":"AI320","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["இலவத்து","இலவம்",1,"maram","simile","silk-cotton tree"],["அசைவளி","வளி",2,"pira","scene","wind"],["உருமுப்படு","உருமு",3,"pira","simile","thunderbolt"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:32"},"ai:321":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":319,"t":["உலறுதலைப் பருந்தின் உளிவாய்ப் பேடை","அலறுதலை ஓமை அம்கவட் டேறிப்","புலம்புகொள விளிக்கும் நிலம்காய் கானத்து","மொழிபெயர் பன்மலை இறப்பினும்","ஒழிதல் செல்லாது ஒண்டொடி குணனே."],"p":"","r":"Crossing a burning forest where the chisel-beaked hen of the dry-headed kite climbs the fork of the omai tree and calls out in loneliness, the man says that though he passes many hills where the language changes, the good nature of the girl with bright bangles does not leave him.","n":"The distance is measured in languages, not miles (மொழிபெயர்): he has left the land where her speech is spoken, and still what stays with him is her nature (குணனே), not her face.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 321","lb":"AI321","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பேடை","பேடை",1,"pul","iraicchi","hen kite"],["ஓமை","ஓமை",2,"maram","scene","omai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பேடை","confirmed","1/1","the lonely call on his road is the call of the woman he has left behind, which is why her nature does not leave him however far he goes"]],"ch":"ai:33"},"ai:322":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":320,"t":["நெடுங்கழை முளிய வேனில் நீடிக்","கடுங்கதிர் ஞாயிறு கல்பகத் தெறுதலின்","வெய்ய வாயினை முன்னே இனியே","ஒண்ணுதல் அரிவையை யுள்ளுதொறும்","தண்ணிய வாயின சுரத்திடை யாறே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the road through the waste that before, when summer lingered, the tall bamboo withered and the fierce sun scorched the rock, it was hot; now, each time he thinks of the girl with the bright forehead, the path has become cool.","n":"The weather has not changed: முன்னே and இனியே divide the same road by his thought, not by the season, so memory of her is made the one thing stronger than the paalai sun.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pirivu","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 322","lb":"AI322","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கழை","கழை",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:33"},"ai:323":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":321,"t":["வள்ளெயிற்றுச் செந்நாய் வயவுறு பிணவிற்குக்","கள்ளியங் கடத்திடைக் கேழல் பார்க்கும்","வெஞ்சுரக் கவலை நீந்தி","வந்த நெஞ்சம் நீ நயந்தோள் பண்பே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart that what came with him across the forking roads of the hot waste, where a strong-toothed red dog watches for a boar among the cactus for his craving pregnant mate, is the nature of the girl it desired.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","nenju","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 323","lb":"AI323","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["செந்நாய்","செந்நாய்",1,"maa","iraicchi","red dog"],["கள்ளியங்","கள்ளி",2,"maram","scene","cactus"],["கேழல்","கேழல்",2,"maa","scene","boar"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","செந்நாய்","confirmed","1/1","the male providing for his mate is what the man has gone out to do, and seeing it he finds her nature, not the errand, has travelled with him"]],"ch":"ai:33"},"ai:324":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":322,"t":["எரிகவர்ந் துண்ட என்றூழ் நீளிடைச்","சிறிதுகண் படுப்பினும் காண்குவென் மன்ற","நள்ளென் கங்குல் நளிமனை நெடுநகர்","வேங்கை வென்ற சுணங்கின்","தம்பாய் கூந்தல் மாஅ யோளே."],"p":"","r":"On the long road where the summer heat has eaten everything like fire, the man says that if he closes his eyes even a little he will surely see, in the great house in the dead of night, the dark girl with spreading hair whose beauty spots outdo the vengai blossom.","n":"The dream is placed exactly: not her in general but her in the house at midnight (நள்ளென் கங்குல் நளிமனை), so the man's longing is for the home life the road has interrupted. The certainty (காண்குவென் மன்ற) is his; whether the dream holds is not asked.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","ninaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 324","lb":"AI324","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",4,"maram","scene","vengai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:33"},"ai:325":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":323,"t":["வேனில் அரையத்து இலையொலி வெரீஇப்","போகில் புகாவுண்ணாது பிறிதுபுலம் படரும்","வெம்பலை அருஞ்சுரம் நலியாது","எம்வெம் காதலி பண்புதுணைப் பெற்றே."],"p":"","r":"The man says the hard waste of scorching heat, where a bird, frightened by the rattle of leaves on the summer peepal, does not go in to feed and flies off to another land, does not harm him, for he has the nature of his beloved as his companion.","n":"The bird gives up and goes elsewhere because the place frightens it; the man, who has also gone elsewhere, says he is not harmed because he does not travel alone. A reader may hear the bird as the man who left; it does nothing a lover should do here, so it stays scene. Line 2's புகாவுண்ணாது is read here as not going in to eat.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panpu","thunai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 325","lb":"AI325","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அரையத்து","அரை",1,"maram","scene","peepal tree"],["போகில்","போகில்",2,"pul","scene","bird"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:33"},"ai:326":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":324,"t":["அழலவிர் நனந்தலை நிழலிடம் பெறாது","மடமான் அம் பிணை மறியொடு திரங்க","நீர்மருங்கு அறுத்த நிரம்பா இயவின்","இன்னா மன்ற சுரமே","இனிய மறையான் ஒழிந்தோள் பண்பே."],"p":"","r":"In the broad land where the blaze shimmers, where a young doe with her fawn withers for want of any shade and the path never fills because its water has been cut off, the man says the waste is truly cruel, yet sweet is the nature of the girl he left behind.","n":"The poem is built on இன்னா against இனிய: the waste is harsh, her nature is sweet. The doe withering with her fawn could be read as the woman at home, but she only suffers and does nothing a person of the situation does, so it stays scene. மறையான் in line 5 is hard: perhaps that she stayed behind in secret or out of sight.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panpu","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 326","lb":"AI326","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மடமான்","மட மான்",2,"maa","scene","deer"],["மறியொடு","மறி",2,"maa","scene","fawn"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:33"},"ai:327":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":325,"t":["பொறிவரித் தடக்கை வேதல் அஞ்சிச்","சிறுகண் யானை நிலந்தொடல் செல்லா","வெயின்முளி சோலைய வேய்உயர் சுரனே","அன்ன ஆர்இடை யானும்","தண்மை செய்தஇத் தகையோள் பண்பே."],"p":"","r":"On the high-bamboo waste where the groves are withered by the sun and a small-eyed elephant, afraid its spotted trunk will burn, will not touch the ground, the man says that even in such a hard place the nature of this fine girl has made things cool.","n":"The elephant, the largest creature, holds its trunk off the ground for fear of burning; the coolness the man claims is measured against that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 327","lb":"AI327","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","scene","elephant"],["வேய்உயர்","வேய்",3,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:33"},"ai:328":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":326,"t":["நுண்மழை தளித்தென நறுமலர் தாஅய்த்","தண்ணிய வாயினும் வெய்ய மன்ற","மடவரல் இன் துணை ஒழியக்","கடமுதிர் சோலைய காடு இறந்தேற்கே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that though the forest of ripe-musth groves he has crossed is cool, with light rain falling and fragrant flowers strewn, it is truly hot for him, since he has left behind his gentle sweet companion.","n":"The decade's other poems have her memory cool the hot road; this one reverses it, and a road the rain has cooled burns because she is not with him. Paalai here is a state of the man, not of the land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 328","lb":"AI328","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நுண்மழை","நுண் மழை",1,"pira","scene","light rain"],["நறுமலர்","நறு மலர்",1,"maram","scene","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:33"},"ai:329":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":327,"t":["ஆள்வழக்கு அற்ற பாழ்படு நனந்தலை","வெம்முனை அருஞ்சுரம் நீந்தி நம்மொடு","மறுதரு வதுகொல் தானே செறிதொடி","கழிந்துகு நிலைய வாக","ஒழிந்தோள் கொண்டஎன் உரங்கெழு நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"Having crossed the vast ruined land where no one goes and the hard waste of hostile frontiers, the man wonders whether his strong heart will come back with him, the heart taken by the girl who stayed behind with her close-fitting bangles slipping off.","n":"The heart is called strong (உரங்கெழு), the strength that let him leave, yet she has kept it; the slipping bangles are the sign at home of what the road costs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 329","lb":"AI329","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:33"},"ai:330":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":328,"t":["வெந்துக ளாகிய வெயில் கடம் நீந்தி","வந்தனம் ஆயினும் ஒழிகஇனிச் செலவே","அழுத கண்ணள் ஆய்நலம் சிதையக்","கதிர்தெறு வஞ்சுரம் நினைக்கும்","அவிர்கோல் ஆய்தொடி உள்ளத்துப் படரே."],"p":"","r":"Though they have come through the sun-scorched forest gone to hot dust, the man says the journey should stop now, for the girl with bright bangles, her eyes wet with weeping and her beauty ruined, keeps thinking in her heart of the scorching waste he is crossing.","n":"The decade's last poem turns him back. What stops him is not the road but her picture of the road: she suffers his journey in her mind (உள்ளத்துப் படரே), so his going is her pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 330","lb":"AI330","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:33"},"ai:331":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":329,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி அவிழிணர்க்","கருங்கால் மராஅத்து வைகிசினை வான்பூ","அருஞ்சுரம் செல்லுநர் ஒழிந்தோர் உள்ள","இனிய கமழும் வெற்பின்","இன்னாது என்ப அவர் சென்ற ஆறே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend that on the hill where the white blossoms on the low boughs of the black-trunked maraam, its clusters opening, smell so sweet that those crossing the hard waste think of the ones they left, people still call the road her man took a painful one.","n":"The poem sets sweet against painful in adjacent lines (இனிய கமழும், இன்னாது): the scent on the road exists to turn travellers' thoughts homeward, so if even that road is called painful, the pain is his choice to keep going, not the land's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 331","lb":"AI331","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மராஅத்து","மரா",2,"maram","scene","kadamba tree"],["வான்பூ","வான் பூ",2,"maram","scene","white flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:34"},"ai:332":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":330,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி என்னதூஉம்","அறன் இல மன்ற தாமே விறன்மிசைக்","குன்றுகெழு கானத்த பண்பின் மாக்கணம்","கொடிதே காதலிப் பிரிதல்","செல்லல் ஐய என்னா தவ்வே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend that the herds of beasts of the forest round the high hills are surely without any virtue, since they did not say to him, 'parting from a beloved is cruel; do not go, sir.'","n":"She calls the beasts lawless (அறன் இல) for failing to state a rule of right, that leaving a beloved is cruel: the reproach that belongs to the man is shifted onto the animals he passed. The beasts are the subject of the poem, not a side image, so the deletion test for iraicchi fails.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 332","lb":"AI332","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மாக்கணம்","மா கணம்",3,"maa","scene","herd of beasts"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:34"},"ai:333":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":331,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி யாவதும்","வல்லா கொல்லோ தாமே அவண","கல்லுடை நன்னாட்டுப் புள்ளினப் பெர்ந்தோடு","யாஅம் துணைபுணர்ந்து உறைதும்","யாங்குப் பிரிந்து உறைதி என்னா தவ்வே."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks her friend whether the great flocks of birds in that good stony land are good for nothing at all, since they did not say to him, 'we live joined with our mates; how will you live apart?'","n":"The birds' imagined speech is the argument she could not make: their own paired life is the proof, so she wishes the creatures had used their example where her words failed. As in 332 they are the poem's subject, so this is not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 333","lb":"AI333","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["புள்ளினப்","புள் இனம்",3,"pul","scene","flock of birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:34"},"ai:334":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":332,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி சிறியிலை","நெல்லி நீடிய கல்வாய் கடத்திடைப்","பேதை நெஞ்சம் பின்செலச் சென்றோர்","கல்லினும் வலியர் மன்ற","பல்லிதழ் உண்கண் அழப்பிர்ந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend that the man who went through the stony waste where small-leaved nelli grows tall, with her foolish heart following behind him, and who left while her many-petalled kohl-lined eyes wept, is surely harder than stone.","n":"The stone comes back twice: he crosses a land of stones (கல்வாய்) and is harder than stone (கல்லினும் வலியர்), so the country he chose has become his character. Her heart went with him (பின்செல), so what is left behind is only the weeping eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 334","lb":"AI334","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",2,"maram","scene","gooseberry tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:34"},"ai:335":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":333,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நம்வயின்","நெய்தோ ரன்ன செவிய எருவை","கற்புடை மருங்கில் கடுமுடை பார்க்கும்","கடுநனி கடிய என்ப","நீடி இவண் வருநர் சென்ற ஆறே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend that the roads taken by those who will be long in coming back to them here are said to be very cruel, where the vulture with ears red as blood watches for stinking carrion on the stony slopes.","n":"நீடி இவண் வருநர், 'those who will come here after delay', admits he will return while fixing on the delay; the vulture waiting for flesh is her fear of what the road does to travellers, a mood, not an act of the love situation, so it stays scene and simile.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 335","lb":"AI335","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["எருவை","எருவை",2,"pul","simile","vulture"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:34"},"ai:336":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":334,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நம்வயின்","பிரியலர் போலப் புணர்ந்தோர் மன்ற","நின்றதில் பொருட்பிணி முற்றிய","என்றூழ் நீடிய சுரன்இறந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend that those who crossed the waste where summer heat lingers, seized by the sickness for wealth that never stays, surely embraced them as if they would never part.","n":"The wealth he chases is called நின்றது இல், 'what does not stay', the very fault she finds in him: the embrace that seemed to promise permanence and the wealth that cannot keep it are set side by side, and he traded the first for the second.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","nilaiyaamai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 336","lb":"AI336","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:34"},"ai:337":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":335,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நம் வயின்","மெய்யுற விரும்பிய கைகவர் முயக்கினும்","இனிய மன்ற தாமே","பனியிரும் குன்றம் சென்றோர்க்குப் பொருளே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend that for the man who went to the cold dark hills, wealth is surely sweeter even than the embrace of clasping hands, desired body against body, with her.","n":"She does not say he loves her less; she says he has weighed two sweetnesses and found wealth the sweeter, so the grief is about a measured choice. The last word of the poem is பொருளே, held back to the end like a verdict.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 337","lb":"AI337","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:34"},"ai:338":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":336,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி சாரல்","இலையில மலர்ந்த ஓங்குநிலை இலவம்","மலையுறு தீயில் சுரமுதல் தோன்றும்","பிரிவருங் காலையும் பிரிதல்","அரிதுவல் லுநர்நம் காத லோரே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend that on the slope the tall silk-cotton, blooming leafless, shows at the edge of the waste as a fire on the mountain, and even in this season when parting is hard, their lover is skilled at the hard thing of parting.","n":"The play on அரு is the point: in a season when parting is hard (பிரிவருங் காலை) he is able at what is hard (அரிது வல்லுநர்), so his strength is named in the very word for her pain. The blossom like a hill fire marks the spring that should keep lovers together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaalam","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 338","lb":"AI338","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["இலவம்","இலவம்",2,"maram","scene","silk-cotton tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:34"},"ai:339":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":337,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி சிறியிலைக்","குறுஞ்சினை வேம்பின் நறும்பழம் உணீஇய","வாவல் உகக்கும் மாலையும்","இன்றுகொல் காதலவர் சென்ற நாடே."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks her friend whether, in the land her lover went to, there is no evening, the hour when bats fly up to eat the fragrant fruit of the short-boughed, small-leaved neem.","n":"The question assumes that evening, if it reached him, would bring him home, since it is the hour of longing; she can only explain his staying by supposing his land has no dusk. The bats going to the fruit at evening could be read as creatures returning to what they crave, but they do nothing the lovers do, so they stay scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 339","lb":"AI339","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",2,"maram","scene","neem tree"],["வாவல்","வாவல்",3,"pira","scene","bat"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:34"},"ai:340":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":338,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி காதலர்","உள்ளார் கொல்நாம் மருள்உற் றனம்கொல்","விட்டுச் சென்றனர் நம்மே","தட்டைத் சென்றனர் நம்மே","தட்டைத் தீயின் ஊரலர் எழவே."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks her friend whether her lover does not think of them, or whether she has fallen into delusion, for he went away and left them while the town's gossip rose like fire in dry bamboo.","n":"Gossip in paalai is unusual: here it does not expose a secret love but mocks a wife who was left, and it spreads with the crackle and speed of a bamboo fire. Her two questions (does he not think of us, or am I deluded) leave open whether the fault is his or hers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 340","lb":"AI340","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தட்டைத்","தட்டை",5,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:34"},"ai:341":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":339,"t":["அவரோ வாரார் தான்வந் தன்றே","குயிற்பெடை இன்குரல் அகவ","அயிர்க்கேழ் நுண்ணறல் நுடங்கும் பொழுதே."],"p":"","r":"She says that he has not come, but the time has come, the time when the female koel calls in her sweet voice and the fine sand-ripples, the colour of grit, sway in the thinning stream.","n":"The refrain sets two subjects against each other: அவர் (he) does not come, தான் (it, the time) has come of itself. The poet chose the female koel, the calling mate, but the poem gives her no answering male, so she is left as a sound of the season rather than an act (scene, not iraicchi); the shrunken stream over fine sand is the first sign of the hot months of paalai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 341","lb":"AI341","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குயிற்பெடை","குயில் பெடை",2,"pul","scene","female koel"],["நுண்ணறல்","அறல்",3,"pira","scene","sand-ripple"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:35"},"ai:342":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":340,"t":["அவரோ வாரார் தான்வந் தன்றே","சுரும்புகளித்து ஆலும் இருஞ்சினைக்","கருங்கால் நுணவம் கமழும் பொழுதே."],"p":"","r":"She says that he has not come, but the time has come when the black-stemmed nunavam, its big branches loud with drunken bees, gives out its scent.","n":"The bees are drunk with the season (களித்து ஆலும) while she is sober with waiting; the revel is only a mood, with no act of a lover in it, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 342","lb":"AI342","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",2,"pira","scene","bee"],["நுணவம்","நுணவம்",3,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:35"},"ai:343":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":341,"t":["அவரோ வாரார் தான்வந் தன்றே","திணிநிலைக் கோங்கம் பயந்த","அணிமிகு கொழுமுகை உடையும் பொழுதே."],"p":"","r":"She says that he has not come, but the time has come when the beautiful fat buds borne by the firm-standing kongu tree burst open.","n":"உடையும் is the bursting of the bud, a ripeness that cannot be held back: the tree's time, like the season, runs on whether or not he keeps his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 343","lb":"AI343","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கோங்கம்","கோங்கு",2,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:35"},"ai:344":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":342,"t":["அவரோ வாரார் தான்வந் தன்றே","எழில்தகை இஅள்முலை பொலியப்","பொரிப்பூம் புன்கின் முறிதிமிர் பொழுதே."],"p":"","r":"She says that he has not come, but the time has come when women smear the tender leaves of the punku, its flowers like puffed rice, on their young breasts to make their beauty shine.","n":"The season's custom is an adornment for a lover's eyes: the young leaves are worn so that the breasts may be seen, and the one who should see them is away, so the beauty the season makes is wasted on her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 344","lb":"AI344","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["புன்கின்","புன்கு",3,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:35"},"ai:345":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":343,"t":["அவரோ வாரார் தான்வந் தன்றே","வலஞ்சுரி மராஅம் வேய்ந்துநம்","மணங்கமழ் தண்பொழில் மலரும் பொழுதே."],"p":"","r":"She says that he has not come, but the time has come when our fragrant cool grove, roofed over with the right-whorled kadamba, breaks into flower.","n":"நம் makes the grove theirs: the place that blooms is the one they shared, so the spring is not only a date he has missed but a meeting place that keeps its appointment without him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 345","lb":"AI345","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மராஅம்","மராம்",2,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["தண்பொழில்","பொழில்",3,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:35"},"ai:346":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":344,"t":["அவரோ வாரார் தான்வந் தன்றே","அஞ்சினைப் பாதிரி அலர்ந்தெனச்","செங்கண இருங்குயில் அறையும் பொழுதே."],"p":"","r":"She says that he has not come, but the time has come when, the paathiri on its lovely branches having blossomed, the dark red-eyed koel cries aloud.","n":"அறையும் is to cry out as a drum proclaims: the koel announces the season the paathiri has opened, so the bird is a herald of the date, and its announcement is public while her waiting is private.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 346","lb":"AI346","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பாதிரி","பாதிரி",2,"maram","scene",""],["இருங்குயில்","குயில்",3,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:35"},"ai:347":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":345,"t":["அவரோ வாரார் தான்வந் தன்றே","எழில்தகை இளமுலை பொலியப்","பொரிப்பூம் புன்கின் முறிதிமிர் பொழுதே."],"p":"","r":"She says that he has not come, but the time has come when women smear the tender leaves of the punku, its flowers like puffed rice, on their young breasts to make their beauty shine.","n":"The season's custom is an adornment for a lover's eyes: the young leaves are worn so that the breasts may be seen, and the one who should see them is away, so the beauty the season makes is wasted on her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 347","lb":"AI347","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["புன்கின்","புன்கு",3,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:35"},"ai:348":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":346,"t":["அவரோ வாரார் தான்வந் தன்றே","வலஞ்சுரி மராஅம் வேய்ந்துநம்","மணங்கமழ் தண்பொழில் மலரும் பொழுதே."],"p":"","r":"She says that he has not come, but the time has come when our fragrant cool grove, roofed over with the right-whorled kadamba, breaks into flower.","n":"நம் makes the grove theirs: the place that blooms is the one they shared, so the spring is not only a date he has missed but a meeting place that keeps its appointment without him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 348","lb":"AI348","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மராஅம்","மராம்",2,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["தண்பொழில்","பொழில்",3,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:35"},"ai:349":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":347,"t":["அவரோ வாரார் தான்வந் தன்றே","பொரிகால் மாஞ்சினை புதைய","எரிகால் இளந்தளிர் ஈனும் பொழுதே."],"p":"","r":"She says that he has not come, but the time has come when the mango with its cracked bark puts out young shoots like flame, so many that its branches are buried in them.","n":"பொரிகால் and எரிகால் rhyme across the two lines: the old trunk looks scorched and the new shoots look like fire, so the tree that seemed burnt out renews itself, which is what the parted woman cannot do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 349","lb":"AI349","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மாஞ்சினை","மா",2,"maram","scene","mango"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:35"},"ai:350":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":348,"t":["அவரோ வாரார் தான்வந் தன்றே","வேம்பின் ஒண்பூ உறப்பத்","தேம்படு கிளவியவர்த் தெளிக்கும் பொழுதே."],"p":"","r":"She says that he has not come, but the time has come, the time of the neem's bright flowers crowding the branches, the very time of which he assured us with honey-sweet words.","n":"The last poem names what the refrain has meant all along: the time that has come is the time his own sweet words fixed (தெளிக்கும், to make one sure), so the decade ends on a broken assurance, not on a season. The neem is the bitter tree, and its bright flower stands beside the honeyed words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","vaaimai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 350","lb":"AI350","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",2,"maram","scene","neem"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:35"},"ai:351":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":349,"t":["அத்த பலவின் வெயில்தின் சிறுகாய்","அருஞ்சுரம் செல்வோர் அருந்தினர் கழியும்","காடுபின் ஒழிய வந்தனர் தீர்கினிப்","பல் இதழ் உண்கண் மடந்தைநின்","நல்லெழில் அல்குல் வாடிய நிலையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that the men have come back, leaving behind them the wasteland where travellers on the hard road eat the small sun-scorched fruit of the desert jackfruit and pass on, so the withered state of her lovely waist may now be cured.","n":"The road is measured by its food: the only meal it offers is a fruit the sun has already eaten (வெயில்தின்), so the men's return is set against a land that half-consumes whatever is in it, as separation had wasted her body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nalam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 351","lb":"AI351","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பலவின்","பலா",1,"maram","scene","jackfruit tree"],["சிறுகாய்","காய்",1,"unavu","gift_food","unripe fruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:36"},"ai:352":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":350,"t":["விழுத்தொடை மறவர் வில்லிடத் தொலைந்தோர்","எழுத்துடை நடுகல் அன்ன விழுப்பிணர்ப்","பெருங்கை யானை இருஞ்சினம் உறைக்கும்","வெஞ்சுரம் அரிய என்னார்","வந்தனர் தோழிநம் காத லோரே."],"p":"","r":"Our lover has come back, says the speaker to her friend, without calling hard the burning waste where the great-trunked elephant, its hide ridged like the lettered hero-stone of men who fell to the arrows of strong-bowed warriors, vents its dark rage.","n":"The hero-stone, the waste's sign of men killed on the road, enters only as the likeness of a living elephant's hide, so the danger is doubled while the poem's point stays that the man did not count it (அரிய என்னார). Lines 1 and 2 repeat a formula of Akananuru 53.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nadukal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 352","lb":"AI352","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மறவர்","மறவர்",1,"seythi","scene","warriors"],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:36"},"ai:353":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":351,"t":["எரிக்கொடி கவைஇய செவ்வரை போலச்","சுடர்ப்பூண் விளங்கும் எந்தெழில் அகலம்","நீ இனிது முயங்க வந்தனர்","மாயிருஞ் சோலை மலையிறந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The men who crossed the hills of great dark groves have come back, so that she may sweetly embrace that beautiful raised chest on which bright ornaments shine like a red hill encircled by a creeper of flame.","n":"The image gives the returning man's chest the look of a hill, the very thing he crossed: the land he left her for comes back to her as the body she embraces. எரிக்கொடி may be a red-flowered creeper or a line of fire; either way the basis is colour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","pirivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 353","lb":"AI353","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["எரிக்கொடி","எரிக்கொடி",1,"maram","simile","flame creeper"],["சோலை","சோலை",4,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:36"},"ai:354":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":352,"t":["ஈர்ம் பிணவு புணர்ந்த செந்நாய் ஏற்றை","மறியுடை மான்பிணை கொள்ளாது கழியும்","அரிய சுரன்வந் தனரே","தெரியிழை அரிவைநின் பண்புதர விரைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that he has crossed back over the hard road where the red wild-dog, having mated with its tender female, passes by the doe with her fawn without seizing her, hurrying because her nature drew him.","n":"The predator of the waste, which in other paalai poems kills on the road, here holds back: the beasts the man passed on the way home already show the restraint and tenderness he is bringing back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 354","lb":"AI354","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["செந்நாய்","செந்நாய்",1,"maa","iraicchi","wild dog"],["மான்பிணை","மான் பிணை",2,"maa","scene","doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","செந்நாய்","confirmed","1/1","a beast at one with its mate spares another's mother and young, as the man, drawn by his wife, left off his own seizing of wealth and hurried home"]],"ch":"ai:36"},"ai:355":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":353,"t":["திருந்திழை அரிவை நின்னலம் உள்ளி","அருஞ்செயல் பொருட்பிணி பெருந்திரு உறுகெனச்","சொல்லாது பெயர்தந் தேனே பல்பொறிச்","சிறுகண் யானை திரிதரும்","நெறிவிலங்கு அதர கானத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his wife that, thinking of her beauty, he turned back without so much as saying 'may the hard work of the wealth-quest win great fortune', from the forest whose tracks cross the path and where small-eyed elephants with spotted faces roam.","n":"He abandons the wealth-quest without even the courtesy of a blessing on it: the omitted farewell (சொல்லாது) is the measure of how completely her beauty outweighed the project.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nalam","ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 355","lb":"AI355","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:36"},"ai:356":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":354,"t":["உள்ளுதற்கு இனிய மன்ற செல்வர்","யானை பிணித்த பொன்புனை கயிற்றின்","ஒள்ளெரி மேய்ந்த சுரத்திடை","உள்ளம் வாங்கத் தந்தநின் குணனே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his wife that her virtues are truly sweet to think of, for in the waste that bright fire has grazed over they drew his heart back like the gold-worked rope with which rich men tie their elephant.","n":"The comparison is made by the case ending of கயிற்றின், not by அன்ன or போல. The rope is costly as well as strong: what pulls him home is fine and precious, and he is the great beast that it holds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","panpu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 356","lb":"AI356","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:36"},"ai:357":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":355,"t":["குரவம் மலர மரவம் பூப்பச்","சுரன்அணி கொண்ட கானம் காணூஉ","அழுங்குக செய்பொருள் செலவுஎன விரும்பிநின்","அம்கலிழ் மாமை கவின","வந்தனர் தோழிநம் காத லோரே."],"p":"","r":"Our lover has come back, the friend tells the woman: seeing the forest dressed for the season, kuravam opening and maravam in flower, he said 'let the journey for wealth be given up' and came, longing for her, so that her lovely dark glowing skin may regain its beauty.","n":"The flowering that usually marks a promised season of return here works on the man himself: the forest he was crossing put on its adornment (அணி) and reminded him of his own wife's beauty, so he turned back mid-journey.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","selvam","pirivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 357","lb":"AI357","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["குரவம்","குரவம்",1,"maram","scene","kuravam"],["மரவம்","மரவம்",1,"maram","scene","maravam"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:36"},"ai:358":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":356,"t":["கோடுயர் பன்மலை இறந்தனர் ஆயினும்","நீடவிடுமோ மற்றே நீடுநினைந்து","துடைத்தொறும் துடைத்தொறும் கலங்கி","உடிஅத்தெழு வெள்ள மாகிய கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"Though he has crossed many high-peaked hills, will her eyes let him stay away long, eyes that, thinking long of him, cloud over each time they are wiped and have become a flood that bursts out?","n":"The subject of நீடவிடுமோ is the eyes: the tears are imagined as a force that will not allow his delay, so grief itself becomes the pledge of his return.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 358","lb":"AI358","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:36"},"ai:359":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":357,"t":["அரும்பொருள் வேட்கைய மாகிநின் துறந்து","பெருங்கல் அதரிடைப் பிரிந்த காலைத்","தவநனி நெடிய வாயின இனியே","அணியிழை உள்ளியாம் வருதலின்","அணிய வாயின சுரத்திடை யாறே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his wife that when he left her, wanting rare wealth, and parted on the rocky tracks, the ways through the waste were very long, but now, as he comes thinking of her, they have become near.","n":"The same road has two lengths: going out for wealth it stretched, coming back for her it shrank, so distance is made a property of desire, not of the land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","ninaivu","pirivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 359","lb":"AI359","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:36"},"ai:360":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":358,"t":["எரிகவர்ந் துண்ட என்றூழ் நீளிடை","அரிய வாயினும் எளிய அன்றே","அவவூறு நெஞ்சம் கவவு நனி விரும்பிக்","கடுமான் திண்தேர் கடைஇ","நெடுமான் நோக்கிநின் உள்ளி யாம் வரவே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his doe-eyed wife that the long way through the summer heat, whose growth fire has seized and eaten, though hard, was easy when he drove his swift-horsed sturdy chariot, his aching heart longing for her embrace, thinking of her as he came.","n":"மான் occurs twice in two lines with two senses, the swift horse he drives and the deer whose look is hers: the creature that carries him and the creature she resembles meet in the last two lines.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 360","lb":"AI360","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கடுமான்","மா",4,"maa","scene","horse"],["நெடுமான்","மா",5,"maa","scene","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:36"},"ai:361":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":359,"t":["உயர்கரைக் கான்யாற்று அவிர்மணல் அகன்துறை","வேனிற் பாதிரி விரிமலர் குவைஇத்","தொடலை தை இய மடவரல் மகளே","கண்ணினும் கதவநின் முலையே","முலையினும் கதவநின் தடமென் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the simple girl who wears a garland strung from summer paathiri flowers heaped on the wide shining sand of a high-banked forest stream that her breasts strike harder than her eyes, and her broad soft shoulders harder than her breasts.","n":"The climb runs against the usual order of the love poems, where the eyes are the weapon: here each part of her is fiercer (கதவ) than the last, so the praise turns into a mock complaint of being attacked, while the paathiri garland shows her at ease in the waste.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 361","lb":"AI361","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["பாதிரி","பாதிரி",2,"maram","ornament","trumpet-flower tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:37"},"ai:362":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":360,"t":["பதுக்கைத் தாய ஒதுக்கருங் கவலைச்","சிறுகண் யானை உறுபகை நினையாது","யாங்கு வந்தனையோ பூந்தார் மார்ப","அருள்புரி நெஞ்சம் உய்த்தர","இருள்பொர நின்ற இரவி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks the man with the flower-garlanded chest how he came in the night, when the dark pressed in, by the forked paths hard to walk and strewn with stone cairns, giving no thought to the deadly hatred of the small-eyed elephant, driven by a heart bent on kindness.","n":"The cairns (பதுக்கை) are the waste's graves of travellers, so the kurinji visit by night is set on a paalai road; the line அருள்புரி நெஞ்சம் turns the reproach into praise, since what drove him across it was kindness, not mere desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","acham","kalaviyal","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 362","lb":"AI362","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["பதுக்கை","பதுக்கை",1,"pira","scene","stone cairns"],["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","scene","elephant"],["பூந்தார்","பூ தார்",3,"maram","ornament","flower garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:37"},"ai:363":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":361,"t":["சிலைவிற் பகழிச் செந்துவ ராடைக்","கொலைவல் எயினர் தங்கைநின் முலைய","சுணங்கென நினைதி நீயே","அணங்கென நினையும்என் அணங்குறு நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the sister of the killing Eyinar, who carry bows with arrows and wear red-dyed cloth, that she takes the pale spots on her breasts for mere freckling, while his stricken heart takes them for a goddess that strikes.","n":"The poem turns on a rhyme, சுணங்கு against அணங்கு: the same mark is a blemish of the skin to her and a dread power to him, and the killing trade of her brothers (கொலைவல் எயினர்) stands behind the girl, so the danger he feels is doubled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kaamam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 363","lb":"AI363","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["எயினர்","எயினர்",2,"seythi","scene","waste-land hunters"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:37"},"ai:364":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":362,"t":["முளமா வல்சி எயினர் தங்கை","இளமா எயிற்றிக்கும் இந்நிலை அறியச்","சொல்லினேன் இரக்கும் அளவை","வெள்வேல் விடலை விரையா தீமே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks the young man of the white spear not to hurry until she has told the young Eyitri, sister of the Eyinar who live on porcupine, how things stand and has pleaded with her.","n":"The -உம் of எயிற்றிக்கும் (to the Eyitri too) suggests the youth has already been told and the girl has not, so the friend stands between his haste and the girl's consent; இளமா may be a young doe or a young mango, either a likeness of the girl.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","pirivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 364","lb":"AI364","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முளமா","முள் மா",1,"maa","gift_food","porcupine"],["எயினர்","எயினர்",1,"seythi","scene","waste-land hunters"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:37"},"ai:365":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":363,"t":["கணமா தொலைச்சித் தன்னையர் தந்த","நிணவூன் வல்சிப் படுபுள் ஒப்பும்","நலமாண் எயிற்றி போலப் பலமிகு","நல்நலம் நயவர உடையை","என்நோற் றனையோ மாஇன் தளிரே."],"p":"","r":"The man asks a tender mango shoot what penance it did to have such desirable beauty, like the lovely-toothed Eyitri who drives off the birds that come at the fat meat her brothers brought home after killing deer from the herd.","n":"The comparison is reversed: the shoot is praised for being like the girl, not the girl for being like the shoot, so the question 'what penance did you do?' measures her beauty as a thing a plant must earn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","nalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 365","lb":"AI365","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["கணமா","கணம் மா",1,"maa","gift_food","deer herd"],["நிணவூன்","நிணம் ஊன்",2,"unavu","gift_food","fat meat"],["படுபுள்","படு புள்",2,"pul","scene","birds"],["மாஇன்","மா",5,"maram","simile","mango"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:37"},"ai:366":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":364,"t":["அன்னாய் வாழிவேண் டன்னை தோழி","பசந்தனள் பெரிதெனச் சிவந்த கண்ணை","கொன்னே கடவுதி யாயின் என்னதூஉம்","அறிய ஆகுமோ மற்றே","முறியிணர்க் கோங்கம் பயந்த மாறே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that if she, with reddened eyes, keeps pressing for no purpose, saying the girl has grown very pale, nothing will come of it: the pallor is because the kongu with its tender-leaved clusters has come into flower.","n":"The answer hides and reveals at once: the flowering kongu is the season the man would have named for his coming, so the friend's seasonal excuse is also a hint to the mother that the pallor has a man behind it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kalaviyal","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 366","lb":"AI366","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோங்கம்","கோங்கு",5,"maram","scene","kongu tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:37"},"ai:367":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":365,"t":["பொரியரைக் கோங்கின் பொன்மருள் பசுவீ","விரியிணர் வேங்கையொடு வேறுபட மிலைச்சி","விரிவுமலர் அணிந்த வேனில் கான்யாற்றுத்","தேரொடு குறுக வந்தோன்","பேரொடு புணர்ந்தன்று அன்னைஇவள் உயிரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that the girl's life is now joined to the name of the man who came near with his chariot by the summer forest stream strewn with spread blossoms, wearing the gold-like fresh flowers of the rough-barked kongu set apart with the open clusters of vengai.","n":"பேரொடு புணர்ந்தன்று is stronger than a story of meeting: her life is united not with the man but with his name, as if already married to it, and that is the friend's whole case to the mother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","uyir","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 367","lb":"AI367","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோங்கின்","கோங்கு",1,"maram","ornament","kongu tree"],["வேங்கையொடு","வேங்கை",2,"maram","ornament","kino tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:37"},"ai:368":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":366,"t":["எரிப்பூ இலவத்து ஊழ்கழி பல் மலர்","பொரிப்பூம் புன்கின் புகர்நிழல் வரிக்கும்","தண்பத வேனில் இன்ப நுகர்ச்சி","எம்மொடு கொண்மோ பெருமநின்","எம்மெல் ஓதி அழிவிலள் எனினே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks the lord to share with them the pleasure of the cool spell of summer, when the many spent blossoms of the fire-flowered silk-cotton stripe the dappled shade of the puffed-flowered pungu, if he wishes her of the soft dark hair not to be ruined.","n":"The dissuasion makes no argument against the journey; it offers a season too sweet to leave, and only the last line, அழிவிலள் எனினே, shows the threat inside the invitation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 368","lb":"AI368","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இலவத்து","இலவம்",1,"maram","scene","silk-cotton tree"],["புன்கின்","புன்கு",2,"maram","scene","pungu tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:37"},"ai:369":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":367,"t":["வளமலர் ததைந்த வண்டுபடு நறும்பொழில்","முளைநிரை முறுவல் ஒருத்தியொடு நெருநல்","குறிநீ செய்தனை என்ப அலரே","குரவ நீள்சினை உறையும்","பருவ மாக்குயில் கௌவையில் பெரிதே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells the man that the gossip, louder than the cry of the great koel of the season that lives on the long kuravam branch, says that yesterday he made a tryst with a woman whose smile is a row of sprouts, in the fragrant grove thick with rich flowers where bees swarm.","n":"கௌவை means both a loud cry and public talk, so the koel's seasonal call and the town's gossip are measured on one scale, and the gossip wins; a tryst only one day old (நெருநல்) is already louder than the season.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 369","lb":"AI369","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வண்டுபடு","வண்டு",1,"pira","scene","bees"],["குரவ","குரவம்",4,"maram","scene","kuravam tree"],["மாக்குயில்","மா குயில்",5,"pul","scene","great koel"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:37"},"ai:370":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":368,"t":["வண்சினைக் கோங்கின் தண்கமழ் படலை","இருஞ்சிறை வண்டின் பெருங்கிளை மொய்ப்ப","நீநயந்து உறையப் பட்டோள்","யாவ ளோஎம் மறையா தீமே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks the man to tell her, and not hide it, who the woman is that he longs for and lives for, while the great swarm of dark-winged bees crowds the cool fragrant garland of rich-branched kongu.","n":"The bee-swarmed garland can be the man's gift or the woman's ornament; either way the bees give away where it has been, and the friend's 'do not hide it from us' presses him to own the love openly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 370","lb":"AI370","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோங்கின்","கோங்கு",1,"maram","ornament","kongu tree"],["வண்டின்","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bees"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:37"},"ai:371":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":369,"t":["மள்ளர் கொட்டின் மஞ்ஞை யாலும்","உயர்நெடும் குன்றம் படுமழை தலைஇச்","சுரநனி இனிய வாகுக தில்ல","அறநெறி இதுவெனத் தெளிந்தஎன்","பிறைநுதற் குறுமகள் போகிய சுரனே."],"p":"","r":"The mother prays that rain may fall on the high hills where peacocks dance to the warriors' drumming, so that the wasteland her crescent-browed girl has gone through may be pleasant, the girl who made up her mind that this was the path of right.","n":"The mother does not blame the girl: she grants that the daughter judged the going to be அறநெறி, the right way, and turns her grief into a wish for rain on the road. The peacocks dancing to a drum are the hill country's festivity imported into a paalai wish.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","aram","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 371","lb":"AI371","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மள்ளர்","மள்ளர்",1,"seythi","scene","warriors"],["கொட்டின்","கொட்டு",1,"parai","scene","drumming"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",1,"pul","scene","peacock"],["படுமழை","மழை",2,"pira","scene","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:38"},"ai:372":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":370,"t":["என்னும் உள்ளினள் கொல்லோ தன்னை","நெஞ்சுணத் தேற்றிய வஞ்சினக் காளையொடு","அழுங்கல் மூதூர் அலரெழச்","செழும்பல் குன்றம் இறந்தஎன் மகளே."],"p":"","r":"The mother asks whether her daughter, who crossed many rich hills with the youth of oaths who won her heart, while gossip rose in the noisy old town, thinks even of her.","n":"என்னும் asks only for the least: not that the girl grieve, but that she remember her mother at all. The youth is named by his வஞ்சினம், the sworn word that persuaded the girl, so the mother places the cause of the going in speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ninaivu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 372","lb":"AI372","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:38"},"ai:373":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":371,"t":["நினைத்தொறும் கலிழும் இடும்பை எய்துக","புலிக்கோட் பிழைத்த கவைக்கோட்டு முதுகலை","மான்பிணை அணைதர ஆண்குரல் விளிக்கும்","வெஞ்சுரம் என்மகள் உய்த்த","வம்பமை வல்வில்விடலை தாயே."],"p":"","r":"The mother wishes on the mother of the strong-bowed youth who led her daughter into the hot wasteland, where an old forked-horned stag that escaped a tiger's grip calls in its male voice for its doe to come to it, a grief that makes her weep each time she thinks of it.","n":"The curse asks the other mother to suffer exactly what the speaker suffers (நினைத்தொறும் கலிழும்), so it is grief demanding a mirror rather than plain hatred. The stag that has just escaped death and at once calls its doe is the danger and the pull of the road in one image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","vekuli","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 373","lb":"AI373","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["vekuli","குடிகோள்"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["புலிக்கோட்","புலி",2,"maa","scene","tiger"],["முதுகலை","கலை",2,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["மான்பிணை","மான் பிணை",3,"maa","iraicchi","doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","முதுகலை","confirmed","1/1","the youth's call drew the daughter into the same danger, so the mother sees on the road the very call that took her girl"]],"ch":"ai:38"},"ai:374":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":372,"t":["பல் ஊழ் நினைப்பினும் நல்லென் றூழ","மீளி முன்பின் காளை காப்ப","முடியகம் புகாக் கூந்தலள்","கடுவனும் அறியாக் காடுஇறந் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The mother, turning it over many times, says that her girl, whose hair is not yet long enough to be gathered into a knot, has crossed, guarded by the youth of fierce strength, a forest that even the male monkey does not know.","n":"Hair too short for the knot (முடியகம் புகாக் கூந்தல்) is the mother's proof that the girl is still a child. Line 1's நல்லென் றூழ is hard to divide: it may be நல் என்று ஊழ (fate called good) or a self-description, so whether the mother consoles herself or despairs is open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 374","lb":"AI374","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கடுவனும்","கடுவன்",4,"maa","scene","male monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:38"},"ai:375":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":373,"t":["இதுவென் பாவை பாவை இதுஎன்","அலமரு நோக்கின் நலம்வரு சுடர்நுதல்","பைங்கிளி எடுத்த பைங்கிளி என்றிவை","காண்தொறும் காண்தொறும் கலங்க","நீங்கின ளோஎன் பூங்க ணோளே."],"p":"","r":"The mother, troubled each time she sees them, says 'this is my doll's doll, this is the green parrot my green parrot raised', and asks whether her flower-eyed girl of the restless glance and bright brow has really gone.","n":"The repetitions are built on two metaphors for the girl: she is herself the பாவை whose doll this is and the பைங்கிளி who reared this parrot, so each object the mother sees names the daughter twice. The doubling of words (பாவை பாவை, பைங்கிளி ... பைங்கிளி, காண்தொறும் காண்தொறும்) enacts the unbroken looking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ninaivu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 375","lb":"AI375","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பைங்கிளி","பைங்கிளி",3,"pul","scene","parrot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:38"},"ai:376":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":374,"t":["நாள்தொறும் கலிழும் என்னினும் இடைநின்று","காடுபடு தீயின் கனலியர் மாதோ","நல்வினை நெடுநகர் கல்லெனக் கலங்கப்","பூப்புரை உண்கண் மடவரல்","போக்கிய புணர்த்த அறனில் பாலே."],"p":"","r":"The mother, though she weeps day after day, wishes that the loveless fate which came between, joined the simple flower-eyed girl to her lover and sent her off, throwing the great house of good deeds into uproar, may burn like a forest fire.","n":"The curse falls not on the youth or the girl but on பால், the destiny that pairs lovers, and calls it அறனில் (without right). Set beside 371, where the girl judged the going அறநெறி, the decade puts the question of right inside the mother's own lament.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","udanpokku","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 376","lb":"AI376","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["vekuli","குடிகோள்"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தீயின்","தீ",2,"pira","simile","fire"],["பூப்புரை","பூ",4,"maram","simile","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:38"},"ai:377":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":375,"t":["நீர்நசைக்கு ஊக்கிய உயவல் யானை","இயம்புணர் தூம்பின் உயிர்க்கும் அத்தம்","சென்றனள் மன்றஎன் மகளே","பந்தும் பாவையும் கழங்கும்எமக்கு ஒழித்தே."],"p":"","r":"The mother says her daughter has surely gone through the wasteland where an elephant, weary and driven by thirst, breathes like a pipe played with the drums, leaving to them her ball, her doll and her playing pebbles.","n":"எமக்கு ஒழித்தே makes the toys a bequest: what the girl leaves to her mother is her childhood, and the elephant's hard breathing measures the road she has gone to instead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 377","lb":"AI377","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","simile","elephant"],["தூம்பின்","தூம்பு",2,"yaazh","simile","pipe"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:38"},"ai:378":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":376,"t":["செல்லிய முயலிப் பாஅய சிறகர்","வாவல் உகக்கும் மாலையாம் புலம்பப்","போகிய அவட்கோ நோவேன் தேமொழித்","துணையிலள் கலிழும் நெஞ்சின்","இணையேர் உண்கண் இவட்கு நோவதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The mother says that in the lonely evening, when the bat spreads its wings and rises to go, she does not grieve for the girl who has gone; she grieves for this one, the friend left without her honey-voiced companion, whose paired kohl eyes weep with her heart.","n":"The turn 'not for her, but for this one' moves the mother's grief from the daughter to the friend who stays behind, so the loss is counted in the companion too. The bat setting out at dusk echoes the girl's going but does nothing a person of the situation does, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","thunai","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 378","lb":"AI378","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வாவல்","வாவல்",2,"pul","scene","bat"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:38"},"ai:379":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":377,"t":["தன்னமர் ஆயமொடு நன்மண நுகர்ச்சியின்","இனிதாங் கொல்லோ தனக்கே பனிவரை","இனக்களிறு வழங்கும் சோலை","வயக்குறு வெள்வேல் அவற்புணர்ந்து செலவே."],"p":"","r":"The mother wonders whether going in union with the man of the polished white spear, through the groves of the cold hills where herds of bull elephants roam, is sweeter to her daughter than enjoying a good wedding among her loving companions.","n":"Unusually the mother imagines the girl's pleasure rather than her pain, and the road is a cool hill grove, not a burnt waste. The herd of elephants (இனக்களிறு) set against the girl's own band (ஆயம்) that she has left is what carries the reproach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","varaivu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 379","lb":"AI379","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இனக்களிறு","இனம் களிறு",3,"maa","iraicchi","elephant herd"],["சோலை","சோலை",3,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","இனக்களிறு","not_confirmed","0/1","even the elephants on her road stay with their herd, while the girl has left her own band of companions and their wedding for one man"]],"ch":"ai:38"},"ai:380":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":378,"t":["அத்தம் நீளிடை அவனொடு போகிய","முத்தேர் வெண்பல் முகிழ்நகை மடவரல்","தாயர் என்னும் பெயரே வல்லாறு","எடுத்தேன் மன்ற யானே","கொடுத்தோர் மன்றஅவள் ஆயத் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The mother says of the simple girl with pearl-white teeth and budding smile who went with him through the long waste: she herself only bore the name of mother, and surely it was the girl's playmates who gave her away.","n":"கொடுத்தோர் is the word for giving a bride: the mother says the giving that should have been hers was done by the girl's companions, who knew and helped. Her self-belittling (தாயர் என்னும் பெயரே) is the grief of one stripped of her office.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","varaivu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 380","lb":"AI380","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["முத்தேர்","முத்து",2,"pira","simile","pearl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:38"},"ai:381":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":379,"t":["பைங்காய் நெல்லி பலவுடன் மிசைந்து","செங்கால் மராஅத்த வரிநிழல் இருந்தோர்","யார்கொல் அளியர் தாமே வார்சிறைக்","குறுங்கால் மகன்றில் அன்ன","உடன்புணர் கொள்கைக் காத லோரே."],"p":"","r":"Onlookers on the road see a pair who ate green nelli fruit together and sit in the striped shade of a red-stemmed maraam, and ask who these pitiable ones are, then answer: lovers whose way is to stay joined, like the long-winged, short-legged magandril.","n":"அளியர் is pity and tenderness at once: the onlookers' question begins as concern for two young people in the waste and ends as admiration, since the magandril pair was held never to part. Eating the sour nelli together is a wasteland meal shared, not a hardship borne alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","punarchi","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 381","lb":"AI381","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["kandor","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",1,"maram","gift_food","gooseberry"],["மராஅத்த","மரா",2,"maram","scene","kadamba tree"],["மகன்றில்","மகன்றில்",4,"pul","simile","water bird"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:39"},"ai:382":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":380,"t":["புள் ஒலிக்கு அமர்த்த கண்ணள் வெள்வேல்","திருந்து கழல் காளையொடு அருஞ்சுரம் கழிவோள்","எல்லிடை அசைந்த கல்லென் சீறூர்ப்","புனையிழை மகளிர்ப் பயந்த","மனைகெழு பெண்டிர்க்கு நோவுமார் பெரிதே."],"p":"","r":"She whose eyes start at every bird-call crosses the hard waste with a youth of white spear and fine war-anklets; in the noisy small hamlet where they rested at nightfall, the housewives who have borne jewelled daughters of their own feel great pain.","n":"The pain is not the girl's but that of strangers: the hamlet's mothers see their own daughters in her and feel ahead of time the loss her mother has suffered. Her fear of birds marks how new she is to the road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 382","lb":"AI382","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["kandor","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["புள்","புள்",1,"pul","scene","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:39"},"ai:383":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":381,"t":["கோள்சுரும்பு அரற்றும் நாள்சுரத்து அமன்ற","நெடுங் கால் மராஅத்துக் குறுஞ்சினை பற்றி","வலஞ்சுரி வாலிணர் கொய்தற்கு நின்ற","மள்ளன் உள்ள மகிழ்கூர்ந்துஅன்றே","பஞ்சாய்ப் பாவைக்கும் தனக்கும்","அம்சாய் கூந்தல் ஆய்வது கண்டே."],"p":"","r":"In the wasteland where bees hum after honey, the warrior who stood holding a low branch of the tall maraam to pluck its white curling clusters was glad at heart, watching the girl of soft lovely hair choose flowers for her rush doll and for herself.","n":"The girl is still a child: she adorns her பஞ்சாய்ப் பாவை, a doll of rush, before herself, and the youth's joy is in that play. The poem shows the eloping man as one who bends the branch for her rather than as a captor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 383","lb":"AI383","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["kandor","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["கோள்சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["மராஅத்துக்","மரா",2,"maram","scene","kadamba tree"],["வாலிணர்","இணர்",3,"maram","ornament","flower cluster"],["பஞ்சாய்ப்","பஞ்சாய்",5,"maram","ornament","rush (doll)"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:39"},"ai:384":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":382,"t":["சேண்புலம் முன்னிய அசைநடை அந்தணிர்","நும்மொன்று இரந்தனென் மொழிவல் எம்மூர்","ஆய்நயந்து எடுத்த ஆய்நலம் கவின","ஆரிடை இறந்தனள் என்மின்","நேர் இறை முன்கைஎன் ஆயத் தோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The girl asks slow-walking brahmins bound for a far land to carry one word to her bangled companions in her town: she has crossed the hard road so that the beauty her mother lovingly raised may flourish.","n":"She frames the elopement as the fulfilment of her mother's care rather than its betrayal: the beauty the mother tended (ஆய் நயந்து எடுத்த) will now come to its bloom. The message goes to the companions, not to the mother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 384","lb":"AI384","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:39"},"ai:385":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":383,"t":["கடுங்கண் காளையொடு நெடுந்தேர் ஏறிக்","கோள்வல் வேங்கை மலை பிறக்கு ஒழிய","வேறுபல் அருஞ்சுரம் இறந்தனள் அவளெனக்","கூறுமின் வாழியோ ஆறுசெல் மாக்கள்","நல்தோள் நயந்துபா ராட்டி","என்கெடுத்து இருந்த அறனில் யாய்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The girl asks travellers on the road to tell her mother, who without justice praised her fine shoulders and kept her undone at home, that she has mounted the tall chariot with the fierce youth, left behind the hill of the killing tiger, and crossed many hard wastes.","n":"அறனில் யாய் turns the charge of wrong back on the mother: the guard that kept a grown girl at home is what the daughter calls unjust. The chariot and the tiger's hill leave no doubt that she went freely and far.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 385","lb":"AI385","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",2,"maa","scene","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:39"},"ai:386":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":384,"t":["புன்கண் யானையொடு புலிவழங்கு அத்தம்","நயந்த காதலற் புணர்ந்துசென் றனளே","நெடுஞ்சுவர் நல்லில் மருண்ட","இடும்பை உறவிநின் கடுஞ்சூல் மகளே."],"p":"","r":"Through the waste where tigers range among distressed elephants, your first-born daughter has gone joined with the lover she desired: so the speaker tells the mother, who wanders bewildered in her tall-walled house like a lost ant.","n":"On this reading உறவி (ant) is a vocative: the mother, searching every corner of the walled house, is pictured as an ant lost inside it, and the walls that guarded the girl now only trap the mother. கடுஞ்சூல் மகள், the child of the first pregnancy, sharpens her loss.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","idumbai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 386","lb":"AI386","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["kandor","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["யானையொடு","யானை",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["புலிவழங்கு","புலி",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["உறவி","உறவி",4,"pira","scene","ant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:39"},"ai:387":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":385,"t":["அறம்புரி அருமறை நவின்ற நாவில்","திறம்புரி கொள்கை அந்தணிர் தொழுவலென்று","ஒள்தொடி வினவும் பேதையம் பெண்டே","கண்டனெம் அம்ம சுரத்திடை அவளை","இன் துணை இனிதுபா ராட்டக்","குன்றுயர் பிறங்கல் மலையிறந்த் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"Brahmins whose tongues have practised the rare scripture devoted to right answer the simple bangled woman who says she will worship them as she asks: we saw her in the waste, her sweet companion fondly caressing her, as she crossed the mountain of piled rocks.","n":"The searcher's worship of the brahmins as men of அறம் gives their answer weight: what they report is not danger but tenderness (இன் துணை இனிது பாராட்ட), so their eyewitness word is itself a quiet verdict on the match.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","andhanar","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 387","lb":"AI387","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["pira","sevili",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:39"},"ai:388":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":386,"t":["நெருப்பு அவிர் கனலி உருப்புச்சினந் தணியக்","கருங்கால் யாத்து வரிநிழல் இரீஇச்","சிறுவரை இறப்பின் காண்குவை செறிதொடிப்","பொன்னேர் மேனி மடந்தையொடு","வென்வேல் விடலை முன்னிய சுரனே."],"p":"","r":"Travellers tell the searching woman to rest in the striped shade of the black-stemmed yaa until the fiery sun's heat abates; past the little hill she will see the waste toward which the youth of conquering spear went with the gold-skinned girl of close bangles.","n":"The advice is practical and kind: the wayfarers do not dissuade her but tell her when and where to go, so the pursuit is treated as a journey to be managed, not a grief to be argued away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 388","lb":"AI388","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["pira","sevili",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யாத்து","யா",2,"maram","scene","yaa tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:39"},"ai:389":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":387,"t":["செய்வினை பொலிந்த செறிகழல் நோன் தாள்","மையணல் காளையொடு பைய இயலிப்","பாவை யன்னஎன் ஆய்தொடி மடந்தை","சென்றனள் என்றிர் ஐய","ஒன்றின வோஅவள் அம்சிலம் படியே."],"p":"","r":"The searching woman, told that her doll-like girl of fine bangles went slowly with the youth of dark beard and strong war-anklets, asks the men whether her feet with their lovely anklets kept pace with his.","n":"Told that the youth walked slowly (பைய), she asks not whether the girl suffered but whether the two were in step, so the worry turns to whether the match itself fits. The anklets still on her feet mark a girl not yet wed by rite.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 389","lb":"AI389","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:39"},"ai:390":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":388,"t":["நல்லோர் ஆங்கண் பரந்துகை தொழுது","பல்லூழ் மறுகி வனவு வோயே","திண்தோள் வல்வில் காளையொடு","கண்டனெம் மன்ற சுரத்திடை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Onlookers tell the woman who goes round and round, folding her hands in worship to every good person and asking, that they surely saw the girl in the waste with the youth of strong shoulders and mighty bow.","n":"மன்ற (surely) answers the searcher's repeated asking: the onlookers give certainty, not comfort, and the youth is described by his strength, as one who can guard her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 390","lb":"AI390","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["kandor","sevili",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:39"},"ai:391":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":389,"t":["மறுவில் தூவிச் சிறுகருங் காக்கை","அன்புடை மரபின்நின் கிளையோடு ஆரப்","பச்சூன் பெய்த பைந்நிண வல்சி","பொலம்புனை கலத்தில் தருகுவென் மாதோ","வெம்சின விறல்வேல் காளையொடு","அம்சில் ஓதியை வரக்கரைந் தீமே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker promises the small black crow of flawless feathers a meal of raw flesh and fresh fat, served in a gold-worked bowl for it to share with its loving kin, if it will caw so that the girl of the few lovely tresses comes home with the youth of the fierce, victorious spear.","n":"The crow's cawing was the household's sign that a guest was on the way; here the reward is offered before the sign, so the meal is a bribe to the omen rather than thanks for it. Promising a gold bowl to a crow measures the household's longing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 391","lb":"AI391","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காக்கை","காக்கை",1,"pul","scene","crow"],["பச்சூன்","பசு ஊன்",3,"unavu","gift_food","raw meat"],["வல்சி","வல்சி",3,"unavu","gift_food","food"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:40"},"ai:392":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":390,"t":["வேய்வனப்பு இழந்த தோளும் வெயில்தெற","வாய்கவின் தொலைந்த நுதலும் நோக்கிப்","பரியல் வாழி தோழி பரியின்","எல்லைஇல் இடும்பை தரூஉம்","நல்வரை நாடனொடு வந்த மாறே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing her shoulders, which have lost their bamboo-like beauty, and her brow, whose fresh glow the sun's heat has destroyed, the girl tells her friend not to grieve, for grieving would bring pain without end, since she came with the man of the good hill country.","n":"The wasted shoulders and sun-burned brow are the road's marks on a girl who has come back; the speaker refuses pity because she chose the journey, and the hill country of the man, not the burnt road, is what she names last. Who speaks to whom is not certain: the friend may be the speaker and the girl the one addressed as தோழி.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 392","lb":"AI392","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வேய்","வேய்",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:40"},"ai:393":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":391,"t":["துறந்ததன் கொண்டு துயரடச் சாஅய்","அறம்புலந்து பழிக்கும் அண்க ணாட்டி","எவ்வ நெஞ்சிற்கு ஏம மாக","வந்தன ளோநின் மகளே","வெந்திறல் வெள்வேல் விடலைமுந் துறவே."],"p":"","r":"Someone asks the mother, who wasted under grief when the girl left and quarrelled with right and blamed it, whether her daughter has come, as a safeguard for her anguished heart, with the youth of fierce strength and white spear walking ahead.","n":"The question pictures the mother at her lowest, blaming அறம் itself for the loss, and offers the return as the remedy for that charge; the youth walking in front is the sign of a lawful homecoming rather than a flight. அண்கணாட்டி is a rare word, read here as a name for the mother.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 393","lb":"AI393","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["pira","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:40"},"ai:394":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":392,"t":["மாண்பில் கொள்கையொடு மயங்குதுயர் செய்த","அன்பில் அறனும் அருளிற்று மன்ற","வெஞ்சுரம் இறந்த அம்சில் ஓதிப்","பெருமட மான்பிணை அலைத்த","சிறுநுதல் குறுமகள் காட்டிய வம்மே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that the loveless right, which with its ignoble ways had caused bewildering grief, has surely shown grace after all, for it has brought before them the small-browed young girl of lovely few tresses who crossed the hot wasteland and whose glance outdid the great innocent doe; come and see.","n":"The same அறம் that the household had called loveless (அன்பில் அறன்) is now said to have shown grace: the return reverses the verdict on the moral order itself. The doe is not likened but defeated (அலைத்த), the girl's look troubling the very creature it would be compared with.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","udanpokku","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 394","lb":"AI394","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மான்பிணை","மான் பிணை",4,"maa","scene","doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:40"},"ai:395":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":393,"t":["முளிவயிர்ப் பிறந்த வளிவளர் கூர்எரிச்","சுடர்விடு நெடுங்கொடி விடர்குகை முழங்கும்","இன்னா அருஞ்சுரம் தீர்ந்தனம் மென்மெல","ஏகுமதி வாழியோ குறுமகள் போதுகலந்து","கறங்கிசை அருவி வீழும்","பிறங்கிரும் சோலைநம் மலைகெழு நாட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the young girl that they have crossed the cruel, hard wasteland where the long flame of a fire born in dry bamboo and fanned by wind roars in the clefts and caves, and bids her walk slowly now toward their hill country, with its high dark groves where a roaring waterfall comes down mixed with blossoms.","n":"The danger is named only as past (தீர்ந்தனம்), and the man's word is to slow down, not to hurry; the fire in the bamboo and the flower-laden waterfall set the two lands side by side, the road of paalai behind and his kurinji home ahead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 395","lb":"AI395","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முளிவயிர்","வயிர்",1,"maram","scene","dry bamboo"],["போது","போது",4,"maram","scene","blossoms"],["அருவி","அருவி",5,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["சோலை","சோலை",6,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:40"},"ai:396":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":394,"t":["புலிப்பொறி வேங்கைப் பொன்னிணர் கொய்து நின்","கதுப்பயல் அணியும் அளவை பைபயச்","சுரத்திடை அயர்ச்சியை ஆறுக மடந்தை","கல்கெழு சிறப்பின் நம்மூர்","எல் விருந்து ஆகிப் புகுக நாமே."],"p":"","r":"The man bids the girl rest her weariness from the wasteland slowly, for as long as it takes him to pluck the golden clusters of the tiger-spotted vengai and set them in her hair, and then they will enter their rock-famed town as evening guests.","n":"The plucking of flowers is a pretext that lets her rest without being told she is tired, and it makes her entry into his town an adorned one. She arrives not as a runaway but as a guest at nightfall (எல் விருந்து), the honoured status a newcomer is received with.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","virundhu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 396","lb":"AI396","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புலிப்பொறி","புலி",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",1,"maram","ornament","vengai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:40"},"ai:397":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":395,"t":["கவிழ்மயிர் எருத்தின் செந்நாய் ஏற்றை","குருளைப் பன்றி கொள்ளாது கழியும்","சுரன்அணி வாரா நின்றனள் என்பது","முன்னுற விரந்தநீர் உரைமின்","இன்நகை முறுவல்என் ஆயத்தோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks those hurrying on ahead to tell her sweet-smiling companions beforehand that she is coming across the wasteland, where the red wild dog with shaggy drooping neck passes by without seizing the young piglet.","n":"The companions she left behind are the first to be told of her return. The wild dog that spares the piglet makes the road gentle and is traditionally read as a sign of forbearance at home; it is kept as scene, since nothing in the poem ties the dog's act to what a person of the situation does (iraicchi condition 2). விரந்த is read as விரைந்த; the speaker may be the girl speaking of herself in the third person.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 397","lb":"AI397","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["செந்நாய்","செந்நாய்",1,"maa","scene","wild dog"],["பன்றி","பன்றி",2,"maa","scene","pig"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:40"},"ai:398":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":396,"t":["புள்ளும் அறியாப் பல்பழம் பழுனி","மடமான் அறியாத் தடநீர் நிலைஇச்","சுரநனி இனிய வாகுக என்று","நினைத்தொறும் கலிழும் என்னினும்","மிகப்பெரிது புலம்பின்று தோழிநம் ஊரே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker wishes the wasteland may be very sweet for the returning pair, with many fruits ripened that even the birds have not found and broad pools standing that even the young deer have not known, and tells her friend that though she weeps each time she thinks of it, the town is lonelier still.","n":"The wish for a kind road, with food and water no creature has yet taken, is reserved wholly for the travellers; the speaker's own grief is then outweighed by the town's, so the girl's absence is a loss to the whole place. நினைத்தொறும் கலிழும் is the formula of the mother's lament in this poet's decade 38.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 398","lb":"AI398","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["புள்ளும்","புள்",1,"pul","scene","birds"],["பல்பழம்","பழம்",1,"unavu","scene","fruit"],["மடமான்","மான்",2,"maa","scene","deer"],["தடநீர்","நீர்",2,"pira","scene","water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:40"},"ai:399":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":397,"t":["நும்மனைச் சிலம்பு கழீஇ அயரினும்","எம்மனை வதுவை நன்மணம் கழிகெனச்","சொல்லின் எவனோ மற்றே வென்வேல்","மையற விளங்கிய கழலடிப்","பொய்வல் காளையை ஈன்ற தாய்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks what harm there would be in telling the mother who bore the lying youth of the victorious spear and the anklet-bright feet: even if you perform the anklet-removal in your house, let the good wedding be done in ours.","n":"The anklet-removal (சிலம்பு கழீஇ), the rite that ends a girl's maidenhood, and the wedding are treated as two rites the two houses can divide; the girl's side concedes the first to the youth's house and claims the second. The youth is still called a liar (பொய்வல் காளை) in the very message that proposes the settlement.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","udanpokku","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 399","lb":"AI399","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சிலம்பு","சிலம்பு",1,"pira","ornament","anklet"],["கழலடிப்","கழல்",4,"pira","ornament","warrior's anklet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:40"},"ai:400":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":398,"t":["மள்ளர் அன்ன மரவந் தழீஇ","மகளிர் அன்ன ஆடுகொடி நுடக்கும்","அரும்பதம் கொண்ட பெரும்பத வேனில்","காதல் புணர்ந்தனள் ஆகி ஆய்கழல்","வெஞ்சின விறல் வேல் காளையொடு","இன்றுபுகு தருமென வந்தன்று தூதே."],"p":"","r":"Word has come that in the season of summer's plenty, when the maravam trees stand like warriors and the swaying creeper, like women, embraces and shakes them, the girl, united in love with the youth of the fierce, victorious spear and fine anklet, will come home today.","n":"The tree and creeper repeat the couple in the season's own figures, the warrior-like tree held by the woman-like creeper, but the similes are stated with அன்ன, so the scene is open comparison, not ullurai. அரும்பதம் கொண்ட பெரும்பத வேனில் is hard to fix: a summer that holds rare food in plenty, or a summer of great heat.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","thoothu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 400","lb":"AI400","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மள்ளர்","மள்ளர்",1,"seythi","simile","warriors"],["மரவந்","மரவம்",1,"maram","simile","maravam tree"],["ஆடுகொடி","கொடி",2,"maram","simile","creeper"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:40"},"ai:401":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":399,"t":["மறியிடைப் படுத்த மான்பிணை போலப்","புதல்வன் நடுவண னாக நன்றும்","இனிது மன்றஅவர் கிடக்கை முனிவின்றி","நீல்நிற வியலகம் கவைஇய","ஈனும் உம்பரும் பெறலரும் குரைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The foster mother says that the way the couple lie, their son in the middle like a fawn lying between a stag and his doe, is truly sweet, a thing that neither this earth ringed by the blue sky nor the heaven above could easily come by.","n":"The sevili sets the bed of the household above both worlds: ஈனும் உம்பரும் (this world and the upper world) together cannot obtain it. The deer family is mullai's own beast, so the simile also places the house in the forest pasture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 401","lb":"AI401","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","nartaay",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மறியிடைப்","மறி",1,"maa","simile","fawn"],["மான்பிணை","மான் பிணை",1,"maa","simile","deer pair"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:41"},"ai:402":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":400,"t":["புதல்வற் கவைஇய தாய்புற முயங்கி","நசையினன் வதிந்த கிடக்கை பாணர்","நரம்புளர் முரற்கை போல","இனிதால் அம்ம பண்புமா ருடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"She says that the way he lies, full of desire, embracing from behind the mother who holds their son, is sweet like the music bards draw by plucking their strings, and it is seemly as well.","n":"பண்புமாருடைத்தே adds a judgment of propriety to the pleasure: the foster mother approves the embrace as fitting the married house, not only as sweet. The embraces are nested, son in the mother's arms, mother in the father's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","punarchi","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 402","lb":"AI402","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","nartaay",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பாணர்","பாணர்",2,"seythi","simile","bards"],["நரம்புளர்","நரம்பு",3,"yaazh","simile","strings"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:41"},"ai:403":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":401,"t":["புணர்ந்தகா தலியின் புதல்வன் தலையும்","அமர்ந்த உள்ளம் பெரிது ஆகின்றே","அகன்பெருஞ் சிறப்பின் தந்தை பெயரன்","முறுவலில் இன்னகை பயிற்றிச்","சிறுதேர் உருட்டும் தளர்நடை கண்டே."],"p":"","r":"She says that his heart, already settled on the beloved he joined, has grown great toward their son as well, as he watches the boy who bears the name of his illustrious grandfather practise sweet smiles and pull a little toy chariot with tottering steps.","n":"தந்தை பெயரன் makes the toddler the carrier of the line: named for the father's father, he continues a house of wide fame, so the sevili's joy is also the household's continuity. Whose heart grows (the husband's, or her own) turns on how தலையும் and அமர்ந்த உள்ளம் are joined.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kudi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 403","lb":"AI403","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","nartaay",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:41"},"ai:404":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":402,"t":["வாள் நுதல் அரிவை மகன்முலை யூட்டத்","தானவன் சிறுபுறம் கவையினன் நன்று","நறும்பூம் தண்புற அணிந்த","குறும்பல் பொறைய நாடுகிழ வோனே."],"p":"","r":"She says it is good that, while the bright-browed woman suckles their son, the lord of the land of many small hills, adorned with fragrant flowers on its cool pasture, holds her small back in his arms.","n":"நன்று is the sevili's verdict: the father does not draw away from the nursing mother but includes himself in the scene by holding her from behind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 404","lb":"AI404","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","nartaay",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["நறும்பூம்","பூ",3,"maram","scene","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:41"},"ai:405":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":403,"t":["ஒண்சுடர்ப் பாண்டில் செஞ்சுடர் போல","மனைக்குவிளக் காயினள் மன்ற கனைப்பெயல்","பூப்பல அணிந்த வைப்பின்","புறவணி நாடன் புதல்வன் தாயே."],"p":"","r":"She says that the mother of the son of the lord of the pasture-adorned land, where heavy rain has decked the place with many flowers, has surely become the lamp of the house, like the red flame of a bright-flamed bowl lamp.","n":"The lamp is earned by motherhood: the closing words புதல்வன் தாயே name her by her son at the moment she is called the house's light. The heavy rain and flowering pasture are mullai's season and land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","makkal","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 405","lb":"AI405","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","nartaay",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பூப்பல","பூ",3,"maram","scene","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:41"},"ai:406":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":404,"t":["மாதர் உண்கண் மகன்விளை யாடக்","காதலித் தழீஇ இனிதிருந் தனனே","தாதார் பிரசம் ஊதும்","போதார் புறவின் நாடுகிழ வோனே."],"p":"","r":"She says the lord of the land of flower-filled pasture, where bees hum over pollen-laden blossoms, sits happily embracing his beloved while their son with lovely kohl-rimmed eyes plays.","n":"The bees at the flowers in the land-epithet echo the man's contentment, but they stand only for a mood, so they are left as scene rather than iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 406","lb":"AI406","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","nartaay",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["பிரசம்","பிரசம்",3,"pira","scene","bees"],["போதார்","போது",4,"maram","scene","blossoms"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:41"},"ai:407":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":405,"t":["நயந்த காதலித் தழீஇப் பாணர்","நயம்படு முரற்கையின் யாத்த பயன்தெரிந்து","இன்புறு புணர்ச்சி நுகரும்","மென்புல வைப்பின் நாடுகிழ வோனே."],"p":"","r":"She says the lord of the soft-soiled land, embracing the beloved he desired, enjoys the delight of union, knowing the worth that bards have bound into their pleasing songs.","n":"Life is said to follow art: the man knows the reward of union from what the bards' songs set down. How யாத்த பயன்தெரிந்து is to be taken (the gain the songs describe, or the gain he understands while they sing) is open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 407","lb":"AI407","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","nartaay",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பாணர்","பாணர்",1,"seythi","scene","bards"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:41"},"ai:408":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":406,"t":["பாணர் முல்லை பாடச் சுடரிழை","வணுதல் அரிவை முல்லை மலைய","இனிதிருந் தனனே நெடுந்தகை","துனிதீர கொள்கைத்தன் புதல்வனொடு பொலிந்தே."],"p":"","r":"She says that as the bards sing the mullai melody and the bright-browed woman in shining jewels wears mullai flowers, the noble man sits happily, his ways now free of sulking, resplendent with his son.","n":"Mullai appears twice, heard as the melody and worn as the flower, so the poem's thinai is enacted in song and garland. துனிதீர் implies a quarrel now past: the house's calm follows a reconciliation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","makkal","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 408","lb":"AI408","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","nartaay",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",1,"yaazh","scene","mullai melody"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","ornament","jasmine"],["பாணர்","பாணர்",1,"seythi","scene","bards"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:41"},"ai:409":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":407,"t":["புதல்வன் கவைஇயினன் தந்தை மென்மொழிப்","புதல்வன் தாயோ இருவரும் கவையினள்","இனிது மன்றஅவர் கிடக்கை","நனியிரும் பரப்பின்இவ் உலகுடன் உறுமே."],"p":"","r":"She says the father embraced the son, and the soft-spoken mother of the son embraced them both; their lying together is truly sweet, worth the whole of this vast wide world.","n":"The embrace widens line by line, father to son, mother to both, and the sevili prices the scene at the whole world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 409","lb":"AI409","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","nartaay",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:41"},"ai:410":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":408,"t":["மாலை முன்றில் குறுங்கால் கட்டில்","மனையோள் துணைவி யாகப் புதல்வன்","மார்பின் ஊரும் மகிழ்நகை இன்பப்","பொழுதிற்கு ஒத்தன்று மன்னே","மென்பிணித் தம்ம பாணனது யாழே."],"p":"","r":"She says that in the evening, on a short-legged cot in the front yard, with the housewife as his companion and the son riding on his chest, the hour of laughing joy was such that the bard's softly strung lute could not match it.","n":"மன்னே marks the scene as already past and fondly recalled. The bard's lute is present in the yard, so the comparison is between two pleasures heard at once, and the family wins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","inbam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 410","lb":"AI410","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","nartaay",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["பாணனது","பாணன்",5,"seythi","scene","bard"],["யாழே","யாழ்",5,"yaazh","scene","lute"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:41"},"ai:411":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":409,"t":["ஆர்குரல் எழிலி அழிதுளி சிதறிக்","கார்தொடங் கின்றால் காமர் புறவே","வீழ்தரு புதுப்புனல் ஆடுகம்","தாழிருங் கூந்தல் வம்மதி விரைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The hero tells his wife that the full-voiced cloud has scattered its heavy drops and the rains have begun in the lovely scrub, and bids her, the woman of long dark hair, come quickly so they may bathe together in the new water that is falling.","n":"The first rain that once marked the deadline of his absence is now an invitation to play: the season the waiting wife dreaded is turned, by his return, into a holiday for both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 411","lb":"AI411","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["எழிலி","எழிலி",1,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["புதுப்புனல்","புனல்",3,"pira","gift_food","new floodwater"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:42"},"ai:412":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":410,"t":["காயா கொன்றை நெய்தல் முல்லை","போதவிழ் தளவொடு பிடவலர்ந்து கவினிப்","பூவணி கொண்டன்றால் புறவே","பேரமர்க் கண்ணி ஆடுகம் விரைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The hero tells his wife that with kaayaa, konrai, water lily and mullai, the thalavam opening its buds and the pidavam in bloom, the scrub has grown lovely and put on an ornament of flowers, and asks her, the woman of large wide eyes, to come quickly and play there.","n":"The catalogue is of mullai's own rain flowers; the scrub is pictured as a woman who has dressed herself (பூவணி கொண்டன்று), the adornment he once could not give his wife while away. The நெய்தல் here is the lily of the scrub's pools, not a token of the seashore.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 412","lb":"AI412","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["காயா","காயா",1,"maram","scene",""],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",1,"maram","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","scene","water lily"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",1,"maram","scene",""],["தளவொடு","தளவம்",2,"maram","scene",""],["பிடவலர்ந்து","பிடவம்",2,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:42"},"ai:413":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":411,"t":["நின்னுதல் நாறும் நறுந்தண் புறவின்","நின்னே போல மஞ்ஞை யாலக்","கார்தொடங் கின்றால் பொழுதே","பேரியல் அரிவை நாநயத்தகவே."],"p":"","r":"The hero tells his wife that in the cool scrub, fragrant as her own brow, the peacocks dance just as she does and the season of rain has begun, a time for the two of them to delight in.","n":"The comparison runs backwards: the woman is the standard and the peacock is likened to her (நின்னே போல), and the scrub smells of her brow rather than she of it; the land is read through her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","azhagu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 413","lb":"AI413","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",2,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:42"},"ai:414":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":412,"t":["புள்ளும் மாவும் புணர்ந்தினது உகளக்","கோட்டவும் கொடியவும் பூப்பல பழுனி","மெல்லியல் அரிவை கண்டிகு","மல்லல் ஆகிய மணங் கமழ் புறவே."],"p":"","r":"The hero bids his gentle wife look at the scrub grown rich and fragrant, where birds and beasts frisk happily in pairs and many flowers have come to fullness on the trees and creepers.","n":"Birds and beasts together, not one pair, so the pairing is the season's general mood rather than an image of the couple; a reader may still feel it as the land joining in their reunion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","paruvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 414","lb":"AI414","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["புள்ளும்","புள்",1,"pul","scene","birds"],["மாவும்","மா",1,"maa","scene","beasts"],["பூப்பல","பூ",2,"maram","scene","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:42"},"ai:415":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":413,"t":["இதுவே மடந்தைநாம் மேவிய பொழுதே","உதுவே மடந்தைநாம் உள்ளிய புறவே","இனிதுடன் கழிக்கின் இளமை","இனிதால் அம்ம இனிஅவர்ப் புணர்வே."],"p":"","r":"The hero tells the young woman that this is the season they longed for and that the scrub they kept in mind; youth is sweet if spent together, and sweet indeed now is the union.","n":"The near and far demonstratives (இதுவே, உதுவே) point at the time and the place as if both had been promised; the season they 'longed for' is the one that ended his absence. Line 4's அவர்ப் புணர்வு, 'union with them', shifts to the third person and is hard to fit to a speech addressed to மடந்தை.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","punarchi","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 415","lb":"AI415","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:42"},"ai:416":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":414,"t":["போதார் நறுந்துகள் கவினிப் புறவில் தாதார்ந்து","களிச்சுரும்பு அரற்றும் காமர் புதலின்","மடப்பிடி தழீஇய மாவே","சுடர்த்தொடி மடவரல் புணர்ந்தனம் யாமே."],"p":"","r":"As the tusker has embraced his young she-elephant in the lovely bush where bees, drunk on pollen from the fragrant blossom dust of the scrub, hum loudly, so we have embraced the girl of shining bangles, says the hero.","n":"The two closing words answer each other, மாவே and யாமே: the hero sets his own embrace beside the elephant's without a marker, so the beasts are a stated parallel, not a hidden one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 416","lb":"AI416","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["போதார்","போது",1,"maram","scene","blossom"],["களிச்சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",2,"pira","scene","bee"],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",3,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["மாவே","மா",3,"maa","scene","tusker"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:42"},"ai:417":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":415,"t":["கார்கலந் தன்றால் புறவே பலவுடன்","நேர்பரந் தனவால் புனமே ஏர்கலந்து","தாதார் பிரசம் மொய்ப்பப்","போதார் கூந்தல் முயங்கினள் எம்மே."],"p":"","r":"The rains have mingled with the scrub and the fields have spread out level with many crops together; and as the pollen-laden bees swarmed in beauty, the woman of flower-filled hair embraced me, says the hero.","n":"ஏர் in line 2 can be beauty or the plough; with the fields spread level it may be the ploughing of the first rains, which would set the couple's embrace beside the farmers' sowing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","mazhai","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 417","lb":"AI417","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பிரசம்","பிரசம்",3,"pira","scene","bees"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:42"},"ai:418":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":416,"t":["வானம் பாடி வறங்களைந்து ஆனாது","அழிதுளி தலைஇய புறவின் காண்வர","வானர மகளோ நீயே","மாண்முலை அடைய முயங்கி யோயே."],"p":"","r":"In the scrub made lovely to see, where the rain-bird's drought has been ended and the heavy drops have poured without stopping, the hero asks his wife whether she is a woman of the sky, she who held him close against her fine breasts.","n":"வானம் பாடி can be the rain-calling bird whose thirst is ended, or the sky that 'sang' (thundered) and drove off the drought; the line division decides it. The hero's question makes the embrace of his own wife a wonder, as if no earthly woman could give it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","nalam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 418","lb":"AI418","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வானம்","வானம்பாடி",1,"pul","scene","rain-bird"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:42"},"ai:419":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":417,"t":["உயிர்கலந்து ஒன்றிய செயிர்தீர் கேண்மைப்","பிரிந்துறல் அறியா விருந்து கவவி","நம்போல் நயவரப் புணர்ந்தன","கண்டிகு மடவரல் புறவின் மாவே."],"p":"","r":"The hero bids the young woman look at the beasts of the scrub: like the two of them, whose faultless bond has mingled their lives into one, the beasts embrace in a fresh union that knows no parting and have joined delightfully.","n":"Again the humans are the standard (நம்போல்): the couple's bond is what the beasts are measured against. பிரிந்துறல் அறியா, 'not knowing parting', is said of the beasts but speaks for a couple who have just come through a parting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","uyir","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 419","lb":"AI419","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மாவே","மா",4,"maa","simile","beasts"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:42"},"ai:420":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":418,"t":["பொன்னெனமலர்ந்த கொன்றை மணியெனத்","தேம்படு காயா மலர்ந்த தோன்றியொடு","நன்னலம் எய்தினை புறவே நின்னைக்","காணிய வருதும் யாமே","வாள்நுதல் அரிவையொடு ஆய்நலம் படர்ந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The hero tells the scrub that it has come to fine beauty, with konrai blooming like gold, honeyed kaayaa like sapphire, and the thondri in flower, and that he will come to see it with his bright-browed woman, drawn by its choice loveliness.","n":"The decade closes with the land addressed as a host: the season he praised all through is now a place he promises to visit with her, not a deadline he must beat.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","azhagu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 420","lb":"AI420","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",1,"maram","simile",""],["காயா","காயா",2,"maram","simile",""],["தோன்றியொடு","தோன்றி",2,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:42"},"ai:421":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":419,"t":["மாலை வெண்காழ் காவலர் வீச","நறும்பூம் புறவின் ஒடுங்குமுயல் இரியும்","புன்புல நாடன் மடமகள்","நலம் கிளர் பணைத்தோள் விலங்கின செலவே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the going he had planned was stopped by the full, bamboo-smooth shoulders of the young daughter of the chief of the poor scrubland, where at evening the watchmen throw their white missiles and the hare hiding in the fragrant flowered woodland bolts.","n":"The journey is not refused by argument: her shoulders 'block' it (விலங்கின), as if they stood across the road. The hare driven from its cover by the watchmen is the one creature in the poem that does leave; the man, by contrast, stays. What the watchmen throw (வெண்காழ், a white hard thing, perhaps sling-stones or pointed sticks) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 421","lb":"AI421","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காவலர்","காவலர்",1,"seythi","scene","watchmen"],["முயல்","முயல்",2,"maa","scene","hare"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:43"},"ai:422":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":420,"t":["கடும்பரி நெடுந்தேர்க் கால்வல் புரவி","நெடுங்கொடி முல்லையொடு தளவமலர் உதிர","விரையுபு கடை இநாம் செல்லின்","நிரைவளை முன்கை வருந்தலோ இலளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that if they drive the tall chariot fast, its strong-legged horses scattering the flowers of the long-stemmed mullai and the thalavam, she with rows of bangles on her forearm will not suffer.","n":"Her suffering is measured by his speed: the conditional (செல்லின்) makes the charioteer's haste the remedy for her pain, and the scattered mullai and thalavam, the flowers of the waiting land, are crushed by the very return they wait for.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 422","lb":"AI422","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புரவி","புரவி",1,"maa","scene","horse"],["முல்லையொடு","முல்லை",2,"maram","scene",""],["தளவமலர்","தளவம்",2,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:43"},"ai:423":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":421,"t":["மா மழை இடியூஉத் தளிசொரிந் தன்றே","வாள்நுதல் பசப்பச் செலவயர்ந் தனையே","யாமே நிந்துறந்து அமையலம்","ஆய்மலர் உண்கணும் நீர்நிறைந் தனவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that the great clouds have thundered and let fall their drops, yet he is set on going while her bright brow turns pale; they cannot bear to be left by him, and her kohl-dark eyes, fine as chosen flowers, are already full of tears.","n":"The friend's argument is the season itself: a man who leaves when the rain has already come breaks the order by which men return in the rains. The rain is mullai's time set inside a scene of departure; the mixing is of time, not of native things.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","paruvam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 423","lb":"AI423","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:43"},"ai:424":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":422,"t":["புறவணி நாடன் காதல் மடமகள்","ஒண்ணுதல் பசப்ப நீசெலின் தெண்ணீர்ப்","போதவிழ் தாமரை அன்னநின்","காதல்அம் புதல்வன் அழும்இனி முலைக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend warns the man that if he goes, making pale the bright brow of the beloved young daughter of the chief of the woodland, his dear son, like a lotus opening its bud in clear water, will now cry for the breast.","n":"The friend names the wife as her father's daughter, as if reminding the husband from whose house he took her, and then names the son as his (நின் புதல்வன்): the cost of going falls first on the child, whose mother's grief will fail him at the breast.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pirivu","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 424","lb":"AI424","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",3,"maram","simile","lotus"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:43"},"ai:425":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":423,"t":["புன்புறப் பேடை சேவல் இன்புற","மன்னர் இயவரின் இரங்கும் கானம்","வல்லை நெடுந்தேர் கடவின்","அல்லல் அருநோய் ஒழித்தல் எமக் கெளிதே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his charioteer that if he drives the tall chariot fast through the woodland, where the dun-backed hen dove croons like the drummers of kings to please her mate, it will be easy to end their sore pain.","n":"The 'our' pain (எமக்கு) is shared: his road-weariness and her waiting are ended by one act, the fast drive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 425","lb":"AI425","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பேடை","பேடை",1,"pul","iraicchi","hen dove"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",1,"pul","iraicchi","cock dove"],["இயவரின்","இயவர்",2,"seythi","simile","drummers"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பேடை","confirmed","1/1","the man, hearing a mate call to her partner, knows his wife waits calling for him, and so hurries home"]],"ch":"ai:43"},"ai:426":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":424,"t":["வென்வேல் வேந்தன் அருந்தொழில் துறந்தினி","நன்னுதல் யானே செலஒழிந் தனனே","முரசுபாடு அதிர ஏவி","அரசுபடக் கடக்கும் அருஞ்சமத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his fair-browed wife that, giving up the hard task of the king of the conquering spear, he has now stopped going to the hard battle where that king sets the drums thundering and overcomes kings.","n":"The war he renounces is painted at its height, drums and conquered kings, in the same breath as the refusal, so the giving up is shown as a real cost and not an easy choice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aalvinai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 426","lb":"AI426","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முரசு","முரசு",3,"parai","scene","war drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:43"},"ai:427":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":425,"t":["பேரமர் மலர்க்கண் மடந்தை நீயே","காரெதிர் பொழுது என விடல்ஒல் லாயே","போருடை வேந்தன் பாசறை","வாரான் அவனெனச் செலவழுங் கினனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the wide-eyed girl that because she would not let him go, saying this is the season that meets the rains, he gave up the going, sending word that he would not come to the war camp of the fighting king.","n":"The girl's argument was the season (காரெதிர் பொழுது), the same argument the friend uses in 423; here it has worked. Whose 'not coming' வாரான் அவன் reports is open: most simply, the man's refusal of the king's summons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 427","lb":"AI427","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:43"},"ai:428":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":426,"t":["தேர்செல அழுங்கத் திருவில் கோலி","ஆர்கலி எழிலி சோர்தொடங் கின்றே","வேந்துவிடு விழுத்தொழில் ஒழிய","யான் தொடங்கினனால் நிற் புறந்தரவே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his wife that the roaring cloud, bending its fine bow across the sky, has begun to pour so that chariots cannot move; the great task the king sent him on is over, and he has begun to stay and care for her.","n":"The rain does the work of the return: it halts the chariots of war, so the king's task ends because the season forbids it, and the same rain that would have tormented a waiting wife now marks the start of his staying (புறந்தர).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","aalvinai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 428","lb":"AI428","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["எழிலி","எழிலி",2,"pira","scene","rain cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:43"},"ai:429":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":427,"t":["பல்லிருங் கூந்தல் பசப்பு நீவிடின்","செல்வேம் தில்ல யாமே செற்றார்","வெல்கொடி அரணம் முருக்கிய","கல்லா யானை வேந்துபகை வெலற்கே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the woman of thick dark hair that if she puts away her pallor he would go to win the king's war, the king whose untrained elephant shattered the fortress of his foes with its conquering flags.","n":"The condition is one she cannot meet: her pallor comes from his going, so 'if you give up your pallor' (பசப்பு நீ விடின்) makes the journey wait on a cure that only his staying brings. The wish particle தில் keeps the going a wish, not a plan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","vendhan","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 429","lb":"AI429","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene","war elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:43"},"ai:430":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":428,"t":["நெடும்பொறை மிசைய குறுங்கால் கொன்றை","அடர்பொன் என்னச் சுடரிதழ் பகரும்","கான்கெழு நாடன் மகளோ","அழுதல் ஆன்றிசின் அழுங்குவல் செலவே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the daughter of the chief of the wooded land, where on the high rock the short-stemmed konrai spreads bright petals like beaten gold, has wept her fill, and so he gives up the going.","n":"The konrai in bloom is the flower of the rains, the season of return; he gives up going in the very season when a man should come home. ஆன்றிசின் may mean she has wept abundantly or be a plea that she stop weeping; either way her tears decide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 430","lb":"AI430","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",1,"maram","simile","konrai"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:43"},"ai:431":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":429,"t":["நன்றே காதலர் சென்ற ஆறே","அணிநிற இரும்பொறை மீமிசை","மணிநிற உருவின தோகையும் உடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Good indeed is the road my lover took, she says: up on the dark rock of lovely hue it has even peacocks whose bodies are the colour of sapphire.","n":"Every poem of the decade repeats the same first line and ends on உடைத்தே ('it has... too'), so the praise is cumulative: each poem adds one more beauty of the rainy road. Whether the praise is comfort (the road is safe and the season he promised has come) or a reproach (the road is so lovely it keeps him) the refrain does not say; the peacock on the rock belongs to the hills, not the mullai scrub.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 431","lb":"AI431","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தோகையும்","தோகை",3,"pul","scene","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:44"},"ai:432":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":430,"t":["நன்றே காதலர் சென்ற ஆறே","சுடுபொன் அன்ன கொன்றை சூடிக்","கடிபுகு வனர்போல் மள்ளரும் உடைத்தே"],"p":"","r":"Good indeed is the road my lover took: it has even warriors crowned with konrai like heated gold, looking like men entering a wedding house.","n":"The konrai that crowns the men is the rainy season's flower, so the road shows the season even on its people; கடி may be the wedding or a guarded house, and on the first reading the men look festive as if going to a marriage, a hint of the union the season should bring.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 432","lb":"AI432","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",2,"maram","simile","cassia"],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",2,"maram","ornament","cassia"],["மள்ளரும்","மள்ளர்",3,"seythi","simile","warriors"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:44"},"ai:433":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":431,"t":["நன்றே காதலர் சென்ற ஆறே","நீர்ப்பட எழிலி வீசும்","கார்ப்பெயற்கு எதிரிய கானமும் உடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Good indeed is the road my lover took: it has even a forest that has met the rains of the season, which the clouds fling down as water.","n":"கார் is named outright: the season the lover would have promised for his return is on his road, so the praise doubles as notice that the time has come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 433","lb":"AI433","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எழிலி","எழிலி",2,"pira","scene","cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:44"},"ai:434":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":432,"t":["நன்றே காதலர் சென்ற ஆறே","மறியுடை மான்பிணை உகளத்","தண்பெயல் பொழிந்த இன்பமும் உடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Good indeed is the road my lover took: where the doe with her fawn leaps about, it has even the joy of the cool rain that has poured down.","n":"The doe is kept with her fawn (மறியுடை), not with a stag; the joy named in line 3 is the creatures' joy, which the waiting woman does not share.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 434","lb":"AI434","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மறியுடை","மறி",2,"maa","scene","fawn"],["மான்பிணை","மான் பிணை",2,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["தண்பெயல்","பெயல்",3,"pira","scene","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மான்பிணை","confirmed","1/1","a mother and young at play together on his road set before the absent man the home and family he should be back with in this season"]],"ch":"ai:44"},"ai:435":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":433,"t":["நன்றே காதலர் சென்ற ஆறே","நிலன் அணி நெய்தல் மலரப்","பொலன்அணி கொன்றையும் பிடவமும் உடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Good indeed is the road my lover took: while the neythal that adorns the ground blooms, it has even konrai bright as golden jewels, and pidavam.","n":"The neythal is a flower of the shore pools set among mullai flowers: the rain has filled the low ground so that a water flower adorns the land itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","azhagu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 435","lb":"AI435","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","scene","water lily"],["கொன்றையும்","கொன்றை",3,"maram","scene","cassia"],["பிடவமும்","பிடவம்",3,"maram","scene","pidavam"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:44"},"ai:436":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":434,"t":["நன்றே காதலர் சென்ற ஆறே","நன்பொன் அன்ன சுடரிணர்க்","கொன்றையொடு மலர்ந்த குருந்துமா ருடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Good indeed is the road my lover took: it has even kurunthu blooming together with konrai whose bright clusters are like fine gold.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","azhagu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 436","lb":"AI436","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொன்றையொடு","கொன்றை",3,"maram","simile","cassia"],["குருந்துமா","குருந்து",3,"maram","scene","kurunthu"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:44"},"ai:437":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":435,"t":["நன்றே காதலர் சென்ற ஆறே","ஆலித் தண்மழை தலைஇய","வாலிய மலர்ந்த முல்லையும் உடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Good indeed is the road my lover took: after cool rain with hailstones has poured, it has even mullai blooming white.","n":"The flower that names the landscape appears in its own decade only here: the road has become fully mullai, the land of waiting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 437","lb":"AI437","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆலித்","ஆலி",2,"pira","scene","hail"],["முல்லையும்","முல்லை",3,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:44"},"ai:438":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":436,"t":["நன்றே காதலர் சென்ற ஆறே","பைம்புதல் பல் பூ மலர","இன்புறத் தகுந பண்புமார் உடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Good indeed is the road my lover took: with many flowers opening on the green bushes, it has even every quality fit to give delight.","n":"Here the list gives way to a summary (இன்புறத் தகுந பண்பு): the road is fit for joy, and the joy she names is one she does not have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 438","lb":"AI438","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பைம்புதல்","புதல்",2,"maram","scene","bush"],["பூ","பூ",2,"maram","scene","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:44"},"ai:439":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":437,"t":["நன்றே காதலர் சென்ற ஆறே","குருந்தங் கண்ணிக் கோவலர்","பெருந்தண் நிலைய பாக்கமும் உடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Good indeed is the road my lover took: it has even hamlets standing in great coolness, where the herders wear garlands of kurunthu.","n":"The herders are settled in cool hamlets for the rains, men at home in the season when the lover is away; the poem does not draw this out, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 439","lb":"AI439","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருந்தங்","குருந்து",2,"maram","ornament","kurunthu"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",2,"seythi","scene","herders"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:44"},"ai:440":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":438,"t":["நன்றே காதலர் சென்ற ஆறே","தண் பெயல் அளித்த பொழுதின்","ஒண்சுடர்த் தோன்றியும் தளவமும் உடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Good indeed is the road my lover took: at the time the cool rain has granted, it has even thondri bright as flame, and thalavam.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 440","lb":"AI440","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தோன்றியும்","தோன்றி",3,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["தளவமும்","தளவம்",3,"maram","scene","thalavam"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:44"},"ai:441":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":439,"t":["ஐய ஆயின செய்யோள் கிளவி","கார்நாள் உருமொடு கையறப் பிரிந்தென","நோய் நன்கு செய்தன எமக்கே","யாம்உறு துயரம் அவள் அறியினோ நன்றே."],"p":"","r":"In the war camp the man says that the words of his red-complexioned wife have become a thing of wonder to him, for he left her helpless on a day of the rains with its thunder, and they now give him real pain; it would be well if she knew the sorrow he suffers.","n":"The pain runs the other way from the usual mullai poem: it is her parting words that wound the man, and his wish is not to see her but that she should know his suffering, a defence against the charge of neglect.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 441","lb":"AI441","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["உருமொடு","உரும்",2,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:45"},"ai:442":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":440,"t":["பெருஞ்சின வேந்தன் அருந்தொழில் தணியின்","விருந்து நனி பெறுதலும் உரியள் மாதோ","இருண்டு தோன்று விசும்பின் உயர்நிலை உலகத்து","அருந்ததி அனைய கற்பின்","குரும்பை மணிப்பூண் புதல்வன் தாயே."],"p":"","r":"If the fierce king's hard campaign subsides, the man says, his wife will be fit to receive guests in plenty, she whose chastity is like Arundhati's in the high world of the dark sky, the mother of a son who wears a gem ornament shaped like a young palm fruit.","n":"Homecoming is pictured not as embrace but as the household's hospitality restored: the wife's role as hostess of guests waits on the king's war.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","karpu","vendhan","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 442","lb":"AI442","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குரும்பை","குரும்பை",5,"maram","ornament","palm bud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:45"},"ai:443":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":441,"t":["நனிசேத்து என்னாது நல்தேர் ஏறிச்சென்று","இலங்கு நிலவின் இளம்பிறை போலக்","காண்குவம் தில்அவள் கவின்பெறு சுடர்நுதல்","விண்ணுயர் அரண்பல வெளவிய","மண்ணுறு முரசின் வேந்துதொழில் விடினே."],"p":"","r":"If the king, whose earth-smeared drum has taken many forts rising to the sky, leaves off his work, the man says, then without calling the way too long he will mount the good chariot and go to see her lovely bright brow, like the young crescent of the shining moon.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 443","lb":"AI443","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["நல்தேர்","தேர்",1,"pira","scene","chariot"],["முரசின்","முரசு",5,"parai","scene","war drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:45"},"ai:444":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":442,"t":["பெருந்தோள் மடவரல் காண்குவெம் தில்ல","நீண்மதில் அரணம் பாய்ந்தெனத் தொடிபிளந்து","வைந்நுதி மழுகிய தடங்கோட்டு யானை","வென்வேல் வேந்தன் பகைதணிந்து","இன்னும் தன்னாட்டு முன்னுதல் பெறினே."],"p":"","r":"We shall see the simple girl of broad shoulders, the man says, if the king of the victorious spear, his enmity calmed, may yet head back to his own land, the king whose great-tusked elephants have split their tusk-rings and blunted their sharp points charging the long walls of forts.","n":"The blunted tusks measure how long the siege has lasted: the elephant's wear is the man's waiting made visible.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 444","lb":"AI444","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:45"},"ai:445":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":443,"t":["புகழ்சால் சிறப்பின் காதலி புலம்பத்","துறந்து வந்தனையே அருந்தொழில் கட்டூர்","நல்லேறு தழீஇ நாகுபெயர் காலை","உள்ளுதொறும் கலிழும் நெஞ்சம்","வல்லே எம்மையும் வர இழைத்தனையே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart, which weeps each time it remembers her at the hour when the young cow comes home close by the good bull, that it left the famed and excellent beloved to lament and came to this camp of hard work, and has quickly drawn him to come as well.","n":"The cattle going home together at evening are what the camp-bound man sees, and they set off the heart's weeping; the poem blames the heart both for leaving her and for its present grief.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","ninaivu","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 445","lb":"AI445","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நல்லேறு","நல் ஏறு",3,"maa","iraicchi","bull"],["நாகு","நாகு",3,"maa","iraicchi","young cow"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","நாகு","confirmed","1/1","even the cattle go home together at dusk while the man stays in the camp away from his wife"]],"ch":"ai:45"},"ai:446":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":444,"t":["முல்லை நாறும் கூந்தல் கமழ் கொள","நல்ல காண்குவ மாஅ யோயே","பாசறை அருந்தொழில் உதவிநம்","காதல்நன் னாட்டுப் போதரும் பொழுதே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his dark wife, absent, that they will see good things when, having helped the hard work of the war camp, they come back to their beloved good country, and her mullai-scented hair breathes its fragrance.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 446","lb":"AI446","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",1,"maram","ornament","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:45"},"ai:447":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":445,"t":["பிணிவீடு பெறுக மன்னவன் தொழிலே","பனிவளர் தளவின் சிரல்வாய்ச் செம்முகை","யாடு சிறைவண்டு அழிப்பப்","பாடல் சான்ற காண்கம்வாள் நுதலே."],"p":"","r":"May the king's work be released from its bond, the man prays, and then they will see the bright brow worthy of song, at the hour when the winged bee works open the red bud, shaped like a kingfisher's beak, of the thalavu that grows in the dew.","n":"The bee prising open the jasmine bud is the evening of the rains and may hint at the reunion he waits for; it carries no act of the lovers, so it is scene, not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vendhan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 447","lb":"AI447","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தளவின்","தளவு",2,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"],["சிரல்வாய்ச்","சிரல் வாய்",2,"pul","scene","kingfisher"],["சிறைவண்டு","சிறை வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:45"},"ai:448":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":446,"t":["தழங்குரல் முரசம் காலை இயம்பக்","கடுஞ்சின வேந்தன் தொழில் எதிர்ந் தனனே.","மெல்லவல் மருங்கின் முல்லை பூப்பப்","பொங்குபெயல் கனைதுளி கார் எதிர்ந் தன்றே","அஞ்சில் ஓதியை உள்ளுதொறும்","துஞ்சாது அலமரல் நாமெதிர்ந் தனமே."],"p":"","r":"The drum of deep voice sounds in the morning and the fierce king has taken up his work; the mullai blooms at the edge of the soft low field and the rains with thick heavy drops have come; and, the man says, thinking each time of her with her few fine tresses, he has met sleepless anguish.","n":"Three parallel clauses end on the same verb (எதிர்ந்தனனே, எதிர்ந்தன்றே, எதிர்ந்தனமே): the king meets his war, the season meets the land, the man meets sleeplessness, so the war and the rains arrive together as his double trial.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","iravu","ninaivu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 448","lb":"AI448","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["முரசம்","முரசம்",1,"parai","scene","war drum"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",3,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:45"},"ai:449":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":447,"t":["முரம்புகண் உடையத் திரியும் திகிரியொடு","பணைநிலை முணைஇய வயமாப் புணர்ந்து","திண்ணிதின் மாண்டன்று தேரே","ஒள்நுதல் காண்குவம் வேந்துவினை முடினே."],"p":"","r":"The chariot is ready and strongly made, its wheels rolling so that the gravel ground breaks, yoked to strong horses weary of standing in the stall; we shall see her bright brow, the man says, if the king finishes his work.","n":"Everything is ready but the king: the horses tired of the stall share the man's impatience.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vendhan","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 449","lb":"AI449","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வயமாப்","வய மா",2,"maa","scene","horse"],["தேரே","தேர்",3,"pira","scene","chariot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:45"},"ai:450":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":448,"t":["முரசுமாறு இரட்டும் அருந்தொழில் பகைதணிந்து","நாடுமுன் னியரோ பீடுகெழ வேந்தன்","வெய்ய உயிர்க்கு நோய்தணியச்","செய்யோள் இளமுலைப் படீஇயர்என் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"May the glorious king, his enmity calmed after the hard work where drums beat in turn, think of his own land, the man prays, so that his eyes may sleep on the young breasts of his red-complexioned wife and the sickness of his hot sighs abate.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","vendhan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 450","lb":"AI450","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["முரசுமாறு","முரசு",1,"parai","scene","war drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:45"},"ai:451":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":449,"t":["கார்செய் காலையொடு கையறப் பிரிந்தோர்","தேர்தரு விருந்தில் தவிர்குதல் யாவது","மாற்றருந் தானை நோக்கி","ஆற்றவும் இருத்தல் வேந்தனது தொழிலே."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks why the men who left her helpless as the rainy season's morning came should keep back from the feast of welcome their chariot would bring, since holding out in patience, watching over an army no one can turn back, is the king's work and not theirs.","n":"இருத்தல், the mullai word for her own patient waiting, is here handed to the king (ஆற்றவும் இருத்தல் வேந்தனது தொழிலே): she argues that endurance in the camp belongs to his duty, so her husband's share of the war ended when the rains came.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 451","lb":"AI451","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:46"},"ai:452":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":450,"t":["வறந்த ஞாலம் தளிர்ப்ப வீசிக்","கறங்குரல் எழிலி கார்செய் தன்றே","பகைவெங் காதலர் திறைதரு முயற்சி","மெல்தோள் ஆய்கவின் மறையப்","பொன்புனை பீரத்து அலர்செய் தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The roaring cloud has spread so the parched earth puts out shoots and has made the rainy season, she says, while her war-loving husband's effort to win tribute has made the pallor bloom like the gold of peerkku flowers on her soft shoulders and hidden their fine beauty.","n":"Two clauses close on the same verb, கார்செய் தன்றே and அலர்செய் தன்றே: the cloud makes the season, his campaign makes her pallor. அலர் is both blossom and the town's talk, so the bloom on her shoulders is also what people will say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 452","lb":"AI452","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["எழிலி","எழிலி",2,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["பீரத்து","பீர்",5,"maram","scene","ridge gourd flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:46"},"ai:453":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":451,"t":["அவல்தொறும் தேரை தெவிட்ட மிசைதொறும்","வெங்குரல் புள்ளினம் ஒலிப்ப உதுக்காண்","கார்தொடங் கின்றால் காலை அதனால்","நீர்தொடங் கினவால் நெடுங்கணவர்","தேர்தொடங் கின்றால் நம்வயினானே."],"p":"","r":"Frogs crowd every hollow and harsh-voiced flocks of birds cry on every height: look, she says, the rains have begun this morning, and so my long eyes have begun to run with water, and his chariot has begun its way toward us.","n":"Three clauses on தொடங்கு tie the season, her tears and his chariot into one beginning. Whether the tears are of grief or of relief turns on how line 4 is divided, and the poem lets both stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","kan","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 453","lb":"AI453","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தேரை","தேரை",1,"pira","scene","frog"],["புள்ளினம்","புள்",2,"pul","scene","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:46"},"ai:454":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":452,"t":["தளவின் பைங்கொடி தழீஇப் பையென","நிலவின் அன்ன நேரும்பு பேணிக்","கார்நயந்து எய்தும் முல்லை அவர்","தேர்நயந்து உறையும் என் மாமைக் கவினே."],"p":"","r":"As the mullai, clasping the green thalavam creeper and slowly tending its even buds bright as moonlight, reaches out for the rains it longs for, so the beauty of her dark skin stays waiting, longing for his chariot.","n":"The comparison uses the mullai's longing and leaves out one thing it shows: the mullai waits clasped to a partner creeper (தழீஇ), which she does not have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","azhagu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 454","lb":"AI454","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தளவின்","தளவம்",1,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",3,"maram","simile","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:46"},"ai:455":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":453,"t":["அரசுபகை தணிய முரசுபடச் சினை இ","ஆர்குரல் எழிலி கார்தொடங் கின்றே","அளியவோ அளிய தாமே ஒளிபசந்து","மின்னிழை ஞெகிழச் சாஅய்த்","தொன்னலம் இழந்த என் தடமெல் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"So that the kings' war may cool, the loud cloud, raging with a war drum's sound, has begun the rains, she says, and pitiable, pitiable indeed are her broad soft shoulders, their glow turned pale, the bright bangles slipping, wasted and stripped of their old beauty.","n":"The rain itself ends the campaign (அரசுபகை தணிய): the season that should free him has come, so the pity she spends on her own shoulders is also a charge that he is late.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","melivu","paruvam","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 455","lb":"AI455","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["எழிலி","எழிலி",2,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["முரசு","முரசு",1,"parai","scene","war drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:46"},"ai:456":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":454,"t":["உள்ளார் கொல்லோ தோழி வெள்ளிதழ்ப்","பகல்மதி உருவைல் பகன்றை மாமலர்","வெண்கொடி ஈங்கை பைம்புதல் அணியும்","அரும்பனி அளை இய கூதிர்","ஒருங்கிவண் உறைதல் தெளித்து அகன்றோரே."],"p":"","r":"She asks her friend whether they have stopped thinking of us, those who assured us we would live here together through the cold season of hard dew and went away, now that the great white-petalled pakanrai flowers, like the moon by day, on white creepers deck the green eengai bushes.","n":"The season named is கூதிர், the cold season, which the Tholkappiyam gives to kurinji; this decade carries the mullai wait past the rains into the dews, so his promise has already overrun its time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","vaaimai","pirivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 456","lb":"AI456","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பகன்றை","பகன்றை",2,"maram","scene","pakanrai creeper"],["ஈங்கை","ஈங்கை",3,"maram","scene","eengai bush"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:46"},"ai:457":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":455,"t":["பெய் பனி நலிய உய்தல்செல் லாது","குருகினம் நரலும் பிரிவருங் காலைத்","துறந்து அமைகல்லார் காதலர்","மறந்தமை கல்லாது என் மடங்கெழு நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"In this season hard to be apart in, when flocks of herons cry out, unable to escape the falling dew that torments them, she tells her simple heart that her lover cannot leave her and rest content, and it cannot forget him and rest.","n":"The two halves turn on the same verb, அமைகல்லார் and அமைகல்லாது: neither he nor her heart can settle. The crying herons were not tagged iraicchi: they suffer and cry, which carries a mood, but do nothing a person of the situation does or should do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu","pirivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 457","lb":"AI457","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","nenju",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருகினம்","குருகு",2,"pul","scene","herons"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:46"},"ai:458":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":456,"t":["துணர்க்காய்க் கொன்றைக் குழற்பழம் ஊழ்த்தன","அதிர் பெயற்கு எதிரிய சிதர்கொள் தண்மலர்","பாணர் பெருமகன் பிரிந்தென","மாண்நலம் இழந்தஎன் கண்போன் றனவே."],"p":"","r":"The kondrai's clustered pods have ripened into long pipe-like fruit, and its cool flowers that met the thundering rain lie wet and scattered: they look like her eyes, which lost their fine beauty when the lord of the bards went away.","n":"The kondrai is the mullai sign that the rains, and so his promised return, have come; here it has already flowered, fruited and been beaten down, so it marks how far past the promise the season has run. The hero is named by பாணர் பெருமகன், a marutham epithet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","nalam","pirivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 458","lb":"AI458","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கொன்றைக்","கொன்றை",1,"maram","simile","laburnum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:46"},"ai:459":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":457,"t":["மெலிறைப் பணைத்தோள் பசலை தீரப்","புல்லவும் இயைவது கொல்லோ புல்லார்","அரண்க டந்த சீர்கெழு தானை","வெல்போர் வேந்தனொடு சென்ற","நல்வய லூரன் நறுந்தண் மார்பே."],"p":"","r":"She wonders whether she will be able to embrace, so that the pallor leaves her soft, bamboo-round shoulders, the fragrant cool chest of the man of the town of good fields, who went with the victorious king whose glorious army crossed the forts of his enemies.","n":"The cure for pallor is not his return in general but touch (புல்லவும்); the doubt (இயைவது கொல்லோ) is whether the king's war will allow it. He is called ஊரன், the marutham man, though the decade is mullai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","punarchi","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 459","lb":"AI459","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:46"},"ai:460":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":458,"t":["பெருஞ்சின வேந்தனும் பாசறை முனியான்","இருங்கலி வெற்பன் தூதும் தோன்றா","ததை இலை வாழை முழுமுதல் அசைய","இன்னா வாடையும் அலைக்கும்","என்ஆகு வன்கொல் அளியென் யானே."],"p":"","r":"The fiercely angry king does not tire of his war camp, no message comes from the lord of the loud hill, and the cruel north wind beats on her too, shaking the plantain with its crowded leaves to its whole trunk: what will become of her, she asks, pitiable as she is.","n":"The plantain shaken to the root by the north wind matches her state, but only as a single match of mood, so it is scene, not ullurai. The hero is a வெற்பன், a hill man, and the plantain is a kurinji plant, set in a mullai wait.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","vendhan","pirivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 460","lb":"AI460","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வாழை","வாழை",3,"maram","scene","plantain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:46"},"ai:461":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":459,"t":["வான்பிசிர்க் கருவியின் பிடவுமுகை தகையக்","கான்பிசிர் கற்பக் கார்தொடங் கின்றே","இனையல் வாழி தோழி எனையதூஉம்","நின்துறந்து அமைகுவர் அல்லர்","வெற்றி வேந்தன் பாசறை யோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine that the rains have begun, the pidavam buds made lovely by the spray of massed clouds and the forest taking on the drizzle, so she must not grieve: the men in the war camp of the victorious king will not rest content having left her.","n":"The friend names the absent men only by their place, the camp of a victorious king (வெற்றி வேந்தன் பாசறை): the comfort is that a winning war ends, so the season and the campaign close together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","thelivu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 461","lb":"AI461","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பிடவுமுகை","பிடவு முகை",1,"maram","scene","pidavam bud"],["கருவியின்","கருவி",1,"pira","scene","cloud mass"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:47"},"ai:462":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":460,"t":["ஏதில பெய்ம்மழை காரென மயங்கிய","பேதையம் கொன்றைக் கோதைநிலை நோக்கி","எவன்இனி மடந்தைநின் கலிழ்வே நின்வயின்","தகையெழில் வாட்டுநர் அல்லர்","முகையவிழ் புறவின் நாடிறந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the girl why she still weeps on seeing the foolish konrai, which took a stray shower for the rains and put out its garlands: those who crossed the woodland of opening buds are not men to let her beauty fade.","n":"The friend does not deny that the konrai has flowered; she denies that the flowering is evidence, calling the tree பேதை, simple, for mistaking an unseasonal shower (ஏதில பெய்ம்மழை) for the true rains. The promise is saved by discrediting the sign.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pedhaimai","nalam","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 462","lb":"AI462","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கொன்றைக்","கொன்றை",2,"maram","scene","konrai"],["பெய்ம்மழை","மழை",1,"pira","scene","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ai:47"},"ai:463":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":461,"t":["புதன்மிசை நறுமலர் கவின்பெறத் தொடரிநின்","நலமிகு கூந்தல் தகைகொளப் புனைய","வாராது அமையலோ இலரே நேரார்","நாடுபடு நன்கலம் தரீஇயர்","நீடினர் தோழிநம் காத லோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says their lovers will surely come to string the fragrant flowers of the bushes, made lovely by the season, into her fine hair; they have only stayed on to bring back the rich treasure that falls to them from the enemies' land.","n":"The delay is given an honourable cause, the spoil of the enemies' land (நேரார் நாடுபடு நன்கலம்), and the return is pictured as an act of adornment, the man dressing her hair. தொடரி may be the thodari shrub whose flowers he will bring, or 'having strung'; the sense of the line turns on which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","selvam","azhagu","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 463","lb":"AI463","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நறுமலர்","நறு மலர்",1,"maram","ornament","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:47"},"ai:464":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":462,"t":["கண்ணெனக் கருவிளை மலரப் பொன்னென","இவர்கொடிப் பீரம் இரும்புதல் மலரும்","அற்சிரம் மறக்குநர் அல்லர்நின்","நல்தோள் மருவரற்கு உலமரு வோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that in the cold season, when the karuvilai opens like eyes and the climbing peerkku vine flowers like gold on the dark bushes, the men who ache to embrace her fine shoulders will not forget the time.","n":"The two similes run backwards from the usual: the flowers are likened to an eye and to gold, the very things the waiting woman's eyes and pallor are elsewhere likened to, so the hedge in bloom already shows her face.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","ninaivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 464","lb":"AI464","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கருவிளை","கருவிளை",1,"maram","simile","karuvilai"],["பீரம்","பீரம்",2,"maram","simile","ridge gourd"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:47"},"ai:465":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":463,"t":["நீர்இகுவு அன்ன நிமிர்பரி நெடுந்தேர்","கார்செய் கானம் பிற்படக் கடைஇ","மயங்கு மலர் அகலம் நீஇனிது முயங்க","வருவர் வாழி தோழி","செருவெம் குருசில் தணிந்தனன் பகையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend promises that he will come, driving his tall chariot, swift as falling water, through the rain-made forest so that she may embrace his chest with its tangled flowers; the war-loving chief's enmity has cooled.","n":"The last line gives the reason after the promise: the return depends on the chief's anger being spent (தணிந்தனன் பகையே), so the lover's time is the king's time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","punarchi","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 465","lb":"AI465","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மலர்","மலர்",3,"maram","ornament","garland flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:47"},"ai:466":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":464,"t":["வேந்துவிடு விழுத்தொழில் எய்தி ஏந்துகோட்டு","அண்ணல் யானை அரசுவிடுத்து இனியே","எண்ணிய நாள்அகம் வருதல் பெண்ணியல்","காமர் சுடர்நுதல் விளங்கும்","தேமொழி அரிவை தெளிந்திசின் யானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the sweet-spoken girl with the bright brow that the man has done the great work the king sent him on and the king of the tall-tusked elephant has let him go, so she is sure he will come within the counted days.","n":"The friend's comfort is not the season but news: the task is done and the king has released him, so she reasons from the court's business to the date. 'Counted days' (எண்ணிய நாள்) implies the wife has been reckoning them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","vendhan","aalvinai","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 466","lb":"AI466","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:47"},"ai:467":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":465,"t":["புனைஇழை நெகிழச் சாஅய் நொந்துநொந்து","இனையல் வாழியோ இகுளை வினைவயின்","சென்றோர் நீடினர் பெரிதெனத் தங்காது","நம்மினும் விரையும் என்ப","வெம்முரண் யானை விறல்போர் வேந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend begs the girl not to waste away grieving again and again till her ornaments slip: they say the king of fierce-horned elephants, victorious in war, thinking that those who went on his work have been gone too long, hurries more than they do themselves.","n":"The comfort is reported (என்ப, they say) and puts the king on the waiting women's side: he is more eager than we are. The friend borrows the court's urgency to quiet the house's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","vendhan","aalvinai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 467","lb":"AI467","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:47"},"ai:468":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":466,"t":["வரிநுணல் கறங்கத் தேரை தெவிட்டக்","கார்தொடங் கின்றே காலை இனிநின்","நேர்இறை பணைத்தோட்கு ஆர்விருந் தாக","வடி மணி நெடுந்தேர் கடைஇ","வருவர் இன்றுநம் காத லோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the rains have begun this morning, striped frogs croaking and toads swarming, and that today their lover will drive here in his tall chariot with its well-made bells, to be a full feast for her even-jointed, bamboo-full shoulders.","n":"The image of the lover as a guest's feast for her shoulders turns the wife into the host and the returning husband into a guest, the mullai wife's virtue of hospitality applied to her own reunion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","punarchi","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 468","lb":"AI468","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["நுணல்","நுணல்",1,"pira","scene","frog"],["தேரை","தேரை",1,"pira","scene","toad"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:47"},"ai:469":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":467,"t":["பைந்தினை உணங்கல் செம்பூழ் கவரும்","வன்புல நாடன் தரீஇய வலன்ஏர்ப்பு","அம்கண் இருவிசும்பு அதிர ஏறொடு","பெயல்தொடங் கின்றே வானம்","காண்குவம் வம்மோ பூங்க ணோயே."],"p":"","r":"The friend calls the flower-eyed girl to come and look: the sky has begun to rain, rising on the right with thunder that shakes the wide heavens, to bring the lord of the hard country where the red quail steal the green millet set out to dry.","n":"The rain does not merely mark the season; it rises on the right (வலன்ஏர்ப்பு), the auspicious side, in order to bring him (தரீஇய), so the friend presents the cloud as the man's escort and its rightward rising as an omen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 469","lb":"AI469","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தினை","தினை",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["பூழ்","பூழ்",1,"pul","scene","quail"],["ஏறொடு","ஏறு",3,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:47"},"ai:470":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":468,"t":["இருநிலம் குளிர்ப்ப வீசி அல்கலும்","அரும்பனி அளை இய அற்சிரக் காலை","உள்ளார் காதல ராயின் ஒள்ளிழை","சிறப்பொடு விளங்கிய காட்சி","மறக்க விடுமோநின் மாமைக் கவினே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the bright-jewelled girl whether, even if her lover forgot her, her dark-gold beauty, shining in its excellence, would let him forget her in this cold-dew season when the wind blows every night chilling the wide earth with hard dew.","n":"The friend concedes the worst case (உள்ளார் காதலர் ஆயின், if he does not think of us) and moves the guarantee from the man's memory to the woman's beauty: it will not let him forget.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","azhagu","ninaivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 470","lb":"AI470","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அரும்பனி","பனி",2,"pira","scene","dew"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:47"},"ai:471":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":469,"t":["எவ்வளை நெகிழ மேனி வாடப்","பல்லிதல் உண்கண் பனி அலைக் கலங்கத்","துறந்தோன் மன்ற மறங்கெழு குருசில்","அதுமற்று உணர்ந்தனை போலாய்","இன்னும் வருதி என்அவர் தகவே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells the bard that the valiant lord has surely abandoned her, so that her bangles slip, her body withers and her painted many-petalled eyes are clouded with floods of tears; the bard seems not to grasp it and keeps on coming, and she asks what sort of decency that is of theirs.","n":"The reproach is double: the lord is called மறங்கெழு குருசில், great in valour, in the same breath as the man who deserted her, and the bard's very persistence in coming is turned into evidence against the party he serves. The plural அவர் lumps bard and master together.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 471","lb":"AI471","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["எவ்வளை","வளை",1,"pira","ornament","bangles"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:48"},"ai:472":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":470,"t":["கைவல் சீறியாழ் பாண நுமரே","செய்த பருவம் வந்துநின் றதுவே","எம்மின் உணரா ராயினும் தம்வயின்","பொய்படு கிளவி நாணலும்","எய்யார் ஆகுதல் நோகோ யானே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells the bard of the skilled small lute that the season his people fixed has come and stands here; even if they do not learn of it from her, what pains her is that they feel no shame for the false word on their own side.","n":"She does not ask for his return; she grieves that the promise-breaker is not ashamed. The season standing (வந்துநின்றது) is itself the accusation, so she need not speak it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","vaaimai","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 472","lb":"AI472","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["சீறியாழ்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","scene","small lute"],["பாண","பாணன்",1,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:48"},"ai:473":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":471,"t":["பலர்புகழ் சிறப்பின் நும் குருசில் உள்ளிச்","செலவுநீ நயந்தனை யாயின் மன்ற","இன்னா அரும்படர் எம்வயின் செய்த","பொய்வ லாளர் போலக்","கைவல் பாணஎம் மறாவா தீமே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells the skilled bard that if, thinking of his much-praised lord, he wants to go back to him, then he should not abandon her the way the liars did who brought her this bitter, hard longing.","n":"The bard has been staying with her and now wants to rejoin his master; she fears he too will turn out one of the பொய்வலாளர், the men skilled in lies. Whether மறாவாதீமே means 'do not forget us' or 'do not refuse us' is not settled by the text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","vaaimai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 473","lb":"AI473","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாண","பாணன்",5,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:48"},"ai:474":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":472,"t":["மையறு சுடர்நுதல் விளங்கக் கறுத்தோர்","செய்யரண் சிதைத்த செருமிகு தானையொடு","கதழ் பரி நெடுந்தேர் அதர்படக் கடைஇச்","சென்றவர்த் தருகுவல் என்னும்","நன்றால் அம்ம பாணனது அறிவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine that the bard says he will bring back the absent man, driving his tall chariot with swift horses so hard that the roads are cut, with his battle-strong army that wrecked the forts of his foes, so that her flawless bright forehead may shine again; fine indeed, she says, is the bard's sense.","n":"நன்றால் அம்ம may be plain praise of the bard's comfort or dry irony at a promise he cannot keep; the decade's other poems, which doubt the bard, lean toward the second. The war matter of lines 2 and 3 tells us the man is away on a king's campaign, the usual mullai absence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","padai","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 474","lb":"AI474","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தானையொடு","தானை",2,"seythi","scene","army"],["பரி","பரி",3,"maa","scene","horse"],["பாணனது","பாணன்",5,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:48"},"ai:475":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":473,"t":["நொடிநிலை கலங்க வாடிய தோளும்","வடிநலன் இழந்தஎன் கண்ணும் நோக்கிப்","பெரிது புலம்பினனே சீறியாழ்ப் பாணன்","எம்வெம் காதலொடு பிரிந்தோர்","தம்மோன் போலான் பேரன் பினனே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that the bard of the small lute, seeing her shoulders withered so that they lost their firmness and her eyes robbed of their shapely beauty, grieved greatly; he is not like his master, who left her for all her ardent love; the bard has the greater love.","n":"The servant is set against the master by the master's own woman: the bard's pity becomes the measure of the lord's want of it. The word read here as நொடிநிலை is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["melivu","anbu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 475","lb":"AI475","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["சீறியாழ்ப்","யாழ்",3,"yaazh","scene","small lute"],["பாணன்","பாணன்",3,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:48"},"ai:476":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":474,"t":["கருவி வானம் கார்சிறந்து ஆர்ப்ப","பருவம் செய்தன பைங்கொடி முல்லை","பல்லான் கோவலர் படலைக் கூட்டும்","அன்புஇல் மாலையும் உடைத்தோ","அன்பு இல் பாண அவர்சென்ற நாடே."],"p":"","r":"Now that the massed clouds roar at the height of the rains and the green mullai creeper has flowered for its season, the heroine asks the loveless bard whether the land where his master went also has this loveless evening, when herdsmen with many cows put together their leaf garlands.","n":"அன்பு இல் is used twice, once of the evening and once of the bard, so the hour and the messenger stand on the same side against her. The herdsmen coming in at dusk do what the absent man does not; they set the hour but are not tagged as iraicchi because the poem's question does not rest on their act.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","paruvam","anbu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 476","lb":"AI476","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வானம்","வானம்",1,"pira","scene","cloudy sky"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["பல்லான்","ஆன்",3,"maa","scene","cattle"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",3,"seythi","scene","herdsmen"],["படலைக்","படலை",3,"maram","ornament","leaf garland"],["பாண","பாணன்",5,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:48"},"ai:477":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":475,"t":["பனிமலர் நெடுங்கண் பசலை பாயத்","துனி மலி துயரமொடு அரும்படர் உழப்போள்","கையறு நெஞ்சிற்கு உயவுத்துணை யாகச்","சிறுவரைத் தங்குவை யாயின்","காண்குவை மன்ஆல் பாணஎம் தேரே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the bard that if he stays a little while as comforting company for the helpless heart of her whose cool flower-like long eyes are spreading with pallor, who labours in grief full of bitterness and hard longing, he will soon see the man's chariot.","n":"Here the decade turns to the man's side: the bard's staying with the woman, which she resented in 471, is his master's instruction, and the chariot is promised as the bard's reward.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","thoothu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 477","lb":"AI477","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பனிமலர்","மலர்",1,"maram","scene","flower"],["பாண","பாணன்",5,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:48"},"ai:478":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":476,"t":["நீடினம் என்று கொடுமை தூற்றி","வாடிய நுதல ளாகிப் பிறிதுநினைந்து","யாம்வெம் காதலி நோய்மிகச் சாஅய்ச்","சொல்லியது உரைமதி நீயே","முல்லை நல்யாழ்ப் பாணமற்று எமக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The man asks the bard of the fine lute in the mullai mode to tell him what his beloved said, she who, saying he had stayed away too long, spread the word of his cruelty, her forehead withered, imagining something else, wasted by her great sickness.","n":"He asks to hear the charge against himself: கொடுமை தூற்றி, she broadcast his cruelty. பிறிதுநினைந்து may mean she suspected another woman or simply feared the worst.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kaamanoy","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 478","lb":"AI478","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",5,"yaazh","scene","mullai mode"],["நல்யாழ்ப்","யாழ்",5,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["பாண","பாணன்",5,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:48"},"ai:479":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":477,"t":["சொல்லுமதி மாண சொல்லுதோறு இனிய","நாடிடை விலங்கிய எம்வயின் நாள்தொறும்","அரும்பனி கலந்த அருளில் வாடை","தனிமை எள்ளும் பொழுதில்","பனிமலர்க் கண்ணி கூறியது எமக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The man asks the excellent bard to tell him again, since it is sweet each time it is told, what the woman of cool flower-like eyes said, now that he is held off by the lands between and every day the merciless north wind, thick with heavy dew, mocks his loneliness.","n":"The wind mocks his solitude, not hers: the man is shown suffering the season too. Asking for the report again and again makes the message a substitute for return.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","inchol","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 479","lb":"AI479","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["பனிமலர்க்","மலர்",5,"maram","scene","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:48"},"ai:480":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":478,"t":["நினக்குயாம் பாணரும் அல்லேம் எமக்கு","நீயும் குருசிலை யல்லை மாதோ","நின்வெம் காதலி தனிமனைப் புலம்பி","ஈரிதழ் உண்கண் உகுத்த","பூசல் கேட்டு மருளா தோயே."],"p":"","r":"The bard tells the man that they are no longer his bards and he is no longer their lord, since he is not shaken on hearing the cry of his beloved, who laments in her lonely house and sheds tears from her moist-lidded painted eyes.","n":"The bond of patron and bard is formally dissolved over the man's treatment of his wife. It closes a decade that opened with the wife counting the bard among the man's party: in the end he sides with her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","thoothu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 480","lb":"AI480","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["paanan","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாணரும்","பாணன்",1,"seythi","scene","bards"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:48"},"ai:481":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":479,"t":["சாய்இறைப் பணைத்தோள் அவ்வரி அல்குல்","சேயிழை மாதரை உள்ளி நோய்விட","முள் இட்டு ஊர்மதி வலவநின்","புள் இயல் கலிமாப் பூண்ட தேரே."],"p":"","r":"Thinking of the woman of red jewels, with shoulders like bamboo sloping at the joint and a lovely lined waist, the man tells his charioteer to put the goad to the bird-swift proud horses yoked to his chariot and drive, so that the sickness may leave.","n":"The goad (முள், a thorn) is asked for outright: the horses are to be hurt so that a sickness ends, and the poem does not say whose sickness (நோய்), his longing or hers, so the one cure serves both. The image of the horses as birds returns word for word in 486.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 481","lb":"AI481","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பணைத்தோள்","பணை",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["புள்","புள்",4,"pul","scene","bird"],["கலிமாப்","கலிமா",4,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:49"},"ai:482":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":480,"t":["தெரியிழை அரிவைக்குப் பெருவிருந் தாக","வல்விரைத்து கடவுமதி பாகவெள்வேல்","வென்றடு தானை வேந்தனொடு","நாளிடைச் சேப்பின் ஊழியின் நெடிதே."],"p":"","r":"Drive very fast, charioteer, so that I come as a great guest to the woman of chosen jewels: to stay even one day more with the king whose white-speared army wins and slays would be longer than an age.","n":"The man names himself the feast (பெருவிருந்து) he will be for his wife, turning the duty of the house to receive guests into the joy of his own return. The king's victory is granted in one line and then measured against a single day, which becomes an aeon (ஊழி): royal service is honoured only as long as it lasts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","vendhan","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 482","lb":"AI482","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:49"},"ai:483":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":481,"t":["ஆறுவனப்பு எய்த அலர்தா யினவே","வேந்துவிட் டனனே மாவிரைந் தனவே","முன்னுறக் கடவுமதி பாக","நன்னுதல் அரிவை தன்னலம் பெறவே."],"p":"","r":"The roads have spread with blossom and grown lovely, the king has let us go, the horses are racing; drive on ahead, charioteer, so that the woman of fair brow may have her beauty back.","n":"Three short clauses, each closed with ஏ, give the release in steps: the season, the king, the horses, and only then the order. Her beauty (தன்னலம்) is what separation took and what his arrival restores, so the drive is a cure for her body as much as a homecoming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","vendhan","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 483","lb":"AI483","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அலர்","அலர்",1,"maram","scene","blossom"],["மாவிரைந்","மா",2,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:49"},"ai:484":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":482,"t":["வேனில் நீங்கக் கார்மழை தலைஇக்","காடுகவின் கொண்டன்று பொழுது பாடுசிறந்து","கடியக் கடவுமதி பாக","நெடிய நீடினம் நேரிழை மறந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Summer has gone, the rain clouds have poured, the forest has taken on its beauty and the season is at its full; drive swiftly, charioteer, for we have stayed far too long, forgetting her of fine jewels.","n":"The man accuses himself: மறந்தே, 'having forgotten', is the charge an abandoned wife makes, here in the husband's own mouth. பாடுசிறந்து may be the swelling sound of the rain or the season reaching its height; either way the land's fullness is the measure of his delay.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 484","lb":"AI484","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கார்மழை","மழை",1,"pira","scene","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:49"},"ai:485":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":483,"t":["அரும்படர் அவலம் அவளும் தீரப்","பெருந்தோள் நலம்வர யாமும் முயங்க","ஏமதி வலவ தேரே","மா மருண்டு உகளும் மலரணிப் புறவே."],"p":"","r":"So that her hard pining grief may end and I may embrace her great shoulders as their beauty comes back, drive the chariot, charioteer, across the flower-decked scrub where the deer leap startled.","n":"அவளும் ... யாமும் sets two griefs side by side: her pining ends and his embrace begins, so the return is said to be needed by both. ஏமதி வலவ தேரே is a stock half-line of the returning man (it opens Natrinai 21's order too), and the startled beasts are the land the chariot breaks into, not a sign.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","nalam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 485","lb":"AI485","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மா","மா",4,"maa","scene","deer"],["மலரணிப்","மலர்",4,"maram","scene","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:49"},"ai:486":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":484,"t":["பெரும்புன் மாலை ஆனாது நினைஇ","அரும்படர் உழத்தல் யாவது என்றும்","புல்லி ஆற்றாப் புரையோள் காண","வள்பு தெரிந்து ஊர்மதி வலவநின்","புள்ளியல் கலைமாப் பூண்டதேரே."],"p":"","r":"Why should she suffer hard pining, thinking without pause through the great dreary evening? So that the noble woman who could never have enough of embracing may see us, drive, charioteer, handling the reins with skill, your chariot yoked with bird-swift horses.","n":"Line 3 (புல்லி ஆற்றாப் புரையோள்) may say she could never be sated with embracing, or that she cannot bear the absence even after embraces; the reading keeps the first. The last line repeats 481's horses but writes கலைமா (stag) where 481 has கலிமா (proud horse); as yoked to the chariot they are horses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","ninaivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 486","lb":"AI486","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புள்ளியல்","புள்",5,"pul","scene","bird"],["கலைமாப்","கலைமா",5,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:49"},"ai:487":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":485,"t":["இதுமன் பிரிந்தோர் உள்ளும் பொழுதே","செறிதொடி உள்ளம் உவப்ப","மதியுடை வலவ ஏமதி தேரே."],"p":"","r":"This, surely, is the hour when those who are apart remember; so that the heart of her with close-set bangles may rejoice, drive the chariot, wise charioteer.","n":"The season is pointed at (இது) rather than described: the man puts his own longing under a common law (those apart remember now) and makes her joy, not his, the reason to hurry. The praise of the charioteer's wit (மதியுடை) asks for skill as well as speed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 487","lb":"AI487","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:49"},"ai:488":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":486,"t":["கருவி வானம் பெயல் தொடங்கின்றே","பெருவிறல் காதலி கருதும் பொழுதே","விரிஉளை நன்மாப் பூட்டிப்","பருவரல் தீரக் கடவுமதி தேரே."],"p":"","r":"The cloud-massed sky has begun to rain, and this is the hour my beloved thinks of me; yoke the good horses with spreading manes, mighty one, and drive the chariot so that her distress may end.","n":"பெருவிறல் may address the charioteer (mighty one) or be the man's own title, 'the beloved of the man of great might'; the reading takes it as the address. The first rain is read as the moment she remembers him: he knows her mind by the season, so the weather itself is her summons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","ninaivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 488","lb":"AI488","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பெயல்","பெயல்",1,"pira","scene","rain"],["நன்மாப்","மா",3,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:49"},"ai:489":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":487,"t":["அஞ்சிறை வண்டின் அரியினம் மொய்ப்ப","மென் புல முல்லை மலரும்மாலைப்","பையுள் நெஞ்சின் தையல் உவப்ப","நுண்புரி வண்கயிறு இயக்கிநின்","வண்பரி நெடுந்தேர் கடவுமதி விரைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"At the evening when mullai blooms on the soft land and swarms of striped bees with lovely wings crowd upon it, ply the fine-twisted strong reins and drive your long chariot of good horses fast, so that the girl of sorrowing heart may rejoice.","n":"Evening and opening jasmine are the hour and flower of mullai, the hour her sorrow (பையுள்) is worst; he times his arrival to meet it. The bees crowding on the opening mullai could be read as the lover drawn to the woman, but the poem gives them no act of a person of the situation, so they stay scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 489","lb":"AI489","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வண்டின்","வண்டு",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["வண்பரி","பரி",5,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:49"},"ai:490":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":488,"t":["அம்தீம் கிளவி தான்தர எம்வயின்","வந்தன்று மாதோ காரே ஆவயின்","ஆய்த்தொடி அரும்படர் தீர","ஆய்மணி நெடுந்தேர் கடவுமதி விரைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The rainy season has come to us, bringing back her lovely sweet speech; so, that the hard pining of her of choice bangles may end, drive fast the long chariot with its fine bells.","n":"அம்தீம் கிளவி தான்தர can be read two ways: the rains bring her sweet words to his mind, or the season came as her sweet word had granted, the time she was told to wait for. Either way the season is tied to her voice, not to the king's business.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 490","lb":"AI490","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:49"},"ai:491":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":489,"t":["காரெதிர் காலையாம் ஓவின்று நலிய","நொந்துநொந்து உயவும் உள்ளமொடு","வம்தனெம் மடந்தைநின் ஏர்தர விரைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The returned man tells the woman that when the season met the rains he suffered without pause, and with a heart that ached again and again he has hurried back so that her beauty may return.","n":"The decade's frame is the arrival, but this first poem gives the man's side of the waiting: the pain of the season was his too, and the purpose of his haste is her beauty (ஏர்), not his own relief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 491","lb":"AI491","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:50"},"ai:492":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":490,"t":["நின்னே போலும் மஞ்ஞை ஆலநின்","நன்னுதல் நாறும் முல்லை மலர","நின்னே போல மாமருண்டு நோக்க","நின்னே உள்ளி வந்தனென்","நன்னுதல் அரிவை காரினும் விரைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the woman that with the peacock dancing like her, the mullai opening with the scent of her forehead and the deer gazing startled as she does, he came thinking of her, faster even than the rain clouds.","n":"The comparisons run backward: she is the standard and the season's creatures are likened to her (நின்னே போலும் மஞ்ஞை), so every sign of the rains was, for him, a likeness of her. 'Faster than the rains' claims he beat the deadline the season sets.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","ninaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 492","lb":"AI492","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",1,"pul","simile","peacock"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","simile","jasmine"],["மாமருண்டு","மா",3,"maa","simile","deer"],["காரினும்","கார்",5,"pira","simile","rain cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:50"},"ai:493":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":491,"t":["ஏறுமுதண் சிறப்ப ஏறெதிர் இரங்க","மாதர் மான்பிணை மறியொடு மறுகக்","கார்தொடங் கின்றே காலை","நேரிறை முன்கைநின் உள்ளியாம் வரவே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the woman that the rains began just as he came, thinking of her of the well-shaped forearm: the bulls grew fierce, the thunder roared back at them, and the lovely doe roamed bewildered with her fawn.","n":"The line puns on ஏறு, bull and thunder, setting the beasts' rut against the sky's answer. The doe that wanders with her fawn is not given an act that maps onto the lovers, so it is left as scene, though a reader may hear the waiting mother in it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 493","lb":"AI493","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஏறுமுதண்","ஏறு",1,"maa","scene","bull"],["ஏறெதிர்","ஏறு",1,"pira","scene","thunder"],["மான்பிணை","மான் பிணை",2,"maa","scene","doe"],["மறியொடு","மறி",2,"maa","scene","fawn"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:50"},"ai:494":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":492,"t":["வண்டுதாது ஊதத் தேரை தெவிட்டத்","தண்கமழ் புறவின் முல்லை மலர","இன்புறுத் தன்று பொழுதே","நின்குறை வாய்த்தனம் திர்கினிப் படரே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the woman that with bees sucking pollen, frogs croaking their fill and the mullai of the cool fragrant scrub in flower, the season has turned to delight: he has accomplished what she needed, so let her grief now end.","n":"நின் குறை வாய்த்தனம் may mean he has done what she lacked (come home in time) or that he finished the task for her sake; either way the poem makes the season's pleasure conditional on his return.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 494","lb":"AI494","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["வண்டு","வண்டு",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["தேரை","தேரை",1,"pira","scene","frog"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:50"},"ai:495":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":493,"t":["செந்நில மருங்கின் பல்மலர் தாஅய்ப்","புலம்பு தீர்ந்து இனிய வாயின புறவே","பின்னிருங் கூந்தல் நன்னலம் புனைய","உள்ளுதொறும் கலிழும் நெஞ்சமொடு","முளெயிற்று அரிவையாம் வந்தமாறே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the woman that the scrub of red earth, strewn with many flowers, has lost its loneliness and turned sweet because he has come, with a heart that wept each time he thought of her, to dress her braided dark hair.","n":"வந்தமாறே makes his coming the cause: the land's loneliness (புலம்பு) ends because he arrived, so the landscape stands for her and its comfort is hers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","punarchi","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 495","lb":"AI495","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பல்மலர்","மலர்",1,"maram","scene","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:50"},"ai:496":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":494,"t":["மாபுதல் சேர வரகு இணர் சிறப்ப","மாமலி புலம்பக் கார்கலித்து அலைப்பப்","பேரமர்க்கண்ணி நின்பிரிந்து உறைநர்","தோள்துணை யாக வந்தனர்","போதுஅவிழ் கூந்தலும் பூவிரும் புகவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman of large lovely eyes that, with beasts sheltering in the bushes, the millet heavy in ear and the rains crashing and lashing, he who lived apart from her has come to be the companion of her shoulders; now let her hair of opening buds long for flowers again.","n":"Hair that 'wants flowers' is hair that may be dressed again: a woman apart from her husband did not wear flowers, so the friend's last line is permission to adorn. The sense of மாமலி புலம்ப in line 2 is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 496","lb":"AI496","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மாபுதல்","மா",1,"maa","scene","beasts"],["வரகு","வரகு",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["போதுஅவிழ்","போது",5,"maram","ornament","buds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:50"},"ai:497":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":495,"t":["குறும்பல் கோதை கொன்றை மலர","நெடுஞ்செம் புற்றம் ஈயல் பகர","மாபசி மறுப்பக் கார்தொடங் கின்றே","பேரியல் அரிவைநின் உள்ளிப்","போர்வெம் குருசில் வந்த மாறே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman of noble bearing that the konrai flowers in many short garlands, the tall red anthills pour out winged termites, the beasts no longer go hungry and the rains have begun, because the war-loving lord has come thinking of her.","n":"As in 495, the arrival is made the cause of the season (வந்த மாறே); the termites that the anthills 'offer' (பகர) are food that ends the beasts' hunger, so the plenty of the rains and the end of her want arrive together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","punarchi","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 497","lb":"AI497","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",1,"maram","scene","cassia"],["புற்றம்","புற்று",2,"pira","scene","anthill"],["ஈயல்","ஈயல்",2,"pira","scene","winged termites"],["மாபசி","மா",3,"maa","scene","beasts"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:50"},"ai:498":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":496,"t":["தோள்கவின் எய்தின தொடிநிலை நின்றன","நீள்வரி நெடுங்கண் வாள்வனப்பு உற்றன","வேந்துகோட்டு யானை வேந்துதொழில் விட்டென","விரைசெலல் நெடுந்தேர் கடைஇ","வரையக நாடன் வந்த மாறே."],"p":"","r":"Her shoulders have regained their beauty, her bangles stay in place and her long lined eyes have taken on a bright loveliness, because the king of the tusked elephants left off his war and the man of the hill country drove his swift tall chariot home.","n":"The recovery is itemised in the order loss was itemised in the poems of separation: shoulders, bangles, eyes. The return depends on the king's release (வேந்து தொழில் விட்டென), not on the man's choice; and the hero is called வரையக நாடன், a man of the hills, in a mullai poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","nalam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 498","lb":"AI498","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:50"},"ai:499":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":497,"t":["பிடவம் மலரத் தளவம் நனையக்","கார்கவின் கொண்ட கானம் காணின்","வருந்துவள் பெரிதென அரும்தொழிற்கு அகலாது","வந்தனர் ஆல்நம் காதலர்","அம்தீம் கிளவிநின் ஆய்நலம் கொண்டே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman of sweet speech that our lover, thinking she would grieve greatly if she saw the forest dressed in the beauty of the rains, with pidavam blooming and thalavam in bud, did not stay away for his hard work but came, bringing back her fine beauty.","n":"He reasons from what the season would do to her, not from his own longing: the forest is dangerous because she would see it. அருந்தொழிற்கு அகலாது says he left the hard task unfinished rather than miss the season.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","punarchi","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 499","lb":"AI499","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பிடவம்","பிடவம்",1,"maram","scene","pidavam"],["தளவம்","தளவம்",1,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:50"},"ai:500":{"book":"ainkurunuru","num":498,"t":["கொன்றைப் பூவின் பசந்த உண்கண்","குன்றக நெடுஞ்சுனைக் குவளை போலத்","தொல்கவின் பெற்றன இவட்கே வெல்போர்","வியன்நெடும் பாசறை நீடிய","வயமான் தோன்றல்நீ வந்த மாறே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the returned lord of strong horses, who lingered long in the wide camp of victorious war, that the kohl-lined eyes of this woman, gone yellow as konrai flowers, have regained their old beauty like the blue lilies of a long mountain pool, now that he has come.","n":"The two flowers measure the change: the konrai, the rainy season's own yellow flower, gives the pallor, and the kuvalai of a hill pool, a kurinji thing, gives the restored eyes. Ending the decade by addressing him, the friend also reminds him how long the camp kept him (நீடிய).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","punarchi","padai","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Ainkurunuru 500","lb":"AI500","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கொன்றைப்","கொன்றை",1,"maram","simile","cassia"],["குவளை","குவளை",2,"maram","simile","blue water-lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ai:50"},"a:0":{"book":"akananuru","num":1,"t":["கார்விரி கொன்றைப் பொன்னேர் புது மலர்த்","தாரன் மாலையன் மலைந்த கண்ணியன்;","மார்பி னஃதே மை இல் நுண்ஞாண்;","நுதலது இமையா நாட்டம்; இகல் அட்டுக்,","கையது கணிச்சியொடு மழுவே ; மூவாய்","வேலும் உண்டு அத் தோலா தோற்கே;","ஊர்ந்தது ஏறே ; சேர்ந்தோள் உமையே-","செவ்வான் அன்ன மேனி, அவ்வான்","இலங்குபிறை அன்ன விலங்குவால் வைஎயிற்று,","எரியகைந் தன்ன அவிர்ந்து விளங்கு புரிசடை,","முதிராத் திங்களொடு சுடரும் சென்னி,","மூவா அமரரும் முனிவரும் பிறரும்","யாவரும் அறியாத் தொன்முறை மரபின்,","வரிகிளர் வயமான் உரிவை தைஇய,","யாழ்கெழு மணிமிடற்று, அந்தணன்","தாவில் தாள்நிழல் தவிர்ந்தன்றால் உலகே"],"p":"","r":"The god wears fresh konrai blossom as garland and chaplet, a fine thread on his chest, an unwinking eye on his brow, axe and pick in hand and a three-pointed spear; he rides the bull with Umai beside him, red as the evening sky, with crescent-white teeth, flame-bright matted hair lit by the young moon, a tiger's hide and a dark throat, and the world rests in the shade of his flawless feet, an old order no god or sage has fathomed.","n":"The poem is a list of possessions in short clauses (மார்பினஃதே, நுதலது, கையது, ஊர்ந்தது, சேர்ந்தோள்) and gives its only verb of the world at the end: the whole world has stopped (தவிர்ந்தன்று) in the shade of his feet. Unlike the Purananuru's opening by a poet of the same name, it sets Umai beside him rather than inside him, a fitting choice for an anthology of love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 0","lb":"A0","poet":"பெருந்தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["கொன்றைப்","கொன்றை",1,"maram","ornament",""],["ஏறே","ஏறு",7,"maa","scene","bull"],["வயமான்","வயமான்",14,"maa","ornament","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:1":{"book":"akananuru","num":2,"t":["வண்டுபடத் ததைந்த கண்ணி, ஒண்கழல்,","உருவக் குதிரை மழவர் ஓட்டிய","முருகன் நற்போர் நெடுவேள் ஆவி,","அறுகோட்டு யானைப் பொதினி யாங்கண்,","சிறுகா ரோடன் பயினொடு சேர்த்திய","கல்போற் பிரியலம் என்ற சொல்தாம்","மறந்தனர் கொல்லோ - தோழி!-சிறந்த","வேய்மருள் பணைத்தோள் நெகிழச் சேய் நாட்டுப்","பொலங்கல வெறுக்கை தருமார்-நிலம்பக","அழல்போல் வெங்கதிர் பைதறத் தெறுதலின்,","நிழல் தேய்ந்து உலறிய மரத்த; அறை காய்பு,","அறுநீர்ப் பைஞ்சுனை ஆமறப் புலர்தலின்","உகுநெல் பொரியும் வெம்மைய; யாவரும்","வழங்குநர் இன்மையின் வௌவுநர் மடிய","சுரம்புல் லென்ற ஆற்ற; அலங்கு சினை","நாரில் முருங்கை நவிரல் வான்பூச்","சூரலம் கடுவளி எடுப்ப, ஆருற்று,","உடைதிரைப் பிதிர்வின் பொங்கி, முன்","கடல்போல் தோன்றல - காடு இறந்தோரோ?"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether he has forgotten his word that they would never part, a bond he likened to stone set with lac at Avi's Pothini, now that he has left, her shoulders slackening, to fetch gold from a far country across a forest where the sun has stripped the trees of shade, dried the springs, parches spilt grain, emptied the road even of robbers, and where wind-tossed murungai blossom looks like sea spray.","n":"She does not accuse; she asks whether he forgot (மறந்தனர் கொல்லோ), which keeps the fault in memory rather than in will. The promise was itself a simile of permanence, stone fixed with lac, set against a land where everything is coming loose: shade, water, people. Line 5 recurs verbatim in Akananuru 356.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 1","lb":"A1","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வண்டுபடத்","வண்டு",1,"pira","ornament","bee"],["குதிரை","குதிரை",2,"maa","scene",""],["மழவர்","மழவர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["யானைப்","யானை",4,"maa","scene",""],["வேய்மருள்","வேய்",8,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["மரத்த","மரம்",11,"maram","absent",""],["பைஞ்சுனை","சுனை",12,"pira","absent",""],["உகுநெல்","நெல்",13,"unavu","scene",""],["வௌவுநர்","வௌவுநர்",14,"seythi","absent","robbers"],["முருங்கை","முருங்கை",16,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["king","ஆவி",3],["town","பொதினி",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:2":{"book":"akananuru","num":3,"t":["கோழிலை வாழைக் கோள்முதிர் பெருங்குலை","ஊழுறு தீங்கனி, உண்ணுநர்த் தடுத்த","சாரற் பலவின் சுளையொடு, ஊழ்படு","பாறை நெடுஞ்சுனை, விளைந்த தேறல்","அறியாது உண்ட கடுவன் அயலது","கறிவளர் சாந்தம் ஏறல் செல்லாது,","நறுவீ அடுக்கத்து மகிழ்ந்து கண்படுக்கும்","குறியா இன்பம், எளிதின், நின் மலைப்","பல்வேறு விலங்கும், எய்தும் நாட!","குறித்த இன்பம் நினக்கெவன் அரிய?","வெறுத்த ஏஎர், வேய்புரை பணைத் தோள்,","நிறுப்ப நில்லா நெஞ்சமொடு நின்மாட்டு","இவளும், இனையள் ஆயின், தந்தை","அருங்கடிக் காவலர் சோர்பதன் ஒற்றிக்","கங்குல் வருதலும் உரியை; பைம்புதல்","வேங்கையும் ஒள்ளிணர் விரிந்தன;","நெடுவெண் திங்களும் ஊர்கொண் டன்றே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hill lord that on his slopes a male monkey drinks without knowing it the wine that ripe jackfruit and plantain have made in a rock pool, cannot climb the pepper-twined sandal tree and sleeps content on the flowered slope; if beasts there find unplanned joy so easily, how can the joy he plans be hard for him, when the girl's heart cannot be held back and he may yet come at night, slipping past her father's watchmen, though the vengai has burst into flower and the full moon has risen.","n":"The friend states the moral of the monkey herself (குறியா இன்பம் ... குறித்த இன்பம்), so this is an open comparison, not ullurai; a traditional further reading makes the drunk monkey that cannot climb the lover who will not rise to marriage. The closing signs are a warning dressed as permission: the vengai's flowering is the season of the girl's confinement and the moon lights the path he would take by night.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","inbam","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 2","lb":"A2","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பகற்குறிக்கட் செறிப்பறிவுறீஇத் தோழி வரைவு கடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வாழைக்","வாழை",1,"maram","scene",""],["பலவின்","பலா",3,"maram","scene",""],["தேறல்","தேறல்",4,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",5,"maa","simile","male monkey"],["கறிவளர்","கறி",6,"maram","scene","pepper vine"],["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",6,"maram","scene","sandal tree"],["விலங்கும்","விலங்கு",9,"maa","scene",""],["வேய்புரை","வேய்",11,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["காவலர்","காவலர்",14,"seythi","scene",""],["வேங்கையும்","வேங்கை",16,"maram","scene",""],["திங்களும்","திங்கள்",17,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:3":{"book":"akananuru","num":4,"t":["இருங்கழி முதலை மேஎந்தோல் அன்ன","கருங்கால் ஓமைக் காண்பின் பெருஞ்சினை","கடியுடை நனந்தலை, ஈன்று இளைப்பட்ட,","கொடுவாய்ப் பேடைக்கு அல்கிரை தரீஇய","மான்றுவேட்டு எழுந்த செஞ்செவி எருவை","வான்தோய் சிமைய விறல்வரைக் கவா அன்,","துளங்குநடை மரையா வலம்படத் தொலைச்சி,","ஒண்செங் குருதி உவற்றியுண்டு அருந்துபு;","புலவுப்புலி துறந்த கலவுக்கழிக் கடுமுடை","கொள்ளை மாந்தரின்- ஆனாது கவரும்","புல்லிலை மராஅத்த அகல்சேண் அத்தம்,","கலந்தரல் உள்ளமொடு கழியக் காட்டிப்","பின்நின்று துரக்கும் நெஞ்சம் நின்வாய்","வாய்போற் பொய்ம்மொழி எவ்வமென் களைமா-","கவிரிதழ் அன்ன காண்பின் செவ்வாய்,","அந்தீங் கிளவி, ஆயிழை மடந்தை","கொடுங் குழைக்கு அமர்த்த நோக்கம்","நெடுஞ்சேண் ஆரிடை விலங்கும் ஞான்றே?"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart, which showed him the long road through the waste and pushed him from behind toward wealth, that its lie, which looks like truth, cannot lift his grief when on that far road the glance of the girl with the red mouth and sweet speech, eyes that rival her curved earrings, comes across his way; and the road is the one where a red-eared vulture, to feed its mate weak from laying in the omai tree, ceaselessly tears like looters at the rotting wild cow a tiger killed and left.","n":"The heart stands behind and drives (பின்நின்று துரக்கும்), the very phrase another paalai poet uses for a heart that stays behind and holds back (Akananuru 19). The vulture that forages for a nesting mate is the man's own errand seen from the road, and its carrion feast makes the wealth he is promised look like plunder.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","selvam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 3","lb":"A3","poet":"எயினந்தை மகனார் இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"முன்னொரு காலத்து நெஞ்சினாற் பொருள்வலிக்கப்பட்டுப் பிரிந்தான் தலைமகன்; பிரிந்து இடைச்சுரத்தினின்று அவள் நலநயந்து மீளலுற்ற நெஞ்சினைக் கழறிப்போய்ப் பொருண் முடித்துவந்தவன் பின்னும் பொருள்வலிக்கப்பட்ட நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லிச் செலவழுங்கியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","செலவழுங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முதலை","முதலை",1,"pira","simile","crocodile"],["ஓமைக்","ஓமை",2,"maram","scene",""],["பேடைக்கு","பேடை",4,"pul","scene",""],["எருவை","எருவை",5,"pul","iraicchi","vulture"],["மரையா","மரையா",7,"maa","scene","wild cow"],["புலவுப்புலி","புலி",9,"maa","scene",""],["மராஅத்த","மரா",11,"maram","scene",""],["கவிரிதழ்","கவிர்",15,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","எருவை","confirmed","1/2","a male's errand is for the mate he leaves weak at home, which is the very claim of the heart's errand for wealth and the reason the man turns back to her"]],"ch":"a:1"},"a:4":{"book":"akananuru","num":5,"t":["முல்லை வைந்நுனை தோன்ற இல்லமொடு","பைங்காற் கொன்றை மெல்பிணி அவிழ,","இரும்பு திரித்தன்ன மாஇரு மருப்பின்,","பரலவல் அடைய, இரலை தெறிப்ப,","மலர்ந்த ஞாலம் புலம்புபுறக் கொடுப்ப,","கருவி வானம் கதழுறை சிதறிக்","கார்செய் தன்றே, கவின் பெறு கானம்;","குரங்குளைப் பொலிந்த கொய்சுவற் புரவி,","நரம்பு ஆர்த்தன்ன, வாங்குவள்பு அரிய,","பூத்த பொங்கர்த் துணையொடு வதிந்த","தாதுண் பறவை பேதுறல் அஞ்சி,","மணிநா ஆர்த்த மாண்வினைத் தேரன்,","உவக்காண் தோன்றும் குறும்பொறை நாடன்,","கறங்கிசை விழவின் உறந்தைக் குணாது,","நெடும்பெருங் குன்றத்து அமன்ற காந்தட்","போதவிழ் அலரின் நாறும்-","ஆய்தொடி அரிவை! - நின் மாணலம் படர்ந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend shows the woman the season: jasmine has put out its tips, konrai buds open, the stag with horns like twisted iron leaps into the gravel hollows and rain has made the forest lovely; look, there comes the chariot of the lord of the low hills, its reins drawn taut as lute-strings and its bell-tongues tied so as not to startle the nectar birds asleep with their mates in the flowering boughs, as he comes thinking of her beauty, fragrant as the kanthal opening on the tall hill east of festive Uranthai.","n":"The man's tying of the bells is the poem's proof of his tenderness: a man who will not wake sleeping pairs of birds is hurrying to his own pair. The rain does the promising before he arrives (கார்செய்தன்றே), as the friend's argument requires.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaalam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 4","lb":"A4","poet":"குறுங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகளைப் பருவங்காட்டி வற்புறுத்தியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்காட்டி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",1,"maram","scene",""],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",2,"maram","scene",""],["இரலை","இரலை",4,"maa","scene","stag"],["புரவி","புரவி",8,"maa","scene",""],["நரம்பு","நரம்பு",9,"yaazh","simile","lute string"],["பறவை","பறவை",11,"pul","iraicchi",""],["காந்தட்","காந்தள்",15,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["town","உறந்தை",14]],"dv":[["iraicchi","பறவை","confirmed","1/1","the man who will not wake a pair asleep is coming home to rest with his own wife"]],"ch":"a:1"},"a:5":{"book":"akananuru","num":6,"t":["அளிநிலை பொறா அது அமரிய முகத்தள்,","விளிநிலை கொள்ளாள், தமியள், மென்மெல,","நலமிகு சேவடி நிலம்வடுக் கொளா அக்","குறுக வந்துதன் கூர்எயிறு தோன்ற","வறிதகத் தெழுந்த வாயல் முறுவலள்","கண்ணிய துணரா அளவை, ஒண்ணுதல்,","வினைதலைப் படுதல் செல்லா நினைவுடன்","முளிந்த ஓமை முதையலம் காட்டுப்,","பளிங்கத் தன்ன பல்காய் நெல்லி,","மோட்டிரும் பாறை ஈட்டுவட்டு ஏய்ப்ப,","உதிர்வன படூஉம் கதிர்தெறு கவாஅன்,","மாய்த்த போல மழுகுநுனை தோற்றி,","பாத்தி யன்ன குடுமிக் கூர்ங்கல்.","விரல்நுதி சிதைக்கும் நிரைநிலை அதர,","பரல்முரம்பு ஆகிய பயம்இல் கானம்","இறப்ப எண்ணுதிர் ஆயின் - 'அறத்தாறு","அன்று' என மொழிந்த தொன்றுபடு கிளவி","அன்ன ஆக என்னுநள் போல,","முன்னம் காட்டி, முகத்தின் உரையா","ஓவச் செய்தியின் ஒன்று நினைந்து ஒற்றி","பாவை மாய்த்த பனிநீர் நோக்கமொடு,","ஆகத்து ஒடுக்கிய புதல்வன் புன்தலைத்","தூநீர் பயந்த துணையமை பிணையல்","மோயினள் உயிர்த்த காலை, மாமலர்","மணிஉரு இழந்த அணியழி தோற்றம்","கண்டு கடிந்தனம், செலவே - ஒண்டொடி","உழையம் ஆகவும் இனைவோள்","பிழையலள் மாதோ, பிரிதும் நாம் எனினே"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart why he gave up the journey: when he thought of crossing the stony, useless forest where gooseberries fall like dice on the rock and sharp stones cut the toes, she came near unasked, with a faint forced smile, said nothing but stood like a painting as if assenting to the old saying that such leaving is not the way of right, and when, eyes swimming, she sighed over the garland on the head of the son held to her breast, its bright flower lost its colour; seeing that he stayed, for she grieves even with him beside her and would not live if they parted.","n":"Her whole argument is unspoken: she refuses by looking (முன்னம் காட்டி, முகத்தின் உரையா), and the decisive sign is not on her but on the child, the garland withering under her breath. The old maxim she seems to accept, அறத்தாறு அன்று, is the poem's only statement of a rule, and it is attributed to tradition, not to her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kurippu","aram","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 5","lb":"A5","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங் கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் வயிற் பிரியக் கருதிய தலைமகன் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லிச் செலவழுங்கியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","செலவழுங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஓமை","ஓமை",8,"maram","absent",""],["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",9,"maram","simile","gooseberry"],["பிணையல்","பிணையல்",23,"maram","ornament","garland"],["மாமலர்","மலர்",24,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:6":{"book":"akananuru","num":7,"t":["அரிபெய் சிலம்பின் ஆம்பலந் தொடலை,","அரம்போழ் அவ்வளைப் பொலிந்த முன்கை,","இழையணி பணைத்தோள், ஐயை தந்தை,","மழைவளம் தரூஉம் மாவண் தித்தன்","பிண்ட நெல்லின் உறந்தை ஆங்கண்","கழைநிலை பெறாஅக் காவிரி நீத்தம்,","குழைமாண் ஒள்ளிழை நீ வெய் யோளொடு,","வேழ வெண்புணை தழீஇப், பூழியர்","கயம்நாடு யானையின் முகனமர்ந் தாங்கு,","ஏந்தெழில் ஆகத்து பூந்தார் குழைய,","நெருநல் ஆடினை புனலே ; இன்று வந்து","'ஆக வனமுலை அரும்பிய சுணங்கின்,","மாசில் கற்பின், புதல்வன் தாய்' என,","மாயப் பொய்ம்மொழி சாயினை பயிற்றி, எம்","முதுமை எள்ளல்; அஃது அமைகும் தில்ல!","சுடர்ப்பூந் தாமரை நீர்முதிர் பழனத்து","அம்தூம்பு வள்ளை ஆய்கொடி மயக்கி,","வாளை மேய்ந்த வள்ளெயிற்று நீர்நாய்,","முள்ளரைப் பிரம்பின் மூதரில் செறியும்,","பல்வேல் மத்தி, கழாஅர் அன்ன எம்","இளமை சென்று தவத்தொல் லஃதே;","இனிமைஎவன் செய்வது, பொய்ம்மொழி, எமக்கே?"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the husband back from a courtesan that yesterday he bathed with his beloved in the Kaveri flood at Uranthai of the generous Thithan, clasping a white reed raft, faces bright like the Poozhiyar's elephant in a pond, his garland crushed on his chest; today he comes and repeats lies about 'the mother of his son, of spotless chastity', mocking her age, which she lets pass: her youth, like Mathi's Kazhar where the otter that fed on vaalai fish among the vallai creepers goes back to rest in the old cane thicket, is long gone, so what can sweet lies do for her.","n":"The otter that feasts in the lotus field and then returns to the old thorny cane is set inside the simile for her lost youth: she pictures him eating among new flowers and sheltering in the old thicket, which is herself. The praise he offers (மாசில் கற்பின், புதல்வன் தாய்) is the true wifely title, and she calls it a lie because it is said to placate her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 6","lb":"A6","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தையிற் பிரிந்து வந்த தலைமகற்குக் கிழத்தி கூறியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","பரத்தையிற் பிரிந்து வந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","மூப்பு"]],"k":[["ஆம்பலந்","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","ornament",""],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",5,"unavu","scene",""],["வேழ","வேழம்",8,"maram","scene","reed"],["யானையின்","யானை",9,"maa","simile",""],["தாமரை","தாமரை",16,"maram","scene",""],["வள்ளை","வள்ளை",17,"maram","ullurai",""],["வாளை","வாளை",18,"pira","ullurai","fish"],["நீர்நாய்","நீர்நாய்",18,"maa","ullurai","otter"],["பிரம்பின்","பிரம்பு",19,"maram","ullurai",""]],"en":[["king","தித்தன்",4],["town","உறந்தை",5],["river","காவிரி",6],["king","மத்தி",20],["town","கழாஅர்",20]],"dv":[["ullurai","நீர்நாய் வாளை வள்ளை பிரம்பின்","confirmed","1/1","the husband, his courtesan and his aging wife"]],"ch":"a:1"},"a:7":{"book":"akananuru","num":8,"t":["முலைமுகம் செய்தன; முள்ளெயிறு இலங்கின;","தலைமுடி சான்று; தண்தழை உடையை;","அலமரல் ஆயமொடு யாங்கணும் படாஅல்;","மூப்புடை முதுபதி தாக்கு அணங்கு உடைய;","காப்பும் பூண்டிசின்; கடையும் போகலை;","பேதை அல்லை - மேதையம் குறுமகள்!","பெதும்பைப் பருவத்து ஒதுங்கினை, புறத்து என,","ஒண்சுடர் நல்லில் அருங்கடி நீவி,","தன்சிதைவு அறிதல் அஞ்சி-இன்சிலை","ஏறுடை இனத்த, நாறு உயிர் நவ்வி!","வலைகாண் பிணையின் போகி, ஈங்கு ஓர்","தொலைவில் வெள்வேல் விடலையொடு, என் மகள்","இச் சுரம் படர்தந் தோளே; ஆயிடை,","அத்தக் கள்வர் ஆதொழு அறுத்தென,","பிற்படு பூசலின் வழிவழி ஓடி","மெய்த்தலைப் படுதல் செல்லேன், இத்தலை,","நின்னொடு வினவல் கேளாய்;- பொன்னொடு","புலிப்பல் கோத்த புலம்புமணித் தாலி,","ஒலிக்குழைச் செயலை உடைமாண் அல்குல்,","ஆய்சுளைப் பலவின் மேய்கலை உதிர்த்த","துய்த்தலை வெண்காழ் பெறூஉம்","கல்கெழு சிறுகுடிக் கானவன் மகளே"],"p":"","r":"The foster mother, following her daughter who has eloped, recalls her warnings (your breasts have formed, you are no child now, keep away from the old town's spirits and do not go out), and asks a doe with its herd, and a hill girl wearing a tiger-tooth tali who gathers the jackfruit seeds the grazing stag drops, whether they have seen her girl, who fled like a hind that sees a net, with a youth of white spear into this waste, while robbers raiding a cattle-pen have blocked her pursuit.","n":"The first lines quote the very prohibitions the girl escaped; the mother's list of the signs of puberty is also her account of why the girl left. Her questions go to a doe and to a hill girl like her daughter, as if the wild had become the only household left to ask.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 7","lb":"A7","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய செவிலித்தாய் பின் சென்று தான் கானகத்தே கண்டதோர் மகளைக் கண்டு சொல்லியது; நவ்விப் பிணாக்கண்டு சொல்லியதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu","தான் கானகத்தே கண்டதோர் மகளைக் கண்டு சொல்லியது"],["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu","நவ்விப் பிணாக்கண்டு சொல்லியதூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தண்தழை","தழை",2,"maram","ornament",""],["நவ்வி","நவ்வி",10,"maa","scene","doe"],["பிணையின்","பிணை",11,"maa","simile","hind"],["கள்வர்","கள்வர்",14,"seythi","scene",""],["ஆதொழு","ஆ",14,"maa","scene","cattle"],["புலிப்பல்","புலி",18,"maa","ornament",""],["செயலை","செயலை",19,"maram","ornament",""],["பலவின்","பலா",20,"maram","scene",""],["மேய்கலை","கலை",20,"maa","scene","stag"],["கானவன்","கானவன்",22,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:8":{"book":"akananuru","num":9,"t":["ஈயற் புற்றத்து ஈர்ம்புறத்து இறுத்த","குரும்பி வல்சிப் பெருங்கை ஏற்றைக்","தூங்குதோல் துதிய வள்உகிர் கதுவலின்,","பாம்பு மதன்அழியும் பானாட் கங்குலும்,","அரிய அல்ல-மன் இகுளை! 'பெரிய","கேழல் அட்ட பேழ்வாய் ஏற்றைப்","பலாவமல் அடுக்கம் புலாவ ஈர்க்கும்","கழை நரல் சிலம்பின் ஆங்கண், வழையொடு","வாழை ஓங்கிய தாழ்கண் அசும்பில்,","படுகடுங் களிற்றின் வருத்தம் சொலியப்","பிடிபடி முறுக்கிய பெருமரப் பூசல்","விண்தோய் விடரகத்து இயம்பும் அவர் நாட்டு,","எண்ணரும் பிறங்கல் மானதர் மயங்காது,","மின்னுவிடச் சிறிய ஒதுங்கி, மென்மெலத்","துளிதலைத் தலைஇய மணியேர் ஐம்பால்","சிறுபுறம் புதைய வாரி, குரல் பிழியூஉ","நெறிகெட விலக்கிய, நீயிர், இச் சுரம்","அறிதலும் அறிதிரோ?' என்னுநர்ப் பெறினே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend, while her lover stands unseen nearby, that even the midnight when the bear clawing termites from the wet anthill makes snakes lose their strength would not be hard to face, if only someone would ask him: in your land, where the tiger drags the boar it killed so the jackfruit slope reeks of flesh and the cow elephant breaks a great tree to ease her mate trapped in a pit, do you who led her off the path even know this road, where she came by lightning flashes without losing her way, gathering her rain-wet hair over her back and wringing it?","n":"She wants the danger she has run spoken to him by someone else; the poem is overheard reproach. The cow elephant crashing down a tree for her mate in the pit is the one tender act among the beasts, and it is what she herself has been doing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu","acham","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 8","lb":"A8","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["ஈயற்","ஈயல்",1,"pira","scene","termite"],["குரும்பி","குரும்பி",2,"pira","gift_food",""],["ஏற்றைக்","ஏற்றை",2,"maa","scene","male bear"],["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",4,"pira","scene",""],["கேழல்","கேழல்",6,"maa","scene","boar"],["ஏற்றைப்","ஏற்றை",6,"maa","scene","male tiger"],["பலாவமல்","பலா",7,"maram","scene",""],["கழை","கழை",8,"maram","scene",""],["வழையொடு","வழை",8,"maram","scene",""],["வாழை","வாழை",9,"maram","scene",""],["களிற்றின்","களிறு",10,"maa","scene",""],["பிடிபடி","பிடி",11,"maa","iraicchi","cow elephant"],["மானதர்","மான்",13,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பிடிபடி","confirmed","1/1","she braves the night for him as the cow labours for her trapped mate, while he does not see what it costs her"]],"ch":"a:1"},"a:9":{"book":"akananuru","num":10,"t":["கொல்வினைப் பொலிந்த, கூர்ங்குறு புழுகின்,","வில்லோர் தூணி வீங்கப் பெய்த","அம்புநுனை ஏய்ப்ப அரும்பிய இருப்பை","செய்படர் அன்ன செங்குழை அகந்தோறு,","இழுதின் அன்ன தீம்புழல் துய்வாய்","உழுதுகாண் துளைய வாகி, ஆர்கழல்பு","ஆலி வானிற் காலொடு பாறித்,","துப்பின் அன்ன செங்கோட்டு இயவின்,","நெய்த்தோர் மீமிசை நிணத்தின் பரிக்கும்","அத்தம் நண்ணிய அங்குடிச் சீறூர்","கொடுநுண் ஓதி மகளிர் ஓக்கிய","தொடிமாண் உலக்கைத் தூண்டுரல் பாணி","நெடுமால் வரைய குடிஞையோடு இரட்டும்","குன்றுபின் ஒழியப் போகி, உரந்துரந்து,","ஞாயிறு படினும், 'ஊர் சேய்த்து' எனாது,","துனைபரி துரக்கும் துஞ்சா செலவின்","எம்மினும், விரைந்து வல்எய்திப் பல்மாண்","ஓங்கிய நல்லில் ஒரு சிறை நிலைஇ,","பாங்கர்ப் பல்லி படுதொறும் பரவிக்","கன்றுபுகு மாலை நின்றோள் எய்தி,","கைகவியாச் சென்று, கண் புதையாக் குறுகி,","பிடிக்கை அன்ன பின்னகம் தீண்டி,","தொடிக்கை தைவரத் தோய்ந்தன்று கொல்லோ-","நாணொடு மிடைந்த கற்பின், வாணுதல்,","அம் தீம் கிளவிக் குறுமகள்","மென்தோள் பெறநசைஇச் சென்றவென் நெஞ்சே?"],"p":"","r":"Returning with his work done, the man tells his heart, the charioteer listening, that it raced ahead of the sleepless chariot past the iruppai-strewn red path and the hill where pestle songs echo with the owl, and asks whether it has already reached her, standing at a corner of the tall house in the evening when calves come home, praying at every chirp of the lizard, and crept up with hand unraised to cover her eyes, touch her braid and stroke her bangled arm.","n":"The man's homecoming is imagined from her side: the evening, the lizard's omen, the game of covering her eyes, all happen in the heart before the body arrives. The road's iruppai and red earth belong to the waste, but the poem's situation is the mullai return and the woman waiting at dusk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","punarchi","ninaivu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 9","lb":"A9","poet":"கல்லாடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றி மீண்ட தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது.","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju","pira","karpu","வினைமுற்றி மீண்ட"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["இருப்பை","இருப்பை",3,"maram","scene",""],["இழுதின்","இழுது",5,"unavu","simile","butter"],["துப்பின்","துப்பு",8,"pira","simile","coral"],["குடிஞையோடு","குடிஞை",13,"pul","scene","owl"],["பல்லி","பல்லி",19,"pira","scene","lizard"],["கன்றுபுகு","கன்று",20,"maa","scene",""],["பிடிக்கை","பிடி",22,"maa","simile","cow elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:10":{"book":"akananuru","num":11,"t":["வான்கடற் பரப்பில் தூவற்கு எதிரிய,","மீன்கண் டன்ன மெல்லரும்பு ஊழ்த்த,","முடவுமுதிர் புன்னைத் தடவுநிலை மாச்சினை,","புள்ளிறை கூரும் மெல்லம் புலம்ப!","நெய்தல் உண்கண் பைதல கலுழப்","பிரிதல் எண்ணினை ஆயின், நன்றும்,","அரிது துற்றனையால். பெரும!- உரிதினின்","கொண்டு ஆங்குப் பெயர்தல்வேண்டும்- கொண்டலொடு","குரூஉத்திரைப் புணரி உடைதரும் எக்கர்ப்","பழந்திமில் கொன்ற புதுவலைப் பரதவர்","மோட்டுமணல் அடைகரைக் கோட்டுமீன் கொண்டி,","மணங்கமழ் பாக்கத்துப் பகுக்கும்","வளங்கெழு தொண்டி அன்ன இவள் நலனே"],"p":"","r":"The friend stops the shore lord leaving after a night meeting and tells him, lord of the coast where birds settle on the old punnai whose buds look like fish eyes, that if he means to part and make the girl's neythal eyes weep he has taken on a hard thing; he must marry her properly and take her, for her beauty is like rich Thondi, where fishermen with new nets share out the horned fish on the sandy shore of the fragrant hamlet.","n":"The friend values the girl in the currency of a Chera port: a public, famous, prosperous town, the opposite of a secret meeting. The fishermen's sharing of the catch is a picture of open, rightful taking, which is what she asks of him (உரிதினின் கொண்டு).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","nalam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 10","lb":"A10","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"இரவுக் குறிவந்து தலைமகளைக் கண்ணுற்று நீங்குந் தலைமகனை எதிர்ப்பட்டு நின்று தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக் குறிவந்து தலைமகளைக் கண்ணுற்று நீங்குந் தலைமகனை"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மீன்கண்","மீன்",2,"pira","simile","fish"],["புன்னைத்","புன்னை",3,"maram","scene",""],["புள்ளிறை","புள்",4,"pul","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",5,"maram","simile",""],["பழந்திமில்","திமில்",10,"seythi","scene","boat"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",10,"seythi","scene",""],["கோட்டுமீன்","கோட்டுமீன்",11,"pira","gift_food","shark"]],"en":[["town","தொண்டி",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:11":{"book":"akananuru","num":12,"t":["வானம் ஊர்ந்த வயங்கொளி மண்டிலம்","நெருப்பெனச் சிவந்த உருப்பவிர் அங்காட்டு,","இலையில மலர்ந்த முகையில் இலவம்","கலிகொள் ஆயம் மலிபுதொகுபு எடுத்த","அஞ்சுடர் நெடுங்கொடி பொற்பத் தோன்றி,","கயந்துகள் ஆகிய பயம்தபு கானம்","எம்மொடு கழிந்தனர் ஆயின், கம்மென,","வம்புவிரித் தன்ன பொங்குமணற் கானயாற்றுப்,","படுசினை தாழ்ந்த பயிலிணர் எக்கர்,","மெய்புகுவு அன்ன கைகவர் முயக்கம்","அவரும் பெறுகுவர் மன்னே! நயவர,","நீர்வார் நிகர்மலர் கடுப்ப, ஓ மறந்து","அறுகுளம் நிறைக்குந போல, அல்கலும்","அழுதல் மேவல வாகிப்","பழிதீர் கண்ணும் படுகுவ மன்னே!"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that had he taken her with him through the sun-scorched forest where the leafless silk-cotton blooms like rows of festival lamps and the pools have turned to dust, he too would have had her close embrace on the sandbank of the forest stream under the drooping flowered boughs, and her eyes, which now weep every night like dripping flowers as if to fill a dry tank, would have slept.","n":"She turns the usual argument round: the road is not too hard for her; it is the place where their embrace could have been. The colophon reads the poem as her assurance that she will bear up, but what it says is that she does not: the assurance lies in her saying it calmly, not in its content.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 11","lb":"A11","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்தவிடத்து ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகள் வேறுபாடுகண்டு ஆற்றாளாய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் ஆற்றுவல் என்பதுபடச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","ஆற்றுவல் என்பதுபடச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["இலவம்","இலவம்",3,"maram","simile","silk-cotton"],["பயிலிணர்","இணர்",9,"maram","scene",""],["நிகர்மலர்","மலர்",12,"maram","simile",""],["அறுகுளம்","குளம்",13,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:12":{"book":"akananuru","num":13,"t":["யாயே, கண்ணினும் கடுங் காதலளே","எந்தையும், நிலன்உரப் பொறாஅன்; 'சீறுடி சிவப்ப,","எவன், இல! குறுமகள்! இயங்குதி! என்னும்;'","யாமே, பிரிவு இன்று இயைந்த துவரா நட்பின்,","இருதலைப் புள்ளின் ஓர் உயிரம்மே;","ஏனல்அம் காவலர் ஆனாது ஆர்த்தொறும்","கிளிவிளி பயிற்றும் வெளில்ஆடு பெருஞ்சினை","விழுக்கோட் பலவின் பழுப்பயம் கொண்மார்,","குறவர் ஊன்றிய குரம்பை புதைய,","வேங்கை தாஅய தேம்பாய் தோற்றம்","புலிசெத்து, வெரீஇய புகர்முக வேழம்,","மழைபடு சிலம்பில் கழைபடப் பெயரும்","நல்வரை நாட! நீ வரின்,","மெல்லியல் ஓரும் தான் வாழலளே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hill lord that the girl's mother loves her more than her own eyes, her father cannot bear her small feet on hard ground, and the two of them are one life like the two-headed bird; on his hill, where an elephant takes the vengai flowers strewn over the hunters' hut for a tiger and flees through the bamboo, if he comes (by night) the gentle girl will not survive.","n":"The household's love is given as the guard itself: the closer they hold her, the less she can slip away. The elephant that bolts from vengai blossom mistaken for a tiger is a hill scene of false alarm; a reader may hear in it the danger of discovery, but it maps to no stated situation. Whether 'we' (யாமே) are the girl and her friend or the girl and her lover is open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","natpu","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 12","lb":"A12","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"\"பகற்குறி வாரா நின்ற தலைமகன் தோழியாற் செறிப்பறிவுறுக்கப்பட்டு இரவுக்குறி வாரா வரைவல் என்றாற்கு, அதுவும் மறுத்து வரைவு கடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி வாரா வரைவல் என்றாற்கு, அதுவும் மறுத்து வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஏனல்அம்","ஏனல்",6,"unavu","scene","millet"],["காவலர்","காவலர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["கிளிவிளி","கிளி",7,"pul","scene",""],["பலவின்","பலா",8,"maram","scene",""],["குறவர்","குறவர்",9,"seythi","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",10,"maram","scene",""],["புலிசெத்து","புலி",11,"maa","scene",""],["வேழம்","வேழம்",11,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கழைபடப்","கழை",12,"maram","scene",""],["புள்ளின்","புள்",5,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:13":{"book":"akananuru","num":14,"t":["தன்கடற் பிறந்த முத்தின் ஆரமும்,","முனைதிரை கொடுக்கும் துப்பின், தன்மலைத்","தெறல் அருமரபின் கடவுட் பேணிக்","குறவர் தந்த சந்தின் ஆரமும்,","இருபேர் ஆரமும் எழில்பெற அணியும்","திருவீழ் மார்பின் தென்னவன் மறவன் -","குழியில் கொண்ட மராஅ யானை","மொழியின் உணர்த்தும் சிறுவரை அல்லது,","வரைநிலை இன்றி இரவலர்க்கு ஈயும்,","வள்வாய் அம்பின் கோடைப் பொருநன்","பண்ணி தைஇய பயம்கெழு வேள்வியின்,","விழுமிது நிகழ்விது ஆயினும்- தெற்குஏர்பு,","கழிமழை பொழிந்த பொழுதுகொள் அமையத்துச்,","சாயல் இன்துணை இவட்பிரிந்து உறையின்,","நோய் இன்றாக செய்பொருள்! வயிற்பட","மாசுஇல் தூமடி விரிந்த சேக்கை,","கவவுஇன் புறாமைக் கழிக- வள வயல்,","அழல்நுதி அன்ன தோகை ஈன்ற","கழனி நெல்லின் கவைமுதல் அலங்கல்","நிரம்புஅகன் செறுவில் வரம்பு அணையாத் துயல்வரப்,","புலம்பொடு வந்த பொழுதுகொள் வாடை,","இலங்குபூங் கரும்பின் ஏர்கழை இருந்த","வெண்குருகு நரல, வீசும்","நுண்பல் துவலைய தண்பனி நாளே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that even if the wealth he would earn were as great as the fruitful sacrifice of the Pandya's warrior, lord of Kodai, who wears the pearl of his sea and the sandal of his hill and gives to suppliants without limit except the short while it takes to teach a pit-caught elephant to obey, he should not leave his gentle companion in these days of cold dew, when the lonely north wind sways the forked paddy in the wide fields and makes the white heron on the sugarcane cry; let the wealth go, and the nights in a clean bed without embrace.","n":"The patron's limitless giving is the measure, and the friend uses it to set a ceiling: no wealth, not even such a man's, is worth a winter apart. The two clauses of wishing (நோய் இன்றாக செய்பொருள்; கவவுஇன் புறாமைக் கழிக) can be read as bitter dismissal or as a blessing on his going, which is why the colophon allows that she consented.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","eegai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 13","lb":"A13","poet":"பெருந்தலைச்சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் வயிற் பிரியலுற்ற தலைமகனைத் தோழி செலவழங்கு வித்தது; உடம்பட்டதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","செலவழங்கு வித்தது"],["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","உடம்பட்டதூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முத்தின்","முத்து",1,"pira","ornament",""],["துப்பின்","துப்பு",2,"pira","ornament","coral"],["கடவுட்","கடவுள்",3,"deyvam","scene",""],["குறவர்","குறவர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["சந்தின்","சந்து",4,"maram","ornament","sandal"],["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","gift_food",""],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",19,"unavu","scene",""],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",22,"maram","scene",""],["வெண்குருகு","குருகு",23,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[["king","தென்னவன்",6],["patron","கோடைப் பொருநன்",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:14":{"book":"akananuru","num":15,"t":["'அரக்கத்து அன்ன செந்நிலப் பெருவழி,","காயாஞ் செம்மல் தாஅய், பலஉடன்","ஈயல் மூதாய் வரிப்பப், பவளமொடு","மணி மிடைந்தன்ன குன்றம் கவைஇய","அம்காட்டு ஆர்இடை, மடப்பிணை தழீஇத்,","திரி மருப்பு இரலை புல்அருந்து உகள,","முல்லை வியன்புலம் பரப்பிக் கோவலர்","குறும்பொறை மருங்கின் நறும்பூ அயரப்","பதவு மேயல் அருந்து மதவுநடை நல்ஆன்","வீங்குமாண் செருத்தல், தீம்பால் பிலிற்ற","கன்றுபயிர் குரல, மன்றுநிறை புகுதரும்","மாலையும் உள்ளார் ஆயின், காலை","யாங்கு ஆகுவம் கொல்? பாண!\" என்ற","மனையோள் சொல்எதிர் சொல்லல் செல்லேன்,","செவ்வழி நல்யாழ் இசையினென், பையெனக்","கடவுள் வாழ்த்திப் பையுள் மெய்ந் நிறுத்து,","அவர்திறம் செல்வேன் கண்டனென், யானே-","விடுவிசைக் குதிரை விலங்குபரி முடுகக்","கல்பொருது இரங்கும் பல்ஆர் நேமிக்","கார்மழை முழக்குஇசை கடுக்கும்,","முனைநல் ஊரன், புனைநெடுந் தேரே!"],"p":"","r":"The bard tells his companions that the wife said to him, 'if he does not remember us even in this evening, when the stag nuzzles its doe and leaps grazing on the red-earth road strewn with kaya flowers, herders deck themselves in the jasmine land and good cows, udders dripping, low for their calves as they crowd into the common, what will become of us by morning, bard?'; unable to answer, he played his lute, praised the god, steadied his grief and went toward the husband, and now he has seen his tall chariot coming, wheels roaring like thunder-clouds.","n":"The bard's own answer to her question is not words but music and prayer, and then a sighting; the poem moves from her evening to his seeing. Evening in the jasmine land is the hour when everything returns to its pair, which is why it is the measure of his forgetting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 14","lb":"A14","poet":"ஒக்கூர் மாசாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பாணன் தனக்குப் பாங்காயினார் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["paanan","pira",null,"karpu","பாணன் தனக்குப் பாங்காயினார் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["காயாஞ்","காயா",2,"maram","scene",""],["மூதாய்","ஈயல் மூதாய்",3,"pira","scene","velvet mite"],["மடப்பிணை","பிணை",5,"maa","scene","doe"],["இரலை","இரலை",6,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",7,"maram","scene",""],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["நல்ஆன்","ஆன்",9,"maa","scene",""],["தீம்பால்","பால்",10,"unavu","scene",""],["கன்றுபயிர்","கன்று",11,"maa","scene",""],["நல்யாழ்","யாழ்",15,"yaazh","scene",""],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",16,"deyvam","scene",""],["குதிரை","குதிரை",18,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","இரலை","confirmed","1/1","in this season even the stag keeps to its mate, yet the husband has not come home to his"]],"ch":"a:1"},"a:15":{"book":"akananuru","num":16,"t":["எம்வெங் காமம் இயைவது ஆயின்,","மெய்ம்மலி பெரும்பூண், செம்மற் கோசர்","கொம்மையம் பசுங்காய்க் குடுமி விளைந்த","பாகல் ஆர்கைப் பறைக்கட் பீலித்","தோகைக் காவின் துளுநாட்டு அன்ன,","வறுங்கை வம்பலர் தாங்கும் பண்பின்","செறிந்த சேரிச் செம்மல் மூதூர்,","அறிந்த மாக்கட்டு ஆகுக தில்ல-","தோழி மாரும் யானும் புலம்பச்,","சூழி யானைச் சுடர்ப்பூண் நன்னன்","பாழி அன்ன கடியுடை வியன்நகர்ச்","செறிந்த காப்புஇகந்து, அவனொடு போகி,","அத்த இருப்பை ஆர்கழல் புதுப்பூத்","துய்த்த வாய, துகள்நிலம் பரக்க,","கொன்றை யம்சினைக் குழற்பழம் கொழுதி,","வன்கை எண்கின் வயநிரை பரக்கும்-","இன்துணைப் படர்ந்த கொள்கையொடு ஒராங்குக்,","குன்ற வேயின் திரண்ட என்","மென்தோள் அஞ்ஞை சென்ற- ஆறே!"],"p":"","r":"The mother whose daughter has eloped wishes that, if her fierce longing can be granted, the old town of the man's many hamlets may know how to welcome her, as the Kosar's Thulu country, with its peacock groves, shelters empty-handed travellers; for her soft-shouldered dear one, leaving her friends and mother lonely, has slipped the strict guard of a house like Nannan's fortress Pazhi and gone with him on the road where bears scatter the iruppai flowers and konrai pods, set on her sweet companion.","n":"Her grief turns into a blessing on the stranger's town. The house is likened to Nannan's guarded Pazhi so that the breach looks like the fall of a fort; the girl's resolve (இன்துணைப் படர்ந்த கொள்கை) is acknowledged, not condemned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["udanpokku","virundhu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 15","lb":"A15","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய தாய் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட் போக்கிய தாய் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பாகல்","பாகல்",4,"maram","gift_food",""],["தோகைக்","தோகை",5,"pul","scene","peacock"],["யானைச்","யானை",10,"maa","scene",""],["இருப்பை","இருப்பை",13,"maram","scene",""],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",15,"maram","scene",""],["எண்கின்","எண்கு",16,"maa","scene","bear"],["வேயின்","வேய்",18,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[["king","நன்னன்",10],["town","பாழி",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:16":{"book":"akananuru","num":17,"t":["நாயுடை முதுநீர்க் கலித்த தாமரைத்","தாதின் அல்லி அவிர் இதழ் புரையும்,","மாசுஇல் அங்கை, மணிமருள் அவ்வாய்","நாவொடு நவிலா நகைபடு தீஞ்சொல்,","யாவரும் விழையும் பொலந்தொடிப் புதல்வனைத்","தேர்வழங்கு தெருவில் தமியோற் கண்டே,","கூர்எயிற்று அரிவை குறுகினள்; யாவரும்","காணுநர் இன்மையின், செத்தனள் பேணிப்","பொலங்கலம் சுமந்த பூண்தாங்கு இளமுலை,","'வருக மாள, என் உயிர்!' எனப் பெரிது உவந்து","கொண்டனள் நின்றோட் கண்டு, நிலைச் செல்லேன்,","'மாசுஇல் குறுமகள்!' எவன் பேதுற்றனை?","நீயும் தாயை இவற்கு?' என யான்தற்","கரைய, வந்து விரைவனென் கவைஇ,","களவு உடம்படுநரின் கவிழ்ந்து, நிலம்கிளையா,","நாணி நின்றோள் நிலை கண்டு, யானும்","பேணினென் அல்லெனோ- மகிழ்ந!- வானத்து","அணங்குஅருங் கடவுள் அன்னோள், நின்","மகன்தாய் ஆதல் புரைவது - ஆங்கு எனவே!"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her husband, who claims he knows no other woman, that she saw a sharp-toothed girl find their little son alone in the chariot street, lift him to her ornamented breast crying 'come, my life!' when no one was looking; she went up and asked 'why so flustered, spotless girl? are you his mother too?', embraced her, and watched her bow her head, scratch the ground and stand ashamed like one caught in a secret; and so, she asks, did she not treat her kindly, since that goddess-like woman is fit to be his son's mother?","n":"The proof against his denial is the courtesan's love for the child, and the wife's weapon is courtesy: she embraces the rival and then, with full irony, ranks her as fit to be the son's mother, which is the title the husband had denied anyone else could claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 16","lb":"A16","poet":"சாகலாசனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தையர் சேரியினின்றும் வந்த தலைமகன் யாரையும் அறியேனென்றாற்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","யாரையும் அறியேனென்றாற்குத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["நாயுடை","நீர்நாய்",1,"maa","scene","otter"],["தாமரைத்","தாமரை",1,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:17":{"book":"akananuru","num":18,"t":["வளம்கெழு திருநகர்ப் பந்து சிறிது எறியினும்,","இளந்துணை ஆயமொடு கழங்கு உடன் ஆடினும்,","'உயங்கின்று, அன்னை! என்மெய்' என்று அசைஇ,","மயங்கு வியர் பொறித்த நுதலள், தண்ணென,","முயங்கினள் வதியும் மன்னே! இனியே,","தொடி மாண் சுற்றமும் எம்மும் உள்ளாள்,","நெடுமொழித் தந்தை அருங்கடி நீவி,","நொதும லாளன் நெஞ்சுஅறப் பெற்றஎன்","சிறுமுதுக் குறைவி சிலம்புஆர் சீறுடி","வல்லகொல், செல்லத் தாமே- கல்லென","ஊர்எழுந் தன்ன உருகெழு செலவின்,","நீர்இல் அத்தத்து ஆர்இடை, மடுத்த,","கொடுங்கோல் உமணர், பகடுதெழி தெள்விளி","நெடும்பெருங் குன்றத்து இமிழ்கொள இயம்பும்,","கடுங்கதிர் திருகிய வேய்பயில் பிறங்கல்,","பெருங்களிறு உரிஞ்சிய மண் அரை யாஅத்து","அருஞ்சுரக் கவலைய அதர்படு மருங்கின்,","நீள்அரை இலவத்து ஊழ்கழி பல்மலர்,","விழவுத் தலைக்கொண்ட பழவிறல் மூதூர்","நெய்உமிழ் சுடரின் கால்பொரச் சில்கி,","வைகுறு மீனின் தோன்றும்","மைபடு மாமலை விலங்கிய சுரனே!"],"p":"","r":"The foster mother remembers that her girl, if she threw a ball a little or played jacks with her friends, would say 'mother, I am tired' and lie in her arms with sweat on her brow; now, forgetting kin and nurse, she has crossed her proud father's strict guard and given her whole heart to a stranger, and the nurse asks whether those little anklet feet can walk the waterless waste where salt-carters' calls echo in the hills and the falling silk-cotton flowers flicker like festival lamps or like stars at dawn, under the dark hill that bars the way.","n":"The whole poem rests on one contrast: a girl too tired for a ball game now walking the waste. The silk-cotton flowers like lamps in an old town's festival bring the home town into the wilderness, the place she has left as a light falling on her road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 17","lb":"A17","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய செவிலித்தாய் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட் போக்கிய செவிலித்தாய் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["உமணர்","உமணர்",13,"seythi","scene","salt merchants"],["பகடுதெழி","பகடு",13,"maa","scene","ox"],["வேய்பயில்","வேய்",15,"maram","scene",""],["பெருங்களிறு","களிறு",16,"maa","scene",""],["யாஅத்து","யா",16,"maram","scene",""],["இலவத்து","இலவம்",18,"maram","simile",""],["மீனின்","மீன்",21,"pira","simile","star"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:18":{"book":"akananuru","num":19,"t":["நீர்நிறம் கரப்ப, ஊழுறுபு உதிர்ந்து,","பூமலர் கஞலிய கடுவரற் கான்யாற்று,","கராஅம் துஞ்சும் கல்உயர் மறிசுழி,","மராஅ யானை மதம்தப ஒற்றி,","உராஅ ஈர்க்கும் உட்குவரு நீத்தம்","கடுங்கண் பன்றியின் நடுங்காது துணிந்து,","நாம அருந்துறைப் பேர்தந்து, யாமத்து","ஈங்கும் வருபவோ?- ஓங்கல் வெற்ப!-","ஒருநாள் விழுமம் உறினும், வழிநாள்,","வாழ்குவள் அல்லள், என் தோழி; யாவதும்","ஊறுஇல் வழிகளும் பயில வழங்குநர்","நீடுஇன்று ஆக இழுக்குவர், அதனால்,","உலமரல் வருத்தம் உறுதும், எம் படப்பைக்","கொடுந்தேன் இழைத்த கோடுஉயர் நெடுவரை,","பழம்தூங்கு நளிப்பிற் காந்தள்அம் பொதும்பில்,","பகல்நீ வரினும் புணர்குவை - அகல்மலை","வாங்குஅமைக் கண்இடை கடுப்ப, யாய்","ஓம்பினள் எடுத்த, தடமென் தோளே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the hill lord whether anyone comes here at midnight, crossing like a fierce boar the dreadful ford of the forest torrent, its water hidden under fallen flowers, where crocodiles sleep in the eddies and the flood drags off a wild elephant; if her friend suffers harm even for a day she will not live after, and even on safe paths those who go often slip, so they live in fear; let him come by day and meet her in the kanthal grove below the honey-combed hill of their garden, and hold the soft shoulders, like bamboo, that her mother reared.","n":"She does not forbid the meeting; she moves it. The danger she names is the road's, but the argument is also about discovery: the frequent traveller slips (பயில வழங்குநர் ... இழுக்குவர்), and a secret repeated becomes known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu","acham","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 18","lb":"A18","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி இரவு வருவானைப் பகல் வர வென்றது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவு வருவானைப் பகல் வர வென்றது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["பூமலர்","மலர்",2,"maram","scene",""],["கராஅம்","கராம்",3,"pira","scene","crocodile"],["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene",""],["பன்றியின்","பன்றி",6,"maa","simile","boar"],["கொடுந்தேன்","தேன்",14,"unavu","scene",""],["காந்தள்அம்","காந்தள்",15,"maram","scene",""],["வாங்குஅமைக்","அமை",17,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:19":{"book":"akananuru","num":20,"t":["அன்றுஅவண் ஒழிந்தன்றும் இலையே; வந்துநனி","வருந்தினை- வாழி, என் நெஞ்சே!- பருந்து இருந்து","உயாவிளி பயிற்றும், யாஉயர் நனந்தலை,","உருள்துடி மகுளியின் பொருள் தெரிந்து இசைக்கும்","கடுங்குரற் குடிஞைய நெடும்பெருங் குன்றம்,","எம்மொடு இறத்தலும் செல்லாய்; பின்நின்று,","ஒழியச் சூழ்ந்தனை ஆயின், தவிராது,","செல்இனி; சிறக்க நின் உள்ளம்! வல்லே","மறவல் ஓம்புமதி; எம்மே - நறவின்","சேயிதழ் அனைய ஆகிக், குவளை","மாஇதழ் புரையும் மலிர்கொள் ஈர்இமை,","உள்ளகம் கனல உள்ளுதொறு உலறி,","பழங்கண் கொண்ட, கதழ்ந்துவீழ், அவிர்அறல்","வெய்ய உகுதர, வெரீஇப், பையென,","சில்வளை சொரிந்த மெல்இறை முன்கை","பூவீழ் கொடியின் புல்லெனப் போகி;","அடர்செய் ஆய்அகல் சுடர் துணை ஆக,","இயங்காது வதிந்த நம் காதலி","உயங்குசாய் சிறுபுறம் முயங்கிய பின்னே!"],"p":"","r":"The man, on his way for wealth, tells his heart that it neither stayed with her then nor will cross the hills with him now, where the kite cries wearily from the tall yaa and the owl hoots like a drum; if it means to stay behind, let it go back, prosper, and not forget him, once it has embraced the tired drooping back of his love, who sits unmoving with a lamp for company, her dark lily eyelids gone red and burning with every thought of him, hot tears falling, bangles slipping from her wrist, wilting like a creeper that has shed its flowers.","n":"Here the heart that stands behind (பின்நின்று) holds back toward her, where in Akananuru 3 the heart behind drives him on; the man splits from his heart and sends it home in his stead. The lamp as her only companion is the poem's centre.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 19","lb":"A19","poet":"பொருந்தில் இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"நெஞ்சினாற் பொருள் வலிக்கப்பட்டுப் பிரிந்த தலைமகன்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","நெஞ்சினாற் பொருள் வலிக்கப்பட்டுப் பிரிந்த தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பருந்து","பருந்து",2,"pul","scene","kite"],["யாஉயர்","யா",3,"maram","scene",""],["உருள்துடி","துடி",4,"parai","simile",""],["குடிஞைய","குடிஞை",5,"pul","scene","owl"],["நறவின்","நறவு",9,"maram","simile","nara flower"],["குவளை","குவளை",10,"maram","simile",""],["கொடியின்","கொடி",16,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:20":{"book":"akananuru","num":21,"t":["பெருநீர் அழுவத்து எந்தை தந்த","கொழுமீன் உணங்கற் படுபுள் ஓப்பி,","எக்கர்ப் புன்னை இன்நிழல் அசைஇ","செக்கர் ஞெண்டின் குண்டு அளை கெண்டி,","ஞாழல் ஓங்குசினைத் தொடுத்த கொடுங்கழித்","தாழை வீழ்கயிற்று ஊசல் தூங்கிக்,","கொண்டல் இடுமணல் குரவை முனையின்","வெண்தலைப் புணரி ஆயமொடு ஆடி,","மணிப்பூம் பைந்தழை தைஇ, அணித்தகப்","பல்பூங் கானல் அல்கினம் வருதல்","கவ்வை நல்அணங்கு உற்ற, இவ்வூர்,","கொடிதுஅறி பெண்டிர் சொற்கொண்டு, அன்னை","கடிகொண் டனளே- தோழி:- பெருந்துறை,","எல்லையும் இரவும் என்னாது கல்லென","வலவன் ஆய்ந்த வண்பரி,","நிலவு மணல் கொட்கும்ஓர் தேர் உண்டு எனவே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl, while the lover stands unseen nearby, that their days of chasing birds from her father's drying fish, resting in the punnai's shade, digging out red crabs, swinging on the thazhai-root swing, playing with the waves and coming home in leaf skirts are over: mother, taking the word of the town's spiteful women, has put her under guard, because there is a chariot with fine horses that circles the moonlit sand of the great ford regardless of day or night.","n":"The list of shore games is a portrait of a girlhood now closed by gossip, and it is spoken for the hidden lover to hear: the last line names his chariot as the cause, so the report is also a summons to marry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 20","lb":"A20","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறி வந்த தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழி","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","பகற்குறி வந்த தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொழுமீன்","மீன்",2,"unavu","scene",""],["படுபுள்","புள்",2,"pul","scene",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",3,"maram","scene",""],["ஞெண்டின்","ஞெண்டு",4,"pira","scene","crab"],["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",5,"maram","scene",""],["தாழை","தாழை",6,"maram","scene",""],["பைந்தழை","தழை",9,"maram","ornament",""],["வண்பரி","பரி",15,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:1"},"a:21":{"book":"akananuru","num":22,"t":["'மனைஇள நொச்சி மௌவல் வால்முகைத்","துணை நிரைத்தன்ன; மாவீழ் வெண்பல்,","அவ்வயிற்று, அகன்ற அல்குல், தைஇத்","தாழ்மென் கூந்தல், தடமென் பணைத்தோள்","மடந்தை மாண்நலம் புலம்பச், சேய்நாட்டுச்","செல்லல் என்று யான் சொல்லவும், ஒல்லாய்","வினைநயந்து அமைந்தனை ஆயினை ; மனைநகப்","பல்வேறு வெறுக்கை தருகம்- வல்லே,","எழுஇனி, வாழி என் நெஞ்சே!- புரி இணர்","மெல்அவிழ் அம்சினை புலம்ப; வல்லோன்","கோடுஅறை கொம்பின் வீஉகத் தீண்டி","மராஅம் அலைத்த மணவாய்த் தென்றல்,","சுரம்செல் மள்ளர் சுரியல் தூற்றும்,","என்றூழ் நின்ற புன்தலை வைப்பில்","பருந்து இளைப்படூஉம் பாறுதலை ஓமை","இருங்கல் விடரகத்து, ஈன்று இளைப்பட்ட","மென்புனிற்று அம்பிணவு பசித்தெனப், பைங்கட்","செந்நாய் ஏற்றை கேழல் தாக்க,","இரியற் பிணவல் தீண்டலில், பரீஇச்","செங்காய் உதிர்த்த பைங்குலை ஈந்தின்","பரல்மண் சுவல முரண்நிலம் உடைத்த","வல்வாய்க் கணிச்சி, கூழார், கோவலர்","ஊறாது இட்ட உவலைக் கூவல்,","வெண்கோடு நயந்த அன்பில் கானவர்","இகழ்ந்தியங்கு இயவின் அகழ்ந்தகுழி செத்து,","இருங்களிற்று இனநிரை தூர்க்கும்","பெருங்கல் அத்தம் விலங்கிய காடே"],"p":"","r":"Halfway across a waterless forest, the man rebukes his heart, which now wants to turn back: it was the heart that refused his plea not to leave the girl with jasmine-bud teeth and settled on the venture, so it must rise at once and help bring home wealth that will make the house glad.","n":"The roles are reversed from the usual quarrel: the man had argued for staying, the heart for going, and now he holds the heart to its own choice. The goal is named as மனைநக, that the house may laugh, so the journey is cast as a gift to the home rather than flight from it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","selvam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 21","lb":"A21","poet":"காவன் முல்லைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் வலிக்கப்பட்டுப் பிரிந்த தலைமகன் இடைச் சுரத்து நின்று மீளலுற்ற நெஞ்சினைக் கழறியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","இடைச் சுரத்து நின்று மீளலுற்ற நெஞ்சினைக் கழறியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நொச்சி","நொச்சி",1,"maram","simile",""],["மௌவல்","மௌவல்",1,"maram","simile",""],["மராஅம்","மரா",12,"maram","scene",""],["பருந்து","பருந்து",15,"pul","scene",""],["ஓமை","ஓமை",15,"maram","scene",""],["பிணவு","பிணவு",17,"maa","iraicchi","female wild dog"],["செந்நாய் ஏற்றை","செந்நாய்",18,"maa","iraicchi","male wild dog"],["கேழல்","கேழல்",18,"maa","scene","boar"],["ஈந்தின்","ஈந்து",20,"maram","scene",""],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",22,"seythi","scene",""],["கானவர்","கானவர்",24,"seythi","scene",""],["இருங்களிற்று","களிறு",26,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","செந்நாய் ஏற்றை","confirmed","2/2","the road itself shows a male who fights to feed the mate he provides for, the very task the heart has abandoned halfway"]],"ch":"a:2"},"a:22":{"book":"akananuru","num":23,"t":["அணங்குடை நெடுவரை உச்சியின் இழிதரும்","கணம்கொள் அருவிக் கான்கெழு நாடன்","மணம்கமழ் வியன்மார்பு அணங்கிய செல்லல்","இதுஎன அறியா மறுவரற் பொழுதில்","'படியோர்த் தேய்த்த பல்புகழ்த் தடக்கை","நெடுவேட் பேண தணிகுவள் இவள்' என,","முதுவாய்ப் பெண்டிர் அதுவாய் கூற,","களம் நன்கு இழைத்துக் கண்ணி சூட்டி,","வளநகர் சிலம்பப் பாடிப் பலி கொடுத்து,","உருவச் செந்தினை குருதியொடு தூஉய்,","முருகுஆற்றுப் படுத்த உருகெழு நடுநாள்,","ஆரம் நாற, அருவிடர்த் ததைந்த","சாரல் பல்பூ வண்டுபடச் சூடி,","களிற்று - இரை தெரீஇய பார்வல் ஒதுக்கின்","ஒளித்து இயங்கும் மரபின் வயப்புலி போல,","நல்மனை, நெடுநகர்க் காவலர் அறியாமை","தன்நசை உள்ளத்து நம்நசை வாய்ப்ப,","இன்உயிர் குழைய முயங்குதொறும் மெய்ம்மலிந்து,","நக்கனென் அல்லெனோ யானே - எய்த்த","நோய்தணி காதலர் வர, ஈண்டு","ஏதில் வேலற்கு உலந்தமை கண்டே?"],"p":"","r":"The girl recalls the midnight rite her mother held to cure her: old women said Murugan's power had seized her, the velan sacrificed and scattered red millet with blood, and in that very night her lover slipped past the house guards like a hunting tiger, so that in his embrace she laughed at an offering wasted on a stranger priest while the true cure of her sickness had come.","n":"She speaks to defend the man her friend has just blamed, and the defence is a confession: he risked the guarded house on the night of the rite. Her laughter judges the rite only as a diagnosis; it does not deny the god, whose fierce presence (அணங்கு, உருகெழு) the poem keeps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kaamanoy","vazhipaadu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 22","lb":"A22","poet":"வெறிபாடிய காமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"(1) வரைவிடை வைத்துப் பிரிந்த காலத்துத் தலைமகள் ஆற்றாளாகத் தோழி தலைமகனை இயற்பழிப்பத் தலைமகள் இயற்பட மொழிந்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வரைவிடை வைத்துப் பிரிந்த காலத்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["அருவிக்","அருவி",2,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["நெடுவேட்","நெடுவேள்",6,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"],["கண்ணி","கண்ணி",8,"maram","ornament",""],["செந்தினை","தினை",10,"unavu","gift_food",""],["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",12,"maram","scene","sandal"],["பல்பூ","பூ",13,"maram","ornament",""],["வண்டுபட","வண்டு",13,"pira","scene",""],["களிற்று","களிறு",14,"maa","simile",""],["வயப்புலி","புலி",15,"maa","simile",""],["வேலற்கு","வேலன்",21,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:2"},"a:23":{"book":"akananuru","num":24,"t":["மண்கண் குளிர்ப்ப வீசி தண்பெயல்,","பாடு உலந்தன்றே, பறைக்குரல் எழிலி;","புதல்மிசைத் தளவின் இதல்முட் செந்நனை","நெருங்குகுலைப் பிடவமொடு ஒருங்குபிணி அவிழக்","காடே கம்மென் றன்றே; அவல,","கோடு உடைந்தன்ன கோடற் பைம்பயிர்,","பதவின் பாவை, முனைஇ, மதவுநடை","அண்ணல்இரலை அமர்பிணை தழீஇத்,","தண்அறல் பருகித் தாழ்ந்துபட் டனவே;","அனையகொல் - வாழி, தோழி!- மனைய","தாழ்வின் நொச்சி. சூழ்வன மலரும்","மௌவல். மாச்சினை காட்டி,","அவ்அளவு என்றார், ஆண்டுச்செய் பொருளே!"],"p":"","r":"The rains have spent themselves, the forest is fragrant with opening jasmine and pidavam, and in the hollows the stag, done grazing, embraces his doe, drinks the cool water and lies down; the woman asks her friend whether this is the time her man meant when he pointed to the jasmine on the house's nochi and said he would return by then from the wealth he went to earn.","n":"The token he chose, the jasmine around the house's own hedge, now blooms on time, so his sign testifies against him. The poem never says he is late; it lets the season and the stag say it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaalam","mazhai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 23","lb":"A23","poet":"ஒரோடோகத்துக் கந்தரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தளவின்","தளவு",3,"maram","scene",""],["பிடவமொடு","பிடவம்",4,"maram","scene",""],["கோடற்","கோடல்",6,"maram","simile",""],["பதவின்","பதவு",7,"maram","scene","grass"],["அண்ணல்இரலை","இரலை",8,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["பிணை","பிணை",8,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["நொச்சி","நொச்சி",11,"maram","scene",""],["மௌவல்","மௌவல்",12,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","அண்ணல்இரலை","confirmed","1/1","the season has brought even the stag to his mate's side, which is where the man who named this season should now be"]],"ch":"a:2"},"a:24":{"book":"akananuru","num":25,"t":["வேளாப் பார்ப்பான் வாளரந் துமித்த","வளைகளைந்து ஒழிந்த கொழுந்தின் அன்ன,","தலைபிணி அவிழா, சுரிமுகப் பகன்றை","சிதரல்அம் துவலை தூவலின், மலரும்","தைஇ நின்ற தண்பெயல் கடைநாள்,","வயங்குகதிர் கரந்த வாடை வைகறை,","விசும்பு உரிவதுபோல், வியல்இடத்து ஒழுகி,","மங்குல் மாமழை, தென்புலம் படரும்","பனிஇருங் கங்குலும் தமியள் நீந்தி,","தம்ஊ ரோளே, நன்னுதல்; யாமே","கடிமதில் கதவம் பாய்தலின், தொடிபிளந்து","நுதிமுகம் மழுகிய மண்ணை வெண்கோட்டுச்,","சிறுகண் யானை நெடுநா ஒண் மணி,","கழிப்பிணிக் கறைத்தோல் பொழிகணை உதைப்பு,","தழங்குகுரல் முரசமொடு முழங்கும் யாமத்து,","கழித்துஉறை செறியா வாளுடை எறுழ்த்தோள்,","இரவு துயில் மடிந்த தானை,","உரவுச்சின வேந்தன் பாசறை யேமே!"],"p":"","r":"At the end of the cold rains, when pakanrai buds open in the drizzle and the north wind hides the dawn, the man says his bright-browed wife swims alone through the long cold nights in her own town, while he is in the camp of a fierce king where elephants with tusks blunted on fort gates jangle their bells, drums roar at midnight and soldiers with unsheathed swords never sleep.","n":"யாமே answers தம்ஊரோளே: the two halves weigh her sleepless night at home against the camp's sleepless night, so neither of them sleeps. The conch-cutter's discarded rings in the first simile are bangles, the wife's own ornament, set among the season's flowers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","padai","kaalam","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 24","lb":"A24","poet":"ஆவூர் மூலங்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"(1) தலைமகன் பருவங் கண்டு சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","பருவங் கண்டு சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேளாப் பார்ப்பான்","பார்ப்பான்",1,"seythi","simile","conch-cutter"],["பகன்றை","பகன்றை",3,"maram","simile",""],["யானை","யானை",13,"maa","scene",""],["முரசமொடு","முரசம்",15,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:2"},"a:25":{"book":"akananuru","num":26,"t":["\"நெடுங்கரைக் கான்யாற்றுக் கடும்புனல் சாஅய,","அவிர்அறல் கொண்ட விரவுமணல் அகன்துறைத்","தண்கயம் நண்ணிய பொழில்தொறும், காஞ்சிப்","பைந்தாது அணிந்த போதுமலி எக்கர்,","வதுவை நாற்றம் புதுவது கஞல,","மாநனை கொழுதிய மணிநிற இருங்குயில்","படுநா விளியா னடுநின்று, அல்கலும்,","உரைப்ப போல, ஊழ் கொள்பு கூவ,","இனச்சிதர் உகுத்த இலவத்து ஆங்கண்","சினைப்பூங் கோங்கின் நுண்தாது பகர்நர்","பவளச் செப்பில் பொன்சொரிந் தன்ன,","இகழுநர் இகழா இளநாள் அமையம்","செய்தோர் மன்ற குறி\" என, நீநின்","பைதல் உண்கண் பனிவார்பு உறைப்ப","வாரா மையின் புலர்ந்த நெஞ்சமொடு,","நோவல், குறுமகள்! நோயியர், என் உயிர்!\" என,","மெல்லிய இனிய கூறி, வல்லே","வருவர் வாழி - தோழி - பொருநர்","செல்சமம் கடந்த வில்கெழு தடக்கைப்","பொதியிற் செல்வன், பொலந்தேர்த் திதியன்","இன்இசை இயத்தின் கறங்கும்","கல்மிசை அருவிய காடு இறந்தோரோ!"],"p":"","r":"Seeing her weep that spring, with its sandbanks of kaanji pollen and its cuckoos calling in turn, is surely the sign her man set for his return, her friend promises that he who crossed the hills whose falls sound like the music of Thithiyan, lord of Pothiyil, will come quickly with soft words of sorrow for her tears.","n":"The friend puts apology into the absent man's mouth before he has come (நோவல், குறுமகள்), so her comfort is a script for his return. The spring is described as a season that even scorners cannot scorn, which makes his absence from it harder to excuse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaalam","inchol","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 25","lb":"A25","poet":"ஒல்லையூர் தந்த பூதப் பாண்டியன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பருவங்கண்டழிந்த தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவங்கண்டழிந்த தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கான்யாற்றுக்","கான்யாறு",1,"pira","scene","forest stream"],["காஞ்சிப்","காஞ்சி",3,"maram","scene",""],["மாநனை","மா",6,"maram","scene","mango"],["இருங்குயில்","குயில்",6,"pul","simile",""],["இனச்சிதர்","சிதர்",9,"pira","scene","bee"],["இலவத்து","இலவம்",9,"maram","scene","silk-cotton"],["கோங்கின்","கோங்கு",10,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["hill","பொதியிற்",20],["king","திதியன்",20]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:2"},"a:26":{"book":"akananuru","num":27,"t":["கூன்முள் முள்ளிக் குவிகுலைக் கழன்ற,","மீன்முள் அன்ன, வெண்கால் மாமலர்","பொய்தல் மகளிர் விழவுஅணி கூட்டும்","அவ்வயல் தண்ணிய வளம்கேழ் ஊரனைப்","புலத்தல் கூடுமோ - தோழி! - அல்கல்","பெருங் கதவு பொருத யானை மருப்பின்","இரும்புசெய் தொடியின் ஏர ஆகி,","'மாக்கண் அடைய மார்பகம் பொருந்தி","முயங்கல் விடாஅல் இவை' என மயங்கி,","'யான் ஓம்' என்னவும் ஒல்லார், தாம்மற்று","இவை பாராட்டிய பருவமும் உளவே ; இனியே","புதல்வற் றடுத்த பாலொடு தடஇத்","திதலை அணிந்த தேம்கொள் மென்முலை","நறுஞ் சாந்து அணிந்த கேழ்கிளர் அகலம்","வீங்க முயங்கல் யாம்வேண் டினமே","தீம்பால் படுதல் தாம் அஞ்சினரே; ஆயிடைக்","கவவுக்கை நெகிழ்ந்தமை போற்றி, மதவுநடைச்","செவிலி கைஎன் புதல்வனை நோக்கி,","'நல்லோர்க்கு ஒத்தனிர் நீயிர் ; இஃதோ","செல்வற்கு ஒத்தனம் யாம்' என, மெல்லஎன்","மகன் வயின் பெயர்தந் தேனே; அதுகண்டு","'யாமும் காதலம், அவற்கு' எனச் சாஅய்","சிறுபுறம் கவையின னாக, உறுபெயல்","தண்துளிக்கு ஏற்ற பலஉழு செஞ்செய்","மண்போல் நெகிழ்ந்து, அவன் கலுழ்ந்தே","நெஞ்சுஅறை போகிய அறிவி னேற்கே?"],"p":"","r":"The wife asks her friend how she could sulk with her husband: once he praised her breasts and would not stop embracing, but lately he held back for fear of spilling the milk meant for their son, so she turned to the boy in the nurse's arms saying he suits the good women and she suits the son, and at that he said he too loved the child, held her from behind and wept, melting like ploughed red earth in the rain.","n":"The child decides the quarrel twice: he is her reason to withdraw and the man's way back to her. The rain-on-ploughed-field simile gives the melting to him, so her question (புலத்தல் கூடுமோ) means that his tears have left her no ground for sulking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","ilvaazhkkai","makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 26","lb":"A26","poet":"பாண்டியன் கானப் பெரெயில் தந்த உக்கிரப்பெருவழுதி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைமகன் தோழியை வாயில் வேண்டி அவளால் தான் வாயில் பெறாது ஆற்றாமையே வாயிலாகப் புக்குக் கூடிய தலைமகனீக்கத்துக்கண் புக்க தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைமகனீக்கத்துக்கண் புக்க தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["முள்ளிக்","முள்ளி",1,"maram","simile",""],["மீன்முள்","மீன்",2,"pira","simile",""],["யானை","யானை",6,"maa","simile",""],["செஞ்செய்","செய்",24,"pira","simile","field"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:2"},"a:27":{"book":"akananuru","num":28,"t":["\"கொடுவரி இரும்புலி தயங்க, நெடுவரை","ஆடுகழை இருவெதிர் கோடைக்கு ஒல்கும்","கானம் கடிய என்னார், நாம்அழ,","நின்றதுஇல் பொருட்பிணிச் சென்றுஇவண் தருமார்,","செல்ப\" என்ப, என்போய்! நல்ல","மடவை மன்ற நீயே; வடவயின்","வேங்கடம் பயந்த வெண்கோட்டு யானை,","மறப்போர்ப் பாண்டியர் அறத்தின் காக்கும்","கொற்கைஅம் பெருந்துறை முத்தின் அன்ன","நகைப்பொலிந்து இலங்கும் எயிறுகெழு துவர்வாய்","தகைப்பத் தங்கலர் ஆயினும், இகப்ப","யாங்ஙனம் விடுமோ மற்றே - தேம்படத்","தெள்நீர்க்கு ஏற்ற திரள்காற் குவளைப்","பெருந்தகை சிதைத்தும், அமையா பருந்துபட","வேந்துஅமர்க் கடந்த வென்றி நல்வேல்","குருதியொடு துயல்வந் தன்னநின்","அரிவேய் உண்கண் அமர்த்த நோக்கே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl, who has paled on hearing that her man will leave to fetch wealth across the tiger's forest, that she is naive: even if her red mouth with teeth like the pearls of Korkai does not hold him, how could her eyes, which outdo the blue lily and fight like a victorious spear swinging with blood, let him go?","n":"The friend argues from the girl's own beauty, which is a comfort and a reproach at once: her body should be his reason to stay. The pearl simile carries a whole Pandya world (the Venkatam elephant, Korkai guarded by aram) into a two-line image of her teeth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","azhagu","kan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 27","lb":"A27","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவுணர்ந்து வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","செலவுணர்ந்து வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","மடன்"]],"k":[["இரும்புலி","புலி",1,"maa","scene",""],["இருவெதிர்","வெதிர்",2,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","scene",""],["முத்தின்","முத்து",9,"pira","simile",""],["குவளைப்","குவளை",13,"maram","simile",""],["பருந்துபட","பருந்து",14,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[["hill","வேங்கடம்",7],["king","பாண்டியர்",8],["town","கொற்கை",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:2"},"a:28":{"book":"akananuru","num":29,"t":["மெய்யின் தீரா மேவரு காமமொடு","எய்யாய் ஆயினும், உரைப்பல் - தோழி!","கொய்யா முன்னும், குரல்வார்பு, தினையே","அருவி ஆன்ற பைங்கால் தோறும்","இருவி தோன்றின பலவே; நீயே,","முருகு முரண்கொள்ளும் தேம்பாய் கண்ணி,","பரியல் நாயொடு பன்மலைப் படரும்","வேட்டுவற் பெறலொடு அமைந்தனை; யாழநின்","பூக்கெழு தொடலை நுடங்க, எழுந்து எழுந்து","கிள்ளைத் தெள்விளி இடைஇடை பயிற்றி","ஆங்காங்கு ஒழுகாய்ஆயின், அன்னை,","'சிறு கிளி கடிதல் தேற்றாள் இவள்' எனப்,","பிறர்த் தந்து நிறுக்குவள் ஆயின்,","உறற்கு அரிது ஆகும், அவன் மலர்ந்த மார்பே!"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening nearby, the friend warns the girl that the millet ears are bowing and harvested stalks are showing in the fields: if she, content with her hunter, does not rise and scare the parrots, her mother will say she cannot guard the crop and send others, and then his broad chest will be hard to reach.","n":"The warning is meant for the overhearing man: the ripening millet dates the end of the field meetings, so he must turn the secret love into marriage before the harvest closes it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kaalam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 28","lb":"A28","poet":"பாண்டியன் அறிவுடை நம்பி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தோழி சொல்லியது. இது, பகலே சிறைப்புறம்.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["தினையே","தினை",3,"unavu","scene","millet"],["இருவி","இருவி",5,"unavu","scene","harvested stalk"],["கண்ணி","கண்ணி",6,"maram","ornament",""],["நாயொடு","நாய்",7,"maa","scene",""],["வேட்டுவற்","வேட்டுவன்",8,"seythi","scene",""],["தொடலை","தொடலை",9,"maram","ornament",""],["கிள்ளைத்","கிள்ளை",10,"pul","scene",""],["கிளி","கிளி",12,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:2"},"a:29":{"book":"akananuru","num":30,"t":["\"தொடங்கு வினை தவிர, அசைவில் நோன்தாள்,","கிடந்துஉயிர் மறுகுவது ஆயினும், இடம்படின்","வீழ்களிறு மிசையாப் புலியினும் சிறந்த","தாழ்வுஇல் உள்ளம் தலைத்தலைச் சிறப்பப்","செய்வினைக்கு அகன்ற காலை, எஃகு உற்று","இருவேறு ஆகிய தெரிதகு வனப்பின்","மாவின் நறுவடி போலக், காண்தொறும்","மேவல் தண்டா மகிழ்நோக்கு உண்கண்","நினையாது கழிந்த வைகல், எனையதூஉம்","வாழலென் யான்\" எனத் தேற்றிப், \"பல்மாண்","தாழக் கூறிய தகைசால் நன்மொழி","மறந்தனிர் போறிர் எம்' எனச் சிறந்தநின்","எயிறுகெழு துவர்வாய் இன்நகை அழுங்க","வினவல் ஆனாப் புனையிழை!- கேள்இனி-","வெம்மை தண்டா எரிஉகு பறந்தலை,","கொம்மை வாடிய இயவுள் யானை","நீர்மருங்கு அறியாது, தேர்மருங்கு ஓடி,","அறுநீர் அம்பியின் நெறிமுதல் உணங்கும்","உள்ளுநர்ப் பனிக்கும் ஊக்கு அருங் கடத்திடை,","எள்ளல் நோனாப் பொருள்தரல் விருப்பொடு","நாணுத் தளைஆக வைகி, மாண்வினைக்கு","உடம்பு ஆண்டு ஒழிந்தமை அல்லதை,","மடம்கெழு நெஞ்சம் நின் உழை யதுவே!"],"p":"","r":"Back from his work, the man answers his wife, who asks with a fading smile whether he forgot her: he left vowing that a day without thinking of her eyes would not be worth living, and in the burning waste where elephants chase mirages and wilt like boats in dried water, only his body stayed there, bound by shame to finish the task, while his foolish heart was always with her.","n":"He splits himself to answer the charge: the body (உடம்பு) was held by நாண், the shame of an unfinished task, and the heart (நெஞ்சம்) never left. The tiger that will not eat prey fallen on its left side measures his resolve by a hunter's code of honour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","naanam","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 29","lb":"A29","poet":"வெள்ளாடியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றி மீண்ட தலைமகன் எம்மையும் நினைத்தறிதிரோ? என்ற தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றி மீண்ட தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வீழ்களிறு","களிறு",3,"maa","simile",""],["புலியினும்","புலி",3,"maa","simile",""],["மாவின்","மா",7,"maram","simile","mango"],["யானை","யானை",16,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:2"},"a:30":{"book":"akananuru","num":31,"t":["நெடுங்கயிறு வலந்த குறுங்கண் அவ்வலை,","கடல்பாடு அழிய, இனமீன் முகந்து,","துணைபுணர் உவகையர் பரத மாக்கள்","இளையரும் முதியரும் கிளையுடன் துவன்றி","உப்புஒய் உமணர் அருந்துறை போக்கும்","ஒழுகை நோன்பகடு ஒப்பக் குழீஇ","அயிர்திணி அடைகரை ஒலிப்ப வாங்கிப்,","பெருங்களம் தொகுத்த உழவர் போல,","இரந்தோர் வறுங்கலம் மல்க வீசி,","பாடுபல அமைத்துக் கொள்ளை சாற்றிக்","கோடுஉயர் திணிமணல் துஞ்சும் துறைவ!","பெருமை என்பது கெடுமோ - ஒருநாள்","மண்ணா முத்தம் அரும்பிய புன்னைத்","தண்நறுங் கானல் வந்து \"நும்","வண்ணம் எவனோ?\" என்றனிர் செலினே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of a shore where fishermen haul in their catch together like ploughmen at a threshing floor, fling fish to fill the empty pots of those who beg, and sleep on the high dunes, that greatness is not lost by coming one day to the cool punnai grove and asking how she fares, and implies that to ask once and leave is no greatness.","n":"The two closing lines can be read as a plea (would your greatness suffer if you came and asked?) or as a reproach (is it greatness to come once, ask, and go?); the generosity of his own fishermen is set before him as a standard either way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["perumai","kalaviyal","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 30","lb":"A30","poet":"முடங்கிக் கிடந்த, நெடுஞ்சேரலாதன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறிவந்த தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகற்குறிவந்த தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இனமீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","scene",""],["பரத மாக்கள்","பரதவர்",3,"seythi","iraicchi",""],["உமணர்","உமணர்",5,"seythi","simile",""],["நோன்பகடு","பகடு",6,"maa","simile",""],["உழவர்","உழவர்",8,"seythi","simile",""],["புன்னைத்","புன்னை",13,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பரத மாக்கள்","not_confirmed","0/1","the lord of a shore whose people give so freely should not withhold from her the small gift of his concern, and with it the promise of marriage"]],"ch":"a:2"},"a:31":{"book":"akananuru","num":32,"t":["நெருப்புஎனச் சிவந்த உருப்புஅவிர் மண்டிலம்","புலங்கடை மடங்க தெறுதலின், ஞொள்கி,","\"நிலம்புடை பெயர்வது அன்றுகொல், இன்று?\" என","மன்உயிர் மடிந்த மழைமாறு அமையத்து,","இலைஇல ஓங்கிய நிலைஉயர் யாஅத்து","மேற்கவட்டு இருந்த பார்ப்பினங் கட்குக்","கல்லுடைக் குறும்பின் வயவர் வில்இட,","நிணவரிக் குறைந்த நிறத்த அதர்தொறும்,","கணவிர மாலை அடூஉக் கழிந்தன்ன","புண்உமிழ் குருதி பரிப்பக் கிடந்தோர்","கண்உமிழ் கழுகின் கானம் நீந்திச்,","'சென்றார்' என்பு இலர் -தோழி!- வென்றியொடு","வில்இலைத்து உண்ணும் வல்ஆண் வாழ்க்கைத்","தமிழ்கெழு மூவர் காக்கும்","மொழிபெயர் தேஎத்த பன்மலை இறந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Hearing it said that she cannot bear the parting, the woman tells her friend of the road her man took: a season of failed rain when the fiery sun scorched the land, where bowmen of the rock forts shoot travellers whose eyes the vultures pluck, beyond which lie the many hills of the land of changed speech, outside the rule of the three Tamil kings.","n":"The road ends at the border of the language: the measure of his distance is where Tamil stops being spoken (மொழிபெயர் தேஎத்த). The sense of 'சென்றார்' என்பு இலர் is unclear to me, and the colophon thurai breaks off before naming the speaker.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","acham","thamizh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 31","lb":"A31","poet":"மாமுலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாயினாளென்று பிறர் சொல்லக் கேட்டு","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாயினாளென்று பிறர் சொல்லக் கேட்டு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["யாஅத்து","யா",5,"maram","scene",""],["பார்ப்பினங்","பார்ப்பு",6,"pul","scene","fledglings"],["வயவர்","வயவர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["கணவிர","கணவிரம்",9,"maram","simile","oleander"],["கழுகின்","கழுகு",11,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:2"},"a:32":{"book":"akananuru","num":33,"t":["நெருநல் எல்லை ஏனல் தோன்றிச்","திருமணி ஒளிர்வரும் பூணன் வந்து,","புரவலன் போலும் தோற்றம் உறழ்கொள,","இரவல் மாக்களின் பணிமொழி பயிற்றிச்,","சிறுதினைப் படுகிளி கடீஇயர், பன்மாண்","குளிர்கொள் தட்டை மதன்இல புடையாச்","'சூரர மகளிரின் நின்ற நீமற்று","யாரையோ? எம் அணங்கியோய்! உண்கு' எனச்","சிறுபுறம் கவையினனாக, அதற்கொண்டு","இகுபெயல் மண்ணின் ஞெகிழ்பு, அஞர்உற்ற என்","உள்அவன் அறிதல் அஞ்சி, உள்இல்","கடிய கூடு, கைபிணி விடாஅ","வெரூஉம் மான் பிணையின் ஒரீஇ, நின்ற","என்உரத் தகைமையின் பெயர்த்து, பிறிதுஎன்வயின்","சொல்ல வல்லிற்றும் இலனே; அல்லாந்து,","இனம்தீர் களிற்றின் பெயர்ந்தோன் இன்றும்","தோலாவாறு இல்லை- தோழி! நாம் சென்மோ,","சாய்இறைப் பணைத்தோட் கிழமை தனக்கே","மாசு இன் றாதலும் அறியான், ஏசற்று,","என்குறைப் புறனிலை முயலும்","அங்க ணாளனை நகுகம், யாமே!"],"p":"","r":"She tells her friend how yesterday a jewelled man, looking like a patron but pleading like a suppliant, came to the millet field, called her a hill goddess who had stricken him and held her from behind; melting inside but afraid he would see it, she broke free like a startled doe and stood firm, and he went off like an elephant parted from its herd, so now she proposes they go and laugh at the gracious man who keeps trying, not knowing her shoulders are his alone.","n":"Puram's pair of giver and receiver (புரவலன், இரவல்) is turned inside out: the man who looks like a patron begs. The plan to laugh at him is an assent in disguise, since in the same breath she says the claim to her shoulders is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalaviyal","naanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 32","lb":"A32","poet":"நல்வெள்ளியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"(1) பின்னின்ற தலைமகற்குக் குறை நேர்ந்த தோழிதலைமகட்குக் குறைநயப்பக் கூறியது. (2) தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொற்றதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","பின்னின்ற தலைமகற்குக் குறை நேர்ந்த தோழி"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொற்றதூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",1,"unavu","scene","millet field"],["சிறுதினைப்","தினை",5,"unavu","scene",""],["படுகிளி","கிளி",5,"pul","scene",""],["மான்","மான்",13,"maa","simile","deer"],["களிற்றின்","களிறு",16,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:2"},"a:33":{"book":"akananuru","num":34,"t":["வினைநன் றாதல் வெறுப்பக் காட்டி,","மனைமாண் கற்பின் வாணுதல் ஒழியக்,","கவைமுறி இழந்த செந்நிலை யாஅத்து","ஒன்றுஓங்கு உயர்சினை இருந்த வன்பறை,","வீளைப் பருந்தின் கோள்வல் சேவல்","வளைவாய்ப் பேடை வருதிறம் பயிரும்","இளிதேர் தீங்குரல் இசைக்கும் அத்தம்","செலவு அருங்குரைய என்னாது, சென்று, அவண்","மலர்பாடு ஆன்ற, மைஎழில், மழைக்கண்","தெளியா நோக்கம் உள்ளினை, உளிவாய்","வெம்பரல் அதர குன்றுபல நீந்தி,","யாமே எமியம்ஆக, நீயே","ஒழியச் சூழ்ந்தனை ஆயின் - முனாஅது","வெல்போர் வானவன் கொல்லி மீமிசை,","நுணங்குஅமை புரையும் வணங்குஇறைப் பணைத் தோள்,","வரிஅணி அல்குல், வால்எயிற் றோள்வயிற்","பிரியாய் ஆயின் நன்றுமற் றில்ல.","அன்றுநம் அறியாய் ஆயினும், இன்றுநம்","செய்வினை ஆற்றுற விலங்கின்","எய்துவை அல்லையோ, பிறர்நகு பொருளோ?"],"p":"","r":"On the road the man tells his heart, which wants to turn back: it chose to leave the virtuous wife and to cross the land where a kite calls its mate, and now, halfway over the gravel hills, it would be better never to have parted from her with shoulders like the bamboo of Vanavan's Kolli; but to break off the work now would only win the laughter of others.","n":"The poem does not deny the heart's longing; it concedes that never leaving would have been good (நன்றுமற் றில்ல) and then argues that turning back now is worse. Shame before others (பிறர்நகு) is the last argument, not wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","pazhi","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 33","lb":"A33","poet":"மதுரை அளக்கர் ஞாழார் மகனார் மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் இடைச்சுரத்துத் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","இடைச்சுரத்துத் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யாஅத்து","யா",3,"maram","scene",""],["சேவல்","பருந்து",5,"pul","iraicchi","male kite"],["பேடை","பேடை",6,"pul","iraicchi","female kite"],["நுணங்குஅமை","அமை",15,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[["king","வானவன்",14],["hill","கொல்லி",14]],"dv":[["iraicchi","சேவல்","confirmed","1/1","even the kite on this dry road calls its mate to its side, while the man has left his wife and may not even turn back to her"]],"ch":"a:2"},"a:34":{"book":"akananuru","num":35,"t":["சிறுகரும் பிடவின் வெண்தலைக் குறும்புதல்","கண்ணியின் மலரும் தண்நறும் புறவில்,","தொடுதோற் கானவன் கவைபொறுத் தன்ன","இருதிரி மருப்பின் அண்ணல் இரலை","செறிஇலைப் பதவின் செங்கோல் மென்குரல்","மறிஆடு மருங்கின் மடப்பிணை அருத்தித்,","தெள்அறல் தழீஇய வார்மணல் அடைகரை,","மெல்கிடு கவுள துஞ்சுபுறம் காக்கும்","பெருந்தகைக்கு உடைந்த நெஞ்சம் ஏமுறச்;","செல்க, தேரே - நல்வலம் பெறுந!","பசைகொல் மெல்விரல், பெருந்தோள், புலைத்தி","துறைவிட் டன்ன தூமயிர் எகினம்","துணையொடு திளைக்கும் காப்புடை வரைப்பிற்,","செந்தார்ப் பைங்கிளி முன்கை ஏந்தி,","'இன்றுவரல் உரைமோ, சென்றிசினோர் திறத்து' என,","இல்லவர் அறிதல் அஞ்சி, மெல்லென","மழலை இன்சொல் பயிற்றும்","நாணுடை அரிவை மாண்நலம் பெறவே!"],"p":"","r":"His task done, the man urges his charioteer to hurry through the cool scrub where a stag feeds tender grass to his young doe and guards her while she sleeps, so that his heart may find ease with his shy wife, who in the house where swans play with their mates holds a parrot on her wrist and softly teaches it to say 'tell me he comes today', afraid the household will hear.","n":"Both partners speak through creatures: his longing is shown in the stag's care, hers in the words she teaches the parrot because she is too shy to say them aloud.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","ilvaazhkkai","naanam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 34","lb":"A34","poet":"மதுரை மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பிடவின்","பிடவு",1,"maram","simile",""],["கானவன்","கானவன்",3,"seythi","simile",""],["இரலை","இரலை",4,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["பதவின்","பதவு",5,"maram","scene","grass"],["மடப்பிணை","பிணை",6,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["புலைத்தி","புலைத்தி",11,"seythi","simile","washerwoman"],["எகினம்","எகினம்",12,"pul","simile","swan"],["பைங்கிளி","கிளி",14,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","இரலை","confirmed","1/1","the stag's care is the care the man is hurrying home to give his wife"]],"ch":"a:2"},"a:35":{"book":"akananuru","num":36,"t":["ஈன்று புறந்தந்த எம்மும் உள்ளாள்,","வான்தோய் இஞ்சி நன்னகர் புலம்பத்-","தனிமணி இரட்டும் தாளுடைக் கடிகை","நுழைநுதி நெடுவேல், குறும்படை மழவர்","முனைஆத் தந்து முரம்பின் வீழ்த்த","வில்ஏர் வாழ்க்கை விழுத்தொடை மறவர்","வல்ஆண் பதுக்கைக் கடவுட் பேண்மார்;","நடுகல் பீலி சூட்டித்; துடிப்படுத்துத்,","தோப்பிக் கள்ளொடு துரூஉப்பலி கொடுக்கும்","போக்குஅருங் கவலைய புலவுநாறு அருஞ்சுரம்","துணிந்து பிறள் ஆயினள் ஆயினும், அணிந்து அணிந்து,","ஆர்வ நெஞ்சமொடு ஆய்நலன் அளைஇத், தன்","மார்பு துணையாகத் துயிற்றுக தில்ல-","துஞ்சா முழவிற் கோவற் கோமான்","நெடுந்தேர்க் காரி கொடுங்கான் முன்துறை,","பெண்ணையம் பேரியாற்று நுண் அறல் கடுக்கும்","நெறிஇருங் கதுப்பின்என் பேதைக்கு,","அறியாத் தேஎத்து ஆற்றிய துணையே!"],"p":"","r":"A mother whose daughter has run off with a man prays: the girl, forgetting those who bore and raised her, left the high-walled house desolate and dared the flesh-smelling road where bowmen honour hero-stones with peacock feathers, drums and toddy; may the stranger who became her companion in an unknown land adorn her again and again and put her to sleep on his chest, her curled hair like the fine ripples of Kari's Pennai river.","n":"The prayer hands the stranger her own office: அணிந்து அணிந்து, adorning again and again, is what a mother does. The road is marked by offerings to the dead, so the daughter's path into marriage is drawn through a landscape of graves.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal","anbu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 35","lb":"A35","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட்போக்கிய நற்றாய் தெய்வத்திற்குப் பராஅயது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,null,"மகட்போக்கிய நற்றாய் தெய்வத்திற்குப் பராஅயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மழவர்","மழவர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["முனைஆத்","ஆ",5,"maa","scene","cattle"],["மறவர்","மறவர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["பீலி","பீலி",8,"pul","scene","peacock feather"],["துடிப்படுத்துத்","துடி",8,"parai","scene",""],["தோப்பிக் கள்ளொடு","கள்",9,"unavu","gift_food",""],["முழவிற்","முழவு",14,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["herostone","பதுக்கைக்",7],["herostone","நடுகல்",8],["town","கோவற்",14],["king","காரி",15],["river","பெண்ணையம்",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:2"},"a:36":{"book":"akananuru","num":37,"t":["பகுவாய் வராஅல் பல்வரி இரும்போத்துக்","கொடுவாய் இரும்பின் கோள்இரை துற்றி,","ஆம்பல் மெல்லடை கிழியக் குவளைக்","கூம்புவிடு பன்மலர் சிதையப் பாய்ந்து, எழுந்து,","அரில்படு வள்ளை ஆய்கொடி மயக்கித்","தூண்டில் வேட்டுவன் வாங்க வாராது,","கயிறுஇடு கதச்சேப் போல மதம்மிக்கு,","நாள், கயம் உழக்கும் பூக்கேழ் ஊர","வருபுனல் வையை வார்மணல் அகன்துறைத்","திருமருது ஓங்கிய விரிமலர்க் காவில்,","நறும்பல் கூந்தற் குறுந்தொடி மடந்தையொடு","வதுவை அயர்ந்தனை என்ப அலரே,","கொய்சுவற் புரவிக் கொடித்தேர்ச் செழியன்","ஆலங் கானத்து அகன்தலை சிவப்பச்,","சேரல், செம்பியன், சினம்கெழு திதியன்,","போர்வல் யானைப் பொலம்பூண் எழினி,","நார்அரி நறவின் எருமை யூரன்,","தேம்கமழ் அகலத்துப் புலர்ந்த சாந்தின்","இருங்கோ வேண்மான், இயல்தேர்ப் பொருநன், என்று","எழுவர் நல்வலம் அடங்க, ஒருபகல்","முரைசொடு வெண்குடை அகப்படுத்து, உரைசெலக்,","கொன்று களம்வேட்ட ஞான்றை,","வென்றிகொள் வீரர் ஆர்ப்பினும் பெரிதே!"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her husband, lord of a town where a great striped varal fish swallows the hook, tears the water-lily leaves, crushes the blue lilies, tangles the vallai creeper and will not come to the angler's pull, raging like a roped bull as it churns the pond each day, that the gossip of his wedding with a young girl in the marutham grove on the Vaigai's sands is louder than the shout of Chezhiyan's warriors on the day he crushed seven kings at Alankanam.","n":"The gossip is measured by the most famous victory shout the audience knew, so a private wrong is given the volume of a public war. The fish's scene is the wife's hidden charge; the battle simile is her open one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","alar","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 36","lb":"A36","poet":"மதுரை நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைமகள் பரத்தையிற் பிரிந்து வந்த தலைமகனோடு புலந்து சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","பரத்தையிற் பிரிந்து வந்த தலைமகனோடு புலந்து சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["vekuli","குடிகோள்"]],"k":[["வராஅல்","வரால்",1,"pira","ullurai","varal fish"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",3,"maram","ullurai",""],["குவளைக்","குவளை",3,"maram","ullurai",""],["வள்ளை","வள்ளை",5,"maram","ullurai",""],["வேட்டுவன்","வேட்டுவன்",6,"seythi","ullurai","angler"],["கதச்சேப்","சே",7,"maa","simile","bull"],["திருமருது","மருது",10,"maram","scene",""],["புரவிக்","புரவி",13,"maa","scene",""],["யானைப்","யானை",16,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["river","வையை",9],["king","செழியன்",13],["battle","ஆலங் கானத்து",14],["king","சேரல்",15],["king","செம்பியன்",15],["king","திதியன்",15],["king","எழினி",16],["king","எருமை யூரன்",17],["king","இருங்கோ வேண்மான்",19],["king","பொருநன்",19]],"dv":[["ullurai","வராஅல்","confirmed","1/1","the husband and his new women"]],"ch":"a:2"},"a:37":{"book":"akananuru","num":38,"t":["மறந்து, அவண் அமையார் ஆயினும், கறங்கு இசைக்","கங்குல் ஓதைக் கலிமகிழ் உழவர்","பொங்கழி முகந்த தாஇல் நுண்துகள்,","மங்குல் வானின், மாதிரம் மறைப்ப,","வைகுபுலர் விடியல் வைபெயர்த்து ஆட்டித்","தொழிற் செருக்கு அனந்தர்வீட, எழில்தகை","வளியொடு சினைஇய வண்தளிர் மாஅத்துக்","கிளிபோல் காய கிளைத்துணர் வடித்துப்","புளிப்பதன் அமைந்த புதுக்குட மலிர்நிறை","வெயில்வெரிந் நிறுத்த பயில்இதழ்ப் பசுங்குடைக்,","கயமண்டு பகட்டின் பருகிக், காண்வரக்","கொள்ளொடு பயறுபால் விரைஇ, வெள்ளிக்","கோல் வரைந்தன்ன வால்அவிழ் மிதவை","வாங்குகை தடுத்த பின்றை ஓங்கிய,","பருதிஅம் குப்பை சுற்றிப், பகல்செல,","மருத மரநிழல், எருதொடு வதியும்","காமர் வேனில்மன் இது,","மாண்நலம் நுகரும் துணைஉடை யோர்க்கே!"],"p":"","r":"Breaking down under her friend's reassurance, the woman says that even if he has not forgotten her, this is the lovely spring: farmers thresh all night under dust like mist, rise at dawn to pluck mangoes green as parrots, drink new brew from leaf cups like oxen at a pond, eat horsegram and lentil porridge with milk, and rest at noon with their oxen in the marutham shade, a sweet season only for those who have partners to enjoy.","n":"The farmers' full day, from night threshing to noon's rest with the oxen, is a picture of shared life; only the last line turns the whole landscape into her lack.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaalam","uzhavu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 37","lb":"A37","poet":"விற்றூற்று மூதெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"(1) தலைமகள் வன்புறை யெதிரழிந்து சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","வன்புறை யெதிரழிந்து சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["உழவர்","உழவர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["மாஅத்துக்","மா",7,"maram","scene","mango"],["கிளிபோல்","கிளி",8,"pul","simile",""],["பகட்டின்","பகடு",11,"maa","simile","ox"],["கொள்ளொடு","கொள்",12,"unavu","gift_food","horsegram"],["பயறுபால்","பயறு",12,"unavu","gift_food",""],["மிதவை","மிதவை",13,"unavu","gift_food","porridge"],["மருத","மருது",16,"maram","scene",""],["எருதொடு","எருது",16,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:2"},"a:38":{"book":"akananuru","num":39,"t":["விரிஇணர் வேங்கை வண்டுபடு கண்ணியன்,","தெரிஇதழ்க் குவளைத் தேம்பாய் தாரன்,","அம்சிலை இடவதுஆக, வெஞ் செலற்","கணைவலம் தெரிந்து, துணை படர்ந்து உள்ளி;","வருதல் வாய்வது வான்தோய் வெற்பன்,","வந்தனன் ஆயின், அம்தளிர்ச் செயலைத்","தாழ்வுஇல் ஓங்குசினைத் தொடுத்த வீழ்கயிற்று","ஊசல் மாறிய மருங்கும், பாய்புஉடன்","ஆடா மையின் கலுழ்புஇல தேறி,","நீடுஇதழ் தலயிய கவின்பெறு நீலம்","கண்என மலர்ந்த சுனையும், வண்பறை","மடக்கிளி எடுத்தல் செல்லாத் தடக்குரல்","குலவுப்பொறை யிறுத்த கோல்தலை மருவி","கொய்துஒழி புனமும், நோக்கி; நெடிதுநினைந்து,","பைதலன் பெயரலன் கொல்லோ? ஐ.தேங்கு","'அவ்வெள் அருவிசூடிய உயர்வரைக்","கூஉம் கணஃது எம்ஊர், என","ஆங்குஅதை அறிவுறல் மறந்திசின், யானே,"],"p":"","r":"The friend imagines the hill-man, garlanded with vengai and blue lily, bow in hand, coming to the old meeting places and finding the swing on the ashoka abandoned, the pool where lilies open like eyes, and the harvested millet field, and wonders whether he will turn back grieving, since she forgot to tell him that their village is the one on the high hill crowned by the white waterfall.","n":"'I forgot to tell him our village' is the real message for the overhearing man: the field trysts are over, and the way to her now runs through the village, that is, through marriage. Line 15 (ஐ.தேங்கு) looks corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","idam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 38","lb":"A38","poet":"வடம வண்ணக்கண் பேரிசாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"(1) தோழி தலைமகன் குறைகூறியது. (2) பகலே சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தலைமகன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியதூஉமாம். (3) தோழி குறிபெயர்த்திட்டுச் சொல்லியதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி தலைமகன் குறைகூறியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","பகலே சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தலைமகன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியதூஉமாம்"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி குறிபெயர்த்திட்டுச் சொல்லியதூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","ornament",""],["வண்டுபடு","வண்டு",1,"pira","scene",""],["குவளைத்","குவளை",2,"maram","ornament",""],["செயலைத்","செயலை",6,"maram","scene","ashoka"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",10,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["மடக்கிளி","கிளி",12,"pul","scene",""],["தடக்குரல்","குரல்",12,"unavu","scene","millet ear"],["அருவிசூடிய","அருவி",16,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:2"},"a:39":{"book":"akananuru","num":40,"t":["'ஒழித்தது பழித்த நெஞ்சமொடு வழிப்படர்ந்து,","உள்ளியும் அறிதிரோ, எம்?\" என, யாழநின்","முள்எயிற்றுத் துவர்வாய் முறுவல் அழுங்க","நோய்முந் துறுத்து நொதுமல் மொழியல ; நின்","ஆய்நலம் மறப்பெனோ மற்றே? சேண்இகந்து","ஒலிகழை பிசைந்த ஞெலிசொரி ஒண்பொறி","படுஞெமல் புதையப் பொத்தி, நெடுநிலை","முளிபுன் மீமிசை வளிசுழற் றுறாஅக்","காடுகவர் பெருந்தீ ஓடுவயின் ஓடலின்,","அதர்கெடுத்து அலறிய சாத்தொடு ஒராங்கு","மதர்புலி வெரீஇய மையல் வேழத்து","இனம்தலை மயங்கிய நனந்தலைப் பெருங்காட்டு,","ஞான்று தோன்று அவிர்சுடர் மான்றால் பட்டெனக்,","கள்படர்ஓதி! நிற்படர்ந்து உள்ளி,","அருஞ்செலவு ஆற்றா ஆர்இடை, ஞெரேரெனப்","பரந்துபடு பாயல் நவ்வி பட்டென,","இலங்குவளை செறியா இகுத்த நோக்கமொடு,","நிலம்கிளை நினைவினை நின்ற நிற்கண்டு,","'இன்னகை! இனையம் ஆகவும், எம்வயின்","ஊடல் யாங்கு வந்தன்று?' என, யாழநின்","கோடுஏந்து புருவமொடு குவவுநுதல் நீவி,","நறுங்கதுப்பு உளரிய நன்னர் அமையத்து,","வறுங்கை காட்டிய வாய்அல் கனவின்","ஏற்று ஏக்கற்ற உலமரல்","போற்றாய் ஆகலின், புலத்தியால் எம்மே!"],"p":"","r":"The returned man tells his wife not to speak like a stranger, asking whether he ever thought of her: in a vast forest swept by a wildfire from rubbing bamboo, where travellers lost the path and elephants fled a tiger, he lay at sunset thinking of her and dreamed he found her brooding with loose bangles, stroked her brow and smoothed her hair asking why she sulked, and the false dream left him with empty hands; it is she who has not cared for his distress.","n":"He turns her charge round: her sulk is answered by his dream of answering her sulk, so the dispute is already rehearsed in the dream, and the empty hand he woke to is his evidence that he thought of her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kanavu","ninaivu","pirivu","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 39","lb":"A39","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஒலிகழை","கழை",6,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["மதர்புலி","புலி",11,"maa","scene",""],["வேழத்து","வேழம்",11,"maa","scene",""],["நவ்வி","நவ்வி",16,"maa","scene","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:2"},"a:40":{"book":"akananuru","num":41,"t":["கானல், மாலைக் கழிப்பூக் கூம்ப,","நீல்நிறப் பெருங்கடல் பாடுஎழுந்து ஒலிப்ப","மீன்ஆர் குருகின் மென்பறைத் தொழுதி","குவைஇரும் புன்னைக் குடம்பை சேர,","அசைவண்டு ஆர்க்கும் அல்குறு காலைத்,","தாழை தளரத் தூக்கி, மாலை","அழிதக வந்த கொண்டலொடு கழிபடர்க்","காமர் நெஞ்சம் கையறுபு இனையத்","துயரம் செய்துநம் அருளார் ஆயினும்-","அறா அ லியரோ அவருடைக் கேண்மை!","அளிஇன் மையின் அவண்உறை முனைஇ,","வாரற்க தில்ல - தோழி! - கழனி","வெண்ணெல் அரிநர் பின்றைத் ததும்பும்","தண்ணுமை வெரீஇய தடந்தாள் நாரை","செறிமடை வயிரின் பிளிற்றிப் பெண்ணை","அகமடல் சேக்கும் துறைவன்","இன்துயில் மார்பில் சென்றஎன் நெஞ்சே!"],"p":"","r":"At evening on the shore, as the backwater flowers close, the sea roars and herons crowd into the punnai nests, the woman tells her friend that though her man has gone without pity and left her heart in grief, may his friendship never break, and may her heart, which went to his breast of sweet sleep, not tire of staying there for want of kindness and come back.","n":"She blesses the bond in the same breath as she names its cruelty. The heron that flees the reapers' drum to roost in the palmyra's inner frond is the picture of her own heart, which fled the evening's pain to his breast.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","nenju","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 40","lb":"A40","poet":"குன்றியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகன் பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்துழிக் கிழத்தி தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்துழிக் கிழத்தி தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கழிப்பூக்","கழிப்பூ",1,"maram","scene",""],["குருகின்","குருகு",3,"pul","scene",""],["புன்னைக்","புன்னை",4,"maram","scene",""],["அசைவண்டு","வண்டு",5,"pira","scene",""],["தாழை","தாழை",6,"maram","scene",""],["வெண்ணெல்","நெல்",13,"unavu","scene",""],["அரிநர்","அரிநர்",13,"seythi","scene",""],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",14,"parai","ullurai",""],["நாரை","நாரை",14,"pul","ullurai",""],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",15,"maram","ullurai","palmyra"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","நாரை","confirmed","2/2","the woman's heart and her absent husband"],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:2"},"a:41":{"book":"akananuru","num":42,"t":["வைகுபுலர், விடியல், மைபுலம் பரப்பக்,","கருநனை அவிழ்ந்த ஊழுறு முருக்கின்","எரிமருள் பூஞ்சினை இனச்சிதர் ஆர்ப்ப,","நெடுநெல் அடைச்சிய கழனிஏர் புகுத்து,","குடுமிக் கட்டிய படப்பையொடு மிளிர,","அரிகால் போழ்ந்த தெரிபகட்டு உழவர்","ஓதைத் தெள்விளி புலந்தொறும் பரப்பக்","கோழிணர் எதிரிய மரத்த கவினிக்,","காடுஅணி கொண்ட காண்தகு பொழுதில்,","நாம்பிரி புலம்பின் நலம் செலச் சாஅய்","நம்பிரிபு அறியா நலமொடு சிறந்த","நல்தோள் நெகிழ, வருந்தினள் கொல்லோ-","மென்சிறை வண்டின் தண்கமழ் பூந்துணர்","தாதுஇன் துவலை தளிர்வார்த் தன்ன","அம்கலுழ் மாமை கிளஇய,","நுண்பல் தித்தி, மாஅ யோளோ?"],"p":"","r":"Away on his errand for wealth, the man looks at a dawn countryside in full beauty, the palasa ablaze with flowers, bees humming and ploughmen calling over the fields, and asks whether the dark-skinned, freckled woman he left is wasting, her shoulders slackening, in the loneliness of his absence.","n":"The landscape gains its beauty (காடுஅணி கொண்ட) in the very lines where she loses hers (நலம் செலச் சாஅய்); the season that adorns the land is the measure of what parting takes from her, and he can only guess it with a question (கொல்லோ).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","melivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 41","lb":"A41","poet":"குன்றியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்த விடத்துக் கிழத்தியை நினைந்து சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்த விடத்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முருக்கின்","முருக்கு",2,"maram","scene",""],["இனச்சிதர்","சிதர்",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["நெடுநெல்","நெல்",4,"unavu","scene",""],["உழவர்","உழவர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["தெரிபகட்டு","பகடு",6,"maa","scene","ox"],["வண்டின்","வண்டு",13,"pira","simile",""],["பூந்துணர்","பூந்துணர்",13,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:3"},"a:42":{"book":"akananuru","num":43,"t":["மலிபெயல் கலித்த மாரிப் பித்திகத்துக்","கொயல்அரு நிலைஇய பெயல்ஏர் மணமுகைச்","செவ்வெரிந் உறழும் கொழுங்கடை மழைக்கண்","தளிர்ஏர் மேனி, மாஅ யோயே!","நாடுவறம் கூர, நாஞ்சில துஞ்சக்","கோடை நீடிய பைதுஅறு காலைக்","குன்று கண்டன்ன கோட்ட, யாவையும்","சென்று சேக்கல்லாப் புள்ள, உள்இல்","என்றூழ் வியன்குளம் நிறைய வீசிப்,","பெரும்பெயல் பொழிந்த ஏம வைகறை,","பல்லோர் உவந்த உவகை எல்லாம்","என்னுள் பெய்தந் தற்று - சேண்இடை","ஓங்கித் தோன்றும் உயர் வரை","வான்தோய் வெற்பன் வந்த மாறே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine that the joy of a whole people when heavy rain at dawn fills a dried-out tank after a long drought has all been poured into her, because the lord of the sky-touching hills has come.","n":"The friend speaks of her own joy, not the heroine's; the drought image measures how long the waiting for marriage had become, and the whole land rejoicing hints that the marriage will be public, the opposite of secret love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","mazhai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 42","lb":"A42","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் வரைவு மலிந்தமை தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு மலிந்தமை"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பித்திகத்துக்","பித்திகம்",1,"maram","simile",""],["நாஞ்சில","நாஞ்சில்",5,"seythi","simile","plough"],["புள்ள","புள்",8,"pul","simile",""],["வியன்குளம்","குளம்",9,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:3"},"a:43":{"book":"akananuru","num":44,"t":["கடல்முகந்து கொண்ட கமஞ்சூல் மாமழை","சுடர்நிமிர் மின்னொடு வலன்ஏர்பு இரங்கி","என்றூழ் உழந்த புன்தலை மடப்பிடி","கைமாய் நீத்தம் களிற்றொடு படீஇய,","நிலனும் விசும்பும் நீர்இயைந்து ஒன்றி,","குறுநீர்க் கன்னல் எண்ணுநர் அல்லது,","கதிர்மருங்கு அறியாது, அஞ்சுவரப் பாஅய்,","தளிமயங் கின்றே தண்குரல் எழிலி, யாமே","கொய்அகை முல்லை காலொடு மயங்கி,","மைஇருங் கானம் நாறும் நறுநுதல்,","பல்இருங் கூந்தல், மெல்இயல் மடந்தை","நல்எழில் ஆகம் சேர்ந்தனம், என்றும்","அளியரோ அளியர்தாமே - அளிஇன்று","ஏதில் பொருட்பிணிப் போகித், தம்","இன்துணைப் பிரியும் மடமை யோரே!"],"p":"","r":"The man tells the heart that urged him to go for wealth that the monsoon has come, the clouds joining land and sky so that only water-clock keepers know where the sun is, and he has held his mullai-scented wife; pitiable are those who, without kindness, leave their sweet companion for a stranger's wealth.","n":"He calls the wealth-seeker's departure மடமை, folly, the word usually kept for the woman's innocence; the cow elephant, worn by summer and now bathing with her bull in the flood, is the rains' gift to the paired, and he places himself with the pair.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","mazhai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 43","lb":"A43","poet":"மதுரையாசிரியர் நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பொருள்கடைக் கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பொருள்கடைக் கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குச்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",3,"maa","iraicchi","cow elephant"],["களிற்றொடு","களிறு",4,"maa","iraicchi","bull elephant"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",9,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மடப்பிடி","confirmed","2/2","the rains bring the parted together, and the man who has stayed to hold his wife is doing what the elephants do"]],"ch":"a:3"},"a:44":{"book":"akananuru","num":45,"t":["வந்துவினை முடித்தனன் வேந்தனும்; பகைவரும்","தம்திறை கொடுத்துத் தமர்ஆ யினரே;","முரண்செறிந் திருந்த தானை இரண்டும்","ஒன்றுஎன அறைந்தன பணையே; நின்தேர்","முன்இயங்கு ஊர்திப் பின்னிலை ஈயாது","ஊர்க, பாக! ஒருவினை, கழிய-","நன்னன், ஏற்றை, நறும்பூண் அத்தி,","துன்அருங் கடுந்திறல் கங்கன், கட்டி,","பொன்அணி வல்வில் புன்றுறை என்றுஆங்கு","அன்றுஅவர் குழீஇய அளப்பு அருங் கட்டூர்,","பருந்துபடப் பண்ணிப், பழையன் பட்டெனக்,","கண்டது நோனானாகித் திண்தேர்க்","கணையன் அகப்படக் கழுமலம் தந்த","பிணையல்அம் கண்ணிப் பெரும்பூட் சென்னி","அழும்பில் அன்ன அறாஅ யாணர்,","பழம்பல் நெல்லின் பல்குடிப் பரவை,","பொங்கடி படிகயம் மண்டிய பசுமிளை,","தண்குட வாயில் அன்னோள்","பண்புடை ஆகத்து இன்துயில் பெறவே!"],"p":"","r":"The king's war is done and the enemy has paid tribute, so the man bids his charioteer drive ahead of the column, that he may sleep on the breast of a wife who is like Kudavayil, a town as rich as Chenni's Azhumbil; Chenni is the king who, unable to bear the sight of Pazhaiyan's fall, when the chiefs Nannan, Etrai, Atti, Kangan, Katti and Punthurai had massed their camp, captured Kanaiyan and took Kazhumalam.","n":"The catalogue of chiefs and the fall of Pazhaiyan fill lines 7 to 17, and the wife is reached only through a chain of likenesses, a king, his town, another town; the war that kept him away is also the stock from which he praises her. Who fought whom in lines 7 to 14 is compressed, and this reading of it is offered with caution.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 44","lb":"A44","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி மீளுந் தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றி மீளுந்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பணையே","பணை",4,"parai","scene","drum"],["பருந்துபடப்","பருந்து",11,"pul","scene",""],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",16,"unavu","scene",""],["பசுமிளை","மிளை",17,"maram","scene","guard forest"]],"en":[["king","நன்னன்",7],["king","ஏற்றை",7],["king","அத்தி",7],["king","கங்கன்",8],["king","கட்டி",8],["king","புன்றுறை",9],["king","பழையன்",11],["king","கணையன்",13],["town","கழுமலம்",13],["king","சென்னி",14],["town","அழும்பில்",15],["town","குட வாயில்",18]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:3"},"a:45":{"book":"akananuru","num":46,"t":["வாடல் உழுஞ்சில் விளைநெற்று அம்துணர்","ஆடுகளப் பறையின், அரிப்பன ஒலிப்பக்","கோடை நீடிய அகன்பெருங் குன்றத்து,","நீர்இல் ஆர்ஆற்று நிவப்பன களிறுஅட்டு","ஆள்இல் அத்தத்து உழுவை உகளும்","காடு இறந்தனரே, காதலர்; மாமை,","அரிநுண் பசலை பாஅய, பீரத்து","எழில்மலர் புரைதல் வேண்டும்,அலரே","அன்னி குறுக்கைப் பறந்தலை, திதியன்","தொல்நிலை முழுமுதல் துமியப் பண்ணி,","புன்னை குறைத்த ஞான்றை, வயிரியர்","இன்இசை ஆர்ப்பினும் பெரிதே; யானே,","காதலற் கெடுத்த சிறுமையொடு நோய்கூர்ந்து","ஆதிமந்தி போலப் பேதுற்று","அலந்தனென் உழல்வென் கொல்வோ - பொலந்தார்,","கடல்கால் கிளர்ந்த வென்றி நல்வேல்,","வான வரம்பன் அடல்முனைக் கலங்கிய","உடைமதில் ஓர் அரண்போல","அஞ்சுவரு நோயொடு துஞ்சா தேனே!"],"p":"","r":"Told to bear up, the heroine says her lover has crossed a summer forest where dry pods rattle like dancing-floor drums and the tiger springs on the waterless track; her skin must pale like peerkku flowers, the gossip is louder than the bards' clamour the day Anni cut down Thithiyan's punnai at Kurukkai, and she will wander distraught like Aathimanthi, sleepless as a broken fort shaken by the Chera's assault.","n":"Every likeness she reaches for is a public disaster: a battlefield's din, a famous madwoman, a sacked fort. Her private loss is told in the scale of puram events, which is how she answers the friend's claim that it can be borne.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","alar","pasalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 45","lb":"A45","poet":"வெள்ளிவீதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"வற்புறுக்குந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"வற்புறுக்குந் தோழிக்குத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["உழுஞ்சில்","உழிஞ்சில்",1,"maram","scene",""],["பறையின்","பறை",2,"parai","simile",""],["களிறுஅட்டு","களிறு",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["உழுவை","உழுவை",5,"maa","scene","tiger"],["பீரத்து","பீர்",7,"maram","simile",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",11,"maram","scene",""],["வயிரியர்","வயிரியர்",11,"seythi","simile",""]],"en":[["king","அன்னி",9],["battle","குறுக்கைப் பறந்தலை",9],["king","திதியன்",9],["king","வான வரம்பன்",17]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:3"},"a:46":{"book":"akananuru","num":47,"t":["சேற்றுநிலை முனைஇய செங்கட் காரான்","ஊர்மடி கங்குலில், நோன் தளை பரிந்து,","கூர்முள் வேலி கோட்டின் நீக்கி","நீர்முதிர் பழனத்து மீன்உடன் இரிய,","அம்தூம்பு வள்ளை மயக்கித் தாமரை","வண்டூது பனிமலர் ஆரும் ஊர!","யாரை யோ? நிற் புலக்கேம், வாருற்று,","உறை இறந்து, ஒளிரும் தாழ்இருங் கூந்தல்,","பிறரும், ஒருத்தியை நம்மனைத் தந்து,","வதுவை அயர்ந்தனை என்ப; அஃது யாம்","கூறேம்; வாழியர், எந்தை! செறுநர்","களிறுடை அருஞ்சமம் ததைய நூறும்","ஒளிறுவாள் தானைக் கொற்றச் செழியன்","பிண்ட நெல்லின் அள்ளூர் அன்னஎன்","ஒண்தொடி நெகிழினும் நெகிழ்க;","சென்றீ, பெரும! நிற் றகைக்குநர் யாரோ?"],"p":"","r":"The friend refuses the man entry: he is lord of a town where a red-eyed buffalo, tired of the mud, breaks its tether in the sleeping night, pushes through the thorn fence, scatters the fish and grazes the lotus; they say he has brought another woman home and married her, and the friend will not say it herself, but her bangle, like Chezhiyan's Allur, may slip if it must; go, lord, who is stopping you?","n":"The refusal is made as a release: யாரையோ நிற் புலக்கேம் (who are we to quarrel with you) and நிற் றகைக்குநர் யாரோ (who is there to stop you) deny her any claim on him, which is the sharpest claim she can make. The report of the wedding is put in others' mouths (என்ப).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 46","lb":"A46","poet":"அள்ளூர் நன்முல்லையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"வாயில் வேண்டிச் சென்ற தலைமகற்குத் தோழி வாயின் மறுத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","வாயின் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["காரான்","கார் ஆன்",1,"maa","ullurai","buffalo"],["மீன்உடன்","மீன்",4,"pira","ullurai",""],["வள்ளை","வள்ளை",5,"maram","ullurai",""],["தாமரை","தாமரை",5,"maram","ullurai",""],["வண்டூது","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene",""],["களிறுடை","களிறு",12,"maa","scene","elephant"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",14,"unavu","simile",""]],"en":[["king","செழியன்",13],["town","அள்ளூர்",14]],"dv":[["ullurai","காரான்","confirmed","2/2","the husband and the new wife he has brought home"]],"ch":"a:3"},"a:47":{"book":"akananuru","num":48,"t":["அழிவில் உள்ளம் வழிவழிச் சிறப்ப","வினைஇவண் முடித்தனம் ஆயின், வல்விரைந்து","எழுஇனி - வாழிய நெஞ்சே! - ஒலிதலை","அலங்குகழை நரலத் தாக்கி, விலங்குஎழுந்து,","கடுவளி உருத்திய கொடிவிடு கூர்எரி","விடர்முகை அடுக்கம் பாய்தலின், உடனியைந்து,","அமைக்கண் விடுநொடி கணக்கலை அகற்றும்","வெம்முனை அருஞ்சுரம் நீந்திக் கைம்மிக்கு","அகன்சுடர் கல்சேர்பு மறைய, மனைவயின்","ஒண்தொடி மகளிர் வெண்திரிக் கொளாஅலின்","குறுநடைப் புறவின் செங்காற் சேவல்","நெடுநிலை வியன்நகர் வீழ்துணைப் பயிரும்","புலம்பொடு வந்த புன்கண் மாலை","\"யாண்டு உளர்கொல்?\" எனக், கலிழ்வோள் எய்தி,","இழைஅணி நெடுந்தேர்க் கைவண் செழியன்","மழைவிளை யாடும் வளம்கெழு சிறுமலைச்","சிலம்பின் கூதளங் கமழும் வெற்பின்","வேய்புரை பணைத்தோள், பாயும்","நோய்அசா வீட, முயங்குகம் பலவே!"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his faltering heart to rise quickly now the work is done, cross the hot wilderness where wind-whipped bamboo catches fire, the flames leap the crags and cracking joints scatter the stags, and at dusk, when lamps are lit and the male dove calls its mate in the tall house, reach her who weeps asking where he is, and embrace many times her bamboo-smooth shoulders, fragrant with the koothalam of Chezhiyan's Sirumalai.","n":"He pictures the dusk at home before he has crossed the fire: the lamps, the calling dove and her question 'யாண்டு உளர்கொல்?' are what make the burning road crossable.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","pirivu","maalai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 47","lb":"A47","poet":"ஆலம்பேரிச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் இடைச்சுரத்து அழிந்த நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","இடைச்சுரத்து அழிந்த நெஞ்சிற்குச்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["அலங்குகழை","கழை",4,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["கணக்கலை","கலை",7,"maa","scene","stag"],["புறவின்","புறா",11,"pul","iraicchi","dove"],["கூதளங்","கூதளம்",17,"maram","scene",""],["வேய்புரை","வேய்",18,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[["king","செழியன்",15],["hill","சிறுமலை",16]],"dv":[["iraicchi","புறவின்","confirmed","2/2","at the hour when even the dove calls its mate home, the husband's absence is felt most, and his return is owed"]],"ch":"a:3"},"a:48":{"book":"akananuru","num":49,"t":["\"அன்னாய்! வாழி! வேண்டு அன்னை! நின்மகள்","பாலும் உண்ணாள், பழங்கண் கொண்டு,","நனிபசந் தனள்\" என வினவுதி; அதன்திறம்","யானும் தெற்றென உணரேன்; மேல்நாள்,","மலிபூஞ் சாரல், என்தோழி மாரோடு","ஒலிசினை வேங்கை கொய்குவம் சென்றுழி","\"புலிபுலி!\" என்னும் பூசல் தோன்ற-","ஒண்செங் கழுநீர்க் கண்போல் ஆய்இதழ்","ஊசி போகிய சூழ்செய் மாலையன்,","பக்கம் சேர்த்திய செச்சைக் கண்ணியன்,","குயம்மண்டு ஆகம் செஞ்சாந்து நீவி,","வரிபுனை வில்லன், ஒருகணை தெரிந்துகொண்டு","\"யாதோ, மற்று அம் மாதிறம் படர்?\" என","வினவிநிற் றந்தோனே. அவற் கண்டு,","எம்முள் எம்முள் மெய்மறைபு ஒடுங்கி","நாணி நின்றனெ மாகப், பேணி,","\"ஐவகை வகுத்த கூந்தல் ஆய்நுதல்","மைஈர் ஓதி மடவீர்! நும் வாய்ப்","பொய்யும் உளவோ?\" என்றனன் பையெனப்","பரிமுடுகு தவிர்த்த தேரன், எதிர்மறுத்து,","நின்மகள் உண்கண் பன்மாண் நோக்கிச்","சென்றோன் மன்ற அக் குன்றுகிழ வோனே!","பகல்மாய் அந்திப் படுசுடர் அமையத்து,","அவன்மறை தேஎம்நோக்கி, \"மற்றுஇவன்","மகனே, தோழி!\" என்றனள்","அதன்அளவு உண்டுகோள், மதிவல் லோர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend answers the foster mother, who asks why her daughter will not drink milk and has paled: when the girls went to pick vengai flowers and someone cried 'tiger', a garlanded young hunter came asking which way the beast went, asked if such girls could lie, gazed long at her daughter's eyes and drove away, and at sunset the daughter looked where he had gone and said 'he is a man indeed'; the wise can measure the rest.","n":"The friend never names love. She gives the scene and the daughter's one sentence and leaves the inference to the foster mother (மதிவல் லோர்க்கே), a disclosure that keeps the girl's modesty. His question about lies plays on their silence: they hid and did not answer where the beast went.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kan","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 48","lb":"A48","poet":"தங்கால் முடக்கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"செவிலித்தாய்க்குத் தோழி அறத்தொடு நின்றது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu","அறத்தொடு நின்றது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"],["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["பாலும்","பால்",2,"unavu","absent",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",6,"maram","scene",""],["புலிபுலி","புலி",7,"maa","scene","tiger"],["கழுநீர்க்","கழுநீர்",8,"maram","ornament",""],["செச்சைக்","செச்சை",10,"maram","ornament",""],["செஞ்சாந்து","சாந்து",11,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:3"},"a:49":{"book":"akananuru","num":50,"t":["'கிளியும், பந்தும், கழங்கும், வெய்யோள்","அளியும், அன்பும், சாயலும், இயல்பும்,","முன்நாள் போலாள்; இறீஇயர், என்உயிர்' என,","கொடுந்தொடைக் குழவியொடு வயின்மரத்து யாத்த","கடுங்கட் கறவையின் சிறுபுறம் நோக்கி,","குறுக வந்து, குவவுநுதல் நீவி,","மெல்லெனத் தழீஇயினே னாக, என் மகள்","நன்னர் ஆகத்து இடைமுலை வியர்ப்ப,","பல்கால் முயங்கினள் மன்னே! அன்னோ!","விறல்மிகு நெடுந்தகை பலபா ராட்டி,","வறன்நிழல் அசைஇ, வான்புலந்து வருந்திய","மடமான் அசாஇனம் திரங்குமரல் சுவைக்கும்","காடுஉடன் கழிதல் அறியின் - தந்தை","அல்குபதம் மிகுந்த கடியுடை வியன்நகர்,","செல்வுழிச் செல்வுழி மெய்ந்நிழல் போல,","கோதை ஆயமொடு ஓரை தழீஇத்","தோடுஅமை அரிச்சிலம்பு ஒலிப்ப, அவள்","ஆடுவழி ஆடுவழி அகலேன் மன்னே!"],"p":"","r":"The foster mother grieves at home: she had seen the girl who loved her parrot, ball and play-seeds grow unlike herself, had come close, stroked her forehead and held her, and the girl had embraced her again and again; had she known the girl would go, praising the tall man, through a forest where tired deer chew withered marul in the rainless shade, she would have followed her like her own shadow wherever she played in her father's house.","n":"Her regret is not that she failed to stop the girl but that she was not near enough: the shadow image (மெய்ந்நிழல் போல) makes her wish to have gone along. The girl's many embraces, read now, were a farewell the mother did not understand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","anbu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 49","lb":"A49","poet":"வண்ணப்புறக் கந்தரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உடன் போயின தலைமகளை நினைந்து செவிலித்தாய் மனையின்கண் வருந்தியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu","உடன் போயின தலைமகளை நினைந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கிளியும்","கிளி",1,"pul","scene",""],["குழவியொடு","குழவி",4,"maa","simile","calf"],["கறவையின்","கறவை",5,"maa","simile","milch cow"],["மடமான்","மான்",12,"maa","scene","deer"],["மரல்","மரல்",12,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:3"},"a:50":{"book":"akananuru","num":51,"t":["கடல்பாடு அவிந்து, தோணி நீங்கி,","நெடுநீர் இருங்கழிக் கடுமீன் கலிப்பினும்;","செவ்வாய்ப் பெண்டிர் கௌவை தூற்றினும்,","மாண்இழை நெடுந்தேர் பாணி நிற்பப்,","பகலும் நம்வயின் அகலா னாகிப்","பயின்றுவரும் மன்னே, பனிநீர்ச் சேர்ப்பன்,","இனியே, மணப்பருங் காமம் தணப்ப நீந்தி,","\"வாராதோர் நமக்கு யாஅர்?\" என்னாது,","மல்லன் மூதூர் மறையினை சென்று,","சொல்லின் எவனோ - பாண! 'எல்லி","மனைசேர் பெண்ணை மடிவாய் அன்றில்","துணைஒன்று பிரியினும் துஞ்சா காண்' எனக்","கண்நிறை நீர்கொடு கரக்கும்","ஒண்நுதல் அரிவை \"யான் என் செய்கோ?\" எனவே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the bard that the shore lord once came often, even by day, despite the leaping fish of the backwater and the red-mouthed women's gossip, and now stays away; she asks him to go secretly to the old town and tell the hero what the bright-browed girl says as she hides her tears: 'see, the anril on the palmyra by the house cannot sleep at night if parted from its mate even once; what shall I do?'","n":"The anril is the girl's own message, not an image the poem lodges: she uses the bird to say that she cannot sleep, so it carries the sense openly rather than as iraicchi. The friend, not the girl, chooses the bard as carrier, and asks him to go secretly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","alar","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 50","lb":"A50","poet":"கருவூர்ப் பூதஞ் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி பாணனுக்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","paanan",null,null,"பாணனுக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தோணி","தோணி",1,"seythi","scene","boat"],["கடுமீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","scene",""],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",11,"maram","scene",""],["அன்றில்","அன்றில்",11,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:3"},"a:51":{"book":"akananuru","num":52,"t":["ஆள்வழக்கு அற்ற சுரத்திடைக் கதிர்தெற,","நீள்எரி பரந்த நெடுந்தாள் யாத்து,","போழ்வளி முழங்கும், புல்லென் உயர்சினை,","முடைநசை இருக்கைப் பெடைமுகம் நோக்கி,","ஊன்பதித் தன்ன வெருவரு செஞ்செவி","எருவைச் சேவல் கரிபுசிறை தீய,","வேனில் நீடிய வேய்உயர் நனந்தலை,","நீஉழந்து எய்தும் செய்வினைப் பொருட்பிணி","பல்இதழ் மழைக்கண் மாஅ யோள்வயிற்","பிரியின் புணர்வது ஆயிற், பிரியாது","ஏந்துமுலை முற்றம் வீங்கப், பல்வீழ்","சேயிழை தெளிர்ப்பக் கவைஇ, நாளும்","மனைமுதல் வினையொடும் உவப்ப,","நினை - மாண் நெஞ்சம்!- நீங்குதன் மறந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart, which urged him to leave, that in the peopleless summer wilderness where a male vulture with flesh-red ears sits scorched on a burnt yaa tree gazing at its hungry mate, the wealth he would toil for is not worth having if it comes only by parting from his wife: better to stay, hold her close so her jewels jingle, and be glad in the household's work.","n":"The hinge is the play on புணர்வு: wealth is 'joined' only by parting, and staying is the other joining, with her. The vulture stays by its mate in the burning tree; the hearth (மனைமுதல் வினை) is set against the work (செய்வினை) abroad.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","ilvaazhkkai","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 51","lb":"A51","poet":"பெருந்தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் கடைக்கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பொருள்வயிற் கடைக்கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யாத்து","யா",2,"maram","scene",""],["பெடைமுகம்","பெடை",4,"pul","scene","female vulture"],["எருவைச்","எருவை",6,"pul","iraicchi","vulture"],["வேய்உயர்","வேய்",7,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","எருவைச்","confirmed","2/2","even the vulture in the fire does not leave its mate, which is the choice the man makes against his heart"]],"ch":"a:3"},"a:52":{"book":"akananuru","num":53,"t":["'வலந்த வள்ளி மரன்ஓங்கு சாரல்,","கிளர்ந்த வேங்கைச் சேண்நெடும் பொங்கர்ப்","பொன்னேர் புதுமலர் வேண்டிய குறமகள்","இன்னா இசைய பூசல் பயிற்றலின்,","\"ஏ கல் அடுக்கத்து இருள் அளைச் சிலம்பின்","ஆகொள் வயப்புலி ஆகும் அஃது\" எனத்தம்","மலைகெழு சீறூர் புலம்பக், கல்லெனச்","சிலையுடை இடத்தர் போதரும் நாடன்","நெஞ்சுஅமர் வியன்மார்பு உடைத்துஎன அன்னைக்கு","அறிவிப் பேம்கொல்? அறியலெம் கொல்?' என","இருபாற் பட்ட சூழ்ச்சி ஒருபாற்","சேர்ந்தன்று - வாழி, தோழி!- 'யாக்கை","இன்உயிர் கழிவது ஆயினும், நின்மகள்","ஆய்மலர் உண்கண் பசலை","காம நோய்' எனச் செய்யா தீமே!"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that the two-sided question, whether to tell mother of the hill lord whose wide chest their heart loves, a lord of a land where a girl's cry for vengai flowers brings the men running with bows for a cattle-seizing tiger, has been settled one way: even if her life leaves her body, the friend must not say that her pallor is love-sickness.","n":"The colophon says she speaks knowing the friend will disclose; her forbidding is modesty speaking, and the oath (இன்உயிர் கழிவது ஆயினும்) shows the cost she sets on silence. The false alarm on the hill, a girl's cry taken for a tiger's threat, is how secrets get out in this land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","naanam","pasalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 52","lb":"A52","poet":"நொச்சி நியமங்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகள் வேறுபட்டமையறிந்த செவிலித்தாய்க்குத் தோழி அறத்தொடு நிற்குமெனத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","அறத்தொடு நிற்குமெனத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["வள்ளி","வள்ளி",1,"maram","scene",""],["வேங்கைச்","வேங்கை",2,"maram","scene",""],["குறமகள்","குறமகள்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["வயப்புலி","புலி",6,"maa","scene","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:3"},"a:53":{"book":"akananuru","num":54,"t":["அறியாய், வாழி, தோழி! இருள்அற","விசும்புடன் விளங்கும் விரைசெலல் திகிரிக்","கடுங்கதிர் எறித்த விடுவாய் நிறைய,","நெடுங்கான் முருங்கை வெண்பூத் தாஅய்,","நீர்அற வறந்த நிரம்பா நீள்இடை,","வள்எயிற்றுச் செந்நாய் வருந்துபசிப் பிணவொடு","கள்ளிஅம் காட்ட கடத்திடை உழிஞ்சில்","உள்ஊன் வாடிய சுரிமூக்கு நொள்ளை","பொரிஅரை புதைத்த புலம்புகொள் இயவின்,","விழுத்தொடை மறவர் வில்இட வீழ்ந்தோர்","எழுத்துடை நடுகல் இன்நிழல் வதியும்","அருஞ்சுரக் கவலை நீந்தி, என்றும்,","'இல்லோர்க்கு இல்' என்று இயைவது கரத்தல்","வல்லா நெஞ்சம் வலிப்ப, நம்மினும்","பொருளே காதலர் காதல்;","'அருளே காதலர்' என்றி, நீயே"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells the friend who urges patience that she does not know: he has crossed the waterless wilderness of drumstick flowers and cactus, where a red dog rests with its starving mate in the shade of a lettered hero-stone, because his heart cannot hide what it has when the needy say they have nothing; his love is for wealth more than for her, and the friend calls it grace.","n":"She grants him the virtue and turns it against her: the inability to refuse the poor is what takes him away, so his generosity and his desertion are the same act. The last two lines set காதல் against காதல் and பொருள் against அருள்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pirivu","eegai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 53","lb":"A53","poet":"சீத்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"வற்புறுக்குந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","வற்புறுக்குந் தோழிக்குத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முருங்கை","முருங்கை",4,"maram","scene",""],["செந்நாய்","செந்நாய்",6,"maa","iraicchi","wild dog"],["பிணவொடு","பிணவு",6,"maa","iraicchi","female wild dog"],["கள்ளிஅம்","கள்ளி",7,"maram","scene",""],["உழிஞ்சில்","உழிஞ்சில்",7,"maram","scene",""],["நொள்ளை","நொள்ளை",8,"pira","scene","snail"]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",11]],"dv":[["iraicchi","செந்நாய்","confirmed","2/2","even the wild dog of that waste stays with its hungry mate, while the man has left his for wealth"]],"ch":"a:3"},"a:54":{"book":"akananuru","num":55,"t":["விருந்தின் மன்னர் அருங்கலம் தெறுப்ப,","வேந்தனும் வெம்பகை தணிந்தனன்; தீம்பெயற்","காரும் ஆர்கலி தலையின்று: தேரும்","ஓவத் தன்ன கோபச் செந்நிலம்,","வள்வாய் ஆழி உள்உறுபு உருளக்,","கடவுக காண்குவம் - பாக! மதவு நடைத்","தாம்புஅசை குழவி வீங்குசுரை மடியக்,","கனையலம் குரல் காற்பரி பயிற்றிப்,","படுமணி மிடற்ற பயநிரை ஆயம்","கொடுமடி உடையர் கோற்கைக் கோவலர்","கொன்றையம் குழலர் பின்றைத் தூங்க,","மனைமனைப் படரும் நனைநகு மாலைத்,","தனக்கென வாழாப் பிறர்க்கு உரியாளன்","பண்ணன் சிறுகுடிப் படப்பை நுண்இலைப்","புன்காழ் நெல்லிப் பைங்காய் தின்றவர்","நீர்குடி சுவையின் தீவிய மிழற்றி,","'முகிழ்நிலாத் திகழ்தரும் மூவாத் திங்கள்!","பொன்னுடைத் தாலி என்மகன் ஒற்றி,","வருகுவை ஆயின், தருகுவென் பால்' என,","விலங்கு அமர்க் கண்ணள் விரல்விளி பயிற்றித்","திதலை அல்குல்எம் காதலி","புதல்வற் பொய்க்கும் பூங்கொடி நிலையே!"],"p":"","r":"The guest kings have paid tribute and the king's anger has cooled, the rains have come, so the man bids his charioteer drive over the red earth that the cochineal beetles make like a painting: at dusk, when the cattle hurry home to their calves with the cowherds behind, he wants to see his wife beckon the young moon with her finger, prattling sweet as water after gooseberries from the garden of Pannan, who lives for others, promising it milk if it brings their son a gold tali.","n":"What he hurries home to is not her waiting but her play: the mother tricking the child with the moon (புதல்வற் பொய்க்கும்). The village whose gooseberry gives the simile is that of Pannan, praised in one line as தனக்கென வாழாப் பிறர்க்கு உரியாளன், a puram patron lodged inside an akam homecoming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","ilvaazhkkai","punarchi","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 54","lb":"A54","poet":"மாற்றூர் கிழார் மகனார் கொற்றங் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுடித்து மீளும் தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினைமுடித்து மீளும்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கோபச்","கோபம்",4,"pira","simile","red velvet mite"],["குழவி","குழவி",7,"maa","iraicchi","calf"],["பயநிரை","நிரை",9,"maa","iraicchi","milch cattle"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",10,"seythi","scene",""],["கொன்றையம்","கொன்றை",11,"maram","scene",""],["நெல்லிப்","நெல்லி",15,"maram","simile",""],["பால்","பால்",19,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[["patron","பண்ணன்",14]],"dv":[["iraicchi","பயநிரை","confirmed","2/2","the man's return at the same hour is the cattle's return to their young: he goes home to his wife and son"]],"ch":"a:3"},"a:55":{"book":"akananuru","num":56,"t":["காய்ந்துசெலற் கனலி கல்பகத் தெறுதலின்,","ஈந்துகுருகு உருகும் என்றூழ் நீள்இடை,","உளிமுக வெம்பரல் அடிவருத் துறாலின்,","விளிமுறை அறியா வேய்கரி கானம்,","வயக்களிற்று அன்ன காளையொடு என்மகள்","கழிந்ததற்கு அழிந்தன்றோ இலனே! ஒழிந்துயாம்","ஊதுஉலைக் குருகின் உள்உயிர்த்து, அசைஇ,","வேவது போலும் வெய்ய நெஞ்சமொடு","கண்படை பெறேன், கனவ - ஒண்படைக்","கரிகால் வளவனொடு வெண்ணிப் பறந்தலைப்","பொருதுபுண் நாணிய சேர லாதன்","அழிகள மருங்கின் வான்வடக் கிருந்தென,","இன்னா இன்உரை கேட்ட சான்றோர்","அரும்பெறல் உலகத்து அவனொடு செலீஇயர்,","பெரும்பிறிது ஆகி யாங்குப், பிரிந்து இவண்","காதல் வேண்டி, எற் றுறந்து","போதல் செல்லாஎன் உயிரொடு புலந்தே!"],"p":"","r":"The mother tells the neighbour women who console her that she does not grieve that her daughter went with a youth strong as an elephant across the sun-cracked waste; she lies sleepless, breathing hot like a forge's bellows, and quarrels with her own life, which will not leave her to go after the girl as the noble men who heard Cheralathan, shamed by his back wound at Venni, sit facing north, died to go with him.","n":"She accepts the match (கழிந்ததற்கு அழிந்தன்றோ இலனே) and turns her anger on her own life for staying. The puram image is chosen for its followers, not its king: the companions who chose death with him are what her life refuses to be.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","uyir","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 55","lb":"A55","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"புணர்ந்துடன் போன தலமகட்கு இரங்கிய தாய் தெருட்டும் அயலிலாட்டியர்க்கு உரைத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,null,"அயலிலாட்டியர்க்கு உரைத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஈந்துகுருகு","ஈந்து",2,"maram","scene","date palm"],["வேய்கரி","வேய்",4,"maram","absent","bamboo"],["வயக்களிற்று","களிறு",5,"maa","simile","elephant"],["குருகின்","குருகு",7,"pira","simile","bellows"]],"en":[["king","கரிகால் வளவனொடு",10],["battle","வெண்ணிப் பறந்தலை",10],["king","சேர லாதன்",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:3"},"a:56":{"book":"akananuru","num":57,"t":["நகை ஆகின்றே - தோழி! - நெருநல்-","மணிகண் டன்ன துணிகயம் துளங்க,","இரும்புஇயன் றன்ன கருங்கோட்டு எருமை,","ஆம்பல் மெல்லடை கிழியக், குவளைக்","கூம்புவிடு பன்மலர் மாந்திக், கரைய","காஞ்சி நுண்தாது ஈர்ம்புறத்து உறைப்ப,","மெல்கிடு கவுள அல்குநிலை புகுதரும்","தண்துறை ஊரன் திண்தார் அகலம்","வதுவை நாள்அணிப் புதுவோர்ப் புணரிய,","பரிவொடு வரூஉம் பாணன் தெருவில்","புனிற்றாப் பாய்ந்தெனக் கலங்கி, யாழ்இட்டு,","எம்மனைப் புகுதந் தோனே; அதுகண்டு","மெய்ம்மலி உவகை மறையினென், எதிர்சென்று,","'இம்மனை அன்று; அஃது உம்மனை' என்ற","என்னும் தன்னும் நோக்கி,","மம்மர் நெஞ்சினோன் தொழுதுநின் றதுவே"],"p":"","r":"The heroine laughs to her friend that yesterday the bard of the lord of the cool ford, where a black-horned buffalo stirs the gem-clear pond, gorges on lilies and comes home dusted with kaanji pollen, was hurrying to bring new brides to the lord's chest when a newly calved cow charged him; he dropped his lute and ran into her house, and when she hid her mirth and said 'this is not your house, that one is', he stood bewildered with folded hands.","n":"The laugh is the weapon: by sending the bard to 'your house' she names the courtesan's house as his true home and her husband's errand-runner as a stranger in hers. The joy she hides (மெய்ம்மலி உவகை மறையினென்) is a joy in his discomfiture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 56","lb":"A56","poet":"மதுரை அறுவை வாணிகன் இளவேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தைமனைக்குச் செல்கின்ற பாணன் தன் மனைக்கு *வந்தசனாகத் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பரத்தைமனைக்குச் செல்கின்ற பாணன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",3,"maa","ullurai","buffalo"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",4,"maram","ullurai",""],["குவளைக்","குவளை",4,"maram","ullurai",""],["காஞ்சி","காஞ்சி",6,"maram","ullurai",""],["புனிற்றாப்","புனிற்று ஆ",11,"maa","scene","newly calved cow"],["யாழ்இட்டு","யாழ்",11,"yaazh","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","எருமை","confirmed","2/2","the husband and his new brides"]],"ch":"a:3"},"a:57":{"book":"akananuru","num":58,"t":["சிறுபைந் தூவிச் செங்காற் பேடை","நெடுநீர் வானத்து, வாவுப்பறை நீந்தி","வெயில்அவிர் உருப்பொடு வந்து, கனி பெறாஅது,","பெறுநாள் யாணர் உள்ளிப், பையாந்து,","புகல்ஏக் கற்ற புல்லென் உலவைக்","குறுங்கால் இற்றிப் புன்தலை நெடுவீழ்","இரும்பிணர்த் துறுகல் தீண்டி, வளி பொரப்,","பெருங்கை யானை நிவப்பின் தூங்கும்","குன்ற வைப்பின் என்றூழ் நீள்இடை","யாமே எமியம் ஆகத், தாமே","பசுநிலா விரிந்த பல்கதிர் மதியிற்","பெருநல் ஆய்கவின் ஒரீஇச், சிறுபீர்","வீஏர் வண்ணம் கொண்டன்று கொல்லோ-","கொய்சுவற் புரவிக் கொடித்தேர்ச் செழியன்","முதுநீர் முன்றுறை முசிறி முற்றிக்","களிறுபட எருக்கிய கல்லென் ஞாட்பின்","அரும்புண் உறுநரின் வருந்தினள், பெரிது அழிந்து,","பானாட் கங்குலும் பகலும்","ஆனாது அழுவோள் - ஆய்சிறு நுதலே!"],"p":"","r":"On the summer road among the hills, where a small green-feathered female bird crosses the sky in the heat, finds no fruit on the bare fig whose roots hang like an elephant's trunk, and faints remembering the days of plenty, the man, alone, wonders whether his wife's beauty, bright as the full moon, has turned to the colour of peerkku flowers, as she weeps night and day like the wounded in Chezhiyan's siege of Musiri.","n":"The bird comes to a tree that once fed it and finds it empty, as the man imagines his wife finding the days of their life together gone; the war simile of the wounded at Musiri makes her grief a casualty of the same world of kings and wealth that took him away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","pasalai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 57","lb":"A57","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் வயிற் பிரிந்த தலைமகன் கிழத்தியை நினைந்து சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","பொருள் வயிற் பிரிந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பேடை","பேடை",1,"pul","iraicchi","female bird"],["கனி","கனி",3,"unavu","absent","fig fruit"],["இற்றிப்","இற்றி",6,"maram","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",8,"maa","simile",""],["சிறுபீர்","பீர்",12,"maram","simile",""],["களிறுபட","களிறு",16,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["king","செழியன்",14],["town","முசிறி",15]],"dv":[["iraicchi","பேடை","confirmed","2/2","the wife, left alone, remembers the full days together and finds them gone"]],"ch":"a:3"},"a:58":{"book":"akananuru","num":59,"t":["இன்இசை உருமொடு கனைதுளி தலைஇ,","மன்னுயிர் மடிந்த பானாட் கங்குல்","காடுதேர் வேட்டத்து விளிவுஇடம் பெறாஅது,","வரிஅதள் படுத்த சேக்கை, தெரிஇழைத்","தேன்நாறு கதுப்பின் கொடிச்சியர் தந்தை,","கூதிர் இல் செறியும் குன்ற நாட!","வனைந்து வரல் இளமுலை ஞெமுங்கப், பல்ஊழ்","விளங்குதொடி முன்கை வளைந்துபுறம் சுற்ற,","நின்மார்பு அடைதலின் இனிது ஆகின்றே-","நும்இல் புலம்பின் நும் உள்ளுதொறும் நலியும்","தண்வரல் அசைஇய பண்புஇல் வாடை","பதம்பெறு கல்லாது இடம்பார்த்து நீடி-","மனைமரம் ஒசிய ஒற்றிப்","பலர்மடி கங்குல், நெடும்புற நிலையே!"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells the hill lord, whose land is one where at midnight in cold-season rain the hunter father of the hill girls, finding no game, keeps to his house on a tiger-skin bed, that it is sweet when her arms clasp his back on his chest; but the cold north wind, which torments her whenever she thinks of him in his absence, waits long outside at midnight, bending the house tree, looking for its chance.","n":"The father kept home by the rain is the danger to their meeting, and the wind that waits outside is like the lover who stood outside; the close of the poem (நெடும்புற நிலையே) may be his long waiting or the wind's, and the reading turns on which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","iravu","kalaviyal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 58","lb":"A58","poet":"மதுரைப் பண்டவாணிகன் இளந்தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சேட்படுத்து வந்த தலைமகற்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","சேட்படுத்து வந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வரிஅதள்","அதள்",4,"pira","scene","tiger skin"],["கொடிச்சியர்","கொடிச்சி",5,"seythi","scene",""],["வாடை","வாடை",11,"pira","scene","north wind"],["மனைமரம்","மரம்",13,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:3"},"a:59":{"book":"akananuru","num":60,"t":["தண்கயத்து அமன்ற வண்டுபடு துணைமலர்ப்","பெருந்தகை இழந்த கண்ணினை, பெரிதும்","வருந்தினை, வாழியர், நீயே!- வடாஅது","வண்புனல் தொழுநை வார்மணல் அகன்துறை,","அண்டர் மகளிர் தண்தழை உடீஇயர்","மரம்செல மிதித்த மாஅல் போலப்","புன்தலை மடப்பிடி உணீஇயர், அம்குழை,","நெடுநிலை யாஅம் ஒற்றி, நனைகவுள்","படிஞிமிறு கடியும் களிறே- தோழி!","சூர்மருங்கு அறுத்த சுடர்இலை நெடுவேல்,","சினம்மிகு முருகன் தண்பரங் குன்றத்து,","அந்துவன் பாடிய சந்துகெழு நெடுவரை,","இன்தீம் பைஞ்சுனை ஈரணிப் பொலிந்த","தண்நறுங் கழுநீர்ச் செண்இயற் சிறுபுறம்","தாம்பா ராட்டிய காலையும் உள்ளார்,","வீங்குஇறைப் பணைத்தோள் நெகிழச், சேய்ந்நாட்டு","அருஞ்செயற் பொருட்பிணி முன்னி, நப்","பிரிந்து, சேண் உறைநர் சென்ற ஆறே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine, whose eyes have lost their flower-like beauty, that on the road taken by the man who went far for wealth a bull elephant bends a tall yaa tree with his head so his young mate can eat its shoots, as Maal trod down a tree on the Yamuna's sands to give the cowherd girls leaf-skirts; yet those men do not remember even the mornings when they praised her back, lovely as the water-lily of Murugan's Parankunram, and have left her shoulders to slacken.","n":"The comfort is double: the elephant who feeds his mate is meant to promise the man's return, while the friend also admits he does not think of her. The god's image of a tree bent for women's sake makes the elephant's care a divine habit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","melivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 59","lb":"A59","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிளநாகன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட கிழத்திக்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வண்டுபடு","வண்டு",1,"pira","scene",""],["துணைமலர்ப்","மலர்",1,"maram","scene",""],["தண்தழை","தழை",5,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["மாஅல்","மால்",6,"deyvam","simile",""],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",7,"maa","iraicchi","cow elephant"],["யாஅம்","யா",8,"maram","scene",""],["படிஞிமிறு","ஞிமிறு",9,"pira","scene",""],["களிறே","களிறு",9,"maa","iraicchi","bull elephant"],["முருகன்","முருகன்",11,"deyvam","scene",""],["சந்துகெழு","சந்து",12,"maram","scene","sandal"],["கழுநீர்ச்","கழுநீர்",14,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["river","தொழுநை",4],["hill","பரங் குன்றத்து",11]],"dv":[["iraicchi","களிறே","confirmed","2/2","on the very road the man took, the male feeds his mate, so he too will remember and come back to care for her"]],"ch":"a:3"},"a:60":{"book":"akananuru","num":61,"t":["பெருங்கடற் பரப்பில் சேயிறா நடுங்கக்","கொடுந்தொழின் முகந்த செங்கோல் அவ்வலை","நெடுந்திமில் தொழிலொடு வைகிய தந்தைக்கு,","உப்புநொடை நெல்லின் மூரல் வெண்சோறு","அயிலை துழந்த அம்புளிச் சொரிந்து,","கொழுமீன் தடியொடு குறுமகள் கொடுக்கும்","திண்தேர்ப் பொறையன் தொண்டி அன்னஎம்","ஒண்தொடி ஞெமுக்கா தீமோ தெய்ய;","'ஊதை ஈட்டிய உயர்மணல் அடைகரை,","கோதை ஆயமொடு வண்டல் தைஇ,","ஓரை ஆடினும் உயங்கும்நின் ஒளி' எனக்","கொன்னும் சிவப்போள் காணின், வென்வேற்","கொற்றச் சோழர் குடந்தை வைத்த","நாடுதரு நிதியினுஞ் செறிய","அருங்கடிப் படுக்குவள், அறன்இல் யாயே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hero: do not crush our bright bangle, fine as Porayan's Tondi where a fisher girl brings her father rice bartered for salt, tamarind fish stew and fat fish; if the mother, who flushes at nothing and says 'playing in the sand with your friends wears away your glow', sees anything, she will shut the girl in more closely than the Chola guards the tribute stored at Kudanthai.","n":"The threat of confinement is the argument for marriage: the friend never asks him to marry but makes the mother's guarded house the alternative. The fisher girl feeding her father in the opening is the life of the shore the heroine is about to lose.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 60","lb":"A60","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகற்குத் தோழி செறிப்பறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","செறிப்பறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["சேயிறா","இறா",1,"pira","scene","prawn"],["அவ்வலை","வலை",2,"seythi","scene","net"],["நெடுந்திமில்","திமில்",3,"seythi","scene","boat"],["உப்புநொடை","உப்பு",4,"pira","scene",""],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",4,"unavu","gift_food",""],["அயிலை","அயிலை",5,"pira","gift_food","fish"],["அம்புளிச்","புளி",5,"maram","gift_food","tamarind"],["கொழுமீன்","மீன்",6,"pira","gift_food",""]],"en":[["king","பொறையன்",7],["town","தொண்டி",7],["king","சோழர்",13],["town","குடந்தை",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:3"},"a:61":{"book":"akananuru","num":62,"t":["'நோற்றோர் மன்ற தாமே கூற்றங்","கோளுற விளியார், பிறர்கொள விளிந்தோர்' எனத்","தாள்வலம் படுப்பச் சேட்புலம் படர்ந்தோர்","நாள்இழை நெடுஞ்சுவர் நோக்கி, நோய்உழந்து","ஆழல் வாழி, தோழி!- தாழாது,","உரும்எனச் சிலைக்கும் ஊக்கமொடு பைங்கால்","வரிமாண் நோன்ஞாண் வன்சிலைக் கொளீஇ,","அருநிறத்து அழுத்திய அம்பினர் பலருடன்","அண்ணல் யானை வெண்கோடு கொண்டு,","நறவுநொடை நெல்லின் நாள்மகிழ் அயரும்","கழல்புனை திருந்துஅடிக் கள்வர் கோமான்","மழபுலம் வணக்கிய மாவண் புல்லி","விழவுடை விழுச்சீர் வேங்கடம் பெறினும்,","பழகுவர் ஆதலோ அரிதே - முனாஅது","முழவுஉறழ் திணிதோள் நெடுவேள் ஆவி","பொன்னுடை நெடுநகர்ப் பொதினி அன்னநின்","ஒண்கேழ் வனமுலைப் பொலிந்த","நுண்பூண் ஆகம் பொருந்துதன் மறந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine not to waste away watching the wall where she scratches off the days since her man left for a distant land, driven by the saying that those who fall at others' hands rather than to Death are blessed; even if he won Pulli's festive Venkatam, where bowmen barter elephant tusks and drink their daily toddy, he could not stay there forgetting her breast, rich as Avi's golden Pothini.","n":"The man's journey is justified with a warriors' maxim about dying in battle rather than of sickness, so a search for wealth is dressed as heroic striving; the friend answers not by disputing the maxim but by setting a famous prize (Venkatam) against a famous city (Pothini) and letting the woman's body outweigh both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aalvinai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 61","lb":"A61","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பொருள்வயிற்பிரிய வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பொருள்வயிற்பிரிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",9,"maa","scene",""],["நறவுநொடை","நறவு",10,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",10,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கள்வர்","கள்வர்",11,"seythi","scene",""],["முழவுஉறழ்","முழவு",15,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[["king","புல்லி",12],["hill","வேங்கடம்",13],["king","ஆவி",15],["town","பொதினி",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:4"},"a:62":{"book":"akananuru","num":63,"t":["அயத்துவளர் பைஞ்சாய் முருந்தின் அன்ன","நகைப்பொலிந்து இலங்கும் எயிறுகெழு துவர்வாய்,","ஆகத்து அரும்பிய முலையள், பணைத்தோள்,","மாத்தாட் குவளை மலர்பிணைத் தன்ன","மாஇதழ் மழைக்கண், மாஅ யோளொடு","பேயும் அறியா மறைஅமை புணர்ச்சி","பூசற் றுடியிற் புணர்வு பிரிந்து இசைப்பக்,","கரந்த கரப்பொடு நாஞ்செலற்கு, அருமையின்,","கடும்புனல் மலிந்த காவிரிப் பேரியாற்று","நெடுஞ்சுழி நீத்தம் மண்ணுநள் போல,","நடுங்கு அஞர் தீர முயங்கி, நெருநல்","ஆகம் அடைதந் தோளே - வென்வேற்","களிறுகெழு தானைப் பொறையன் கொல்லி","ஒளிறுநீர் அடுக்கத்து வியல்அகம் பொற்பக்","கடவுள் எழுதிய பாவையின்,","மடவது மாண்ட மாஅ யோளே!"],"p":"","r":"After a tryst that failed, the man tells his heart how the dark girl of their secret union, when talk broke it as loud as a war drum and meeting grew hard, yesterday clasped him to end her trembling as if she were plunging into the whirling Kaveri flood, she whose innocence is as perfect as the image the god made on the bright slopes of Poraiyan's Kolli.","n":"Tonight's failure is answered with yesterday's embrace: the poem is a memory held against a broken tryst, and the closing likeness to a god-made image on Kolli sets the girl at once beyond reach and beyond blame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","punarchi","alar","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 62","lb":"A62","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அல்லகுறிப்பட்டுழித் தலைமகன் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","அல்லகுறிப்பட்டுழித்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பைஞ்சாய்","பைஞ்சாய்",1,"maram","simile","sedge"],["குவளை","குவளை",4,"maram","simile",""],["றுடியிற்","துடி",7,"parai","simile",""],["களிறுகெழு","களிறு",13,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",9],["king","பொறையன்",13],["hill","கொல்லி",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:4"},"a:63":{"book":"akananuru","num":64,"t":["கேளாய் வாழியோ மகளை! நின் தோழி,","திருநகர் வரைப்பகம் புலம்ப அவனொடு-","பெருமலை இறந்தது நோவேன்; நோவல்-","கடுங்கண் யானை நெடுங்கை சேர்த்தி,","முடங்குதாள் உதைத்த பொலங்கெழு பூழி","பெரும்புலர் விடியல் விரிந்து, வெயில் எறிப்பக்","கருந்தாள் மிடற்ற செம்பூழ்ச் சேவல்","சிறுபுன் பெடையொடு குடையும் ஆங்கண்,","அஞ்சுவரத் தகுந கானம் நீந்திக்.","கன்று காணாது, புன்கண்ண, செவிசாய்த்து","மன்றுநிறை பைதல் கூறப், பல உடன்","கறவை தந்த கடுங்கான் மறவர்","கல்லென் சீறூர் எல்லியின் அசைஇ,","முதுவாய்ப் பெண்டின் செதுகாற் குரம்பை,","மடமயில் அன்னஎன் நடைமெலி பேதை","தோள்துணை யாகத் துயிற்றத் துஞ்சாள்,","'வேட்டக் கள்வர் விசியுறு கடுங்கண்","சேக்கோள் அறையும் தண்ணுமை","கேட்குநள் கொல்?, எனக் கலுழும்என் நெஞ்சே!"],"p":"","r":"The foster mother tells her own daughter that she does not grieve that the girl left the great house and crossed the mountain with her lover; she grieves that her peacock-gentle child, sheltering for the night in a raiders' noisy hamlet in an old woman's rotting hut, will lie awake on his shoulder listening for the drum of cattle thieves.","n":"She accepts the elopement in a single clause and spends the poem on a night she imagines in detail; at dawn on that road the red quail cock and his small hen bathe in the dust together, a pair at ease where the mother can see only danger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","acham","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 63","lb":"A63","poet":"கருவூர்க் கண்ணம் புல்லனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகள் புணர்ந்துடன் செல்லச் செவிலி தன் மகளுக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["sevili","thozhi",null,"kalavu","புணர்ந்துடன் செல்ல"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene",""],["செம்பூழ்ச்","செம்பூழ்",7,"pul","scene","quail"],["பெடையொடு","பெடை",8,"pul","scene",""],["கன்று","கன்று",10,"maa","scene","calf"],["கறவை","கறவை",12,"maa","scene","milch cow"],["மறவர்","மறவர்",12,"seythi","scene",""],["மடமயில்","மயில்",15,"pul","simile",""],["கள்வர்","கள்வர்",17,"seythi","scene",""],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",18,"parai","scene",""],["சேக்கோள்","சே",18,"maa","scene","bull"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:4"},"a:64":{"book":"akananuru","num":65,"t":["களையும் இடனாற் - பாக! உளை அணி","உலகுகடப் பன்ன புள்இயற் கலிமா","வகைஅமை வனப்பின் வள்புநீ தெரியத்","தளவுப்பிணி அவிழ்ந்த தண்பதப் பெருவழி,","ஐதுஇலங்கு அகல்இலை நெய்கனி நோன்காழ்","வென்வேல் இளையர் வீங்குபரி முடுகச்,","செலவுநாம் அயர்ந்தனம் ஆயிற், பெயல","கடுநீர் வரித்த செந்நில மருங்கின்,","விடுநெறி ஈர்மணல், வாரணம் சிதரப்,","பாம்புஉறை புற்றத்து ஈர்ம்புறங் குத்தி,","மண்ணுடைக் கோட்ட அண்ணல் ஏஎறு","உடன்நிலை வேட்கையின் மடநாகு தழீஇ,","ஊர்வயிற் பெயரும் பொழுதிற், சேர்புஉடன்,","கன்றுபயிர் குரல, மன்றுநிறை புகுதரும்","ஆபூண் தெண்மணி ஐதுஇயம்பு இன்இசை","புலம்புகொள் மாலை கேட்டொறும்","கலங்கினள் உறைவோள் கையறு நிலையே"],"p":"","r":"Returning with his task done, the man urges his charioteer through the wet red country of the rains: at this hour the bull with earth on its horns leads the young heifer home and the cows' clear bells fill the village common, and she who waits for him is undone each time she hears them in the lonely evening.","n":"The sound that marks every other homecoming (the cows' bells at dusk) is the sound that torments her, so his haste is set against a sound she hears without him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 64","lb":"A64","poet":"ஆர்க்காடு கிழார் மகனார் வெள்ளைக் கண்ணத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றி மீளும் தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றி மீளும்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கலிமா","மா",2,"maa","scene","horse"],["புள்இயற்","புள்",2,"pul","simile",""],["தளவுப்பிணி","தளவு",4,"maram","scene",""],["இளையர்","இளையர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["வாரணம்","வாரணம்",9,"pul","scene","jungle fowl"],["பாம்புஉறை","பாம்பு",10,"pira","scene",""],["புற்றத்து","புற்று",10,"pira","scene",""],["ஏஎறு","ஏறு",11,"maa","iraicchi","bull"],["மடநாகு","நாகு",12,"maa","iraicchi","heifer"],["கன்றுபயிர்","கன்று",14,"maa","scene","calf"],["ஆபூண்","ஆ",15,"maa","scene","cow"],["தெண்மணி","மணி",15,"pira","scene","bell"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","ஏஎறு","confirmed","1/1","the man's own homecoming is this one, and he hurries so that she is not left alone at the hour when even the herd is paired"]],"ch":"a:4"},"a:65":{"book":"akananuru","num":66,"t":["உன்னங் கொள்கையொடு உளம்கரந்து உறையும்","அன்னை சொல்லும் உய்கம்; என்னதூஉம்","ஈரம்சேரா இயல்பிற் பொய்ம் மொழிச்","சேரிஅம் பெண்டிர் சௌவையும் ஒழிகம்;","நாடுகண் அகற்றிய உதியஞ் சேரற்","பாடிச் சென்ற பரிசிலர் போல","உவஇனி - வாழி, தோழி! அவரே,","பொம்மல் ஓதி! நம்மொடு ஒராங்குச்","செலவு அயர்ந்தனரால் இன்றே - மலைதொறும்","மால்கழை பிசைந்த கால்வாய் கூர்எரி,","மீன்கொள் பரதவர் கொடுந்திமில் நளிசுடர்,","வான்தோய் புணரி மிசைக்கண் டாங்கு,","மேவரத் தோன்றும் யாஅஉயர் நனந்தலை","உயவல் யானை வெரிநுச்சென் றன்ன","கல்ஊர்பு இழிதரும் புல்சாய் சிறுநெறிக்,","காடுமீக் கூறும் கோடுஏந்து ஒருத்தல்","ஆறுகடி கொள்ளும் அருஞ்சுரம், 'பணைத்தோள்,","நாறுஐங் கூந்தல், கொம்மை வரிமுலை,","நிரைஇதழ் உண்கண், மகளிர்க்கு","அரியவால்' என அழுங்கிய செலவே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine to rejoice like the bards who went singing to Udhiyan Cheral, for they are free of the suspicious mother and the lying women of the street: the man has today set out with them on the very journey he once put off, saying the hard road over the hills, lit with bamboo fires like fishing boats' lamps at sea and guarded by a tusker, was too hard for women.","n":"The friend quotes his old refusal in full, the road's beauty and danger included, so the elopement is told as his change of mind; the bards' joy before a famous giver is the measure of the girls' release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 65","lb":"A65","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தலைமகன் உடன்போக்கு வலித்தமை தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","உடன்போக்கு வலித்தமை"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பரிசிலர்","பரிசிலர்",6,"seythi","simile",""],["மால்கழை","கழை",10,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",11,"seythi","simile",""],["கொடுந்திமில்","திமில்",11,"pira","simile","boat"],["புணரி","புணரி",12,"pira","simile","sea"],["யானை","யானை",14,"maa","simile",""],["ஒருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",16,"maa","scene","tusker"]],"en":[["king","உதியஞ் சேரற்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:4"},"a:66":{"book":"akananuru","num":67,"t":["'இம்மை உலகத்து இசையொடும் விளங்கி,","மறுமை உலகமும் மறுஇன்று எய்துப","செறுநரும் விழையும் செயிர்தீர் காட்சிச்","சிறுவர்ப் பயந்த செம்மலோர்' எனப்","பல்லோர் கூறிய பழமொழி எல்லாம்","வாயே ஆகுதல் வாய்த்தனம் - தோழி!","நிரைதார் மார்பன் நெருநல் ஒருத்தியொடு","வதுவை அயர்தல் வேண்டிப், புதுவதின்","இயன்ற அணியன், இத்தெரு இறப்போன்","மாண்தொழில் மாமணி கறங்கக், கடை கழிந்து","காண்டல் விருப்பொடு தளர்புதளர்பு ஓடும்","பூங்கட் புதல்வனை நோக்கி, 'நெடுந்தேர்","தாங்குமதி, வலவ!' என்று இழிந்தனன், தாங்காது,","மணிபுரை செவ்வாய் மார்பகம் சிவணப்","புல்லிப் பெரும! செல்இனி, அகத்து' எனக்","கொடுப்போற்கு ஒல்லான் கலுழ்தலின், 'தடுத்த","மாநிதிக் கிழவனும் போன்ம்' என, மகனொடு","தானே புகுதந் தோனே; யான் அது","படுத்தனென் ஆகுதல் நாணி, இடித்து, 'இவற்","கலக்கினன் போலும், இக்கொடியோன்' எனச்சென்று","அலைக்கும் கோலொடு குறுகத், தலைக்கொண்டு","இமிழ்கண் முழவின் இன்சீர் அவர்மனைப்","பயிர்வன போலவந்து இசைப்பவும், தவிரான்","கழங்குஆடு ஆயத்து அன்றுநம் அருளிய","பழங்கண் ணோட்டமும் நலிய,","அழுங்கினன் அல்லனோ, அயர்ந்த தன் மணனே!"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells the friend who came on her husband's behalf that the old saying (sons bring fame here and a clean passage hereafter) has proved true: yesterday, dressed to wed another, he stopped his chariot when their little son ran out to see him, could not hand the crying boy back, came into the house with him, and, held by the memory of the kindness he once showed her among the girls at play, gave up the wedding though its drums kept calling him.","n":"The proverb about sons is a puram commonplace about fame and the next world; she tests it on a household quarrel and finds it true in a way the proverb never meant, since the son's merit is that he keeps his father at home. Her own part is comic and rueful: ashamed to seem the cause, she makes a show of beating the boy.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","karpu","kannottam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 66","lb":"A66","poet":"செல்லூர்க் கோசிகன் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தையிற் பிரிந்த தலைமகற்கு வாயிலாய்ப்புக்க தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","வாயிலாய்ப்புக்க தோழிக்குத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாமணி","மணி",10,"pira","scene","bell"],["மணிபுரை","மணி",14,"pira","simile","gem"],["முழவின்","முழவு",22,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:4"},"a:67":{"book":"akananuru","num":68,"t":["யான்எவன் செய்கோ? தோழி! பொறிவரி","வானம் வாழ்த்தி பாடவும், அருளாது","உறைதுறந்து எழிலி நீங்கலிற், பறைபு உடன்,","மரம்புல் லென்ற முரம்புஉயர் நனந்தலை;","அரம்போழ் நுதிய வாளி அம்பின்","நிரம்பா நோக்கின்; நிரயம் கொண்மார்","நெல்லி நீளிடை எல்லி மண்டி,","நல்அமர்க் கடந்த நாணுடை மறவர்","பெயரும் பீடும் எழுதி யதர்தொறும்","பீலி சூட்டிய பிறங்குநிலை நடுகல்","வேல்ஊன்று பலகை வேற்றுமுனை கடுக்கும்","மொழிபெயர் தேஎம் தருமார், மன்னர்","கழிப்பிணிக் கறைத்தோல் நிரைகண் டன்ன","உவல்இடி பதுக்கை ஆள்உகு பறந்தலை,","'உருஇல் பேஎய் ஊராத் தேரோடு","நிலம்படு மின்மினி போலப் பலஉடன்","இலங்கு பரல் இமைக்கும்' என்ப - நம்","நலம்துறந்து உறைநர் சென்ற ஆறே!"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend what she can do, when they say the road her man took is a rainless gravel waste where marauders rush at night, hero-stones crowned with peacock feathers stand like a battle front, stone heaps mark where men fell, and the gravel glitters like fireflies beside a ghost's mirage.","n":"Everything she describes is hearsay (என்ப): the dread comes from report, and the poem's last line puts the road beside the beauty he gave up to travel it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","acham","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 67","lb":"A67","poet":"நோய்பாடியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்தவழி வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பொறிவரி","வானம்பாடி",1,"pul","scene","spotted bird"],["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",7,"maram","scene",""],["மறவர்","மறவர்",8,"seythi","scene",""],["பீலி","பீலி",10,"pul","ornament","peacock feather"],["மின்மினி","மின்மினி",16,"pira","simile","firefly"],["பரல்","பரல்",17,"pira","simile","gravel"]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",10]],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:4"},"a:68":{"book":"akananuru","num":69,"t":["'அன்னாய்! வாழி, வேண்டு அன்னை! நம் படப்பைத்","தணஅயத்து அமன்ற கூதளம் குழைய,","இன்இசை அருவிப் பாடும் என்னதூஉம்","கேட்டியோ? வாழி, வேண்டு அன்னை! நம் படப்பை","ஊட்டி யன்ன ஒண்தளிர்ச் செயலை","ஓங்குசினைத் தொடுத்த ஊசல், பாம்புஎன,","முழுமுதல் துமிய உரும்எறிந் தன்றே;","பின்னும் கேட்டியோ?' எனவும், அஃது அறியாள்,","அன்னையும் கனைதுயில் மடிந்தனள்; அதன்தலை","மன்உயிர் மடிந்தன்றால் பொழுதே ; காதலர்","வருவர் ஆயின், பருவம்இது' எனச்","சுடர்ந்து இலங்கு எல்வளை நெகிழ்ந்த நம்வயின்","படர்ந்த உள்ளம் பழுதுஅன் றாக,","வந்தனர் - வாழி, தோழி!- அந்தரத்து","இமிழ்பெயல் தலைஇய இனப்பல் கொண்மூத்","தவிர்வுஇல் வெள்ளம் தலைத்தலை சிறப்பக்","கன்றுகால் ஒய்யும் கடுஞ்சுழி நீத்தம்","புன்தலை மடப்பிடிப் பூசல் பலஉடன்","வெண்கோட்டு யானை விளிபடத் துழவும்","அகல்வாய்ப் பாந்தட் படாஅர்ப்","பகலும் அஞ்சும் பனிக்கடு சுரனே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine how, testing whether their mother slept, she asked her whether she had heard the waterfall shaking the koothalam and the thunder that split the asoka where their swing hangs; the mother, hearing nothing, sank into sleep, all creatures slept, and the man, as their hearts had hoped, came through the cold road of torrents, crying elephants and pythons.","n":"The friend's innocent-sounding questions are a sleep test, and the noise of the night (falls, thunder, flood) is both the cover for his coming and the danger he braved to come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 68","lb":"A68","poet":"ஊட்டியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் இரவுக்குறி வந்தமையறிந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி வந்தமையறிந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கூதளம்","கூதளம்",2,"maram","scene",""],["அருவிப்","அருவி",3,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["செயலை","செயலை",5,"maram","scene","asoka"],["பாம்புஎன","பாம்பு",6,"pira","simile",""],["உரும்எறிந்","உரும்",7,"pira","scene","thunder"],["கொண்மூத்","கொண்மூ",15,"pira","scene","cloud"],["மடப்பிடிப்","பிடி",18,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["யானை","யானை",19,"maa","scene",""],["பாந்தட்","பாந்தள்",20,"pira","scene","python"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:4"},"a:69":{"book":"akananuru","num":70,"t":["ஆய்நலம் தொலைந்த மேனியும், மாமலர்த்","தகை வனப்புஇழந்த கண்ணும், வகைஇல","வண்ணம் வாடிய வரியும் நோக்கி,","ஆழல் ஆன்றிசின் நீயே; உரிதினின்","ஈதல் இன்பம் வெஃகி, மேவரச்","செய்பொருள் திறவர் ஆகிப், புல்இலைப்","பராரை நெல்லி அம்புளித் திரள்காய்","கான மடமரைக் கணநிரை கவரும்","வேனில் அத்தம் என்னாது, ஏமுற்று,","விண்பொரு நெடுங்குடை இயல்தேர் மோரியர்","பொன்புனை திகிரி திரிதர குறைத்த","அறைஇறந்து அகன்றனர் ஆயினும், எனையதூஉம்","நீடலர் - வாழி, தோழி! - ஆடுஇயல்","மடமயில் ஒழித்த பீலிவார்ந்து, தம்","சிலைமாண் வல்வில் சுற்றிப், பலமாண்","அம்புடைக் கையர் அரண்பல நூறி,","நன்கலம் தரூஉம் வயவர் பெருமகன்","சுடர்மணிப் பெரும்பூண் ஆஅய் கானத்துத்","தலைநாள் அலரின் நாறும்நின்","அலர்முலை ஆகத்து இன்துயில் மறந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine, wasted and dimmed in eye and colour, to stop weeping: though the man, wanting the joy of giving, has gone past the rock the Moriyar's chariot wheels cut, through summer wastes where deer herds strip the nelli, he will not stay long forgetting sweet sleep on her breast, fragrant as the first-day blossom of Aay's forest.","n":"The friend grants his motive full dignity (he went for wealth to give, ஈதல் இன்பம்), and uses that dignity as her argument: a man moved by giving will not hoard his absence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 69","lb":"A69","poet":"உமட்டூர்கிழார் மகனார் பரங்கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்து நீட்டித்தான் தலைமகன் எனக் கவன்ற தலைமகட்கு வருவரென்பதுபடச் சொல்லித் தோழி ஆற்றுவித்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","வருவரென்பதுபடச்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மாமலர்த்","மாமலர்",1,"maram","simile",""],["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",7,"maram","scene",""],["மடமரைக்","மரை",8,"maa","scene","deer"],["மடமயில்","மயில்",14,"pul","scene",""],["பீலிவார்ந்து","பீலி",14,"pul","ornament","peacock feather"],["வயவர்","வயவர்",17,"seythi","scene",""],["அலரின்","அலர்",19,"maram","simile","blossom"]],"en":[["king","மோரியர்",10],["king","ஆஅய்",18]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:4"},"a:70":{"book":"akananuru","num":71,"t":["கொடுந்திமிற் பரதவர் வேட்டம் வாய்த்தென","இருபுலாக் கமழும் சிறுகுடிப் பாக்கத்துக்","குறுங்கண் அவ்வலைப் பயம்பா ராட்டி,","கொழுங்கண் அயிலை பகுக்கும் துறைவன்","நம்மொடு புணர்ந்த கேண்மை முன்னே","அலர்வாய்ப் பெண்டிர் அம்பல் தூற்றப்,","பலரும் ஆங்கு அறிந்தனர் மன்னே; இனியே","வதுவை கூடிய பின்றைப், புதுவது","பொன்வீ ஞாழலொடு புன்னை வரிக்கும்","கானல்அம் பெருந்துறைக் கவினி மாநீர்ப்","பாசடைக் கலித்த கணைக்கால் நெய்தல்","விழவுஅணி மகளிர் தழைஅணிக் கூட்டும்","வென்வேற் கவுரியர் தொல்முது கோடி","முழங்குஇரும் பௌவம் இரங்கும் முன் துறை","வெல்போர் இராமன் அருமறைக்கு அவித்த","பல்வீழ் ஆலம் போல,","ஒலிஅவிந் தன்றுஇவ், அழுங்கல் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine that their love for the lord of the fishing shore, once broadcast by gossiping women, is now crowned by the wedding, and the noisy town has fallen silent, like the banyan whose birds Rama, victor in war, hushed for his secret counsel at the ancient Kodi of the Kavuriyar.","n":"Gossip, the force that drives so many akam poems, is here simply shut off by marriage; the Rama legend supplies the image of a silence imposed on a whole clamorous tree.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 70","lb":"A70","poet":"மதுரைத் தமிழ்க் கூத்தனார் கடுவன் மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகன் வரைவு மலிந்தமை தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","வரைவு மலிந்தமை"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கொடுந்திமிற்","திமில்",1,"pira","scene","boat"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["இருபுலாக்","புலா",2,"unavu","scene","fish flesh"],["அவ்வலைப்","வலை",3,"pira","scene","net"],["அயிலை","அயிலை",4,"pira","gift_food","fish"],["ஞாழலொடு","ஞாழல்",9,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",9,"maram","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",11,"maram","ornament",""],["பௌவம்","பௌவம்",14,"pira","scene","sea"],["ஆலம்","ஆலம்",16,"maram","simile","banyan"]],"en":[["king","கவுரியர்",13],["town","கோடி",13],["king","இராமன்",15]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:4"},"a:71":{"book":"akananuru","num":72,"t":["நிறைந்தோர்த் தேரும் நெஞ்சமொடு, குறைந்தோர்","பயன்இன் மையின் பற்றுவிட்டு, ஒரூஉம்","நயன்இல் மாக்கள் போல, வண்டினம்","சுனைப்பூ நீத்துச், சினைப்பூப் படர,","மைஇல் மான்இனம் மருளப், பையென","வெந்துஆறு பொன்னின் அந்தி பூப்ப,","ஐயறிவு அகற்றும் கையறு படரோடு","அகல்இரு வானம் அம்மஞ்சு ஈனப்,","பகல்ஆற்றுப் படுத்த பழங்கண் மாலை,","காதலர்ப் பிரிந்த புலம்பின் நோதக,","ஆர்அஞர் உறுநர் அருநிறம் சுட்டிச்","கூர்எஃகு எறிஞரின் அலைத்தல் ஆனாது,","எள்அற இயற்றிய அழல்காண் மண்டிலத்து","உள்ஊது ஆவியின் பைப்பய நுணுகி,","மதுகை மாய்தல் வேண்டும் - பெரிது அழிந்து,","இதுகொல் - வாழி, தோழி! என் உயிர்","விலங்குவெங் கடுவளி எடுப்பத்","துளங்குமரப் புள்ளின் துறக்கும் பொழுதே?"],"p":"","r":"In the evening that ends the day, when bees leave pool flowers for branch flowers like graceless men who drop the poor, deer stand bewildered and the sky blooms like cooling gold, the speaker tells her friend that the evening's pain strikes like spearmen aiming at a wounded man's vital spot, her strength thins like breath in a furnace, and asks if this is the hour her life will leave her like a bird quitting a tree in a gale.","n":"The opening simile is about bees, but its vehicle (men who leave those who are no longer useful) falls on the absent lover without naming him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 71","lb":"A71","poet":"அந்தி யிளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்த விடத்து ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது,","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகட்குத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",3,"pira","simile","bee"],["சுனைப்பூ","சுனைப்பூ",4,"maram","simile",""],["சினைப்பூப்","சினைப்பூ",4,"maram","simile",""],["மான்இனம்","மான்",5,"maa","scene","deer"],["துளங்குமரப்","மரம்",18,"maram","simile",""],["புள்ளின்","புள்",18,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:4"},"a:72":{"book":"akananuru","num":73,"t":["இருள்கிழிப் பதுபோல் மின்னி, வானம்","துளிதலைக் கொண்ட நளிபெயல் நடுநாள்,","மின்னி மொய்த்த முரவுவாய்ப் புற்றம்","பொன்எறி பிதிரிற் சுடர வாங்கிக்,","குரும்பி, கெண்டும் பெருங்கை ஏற்றை","இரும்புசெய் கொல்எனத் தோன்றும் ஆங்கண்,","ஆறே அருமர பினவே; யாறே","சுட்டுநர்ப் பனிக்கும் சூருடை முதலைய;","கழைமாய் நீத்தம் கல்பொருது இரங்க,","'அஞ்சுவம் தமியம்' என்னாது, மஞ்சு சுமந்து,","ஆடுகழை நரலும் அணங்குடைக் கவாஅன்,","ஈர்உயிர்ப் பிணவின் வயவுப்பசி களைஇய,","இருங்களிறு அட்ட பெருஞ்சின உழுவை","நாம நல்லராக் கதிர்பட உமிழ்ந்த","மேய்மணி விளக்கின் புலர ஈர்க்கும்","வாள்நடந் தன்ன வழக்கு அருங் கவலை,","உள்ளுநர் உட்கும் கல்அடர்ச் சிறுநெறி,","அருள்புரி நெஞ்சமொடு எஃகு துணையாக","வந்தோன் கொடியனும் அல்லன்; தந்த","நீ தவறு உடையையும் அல்லை; நின்வயின்","ஆனா அரும்படர் செய்த","யானே, தோழி, தவறுஉடை யேனே!"],"p":"","r":"With the man standing outside at the night tryst, the heroine tells her friend that the road he came by at midnight in the rain is fearful (lightning on the anthills where a bear digs, rivers with crocodiles, a tiger that killed an elephant for its pregnant mate, paths like walking a sword's edge), and that he who came with a loving heart and only a spear for company is not cruel, nor is the friend who brought him at fault: the fault is hers.","n":"Spoken for his ears, the self-blame is a plea: by listing the road's terrors and taking the guilt on herself she tells him the night visits must end in marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kalaviyal","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 72","lb":"A72","poet":"எருமை வெளியனார் மகனார் கடலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"(1) தலைமகன் இரவுக்குறிக்கண் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறிக்கண் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["புற்றம்","புற்று",3,"pira","scene","anthill"],["குரும்பி","குரும்பி",5,"pira","scene","grubs"],["ஏற்றை","ஏற்றை",5,"maa","scene","male bear"],["முதலைய","முதலை",8,"pira","scene","crocodile"],["கழைமாய்","கழை",9,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பிணவின்","பிணவு",12,"maa","iraicchi","tigress"],["இருங்களிறு","களிறு",13,"maa","scene","elephant"],["உழுவை","உழுவை",13,"maa","iraicchi","tiger"],["நல்லராக்","அரா",14,"pira","scene","cobra"],["மேய்மணி","மணி",15,"pira","scene","gem"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","உழுவை","confirmed","1/1","the man who braves such a road at night does it out of the same care for her, so he is not cruel"]],"ch":"a:4"},"a:73":{"book":"akananuru","num":74,"t":["பின்னொடு முடித்த மண்ணா முச்சி","நெய்கனி வீழ்குழல் அகப்படத் தைஇ;","வெருகுஇருள் நோக்கி யன்ன கதிர் விடுபு","ஒருகாழ் முத்தம் இடைமுலை விளங்க,","அணங்குறு கற்பொடு மடம்கொளச் சாஅய்,","நின்நோய்த் தலையையும் அல்லை; தெறுவர","'என்ஆகுவள்கொல், அளியள் தான்?' என,","என்அழிபு இரங்கும் நின்னொடு யானும்","ஆறுஅன்று என்னா வேறுஅல் காட்சி","இருவேம் நம்படர் தீர வருவது","காணிய வம்மோ - காதல்அம் தோழி!","கொடிபிணங்கு அரில இருள்கொள் நாகம்","மடிபதம் பார்க்கும், வயமான் துப்பின்,","ஏனல்அம் சிறுதினைச் சேணோன் கையதைப்","பிடிக்கை அமைந்த கனல்வாய்க் கொள்ளி","விடுபொறிச் சுடரின் மின்னி அவர்","சென்ற தேஎத்து நின்றதால் மழையே"],"p":"","r":"One woman calls to the other (the colophon implies the friend speaks; the poem addresses the friend) that, since their pain is one, they should go and see what is coming to end it: over the land where he went the rain cloud stands, flashing like the sparks from a millet-guard's brand.","n":"The rain cloud is both comfort and accusation: it is the season he named for his return, seen over his country, not yet over hers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","mazhai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 73","lb":"A73","poet":"எருமை வெளியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பொருள்வயிற் பிரிகின்றான். குறித்த பருவவரவு கண்டு அழிந்த தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","குறித்த பருவவரவு கண்டு அழிந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வெருகுஇருள்","வெருகு",3,"maa","simile","wildcat"],["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",4,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["நாகம்","நாகம்",12,"pira","scene","snake"],["வயமான்","வயமான்",13,"maa","simile","tiger"],["ஏனல்அம்","ஏனல்",14,"unavu","scene","millet field"],["சேணோன்","சேணோன்",14,"seythi","simile","platform watcher"],["கொள்ளி","கொள்ளி",15,"pira","simile","brand"],["மழையே","மழை",17,"pira","scene","rain cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:4"},"a:74":{"book":"akananuru","num":75,"t":["வினைவலம் படுத்த வென்றியொடு மகிழ்சிறந்து,","போர்வல் இளையர் தாள்வலம் வாழ்த்தத்,","தண்பெயல் பொழிந்த பைதுறு காலை,","குருதி உருவின் ஒண்செம் மூதாய்","பெருவழி மருங்கில் சிறுபல வரிப்பப்,","பைங்கொடி முல்லை மென்பதப் புதுவீ","வெண்களர் அரிமணல் நன்பல் தாஅய்,","வண்டுபோது அவிழ்க்கும் தண்கமழ் புறவில்,","கருங்கோட்டு இரலைக் காமர் மடப்பிணை","மருண்டமான் நோக்கம் காண்தொறும், 'நின் நினைந்து","\"திண்தேர் வலவ! கடவு' எனக் கடைஇ,","இன்றே வருவர்; ஆன்றிகம் பனி' என,","வன்புறை இன்சொல் நன்பல பயிற்றும்","நின்வலித்து அமைகுவென் மன்னோ - அல்கல்","புன்கண் மாலையொடு பொருந்திக், கொடுங்கோற்","கல்லாக் கோவலர் ஊதும்","வல்வாய் சிறுகுழல் வருத்தாக் காலே!"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells the friend who keeps assuring her that the man, his task won, will see the startled doe of the stag in the rain-washed scrub, think of her and order his charioteer to hurry, that she could hold firm on that comfort if only the cowherds' small harsh flute did not torment her in the evening.","n":"Almost the whole poem is the friend's comfort, quoted back; the last three lines undo it with a single sound of her own mullai land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 74","lb":"A74","poet":"மதுரைக் கவுணியன் பூதத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் அழிந்த கிழத்தி வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குச்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["இளையர்","இளையர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["மூதாய்","மூதாய்",4,"pira","scene","velvet mite"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",6,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டுபோது","வண்டு",8,"pira","scene","bee"],["இரலைக்","இரலை",9,"maa","scene","stag"],["மடப்பிணை","பிணை",9,"maa","scene","doe"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",16,"seythi","scene",""],["சிறுகுழல்","குழல்",17,"yaazh","scene","flute"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:4"},"a:75":{"book":"akananuru","num":76,"t":["\"அருள் அன்று ஆக, ஆள்வினை, ஆடவர்","பொருள்\" என வலித்த பொருள்அல் காட்சியின்","மைந்துமலி உள்ளமொடு துஞ்சல் செல்லாது,","எரிசினம் தவழ்ந்த இருங்கடற்று அடைமுதல்","கரிகுதிர் மரத்த கான வாழ்க்கை,","அடுபுலி முன்பின், தொடுகழல் மறவர்","தொன்றுஇயல் சிறுகுடி மன்றுநிழற் படுக்கும்","அண்ணல் நெடுவரை, ஆம்அறப் புலர்ந்த","கல்நெறிப் படர்குவர் ஆயின் - நல்நுதல்,","செயிர்தீர் கொள்கை, சில்மொழி துவர்வாய்,","அவிர்தொடிய முன்கை, ஆய்இழை, மகளிர்","ஆரம் தாங்கிய அலர்முலை ஆகத்து,","ஆராக் காதலொடு தாரிடைக் குழையாது","சென்றுபடு விறற்கவின் உள்ளி, என்றும்","இரங்குநர் அல்லது, பெயர்தந்து, யாவரும்,","தருநரும் உளரோ, இவ் உலகத் தான்?, என-","மாரி ஈங்கை மாத்தளிர் அன்ன","அம்மா மேனி, ஐது அமை நுசுப்பின்;","பல்காசு நிரைத்த, கோடுஏந்து, அல்குல்;","மெல்இயல் குறுமகள்!- புலந்துபல கூறி","ஆனா நோலை ஆக, யானே","பிரியச் சூழ்தலும் உண்டோ ,","அரிதுபெறு சிறப்பின் நின்வயி னானே?\""],"p":"","r":"Answering the woman's fear that he will leave, the man recalls the saying that effort, not grace, is a man's wealth, says that those who follow it across burnt hills only grieve ever after for beauty that wasted unembraced and that no one in this world can give back, and asks whether he could ever plan to leave her, so hard a prize to win.","n":"The opening maxim is quoted to be refuted, and the refutation turns on a pun: the vision that decided on wealth is பொருள்அல் காட்சி, a view that misses the point (பொருள்) even as it pursues wealth (பொருள்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","arul","selvam","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 75","lb":"A75","poet":"மதுரைப் போத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிவரென வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குப் பிரியேனெனத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பிரியேனெனத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அடுபுலி","புலி",6,"maa","simile",""],["மறவர்","மறவர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["ஈங்கை","ஈங்கை",17,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:4"},"a:76":{"book":"akananuru","num":77,"t":["மண்கனை முழவொடு மகிழ்மிகத் தூங்கத்,","தண்துறை ஊரன் எம்சேரி வந்தென","இன்கடுங் கள்ளின் அஃதை களிற்றொடு","நல்கலம் ஈயும் நாள்மகிழ் இருக்கை","அவைபுகு பொருநர் பறையின், ஆனாது,","கழறுப என்ப, அவன் பெண்டிர்; அந்தில்","கச்சினன், கழலினன், தேம்தார் மார்பினன்","வகைஅமைப் பொலிந்த, வனப்பு அமை தெரியல்,","சுரியல்அம் பொருநனைக் காண்டிரோ?' என,","ஆதி மந்தி பேதுற்று இனைய,","சிறைபறைந்து உறைஇச் செங்குணக்கு ஒழுகும்","அம்தண் காவிரி போல,","கொண்டுகை வலித்தல் சூழ்ந்திசின், யானே!"],"p":"","r":"Told that the wife's people rail at her, the courtesan says within their hearing that his women may keep on clamouring like bards' drums at a patron's morning feast; as Aathi Manthi wandered distraught asking who had seen her curly-haired dancer and the cool Kaveri held him, so she has resolved to seize the man and hold him fast.","n":"The courtesan borrows the Aathi Manthi legend and takes the river's part in it, leaving the wife the role of the bereaved queen.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 76","lb":"A76","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைமகளை நயப்பித்துக் கொண்டாளென்று கழறக்கேட்ட பரத்தை தலைமகள் பாங்காயினார் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","pira",null,null,"கழறக்கேட்ட பரத்தை"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முழவொடு","முழவு",1,"parai","scene",""],["கள்ளின்","கள்",3,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["களிற்றொடு","களிறு",3,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["பொருநர்","பொருநர்",5,"seythi","simile",""],["பறையின்","பறை",5,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[["patron","அஃதை",3],["river","காவிரி",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:4"},"a:77":{"book":"akananuru","num":78,"t":["'நல்நுதல் பசப்பவும், ஆள்வினை தரீஇயர்,","துன்அருங் கானம் துன்னுதல் நன்று' எனப்","பின்னின்று சூழ்ந்தனை ஆயின், நன்று இன்னாச்","சூழ்ந்திசின் - வாழிய நெஞ்சு!- வெய்துற","இடிஉமிழ் வானம் நீங்கி, யாங்கணும்","குடிபதிப் பெயர்ந்த சுட்டுடை முதுபாழ்,","கயிறுபிணிக் குழிசி ஓலை கொண்மார்,","பொறிகண்டு அழிக்கும் ஆவண மாக்களின்,","உயிர்திறம் பெயர, நல்அமர்க் கடந்த","தறுக ணாளர் குடர் தரீஇத் தெறுவச்,","செஞ்செவி எருவை, அஞ்சுவர இருக்கும்","கல்அதர்க் கவலை போகின், சீறூர்ப்","புல்அரை இத்திப் புகர்படு நீழல்","எல்வளி அலைக்கும், இருள்கூர் மாலை,","வானவன் மறவன், வணங்குவில் தடக்கை,","ஆனா நறவின் வண்மகிழ் பிட்டன்","பொருந்தா மன்னர் அருஞ்சமத்து உயர்த்த","திருந்துஇலை எஃகம் போல,","அருந்துயர் தரும், இவள் பனிவார் கண்ணே!"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart that if it plots to cross the forest for wealth though her brow pale, it plots ill: past ruined villages where vultures pull out the guts of fallen warriors, at the dark evening in a hamlet's ithi shade, her tear-filled eyes will wound him like the spear Pittan, Chera's generous warrior, raised against hostile kings.","n":"The parting is stopped in the imagination: the man pictures the evening of the journey, and what decides is not the road's danger but her eyes as he will remember them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aalvinai","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 77","lb":"A77","poet":"மருதனிளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரியக் கருதிய நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லி, செலவழுங்குவித்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","செலவழுங்குவித்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["குழிசி","குழிசி",7,"pira","simile","pot"],["எருவை","எருவை",11,"pul","scene","vulture"],["இத்திப்","இத்தி",13,"maram","scene",""],["நறவின்","நறவு",16,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"]],"en":[["king","வானவன்",15],["patron","பிட்டன்",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:4"},"a:78":{"book":"akananuru","num":79,"t":["'நனந்தலைக் கானத்து ஆளி அஞ்சி,","இனம்தலைத் தரூஉம் எறுழ்கிளர் முன்பின்,","வரிஞிமிறு ஆர்க்கும், வாய்புகு கடாஅத்துப்","பொறிநுதற் பொலிந்த வயக்களிற்று ஒருத்தல்","இரும்பிணர்த் தடக்கையில், ஏமுறத் தழுவ,","கடுஞ்சூல் மடப்பிடி நடுங்கும் சாரல்,","தேம்பிழி நறவின் குறவர் முன்றில்","முந்தூழ் ஆய்மலர் உதிரக், காந்தள்","நீடுஇதழ் நெடுந்துடுப்பு ஒசியத், தண்ணென","வாடை தூக்கும் வருபனி அற்சிரம்,","நம்இல் புலம்பின், நம் ஊர்த் தமியர்","என்ஆ குவர்கொல் அளியர் தாம்?' என","எம்விட்டு அகன்ற சின்னாள், சிறிதும்,","உள்ளியும் அறிதிரோ - ஓங்குமலை நாட!","உலகுடன் திரிதரும் பலர்புகழ் நல்இசை","வாய்மொழிக் கபிலன் சூழச் சேய்நின்று","செழுஞ்செய்ந் நெல்லின் விளைகதிர் கொண்டு,","தடந்தாள் ஆம்பல் மலரொடு கூட்டி,","யாண்டுபல கழிய, வேண்டுவயிற் பிழையாது,","ஆள்இடூஉக் கடந்து, வாள்அமர் உழக்கி,","ஏந்துகோட்டு யானை வேந்தர்ஓட்டிய","நெடும்பரிப் புரவிக் கைவண் பாரி","தீம்பெரும் பைஞ்சுனைப் பூத்த","தேம்கமழ் புதுமலர் நாறும் - இவள் நுதலோ?"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the hill-lord, back from his absence, whether in his few days away he ever once thought of them in the cold dewy season, when the tusker on the slope, afraid of the yali, wraps his trembling pregnant mate in his trunk; and she says the girl's brow smells of the new flowers of Paari's pool, Paari who with Kapilar beside him held out for years and drove the kings away.","n":"The friend puts the right thought into his mouth (how will they fare, poor things, alone in our town?) and then asks if he ever had it; the brow fragrant as Paari's pool makes the girl a prize kings could not take.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 78","lb":"A78","poet":"மதுரை நக்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"களவுக் காலத்துப் பிரிந்து வந்த தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","களவுக் காலத்துப் பிரிந்து வந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆளி","ஆளி",1,"maa","scene","yali"],["வரிஞிமிறு","ஞிமிறு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["வயக்களிற்று","களிறு",4,"maa","iraicchi","tusker"],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",6,"maa","iraicchi","she-elephant"],["நறவின்","நறவு",7,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["குறவர்","குறவர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",8,"maram","scene",""],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",17,"unavu","gift_food",""],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",18,"maram","gift_food",""],["புரவிக்","புரவி",22,"maa","scene","horse"],["புதுமலர்","மலர்",24,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","பாரி",22]],"dv":[["iraicchi","வயக்களிற்று","confirmed","2/2","the man should guard the girl who trembles alone through the cold season, as the beast of his own hill guards his mate, instead of leaving her"]],"ch":"a:4"},"a:79":{"book":"akananuru","num":80,"t":["தோட்பதன் அமைத்த கருங்கை ஆடவர்","கனைபொறி பிறப்ப நூறி, வினைப் படர்ந்து","கல்லுறுத்து இயற்றிய வல்உயர்ப் படுவில்,","பார்உடை மருங்கின் ஊறல் மண்டிய","வன்புலம் துமியப் போகிக், கொங்கர்","படுமணி ஆயம் நீர்க்கு நிமிர்ந்து செல்லும்","சேதா எடுத்த செந்நிலக் குரூஉத் துகள்","அகல்இரு விசும்பின் ஊன்றித் தோன்றும்","நனந்தலை அழுவம், நம்மொடு துணைப்ப,","'வல்லாங்கு வருதும்' என்னாது, அல்குவர","வருந்தினை - வாழி என் நெஞ்சே!- இருஞ்சிறை","வளைவாய்ப் பருந்தின் வான்கட் பேடை,","ஆடுதொறு கனையும் அவ்வாய்க் கடுந்துடிக்","கொடுவில் எயினர் கோட்சுரம் படர","நெடுவிளி பயிற்றும் நிரம்பா நீள்இடை,","கல்பிறங்கு அத்தம் போகி","நில்லாப் பொருட்பிணிப் பிரிந்த நீயே!"],"p":"","r":"Midway across the waste, the man tells his heart that it has suffered for wealth that never stays, crossing the hard ground of stone-cut wells, the red dust of the Kongar herds rushing to water, and the long road where the kite's mate calls while the Eyinar bowmen go out raiding to their drum.","n":"The last word is the charge: the wealth that drew him is நில்லாப் பொருள், wealth that does not stay, so the hardship is spent on something already slipping away.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 79","lb":"A79","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்த தலைமகன் இடைச் சுரத்துத் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","இடைச் சுரத்துத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",1,"seythi","scene","well-diggers"],["ஆயம்","ஆயம்",6,"maa","scene","herd"],["சேதா","சேதா",7,"maa","scene","red cow"],["பருந்தின்","பருந்து",12,"pul","scene","kite"],["பேடை","பேடை",12,"pul","scene",""],["கடுந்துடிக்","துடி",13,"parai","scene",""],["எயினர்","எயினர்",14,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:4"},"a:80":{"book":"akananuru","num":81,"t":["கொடுந்தாள் முதலையொடு கோட்டுமீன் வழங்கும்","இருங்கழி இட்டுச்சுரம் நீந்தி, இரவின்","வந்தோய் மன்ற - தண்கடற் சேர்ப்ப !-","நினக்குஎவன் அரியமோ, யாமே? எந்தை","புணர்திரைப் பரப்பகம் துழைஇத் தந்த","பல்மீன் உணங்கற் படுபுள் ஓப்புதும்","முண்டகம் கலித்த முதுநீர் அடைகரை","ஒண்பன் மலரக் கவட்டுஇலை அடும்பின்","செங்கேழ் மென்கொடி ஆழி அறுப்ப","இனமணிப் புரவி நெடுந்தேர் கடைஇ,","மின்இலைப் பொலிந்த விளங்கிணர் அவிழ்பொன்","தண்நறும் பைந்தாது உறைக்கும்","புன்னைஅம் கானல், பகல்வந் தீமே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the cool shore that he has truly come by night, swimming the dark backwater where crocodiles and sharks move; asking whether they are so hard for him to reach, she says they spend the day driving birds from their father's drying fish, and bids him drive his chariot over the adumbu creepers and come by day to the punnai grove.","n":"The invitation by day is a refusal of the night: a chariot that cuts the shore creepers leaves a track everyone can see, so the friend is asking for a courtship in the open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 80","lb":"A80","poet":"மருங்கூர் கிழார் பெருங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"இரவுக் குறிவந்த தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக் குறிவந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["முதலையொடு","முதலை",1,"pira","scene","crocodile"],["கோட்டுமீன்","கோட்டுமீன்",1,"pira","scene","shark"],["பல்மீன்","மீன்",6,"unavu","scene","dried fish"],["படுபுள்","புள்",6,"pul","scene",""],["முண்டகம்","முண்டகம்",7,"maram","scene",""],["அடும்பின்","அடும்பு",8,"maram","scene",""],["புரவி","புரவி",10,"maa","scene","horse"],["புன்னைஅம்","புன்னை",13,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:4"},"a:81":{"book":"akananuru","num":82,"t":["நாள்உலா எழுந்த கோள்வல் உளியம்","ஓங்குசினை இருப்பைத் தீம்பழம் முனையின்,","புல்அளைப் புற்றின் பல்கிளைச் சிதலை","ஒருங்கு முயன்று எடுத்த நனைவாய் நெடுங்கோடு","அரும்புஊது குருகின், இடந்து, இரை தேரும்","மண்பக வறந்த ஆங்கண் கண்பொரக்","கதிர்தெறக் கவிழ்ந்த உலறுதலை நோன்சினை","நெறிஅயல் மராஅம் ஏறிப், புலம்புகொள","எறிபருந்து உயவும் என்றூழ் நீள்இடை","வெம்முனை அருஞ்சுரம் நீந்திச் - சிறந்த","செம்மல் உள்ளம் துரத்தலின், கறுத்தோர்","ஒளிறுவேல் அழுவம் களிறுபடக் கடக்கும்","மாவண் கடலன் விளங்கில் அன்ன, எம்","மைஎழில் உண்கண் கலுழ-","ஐய! சேறிரோ, அகன்றுசெய் பொருட்கே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend, having read the woman's heart, asks the man who has announced his going whether he will really cross the long burning waste for wealth made by going away, where a bear tired of sweet iruppai fruit digs out a termite mound and a kite pines alone on a sun-scorched maraa tree, while her lovely kohl-dark eyes, fair as Kadalan's town Vilangil, run with tears.","n":"The question is put in the plural of respect (ஐய! சேறிரோ) and closes on the wealth itself, அகன்றுசெய் பொருள், wealth that is made by leaving: the friend names the price inside the word for the prize. The bear that turns from sweet fruit when it is sated to dig in a hard mound for a meal could be read as the man who leaves a sweet home for hard-won wealth, but the poem draws no pairing, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","selvam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 81","lb":"A81","poet":"ஆலம்பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவுணர்த்திய தலைமகற்குத் தோழி தலைமகள் குறிப்பறிந்து வந்து சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","பிரிவுணர்த்திய தலைமகற்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["உளியம்","உளியம்",1,"maa","scene","bear"],["இருப்பைத்","இருப்பை",2,"maram","scene",""],["சிதலை","சிதலை",3,"pira","scene","termite"],["மராஅம்","மராஅம்",8,"maram","scene",""],["பருந்து","பருந்து",9,"pul","scene","kite"]],"en":[["patron","கடலன்",13],["town","விளங்கில்",13]],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:5"},"a:82":{"book":"akananuru","num":83,"t":["ஆடுஅமைக் குயின்ற அவிர்துளை மருங்கின்","கோடை அவ்வளி குழலிசை ஆக,","பாடுஇன் அருவிப் பனிநீர் இன்இசைத்","தோடுஅமை முழவின் துதைகுரல் ஆகக்","கணக்கலை இகுக்கும் கடுங்குரற் றூம்பொடு","மலைப்பூஞ் சாரல் வண்டுயாழ் ஆக","இன்பல் இமிழ்இசை கேட்டுக், கலிசிறந்து,","மந்தி நல்அவை மருள்வன நோக்கக்","கழைவளர் அடுக்கத்து, இயலி ஆடுமயில்","நனவுப்புகு விறலியின் தோன்றும் நாடன்!","உருவவல் விற்பற்றி, அம்புதெரிந்து,","செருச்செய் யானை செல்நெறி வினாஅய்ப்,","புலர்குரல் ஏனற் புழையுடை ஒருசிறை,","மலர்தார் மார்பன், நின்றோற் கண்டோர்","பலர்தில், வாழி - தோழி - அவருள்,","ஆர்இருட் கங்குல் அணையொடு பொருந்தி,","ஓர்யான் ஆகுவது எவன்கொல்,","நீர்வார் கண்ணொடு, நெகிழ்தோ ளேனே?"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the hill man's country is a stage where wind in bored bamboo is the flute, the waterfall the drum, the stag's call the horn and the bees the lute, the monkeys an amazed audience and the peacock a dancer; many saw him standing at the gap of the millet field with his bow, asking after the elephant he had hunted, so why should she alone lie through the dark night with streaming eyes and slackened arms?","n":"Telling the friend this is the first step of disclosure (அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்): she admits she met him, but in the form of a complaint that many saw him and only she suffers. The hill's whole orchestra plays to an audience of monkeys, and the one hearer it truly moves is she, alone at night.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kaamanoy","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 82","lb":"A82","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழிக்குத் தலைவி அறத்தொடு நின்றது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","அறத்தொடு நின்றது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஆடுஅமைக்","அமை",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["அருவிப்","அருவி",3,"pira","scene",""],["கணக்கலை","கலை",5,"maa","scene","stag"],["வண்டுயாழ்","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene",""],["மந்தி","மந்தி",8,"maa","scene",""],["மயில்","மயில்",9,"pul","simile",""],["யானை","யானை",12,"maa","scene",""],["ஏனற்","ஏனல்",13,"unavu","scene","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:5"},"a:83":{"book":"akananuru","num":84,"t":["வலம்சுரி மராஅத்துச் சுரம்கமழ் புதுவீச்","சுரிஆர் உளைத்தலை பொலியச் சூடி,","கறைஅடி மடப்பிடி கானத்து அலறக்,","களிற்றுக் கன்று ஒழித்த உவகையர், கலிசிறந்து,","கருங்கால் மராஅத்து கொழுங்கொம்பு பிளந்து,","பெரும்பொழி வெண்நார் அழுந்துபடப் பூட்டி,","நெடுங்கொடி நுடங்கும் நியம மூதூர்,","நறவுநொடை நல்இல் பதவுமுதற் பிணிக்கும்","கல்லா இளையர் பெருமகன் புல்லி","வியன்தலை நல்நாட்டு வேங்கடம் கழியினும்","சேயர் என்னாது, அன்புமிகக் கடைஇ,","எய்தவந் தனவால் தாமே - நெய்தல்","கூம்புவிடு நிகர்மலர் அன்ன","ஏந்துஎழில் மழைக்கண்எம் காதலி குணனே!"],"p":"","r":"On the road, the man tells his heart that though he has passed the Venkatam hill of Pulli, chief of the unlettered young men who wear maraa flowers, tear an elephant calf from its wailing mother and tie it at the toddy-houses of their flag-lined town in exchange for drink, his love's virtues, her rain-moist eyes like an opening neythal flower, did not count the distance and have come all the way to reach him.","n":"Paalai usually makes the woman the one whom memory reaches; here her qualities (குணனே) are the travellers, driven by love (அன்புமிகக் கடைஇ) across the frontier he has just crossed. The cow elephant crying in the forest for the calf the young men have taken sits behind his thought of the woman he left, but the poem does not draw the pairing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 83","lb":"A83","poet":"கல்லாடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் இடைச்சுரத்துத் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"இடைச்சுரத்துத் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மராஅத்துச்","மராஅம்",1,"maram","ornament",""],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",3,"maa","scene",""],["களிற்றுக்","களிறு",4,"maa","scene",""],["மராஅத்து","மராஅம்",5,"maram","scene",""],["நறவுநொடை","நறவு",8,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["இளையர்","இளையர்",9,"seythi","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",12,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["king","புல்லி",9],["hill","வேங்கடம்",10]],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:5"},"a:84":{"book":"akananuru","num":85,"t":["மலைமிசைக் குலஇய உருகெழு திருவில்","பணைமுழங்கு எழிலி பௌவம் வாங்கித்","தாழ்பெயற் பெருநீர், வலன்ஏர்பு வளைஇ,","மாதிரம் புதைப்பப் பொழிதலின், காண்வர","இருநிலம் கவினிய ஏமுறு காலை","நெருப்பின் அன்ன சிறுகட் பன்றி,","அயிர்க்கட் படாஅர்த் துஞ்சுபுறம் புதைய,","நறுவீ முல்லை நாண்மலர் உதிரும்","புறவு அடைந் திருந்த அருமுனை இயவிற்","சீறூ ரோளே, ஒண்ணுதல்! - யாமே,","எரிபுரை பன்மலர் பிறழ வாங்கி,","அரிஞ்ர் யாத்த அலங்குதலைப் பெருஞ்சூடு","கள்ஆர் வினைஞர் களந்தொறும் மறுகும்","தண்ணடை தழீஇய கொடிநுடங்கு ஆர்எயில்","அருந்திறை கொடுப்பவும் கொள்ளான், சினம்சிறந்து,","வினைவயின் பெயர்க்குந் தானைப்,","புனைதார், வேந்தன் பாசறை யேமே!"],"p":"","r":"In the war camp the man says that the rains have come, a rainbow on the hill and drum-voiced clouds pouring till the directions vanish, and she of the bright brow is in her small village on the frontier woodland where mullai flowers drop on a sleeping boar's back; while he is in the camp of a garlanded king who, angry, will not accept even the rare tribute of a fortress set among rich fields of reapers and toddy-drinking labourers, and moves his army on to fight.","n":"Two lands face each other across the poem: her mullai woodland in the promised rains, and the enemy's marutham fields where the harvest goes on. The rains that should send him home have come; what keeps him is the king's anger, which turns down the very tribute that would end the war (அருந்திறை கொடுப்பவும் கொள்ளான்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","mazhai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 84","lb":"A84","poet":"மதுரை எழுத்தாளன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பாசறை இருந்து சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","பாசறை இருந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பன்றி","பன்றி",6,"maa","simile",""],["படாஅர்த்","படாஅர்",7,"maram","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",8,"maram","scene",""],["பன்மலர்","மலர்",11,"maram","simile",""],["பெருஞ்சூடு","சூடு",12,"unavu","scene","sheaf"],["வினைஞர்","வினைஞர்",13,"seythi","scene",""],["கள்ஆர்","கள்",13,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:5"},"a:85":{"book":"akananuru","num":86,"t":["நன்னுதல் பசப்பவும், பெருந்தோள் நெகிழவும்","உண்ணா உயக்கமொடு உயிர்செலச் சாஅய்","இன்னம் ஆகவும், இங்குநத் துறந்தோர்","அறவர் அல்லர் அவர்' எனப் பலபுலந்து","ஆழல் - வாழி, தோழி!- 'சாரல்,","ஈன்றுநாள் உலந்த மென்னடை மடப்பிடி,","கன்றுபசி களைஇய, பைங்கண் யானை","முற்றா மூங்கில் முளைதருபு, ஊட்டும்","வென்வேல் திரையன் வேங்கட நெடுவரை;","நல்நாள் பூத்த நாகுஇள வேங்கை","நறுவீ ஆடிய பொறிவரி மஞ்ஞை","நனைப்பசுங் குருந்தின் நாறுசினை இருந்து,","துணைப்பயிர்ந்து அகவும் துணைதரு தண்கார்,","வருதும், யாம்' எனத் தேற்றிய","பருவம் காண் அது; பாயின்றால் மழையே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman not to weep in reproach that the man who left them is without virtue while her brow pales and her arms waste: he assured them he would come back in the cool rains, when on the slopes of Thiraiyan's tall Venkatam hill the bull elephant breaks young bamboo shoots to feed the hungry calf of his soft-stepping mate, and the peacock, bathed in vengai pollen, calls its mate from the kurunthu branch; that is the season, and the rain has spread.","n":"The comfort is built from his own words: the friend quotes the promise, scene and all, and then points to the sky. The elephant feeding his mate's calf is inside that promise, so the man himself had chosen the image of a male who provides.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","mazhai","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 85","lb":"A85","poet":"காட்டூர் கிழார் மகனார் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிய வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது.","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",6,"maa","iraicchi",""],["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","iraicchi",""],["மூங்கில்","மூங்கில்",8,"maram","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",10,"maram","scene",""],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",11,"pul","scene",""],["குருந்தின்","குருந்து",12,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","திரையன்",9],["hill","வேங்கட",9]],"dv":[["iraicchi","யானை","confirmed","1/1","the man who named this scene in his promise is one who provides for his own, so his word to return can be trusted"]],"ch":"a:5"},"a:86":{"book":"akananuru","num":87,"t":["உழுந்துதலைப் பெய்த கொழுங்கனி மிதவை","பெருஞ்சோற்று அமலை நிற்ப, நிரைகால்","தண்பெரும் பந்தர்த் தருமணல் ஞெமிரி,","மனைவிளக் குறுத்து, மாலை தொடரிக்,","கனைஇருள் அகன்ற கவின்பெறு காலைக்,","கோள்கால் நீங்கிய கொடுவெண் திங்கள்","கேடில் விழுப்புகழ் நாள்தலை வந்தென,","உச்சிக் குடத்தர், புத்துகன் மண்டையர்,","பொதுசெய் கம்பலை முதுசெம் பெண்டிர்","முன்னவும் பின்னவும் முறைமுறை தரத்தரப்,","புதல்வற் பயந்த திதலை! அவ் வயிற்று","வால்இழை மகளிர் நால்வர் கூடிக்,","'கற்பினின் வழாஅ, நற்பல உதவிப்","பெற்றோற் பெட்கும் பிணையை ஆக'- என","நீரொடு சொரிந்த ஈர்இதழ் அலரி","பல்இருங் கதுப்பின் நெல்லொடு தயங்க","வதுவை நன்மணம் கழிந்த பின்றைக்,","கல்லென் சும்மையர் ஞெரேரெனப் புகுதந்து,","'பேர்இற் கிழத்தி ஆக' எனத் தமர்தர;","ஓர்இற் கூடிய உடன்புணர் கங்குல்,","கொடும்புறம் வளஇக், கோடிக் கலிங்கத்து","ஒடுங்கினள் கிடந்த ஓர்புறம் தழீஇ,","முயங்கல் விருப்பொடு முகம்புதை திறப்ப,","அஞ்சினள் உயிர்த்த காலை, யாழநின்","நெஞ்சம் படர்ந்தது எஞ்சாது உரை' என","இன்நகை இருக்கை, பின்யான் வினவலின்-","செஞ்சூட்டு ஒண்குழை வண்காது துயல்வர","அகமலி உவகையள் ஆகி, முகன் இகுத்து,","ஒய்யென இறைஞ்சி யோளே - மாவின்","மடம்கொள் மதைஇய நோக்கின்","ஒடுங்குஈர் ஓதி, மா அ யோளே"],"p":"","r":"The man recalls the wedding: rice cooked with black gram heaped high, sand spread under the cool pandal, lamps and garlands, the auspicious dawn when the moon stood free of the evil planet; matrons with pots and bowls passing things in order; four mothers of sons pouring water with petals and paddy on her hair and blessing her never to fail in chastity and to love the man who took her; her kin then giving her as lady of the great house; and the first night, when she lay curled in her new cloth with her face hidden, and asked what her heart felt, she only bowed her head in deep joy.","n":"The blessing (கற்பினின் வழாஅ, நற்பல உதவிப் பெற்றோற் பெட்கும் பிணையை ஆக) is the karpu ideal spoken by women who have kept it. By the colophon's first reading the man tells all this to the friend who has shut the door on him, so the memory of the wedding becomes his plea against a quarrel the poem never names. Her answer to his question is a gesture, not a word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","punarchi","naanam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 86","lb":"A86","poet":"நல்லாவூர்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"வாயில் மறுத்த தோழிக்கு தலைமகன் சொல்லியது தலைமகளைக் கூடி இன்புற்றிருந்த தலைமகன் பண்டு நிகழ்ந்தது சொற்று இன்புற்றிருந்ததூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"karpu","வாயில் மறுத்த தோழிக்கு தலைமகன் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பண்டு நிகழ்ந்தது சொற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["உழுந்துதலைப்","உழுந்து",1,"unavu","gift_food","black gram"],["பெருஞ்சோற்று","சோறு",2,"unavu","gift_food",""],["தருமணல்","மணல்",3,"pira","scene",""],["அலரி","அலரி",15,"maram","ornament",""],["நெல்லொடு","நெல்",16,"unavu","ornament",""],["மாவின்","மா",29,"maa","scene","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:5"},"a:87":{"book":"akananuru","num":88,"t":["தீந்தயிர் கடைந்த திரள்கால் மத்தம்","கன்றுவாய் சுவைப்ப, முன்றில் தூங்கும்","படலைப் பந்தர்ப் புல்வேய் குரம்பை,","நல்கூர் சீறூர் எல்லித் தங்கிக்,","குடுமி நெற்றி நெடுமரச் சேவல்","தலைக்குரல் விடியற் போகி, முனாஅது,","கடுங்கண் மறவர் கல்லெழு குறும்பின்","எழுந்த தண்ணுமை இடங்கட்பாணி,","அருஞ்சுரம் செல்வோர் நெஞ்சம் துண்ணெனக்,","குன்றுசேர் கவலை, இசைக்கும் அத்தம்,","நனிநீடு உழந்தனை மன்னே! அதனால்","உவஇனி - வாழிய, நெஞ்சே - மைஅற","வைகுசுடர் விளங்கும் வான்தோய் வியனகர்ச்","சுணங்குஅணி வனமுலை நலம்பா ராட்டித்,","தாழ்இருங் கூந்தல்நம் காதலி","நீள்அமை வனப்பின் தோளுமார் அணைந்தே!"],"p":"","r":"Coming home with his work done, the man tells his heart that it suffered long on the road, lodging for the night in a poor hamlet of grass huts where a calf sucks the churning stick hung in the yard, leaving at the cock's first call, and passing hill forks where the drum of fierce marauders from their rock forts makes travellers' hearts quake; now let it rejoice, for they will praise her breasts and lie in her bamboo-smooth arms in their tall house where the lamp burns all night.","n":"The hamlet's small homely detail, a calf mouthing the curd-stick under a creeper pandal, is the first sight of a home on the way back and foreshadows his own; the heart, not the man, is the traveller who suffered (நனிநீடு உழந்தனை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 87","lb":"A87","poet":"மதுரைப் பேராலவாயார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றி மீளுந் தலைமகன் இடைச்சுரத்துத் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றி மீளுந் தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["தீந்தயிர்","தயிர்",1,"unavu","scene","curd"],["கன்றுவாய்","கன்று",2,"maa","scene","calf"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",5,"pul","scene","cock"],["மறவர்","மறவர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",8,"parai","scene",""],["நீள்அமை","அமை",16,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:5"},"a:88":{"book":"akananuru","num":89,"t":["முதைச்சுவற் கலித்த மூரிச் செந்தினை","ஓங்குவணர்ப் பெருங்குரல் உணீஇய, பாங்கர்ப்","பகுவாய்ப் பல்லி பாடுஓர்த்துக் குறுகும்","புருவைப் பன்றி வருதிறம் நோக்கி,","கடுங்கைக் கானவன் கழுதுமிசைக் கொளீஇய","நெடுஞ்சுடர் விளக்கம் நோக்கி வந்து, நம்","நடுங்குதுயர் களைந்த நன்ன ராளன்","சென்றனன் கொல்லோ தானே - குன்றத்து","இரும்புலி தொலைத்த பெருங்கை யானைக்","கவுள்மலிபு இழிதரும் காமர் கடாஅம்","இருஞ்சிறைத் தொழுதி ஆர்ப்ப, யாழ்செத்து,","இருங்கல் விடர்அளை அசுணம் ஓர்க்கும்","காம்புஅமல் இறும்பில் பாம்புபடத் துவன்றிக்,","கொடுவிரல் உளியம் கெண்டும்","வடுஆழ் புற்றின வழக்குஅரு நெறியே?"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening outside, the friend wonders whether the good man who came guided by the torch a hunter had set on his platform to watch for the boar stealing to the red millet, and who ended their trembling grief, has gone back by that path no one can tread, where a tiger-killing elephant's musth draws bees whose hum the asunam takes for a lute, where snakes crowd the bamboo thicket and the bear digs at deep anthills.","n":"The catalogue of dangers is spoken for the listener outside: a road this perilous cannot be walked every night, and only marriage ends the need. The torch set to catch a thief is the light the lover came by.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","iravu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 88","lb":"A88","poet":"ஈழத்துப் பூதன் தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி வந்த தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறி வந்த தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["செந்தினை","தினை",1,"unavu","ullurai","millet"],["பல்லி","பல்லி",3,"pira","scene","lizard"],["பன்றி","பன்றி",4,"maa","ullurai",""],["கானவன்","கானவன்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["இரும்புலி","புலி",9,"maa","scene",""],["யானைக்","யானை",9,"maa","scene",""],["தொழுதி","தொழுதி",11,"pira","scene","bee swarm"],["அசுணம்","அசுணம்",12,"pira","scene",""],["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",13,"pira","scene",""],["உளியம்","உளியம்",14,"maa","scene","bear"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","பன்றி செந்தினை","confirmed","2/2","the lover who comes by night to the guarded girl"]],"ch":"a:5"},"a:89":{"book":"akananuru","num":90,"t":["தெறுகதிர் ஞாயிறு நடுநின்று காய்தலின்,","உறுபெயல் வறந்த ஓடுதேர் நனந்தலை,","உருத்துஎழு குரல குடிஞைச் சேவல்,","புல்சாய் விடரகம் புலம்ப, வரைய","கல்லெறி இசையின் இரட்டும் ஆங்கண்,","சிள்வீடு கறங்கும் சிறியிலை வேலத்து","ஊழுறு விளைநெற்று உதிரக், காழியர்","கவ்வைப் பரப்பின் வெவ்வுவர்ப்பு ஒழியக்,","களரி பரந்த கல்நெடு மருங்கின்,","விளர்ஊன் தின்ற வீங்குசிலை மறவர்","மைபடு திண்தோள் மலிர வாட்டிப்,","பொறைமலி கழுதை நெடுநிரை தழீஇய","திருந்துவாள் வயவர் அருந்தலை துமித்த","படுபுலாக் கமழும் ஞாட்பில், துடிஇகுத்து","அருங்கலம் தெறுத்த பெரும்புகல் வலத்தர்,","விலங்கெழு குறும்பில் கோள்முறை பகுக்கும்","கொல்லை இரும்புனம் நெடிய என்னாது,","மெல்லென் சேவடி மெலிய ஏக","வல்லுநள் கொல்லோ தானே - தேம்பெய்து","அளவுறு தீம்பால் அலைப்பவும் உண்ணாள்,","இடுமணற் பந்தருள் இயலும்,","நெடுமென் பணைத்தோள், மாஅ யோளே?"],"p":"","r":"The foster mother, after the girl has gone off with her man, asks whether she can walk on her soft red feet across the wide burning land where the rains failed and the mirage runs, where the owl booms like struck stone in the crevices, cicadas shrill in the thorn trees, and marauders who have eaten pale meat cut down the guards of a donkey caravan and share out the loot to the beat of the drum, she who would not drink honeyed milk even when coaxed and played under the sand-strewn pandal.","n":"The road is all violence and the man does not appear at all: the mother can only measure the girl's feet against the land. The last lines bring the reader back into the house, to the milk she refused and the pandal where she played, so the loss is set in the very place the girl has left.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","paasam","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 89","lb":"A89","poet":"மதுரைக் காஞ்சிப் புலவர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட்போக்கிய செவிலித்தாய் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட்போக்கிய செவிலித்தாய்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குடிஞைச்","குடிஞை",3,"pul","scene","owl"],["சிள்வீடு","சிள்வீடு",6,"pira","scene","cicada"],["வேலத்து","வேலம்",6,"maram","scene",""],["விளர்ஊன்","ஊன்",10,"unavu","gift_food",""],["மறவர்","மறவர்",10,"seythi","scene",""],["கழுதை","கழுதை",12,"maa","scene","donkey"],["துடிஇகுத்து","துடி",14,"parai","scene",""],["தீம்பால்","பால்",20,"unavu","gift_food","milk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:5"},"a:90":{"book":"akananuru","num":91,"t":["மூத்தோர் அன்ன வெண்தலைப் புணரி","இளையோர் ஆடும் வரிமனை சிதைக்கும்","தளைஅவிழ் தாழைக் கானல்அம் பெருந்துறை","சில்செவித்து ஆகிய புணர்ச்சி அலர்எழ,","இல்வயிற் செறித்தமை அறியாய்; பன்னாள்","வருமுலை வருத்தா, அம்பகட்டு மார்பின்","தெருமரல் உள்ளமொடு வருந்தும் நின்வயின்,","'நீங்குக' என்று, யான் யாங்ஙனம் மொழிகோ?","அருந்திறற் கடவுட் செல்லூர்க் குணாஅது","பெருங்கடல் முழக்கிற்று ஆகி, யாணர்,","இரும்புஇடம் படுத்த வடுவுடை முகத்தர்,","கருங்கட் கோசர் நியமம் ஆயினும்","'உறும்' எனக் கொள்குநர் அல்லர்-","நறுநுதல் அரிவை பாசிலை விலையே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend meets the man leaving after a day tryst on the shore where white-crested waves like old men wreck the sand houses of the young girls, and tells him that gossip of their union, known to few, has risen and the girl has been shut up at home; she cannot tell him to go away while he suffers so, but her people would not take even the rich market of the dark-eyed Kosar east of Chellur as enough for her.","n":"The waves likened to old men (மூத்தோர் அன்ன) that break the play-houses of the young are the elders who have shut the girl in and broken off the lovers' meetings, and the friend never says so. The closing bride-price line turns on பாசிலை, whose sense here (a green leaf of a courtship gift, or her worth) the text does not settle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 90","lb":"A90","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறி வந்து கண்ணுற்று நீங்கும் தலைமகனைத் தோழி எதிர்ப்பட்டு நின்று இற்செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இற்செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புணரி","புணரி",1,"pira","ullurai","waves"],["தாழைக்","தாழை",3,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["town","செல்லூர்",9]],"dv":[["ullurai","புணரி","not_confirmed","0/2","the girl's elders, who have shut her in and ended the meetings"],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:5"},"a:91":{"book":"akananuru","num":92,"t":["விளங்குபகல் உதவிய பல்கதிர் ஞாயிறு","வளம்கெழு மாமலை பயம்கெடத் தெறுதலின்,","அருவி ஆன்ற பெருவரை மருங்கில்","சூர்ச்சுனை துழைஇ நீர்ப்பயம் காணாது,","பாசி தின்ற பைங்கண் யானை","ஓய்பசிப் பிடியொடு ஒருதிறன் ஒடுங்க,","வேய்கண் உடைந்த வெயில்அவிர் நனந்தலை","அரும்பொருள் வேட்கையின் அகன்றனர் ஆயினும்,","பெரும்பேர் அன்பினர் - தோழி!- இருங்கேழ்","இரலை சேக்கும், பரல்உயர் பதுக்கைக்","கடுங்கண் மழவர் களவுஉழவு எழுந்த","நெடுங்கால் ஆசினி ஒடுங்காட்டு உம்பர்,","விசிபிணி முழவின் குட்டுவன் காப்பப்,","பசிஎன அறியாப் பணைபயில் இருக்கைத்,","தடமருப்பு எருமை தாமரை முனையின்,","முடமுதிர் பலவின் கொழுநிழல் வதியும்,","குடநாடு பெறினும், தவிரலர்-","மடமாண் நோக்கி! நின் மாண்நலம் மறந்தே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the wasting woman that though the man left for rare wealth across a waste where the sun has dried the falls, and an elephant that found no water in the dread pool ate moss and shrank together with his hungry mate, he has great love: even if he gained Kuttuvan's Kudanadu beyond the marauders' scrub, a land of drums that knows no hunger, where the buffalo tired of lotus rests in the shade of old jackfruit trees, he would not stay there forgetting her beauty.","n":"The wealth the man went for is measured by the richest land the friend can name, and the claim is that he would refuse it. The elephant pair is on his road, the buffalo in the land he would refuse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 91","lb":"A91","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","iraicchi",""],["பிடியொடு","பிடி",6,"maa","iraicchi",""],["பாசி","பாசி",5,"pira","scene","moss"],["வேய்கண்","வேய்",7,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["இரலை","இரலை",10,"maa","scene",""],["மழவர்","மழவர்",11,"seythi","scene",""],["ஆசினி","ஆசினி",12,"maram","scene",""],["முழவின்","முழவு",13,"parai","scene",""],["எருமை","எருமை",15,"maa","scene",""],["தாமரை","தாமரை",15,"maram","scene",""],["பலவின்","பலா",16,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவன்",13],["town","குடநாடு",17]],"dv":[["iraicchi","யானை","confirmed","2/2","even beasts in the drought keep together, so the man who crossed that land has not stopped loving her"]],"ch":"a:5"},"a:92":{"book":"akananuru","num":93,"t":["நெடுமலை அடுக்கம் கண்கெட மின்னிப்,","படுமழை பொழிந்த பானாட் கங்குல்,","குஞ்சரம் நடுங்கத் தாக்கிக், கொடுவரிச்","செங்கண் இரும்புலி குழுமும் சாரல்","வாரல் - வாழியர், ஐய! நேர்இறை","நெடுமென் பணைத்தோள் இவளும் யானும்","காவல் கண்ணினம் தினையே; நாளை","மந்தியும் அறியா மரம்பயில் இறும்பின்","ஒண்செங் காந்தள் அவிழ்ந்த ஆங்கண்","தண்பல் அருவித் தாழ்நீர் ஒருசிறை,","உருமுச் சிவந்து எறிந்த உரன்அழி பாம்பின்","திருமணி விளக்கிற் பெறுகுவை-","இருள்மென் கூந்தல் ஏமுறு துயிலே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man not to come on such nights, when lightning blinds the slopes and in the pouring midnight rain a striped tiger strikes an elephant and roars; tomorrow she and the girl will be guarding millet, and in a grove so thick that not even the monkeys know it, by a cool fall where red kanthal has opened, he may have the sleep of her soft dark hair, by the light of a gem from a snake that thunder has struck.","n":"The day tryst she offers is made of impossibilities, a grove unknown to monkeys and a lamp from a thunder-killed snake's gem, so that the man hears what the colophon says she means: marriage, not meetings (வரைவு கடாயது).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","iravu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 92","lb":"A92","poet":"மதுரைப் பாலாசிரியர் நற்றாமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிச் சென்று தலைமகளைக் கண்ணுற்று நீங்குத் தலைமகனைப் பகற்குறி நேர்ந்த வாய்பாட்டால் தோழி வரைவு கடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["குஞ்சரம்","குஞ்சரம்",3,"maa","scene","elephant"],["இரும்புலி","புலி",4,"maa","scene",""],["தினையே","தினை",7,"unavu","scene","millet"],["மந்தியும்","மந்தி",8,"maa","scene",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",9,"maram","scene",""],["அருவித்","அருவி",10,"pira","scene",""],["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",11,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:5"},"a:93":{"book":"akananuru","num":94,"t":["கேள்கேடு ஊன்றவும் கிளைஞர் ஆரவும்,","கேள்அல் கேளிர் கெழீஇயினர் ஒழுகவும்,","ஆள்வினைக்கு எதிரிய ஊக்கமொடு புகல்சிறந்து,","ஆரங் கண்ணி அடுபோர்ச் சோழர்","அறம்கெழு நல்அவை உறந்தை அன்ன","பெறல்அரு நல்கலம் எய்தி நாடும்","செயல்அருஞ் செய்வினை முற்றினம் ஆயின்","அரண்பல கடந்த, முரண்கொள் தானை,","வாடா வேம்பின், வழுதி கூடல்","நாள்அங் காடி நாறும் நறுநுதல்","நீள்இருங் கூந்தன் மாஅ யோளொடு","வரைகுயின் றன்ன வான்தோய் நெடுநகர்","நுரைமுகந் தன்ன மென்பூஞ் சேக்கை","நிவந்த பள்ளி, நெடுஞ்சுடர் விளக்கத்து,","நலம்கேழ் ஆகம் பூண்வடுப் பொறிப்ப,","முயங்குகம் சென்மோ - நெஞ்சே! வரிநுதல்","வயம்திகழ்பு இழிதரும் வாய்புகு கடாஅத்து ,","மீளி மொய்ம்பொடு நிலன்எறியாக் குறுகி,","ஆள்கோள் பிழையா, அஞ்சுவரு தடக்கைக்,","கடும்பகட்டு யானை நெடுந்தேர்க் கோதை","திருமா வியனகர்க் கருவூர் முன்துறைத்","தெண்நீர் உயர்கரைக் குவைஇய","தன்ஆன் பொருநை மணலினும் பலவே!"],"p":"","r":"Coming home with his work done, the man tells his heart: to end the kin's ruin, feed the relatives and make friends of strangers, he went with zeal for effort and won rare treasure as fine as the just assembly of the Cholas at Uraiyur; now let them go and embrace, more times than the sands heaped on the banks of the Porunai at Kothai's Karuvur, the dark woman whose brow smells like the morning market of Vazhuthi's Koodal, in their sky-high house on a bed soft as gathered foam by the tall lamp, till her ornaments print their marks on her.","n":"The opening gives the paalai journey a household reason: wealth is for the kin, the relatives and the stranger made friend. The three crowned houses each lend one simile: the Chola court for wealth, the Pandya market for her scent, the Chera river's sands for the count of embraces.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","selvam","punarchi","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 93","lb":"A93","poet":"கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றி மீளலுறுந் தலைமகன் இடைச்சுரத்துத் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றி மீளலுறுந் தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஆரங்","ஆரம்",4,"maram","ornament",""],["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",9,"maram","ornament",""],["யானை","யானை",20,"maa","scene",""],["மணலினும்","மணல்",23,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",4],["town","உறந்தை",5],["king","வழுதி",9],["town","கூடல்",9],["king","கோதை",20],["town","கருவூர்",21],["river","பொருநை",23]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:5"},"a:94":{"book":"akananuru","num":95,"t":["தேம்படு சிமயப் பாங்கர்ப் பம்பிய","குவைஇலை முசுண்டை வெண்பூக் குழைய,","வான்எனப் பூத்த பானாட் கங்குல்,","மறித்துரூஉத் தொகுத்த பறிப்புற இடையன்","தண்கமழ் முல்லை தோன்றியொடு விரைஇ,","வண்டுபடத் தொடுத்த நீர்வார் கண்ணியன்,","ஐதுபடு கொள்ளி அங்கை காயக்,","குறுநரி உளம்பும் கூர்இருள் நெடுவிளி","சிறுகட் பன்றிப் பெருநிரை கடிய,","முதைப்புனம் காவலர் நினைத்திருந்து ஊதும்","கருங்கோட்டு ஓசை யொடு ஒருங்குவந்து இசைக்கும்","வன்புலக் காட்டுநாட் டதுவே - அன்புகலந்து","ஆர்வம் சிறந்த சாயல்,","இரும்பல் கூந்தல், திருந்திழை ஊரே!"],"p":"","r":"Returning, the man tells his charioteer that her town, the town of the woman of thick dark hair and loving gentleness, lies in the hard woodland country where at midnight the musundai blooms white like the sky, a goatherd with a leather bag, wearing a dripping garland of mullai and thondri, warms his palms at a sputtering brand and whistles long into the dark where the jackal howls, together with the black horn the field-guards blow to drive off herds of boar.","n":"Her town is reached through its night sounds, the herdsman's whistle and the guards' horn, heard as one; the herdsman out in the dark, warming his hands far from home, is a figure of the man himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","anbu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 94","lb":"A94","poet":"நன்பலூர்ச் சிறுமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றி மீளுந் தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது தலைமகன் பாங்கற்குச் சொற்றதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivan","paangan",null,"karpu","பாங்கற்குச் சொற்றதூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முசுண்டை","முசுண்டை",2,"maram","scene",""],["மறித்துரூஉத்","மறி",4,"maa","scene","goat"],["இடையன்","இடையன்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",5,"maram","ornament",""],["தோன்றியொடு","தோன்றி",5,"maram","ornament",""],["வண்டுபடத்","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene",""],["குறுநரி","நரி",8,"maa","scene",""],["பன்றிப்","பன்றி",9,"maa","scene",""],["கருங்கோட்டு","கோடு",11,"yaazh","scene","horn"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:5"},"a:95":{"book":"akananuru","num":96,"t":["பைப்பயப் பசந்தன்று நுதலும்; சாஅய்,","ஐதுஆ கின்று, என் தளிர்புரை மேனியும்,","பலரும் அறியத் திகழ்தரும் அவலமும்;","உயிர்கொடு கழியின் அல்லதை; நினையின்","எவனோ?- வாழி, தோழி!- பொரிகாற்","பொகுட்டுஅரை இருப்பைக் குவிகுலைக் கழன்ற","ஆலிஒப்பின் தூம்புடைத் திரள்வீ,","ஆறுசெல் வம்பலர் நீள்இடை அழுங்க,","ஈனல் எண்கின் இருங்கிளை கவரும்","சுரம்பல கடந்தோர்க்கு இரங்குப என்னார்,","கௌவை மேவலர் ஆகி, 'இவ் ஊர்","நிரையப் பெண்டிர் இன்னா கூறுவ","புரைய அல்ல, என் மகட்கு' எனப் பரைஇ,","நம்உணர்ந்து ஆறிய கொள்கை","அன்னை முன்னர், யாம்என், இதற்படலே?"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that her brow has paled, her leaf-tender body thinned and her grief shows for all to see, and thinking can end it only with her life; the town, fond of gossip, will not say she grieves for one who crossed the wastes where a bear and her cubs carry off the hail-like iruppai flowers, and her mother has answered the women of the town that their harsh words do not fit her daughter: before such a mother, who understands and is calm, what can she do in this state?","n":"The mother here does not scold; she defends the girl against the town, and that is what the girl cannot bear, since the mother's trust is what she is about to betray. The colophon says the speaker is தலைமகன், but the poem's first person is the woman (என் தளிர்புரை மேனி, என் மகட்கு in the mother's mouth).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","melivu","udanpokku"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 95","lb":"A95","poet":"ஒரோடோகத்துக் கந்தரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"போக்குடன் பட்ட தலைமகன் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","போக்குடன் பட்ட"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["தளிர்புரை","தளிர்",2,"maram","simile",""],["இருப்பைக்","இருப்பை",6,"maram","scene",""],["தூம்புடைத் திரள்வீ","வீ",7,"maram","scene",""],["வம்பலர்","வம்பலர்",8,"seythi","scene","travellers"],["எண்கின்","எண்கு",9,"maa","scene","bear"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:5"},"a:96":{"book":"akananuru","num":97,"t":["நறவுண் மண்டை நுடக்கலின் இறவுக்கலித்துப்,","பூட்டுஅறு வில்லிற் கூட்டுமுதல் தெறிக்கும்","பழனப் பொய்கை அடைகரைப் பிரம்பின்","அரவாய் அன்ன அம்முள் நெடுங்கொடி","அருவி ஆம்பல் அகல்அடை துடக்கி,","அசைவரல் வாடை தூக்கலின், ஊதுஉலை","விசைவாங்கு தோலின், வீஞ்குபு ஞெகிழும்","கழனிஅம் படப்பைக் காஞ்சி ஊர!","'ஒண்தொடி ஆயத் துள்ளும்நீ நயந்து","கொண்டனை' என்ப 'ஓர் குறுமகள்'; அதுவே","செம்பொற் சிலம்பின், செறிந்த குறங்கின்","அம்கலுழ் மாமை, அஃதை தந்தை,","அண்ணல் யானை அடுபோர்ச் சோழர்,","வெண்ணெல் வைப்பின் பருவூர்ப் பறந்தலை,","இருபெரு வேந்தரும் பொருதுகளத்து ஒழிய,","ஒளிறுவாள் நல்அமர்க் கடந்த ஞான்றை,","களிறுகவர் கம்பலை போல,","அலர்ஆ கின்றது, பலர்வாய்ப் பட்டே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend, refusing the man entry, calls him lord of a kaanji town where in the field-pond prawns leap like a bow sprung loose, and a thorny cane, its long stem toothed like a file, catches the broad leaves of the water lily and, swung by the north wind, swells and slackens like a forge's bellows; they say he has chosen one young girl out of all the bangled band, and that news has spread through many mouths like the uproar of elephants taken on the day the Chola, father of Aqthai, beat both great kings on the battlefield of Paruvur.","n":"The friend matches his private conquest to a royal victory's din: what he thought hidden is as public as a war. The cane that catches the lily and swells and slackens with every wind is his way with women.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","alar","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 96","lb":"A96","poet":"மருதம் பாடிய இளங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி வாயின் மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தோழி வாயின் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["இறவுக்கலித்துப்","இறவு",1,"pira","scene","prawn"],["பிரம்பின்","பிரம்பு",3,"maram","ullurai","cane"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",5,"maram","ullurai",""],["காஞ்சி","காஞ்சி",8,"maram","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",13,"maa","scene",""],["வெண்ணெல்","நெல்",14,"unavu","scene",""],["களிறுகவர்","களிறு",17,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",13],["battle","பருவூர்ப் பறந்தலை",14]],"dv":[["ullurai","பிரம்பின் ஆம்பல்","confirmed","1/1","the husband and the young girl he has taken"]],"ch":"a:5"},"a:97":{"book":"akananuru","num":98,"t":["'கள்ளிஅம் காட்ட புள்ளிஅம் பொறிக்கலை","வறன்உறல் அம்கோடு உதிர, வலம்கடந்து,","புலவுப்புலி துறந்த கலவுக்கழிக் கடுமுடை","இரவுக்குறும்பு அலற நூறி, நிரைபகுத்து,","இருங்கல் முடுக்கர்த் திற்றி கெண்டும்","கொலைவில் ஆடவர் போலப், பலவுடன்","பெருந்தலை எருவையொடு பருந்துவந்து இறுக்கும்","அருஞ்சுரம் இறந்த கொடியோர்க்கு அல்கலும்,","இருங்கழை இறும்பின் ஆய்ந்துகொண்டு அறுத்த","நுணங்குகட் சிறுகோல் வணங்குஇறை மகளிரொடு","அகவுநர்ப் புரந்த அன்பின் கழல்தொடி,","நறவுமகிழ் இருக்கை, நன்னன் வேண்மான்","வயலை வேலி வியலூர் அன்னநின்","அலர்முலை ஆகம் புலம்பப் பல நினைந்து,","ஆழேல், என்றி - தோழி! யாழ என்","கண்பனி நிறுத்தல் எளிதோ - குரவுமலர்ந்து,","அற்சிரம் நீங்கிய அரும்பத வேனில்","அறல்அவிர் வார்மணல் அகல்யாற்று அடைகரைத்","துறைஅணி மருத தொகல்கொள ஓங்கிக்,","கலிழ்தளிர் அணிந்த இருஞ்சினை மாஅத்து","இணர்ததை புதுப்பூ நிரைத்த பொங்கர்ப்","புகைபுரை அம்மஞ்சு ஊர,","நுகர்குயில் அகவும் குரல்கேட் போர்க்கே?"],"p":"","r":"The woman answers her friend, who tells her not to weep thinking of the cruel man who crossed a waste where the spotted stag's horns fall in the drought and vultures and kites gather at a stench the tiger left, like marauders cutting up meat in rock clefts after a night raid on cattle: how can she easily stop her tears, hearing the sated koel call in spring, when the kuravam blooms, the cold season is over, marutham trees crowd the river ford and new mango flowers crown the boughs under smoky mist?","n":"The friend's comfort carries a compliment (her bosom fair as Nannan's Viyalur, a court that cherished bards), and the answer sets against it the joy of spring: the koel is நுகர்குயில், a bird that has had its fill, and it is the season's plenty, not the waste, that makes tears impossible to stop.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaalam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 97","lb":"A97","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"வற்புறுந்துந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கள்ளிஅம்","கள்ளி",1,"maram","scene",""],["பொறிக்கலை","கலை",1,"maa","scene","stag"],["புலவுப்புலி","புலி",3,"maa","scene",""],["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",6,"seythi","simile",""],["எருவையொடு","எருவை",7,"pul","scene","vulture"],["பருந்து","பருந்து",7,"pul","scene",""],["குரவுமலர்ந்து","குரவு",16,"maram","scene",""],["மருத","மருதம்",19,"maram","scene",""],["மாஅத்து","மா",20,"maram","scene","mango"],["நுகர்குயில்","குயில்",23,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[["king","நன்னன்",12],["town","வியலூர்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:5"},"a:98":{"book":"akananuru","num":99,"t":["பனிவரை நிவந்த பயம்கெழு கவாஅன்","துனிஇல் கொள்கையொடு அவர்நமக்கு உவந்த","இனிய உள்ளம் இன்னா ஆக,","முனிதக நிறுத்த நல்கல் எவ்வம்","சூர்உறை வெற்பன் மார்புஉறத் தணிதல்","அறிந்தனள் அல்லள், அன்னை; வார்கோல்","செறிந்துஇலங்கு எல்வளை நெகிழ்ந்தமை நோக்கிக்,","கையறு நெஞ்சினள் வினவலின், முதுவாய்ப்","பொய்வல் பெண்டிர் பிரப்புஉளர்பு இரீஇ,","'முருகன் ஆர் அணங்கு' என்றலின், அது செத்து","ஓவத் தன்ன வினைபுனை நல்இல்","'பாவை அன்ன பலர்ஆய் மாண்கவின்","பண்டையின் சிறக்க, என் மகட்கு' எனப் பரைஇ,","கூடுகொள் இன்இயம் கறங்கக், களன் இழைத்து,","ஆடுஅணி அயர்ந்த அகன்பெரும் பந்தர்,","வெண்போழ் கடம்பொடு சூடி, இன்சீர்","ஐதுஅமை பாணி இரீஇக், கைபெயராச்,","செல்வன் பெரும்பெயர் ஏத்தி, வேலன்","வெறிஅயர் வியன்களம் பொற்ப வல்லோன்","பொறிஅமை பாவையிற் றூங்கல் வேண்டின்,","என்ஆம் கொல்லோ?- தோழி!- மயங்கிய","மையற் பெண்டிற்க்கு நொவ்வல் ஆக","ஆடிய பின்னும், வாடிய மேனி","பண்டையிற் சிறவாது ஆயின், இம்மறை","அலர்ஆ காமையோ அரிதே, அஃதான்று,","அறிவர் உறுவிய அல்லல்கண் டருளி,","வெறிகமழ் நெடுவேள் நல்குவ னேயெனின்,","'செறிதொடி உற்ற செல்லலும் பிறிது' எனக்","கான்கெழு நாடன் கேட்பின்,","யான்உயிர் வாழ்தல் அதனினும் அரிதே!"],"p":"","r":"With the man standing by, the girl tells her friend that her mother does not know the ache of his withheld grace can be eased only on his chest; seeing her bangles slip, the mother asked the old lying women, who said it was Murugan's seizure, and so a velan dances in the decorated pandal to restore her beauty: if after all that her wasted body does not recover the secret will become gossip, and worse, if the god relieves her and the hill man hears that her sickness was something else, living will be harder still.","n":"The rite is described with care and mocked in the same breath (மையற் பெண்டிற்க்கு நொவ்வல்): the women are deluded, the velan dangles like a puppet. Her deepest fear is not exposure but that the rite might work, and the man conclude she never pined for him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","alar","vazhipaadu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 98","lb":"A98","poet":"வெறிபாடிய காமக்கண்ணியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது. தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லிய தூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"],["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["முருகன்","முருகன்",10,"deyvam","scene",""],["இன்இயம்","இயம்",14,"parai","scene","instruments"],["வெண்போழ்","போழ்",16,"maram","ornament","palm leaf"],["கடம்பொடு","கடம்பு",16,"maram","ornament",""],["பாணி","பாணி",17,"yaazh","scene","beat"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",18,"seythi","scene",""],["நெடுவேள்","நெடுவேள்",27,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:5"},"a:99":{"book":"akananuru","num":100,"t":["வாள்வரி வயமான் கோள்உகிர் அன்ன","செம்முகை அவிழ்ந்த முள்முதிர் முருக்கின்","சிதரார் செம்மல் தா அய், மதர்எழில்","மாண் இழை மகளிர் பூணுடை முலையின்","முகைபிணி அவிழ்ந்த கோங்கமொடு அசைஇ, நனை","அதிரல் பரந்த அம்தண் பாதிரி","உதிர்வீ அம்சினை தாஅய், எதிர்வீ","மராஅ மலரொடு விராஅய்ப் பராஅம்","அணங்குடை நகரின் மணந்த பூவின்","நன்றே, கானம்; நயவரும் அம்ம,","கண்டிசின வாழியோ - குறுமகள்! நுந்தை","அடுகளம் பாய்ந்த தொடிசிதை மருப்பின்,","பிடிமிடை களிற்றின் தோன்றும்","குறுநெடுந் துணைய குன்றமும் உடைத்தோ!"],"p":"","r":"Leading the girl who has gone off with him, the man coaxes her to look at the forest, strewn with palasa buds red as a tiger's claws, kongam opening like jewelled breasts, athiral and paathiri petals and maraa blossoms, lovely as the flower-strewn town of a dread god, and asks whether it also has hills of all heights that look like her father's war elephants, tusk-rings broken, crowded by their mates.","n":"This is the same kind of road that the foster mother of 89 fills with marauders; the lover makes it a temple town of flowers. His last question teases and reassures at once: even the hills are her father's elephants, so she has not left home behind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 99","lb":"A99","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உடன் போகிய தலைமகளைத் தலைமகன் மருட்டிச் சொல்லியது,","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","உடன் போகிய தலைமகளைத் தலைமகன் மருட்டிச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["வயமான்","மான்",1,"maa","simile","tiger"],["முருக்கின்","முருக்கு",2,"maram","scene",""],["கோங்கமொடு","கோங்கம்",5,"maram","scene",""],["அதிரல்","அதிரல்",6,"maram","scene",""],["பாதிரி","பாதிரி",6,"maram","scene",""],["மராஅ","மராஅம்",8,"maram","scene",""],["களிற்றின்","களிறு",13,"maa","simile",""],["பிடிமிடை","பிடி",13,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:5"},"a:100":{"book":"akananuru","num":101,"t":["அரையுற்று அமைந்த ஆரம் நீவிப்,","புரையப் பூண்ட கோதை மார்பினை,","நல்லகம் வடுக்கொள முயங்கி, நீ வந்து,","எல்லினில் பெயர்தல் எனக்குமார் இனிதே!","பெருந்திரை முழக்கமொடு இயக்குஅவிந் திருந்த","கொண்டல் இரவின் இருங்கடன் மடுத்த","கொழுமீன் கொள்பவர் இருள்நீங்கு ஒண்சுடர்","ஓடாப் பூட்கை வேந்தன் பாசறை","ஆடுஇயல் யானை அணிமுகத்து அசைத்த","ஓடை ஒண்சுடர் ஒப்பத் தோன்றும்","பாடுநர்த் தொடுத்த கைவண் கோமான்,","பரியுடை நற்றோர்ப் பெரியன், விரிஇணர்ப்","புன்னைஅம் கானற் புறந்தை முன்துறை","வம்ப நாரை இனன்ஒலித் தன்ன","அம்பல் வாய்த்த தெய்ய - தண்புலர்","வைகுறு விடியற் போகிய எருமை","நெய்தல்அம் புதுமலர் மாந்தும்","கைதைஅம் படப்பைஎம் அழுங்கல் ஊரே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that it is sweet to her too that he comes, wipes off his sandal, embraces the girl till her breast is marked, and leaves in the dark; but the gossip has come true, loud as a newly arrived flock of storks at the punnai-grove ford of Puranthai, town of the bards' patron Periyan, where fishers' lamps on the stilled sea at night shine like the face-lamps of a king's dancing war elephants; theirs is a sorrowing town of screwpine gardens where the buffalo that went out at cold dawn feeds on fresh neythal flowers.","n":"Line 4 is irony: what is sweet to the friend is exactly what she is ending. The buffalo that goes out at dawn and grazes the fresh neythal flower of their own town is the man who takes the girl's youth and leaves before light.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalavu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 100","lb":"A100","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி வரைவு கடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",1,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["கோதை","கோதை",2,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கொழுமீன்","மீன்",7,"pira","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",9,"maa","simile",""],["புன்னைஅம்","புன்னை",13,"maram","scene",""],["நாரை","நாரை",14,"pul","simile",""],["எருமை","எருமை",16,"maa","ullurai",""],["நெய்தல்அம்","நெய்தல்",17,"maram","ullurai",""],["கைதைஅம்","கைதை",18,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","பெரியன்",12],["town","புறந்தை",13]],"dv":[["ullurai","எருமை","confirmed","2/2","the lover who takes the girl's fresh youth by night and is gone before light"]],"ch":"a:5"},"a:101":{"book":"akananuru","num":102,"t":["அம்ம வாழி, தோழி! 'இம்மை","நன்றுசெய் மருங்கில் தீதுஇல்' என்னும்","தொன்றுபடு பழமொழி இன்றுபொய்த் தன்றுகொல்?-","தகர்மருப்பு ஏய்ப்பச் சுற்றுபு சுரிந்த","சுவல்மாய் பித்தைச் செங்கண் மழவர்","வாய்ப்பகை கடியும் மண்ணொடு கடுந்திறல்","தீப்படு சிறுகோல் வில்லொடு பற்றி,","நுரைதெரி மத்தம் கொளீஇ, நிரைப் புறத்து,","அடிபுதை தொடுதோல் பறைய ஏகிக்,","கடிபுலம் கவர்ந்த கன்றுடைக் கொள்ளையர்,","இனம்தலை பெயர்க்கும் நனந்தலைப் பெருங்காட்டு,","அகல் இரு விசும்பிற்கு ஓடம் போலப்,","பகலிடை நின்ற பல்கதிர் ஞாயிற்று","உருப்பு அவிர்பு ஊரிய சுழன்றுவரு கோடைப்","புன்கான் முருங்கை ஊழ்கழி பன்மலர்,","தண்கார் ஆலியின், தாவன உதிரும்,","பனிபடு பன்மலை இறந்தோர்க்கு,","முனிதகு பண்புயாம் செய்தன்றோ இலமே!"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend whether the old proverb that no harm comes in this life to those who do good has turned false today, since the man who crossed the many dewy hills, by a vast forest where red-eyed Mazhavar raiders with fire-sticks and bows drive off herds with their calves and where the whirling midday wind shakes the murungai's spent flowers down like cold hail, has gone though she did nothing to deserve his displeasure.","n":"The proverb is about this life (இம்மை), not a reward hereafter: she asks whether right conduct protects anyone now. The image of cool hail made out of flowers shaken down by scorching wind turns the comfort of rain into a sign of heat.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vinai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 101","lb":"A101","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட கிழத்தி, தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது; தோழி கிழத்திக்குச் சொல்லியதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட கிழத்தி, தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தோழி கிழத்திக்குச் சொல்லியதூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தகர்மருப்பு","தகர்",4,"maa","simile","ram"],["மழவர்","மழவர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["கன்றுடைக்","கன்று",10,"maa","scene","calf"],["முருங்கை","முருங்கை",15,"maram","simile",""],["ஆலியின்","ஆலி",16,"pira","simile","hailstone"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:6"},"a:102":{"book":"akananuru","num":103,"t":["உளைமான் துப்பின், ஓங்குதினைப் பெரும்புனத்துக்","கழுதில் கானவன் பிழிமகிழ்ந்து வதிந்தென,","உரைத்த சந்தின் ஊரல் இருங்கதுப்பு","ஐதுவரல் அசைவளி ஆற்றக், கைபெயரா","ஒலியல் வார்மயிர் உளரினள், கொடிச்சி","பெருவரை மருங்கிற் குறிஞ்சி பாடக்","குரலும் கொள்ளாது, நிலையினும் பெயராது","படாஅப் பைங்கண் பாடுபெற்று, ஒய்யென","மறம்புகல் மழ களிறு உறங்கும் நாடன்;","ஆர மார்பின் வரிஞிமிறு ஆர்ப்பத்,","தாரன் கண்ணியன், எஃகுடை வலத்தன்,","காவலர் அறிதல் ஓம்பிப், பையென","வீழாக் கதவம் அசையினன் புகுதந்து,","உயங்குபடர் அகலம் முயங்கித், தோள்மணந்து","இன்சொல் அளைஇப், பெயர்ந்தனன் - தோழி!","இன்றுஎவன் கொல்லோ கண்டிகும் - மற்றுஅவன்","நல்கா மையின் அம்பல் ஆகி,","ஒருங்குவந்து உவக்கும் பண்பின்","இருஞ்சூழ் ஓதி ஒண்நுதற் பசப்பே!"],"p":"","r":"Speaking to her friend so that the hero standing outside will hear, the heroine recalls how the man of the country where a fierce young elephant falls suddenly asleep to a hill girl's kurinji song, while the millet watcher lies drunk on his platform, came softly past the guards through the unbarred door, bees humming on his sandal-smeared chest, embraced her and left with sweet words; and she asks what this pallor is that has now come over her brow along with the gossip, because he has not given her more.","n":"The speech is aimed at the overhearer: the night visit is told as past and tender, the pallor and gossip as the present cost. The fierce elephant lulled by the girl's song and the drunk watchman asleep set a hill where every guard is off duty, which is what made the visits possible; a reader may also hear in the lulled elephant the hero lulled out of his purpose, but no part-by-part match is stated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","alar","pasalai","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 102","lb":"A102","poet":"மதுரை இளம்பாலாசிரியன் சேந்தங்கூத்தன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக் குறிக்கண் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக் குறிக்கண் சிறைப்புறமாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["ஓங்குதினைப்","தினை",1,"unavu","scene",""],["கானவன்","கானவன்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["பிழிமகிழ்ந்து","பிழி",2,"unavu","gift_food","brew"],["சந்தின்","சந்து",3,"maram","ornament","sandal"],["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",5,"seythi","scene",""],["குறிஞ்சி","குறிஞ்சி",6,"yaazh","scene","kurinji tune"],["களிறு","களிறு",9,"maa","scene",""],["ஆர","ஆரம்",10,"maram","ornament","sandal"],["வரிஞிமிறு","ஞிமிறு",10,"pira","scene","bee"],["தாரன்","தார்",11,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:6"},"a:103":{"book":"akananuru","num":104,"t":["நிழல்அறு நனந்தலை, எழில்ஏறு குறித்த","கதிர்த்த சென்னி நுணங்கு செந்நாவின்,","விதிர்த்த போலும் அம்நுண் பல்பொறிக்,","காமர் சேவல் ஏமம் சேப்ப;","முளிஅரில் புலம்பப் போகி, முனாஅது","முரம்பு அடைந் திருந்த மூரி மன்றத்து,","அதர்பார்த்து அல்கும் ஆகெழு சிறுகுடி","உறையுநர் போகிய ஓங்குநிலை வியன்மலை ;","இறைநிழல் ஒருசிறைப் புலம்புஅயா உயிர்க்கும்","வெம்முனை அருஞ்சுரம் நீந்தித், தம்வயின்","ஈண்டுவினை மருங்கின் மீண்டோர் மன்என,","நள்ளென் யாமத்து உயவுத்துணை ஆக","நம்மொடு பசலை நோன்று, தம்மொடு","தானே சென்ற நலனும்","நல்கார் கொல்லோ, நாம் நயந்திசி னோரோ?"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that, thinking the man would have finished his work and come back across the hot, hard waste, past a cattle village whose people have left the tall wide hill, she lay at midnight with pallor as her only companion, and asks whether the one they love will not give back the beauty that went away with him of its own accord.","n":"She does not ask for him but for her beauty, which she says went with him of itself (தம்மொடு தானே சென்ற நலனும்); the deserted cattle village on his road mirrors her own emptied state. Lines 1 to 4 (the spotted cock and what it watches) are obscure in our text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","nalam","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 103","lb":"A103","poet":"காவிரிப் பூம்பட்டினத்துச் செங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொற்றது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொற்றது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["சேவல்","சேவல்",4,"pul","scene","cock bird"],["முளிஅரில்","அரில்",5,"maram","absent","thicket"],["ஆகெழு","ஆ",7,"maa","scene","cow"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:6"},"a:104":{"book":"akananuru","num":105,"t":["வேந்துவினை முடித்த காலைத், தேம்பாய்ந்து","இனவண்டு ஆர்க்கும் தண்நறும் புறவின்","வென்வேல் இளையர் இன்புற, வலவன்","வள்புவலித்து ஊரின் அல்லது, முள்உறின்","முந்நீர் மண்டிலம் ஆதி ஆற்றா","நன்னால்கு பூண்ட கடும்பரி நெடுந்தேர்","வாங்குசினை பொலிய ஏறிப்; புதல","பூங்கொடி அவரைப் பொய்அதள் அன்ன","உள்இல் வயிற்ற, வெள்ளை வெண்மறி,","மாழ்கி யன்ன தாழ்பெருஞ் செவிய,","புன்றலை சிறாரோடு உகளி, மன்றுழைக்","கவைஇலை ஆரின் அங்குழை கறிக்கும்","சீறூர் பலபிறக்கு ஒழிய, மாலை","இனிதுசெய் தனையால் - எந்தை! வாழிய!-","பனிவார் கண்ணள் பலபுலந்து உறையும்","ஆய்தொடி அரிவை கூந்தற்","போதுகுரல் அணிய வேய்தந் தோயே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend blesses the hero who, his king's war finished, drove his swift four-horsed chariot through the cool, bee-loud woodland, leaving behind many small villages where white kids with hollow bellies like empty avarai pods and drooping ears frisk with the children and nibble the aar tree's shoots on the common, and arrived at evening to set flowers in the hair of his bangled wife, who had lived in tears and many reproaches.","n":"The friend praises the return by one act (இனிதுசெய் தனையால்): setting flowers in the wife's hair, the ornament she had given up while he was away. The playful kids of the small villages show the settled domestic life he rides through to reach his own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 104","lb":"A104","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றி மீளுந் தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றி மீளுந் தலைமகற்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["இனவண்டு","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bee"],["கடும்பரி","பரி",6,"maa","scene","horse"],["அவரைப்","அவரை",8,"maram","simile",""],["வெண்மறி","மறி",9,"maa","simile","kid"],["ஆரின்","ஆர்",12,"maram","scene",""],["போதுகுரல்","போது",17,"maram","ornament","blossom"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:6"},"a:105":{"book":"akananuru","num":106,"t":["அகல்அறை மலர்ந்த அரும்புமுதிர் வேங்கை","ஒள்இலைத் தொடலை தைஇ, மெல்லென","நல்வரை நாடன் தற்பா ராட்ட","யாங்குவல் லுநள்கொல் தானே - தேம்பெய்து,","மணிசெய் மண்டைத் தீம்பால் ஏந்தி,","ஈனாத் தாயர் மடுப்பவும் உண்ணாள்,","நிழற்கயத் தன்ன நீணகர் வரைப்பின்","எம்முடைச் செல்வமும் உள்ளாள், பொய்ம்மருண்டு","பந்துபுடைப் பன்ன பாணிப் பல்லடிச்","சில்பரிக் குதிரை, பல்வேல் எழினி","கெடல்அருந் துப்பின் விடுதொழில் முடிமார்,","கனைஎரி நடந்த கல்காய் கானத்து","வினைவல் அம்பின் விழுத்தொடை மறவர்","தேம்பிழி நறுங்கள் மகிழின், முனைகடந்து","வீங்குமென் சுரைய ஏற்றினம் தரூஉம்","முகைதலை திறந்த வேனிற்","பகைதலை மணந்த பல்அதர்ச் செலவே?"],"p":"","r":"The mother wonders how her daughter, who would not drink the sweet milk her nurses held to her in a jewelled bowl and gave no thought to the family's wealth in their great house cool as a shaded pool, but believed false words, will manage the many-pathed summer journey with the hill man who praises her and weaves her a garland of vengai leaves, through a scorched forest of hot stones where bowmen fulfilling the commission of Ezhini of many spears and the rhythmic-stepping horse drink honeyed toddy and drive off herds of full-uddered cattle across the frontier.","n":"The mother's doubt is about fitness, not morals: a girl who would not even take milk from her nurses now walks a road of cattle raiders. பொய்ம்மருண்டு (deceived by falsehood) is the mother's only judgment of the lover, and the vengai garland he weaves is a hill thing carried into the waste.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 105","lb":"A105","poet":"தாயங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட்போக்கிய தாய் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,null,"மகட்போக்கிய தாய்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","ornament",""],["தீம்பால்","பால்",5,"unavu","gift_food","milk"],["குதிரை","குதிரை",10,"maa","simile","horse"],["மறவர்","மறவர்",13,"seythi","scene",""],["நறுங்கள்","கள்",14,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["ஏற்றினம்","ஏறு",15,"maa","scene","bull"]],"en":[["king","எழினி",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:6"},"a:106":{"book":"akananuru","num":107,"t":["எரிஅகைந் தன்ன தாமரைப் பழனத்துப்,","பொரிஅகைந் தன்ன பொங்குபல் சிறுமீன்,","வெறிகொள் பாசடை, உணீஇயர், பைப்பயப்","பறைதபு முதுசிரல் அசைபுவந்து இருக்கும்","துறைகேழ் ஊரன் பெண்டுதன், கொழுநனை","நம்மொடு புலக்கும் என்ப - நாம்அது","செய்யாம் ஆயினும் உய்யா மையின்,","செறிதொடி தெளிர்ப்ப வீசிச், சிறிதுஅவண்","உலமந்து வருகம் சென்மோ - தோழி!-","ஒளிறுவாட் டானைக் கொற்றச் செழியன்","வெளிறுஇல் கற்பின் மண்டுஅமர் அடுதொறும்","களிறுபெறு வல்சிப் பாணன் எறியும்","தண்ணுமை கண்ணின் அலைஇயர், தன் வயிறே!"],"p":"","r":"Hearing that the wife of the man of the bright ford, where an old kingfisher too spent to fly rests on fragrant lotus leaves in a fire-red lotus pond, waiting to eat small fish that leap like popped rice, quarrels with her husband on their account, the courtesan tells her friend that since they cannot escape the blame though they did nothing, they should go there swinging their tight bangles and stroll a little, and let the wife beat her own belly like the drum the bard, who is fed on elephants, beats each time Chezhiyan of the shining-sword army wins a battle.","n":"The courtesan's mockery is in the landscape before it is in her words: the old kingfisher that cannot fly and only waits for the leaping fish is her picture of the wife. Chezhiyan's war drum, beaten at every victory, turns the curse into a public spectacle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","purangooral"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 106","lb":"A106","poet":"ஆலங்குடி வங்கனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைமகள் தன்னைப் புறங்கூறினாளாகக் கேட்ட பரத்தை அவட்குப் பாங்காயினார் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","pira","thozhi","karpu","அவட்குப் பாங்காயினார் கேட்ப"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",1,"maram","simile",""],["சிறுமீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","ullurai","fish"],["பாசடை","அடை",3,"maram","scene","lotus leaf"],["முதுசிரல்","சிரல்",4,"pul","ullurai","kingfisher"],["பாணன்","பாணன்",12,"seythi","simile",""],["களிறுபெறு","களிறு",12,"maa","gift_food",""],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",13,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[["king","செழியன்",10]],"dv":[["ullurai","முதுசிரல் சிறுமீன்","confirmed","1/1","the hero's wife and the young women around her husband, as the courtesan sees them"]],"ch":"a:6"},"a:107":{"book":"akananuru","num":108,"t":["நீசெலவு அயரக் கேட்டொறும், பலநினைந்து,","அன்பின் நெஞ்சத்து, அயாஅப் பொறை மெலிந்த","என்அகத்து இடும்பை களைமார், நின்னொடு","கருங்கல் வியல்அறை கிடப்பி, வயிறுதின்று","இரும்புலி துறந்த ஏற்றுமான் உணங்கல்","நெறிசெல் வம்பலர் உவந்தனர் ஆங்கண்,","ஒலிகழை நெல்லின் அரிசியொடு ஓராங்கு","ஆன்நிலைப் பள்ளி அளைசெய்து அட்ட","வால்நிணம் உருக்கிய வாஅல் வெண்சோறு","புகர்அரைத் தேக்கின் அகல்இலை மாந்தும்","கல்லா நீள்மொழிக் கதநாய் வடுகர்","வல்லாண் அருமுனை நீந்தி, அல்லாந்து,","உகுமண்ஊறு அஞ்சும் ஒருகாற் பட்டத்து","இன்னா ஏற்றத்து இழுக்கி, முடம் கூர்ந்து,","ஒருதனித்து ஒழிந்த உரனுடை நோன்பகடு","அம்குழை இருப்பை அறைவாய் வான்புழல்","புல்உளைச் சிறாஅர் வில்லின் நீக்கி,","மரைகடிந்து ஊட்டும் வரையகச் சீறூர்","மாலை இன்துணை ஆகிக், காலைப்","பசுநனை நறுவீப் பரூஉப்பரல் உறைப்ப,","மணமனை கமழும் கானம்","துணைஈர் ஓதிஎன் தோழியும் வருமே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hero, who is preparing to leave, that to end the grief she has borne each time she heard of his going, her friend with thick parted hair will go with him: across the hard frontier of the Vadukar, men of strange long speech with fierce dogs who eat white rice cooked with fat on teak leaves, where travelers rejoice at venison a tiger left on the black rock, past the steep slope where a lamed ox left alone is fed iluppai flowers by boys of a hill hamlet who drive the deer off with their bows, she will be his sweet companion at evening, and at dawn the forest will smell like a wedding house as fresh blossoms fall on the gravel.","n":"The friend turns the paalai road into a feast and a wedding: travelers' venison, the Vadukar's rice, a hamlet's evening and a forest that smells like a marriage house. The lamed ox left alone yet fed by strangers stands quietly against the fear of being left behind, though the poem does not draw that sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","pirivu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 107","lb":"A107","poet":"காவரிப்பூம்பட்டினத்துக் காரிக்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகள் குறிப்பறிந்து வந்து தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,"தலைமகள் குறிப்பறிந்து வந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இரும்புலி","புலி",5,"maa","scene",""],["ஏற்றுமான்","மான்",5,"maa","gift_food","stag"],["நெல்லின்","கழை நெல்",7,"unavu","gift_food","bamboo rice"],["வெண்சோறு","சோறு",9,"unavu","gift_food",""],["தேக்கின்","தேக்கு",10,"maram","scene",""],["கதநாய்","நாய்",11,"maa","scene",""],["வடுகர்","வடுகர்",11,"seythi","scene",""],["நோன்பகடு","பகடு",15,"maa","scene","ox"],["இருப்பை","இருப்பை",16,"maram","gift_food",""],["மரைகடிந்து","மரை",18,"maa","scene","deer"],["நறுவீப்","வீ",20,"maram","scene","blossom"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:6"},"a:108":{"book":"akananuru","num":109,"t":["புணர்ந்தோர் புன்கண் அருளலும் உணர்ந்தோர்க்கு","ஒத்தன்று மன்னால்! எவன்கொல்? முத்தம்","வரைமுதற் சிதறிய வைபோல், யானைப்","புகர்முகம் பொருத புதுநீர் ஆலி","பளிங்குசொரி வதுபோற் பாறை வரிப்பக்,","கார்கதம் பட்ட கண்அகன் விசும்பின்","விடுபொறி ஞெகிழியிற் கொடிபட மின்னி,","படுமழை பொழிந்த பானாட் கங்குல்,","ஆர்உயிர்த் துப்பின் கோள்மா வழங்கும்","இருளிடைத் தமியன் வருதல் யாவதும்","அருளான் - வாழி தோழி!- அல்கல்","விரவுப்பொறி மஞ்ஞை வெரீஇ அரவின்","அணங்குடை அருந்தலை பைவிரிப் பவைபோற்,","காயா மென்சினை தோய நீடிப்","பல்துடுப்பு எடுத்த அலங்குகுலைக் காந்தள்","அணிமலர் நறுந்தாது ஊதும் தும்பி","கைஆடு வட்டின் தோன்றும்","மைஆடு சென்னிய மலைகிழ வோனே!"],"p":"","r":"Speaking to the heroine so that the hero outside will hear, the friend says that pitying the pain of those one has joined is fitting for those who understand, yet the lord of the cloud-capped mountain, where kaanthal flowers rise like cobras spreading their hoods at a peacock and a bee at their pollen looks like a spinning top, shows no such pity, for he comes alone through midnight dark of pelting rain, where hail struck from an elephant's face streams on the rock like scattered pearls, lightning flashes like sparks of a whirled firebrand, and beasts of prey roam.","n":"The friend turns his courage into a want of kindness (அருளான்): by braving the night path he makes the women suffer fear for him, so true pity would be to stop coming that way, that is, to marry. The snake-hooded kaanthal carries the path's dread into the closing praise of his hill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu","arul","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 108","lb":"A108","poet":"தங்காற் பொற்கொல்லனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",2,"pira","simile","pearl"],["யானைப்","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""],["கோள்மா","கோள்மா",9,"maa","scene","beast of prey"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",12,"pul","simile","peacock"],["அரவின்","அரவு",12,"pira","simile","snake"],["காயா","காயா",14,"maram","scene",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",15,"maram","simile",""],["தும்பி","தும்பி",16,"pira","simile","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:6"},"a:109":{"book":"akananuru","num":110,"t":["பல்இதழ் மென்மலர் உண்கண், நல்யாழ்","நரம்புஇசைத் தன்ன இன்தீம் கிளவி,","நலம்நல்கு ஒருத்தி இருந்த ஊரே-","கோடுழு களிற்றின் தொழுதி ஈண்டிக்","காடுகால் யாத்த நீடுமரச் சோலை","விழைவெளில் ஆடும் கழைவளர் நனந்தலை;","வெண்நுனை அம்பின் விசைஇட வீழ்ந்தோர்","எண்ணுவரம்பு அறியா உவல்இடு பதுக்கைச்","சுரம்கெழு கவலை கோட்பாற் பட்டென","வழங்குநர் மடிந்த அத்தம் இறந்தோர்,","கைப்பொருள் இல்லை ஆயினும், மெய்க்கொண்டு","இன்உயிர் செகாஅர் விட்டுஅகல் தப்பற்குப்","பெருங்களிற்று மருப்பொடு வரி அதள் இறுக்கும்","அறன்இல் வேந்தன் ஆளும்","வறன்உறு குன்றம் பலவிலங் கினவே"],"p":"","r":"Halfway along the road, the man tells his heart that the town where she lives, she of soft eyes like many-petalled flowers and words as sweet as a well-tuned yazh string, is now cut off by many dry hills: a wide land of tall groves and bamboo where herds of tusk-ploughing elephants gather, forked tracks heaped with countless cairns over men felled by white-tipped arrows, where travelers are killed though they carry nothing, and which a king without aram rules, taking great tusks and striped hides.","n":"Three lines give her, twelve give the road; the killing ground is described so fully that it explains why the hills 'lie across' (விலங்கின) his way back. Lines 11 to 13 (whether the robbers kill to stop witnesses escaping, and whether the king takes tusks and hides as tribute) are hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","kodungol","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 109","lb":"A109","poet":"கடுந்தொடைக் காவினார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"இடைச்சுரத்துத் தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"இடைச்சுரத்துத் தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["மென்மலர்","மலர்",1,"maram","simile",""],["நல்யாழ்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","simile",""],["களிற்றின்","களிறு",4,"maa","scene",""],["நீடுமரச்","மரம்",5,"maram","scene",""],["கழைவளர்","கழை",6,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பெருங்களிற்று","களிறு",13,"maa","scene",""],["அதள்","அதள்",13,"maa","scene","hide"]],"en":[["herostone","பதுக்கைச்",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:6"},"a:110":{"book":"akananuru","num":111,"t":["அன்னை அறியினும் அறிக; அலர்வாய்","அம்மென் சேரி கேட்பினும் கேட்க;","பிறிதுஒன்று இன்மை அறியக் கூறிக்,","கொடுஞ்சுழிப் புகாஅர்த் தெய்வம் நோக்கிக்,","கடுஞ்சூள் தருகுவன், நினக்கே; கானல்","தொடலை ஆயமொடு கடல்உடன் ஆடியும்","சிற்றில் இழைத்தும், சிறுசோறு குவைஇயும்","வருந்திய வருத்தம் தீர யாம் சிறிது","இருந்தன மாக எய்த வந்து","'தடமென் பணைத்தோள் மடநல் லீரோ!","எல்லும் எல்லின்று; அசைவுமிக உடையேன்;","மெல்இலைப் பரப்பின் விருந்துஉண்டு, யானும்இக்","கல்லென் சிறுகுடித் தங்கின்மற்று எவனோ?'","எனமொழிந் தனனே ஒருவன்; அவற்கண்டு,","இறைஞ்சிய முகத்தேம் புறம்சேர்பு பொருந்தி,","'இவைநுமக்கு உரிய அல்ல; இழிந்த","கொழுமீன் வல்சி' என்றனம்; இழுமென","'நெடுங்கொடி நுடங்கும் நாவாய் தோன்றுவ","காணாமோ?' எனக் காலின் சிதையா,","நில்லாது பெயர்ந்த பல்லோர் ருள்ளும்","என்னே குறித்த நோக்கமொடு 'நன்னுதால்!","ஒழிகோ யான்?' என அழிதகக் கூறி,","யான் 'பெயர்க' என்ன நோக்கித் தான்தன்","நெடுந்தேர்க் கொடிஞ்சி பற்றி,","நின்றோன் போலும் இன்றும்என் கட்கே!"],"p":"","r":"Disclosing the secret to the foster mother, the friend says let the mother know and let the gossiping street hear, and swears before the god of the whirling river mouth that there was nothing else: while the girls rested after bathing in the sea, building sand houses and heaping play rice, a man came, said the day was done and he was very tired, and asked whether he might eat with them on spread leaves and stay in their noisy hamlet; they, heads bowed, said the fish food was not fit for him; when the others ran off, crying that a ship with a tall flag was in sight, he looked at her alone and asked, 'Bright-browed one, shall I stay?', and when she said 'go', he stood holding the pole of his tall chariot, gazing — and she says he seems to stand so before her eyes even today.","n":"The friend tells the meeting in her own first person (என்னே குறித்த நோக்கமொடு, என் கட்கே), as if the man had looked at her; whether she speaks for the heroine or shields her by taking the story on herself is left open. The oath before the river-mouth god and the girls' modest refusal (இவைநுமக்கு உரிய அல்ல) are the proofs offered that nothing improper happened.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","alar","vaaimai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 110","lb":"A110","poet":"போந்தைப் பசலையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி செவிலித் தாய்க்கு அறத்தொடு நின்றது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu","செவிலித் தாய்க்கு அறத்தொடு நின்றது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தெய்வம்","தெய்வம்",4,"deyvam","scene",""],["சிறுசோறு","சோறு",7,"unavu","scene","play rice"],["கொழுமீன்","மீன்",17,"unavu","gift_food","fish"],["நாவாய்","நாவாய்",18,"pira","scene","ship"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:6"},"a:111":{"book":"akananuru","num":112,"t":["உள் ஆங்கு உவத்தல் செல்லார், கறுத்தோர்","எள்ளல் நெஞ்சத்து ஏஎச்சொல் நாணி","வருவர் - வாழி, தோழி!- அரச","யானை கொண்ட துகிற்கொடி போல,","அலந்தலை ஞெமையத்து வலந்த சிலம்பி","ஓடைக் குன்றத்துக் கோடையொடு துயல்வர","மழைஎன மருண்ட மம்மர் பலஉடன்","ஓய்களிறு எடுத்த நோயுடை நெடுங்கை","தொகுசொற் கோடியர் தூம்பின் உயிர்க்கும்","அத்தக் கேழல் அட்ட நற்கோள்","செந்நாய் ஏற்றைக் கம்மென ஈர்ப்பக்,","குருதி ஆரும் எருவைச் செஞ்செவி,","மண்டுஅமர் அழுவத்து எல்லிக் கொண்ட","புண்தேர் விளக்கின், தோன்றும்","விண்தோய் பிறங்கல் மலைஇறந் தோரோ!"],"p":"","r":"The friend assures the heroine that the man who crossed the sky-touching hills will not stay content where he is and, ashamed of the scornful words of hostile men, will come back; she pictures his road, where a spider's web on a withered nemai tree swings in the hot wind like the cloth banner on a royal elephant, weary elephants mistake it for a rain-cloud and breathe through their aching trunks like the pipes of performers, and vultures feeding on a boar the red dog has killed show red ears like the lamps carried at night over a battlefield to search out the wounded.","n":"The comfort rests on shame, not longing: he will return because staying away invites scorn. The thirsty elephants deceived by a cobweb into hoping for rain sit uneasily beside a promise of return. Lines 1 to 3 (whose mockery, and why he would be ashamed) can be read more than one way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 111","lb":"A111","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தோழி தலைமளை யாற்றுவித்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தோழி தலைமளை யாற்றுவித்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","simile",""],["ஞெமையத்து","ஞெமை",5,"maram","scene",""],["சிலம்பி","சிலம்பி",5,"pira","simile","spider"],["ஓய்களிறு","களிறு",8,"maa","simile",""],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",9,"seythi","simile",""],["தூம்பின்","தூம்பு",9,"yaazh","simile","pipe"],["கேழல்","கேழல்",10,"maa","scene","boar"],["செந்நாய்","செந்நாய்",11,"maa","scene",""],["எருவைச்","எருவை",12,"pul","simile","vulture"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:6"},"a:112":{"book":"akananuru","num":113,"t":["கூனல் எண்கின் குறுநடைத் தொழுதி","சிதலை செய்த செந்நிலைப் புற்றின்","மண்புனை நெடுங்கோடு உடைய வாங்கி,","இரைநசைப் பரிக்கும் அரைநாட் கங்குல்","ஈன்றுஅணி வயவுப்பிணப் பசித்தென மறப்புலி","ஒளிறுஏந்து மருப்பின் களிறுஅட்டுக் குழுமும்","பனிஇருஞ் சோலை எமியம் என்னாய்","தீங்குசெய் தனையே, ஈங்குவந் தோயே;","நாள்இடைப் படின், என் தோழி வாழாள்;","தோளிடை முயக்கம் நீயும் வெய்யை;","கழியக் காதலர் ஆயினும், சான்றோர்","பழியொடு வரூஉம் இன்பம் வெஃகார்","வரையின் எவனோ?- வான்தோய் வெற்ப!-","கணக்கலை இகுக்கும் கறிஇவர் சிலம்பின்","மணப்புஅருங் காமம் புணர்ந்தமை அறியார்,","தொன்றுஇயல் மரபின் மன்றல் அயரப்","பெண்கோள் ஒழுக்கம் கண்கொள் நோக்கி,","நொதுமல் விருந்தினம் போல, இவள்","புதுநாண் ஒடுக்கமும் காண்குவம், யாமே!"],"p":"","r":"Meeting the hero who has come at night, the friend tells him he has done wrong to come here, not thinking of them alone, through the cold dark grove where at midnight a hunchbacked bear tears open a termite mound and a tiger whose mate is hungry after giving birth kills a tusked elephant and roars; if a day passes without him her friend will not live, and he too desires her embrace, but the worthy, however deep in love, do not want a pleasure that comes with blame, so why not marry? Then, not knowing of the union already made, they will watch the ancient rite and see her new shyness like strangers come as guests.","n":"The closing joke turns the secret into a performance: at the wedding the friend will pretend, like a stranger, not to know that the union already happened, and enjoy the bride's shyness. The maxim on the worthy (சான்றோர் பழியொடு வரூஉம் இன்பம் வெஃகார்) is the friend's lever.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","pazhi","saandror","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 112","lb":"A112","poet":"நெய்தற் சாய்த்துய்த்த அவூர்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி வந்த தலைமகனை யெதிர்ப்பட்டு நின்று தோழி சொல்லி வரைவு கடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி வந்த தலைமகனை யெதிர்ப்பட்டு நின்று தோழி சொல்லி வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["எண்கின்","எண்கு",1,"maa","scene","bear"],["சிதலை","சிதலை",2,"pira","scene","termite"],["மறப்புலி","புலி",5,"maa","iraicchi","tiger"],["வயவுப்பிணப்","பிணா",5,"maa","iraicchi","tigress"],["களிறுஅட்டுக்","களிறு",6,"maa","scene",""],["கணக்கலை","கலை",14,"maa","scene","stag"],["கறிஇவர்","கறி",14,"maram","scene","pepper vine"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மறப்புலி","confirmed","1/1","even the fierce tiger provides for its mate; the hero, who braves the same dark for stolen meetings, should take her home as his wife and provide for her"]],"ch":"a:6"},"a:113":{"book":"akananuru","num":114,"t":["நன்றுஅல் காலையும் நட்பின் கோடார்,","சென்று வழிப்படூஉம் திரிபுஇல் சூழ்ச்சியிற்,","புன்தலை மடப்பிடி அகவுநர் பெருமகன்","அமர்வீசு வண்மகிழ் அஃதைப் போற்றிக்,","காப்புக் கைந்நிறுத்த பல்வேற் கோசர்","இளங்கள் கமழும் நெய்தல்அம் செறுவின்","வளம்கெழு நன்னாடு அன்னஎன் தோள்மணந்து,","அழுங்கன் மூதூர் அலர்எடுத்து அரற்ற,","நல்காது துறந்த காதலர், 'என்றும்","கல்பொரூஉ மெலியாப் பாடின் நோன்அடியன்","அல்கு வன்சுரைப் பெய்த வல்சியர்","இகந்தன ஆயினும், இடம்பார்த்துப் பகைவர்","ஓம்பினர் உறையும் கூழ்கெழு குறும்பிற்","குவைஇமில் விடைய வேற்றுஆ ஒய்யும்","கனைஇருஞ் சுருணைக் கனிகாழ் நெடுவேல்","விழவுஅயர்ந் தன்ன கொழும்பல் திற்றி","எழாஅப் பாணன் நன்னாட்டு உம்பர்,","நெறிசெல் வம்பலர்க் கொன்ற தெவ்வர்","எறிபடை கழீஇய சேயரிச் சின்னீர்","அறுதுறை அயிர்மணற் படுகரைப் போகிச்,","சேயர்' என்றலின், சிறுமை உற்றஎன்","கையறு நெஞ்சத்து எவ்வம் நீங்க,","அழாஅம் உறைதலும் உரியம் - பராரை","அலங்கல் அம்சினைக் குடம்பை புல்லெனப்","புலம்பெயர் மருங்கிற் புள்எழுந் தாங்கு,","மெய்இவண் ஒழியப் போகி, அவர்","செய்வினை மருங்கிற் செலீஇயர், என் உயிரே!"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that the man who joined her shoulders, as rich as the good land of the Kosar (those of many spears who, never swerving from friendship even in bad times, follow and guard Ahthai, giver of elephants), with its neythal fields fragrant of new toddy, abandoned her without kindness while the old town raised its gossip, and is now said to be far off beyond the land of Paanan, past a shrunken stream where enemies who killed travelers washed their weapons; so that her helpless heart may be eased and she can stay without weeping, she wishes her life would leave her body here and go to where he works, as a bird leaves its nest on a swaying branch when the place grows empty.","n":"The Kosar's loyalty 'even in bad times' frames, by contrast, a man who would not stay kind; the body left here like an empty nest is her image of herself. Lines 10 to 16, on the far land and its people, are hard to construe in our text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir","alar","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 113","lb":"A113","poet":"கல்லாடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",3,"maa","gift_food","she-elephant"],["இளங்கள்","கள்",6,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["நெய்தல்அம்","நெய்தல்",6,"maram","simile",""],["விடைய","விடை",14,"maa","scene","bull"],["திற்றி","திற்றி",16,"unavu","simile","meat"],["குடம்பை","குடம்பை",24,"pira","simile","nest"],["புள்எழுந்","புள்",25,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[["patron","அஃதை",4],["king","பாணன்",17]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:6"},"a:114":{"book":"akananuru","num":115,"t":["கேளாய், எல்ல! தோழி! வேலன்","வெறிஅயர் களத்துச் சிறுபல தாஅய","விரவுவீ உறைத்த ஈர்நறும் புறவின்,","உரவுக்கதிர் மழுங்கிய கல்சேர் ஞாயிறு,","அரவுநுங்கு மதியின், ஐயென மறையும்","சிறுபுன் மாலையும் உள்ளார், அவர்என","நம்புலந்து உறையும் எவ்வம், நீங்க","நூல்அறி வலவ! கடவுமதி, உவக்காண்","நெடுங்கொடி நுடங்கும் வான்தோய் புரிசை,","யாமம் கொள்பவர் நாட்டிய நளிசுடர்","வானக மீனின் விளங்கித் தோன்றும்,","அருங்கடிக் காப்பின், அஞ்சுவரு மூதூர்த்","திருநகர் அடங்கிய மாசுஇல் கற்பின்,","அரிமதர் மழைக்கண், அமைபுரை பணைத்தோள்,","அணங்குசால், அரிவையைக் காண்குவம்-","பொலம்படைக் கலிமாப் பூண்ட தேரே!"],"p":"","r":"The returning hero, imagining his wife telling her friend that he does not remember them even in the small dull evening when the weakened sun sinks behind the hill like the moon swallowed by the snake, bids his charioteer, learned in the craft, drive on to end her sorrow: there the tall flags wave on the sky-touching walls of the old guarded town where the watchmen's lamps shine like stars, and they will see the beautiful woman of faultless chastity, with red-streaked rain-dark eyes and shoulders like bamboo.","n":"Lines 1 to 7 are the wife's complaint as the hero imagines it, addressed to her friend (கேளாய், எல்ல! தோழி!); the poem thus holds both voices, hers at home and his on the road. Evening, which makes her grieve, is the hour he races against.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maalai","karpu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 114","lb":"A114","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி மீளுந் தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றி மீளுந் தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வேலன்","வேலன்",1,"seythi","simile",""],["விரவுவீ","வீ",3,"maram","scene","blossom"],["அரவுநுங்கு","அரவு",5,"pira","simile","snake"],["கலிமாப்","மா",16,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:6"},"a:115":{"book":"akananuru","num":116,"t":["அழியா விழவின், அஞ்சுவரு மூதூர்ப்","பழிஇலர் ஆயினும், பலர்புறங் கூறும்","அம்பல் ஒழுக்கமும் ஆகியர், வெஞ்சொல்","சேரிஅம் பெண்டிர் எள்ளினும் எள்ளுக;","நுண்பூண் எருமை குடநாட் டன்னஎன்","ஆய்நலம் தொலையினும் தொலைக; என்றும்","நோய்இல ராக, நம் காதலர் - வாய்வாள்","எவ்வி வீழ்ந்த செருவில் பாணர்","கைதொழு மரபின்முன் பரித்துஇடூஉப் பழிச்சிய","வள்உயிர் வணர்மருப்பு அன்ன, ஒள்இணர்ச்","சுடர்ப்பூங் கொன்றை ஊழுறு விளைநெற்று","அறைமிசைத் தாஅம் அத்த நீளிடைப்,","பிறைமருள் வான்கோட்டு அண்ணல் யானை,","சினம்மிகு முன்பின், வாமான், அஞ்சி","இனம்கொண்டு ஒளிக்கும் அஞ்சுவரு கவலை","நன்னர் ஆய்கவின் தொலையச் சேய்நாட்டு","நம்நீத்து உறையும் பொருட்பிணிக்","கூடா மையின், நீடி யோரே!"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells the friend who urges patience: let the old town of endless festivals gossip though we are blameless, let the harsh-tongued women of the street mock, let my beauty, like the Kudanadu of Erumai of fine ornaments, waste away — only let my lover be free of illness always, he who, bound by the need for wealth, stays far from us across the long road where the konrai's ripe pods, curved like the yazh necks the bards broke off and laid down in worship at the battle where Evvi of the true sword fell, lie strewn on the rock, and where tusked elephants with tusks like the crescent moon hide their herd in fear of a fierce leaping beast.","n":"Her wish is for his health, not his return; she gives up name, mockery and beauty in three concessive clauses (எள்ளினும் எள்ளுக, தொலையினும் தொலைக). The Evvi simile brings a patron's death and the bards' mourning rite into a love poem, so the very pods on his road recall broken harps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","alar","selvam","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 115","lb":"A115","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வற்புறுக்குந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை வற்புறுக்குந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாணர்","பாணர்",8,"seythi","simile",""],["வணர்மருப்பு","மருப்பு",10,"yaazh","simile","harp neck"],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",11,"maram","simile",""],["யானை","யானை",13,"maa","scene",""],["வாமான்","வாமான்",14,"maa","scene","leaping beast"]],"en":[["king","எருமை",5],["town","குடநாட்",5],["king","எவ்வி",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:6"},"a:116":{"book":"akananuru","num":117,"t":["எரியகைந் தன்ன தாமரை இடைஇடை","அரிந்துகால் குவித்த செந்நெல் வினைஞர்","கட்கொண்டு மறுகும் சாகாடு அளற்றுஉறின்,","ஆய்கடும்பு அடுக்கும் பாய்புனல் ஊர!","பெரிய நாண்இலை மன்ற; பொரிஎனப்","புன்குஅவிழ் அகன்துறைப் பொலிய, ஒள்நுதல்,","நறுமலர்க் காண்வரும் குறும்பல் கூந்தல்","மாழை நோக்கின், காழ்இயன் வனமுலை,","எஃகுடை எழில்நலத்து ஒருத்தியொடு நெருநை","வைகுபுனல் அயர்ந்தனை' என்ப; அதுவே,","பொய்புறம் பொதிந்துயாம் கரப்பவும் கையிகந்து","அலர்ஆ கின்றால் தானே; மலர் தார்,","மையணி யானை, மறப்போர்ச் செழியன்","பொய்யா விழவின் கூடற் பறந்தலை,","உடன்இயைந்து எழுந்த இருபெரு வேந்தர்","கடன்மருள் பெரும்படை கலங்கத் தாக்கி,","இரங்குஇசை முரசம் ஒழியப், பரந்துஅவர்","ஓடுபுறம் கண்ட ஞான்றை,","ஆடுகொள் வியன்களத்து ஆர்ப்பினும் பெரிதே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend refuses the man of the town of rushing water, where field workers reaping red paddy amid flame-red lotuses prop the toddy cart sunk in the mire with adumpu creepers, telling him he has no shame: people say that yesterday he played in the river with a woman of bright brow, short flower-decked hair, soft eyes and full breasts, at the wide ford where punku flowers open like popped rice; and though they hid it behind lies, the gossip slipped from their hands and grew louder than the shout on the wide field of Koodal when Chezhiyan of the elephant army routed the sea-like hosts of two great kings who had risen together and saw them flee, leaving their drums.","n":"The friend admits the women tried to hide his conduct with lies (பொய்புறம் பொதிந்துயாம் கரப்பவும்), so the refusal is also a confession that the household's loyalty has been spent. The cart sunk in mire and propped with creepers is a scene of the marutham fields; a reader may see the hidden affair being shored up in it, but no part-by-part match is anchored.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","alar","oodal","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 116","lb":"A116","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகனை வாயில் மறுத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தோழி தலைமகனை வாயில் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",1,"maram","simile",""],["செந்நெல்","நெல்",2,"unavu","scene",""],["வினைஞர்","வினைஞர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["கட்கொண்டு","கள்",3,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["ஆய்கடும்பு","அடும்பு",4,"maram","scene",""],["புன்குஅவிழ்","புன்கு",6,"maram","simile",""],["யானை","யானை",13,"maa","scene",""],["முரசம்","முரசம்",17,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["king","செழியன்",13],["battle","கூடற் பறந்தலை",14]],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:6"},"a:117":{"book":"akananuru","num":118,"t":["மௌவலொடு மலர்ந்த மாக்குரல் நொச்சியும்,","அவ்வரி அல்குல் ஆயமும் உள்ளாள்,","ஏதிலன் பொய்ம்மொழி நம்பி, ஏர்வினை","வளம்கெழு திருநகர் புலம்பப் போகி,","வெருவரு கவலை ஆங்கண், அருள்வரக்,","கருங்கால் ஓமை ஏறி, வெண்தலைப்","பருந்து பெடைபயிரும் பாழ்நாட்டு ஆங்கண்,","பொலந்தொடி தெளிர்ப்ப வீசிச், சேவடிச்","சிலம்புநக இயலிச் சென்றஎன் மகட்கே-","சாந்துஉளர் வணர்குரல் வாரி, வகைவகுத்து","யான்போது துணைப்பத், தகரம் மண்ணாள்,","தன்ஓ ரன்ன தகைவெங் காதலன்","வெறிகமழ் பன்மலர் புனையப் பின்னுவிடச்","சிறுபுறம் புதைய நெறிபுதாழ்ந் தனகொல்-","நெடுங்கால் மாஅத்து ஊழுறு வெண்பழம்","கொடுந்தாள் யாமை பார்ப்பொடு கவரும்","பொய்கை சூழ்ந்த, பொய்யா யாணர்,","வாணன் சிறுகுடி வடாஅது","தீம்நீர்க் கான்யாற்று அவிர்அறல் போன்றே?"],"p":"","r":"The foster mother grieves for the girl who, trusting a stranger's false words and forgetting the nochchi that flowers with jasmine and her playmates, left the rich house desolate and went, bangles jingling and anklets ringing on her red feet, through a waste of fearful forked paths where a white-headed kite on a black-stemmed omai calls its mate; she asks whether, since the girl would not let her wash her hair with tagaram when she combed and parted it, that hair, now that her lover as fine as she weaves fragrant flowers into it, falls over her back like the bright ripples of the sweet forest river north of Vaanan's Sirukudi, where a crooked-legged tortoise and its young snatch ripe white mangoes in the encircling pond.","n":"The foster mother's grief is jealous of a task: she combed and dressed the hair the girl would not let her wash, and now pictures the lover doing it. The kite calling its mate on the waste, 'so that pity comes' (அருள்வர), is the pair the girl has become.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["udanpokku","ninaivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 117","lb":"A117","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய செவிலித்தாய் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,null,"மகட் போக்கிய செவிலித்தாய் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மௌவலொடு","மௌவல்",1,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["நொச்சியும்","நொச்சி",1,"maram","scene",""],["ஓமை","ஓமை",6,"maram","scene",""],["பருந்து","பருந்து",7,"pul","scene","kite"],["தகரம்","தகரம்",11,"maram","ornament","hair paste"],["பன்மலர்","மலர்",13,"maram","ornament",""],["மாஅத்து","மா",15,"maram","scene","mango"],["யாமை","யாமை",16,"pira","scene","tortoise"],["அவிர்அறல்","அறல்",19,"pira","simile","ripple"]],"en":[["king","வாணன்",18],["town","சிறுகுடி",18]],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:6"},"a:118":{"book":"akananuru","num":119,"t":["கறங்குவெள் அருவி பிறங்குமலைக் கவாஅன்,","தேங்கமழ் இணர வேங்கை சூடித்,","தொண்டகப் பறைச்சீர்ப் பெண்டிரொடு விரைஇ,","மறுகில் தூங்கும் சிறுகுடிப் பாக்கத்து,","இயல்முருகு ஒப்பினை, வயநாய் பிற்பட","பகல்வரின் கவ்வை அஞ்சுதும்; இகல்கொள,","இரும்பிடி கன்றொடு விரைஇய கயவாய்ப்,","பெருங்கை யானைக் கோள்பிழைத்து, இரீஇய","அடுபுலி வழங்கும் ஆர்இருள் நடுநாள்","தனியை வருதல் அதனினும் அஞ்சுதும்;","என்ஆ குவள்கொல் தானே? பல்நாள்","புணர்குறி செய்த புலர்குரல் ஏனல்","கிளிகடி பாடலும் ஒழிந்தனள்;","அளியள் தான்,நின் அளி அலது இலளே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hero, who is handsome as Murugan himself, that if he comes by day, his strong hunting dogs behind him, to the small hill village by the roaring white waterfall where women wearing honey-scented vengai flowers dance in the street to the tondakam drum, they fear the gossip, and they fear still more his coming alone at pitch-dark midnight when the tiger that missed the great elephant hurrying with its mate and calf lies in wait; what will become of her friend, who has even stopped singing the parrot-scaring songs in the ripening millet where they met for many days — pitiable, she has nothing but his grace.","n":"Both trysts are closed in turn, day by gossip and night by the tiger, and the millet field is closed too, since she no longer guards it; only marriage is left, though the friend never names it. The elephant that takes its mate and calf out of the tiger's reach is the path's danger here; the poem does not turn it on the hero.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","alar","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 118","lb":"A118","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"செறிப்பறிவுறீஇ இரவும் பகலும் வாரலென்று வரைவு","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","செறிப்பறிவுறீஇ இரவும் பகலும் வாரலென்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",2,"maram","ornament",""],["தொண்டகப்","தொண்டகம்",3,"parai","scene",""],["இயல்முருகு","முருகு",5,"deyvam","simile",""],["வயநாய்","நாய்",5,"maa","scene",""],["இரும்பிடி","பிடி",7,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["யானைக்","யானை",8,"maa","scene",""],["அடுபுலி","புலி",9,"maa","scene",""],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",12,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கிளிகடி","கிளி",13,"pul","scene","parrot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:6"},"a:119":{"book":"akananuru","num":120,"t":["'நுதலும், தோளும், திதலை அல்குலும்,","வண்ணமும், வனப்பும், வரியும் வாட","வருந்துவள், இவள்' எனத் திருந்துபு நோக்கி,","'வரைவுநன்று' என்னாது அகலினும், அவர் - வறிது,","ஆறுசெல் மாக்கள் அறுத்த பிரண்டை,","ஏறுபெறு பாம்பின் பைந்துணி கடுப்ப,","நெறியயல் திரங்கும் அத்தம்; வெறிகொள,","உமண்சாத்து இறந்த ஒழிகல் அடுப்பில்,","நோன்சிலை மழவர் ஊன்புழுக்கு அயரும்","சுரன்வழக்கு அற்றது என்னாது, உரஞ்சிறந்து,","நெய்தல் உருவின் ஐதுஇலங்கு அகல்இலைத்,","தொடைஅமை பீலிப் பொலிந்த கடிகை,","மடைஅமை திண்சுரை, மரக்காழ் வேலொடு","தணிஅமர் அழுவம் தம்மொடு துணைப்பத்,","துணிகுவர் கொல்லோ தாமே - துணிகொள","மறப்புலி உழந்த வசிபடு சென்னி","உறுநோய் வருத்தமொடு உணீஇய மண்டிப்","படிமுழம் ஊன்றிய நெடுநல் யானை","கைதோய்த்து உயிர்க்கும் வறுஞ்சுனை,","மைதோய் சிமைய, மலைமுதல் ஆறே!"],"p":"","r":"Told that he will leave, the heroine (or, in the colophon's second reading, the friend) asks whether the man who looked closely and said 'she will suffer, her brow, shoulders and spotted waist losing colour, beauty and lines', and still goes without thinking marriage good, will truly venture, with a peacock-feathered spear broad-bladed like a neythal petal as his only companion, along a mountain road of dry tracks where pirandai vines cut by travelers wither by the path like the green pieces of a snake struck by thunder, where bow-strong Mazhavar roast meat on the abandoned hearth stones of salt caravans, and where a great elephant whose head the tiger gashed dips its trunk in a dry spring and sighs.","n":"He foresaw her wasting in detail and still did not choose marriage (வரைவுநன்று என்னாது): the complaint is that his knowledge did not change his act. The wounded elephant at the dry spring and the spear as his only companion make the road a place where even strength is alone and thirsty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","varaivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 119","lb":"A119","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவுணர்த்திய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொற்றது. தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொற்றதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"செலவுணர்த்திய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொற்றது"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொற்றதூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["பிரண்டை","பிரண்டை",5,"maram","simile",""],["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",6,"pira","simile","snake"],["ஏறுபெறு","ஏறு",6,"pira","simile","thunder"],["உமண்சாத்து","உமணர்",8,"seythi","scene","salt traders"],["மழவர்","மழவர்",9,"seythi","scene",""],["ஊன்புழுக்கு","ஊன்",9,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",11,"maram","simile",""],["பீலிப்","பீலி",12,"pul","ornament","peacock feather"],["மறப்புலி","புலி",16,"maa","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",18,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:6"},"a:120":{"book":"akananuru","num":121,"t":["நெடுவேள் மார்பின் ஆரம் போலச்,","செவ்வாய் வானம் தீண்டி மீன் அருந்தும்","பைங்காற் கொக்கினம் நிரைபறை உகப்ப,","எல்லை பைப்பய கழிப்பிக், குடவயின்","கல்சேர்ந் தன்றே, பல்கதிர் ஞாயிறு","மதர்எழில் மழைக்கண் கலுழ, இவளே","பெருநாண் அணிந்த நறுமென் சாயல்","மாண்நலம் சிதைய ஏங்கி, ஆனாது","அழல்தொடங் கினளே - பெரும;- அதனால்","கழிச்சுறா எறிந்த புண்தாள் அத்திரி","நெடுநீர் இருங்கழி பரிமெலிந்து அசைஇ,","வல்வில் இளையரொடு எல்லிச் செல்லாது","சேர்ந்தனை செலினே சிதைகுவது உண்டோ -","பெண்ணை ஓங்கிய வெண்மணற் படப்பை","அன்றில் அகவும் ஆங்கண்","சிறுகுரல் நெய்தல்எம் பெருங்கழி நாட்டே!"],"p":"","r":"Having brought the heroine home from the daytime meeting, the friend tells the hero that the sun has sunk in the west as green-legged herons, flying in a line like the garland on tall Murugan's chest, cross the red sky after fish, and her friend, her proud rain-dark eyes in tears and her fragrant beauty spoiled, has begun to burn with longing; so would anything be lost if, rather than setting out at night with his bowmen and his mule, its leg wounded by a backwater shark and worn out by the long waters, he stayed and then went from their country of the great backwater, where palmyras rise in white-sand gardens, the anril calls and small-budded neythal grows?","n":"The invitation is phrased as a harmless question (சிதைகுவது உண்டோ): staying one night would cost him nothing, while her weeping has already begun. The anril, the bird of pairs that cannot part, calls in the last lines; the poem leaves it as the sound of the place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kalaviyal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 120","lb":"A120","poet":"நக்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி பகற்குறிக்கண் தலைமகளை இடத்துய்த்து வந்து","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி பகற்குறிக்கண் தலைமகளை இடத்துய்த்து வந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நெடுவேள்","வேள்",1,"deyvam","simile","Murugan"],["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",1,"maram","simile","garland"],["மீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","scene","fish"],["கொக்கினம்","கொக்கு",3,"pul","simile","heron"],["கழிச்சுறா","சுறா",10,"pira","scene","shark"],["அத்திரி","அத்திரி",10,"maa","scene","mule"],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",14,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["அன்றில்","அன்றில்",15,"pul","scene",""],["நெய்தல்எம்","நெய்தல்",16,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:6"},"a:121":{"book":"akananuru","num":122,"t":["நாம்நகை யுடையம் நெஞ்சே!- கடுந்தெறல்","வேனில் நீடிய வானுயர் வழிநாள்,","வறுமை கூரிய மண்நீர்ச் சிறுகுளத்","தொடுகுழி மருங்கில் துவ்வாக் கலங்கல்","கன்றுடை மடப்பிடிக் கயந்தலை மண்ணிச்","சேறுகொண் டாடிய வேறுபடு வயக்களிறு","செங்கோல் வாலிணர் தயங்கத் தீண்டிச்","சொறிபுறம் உரிஞிய நெறியியல் மராஅத்து","அல்குறு வரிநிழல் அசைஇ, நம்மொடு","தான்வரும் என்ப, தடமென் தோளி","உறுகண மழவர் உருள்கீண் டிட்ட","ஆறுசெல் மாக்கள் சோறுபொதி வெண்குடைக்","கணைவிசைக் கடுவளி எடுத்தலின், துணைசெத்து","வெருளேறு பயிரும் ஆங்கண்,","கருமுக முசுவின் கானத் தானே"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart they have cause for joy: the girl of broad soft shoulders, they say, will come with him into the summer forest of black-faced langurs, where a bull elephant washes his young mate's head with the muddy water of a dug pit in a shrunken pond and rests in the striped shade of a kadamba, and where a startled beast calls to a traveller's white rice-bundle blown by the wind, taking it for its mate.","n":"The road is shown at its worst (the shrunken pond, the travellers killed by Mazhavar raiders, their food parcels blowing loose), so the joy of the first line is the measure of what she is willing to cross with him. The beast that mistakes a dead man's rice-bundle for its mate turns the image of companionship into one of loss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","anbu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 121","lb":"A121","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழியால் தலைமகளை உடன்வருமெனக் கேட்ட தலைமகன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","தோழியால் தலைமகளை உடன்வருமெனக் கேட்ட தலைமகன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மடப்பிடிக்","பிடி",5,"maa","iraicchi",""],["வயக்களிறு","களிறு",6,"maa","iraicchi",""],["மராஅத்து","மரா",8,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["மழவர்","மழவர்",11,"seythi","scene",""],["சோறுபொதி","சோறு",12,"unavu","scene",""],["வெருளேறு","ஏறு",14,"maa","scene","male beast"],["முசுவின்","முசு",15,"maa","scene","langur"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வயக்களிறு","confirmed","1/1","as the tusker tends his mate on the parched road, so the man will tend the girl who crosses it with him"]],"ch":"a:7"},"a:122":{"book":"akananuru","num":123,"t":["இரும்பிழி மகாஅரிவ் அழுங்கல் மூதூர்","விழவின் றாயினும் துஞ்சா தாகும்:","மல்லல் ஆவணம் மறுகுடன் மடியின்,","வல்லுரைக் கடுஞ்சொல் அன்னை துஞ்சாள்;","பிணிகோள் அருஞ்சிறை அன்னை துஞ்சின்","துஞ்சாக் கண்ணர் காவலர் கடுகுவர்;","இலங்குவேல் இளையர் துஞ்சின், வைஎயிற்று","வலஞ்சுரித் தோகை ஞாளி மகிழும்;","அரவவாய் ஞமலி மகிழாது மடியின்,","பகலுரு உறழ நிலவுக்கான்று விசும்பின்","அகல்வாய் மண்டிலம் நின்றுவிரி யும்மே;","திங்கள் கல்சேர்வு கனைஇருள் மடியின்,","இல்எலி வல்சி வல்வாய்க் கூகை","கழுதுவழங்கு யாமத்து அழிதகக் குழறும்;","வளைக்கண் சேவல் வாளாது மடியின்,","மனைச்செறி கோழி மாண்குரல் இயம்பும்;","எல்லாம் மடிந்த காலை, ஒருநாள்","நில்லா நெஞ்சத்து அவர்வா ரலரே; அதனால்","அரிபெய் புட்டில் ஆர்ப்பப் பரிசிறந்து,","ஆதி போகிய பாய்பரி நன்மா","நொச்சி வேலித் தித்தன் உறந்தைக்","கல்முதிர் புறங்காட் டன்ன","பல்முட் டின்றால் - தோழி!- நம் களவே"],"p":"","r":"With her lover listening outside, the girl tells her friend that their secret love has as many hindrances as the old stony outskirts of Thithan's Uranthai: the noisy town never sleeps, and when it does the mother does not, then the watchmen, the young spearmen, the dog, the bright moon, the owl at the ghost-hour and the cock each keep watch in turn, and on the one night when all are asleep he does not come.","n":"The poem is a chain: each sleeper hands the watch to the next, and the last link is the lover's own absence, so the complaint aimed at the overhearing man is that he is the final obstacle. The listing presses him toward marriage without saying so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 122","lb":"A122","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தலைமகள் சொற்றது. தோழி சொல்லெடுப்பத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தலைமகள் சொற்றது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தோழி சொல்லெடுப்பத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["காவலர்","காவலர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["ஞாளி","ஞாளி",8,"maa","scene","dog"],["ஞமலி","ஞமலி",9,"maa","scene","dog"],["மண்டிலம்","மண்டிலம்",11,"pira","scene","moon"],["எலி","எலி",13,"maa","scene",""],["கூகை","கூகை",13,"pul","scene","owl"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",15,"pul","scene",""],["கோழி","கோழி",16,"pul","scene",""],["நன்மா","மா",20,"maa","scene","horse"],["நொச்சி","நொச்சி",21,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","தித்தன்",21],["town","உறந்தை",21]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:7"},"a:123":{"book":"akananuru","num":124,"t":["உண்ணா மையின் உயங்கிய மருங்கின்","ஆடாப் படிவத் தான்றோர் போல,","வரைசெறி சிறுநெறி நிரைபுடன் செல்லும்","கான யானை கவினழி குன்றம்","இறந்து, பொருள் தருதலும் ஆற்றாய்: சிறந்த","சில்லைங் கூந்தல் நல்லகம் பொருந்தி","ஒழியின், வறுமை அஞ்சுதி; அழிதகவு","உடைமதி-வாழிய, நெஞ்சே!-நிலவு என","நெய்கனி நெடுவேல் எஃகிலை இமைக்கும்","மழைமருள் பல்தோல் மாவண் சோழர்","கழைமாய் காவிரிக் கடல்மண்டு பெருந்துறை,","இறவொடு வந்து கோதையொடு பெயரும்","பெருங்கடல் ஓதம் போல,","ஒன்றிற் கொள்ளாய், சென்றுதரு பொருட்கே"],"p":"","r":"In the middle of the desert the man rebukes his heart: you cannot cross the hills where elephants, gaunt like fasting ascetics, file along narrow paths and bring back wealth, yet if you stay embracing her you fear poverty; like the tide at the Chola's Kaveri mouth that comes in with prawns and goes out with garlands, you hold to neither, so be ruined.","n":"The tide image is exact: the heart neither goes nor stays but swings between the two each day. The elephants likened to ascetics who neither eat nor bathe make the road itself a place of renunciation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","selvam","nenju","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 123","lb":"A123","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம் பட்டினத்துக் காரிக்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் இடைச்சுரத்துத் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","தலைமகன் இடைச்சுரத்துத் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","simile",""],["இறவொடு","இறவு",12,"pira","scene","prawn"],["கோதையொடு","கோதை",12,"maram","scene","garland"],["ஓதம்","ஓதம்",13,"pira","simile","tide"]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",10],["river","காவிரி",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:7"},"a:124":{"book":"akananuru","num":125,"t":["'நன்கலம் களிற்றொடு நண்ணார் ஏந்தி,","வந்துதிறை கொடுத்து, வணங்கினர், வழிமொழிந்து","'சென்றீக' என்ப ஆயின், வேந்தனும்","நிலம் வகுந்துறாஅ ஈண்டிய தானையொடு","இன்றே புகுதல் வாய்வது; நன்றே,","மாட மாண்நகர்ப் பாடமை சேக்கைத்","துனிதீர் கொள்கைநம் காதலி இனிதுறப்,","பாசறை வருத்தம் வீட, நீயும்-","மின்னுநிமிர்ந் தன்ன பொன்னியற் புனைபடைக்,","கொய்சுவல் புரவிக், கைகவர் வயங்குபரி","வண்பெயற்கு அவிழ்ந்த பைங்கொடி முல்லை","வீகமழ் நெடுவழி ஊதுவண் டிரிய,","காலை எய்தக், கடவு மதி - மாலை","அந்திக் காவலர் அம்பணை இமிழ்இசை","அரமிய வியலகத்து இயம்பும்","நிரைநிலை ஞாயில் நெடுமதில் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"The man, his campaign ending, tells the charioteer that if the enemy bring tribute and elephants and bow and ask the king to go, the king's army will enter today; so, to end the camp's hardship and gladden their beloved in the mansion, he must drive the swift horses with harness like lightning down the long road fragrant with rain-opened mullai, scattering the bees, and reach by morning the walled town where the evening drum sounds.","n":"The man's homecoming waits on the enemy's surrender: the puram event decides the akam one, and he plans the drive before the tribute has even come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","pirivu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 124","lb":"A124","poet":"மதுரை அறுவை வாணிகன் இளவேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்கு உரைத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்கு உரைத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["களிற்றொடு","களிறு",1,"maa","gift_food",""],["புரவி","புரவி",10,"maa","scene","horse"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",11,"maram","scene",""],["ஊதுவண்","வண்டு",12,"pira","scene","bee"],["அம்பணை","பணை",14,"parai","scene","drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:7"},"a:125":{"book":"akananuru","num":126,"t":["அரம்போழ் அவ்வளை தோள்நிலை நெகிழ,","நிரம்பா வாழ்க்கை நேர்தல் வேண்டி-","ஈர்ங்காழ் அன்ன அரும்புமுதிர் ஈங்கை","ஆலி யன்ன வால்வீ தாஅய்","வைவால் ஓதி மையணல் ஏய்ப்பத்","தாதுஉறு குவளைப் போதுபணி அவிழப்,","படாஅப் பைங்கண் பாவடிக் கயவாய்க்","கடாஅம் மாறிய யானை போலப்,","பெய்துவறிது ஆகிய பொங்குசெலற் கொண்மூ","மைதோய் விசும்பின் மாதிரத்து உழிதரப்,","பனிஅடூஉ நின்ற பானாட் கங்குல்","தனியோர் மதுகை தூக்காய், தண்ணென,","முனிய அலைத்தி, முரண்இல் காலை;","கைதொழு மரபின் கடவுள் சான்ற","செய்வினை மருங்கிற் சென்றோர் வல்வரின்","விரியுளைப் பொலிந்த பரியுடை நன்மான்","வெருவரு தானையொடு வேண்டுபுலத்து இறுத்த","பெருவளக் கரிகால் முன்னிலைச் செல்லார்,","சூடா வாகைப் பறந்தலை, ஆடுபெற","ஒன்பது குடையும் நன்பகல் ஒழித்த","பீடில் மன்னர் போல,","ஓடுவை மன்னால் - வாடை!- நீ எமக்கே"],"p":"","r":"Addressing the north wind, the speaker says that while he is away for wealth, and the rain-clouds that have emptied themselves roam the sky like an elephant past its must, the wind torments the lonely in the cold midnight; but if the man gone on his worthy work comes back soon, the wind will flee like the nine kings without honour who left their nine umbrellas at midday on the Vaakai field before great Karikal.","n":"The colophon gives the friend speaking to the heroine on hearing he has returned, but the poem itself is spoken to the wind in the first person plural (எமக்கே), and the return is still conditional (வல்வரின்). The defeated kings are made a measure of disgrace: the wind will run as they ran.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","iravu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 125","lb":"A125","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"2தலைமகன் வினைமுற்றி மீண்டமை யுணர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகட்கச் சொல்லியது.","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தலைமகன் வினைமுற்றி மீண்டமை யுணர்ந்த தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஈங்கை","ஈங்கை",3,"maram","scene",""],["குவளைப்","குவளை",6,"maram","scene",""],["ஓதி","ஓதி",5,"pira","simile","chameleon"],["யானை","யானை",8,"maa","simile",""],["கொண்மூ","கொண்மூ",9,"pira","scene","cloud"],["நன்மான்","மா",16,"maa","scene","horse"],["வாடை","வாடை",22,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[["king","கரிகால்",18],["battle","வாகைப் பறந்தலை",19]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:7"},"a:126":{"book":"akananuru","num":127,"t":["நினவாய் செத்து நீபல உள்ளிப்,","பெரும்புன் பைதலை வருந்தல் அன்றியும்,","மலைமிசைத் தொடுத்த மலிந்துசெலல் நீத்தம்","தலைநாள் மாமலர் தண்துறைத் தயங்கக்","கடற்கரை மெலிக்குங் காவிரிப் பேரியாற்று","அறல்வார் நெடுங்கயத்து அருநிலை கலங்க,","மாலிருள் நடுநாட் போகித் தன்னையர்","காலைத் தந்த கணைக்கோட்டு வாளைக்கு,","அவ்வாங்கு உந்தி, அஞ்சொல் பாண்மகள்,","நெடுங்கொடி நுடங்கு நறவுமலி மறுகில்","பழஞ்செந் நெல்லின் முகவை கொள்ளாள்,","கழங்குறழ் முத்தமொடு நன்கலம் பெறூஉம்","பயங்கெழு வைப்பிற் பல்வேல் எவ்வி","நயம்புரி நன்மொழி அடக்கவும் அடங்கான்,","பொன்னிணர் நறுமலர்ப் புன்னை வெஃகித்,","திதியனொடு பொருத அன்னி போல","விளிகுவை கொல்லோ, நீயே - கிளியெனச்","சிறிய மிழற்றுஞ் செவ்வாய்ப், பெரிய","கயலென அமர்த்த உண்கண், புயலெனப்","புறந்தாழ்பு இருளிய பிறங்குகுரல் ஐம்பால்:","மின்னோர் மருங்குல், குறுமகள்","பின்னிலை விடாஅ மடங்கெழு நெஞ்சே!"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart, which will not stop following the young girl with a parrot's prattle, kayal eyes, cloud-dark hair and lightning waist, that besides its own long sorrow it may perish like Anni, who would not be held back by the kind counsel of Evvi, lord of a rich Kaveri land where a bard's daughter refuses old paddy for the vaalai fish her brothers caught by night and takes pearls instead, and who fought Thithiyan for his punnai tree.","n":"The Anni story is a warning of ruin from obstinate desire, and Evvi's rejected counsel stands for the advice the heart will not take. Nothing in the poem fixes whether the girl is a wife in sulking (alternative 1 of the colophon) or a girl not yet won (alternatives 2 and 3).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nenju","oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 126","lb":"A126","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"(1) உணர்ப்புவயின் வாரா ஊடற்கண் தலைமகன் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது. (2) அல்ல குறிப்பட் டழிந்ததுமாம். (3) தோழியைப் பின்னின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியதுமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","உணர்ப்புவயின் வாரா ஊடற்கண் தலைமகன் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","அல்ல குறிப்பட் டழிந்ததுமாம்"],["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","தோழியைப் பின்னின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியதுமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மாமலர்","மலர்",4,"maram","scene",""],["வாளைக்கு","வாளை",8,"pira","gift_food",""],["நறவுமலி","நறவு",10,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",11,"unavu","gift_food",""],["முத்தமொடு","முத்தம்",12,"pira","gift_food",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",15,"maram","scene",""],["கிளியென","கிளி",17,"pul","simile",""],["கயலென","கயல்",19,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",5],["king","எவ்வி",13],["king","திதியனொடு",16],["king","அன்னி",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:7"},"a:127":{"book":"akananuru","num":128,"t":["இலங்குவளை நெகிழச் சாஅய், அல்கலும்,","கலங்கஞர் உழந்து, நாம்இவண் ஒழிய","வலம்படு முரசின் சேர லாதன்","முந்நீர் ஓட்டிக் கடம்புஅறுத்து, இமயத்து","முன்னோர் மருள வணங்குவிற் பொறித்து,","நன்னகர் மாந்தை முற்றத்து ஒன்னார்","பணிதிறை தந்த பாடுசால் நன்கலம்","பொன்செய் பாவை வயிரமொடு ஆம்பல்","ஒன்றுவாய் நிறையக் குவைஇ, அன்றவண்","நிலம்தினத் துறந்த நிதியத்து அன்ன,","ஒருநாள் ஒருபகற் பெறினும், வழிநாள்","தங்கலர் - வாழி, தோழி!- செங்கோற்","கருங்கால் மராஅத்து வாஅல் மெல்இணர்ச்","சுரிந்துவணர் பித்தை பொலியச் சூடிக்,","கல்லா மழவர் வில்லிடம் தழீஇ,","வருநர்ப் பார்க்கும் வெருவரு கவலை","மொழிபெயர் தேஎத்தர் ஆயினும்,","பழிதீர் காதலர் சென்ற நாட்டே"],"p":"","r":"The friend comforts the wasting girl: even if in a single day he could win treasure like the hoard of tribute that Cheralathan, who drove off the sea-foe, cut the kadamba and carved his bow on the Himalaya, heaped at Maanthai and left for the earth to eat, he would not stay a day longer in that land of strange speech where bowmen crowned with kadamba wait at the forks of the road.","n":"The argument runs by scale: the largest wealth the hearers know, a king's buried tribute, would not hold him one extra day, so he will surely come. The kadamba appears twice, once as the enemy's guardian tree that Cheralathan felled and once as the wild bowmen's crown.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 127","lb":"A127","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முரசின்","முரசு",3,"parai","scene",""],["கடம்புஅறுத்து","கடம்பு",4,"maram","scene",""],["மராஅத்து","மரா",13,"maram","ornament","kadamba"],["மழவர்","மழவர்",15,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[["king","சேர லாதன்",3],["hill","இமயத்து",4],["town","மாந்தை",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:7"},"a:128":{"book":"akananuru","num":129,"t":["மன்றுபா டவிந்து மனைமடிந் தன்றே;","கொன்றோ ரன்ன கொடுமையோ டின்றே","யாமம் கொளவரின் கனைஇக், காமங்","கடலிலும் உரைஇக், கரைபொழி யும்மே","எவன்கொல்- வாழி, தோழி!- மயங்கி","இன்னம் ஆகவும், நன்னர் நெஞ்சம்","என்னொடும் நின்னொடும் சூழாது, கைம்மிக்கு,","இறும்புபட்டு இருளிய இட்டருஞ் சிலம்பிற்","குறுஞ்சுனைக் குவளை வண்டுபடச் சூடிக்,","கான நாடன் வரூஉம், யானைக்","கயிற்றுப்புறத் தன்ன, கன்மிசைச் சிறுநெறி,","மாரி வானந் தலைஇ நீர்வார்பு,","இட்டருங் கண்ண படுகுழி இயவின்,","இருளிடை மிதிப்புழி நோக்கி, அவர்","தளர்அடி தாங்கிய சென்றது, இன்றே?"],"p":"","r":"With her lover outside, the girl tells her friend that the village has fallen silent and midnight comes on cruel as a killer, and her love overflows its banks like a sea; why then does her heart, without consulting either of them, go out tonight to the narrow path over the rocks, narrow as the rope on an elephant's back, slippery with rain and full of hidden pits, to watch his steps and hold up his faltering feet as he comes wearing kuvalai from the mountain spring?","n":"The fear is turned around: the girl is anxious not for herself but for him on the night path, and since he is listening, the danger she describes is the argument for ending the night meetings by marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 128","lb":"A128","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி வந்ததலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தலைமகள்தோழிக்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறி வந்ததலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["குறுஞ்சுனைக்","சுனை",9,"pira","scene",""],["குவளை","குவளை",9,"maram","ornament",""],["வண்டுபட","வண்டு",9,"pira","scene","bee"],["யானைக்","யானை",10,"maa","simile",""],["மாரி","மாரி",12,"pira","scene","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:7"},"a:129":{"book":"akananuru","num":130,"t":["'உள்ளல் வேண்டும் ஒழிந்த பின்' என","நள்ளென் கங்குல் நடுங்குதுணை யாயவர்","நின்மறந்து உறைதல் யாவது? 'புல் மறைந்து","அலங்கல் வான்கழை உதிர்நெல் நோக்கிக்'","கலைபிணை விளிக்கும் கானத்து ஆங்கண்,","கல்சேர்பு இருந்த கதுவாய்க் குரம்பைத்","தாழிமுதற் கலித்த கோழிலைப் பருத்திப்","பொதிவயிற்று இளங்காய் பேடை ஊட்டிப்,","போகில்பிளந் திட்ட பொங்கல் வெண்காழ்","நல்கூர் பெண்டிர் அல்குற் கூட்டும்","கலங்குமுனைச் சீறூர் கைதலை வைப்பக்,","கொழுப்புஆ தின்ற கூர்ம்படை மழவர்,","செருப்புடை அடியர், தெண்சுனை மண்டும்","அருஞ்சுரம் அரிய வல்ல; வார்கோல்","திருந்திழைப் பணைத்தோள் தேன்நாறு கதுப்பின்","குவளை உண்கண், இவளொடு செலற்கு 'என","நெஞ்சுவாய் அவிழ்ந்தனர் காதலர்-","அஞ்சில் ஓதி ஆயிழை!- நமக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the man who shared her trembling nights and said one must keep her in mind after parting cannot forget her, for, his heart opening, he said that the hard desert, where a stag calls its doe to rice fallen from the bamboo, where in a ruined border hamlet a bird feeds its mate young cotton bolls and poor women gather the burst seed, and where raiders who ate fat cattle crowd the clear spring, would not be hard to cross with her.","n":"Two creatures feed their mates in his description of the road (the stag calling its doe to food, the bird feeding its mate), and the friend sets them before the girl as his own words. The quotation marks of the base text are unclear at lines 1 and 3, so which words are the man's and which the friend's is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 129","lb":"A129","poet":"குடவாயிற்கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்டதலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்டதலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கழை","கழை",4,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["உதிர்நெல்","நெல்",4,"unavu","scene","bamboo rice"],["கலைபிணை","கலை பிணை",5,"maa","scene","stag and doe"],["பருத்திப்","பருத்தி",7,"maram","scene",""],["பேடை","பேடை",8,"pul","iraicchi",""],["போகில்பிளந்","போகில்",9,"pul","iraicchi",""],["கொழுப்புஆ","ஆ",12,"maa","gift_food","cow"],["மழவர்","மழவர்",12,"seythi","scene",""],["தெண்சுனை","சுனை",13,"pira","scene",""],["குவளை","குவளை",16,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","போகில்பிளந்","confirmed","1/1","the man who noticed a bird feeding its mate on that road will not forget to care for the girl"]],"ch":"a:7"},"a:130":{"book":"akananuru","num":131,"t":["அம்ம வாழி, கேளிர்! முன்நின்று","கண்டனிர் ஆயின், கழறலிர் மன்னோ-","நுண்தாது பொதிந்த செங்காற் கொழுமுகை","முண்டகம் கெழீஇய மோட்டுமணல் அடைகரைப்,","பேஎய்த் தலைய பிணர் அரைத் தாழை","எயிறுடை நெடுந்தோடு காப்பப், பலவுடன்","வயிறுடைப் போது வாலிதின் விரீஇப்,","புலவுப்பொருது அழித்த பூநாறு பரப்பின்","இவர்திரை தந்த ஈர்ங்கதிர் முத்தம்","கவர்நடைப் புரவிக் கால்வடுத் தபுக்கும்","நற்றேர் வழுதி கொற்கை முன்துறை","வண்டுவாய் திறந்த வாங்குகழி நெய்தற்","போதுபுறங் கொடுத்த உண்கண்","மாதர் வாண்முகம் மதைஇய நோக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The man answers the friends who rebuke him: had you seen her face to face you would not rebuke me, for the bold look of that young woman's bright face, her painted eyes that put to shame the bee-opened neythal of the curving backwater, at the harbour of Korkai of chariot-lord Vazhuthi, where thaazhai guards its white flowers and horses' hooves crush the pearls the waves bring in.","n":"The whole shore of Korkai is laid out as the setting of a single look: the pearls trodden underfoot by horses tell how rich the place is, and the girl's eyes defeat even the flowers of such a shore.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 130","lb":"A130","poet":"வெண்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"கழறிய பாங்கற்குத்தலைமகன் கழற்றெதிர் மறுத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu","கழறிய பாங்கற்குத்தலைமகன் கழற்றெதிர் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["முண்டகம்","முண்டகம்",4,"maram","scene",""],["தாழை","தாழை",5,"maram","scene","screw-pine"],["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",9,"pira","scene","pearl"],["புரவிக்","புரவி",10,"maa","scene","horse"],["வண்டுவாய்","வண்டு",12,"pira","scene","bee"],["நெய்தற்","நெய்தல்",12,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","வழுதி",11],["town","கொற்கை",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:7"},"a:131":{"book":"akananuru","num":132,"t":["'விசும்புற நிவந்த மாத்தாள் இதணைப்","பசுங்கேழ் மெல்லிலை அருகுநெறித் தன்ன,","வண்டுபடுபு இருளிய, தாழ்இருங் கூந்தல்","சுரும்புஉண விரிந்த பெருந்தண் கோதை","இவளினும் சிறந்தன்று, ஈதல் நமக்கு என","வீளை அம்பின் விழுத்தொடை மழவர்","நாள்ஆ உய்த்த நாமவெஞ் சுரத்து","நடைமெலிந்து ஒழிந்த சேட்படர் கன்றின்","கடைமணி உகுநீர் துடைத்த ஆடவர்","பெயரும் பீடும் எழுதி, அதர்தொறும்,","பீலி சூட்டிய பிறங்குநிலை நடுகல்","வேல்ஊன்று பலகை வேற்றுமுனை கடுக்கும்","வெருவரு தகுந கானம் 'நம்மொடு","வருக' என்னுதி ஆயின்,","வாரேன்; நெஞ்சம்! வாய்க்கநின் வினையே"],"p":"","r":"The man refuses his heart, which says that giving is worth more than the girl with bee-dark hanging hair and a cool garland, and bids him come through the dread forest where the hero-stones of men who died recovering cattle driven off at dawn by bowmen, crowned with peacock feathers and inscribed with name and fame, stand along every path like a hostile front of planted spears and shields: I will not come, heart; may your work succeed.","n":"The heart argues from a puram value (ஈதல், the giving that needs wealth), and the man answers with the price of that value, the hero-stones of men killed on the road. The dismissal, வாய்க்கநின் வினையே, turns the usual blessing on the departing man into an ironic farewell to his own heart.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","eegai","nenju","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 131","lb":"A131","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் கடைக்கூட்டியநெஞ்சிற்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பொருள் கடைக்கூட்டியநெஞ்சிற்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["மெல்லிலை","இலை",2,"maram","simile",""],["வண்டுபடுபு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["கோதை","கோதை",4,"maram","ornament","garland"],["மழவர்","மழவர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["நாள்ஆ","ஆ",7,"maa","scene","cattle"],["கன்றின்","கன்று",8,"maa","scene",""],["பீலி","பீலி",11,"pul","ornament","peacock feather"]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:7"},"a:132":{"book":"akananuru","num":133,"t":["ஏனலும் இறங்குகுரல் இறுத்தன; நோய்மலிந்து,","ஆய்கவின் தொலைந்த இவள் நுதலும்; நோக்கி","ஏதில மொழியும் இவ்வூரும்; ஆகலின்,","களிற்றுமுகந் திறந்த கவுளுடைப் பகழி,","வால்நிணப் புகவின், கானவர் தங்கை","அம்பணை மென்தோள் ஆயஇதழ் மழைக்கண்","ஒல்கியற் கொடிச்சியை நல்கினை ஆயின்,","கொண்டனை சென்மோ நுண்பூண் மார்ப!","துளிதலைத் தலைஇய சாரல் நளிசுனைக்","கூம்புமுகை அவிழ்த்த குறுஞ்சிறைப் பறவை","வேங்கை விரியிணர் ஊதிக், காந்தள்","தேனுடைக் குவிகுலைத் துஞ்சி, யானை","இருங்கவுட் கடாஅம் கனவும்,","பெருங்கல் வேலி, நும் உறைவின் ஊர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"Meeting the lover after leading the girl home, the friend tells him that the millet is harvested, the girl's forehead has lost its beauty to lovesickness and the town eyes her and talks; so if he loves this hunters' sister with bamboo shoulders and rain-cool eyes, let him take her away to his own town walled by great hills, where a small-winged bee that opened the buds by the spring sips the vengai, sleeps in the honeyed kaanthal and dreams of the elephant's must.","n":"The harvest of the millet ends the daytime meetings in the field, which is why the friend speaks now. A traditional reading takes the bee that moves from vengai to kaanthal as a hint at the lover; the poem gives only one correspondence, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["udanpokku","alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 132","lb":"A132","poet":"தாயங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகளைஇடத்துய்த்துவந்து தலைமகனை எதிர்ப்பட்டு நின்றுவரைவுகடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி தலைமகளைஇடத்துய்த்துவந்து தலைமகனை எதிர்ப்பட்டு நின்றுவரைவுகடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஏனலும்","ஏனல்",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["களிற்றுமுகந்","களிறு",4,"maa","scene",""],["நிணப்","நிணம்",5,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கானவர்","கானவர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["பறவை","பறவை",10,"pira","scene","bee"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",11,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",11,"maram","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",12,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:7"},"a:133":{"book":"akananuru","num":134,"t":["'குன்றி அன்ன கண்ண, குருஉமயிர்ப்,","புன்தாள், வெள்ளெலி மோவாய் ஏற்றை","செம்பரல் முரம்பில் சிதர்ந்த பூழி,","நல்நாள் வேங்கைவீ நன்களம் வரிப்பக்,","கார்தலை மணந்த பைம்புதற் புறவின்,","வில்எறி பஞ்சியின் வெண்மழை தவழும்","கொல்லை இதைய குறும்பொறை மருங்கில்,","கரிபரந் தன்ன காயாஞ் செம்மலொடு","எரிபரந் தன்ன இலமலர் விரைஇப்,","பூங்கலுழ் சுமந்த தீம்புனற் கான்யாற்று","வான்கொள் தூவல் வளிதர உண்கும்;","எம்மொடு வருதல் வல்லையோ மற்று?' எனக்","கொன்ஒன்று வினவினர் மன்னே- தோழி!-","இதல்முள் ஒப்பின் முகைமுதிர் வெட்சி","கொல்புனக் குருந்தொடு கல்அறை தாஅம்","மிளைநாட்டு அத்தத்து ஈர்ஞ்சுவற் கலித்த","வரிமரல் கறிக்கும் மடப்பிணைத்","திரிமருப்பு இரலைய காடிறந் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her worried friend that he once asked her, in vain, whether she could come with him to drink the wind-blown spray of a flower-laden forest stream in a rain-fresh pasture land of vengai, kaayaa and ilavam where white clouds drift like bow-beaten cotton; now he has crossed alone the wild Milai track where a twisted-horned stag keeps his young doe that chews the marul grass.","n":"The remembered invitation is all mullai freshness, the present road is paalai; she sets the land he offered her against the one he took alone. The colophon says she means to show she can bear it, but கொன் ('in vain') carries a reproach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","udanpokku"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 133","lb":"A133","poet":"உறையூர் மருத்துவன் தாமோதரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடையாற்றாளாயினாளெனக் கவன்ற தோழிக்குத்தலைமகள் ஆற்றுவலென்பதுபடச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவிடையாற்றாளாயினாளெனக் கவன்ற தோழிக்குத்தலைமகள் ஆற்றுவலென்பதுபடச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வெள்ளெலி","எலி",2,"maa","scene","white rat"],["வேங்கைவீ","வேங்கை",4,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["காயாஞ்","காயா",8,"maram","simile",""],["இலமலர்","இலவம்",9,"maram","simile",""],["இதல்முள்","இதல்",14,"pul","simile","quail"],["வெட்சி","வெட்சி",14,"maram","simile",""],["குருந்தொடு","குருந்து",15,"maram","scene",""],["வரிமரல்","மரல்",17,"maram","scene",""],["மடப்பிணைத்","பிணை",17,"maa","iraicchi",""],["இரலைய","இரலை",18,"maa","iraicchi","stag"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","இரலைய","confirmed","1/1","even the stag of that forest stays with its mate, yet he crossed it without her"]],"ch":"a:7"},"a:134":{"book":"akananuru","num":135,"t":["வானம் வாய்ப்பக் கவினிக் கானம்","கமஞ்சூல் மாமழை கார்பயந்து இறுத்தென;","மணிமருள் பூவை அணிமலர் இடையிடைச்,","செம்புற மூதாய் பரத்தலின், நன்பல","முல்லை வீகழல் தாஅய், வல்லோன்","செய்கை அன்ன செந்நிலப் புறவின்;","வாஅப் பாணி வயங்குதொழிற் கலிமாத்","தாஅத் தாளிணை மெல்ல ஒதுங்க,","இடிமறந்து, ஏமதி- வலவ! குவிமுகை","வாழை வான்பூ ஊழுறுபு உதிர்ந்த","ஒழிகுலை அன்ன திரிமருப்பு ஏற்றொடு","கணைக்கால் அம்பிணைக் காமர் புணர்நிலை","கடுமான் தேர்ஒலி கேட்பின்,","நடுநாட் கூட்டம் ஆகலும் உண்டே"],"p":"","r":"Returning with his work done, the man tells the charioteer that the rains have come and the forest is lovely, with sapphire-blue poovai, red velvet-mites and fallen mullai on red soil like a master's painting; let the horses step softly and forget the goad, for if the twisted-horned stag and his doe in their loving union hear the swift chariot, [they will be parted], and still a midnight meeting can be theirs at home.","n":"The man spares the mating deer the noise of his chariot on his way to his own reunion; his care for them is the tenderness he brings home. The syntax of the last two lines is compressed, and whose union the midnight meeting is (the deer's restored, or his with his wife) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","karpu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 134","lb":"A134","poet":"சீத்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி மீண்டதலைமகன் பாகற் குரைத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றி மீண்டதலைமகன் பாகற் குரைத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பூவை","பூவை",3,"maram","simile","kaayaa flower"],["மூதாய்","மூதாய்",4,"pira","scene","velvet mite"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",5,"maram","scene",""],["கலிமாத்","மா",7,"maa","scene","horse"],["வாழை","வாழை",10,"maram","simile",""],["ஏற்றொடு","ஏறு",11,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["அம்பிணைக்","பிணை",12,"maa","iraicchi","doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","ஏற்றொடு","confirmed","1/1","the deer's union is the one the man is hurrying home to, and the care he takes not to break it is the care he brings to his wife"]],"ch":"a:7"},"a:135":{"book":"akananuru","num":136,"t":["திதலை மாமை தளிர்வனப்பு அழுங்கப்,","புதலிவர் பீரின் எதிர்மலர் கடுப்பப்,","பசலை பாய்ந்த நுதலேன் ஆகி,","எழுதெழில் மழைக்கண் கலுழ, நோய் கூர்ந்து,","ஆதி மந்தியின் அறிவுபிறி தாகிப்","பேதுற் றிசினே - காதல்அம் தோழி!","காய்கதிர் திருகலின் கனைந்துகால் கடுகி,","ஆடுதளிர் இருப்பைக் கூடுகுவி வான்பூக்","கோடுகடை கழங்கின், அறைமிசைத் தாஅம்","காடிறந் தனரே, காதலர்; அடுபோர்,","வீயா விழுப்புகழ், விண்தோய் வியன்குடை,","ஈர்-எழு வேளிர் இயந்துஒருங்கு எறிந்த","கழுவுள் காமூர் போலக்","கலங்கின்று மாது, அவர்த் தெளிந்தஎன் நெஞ்சே"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that her skin has lost its tender sheen, her forehead is spread with pallor like the peerkku flower, her eyes weep, and her mind is changed like Aathi Manthi's; her lover has crossed the forest where iruppai flowers fall on the rock like dice in the burning wind, and her heart that trusted him is shaken like Kazhuvul's Kamur when fourteen Velir of undying fame attacked it together.","n":"Two legendary disasters frame her state: Aathi Manthi who lost her husband to the river and went mad, and the siege of Kamur by fourteen chiefs at once. Her trust (தெளிந்த) is the fortress that has fallen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 135","lb":"A135","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண்வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொற்றது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண்வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொற்றது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பீரின்","பீர்",2,"maram","simile",""],["இருப்பைக்","இருப்பை",8,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["king","வேளிர்",12],["king","கழுவுள்",13],["town","காமூர்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:7"},"a:136":{"book":"akananuru","num":137,"t":["மைப்புஅறப் புழுக்கின் நெய்க்கனி வெண்சோறு","வரையா வண்மையொடு புரையோர்ப் பேணிப்,","புள்ளுப் புணர்ந்து இனிய ஆகத் தெள்ஒளி","அம்கண் இருவிசும்பு விளங்கத், திங்கட்","சகடம் மண்டிய துகள்தீர் கூட்டத்துக்,","கடிநகர் புனைந்து, கடவுட் பேணிப்,","படுமண முழவொடு பரூஉப்பணை இமிழ,","வதுவை மண்ணிய மகளிர் விதுப்புற்றுப்,","பூக்கணும் இமையார் நோக்குபு மறைய,","மென்பூ வாகைப் புன்புறக் கவட்டிலை,","பழங்கன்று கறித்த பயம்பமல் அறுகைத்","தழங்குகுரல் வானின் தலைப்பெயற்கு ஈன்ற","மண்ணுமணி அன்ன மாஇதழ்ப் பாவைத்","தண்நறு முகையொடு வெந்நூல் சூட்டித்,","தூஉடைப் பொலிந்து மேவரத் துவன்றி,","மழைபட் டன்ன மணன்மலி பந்தர்,","இழைஅணி சிறப்பின் பெயர்வியர்ப்பு ஆற்றித்","தமர்நமக்கு ஈத்த தலைநாள் இரவின்,","'உவர்நீங்கு கற்பின்எம் உயிர்உடம் படுவி!","முருங்காக் கலிங்கம் முழுவதும் வளைஇப்,","பெரும்புழுக் குற்றநின் பிறைநுதற் பொறிவியர்","உறுவளி ஆற்றச் சிறுவரை திற' என","ஆர்வ நெஞ்சமொடு போர்வை வவ்வலின்,","உறைகழி வாளின் உருவுபெயர்ந்து இமைப்ப,","மறைதிறன் அறியாள் ஆகி, ஒய்யென","நாணினள் இறைஞ்சி யோளே- பேணிப்","பரூஉப்பகை ஆம்பற் குரூஉத்தொடை நீவிச்","சுரும்பிமிர் ஆய்மலர் வேய்ந்த","இரும்பல் கூந்தல் இருள்மறை ஒளித்தே"],"p":"","r":"The man, kept out by his wife's unyielding sulk, recalls to his heart their wedding: the feast of ghee-rich rice, the good omens and the moon in Rohini, the drums, the bathing of the bride, the arugu and vaakai garland tied with white thread, the sand-strewn pandal; and on the first night, when he asked her to loosen the cloth that made her forehead sweat and pulled the covering, her body flashed like a sword from its sheath, and not knowing how to hide she bowed her head in shame and hid in the darkness of her flowered hair.","n":"This is the fullest account of a wedding in the anthology. The sulk of the colophon is not in the poem: the man answers present refusal only by remembering her first shyness, and the poem ends inside the memory.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","naanam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 136","lb":"A136","poet":"விற்றூற்று மூதெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"உணர்ப்புவயின் வாராஊடற்கண் தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","உணர்ப்புவயின் வாராஊடற்கண் தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வெண்சோறு","சோறு",1,"unavu","gift_food",""],["புள்ளுப்","புள்",3,"pul","scene","omen bird"],["திங்கட்","திங்கள்",4,"pira","scene","moon"],["முழவொடு","முழவு",7,"parai","scene",""],["பரூஉப்பணை","பணை",7,"parai","scene","drum"],["வாகைப்","வாகை",10,"maram","ornament",""],["பழங்கன்று","கன்று",11,"maa","scene",""],["அறுகைத்","அறுகை",11,"maram","ornament",""],["ஆம்பற்","ஆம்பல்",27,"maram","ornament",""],["சுரும்பிமிர்","சுரும்பு",28,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:7"},"a:137":{"book":"akananuru","num":138,"t":["ஆறுசெல் வம்பலர் சேறுகிளைத்து உண்ட","சிறும்பல் கேணிப் பிடியடி நசைஇச்,","களிறுதொடூஉக் கடக்குங் கான்யாற்று அத்தம்","சென்றுசேர்பு ஒல்லார் ஆயினும், நினக்கே-","வென்றெறி முரசின் விறற்போர்ச் சோழர்","இன்கடுங் கள்ளின் உறந்தை ஆங்கண்,","வருபுனல் நெரிதரும் இகுகரைப் பேரியாற்று","உருவ வெண்மணல் முருகுநாறு தண்பொழிற்","பங்குனி முயக்கம் கழிந்த வழிநாள்","வீஇலை அமன்ற மரம்பயில் இறும்பில்","தீஇல் அடுப்பின் அரங்கம் போலப்,","பெரும்பாழ் கொண்டன்று, நுதலே, தோளும்,","தோளா முத்தின் தெண்கடற் பொருநன்","திண்தேர்ச் செழியன் பொருப்பிற் கவாஅன்","நல்லெழில் நெடுவேய் புரையும்","தொல்கவின் தொலைந்தன: நோகோ யானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl who fears he will leave that although he has not even set out for the forest track where a tusker, longing for his mate, follows her footprints past the travellers' mud wells, her forehead has already become a waste like the empty theatre with cold hearths in the grove at the Chola's Uranthai the day after the Panguni festival, and her shoulders, once like the tall bamboo on Chezhiyan's hill, have lost their old beauty: I grieve.","n":"The friend's point is the girl's haste: she wastes for a parting that has not happened. The tusker following its mate's tracks on the very road she fears quietly answers her fear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 137","lb":"A137","poet":"உறையூர் முதுகூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன்பிரியுமெனக் கருதி வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழிசொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தலைமகன்பிரியுமெனக் கருதி வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழிசொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கேணிப்","கேணி",2,"pira","scene","well"],["பிடியடி","பிடி",2,"maa","iraicchi",""],["களிறுதொடூஉக்","களிறு",3,"maa","iraicchi",""],["முரசின்","முரசு",5,"parai","scene",""],["கள்ளின்","கள்",6,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["தோளா முத்தின்","முத்து",13,"pira","scene","pearl"],["நெடுவேய்","வேய்",15,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",5],["town","உறந்தை",6],["king","செழியன்",14]],"dv":[["iraicchi","களிறுதொடூஉக்","not_confirmed","0/2","on the road she fears he will take, even the tusker cannot be parted from his mate, so he will not leave her"]],"ch":"a:7"},"a:138":{"book":"akananuru","num":139,"t":["இகுளை! கேட்டிசின் காதலம் தோழி !","குவளை உண்கண் தெண்பனி மல்க,","வறிதியான் வருந்திய செல்லற்கு அன்னை","பிறிதொன்று கடுத்தனள் ஆகி - வேம்பின்","வெறிகொள் பாசிலை நீலமொடு சூடி,","உடலுநர்க் கடந்த கடல்அம் தானைத்,","திருந்துஇலை நெடுவேல் தென்னவன் - பொதியில்","அருஞ்சிமை இழிதரும் ஆர்த்துவரல் அருவியின்","ததும்புசீர் இன்னியங் கறங்கக், கைதொழுது,","உருகெழு சிறப்பின் முருகுமனைத் தரீஇக்,","கடம்பும் களிறும் பாடி, நுடங்குபு","தோடுந் தொடலையும் கைக்கொண்டு, அல்கலும்","தேடினர் ஆதல் நன்றோ?- நீடு","நின்னொடு தெளித்த நன்மலை நாடன்","குறிவரல் அரைநாட் குன்றத்து உச்சி,","நெறிகெட வீழ்ந்த துன்னருங் கூர்இருள்,","திருமணி உமிழ்ந்த நாகம் காந்தட்","கொழுமடற் புதுப்பூ ஊதுந் தும்பி","நன்னிறம் மருளும் அருவிடர்","இன்னா நீள்இடை நினையும்என் நெஞ்சே"],"p":"","r":"With her lover outside, the girl tells her friend that when she wept for no cause her mother suspected something else and every night, wearing neem and blue lily, with music like the Pothiyil falls of the Pandya, has Murugu brought into the house and sings of the kadamba and the elephant, searching for the cause: is that good? Her own heart thinks only of the long hard way by which the hill lord comes at midnight, over the peak where in pitch darkness a dragonfly sipping kaanthal is bewildered by the colour of a snake that has spat its jewel.","n":"The mother's veriyaattu is described in full (Murugu brought into the house, the kadamba and the elephant sung) and dismissed by the girl as a vain search; aimed at the listening lover, it warns that the household is already looking for the cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","anangu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 138","lb":"A138","poet":"எழூஉப்பன்றி நாகன் குமரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன்சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழிக்குச்சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன்சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழிக்குச்சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"],["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["குவளை","குவளை",2,"maram","scene",""],["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",4,"maram","ornament","neem"],["நீலமொடு","நீலம்",5,"maram","ornament","blue lily"],["அருவியின்","அருவி",8,"pira","simile",""],["முருகு","முருகு",10,"deyvam","scene",""],["கடம்பும்","கடம்பு",11,"maram","scene",""],["களிறும்","களிறு",11,"maa","scene",""],["நாகம்","நாகம்",17,"pira","scene","snake"],["காந்தட்","காந்தள்",17,"maram","scene",""],["தும்பி","தும்பி",18,"pira","scene","dragonfly"]],"en":[["king","தென்னவன்",7],["hill","பொதியில்",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:7"},"a:139":{"book":"akananuru","num":140,"t":["துஞ்சுவது போலஇருளி, விண்பக","இமைப்பது போலமின்னி, உறைக்கொண்டு","ஏறுவதுப் போலப் பாடுசிறந்து உரைஇ","நிலம்நெஞ்சு உட்க ஓவாது சிலைத்தாங்கு,","ஆர்தளி பொழிந்த வார்பெயற் கடைநாள்;","ஈன்றுநாள் உலந்த வாலா வெண்மழை","வான்தோய் உயர்வரை ஆடும் வைகறைப்","புதல்ஒளி சிறந்த காண்பின் காலைத்,","தண்நறும் படுநீர் மாந்திப், பதவு அருந்து","வெண்புறக்கு உடைய திரிமருப்பு இரலை;","வார்மணல் ஒருசிறைப் பிடவுஅவிழ் கொழுநிழல்,","காமர் துணையொடு ஏமுற வதிய;","அரக்குநிற உருவின் ஈயல் மூதாய்","பரப்பி யவைபோற் பாஅய்ப், பலவுடன்","நீர்வார் மருங்கின் ஈர்அணி திகழ;","இன்னும் வாரார் ஆயின்- நன்னுதல்!","யாதுகொல் மற்றுவர் நிலையே? காதலர்","கருவிக் கார்இடி இரீஇய","பருவம் அன்று, அவர்: 'வருதும்' என்றதுவே"],"p":"","r":"The wasting girl tells her friend that the rains have had their last day of darkening, flashing and roaring, the empty clouds play on the high hills at dawn, the stag has drunk and grazed and rests with his lovely mate in the pidavam shade, and red velvet-mites shine by the running water; if he still does not come, what has become of him, for was this not the thundering season in which he said he would come?","n":"The landscape is all fulfilment (the rain spent, the stag at rest with its mate), so the only thing missing is the man. The last two lines can be read as 'this is not the season he named' (a comfort she tries on herself) or 'was it not this season' (the broken promise); the second fits her fear in line 17.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","mazhai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 139","lb":"A139","poet":"இடைக்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்ததலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை மெலிந்ததலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பதவு","பதவு",9,"maram","scene","grass"],["இரலை","இரலை",10,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["பிடவுஅவிழ்","பிடவு",11,"maram","scene",""],["மூதாய்","மூதாய்",13,"pira","simile","velvet mite"],["ஈயல்","ஈயல்",13,"pira","simile","winged termite"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","இரலை","confirmed","1/1","the season has brought every creature home to its mate, so the man's absence has no excuse"]],"ch":"a:7"},"a:140":{"book":"akananuru","num":141,"t":["பெருங்கடல் வேட்டத்துச் சிறுகுடிப் பரதவர்","இருங்கழி செறுவின் உழாஅது செய்த","வெண்கல் உப்பின் கொள்ளை சாற்றி,","என்றூழ் விடர குன்றம் போகும்","கதழ்கோல் உமணர் காதல் மடமகள்","சில்கோல் எல்வளை தெளிர்ப்ப வீசி","'நெல்லின் நேரே வெண்கல் உப்பு' எனச்","சேரி விலைமாறு கூறலின், மனைய","விளியறி ஞமலி குரைப்ப, வெரீஇய","மதர்கயல் மலைப்பின் அன்னகண் எனக்கு,","இதைமுயல் புனவன் புகைநிழல் கடுக்கும்","மாமூ தள்ளல் அழுந்திய சாகாட்டு","எவ்வந் தீர வாங்குந் தந்தை","கைபூண் பகட்டின் வருந்தி","வெய்ய உயிர்க்கும் நோயா கின்றே"],"p":"","r":"After his first meeting with her, the man tells his companion that the salt-merchant's young daughter, crying 'white salt for the same measure of paddy!' through the street with her bangles jingling, startled by a barking house dog, turned on him eyes like fighting kayal fish, and they have become a sickness that makes him strain and breathe hot like the ox yoked to her father's cart sunk in deep old mud.","n":"The simile turns on her own family: the man compares himself to the beast that pulls her father's salt cart out of the mire, so his love has already yoked him to her household.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 140","lb":"A140","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"இயற்கைப் புணர்ச்சிபுணர்ந்து நீங்கும் தலைமகன் பாங்கற்குரைத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu","இயற்கைப் புணர்ச்சிபுணர்ந்து நீங்கும் தலைமகன் பாங்கற்குரைத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["உப்பின்","உப்பு",3,"pira","scene","salt"],["உமணர்","உமணர்",5,"seythi","scene","salt merchants"],["எல்வளை","வளை",6,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",7,"unavu","scene",""],["ஞமலி","ஞமலி",9,"maa","scene","dog"],["கயல்","கயல்",10,"pira","simile","kayal fish"],["புனவன்","புனவன்",11,"seythi","simile",""],["பகட்டின்","பகடு",14,"maa","simile","ox"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:7"},"a:141":{"book":"akananuru","num":142,"t":["அம்ம வாழி, தோழி ! கைம்மிகக்","கனவுங் கங்குல்தோ றினிய: நனவும்","புனைவினை நல்இல் புள்ளும் பாங்கின!","நெஞ்சும் நனிபுகன்று உறையும்; எஞ்சாது","உலகுதொழில் உலந்து, நாஞ்சில் துஞ்சி","மழைகால் நீங்கிய மாக விசும்பில்","குறுமுயல் மறுநிறம் கிளர, மதி நிறைந்து,","அறுமீன் சேறும் அகல்இருள் நடுநாள்:","மறுகுவிளக் குறுத்து, மாலை தூக்கிப்,","பழவிறல் மூதூர்ப் பலருடன் துவன்றிய","விழவுஉடன் அயர, வருகதில் அம்ம!","துவரப் புலர்ந்து தூமலர் கஞலித்,","தகரம் நாறுந் தண்நறுங் கதுப்பின்","புதுமண மகடூஉ அயினிய கடிநகர்ப்","பல்கோட்டு அடுப்பில் பால்உலை இரீஇ","கூழைக் கூந்தற் குறுந்தொடி மகளிர்","பெருஞ்செய் நெல்லின் வாங்குகதிர் முறித்துப்","பாசவல் இடிக்கும் இருங்காழ் உலக்கைக்","கடிதுஇடி வெரீஇய கமஞ்சூல் வெண்குருகு","தீங்குலை வாழை ஓங்குமடல் இராது:","நெடுங்கால் மாஅத்துக் குறும்பறை பயிற்றுஞ்","செல்குடி நிறுத்த பெரும்பெயர்க் கரிகால்","வெல்போர்ச் சோழன் இடையாற்று அன்ன","நல்லிசை வெறுக்கை தருமார், பல்பொறிப்","புலிக்கேழ் உற்ற பூவிடைப் பெருஞ்சினை","நரந்த நறும்பூ நாள்மலர் உதிரக்,","கலைபாய்ந்து உகளும், கல்சேர் வேங்கைத்,","தேம்கமழ் நெடுவரைப் பிறங்கிய","வேங்கட வைப்பிற் சுரன்இறந் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"Answering a friend who fears she cannot bear the parting, the woman says her dreams are sweet night after night, the house birds give good omens and her heart is eager: may he come back for the town's festival of lamps on the full-moon midnight of the six stars, he who crossed the Vengadam desert to bring wealth as famous as Karikal's town Idaiyaru.","n":"Her answer to the friend's worry is a date, not resignation: she has fixed the Karthigai full moon as the night of his return. The wealth he went for is pictured not as gold but as a Chola town's household plenty (a new bride fed in her house, girls pounding fresh rice, a pregnant heron startled by the pestle), so even his errand is imagined as home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kanavu","selvam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 141","lb":"A141","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளெனக்கவன்ற தோழிக்குக் கிழத்தியுரைத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளெனக்கவன்ற தோழிக்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["புள்ளும்","புள்",3,"pul","scene","omen bird"],["நாஞ்சில்","நாஞ்சில்",5,"seythi","scene","plough"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",17,"unavu","scene",""],["பாசவல்","அவல்",18,"unavu","scene","flattened rice"],["உலக்கைக்","உலக்கை",18,"pira","scene","pestle"],["வெண்குருகு","குருகு",19,"pul","scene","heron"],["வாழை","வாழை",20,"maram","scene",""],["மாஅத்துக்","மா",21,"maram","scene","mango"],["நரந்த","நரந்தம்",26,"maram","scene",""],["கலை","கலை",27,"maa","scene","stag"],["வேங்கைத்","வேங்கை",27,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","கரிகால்",22],["town","இடையாற்று",23],["hill","வேங்கட",29]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:142":{"book":"akananuru","num":143,"t":["இலமலர் அன்ன அம்செந் நாவிற்","புலம்மீக் கூறும் புரையோர் ஏத்தப்,","பலர்மேந் தோன்றிய கவிகை வள்ளல்","நிறையருந் தானை வெல்போர் மாந்தரம்-","பொறையன் கடுங்கோப் பாடிச் சென்ற","குறையோர் கொள்கலம் போல, நன்றும்","உவஇனி - வாழிய, நெஞ்சே!- காதலி","முறையின் வழா அது ஆற்றிப் பெற்ற","கறையடி யானை நன்னன் பாழி,","ஊட்டரு மரபின் அஞ்சு வரு பேய்எக்","கூட்டெதிர் கொண்ட வாய்மொழி மிஞிலி","புள்ளிற்கு ஏமம் ஆகிய பெரும்பெயர்","வெள்ளத் தானை அதிகற் கொன்றுஉவந்து","ஒள்வாள் அமலை ஞாட்பிற்,","பலர் அறி வுறுதல் அஞ்சிப், பைப்பய,","நீர்த்திரள் கடுக்கும் மாசில் வெள்ளிச்","சூர்ப்புறு கோல்வளை செறித்த முன்கைக்","குறை அறல் அன்ன இரும்பல் கூந்தல்,","இடனில் சிறுபுறத்து இழையொடு துயல்வரக்,","கடல்மீன் துஞ்சும் நள்ளென் யாமத்து","உருவுகிளர் ஏர்வினைப் பொலிந்த பாவை","இயல்கற் றன்ன ஒதுக்கினள் வந்து,","பெயல் அலைக் கலங்கிய மலைப்பூங் கோதை","இயல்எறி பொன்னின் கொங்குசோர்பு உறைப்பத்","தொடிக்கண் வடுக்கொள - முயங்கினள்:","வடிப்புறு நரம்பின் தீவிய மொழிந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Leaving the night tryst, the man tells his heart to rejoice like the needy who carried full vessels away from Poraiyan Kadungo of Maantharam: she came at midnight, slowly, fearing that many would know, her dark hair swaying, walking like a doll that had learned to move, and embraced him till her bangle left its mark, speaking words sweet as tuned strings.","n":"Lines 7 to 14 set a puram battle (Minjili killing Athikan, 'a protection to the birds', at Nannan's Paazhi) beside her coming, but how the battle attaches to the sentence is not clear in our text: it may measure his joy or her daring. The patron simile is pointed: he is a suppliant who has received.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","punarchi","iravu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 142","lb":"A142","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி வந்துநீங்குந் தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி வந்துநீங்குந் தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["இலமலர்","இலவம் மலர்",1,"maram","simile","silk-cotton flower"],["யானை","யானை",9,"maa","scene",""],["கடல்மீன்","மீன்",20,"pira","scene","fish"],["மலைப்பூங்","மலைப்பூ",23,"maram","ornament",""],["நரம்பின்","நரம்பு",26,"yaazh","simile","lute string"]],"en":[["king","பொறையன் கடுங்கோ",5],["king","நன்னன்",9],["town","பாழி",9],["king","மிஞிலி",11],["king","அதிகற்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:143":{"book":"akananuru","num":144,"t":["செய்வினைப் பிரிதல் எண்ணிக், கைம்மிகக்","காடுகவின் ஒழியக் கடுங்கதிர் தெறுதலின்,","நீடுசினை வறிய வாக, ஒல்லென","வாடுபல் அகலிலை கோடைக்கு ஒய்யும்","தேக்கு அமல் அடுக்கத்து ஆங்கண் மேக்கெழுபு","முளிஅரிற் பிறந்த வளிவளர் கூர்எரிச்","சுடர்நிமிர் நெடுங்கொடி விடர்முகை முழங்கும்","'வெம்மலை அருஞ்சுரம் நீந்தி - ஐய!","சேறும்' என்ற சிறுசொற்கு... இவட்கே,","வசைஇல் வெம்போர் வானவன் மறவன்","நசையின் வாழ்நர்க்கு நன்கலஞ் சுரக்கும்,","பொய்யா வாய்வாள், புனைகழல் பிட்டன்","மைதவழ் உயர்சிமைக் குதிரைக் கவாஅன்","அகல்அறை நெடுஞ்சுனை, துவலையின் மலர்ந்த","தண்கமழ் நீலம் போலக்,","கண்பனி கலுழ்ந்தன; நோகோ யானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that when he said, 'We will go, crossing the burning hills where the sun has stripped the teak and wind-fed fire roars in the clefts', at that small word the woman's eyes ran with tears like the blue lilies opened by spray in a pool on Kudhirai hill of Pittan, the Chera's warrior who showers fine vessels on those who live by hope; and the friend grieves.","n":"The long, vivid account of the road is the man's own speech, quoted back; the friend's reply is one phrase, 'small word', and the tears. The colophon says it worked: he gave up the journey.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kan","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 143","lb":"A143","poet":"ஆலம்பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரியக்கருதிய தலைகனைத் தோழி தலைமகளது ஆற்றாமை கண்டுசெலவழுங்குவித்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,"பொருள்வயிற் பிரியக்கருதிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தேக்கு","தேக்கு",5,"maram","scene",""],["நீலம்","நீலம்",15,"maram","simile","blue lily"]],"en":[["king","வானவன்",10],["patron","பிட்டன்",12],["hill","குதிரைக்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:144":{"book":"akananuru","num":145,"t":["\"வருதும்\" என்ற நாளும் பொய்த்தன;","அரியேர் உண்கண் நீரும் நில்லா;","தண்கார்க்கு ஈன்ற பைங்கொடி முல்லை","வைவாய் வான்முகை அவிழ்ந்த கோதை","பெய்வனப்பு இழந்த கதுப்பும் உள்ளார்,","அருள்கண் மாறலோ மாறுக- அந்தில்","அறன்அஞ் சலரே!- ஆயிழை! நமர்' எனச்","சிறிய சொல்லிப் பெரிய புலப்பினும்,","பனிபடு நறுந்தார் குழைய, நம்மொடு,","துனிதீர் முயக்கம் பெற்றோள் போல","உவக்குநள்- வாழிய, நெஞ்சே!- விசும்பின்","ஏறெழுந்து முழங்கினும் மாறெழுந்து சிலைக்கும்","கடாஅ யானை கொட்கும் பாசறைப்,","பார்வேட்டு எழுந்த மள்ளர் கையதை","கூர்வாட் குவிமுகஞ் சிதைய நூறி","மானடி மருங்கில் பெயர்த்த குருதி","வான மீனின் வயின்வயின் இமைப்ப,","அமரோர்த்து, அட்ட செல்வம்","தமர்விரைந்து உரைப்பக் கேட்கும் ஞான்றே"],"p":"","r":"His war done, the man tells his heart (for the charioteer to hear) that his wife may say the days he promised have proved false, her tears will not stop, he forgets her hair bare of mullai garlands, he has no fear of wrong; but on the day his people hurry home to tell of the wealth won in battle, from a camp where the rutting elephant trumpets back at the thunder, she will rejoice as if she already had the embrace that ends all sulking.","n":"He imagines her complaint in her own words before he imagines her joy, and her joy comes from the news before he arrives: the report of the victory does the work of the embrace (துனிதீர் முயக்கம் பெற்றோள் போல).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","oodal","ninaivu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 144","lb":"A144","poet":"மதுரை அளக்கர் ஞாழலார் மகனார் மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன்தன்னெஞ்சிற்கு உரைப்பானாய்ப் பாகற்குச்சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",3,"maram","absent",""],["யானை","யானை",13,"maa","scene","war elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:145":{"book":"akananuru","num":146,"t":["வேர்முழுது உலறி நின்ற புழற்கால்,","தேர்மணி இசையின் சிள்வீடு ஆர்க்கும்,","வற்றல் மரத்த பொன்தலை ஓதி","வெயிற்கவின் இழந்த வைப்பின் பையுள் கொள,","நுண்ணிதின் நிவக்கும் வெண்ஞெமை வியன்காட்டு","ஆளில் அத்தத்து, அளியள் அவனொடு-","வாள்வரி பொருத புண்கூர் யானை","புகர்சிதை முகத்த குருதி வார,","உயர்சிமை நெடுங்கோட்டு உருமென முழங்கும்","'அருஞ்சுரம் இறந்தனள்' என்ப- பெருஞ்சீர்","அன்னி குறுக்கைப் பறந்தலைத், திதியன்","தொல்நிலை முழுமுதல் துமியப் பண்ணிய","நன்னர் மெல்லிணர்ப் புன்னை போலக்,","கடுநவைப் படீஇயர் மாதோ - களி மயில்","குஞ்சரக் குரல குருகோடு ஆலும்,","துஞ்சா முழவின் துய்த்தியல் வாழ்க்கைக்,","கூழுடைத் தந்தை இடனுடை வரைப்பின்,","ஊழடி ஒதுங்கினும் உயங்கும் ஐம்பாற்","சிறுபல் கூந்தற் போதுபிடித்து அருளாது,","எறிகோல் சிதைய நூறவும், சிறுபுறம்,","'எனக்குஉரித்து' என்னாள், நின்ற என்","அமர்க்கண் அஞ்ஞையை அலைத்த கையே!"],"p":"","r":"The foster mother hears that her girl has crossed with her man the empty, sun-stripped forest where cicadas shrill like chariot bells and a tiger-wounded elephant roars like thunder; and she curses her own hand, which in the rich father's house once seized the girl's small hair and beat her till the stick broke: may it come to harsh ruin like the punnai felled at Kurukkai in the war of Anni and Thithiyan.","n":"The curse falls on her own hand, not on the man who took the girl: the elopement is read back as the result of her harshness at home. The girl who tired after a single step in her father's house is set against the road she has now crossed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 145","lb":"A145","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட்போக்கியசெவிலித்தாய் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட்போக்கியசெவிலித்தாய்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["vekuli","அலை"]],"k":[["சிள்வீடு","சிள்வீடு",2,"pira","simile","cicada"],["ஓதி","ஓதி",3,"pira","scene","lizard"],["ஞெமை","ஞெமை",5,"maram","scene",""],["வாள்வரி","வரி",7,"maa","scene","tiger"],["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","scene",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",13,"maram","simile",""],["மயில்","மயில்",14,"pul","scene",""],["குருகோடு","குருகு",15,"pul","scene",""],["முழவின்","முழவு",16,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["king","அன்னி",11],["battle","குறுக்கைப் பறந்தலை",11],["king","திதியன்",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:146":{"book":"akananuru","num":147,"t":["வலிமிகு முன்பின் அண்ணல் ஏஎறு","பனிமலர்ப் பொய்கைப் பகல்செல மறுகி","மடக்கண் எருமை மாண்நாகு தழீஇ,","படப்பை நண்ணிப், பழனத்து அல்கும்","கலிமகிழ் ஊரன் ஒலிமணி நெடுந்தேர்","ஒள்ளிமழை மகளிர் சேரிப், பல்நாள்","இயங்கல் ஆனாது ஆயின்; வயங்கிழை","யார்கொல் அனியள் தானே- எம்போல்","மாயப் பரத்தன் வாய்மொழி நம்பி","வளிபொரத் துயல்வரும் தளிபொழி மலரின்","கண்பனி ஆகத்து உறைப்பக், கண் பசந்து","ஆயமும் அயலும் மருளத்,","தாயோம்பு ஆய்நலம் வேண்டா தோளோ?"],"p":"","r":"Refusing the bard sent to plead for her husband, the woman says that if his tall belled chariot will not stop going for days on end to the street of the bright-jewelled women, then whoever she is, the woman who now trusts that deceiving philanderer's word as she once did will weep like a wind-shaken flower dripping dew, go pale, and give up the beauty her mother guarded.","n":"She speaks of the new woman with pity rather than anger (அளியள்), and the pity is self-description: எம்போல், like me. The opening buffalo, which spends the day in the flowered pond and then lies down by a young cow near the garden, carries the charge without her stating it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","pasalai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 146","lb":"A146","poet":"உவர்க்கண்ணூர்ப் புல்லங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"வாயில் வேண்டிச் சென்றபாணற்குத் தலைமகள் வாயில் மறுத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu","வாயில் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["ஏஎறு","ஏறு",1,"maa","ullurai","bull buffalo"],["பனிமலர்ப்","மலர்",2,"maram","scene",""],["நாகு","நாகு",3,"maa","ullurai","young female buffalo"],["மலரின்","மலர்",10,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","ஏஎறு நாகு","confirmed","2/2","the husband and the new young woman he has taken up"]],"ch":"a:8"},"a:147":{"book":"akananuru","num":148,"t":["ஓங்குமலைச் சிலம்பில் பிடவுடன் மலர்ந்த","வேங்கை வெறித்தழை வேறுவகுத் தன்னை","ஊன்பொதி அவிழாக் கோட்டுகிர்க் குருளை","மூன்றுடன் ஈன்ற முடங்கர் நிழத்த,","துறுகல் விடரளைப் பிணவுபசி கூர்ந்தெனப்,","பொறிகிளர் உழுவைப் போழ்வாய் ஏற்றை","அறுகோட்டு உழைமான் ஆண்குரல் ஓர்க்கும்","நெறிபடு கவலை நிரம்பா நீளிடை,","வெள்ளி வீதியைப் போல நன்றும்","செலவு அயர்ந் திசினால் யானே; பல புலந்து,","உண்ணா உயக்கமொடு உயிர்செலச் சா அய்,","தோளும் தொல்கவின் தொலைய, நாளும்","பிரிந்தோர் பெயர்வுக்கு இரங்கி,","மருந்துபிறிது இன்மையின், இருந்துவினை இலனே!"],"p":"","r":"Told by her friend that the man is leaving, the woman says that, like Velli Veethi, she has made herself ready to go on that endless forked road where a spotted tiger listens for the stag's call to feed his mate, starving in a rock cleft after bearing three cubs; wasting unfed, her shoulders losing their beauty, grieving daily for his return, she has no will to stay behind, since there is no other medicine.","n":"Velli Veethi is the name of a woman poet (Akananuru 45 is under her name); the heroine claims her as a precedent for a woman who goes after the man. The hungry tigress fed by her mate, and the woman who does not eat (உண்ணா உயக்கம்), stand a few lines apart.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","melivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 147","lb":"A147","poet":"ஒளவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவுணர்த்தியதோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"செலவுணர்த்தியதோழிக்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பிடவுடன்","பிடவு",1,"maram","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",2,"maram","simile",""],["குருளை","குருளை",3,"maa","scene","tiger cub"],["பிணவு","பிணவு",5,"maa","iraicchi","tigress"],["ஏற்றை","ஏற்றை",6,"maa","iraicchi","male tiger"],["உழைமான்","உழை",7,"maa","scene","stag"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","ஏற்றை","confirmed","1/1","on the very road he will take, the beast provides for its mate in her need, while he leaves her to waste unfed"]],"ch":"a:8"},"a:148":{"book":"akananuru","num":149,"t":["பனைத்திரள் அன்ன பருஏர் எறுழ்த் தடக்கைச்","கொலைச்சினந் தவிரா மதனுடை முன்பின்,","வண்டுபடு கடாஅத்து, உயர்மருப்பு யானை","தண்கமழ் சிலம்பின் மரம்படத் தொலைச்சி:","உறுபுலி உரறக் குத்தி; விறல்கடிந்து,","சிறுதினைப் பெரும்புனம் வவ்வும் நாட!","கடும்பரிக் குதிரை ஆஅய் எயினன்","நெடுந்தேர் மிஞிலியொடு பொருது, களம் பட்டெனக்","காணிய செல்லாக் கூகை நாணிக்","கடும்பகல் வழங்கா தாஅங்கு, இடும்பை","பெரிதால் அம்ம இவட்கே: அதனால்","மாலை வருதல் வேண்டும் - சோலை","முளைமேய் பெருங்களிறு வழங்கும்","மலைமுதல் அடுக்கத்த சிறுகல் ஆறே"],"p":"","r":"To the man of the land where a rutting elephant, after smashing trees and goring a tiger, raids the millet field, the friend says the girl's distress is great, like the owl that, ashamed when Aay Eyinan fell fighting Minjili, does not go abroad in broad day; so he must come at nightfall, by the small stony path where the great tusker grazes shoots.","n":"The owl's shame belongs to the Eyinan legend in which the birds shaded the fallen hero from the sun: the night bird could not join them. The friend turns that daylight shame into the reason the meetings must move from day to night. The raiding elephant of the address may hint at his daytime visits to the field she guards, but the poem never makes the match.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","iravu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 148","lb":"A148","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பகல் வருவானை இரவுவருகென்றது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகல் வருவானை"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""],["உறுபுலி","புலி",5,"maa","scene",""],["சிறுதினைப்","தினை",6,"unavu","scene",""],["கூகை","கூகை",9,"pul","simile","owl"],["களிறு","களிறு",13,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["king","ஆஅய் எயினன்",7],["king","மிஞிலி",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:149":{"book":"akananuru","num":150,"t":["சிறுபுன் சிதலை சேண்முயன்று எடுத்த;","நெடுஞ்செம் புற்றத்து ஒடுங்கிரை முனையின்,","புல்லரை இருப்பைத் தொள்ளை வான்பூப்,","பெருங்கை எண்கின் இருங்கிளை கவரும்","அத்த நீள்இடைப் போகி, நன்றும்","அரிதுசெய் விழுப்பொருள் எளிதினிற் பெறினும்","வாரேன்- வாழி, என் நெஞ்சே!- சேரலர்","சுள்ளியம் பேரியாற்று வெண்நுரை கலங்க,","யவனர் தந்த வினைமாண் நன்கலம்","பொன்னொடு வந்து கறியொடு பெயரும்","வளம்கெழு முசிறி ஆர்ப்பெழ வளஇ,","அருஞ்சமம் கடந்து படிமம் வவ்விய","நெடுநல் யானை அடுபோர்ச் செழியன்","கொடிநுடங்கு மறுகின் கூடற் குடாஅது,","பல்பொறி மஞ்ஞை வெல்கொடி உயரிய,","ஒடியா விழவின், நெடியோன் குன்றத்து,","வண்டுபட நீடிய குண்டுசுனை நீலத்து","எதிர்மலர்ப் பிணையல் அன்ன இவள்","அரிமதர் மழைக்கண் தெண்பனி கொளவே"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart he will not go down the long desert road, where bears raid the iruppai blossoms when tired of termites, even if the precious wealth that is hard to win came easily, for it would fill with tears her eyes, which are like the paired blue lilies of the deep pool on the Tall One's hill west of Koodal, the city of Chezhiyan, who stormed Muziri where Yavana ships come with gold and leave with pepper.","n":"He refuses not hard-won wealth but wealth gained easily, the strongest form of the refusal. Muziri's trade, gold in and pepper out, stands in the same sentence, so the richest wealth the poet knows is weighed against one woman's tears.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","kan","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 149","lb":"A149","poet":"எருக்காட்டூர்த் தாயங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் தன்நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லிச் செலவழுங்கியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"தன்நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லிச் செலவழுங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சிதலை","சிதல்",1,"pira","scene","termite"],["இருப்பைத்","இருப்பை",3,"maram","scene",""],["எண்கின்","எண்கு",4,"maa","scene","bear"],["கறியொடு","கறி",10,"unavu","scene","pepper"],["யானை","யானை",13,"maa","scene",""],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",15,"pul","scene",""],["நீலத்து","நீலம்",17,"maram","simile","blue lily"]],"en":[["king","சேரலர்",7],["river","சுள்ளியம் பேரியாற்று",8],["town","முசிறி",11],["king","செழியன்",13],["town","கூடற்",14],["hill","நெடியோன் குன்றத்து",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:150":{"book":"akananuru","num":151,"t":["பின்னுவிட நெறித்த கூந்தலும், பொன்னென","ஆகத்து அரும்பிய சுணங்கும், வம்புவிடக்","கண்ணுருத்து எழுதரு முலையும் நோக்கி,","'எல்லினை பெரிது' எனப் பன்மாண் கூறிப்","பெருந்தோள் அடைய முயங்கி, நீடு நினைந்து,","அருங்கடிப் படுத்தனள் யாயே; கடுஞ்செலல்","வாட்சுறா வழங்கும் வளைமேய் பெருந்துறைக்,","கனைத்த நெய்தற் கண்போன் மாமலர்","நனைத்த செருந்திப் போதுவாய் அவிழ,","மாலை மணியிதழ் கூம்பக் காலைக்","கள்நாறு காவியொடு தண்ணென் மலருங்","கழியுங், கானலுங் காண்தொறும் பலபுலந்து;","வாரார் கொல்? எனப் பருவரும்-","தார் ஆர் மார்ப! நீ தணந்த ஞான்றே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man who comes for day meetings that the mother, seeing the girl's hair now loosed from its braid, the gold spots budding on her chest and her breasts rising, praised her, held her close, thought long, and put her under strict guard; and since he left, every time the girl sees the backwater and the shore grove where neythal closes at evening and opens with the kaavi at morning, she grieves, asking whether he will not come.","n":"The mother's praise and embrace are themselves the act of confinement: the signs of the girl's coming of age are what close the house to her. The flowers that close at dusk and open at dawn mark the days she counts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","varaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 150","lb":"A150","poet":"குறுவழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறி வந்துகண்ணுற்று நீங்கும் தலைமகனைத் தோழி தலைமகளை இடத்துய்த்து வந்து, செறிப்பறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","செறிப்பறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வாட்சுறா","சுறா",7,"pira","scene","sawfish"],["வளைமேய்","வளை",7,"pira","scene","conch"],["நெய்தற்","நெய்தல்",8,"maram","scene",""],["செருந்திப்","செருந்தி",9,"maram","scene",""],["காவியொடு","காவி",11,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:151":{"book":"akananuru","num":152,"t":["'தம்நயந்து உறைவோர்த் தாங்கித் தாம்நயந்து","இன்னமர் கேளிரொடு ஏமுறக் கெழீஇ,","நகுதல் ஆற்றார் நல்கூர்ந் தோர்!' என,","மிகுபொருள் நினையும் நெஞ்சமொடு அருள்பிறிது","ஆபமன் - வாழி, தோழி ! கால் விரிபு","உறுவளி எறிதொறும் கலங்கிய பொறிவரிக்","கலைமான் தலையின் முதன்முதற் கவர்த்த","கோடலம் கவட்ட குறுங்கால் உழுஞ்சில்","தாறுசினை விளைந்த நெற்றம், ஆடுமகள்","அரிக்கோற் பறையின், ஐயென ஒலிக்கும்","பதுக்கை ஆய செதுக்கை நீழற்,","கள்ளி முள்ளரைப் பொருந்திச் செல்லுநர்க்கு","உறுவது கூறுஞ், சிறுசெந் நாவின்","மணிஓர்த் தன்ன தெண்குரல்","கணிவாய்ப் பல்லிய காடிறந் தோரே!"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the man, holding that the poor cannot support those who depend on them nor laugh at ease among their kin, has a heart set on great wealth for which grace counts for nothing, and has crossed the forest where uzhinjil pods rattle like a dancer's drum in the wind, and a small red-tongued lizard by a cairn's withered shade tells travellers what will befall them.","n":"She quotes his reason fairly before judging it: the maxim is about duty to dependants and kin, not greed, and her complaint is only that it leaves no room for grace (அருள் பிறிது). The cairn and the omen-lizard put death and chance on the road his reason sends him down.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","inmai","selvam","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 151","lb":"A151","poet":"காவன் முல்லைப் பூதரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண்வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவின்கண்வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கலைமான்","கலைமான்",7,"maa","simile","stag"],["உழுஞ்சில்","உழுஞ்சில்",8,"maram","scene",""],["பறையின்","பறை",10,"parai","simile",""],["கள்ளி","கள்ளி",12,"maram","scene",""],["பல்லிய","பல்லி",15,"pira","scene","lizard"]],"en":[["herostone","பதுக்கை",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:152":{"book":"akananuru","num":153,"t":["நெஞ்சுநடுங்கு அரும்படர் தீர வந்து,","குன்றுழை நண்ணிய சீறூர் ஆங்கண்","செலீஇய பெயர்வோள் வணர்சுரி ஐம்பால்-","நுண்கோல் அகவுநர்ப் புரந்த பேரிசைச்","சினங்கெழு தானைத் தித்தன் வெளியன்","இரங்குநீர்ப் பரப்பின் கானலம் பெருந்துறைத்,","தனம்தரு நன்கலம் சிதையத் தாக்கும்","சிறுவெள் இறவின் குப்பை அன்ன","உறுபகை தரூஉம் மொய்ம்மூசு பிண்டன்","முனைமுரண் உடையக் கடந்த வென்வேல்,","இசைநல் ஈகைக் களிறுவீசு வண்மகிழ்ப்","பாரத்துத் தலைவன், ஆர நன்னன்;","ஏழில் நெடுவரைப் பாழிச் சிலம்பிற்","களிமயிற் கலாவத் தன்ன தோளே-","வல்வில் இளையர் பெருமகன்; நள்ளி","சோலை அடுக்கத்துச் சுரும்புஉண விரிந்த","கடவுட் காந்தள் உள்ளும் பலவுடன்","இறும்பூது கஞலிய ஆய்மலர் நாறி-","வல்லினும் வல்லார் ஆயினும் சென்றோர்க்குச்","சாலவிழ் நெடுங்குழி நிறைய வீசும்,","மாஅல் யானை ஆஅய் கானத்துத்","தலையாற்று நிலைஇய சேயுயர் பிறங்கல்","வேயமைக் கண்ணிடை புரைஇச்","சேய ஆயினும், நடுங்குதுயர் தருமே"],"p":"","r":"Leaving the night tryst, the man tells his heart that the woman who came to end his trembling grief and is now going home to her small hill village, her curled hair like the peacock's train on the Ezhil hill at Nannan's Paazhi, her shoulders fragrant beyond the kaandhal of Nalli's slopes and smooth as the bamboo on the crags of Aay's forest, still gives him trembling pain though she is far away.","n":"The chain of patrons (Thithan Veliyan's shore, Pindan defeated by Nannan, Nalli, Aay) is used only to grade her beauty: every great man's land supplies one feature. The phrase about Aay, who gives to all who come 'whether able or not', is a patron's praise carried whole into a love poem.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","azhagu","pirivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 152","lb":"A152","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிவந்து நீங்குந்தலைமகன்தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறிவந்து நீங்குந்தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["இறவின்","இறா",8,"pira","simile","prawn"],["மயிற்","மயில்",14,"pul","simile",""],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",16,"pira","scene","bee"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",17,"maram","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",21,"maa","scene",""],["வேயமைக்","வேய்",23,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[["king","தித்தன் வெளியன்",5],["king","பிண்டன்",9],["king","நன்னன்",12],["hill","ஏழில் நெடுவரை",13],["town","பாழிச்",13],["patron","நள்ளி",15],["patron","ஆஅய்",21]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:153":{"book":"akananuru","num":154,"t":["நோகோ யானே; நோதகும் உள்ளம்;","அம்தீங் கிளவி ஆயமொடு கெழீஇப்,","பந்துவழிப் படர்குவள் ஆயினும், நொந்துநனி","வெம்புமன், அளியள் தானே - இனியே,","வன்க ணாளன் மார்புஉற வளஇ,","இன்சொற் பிணிப்ப நம்பி, நம்கண்","உறுதரு விழுமம் உள்ளாள், ஒய்யெனத்","தெறுகதிர் உலைஇய வேனில் வெங்காட்டு,","உறுவளி ஒலிகழைக் கண்ணுறுபு தீண்டலின்","பொறிபிதிர்பு எடுத்த பொங்கெழு கூர் எரிப்","பைதறு சிமையப் பயம்நீங்கு ஆர்இடை","நல் அடிக்கு அமைந்த அல்ல; மெல்லியல்","வல்லுநள் கொல்லோ தானே- எல்லி","ஓங்குவரை அடுக்கத்து உயர்ந்த சென்னி","மீனொடு பொலிந்த வானின் தோன்றித்","தேம்பாய்ந்து ஆர்க்குந் தெரியிணர்க் கோங்கின்","காலுறக் கழன்ற கள்கமழ் புதுமலர்","கைவிடு சுடரின் தோன்றும்","மைபடு மாமலை விலங்கிய சுரனே?"],"p":"","r":"The foster mother grieves that the girl, who used to tire even chasing a ball with her sweet-voiced friends, has now trusted a hard man's chest and sweet words and gone without a thought for their sorrow into the summer forest where wind-struck bamboo throws up sparks and fire; she asks whether those soft feet can bear the barren desert under the dark hill, where at night kongu flowers fall like lamps let slip from the hand against a starry sky.","n":"The only beauty in the poem is at the end, and it is a night image of falling lights on the hill the girl must pass under: the mother's fear and her eye for the land are in the same line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 153","lb":"A153","poet":"சேரமான் இளங்குட்டுவன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கியசெவிலித்தாய் சொற்றது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட் போக்கியசெவிலித்தாய்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஒலிகழைக்","கழை",9,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["கோங்கின்","கோங்கு",16,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:154":{"book":"akananuru","num":155,"t":["படுமழை பொழிந்த பயமிகு புறவின்","நெடுநீர் அவல பகுவாய்த் தேரை","சிறுபல் இயத்தின் நெடுநெறிக் கறங்கக்","குறும்புதற் பிடவின் நெடுங்கால் அலரி","செந்நில மருங்கின் நுண்அயிர் வரிப்ப,","வெஞ்சின அரவின் பைஅணந் தன்ன","தண்கமழ் கோடல் தாதுபிணி அவிழத்,","திரிமருப்பு இரலை தெள்அறல் பருகிக்","காமர் துணையொடு ஏமுற வதியக்,","காடுகவின் பெற்ற தண்பதப் பெருவழி","ஓடுபரி மெலியாக் கொய்சுவற் புரவித்","தாள்தாழ் தார்மணி தயங்குபு இயம்ப","ஊர்மதி - வலவ! தேரே- சீர்மிகுபு","நம்வயிற் புரிந்த கொள்கை","அம்மா அரிவையைத் துன்னுகம், விரைந்தே"],"p":"","r":"His work done, the man bids the charioteer drive fast over the rain-washed woodland, where frogs croak like many small instruments, pidavu blossoms streak the red sand, kodal buds open like a cobra's raised hood, and the twist-horned stag drinks clear water and rests in safety with his mate, so they may hasten to the lovely woman whose ways are bent on him.","n":"The stag drinks and rests 'in safety' (ஏமுற) with his mate: the word for the beasts' ease is the word another homecoming poem uses for the man's own heart (Akananuru 34).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","mazhai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 154","lb":"A154","poet":"பொதும்பிற் புல்லாளங் கண்ணியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன்தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["தேரை","தேரை",2,"pira","simile","frog"],["பிடவின்","பிடவு",4,"maram","scene",""],["அரவின்","அரவு",6,"pira","simile","cobra"],["கோடல்","கோடல்",7,"maram","simile",""],["இரலை","இரலை",8,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["துணையொடு","துணை",9,"maa","iraicchi","doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","இரலை","confirmed","1/1","the man, his work done, means to come home and rest with his wife as the stag does with his mate"]],"ch":"a:8"},"a:155":{"book":"akananuru","num":156,"t":["'அறன்கடைப் படாஅ வாழ்க்கையும், என்றும்","பிறன்கடைச் செலாஅச் செல்வமும், இரண்டும்","பொருளின் ஆகும், புனையிழை!' என்றுநம்","இருளேர் ஐம்பால் நீவி யோரே-","நோய்நாம் உழக்குவம் ஆயினும், தாந்தம்","செய்வினை முடிக்க தோழி ! பல்வயின்","பயநிரை சேர்ந்த பாண்நாட்டு ஆங்கண்","நெடுவிளிக் கோவலர் கூவல் தோண்டிய","கொடுவாய்ப் பத்தல் வார்ந்துகு சிறுகுழி,","நீர்காய் வருத்தமொடு சேர்விடம் பெறாது","பெருங்களிறு மிதித்த அடியகத்து, இரும்புலி","ஒதுங்குவன கழிந்த செதும்பல் ஈர்வழி,","செயிர்தீர் நாவின் வயிரியர் பின்றை","மண்ஆர் முழவின் கண்ணகத்து அசைத்த","விரலூன்று வடுவில் தோன்றும்","மரல்வாடு மருங்கின் மலைஇறந் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the man who stroked her hair saying 'A life that never comes to the door of wrong, and wealth that never goes to another's door: both come from wealth, my jewelled one' may finish his work though she suffers; he has crossed the hills where the marul withers, where in Paan country the tiger's pugmarks in an elephant's footprint by a herdsmen's dried well look like finger marks pressed into a drum's head.","n":"She consents in his own words, and the consent is real (தாந்தம் செய்வினை முடிக்க), but the two goods his maxim names are virtue and independence; the pleasure of the household, which his going costs, is not among them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","aram","porai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 155","lb":"A155","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங் கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின் கண்வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவின் கண்வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பயநிரை","நிரை",7,"maa","scene","cattle"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",8,"seythi","scene","herdsmen"],["பெருங்களிறு","களிறு",11,"maa","scene",""],["இரும்புலி","புலி",11,"maa","scene",""],["முழவின்","முழவு",14,"parai","scene",""],["மரல்வாடு","மரல்",16,"maram","absent",""]],"en":[["town","பாண்நாட்டு",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:156":{"book":"akananuru","num":157,"t":["முரசுடைச் செல்வர் புரவிச் சூட்டும்","மூட்டுறு கவரி தூக்கி யன்ன","செழுஞ்செய் நெல்லின் சேயரிப் புனிற்றுக் கதிர்","மூதா தின்றல் அஞ்சிக், காவலர்","பாகல் ஆய்கொடிப் பகன்றையொடு பரீஇக்","காஞ்சியின் அகத்துக், கரும்பருத்தி, யாக்கும்","தீம்புனல் ஊர! திறவதாகக்","குவளை உண்கண் இவளும் யானும்","கழனி ஆம்பல் முழுநெறிப் பைந்தழை","காயா ஞாயிற் றாகத், தலைப்பெய,","'பொய்தல் ஆடிப் பொலிக!' என வந்து,","நின்நகாப் பிழைத்த தவறே - பெரும!","கள்ளுங் கண்ணியும் கையுறை யாக","நிலைக்கோட்டு வெள்ளை நாள்செவிக் கிடாஅய்","நிலைத்துறைக் கடவுட்கு உளப்பட ஓச்சித்,","தணிமருங்கு அறியாள், யாய்அழ,","மணிமருள் மேனி பொன்னிறம் கொளலே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the town, where guards fence the drooping rice ears from an old cow with gourd vines and cotton, that since she and the girl, playing in the fields in leaf skirts of ambal, met him, the girl's gem-dark skin has turned gold with pallor, and her mother, weeping and finding no cure, offers toddy, garlands and a white goat to the god of the ford: was it their fault?","n":"The mother treats the pallor as a god's seizure and sacrifices for it; the friend lets the man know that only he can cure it. Line 12 (நின்நகாப் பிழைத்த தவறே) is hard and we cannot fix whose laughing or not laughing is the fault. The guarded rice may point to the girl's guarded youth, but the poem does not name the guarding, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","pasalai","kalavu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 156","lb":"A156","poet":"ஆவூர் மூலங்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலை மகளைஇடத்துய்த்துவந்த தோழி தலைமகனை வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நெல்லின்","நெல்",3,"unavu","scene",""],["மூதா","ஆ",4,"maa","scene","old cow"],["காவலர்","காவலர்",4,"seythi","scene","field guards"],["பாகல்","பாகல்",5,"maram","scene",""],["பகன்றையொடு","பகன்றை",5,"maram","scene",""],["காஞ்சியின்","காஞ்சி",6,"maram","scene",""],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",9,"maram","ornament",""],["கள்ளுங்","கள்",13,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கிடாஅய்","கிடாய்",14,"maa","gift_food","goat"],["கடவுட்கு","கடவுள்",15,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:8"},"a:157":{"book":"akananuru","num":158,"t":["அரியற் பெண்டிர் அலகுற் கொண்ட","பகுவாய்ப் பாளைக் குவிமுலை சுரந்த","வரிநிறக் கலுழி ஆர மாந்திச்","செருவேட்டுச், சிலைக்கும் செங்கண் ஆடவர்,","வில்லிட வீழ்ந்தோர் பதுக்கைக் கோங்கின்","எல்லி மலர்ந்த பைங்கொடி அதிரல்","பெரும்புலர் வைகறை அரும்பொடு வாங்கிக்","கான யானை கவளங் கொள்ளும்","அஞ்சுவரு நெறியிடைத் தமியர் சென்மார்","நெஞ்சுண மொழிப மன்னே - தோழி","முனைபுலம் பெயர்த்த புல்லென் மன்றத்துப்","பெயலுற நெகிழ்ந்து, வெயிலுறச் சாஅய்","வினையழி பாவையின் உலறி,","மனைஒழிந் திருத்தல் வல்லு வோர்க்கே!"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that men who mean to go alone down the fearful road, where red-eyed bowmen drunk on palm toddy roar for war and a forest elephant at dawn eats the athiral creeper that bloomed by night over the cairns of the slain, would speak such heart-consuming words only to women able to stay home, withering like a ruined painted doll in the deserted square of a raided village.","n":"She defines the woman who can be left as a thing already ruined, a doll rotted by rain and sun in an emptied village; by implication she is not that woman, and his words are wasted on her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","melivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 157","lb":"A157","poet":"வேம்பாற்றூர்க் குமரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவுணர்த்தியதோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவுணர்த்தியதோழிக்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பாளைக்","பாளை",2,"maram","scene","palm spathe"],["கலுழி","கலுழி",3,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",4,"seythi","scene","bowmen"],["கோங்கின்","கோங்கு",5,"maram","scene",""],["அதிரல்","அதிரல்",6,"maram","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",8,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["herostone","பதுக்கைக்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:158":{"book":"akananuru","num":159,"t":["'உருமுரறு கருவிய பெருமழை தலைஇப்","பெயல்ஆன்று அவிந்த தூங்கிருள் நடுநாள்,","மின்னு நிமிர்ந்தன்ன கனங்குழை இமைப்பப்,","பின்னுவிடு நெறியிற் கிளைஇய கூந்தலள்,","வரைஇழி மயிலின் ஒல்குவனள் ஒதுங்கி,","மிடைஊர்பு இழியக்கண்டனென், இவள் என","அலையல் - வாழிவேண்டு அன்னை!- நம் படப்பைச்","சூருடைச் சிலம்பில், சுடர்ப்பூ வேய்ந்து","தாம்வேண்டு உருவின் அணங்குமார் வருமே;","நனவின் வாயே போலத் துஞ்சுநர்க்","கனவாண்டு மருட்டலும் உண்டே: இவள்தான்","சுடரின்று தமியளும் பனிக்கும்: வெருவர","மன்ற மராஅத்த கூகை குழறினும்","நெஞ்சழிந்து அரணஞ் சேரும்: அதன்தலைப்","புலிக்கணத் தன்ன நாய்தொடர் விட்டு,","முருகன் அன்ன சீற்றத்துக் கடுந்திறல்","எந்தையும் இல்லன் ஆக,","அஞ்சுவள் அல்லளோ, இவளிது செயலே!"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening nearby, the friend tells the foster mother not to torment the girl for what she says she saw at midnight after the storm, earrings flashing like lightning, swaying like a peacock coming down a hill: on their garden hill spirits come in any form they please and dreams fool sleepers like waking truth, and this girl shivers alone without a lamp, runs for shelter when the owl hoots in the kadamba, and with their fierce father away would she ever dare such a thing?","n":"The defence offers two explanations (a hill spirit, a dream) and a character witness (her timidity), and every word of it also reaches the hidden lover: the mother has seen, and the night meetings cannot go on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","anangu","kanavu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 158","lb":"A158","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன்சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழி செவிலித்தாய்க்குச்சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தலைமகன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["மயிலின்","மயில்",5,"pul","simile",""],["சுடர்ப்பூ","சுடர்ப்பூ",8,"maram","ornament",""],["அணங்குமார்","அணங்கு",9,"deyvam","scene",""],["மராஅத்த","மரா",13,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["கூகை","கூகை",13,"pul","scene","owl"],["புலிக்கணத்","புலி",15,"maa","simile",""],["நாய்தொடர்","நாய்",15,"maa","scene","hunting dogs"],["முருகன்","முருகன்",16,"deyvam","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:159":{"book":"akananuru","num":160,"t":["தெண்கழி விளைந்த வெண்கல் உப்பின்","கொள்ளை சாற்றிய கொடுநுக ஒழுகை","உரறுடைச் சுவல பகடுபல பரப்பி","உமண் உயிர்த்து இறந்த ஒழிகல் அடுப்பின்","வடியுறு பகழிக் கொடுவில் ஆடவர்","அணங்குடை நோன்சிலை வணங்க வாங்கிப்","பல்ஆன் நெடுநிரை தழீஇக் கல்லென","அருமுனை அலைத்த பெரும்புகல் வலத்தர்,","கனைகுரற் கடுந்துடிப் பாணி தூங்கி,","உவலைக் கண்ணியர் ஊன்புழுக்கு அயரும்","கவலை, 'காதலர் இறந்தனர்' என, நனி","அவலம் கொள்ளன்மா காதல் அம் தோழி!","விசும்பின் நல்லேறு சிலைக்குஞ் சேட்சிமை","நறும்பூஞ் சாரற் குறும்பொறைக் குணாஅது","வில்கெழு தடக்கை வெல்போர் வானவன்","மிஞிறுமூசு கவுள சிறுகண் யானைத்","தொடியுடைத் தடமறுப்பு ஒடிய நூறிக்","கொடுமுடி காக்குங் குரூஉக்கண் நெடுமதில்","சேண்விளங்கு சிறப்பின் - ஆமூர் எய்தினும்,","ஆண்டமைந்து உறையுநர் அல்லர், நின்","பூண்தாங்கு ஆகம் பொருந்துதன் மறந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman not to grieve that her lover has crossed the forked road where bowmen, back from raiding cattle on the frontier, cook meat to the beat of the tudi beside the cold hearth stones left by salt carters; even if he reached Aamur with its high walls east of Vaanavan's hill, where that king broke the tusks of elephants, he is not a man to stay there and forget her breast.","n":"The road's dangers are named only to be set aside: the comfort rests not on the road being safe but on the man's attachment outlasting even a famous city.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 159","lb":"A159","poet":"ஆமூர்க் கவுதமன் சாதேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்டதலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["உப்பின்","உப்பு",1,"pira","scene","salt"],["பகடுபல","பகடு",3,"maa","scene","ox"],["உமண்","உமணர்",4,"seythi","scene","salt merchants"],["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",5,"seythi","scene","bowmen"],["பல்ஆன்","ஆன்",7,"maa","scene","cattle"],["கடுந்துடிப்","துடி",9,"parai","scene",""],["ஊன்புழுக்கு","ஊன்",10,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["யானைத்","யானை",16,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["king","வானவன்",15],["town","ஆமூர்",19]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:8"},"a:160":{"book":"akananuru","num":161,"t":["ஒடுங்கீர் ஓதி நினக்கும் அற்றே?","நடுங்கின்று, அளித்தென் நிறையில் நெஞ்சம்","அடும்புகொடி சிதைய வாங்கிக் கொடுங்கழிக்","குப்பை வெண்மணற் பக்கம் சேர்த்தி,","நிறைச்சூல் யாமை மறைத்து ஈன்று புதைத்த","கோட்டுவட்டு உருவின் புலவுநாறு முட்டைப்","பார்புஇடன் ஆகும் அளவை, பகுவாய்க்","கணவன் ஓம்பும் கானலஞ் சேர்ப்பன்","முள்ளுறின் சிறத்தல் அஞ்சி, மெல்ல","வாவுஉடை மையின் வள்பிற் காட்டி,","ஏத்தொழில் நவின்ற எழில்நடைப் புரவி","செழுநீர்த் தண்கழி நீந்தலின், ஆழி","நுதிமுகங் குறைந்த பொதிமுகிழ் நெய்தல்,","பாம்புஉயர் தலையின் சாம்புவன நிவப்ப,","இரவந் தன்றால் திண்தேர்; கரவாது","ஒல்லென ஒலிக்கும் இளையரொடு வல்வாய்","அரவச் சீறூர் காணப்","பகல்வந் தன்றல், பாய்பரி சிறந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the girl whether her heart too is trembling: the chariot of the lord of the shore, where the open-mouthed male turtle guards the eggs his mate buried in the white sand until they hatch, used to come at night, the driver easing the horse through the backwater so the wheel cut the neythal buds, which rose like snakes' raised heads; now it has come by day, openly, with shouting young men, for the noisy little village to see.","n":"Night against day, hiding against showing (கரவாது): the chariot's daylight arrival is the public sign of marriage, and the village whose gossip was the danger is now the witness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalavu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 160","lb":"A160","poet":"குமுழி ஞாழலார் நப்பசலையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி வரைவு மலிந்துசொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு மலிந்துசொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["அடும்புகொடி","அடும்பு",3,"maram","scene",""],["யாமை","யாமை",5,"pira","iraicchi","turtle"],["கணவன்","கணவன்",8,"pira","iraicchi","male turtle"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",13,"maram","simile",""],["பாம்புஉயர்","பாம்பு",14,"pira","simile","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கணவன்","confirmed","1/1","the man who now comes openly will guard her and their household as the turtle guards its brood"]],"ch":"a:8"},"a:161":{"book":"akananuru","num":162,"t":["வினைவயிற் பிரிதல் யாவது?- 'வணர்சுரி","வடியாப் பித்தை வன்கண், ஆடவர்","அடியமை பகழி ஆர வாங்கி;","வம்பலர்ச் செகுத்த அஞ்சுவரு கவலைப்","படுமுடை நசைஇய வாழ்க்கைச் செஞ்செவி","எருவைச் சேவல் ஈண்டுகிளை பயிரும்","வெருவரு கானம் நீந்தி, பொருள் புரிந்து","இறப்ப எண்ணினர்' என்பது சிறப்பக்","கேட்டனள் கொல்லோ தானே? தோழ் தாழ்பு","சுரும்பு உண ஒலிவரும் இரும்பல் கூந்தல்,","அம்மா மேனி, ஆயிழைக் குறுமகள்","சுணங்கு சூழ் ஆகத்து அணங்குஎன உருத்த","நல்வரல் இளமுலை நனைய;","பல்லிதழ் உண்கண் பரந்தன பனியே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man what this leaving for work can mean: has the girl already heard that he means to cross, for wealth, the frightening forest where hard-eyed archers kill travellers and the red-eared vulture calls its kin to the carrion, since her many-petalled eyes are already pouring tears onto her young breasts?","n":"The friend never reports the departure to the girl; she argues that the girl has read it in him before any word was spoken, so her tears are the friend's whole case for stopping him. The forest is given as the man's own reported resolve (பொருள் புரிந்து இறப்ப எண்ணினர்), set inside quotation, as if overheard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kurippu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 161","lb":"A161","poet":"மதுரைப் புல்லங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவுணர்த்தியதோழி, தலைமகளதுவேறுபாடு கண்டு முன்னமேஉணர்ந்தாள் நம்பெருமாட்டியென்று தலைமகனைச்செலவு விலக்கியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தலைமகனைச்செலவு விலக்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["எருவைச் சேவல்","எருவை",6,"pul","scene","vulture"],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",10,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:9"},"a:162":{"book":"akananuru","num":163,"t":["கொளக்குறை படாஅக் கோடுவளர் குட்டத்து","அளப்பு அரிது ஆகிய குவைஇருந் தோன்றல,","கடல்கண் டன்ன மாக விசும்பின்","அழற்கொடி அன்ன மின்னுவசிபு நுடங்கக்","கடிதுஇடி உருமொடு கதழுறை சிதறி","விளிவுஇடன் அறியா வான்உமிழ் நடுநாள்","அருங்கடிக் காவலர் இகழ்பதம் நோக்கிப்,","பனிமயங்கு அசைவளி அலைப்பத், தந்தை","நெடுநகர் ஒருசிறை நின்றனென் ஆக;","அறல்என அவிரும் கூந்தல் மலர்என","வாண்முகத்து அலமரும் மாஇதழ் மழைக்கண்,","முகைநிரைத் தன்ன மாவீழ் வெண்பல்;","நகைமாண்டு இலங்கும் நலம்கெழு துவர்வாய்க்,","கோல்அமை விழுத்தொடி விளங்க வீசிக்,","கால்உறு தளிரின் நடுங்கி ஆனாது,","நோய்அசா வீட முயங்கினன் - வாய்மொழி","நல்லிசை தரூஉம் இரவலர்க்கு உள்ளிய","நசைபிழைப்பு அறியாக் கழல்தொடி அதிகன்","கோள்அறவு அறியாப் பயம்கெழு பலவின்","வேங்கை சேர்ந்த வெற்பகம் பொலிய,","வில்கெழு தானைப் பசும்பூண் பாண்டியன்","களிறணி வெல்கொடி கடுப்பக் காண்வர","ஒளிறுவன இழிதரும் உயர்ந்துதோன்று அருவி","நேர்கொள் நெடுவரை கவா அன்","சூரா மகளிரிற் பெறற்குஅரி யோளே"],"p":"","r":"Leaving after a night meeting, the man tells his heart how, at midnight in a storm whose lightning split a sky like the sea, he stood by one corner of her father's guarded house until the guards slackened, and she came, trembling like a shoot in the wind, and held him till his sickness eased: she who is as hard to win as the goddesses of the high slope where waterfalls shine like the Pandiyan's flag, on the hill of Athigan who never fails a suppliant.","n":"The danger he braved (the storm, the guards, the father's house) is the proof of love he offers his own heart, and the closing image places her among the hill goddesses, beyond reach even while just embraced. Lines 1 and 2 are obscure: they seem to describe the unmeasurable dark mass of cloud drawn from the deep, but the word division is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kalaviyal","kuri","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 162","lb":"A162","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிக்கண்தலைமகளைக் கண்ணுற்று நீங்கிய தலைமகன் தன்நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறிக்கண்தலைமகளைக் கண்ணுற்று நீங்கிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மலர்என","மலர்",10,"maram","simile",""],["முகைநிரைத்","முகை",12,"maram","simile",""],["தளிரின்","தளிர்",15,"maram","simile",""],["பலவின்","பலா",19,"maram","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",20,"maram","scene",""],["களிறணி","களிறு",22,"maa","simile",""],["அருவி","அருவி",23,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["சூரா மகளிரிற்","சூர் அர மகளிர்",25,"deyvam","simile","hill goddesses"]],"en":[["patron","அதிகன்",18],["king","பாண்டியன்",21]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:9"},"a:163":{"book":"akananuru","num":164,"t":["விண்அதிர்பு தலைஇய விரவுமலர் குழையத்","தண்மழை பொழிந்த தாழ்பெயற் கடைநாள்,","எமியம் ஆகத் துனிஉளம் கூரச்","சென்றோர் உள்ளிச் சில்வளை நெகிழப்","பெருநகை உள்ளமொடு வருநசை நோக்கி","விளியும் எவ்வமொடு 'அளியள்' என்னாது","களிறுஉயிர்த் தன்ன கண்அழி துவலை","முளரி கரியும் முன்பனிப் பானாள்,","குன்றுநெகிழ்பு அன்ன குளிர்கொள் வாடை!","எனக்கே வந்தனை போறி ! புனற்கால்","அயிர்இடு குப்பையின் நெஞ்சு நெகிழ்ந்து அவிழக்,","கொடியோர் சென்ற தேஎத்து, மடியாது","இனையை ஆகிச் செல்மதி;","வினைவிதுப் புறுநர் உள்ளலும் உண்டே!"],"p":"","r":"Alone at the end of the rains, bangles slipping as she thinks of the man who went, the woman turns on the cold north wind of the early-dew midnight, whose spray like an elephant's breath scorches the lotus: it seems to have come for her alone, so let it go, as unrelenting as she is, to the land where the cruel one went; those eager in their work may yet remember.","n":"She sends her tormentor as her messenger: the wind that melts her heart like a heap of fine sand in a stream is told to do the same to him. The last line is a hope that slips out under the anger: men intent on work also remember.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thoothu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 163","lb":"A163","poet":"கழார்க்கீர னெயிற்றியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவின்கண்வற்புறுக்குந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் ஆற்றாமைமீதூரச் சொல்லியது.","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","ஆற்றாமைமீதூரச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["விரவுமலர்","மலர்",1,"maram","scene",""],["சில்வளை","வளை",4,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["களிறுஉயிர்த்","களிறு",7,"maa","simile",""],["முளரி","முளரி",8,"maram","scene","lotus"],["வாடை","வாடை",9,"pira","scene","north wind"],["அயிர்இடு","அயிர்",11,"pira","simile","fine sand"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:9"},"a:164":{"book":"akananuru","num":165,"t":["கதிர்கை யாக வாங்கி ஞாயிறு","பைதறத் தெறுதலின் பயங்கரந்து மாறி,","விடுவாய்ப் பட்ட வியன்கண் மாநிலம்","காடுகவின் எதிரக் கனைபெயல் பொழிதலின்","பொறிவரி இனவண்டு ஆர்ப்பப் பலவுடன்","நறுவீ முல்லையொடு தோன்றி தோன்ற'","வெறியேன் றன்றே வீகமழ் கானம்-","'எவன்கொல் மற்று அவர் நிலை?' என மயங்கி,","இகுபனி உறைக்குங் கண்ணொடு இனைபு ஆங்கு","இன்னாது உறைவி தொன்னலம் பெறூஉம்","இதுநற் காலம்; கண்டிசின் - பகைவர்","மதின்முகம் முருக்கிய தொடிசிதை மருப்பின்;","கந்துகால் ஒசிக்கும் யானை;","வெஞ்சின வேந்தன் வினைவிடப் பெறினே!"],"p":"","r":"In the war camp the man tells his heart that the rains have restored the sun-cracked land, bees hum over mullai and glory lilies and the forest is fragrant: this is the season when she who lives in pain, eyes dripping, wondering how he fares, would regain her old beauty, if only the fierce king would release them from the work of his fort-breaking elephants.","n":"The season he promised has come and he is held by another's war: the elephant that smashes forts and snaps its tethering post is the king's power, and also an image of the restraint he himself cannot break.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","kaalam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 164","lb":"A164","poet":"மதுரைத் தமிழ்க்கூத்தன் நாகன்றேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பாசறைக்கண் இருந்ததலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பாசறைக்கண் இருந்ததலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வண்டு","வண்டு",5,"pira","scene","bee"],["முல்லையொடு","முல்லை",6,"maram","scene",""],["தோன்றி","தோன்றி",6,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["யானை","யானை",13,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:9"},"a:165":{"book":"akananuru","num":166,"t":["கயந்தலை மடப்பிடி பயம்பில் பட்டெனக்","களிறுவிளிப் படுத்த கம்பலை வெரீஇ,","ஒய்யென எந்த செவ்வாய்க் குழவி","தாதுஎரு மறுகின் மூதூர் ஆங்கண்","எருமை நல் ஆன் பெறுமுலை மாந்தும்","நாடுபல இறந்த நன்ன ராட்டிக்கு","ஆயமும் அணிஇழந்து அழுங்கின்று! தாயும்","'இன்தோள் தாராய், இறீஇயர்என் உயிர்! என,","கண்ணும் நுதலும் நீவித், தண்ணெனத்,","தடவுநிலை நொச்சி வரிநிழல் அசைஇத்,","தாழிக் குவளை வாடுமலர் சூட்டித்,","'தருமணற் கிடந்த பாவைஎன்","அருமக ளே' என முயங்கினள் அழுமே!"],"p":"","r":"Onlookers describe the house after the daughter has gone off across many lands with her lover: her playmates have put off their ornaments and mourn, and the mother, crying 'give me your sweet shoulder, let my life end', strokes eyes and brow, rests in the striped shade of the nochi the girl used to touch, crowns her doll with fading kuvalai from the water-pot and embraces it weeping, 'this doll lying in the sand is my precious daughter'.","n":"Lines 1 to 5 describe the lands the girl crossed: an elephant calf, frightened by the male's trumpeting when its mother fell into a pit, drinking from a buffalo's udder in an old town. Read as a hidden sense (the young parted from its mother and fed at another's breast) it would mirror the daughter, but the syntax of line 3 (எந்த) is unclear, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["udanpokku","makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 165","lb":"A165","poet":"கழார்க்கீர னெயிற்றியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட்போக்கிய தாயதுநிலைமை கண்டார் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["kandor","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட்போக்கிய தாயதுநிலைமை கண்டார் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",1,"maa","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",2,"maa","scene",""],["குழவி","குழவி",3,"maa","scene","elephant calf"],["எருமை","எருமை",5,"maa","scene",""],["நொச்சி","நொச்சி",10,"maram","scene",""],["குவளை","குவளை",11,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:9"},"a:166":{"book":"akananuru","num":167,"t":["'நல்மரங் குழீஇய நனைமுதிர் சாடி","பல்நாள் அரித்த கோஒய் உடைப்பின்,","மயங்குமழைத் துவலையின் மறுகுடன் பனிக்கும்","பழம்பல் நெல்லின் வேளூர் வாயில்,","நறுவிரை தெளித்த நாறிணர் மாலைப்,","பொறிவரி இனவண்டு ஊதல கழியும்","உயர்பலி பெறூஉம் உருகெழு தெய்வம்,","புனைஇருங் கதுப்பின் நீகடுத் தோள்வயின்","அனையேன் ஆயின், அணங்குக, என்!' என","மனையோட் தேற்றும் மகிழ்நன் ஆயின்,","யார்கொல்- வாழி, தோழி !- நெருநல்","தார்பூண் களிற்றின் தலைப்புணை தழீஇ,","வதுவை ஈர்அணிப் பொலிந்து, நம்மொடு,","புதுவது வந்த காவிரிக்","கோடுதோய் மலிர்நிறை, ஆடி யோரே?"],"p":"","r":"The other woman mocks the husband who soothes his wife with an oath ('may the fearsome god at the gate of Velur strike me if I was ever at that woman's shoulder'): if that is he, then who was it, friend, that yesterday clasped the head-float with us and, decked as for a wedding, bathed in the Kaveri's new flood?","n":"She speaks so that the wife's people will hear, turning the god he swore by against him; the oath is set at Velur, where broken toddy jars chill the street like rain, so the shrine's grandeur makes the perjury worse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","sool","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 166","lb":"A166","poet":"இடையன் நெடுங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தையொடு புனல்ஆடிய தலைமகன் தலைமகளிடைப் புக்கு யான்-ஆடிற்றிலன் என்று சூளுற்றான் என்பது கேட்ட 3பரத்தைதன் பாங்காயினார் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi","pira","karpu","பாங்காயினார் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["நெல்லின்","நெல்",4,"unavu","scene",""],["நாறிணர் மாலை","மாலை",5,"maram","ornament","garland"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene","bee"],["தெய்வம்","தெய்வம்",7,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[["town","வேளூர்",4],["river","காவிரி",14]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:9"},"a:167":{"book":"akananuru","num":168,"t":["வயங்குமணி பொருத வகையமை வனப்பின்","பசுங்காழ் அல்குல் மாஅயோ ளொடு","வினைவனப்பு எய்திய புனைபூஞ் சேக்கை,","விண்பொரு நெடுநகர்த் தங்கி, இன்றே","இனிதுடன் கழிந்தன்று மன்னே; நாளைப்","பொருந்தாக் கண்ணேம் புலம்வந்து உறுதரச்","சேக்குவம் கொல்லோ, நெஞ்சே! சாத்துஎறிந்து","அதர்கூட் டுண்ணும் அணங்குடைப் பகழிக்","கொடுவில் ஆடவர் படுபகை வெரீஇ","ஊர்எழுந்து உலறிய பீர்எழு முதுபாழ்","முருங்கை மேய்ந்த பெருங்கை யானை","வெரிந்ஓங்கு சிறுபுறம் உரிஞ ஒல்கி","இட்டிகை நெடுஞ்சுவர் விட்டம் வீழ்ந்தென,","மணிப்புறாத் துறந்த மரம்சோர் மாடத்து","எழுதுஅணி கடவுள் போகலின், புல்லென்று","ஒழுகுபலி மறந்த மெழுகாப் புன் திணைப்","பால்நாய் துள்ளிய பறைக்கட் சிற்றில்,","குயில்காழ் சிதைய மண்டி, அயில்வாய்க்","கூர்முகச் சிதலை வேய்ந்த","போர்மடி நல்இறைப் பொதியி லானே!"],"p":"","r":"Pressed by his heart to go for wealth, the man asks it: after tonight, spent sweetly with his dark-skinned wife in a sky-touching mansion, shall they tomorrow lie sleepless in the ruined public hall of a village the people fled for fear of caravan-raiding archers, where an elephant rubbing its back has toppled the brick wall, the doves have left, the painted god has gone and the unswept floor forgets its offerings, and termites roof the rafters? So he gives up the journey.","n":"The ruin is built point by point against the bedroom: gems against termites, a made bed against a cracked floor, a full house against one even its god has deserted. The desolation of the road is what keeps him home, not her plea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","paazh","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 167","lb":"A167","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன்பொருள்கடைக்கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லிச்செலவு அழுங்கியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","செலவு அழுங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",9,"seythi","scene",""],["பீர்","பீர்",10,"maram","scene",""],["முருங்கை","முருங்கை",11,"maram","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",11,"maa","scene",""],["மணிப்புறா","புறா",14,"pul","absent",""],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",15,"deyvam","absent",""],["பால்நாய்","நாய்",17,"maa","scene",""],["சிதலை","சிதலை",19,"pira","scene","termite"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:9"},"a:168":{"book":"akananuru","num":169,"t":["யாமம் நும்மொடு கழிப்பி, நோய்மிக,","பனிவார் கண்ணேம் வைகுதும், இனியே:","ஆன்றல் வேண்டும் வான்தோய் வெற்ப!","பல்ஆன் குன்றில் படுநிழல் சேர்ந்த","நல்ஆன் பரப்பின் குழுமூர் ஆங்கண்","கொடைக்கடன் ஏன்ற கோடா நெஞ்சின்","உதியன் அட்டில் போல ஒலிஎழுந்து,","அருவி ஆர்க்கும் பெருவரைச் சிலம்பின்","ஈன்றணி இரும்பிடி தழீஇக் களிறு தன்","தூங்குநடைக் குழவி துயில்புறங் காப்ப,","ஒடுங்குஅளை புலம்பப் போகிக், கடுங்கண்","வாள்வரி வயப்புலி நன்முழை உரற,","கானவர் மடிந்த கங்குல்,","மான்அதர்ச் சிறுநெறி வருதல், நீயே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hill-man that, having spent the night watch with him, they are left love-sick with wet eyes, and he must stop: will he still come at night, when the hunters sleep, down the narrow deer-path of the great slope where waterfalls roar like the kitchen of Udhiyan at Kuzhumur, where a male elephant, embracing the female who has just calved, guards the sleep of their swaying calf, and a fierce striped tiger roars in its cave?","n":"The path is drawn to frighten (the tiger, the dark) and to instruct (the elephant family): the danger argues against night visits, the guarded calf argues for a household.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kuri","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 168","lb":"A168","poet":"கோட்டம்பலத்துத் துஞ்சிய சேரமான்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி வந்ததலைமகளை இரவுக்குறி விலக்கி வரைவு கடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி விலக்கி வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["நல்ஆன்","ஆன்",5,"maa","simile","cattle"],["அருவி","அருவி",8,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["இரும்பிடி","பிடி",9,"maa","iraicchi",""],["களிறு","களிறு",9,"maa","iraicchi",""],["குழவி","குழவி",10,"maa","scene","elephant calf"],["வயப்புலி","புலி",12,"maa","scene",""],["கானவர்","கானவர்",13,"seythi","scene",""],["மான்","மான்",14,"maa","scene","deer"]],"en":[["town","குழுமூர்",5],["king","உதியன்",7]],"dv":[["iraicchi","களிறு","confirmed","1/1","the man who crosses this slope sees what he should be: a mate keeping a household, not a night visitor"]],"ch":"a:9"},"a:169":{"book":"akananuru","num":170,"t":["மரம்தலை கரிந்து நிலம்பயம் வாட,","அலங்குகதிர் வேய்ந்த அழல்திகழ் புனந்தலைப்,","புலிதொலைத்து உண்ட பெருங்களிற்று ஒழிஊன்","கலிகெழு மறவர் காழ்க்கோத்து ஒழிந்ததை","ஞெலிகோற் சிறுதீ மாட்டி, ஒலிதிரைக்","கடல்விளை அமிழ்தின் கணஞ்சால் உமணர்","சுனைகொள் தீநீர்ச் சோற்றுஉலைக் கூட்டும்","சுரம்பல கடந்த நம்வயின் படர்ந்து: நனி","பசலை பாய்ந்த மேனியள், நெடிது நினைந்து","செல்கதிர் மழுகிய புலம்புகொள் மாலை","மெல்விரல் சேர்த்திய நுதலள், மல்கிக்","கயலுமிழ் நீரின் கண்பனி வாரப்,","பெருந்தோள் நெகிழ்ந்த செல்லலொடு","வருந்துமால், அளியள் திருந்திழை தானே!"],"p":"","r":"Halfway across the waste the man tells his heart how she must be suffering: on a burnt clearing where hunters roast on a fire-drill blaze the elephant meat a tiger left and salt-carriers cook rice with spring water and the sea's nectar, he has crossed many such wastes, while she, pale and thinking long in the lonely dimming evening, fingers to her brow, tears running, shoulders wasted, grieves.","n":"Both halves are pictures of evening: the travellers' camp fire on the road and her vigil at home, so the man sees his own night and hers at once. The tears 'like water a kayal fish spits' turn her fish-shaped eyes into the simile's source.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 169","lb":"A169","poet":"தொண்டி ஆமூர்ச்சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன்,இடைச்சுரத்துத் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","இடைச்சுரத்துத் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புலி","புலி",3,"maa","scene",""],["பெருங்களிற்று","களிறு",3,"maa","scene",""],["மறவர்","மறவர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["உமணர்","உமணர்",6,"seythi","scene","salt merchants"],["கயலுமிழ்","கயல்",12,"pira","simile","carp"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:9"},"a:170":{"book":"akananuru","num":171,"t":["கானலும் கழறாது: கழியும் கூறாது:","தேன்இமிர் நறுமலர்ப் புன்னையும் மொழியாது:","ஒருநின் அல்லது பிறிதுயாதும் இலனே:","இருங்கழி மலர்ந்த கண்போல் நெய்தல்","கமழ்இதழ் நாற்றம் அமிழ்துஎன நசைஇத்:","தண்தாது ஊதிய வண்டினம் களிசிறந்து,","பறைஇய தளரும் துறைவனை, நீயே,","சொல்லல் வேண்டுமால் - அலவ! பல்கால்","கைதையம் படுசினை எவ்வமொடு அசாஅம்","கடற்சிறு காக்கை காமர் பெடையொடு","கோட்டுமீன் வழங்கும் வேட்டமடி பரப்பின்","வெள்இறாக் கனவும் நள்ளென் யாமத்து,","'நின்னுறு விழுமம் களைந்தோள்","தன்னுறு விழுமம் நீந்துமோ!' எனவோ!"],"p":"","r":"With no one else to carry her word (the grove, the backwater and the punnai will not speak), the woman begs the crab to tell the lord of the shore, where bees drunk on neythal scent falter in flight, that at midnight, while the small sea-crow wearied on the thazhai bough dreams of prawns beside its mate, she asks whether the one who cured his pain must now swim through her own.","n":"Her message turns on a reversal: she once relieved his distress, and he leaves her to drown in hers. The bees that sip and then falter echo the lord of the shore himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kaamanoy","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 170","lb":"A170","poet":"மதுரைக் கள்ளிற் கடையத்தான் வெண்ணாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகள், காமமிக்ககழிபடர்கிளவியாற் சொற்றது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,"காமமிக்ககழிபடர்கிளவியாற் சொற்றது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["புன்னையும்","புன்னை",2,"maram","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",4,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene","bee"],["அலவ","அலவன்",8,"pira","scene","crab"],["கைதையம்","கைதை",9,"maram","scene",""],["காக்கை","கடற்காக்கை",10,"pul","iraicchi","sea crow"],["பெடையொடு","பெடை",10,"pul","iraicchi",""],["கோட்டுமீன்","கோட்டுமீன்",11,"pira","scene","shark"],["வெள்இறா","இறா",12,"pira","scene","prawn"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","காக்கை","confirmed","2/2","even the weary crow sleeps paired while she lies awake alone, which is the wrong he has done her"]],"ch":"a:9"},"a:171":{"book":"akananuru","num":172,"t":["'நுதலும் நுண்பசப்பு இவரும், தோளும்","அகன்மலை இறும்பின் ஆய்ந்துகொண்டு அறுத்த","பணைஎழில் அழிய வாடும்; நாளும்","நினைவல் மாதுஅவர் பண்பு' என்று ஓவாது","இனையல் - வாழி, தோழி ! புணர்வர்","இலங்குகோல் ஆய்தொடி நெகிழப், பொருள்புரிந்து","அலந்தலை ஞெமையத்து அதர்அடைந் திருந்த","மால்வரைச் சீறூர் மருள்பன் மாக்கள்","கோள்வல் ஏற்றை ஓசை ஓர்மார்,","திருத்திக் கொண்ட அம்பினர், நோன்சிலை","எருத்தத்து இரீஇ, இடந்தொறும் படர்தலின்","கீழ்ப்படு தாரம் உண்ணா, மேற்சினைப்","பழம்போற் சேற்ற தீம்புழல் உணீஇய,","கருங்கோட்டு இருப்பை ஊரும்","பெருங்கை எண்கின் சுரன்இறந் தோரே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman not to grieve endlessly that her brow pales, her shoulders lose their bamboo-like beauty and she thinks of him daily: he will come back to her, the man who, set on wealth, crossed the waste where, because hill villagers roam with bows to hear the tiger's call, the bear leaves the honey below and climbs the black iruppai to eat its sweet flowers like fruit.","n":"The bear that changes its food for fear of the hunters is only the road, but it shows a creature adapting to danger, which is what the friend asks of her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 171","lb":"A171","poet":"கல்லாடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறீஇயது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","வற்புறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பணைஎழில்","பணை",3,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["ஞெமையத்து","ஞெமை",7,"maram","scene",""],["மாக்கள்","மாக்கள்",8,"seythi","scene",""],["ஏற்றை","ஏற்றை",9,"maa","scene","male tiger"],["தாரம்","தாரம்",12,"unavu","scene","honey"],["இருப்பை","இருப்பை",14,"maram","scene",""],["எண்கின்","எண்கு",15,"maa","scene","bear"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:9"},"a:172":{"book":"akananuru","num":173,"t":["வாரணம் உரறும் நீர்திகழ் சிலம்பில்","பிரசமொடு விரைஇய வயங்குவெள் அருவி","இன்இசை இமிழ்இயம் கடுப்ப, இம்மெனக்","கல்முகை விடர்அகம் சிலம்ப, வீழும்","காம்புதலை மணந்த ஓங்குமலைச் சாரல்,","இரும்புவடித் தன்ன கருங்கைக் கானவன்","விரிமலர் மராஅம் பொருந்திக், கோல்தெரிந்து,","வரிநுதல் யானை அருநிறத்து அழுத்தி,","இகல்இடு முன்பின் வெண்கோடு கொண்டுதன்","புல்வேய் குரம்பை புலர ஊன்றி,","முன்றில் நீடிய முழவுஉறழ் பலவின்","பிழிமகிழ் உவகையன், கிளையொடு கலிசிறந்து,","சாந்த ஞெகிழியின் ஊன்புழுக்கு அயரும்","குன்ற நாட! நீ அன்பிலை ஆகுதல்","அறியேன் யான்; அஃது அறிந்தனென் ஆயின்","அணி இழை, உண்கண், ஆய்இதழ்க் குறுமகள்","மணிஏர் மாண்நலம் சிதையப்,","பொன்னேர் பசலை பாவின்று மன்னே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hill-man that on his slopes, where a honeyed waterfall sounds like drums, the iron-armed hunter kills an elephant, plants its tusk at his grass hut and feasts with his kin on jackfruit liquor and meat roasted on sandal brands; she never knew he was loveless, and had she known, gold-coloured pallor would not have spread over the girl's gem-like beauty.","n":"The hunter's catalogue is of a man who takes, keeps and celebrates with his kin; the friend's reproach is that the hill-man has done only the taking. The reproach is aimed at marriage, as the colophon says.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","pasalai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 172","lb":"A172","poet":"மதுரைப் பாலாசிரியர் நப்பாலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகளை இடத்துஉய்த்துவந்து தலைமகனை வரைவு கடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தலைமகனை வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வாரணம்","வாரணம்",1,"pul","scene","jungle fowl"],["பிரசமொடு","பிரசம்",2,"pira","scene","honey"],["காம்பு","காம்பு",5,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["கானவன்","கானவன்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["மராஅம்","மரா",7,"maram","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",8,"maa","scene",""],["பலவின்","பலா",11,"maram","scene",""],["பிழிமகிழ்","மகிழ்",12,"unavu","gift_food","liquor"],["ஊன்புழுக்கு","ஊன்",13,"unavu","gift_food",""],["சாந்த","சாந்தம்",13,"maram","scene","sandal"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:9"},"a:173":{"book":"akananuru","num":174,"t":["'அறம் தலைப்பிரியாது ஒழுகலும், சிறந்த","கேளிர் கேடுபல ஊன்றலும், நாளும்","வருந்தா உள்ளமொடு இருந்தோர்க்கு இல்' எனச்","செய்வினை புரிந்த நெஞ்சினர், 'நறுநுதல்","மைஈர் ஓதி ! அரும்படர் உழத்தல்","சிலநாள் தாங்கல் வேண்டும்' என்று, நின்","நல்மாண் எல்வளை திருத்தினர் ஆயின்,","வருவர் - வாழி, தோழி !- பலபுரி","வார்கயிற்று ஒழுகை நோன்சுவற் கொளீஇ,","பகடுதுறை ஏற்றத்து உமண்விளி வெரீஇ,","உழைமான் அம்பிணை இனன்இரிந்து ஓடக்,","காடுகவின் அழிய உறைஇக், கோடை","நின்றுதின விளிந்த அம்புணை, நெடுவேய்க்","கண்விடத் தெறிக்கும் மண்ணா முத்தம்","கழங்குஉறழ் தோன்றல, பழங்குழித் தாஅம்","இன்களி நறவின் இயல்தேர் நன்னன்","விண்பொரு நெடுவரைக் கவாஅன்","பொன்படு மருங்கின் மலைஇறந் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman he will come: set on his work, he said that never straying from dharma and propping up kin in their misfortunes are not for those who sit at ease, asked her to bear the hard sorrow a few days and straightened her bangles; then he crossed the gold-bearing slopes of Nannan's sky-touching range, where deer flee the salt-carriers' shouts and drought-split bamboo throws out pearls like kazhangu seeds.","n":"The comfort is built on the man's own maxim: wealth is what makes dharma and help to kin possible, so his going is a duty, and the gesture of straightening her bangles seals the promise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pirivu","aram","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 173","lb":"A173","poet":"முள்ளியூர்ப் பூதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பகடு","பகடு",10,"maa","scene","ox"],["உமண்","உமணர்",10,"seythi","scene","salt merchants"],["உழைமான்","உழை",11,"maa","scene","spotted deer"],["வேய்","வேய்",13,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",14,"pira","scene","bamboo pearl"],["கழங்கு","கழங்கு",15,"maram","simile",""],["நறவின்","நறவு",16,"unavu","scene","toddy"]],"en":[["king","நன்னன்",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:9"},"a:174":{"book":"akananuru","num":175,"t":["'இருபெரு வேந்தர் மாறுகொள் வியன்களத்து,","ஒருபடை கொண்டு, வருபடை பெயர்க்கும்","செல்வம் உடையோர்க்கு நின்றன்று விறல்' எனப்,","பூக்கோள் ஏய தண்ணுமை விலக்கிச்","செல்வேம் ஆதல் அறியாள், முல்லை","நேர்கால் முதுகொடி குழைப்ப, நீர் சொரிந்து,","காலை வானத்துக் கடுங்குரற் கொண்மூ","முழங்குதொறும் கையற்று, ஒடுங்கி, நப் புலந்து,","பழங்கண் கொண்ட பசலை மேனியள்","யாங்குஆ குவள் கொல் தானே - வேங்கை","ஊழுறு நறுவீ கடுப்பக் கேழ்கொள,","ஆகத்து அரும்பிய மாசுஅறு சுணங்கினள்,","நன்மணல் வியலிடை நடந்த","சின்மெல் ஒதுக்கின், மாஅ யோளே?"],"p":"","r":"In the war camp, having declared that victory now rests with the side that can turn back the attacking army with one force and set aside the drum that calls men to take the battle-flower, the man tells his heart she does not know he is coming: as the mullai sprouts under the morning rain, at every thunder she must shrink, helpless and resentful, pale with sorrow, she with spots on her breast like ripe vengai flowers and soft few steps on the sand.","n":"The reading of lines 1 to 5 is uncertain: they may mean that he has declined the call to further battle and is going home, or that he went to war without her knowing. Either way the poem's present is her not knowing, while the rain she reads as betrayal is the season of his return.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaalam","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 174","lb":"A174","poet":"மதுரை அளக்கர் ஞாழலார் மகனார் மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பாசறைக்கண் தலைமகன்தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பாசறைக்கண் தலைமகன்தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",4,"parai","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",5,"maram","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",10,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:9"},"a:175":{"book":"akananuru","num":176,"t":["வீங்கு விளிம்பு உரீஇய விசைஅமை நோன்சிலை","வாங்கு தொடை பிழையா வன்கண் ஆடவர்","விடுதொறும் விளிக்கும் செவ்வாய் வாளி","ஆறுசெல் வம்பலர் உயிர்செலப் பெயர்ப்பின்,","பாறுகிளை பயிர்ந்து படுமுடை கவரும்","வெஞ்சுரம் இறந்த காதலர் நெஞ்சுஉணர","அரிய வஞ்சினம் சொல்லியும் பல்மாண்","தெரிவளை முன்கை பற்றியும், 'வினைமுடித்து","வருதும்' என்றனர் அன்றே - தோழி !-","கால்இயல் நெடுந்தேர்க் கைவண் செழியன்","ஆலங் கானத்து அமர்கடந்து உயர்த்த","வேலினும் பல்ஊழ் மின்னி, முரசு என","மாஇரு விசும்பிற் கடிஇடி பயிற்றி,","நேர்கதிர் நிறைத்த நேமியம் செல்வன்","போர்அடங்கு அகலம் பொருந்திய தார்போல்","திருவில் தேஎத்துக் குலைஇ, உருகெழு","மண்பயம் பூப்பப் பாஅய்த்,","தண்பெயல் எழிலி தாழ்ந்த போழ்தே!"],"p":"","r":"The woman reminds her friend that the man who crossed the hot waste, where archers' whistling arrows take travellers' lives and vultures call their flock to the carrion, swore hard vows, held her bangled wrist and said he would come back with the work done when the cool clouds came down, flashing more often than the spear Chezhiyan raised after winning at Alangaanam, thundering like drums, the rainbow arched like the garland on the wheel-bearing god's chest, and the earth's yield flowering: and that time is now.","n":"The poem calls the lover's promise a வஞ்சினம், the word for a warrior's vow, and frames the season of its testing with a king's victory and a god's garland, so a private promise is measured against the public oaths of puram. Against the friend's comfort she sets the season itself as evidence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sool","pirivu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 175","lb":"A175","poet":"ஆலம்பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவின்கண்வற்புறுக்குந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச்சொற்றதூ உமாம்.","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","வற்புறுக்குந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச்சொற்றதூ"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["பாறு","பாறு",5,"pul","scene","vulture"],["தெரிவளை","வளை",8,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["முரசு","முரசு",12,"parai","simile",""],["செல்வன்","நேமியம் செல்வன்",14,"deyvam","simile","Thirumal"],["தார்","தார்",15,"maram","simile","garland"]],"en":[["king","செழியன்",10],["battle","ஆலங் கானத்து",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:9"},"a:176":{"book":"akananuru","num":177,"t":["கடல்கண் டன்ன கண்அகன் பரப்பின்","நிலம்பக வீழ்ந்த வேர்முதிர் கிழங்கின்","கழைகண் டன்ன தூம்புடைத் திரள்கால்,","களிற்றுச்செவி அன்ன பாசடை மருங்கில்,","கழுநிவந் தன்ன கொழுமுகை இடைஇடை","முறுவல் முகத்தின் பன்மலர் தயங்கப்,","பூத்த தாமரைப் புள் இமிழ் பழனத்து,","வேப்புநனை அன்ன நெடுங்கண் நீர்ஞெண்டு","இரைதேர் வெண்குருகு அஞ்சி, அயலது","ஒலித்த பகன்றை இருஞ்சேற்று அள்ளல்","திதலையின் வரிப்ப ஓடி, விரைந்து தன்","நீர்மலி மண்அளைச் செறியும் ஊர!","மனைநகு வயலை மரன்இவர் கொழுங்கொடி","அரிமலர் ஆம்பலொடு ஆர்தழை தைஇ,","விழவுஆடு மகளிரொடு தழூஉ அணிப் பொலிந்து,","மலர்ஏர் உண்கண் மாண்இழை முன்கைக்","குறுந்தொடி துடக்கிய நெடுந்தொடர் விடுத்தது","உடன்றனள் போலும், நின் காதலி?' எம்போல்","புல்லுளைக் குடுமிப் புதல்வற் பயந்து,","நெல்லுடை நெடுநகர் நின்னின்று உறைய,","என்ன கடத்தளோ, மற்றே? தன் முகத்து","எழுதெழில் சிதைய அழுதனள் ஏங்கி,","அடித்தென உருத்த தித்திப் பல்ஊழ்","நொடித்தெனச் சிவந்த மெல்விரல் திருகுபு,","கூர்நுனை மழுகிய எயிற்றள்","ஊர்முழுது நுவலும்நிற் காணிய சென்மே"],"p":"","r":"The friend refuses the husband at the door: lord of a town where, in a lotus pond wide as the sea, a long-eyed water crab, afraid of the white heron hunting for food, runs across the mud and hides in its water-filled hole, has your new beloved turned angry because you let go her bangled hand in the festival dance, so you come here? what does she owe, she who has borne no son and does not keep your house as we do? she weeps her painted face away, snaps her fingers red and blunts her teeth: go, so that she who tells the whole town about you may see you.","n":"The crab's flight from the heron into its hole is the husband's flight from the other woman's rage into his wife's house, which the friend reads aloud only by refusing him entry. Lines 16 to 26 are dense and partly uncertain, especially who owes what in line 21.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 176","lb":"A176","poet":"மருதம்பாடிய இளங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி, தலைமகனை வாயின்மறுத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","வாயின்மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["கழை","கழை",3,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["களிற்றுச்செவி","களிறு",4,"maa","simile",""],["தாமரை","தாமரை",7,"maram","scene",""],["புள்","புள்",7,"pul","scene",""],["வேப்புநனை","வேம்பு",8,"maram","simile",""],["நீர்ஞெண்டு","ஞெண்டு",8,"pira","ullurai","crab"],["வெண்குருகு","குருகு",9,"pul","ullurai","heron"],["பகன்றை","பகன்றை",10,"maram","scene",""],["வயலை","வயலை",13,"maram","ornament",""],["ஆம்பலொடு","ஆம்பல்",14,"maram","ornament",""],["நெல்லுடை","நெல்",20,"unavu","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","நீர்ஞெண்டு வெண்குருகு","confirmed","1/1","the husband fleeing the other woman's anger back to his wife's house"]],"ch":"a:9"},"a:177":{"book":"akananuru","num":178,"t":["'தொன்னலம் சிதையச் சாஅய், அல்கலும்","இன்னும் வாரார்: இனி எவன் செய்கு? எனப்,","பெரும்புலம் புறுதல் ஓம்புமதி - சிறுகண்","இரும்பிடித் தடக்கை மான, நெய்அருந்து","ஒருங்குபிணித்து இயன்ற நெறிகொள் ஐம்பால்","தேம்கமழ் வெறிமலர் பெய்ம்மார், காண்பின்","கழைஅமல் சிலம்பின் வழைதலை வாடக்","கதிர்கதம் கற்ற ஏகல் நெறியிடைப்,","பைங்கொடிப் பாகற் செங்கனி நசைஇக்,","கான மஞ்ஞைக் கமஞ்சூல் மாப்பெடை","அயிரியாற்று அடைகரை வயிரின் நரலும்","காடுஇறந்து அகன்றோர் நீடினர் ஆயினும்","வல்லே வருவர் போலும் - வென்வேல்","இலைநிறம் பெயர ஓச்சி, மாற்றோர்","மலைமருள் யானை மண்டுஅமர் ஒழித்த","கழற்கால் பண்ணன் காவிரி வடவயின்","நிழற்கயம் தழீஇய நெடுங்கால் மாவின்","தளிர்ஏர் ஆகம் தகைபெற முகைந்த","அணங்குடை வனமுலைத் தாஅய நின்","சுணங்கிடை வரித்த தொய்யிலை நினைந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman not to despair that her old beauty is gone and he still does not come: though the man who went across the forest (where the sun withers the wazhai on bamboo slopes and a heavy-bellied peahen, craving red paagal fruit, cries like a horn on the Ayiri's bank) has stayed long, he will surely hurry back, remembering the painted designs among the spots on her breasts, budded like the shoots of the tall mango by the shaded pond on the Kaveri's north bank, in the land of Pannan whose spear stopped the hill-like elephants of his foes.","n":"The comfort rests on his desire rather than his word: what will bring him back is the memory of her body, and the land the friend borrows to praise it is a warrior-patron's fertile riverbank.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 177","lb":"A177","poet":"செயலூர் இளம்பொன் சாத்தன் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடைவேறுபட்டதலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இரும்பிடித்","பிடி",4,"maa","simile",""],["வெறிமலர்","மலர்",6,"maram","ornament",""],["கழை","கழை",7,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["வழை","வழை",7,"maram","absent",""],["பாகற்","பாகல்",9,"maram","scene",""],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",10,"pul","scene","peahen"],["யானை","யானை",15,"maa","simile",""],["மாவின்","மா",17,"maram","simile","mango"]],"en":[["river","அயிரியாற்று",11],["patron","பண்ணன்",16],["river","காவிரி",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:9"},"a:178":{"book":"akananuru","num":179,"t":["வயிரத் தன்ன வைஏந்து மருப்பின்,","வெதிர்வேர் அன்ன பரூஉமயிர்ப் பன்றி","பறைக்கண் அன்ன நிறைச்சுனை பருகி,","நீலத் தன்ன அகல்இலைச் சேம்பின்","பிண்டம் அன்ன கொழுங்கிழங்கு மாந்தி,","பிடிமடிந் தன்ன கல்மிசை ஊழ் இழிபு,","யாறுசேர்ந் தன்ன ஊறுநீர்ப் படாஅர்ப்","பைம்புதல் நளிசினைக் குருகுஇருந் தன்ன,","வண்பிணி அவிழ்ந்த வெண்கூ தாளத்து","அலங்குகுலை அலரி தீண்டித், தாது உக,","பொன்உரை கட்டளை கடுப்பக் காண்வரக்,","கிளைஅமல் சிறுதினை விளைகுரல் மேய்ந்து","கண்இனிது படுக்கும் நன்மலை நாடனொடு","உணர்ந்தனை புணர்ந்த நீயும், நின்தோட்","பணைக்கவின் அழியாது துணைப்புணர்ந்து, என்றும்,","தவல்இல் உலகத்து உறைஇயரோ - தோழி -","எல்லையும் இரவும் என்னாது, கல்லெனக்","கொண்டல் வான்மழை பொழிந்த வைகறைத்","தண்பனி அற்சிரம் தமியோர்க்கு அரிது' எனக்,","கனவிலும் பிரிவு அறியலனே: அதன்தலை","முன்தான் கண்ட ஞான்றினும்","பின்பெரிது அளிக்கும், தன் பண்பினானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, glad that marriage is settled, tells the woman she joined knowingly with the lord of a good hill where a boar with diamond-sharp tusks drinks from a drum-round spring, gorges on chempu tubers, ambles down a rock shaped like a lying elephant, brushes pollen from the koothalam's flowers and, having grazed the millet, sleeps content: may she live with him for ever, her shoulders unspoiled, for he says nights alone in the cold dew are hard, knows no parting even in dreams, and by his nature loves her more than on the day he first saw her.","n":"The boar that eats from the guarded millet field and then sleeps easy can be read as the man whose secret love is now made safe, but the poem gives no anchor for that, so it is left as scene. The friend quotes the man's own words as the guarantee.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","karpu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 178","lb":"A178","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி வரைவு மலிந்துசொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு மலிந்துசொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பன்றி","பன்றி",2,"maa","scene",""],["வெதிர்வேர்","வெதிர்",2,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["சேம்பின்","சேம்பு",4,"maram","scene",""],["கொழுங்கிழங்கு","கிழங்கு",5,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பிடிமடிந்","பிடி",6,"maa","simile",""],["குருகுஇருந்","குருகு",8,"pul","simile",""],["கூதாளத்து","கூதாளம்",9,"maram","scene",""],["சிறுதினை","தினை",12,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:9"},"a:179":{"book":"akananuru","num":180,"t":["விண்தோய் சிமைய விறல்வரைக் கவாஅன்,","வெண்தேர் ஓடும் கடம்கால் மருங்கில்,","துனைஎரி பரந்த துன்அரும் வியன்காட்டுச்,","சிறுகண் யானை நெடுங்கை நீட்டி","வான்வாய் திறந்தும் வண்புனல் பெறாஅது,","கான்புலந்து கழியும் கண் அகன் பரப்பின்","விடுவாய்ச் செங்கணைக் கொடுவில் ஆடவர்","நல்நிலை பொறித்த கல்நிலை அதர,","அரம்புகொள் பூசல் களையுநர்க் காணாச்","சுரம்செல விரும்பினிர் ஆயின் - இன் நகை,","முருந்துஎனத் திரண்ட முள்எயிற்றுத் துவர்வாய்,","குவளை நாண்மலர் புரையும் உண்கண், இம்","மதிஏர் வாள்நுதல் புலம்ப,","பதிபெயர்ந்து உறைதல் ஒல்லுமோ, நுமக்கே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man who has announced his going: if he wants to cross the waste of the sky-touching peak, where the mirage runs, fire has spread, the elephant raises its trunk to the sky and finds no water, the paths are lined with stones carved with archers' stances and no one quells the robbers' uproar, can he bear to live away from home while she of the sweet smile, quill-like teeth, kuvalai eyes and moon-bright brow is left alone?","n":"The road's hero-stones are given as a plain warning of death on the path; the friend sets them beside the woman's face, feature by feature, and leaves the choice as a question.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 179","lb":"A179","poet":"கொடிமங்கலத்து வாதுளி நற்சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவுணர்த்தியதலைமகற்குத் தோழி செலவு அழுங்கச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","செலவு அழுங்கச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene",""],["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["முருந்து","முருந்து",11,"pira","simile","peacock quill"],["குவளை","குவளை",12,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["herostone","கல்நிலை",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:9"},"a:180":{"book":"akananuru","num":181,"t":["நகைநனி உடைத்தால் - தோழி ! தகைமிக,","கோதை ஆயமொடு குவவுமணல் ஏறி,","வீததை கானல் வண்டல் அயர,","கதழ்பரித் திண்தேர் கடைஇ வந்து,","தண்கயத்து அமன்ற ஒண்பூங் குவளை","அரும்புஅலைத்து இயற்றிய சுரும்புஆர் கண்ணி","பின்னுப்புறம் தாழக் கொன்னே சூட்டி,","நல்வரல் இளமுலை நோக்கி, நெடிது நினைந்து,","நில்லாது பெயர்ந்தனன், ஒருவன்; அதற்கே","புலவுநாறு இருங்கழி துழைஇப் பலஉடன்","புள்இறை கொண்ட முள்ளுடை நெடுந்தோட்டுத்","தாழை மணந்து ஞாழலொடு கெழீஇப்","படப்பை நின்ற முடத்தாட் புன்னைப்","பொன்நேர் நுண்தாது நோக்கி,","என்னும் நோக்கும், இவ் அழுங்கல் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"It is laughable, friend: while we played at sand houses on the dunes of the grove, one man drove up in his chariot, needlessly hung a bee-thronged wreath of pond kuvalai down her braided back, gazed at her breasts, thought long and left without a word; and for that alone this noisy town now looks at the gold pollen of the bent punnai in the garden and looks at me too.","n":"Under the mockery the friend is reporting a suitor's first approach and testing the girl's response, as the colophon says (குறைநயப்ப); the town's gaze at the pollen and at her is the first stir of gossip.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 180","lb":"A180","poet":"கருவூர்க் கண்ணம்பாளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"இரந்து பின்னின்றதலைமகற்குக் குறைநேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகளைக்குறைநயப்பக் கூறியது. தலைமகன்சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழிக்குச்சொல்லியதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தலைமகளைக்குறைநயப்பக் கூறியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன்சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழிக்குச்சொல்லியதூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["கோதை","கோதை",2,"maram","ornament","garland"],["குவளை","குவளை",5,"maram","ornament",""],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",6,"pira","scene","bee"],["புள்","புள்",11,"pul","scene",""],["தாழை","தாழை",12,"maram","scene",""],["ஞாழலொடு","ஞாழல்",12,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",13,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:9"},"a:181":{"book":"akananuru","num":182,"t":["துன்அருங் கானமும் துணிதல் ஆற்றாய்,","பின்நின்று பெயரச் சூழ்ந்தனை ஆயின்,","என்நிலை உரைமோ - நெஞ்சே!- ஒன்னார்","ஓம்பரண் கடந்த வீங்குபெருந் தானை","அடுபோர் மிஞிலி செருவேல் கடைஇ,","முருகுறழ் முன்பொடு பொருதுகளம் சிவப்ப,","ஆஅய் எயினன் வீழ்ந்தென, ஞாயிற்று","ஒண்கதிர் உருப்பம் புதைய ஓராங்கு","வம்பப் புள்ளின் கம்பலைப் பெருந்தோடு","விசும்பிடை தூரஆடி, மொசிந்து உடன்,","பூவிரி அகன்துறைக் கணைவிசைக் கடுநீர்க்","காவிரிப் பேர்யாற்று அயிர்கொண்டு ஈண்டி,","எக்கர் இட்ட குப்பை வெண்மணல்","வைப்பின் யாணர் வளம்கெழு வேந்தர்","ஞாலம் நாறும் நலம்கெழு நல்இசை,","நான்மறை முதுநூல் முக்கட் செல்வன்,","ஆல முற்றம் கவின்பெறத் தைஇய","பொய்கை சூழ்ந்த பொழில்மனை மகளிர்","கைசெய் பாவைத் துறைக்கண் இறுக்கும்","மகர நெற்றி வான்தோய் புரிசைச்","சிகரம் தோன்றாச் சேண்உயர் நல்இல்","புகாஅர் நல்நாட் டதுவே - பகாஅர்","பண்டம் நாறும் வண்டுஅடர் ஐம்பால்,","பணைத்தகைத் தடைஇய காண்புஇன் மென்தோள்,","அணங்குசால், அரிவை இருந்த","மணம்கமழ் மறுகின் மணற்பெருங் குன்றே"],"p":"","r":"A man on the road tells his heart, which cannot face the hard forest and wants to turn back, to go and report his state to the girl who lives on a great sand dune in a fragrant street of Pukar, a town of high walls, a Shiva shrine and a ford where women leave their dolls.","n":"Most of the poem is the address of the girl's home, built up as a chain of places; the fall of Aay Eyinan to Minjili, with a flock of birds massing over the field and hiding the sun, is set beside the Kaveri's heaped white sand, so a battle memory becomes the measure of how thick that sand lies. The heart is not rebuked into going on, as in Akananuru 21; it is sent back as a messenger while the man goes forward.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","ninaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 181","lb":"A181","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"இடைச்சுரத்து ஒழியக்கருதிய நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"இடைச்சுரத்து ஒழியக்கருதிய நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புள்ளின்","புள்",9,"pul","scene",""],["காவிரிப்","காவிரி",12,"pira","scene","river water"],["அயிர்","அயிர்",12,"pira","scene","fine sand"],["வெண்மணல்","வெண்மணல்",13,"pira","scene","white sand"],["முக்கட் செல்வன்","முக்கண் செல்வன்",16,"deyvam","scene",""],["ஆல","ஆல்",17,"maram","scene",""],["பொய்கை","பொய்கை",18,"pira","scene",""],["பாவைத்","பாவை",19,"pira","scene","doll"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",23,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["king","மிஞிலி",5],["king","ஆஅய் எயினன்",7],["river","காவிரிப்",12],["town","புகாஅர்",22]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:10"},"a:182":{"book":"akananuru","num":183,"t":["பூங்கண் வேங்கைப் பொன்னிணர் மிலைந்து,","வாங்கமை நோன்சிலை எருத்தத்து இரீஇ,","தீம்பழப் பலவின் சுளைவிளை தேறல்","வீளைஅம்பின் இளையரொடு மாந்தி,","ஓட்டியல் பிழையா வயநாய் பிற்பட,","வேட்டம் போகிய குறவன் காட்ட","குளவித் தண்புதல் குருதியொடு துயல்வர,","முளவுமாத் தொலைச்சும் குன்ற நாடே!","அரவுஎறி உருமோடு ஒன்றிக் கால்வீழ்த்து","உரவுமழை பொழிந்த பானாட் கங்குல்,","தனியை வந்த ஆறுநினைந்து அல்கலும்,","பனியொடுகலுழும் இவள் கண்ணே: அதனால்,","கடும்பகல் வருதல் வேண்டும் - தெய்ய","அதிர்குரல் முதுகலை கறிமுறி முனைஇ,","உயர்சிமை நெடுங்கோட்டு உகள, உக்க","கமழ்இதழ் அலரி தாஅய் வேலன்","வெறிஅயர் வியன்களம் கடுக்கும்","பெருவரை நண்ணிய சார லானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the hill country, where the hunter drinks jackfruit toddy and kills a porcupine, that the girl weeps every night thinking of him coming alone through midnight storms that strike down snakes, so he must come by full daylight instead.","n":"The opening hunter, who returns with his kill through bloodied grass, is the hill's ordinary daytime courage set against the man's lonely night journey; the closing slope strewn with alari petals is likened to the velan's ground of possession, a hint of the mother's suspicion that the friend does not voice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu","acham","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 182","lb":"A182","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி இரா வருவானைப்பகல் வர என்றது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரா வருவானைப்பகல் வர என்றது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",1,"maram","ornament",""],["வாங்கமை","அமை",2,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பலவின்","பலா",3,"maram","scene",""],["தேறல்","தேறல்",3,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வயநாய்","நாய்",5,"maa","scene",""],["குறவன்","குறவன்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["குளவித்","குளவி",7,"maram","scene",""],["முளவுமாத்","முளவுமா",8,"maa","scene","porcupine"],["அரவு","அரவு",9,"pira","scene","snake"],["முதுகலை","கலை",14,"maa","scene","stag"],["கறிமுறி","கறி",14,"maram","scene","pepper"],["அலரி","அலரி",16,"maram","simile",""],["வேலன்","வேலன்",16,"seythi","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:10"},"a:183":{"book":"akananuru","num":184,"t":["'குவளை உண்கண் கலுழவும், திருந்திழைத்","திதலை அல்குல் அவ்வரி வாடவும்,","அத்தம்ஆர் அழுவம் நத்துறந்து அருளார்","சென்றுசேண் இடையர் ஆயினும், நன்றும்","நீடலர், என்றி - தோழி !- பாடுஆன்று","பனித்துறைப் பெருங்கடல் இறந்து, நீர் பருகி,","குவவுத்திரை அருந்து கொள்ளைய குடக்குஏர்பு,","வயவுப்பிடி இனத்தின் வயின்வயின் தோன்றி","இருங்கிளைக் கொண்மூ ஒருங்குடன் துவன்றி","காலை வந்தன்றால் காரே - மாலைக்","குளிர்கொள் பிடவின் கூர்முகை அலரி","வண்டுவாய் திறக்கும் தண்டா நாற்றம்","கூதிர்அற் சிரத்து ஊதை தூற்ற,","பனிஅலைக் கலங்கிய நெஞ்சமொடு","வருந்துவம் அல்லமோ, பிரிந்திசினோர் திறத்தே?"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that she was promised he would not stay long, though he went far across the waste; now the rain clouds, having drunk the sea, have come up from the west like herds of she-elephants, the cold wind spreads the scent of opening pidavu buds, and she asks whether those who are parted do not suffer.","n":"The season he named has arrived on time while he has not, so the clouds prove the friend's comfort false; the clouds are described as a herd of pregnant she-elephants gathering with their kin, a picture of fullness and company set against her solitude.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","mazhai","kaalam","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 183","lb":"A183","poet":"கருவூர்க் கலிங்கத்தார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் குறித்த பருவவரவு கண்டு தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைமகன் குறித்த பருவவரவு கண்டு தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["குவளை","குவளை",1,"maram","simile",""],["வயவுப்பிடி","பிடி",8,"maa","simile",""],["கொண்மூ","கொண்மூ",9,"pira","scene","cloud"],["பிடவின்","பிடவு",11,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",12,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:10"},"a:184":{"book":"akananuru","num":185,"t":["கடவுட் கற்பொடு குடிக்குவிளக்கு ஆகிய","புதல்வற் பயந்த புகழ்மிகு சிறப்பின்","நன்ன ராட்டிக்கு அன்றியும், எனக்கும்","இனிதுஆ கின்றால், சிறக்க, நின் ஆயுள்!","அருந்தொழில் முடித்த செம்மல் உள்ளமொடு","சுரும்புஇமிர் மலர கானம் பிற்பட","வெண்பிடவு அவிழ்ந்த வீகமழ் புறவில்","குண்டைக் கோட்ட குறுமுள் கள்ளிப்","புன்தலை புதைத்த கொழுங்கொடி முல்லை","ஆர்கழல் புதுப்பூ உயிர்ப்பின் நீக்கித்,","தெள்அறல் பருகிய திரிமருப்பு எழிற்கலை","புள்ளிஅம் பிணையொடு வதியும் ஆங்கண்,","கோடுடைக் கையர், துளர்எறி வினைஞர்,","அரியல் ஆர்கையர், விளைமகிழ் தூங்கச்,","செல்கதிர் மழுகிய உருவ ஞாயிற்றுச்","செக்கர் வானம் சென்ற பொழுதில்,","கற்பால் அருவியின் ஒலிக்கும் நற்றேர்த்","தார்மணி பலஉடன் இயம்ப-","சீர்மிகு குருசில்!- நீ வந்துநின் றதுவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend greets the returning man: his coming at red dusk, his chariot bells ringing like a waterfall on rock, past fields where pidavu and jasmine bloom and the stag rests with its doe, is sweet not only to his wife, the light of the house who bore him a son, but to her too.","n":"The friend's blessing 'may your life flourish' and her claim that the joy is hers as well make the welcome public; the wife is praised as mother and as 'light of the clan' before anything is said of her love, the karpu household seen from outside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","ilvaazhkkai","makkal","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 184","lb":"A184","poet":"மதுரை மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைமகன் வினைவயிற்பிரிந்து வந்து எய்தியவிடத்து தோழி புல்லுமகிழ்வு உரைத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","புல்லுமகிழ்வு உரைத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",6,"pira","scene",""],["வெண்பிடவு","பிடவு",7,"maram","scene",""],["கள்ளிப்","கள்ளி",8,"maram","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",9,"maram","scene",""],["எழிற்கலை","கலை",11,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["பிணையொடு","பிணை",12,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["வினைஞர்","வினைஞர்",13,"seythi","scene",""],["அரியல்","அரியல்",14,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","எழிற்கலை","confirmed","1/1","the man who has finished his hard work now stays with his wife, as the stag on his road stays with its doe"]],"ch":"a:10"},"a:185":{"book":"akananuru","num":186,"t":["எல்வளை ஞெகிழச் சாஅய், ஆய்இழை","நல்எழிற் பணைத்தோள் இருங்கவின் அழிய,","பெருங்கை யற்ற நெஞ்சமொடு நத்துறந்து,","இரும்பின் இன்உயிர் உடையோர் போல,","வலித்து வல்லினர், காதலர்: வாடல்","ஒலிகழை நிவந்த நெல்லுடை நெடுவெதிர்","கலிகொள் மள்ளர் வில்விசையின் உடைய,","பைதுஅற வெம்பிய கல்பொரு பரப்பின்","வேனில் அத்தத்து ஆங்கண், வான்உலந்து","அருவி ஆன்ற உயர்சிமை மருங்கில்","பெருவிழா விளக்கம் போலப், பலவுடன்","இலைஇல மலர்ந்த இலவமொடு","நிலையுயர் பிறங்கல் மலைஇறந் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that her bangles slip and her shoulders lose their beauty while her lover, as if his life were made of iron, has hardened himself and crossed the scorched summer hills where bamboo bursts like a warrior's bowstring and leafless ilavam blazes like the lamps of a great festival.","n":"Her only accusation is the iron simile: he is not cruel but able, வலித்து வல்லினர, to will the parting, and that ability is what she cannot match; the festival lamps on the bare ilavam are a celebration seen from inside her loss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","pasalai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 185","lb":"A185","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்டதலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்டதலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நெடுவெதிர்","வெதிர்",6,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["நெல்லுடை","நெல்",6,"maram","scene","bamboo seed"],["மள்ளர்","மள்ளர்",7,"seythi","simile",""],["அருவி","அருவி",10,"pira","absent",""],["இலவமொடு","இலவம்",12,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:10"},"a:186":{"book":"akananuru","num":187,"t":["வானம் வேண்டா வறனில் வாழ்க்கை","நோன்ஞாண் வினைஞர் கோளறிந்து ஈர்க்கும்","மீன்முதிர் இலஞ்சிக் கலித்த தாமரை","நீர்மிசை நிவந்த நெடுந்தாள் அகலிலை","இருங்கயம் துளங்கக், கால்உறு தொறும்","பெருங்களிற்றுச் செவியின் அலைக்கும் ஊரனொடு","எழுந்த கௌவையோ பெரிதே: நட்பே,","கொழுங்கோல் வேழத்துப் புணைதுணை யாகப்","புனல்ஆடு கேண்மை அனைத்தே; அவனே,","ஒண்தொடி மகளிர் பண்டையாழ் பாட,","ஈர்ந்தண் முழவின் எறிகுணில் விதிர்ப்ப,","தண்நறுஞ் சாந்தம் கமழும் தோள்மணந்து,","இன்னும் பிறள்வயி னானே: மனையோள்","எம்மொடு புலக்கும் என்ப, வென்வேல்","மாரி அம்பின், மழைத்தோற் பழையன்","காவிரி வைப்பின் போஒர் அன்ன, என்","செறிவளை உடைத்தலோ இலனே: உரிதினின்","யாம்தன் பகையேம் அல்லேம்; சேர்ந்தோர்","திருநுதல் பசப்ப நீங்கும்","கொழுநனும் சாலும், தன் உடன்உறை பகையே"],"p":"","r":"The courtesan, speaking so that the wife's companions hear, says the gossip about her and the lord of the town is loud, though their bond was only a bathing friendship on a raft of reed; he is now with another woman, and if the wife quarrels with her, let her know that it is her own husband, who leaves women pale, that is the enemy living in her house.","n":"The courtesan both disowns and boasts: he has never broken her bangles, which stand firm like Pazhaiyan's fort of Por, so she is not the one he leaves; the lotus leaf that flaps like an elephant's ear at every gust may be read as his fickleness, but the poem does not make the match itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","alar","oodal","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 186","lb":"A186","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைமகட்குப்பாங்காயினார் கேட்ப இல்லிடைப் பரத்தைசொல்லி நெருங்கியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","pira","thozhi","karpu","இல்லிடைப் பரத்தைசொல்லி நெருங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வினைஞர்","வினைஞர்",2,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["மீன்முதிர்","மீன்",3,"pira","scene",""],["தாமரை","தாமரை",3,"maram","simile",""],["பெருங்களிற்றுச்","களிறு",6,"maa","simile",""],["வேழத்துப்","வேழம்",8,"maram","scene","reed"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",10,"yaazh","scene",""],["முழவின்","முழவு",11,"parai","scene",""],["சாந்தம்","சாந்து",12,"pira","ornament","sandal paste"]],"en":[["king","பழையன்",15],["river","காவிரி",16],["town","போஒர்",16]],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:10"},"a:187":{"book":"akananuru","num":188,"t":["தோள்புலம்பு அகலத் துஞ்சி, நம்மொடு","நாள்பல நீடிய கரந்துஉறை புணர்ச்சி","நாண் உடைமையின் நீங்கிச், சேய்நாட்டு","அரும்பொருள் வலித்த நெஞ்சமொடு ஏகி,","நம் உயர்வு உள்ளினர் காதலர் - கறுத்தோர்","தெம்முனை சிதைத்த கடும்பரிப் புரவி,","வார்கழற் பொலிந்த வன்கண் மழவர்","பூந்தொடை விழவின் தலைநாள் அன்ன,","தருமணல் ஞெமிரிய திருநகர் முற்றம்","புலம்புறும் கொல்லோ - தோழி !- சேண்ஓங்கு","அலந்தலை ஞெமையத்து ஆள்இல் ஆங்கண்,","கல்சேர்பு இருந்த சில்குடிப் பாக்கத்து,","எல்விருந்து அயர, ஏமத்து அல்கி,","மனைஉறை கோழி அணல்தாழ்பு அன்ன","கவைஒண் தளிர கருங்கால் யாஅத்து","வேனில் வெற்பின் கானம் காய,","முனைஎழுந்து ஓடிய கெடுநாட்டு ஆர்இடை,","பனைவெளிறு அருந்து பைங்கண் யானை","ஒண்சுடர் முதிரா இளங்கதிர் அமையத்து,","கண்படு பாயல் கைஒடுங்கு அசைநிலை","வாள்வாய்ச் சுறவின் பனித்துறை நீந்தி,","நாள்வேட்டு எழுந்த நயன்இல் பரதவர்","வைகுகடல் அம்பியின் தோன்றும்","மைபடு மாமலை விலங்கிய சுரனே?"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether their sanded courtyard, lovely as the first day of a warrior festival, will grow lonely now that her lover, ashamed to prolong their hidden union, has gone for wealth to a far land, across hills where a sleeping elephant looks like a fishing boat at rest on the sea.","n":"The union was hidden and long, and he leaves out of shame at its length (நாண் உடைமையின்) and with thoughts of 'our standing' (நம் உயர்வு): the journey is for the marriage that will end the secrecy. The road is drawn in detail, a deserted country emptied by raids and a hamlet that shelters guests at night.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","varaivu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 187","lb":"A187","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவுணர்த்தியதோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது. தலைமகன்பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","பிரிவுணர்த்தியதோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தலைமகன்பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியதூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புரவி","புரவி",6,"maa","scene",""],["மழவர்","மழவர்",7,"seythi","simile",""],["ஞெமையத்து","ஞெமை",11,"maram","scene",""],["கோழி","கோழி",14,"pul","simile",""],["யாஅத்து","யா",15,"maram","simile",""],["பனைவெளிறு","பனை",18,"maram","gift_food",""],["யானை","யானை",18,"maa","simile",""],["சுறவின்","சுறவு",21,"pira","scene","shark"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",22,"seythi","simile",""],["அம்பியின்","அம்பி",23,"pira","simile","boat"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:10"},"a:188":{"book":"akananuru","num":189,"t":["பெருங்கடல் முகந்த இருங்கிளைக் கொண்மூ!","இருண்டுஉயர் விசும்பின் வலன்ஏர்பு வளைஇப்,","போர்ப்புஉறு முரசின் இரங்கி, முறைபுரிந்து","அறன்நெறி பிழையாத் திறன்அறி மன்னர்","அருஞ்சமத்து எதிர்ந்த பெருஞ்செய் ஆடவர்","கழித்துஎறி வாளின், நளிப்பன விளங்கும்","மின்னுடைக் கருவியை ஆகி, நாளும்","கொன்னே செய்தியோ, அரவம்? பொன்னென","மலர்ந்த வேங்கை மலிதொடர் அடைச்சிப்,","பொலிந்த ஆயமொடு காண்தக இயலித்,","தழலை வாங்கியும், தட்டை ஓப்பியும்,","அழலேர் செயலை அம்தழை அசைஇயும்,","குறமகள் காக்கும் ஏனல்","புறமும் தருதியோ? வாழிய, மழையே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend, at night and within the hidden lover's hearing, scolds the thundering, lightning-flashing cloud for raging to no purpose, and asks it whether it will also bring to an end the millet field the hill girl guards with her companions, wearing vengai flowers and scaring birds.","n":"Addressing the cloud is a way of speaking to the man: the rain will ripen the millet, the harvest will end the guarding, and with it the day meetings, so he should act. The cloud's lightning is likened to the swords of just kings' warriors, a puram image used to scare a lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","varaivu","mazhai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 188","lb":"A188","poet":"வீரை வெளியன் தித்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவில் சிறைப்புறமாகத்தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவில் சிறைப்புறமாகத்தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முரசின்","முரசு",3,"parai","simile",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",9,"maram","ornament",""],["தழலை","தழல்",11,"pira","scene","sling"],["தட்டை","தட்டை",11,"pira","scene","rattle"],["செயலை","செயலை",12,"maram","ornament",""],["குறமகள்","குறமகள்",13,"seythi","scene",""],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",13,"unavu","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:10"},"a:189":{"book":"akananuru","num":190,"t":["பசும்பழப் பலவின் கானம் வெம்பி,","விசும்புகண் அழிய, வேனில் நீடிக்,","கயம்கண் அற்ற கல்லோங்கு வைப்பின்","நாறுஉயிர் மடப்பிடி தழீஇ, வேறுநாட்டு","விழவுப்படர் மள்ளரின் முழவெடுத்து உயரிக்,","களிறுஅதர்ப் படுத்த கல்லுயர் 'கவாஅன்","வெவ்வரை அத்தம் சுட்டிப்' பையென,","வயல்அம் பிணையல் வார்ந்த கவாஅன்","திதலை அல்குல் குறுமகள் அவனொடு","சென்று பிறள் ஆகிய அளவை, என்றும்","படர்மலி எவ்வமொடு மாதிரம் துழைஇ,","மனைமருண்டு இருந்த என்னினும், நனைமகிழ்","நன்ன ராளர் கூடுகொள் இன்னியம்","தேர்ஊர் தெருவில் ததும்பும்","ஊர்இழந் தன்று, தன் வீழ்வுஉறு பொருளே"],"p":"","r":"The foster mother laments that the little girl has gone off with the youth across the scorched hills, where a tusker clutching his mate breaks a path, and has become another's; she herself wanders bewildered at home, and the town, whose streets ring with music, has lost the thing it loved.","n":"Her grief widens from herself to the town: the girl was the town's treasure, so the loss is public. The tusker that shelters its young mate and treads a path through the drought answers her fear with a picture of a guarded crossing; on our reading that is the sense the poem lets rise without saying it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","inmai","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 189","lb":"A189","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய செவிலிசொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட் போக்கிய செவிலிசொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பலவின்","பலா",1,"maram","scene",""],["கயம்","கயம்",3,"pira","absent",""],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",4,"maa","iraicchi",""],["மள்ளரின்","மள்ளர்",5,"seythi","simile",""],["முழவெடுத்து","முழவு",5,"parai","simile",""],["களிறு","களிறு",6,"maa","iraicchi",""],["இன்னியம்","இயம்",13,"yaazh","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","களிறு","confirmed","1/1","the youth who has taken the girl across the same land may guard and lead her as the tusker does its mate"]],"ch":"a:10"},"a:190":{"book":"akananuru","num":191,"t":["திரைஉழந்து அசைஇய நிரைவளை ஆயமொடு","உப்பின் குப்பை ஏறி எற்பட,","வருதிமில் எண்ணும் துறைவனொடு, ஊரே","ஒருதன் கொடுமையின் அலர்பா டும்மே;","அலமரல் மழைக்கண் அமர்ந்து நோக்காள்;","அலையல் - வாழி ! வேண்டு, அன்னை!- உயர்சிமைப்","பொதும்பில், புன்னைச் சினைசேர்பு இருந்த","வம்ப நாரை இரிய, ஒருநாள்,","பொங்குவரல் ஊதையொடு புணரி அலைப்பவும்,","உழைக்கடல் வழங்கலும் உரியன்; அதன்தலை","இருங்கழி புகாஅர் பொருந்தத் தாக்கி","வயச்சுறா எறிந்தென: வலவன் அழிப்ப,","எழிற்பயம் குன்றிய சிறைஅழி தொழில","நிரைமணிப் புரவி விரைநடை தவிர,","இழுமென் கானல் விழுமணல் அசைஇ,","ஆய்ந்த பரியன் வந்து, இவண்","மான்ற மாலைச் சேர்ந்தன்றே இலனே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the foster mother not to fret over the town's gossip about the girl and the lord of the shore: one evening, as the wind-driven sea beat the bay, a fierce shark struck in the estuary, he speared it, and with his tired horses he came and rested by them on the sand at dusk.","n":"This is the friend's disclosure (அறத்தொடு நிற்றல): she presents the bond as born of a rescue at sea, which gives the family an honourable reason to accept him. The closing words சேர்ந்தன்றே இலனே are hard to construe, so who came to whom, and whether he stayed, is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","varaivu","nanri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 190","lb":"A190","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி,செவிலித்தாய்க்கு அறத்தொடு நின்றது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu","செவிலித்தாய்க்கு அறத்தொடு நின்றது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["உப்பின்","உப்பு",2,"pira","scene",""],["திமில்","திமில்",3,"pira","scene","fishing boat"],["புன்னைச்","புன்னை",7,"maram","scene",""],["நாரை","நாரை",8,"pul","scene",""],["வயச்சுறா","சுறா",12,"pira","scene","shark"],["புரவி","புரவி",14,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:10"},"a:191":{"book":"akananuru","num":192,"t":["அத்தப் பாதிரித் துய்த்தலைப் புதுவீ","எரிஇதழ் அலரியொடு இடைப்பட விரைஇ,","வண்தோட்டுத் தொடுத்த வண்டுபடு கண்ணி,","தோல்புதை சிரற்றுஅடிக், கோலுடை உமணர்","ஊர்கண் டன்ன ஆரம் வாங்கி,","அருஞ்சுரம் இவர்ந்த அசைவுஇல் நோன்தாள்","திருந்துபகட்டு இயம்பும் கொடுமணி, புரிந்துஅவர்","மடிவிடு வீளையொடு, கடிதுஎதிர் ஓடி,","ஓமைஅம் பெருங்காட்டு வரூஉம் வம்பலர்க்கு","ஏமம் செப்பும் என்றூழ் நீள்இடை,","அரும்பொருள் நசைஇப், பிரிந்துஉறை வல்லி","சென்று: வினை எண்ணுதி ஆயின், நன்று,","உரைத்திசின் வாழி - என் நெஞ்சே!- நிரைமுகை","முல்லை அருந்தும் மெல்லிய ஆகி,","அறல்என விரிந்த உறல்இன் சாயல்","ஒலிஇருங் கூந்தல் தேறும்' என,","வலிய கூறவும் வல்லையோ, மற்றே?"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart that if, having gone, it plans to stay away for wealth across the hot road where the salt-carriers' bullock bells tell travellers they are safe, it should first be able to say firmly to the woman with soft hair like rippled sand that she will bear it.","n":"He does not refuse the journey outright; he sets the heart a test it cannot pass, telling her to her face, and so gives up going. The bells of the salt-carriers, which reassure travellers, are the reassurance he cannot give her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","selvam","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 191","lb":"A191","poet":"ஒரோடோகத்துக் கந்தரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் தன்நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லிச் செலவழுங்கியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"தன்நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லிச் செலவழுங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாதிரித்","பாதிரி",1,"maram","ornament",""],["அலரியொடு","அலரி",2,"maram","ornament",""],["உமணர்","உமணர்",4,"seythi","scene","salt merchants"],["பகட்டு","பகடு",7,"maa","scene","bullock"],["ஓமைஅம்","ஓமை",9,"maram","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",14,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:10"},"a:192":{"book":"akananuru","num":193,"t":["மதிஇருப் பன்ன மாசுஅறு சுடர்நுதல்","பொன்நேர் வண்ணம் கொண்டன்று: அன்னோ?","யாங்குஆ குவள்கொல் தானே? விசும்பின்","எய்யா வரிவில் அன்ன பைந்தார்ச்,","செவ்வாய்ச், சிறுகிளி சிதைய வாங்கி,","பொறைமெலிந் திட்ட புன்புறப் பெருங்குரல்","வளைசிறை வாரணம் கிளையொடு கவர,","ஏனலும் இறங்குபொறை உயிர்த்தன; பானாள்","வந்து அளிக்குவை எனினே மால்வரை","மைபடு விடரகம் துழைஇ, ஒய்யென","அருவிதந்த, அரவுஉமிழ், திருமணி","பெருவரைச் சிறுகுடி மறுகுவிளக் குறுத்தலின்","இரவும் இழந்தனள்: அளியள்- உரவுப்பெயல்","உருமிறை கொண்ட உயர்சிமைப்","பெருமலை நாட - நின் மலர்ந்த மார்பே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the high hills that the girl's moon-bright forehead has turned the colour of gold with longing; the millet has been reaped by parrots and junglefowl, and a snake-spat gem washed down by the falls lights up the village street, so if he comes at midnight she has lost even the night, and pity her.","n":"Each detail closes a way: the end of the harvest ends her days in the field, and the street lit by the gem ends the dark that hid night visits; the friend says this to turn him from secret meeting toward marriage without naming it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","varaivu","pasalai","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 192","lb":"A192","poet":"பொதும்பில் கிழான் வெண்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி, தலைமகனைச் 1செறிப்புஅறிவுறீஇ இரவுக்குறி மறுத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சிறுகிளி","கிளி",5,"pul","scene",""],["வாரணம்","வாரணம்",7,"pul","scene","junglefowl"],["ஏனலும்","ஏனல்",8,"unavu","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",11,"pira","scene",""],["அரவு","அரவு",11,"pira","scene","snake"],["திருமணி","மணி",11,"pira","scene","gem"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:10"},"a:193":{"book":"akananuru","num":194,"t":["கானுயர் மருங்கில் கவலை அல்லது,","வானம் வேண்டா வில்லேர் உழவர்","பெருநாள் வேட்டம், கிளைஎழ வாய்த்த","பொருகளத்து ஒழிந்த குருதிச் செவ்வாய்ப்,","பொறித்த போலும் வால்நிற எருத்தின்:","அணிந்த போலும் செஞ்செவி, எருவை:","குறும்பொறை எழுந்த நெடுந்தாள் யாஅத்து","அருங்கவட்டு உயர்சினைப் பிள்ளை ஊட்ட,","விரைந்துவாய் வழுக்கிய கொழுங்கண் ஊன்தடி","கொல்பசி முதுநரி வல்சி ஆகும்","சுரன்நமக்கு எளிய மன்னே; நல்மனைப்","பன்மாண் தங்கிய சாயல், இன்மொழி,","முருந்தேர் முறுவல், இளையோள்","பெருந்தோள் இன்துயில் கைவிடு கலனே"],"p":"","r":"The man, giving up the journey his heart had settled on, says the road would be easy, a waste where hunters with bows leave blood on the field, a red-eared vulture feeds its young in a yaa tree and dropped flesh becomes an old jackal's meal; what he cannot do is leave the sweet sleep on the shoulders of his young wife.","n":"The danger of the road is granted and then dismissed: the waste is 'easy for us'; the only hardship that counts is the bed at home. The vulture feeding its chick is the road's one image of care, and the scraps falling to the jackal show how that care spills over to others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","ilvaazhkkai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 193","lb":"A193","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள வலித்தநெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லித் தலைமகன் செலவுஅழுங்கியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"செலவுஅழுங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வில்லேர் உழவர்","உழவர்",2,"seythi","scene","hunters"],["எருவை","எருவை",6,"pul","scene","vulture"],["யாஅத்து","யா",7,"maram","scene",""],["ஊன்தடி","ஊன்",9,"unavu","gift_food",""],["முதுநரி","நரி",10,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:10"},"a:194":{"book":"akananuru","num":195,"t":["பேர்உறை தலைஇய பெரும்புலர் வகைறை,","ஏர்இடம் படுத்த இருமறுப் பூழிப்","புறமாறு பெற்ற பூவல் ஈரத்து,","ஊன்கிழித் தன்ன செஞ்சுவல் நெடுஞ்சால்,","வித்திய மருங்கின் விதைபல நாறி,","இரலைநல் மானினம் பரந்தவை போலக்,","கோடுடைத் தலைக்குடை சூடிய வினைஞர்,","கறங்குபறை சீரின் இரங்க வாங்கி,","களைகால் கழீஇய பெரும்புன வரகின்","கவைக்கதிர் இரும்புறம் கதூஉ உண்ட,","குடுமி நெற்றி நெடுமாத் தோகை","காமர் கலவம் பரப்பி, ஏமுறக்","கொல்லை உழவர் கூழ்நிழல் ஒழித்த","வல்லிலைக் குருந்தின் வாங்குசினை இருந்து,","கிளிகடி மகளிரின் விளிபடப் பயிரும்","கார்மன் இதுவால் - தோழி !- 'போர்மிகக்","கொடுஞ்சி நெடுந்தேர் பூண்ட, கடும்பரி,","விரிஉளை, நல்மான் கடைஇ","வருதும்' என்று, அவர் தெளித்த போழ்தே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that this is the very rainy season he named when he promised to come driving his chariot: rain has soaked the ploughed red furrows, the sown seed has sprouted like a spread herd of deer, and a peacock on a kurundhu branch calls like girls shooing parrots.","n":"The whole description is of a working field (ploughing, weeding to the beat of a drum, the varagu harvest), so the season is fixed by labour as much as by rain; the peacock's call, compared to girls' shouts in the millet field, is the only sound, and it calls no one home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaalam","mazhai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 194","lb":"A194","poet":"இடைக்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பருவங் கண்டு ஆற்றாமைமீதூரத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பருவங் கண்டு ஆற்றாமைமீதூரத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["இரலைநல் மானினம்","மான்",6,"maa","simile",""],["வினைஞர்","வினைஞர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["பறை","பறை",8,"parai","scene",""],["வரகின்","வரகு",9,"unavu","scene",""],["தோகை","மயில்",11,"pul","scene","peacock"],["கொல்லை உழவர்","உழவர்",13,"seythi","scene",""],["குருந்தின்","குருந்து",14,"maram","scene",""],["கிளிகடி மகளிரின்","மகளிர்",15,"seythi","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:10"},"a:195":{"book":"akananuru","num":196,"t":["'அருஞ்சுரம் இறந்தஎன் பெருத்தோட் குறுமகள்","திருந்துவேல் விடலையொடு வரும்' எனத் தாயே","புனைமாண் இஞ்சி பூவல் ஊட்டி,","மனைமணல் அடுத்து, மாலை நாற்றி,","உவந்து, இனிது அயரும் என்ப: யானும்,","மான்பிணை நோக்கின் மடநல் லாளை","ஈன்ற நட்பிற்கு அருளான் ஆயினும்","இன்னகை முறுவல் ஏழையைப் பல்நாள்,","கூந்தல் வாரி: நுசுப்புஇவர்ந்து, ஓம்பிய","நலம்புனை உதவியோ உடையன் மன்னே:","அஃது அறி கிற்பினோ நன்றுமன் தில்ல,","அறுவை தோயும் ஒருபெருங் குடுமி,","சிறுபை நாற்றிய பல்தலைக் கொடுங்கோல்,","ஆகுவது அறியும் முதுவாய், வேல!","கூறுக மாதோ, நின் கழங்கின் திட்பம்;","மாறா வருபனி கலுழும் கங்குலில்,","ஆனாது துயருமெம் கண்இனிது படீஇயர்","எம்மனை முந்துறத் தருமோ?","தன்மனை உய்க்குமோ? யாதவன் குறிப்பே?"],"p":"","r":"The mother says the other mother will gladly paint her walls and spread sand for the return of her son with the girl, while she herself, who combed the girl's hair and carried her on her hip, asks the diviner with his staff and his molucca beads whether the youth will bring the girl first to her house or take her straight to his own.","n":"Her claim is not on the youth's love but on her own care for the girl; she knows she has no right to his kindness (அருளான் ஆயினும்) and asks only for a sight of her daughter. The velan is called here not to cure lovesickness but to read the eloped pair's road home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","velan","makkal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 195","lb":"A195","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட்போக்கிய நற்றாய்சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட்போக்கிய நற்றாய்சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மான்பிணை","பிணை",6,"maa","scene","doe"],["வேல","வேலன்",14,"seythi","scene","diviner"],["கழங்கின்","கழங்கு",15,"pira","scene","divining seeds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:10"},"a:196":{"book":"akananuru","num":197,"t":["நெடுங்கொடி நுடங்கும் நறவுமலி பாக்கத்து,","நாள்துறைப் பட்ட மோட்டிரு வராஅல்","துடிக்கண் கொழுங்குறை நொடுத்து, உண்டுஆடி","வேட்டம் மறந்து, துஞ்சும் கொழுநர்க்குப் பாட்டி","ஆம்பல் அகலிலை, அமலைவெஞ் சோறு","தீம்புளிப் பிரம்பின் திரள்கனி பெய்து,","விடியல் வைகறை இடூஉம் ஊர!","தொடுகலம்: குறுக வாரல் - தந்தை","கண்கவின் அழித்ததன் தப்பல், தெறுவர,","ஒன்றுமொழிக் கோசர்க் கொன்று, முரண்போகிய,","கடுந்தேர்த் திதியன் அழுந்தை, கொடுங்குழை","அன்னி மிஞிலியின் இயலும்","நின்நலத் தகுவியை முயங்கிய மார்பே!"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man of the town, where women at dawn bring hot rice and cane fruit on lily leaves to husbands asleep after eating varaal fish and drinking, that he must not come near or touch her, since his chest has embraced the woman fit for him who walks as proud as Anni Minjili after Thithiyan killed the Kosar.","n":"The comparison is barbed: Anni Minjili's pride was won by revenge for her father's blinding, so the rival is cast as a woman triumphant over the wife. The opening scene of men who forget their fishing after a feast while their women still feed them at dawn may be heard as the wife's own household, but the poem leaves it as the town's picture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","karpu","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 196","lb":"A196","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தையிற்பிரிந்துவந்த தலைமகற்குக் கிழத்தி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","பரத்தையிற்பிரிந்துவந்த தலைமகற்குக் கிழத்தி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["vekuli","அலை"]],"k":[["நறவு","நறவு",1,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வராஅல்","வரால்",2,"pira","scene","murrel fish"],["பாட்டி","பாட்டி",4,"seythi","scene",""],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",5,"maram","scene",""],["சோறு","சோறு",5,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பிரம்பின்","பிரம்பு",6,"maram","gift_food",""]],"en":[["king","திதியன்",11],["town","அழுந்தை",11]],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:10"},"a:197":{"book":"akananuru","num":198,"t":["மாமலர் வண்ணம் இழந்த கண்ணும்,","பூநெகிழ் அணையின் சாஅய தோளும்","நன்னர் மாக்கள் விழைவனர் ஆய்ந்த","தொன்னலம் இழந்த துயரமொடு, என்னதூஉம்","இனையல் - வாழி; தோழி !- முனை எழ","முன்னுவர் ஓட்டிய முரண்மிகு திருவின்,","மறமிகு தானைக், கண்ணன் எழினி","தேமுது குன்றம் இறந்தனர் ஆயினும்,","நீடலர் யாழநின் நிரைவளை நெகிழத்-","தோள்தாழ்வு இருளிய குவைஇருங் கூந்தல்","மடவோள் தழீஇய விறலோன் மார்பில்","புன்தலைப் புதல்வன் ஊர்புஇழிந் தாங்கு,","கடுஞ்சூல் மடப்பிடி தழீஇய வெண்கோட்டு","இனம்சால் வேழம், கன்றுஊர்பு இழிதரப்,","பள்ளி கொள்ளும் பனிச்சுரம் நீந்தி,","ஒள்ளிணர்க் கொன்றை ஓங்குமலை அத்தம்","வினைவலி யுறூஉம் நெஞ்சமொடு","இனையர்ஆகி, நம் பிரிந்திசி னோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the grieving woman not to despair: though her lover has crossed Kannan Ezhini's Themuthu hill, he will not stay long, for on that cold road a tusker sleeps embracing his pregnant mate while their calf climbs on and slides off him, as a small son clambers on the chest of a father who holds his wife.","n":"The simile runs backwards: the human family is the vehicle and the elephants the tenor, so the man on the road is shown his own household in the beasts. The friend's comfort rests on that sight: a man who has seen it cannot linger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","makkal","ilvaazhkkai","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 197","lb":"A197","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்டதலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறீஇயது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",13,"maa","iraicchi",""],["வேழம்","வேழம்",14,"maa","iraicchi","elephant"],["கன்றுஊர்பு","கன்று",14,"maa","iraicchi",""],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",16,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","கண்ணன் எழினி",7],["hill","தேமுது குன்றம்",8]],"dv":[["iraicchi","வேழம்","confirmed","2/2","the man who passes this family on the road will think of his wife and child and hurry home"]],"ch":"a:10"},"a:198":{"book":"akananuru","num":199,"t":["'கூறுவம் கொல்லோ? கூறலம் கொல்?' எனக்","கரந்த காமம் கைந்நிறுக் கல்லாது,","நயந்துநாம் விட்ட நல்மொழி நம்பி,","அரைநாள் யாமத்து விழுமழை கரந்து,","கார்விரை கமழும் கூந்தல், தூவினை","நுண்நூல் ஆகம் பொருந்தினன், வெற்பின்","இளமழை சூழ்ந்த மடமயில் போல,","வண்டுவழிப் படரத், தண்மலர் மேய்ந்து,","வில்வகுப் புற்ற நல்வாங்கு குடைச்சூல்","அஞ்சிலம்பு ஒடுக்கி அஞ்சினள் வந்து,","துஞ்சுஊர் யாமத்து முயங்கினள், பெயர்வோள்,","ஆன்ற கற்பின் சான்ற பெரியள்,","அம்மா அரிவையோ அல்லள்: தெனாஅது","ஆஅய் நல்நாட்டு அணங்குடைச் சிலம்பிற்,","கவிரம் பெயரிய உருகெழு கவாஅன்,","ஏர்மலர் நிறைசுனை உறையும்","சூர்மகள் மாதோ என்னும்- என் நெஞ்சே!"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart that the girl who, trusting his words, came at midnight through the rain with her anklets hushed, embraced him while the town slept and went away, was not a mere woman of perfect virtue but surely the dread maiden who lives in the flower-filled spring on the peak called Kaviram in Aay's southern land.","n":"The awe follows the union, not first sight: having held her, he still cannot think her mortal. The peacock wreathed in young cloud gives her coming its look, while the spirit of the mountain spring gives it its danger, and both images belong to the hill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","anangu","azhagu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 198","lb":"A198","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"புணர்ந்து நீங்கியதலைமகளது போக்கு நோக்கிய தலைமகன் தன்நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","தலைமகன் தன்நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["மடமயில்","மயில்",7,"pul","simile",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",8,"pira","scene",""],["சுனை","சுனை",16,"pira","scene",""],["சூர்மகள்","சூர்மகள்",17,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[["king","ஆஅய்",14],["hill","கவிரம்",15]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:10"},"a:199":{"book":"akananuru","num":200,"t":["கரைபாய் வெண்திரை கடுப்பப், பலஉடன்,","நிரைகால் ஒற்றலின், கல்சேர்பு உதிரும்","வரைசேர் மராஅத்து ஊர்மலர் பெயல் செத்து,","உயங்கல் யானை நீர்நசைக்கு அலமரச்,","சிலம்பி வலந்த வறுஞ்சினை வற்றல்","அலங்கல் உலவை அரிநிழல் அசைஇத்,","திரங்குமரல் கவ்விய கையறு தொகுநிலை,","அரம்தின் ஊசித் திரள்நுதி அன்ன,","திண்ணிலை எயிற்ற செந்நாய் எடுத்தலின்:","வளிமுனைப் பூளையன் ஒய்யென்று அலறிய","கெடுமான் இனநிரை தரீஇய கலையே","கதிர்மாய் மாலை ஆண்குரல் விளிக்கும்","கடல்போற் கானம் பிற்படப், 'பிறர்போல்","செல்வேம்ஆயின், எம் செலவுநன்று' என்னும்","ஆசை உள்ளம் அசைவின்று துரப்ப,","நீ செலற்கு உரியை - நெஞ்சே!- வேய்போல்","தடையின மன்னும், தண்ணிய, திரண்ட,","பெருந்தோள் அரிவை ஒழியக், குடாஅது,","இரும்பொன் வாகைப் பெருந்துறைச் செருவில்,","பொலம்பூண் நன்னன் பொருதுகளத்து ஒழிய,","வலம்படு கொற்றம் தந்த வாய்வாள்,","களங்காய்க் கண்ணி நார்முடிச் சேரல்","இழந்த நாடு தந்தன்ன","வளம்பெரிது பெறினும், வாரலென் யானே"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart, which urges him to go for wealth like other men, that it may go, but he will not leave his wife with her bamboo-smooth shoulders, not even for riches as great as the land Narmudi Cheral won back when Nannan fell at the battle of Vakai.","n":"The road is drawn as a place of failing care: the thirsty elephant mistakes falling flowers for rain, the wild dogs scatter the deer, and only the stag at sunset calls its lost herd back with a male cry. That stag is the one creature doing what the man chooses to do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","selvam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 199","lb":"A199","poet":"கல்லாடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் கடைக்கூட்டியநெஞ்சிற்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"பொருள் கடைக்கூட்டியநெஞ்சிற்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மராஅத்து","மரா",3,"maram","simile",""],["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene",""],["சிலம்பி","சிலம்பி",5,"pira","scene","spider"],["உலவை","உலவை",6,"maram","scene",""],["மரல்","மரல்",7,"maram","scene",""],["செந்நாய்","செந்நாய்",9,"maa","simile",""],["பூளையன்","பூளை",10,"maram","scene",""],["கலையே","கலை",11,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["வேய்போல்","வேய்",16,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["battle","வாகைப் பெருந்துறைச்",19],["king","நன்னன்",20],["king","நார்முடிச் சேரல்",22]],"dv":[["iraicchi","கலையே","confirmed","1/1","even the stag on the road gathers its own at nightfall, so the man who stays with his wife is doing what the road itself shows"]],"ch":"a:10"},"a:200":{"book":"akananuru","num":201,"t":["நிலாவின் இலங்கு மணல்மலி மறுகில்,","புலால்அம் சேரிப், புல்வேய் குரம்பை,","ஊர்என உணராச் சிறுமையொடு, நீர்உடுத்து,","இன்னா உறையுட்டு ஆயினும், இன்பம்","ஒருநாள் உறைந்திசி னோர்க்கும், வழிநாள்,","தம்பதி மறக்கும் பண்பின் எம்பதி","வந்தனை சென்மோ - வளைமேய் பரப்ப!-","பொம்மற் படுதிரை கம்மென உடைதரும்","மரன்ஓங்கு ஒருசிறை பல பாராட்டி,","எல்லை எம்மொடு கழிப்பி, எல்உற,","நல்தேர் பூட்டலும் உரியீர்: அற்றன்று,","சேந்தனிர் செல்குவிர் ஆயின், யாமும்","எம்வரை அளவையின் பெட்குவம்","நும் ஒப்பதுவோ? உரைத்திசின் எமக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, having read the girl's wish, invites the man of the shore to their fishing hamlet of thatched huts that smells of fish and is hardly a town, yet makes anyone who stays one day forget his own city; he may yoke his chariot and go by evening, but if he stays, they too will welcome him as far as their station allows.","n":"The invitation is made through modesty about the village (புலால்அம் சேரி, a fish-smelling settlement) and a boast that it makes guests forget home, so the friend accepts his suit while stating the gap in rank (எம்வரை அளவையின்) that he must accept with it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","virundhu","kudi","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 200","lb":"A200","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகள் குறிப்பறிந்ததோழி தலைமகற்குக் குறைநயப்பக் கூறியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","குறைநயப்பக் கூறியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புல்வேய்","புல்",2,"maram","scene","thatch grass"],["வளைமேய்","வளை",7,"pira","scene","conch"],["மரன்","மரம்",9,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:10"},"a:201":{"book":"akananuru","num":202,"t":["அம்ம, வாழி - தோழி - பொன்னின்","அவிர்எழில் நுடங்கும் அணிகிளர் ஓடை","வினைநவில் யானை விறற்போர்ப் பாண்டியன்","புகழ்மலி சிறப்பின் கொற்கை முன்துறை,","அவிர்கதிர் முத்தமொடு வலம்புரி சொரிந்து,","தழைஅணிப் பொலிந்த கோடுஏந்து அல்குல்","பழையர் மகளிர் பனித்துறைப் பரவ,","பகலோன் மறைந்த அந்தி ஆர்இடை,","உருகெழு பெருங்கடல் உவவுக் கிளர்ந்தாங்கு,","அலரும் மன்று பட்டன்றே: அன்னையும்","பொருந்தா கண்ணள். வெய்ய உயிர்க்கும்' என்று","எவன் கையற்றனை, இகுளை? சோழர்","வெண்ணெல் வைப்பின் நல்நாடு பெறினும்,","ஆண்டு அமைந்து உறைகுநர் அல்லர்- முனா அது","வான்புகு தலைய குன்றத்து கவாஅன்,","பெருங்கை எண்கின் பேழ்வாய் ஏற்றை","இருள்துணிந் தன்ன குவவுமயிர்க் குருளைத்","தோல்முலைப் பிணவொடு திளைக்கும்","வேனில் நீடிய சுரன் இறந்தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the grieving woman why she despairs that gossip has filled the common ground like the sea swelling at full moon off the Pandya's Korkai and that her mother lies sleepless and sighing: the man who crossed the long summer desert, where a male bear sports with his she-bear and their cub on the hill slope, would not settle there even if he were given the rich rice country of the Cholas.","n":"The friend first repeats the woman's own complaint (lines 1 to 11) and only then answers it, so the gossip and the mother's sighing are given in the woman's voice. The comfort rests on a hyperbole of refusal (not even the Chola land) rather than on a promise of his return date.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","alar","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 201","lb":"A201","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்டதலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""],["முத்தமொடு","முத்தம்",5,"pira","scene","pearl"],["வலம்புரி","வலம்புரி",5,"pira","scene","right-whorled conch"],["பழையர்","பழையர்",7,"seythi","scene","fisher folk"],["பெருங்கடல்","கடல்",9,"pira","simile",""],["வெண்ணெல்","நெல்",13,"unavu","scene",""],["ஏற்றை","எண்கு ஏற்றை",16,"maa","iraicchi","male bear"],["குருளைத்","குருளை",17,"maa","scene","cub"],["பிணவொடு","பிணவு",18,"maa","iraicchi","she-bear"]],"en":[["king","பாண்டியன்",3],["town","கொற்கை",4],["king","சோழர்",12]],"dv":[["iraicchi","ஏற்றை","confirmed","1/1","the man who saw even the bear keep to its mate and young on that road will not stay away from his own woman"]],"ch":"a:11"},"a:202":{"book":"akananuru","num":203,"t":["வயங்குவெள் அருவிய குன்றத்துக் கவாஅன்,","கயந்தலை மடப்பிடி இனன் ஏமார்ப்பப்,","புலிப்பகை வென்ற புண்கூர் யானை","கல்லகச் சிலம்பில் கைஎடுத்து உயிர்ப்பின்,","நல்இணர் வேங்கை நறுவீ கொல்லன்","குருகுஊது மிதிஉலைப் பிதிர்வின் பொங்கி,","சிறுபல் மின்மினி போலப், பலஉடன்","மணிநிற இரும்புதல் தாவும் நாட!","யாமே அன்றியும் உளர்கொல் - பானாள்,","உத்தி அரவின் பைத்தலை துமிய","உரஉரும் உட்குவரு நனந்தலைத்,","தவிர்வுஇல் உள்ளமொடு எஃகு துணையாகக்,","கனைஇருள் பரந்த கல்லதர்ச் சிறுநெறி","தேராது வரூஉம் நின்வயின்","ஆர்அஞர் அரும்படர் நீந்து வோரே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lover who leaves after a night meeting that in his hill, where a wounded tusker that beat off a tiger lifts its trunk and sighs among its young she-elephants and the vengai blossoms fly up like a smith's sparks onto the dark bushes, she wonders whether anyone but themselves suffers as they do while he comes at midnight, spear for company, down the dark stony path where thunder splits the hooded snake.","n":"The question 'are there others besides us?' turns the women's fear for him into pressure to marry: the colophon says she is urging marriage, and the poem does it only by counting the dangers. A traditional reading takes the wounded tusker among its she-elephants as the lover who braves the night to reach the girl; it is left as scene here because only one correspondence (danger overcome) is clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","varaivu","iravu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 202","lb":"A202","poet":"ஆவூர்கிழார் மகனார் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக் குறிக்கண் வந்து நீங்கும் தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லி வரைவு கடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["அருவிய","அருவி",1,"pira","scene",""],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",2,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["புலிப்பகை","புலி",3,"maa","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",5,"maram","scene",""],["கொல்லன்","கொல்லன்",5,"seythi","simile","smith"],["மின்மினி","மின்மினி",7,"pira","simile","firefly"],["இரும்புதல்","புதல்",8,"maram","scene",""],["அரவின்","அரவு",10,"pira","scene","snake"],["உரஉரும்","உரும்",11,"pira","scene","thunder"],["எஃகு","எஃகு",12,"seythi","ornament","spear"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:11"},"a:203":{"book":"akananuru","num":204,"t":["'உவக்குநள் ஆயினும், உடலுநள் ஆயினும்,","யாய் அறிந்து உணர்க' என்னார், தீ வாய்","அலர்வினை மேவல் அம்பற் பெண்டிர்,","'இன்னள் இனையள், நின்மகள்' எனப் பல்நாள்","எனக்கு வந்து உரைப்பவும், தனக்குஉரைப்பு அறியேன்,","'நாணுவள் இவள்' என, நனிகரந்து உறையும்","யான்இவ் வறுமனை ஒழிய, தானே,","'அன்னை அறியின், இவணுறை வாழ்க்கை","எனக்கு எளிது ஆகல் இல்' எனக், கழற்கால்","மின்னொளிர் நெடுவேல் இளையோன் முன்னுறப்,","பன்மலை அருஞ்சுரம் போகிய தனக்கு, யான்","அன்னேன் அன்மை நன்வாய் யாக,","மான்அதர் மயங்கிய மலைமுதல் சிறுநெறி","வெய்து இடையுறாஅது எய்தி, முன்னர்ப்","புல்லென் மாமலைப் புலம்புகொள் சீறூர்","செல்விருந்து ஆற்றித், துச்சில் இருத்த,","நுனை குழைத்து அலமரும் நொச்சி","மனைகெழு பெண்டுயான் ஆகுக மன்னே!"],"p":"","r":"The mother whose daughter has eloped says that she kept silent when the gossiping women told her day after day what the girl was doing, sparing her shame, but the girl feared her mother would know and left with her spear-bearing young man across the hills; may it prove that she was not such a mother, and may she herself be the housewife of the lonely hill hamlet with its nochi hedge who takes the girl in as a passing guest when she arrives safe.","n":"Her wish is not that the girl come back but that she be the stranger on the road who shelters her: a mother's care transferred to the place the daughter has chosen. Her silence, meant as kindness, is what the daughter read as danger, so the poem turns on a misunderstanding between them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","alar","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 203","lb":"A203","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய 1தாய்சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["nartaay","nenju",null,"kalavu","மகட் போக்கிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கழற்கால்","கழல்",9,"pira","ornament","warrior's anklet"],["நெடுவேல்","வேல்",10,"seythi","ornament","spear"],["மான்அதர்","மான்",13,"maa","scene","deer"],["நொச்சி","நொச்சி",17,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:204":{"book":"akananuru","num":205,"t":["உலகுடன் நிழற்றிய தொலையா வெண்குடைக்,","கடல்போல் தானைக், கலிமா வழுதி","வென்றுஅமர் உழந்த வியன்பெரும் பாசறைச்","சென்றுவினை முடித்தனம் ஆயின், இன்றே","கார்ப்பெயற்கு எதிரிய காண்தகு புறவில்,","கணங்கொள் வண்டின் அம்சிறைத் தொழுதி","மணங்கமழ் முல்லை மாலை ஆர்ப்ப,","உதுக்காண் வந்தன்று பொழுதே: வல்விரைந்து,","செல்க, பாக! நின் நல்வினை நெடுந்தேர்-","வெண்ணெல் அரிநர் மடிவாய்த் தண்ணுமை","பன்மலர்ப் பொய்கைப் படுபுள் ஓப்பும்","காய்நெல் படப்பை வாணன் சிறுகுடித்","தண்டலை கமழும் கூந்தல,","ஒண்தொடி மடந்தை தோள்இணை பெறவே"],"p":"","r":"The man, his work done in the Pandya's great camp, tells his charioteer that the evening of the rains has come over the grassland, with bee swarms humming at the fragrant mullai, so he must drive fast to reach the shoulders of his bright-bangled wife, whose hair smells like the groves of Vaanan's Sirukudi where the reapers' drums scare birds from the flowered pools.","n":"The season he promised to return in has arrived on its own (உதுக்காண் வந்தன்று பொழுதே), so his haste is the keeping of a word. The camp opens with the king's world-shading umbrella and sea-like army; the close sets that public scene against a farming village's fragrance in his wife's hair.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 204","lb":"A204","poet":"மதுரைக் காமக்கணி நப்பாலத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன்தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கலிமா","மா",2,"maa","scene","horse"],["வண்டின்","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene","bee"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",7,"maram","scene",""],["வெண்ணெல்","நெல்",10,"unavu","simile",""],["அரிநர்","அரிநர்",10,"seythi","simile","reapers"],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",10,"parai","simile",""],["படுபுள்","புள்",11,"pul","simile",""],["காய்நெல்","நெல்",12,"unavu","simile",""]],"en":[["king","வழுதி",2],["patron","வாணன்",12],["town","சிறுகுடித்",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:205":{"book":"akananuru","num":206,"t":["'உயிர்கலந்து ஒன்றிய தொன்றுபடு நட்பின்","செயிர்தீர் நெஞ்சமொடு செறிந்தோர் போலத்,","தையல்! நின்வயின் பிரியலம் யாம்' எனப்","பொய்வல் உள்ளமொடு புரிவுஉணக் கூறி,","துணிவில் கொள்கையர் ஆகி, இனியே","நோய்மலி வருத்தமொடு நுதல்பசப் பூர","நாம்அழத் துறந்தனர் ஆயினும், தாமே","வாய்மொழி நிலைஇய சேண்விளங்கு நல்லிசை","வளங்கெழு கோசர் விளங்குபடை நூறி,","நிலங்கொள வெஃகிய பொலம்பூண் கிள்ளி,","பூவிரி நெடுங்கழி நாப்பண், பெரும்பெயர்க்","காவிரிப் படப்பைப் பட்டினத் தன்ன","செழுநகர் நல்விருந்து அயர்மார், ஏமுற","விழுநிதி எளிதினின் எய்துக தில்ல-","மழைகால் அற்சிரத்து மாலிருள் நீக்கி,","நீடுஅமை நிவந்த நிழல்படு சிலம்பில்;","கடாஅ யானைக் கவுள்மருங்கு உதிர","ஆம்ஊர்பு இழிதரு காமர் சென்னி,","புலிஉரி வரியதள் கடுப்பக், கலிசிறந்து,","நாட்பூ வேங்கை நறுமலர் உதிர,","மேக்குஎழு பெருஞ்சினை ஏறிக் கணக்கலை","கூப்பிடூஉ உகளும் குன்றகச் சிறுநெறிக்","கல்பிறங்கு ஆரிடை விலங்கிய","சொல்பெயர் தேஎத்த சுரன் இறந்தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the friend who urges her to bear up that her lover, who swore with a lying heart that he would never leave her, like those joined in an old flawless friendship of mingled lives, has gone and left her weeping; even so, may he easily gain wealth enough to feed guests in a house as rich as Killi's Kaveri port, as he crosses the hill path of strange speech in the cool season where an elephant's musth runs and the male monkey calls and leaps on the high branch.","n":"The reproach and the blessing come in one breath: she names his oath a lie and still wishes his errand success, and what she wishes him is wealth for hospitality, the householder's duty, not riches for himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vaaimai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 205","lb":"A205","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண்வேறுபட்ட தலைவி வற்புறுக்கும் தோழிக்குச்சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"வற்புறுக்கும் தோழிக்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நீடுஅமை","அமை",16,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["யானைக்","யானை",17,"maa","scene",""],["புலிஉரி","புலி",19,"maa","simile","tiger"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",20,"maram","simile",""],["கணக்கலை","கலை",21,"maa","scene","male monkey"]],"en":[["king","கிள்ளி",10],["river","காவிரிப்",12],["town","பட்டினத்",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:206":{"book":"akananuru","num":207,"t":["என்னெனப் படுங்கொல்- தோழி !- நல்மகிழ்ப்","பேடிப் பெண்கொண்டு! ஆடுகை கடுப்ப","நகுவரப் பணைத்த திரிமருப்பு எருமை","மயிர்க்கவின் கொண்ட மாத்தோல் இரும்புறம்,","சிறுதொழில் மகாஅர் ஏறிச், சேணோர்க்குத்","துறுகல் மந்தியின் தோன்றும் ஊரன்,","மாரி ஈங்கை மாந்தளிர் அன்ன","அம்மா மேனி, ஆயிழை, மகளிர்","ஆரந் தாங்கிய அலர்முலை ஆகத்து","ஆராக் காதலொடு தாரிடை குழைய,","முழவுமுகம் புலரா விழவுடை வியனகர்","வதுவை மேவலன் ஆகலின், அதுபுலந்து","அடுபோர் வேளிர் வீரை முன்றுறை,","நெடுவெள் உப்பின் நிரம்பாக் குப்பை,","பெரும் பெயற்கு உருகி யா அங்குத்","திருந்திழை நெகிழ்ந்தன, தடமென் தோளே!"],"p":"","r":"The woman, refusing the go-between, asks her friend what this will be called: the lord of a town where small boys climb the dark back of a buffalo whose twisted horns spread as ludicrously as a dancer's gestures, looking from afar like monkeys on a boulder, delights in marrying women with mango-sprout skin in his festive city, and sulking at it her broad soft shoulders have melted like the salt heaps of Veerai in heavy rain.","n":"The mockery is in the image: the great buffalo carries boys about like a toy and looks absurd, and so does he, ridden by every woman. Her own body, not her words, gives the refusal its force: the shoulders that waste like rained-on salt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 206","lb":"A206","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"வாயில் வேண்டிச் சென்றவிறலிக்குத் தலைமகள் வாயில் மறுத்து.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","virali",null,"karpu","வாயில் மறுத்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",3,"maa","ullurai",""],["மந்தியின்","மந்தி",6,"maa","simile","monkey"],["ஈங்கை","ஈங்கை",7,"maram","simile",""],["மாந்தளிர்","மா",7,"maram","simile","mango"],["ஆரந்","ஆரம்",9,"pira","ornament","necklace"],["முழவுமுகம்","முழவு",11,"parai","scene",""],["உப்பின்","உப்பு",14,"pira","simile","salt"]],"en":[["town","வீரை",13]],"dv":[["ullurai","எருமை","confirmed","1/1","the husband and the women who make free with him"]],"ch":"a:11"},"a:207":{"book":"akananuru","num":208,"t":["அணங்குடை முந்நீர் பரந்த செறுவின்","உணங்குதிறம் பெயர்ந்த வெண்கல் அமிழ்தம்","குடபுல மருங்கின் உய்ம்மார், புள்ளோர்த்துப்","படைஅமைத்து எழுந்த பெருஞ்செய் ஆடவர்","நிரைப்பரம் பொறைய நரைப்புற கழுதைக்","குறைக்குளம்பு உதைத்த கற்பிறழ் இயவின்,","வெஞ்சுரம் போழ்ந்த அஞ்சுவரு கவலை,","மிஞிறுஆர் கடாஅம் கரந்துவிடு கவுள,","வெயில்தின வருந்திய, நீடுமருப்பு ஒருத்தல்","பிணரழி பெருங்கை புரண்ட கூவல்","தெண்கண் உவரிக் குறைக்குட முகவை,","அறனிலாளன் தோண்ட, வெய் துயிர்த்துப்,","பிறைநுதல் வியர்ப்ப, உண்டனள் கொல்லோ-","தேம்கலந்து அளைஇய தீம்பால் ஏந்திக்","கூழை உளர்ந்து மொழிமை கூறவும்,","மறுத்த சொல்லள் ஆகி,","வெறுத்த உள்ளமொடு உண்ணா தோளே!"],"p":"","r":"The mother whose daughter eloped wonders whether the girl, who refused with a sick heart the sweet milk mixed with honey that her mother held out while combing her hair and coaxing her, has drunk, sighing and with sweat on her crescent brow, the brackish pot of water her heartless lover drew from a well dug where a sun-scorched tusker had rolled, on the dread forked desert path cut by the hooves of the salt traders' grey donkeys.","n":"The road is built from the salt trade, carried from the sea to the western uplands, so the desert here is a merchant road, not wild land. The poem's last image is of the girl at home refusing milk, so the mother's question about the well water is also about the comfort she failed to give.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 207","lb":"A207","poet":"மதுரை எழுத்தாளன் சேந்தம்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய 4தாய்சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["nartaay","nenju",null,"kalavu","மகட் போக்கிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முந்நீர்","முந்நீர்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["செறுவின்","செறு",1,"pira","scene","salt pan"],["புள்ளோர்த்துப்","புள்",3,"pul","scene","omen bird"],["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",4,"seythi","scene","salt traders"],["கழுதைக்","கழுதை",5,"maa","scene",""],["மிஞிறுஆர்","மிஞிறு",8,"pira","scene","bee"],["ஒருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",9,"maa","scene","tusker"],["கூவல்","கூவல்",10,"pira","scene","well"],["உவரிக்","உவர்",11,"pira","gift_food","brackish water"],["தேம்கலந்து","தேன்",14,"unavu","gift_food","honey"],["தீம்பால்","பால்",14,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:208":{"book":"akananuru","num":209,"t":["யாம இரவின் நெடுங்கடை நின்று,","தேமுதிர் சிமையக் குன்றம் பாடும்","நுண்கோல் அகவுநர் வேண்டின், வெண்கோட்டு","அண்ணல் யானை ஈயும் வண்மகிழ்","வெளியன் வேண்மான் ஆஅய் எயினன்,","அளிஇயல் வாழ்க்கைப் பாழிப் பறந்தலை,","இழையணி யானை இயல்தேர் மிஞிலியொடு","நண்பகல் உற்ற செருவில் புண்கூர்ந்து,","ஒள்வாள் மயங்குஅமர் வீழ்ந்தெனப், 'புள்ஒருங்கு","அங்கண் விசும்பின் விளங்கு ஞாயிற்று","ஒண்கதிர் தெறாமை சிறகரிற் கோலி,","நிழல்செய்து உழறல் காணேன், யான் எனப்","படுகளம் காண்டல் செல்லான், சினஞ் சிறந்து,","உருவினை நன்னன், அருளான் கரப்பப்,","பெருவிதுப் புற்ற பல்வேள் மகளிர்","குரூஉப்பூம் பைந்தார் அருக்கிய பூசல்,","வசைவிடக் கடக்கும் வயங்குபெருந் தானை","அகுதை கிளைதந் தாங்கு, மிகுபெயல்","உப்புச்சிறை நில்லா வெள்ளம் போல,","நாணுவரை நில்லாக் காமம் நண்ணி","நல்கினள், வாழியர், வந்தே- ஓரி","பல்பழப் பலவின் பயங்கெழு கொல்லிக்","கார்மலர் கடுப்ப நாறும்,","ஓர்நுண் ஓதி மாஅ யோளே!"],"p":"","r":"The lover, leaving after their meeting, blesses the girl of fine dark hair, fragrant as the flowers of Ori's Kolli, who came and gave herself with a desire that shame could not hold back, like a flood the salt-pan bank cannot stop and like Akuthai who brought back the kin after the outcry of the Vel women when Aay Eyinan fell to Minjili at Paazhi and Nannan would not look on the field.","n":"Most of the poem is a puram story told inside a single simile: Eyinan the generous, killed at noon, the birds that should have shaded him, Nannan's refusal to see. What it measures is the girl's boldness, a thing usually blamed in akam, here blessed. Line 18 (அகுதை கிளைதந்தாங்கு) is obscure, and the turn of the Nannan episode is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","naanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 208","lb":"A208","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"புணர்ந்து நீங்கும்தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","புணர்ந்து நீங்கும்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","gift_food","elephant given"],["புள்ஒருங்கு","புள்",9,"pul","scene",""],["உப்புச்சிறை","உப்பு",19,"pira","simile","salt pan"],["வெள்ளம்","வெள்ளம்",19,"pira","simile","flood"],["பலவின்","பலா",22,"maram","scene",""],["கார்மலர்","மலர்",23,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["patron","ஆஅய் எயினன்",5],["battle","பாழிப் பறந்தலை",6],["king","மிஞிலியொடு",7],["king","நன்னன்",14],["king","அகுதை",18],["king","ஓரி",21],["hill","கொல்லிக்",22]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:209":{"book":"akananuru","num":210,"t":["'தோளும் தொல்கவின் தொலைந்தன; நாளும்","அன்னையும் அருந்துயர் உற்றனள், அலரே,","பொன்னணி நெடுந்தேர்த் தென்னர் கோமான்,","எழுஉறழ் திணிதோள் இயல்தேர்ச் செழியன்,","நேரா எழுவர் அடிப்படக் கடந்த","ஆலங் கானத்து ஆர்ப்பினும் பெரிது என,","ஆழல் வாழி, தோழி !- அவரரே,","மாஅல் யானை மறப்போர்ப் புல்லி","காம்புடை நெடுவரை வேங்கடத்து உம்பர்","அறைஇறந்து அகன்றனர் ஆயினும், நிறைஇறந்து","உள்ளார் ஆதலோ அரிதே - செவ்வேல்","முள்ளூர் மன்னன் கழல்தொடிக் காரி","செல்லா நல்லிசை நிறுத்த வல்வில்","ஓரிக் கொன்று சேரலர்க்கு ஈத்த","செவ்வேர்ப் பலவின் பயங்கெழு கொல்லி,","நிலைபெறு கடவுள் ஆக்கிய,","பலர்புகழ் பாவை அன்னநின் நலனே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman not to weep that her shoulders have lost their beauty, her mother grieves and the gossip is louder than the roar at Alankanam when Chezhiyan beat the seven: though her man has gone beyond Pulli's Vengadam, he cannot forget her beauty, which is like the statue set up by the god on Kolli, the hill Kaari of Mullur gave to the Cheras after killing Ori.","n":"The friend first speaks the woman's complaint in her own words and then answers it, as in 201. Both measures in the poem come from puram: a Pandya victory for the gossip, a Kolli statue that outlived the change of its lords for her beauty, which is the friend's point: his love will last as the statue did.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","alar","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 209","lb":"A209","poet":"கல்லாடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்டதலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறீஇயது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"வற்புறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",8,"maa","scene",""],["காம்புடை","காம்பு",9,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பலவின்","பலா",15,"maram","simile",""],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",16,"deyvam","simile",""]],"en":[["king","தென்னர் கோமான்",3],["king","செழியன்",4],["battle","ஆலங் கானத்து",6],["king","புல்லி",8],["hill","வேங்கடத்து",9],["town","முள்ளூர்",12],["king","காரி",12],["king","ஓரிக்",14],["king","சேரலர்க்கு",14],["hill","கொல்லி",15]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:210":{"book":"akananuru","num":211,"t":["குறியிறைக் குரம்பைக் கொலைவெம் பரதவர்","எறியுளி பொருத ஏமுறு பெருமீன்","புண்ணுமிழ் குருதி புலவுக்கடல் மறுப்பட","விசும்பணி வில்லின் போகி, பசும்பிசிர்த்","திரைபயில் அழுவம் உழக்கி, உரனழிந்து,","நிரைதிமில் மருங்கில் படர்தரும் துறைவன்,","பானாள் இரவில்நம், பணைத்தோள் உள்ளி,","தானிவண் வந்த காலை, நம்ஊர்க்","கானலம் பெருந்துறைக் கவின்பா ராட்டி,","ஆனாது புகழ்ந்திசி னோனே; இனித்தன்,","சாயல் மார்பின் பாயல் மாற்றிக்,","'கைதைஅம் படுசினைக் கடுந்தேர் விலங்கச்","செலவுஅரிது என்னும்' என்பது","பலகேட் டனமால் - தோழி !- நாமே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, knowing the lover stands by unseen, tells the woman that the lord of the shore, where a great fish struck by the fishermen's harpoon stains the sea with its blood, arcs through the waves like a rainbow and follows the boats with its strength gone, once came at midnight thinking of her shoulders and praised their seaside grove endlessly, and now, they hear, says the screw-pine thickets block his chariot and the way is hard.","n":"The friend sets his first praise of their shore against his present excuse about the same shore, and lets him overhear the comparison. The wounded fish that still follows the boats is sometimes read as a hidden image of a lover hurt yet clinging; it is left as scene because its matching to the people is not fixed by the poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 210","lb":"A210","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி, தலைமகன்சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லு வாளாய்ச்சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",1,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["எறியுளி","உளி",2,"seythi","scene","harpoon"],["பெருமீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","scene","great fish"],["திமில்","திமில்",6,"seythi","scene","fishing boats"],["கானலம்","கானல்",9,"maram","scene","seaside grove"],["கைதைஅம்","கைதை",12,"maram","scene","screw-pine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:211":{"book":"akananuru","num":212,"t":["கேளாய், எல்ல! தோழி - வாலிய","சுதைவிரிந் தன்ன பல்பூ மராஅம்","பறைகண் டன்ன பாவடி நோன்தாள்","திண்நிலை மருப்பின் வயக்களிறு உரிஞுதொறும்,","தண்மழை ஆலியின் தாஅய், உழவர்","வெண்ணெல் வித்தின் அறைமிசை உணங்கும்","பனிபடு சோலை வேங்கடத்து உம்பர்,","மொழிபெயர் தேஎத்தர் ஆயினும், நல்குவர்-","குழியிடைக் கொண்ட கன்றுடைப் பெருநிரை","பிடிபடு பூசலின் எய்தாது ஒழியக்,","கடுஞ்சின வேந்தன் ஏவலின் எய்தி,","நெடுஞ்சேண் நாட்டில் தலைத்தார்ப் பட்ட","கல்லா எழினி பல்லெறிந்து அழுத்திய","வன்கண் கதவின் வெண்மணி வாயில்,","மத்தி நாட்டிய கல்கெழுப் பனித்துறை,","நீர்ஒலித் தன்ன பேஎர்","அலர்நமக்கு ஒழிய, அழப்பிரிந் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, seeing the woman changed, tells her that though her man is in the land of strange speech beyond Vengadam, where a tusker rubbing against the maraam scatters its lime-white blossoms like hail onto the rock where farmers dry their rice seed, he will be kind and return, he who left her weeping with gossip roaring like the water at Mathi's stony ford, where Ezhini's teeth were set into the gate of Venmani.","n":"The gossip is again measured by a war story, here a grim trophy: a fallen raider's teeth driven into a fortress door. Lines 9 to 15 compress the Ezhini episode so tightly that its sequence (the cattle, the outcry, the king's command) cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 211","lb":"A211","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்டதலைமகளது வேறுபாடு கண்டு தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"வேறுபாடு கண்டு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["மராஅம்","மராஅம்",2,"maram","scene",""],["வயக்களிறு","களிறு",4,"maa","scene",""],["ஆலியின்","ஆலி",5,"pira","scene","hail"],["உழவர்","உழவர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["வெண்ணெல்","நெல்",6,"unavu","scene",""],["சோலை","சோலை",7,"maram","scene","grove"],["பெருநிரை","நிரை",9,"maa","scene","cattle"]],"en":[["hill","வேங்கடத்து",7],["king","எழினி",13],["town","வெண்மணி",14],["king","மத்தி",15]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:212":{"book":"akananuru","num":213,"t":["தாஇல் நன்பொன் தைஇய பாவை","விண்தவழ் இளவெயிற் கொண்டுநின் றன்ன,","மிகுகவின் எய்திய, தொகுகுரல் ஐம்பால்,","கிளைஅரில் நாணற் கிழங்கு மணற்கு ஈன்ற","முளைஓ ரன்ன மின்எயிற்றுத் துவர் வாய்,","நயவன் தைவரும் செவ்வழி நல்யாழ்","இசைஓர்த் தன்ன இன்தீங் கிளவி,","அணங்குசால் அரிவையை நசைஇப், பெருங்களிற்று","இனம்படி நீரின் கலங்கிய பொழுதில்,","பெறலருங் குரையள் என்னாய், வைகலும்,","இன்னா அருஞ்சுரம் நீந்தி, நீயே","என்னை இன்னற் படுத்தனை; மின்னுவசிபு","உரவுக்கார் கடுப்ப மறலி மைந்துற்று,","விரவுமொழிக் கட்டூர் வேண்டுவழிக் கொளீஇ,","படைநிலா இலங்கும் கடல்மருள் தானை","மட்டவிழ் தெரியல் மறப்போர்க் குட்டுவன்","பொருமுரண் பெறாஅது விலங்குசினஞ் சிறந்து,","செருச்செய் முன்பொடு முந்நீர் முற்றி,","ஓங்குதிரைப் பௌவம் நீங்க ஓட்டிய","நீர்மாண் எஃகம் நிறத்துச்சென்று அழுந்தக்","கூர்மதன் அழியரோ- நெஞ்சே!- ஆனாது","எளியள் அல்லோட் கருதி,","விளியா எவ்வம் தலைத் தந்தோயே"],"p":"","r":"The lover, after a meeting that failed, curses his heart: desiring a girl whose beauty is like a golden statue holding the young sunlight, whose teeth are like the shoots of reed tubers in sand and whose speech is like the music of a lute played in sevvazhi, it never thought she was hard to win and crossed the bitter waste every day, bringing him endless pain; may its keen pride be broken as the sea was when Kuttuvan's tempered spear drove it back.","n":"The curse runs through a puram image, the Chera who besieged the ocean itself; the heart's pride is set against the sea's, and both are pierced. Line 9 (the water troubled by the elephant herd) is unclear in its connection.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kuri","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 212","lb":"A212","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அல்லகுறிப்பட்டு நீங்குந் தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சினை 1நெருங்கிச்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","அல்லகுறிப்பட்டு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நாணற்","நாணல்",4,"maram","simile","reed"],["நல்யாழ்","யாழ்",6,"yaazh","simile",""],["பெருங்களிற்று","களிறு",8,"maa","scene",""],["கார்","கார்",13,"pira","simile","rain cloud"],["பௌவம்","பௌவம்",19,"pira","simile","sea"]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவன்",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:213":{"book":"akananuru","num":214,"t":["வினைநவில் யானை விறற்போர்த் தொண்டையர்","இனமழை தவழும் ஏற்றரு நெடுங்கோட்டு","ஓங்குவெள் அருவி வேங்கடத்து உம்பர்க்,","கொய்குழை அதிரல் வைகுபுலர் அலரி","சுரிஇரும் பித்தை சுரும்புபடச் சூடி,","இகல்முனைத் தரீஇய ஏருடைப் பெருநிரை","நனைமுதிர் நறவின் நாட்பலி கொடுக்கும்","வால்நிணப் புகவின் வடுகர் தேஎத்து,","நிழற்கவின் இழந்த நீர்இல் நீள்இடை","அழலவிர் அருஞ்சுரம் நெடிய என்னாது","அகறல் ஆய்ந்தனர் ஆயினும், பகல்செலப்","பல்கதிர் வாங்கிய படுசுடர் அமையத்துப்","பெருமரம் கொன்ற கால்புகு வியன்புனத்து,","எரிமருள் கதிர திருமணி இமைக்கும்","வெல்போர் வானவன் கொல்லிக் குடவரை","வேய்ஒழுக்கு அன்ன, சாய்இறைப் பணைத்தோள்","பெருங்கவின் சிதைய நீங்கி, ஆன்றோர்","அரும்பெறல் உலகம் அமிழ்தொடு பெறினும்","சென்று, தாம் நீடலோ இலரே: என்றும்","கலம்பெயக் கவிழ்ந்த கழல்தொடித் தடக்கை,","வலம்படு வென்றி வாய்வாள், சோழர்","இலங்குநீர்க் காவிரி இழிபுனல் வரித்த","அறலென நெறிந்த கூந்தல்,","உறலின் சாயலொடு ஒன்றுதல் மறந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that though her man chose to cross the shadeless waterless waste beyond the Tondaiyar's Vengadam, into the land of the Vadugar who wear wilted athiral in their curly hair and offer toddy to the cattle they raid, he will not linger there even if he were given the world of the worthy together with its nectar, forgetting to join her shoulders like the bamboo of Kolli and her hair rippled like Kaveri sand.","n":"The friend's hyperbole goes past any kingdom to heaven itself: not even the gods' world and its nectar outweighs the woman's embrace. The road is drawn from its people, a raiding folk with their own rites, which makes the distance cultural as well as physical.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 213","lb":"A213","poet":"தாயங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறீஇயது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"வற்புறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene",""],["அதிரல்","அதிரல்",4,"maram","ornament",""],["அலரி","அலரி",4,"maram","ornament","blossom"],["சுரும்புபடச்","சுரும்பு",5,"pira","scene","bee"],["பெருநிரை","நிரை",6,"maa","scene","cattle"],["நறவின்","நறவு",7,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["வால்நிணப்","நிணம்",8,"unavu","gift_food","fat"],["வியன்புனத்து","புனம்",13,"seythi","scene","cleared hill field"],["திருமணி","மணி",14,"pira","scene","gem"],["வேய்ஒழுக்கு","வேய்",16,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["அறலென","அறல்",23,"pira","simile","sand ripples"]],"en":[["hill","வேங்கடத்து",3],["king","வானவன்",15],["hill","கொல்லிக்",15],["king","சோழர்",21],["river","காவிரி",22]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:214":{"book":"akananuru","num":215,"t":["அகலிரு விசும்பகம் புதையப் பாஅய்ப்,","பகலுடன் கரந்த, பல்கதிர் வானம்","இருங்களிற்று இனநிரை குளிர்ப்ப வீசிப்,","பெரும்பெயல் அழிதுளி பொழிதல் ஆனாது,","வேந்தனும் வெம்பகை முரணி, ஏந்திலை,","விடுகதிர் நெடுவேல் இமைக்கும் பாசறை,","அடுபுகழ் மேவலொடு கண்படை இலனே;","அமரும் நம்வயி னதுவே; நமர்என","நம்மறிவு தெளிந்த பொம்மல் ஓதி","யாங்குஆ குவள்கொள் தானே -- ஓங்குவிடைப்","படுசுவற் கொண்ட பகுவாய்த் தெள்மணி","ஆபெயர் கோவலர் ஆம்பலொடு அளஇப்,","பையுள் நல்யாழ் செவ்வழி வகுப்ப,","ஆருயிர் அணங்கும் தெள்இசை","மாரி மாலையும் தமியள் கேட்டே?"],"p":"","r":"In the war camp, where the rain pours ceaselessly from a sky that has hidden the day, chilling the elephant herds, and the king, eager for glory, does not sleep, the man tells his heart that the war is their duty, and asks how she of thick hair, who knew him as her own, will fare as she hears alone in the rainy evening the sad sevvazhi lute and the flute of cowherds driving home cattle with bells.","n":"The colophon gives the speech to the woman, but the speaker is in the camp and says the war is ours (அமரும் நம்வயினதுவே), so it can only be the man; he imagines her evening, not his own. The music that wounds her is his invention, placed in her hearing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 214","lb":"A214","poet":"வடமவண்ணக்கன் பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பாசறைக்கண் தலைமகள் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பாசறைக்கண்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இருங்களிற்று","களிறு",3,"maa","scene",""],["ஓங்குவிடைப்","விடை",10,"maa","scene","bull"],["தெள்மணி","மணி",11,"pira","scene","cattle bell"],["ஆபெயர்","ஆ",12,"maa","scene","cow"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",12,"seythi","scene","cowherds"],["ஆம்பலொடு","ஆம்பல்",12,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["நல்யாழ்","யாழ்",13,"yaazh","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:215":{"book":"akananuru","num":216,"t":["'விலங்குருஞ் சிமையக் குன்றத்து உம்பர்,","வேறுபன் மொழிய தேஎம் முன்னி,","வினைநசைஇப் பரிக்கும் உரன்மிகு நெஞ்சமொடு","புனைமாண் எஃகம் வலவயின் ஏந்தி,","செலல்மாண்பு உற்ற நும்வயின், வல்லே,","வலன்ஆகு! என்றலும் நன்றுமன் தில்ல","கடுத்தது பிழைக்குவது ஆயின், தொடுத்த","கைவிரல் கவ்வும் கல்லாக் காட்சிக்,","கொடுமரம் பிடித்த கோடா வன்கண்,","வடிநவில் அம்பின் ஏவல் ஆடவர்,","ஆளழித்து உயர்த்த அஞ்சுவரு பதுக்கைக்,","கூர்நுதிச் செவ்வாய் எருவைச் சேவல்","படுபிணப் பைந்தலை தொடுவன குழீஇ,","மல்லல் மொசிவிரல் ஒற்றி, மணிகொண்டு,","வல்வாய்ப் பேடைக்குச் சொரியும் ஆங்கண்,","கழிந்தோர்க்கு இரங்கும் நெஞ்சமொடு","ஒழிந்திவண் உறைதல் ஆற்று வோர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man, who stands ready with spear in hand to cross the hills for work in a land of other tongues, that wishing him a swift and successful going would be well enough for those who could bear to stay behind grieving for the absent, on a road where bowmen who bite the finger that missed its mark raise dread stone heaps over men they kill and a red-beaked male vulture picks the eyes from the heads of corpses and drops them for his mate.","n":"The blessing is withheld by a conditional: it would be fine to say 'go well', but only to someone able to stay behind, which the woman is not. The vulture feeding its mate is the poem's sharpest stroke: on a road of killing, even the carrion bird provides for its partner without leaving her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","acham","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 215","lb":"A215","poet":"இறங்குகுடிக் குன்ற நாடன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவுணர்த்திய தோழி தலைமகள் குறிப்பறிந்து தலைமகனைச் செலவழுங்குவித்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,"செலவழுங்குவித்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["கொடுமரம்","கொடுமரம்",9,"seythi","scene","bow"],["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",10,"seythi","scene","bowmen"],["பதுக்கைக்","பதுக்கை",11,"pira","scene","stone heap"],["எருவைச்","எருவை சேவல்",12,"pul","iraicchi","male vulture"],["பேடைக்குச்","பேடை",15,"pul","iraicchi","female vulture"]],"en":[["herostone","பதுக்கைக்",11]],"dv":[["iraicchi","எருவைச்","confirmed","1/1","even the vulture of that road provides for its mate without leaving her, so the man need not part from his wife to provide"]],"ch":"a:11"},"a:216":{"book":"akananuru","num":217,"t":["நாண்கொள் நுண்கோலின் மீன்கொள் பாண்மகள்-","தாளபுனல் அடைகரைப் படுத்த வராஅல்,","நாரரி நறவுண்டு இருந்த தந்தைக்கு,","வஞ்சி விறகின் சுட்டு, வாய் உறுக்கும்","தண்துறை ஊரன் பெண்டிர் எம்மைப்","பெட்டாங்கு மொழிப' என்ப; அவ்வலர்","பட்டனம் ஆயின், இனியெவன் ஆகியர்;","கடலாடு மகளிர் கொய்த ஞாழலும்,","கழனி உழவர் குற்ற குவளையும்,","கடிமிளைப் புறவின் பூத்த முல்லையொடு,","பல்லிளங் கோசர் கண்ணி அயரும்","மல்லல் யாணர்ச் செல்லிக் கோமான்","எறிவிடத்து உலையாச் செறிசுரை வெள்வேல்","ஆதன் எழினி அருநிறத்து அழுத்திய","பெருங்களிற்று எவ்வம் போல","வருந்துப மாதுஅவர் சேரியாம் செலினே!"],"p":"","r":"The courtesan, speaking so that the wife's women will hear, says the wives of the lord of the cool-ford town, where a bard's daughter fishes with a fine rod and roasts the varaal on vanji wood for her toddy-drinking father, are said to talk of her as they please; if she is caught in that gossip, so be it, but if she goes to their street they will suffer like the great tusker when the Chelli lord's white spear struck Aathan Ezhini's chest.","n":"Her threat is to visit, not to fight; she makes her mere presence in their street a wound. The simile's referents are uncertain: whether the suffering tusker is Ezhini's own elephant or Ezhini himself called a tusker cannot be settled from the line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 216","lb":"A216","poet":"ஐயூர் முடவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைமகட்குப் பாங்காயினார் கேட்பத் தனக்குப் பாங்காயினார்க்குப் பரத்தை சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["parathai","pira","thozhi","karpu","பரத்தை சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["நுண்கோலின்","கோல்",1,"seythi","scene","fishing rod"],["பாண்மகள்","பாண்மகள்",1,"seythi","scene","bard's daughter"],["வராஅல்","வரால்",2,"pira","gift_food","varaal fish"],["நறவுண்டு","நறவு",3,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["வஞ்சி","வஞ்சி",4,"maram","scene",""],["ஞாழலும்","ஞாழல்",8,"maram","scene",""],["உழவர்","உழவர்",9,"seythi","scene",""],["குவளையும்","குவளை",9,"maram","scene",""],["முல்லையொடு","முல்லை",10,"maram","scene",""],["பெருங்களிற்று","களிறு",15,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[["town","செல்லிக்",12],["king","ஆதன் எழினி",14]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:217":{"book":"akananuru","num":218,"t":["பெய்துபுலந் திறந்த பொங்கல் வெண்மழை,","எஃகுஉறு பஞ்சித் துய்ப்பட் டன்ன,","துவலை தூவல் கழிய, அகல்வயல்","நீடுகழைக் கரும்பின் கணைக்கால் வான்பூக்","கோடைப் பூளையின் வரடையொடு துயல்வர,","பாசிலை பொதுளிய புதல்தொறும் பகன்றை","நீல்உண் பச்சை நிறமறைத்து அடைச்சிய","'தோலெறி பாண்டிலின் வாலிய மலரக்","கோழிலை அவரைக் கொழுமுகை அவிழ,","ஊழுறு தோன்றி ஒண்பூத் தளைவிட,","புலம்தொறும் குருகினம் நரலக் கல்லென","அகன்றுறை மகளிர் அணிதுறந்து நடுங்க,","அற்சிரம் வந்தன்று; அமைந்தன்று இதுவென,","எப்பொருள் பெறினும், பிரியன்மினோ, எனச்","செப்புவல் வாழியோ, துணையடை யீர்க்கே;","நல்காக் காதலர் நலன்உண்டு துறந்த","பாழ்படு மேனி நோக்கி நோய்பொர,","இணர்இறுபு உடையும் நெஞ்சமொடு, புணர்வு வேட்டு,","எயிறுதீப் பிறப்பத் திருகி","நடுங்குதும் - பிரியின்யாம் கடும்பனி உழந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Told of the coming parting, the woman says that now the cold season has come, with white rain-clouds like carded cotton, cane flowers swaying, pakandrai blooming white, avarai and thondri opening, herons crying in every field and women at the ford shivering without their ornaments, she will proclaim to every couple 'whatever wealth you gain, do not part', for her body is ruined by the lover who took her beauty and left, and she shivers in the hard cold with chattering teeth.","n":"Her answer to news of his going is a public maxim addressed to all lovers, made from her own case. The long catalogue of the cold season is not decoration: every flower and bird marks the time he should be home, and the women shivering at the ford are a picture of her own state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 217","lb":"A217","poet":"கழார்க் கீரனெயிற்றியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவுணர்த்திய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் ஆற்றாமைமீதூரச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவுணர்த்திய தோழிக்குத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வெண்மழை","மழை",1,"pira","scene","cloud"],["பஞ்சித்","பஞ்சி",2,"pira","simile","cotton"],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",4,"maram","scene","sugarcane"],["பூளையின்","பூளை",5,"maram","scene",""],["பகன்றை","பகன்றை",6,"maram","scene",""],["அவரைக்","அவரை",9,"maram","scene",""],["தோன்றி","தோன்றி",10,"maram","scene",""],["குருகினம்","குருகு",11,"pul","scene","heron"],["இணர்இறுபு","இணர்",18,"maram","simile","flower cluster"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:218":{"book":"akananuru","num":219,"t":["'கிளைபா ராட்டும் கடுநடை வயக்களிறு","முளைதருபு ஊட்டி, வேண்டுகுளகு அருத்த,","வாள்நிற உருவின் ஒளிறுபு மின்னி,","பரூஉஉறைப் பல்துளி சிதறி, வான் நாவின்று,","பெருவரை நளிர்சிமை அதிர வட்டித்துப்","புயலேறு உறைஇய வியலிருள் நடுநாள்,","விறலிழைப் பொலிந்த காண்பின் சாயல்,","தடைஇத் திரண்டநின் தோள்சேர்பு அல்லதை","படாஅ வாகும், எம் கண் என, நீயும்","இருள்மயங்கு யாமத்து இயவுக்கெட விலங்கி,","வரிவயங்கு இரும்புலி வழங்குநர்ப் பார்க்கும்","பெருமலை விடரகம் வர அரிது; என்னாய்,","வரவெளி தாக எண்ணுதி, அதனால்,","நுண்ணிதின் கூட்டிய படுமாண் ஆரம்","தண்ணிது கமழும்நின் மார்பு, ஒருநாள்","அடைய முயங்கேம் ஆயின், யாமும்","விறலிழை நெகிழச் சாஅய்தும்; அதுவே","அன்னை அறியினும் அறிக! அலர்வாய்","அம்பல் மூதூர் கேட்பினும் கேட்க!","வண்டிறை கொண்ட எரிமருள் தோன்றியொடு","ஒண்பூ வேங்கை கமழும்","தண்பெருஞ் சாரல் பகல்வந் தீமே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lover, who said his eyes would not close except on her shoulders on nights when the storm cloud flashes and thunders over the peaks, after the tusker has fed sprouts and fodder to his herd, and who thinks it easy to come at midnight through the mountain clefts where the tiger waits for travellers, that if they cannot embrace his chest even one day they too will waste; let mother know and the old town gossip: let him come by day to the cool slope where vengai and thondri smell sweet.","n":"Moving the meeting to daytime is a dare: the friend accepts the risk of discovery (let mother know, let the town hear) rather than his risk on the night path, which forces the matter toward marriage. She quotes his own lover's oath back to him before setting her terms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","varaivu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 218","lb":"A218","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகளை இடத்துய்த்து வந்து பகற்குறிநேர்ந்த வாய் பாட்டால் தலைமகனை வரைவுகடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவுகடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["வயக்களிறு","களிறு",1,"maa","iraicchi","tusker"],["குளகு","குளகு",2,"unavu","gift_food","fodder"],["புயலேறு","ஏறு",6,"pira","scene","thunder"],["இரும்புலி","புலி",11,"maa","scene",""],["வண்டிறை","வண்டு",20,"pira","scene","bee"],["தோன்றியொடு","தோன்றி",20,"maram","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",21,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வயக்களிறு","confirmed","1/1","as the tusker openly provides for his own, the lover should take up the care of the girl in marriage rather than steal visits by night"]],"ch":"a:11"},"a:219":{"book":"akananuru","num":220,"t":["சீர்கெழு வியனகர்ச் சிலம்புநக இயலி.","ஓரை ஆயமொடு பந்துசிறிது எறியினும்,","'வாராயோ!' என்று ஏத்திப் பேர்இலைப்","பகன்றை வான்மலர் பனிநிறைந் ததுபோல்","பால்பெய் வள்ளம் சால்கை பற்றி,","'என்பாடு உண்டனை ஆயின் ஒருகால்","நுந்தை பாடும் உண்' என்று ஊட்டிப்,","'பிறந்ததற் கொண்டும் சிறந்தவை செய்துயான்,","நலம்புனைந்து எடுத்தஎன் பொலந்தொடிக் குறுமகள்","அறனி லாளனொடு இறந்தனள் இனி' என,","மறந்து அமைந்து இராஅ நெஞ்சம் நோவேன்-","பொன்வார்ந் தன்ன வைவால் எயிற்றுச்","செந்நாய் வெரீஇய புகர்உழை ஒருத்தல்;","பொரிஅரை விளவின் புன்புற விளைபுழல்,","அழல்எறி கோடை தூக்கலின், கோவலர்,","குழல்என நினையும் நீர்இல் நீள்இடை,","மடத்தகை மெலியச் சாஅய்,","நடக்கும் கொல்?\" என, நோவல் யானே"],"p":"","r":"The mother whose daughter has gone grieves that the girl who ran ringing her anklets through their great house and played ball with her friends, whom she fed from a bowl of milk like a white pakandrai flower brimming with dew ('drink your father's share too'), and adorned from birth, has gone with a heartless man; she cannot forget and wonders whether the girl walks, wasting, the long waterless road where a stag flees the red dog and the hot wind in the dry vila pods sounds to cowherds like a flute.","n":"The feeding scene is told in the mother's own coaxing words, which makes the loss concrete: the voice that coaxed is still speaking, with no child to hear it. The flute that the cowherds imagine in the wind is the road's one sound of home, and it is false.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","anbu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 219","lb":"A219","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய தாய் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["nartaay","nenju",null,"kalavu","மகட் போக்கிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பகன்றை","பகன்றை",4,"maram","simile",""],["பால்பெய்","பால்",5,"unavu","gift_food",""],["செந்நாய்","செந்நாய்",13,"maa","scene","red dog"],["புகர்உழை","உழை",13,"maa","scene","spotted deer"],["விளவின்","விளா",14,"maram","scene",""],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",15,"seythi","scene","cowherds"],["குழல்என","குழல்",16,"yaazh","simile","flute"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:220":{"book":"akananuru","num":221,"t":["ஊருஞ் சேரியும் உடன்இயைந்து அலர்எழத்,","தேரொடு மறுகியும், பணிமொழி பயிற்றியும்,","கெடாஅத் தீயின் உருகெழு செல்லூர்க்,","கடாஅ யானைக் குழூஉச் சமம் ததைய","மன்மருங்கு அறுத்த மழுவாள் நெடியோன்","முன்முயன்று அரிதினின் முடித்த வேள்வி,","கயிறுஅரை யாத்த காண்தகு வனப்பின்","அருங்கடி நெடுந்தூண் போல, யாவரும்","காண லாகா மாண் எழில் ஆகம்","உள்ளுதொறும் பனிக்கும் நெடுஞ்சினை, நீயே","நெடும்புற நிலையினை, வருந்தினை ஆயின்,","முழங்குகடல் ஓதம் காலைக் கொட்கும்,","பழம்பல் நெல்லின் ஊணூர் ஆங்கண்","நோலா இரும்புள் போல, நெஞ்சு அமர்ந்து,","காதல் மாறாக் காமர் புணர்ச்சியின்,","இருங்கழி முகந்த செங்கோல் அவ்வலை","முடங்குபுற இறவொடு இனமீன் செறிக்கும்","நெடுங்கதிர்க் கழனித் தண்சாய்க் கானத்து","யாணர்த் தண்பணை உறும் எனக் கானல்","ஆயம் ஆய்ந்த சாய்இறைப் பணைத்தோள்","நல்எழில் சிதையா ஏமம்","சொல்லினித் தெய்ய, யாம் தெளியு மாறே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, meeting the lover as he leaves the night tryst, tells him that while gossip rises in town and street he goes about in his chariot and speaks humbly, shivering whenever he thinks of her body, unapproachable as the guarded pillar of the sacrifice the axe-bearing tall one completed at Chellur; he stands outside and suffers, and should instead, like the great bird at Oonur by the sea, settle in a steady union and speak the words that protect her beauty, so that they may be at ease.","n":"The sacrificial pillar roped at its waist, which none may approach, makes the girl's guarded state a sacred enclosure that only a public rite can open: the friend is asking for marriage. Several phrases (நோலா இரும்புள், the whole of lines 10 to 21) are obscure and the reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","alar","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 220","lb":"A220","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிவந்து நீங்குச் தலைமகனை யெதிர்ப்பட்டுத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறிவந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானைக்","யானை",4,"maa","scene",""],["ஓதம்","ஓதம்",12,"pira","scene","tide"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",13,"unavu","scene",""],["இரும்புள்","புள்",14,"pul","simile","great bird"],["அவ்வலை","வலை",16,"seythi","scene","net"],["இறவொடு","இறா",17,"pira","scene","prawn"],["இனமீன்","மீன்",17,"pira","scene",""],["கழனித்","கழனி",18,"pira","scene","field"],["தண்சாய்க்","சாய்",18,"maram","scene","sedge"]],"en":[["town","செல்லூர்க்",3],["town","ஊணூர்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:11"},"a:221":{"book":"akananuru","num":222,"t":["நனைவிளை நறவின் தேறல் மாந்திப்,","புனைவினை நல்லில் தருமணல் குவைஇப்,","'பொம்மல் ஓதி எம்மகள் மணன்' என,","வதுவை அயர்ந்தனர் நமரே அதனால்","புதுவது புனைந்த சேயிலை வெள்வேல்","மதிஉடம் பட்ட மைஅணற் காளை","வாங்குசினை மலிந்த திரளரை மராஅத்துத்","தேம்பாய் மெல்லிணர் தளிரொடு கொண்டு, நின்","தண்நறு முச்சி புனைய, அவனொடு","கழைகவின் போகிய மழைஉயர் நனந்தலை,","களிற்றிரை பிழைத்தலின் கயவாய் வேங்கை","காய்சினம் சிறந்து, குழுமலின் வெரீஇ,","இரும்பிடி இரியும் சோலை","அருஞ்சுரம் சேறல் அயர்ந்தனென், யானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that, since their people have begun the wedding rites with toddy and heaped sand, she has arranged for her to leave with the dark-bearded young man of the new spear, who will crown her hair with kadamba blossoms, along the hard forest track where a tiger that missed its elephant roars and the she-elephant bolts.","n":"The poem never says whom the kin mean to marry her to; that the wedding is not to her lover is the colophon's reading (போக்குடன்பட), and it is what makes the friend's arrangement urgent. The kadamba spray the young man will pick for her hair is a small wedding of their own set against the kin's wedding at home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 221","lb":"A221","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகற்குப் போக்குடன்பட்ட தோழி, தலைமகட்குப் போக்குடன்படச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தலைமகட்குப் போக்குடன்படச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["நறவின்","நறவு",1,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["மணல்","மணல்",2,"pira","scene",""],["மராஅத்துத்","மரா",7,"maram","ornament","kadamba"],["கழை","கழை",10,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["களிற்றிரை","களிறு",11,"maa","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",11,"maa","scene","tiger"],["இரும்பிடி","பிடி",13,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:222":{"book":"akananuru","num":223,"t":["வானுற நிவந்த நீல்நிறப் பெருமலைக்","கான நாடன் இறீஇய நோய்க்கு, என்","மேனி ஆய்நலம் தொலைதலின், மொழிவென்;","முழவுமுகம் புலராக் கலிகொள் ஆங்கண்,","கழாஅர்ப் பெருந்துறை விழவின் ஆடும்","ஈட்டெழில் பொலிந்த ஏந்துகுவவு மொய்ம்பின்","ஆட்டன் அத்தி நலன்நயந்து உரைஇத்,","தாழிருங் கதுப்பின் காவிரி வவ்வலின்","மரதிரம் துழைஇ, மதிமருண்டு அலந்த","ஆதி மந்தி காதலற் காட்டிப்","படுகடல் புக்க பாடல்சால் சிறப்பின்","மருதி அன்ன மாண்புகழ் பெறீஇயர்","சென்மோ - வாழி, தோழி - பல்நாள்","உரவுரும் ஏறொடு மயங்கி,","இரவுப்பெயல் பொழிந்த ஈர்ந்தண் ஆறே"],"p":"","r":"With the hill lord listening, the friend says that since the sickness he caused is wasting her beauty she will speak out: let us go, along the river cold with many nights of thunder and rain, to win a fame like Maruthi's, who showed Aathi Manthi her lover taken by the Kaveri at the Kazhar festival and then went into the sea.","n":"The friend borrows a known story of love and drowning to hint at a desperate act; aimed at the overhearing hero, the hint presses him to marry rather than keep coming by the flooded night path. The Maruthi part of the legend is told only here in this chapter; poem 236 uses the same story from Manthi's side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 222","lb":"A222","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முழவுமுகம்","முழவு",4,"parai","scene",""],["உரவுரும்","உரும்",14,"pira","scene","thunder"],["ஏறொடு","ஏறு",14,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[["town","கழாஅர்",5],["river","காவிரி",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:223":{"book":"akananuru","num":224,"t":["'பிரிதல் வல்லியர்; இது, நத் துறந்தோர்","மறந்தும் அமைகுவர் கொல்?' என்று எண்ணி,","ஆழல் - வாழி, தோழி!- கேழல்","வளைமருப்பு உறழும் உளைநெடும் பெருங்காய்","நனைமுதிர் முருக்கின் சினைசேர் பொங்கல்,","காய்சினக் கடுவளி எடுத்தலின் வெங்காட்டு","அழல்பொழி யானையின் ஐயெனத் தோன்றும்","நிழலில் ஓமை நீரில் நீளிடை,","இறந்தனர் ஆயினும், காதலர் நம்வயின்","மறந்து கண்படுதல் யாவது - புறம் தாழ்","அம்பணை நெடுந்தோள் தங்கித், தும்பி","அரியினம் கடுக்கும் சுரிவணர் ஐம்பால்","நுண்கேழ் அடங்க வாரிப் பையுள் கெட,","நன்முகை அதிரல் போதொடு குவளைத்","தண்நறுங் கமழ்தொடை வேய்ந்த, நின்","மண்ஆர் கூந்தல் மரீஇய துயிலே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the grieving girl not to fear that the man who could leave her will forget her: though he has crossed the long shadeless waterless track where wind-tossed flame-of-the-forest pods curved like a boar's tusk blaze like a fire-spouting elephant, how could he sleep forgetting the sleep he found in her garlanded hair?","n":"The comfort rests on his memory of her hair, which he once gathered over her shoulders and decked with athiral and kuvalai; the friend argues from the body's habit, not from his promise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 223","lb":"A223","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கேழல்","கேழல்",3,"maa","simile","boar"],["முருக்கின்","முருக்கு",5,"maram","simile",""],["யானையின்","யானை",7,"maa","simile",""],["ஓமை","ஓமை",8,"maram","scene",""],["தும்பி","தும்பி",11,"pira","simile",""],["அதிரல்","அதிரல்",14,"maram","ornament",""],["குவளைத்","குவளை",14,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:224":{"book":"akananuru","num":225,"t":["செல்க, பாக! எல்லின்று பொழுதே-","வல்லோன் அடங்குகயிறு அமைப்பக், கொல்லன்","விசைத்துவாங்கு துருத்தியின் வெய்ய உயிராக்,","கொடுநுகத்து யாத்த தலைய, கடுநடைக்,","கால்கடுப்பு அன்ன கடுஞ்செலல் இவுளி,","பால்கடை நுரையின் பரூஉ மிதப்பு அன்ன,","வால்வெண் தெவிட்டல் வழிவார் நுணக்கம்","சிலம்பி நூலின் நுணங்குவன பாறிச்,","சாந்துபுலர் அகலம் மறுப்பக் காண்தகப்","புதுநலம் பெற்ற வெய்துநீங்கு புறவில்,","தெறிநடை மரைக்கணம் இரிய மனையோள்,","ஐதுணங்கு வல்சி பெய்துமுறுக்கு உறுத்த","திரிமரக் குரலிசைப் கடுப்ப, வரிமணல்","அலங்குகதிர் திகிரி ஆழி போழ,","வரும்கொல்- தோழி!- நம் இன்உயிர்த் துணைஎனச்,","சில்கோல் எல்வளை ஒடுக்கிப் பல்கால்","அருங்கடி வியனகர் நோக்கி,","வருந்துமால் அளியள் திருந்திழை தானே"],"p":"","r":"The hero, his task done, tells his charioteer to drive on as the day ends, horses breathing hot as a smith's bellows and flecked with milk-white froth, the wheel cutting the sand with the sound of a housewife's quern, while he pictures his wife pushing back her bangles and looking again and again toward the gate, asking whether he is coming.","n":"The two domestic similes, the smith's bellows and the quern turned by a housewife, bring the home into the journey before he reaches it; the wife he imagines speaks only one line, and it is a question.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 224","lb":"A224","poet":"ஆவூர் மூலங்கிழார் மகனார் பெருந்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கொல்லன்","கொல்லன்",2,"seythi","simile",""],["இவுளி","இவுளி",5,"maa","simile","horse"],["பால்கடை","பால்",6,"unavu","simile",""],["சிலம்பி","சிலம்பி",8,"pira","simile","spider"],["மரைக்கணம்","மரை",11,"maa","scene","deer"],["வல்சி","வல்சி",12,"unavu","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:225":{"book":"akananuru","num":226,"t":["அன்பும், மடனும், சாயலும், இயல்பும்,","என்பு நெகிழ்க்கும் கிளவியும் பிறவும்,","ஒன்றுபடு கொள்கையொடு ஓராங்கு முயங்கி,","இன்றே இவணம் ஆகி, நாளைப்,","புதலிவர் ஆடுஅமைத் தும்பி குயின்ற","அகலா அந்துளை கோடை முகத்தலின்","நீர்க்கியங்கு இனநிரைப் பின்றை, வார்கோல்","ஆய்க்குழல் பாணியின் ஐதுவந்து இசைக்கும்,","தேக்கமல் சோலைக் கடறேங்கு அருஞ்சுரத்து,","யாத்த தூணித் தலைதிறந் தவைபோல்,","பூத்த இருப்பைக் குழைபொதி குவிஇணர்","கழல்துளை முத்தின் செந்நிலத்து உதிர,","மழைதுளி மறந்த அங்குடிச் சீறூர்ச்","சேக்குவம் கொல்லோ- நெஞ்சே!- பூப்புனை","புயலென ஒலிவரும் தாழிருங் கூந்தல்,","செறிதொடி முன்கை நம் காதலி","அறிவஞர் நோக்கமும் புலவியும் நினைந்தே?"],"p":"","r":"The hero asks his heart whether, after being one with her today in love, innocence and gentle speech, they will lodge tomorrow in a rainless hamlet on the hard track, where wind through bee-bored bamboo sounds like a herdsman's flute and iruppai flowers drop like loosened pearls, thinking of her anguished look and her sulking.","n":"Today and tomorrow (இன்றே, நாளை) frame the poem: the whole journey is imagined from inside the embrace. The music of the bamboo is the one soft thing on the track, and it belongs to the herders' land, not the desert.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","nenju","selvam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 225","lb":"A225","poet":"எயினந்தை மகனார் இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் கடைக்கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"பொருள் கடைக்கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அமைத்","அமை",5,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",5,"pira","scene",""],["இனநிரைப்","நிரை",7,"maa","scene","herd"],["ஆய்க்குழல்","குழல்",8,"yaazh","simile",""],["தேக்கமல்","தேக்கு",9,"maram","scene",""],["இருப்பைக்","இருப்பை",11,"maram","simile",""],["முத்தின்","முத்து",12,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:226":{"book":"akananuru","num":227,"t":["உணர்குவென் அல்லென்; உரையல்நின் மாயம்;","நாணிலை மன்ற- யாணர் ஊர!-","அகலுள் ஆங்கண் அம்பகை மடிவைக்,","குறுந்தொடி, மகளிர் குரூஉப்புனல் முனையின்,","பழனப் பைஞ்சாய் கொழுதிக் கழனிக்","கரந்தைஅம் செறுவின் வெண்குருகு ஓப்பும்,","வல்வில் எறுழ்த்தோள் பரதவர் கோமான்,","பல்வேல் மத்தி கழாஅர் முன்துறை,","நெடுவெண் மருதொடு வஞ்சி சாஅய்,","விடியல் வந்த பெருநீர்க் காவிரி,","தொடிஅணி முன்கை நீ வெய் யோளொடு","முன்நாள் ஆடிய கவ்வை இந்நாள்,","வலிமிகும் முன்பின் பாணனொடு மலிதார்த்","தித்தன் வெளியன் உறந்தை நாளவைப்","பாடுஇன் தெண்கிணைப் பாடுகேட்டு அஞ்சி,","போரடு தானைக் கட்டி","பொராஅது ஓடிய ஆர்ப்பினும் பெரிதே"],"p":"","r":"The friend refuses the lord of the rich town his tricks: his bathing yesterday in the Kaveri's dawn flood at Matthi's Kazhar ford with the woman he desires has raised more uproar than the shout when Katti fled without a fight on hearing the kinai drum at Thithan Veliyan's court in Uranthai.","n":"The scale is the joke: a private bathe is measured against a famous rout, and the hero is mocked by being set beside a king who ran. The women scaring herons with sedge in the karanthai fields are the town's ordinary life, which has now seen him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 226","lb":"A226","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைமகற்குத் தோழி வாயின் மறுத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","வாயின் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பைஞ்சாய்","சாய்",5,"maram","scene","sedge"],["கரந்தைஅம்","கரந்தை",6,"maram","scene",""],["வெண்குருகு","குருகு",6,"pul","scene","heron"],["மருதொடு","மருது",9,"maram","scene",""],["வஞ்சி","வஞ்சி",9,"maram","scene",""],["பாணனொடு","பாணன்",13,"seythi","simile",""],["தெண்கிணைப்","கிணை",15,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[["king","மத்தி",8],["town","கழாஅர்",8],["river","காவிரி",10],["king","தித்தன் வெளியன்",14],["town","உறந்தை",14],["king","கட்டி",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:227":{"book":"akananuru","num":228,"t":["'நுதல்பசந் தன்றே; தோள்சா யினவே;","திதலை அல்குல் வரியும் வாடின;","என்ஆ குவள்கொல் இவள்?' எனப் பல்மாண்","நீர்மலி கண்ணொடு நெடிது நினைந்து ஒற்றி,","இனையல்- வாழி, தோழி!- நனை கவுள்","காய்சினம் சிறந்த வாய்புகு கடாத் தொடு","முன்னிலை பொறாஅது முரணிப், பொன்னிணர்ப்","புலிக்கேழ் வேங்கைப் பூஞ்சினை புலம்ப,","முதல்பாய்ந் திட்ட முழுவலி ஒருத்தல்","செந்நிலப் படுநீறு ஆடிச், செருமலைந்து,","களம்கொள் மள்ளரின் முழங்கும் அத்தம்","பலஇறந்து அகன்றனர் ஆயினும், நிலைஇ,","நோய்இல ராக, நம் காதலர்!- வாய்வாள்,","தமிழ் அகப் படுத்த இமிழிசை முரசின்","வருநர் வரையாப் பெருநாள் இருக்கை,","தூங்கல் பாடிய ஓங்குபெரு நல்லிசைப்","பிடிமிதி வழுதுணைப் பெரும்பெயர்த் தழும்பன்","கடிமதில் வரைப்பின் ஊணூர் உம்பர்,","விழுநிதி துஞ்சும் வீறுபெறு திருநகர்.","இருங்கழிப் படப்பை மருங்கூர்ப் பட்டினத்து,","எல்லுமிழ் ஆவணத்து அன்ன,","கல்லென் கம்பலை செய்து அகன்றோரே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl to stop brooding over her pale forehead and slack shoulders: though he has crossed many tracks where a lone tusker in musth tears at a vengai and roars like warriors taking a field, may our lover stay well, he who left with a clamour like the bright market of Marungur beyond Thazhumban's Unur.","n":"The friend's blessing, நோய்இலராக, turns comfort into a wish for the absent man. Thazhumban is praised for a drum that brought Tamil under it (தமிழ் அகப்படுத்த), an early use of தமிழ் for a land or people; line 17's பிடிமிதி வழுதுணை is obscure to us.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 227","lb":"A227","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது. பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",8,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["ஒருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",9,"maa","simile","tusker"],["மள்ளரின்","மள்ளர்",11,"seythi","simile",""],["முரசின்","முரசு",14,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["king","தழும்பன்",17],["town","ஊணூர்",18],["town","மருங்கூர்",20]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:228":{"book":"akananuru","num":229,"t":["பிரசப் பல்கிளை ஆர்ப்பக், கல்லென","வரைஇழி அருவிஆரம் தீண்டித்","தண்என நனைக்கும் நளிர்மலைச் சிலம்பில்,","கண்என மலர்ந்த மாஇதழ்க் குவளைக்","கல்முகை நெடுஞ்சுனை நம்மொடு ஆடிப்,","பகலே இனிதுடன் கழிப்பி, இரவே","செல்வர் ஆயினும், நன்றுமன் தில்ல-","வான்கண் விரிந்த பகல்மருள் நிலவின்","சூரல் மிளைஇய சாரல் ஆர்ஆற்று,","ஓங்கல் மிசைய வேங்கை ஒள்வீப்","புலிப்பொறி கடுப்பத் தோன்றலின், கயவாய்","இரும்பிடி இரியும் சோலைப்","பெருங்கல் யாணர்த்தம் சிறுகுடி யானே"],"p":"","r":"With the hero listening, the friend says it would be well enough if he spent the days with them bathing in the kuvalai pool under the cold slope, and went home at night; but on the moonlit path to his hamlet the vengai blossoms look like a tiger's spots and the she-elephant bolts.","n":"The danger is an illusion of moonlight, yet the friend uses it to turn him from night meetings to day ones, and in the end toward marriage. The she-elephant fleeing a flowering tree it takes for a tiger is a traditional candidate for a hidden sense (the girl's fears for him); it does not pass the act condition here, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 228","lb":"A228","poet":"அண்டர்மகன் குறுவழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["பிரசப்","பிரசம்",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["அருவி","அருவி",2,"pira","scene",""],["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",2,"maram","scene","sandal"],["குவளைக்","குவளை",4,"maram","simile",""],["சூரல்","சூரல்",9,"maram","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",10,"maram","simile","kino tree"],["புலிப்பொறி","புலி",11,"maa","simile",""],["இரும்பிடி","பிடி",12,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:229":{"book":"akananuru","num":230,"t":["பகல்செய் பல்கதிர்ப் பருதியம் செல்வன்","அகல்வாய் வானத்து ஆழ் போழ்ந்தென,","நீர்அற வறந்த நிரம்பா நீளிடைக்","கயந்தலைக் குழவிக் கவிஉகிர் மடப்பிடி","குளகுமறுத்து உயங்கிய மருங்குல் பலவுடன்","பாழூர்க் குரம்பையின் தோன்றும் ஆங்கண்,","நெடுஞ்சேண் இடைய குன்றம் போகி,","பொய்வ லாளர் முயன்றுசெய் பெரும்பொருள்","நம்இன்று ஆயினும் முடிக, வல்லெனப்,","பெருந்துனி மேவல்!- நல்கூர் குறுமகள்!","நோய்மலிந்து உகுத்த நொசிவரல் சில்நீர்","பல்லிதழ் மழைக்கண் பாவை மாய்ப்பப்,","பொன்னேர் பசலை ஊர்தரப், பொறிவரி","நல்மா மேனி தொலைதல் நோக்கி,","இனையல் என்றி; தோழி! சினைய","பாசரும்பு ஈன்ற செம்முகை முருக்கின","போதவிழ் அலரி கொழுதித், தாது அருந்து","அம்தளிர் மா அத்து அலங்கல் மீமிசைச்,","செங்கண் இருங்குயில் நயவரக் கூஉம்","இன்இள வேனிலும் வாரார்.","'இன்னே வருதும்' எனத்தெளித் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The girl answers her friend's comfort: you tell me not to grieve as tears blur my eyes and gold-like pallor spreads, yet he who crossed the far hills of a waterless track where starving she-elephants stand like huts of a deserted village, and promised to come at once, has not come even in the sweet young spring when the cuckoo calls from the mango.","n":"The spring is the proof: the cuckoo's call marks the season by which he promised to be back, so the beauty of the season is itself the evidence against him. The poem gives his own words, இன்னே வருதும், only to set them against the calendar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 229","lb":"A229","poet":"மதுரைக் கூலவாணிகன் சீத்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் வன்புறை யெதிரழிந்து சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் வன்புறை யெதிரழிந்து சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குழவிக்","குழவி",4,"maa","scene",""],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",4,"maa","simile",""],["குளகுமறுத்து","குளகு",5,"unavu","absent",""],["முருக்கின","முருக்கு",16,"maram","scene",""],["மா அத்து","மா",18,"maram","scene","mango"],["இருங்குயில்","குயில்",19,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:230":{"book":"akananuru","num":231,"t":["'உறுகழி மருங்கின் ஓதமொடு மலர்ந்த","சிறுகரு நெய்தற் கண்போல் மாமலர்ப்","பெருந்தண் மாத்தழை இருந்த அல்குல்,","ஐய அரும்பிய சுணங்கின் வைஎயிற்று,","மைஈர்ஓதி, வாள் நுதல் குறுமகள்!","விளையாட்டு ஆயமொடு வெண்மணல் உதிர்த்த","புன்னை நுண்தாது பொன்னின் நொண்டு,","மனைபுறந் தருதி ஆயின், எனையதூஉம்,","இம்மனைக் கிழமை எம்மொடு புணரின்","தீதும் உண்டோ , மாத ராய்?' எனக்","கடும்பரி நல்மான், கொடிஞ்சி நெடுந்தேர்","கைவல் பாகன் பையென இயக்க,","யாம்தற் குறுகினமாக ஏந்தெழில்","அரிவேய் உண்கண் பனிவரல் ஒடுக்கிச்","சிறிய இறைஞ்சினள், தலையே-","பெரிய எவ்வம் யாமிவண் உறவே!"],"p":"","r":"The hero tells his heart how he asked the shore girl in her leaf skirt, gathering punnai pollen like gold with her playmates, whether any harm would come if the right of this house were joined to him, and how, as his chariot drew near, she held back her tears and bowed her head a little, leaving him in great pain.","n":"Her answer is a gesture, not a word: the bowed head and checked tears can be read as consent or as refusal, and the hero's pain comes from not knowing which. His proposal is couched in the language of household right (மனைக் கிழமை), unusual in a first meeting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalaviyal","kurippu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 230","lb":"A230","poet":"மதுரை அறுவைவாணிகன் இளவேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகளைக் கண்ணுற்று நீங்கும் தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","தலைமகளைக் கண்ணுற்று நீங்கும் தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஓதமொடு","ஓதம்",1,"pira","scene","tide"],["நெய்தற்","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","simile",""],["மாத்தழை","தழை",3,"maram","ornament",""],["மணல்","மணல்",6,"pira","scene",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",7,"maram","simile",""],["நல்மான்","மா",11,"maa","scene","horse"],["பாகன்","பாகன்",12,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:231":{"book":"akananuru","num":232,"t":["'செறுவோர் செம்மல் வாட்டலும், சேர்ந்தோர்க்கு","உறுமிடத்து உவக்கும் உதவி ஆண்மையும்,","இல்லிருந்து அமைவோர்க்கு இல்' என்று எண்ணி,","நல்லிசை வலித்த நாணுடை மனத்தர்","கொடுவிற் கானவர் கணைஇடத் தொலைந்தோர்,","படுகளத்து உயர்த்த மயிர்த்தலைப் பதுக்கைக்,","கள்ளியம் பறந்தலைக் களர்தொறும் குழீஇ,","உள்ளுநர்ப் பனிக்கும் ஊக்கருங் கடத்திடை","வெஞ்சுரம் இறந்தனர் ஆயினும் நெஞ்சுருக","வருவர் - வாழி; தோழி!- பொருவர்","செல்சமம் கடந்த செல்லா நல்லிசை,","விசும்பிவர் வெண்குடைப், பசும்பூட் பாண்டியன்","பாடுபெறு சிறப்பின் கூடல் அன்னநின்","ஆடுவண்டு அரற்றும் முச்சித்","தோடுஆர் கூந்தல் மரீஇ யோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that men who prize fame, holding that crushing foes and helping kin in need are not for those who stay home, have crossed the dreaded track of hunters' victims under stone heaps, yet they will come back with melting hearts, those who lay in her bee-loud hair, which is like the Pandyan's famed Koodal.","n":"The friend argues with a puram value: glory is earned away from home, so the parting is honourable, and the same honour guarantees the return. The stone heaps over men killed by forest bowmen are the road's reminder of what that glory costs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pugazh","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 231","lb":"A231","poet":"மதுரை ஈழத்துப் பூதன் தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["கானவர்","கானவர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["கள்ளியம்","கள்ளி",7,"maram","scene",""],["ஆடுவண்டு","வண்டு",14,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["herostone","பதுக்கைக்",6],["king","பாண்டியன்",12],["town","கூடல்",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:232":{"book":"akananuru","num":233,"t":["காண்இனி- வாழி, தோழி!- பானாள்,","மழைமுழங்கு அரவம் கேட்ட, கழைதின்,","மாஅல் யானை புலிசெத்து வெரீஇ,","இருங்கல் விடரகம் சிலம்பப் பெயரும்","பெருங்கல் நாடன் கேண்மை, இனியே,","குன்ற வேலிச் சிறுகுடி ஆங்கண்,","மன்ற வேங்கை மணநாட் பூத்த","மணிஏர் அரும்பின் பொன்வீ தாஅய்","வியலறை வரிக்கும் முன்றில், குறவர்","மனைமுதிர் மகளிரொடு குரவை தூங்கும்","ஆர்கலி விழவுக் களம்கடுப்ப, நாளும்,","விரவுப்பூம் பலியொடு விரைஇ! அன்னை","கடியுடை வியல்நகர்க் காவல் கண்ணி","'முருகு' என வேலன் தரூஉம்","பருவ மாகப் பயந்தன்றால், நமக்கே!"],"p":"","r":"With the hero listening, the friend tells the girl that her bond with the lord of the rock country, where an elephant takes midnight thunder for a tiger and flees, has now brought on the season when mother, guarding the house, has the velan come, saying it is Murugu, with mixed flower offerings, in a courtyard strewn with vengai gold like a festival ground where the kuravar women dance.","n":"The velan's rite shows the secret is close to discovery: the mother reads the girl's lovesickness as Murugu's seizure. The elephant mistaking thunder for a tiger matches the mother mistaking love for possession; we leave it as scene because only one correspondence holds firmly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velan","kalaviyal","varaivu","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 232","lb":"A232","poet":"கொடிமங்கலத்து வாதுளி நற்சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி, தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["மழைமுழங்கு","மழை",2,"pira","scene",""],["கழை","கழை",2,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""],["புலிசெத்து","புலி",3,"maa","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",7,"maram","simile","kino tree"],["குறவர்","குறவர்",9,"seythi","scene",""],["குரவை","குரவை",10,"yaazh","scene",""],["பலியொடு","பலி",12,"pira","scene",""],["முருகு","முருகு",14,"deyvam","scene",""],["வேலன்","வேலன்",14,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:233":{"book":"akananuru","num":234,"t":["அலமரல் மழைக்கண் மல்குபனி வார, நின்","அலர்முலை நனைய, அழாஅல்- தோழி!-","எரிகவர்பு உண்ட கரிபுறப் பெருநிலப்","பீடுகெழு மருங்கின் ஓடுமழை துறந்தென,","ஊனில் யானை உயங்கும் வேனில்,","மறப்படைக் குதிரை, மாறா மைந்தின்","துறக்கம் எய்திய தொய்யா நல்லிசை","முதியர்ப் பேணிய, உதியஞ் சேரல்","பெருஞ்சோறு கொடுத்த ஞான்றை, இரும்பல்","கூளிச் சுற்றம் குழீஇயிருந் தாங்கு,","குறியவும் நெடியவும் குன்றுதலை மணந்த","சுரன்இறந்து அகன்றனர் ஆயினும், மிகநனி","மடங்கா உள்ளமொடு மதிமயக் குறாஅ,","பொருள்வயின் நீடலோ இலர் - நின்","இருள்ஐங் கூந்தல் இன்துயில் மறந்தே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the weeping girl that though he has crossed a summer track of fire-blackened land where lean elephants languish and short and tall hills crowd together like the host of ghouls gathered at Uthiyan Cheral's great feast, he will not linger over wealth, forgetting the sweet sleep of her dark hair.","n":"The Cheral's great feast, a puram fame, is turned into a picture of the landscape: the hills crowd like the ghoulish guests. The friend's assurance turns on the same memory of sleep in her hair that poem 223 uses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 233","lb":"A233","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","scene",""],["குதிரை","குதிரை",6,"maa","simile",""],["பெருஞ்சோறு","சோறு",9,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கூளிச்","கூளி",10,"pira","simile","ghoul"]],"en":[["king","உதியஞ் சேரல்",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:234":{"book":"akananuru","num":235,"t":["கார்பயம் பொழிந்த நீர்திகழ் காலை,","நுண்ணயிர் பரந்த தண்ணய மருங்கின்,","நிரைபறை அன்னத்து அன்ன, விரைபரிப்","புல்உளைக் கலிமா மெல்லிதின் கொளீஇய,","வள்புஒருங்கு அமையப் பற்றி, முள்கிய","பல்கதிர் ஆழி மெல்வழி அறுப்பக்,","கால்என மருள, ஏறி, நூல்இயல்","கண்நோக்கு ஒழிக்கும் பண்ணமை நெடுந்தேர்","வல்விரைந்து ஊர்மதி!- நல்வலம் பெறுந!","ததர்தழை முனைஇய தெறிநடை மடப்பிணை","ஏறுபுணர் உவகைய ஊறுஇல உகள,","அம்சிறை வண்டின் மென்பறைத் தொழுதி","முல்லை நறுமலர்த் தாதுநயந்து ஊத,","எல்லை போகிய புல்லென் மாலைப்","புறவுஅடைந் திருந்த உறைவுஇன் நல்ஊர்,","கழிபடர் உழந்த பனிவார் உண்கண்","நல்நிறம் பரந்த பசலையள்","மின்நேர் ஓதிப் பின்னுப்பிணி விடவே"],"p":"","r":"The hero tells his charioteer to drive the swan-swift horses fast over the rain-washed sand, so that by dusk, when the doe frolics with the stag and bees hum at the mullai, he may reach the pleasant woodland town where his pale, tear-eyed wife can at last undo her braid.","n":"The braid is a sign: she bound her hair at his leaving and will loosen it at his return, so his speed is measured by her hair. The doe at play with the stag is what the arrival will be.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","maalai","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 234","lb":"A234","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைமமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["அன்னத்து","அன்னம்",3,"pul","simile","swan"],["கலிமா","மா",4,"maa","simile","horse"],["தழை","தழை",10,"maram","scene",""],["மடப்பிணை","பிணை",10,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["ஏறுபுணர்","ஏறு",11,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["வண்டின்","வண்டு",12,"pira","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",13,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மடப்பிணை","confirmed","2/2","the wife's release at his return will be as glad and as safe as the doe's play with her mate"]],"ch":"a:12"},"a:235":{"book":"akananuru","num":236,"t":["அம்ம- வாழி, தோழி!- பொருள் புரிந்து","உள்ளார் கொல்லோ, காதலர்? உள்ளியும்,","சிறந்த செய்தியின் மறந்தனர் கொல்லோ?-","பயன்நிலம் குழைய வீசிப், பெயல் முனிந்து,","விண்டு முன்னிய கொண்டல் மாமழை","மங்குல் அற்கமொடு பொங்குபு துளிப்ப,","வாடையொடு நிவந்த ஆய்இதழ்த் தோன்றி","சுடர்கொள் அகலின் சுருங்குபிணி அவிழச்","சுரிமுகிழ் முசுண்டைப் பொதிஅவிழ் வான்பூ","விசும்புஅணி மீனின் பசும்புல் அணியக்,","களவன் மண்அளைச் செறிய, அகல்வயல்","கிளைவிரி கரும்பின் கணைக்கால் வான்பூ","மாரிஅம் குருகின் ஈரிய குரங்க,","நனிகடுஞ் சிவப்பொடு நாமம் தோற்றிப்,","பனிகடி கொண்ட பண்பில் வாடை","மருளின் மாலையொடு அருள்இன்றி நலிய,","'நுதல்இறை கொண்ட அயல்அறி பசலையொடு","தொன்னலம் சிதையச் சாஅய்'","என்னள்கொல் அளியள்?' என்னா தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The girl asks her friend whether, set on wealth, he has not thought of them, or thinking has forgotten for his greater work, while the cold north wind and the drizzling east cloud open the thonri like a lamp and the musundai like stars on the grass, the crab hides in its hole, and the graceless wind torments her at evening, he who never asked what would become of her, pale and wasted.","n":"The season is the rains and the cold wind, and the torment is the evening: by the Tholkappiyam's times this is mullai waiting, though the colophon files the poem as paalai because he went for wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","pasalai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 235","lb":"A235","poet":"கழார்க் கீரனெயிற்றியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் ஆற்றாமைமீதூரத் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"ஆற்றாமைமீதூரத் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தோன்றி","தோன்றி",7,"maram","simile",""],["முசுண்டைப்","முசுண்டை",9,"maram","simile",""],["மீனின்","மீன்",10,"pira","simile","star"],["பசும்புல்","புல்",10,"maram","scene",""],["களவன்","களவன்",11,"pira","scene","crab"],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",12,"maram","simile",""],["குருகின்","குருகு",13,"pul","simile","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:12"},"a:236":{"book":"akananuru","num":237,"t":["மணிமருள் மலர முள்ளி அமன்ற,","துணிநீர், இலஞ்சிக் கொண்ட பெருமீன்","அரிநிறக் கொழுங்குறை வௌவினர் மாந்தி,","வெண்ணெல் அரிநர் பெயர்நிலைப் பின்றை,","இடைனில நெரிதரு நெடுங்கதிர்ப் பல்சூட்டுப்","பனிபடு சாய்ப்புறம் பரிப்பக், கழனிக்","கருங்கோட்டு மாஅத்து அலங்குசினைப் புதுப்பூ","மயங்குமழைத் துவலையின் தாஅம் ஊரன்","காமம் பெருமை அறியேன், நன்றும்","உய்ர்த்தனென்- வாழி, தோழி!- அல்கல்","அணிகிளர் சாந்தின் அம்பட்டு இமைப்பக்,","கொடுங்குழை மகளிரின் ஒடுங்கிய இருக்கை","அறியா மையின் அழிந்த நெஞ்சின்,","'ஏற்றுஇயல் எழில்நடைப் பொழிந்த மொய்ம்பின்,","தோட்டுஇருஞ் சுரியன் மணந்த பித்தை,","ஆட்டன் அத்தியை காணீரோ?' என","நாட்டின் நாட்டின், ஊரின் ஊரின்,","'கடல்கொண் டன்று' எனப், 'புனல் ஒளித் தன்று' எனக்","கலுழ்ந்த கண்ணள், காதலற் கெடுத்த","ஆதி மந்தி போல,","ஏதம் சொல்லிப், பேதுபெரிது உறலே!"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that, not knowing how great the town lord's love was, she suffered: last night, not knowing he lay among bejewelled women, she went about with a broken heart telling her trouble, like Aathi Manthi who lost her lover and asked land after land whether anyone had seen Attan Atthi, crying that the sea had taken him, the flood had hidden him.","n":"The phrase about the greatness of his love is bitter: the love she did not know was his love for others. She casts herself as the tragic Manthi while he was safe among other women, and the gap between the legend and his conduct is the reproach.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 236","lb":"A236","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"ஆற்றாமை வாயிலாகப் புக்க தலைமகன் நீக்கத்துக்கண் புக்க தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","ஆற்றாமை வாயிலாகப் புக்க தலைமகன் நீக்கத்துக்கண் புக்க தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முள்ளி","முள்ளி",1,"maram","simile",""],["பெருமீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","gift_food","fish"],["வெண்ணெல்","நெல்",4,"unavu","scene",""],["அரிநர்","அரிநர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["மாஅத்து","மா",7,"maram","simile","mango"],["சாந்தின்","சாந்து",11,"pira","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:237":{"book":"akananuru","num":238,"t":["'புன்காற் பாதிரி அரிநிறத் திரள்வீ","நுண்கொடி அதிரலொடு நுணங்கறல் வரிப்ப,","அரவுஎயிற்று அன்ன அரும்புமுதிர் குரவின்","தேன்இமிர் நறுஞ்சினைத் தென்றல் போழக்,","குயில்குரல் கற்ற வேனிலும் துயில்துறந்து","இன்னா கழியும் கங்குல்' என்றுநின்","நல்மா மேனி அணிநலம் புலம்ப,","இனைதல் ஆன்றிசின்- ஆயிழை! கனைதிறல்","செந்தீ அணங்கிய செழுநிணக் கொழுங்குறை","மென்தினை புன்கம் உதிர்த்த மண்டையொடு,","இருங்கதிர் அலமரும் கழனிக் கரும்பின்","விளைகழை பிழிந்த அம்தீஞ் சேற்றொடு,","பால்பெய் செந்நெற் பாசவல் பகுக்கும்","புனல்பொரு புதவின்; உறந்தை எய்தினும்,","வினைபொரு ளாகத் தவிரலர்- கடைசிவந்து","ஐய அமர்த்த உண்கண்நின்","வைஏர் வால்எயிறு ஊறிய நீரே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl to stop grieving that the spring nights, with paathiri flowers on the sand and the south wind in the kuravam, pass sleepless: even if he reached rich Uranthai, where roast meat, millet, cane syrup and milk-soaked rice flakes are shared out, he would not stay there for his work's sake, away from the sweetness of her mouth.","n":"The friend answers spring with plenty: the feast-town of Uranthai is the strongest temptation she can name, and she sets the girl's mouth above it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaalam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 237","lb":"A237","poet":"தாயங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறீஇயது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பாதிரி","பாதிரி",1,"maram","scene",""],["அதிரலொடு","அதிரல்",2,"maram","scene",""],["அரவுஎயிற்று","அரவு",3,"pira","simile","snake"],["குரவின்","குரவம்",3,"maram","simile",""],["குயில்","குயில்",5,"pul","scene",""],["செழுநிணக்","நிணம்",9,"unavu","gift_food",""],["மென்தினை","தினை",10,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",11,"maram","scene",""],["செந்நெற்","நெல்",13,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பால்பெய்","பால்",13,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[["town","உறந்தை",14]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:238":{"book":"akananuru","num":239,"t":["மான்றமை அறியா மரம்பயில் இடும்பின்,","ஈன்றுஇளைப் பட்ட வயவுப்பிணப் பசித்தென,","மடமான் வல்சி தரீஇய, நடுநாள்,","இருள்முகைச் சிலம்பின், இரைவேட்டு எழுந்த","பணைமருள் எருத்தின் பல்வரி இரும்போத்து,","மடக்கண் ஆமான் மாதிரத்து அலறத்,","தடக்கோட்டு ஆமான் அண்ணல் ஏஎறு","நனந்தலைக் கானத்து வலம்படத் தொலைச்சி","இருங்கல் வியல்அறை சிவப்ப ஈர்க்கும்","பெருகல் நாட; பிரிதி ஆயின்,","மருந்தும் உடையையோ மற்றே- இரப்போர்க்கு","இழை அணி நெடுந்தேர் களிறொடு என்றும்","மழைசுரந் தன்ன ஈகை, வண்மகிழ்க்,","கழல்தொடித் தடக்கைக், கலிமான், நள்ளி","நளிமுகை உடைந்த நறுங்கார் அடுக்கத்து,","போந்தை முழுமுதல் நிலைஇய காந்தள்","மென்பிணி முகை அவிழ்ந்து அலர்ந்த","தண்கமழ் புதுமலர் நாறும்நறு நுதற்கே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the hill lord who has come by night, where a tiger rises at midnight to fetch food for his mate weak after bearing young, kills a bull gaur and drags it reddening the rock, whether, if he parts from her, he has any cure for her forehead, fragrant as the kaanthal on the slopes of Nalli, whose giving pours like rain.","n":"Kapilar sets the tiger's care for his new mother against the hero's night visits: the beast provides, and the question is whether the man will. Nalli's generosity, praised in passing, gives the girl's fragrance a patron's fame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 238","lb":"A238","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி வந்த தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி வந்த தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["மரம்பயில்","மரம்",1,"maram","scene",""],["வயவுப்பிணப்","பிணா",2,"maa","iraicchi","tigress"],["மடமான்","மான்",3,"maa","gift_food","deer"],["இரும்போத்து","போத்து",5,"maa","iraicchi","tiger"],["ஆமான்","ஆமான்",6,"maa","scene","gaur"],["ஏஎறு","ஏறு",7,"maa","scene","bull gaur"],["களிறொடு","களிறு",12,"maa","gift_food",""],["கலிமான்","மா",14,"maa","scene","horse"],["போந்தை","போந்தை",16,"maram","scene",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",16,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","நள்ளி",14]],"dv":[["iraicchi","இரும்போத்து","confirmed","2/2","the man who comes by night should, like the tiger, take on the care of the woman rather than leave her"]],"ch":"a:12"},"a:239":{"book":"akananuru","num":240,"t":["அளிதோ தானே; எவன்ஆ வதுகொல்!","மன்றும் தோன்றாது, மரனும் மாயும்-","'புலிஎன உலம்பும் செங்கண் ஆடவர்,","ஞெலியொடு பிடித்த வார்கோல் அம்பினர்","எல்ஊர் எறிந்து, பல்ஆத் தழீஇய","விளிபடு பூசல் வெஞ்சுரத்து இரட்டும்","வேறுபல் தேஅத்து ஆறுபல நீந்திப்","புள்ளித் தொய்யில், பொறிபடு சுணங்கின்,","ஒள்இழை மகளிர் உயர்பிறை தொழூஉம்","புல்லென் மாலை, யாம்இவண் ஒழிய,","ஈட்டுஅருங் குரைய பொருள்வயிற் செலினே,","நீட்டுவிர் அல்லிரோ, நெடுந்தகையீர்? எனக்,","குறுநெடு புலவி கூறி, நம்மொடு","நெருநலும் தீம்பல மொழிந்த","சிறுநல் ஒருத்தி பெருநல் ஊரே!"],"p":"","r":"Leaving for wealth, the hero tells his heart how pitiable is the fine town of the little girl who only yesterday, with long and short sulks and many sweet words, asked whether he would not stay long away, crossing rivers of strange lands on tracks where red-eyed raiders roaring like tigers seize cattle at night, while the women here worship the crescent at dusk.","n":"Her question is quoted whole inside his speech, so the poem is her protest overheard in his memory. The cattle raid on the track is puram matter (vetchi) seen from the akam side, as a danger the girl names.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","nenju","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 239","lb":"A239","poet":"எயினந்தை மகனிளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்துபோகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்துபோகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["மரனும்","மரம்",2,"maram","scene",""],["புலிஎன","புலி",3,"maa","simile",""],["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["பல்ஆத்","ஆ",5,"maa","scene","cattle"],["பிறை","பிறை",9,"pira","scene","crescent moon"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:12"},"a:240":{"book":"akananuru","num":241,"t":["செவ்வீ ஞாழற் கருங்கோட்டு இருஞ்சினைத்","தனிப்பார்ப்பு உள்ளிய தண்பறை நாரை","மணிப்பூ நெய்தல் மாக்கழி நிவப்ப,","இனிப்புலம் பின்றே கானலும், நளிகடல்","திரைச்சுரம் உழந்த திண்திமில் விளக்கில்","பன்மீன் கூட்டம் என்னையர் காட்டிய","எந்தையும் செல்லுமார் இரவே; அந்தில்","அணங்குடைப் பனித்துறை கைதொழுது ஏத்தி,","யாயும் ஆயமோடு அயரும்; நீயும்,","தேம்பாய் ஓதி திருநுதல் நீவிக்,","கோங்குமுகைத் தன்ன குவிமுலை ஆகத்து,","இன்துயில் அமர்ந்தனை ஆயின், வண்டுபட","விரிந்த செருந்தி வெண்மணல் முடுக்கர்ப்,","பூவேய் புன்னை அம் தண்பொழில்,","வாவே தெய்ய, மணந்தனை செலற்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man who came by night that the shore grove is no longer empty at night, for father goes out to the fishing lamps and mother worships at the haunted ford with her companions, so if he wants to sleep on the girl's breast he should come to the punnai grove in the white-sand nook where the seruthi blooms.","n":"The stork flying over the backwater, mindful of its lone nestling, could carry a sense for a man who leaves a girl alone; we leave it as scene because the poem's own point is the household's night movements. Line 4's புலம்பின்றே can be read as 'not lonely' (crowded) or the reverse, and the advice turns on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 240","lb":"A240","poet":"எழூஉப்பன்றி நாகன் குமரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி, இரவுக்குறிவந்த தலைமகற்குப் பகற்குறி நேர்ந்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறிவந்த தலைமகற்குப் பகற்குறி நேர்ந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஞாழற்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene",""],["தனிப்பார்ப்பு","பார்ப்பு",2,"pul","scene",""],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","scene","stork"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","scene",""],["திமில்","திமில்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["பன்மீன்","மீன்",6,"pira","scene","fish"],["அணங்குடைப்","அணங்கு",8,"deyvam","scene",""],["கோங்குமுகைத்","கோங்கு",11,"maram","simile",""],["செருந்தி","செருந்தி",13,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",14,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:12"},"a:241":{"book":"akananuru","num":242,"t":["'துனிஇன்று இயைந்த துவரா நட்பின்","இனியர் அம்ம, அவர்' என முனியாது","நல்குவர் நல்ல கூறினும், அல்கலும்,","பிரியாக் காதலொடு உழையர் ஆகிய","நமர்மன்- வாழி, தோழி!- உயர்மிசை","மூங்கில் இளமுளை திரங்கக், காம்பின்","கழைநரல் வியலகம் வெம்ப, மழைமறந்து","அருவி ஆன்ற வெருவரு நனந்தலைப்","பேஎய் வெண்தேர்ப் பெயல்செத்து ஓடி,","தாஅம் பட்ட தனிமுதிர் பெருங்கலை","புலம்பெயர்ந்து உறைதல் செல்லாது அலங்குதலை","விருந்தின் வெங்காட்டு வருந்தி வைகும்","அத்த நெல்லித் தீஞ்சுவைத் திரள்காய்","வட்டக் கழங்கின் தாஅய்த், துய்த்தலைச்","செம்முக மந்தி ஆடும்","நல்மர மருங்கின் மலைஇறந் தோரே!"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that, whatever kind words the friend says of the man's untiring, quarrel-free love, he who once never left her side was hers only once: he has crossed the hills where bamboo shoots shrivel, the rains have forgotten the land and the falls have run dry, where a lone old stag that ran after the white mirage taking it for rain lies spent in the hot forest, and where a red-faced she-monkey plays with fallen nelli fruit scattered like round play-pebbles.","n":"The single particle மன் in நமர்மன் carries the poem: he was ours, once. The stag fooled by the mirage (பேஎய் வெண்தேர்ப் பெயல்செத்து) is left as scene, since it does nothing a person of the situation does; a reader may still hear in it the woman's own hope, chasing comfort that the friend's words hold out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","natpu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 241","lb":"A241","poet":"காவன் முல்லைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள், வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மூங்கில்","மூங்கில்",6,"maram","scene",""],["கழைநரல்","கழை",7,"maram","scene",""],["பெருங்கலை","கலை",10,"maa","scene","stag"],["நெல்லித்","நெல்லி",13,"maram","simile",""],["மந்தி","மந்தி",15,"maa","scene","she-monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:13"},"a:242":{"book":"akananuru","num":243,"t":["அரும்புமுதிர் வேங்கை அலங்கல் மென்சினைச்","சுரும்புவாய் திறந்த பொன்புரை நுண்தாது","மணிமருள் கலவத்து உறைப்ப, அணிமிக்கு","அவிர்பொறி மஞ்ஜை ஆடும் சோலைப்,","பைந்தாட் செந்தினைக் கொடுங்குரல் வியன்புனம்,","செந்தார்க் கிள்ளை நம்மொடு கடிந்தோன்","பண்புதர வந்தமை அறியாள், 'நுண்கேழ்","முறிபுரை எழில்நலத்து என்மகள் துயர்மருங்கு","அறிதல் வேண்டும்' எனப், பல்பிரப்பு இரீஇ","அறியா வேலற் றரீஇ, அன்னை","வெறிஅயர் வியன்களம் பொலிய ஏத்தி","மறிஉயிர் வழங்கா அளவை, சென்றுயாம்","செலவரத் துணிந்த, சேண்விளங்கு, எல்வளை","நெகிழ்ந்த முன்கை, நேர்இறைப் பணைத்தோள்,","நல்எழில் அழிவின் தொல்கவின் பெறீஇய,","முகிழ்த்து வரல் இளமுலை மூழ்கப், பல்ஊழ்","முயங்கல் இயைவன் மன்னோ-தோழி!-","நறைகால் யாத்த நளிர்முகைச் சிலம்பில்","பெருமலை விடரகம் நீடிய சிறுயிலைச்","சாந்த மென்சினை தீண்டி, மேலது","பிரசம் தூங்கும் சேண்சிமை,","வரையக வெற்பன் மணந்த மார்பே!"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening outside, the friend tells the woman that their mother, not knowing that the man who chased parrots with them from the millet field came to her in love, wants to learn the cause of her daughter's sickness, has set out offerings and brought the ignorant velan for the veri rite; before the goat's life is given, he would do well to come and hold her often, so her slack bangles and wasted shoulders regain their old beauty.","n":"The deadline is exact: மறி உயிர் வழங்கா அளவை, before the kid is slain. The diviner is called அறியா வேலன், the one who does not know, so the rite is mocked even as it threatens to expose the secret. Spoken for the man's ears, the poem presses him to marry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 242","lb":"A242","poet":"பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene",""],["சுரும்புவாய்","சுரும்பு",2,"pira","scene","bee"],["மஞ்ஜை","மஞ்ஞை",4,"pul","scene","peacock"],["செந்தினைக்","தினை",5,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கிள்ளை","கிள்ளை",6,"pul","scene","parrot"],["வேலற்","வேலன்",10,"seythi","scene",""],["மறிஉயிர்","மறி",12,"maa","scene","goat kid"],["சாந்த","சாந்தம்",20,"maram","scene","sandal"],["பிரசம்","பிரசம்",21,"pira","scene","honeycomb"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:13"},"a:243":{"book":"akananuru","num":244,"t":["அவரை ஆய்மலர் உதிரத், துவரின்","வாங்குதுளைத் துகிரின் ஈங்கை பூப்ப","இறங்குபோது அவிழ்ந்த ஈர்ம்புதல் பகன்றைக்","கறங்குநுண் துவலையின் ஊருழை அணியப்,","பெயல்நீர் புதுவரல் தவிரச், சினைநேர்பு","பீள் விரிந்து இறைஞ்சிய பிறங்குகதிர்க் கழனி","நெல்ஒலி பாசவல் துழைஇக், கல்லெனக்","கடிதுவந்து இறுத்த கண்இல், வாடை!","'நெடிதுவந் தனை' என நில்லாது ஏங்கிப்","பலபுலந்து உறையும் துணைஇல் வாழ்க்கை","நம்வலத்து அன்மை கூறி, அவர்நிலை","அறியுநம் ஆயின், நன்றுமன் தில்ல;","பனிவார் கண்ணேம் ஆகி, இனிஅது","நமக்கே எவ்வம் ஆகின்று;","அனைத்தால் தோழி! நம் தொல்வினைப் பயனே!"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the merciless north wind has come and settled, now that the avarai flowers fall, eengai blooms like red coral, pagandrai opens around the town, the new rains have stopped and the paddy hangs in ripe ears; if they could make him know their lonely state, which is past their bearing, it would be well, but now, with streaming eyes, the pain is theirs alone, and such is the fruit of their old deeds.","n":"She ends not with blame but with தொல்வினைப் பயன், the fruit of former deeds, a word the later ethics books make doctrine; here it explains nothing away and comforts no one. The wind is கண்இல், eyeless or pitiless, the only one who has come.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vinai","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 243","lb":"A243","poet":"கொடியூர்கிழார் மகனார் நெய்தல்தத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் ஆற்றேன் என்பதுபடச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"ஆற்றேன் என்பதுபடச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அவரை","அவரை",1,"maram","scene",""],["ஈங்கை","ஈங்கை",2,"maram","simile",""],["பகன்றைக்","பகன்றை",3,"maram","scene",""],["நெல்ஒலி","நெல்",7,"unavu","scene","paddy"],["வாடை","வாடை",8,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:13"},"a:244":{"book":"akananuru","num":245,"t":["\"பசைபடு பச்சை நெய்தோய்த் தன்ன","சேயுயர் சினைய மாச்சிறைப் பறவை","பகலுறை முதுமரம் புலம்பப் போகி,","முகைவாய் திறந்த நகைவாய் முல்லை","கடிமகள் கதுப்பின் நாறிக் கொடிமிசை","வண்டினம் தவிர்க்கும் தண்பதக் காலை","வரினும், வாரார் ஆயினும், ஆண்டு அவர்க்கு","இனிதுகொல், வாழி தோழி?\" எனத்தன்","பல்லிதழ் மழைக்கண் நல்லகஞ் சிவப்ப,","'அருந்துயர் உடையள் அவள்' என விரும்பிப்","பாணன் வந்தனன், தூதே; நீயும்","புல்லார் புரவி, வல்விரைந்து, பூட்டி,","நெடுந்தேர் ஊர்மதி, வலவ!-","முடிந்தன்று அம்ம, நாம் முன்னிய வினையே!"],"p":"","r":"The man, his work done, tells his charioteer that the bard has come as a messenger, moved by her grief, reporting how she asked her friend, her eyes reddening, whether it is sweet for him there in this cool hour when the dark-winged birds leave their old tree and the opening mullai smells like a wedded woman's hair; so he bids the charioteer yoke the horses fast and drive, for the task is finished.","n":"The woman's question is quoted within the bard's report within the man's speech: three voices nested in fourteen lines. Her words வரினும், வாரார் ஆயினும் (whether he comes or not) put the season's sweetness against his absence; the man answers with action, not words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 244","lb":"A244","poet":"மதுரை...மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பறவை","பறவை",2,"pul","simile","bat"],["முதுமரம்","முதுமரம்",3,"maram","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",4,"maram","simile",""],["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene","bee"],["பாணன்","பாணன்",11,"seythi","scene",""],["புரவி","புரவி",12,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:13"},"a:245":{"book":"akananuru","num":246,"t":["'உயிரினும் சிறந்த ஒண்பொருள் தருமார்","நன்றுபுரி காட்சியர் சென்றனர், அவர்' என","மனைவலித்து ஒழியும் மதுகையள் ஆதல்","நீ நற்கு அறிந்தனை ஆயின், நீங்கி","மழைபெயன் மறந்த கழைதிரங்கு இயவில்","செல்சாத்து எறியும் பண்பில் வாழ்க்கை","வல்வில் இளையர் தலைவர், எல்லுற,","வரிகிளர் பணைத்தோள், வயிறணி திதலை,","அரிய லாட்டியர் அல்குமனை வரைப்பில்,","மகிழ்நொடை பெறாஅ ராகி, நனைகவுள்","கான யானை வெண்கோடு சுட்டி,","மன்றுஓடு புதல்வன் புன்தலை நீவும்","அருமுனைப் பாக்கத்து அல்கி, வைகுற,","நிழல்படக் கவின்ற நீள்அரை இலவத்து","அழல் அகைந் தன்ன அலங்குசினை ஒண்பூக்","குழல்இசைத் தும்பி ஆர்க்கும் ஆங்கண்,","குறும்பொறை உணங்கும் ததர்வெள் என்பு","கடுங்கால் ஒட்டகத்து அல்குபசி தீர்க்கும்","கல்நெடுங் கவலைய கானம் நீந்தி,","அம்மா அரிவை ஒழிய,","சென்மோ- நெஞ்சம்!- வாரலென் யானே"],"p":"","r":"The man rebukes his heart, which has driven him toward wealth: if it knows well that she has the strength to hold the house, telling herself he went with good intent to bring wealth dearer than life, then let it go alone through the rainless bamboo tracks where bandit chiefs who live by robbing caravans pledge a wild elephant's tusk for toddy and stroke a child's head in the frontier hamlet, past flame-red ilavam flowers humming with bees and white bones that feed the camel's hunger; as for him, he will not come.","n":"The quoted line உயிரினும் சிறந்த ஒண்பொருள் is what he imagines her saying to steady herself, and it is her steadiness that stops him: he cannot leave a woman who would bear it so well. The camel (ஒட்டகம்) eating bones is a rare beast in this poetry and marks the land as truly far.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 245","lb":"A245","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் கடைக்கூட்டிய நெஞ்சினைக் கழறித் தலைமகன் சொல்லிச் செலவழுங்கியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","செலவழுங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["கழைதிரங்கு","கழை",5,"maram","scene",""],["வல்வில்","வில் இளையர்",7,"seythi","scene","bandits"],["அரிய லாட்டியர்","அரியலாட்டி",9,"seythi","scene","toddy-women"],["யானை","யானை",11,"maa","scene",""],["இலவத்து","இலவம்",14,"maram","simile",""],["தும்பி","தும்பி",16,"pira","scene","bee"],["என்பு","என்பு",17,"pira","scene","bone"],["ஒட்டகத்து","ஒட்டகம்",18,"maa","scene","camel"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:13"},"a:246":{"book":"akananuru","num":247,"t":["பிணர்மோட்டு நந்தின் பேழ்வாய் ஏற்றை","கதிர்மூக்கு ஆரல் களவன் ஆக,","நெடுநீர்ப் பொய்கைத் துணையொடு புணரும்","மலிநீர் அகல்வாய் யாணர் ஊர!","போதுஆர் கூந்தல் நீவெய் யோளொடு","தாதுஆர் காஞ்சித் தண்பொழில் அகல்யாறு","ஆடினை என்ப நெருநை; அலரே","காய்சின மொய்ம்பின் பெரும்பெயர்க் கரிகால்","ஆர்கலி நறவின் வெண்ணி வாயில்,","சீர்கெழு மன்னர் மறலிய ஞாட்பின்","இமிழிசை முரசம் பொருகளத்து ஒழியப்,","பதினொரு வேளிரொடு வேந்தர் சாய,","மொய்வலி அறுத்த ஞான்றை,","தொய்யா அழுந்தூர் ஆர்ப்பினும் பெரிதே"],"p":"","r":"The friend refuses the husband entry: lord of a rich town where the rough-backed male snail mates with its partner in the deep pond, with the aaral fish and the crab about, they say that yesterday you bathed in the wide river by the kaanji grove with the flower-haired woman you desire; the gossip is louder than the shout at Azhundur on the day Karikal, at the gate of Venni, broke the might of the kings and the eleven Velir, their drums left on the field.","n":"Paranar measures a private scandal by the noise of a dynastic victory, the Chola's own town of Azhundur roaring for Venni. The snail's open mating in a pond full of watchers is the friend's way of telling the man that what he did in the river was seen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","alar","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 246","lb":"A246","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகற்கு வாயில் மறுத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தலைமகற்கு வாயில் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["நந்தின்","நந்து",1,"pira","ullurai","snail"],["ஆரல்","ஆரல்",2,"pira","ullurai","fish"],["களவன்","களவன்",2,"pira","ullurai","crab"],["காஞ்சித்","காஞ்சி",6,"maram","scene",""],["நறவின்","நறவு",9,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["முரசம்","முரசம்",11,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["king","கரிகால்",8],["battle","வெண்ணி வாயில்",9],["town","அழுந்தூர்",14]],"dv":[["ullurai","நந்தின் ஆரல் களவன்","confirmed","2/2","the husband and the woman he bathed with, seen by the town"]],"ch":"a:13"},"a:247":{"book":"akananuru","num":248,"t":["மண்ணா முத்தம் ஒழுக்கிய வனமுலை","நன்மாண் ஆகம் புலம்பத் துறந்தோர்","அருளிலர்- வாழி, தோழி!- பொருள்புரிந்து,","இருங்கிளை எண்கின் அழல்வாய் ஏற்றை","கருங்கோட்டு இருப்பை வெண்பூ முனையின்","பெருஞ்செம் புற்றின் இருந்தலை இடக்கும்","அரிய கானம் என்னார், பகைபட","முனைபாழ் பட்ட ஆங்கண், ஆள்பார்த்துக்","கொலைவல் யானை சுரம்கடி கொள்ளும்","ஊறுபடு கவலைய ஆறுபல நீந்திப்","படுமுடை நசைஇய பறைநெடுங் கழுத்தின்,","பாறுகிளை சேக்கும் சேண்சிமைக்","கோடுயர் பிறங்கல் மலைஇறந் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the man who left her pearl-strung breast lonely has no grace: set on wealth, he did not think how hard the forest is, where the fiery-mouthed male bear with its clan, tired of white iruppai blossom, digs at the top of a red anthill, where a killer elephant watches the waste frontier for men, and where flocks of long-necked vultures, hungry for carrion, roost on the high peaks he crossed.","n":"Her charge is அருளிலர், he lacks compassion, not that he lacks love; she reproaches the choice of wealth over care. Every creature on the road is a feeder (bear, elephant, vulture), which makes the land a place that consumes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","arul","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 247","lb":"A247","poet":"மதுரை மருதங்கிழார் மகனார் பெருங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளது வேறுபாடு கண்டு வேறுபட்ட தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"வேறுபட்ட தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",1,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["எண்கின்","எண்கு",4,"maa","scene","bear"],["இருப்பை","இருப்பை",5,"maram","scene",""],["புற்றின்","புற்று",6,"pira","scene","anthill"],["யானை","யானை",9,"maa","scene",""],["பாறுகிளை","பாறு",12,"pul","scene","vulture"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:13"},"a:248":{"book":"akananuru","num":249,"t":["நகைநீ கோளாய்- தோழி!- அல்கல்","வயநாய் எறிந்து, வன்பறழ் தழீஇ,","இளையர் எய்துதல் மடக்கிக், கிளையொடு","நான்முலைப் பிணவல் சொலியக், கான் ஒழிந்து,","அரும்புழை முடுக்கர் ஆள்குறித்து நின்ற","தறுகட் பன்றி நோக்கிக், கானவன்","குறுகினன் தொடுத்த கூர்வாய்ப் பகழி","மடைசெலல் முன்பின்தன் படைசெலச் செல்லாது,","அருவழி விலக்கும்எம் பெருவிறல் போலும்' என,","எய்யாது பெயரும் குன்ற நாடன்","செறிஅரில் துடக்கலின், பரீஇப் புரிஅவிழ்ந்து,","ஏந்துகுவவு மொய்ம்பிற் பூச்சோர் மாலை,","ஏற்றுஇமில் கயிற்றின், எழில்வந்து துயல்வர","இல்வந்து நின்றோற் கண்டனள் அன்னை;","வல்லே என்முகம் நோக்கி.","'நல்லை மன்!' என நகூஉப் பெயர்ந் தோளே!"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening outside, the friend tells the woman a joke: last night, after a hunter spared a boar that had killed his hound and stood guarding its young in a narrow pass because it looked like his own chief holding the path while his army fell back, the hill lord came to the house, his garland torn by the thicket and swinging on his shoulders like the rope on a bull's hump, and their mother saw him, looked straight at my face, said 'you are a fine one!' and went off laughing.","n":"The joke is a warning. The mother has seen the man at the door and chosen, for now, to laugh, as the hunter saw the boar and chose not to shoot; the next sight may not end so lightly, so the man should stop coming by night and ask for her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 248","lb":"A248","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிச் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தலைமகன் கேட்பத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறிச் சிறைப்புறமாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"],["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["வயநாய்","நாய்",2,"maa","scene","hound"],["பிணவல்","பிணவல்",4,"maa","scene","sow"],["பன்றி","பன்றி",6,"maa","simile","boar"],["கானவன்","கானவன்",6,"seythi","iraicchi",""],["மாலை","மாலை",12,"maram","ornament","garland"],["ஏற்றுஇமில்","ஏறு",13,"maa","simile","bull"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கானவன்","not_confirmed","0/1","the mother too has seen the lover and let him go with a laugh, a reprieve that will not last if he keeps coming by night"],["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:13"},"a:249":{"book":"akananuru","num":250,"t":["அம்ம- வாழி, தோழி!- பல்நாள்","இவ்ஊர் அம்பல் எவனோ? வள்வார்","விசிபிணித்து யாத்த அரிகோல் தெண்கிணை","இன்குரல் அகவுநர் இரப்பின் நாடொறும்","பொன்கோட்டுச் செறித்துப், பொலந்தார் பூட்டிச்,","சாந்தம் புதைத்த ஏந்துதுவங்கு எழிலிமில்","ஏறுமுந் துறுத்துச், சால்பதம் குவைஇ,","நெடுந்தேர் களிற்றொடு சுரக்கும் கொடும்பூண்","பல்வேல் முசுண்டை வேம்பி அன்னஎன்","நல்லெழில் இளநலம் தொலையினம், நல்கார்","பல்பூங் கானத்து அல்குநிழல் அசைஇத்,","தோகைத் தூவித் தொடைத்தார் மழவர்","நாகுஆ வீழ்த்துத், திற்றி தின்ற","புலவுக்களம் துழைஇய துகள்வாய்க் கோடை","நீள்வரைச் சிலம்பின் இரைவேட்டு எழுந்த","வாள்வரி வயப்புலி தீண்டிய விளிசெத்து,","வேறுவேறு கவலைய ஆறுபரிந்து, அலறி,","உழைமான் இனநிரை ஓடும்","கழைமாய் பிறங்கல் மலைஇறந் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend what the town's long gossip is for, when her young beauty, fine as Vembi, town of Musundai of many spears who daily gives bards gold-horned bulls, garlands, heaps of food, chariots and elephants, is wasting and he shows no grace, having crossed the bamboo hills where Mazhavar in peacock-feather garlands kill a young cow and eat its roast flesh, and the dusty summer wind over that place sounds so like a hungry tiger's roar that the deer herds scatter on every path.","n":"The gossip is idle because there is nothing left to gossip about: the beauty it talks of is already going. Her beauty is measured by a patron's giving, so her worth is set on the scale of puram generosity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","nalam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 249","lb":"A249","poet":"நக்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளது வேறுபாடு கண்டு ஆற்றாளாய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"ஆற்றாளாய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தெண்கிணை","கிணை",3,"parai","scene",""],["ஏறுமுந்","ஏறு",7,"maa","gift_food","bull"],["களிற்றொடு","களிறு",8,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["தோகைத்","தோகை",12,"pul","ornament","peacock feather"],["மழவர்","மழவர்",12,"seythi","scene",""],["நாகுஆ","நாகு ஆ",13,"maa","gift_food","young cow"],["திற்றி","திற்றி",13,"unavu","gift_food","roast meat"],["வயப்புலி","புலி",16,"maa","scene","tiger"],["உழைமான்","உழைமான்",18,"maa","scene","deer"]],"en":[["patron","முசுண்டை",9],["town","வேம்பி",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:13"},"a:250":{"book":"akananuru","num":251,"t":["எவன்கொல்?- வாழி, தோழி!- மயங்குபிசிர்","மல்குதிரை உழந்த ஒல்குநிலைப் புன்னை","வண்டிமிர் இணர நுண்தாது வரிப்ப","மணம்கமழ் இளமணல் எக்கர்க் காண்வரக்,","கணம்கொள் ஆயமொடு புணர்ந்து விளையாடக்,","கொடுஞ்சி நெடுந்தேர் இளையரொடு நீக்கித்,","தாரன், கண்ணியன், சேரவந்து, ஒருவன்,","வரிமனை புகழ்ந்த கிளவியன் யாவதும்","மறுமொழி பெறாஅன் பெயர்ந்தனன்; அதற்கொண்டு","அரும்படர் எவ்வமொடு பெருந்தோள் சாஅய்","அவ்வலைப் பரதவர் கானல்ஞ் சிறுகுடி","செவ்வாய்ப் பெண்டிர் கவ்வையின் கலங்கி,","இறைவளை நெகிழ்ந்த நம்மொடு","துறையும் துஞ்சாது, கங்கு லானே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the woman what has happened: while they played with their companions on the fragrant dune under the wave-beaten punnai, a man with garland and chaplet left his chariot with his young men, came close praising their sand-house, got no word in reply and went away; since then he has wasted with longing, the fishing hamlet's women talk, and even the shore stays awake at night with us whose bangles slip.","n":"The friend is pleading for the man (குறைநயப்பு): she tells the meeting as a scene the woman herself took part in, and joins the woman's slipping bangles to his wasting, so that the refusal begins to look like a shared hurt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","alar","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 250","lb":"A250","poet":"செல்லூர்கிழார் மகனார் பெரும்பூதங்கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகற்குக் குறைநோந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குக் குறைநயப்பக் கூறியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தலைமகற்குக் குறைநோந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குக் குறைநயப்பக் கூறியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",2,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டிமிர்","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",11,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:13"},"a:251":{"book":"akananuru","num":252,"t":["தூதும் சென்றன; தோளும் செற்றும்;","ஓதி ஒண்நுதல் பசலையும் மாயும்;","வீங்கிழை நெகிழச் சாஅய்ச்; செல்லலொடு","நாம்படர் கூரும் அருந்துயர் கேட்பின்,","நந்தன் வெறுக்கை எய்தினும் மற்றவண்","தங்கலர்- வாழி, தோழி!- வெல்கொடித்","துனைகால் அன்ன புனைதேர்க் கோசர்","தொல்மூ தாலத்து அரும்பணைப் பொதியில்,","இன்இசை முரசம் கடிப்பிகுத்து இரங்கத்,","தெம்முனை சிதைத்த ஞான்றை; மோகூர்","பணியா மையின், பகைதலை வந்த","மாகெழு தானை வம்ப மோரியர்","புனைதேர் நேமி உருளிய குறைத்த","இலங்குவெள் அருவிய அறைவாய் உம்பர்,","மாசில் வெண்கோட்டு அண்ணல் யானை","வாயுள் தப்பிய அருங்கேழ், வயப்புலி","மாநிலம் நெளியக் குத்திப், புகலொடு","காப்புஇல வைகும் தேக்கமல் சோலை","நிரம்பா நீளிடைப் போகி-","அரம்போழ் அவ்வளை நிலைநெகிழ்த் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that the messages have gone, her shoulders will fill out and her pallor fade, for if he hears of their grief he will not stay there even for Nanda's treasure, he who loosened her bangles and went the long way past the rock-cut road the Moriyar's wheels rolled through when they came against Mokur that would not submit, after the Kosar of wind-swift chariots had broken the enemy at the hall of the old banyan, into the teak groves where a tiger that escaped the elephant's tusk lies unguarded.","n":"Mamulanar's long relative clause runs history into the road: the Kosar's victory, Mokur's refusal, the northern Moriyar cutting a road for their chariots. The man's path is laid down by empires, and the friend still bets that the woman's grief outweighs Nanda's gold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thoothu","pasalai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 251","lb":"A251","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளது வேறுபாடு கண்டு தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"வேறுபாடு கண்டு தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாலத்து","ஆலம்",8,"maram","scene","banyan"],["முரசம்","முரசம்",9,"parai","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",15,"maa","scene",""],["வயப்புலி","புலி",16,"maa","scene","tiger"],["தேக்கமல்","தேக்கு",18,"maram","scene","teak"]],"en":[["king","நந்தன்",5],["town","மோகூர்",10],["king","மோரியர்",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:13"},"a:252":{"book":"akananuru","num":253,"t":["இடம்படுபு அறியா வலம்படு வேட்டத்து","வாள்வரி நடுங்கப் புகல்வந்து, ஆளி","உயர்நுதல் யானைப் புகர்முகத்து ஒற்றி,","வெண்கோடு புய்க்கும் தண்கமழ் சோலைப்","பெருவரை அடுக்கத்து ஒருவேல் ஏந்தித்","தனியன் வருதல் அவனும் அஞ்சான்;","பனிவார் கண்ணேன் ஆகி, நோய்அட,","எமியேன் இருத்தலை யானும் ஆற்றேன்;","பாங்குச் செய்வாம்கொல்- தோழி! ஈங்கைத்","துய்அவிழ் பனிமலர் உதிர வீசித்","தொழில்மழை பொழிந்த பானாட் கங்குல்","எறிதிரைத் திவலை தூஉம் சிறுகோட்டுப்","பெருங்குளம் காவலன் போல,","அருங்கடி அன்னையம் துயில்மறந் தனளே!"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening outside, the woman tells her friend that he is not afraid to come alone with one spear down the hill slope where the yali, making the tiger tremble, strikes an elephant's spotted brow and tears out its tusk, but she cannot bear to stay alone with streaming eyes; shall they make some plan, for their strict mother has forgotten sleep like the watchman of a great tank at midnight when the rain has poured and scattered the eengai blossom.","n":"Two kinds of courage face each other: his on the beast-haunted path, hers that fails at home. The mother as a sleepless tank-keeper is a sharp image: she guards the girl as one guards water that may break its banks.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 252","lb":"A252","poet":"நக்கண்ணையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["ஆளி","ஆளி",2,"maa","scene","yali"],["வாள்வரி","வாள்வரி",2,"maa","scene","tiger"],["யானைப்","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""],["ஈங்கைத்","ஈங்கை",9,"maram","scene",""],["காவலன்","காவலன்",13,"seythi","simile","tank watchman"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:13"},"a:253":{"book":"akananuru","num":254,"t":["'வைகல் தோறும் பசலை பாய, என்","மெய்யும் பெரும்பிறிது ஆகின்று; ஒய்யென,","அன்னையும் அமரா முகத்தினள்; அலரே,","வாடாப் பூவிற் கொங்கர் ஓட்டி,","நாடுபல தந்த பசும்பூண் பாண்டியன்","பொன்மலி நெடுநகர்க் கூடல் ஆடிய","இன்இசை ஆர்ப்பினும் பெரிதே; ஈங்குயான்","சிலநாள் உய்யலென் போன்ம்' எனப் பலநினைந்து","ஆழல்- வாழி, தோழி!- வடாஅது,","ஆர்இருள் நடுநாள் ஏர்ஆ ஒய்யப்","பகைமுனை அறுத்துப் பல்இனம் சாஅய்","கணம்சால் கோவலர் நெடுவிளிப் பயிர்அறிந்து,","இனம்தலைத் தரூஉம் துளங்குஇமில் நல்ஏற்றுத்","தழூஉப்பிணர் எருத்தம் தாழப் பூட்டிய","அம்தூம்பு அகல்அமைக் கமஞ்செலப் பெய்த","துறுகாழ் வல்சியர் தொழுஅறை வௌவி,","கன்றுடைப் பெருநிரை மன்றுநிறை தரூஉம்","நேரா வன்தோள் வடுகர் பெருமகன்,","பேர்இசை எருமை நல்நாட்டு உள்ளதை","அயிரியாறு இறந்தனர் ஆயினும், மயர்இறந்து","உள்ளுப தில்ல தாமே- பணைத்தோள்,","குரும்பை மென்முலை, அரும்பிய சுணங்கின்,","நுசுப்புஅழித்து ஒலிவரும் தாழ்இருங் கூந்தல்,","மாக விசும்பின் திலகமொடு பதித்த","திங்கள் அன்னநின் திருமுகத்து,","ஒண்சூட்டு அவிர்குழை மலைந்த நோக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman not to weep, thinking that pallor spreads each day, her body has changed, the mother frowns and the gossip is louder than the cheering at Kudal when the Pandyan drove out the Kongar, so that she will not live many days: though he has crossed the Ayiri river into the land of Erumai, chief of the Vadugar whose men raid cattle at midnight from the rock-folds, he will think, without wavering, of her shoulders, her breasts, her long hair and her moon-like face with its bright ear-rings.","n":"The friend answers the woman's three fears (her body, her mother, the town) with a catalogue of the body that the man will remember, so the very beauty she thinks is lost becomes the guarantee of his return. The cattle-raid scene is puram matter (vetchi) placed on his road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","alar","ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 253","lb":"A253","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",12,"seythi","scene","herdsmen"],["நல்ஏற்றுத்","ஏறு",13,"maa","scene","bull"],["பெருநிரை","நிரை",17,"maa","scene","cattle"],["குரும்பை","குரும்பை",22,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["king","பாண்டியன்",5],["town","கூடல்",6],["king","வடுகர் பெருமகன்",18],["king","எருமை",19],["river","அயிரியாறு",20]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:13"},"a:254":{"book":"akananuru","num":255,"t":["'நரைவிரா வுற்ற நறுமென் கூந்தற்","செம்முது செவிலியர் பலபா ராட்டப்","பொலன்செய் கிண்கிணி நலம்பெறு சேவடி","மணன்மலி முற்றத்து, நிலம்வடுக் கொளாஅ,","மனைஉறை புறவின் செங்காற் சேவல்","துணையொடு குறும்பறை பயிற்றி மேல்செல,","விளையாடு ஆயத்து இளையோர்க் காண்தொறும்","நம்வயின் நினையும் நல்நுதல் அரிவை","புலம்பொடு வதியும் கலங்கு அஞர் அகல,","வேந்துஉறு தொழிலொடு வேறுபுலத்து அல்கி,","வந்துவினை முடித்தனம் ஆயின், நீயும்,","பணைநிலை முனஇய, வினைநவில் புரவி","இழைஅணி நெடுந்தேர் ஆழி உறுப்ப,","நுண்கொடி மின்னின்; பைம்பயிர் துமியத்,","தளவ முல்லையொடு தலைஇத், தண்ணென","வெறிகமழ் கொண்ட வீததை புறவின்","நெடிஇடை பின்படக் 'கடவுமதி' என்று யான்","சொல்லிய அளவை, நீடாது, வல்லெனத்,","தார்மணி மாஅறி வுறாஅ,","ஊர்நணித் தந்தனை, உவகையாம் பெறவே!"],"p":"","r":"Back from the king's work, the man tells his charioteer that she, who thinks of him whenever she sees the children of the playing troop, praised by grey-haired foster mothers, toddling in golden anklets across the sanded yard, and the red-legged house dove flying short flights with its mate, has had her lonely grief lifted, for at his word the charioteer drove fast through the fragrant forest of thalavam and mullai, the wheels cutting the green crop, and brought them near the town so they might have joy.","n":"What reminds her of him are two pictures of the household: the children and the mated dove. புறவு names the dove in line 5 and the forest in line 16; the poem's homecoming runs from one to the other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","aalvinai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 254","lb":"A254","poet":"மதுரை அறுவை வாணிகன் இளவேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றி வந்தெய்திய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றி வந்தெய்திய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["சேவல்","சேவல்",5,"pul","iraicchi","male dove"],["புரவி","புரவி",12,"maa","scene","horse"],["பைம்பயிர்","பயிர்",14,"maram","scene",""],["தளவ","தளவம்",15,"maram","scene",""],["முல்லையொடு","முல்லை",15,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","சேவல்","confirmed","1/1","the pair that stays together in the house shows the wife what his absence takes from her, and why his return must be quick"]],"ch":"a:13"},"a:255":{"book":"akananuru","num":256,"t":["உலகுகிளர்ந் தன்ன உருகெழு வங்கம்","புலவுத்திரைப் பெருங்கடல் நீர்இடைப் போழ,","இரவும் எல்லையும் அசைவின்று ஆகி,","விரைசெலல் இயற்கை வங்கூழ் ஆட்டக்,","கோடுஉயர் திணிமணல் அகன்துறை, நீகான்","மாட ஒள்எரி மருங்கு அறிந்து ஒய்ய,","ஆள்வினைப் பிரிந்த காதலர் நாள்பல","கழியா மையே, அழிபடர் அகல,","வருவர் மன்னால்- தோழி!- தண்பணைப்","பொருபுனல் வைப்பின் நம்ஊர் ஆங்கண்","கருவிளை முரணிய தண்புதல் பகன்றைப்","பெருவளம் மலர அல்லி தீண்டிப்,","பலவுக்காய்ப் புறத்த பசும்பழப் பாகல்","கூதள மூதிலைக் கொடிநிரைத் தூங்க,","அறன்இன்று அலைக்கும் ஆனா வாடை","கடிமனை மாடத்துக் கங்குல் வீசத்,","'திருந்திழை நெகிழ்ந்து பெருங்கவின் சாய,","நிரைவளை ஊருந் தோள்' என,","உரையொடு செல்லும் அன்பினர்ப் பெறினே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the man who left for work would come before many days pass, as a great ship splitting the reeking sea, driven night and day by the wind, finds the shore when its pilot sees the bright flame on the tower, if only they had loving people to carry the word that her ornaments slip and her beauty fades, when in their town among cool fields the unjust north wind blows all night into the guarded house, the pagandrai blooms and the paagal hangs from the koothalam leaves.","n":"The ship and the harbour flame are a picture of a sure return, set as a condition (பெறினே, if only we had): the guidance exists for ships, not for her. The wind is அறன்இன்று, without justice, as if it too should keep a law.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thoothu","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 255","lb":"A255","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் ஆற்றாமை மீதூரத் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"ஆற்றாமை மீதூரத் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வங்கம்","வங்கம்",1,"pira","simile","ship"],["நீகான்","நீகான்",5,"seythi","scene","pilot"],["கருவிளை","கருவிளை",11,"maram","scene",""],["பகன்றைப்","பகன்றை",11,"maram","scene",""],["பாகல்","பாகல்",13,"maram","scene",""],["கூதள","கூதளம்",14,"maram","scene",""],["வாடை","வாடை",15,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:13"},"a:256":{"book":"akananuru","num":257,"t":["பிணங்குஅரில் வள்ளை நீடுஇலைப் பொதும்பின்","மடிதுயில் முனைஇய வள்உகிர் யாமை","கொடிவிடு கல்லிற் போகி, அகன்துறைப்","பகுவாய் நிறைய, நுங்கின் கள்ளின்","நுகர்வார் அருந்து மகிழ்புஇயங்கு நடையொடு","தீம்பெரும் பழனம் உழக்கி, அயலது","ஆம்பல் மெல்அடை ஒடுங்கும் ஊர!","பொய்யால்; அறிவென்; நின் மாயம் அதுவே","கையகப் பட்டவும் அறியாய்; நெருநை","மைஎழில் உண்கண் மடந்தையொடு வையை","ஏர்தரு புதுப்புனல் உரிதினின் நுகர்ந்து,","பரத்தை ஆயம் கரப்பவும், ஒல்லாது","கவ்வை ஆகின்றால், பெரிதே; காண்தகத்","தொல்புகழ் நிறைந்த பல்பூங் கழனிக்,","கரும்பமல் படப்பைப், பெரும்பெயர்க் கள்ளூர்த்","திருநுதற் குறுமகள் அணிநலம் வவ்விய","அறனி லாளன், 'அறியேன்' என்ற","திறன்இல் வெஞ்சூள் அறிகரி கடாஅய்","முறிஆர் பெருங்கிளை செறியப் பற்றி","நீறுதலைப் பெய்த ஞான்றை;","வீறுசால் அவையத்து ஆர்ப்பினும் பெரிதே"],"p":"","r":"The friend refuses the husband: lord of a town where the sharp-clawed tortoise, tired of sleep under the vallai leaves, crawls over the stone, fills its mouth, reels like a palm-toddy drinker, tramples the field and hides under the soft aambal leaf, do not lie, I know your trick; yesterday you enjoyed the new Vaigai flood with a kohl-eyed girl, and though the courtesans' troop hid it, the gossip is louder than the shout in the assembly at Kallur when a man who had taken a girl's beauty and sworn 'I do not know her' was proved false by witnesses, tied to a tree and had ash poured on his head.","n":"The comparison is a threat: the shout she names was raised against a man who denied an affair under oath, and the husband is now denying his. The tortoise, out of its shelter, reeling and then hiding under a leaf, is the husband's night in small.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","alar","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 256","lb":"A256","poet":"மதுரைத் தமிழ்க்கூத்தனார் கடுவன் மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகற்கு வாயின் மறுத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தோழி தலைமகற்கு வாயின் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வள்ளை","வள்ளை",1,"maram","scene",""],["யாமை","யாமை",2,"pira","ullurai","tortoise"],["நுங்கின்","நுங்கு",4,"unavu","scene","palm fruit"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",7,"maram","ullurai",""],["கரும்பமல்","கரும்பு",15,"maram","scene","sugarcane"]],"en":[["river","வையை",10],["town","கள்ளூர்",15]],"dv":[["ullurai","யாமை ஆம்பல்","confirmed","1/1","the husband's night out with the courtesan and his hiding of it"]],"ch":"a:13"},"a:257":{"book":"akananuru","num":258,"t":["வேனிற் பாதிரிக் கூனி மாமலர்","நறைவாய் வாடல் நாறும் நாள், சுரம்,","அரிஆர் சிலம்பின் சீறடி சிவப்ப,","எம்மொடு ஓர்ஆறு படீஇயர், யாழநின்","பொம்மல் ஓதி பொதுள வாரி","அரும்புஅற மலர்ந்த ஆய்பூ மராஅத்துச்","சுரும்புசூழ் அலரி தைஇ, வேய்ந்த நின்","தேம்பாய் கூந்தல் குறும்பல மொசிக்கும்","வண்டுகடிந்து ஓம்பல் தேற்றாய்; அணிகொள","நுண்கோல் எல்வளை தெளிர்க்கும் முன்கை","மெல்இறைப் பணைத்தோள் விளங்க வீசி,","வல்லுவை மன்னால் நடையே- கள்வர்","பகைமிகு கவலைச் செல்நெறி காண்மார்,","மிசைமரம் சேர்த்திய கவைமுறி யாஅத்து.","நார்அரை மருங்கின் நீர்வரப் பொளித்துக்,","களிறுசுவைத் திட்ட கோதுடைத் ததரல்","கல்லா உமணர்க்குத் தீமூட்டு ஆகும்,","துன்புறு தகுவன ஆங்கண், புன்கோட்டு","அரிலிவர் புற்றத்து அல்குஇரை நசைஇ,","வெள்அரா மிளிர வாங்கும்","பிள்ளை எண்கின் மலைவயி னானே"],"p":"","r":"As they elope, the man tells the woman that, on these summer days when the paathiri blossom wilts, he gathers her full hair, decks it with maraa flowers, and she cannot keep the bees off her honeyed hair, yet she walks well, swinging her bamboo-smooth arms with bangles ringing, on the way through the hills where robbers climb the forked yaa tree to watch the roads, where the fibre an elephant chewed for water serves the salt-merchants as kindling, and where a young bear pulls a white snake out of an anthill.","n":"Praise and danger run together: he admires her walk (வல்லுவை மன்னால் நடையே) in the very lines that list robbers, a thirsty elephant and a bear dragging a snake, so the compliment reassures her and himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 257","lb":"A257","poet":"உறையூர் மருத்துவன் தாமோதரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உடன் போகாநின்ற தலைமகட்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","உடன் போகாநின்ற தலைமகட்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பாதிரிக்","பாதிரி",1,"maram","scene",""],["மராஅத்துச்","மராஅம்",6,"maram","ornament",""],["சுரும்புசூழ்","சுரும்பு",7,"pira","scene","bee"],["வண்டுகடிந்து","வண்டு",9,"pira","scene","bee"],["கள்வர்","கள்வர்",12,"seythi","scene",""],["யாஅத்து","யா",14,"maram","scene",""],["களிறுசுவைத்","களிறு",16,"maa","scene","elephant"],["உமணர்க்குத்","உமணர்",17,"seythi","scene","salt-merchants"],["வெள்அரா","அரா",20,"pira","scene","snake"],["எண்கின்","எண்கு",21,"maa","scene","bear"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:13"},"a:258":{"book":"akananuru","num":259,"t":["நன்னன் உதியன் அருங்கடிப் பாழித்,","தொன்முதிர் வேளிர் ஓம்பினர் வைத்த","பொன்னினும் அருமைநற்கு அறிந்தும், அன்னோள்","துன்னலம் மாதோ எனினும், அஃது ஒல்லாய்-","தண்மழை தவழும் தாழ்நீர் நனந்தலைக்","கடுங்காற்று எடுக்கும் நெடும்பெருங் குன்றத்து","மாய இருள்அளை மாய்கல் போல,","மாய்கதில்- வாழிய, நெஞ்சே!- நாளும்,","மெல்இயர் குறுமகள் நல்அகம் நசைஇ,","அரவுஇயல் தேரும் அஞ்சுவரு சிறுநெறி,","இரவின் எய்தியும் பெறாஅய், அருள்வரப்","புல்லென் கண்ணை புலம்புகொண்டு, உலகத்து","உள்ளோர்க்கு எல்லாம் பெருநகை யாக;","காமம் கைம்மிக உறுதர,","ஆனா அரும்படர் தலைத்தந் தோயே!"],"p":"","r":"The man, whose night tryst failed, tells his heart that though it knew she was as hard to reach as the gold the ancient Velir kept in Nannan Uthiyan's guarded Paazhi, it would not accept it; let it perish like a stone falling into a dark cave on the windy hill, for longing for her it came every night by the narrow, snake-haunted path and did not get her, and now, its face dull, it has made itself a laughing-stock to all the world and brought on unending pain.","n":"The colophon's அல்லகுறி is the night signal that turned out false, and the poem is the man's anger at himself, not at her. Paazhi's buried gold is the measure of her guardedness, a puram treasure-story turned into a picture of a girl beyond reach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","nenju","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 258","lb":"A258","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அல்ல 1குறிப்பட்டுப் பதிப்பெயர்ந்த தலைமகன் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","குறிப்பட்டுப் பதிப்பெயர்ந்த தலைமகன் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["அரவுஇயல்","அரவு",10,"pira","scene","snake"]],"en":[["king","நன்னன் உதியன்",1],["town","பாழி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:13"},"a:259":{"book":"akananuru","num":260,"t":["வேலும் விளங்கின; இளையரும் இயன்றனர்;","தாரும் தையின; தழையும் தொடுத்தன;","நிலம்நீர் அற்ற வெம்மை நீங்கப்","பெயல்நீர் தலைஇ, உலவைஇலை நீத்துக்","குறுமுறி ஈன்றன, மரனே; நறுமலர்","வேய்ந்தன போலத் தோன்றிப், பலஉடன்","தேம்படப் பொதுளின பொழிலே; கானமும்,","நனிநன்று ஆகிய பனிநீங்கு வழிநாள்,","பால்எனப் பரத்தரும் நிலவின் மாலைப்","போதுவந் தன்று, தூதே; நீயும்","கலங்கா மனத்தை ஆகி, என்சொல்","நயந்தனை கொண்மோ- நெஞ்சுஅமர் தகுவி!","தெற்றி உலறினும், வயலை வாடினும்,","நொச்சி மென்சினை வணர்குரல் சாயினும்,","நின்னினும் மடவள் நனிநின் நயந்த","அன்னை அல்லல் தாங்கி,நின் ஐயர்","புலிமருள் செம்மல் நோக்கி,","வலியாய் இன்னும் தோய்க, நின் முலையே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman, whose elopement is agreed, that the spears gleam, the young men are ready, garlands and leaf-skirts are made, rain has ended the earth's heat and the trees put out new leaves, the groves look roofed with flowers, the forest is at its best, and the message has come on this evening of milk-white moonlight; so with a steady mind let her take her word: even if the terrace dries and the vayalai and nochchi she tended wither, bearing the grief of the mother who, more innocent than she, loved her so, and facing her brothers' tiger-like pride, let her be strong and hold him to her breast still.","n":"The friend turns the paalai land green for the journey: the season itself has been arranged in the lovers' favour. The withering vayalai and nochchi are the girl's own garden plants, the home she leaves untended. Who bears the mother's grief (அன்னை அல்லல் தாங்கி) is not stated; we read it as the girl, and the closing wish (வலியாய் இன்னும் தோய்க, நின் முலையே) is terse, so the sense is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 259","lb":"A259","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உடன்போக்கு நேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","உடன்போக்கு நேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தழையும்","தழை",2,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["மரனே","மரம்",5,"maram","scene",""],["வயலை","வயலை",13,"maram","absent",""],["நொச்சி","நொச்சி",14,"maram","absent",""],["புலிமருள்","புலி",17,"maa","simile","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:13"},"a:260":{"book":"akananuru","num":261,"t":["மண்டிலம் மழுக, மலைநிறம் கிளர,","வண்டினம் மலர்பாய்ந்து ஊத, மீமிசைக்","கண்டற் கானல் குருகினம் ஒலிப்பக்,","கரைஆடு அலவன் அளைவயின் செறியத்,","திரைபாடு அவியத், திமில் தொழில் மறப்பச்,","செக்கர் தோன்றத், துணைபுணர் அன்றில்","எக்கர்ப் பெண்ணை அகமடல் சேரக்,","கழிமலர் கமழ்முகம் கரப்பப், பொழில்மனைப்","புன்னை நறுவீ பொன்நிறம் கொளாஅ,","எல்லை பைப்பய கழிப்பி எல்உற,","யாங்குஆ குவள்கொல்? யானே நீங்காது,","முதுமரத்து உறையும், முரவுவாய் முதுபுள்","கதுமெனக் குழறும், கழுதுவழங்கு, அரைநாள்,","நெஞ்சுநெகிழ் பருவரல் செய்த","அன்பி லாளன் அறிவுநயந் தேனே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening outside, prompted by her friend, the woman says that as the sun dims, the hills glow, the bees dive into flowers, herons cry on the kandal shore, the crab slips into its hole, the waves fall quiet, the boats stop work, the sky reddens, the paired anril settles in the palmyra's inner frond, the backwater flowers close and the punnai blossom loses its gold, and the day drains away, she wonders what will become of her, she who still longs for the loveless man's mind, at midnight when the old owl hoots and ghosts walk.","n":"Ten clauses of closing (each thing withdraws to its place) end in the one who has no place to go. Spoken for the man's ears, the paired anril in its nest is the reproach the woman will not make aloud.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kuri","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 260","lb":"A260","poet":"மோசிக் கரையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"இரவுக் குறிக்கண் தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழியாற் சொல்லெடுக்கப்பட்டுத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக் குறிக்கண் தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறமாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bee"],["கண்டற்","கண்டல்",3,"maram","scene",""],["குருகினம்","குருகு",3,"pul","scene","heron"],["அலவன்","அலவன்",4,"pira","scene","crab"],["திமில்","திமில்",5,"seythi","scene","fishing boat"],["அன்றில்","அன்றில்",6,"pul","iraicchi",""],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",7,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",9,"maram","scene",""],["முதுபுள்","புள்",12,"pul","scene","owl"],["கழுது","கழுது",13,"pira","scene","ghost"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","அன்றில்","confirmed","1/1","even the birds are with their mates at dark, while the man leaves her waiting alone through the night"]],"ch":"a:13"},"a:261":{"book":"akananuru","num":262,"t":["கானப் பாதிரிக் கருந்தகட்டு ஒள்வீ","வேனில் அதிரலொடு விரைஇக், காண்வர,","சில்ஐங் கூந்தல் அழுத்தி, மெல்லிணர்த்","தேம்பாய் மராஅம் அடைச்சி, வான்கோல்","இலங்குவளை தெளிர்ப்ப வீசிச், சிலம்புநகச்","சிலமெல் ஒதுக்கமொடு மென்மெல இயலி, 'நின்","அணிமாண் சிறுபுறம் காண்கம்; சிறுநனி","ஏகு' என, ஏகல் நாணி, ஒய்யென","மாகொல் நோக்கமொடு மடம்கொளச் சாஅய்,","நின்றுதலை இறைஞ்சி யோளே; அதுகண்டு,","யாமுந் துறுதல் செல்லேம், ஆயிடை","அருஞ்சுரத்து அல்கி யேமே- இரும்புலி","களிறுஅட்டுக் குழுமும் ஓசையும், களிபட்டு","வில்லோர் குறும்பில் ததும்பும்,","வல்வாய்க் கடுந்துடிப் பாணியும் கேட்டே"],"p":"","r":"Back home, the man tells the friend how on the elopement road the girl, flowers of the forest pressed into her hair and bangles chiming, was too shy to walk ahead when he asked to see her back, and stood with bowed head, so he slowed and they camped in the wild, hearing a tiger roar over a killed elephant and the drums of drunk bowmen in their fort.","n":"The danger of the road (tiger, bowmen's drums) comes only after the moment of shyness and is told as background to it: the memory he brings back to the friend is her நாண் on the road, not the peril. His small joke, asking to see her back, turns flight into courtship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","naanam","punarchi","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 261","lb":"A261","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"புணர்ந்துடன் போயினகாலை இடைச்சுரத்துப் பட்டதனை மீண்டு வந்த காலத்துத் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu","மீண்டு வந்த காலத்துத் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பாதிரிக்","பாதிரி",1,"maram","ornament",""],["அதிரலொடு","அதிரல்",2,"maram","ornament",""],["மராஅம்","மரா",4,"maram","ornament",""],["மாகொல்","மா",9,"maa","scene","deer"],["இரும்புலி","புலி",12,"maa","scene",""],["களிறுஅட்டுக்","களிறு",13,"maa","scene",""],["வில்லோர்","வில்லோர்",14,"seythi","scene",""],["கடுந்துடிப்","துடி",15,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:262":{"book":"akananuru","num":263,"t":["முதைபடு பசுங்காட்டு அரில்பவர் மயக்கிப்,","பகடுபல பூண்ட உழவுறு செஞ்செய்,","இடுமுறை நிரம்பி, ஆகுவினைக் கலித்துப்,","பாசிலை அமன்ற பயறுஆ புக்கென,","வாய்மொழித் தந்தையைக் கண்களைந்து, அருளாது,","ஊர்முது கோசர் நவைத்த சிறுமையின்,","கலத்தும் உண்ணாள், வாலிதும் உடாஅள்,","சினத்தின் கொண்ட படிவம் மாறாள்,","மறம்கெழு தானைக் கொற்றக் குறும்பியன்,","செருஇயல் நல்மான் திதியற்கு உரைத்து, அவர்","இன்உயிர் செகுப்பக் கண்டு, சினம் மாறிய","அன்னி மிஞிலி போல, மெய்ம்மலிந்து,","ஆனா உவகையேம் ஆயினெம் - பூ மலிந்து","அருவி ஆர்க்கும் அயம்திகழ் சிலம்பின்","நுண்பல துவலை புதல்மிசை நனைக்கும்","வண்டுபடு நறவின் வண்மகிழ்ப் பேகன்","கொண்டல் மாமலை நாறி,","அம்தீம் கிளவி வந்த மாறே"],"p":"","r":"Leaving the night meeting, the man tells his heart he is full of joy because her sweet voice came to him smelling of Pekan's rain-wrapped hill, joy like that of Anni Minili, who after the Kosar blinded her father over a cow that strayed into the gram field fasted and wore no clean cloth until Thithiyan killed them, and then let her anger go.","n":"The simile is a whole revenge story: his joy is measured not by a happy thing but by the release of a vow kept through long self-denial, which hints at how long he has waited and suffered. Pekan, the patron known for giving, lends his hill's rain-smell to her speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kuri","nenju","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 262","lb":"A262","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிக்கண் தலைமகளைப் புணர்ந்து நீங்குந் தலைமகன் தன் னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறிக்கண் தலைமகளைப் புணர்ந்து நீங்குந் தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பகடுபல","பகடு",2,"maa","scene","ox"],["பயறுஆ","பயறு",4,"maram","scene",""],["பயறுஆ","ஆ",4,"maa","scene","cow"],["அருவி","அருவி",14,"pira","scene",""],["வண்டுபடு","வண்டு",16,"pira","scene",""],["நறவின்","நறவு",16,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[["king","குறும்பியன்",9],["king","திதியற்கு",10],["patron","பேகன்",16],["hill","கொண்டல் மாமலை",17]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:263":{"book":"akananuru","num":264,"t":["தயங்குதிரைப் பெருங்கடல், உலகுதொழத் தோன்றி,","வயங்குகதிர் விரிந்த, உருகெழு மண்டிலம்","கயம்கண் வறப்பப் பாஅய், நல்நிலம்","பயம்கெடத் திருகிய பைதுஅறு காலை,","வேறுபல் கவலைய வெருவரு வியன்காட்டு,","ஆறுசெல் வம்பலர் வருதிறம் காண்மார்","வில்வல் ஆடவர் மேலான் ஒற்றி,","நீடுநிலை யாஅத்துக் கோடுகொள் அருஞ்சுரம்","கொண்டனன் கழிந்த வன்கண் காளைக்கு,","அவள் துணிவு அறிந்தனென் ஆயின், அன்னோ!","ஒளிறுவேல் கோதை ஓம்பிக் காக்கும்","வஞ்சி அன்னஎன் வளநகர் விளங்க,","இனிதினிற் புணர்க்குவென் மன்னே - துனிஇன்று","திருநுதல் பொலிந்தவென் பேதை","வருமுலை முற்றத்து ஏமுறு துயிலே!"],"p":"","r":"A mother whose daughter has gone off with a bold young man across the sun-scorched waste, where bowmen watch from raised ground for travellers, says that had she known the girl's resolve she would gladly have married her to him in her rich house, splendid as Kothai's spear-guarded Vanji.","n":"The lament is not against the elopement but against having been left out of it: the mother would have given the wedding herself. The closing lines, about the untroubled sleep on the young breast, are hard to construe; they may picture the girl asleep in his arms as she would have been at home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","varaivu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 263","lb":"A263","poet":"கருவூர்க் கண்ணம்பாளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய தாய் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட் போக்கிய தாய் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["யாஅத்துக்","யா",8,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","கோதை",11],["town","வஞ்சி",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:264":{"book":"akananuru","num":265,"t":["மழையில் வானம் மீன்அணிந் தன்ன,","குழையமல் முசுண்டை வாலிய மலர,","வரிவெண் கோடல் வாங்குகுலை வான்பூப்","பெரிய துடிய கவர்கோற் கோவலர்,","எல்லுப்பெயல் உழந்த பல்லான் நிரையொடு,","நீர்திகழ் கண்ணியர், ஊர்வயின் பெயர்தர;","நனிசேண் பட்ட மாரி தளிசிறந்து,","ஏர்தரு கடுநீர் தெருவுதொறு ஒழுகப்,","பேரிசை முழக்கமொடு சிறந்துநனி மயங்கிக்,","கூதிர்நின் றன்றால்; பொழுதே! காதலா","நம்நிலை அறியார் ஆயினும், தம்நிலை","அறிந்தனர் கொல்லோ தாமே- ஓங்குநடைக்","காய்சின யானை கங்குல் சூழ,","அஞ்சுவர இறுத்த தானை","வெஞ்சின வேந்தன் பாசறை யோரே?"],"p":"","r":"As the cold season sets in, with musundai flowers white as stars, cowherds coming home in the rain with their wet garlands and the water running down every street, the woman asks whether the men in the fierce king's camp, ringed by elephants at night, even if they do not know her plight, at least know their own.","n":"The cowherds going home at the rain's appointed time are the silent contrast: everyone returns with the season except the men in the camp. Her barb (they may not know our state, but do they know theirs?) turns pity into reproach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaalam","mazhai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 264","lb":"A264","poet":"உம்பற்காட்டு இளங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பருவங் கண்டு வன்புறை யெதிரழிந்து தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது; தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியதுமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பருவங் கண்டு வன்புறை யெதிரழிந்து தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியதுமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முசுண்டை","முசுண்டை",2,"maram","simile",""],["கோடல்","கோடல்",3,"maram","scene",""],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["துடிய","துடி",4,"parai","scene",""],["பல்லான்","ஆன்",5,"maa","scene","cattle"],["யானை","யானை",13,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:265":{"book":"akananuru","num":266,"t":["புகையின் பொங்கி, வியல்விசும்பு உகந்து,","பனிஊர் அழற்கொடி கடுப்பத் தோன்றும்","இமயச் செவ்வரை மானும் கொல்லோ?","பல்புகழ் நிறைந்த வெல்போர் நந்தர்","சீர்மிகு பாடலிக் குழீஇக், கங்கை","நீர்முதற் கரந்த நிதியம் கொல்லோ?","எவன்கொல்? வாழி, தோழி! வயங்கொளி","நிழற்பால் அறலின் நெறித்த கூந்தல்,","குழற்குரல், பாவை இரங்க, நத்துறந்து,","ஒண்தொடி நெகிழச் சாஅய்ச், செல்லலொடு","கண்பனி கலுழ்ந்துயாம் ஒழியப், பொறை அடைந்து,","இன்சிலை எழிலேறு கெண்டிப், புரைய","நிணம்பொதி விழுத்தடி நெருப்பின் வைத்துஎடுத்து,","அணங்கரு மரபின் பேஎய் போல","விளரூன் தின்ற வேட்கை நீங்கத்,","துகளற விளைந்த தோப்பி பருகித்,","குலாஅ வல்வில் கொடுநோக்கு ஆடவர்","புலாஅல் கையர், பூசா வாயர்,","ஒராஅ உருட்டுங் குடுமிக் குராலொடு","மராஅஞ் சீறூர் மருங்கில் தூங்கும்","செந்நுதல் யானை வேங்கடம் தழீஇ,","வெம்முனை அருஞ்சுரம் இறந்தோர்","நம்மினும் வலிதாத் தூக்கிய பொருளே!"],"p":"","r":"Her bangles slipping and her eyes wet, the woman asks her friend what the wealth can be that her man rated above her, crossing the Venkatam wastes where bowmen roast flesh and drink beer: is it as great as the red Himalayan peak, or the treasure the Nandas hid under the Ganges at Pataliputra?","n":"The two guesses are the largest measures of value a Tamil poet could name, one natural and one royal and northern; set against them, her own worth is the unspoken third term. The bowmen eating 'like demons' make the road the price of that wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 265","lb":"A265","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் ஆற்றாமை மீதூரத் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் ஆற்றாமை மீதூரத் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["எழிலேறு","ஏறு",12,"maa","gift_food","bull"],["பேஎய்","பேய்",14,"pira","simile",""],["தோப்பி","தோப்பி",16,"unavu","gift_food",""],["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",17,"seythi","scene",""],["குராலொடு","குரால்",19,"pul","scene","owl"],["மராஅஞ்","மரா",20,"maram","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",21,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["hill","இமயச்",3],["king","நந்தர்",4],["town","பாடலிக்",5],["river","கங்கை",5],["hill","வேங்கடம்",21]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:266":{"book":"akananuru","num":267,"t":["கோடுற நிவந்த நீடுஇரும் பரப்பின்","அந்திப் பராஅய புதுப்புனல், நெருநை,","மைந்துமலி களிற்றின் தலைப்புணை தழீஇ,","நரந்தம் நாறும் குவைஇருங் கூந்தல்","இளந்துணை மகளிரொடு ஈர்அணிக் கலைஇ,","நீர்பெயர்ந்து ஆடிய ஏந்துஎழில் மழைக்கண்","நோக்குதொறும் நோக்குதொறும் தவிர்விலை யாகிக்,","காமம் கைம்மிகச் சிறத்தலின், நாண்இழந்து,","ஆடினை என்ப மகிழ்ந! அதுவே","யாழ்இசை மறுகின் நீடூர் கிழவோன்","வாய்வாள் எவ்வி ஏவன் மேவார்","நெடுமிடல் சாய்த்த பசும்பூண் பொருந்தலர்","அரிமண வாயில் உறத்தூர் ஆங்கண்,","கள்ளுடைப் பெருஞ்சோற்று எல்இமிழ் அன்ன,","கவ்வை ஆகின்றால் பெரிதே; இனிஅஃது","அவலம் அன்றுமன், எமக்கே; அயல","கழனி உழவர் கலிசிறந்து எடுத்த","கறங்குஇசை வெரீஇப் பறந்த தோகை","அணங்குடை வரைப்பகம் பொலியவந்து இறுக்கும்","திருமணி விளக்கின் அலைவாய்ச்","செருமிகு சேஎயொடு உற்ற சூளே!"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her husband that his bathing yesterday in the evening flood with young women, clinging to a float and losing all shame as he gazed at one woman's eyes, is now gossip as loud as the feast at Uraththur after Evvi's men won; that does not grieve her now, but the oath he swore to her by Murugan of Alaivay does.","n":"The point is in the last turn: public scandal she can bear, a broken oath sworn before the god she cannot. The image of peacocks scared by ploughmen's shouts flying in to the temple precinct carries the oath's setting from field to shrine. Lines 10 to 13, Evvi's victory at Uraththur, are syntactically hard; the sense of the simile, a famous loud feast, is clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","alar","parathai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 266","lb":"A266","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தையிற் பிரிந்துவந்து கூடிய தலைமகற்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","பரத்தையிற் பிரிந்துவந்து கூடிய தலைமகற்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["களிற்றின்","களிறு",3,"maa","scene","elephant"],["நரந்தம்","நரந்தம்",4,"maram","ornament",""],["கள்ளுடைப்","கள்",14,"unavu","gift_food",""],["உழவர்","உழவர்",17,"seythi","scene",""],["தோகை","தோகை",18,"pul","scene","peacock"],["சேஎயொடு","சேய்",21,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[["town","நீடூர்",10],["king","எவ்வி",11],["town","உறத்தூர்",13],["town","அலைவாய்ச்",20]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:267":{"book":"akananuru","num":268,"t":["'நெஞ்சு நெகிழ்தகுந கூறி, அன்புகலந்து,","அறாஅ வஞ்சினம் செய்தோர், வினைபுரிந்து","திறம்வேறு ஆகல் எற்று?' என்று ஒற்றி,","இனைதல் ஆன்றிசின், நீயே; சினைபாய்ந்து.","உதிர்த்த கோடை, உட்குவரு கடத்திடை,","வெருக்குஅடி அன்ன குவிமுகிழ் இருப்பை,","மருப்புக் கடைந்தன்ன, கொள்ளை வான்பூ","மயிர்க்கால் எண்கின் ஈர்இனம் கவர,","மைபட் டன்ன மாமுக முசுவினம்","பைதுஅறு நெடுங்கழை பாய்தலின் ஒய்யென","வெதிர்படு வெண்ணெல் வெவ்அறைத் தாஅய்,","உகிர்நெறி ஓசையிற் பொங்குவன பொரியும்","ஓங்கல் வெற்பின் சுரம்பல இறந்தோர்","தாம்பழி உடையர் அல்லர்; நாளும்","நயந்தோர்ப் பிணித்தல் தேற்றா, வயங்குவினை","வால்ஏர் எல்வளை நெகிழ்த்த,","தோளே!- தோழி - தவறுஉடை யவ்வே!"],"p":"","r":"When her friend grieves that the man who swore unbreakable oaths has changed for the sake of work, the wife answers that the men who crossed the summer hills, where bears raid iruppai blossoms and monkeys leaping on bamboo scatter its rice to pop on hot rock, are not to blame; her own shoulders are, for letting the bangles slip and not knowing how to hold him.","n":"She shifts blame from the absent man to her own body, a loyal defence that is also an accusation of the friend who called him faithless. The hill scene is crowded with creatures taking and scattering food, abundance in a dry season.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","karpu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 267","lb":"A267","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளது ஆற்றாமை கண்டு ஆற்றாளாகிய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","ஆற்றாளாகிய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வெருக்குஅடி","வெருகு",6,"maa","simile","wild cat"],["இருப்பை","இருப்பை",6,"maram","simile",""],["எண்கின்","எண்கு",8,"maa","scene","bear"],["முசுவினம்","முசு",9,"maa","simile",""],["நெடுங்கழை","கழை",10,"maram","scene",""],["வெண்ணெல்","வெதிர் நெல்",11,"unavu","scene","bamboo rice"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:268":{"book":"akananuru","num":269,"t":["அறியாய்- வாழி, தோழி!- பொறியரிப்","பூநுதல் யானையொடு புலிபொரக் குழைந்த","குருதிச் செங்களம் புலவுஅற, வேங்கை","உருகெழு நாற்றம் குளவியொடு விலங்கும்","மாமலை நாடனொடு மறுஇன்று ஆகிய","காமம் கலந்த காதல் உண்டெனின்,","நன்றுமன்; அதுநீ நாடாய், கூறுதி;","நாணும் நட்பும் இல்லோர்த் தேரின்,","யான் அலது இல்லை, இவ் உலகத் தானே-","இன்னுயிர் அன்ன நின்னொடுஞ் சூழாது,","முளைஅணி மூங்கிலின் கிளையொடு பொலிந்த","பெரும்பெயர் எந்தை அருங்கடி நீவிச்,","செய்துபின் இரங்கா வினையொடு","மெய்அல் பெரும்பழி எய்தினென் யானே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend, having taken up the man's suit, tells the girl that if she truly loves the lord of the hill where vengai and wild jasmine sweeten a field bloodied by an elephant and tiger, she should say so; for she herself, without consulting the girl dear as life, has already slipped past their father's strict guard and taken on a great blame that is not deserved.","n":"The friend's confession puts the girl in her debt: she has risked the shame already, so the girl can only say yes. The hill whose flower-scent drives off the blood-smell may hint that love will cover the scandal; that reading is traditional and not forced by the poem.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","natpu","pazhi","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 268","lb":"A268","poet":"வடமவண்ணக்கன் பேரிசாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"குறைவேண்டிப் பின்னின்ற தலைமகனுக்குக் குறைநேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குக் குறைநயப்பக் கூறியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","குறைநேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குக் குறைநயப்பக் கூறியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானையொடு","யானை",2,"maa","scene",""],["புலிபொரக்","புலி",2,"maa","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",3,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["குளவியொடு","குளவி",4,"maram","scene",""],["மூங்கிலின்","மூங்கில்",11,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:14"},"a:269":{"book":"akananuru","num":270,"t":["தொடிதோள் இவர்க! எவ்வமுந் தீர்க!","நெறிஇருங் கதுப்பின் கோதையும் புனைக!","ஏறுடை இனநிரை பெயரப்; பெயராது","செறிசுரை வெள்வேல் மழவர்த் தாங்கிய","தறுக ணாளர் நல்லிசை நிறுமார்,","பிடிமடிந் தன்ன குறும்பொறை மருங்கின்,","நட்ட போலும் நடாஅ நெடுங்கல்","அகலிடம் குயின்ற பல்பெயர் மண்ணி,","நறுவிரை மஞ்சள் ஈர்ம்புறம் பொலிய","அம்புகொண்டு அறுத்த ஆர்நார் உரிவையின்","செம்பூங் கரந்தை புனைந்த கண்ணி","வரிவண்டு ஆர்ப்பச் சூட்டிக் கழற்கால்","இளையர்பதிப் பெயரும் அருஞ்சுரம் இறந்தோர்,","தைஇ நின்ற தண்பெயல் கடைநாள்,","பொலங்காசு நிரைத்த கோடுஏந்து அல்குல்","நலம்கேழ் மாக்குரல் குழையொடு துயல்வரப்,","பாடுஊர்பு எழுதரும் பகுவாய் மண்டிலத்து","வயிர்இடைப் பட்ட தெள்விளி இயம்ப","வண்டற் பாவை உண்துறை தரீஇத்,","திருநுதல் மகளிர் குரவை அயரும்","பெருநீர்க் கானல் தழீஇய இருக்கை,","வாணன் சிறுகுடி, வணங்குகதிர் நெல்லின்","யாணர்த் தண்பணைப் போதுவாய் அவிழ்ந்த","ஒண்செங் கழுநீர் அன்ன நின்","கண்பனி துடைமார் வந்தனர் விரைந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the wife to let her bangles climb back and her hair be dressed, for the man who crossed the road of hero-stones, set where warriors who held off the Mazhavar's spears fell and now washed, smeared with turmeric and crowned with red karanthai, has hurried home at the end of Thai's cool rains to wipe her tears, eyes like the red water-lilies of Vaanan's Sirukudi.","n":"The hero-stones carry no hidden sense of the lovers (the brief's model of a failed iraicchi); they mark the road's danger and its honour. The return is timed by a seaside festival, women bringing sand dolls to the ghat and dancing the kuravai to horn and drum, so comfort arrives with a feast.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nadukal","pugazh","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 269","lb":"A269","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஏறுடை","ஏறு",3,"maa","scene","bull"],["மழவர்த்","மழவர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["பிடிமடிந் தன்ன","பிடி",6,"maa","simile",""],["மஞ்சள்","மஞ்சள்",9,"maram","ornament",""],["கரந்தை","கரந்தை",11,"maram","ornament",""],["வரிவண்டு","வண்டு",12,"pira","scene",""],["இளையர்பதிப்","இளையர்",13,"seythi","scene",""],["மண்டிலத்து","மண்டிலம்",17,"parai","scene","drum"],["வயிர்இடைப்","வயிர்",18,"yaazh","scene","horn"],["குரவை","குரவை",20,"yaazh","scene",""],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",22,"unavu","scene",""],["கழுநீர்","கழுநீர்",24,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["herostone","நெடுங்கல்",7],["patron","வாணன்",22],["town","சிறுகுடி",22]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:270":{"book":"akananuru","num":271,"t":["இருங்கழி மலர்ந்த வள்ளிதழ் நீலம்,","புலாஅல் மறுகின் சிறுகுடிப் பாக்கத்து","இனமீன் வேட்டுவர், ஞாழலொடு மிலையும்","மெல்லம் புலம்ப! நெகிழ்ந்தன, தோளே;","சேயிறாத் துழந்த நுரைபிதிர்ப் படுதிரை","பராஅரைப் புன்னை வாங்குசினைத் தோயும்","கானல் பெருந்துறை நோக்கி, இவளே,","கொய்சுவற் புரவிக் கைவண் கோமான்","நல்தேர்க் குட்டுவன் கழுமலத்து அன்ன,","அம்மா மேனி தொல்நலம் தொலைய,","துஞ்சாக் கண்ணள் அலமரும், நீயே,","கடவுள் மரத்த முள்மிடை குடம்பைச்","சேவலொடு புணராச் சிறுகரும் பேடை","இன்னாது உயங்கும் கங்குலும்,","நும்ஊர் உள்ளுவை; நோகோ, யானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore, where fishermen wear backwater lilies and njazhal, that the girl's shoulders have thinned and her beauty, once like Kuttuvan's Kazhumalam, is wasting as she gazes sleepless at the bay, while he, even on nights when the little black hen-bird in the thorny nest of the sacred tree suffers apart from her mate, thinks only of his own town.","n":"The demand for marriage is made without the word: the friend sets the girl's nights beside his, and lets the unpaired hen-bird make the case. நோகோ யானே, 'shall I grieve, then?', closes on the friend's own helplessness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kuri","melivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 270","lb":"A270","poet":"சாகலாசனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறிக்கண் வந்துநீங்குந் தலைமகனைத் தோழி வரைவுகடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகற்குறிக்கண் வந்துநீங்குந் தலைமகனைத் தோழி வரைவுகடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நீலம்","நீலம்",1,"maram","ornament","blue lily"],["வேட்டுவர்","மீன் வேட்டுவர்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["ஞாழலொடு","ஞாழல்",3,"maram","ornament",""],["சேயிறாத்","இறா",5,"pira","scene","prawn"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",6,"maram","scene",""],["புரவிக்","புரவி",8,"maa","scene","horse"],["கடவுள் மரத்த","கடவுள் மரம்",12,"maram","scene",""],["சேவலொடு","சேவல்",13,"pul","absent",""],["பேடை","பேடை",13,"pul","iraicchi",""]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவன்",9],["town","கழுமலத்து",9]],"dv":[["iraicchi","பேடை","confirmed","2/2","the girl spends the same nights apart from him, so he should marry and not leave her mateless"]],"ch":"a:14"},"a:271":{"book":"akananuru","num":272,"t":["பொறிவரிப் புறவின் செங்காற் சேவல்","சிறுபுன் பெடையொடு சேண்புலம் போகி,","அரிமணல் இயவில் பரல்தேர்ந்து உண்டு,","வரிமரல் வாடிய வறன்நீங்கு நனந்தலைக்","குறும்பொறை மருங்கின் கோட்சுரம் நீந்தி,","நெடுஞ்சேண் வந்த நீர்நசை வம்பலர்","செல்லுயிர் நிறுத்த சுவைக்காய் நெல்லிப்","பல்காய் அஞ்சினை அகவும் அத்தம்","சென்று, நீர் அவணிர் ஆகி, நின்றுதரும்","நிலைஅரும் பொருட்பிணி நினைந்தனிர் எனினே,","வல்வதாக, நும் செய்வினை! இவட்கே,","களிமலி கள்ளின் நல்தேர் அவியன்","ஆடியல் இளமழை சூடித் தோன்றும்","பழம்தூங்கு விடரகத்து எழுந்த காம்பின்","கண்ணிடை புரையும் நெடுமென் பணைத்தோள்,","திருந்துகோல் ஆய்தொடி ஞெகிழின்,","மருந்தும் உண்டோ , பிரிந்துறை நாட்டே?"],"p":"","r":"Knowing the girl's mind, the friend tells the man who has announced his journey that if he means to cross the waste where doves peck grit with their mates and the nelli's tart fruit holds back the departing lives of thirsty travellers, may his work prosper; but if the bangles slip from her shoulders, smooth as the bamboo of Aviyan's cloud-capped hill, is there a medicine for her in the land where he will stay?","n":"The road has a remedy for travellers (the nelli that restores them) and the dove that travels with its mate; she will have neither. The blessing on his work is ironic, the real sentence is the closing question.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","marundhu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 271","lb":"A271","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம்பட்டினத்துச் செங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"1செலவுணர்த்திய தோழி தலைமகளது குறிப்பறிந்து தலைமகனைச் செலவழுங்கச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தலைமகனைச் செலவழுங்கச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["சேவல்","சேவல்",1,"pul","iraicchi",""],["பெடையொடு","பெடை",2,"pul","iraicchi",""],["மரல்","மரல்",4,"maram","absent",""],["நெல்லிப்","நெல்லி",7,"maram","scene",""],["கள்ளின்","கள்",12,"unavu","gift_food",""],["காம்பின்","காம்பு",14,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[["patron","அவியன்",12]],"dv":[["iraicchi","சேவல்","confirmed","1/1","even the dove takes its mate along on a far journey, while he means to go alone and leave her"]],"ch":"a:14"},"a:272":{"book":"akananuru","num":273,"t":["இரும்புலி தொலைத்த பெருங்கை வேழத்துப்","புலவுநாறு புகர்நுதல் கழுவக், கங்குல்","அருவி தந்த அணங்குடை நெடுங்கோட்டு","அஞ்சுவரு விடர்முகை ஆர்இருள் அகற்றிய,","மின்ஒளிர் எஃகம் செல்நெறி விளக்கத்,","தனியன் வந்து, பனிஅலை முனியான்","நீர்இழி மருங்கின் ஆர்இடத்து அமன்ற","குளவியொடு மிடைந்த கூதளங் கண்ணி","அசையா நாற்றம் அசைவளி பகரத்","துறுகல் நண்ணிய கறிஇவர் படப்பைக்","குறிஇறைக் குரம்பைநம் மனைவயின் புகுதரும்,","மெய்ம்மலி உவகையன்; அந்நிலை கண்டு,","'முருகு' என உணர்ந்து, முகமன் கூறி,","உருவச் செந்தினை நீரொடு தூஉய்,","நெடுவேள் பரவும், அன்னை, அன்னோ!","என்ஆ வதுகொல் தானே - பொன்னென","மலர்ந்த வேங்கை அலங்குசினை பொலிய","மணிநிற மஞ்ஜை அகவும்","அணிமலை நாடனொடு அமைந்தநம் தொடர்பே?"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening outside, the friend tells the girl how he comes alone through the dark cleft of the hill, his spear lighting the path, wearing a garland of koothalam and wild jasmine whose scent the wind carries into their hut, and how the mother, seeing him so, took him for Murugu, greeted him and scattered red millet in worship of Nedu Vel; what will become of their bond with the lord of the hill?","n":"The friend's comedy has a point: the mother's piety is the measure of how near discovery is, and the man who hears it is told that night visits cannot go on. Who is மெய்ம்மலி உவகையன் and what the mother actually sees are not certain; the reading here takes the comer as the man, mistaken for the god he resembles by spear and garland.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal","anangu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 272","lb":"A272","poet":"மதுரை அறுவை வாணிகன் இளவேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி வந்த தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறி வந்த தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["இரும்புலி","புலி",1,"maa","scene",""],["வேழத்துப்","வேழம்",1,"maa","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",3,"pira","scene",""],["குளவியொடு","குளவி",8,"maram","ornament",""],["கூதளங்","கூதளம்",8,"maram","ornament",""],["கறிஇவர்","கறி",10,"maram","scene","pepper vine"],["முருகு","முருகு",13,"deyvam","scene",""],["செந்தினை","தினை",14,"unavu","gift_food",""],["நெடுவேள்","நெடுவேள்",15,"deyvam","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",17,"maram","simile","kino tree"],["மஞ்ஜை","மஞ்ஞை",18,"pul","scene","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:273":{"book":"akananuru","num":274,"t":["விசும்பு விசைத்துஎறிந்த கூதளங் கோதையிற்,","பசுங்கால் வெண்குருகு வாப்பறை வளைஇ,","ஆர்கலி வளவயின் போதொடு பரப்பப்,","புலம்புனிறு தீர்ந்த புதுவரல் அற்சிரம்,","நலம்கவர் பசலை நலியவும், நந்துயர்","அறியார் கொல்லோ தாமே? அறியினும்,","நம்மனத்து அன்ன மென்மை இன்மையின்,","நம்முடை உலகம் உள்ளார் கொல்லோ?","யாங்கென உணர்கோ, யானே?- வீங்குபு","தலைவரம்பு அறியாத் தகைவரல் வாடையொடு","முலையிடைத் தோன்றிய நோய்வளர் இளமுளை","அசைவுடை நெஞ்சத்து உயவுத்திரள் நீடி,","ஊரோர் எடுத்த அம்பல் அம்சினை,","ஆராக் காதல் அவிர்தளிர் பரப்பிப்","புலவர் புகழ்ந்த நாணில் பெருமரம்","நிலவரை எல்லாம் நிழற்றி,","அலர்அரும்பு ஊழ்ப்பவும், வாரா தோரே"],"p":"","r":"As the new cold season comes, with white herons wheeling like a garland flung across the sky, the woman, wasting with pallor, asks whether he does not know her pain or, knowing it, lacks a heart soft as hers; and she pictures her love-sickness as a great shameless tree, sprouting between her breasts, swelling with the north wind, branching with the town's gossip and budding with scandal over the whole earth, while he does not come.","n":"The tree is built part by part: sprout, trunk, branches of அம்பல், shoots of love, buds of அலர். 'Praised by poets' and 'shameless' make it a public thing, the opposite of a secret love. The poem is about waiting through a season, which is why the inferred thinai is mullai against the colophon's paalai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 273","lb":"A273","poet":"ஒளவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகள் அறிவுமயங்கிச் சொல்லியது.","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","nenju",null,"karpu","பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகள் அறிவுமயங்கிச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கூதளங்","கூதளம்",1,"maram","simile",""],["வெண்குருகு","குருகு",2,"pul","simile","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:274":{"book":"akananuru","num":275,"t":["இருவிசும்பு அதிர முழங்கி, அரநலிந்து,","இகுபெயல் அழிதுளி தலைஇ, வானம்","பருவஞ் செய்த பானாட் கங்குல்,","ஆடுதலைத் துருவின் தோடு ஏமார்ப்ப,","கடைகோல் சிறுதீ அடைய மாட்டித்,","திண்கால் உறியன், பானையன், அதளன்,","நுண்பல் துவலை ஒருதிறம் நனைப்பத்,","தண்டுகால் ஊன்றிய தனிநிலை இடையன்,","மடிவிடு வீளை, கடிதுசென்று இசைப்பத்,","தெறிமறி பார்க்கும் குறுநரி வெரீஇ","முள்ளுடைக் குறுந்தூறு இரியப் போகும்","தண்ணறும் புறவி னதுவே - நறுமலர்","முல்லை சான்ற கற்பின்","மெல்லியற் குறுமகள் உறைவின் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"Returning, the man tells his charioteer that her town lies in the cool fragrant scrub where, on a midnight of the rainy season, a lone herdsman stands on one leg propped on his staff with his pot, sling and hide, a small fire lit with a drill, the drizzle wetting one side, and his whistle sends the jackal watching his leaping lambs scurrying into the thorn bushes.","n":"The poem is almost all scene; the woman appears only in the last two lines, as one whose chastity is worthy of the mullai flower. The herdsman keeping lone watch over his flock through the rainy night can be felt as a picture of her guarded steadfastness, but he does nothing of the lovers' situation, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","ninaivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 274","lb":"A274","poet":"இடைக்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["துருவின்","துரு",4,"maa","scene","sheep"],["இடையன்","இடையன்",8,"seythi","scene",""],["மறி","மறி",10,"maa","scene","lamb"],["குறுநரி","நரி",10,"maa","scene",""],["குறுந்தூறு","தூறு",11,"maram","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",13,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:275":{"book":"akananuru","num":276,"t":["ஓங்குநிலைத் தாழி மல்கச் சார்த்திக்,","குடைஅடை நீரின் மடையினள் எடுத்த","பந்தர் வயலைப், பந்து எறிந்து ஆடி,","இளமைத் தகைமையை வளமனைக் கிழத்தி!","'பிதிர்வை நீரை வெண்நீறு ஆக;' என,","யாம்தற் கழறுங் காலைத், தான்தன்","மழலை இன்சொல், கழறல் இன்றி,","இன்உயிர் கலப்பக் கூறி, நன்னுதல்","பெருஞ்சோற்று இல்லத்து ஒருங்குஇவண் இராஅள்,","ஏதி லாளன் காதல் நம்பித்,","திரளரை இருப்பைத் தொள்ளை வான்பூக்","குருளை எண்கின் இருங்கிளை கவரும்","வெம்மலை அருஞ்சுரம், நம்இவண் ஒழிய,","இருநிலன் உயிர்க்கும் இன்னாக் கானம்,","நெருநைப் போகிய பெருமடத் தகுவி","ஐதுஅகல் அல்குல் தழையணிக் கூட்டும்","கூழை நொச்சிக் கீழது, என்மகள்","செம்புடைச் சிறுவிரல் வரித்த","வண்டலும் காண்டீரோ, கண்உடை யீரே?"],"p":"","r":"The mother whose daughter left yesterday with a stranger, trusting his love, across the hot hills where bear cubs and their clan raid the iruppai's white flowers, remembers the girl watering the vayalai on the pandal and playing ball, answering scolding with lisping sweet words, and asks those who have eyes to look at the sand house her small red fingers traced under the nochi.","n":"The last image is the whole grief: a child's sand house still standing after the child has gone into the waste. Lines 4 and 5 (the address வளமனைக் கிழத்தி and the scolding பிதிர்வை நீரை வெண்நீறு ஆக) are obscure, so who is addressed and what she was scolded for cannot be fixed. The bear cubs feeding with their kin are scene, though a reader may feel the contrast with the girl who left hers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal","ilamai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 275","lb":"A275","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய 1தாய் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","kandor",null,"kalavu","மகட் போக்கிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வயலைப்","வயலை",3,"maram","scene",""],["இருப்பைத்","இருப்பை",11,"maram","scene",""],["எண்கின்","எண்கு",12,"maa","scene","bear"],["தழையணிக்","தழை",16,"maram","ornament",""],["நொச்சிக்","நொச்சி",17,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:276":{"book":"akananuru","num":277,"t":["நீளிரும் பொய்கை இரைவேட்டு எழுந்த","வாளை வெண்போத்து உணீஇய, நாரைதன்","அடிஅறி வுறுதல் அஞ்சிப், பைப்பயக்","கடிஇலம் புகூஉம் கள்வன் போலச்,","சாஅய் ஒதுங்குந் துறைகேழ் ஊரனொடு","ஆவதுஆக! இனிநாண் உண்டோ ?","வருகதில் அம்ம, எம் சேரி சேர!","அரிவேய் உண்கண் அவன்பெண்டிர் காணத்,","தாரும் தானையும் பற்றி, ஆரியர்","பிடிபயின்று தரூஉம் பெருங்களிறு போலத்","தோள்கந் தாகக் கூந்தலின் பிணித்து, அவன்","மார்புகடி கொள்ளேன் ஆயின், ஆர்வுற்று","இரந்தோர்க்கு ஈயாது ஈட்டியோன் பொருள்போல்,","பரந்து வெளிப்படாது ஆகி,","வருந்துக தில்ல, யாய் ஓம்பிய நலனே!"],"p":"","r":"The courtesan, so that the wife's women will hear, says that the lord of the ford, who slips aside like a thief creeping into a guarded house, as the stork stalks the white vaalai fish softly so its steps are not known, may come to her street; before his wives' eyes she will seize his garland and cloth and bind him with her hair like the bull elephant the Aryans lure with a she-elephant, or else let her mother-guarded beauty go to waste like the wealth of a man who hoarded and gave nothing to those who asked.","n":"Her oath ends on the image of the miser's wealth: beauty not spent is wasted as wealth not given is wasted. She speaks as a challenger, and the furtive stork makes the husband a thief before the wives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 276","lb":"A276","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைமகட்ருப் பாங்காயினார் கேட்பப் பரத்தை சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","pira","thozhi","karpu","பாங்காயினார் கேட்பப் பரத்தை சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வாளை","வாளை",2,"pira","ullurai","fish"],["நாரைதன்","நாரை",2,"pul","ullurai","stork"],["பிடிபயின்று","பிடி",10,"maa","simile",""],["பெருங்களிறு","களிறு",10,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","நாரைதன் வாளை","confirmed","2/2","the husband stealing away to the courtesan while hiding it from his wives"]],"ch":"a:14"},"a:277":{"book":"akananuru","num":278,"t":["தண்கதிர் மண்டிலம் அவிர்அறச் சாஅய்ப்","பகலழி தோற்றம் போலப், பையென","நுதல்ஒளி கரப்பவும், ஆள்வினை தருமார்,","தவலில் உள்ளமொடு எஃகுதுணை ஆகக்,","கடையல குரலம் வாள்வரி உழுவை","பேழ்வாய்ப் பிணவின் விழுப்பசி நோனாது","இரும்பனஞ் செறும்பின் அன்ன பரூஉமயிர்ச்,","சிறுகண், பன்றி வருதிறம் பார்க்கும்","அத்தம்ஆர் அழுவத்து ஆங்கண் நனந்தலை,","பொத்துடை மரத்த புகர்படு நீழல்,","ஆறுசெல் வம்பலர் அசையுநர் இருக்கும்,","ஈரம்இல், வெஞ்சுரம் இறந்தோர் நம்வயின்","வாராஅளவை- ஆயிழை!- கூர்வாய்","அழல்அகைந் தன்ன காமர் துதைமயிர்","மனைஉறை கோழி மறனுடைச் சேவல்","போர்எரி எருத்தம் போலக் கஞலிய","பொங்கழல் முருக்கின் எண்குரல் மாந்தி,","சிதர்சிதர்ந்து உகுத்த செவ்வி வேனில்","வந்தன்று அம்ம, தானே;","வாரார் தோழி! நம் காத லோரே"],"p":"","r":"Her forehead's glow fading like the moon by day, the woman tells her friend that before the man returns from the dry road, where a tiger that cannot bear its hungry mate's hunger watches for a bristly boar and travellers rest in the spotted shade of hollow trees, the spring has come, the murukku in flame-red bloom like the fighting cock's neck, its blossoms shaken down by bees; he has not come.","n":"The season is announced with violent colour, a domestic cock's fighting hackles for the red flowers. The tiger hunting for its mate makes the road itself an argument for why he went and why he should return.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaalam","pasalai","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 277","lb":"A277","poet":"கருவூர் நன்மார்பனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குப் பருவங்கண் டழிந்து சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைமகள் தோழிக்குப் பருவங்கண் டழிந்து சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["உழுவை","உழுவை",5,"maa","iraicchi","tiger"],["பிணவின்","பிணவு",6,"maa","iraicchi","tigress"],["இரும்பனஞ்","பனை",7,"maram","simile",""],["பன்றி","பன்றி",8,"maa","scene",""],["கோழி","கோழி",15,"pul","simile",""],["முருக்கின்","முருக்கு",17,"maram","simile",""],["சிதர்சிதர்ந்து","சிதர்",18,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","உழுவை","confirmed","1/1","a male who goes out for his mate's need, as he did, should bring what he hunts home to her, yet the season has come and he has not"]],"ch":"a:14"},"a:278":{"book":"akananuru","num":279,"t":["குணகடல் முகந்த கொள்ளை வானம்","பணைகெழு வேந்தர் பல்படைத் தானைத்","தோல்நிரைத் தனைய ஆகி, வலன்ஏர்பு","கோல்நிமிர் கொடியின் வசிபட மின்னி,","உரும்உரறு அதிர்குரல் தலைஇப், பானாள்,","பெருமலை மீமிசை முற்றின ஆயின்,","வாள்இலங்கு அருவி தாஅய், நாளை","இருவெதிர் அம்கழை ஒசியத் தீண்டி","வருவது மாதோ, வண்பரி உந்தி,","நனிபெரும் பரப்பின் நம்ஊர் முன்துறைப்,","பனிபொரு மழைக்கண் சிவந்த, பானாள்","முனிபடர் அகல மூழ்குவம் கொல்லோ!","மணிமருள் மேனி ஆய்நலம் தொலைய,","தணிவுஅருந் துயரம் செய்தோன்","அணிகிளர் நெடுவரை ஆடிய நீரே?"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening outside, the friend tells the girl that the clouds drawn from the eastern sea have massed over the mountain at midnight like the shields of kings' armies, with lightning and thunder; tomorrow the falls will come down bending the bamboo to the ford of their town, and they will bathe in that water, which played on the mountain of the man who wasted her beauty, to ease the pain of their sleepless nights.","n":"The storm warns the listening man that the night path is dangerous and the waiting long; the bath in water from his own mountain is the only contact with him left to them, which presses him toward marriage without naming it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","mazhai","melivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 278","lb":"A278","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிக்கண் தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறிக்கண் தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",7,"pira","scene",""],["இருவெதிர்","வெதிர்",8,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:279":{"book":"akananuru","num":280,"t":["'நட்டோர் இன்மையும், கேளிர் துன்பமும்,","ஒட்டாது உறையுநர் பெருக்கமும், காணூஉ","ஒருபதி வாழ்தல் ஆற்றுப தில்ல","பொன்னவிர் சுணங்கொடு செறிய வீங்கிய","மென்முலை முற்றம் கடவா தோர்' என,","நள்ளென் கங்குலும் பகலும் இயைந்து இயைந்து","உள்ளம் பொத்திய உரம்சுடு கூர்எரி","ஆள்வினை மாரியின் அவியா, நாளும்","கடறுஉழந்து இவணம் ஆகப், படர்உழந்து","யாங்குஆ குவள்கொல் தானே - தீம்தொடை","விளரி நரம்பின் நயவரு சீறுயாழ்","மலிபூம் பொங்கர் மகிழ்குரற் குயிலொடு","புணர்துயில் எடுப்பும் புனல்தெளி காலையும்,","நம்முடை மதுகையள் ஆகி, அணிநடை","அன்னமாண் பெடையின் மென்மெல இயலிக்,","கையறு நெஞ்சினள், அடைதரும்","மைஈர் ஓதி மாஅ யோளே?"],"p":"","r":"Setting out for wealth, the man tells his heart that the fire of the old saying, that only men who never leave their wives' breasts can bear to live in one town watching friends in want and kin in distress, burned in him night and day and was not quenched by the rain of effort; but while he toils in the waste, how will she fare in the mornings when the koel wakes sleeping pairs, walking softly like a swan, helpless of heart?","n":"Duty to friends and kin is the reason given for leaving, not desire for wealth itself, and it is put as a proverb he cannot answer. The swan's gait and the paired sleepers waked by the koel make her mornings the cost. Lines 11 to 13, joining a lute and the koel, are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","sutram","pirivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 279","lb":"A279","poet":"இருங்கோன் ஒல்லையாயன் செங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்து போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்து போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சீறுயாழ்","யாழ்",11,"yaazh","scene",""],["குயிலொடு","குயில்",12,"pul","scene","koel"],["அன்னமாண்","அன்னம்",15,"pul","simile","swan"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:280":{"book":"akananuru","num":281,"t":["பொன் அடர்ந் தன்ன ஒள்ளிணர்ச் செருந்திப்","பன்மலர் வேய்ந்த நலம்பெறு கோதையள்,","திணிமணல் அடைகரை அலவன் ஆட்டி","அசையினள் இருந்த ஆய்தொடிக் குறுமகள்,","நலம்சால் விழுப்பொருள் கலம்நிறை கொடுப்பினும்,","பெறல்அருங் குரையள் ஆயின், அறம்தெரிந்து,","நாம்உறை தேஎம் மரூஉப்பெயர்ந்து, அவனொடு","இருநீர்ச் சேர்ப்பின் உப்புடன் உழுதும்,","பெருநீர்க் குட்டம் புணையொடு புக்கும்,","படுத்தனம் பணிந்தனம், அடுத்தனம், இருப்பின்,","தருகுவன் கொல்லோ தானே - விரிதிரைக்","கண்திரள் முத்தம் கொண்டு ஞாங்கர்த்","தேனிமிர் அகன்கரைப் பகுக்கும்","கானலம் பெருந்துறைப் பரதவன் எமக்கே?"],"p":"","r":"Having seen the girl with her garland of gold-bright serunthi playing at chasing crabs on the shore, the man tells his heart that since even a shipload of wealth would not win her, he will leave his country, plough salt and go into the deep sea on a float with her father, and serve him humbly, in the hope that the fisherman who shares out pearls on the wide shore will give her to him.","n":"A suitor proposes to earn a bride by servitude, not by bride-price, because the father's pride makes wealth useless; அறம் தெரிந்து makes the humbling a matter of right conduct, not of desperation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","panivu","kaamam","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 280","lb":"A280","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகளைக் கண்ணுற்று நீங்குந் தலைமகன் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது. அல்லகுறிப்பட்டுப் போகாநின்றவன் சொல்லியதுமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","தலைமகளைக் கண்ணுற்று நீங்குந் தலைமகன் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","அல்லகுறிப்பட்டுப் போகாநின்றவன் சொல்லியதுமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["செருந்திப்","செருந்தி",1,"maram","ornament",""],["அலவன்","அலவன்",3,"pira","scene","crab"],["உப்புடன்","உப்பு",8,"pira","scene",""],["முத்தம்","முத்து",12,"pira","scene",""],["தேனிமிர்","தேன்",13,"pira","scene","bee"],["பரதவன்","பரதவன்",14,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:14"},"a:281":{"book":"akananuru","num":282,"t":["செய்வது தெரிந்திசின்- தோழி! அல்கலும்,","அகலுள் ஆண்மை அச்சறக் கூறிய","சொல்பழுது ஆகும் என்றும் அஞ்சாது,","ஒல்குஇயல் மடமயில் ஒழித்த பீலி","வான்போழ் வல்வில் சுற்றி, நோன்சிலை","அவ்வார் விளிம்பிற்கு அமைந்த நொவ்வியல்","கனைகுரல் இசைக்கும் விரைசெல் கடுங்கணை","முரண்மிகு வடுகர் முன்னுற, மோரியர்","தென்திசை மாதிரம் முன்னிய வரவிற்கு","விண்ணுற ஓங்கிய பனிஇருங் குன்றத்து,","எண்கதிர்த் திகிரி உருளிய குறைத்த","அறைஇறந்து, அவரோ சென்றனர்","பறையறைந் தன்ன அலர்நமக்கு ஒழித்தே"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend what she should do: the man who spoke boldly of his manhood went off without fear that his word would prove false, a peacock's shed feather wound on his bow, across the rock the Moriyar's wheels cut on their march south with the Vadugar in the van, leaving her only gossip loud as a beaten drum.","n":"The bow wound with peacock feather and the Moriyar's rock-cut pass are the very images Akananuru 69 uses to promise a quick return; here they describe a man already gone, and the only thing he has left behind is talk. The Moriyar road is history used as distance: he has gone as far as an emperor's army once came.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","alar","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 281","lb":"A281","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளது வேறுபாடு கண்டு ஆற்றாளாகிய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளது வேறுபாடு கண்டு ஆற்றாளாகிய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["மடமயில்","மயில்",4,"pul","scene","peacock"],["பீலி","பீலி",4,"pul","ornament","peacock feather"],["பறையறைந்","பறை",13,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[["king","மோரியர்",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:15"},"a:282":{"book":"akananuru","num":283,"t":["பெருமலைச் சிலம்பின் வேட்டம் போகிய,","செறிமடை அம்பின், வல்வில், கானவன்","பொருதுதொலை யானை வெண்கோடு கொண்டு,","நீர்திகழ் சிலம்பின் நன்பொன் அகழ்வோன்,","கண்பொருது இமைக்கும் திண்மணி கிளர்ப்ப,","வைந்நுதி வால்மருப்பு ஒடிய உக்க","தெண்நீர் ஆலி கடுக்கும் முத்தமொடு","மூவேறு தாரமும் ஒருங்குடன் கொண்டு","சாந்தம் பொறைமரம் ஆக, நறைநார்","வேங்கைக் கண்ணியன் இழிதரும் நாடற்கு","இன்தீம் பலவின் ஏர்கெழு செல்வத்து","எந்தையும் எதிர்ந்தனன், கொடையே; அலர்வாய்","அம்பல் ஊரும் அவனொடு மொழியும்;","சாயிறைத் திரண்ட தோள்பா ராட்டி,","யாயும், 'அவனே என்னும்; யாமும்,","'வல்லே வருக, வரைந்த நாள்; 'என,","நல்இறை மெல்விரல் கூப்பி,","இல்லுறை கடவுட்கு ஓக்குதும், பலியே!"],"p":"","r":"Within the hero's hearing, the friend tells the woman that their father has agreed to give her to the hill-lord whose hunters bring down ivory, gold, gems and pearls together; the gossiping town now speaks on his side, the mother praises his shoulders and says he is the one, and they themselves fold their fingers to the house god praying that the fixed day come quickly.","n":"The three kinds of mountain wealth carried down together on a sandal-wood pole read like the bride-gifts the man should bring, and the poem is meant to be overheard: everyone has consented, so the only delay left is his. Even the alar that threatened secret love has turned to approval.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","alar","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 282","lb":"A282","poet":"தொல் கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிக்கண் தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது. தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதுமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறிக்கண் தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதுமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கானவன்","கானவன்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene","elephant"],["முத்தமொடு","முத்தம்",7,"pira","simile","pearl"],["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",9,"maram","scene","sandal tree"],["வேங்கைக்","வேங்கை",10,"maram","ornament","vengai tree"],["பலவின்","பலா",11,"maram","scene",""],["கடவுட்கு","கடவுள்",18,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:15"},"a:283":{"book":"akananuru","num":284,"t":["நன்னெடுங் கதுப்பொடு பெருந்தோள் நீவிய!","நின்னிவண் ஒழிதல் அஞ்சிய என்னினும்,","செலவுதலைக் கொண்ட பெருவிதுப்பு உறுவி","பல்கவர் மருப்பின் முதுமான் போக்கிச்,","சில்உணாத் தந்த சீறூர்ப் பெண்டிர்","திரிவயின், தெவுட்டும் சேண்புலக் குடிஞைப்","பைதன் மென்குரல் ஐதுவந்து இசைத்தொறும்,","போகுநர் புலம்பும் ஆறே ஏகுதற்கு","அரிய ஆகும்என் னாமைக், கரிமரம்","கண்அகை இளங்குழை கால்முதற் கவினி,","விசும்புடன் இருண்டு, வெம்மை நீங்கப்,","பசுங்கண் வானம் பாய்தளி பொழிந்தெனப்,","புல்நுகும்பு எடுத்த நல்நெடுங் கானத்து,","ஊட்டுறு பஞ்சிப் பிசிர்பரந் தன்ன,","வண்ண மூதாய் தண்நிலம் வரிப்ப,","இனிய ஆகுக தணிந்தே","இன்னா நீப்பின் நின்னொடு செலற்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man who has resolved to take the woman away with him that she is eager to go, and asks him not to think the road too hard; she wishes the rains to fall on the long forest so that its heat is quenched, grass springs up and the red velvet mites streak the cool ground like tufts of dyed cotton, making the way sweet for her to leave hardship and go with him.","n":"The friend's blessing is a wish that the paalai road turn into mullai: rain, new grass and the red mites of the wet season. The village women who give scant food and the owl's thin cry belong to the hard road she hopes will soften.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","mazhai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 283","lb":"A283","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உடன்போக்கு வலித்த தோழி தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","உடன்போக்கு வலித்த தோழி தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முதுமான்","மான்",4,"maa","scene","old deer"],["குடிஞைப்","குடிஞை",6,"pul","scene","owl"],["கரிமரம்","கரிமரம்",9,"maram","scene",""],["புல்நுகும்பு","புல்",13,"maram","scene","grass shoots"],["மூதாய்","மூதாய்",15,"pira","scene","red velvet mite"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:15"},"a:284":{"book":"akananuru","num":285,"t":["சிறியிலை நெல்லிக் காய்கண் டன்ன","குறுவிழிக் கண்ண கூரல்அம் குறுமுயல்","முடந்தை வரகின் வீங்குபீள் அருந்துபு,","குடந்தைஅம் செவிய கோட்பவர் ஒடுங்கி,","இன்துயில் எழுந்து, துணையொடு போகி,","முன்றில் சிறுநிறை நீர்கண்டு உண்ணும்","புன்புலம் தழீஇய பொறைமுதற் சிறுகுடித்,","தினைகள் உண்ட தெறிகோல் மறவர்,","விதைத்த வில்லர், வேட்டம் போகி,","முல்லைப் படப்பைப் புல்வாய் கெண்டும்","காமர் புறவி னதுவே- காமம்","நம்மினும் தான்தலை மயங்கிய","அம்மா அரிவை உறைவின் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"The man, his work done, says that the town of the fair woman whose love is more distracted than his own lies in the lovely forest where the small-eyed hare eats swollen varagu ears, sleeps curled, and wakes to go with its mate and drink at a little pool in a doorway, where millet-fed bowmen hunt deer in the jasmine gardens.","n":"The whole first half is the home country seen with longing as he nears it; the hare that rises and goes off with its partner is the image of what he is about to do. The last lines turn the usual complaint around: he grants that her longing is greater than his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kaamam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 284","lb":"A284","poet":"இடைக்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது. தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியதுமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியதுமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["நெல்லிக்","நெல்லி",1,"maram","simile",""],["குறுமுயல்","முயல்",2,"maa","iraicchi","hare"],["வரகின்","வரகு",3,"unavu","scene",""],["தினைகள்","தினை",8,"unavu","scene",""],["மறவர்","மறவர்",8,"seythi","scene","hunters"],["புல்வாய்","புல்வாய்",10,"maa","scene","deer"],["முல்லைப்","முல்லை",10,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","குறுமுயல்","confirmed","1/1","the man, his work done, will now go back to live beside his wife"]],"ch":"a:15"},"a:285":{"book":"akananuru","num":286,"t":["'ஒழியச் சென்மார், செல்ப' என்று, நாம்","அழிபடர் உழக்கும் அவல நெஞ்சத்து","எவ்வம் இகந்துசேண் அகல, வைஎயிற்று","ஊன்நகைப் பிணவின் உறுபசி களைஇயர்,","காடுதேர் மடப்பிணை அலறக் கலையின்","ஓடுகுறங்கு அறுத்த செந்நாய் ஏற்றை","வெயில்புலந்து இளைக்கும் வெம்மைய, பயில்வரி","இரும்புலி வேங்கைக் கருந்தோல் அன்ன","கல்எடுத்து எறிந்த பல்கிழி உடுக்கை","உலறுகுடை வம்பலர் உயர்மரம் ஏறி,","ஏறுவேட்டு எழுந்த இனம்தீர் எருவை","ஆடுசெவி நோக்கும் அத்தம், பணைத்தோள்","குவளை உண்கண் இவளும் நம்மொடு","வரூஉம் என்றனரே, காதலர்","வாராய், தோழி! முயங்குகம் பலவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, having won the woman's consent to elope, says that where they once grieved that the men would go and leave them, now the lover has said that she too will come with them through the desert where the red dog cuts down a stag to feed its hungry mate, travellers in stone-torn rags climb trees and the vulture watches; so come, friend, let us embrace many times.","n":"The road is described at its cruellest just as the friend announces joy: the grief of being left is undone by the decision to go together. The red dog hunting for its mate shows the provision the man will make for her on that road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","pirivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 285","lb":"A285","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம் பட்டினத்துக் காரிக்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"1உடன்போக்குடன் படுவித்த தோழி, தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","உடன்போக்குடன் படுவித்த தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பிணவின்","பிணவு",4,"maa","scene","female dog"],["மடப்பிணை","பிணை",5,"maa","scene","doe"],["கலையின்","கலை",5,"maa","scene","stag"],["செந்நாய் ஏற்றை","செந்நாய்",6,"maa","iraicchi","wild dog"],["வேங்கைக்","வேங்கை",8,"maa","simile","tiger"],["வம்பலர்","வம்பலர்",10,"seythi","scene","travellers"],["எருவை","எருவை",11,"pul","scene","vulture"],["குவளை","குவளை",13,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","செந்நாய் ஏற்றை","confirmed","2/2","the man who takes her through that land will provide for her as the beast does for its mate"]],"ch":"a:15"},"a:286":{"book":"akananuru","num":287,"t":["வெள்ளி விழுந்தொடி மென்கருப்பு உலக்கை,","வள்ளி நுண்இடை வயின்வயின் நுடங்க,","மீன்சினை அன்ன வெண்மணல் குவைஇக்,","காஞ்சி நீழல், தமர்வளம் பாடி,","ஊர்க்குறு மகளிர் குறுவழி, விறந்த","வராஅல் அருந்திய சிறுசிரல் மருதின்","தாழ்சினை உறங்கும் தண்துறை ஊர!","விழையா உள்ளம் விழையும் ஆயினும்,","என்றும், கேட்டவை தோட்டி ஆகமீட்டு ஆங்கு,","அறனும் பொருளும் வழாமை நாடி,","தற்றகவு உடைமை நோக்கி, மற்றதன்","பின்ஆ கும்மே, முன்னியது முடித்தல்;","அனைய, பெரியோர் ஒழுக்கம்; அதனால்,","அரிய பெரியோர்த் தெரியுங் காலை","நும்மோர் அன்னோர் மாட்டும், இன்ன","பொய்யொடு மிடைந்தவை தோன்றின்,","மெய்யாண்டு உளதோ, இவ்வுலகத் தானே?"],"p":"","r":"When the man takes her hand as if swearing, the friend, addressing him as lord of the cool-ford town where girls pound sand in the kaanchi shade and a kingfisher full of fish sleeps on a marutham branch, says the great hold back even what the heart desires, weigh virtue and wealth and their own worth, and only then carry out what they intended; if falsehood appears even in men like him, where is truth to be found in the world?","n":"She raises him to the rank of the great precisely so that a broken promise would be a failure of truth for the whole world; the maxim is a trap built to hold him to his oath of marriage. Her reading of his gesture as an oath is the colophon's point.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","saandror","varaivu","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 286","lb":"A286","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"வரைந் தெய்துவலென்று நீங்குந் தலைமகன் தலைமகளை ஆற்று வித்துக் கொண்டிருத்தல் வேண்டுமென்று தோழியைக் கைப் பற்றியது. தன்னைத் தொட்டுச் சூளுறுவானாகக் கருதித் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தன்னைத் தொட்டுச் சூளுறுவானாகக் கருதித் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["உலக்கை","உலக்கை",1,"pira","scene","pestle"],["வள்ளி","வள்ளி",2,"maram","simile","creeper"],["மீன்சினை","மீன்",3,"pira","simile","fish roe"],["காஞ்சி","காஞ்சி",4,"maram","scene",""],["வராஅல்","வரால்",6,"pira","scene","murrel fish"],["சிறுசிரல்","சிரல்",6,"pul","scene","kingfisher"],["மருதின்","மருது",6,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:15"},"a:287":{"book":"akananuru","num":288,"t":["தொடிஅணி முன்கைத் தொகுவிரல் குவைஇப்,","படிவ நெஞ்சமொடு பகல்துணை ஆக,","நோம்கொல்? அளியள் தானே!- தூங்குநிலை,","மரைஏறு சொறிந்த மாத்தட் கந்தின்","சுரைஇவர் பொதியில் அம்குடிச் சீறூர்","நாட்பலி மறந்த நரைக்கண் இட்டிகை,","புரிசை மூழ்கிய பொரி அரை ஆலத்து","ஒருதனி நெடுவீழ் உதைத்த கோடை","துணைப் புறா இரிக்கும் தூய்மழை நனந்தலைக்,","கணைக்கால் அம்பிணை ஏறுபுறம் நக்க,","ஒல்குநிலை யாஅத்து ஓங்குசினை பயந்த","அல்குறு வரிநிழல் அசையினம் நோக்க,","அரம்புவந்து அலைக்கும் மாலை,","நிரம்பா நீள்இடை வருந்துதும் யாமே"],"p":"","r":"On the road, the man asks his heart whether the poor woman suffers at home, praying with bunched fingers and a vow-bound heart for company by day, while he toils on the endless way through deserted hamlets where the shrine has forgotten its daily offering, the west wind scatters a pair of doves from a banyan, a doe licks a stag's back and the herd gazes at thin shade as evening comes on.","n":"The abandoned shrine that has forgotten its daily offering sits beside the woman who keeps her daily vow: the desert is full of things given up, while she is not. The pair of doves driven apart by the wind and the doe comforting the stag frame the two kinds of pairing he has in mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","maalai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 287","lb":"A287","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிந்து போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் இடைச்சுரத்து நின்ற தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"இடைச்சுரத்து நின்ற தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["மரைஏறு","மரை",4,"maa","scene","wild ox"],["சுரைஇவர்","சுரை",5,"maram","scene","gourd vine"],["ஆலத்து","ஆல்",7,"maram","scene",""],["துணைப் புறா","புறா",9,"pul","scene",""],["அம்பிணை","பிணை",10,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["யாஅத்து","யா",11,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","அம்பிணை","confirmed","2/2","even beasts on that road soothe their partners, while he has left her to bear her pain alone"]],"ch":"a:15"},"a:288":{"book":"akananuru","num":289,"t":["சென்மதி; சிறக்க; நின் உள்ளம்! நின்மலை","ஆரம் நீவிய அம்பகட்டு மார்பினை,","சாரல் வேங்கைப் படுசினைப் புதுப்பூ","முருகுமுரண் கொள்ளும் உருவக் கண்ணியை,","எரிதின் கொல்லை இறைஞ்சிய ஏனல்,","எவ்வம் கூரிய, வைகலும் வருவோய்!","கனிமுதிர் அடுக்கத்துஎம் தனிமை காண்டலின்","எண்மை செய்தனை ஆகுவை; நண்ணிக்","கொடியோர் குறுகும் நெடிஇருங் குன்றத்து,","இட்டுஆறு இரங்கும் விட்டுஒளிர் அருவி","அருவரை இழிதரும் வெருவரு படாஅர்க்","கயந்தலை மந்தி உயங்குபசி களைஇயர்,","பார்ப்பின் தந்தை பழச்சுளை தொடினும்,","நனிநோய் ஏய்க்கும் பனிகூர் அடுக்கத்து,","மகளிர் மாங்காட்டு அற்றே துகள் அறக்","கொங்தொடு உதிர்த்த கதுப்பின்,","அம்தீம் கிளவித் தந்தை காப்பே!"],"p":"","r":"On a day meeting the friend tells the hill man, who comes daily in sandal paste and a vengai garland and has made light of them on finding them alone, that he may go; their father now guards the sweet-voiced girl as closely as the sacred mango grove of the hill goddesses on the cold slope, where even a monkey father who touches a fruit to feed his hungry mate is struck with sickness.","n":"She tells him the girl is now shut in (செறிப்பு) without saying so: her guarded state is a forbidden grove where touching brings affliction. The monkey that cannot even take a fruit for its mate is the hero who can no longer reach her unless he marries.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivu","kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 288","lb":"A288","poet":"விற்றூற்று மூதெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பகற்குறிக்கண் தோழி செறிப்பறிவு றீ இ வரைவு கடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",2,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",3,"maram","ornament","vengai tree"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",5,"unavu","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",10,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",12,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["பழச்சுளை","பழம்",13,"unavu","scene",""],["மாங்காட்டு","மா",15,"maram","simile","mango"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:15"},"a:289":{"book":"akananuru","num":290,"t":["சிலைஏ றட்ட கணைவீழ் வம்பலர்","உயர்பதுக்கு இவர்ந்த ததர்கொடி அதிரல்","நெடுநிலை நடுகல் நாட்பலிக் கூட்டும்","சுரனிடை விலங்கிய மரன்ஓங்கு இயவின்,","வந்து, வினை வலித்த நம்வயின், என்றும்","தெருமரல் உள்ளமொடு வருந்தல் ஆனாது,","நெகிழா மென்பிணி வீங்கிய கைசிறிது","அவிழினும், உயவும் ஆய்மடத் தகுவி;","சேண்உறை புலம்பின் நாள்முறை இழைத்த","திண்சுவர் நோக்கி, நினைந்து கண்பனி,","நெகிழ்நூல் முத்தின், முகிழ்முலைத் தெறிப்ப,","மைஅற விரிந்த படைஅமை சேக்கை","ஐமென் தூவி அணைசேர்பு அசைஇ","மையல் கொண்ட மதன்அழி இருக்கையள்","பகுவாய்ப் பல்லி படுதொறும் பரவி;","'நல்ல கூறு' என நடுங்கிப்","புல்லென் மாலையொடு பொரும்கொல் தானே?"],"p":"","r":"On a desert way of arrow-killed travellers' cairns and tall hero-stones given daily offerings, the man who has set his mind on his work asks his heart whether the girl who fretted if his embrace loosened even a little now sits dazed on her feathered bed, watching the wall where she marks the days, tears falling on her breast like pearls off a slack string, praying 'speak good' each time the lizard chirps, and fighting the dull evening.","n":"Everything he imagines her doing (scoring the days on the wall, praying at the lizard's call) is the ritual of waiting, matched against the stones on his road that receive their own daily offering: both she and the dead are kept by daily rites.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","maalai","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 289","lb":"A289","poet":"எயினந்தை மகன் இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிந்து போகா நின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச்சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"பிரிந்து போகா நின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச்சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["அதிரல்","அதிரல்",2,"maram","scene",""],["முத்தின்","முத்து",11,"pira","scene","pearl"],["தூவி","தூவி",13,"pul","scene","feather down"],["பல்லி","பல்லி",15,"pira","scene","house lizard"]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:15"},"a:290":{"book":"akananuru","num":291,"t":["குடுமிக் கொக்கின் பைங்காற் பேடை","இருஞ்சேற்று அள்ளல் நாட்புலம் போகிய","கொழுமீன் வல்சிப் புன்தலைச் சிறாஅர்,","நுண்ஞாண் அவ்வலைச் சேவல் பட்டென,","அல்குறு பொழுதின் மெல்குஇரை மிசையாது,","பைதல் பிள்ளை தழீஇ, ஒய்யென,","அம்கண் பெண்ணை அன்புற நரலும்","சிறுபல் தொல்குடிப் பெருநீர்ச் சேர்ப்பன்","கழிசேர் புன்னை அழிபூங் கானல்,","தணவா நெஞ்சமொடு தமியன் வந்து நம்","மணவா முன்னும் எவனோ, தோழி?","வெண்கோட்டு யானை விறற்போர்க் குட்டுவன்","தென்திரைப் பரப்பின் தொண்டி முன்துறைச்,","கரும்புஉண மலர்ந்த பெருந்தண் நெய்தல்","மணிஏர் மாண்நலம் ஒரீஇப்,","பொன்நேர் வண்ணம் கொண்டஎன் கண்ணே?"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening outside at night, the woman asks her friend why, though he came alone with an unwavering heart to the backwater grove even before marrying her, her eyes, once lovely as the cool neythal of Kuttuvan's Thondi harbour, have lost their sapphire beauty and taken on the hue of gold, in his shore where a heron hen whose mate was caught in the fisher boys' net refuses food at evening and cries clutching her chick.","n":"'Even before marrying' turns the question into a warning: if he wastes her like this now, what after? The neythal of Thondi, the emblem of Kuttuvan's port, is what her eyes have stopped resembling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","varaivu","kan","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 290","lb":"A290","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"இரவுக் குறிக்கண் தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக் குறிக்கண் தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பேடை","பேடை",1,"pul","iraicchi","heron hen"],["கொழுமீன்","மீன்",3,"pira","scene",""],["சிறாஅர்","சிறார்",3,"seythi","scene","fisher boys"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",4,"pul","scene","male heron"],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",7,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",9,"maram","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",12,"maa","scene","elephant"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",14,"maram","simile","blue water-lily"]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவன்",12],["town","தொண்டி",13]],"dv":[["iraicchi","பேடை","confirmed","2/2","in his own shore a bird fasts and cries for a lost mate, so he should see what his delay does to the woman"]],"ch":"a:15"},"a:291":{"book":"akananuru","num":292,"t":["வானம் யெல்வளம் கரப்பக், கானம்","உலறி இலைஇல வாகப், பல உடன்","ஏறுடை ஆயத்து இனம்பசி தெறுப்பக்","கயன்அற வறந்த கோடையொடு நயன் அறப்","பெருவரை நிவந்த மருங்கில், கொடுவரிப்","புலியொடு பொருது சினஞ்சிவந்து, வலியோடு","உரவுக்களிறு ஒதுங்கிய மருங்கில் பரூஉப்பரல்","சிறுபல் மின்மினி கடுப்ப எவ்வாயும்","நிறைவன இமைக்கும் நிரம்பா நீள்இடை,","எருவை இருஞ்சிறை இரீஇய, விரிஇணர்த்","தாதுஉண் தும்பி முரல்இசை கடுப்பப்,","பரியினது உயிர்க்கும் அம்பினர், வெருவர","உவலை சூடிய தலையர், கவலை","ஆர்த்து, உடன் அரும்பொருள் வவ்வலின், யாவதும்","சாத்துஇடை வழங்காச் சேண்சிமை அதரச்","சிறியிலை நெல்லித் தீம்சுவைத் திரள்காய்","உதிர்வன தாஅம் அத்தம் தவிர்வுஇன்று,","புள்ளிஅம் பிணை உணீஇய உள்ளி,","அறுமருப்பு ஒழித்த தலைய, தோல்பொதி","மறுமருப்பு இளங்கோடு அதிரக் கூஉம்","சுடர்தெற வருந்திய அருஞ்சுரம் இறந்து, ஆங்கு","உள்ளினை வாழிய, நெஞ்சே! போதுஎனப்","புலம்கமழ் நாற்றத்து இரும்பல் கூந்தல்,","நல்லெழில், மழைக்கண், நம் காதலி","மெல்லிறைப் பணைத்தோள் விளங்கும்மாண் கவினே"],"p":"","r":"The man going for wealth tells his heart, live long, that crossing the sun-scorched desert where the sky has withheld its rain, pebbles glitter like fireflies on slopes where an elephant fought a tiger, leaf-crowned robbers with arrows humming like bees seize the caravans' goods, and a stag with new horns in velvet calls out meaning to feed its spotted doe, it has remembered the fine beauty of their beloved's hair, eyes and shoulders.","n":"The heart's remembering is blessed (வாழிய) rather than rebuked: the desert has not driven her out of his mind. The stag that has lost its old horns and calls for its doe is the one tender thing on the road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 291","lb":"A291","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் இடைச்சுரத்துத் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"இடைச்சுரத்துத் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["புலியொடு","புலி",6,"maa","scene","tiger"],["உரவுக்களிறு","களிறு",7,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மின்மினி","மின்மினி",8,"pira","simile","firefly"],["எருவை","எருவை",10,"pul","scene","vulture"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",11,"pira","simile","bee"],["உவலை","உவலை",13,"maram","ornament","leaf garland"],["நெல்லித்","நெல்லி",16,"maram","scene",""],["புள்ளிஅம் பிணை","பிணை",18,"maa","iraicchi","doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","புள்ளிஅம் பிணை","confirmed","1/1","the man too, far on the road, thinks of the one he means to provide for"]],"ch":"a:15"},"a:292":{"book":"akananuru","num":293,"t":["கூறாய், செய்வது தோழி! வேறுஉணர்ந்து,","அன்னையும் பொருள்உகுத்து அலமரும்; மென்முறிச்","சிறுகுளகு அருந்து, தாய்முலை பெறாஅ,","மறிகொலைப் படுத்தல் வேண்டி, வெறிபுரி","ஏதில் வேலன் கோதை துயல்வரத்","தூங்கும் ஆயின், அதூஉம் நாணுவல்,","இலங்குவளை நெகிழ்ந்த செல்லல்; புலம்படர்ந்து","இரவின் மேயல் மரூஉம் யானைக்","கால்வல் இயக்கம் ஒற்றி, நடுநாள்,","வரையிடைக் கழுதின் வன்கைக் கானவன்","கடுவிசைக் கவணின் எறிந்த சிறுகல்","உடுஉறு கணையின் போகிச் சாரல்","வேங்கை விரிஇணர் சிதறித் தேன் சிதையூஉ,","பலவின் பழத்துள் தங்கும்","மலைகெழு நாடன் மணவாக் காலே!"],"p":"","r":"The woman, afraid of the veri rite, asks her friend what to do: mother has misread her loosened bangles and spends wealth in worry, and if the stranger velan, to kill a kid not yet weaned, sways in his garland she will be ashamed, all because the lord of the hill, where a hunter's sling-stone flung at a night-grazing elephant flies off scattering vengai blooms and honey and lodges in a jackfruit, has not married her.","n":"The sling-stone that misses the elephant and ends in the jackfruit, wrecking flowers and honey on its way, may be read as the mother's rite aimed at the wrong cause; but the poem gives no second correspondence firm enough to make it a hidden comparison, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","velan","naanam","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 292","lb":"A292","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வெறியச்சுறீஇத் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வெறியச்சுறீஇத் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["குளகு","குளகு",3,"maram","scene","leaf fodder"],["மறிகொலைப்","மறி",4,"maa","scene","goat kid"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["கோதை","கோதை",5,"maram","ornament","garland"],["யானைக்","யானை",8,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கானவன்","கானவன்",10,"seythi","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",13,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["தேன்","தேன்",13,"pira","scene","honey"],["பலவின்","பலா",14,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:15"},"a:293":{"book":"akananuru","num":294,"t":["இலைஒழித்து உலறிய புன்தலை உலவை","வலைவலந் தனைய ஆகப், பலஉடன்","சிலம்பி சூழ்ந்த புலம்கெடு வைப்பின்,","துகில்ஆய் செய்கைப் பாவிரிந் தன்ன","வெயில்அவிர்பு நுடங்கும் வெவ்வெங் களரி,","குயிற்கண் அன்ன குரூஉக்காய் முற்றி","மணிக்காசு அன்ன மால்நிற இருங்கனி","உகாஅ மென்சினை உதிர்வன கழியும்","வேனில் வெஞ்சுரம் தமியர் தாமே","செல்ப என்ப தோழி! யாமே,","பண்பில் கோவலர் தாய்பிரித்து யாத்த","நெஞ்சமர் குழவிபோல, நொந்து நொந்து,","இன்னா மொழிதும் என்ப;","என்மயங் கினர்கொல், நம் காத லோரே?"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that they say he will go alone through the summer desert, where a dead leafless tree is netted with spider webs, heat shimmers like spread silk and dark fruits like gem-coins fall, and they say that she, aching like a calf tied apart from its mother by heartless herdsmen, will only speak bitter words; what has confused their lover?","n":"Twice 'they say' (என்ப): the plan to leave reaches her as report, and so does the prediction of how she will take it. The calf penned away from its mother is a mullai image brought into the desert to name her helplessness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 293","lb":"A293","poet":"காவன் முல்லைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரியக்கருதிய தலைமகன் குறிப்பறிந்து தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது. தலைமகனாற் பிரிவுணர்த்தப்பட்ட தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பொருள்வயிற் பிரியக்கருதிய தலைமகன் குறிப்பறிந்து"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தலைமகனாற் பிரிவுணர்த்தப்பட்ட தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["உலவை","உலவை",1,"maram","absent","dead tree"],["சிலம்பி","சிலம்பி",3,"pira","scene","spider"],["குயிற்கண்","குயில்",6,"pul","simile","koel"],["இருங்கனி","கனி",7,"maram","scene",""],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",11,"seythi","simile","herdsmen"],["குழவிபோல","குழவி",12,"maa","simile","calf"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:15"},"a:294":{"book":"akananuru","num":295,"t":["மங்குல் மாமழை விண்அதிர்பு முழங்கித்,","துள்ளுப்பெயல் கழிந்த பின்றை, புகையுறப்","புள்ளிநுண் துவலைப் பூவகம் நிறையக்,","காதலர்ப் பிரிந்த கையறு மகளிர்","நீர்வார் கண்ணின் கருவிளை மலரத்,","துய்த்தலைப் பூவின் புதலிவர் ஈங்கை","நெய்தோய்ந் தன்ன நீர்நனை அம்தளிர்","இருவகிர் ஈருளின் ஈரிய துயல்வர,","அவரைப் பைம்பூப் பயில, அகல்வயல்","கதிர்வார் காய்நெல் கட்குஇனிது இறைஞ்சக்","சிதர்சினை தூங்கும் அற்சிர அரைநாள்,","'காய்சின வேந்தன் பாசறை நீடி,","நம்நோய் அறியா அறனி லாளர்","இந்நிலை களைய வருகுவர், கொல்?' என","ஆனாது எறிதரும் வாடையொடு","நோனேன், தோழி! என் தனிமை யானே!"],"p":"","r":"To the friend who urges patience as the season comes, the woman says that after the thunder and downpour, with karuvilai flowers wet like the eyes of women left by their lovers, eengai shoots glistening as if dipped in ghee, avarai thick in flower and ripe paddy bowing in the fields, at midnight in the dewy season, lashed by the north wind and wondering whether the men who know nothing of her pain, kept in the angry king's war camp, will ever come, she cannot bear her loneliness.","n":"The season she was promised has come and every plant shows it, including the flowers that look like weeping women; the comfort the friend offers is undone by the very signs that were meant to bring him. The king's camp names a war, not wealth, as the cause of his absence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","mazhai","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 294","lb":"A294","poet":"கழார்க்கீர னெயிற்றியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பருவவரவின்கண் வற்புறுக்குந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பருவவரவின்கண் வற்புறுக்குந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கருவிளை","கருவிளை",5,"maram","simile",""],["ஈங்கை","ஈங்கை",6,"maram","scene",""],["அவரைப்","அவரை",9,"maram","scene",""],["காய்நெல்","நெல்",10,"unavu","scene",""],["வாடையொடு","வாடை",15,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:15"},"a:295":{"book":"akananuru","num":296,"t":["நிலம்நீர் அற்று நீள்சுனை வறப்பக்","குன்றுகோடு அகையக், கடுங்கதிர் தெறுதலின்,","என்றூழ் நீடிய வேய்படு நனந்தலை,","நிலவுநிற மருப்பின் பெருங்கை சேர்த்தி,","வேங்கை வென்ற வெருவரு பணைத்தோள்","ஓங்கல் யானை உயங்கி, மதம் தேம்பிப்","பல்மர ஒருசிறைப் பிடியொடு வதியும்","கல்லுடை அதர கானம் நீந்திக்","கடல்நீர் உப்பின் கணஞ்சால் உமணர்","உயங்குபகடு உயிர்ப்ப அசைஇ, முரம்பு இடித்து","அகல்இடம் குழித்த அகல்வாய்க் கூவல்","ஆறுசெல் வம்பலர் அசைவிட ஊறும்,","புடையலம் கழற்கால் புல்லி குன்றத்து,","நடைஅருங் கானம் விலங்கி, நோன்சிலைத்","தொடைஅமை பகழித் துவன்றுநிலை வடுகர்,","பிழிஆர் மகிழ்நர், கலிசிறந்து ஆர்க்கும்","மொழிபெயர் தேஎம் இறந்தனர் ஆயினும்,","பழிதீர் மாண்நலம் தருகுவர் மாதோ;","மாரிப் பித்திகத்து ஈர்இதழ் புரையும்","அங்கலுழ் கொண்ட செங்கடை மழைக்கண்,","மணங்கமழ் ஐம்பால், மடந்தை! நின்","அணங்குநிலை பெற்ற தடமென் தோளே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman whose shoulders have taken on affliction that though he has crossed the waterless forest where a tusker that beat a tiger stands weary beside its cow, the salt-merchants' well dug for passing travellers, the hill of Pulli and the land beyond where the Vadugar archers shout over toddy and the language changes, he will bring back her flawless beauty.","n":"Pulli's hill is the edge of the known world in these poems; beyond it the speech itself changes. The friend's comfort measures his distance precisely in order to say it does not matter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","nalam","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 295","lb":"A295","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேய்படு","வேய்",3,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",5,"maa","scene","tiger"],["யானை","யானை",6,"maa","iraicchi","elephant"],["பிடியொடு","பிடி",7,"maa","scene","cow elephant"],["உப்பின்","உப்பு",9,"pira","scene",""],["உமணர்","உமணர்",9,"seythi","scene","salt merchants"],["பகடு","பகடு",10,"maa","scene","bullock"],["பித்திகத்து","பித்திகம்",19,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["king","புல்லி",13],["hill","குன்றத்து",13]],"dv":[["iraicchi","யானை","confirmed","1/1","even in drought the beast keeps by its mate, so the man who went through that land is bound to her and will return"]],"ch":"a:15"},"a:296":{"book":"akananuru","num":297,"t":["கோதை இணர, குறுங்கால், காஞ்சிப்","போதுஅவிழ் நறுந்தாது அணிந்த கூந்தல்,","அரிமதர் மழைக்கண், மாஅ யோளொடு","நெருநையும் கமழ்பொழில் துஞ்சி, இன்றும்,","பெருநீர் வையை அவளொடு ஆடிப்,","புலரா மார்பினை வந்துநின்று, எம்வயின்","கரத்தல் கூடுமோ மற்றே பரப்பில்","பன்மீன் கொள்பவர் முகந்த இப்பி","நார்அரி நறவின் மகிழ்நொடைக் கூட்டும்","பேர்இசை கொற்கைப் பொருநன், வென்வேல்","கடும்பகட்டு யானை நெடுந்தேர்ச் செழியன்,","மலைபுரை நெடுநகர்க் கூடல் நீடிய","மலிதரு கம்பலை போல,","அலர்ஆ கின்று, அது பலர்வாய்ப் பட்டே"],"p":"","r":"The friend refusing the man entry says he slept yesterday in the fragrant grove with the dark girl who wears kaanchi pollen in her hair, bathed with her today in the great Vaigai, and comes with his chest still wet: can he hide it from them, when it has fallen into many mouths and become gossip like the swelling clamour of Koodal, the hill-high city of Chezhiyan, lord of Korkai where pearl oysters are traded for toddy?","n":"The proof against him is bodily (the undried chest), and the gossip is measured against the ordinary noise of the Pandya capital, not a battle: his affair is as public as Madurai's markets.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","alar","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 296","lb":"A296","poet":"மதுரைப் ரோலவாயார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"வாயில்வேண்டிச் சென்ற தலைமகற்கு வாயின்மறுக்குந் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","வாயில்வேண்டிச் சென்ற தலைமகற்கு வாயின்மறுக்குந் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காஞ்சிப்","காஞ்சி",1,"maram","ornament","kaanchi tree"],["பன்மீன்","மீன்",8,"pira","scene","fish"],["இப்பி","இப்பி",8,"pira","scene","pearl oyster"],["நறவின்","நறவு",9,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["யானை","யானை",11,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["river","வையை",5],["town","கொற்கைப்",10],["king","செழியன்",11],["town","கூடல்",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:15"},"a:297":{"book":"akananuru","num":298,"t":["பானாட் கங்குலும், பெரும்புன் மாலையும்,","ஆனா நோயொடு, அழிபடர்க் கலங்கி,","நம்வயின் இனையும் இடும்பை கைம்மிக,","என்னை ஆகுமோ, நெஞ்சே! நம் வயின்;","இருங்கவின் இல்லாப் பெரும்புன் தாடிக்,","கடுங்கண் மறவர் பகழி மாய்த்தென,","மருங்குல் நுணுகிய பேஎம் முதிர் நடுகல்","பெயர்பயம் படரத் தோன்றுகுயில் எழுத்து","இயைபுடன் நோக்கல் செல்லாது, அசைவுடன்","ஆறுசெல் வம்பலர் விட்டனர் கழியும்","சூர்முதல் இருந்த ஓமையம் புறவின்,","நீர்முள் வேலிப் புலவுநாறு முன்றில்,","எழுதி யன்ன கொடிபடு வெருகின்","பூளை அன்ன பொங்குமயிர்ப் பிள்ளை,","மதிசூழ் மீனின், தாய்வழிப் படூஉம்","சிறுகுடி மறவர் சேக்கோள் தண்ணுமைக்கு","எருவைச் சேவல் இருஞ்சிறை பெயர்க்கும்","வெருவரு கானம், நம்மொடு,","'வருவல்' என்றோள் மகிழ்மட நோக்கே?"],"p":"","r":"Going for wealth, the man asks his heart what will become of her grief, which overflows at midnight and the dull evening, and of the shy glad look of the girl who said she would come with him through the fearful forest where travellers pass by an old hero-stone without stopping to read its worn inscription, a wildcat's kitten follows its mother like stars round the moon, and the warriors' raiding drum makes the vulture shift its wings.","n":"The hero-stone's letters, carved to keep a name, are now passed unread by weary travellers: the road is a place where even fame is forgotten, and he fears forgetting is not what she will do. Her offer to come with him is the elopement he refused.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","ninaivu","udanpokku"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 297","lb":"A297","poet":"மதுரை மருத னிளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"பொருள்வயிற் போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["மறவர்","மறவர்",6,"seythi","scene","bowmen"],["ஓமையம்","ஓமை",11,"maram","scene",""],["எழுதி","எழுது",13,"pira","simile","painting"],["வெருகின்","வெருகு",13,"maa","scene","wildcat"],["பூளை","பூளை",14,"maram","simile",""],["பிள்ளை","பிள்ளை",14,"maa","iraicchi","kitten"],["தண்ணுமைக்கு","தண்ணுமை",16,"parai","scene",""],["எருவைச்","எருவை",17,"pul","scene","vulture"]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",7]],"dv":[["iraicchi","பிள்ளை","confirmed","2/2","she wanted to keep as close to him on this road, which is why her offer to come haunts him"]],"ch":"a:15"},"a:298":{"book":"akananuru","num":299,"t":["பயங்கெழு திருவின் பல்கதிர் ஞாயிறு","வயங்குதொழில் தரீஇயர், வலன்ஏர்பு விளங்கி,","மல்குகடல் தோன்றி யாங்கு, மல்குடை,","மணிமருள் மாலை, மலர்ந்த வேங்கை","ஒண்தளிர் அவிர்வரும் ஒலிகெழு பெருஞ்சினைத்","தண்துளி அசைவளி தைவரும் நாட!","கொன்றுசினம் தணியாது வென்றுமுரண் சாம்பாது,","இரும்பிடித் தொழுதியின் இனந்தலை மயங்காது,","பெரும்பெயற் கடாஅம் செருக்கி, வளமலை","இருங்களிறு இயல்வரும் பெருங்காட்டு இயவின்,","ஆர்இருள் துமிய வெள்வேல் ஏந்தி,","தாம்பூங் கோதை ஊதுவண்டு இரீஇ,","மென்பிணி அவிழ்ந்த அரைநாள் இரவு, இவண்","நீவந் ததனினும், இனிதுஆ கின்றே;","தூவல் கள்ளின் துணைதேர், எந்தை","கடியுடை வியல்நகர் ஓம்பினள் உறையும்","யாய்அறி வுறுதல் அஞ்சிப் பானாள்,","காவல் நெஞ்சமொடு காமம் செப்பேன்,","யான்நின் கொடுமை கூற, நினைபுஆங்கு,","இனையல்; வாழி, தோழி! நம் துறந்தவர்","நீடலர் ஆகி வருவர், வல்லென;","கங்குல் உயவுத்துணை ஆகிய","துஞ்சாது உறைவி இவளுவந் ததுவே!"],"p":"","r":"At a midnight meeting the woman tells the lord of the hill, where vengai shoots glow like the sun rising from the full sea, that his coming here through the tusker-haunted forest path, white spear in hand and bees on his garland, is sweet, yet out of fear that her mother would learn of it she has kept her love unspoken, and the sleepless friend who shares her nights has gladly assured her he will not delay.","n":"The lines are tangled: she seems to weigh his dangerous night visit against the friend's comfort that he will soon come for good. What she will not say aloud is the point: her love is guarded by fear of the house.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu","kuri","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 298","lb":"A298","poet":"மதுரைப் பண்டவாணிகன் இளந்தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிக்கண் தலைமகற்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறிக்கண் தலைமகற்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",4,"maram","simile","vengai tree"],["இரும்பிடித்","பிடி",8,"maa","scene","cow elephant"],["இருங்களிறு","களிறு",10,"maa","scene","tusker"],["கோதை","கோதை",12,"maram","ornament","garland"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",12,"pira","scene","bee"],["கள்ளின்","கள்",15,"unavu","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:15"},"a:299":{"book":"akananuru","num":300,"t":["எல்லையும் இரவும், வினைவயின் பிரிந்த","முன்னம், முன்உறுபு அடைய உள்ளிய","பதிமறந்து உறைதல் வல்லுநம் ஆயினும்","அதுமறந்து உறைதல் அரிது ஆகின்றே","கடுவளி எடுத்த கால்வழி தேக்கிலை","நெடுவிளிப் பருந்தின் வெறிஎழுந் தாங்கு,","விசும்புகண் புதையப் பாஅய்ப், பலஉடன்","அகல்இடம் செல்லுநர் அறிவுகெடத் தாஅய்க்,","கவலை சுரக்கும் காடுஅகல் அத்தம்,","செய்பொருள் மருங்கின் செலவுதனக்கு உரைத்தென","வைகுநிலை மதியம் போலப், பையெனப்,","புலம்புகொள் அவலமொடு, புதுக்கவின் இழந்த","நலம்கெழு திருமுகம் இறைஞ்சி, நிலம் கிளையா,","நீரொடு பொருத ஈர்இதழ் மழைக்கண்","இகுதரு தெண்பனி ஆகத்து உறைப்பக்,","கால்நிலை செல்லாது, கழிபடர்க் கலங்கி,","நாநடுக் குற்ற நவிலாக் கிளவியொடு,","அறல்மருள் கூந்தலின் மறையினள், 'திறல் மாண்டு","திருந்துக மாதோ, நும்செலவு' என வெய்து உயிராப்,","பருவரல் எவ்வமொடு அழிந்த","பெருவிதுப் புறுவி பேதுறு நிலையே"],"p":"","r":"On the road the man tells his heart that though they might live forgetting home, they cannot forget how she took the news of his going through the forest where wind-blown teak leaves fly like screaming kites: her face bowed like the waning moon, tears falling on her breast, unable to stand, she hid in her hair and, sighing, wished his going well in faltering words.","n":"Her blessing on his journey is the most painful thing he remembers: she says what a wife should say, with every sign of her body saying the opposite.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","nenju","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 299","lb":"A299","poet":"எயினந்தைமகனார் இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"இடைச்சுரத்துப் போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"இடைச்சுரத்துப் போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தேக்கிலை","தேக்கு",5,"maram","scene","teak"],["பருந்தின்","பருந்து",6,"pul","simile","kite"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:15"},"a:300":{"book":"akananuru","num":301,"t":["நாள்வலை முகந்த கோள்வல் பரதவர்","நுணங்குமணல் ஆங்கண் உணங்கப் பெய்ம்மார்,","பறிகொள் கொள்ளையர், மறுக உக்க","மீன்ஆர் குருகின் கானலம் பெருந்துறை,","எல்லை தண்பொழில் சென்றெனச் செலீஇயர்,","தேர்பூட்டு அயர ஏஎய், வார்கோல்","செறிதொடி திருத்திப் பாறுமயிர் நீவிச்,","'செல்இனி, மடந்தை! நின் தோழியொடு, மனை' எனச்","சொல்லிய அளவை, தான்பெரிது கலுழ்ந்து","தீங்குஆ யினள்இவள் ஆயின் தாங்காது,","நொதுமலர் போலப் பிரியின் கதுமெனப்","பிறிதுஒன்று ஆகலும் அஞ்சுவல்; அதனால்,","சேணின் வருநர் போலப் பேணா,","இருங்கலி யாணர்எம் சிறுகுடித் தோன்றின்,","வல்லெதிர் கொண்டு, மெல்லிதின் வினைஇத்,","துறையும் மான்றன்று பொழுதே; சுறவும்","ஓதம் மல்கலின், மறு ஆயினவே;","எல்லின்று; தோன்றல்! செல்லாதீம்' என,","எமர்குறை கூறத் தங்கி, ஏமுற,","இளையரும் புரவியும் இன்புற, நீயும்","இல்லுறை நல்விருந்து அயர்தல்","ஒல்லுதும், பெரும! நீ நல்குதல் பெறினே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man leaving a day meeting on the shore that the girl wept bitterly when he bade her go home, and she fears that if he parts like a stranger the girl will come to harm; so let him arrive at their busy hamlet as if from afar, and their kin will receive him, say the ford is dark and the tide brings sharks and press him to stay, and they will make him a welcome guest of the house, if he will only consent.","n":"The friend writes a little play: the lover arrives as a stranger and is kept for the night as a guest, a way of bringing him under the family's roof before marriage. Hospitality is turned into a step toward varaivu.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 300","lb":"A300","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறி வந்து நீங்குந் தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகற்குறி வந்து நீங்குந் தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["மீன்ஆர்","மீன்",4,"pira","scene",""],["குருகின்","குருகு",4,"pul","scene","heron"],["சுறவும்","சுறா",16,"pira","scene","shark"],["புரவியும்","புரவி",20,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:15"},"a:301":{"book":"akananuru","num":302,"t":["'வறன் உறு செய்யின் வாடுபு வருந்திப்","படர்மிகப் பிரிந்தோர் உள்ளுபு நினைதல்","சிறுநனி ஆன்றிகம் என்றி- தோழி-","நல்குநர் ஒழித்த கூலிச் சில்பதம்","ஒடிவை இன்றி ஓம்பாது உண்டு","நீர்வாழ் முதலை ஆவித் தன்ன","ஆரை வேய்ந்த அறைவாய்ச் சகடத்து","ஊர்இஃது என்னாஅர் ஊறில் வாழ்க்கை","சுரமுதல் வருத்தம் மரமுதல் வீட்டிப்","பாடுஇன் தெண்கிணை கறங்கங் காண்வரக்","குவிஇணர் எருக்கின் ததர்பூங் கண்ணி","ஆடூஉச் சென்னித் தகைப்ப மகடூஉ","முளரித் தீயின் முழங்கு அழல் விளக்கத்துக்","களரி யாவிரைக் கிளர்பூங் கோதை","வண்ண மார்பின் வனமுலைத் துயல்வரச்","செறிநடைப் பிடியொடு களிறுபுணர்ந் தென்னக்","குறுநெடுந் தூம்பொடு முழவுப்புணர்ந் திசைப்பக்","கார்வான் முழக்கின் நீர்மிசைத் தெவுட்டும்","தேரை ஒலியின் மாணச் சீர் அமைத்து","சில்லரி கறங்கும் சிறுபல் லியத்தொடு","பல்லூர் பெயர்வனர் ஆடி ஒல்லெனத்","தலைப்புணர்த்து அசைத்த பல்தொகைக் கலப்பையர்","இரும்பேர் ஒக்கல் கோடியர் இறந்த","புன்றலை மன்றங் காணின் வழிநாள்","அழுங்கன் மூதூர்க்கு இன்னா தாகும்;","அதுவே மறுவினம் மாலை யதனால்","காதலர் செய்த காதல்","நீடின்று மறத்தல் கூடுமோ, மற்றே?"],"p":"","r":"Answering the friend who says she has borne her absent lover's memory fairly well, the heroine says each returning evening is as desolate as an old town's commons on the day after a troupe of wandering performers, who spend their wages without saving and roam with drums, pipes and flower garlands from town to town, has danced there and moved on; so how could the love he gave be forgotten just because he stays away long?","n":"Twenty lines celebrate the performers' rootless, music-filled life only so that their absence can be felt: the joy is drawn at length to measure the emptiness it leaves. The troupe always leaves, as evening always comes back, so the desolation is a daily one. The word மறுவினம் (line 26) is obscure to me; I read it as the evening that comes round again.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 301","lb":"A301","poet":"அதியன் விண்ணத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முதலை","முதலை",6,"pira","simile","crocodile"],["ஆரை","ஆரை",7,"maram","scene",""],["தெண்கிணை","கிணை",10,"parai","scene",""],["எருக்கின்","எருக்கு",11,"maram","ornament",""],["யாவிரைக்","ஆவிரை",14,"maram","ornament",""],["பிடியொடு","பிடி",16,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["களிறு","களிறு",16,"maa","simile","tusker"],["தூம்பொடு","தூம்பு",17,"yaazh","scene",""],["முழவு","முழவு",17,"parai","scene",""],["தேரை","தேரை",19,"pira","simile","frog"],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",23,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:16"},"a:302":{"book":"akananuru","num":303,"t":["சிலம்பிற் போகிய செம்முக வாழை","அலங்கல் அம்தோடு அசைவளி யுறுதொறும்","பள்ளி யானைப் பரூஉப்புறம் தைவரும்","நல்வரை நாடனொடு அருவி ஆடியும்","பல்லிதழ் நீலம் படுசுனைக் குற்றும்","நறுவீ வேங்கை இனவண் டார்க்கும்","வெறிகமழ் சோலை நயந்துவிளை யாடலும்","அரிய போலும்- காதல்அம் தோழி!-","இருங்கல் அடுக்கத்து என்னையர் உழுத","கரும்பென கவினிய பெருங்குரல் ஏனல்","கிளிபட விளைந்தமை யறிந்தும் 'செல்க' என","நம்மவண் விடுநள் போலாள் கைம்மிகச்","சில்சுணங் கணிந்த செறிந்துவீங் கிளமுலை","மெல்லியல் ஒலிவரும் கதுப்பொடு","பல்கால் நோக்கும்- அறனில் யாயே"],"p":"","r":"With the hero listening outside, the friend tells the heroine that bathing in the falls with the lord of the hills where plantain leaves stroke a sleeping elephant's back, picking blue lilies in the pool and playing in the fragrant vengai grove all seem to have become hard, for though the millet their brothers sowed has ripened enough to draw the parrots, their unkind mother shows no sign of sending them out to guard it and keeps looking at the girl's swelling breasts and thick hair.","n":"The millet and the girl ripen together, and both end the field-guarding that made the day meetings possible: the mother's watching eyes mean confinement at home. Spoken for the hero to overhear, the friend's complaint tells him that only marriage is left. The plantain leaf stroking the sleeping elephant (lines 1 to 3) may suggest the tenderness of the hills' lord, but the leaf moves with the wind and does no act, so I leave it as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 302","lb":"A302","poet":"மதுரை அறுவைவாணிகன் இளவேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பகலே சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","பகலே சிறைப்புறமாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வாழை","வாழை",1,"maram","scene",""],["யானைப்","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",4,"pira","scene",""],["நீலம்","நீலம்",5,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",6,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["இனவண் டார்க்கும்","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene",""],["சோலை","சோலை",7,"maram","scene",""],["கரும்பென","கரும்பு",10,"maram","simile",""],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",10,"unavu","scene",""],["கிளிபட","கிளி",11,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:16"},"a:303":{"book":"akananuru","num":304,"t":["இடைபிறர் அறிதல் அஞ்சி மறைகரந்து","பேஎய் கண்ட கனவிற பன்மாண்","நுண்ணிதின் இயைந்த காமம் வென்வேல்","மறமிகு தானைப் பசும்பூண் பொறையன்","கார்புகன் றெடுத்த சூர்புகல் நனந்தலை","மாஇருங் கொல்லி யுச்சித் தாஅய்த்","ததைந்துசெல் அருவியின் அலர்எழப் பிரிந்தோர்","புலம்கந் தாக இரவலர் செலினே","வரைபுரை களிற்றொடு நன்கலன் ஈயும்","உரைசால் வண்புகழ்ப் பாரி பறம்பின்","நிரைபறைக் குரீஇயினம் காலைப் போகி","முடங்குபுறச் செந்நெல் தரீஇயர் ஓராங்கு","இரைதேர் கொட்பின் வாகிப் பொழுதுபடப்","படர்கொள் மாலைப் படர்தந் தாங்கு","வருவாஎன்று உணர்ந்த மடங்கெழு நெஞ்சம்!","ஐயந் தெளியரோ நீயே பலவுடன்","வறன்மரம் பொருந்திய சிள்வீ டுமணர்","கணநிரை மணியின் ஆர்க்கும் சுரனிறந்து","அழிநீர் மீன்பெயர்ந் தாங்கவர்","வழிநடைச் சேறல் வலித்திசின் யானே"],"p":"","r":"The heroine scolds her foolish heart: their love, hidden from all like a ghoul's dream, was exposed by gossip loud as the cascade on Porayan's Kolli hill, and the man left; yet the heart believed he would come back as surely as the sparrows of Paari's Parambu fly off at dawn for red paddy and return together at dusk; let it be rid of that doubt, for she has resolved to follow his road across the wasteland, as fish leave a draining pool.","n":"The Paari simile is turned against the heart that made it: sparrows return every evening, he has not. The last image reverses the direction of the poem: not he comes back to her, but she leaves, as fish leave water that is running out, the town that gossip has drained. The resolve is spoken to her own heart and may not be meant to be carried out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","alar","pirivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 303","lb":"A303","poet":"ஒளவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் வேட்கை மீதூர்ந்த தலைமகள் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","nenju",null,null,"வேட்கை மீதூர்ந்த தலைமகள் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவியின்","அருவி",7,"pira","simile",""],["களிற்றொடு","களிறு",9,"maa","gift_food",""],["குரீஇயினம்","குரீஇ",11,"pul","simile",""],["செந்நெல்","நெல்",12,"unavu","simile",""],["வறன்மரம்","மரம்",17,"maram","absent",""],["சிள்வீ","சிள்வீடு",17,"pira","simile","cicada"],["டுமணர்","உமணர்",17,"seythi","simile",""],["மீன்பெயர்ந்","மீன்",19,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[["king","பொறையன்",4],["hill","கொல்லி",6],["patron","பாரி",10],["hill","பறம்பின்",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:16"},"a:304":{"book":"akananuru","num":305,"t":["இருவிசும்பு இவர்ந்த கருவி மாமழை","நீர்செறி நுங்கின் கண்சிதர்ந் தவைபோல்","சூர்ப்பனி பன்ன தண்வரல் ஆலியொடு","பரூஉப்பெயல் அழிதுளி தலைஇ வான்நவின்று","குரூஉத்துளி பொழிந்த பெரும்புலா வைகறை","செய்துவிட் டன்ன செந்நில மருங்கிற்","செறித்துநிறுத் தன்ன தெள்ளறல் பருகிச்","சிறுமறி தழீஇய தெறிநடை மடப்பிணை","வலந்திரி மருப்பின் அண்ணல் இரலையொடு","அலங்குசினைக் குருந்தின் அல்குநிழல் வதியச்","சுரும்புமிர்பு ஊதப் பிடவுத்தளை அவிழ","அரும்பொறி மஞ்ஜை ஆல வரிமணல்","மணிமிடை பவளம் போல அணிமிகக்","காயாஞ் செம்மல் தாஅய்ப் பலவும்","ஈயல் மூதாய் ஈர்ம்புறம் வரிப்பப்","புலனணி கொண்ட காரெதிர் காலை","'ஏந்துகோட்டு யானை வேந்தன் பாசறை","வினையொடு வேறுபுலத்து அல்கி நன்றும்","அறவர் அல்லர்நம் அருளா தோரன","நந்நோய் தன்வயின் அறியாள்","எந்நொந்து புலக்குங்கொல் மாஅ யோளே?"],"p":"","r":"In the war camp, as the first rains fall at dawn and the land takes on the season's beauty, the doe with her fawn resting with the stag in the kurunthu shade, peacocks dancing and kaayaa flowers strewn among red velvet mites like coral set among sapphires, the hero tells his heart that his dark wife, not knowing his own pain, must be saying that a man who lingers abroad on the king's work has no righteousness and no grace, and sulking at him.","n":"He hears himself judged in her words (lines 18 and 19: அறவர் அல்லர் நம் அருளாதோர்) and does not argue; his only defence, that she cannot know his pain, is also a confession that the season's beauty is hurting him too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","mazhai","oodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 304","lb":"A304","poet":"இடைக்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"1பாசறைக்கண் தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பாசறைக்கண் தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நுங்கின்","நுங்கு",2,"maram","simile","palmyra fruit"],["சிறுமறி","மறி",8,"maa","iraicchi","fawn"],["மடப்பிணை","பிணை",8,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["இரலையொடு","இரலை",9,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["குருந்தின்","குருந்து",10,"maram","scene",""],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",11,"pira","scene",""],["பிடவு","பிடவு",11,"maram","scene",""],["மஞ்ஜை","மஞ்ஞை",12,"pul","scene",""],["காயாஞ்","காயா",14,"maram","simile",""],["மூதாய்","மூதாய்",15,"pira","simile","velvet mite"],["யானை","யானை",17,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மடப்பிணை","confirmed","1/1","even the deer are together at the season of return, which is exactly what makes his staying away graceless in her eyes"]],"ch":"a:16"},"a:305":{"book":"akananuru","num":306,"t":["பகலினும் அகலா தாகி யாமம்","தவலில் நீத்தமொடு ஐயெனக் கழியத்","தளிமழை பொழிந்த தண்வரல் வாடையொடு","பனிமீக் கூரும் பைதற் பானாள்","பல்படை நிவந்த வறுமையில் சேக்கைப்","பருகு வன்ன காதலொடு திருகி","மெய்புகு வன்ன கைகவர் முயக்கத்து","ஓருயிர் மாக்களும் புலம்புவர் மாதோ","அருளி லாளர் பொருள்வயின் அகல","எவ்வம் தாங்கிய இடும்பை நெஞ்சத்து","யானெவன் உளனே- தோழி!- தானே","பராரைப் பெண்ணைச் சேக்குங் கூர்வாய்","ஒரு தனி அன்றில் உயவுக்குரல் கடைஇய","உள்ளே கனலும் உள்ளம் மெல்லெனக்","கனைஎரி பிறப்ப ஊதும்","நினையா மாக்கள் தீங்குழல் கேட்டே?"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that on these cold midnights of north wind and drizzle, when the night will not pass, even lovers locked in an embrace that seems to drink each other and enter each other's bodies feel lonely; so how is she to live, now that the graceless man is going away for wealth, when the pained cry of a lone anril on the palmyra stirs her smouldering heart and the herdsmen's sweet flute blows it into flame?","n":"An argument from the stronger case: if the embraced suffer such nights, the one left alone cannot bear them. The flute-players are நினையா மாக்கள், people who do not think: the music is innocent and only her heart turns it into a bellows. The lone anril, a bird famed for never leaving its mate, may carry more than its cry, but it acts here only as a sound, so I leave it as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 305","lb":"A305","poet":"வடமவண்ணக்கன் பேரிசாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவுணர்த்தப்பட்ட தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது. தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியதுமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவுணர்த்தப்பட்ட தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியதுமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பெண்ணைச்","பெண்ணை",12,"maram","scene",""],["அன்றில்","அன்றில்",13,"pul","scene",""],["தீங்குழல்","குழல்",16,"yaazh","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:16"},"a:306":{"book":"akananuru","num":307,"t":["பெரும்பெயர் மகிழ்ந! பேணா தகன்மோ!","பரந்த பொய்கைப் பிரம்பொடு நீடிய","முட்கொம் பீங்கைத் துய்த்தலைப் புதுவீ","ஈன்ற மாத்தின் இளந்தளிர் வருட","ஆர்குருகு உறங்கும் நீர்சூழ் வளவயற்","கழனிக் கரும்பின் சாய்ப்புறம் ஊர்ந்து","பழன யாமை பசுவெயிற் கொள்ளும்","நெல்லுடை மறுகின் நன்னர் ஊர!","இதுவோ மற்றுநின் செம்மல்? மாண்ட","மதியேர் ஒள்நுதல் வயங்கிழை ஒருத்தி","இகழ்ந்த சொல்லுஞ் சொல்லிச் சிவந்த","ஆயிதழ் மழைக்கண் நோயுற நோக்கித்","தண்ணறுங் கமழ்தார் பரீஇயினள் நும்மொடு","ஊடினள்- சிறுதுனி செய்தெம்","மணன்மலி மறுகின் இறந்திசி னோளே"],"p":"","r":"Turning the reveller away from the door, the friend mocks his fine reputation: a woman with a moon-bright forehead has just scolded him, looked at him with reddened, tearful eyes, torn off his cool garland in a quarrel and walked away through their own street.","n":"The rival performs in public the oodal that the wife is refused, and does it in front of the wife's house; the friend needs no other reason to shut the door. The well-fed heron asleep under stroking mango shoots and the tortoise basking on the cane (lines 2 to 7) may hint at the pampered hero, but only one correspondence maps clearly, so I keep them as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 306","lb":"A306","poet":"மதுரைக் கூலவாணிகன் சீத்தலைச்சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகற்கு வாயின் மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","வாயின் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பிரம்பொடு","பிரம்பு",2,"maram","scene",""],["பீங்கைத்","ஈங்கை",3,"maram","scene",""],["மாத்தின்","மா",4,"maram","scene","mango"],["குருகு","குருகு",5,"pul","scene",""],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",6,"maram","scene",""],["யாமை","யாமை",7,"pira","scene","tortoise"],["நெல்லுடை","நெல்",8,"unavu","scene",""],["கமழ்தார்","தார்",13,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:16"},"a:307":{"book":"akananuru","num":308,"t":["'சிறுநுதல் பசந்து பெருந்தோள் சாஅய்","அகலெழில் அல்குல் அவ்வரி வாடப்","பகலுங் கங்குலும் மயங்கிப் பையெனப்","பெயல்உறு மலரின் கண்பனி வார","ஈங்கிவள் உழக்கும்' என்னாது வினைநயந்து","நீங்கல் ஒல்லுமோ- ஐய!- வேங்கை","அடுமுரண் தொலைத்த நெடுநல் யானை","மையலம் கடாஅஞ் செருக்கி மதஞ்சிறந்து","இயங்குநர்ச் செருக்கும் எய்படு நனந்தலைப்","பெருங்கை எண்கினம் குரும்பி தேரும்","புற்றுடைச் சுவர புதலிவர் பொதியிற்","கடவுள் போகிய கருந்தாட் கந்தத்து","உடனுறை பழமையின் துறத்தல் செல்லாது","இரும்புறாப் பெடையொடு பயிரும்","பெருங்கல் வைப்பின் மலைமுதல் ஆறே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man set on his work whether he can leave without thinking how the girl here will suffer, her forehead pale, her shoulders thin, her tears falling like rain on flowers, when the mountain road he means to take runs past a ruined hall overgrown with bushes where, on the black pillar the god has abandoned, a dove still calls with its mate, unable to leave because they have lived there together so long.","n":"The dove clings to a home that even its god has deserted; the man is about to desert a living one. The road's other creatures, the must-maddened tusker that killed a tiger and the bears digging out termites, make it frightening; the dove makes it a reproach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pazhaimai","pasalai","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 307","lb":"A307","poet":"மதுரை ஈழத்துப் பூதன் தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவுணர்த்தப்பட்ட தோழி தலைமகனைச் செலவு விலக்கியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,"செலவு விலக்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மலரின்","மலர்",4,"maram","simile",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",6,"maa","scene","tiger"],["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","scene",""],["எய்படு","எய்",9,"maa","scene","porcupine"],["எண்கினம்","எண்கு",10,"maa","scene","bear"],["குரும்பி","குரும்பி",10,"pira","scene","termite brood"],["இரும்புறாப்","புறா",14,"pul","iraicchi",""],["பெடையொடு","பெடை",14,"pul","iraicchi",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","இரும்புறாப்","confirmed","1/1","a bird will not desert an old shared home even when its god has gone, so a man should not leave the woman he has lived with"]],"ch":"a:16"},"a:308":{"book":"akananuru","num":309,"t":["உழுவையொ டுழந்த உயங்குநடை ஒருத்தல்","நெடுவகிர் விழுப்புண் கழாஅக் கங்குல்","ஆலி அழிதுளி பொழிந்த வைகறை","வால்வெள் அருவிப் புனல்மலிந் தொழுகலின்","இலங்குமலை புதைய வெண்மழை கவைஇக்","கலஞ்சுடு புகையிற் றோன்றும் நாட!","இரவின் வருதல் எவனோ? பகல்வரின்","தொலையா வேலின் வண்மகிழ் எந்தை","களிறணந் தெய்தாக் கன்முகை இதணத்துச்","சிறுதினைப் படுகிளி எம்மொடு ஓப்பி","மல்ல லறைய மலிர்சுனைக் குவளை","தேம்பாய் ஒண்பூ நறும்பல அடைச்சிய","கூந்தல் மெல்லணைத் துஞ்சிப் பொழுதுபடக்","காவலர்க் கரந்து கடிபுனம் துழைஇய","பெருங்களிற்று ஒருத்தலின் பெயர்குவை","கருங்கோற் குறிஞ்சிநும் உறைவி னூர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the rain-washed hills, where a tusker still carries the long wound of its fight with a tiger and white clouds wrap the mountain like smoke from a potter's kiln, why he comes at night: let him come by day, chase parrots from the millet with them on the high platform of their spear-bearing father, sleep pillowed on her hair dressed with lilies from the rock pools, and at dusk slip past the guards like a raiding tusker back to his village of kurinji shrubs.","n":"The opening wounded tusker shows the night road's danger without saying so. The day offer is not simple: the field is guarded, the father carries an 'unfailing spear', and the hero must leave like a crop-raiding elephant, so the day meeting carries its own risk. The colophon names only the request to come by day.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 308","lb":"A308","poet":"பிசிராந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவு வருவானைப் பகல் வருகென்றது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவு வருவானைப் பகல் வருகென்றது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["உழுவையொ","உழுவை",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["ஒருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",1,"maa","scene","tusker"],["அருவிப்","அருவி",4,"pira","scene",""],["சிறுதினைப்","தினை",10,"unavu","scene",""],["படுகிளி","கிளி",10,"pul","scene",""],["குவளை","குவளை",11,"maram","ornament",""],["பெருங்களிற்று","களிறு",15,"maa","simile",""],["குறிஞ்சி","குறிஞ்சி",16,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:16"},"a:309":{"book":"akananuru","num":310,"t":["வயவாள் எறிந்து வில்லின் நீக்கி","பயநிரை தழீஇய கடுங்கண் மழவர்","அம்புசேண் படுத்து வன்புலத்து உய்த்தெனத்","தெய்வஞ் சேர்ந்த பராரை வேம்பிற்","கொழுப்பா எறிந்து குருதி தூஉய்ப்","புலவுப் புழுக்குண்ட வான்கண் அகலறைக்","களிறுபுறம் உரிஞ்சிய கருங்கால் இலவத்து","அரலை வெண்காழ் ஆலியின் தாஅம்","காடுமிக நெடிய என்னார் கோடியர்","பெரும்படைக் குதிரை நற்போர் வானவன்","திருந்துகழற் சேவடி நசைஇப் படர்ந்தாங்கு","நாஞ்செலின் எவனோ- தோழி!- காம்பின்","விளைகழை உடைந்த கவண்விசைக் கடிஇடிக்","கனைசுடர் அமையத்து வழங்கல் செல்லாது","இரவுப்புனம் மேய்ந்த உரவுச்சின வேழம்","தண்பெரும் படாஅர் வெரூஉம்","குன்றுவிலங் கியவினவர் சென்ற நாட்டே?"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend, who has grown sad at seeing her change, why they should not set out, as wandering performers set out for the Chera Vaanavan's feet without counting the forest long, through a land where Mazhavar raiders drive off milch cattle and feast on a cow killed at the god's neem tree, to the country beyond the hills where he went, where a night-raiding elephant takes fright at dawn.","n":"She borrows the performers' courage: the gift-seekers cross the raiders' forest for a patron, so she can cross it for her man. The closing lines (the cracking bamboo, the sling and the elephant afraid of the thicket) are obscure to me and the reading of them is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","parisil","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 309","lb":"A309","poet":"கருவூர்க் கந்தப்பிள்ளைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளது வேறுபாடு கண்டு வேறுபட்ட தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"வேறுபட்ட தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மழவர்","மழவர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["பயநிரை","நிரை",2,"maa","scene","milch cattle"],["வேம்பிற்","வேம்பு",4,"maram","scene",""],["கொழுப்பா","ஆ",5,"maa","gift_food","cow"],["களிறுபுறம்","களிறு",7,"maa","scene",""],["இலவத்து","இலவம்",7,"maram","scene",""],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",9,"seythi","simile",""],["குதிரை","குதிரை",10,"maa","scene",""],["காம்பின்","காம்பு",12,"maram","scene",""],["வேழம்","வேழம்",15,"maa","scene",""],["படாஅர்","படர்",16,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","வானவன்",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:16"},"a:310":{"book":"akananuru","num":311,"t":["கடுந்தேர் இளையரொடு நீக்கி நின்ற","நெடுந்தகை நீர்மையை அன்றி நீயும்","தொழுதகு மெய்யை அழிவுமுந் துறுத்துப்","பன்னாள் வந்து பணிமொழி பயிற்றலின்","குவளை யுண்கண் கலுழ நின்மாட்டு","இவளும் பெரும்பே துற்றனள் ஓரும்","தாயுடை நெடுநகர்த் தமர்பா ராட்டக்","காதலின் வளர்ந்த மாதர் ஆகலின்","பெருமடம் உடையரோ சிறிதே; அதனால்","குன்றின் தோன்றும் குவவுமணற் சேர்ப்ப!","இன்றிவண் விரும்பா தீமோ! சென்றப்","பூவிரி புன்னை மீதுதோன்று பெண்ணைக்","கூஉம்கண் ணஃதே தெய்ய- ஆங்கண்","உப்பொய் உமணர் ஒழுகையொடு வந்த","இளைப்படு பேடை இரியக் குரைத்தெழுந்து","உருமிசைப் புணரி யுடைதரும்","பெருநீர் வேலிஎம் சிறுநல் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"Having agreed to help the man who has sent his chariot and men away and come humbly pleading for many days, the friend tells him that the girl too is deeply moved, but as a daughter raised in love in her mother's house she is still shy and innocent, so he must not press here today; let him go to the calling-place under the palmyra above the flowering punnai, near their small village fenced by the roaring sea.","n":"The friend grants his suit by naming a place, not by bringing the girl: the meeting is set within call of the village, where the girl's innocence and the friend's control are both kept. Where exactly she sends him (கூஉம்கண், the bird of line 15) is not clear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 310","lb":"A310","poet":"நக்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகற்குக் குறைநேர்ந்த தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","குறைநேர்ந்த தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குவளை","குவளை",5,"maram","simile",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",12,"maram","scene",""],["பெண்ணைக்","பெண்ணை",12,"maram","scene",""],["உப்பொய்","உப்பு",14,"pira","scene",""],["உமணர்","உமணர்",14,"seythi","scene",""],["பேடை","பேடை",15,"pul","scene",""],["புணரி","புணரி",16,"pira","scene","breakers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:16"},"a:311":{"book":"akananuru","num":312,"t":["இரும்பிடிப் பரிசிலர் போலக் கடைநின்று","அருங்கடிக் காப்பின் அகனகர் ஒருசிறை","எழுதி யன்ன திண்ணிலைக் கதவம்","கழுதுவழங்கு அரைநாள் காவலர் மடிந்தெனத்","திறந்துநப் புணர்ந்து 'நும்மிற் சிறந்தோர்","இம்மை உலகத்து இல்'லெனப் பன்னாள்","பொம்மல் ஓதி நீவிய காதலொடு","பயந்தலை பெயர்ந்து மாதிரம் வெம்ப","வருவழி வம்பலர்ப் பேணிக் கோவலர்","மழவிடைப் பூட்டிய குழாஅய்த் தீம்புளி","செவியடை தீரத தேக்கிலைப் பகுக்கும்","புல்லி நன்னாட்டு உம்பர் செல்லருஞ்","சுரமிறந்து ஏகினும் நீடலர்","அருண்மொழி தேற்றிநம் அகன்றிசி னோரே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend reminds the heroine how he once waited at the painted, guarded door like gift-seekers waiting for an elephant, came in at midnight when the guards slept, swore that no one in this world surpassed her and stroked her hair for days; so though he has crossed the scorched wasteland beyond Pulli's good land, where herdsmen share sweet tamarind food with travellers on teak leaves, the man who left her with gracious assurances will not stay long.","n":"The friend's proof is the past: a man who waited at the door like a suppliant for a gift will not linger away from the gift itself. The herdsmen's hospitality softens the road she fears.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","arul","thelivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 311","lb":"A311","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இரும்பிடிப்","பிடி",1,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["கழுது","கழுது",4,"pira","scene","ghoul"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",9,"seythi","scene",""],["மழவிடைப்","விடை",10,"maa","scene","young bull"],["தீம்புளி","புளி",10,"unavu","gift_food",""],["தேக்கிலைப்","தேக்கு",11,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","புல்லி",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:16"},"a:312":{"book":"akananuru","num":313,"t":["நெஞ்சுடன் படுதலின் ஒன்றுபுரிந் தடங்கி","இரவின் வரூஉம் இடும்பை நீங்க","வரையக் கருதும் ஆயின் பெரிதுவந்து","ஓங்குவரை இழிதரும் வீங்குபெயல் நீத்தம்","காந்தளஞ் சிறுகுடிக் கௌவை பேணாது","அரிமதர் மழைக்கண் சிவப்ப நாளைப்","பெருமலை நாடன் மார்புபுணை யாக","ஆடுகம் வம்மோ- காதலம் தோழி!","வேய்பயில் அடுக்கம் புதையக் கால்வீழ்த்து","இன்னிசை முரசின் இரங்கி ஒன்னார்","ஓடுபுறம் கண்ட தாள்தோய் தடக்கை","வெல்போர் வழுதி செல்சமத் துயர்த்த","அடுபுகழ் எஃகம் போலக்","கொடிபட மின்னிப் பாயின்றால் மழையே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend, letting the hero overhear, says that if he means to marry and end the hardship of night visits they will rejoice, and tomorrow, careless of the village's gossip, bathe in the flood coming down the hill with his chest for their float, for the rain has come down over the bamboo slopes, roaring like a drum and flashing lightning like the spear the victorious Pandya Vazhuthi raises in battle.","n":"The public bath with the lover is promised on a condition (வரையக் கருதும் ஆயின்): only marriage makes the gossip harmless. The storm borrows a king's victory to make the coming flood, and the union it stands for, triumphal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 312","lb":"A312","poet":"மதுரை மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி சொல்லியது. தலைமகள் சொல்லியதுமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி சொல்லியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைமகள் சொல்லியதுமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["காந்தளஞ்","காந்தள்",5,"maram","scene",""],["வேய்பயில்","வேய்",9,"maram","scene",""],["முரசின்","முரசு",10,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[["king","வழுதி",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:16"},"a:313":{"book":"akananuru","num":314,"t":["'இனிப்பிறி துண்டோ ? அஞ்சல் ஓம்பென!'","அணிக்கவின் வளர முயங்கி நெஞ்சம்","பிணித்தோர் சென்ற ஆறுநினைந்து அல்கலும்","குளித்துப்பொரு கயலிற் கண்பனி மல்க","ஐய வாக வெய்ய உயிரா","இரவும் எல்லையும் படரட வருந்தி","அரவுநுங்கு மதியின் நுதலொளி கரப்பத்","தம்மல தில்லா நம்மிவண் ஒழியப்","பொருள்புரிந்து அகன்றன ராயினும் அருள்புரிந்து","வருவர்- வாழி, தோழி!- பெரிய","நிதியஞ் சொரிந்த நீவி போலப்","பாம்பூன் தேம்பும் வறங்கூர் கடத்திடை","நீங்கா வம்பலர் கணையிடத் தொலைந்தோர்","வசிபடு புண்ணின் குருதி மாந்தி","ஒற்றுச்செல் மாக்களின் ஒடுங்கிய குரல","இல்வழிப் படூஉங் காக்கைக்","கல்லுயர் பிறங்கல் மலைஇறந் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine, who weeps night and day, sighs hot breaths and has lost her forehead's glow like a moon swallowed by the snake, that the man who held her close saying 'what else is there now? do not fear', though he left for wealth across rock-piled hills where starving snakes shrink like emptied purses and crows drink the blood of travellers shot down by bandits, will come back out of grace.","n":"The friend's comfort rests on his own quoted words (line 1); the road she describes in full is the danger she argues past. The crows that drink quietly, like spies in a deserted place, are the poem's chilling detail.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","arul","selvam","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 313","lb":"A313","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங்கடுகோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கயலிற்","கயல்",4,"pira","simile",""],["பாம்பூன்","பாம்பு",12,"pira","simile",""],["காக்கைக்","காக்கை",16,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:16"},"a:314":{"book":"akananuru","num":315,"t":["நீலத் தன்ன நீர்பொதி கருவின்","மாவிசும் பதிர முழங்கி ஆலியின்","நிலம்தண் ணென்று கானங் குழைப்ப","இனந்தேர் உழவர் இன்குரல் இயம்ப","மறியுடை மடப்பிணை தழீஇப் புறவின்","திரிமருப் பிரலை பைம்பயிர் உகள","ஆர்பெயல் உதவிய கார்செய் காலை","நூனெறி நுணங்கிய கானவில் புரவிக்","கல்லெனக் கறங்குமணி இயம்ப வல்லோன்","வாய்ச்செல வணக்கிய தாப்பரி நெடுந்தேர்","ஈர்ம்புறவு இயங்குவழி அறுப்பத் தீந்தொடைப்","பையுள் நல்யாழ் செவ்வழி பிறப்ப","இந்நிலை வாரார் ஆயின் தந்நிலை","எவன்கொல்? பாண! உரைத்திசிற் சிறிதெனக்","கடவுட் கற்பின் மடவோள் கூறச்","செய்வினை அழிந்த மையல் நெஞ்சில்","துனிகொள் பருவரல் தீர வந்தோய்!","இனிதுசெய் தனையால் வாழ்கநின் கண்ணி!","வேலி சுற்றிய வால்வீ முல்லைப்","பெருந்தார் கமழும் விருந்தொலி கதுப்பின்","இன்னகை இளையோள் கவவ","மன்னுக பெரும! நின் மலர்ந்த மார்பே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend welcomes the hero home from finished work: in the rains, as the stag frisked in the green crops with his doe and fawn and the lute played its evening melody of grief, his wife of godlike chastity had asked the bard what would become of her if he did not come now, and he arrived in time to end her grief; so may his garland flourish and his chest, held by the young woman whose hair smells of the white mullai on the fence, last for ever.","n":"The mullai on the fence comes back in her hair at the end: the flower of the land of waiting becomes the scent of the embrace. Her question is reported in her own words, so the friend's praise is also a record of how near the despair was.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","mazhai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 314","lb":"A314","poet":"மதுரை அளக்கர் ஞாழார் மகனார் அம்மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிப் புகுந்த தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றிப் புகுந்த தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["உழவர்","உழவர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["மறியுடை","மறி",5,"maa","iraicchi","fawn"],["மடப்பிணை","பிணை",5,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["திரிமருப் பிரலை","இரலை",6,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["பைம்பயிர்","பயிர்",6,"maram","scene",""],["புரவிக்","புரவி",8,"maa","scene",""],["நல்யாழ்","யாழ்",12,"yaazh","scene",""],["முல்லைப்","முல்லை",19,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","திரிமருப் பிரலை","confirmed","1/1","the season that keeps the deer family together is the season the husband was bound to come home to his wife"]],"ch":"a:16"},"a:315":{"book":"akananuru","num":316,"t":["'கூழையுங் குறுநெறிக் கொண்டன முலையும்","சூழி மென்முகஞ் செப்புடன் எதிரின","பெண்துணை சான்றனள் இவளெனப் பன்மாண்","கண்துணை ஆக நோக்கி நெருநையும்","அயிர்த்தன்று மன்னே நெஞ்சம்! பெயர்த்தும்","அறியா மையிற் செறியேன் யானே","பெரும்பெயர் வழுதி கூடல் அன்னதன்","அருங்கடி வியனகர்ச் சிலம்புங் கழியாள்","சேணுறச் சென்று வறுஞ்சுனைக்கு ஒல்கி","புறவுக்குயின்று உண்ட புன்காய் நெல்லிக்","கோடை யுதிர்த்த குவிகண் பசுங்காய்","அறுநூற் பளிங்கின் துளைக்காசு கடுப்ப","வறுநிலத் துதிரும் அத்தம் கதுமெனக்","கூர்வேல் விடலை பொய்ப்பப் போகிச்","சேக்குவள் கொல்லோ தானே- தேக்கின்","அகலிலை குவித்த புதல்போல் குரம்பை","ஊன்புழுக்கு அயரும் முன்றில்","கான்கெழு வாழ்நர் சிறுகுடி யானே"],"p":"","r":"The mother whose daughter has gone off with her lover blames herself: she saw the girl's hair curl and breasts swell and even yesterday suspected, yet did not shut her in; and now the girl, who had not yet stepped out of a house guarded like Vazhuthi's city of Koodal, follows the lying spearman over a summer path strewn with green gooseberries like crystal beads from a broken string, and will sleep, perhaps, in a forest hamlet where meat is cooked before huts like heaped teak leaves.","n":"I read சிலம்புங் கழியாள் (line 8) as 'she whose anklets were not yet removed': the girl has gone before the anklet ceremony that comes before a wedding, so the mother counts the rite the elopement skipped. She calls the young man a deceiver (பொய்ப்ப), which is her view, not the poem's verdict.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 315","lb":"A315","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட்போக்கிய தாய் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட்போக்கிய தாய் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வறுஞ்சுனைக்கு","சுனை",9,"pira","absent",""],["புறவுக்குயின்று","புறவு",10,"pul","scene","dove"],["நெல்லிக்","நெல்லி",10,"maram","scene",""],["பசுங்காய்","காய்",11,"maram","simile",""],["தேக்கின்","தேக்கு",15,"maram","simile",""],["ஊன்புழுக்கு","ஊன்",17,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வாழ்நர்","கான் வாழ்நர்",18,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[["king","வழுதி",7],["town","கூடல்",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:16"},"a:316":{"book":"akananuru","num":317,"t":["துறைமீன் வழங்கும் பெருநீர்ப் பொய்கை","அரிமலர் ஆம்பல் மேய்ந்த நெறிமருப்பு","ஈர்ந்தண் எருமைச் சுவல்படு முதுபோத்துத்","தூங்குசேற்று அள்ளல் துஞ்சிப் பொழுதுபடப்","பைந்நிண வராஅல் குறையப் பெயர்தந்து","குரூஉக்கொடிப் பகன்றை சூடி மூதூர்ப்","போர்ச்செறி மள்ளரிற் புகுதரும் ஊரன்","தேர்தர வந்த தெரிஇழை நெகிழ்தோள்","ஊர்கொள் கல்லா மகளிர் தரத்தரப்","பரத்தைமை தாங்கலோ இலனென வறிதுநீ","புலத்தல் ஒல்லுமோ?- மனைகெழு மடந்தை","அதுபுலந்து உறைதல் வல்லி யோரே","செய்யோள் நீங்கச் சில்பதங் கொழித்துத்","தாமட்டு உண்டு தமியர் ஆகித்","தேமொழிப் புதல்வர் திரங்குமுலை சுவைப்ப","வைகுநர் ஆகுதல் அறிந்தும்","அறியார் அம்மவஃது உடலு மோரே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend, having agreed to let in the hero, whose chariot keeps bringing courtesans, asks the wife whether she can afford to sulk in vain because he cannot give them up, since women who were able to keep up their sulking ended alone when fortune left them, sifting and cooking a little food for themselves while their sweet-voiced sons sucked shrunken breasts, and others still do not learn from it.","n":"Unusually blunt: the friend argues from the household's livelihood, not from love. The opening buffalo, which grazes the lilies, sleeps in the mud and comes home at dusk crowned with pakanrai, carries the hero's conduct without naming it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","inmai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 316","lb":"A316","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைமகற்கு வாயில் நேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகளை நெருங்கிச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தலைமகற்கு வாயில் நேர்ந்த தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["துறைமீன்","மீன்",1,"pira","scene",""],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",2,"maram","scene",""],["எருமைச்","எருமை",3,"maa","ullurai","buffalo"],["வராஅல்","வரால்",5,"pira","scene",""],["பகன்றை","பகன்றை",6,"maram","ornament",""],["மள்ளரிற்","மள்ளர்",7,"seythi","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","எருமைச்","confirmed","1/1","the husband who spends his days with courtesans and comes home at evening"]],"ch":"a:16"},"a:317":{"book":"akananuru","num":318,"t":["\"மாக விசும்பின் மழைதொழில் உலந்தெனப்","பாஅய் அன்ன பகலிருள் பரப்பிப்","புகைநிற உருவின் அற்சிரம் நீங்கக்","குவிமுகை முருக்கின் கூர்நுனை வைஎயிற்று","நகைமுக மகளிர் ஊட்டுகிர் கடுக்கும்","முதிராப் பல்லிதழ் உதிரப் பாய்ந்துடன்","மலருண் வேட்கையின் சிதர்சிதர்ந் துகுப்பப்","பொன்செய் கன்னம் பொலிய வெள்ளி","நுண்கோல் அறைகுறைந்து உதிர்வன போல","அரவ வண்டினம் ஊதுதொறுங் குரவத்து","ஓங்குசினை நறுவீ கோங்கலர் உறைப்பத்","துவைத்துஎழு தும்பி தவிர்இசை விளரி","உதைத்துவிடு நரம்பின் இம்மென இமிரும்","மரனே முற்ற காமர் வேனில்","வெயிலவிர் புரையும் வீததை மாஅத்துக்","குயிலிடு பூசல் எம்மொடு கேட்ப","வருவேம்\" என்ற பருவம் ஆண்டை","இல்லை கொல்லென மெல்ல நோக்கி","நினைந்தனம் இருந்தன மாகநயந் தாங்கு","உள்ளிய மருங்கின் உள்ளம் போல","வந்துநின் றனரே காதலர் நத்துறந்து","என்னுழி யதுகொல் தானே பன்னாள்","அன்னையும் அறிவுற அணங்கி","நன்னுதற் பாஅய பசலை நோயே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine that while they sat looking out and wondering whether the season he had named had failed to come where he was, the season he promised when he said he would come back to hear the koel in the mango with her, in lovely spring when the palasa buds redden like dyed nails and bees and humming insects shake down the kuravam and kongu blossoms, her lover has come and stands here, as swiftly as the heart goes to what it longs for; so where now is the pallor that spread on her forehead for so many days, until even her mother noticed?","n":"The long spring catalogue (lines 1 to 17) is the hero's own promise, quoted, so the description of the season is also the evidence that his word was kept. The final question treats the pallor as already gone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kaalam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 317","lb":"A317","poet":"வடமோதங்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் வரவுணர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தலைமகன் வரவுணர்ந்த தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["முருக்கின்","முருக்கு",4,"maram","simile",""],["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",10,"pira","scene",""],["குரவத்து","குரவம்",10,"maram","scene",""],["கோங்கலர்","கோங்கு",11,"maram","scene",""],["தும்பி","தும்பி",12,"pira","simile",""],["மாஅத்துக்","மா",15,"maram","simile","mango"],["குயிலிடு","குயில்",16,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:16"},"a:318":{"book":"akananuru","num":319,"t":["கான மானதர் யானையும் வழங்கும்","வான மீமிசை உருமுநனி உரறும்","அரவும் புலியும் அஞ்சுதக வுடைய","இரவழங்கு சிறுநெறி தமியை வருதி-","வரையிழி யருவிப் பாட்டொடு பிரசம","முழவுச்சேர் நரம்பின் இம்மென இமிரும்","பழவிறல் நனந்தலைப் பயமலை நாட!-","மன்றல் வேண்டினும் பெறுகுவை ஒன்றோ","இன்றுதலை யாக வாரல் வரினே","ஏமுறு துயரமொடு யாமிவண் ஒழிய","எற்கண்டு பெயருங் காலை யாழநின்","கற்கெழு சிறுகுடி எய்திய பின்றை","ஊதல் வேண்டுமாற் சிறிதே- வேட்டொடு","வேய்பயில் அழுவத்துப் பிரிந்தநின்","நாய்பயிர் குறிநிலை கொண்ட கோடே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the rich hills, who comes alone at night by a narrow path where elephants walk, thunder roars and snakes and tigers make it fearful, that marriage he can have if he wants it, but from today he must not come; and if he does come, then when he has left her and reached his own rocky village, he must blow the horn that calls together his hunting dogs, so that they, left here in anxiety, know he is safe.","n":"The ban and the care sit in one breath: the friend who forbids the visits still needs to hear the horn. மன்றல் வேண்டினும் பெறுகுவை makes marriage sound easily granted, which takes away his reason for the night road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kuri","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 318","lb":"A318","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி வந்த தலைமகனை வரவு விலக்கி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி வந்த தலைமகனை வரவு விலக்கி வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["யானையும்","யானை",1,"maa","scene",""],["உருமுநனி","உரும்",2,"pira","scene","thunder"],["அரவும்","அரவு",3,"pira","scene",""],["புலியும்","புலி",3,"maa","scene",""],["யருவிப்","அருவி",5,"pira","scene",""],["பிரசம","பிரசம்",5,"pira","simile","honeybee"],["வேய்பயில்","வேய்",14,"maram","scene",""],["நாய்பயிர்","நாய்",15,"maa","scene",""],["கோடே","கோடு",15,"yaazh","scene","horn"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:16"},"a:319":{"book":"akananuru","num":320,"t":["மணிவாய்க் காக்கை மாநிறப் பெருங்கிளை","பிணிவீழ் ஆலத் தலங்குசினை ஏறிக்","கொடுவில் எயினர் குறும்பிற் கூக்கும்","கடுவினை மறவர் வில்லிடத் தொலைந்தோர்","படுபிணங் கவரும் பாழ்படு நனந்தலை","அணங்கென உருத்த நோக்கின் ஐயென","நுணங்கிய நுசுப்பின் நுண்கேழ் மாமைப்","பொன்வீ வேங்கைப் புதுமலர் புரைய","நன்னிறத் தெழுந்த சுணங்கணி வனமுலைச்","சுரும்பார் கூந்தற் பெருந்தோள் இவள்வயிற்","பிரிந்தனிர் அகறல் சூழின் அரும்பொருள்","எய்துக மாதோ நுமக்கே கொய்குழைத்","தளிரேர் அன்ன தாங்கரு மதுகையள்","மெல்லியள் இளையள் நனிபேர் அன்பினள்","'செல்வேம்' என்னும் நும்மெதிர்","'ஒழிவேம்' என்னும் ஒண்மையோ இலளே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man who has announced his journey that if he means to leave her, the girl whose look strikes like a goddess, slim-waisted, her breasts freckled like new vengai blossom, for a wasteland where flocks of crows on the banyan call over the hunters' forts and carry off the corpses of travellers shot by raiders, then may he get his rare wealth: she, tender as a fresh shoot, young and full of love, has not even the boldness to answer his 'we will go' with 'then we will stay behind'.","n":"The blessing (எய்துக மாதோ நுமக்கே) is a curse in form. The two descriptions weigh against each other: the carrion road and her beauty. The last line may also be read as 'she cannot say she will die', since ஒழிவேம் can mean both; either way it is her silence that the friend speaks for.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 319","lb":"A319","poet":"எருக்காட்டூர்த் தாயம்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவுணர்த்திய தலைமகற்குத் தோழி செலவழுங்கச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,"செலவுணர்த்திய தலைமகற்குத் தோழி செலவழுங்கச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காக்கை","காக்கை",1,"pul","scene",""],["ஆலத்","ஆலம்",2,"maram","scene",""],["எயினர்","எயினர்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["மறவர்","மறவர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",8,"maram","simile","kino tree"],["சுரும்பார்","சுரும்பு",10,"pira","scene",""],["தளிரேர்","தளிர்",13,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:16"},"a:320":{"book":"akananuru","num":321,"t":["ஓங்குதிரைப் பரப்பின் வாங்குவிசைக் கொளீஇத்","திமிலோன் தந்த கடுங்கண் வயமீன்","தழைஅணி அல்குல் செல்வத் தங்கையர்","விழவுஅயர் மறுகின் விலைஎனப் பகரும்","கானல்அம் சிறுகுடிப் பெருநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப!","மலர்ஏர் உண்கண்எம் தோழி எவ்வம்","அலர்வாய் நீங்கநீ அருளாய் பொய்ப்பினும்","நெடுங்கழி துழைஇய குறுங்கால் அன்னம்","அடும்புஅமர் எக்கர் அம்சிறை உளரும்","தடவுநிலைப் புன்னைத் தாதுஅணி பெருந்துறை","நடுங்குஅயிர் போழ்ந்த கொடுஞ்சி நெடுந்தேர்","வண்டற் பாவை சிதைய வந்துநீ","தோள்புதிது உண்ட ஞான்றைச்","சூளும் பொய்யோ கடல்அறி கரியே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the shore, where the fishermen's catch is cried for sale by their well-dressed sisters in the festive streets, why he shows no grace to free her friend's grief from gossiping mouths: even if he lies now, was the oath also false that he swore on the day he first came in his tall chariot, its wheels crushing the girls' sand dolls, to the ford dusted with punnai pollen where the swan preens on the dunes, and first held her? The sea knows; it was the witness.","n":"The chariot that ruins the girls' sand dolls (line 12) on the day of the first meeting reads to me as the end of her girlhood, though nothing in the poem says so. Calling the sea as witness makes the shore itself the court where his oath stands.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","sool","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 320","lb":"A320","poet":"மதுரைக் கூலவாணிகன் சீத்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறிக்கண் வந்த தலைமகனைத் தோழி வரைவுகடாயது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகற்குறிக்கண் வந்த தலைமகனைத் தோழி வரைவுகடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["திமிலோன்","திமிலோன்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["வயமீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","scene",""],["தழைஅணி","தழை",3,"maram","ornament",""],["மலர்ஏர்","மலர்",6,"maram","simile",""],["அன்னம்","அன்னம்",8,"pul","scene","swan"],["அடும்புஅமர்","அடும்பு",9,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னைத்","புன்னை",10,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:16"},"a:321":{"book":"akananuru","num":322,"t":["பசித்த யானைப் பழங்கண் அன்ன","வறுஞ்சுனை முகந்த கோடைத் தெள்விளி","விசித்துவாங்கு பறையின் விடரகத்து இயம்பக்","கதிர்க்கால் அம்பிணை உணீஇய புகல்ஏறு","குதிர்க்கால் இருப்பை வெண்பூ உண்ணாது","ஆண்குரல் விளிக்கும் சேண்பால் வியன்சுரைப்","படுமணி இனநிரை உணீஇய கோவலர்","விடுநிலம் உடைத்த கலுழ்கண் கூவல்","கன்றுடை மடப்பிடி களிறொடு தடவரும்","புன்றலை மன்றத்து அம்குடிச் சீறூர்","துணையொடு துச்சில் இருக்கும் கொல்லோ?","கணையோர் அஞ்சாக் கடுங்கண் காளையொடு","எல்லி முன்னுறச் செல்லும் கொல்லோ?","எவ்வினை செயுங்கொல்? நோகோ யானே!-","அரிபெய்து பொதிந்த தெரிசிலம்பு கழீஇ","யாயறி வுறுதல் அஞ்சி","வேய்உயர் பிறங்கல் மலையிறந் தோளே!"],"p":"","r":"After the girl has eloped, her foster mother pictures the summer road of dry springs, a stag calling its doe to food, cowherds' muddy wells where elephants come with their calf, and asks whether the girl will rest in some hamlet shelter with her companion or walk on into the night with the bold youth, since she slipped off her pebble-filled anklets so her mother would not hear and crossed the bamboo hills.","n":"Taking off the anklets (சிலம்பு கழீஇ) is done so their sound will not betray her, but it is also the act that marks a girl's passage to marriage; here she performs it herself, in secret, so the sign of the wedding and the sign of the flight are one act.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","pirivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 321","lb":"A321","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய செவிலி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட் போக்கிய செவிலி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["யானைப்","யானை",1,"maa","simile","elephant"],["பறையின்","பறை",3,"parai","simile",""],["அம்பிணை","பிணை",4,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["புகல்ஏறு","ஏறு",4,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["இருப்பை","இருப்பை",5,"maram","scene",""],["இனநிரை","நிரை",7,"maa","scene","cattle"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["கூவல்","கூவல்",8,"pira","scene",""],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",9,"maa","scene",""],["களிறொடு","களிறு",9,"maa","scene",""],["தெரிசிலம்பு","சிலம்பு",15,"pira","ornament",""],["வேய்உயர்","வேய்",17,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","புகல்ஏறு","confirmed","1/1","the creatures of the road she took show a male who feeds his mate before himself, so the mother's fear holds a hope that the youth will so tend the girl"]],"ch":"a:17"},"a:322":{"book":"akananuru","num":323,"t":["வயங்குவெயில் ஞெமியப் பாஅய் மின்னுவசிபு","மயங்குதுளி பொழிந்த பானாட் கங்குல்","ஆராக் காமம் அடூஉநின்று அலைப்ப","இறுவரை வீழ்நரின் நடுங்கித் தெறுவரப்","பாம்புஎறி கோலிற் றமியை வைகி","தேம்புதி கொல்லோ?- நெஞ்சே! உருமிசைக்","களிறுகண் கூடிய வாள்மயங்கு ஞாட்பின்","ஒளிறுவேல் தானைக் கடுந்தேர்த் திதியன்","வருபுனல் இழிதரு மரம்பயில் இறும்பிற்","பிறையுறழ் மருப்பின் கடுங்கண் பன்றிக்","குறைஆர் கொடுவரி குழுமுஞ் சாரல்","அறையுறு தீந்தேன் குறவர் அறுப்ப","முயலுநர் முற்றா ஏற்றுஅரு நெடுஞ்சிமைப்","புகலரும் பொதியில் போலப்","பெறலருங் குரையள்எம் அணங்கி யோளே!"],"p":"","r":"Turning back at midnight in a storm from a tryst whose signal proved false, the man asks his heart whether it will languish alone, trembling like one falling from a broken cliff, and says the girl who torments him is as hard to win as Thithiyan's Pothiyil, whose honey-hung peak no climber can finish.","n":"The simile turns puram fame to akam use: Thithiyan's war elephants and spears are named before his hill, so Pothiyil is hard to enter not only for its steepness but because a fierce lord guards it, as her people guard her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kaamam","nenju","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 322","lb":"A322","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அல்லகுறிப்பட்டுப் போகின்ற தலைமகன் தன் னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","அல்லகுறிப்பட்டுப் போகின்ற தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["பாம்புஎறி","பாம்பு",5,"pira","simile","snake"],["களிறுகண்","களிறு",7,"maa","scene",""],["பன்றிக்","பன்றி",10,"maa","scene",""],["கொடுவரி","கொடுவரி",11,"maa","scene","tiger"],["தீந்தேன்","தேன்",12,"unavu","scene",""],["குறவர்","குறவர்",12,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[["king","திதியன்",8],["hill","பொதியில்",14]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:17"},"a:323":{"book":"akananuru","num":324,"t":["இம்மென் பேர் அலர் இவ்வூர் நம்வயின்","செய்வோர் ஏச்சொல் வாடக் காதலர்","வருவர் என்பது வாய்வ தாக","ஐய செய்ய மதனில சிறியநின்","அடிநிலன் உறுதல் அஞ்சிப் பையத்","தடவரல் ஒதுக்கம் தகைகொள இயலிக்","காணிய வம்மோ?- கற்புமேம் படுவி!-","பலவுப்பல தடைஇய வேய்பயில் அடுக்கத்து","யானைச் செல்லினம் கடுப்ப வானத்து","வயங்குகதிர் மழுங்கப் பாஅய்ப் பாம்பின்","பைபட இடிக்கும் கடுங்குரல் ஏற்றொடு","ஆலி அழிதுளி தலைஇக்","கால்வீழ்த் தன்றுநின் கதுப்புறழ் புயலே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend bids the heroine, whom she hails as one who excels in wifely virtue, to step out gently on her small feet and see: the cloud, dark as her hair, has come down over the bamboo slopes with thunder and hail like a moving herd of elephants, so the town's gossip will wither and the word that her lover will come will prove true.","n":"The friend reads the rain as proof because the returning season was his pledge. The colophon files the poem under paalai, but its matter is waiting through the promised rains, which porul 1.6 gives to mullai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","pirivu","alar","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 323","lb":"A323","poet":"புறநாட்டுப் பெருங் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது.","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பிரிவின்கண் வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பலவுப்பல","பலவு",8,"maram","scene",""],["வேய்பயில்","வேய்",8,"maram","scene",""],["யானைச்","யானை",9,"maa","simile","elephant"],["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",10,"pira","scene","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:17"},"a:324":{"book":"akananuru","num":325,"t":["விருந்தும் பெறுகுநள் போலும் திருந்திழைத்","தடமென் பணைத்தோள் மடமொழி அரிவை-","தளிரியல் கிள்ளை இனிதின் எடுத்த","வளராப் பிள்ளைத் தூவி அன்ன","வார்பெயல் வளர்த்த பைம்பயிர்ப் புறவில்","பறைக்கண் அன்ன நிறைச்சுனை தோறும்","துளிபடு மொக்குள் துள்ளுவன சாலத்","தொளிபொரு பொகுட்டுத் தோன்றுவன மாய","வளிசினை உதிர்த்தலின் வெறிகொள்பு தாஅய்ச்","சிரல்சிறகு ஏய்ப்ப அறற்கண் வரித்த","வண்டுண் நறுவீ துமித்த நேமி","தண்ணில மருங்கிற் போழ்ந்த வழியுள்","நிறைசெல் பாம்பின் விரைபுநீர் முடுகச்","செல்லும் நெடுந்தகை தேரே-","முல்லை மாலை நகர்புகல் ஆய்ந்தே!"],"p":"","r":"Reading their lord's mind after the campaign, his attendants say that his wife will now even have guests to feed, for his chariot is racing through the rain-fed forest, past brimming pools and flower-strewn sand, its wheels cutting tracks where the water runs like a snake, to reach town in the jasmine evening.","n":"விருந்தும், 'guests too': with the husband away the house could not receive guests, so his homecoming restores her duty as host as well as her joy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","ilvaazhkkai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 324","lb":"A324","poet":"ஒக்கூர் மாசாத்தியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன் கருத்துணர்ந்து உழையர் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["ilaiyar","pira",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன் கருத்துணர்ந்து உழையர் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கிள்ளை","கிள்ளை",3,"pul","simile",""],["பைம்பயிர்ப்","பயிர்",5,"maram","simile",""],["பறைக்கண்","பறை",6,"parai","simile",""],["சிரல்சிறகு","சிரல்",10,"pul","simile",""],["வண்டுண்","வண்டு",11,"pira","scene",""],["நறுவீ","வீ",11,"maram","scene",""],["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",13,"pira","simile","snake"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",15,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:17"},"a:325":{"book":"akananuru","num":326,"t":["அம்ம! வாழி தோழி! காதலர்","'வெண்மணல் நிவந்த பொலங்கடை நெடுநகர்,","நளிஇருங் கங்குல் புணர்குறி வாய்த்த","களவும் கைம்மிக அலர்ந்தன்று அன்னையும்","உட்கொண் டோ வாள் காக்கும் பிற்பெரிது","இவண்உறைபு எவனோ? அளியள்!' என்று அருவி","'ஆடுநடைப் பொலிந்த புகற்சியின், நாடுகோள்","அள்ளனைப் பணித்த அதியன் பின்றை","வள்ளுயிர் மாக்கிணை கண்ணவிந் தாங்கு","மலைகவின் அழிந்த கனைகடற்று அருஞ்சுரம்","வெய்ய மன்றநின் வைஎயிறு உணீஇய","தண்மழை ஒருநாள் தலைஇய ஒண்ணுதல்","ஒல்கியல் அரிவை நின்னொடு செல்கம்!","சில்நாள் ஆன்றனை யாகஎனப் பன்னாள்","உலைவில் உள்ளமொடு வினைவலி உறீஇ","எல்லாம் பெரும்பிறி தாக வடாஅது","நல்வேற் பாணன் நல்நாட்டு உள்ளதை","வாட்கண் வானத்து என்றூழ் நீள்இடை","ஆட்கொல் யானை அதர்பார்த்து அல்கும்","சோலை அத்தம் மாலைப் போகி","ஒழியச் சென்றோர் மன்ற","பழியெவன் ஆங்கொல் நோய்தரு பாலே?"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that her lover, seeing their secret love become gossip and her mother on guard, promised to take her with him once a day of cool rain had tamed the desert and asked her to wait a few days; instead, hardening his heart for his venture, he went alone at evening through the elephant-haunted waste toward Paanan's land in the north, and she asks what blame can fall on the fate that brings her this pain.","n":"The desert is measured by a fallen patron: the hill lost its waterfalls as Athiyan's great drum went dead after him. She lays the broken promise on fate (பால்), not on him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","udanpokku","alar","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 325","lb":"A325","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"1கொண்டு நீங்கக் கருதி யொழிந்த தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","கொண்டு நீங்கக் கருதி யொழிந்த தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",6,"pira","scene",""],["மாக்கிணை","கிணை",9,"parai","simile",""],["யானை","யானை",19,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["king","அள்ளனைப்",8],["king","அதியன்",8],["king","பாணன்",17]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:17"},"a:326":{"book":"akananuru","num":327,"t":["ஊரல் அவ்வாய் உருத்த தித்திப்","பேரமர் மழைக்கண் பெருந்தோட் சிறுநுதல்","நல்லள் அம்ம குறுமகள்- செல்வர்","கடுந்தேர் குழித்த ஞெள்ளல் ஆங்கண்","நெடுங்கொடி நுடங்கும் அட்ட வாயில்","இருங்கதிர்க் கழனிப் பெருங்கவின் அன்ன","நலம்பா ராட்டி நடையெழில் பொலிந்து","விழவிற் செலீஇயர் வேண்டும் வென்வேல்","இழையணி யானைச் சோழர் மறவன்","கழை யளந்து அறியாக் காவிரிப் படப்பைப்","புனன்மலி புதவிற் போஒர் கிழவோன்","பழையன் ஓக்கிய வேல்போற்","பிழையல கண்ணவள் நோக்கியோர் திறத்தே!"],"p":"","r":"Refusing the husband entry, the friend praises the young woman he courts, with her freckled lips, wide cool eyes and small forehead, lovely as the paddy fields by the bannered gate, says she must go to the festival in her finery, and adds that her eyes never miss whoever looks at them, as the spear Pazhaiyan of Por raised never missed.","n":"The praise is ironic: eyes that never miss make the husband a target, not a chooser. Who the குறுமகள் is turns on reading: most likely the rival, though the lines could praise the wife's own beauty as wasted on him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","kan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 326","lb":"A326","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகனை வாயில் மறுத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தோழி தலைமகனை வாயில் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["இருங்கதிர்க்","கதிர்",6,"maram","simile","paddy ear"],["யானைச்","யானை",9,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",9],["river","காவிரி",10],["town","போஒர்",11],["king","பழையன்",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:17"},"a:327":{"book":"akananuru","num":328,"t":["'இன்பமும் இடும்பையும் புணர்வும் பிரிவும்","நன்பகல் அமையமும் இரவும் போல","வேறுவேறு இயல ஆகி மாறெதிர்ந்து","உளவென உணர்ந்தனை ஆயின் ஒரூஉம்","இன்னா வெஞ்சுரம் நன்னசை துரப்பத்","துன்னலும் தகுமோ?- துணிவில் நெஞ்சே!-","நீசெல வலித்தனை ஆயின், யாவதும்","நினைதலும் செய்தியோ எம்மோ- கனைகதிர்","ஆவி அவ்வரி நீரென நசைஇ","மாதவப் பரிக்கும் மரல்திரங்கு நனந்தலைக்","களர்கால் யாத்த கண்ணகன் பரப்பிற்","செவ்வரை கொழிநீர் கடுப்ப அரவின்","அவ்வரி உரிவை அணவரும் மருங்கிற்","புற்றரை யாத்த புலர்சினை மரத்த","மைந்நிற உருவின் மணிக்கட் காக்கை","பைந்நிணங் கவரும் படுபிணக் கவலைச்","சென்றோர் செல்புறத்து இரங்கார் கொன்றோர்","கோல்கழிபு இரங்கும் அதர","பேய்பயில் அழுவம் இறந்த பின்னே"],"p":"","r":"The man rebukes his heart, which has resolved to go for wealth: if it knows that joy and grief, union and parting come by turns like midday and night, why should it cross a desert of mirages, withered marul and crows tearing at corpses, where killers pity no one and grieve only when their arrow misses; and if it goes, will it ever think of him again?","n":"The opening is a sage's premise, that joy and grief alternate, used not to counsel acceptance but to argue against the journey: if hardship comes anyway in its turn, there is no need to go seeking it for wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 327","lb":"A327","poet":"மருங்கூர்ப்பாகைச் சாத்தன் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் கடைக் கூட்டிய 2நெஞ்சினைக் கழறியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பொருள் கடைக் கூட்டிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["மாதவப்","மா",10,"maa","scene","deer"],["மரல்திரங்கு","மரல்",10,"maram","scene",""],["அரவின்","அரவு",12,"pira","simile","snake"],["காக்கை","காக்கை",15,"pul","scene",""],["பேய்பயில்","பேய்",19,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:17"},"a:328":{"book":"akananuru","num":329,"t":["வழையமல் அடுக்கத்து வலனேர்பு வயிரியர்","முழவதிர்ந் தன்ன முழக்கத்து ஏறோடு","உரவுப்பெயல் பொழிந்த நள்ளென் யாமத்து","அரவின் பைந்தலை இடறிப் பானாள்","இரவின் வந்தெம் இடைமுலை முயங்கித்","துனிகண் அகல வளைஇக் கங்குலின்","இனிதின் இயைந்த நண்பவர் முனிதல்","தெற்றுஆ குதல்நற்கு அறிந்தனம் ஆயின்","இலங்குவளை நெகிழப் பரந்துபடர் அலைப்பயாம்","முயங்குதொறும் முயங்குதொறும் உயங்க முகந்துகொண்டு","அடக்குவம் மன்னோ- தோழி!- மடப்பிடி","மழைதவழ் சிலம்பிற் கடுஞ்சூல் ஈன்று","கழைதின் யாக்கை விழைகளிறு தைவர","வாழையஞ் சிலம்பிற் றுஞ்சும்","சாரல் நாடன் சாயல் மார்பே!"],"p":"","r":"Speaking to her friend while the lover stands unseen nearby, the heroine says that had they known the man who came through the midnight storm, treading on snakes, to embrace her would grow weary of her, they would have held his chest again and again and never let it go: the chest of the man of the hill where a tusker strokes his mate as she sleeps among the plantains after calving.","n":"Spoken to be overheard, the regret is a warning: the lover learns that his nights are being read as waning love, and the elephant pair beside its new calf shows the settled life he should offer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","punarchi","iravu","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 328","lb":"A328","poet":"மதுரை பண்டவாணிகன் இளந்தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிச் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி சொல்லெடுப்பத் 1தலைமகள் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறிச் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி சொல்லெடுப்ப"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["வழையமல்","வழை",1,"maram","scene",""],["வயிரியர்","வயிரியர்",1,"seythi","simile",""],["முழவதிர்ந்","முழவு",2,"parai","simile",""],["அரவின்","அரவு",4,"pira","scene","snake"],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",11,"maa","iraicchi",""],["கழைதின்","கழை",13,"maram","scene",""],["விழைகளிறு","களிறு",13,"maa","iraicchi",""],["வாழையஞ்","வாழை",14,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","விழைகளிறு","confirmed","1/1","the man of that hill, overhearing, is shown a male who stays and tends the mother of his young, the married life the heroine's words press him toward"]],"ch":"a:17"},"a:329":{"book":"akananuru","num":330,"t":["பூங்கணும் நுதலும் பசப்ப நோய்கூர்ந்து","ஈங்கியான் வருந்தவும் நீங்குதல் துணிந்து","வாழ்தல் வல்லுநர் ஆயின் காதலர்","குவிந்த குரம்பை அங்குடிச் சீறூர்ப்","படுமணி இயம்பப் பகலியைந்து உமணர்","கொடுநுகம் பிணித்த செங்கயிற்று ஒழுகைப்","பகடுஅயாக் கொள்ளும் வெம்முனைத் துகள்தொகுத்து","எறிவளி சுழற்றும் அத்தம் சிறிதசைந்து","ஏகுவர் கொல்லோ தாமே.. பாய்கொள்பு","உறுவெரிந் ஒடிக்கும் சிறுவரிக் குருளை","நெடுநல் யானை நீர்நசைக் கிட்ட","கைகறித்து உரறும் மைதூங்கு இறும்பில்","புலிபுக்கு ஈனும் வறுஞ்சுனைப்","பனிபடு சிமையப் பன்மலை இறந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Her eyes and forehead pale with longing, the heroine tells her friend that if her lover can bear to live apart while she suffers, she wonders whether he will really go, resting only briefly, along the dusty road where salt merchants' carts wear out their oxen and over the hills of dry springs where a tigress whelps and a cub bites the trunk of a thirsty elephant.","n":"She voices no fear for him on that violent road, only the question whether he can bear to live apart: the danger is set down as the land's own law, and the doubt is about his heart.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 329","lb":"A329","poet":"உறையூர் முதுகூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது. பிரிவுணர்த்திய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவுணர்த்திய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["உமணர்","உமணர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["பகடுஅயாக்","பகடு",7,"maa","scene",""],["குருளை","குருளை",10,"maa","scene","tiger cub"],["யானை","யானை",11,"maa","scene","elephant"],["புலிபுக்கு","புலி",13,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:17"},"a:330":{"book":"akananuru","num":331,"t":["கழிப்பூக் குற்றுங் கானல் அல்கியும்","வண்டல் பாவை வரிமணல் அயர்ந்தும்","இன்புறப் புணர்ந்தும் இளிவரப் பணிந்தும்","தன்றுயர் வெளிப்பட தவறில் நம்துயர்","அறியா மையின் அயர்ந்த நெஞ்சமொடு","செல்லும் அன்னோ மெல்லம் புலம்பன்!","செல்வோன் பெயர்புறத்து இரங்கி முன்னின்று","தகைஇய சென்றவென் நிறையில் நெஞ்சம்","எய்தின்று கொல்லோ தானே? எய்தியும்","காமஞ் செப்ப நாணின்று கொல்லோ","உதுவ காணவர் ஊர்ந்த தேரே","குப்பை வெண்மணற் குவவுமிசை யானும்","எக்கர்த் தாழை மடல்வயி னானும்","ஆய்தொடிப் பாசடும்பு அரிய ஊர்பிழிபு","சிறுகுடிப் பரதவர் பெருங்கடன் மடுத்த","கடுஞ்செலற் கொடுந்திமில் போல","நிவந்துபடு தோற்றமொடு இகந்துமா யும்மே!"],"p":"","r":"Pleading the lover's case, the friend tells the heroine how he picked backwater flowers, lingered in the seaside grove, played at sand dolls, delighted them and humbled himself, and now goes away dejected because they have not understood his pain; her own unruly heart ran after him to stop him, and she wonders if it reached him and was too shy to speak, while his chariot over the white dunes rises and vanishes like a fisherman's boat in the open sea.","n":"The friend speaks of her own heart running after him, lending her longing to win the heroine's; the chariot sinking and rising among the dunes shows the lover already slipping out of reach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","nenju","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 330","lb":"A330","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகற்குக் குறைநேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குக் குறைநயப்பக் கூறியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தலைமகற்குக் குறைநேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குக் குறைநயப்பக் கூறியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["கழிப்பூக்","கழிப்பூ",1,"maram","scene",""],["தாழை","தாழை",13,"maram","scene",""],["பாசடும்பு","அடும்பு",14,"maram","scene",""],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",15,"seythi","simile",""],["கொடுந்திமில்","திமில்",16,"pira","simile","fishing boat"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:17"},"a:331":{"book":"akananuru","num":332,"t":["நீடுநிலை அரைய செங்குழை இருப்பைக்","கோடுகடைந் தன்ன கொள்ளை வான்பூ","ஆடுபரந் தன்ன ஈனல் எண்கின்","சேடுசினை உரீஇ உண்ட மிச்சில்","பைங்குழை தழையர் பழையர் மகளிர்","கண்திரள் நீள்அமைக் கடிப்பிற் றொகுத்து","குன்றகச் சிறுகுடி மறுகுதொறும் மறுகும்","சீறூர் நாடு பலபிறக் கெரழியச்","சென்றோர் அன்பிலர்- தோழி!- என்றும்","அருந்துறை முற்றிய கருங்கோட்டுச் சீறியாழ்ப்","பாணர் ஆர்ப்பப் பல்கலம் உதவி","நாளவை இருந்த நனைமகிழ் திதியன்","வேளிரொடு பொரீஇய கழித்த","வாள்வாய் அன்ன வறுஞ்சுரம் இறந்தே!"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that the man who left is without love: he went past many hill hamlets where women in leaf skirts carry from street to street the white iluppai flowers a bear has left, and across a waste as cruel as the sword Thithiyan, generous host of bards, drew against the Velir.","n":"Two kinds of giving frame her complaint: hill women live on what the bear leaves, and Thithiyan fills bards' hands in his daily court; the waste the lover crossed is likened to that same giver's sword, his other face.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 331","lb":"A331","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["இருப்பைக்","இருப்பை",1,"maram","scene",""],["ஆடுபரந்","ஆடு",3,"maa","simile","goat"],["எண்கின்","எண்கு",3,"maa","scene",""],["தழையர்","தழை",5,"maram","ornament",""],["பழையர்","பழையர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["நீள்அமைக்","அமை",6,"maram","scene",""],["சீறியாழ்ப்","யாழ்",10,"yaazh","scene",""],["பாணர்","பாணர்",11,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[["king","திதியன்",12],["king","வேளிரொடு",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:17"},"a:332":{"book":"akananuru","num":333,"t":["முளைவளர் முதல மூங்கில் முருக்கிக்","கிளையொடு மேய்ந்த கேழ்கிளர் யானை","நீர்நசை மருங்கின் நிறம்பார்த்து ஒடுங்கிய","பொருமுரண் உழுவை தொலைச்சிக் கூர்நுனைக்","குருதிச் செங்கோட்டு அழிதுளி கழாஅக்","கன்முகை அடுக்கத்து மென்மெல இயலிச்","செறுபகை வாட்டிய செம்மலொடு அறுகால்","யாழிசைப் பறவை இமிரப் பிடிபுணர்ந்து","வாழையம் சிலம்பில் துஞ்சும் நாடன்","நின்புரைத் தக்க சாயலன் எனநீ","அன்புரைத் தடங்கக் கூறிய இன்சொல்","வாய்த்தன- வாழி, தோழி!- வேட்டோர்க்கு","அமிழ்தத்து அன்ன கமழ்தார் மார்பின்","வண்டிடைப் படாஅ முயக்கமும்","தண்டாக் காதலும் தலைநாள் போன்மே!"],"p":"","r":"Speaking so the lover outside can hear, the heroine tells her friend that the words she once said lovingly, that the man of the hill where a tusker kills a tiger and then sleeps beside his mate among plantains is a man of grace fit for her, have come true: his embrace, which leaves no room even for a bee, and his love are as on the first day.","n":"Praise spoken for the lover's ears: the tusker that kills the tiger and then rests with his mate gives the hero both faces the heroine claims for him, protector and constant companion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kuri","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 332","lb":"A332","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிக்கண் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழிக்குத் 1தலைமகள் இயற்பட மொழிந்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறிக்கண் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழிக்குத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மூங்கில்","மூங்கில்",1,"maram","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","iraicchi","elephant"],["உழுவை","உழுவை",4,"maa","scene","tiger"],["பறவை","பறவை",8,"pira","scene","bee"],["பிடிபுணர்ந்து","பிடி",8,"maa","iraicchi",""],["வாழையம்","வாழை",9,"maram","scene",""],["கமழ்தார்","தார்",13,"maram","ornament",""],["வண்டிடைப்","வண்டு",14,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","யானை","not_confirmed","0/1","the man of that hill is one who both guards and stays, the grace the heroine says she found in him"],["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:17"},"a:333":{"book":"akananuru","num":334,"t":["'யாஅ ஒண்தளிர் அரக்குவிதிர்த் தன்னநின்","ஆக மேனி அம்பசப்பு ஊர","அழிவுபெரிது உடையை யாகி அவர்வயின்","பழிதலைத் தருதல் வேண்டுதி மொழிகொண்டு","தாங்கல் ஒல்லுமோ மற்றே ஆங்குநின்","எவ்வம் பெருமை உரைப்பின் செய்பொருள்","வயங்காது ஆயினும் பயம்கெடத் தூக்கி","நீடலர்- வாழி, தோழி!- கோடையிற்","குருத்திறுபு உக்க வருத்தம் சொலாது","தூம்புடைத் துய்த்தலைக் கூம்புபு திரங்கிய","வேனில் வெளிற்றுப்பனை போலக் கையெடுத்து","யானைப் பெருநிரை வானம் பயிரும்","மலைச்சேண் இகந்தனர் ஆயினும் நிலைபெயர்ந்து","நாள்இடைப் படாமை வருவர் நமர்எனப்","பயம்தரு கொள்கையின் நயம்தலை திரியாது","நின்வாய் இன்மொழி நன்வா யாக","வருவர் ஆயினோ நன்றே வாராது","அவணர் காதலர் ஆயினும் இவண்நம்","பசலை மாய்தல் எளிதுமன் தில்ல","சென்ற தேஎத்துச் செய்வினை முற்றி","மறுதரல் உள்ளத்தர் எனினும்","குறுகுபெரு நசையொடு தூதுவரப் பெறினே"],"p":"","r":"Answering a friend who scolds her for wasting with pallor and blaming him, and who says he would hurry home if told of her grief, the heroine prays the friend's sweet words come true, but says that even if he stays beyond the hills where elephants lift their trunks like withered summer palms, her pallor would easily fade if only a messenger came while he finishes his work, bringing his eager wish to return.","n":"She asks for less than his return, only a messenger: what wastes her is the silence, not the distance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","thoothu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 333","lb":"A333","poet":"கல்லாடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["யாஅ","யா",1,"maram","simile",""],["அரக்குவிதிர்த்","அரக்கு",1,"pira","simile",""],["வெளிற்றுப்பனை","பனை",11,"maram","simile",""],["யானைப்","யானை",12,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:17"},"a:334":{"book":"akananuru","num":335,"t":["ஓடா நல்லேற்று உரிவை தைஇய","ஆடுகொள் முரசம் இழுமென முழங்க","நாடுதிறை கொண்டனம் ஆயின் பாக!","பாடுஇமிழ் கடலின் எழுந்த சும்மையொடு","பெருங்களிற்றுத் தடக்கை புரையக் கால்வீழ்த்து","இரும்பிடித் தொழுதியின் ஈண்டுவன குழீஇ","வணங்கிறை மகளிர் அயர்ந்தனர் ஆடும்","கழங்குறழ் ஆலியொடு கதழுறை சிதறிப்","பெயல்தொடங் கின்றால் வானம் வானின்","வயங்குசிறை அன்னத்து நிரைபறை கடுப்ப","நால்குடன் பூண்ட கால்நவில் புரவிக்","கொடிஞ்சி நெடுந்தேர் கடும்பரி தவிராது","இனமயில் அகவும் கார்கொள் வியன்புனத்து","நோன்சூட்டு ஆழி ஈர்நிலம் துமிப்ப","ஈண்டே காணக் கடவுமதி பூங்கேழ்ப்","பொலிவன அமர்த்த உண்கண்","ஒலிபல் கூந்தல் ஆய்சிறு நுதலே!"],"p":"","r":"His campaign done and tribute taken, the hero tells his charioteer that the rains have begun, falling in streams like tuskers' trunks, with hail like girls' play-beads, and bids him drive the four horses, swift as a line of swans, without slackening through the peacock-filled fields, so that he may see at once his wife's kohl eyes, thick hair and small forehead.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 334","lb":"A334","poet":"மதுரைக் கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நல்லேற்று","ஏறு",1,"maa","scene","bull"],["முரசம்","முரசு",2,"parai","scene",""],["பெருங்களிற்றுத்","களிறு",5,"maa","simile",""],["இரும்பிடித்","பிடி",6,"maa","simile",""],["கழங்குறழ்","கழங்கு",8,"pira","simile",""],["அன்னத்து","அன்னம்",10,"pul","simile","swan"],["புரவிக்","புரவி",11,"maa","scene",""],["இனமயில்","மயில்",13,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:17"},"a:335":{"book":"akananuru","num":336,"t":["இருள்படு நெஞ்சத்து இடும்பை தீர்க்கும்","அருள்நன்கு உடையர் ஆயினும், ஈதல்","பொருள் இல்லோர்க்கு அஃது இயையாது ஆகுதல்","யானும் அறிவென் மன்னே யானைதன்","கொன்மருப்பு ஒடியக் குத்திச் சினஞ்சிறந்து","இன்னா வேனில் இன்றுணை ஆர","முளிசினை மராஅத்துக் பொளிபிளந்து ஊட்ட","புலம்புவீற் றிருந்த நிலம்பகு- வெஞ்சுரம்","அரிய அல்லமன் நமக்கே- விரிதார்","ஆடுகொள் முரசின் அடுபோர்ச் செழியன்","மாட மூதூர் மதிற்புறம் தழீஇ","நீடுவெயில் உழந்த குறியிறைக் கணைக்கால்","தொடைஅமை பன்மலர்த் தோடு பொதிந்து யாத்த","குடைஓ ரன்ன கோள்அமை எருத்திற்","பாளை பற்றிழிந்து ஒழியப் புறம் சேர்பு","வாள்வடித் தன்ன வயிறுடைப் பொதிய","நாளுறத் தோன்றிய நயவரு வனப்பின்","ஆரத்து அன்ன அணிகிளர் புதுப்பூ","வாருறு கவரியின் வண்டுண விரிய","முத்தின் அன்ன வெள்வீ தாஅய்","அலகின் அன்ன அரிநிறத்து ஆலி","நகைநனி வளர்க்குஞ் சிறப்பின் தகைமிகப்","பூவொடு வளர்ந்த மூவாப் பசுங்காய்","நீரினும் இனிய ஆகிக் கூர்எயிற்று","அமிழ்தம் ஊறும் செவ்வாய்","ஒண்தொடிக் குறுமகட் கொண்டனம் செலினே!"],"p":"","r":"The man grants his heart that even the most compassionate cannot give without wealth, but says the cracked, lonely summer waste, where a tusker breaks his tusk stripping marā bark to feed his mate, would not be hard for them if they went taking the bright-bangled girl, whose red mouth, sweeter than the young areca of Chezhiyan's walled old town, flows with nectar; and so he gives up going alone.","n":"The opening concedes the moral case for wealth in a maxim's terms: giving (ஈதல்) needs means. The answer is not a counter-maxim but the elephant, which provides for its mate at her side. Most of the poem is a description of the areca palm by Madurai's walls; how it maps onto her mouth is my reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","arul","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 335","lb":"A335","poet":"மதுரைத் தத்தங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பொருள்கடைக் கூட்டிய நெஞ்சினைக் கழறிச் செலவழுங்கியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","நெஞ்சினைக் கழறிச் செலவழுங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானைதன்","யானை",4,"maa","iraicchi","elephant"],["இன்றுணை","துணை",6,"maa","iraicchi","his mate"],["மராஅத்துக்","மரா",7,"maram","scene",""],["முரசின்","முரசு",10,"parai","scene",""],["பாளை","பாளை",15,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டுண","வண்டு",19,"pira","scene",""],["முத்தின்","முத்து",20,"pira","simile",""],["பசுங்காய்","காய்",23,"maram","scene","areca nut"]],"en":[["king","செழியன்",10]],"dv":[["iraicchi","யானைதன்","confirmed","2/2","the male provides for his mate at her side, at his own cost, so the man sees that providing need not mean leaving her"]],"ch":"a:17"},"a:336":{"book":"akananuru","num":337,"t":["குழற்காற் சேம்பின் கொழுமடல் அகலிலைப்","பாசிப் பரப்பிற் பறழொடு வதிந்த","உண்ணாப் பிணவின் உயக்கஞ் சொலிய","நாளிரை தரீஇய எழுந்த நீர்நாய்","வாளையொடு உழப்பத் துறைகலுழ்ந் தமையின்","தெண்கட் டேறல் மாந்தி மகளிர்","நுண்செயல் அம்குடம் இரீஇப் பண்பின்","மகிழ்நன் பரத்தைமை பாடி அவிழிணர்க்","காஞ்சி நீழற் குரவை அயரும்","தீம்பெரு பொய்கைத் துறைகேழ் ஊரன்","தேர்தர வந்த நேரிழை மகளிர்","ஏசுப என்பவென் நலனே அதுவே","பாகன் நெடிதுயிர் வாழ்தல் காய்சினக்","கொல்களிற்று யானை நல்கல் மாறே","தாமும் பிறரும் உளர்போல் சேறல்","முழவிமிழ் துணங்கை தூங்கும் விழவின்","யானவண் வாராமாறே வரினே வானிடைச்","சுடரொடு திரிதரும் நெருஞ்சி போல","என்னொடு திரியான் ஆயின், வென்வேல்","மாரி யம்பின் மழைத்தோற் சோழர்","வில்லீண்டு குறும்பின் வல்லத்துப் புறமிளை","ஆரியர் படையின் உடைகவென்","நேரிறை முன்கை வீங்கிய வளையே!"],"p":"","r":"The courtesan the lord now favours says, for the other mistress's companions to hear, that the women who came in his chariot mock her beauty, but they go about as if they mattered only because she stays away from the festival, as a mahout lives on only by the killer elephant's mercy; if she comes and the lord of the otter-haunted ford does not turn with her as the nerunji turns with the sun, may her bangles break as the Aryan army broke at Vallam.","n":"She stakes her bangles in the form of a warrior's oath on a Chola victory; but the bangle that slips or breaks is the sign of a woman abandoned, so the boast carries a fear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","nalam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 336","lb":"A336","poet":"பாவைக் கொட்டிலார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"நயப்புப்பரத்தை இற்பரத்தைக்குப் பாங்காயினார் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","pira","pira","karpu","நயப்புப்பரத்தை இற்பரத்தைக்குப் பாங்காயினார் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["சேம்பின்","சேம்பு",1,"maram","scene",""],["பாசிப்","பாசி",2,"pira","scene",""],["பிணவின்","பிணவு",3,"maa","iraicchi","female otter"],["நீர்நாய்","நீர்நாய்",4,"maa","iraicchi",""],["வாளையொடு","வாளை",5,"pira","scene",""],["டேறல்","தேறல்",6,"unavu","gift_food",""],["காஞ்சி","காஞ்சி",9,"maram","scene",""],["கொல்களிற்று","களிறு",14,"maa","simile",""],["முழவிமிழ்","முழவு",16,"parai","scene",""],["நெருஞ்சி","நெருஞ்சி",18,"maram","simile",""],["வளையே","வளை",23,"pira","ornament",""]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",20],["town","வல்லத்து",21],["battle","வல்லத்துப் புறமிளை",21]],"dv":[["iraicchi","நீர்நாய்","confirmed","1/1","in the lord's own town a male provides for a mate weak with young, which recalls the wife and child he owes, against which the courtesan's boast is made"]],"ch":"a:17"},"a:337":{"book":"akananuru","num":338,"t":["'சாரல் யாஅத்து உயர்சினை குழைத்த","மாரி ஈர்ந்தளிர் அன்ன மேனிப்","பேர்அமர் மழைக்கண் புலம்புகொண்டு ஒழிய","ஈங்குப்பிரிந்து உறைதல் இனிதன்று ஆகலின்","அவணதாகப் பொருள் என்று உமணர்","கணநிரை அன்ன பல்காற் குறும்பொறைத்","தூதொய் பார்ப்பான் மடிவெள் ளோலைப்","படையுடைக் கையர் வருதிறம் நோக்கி","'உண்ணா மருங்குல் இன்னோன் கையது","பொன்னா குதலும் உண்டு' எனக் கொன்னே","தடிந்துஉடன் வீழ்த்த கடுங்கண் மழவர்","திறன்இல் சிதாஅர் வறுமை நோக்கிச்","செங்கோல் அம்பினர் கைந்நொடியாப் பெயரக்","கொடிவிடு குருதித் தூங்குகுடர் கறீஇ","வரிமரல் இயவின் ஒருநரி ஏற்றை","வெண்பரல் இமைக்கும் கண்பறி கவலைக்","கள்ளி நீழற் கதறுபு வதிய","மழைகண் மாறிய வெங்காட்டு ஆர்இடை","எமியம் கழிதந் தோயே- பனிஇருள்","பெருங்கலி வானம் தலைஇய","இருங்குளிர் வாடையொடு வருந்துவள் எனவே"],"p":"","r":"When his heart again presses him to go for wealth, the man reminds it how once, finding it no joy to stay apart from her, it said 'let wealth stay where it is' and came back alone through the burnt, rainless waste where fierce Mazhavar cut down a starving brahmin messenger in case he carried gold, left in disgust at his rags, and a jackal dragged his entrails into the kalli shade, all because it feared she would suffer when the cold north wind came with the rains.","n":"The murdered messenger is the poem's argument: on the wealth road a man is killed for what he might carry and found to carry nothing. The colophon names the speaker தலைமகள், but the one who went, came back and is tempted again is the man.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","selvam","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 337","lb":"A337","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"முன்னொரு காலத்துப் பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்துவந்த தலைமகள் பின்னும் பொருள் கடைக் கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பின்னும் பொருள் கடைக் கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["யாஅத்து","யா",1,"maram","simile",""],["உமணர்","உமணர்",5,"seythi","simile",""],["பார்ப்பான்","பார்ப்பான்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["மழவர்","மழவர்",11,"seythi","scene",""],["வரிமரல்","மரல்",15,"maram","scene",""],["நரி","நரி",15,"maa","scene",""],["கள்ளி","கள்ளி",17,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:17"},"a:338":{"book":"akananuru","num":339,"t":["குன்றேங்கு வைப்பின் நாடுமீக் கூறும்","மறம்கெழு தானை அரச ருள்ளும்","அறம்கடைப் பிடித்த செங்கோ லுடன்அமர்","மறம்சாய்த்து எழுந்த வலன்உயர் திணிதோள்","பலர்புகழ் திருவின் பசும்பூட் பாண்டியன்","அணங்குடை உயர்நிலைப் பொறுப்பின் கவாஅன்","'சினையொண் காந்தள் நாறும் நறுநுதல்","துணைஈர் ஓதி மாஅ யோள்வயின்","நுண்கோல் அவிர்தொடி வண்புறஞ் சுற்ற","முயங்கல் இயையாது ஆயினும் என்றும்","வயவுஉறு நெஞ்சத்து உயவுத்துணை யாக","ஒன்னார் தேஎம் பாழ்பட நூறும்","துன்னருந் துப்பின் வென்வேற் பொறையன்","அகலிருங் கானத்துக் கொல்லி போலத்","தவாஅ லியரே நட்பே அவள்வயின்","அறாஅ லியரே தூதே- பொறாஅர்","விண்பொரக் கழித்த திண்பிடி ஒள்வாள்","புனிற்றான் தரவின் இளையர் பெருமகன்","தொகுபோர்ச் சோழன் பொருள்மலி பாக்கத்து","வழங்கல் ஆனாப் பெருந்துறை","முழங்குஇரு முந்நீர்த் திரையினும் பலவே!"],"p":"","r":"After a tryst that failed, the man tells his heart that even if he cannot have the embrace of the dark girl whose forehead smells of kaanthal on the slope of the Pandyan's sacred hill, may their love last like the Porai's Kolli and may messages to her never stop, more numerous than the waves at the Chola's busy port.","n":"Each crowned house lends a measure: the Pandyan's hill for where she lives, the Chera's Kolli for how long love should last, the Chola's sea for how many messages; what he asks is modest, that love and messages last, not union.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kuri","thoothu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 338","lb":"A338","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அல்ல குறிப்பிட்ட தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","அல்ல குறிப்பிட்ட தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",7,"maram","scene",""],["புனிற்றான்","ஆன்",18,"maa","scene","cow"]],"en":[["king","பாண்டியன்",5],["king","பொறையன்",13],["hill","கொல்லி",14],["king","சோழன்",19]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:17"},"a:339":{"book":"akananuru","num":340,"t":["வீங்குவிசைப் பிணித்த விரைபரி நெடுந்தேர்","நோன்கதிர் சுமந்த ஆழிஆழ் மருங்கிற்","பாம்புஎன முடுகுநீர் ஓடக் கூம்பிப்","பற்றுவிடு விரலின் பயறுகாய் ஊழ்ப்ப","அற்சிரம் நின்றன்றாற் பொழுதே முற்பட","ஆள்வினைக்கு எழுந்த அசைவில் உள்ளத்து","ஆண்மை வாங்கக் காமம் தட்பக்","கவைபடு நெஞ்சம் கட்கண் அகைய","இருதலைக் கொள்ளி இடைநின்று வருந்தி","ஒருதலைப் படாஅ உறவி போன்றனம்","நோம்கொல்? அளியள் தானே- யாக்கைக்கு","உயிர்இயைந் தன்ன நட்பின் அவ்வுயிர்","வாழ்தல் அன்ன காதல்","சாதல் அன்ன பிரிவுஅரி யோளே"],"p":"","r":"Setting out as the cold season comes, when wheel ruts run with water like a snake and pulse pods split like loosening fingers, the man tells his heart that, pulled by manly duty and held by love, he is like an ant on a firebrand burning at both ends, and wonders whether she will suffer, she whose bond with him is as life joined to the body, whose love is like living and whose parting is like death.","n":"Both ends of the firebrand burn, so the middle is no refuge: duty and love are equally fire. The closing triad gathers in three similes what the Kural says in two couplets (1122, 1124).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","uyir","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 339","lb":"A339","poet":"நரைமுடி நெட்டையர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"போகா நின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","போகா நின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["பாம்புஎன","பாம்பு",3,"pira","simile","snake"],["பயறுகாய்","பயறு",4,"maram","simile",""],["உறவி","உறவி",10,"pira","simile","ant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:17"},"a:340":{"book":"akananuru","num":341,"t":["பன்னாள் எவ்வம் தீரப் பகல்வந்து","புன்னைஅம் பொதும்பின் இன்னிழற் கழிப்பி","மாலை மால்கொள நோக்கிப் பண்ஆய்ந்து","வலவன் வண்தேர் இயக்க நீயும்","செலவுவிருப் புறுதல் ஒழிகதில் அம்ம","'செல்லா நல்லிசைப் பொலம்பூண் திரையன்","பல்பூங் கானற் பவத்திரி அனவிவள்","நல்லெழில் இளநலம் தொலைய ஒல்லெனக்","கழியே ஓதம் மல்கின்று வழியே","வள்ளெயிற்று அரவொடு வயமீன் கொட்கும்","சென்றோர் மன்ற மான்றன்று பொழுது' என","நின்திறத்து அவலம் வீட இன்றிவண்","சேப்பின் எவனோ- பூக்கேழ் புலம்ப-","பசுமீன் நொடுத்த வெந்நெல் மாஅத்","தயிர்மிதி மிதவை ஆர்த்துவம் நினக்கே","வடவர் தந்த வான்கேழ் வட்டம்","குடபுல உறுப்பின் கூட்டுபு நிகழ்த்திய","வண்டிமிர் நறுஞ்சாந்து அணிகுவம்- திண்திமில்","எல்லுத்தொழின் மடுத்த வல்வினைப் பரதவர்","கூர்உளிக் கடுவிசை மாட்டலின் பாய்புடன்","கோட்சுறாக் கிழித்த கொடுமுடி நெடுவலை","தண்கடல் அசைவளி எறிதொறும் வினைவிட்டு","முன்றில் தாழைத் தூங்கும்","தெண்கடற் பரப்பினெம் உறைவின் ஊர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"As evening falls after a day meeting in the punnai shade, the friend asks the lover to give up leaving, since the tide has filled the backwaters, snakes and fierce fish crowd the path and the girl, lovely as Thiraiyan's Pavaththiri, is fading, and to stay the night in their fishing village, where they will feed him rice and curd porridge bought with fresh fish and anoint him with sandal paste, where the shark-torn nets hang idle on the screwpine.","n":"Fed and anointed as a guest, the lover would be received openly by her people: the invitation to stay is the friend's push toward marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","virundhu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 340","lb":"A340","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற் குறிக்கண் தோழி தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகற் குறிக்கண் தோழி தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["புன்னைஅம்","புன்னை",2,"maram","scene",""],["அரவொடு","அரவு",10,"pira","scene","snake"],["வயமீன்","மீன்",10,"pira","scene","crocodile"],["பசுமீன்","மீன்",14,"pira","scene","fresh fish"],["வெந்நெல்","நெல்",14,"unavu","gift_food",""],["மிதவை","மிதவை",15,"unavu","gift_food",""],["நறுஞ்சாந்து","சாந்து",18,"pira","ornament",""],["வண்டிமிர்","வண்டு",18,"pira","scene",""],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",19,"seythi","scene",""],["கோட்சுறாக்","சுறா",21,"pira","scene",""],["தாழைத்","தாழை",23,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","திரையன்",6],["town","பவத்திரி",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:17"},"a:341":{"book":"akananuru","num":342,"t":["உய்தகை இன்றால்- தோழி- பைபயக்","கோங்கும் கொய்குழை உற்றன குயிலும்","தேம்பாய் மாஅத்து ஓங்குசினை விளிக்கும்","நாடுஆர் காவிரிக் கோடுதோய் மலிர்நிறைக்","கழைஅழி நீத்தம் சாஅய வழிநாள்","மழைகழிந் தன்ன மாக்கால் மயங்குஅறல்","பதவுமேயல் அருந்து துளங்குஇமில் நல்லேறு","மதவுடை நாகொடு அசைவீடப் பருகி","குறுங்காற் காஞ்சிக் கோதை மெல்லிணர்ப்","பொன்தகை நுண்தாது உறைப்பத் தொக்குஉடன்","குப்பை வார்மணல் எக்கர்த் துஞ்சும்","யாணர் வேனில்மன் இது-","மாண்நலம் நுகரும் துணையுடை யோர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend there is no way through: the kongu has leaves fit to pluck, the koel calls from the mango, and on the Kaveri's sand after the flood has fallen the bull drinks with his cow and sleeps with her under the kaanji's falling pollen; this rich season is for those who have their partner beside them.","n":"The last line turns the whole riverside scene into a list of what she lacks: every couple in it, animal and human, has a companion to enjoy the season with. The season's signs are spring's, so the poem implies that he had promised to be back by now.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 341","lb":"A341","poet":"ஆவூர் மூலங்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவின்கட் டோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவின்கட் டோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கோங்கும்","கோங்கு",2,"maram","scene",""],["குயிலும்","குயில்",2,"pul","scene",""],["மாஅத்து","மா",3,"maram","scene","mango"],["கழைஅழி","கழை",5,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பதவுமேயல்","பதவு",7,"maram","scene","grass"],["நல்லேறு","ஏறு",7,"maa","iraicchi","bull"],["நாகொடு","நாகு",8,"maa","iraicchi","cow"],["காஞ்சிக்","காஞ்சி",9,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["river","காவிரிக்",4]],"dv":[["iraicchi","நல்லேறு","confirmed","1/1","even the cattle spend the season with their mates, while her husband stays away"]],"ch":"a:18"},"a:342":{"book":"akananuru","num":343,"t":["ஒறுப்ப ஓவலை நிறுப்ப நில்லலை","புணர்ந்தோர் போலப் போற்றுமதி நினக்குயான்","கிளைஞன் அல்லனோ- நெஞ்சே- தெனாஅது","வெல்போர்க் கவுரியர் நல்நாட்டு உள்ளதை","மண்கொள் புற்றத்து அருப்புஉழை திறப்பின்","ஆகொள் மூதூர்க் கள்வர் பெருமகன்","ஏவல் இளையர் தலைவன் மேவார்","அருங்குறும்பு எறிந்த ஆற்றலொடு பருந்துபடப்","பல்செருக் கடந்த செல்லுறழ் தடக்கை","கெடாஅ நல்இசைத் தென்னன் தொடாஅ","நீர்இழி மருங்கில் கல்லளைக் கரந்தஅவ்","வரையர மகளிரின் அரியள்","அவ்வரி அல்குல் அணையாக் காலே!"],"p":"","r":"The hero rebukes his heart, which neither stops when checked nor stays when held: am I not your kinsman? Cherish me as if we had already embraced; she is harder to reach than the mountain goddesses hidden in the rock caves of the untouched waterfall, in the land of the victorious Pandya, while I cannot lie on her lovely waist.","n":"The long middle (lines 4 to 11) praises the Pandya, called தென்னன் and of the கவுரியர் line, as the destroyer of a cattle-raiders' stronghold; it serves only to place the cave of the hill goddesses, the image of a woman out of reach. The poem is the hero's half of the kalavu when the friend has said no.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kalaviyal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 342","lb":"A342","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அல்ல குறிப்பிட்ட தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","அல்ல குறிப்பிட்ட தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆகொள்","ஆ",6,"maa","scene","cattle"],["புற்றத்து","புற்று",5,"pira","scene","ant-hill"],["வரையர மகளிரின்","வரையர மகளிர்",12,"deyvam","simile",""]],"en":[["king","கவுரியர்",4],["king","தென்னன்",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:18"},"a:343":{"book":"akananuru","num":344,"t":["வாங்குஅமை புரையும் வீங்குஇறைப் பணைத்தோள்","சில்சுணங்கு அணிந்த பல்பூண் மென்முலை","நல்லெழில் ஆகம் புல்லுதல் நயந்து","மரம்கோள் உமண்மகன் பெயரும் பருதிப்","புன்தலை சிதைத்த வன்தலை நடுகல்","கண்ணி வாடிய மண்ணா மருங்குல்","கூர்உளி குயின்ற கோடுமாய் எழுத்துஅவ்","ஆறுசெல் வம்பலர் வேறுபயம் படுக்கும்","கண்பொரி கவலைய கானத்து ஆங்கண்","நனந்தலை யாஅத்து அம்தளிர்ப் பெருஞ்சினை","இல்போல் நீழல் செல்வெயில் ஒழிமார்","நெடுஞ்செவிக் கழுதைக் குறுங்கால் ஏற்றைப்","புறம்நிறை பண்டத்துப் பொறைஅசாஅக் களைந்த","பெயர்படை கொள்ளார்க்கு உயவுத்துணை ஆகி","உயர்ந்த ஆள்வினை புரிந்தோய் பெயர்ந்துநின்று","உள்ளினை- வாழிஎன் நெஞ்சே- கள்ளின்","மகிழின் மகிழ்ந்த அரிமதர் மழைக்கண்","சின்மொழிப் பொலிந்த துவர்வாய்ப்","பன்மாண் பேதையின் பிரிந்தநீயே"],"p":"","r":"On the road for wealth, the hero tells his heart, which wants to turn back to the girl with shoulders like bamboo, that it has taken up a high endeavour and should not now look back: the path runs past a hero-stone whose top was chipped by the salt-carriers' cart wheels, its garland withered and its letters worn, through a forest where under the yaa's shade the salt-traders unload their donkeys.","n":"The hero-stone with its worn inscription, which travellers use for another purpose (வேறுபயம்), is part of what the heart must pass: the dead are marks on a trade road. Lines 13 and 14 (the traders' load and the companion in distress) are compressed and their construction is not certain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","nenju","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 343","lb":"A343","poet":"மதுரை மருத னிளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் இடைச்சுரத்து மீளக் கருதிய நெஞ்சினைக் கழறிப்போயது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"இடைச்சுரத்து மீளக் கருதிய நெஞ்சினைக் கழறிப்போயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["உமண்மகன்","உமணன்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["யாஅத்து","யா",10,"maram","scene",""],["கழுதைக்","கழுதை",12,"maa","scene",""],["அமை","அமை",1,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:18"},"a:344":{"book":"akananuru","num":345,"t":["வளமழை பொழிந்த வால்நிறக் களரி","உளர்தரு தண்வளி உறுதொறும் நிலவெனத்","தொகுமுகை விரிந்த முடககாற் பிடவின்","வைஏர் வால்எயிற்று ஒள்நுதல் மகளிர்","கைமாண் தோணி கடுப்பப் பையென","மயிலினம் பயிலும் மரம்பயில் கானம்","எல்லிடை உறாஅ அளவை வல்லே","கழல்ஒளி நாவின் தெண்மணி கறங்க","நிழல்ஒலிப் பன்ன நிமிர்பரிப் புரவி","வயக்குஉறு கொடிஞ்சி பொலிய வள்பு ஆய்ந்து","இயக்குமதி- வாழியோ கையுடை வலவ!","பயப்புறு படர்அட வருந்திய","நயப்புஇன் காதலி நகைமுகம் பெறவே"],"p":"","r":"His task done, the hero tells his charioteer to drive fast, bells ringing and horses leaping, through the forest where rain has fallen on the white saline ground, the pidavu buds open like moonlight in the cool wind and peacocks gather, so that before evening falls his beloved, worn with the pallor of longing, may show a smiling face.","n":"The rain season has come, which is the time he promised, so speed is a matter of keeping his word as much as of desire. The peacocks are compared to something the bright-browed women make or play with (கைமாண் தோணி), and that object is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 344","lb":"A344","poet":"மதுரை அளக்கர் ஞாழார் மகனார் மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றிய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றிய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பிடவின்","பிடவு",3,"maram","scene",""],["மயிலினம்","மயில்",6,"pul","simile",""],["புரவி","புரவி",9,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:18"},"a:345":{"book":"akananuru","num":346,"t":["'விசும்புதளி பொழிந்து வெம்மை நீங்கித்","தண்பதம் படுதல் செல்கெனப் பன்மாண்","நாம்செல விழைந்தன மாக' ஓங்குபுகழ்க்","கான்அமர் செல்வி அருளலின் வெண்கால்","பல்படைப் புரவி எய்திய தொல்லிசை","நுணங்குநுண் பனுவற் புலவன் பாடிய","இனமழை தவழும் ஏழில் குன்றத்துக்","கருங்கால் வேங்கைச் செம்பூம் பிணையல்","ஐதுஏந்து அல்குல் யாம் அணிந்து உவக்கும்","சின்னாள் கழிக! என்று முன்னாள்","நம்மொடு பொய்த்தனர் ஆயினும் தம்மொடு","திருந்துவேல் இளையர் சுரும்புண மலைமார்","மாமுறி ஈன்று மரக்கொம்பு அகைப்ப","உறைகழிந்து உலந்த பின்றைப் பொறைய","சிறுவெள் அருவித் துவலையின் மலர்ந்த","கருங்கால் நுணவின் பெருஞ்சினை வான்பூச்","செம்மணற் சிறுநெறி கம்மென வரிப்பக்","காடுகவின் பெறுக- தோழி- ஆடுவளிக்கு","ஒல்குநிலை இற்றி ஒருதனி நெடுவீழ்","கல்கண் சீக்கும் அத்தம்","அல்குவெயில் நீழல் அசைந்தனர் செலவே"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that he promised to go only when rain had cooled the land, and to spend a few days first with her wearing vengai garlands from Ezhil hill, which a famous poet sang; though he has broken that word, she wishes his road well: may the forest grow lovely with nunavu flowers on the red sand path and trees putting out dark shoots for his young spearmen to wear, and may they rest in the itti's shade.","n":"Complaint turns to blessing in the middle of the poem: she names his lie and in the same breath prays that the road be cool for him. Ezhil hill is fixed by its fame in song (a poet given horses by the grace of the forest goddess), so a place in praise poetry measures her lost happiness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 345","lb":"A345","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["செல்வி","கான் அமர் செல்வி",4,"deyvam","scene",""],["புரவி","புரவி",5,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["வேங்கைச்","வேங்கை",8,"maram","ornament",""],["மாமுறி","மா",13,"maram","ornament","mango"],["நுணவின்","நுணா",16,"maram","scene",""],["இற்றி","இற்றி",19,"maram","scene",""],["அருவித்","அருவி",15,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["hill","ஏழில் குன்றத்து",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:18"},"a:346":{"book":"akananuru","num":347,"t":["நகைநன்று அம்ம தானே- இறைமிசை","மாரிச் சுதையின் ஈர்ம்புறத்து அன்ன","கூரல் கொக்கின் குறும்பறைச் சேவல்","வெள்ளி வெண்தோடு அன்ன கயல்குறித்துக்","கள்ளார் உவகைக் கலிமகிழ் உழவர்","காஞ்சிஅம் குறுந்தறி குத்தித் தீஞ்சுவை","மென்கழைக் கரும்பின் நன்பல மிடைந்து","பெருஞ்செய் நெல்லின் பாசவல் பொத்தி","வருத்திக் கொண்ட வல்வாய்க் கொடுஞ்சிறை","மீதுஅழி கடுநீர் நோக்கிப் பைப்பயப்","பார்வல் இருக்கும் பயம்கேழ் ஊர-","யாம்அது பேணின்றோ இலமே- நீ நின்","பண்ணமை நல்யாழ்ப் பாணனொடு விசிபிணி","மண்ணார் முழவின் கண்ணதிர்ந்து இயம்ப","மகிழ்துணைச் சுற்றமொடு மட்டு மாந்தி","எம்மனை வாரா யாகி முன்னாள்","நும்மனைச் சேர்ந்த ஞான்றை அம்மனைக்","குறுந்தொடி மடந்தை உவந்தனள்- நெடுந்தேர்","இழையணி யானைப் பழையன் மாறன்","மாடமலி மறுகின் கூடல் ஆங்கண்","வெள்ளத் தானையொடு வேறுபுலத்து இறுத்த","கிள்ளி வளவன் நல்அமர் சாஅய்க்","கடும்பரிப் புரவியொடு களிறுபல வவ்வி","ஏதின் மன்னர் ஊர்கொளக்","கோதை மார்பன் உவகையிற் பெரிதே"],"p":"","r":"The friend turns the husband away with a laugh: lord of the town where the heron, its back like wet plaster, waits watching the overflow of the dam the toddy-glad farmers built of kaanji stakes, sugarcane and sod, eyeing the silver kayal fish, we no longer care; the day you went to her house with your lute-player, drum and drinking friends, that bangled girl rejoiced more than the Chera with the kothai garland when Pazhaiyan Maran routed Killi Valavan's army at Koodal and seized his horses and elephants.","n":"The scale of the other woman's joy is a historical defeat: a Chola camped before Madurai, beaten, his horses and elephants taken, and a third king delighting in it. The friend's mockery (நகைநன்று) frames both the heron and the victory as the rival's gains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 346","lb":"A346","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகற்கு வாயின் மறுத்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தோழி தலைமகற்கு வாயின் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["கொக்கின்","கொக்கு",3,"pul","ullurai",""],["கயல்குறித்துக்","கயல்",4,"pira","ullurai","fish"],["உழவர்","உழவர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["காஞ்சிஅம்","காஞ்சி",6,"maram","scene",""],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",7,"maram","scene",""],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",8,"unavu","scene",""],["யாழ்ப்","யாழ்",13,"yaazh","scene",""],["முழவின்","முழவு",14,"parai","scene",""],["மட்டு","மட்டு",15,"unavu","gift_food",""],["புரவியொடு","புரவி",23,"maa","scene","horse"],["களிறுபல","களிறு",23,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["king","பழையன் மாறன்",19],["town","கூடல்",20],["king","கிள்ளி வளவன்",22],["king","கோதை மார்பன்",25]],"dv":[["ullurai","கொக்கின் கயல்குறித்துக்","confirmed","1/1","the other woman lying in wait for the husband"]],"ch":"a:18"},"a:347":{"book":"akananuru","num":348,"t":["தோளும் தொல்கவின் தொலைய நாளும்","நலங்கவர் பசலை நல்கின்று நலியச்","சால்பெருந் தானைச் சேர லாதன்","மால்கடல் ஓட்டிக் கடம்பு அறுத்து இயற்றிய","பண்ணமை முரசின் கண் அதிர்ந்தன்ன","கவ்வை தூற்றும் வெவ்வாய்ச் சேரி","அம்பல் மூதூர் அலர்நமக்கு ஒழியச்","சென்றனர் ஆயினும் செய்வினை அவர்க்கே","வாய்க்கதில்- வாழி தோழி- வாயாது","மழைகரந்து ஒளித்த கழைதிரங்கு அடுக்கத்து","ஒண்கேழ் வயப்புலி பாய்ந்தென குவவுஅடி","வெண்கோட்டு யானை முழக்கிசை வெரீஇக்","கன்றொழித்து ஒடிய புன்தலை மடப்பிடி","கைதலை வைத்த மையல் விதுப்பொடு","கெடுமகப் பெண்டிரின் தேரும்","நெடுமர மருங்கின் மலைஇறந் தோரே!"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that though he left her, with her shoulders losing their old beauty, pallor eating her looks and the harsh street's gossip loud as the drum of Cheralathan who drove back the sea and cut the kadambu, may his work succeed; he has crossed the mountains where, after a tiger's leap, a young cow-elephant frightened by the tusker's roar has lost her calf and searches with her trunk on her head like a woman who has lost her child.","n":"The blessing (வாய்க்கதில்) is spoken in the middle of her own wasting. The cow-elephant searching like a bereaved mother is a picture of the road's terror, and also of a loss being searched for, which is her own state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","alar","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 347","lb":"A347","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கழைதிரங்கு","கழை",10,"maram","absent","bamboo"],["வயப்புலி","புலி",11,"maa","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",12,"maa","scene",""],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",13,"maa","simile",""],["முரசின்","முரசு",5,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[["king","சேர லாதன்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:18"},"a:348":{"book":"akananuru","num":349,"t":["என்ஆ வதுகொல் தானே- முன்றில்","தேன்தேர் சுவைய திரள்அரை மாஅத்துக்","கோடைக்கு ஊழ்த்த கமழ்நறுந் தீங்கனிப்","பயிர்ப்புறுப் பலவின் எதிர்ச்சுளை அளைஇ","இறாலொடு கலந்த வண்டுமூசு அரியல்","நெடுங்கண் ஆடுஅமைப் பழுநிக் கடுந்திறல்","பாப்புக்கடுப்பு அன்ன தோப்பி வான்கோட்டுக்","கடவுள்ஓங்கு வரைக்கு ஓக்கிக் குறவர்","முறித்தழை மகளிர் மடுப்ப மாந்தி","அடுக்கல் ஏனல் இரும்புனம் மறந்துழி","யானை வவ்வின தினைஎன நோனாது","இளையரும் முதியரும் கிளையுடன் குழீஇச்","சிலைஆய்ந்து திரிதரும் நாடன்","நிலையா நல்மொழி தேறிய நெஞ்சே?"],"p":"","r":"With the lover listening, the heroine asks what will become of her heart, which trusted the fine but unstable words of the lord of the land where the hill folk drink toddy of jackfruit and honey matured in bamboo, offer it to the god's high peak, and forget their millet field until elephants take the grain and young and old gather angrily with their bows.","n":"The friend has started the talk and the heroine speaks for the man to hear: the drunken hill folk who let the field go and then rouse the whole clan are her warning of what his delay invites.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 348","lb":"A348","poet":"மதுரை இளம்பாலாசிரியன் சேந்தன் கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் 1சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழி சொல்லெடுப்பத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","தோழி சொல்லெடுப்பத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாஅத்துக்","மா",2,"maram","scene","mango"],["பலவின்","பலா",4,"maram","scene",""],["இறாலொடு","இறால்",5,"pira","scene","honeycomb"],["வண்டுமூசு","வண்டு",5,"pira","scene",""],["அமைப்","அமை",6,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["தோப்பி","தோப்பி",7,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கடவுள்ஓங்கு","கடவுள்",8,"deyvam","scene",""],["குறவர்","குறவர்",8,"seythi","ullurai",""],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",10,"maram","ullurai",""],["யானை","யானை",11,"maa","ullurai",""],["பாப்புக்கடுப்பு","பாம்பு",7,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","குறவர் ஏனல் யானை","confirmed","1/1","the lover who delays marriage while the heroine is left unguarded"]],"ch":"a:18"},"a:349":{"book":"akananuru","num":350,"t":["அரம்போழ் அவ்வளை செறிந்த முன்கை","வரைந்துதாம் பிணித்த தொல்கவின் தொலைய","எவன்ஆய்ந் தனர்கொல்- தோழி! ஞெமன்","தெரிகோல் அன்ன செயிர்தீர் செம்மொழி","உலைந்த ஒக்கல் பாடுநர் செலினே","உரன்மலி உள்ளமொடு முனைபா ழாக","அருங்குறும்பு எறிந்த பெருங்கல வெறுக்கை","சூழாது சுரக்கும் நன்னன் நல்நாட்டு","எழிற் குன்றத்துக் கவாஅன் கேழ்கொளத்","திருந்துஅரை நிவந்த கருங்கால் வேங்கை","எரிமருள் கவளம் மாந்திக் களிறுதன்","வரிநுதல் வைத்த வலிதேம்பு தடக்கை","கல்லூர் பாம்பின் தோன்றும்","சொல்பெயர் தேஎத்த சுரன்இறந் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend what he was thinking of, letting the old beauty of the arm he bound in marriage fade, when he crossed into the land of strange speech, through the country of Nannan, whose word is true as a balance beam and who gives the spoils of broken forts unasked to weary bards, where on the beautiful hill a tusker that has eaten flame-red vengai rests its tired trunk on its brow like a snake climbing a rock.","n":"வரைந்துதாம் பிணித்த says it was he who bound her arm in marriage, so the loss is a broken undertaking, not only a sorrow. Nannan's unhesitating giving stands against the husband who left to get wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","karpu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 349","lb":"A349","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அவ்வளை","வளை",1,"pira","ornament","conch bangle"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",10,"maram","scene",""],["களிறுதன்","களிறு",11,"maa","scene",""],["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",13,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[["king","நன்னன்",8],["hill","எழிற் குன்றத்து",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:18"},"a:350":{"book":"akananuru","num":351,"t":["கழியே, சிறுகுரல் நெய்தலொடு காவிகூம்ப","எறிதிரை ஓதம் தரல்ஆ னாதே","துறையே, மருங்கின் போகிய மாக்கவை மருப்பின்","இருஞ்சேற்று ஈர்அளை அலவன் நீப்ப","வழங்குநர் இன்மையின் பாடுஆன் றன்றே;","கொடுநுகம் நுழைந்த கணைக்கால் அத்திரி","வடிமணி நெடுந்தேர் பூண ஏவாது","ஏந்துஎழில் மழைக்கண் இவள்குறை யாகச்","சேந்தனை சென்மோ- பெருநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப!-","இலங்குஇரும் பரப்பின் எறிசுறா நீக்கி","வலம்புரி மூழ்கிய வான்திமிற் பரதவர்","ஒலிதலைப் பணிலம் ஆர்ப்பக் கல்லெனக்","கலிகெழு கொற்கை எதிர்கொள இழிதரும்","குவவுமணல் நெடுங்கோட்டு ஆங்கண்","உவக்காண் தோன்றும்எம் சிறுநல் ஊரே!"],"p":"","r":"To the lover leaving after a daytime meeting, the friend says the backwater's flowers are closing with the tide, the ford is silent with no one passing and the crab gone from its burrow: do not yoke your mules to the chariot, but for her sake stay and then go; see, there is our little town, on the long sandbank where the fishermen who dive for conch come in to Korkai's welcome, blowing their shells.","n":"Pointing to her own town at dusk is an invitation to stay and also a hint to come openly to the village, which is how the friend nudges the lover toward marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","varaivu","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 350","lb":"A350","poet":"சேந்தன் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறி வந்து நீங்குந் தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகற்குறி வந்து நீங்குந் தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெய்தலொடு","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","scene",""],["காவிகூம்ப","காவி",1,"maram","scene",""],["அலவன்","அலவன்",4,"pira","scene","crab"],["அத்திரி","அத்திரி",6,"maa","scene","mule"],["சுறா","சுறா",10,"pira","scene",""],["வலம்புரி","வலம்புரி",11,"pira","scene","conch"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",11,"seythi","scene",""],["பணிலம்","பணிலம்",12,"pira","scene","conch"]],"en":[["town","கொற்கை",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:18"},"a:351":{"book":"akananuru","num":352,"t":["வேற்றுநாட்டு உறையுள் விருப்புறப் பேணி","பெறல்அருங் கேளிர் பின்வந்து விடுப்ப","பொருள்அகப் படுத்த புகல்மலி நெஞ்சமொடு","குறைவினை முடித்த நிறைவின் இயக்கம்","அறிவுறூஉம் கொல்லோ தானே- கதிர்தெற","கழலிலை உகுத்த கால்பொரு தாழ்சினை","அழல் அகைந் தன்ன அம்குழைப் பொதும்பில்","புழல்வீ இருப்பைப் புன்காட்டு அத்தம்","மறுதரல் உள்ளமொடு குறுகத் தோற்றிய","செய்குறி ஆழி வைகல்தோறு எண்ணி","எழுதுசுவர் நினைந்த அழுதுவார் மழைக்கண்","விலங்குவீழ் அரிப்பனி பொலங்குழைத் தெறிப்ப","திருந்திழை முன்கை அணல் அசைத்து ஊன்றி","இருந்துஅணை மீது பொருந்துவீக் கிடக்கை","வருந்துதோள் பூசல் களையும் மருந்தென","உள்ளுதொறு படூஉம் பல்லி","புள்ளுத்தொழுது உறைவி செவிமுத லானே?"],"p":"","r":"Returning with wealth won abroad, the hero asks his heart whether she knows yet that his task is done: she who counts each day the marks drawn on the wall for the days he set, with tears falling on her gold earring, chin on her hand on the bed, worshipping the bird omens, may hear at her ear the lizard that chirps whenever she thinks of him, as a medicine for her aching shoulders.","n":"The man imagines the woman's waiting in close detail, the tally marks, the omens of bird and lizard, so that what he hopes to bring home is the end of a routine he knows she keeps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pirivu","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 351","lb":"A351","poet":"பொருந்தில் இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்முற்றி மறுத்தராநின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பொருள்முற்றி மறுத்தராநின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இருப்பைப்","இருப்பை",8,"maram","scene",""],["பல்லி","பல்லி",16,"pira","scene","lizard"],["புள்ளுத்தொழுது","புள்",17,"pul","scene","omen bird"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:18"},"a:352":{"book":"akananuru","num":353,"t":["'முடவுமுதிர் பலவின் குடம்மருள் பெரும்பழம்","பல்கிளைத் தலைவன் கல்லாக் கடுவன்","பாடிமிழ் அருவிப் பாறை மருங்கின்","ஆடுமயில் முன்னது ஆகக் கோடியர்","விழவுகொள் மூதூர் விறலி பின்றை","முழவன் போல அகப்படத் தழீஇ","இன்துணைப் பயிரும் குன்ற நாடன்","குடிநன்கு உடையன் கூடுநர்ப் பிரியலன்","கெடுநா மொழியலன் அன்பினன்' என நீ","வல்ல கூறி வாய்வதின் புணர்த்தோய்","நல்லை காண் இனிக்- காதல் அம்தோழீஇ!-","கடும்பரி புரவி நெடுந்தேர் அஞ்சி","நல்லிசை நிறுத்த நயம்வரு பனுவல்","தொல்லிசை நிறீஇய உரைசால் பாண்மகன்","எண்ணுமுறை நிறுத்த பண்ணின் னுள்ளும்","புதுவது புனைந்த திறத்தினும்","வதுவை நாளினும் இனியனால் எமக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The bride tells her friend that she spoke well and truly in praising the hill lord, where the male monkey clasps a pot-sized jackfruit as a drummer clasps his drum behind a dancing girl, with a dancing peacock before him, and calls to his sweet mate by the waterfall, as a man of good family who would not leave her nor speak falsely: the friend is sweeter to her than the new mode the bard composed for Anji's famous song, and sweeter even than the wedding day.","n":"The friend's earlier speech is quoted, with its own scene of the monkey keeping its mate, so the poem confirms after the wedding what the go-between promised before it. The second alternative of the colophon puts the speech before the wedding, when it is only arranged.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","karpu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 352","lb":"A352","poet":"அஞ்சியத்தை மகள் நாகையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரைந்து எய்திய பின்றை மணமனைக்கட் சென்ற தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது. வரைவு மலிந்து சொல்லிய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","வரைந்து எய்திய பின்றை மணமனைக்கட் சென்ற தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு மலிந்து சொல்லிய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பலவின்","பலா",1,"maram","scene",""],["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",2,"maa","iraicchi","male monkey"],["ஆடுமயில்","மயில்",4,"pul","simile",""],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",4,"seythi","simile",""],["விறலி","விறலி",5,"seythi","simile",""],["முழவன்","முழவன்",6,"seythi","simile",""],["அருவிப்","அருவி",3,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","அஞ்சி",12]],"dv":[["iraicchi","கடுவன்","confirmed","1/1","the hill lord, like the monkey of his hill, keeps the one he has joined and does not leave her"]],"ch":"a:18"},"a:353":{"book":"akananuru","num":354,"t":["ஆள்வினைப் பிரிதலும் உண்டோ ? பிரியினும்","கேளினி- வாழிய நெஞ்சே!- நாளும்","கனவுக்கழிந் தனைய வாகி நனவின்","நாளது செலவும் மூப்பினது வரவும்","அரிதுபெறு சிறப்பின் காமத்து இயற்கையும்","இந்நிலை அறியாய் ஆயினும் செந்நிலை","அமைஆடு அங்கழை தீண்டிக் கல்லென","ஞெமைஇலை உதிர்த்த எரிவாய்க் கோடை","நெடுவெண் களரி நீறுமுகந்து சுழலக்","கடுவெயில் திருகிய வேனில்வெங் காட்டு","உயங்குநடை மடப்பிணை தழீஇய வயங்குபொறி","அறுகோட்டு எழிற்கலை அறுகயம் நோக்கித்","தெண்நீர் வேட்ட சிறுமையின் தழைமறந்து","உண்நீர் இன்மையின் ஒல்குவன தளர","மரம்நிழல் அற்ற இயவின் சுரனிறந்து","உள்ளுவை அல்லையோ மற்றே- உள்ளிய","விருந்து ஒழிவு அறியாப் பெருந்தண் பந்தர்","வருந்தி வருநர் ஓம்பித் தண்ணெனத்","தாதுதுகள் உதிர்த்த தாழைஅம் கூந்தல்","வீழ்இதழ் அலரி மெல்லகம் சேர்த்தி","மகிழ்அணி முறுவல் மாண்ட சேக்கை","நம்மொடு நன்மொழி நவிலும்","பொம்மல் ஓதிப் புனையிழை குணனே?"],"p":"","r":"Home again with wealth, the hero answers his heart, which urges a second journey: the days of waking life pass like a dream, age comes, and love's rare joy has its season; even if you do not see this, will you not think, crossing the treeless summer waste where a stag embraces his drooping doe and both turn from the leaves for want of water at a dried pond, of the virtue of the long-haired woman who tends weary guests in our cool hall and speaks sweetly with us in bed?","n":"The argument against going is made from impermanence: time and age are reasons to stay with love and household, not to renounce it. Her virtue is defined by the guests she feeds, so the home he would leave is a place of giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","nilaiyaamai","virundhu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 353","lb":"A353","poet":"மதுரை அளக்கர் ஞாழலார் மகனார் மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"முன்னொரு காலத்துப் பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்த தலைமகன் பொருண்முற்றி வந்திருந்த காலத்து மீண்டும் பொருள் கடாவின நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","மீண்டும் பொருள் கடாவின நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அமைஆடு","அமை",7,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["ஞெமைஇலை","ஞெமை",8,"maram","scene",""],["மடப்பிணை","பிணை",11,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["எழிற்கலை","கலை",12,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["தாழைஅம்","தாழை",19,"maram","simile",""],["அலரி","அலரி",20,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","எழிற்கலை","confirmed","1/1","even the stag keeps by his mate in hardship, so the man should not leave his wife again for wealth"]],"ch":"a:18"},"a:354":{"book":"akananuru","num":355,"t":["மதவலி யானை மறலிய பாசறை","இடிஉமிழ் முரசம் பொருகளத்து இயம்ப","வென்றுகொடி எடுத்தனன் வேந்தனும் கன்றொடு","கறவைப் பல்லினம் புறவுதொறு உகளக்","குழல்வாய் வைத்தனர் கோவலர் வல்விரைந்து","இளையர் ஏகுவனர் பரிய விரியுளைக்","கடுநடைப் புரவி வழிவாய் ஓட","வலவன் வள்புவலி உறுப்பப் புலவர்","புகழ்குறி கொண்ட பொலந்தார் அகலத்துத்","தண்கமழ் சாந்தம் நுண்துகள் அணிய","வென்றிகொள் உவகையொடு புகுதல் வேண்டின்","யாண்டுஉறை வதுகொல் தானே...மாண்ட","போதுஉறழ் கொண்ட உண்கண்","தீதிலாட்டி திருநுதற் பசப்பே?"],"p":"","r":"The attendants tell the hero, his work done: the king has won and raised his flag in the camp where elephants clashed, cows frisk with their calves in every grove and herdsmen raise their flutes; if you would enter home in the joy of victory, sandal dust on your gold-garlanded chest that poets praise, where then will the pallor go from the brow of your faultless lady with flower-like eyes?","n":"The victory and the cowherds' evening are both reasons to go now; the question at the end means her pallor will vanish the moment he arrives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 354","lb":"A354","poet":"மதுரைத் தமிழ்க்கூத்தன் கடுவன் மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகற்கு உழையர் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["pira","thalaivan",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகற்கு உழையர் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene",""],["முரசம்","முரசு",2,"parai","scene",""],["கன்றொடு","கன்று",3,"maa","scene",""],["கறவைப்","கறவை",4,"maa","scene",""],["குழல்வாய்","குழல்",5,"yaazh","scene",""],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["புரவி","புரவி",7,"maa","scene","horse"],["சாந்தம்","சாந்து",10,"pira","ornament",""],["போதுஉறழ்","போது",13,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:18"},"a:355":{"book":"akananuru","num":356,"t":["மாவும் வண்தளிர் ஈன்றன குயிலும்","இன்தீம் பல்குரல் கொம்பர் நுவலும்","மூதிலை ஒழித்த போதுஅவிழ் பெருஞ்சினை","வல்லோன் தைவரும் வள்ளுயிர்ப் பாலை","நரம்புஆர்த் தன்ன வண்டினம் முரலும்","துணிகயம் துன்னிய தூமணல் எக்கர்த்","தாதுஉகு தண்பொழில் அல்கிக் காதலர்","செழுமனை மறக்கும் செவ்விவேனில்","தானே வந்தன்று ஆயின் ஆனாது","இலங்குவளை நெகிழ்ந்த எவ்வம் காட்டிப்","புலந்தனம் வருகம் சென்மோ- தோழி!","'யாமே எமியம் ஆக நீயே","பொன்நயந்து அருள்இலை யாகி","இன்னை ஆகுதல் ஒத்தன்றால்' எனவே"],"p":"","r":"Told that he is going, the heroine answers her friend: when spring comes of itself, when the mango puts out shoots, the koel calls and bees hum on the budding branch like the strings of a paalai lute touched by a master, the season in which lovers forget their rich homes in cool groves, let us go and show him the loosened bangles, quarrel with him and come back, saying: while we are alone, you who love gold and have no grace have become like this.","n":"She rehearses her reproach before he has gone, and the reproach is about the season: he will be away in the time made for lovers. The paalai lute in the simile names the very mode of parting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","oodal","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 355","lb":"A355","poet":"தங்காற் பொற்கொல்லனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவுணர்த்திய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவுணர்த்திய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாவும்","மா",1,"maram","scene","mango"],["குயிலும்","குயில்",1,"pul","scene",""],["பாலை","பாலை",4,"yaazh","simile","paalai lute"],["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",5,"pira","scene",""],["இலங்குவளை","வளை",10,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:18"},"a:356":{"book":"akananuru","num":357,"t":["மேல்துறைக் கொளீஇய கழாலின் கீழ்த்துறை","உகுவார் அருந்தப் பகுவாய் யாமை","கம்புள் இயவன் ஆக விசிபிணித்","தெண்கண் கிணையின் பிறழும் ஊரன்","இடைநெடுந் தெருவிற் கதுமெனக் கண்டென்","பொற்றெடி முன்கை பற்றின னாக","'அன்னாய்!' என்றனென் அவன்கைவிட் டனனே","தொன்னசை சாலாமை நன்னன் பறம்பில்","சிறுகா ரோடன் பயினொடு சேர்த்திய","கற்போல் நாவினே னாகி மற்றுது","செப்பலென் மன்னால் யாய்க்கே நல்தேர்க்","கடும்பகட்டு யானைச் சோழர் மருகன்","நெடுங்கதிர் நெல்லின் வல்லம் கிழவோன்","நல்லடி உள்ளா னாகவும் ஒல்லார்","கதவ முயறலும் முயல்ப அதாஅன்று","ஒலிபல் கூந்தல் நம்வயின் அருளாது","கொன்றனன் ஆயினும் கொலைபழுது அன்றே","அருவி ஆம்பல் கலித்த முன்துறை","நன்னன் ஆஅய் பிரம்பு அன்ன","மின்னீர் ஓதி!- என்னைநின் குறிப்பே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine how the lord of the town, where the tortoise at the lower ford drinks the washing water and rolls like a kinai drum with the water-hen as its player, saw me in the long street and seized my gold-bangled arm; I cried 'Mother!' and he let go, and since then, so as not to betray our old affection, I have kept a tongue like stone glued with lac and told our mother nothing; even if he showed us no grace and killed, the killing would be no fault: so what is your mind, you with hair like Nannan's cane?","n":"The friend tells of her own encounter as a test: she has been seized and stayed silent, and she now asks the heroine to accept the man's suit. Lines 11 to 17, with the Chola lord of Vallam and the enemies at the door, are obscure in our text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","naanam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 356","lb":"A356","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பின்னின்ற தலைமகற்குக் குறைநேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகளைக் குறை நயப்பக் கூறியது.","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","பின்னின்ற தலைமகற்குக் குறைநேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகளைக் குறை நயப்பக் கூறியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யாமை","யாமை",2,"pira","scene","tortoise"],["கம்புள்","கம்புள்",3,"pul","scene",""],["கிணையின்","கிணை",4,"parai","simile",""],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",18,"maram","scene",""],["பிரம்பு","பிரம்பு",19,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["king","நன்னன்",8],["hill","பறம்பில்",8],["king","சோழர்",12],["town","வல்லம்",13],["king","நன்னன்",19]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:18"},"a:357":{"book":"akananuru","num":358,"t":["கொடுமுள் ஈங்கைச் சூரலொடு மிடைந்த","வான்முகை இறும்பின் வயவொடு வதிந்த","உண்ணாப் பிணவின் உயக்கம் தீரிய","தடமருப்பு யானை வலம்படத் தொலைச்சி","வியலறை சிவப்ப வாங்கி முணங்கு நிமிர்ந்து","புலவுப்புலி புரண்ட புல்சாய் சிறுநெறி","பயில்இருங் கானத்து வழங்கல் செல்லாது","பெருங்களிற்று இனநிரை கைதொடூஉப் பெயரும்","தீஞ்சுளைப் பலவின் தொழுதி உம்பற்","பெருங்காடு இறந்தனர் ஆயினும் யாழநின்","திருந்திழைப் பணைத்தோள் வருந்த நீடி","உள்ளாது அமைதலோ இலரே நல்குவர்","மிகுபெயல் நிலைஇய தீநீர்ப் பொய்கை","அடைஇறந்து அவிழ்ந்த தண்கமழ் நீலம்","காலொடு துயல்வந் தன்னநின்","ஆய்இதழ் மழைக்கண் அமர்த்த நோக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend comforts the heroine: though he has crossed the great forest of Umbar, where a tiger kills a tusker to end the weakness of his hungry mate and rolls on the reddened rock, and the elephant herd turns back holding each other's trunks, he will not stay away without thinking of your shoulders; he will be kind, remembering your glance, like a blue lily swaying in the wind on a rain-filled pond.","n":"The tiger's kill is violent, but what it does is provide for a mate weak with pregnancy; that is the reassurance, as the colophon's வற்புறீஇயது expects.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 357","lb":"A357","poet":"எருக்காட்டூர்த் தாயங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறீஇயது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஈங்கைச்","ஈங்கை",1,"maram","scene",""],["சூரலொடு","சூரல்",1,"maram","scene",""],["பிணவின்","பிணவு",3,"maa","iraicchi","tigress"],["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene",""],["புலவுப்புலி","புலி",6,"maa","iraicchi",""],["பெருங்களிற்று","களிறு",8,"maa","scene",""],["பலவின்","பலா",9,"maram","scene",""],["நீலம்","நீலம்",14,"maram","simile","blue lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","புலவுப்புலி","confirmed","1/1","as the tiger toils for his mate, the man is away working for her and will come back to her"]],"ch":"a:18"},"a:358":{"book":"akananuru","num":359,"t":["நீலத்து அன்ன நிறம்கிளர் எருத்தின்","காமர் பீலி ஆய்மயில் தோகை","இன்தீம் குரல துவன்றி மென்சீர்","ஆடுதகை எழில்நலம் கடுப்பக் கூடி","கண்ணேர் இதழ தண்நறுங் குவளை","குறுந்தொடர் அடைச்சிய நறும்பல் கூழை","நீடுநீர் நெடுஞ்சுனை ஆயமொடு ஆடாய்","உயங்கிய மனத்தை யாகிப் புலம்புகொண்டு","இன்னை ஆகிய நின்நிறம் நோக்கி","அன்னை வினவினள் ஆயின், அன்னோ!","என்னென உரைக்கோ யானே- துன்னிய","பெருவரை இழிதரும் நெடுவெள் அருவி","ஓடை யானை உயர்மிசை எடுத்த","ஆடுகொடி கடுப்பத் தோன்றும்","கோடுயர் வெற்பன் உறீஇய நோயே?"],"p":"","r":"By day, with the lover hidden nearby, the friend tells the heroine that she no longer plays with her companions in the deep spring, hair dressed with cool kuvalai, graceful as a blue-necked peacock dancing; if mother sees her changed colour and asks, what can the friend say of the sickness given by the lord of the high hill, where the long white waterfall looks like a flag on a war elephant?","n":"The question is meant for the man to hear: the mother has noticed, and only marriage can answer her. The waterfall like a flag on an elephant is the hill lord's own country turned into a banner.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","pasalai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 358","lb":"A358","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பகலே சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி தலைமட்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","பகலே சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆய்மயில்","மயில்",2,"pul","simile",""],["குவளை","குவளை",5,"maram","ornament",""],["அருவி","அருவி",12,"pira","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",13,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:18"},"a:359":{"book":"akananuru","num":360,"t":["'பனிவார் உண்கணும் பசந்த தோளும்","நனிபிறர் அறியச் சாஅய நாளும்","கரந்தனம் உறையும் நம்பண்பு அறியார்","நீடினர் மன்னோ காதலர்' எனநீ","எவன்கை யற்றனை?- இகுளை!- அவரே","வான வரம்பன் வெளியத்து அன்னநம்","மாணலம் தம்மொடு கொண்டனர்- முனாஅது","அருஞ்சுரக் கவலை அசைஇய கோடியர்","பெருங்கல் மீமிசை இயம்எழுந் தாங்கு","வீழ்பிடி கெடுத்த நெடுந்தாள் யானை","சூர்புகல் அடுக்கத்து மழைமாறு முழங்கும்","பொய்யா நல்லிசை மாவண் புல்லி","கவைக்கதிர் வரகின் யாணர்ப் பைந்தாள்","முதைச்சுவல் மூழ்கிய கான்சுடு குரூஉப்புகை","அருவித் துவலையொடு மயங்கும்","பெருவரை அத்தம் இயங்கி யோரே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the heroine why she is helpless, saying he has stayed away not knowing how she hides her tearful eyes and pale shoulders: he has taken their beauty with him, rich as the Chera's Veliyam, on the mountain road of Pulli, where an elephant that has lost its beloved mate trumpets back to the clouds like the music of travelling players on a great rock, and smoke from burning fields mingles with waterfall spray.","n":"Her beauty is not lost but carried off, held by him on the road; the friend reframes the wasting as a trust he holds. The elephant's cry for its lost mate shows that the road itself mourns a separation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","nirai","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 359","lb":"A359","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",8,"seythi","simile",""],["வீழ்பிடி","பிடி",10,"maa","absent",""],["யானை","யானை",10,"maa","iraicchi",""],["வரகின்","வரகு",13,"unavu","scene",""],["அருவித்","அருவி",15,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["king","வான வரம்பன்",6],["town","வெளியத்து",6],["patron","புல்லி",12]],"dv":[["iraicchi","யானை","confirmed","1/1","the man on that road, parted as the elephant is, feels the loss too and will turn back"]],"ch":"a:18"},"a:360":{"book":"akananuru","num":361,"t":["பல்பூந் தண்பொழில் பகல்உடன் கழிப்பி","ஒருகால் ஊர்திப் பருதிஅம் செல்வன்","குடவயின் மாமலை மறையக் கொடுங்கழித்","தண்சேற்று அடைஇய கணைக்கால் நெய்தல்","நுண்தாது உண்டு வண்டினம் துறப்ப","வெருவரு கடுந்திறல் இருபெரும் தெய்வத்து","உருவுடன் இயைந்த தோற்றம் போல","அந்தி வானமொடு கடலணி கொளாஅ","வந்த மாலை பெயரின் மற்றிவள்","பெரும்புலம் பினளே தெய்ய அதனால்","பாணி பிழையா மாண்வினைக் கலிமா","துஞ்சு ஊர் யாமத்துத் தெவிட்டல் ஓம்பி","நெடுந்தேர் அகல நீக்கிப் பையெனக்","குன்றுஇழி களிற்றின் குவவுமணல் நந்தி","இரவின் வம்மோ- உரவுநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப!","இனமீன் அருந்து நாரையொடு பனைமிசை","அன்றில் சேக்கும் முன்றில் பொன்னென","நன்மலர் நறுவீ தாஅம்","புன்னை நறும்பொழில் செய்தநம் குறியே"],"p":"","r":"Refusing a daytime meeting, the friend tells the lover that after the day spent in the flowering grove, when the sun goes behind the western hill, bees leave the neythal and sky and sea at dusk take on the joined form of the two great gods, his going leaves her desolate; so let him come at night, keeping his trained horse from neighing while the town sleeps, leaving the chariot far off and crossing the sand softly like an elephant coming down a hill, to their trysting place in the punnai grove where the anril roosts on the palm.","n":"The friend's detailed instructions for secrecy (the muffled horse, the chariot left behind) show how dangerous the night tryst is, which is also an argument for marriage. The two gods of the simile are not named; the sky and sea joined suggest two figures of different colour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","maalai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 360","lb":"A360","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறி வந்த தலைமகற்குத் தோழி பகற்குறி மறுத்து இரவுக் கறி நேர்ந்தது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகற்குறி மறுத்து இரவுக்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",4,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",5,"pira","scene",""],["தெய்வத்து","தெய்வம்",6,"deyvam","simile",""],["கலிமா","மா",11,"maa","scene","horse"],["களிற்றின்","களிறு",14,"maa","simile",""],["நாரையொடு","நாரை",16,"pul","scene",""],["பனைமிசை","பனை",16,"maram","scene",""],["அன்றில்","அன்றில்",17,"pul","scene",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",19,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"a:18"},"a:361":{"book":"akananuru","num":362,"t":["'தூமலர்த் தாமரைப் பூவின் அங்கண்","மாஇதழ்க் குவளை மலர்பிணைத் தன்ன","திருமுகத்து அலமரும் பெருமதர் மழைக்கண்","அணிவளை முன்கை ஆயிதழ் மடந்தை","வார்முலை முற்றத்து நூலிடை விலங்கினும்","கவவுப்புலந்து உறையும் கழிபெருங் காமத்து","இன்புறு நுகர்ச்சியிற் சிறந்ததொன்று இல்லென","அன்பால் மொழிந்த என்மொழி கொள்ளாய்","பொருள்புரி வுண்ட மருளி நெஞ்சே!-","கரியாப் பூவின் பெரியோர் ஆர","அழலெழு தித்தியம் மடுத்த யாமை","நிழலுடை நெடுங்கயம் புகல்வேட் டாஅங்கு","உள்ளுதல் ஓம்புமதி இனிநீ முள்ளெயிற்றுச்","சின்மொழி அரிவை தோளே- பன்மலை","வெவ்வறை மருங்கின் வியன்சுரம்","எவ்வம் கூர இறந்தனம் யாமே!"],"p":"","r":"On the wide hot track he has already entered, the man tells his heart, which would not take his word that nothing surpasses the embrace of a girl who sulks if even a thread comes between them, and chose wealth instead, to stop thinking of her shoulders now, as a turtle dropped in a pot over the fire longs in vain for the shaded pool.","n":"The argument the heart rejected is quoted in full, so the poem's real speaker is the losing side of a debate: the man has already obeyed the heart and gone, and his order to it to stop remembering is the measure of how much he remembers. The turtle in the pot pictures a longing that comes too late; the phrase about the elders in line 10 (கரியாப் பூவின் பெரியோர்) is obscure, but the turtle's plight is clear without it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 361","lb":"A361","poet":"எயினந்தை மகனார் இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்து போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்து போகாநின்ற"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",1,"maram","simile","lotus"],["குவளை","குவளை",2,"maram","simile",""],["யாமை","யாமை",11,"pira","simile","turtle"],["கயம்","கயம்",12,"pira","simile","pond"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:362":{"book":"akananuru","num":363,"t":["பாம்புடை விடர பனிநீர் இட்டுத்துறை","தேம்கலந்து ஒழுக யாறுநிறைந் தனவே","வெண்கோட்டு யானை பொருத புண்கூர்ந்து","பைங்கண் வல்லியம் கல்லளைச் செறிய","முருக்கரும்பு அன்ன வள்ளுகிர் வயப்பிணவு","கடிகொள வழங்கார் ஆறே ஆயிடை","எல்லிற்று என்னான் வென்வேல் ஏந்தி","நசைதர வந்த நன்ன ராளன்","நெஞ்சுபழு தாக வறுவியன் பெயரின்","இன்றிப் பொழுதும் யான்வா ழலனே","எவன்கொல்?- வாழி தோழி!- நம் இடைமுலைச்","சுணங்கணி முற்றத்து ஆரம் போலவும்","சிலம்புநீடு சோலைச் சிதர்தூங்கு நளிர்ப்பின்","இலங்குவெள் அருவி போலவும்","நிழல்கொண் டனவால், திங்கள்அம் கதிரே!"],"p":"","r":"With the hero hidden nearby, the friend tells the girl that the hill streams are in flood and the path is shut by a tigress guarding her mate, wounded by an elephant, so that if the good man who came by night with his spear goes back with nothing, she will not live a day; and she asks why the moon has made the night as bright as the necklace on their breasts and the white waterfall.","n":"Every danger she lists is the man's, and the bright moon is a further danger to secret meeting, so the speech to the girl is a warning to the listener that night visits cannot go on; it presses toward marriage without naming it. The tigress keeping watch over her wounded mate stays a sign of the path's danger here: the poem draws nothing from her care.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 362","lb":"A362","poet":"வெள்ளி வீதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிச் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறிச் சிறைப்புறமாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["பாம்புடை","பாம்பு",1,"pira","scene","snake"],["தேம்","தேன்",2,"pira","scene","honey"],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""],["வல்லியம்","வல்லியம்",4,"maa","scene","tiger"],["முருக்கரும்பு","முருக்கு அரும்பு",5,"maram","simile",""],["வயப்பிணவு","பிணவு",5,"maa","scene","tigress"],["சோலைச்","சோலை",13,"maram","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",14,"pira","simile",""],["திங்கள்அம்","திங்கள்",15,"pira","scene","moon"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:363":{"book":"akananuru","num":364,"t":["நிறைசெலல் இவுளி விரைவுடன் கடைஇ","அகலிரு விசும்பிற் பகல்செலச் சென்று","மழுகுசுடர் மண்டிலம் மாமலை மறைய","பொழுதுகழி மலரிற் புனையிழை! சாஅய்!","அணை அணைந்து இனையை ஆகல்- கணையரைப்","புல்லிலை நெல்லிப் புகரில் பசுங்காய்","கல்லதர் மருங்கில் கடுவளி உதிர்ப்ப","பொலஞ்செய் காசிற் பொற்பத் தாஅம்","அத்தம் நண்ணி அதர்பார்த் திருந்த","கொலைவெங் கொள்கைக் கொடுந்தொழின் மறவர்","ஆறுசெல் மாக்கள் அருநிறத்து எறிந்த","எஃகுஉறு விழுப்புண் கூர்ந்தோர் எய்திய","வளைவாய்ப் பருந்தின் வள்ளுகிர்ச் சேவல்","கிளைதரு தெள்விளி கெழுமுடைப் பயிரும்","இன்னா வெஞ்சுரம் இறந்தோர் முன்னிய","செய்வினை வலத்தர் ஆகி இவணயந்து","எய்தவந் தனரே!- தோழி!- மையெழில்","துணையேர் எதிர்மலர் உண்கண்","பிணையேர் நோக்கம் பெருங்கவின் கொளவே"],"p":"","r":"As the sun's chariot sets behind the mountain, the friend tells the girl, faded like a flower past its hour and lying on her bed, not to grieve: the man who crossed the cruel wasteland, where the wind scatters gooseberries like gold coins on the stony path and the hawk calls its kin to men speared by waiting robbers, has done his work and is coming back, so that her eyes will regain their beauty.","n":"The terrors of the road are given at length only to be put behind: they are the measure of what he has already passed, and so of how sure his return now is. The gooseberries that look like gold coins hint at the wealth he went for, lying on the very road of danger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 363","lb":"A363","poet":"மதுரைப் போன்செய் கொல்லன் வெண்ணாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["இவுளி","இவுளி",1,"maa","scene","horse"],["மலரிற்","மலர்",4,"maram","simile",""],["நெல்லிப்","நெல்லி",6,"maram","simile",""],["மறவர்","மறவர்",10,"seythi","scene",""],["பருந்தின்","பருந்து",13,"pul","scene",""],["பிணையேர்","பிணை",19,"maa","simile","doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:364":{"book":"akananuru","num":365,"t":["மாதிரம் புதையப் பாஅய்க் கால்வீழ்த்து","ஏறுடைப் பெருமழை பொழிந்தென அவல்தோறு","ஆடுகளப் பறையின் வரிநுணல் கறங்க","ஆய்பொன் அவிர்இழை தூக்கி யன்ன","நீடிணர்க் கொன்றை- கவின்பெறக் காடுடன்","சுடர்புரை தோன்றிப் புதல்தலைக் கொளாஅ","முல்லை இல்லமொடு மலரக் கல்ல","பகுவாய்ப் பைஞ்சுனை மாவுண மலிரக்","கார்தொடங் கின்றே காலை காதலர்","வெஞ்சின வேந்தன் வியன்பெரும் பாசறை","வென்றி வேட்கையொடு நம்மும் உள்ளார்","யாதுசெய் வாங் கொல்?- தோழி!- நோதகக்","கொலைகுறித் தன்ன மாலை","துனைதரு போழ்தின் நீந்தலோ அரிதே!"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that the rains have come with thunder, frogs drum in every hollow, the kondrai hangs like gold, thondri and mullai bloom and the rock pools fill, yet her lover, in the great camp of a king eager for victory, does not think of them; and the evening that comes like one bent on killing is too much to swim through.","n":"The season he promised to return by is described as complete and lovely, and only then measured against his absence; the more perfect the rains, the plainer it is that he has broken his word. The camp belongs to a king, not to him: he stays for another man's victory.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","mazhai","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 364","lb":"A364","poet":"மதுரை மருதங்கிழார் மகனார் பெருங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பருவங் கண்டழிந்த தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பருவங் கண்டழிந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஏறுடைப்","ஏறு",2,"pira","scene","thunder"],["பறையின்","பறை",3,"parai","simile",""],["நுணல்","நுணல்",3,"pira","simile","frog"],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",5,"maram","simile",""],["தோன்றிப்","தோன்றி",6,"maram","simile",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",7,"maram","scene",""],["இல்லமொடு","இல்லம்",7,"maram","scene",""],["மாவுண","மா",8,"maa","scene","beasts"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:365":{"book":"akananuru","num":366,"t":["அகல்வாய் வானம் ஆலிருள் பரப்ப","பகல்ஆற்றுப் படுத்த பையென் தோற்றமொடு","சினவல் போகிய புன்கண் மாலை","அத்தம் நடுகல் ஆள்என உதைத்த","கான யானைக் கதுவாய் வள்ளுகிர்","இரும்பனை இதக்கையின் ஒடியும் ஆங்கண்","கடுங்கண் ஆடவர் ஏமுயல் கிடக்கை","வருநர் இன்மையின் களையுநர்க் காணா","என்றூழ் வெஞ்சுரம் தந்த நீயே!","துயர்செய்து ஆற்றா யாகிப் பெயர்பாங்கு","உள்ளினை- வாழிய- நெஞ்சே!- வென்வேல்","மாவண் கழுவுள் காமூர் ஆங்கண்","பூதம் தந்த பொரியரை வேங்கைத்","தண்கமழ் புதுமலர் நாறும்","அஞ்சில் ஓதி ஆய்மடத் தகையே"],"p":"","r":"At dusk in the burning wasteland, where wild elephants kick a hero-stone taking it for a man and snap their nails, and fallen bowmen lie with none to tend them, the man tells his heart, which brought him there and, unable to bear the pain it caused, now thinks of turning back, that it is the girl's soft dark hair, fragrant with the new vengai flowers of Kazhuvul's Kaamur, that draws it.","n":"The heart that drove him out is the same heart that now wants to go home; the poem catches it in the act of changing sides. Kaamur's vengai, given by a spirit, makes her hair a thing of a famous and blessed place set against the desolate place he stands in. Lines 7 and 8 are hard to construe, and the turn in lines 10 and 11 can be read as rebuke or as relief.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","ninaivu","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 365","lb":"A365","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் இடைச்சுரத்து நின்று சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"இடைச்சுரத்து நின்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["யானைக்","யானை",5,"maa","scene",""],["இரும்பனை","பனை",6,"maram","simile","palmyra"],["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["பூதம்","பூதம்",13,"deyvam","scene",""],["வேங்கைத்","வேங்கை",13,"maram","scene","kino tree"]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்",4],["patron","கழுவுள்",12],["town","காமூர்",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:366":{"book":"akananuru","num":367,"t":["தாழ்சினை மருதம் தகைபெறக் கவினிய","நீர்சூழ் வியன்களம் பொலியப் போர்ப்பஅழித்து","கள்ளார் களமர் பகடுதலை மாற்றி","கடுங்காற்று எறியப் போகிய துரும்புடன்","காயல் சிறுதடிக் கண்கெடப் பாய்தலின்","இருநீர்ப் பரப்பின் பனித்துறைப் பரதவர்","தீம்பொழி வெள்ளுப்புச் சிதைதலின் சினைஇக்","கழனி உழவரொடு மாறுஎதிர்ந்து மயங்கி","இருஞ்சேற்று அள்ளல் எறிசெருக் கண்டு","நரைமூ தாளர் கைபிணி விடுத்து","நனைமுதிர் தேறல் நுளையர்க்கு ஈயும்","பொலம்பூண் எவ்வி நீழல் அன்ன","நலம்பெறு பணைத்தோள் நன்னுதல் அரிவையொடு","மணங்கமழ் தண்பொழில் அல்கி நெருநை","நீதற் பிழைத்தமை அறிந்து","கலுழ்ந்த கண்ணளெம் அணங்கன் னானே"],"p":"","r":"Refusing the husband entry, the friend tells him that their lady, lovely as a goddess, wept when she learned he had wronged her yesterday by staying in a fragrant grove with the bright-browed girl whose shoulders are as comforting as the shade of Evvi, the lord who, when farmers and salt-makers came to blows over salt pans spoilt by threshing chaff, had the grey elders part them and gave toddy to the fishermen.","n":"The long scene of the brawl belongs to the simile: it shows Evvi as a peacemaker and giver, and so makes the rival's shoulders a place of shelter and peace, a generous praise of the other woman that sharpens the wife's tears. The friend's charge is in two words, நீதற் பிழைத்தமை: you wronged her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 366","lb":"A366","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தையிற் பிரிந்துவந்த தலைமகன் வாயில் வேண்டியவிடத்துத் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","வாயில் வேண்டியவிடத்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["மருதம்","மருதம்",1,"maram","scene",""],["கள்ளார்","கள்",3,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["களமர்","களமர்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["பகடு","பகடு",3,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["வெள்ளுப்புச்","உப்பு",7,"pira","scene",""],["உழவரொடு","உழவர்",8,"seythi","scene",""],["தேறல்","தேறல்",11,"unavu","gift_food",""],["நுளையர்க்கு","நுளையர்",11,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","எவ்வி",12]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:367":{"book":"akananuru","num":368,"t":["இலங்குசுடர் மண்டிலம் புலந்தலைப் பெயர்ந்து","பல்கதிர் மழுகிய கல்சேர் அமையத்து","அலந்தலை மூதேறு ஆண்குரல் விளிப்ப","மனைவளர் நொச்சி மாசேர்பு வதிய","முனையுழை இருந்த அம்குடிச் சீறூர்க்","கருங்கால் வேங்கைச் செஞ்சுவல் வரகின்","மிகுபதம் நிறைந்த தொகுகூட்டு ஒருசிறைக்","குவியடி வெருகின் பைங்கண் ஏற்றை","ஊன்நசைப் பிணவின் உயங்குபசி களைஇயர்","தளிர்புரை கொடிற்றின் செறிமயிர் எருத்திற்","கதிர்த்த சென்னிக் கவிர்ப்பூ அன்ன","நெற்றிச் சேவல் அற்றம் பார்க்கும்","புல்லென் மாலையும் இனிது மன்றம்ம-","நல்லக வனமுலை அடையப் புல்லுதொறும்","உயிர்குழைப் பன்ன சாயற்","செயிர்தீர் இன்துணைப் புணர்ந்திசி னோர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"Answering the friend who urges her to bear up, the girl says that the dreary evening, when the old bull bellows, the cattle rest by the nochchi hedge of the frontier hamlet and the male civet lies in wait by the millet store to seize the red-combed rooster and ease his hungry mate, must indeed be sweet to those who hold close their faultless companion, whose softness makes life melt at every embrace.","n":"She never says the evening is bitter for her; she says it is sweet for the joined, and lets the friend draw the rest. Inside that sweet scene the civet hunting for his mate is the one creature that provides for a partner at nightfall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","maalai","punarchi","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 367","lb":"A367","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் வற்புறுக்கும் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","வற்புறுக்கும் தோழிக்குச்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மூதேறு","ஏறு",3,"maa","scene","bull"],["நொச்சி","நொச்சி",4,"maram","scene",""],["வேங்கைச்","வேங்கை",6,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["வரகின்","வரகு",6,"unavu","scene",""],["வெருகின்","வெருகு",8,"maa","iraicchi","civet"],["தளிர்புரை","தளிர்",10,"maram","simile",""],["கவிர்ப்பூ","கவிர்",11,"maram","simile",""],["சேவல்","சேவல்",12,"pul","scene","rooster"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வெருகின்","confirmed","2/2","even a wild male stays at nightfall to feed his mate, while her own man has gone and left her to the evening alone"]],"ch":"a:19"},"a:368":{"book":"akananuru","num":369,"t":["தொடுதோற் கானவன் சூடுறு வியன்புனம்","கரிபுறம் கழீஇய பெரும்பாட்டு ஈரத்துக்","தொடுவளர் பைந்தினை நீடுகுரல் காக்கும்","ஒண்தொடி மகளிர்க்கு ஊசல் ஆக","ஆடுசினை ஒழித்த கோடுஇணர் கஞலிய","குறும்பொறை அயலது நெடுந்தாள் வேங்கை","மடமயிற் குடுமியின் தோன்றும் நாடன்","உயர்வரை மருங்கின் காந்தளம் சோலைக்","குரங்குஅறி வாரா மரம்பயில் இறும்பிற்","கடிசுனைத் தெளிந்த மணிமருள் தீநீர்","பிடிபுணர் களிற்றின் எம்மொடு ஆடிப்","பல்நாள் உம்பர்ப் பெயர்ந்து சில்நாள்","கழியா மையே வழிவழிப் பெருகி","அம்பணை விளைந்த தேக்கட் டேறல்","வண்டுபடு கண்ணியர் மகிழும் சீறூர்","எவன்கொல்- வாழி, தோழி!- கொங்கர்","மணியரை யாத்து மறுகின் ஆடும்","உள்ளி விழவின் அன்ன","அலர்ஆ கின்றது பலர்வாய்ப் பட்டே?"],"p":"","r":"With the hero hidden by day, the friend asks the girl why, when the hill lord, in whose land the vengai flowers like a peahen's crest above the millet-guarding girls' swing, played with them like a tusker with his cow in a clear spring of a grove no monkey reaches, and went away for many days, gossip has grown in the toddy-drinking hamlet, in many mouths, like the Ulli festival of the Kongar who dance in the streets with bells at their waists.","n":"The question is for the listener: the meeting was rare and long ago, yet the talk is already as loud as a festival, so secret love can no longer stay secret. The day tryst is at a place no monkey can reach, which makes the town's knowing all the stranger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","kuri","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 368","lb":"A368","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பகலே சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகட்குத் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","பகலே சிறைப்புறமாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கானவன்","கானவன்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["பைந்தினை","தினை",3,"unavu","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",6,"maram","simile","kino tree"],["மடமயிற்","மயில்",7,"pul","simile",""],["காந்தளம்","காந்தள்",8,"maram","scene",""],["குரங்குஅறி","குரங்கு",9,"maa","scene",""],["பிடிபுணர்","பிடி",11,"maa","simile",""],["களிற்றின்","களிறு",11,"maa","simile",""],["அம்பணை","பணை",14,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["டேறல்","தேறல்",14,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வண்டுபடு","வண்டு",15,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:369":{"book":"akananuru","num":370,"t":["கண்டிசின்- மகளே!- கெழீஇ இயைவெனை","ஒண்தொடி செறித்த முன்கை ஊழ்கொள்பு","மங்கையர் பலபா ராட்டச் செந்தார்க்","கிள்ளையும் தீம்பால் உண்ணா மயிலியற்","சேயிழை மகளிர் ஆயமும் அயரா","தாழியும் மலர்பல அணியா கேழ்கொளக்","காழ்புனைந்து இயற்றிய வனப்பமை நோன்சுவர்","பாவையும் பலிஎனப் பெறாஅ நோய்பொர","இவை கண்டு இனைவதன் தலையும் நினைவிலேன்","கொடியோள் முன்னியது உணரேன் 'தொடியோய்!","இன்றுநின் ஒலிகுரல் மண்ணல்' என்றதற்கு,","எற்புலந்து அழிந்தன ளாகித் தற்றகக்","கடல்அம் தானைக் கைவண் சோழர்","கெடல்அரு நல்லிசை உறந்தை அன்ன","நிதியுடை நல்கர்ப் புதுவது புனைந்து","தமர்மணன் அயரவும் ஒல்லாள் கவர்முதல்","ஓமை நீடிய உலவை நீளிடை","மணியணி பலகை மாக்காழ் நெடுவேல்","துணிவுடை உள்ளமொடு துதைந்த முன்பின்","அறியாத் தேஎத்து அருஞ்சுரம் மடுத்த","சிறியோற்கு ஒத்தஎன் பெருமடத் தகுவி","'சிறப்பும் சீரும் இன்றிச் சீறூர்","நல்கூர் பெண்டின் புல்வேய் குரம்பை","ஓர்ஆ யாத்த ஒருதூண் முன்றில்","ஏதில் வறுமனைச் சிலம்புடன் கழீஇ","மேயினள் கொல்?' என நோவல் யானே"],"p":"","r":"The foster mother grieves that, since the girl went, the parrot will not drink its milk, her playmates do not play, the potted plant gets no flowers and the doll no offering; she missed the girl's purpose when she offered to wash her hair, and the girl, hurt, refused the rich wedding her kin were preparing in a house like Uranthai of the Cholas, and went over the wasteland with a young man of steady heart and long spear, and now the mother wonders whether her daughter had her anklets taken off in a poor woman's grass hut with one pillar and one cow, without honour or ceremony.","n":"The house is described through the girl's things left without her, and the grief turns at the end from her loss to her status: what hurts is that the anklet-removal of a wedding happened in a stranger's poor hut. The words கண்டிசின் மகளே (line 1) address someone the speaker calls daughter; whether it is the girl's own mother is not clear from the poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["udanpokku","varaivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 369","lb":"A369","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய செவிலி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட் போக்கிய செவிலி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கிள்ளையும்","கிள்ளை",4,"pul","scene",""],["தீம்பால்","பால்",4,"unavu","absent",""],["மயிலியற்","மயில்",4,"pul","simile",""],["ஓமை","ஓமை",17,"maram","scene",""],["புல்வேய்","புல்",23,"maram","scene",""],["ஓர்ஆ","ஆ",24,"maa","scene","cow"]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",13],["town","உறந்தை",14]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:370":{"book":"akananuru","num":371,"t":["'வளைவாய்க் கோதையர் வண்டல் தைஇ","இளையோர் செல்ப எல்லும் எல்லின்று","அகலிலைப் புன்னைப் புகர்இல் நீழல்","பகலே எம்மொடு ஆடிஇரவே","காயல் வேய்ந்த தேயா நல்லில்","நோயொடு வைகுதி ஆயின் நுந்தை","அருங்கடிப் படுவலும்' என்றி; மற்று 'நீ","செல்லல்' என்றலும் ஆற்றாய் 'செலினே","வாழலென்' என்றி ஆயின் ஞாழல்","வண்டுபடத் ததைந்த கண்ணி நெய்தல்","தண்ணரும் பைந்தார் துயல்வர அந்திக்","கடல்கெழு செல்வி கரைநின் றாங்கு","நீயே கானல் ஒழிய யானே","வெறிகொள் பாவையிற் பொலிந்தஎன் அணிதுறந்து","ஆடுமகள் போலப் பெயர்தல்","ஆற்றேன் தெய்ய அலர்கவிவ் வூரே!"],"p":"","r":"With the hero hidden by day, the friend reminds the girl that she once said that if, after playing with them in the punnai shade by day, he lay lovesick at night, she would fall under her father's strict guard, and that she cannot bring herself to tell him not to go yet says she will not live if he goes; so, with the girl staying in the grove like the sea goddess on the shore at dusk, the friend cannot bear to go home stripped of her own finery like a dancing girl, and says let the town talk.","n":"Whose words are quoted in lines 1 to 9, and who will 'fall under guard', can be read more than one way; on any reading the friend's closing அலர்கவிவ் வூரே is spoken for the man to hear, daring the gossip so that he will end it by marriage.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kuri","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 370","lb":"A370","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகலே சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தோழி சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","பகலே சிறைப்புறமாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["புன்னைப்","புன்னை",3,"maram","scene",""],["காயல்","காயல்",5,"pira","scene",""],["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",9,"maram","ornament",""],["வண்டுபடத்","வண்டு",10,"pira","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",10,"maram","ornament",""],["செல்வி","கடல்கெழு செல்வி",12,"deyvam","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:371":{"book":"akananuru","num":372,"t":["அவ்விளிம்பு உரீஇய விசையமை நோன்சிலை","செவ்வாய்ப் பகழிச் செயிர்நோக்கு ஆடவர்","கணையிடக் கழிந்ததன் வீழ்துணை உள்ளிக்","குறுநெடுந் துணைய மறிபுடை ஆடப்","புன்கண் கொண்ட திரிமருப்பு இரலை","மேய்பதம் மறுத்த சிறுமையொடு நோய்கூர்ந்து","நெய்தலம் படுவில் சில்நீர் உண்ணாது","எஃகுஉறு மாந்தரின் இனைந்துகண் படுக்கும்","பைதற வெம்பிய பாழ்சேர் அத்தம்","எமியம் நீந்தும் எம்மினும் பனிவார்ந்து","என்ன ஆம்கொல் தாமே 'தெண்நீர்","ஆய்சுனை நிகர்மலர் போன்ம்' என நசைஇ","வீதேர் பறவை விழையும்","போதார் கூந்தல்நம் காதலி கண்ணே?"],"p":"","r":"Crossing alone the scorched waste where a twist-horned stag, his mate shot by hunters, grieves with the fawns playing at his side, refuses to graze, will not drink the little water of the pond and lies down stricken like a speared man, the man asks his heart what will become of his beloved's eyes, which bees take for flowers of a clear spring, streaming tears even more than he.","n":"He measures her grief by his own and finds hers greater: the stag, who has really lost his mate, is the image of what the man's going does to the one left. The fawns playing unaware at the stag's side make the loss sharper.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 371","lb":"A371","poet":"எயினந்தை மகன் இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்து போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்து போகாநின்ற"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["மறிபுடை","மறி",4,"maa","scene","fawn"],["இரலை","இரலை",5,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["நெய்தலம்","நெய்தல்",7,"maram","scene",""],["சுனை","சுனை",12,"pira","simile",""],["பறவை","பறவை",13,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","இரலை","confirmed","1/1","even a beast pines when parted from its mate, so his leaving will waste the woman he leaves behind"]],"ch":"a:19"},"a:372":{"book":"akananuru","num":373,"t":["அருந்தெறன் மரபின் கடவுள் காப்பப்","பெருந்தேன் தூங்கும் நாடுகாண் நனந்தலை","அணங்குடை வரைப்பிற் பாழி ஆங்கண்","வேண்முது மாக்கள் வியனகர்க் கரந்த","அருங்கல வெறுக்கையின் அரியோள் பண்புநினைந்து","வருந்தினம் மாதோ எனினும் அஃது ஒல்லாய்","இரும்பணை தொடுத்த பலராடு ஊசல்","ஊர்ந்திழி கயிற்றின் செலவர வருந்தி","நெடுநெறிக் குதிரைக் கூர்வேல் அஞ்சி","கடுமுனை அலைத்த கொடுவில் ஆடவர்","ஆடுகொள் பூசலின் பாடுசிறந்து எறியும்","பெருந்துடி வள்பின் வீங்குபு நெகிழா","மேய்மணி இழந்த பாம்பின் நீநனி","தேம்பினை- வாழிஎன் நெஞ்சே!- வேந்தர்","கோண்தணி எயிலிற் காப்புச் சிறந்து","ஈண்டுஅருங் குரையள்நம் அணங்கி யோளே"],"p":"","r":"Turning back from a tryst that a false signal spoiled, the man tells his heart, which would not accept that the girl is as hard to win as the treasure the old Velir hid in their city at Pazhi under a dread god's guard, that it has swung to and fro like a swing-rope on a tall palm, tightened and slackened like the strap of a raider's war drum, and pined like a snake that has lost its gem, for she is guarded closer than a kings' fortress.","n":"Each simile measures the hardness of the girl's guard, and each is one the hero may use openly. The poem shares its opening thought and its turn எனினும் அஃது ஒல்லாய் with Akananuru 258, which is under the same occasion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","nenju","kaamam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 372","lb":"A372","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அல்ல குறிப்பிட்டுப் போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","அல்ல குறிப்பிட்டுப் போகாநின்ற"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",1,"deyvam","scene",""],["பெருந்தேன்","தேன்",2,"pira","scene","honeycomb"],["இரும்பணை","பணை",7,"maram","simile","palm"],["குதிரைக்","குதிரை",9,"maa","scene",""],["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",10,"seythi","scene",""],["பெருந்துடி","துடி",12,"parai","simile",""],["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",13,"pira","simile","snake"]],"en":[["town","பாழி",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:373":{"book":"akananuru","num":374,"t":["முனைகவர்ந்து கொண்டெனக் கலங்கிப் பீர்எழுந்து","மனைபாழ் பட்ட மரைசேர் மன்றத்துப்","பணைத்தாள் யானை பரூஉப்புறம் உரிஞ்சக்","செதுகாழ் சாய்ந்த முதுகாற் பொதியில்","அருஞ்சுரம் நீந்திய வருத்தமொடு கையற்றுப்","பெரும்புன் மாலை புலம்புவந்து உறுதர","மீளிஉள்ளம் செலவுவலி யுறுப்பத்","தாள்கை பூட்டிய தனிநிலை இருக்கையொடு","தன்னிலை உள்ளும் நந்நிலை உணராள்","இரும்பல் கூந்தல் சேயிழை மடந்தை","கனையிருள் நடுநாள் அணையொடு பொருந்தி","வெய்துற்றுப் புலக்கும் நெஞ்சமொடு ஐதுஉயிரா","ஆயிதழ் மழைக்கண் மல்கநோய் கூர்ந்து","பெருந்தோள் நனைக்கும் கலுழ்ந்துவார் அரிப்பனி","மெல்விரல் உகிரின் தெறியினள் வென்வேல்","அண்ணல் யானை அடுபோர் வேந்தர்","ஒருங்குஅகப் படுத்த முரவுவாய் ஞாயில்","ஓர்எயில் மன்னன் போலத்","துயில்துறந் தனள்கொல்? அளியள் தானே!"],"p":"","r":"Worn out by the wasteland and sitting alone at a desolate dusk with his hands round his knees, in the ruined hall of a village emptied by raiders where an elephant rubs against the rotting post, the man pictures the girl, who knows her own state but not his, lying on her bed at midnight sighing and sulking, flicking away with her nails the tears that soak her shoulders, and asks whether she has given up sleep like the king of a single fort besieged by many kings, poor girl.","n":"He imagines her blaming him (புலக்கும் நெஞ்சமொடு) because she cannot see that he too is suffering: the pity of the last line is partly for himself. The besieged chieftain is an image of sleepless watching against odds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 373","lb":"A373","poet":"பாண்டியன் ஏனாதி நெடுங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிந்து போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் இடைச்சுரத்துத் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"இடைச்சுரத்துத் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["பீர்எழுந்து","பீர்",1,"maram","scene",""],["மரைசேர்","மரை",2,"maa","scene","deer"],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",16,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:374":{"book":"akananuru","num":375,"t":["மாக்கடல் முகந்து மாதிரத்து இருளி","மலர்தலை உலகம் புதைய வலன்ஏர்பு","பழங்கண் கொண்ட கொழும்பல் கொண்மூ","போழ்ந்த போலப் பலவுடன் மின்னி","தாழ்ந்த போல நனியணி வந்து","சோர்ந்த போலச் சொரிவன பயிற்றி","இடியும் முழக்கும் இன்றிப் பாணர்","வடியுறு நல்யாழ் நரம்புஇசைத் தன்ன","இன்குரல் அழிதுளி தலைஇ நன்பல","பெயல்பெய்து கழிந்த பூநாறு வைகறைச்","செறிமணல் நிவந்த களர்தோன்று இயவில்","குறுமோட்டு மூதாய் குறுகுறு ஓடி","மணிமண்டு பவளம் போலக் காயா","அணிமிகு செம்மல் ஒளிப்பன மறையக்","கார்கவின் கொண்ட காமர் காலைச்","செல்க தேரே- நல்வலம் பெறுந!","பெருந்தோள் நுணுகிய நுசுப்பின்","திருந்திழை அரிவை விருந்தெதிர் கொளவே!"],"p":"","r":"His campaign over, the man tells his charioteer to drive on in this lovely morning of the rains, when clouds that drank the sea flash and pour sweetly without thunder, like a bard's tuned lute, and on the sandy track the red mites run, like coral among sapphires, hiding among the fallen kaayaa blossoms, so that the slender girl may welcome him as a guest.","n":"The rain is made gentle and musical, without thunder, because for him it is now a season of return, not of reproach. The last word, விருந்து, makes the husband a guest to be received, the honour of a homecoming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","mazhai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 374","lb":"A374","poet":"இடைக்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பாசறை முற்றிய தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","பாசறை முற்றிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கொண்மூ","கொண்மூ",3,"pira","scene","cloud"],["பாணர்","பாணர்",7,"seythi","simile",""],["நல்யாழ்","யாழ்",8,"yaazh","simile",""],["மூதாய்","மூதாய்",12,"pira","simile","red mite"],["காயா","காயா",13,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:375":{"book":"akananuru","num":376,"t":["'சென்று நீடுநர் அல்லர்; அவர்வயின்","இனைதல் ஆனாய்' என்றிசின் இகுளை!","அம்புதொடை அமைதி காண்மார் வம்பலர்","கலனிலர் ஆயினும் கொன்றுபுள் ஊட்டும்","கல்லா இளையர் கலித்த கவலைக்","கணநரி இனனொடு குழீஇ நிணனருந்தும்","நெய்த்தோர் ஆடிய மல்லல் மொசிவிரல்","அத்த எருவைச் சேவல் சேர்ந்த","அரைசேர் யாத்த வெண்திரள் வினைவிறல்","எழாஅத் திணிதோள் சோழர் பெருமகன்","விளங்குபுகழ் நிறுத்த இளம்பெருஞ் சென்னி","குடிக்கடன் ஆகலின் குறைவினை முடிமார்","செம்புஉறழ் புரிசைப் பாழி நூறி","வம்ப வடுகர் பைந்தலை சவட்டிக்","கொன்ற யானைக் கோட்டின் தோன்றும்","அஞ்சுவரு மரபின் வெஞ்சுரம் இறந்தோர்","நோயிலர் பெயர்தல் அறியின்","ஆழல மன்னோ தோழி! என் கண்ணே"],"p":"","r":"To the friend who said he will not stay away long, the girl answers that if she knew that the man who crossed the dread wasteland, where raw youths kill even empty-handed travellers to test their arrows and feed the birds, and where the vulture, its claws wet with blood, perched on the white yaa trunk looks like the tusk of an elephant of Ilamperunchenni, the Chola who broke Pazhi's copper-strong walls and crushed the Vadukar, would come back unharmed, her eyes would not weep.","n":"Her answer turns the friend's comfort from 'he will return soon' to 'will he return alive': her fear is not delay but death on the road. The Chola's war elephant, the vulture and the arrows make the wasteland a battlefield.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","acham","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 375","lb":"A375","poet":"இடையன் சேந்தங் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["புள்","புள்",4,"pul","scene",""],["இளையர்","இளையர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["கணநரி","நரி",6,"maa","scene",""],["எருவைச்","எருவை",8,"pul","simile","vulture"],["யாத்த","யா",9,"maram","scene",""],["யானைக்","யானை",15,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",10],["king","இளம்பெருஞ் சென்னி",11],["town","பாழி",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:376":{"book":"akananuru","num":377,"t":["செல்லல் மகிழ்ந! நிற் செய்கடன் உடையென்மன்-","கல்லா யானை கடிபுனல் கற்றென","மலிபுனல் பொருத மருதொங்கு படப்பை","ஒலிகதிர்க் கழனிக் கழாஅர் முன்துறை","கலிகொள் சுற்றமொடு கரிகால் காணத்","தண்பதம் கொண்டு தவிர்த்த இன்னிசை","ஒண்பொறிப் புனைகழல் சேவடி புரளக்","கருங்கச்சு யாத்த காண்பின் அவ்வயிற்று","இரும்பொலப் பாண்டில் மணியொடு தெளிர்ப்பப்","புனல்நயந்து ஆடும் அத்தி அணிநயந்து","காவிரி கொண்டுஒளித் தாங்கு மன்னோ!","நும்வயிற் புலத்தல் செல்லேம்; எம்வயின்","பசந்தன்று காண்டிசின் நுதலே; அசும்பின்","அம்தூம்பு வள்ளை அழற்கொடி மயக்கி","வண்தோட்டு நெல்லின் வாங்குபீள் விரியத்","துய்த்தலை முடங்குஇறாத் தெறிக்கும் பொற்புடைக்","குரங்குஉளைப் புரவிக் குட்டுவன்","மரந்தை அன்னஎன் நலம்தந்து சென்மே!"],"p":"","r":"The beloved courtesan tells the lord not to go: once she owed him every duty, but now, as the Kaveri seized and hid Atthi when he danced adorned in its flood at Kazhar before Karikal and his crowd, he has been taken from her; she will not sulk at him, but let him see her paled brow, give back her beauty, which was like Kuttuvan's Maranthai, and then go.","n":"The Atthi legend is here turned around: the woman who loses the man to the river is not his wife but the courtesan, and the river that keeps him is, by implication, his household. The closing demand நலம்தந்து சென்மே is the same as Akananuru 396's, also a courtesan's. The first two lines are compressed and their sense uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 376","lb":"A376","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"காதற் பரத்தை புலந்து சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thalaivan",null,"karpu","காதற் பரத்தை புலந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","scene",""],["மருதொங்கு","மருது",3,"maram","scene",""],["ஒலிகதிர்க்","கதிர்",4,"maram","scene","paddy ear"],["வள்ளை","வள்ளை",14,"maram","simile",""],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",15,"maram","simile",""],["முடங்குஇறாத்","இறா",16,"pira","simile","prawn"],["புரவிக்","புரவி",17,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["town","கழாஅர்",4],["king","கரிகால்",5],["river","காவிரி",11],["king","குட்டுவன்",17],["town","மரந்தை",18]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:377":{"book":"akananuru","num":378,"t":["கோடை நீடலின் வாடுபுலத்து உக்க","சிறுபுல் உணவு நெறிபட மறுகி","நுண்பல் எறும்பி கொண்டளைச் செறித்த","வித்தா வல்சி வீங்குசிலை மறவர்","பல்லூழ் புக்குப் பயன்நிரை கவரக்","கொழுங்குடி போகிய பெரும்பாழ் மன்றத்து","நரைமூ தாளர் அதிர்தலை இறக்கிக்","கவைமனத்து இருத்தும் வல்லுவனப்பு அழிய","வரிநிறச் சிதலை அரித்தலின் புல்லென்று","பெருநலம் சிதைந்த பேஎம்முதிர் பொதியில்","இன்னா ஒருசிறைத் தங்கி இன்னகைச்","சிறுமென் சாயல் பெருநலம் உள்ளி","வம்பலர் ஆகியும் கழிப மன்ற-","நசைதர வந்தோர் இரந்தவை","இசைபடப் பெய்தல் ஆற்று வோரே!"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart, which has urged him to go for wealth, that those who can give with renown what people who come in hope ask of them must surely pass their nights as wanderers in some corner of a haunted common hall, its dice board crumbled and its pillars eaten by termites, in a village whose families fled when bowmen raided its cattle and where ants store fallen grass seed, thinking all the while of the soft beauty of their smiling women.","n":"The poem gives the price of the giver's fame: the wealth for giving is earned on roads of ruin, and the giver sleeps among ruins with his wife only in his thoughts. The ants' hoard in the drought-dried field is the one store the raiders could not take.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","pirivu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 377","lb":"A377","poet":"மாநோக்கத்துக் காமக்கணி நப்பாலத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் கடைக்கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பொருள் கடைக்கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["சிறுபுல்","புல்",2,"maram","scene",""],["எறும்பி","எறும்பு",3,"pira","scene","ant"],["வல்சி","வல்சி",4,"unavu","scene",""],["மறவர்","மறவர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["நிரை","நிரை",5,"maa","scene","cattle"],["சிதலை","சிதலை",9,"pira","scene","termite"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:19"},"a:378":{"book":"akananuru","num":379,"t":["'நிதியம் துஞ்சும் நிவந்தோங்கு வரைப்பின்","வதுவை மகளிர் கூந்தல் கமழ்கொள","வங்கூழ் ஆட்டிய அம்குழை வேங்கை","நன்பொன் அன்ன நறுந்தாது உதிரக்","காமர் பீலி ஆய்மயில் தோகை","வேறுவேறு இனத்த வரைவாழ் வருடைக்","கோடுமுற்று இளந்தகர் பாடுவிறந்து இயல","ஆடுகள வயிரின் இனிய ஆலிப்","பசும்புற மென்சீர் ஒசிய விசும்புஉகந்து","இருங்கண் ஆடுஅமைத் தயங்க இருக்கும்","பெருங்கல் நாடன் பிரிந்த புலம்பும்","உடன்ற அன்னை அமரா நோக்கமும்","வடந்தை தூக்கும் வருபனி அற்சிரச்","சுடர்கெழு மண்டிலம் அழுங்க ஞாயிறு","குடகடல் சேரும் படர்கூர் மாலையும்","அனைத்தும் அடூஉநன்று நலிய உஞற்றி","யாங்ஙனம் வாழ்தி?' என்றி- தோழி!-","நீங்கா வஞ்சினம் செய்துநத் துறந்தோர்","உள்ளார் ஆயினும் உளெனே- அவர் நாட்டு","அள்ளிலைப் பலவின் கனிகவர் கைய","கல்லா மந்தி கடுவனோடு உகளும்","கடுந்திறல் அணங்கின் நெடும்பெருங் குன்றத்துப்","பாடின் அருவி சூடி","வான்தோய் சிமையம் தோன்ற லானே"],"p":"","r":"With the hero hidden by night, the girl answers the friend who asked how she can live through the lord's parting, her angry mother's look and the anguished evenings of the cold season, in a land where wind shakes gold-like vengai pollen into brides' hair and the peacock cries sweet as a festival trumpet among the swaying bamboo: though the man who left after an oath does not think of her, she lives because the sky-touching peak of his hill, crowned with a singing waterfall where monkeys snatch jackfruit and frolic with their mates, is in sight.","n":"The friend's question is put so that the hidden man hears the full list of what the girl bears, and the girl's answer, that she lives on the sight of his hill, is a reproach he cannot miss. The pair of monkeys at play in his own land is the togetherness he has denied her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","kalaviyal","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 378","lb":"A378","poet":"காவட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிச் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி சொல்லெடுப்பத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறிச் சிறைப்புறமாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",3,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["மயில்","மயில்",5,"pul","scene",""],["வருடைக்","வருடை",6,"maa","scene",""],["இளந்தகர்","தகர்",7,"maa","scene",""],["வயிரின்","வயிர்",8,"yaazh","simile","horn"],["ஆடுஅமைத்","அமை",10,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["வடந்தை","வடந்தை",13,"pira","scene","north wind"],["பலவின்","பலா",20,"maram","scene",""],["மந்தி","மந்தி",21,"maa","iraicchi",""],["அணங்கின்","அணங்கு",22,"deyvam","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",23,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மந்தி","confirmed","2/2","in his own land even the monkeys keep their mates by them, while he who swore not to leave lives apart from her"]],"ch":"a:19"},"a:379":{"book":"akananuru","num":380,"t":["நந்நயந்து உறைவி தொன்னலம் அழியத்","தெருளா மையின் தீதொடு கெழீஇ","அருளற நிமிர்ந்த முன்பொடு பொருள்புரிந்து","ஆள்வினைக்கு எதிரிய மீளிநெஞ்சே!","நினையினை ஆயின் எனவ கேண்மதி!","விரிதிரை முந்நீர் மண்திணி கிடக்கைப்","பரிதிஅம் செல்வம் பொதுமை இன்றி","நனவின் இயன்றது ஆயினும் கங்குற்","கனவின் அற்று அதன் கழிவே அதனால்","விரவுறு பன்மலர் வண்டுசூழ்பு அடைச்சிச்","சுவல்மிசை அரைஇய நிலைதயங்கு உறுமுடி","ஈண்டுபல் நாற்றம் வேண்டுவயின் உவப்பச்","செய்வுறு விளங்கிழைப் பொலிந்த தோள்சேர்பு","எய்திய கனைதுயில் ஏற்றொறும் திருகி","மெய்புகு வன்ன கைகவர் முயக்கின்","மிகுதிகண் டன்றோ இலெனே நீ நின்","பல்பொருள் வேட்கையின் சொல்வரை நீவிச்","செலவுவலி யுறுத்தனை ஆயிற் காலொடு","கனைஎரி நிகழ்ந்த இலையில் அம் காட்டு","உழைப்புறத்து அன்ன புள்ளி நீழல்","அசைஇய பொழுதில் பசைஇய வந்துஇவள்","மறப்புஅரும் பல்குணம் நிறத்துவந்து உறுதர","ஒருதிறம் நினைத்தல் செல்லாய் திரிபுநின்று","உறுபுலி உழந்த வடுமருப்பு ஒருத்தற்குப்","பிடியிடு பூசலின் அடிபடக் குழிந்த","நிரம்பா நீளிடைத் தூங்கி","இரங்குவை அல்லையோ உரங்கெட மெலிந்தே?"],"p":"","r":"The man, home once with wealth and now pressed again by his heart, tells it that even sole rule of the sea-girt earth passes like a dream of the night, that he has not yet had his fill of her clinging embrace as she sleeps on his shoulder with her flower-crowned hair, and that if it makes him break his word and go, it will rest in the dappled shade of a burnt leafless forest, be struck by her many virtues, and pine on the endless road where a cow elephant trumpets for her tusker wounded by a tiger.","n":"The argument against going is the impermanence of wealth, a puram commonplace, put to an akam use: since wealth passes, the embrace, not the treasure, is what should be held. The cow elephant crying for her wounded mate is what he expects to hear when the memory of the wife strikes him on the road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 379","lb":"A379","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"முன்னொரு காலத்துப் பெருண்முற்றி வந்த தலைமகன் பின்னும் பொருள் கடைக்கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பொருள் கடைக்கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குச்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["வண்டுசூழ்பு","வண்டு",10,"pira","scene",""],["உழைப்புறத்து","உழை",20,"maa","simile","deer"],["உறுபுலி","புலி",24,"maa","scene","tiger"],["ஒருத்தற்குப்","ஒருத்தல்",24,"maa","scene","tusker"],["பிடியிடு","பிடி",25,"maa","iraicchi","cow elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பிடியிடு","confirmed","1/1","a mate cries out for the partner in danger, as the wife left behind will grieve for him on that road"]],"ch":"a:19"},"a:380":{"book":"akananuru","num":381,"t":["தேர்சேண் நீக்கித் தமியன் வந்து, நும்","ஊர்யாது? என்ன நணிநணி ஒதுங்கி","முன்னாள் போகிய துறைவன் நெருநை","அகலிலை நாவல் உண்துறை உதிர்த்த","கனிகவின் சிதைய வாங்கிக் கொண்டுதன்","தாழை வேர்அளை வீழ்துணைக்கு இடூஉம்","அலவற் காட்டி, 'நற்பாற்று இது' என","நினைந்த நெஞ்சமொடு நெடிதுபெயர்ந் தோனே","உதுக்காண் தோன்றும் தேரே இன்றும்","நாம்எதிர் கொள்ளா மாயின் தான்அது","துணிகுவன் போலாம் நாணுமிக உடையன்","வெண்மணல் நெடுங்கோட்டு மறைகோ?-","அம்ம, தோழி!- கூறுமதி நீயே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend, having agreed to help the hero, tells the girl that the lord of the shore who came alone, leaving his chariot far off, and drew near asking the name of their town, went away yesterday thoughtful after showing them a crab that tore a naaval fruit at the ford and carried it to its mate in its burrow under the screwpine, saying 'this is a fine way'; now his chariot is in sight, and he is so modest that if they do not receive him today he may resolve on that; should she hide behind the long white dune?","n":"The man's comment on the crab is his only open speech, and it carries his intent: the crab that brings food home to its mate is the life he offers her. What he 'may resolve on' (அது) is left unsaid, and the friend's last question hands the decision to the girl.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","madal","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 380","lb":"A380","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பின்னின்ற தலைமகற்குக் குறைநேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குக் குறைநயப்பக் கூறியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","குறைநேர்ந்த தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நாவல்","நாவல்",4,"maram","scene",""],["கனிகவின்","கனி",5,"unavu","gift_food","fruit"],["தாழை","தாழை",6,"maram","scene",""],["அலவற்","அலவன்",7,"pira","iraicchi","crab"],["வெண்மணல்","மணல்",12,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","அலவற்","confirmed","1/1","the man who points it out means to take the girl home and provide for her as the crab does for its mate"]],"ch":"a:19"},"a:381":{"book":"akananuru","num":382,"t":["ஆளி நன்மான் அணங்குடை ஒருத்தல்","மீளி வேழத்து நெடுந்தகை புலம்ப","ஏந்தல் வெண்கோடு வாங்கிக் குருகருந்தும்","அஞ்சுவரத் தகுந ஆங்கண் மஞ்சுதப","அழல்கான்று திரிதரும் அலங்குகதிர் மண்டிலம்","நிழல்சூன்று உண்ட நிரம்பா நீளிடை","கற்றுரிக் குடம்பைக் கதநாய் வடுகர்","விற்சினம் தணிந்த வெருவரு கவலை","குருதி ஆடிய புலவுநாறு இருஞ்சிறை","எருவைச் சேவல் ஈண்டுகிளைத் தொழுதி","பச்சூன் கொள்ளை சாற்றிப் பறைநிவந்து","செக்கர் வானின் விசும்பணி கொள்ளும்","அருஞ்சுரம் நீந்திய நம்மினும் பொருந்தார்","முனைஅரண் கடந்த வினைவல் தானைத்","தேனிமிர் நறுந்தார் வானவன் உடற்றிய","ஒன்னாத் தெவ்வர் மன்னெயில் போலப்","பெரும்பாழ் கொண்ட மேனியள் நெடிதுயிர்த்து","வருந்தும்கொல்? அளியள் தானே!- சுரும்புண","நெடுநீர் பயந்த நிரைஇதழ்க் குவளை","எதிர்மலர் இணைப்போது அன்னதன்","அரிமதர் மழைக்கண் தெண்பனி கொளவே!"],"p":"","r":"Halfway across the wasteland the man tells his heart that she at home must be suffering more than they who crossed it: her body wasted like an enemy fort the Chera Vaanavan's army razed, sighing long, her eyes like a pair of blue water-lilies brimming with tears.","n":"The road is built of killers (the yali tearing the elephant's tusk, the sun that has eaten the shade, the Vadukar with their dogs, the blood-bathed vultures that make the sky look like dusk), and the turn at line 13 (நம்மினும்) makes all of it a measure of her pain rather than his danger. Line 3 (குருகருந்தும்) and line 7 (கற்றுரிக் குடம்பை) are hard words in our text.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 381","lb":"A381","poet":"மதுரை இளங்கௌசிகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் இடைச் சுரத்துத் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","தலைமகன் இடைச் சுரத்துத் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"],["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["ஆளி","ஆளி",1,"maa","scene",""],["வேழத்து","வேழம்",2,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கதநாய்","நாய்",7,"maa","scene",""],["வடுகர்","வடுகர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["எருவைச்","எருவை",10,"pul","scene","vulture"],["சுரும்புண","சுரும்பு",18,"pira","simile",""],["குவளை","குவளை",19,"maram","simile","blue water-lily"]],"en":[["king","வானவன்",15]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:382":{"book":"akananuru","num":383,"t":["பிறருறு விழுமம் பிறரும் நோப","தம்முறு விழுமம் தமக்கோ தஞ்சம்","கடம்புகொடி யாத்துக் கண்ணி சூட்டி","வேறுபல் குரல ஒருதூக்கு இன்னியம்","காடுகெழு நெடுவேட் பாடுகொளைக்கு ஏற்ப","அணங்கயர் வியன் களம் பொலியப் பையத்","தூங்குதல் புரிந்தனர் நமரென ஆங்கவற்கு","அறியக் கூறல் வேண்டும்- தோழி!-","அருவி பாய்ந்த கருவிரல் மந்தி","செழுங்கோட் பலவின் பழம்புணை யாகச்","சாரல் பேரூர் முன்துறை இழிதரும்","வறனுறல் அறியாச் சோலை","விறன்மலை நாடன் சொல்நயந் தோயே!"],"p":"","r":"With the lover listening outside at night, the friend tells the girl that their kin have resolved to hold the possession rite for Murugan, with kadamba tied, garlands, and many-voiced instruments, and that he, lord of the hill where a monkey floats down a waterfall on a jackfruit to the town's ford, must be told of it.","n":"The opening maxim (others feel another's trouble; one's own trouble is one's own to bear) is aimed at the man: the rite would publish a sickness that is his doing, and the friend sends the news to him so that he will act. The monkey riding the jackfruit down to the big town's ford can be read as a hidden image of the girl carried into public talk, but no second correspondence is anchored, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","kalavu","varaivu","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 382","lb":"A382","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிச் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி தலைமகட்கச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறிச் சிறைப்புறமாகத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["கடம்பு","கடம்பு",3,"maram","scene",""],["இன்னியம்","இன்னியம்",4,"parai","scene","instruments"],["நெடுவேட்","நெடுவேள்",5,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"],["அருவி","அருவி",9,"pira","scene",""],["மந்தி","மந்தி",9,"maa","scene","she-monkey"],["பலவின்","பலா",10,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:383":{"book":"akananuru","num":384,"t":["தற்புரந்து எடுத்த எற்றுந்து உள்ளாள்","ஊருஞ் சேரியும் ஓராங்கு அலர்எழக்","காடுங் கானமும் அவனொடு துணிந்து","நாடுந் தேயமும் நனிபல இறந்த","சிறுவன் கண்ணிக்கு ஏர்தே றுவரென","வாடினை- வாழியோ, வயலை!- நாள்தொறும்","பல்கிளைக் கொடிகொம்பு அலமர மலர்ந்த","அல்குல் தலைக்கூட்டு அம்குழை உதவிய","வினையமை வரனீர் விழுத்தொடி தத்தக்","கமஞ்சூற் பெருநிறை தயங்க முகந்துகொண்டு","ஆய்மடக் கண்ணள் தாய்முகம் நோக்கிப்","பெய்சிலம்பு ஒலிப்பப் பெயர்வனள் வைகலும்","ஆரநீர் ஊட்டிப் புரப்போர்","யார்மற்றுப் பெறுகுவை அளியை நீயே!"],"p":"","r":"The mother whose daughter has run off with a youth speaks to the vayalai creeper the girl watered every day and stripped for her leaf-skirt: the girl who never thought of the mother who raised her has crossed many lands with him amid the town's gossip, and now no one will water the withering creeper.","n":"The mother cannot address the girl, so she addresses the plant, and its needs become an inventory of the daughter's lost daily life: the full water-pot, the jumping bangles, the ringing anklets, the glance at her mother's face as she came back. Line 5 suggests the creeper's leaves were also sought for the youth's garland.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ninaivu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 383","lb":"A383","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய தாய் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட் போக்கிய தாய் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வயலை","வயலை",6,"maram","absent",""],["அம்குழை","குழை",8,"maram","ornament","tender leaves"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:384":{"book":"akananuru","num":385,"t":["இருந்த வேந்தன் அருந்தொழில் முடித்தெனப்","புரிந்த காதலொடு பெருந்தேர் யானும்","ஏறியது அறிந்தன்று அல்லது வந்த","ஆறுநனி அறிந்தன்றொ இலெனே! \"தா அய்","முயற்பறழ் உகளும் முல்லையம் புறவிற்","கவைக்கதிர் வரகின் சீறூர் ஆங்கண்","மெல்லியல் அரிவை இல்வயின் நிறீஇ","இழிமின்\" என்றநின் மொழிமருண் டிசினே!","வான்வழங்கு இயற்கை வளி பூட் டினையோ?","மானுரு ஆகநின் மனம்பூட் டினையோ","உரைமதி- வாழியோ வலவ! - எனத்தன்","வரைமருள் மார்பின் அளிப்பனன் முயங்கி","மனைக்கொண்டு புக்கனன் நெடுந்தகை","விருந்தேர் பெற்றனள் திருந்திழை யோளே!"],"p":"","r":"Home from the king's campaign, the man asks his charioteer whether he yoked the wind or his own mind as horses, since he knew only that he mounted the chariot and then heard 'get down' at his wife's house in the mullai woodland; then he embraces him, takes him in, and the wife receives a guest.","n":"The return is told as a lapse of time: longing has abolished the road. The last line turns the charioteer into the household's first guest, so the reunion is completed as hospitality.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","virundhu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 384","lb":"A384","poet":"ஒக்கூர் மாசாத்தியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன் வரவுகண்டு உழையர் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["pira","pira",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றிய தலைமகன் வரவுகண்டு உழையர்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["முயற்பறழ்","முயல்",5,"maa","scene","young hare"],["முல்லையம்","முல்லை",5,"maram","scene",""],["வரகின்","வரகு",6,"unavu","scene","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:385":{"book":"akananuru","num":386,"t":["தன்னோ ரன்ன ஆயமும் மயிலியல்","என்னோ ரன்ன தாயரும் காணக்","கைவல் யானைக் கடுந்தேர்ச் சோழர்","காவிரிப் படப்பை உறந்தை அன்ன","பொன்னுடை நெடுநகர் புரையோர் அயர","நன்மாண் விழவில் தகரம் மண்ணி","யாம்பல புணர்ப்பச் சொல்லாள் காம்பொடு","நெல்லி நீடிய கல்லறைக் கவாஅன்","அத்த ஆலத்து அலந்தலை நெடுவீழ்","தித்திக் குறங்கில் திருந்த உரிஞ","வளையுடை முன்கை அளைஇக் கிளைய","பயிலிரும் பிணையல் பசுங்காழ்க் கோவை","அகலமை அல்குல் பற்றிக் கூந்தல்","ஆடுமயிற் பீலியின் பொங்க நன்றும்","தானமர் துணைவன் ஊக்க ஊங்கி","உள்ளாது கழிந்த முள்ளெயிற்றுத் துவர்வாய்ச்","சிறுவன் கண்ணி சிலம்பு கழீஇ","அறியாத் தேஎத்தள் ஆகுதல் கொடிதே!"],"p":"","r":"The foster mother grieves that the girl, who said nothing while her playmates and mothers prepared a festival in their house rich as the Chola's Uranthai, has gone off with her lover, swinging joyfully on a banyan's root in the wasteland as he pushes her, and that she should shed her anklets for him and go to an unknown land is cruel.","n":"The swing scene is imagined, and imagined as happiness: the cruelty the foster mother names is not the girl's danger but her forgetting. The anklet removal (சிலம்பு கழீஇ) belongs to the wedding at home; the girl performs it, in the mother's mind, for the youth on the road. How lines 6 to 7 and 17 attach is not certain in our text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","pirivu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 385","lb":"A385","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய செவிலித்தாய் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட் போக்கிய செவிலித்தாய் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மயிலியல்","மயில்",1,"pul","simile","peacock"],["தகரம்","தகரம்",6,"maram","ornament","hair fragrance"],["காம்பொடு","காம்பு",7,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",8,"maram","scene",""],["ஆலத்து","ஆல்",9,"maram","scene","banyan"],["மயிற்","மயில்",14,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",3],["river","காவிரி",4],["town","உறந்தை",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:386":{"book":"akananuru","num":387,"t":["பொய்கை நீர்நாய்ப் புலவுநாறு இரும்போத்து","வாளை நாளிரை தேரும் ஊர!","நாணினென் பெரும! யானே- பாணன்","மல்லடு மார்பின் வலியுற வருந்தி","எதிர்தலைக் கொண்ட ஆரியப் பொருநன்","நிறைத்திரண் முழவுத்தோள் கையகத்து ஒழிந்த","திறன்வேறு கிடக்கை நோக்கி நற்போர்க்","கணையன் நாணி யாங்கு- மறையினள்","மெல்ல வந்து நல்ல கூறி","மைஈர் ஓதி மடவோய்! யானும்நின்","சேரி யேனே அயலி லாட்டியேன்","நுங்கை ஆகுவென் நினக்கெனத் தன்கைத்","தொடுமணி மெல்விரல் தண்ணெனத் தைவர","நுதலும் கூந்தலும் நீவி","பகல்வந்து பெயர்ந்த வாணநுதற் கண்டே!"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the husband, lord of a town where the otter hunts vaalai fish each day, that she felt the shame Kanaiyan felt when Paanan broke the Aariya wrestler, when his mistress came in secret by day, called herself a neighbour and a younger sister, stroked her brow and hair, and left.","n":"The shame is double: she was flattered and touched by the rival who now has her husband, and she recognised the trick only after. The comparison to Kanaiyan's shame is a public one: a patron watching his champion fall in the ring. The otter's daily hunt for fresh fish is the husband's appetite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 386","lb":"A386","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி வாயின் மறத்தது. தலைமகள் தகுதி சொல்லியதூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தோழி வாயின் மறத்தது"],["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தலைமகள் தகுதி சொல்லியதூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["நீர்நாய்ப்","நீர்நாய்",1,"maa","ullurai","otter"],["வாளை","வாளை",2,"pira","ullurai","fish"],["முழவுத்தோள்","முழவு",6,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[["king","பாணன்",3],["king","கணையன்",8]],"dv":[["ullurai","நீர்நாய்ப் வாளை","confirmed","1/1","the husband and the women he seeks"]],"ch":"a:20"},"a:387":{"book":"akananuru","num":388,"t":["திருந்திழை நெகிழ்ந்து பெருந்தோள் சாஅய்","அரிமதர் மழைக்கண் கலுழச் செல்வீர்!","வருவீர் ஆகுதல் உரைமின் மன்னோ","உவருணப் பறைந்த ஊன்தலைச் சிறாஅரொடு","அவ்வரி கொன்ற கறைசேர் வள்ளுகிர்ப்","பசைவிரற் புலைத்தி நெடிதுபிசைந்து ஊட்டிய","பூந்துகில் இமைக்கும் பொலன்காழ் அல்குல்","அவ்வரி சிதைய நோக்கி வெவ்வினைப்","பயிலரிற் கிடந்த வேட்டுவிளி வெரீஇ","வரிப்புற இதலின் மணிக்கட் பேடை","நுண்பொறி அணிந்த எருத்தின் கூர்முட்","செங்காற் சேவற் பயிரும் ஆங்கண்","வில்லீண்டு அருஞ்சமம் ததைய நூறி","நல்லிசை நிறுத்த நாணுடை மறவர்","நிரைநிலை நடுகற் பொருந்தி இமையாது","இரைநசைஇக் கிடந்த முதுவாய்ப் பல்லி","சிறிய தெற்றுவ தாயிற் பெரிய","ஓடை யானை உயர்ந்தோர் ஆயினும்","நின்றாங்குப் பெயரும் கானம்","சென்றோர் மன்னென இருக்கிற் போர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man leaving while his wife's jewels slip and her eyes weep to say, if he can, that he will come back, for the forest he goes to is one where a quail hen, frightened by the hunters' call, cries to her mate, and where at a lizard's small cry among the hero-stones even lords with elephants halt and turn back.","n":"மன்னோ makes the request ironic: on a road ruled by omens no traveller can promise a return. Lines 4 to 8 (the washerwoman's starched cloth) seem to describe the quail's striped markings, but our text does not settle the construction, and the last line is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 387","lb":"A387","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகளது குறிப்பறிந்து தோழி தலைமகனைச் செலவபங்கச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தலைமகனைச் செலவபங்கச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புலைத்தி","புலைத்தி",6,"seythi","scene","washerwoman"],["வேட்டுவிளி","வேட்டுவர்",9,"seythi","scene","hunters"],["பேடை","இதல் பேடை",10,"pul","iraicchi","quail hen"],["சேவற்","இதல் சேவல்",12,"pul","iraicchi","quail cock"],["பல்லி","பல்லி",16,"pira","scene","lizard"],["யானை","யானை",18,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகற்",15]],"dv":[["iraicchi","பேடை","confirmed","1/1","even the hen in danger calls her mate close, so the wife, afraid, needs the man beside her and he should not go"]],"ch":"a:20"},"a:388":{"book":"akananuru","num":389,"t":["அம்ம!- வாழி தோழி!- நம்மலை","அமையறுத்து இயற்றிய வெவ்வாய்த் தட்டையின்","நறுவிரை ஆரம் அறவெறிந்து உழுத","உளைக்குரல் சிறுதினை கவர்தலின் கிளையமல்","பெருவரை அடுக்கத்துக் குரீஇ ஓப்பி","ஓங்கிருஞ் சிலம்பின் ஒள்ளிணர் நறுவீ","வேங்கையம் கவட்டிடை நிவந்த இதணத்துப்","பொன்மருள் நறுந்தாது ஊதும் தும்பி","இன்னிசை ஓரா இருந்தன மாக","'மையீர் ஓதி மடநல் லீரே!","நொவ்வியற் பகழி பாய்ந்தெனப் புண்கூர்ந்து","எவ்வமொடு வந்த உயர்மருப்பு ஒருத்தல்நும்","புனத்துழிப் போகல் உறுமோ மற்று' என","சினவுக்கொள் ஞமலி செயிர்த்துப்புடை ஆடச்","சொல்லிக் கழிந்த வல்விற் காளை","சாந்தார் அகலமும் தகையும் மிகநயந்து","ஈங்குநாம் உழக்கும் எவ்வம் உணராள்","நன்னர் நெஞ்சமொடு மயங்கி வெறியென","அன்னை தந்த முதுவாய் வேலன்","'எம்மிறை அணங்கலின் வந்தன்று இந்நோய்","தணிமருந்து அறிவல்' என்னும் ஆயின்","வினவின் எவனோ மற்றே- கனல்சின","மையல் வேழம் மெய்யுளம் போக","ஊட்டி யன்ன ஊன்புரள் அம்பொடு","காட்டுமான் அடிவழி ஒற்றி","வேட்டம் செல்லுமோ நும்மிறை? எனவே"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend, with the lover listening, how a young hunter with a hound came to their millet platform asking whether a wounded tusker had passed, and how, aching for him, she is now held by the mother's diviner, who says his god caused the sickness; she would ask the velan whether his god goes hunting with a bloodied arrow on a deer's track.","n":"The question is a joke with a point: the 'god' who seized her is the hunter, and asking it would tell the truth to the household without a confession. The hunter's pretext (a wounded elephant) is the usual opening of the day meeting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","anangu","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 388","lb":"A388","poet":"ஊட்டியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிச் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது. தோழ தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியதுமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறிச் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியதுமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["அமையறுத்து","அமை",2,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["தட்டையின்","தட்டை",2,"pira","scene","bird-scarer"],["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",3,"maram","scene","sandal"],["சிறுதினை","தினை",4,"unavu","scene","millet"],["குரீஇ","குரீஇ",5,"pul","scene","sparrow"],["வேங்கையம்","வேங்கை",7,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",8,"pira","scene","bee"],["ஒருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",12,"maa","scene","male elephant"],["ஞமலி","ஞமலி",14,"maa","scene","hound"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",19,"seythi","scene","diviner"],["எம்மிறை","இறை",20,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",23,"maa","simile","elephant"],["காட்டுமான்","மான்",25,"maa","scene","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:389":{"book":"akananuru","num":390,"t":["அறியாய்- வாழி தோழி!- நெறிகுரல்","சாந்தார் கூந்தல் உளரிப் போதணிந்து","தேங்கமழ் திருநுதல் திலகம் தைஇயும்","பல்லிதழ் எதிர்மலர் கிள்ளி வேறுபட","நல்லிள வனமுலை அல்லியொடு அப்பியும்","பெருந்தோள் தொய்யில் வரித்தும் சிறுபரட்டு","அஞ்செஞ் சீறடிப் பஞ்சி ஊட்டியும்","எற்புறந் தந்து நிற்பா ராட்டிப்","பல்பூஞ் சேக்கையிற் பகலும் நீங்கார்","மனைவயின் இருப்பவர் மன்னே- துனைதந்து","இரப்போர் ஏந்துகை நிறையப் புரப்போர்","புலம்பில் உள்ளமொடு புதுவதந்து உவக்கும்","அரும்பொருள் வேட்டம் எண்ணி கறுத்தோர்","சிறுபுன் கிளவிச் செல்லல் பாழ்பட","நல்லிசை தம்வயின் நிறுமார் வல்வேல்","வான வரம்பன் நல்நாட்டு உம்பர்","வேனில் நீடிய வெங்கடற்று அடைமுதல்","ஆறுசெல் வம்பலர் வேறுபிரிந்து அலறக்","கொலைவெம் மையின் நிலைபெயர்ந்து உறையும்","பெருங்களிறு தொலைச்சிய இருங்கேழ் ஏற்றை","செம்புல மருங்கிற் றன்கால் வாங்கி","வலம்படு வென்றியொடு சிலம்பகம் சிலம்பப்","படுமழை உருமின் முழங்கும்","நெடுமர மருங்கின் மலைஇறந் தோரே!"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend that the man who once dressed her hair, painted her brow and breasts and shoulders, reddened her feet and would not leave her bed even by day has gone, to fill beggars' hands, silence his scorners and fix his fame, beyond the Chera Vaanavaramban's land into summer forest where a tiger that has killed a tusker roars like thunder.","n":"She gives his reasons in her own voice, and they are the puram virtues (giving, honour, fame); the complaint is that those virtues are what took him. The adornments she lists are acts of his hands, so the memory is of being cared for, not only of being loved.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","selvam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 389","lb":"A389","poet":"நக்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் வற்புறுக்குந் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் வற்புறுக்குந் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["போதணிந்து","போது",2,"maram","ornament","flower"],["அல்லியொடு","அல்லி",5,"maram","ornament","pollen"],["பஞ்சி","பஞ்சி",7,"pira","ornament","red dye"],["பெருங்களிறு","களிறு",20,"maa","scene","tusker"],["ஏற்றை","ஏற்றை",20,"maa","scene","male tiger"],["உருமின்","உரும்",23,"pira","simile","thunder"]],"en":[["king","வான வரம்பன்",16]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:390":{"book":"akananuru","num":391,"t":["உவர்விளை உப்பின் கொள்ளை சாற்றி","அதர்படு பூழிய சேட்புலம் படரும்","ததர்கோல் உமணர் பதிபோகு நெடுநெறிக்","கணநிரை வாழ்க்கைதான் நன்று கொல்லோ","வணர்சுரி முச்சி முழுதுமற் புரள","ஐதகல் அல்குல் கவின்பெறப் புனைந்த","பல்குழைத் தொடலை ஒல்குவயின் ஒல்கி","நெல்லும் உப்பும் நேரே ஊரீர்!","கொள்ளீரோ எனச் சேரிதொறும் நுவலும்","அவ்வாங்கு உந்தி அமைத்தோ ளாய்! நின்","மெய்வாழ் உப்பின் விலைஎய் யாம்எனச்","சிறிய விலங்கின மாகப் பெரியதன்","அரிவேய் உண்கண் அமர்த்தனள் நோக்கி","யாரீ ரோஎம் விலங்கி யீஇரென","மூரல் முறுவலள் பேர்வனள் நின்ற","சில்நிரை வால்வளைப் பொலிந்த","பல்மாண் பேதைக்கு ஒழிந்ததென் நெஞ்சே!"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his companion, or his heart, that his heart is lost to a salt-trader's girl who went from street to street calling 'paddy for salt, measure for measure', who, when he blocked her way and joked that he could not price the salt of her body, looked at him angrily, asked who he was to stop her, and walked off smiling.","n":"The opening question (is the salt-carriers' wandering life a good one, then?) is the lover's envy of the people she belongs to. Her look is war and her smile is release, in one movement; the joke about the salt 'living in your body' is a flirtation she rebuffs and enjoys.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nenju","kan","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 390","lb":"A390","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகன் பாங்கற்குச் சொல்லியது; நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லிய தூஉமாம்.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu","தலைமகன் பாங்கற்குச் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லிய தூஉமாம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["உப்பின்","உப்பு",1,"pira","scene","salt"],["உமணர்","உமணர்",3,"seythi","scene","salt traders"],["கணநிரை","நிரை",4,"maa","scene","cattle"],["பல்குழைத்","குழை",7,"maram","ornament","leaves"],["நெல்லும்","நெல்",8,"unavu","scene",""],["அமைத்தோ","அமை",10,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["வால்வளைப்","வளை",16,"pira","ornament","conch bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:391":{"book":"akananuru","num":392,"t":["பார்வல் வெருகின் கூர்எயிற்று அன்ன","வரிமென் முகைய நுண்கொடி அதிரல்","மல்ககல் வட்டியர் கொள்விடம் பெறாஅர்","விலைஞர் ஒழித்த தலைவேய் கான்மலர்","தேம்பாய் முல்லையொடு ஞாங்கர்ப் போக்கி","தண்ணறுங் கதுப்பிற் புணர்ந்தோர்- புனைந்தவென்","பொதிமாண் முச்சி காண்தொறும் பண்டைப்","பழவணி உள்ளப் படுமால்- தோழி!","இன்றொடு சில்நாள் வரினுஞ் சென்றுநனி","படாஅ வாகுமெம் கண்ணே கடாஅ","வான்மருப்பு அசைத்தல் செல்லாது யானைதன்","வாய்நிறை கொண்ட வலிதேம்பு தடக்கை","குன்றுபுகு பாம்பின் தோன்றும்","என்றூழ் வைப்பின் சுரனிறந் தோரே!"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend that each time she sees the tuft of hair her lover dressed with athiral flowers the sellers could not sell and with mullai, she remembers the old adornment; even if he returns in a few days her eyes will not sleep, for he crossed summer wasteland where an elephant's limp trunk looks like a snake entering a hill.","n":"The flowers he chose were the cheap ones the hawkers threw away, which makes the memory more intimate, not less. The elephant too weak to lift its tusks is the hot land's exhaustion, set against the care she remembers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 391","lb":"A391","poet":"காவன் முல்லைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை வற்புறுத்துந் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வெருகின்","வெருகு",1,"maa","simile","wildcat"],["அதிரல்","அதிரல்",2,"maram","ornament",""],["விலைஞர்","விலைஞர்",4,"seythi","scene","flower sellers"],["முல்லையொடு","முல்லை",5,"maram","ornament",""],["யானைதன்","யானை",11,"maa","scene",""],["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",13,"pira","simile","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:392":{"book":"akananuru","num":393,"t":["தாழ்பெருந் தடக்கை தலைஇய கானத்து","வீழ்பிடி கெடுத்த வெண்கோட்டு யானை","உண்குளகு மறுத்த உயக்கத் தன்ன","பண்புடை யாக்கைச் சிதைவுநன்கு அறீஇப்","பின்னிலை முனியா னாகி நன்றும்","தாதுசெல் பாவை அன்ன தையல்","மாதர் மெல்லியல் மடநல் லோள்வயின்","தீதின் றாக நீபுணை புகுகென","என்னும் தண்டும் ஆயின் மற்றவன்","அழிதகப் பெயர்தல் நனிஇன் னாதே","ஒல்லினி வாழி தோழி!- கல்லெனக்","கணமழை பொழிந்த கான்படி இரவில்","தினைமேய் யானை இனனிரிந்து ஓடக்","கல்லுயர் கழுதில் சேணோன் எறிந்த","வல்வாய்க் கவணின் கடுவெடி ஒல்லென","மறப்புலி உரற வாரணம் கதற","நனவுறு கட்சியின் நன்மயில் ஆல","மலையுடன் வெரூஉம் மாக்கல் வெற்பன்","பிரியுநள் ஆகலே அரிதே அதாஅன்று","உரிதல் பண்பிற் பிரியுநன் ஆயின்","வினைதவப் பெயர்ந்த வென்வேல் வேந்தன்","முனைகொல் தானையொடு முன்வந்து இறுப்பத்","தன்வரம்பு ஆகிய மன்னெயில் இருக்கை","ஆற்றா மையின் பிடித்த வேல்வலித்","தோற்றம் பிழையாத் தொல்புகழ் பெற்ற","விழைதக ஓங்கிய கழைதுஞ்சு மருங்கிற்","கானமர் நன்னன் போல","யான்ஆ குவல்நின் நலம்தரு வேனே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, having agreed to help the pleading lover, asks the girl to consent: his wasted body is like a tusker's that has lost its mate and refuses fodder, it would hurt to send him away ruined, and the lord of that roaring hill will hardly leave her; if he ever did, she herself would stand fast like Nannan and restore her beauty.","n":"The night scene (a sling's crack scattering millet-raiding elephants, the tiger's roar, the jungle fowl's cry, the peacock dancing, the whole mountain afraid) shows a land in which one sound sets everything moving, as the friend hopes her word will. The story of Nannan holding his spear against a besieging king is told too briefly to be sure of.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","kaamanoy","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 392","lb":"A392","poet":"மோசிகீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பின்னின்ற தலைமகற்குக் குறைநேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குக் குறைநயப்பக் கூறியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","குறைநேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குக் குறைநயப்பக் கூறியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","simile",""],["குளகு","குளகு",3,"maram","simile","fodder"],["தினைமேய்","தினை",13,"unavu","scene","millet"],["சேணோன்","சேணோன்",14,"seythi","scene","field watcher"],["மறப்புலி","புலி",16,"maa","scene",""],["வாரணம்","வாரணம்",16,"pul","scene","jungle fowl"],["நன்மயில்","மயில்",17,"pul","scene",""],["கழைதுஞ்சு","கழை",26,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[["king","நன்னன்",27]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:393":{"book":"akananuru","num":394,"t":["கோடுயர் பிறங்கற் குன்றுபல நீந்தி","வேறுபுலம் படர்ந்த வினைதரல் உள்ளத்து","ஆறுசெல் வம்பலர் காய்பசி தீரிய","முதைச்சுவற் கலித்த ஈர்இலை நெடுந்தோட்டுக்","கவைக்கதிர் வரகின் கால்தொகு பொங்கழி","கவட்டடிப் பொருத பல்சினை உதிர்வை","அகன்கண் பாறைச் செவ்வயின் தெறீஇ","வரியணி பணைத்தோள் வார்செவித் தன்னையர்","பண்ணை வெண்பழத்து அரிசி ஏய்ப்பச்","சுழல்மரம் சொலித்த சுளகுஅலை வெண்காழ்","தொடிமாண் உலக்கை ஊழின் போக்கி","உரல்முகம் காட்டிய சுரைநிறை கொள்ளை","ஆங்கண் இருஞ்சுனை நீரொடு முகவாக்","களிபடு குழிசிக் கல்லடுப்பு ஏற்றி","இணர்ததை கடுக்கை ஈண்டிய தாதிற்","குடவர் புழுக்கிய பொங்கவிழ்ப் புன்கம்","மதர்வை நல்லான் பாலொடு பகுக்கும்","நிரைபல குழீஇய நெடுமொழிப் புல்லி","தேன்தூங்கு உயர்வரை நல்நாட்டு உம்பர்","வேங்கடம் இறந்தனர் ஆயினும் ஆண்டவர்","நீடலர்- வாழி தோழி!- தோடுகொள்","உருகெழு மஞ்ஞை ஒலிசீர் ஏய்ப்பத்","தகரம் மண்ணிய தண்ணறு முச்சிப்","புகரில் குவளைப் போதொடு தெரிஇதழ்","வேனில் அதிரல் வேய்ந்தநின்","ஏமுறு புணர்ச்சி இன்துயில் மறந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the wife that though he has gone beyond Venkadam, past the land of Pulli where herders cook varagu millet and share it with milk among hungry travellers, he will not stay long, having forgotten the sweet sleep of her hair washed with thagaram and dressed with blue lily and athiral.","n":"Most of the poem is the herders' cooking, told step by step (threshing on the rock, pounding, boiling in a stone hearth), and its point is that travellers on that road are fed: the comfort is that he is safe there. The closing memory of her dressed hair answers 391, where the wife looks at the same flowers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","virundhu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 393","lb":"A393","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வரகின்","வரகு",5,"unavu","gift_food","millet"],["அரிசி","அரிசி",9,"unavu","simile","rice"],["கடுக்கை","கடுக்கை",15,"maram","scene","konrai"],["குடவர்","குடவர்",16,"seythi","scene","herders"],["புன்கம்","புன்கம்",16,"unavu","gift_food","boiled grain"],["நல்லான்","ஆன்",17,"maa","scene","cow"],["பாலொடு","பால்",17,"unavu","gift_food",""],["தேன்தூங்கு","தேன்",19,"pira","scene","honey"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",22,"pul","simile","peacock"],["தகரம்","தகரம்",23,"maram","ornament","hair fragrance"],["குவளைப்","குவளை",24,"maram","ornament","blue water-lily"],["அதிரல்","அதிரல்",25,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[["patron","புல்லி",18],["hill","வேங்கடம்",20]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:394":{"book":"akananuru","num":395,"t":["களவும் புளித்தன; விளவும் பழுநின;","சிறுதலைத் துருவின் பழுப்புறு விளைதயிர்","இதைப்புன வரகின் அவைப்புமாண் அரிசியொடு","கார்வாய்த்து ஒழிந்த ஈர்வாய்ப் புற்றத்து","ஈயல்பெய்து அட்ட இன்புளி வெஞ்சோறு","சேதான் வெண்ணெய் வெம்புறத்து உருக","இளையர் அருந்தப் பின்றை, நீயும்","இடுமுள் வேலி முடக்காற் பந்தர்ப்","புதுக்கலத்து அன்ன செவ்வாய்ச் சிற்றிற்","புனையிருங் கதுப்பின்நின் மனையோள் அயரப்","பாலுடை அடிசில் தொடீஇய ஒருநாள்","மாவண் தோன்றல்! வந்தனை சென்மோ-","காடுறை இடையன் யாடுதலைப் பெயர்க்கும்","மடிவிடு வீளை வெரீஇக் குறுமுயல்","மன்ற இரும்புதல் ஒளிக்கும்","புன்புல வைப்பின்எம் சிறுநல் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"Having brought the girl to the night meeting, the friend invites the lover, lord of great gifts, to come one day to their little home in the poor-soil village where a hare hides at the goatherd's whistle, and, after his young men have eaten termite-rice with curd and butter, to eat milk rice that his wife prepares there.","n":"Line 1 is a pun: களவும் புளித்தன says the karanda fruit has soured, and also that the secret love has gone sour. 'Your wife' (நின் மனையோள்) names the girl as what she would be once married, so the invitation to a meal is a demand for marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","virundhu","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 394","lb":"A394","poet":"நன்பலூர்ச் சிறுமேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"இரவுக்குறித் தலைமகளை இடத்துய்த்து வந்து தோழி தலைமகனை வரைவு கடாஅயது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி தலைமகனை வரைவு கடாஅயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["களவும்","களா",1,"maram","scene","karanda fruit"],["விளவும்","விளா",1,"maram","scene","wood-apple"],["துருவின்","துரு",2,"maa","scene","sheep"],["விளைதயிர்","தயிர்",2,"unavu","gift_food","curd"],["வரகின்","வரகு",3,"unavu","gift_food","millet"],["ஈயல்பெய்து","ஈயல்",5,"unavu","gift_food","winged termites"],["வெண்ணெய்","வெண்ணெய்",6,"unavu","gift_food","butter"],["அடிசில்","அடிசில்",11,"unavu","gift_food","milk rice"],["இடையன்","இடையன்",13,"seythi","scene","goatherd"],["யாடு","யாடு",13,"maa","scene","goats"],["குறுமுயல்","முயல்",14,"maa","scene","hare"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:395":{"book":"akananuru","num":396,"t":["தண்கயம் பயந்த வண்காற் குவளை","மாரி மாமலர் பெயற்குஏற் றன்ன","நீரொடு நிறைந்த பேரமர் மழைக்கண்","பனிவார் எவ்வம் தீர இனிவரின்","நன்றுமன்- வாழி தோழி!- தெறுகதிர்","ஈரம் நைத்த நீர்அறு நனந்தலை","அழல்மேய்ந்து உண்ட நிழன்மாய் இயவின்","வறன்மரத்து அன்ன கவைமருப்பு எழிற்கலை","இறல்அவிர்ந் தன்ன தேர்நசைஇ ஓடிப்","புறம்புவழிப் பட்ட உலமரல் உள்ளமொடு","மேய்பிணைப் பயிரும் மெலிந்தழி படர்குரல்","அருஞ்சுரம் செல்லுநர் ஆள்செத்து ஓர்க்கும்","திருந்தரை ஞெமைய பெரும்புனக் குன்றத்து","ஆடுகழை இருவெதிர் நரலும்","கோடுகாய் கடற்ற காடிறந் தோரே!"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend it would be good if he came back now to end the tears of eyes like rain-filled blue lilies, he who crossed the bamboo hills of dry forest where a stag, chasing a mirage and lost off the path, calls its grazing doe in a broken voice that travellers mistake for a man's.","n":"The stag that ran after a mirage and lost its mate is set on his road: it is an image of what that land does to partners, and of the cry he will hear there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 395","lb":"A395","poet":"எயினந்தை மகனார் இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடைத் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடைத் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குவளை","குவளை",1,"maram","simile","blue water-lily"],["வறன்மரத்து","மரம்",8,"maram","simile","dead tree"],["எழிற்கலை","கலை",8,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["மேய்பிணைப்","பிணை",11,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["ஞெமைய","ஞெமை",13,"maram","scene",""],["இருவெதிர்","வெதிர்",14,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","எழிற்கலை","confirmed","1/1","the man on that road will hear a parted creature crying for its mate and so feel his own parting and come back"]],"ch":"a:20"},"a:396":{"book":"akananuru","num":397,"t":["தொடுத்தேன் மகிழ்ந! செல்லல்- கொடித்தேர்ப்","பொலம்பூண் நன்னன் புன்னாடு கடிந்தென","யாழிசை மறுகின் பாழி ஆங்கண்","'அஞ்சல்' என்ற ஆஅய் எயினன்","இகலடு கற்பின் மிஞிலியொடு தாக்கித்","தன்னுயிர் கொடுத்தனன் சொல்லியது அமையாது","தெறலருங் கடவுள் முன்னர்த் தேற்றி","மெல்லிறை முன்கை பற்றிய சொல்லிறந்து","ஆர்வ நெஞ்சம் தலைத்தலை சிறப்பநின்","மார்புதரு கல்லாய் பிறன் ஆயினையே","இனியான் விடுக்குவென் அல்லென் மந்தி","பனிவார் கண்ணள் பலபுலந்து உறையக்","கருந்திறல் அத்தி ஆடுஅணி நசைஇ","நெடுநீர்க் காவிரி கொண்டொளித் தாங்குநின்","மனையோள் வவ்வலும் அஞ்சுவல் சினைஇ","ஆரியர் அலறத் தாக்கிப் பேர்இசைத்","தொன்றுமுதிர் வடவரை வணங்குவில் பொறித்து","வெஞ்சின வேந்தரைப் பிணித்தோன்","வஞ்சி யன்னவென் னலம்தந்து சென்மே!"],"p":"","r":"The courtesan holds her lover and will not let him go: Aay Eyinan died fighting Minjili at Paazhi to keep his word to those Nannan drove out, but he, after swearing before the god and taking her wrist, has become another's; she fears his wife will snatch him as the Kaviri snatched Aththi from Manthi, so he must give back her beauty, splendid as the Chera's Vanji, before he goes.","n":"Each legend is chosen against him: Eyinan kept a promise of refuge with his life, the river's seizing of Aththi stands for the wife's claim, and Vanji, seat of the king who carved his bow on the northern mountain, prices the beauty he owes her. Line 10 (மார்புதரு கல்லாய்) can also be read as 'you who never learned to give your chest'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","vaaimai","oodal","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 396","lb":"A396","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"காதற்பரத்தை தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thalaivan",null,"karpu","காதற்பரத்தை தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["யாழிசை","யாழ்",3,"yaazh","scene",""],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",7,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[["king","நன்னன்",2],["town","புன்னாடு",2],["town","பாழி",3],["king","ஆஅய் எயினன்",4],["king","மிஞிலி",5],["king","அத்தி",13],["river","காவிரி",14],["hill","வடவரை",17],["town","வஞ்சி",19]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:397":{"book":"akananuru","num":398,"t":["என்மகள் பெருமடம் யான்பா ராட்டத்","தாய்தன் செம்மல் கண்டுகடன் இறுப்ப","முழவுமுகம் புலரா விழவுடை வியனகர்","மணனிடை யாகக் கொள்ளான் 'கல்பகக்","கணமழை துறந்த கான்மயங்கு அழுவம்","எளிய வாக ஏந்துகொடி பரந்த","பொறிவரி அல்குல் மாஅயோட்கு' எனத்","தணிந்த பருவம் செல்லான் படர்தரத்","துணிந்தோன் மன்ற துனைவெங் காளை","கடும்பகட்டு ஒருத்தல் நடுங்கக் குத்திப்","போழ்புண் படுத்த பொரியரை ஓமைப்","பெரும்பொளிச் சேயரை நோக்கி ஊன்செத்துச்","கருங்கால் யாத்துப் பருந்துவந்து இறுக்கும்","சேண்உயர்ந்து ஓங்கிய வானுயர் நெடுங்கோட்டுக்","கோடை வெவ்வளிக்கு உலமரும்","புல்லிலை வெதிர நெல்விளை காடே"],"p":"","r":"The foster mother says the hasty youth, instead of taking her daughter in marriage at their festive house, decided the cloudless wasteland would be easy for the girl and led her off before the cool season, through forest where a kite perches on a yaa tree, taking the red gash an elephant tore in an omai trunk for flesh.","n":"Her grief is set against the wedding that should have been: the house where the drum never dries is named so that the elopement reads as a festival refused. The kite's mistake (red bark for meat) is the land's hunger. Line 2 is hard to construe in our text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","varaivu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 397","lb":"A397","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய செவிலித்தாய் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu","மகட் போக்கிய செவிலித்தாய் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முழவுமுகம்","முழவு",3,"parai","scene","drum"],["ஒருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",10,"maa","scene","male elephant"],["ஓமைப்","ஓமை",11,"maram","scene",""],["பருந்துவந்து","பருந்து",13,"pul","scene","kite"],["யாத்துப்","யா",13,"maram","scene",""],["வெதிர","வெதிர்",16,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["நெல்விளை","நெல்",16,"unavu","scene","bamboo rice"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:398":{"book":"akananuru","num":399,"t":["'இழைநிலை நெகிழ்ந்த எவ்வம் கூரப்","படர்மலி வருத்தமொடு பலபுலந்து அசைஇ","மென்தோள் நெகிழச் சாஅய்க் கொன்றை","ஊழுறு மலரின் பாழ்பட முற்றிய","பசலை மேனி நோக்கி நுதல்பசந்து","இன்னேம் ஆகிய எம்மிவண் அருளான்","நும்மோன் செய்த கொடுமைக்கு இம்மென்று","அலமரல் மழைக்கண் தெண்பனி மல்க","நன்று புறமாறி அகறல் யாழநின்","குன்றுகெழு நாடற்கு என்னெனப் படுமோ?","கரைபொரு நீத்தம்! உரைஎனக் கழறி","நின்னொடு புலத்தல் அஞ்சி அவர்மலைப்","பன்மலர் போர்த்து நாணுமிக ஒடுங்கி","மறைந்தனை கழியும் நிற்றந்து செலுத்தி","நயன்அறத் துறத்தல் வல்லி யோரோ!","நொதும லாளர் அதுகண் ணோடாது","அழற்சினை வேங்கை நிழல்தவிர்ந்து அசைஇ","மாரி புரந்தர நந்தி ஆரியர்","பொன்படு நெடுவரை புரையும் எந்தை","பல்பூங் கானத்து அல்கி இன்றிவண்","சேந்தனை செலினே சிதைகுவது உண்டோ ?","குயவரி இரும்போத்துப் பொருதபுண் கூர்ந்து","உயங்குபிடி தழீஇய மதனழி யானை","வாங்கமைக் கழையின் நரலும் அவர்","ஓங்குமலை நாட்டின் வருஉ வோயே!"],"p":"","r":"Wasting with longing, the girl speaks to the flooding stream from her lover's hill: she reproaches it for slipping away ashamed under his hill's flowers instead of telling him of her pallor and tears, and asks what harm there would be if it rested a while in her father's flowering forest, before going back to the country where a spent elephant groans as it embraces its tired mate.","n":"The stream is a messenger from his hill that she blames for his silence, and she offers it the hospitality she cannot offer him. The elephant that, wounded and spent, still holds its mate is the care his land knows and he withholds. Several lines (9 to 16) are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kaamanoy","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 398","lb":"A398","poet":"இம்மென்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"காமமிக்க கழிபடர் கிளவியால் (விரைவிடத்துக்கண்) தலைமகள் தலைமகன் வரையினின்றும் போந்த ஆற்றொடு புலந்து சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu","காமமிக்க கழிபடர் கிளவியால்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",3,"maram","simile",""],["பன்மலர்","மலர்",13,"maram","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",17,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["இரும்போத்துப்","போத்து",22,"maa","scene","tiger"],["உயங்குபிடி","பிடி",23,"maa","iraicchi","she-elephant"],["யானை","யானை",23,"maa","iraicchi",""],["கழையின்","கழை",24,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","யானை","confirmed","1/1","even the elephants of his hill hold their mates in distress, yet their lord leaves her to waste"]],"ch":"a:20"},"a:399":{"book":"akananuru","num":400,"t":["சிமைய குரல சாந்துஅருந்தி இருளி","இமையக் கானம் நாறும் கூந்தல்","நந்நுதல் அரிவை! இன்னுறல் ஆகம்","பருகு வன்ன காதல் உள்ளமொடு","திருகுபு முயங்கல் இன்றியவண் நீடார்","கடற்றடை மருங்கின் கணிச்சியின் குழித்த","உடைக்கண் நீடமை ஊறல் உண்ட","பாடின் தெண்மணி பயங்கெழு பெருநிரை","வாடுபுலம் புக்கெனக் கோடுதுவைத்து அகற்றி","ஒல்குநிலைக் கடுக்கை அல்குநிழல் அசைஇப்","பல்லான் கோவலர் கல்லாது ஊதும்","சிறுவெதிர்ந் தீங்குழற் புலம்புகொள் தெள்விளி","மையில் பளிங்கின் அன்ன தோற்றப்","பல்கோள் நெல்லிப் பைங்கால் அருந்தி","மெல்கிடு மடமரை ஓர்க்கும் அத்தம்","காய்கதிர் கடுகிய கவினழி பிறங்கல்","வேய்கண் உடைந்த சிமைய","வாய்படு மருங்கின் மலைஇறந் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the wife, whose hair smells like the Himalayan forest, that her husband will not stay away without coming back to embrace her as if drinking her in, though he crossed sun-scorched hills where a doe, chewing nelli fruit, listens to the lonely notes of herders' bamboo flutes as they rest their cattle in konrai shade.","n":"The herders' flute is called lonely (புலம்புகொள்) and the doe listens to it: the road he is on is full of sounds of solitude that will turn him home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 399","lb":"A399","poet":"எயினந்தை மகனார் இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது.","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தலைமகன் பிரிவின்கண் தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சாந்துஅருந்தி","சாந்து",1,"maram","ornament","sandal"],["நீடமை","அமை",7,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பெருநிரை","நிரை",8,"maa","scene","cattle"],["கடுக்கை","கடுக்கை",10,"maram","scene","konrai"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",11,"seythi","scene","herders"],["தீங்குழற்","குழல்",12,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["நெல்லிப்","நெல்லி",14,"maram","scene",""],["மடமரை","மரை",15,"maa","scene","doe"]],"en":[["hill","இமைய",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"a:400":{"book":"akananuru","num":401,"t":["நகைநன்று அம்ம தானே 'அவனொடு","மனைஇறந்து அல்கினும் அல'ரென நயந்து","கானல் அல்கிய நம்களவு அகல","பல்புரிந்து இயறல் உற்ற நல்வினை","நூல்அமை பிறப்பின் நீல உத்திக்","கொய்ம்மயிர் எருத்தம் பிணர்படப் பெருகி","நெய்ம்மிதி முனைஇய கொழுஞ்சோற்று ஆர்கை","நிரலியைந்து ஒன்றிய செலவின் செந்தினைக்","குரல்வார்ந் தன்ன குவவுத்தலை நந்நான்கு","வீங்குசுவல் மொசியத் தாங்குநுகம் தழீஇப்","பூம்பொறிப் பல்படை ஒலிப்பப் பூட்டி","மதியுடைய வலவன் ஏவலின் இகுதுறைப்","புனல்பாய்ந் தன்ன வாமான் திண்தேர்க்","கணைகழிந் தன்ன நோன்கால் வண்பரிப்","பால்கண்ட டன்ன ஊதை வெண்மணற்","கால்கண்ட டன்ன வழிபடப் போகி","அயிர்ச்சேற்று அள்ளல் அழுவத்து ஆங்கண்","இருள்நீர் இட்டுச்சுரம் நீந்தித் துறைகெழு","மெல்லம் புலம்பன் வந்த ஞான்றை","பூமலி இருங்கழித் துயல்வரும் அடையொடு","நேமி தந்த நெடுநீர் நெய்தல்","விளையா இளங்கள் நாறப் பலவுடன்","பொதிஅவிழ் தண்மலர் கண்டும் நன்றும்","புதுவது ஆகின்று அம்ம- பழவிறல்","பாடுஎழுந்து இரங்கு முந்நீர்","நீடிரும் பெண்ணை நம் அழுங்கல் ஊரே!"],"p":"","r":"After the wedding the friend tells the bride it is laughable now that they once feared gossip even if she ran off with him: the day the lord of the shore came, his strong chariot racing over milk-white sand and mud like water down a ford, their old town of palmyras by the roaring sea looked new, the neythal buds opening and smelling of new toddy.","n":"The chariot passage is the same apparatus akam uses for the lover's secret night visits, here driven openly to the wedding, so speed now means eagerness rather than stealth. The town that gossiped (அழுங்கல் ஊர்) is the town that looks new.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","alar","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Akananuru 400","lb":"A400","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகன் வரைந் தெய்திய பின்றைத் தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது.","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தலைமகன் வரைந் தெய்திய பின்றைத் தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["செந்தினைக்","தினை",8,"unavu","simile","millet"],["வாமான்","மா",13,"maa","scene","horse"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",21,"maram","scene",""],["இளங்கள்","கள்",22,"unavu","simile","toddy"],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",26,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"a:20"},"kl:1":{"book":"kalittokai","num":1,"t":["ஆறு அறி அந்தணர்க்கு, அருமறை பல பகர்ந்து,","தேறு நீர் சடைக் கரந்து, திரிபுரம் தீ மடுத்துக்,","கூறாமல் குறித்ததன் மேல் செல்லும், கடும் கூளி,","மாறாப் போர் மணி மிடற்று எண் கையாய்! கேள் இனி;","படு பறை பல இயம்பப், பல் உருவம் பெயர்த்து நீ","கொடுகொட்டி ஆடும்கால், கோடு உயர் அகல் அல்குல்","கொடிபுரை நுசுப்பினாள், கொண்ட சீர் தருவாளோ?","மண்டு அமர் பல கடந்து, மதுகையால் நீறு அணிந்து,","பண்டரங்கம் ஆடும்கால், பணை எழில் அணை மென்தோள்","வண்டு அரற்றும் கூந்தலாள், வளர் தூக்கு தருவாளோ?","கொலை உழுவைத் தோல் அசைஇக், கொன்றைத்தார் சுவல் புரளத்","தலை அங்கை கொண்டு நீ காபாலம் ஆடும்கால்,","முலை அணிந்த முறுவலாள் முன் பாணி தருவாளோ?","என ஆங்கு,","பாணியும் தூக்கும் சீரும் என்று இவை","மாண் இழை அரிவை காப்ப,","ஆணம் இல் பொருள் எமக்கு அமர்ந்தனை ஆடி."],"p":"","r":"The invocation asks Shiva, who spoke the Vedas to the brahmins, hid the Ganga in his matted hair, burned the three cities and goes eight-armed and dark-throated with fierce goblins behind him, whether, when he dances the kodukotti, the pandarangam and the kapalam, his consort does not keep for him the beat, the measure and the rhythm; and says that with her keeping them he danced for us.","n":"The three dances are named in the order of the three musical terms the goddess supplies (சீர், தூக்கு, பாணி), and line 15 gathers the terms back in reverse; the god is made a dancer who needs a partner to keep time, a fitting opening for an anthology in the kali metre, which was sung. The last line (ஆணம் இல் பொருள் எமக்கு அமர்ந்தனை ஆடி) turns on the rare word ஆணம், and its sense is not settled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thirukkoothu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 1","lb":"KL1","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["பறை","பறை",5,"parai","scene",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",10,"pira","scene","bee"],["உழுவைத்","உழுவை",11,"maa","ornament","tiger"],["கொன்றைத்தார்","கொன்றை",11,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:2":{"book":"kalittokai","num":2,"t":["தொடங்கல் கண் தோன்றிய முதியவன் முதலாக,","அடங்காதார் மிடல் சாய, அமரர் வந்து இரத்தலின்,","மடங்கல் போல் சினைஇ, மாயம் செய் அவுணரைக்","கடந்து அடு முன்பொடு, முக்கண்ணான் மூவெயிலும்","உடன்றக்கால், முகம் போல ஒண் கதிர் தெறுதலின்","சீறு அரும் கணிச்சியோன் சினவலின், அவ்வெயில்","ஏறு பெற்று உதிர்வன போல், வரை பிளந்து, இயங்குநர்","ஆறு கெட விலங்கிய அழல் அவிர் ஆர் இடை -","மறப்பு அரும் காதல் இவள் ஈண்டு ஒழிய,","இறப்பத் துணிந்தனிர், கேண்மின் மற்று ஐய!","தொலைவு ஆகி, இரந்தோர்க்கு ஒன்று ஈயாமை இழிவு என","மலை இறந்து செயல் சூழ்ந்த பொருள் பொருள் ஆகுமோ -","நிலைஇய கற்பினாள், நீ நீப்பின் வாழாதாள்,","முலை ஆகம் பிரியாமை பொருள் ஆயின் அல்லதை;","இல் என இரந்தோர்க்கு ஒன்று ஈயாமை இழிவு எனக்","கல் இறந்து செயல் சூழ்ந்த பொருள் பொருள் ஆகுமோ -","தொல் இயல் வழாஅமைத் துணை எனப் புணர்ந்தவள்","புல் ஆகம் பிரியாமை பொருள் ஆயின் அல்லதை;","இடன் இன்றி இரந்தோர்க்கு ஒன்று ஈயாமை இழிவு எனக்","கடன் இறந்து செயல் சூழ்ந்த பொருள் பொருள் ஆகுமோ -","வடமீன் போல் தொழுது ஏத்த வயங்கிய கற்பினாள்","தட மென் தோள் பிரியாமை பொருள் ஆயின் அல்லதை;","என இவள்,","புன்கண் கொண்டு இனையவும், பொருள் வயின் அகறல்","அன்பு அன்று, என்று யான் கூற, அன்புற்றுக்","காழ் வரை நில்லாக் கடும் களிற்று ஒருத்தல்","யாழ் வரைத் தங்கியாங்குத், தாழ்பு, நின்","தொல்கவின் தொலைதல் அஞ்சி, என்","சொல்வரைத் தங்கினர், காதலோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that when the man resolved to cross a waste split and burning as if Shiva had struck the three forts, she asked him three times whether wealth sought across hills because refusing a suppliant is shameful is truly wealth, unless it be not leaving the breast of the chaste wife who will not live without him, and that, told this is not love, he bowed like a tusker that no post can hold yet stops at the sound of the lute, and stayed by her word, afraid her old beauty would fade.","n":"The friend grants the man the most honourable motive the Sangam knows, wealth to give to those who ask (இரந்தோர்க்கு ஒன்று ஈயாமை இழிவு), and still sets the wife's shoulders above it; the triple refrain changes only the word for what he crosses (மலை, கல், கடன்) and the word for her body. The tusker that breaks every tether but stops for the lute makes her speech the music that tames him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","eegai","karpu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 2","lb":"KL2","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["களிற்று","களிறு",26,"maa","simile","tusker"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",27,"yaazh","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:3":{"book":"kalittokai","num":3,"t":["அறன் இன்றி அயல் தூற்றும் அம்பலை நாணியும்,","வறன் நீந்தி நீ செல்லும் நீள் இடை நினைப்பவும் -","இறை நில்லா வளை ஓட, இதழ் சோர்பு பனி மல்கப்,","பொறை நில்லா நோயோடு புல்லென்ற நுதல் இவள்","விறல் நலன் இழப்பவும், வினை வேட்டாய்! கேஎள் இனி;","'உடை இவள் உயிர் வாழாள், நீ நீப்பின்' எனப், பல","இடை கொண்டு யாம் இரப்பவும், எம கொள்ளாய், ஆயினை;","கடைஇய ஆற்று இடை, நீர் நீத்த வறும் சுனை,","அடையொடு வாடிய அணி மலர் - தகைப்பன.","'வல்லை நீ துறப்பாயேல், வகை வாடும் இவள்' என,","ஒல்லாங்கு யாம் இரப்பவும், உணர்ந்து ஈயாய் ஆயினை;","செல்லு நீள் ஆற்று இடைச், சேர்ந்து எழுந்த மரம் வாடப்,","புல்லு விட்டு இறைஞ்சிய பூங் கொடி - தகைப்பன.","'பிணிபு நீ விடல் சூழின், பிறழ்தரும் இவள்' எனப்","பணிபு வந்து இரப்பவும், பல சூழ்வாய் ஆயினை;","துணிபு நீ செலக் கண்ட ஆற்று இடை, அம்மரத்து","அணி செல, வாடிய அம் தளிர் - தகைப்பன.","என ஆங்கு,","யாம் நின் கூறவும் எம கொள்ளாய் ஆயினை;","ஆனாது இவள் போல் அருள் வந்தவை காட்டி,","மேல் நின்று மெய் கூறும் கேளிர் போல், நீ செல்லும்","கானம் - தகைப்ப செலவு."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man who is set on his venture that, since he would not heed their pleas that she, shamed by the gossip and wasting, will not live if he leaves, the things he will meet on the road (a dried spring with its flowers withered, a flowering creeper drooping from a withered tree, scorched young leaves) will themselves stop him, showing him, like kin who speak the truth, what has suffered as she has.","n":"The friend gives up on persuasion and hands the argument to the landscape: each stanza pairs a plea refused with a withered thing that will stop him (தகைப்பன), so the paalai land, usually the danger, becomes her advocate. Because the poem states the likeness itself (இவள் போல்), the withered things are an open comparison, not a hidden one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","alar","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 3","lb":"KL3","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["சுனை","சுனை",8,"pira","simile",""],["மலர்","மலர்",9,"maram","simile",""],["மரம்","மரம்",12,"maram","simile",""],["கொடி","கொடி",13,"maram","simile","creeper"],["தளிர்","தளிர்",17,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:4":{"book":"kalittokai","num":4,"t":["வலி முன்பின், வல்லென்ற யாக்கைப் புலி நோக்கின் -","சுற்றுஅமை வில்லர், சுரி வளர் பித்தையர்,","அற்றம் பார்த்து அல்கும் - கடுங்கண் மறவர் தாம்","கொள்ளும் பொருள் இலர் ஆயினும், வம்பலர்,","துள்ளுநர்க் காண்மார் தொடர்ந்து, உயிர் வௌவலின்,","புள்ளும் வழங்காப் புலம்பு கொள் ஆர் இடை,","வெள் வேல் வலத்திர் பொருள் தரல் வேட்கையின்,","உள்ளினிர் என்பது அறிந்தனள், என் தோழி;","'காழ் விரி கவை ஆரம் மீவரும் இளமுலை","போழ்து இடைப்படாஅமல் முயங்கியும் அமையார், என்","தாழ் கதுப்பு அணிகுவர், காதலர்; மற்று, அவர்","சூழ்வதை எவன் கொல்? அறியேன்!' என்னும்;","'முள் உறழ் முளை எயிற்று அமிழ்து ஊறும் தீ நீரைக்","கள்ளினும் மகிழ் செயும் என உரைத்தும் அமையார், என்","ஒள் இழை திருத்துவர், காதலர்; மற்று, அவர்","உள்ளுவது எவன் கொல்? அறியேன்!' என்னும்;","'நுண் எழில் மாமை சுணங்கு அணி ஆகம் தம்","கண்ணொடு தொடுத்து என நோக்கியும் அமையார், என்","ஒண் நுதல் நீவுவர், காதலர்; மற்று, அவர்","எண்ணுவது எவன் கொல்? அறியேன்!' என்னும்;","என ஆங்கு,","'கழி பெரு நல்கல் ஒன்று உடைத்து!' என, என் தோழி","அழிவொடு கலங்கிய எவ்வத்தள்; ஒருநாள், நீர்,","பொழுது இடைப்பட நீப்பின், வாழ்வாளோ?","ஒழிக இனிப் பெரும! நின் பொருள் பிணிச் செலவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that the woman knows he means to cross for wealth the waste where tiger-eyed bowmen kill travellers even with nothing to take, only to watch them writhe: she says that, not content with embracing her, he dresses her hair, not content with praising her mouth as sweeter than toddy, he sets her jewels straight, not content with gazing, he strokes her brow, and that this excess of tenderness hides something; she would not live a day without him, so let him give up this journey.","n":"The woman reads the leaving in the caresses themselves: each refrain begins with what he does too much (அமையார், 'not content') and ends 'what is he thinking? I do not know'. The bowmen of the opening kill those who carry nothing, so the friend quietly answers the wealth motive in advance: on this road wealth protects no one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kurippu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 4","lb":"KL4","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["புலி","புலி",1,"maa","simile","tiger"],["மறவர்","மறவர்",3,"seythi","scene","bowmen"],["புள்ளும்","புள்",6,"pul","absent",""],["முள்","முள்",13,"maram","simile","thorn"],["கள்ளினும்","கள்",14,"unavu","simile","toddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:5":{"book":"kalittokai","num":5,"t":["பாஅல் அம் செவிப் பணைத் தாள் மா நிரை","மாஅல் யானையொடு மறவர் மயங்கித்","தூறு அதர்பட்ட ஆறு மயங்கு அருஞ்சுரம்,","இறந்து, நீர் செய்யும் பொருளினும், யாம் நுமக்குச்","சிறந்தனம் ஆதல் அறிந்தனிர் ஆயின்,","நீள் இரு முந்நீர் வளி கலன் வௌவலின்","ஆள்வினைக்கு அழிந்தோர் போறல் அல்லதைக்,","கேள் பெருந்தகையோடு எவன் பல மொழிகுவம்?","நாளும் கோள்மீன் தகைத்தலும் தகைமே;","கல்லெனக் கவின் பெற்ற விழவு ஆற்றுப்படுத்த பின்,","புல்லென்ற களம் போலப் புலம்பு கொண்டு, அமைவாளோ?","ஆள்பவர் கலக்குற அலை பெற்ற நாடு போல்,","பாழ்பட்ட முகத்தோடு பைதல் கொண்டு, அமைவாளோ?","ஓர் இரா வைகலுள், தாமரைப் பொய்கையுள்","நீர் நீத்த மலர் போல, நீ நீப்பின், வாழ்வாளோ?","என ஆங்கு,","பொய் நல்கல் புரிந்தனை புறந்தரல் கைவிட்டு,","எந்நாளோ, நெடுந்தகாய்! நீ செல்வது,","அந்நாள் கொண்டு இறக்கும், இவள் அரும்பெறல் உயிரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that if he knew they were dearer to him than the wealth he would win across a waste where herds and elephants mingle with bowmen and paths are lost, he would not be like traders ruined when the wind seizes their ships; she asks whether the woman will bear it, desolate like a festival ground after the festival, like a land ravaged by its own rulers, or live like a lotus a night out of water, and says that on whatever day he goes her life will go.","n":"Line 9 (நாளும் கோள்மீன் தகைத்தலும் தகைமே) is obscure; on one reading the friend says that holding back even the planets on their course would be fitting for such a man, an ironic concession. The close does not ask him to stay: it makes the date of his leaving the date of her death, so that he must choose a day knowing what it names.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 5","lb":"KL5","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மா","மா",1,"maa","scene","beasts"],["யானையொடு","யானை",2,"maa","scene",""],["மறவர்","மறவர்",2,"seythi","scene","bowmen"],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",14,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:6":{"book":"kalittokai","num":6,"t":["மரையா மரல் கவர, மாரி வறப்ப -","வரை ஓங்கு அரும் சுரத்து ஆர் இடைச் செல்வோர்,","சுரை அம்பு மூழ்கச் சுருங்கிப், புரையோர் தம்","உள் நீர் வறப்பப் புலர் வாடு நாவிற்குத் -","தண்ணீர் பெறாஅத் தடுமாற்று அரும் துயரம்","கண்ணீர் நனைக்கும் கடுமைய காடு என்றால்,","என் நீர் அறியாதீர் போல இவை கூறல்?","நின் நீர அல்ல நெடுந்தகாய்! எம்மையும்,","அன்பு அறச் சூழாதே, ஆற்று இடை நும்மொடு","துன்பம் துணை ஆக நாடின், அது அல்லது","இன்பமும் உண்டோ, எமக்கு?"],"p":"","r":"The woman answers the man who has painted the waste to her (antelope eating marral, the rains failed, travellers shot and shrunk, tongues dry for water, tears the only wetness) by asking why he speaks as if he did not know her: it is unlike him; if he would think of her with love and take her along with hardship for a companion, what other pleasure could she want?","n":"She turns his deterrent catalogue round: the harder the road, the more it needs a companion; துன்பம் துணை ஆக makes hardship itself the shared thing. The plural எம் may be the friend speaking for her, as often in this book.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vaazhkkaithunai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 6","lb":"KL6","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மரையா","மரையா",1,"maa","scene","wild cow"],["மரல்","மரல்",1,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:7":{"book":"kalittokai","num":7,"t":["வேனில் உழந்த வறிது உயங்கு ஓய் களிறு","வான் நீங்கு வைப்பின் வழங்காத் தேர் நீர்க்கு அவாஅம்","கானம் கடத்திர், எனக் கேட்பின், யான் ஒன்று","உசாவுகோ - ஐய! சிறிது;","நீயே, செய் வினை மருங்கில் செலவு அயர்ந்து, யாழ நின்","கை புனை வல் வில் ஞாண் உளர்தீயே;","இவட்கே, செய்வுறு மண்டிலம் மையாப்பது போல்,","மை இல் வாள் முகம் பசப்பு ஊருமே;","நீயே, வினை மாண் காழகம் வீங்கக் கட்டிப்","புனை மாண் மரீஇய அம்பு தெரிதீயே;","இவட்கே, சுனை மாண் நீலம் கார் எதிர்பவை போல்,","இனை நோக்கு உண் கண் நீர் நில்லாவே;","நீயே, புலம்பு இல் உள்ளமொடு பொருள் வயின் செலீஇய","வலம் படு திகிரி வாய் நீவுதியே;","இவட்கே, அலங்கு இதழ்க் கோடல் வீ உகுபவை போல்","இலங்கு ஏர் எல் வளை இறை ஊரும்மே;","என நின்,","செல் நவை அரவத்தும் இனையவள் நீ நீப்பின்,","தன் நலம் கடைகொளப்படுதலின், மற்று இவள்","இன் உயிர் தருதலும் ஆற்றுமோ -","முன்னிய தேஎத்து முயன்று செய் பொருளே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man, who means to cross a forest where a tusker worn by summer craves mirage water, one thing: as he plucks his bowstring her bright face darkens like the sun's disc clouded, as he binds his quiver and picks his arrows her eyes overflow like spring lilies meeting rain, as he strokes his chariot wheel her bangles slip like falling kodal petals; she suffers at the very noise of his going, so can the wealth he toils for in that land give her back her life?","n":"The three stanzas pair each of his war-like preparations with a sign in her body (நீயே ... இவட்கே), so that the leaving happens twice, in his gear and in her; line 18 makes the point that she is undone by the noise of the preparations (அரவத்தும்) before he has gone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","uyir","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 7","lb":"KL7","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",1,"maa","scene","tusker"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",11,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["கோடல்","கோடல்",15,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:8":{"book":"kalittokai","num":8,"t":["நடுவு இகந்து ஒரீஇ நயன் இல்லான் வினை வாங்கக்,","கொடிது ஓர்த்த மன்னவன் கோல் போல, ஞாயிறு","கடுகுபு கதிர் மூட்டிக் காய்சினம் தெறுதலின்,","உறல் ஊறு கமழ் கடாத்து ஒல்கிய, எழில் வேழம்","வறன் உழு நாஞ்சில் போல் மருப்பு ஊன்ற, நிலம் சேர,","விறல் மலை வெம்பிய போக்கு அரு வெஞ்சுரம்,","சொல்லாது இறப்பத் துணிந்தனிர்க்கு, ஒரு பொருள்","சொல்லுவது உடையேன், கேள்மின், மற்று ஐஇய!","வீழுநர்க்கு இறைச்சியாய் விரல் கவர்பு இசைக்கும் கோல்","ஏழும், தம் பயன் கெட இடை நின்ற நரம்பு அறூஉம்","யாழினும், நிலை இல்லாப் பொருளையும் நச்சுபவோ?","மரீஇத் தாம் கொண்டாரைக் கொண்டக்கால் போலாது,","பிரியும்கால் பிறர் எள்ளப், பீடு இன்றி புறம் மாறும்","திருவினும், நிலை இல்லாப் பொருளையும் நச்சுபவோ?","புரை தவப் பயன் நோக்கார் தம் ஆக்கம் முயல்வாரை","வரைவு இன்றிச் செறும் பொழுதில், கண் ஓடாது உயிர் வௌவும்","அரைசினும், நிலை இல்லாப் பொருளையும் நச்சுபவோ?","என ஆங்கு,","நச்சல் கூடாது பெரும இச்செலவு","ஒழிதல் வேண்டுவல், சூழின் பழி இன்று;","மன்னவன் புறந்தர, வரு விருந்து ஓம்பித்,","தன் நகர் விழையக் கூடின்,","இன் உறல் வியன் மார்ப! அது மனும் பொருளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man, who resolved without a word to cross a burnt hill-waste where the sun scorches like the sceptre of an unjust king and a weakened elephant leans on its tusks like a plough, that wealth is less stable than a lute whose seven strings fail when the middle one snaps, than Fortune who leaves those she embraced to be mocked, than a king who kills without mercy; he should not desire it and should stay, for to live in his own house under the king's protection, feeding guests, is wealth indeed.","n":"Line 9 uses இறைச்சி in its plain sense, the delight the lute gives its lovers, not the poetic device. The poem ends by redefining wealth (அது மனும் பொருளே): the household that feeds guests under a just king is already what the journey seeks, which lets the friend oppose the journey on the householder's own terms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","ilvaazhkkai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 8","lb":"KL8","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேழம்","வேழம்",4,"maa","simile","elephant"],["யாழினும்","யாழ்",11,"yaazh","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:9":{"book":"kalittokai","num":9,"t":["எறித்தரு கதிர் தாங்கி ஏந்திய குடை நீழல்,","உறித் தாழ்ந்த கரகமும், உரை சான்ற முக்கோலும்,","நெறிப்பட சுவல் அசைஇ, வேறு ஓரா நெஞ்சத்துக்","குறிப்பு ஏவல் செயல் மாலைக், கொளை நடை அந்தணீர்!-","வெவ் இடைச் செலல் மாலை ஒழுக்கத்தீர்; இவ் இடை","என் மகள் ஒருத்தியும், பிறள் மகன் ஒருவனும்,","தம் உளே புணர்ந்த தாம் அறி புணர்ச்சியர்;","அன்னார் இருவரை காணிரோ?- பெரும!","காணேம் அல்லேம், கண்டனம், கடத்து இடை;","ஆண் எழில் அண்ணலோடு அரும் சுரம் முன்னிய","மாண் இழை மடவரல் தாயிர் நீர் போறிர்;","பல உறு நறும் சாந்தம் படுப்பவர்க்கு அல்லதை,","மலை உளே பிறப்பினும், மலைக்கு அவை தாம் என் செய்யும்?","நினையும்கால் நும்மகள் நுமக்கும் ஆங்கு அனையளே!","சீர்கெழு வெண் முத்தம் அணிபவர்க்கு அல்லதை,","நீர் உளே பிறப்பினும், நீர்க்கு அவை தாம் என் செய்யும்?","தேரும்கால் நும் மகள் நுமக்கும் ஆங்கு அனையளே!","ஏழ் புணர் இன் இசை முரல்பவர்க்கு அல்லதை,","யாழ் உளே பிறப்பினும், யாழ்க்கு அவை தாம் என் செய்யும்?","சூழும்கால், நும்மகள் நுமக்கும் ஆங்கு அனையளே!","என ஆங்கு,","இறந்த கற்பினாட்கு எவ்வம் படரன்மின்;","சிறந்தானை வழிபடீஇச் சென்றனள்;","அறம் தலை பிரியா ஆறும் மற்று அதுவே."],"p":"","r":"A mother following her eloped daughter asks brahmin ascetics with umbrella, water-pot and triple staff whether they saw a girl and a boy joined in a love only they knew; they answer that they did, and, calling her the girl's mother, tell her that sandal is no use to the hill it grows on, pearls none to the sea, music none to the lute, but to those who wear and play them: so her daughter is to her; let her not grieve for a girl of surpassing chastity who has gone to follow the man she chose, for that is the way that never leaves virtue.","n":"The answer comes from renouncers, the people least bound to household claims, and they judge the elopement by aram: following the chosen man is itself the righteous way (அறம் தலை பிரியா ஆறு). The three images all say that a thing's worth lies in its use by another, which cuts the parent's claim at the root.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","karpu","aram","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 9","lb":"KL9","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["pira","nartaay",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",12,"maram","simile","sandal"],["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",15,"pira","simile","pearl"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",19,"yaazh","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:10":{"book":"kalittokai","num":10,"t":["வறியவன் இளமை போல், வாடிய சினையவாய்ச்,","சிறியவன் செல்வம் போல், சேர்ந்தார்க்கு நிழல் இன்றி,","யார் கண்ணும் இகந்து செய்து இசை கெட்டான் இறுதி போல்,","வேரொடு மரம் வெம்ப, விரி கதிர் தெறுதலின்","அலவுற்றுக் குடி கூவ, ஆறு இன்றிப் பொருள் வெஃகிக்,","கொலை அஞ்சா வினைவரால், கோல் கோடியவன் நிழல்","உலகு போல், உலறிய உயர் மர வெஞ்சுரம் -","இடை கொண்டு பொருள் வயின் இறத்தி நீ எனக் கேட்பின்,","உடைபு நெஞ்சு உக ஆங்கே ஒளி ஓடற்பாள் மன்னோ!","படை அமை சேக்கையுள் பாயலின் அறியாய் நீ","புடை பெயர்வாய் ஆயினும், புலம்பு கொண்டு இனைபவள்?","முனிவு இன்றி முயல் பொருட்கு இறத்தி நீ எனக் கேட்பின்,","பனிய கண் படல் ஒல்லா படர் கூர்கிற்பாள் மன்னோ!","நனி கொண்ட சாயலாள் நயந்து நீ நகை ஆகத்","துனி செய்து நீடினும், துறப்பு அஞ்சிக் கலுழ்பவள்?","பொருள் நோக்கிப் பிரிந்து நீ போகுதி எனக் கேட்பின்,","மருள் நோக்கம் மடிந்து ஆங்கே மயல் கூர்கிற்பாள் மன்னோ!","இருள் நோக்கம் இடையின்றி, ஈரத்தின் இயன்ற நின்","அருள் நோக்கம் அழியினும், அவலம் கொண்டு அழிபவள்?","என ஆங்கு,","'வினை வெஃகி நீ செலின், விடும் இவள் உயிர்' எனப்","புனை இழாய்! நின் நிலை யான் கூறப், பையென,","நிலவு வேல் நெடுந்தகை, நீள் இடைச்","செலவு ஒழிந்தனனால்; செறிக, நின் வளையே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that she warned the man that the waste, its trees withered like a poor man's youth, shadeless like a mean man's wealth, burnt to the root like the end of one who harmed all and lost his name, dried like a world under a crooked-sceptred king whose men rob and kill, would break her who weeps if he only turns in bed, frets if he lingers in play, and grieves if his kind look fails; told that she would die if he went, the spear-bearing lord gave up the journey, so let her bangles sit tight again.","n":"Each simile for the waste is a moral failure: the poor man's wasted youth, the mean man's wealth, the infamous man's end, the tyrant's realm. The paalai land is drawn from the ethics of the state and of giving, so the man who crosses it for wealth is shown the fate of wealth misused before he sets out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kodungol","selvam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 10","lb":"KL10","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மரம்","மரம்",4,"maram","simile",""],["வளையே","வளை",24,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:11":{"book":"kalittokai","num":11,"t":["'அரிது ஆய அறன் எய்தி அருளியோர்க்கு அளித்தலும்,","பெரிது ஆய பகை வென்று பேணாரைத் தெறுதலும்,","புரிவு அமர் காதலின் புணர்ச்சியும் தரும்' எனப்","பிரிவு எண்ணிப் பொருள் வயின் சென்ற நம் காதலர்","வருவர்கொல், வயங்கு இழாஅய்! வலிப்பல், யான்; கேஎள் இனி:","'அடி தாங்கும் அளவு இன்றி, அழல் அன்ன வெம்மையால்,","கடியவே, கனம் குழாஅய்! காடு' -என்றார்; அக்காட்டுள்,","துடிஅடிக் கயம்தலை கலக்கிய சின் நீரைப்","பிடி ஊட்டிப், பின் உண்ணும் களிறு, எனவும், உரைத்தனரே;","'இன்பத்தின் இகந்து ஒரீஇ, இலை தீந்த உலவையால்,","துன்புறூஉம் தகையவே காடு' -என்றார்; அக்காட்டுள்,","அன்புகொள் மடப் பெடை அசைஇய வருத்தத்தை","மென் சிறகரால் ஆற்றும் புறவு, எனவும், உரைத்தனரே;","'கல் மிசை வேய் வாடக் கனை கதிர் தெறுதலான்,","துன்னரூஉம் தகையவே காடு' - என்றார்; அக்காட்டுள்,","இன்நிழல் இன்மையான் வருந்திய மட பிணைக்குத்","தன் நிழலைக் கொடுத்து அளிக்கும் கலை, எனவும், உரைத்தனரே.","என ஆங்கு,","இனை நலம் உடைய கானம் சென்றோர்","புனை நலம் வாட்டுநர் அல்லர்; மனை வயின்","பல்லியும் பாங்கு ஒத்து இசைத்தன;","நல்எழில் உண் கண்ணும் ஆடுமால், இடனே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend she is sure their lover, who left for wealth saying it brings the means to give to the deserving, victory over enemies and the joy of union, will return: when he said the forest was too hot to tread, leafless and burning, he also said that in it the tusker lets his mate drink the little muddy water before him, the dove fans its tired mate with its soft wings, and the stag gives its own shadow to the shadeless doe; such a forest will not make him wither her beauty, and the house lizard has chirped well and her eye throbs.","n":"Three pairs carry the same iraicchi (the tusker that lets his mate drink first, the dove that fans its mate, the stag that shades the doe); the schema holds one carrier, so the elephant is tagged and the dove and stag are marked with the same role. Deleting any of them leaves her conclusion and the omens of lines 21 and 22 standing. Lines 1 to 3 name the three ends of wealth, giving, conquest and love, so the absence is justified by aram, porul and inbam at once; and she reads his own warnings about the forest against him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 11","lb":"KL11","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",9,"maa","iraicchi","tusker"],["பிடி","பிடி",9,"maa","iraicchi","cow elephant"],["உலவையால்","உலவை",10,"maram","absent","bare branch"],["புறவு","புறவு",13,"pul","iraicchi","dove"],["வேய்","வேய்",14,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["கலை","கலை",17,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["பல்லியும்","பல்லி",21,"pira","scene","lizard"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","களிறு","confirmed","2/2","the man who saw such care on his road will keep faith and come back to care for her"]],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:12":{"book":"kalittokai","num":12,"t":["இடு முள் நெடு வேலி போலக், கொலைவர்","கொடுமரம் தேய்த்தார் பதுக்கை நிரைத்த","கடு நவை ஆர் ஆற்று, அறு சுனை முற்றி,","உடங்கு நீர் வேட்ட உடம்பு உயங்கு யானை","கடும் தாம் பதிபு, ஆங்கு கை தெறப்பட்டு,","வெறி நிரை வேறு ஆகச் சார்ச் சாரல் ஓடி,","நெறி மயக்குற்ற நிரம்பா நீடு அத்தம் -","சிறு நனி நீ துஞ்சி ஏற்பினும், அஞ்சும்","நறு நுதல் நீத்துப் பொருள் வயின் செல்வோய்!","உரன் உடை உள்ளத்தை, செய் பொருள் முற்றிய","வளமையான் ஆகும் பொருள் இது என்பாய்!","இளமையும் காமமும் நின் பாணி நில்லா -","இடை முலைக் கோதை குழைய முயங்கும்","முறை நாள் கழிதல் உறாஅமைக் காண்டை -","கடை நாள் இது என்று அறிந்தாரும் இல்லை;","போற்றாய் - பெரும! நீ; காமம் புகர்பட","வேற்றுமைக் கொண்டு, பொருள் வயின் போகுவாய்,","கூற்றமும் மூப்பும் மறந்தாரோடு ஓராஅங்கு","மாற்றுமைக் கொண்ட வழி."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man, who would leave her of the fragrant brow, who fears even if he sleeps a little apart, to cross a road where cairns over men killed by bowmen stand like a thorn fence and thirsty elephants lose their way among dried springs, that he calls the yield of his work true wealth, but youth and love will not stay in his hand; let him not let the days of embrace pass, for no one knows which day is the last, and he who spoils love for wealth goes the way of those who forget death and age.","n":"The cairns (பதுக்கை) heaped over travellers killed on the road are memorials of violent death, not hero-stones, so they are scene here. The argument is a memento mori turned toward love: because the last day is unknown (கடை நாள் இது என்று அறிந்தாரும் இல்லை), the present embrace, not future wealth, is what must not be wasted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 12","lb":"KL12","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["கொலைவர்","கொலைவர்",1,"seythi","scene","killers"],["சுனை","சுனை",3,"pira","absent","spring"],["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:13":{"book":"kalittokai","num":13,"t":["செரு மிகு சின வேந்தன் சிவந்து இறுத்த புலம் போல,","எரி மேய்ந்த கரி வறல் வாய் புகுவ காணாவாய்ப்,","பொரி மலர்ந்தன்ன பொறிய மட மான்,","திரி மருப்பு ஏறொடு தேர் அறற்கு ஓட,","மரல் சாய மலை வெம்ப, மந்தி உயங்க,","உரல் போல் அடிய உடம்பு உயங்கு யானை,","ஊறு நீர் அடங்கலின், உண் கயம் காணாது,","சேறு சுவைத்துத், தம் செல் உயிர் தாங்கும்","புயல் துளி மாறிய, போக்கு அரு, வெஞ்சுரம் -","எல் வளை! எம்மொடு நீ வரின், யாழ நின்","மெல் இயல் மேவந்த சீறடித், தாமரை,","அல்லி சேர் ஆய் இதழ் அரக்குத் தோய்ந்தவை போலக்","கல் உறின், அவ்வடி கறுக்குந அல்லவோ?","நலம்பெறு சுடர் நுதால்! எம்மொடு நீ வரின்,","இலங்கு மாண் அவிர் தூவி அன்ன மென் சேக்கையுள்,","துலங்கு மான் மேல் ஊர்தித் துயில் ஏற்பாய், மற்று ஆண்டை","விலங்கு மான் குரல் கேட்பின், வெருவுவை அல்லையோ?","கிளி புரை கிளவியாய்! எம்மொடு நீ வரின்,","தளி பொழி தளிர் அன்ன எழில் மேனி கவின் வாட,","முளி அரில் பொத்திய முழங்கு அழல் இடை போழ்ந்த","வளி உறின், அவ் எழில் வாடுவை அல்லையோ?","என ஆங்கு,","அனையவை காதலர் கூறலின், 'வினை வயின்","பிரிகுவர்' எனப் பெரிது அழியாது, திரிபு உறீஇக்,","கடுங்குரை அருமைய காடு எனின், அல்லது,","கொடுங் குழாய்! துறக்குநர் அல்லர் -","நடுங்குதல் காண்மார், நகை குறித்தனரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman not to despair that he will leave: the man said that if she came with him across the burnt waste (black as a field a raging king has fired, where spotted does and stags run to mirages, monkeys droop and mortar-footed elephants suck mud) her lotus soles would blacken on the stones, she who sleeps on swan-soft beds would start at the cries of beasts, and her leaf-like body would wither in the wind off burning brush; unless the forest is truly hard he will not leave her; he said it in jest, to see her tremble.","n":"The friend reinterprets the man's catalogue of dangers as a lover's teasing (நகை குறித்தனரே), so the same speech that elsewhere announces a parting is here a test of her fear. Line 16 (துலங்கு மான் மேல் ஊர்தி) is unclear: மான் may be the lion-carved bed or a mount.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nalam","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 13","lb":"KL13","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"],["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["மான்","மான்",3,"maa","simile","doe"],["ஏறொடு","ஏறு",4,"maa","scene","stag"],["மரல்","மரல்",5,"maram","absent",""],["மந்தி","மந்தி",5,"maa","scene","monkey"],["யானை","யானை",6,"maa","simile",""],["தாமரை","தாமரை",11,"maram","simile",""],["மான்","மான்",17,"maa","scene","beast"],["கிளி","கிளி",18,"pul","simile","parrot"],["தளிர்","தளிர்",19,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:14":{"book":"kalittokai","num":14,"t":["அணை மருள் இன் துயில் அம் பணைத் தட மென் தோள்,","துணை மலர் எழில் நீலத்து ஏந்து எழில் மலர் உண் கண்,","மண மௌவல் முகை அன்ன மா வீழ் வார் நிரை வெண்பல்,","மணம் நாறு நறு நுதல், மாரி வீழ் இரும் கூந்தல்,","அலர் முலை ஆகத்து, அகன்ற அல்குல்,","சில நிரை வால் வளைச் செய்யாயோ! எனப்,","பல பல கட்டுரை பண்டையின் பாராட்டி,","இனிய சொல்லி, இன்னாங்குப் பெயர்ப்பது","இனி அறிந்தேன் அது துனி ஆகுதலே;","'பொருள் அல்லால் பொருளும் உண்டோ?' என, யாழ நின்","மருளி கொள் மட நோக்கம், மயக்கப்பட்டு அயர்த்தாயோ?","'காதலார் எவன் செய்ப, பொருள் இல்லாதார்க்கு?' என,","ஏதிலார் கூறும் சொல் பொருள் ஆக மதித்தாயோ?","செம்மையின் இகந்து ஒரீஇப் பொருள் செய்வார்க்கு அப்பொருள்","இம்மையும் மறுமையும் பகை ஆவது அறியாயோ?","அதனால்,","எம்மையும் பொருள் ஆக மதித்தீத்தை; நம்முள் நாம்","கவவுக் கைவிடப் பெறும் பொருள் திறத்து","அவவுக் கைவிடுதல்; அது மனும் பொருளே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the man that she now knows that his old praise of her shoulders, eyes, teeth, brow, hair and breasts, sweetly spoken and then turned away, means estrangement; she asks whether, deluded, he has forgotten, and whether he has taken for truth strangers' talk that 'there is no wealth but wealth' and 'what can lovers do for those who have none', not knowing that wealth made by leaving the straight way is an enemy in this life and the next; let him count her as wealth too: to give up the craving for wealth gained by letting go their embrace is wealth indeed.","n":"The poem quotes two proverbs of the wealth party (பொருள் அல்லால் பொருளும் உண்டோ; காதலார் எவன் செய்ப பொருள் இல்லாதார்க்கு) and assigns them to strangers (ஏதிலார்), then answers with the ethical claim that wealth got by leaving the straight way is hostile in both worlds. The last two lines pun on பொருள, wealth and true worth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naduvu","marumai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 14","lb":"KL14","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நீலத்து","நீலம்",2,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["மௌவல்","மௌவல்",3,"maram","simile",""],["வளைச்","வளை",6,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:15":{"book":"kalittokai","num":15,"t":["அரி மான் இடித்தன்ன, அம் சிலை வல் வில்","புரி நாண், புடையின், புறம் காண்டல் அல்லால் -","இணைப் படைத் தானை அரசோடு உறினும் -","கணைத் தொடை நாணும், கடும் துடி ஆர்ப்பின்,","எருத்து வலிய எறுழ் நோக்கு இரலை","மருப்பின் திரிந்து மறிந்து வீழ் தாடி,","உருத்த கடும் சினத்து, ஓடா மறவர்,","பொருள் கொண்டு புண் செயின் அல்லதை, அன்போடு","அருள் புறம் மாறிய ஆர் இடை அத்தம் -","புரிபு நீ புறம் மாறிப், போக்கு எண்ணிப், புதிது ஈண்டிப்","பெருகிய செல்வத்தான் பெயர்த்தரல் ஒல்வதோ -","செயலை அம் தளிர் ஏய்க்கும் எழில் நலம்; அந் நலம்","பயலையால் உணப்பட்டுப் பண்டை நீர் ஒழிந்தக் கால்?","பொய் அற்ற கேள்வியால், புரையோரைப் படர்ந்து, நீ","மை அற்ற படிவத்தான் மறுத்தரல் ஒல்வதோ -","தீம் கதிர் மதி ஏய்க்கும் திருமுகம்; அம் முகம்,","பாம்பு சேர் மதி போலப் பசப்பு ஊர்ந்து தொலைந்தக் கால்?","பின்னிய தொடர் நீவிப், பிறர் நாட்டுப் படர்ந்து, நீ","மன்னிய புணர்ச்சியான் மறுத்தரல் ஒல்வதோ -","புரி அவிழ் நறு நீலம் புரை உண் கண் கலுழ்பு ஆனாத்,","திரி உமிழ் நெய்யே போல், தெண் பனி உறைக்கும்கால்?","என ஆங்கு,","அனையவை போற்ற, நினைஇயன நாடிக் காண்;","வளமையோ வைகலும் செயல் ஆகும்; மற்று இவள்","முளை நிரை முறுவலார் ஆயத்துள் எடுத்து ஆய்ந்த","இளமையும் தருவதோ, இறந்த பின்னே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man, who would cross a road where bowmen with bowstrings that roar like lions and beards curled like a stag's horns, who do not flee even from kings' armies, wound men for gain without mercy, whether fresh wealth can restore her beauty, like young asoka leaves, once pallor eats it, whether learning and pure penance can restore her moon face once pallor covers it like the snake on the moon, whether lasting union can restore her lily eyes once they drip like a lamp spitting oil: wealth can be made any day, but will it bring back her youth once it has gone?","n":"The three questions pit three goods the man might gain abroad, wealth, learning and penance, and lasting union, against three losses that none of them can restore. The close makes the asymmetry explicit: wealth recurs daily, youth does not, so the wealth journey is a bad exchange by the merchant's own reckoning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","selvam","pasalai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 15","lb":"KL15","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["மான்","மான்",1,"maa","simile","lion"],["துடி","துடி",4,"parai","scene",""],["இரலை","இரலை",5,"maa","simile","stag"],["மறவர்","மறவர்",7,"seythi","scene","bowmen"],["செயலை","செயலை",12,"maram","simile","asoka"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",20,"maram","simile","blue lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:16":{"book":"kalittokai","num":16,"t":["பாடு இன்றிப் பசந்தகண் பைதல பனிமல்க,","வாடுபு வனப்பு ஓடி, வணங்கு இறை வளை ஊர,","ஆடு எழில் அழிவு அஞ்சாது, அகன்றவர் திறத்து இனி","நாடும்கால், நினைப்பது ஒன்று உடையேன் மன்? அதுவும் தான்:","தொல்நலம் தொலைபு, ஈங்கு, யாம் துயர் உழப்பத் துறந்து உள்ளார்,","துன்ன, நம் காதலர், துறந்து ஏகும் ஆர் இடைக்","'கல் மிசை உருப்பு அறக் கனை துளி சிதறு!' என,","இன் இசை எழிலியை இரப்பவும் இயைவதுவோ?","புனை இழாய்! ஈங்கு நாம் புலம்பு உறப் பொருள் வெஃகி,","முனை என்னார் காதலர் முன்னிய ஆர் இடைச்,","'சினை வாடச் சிறக்கும் நின் சினம் தணிந்தீக!' எனக்,","கனை கதிர்க் கனலியைக் காமுறல் இயைவதுவோ?","ஒளி இழாய்! ஈங்கு நாம் துயர் கூரப், பொருள் வயின்,","அளி ஒரீஇக் காதலர் அகன்று ஏகும் ஆர் இடை,","'முளி முதல் மூழ்கிய வெம்மை தீர்ந்து உறுக' என,","வளி தரும் செல்வனை வாழ்த்தவும் இயைவதுவோ?","என ஆங்கு,","செய் பொருள் சிறப்பு எண்ணிச் செல்வார் மாட்டு, இனையன","தெய்வத்துத் திறன் நோக்கத், தெருமரல் - தேமொழி! -","'வறன் ஓடின் வையகத்து வான் தரும் கற்பினாள்","நிறன் ஓடிப் பசப்பு ஊர்தல் உண்டு' என,","அறன் ஓடி விலங்கின்று, அவர் ஆள்வினை திறத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman, whose eyes are pale and tearful and whose bangles slip, that she too has wished to beg the cloud to cool with rain the rocks on their lover's road, the sun to calm its rage, the wind-god to blow cool, and that it is useless to fret looking to gods for one who leaves for wealth; but virtue itself has run to block his venture, saying that the chaste woman who brings rain to the world in drought must not lose her colour to pallor.","n":"The cloud, the sun and the wind are addressed as powers to be begged and praised, and the friend calls that hope vain; but she then invokes the one power she trusts, the chaste wife's own power over rain. The woman who cannot get the cloud to cool his road is herself the one who brings rain to the world, so virtue (அறன்) takes her part.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","mazhai","aram","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 16","lb":"KL16","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வளை","வளை",2,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["சினை","சினை",11,"maram","absent","branch"],["தெய்வத்துத்","தெய்வம்",19,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:17":{"book":"kalittokai","num":17,"t":["படை பண்ணிப் புனையவும், பா மாண்ட பல அணைப்","புடை பெயர்ந்து ஒடுங்கவும், புறம் சேர உயிர்ப்பவும்,","'உடையதை எவன் கொல்?' என்று ஊறு அளந்தவர் வயின்","நடை செல்லாய், நனி ஏங்கி நடுங்கல் காண் - நறு நுதால்!","தொல் எழில் தொலைபு இவள் துயர் உழப்பத், துறந்து நீ,","வல் வினை வயக்குதல் வலித்திமன்; வலிப்பளவை,","நீள் கதிர் அவிர் மதி நிறைவு போல் நிலையாது,","நாளினும் நெகிழ்பு ஓடும் நலன் உடன் நிலையுமோ?","ஆற்றா நோய் அட, இவள் அணி வாட, அகன்று நீ,","தோற்றம் சால் தொகு பொருள் முயறிமன், முயல்வளவை","நாற்றம் சால் நளி பொய்கை அடை முதிர் முகையிற்குக்","கூற்று ஊழ் போல் குறைபடூஉம் வாழ் நாளும் நிலையுமோ?","வகை எழில் வனப்பு எஞ்ச, வரை போக வலித்து நீ,","பகை அறு பய வினை முயறிமன்; முயல்வளவைத்,","தகை வண்டு புதிது உண்ணத் தாது அவிழ் தண் போதின்","முகை வாய்த்த தடம் போலும் இளமையும் நிலையுமோ?","என ஆங்கு,","பொருந்தி யான் தான் வேட்ட பொருள் வயின் நினைந்த சொல்,","திருந்திய யாக்கையுள் மருத்துவன் ஊட்டிய","மருந்து போல், மருந்து, ஆகி மனன் உவப்பப்","பெரும் பெயர் மீளி - பெயர்ந்தனன் செலவே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman how she spoke to the man as he resolved to leave while she wasted: will her beauty, which wanes daily unlike the full moon, last until he returns? will life, cut short as death cuts a bud in a fragrant pool, last? will youth, fresh as a pool of opening flowers where bees feed new, last? and says that her words, like medicine a physician gives to a sound body, became a medicine that gladdened his heart, and the great hero gave up his going.","n":"The moon simile is turned: the moon wanes and fills again, her beauty only wanes (நிலையாது). The physician's medicine given to a body already well suggests the man was never truly bent on going; her words only confirmed what his heart wanted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","nilaiyaamai","pirivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 17","lb":"KL17","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பொய்கை","பொய்கை",11,"pira","simile","pool"],["முகையிற்குக்","முகை",11,"maram","simile","bud"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",15,"pira","simile","bee"],["போதின்","போது",15,"maram","simile","flower"],["மருத்துவன்","மருத்துவன்",19,"seythi","simile","physician"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:18":{"book":"kalittokai","num":18,"t":["அரும் பொருள் வேட்கையின் உள்ளம் துரப்பப்,","பிரிந்து உறை சூழாதி -ஐய! - விரும்பி நீ","என் தோள் எழுதிய தொய்யிலும், யாழ நின்","மைந்து உடை மார்பில் சுணங்கும். நினைத்துக் காண்;","சென்றோர் முகப்பப் பொருளும் கிடவாது;","ஒழிந்தவர் எல்லாரும் உண்ணாதும் செல்லார்;","இளமையும், காமமும் ஓராங்குப் பெற்றார்","வளமை விழைதக்கது உண்டோ? உள நாள்,","ஒரோஒ கை தம்முள் தழீஇ, ஒரோஒ கை","ஒன்றன் கூறு ஆடை உடுப்பவரே ஆயினும்,","ஒன்றினார் வாழ்க்கையே வாழ்க்கை; அரிது அரோ","சென்ற இளமை தரற்கு!"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the man not to plan to leave her, driven by desire for rare wealth: let him remember the designs he painted on her shoulders and the marks on his own chest; wealth does not lie waiting for those who go, and those who stay do not all starve; for those who have youth and love together, is wealth worth wanting? while their days last, even if they share one cloth, each holding the other with one hand, the life of the united is the only life, and youth once gone is hard to bring back.","n":"The one-cloth couple (ஒன்றன் கூறு ஆடை உடுப்பவரே ஆயினும்) concedes poverty outright: the claim is not that they will be rich enough but that union outweighs want. Lines 5 and 6 answer the merchant's fear with plain observation, as if from experience.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam","inmai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 18","lb":"KL18","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:1"},"kl:19":{"book":"kalittokai","num":19,"t":["செவ்விய தீவிய சொல்லி, அவற்றொடு","பைய முயங்கிய அஞ்ஞான்று, அவை எல்லாம்","பொய் ஆதல் யான் யாங்கு அறிகோ, மற்று? - ஐய! -","அகல் நகர் கொள்ளா அலர் தலைத் தந்து,","பகல் முனி வெஞ்சுரம் உள்ளல் அறிந்தேன்;","மகன் அல்லை மன்ற, இனி;","செல் இனிச் சென்று நீ செய்யும் வினை முற்ற,","அன்பு அற மாறி, 'யாம் உள்ளத் துறந்தவள்","பண்பும் அறிதிரோ' என்று, வருவாரை","என் திறம் யாதும் வினவல்; வினவின்,","பகலின் விளங்கு நின் செம்மல் சிதையத்","தவல் அரு செய் வினை முற்றாமல், ஆண்டு ஓர்","அவலம் படுதலும் உண்டு."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the man that she could not have known his sweet words and gentle embrace were false; now that gossip the wide town cannot hold has come and she knows he means to cross the waste that even daylight shuns, he is no true man; let him go and finish his work, but let him not, his love gone, ask those who come from her how she whom he left is faring, for if he asks, his name bright as daylight may be marred and grief may overtake him there with the work undone.","n":"The close is a blessing turned inside out: she protects his success by asking him to forget her, so that the grief of remembering would fall on him and his work, not on her. மகன் அல்லை ('you are no man') refuses him the name of a man of worth in one half-line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vaaimai","alar","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 19","lb":"KL19","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:20":{"book":"kalittokai","num":20,"t":["பல் வளம் பகர்பு ஊட்டும் பயன் நிலம் பைது அறச்,","செல் கதிர் ஞாயிறு செயிர் சினம் சொரிதலின்,","தணிவு இல் வெம் கோடைக்குத் தண் நயந்து அணி கொள்ளும்","பிணி தெறல் உயக்கத்த பெருங் களிற்று இனம் தாங்கும்","மணி திகழ் விறல் மலை வெம்ப மண் பகத்,","துணி கயம் துகள் பட்ட தூங்கு அழல் வெஞ்சுரம்-","'கிளி புரை கிளவியாய்! நின் -அடிக்கு எளியவோ,","தளி உறுபு அறியாவே, காடு!' எனக் கூறுவீர்!","வளியினும் வரை நில்லா வாழு நாள், நும் ஆகத்து","அளி என உடையேன் யான்; அவலம் கொண்டு அழிவலோ?","'ஊறு நீர் அமிழ்து ஏய்க்கும் எயிற்றாய்! நீ உணல் வேட்பின்,","ஆறு நீர் இல' என, அறன் நோக்கிக் கூறுவீர்!","யாறு நீர், கழிந்தன்ன இளமை, நும் நெஞ்சு என்னும்","தேறு நீர், உடையேன் யான், தெருமந்து ஈங்கு ஒழிவலோ?","'மாண் எழில் வேய் வென்ற தோளாய்! நீ வரின், தாங்கும்","மாண் நிழல் இல ஆண்டை மரம்' எனக் கூறுவீர்!","நீள் நிழல் தளிர் போல நிறன் ஊழ்த்தல் அறிவேன்; நும்","தாள் நிழல் கைவிட்டு யான் தவிர்தலைச் சூழ்வலே?","என ஆங்கு,","'அணை அரு வெம்மைய காடு' எனக் கூறுவீர்;","கணை கழிகல்லாத கல் பிறங்கு ஆர் இடைப்","பணை எருத்து எழில் ஏற்றின் பின்னர்ப்","பிணையும் காணிரோ? பிரியுமோ, அவையே?"],"p":"","r":"The woman answers the man who warns her off the waste where the sun's rage has dried the land, elephants languish, the hill burns and the pools are dust: to his 'can your feet bear a forest that never knew rain?' she says the days of life stay less than wind and she has his chest as her grace, so why perish in grief; to his 'there is no water on the road' she says youth runs off like river water and she has his heart for clear water, so why stay here bewildered; to his 'there are no shady trees there' she says she knows her colour will fade like shaded leaves, so why leave the shade of his feet; and to his 'the forest is too hot to approach' she asks whether he will not see, on the rocky track where no arrow passes, the doe following the handsome stag, and whether they part.","n":"Each of his warnings flatters a part of her (parrot speech, nectar mouth, bamboo shoulders) and each answer turns it: her life is shorter than wind, her youth flows away like the river, her colour already fades; the poem ends not with argument but with the deer pair he will meet on the very road, which she makes his own witness against going alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ilamai","nilaiyaamai","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 20","lb":"KL20","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["களிற்று","களிறு",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கிளி","கிளி",7,"pul","simile","parrot"],["வேய்","வேய்",15,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["மரம்","மரம்",16,"maram","absent",""],["தளிர்","தளிர்",17,"maram","simile",""],["ஏற்றின்","ஏறு",22,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["பிணையும்","பிணை",23,"maa","iraicchi","doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பிணையும்","confirmed","1/1","if even the deer cross the waste together, the wife should not be left behind by her husband"]],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:21":{"book":"kalittokai","num":21,"t":["'பால் மருள் மருப்பின், உரல் புரை பாவடி,","ஈர் நறும் கமழ் கடாஅத்து, இனம் பிரி ஒருத்தல்","ஆறு கடி கொள்ளும் வேறு புலம் படர்,","பொருள் வயின் பிரிதல் வேண்டும்' என்னும்","அருள் இல் சொல்லும், நீ சொல்லினையே!","நன்னர் நறு நுதல் நயந்தனை நீவி,","'நின்னின் பிரியலென் அஞ்சல் ஓம்பு' என்னும்","நன்னர் மொழியும் நீ மொழிந்தனையே!","அவற்றுள், யாவோ வாயின? மாஅல் மகனே!","'கிழவர் இன்னோர்' என்னாது, பொருள் தான்,","பழ வினை மருங்கின், பெயர்பு பெயர்பு உறையும்;","அன்ன பொருள் வயின் பிரிவோய் - நின் இன்று","இமைப்பு வரை வாழாள் மடவோள்","அமை கவின் கொண்ட தோள் இணை மறந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that he has spoken two things, the graceless word that he must leave for wealth to the far land where a lone tusker, cut off from its herd, with milk-white tusks and mortar-like feet, guards the road, and the kind word, stroking her brow, that he will not leave her and she must not fear; which of them is true, she asks, when wealth does not stay with those who call themselves its owners but moves from one to another by former deeds; for such wealth he leaves, and without him the girl will not live the space of a blink, forgetting the beauty of her shoulders.","n":"The friend turns the man's reason against him: if wealth comes and goes by பழ வினை, no journey can earn it, while the girl's life cannot be had again. The lone tusker that has left its herd (இனம் பிரி ஒருத்தல்) is a near miss for iraicchi: it mirrors the man who leaves his own, but it stands inside his own description of the road and does nothing a person of the situation does beyond being alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","oozh","pirivu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 21","lb":"KL21","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஒருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",2,"maa","scene","tusker"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:22":{"book":"kalittokai","num":22,"t":["உண் கடன் வழிமொழிந்து இரக்கும்கால் முகனும், தாம்","கொண்டது கொடுக்கும்கால் முகனும், வேறு ஆகுதல்","பண்டும் இவ் உலகத்து இயற்கை; அஃது இன்றும்","புதுவது அன்றே - புலன் உடை மாந்திர்! -","தாய் உயிர் பெய்த பாவை போல,","நலன் உடையார் மொழிக் கண் தாவார்; தாம் தம் நலம்","தாது தேர் பறவையின் அருந்து, இறல் கொடுக்கும்கால்,","ஏதிலார் கூறுவது எவனோ நின் பொருள் வேட்கை?","நறு முல்லை நேர் முகை ஒப்ப நிரைத்த","செறி முறை பாராட்டினாய்; மற்று, எம் பல்லின்","பறி முறை பாராட்டினையோ? - ஐய!","நெய் இடை நீவி மணி ஒளி விட்டன்ன","ஐவகை பாராட்டினாய்; மற்று, எம் கூந்தல்","செய் வினை பாராட்டினையோ? - ஐய!","குளன் அணி தாமரைப் பாசு அரும்பு ஏய்க்கும்","இளமுலை பாராட்டினாய்; மற்று, எம் மார்பில்","தளர்முலை பாராட்டினையோ? - ஐய!","என ஆங்கு,","அடர் பொன் அவிர் ஏய்க்கும் அவ் வரி வாடச்,","சுடர் காய் சுரம் போகும் நும்மை யாம் எம் கண்","படர் கூற நின்றதும் உண்டோ - தொடர் கூரத்,","துவ்வாமை வந்தக் கடை?"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the man that a borrower's face when he asks and when he repays differ, as the world has always known; women of beauty go where their lovers' word leads them, like dolls a mother has given life to, and once their beauty has been sipped as a bee sips pollen, what is this wealth strangers talk of? He praised her teeth set like jasmine buds, her hair glowing like gems through oil, her breasts like the green lotus buds of the pond; did he ever praise the teeth falling, the hair's later care, the slack breasts? And as he goes to the sun-scorched waste while the gold-bright lines of her skin fade, she asks whether they ever stood before him telling their grief when want pressed hard.","n":"The opening proverb makes love a loan: he borrowed her youth with one face and will repay it with another. Each praise is answered by the same part grown old, so his catalogue of beauty becomes a contract for her whole life. The closing question seems to say they never complained to him of poverty, so his journey answers a need they never voiced; lines 5 to 8 and 19 to 22 are hard and this reading is not settled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","nilaiyaamai","selvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 22","lb":"KL22","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","மூப்பு"]],"k":[["பறவையின்","பறவை",7,"pul","simile","bee"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",9,"maram","simile",""],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",15,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:23":{"book":"kalittokai","num":23,"t":["இலங்கு ஒளி மருப்பின் கைம்மா உளம்புநர்,","புலம் கடி கவணையின், பூஞ் சினை உதிர்க்கும்","விலங்கு மலை வெம்பிய போக்கு அரு வெஞ்சுரம்","தனியே இறப்ப, யான் ஒழிந்து இருத்தல்","நகுதக்கன்று, இவ் அழுங்கல் ஊர்க்கே;","இனி யான்,","உண்ணலும் உண்ணேன்; வாழலும் வாழேன்;","தோள் நலம் உண்டு துறக்கப்பட்டோர்,","வேள் நீர் உண்ட குடை ஓரன்னர்;","நல்குநர் புரிந்து நலன் உணப்பட்டோர்,","அல்குநர் போகிய ஊர் ஓரன்னர்;","கூடினர் புரிந்து குணன் உணப்பட்டோர்.","சூடினர் இட்ட பூ ஓரன்னர்;","என ஆங்கு,","யானும் நின் அகத்து அனையேன்; ஆனாது,","கொலை வெம் கொள்கையொடு நாய் அகப்படுப்ப,","வலைவர்க்கு அமர்ந்த மட மான் போல,","நின் ஆங்கு வரூஉம் என் நெஞ்சினை","என் ஆங்கு வாராது ஓம்பினை கொண்மே!"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the man that for him to cross alone the burnt waste of blocking hills, where elephant-scarers' slings knock the flowers from the boughs, while she stays behind, would make her a mockery to this gossiping town; she will neither eat nor live, for women whose beauty is enjoyed and then abandoned are like a leaf cup once its water is drunk, like a town its dwellers have left, like a flower thrown away once worn, and she is such in his heart; so let him keep her heart, which runs to him like a young doe that, hunted by killing dogs, takes refuge with the netters, and not send it back to her.","n":"The doe that flees the dogs into the hunters' nets is the poem's sharpest figure: her heart goes for safety to the very man whose leaving hurts her, and she asks him to keep it rather than return it. குடை in line 9 seems to be a leaf cup thrown away after drinking; வேள் நீர் is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","alar","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 23","lb":"KL23","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["கைம்மா","கைம்மா",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["உளம்புநர்","உளம்புநர்",1,"seythi","scene","elephant-scarers"],["சினை","சினை",2,"maram","scene","bough"],["பூ","பூ",13,"maram","simile","flower"],["நாய்","நாய்",16,"maa","simile","dog"],["வலைவர்க்கு","வலைவர்",17,"seythi","simile","netters"],["மான்","மான்",17,"maa","simile","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:24":{"book":"kalittokai","num":24,"t":["'நெஞ்சு நடுக்குறக் கேட்டும், கடுத்தும், தாம்","அஞ்சியது ஆங்கே அணங்கு ஆகும்', என்னும் சொல் -","இன் தீம் கிளவியாய்! - வாய் மன்ற; நின் கேள்","புதுவது பல் நாளும் பாராட்ட, யானும்,","'இது ஒன்று உடைத்து' என எண்ணி, அது தேர,","மாசு இல் வண் சேக்கை மணந்த புணர்ச்சியுள்,","பாயல் கொண்டு என் தோள் கனவுவார்; ஆய் கோல்,","தொடி நிரை முன்கையாள் கையாறு கொள்ளாள்,","கடி மனை காத்து ஓம்ப வல்லுவள் கொல்லோ?","'இடு மருப்பு யானை இலங்கு தேர்க்கு ஓடும்","நெடுமலை வெஞ்சுரம் போகி நடு நின்று","செய் பொருள் முற்றும் அளவு?” என்றார்; ஆய் இழாய்!","தாம் இடை கொண்டது அது ஆயின், தம் இன்றி","யாம் உயிர் வாழும் மதுகை இலேம் ஆயின்,","'தொய்யில் துறந்தார் அவர்' என தம் வயின்,","நொய்யார் நுவலும் பழி நிற்பத் தம்மொடு","போயின்று, சொல், என் உயிர்."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the saying 'what one fears, hearing it and suspecting it with a trembling heart, becomes the very affliction' is true: when her man kept praising her as if she were new, she sensed something and, to find it out, lay with him on the clean wide bed; asleep and dreaming of her shoulders he said, 'Will she of the bangled wrists bear it without breaking and guard the house while I cross the long-hilled waste, where tusked elephants run to the mirage, and stay until the wealth is won?'; if that is his mind, since she has no strength to live without him, and lest small people blame him as the man who left her, her life has gone with him: let the friend tell him so.","n":"The departure is learned from sleep-talk: he says in a dream what he will not say awake, and the proverb that opens the poem (அஞ்சியது ஆங்கே அணங்கு ஆகும்) frames it as a fear made real by being looked into. Her life leaving with him is offered to save his name, not hers. Line 11 is supplied from another witness (see source_issues).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kanavu","acham","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 24","lb":"KL24","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",10,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:25":{"book":"kalittokai","num":25,"t":["வயக்குறு மண்டிலம் வடமொழிப் பெயர் பெற்ற","முகத்தவன் மக்களுள் முதியவன் புணர்ப்பினால்,","'ஐவர்' என்று உலகு ஏத்தும் அரசர்கள் அகத்தராக்,","கை புனை அரக்கு இல்லைக் கதழ் எரி சூழ்ந்தாங்குக்,","களி திகழ் கடாஅத்த கடும் களிறு அகத்தவா,","முளி கழை உயர் மலை முற்றிய முழங்கு அழல்,","ஒள் உரு அரக்கு இல்லை வளிமகன் உடைத்துத் தன்","உள்ளத்துக் கிளைகளோடு உயப் போகுவான் போல,","எழு உறழ் தடக்கையின் இனம் காக்கும் எழில் வேழம்,","அழுவம் சூழ் புகை அழல் அதர்பட மிதித்துத் தம்","குழுவொடு புணர்ந்து போம், குன்று அழல் வெஞ்சுரம்","இறத்திரால், ஐய! மற்று இவள் நிலைமை கேட்டீமின்;","மணக்கும்கால், மலர் அன்ன தகையவாய்ச், சிறிது நீர்","தணக்கும்கால், கலுழ்பு ஆனாக் கண் எனவும் உள அன்றோ?","சிறப்புச் செய்து, உழையராப், புகழ்பு ஏத்தி, மற்று அவர்","புறக்கொடையே பழி தூற்றும் புல்லியார் தொடர்பு போல்;","ஈங்கு நீர் அளிக்கும்கால் இறை சிறந்து, ஒரு நாள் நீர்","நீங்கும்கால், நெகிழ்பு ஓடும் வளை எனவும் உள அன்றோ?","செல்வத்துள் சேர்ந்து அவர் வளன் உண்டு, மற்று அவர்","ஒல்கு இடத்து உலப்பு இலா உணர்வு இலார் தொடர்பு போல்;","ஒரு நாள் நீர் அளிக்கும்கால் ஒளி சிறந்து ஒரு நாள் நீர்","பாராட்டாக்கால் பசக்கும் நுதல் எனவும் உள அன்றோ?","பொருந்திய கேண்மையின் மறை உணர்ந்து, அம் மறை","பிரிந்தக்கால் பிறர்க்கு உரைக்கும் பீடு இலார் தொடர்பு போல்;","என ஆங்கு,","யாம் நின் கூறுவது எவன் உண்டு? எம்மினும்","நீ நற்கு அறிந்தனை, நெடுந்தகை! - வானம்","துளி மாறு பொழுதின், இவ் உலகம் போலும் - நின்","அளி மாறு பொழுதின், இவ் ஆய் இழை கவினே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that he would cross the waste where fire rings a hill of dry bamboo with rutting elephants inside, as fire ringed the lac house built by the plot of the blind king's eldest son with the five kings within, and where the bull elephant, as the wind's son broke the lac house and went out with his kin, tramples a path through the smoke and leaves with his herd; let him hear her state: her eyes, flower-like while he is near, weep when he is a little away, like the bond of base men who praise to the face and slander behind; her bangles, firm while he loves her, slip when he goes, like the bond of those who eat a man's wealth and fail him in decline; her brow, bright when he praises her, pales when he does not, like the bond of mean men who tell a secret once parted; he knows all this better than they do: her beauty when his grace is withdrawn is like the world when the sky withholds its rain.","n":"The opening retells the burning of the lac house from the Bharata story, with Bhima, the wind's son, breaking out with his kin; the elephant that leads its herd out of the fire is the hero of that simile, and the man is about to do the opposite. The three refrains make her eyes, bangles and brow behave like false friends, loyal only while he is near and betraying her state once he goes. The phrase for the Kauravas' father in lines 1 and 2 (வயக்குறு மண்டிலம் வடமொழிப் பெயர் பெற்ற முகத்தவன்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ali","natpu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 25","lb":"KL25","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",5,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கழை","கழை",6,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",9,"maa","iraicchi","elephant"],["மலர்","மலர்",13,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வேழம்","confirmed","1/1","even the beast in the fire takes its own out with it, while the man would cross that land leaving his wife behind"]],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:26":{"book":"kalittokai","num":26,"t":["ஒரு குழை ஒருவன் போல், இணர் சேர்ந்த மராஅமும்,","பருதி அம் செல்வன் போல், நனை ஊழ்த்த செருந்தியும்,","மீன் ஏற்றுக் கொடியோன் போல், மிஞிறு ஆர்க்கும் காஞ்சியும்,","ஏனோன் போல், நிறம் கிளர்பு கஞலிய ஞாழலும்,","ஆன் ஏற்றுக் கொடியோன் போல், எதிரிய இலவமும், ஆங்குத்","தீது தீர் சிறப்பின் ஐவர்கள் நிலை போலப்,","போது அவிழ் மரத்தொடு பொரு கரை கவின் பெற,","நோதக வந்தன்றால், இளவேனில் மேதக;","பல் வரி இன வண்டு புதிது உண்ணும் பருவத்துத்,","தொல் கவின் தொலைந்த என் தட மென் தோள் உள்ளுவார்!","ஒல்குபு நிழல் சேர்ந்தார்க்கு உலையாது காத்து ஓம்பி,","வெல் புகழ் உலகு ஏத்த விருந்து நாட்டு உறைபவர்;","திசை திசை தேன் ஆர்க்கும் திருமருதமுன்துறை,","வசை தீர்ந்த என் நலம் வாடுவது அருளுவார்!","நசை கொண்டு தம் நீழல் சேர்ந்தாரைத் தாங்கித், தம்","இசை பரந்து உலகு ஏத்த, ஏதில் நாட்டு உறைபவர்;","அறல் சாஅய் பொழுதோடு, எம் அணி நுதல் வேறு ஆகித்,","திறல் சான்ற பெரு வனப்பு இழப்பதை அருளுவார்!","ஊறு அஞ்சி நிழல் சேர்ந்தார்க்கு, உலையாது காத்து ஓம்பி,","ஆறு இன்றிப் பொருள் வெஃகி அகன்ற நாட்டு உறைபவர்;","என நீ,","தெருமரல் வாழி, தோழி! நம் காதலர்","பொரு முரண் யானையர், போர் மலைந்து எழுந்தவர்,","செரு மேம்பட்ட வென்றியர்,","'வரும்' என வந்தன்று, அவர் வாய்மொழித் தூதே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend recalls the woman's lament: spring has come to pain her, the kadamba in clusters like the god of the single earring, the seruntti in bud like the sun, the kaanji loud with bees like the fish-bannered god, the gnaazhal bright like the other god, the silk-cotton like the bull-bannered god, so that the bank is lovely like the five together; in this season when bees drink new honey, will he think of her shoulders that have lost their beauty; he who guards unfailingly those who come weary to his shade and is praised by the world lives in a stranger's land; will he pity the fading of her beauty at the holy Marudam ford, the change of her brow; and she tells her not to fret: the true word of their lover, with fighting elephants and victorious in battle, has come by messenger that he will come.","n":"Each flowering tree is matched to a god by colour: the white kadamba to the one-earringed god, the seruntti to the sun, the kaanji to Kama of the fish banner, the gnaazhal to the other (probably the dark god), the red silk-cotton to Shiva of the bull banner. The refrain sets the man's public virtue, shelter to all who come to his shade, against his neglect of the one at home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","thoothu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 26","lb":"KL26","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மராஅமும்","மராஅம்",1,"maram","simile","kadamba"],["செருந்தியும்","செருந்தி",2,"maram","simile",""],["மிஞிறு","மிஞிறு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["காஞ்சியும்","காஞ்சி",3,"maram","simile",""],["ஞாழலும்","ஞாழல்",4,"maram","simile",""],["இலவமும்","இலவம்",5,"maram","simile","silk-cotton"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",9,"pira","scene","bee"],["யானையர்","யானை",23,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["river","திருமருதமுன்துறை",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:27":{"book":"kalittokai","num":27,"t":["ஈதலில் குறை காட்டாது, அறன் அறிந்து ஒழுகிய","தீது இலான் செல்வம் போல், தீம் கரை மரம் நந்தப்,","பேதுறு மட மொழிப், பிணை எழில் மான் நோக்கின்,","மாதரார் முறுவல் போல் மண மௌவல் முகை ஊழ்ப்பக்","காதலர்ப் புணர்ந்தவர் கதுப்பு போல், கழல்குபு","தாதொடும் தளிரொடும், தண் அறல் தகைபெறப்,","பேதையோன் வினை வாங்கப்ப் பீடு இலா அரசன் நாட்டு,","ஏதிலான் படை போல, இறுத்தந்தது, இளவேனில்;","நிலம் பூத்த மர மிசை நிமிர்பு ஆலும் குயில் எள்ள,","நலம் பூத்த நிறம் சாய, நம்மையோ மறந்தைக்க;","கலம் பூத்த அணியவர் காரிகை மகிழ் செய்யப்","புலம் பூத்துப் புகழ்பு ஆனாக் கூடலும் உள்ளார் கொல்?","கல் மிசை மயில் ஆலக், கறங்கி ஊர் அலர் தூற்றத்","தொல் நலம் நனி சாய, நம்மையோ மறந்தைக்க;","ஒன்னாதார்க் கடந்து அடூஉம், உரவு நீர் மா கொன்ற,","வென் வேலான் குன்றின் மேல் விளையாட்டும் விரும்பார் கொல்?","மை எழில் மலர் உண்கண் மரு ஊட்டி மகிழ் கொள்ளப்,","பொய்யினால் புரிவுண்ட நம்மையோ மறந்தைக்க;","தைஇய மகளிர்தம் ஆயமோடு அமர்ந்து ஆடும்","வையை வார் உயர் எக்கர் நுகர்ச்சியும் உள்ளார் கொல்?","என ஆங்கு,","நோய் மலி நெஞ்சமோடு இனையல், தோழி!","'நாம் இல்லாப் புலம்பு ஆயின், நடுக்கம் செய் பொழுது ஆயின்,","காமவேள் விழவு ஆயின், கலங்குவள் பெரிது' என","ஏமுறு கடும் திண் தேர் கடவ,","நாம் அமர் காதலர் துணை தந்தார், விரைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that spring has encamped like an enemy army in the land of an unworthy king whose foolish minister lets it in, with the riverbank trees flourishing like the wealth of a man who gives without stint and walks in virtue, mouval buds opening like women's smiles and the cool sand-stream streaked with pollen like the loosened hair of women joined with their lovers; she recalls the woman's laments (has he forgotten us while the cuckoo mocks and our beauty fades; does he not remember Koodal and its jewelled women; nor the play on the hill of the victorious spear-god who killed the sea-mango; nor the high sandbanks of the Vaigai where girls play) and tells her not to grieve with her pained heart: thinking she would be lost in loneliness, in the trembling season, at Kama's festival, their beloved drove his chariot fast and has come to her.","n":"The similes are drawn from statecraft: spring is an enemy army let in by a foolish minister, and the trees thrive like a just giver's wealth; the season attacks the woman as a foe attacks a badly ruled land. The places of Madurai (Koodal, the spear-god's hill, the Vaigai) make the man's absence a loss of the city's shared pleasures.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","alar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 27","lb":"KL27","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மரம்","மரம்",2,"maram","scene",""],["மௌவல்","மௌவல்",4,"maram","simile",""],["குயில்","குயில்",9,"pul","scene","cuckoo"],["மயில்","மயில்",13,"pul","scene","peacock"],["வேலான்","வேலான்",16,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"]],"en":[["town","கூடலும்",12],["hill","குன்றின்",16],["river","வையை",20]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:28":{"book":"kalittokai","num":28,"t":["பாடல் சால் சிறப்பின் சினையவும், சுனையவும்,","நாடினர் கொயல் வேண்டா, நயந்து தாம் கொடுப்ப போல்,","தோடு அவிழ் கமழ் கண்ணி தையுபு புனைவார் கண்","தோடு உறத் தாழ்ந்து, துறை துறை கவின் பெறச்,","செய்யவள் அணி அகலத்து ஆரமொடு அணி கொள்பு,","தொய்யகம் தாழ்ந்த கதுப்புப் போல் துவர் மணல்","வையை வார் அவிர் அறல், இடை போழும் பொழுதினான்;","விரிந்து ஆனா மலர் ஆயின், விளித்து ஆலும் குயில் ஆயின்,","பிரிந்து உள்ளார் அவர் ஆயின், பேதுறூஉம் பொழுது ஆயின்,","அரும் படர் அவல நோய் ஆற்றுவள் என்னாது","வருந்த, நோய் மிகும் ஆயின் - வணங்கு இறை! அளி என்னோ?","புதலவை மலர் ஆயின், பொங்கர் இன வண்டு ஆயின்,","அயலதை அலர் ஆயின், அகன்று உள்ளார் அவர் ஆயின்,","மதலை இல் நெஞ்சொடு மதன் இலள் என்னாது,","நுதல் ஊரும் பசப்பு ஆயின் - நுணங்கு இறை! அளி என்னோ?","தோயின அறல் ஆயின், சுரும்பு ஆர்க்கும் சினை ஆயின்","மாவின தளிர் ஆயின், மறந்து உள்ளார் அவர் ஆயின்,","பூ எழில் இழந்த கண் புலம்பு கொண்டு அமையாது","பாயல் நோய் மிகும் ஆயின் - பைந் தொடி அளி என்னோ?","என ஆங்கு,","ஆய் இழாய்! ஆங்கனம் உரையாதி; சேயார்க்கு","நாம் தூது மொழிந்தனம் விடல் வேண்டா; நம்மினும்","தாம் பிரிந்து உறைதல் ஆற்றலர்,","பரிந்து எவன் செய்தி - வருகுவர் விரைந்தே!"],"p":"","r":"When boughs and pools give their flowers as if unasked, garlands hang to the wearers' ears and the Vaigai's long clear stream parts the red sand like the hair of the lady of the lotus hung with ornament, the woman complains that if the flowers bloom, the cuckoo calls and he does not remember, he cannot think whether she can bear her grief, and what pity is that, as pallor creeps over her brow and her eyes lose their flower-beauty; the answer comes: do not speak so, there is no need to send the far one a message; he can bear separation no better than they; why grieve, he will come quickly.","n":"The three refrains end in அளி என்னோ ('what kind of grace is this?'), a complaint against his lack of pity. Who answers in lines 21 to 24 is not marked: if the friend, it is the usual consolation; if the woman herself, she refuses to send a messenger and trusts that he suffers as much as she does, the stronger reading. Hence two speech situations.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thoothu","pasalai","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 28","lb":"KL28","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கண்ணி","கண்ணி",3,"maram","ornament","garland"],["மலர்","மலர்",8,"maram","scene",""],["குயில்","குயில்",8,"pul","scene","cuckoo"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",12,"pira","scene","bee"],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",16,"pira","scene","bee"],["மாவின","மா",17,"maram","scene","mango"]],"en":[["river","வையை",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:29":{"book":"kalittokai","num":29,"t":["தொல் எழில் வரைத்து அன்றி வயவு நோய் நலிதலின்,","அல்லாந்தார் அலவுற ஈன்றவள் கிடக்கை போல்,","பல் பயம் உதவிய பசுமை தீர் அகல் ஞாலம்","புல்லிய புனிறு ஒரீஇ, புது நலம் ஏர்தர;","வளையவர் வண்டல் போல், வார் மணல் வடுக் கொள,","இளையவர் ஐம்பால் போல் எக்கர் போழ்ந்து அறல் வார,","மா ஈன்ற தளிர் மிசை, மாயவள் திதலை போல்,","ஆய் இதழ்ப் பல் மலர் ஐய கொங்கு உறைத்தர,","மேதக இளவேனில் இறுத்தந்த பொழுதின் கண்;","சேயார் கண் சென்ற என் நெஞ்சினைச் - சின் மொழி! -","நீ கூறும் வரைத்து அன்றி, நிறுப்பென்மன்? நிறை நீவி,","வாய் விரிபு பனி ஏற்ற விரவுப் பல் மலர் தீண்டி,","நோய் சேர்ந்த வைகலான், வாடை வந்து அலைத்தரூஉம்;","போழ்து உள்ளார் துறந்தார் கண் புரி வாடும் கொள்கையைச்","சூழ்பு ஆங்கே - சுடர் இழாய்! கரப்பென்மன்? கை நீவி","வீழ் கதிர் விடுத்த பூ விருந்து உண்ணும் இரும் தும்பி","யாழ் கொண்ட இமிழ் இசை இயல் மாலை அலைத்தரூஉம்;","தொடி நிலை நெகிழ்த்தார்கண் தோயும் என் ஆர்உயிர் -","வடு நீங்கு கிளவியாய்! வலிப்பென் மன்? வலிப்பவும்,","நெடு நிலாத் திறந்து உண்ண, நிரை இதழ் வாய்விட்ட","கடி மலர் கமழ் நாற்றம், கங்குல் வந்து அலைத்தரூஉம்;","என ஆங்கு,","வருந்தினை வதிந்த நின் வளை நீங்கச், சேய் நாட்டுப்","பிரிந்து செய் பொருள் பிணி பின் நோக்காது - ஏகி, நம்","அரும் துயர் களைஞர் வந்தனர் -","திருந்து எயிறு இலங்கு நின் தேமொழி படர்ந்தே."],"p":"","r":"When spring has come, the wide earth that gave many goods shedding its spent look and taking fresh beauty like a mother lying weak after childbirth, the long sand rippled like girls' play-sand, the stream parting the sandbanks like young girls' braided hair, pollen on the mango shoots like the spots on a dark girl's skin, the woman asks her friend whether she can hold back her heart, gone to the far one, beyond what the friend says, when the north wind touching the dewy flowers torments her; whether she can hide her state, when the evening with the dragonfly's lute-like hum torments her; whether she can be firm, when the scent of night-opened flowers torments her at night; and the friend answers that the one who removes their grief has come, not looking back to the wealth that bound him, drawn by her sweet speech.","n":"The three torments run through the day: the north wind by day, the evening's hum, the night scent; the woman's three questions (can I hold, can I hide, can I be firm) answer the friend's counsel of restraint. Lines 10 to 21 are hers, the closing lines the friend's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","paruvam","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 29","lb":"KL29","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மா","மா",7,"maram","scene","mango"],["மலர்","மலர்",8,"maram","scene",""],["வாடை","வாடை",13,"pira","scene","north wind"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",16,"pira","scene","dragonfly"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",17,"yaazh","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:30":{"book":"kalittokai","num":30,"t":["அரும் தவம் ஆற்றியார் நுகர்ச்சி போல், அணி கொள","விரிந்து ஆனாச் சினை தொறூஉம், வேண்டும் தாது அமர்ந்து ஆடிப்,","புரிந்து ஆர்க்கும் வண்டொடு, புலம்பு தீர்ந்து எவ்வாயும்,","இரும் தும்பி, இறைகொள எதிரிய வேனிலான் -","துயில் இன்றி யாம் நீந்தத், தொழுவை அம் புனல் ஆடி,","மயில் இயலார் மரு உண்டு, மறந்து அமைகுவான் மன்னோ -","'வெயில் ஒளி அறியாத விரி மலர்த் தண் காவில்","குயில் ஆலும் பொழுது' எனக் கூறுநர் உளர் ஆயின்?","பானாள் யாம் படர் கூரப் பணை எழில் அணை மென் தோள்","மான் நோக்கினவரொடு மறந்து அமைகுவான் மன்னோ -","'ஆனாச் சீர் கூடலுள் அரும்பு அவிழ் நறு முல்லைத்","தேன் ஆர்க்கும் பொழுது' எனத் தெளிக்குநர் உளர் ஆயின்?","உறலி யாம் ஒளி வாட, உயர்ந்தவன் விழவினுள்","விறல் இழையவரோடு விளையாடுவான் மன்னோ -","'பெறல் அரும் பொழுதோடு பிறங்கு இணர்த் துருத்தி சூழ்ந்து","அறல் வாரும், வையை' என்று அறையுநர் உளர் ஆயின்?","என ஆங்கு,","தணியா நோய் உழந்து ஆனாத் தகையவள் - தகைபெற,","அணி கிளர் நெடும் திண் தேர் அயர்மதி - பணிபு நின்","காமர் கழல் அடி சேரா","நாமம் சால் தெவ்வரின் நடுங்கினள் பெரிதே!"],"p":"","r":"In spring, when bees and dark dragonflies settle on boughs flowering like the joys of those who have done hard penance, the woman asks whether he would stay forgetting, bathing in the stream with peacock-like women while she swims sleepless, if someone told him 'the cuckoos are calling in the cool grove the sun never reaches'; whether he would forget among doe-eyed women at midnight if someone told him 'the bees hum in the budding jasmine of glorious Koodal'; whether he would play with bright-jewelled women at the high one's festival if someone announced 'the Vaigai runs round the flowering islet'; and the friend tells the man to make ready his tall chariot, for she, worn by unending sickness, trembles more than his famed enemies who would not bow at his feet.","n":"The refrain is conditional (மன்னோ ... உளர் ஆயின்): he would not stay forgetting if only someone told him the season had come, so the blame falls on the missing messenger more than on him. The closing simile flatters the warrior (his enemies tremble) while making the woman's fear greater than theirs. The festival of உயர்ந்தவன் is a spring feast at Madurai; the god is not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 30","lb":"KL30","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வண்டொடு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",4,"pira","scene","dragonfly"],["மயில்","மயில்",6,"pul","simile","peacock"],["குயில்","குயில்",8,"pul","scene","cuckoo"],["மான்","மான்",10,"maa","simile","deer"],["முல்லைத்","முல்லை",11,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["town","கூடலுள்",11],["river","வையை",16]],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:31":{"book":"kalittokai","num":31,"t":["கடும் புனல் கால் பட்டுக் கலுழ் தேறிக் கவின் பெற,","நெடும் கயத்து அயல் அயல் அயிர் தோன்ற, அம்மணல்","வடுத்து ஊர வரிப்ப போல் ஈங்கை வாடு உதிர்பு உகப்,","பிரிந்தவர் நுதல் போலப் பீர் வீயக், காதலர்ப்","புணர்ந்தவர் முகம் போலப் பொய்கை பூப் புதிது ஈன,","மெய் கூர்ந்த பனியொடு மேல் நின்ற வாடையால்,","கையாறு கடைக்கூட்டக் கலக்குறூஉம் பொழுதுமன் -","'பொய்யேம்' என்று, ஆய் இழாய்! புணர்ந்தவர் உரைத்ததை;","மயங்கு அமர் மாறு அட்டு, மண் வௌவி வருபவர்,","தயங்கிய களிற்றின் மேல், தகை காண விடுவதோ -","பயம் கெழு பல் கதிர் பால் போலும் பொழுதொடு,","வயங்கு இழை தண்ணென, வந்த இவ் அசை வாடை?","தாள் வலம்பட வென்று, தகை நல் மா மேல் கொண்டு,","வாள் வென்று வருபவர் வனப்பு ஆர விடுவதோ -","நீள் கழை நிவந்த பூ நிறம் வாடத் தூற்றுபு,","தோள் அதிர்பு அகம் சேரத் துவற்றும் இச் சில் மழை?","பகை வென்று திறை கொண்ட பாய் திண்தேர் மிசையவர்","வகை கொண்ட செம்மல் நாம் வனப்பு ஆர விடுவதோ -","புகை எனப் புதல் சூழ்ந்து பூ அம் கள் பொதி செய்யா","முகை வெண் பல் நுதி பொர, முற்றிய கடும் பனி?","என ஆங்கு","வாளாதி வயங்கு இழாய்! 'வருந்துவள் இவள்' என,","நாள் வரை நிறுத்துத் தாம் சொல்லிய பொய் அன்றி,","மீளி வேல் தானையர் புகுதந்தார் -","நீள் உயர் கூடல் நெடு கொடி எழவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that in this season, when the flood has cleared and the long pools are lovely, withered ingai flowers drop as if drawing lines on the sand, the beer flowers are pale like the brows of women parted from their lovers and the ponds bloom like the faces of the united, the cold dew and north wind come to confound them, beyond his vow that he would not lie; will this north wind, coming as the grain ears fill with milk, let them see him come in splendour on his elephant, having won the confused battle and seized land; will this light rain that blows on the bamboo blossoms let them see him come on his fine horse, sword victorious; will this hard dew let them see him come on his chariot with tribute; and she tells her not to be anxious: not failing the day he set, thinking she would suffer, the warriors with fierce spears have entered, as the tall flags of Koodal rise.","n":"The season here is the cold of dew and north wind with ripening grain, not spring, and the man is away at war, not after wealth; his promised return from conquest at a set time is the mullai situation, but the woman's distress keeps the poem in separation. The three questions (விடுவதோ) fear that the season will kill her before she can see his triumph.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","vaaimai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 31","lb":"KL31","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஈங்கை","ஈங்கை",3,"maram","scene",""],["பீர்","பீர்",4,"maram","simile",""],["பூப்","பூ",5,"maram","simile","flower"],["வாடையால்","வாடை",6,"pira","scene","north wind"],["களிற்றின்","களிறு",10,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கதிர்","கதிர்",11,"maram","scene","grain ears"],["மா","மா",13,"maa","scene","horse"],["கழை","கழை",15,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["மழை","மழை",16,"pira","scene","rain"]],"en":[["town","கூடல்",25]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:32":{"book":"kalittokai","num":32,"t":["எஃகு இடை தொட்ட, கார்க் கவின் பெற்ற ஐம்பால் போல் -","மை அற - விளங்கிய, துவர் மணல் அது; அது","ஐது ஆக நெறித்தன்ன அறல் அவிர் நீள் ஐம்பால்","அணி நகை இடையிட்ட ஈகை அம் கண்ணி போல்,","பிணி நெகிழ் அலர் வேங்கை விரிந்த பூ, வெறி கொளத் -","துணி நீரால், தூ மதி நாளால், அணிபெற -","ஈன்றவள் திதலை போல் ஈர் பெய்யும் தளிரொடும்,","ஆன்றவர் அடக்கம் போல் அலர்ச் செல்லாச் சினையொடும்,","வல்லவர் யாழ் போல வண்டு ஆர்க்கும் புதலொடும்,","நல்லவர் நுடக்கம் போல் நயம் வந்த கொம்பொடும்,","உணர்ந்தவர் ஈகை போல் இணர் ஊழ்த்த மரத்தொடும்,","புணர்ந்தவர் முயக்கம் போல் புரிவுற்ற கொடியொடும் -","நயந்தார்க்கோ நல்லைமன், இளவேனில்! எம் போல?","பசந்தவர் பைதல் நோய், பகை எனத் தணித்து, நம்","இன் உயிர் செய்யும் மருந்து ஆகப், பின்னிய","காதலர் - எயிறு ஏய்க்கும் தண் அருவி நறு முல்லைப்","போது ஆரக் கொள்ளும் கமழ் குரற்கு என்னும் -","தூது வந்தன்றே, தோழி!","துயர் அறு கிளவியோடு! அயர்ந்தீகம் விருந்தே!"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that spring has come, its red sand gleaming like comb-parted dark hair, vengai blossoms spread like a gold garland set in braided hair, tender leaves dripping like the spots on a new mother, boughs not yet blossoming like the restraint of the wise who spread no talk, bushes humming with bees like a master's lute, branches swaying like good women dancing, trees heavy with clusters like the giving of the discerning and creepers twined like lovers' embrace; spring is good to those in love, she says, but not to her; yet now their lover has sent word, a medicine for her life against the pallor, that he comes for the fragrant jasmine by the cool falls, buds like her teeth; the message has come with words that end grief, so let them prepare the feast.","n":"Line 8 plays on அலர், blossom and gossip: the boughs that have not yet burst into flower are like the restrained who do not spread talk, so the season keeps the lovers' secret. The eight similes of lines 7 to 12 all draw on human virtue or love. The message in lines 14 to 17 is hard to construe; this reading takes the jasmine 'like her teeth' as the season he names for his return.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","paruvam","virundhu","adakkam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 32","lb":"KL32","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",5,"maram","scene",""],["யாழ்","யாழ்",9,"yaazh","simile",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",9,"pira","simile","bee"],["அருவி","அருவி",16,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["முல்லைப்","முல்லை",16,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:33":{"book":"kalittokai","num":33,"t":["வீறு சால் ஞாலத்து வியல் அணி காணிய","யாறு கண் விழித்த போல், கயம் நந்திக் கவின் பெற,","மணி புரை வயங்கலுள் துப்பு எறிந்தவை போலப்,","பிணி விடு முருக்கு இதழ் அணி கயத்து உதிர்ந்து உகத்,","துணி கய நிழல் நோக்கித் துதைபு உடன் வண்டு ஆர்ப்ப,","மணி போல அரும்பு ஊழ்த்து, மரம் எல்லாம் மலர் வேயக்","காதலர்ப் புணர்ந்தவர் கவவு கை நெகிழாது,","தாது அவிழ் வேனிலோ வந்தன்று; வாரார், நம்","போது எழில் உண் கண் புலம்ப நீத்தவர்;","எரி உரு உறழ இலவம் மலரப்,","பொரி உரு உறழப் புன்கு பூ உதிரப்,","புது மலர்க் கோங்கம் பொன் எனத் தாது ஊழ்ப்பத்,","தமியார்ப் புறத்து எறிந்து எள்ளி, முனிய வந்து,","ஆர்ப்பது போலும் பொழுது; என் அணி நலம்","போர்ப்பது போலும் பசப்பு;","நொந்து நகுவன போல் நந்தின கொம்பு; நைந்து உள்ளி","உகுவது போலும், என் நெஞ்சு; எள்ளித்","தொகுபு உடன் ஆடுவ போலும், மயில்; கையில்","உகுவன போலும், வளை; என் கண் போல்","இகுபு அறல் வாரும் பருவத்தும் வாரார்;","மிகுவது போலும் இந் நோய்;","நரம்பின் தீம் குரல் நிறுக்கும் குழல் போல்","இரங்கு இசை மிஞிறொடு தும்பி தாது ஊதத் -","தூது அவர் விடுதரார்; துறப்பார் கொல்? நோதக","இரும் குயில் ஆலும் அரோ;","என ஆங்கு,","புரிந்து நீ எள்ளும் குயிலையும், அவரையும், புலவாதி;","நீல் இதழ் உண் கண்ணாய் நெறி கூந்தல் பிணி விட","நாள் வரை நிறுத்துத் தாம் சொல்லிய பொய் அன்ற,","மாலை தாழ் வியன் மார்பர் துனைதந்தார் -","கால் உறழ் கடு திண் தேர் கடவினர் விரைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Spring has come, the pools swelling as if the rivers had opened their eyes to see the world's beauty, murukku petals scattering like coral on a gem-clear pool, buds opening like gems on every tree, and those joined with lovers do not loosen their embrace; but he who left her flower-like eyes lonely does not come; the silk-cotton flowers like fire, the punku drops petals like puffed rice, the kongam sheds pollen like gold; the season roars as if to mock the lonely, pallor covers her beauty, the boughs flourish as if laughing at her pain, her heart melts, the peacocks dance as if in scorn, her bangles slip, the streams run like her tears, and still he does not come or send word while bees hum like a flute holding the lute's note and the cuckoo calls; the friend tells her not to quarrel with the cuckoo or with him: not failing the day he set, the broad-chested man with his garland has come swiftly, driving his wind-fast chariot.","n":"The middle of the poem is a run of 'as if' clauses (போலும்) in which the whole season seems to act against her: it roars, mocks, laughs and dances in scorn; her own body answers each one (pallor, melting heart, slipping bangles, tears). The return line (நாள் வரை நிறுத்துத் தாம் சொல்லிய பொய் அன்ற) repeats poem 31 almost word for word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","pasalai","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 33","lb":"KL33","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["முருக்கு","முருக்கு",4,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",5,"pira","scene","bee"],["இலவம்","இலவம்",10,"maram","simile","silk-cotton"],["புன்கு","புன்கு",11,"maram","simile",""],["கோங்கம்","கோங்கம்",12,"maram","simile",""],["மயில்","மயில்",18,"pul","scene","peacock"],["நரம்பின்","நரம்பு",22,"yaazh","simile","lute string"],["குழல்","குழல்",22,"yaazh","simile","flute"],["மிஞிறொடு","மிஞிறு",23,"pira","simile","bee"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",23,"pira","simile","dragonfly"],["குயில்","குயில்",25,"pul","scene","cuckoo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:34":{"book":"kalittokai","num":34,"t":["மன் உயிர் ஏமுற, மலர் ஞாலம் புரவு ஈன்று,","பல் நீரால் பாய் புனல் பரந்து ஊட்டி, இறந்த பின்,","சில் நீரால் அறல் வார, அகல் யாறு கவின் பெற,","முன் ஒன்று தமக்கு ஆற்றி, முயன்றவர் இறுதிக் கண்","பின் ஒன்று பெயர்த்து ஆற்றும் பீடு உடையாளர் போல்,","பல் மலர் சினை உகச் சுரும்பு இமிர்ந்து வண்டு ஆர்ப்ப,","இன் அமர் இளவேனில் இறுத்தந்த பொழுதினான்;","விரி காஞ்சித் தாது ஆடி இரும் குயில் விளிப்பவும்,","பிரிவு அஞ்சாதவர் தீமை மறைப்பென்மன்; மறைப்பவும்,","கரி பொய்த்தான் கீழ் இருந்த மரம் போலக் கவின் வாடி,","எரி பொத்தி, என் நெஞ்சம் சுடும் ஆயின், எவன் செய்கோ?","பொறை தளர் கொம்பின் மேல் சிதர் இனம் இறைகொள","நிறை தளராதவர் தீமை மறைப்பென்மன்; மறைப்பவும்,","முறை தளர்ந்த மன்னவன் கீழ்க் குடி போலக் கலங்குபு,","பொறை தளர்பு பனி வாரும் கண் ஆயின், எவன் செய்கோ?","தளை அவிழ் பூஞ் சினைச் சுரும்பு யாழ் போல இசைப்பவும்,","கொளை தளராதவர் தீமை மறைப்பென்மன்; மறைப்பவும்,","கிளை அழிய வாழ்பவன் ஆக்கம்போல் புல் என்று,","வளை ஆனா நெகிழ்பு ஓடும் தோள் ஆயின், எவன் செய்கோ?","என ஆங்கு,","நின் உள் நோய் நீ உரைத்து அலமரல்; எல்லா! நாம்","எண்ணிய நாள் வரை இறவாது, காதலர்","பண்ணிய மாவினர் புகுதந்தார்","கண் உறு பூசல் கை களைந்தாங்கே."],"p":"","r":"As the wide river, which in flood spread over the land and fed the world for the good of all living things, now runs lovely in thin streams, like men of worth who, having served one end first, turn at the last to serve another, spring has come with flowers falling and bees humming; the woman says she would hide the wrong of the man who did not fear parting, though the cuckoo calls bathed in kaanji pollen, but what can she do if her heart, withered like a tree under which a false witness sat, burns as if set on fire; if her eyes stream like the subjects under a king whose justice has failed; if her shoulders shrink and shed their bangles like the prosperity of one who lives on while his kin are ruined; and the friend tells her not to fret over her inner pain: not passing the day they counted, their lover has come with his trained horses, as a hand clears what troubles the eye.","n":"All three refrain similes are moral and political: the tree that withers where a false witness sat (a belief also known from later texts), the people under a king who has failed in justice, and the wealth of one who thrives while his kin perish; her body's decline is a judgement on his conduct. The sense of the opening simile in lines 4 and 5 (men who serve one end and then another) is not certain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 34","lb":"KL34","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வண்டு","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene","bee"],["காஞ்சித்","காஞ்சி",8,"maram","scene",""],["குயில்","குயில்",8,"pul","scene","cuckoo"],["மரம்","மரம்",10,"maram","simile",""],["சிதர்","சிதர்",12,"pira","scene","bee"],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",16,"pira","simile","bee"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",16,"yaazh","simile",""],["மாவினர்","மா",23,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:35":{"book":"kalittokai","num":35,"t":["மடி இலான் செல்வம் போல் மரன் நந்த, அச் செல்வம்","படி உண்பார் நுகர்ச்சி போல் பல் சினை மிஞிறு ஆர்ப்ப,","மாயவள் மேனி போல் தளிர் ஈன, அம் மேனித்","தாய சுணங்கு போல் தளிர் மிசைத் தாது உக,","மலர் தாய பொழில் நண்ணி மணி நீர கயம் நிற்ப,","அலர் தாய துறை நண்ணி அயிர் வரித்து அறல் வார,","நனி எள்ளும் குயில் நோக்கி இனைபு உகு நெஞ்சத்தால்,","'துறந்து உள்ளார் அவர்' எனத், துனி கொள்ளல், எல்லா! நீ;","வண்ண வண்டு இமிர்ந்து ஆனா வையை வார் உயர் எக்கர்த்","தண் அருவி நறு முல்லைத் தாது உண்ணும் பொழுது அன்றோ-","கண் நிலா நீர் மல்கக் கவவி, நாம் விடுத்தக்கால்","ஒள் நுதால்! நமக்கு அவர் 'வருதும்' என்று உரைத்ததை?","மல்கிய துருத்தியுள் மகிழ் துணைப் புணர்ந்து, அவர்","வில்லவன் விழவினுள் விளையாடும் பொழுது அன்றோ -","'வலன் ஆக, வினை!' என்று வணங்கி, நாம் விடுத்தக்கால்,","ஒளி இழாய்! நமக்கு அவர் 'வருதும்' என்று உரைத்ததை?","நிலன் நாவில் திரிதரூஉம் நீள் மாட கூடலார்","புலன் நாவில் பிறந்த சொல் புதிது உண்ணும் பொழுது அன்றோ-","பல நாடு நெஞ்சினேம் பரிந்து, நாம் விடுத்தக்கால்,","சுடர் இழாய்! நமக்கு அவர் 'வருதும்' என்று உரைத்ததை?","என ஆங்கு,","உள்ளுதொறு உடையும் நின் உயவு நோய்க்கு உயிர்ப்பு ஆகி,","எள் அறு காதலர் இயைதந்தார் - புள் இயல்","காமர் கடும் திண்தேர் பொருப்பன்,","வாய்மை அன்ன வைகலொடு புணர்ந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman, as the trees flourish like the wealth of a man free of sloth, bees swarm in the boughs like those who share that wealth, tender leaves come like a dark girl's skin and pollen falls on them like the spots on that skin, not to sulk, looking at the mocking cuckoo with a breaking heart, that he has forgotten them: is this not the time, when bees drink the jasmine pollen by the cool falls on the Vaigai's high sandbanks, that he named when they sent him off in tears; the time of the bowman god's festival among the islands of the river; the time when the people of Koodal of tall mansions savour the new words born on the tongues of poets; and now, to ease the sickness that breaks her each time she remembers, the faultless lover, lord of the hill, has come on his bird-swift chariot with a day as true as truth.","n":"The third season-sign is a literary one: the people of Madurai savouring new words born on poets' tongues (புலன் நாவில் பிறந்த சொல்), a picture of the city as a place of poetry. The returning man is called பொருப்பன், lord of the hill, a kurinji title in a paalai poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 35","lb":"KL35","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மரன்","மரம்",1,"maram","scene",""],["மிஞிறு","மிஞிறு",2,"pira","simile","bee"],["தளிர்","தளிர்",3,"maram","simile",""],["குயில்","குயில்",7,"pul","scene","cuckoo"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",9,"pira","scene","bee"],["அருவி","அருவி",10,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["முல்லைத்","முல்லை",10,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["river","வையை",9],["town","கூடலார்",17]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:36":{"book":"kalittokai","num":36,"t":["கொடு மிடல் நாஞ்சிலான் தார் போல், மராத்து","நெடு மிசைச் சூழும் மயில் ஆலும் சீர,","வடி நரம்பு இசைப்ப போல் வண்டொடு சுரும்பு ஆர்ப்பத்,","தொடி மகள் முரற்சி போல் தும்பி வந்து இமிர்தர,","இயன் எழீஇயவை போல, எவ்வாய்யும் 'இம்' மெனக்","கயன் அணி பொதும்பருள் கடி மலர்த் தேன் ஊத,","மலர் ஆய்ந்து வயின் வயின் விளிப்ப போல் மரன் ஊழ்ப்ப,","இரும் குயில் ஆலப், பெரும் துறை கவின் பெறக்","குழவி வேனில் விழவு எதிர்கொள்ளும்","சீரார் செவ்வியும் வந்தன்று;","வாரார் தோழி நம் காதலோரே;","பாஅய்ப் பாஅய்ப் பசந்தன்று, நுதல்;","சாஅய்ச் சாஅய் நெகிழ்ந்தன தோள்;","நனி அறல் வாரும் பொழுது என, வெய்ய","பனி அறல் வாரும், என் கண்;","மலை இடை போயினர் வரல் நசைஇ நோயொடு","முலை இடைக் கனலும், என் நெஞ்சு;","காதலின் பிரிந்தார் கொல்லோ? வறிது, ஓர்","தூதொடு மறந்தார் கொல்லோ? நோதகக்,","காதலர் காதலும் காண்பாம் கொல்லோ?","துறந்தவர் ஆண்டு ஆண்டு உறைகுவர் கொல்லோ? யாவது -","'நீள் இடைப்படுதலும் ஒல்லும், யாழ நின்","வாள் இடைப்படுத்த வயங்கு ஈர் ஓதி!","நாள் அணி சிதைத்தலும் உண்டு' என நயவந்து","கேள்வி அந்தணர் கடவும்","வேள்வி ஆவியின் உயிர்க்கும், என் நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"Peacocks dance around the high kadamba like the garland of the plough-armed god, bees hum like tuned strings, dragonflies like a bangled girl singing, honey-bees drone 'im' everywhere as if tuning, the trees blossom as if calling from place to place, the cuckoos call and the lovely season has come to welcome the festival of young spring; but, the woman tells her friend, their lover does not come: her brow is pale, her shoulders shrink, her eyes run hot like the season's streams, her heart burns between her breasts longing for the one who went through the hills; did he leave for love, did he forget with a single message, will they see his love, will he stay there; and her heart sighs like the smoke of the sacrifice that learned brahmins tend, remembering his saying that a long distance might come between them and mar her hair's beauty.","n":"Unlike the poems before it, this one ends with no return: four questions (did he leave, did he forget, will we see, will he stay) and a sigh. The closing simile of sacrificial smoke and the words in lines 22 to 24 are hard to place: this reading takes them as the man's own parting words that she recalls.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","paruvam","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 36","lb":"KL36","poet":"பெருங்கொடுங்கோன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மராத்து","மராஅம்",1,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["மயில்","மயில்",2,"pul","scene","peacock"],["நரம்பு","நரம்பு",3,"yaazh","simile","lute string"],["வண்டொடு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",4,"pira","scene","dragonfly"],["குயில்","குயில்",8,"pul","scene","cuckoo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:2"},"kl:37":{"book":"kalittokai","num":37,"t":["கய மலர் உண் கண்ணாய்! காணாய்; ஒருவன்","வய மான் அடித் தேர்வான் போலத், தொடை மாண்ட","கண்ணியன், வில்லன், வரும்; என்னை நோக்குபு,","முன்னத்தின் காட்டுதல் அல்லது, தான் உற்ற","நோய் உரைக்கல்லான் பெயரும்மன், பல் நாளும்!","பாயல் பெறேஎன், படர் கூர்ந்து, அவன் வயின்,","சேயேன்மன் யானும் துயர் உழப்பேன்; ஆயிடைக்","கண் நின்று கூறுதல் ஆற்றான், அவன் ஆயின்;","பெண் அன்று, உரைத்தல் நமக்கு ஆயின்; 'இன்னதூஉம்","காணான், கழிதலும் உண்டு' என்று, ஒரு நாள், என்","தோள் நெகிழ்பு உற்ற துயரால் துணிதந்து, ஓர்","நாண் இன்மை செய்தேன், நறு நுதால்! ஏனல்","இனக் கிளி யாம் கடிந்து ஓம்பும் புனத்து அயல்,","ஊசல் ஊர்ந்து ஆட, ஒரு ஞான்று வந்தானை,","'ஐய! சிறிது என்னை ஊக்கி' எனக் கூறத்,","'தையால்! நன்று' என்று அவன் ஊக்கக், கை நெகிழ்பு,","பொய்யாக வீழ்ந்தேன், அவன் மார்பில்; வாய்யாச் செத்து,","ஒய்யென ஆங்கே எடுத்தனன்; கொண்டான் மேல்","மெய் அறியாதேன் போல் கிடந்தேன்மன்; ஆயிடை","மெய் அறிந்து ஏற்று எழுவேன் ஆயின், மற்று ஒய்யென,","'ஒண் குழாய்! செல்க' எனக் கூறி விடும் பண்பின்","அங்கண் உடையன் அவன்!"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend about a young man who kept coming with a garland and a bow, like a hunter tracking a beast, looking at her and signing but never speaking of his sickness; sleepless, and afraid he might pass on without her ever seeing, she dared one shameless act: by the millet field where they scare off the parrots she asked him to push her on the swing, let go, and fell as if by accident onto his chest; he caught her up at once, she lay there as if she had fainted, and the moment she stirred and rose he sent her off with 'go, girl of the bright locks', such is his fine decency.","n":"Lines 8 and 9 give the rule she breaks: he cannot speak to her face, and for a woman to speak is unwomanly, so a staged fall does the work of the word. The comedy has two pretences, her fall and her swoon, and line 17 says he took the fall for real (வாய்யாச் செத்து); his sending her away the instant she stirs leaves open whether he saw through her, and it is that restraint she praises.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","naanam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 37","lb":"KL37","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"],["nakai","இளமை"]],"k":[["மான்","மான்",2,"maa","simile","beast"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",12,"unavu","scene",""],["கிளி","கிளி",13,"pul","scene",""],["ஊசல்","ஊசல்",14,"pira","scene","swing"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:38":{"book":"kalittokai","num":38,"t":["இமைய வில் வாங்கிய ஈர்ஞ் சடை அந்தணன்","உமை அமர்ந்து உயர் மலை இருந்தனன் ஆக,","ஐ இரு தலையின் அரக்கர் கோமான்","தொடிப் பொலி தடக் கையின் கீழ் புகுத்து, அம் மலை","எடுக்கல் செல்லாது உழப்பவன் போல -","உறு புலி உரு ஏய்ப்பப் பூத்த வேங்கையைக்","கறுவு கொண்டு, அதன் முதல் குத்திய மத யானை,","நீடு இரு விடர் அகம் சிலம்பக் கூய்த், தன்","கோடு புய்க்கல்லாது, உழக்கும் நாட! கேள்;","ஆர் இடை என்னாய் நீ அரவு அஞ்சாய் வந்தக்கால்,","நீர் அற்ற புலமே போல் புல்லென்றாள், வைகறை,","கார் பெற்ற புலமே போல், கவின் பெறும்; அக் கவின்","தீராமல் காப்பது ஓர் திறன் உண்டேல் உரைத்தைக் காண்!","இருள் இடை என்னாய் நீ இரவு அஞ்சாய் வந்தக்கால்,","பொருள் இல்லான் இளமை போல் புல்லென்றாள், வைகறை,","அருள் வல்லான் ஆக்கம் போல் அணிபெறும்; அவ் அணி","தெருளாமல் காப்பது ஓர் திறன் உண்டேல், உரைத்தைக் காண்;","மறந்திருந்தார் என்னாய் நீ மலை இடை வந்தக்கால்,","அறம் சாரான் மூப்பே போல் அழிதக்காள், வைகறை,","திறம் சேர்ந்தான் ஆக்கம் போல் திருத்தகும்; அத் திருப்","புறங்கூற்றுத் தீர்ப்பது ஓர் பொருள் உண்டேல் உரைத்தைக் காண்;","என ஆங்கு,","நின் உறு விழுமம் கூறக் கேட்டு","வருமே, தோழி! நல் மலை நாடன் -","வேங்கை விரிவு இடம் நோக்கி,","வீங்கு இறைப் பணைத் தோள் வரைந்தனன் கொளற்கே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl how she spoke to the hill lord, in whose land a rutting elephant, taking a flowering vengai for a tiger, gores its trunk and, unable to pull its tusk free, bellows through the clefts like the ten-headed demon king straining beneath the god's mountain: when he comes by night through the hard path, heedless of snakes and the dark and the hill, she who had drooped like a field without water, like a poor man's youth, like the old age of one who never kept to virtue, grows lovely at dawn like a field after rain, like the wealth of a gracious man and the gain of a capable one; has he a way to keep that beauty from fading and from slander? and now, having heard her grief told, the good hill lord comes, seeing the vengai in flower, to marry her.","n":"The three stanzas use the moral similes of the didactic books (the poor man's youth, the gracious man's wealth) as a scale for a girl's face, and each ends with a challenge: if he can keep this beauty, let him say how; the only way is marriage, and the close says he has understood. The elephant that wounds the vengai in error and is caught by its own tusk may glance at the hero, and the vengai flowering is the wedding season (line 25), but only one correspondence is drawn, so ullurai is not tagged.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","iravu","purangooral","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 38","lb":"KL38","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["அந்தணன்","அந்தணன்",1,"deyvam","simile","Shiva"],["புலி","புலி",6,"maa","simile",""],["வேங்கையைக்","வேங்கை",6,"maram","simile","vengai tree"],["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","scene",""],["அரவு","அரவு",10,"pira","scene","snake"],["கார்","கார்",12,"pira","simile","rain cloud"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",25,"maram","scene","vengai tree"]],"en":[["hill","இமைய",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:39":{"book":"kalittokai","num":39,"t":["காமர் கடும் புனல் கலந்து எம்மோடு ஆடுவாள்,","தாமரைக் கண் புதைத்து, அஞ்சித் தளர்ந்து, அதனோடு ஒழுகலான்","நீள் நாக நறும் தண் தார் தயங்கப் பாய்ந்து, அருளினால்,","பூண் ஆகம் உற தழீஇ போத்தந்தான் அகல் அகலம்,","வரு முலை புணர்ந்தன என்பதனால், என் தோழி","அரு மழை தரல் வேண்டின் தருகிற்கும் பெருமையளே;","அவனும் தான், ஏனல் இதணத்து அகில் புகை உண்டு இயங்கும்","வான் ஊர் மதியம் வரை சேரின், அவ் வரைத்,","'தேனின் இறால்' என, ஏணி இழைத்திருக்கும்","கான் அகல் நாடன் மகன்;","சிறுகுடியீரே! சிறுகுடியீரே! -","வள்ளி கீழ் வீழா; வரை மிசைத் தேன் தொடா;","கொல்லை குரல் வாங்கி ஈனா - மலை வாழ்நர்","அல்ல புரிந்து ஒழுகலான்;","காந்தள் கடி கமழும், கண் வாங்கு, இரும் சிலம்பின்","வாங்கு அமை மென் தோள் குறவர் மட மகளிர்","தாம் பிழையார்; கேள்வர்த் தொழுது எழலால், தம் ஐயரும்","தாம் பிழையார் தாம் தொடுத்த கோல்;","என ஆங்கு,","அறத்தொடு நின்றேனைக் கண்டு திறப்பட","என்னையர்க்கு உய்த்து உரைத்தாள் யாய்;","அவரும், தெரி கணை நோக்கிச் சிலை நோக்கிக், கண் சேந்து","ஒரு பகல் எல்லாம் உருத்து எழுந்து, ஆறி,","'இருவர் கண் குற்றமும் இல்லையால்' என்று,","தெருமந்து சாய்த்தார் தலை;","தெரி இழாய்! நீயும் நின் கேளும் புணர,","வரை உறை தெய்வம் உவப்ப, உவந்து","குரவை தழீஇ யாம் ஆடக் குரவையுள்","கொண்டு நிலை பாடிக் காண்;","நல்லாய் -","நன்னாள் தலை வரும் எல்லை, நமர் மலைத்","தம் நாண் தாம் தாங்குவார், என் நோற்றனர் கொல்?","புன வேங்கைத் தாது உறைக்கும் பொன் அறை முன்றில்","நனவில் புணர்ச்சி நடக்குமாம் அன்றோ?","நனவில் புணர்ச்சி நடக்கலும் ஆங்கே,","கனவில் புணர்ச்சி கடிதுமாம் அன்றோ?","விண் தோய் கல் நாடனும் நீயும் வதுவையுள்","பண்டு அறியாதீர் போல் படர்கிற்பீர்மன் கொல்லோ?","பண்டு அறியாதீர் போல் படர்ந்தீர் பழம் கேண்மை","கண்டு அறியாதேன் போல் கரக்கிற்பென்மன் கொல்லோ?","மை தவழ் வெற்பன் மண அணி காணாமல்,","கையால் புதை பெறூஉம் கண்களும் கண்களோ?","என்னைமன், நின் கண்ணால் காண்பென்மன், யான்;","நெய்தல் இதழ் உண் கண், நின் கண் ஆக, என் கண் மன;","என ஆங்கு,","நெறி அறி செறி குறி புரி திரிபு அறியா அறிவனை முந்துறீஇத்,","தகை மிகு தொகை வகை அறியும் சான்றவர் இனம் ஆக,","வேய் புரை மென் தோள் பசலையும், அம்பலும்,","மாய புணர்ச்சியும், எல்லாம் உடன் நீங்கச்,","சேய் உயர் வெற்பனும் வந்தனன்;","பூ எழில் உண் கணும் பொலிக மா இனியே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl how she told the village that the girl, swept off by a swift flood in which she was bathing, her eyes shut in fear, was saved by a hill chief's son who leapt in, garland swinging, and carried her out held to his chest, so that by that embrace she is one who could bring rain at need; that the hill people keep to the right, so the valli does not fail, the honey is not lost and the millet yields, and because their women rise worshipping their husbands their men's arrows never miss; that the mother told the brothers, who glared a whole day at their bows and arrows, then said neither of the two was at fault and bowed their heads; and she bids the girl dance the kuravai with her lover to please the god of the hill, imagining how at the wedding the two will act as strangers, and how the hill lord has now come with the wise and the elders to end her pallor, the gossip and the stolen meetings.","n":"Lines 5-6 and 17-18 make a claim close to the one the Kural makes of the wife who rises worshipping her husband and commands the rain, but here the power is earned by one embrace before marriage and extended to the hill's whole livelihood (tubers, honey, millet, the hunters' arrows): the friend's disclosure is an argument that the girl has already kept faith. Line 46's chain of rhyming words describes someone who knows the signs and the reckoning, probably the one who fixes the wedding day; the referent is not certain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","karpu","aram","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 39","lb":"KL39","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["புனல்","புனல்",1,"pira","scene",""],["தாமரைக்","தாமரை",2,"maram","simile",""],["நாக","நாகம்",3,"maram","ornament","naagam flower"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",7,"unavu","scene",""],["அகில்","அகில்",7,"maram","scene",""],["இறால்","இறால்",9,"pira","scene","honeycomb"],["வள்ளி","வள்ளி",12,"maram","scene","valli yam"],["தேன்","தேன்",12,"unavu","scene",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",15,"maram","scene",""],["குறவர்","குறவர்",16,"seythi","scene",""],["தெய்வம்","தெய்வம்",27,"deyvam","scene",""],["குரவை","குரவை",28,"yaazh","scene",""],["வேங்கைத்","வேங்கை",33,"maram","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",44,"maram","simile",""],["வேய்","வேய்",48,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:40":{"book":"kalittokai","num":40,"t":["அகவினம் பாடுவாம், தோழி! - அமர்க் கண்","நகை மொழி, நல்லவர் நாணும் நிலை போல்,","தகை கொண்ட ஏனலுள் தாழ் குரல் உரீஇ,","முகை வளர் சாந்து உரல், முத்து ஆர் மருப்பின்","வகை சால் உலக்கை வயின் வயின் ஓச்சிப்,","பகை இல் நோய் செய்தான் பய மலை ஏத்தி,","அகவினம் பாடுவாம், நாம்.","ஆய் நுதல், அணி கூந்தல், அம் பணைத் தட மென் தோள்","தேன் நாறு கதுப்பினாய்! - யானும் ஒன்று ஏத்துகு -","வேய் நரல் விடர் அகம் நீ ஒன்று பாடித்தை;","கொடிச்சியர் கூப்பி வரை தொழு கை போல்,","எடுத்த நறவின் குலை அலங்கு காந்தள்","தொடுத்த தேன் சோரத், தயங்கும் - தன் உற்றார்","இடுக்கண் தவிர்ப்பான் மலை;","கல்லாக் கடுவன் கணம் மலி சுற்றத்து,","மெல் விரல் மந்தி குறை கூறும் செம்மற்றே -","தொல் எழில் தோய்ந்தார் தொலையின், அவரினும்","அல்லல் படுவான் மலை;","புரி விரி, புதை துதை, பூத் ததைந்த தாழ் சினைத்","தளிர் அன்ன எழில் மேனி தகை வாட, நோய் செய்தான்","அரு வரை அடுக்கம் நாம் அழித்து ஒன்று பாடுவாம்;","விண் தோய் வரைப், பந்து எறிந்த அயா வீடத்,","தண் தாழ் அருவி, அரமகளிர், ஆடுபவே -","பெண்டிர் நலம் வௌவித் தண் சாரல் தாது உண்ணும்","வண்டின் துறப்பான் மலை;","ஒடுங்கா எழில் வேழம் வீழ் பிடிக்கு உற்ற","கடுஞ் சூல் வயாவிற்கு அமர்ந்து, நெடுஞ் சினைத்","தீம் கண் கரும்பின் கழை வாங்கும் - 'உற்றாரின்","நீங்கலம்' என்பான் மலை;","என நாம்,","தன் மலை பாட, நயவந்து கேட்டு அருளி,","மெய் மலி உவகையன் புகுதந்தான் - புணர்ந்து ஆரா","மென் முலை ஆகம் கவின் பெறச்","செம்மலை ஆகிய மலை கிழவோனே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend calls the girl to sing a valli song as they pound the millet heads in a sandal mortar with a pearl-set tusk for pestle, praising the hill of the man who gave her a sickness without enmity: the friend sings it as the hill of one who ends his kin's distress, where kanthal clusters like the joined hands of hill women worshipping the peak drip honey, and of one who would grieve more than those he loves if their old beauty faded, where a soft-fingered she-monkey pleads her want amid an untaught male's crowding band; the girl, turning it against him, sings the hill of one who takes a woman's beauty and leaves her like a bee that drinks pollen on the slope, where nymphs bathe in the cold falls after ball-play; and the last stanza praises the hill of one who says he will never leave his own, where a tusker bends down tall sugarcane for his mate's craving in pregnancy; and the friend says he heard their song, came in full of joy, and made the girl's breasts bloom, the lord of the hill.","n":"The song alternates praise and reproach, each stanza setting a hill-thing beside a claim about the man; the reproach is sung so the man will hear it, and the last stanza's tusker answers the girl's bee: the one leaves the flower, the other bends the cane for his mate. Lines 8 to 28 and 31 to 34 are not in the sangamtamil.com text, so this reading rests on one witness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","punarchi","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 40","lb":"KL40","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஏனலுள்","ஏனல்",3,"unavu","scene",""],["சாந்து","சாந்து",4,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["மருப்பின்","மருப்பு",4,"maa","scene","tusk"],["வேய்","வேய்",10,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["கொடிச்சியர்","கொடிச்சி",11,"seythi","simile",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",12,"maram","simile",""],["தேன்","தேன்",13,"unavu","scene",""],["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",15,"maa","scene","male monkey"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",16,"maa","scene","she-monkey"],["தளிர்","தளிர்",20,"maram","simile",""],["அருவி","அருவி",23,"pira","scene",""],["அரமகளிர்","அரமகள்",23,"deyvam","scene",""],["வண்டின்","வண்டு",25,"pira","simile","bee"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",26,"maa","iraicchi",""],["பிடிக்கு","பிடி",26,"maa","scene",""],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",28,"maram","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வேழம்","confirmed","1/1","the man who says he will not leave his own will care for her as the tusker cares for his mate"]],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:41":{"book":"kalittokai","num":41,"t":["பாடுகம், வா - வாழி, தோழி! வயக் களிற்றுக்","கோடு உலக்கை ஆக, நல் சேம்பின் இலை சுளகா","ஆடு கழை நெல்லை அறை உரலுள் பெய்து, இருவாம்","பாடுகம், வா - வாழி, தோழ! நல் தோழி! - பாடுற்று;","இடி உமிழ்பு இரங்கிய விரவு பெயல் நடுநாள்,","கொடி விடுபு இருளிய மின்னுச் செய் விளக்கத்துப்,","பிடியொடு மேயும் புன்செய் யானை","அடி ஒதுங்கு இயக்கம் கேட்ட கானவன்,","நெடு வரை ஆசினிப் பணவை ஏறிக்","கடு விசை கவணையில் கல் கை விடுதலின்,","இறு வரை வேங்கையின் ஒள் வீ சிதறி,","ஆசினி மெல் பழம் அளிந்தவை உதிராத் ,","தேன் செய் இறாஅல் துளைபடப் போகி,","நறு வடி மாவின் பை துணர் உழக்கிக்,","குலை உடை வாழைக் கொழு மடல் கிழியாப்,","பலவின் பழத்துள் தங்கும், மலை கெழு வெற்பனைப்","பாடுகம், வா - வாழி, தோழி! நல் தோழி! - பாடுற்று;","இலங்கும் அருவித்து, இலங்கும் அருவித்தே;","வானின் இலங்கும் அருவித்தே - தான் உற்ற","சூள் பேணான் பொய்த்தான் மலை;","பொய்த்தற்கு உரியனோ? பொய்த்தற்கு உரியனோ?","'அஞ்சல் ஓம்பு' என்றாரைப் பொய்த்தற்கு உரியனோ?","குன்று அகல் நல் நாடன் வாய்மையில் பொய் தோன்றின்","திங்களுள் தீத் தோன்றியற்று;","இள மழை ஆடும்; இள மழை ஆடும்;","இள மழை வைகலும் ஆடும் - என் முன்கை","வளை நெகிழ வாராதோன் குன்று;","வாராது அமைவானோ? வாராது அமைவானோ?","வாராது அமைகுவான் அல்லன்; மலை நாடன்,","ஈரத்துள் இன்னவை தோன்றின், நிழல் கயத்து","நீருள் குவளை வெந்தற்று;","மணி போலத் தோன்றும்; மணி போலத் தோன்றும்;","மண்ணா மணி போலத் தோன்றும் - என் மேனியைத்","துன்னான் துறந்தான் மலை;","துறக்குவன் அல்லன்; துறக்குவன் அல்லன்;","தொடர் வரை வெற்பன் துறக்குவன் அல்லன் -","தொடர்புள் இனையவை தோன்றின், விசும்பில்","சுடருள் இருள் தோன்றியற்று;","என ஆங்கு,","நன்று ஆகின்றால் -தோழி! நம் வள்ளையுள்","ஒன்றி நாம் பாட, மறை நின்று கேட்டு அருளி,","மென் தோள் கிழவனும் வந்தனன்; நுந்தையும்","மன்றல் வேங்கைக் கீழ் இருந்து,","மணம் நயந்தனன், அம் மலை கிழவோற்கே."],"p":"","r":"The friend calls the girl to pound bamboo rice in a rock mortar, a tusk for pestle and a chembu leaf for winnow, and sing of the hill lord in whose land, at a thundering midnight lit by lightning, a hunter hears an elephant feeding with its mate, climbs his platform and slings a stone that scatters vengai flowers, brings down ripe aasini fruit, pierces a honeycomb, tears through mango clusters and a plantain leaf and lodges in a jackfruit; they sing his hill as the hill of one who broke his oath, then deny he could lie, for a falsehood in his truth would be fire in the moon; as the hill of one who has not come though her bangles slip, then deny he could stay away, for that in a kind man would be a kuvalai scorched in a shaded pool; as the hill of one who has left her, then deny he would, for that in a bond would be darkness in the sun; and she ends that it went well: the man, listening hidden, came, and her father, sitting under the wedding vengai, consented to the match.","n":"The stone's flight (lines 10 to 16) runs through five trees and a honeycomb in one sentence before it comes to rest, one throw disturbing the whole hill; nothing in the poem matches it to the lovers, so it is scene. Each refrain states the reproach and then takes it back with an impossible image, so the girls accuse and acquit him in the same breath for the hidden listener.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","vaaimai","sool","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 41","lb":"KL41","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["களிற்றுக்","களிறு",1,"maa","scene",""],["சேம்பின்","சேம்பு",2,"maram","scene",""],["நெல்லை","நெல்",3,"unavu","scene","bamboo rice"],["பிடியொடு","பிடி",7,"maa","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","scene",""],["கானவன்","கானவன்",8,"seythi","scene",""],["ஆசினிப்","ஆசினி",9,"maram","scene",""],["வேங்கையின்","வேங்கை",11,"maram","scene",""],["இறாஅல்","இறால்",13,"pira","scene","honeycomb"],["மாவின்","மா",14,"maram","scene","mango"],["வாழைக்","வாழை",15,"maram","scene",""],["பலவின்","பலா",16,"maram","scene",""],["அருவித்து","அருவி",18,"pira","scene",""],["குவளை","குவளை",31,"maram","simile",""],["மணி","மணி",33,"pira","simile","gem"],["வேங்கைக்","வேங்கை",43,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:42":{"book":"kalittokai","num":42,"t":["மறம் கொள் இரும் புலித் தொல் முரண் தொலைத்த","முறம் செவி வாரணம் முன் குளகு அருந்திக்","கறங்கு வெள் அருவி ஓலின் துஞ்சும்","பிறங்கு இரும் சோலை நல் மலை நாடன்","மறந்தான்; மறக்க; இனி, எல்லா! நமக்குச்","சிறந்தமை நாம் நற்கு அறிந்தனம்; ஆயின், அவன் திறம்","கொல் யானைக் கோட்டால் வெதிர் நெல் குறுவாம் நாம்.","வள்ளை அகவுவம் வா, இகுளை! நாம்","வள்ளை அகவுவம், வா;","காணிய வா - வாழி தோழி! - வரைத் தாழ்பு -","வாள் நிறம் கொண்ட அருவித்தே, நம் அருளா","நாண் இலி நாட்டு மலை;","ஆர்வுற்றார் நெஞ்சம் அழிய விடுவானோ -","ஓர்வு உற்று ஒரு திறம் ஒல்காத நேர்கோல்","அறம் புரி நெஞ்சத்தவன்?","தண் நறும் கோங்கம் மலர்ந்த வரை எல்லாம்","பொன் அணி யானை போல் தோன்றுமே - நம் அருளாக்","கொன்னாளன் நாட்டு மலை;","கூரு நோய் ஏய்ப்ப விடுவானோ? - தன் மலை","நீரினும் சாயல் உடையன், நயந்தோர்க்குத்","தேர் ஈயும் வண் கையவன்;","வரை மிசை மேல் தொடுத்த நெய் கண் இறாஅல்","மழை நுழை திங்கள் போல் தோன்றும் - இழை நெகிழ","எவ்வம் உறீஇயினான் குன்று;","எஞ்சாது, எல்லா! கொடுமை நுவலாதி -","அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சா அறன் இலி அல்லன், என்","நெஞ்சம் பிணிக்கொண்டவன்!","என்று யாம் பாட, மறை நின்று கேட்டனன்;","தாழ் இரும் கூந்தல் என் தோழியைக் கை கவியாச்,","சாயல் இன் மார்பன் சிறுபுறம் சார்தர,","ஞாயிற்று முன்னர் இருள் போல மாய்ந்தது என்","ஆய் இழை மேனிப் பசப்பு."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the lord of the good hill, where a fan-eared elephant that ended its old feud with a tiger eats leaves and sleeps to the roar of the white falls, has forgotten them, so let him forget, they know well what he was to them; let them pound bamboo rice with a killer elephant's tusk and sing: his hill's falls gleam like swords, yet he is shameless; would a man of straight, unbending, right-loving heart abandon those who long for him? the kongu flowers make his hill look like elephants decked in gold, yet he is a waster; would one gentler than his hill's water, who gives chariots to those who seek him, leave her to pining? the honeycomb on his peak shows like the moon slipping into cloud, yet he has hurt her; then she stops herself: do not speak of his cruelty, he is no lawless man who fears nothing that should be feared; he heard them from hiding, drew the girl by the hand to his chest, and her pallor vanished like darkness before the sun.","n":"The defence is in the vocabulary of kingship and law (நேர்கோல், அறம் புரி நெஞ்சத்தவன், அறன் இலி), so the girls acquit the lover by the standard of a just ruler; line 26 recalls the maxim that the wise fear what ought to be feared. Lines 11 to 22 and 25 to 32 are not in the sangamtamil.com text, and line 31 recurs in poem 145.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","aram","pochchaappu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 42","lb":"KL42","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["புலித்","புலி",1,"maa","scene",""],["வாரணம்","வாரணம்",2,"maa","scene","elephant"],["குளகு","குளகு",2,"unavu","scene","leaf fodder"],["அருவி","அருவி",3,"pira","scene",""],["யானைக்","யானை",7,"maa","scene",""],["நெல்","நெல்",7,"unavu","scene","bamboo rice"],["அருவித்தே","அருவி",11,"pira","simile",""],["கோங்கம்","கோங்கு",16,"maram","simile",""],["யானை","யானை",17,"maa","simile",""],["இறாஅல்","இறால்",22,"pira","simile","honeycomb"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:43":{"book":"kalittokai","num":43,"t":["வேங்கை தொலைத்த வெறி பொறி வாரணத்து","ஏந்து மருப்பின், இன வண்டு இமிர்பு ஊதும்","சாந்த மரத்தின், இயன்ற உலக்கையால்,","ஐவன வெண் நெல் அறை உரலுள் பெய்து, இருவாம்,","ஐயனை ஏத்துவாம் போல, அணிபெற்ற","மை படு சென்னிப் பய மலை நாடனைத்","தையலாய்! பாடுவாம் நாம்;","தகையவர் கைச் செறித்த தாள் போலக் காந்தள்","முகையின் மேல் தும்பி இருக்கும் - பகை எனின்,","கூற்றம் வரினும் தொலையான், தன் நட்டார்க்குத்","தோற்றலை நாணாதோன் குன்று;","வெருள்பு உடன் நோக்கி, வியல் அறை யூகம்,","இருள் தூங்கு இறு வரை ஊர்பு இழிபு ஆடும்","வருடைமான் குழவிய வள மலை நாடனைத்","தெருளத் தெரி இழாய்! - நீ ஒன்று பாடித்தை;","நுண் பொறி மான் செவி போல, வெதிர் முளைக்","கண் பொதி பாளை கழன்று உகும் பண்பிற்றே -","மாறு கொண்டு ஆற்றார் எனினும், பிறர் குற்றம்","கூறுதல் தேற்றாதோன் குன்று;","புணர் நிலை வளகின் குளகு அமர்ந்து உண்ட","புணர் மருப்பு எழில் கொண்ட வரை புரை செலவின்","வயங்கு எழில் யானைப் பய மலை நாடனை -","மணம் நாறு கதுப்பினாய்! - மறுத்து ஒன்று பாடித்தை;","கடும் கண் உழுவை அடி போல வாழைக்","கொடும் காய் குலை தொறூஉம் தூங்கும் - இடும்பையால்","இன்மை உரைத்தார்க்கு, அது நிறைக்கல் ஆற்றாக்கால்,","தன் மெய் துறப்பான் மலை;","என ஆங்கு,","கூடி அவர் திறம் பாட, என் தோழிக்கு","வாடிய மென் தோளும் வீங்கின -","ஆடு அமை வெற்பன் அளித்தக்கால் போன்றே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend calls the girl to pound white hill paddy in a rock mortar with a pestle of the tusk of an elephant that killed a tiger and of sandalwood, and to sing the man of the rich hill as one praises a lord: she sings his hill, where a dragonfly sits on kanthal buds like rings on the fingers of noble women, as the hill of one who will not give way to an enemy though death come and is not ashamed to lose to his friends; the girl sings it, where bamboo sheaths fall like the ears of a spotted deer, as the hill of one who never speaks of others' faults though they wrong him; and again, where plantain bunches hang like a tiger's paw, as the hill of one who, if he could not fill the want of one who told him of poverty, would give up his own body; and as they sang his worth together the girl's wasted shoulders swelled, as if he had come and been gracious.","n":"The three virtues sung are puram virtues (steadfastness in enmity, restraint of speech, giving to the point of death), so the akam lover is praised as a hero; the cure is worked by the praise itself before he comes (போன்றே, as if). The hill-things and the virtues are only set side by side, with no part matched to part, so neither ullurai nor iraicchi is tagged.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","purangooral","natpu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 43","lb":"KL43","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["வாரணத்து","வாரணம்",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bee"],["சாந்த","சாந்தம்",3,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["நெல்","நெல்",4,"unavu","scene",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",8,"maram","simile",""],["தும்பி","தும்பி",9,"pira","simile",""],["யூகம்","யூகம்",12,"maa","scene","black monkey"],["வருடைமான்","வருடை மான்",14,"maa","scene","mountain goat"],["மான்","மான்",16,"maa","simile","deer"],["வெதிர்","வெதிர்",16,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["யானைப்","யானை",22,"maa","scene",""],["உழுவை","உழுவை",24,"maa","simile","tiger"],["வாழைக்","வாழை",24,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:44":{"book":"kalittokai","num":44,"t":["கதிர் விரி கனை சுடர்க் கவின் கொண்ட நனம் சாரல்","எதிர் எதிர் ஓங்கிய மால் வரை அடுக்கத்து,","அதிர் இசை அருவி தன் அம் சினை மிசை வீழ,","முதிர் இணர் ஊழ் கொண்ட முழவுத் தாள் எரி வேங்கை,","வரி நுதல் எழில் வேழம் பூ நீர் மேல் சொரிதரப்,","புரி நெகிழ் தாமரை மலர் அம் கண் வீறு எய்தித்","திரு நயந்து இருந்தன்ன தேம் கமழ் விறல் வெற்ப!","தன் எவ்வம் கூரினும், நீ செய்த அருள் இன்மை","என்னையும் மறைத்தாள், என் தோழி - அது கேட்டு","நின்னை யான் பிறர் முன்னர்ப் பழி கூறல் தான் நாணி;","கூரும் நோய் சிறப்புவும், நீ செய்த அருள் இன்மை","சேரியும் மறைத்தாள், என் தோழி -அது கேட்டாங்கு,","'ஓரும் நீ நிலையலை' எனக் கூறல் தான் நாணி;","நோய் அட வருந்தியும், நீ செய்த அருள் இன்மை","ஆயமும் மறைத்தாள், என் தோழி - அது கேட்டு","மாய நின் பண்பு இன்மை பிறர் கூறல் தான் நாணி;","என ஆங்கு,","இனையன தீமை நினைவனள் காத்தாங்கு,","அனை அரு பண்பினான், நின் தீமை காத்தவள்","அரும் துயர் ஆர் அஞர் தீர்க்கும்","மருந்து ஆகி செல்கம், பெரும! நாம் விரைந்தே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the fragrant hill, where a loud waterfall shakes an old vengai so that its ripe flowers fall on a handsome elephant and then on a lotus, which glows as if the goddess of fortune sat there in delight, that though her own grief grew the girl hid his ungraciousness even from the friend, ashamed that the friend would blame him before others; hid it from the street, ashamed they would say he does not keep his word; hid it from her companions, ashamed they would speak of his false want of character; and since, with a nature rare even in a mother, she guarded him from blame, let them hurry to her, lord, as the medicine for her grief.","n":"The friend tells the man the fault the girl hid by praising her for hiding it, so the praise is the reproach, and he learns of it from the very person it was hidden from. The opening lotus under falling vengai flowers is the image of fortune at rest, set against a girl who has none.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karavu","pazhi","naanam","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 44","lb":"KL44","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",3,"pira","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",4,"maram","scene",""],["வேழம்","வேழம்",5,"maa","scene",""],["தாமரை","தாமரை",6,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:45":{"book":"kalittokai","num":45,"t":["விடியல் வெம் கதிர் காயும் வேய் அமல் அகல் அறைக்,","கடி சுனைக் கவினிய காந்தள் அம் குலையினை,","அரு மணி அவிர் உத்தி அரவு நீர் உணல் செத்துப்,","பெரு மலை மிளிர்ப்பன்ன காற்றுடைக் கனைப் பெயல்","உருமுக் கண்ணுறுதலின், உயர் குரல் ஒலி ஓடி,","நறு வீய நனம் சாரல் சிலம்பலின், கதுமெனச்,","சிறுகுடி துயில் எழூஉம் சேண் உயர் விறல் வெற்ப!","கால் பொர நுடங்கல கறங்கு இசை அருவி நின்","மால் வரை மலி சுனை மலர் ஏய்க்கும் என்பதோ -","புல் ஆராப் புணர்ச்சியால் புலம்பிய என் தோழி","பல் இதழ் மலர் உண் கண் பசப்ப! நீ சிதைத்ததை?","புகர் முகக் களிறொடு புலி பொருது உழக்கும் நின்","அகல் மலை அடுக்கத்த அமை ஏய்க்கும் என்பதோ -","கடை எனக் கலுழும் நோய் கைம்மிக, என் தோழி","தடையின திரண்ட தோள் தகை வாடச் சிதைத்ததை?","சுடர் உற உற நீண்ட சுரும்பு இமிர் அடுக்கத்த","விடர் வரை எரி வேங்கை இணர் ஏய்க்கும் என்பதோ -","யாமத்தும் துயிலலள் அலமரும் என் தோழி","காமரு நல் எழில் கவின் வாடச் சிதைத்ததை?","என ஆங்கு,","தன் தீமை பல கூறிக் கழறலின், என் தோழி","மறையில் தான் மருவுற மணந்த நட்பு அருகலான்","பிறை புரை நுதல்! அவர்ப் பேணி நம்","உறை வரைந்தனர், அவர் உவக்கும் நாளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl how she asked the lord of the high hill, where at dawn the hot sun lights a kanthal cluster by a spring, thunder in a mountain-shaking storm strikes it, taking it for a jewel-hooded snake drinking, and the small village wakes to the echo, whether he ruined the girl's flower eyes to pallor because they resembled the flowers of his hill's springs, wasted her shoulders because they resembled the bamboo of his slopes where tusker and tiger fight, withered her beauty because it resembled the blazing vengai of his clefts; and that since she reproached him with his many faults and their secret meetings grew rare, he has fixed their wedding for a day that pleases him.","n":"The three questions run the stock comparisons of beauty backwards: the likeness of her eyes, shoulders and beauty to the things of his hill becomes the mock reason he destroyed them, as if he treated her as part of his land. The thunder that strikes a flower mistaken for a snake is another scene of harm by mistaken identity, as in poems 38 and 49, but no pairs are anchored, so it is scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","pasalai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 45","lb":"KL45","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வேய்","வேய்",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",2,"maram","simile",""],["அரவு","அரவு",3,"pira","simile","snake"],["அருவி","அருவி",8,"pira","scene",""],["மலர்","மலர்",9,"maram","simile",""],["களிறொடு","களிறு",12,"maa","scene",""],["புலி","புலி",12,"maa","scene",""],["அமை","அமை",13,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",16,"pira","scene","bee"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",17,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:46":{"book":"kalittokai","num":46,"t":["வீ அகம் புலம்ப, வேட்டம் போகிய","மாஅல் அம் சிறை மணி நிறத் தும்பி,","வாய் இழி கடாத்த, வால் மருப்பு ஒருத்தலோடு","ஆய் பொறி உழுவை தாக்கிய பொழுதின்,","'வேங்கை அம் சினை' என விறல் புலி முற்றியும்","பூம் பொறி யானைப் புகர் முகம் குறுகியும்,","வலி மிகு வெகுளியான் வாள் உற்ற மன்னரை","நயன் நாடி நட்பு ஆக்கும் வினைவர் போல், மறிதரும்","அயம் இழி அருவிய அணி மலை நல் நாட!","ஏறு இரங்கு இருள் இடை இரவினில் பதம் பெறாஅன்,","மாறினென் எனக் கூறி மனம் கொள்ளும், தான் என்ப -","கூடுதல் வேட்கையான், குறி பார்த்துக் குரல் நொச்சிப்","பாடு ஓர்க்கும் செவியோடு பைதலேன் யான் ஆக;","அரும் செலவு ஆர் இடை அருளி வந்து, அளி பெறாஅன்","வருந்தினென் என பல வாய்விடூஉம், தான் என்ப -","நிலை உயர் கடவுட்குக் கடம் பூண்டு தன் மாட்டுப்","பல சூழும் மனத்தோடு பைதலேன் யான் ஆக;","கனை பெயல் நடுநாள் யான் கண் மாறக், குறி பெறாஅன்,","புனை இழாய்! என் பழி நினக்கு உரைக்கும், தான் என்ப -","துளி நசை வேட்கையான் மிசை பாடும் புள்ளின், தன்","அளி நசை ஆர்வுற்ற அன்பினேன் யான் ஆக;","என ஆங்கு,","கலந்த நோய் கைம்மிகக் கண் படா என் வயின்","புலந்தாயும் நீ ஆயின், பொய்யானே வெல்குவை -","இலங்கு தாழ் அருவியோடு அணி கொண்ட நின் மலைச்","சிலம்பு போல், கூறுவ கூறும்","இலங்கு ஏர் எல் வளை, இவள் உடை நோயே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the fine hill, where a dark-winged bee that left its flowers to hunt flits between a tusker and a tiger in fight, settling on the tiger as on a vengai bough and on the elephant's spotted face, like envoys who make friends of angry kings, that the girl hears he complains: that on a thundering night he got no chance and turned back, while she, longing to meet, lay pining at the trysting place with her ears strained to the nochchi; that he crossed the hard way and found no grace, while she, under a vow to the high god, pined with many cares; that on a stormy midnight he found no signal because she slept, and will blame her to her friend, while she longed for his grace like the bird that sings up to the sky for raindrops; and the friend says that if he too turns angry with her, sleepless in her sickness, he wins only by falsehood, for her bright bangles, like the echo of his hill, tell all of her sickness.","n":"The speaker shifts: lines 10 to 21 are in the girl's first person and address a woman (புனை இழாய்), while lines 1 to 9 and 23 to 27 address the man and speak of the girl as இவள்; the reading takes the friend as voicing the girl's words, which is not certain. The bee that blunders between two fighters like a peacemaking envoy is close to the friend's own position between quarrelling lovers, but the marker joins it to envoys, not to the lovers, so ullurai condition 2 fails; a near miss.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu","pazhi","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 46","lb":"KL46","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["வீ","வீ",1,"maram","scene","flower"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",2,"pira","simile","bee"],["ஒருத்தலோடு","ஒருத்தல்",3,"maa","scene","tusker"],["உழுவை","உழுவை",4,"maa","scene","tiger"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",5,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["புலி","புலி",5,"maa","scene",""],["யானைப்","யானை",6,"maa","scene",""],["அருவிய","அருவி",9,"pira","scene",""],["நொச்சிப்","நொச்சி",12,"maram","scene",""],["கடவுட்கு","கடவுள்",16,"deyvam","scene",""],["புள்ளின்","புள்",20,"pul","simile","rain bird"],["அருவியோடு","அருவி",25,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:47":{"book":"kalittokai","num":47,"t":["ஒன்று இரப்பான் போல் எளிவந்தும் சொல்லும், உலகம்","புரப்பான் போல்வது ஓர் மதுகையும் உடையன்;","வல்லாரை வழிபட்டு ஒன்று அறிந்தான் போல்,","நல்லார் கண் தோன்றும் அடக்கமும் உடையன்;","இல்லோர் புன்கண், ஈகையின் தணிக்க","வல்லான் போல்வது ஓர் வன்மையும் உடையன்;","அன்னான் ஒருவன் தன் ஆண்தகை விட்டு, என்னைச்","சொல்லும் சொல், கேட்டீ - சுடர் இழாய்! பல் மாணும்;","'நின் இன்றி அமையலேன், யான்' என்னும் அவன் ஆயின்,","அன்னான் சொல் நம்புண்டல் யார்க்கும் இங்கு அரிது ஆயின்,","என் உற்ற பிறர்க்கும் ஆங்கு உள கொல்லோ? - நறு நுதால்!","'அறியாய் நீ, வருந்துவல் யான்' என்னும் அவன் ஆயின்,","தமியரே துணிகிற்றல் பெண்டிர்க்கும் அரிது ஆயின்,","அளியரோ, எம் போல ஈங்கு இவன் வலைப்பட்டார்?","'வாழலேன், யான்' என்னும் 'நீ நீப்பின்' அவன் ஆயின்,","ஏழையர் எனப் பலர் கூறும் சொல் பழி ஆயின்,","சூழும்கால், நினைப்பது ஒன்று அறிகலேன், வருந்துவல்;","சூழும்கால், நறு நுதால்! நம் உளே சூழ்குவம்.","அவனை,","நாண் அடப் பெயர்த்தல் நமக்கும் ஆங்கு ஒல்லாது;","'பேணினர்' எனப்படுதல் பெண்மையும் அன்று; அவன்","'வௌவினன் முயங்கும் மாத்திரம் வா' எனக்","கூறுவென் போலக் காட்டி","மற்று அவன் மேஎவழி மேவாய் நெஞ்சே!"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend of a man who speaks humbly like one begging yet has the strength of one who could guard the world, the restraint of one who has learned from the wise, and the power of one who can ease the poor by giving: such a man, putting off his manliness, tells her that he cannot live without her, that she does not know how he suffers, that he will not live if she leaves him; yet his words are hard to believe, a woman can hardly decide alone, and to be called the one who gave way is a reproach; they must think it out together; she will make as if to say 'come, only for an embrace', and she tells her heart not to go the way he wants.","n":"The four portraits at the start are all built with போல், so his virtues are given as appearances, which suits a speaker deciding whether to trust him. Lines 9 to 17 set three of his sayings against three reasons for doubt in a run of conditionals (ஆயின்), and the poem ends not with a decision but with a feint: she will seem to consent and keep her heart back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","naanam","aanmai","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 47","lb":"KL47","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:48":{"book":"kalittokai","num":48,"t":["ஆம் இழி அணி மலை அலர் வேங்கைத் தகை போலத்,","தே மூசு, நனை கவுள், திசை காவல் கொளற்கு ஒத்த","வாய் நில்லா வலி முன்பின், வண்டு ஊது புகர் முகம்","படு மழை அடுக்கத்த, மா விசும்பு ஓங்கிய","கடி மரத் துருத்திய, கமழ் கடாம் திகழ்தரும்","பெரு களிற்று இனத்தொடு, வீங்கு எருத்து எறுழ் முன்பின்","இரும் புலி மயக்குற்ற இகல் மலை நல் நாட!","வீழ் பெயல் கங்குலின் விளி ஓர்த்த ஒடுக்கத்தால்,","வாழும் நாள் சிறந்தவள் வருந்து தோள் தவறு உண்டோ -","தாழ் செறி கடும் காப்பின் தாய் முன்னர், நின் சாரல்","ஊழுறு கோடல் போல், எல் வளை உகுபவால்?","இனை இருள் இது என ஏங்கி, நின் வரல் நசைஇ,","நினை துயர் உழப்பவள் பாடு இல் கண் பழி உண்டோ -","'இனையள்' என்று எடுத்து அரற்றும் அயல் முன்னர், நின் சுனைக்","கனை பெயல் நீலம் போல், கண் பனி கலுழ்பவால்?","பல் நாளும் படர் அடப், பசலையால் உணப்பட்டாள்","பொன் உரை மணி அன்ன, மாமைக் கண் பழி உண்டோ -","இன் நுரைச் செதும்பு அரற்றும் செவ்வியுள், நின் சோலை -","மின் உகு தளிர் அன்ன, மெலிவு வந்து உரைப்பதால்?","என ஆங்கு,","பின் ஈதல் வேண்டும், நீ பிரிந்தோள் நட்பு - என நீவிப்","பூங் கண் படுதலும் அஞ்சுவல்; தாங்கிய","அரும் துயர் அவலம் தூக்கின்,","மருங்கு அறிவாரா மலையினும் பெரிதே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the hill, where a great tiger, its neck swollen with strength, is confused among a herd of tuskers whose spotted, bee-hummed, ichor-wet faces look like flowering vengai, asking whether the girl's shoulders are at fault that, listening for him through the rainy nights, her bangles slip before her watchful mother like the ripe kodal flowers of his slope; whether her eyes are at fault that, sleepless in the dark and longing for him, they weep before the clamouring neighbours like the rain-beaten blue lily of his spring; whether her skin, like a gem tried on gold, is at fault that its pallor tells her wasting like the trembling shoots of his grove; she fears even to soothe the girl to sleep with the hope that he will return the love he left, for the grief she bears, weighed, is greater than the hill whose bounds no one knows.","n":"Each question clears a part of the girl (shoulders, eyes, skin) of a fault and so lays it on him, and each likens the sign of her grief to a thing of his own land, as if his country testified against him. Lines 21 and 22 are hard to construe: the reading takes பின் ஈதல் வேண்டும் ... நட்பு as the hope the friend fears to offer, which is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","alar","melivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 48","lb":"KL48","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வேங்கைத்","வேங்கை",1,"maram","simile",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["களிற்று","களிறு",6,"maa","scene",""],["புலி","புலி",7,"maa","scene",""],["கோடல்","கோடல்",11,"maram","simile",""],["நீலம்","நீலம்",15,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["மணி","மணி",17,"pira","simile","gem"],["தளிர்","தளிர்",19,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:49":{"book":"kalittokai","num":49,"t":["கொடுவரி தாக்கி வென்ற வருத்தமொடு","நெடு வரை மருங்கின் துஞ்சும் யானை,","நனவில் தான் செய்தது மனத்தது ஆகலின்,","கனவில் கண்டு, கதுமென வெரீஇப்","புதுவது ஆக மலர்ந்த வேங்கையை","'அது' என உணர்ந்து, அதன் அணி நலம் முருக்கிப்","பேணா முன்பின் தன் சினம் தணிந்து, அம் மரம்","காணும் பொழுதின் நோக்கல் செல்லாது,","நாணி இறைஞ்சும் நல் மலை நல் நாட!","போது எழில் மலர் உண் கண் இவள் மாட்டு நீ இன்ன","காதலை என்பதோ இனிது - மற்று இன்னாதே,","மின் ஓரும் கண் ஆக, இடி என்னாய், பெயல் என்னாய்,","இன்னது ஓர் ஆர் இடை, ஈங்கு நீ வருவதை;","இன்புற அளித்தனை இவள் மாட்டு நீ இன்ன","அன்பினை என்பதோ இனிது - மற்று இன்னாதே,","மணம் கமழ் மார்பினை, மஞ்சு இவர் அடுக்கம் போழ்ந்து,","அணங்கு உடை ஆர் இடை, ஈங்கு நீ வருவதை;","இருள் உறழ் இரும் கூந்தல் இவள் மாட்டு நீ இன்ன","அருளினை என்பதோ இனிது - மற்று இன்னாதே,","ஒளிறு வேல் வலன் ஏந்தி, 'ஒருவன் யான்' என்னாது,","களிறு இயங்கு ஆர் இடை, ஈங்கு நீ வருவதை;","அதனால்,","இரவின் வாரல்; ஐய! விரவு வீ","அகல் அறை வரிக்கும் சாரல்,","பகலும் பெறுவை, இவள் தட மென் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the good hill, where an elephant sleeping on the slope, weary from beating off a tiger, sees the fight again in a dream, starts up in fright, takes a newly flowered vengai for the tiger and wrecks its beauty, then, its rage spent, cannot bear to look at the tree and bows its head in shame, that his love for the girl is sweet but his coming is bitter: through a hard path in lightning, thunder and rain; through cloud-wrapped slopes haunted by spirits; spear in hand, not counting that he is alone, through paths where elephants roam; so let him not come by night, and by day he will have her broad soft shoulders, on the slope where mingled flowers stripe the wide rock.","n":"The elephant that wrecks the vengai in error and then cannot look at it may glance at the lover, whose night visits endanger the girl he loves; but only one correspondence is drawn (harm in error, then shame), so by condition 3 it is scene, a near miss for ullurai. The refrain separates the man's love, which is sweet, from his coming, which is bitter, so that refusing the night tryst does not refuse him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kuri","acham","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 49","lb":"KL49","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["கொடுவரி","கொடுவரி",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","scene",""],["வேங்கையை","வேங்கை",5,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["மலர்","மலர்",10,"maram","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",21,"maa","scene",""],["வீ","வீ",23,"maram","scene","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:50":{"book":"kalittokai","num":50,"t":["வாங்கு கோல் நெல்லொடு வாங்கி, வரு வைகல்,","மூங்கில் மிசைந்த முழந்தாள் இரும் பிடி,","தூங்கு இலை வாழை நளி புக்கு, ஞாங்கர்","வருடை மட மறி ஊர்வு இடைத் துஞ்சும்","இருள் தூங்கு சோலை, இலங்கு நீர் வெற்ப!","அரவின் பொறியும் அணங்கும் புணர்ந்த","உரவு வில் மேல் அசைத்த கையை, ஓராங்கு","நிரை வளை முன்கை என் தோழியை நோக்கிப்,","படி கிளி பாயும் பசும் குரல் ஏனல்","கடிதல் மறப்பித்தாய் ஆயின், இனி நீ","நெடிது உள்ளல் ஓம்புதல் வேண்டும்; இவளே","பல் கோள் பலவின் பயிர்ப்பு உறு தீம் கனி","அல்கு அறைக் கொண்டு ஊண் அமலைச் சிறுகுடி","நல்கூர்ந்தார் செல்வ மகள்.","நீயே, வளியின் இகல் மிகும் தேரும், களிறும்","தளியின் சிறந்தனை - வந்த புலவர்க்கு","அளியொடு கைதூவலை;","அதனால்,","கடு மா கடவுறூஉம் கோல் போல், எனைத்தும்","கொடுமை இலை ஆவது அறிந்தும், அடுப்பல் -","வழை வளர் சாரல் வருடை நல் மான்","குழவி வளர்ப்பவர் போலப் பாராட்டி,","உழையின் பிரியின், பிரியும்,","இழை அணி அல்குல் என் தோழியது கவினே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shining hill streams, where a great she-elephant that has eaten bamboo and curved paddy stalks enters a plantain thicket and a young mountain goat sleeps where she passed, that since, with his hand resting on his strong bow, he looked at her friend until she forgot to drive the parrots from the millet, he must not now put it off long: the girl is the dear daughter of poor folk in a small village that eats jackfruit ripened on the rock, while he surpasses the rain in giving wind-swift chariots and elephants to the poets who come; and though she knows he has no cruelty, like a whip that only touches a swift horse, she urges him: her friend's beauty, cherished as people rear a mountain goat's kid, leaves her when he leaves her side.","n":"Lines 12 to 17 set the girl's poor family against the man's wealth and his fame for giving to poets; the friend praises the patron's giving to shame him into giving the girl her due, so a puram virtue is turned into an akam obligation. The whip simile excuses the urging itself: even a good horse is touched.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","eegai","inmai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 50","lb":"KL50","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெல்லொடு","நெல்",1,"unavu","scene",""],["மூங்கில்","மூங்கில்",2,"maram","scene",""],["பிடி","பிடி",2,"maa","scene",""],["வாழை","வாழை",3,"maram","scene",""],["வருடை","வருடை",4,"maa","scene","mountain goat"],["கிளி","கிளி",9,"pul","scene",""],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",9,"unavu","scene",""],["பலவின்","பலா",12,"maram","gift_food",""],["களிறும்","களிறு",15,"maa","gift_food",""],["மா","மா",19,"maa","simile","horse"],["வருடை","வருடை",21,"maa","simile","mountain goat"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:51":{"book":"kalittokai","num":51,"t":["சுடர் தொடீஇ! கேளாய் - தெருவில் நாம் ஆடும்","மணல் சிற்றில் காலின் சிதையா, அடைச்சிய","கோதை பரிந்து, வரிப் பந்து கொண்டு ஓடி ,","நோதக்க செய்யும் சிறு பட்டி, மேல் ஓர் நாள்,","அன்னையும் யானும் இருந்தேமா, 'இல்லிரே!","உண்ணு நீர் வேட்டேன்' என வந்தாற்கு, அன்னை","'அடர் பொன் சிரகத்தால் வாக்கிச், சுடர் இழாய்!","உண்ணு நீர் ஊட்டி வா' என்றாள்; என, யானும்","தன்னை அறியாது சென்றேன்; மற்று, என்னை","வளை முன்கை பற்றி நலியத், தெருமந்திட்டு,","'அன்னாய்! இவன் ஒருவன் செய்தது காண்!' என்றேனா,","அன்னை அலறிப் படர்தர, தன்னை யான்,","'உண்ணு நீர் விக்கினான்' என்றேனா, அன்னையும்","தன்னைப் புறம்பு அழித்து நீவ, மற்று என்னைக்","கடைக் கண்ணால் கொல்வான் போல் நோக்கி, நகை கூட்டம்","செய்தான், அக் கள்வன் மகன்!"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend of the naughty boy who used to kick down the sand houses they built in the street, pull the garlands from their hair and run off with their striped ball: one day, while she and her mother sat at home, he came asking for drinking water; her mother told her to pour it from the golden vessel and give it to him; she went, not knowing who it was, and he seized her bangled wrist and squeezed it; in panic she cried 'mother, see what this one did', and as her mother came running she said 'he choked on the water', and her mother stroked his back, while he, looking at her from the corner of his eye as if to kill her, smiled at her, that thief's son.","n":"The turn is her quick lie: having cried out, she covers for him, and the mother's stroking of his back makes the mother an unknowing party to the secret; his side-glance and smile seal a complicity the girl has chosen. கள்வன் மகன் is affectionate abuse: the thief is the one who has stolen her heart, and the old playmate who broke their sand houses has returned as a lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kurippu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 51","lb":"KL51","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","இளமை"],["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:3"},"kl:52":{"book":"kalittokai","num":52,"t":["முறம் செவி மறைப் பாய்பு முரண் செய்த புலி செத்து,","மறம் தலைக்கொண்ட நூற்றுவர் தலைவனைக்","குறங்கு அறுத்திடுவான் போல், கூர் நுதி மடுத்து, அதன்","நிறம் சாடி முரண் தீர்ந்த நீள் மருப்பு எழில் யானை,","மல்லரை மறம் சாய்த்த மால் போல், தன் கிளை நாப்பண்,","கல் உயர் நனம் சாரல், கலந்து இயலும் நாட! கேள்;","தாமரைக் கண்ணியைத், தண் நறும் சாந்தினை,","நேர் இதழ்க் கோதையாள் செய் குறி நீ வரின்,","'மணம் கமழ் நாற்றத்த மலை நின்று பலி பெறூஉம்","அணங்கு' என அஞ்சுவர், சிறுகுடியோரே;","ஈர்ந் தண் ஆடையை, எல்லி மாலையை,","சோர்ந்து வீழ் கதுப்பினாள் செய் குறி நீ வரின்,","ஒளி திகழ் ஞெகிழியர், கவணையர், வில்லர்,","'களிறு' என ஆர்ப்பவர், ஏனல் காவலரே;","ஆர மார்பினை, அண்ணலை, அளியை","ஐது அகல் அல்குலாள் செய் குறி நீ வரின்,","'கறி வளர் சிலம்பில் வழங்கல் ஆனாப்","புலி' என்று ஓர்க்கும், இக் கலி கேழ் ஊரே;","என ஆங்கு,","விலங்கு ஓரார், மெய் ஓர்ப்பின், இவள் வாழாள், இவள் அன்றிப்","புலம் புகழ் ஒருவ! யானும் வாழேன்;","அதனால் பொதி அவிழ் வைகறை வந்து நீ குறை கூறி,","வதுவை அயர்தல் வேண்டுவல், ஆங்குப்","புதுவை போலும் நின் வரவும், இவள்","வதுவை நாண் ஒடுக்கமும், காண்குவல் யானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hill lord, whose tusker has killed the tiger that leapt at it and walks among its herd, that when he comes by night to her trysts the hamlet takes him by his lotus chaplet for the mountain goddess, the millet watchers with torches, slings and bows shout that he is an elephant, and the town hears him as a tiger on the pepper slopes; if they ever find the man instead of the beast she will not live, nor will the friend, so let him come at dawn, ask for her and marry her, and the friend will watch his new look and her bride's shyness.","n":"The three refrains pair his three night-costumes (lotus chaplet and sandal, wet cloth and evening garland, the chest with its necklace) with three misreadings, goddess, elephant, tiger, so the danger is spelled out as the one mistake not yet made: the true one. The opening tusker likened to Bhima breaking the thigh of the leader of the hundred and to Mal among the wrestlers is the lover's strength that the dawn proposal asks him to use openly; it has no second pair of correspondences, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kuri","iravu","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 52","lb":"KL52","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["புலி","புலி",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","simile","elephant"],["தாமரைக்","தாமரை",7,"maram","ornament",""],["சாந்தினை","சாந்து",7,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["அணங்கு","அணங்கு",10,"deyvam","scene",""],["ஞெகிழியர்","ஞெகிழி",13,"seythi","scene","torch-bearers"],["களிறு","களிறு",14,"maa","scene","elephant"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",14,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கறி","கறி",17,"maram","scene","pepper"],["புலி","புலி",18,"maa","scene","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:4"},"kl:53":{"book":"kalittokai","num":53,"t":["வறன் உறல் அறியாத வழை அமை நறும் சாரல்","விறல் மலை வியல் அறை, வீழ் பிடி உழையதா","மறம் மிகு வேழம், தன் மாறு கொள் மைந்தினான்,","புகர் நுதல் புண் செய்த புய் கோடு போல","உயர் முகை நறும் காந்தள் நாள் தோறும் புதிது ஈன,","அயம் நந்தி அணிபெற, அருவி ஆர்த்து இழிதரும்","பய மழை தலைஇய பாடு சால் விறல் வெற்ப!","மறையினின் மணந்து, ஆங்கே மருவு அறத் துறந்த பின்","இறை வளை நெகிழ்பு ஓட, ஏற்பவும் ஒல்லும்மன் -","அயல் அலர் தூற்றலின், ஆய் நலன் இழந்த, கண்!","கயல் உமிழ் நீர் போலக், கண் பனி கலுழாக்கால்?","இனிய செய்து அகன்று, நீ இன்னாதாத் துறத்தலின்,","'பனி இவள் படர்' எனப் பரவாமை ஒல்லும்மன் -","ஊர் அலர் தூற்றலின், ஒளி ஓடி நறு நுதல்","பீர் அலர் அணி கொண்டு, பிறை வனப்பு இழவாக்கால்?","'அஞ்சல்' என்று அகன்று, நீ அருளாது துறத்தலின்,","நெஞ்சு அழி துயர் அட, நிறுப்பவும் இயையும்மன் -","நனவினால் நலம் வாட, நலிதந்த நடுங்கு அஞர்,","கனவினால் அழிவுற்றுக், கங்குலும் ஆற்றாக்கால்?","என ஆங்கு,","விளியா நோய் உழந்து ஆனா என் தோழி, நின் மலை","முளிவுற வருந்திய முளை முதிர் சிறு தினை","தளி பெறத் தகைபெற்றாங்கு, நின்","அளி பெற நந்தும், இவள் ஆய் நுதல் கவினே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the rain-fed hill, where a tusker stays beside his loving cow after wounding a rival and kaanthal buds open like its tusks, that after he took the girl in secret and then left her she might have borne the slipping bangles, the neighbours' talk and the heart's sorrow, had her eyes not run like water spat by carp, her brow not paled like the peerkku flower, and her nights not been broken by dreams; and that, as the drying millet on his hill revives when rain falls, her beauty will revive with his grace.","n":"Each refrain grants a loss that could be endured and then names the sign that cannot be hidden from the town (tears, pallor, sleeplessness), so the argument for marriage is built from what gossip can see. The closing millet simile takes the hill's own crop for the girl, so the plea to marry is put in the hero's own landscape.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","alar","arul","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 53","lb":"KL53","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வழை","வழை",1,"maram","scene",""],["அமை","அமை",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பிடி","பிடி",2,"maa","iraicchi","she-elephant"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",3,"maa","iraicchi","tusker"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",5,"maram","simile",""],["அருவி","அருவி",6,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["கயல்","கயல்",11,"pira","simile","carp"],["பீர்","பீர்",15,"maram","simile",""],["தினை","தினை",22,"unavu","simile","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வேழம்","confirmed","1/1","even the beast on his hill keeps by its mate, while the hero has taken the girl and left her"]],"ch":"kl:4"},"kl:54":{"book":"kalittokai","num":54,"t":["'கொடியவும் கோட்டவும் நீர் இன்றி நிறம் பெறப்","பொடி அழல் புறந்தந்த பூவாப் பூம் பொலன் கோதைத்,","தொடி செறி யாப்பு அமை அரி முன்கை, அணைத் தோளாய்!","அடி உறை அருளாமை ஒத்ததோ நினக்கு?' என்ன,","நரந்தம் நாறு இரும் கூந்தல் எஞ்சாது நனி பற்றிப்,","பொலம் புனை மகரவாய் நுங்கிய சிகழிகை,","நலம்பெறச் சுற்றிய குரல் அமை ஒரு காழ்","விரல் முறை சுற்றி, மோக்கலும் மோந்தனன்;","நறாஅ அவிழ்ந்தன்ன என் மெல் விரல் போது கொண்டு","செறாஅச் செங் கண் புதைய வைத்துப்","பறாஅக் குருகின் உயிர்த்தலும் உயிர்த்தனன்;","தொய்யில் இள முலை இனிய தைவந்து,","தொய்யல் அம் தடம் கையின், வீழ் பிடி அளிக்கும்","மையல் யானையின், மருட்டலும் மருட்டினன்;","அதனால்,","அல்லல் களைந்தனன் தோழி! நம் நகர்","அரும் கடி நீவாமை கூறின்,'நன்று' என,","நின்னொடு சூழ்வல், தோழி, 'நயம் புரிந்து,","இன்னது செய்தாள் இவள்' என","மன்னா உலகத்து மன்னுவது புரைமே!"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend how her lover, asking whether it was fitting that she would not grant grace to one who served at her feet, took her fragrant hair and smelled the braid, laid her fingers over his eyes and sighed like a bird that cannot fly, and stroked her painted breast as a love-mad tusker soothes his cow; he has taken away her distress, so if the friend will speak so that the house's guard is not broken, she will plan it with her, that it may be said she did this out of love, which in an unlasting world is the thing that lasts.","n":"The heroine borrows the puram formula for fame in a world that does not last (மன்னா உலகத்து மன்னுவது) for a woman's choice of her lover: the thing she wants remembered is that she acted from love. Lines 16 and 17 (நம் நகர் அரும் கடி நீவாமை கூறின்) are compressed, and whether she means disclosure to the family is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kalaviyal","pugazh","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 54","lb":"KL54","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["நரந்தம்","நரந்தம்",5,"maram","ornament",""],["குருகின்","குருகு",11,"pul","simile","bird"],["தொய்யில்","தொய்யில்",12,"pira","ornament","breast painting"],["பிடி","பிடி",13,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["யானையின்","யானை",14,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:4"},"kl:55":{"book":"kalittokai","num":55,"t":["மின் ஒளிர் அவிர் அறல் இடை போழும் பெயலே போல்,","பொன் அகை தகை வகிர் வகை நெறி வயங்கிட்டுப்","போழ் இடை இட்ட கமழ் நறும் பூங் கோதை,","இன் நகை, இலங்கு எயிற்றுத், தேமொழித், துவர்ச் செவ்வாய்","நல் நுதால் நினக்கு ஒன்று கூறுவாம்; கேள், இனி;","'நில்' என, நிறுத்தான்; நிறுத்தே வந்து.","நுதலும், முகனும், தோளும், கண்ணும்,","இயலும், சொல்லும், நோக்குபு நினைஇ,","'ஐ தேய்ந்தன்று, பிறையும் அன்று;","மை தீர்ந்தன்று, மதியும் அன்று;","வேய் அமன்றன்று, மலையும் அன்று;","பூ அமன்றன்று, சுனையும் அன்று;","மெல்ல இயலும், மயிலும் அன்று;","சொல்லத் தளரும், கிளியும் அன்று'","என ஆங்கு,","அனையன பல பாராட்டிப், பையென,","வலைவர் போலச் சோர் பதன் ஒற்றிப்,","புலையர் போலப் புன்கண் நோக்கித்,","தொழலும் தொழுதான்; தொடலும் தொட்டான்;","காழ் வரை நில்லாக் கடும் களிறு அன்னோன்","தொழூஉம்; தொடூஉம்; அவன் தன்மை","ஏழைத் தன்மையோ இல்லை, தோழி!"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that he stopped her, looked at her brow, face, shoulders, eyes, gait and speech, and said each was not a crescent for it does not wane, not the moon for it has no stain, not a hill though bamboo crowds it, not a pool though flowers crowd it, not a peacock though it moves softly, not a parrot though it falters; then, praising her, he watched his moment like a netter and looked pitifully like a beggar, bowed and touched her, he who is like a tusker no post can hold, and his nature, friend, is not a simple man's.","n":"The six denials are the hero's praise in the form of corrected similes: each standard vehicle is named and refused for a flaw it has and she lacks, so the praise works by finding fault with the moon. The netter and the pulaiyar similes let the girl see the calculation in his humility, which is why she ends by saying he is no simpleton.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 55","lb":"KL55","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["வேய்","வேய்",11,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["பூ","பூ",12,"maram","simile",""],["மயிலும்","மயில்",13,"pul","simile","peacock"],["கிளியும்","கிளி",14,"pul","simile","parrot"],["வலைவர்","வலைவர்",17,"seythi","simile","netters"],["புலையர்","புலையர்",18,"seythi","simile",""],["களிறு","களிறு",20,"maa","simile","tusker"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:4"},"kl:56":{"book":"kalittokai","num":56,"t":["ஊர்க் கால் நிவந்த பொதும்பருள், நீர்க் கால்,","கொழு நிழல் ஞாழல் முதிர் இணர் கொண்டு,","கழும முடித்துக், கண் கூடு கூழை","சுவல் மிசைத் தாதொடு தாழ, அகல் மதி","தீம் கதிர் விட்டது போல, முகன் அமர்ந்து","ஈங்கே வருவாள் இவள் யார் கொல்? ஆங்கே, ஓர்","வல்லவன் தைஇய பாவை கொல்? நல்லார்","உறுப்பு எலாம் கொண்டு, இயற்றியாள் கொல்? வெறுப்பினால்","வேண்டு உருவம் கொண்டது ஓர் கூற்றம் கொல்? - ஆண்டார்,","கடிது, இவளைக் காவார் விடுதல்; கொடி இயல்,","பல் கலைச் சில் பூங் கலிங்கத்தள் - ஈங்கு இது ஓர்","நல்கூர்ந்தார் செல்வ மகள்!","இவளை சொல்லாடிக் காண்பேன், தகைத்து;","நல்லாய்! கேள்;","ஆய் தூவி அனம் என, அணி மயில் பெடை எனத்,","தூது உண் அம் புறவு எனத், துதைந்த நின் எழில் நலம் -","மாதர் கொள் மான் நோக்கின் மட நல்லாய்! - நின் கண்டார்ப்","பேதுறூஉம் என்பதை அறிதியோ? அறியாயோ?","நுணங்கு அமைத் திரள் என, நுண் இழை அணை என,","முழங்கு நீர்ப் புணை என, அமைந்த நின் தட மென் தோள் -","வணங்கு இறை வால் எயிற்று அம் நல்லாய்! - நின் கண்டார்க்கு,","அணங்கு ஆகும் என்பதை அறிதியோ? அறியாயோ?","முதிர் கோங்கின் முகை என, முகம் செய்த குரும்பை எனப்,","பெயல் துளி முகிழ் எனப், பெருத்த நின் இள முலை -","மயிர் வார்ந்த வரி முன்கை மட நல்லாய்! - நின் கண்டார்","உயிர் வாங்கும் என்பதை உணர்தியோ? உணராயோ?","என ஆங்கு,","பேதுற்றாய்ப் போலப் பிறர் எவ்வம் நீ அறியாய்,","யாது ஒன்றும் வாய் வாளாது இறந்தீவாய்! கேள், இனி;","நீயும் தவறு இலை; நின்னைப் புறங்கடை","போதர விட்ட நுமரும், தவறு இலர்;","நிறை அழி கொல் யானை நீர்க்கு விட்டாங்குப்","'பறை அறைந்து அல்லது செல்லற்க' என்னா","இறையே தவறுடையான்."],"p":"","r":"The man, seeing a girl come with ngaazhal clusters in her hair and a face like the moon, asks whether she is a doll a craftsman made, a woman built from all good women's parts, or Death in a form it chose, says her people are wrong to leave her unguarded, and tells her that her beauty like swan, peahen and dove bewilders those who see her, her shoulders like bamboo, pillow and raft torment them, and her young breasts take their lives; then, as she passes without a word, he says neither she nor her people are at fault, only the king, who does not have a drum beaten before her as before a killer elephant let out to water.","n":"The girl is too young to know what she does (பேதுற்றாய்ப் போல... நீ அறியாய்), so the address is one-sided, the kaikkilai of porul 1.53. The closing conceit turns the blame into a civic complaint: she is a public danger the king should announce by drum like a must elephant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kootru","ilamai","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 56","lb":"KL56","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kaikkilai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",2,"maram","ornament",""],["அனம்","அனம்",15,"pul","simile","swan"],["மயில்","மயில்",15,"pul","simile","peahen"],["புறவு","புறவு",16,"pul","simile","dove"],["மான்","மான்",17,"maa","simile","deer"],["அமைத்","அமை",19,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["கோங்கின்","கோங்கு",23,"maram","simile",""],["குரும்பை","குரும்பை",23,"maram","simile","coconut bud"],["யானை","யானை",32,"maa","simile","elephant"],["பறை","பறை",33,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:4"},"kl:57":{"book":"kalittokai","num":57,"t":["வேய் எனத் திரண்ட தோள், வெறி கமழ் வணர் ஐம்பால்,","மா வென்ற மட நோக்கின், மயில் இயல், தளர்பு ஒல்கி -","ஆய் சிலம்பு அரி ஆர்ப்ப, அவிர் ஒளி இழை இமைப்பக்","கொடி என, மின் என, அணங்கு என, யாது ஒன்றும்","தெரிகல்லா இடையின் கண் கண் கவர்பு ஒருங்கு ஓட,","வளமை சால் உயர் சிறப்பின் நுந்தை தொல் வியல் நகர்","இளமையான் எறி பந்தொடு - இகத்தந்தாய்! கேள் இனி;","பூம் தண் தார்ப் புலர் சாந்தின், தென்னவன் உயர் கூடல்","தேம் பாய அவிழ் நீலத்து அலர் வென்ற அமர் உண் கண்","ஏந்து கோட்டு எழில் யானை ஒன்னாதார்க்கு அவன் வேலின்,","சேந்து நீ இனையையால்; ஒத்ததோ? - சின் மொழி!","பொழி பெயல் வண்மையான் அசோகம் தண் காவினுள்,","கழி கவின் இள மாவின் தளிர் அன்னாய்! அதன் தலைப்","பணை அமை பாய் மான் தேர் அவன் செற்றார் நிறம் பாய்ந்த","கணையினும், நோய் செய்தல் கடப்பு அன்றோ? - கனம் குழாய்!","வகை அமை தண் தாரான் கோடு உயர் பொருப்பின் மேல்,","தகை இணர் இள வேங்கை மலர் அன்ன சுணங்கினாய்!","மத வலி மிகு கடாஅத்து அவன் யானை மருப்பினும்","கதவவால் - தக்கதோ? காழ் கொண்ட இள முலை!","என ஆங்கு,","இனையன கூற, இறைஞ்சுபு நிலம் நோக்கி,","நினையுபு, நெடிது ஒன்று நினைப்பாள் போல், மற்று ஆங்கே,","துணை அமை தோழியர்க்கு அமர்த்த கண்ணள்,","மனை ஆங்குப் பெயர்ந்தாள், என் அறிவு அகப்படுத்தே!"],"p":"","r":"The man tells the girl who ran past from her father's old house with her ball, her waist so slight it could not be told from creeper, lightning or goddess, that with eyes finer than the blue lily of the Pandya's Koodal she has reddened him like that king's spear against hostile elephants, that she wounds worse than his arrows that pierce enemies' breasts, and that her breasts are fiercer than his elephant's tusks, asking whether this is fitting; at that she bowed, looked at the ground, glanced sharply at her companions and went home, taking his wits with her.","n":"Each praise runs through the Pandya's war gear (spear, arrows, tusked elephant), so the girl's harm is measured against a king's violence and found greater. Her silent going, eyes lowered and then flashing at her friends, is the first sign of a response, but the address remains his alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ilamai","azhagu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 57","lb":"KL57","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kaikkilai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["மயில்","மயில்",2,"pul","simile","peacock"],["மா","மா",2,"maa","simile","deer"],["நீலத்து","நீலம்",9,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["யானை","யானை",10,"maa","simile","elephant"],["அசோகம்","அசோகம்",12,"maram","scene",""],["மாவின்","மா",13,"maram","simile","mango"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",17,"maram","simile","kino tree"]],"en":[["king","தென்னவன்",8],["town","கூடல்",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:4"},"kl:58":{"book":"kalittokai","num":58,"t":["வார் உறு வணர் ஐம்பால், வணங்கு இறை நெடு மென் தோள்,","பேர் எழில் மலர் உண் கண், பிணை எழில் மான் நோக்கின்,","கார் எதிர் தளிர் மேனிக், கவின் பெறு சுடர் நுதல்,","கூர் எயிற்று முகை வெண் பல், கொடி புரையும் நுசுப்பினாய்,","நேர் சிலம்பு அரி ஆர்ப்ப நிரை தொடிக் கை வீசினை,","ஆர் உயிர் வௌவிக்கொண்டு அறிந்தீயாது இறப்பாய்! கேள்;","உளனா, என் உயிரை உண்டு, உயவு நோய் கைம்மிக,","இளமையான் உணராதாய்! நின் தவறு இல்லானும்,","களைநர் இல் நோய் செய்யும் கவின் அறிந்து, அணிந்து, தம்","வளமையான் போத்தந்த நுமர் தவறு இல் என்பாய்;","நடை மெலிந்து, அயர்வு உறீஇ, நாளும் என் நலியும் நோய்","மடமையான் உணராதாய்! நின் தவறு இல்லானும்,","இடை நில்லாது எய்க்கும் நின் உரு அறிந்து, அணிந்து, தம்","உடைமையால் போத்தந்த நுமர் தவறு இல் என்பாய்;","அல்லல் கூர்ந்து அழிவுற, அணங்கு ஆகி அடரும் நோய்","சொல்லினும் அறியாதாய்! நின் தவறு இல்லானும்,","ஒல்லையே உயிர் வௌவும் உரு அறிந்து, அணிந்து, தம்","செல்வத்தால் போத்தந்த நுமர் தவறு இல் என்பாய்;","என ஆங்கு,","ஒறுப்பின், யான் ஒறுப்பது நுமரை; யான், மற்று இந் நோய்","பொறுக்கலாம் வரைத்து அன்றிப் பெரிது ஆயின், பொலம் குழாய்!","மறுத்து இவ் ஊர் மன்றத்து மடல் ஏறி,","நிறுக்குவென் போல்வல் யான், நீ படு பழியே!"],"p":"","r":"The man tells the girl, long-haired, deer-eyed, with a body like sprouts that meet the rains and a waist like a creeper, who swings her bangled arms and passes after taking his life, that because of her youth she does not know the sickness she causes and is not at fault, and her people will say they are not at fault for dressing her and sending her out in their wealth; so if he punishes anyone it will be her people, and if the sickness grows past bearing he will mount the palm-stem horse in the town square and fix the blame on her.","n":"The madal threat here is aimed not at winning her but at blame: riding it will make the town see her as the cause (நீ படு பழியே). The three refrains rotate the reason her people dressed her (wealth, possession, riches), so the family's display is the real culprit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamanoy","ilamai","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 58","lb":"KL58","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kaikkilai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["மான்","மான்",2,"maa","simile","deer"],["தளிர்","தளிர்",3,"maram","simile","sprout"],["கொடி","கொடி",4,"maram","simile","creeper"],["மடல்","மடல்",22,"maram","scene","palm-stem horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:4"},"kl:59":{"book":"kalittokai","num":59,"t":["தளை நெகிழ், பிணி நிவந்த பாசு அடைத் தாமரை","முளை நிமிர்ந்தவை போலும் முத்துக் கோல் அவிர் தொடி,","அடுக்கம் நாறு அலர் காந்தள் நுண் ஏர் தண் ஏர் உருவின்","துடுப்பு எனப் புரையும் நின் திரண்ட, நேர் அரி முன்கைச்","சுடர் விரி வினை வாய்ந்த தூதையும் பாவையும்","விளையாட, அரிப் பெய்த அழகு அமை புனை வினை","ஆய் சிலம்பு எழுந்து ஆர்ப்ப, அம் சில இயலும் நின்","பின்னு விட்டு இருளிய ஐம்பால் கண்டு, என் பால","என்னை விட்டு இகத்தர, இறந்தீவாய்! கேள், இனி;","'மருளி, யான் மருள் உற இவன் உற்றது எவன்?' என்னும்","'அருள் இலை இவட்கு' என அயலார் நின் பழிக்கும்கா,","வை எயிற்றவர் நாப்பண், வகை அணிப் பொலிந்து, நீ","தையில் நீர் ஆடிய தவம் தலைப்படுவாயோ?","உருள் இழாய்! 'ஒளி வாட, இவன் உள் நோய் யாது' என்னும்","'அருள் இலை இவட்கு' என அயலார் நின் பழிக்கும்கால்,","பொய்தல மகளையாய்ப், பிறர் மனைப் பாடி, நீ","எய்திய பலர்க்கு ஈத்த பயம் பயக்கிற்பதோ?","ஆய் தொடி! 'ஐது உயிர்த்து, இவன் உள் நோய் யாது?' என்னும்","'நோய் இலை இவட்கு' என நொதுமலர் பழிக்கும்கால்,","சிறு முத்தனைப் பேணிச் சிறு சோறு மடுத்து, நீ","நறு நுதலவரொடு நக்கது நன்கு இயைவதோ?","என ஆங்கு,","அனையவை - உளையவும், யான் நினக்கு உரைத்ததை","இனைய நீ செய்தது உதவாய் ஆயின், சே இழாய்!","செய்ததன் பயம் பற்று விடாது;","நயம் பற்று விடின் - இல்லை - நசைஇயோர் திறத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the girl who plays with toy pots and dolls, her bangles like lotus shoots and her forearm like the kaanthal's spathe, who passed leaving him without himself, that when the neighbours blame her as merciless to the one who suffers, the merit of her bathing in Thai among her friends, of the gifts she gave playing the little mother in others' houses, and of feeding her doll-baby and laughing with her friends will not help her; if she does not do what he asks, the fruit of her deeds will not let go, and there is nothing for those who desire once kindness lets go.","n":"Each refrain sets a girl's ritual or play (the Thai bath, playing house, feeding the doll) against the neighbours' charge that she has no pity, so her childhood merit is weighed against a grown man's claim. Line 11 reads பழிக்கும்கா where the parallel lines 15 and 19 have பழிக்கும்கால்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","ilamai","thavam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 59","lb":"KL59","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kaikkilai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",1,"maram","simile","lotus"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",3,"maram","simile",""],["சோறு","சோறு",20,"unavu","gift_food","rice"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:4"},"kl:60":{"book":"kalittokai","num":60,"t":["சுணங்கு அணி வன முலைச், சுடர் கொண்ட நறு நுதல்,","மணம் கமழ் நறும் கோதை மாரி வீழ் இரும் கூந்தல்,","நுணங்கு எழில் ஒள் தித்தி, நுழை நொசி மட மருங்குல்,","வணங்கு இறை வரி முன்கை, வரி ஆர்ந்த அல்குலாய்!","'கண் ஆர்ந்த நலத்தாரைக், கதுமெனக், கண்டவர்க்கு,","உள் நின்ற நோய் மிக, உயிர் எஞ்சு துயர் செய்தல்","பெண் அன்று, புனை இழாய்!' எனக் கூறி தொழூஉம்; தொழுதே","கண்ணும் நீர் ஆக நடுங்கினன்; இன் நகாய்!","என் செய்தான் கொல்லோ! இஃது ஒத்தன் தன் கண்","பொரு களிறு அன்ன தகை சாம்பி, உள் உள்","உருகுவான் போலும், உடைந்து?","தெருவின் கண் காரணம் இன்றிக் கலங்குவார்க் கண்டு, நீ","வாரணவாசிப் பதம் பெயர்த்தல், ஏதில,","நீ நின் மேல் கொள்வது, எவன்?","அலர் முலை, ஆய் இழை நல்லாய்! கதுமெனப்","பேர் அமர் உண் கண் நின் தோழி உறீஇய","ஆர் அஞர் எவ்வம் உயிர் வாங்கும்;","மற்று இந் நோய் தீரும் மருந்து அருளாய், ஒண் தொடீ!","'நின் முகம் காணும் மருந்தினேன்' என்னுமால்;","நின் முகம் தான் பெறின் அல்லது, கொன்னே","மருந்து பிறிது யாதும் இல்லேல், திருந்து இழாய்!","என் செய்வாம் கொல், இனி நாம்?","பொன் செய்வாம்;","ஆறு விலங்கித் தெருவின் கண் நின்று ஒருவன்","கூறும் சொல் வாய் எனக் கொண்டு, அதன் பண்பு உணராம்,","'தேறல் எளிது' என்பாம் நாம்;","'ஒருவன் சாம் ஆறு எளிது' என்பாம் மற்று;","சிறிது, ஆங்கே - மாணா ஊர் அம்பல் அலரின் அலர்க என,","நாணும் நிறையும் நயப்பு இல் பிறப்பு இலி,","பூண் ஆகம் நோக்கி, இமையான், நயந்து நம்","கேண்மை விருப்புற்றவனை, எதிர் நின்று,","நாண் அடப், பெயர்த்தல் நயவரவு இன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the man who said it is unwomanly to give life-draining pain to those who glimpse such beauty bowed with tears and trembled, wasting like a fighting tusker, and asked the friend for the cure of the sickness her friend's eyes gave him, saying the only medicine is to see her face; what shall they do, she asks, and answers that though they once thought trusting a stranger in the street easy, it is not kind to send away with shame a man who gazes unblinking and longs for their friendship.","n":"The friend reports the hero's plea as evidence and then turns it on the girl, ending in the persuasion that she accept him. Several phrases are obscure (வாரணவாசிப் பதம் பெயர்த்தல், line 13; பொன் செய்வாம், line 23), and lines 15 to 32 are not in sangamtamil.com's text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","marundhu","kaamanoy","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 60","lb":"KL60","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கோதை","கோதை",2,"maram","ornament","garland"],["களிறு","களிறு",10,"maa","simile","tusker"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:4"},"kl:61":{"book":"kalittokai","num":61,"t":["எல்லா! இஃது ஒத்தன் என் பெறான்? கேட்டைக் காண்;","செல்வம் கடைகொளச், சாஅய்ச், சான்றவர்","அல்லல் களை தக்க கேளிர் உழைச் சென்று","சொல்லுதல் உற்று, உரைகல்லாதவர் போலப்","பல் ஊழ் பெயர்ந்து என்னை நோக்கும்; மற்று யான் நோக்கின்","மெல்ல இறைஞ்சும் தலை;","எல்லா! நீ, முன்னத்தான் ஒன்று குறித்தாய் போல் காட்டினை;","நின்னின் விடாஅ நிழல் போல் திரிதருவாய்!","என் நீ பெறாதது? ஈது என்?","சொல்லின், மறாது ஈவாள் மன்னோ, இவள்?","செறாஅது, ஈதல் இரந்தார்க்கு, ஒன்று ஆற்றாது வாழ்தலின்,","சாதலும் கூடுமாம் மற்று;","இவள் தந்தை, காதலின் யார்க்கும் கொடுக்கும் விழுப் பொருள்;","யாது நீ வேண்டியது;","பேதாய்! பொருள் வேண்டும் புன்கண்மை ஈண்டு இல்லை; யாழ","மருளி மட நோக்கின் நின் தோழி என்னை","அருளீயல் வேண்டுவல், யான்;","'அன்னையோ!' மண்டு அமர் அட்ட களிறு அன்னான், தன்னை ஒரு","பெண்டிர் அருளக் கிடந்தது எவன் கொலோ?","ஒண் தொடீ! நாண் இலன் மன்ற; இவன்;","ஆயின், ஏஎ!","'பல்லார் நக்கு எள்ளப்படு மடல்மா ஏறி,","மல்லல் ஊர் ஆங்கண் படுமே, நறு நுதல்","நல்காள் கண்மாறிவிடின்' எனச் செல்வான் - நாம்","எள்ளி நகினும் வரூஉம்; இடை இடைக்","கள்வர் போல் நோக்கினும் நோக்கும்; குறித்தது","கொள்ளாது போகாக் குணன் உடையன், எந்தை தன்","உள்ளம் குறைபடா ஆறு."],"p":"","r":"The friend, telling the girl of the man who keeps looking at her like a ruined noble who comes to kin but cannot say his need, asks him what he lacks, since the girl, whose father gives prized wealth to all and who holds that living without giving to suppliants is worse than death, would give it; he answers that he wants no wealth, only that her deer-eyed friend show him grace; she laughs that a man like a battle-winning tusker should lie waiting on a woman's grace and is shameless, yet he goes saying he will ride the mocked palm-stem horse if refused, comes back however they laugh, and will not leave without what he came for, so they must act before her father's heart is wounded.","n":"The friend first treats him as a suppliant and offers the household's giving ethic, the puram creed that dying is sweeter than being unable to give; his answer moves the suppliant's role from wealth to love, which the giving household cannot refuse on its own terms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","eegai","naanam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 61","lb":"KL61","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",18,"maa","simile","tusker"],["மடல்மா","மடல்",22,"maram","scene","palm-stem horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:4"},"kl:62":{"book":"kalittokai","num":62,"t":["ஏஎ! இஃது ஒத்தன், நாண் இலன் - தன்னொடு","மேவேம் என்பாரையும் மேவினன் கைப்பற்றும்;","மேவினும், மேவாக்கடையும் அஃது எல்லாம்","நீ அறிதி; யான் அஃது அறிகல்லேன்; பூ அமன்ற","மெல் இணர் செல்லாக் கொடி அன்னாய்! நின்னை யான்","புல் இனிது ஆகலின், புல்லினென்; - எல்லா!","தமக்கு இனிது என்று, வலிதின் பிறர்க்கு இன்னா","செய்வது நன்று ஆமோ மற்று?","சுடர் தொடீ! போற்றாய் களை, நின் முதுக்குறைமை; போற்றக் கேள்","வேட்டார்க்கு இனிது ஆயின் அல்லது, நீர்க்கு இனிது என்று","உண்பவோ, நீர் உண்பவர்?","செய்வது அறிகல்லேன்; யாது செய்வேன் கொலோ -","ஐ வாய் அரவின் இடைப்பட்டு, நை வாரா?","'மை இல் மதியின் விளங்கும் முகத்தாரை","வௌவிக் கொளலும் அறன்' எனக் கண்டன்று;","அறனும் அது கண்டற்று ஆயின், திறன் இன்றிக்","கூறும் சொல் கேளான், நலிதரும்; பண்டு நாம்","வேறு அல்லம் என்பது ஒன்று உண்டால்; அவனொடு","மாறு உண்டோ, நெஞ்சே! நமக்கு?"],"p":"","r":"The girl, speaking of a shameless man who takes the hand even of those who refuse him, reports how he said he embraced her because embracing her was sweet, how she asked whether it is right to force on others what pains them because it is sweet to oneself, and how he answered that people drink water because it is sweet to the thirsty, not to the water, and that he holds seizing moon-faced women to be virtue; if virtue too sees it so, she tells her heart, he will not hear reason and will torment her, and since they once said they were not two, what quarrel has her heart with him?","n":"The exchange is a moral debate in miniature: she states the rule against doing to others what is sweet to oneself and painful to them, and he answers with a theory of pleasure (sweetness lies in the one who needs) and calls the seizing virtue; she gives way not to his argument but to their former bond. The speaker of line 12 to 13 (caught between the five-mouthed snake) may be either.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","aram","nenju","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 62","lb":"KL62","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","nenju",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொடி","கொடி",5,"maram","simile","creeper"],["அரவின்","அரவு",13,"pira","simile","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:4"},"kl:63":{"book":"kalittokai","num":63,"t":["நோக்கும்கால், நோக்கித் தொழூஉம், பிறர் காண்பார்","தூக்கு இலி; தூற்றும் பழி என கை கவித்துப்","போக்கும்கால், போக்கு நினைந்திருக்கும்; மற்று நாம்","காக்கும் இடம் அன்று, இனி;","எல்லா! எவன் செய்வாம்?","பூங் குழாய்! செல்லல் அவன் உழைக் கூஉய்க் கூஉய்","விரும்பி யான் விட்டேனும் போல்வல்; என் தோள் மேல்","கரும்பு எழுது தொய்யிற்குச் செல்வல்; 'ஈங்கு ஆக","இருந்தாயோ?' என்று ஆங்கு இற;","அவன் நின் திருந்து அடி மேல் வீழ்ந்து இரக்கும், நோய் தீர்க்கும்","மருந்து நீ ஆகுதலான்;","இன்னும், கடம் பூண்டு ஒரு கால் நீ வந்தை, 'உடம்பட்டாள்'","என்னாமை என் மெய் தொடு;","இஃதோ அடங்கக் கேள்;","நின்னொடு சூழும்கால், நீயும் நிலம் கிளையா,","என்னொடு நிற்றல் எளிது அன்றோ? மற்று அவன்","தன்னொடு நின்று விடு."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the man stares and bows to her careless of onlookers, and when she waves him off for fear of gossip he only waits to come again, so the place can no longer be guarded; she proposes that the girl not go to him herself, while she calls him as if she wanted him and then goes off to have sugarcane painted on her shoulder, telling her to ask him 'were you here?' and pass, for he will fall at her feet as the cure for his sickness; and, speaking to him, she makes him swear not to say the girl consented, and tells her that standing with him will be as easy as standing with her friend.","n":"The friend stages a tryst made to look accidental, with herself as the caller and an errand (the sugarcane painting) as her excuse to leave, so that the girl's consent can be denied. The voices change without notice (lines 12 and 13 seem addressed to the man), so who says which part is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal","marundhu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 63","lb":"KL63","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["கரும்பு","கரும்பு",8,"maram","ornament","sugarcane painting"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:4"},"kl:64":{"book":"kalittokai","num":64,"t":["அணி முகம் மதி ஏய்ப்ப, அம் மதியை நனி ஏய்க்கும்","மணி முகம்; மா மழை, நின் பின் ஒப்ப, பின்னின் கண்","விரி நுண் நூல் சுற்றிய ஈர் இதழ் அலரி","அரவுக் கண் அணி உறழ் ஆரல் மீன் தகை ஒப்ப,","அரும் படர் கண்டாரைச் செய்து, ஆங்கு இயலும்","விரிந்து ஒலி கூந்தலாய்! கண்டை! எமக்குப்","பெரும் பொன் படுகுவை பண்டு;","ஏஎ! எல்லா, மொழிவது கண்டை, இஃது ஒத்தன்; தொய்யில்","எழுதி இறுத்த பெரு பொன் படுகம்?","உழுவது உடையமோ, யாம்?;","உழுதாய்;","சுரும்பு இமிர் பூங் கோதை அம் நல்லாய்! யான் நின்","திருந்து இழை மென் தோள் இழைத்த, மற்று இஃதோ,","கரும்பு எல்லாம் நின் உழவு அன்றோ? ஒருங்கே","துகள் அறு வாள் முகம் ஒப்ப மலர்ந்த","குவளையும், நின் உழவு அன்றோ? இகலி","முகை மாறு கொள்ளும் எயிற்றாய்! இவை அல்ல,","என் உழுவாய் நீ, மற்று இனி?","எல்லா! நல் தோள் இழைத்த கரும்புக்கு நீ கூறு;","முற்று எழில் நீல மலர் என உற்ற,","இரும்பு ஈர் வடி அன்ன, உண்கட்கும், எல்லாம்,","பெரும் பொன் உண்டு என்பாய்! இனி;","நல்லாய்! இகுளை! கேள்;","ஈங்கே தலைப்படுவன், உண்டான் தலைப்பெயின்,","வேந்து கொண்டன்ன பல;","ஆங்கு ஆக! அத்திறம் அல்லாக்கால், வேங்கை வீ","முற்று எழில் கொண்ட சுணங்கு அணி பூண் ஆகம்","பொய்த்து ஒருகால் எம்மை முயங்கினை சென்றீமோ","முத்து ஏர் முறுவலாய்! நீ படும் பொன் எல்லாம்","உத்தி எறிந்துவிடற்கு."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the woman, whose face is like the moon and whose dark hair, wound with alari flowers, is like the aaral fish, that she owes him a great sum of gold from long ago; the friend asks what gold they owe for the painting on her shoulder, since they have ploughed nothing, and he answers that the sugarcane drawn on her shoulders and the blue lily blooming like her face are her ploughing; the friend replies that he claims gold for her eyes too, and tells the girl that he would come at once if paid, as kings take tribute, but otherwise she should embrace him once in pretence and so cast off the whole debt.","n":"The whole poem is a mock lawsuit: love is a debt of gold, the breast-painting is ploughed land, and an embrace pays it off. Several turns (lines 24 and 25, the closing உத்தி எறிந்துவிடற்கு) are obscure and the assignment of speakers is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kalaviyal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 64","lb":"KL64","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["அலரி","அலரி",3,"maram","ornament",""],["ஆரல்","ஆரல்",4,"pira","simile","aaral fish"],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",12,"pira","scene","bee"],["கரும்பு","கரும்பு",14,"maram","ornament","sugarcane painting"],["குவளையும்","குவளை",16,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["நீல","நீலம்",20,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",26,"maram","simile","kino tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:4"},"kl:65":{"book":"kalittokai","num":65,"t":["திருந்து இழாய்! கேளாய், நம் ஊர்க்கு எல்லாம் சாலும்","பெரு நகை! அல்கல் நிகழ்ந்தது; ஒரு நிலையே","மன்பதை எல்லாம் மடிந்த இரும் கங்குல்,","அம் துகில் போர்வை அணிபெற தைஇ, நம்","இன் சாயல் மார்பன் குறி நின்றேன் யான் ஆகத் -","தீரத் தறைந்த தலையும், தன் கம்பலும்","காரக் குறைந்து, கறைப்பட்டு வந்து, நம்","சேரியின் போகா முட முதிர் பார்ப்பானைத்,","தோழி! நீ போற்றுதி என்றி - அவன் ஆங்கே,","பாராக், குறழாப், பணியாப், 'பொழுது அன்றி","யார் இவண் நின்றீர்?' எனக் கூறிப், பையென","வை காண் முது பகட்டின், பக்கத்தின் போகாது,","'தையால்! தம்பலம் தின்றியோ?' என்று, தன்","பக்கு அழித்துக், 'கொண்டீ' எனத் தரலும் - யாது ஒன்றும்","வாய்வாளேன் நிற்பக் - கடிது அகன்று கைமாறிக்","'கைப்படுக்கப்பட்டாய் சிறுமி! நீ மற்று, யான்","ஏனை பிசாசு, அருள் என்னை நலிதரின்,","இவ் ஊர்ப் பலி நீ பெறாஅமல் கொள்வேன்'","எனப் பலவும் தாங்காது வாய் பாடி நிற்ப -","முது பார்ப்பான் அஞ்சினன் ஆதல் அறிந்து, யான், எஞ்சாது","ஒரு கை மணல் கொண்டு, மேல் தூவக் கண்டே","கடிது அரற்றிப் பூசல் தொடங்கினன், ஆங்கே,","ஒடுங்கா வயத்தின், கொடும் கேழ்க், கடுங்கண்","இரும் புலி கொண்மார் நிறுத்த வலையுள் ஓர்","ஏதில் குறு நரி பட்டற்றால்; காதலன்","காட்சி அழுங்க, நம் ஊர்க்கு எலாஅம்","ஆகுலம் ஆகி விளைந்ததை என்றும் தன்","வாழ்க்கை அது ஆகக் கொண்ட முது பார்ப்பான்","வீழ்க்கைப் பெரும் கரும் கூத்து."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend of the night's farce, a joke for the whole town: as she waited wrapped in a fine cloth at her lover's tryst, the old crippled brahmin of their street, shaven-headed in a stained blanket, saw her, asked who stood there out of time, edged up like an old bull to straw, offered her betel from his pouch, and when she stood silent backed off crying that he was a goblin who would take the town's offerings; seeing him afraid, she threw a handful of sand on him and he raised a cry, so that like a stray little jackal caught in a net set for a fierce tiger, the old man spoiled her lover's coming and made an uproar for the town.","n":"The comedy inverts the night tryst's dangers: the threat is no beast or guard but a frightened old man who mistakes her for a ghost and is mistaken by her for one in turn. The closing simile makes the brahmin the jackal and leaves the tiger the net was set for unnamed: the lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 65","lb":"KL65","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["பகட்டின்","பகடு",12,"maa","simile","bull"],["தம்பலம்","தம்பலம்",13,"unavu","gift_food","betel"],["மணல்","மணல்",21,"pira","scene","sand"],["புலி","புலி",24,"maa","simile","tiger"],["நரி","நரி",25,"maa","simile","jackal"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:4"},"kl:66":{"book":"kalittokai","num":66,"t":["வீங்கு நீர் அவிழ் நீலம் பகர்பவர் வயல் கொண்ட","ஞாங்கர் மலர் சூழ்தந்து, ஊர் புகுந்த வரி வண்டு,","ஓங்கு உயர் எழில் யானைக் கனை கடாம் கமழ் நாற்றம்","ஆங்கு அவை விருந்து ஆற்றப், பகல் அல்கிக், கங்குலான்,","வீங்கு இறை வடுக் கொள, வீழுநர்ப் புணர்ந்தவர்","தேம் கமழ் கதுப்பின் உள் அரும்பு அவிழ் நறு முல்லைப்","பாய்ந்து ஊதிப் படர் தீர்ந்து, பண்டு தாம் மரீஇய","பூம் பொய்கை மறந்து உள்ளாப் புனல் அணி நல் ஊர!","அணை மென் தோள் யாம் வாட, அமர் துணை புணர்ந்து நீ,","'மண மனையாய்!' என வந்த மல்லலின் மாண்பு அன்றோ -","பொதுக் கொண்ட கவ்வையுள் பூ அணிப் பொலிந்த நின்","வதுவை அம் கமழ் நாற்றம் வைகறைப் பெற்றதை;","கனலும் நோய்த் தலையும், 'நீ கனம் குழை அவரொடு","புனல் உளாய்' என வந்த பூசலின் பெரிது அன்றோ -","தார் கொண்டாள் தலைக் கோதை தடுமாறிப் பூண்ட நின்","ஈர் அணி சிதையாது, எம் இல் வந்து நின்றதை;","தணந்ததன் தலையும், 'நீ தளர் இயல் அவரொடு","துணங்கையாய்' என வந்த கவ்வையின் கடப்பு அன்றோ -","ஒளி பூத்த நுதலாரோடு ஓர் அணிப் பொலிந்த நின்","களி தட்ப வந்த இக் கவின் காண இயைந்ததை;","என ஆங்கு,","அளி பெற்றேம், எம்மை நீ அருளினை; விளியாது","வேட்டோர் திறத்து விரும்பிய நின் பாகனும்","'நீட்டித்தாய்' என்று, கடாஅம், கடும் திண் தேர்,","பூட்டு விடாஅ நிறுத்து."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man of the town where the striped bee, after the blue lilies and the musk of the rutting elephant, spends the night in the jasmine of lovers' hair and forgets the pond it once haunted, that the gossip of his new wedding, his bathing and his dancing with other women was no grief to her but his own glory brought to her door at dawn, and that she has had his grace: his charioteer, crying that he is late, keeps the chariot yoked.","n":"Every refrain thanks him for an injury: the scent of his wedding garland reaching her at dawn, the ornaments he has not even rearranged, the beauty of his revelry she is allowed to see. The close turns the visit into the last insult, since the chariot is never unyoked and he is only passing between houses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 66","lb":"KL66","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["நீலம்","நீலம்",1,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",2,"pira","ullurai","bee"],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene","elephant"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",6,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["பாகனும்","பாகன்",23,"seythi","scene","charioteer"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","வண்டு","confirmed","1/1","the husband and the women he goes to"]],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:67":{"book":"kalittokai","num":67,"t":["கார் முற்றி, இணர் ஊழ்த்த கமழ் தோட்ட மலர் வேய்ந்து,","சீர் முற்றிப், புலவர் வாய்ச் சிறப்பு எய்தி, இரு நிலம்","தார் முற்றியது போலத், தகை பூத்த வையை தன்","நீர் முற்றி, மதில் பொரூஉம் பகை அல்லால், நேராதார்","போர் முற்று ஒன்று அறியாத புரிசை சூழ் புனல் ஊரன் -","நலம் தகை, எழில் உண் கண் நல்லார் தம் கோதையால்","அலைத்த, புண், வடுக் காட்டி, அன்பு இன்றி வரின் - எல்லா! -","புலப்பேன் யான் என்பேன்மன்? அந்நிலையே, அவன் காணின்,","கலப்பேன் என்னும், இக் கையறு நெஞ்சே;","கோடு எழில் அகல் அல்குல் கொடி அன்னார் முலை மூழ்கப்","பாடு அழி சாந்தினன், பண்பு இன்றி வரின் - எல்லா! -","ஊடுவேன் என்பேன்மன்? அந்நிலையே அவன் காணின்","கூடுவேன் என்னும், இக் கொள்கை இல் நெஞ்சே;","இனிப் புணர்ந்த எழில் நல்லார் இலங்கு எயிறு உறாஅலின்,","நனிச் சிவந்த வடுக் காட்டி, நாண் இன்றி வரின்- எல்லா! -","துனிப்பேன் யான் என்பேன்மன்? அந்நிலையே, அவன் காணின்,","தனித்தே தாழும், இத் தனி இல் நெஞ்சே;","என ஆங்கு,","பிறை புரை ஏர் நுதால்! தாம் எண்ணியவை எல்லாம்","துறைபோதல் ஒல்லுமோ - தூ ஆகாது ஆங்கே","அறைபோகு நெஞ்சு உடையார்க்கு?"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend that whenever the man of the walled town on the flooding Vaiyai comes back showing the wounds and marks of other women's garlands, paste and teeth, she says she will sulk, quarrel and turn cold, but the moment he is seen her helpless heart says it will join him; can those whose heart goes over to the enemy carry out what they planned?","n":"The three refrains set her vow (என்பேன்மன், 'I would say') against what the heart does at the sight of him. The close calls the heart a traitor (அறைபோகு நெஞ்சு), a word from siege warfare: the fortress is given up from inside. The opening praise of the Vaiyai town that no enemy but its own flood attacks quietly sets up that siege.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 67","lb":"KL67","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மலர்","மலர்",1,"maram","scene","flowers"],["கோதையால்","கோதை",6,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கொடி","கொடி",10,"maram","simile","creeper"],["சாந்தினன்","சாந்து",11,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"]],"en":[["river","வையை",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:68":{"book":"kalittokai","num":68,"t":["பொது மொழி பிறர்க்கு இன்றி முழுது ஆளும் செல்வர்க்கு","மதி மொழி இடல் மாலை வினைவர் போல், வல்லவர்","செது மொழி சீத்த செவி செறு ஆக,","முது மொழி நீராப், புலன் நா உழவர்","புது மொழி கூட்டுண்ணும், புரிசை சூழ், புனல் ஊர!","'ஊரன்மன் உரன் அல்லன், நமக்கு' என்ன, உடன் வாளாது,","ஓர் ஊர் தொக்கு இருந்த நின் பெண்டிருள் நேர் ஆகிக்,","களையா நின் குறி வந்து, எம் கதவம் சேர்ந்து அசைத்த கை","வளையின் வாய்விடல் மாலை மகளிரை நோவேமோ -","'கேள் அலன், நமக்கு அவன், குறுகன்மின்' என, மற்று எம்","தோளொடு பகைபட்டு நினை வாடு நெஞ்சத்தேம்?","'ஊடியார் நலம் தேம்ப, ஒடியெறிந்து, அவர் வயின்","மால் தீர்க்கும் அவன் மார்பு' என்று எழுந்த சொல் நோவேமோ -","முகை வாய்த்த முலை பாயக் குழைந்த நின் தார் எள்ள,","வகை வரிச் செப்பினுள் வைகிய கோதையேம்?","சேரியால் சென்று, நீ சேர்ந்த இல் வினாயினன்,","தேரொடு திரிதரும் பாகனைப் பழிப்பேமோ -","ஒலி கொண்ட சும்மையான் மண மனை குறித்து, எம் இல்","'பொலிக' எனப் புகுந்த நின் புலையனைக் கண்ட யாம்?","என ஆங்கு;","நனவினான் வேறு ஆகும் வேளா முயக்கம் -","மனை வரின், பெற்று உவந்து, மற்று எம் தோள் வாட,","'இனையர்' என உணர்ந்தார் என்று ஏக்கற்று, ஆங்குக்","கனவினான் எய்திய செல்வத்து அனையதே -","ஐய! எமக்கு நின் மார்பு."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man of the walled town, where poets plough with the tongue, water the field of the ear with old sayings and harvest new words like counsellors to a king, that she will not blame the women who rattled her door at his signal, nor the talk that his chest cures every sulk, nor the charioteer asking street by street for the house he entered, she who saw his bard come in blessing the wedding house: his embrace, when he comes home, is for her like wealth won in a dream, gone on waking.","n":"Each refrain asks 'shall I blame them?' and answers with what she herself has become: at war with her own shoulders, a garland left unworn in its casket. The opening simile of poets as tongue-ploughmen makes the town a place of fine words, which is the husband's own art.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","kanavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 68","lb":"KL68","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["வளையின்","வளை",9,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["தார்","தார்",14,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கோதையேம்","கோதை",15,"maram","simile","garland"],["பாகனைப்","பாகன்",17,"seythi","scene","charioteer"],["புலையனைக்","புலையன்",19,"seythi","scene","low-caste messenger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:69":{"book":"kalittokai","num":69,"t":["போது அவிழ் பனிப் பொய்கைப், புதுவது தளைவிட்ட","தாது சூழ் தாமரைத் தனி மலர் புறம் சேர்பு -","காதல் கொள் வதுவை நாள், கலிங்கத்துள் ஒடுங்கிய","மாதர் கொள் மான் நோக்கின் மடந்தை தன் துணை ஆக,","ஓது உடை அந்தணன் எரி வலம் செய்வான் போல்,","ஆய் தூவி அன்னம் தன் அணி நடைப் பெடையொடு -","மேதகத் திரிதரூஉம் மிகு புனல் நல் ஊர!","தெள் அரிச் சிலம்பு ஆர்ப்பத், தெருவின் கண் தாக்கி, நின்","உள்ளம் கொண்டு, ஒழித்தாளைக் குறை கூறிக் கொள நின்றாய்;","துணிந்தது பிறிது ஆகத், 'துணிவு இலள் இவள்' எனப்,","பணிந்தாய் போல் வந்து, ஈண்டுப், பயன் இல மொழிவாயோ?","பட்டுழி அறியாது, பாகனைத் தேரொடும்","விட்டு, அவள் வரல் நோக்கி, விருந்து ஏற்றுக்கொள நின்றாய்;","நெஞ்சத்த பிற ஆக, 'நிறை இலள் இவள்' என,","வஞ்சத்தான் வந்து ஈங்கு வலி அலைத்து ஈவாயோ?","இணர் ததை தண் காவின், இயன்ற நின் குறி வந்தாள்","புணர்வினில் புகன்று, ஆங்கே புனல் ஆடப் பண்ணியாய்;","தருக்கிய பிற ஆகத், 'தன் இலள் இவள்' எனச்","செருக்கினால் வந்து, ஈங்குச் சொல் உகுத்து ஈவாயோ?","என ஆங்கு,","தருக்கேம் பெரும! நின் நல்கல் விருப்புற்றுத்","தாழ்ந்தாய் போல் வந்து, தகவு இல செய்யாது,","சூழ்ந்தவை செய்து, மற்று எம்மையும் உள்ளுவாய் -","வீழ்ந்தார் விருப்பு அற்றக் கால்."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man of the flooded town, where the swan with its graceful mate goes round the lone lotus as a Veda-reciting brahmin goes round the fire with his shy bride, that he stood to be blamed by the girl who took his heart in the street, left his charioteer to wait for her and made her bathe with him in the stream, and now comes pretending humility to say that she is weak and without restraint: let him do what he has planned and remember her only when those who love him tire.","n":"Each refrain catches him doing one thing and saying another (துணிந்தது பிறிது ஆக, நெஞ்சத்த பிற ஆக). The opening wedding simile is pointed: the bird pair parades like a newly married couple, which is how he goes about with his new woman.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 69","lb":"KL69","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["தாமரைத்","தாமரை",2,"maram","scene","lotus"],["மான்","மான்",4,"maa","simile","deer"],["அந்தணன்","அந்தணன்",5,"seythi","simile","brahmin"],["அன்னம்","அன்னம்",6,"pul","ullurai","swan"],["சிலம்பு","சிலம்பு",8,"pira","ornament","anklet"],["பாகனைத்","பாகன்",12,"seythi","scene","charioteer"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","அன்னம்","not_confirmed","0/2","the husband and the girl he has taken up"],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:70":{"book":"kalittokai","num":70,"t":["மணி நிற மலர்ப் பொய்கை, மகிழ்ந்து ஆடும் அன்னம் தன்","அணி மிகு சேவலை அகல் அடை மறைத்தெனக்","கதுமெனக், காணாது, கலங்கி, அம் மடப் பெடை","மதி நிழல் நீர் உள் கண்டு, அது என உவந்து ஓடித்,","துன்னத் தன் எதிர் வரூஉம் துணை கண்டு, மிக நாணிப்,","பல் மலர் இடை புகூஉம் பழனம் சேர் ஊ! கேள்;","நலம் நீப்பத் துறந்து, எம்மை நல்காய் நீ விடுதலின்,","பல நாளும் படாத கண், பாயல் கொண்டு, இயைபவால்;","துணை மலர்க் கோதையார் வைகலும் பாராட்ட,","மண மனைத் ததும்பும் நின் மண முழ வந்து எடுப்புமே!","அகல நீ துறத்தலின், அழுது ஓவா உண் கண், எம்","புதல்வனை மெய் தீண்டப், பொருந்துதல் இயைபவால்;","நினக்கு ஒத்த நல்லாரை நெடு நகர்த் தந்து, நின்","தமர் பாடும் துணங்கையுள் அரவம் வந்து எடுப்புமே!","வாராய் நீ துறத்தலின், வருந்திய எமக்கு, ஆங்கே","நீர் இதழ் புலராக் கண் இமை கூம்ப இயைபவால்;","நேர் இழை நல்லாரை நெடு நகர்த் தந்து, நின்","தேர் பூண்ட நெடு நல் மான் தெள் மணி வந்து எடுப்புமே!","என ஆங்கு,","மெல்லியான் செவி முதல், மேல்வந்தான் காலை போல்,","எல்லாம் துயிலோ எடுப்புக; நின் பெண்டிர்,","இல்லின் எழீஇய யாழ் தழீஇ கல்லா வாய்ப்","பாணன் புகுதராக் கால்!"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man of the town where a young hen swan, losing sight of her mate behind a lotus leaf, runs gladly to the moon's reflection in the water and then, meeting her mate, hides ashamed among the flowers, that since he left her, the eyes that had not slept for days, that wept without end and touched only her son, can at last close, only to be woken by his wedding drums, the dance of his kin and the bells of his chariot horses; let all of it wake her, so long as his bard with the lute does not come into her house.","n":"The refrains reverse the usual complaint: his desertion is what lets her sleep, and his celebrations are what break it. Line 20 (மெல்லியான் செவி முதல் மேல்வந்தான் காலை போல்) is obscure; the sense of the close does not depend on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 70","lb":"KL70","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["அன்னம்","அன்னம்",1,"pul","scene","swan"],["பெடை","பெடை",3,"pul","iraicchi","hen swan"],["முழ","முழவு",10,"parai","scene","drum"],["மான்","மான்",18,"maa","scene","horse"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",22,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["பாணன்","பாணன்",23,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பெடை","not_confirmed","0/2","a wife who loses her husband even briefly is as anxious as the bird, so his long desertion is the greater cruelty"],["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:71":{"book":"kalittokai","num":71,"t":["விரி கதிர் மண்டிலம் வியல் விசும்பு ஊர்தரப்","புரி தலை தளை அவிழ்ந்த பூ அங்கண் புணர்ந்து ஆடி,","வரி வண்டு வாய் சூழும் வளம் கெழு பொய்கையுள் -","துனி சிறந்து இழிதரும் கண்ணின் நீர் அறல் வார,","இனிது அமர் காதலன் இறைஞ்சி தன் அடி சேர்பு,","நனி விரைந்து அளித்தலின், நகுபவள் முகம் போலப் -","பனி ஒரு திறம் வாரப், பாசடைத் தாமரைத்","தனி மலர் தளை விடூஉம் தண் துறை நல் ஊர!","'ஒரு நீ, பிறர் இல்லை, அவன் பெண்டிர்' என உரைத்துத்,","தேரொடும் தேற்றிய பாகன் வந்தீயான் கொல் -","ஓர் இல் தான் கொணர்ந்து உய்த்தார் புலவியுள் பொறித்த புண்","பாரித்துப் புணர்ந்த நின் பரத்தைமை காணிய?","'மடுத்து அவன் புகு வழி மறையேன்' என்று, யாழொடும்","எடுத்துச் சூள் பல உற்ற பாணன் வந்தீயான் கொல் -","அடுத்துத் தன் பொய் உண்டார்ப் புணர்ந்த நின் எருத்தின் கண்","எடுத்துக் கொள்வது போலும் தொடி வடு காணிய?","'தணந்தனை' எனக் கேட்டுத், தவறு ஓராது, எமக்கு நின்","குணங்களைப் பாராட்டும் தோழன் வந்தீயான் கொல் -","கணம் குழை நல்லவர் கதுப்பு அறல் அணைத் துஞ்சி,","அணங்கு போல் கமழும் நின் அலர் மார்பு காணிய?","என்று நின்,","தீரா முயக்கம் பெறுநர்ப் புலப்பவர்","யார்? - நீ வரு நாள் போல் அமைகுவம் யாம்; புக்கீமோ! -","மாரிக்கு அவாவுற்றுப் பீள் வாடும் நெல்லிற்கு, ஆங்கு","ஆராத் துவலை அளித்தது போலும், நீ","ஓர் யாட்டு ஒரு கால் வரவு."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man of the town where, as the sun climbs, the lone lotus opens with dew on one side like the face of a woman who weeps in a sulk and laughs when her husband bows to her feet, that the charioteer who swore she was his only wife, the bard who swore on his lute and the friend who praises his virtues have not come to see his wounds, bangle scars and scented chest; she will not sulk, he may come in, but his coming once a year is like a drizzle that cannot satisfy paddy withering in the sheath for want of rain.","n":"Each refrain names a go-between who vouched for him and asks why that witness does not come now to see the evidence on his body. The opening simile shows the reconciliation she will not perform: tears on one side, laughter on the other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 71","lb":"KL71","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"],["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["வண்டு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["தாமரைத்","தாமரை",7,"maram","simile","lotus"],["பாகன்","பாகன்",10,"seythi","scene","charioteer"],["யாழொடும்","யாழ்",13,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["பாணன்","பாணன்",14,"seythi","scene","bard"],["நெல்லிற்கு","நெல்",24,"unavu","simile","paddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:72":{"book":"kalittokai","num":72,"t":["'இணைபட நிவந்த நீல மென் சேக்கையுள்,","துணை புணர் அன்னத்தின் தூவி மெல் அணை அசைஇச்,","சேடு இயல் வள்ளத்துப் பெய்த பால் சில காட்டி,","ஊடும் மெல் சிறு கிளி உணர்ப்பவள் முகம் போலப் ,","புது நீர புதல் ஒற்றப், புணர் திரைப் பிதிர் மல்க,","மதி நோக்கி அலர் வீத்த ஆம்பல் வால் மலர் நண்ணிக்,","கடி கயத் தாமரைக் கமழ் முகை, கரை மாவின்","வடி தீண்ட, வாய் விடூஉம் வயல் அணி நல் ஊர!","கண்ணி, நீ கடி கொண்டார்க் கனைதொறும், யாம் அழப்,","பண்ணினால் களிப்பிக்கும் பாணன் காட்டு என்றானோ -","'பேணான்' என்று உடன்றவர் உகிர் செய்த வடுவினான்,","மேல் நாள் நின் தோள் சேர்ந்தார் நகை சேர்ந்த இதழினை?","நாடி நின் தூது ஆடித், துறைச் செல்லாள், ஊரவர்","ஆடை கொண்டு, ஒலிக்கும், நின் புலைத்தி காட்டு என்றாளோ -","கூடியார் புனல் ஆடப் புணை ஆய மார்பினில்,","ஊடியார் எறிதர ஒளி விட்ட அரக்கினை?","வெறிது நின் புகழ்களை வேண்டார் இல் எடுத்து ஏத்தும்","அறிவு உடை அந்தணன் அவளைக் காட்டு என்றானோ -","களி பட்டார் கமழ் கோதை கயம்பட்ட உருவின் மேல்","குறி பெற்றார் குரல் கூந்தல் கோடு உளர்ந்த துகளினை?","என ஆங்கு,","செறிவுற்றேம்; எம்மை நீ செறிய, அறிவுற்று,","அழிந்து உகு நெஞ்சத்தேம், அல்லல் உழப்பக்","கழிந்தவை உள்ளாது, கண்ட இடத்தே,","அழிந்து நின் பேணிக் கொளலின் இழிந்ததோ -","இந் நோய் உழத்தல் எமக்கு?"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man of the town where the lotus bud opens when a mango's young fruit touches it, like the face of a girl on a swan-down pillow coaxing a sulking parrot with a little milk in a bowl, that his bard, his washerwoman go-between and his brahmin flatterer surely sent him to show her his nail-scarred lip, the lac on his chest and the dust of women's hair; she is crushed, but enduring this pain is less base for her than forgetting the past and welcoming him whenever he appears.","n":"The three go-betweens (bard, washerwoman, brahmin) are named one per refrain, each mocked as if he had ordered the husband to bring the evidence home. Her final choice is dignity over reconciliation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 72","lb":"KL72","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["அன்னத்தின்","அன்னம்",2,"pul","scene","swan down"],["கிளி","கிளி",4,"pul","simile","parrot"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",6,"maram","scene","water lily"],["தாமரைக்","தாமரை",7,"maram","simile","lotus"],["மாவின்","மா",7,"maram","scene","mango"],["பாணன்","பாணன்",10,"seythi","scene","bard"],["புலைத்தி","புலைத்தி",14,"seythi","scene","washerwoman"],["அந்தணன்","அந்தணன்",18,"seythi","scene","brahmin"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:73":{"book":"kalittokai","num":73,"t":["அகல் துறை அணிபெறப், புதலொடு தாழ்ந்த","பகன்றைப் பூ உற நீண்ட பாசடைத் தாமரை,","கண் பொர ஒளி விட்ட வெள்ளிய வள்ளத்தால்","தண் கமழ் நறும் தேறல் உண்பவள் முகம் போல,","வண் பிணி தளை விடூஉம் வயல் அணி நல் ஊர!","'நோதக்காய்' என நின்னை நொந்தீவார் இல் வழித்,","'தீது இலேன் யான்' எனத் தேற்றிய வருதிமன் -","ஞெகிழ் தொடி இளையவர் இடை முலைத் தாது சோர்ந்து,","இதழ் வனப்பு இழந்த நின் கண்ணி வந்து உரையாக்கால்?","கனற்றி நீ செய்வது கடிந்தீவார் இல்வழி,","'மனத்தில் தீது இலன்' என மயக்கிய வருதிமன் -","அலமரல் உண் கண்ணார் ஆய் கோதை குழைத்த நின்","மலர் மார்பின் மறுப்பட்ட சாந்தம் வந்து உரையாக்கால்?","என்னை நீ செய்யினும், உரைத்தீவார் இல் வழி,","முன் அடிப் பணிந்து, எம்மை உணர்த்திய வருதிமன் -","நிரை தொடி நல்லவர் துணங்கையுள் தலைக் கொள்ளக்,","கரை இடைக் கிழிந்த நின் காழகம் வந்து உரையாக்கால்?","என ஆங்கு,","மண்டு நீர் ஆரா மலி கடல் போலும் நின்","தண்டாப் பரத்தை தலைக் கொள்ள, நாளும்","புலத் தகைப் பெண்டிரைத் தேற்ற; மற்று யாம் எனின்,","தோலாமோ நின் பொய் மருண்டு?"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man of the town where the lotus opens like the face of a woman drinking cool wine from a shining silver bowl, that he used to come assuring her he was blameless whenever no one accused him, as long as his crushed garland, the smeared paste on his chest and the cloth torn in the women's dance did not speak; his whoring, endless as the sea that no water fills, keeps him busy soothing sulking women every day, and she will not be fooled again by his lies.","n":"The refrains depend on what she finds on him, not on what anyone tells her: the garland, the paste and the torn cloth are the witnesses. The last line (தோலாமோ நின் பொய் மருண்டு) can be read as 'shall I lose, bewildered by your lies?' and is taken here as a refusal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 73","lb":"KL73","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பகன்றைப்","பகன்றை",2,"maram","scene","pakanrai flower"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",2,"maram","simile","lotus"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",4,"unavu","simile","wine"],["கண்ணி","கண்ணி",9,"maram","ornament","garland"],["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",13,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["கடல்","கடல்",19,"pira","simile","sea"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:74":{"book":"kalittokai","num":74,"t":["பொய்கைப் பூ புதிது உண்ட வரி வண்டு கழி பூத்த","நெய்தல் தாது அமர்ந்து ஆடிப், பாசடைச் சேப்பினுள்","செய்து இயற்றியது போல வயல் பூத்த தாமரை,","மை தபு, கிளர் கொட்டை மாண் பதி படர்தரூஉம்,","கொய்குழை அகை காஞ்சித் துறை அணி நல் ஊர!","'அன்பு இலன், அறன் இலன், எனப்படான்' என ஏத்தி,","நின் புகழ் பல பாடும் பாணனும் ஏமுற்றான்;","நஞ்சு உயிர் செகுத்தலும் அறிந்து உண்டாங்கு, அளி இன்மை","கண்டுநின் மொழி தேறும் பெண்டிரும் ஏமுற்றார்.","முன்பகல் தலைக்கூடி, நன்பகல் அவள் நீத்துப்,","பின்பகல் பிறர்த் தேரும் நெஞ்சமும் ஏமுற்றாய்;","என ஆங்கு;","'கிண்கிணி மணித் தாரொடு ஒலித்து ஆர்ப்ப, ஒண் தொடிப்","பேர் அமர்க் கண்ணார்க்கும் படு வலை இது' என,","ஊரவர் உடன் நகத் திரிதரும்","தேர் ஏமுற்றன்று, நின்னினும் பெரிதே!"],"p":"","r":"To the man of the town where the striped bee, newly fed on pond flowers and the neythal of the backwater, moves on to the field lotus and its stamens, the wife says that the bard who praises him as loving and just is deluded, the women who trust his words though they see his lack of grace are deluded as those who drink poison knowing it kills, he is deluded who joins one woman at forenoon, leaves her at noon and looks for others in the afternoon, and his chariot, that the town laughs at as a net for big-eyed women, is more deluded than he.","n":"The four refrains share one verb (ஏமுற்றான், ஏமுற்றார், ஏமுற்றாய், ஏமுற்றன்று), and the last falls on the chariot, an object, as if it were the most besotted of all. The morning-noon-afternoon schedule of line 10 and 11 mirrors the bee's flower-to-flower course.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","pedhaimai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 74","lb":"KL74","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வண்டு","வண்டு",1,"pira","ullurai","bee"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","scene","neythal lily"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",3,"maram","simile","lotus"],["காஞ்சித்","காஞ்சி",5,"maram","scene","kaanji tree"],["பாணனும்","பாணன்",7,"seythi","scene","bard"],["நஞ்சு","நஞ்சு",8,"pira","simile","poison"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","வண்டு","confirmed","1/1","the husband and his succession of women"]],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:75":{"book":"kalittokai","num":75,"t":["நீர் ஆர் செறுவில் நெய்தலொடு நீடிய","நேர் இதழ் ஆம்பல் நிரை இதழ் கொண்மார்,","சீர் ஆர் சே இழை ஒலிப்ப, ஓடும்","ஓரை மகளிர் ஓதை வெரீஇ எழுந்து,","ஆரல் ஆர்கை அம் சிறைத் தொழுதி","உயர்ந்த பொங்கர் உயர் மரம் ஏறி,","அமர்க்கண் மகளிர் அலப்பிய அந் நோய் ,","தமர்க்கு உரைப்பன போல், பல் குரல் பயிற்றும்,","உயர்ந்த போரின் ஒலி நல் ஊரன்","புதுவோர் புணர்தல் வெய்யன் ஆயின்,","வதுவை நாளால் வைகலும், அஃது யான்","நோவேன், தோழி! நோவாய் நீ, என","என் பார்த்து உறுவோய்! கேள், இனித் தெற்றென;","'எல்லினை வருதி, எவன் குறித்தனை?' எனச்","சொல்லாதிருப்பேன் ஆயின், ஒல்லென,","விரி உளைக் கலி மான் தேரொடு வந்த","விருந்து எதிர்கோடலின், மறப்பல், என்றும்;","'வாடிய பூவொடு வாரல், எம்மனை' என","ஊடி இருப்பேன் ஆயின், நீடாது,","அச்சு ஆறு ஆக உணரிய வருபவன்","பொய்ச் சூள் அஞ்சிப் புலவேன் ஆகுவல்;","'பகல் ஆண்டு அல்கினை, பரத்த' என்று யான்","இகலியிருப்பேன் ஆயின், தான் தன்","முதல்வன் பெரும் பெயர் முறை உளிப் பெற்ற","புதல்வன் புல்லிப் பொய் துயில் துஞ்சும்;","ஆங்க ,","விருந்து எதிர் கொள்ளவும், பொய்ச் சூள் அஞ்சவும்,","அரும் பெறல் புதல்வனை முயங்கக் காணவும்,","ஆங்கு அவிந்து ஒழியும், என் புலவி தாங்காது,","அவ் அவ் இடத்தான் அவை அவை காணப் -","பூங் கண் மகளிர் புனை நலம் சிதைக்கும்","மாய மகிழ்நன் பரத்தைமை","நோவென், தோழி! கடன் நமக்கு எனவே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend, who watches her and grieves, that she does not grieve that the man of the rich town, where birds startled by girls at play fly into the trees crying as if to tell their kin what the girls did to them, loves new women as if every day were a wedding: when she would ask why he comes late, guests arrive and she forgets; when she sulks at his faded flowers, he comes to soothe her and she fears his false oaths; when she calls him a whoremonger, he hugs their son and pretends to sleep; so her sulk dies each time, and bearing it, she says, is her duty.","n":"Three moves defeat her sulk: the duty to receive guests, the fear of making him swear falsely, and the son he holds as a shield while feigning sleep (பொய் துயில்). The birds that cry to their kin about the girls' raid are her opposite: they complain, she will not. The last line (நோவென்) can be read 'I grieve' or 'I do not grieve'; the reading follows line 12 (நோவேன்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","virundhu","makkal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 75","lb":"KL75","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெய்தலொடு","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","scene","neythal lily"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",2,"maram","scene","water lily"],["ஆரல்","ஆரல்",5,"pira","scene","eel fish"],["தொழுதி","தொழுதி",5,"pul","simile","flock of birds"],["மான்","மான்",16,"maa","scene","horse"],["பூவொடு","பூ",18,"maram","ornament","faded flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:76":{"book":"kalittokai","num":76,"t":["புனை இழை நோக்கியும், புனல் ஆடப் புறம் சூழ்ந்தும்,","அணி வரி தைஇயும், நம் இல் வந்து வணங்கியும்,","நினையுபு வருந்தும் இந் நெடுந்தகை திறத்து, இவ் ஊர்","'இனையள்' என்று எடுத்து ஓதற்கு அனையையோ, நீ? என","வினவுதி ஆயின், விளங்கு இழாய்! கேள், இனி;","'செவ் விரல் சிவப்பு ஊரச், சேண் சென்றாய்' என்று, அவன்","பௌவ நீர்ச் சாய்க் கொழுதிப் பாவை தந்தனைத்தற்கே -","'கௌவை நோய் உற்றவர், காணாது கடுத்த சொல்","ஒவ்வா' என்று உணராய், நீ ஒரு நிலையே உரைத்ததை?","ஒடுங்கி, யாம் புகல் ஒல்லேம் பெயர்தர, அவன் கண்டு,","நெடும் கய மலர் வாங்கி, நெறித்துத் தந்தனைத்தற்கோ -","விடுந்தவர் விரகு இன்றி எடுத்த சொல் பொய் ஆகக்","கடிந்ததும் இலையாய், நீ கழறிய வந்ததை?","'வரி தேற்றாய், நீ' என, வணங்கு இறை அவன் பற்றித்,","தெரி வேய்த் தோள் கரும்பு எழுதித் தொய்யில் செய்தனைத்தற்கோ -","புரிபு நம் ஆயத்தார் பொய் ஆக எடுத்த சொல்","உரிது என உணராய், நீ உலமந்தாய் போன்றதை?","என ஆங்கு,","அரிது இனி, ஆய் இழாய்! அது தேற்றல், புரிபு ஒருங்கு,","அன்று நம் வதுவையுள் நமர் செய்வது இன்று, ஈங்கே,","தான் நயந்து இருந்தது இவ் ஊர் ஆயின், எவன் கொலோ -","நாம் செயற்பாலது இனி?"],"p":"","r":"The friend answers the girl who blames her for the town's talk about the man who gazed at her ornaments, followed her bathing, painted lines on her and bowed at their house: the talk came from his own attentions to the girl, the sedge doll from the sea, the flowers from the pond, the sugarcane drawn on her shoulder, yet she would not see that gossip is false and scolded the friend instead; now, the friend says, if the town has already done what their kin would do at the wedding, what is left for them to do?","n":"The close turns the gossip into a wedding announcement: the town has published the match before the families have, so the only step left is marriage. The speaker and addressee are inferred; the poem's 'you' and 'we' could also be read the other way round, with the girl answering the friend.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 76","lb":"KL76","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சாய்க்","சாய்",7,"maram","scene","sedge"],["மலர்","மலர்",11,"maram","gift_food","pond flower"],["கரும்பு","கரும்பு",15,"maram","ornament","sugarcane design"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:77":{"book":"kalittokai","num":77,"t":["இணை இரண்டு இயைந்து ஒத்த முகை நாப்பண், பிறிது யாதும்","துணை இன்றித் தளை விட்ட, தாமரை தனி மலர்,","திரு முகம் இறைஞ்சினள், வீழ்பவற்கு, இனைபவள்","அரி மதர் மழைக் கண் நீர் அலர் முலை மேல் தெறிப்ப போல்,","தகை மலர்ப் பழனத்த புள் ஒற்ற, ஒசிந்து ஒல்கி,","மிக நனி சேர்ந்த அம் முகை மிசை அம் மலர்","அக இதழ்த் தண் பனி உறைத்தரும் ஊர! கேள்:","தண் தளிர்த் தகை பூத்த தாது எழில் நலம் செலக்","கொண்டு, நீ மாறிய கவின் பெறல் வேண்டேன்மன் -","உண்டாதல் சாலா என் உயிர் சாதல் உணர்ந்து, 'நின்","பெண்டு' எனப் பிறர் கூறும் பழி மாறப் பெறுகற்பின்?","பொன் எனப் பசந்த, கண் போது எழில் நலம் செலத்,","தொல் நலம் இழந்த கண் துயில் பெறல் வேண்டேன்மன் -","நின் அணங்கு உற்றவர் நீ செய்யும் கொடுமைகள்","என் உழை வந்து, நொந்து உரையாமை பெறுகற்பின்?;","மாசு அற மண்ணுற்ற மணி ஏசும் இருங் கூந்தல்","வீ சேர்ந்து வண்டு ஆர்க்கும் கவின் பெறல் வேண்டேன்மன் -","நோய் சேர்ந்த திறம் பண்ணி, நின் பாணன், எம்மனை","நீ சேர்ந்த இல் வினாய் வாராமை பெறுகற்பின்?","ஆங்க,","'கடைஇய நின் மார்பு தோயலம்' என்னும்,","இடையும், நிறையும் எளிதோ - நின் காணின்","கடவுபு, கை தங்கா, நெஞ்சு என்னும் தம்மோடு","உடன் வாழ் பகை உடையார்க்கு?"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man of the town where a lone lotus between two buds, touched by a bird, drops dew on them like a woman who bows her head grieving for her lover and lets tears fall on her breasts, that she does not ask back the beauty, the sleep and the flowered hair he took from her, if only she could be spared the taunt 'his woman', the complaints of the women he wrongs and his bard asking at her door for his other houses; but can she keep the resolve not to touch his chest, when her heart, an enemy living with her, rushes out at the sight of him?","n":"The three refrains give up her own losses to ask only that the public shame stop. The close names the heart as an enemy who shares her house (உடன் வாழ் பகை), so her restraint (நிறை) is lost before any battle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","nirai","oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 77","lb":"KL77","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",2,"maram","simile","lotus"],["புள்","புள்",5,"pul","scene","bird"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",17,"pira","scene","bee"],["பாணன்","பாணன்",18,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:78":{"book":"kalittokai","num":78,"t":["பல் மலர்ப் பழனத்த பாசடைத் தாமரை","இன் மலர் இமிர்பு ஊதும் துணை புணர் இரும் தும்ப,","உண் துறை உடைந்த பூப் புனல் சாய்ப்பப், புலந்து ஊடிப்","பண்பு உடை நல் நாட்டுப் பகை தலை வந்தென,","அது கைவிட்டு அகன்று ஒரீஇக், காக்கிற்பான் குடை நீழல்","பதி படர்ந்து, இறைகொள்ளும் குடி போலப் - பிறிதும் ஒரு","பொய்கை தேர்ந்து அலமரும் பொழுதினான், மொய் தப","இறை பகை தணிப்ப அக் குடி பதிப் பெயர்ந்தாங்கு,","நிறை புனல் நீங்க வந்து, அத்தும்பி அம் மலர்ப்","பறை தவிர்பு அசைவிடூஉம் பாய் புனல் நல் ஊர!","'நீக்கும்கால் நிறம் சாய்ந்து, புணரும்கால் புகழ் பூத்து","நாம் கொண்ட குறிப்பு, இவள் நலம்' என்னும் தகையோ தான் -","எரி இதழ் சோர்ந்து உக ஏதிலார்ப் புணர்ந்தமை","கரி கூறும் கண்ணியை, ஈங்கு எம் இல் வருவதை?","'சுடர் நோக்கி மலர்ந்து, ஆங்கே படின் கூம்பும் மலர் போல், என்","தொடர் நீப்பின், தொகும், இவள் நலம்' என்னும் தகையோ தான் -","அலர் நாணிக் கரந்த நோய் கைம்மிகப், பிறர் கூந்தல்","மலர் நாறும் மார்பினை, ஈங்கு எம் இல் வருவதை?","'பெயின் நந்தி, வறப்பின் சாம், புலத்திற்குப் பெயல் போல் யான்","செலின் நந்திச், செறின் சாம்பும், இவள்' என்னும் தகையோ தான்-","முடி உற்ற கோதை போல் யாம் வாட, ஏதிலார்","தொடி உற்ற வடுக் காட்டி, ஈங்கு எம் இல் வருவதை?","ஆங்க,","ஐய! அமைந்தன்று; அனைத்து ஆகப் புக்கீமோ,","வெய்யாரும் வீழ்வாரும் வேறு ஆகக், கையின்","முகை மலர்ந்தன்ன முயக்கில் தகை இன்றே,","தண் பனி வைகல் எமக்கு?"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man of the town where a pair of bees, angry when the flood bends their lotus, leave for another pond as people flee to the king's shade when an enemy comes and return when he has quelled it, that he comes to her house wearing the proof of other women while claiming that her beauty withers when he leaves and blooms when he returns, like a flower that opens to the sun or a field that lives by rain; enough: those who desire him and those who love him are different, and his embrace, forced open like a bud in the hand, has no charm for her in the cold dew.","n":"The three refrains quote his own boast about her dependence (each a simile of his: her beauty, the sun-flower, the rain-field) and answer each with the evidence he carries. The puram simile of the opening, a people sheltering under a king, makes the bee's return a return of subjects, which is how he sees her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 78","lb":"KL78","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",1,"maram","scene","lotus"],["தும்ப","தும்பி",2,"pira","ullurai","bee"],["மலர்","மலர்",15,"maram","simile","flower"],["கோதை","கோதை",21,"maram","simile","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","தும்ப","not_confirmed","0/1","the husband who leaves the wife and returns at his convenience"]],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:79":{"book":"kalittokai","num":79,"t":["புள் இமிழ் அகல் வயல் ஒலி செந்நெல் இடைப் பூத்த","முள் அரைத் தாமரை முழு முதல் சாய்த்து, அதன்","வள் இதழ் உற நீடி, வயங்கிய ஒரு கதிர்,","அவை புகழ் அரங்கின் மேல் ஆடுவாள் அணி நுதல்","வகை பெற செரீஇய வயந்தகம் போல், தோன்றும்","தகைபெறு கழனி அம் தண் துறை ஊர! கேள்:","அணியொடு வந்து ஈங்கு எம் புதல்வனைக் கொள்ளாதி;","மணி புரை செவ் வாய் நின் மார்பு அகலம் நனைப்பதால்,","'தோய்ந்தாரை அறிகுவேன், யான்' எனக், கமழும் நின்","சாந்தினால் குறி கொண்டாள் சாய்குவள் அல்லளோ?","புல்லல் எம் புதல்வனைப்; புகல் அகல் நின் மார்பில்","பல் காழ் முத்து அணி ஆரம் பற்றினன் பரிவானால்,","மாண் இழை மட நல்லார் முயக்கத்தை நின் மார்பில்","பூணினால் குறி கொண்டாள் புலக்குவள் அல்லளோ?","கண்டே எம் புதல்வனைக் கொள்ளாதி; நின் சென்னி","வண்டு இமிர் வகை இணர் வாங்கினன் பரிவானால்,","'நண்ணியார்க் காட்டுவது இது' எனக் கமழும் நின்","கண்ணியால் குறி கொண்டாள் காய்குவள் அல்லளோ?","என ஆங்கு,","பூங் கண் புதல்வனைப் பொய் பல பாராட்டி,","நீங்காய் இகவாய் நெடும் கடை நில்லாதி;","ஆங்கே அவர் வயின் சென்றீ - அணி சிதைப்பான் -","ஈங்கு எம் புதல்வனைத் தந்து."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man of the town, where one bright ear of paddy leaning on a lotus petal looks like the jewel on a dancer's brow, not to come here and take up their son: the boy's wet mouth will wash away the sandal paste, his hands will pull the pearl chain and the garland by which the other women marked him, and they will be angry; let him stop praising the boy with lies, leave the doorway, give the son back and go to them.","n":"Her argument is mock concern for the other women: the child would undo their marks. So the son, usually the way back into the house, becomes the reason to send him away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 79","lb":"KL79","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["புள்","புள்",1,"pul","scene","birds"],["செந்நெல்","செந்நெல்",1,"unavu","scene","red paddy"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",2,"maram","scene","lotus"],["கதிர்","கதிர்",3,"unavu","simile","paddy ear"],["சாந்தினால்","சாந்து",10,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["முத்து","முத்து",12,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["இணர்","இணர்",16,"maram","ornament","flower cluster"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:80":{"book":"kalittokai","num":80,"t":["நயம் தலை மாறுவார் மாறுக; மாறாக்,","கயம் தலை மின்னும் கதிர் விடு முக் காழ்ப்,","பயந்த எம் கண் ஆர யாம் காண நல்கித் -","திகழ் ஒளி முத்து அங்கு அரும்பு ஆகத் தைஇப்","பவழம் புனைந்த பருதி சுமப்பக்","கவழம் அறியா நின் கை புனை வேழம்","புரி புனை பூங் கயிற்றின் பைபய வாங்கி,","அரி புனை புட்டிலின் ஆங்கண் ஈர்த்து, ஈங்கே","வருக! - எம் பாக மகன்!","கிளர் மணி ஆர்ப்ப ஆர்ப்பச் சாஅய்ச் சாஅய்ச் செல்லும்","தளர் நடை காண்டல் இனிது; மற்று இன்னாதே,","'உளம்' என்னா நுந்தை மாட்டு எவ்வம் உழப்பார்","வளை நெகிழ்பு யாம் காணும் கால்;","ஐய! காமரு நோக்கினை, 'அத்தத்தா, என்னும் நின்","தே மொழி கேட்டல் இனிது; மற்று இன்னாதே,","உய்வு இன்றி நுந்தை நலன் உணச் சாஅய்ச் சாஅய்மார்","எவ்வ நோய் யாம் காணும் கால்;","ஐய! 'திங்கள் குழவி! வருக!' என, யான் நின்னை","அம்புலி காட்டல் இனிது; மற்று இன்னாதே,","நல்காது, நுந்தை புறம் மாறப்பட்டவர்","அல்குல் வரி யாம் காணும் கால்;","ஐய! எம் காதில் கனம் குழை வாங்கிப், பெயர்தொறும்,","போது இல் வறும் கூந்தல், கொள்வதை, நின்னை யாம் -","ஏதிலார் கண் சாய - நுந்தை வியல் மார்பில்","தாது தேர் வண்டின் கிளை பாடத், தைஇய","கோதை பரிபு ஆடக், காண்கும்."],"p":"","r":"The mother tells her little son, whom she calls her charioteer as he drags his toy elephant, that his tottering walk, his sweet 'attatta' and showing him the moon are a joy, but a pain when she sees the loosened bangles, the wasting and the stretch-marks of the women his father has used and turned his back on; still, she says, when he pulls the earring from her ear she will watch him swing on his father's broad chest among the garland and the bees, while the strangers' eyes look away.","n":"The pity is aimed at the father's discarded women, whose state warns her of her own. Lines 1 to 3 (the three-strand ornament, கயம் தலை) are difficult and the reading of them is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","parathai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 80","lb":"KL80","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"],["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["முத்து","முத்து",4,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["பவழம்","பவழம்",5,"pira","ornament","coral"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",6,"maa","scene","toy elephant"],["வண்டின்","வண்டு",25,"pira","scene","bee"],["கோதை","கோதை",26,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:81":{"book":"kalittokai","num":81,"t":["மை அற விளங்கிய மணி மருள் அவ் வாய் தன்","மெய் பெறா மழலையின் விளங்கு பூண் நனைத்தரப்,","பொலம் பிறையுள் தாழ்ந்த புனை வினை உருள் கலன்,","நலம்பெறு கமழ் சென்னி, நகையொடு துயல்வர,","உரு எஞ்சாது இடை காட்டும் உடை கழல் அம் துகில்,","அரி பொலி கிண்கிணி ஆர்ப்பு ஓவா அடி தட்பப்,","பாலோடு அலர்ந்த முலை மறந்து, முற்றத்துக்","கால் வல் தேர் கையின் இயக்க, நடை பயிற்றா,","ஆல் அமர் செல்வன் அணி சால் பெரு விறல்","போல, வரும் என் உயிர்!","பெரும! விருந்தொடு கைதூவா எம்மையும் உள்ளாய்;","பெருந் தெருவில் கொண்டாடி ஞாயர் பயிற்றத்,","திருந்துபு நீ கற்ற சொற்கள் யாம் கேட்ப,","மருந்து ஓவா நெஞ்சிற்கு அமிழ்தம் அயின்றற்றாப்","பெருந்தகாய்! கூறு சில.","எல் இழாய்! சேய் நின்று, நாம் கொணர்ந்த பாணன் சிதைந்து, ஆங்கே","வாய் ஓடி, 'ஏனாதிப்பாடியம்' என்றற்றா,","'நோய் நாம் தணிக்கும் மருந்து' எனப் பாராட்ட,","ஓவாது அடுத்து அடுத்து, 'அத்தத்தா!' என்பான் மாண","வேய் மென் தோள் வேய்த் திறம் சேர்த்தலும், மற்று இவன்","வாய் உள்ளின் போகான் அரோ!","உள்ளி, உழையே ஒருங்கு படை விடக்","கள்ளர் படர்தந்தது போலத், தாம் எம்மை","எள்ளுமார் வந்தாரே, ஈங்கு!","ஏதப்பாடு எண்ணிப், புரிசை வியல் உள்ளோர்","கள்வரைக் காணாது, 'கண்டேம்' என்பார் போலச்","சேய் நின்று, செய்யாத சொல்லிச் சினவல்; நின்","ஆணை கடக்கிற்பார் யார்?","அதிர்வு இல் படிறு எருக்கி, வந்து என் மகன் மேல்,","முதிர் பூண் முலை பொருத ஏதிலாள் முச்சி","உதிர் துகள் உக்க நின் ஆடை ஒலிப்ப,","எதிர் வளி நின்றாய், நீ செல்;","இனி! 'எல்லா! யாம், தீது இலேம்' என்று தெளிப்பவும், கைந் நீவி","யாது ஒன்றும் எம் கண் மறுத்தரவு இல் ஆயின்,","மேதக்க எந்தை பெயரனை யாம் கொள்வேம்,","தாவா விருப்பொடு கன்று யாத்த உழிச் செல்லும்","ஆ போல் படர் தக நாம்!"],"p":"","r":"In a dialogue, the wife watches their son toddle in the yard pulling his toy chariot, bright as the young son of the god under the banyan, and asks him to repeat the words the 'mothers' in the big street taught him; the husband pleads that the bard babbled them and the boy will not let them go, she answers that he came like thieves after the army has left and must go, standing in the wind with another woman's hair-dust on his cloth; and he ends that, if nothing is refused, he will take up his father's namesake and come like a cow to where its calf is tied.","n":"The street women (ஞாயர், 'mothers') have taught the child words, so the boy carries the other houses into hers in his speech. The speakers shift without notice; the division of lines 16 to 37 between wife and husband is a reading, and ஏனாதிப்பாடியம் (line 17) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 81","lb":"KL81","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["கிண்கிணி","கிண்கிணி",6,"pira","ornament","anklet bells"],["ஆல் அமர் செல்வன்","ஆல் அமர் செல்வன்",9,"deyvam","simile","the god under the banyan"],["பாணன்","பாணன்",16,"seythi","scene","bard"],["கன்று","கன்று",36,"maa","simile","calf"],["ஆ","ஆ",37,"maa","simile","cow"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:82":{"book":"kalittokai","num":82,"t":["ஞாலம் வறம் தீரப் பெய்யக், குணக்கு ஏர்பு","காலத்தில் தோன்றிய கொண்மூ போல், எம் முலை","பாலொடு வீங்கத் தவ நெடிது ஆயினை,","புத்தேளிர் கோட்டம் வலம் செய்து இவனொடு","புக்க வழி எல்லாம் கூறு;","கூறுவேன்; மேயாயே போல வினவி, வழிமுறைக்","காயாமை வேண்டுவல் யான்;","காயேம்;","மடக் குறு மாக்களோடு ஓரை அயரும்","அடக்கம் இல் போழ்தின் கண், தந்தை காமுற்ற","தொடக்கத்துத் தாய் உழை புக்காற்கு, அவளும்","மருப்புப் பூண் கையுறை ஆக அணிந்து -","'பெருமான்! நகை முகம் காட்டு' என்பாள், கண்ணீர்","சொரி முத்தம் காழ் சோர்வ போன்றன; மற்றும்,","வழிமுறைத் தாய் உழைப் புக்காற்கு, அவளும்","மயங்கு நோய் தாங்கி, மகன் எதிர் வந்து,","முயங்கினள் முத்தினள் நோக்கி, நினைந்தே,","'நினக்கு யாம் யாரேம் ஆகுதும்?' என்று,","வனப்பு உறக் கொள்வன நாடி அணிந்தனள்,","ஆங்கே, 'அரி மதர் உண் கண் பசப்ப நோய் செய்யும்","பெருமான் பரத்தைமை ஒவ்வாத' என்றாள்;","அவட்கு இனிது ஆகி விடுத்தனன் போகித்","தலைக் கொண்டு நம்மொடு காயும் மற்று ஈது ஓர்","புலத் தகை புத்தேள் இல் புக்கான்- அலைக்கு ஒரு","கோல் தா; நினக்கு அவள் யார் ஆகும்? - எல்லா!","வருந்தி யாம் நோய் கூர, நுந்தையை என்றும்","பருந்து எறிந்தற்று ஆகக் கொள்ளும்; கொண்டாங்கே","தொடியும் உகிரும் படை ஆக நுந்தை","கடி உடை மார்பின் சிறு கண்ணும் உட்காள்,","வடுவும் குறித்தாங்கே செய்யும்; விடு, இனி;","அன்ன பிறவும், பெருமான் அவள் வயின்","துன்னுதல் ஓம்பித், திறவது இல் முன்னி, நீ","ஐயம் இல்லாதவர் இல் ஒழிய, எம் போலக்","கையாறு உடையவர் இல் அல்லால் செல்லல்;","அமைந்தது; இனி நின் தொழில்."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks the woman who took their son round the temples why she kept him so long while her breasts swelled with milk, and hears that the boy went into the house of the woman his father first loved, who adorned him with ivory and wept pearls of tears, and then into the house of the next, who kissed him, asked 'what am I to you?' and called his father's whoring unworthy; the wife cries for a stick, tells the boy how that woman seizes his father like a kite and scars him with nails, and orders the attendant to keep the boy from such houses: that is her job.","n":"The father's women speak to the son as stepmothers would, and one of them blames the father in the wife's own words. The poem is the wife's jealousy turned on her husband's past through what her child saw.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","makkal","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 82","lb":"KL82","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","sevili",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["vekuli","குடிகோள்"]],"k":[["கொண்மூ","கொண்மூ",2,"pira","simile","cloud"],["மருப்புப்","மருப்பு",12,"maa","ornament","ivory"],["முத்தம்","முத்தம்",14,"pira","simile","pearls"],["பருந்து","பருந்து",27,"pul","simile","kite"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:83":{"book":"kalittokai","num":83,"t":["பெரு திரு நிலைஇய வீங்கு சோற்று அகல் மனைப்,","பொருந்து நோன் கதவு ஒற்றிப் புலம்பி, யாம் உலமர","இளையவர் தழூஉ ஆடும் எக்கர் வாய் வியன் தெருவின்","விளையாட்டிக் கொண்டு வரற்கு எனச் சென்றாய்,","உளைவு இலை; ஊட்டலென் தீம் பால் பெருகும் அளவு எல்லாம்","நீட்டித்த காரணம் என்?","கேட்டீ -","பெரு மடல் பெண்ணைப் பிணர்த் தோட்டுப் பைங் குரும்பைக்","குட வாய்க் கொடிப் பின்னல் வாங்கித், தளரும்","பெரு மணித் திண் தேர்க் குறுமக்கள் நாப்பண்,","அகல் நகர் மீள்தருவான் ஆகப், புரி ஞெகிழ்பு","நீல நிரைப் போது உறு காற்கு உலைவன போல்,","சாலகத்து ஒல்கிய கண்ணர், 'உயர் சீர்த்தி","ஆல் அமர் செல்வன் அணி சால் மகன் விழாக்","கால்கோள்' என்று ஊக்கிக், கதுமென நோக்கித்,","திருந்து அடி நூபுரம் ஆர்ப்ப இயலி, விருப்பினால்","'கண்ணும், நுதலும், கவுளும், கவவியார்க்கு,","ஒண்மை எதிரிய அம் கையும், தண் எனச்","செய்வன சிறப்பின் சிறப்புச் செய்து, இவ் இரா","எம்மொடு சேர்ந்து சென்றீவாயால்; செம்மால்!","நலம் புதிது உண்டு உள்ளா நாண் இலி செய்த","புலம்பு எலாம் தீர்க்குவேம்மன், என்று இரங்குபு,","வேற்று ஆனாத் தாயர் எதிர்கொள்ள, மாற்றாத","கள்வனால் தங்கியது, அல்லால், கதியாதி,","ஒள் இழாய்! யான் தீது இலேன்;","எள்ளலான், அம் மென் பணை தோள் நுமர் வேய்ந்த கண்ணியோடு","எம் இல் வருதியோ? எல்லா! நீ; தன் மெய்க் கண்","அம் தீம் சொல் நல்லார் அணிந்த கலம் காட்டி,","முந்தை இருந்து மகன் செய்த நோய்த் தலை,","'வெந்த புண் வேல் எறிந்தற்றால்' வடுவொடு","தந்தையும் வந்து நிலை."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks why the boy, taken out to play in the sandy street, stayed away so long while her milk ran; she is told that as he came home with his toy chariot of palmyra fruit, women at the windows, eyes trembling like blue lilies in the wind, cried that the festival of the god's son had begun, came down and claimed him for the night to ease the loneliness his shameless father left them in; then the father too arrives, with ornaments and scars, and she says it is like a spear thrust into a burn.","n":"The festival of Murugan, the son of the god under the banyan, is the women's excuse to hold the son, who is himself compared to that god's child in poem 81. The son first and the father next bring the same marks home, so the wound is given twice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 83","lb":"KL83","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["பெண்ணைப்","பெண்ணை",8,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["குரும்பைக்","குரும்பை",8,"maram","scene","palmyra fruit"],["நீல","நீல",12,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["ஆல் அமர் செல்வன்","ஆல் அமர் செல்வன்",14,"deyvam","scene",""],["நூபுரம்","நூபுரம்",16,"pira","ornament","anklet"],["கண்ணியோடு","கண்ணி",26,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:5"},"kl:84":{"book":"kalittokai","num":84,"t":["உறு வளி தூக்கும் உயர் சினை மாவின்","நறு வடி ஆர் இற்றவை போல் அழியக்,","கரந்து யான் அரக்கவும், கை நில்லா வீங்கிச்,","சுரந்த என் மெல் முலைப் பால் பழுது ஆக - நீ","நல் வாயில் போத்தந்த பொழுதினான், 'எல்லா!","கடவுள் கடி நகர் தோறும் இவனை","வலம் கொளீஇ வா' எனச், சென்றாய், விலங்கினை","ஈரம் இலாத இவன் தந்தை பெண்டிருள்","யார் இல் தவிர்ந்தனை? கூறு;","நீருள் அடை மறை ஆய் இதழ்ப் போது போல் கொண்ட","குடை நிழல் தோன்றும் நின் செம்மலைக் காணூஉ","'இவன் மன்ற, யான் நோவ உள்ளம் கொண்டு, உள்ளா","மகன் அல்லான் பெற்ற மகன்' என்று அகல் நகர்","வாயில் வரை இறந்து போத்தந்து, தாயர்","தெருவில் தவிர்ப்பத் தவிர்ந்தனன்; மற்று, அவர்","தம் தம் கலங்களுள், 'கையுறை' என்று இவற்கு,","ஒத்தவை ஆராய்ந்து, அணிந்தார்; 'பிறன் பெண்டிர்","ஈத்தவை கொள்வான் ஆம், இஃது ஒத்தன்; சீத்தை!","செறு தக்கான் மன்ற பெரிது';","சிறு பட்டி! ஏதிலார் கை, எம்மை எள்ளுபு நீ தொட்ட","மோதிரம் யாவோ? யாம் காண்கு;","அவற்றுள், நறா இதழ் கண்டன்ன செவ் விரற்கு ஏற்பச்","சுறா ஏறு எழுதிய மோதிரம் தொட்டாள்;","குறி அறிந்தேன்; 'காமன் கொடி எழுதி, என்றும்","செறியாப் பரத்தை இவன் தந்தை மார்பில்","பொறி ஒற்றிக் கொண்டு ஆள்வல்' என்பது தன்னை","அறீஇய செய்த வினை;","அன்னையோ? இஃது ஒன்று;","முந்தைய கண்டும் எழுகல்லாத என் முன்னர்","வெந்த புண் வேல் எறிந்தற்றா, இஃது ஒன்று;","தந்தை இறைத் தொடி மற்று இவன் தன் கைக் கண்","தந்தார் யார், எல்லாஅ! இது?","இஃது ஒன்று, என் ஒத்துக் காண்க, பிறரும் இவற்கு என்னும்","தன் நலம் பாடுவி, தந்தாளா? நின்னை,","இது தொடுக என்றவர் யார்?","அஞ்சாதி; நீயும் தவறு இலை; நின் கை இது தந்த","பூ எழில் உண் கண் அவளும் தவறு இலள்!","வேனில் புனல் அன்ன நுந்தையை நோவார் யார்?","மேல் நின்றும் எள்ளி, இது இவன் கைத் தந்தாள் -","தான் யாரோ? என்று வினவிய, நோய்ப்பாலேன்","யானே தவறு உடையேன்!"],"p":"","r":"The wife, her unsucked milk swelling and spoiling like wind-felled mangoes, asks the attendant she sent to take her son round the temples in which of his father's women's houses he stopped; told that the women, seeing the boy under his parasol as the son of the man who forgets them, came out, held him in the street and decked him with gifts, she rails at the boy for wearing a stranger's ring engraved with the shark of Kama's banner, a sign meant for her, and his father's own bracelet, and ends by saying that neither the boy nor that woman is at fault, nor the father who is like a summer flood, only she who asked.","n":"The other women are called the boy's mothers (தாயர், line 14), and their gift is a seal: the shark ring is Kama's banner, which the courtesan means to stamp on the father's chest so that the wife will read it (குறி அறிந்தேன்). The closing self-blame (யானே தவறு உடையேன்) is the bitterest line, since it clears everyone the wife has just accused.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","makkal","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 84","lb":"KL84","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","sevili",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["மாவின்","மா",1,"maram","simile","mango"],["போது","போது",10,"maram","simile","flower"],["நறா","நறா",22,"maram","simile","flower"],["சுறா","சுறா",23,"pira","ornament","shark"],["புனல்","புனல்",38,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:85":{"book":"kalittokai","num":85,"t":["காலவை, சுடு பொன் வளைஇய ஈர் அமை சுற்றொடு","பொடி அழல் புறம் தந்த செய்வுறு கிண்கிணி;","உடுத்தவை கைவினைப் பொலிந்த காசு அமை பொலம் காழ் மேல்","மை இல் செந் துகிர்க் கோவை அவற்றின் மேல்","தைஇய பூந் துகில் ஐது கழல் ஒரு திரை","கையதை, அலவன் கண் பெற அடங்கச் சுற்றிய","பல உறு கண்ணுள் சில கோல் அவிர் தொடி;","பூண்டவை, எறியா வாளும் எற்றா மழுவும்","செறியக் கட்டி ஈர் இடைத் தாழ்ந்த","பெய் புல மூதாய்ப் புகர் நிறத் துகிரின்","மை அற விளங்கிய ஆன் ஏற்று அவிர் பூண்;","சூடின, இரும் கடல் முத்தமும் பல் மணி பிறவும் ஆங்கு","ஒருங்கு உடன் கோத்த உருள் அமை மூக் காழ் மேல்","சுரும்பு ஆர் கண்ணிக்குச் சூழ் நூல் ஆக","அரும்பு அவிழ் நீலத்து ஆய் இதழ் நாணச்","சுரும்பு ஆற்றுப்படுத்த மணி மருள் மாலை;","ஆங்க, அவ்வும் பிறவும் அணிக்கு அணி ஆக நின்","செல்வு உறு திண் தேர் கொடும் சினை கைப் பற்றிப்","பைபயத் தூங்கும் நின் மெல் விரல் சீறடி","நோதலும் உண்டு ஈங்கு என் கை வந்தீ","செம்மால் நின் பால் உண்ணிய;","பொய் போர்த்துப், பாண் தலை இட்ட பல வல் புலையனைத்","தூண்டிலா விட்டுத் துடக்கித் தான் வேண்டியார்","நெஞ்சம் பிணித்தல் தொழிலாத் திரிதரும்","நுந்தை பால் உண்டி சில;","நுந்தை வாய், மாயச் சூள் தேறி மயங்கு நோய் கைமிகப்","பூ எழில் உண் கண் பனி பரப்பக் கண் படா","ஞாயர் பால் உண்டி சில;","அன்னையோ, யாம் எம் மகனைப் பாராட்ட கதுமெனத்","தாம் வந்தார் தம் பால் அவரொடு தம்மை","வருக என்றார் யார் கொல்லோ ஈங்கு;","என் பாலல், பாராட்டு உவந்தோய் குடி உண்டீத்தை என்","பாராட்டைப் பாலோ சில;","செரு குறித்தாரை உவகைக் கூத்தாட்டும்","வரிசைப் பெரு பாட்டொடு எல்லாம் பருகீத்தை","தண்டுவென் ஞாயர் மாட்டைப் பால்."],"p":"","r":"The mother, listing her little son's ornaments (anklet bells, a coral string over his cloth, toy sword and axe and a bull pendant on his chest, a garland of pearls and gems), calls him off the toy chariot to drink at her breast, then shares the milk out in mockery: some for his father, who uses the lying bard as a fishhook to bind the hearts of women he wants, some for his other 'mothers', whose eyes weep after trusting his false oaths, and asks who invited the man who has come in on her fondling.","n":"The poem is a lullaby turned into an indictment: the milk is divided into shares (நுந்தை பால், ஞாயர் பால், பாராட்டைப் பால்) as if the child owed each person in the father's life a mouthful. The last lines (32 to 36) are hard to construe and the shares there are not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["makkal","parathai","sool","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 85","lb":"KL85","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"],["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["அலவன்","அலவன்",6,"pira","ornament","crab"],["மூதாய்ப்","மூதாய்",10,"pira","simile","velvet mite"],["துகிரின்","துகிர்",10,"pira","ornament","coral"],["ஆன் ஏற்று","ஆன் ஏறு",11,"maa","ornament","bull"],["முத்தமும்","முத்தம்",12,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",14,"pira","scene","bee"],["நீலத்து","நீலம்",15,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["புலையனைத்","புலையன்",22,"seythi","simile","bard"],["பால்","பால்",21,"unavu","gift_food","milk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:86":{"book":"kalittokai","num":86,"t":["மை படு சென்னி மழ களிற்று ஓடை போல்,","கை புனை முக் காழ் கயம் தலைத் தாழப்,","பொலம் செய் மழுவொடு வாள் அணி கொண்ட","நலம் கிளர் ஒள் பூண் நனைத்தரும் அவ் வாய்","கலந்து கண் நோக்கு ஆரக், காண்பு இன் துகிர் மேல்","பொலம் புனை செம் பாகம் போர் கொண்டு இமைப்பக்,","கடி அரணம் பாயா நின் கை புனை வேழம்,","தொடியோர் மணலின் உழக்கி, அடி ஆர்ந்த","தேரை வாய்க் கிண்கிணி ஆர்ப்ப, இயலும் என்","போர் யானை, வந்தீக, ஈங்கு;","செம்மால்! வனப்பு எலாம் நுந்தையை ஒப்பினும், நுந்தை","நிலைப் பாலுள் ஒத்த குறி என் வாய்க் கேட்டு ஒத்தி;","கன்றிய தெவ்வர்க் கடந்து களம் கொள்ளும்","வென்றி மாட்டு ஒத்தி; பெரும! - மற்று ஒவ்வாதி,","'ஒன்றினேம் யாம்' என்று உணர்ந்தாரை, நுந்தை போல்,","மென் தோள் நெகிழ விடல்;","பால் கொளல் இன்றிப், பகல் போல் முறைக்கு ஒல்கா","கோல் செம்மை ஒத்தி, பெரும! - மற்று ஒவ்வாதி","கால் பொரு பூவின் கவின் வாட, நுந்தை போல்,","சால்பு ஆய்ந்தார் சாய விடல்;","வீதல் அறியா விழுப் பொருள் நச்சியார்க்கு","ஈதல் மாட்டு ஒத்தி, பெரும! மற்று ஒவ்வாதி,","மாதர் மெல் நோக்கின் மகளிரை, நுந்தை போல்.","நோய் கூர நோக்காய் விடல்;","ஆங்க,","திறன் அல்ல யாம் கழற, யாரை நகும், இம்","மகன் அல்லான் பெற்ற மகன்;","மறை நின்று, தாம் மன்ற வந்தீத்தனர்;","'ஆய் இழாய்! தாவாத எனக்குத் தவறு உண்டோ காவாது ஈங்கு","ஈத்தை, இவனை யாம் கோடற்குச், சீத்தை, யாம்","கன்றி அதனைக் கடியவும், கை நீவிக்,","குன்ற இறுவரைக் கோண்மா இவர்ந்தாங்குத்,","தந்தை வியல் மார்பில் பாய்ந்தான் - அறன் இல்லா","அன்பு இலி பெற்ற மகன்."],"p":"","r":"The mother calls her little son, decked like a young tusker with a gold forehead plate and toy sword and axe, her 'war elephant', and tells him that he is like his father in beauty, may he be like him in winning battles, in a sceptre straight as daylight and in giving to those who seek lasting wealth, but not like him in leaving the women who trusted him to waste; then, as the father appears from hiding and asks for the boy, she refuses, but the boy slips her hand and leaps onto his father's chest like a lion climbing a cliff, 'son of a loveless man'.","n":"The triple refrain (ஒத்தி... ஒவ்வாதி) splits the father into his public virtues (valour, justice, giving) and his private fault, and asks the son to inherit only the first; the son's leap to the father's chest answers her at once. 'Son of a man who is no man' (மகன் அல்லான் பெற்ற மகன், also in 84) is the household's verdict on the father.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","parathai","sengol","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 86","lb":"KL86","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["களிற்று","களிறு",1,"maa","simile","tusker"],["தேரை","தேரை",9,"pira","ornament","frog"],["யானை","யானை",10,"maa","simile","elephant"],["பூவின்","பூ",19,"maram","simile","flower"],["கோண்மா","கோள் மா",32,"maa","simile","lion"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:87":{"book":"kalittokai","num":87,"t":["ஒரூஉ நீ; எம் கூந்தல் கொள்ளல் - யாம் நின்னை","வெரூஉதும், காணும் கடை;","தெரி இழாய்! செய் தவறு இல் வழி, யாங்குச் சினவுவாய்,","மெய் பிரிந்தன்னவர் மாட்டு?","ஏடா! நினக்குத் தவறு உண்டோ? நீ வீடு பெற்றாய்;","இமைப்பின் இதழ் மறைபு ஆங்கே கெடுதி;","நிலைப் பால் அறியினும், நின் நொந்து நின்னைப்","புலப்பார் உடையர் தவறு;","அணைத் தோளாய்! தீயாரைப் போலத் திறன் இன்று உடற்றுதி;","காயும் தவறு இலேன் யான்;","மான் நோக்கி! நீ அழ நீத்தவன் ஆனாது","நாண் இலன் ஆயின், நலிதந்து அவன் வயின்","ஊடுதல் என்னோ, இனி?","'இனி யாதும் மீக்கூற்றம் யாம் இலம்' என்னும்","தகையது காண்டைப்பாய், நெஞ்சே! பனி ஆனாப்","பாடு இல் கண் பாயல் கொள!"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her husband to keep off and not touch her hair; he asks how she can be angry with one who is her own body parted from her, when he has done no wrong; she answers that he has his freedom and is gone in the blink of an eye, and the fault is hers for sulking at a man who knows his place; then she tells her heart that sulking at a shameless man who left her weeping is useless, and that it will see her give up all complaint so her sleepless, tearful eyes may rest.","n":"'You have got release' (நீ வீடு பெற்றாய்) borrows the word for liberation to mock the husband's freedom from the household. The last speech, to the heart, gives up the quarrel not because he is forgiven but because sulking needs a partner with shame. Lines 11, 12, 15 and 16 are missing from the sangamtamil.com text.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nenju","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 87","lb":"KL87","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:88":{"book":"kalittokai","num":88,"t":["ஒரூஉக்! கொடி இயல் நல்லார் குரல் நாற்றத்து உற்ற","முடி உதிர் பூந் தாது மொய்ம்பின ஆகத்,","தொடிய, எமக்கு நீ யாரை? பெரியார்க்கு","அடியரோ, ஆற்றாதவர்?","கடியர் தமக்கு, யார் சொல்லத் தக்கார் மாற்று?","வினை கெட்டு, வாய் அல்லா வெண்மை உரையாது, கூறு நின்","மாயம், மருள்வார் அகத்து;","ஆய் இழாய்! நின் கண் பெறின் அல்லால், இன் உயிர் வாழ்கல்லா","என் கண் எவனோ, தவறு?","இஃது ஒத்தன்! புள்ளிக் களவன் புனல் சேர் பொதுக்கம் போல்","வள் உகிர் போழ்ந்தனவும், வாள் எயிறு உற்றனவும்,","ஒள் இதழ் சோர்ந்த நின் கண்ணியும், நல்லார்","சிரறுபு சீறச் சிவந்த நின் மார்பும்,","தவறு ஆதல் சாலாவோ? கூறு;","அது தக்கது; வேற்றுமை என் கண்ணோ ஓராதி; தீது இன்மை","தேற்றக் கண்டீயாய்; தெளிக்கு;","இனி தேற்றேம் யாம்;","தேர் மயங்கி வந்த தெரி கோதை அம் நல்லார்","தார் மயங்கி வந்த தவறு அஞ்சிப், போர் மயங்கி,","நீ உறும் பொய்ச் சூள் அணங்கு ஆகின், மற்று இனி","யார் மேல் விளியுமோ? கூறு."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her husband, his shoulders thick with pollen from the hair of fine women, to keep off, asking what he is to her; he says he cannot live without her eyes; she points to the evidence on him, nail scratches and tooth marks like the holes a spotted crab makes in the wet bank, a faded garland, a chest reddened by angry women; and when he offers to swear his innocence she refuses, asking on whom his false oath, if it turns into a striking spirit, will fall.","n":"Her refusal of the oath is also care: an oath sworn falsely becomes an அணங்கு that strikes, and she asks who it would strike (யார் மேல் விளியுமோ), meaning him. The crab's holes in the mud bank are a marutham image used as forensic evidence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","sool","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 88","lb":"KL88","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["பூந் தாது","பூந் தாது",2,"maram","scene","pollen"],["களவன்","களவன்",10,"pira","simile","crab"],["கண்ணியும்","கண்ணி",12,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:89":{"book":"kalittokai","num":89,"t":["யார் இவன்? எம் கூந்தல் கொள்வான்? இதுவும் ஓர்","ஊராண்மைக்கு ஒத்த படிறு உடைத்து; எம் மனை","வாரல் நீ; வந்தாங்கே மாறு;","என் இவை; ஓர் உயிர்ப் புள்ளின் இரு தலையுள் ஒன்று","போர் எதிர்ந்தற்றாப் புலவல்; நீ கூறின், என்","ஆர் உயிர் நிற்கும் ஆறு யாது?","ஏஎ! தெளிந்தேம் யாம்; காயாதி - எல்லாம் வல் - எல்லா!","பெரும் காட்டுக் கொற்றிக்குப் பேய் நொடித்தாங்கு,","வருந்தல் நின் வஞ்சம் உரைத்து;","மருந்து இன்று - மன்னவன் சீறின், தவறு உண்டோ? நீ நயந்த","இன் நகை! தீதோ இலேன்;","மாண மறந்து உள்ளா நாண் இலிக்கு இப் போர்","புறம் சாய்ந்து காண்டைப்பாய் - நெஞ்சே! உறழ்ந்து இவனைப்","பொய்ப்ப விடேஎம் என நெருங்கின், தப்பினேன்","என்று அடி சேர்தலும் உண்டு."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks who this is that grabs her hair, a trick fit for a townsman's lordship, and tells him to go back; he says her sulking is like one head of a two-headed bird fighting the other and asks how his life can last; she tells him not to wear her out with his tales like a ghoul telling stories to the forest goddess; he says that when a king is angry there is no remedy, and he has done no wrong; she tells her heart to watch this shameless man turn tail, for if pressed he will fall at their feet confessing.","n":"The two-headed bird with one life is the husband's best argument: a quarrel between them is self-harm. She answers it with an image of deceit (the ghoul's tales to the goddess of the wild), and her final aside expects not reconciliation but his collapse at her feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","uyir","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 89","lb":"KL89","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["புள்ளின்","புள்",4,"pul","simile","bird"],["கொற்றிக்குப்","கொற்றி",8,"deyvam","simile","forest goddess"],["பேய்","பேய்",8,"deyvam","simile","ghoul"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:90":{"book":"kalittokai","num":90,"t":["கண்டேன், நின் மாயம் களவு ஆதல்; பொய்ந் நகா,","மண்டாத சொல்லித் தொடாஅல்; தொடீஇய நின்","பெண்டிர் உளர் மன்னோ, ஈங்கு?","ஒண் தொடி! நீ கண்டது எவனோ தவறு?","கண்டது நோயும் வடுவும் கரந்து, மகிழ் செருக்கிப்,","பாடு பெயல் நின்ற பானாள் இரவில் -","தொடி பொலி தோளும், முலையும், கதுப்பும்,","வடிவு ஆர் குழையும், இழையும், பொறையா","ஒடிவது போலும் நுசுப்போடு, அடி தளரா","ஆராக் கவவின் ஒருத்தி வந்து - அல்கல் தன்","சீர் ஆர் ஞெகிழம் சிலம்பச், சிவந்து, நின்","போர் ஆர் கதவம் மிதித்தது அமையுமோ?","ஆய் இழை ஆர்க்கும் ஒலி கேளா, அவ் எதிர்","தாழாது எழுந்து நீ சென்றது அமையுமோ?","மாறாள் சினைஇ, அவள் ஆங்கே, நின் மார்பில்","நாறு இணப் பைந் தார் பரிந்தது அமையுமோ?","'தேறு நீ; தீயேன் அலேன்' என்று மற்று அவள்","சீறு அடி தோயா இறுத்தது அமையுமோ?","கூறு இனிக், காயேமா யாம்;","தேறின், பிறவும் தவறு இலேன் யான்;","அல்கல் கனவு கொல் நீ கண்டது?","கனை பெயல் தண் துளி வீசும் பொழுதில் குறி வந்தாள்","கண்ட கனவு எனக், காணாது, மாறு உற்றுப் -","பண்டைய அல்ல, நின் பொய்ச் சூள், நினக்கு; எல்லா! -","நின்றாய், நின் புக்கில் பல;","மென் தோளாய்! நல்கு, நின் நல் எழில் உண்கு;","ஏடா! குறை உற்று நீ எம் உரையல் - நின் தீமை","பொறை ஆற்றேம் என்றல் பெறுதுமோ, யாழ","நிறை ஆற்றா நெஞ்சு உடையேம்?"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her husband she has found out his trickery and not to touch her; when he asks what fault she saw, she tells it as a string of questions: in last night's midnight rain a woman with shining shoulders came, her anklet ringing, and kicked his door, he rose at once and went, she tore his garland, he bowed to her small feet saying he was not wicked; is that enough? He asks whether she dreamed it; she says his oaths are not what they were and he has many homes; he begs to enjoy her beauty, and she yields, asking whether women whose hearts cannot hold out can say they will not bear his wrongs.","n":"The refrain அமையுமோ ('is that enough?') turns a reported scene into a cross-examination, each question adding one act. His defence that she dreamed it is answered by repeating his own words back (கண்ட கனவு). The close is a surrender framed as a reproach: the wife's lack of நிறை, not his innocence, ends the quarrel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","kanavu","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 90","lb":"KL90","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பைந் தார்","தார்",16,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:91":{"book":"kalittokai","num":91,"t":["அரி நீர் அவிழ் நீலம், அல்லி, அனிச்சம்,","புரி நெகிழ் முல்லை, நறவோடு அமைந்த","தெரி மலர்க் கண்ணியும் தாரும் நயந்தார்","பொரு முரண் சீறச் சிதைந்து, நெருநையின்","இன்று நன்று, என்னை அணி;","அணை மென் தோளாய்! செய்யாத சொல்லிச் சினவுவது ஈங்கு எவன்,","ஐயத்தால்? என்னை கதியாதி; தீது இன்மை","தெய்வத்தான் - கண்டீ தெளிக்கு;","மற்று அது, அறிவல், யான் நின் சூள்; அனைத்து ஆக நல்லார்","செறி தொடி உற்ற வடுவும், குறி பொய்த்தார்","கூர் உகிர் சாடிய மார்பும், குழைந்த நின்","தாரும், ததர் பட்ட சாந்தமும், சேரி","அரி மதர் உண் கண்ணார், ஆராக் கவவின்","பரிசு அழிந்து யாழ நின் மேனி கண்டு, யானும்","செரு ஒழிந்தேன்; சென்றீ, இனி;","தெரி இழாய்! தேற்றாய் சிவந்தனை - காண்பாய், நீ - தீது இன்மை","ஆற்றின் நிறுப்பல் பணிந்து;","அன்னதேல், ஆற்றல் காண்;","வேறுபட்டாங்கே கலுழ்தி; அகப்படின்,","மாறுபட்டாங்கே மயங்குதி; யாது ஒன்றும்","கூறி உணர்த்தலும் வேண்டாது, மற்று நீ","மாணா செயினும், மறுத்து, ஆங்கே, நின் வயின்","காணின் நெகிழும் என் நெஞ்சு ஆயின், என் உற்றாய்","பேணாய் நீ பெட்பச் செயல்?"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her husband that the garland of lilies, jasmine and anicham he loves was crushed by his women's anger and asks him to adorn her with it; he asks why she is angry from mere suspicion and offers to prove his innocence by an oath before a god; she says she knows his oath and lists the bangle scars, the nail scratches, the crumpled garland and smeared sandal that the street's women left on him, says she has given up the fight and tells him to go; when he offers to bow, she says that since her heart melts at the sight of him whatever he does, he need not trouble to please her.","n":"The last argument turns her weakness into his licence: because her heart gives way whenever she sees him, he has no reason to behave (பேணாய் நீ பெட்பச் செயல்). The mullai jasmine in a marutham garland is only an ornament.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","sool","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 91","lb":"KL91","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["நீலம்","நீலம்",1,"maram","ornament","blue lily"],["அல்லி","அல்லி",1,"maram","ornament","water lily"],["அனிச்சம்","அனிச்சம்",1,"maram","ornament",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","ornament","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:92":{"book":"kalittokai","num":92,"t":["புன வளர் பூங் கொடி அன்னாய்! கழியக்","கனவு எனப்பட்டது ஓர் காரிகை நீர்த்தே!","முயங்கிய நல்லார் முலை இடை மூழ்கி,","மயங்கி, மற்று ஆண்டு ஆண்டுச் சேறலும் செல்லாது,","உயங்கி இருந்தார்க்கு உயர்ந்த பொருளும்,","அரிதின் அறம் செய்யா, ஆன்றோர் உலகும்,","உரிதின் ஒருதலை எய்தலும் - வீழ்வார்ப்","பிரிதலும், ஆங்கே புணர்தலும், தம்மில்","தருதல் தகையாதால் மற்று;","நனவினால் போலும், நறு நுதால்! அல்கல்","கனவினால் சென்றேன் - கலி கெழு கூடல்","வரை உறழ் நீள் மதில் வாய் சூழ்ந்த வையைக்","கரை அணி காவின் அகத்து;","உரை, இனி - தண்டாத் தீம் சாயல் நெடுந்தகாய்! அவ் வழிக்","கண்டது எவன் மற்று நீ?","கண்டது - உடன் அமர் ஆயமொடு, அவ் விசும்பு ஆயும்","மட நடை மா இனம், அந்தி அமையத்து,","இடன் விட்டு இயங்கா இமையத்து ஒரு பால்,","இறைகொண்டு இருந்தன்ன - நல்லாரைக் கண்டேன்;","துறை கொண்டு உயர் மணல் மேல் ஒன்றி நிறைவதை","ஓர்த்தது இசைக்கும் பறை போல், நின் நெஞ்சத்து","வேட்டதே கண்டாய் கனா;","கேட்டை, விரையல் நீ; மற்று வெகுள்வாய்! - உரை - ஆண்டு","இது ஆகும், இன் நகை நல்லாய்! பொது ஆகத்,","தாம் கொடி அன்ன தகையார் எழுந்தது ஓர்","பூங் கொடி வாங்கி, இணர் கொய்ய, ஆங்கே","சினை அலர் வேம்பின் பொருப்பன் பொருத","முனை அரண் போல உடைந்தன்று, அக் காவில்","துனை வரி வண்டின் இனம்;","மற்று ஆங்கே, நேர் இணர் மூசிய வண்டு எல்லாம் அவ் வழிக்","காரிகை நல்லார் நலம் கவர்ந்து உண்ப போல் ஓராங்கு மூச,","அவருள்,","ஒருத்தி, செயல் அமை கோதை நகை,","ஒருத்தி, இயல் ஆர் செருவில் தொடியொடு தட்ப,","ஒருத்தி, தெரி முத்தம் சேர்ந்த திலகம்,","ஒருத்தி, அரி மாண் அவிர் குழை ஆய் காது வாங்க,","ஒருத்தி, வரி ஆர் அகல் அல்குல் காழகம்,","ஒருத்தி, அரி ஆர் ஞெகிழத்து அணி சுறாத் தட்ப,","ஒருத்தி, புலவியால் புல்லாது இருந்தாள், அலவுற்று","வண்டு இனம் ஆர்ப்ப, இடை விட்டுக் காதலன்","தண் தார் அகலம் புகும்;","ஒருத்தி, அடி தாழ் கலிங்கம் தழீஇ, ஒரு கை","முடி தாழ் இரும் கூந்தல் பற்றிப், பூ வேய்ந்த","கடி கயம் பாயும், அலந்து;","ஒருத்தி, கணம் கொண்டு அவை மூசக், கை ஆற்றாள், பூண்ட","மணம் கமழ் கோதை பரிபு கொண்டு, ஓச்சி","வணங்கு காழ் வங்கம் புகும்;","ஒருத்தி, இறந்த களியான் இதழ் மறைந்த கண்ணள்,","பறந்தவை மூசக் கடிவாள், கடியும்","இடம் தேற்றாள் சோர்ந்தனள், கை;","ஆங்க, கடி காவில் கால் ஒற்ற, ஒல்கி ஒசியாக்","கொடி கொடி தம்மில் பிணங்கியவை போல்,","தெரி இழை ஆர்ப்ப மயங்கி இரிவுற்றார் வண்டிற்கு","வண்டலவர்; கண்டேன், யான்;","நின்னை நின் பெண்டிர் புலந்தனவும், நீ அவர்","முன் அடி ஒல்கி உணர்த்தினவும், பல் மாண்","கனவின் தலையிட்டு உரையல்; சினைஇ யான்","செய்வது இல் என்பதோ? கூறு;","பொய் கூறேன் - அன்ன வகையால் யான் கண்ட கனவு தான்","நல் வாயாக் காண்டை - நறு நுதால்! பல் மாணும்","கூடிப் புணர்ந்தீர்! பிரியன்மின்; நீடிப்","பிரிந்தீர்! புணர் தம்மின், என்பன போல","அரும்பு அவிழ் பூஞ் சினை தோறும் இரும் குயில்","ஆனாது அகவும் பொழுதினான், மேவர,","நான்மாடக்கூடல் மகளிரும் மைந்தரும்","தேன் இமிர் காவில் புணர்ந்து இருந்து ஆடுமார்,","ஆனா விருப்போடு அணி அயர்ப, காமற்கு","வேனில் விருந்து எதிர்கொண்டு."],"p":"","r":"The husband tells his wife of a dream in which he went to the grove on the Vaigai's bank at Koodal and saw women gathered like a flock of swans resting on the Himalaya at dusk; when one pulled down a flowering creeper the bees broke out like a fort the Pandya stormed and swarmed them as if to feast on their beauty, and he describes each woman's plight, until they fled tangled like wind-blown creepers; the wife says he is dressing up in a dream his women's sulks and his bowing to them; he answers that the dream will come true when the koels call to lovers 'united, do not part; parted, unite', and the men and women of Koodal sport in the groves to welcome spring for Kama.","n":"The opening maxim (lines 3 to 9) says that some things no one can give himself, among them lovers' parting and union; the dream then stages a crowd of women each undone by bees, which the wife reads as a coded account of his own affairs. His last move recasts the dream as a prophecy of the spring festival, where all couples unite, so his straying becomes the season's law. Lines 6 to 9 are compressed and their construction is not sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","oodal","parathai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 92","lb":"KL92","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["கொடி","கொடி",1,"maram","simile","creeper"],["மா","மா",17,"pul","simile","swan"],["பறை","பறை",21,"parai","simile",""],["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",27,"maram","ornament","neem"],["வண்டின்","வண்டு",29,"pira","simile","bee"],["சுறாத்","சுறா",38,"pira","ornament","shark"],["கயம்","கயம்",44,"pira","scene","pool"],["குயில்","குயில்",63,"pul","simile","koel"]],"en":[["town","கூடல்",11],["river","வையைக்",12],["hill","இமையத்து",18],["king","வேம்பின் பொருப்பன்",27],["town","நான்மாடக்கூடல்",65]],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:93":{"book":"kalittokai","num":93,"t":["வண்டு ஊது சாந்தம் வடுக் கொள நீவிய,","தண்டாத் தீம் சாயல் பரத்தை, வியல் மார்ப!","பண்டு, இன்னை அல்லைமன்; ஈங்கு எல்லி வந்தீயக்,","கண்டது எவன்? மற்று உரை;","நன்றும், தடைஇய மென் தோளாய்! கேட்டீவாய் ஆயின் -","உடன் உறை வாழ்க்கைக்கு உதவி உறையும்","கடவுளர் கண் தங்கினேன்;","சோலை மலர் வேய்ந்த மான் பிணை அன்னார் பலர், நீ","கடவுண்மை கொண்டு ஒழுகுவார்;","அவருள், எக் கடவுள்? மற்று அக் கடவுளைச் செப்பீமன்;","முத்து ஏர் முறுவலாய் நாம் மணம் புக்கக் கால்","இப் போழ்து போழ்து என்று அது வாய்ப்பக் கூறிய","அக் கடவுள், மற்று அக் கடவுள்; - அது ஒக்கும்","நாவுள் அழுந்து தலை சாய்த்து நீ கூறும்","மாயமோ; கைப்படுக்கப்பட்டாய், நீ; கண்டாரை","வாய் ஆக யாம் கூற வேட்டீவாய்! கேள் இனி;","பெறல் நசை வேட்கையின் நின் குறி வாய்ப்பப்,","பறி முறை நேர்ந்த நகார் ஆகக், கண்டார்க்கு","இறு முறை செய்யும் உருவொடு, நும் இல்,","செறி முறை வந்த கடவுளைக் கண்டாயோ?","நறும் தண் தகரமும் நானமும் நாறும்","நெறிந்த குரல் கூந்தல் நாள் அணிக்கு ஒப்ப,","நோக்கின் பிணி கொள்ளும் கண்ணொடு, மேல் நாள், நீ","பூப் பலி விட்ட கடவுளைக் கண்டாயோ?","ஈர் அணிக்கு ஏற்ற ஒடியாப் படிவத்துச்","சூர் கொன்ற செவ்வேலான் பாடிப், பல நாளும்,","ஆராக் கனை காமம் குன்றத்து நின்னொடு","மாரி இறுத்த கடவுளைக் கண்டாயோ?","கண்ட கடவுளர் தம் உள்ளும், நின்னை","வெறி கொள் வியல் மார்பு வேறு ஆகச் செய்து,","குறி கொளச் செய்தார் யார்? செப்பு; மற்று, யாரும்","சிறு வரைத் தங்கின் வெகுள்வர்; செறு தக்காய்!","தேறினேன்; சென்றீ; நீ செல்லா விடுவாயேல்,","நல் தார் அகலத்துக்கு ஓர் சார மேவிய","நெட்டு இரும் கூந்தல் கடவுளர் எல்லார்க்கும்","முட்டுப்பாடு ஆகலும் உண்டு."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks her husband, his sandal smeared and marked, what he saw to come home at night; he says he stayed with the gods who help a household; she asks which god, since many deer-like women act as gods to him, and runs through them: the god who named their wedding hour? a lie told with tongue pressed and head bent; did he see the goddess who came to his house to be plucked, the one with binding eyes to whom he offered flowers, the one who sang of the red-speared slayer of Sur with him on the hill through the rains? which of them marked his chest? she believes him; let him go, or all the long-haired gods on his chest may be put out.","n":"The whole poem plays on கடவுள், god and goddess-like woman: his excuse of worship is taken literally and turned into an inventory of women, each 'god' defined by a rite (pluck, flower offering, songs to Murugan on the hill). Line 2 calls the husband himself பரத்தை, the masculine use.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 93","lb":"KL93","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வண்டு","வண்டு",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["மான்","மான்",8,"maa","simile","deer"],["முத்து","முத்து",11,"pira","simile","pearl"],["செவ்வேலான்","செவ்வேலான்",26,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"]],"en":[["hill","குன்றத்து",27]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:94":{"book":"kalittokai","num":94,"t":["என் நோற்றனை கொல்லோ? -","நீர் உள் நிழல் போல் நுடங்கிய மென் சாயல்","ஈங்கு உருச் சுருங்கி","இயலுவாய்! நின்னோடு உசாவுவேன்; நின்றீத்தை;","அன்னையோ! காண் தகை இல்லாக் குறள் நாழிப் போழ்தினான்","ஆண்டலைக்கு ஈன்ற பறழ் மகனே! நீ எம்மை","'வேண்டுவல்' என்று விலக்கினை; நின் போல்வார்","தீண்டப் பெறுபவோ மற்று?","மாண்ட, எறித்த படை போல் முடங்கி மடங்கி,","நெறித்துவிட்டன்ன நிறை ஏரால், என்னைப்","பொறுக்கல்லா நோய் செய்தாய்; பொறீஇ நிறுக்கல்லேன்;","நீ நல்கின் உண்டு, என் உயிர்;","குறிப்புக் காண் - வல்லுப்பலகை எடுத்து நிறுத்தன்ன","கல்லாக் குறள! கடும் பகல் வந்து எம்மை,","'இல்லத்து வா' என, மெய் கொளீஇ, எல்லா! நின்","பெண்டிர் உளர் மன்னோ? கூறு;","நல்லாய் கேள்! உக்கத்து மேலும் நடு உயர்ந்து வாள் வாய","கொக்கு உரித்தன்ன கொடு மடாய்! நின்னை யான்","புக்கு அகலம் புல்லின், நெஞ்சு ஊன்றும்; புறம் புல்லின்","அக்குளுத்துப் புல்லலும் ஆற்றேன்; அருளீமோ.","பக்கத்துப் புல்லச் சிறிது;","போ, சீத்தை! மக்கள் முரியே! நீ மாறு, இனித், தொக்க","மரக் கோட்டம் சேர்ந்து எழுந்த பூங் கொடி போல,","நிரப்பம் இல் யாக்கை தழீஇயினர், எம்மைப்,","புரப்பேம் என்பாரும் பலரால்; பரத்தை, என்","பக்கத்துப் புல்லீயாய் என்னுமால்; தொக்க","உழுந்தினும் துவ்வாக், குறு வட்டா! நின்னின்","இழிந்ததோ, கூனின் பிறப்பு? - கழிந்து ஆங்கே,","'யாம் வீழ்தும்' என்று தன் பின் செலவும், உற்றீயாக்","கூனி குழையும் குழைவு காண்;","யாமை எடுத்து நிறுத்தற்றால், தோள் இரண்டும் வீசி,","யாம் வேண்டேம் என்று விலக்கவும், எம் வீழும்","காமர் நடக்கும் நடை காண் - கவர் கணைச்","சாமனார் தம் முன் செலவு காண்;","ஓஒ! காண், நம்முள் நகுதல் தொடீஇயர், நம்முள் நாம்","உசாவுவம்; கோன் அடி தொட்டேன்;","ஆங்கு ஆக! சாயல் இன் மார்ப! அடங்கினேன்; ஏஎ!","பேயும் பேயும் துள்ளல் உறும் எனக்","கோயில் உள் கண்டார் நகாமை வேண்டுவல்;","தண்டாத் தகடு உருவ! வேறு ஆகக் காவின் கீழ்ப்","போதர்; அகடு ஆரப் புல்லி முயங்குவேம் -","துகள் தபு காட்சி அவையத்தார் ஓலை","முகடு காப்பு யாத்துவிட்டாங்கு."],"p":"","r":"A dwarf courts a hunchbacked woman in the palace: he calls her lovely and says her bent body has made him sick with love; she mocks him as an owl's brat, a dice board stood on end, a heap smaller than black gram, asks whether he has no women of his own and whether a hunchback's birth is lower than his; he asks at least to embrace her from the side, since from the front her chest and from behind her hump prevent him; when she follows him all the same he mocks her walk, and at last they agree, he swearing by the king's feet and she asking him to come under the grove so the palace will not laugh at two goblins dancing.","n":"A comic poem: the insults are similes in the high style turned on bodies the tradition does not allow to be lovers, which is why the thinai is perunthinai though the colophon files it under marutham. The two still use the lovers' forms: the oath by the king's foot, the fear of being laughed at, the tryst in the grove. Lines 13 to 43 are missing from the sangamtamil.com text, and several words (குறு வட்டா, தகடு உருவ) are rare.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 94","lb":"KL94","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["pira","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஆண்டலைக்கு","ஆண்டலை",6,"pul","simile","owl"],["கொக்கு","கொக்கு",18,"pul","simile","crane"],["பூங் கொடி","பூங் கொடி",23,"maram","simile","creeper"],["உழுந்தினும்","உழுந்து",27,"unavu","simile","black gram"],["யாமை","யாமை",31,"pira","simile","tortoise"],["காவின்","கா",40,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:95":{"book":"kalittokai","num":95,"t":["நில், ஆங்கு; நில், ஆங்கு; இவர்தரல் - எல்லா! நீ","நாறு இரும் கூந்தலார் இல் செல்வாய், இவ் வழி","ஆறு மயங்கினை போறி! நீ வந்தாங்கே","மாறு; இனி நின் ஆங்கே நின், சே அடி சிவப்பச்,","செறிந்து ஒளிர் வெண் பல்லாய்! யாம் வேறு இயைந்த","குறும்பூழ் போர் கண்டேம்; அனைத்தல்லது, யாதும்","அறிந்ததோ இல்லை, நீ வேறு ஓர்ப்பது;","குறும்பூழ்ப் போர் கண்டமை கேட்டேன், நீ என்றும்;","புதுவன ஈகை வளம் பாடிக், காலின்","பிரியாக் கவி கை புலையன் தன் யாழின்","இகுத்த செவி சாய்த்து, இனி இனிப் பட்டன","ஈகைப் போர் கண்டாயும் போறி; மெய் எண்ணின்,","தபுத்த புலர்வு இல புண்;","ஊரவர் கவ்வை உளைந்து ஈயாய், அல்கல் நின்","தாரின் வாய்க் கொண்டு முயங்கிப், பிடி மாண்டு,","போர் வாய்ப்பக் காணினும் போகாது கொண்டு, ஆடும்","பார்வைப் போர் கண்டாயும் போறி; நின் தோள் மேலாம்","ஈரம் ஆய் விட்டன புண்;","கொடிற்றுப் புண் செய்யாது, மெய்ம் முழுதும் கையின்","துடைத்து, நீ வேண்டினும் வெல்லாது கொண்டு, ஆடும்","ஒட்டிய போர் கண்டாயும் போறி; முகம் தானே","கொட்டிக் கொடுக்கும் குறிப்பு;","ஆயின், ஆய் இழாய்! அன்னவை யான் ஆங்கு அறியாமை","போற்றிய, நின் மெய் தொடுகு;","அன்னையோ! மெய்யை பொய் என்று மயங்கிய, கை ஒன்று,","அறிகல்லாய் போறி காண், நீ;","நல்லாய்! பொய் எல்லாம் ஏற்றி தவறு தலைப்பெய்து,","கையொடு கண்டாய்; பிழைத்தேன்; அருள் இனி;","அருளுகம் யாம்; யாரேம்; எல்லா! தெருள?","அளித்து நீ பண்ணிய பூழ் எல்லாம் - இன்னும்","விளித்து, நின் பாணனோடு ஆடி, அளித்தி -","விடலை நீ நீத்தலின், நோய் பெரிது ஏய்க்கும்;","நடலைப்பட்டு, எல்லாம் நின் பூழ்."],"p":"","r":"The wife stops her husband at the door, saying he has lost his way to the fragrant-haired women's houses; he says he only watched a quail fight; she agrees he saw quail fights: a 'gift fight' to the bard's lute that left unhealed wounds, a 'decoy fight' in which a woman held his garland in her beak and would not let go, leaving wet wounds on his shoulders, a 'clinging fight' that his face betrays; he offers to swear on her body; she says he cannot tell truth from lies; he admits he is caught and asks for grace; she says she will grant it, and tells him to go and play with his bard and tend all the quails he trained, which pine since he left them.","n":"The husband's alibi gives her the metaphor: each kind of quail fight she names is a kind of love bout, and the wounds are its evidence. Her pardon ends on the quails themselves: he is sent back to the birds he trained, which suffer when he leaves them, so the wife speaks for her rivals too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 95","lb":"KL95","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["குறும்பூழ்","குறும்பூழ்",6,"pul","scene","quail"],["புலையன்","புலையன்",10,"seythi","scene","bard"],["யாழின்","யாழ்",10,"yaazh","scene",""],["பாணனோடு","பாணன்",31,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:96":{"book":"kalittokai","num":96,"t":["ஏந்து எழில் மார்ப! எதிர் அல்ல, நின் வாய் சொல்;","பாய்ந்து ஆய்ந்த தானைப் பரிந்து ஆனா மைந்தினை;","சாந்து அழி வேரை; சுவல் தாழ்ந்த கண்ணியை;","யாங்குச் சென்று, ஈங்கு வந்தீத்தந்தாய்? கேள், இனி;","ஏந்தி, எதிர் இதழ் நீலம் பிணைந்தன்ன கண்ணாய்!","குதிரை வழங்கி வருவல்;","அறிந்தேன், குதிரை தான்!","பால் பிரியா ஐங்கூந்தல் பல் மயிர்க் கொய் சுவல்,","மேல் விரித்து யாத்த சிகழிகைச் செவ் உளை,","நீல மணிக் கடிகை வல்லிகை, யாப்பின் கீழ்","ஞால் இயல் மெல் காதின் புல்லிகைச் சாமரை,","மத்திகைக் கண்ணுறை ஆகக் கவின் பெற்ற","உத்தி ஒரு காழ், நூல் உத்தரியத் திண் பிடி,","நேர் மணி நேர் முக் காழ்ப் பல் பல கண்டிகைத்,","தார் மணி பூண்ட தமனிய மேகலை,","நூபுரப் புட்டில், அடியொடு அமைத்து யாத்த","வார் பொலம் கிண்கிணி ஆர்ப்ப இயற்றி, நீ,","காதலித்து ஊர்ந்த நின் காமக் குதிரையை,","ஆய் சுதை மாடத்து, அணி நிலா முற்றத்துள்,","ஆதி கொளீஇய, அசையினை ஆகுவை,","வாதுவன்; வாழிய நீ;","சேகா! கதிர் விரி வைகலில், கை வாரூஉக் கொண்ட","மதுரைப் பெரு முற்றம் போல, நின் மெய்க் கண்","குதிரையோ, வீறியது?","கூர் உகிர் மாண்ட குளம்பின் அது; நன்றே","கோரமே - வாழி! - குதிரை;","வெதிர் உழக்கு நாழியால் சேதிகைக் குத்தக்","குதிரை உடல் அணி போல, நின் மெய்க் கண்","குதிரையோ, கவ்வியது?","சீத்தை! பயம் இன்றி ஈங்குக் கடித்தது; நன்றே","வியமமே வாழி! - குதிரை;","மிக நன்று; இனி அறிந்தேன், இன்று நீ ஊர்ந்த குதிரை;","பெரு மணம் பண்ணி, அறத்தினில் கொண்ட","பருமக் குதிரையோ அன்று; பெரும! நின்","ஏதில் பெரும் பாணன் தூது ஆட, ஆங்கே ஓர்","வாதத்தான் வந்த வளிக் குதிரை; ஆதி","உரு அழிக்கும் அக் குதிரை ஊரல்; நீ ஊரின், பரத்தை","பரியாக, வாதுவனாய், என்றும் மற்று அச்சார்த்","திரி, குதிரை ஏறிய செல்."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks her husband, with torn cloth, smeared sandal and slipping garland, where he has been; he says he went riding a horse; she says she knows that horse, describes a woman as a horse with mane, plume and harness of jewels, which he rode on the moonlit terrace of a white mansion, asks whether it was the horse that scratched him like the swept courtyard of Madurai and bit him, and concludes that it is not the caparisoned horse he married by dharma but a wind-horse brought by the bard's go-between, and tells him to ride it and be the courtesan's groom for ever.","n":"The metaphor sets two horses against each other: the caparisoned one married with rites (the wife) and the wind-horse that arrives on the bard's errand (the courtesan); the last line makes the husband no rider but a groom, the courtesan's servant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 96","lb":"KL96","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["நீலம்","நீலம்",5,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["குதிரை","குதிரை",6,"maa","simile","horse"],["பாணன்","பாணன்",35,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[["town","மதுரைப்",23]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:97":{"book":"kalittokai","num":97,"t":["அன்னை; கடுஞ் சொல் அறியாதாய் போல, நீ","என்னைப் புலப்பது, ஒறுக்குவென் மன் யான் -","சிறுகாலை இற்கடை வந்து, குறி செய்த","அவ் வழி என்றும் யான் காணேன் திரிதர,","எவ் வழிப் பட்டாய் சமன் ஆக, இவ் எள்ளல்;","முத்து ஏர் முறுவலாய்! நம் வலைப் பட்டது ஓர்","புத்தி யானை வந்தது காண்பான் யான் தங்கினேன்;","ஒக்கும்;","அவ் யானை, வனப்பு உடைத்து ஆகலும் கேட்டேன்;","அவ் யானை தான் சுண்ண நீறு ஆடி, நறு நறா நீர் உண்டு -","ஒள் நுதல் யாத்த திலக அவிர் ஓடைத்,","தொய்யில் பொறித்த வன முலை வான் கோட்டுத்,","தொய்யகம் தோட்டிக், குழை தாழ் வடி மணி,","உத்தி பொறித்த புனை பூண் பருமத்து -","முத்து ஏய்க்கும் வெண் பல் நகை திறந்து,","நல் நகர் வாயில் கதவ வெளில் சார்ந்து,","தன் நலம் காட்டித், தகையினால், கால் தட்டி வீழ்க்கும்,","தொடர் தொடர் ஆக வலந்து, படர் செய்யும்","மென் தோள் தடக் கையின் வாங்கித், தன் கண்டார்","நலம் கவளம் கொள்ளும் நகை முக வேழத்தை,","இன்று கண்டாய் போல், எவன் எம்மைப் பொய்ப்பது, நீ?","எல்லா! கெழீஇத், தொடி செறித்த தோள் இணை, தத்திக்","தழீஇ கொண்டு ஊர்ந்தாயும் நீ;","குழீஇ, அவாவினால் தேம்புவார் இல் கடை ஆறா,","உவா அணி ஊர்ந்தாயும் நீ;","மிகாஅது, சீர்ப்பட உண்ட சிறு களி ஏர் உண் கண்","நீர்க்கு விட்டு, ஊர்ந்தாயும் நீ;","சார்ச் சார் நெறி தாழ் இரும் கூந்தல் நின் பெண்டிர் எல்லாம்","சிறு பாகர் ஆக சிரற்றாது, மெல்ல,","விடாஅது நீ எம் இல் வந்தாய்; அவ் யானை","கடாஅம் படும் இடத்து ஓம்பு."],"p":"","r":"The wife, mocked for scolding though she never spoke harshly, asks where her husband went after coming to her door at dawn; he says a new elephant had fallen into their net and he stayed to see it; she agrees, describing the elephant: it bathed in powder and drank toddy, wore a tilaka for a forehead plate and painted breasts for tusks, smiled with pearl teeth, leaned at the door post, tripped and caught men with its soft arms and swallowed the beauty of those who saw it; why pretend he saw it only today, when he rode it on festival days and after drinking, while his women stood by like little mahouts; let him beware when it is in must.","n":"Each part of a war elephant's gear is matched to the courtesan's adornment (forehead plate to tilaka, tusks to painted breasts, goad and bells to ornaments), and the wild elephant who should be caught is the one catching men. His wives as 'little mahouts' who do not complain (சிறு பாகர்) puts the wife among them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 97","lb":"KL97","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","simile","elephant"],["நறா","நறா",10,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["முத்து","முத்து",15,"pira","simile","pearl"],["வேழத்தை","வேழம்",20,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:98":{"book":"kalittokai","num":98,"t":["யாரை நீ எம் இல் புகுதர்வாய்? ஓரும்","புதுவ மலர் தேரும் வண்டே போல் - யாழ","வதுவை விழவு அணி வைகலும் காட்டினையாய் -","மாட்டு மாட்டு ஓடி, மகளிர் தரத் தரப்,","பூட்டு மான் திண் தேர் புடைத்த மறுகு எல்லாம்","பாட்டு ஆதல் சான்ற நின் மாயப் பரத்தைமை -","காட்டிய வந்தமை கைப்படுத்தேன் - பண்டு எலாம்","கேட்டும் அறிவேன்மன், யான்;","தெரி கோதை அம் நல்லாய்! தேறீயல் வேண்டும் -","பொரு கரை வாய் சூழ்ந்த பூ மலி வையை","வரு புனல் ஆடத் தவிர்ந்தேன்; பெரிது என்னைச்","செய்யா மொழிவது எவன்?","ஓஒ! புனல் ஆடினாய் எனவும் கேட்டேன்; புனல் ஆங்கே","நீள் நீர் நெறி கதுப்பு வாரும் அறல் ஆக,","மாண் எழில் உண் கண், பிறழும் கயல் ஆகக்,","கார் மலர் வேய்ந்த கமழ் பூம் பரப்பு ஆக","நாணுச் சிறை அழித்து நன்பகல் வந்த அவ்","யாணர் புதுப் புனல் ஆடினாய், முன் மாலைப்","பாணன் புணை ஆகப் புக்கு;","ஆனாது, அளித்து அமர் காதலோடு அப் புனல் ஆடி,","வெளிப்படு கவ்வையை யான் அறிதல் அஞ்சிக்,","குளித்து ஒழுகினாய் எனவும் கேட்டேன்; குளித்தாங்கே,","போர்த்த சினத்தான் புருவத் திரை இடா,","ஆர்க்கும் ஞெகிழத்தான் நன் நீர் நடை தட்பச்","சீர்த் தக வந்த புதுப் புனல் நின்னைக் கொண்டு","ஈர்த்து உய்ப்பக் கண்டார் உளர்;","ஈர்த்தது, உரை சால் சிறப்பின் நின் நீர் உள்ளம் வாங்கப்,","புரை தீர் புதுப் புனல் வெள்ளத்தின் இன்னும்","கரை கண்டதூ உம் இலை;","நிரை தொடீஇ! பொய்யா வாள் தானைப், புனை கழல் கால் தென்னவன்","வையைப் புதுப் புனல் ஆடத் தவிர்ந்ததைத்","தெய்வத்தின் தேற்றித் தெளிப்பேன்; பெரிது என்னைச்","செய்யா மொழிவது எவன்;","மெய்யதை, மல்கு மலர் வேய்ந்த மாயப் புதுப் புனல்","பல் காலும் ஆடிய செல்வுழி, ஒல்கிக்","களைஞரும் இல் வழிக் கால் ஆழ்ந்து தேரோடு","இள மணலுள் படல் ஓம்பு - முளை நேர்","முறுவலார்க்கு ஓர் நகை செய்து."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks who her husband is to enter her house, a bee seeking new flowers whose philandering is the song of every chariot street, now caught; he swears he stayed to bathe in the new flood of the Vaigai; she agrees he bathed in a flood, a woman whose hair was its sand-ripples, whose eyes its leaping carp, whose flowers its surface, who broke the dam of modesty at noon, with the bard as his raft; that he bathed in secret fearing the gossip she would hear, and the flood, with frowning waves and ringing anklets, swept him off so that he has not yet reached the shore; when he swears by a god that he bathed in the Pandya's Vaigai, she warns him not to let his chariot sink in the soft sand, where no one will pull him out, and make the women laugh.","n":"Each feature of the river is mapped to a woman (ripples to hair, carp to eyes, the floating flowers to her garlands, the broken embankment to her lost modesty), so his alibi becomes the confession. The closing warning about sinking in sand keeps the river metaphor to the end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 98","lb":"KL98","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வண்டே","வண்டு",2,"pira","simile","bee"],["புனல்","புனல்",11,"pira","scene","flood"],["கயல்","கயல்",15,"pira","simile","carp"],["பாணன்","பாணன்",19,"seythi","simile","bard"],["முளை","முளை",37,"maram","simile","sprout"]],"en":[["river","வையை",10],["king","தென்னவன்",30]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:99":{"book":"kalittokai","num":99,"t":["நறவினை வரைந்தார்க்கும் வரையார்க்கும், அவை எடுத்து","அற வினை இன்புறூஉம் அந்தணர் இருவரும்","திறம் வேறு செய்தியின் நூல் நெறி பிழையாது,","குழவியைப் பார்த்து உறூஉம் தாய் போல், உலகத்து","மழை சுரந்து அளித்து ஓம்பும் நல் ஊழி யாவர்க்கும்","பிழையாது வருதல் நின் செம்மையின் தர, வாய்ந்த","இழை அணி கொடித் திண் தேர் இன மணி யானையாய்!","அறன் நிழல் எனக் கொண்டாய், ஆய் குடை; அக் குடை","புற நிழல் கீழ்ப் பட்டாளோ, இவள்? இவள் காண்டிகா-","பிறை நுதல் பசப்பு ஊரப் பெரு விதுப்பு உற்றாளை!","பொய்யாமை நுவலும் நின் செங்கோல்; அச் செங்கோலின்","செய் தொழில் கீழ்ப் பட்டாளோ, இவள்? இவள் காண்டிகா-","காம நோய் கடைக் கூட்ட வாழும் நாள் முனிந்தாளை!","ஏமம் என்று இரங்கும், நின் எறி முரசம்; அம் முரசின்","ஏமத்து இகந்தாளோ, இவள்? இவள் காண்டிகா-","வேய் நலம் இழந்த தோள் கவின் வாட இழப்பாளை!","ஆங்கு;","நெடிது சேண் இகந்தவை காணினும் தான் உற்ற","வடுக் காட்டக், கண் காணாதற்று ஆக, என் தோழி","தொடி கொட்ப நீத்த கொடுமையைக்","கடிது என உணராமை கடிந்ததோ, நினக்கே?"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hero, addressed as a king whose rightness keeps the rains coming as a mother watches her infant: you hold your parasol as the shade of virtue; did she fall outside its shade, she whose brow is pale with longing? your sceptre proclaims truth; did she fall outside its working, she who is weary of living from love's sickness? your drum proclaims protection; is she beyond it, whose bamboo-smooth shoulders are losing their beauty? Like an eye that sees far things but not its own scar, has it kept you from seeing that leaving my friend was cruel?","n":"The three royal insignia, parasol, sceptre and drum, each promise to cover all subjects, and the refrain asks whether the wife alone fell outside them. The closing simile, the eye that sees far but not the scar beside it, makes his blindness to her a defect of the same faculty that makes him a good ruler.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","pasalai","ali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 99","lb":"KL99","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மழை","மழை",5,"pira","scene","rain"],["யானையாய்","யானை",7,"maa","scene","elephant"],["முரசம்","முரசம்",14,"parai","scene","war drum"],["வேய்","வேய்",16,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:100":{"book":"kalittokai","num":100,"t":["ஈண்டு நீர் மிசைத் தோன்றி இருள் சீக்கும் சுடரே போல்,","வேண்டாதார் நெஞ்சு உட்க, வெரு வந்த கொடுமையும்,","நீண்டு தோன்று உயர் குடை நிழல் என சேர்ந்தார்க்குக்","காண்தகு மதி என்னக் கதிர் விடு தண்மையும்,","மாண்ட நின் ஒழுக்கத்தால் மறு இன்றி, வியன் ஞாலத்து","யாண்டோரும் தொழுது ஏத்தும் இரங்கு இசை முரசினாய்!","'ஐயம் தீர்ந்து யார் கண்ணும் அரும் தவ முதல்வன் போல்","பொய் கூறாய்' என நின்னைப் புகழ்வது கெடாதோ தான்-","நல்கி நீ தெளித்த சொல் நசை என தேறியாள்","பல் இதழ் மலர் உண் கண் பனி மல்கக் காணும் கால்!","'சுரந்த வான் பொழிந்தற்றாச் சூழ நின்று யாவர்க்கும்","இரந்தது நசை வாட்டாய்' என்பது கெடாதோ தான்-","கலங்கு அஞர் உற்று, நின் கமழ் மார்பு நசைஇயாள்","இலங்கு கோல் அவிர் தொடி இறை ஊரக் காணும் கால்!","'உறை வரை நிறுத்த கோல் உயிர் திறம் பெயர்ப்பான் போல்,","முறை செய்தி' என நின்னை மொழிவது கெடாதோ தான்-","அழி படர் வருத்த நின் அளி வேண்டிக் கலங்கியாள்","பழி தபு வாள் முகம் பசப்பு ஊரக் காணும் கால்!","ஆங்கு;","தொல் நலம் இழந்தோள், நீ துணை எனப் புணர்ந்தவள்;","இன் உறல் வியன் மார்ப! 'இனையையால் கொடிது' என","நின்னை யான் கழறுதல் வேண்டுமோ,","என்னோர்கள் இடும்பையும் களைந்தீவாய் நினக்கே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hero, a king of famous drum, harsh to foes like the sun that rises from the sea to drive off darkness and cool like the moon to those under his parasol, that his praise as one who like the great ascetic never lies will be ruined when she who trusted his word weeps, his praise as one who like the rain never disappoints a suppliant will be ruined when her bangles slip, and his praise as one who does justice like Death weighing lives will be ruined when her face pales; she who lost her beauty is the one he took as partner; need I reproach him, who removes the sorrow of people like me?","n":"The three praises (truth, giving, justice) are each said to be undone by one sign of the wife's wasting: tears, slipping bangles, pallor. The last line is irony: the man who relieves everyone's suffering is asked whether he needs to be told of hers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","vaaimai","pugazh","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 100","lb":"KL100","poet":"மருதநிலங்கன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["முரசினாய்","முரசு",6,"parai","scene","war drum"],["மலர்","மலர்",10,"maram","scene","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:6"},"kl:101":{"book":"kalittokai","num":101,"t":["தளி பெறு தண் புலத்துத் தலை பெயற்கு அரும்பு ஈன்று,","முளி முதல் பொதுளிய முள் புற பிடவமும்;","களி பட்டான் நிலையே போல் தடவுபு துடுப்பு ஈன்று,","ஞெலிபு உடன் நிரைத்த ஞெகிழ் இதழ் கோடலும்;","மணி புரை உருவின காயாவும்; பிறவும்;","அணி கொள மலைந்த கண்ணியர்- தொகுபு உடன்,","மாறு எதிர்கொண்ட தம் மைந்துடன் நிறுமார்,","சீறு அரு முன்பினோன் கணிச்சி போல் கோடு சீஇ,","ஏறு தொழூஉப் புகுத்தனர் இயைபு உடன் ஒருங்கு.","அவ் வழி, முழக்கு என, இடி என, முன் சமத்து ஆர்ப்ப-","வழக்கு மாறு கொண்டு, வருபு வருபு ஈண்டி-","நறையொடு துகள் எழ நல்லவர் அணி நிற்பத்,","துறையும், ஆலமும், தொல் வலி மராஅமும்,","முறை உளி பராஅய்ப், பாய்ந்தனர் தொழூஉ.","மேல் பாட்டு உலண்டின் நிறன் ஒக்கும் புன் குருக் கண்","நோக்கு அஞ்சான் பாய்ந்த பொதுவனைச் சாக் குத்திக்,","கோட்டு இடைக் கொண்டு, குலைப்பதன் தோற்றம் காண்-","அம் சீர் அசை இயல் கூந்தல் கை நீட்டியான்","நெஞ்சம் பிளந்து இட்டு, நேரார் நடுவண், தன்","வஞ்சினம் வாய்த்தானும் போன்ம்!","சுடர் விரிந்தன்ன சுரி நெற்றிக் காரி,","விடர் இயம் கண்ணிப் பொதுவனைச் சாடிக்,","குடர் சொரியக் குத்திக், குலைப்பதன் தோற்றம் காண்-","படர் அணி அந்திப், பசும் கண் கடவுள்","இடரிய ஏற்று எருமை நெஞ்சு இடந்து இட்டுக்","குடர் கூளிக்கு ஆர்த்துவான் போன்ம்!","செவி மறை நேர் மின்னும் நுண் பொறி வெள்ளைக்","கதன் அஞ்சான், பாய்ந்த பொதுவனைச் சாடி,","நுதி நுனைக் கோட்டால் குலைப்பதன் தோற்றம் காண்-","ஆர் இருள் என்னான், அரும் கங்குல் வந்து, தன்","தாளின் கடந்து அட்டுத், தந்தையைக் கொன்றானைத்","தோளின் திருகுவான் போன்ம்!","என ஆங்கு;","அணி மாலைக் கேள்வன் தரூஉமார், ஆயர்","மணி மாலை ஊதும் குழல்.","கடாஅக் களிற்றினும் கண்ணஞ்சா ஏற்றை","விடாஅது நீ கொள்குவை ஆயின், படாஅகை","ஈன்றன ஆய மகள் தோள்.","பகலிடக் கண்ணியன், பைதல் குழலன்,","சுவல் மிசைக் கோல் அசைத்த கையன், அயலது,","கொல் ஏறு சாட இருந்தார்க்கு, எம் பல் இரும்","கூந்தல் அணை கொடுப்பேம் யாம்.","'கோளாளர் என் ஒப்பார் இல்' என நம் ஆன் உள்,","தாளாண்மை கூறும் பொதுவன், நமக்கு ஒரு நாள்,","கேளாளன் ஆகாமை இல்லை; அவன் கண்டு","வேளாண்மை செய்தன கண்.","ஆங்கு, ஏறும் வருந்தின; ஆயரும் புண் கூர்ந்தார்;","நாறு இரும் கூந்தல் பொதுமகளிர் எல்லாரும்","முல்லை அம் தண் பொழில் புக்கார், பொதுவரொடு,","எல்லாம் புணர் குறி கொண்டு."],"p":"","r":"When the first rains have budded the pidavam, the kodal and the kaya, the herders, crowned with those flowers, drive their sharp-horned bulls into the pen, pray at the ford, the banyan and the old maraa tree and leap in; three bulls gore their catchers, each like an epic killer (the one who tore open the chest of the man who seized a woman's hair, the god tearing the buffalo's chest at dusk, the son who twisted his father's killer in the night); the herd girls say the girl's shoulders go to the one who takes a bull that fears not even a rutting tusker, ask whether they should give their hair for pillow to those who sat aside, and say the boaster of the herd will be their man one day, until bulls and men are spent and the girls go with the herders into the mullai grove, every tryst kept.","n":"The three goring scenes each end on an epic simile with போன்ம், so the village sport is told in the register of revenge and battle. Lines 39 to 42 are a taunt: the girls will not give their hair as pillow to the one who stood by with flute and staff while the bull gored others. The deity of lines 24 to 26 (பசும் கண் கடவுள், tearing the buffalo's chest and feeding the entrails to the goblins) is not named, and who is meant is not settled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["erukol","maram","punarchi","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 101","lb":"KL101","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பிடவமும்","பிடவம்",2,"maram","scene",""],["கோடலும்","கோடல்",4,"maram","simile",""],["காயாவும்","காயா",5,"maram","scene",""],["கணிச்சி","கணிச்சி",8,"deyvam","simile","axe of the god"],["ஏறு","ஏறு",9,"maa","scene","bull"],["ஆலமும்","ஆலம்",13,"maram","scene",""],["மராஅமும்","மராஅம்",13,"maram","scene",""],["உலண்டின்","உலண்டு",15,"pira","simile","insect"],["பொதுவனைச்","பொதுவன்",16,"seythi","scene",""],["காரி","காரி",21,"maa","scene","black bull"],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",24,"deyvam","simile",""],["எருமை","எருமை",25,"maa","simile","buffalo"],["வெள்ளைக்","வெள்ளை",27,"maa","scene","white bull"],["குழல்","குழல்",35,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["களிற்றினும்","களிறு",36,"maa","scene","tusker"],["ஆன்","ஆன்",43,"maa","scene","cattle"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",49,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:102":{"book":"kalittokai","num":102,"t":["கண் அகல் இரு விசும்பில் கதழ் பெயல் கலந்து ஏற்ற,","தண் நறு பிடவமும், தவழ் கொடித் தளவமும்,","வண்ண வண் தோன்றியும், வயங்கு இணர்க் கொன்றையும்,","அன்னவை பிறவும், பல் மலர் துதையத்","தழையும் கோதையும் இழையும் என்று இவை","தைஇயினர், மகிழ்ந்து திளைஇ விளையாடும்","மட மொழி ஆயத்தவருள் இவள் யார்- உடம்போடு","என் உயிர் புக்கவள், இன்று?","ஓஒ! இவள், 'பொரு புகல் நல் ஏறு கொள்பவர் அல்லால்,","திரு மா மெய் தீண்டலர்' என்று கருமமா,","எல்லாரும் கேட்ப அறைந்து, எப்பொழுதும்","சொல்லால் தரப்பட்டவள்.","'சொல்லுக, பாணியேம்' என்றார்; 'அறைக' என்றார்; பாரித்தார்.","மாண் இழை ஆறு ஆகச் சாறு.","சாற்றுள், பெடை அன்னார் கண் பூத்து, நோக்கும் வாய் எல்லாம்","மிடை பெறின், நேராத் தகைத்து.","தகை வகை மிசை மிசைப் பாயியர் ஆர்த்து, உடன்","எதிர் எதிர் சென்றார் பலர்.","கொலை மலி சிலை செறி செயிர் அயர் சினம் சிறந்து,","உருத்து எழுந்து ஓடின்று மேல்.","எழுந்தது துகள்;","ஏற்றனர் மார்பு;","கவிழ்ந்தன மருப்பு;","கலங்கினர் பலர்.","அவருள், மலர்மலி புகல்எழ அலர்மலி மணிபுரை நிமிர்தோள் பிணைஇ","எருத்தோடு இமில் இடைத் தோன்றினன், தோன்றி,","வருத்தினான் மன்ற, அவ் ஏறு.","ஏறு எவ்வம் காணா எழுந்தார்- எவன் கொலோ-","ஏறு உடை நல்லார்; பகை?","மடவரே நல் ஆயர் மக்கள்- நெருநை,","அடல் ஏற்று எருத்து இறுத்தார்க் கண்டும், மற்று இன்றும்,","உடல் ஏறு கோள் சாற்றுவார்!","ஆங்கு இனித்;","தண்ணுமைப் பாணி தளராது எழூஉக-","பண் அமை இன் சீர் குரவையுள், தெள் கண்ணித்","திண் தோள், திறல் ஒளி, மாயப் போர், மா மேனி,","அம் துவர் ஆடைப் பொதுவனோடு, ஆய்ந்த","முறுவலாள் மென் தோள் பாராட்டிச், சிறுகுடி","மன்றம் பரந்தது, உரை!"],"p":"","r":"A man watching the herd girls play in the rains, decked with pidavam, thalavam, thondri and konrai, asks who among them has today entered his life with her body, and is told she is the girl of whom it was cried before all that no one but the taker of a fighting bull may touch her; the festival is proclaimed, many men leap at the bulls and are thrown, then one herder appears clinging between neck and hump and wears his bull down; the speaker wonders that the herders' daughters, having seen a bull's neck broken yesterday, proclaim the bull-catch again today, and bids the drum keep up the kuravai while the hamlet's common rings with praise of the herder and the smiling girl.","n":"The girl is promised in advance by public cry (எல்லாரும் கேட்ப அறைந்து), so the contest is a betrothal by ordeal rather than a sport. Lines 19 to 24 change metre into short clipped phrases (எழுந்தது துகள்; ஏற்றனர் மார்பு; கவிழ்ந்தன மருப்பு; கலங்கினர் பலர்) that enact the rush of the fight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["erukol","kuravai","kaamam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 102","lb":"KL102","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["பிடவமும்","பிடவம்",2,"maram","ornament",""],["தளவமும்","தளவம்",2,"maram","ornament",""],["தோன்றியும்","தோன்றி",3,"maram","ornament",""],["கொன்றையும்","கொன்றை",3,"maram","ornament",""],["ஏறு","ஏறு",9,"maa","scene","bull"],["பெடை","பெடை",15,"pul","simile","hen bird"],["ஆயர்","ஆயர்",30,"seythi","scene",""],["தண்ணுமைப்","தண்ணுமை",34,"parai","scene",""],["குரவையுள்","குரவை",35,"yaazh","scene","round dance"],["பொதுவனோடு","பொதுவன்",37,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:103":{"book":"kalittokai","num":103,"t":["மெல் இணர்க் கொன்றையும், மென் மலர்க் காயாவும்,","புல் இலை வெட்சியும் பிடவும், தளவும்,","குல்லையும், குருந்தும், கோடலும், பாங்கரும்-","கல்லவும், கடத்தவும் கமழ் கண்ணி மலைந்தனர்,","பல ஆன் பொதுவர்; கதழ் விடை கோள் காண்மார்-","முல்லை முகையும் முருந்தும் நிரைத்தன்ன","பல்லர், பெரு மழைக் கண்ணர், மடம் சேர்ந்த","சொல்லர், சுடரும் கனம் குழைக் காதினர்,","நல்லவர்- கொண்டார், மிடை;","அவர் மிடை கொள-","மணி வரை மருங்கின் அருவி போல","அணி வரம்பு அறுத்த வெண் கால் காரியும்,","மீன் பூத்து அவிர் வரும் அந்தி வான் விசும்பு போல்","வான் பொறி பரந்த புள்ளி வெள்ளையும்,","கொலைவன் சூடிய குழவித் திங்கள் போல்","வளையுபு மலிந்த கோடு அணி சேயும்,","பொரு முரண் முன்பின் புகல் ஏறு பல பெய்து-","அரிமாவும், பரிமாவும், களிறும், கராமும்,","பெரு மலை விடர் அகத்து, ஒருங்கு உடன் குழீஇ,","படு மழை ஆடும் வரை அகம் போலும்-","கொடி நறை சூழ்ந்த தொழூஉ.","தொழுவினுள், புரிபு புரிபு புக்க பொதுவரைத்","தெரிபு தெரிபு குத்தின, ஏறு.","ஏற்றின், அரி பரிபு அறுப்பன, சுற்றி,","எரி திகழ் கணிச்சியோன் சூடிய பிறைக் கண்","உருவ மாலை போலக்","குருதிக் கோட்டொடு குடர் வலந்தன;","கோட்டொடு சுற்றிக் குடர் வலந்த ஏற்றின் முன்","ஆடி நின்று, அக்குடர் வாங்குவான் பீடு காண்-","செந் நூல் கழி ஒருவன் கைப்பற்ற, அந்நூலை","முந் நூலாக் கொள்வானும் போன்ம்!","இகுளை! இஃது ஒன்று கண்டை; இஃது ஒத்தன்;","கோட்டு இனத்து ஆயர் மகன் அன்றே- மீட்டு ஒரான்-","போர் புகல் ஏற்றுப் பிணர் எருத்தில் தத்துபு,","தார் போல் தழீஇயவன்!","இகுளை! இஃது ஒன்று கண்டை; இஃது ஒத்தன்;","கோ இனத்து ஆயர் மகன் அன்றே- ஓவான்-","மறை ஏற்றின் மேல் இருந்து ஆடித் துறை அம்பி","ஊர்வான் போல் தோன்றும் அவன்!","தொழீஇஇ! காற்றுப் போல வந்த கதழ் விடைக் காரியை","ஊற்று களத்தே அடங்கக் கொண்டு, அட்டு, அதன்","மேல் தோன்றி நின்ற பொதுவன் தகை கண்டை-","ஏற்று எருமை நெஞ்சம் வடிம்பின் இடந்து இட்டுச்","சீற்றமொடு ஆர் உயிர் கொண்ட ஞான்று இன்னன் கொல்","கூற்று என, உட்கிற்று, என் நெஞ்சு!","இகுளை! இஃது ஒன்று கண்டை; இஃது ஒத்தன்;","புல் இனத்து ஆயர் மகன் அன்றே- புள்ளி","வெறுத்த வய வெள் ஏற்று அம்புடைத் திங்கள்","மறுப் போல் பொருந்தியவன்!","ஓவா வேகமோடு உருத்துத் தன் மேல் சென்ற","சேஎச் செவி முதல் கொண்டு, பெயர்த்து ஒற்றும்","காயாம் பூங் கண்ணிப் பொதுவன் தகை கண்டை-","மேவார் விடுத்தந்த கூந்தல் குதிரையை","வாய் பகுத்து இட்டுப் புடைத்த ஞான்று, இன்னன் கொல்","மாயோன் என்று உட்கிற்று, என் நெஞ்சு!","ஆங்கு, இரும் புலித் தொழுதியும் பெரும் களிற்று இனமும்","மாறு மாறு உழக்கியாங்கு உழக்கிப், பொதுவரும்","ஏறு கொண்டு, ஒருங்கு தொழூஉ விட்டனர்- விட்டாங்கே","மயில் எருத்து உறழ் அணி மணி நிலத்துப் பிறழப்-","பயில் இதழ் மலர் உண் கண்","மாதர் மகளிரும் மைந்தரும் மைந்து உற்றுத்","தாது எரு மன்றத்து அயர்வர், தழூஉ.","கொல் ஏற்றுக் கோடு அஞ்சுவானை மறுமையும்","புல்லாளே, ஆய மகள்.","அஞ்சார் கொலை ஏறு கொள்பவர் அல்லதை,","நெஞ்சு இலார் தோய்தற்கு அரிய- உயிர் துறந்து-","நைவாரா ஆய மகள் தோள்.","வளியா அறியா உயிர், காவல் கொண்டு,","நளிவாய் மருப்பு அஞ்சும் நெஞ்சினார் தோய்தற்கு","எளியவோ, ஆய மகள் தோள்?","விலை வேண்டார், எம் இனத்து ஆயர் மகளிர்-","கொலை ஏற்றுக் கோட்டு இடைத் தாம் வீழ்வார் மார்பின்","முலை இடைப் போலப், புகின்.","ஆங்கு;","குரவை தழீஇ யாம், மரபுளி பாடி,","தேயா விழுப் புகழ்த் தெய்வம் பரவுதும்-","மாசு இல் வான் முந்நீர்ப் பரந்த தொல் நிலம்","ஆளும் கிழமையொடு புணர்ந்த","எம் கோ வாழியர், இம் மலர் தலை உலகே!"],"p":"","r":"Herders crowned with konrai, kaya, vetchi, pidavu, thalavu, kullai, kurunthu, kodal and paangar come to see the bull-catch while girls with teeth like jasmine buds throng the stand; bulls black with white feet, white with spots like the starred evening sky and red with horns curved like the crescent on the slayer's head fill a pen like a mountain cleft crowded with lion, horse, elephant and crocodile; they gore the herders until entrails wind round their horns like the god's crescent garland, and one herder drawing out entrails is like a man taking a red thread for the sacred triple thread; a girl points out to her friend herder after herder, one clinging to the hump like a garland, one riding like a boatman, one standing over the slain black bull so that her heart quaked as before Death tearing the buffalo's chest, one wrenching a red bull by the ear like Mayon splitting the horse's jaw; then men and girls dance the kuravai, and the girls say the herd girl will not embrace even in another birth the man who fears the horn, that her shoulders are not for the faint-hearted, and that herders' daughters ask no price but that a man fall between the killer bull's horns as between their breasts; the song ends with praise of the god and the king.","n":"The girl's fear (என் நெஞ்சு உட்கிற்று, lines 45 and 55) is told twice through a god's terrible deed, so the herder she admires is seen as divine and dangerous at once. The closing lines put the terms of marriage in the girls' own mouths: no bride-price, only courage before the horn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["erukol","maram","anjaamai","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 103","lb":"KL103","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கொன்றையும்","கொன்றை",1,"maram","ornament",""],["காயாவும்","காயா",1,"maram","ornament",""],["வெட்சியும்","வெட்சி",2,"maram","ornament",""],["பிடவும்","பிடவு",2,"maram","ornament",""],["தளவும்","தளவு",2,"maram","ornament",""],["குல்லையும்","குல்லை",3,"maram","ornament",""],["குருந்தும்","குருந்து",3,"maram","ornament",""],["கோடலும்","கோடல்",3,"maram","ornament",""],["பாங்கரும்","பாங்கர்",3,"maram","ornament",""],["பொதுவர்","பொதுவர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["முல்லை முகையும்","முல்லை",6,"maram","simile",""],["முருந்தும்","முருந்து",6,"pira","simile","peacock quill"],["அருவி","அருவி",11,"pira","simile",""],["காரியும்","காரி",12,"maa","scene","black bull"],["வெள்ளையும்","வெள்ளை",14,"maa","scene","white bull"],["சேயும்","சே",16,"maa","scene","red bull"],["அரிமாவும்","அரிமா",18,"maa","simile","lion"],["பரிமாவும்","பரிமா",18,"maa","simile","horse"],["களிறும்","களிறு",18,"maa","simile","tusker"],["கராமும்","கராம்",18,"pira","simile","crocodile"],["கணிச்சியோன்","கணிச்சியோன்",25,"deyvam","simile",""],["கூற்று","கூற்று",45,"deyvam","simile",""],["திங்கள்","திங்கள்",48,"pira","simile","moon"],["குதிரையை","குதிரை",53,"maa","simile","horse"],["மாயோன்","மாயோன்",55,"deyvam","simile",""],["புலித்","புலி",56,"maa","simile","tiger"],["மயில்","மயில்",59,"pul","simile","peacock"],["குரவை","குரவை",75,"yaazh","scene","round dance"],["தெய்வம்","தெய்வம்",76,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:104":{"book":"kalittokai","num":104,"t":["மலி திரை ஊர்ந்து தன் மண் கடல் வௌவலின்,","மெலிவு இன்றி மேல் சென்று, மேவார் நாடு இடம்படப்,","புலியொடு வில் நீக்கிப், புகழ் பொறித்த கிளர் கெண்டை,","வலியினான் வணக்கிய, வாடாச் சீர் தென்னவன்","தொல் இசை நட்ட குடியொடு தோன்றிய","நல் இனத்து ஆயர், ஒருங்கு தொக்கு, எல்லாரும்-","வான் உற ஓங்கிய வயங்கு ஒளிர் பனைக் கொடிப்","பால் நிற வண்ணன் போல் பழி தீர்ந்த வெள்ளையும்,","பொரு முரண் மேம்பட்ட பொலம் புனை புகழ் நேமித்","திரு மறு மார்பன் போல் திறல் சான்ற காரியும்,","மிக்கு ஒளிர் தாழ் சடை மேவரும் பிறை நுதல்","முக்கண்ணான் உருவே போல் முரண் மிகு குராலும்,","மா கடல் கலக்குற மா கொன்ற மடங்காப் போர்","வேல் வல்லான் நிறனே போல் வெரு வந்த சேயும், ஆங்கு அப்","பொரு வரும் பண்பினவ்வையும், பிறவும்","உருவப் பல் கொண்மூக் குழீஇயவை போலப்,","புரிபு புரிபு புகுத்தனர், தொழூஉ.","அவ் வழி, முள் எயிற்று ஏஎர் இவளை பெறும், இது ஓர்","வெள் ஏற்று எருத்து அடங்குவான்.","ஒள் இழை, வாருறு கூந்தல் துயில் பெறும், வை மருப்பின்","காரி கதன் அந்ஜ்சான் கொள்பவன்,- ஈர் அரி","வெரூஉப் பிணை மான் நோக்கின் நல்லாள் பெறூஉம், இக்","குரூஉக் கண் கொலை ஏறு கொள்வான்,- வரிக் குழை","வேய் உறழ் மென் தோள் துயில் பெறும், வெந்துப்பின்","சேஎய் சினன் அஞ்சான் சார்பவன்,- என்று ஆங்கு","அறைவனர், நல்லாரை, ஆயர் முறையினால்","நாள்மீன் வாய் சூழ்ந்த மதி போல், மிடை மிசைப்","பேணி நிறுத்தார் அணி.","அவ் வழி பறை எழுந்து இசைப்பப், பல்லவர் ஆர்ப்பக்,","குறையா மைந்தர் கோள் எதிர் எடுத்த-","நறை வலம் செய விடா இறுத்தன ஏறு.","அவ் ஏற்றின்,","மேல் நிலை மிகல் இகலின், மிடை கழிபு இழிபு, மேல் சென்று,","வேல் நுதி புரை விறல் திறன் நுதி மருப்பின் மாறு அஞ்சான்,","பால் நிற வெள்ளை எருத்தத்து பாய்ந்தானை","நோனாது குத்தும் இளம் காரித் தோற்றம் காண்-","பால் மதி சேர்ந்த அரவினைக் கோள் விடுக்கும்","நீல் நிற வண்ணனும் போன்ம்.","இரிபு எழுபு அதிர்பு அதிர்பு இகந்து உடன் பலர் நீங்க,","அரிபு அரிபு இறுபு இறுபு குடர் சோரக் குத்தித் தன்","கோடு அழியக் கொண்டானை ஆட்டித் திரிபு உழக்கும்","வாடில் வெகுளி எழில் ஏறு கண்டை- இஃது ஒன்று-","வெரு வரு தூமம் எடுப்ப, வெகுண்டு","திரிதரும் கொல் களிறும் போன்ம்.","தாள் எழு துணி பிணி, இசை தவிர்பு இன்றித் தலைச் சென்று,","தோள் வலி துணி பிணி, துறந்து இறந்து எய்தி, மெய் சாய்ந்து","கோள் வழுக்கித் தன் முன்னர் வீழ்ந்தான் மேல் செல்லாது,","மீளும் புகர் ஏற்றுத் தோற்றம் காண்- மண்டு அமருள்","வாள் அகப்பட்டானை 'ஒவ்வான்' எனப் பெயரும்","மீளி மறவனும் போன்ம்.","ஆங்க, செறுத்து அறுத்து உழக்கி ஏற்று எதிர் நிற்ப,","மறுத்து மறுத்து மைந்தர் சாரத்,","தடி குறை இறுபு இறுபு தாயின கிடப்ப-","இடி உறழ் இசையின் இயம் எழுந்து ஆர்ப்பப்-","பாடு ஏற்றுக் கொள்பவர் பாய்ந்து மேல் ஊர்பவர்,","கோடு இடை நுழைபவர், கோள் சாற்றுபவரொடு-","புரிபு மேல் சென்ற நூற்றுவர் மடங்க,","வரி புனை வல் வில் ஐவர் அட்ட","பொரு களம் போலும், தொழூஉ.","தொழுவினுள் கொண்ட ஏறு எல்லாம் புலம் புகத் தண்டாச் சீர்","வாங்கு எழில் நல்லாரும், மைந்தரும், மல்லல் ஊர்","ஆங்கண் அயர்வர், தழூஉ.","பாடுகம் வம்மின்- பொதுவன் கொலை ஏற்றுக்","கோடு குறி செய்த மார்பு.","நெற்றிச் சிவலை நிறை அழித்தான் நீள் மார்பில்,","செற்றார் கண் சாய, யான் சாராது அமைகல்லேன்,","பெற்றத்தார் கவ்வை எடுப்ப, அது பெரிது","உற்றீயாள், ஆயர் மகள்.","தொழீஈ ஒருக்கு நாம் ஆடும் குரவையுள் நம்மை","அருக்கினான் போல் நோக்கி, அல்லல் நோய் செய்தல்,","'குரூஉக் கண் கொலை ஏறு கொண்டேன், யான்' என்னும்","தருக்கு அன்றோ- ஆயர் மகன்?","நேர் இழாய்! கோள் அரிது ஆக நிறுத்த கொலை ஏற்றுக்","காரி கதன் அஞ்சான் பாய்ந்த பொதுவற்கே,","ஆர்வுற்று, எமர், கொடை நேர்ந்தார்- அலர் எடுத்த","ஊராரை உச்சி மிதித்து.","ஆங்கு;","தொல் கதிர் திகிரியான் பரவுதும்- ஒல்கா","உரும் உறழ் முரசின் தென்னவற்கு","ஒரு மொழி கொள்க, இவ் உலகு உடன், எனவே."],"p":"","r":"The good herders of the ancient line that rose with the Pandya, who when the sea took his land went unwearied against enemy lands and set his fish emblem in place of the tiger and the bow, gather and pen bulls white like the palmyra-bannered god, black like the god with Sri on his chest, grey like the three-eyed one and red like the spear-bearer, massed like clouds; the girls are set on the stand like the moon ringed by stars, each promised to the taker of a named bull; a young black bull gores the man who leapt on the white bull's neck, like the dark god freeing the moon from the serpent, another bull rages with entrails on its horns like a killer elephant in smoke, a spotted bull turns away from a fallen man like a warrior who will not strike a foe who has lost his sword, and the pen is like the field where the five archers killed the hundred; after the kuravai a girl says she cannot rest without the chest of the man who broke the red-browed bull's pride though the cattle-folk talk, and the friend tells her that her kin have gladly agreed to give her to the herder who leapt on the black bull, trampling on the heads of the town that raised the gossip; the song ends with praise of the god of the discus and the Pandya.","n":"The poem opens with Pandya history (the sea that swallowed his land, the Chola tiger and Chera bow displaced by the fish) and so ties the herders' festival to the dynasty. The spotted bull that spares a fallen man (lines 45 to 50) carries the battlefield rule of not striking the disarmed into the bull-pen. The bull-catch ends the gossip by producing the kin's consent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["erukol","alar","varaivu","kuravai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 104","lb":"KL104","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கெண்டை","கெண்டை",3,"pira","scene","carp emblem"],["ஆயர்","ஆயர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["பனைக்","பனை",7,"maram","simile","palmyra banner"],["பால் நிற வண்ணன்","பால் நிற வண்ணன்",8,"deyvam","simile",""],["வெள்ளையும்","வெள்ளை",8,"maa","scene","white bull"],["காரியும்","காரி",10,"maa","scene","black bull"],["முக்கண்ணான்","முக்கண்ணான்",12,"deyvam","simile",""],["குராலும்","குரால்",12,"maa","scene","grey bull"],["வேல் வல்லான்","வேல் வல்லான்",14,"deyvam","simile",""],["சேயும்","சே",14,"maa","scene","red bull"],["வேய்","வேய்",24,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["பறை","பறை",29,"parai","scene",""],["ஏறு","ஏறு",31,"maa","scene","bull"],["நீல் நிற வண்ணனும்","நீல் நிற வண்ணன்",38,"deyvam","simile",""],["களிறும்","களிறு",44,"maa","simile","tusker"],["பெற்றத்தார்","பெற்றத்தார்",67,"seythi","scene","cattle folk"],["குரவையுள்","குரவை",69,"yaazh","scene","round dance"],["முரசின்","முரசு",79,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[["king","தென்னவன்",4],["king","தென்னவற்கு",79]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:105":{"book":"kalittokai","num":105,"t":["அரைசு படக் கடந்து அட்டு, ஆற்றின் தந்த","முரைசு கெழு முது குடி முரண் மிகு செல்வற்குச்-","சீர் மிகு சிறப்பினோன் தொல் குடிக்கு உரித்து எனப்","பார் வளர், முத்தமொடு படு கடல் பயந்த","ஆர் கலி உவகையர் ஒருங்கு உடன் கூடித்,","'தீது இன்று பொலிக!' என தெய்வக் கடி அயர்மார்,","வீவு இல் குடிப் பின் இரும் குடி ஆயரும்,","தா இல் உள்ளமொடு துவன்றி, ஆய்பு உடன்,","வள் உருள் நேமியான் வாய் வைத்த வளை போலத்","தெள்ளிதின் விளங்கும் சுரி நெற்றக் காரியும்,","ஒரு குழையவன் மார்பில் ஒள் தார் போல் ஒளி மிகப்","பொரு அறப் பொருந்திய செம் மறு வெள்ளையும்,","பெரும் பெயர் கணிச்சியோன் மணி மிடற்று அணி போல","இரும் பிணர் எருத்தின் ஏந்து இமில் குராலும்","அணங்கு உடை வச்சிரத்தோன் ஆயிரம் கண் ஏய்க்கும்","கணம் கொள் பல் பொறிக் கடும் சினப் புகரும்","வேல் வலான் உடைத் தாழ்ந்த விளங்கு வெண் துகில் ஏய்ப்ப","வாலிது கிளர்ந்த வெண் கால் சேயுஉம்","கால முன்பின் பிறவும், சால","மடங்கலும், கணிச்சியும், காலனும், கூற்றும்,","தொடர்ந்து செல் அமையத்து துவன்று உயிர் உணீஇய,","உடங்கு கொட்பன போல் புகுத்தனர், தொழூஉ.","அவ் வழி;","கார் எதிர் கலி ஒலி கடி இடி உருமின் இயம் கறங்க,","ஊர்பு எழு கிளர்பு உளர் புயல் மங்குலின் நறை பொங்க,","நேர் இதழ் நிரை நிரை நெறி வெறக் கோதையர் அணி நிற்பச்,","சீர் கெழு சிலை நிலைச் செயிர் இகல் மிகுதியின், சினப் பொதுவர்","தூர்பு எழு துதை புதை துகள் விசும்பு உற எய்த,","ஆர்பு, உடன் பாய்ந்தார், அகத்து.","மருப்பில் கொண்டும், மார்பு உற தழீஇயும்,","எருத்து இடை அடங்கியும், இமில் இறப் புல்லியும்","தோள் இடைப் புகுதந்தும், துதைந்து பாடு ஏற்றும்,","நிரைபு மேல் சென்றாரை நீள் மருப்பு உறச் சாடிக்,","கொள இடம் கொள விடா நிறுத்தன, ஏறு.","கொள்வாரைக் கொள்வாரைக் கோட்டு வாய் சாக் குத்திக்,","கொள்வார் பெறாஅக் குரூஉச் செகில் காணிகா-","செயிரின் குறை நாளால் பின் சென்று சாடி,","உயிர் உண்ணும் கூற்றமும் போன்ம்!","பாடு ஏற்றவரைப் படக் குத்திச் செங் காரிக்","கோடு எழுந்து ஆடும் கண மணி காணிகா-","நகை சால் அவிழ் பதம் நோக்கி, நறவின்","முகை சூழும் தும்பியும் போன்ம்!","இடைப் பாய்ந்து எருத்தத்துக் கொண்டானோடு எய்தி,","மிடைப் பாயும் வெள் ஏறு கண்டைகா-","வாள் பொரு வானத்து, அரவின் வாய் கோட்பட்டுப்","போதரும் பால் மதியும் போன்ம்!","ஆங்க, ஏறும் பொதுவரும் மாறுற்று, மாறா","இரு பெரு வேந்தரும் இகலி கண்ணுற்ற","பொரு களம் போலும், தொழூஉ;","வெல் புகழ் உயர்நிலைத் தொல் இயல் துதை புதை துளங்கு இமில்","நல் ஏறு கொண்ட, பொதுவன் முகன் நோக்கிப்,","பாடு இல, ஆய மகள் கண்.","நறு நுதால்!- என் கொல்- ஐங்கூந்தல் உளரச்","சிறு முல்லை நாறியதற்கு குறு மறுகி,","ஒல்லாது உடன்று, எமர் செய்தார், அவன் கொண்ட","கொல் ஏறு போலும் கதம்?","நெட்டிரும் கூந்தலாய்! கண்டை, இஃது ஓர் சொல்;","கோட்டு இனத்து ஆயர் மகனொடு யாம் பட்டதற்கு","எம் கண் எமரோ பொறுப்பர்; பொறாதார்","தம் கண் பொடிவது எவன்?","ஒள் நுதால்","இன்ன உவகை பிறிது யாது-யாய் என்னைக்","கண் உடைக் கோலள் அலைத்ததற்கு, என்னை","மலர் அணி கண்ணிப் பொதுவனோடு எண்ணி,","அலர் செய்துவிட்டது இவ் ஊர்?","ஒன்றிப் புகர் இனத்து ஆய மகற்கு- ஒள் இழாய்!-","இன்று எவன், என்னை எமர் கொடுப்பது- அன்று, அவன்","மிக்குத் தன் மேல் சென்ற செங் காரிக் கோட்டு இடைப்","புக்கக் கால் புக்கது, என் நெஞ்சு!","என;","பாடு இமிழ் பரப்பு அகத்து அரவணை அசைஇய","ஆடு கொள் நேமியான் பரவுதும்-'நாடு கொண்டு","இன் இசை முரசின் பொருப்பன், மன்னி","அமை வரல் அருவி ஆர்க்கும்","இமையத்து உம்பர் உம் விளங்குக!' எனவே."],"p":"","r":"The herders of the old line gather to hold the god's guarding rite for the ancient drum-bearing house of the conqueror to whom the sea yielded pearls, and pen bulls whose marks are the gods' (black with a white curl like the conch at the discus-bearer's lips, white with a red spot like the garland of the one-earringed god, grey with a hump like the axe-bearer's dark throat, spotted like the thunderbolt-bearer's thousand eyes, red with white feet like the spear-bearer's white cloth), together like lion, axe, Kaalan and Death come to eat lives; the herders leap in amid thunderous drums and dust, the bulls hold them off, a red bull gores like Death, bells on another's horns are like bees round toddy buds, and the white bull carrying a man is like the moon slipping from the serpent's mouth; the herd girl's eyes cannot close looking at the face of the herder who took the bull, and she says to her friend: why did her kin rage like his bull because her hair smelled of mullai; what is it to those who cannot bear it, if her own kin bear what she has done with the herder's son; what joy equals this, that her mother beat her with a stick and the town made gossip linking her to the herder; and why give her now, when her heart went into the red bull's horns that day with him; the song ends with praise of the god on the serpent couch and the Pandya.","n":"The girl counts her mother's beating and the town's gossip as joy, because they have made the bond public and so forced the marriage; her last lines date the real union to the moment the herder went between the horns. Each bull is coloured by a god's emblem, so the pen is a gathering of gods and of death at once (lines 20 to 22).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["erukol","alar","varaivu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 105","lb":"KL105","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["முத்தமொடு","முத்தம்",4,"pira","scene","pearl"],["ஆயரும்","ஆயர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["நேமியான்","நேமியான்",9,"deyvam","simile",""],["வளை","வளை",9,"pira","simile","conch"],["காரியும்","காரி",10,"maa","scene","black bull"],["வெள்ளையும்","வெள்ளை",12,"maa","scene","white bull"],["கணிச்சியோன்","கணிச்சியோன்",13,"deyvam","simile",""],["குராலும்","குரால்",14,"maa","scene","grey bull"],["வச்சிரத்தோன்","வச்சிரத்தோன்",15,"deyvam","simile",""],["புகரும்","புகர்",16,"maa","scene","spotted bull"],["வேல் வலான்","வேல் வலான்",17,"deyvam","simile",""],["சேயுஉம்","சே",18,"maa","scene","red bull"],["மடங்கலும்","மடங்கல்",20,"maa","simile","lion"],["பொதுவர்","பொதுவர்",27,"seythi","scene",""],["தும்பியும்","தும்பி",42,"pira","simile","bee"],["அரவின்","அரவு",45,"pira","simile","serpent"],["பால் மதியும்","மதி",46,"pira","simile","moon"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",54,"maram","ornament",""],["நேமியான்","நேமியான்",72,"deyvam","scene",""],["முரசின்","முரசு",73,"parai","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",74,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["hill","இமையத்து",75]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:106":{"book":"kalittokai","num":106,"t":["கழுவொடு சுடு படை சுருக்கிய தோல் கண்.","இமிழ் இசை மண்டை உறியொடு தூக்கி,","ஒழுகிய கொன்றைத் தீம் குழல் முரற்சியர்,","வழூஉ சொல் கோவலர், தம் தம் இன நிரை","பொழுதொடு தோன்றிய கார் நனை வியன் புலத்தார்.","அவ் வழி","நீறு எடுப்பவை, நிலம் சாடுபவை,","மாறு ஏற்றுச் சிலைப்பவை, மண்டிப் பாய்பவையாய்த்","துளங்கு இமில் நல் ஏற்று இனம் பல - களம் புகும்","மள்ளர் வனப்பு ஒத்தன.","தாக்குபு தம் உள் பெயர்த்து ஒற்றி, எவ் வாயும்,","வை வாய் மருப்பினால் மாறாது குத்தலின்,","மெய் வார் குருதிய ஏறு எல்லாம் - பெய் காலைக்","கொண்டல் நிரை ஒத்தன.","அவ் ஏற்றை","பிரிவு கொண்டு, இடைப் போக்கி, இனத்தோடு புனத்து ஏற்றி,","இரு திறனா நீக்கும் பொதுவர் -","உரு கெழு மா நிலம் இயற்றுவான்,","விரி திரை நீக்குவான், வியன் குறிப்பு - ஒத்தனர்.","அவரைக் கழல உழக்கி, எதிர் சென்று சாடி,","அழல் வாய் மருப்பினால் குத்தி, உழலை","மரத்தைப் போல் தொட்டன - ஏறு.","தொட்ட தம், புண் வார் குருதியால் கை பிசைந்து, மெய் திமிரித்","தங்கார் - பொதுவர் - கடலுள் பரதவர்","அம்பி ஊர்ந்தாங்கு, ஊர்ந்தார், ஏறு.","ஏறு தம் கோலம் செய் மருப்பினால் தோண்டிய வரிக் குடர்","ஞாலம் கொண்டு எழூஉம் பருந்தின் வாய் வழீஇ,","ஆலும் கடம்பும் அணிமார் விலங்கிட்ட","மாலை போல், தூங்கும் சினை.","ஆங்கு,","தம் புல ஏறு பரத்தர உய்த்த தம்","அன்பு உறு காதலர் கை பிணைந்து, ஆய்ச்சியர்","இன்புற்று அயர்வர், தழூஉ.","முயங்கிப் பொதிவேம்; முயங்கிப் பொதிவேம்;","முலை வேதின் ஒற்றி முயங்கிப் பொதிவேம் -","கொலை ஏறு சாடிய புண்ணை - எம் கேளே!","பல் ஊழ் தயிர் கடையத் தாஅய புள்ளி மேல்","கொல் ஏறு கொண்டான் குருதி மயக்குறப்","புல்லல் எம் தோளிற்கு அணியோ! எம் கேளே!","ஆங்கு, போர் ஏற்று அரும் தலை அஞ்சலும் ஆய்ச்சியர்","காரிகை தோள் காமுறுதலும் இவ் இரண்டும்","ஓராங்குச் சேறல் இலவோ? - எம் கேளே!","'கொல் ஏறு கொண்டான் இவள் கேள்வன்' என்று ஊரார்","சொல்லும் சொல் கேளா, அளை மாறி யாம் வரும்","செல்வம் எம் கேள்வன் தருமோ? - எம் கேளே!","ஆங்க,","அரும் தலை ஏற்றொடு காதலர்ப் பேணிச்,","சுரும்பு இமிர் கானம் நாம் பாடினம் பரவுதும்;","ஏற்றவர் புலம் கெடத் திறை கொண்டு,","மாற்றாரைக் கடக்க, எம் மறம் கெழு கோவே!"],"p":"","r":"Herders carrying their gear and sounding pots slung in nets, playing sweet flutes of konrai pod, come with their herds at the rainy hour to the wide wet field; the bulls, raising dust, pawing, bellowing and charging, are like warriors entering battle and, bloodied, like a line of rain clouds; the herders who part them into two groups are like the god who made the earth by parting the waves; the bulls gore them as if they were posts, yet the wounded herders do not rest but ride the bulls as fishermen ride their boats, and entrails dropped by kites hang on the branches like garlands on the banyan and kadamba; then the herd girls dance the kuravai holding the hands of the lovers who drove the bulls back, singing that they will bind the bull-gored wounds against their breasts, that the blood of the tamer on their curd-spotted shoulders is an ornament, that fear of the bull's head and desire for the herd girls' shoulders cannot go together, and asking whether their man will give them the wealth of hearing the town say 'her man took the killer bull' as they go selling buttermilk; they close by praising the forest and their warlike king.","n":"The girls' song values the wound as the warrior poems value a wound taken facing the enemy, and asks for fame (the town's word) as the 'wealth' a husband brings. The fishermen's boats (line 24) bring a shore image into the herders' poem. Line 1, a list of the herders' gear, is obscure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["erukol","kuravai","maram","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 106","lb":"KL106","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கொன்றைத்","கொன்றை",3,"maram","scene",""],["குழல்","குழல்",3,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["ஏற்று","ஏறு",9,"maa","scene","bull"],["பொதுவர்","பொதுவர்",17,"seythi","scene",""],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",24,"seythi","simile",""],["பருந்தின்","பருந்து",27,"pul","scene","kite"],["ஆலும்","ஆல்",28,"maram","simile",""],["கடம்பும்","கடம்பு",28,"maram","simile",""],["ஆய்ச்சியர்","ஆய்ச்சியர்",32,"seythi","scene",""],["தயிர்","தயிர்",37,"unavu","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:107":{"book":"kalittokai","num":107,"t":["எல்லா! இஃது ஒன்று - கூறு குறும்பு இவர்","புல் இனத்தார்க்கும், குடம் சுட்டவர்க்கும், எம்","கொல் ஏறு கோடல் குறை எனக், கோவினத்தார்","பல் ஏறு பெய்தார் தொழூஉ.","தொழுவத்து","சில்லைச் செவி மறைக் கொண்டவன் சென்னிக் குவி முல்லை","கோட்டம் காழ் கோட்டின் எடுத்துக்கொண்டு, ஆட்டிய","ஏழை இரும் புகர் பொங்க, அப் பூ வந்து என்","கூழையுள் வீழ்ந்தன்று மன்;","அதனைக், கெடுத்தது பெற்றார் போல், கொண்டு யான் முடித்தது","கேட்டனள், என்பவோ, யாய்?","கேட்டால் எவன் செய்ய வேண்டுமோ? மற்று இகா?","அவன் கண்ணி அன்றோ, அது?","'பெய் போது அறியாத் தன் கூழையுள் ஏதிலான்","கை புனை கண்ணி முடித்தாள்' என்று, யாய் கேட்பின்","செய்வது இல் ஆகுமோ மற்று?","எல்லாத் தவறும் அறும்.","ஓஒ! அஃது அறும் ஆறு?","ஆயர் மகன் ஆயின், ஆய மகள் நீ ஆயின்,","நின் வெய்யன் ஆயின், அவன் வெய்யை நீ ஆயின்,","அன்னை நோதக்கதோ இல்லை மன்; - நின் நெஞ்சம்,","அன்னை நெஞ்சு ஆகப் பெறின்.","அன்னையோ?","ஆயர் மகனையும் காதலை; கைம்மிக","ஞாயையும் அஞ்சுதி; ஆயின், அரிது அரோ","நீ உற்ற நோய்க்கு மருந்து.","மருந்து இன்று, யான் உற்ற துயர் ஆயின், - எல்லா!","வருந்துவேன் அல்லனோ, யான்?","வருந்தாதி -","மண்ணி மாசு அற்ற நின் கூழையுள் ஏறு அவன்","கண்ணி தந்திட்டது எனக் கேட்டுத், 'திண்ணிதாத்","தெய்வ மால், காட்டிற்று இவட்கு' என, நின்னை அப்","பொய் இல் பொதுவற்கு அடை சூழ்ந்தார் - தந்தையோடு","ஐயன்மார் எல்லாம் ஒருங்கு."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that when the cattle-herders penned their bulls as a challenge to the sheep-herders and others, the great spotted bull tossed with its horn the mullai chaplet from the head of the man who caught it by the ear and it fell into her hair, and she tied it up as if she had found something lost: will her mother hear? The friend asks what of it, it is his chaplet; the girl fears the mother will hear she tied a stranger's chaplet in her hair; the friend says all fault is cut away if he is a herder's son and she a herder's daughter and they love each other, if only her heart can become her mother's; the girl asks how she is to bear a sickness with no cure, and the friend tells her not to grieve: hearing that the bull put his chaplet in her washed hair, her father and brothers have all agreed that a great god has shown it, and have resolved to give her to that true herder.","n":"The bull acts as the go-between: the kin read the chaplet falling into her hair as a god's sign (தெய்வ மால் காட்டிற்று), so chance becomes betrothal. The bull's act cannot be struck out without the poem's sense falling, so it is the subject, not iraicchi. Lines 1 to 4 (the challenge to the புல் இனத்தார் and the குடம் சுட்டவர்) are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","erukol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 107","lb":"KL107","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஏறு","ஏறு",3,"maa","scene","bull"],["குவி முல்லை","முல்லை",6,"maram","ornament",""],["புகர்","புகர்",8,"maa","scene","spotted bull"],["ஆயர்","ஆயர்",19,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:108":{"book":"kalittokai","num":108,"t":["இகல் வேந்தன் சேனை இறுத்த வாய் போல -","அகல் அல்குல் தோள் கண் என மூ வழிப் பெருகி,","நுதல், அடி, நுசுப்பு என மூ வழி சிறுகிக்,","கவலையால் காமனும் படை விடு வனப்பினோடு,","அகல் ஆங்கண் அளை மாறி, அலமந்து பெயரும்கால்,","'நகை வல்லேன் யான்' என்று என் உயிரோடு படை தொட்ட","இகலாட்டி! நின்னை எவன் பிழைத்தேன், எல்லா! யான்?","அஃது அவலம் அன்று மன;","ஆயர் எமர் ஆனால் ஆய்த்தியேம் யாம் மிகக்;","காயாம் பூம் கண்ணிக் கரும் துவர் ஆடையை,","மேயும் நிரை முன்னர்க் கோல் ஊன்றி நின்றாய், ஓர்","ஆயனை அல்லை; பிறவோ அமரர் உள்","ஞாயிற்றுப் புத்தேள் மகன்?","அதனால் வாய்வாளேன்;","முல்லை முகையும் முருந்தும் நிரைத்தன்ன","பல்லும் பணைத் தோளும் பேர் அமர் உண் கண்ணும்,","'நல்லேன், யான்,' என்று, நலம் தகை நம்பிய","சொல்லாட்டி! நின்னொடு சொல் ஆற்றுகிற்பார் யார்?","சொல்லாதி;","'நின்னை தகைத்தனென்,' அல்லல் காண்மன்.","மண்டாத கூறி, மழ குழக்கு ஆகின்றே,","கண்ட பொழுதே கடவரைப் போல, நீ","பண்டம் வினாய படிற்றால் தொடீஇய, நின்","கொண்டது எவன் - எல்லா! - யான்?","கொண்டது;","அளை மாறிப் பெயர் தருவாய்! - அறிதியோ? - அஞ்ஞான்று","தளவ மலர் ததைந்தது ஓர் கானச் சிற்றாற்று அயல்,","இள மாங்காய் போழ்ந்தன்ன கண்ணினா, என் நெஞ்சம்","களமாக் கொண்டு ஆண்டாய், ஓர் கள்வியை அல்லையோ?","நின் நெஞ்சம் களமாக் கொண்டு யாம் ஆளல் எமக்கு எவன் எளிது ஆகும்","புனத்து உளான் என்னைக்குப் புகா உய்த்துக் கொடுப்பதோ?","இனத்து உளான் எந்தைக்குக் கலத்தொடு செல்வதோ?","தினைக் காலுள் யாய் விட்ட கன்று மேய்க்கிற்பதோ?","அனைத்து ஆக","வெண்ணெய்த் தெழி கேட்கும் அண்மையால், சேய்த்து அன்றி,","அண்ண அணித்து ஊர் ஆயின், நன்பகல் போழ்து ஆயின்,","கண் நோக்கு ஒழிக்கும் கவின் பெறு பெண் நீர்மை","மயில் எருத்து வண்ணத்து மாயோய்! மற்று இன்ன","வெயிலொடு, எவன், விரைந்து சேறி? உது காண்.","பிடி துஞ்சு அன்ன அறை மேல, நுங்கின்","தடி கண் புரையும் குறும் சுனை ஆடிப்,","பனிப் பூம் தளவொடு முல்லை பறித்துத்,","தனிக் காயாம் தண் பொழில், எம்மொடு வைகிப்,","பனிப் படச் செல்வாய், நும் ஊர்க்கு.","இனிச் செல்வேம் யாம்;","மா மருண்டன்ன மழைக் கண் சி(ற்)று ஆய்த்தியர்","நீ மருட்டும் சொல்க் கண் மருள்வார்க்கு உரை, அவை;","ஆ முனியா ஏறு போல் வைகல், பதின்மரைக்","காமுற்றுச் செல்வாய்; ஓர் கண் குத்தி கள்வனை;","நீ எவன் செய்தி, பிறர்க்கு?","யாம் எவன் செய்தும், நினக்கு?","கொலை உண் கண் கூர் எயிற்றுக் கொய் தளிர் மேனி,","இனை வனப்பின், மாயோய்! நின்னின் சிறந்தார்","நில உலகத்து இன்மை தெளி; நீ வருதி;","மலையொடு மார்பு அமைந்த செல்வன் அடியைத்","தலையினால் தொட்டு உற்றேன், சூள்.","ஆங்கு உணரார் நேர்ப; அது பொய்ப்பாய் நீ; ஆயின் -","தேம் கொள் பொருப்பன் சிறுகுடி எம் ஆயர்","வேந்து ஊட்டு அரவத்து, நின் பெண்டிர் காணாமை,","காஞ்சித் தாது உக்கன்ன தாது எரு மன்றத்துத்","தூங்கும் குரவையுஉள் நின் பெண்டிர் கேளாமை,","ஆம்பல் குழலால் பயிர் பயிர் - எம் படப்பைக்","காஞ்சிக் கீழ் செய்தேம் குறி."],"p":"","r":"A herder meets a buttermilk girl and they trade words: he calls her the warrior who, wide in hips, shoulders and eyes and slender in brow, feet and waist, took up arms against his life, and asks how he wronged her; she mocks him as no herder, in his kaya chaplet and dark cloth leaning on his staff, but perhaps the sun-god's son; he says he will not argue with one who trusts her mullai-bud teeth, bamboo shoulders and battle eyes; she asks what she took from him that he stops her like a creditor; he says that by a stream rich in thalavam she ruled his heart as a battlefield with eyes like split young mangoes; she asks how she could rule his heart when her days go in taking food to her brother, pots to her father and the calves to graze; he urges her, since it is noon and the village near, to bathe in the rock pool, gather thalavam and mullai and stay with him in the kaya grove till dew falls; she tells him to keep such words for little herd girls, since like a bull that never tires of cows he goes after ten a day; he swears by the feet of the lord whose chest is like a mountain that none on earth surpasses her; and she, saying he will break that oath, sets the tryst: when the herders feast and dance the kuravai, unseen and unheard by his women, he is to call with the aambal flute, under the kaanchi tree in their garden.","n":"The division of speakers is ours and several lines (20, 21, 49) are hard. The girl's mockery is courtship: she accuses him of many loves and then names the place, the signal and the hour of their meeting. The oath by the lord 'whose chest is joined with the hill' (மலையொடு மார்பு அமைந்த செல்வன்) names a god we cannot fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","sool","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 108","lb":"KL108","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"],["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["காயாம் பூம்","காயா",10,"maram","ornament",""],["நிரை","நிரை",11,"maa","scene","herd"],["ஞாயிற்றுப் புத்தேள்","ஞாயிறு",13,"deyvam","scene",""],["முல்லை முகையும்","முல்லை",15,"maram","simile",""],["தளவ","தளவம்",27,"maram","scene",""],["மாங்காய்","மாங்காய்",28,"maram","simile",""],["தினைக்","தினை",33,"maram","scene","millet"],["வெண்ணெய்த்","வெண்ணெய்",35,"unavu","scene",""],["மயில்","மயில்",38,"pul","scene","peacock"],["பிடி","பிடி",40,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["நுங்கின்","நுங்கு",40,"maram","simile","palm fruit"],["தளவொடு","தளவம்",42,"maram","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",42,"maram","scene",""],["மா","மா",46,"maa","simile","deer"],["ஏறு","ஏறு",48,"maa","simile","bull"],["காஞ்சித்","காஞ்சி",60,"maram","simile",""],["ஆம்பல் குழலால்","ஆம்பல் குழல்",62,"yaazh","scene","aambal flute"],["காஞ்சிக்","காஞ்சி",63,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:109":{"book":"kalittokai","num":109,"t":["கார் ஆரப் பெய்த கடி கொள் வியன் புலத்துப்","பேராது சென்று, பெரும் பதவப் புல் மாந்தி,","நீர் ஆர் நிழல குடம் சுட்டு இனத்து உள்ளும்","போர் ஆரா ஏற்றின், பொரு நாகு, இள பாண்டில்","தேர் ஊரச் செம்மாந்தது போல், மதைஇனள் -","பேர் ஊரும் சி(ற்)று ஊரும் கௌவை எடுப்பவள் போல்,","மோரோடு வந்தாள் - தகை கண்டை; யாரோடும்","சொல்லியாள் அன்றே வனப்பு!","பண்ணித் தமர் தந்து ஒரு புறம் தைஇய","கண்ணி எடுக்கல்லாக் கோடு ஏந்து அகல் அல்குல் -","புண் இல்லார் புண் ஆக நோக்கும்; முழு மெய்யும்","கண்ணளோ? - ஆய மகள்!","இவள் தான் திருத்தாச் சுமட்டினள், ஏனைத் தோள் வீசி","வரிக் கூழ வட்டி தழீஇ, அரிக் குழை","ஆடல் தகையள்; கழுத்தினும் வாலிது","நுண்ணிதாத் தோன்றும், நுசுப்பு.","இடை தெரியா ஏஎர் இருவரும் தம் தம்","உடை வனப்பு எல்லாம் இவட்கு ஈத்தார் கொலோ?","படை இடுவான் மன் கண்டீர், காமன் - மடை அடும்","பாலொடு கோட்டம் புகின்,","இவள் தான், வருந்த நோய் செய்து இறப்பின் அல்லால், மருந்து அல்லள் -","'யார்க்கும் அணங்கு ஆதல் சான்றாள்' என்று ஊர்ப் பெண்டிர்,","'மாங்காய் நறும் காடி கூட்டுவேம், யாங்கும்","எழு நின் கிளையொடு போக' என்று, தம் தம்","கொழுநரைப் போகாமல் காத்து, முழு நாளும்,","வாயில் அடைப்ப, வரும்."],"p":"","r":"A man tells a companion to look at the herd girl who comes with buttermilk, proud as the young heifer of an untiring fighting bull in a herd grazing the great grass of the rain-soaked field, as if she would raise gossip in big town and small: her beauty speaks to everyone, her eyes wound even the unwounded, her waist shows finer than her throat, as if the two beauties had given her all theirs, and Kama would lay down his arms if she entered the pen with the milk; the town's wives, agreeing she is fit to be an anangu to all, offer her mango pickle to send her away with her kin, guard their husbands and shut their doors all day when she comes.","n":"The comedy is in the town's response: what the man sees as beauty the wives treat as a public danger and fence against. Lines 17 to 20 (the two beauties without waists, Kama laying down his weapons, the cattle-pen) are hard, and line 6 has a bracketed editorial letter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["anangu","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 109","lb":"KL109","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["பதவப் புல்","பதவம்",2,"maram","scene","grass"],["ஏற்றின்","ஏறு",4,"maa","simile","bull"],["நாகு","நாகு",4,"maa","simile","heifer"],["மோரோடு","மோர்",7,"unavu","scene",""],["பாலொடு","பால்",20,"unavu","scene",""],["மாங்காய்","மாங்காய்",23,"maram","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:110":{"book":"kalittokai","num":110,"t":["கடி கொள் இரும் காப்பில் புல் இனத்து ஆயர்","குடி தொறும் நல்லாரை வேண்டுதி - எல்லா! -","இடு தேள் மருந்தோ, நின் வேட்கை? தொடுதரத்","துன்னித் தந்தாங்கே நகை குறித்து, எம்மைத்","திளைத்தற்கு எளியமாக் கண்டை. 'அளைக்கு எளியாள்","வெண்ணெய்க்கும் அன்னள்' எனக் கொண்டாய் - ஒள் நுதால்","ஆங்கு நீ கூறின், அனைத்து ஆக; நீங்குக;","அச்சத்தான் மாறி, அசைவினான் போத்தந்து","நிச்சம் தடுமாறும் - மெல் இயல் ஆய் மகள்!","மத்தம் பிணித்த கயிறு போல் நின் நலம்","சுற்றிச் சுழலும் என் நெஞ்சு.","விடிந்த பொழுதினும் இல் வயின் போகாது,","கொடும் தொழுவினுள் பட்ட கன்றிற்குச் சூழும்","கடும் சூல் ஆ நாகு போல், நின் கண்டு நாளும்","நடுங்கு அஞர் உற்றது - என் நெஞ்சு.","எவ்வம் மிகுதர, எம் திறத்து, எஞ்ஞான்றும்,","நெய் கடை பாலின் பயன் யாதும் இன்று ஆகிக்,","கை தோயல் மாத்திரை அல்லது, செய்தி","அறியாது - அளித்து என் உயிர்.","அன்னையோ? - மன்றத்துக் கண்டாங்கே, 'சான்றார் மகளிரை","இன்றி அமையேன்' என்று, இன்னவும் சொல்லுவாய்;","நின்றாய்; நீ சென்றீ; எமர் காண்பர்; நாளையும்","கன்றொடு சேறும், புலத்து."],"p":"","r":"The herd girl tells the man that he goes after the good girls of every herder's house as if his desire were a cure for a scorpion sting, and takes her for easy, thinking a girl easy to buy buttermilk from is easy for butter too; he answers that his heart, as she shrinks back in fear and comes forward in weariness, circles her beauty like the rope round the churning stick, trembles daily like a heavy-bellied young cow that will not go home at dawn but circles her calf caught in the pen, and like milk churned for ghee yielding nothing, only wets the hand; she says he speaks so to every good woman he sees on the common, tells him to go before her kin see, and adds that tomorrow too she will go with the calves to the field.","n":"Her last line, after the rebuff, tells him where she will be tomorrow, so the refusal carries an appointment. All his similes come from her own work of milking and churning, as if he speaks her trade back to her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","nenju","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 110","lb":"KL110","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"],["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["ஆயர்","ஆயர்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["வெண்ணெய்க்கும்","வெண்ணெய்",6,"unavu","scene",""],["மத்தம்","மத்தம்",10,"pira","simile","churning stick"],["கன்றிற்குச்","கன்று",13,"maa","simile","calf"],["ஆ நாகு","நாகு",14,"maa","simile","young cow"],["பாலின்","பால்",17,"unavu","simile",""],["கன்றொடு","கன்று",23,"maa","scene","calf"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:111":{"book":"kalittokai","num":111,"t":["தீம் பால் கறந்த கலம் மாற்றக், கன்று எல்லாம்","தாம்பின் பிணித்து, மனை நிறீஇ, யாய் தந்த","பூம் கரை நீலம் புடை தாழ மெய் அசைஇ, பாங்கரும்","முல்லையும் தாய பாட்டம் கால் - தோழி! - நம்","புல் இனத்து ஆயர் மகளிரோடு எல்லாம்","ஒருங்கு விளையாட, அவ் வழி வந்த","குருந்தம் பூம் கண்ணிப் பொதுவன், மற்று என்னை,","'முற்று இழை ஏஎர் மட நல்லாய்! நீ ஆடும்","சிற்றில் புனைகோ சிறிது?' என்றான்; 'எல்லா! நீ","'பெற்றேம் யாம்' என்று பிறர் செய்த இல் இருப்பாய்;","கற்றது இலை மன்ற காண்' என்றேன்; 'முற்று இழாய்!","தாது சூழ் கூந்தல் தகைபெறத் தைஇய","கோதை புனைகோ நினக்கு?' என்றான்; 'எல்லா! நீ","ஏதிலார் தந்த பூக் கொள்வாய்; நனி மிகப்","பேதையை மன்ற பெரிது' என்றேன்; 'மாதராய்!","ஐய பிதிர்ந்த சுணங்கு அணி மென் முலை மேல்","தொய்யில் எழுதுகோ மற்று?' என்றான்; 'யாம் பிறர்","செய் புறம் நோக்கி இருத்துமோ? நீ பெரிது","மையலை மாதோ; விடுக' என்றேன். தையலாய்!","சொல்லிய ஆறு எல்லாம் மாறு மாறு யான் பெயர்ப்,","அல்லாந்தான் போலப் பெயர்ந்தான் அவனை நீ","ஆயர் மகளிர் இயல்பு உரைத்து, எந்தையும்","யாயும் அறிய உரைத்தீயின், யான் உற்ற","நோயும் களைகுவை மன்."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that when she had tethered the calves after milking and gone in the blue-lily-bordered cloth her mother gave her to play with the herd girls where paangar and mullai spread, a herder in a kurunthu chaplet asked whether he might build her play-house, then tie a garland in her hair, then paint designs on her breasts, and she turned each offer back (he would sit in a house others built; a girl who took a stranger's flowers would be a great fool; should they wait for what others paint on them?), so that he went off as if stricken; and she asks the friend to tell her father and mother the ways of herd girls, so that the sickness she has caught may be cured.","n":"The three refusals keep the herd girls' rule that a stranger's gifts are not taken, and her last sentence shows that she loves him: she wants him by the open road, through her parents. That is the friend's task of disclosure (அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்), asked for in so many words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","naanam","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 111","lb":"KL111","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["பால்","பால்",1,"unavu","scene",""],["கன்று","கன்று",1,"maa","scene","calf"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",3,"maram","ornament","blue lily"],["பாங்கரும்","பாங்கர்",3,"maram","scene",""],["முல்லையும்","முல்லை",4,"maram","scene",""],["ஆயர்","ஆயர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["குருந்தம் பூம்","குருந்து",7,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:112":{"book":"kalittokai","num":112,"t":["யார் இவன், என்னை விலக்குவான்? நீர் உளர்","பூம் தாமரைப் போது தந்த விரவுத் தார்க்","கல்லாப் பொதுவனை; நீ மாறு; நின்னொடு","சொல்லல் ஓம்பு என்றார், எமர்.","எல்லா! 'கடாஅய கண்ணால், கலைஇய நோய் செய்யும்","நடாஅக் கரும்பு அமன்ற தோளாரைக் காணின்,","விடாஅல், ஓம்பு' என்றார், எமர்.","கடாஅயார், நல்லாரைக் காணின், விலக்க, நயந்து, அவர்","பல் இதழ் உண் கண்ணும் தோளும் புகழ் பாட,","நல்லது கற்பித்தார் மன்ற, நுமர். பெரிதும்","வல்லர், எமர் கண் செயல்.","ஓஒ! வழங்காப் பொழுது நீ கன்று மேய்ப்பாய் போல்,","வழங்கல் அறிவார் உரையாரேல், எம்மை","இகழ்ந்தாரே அன்றோ, எமர்?","ஒக்கும்; அறிவல் - யான் எல்லா! - விடு.","'விடேன், யான்; என், நீ குறித்தது? - இரும் கூந்தால்!","நின்னை, \"என் முன் நின்று","சொல்லல் ஓம்பு\" என்றமை அன்றி, \"அவனை நீ","புல்லல் ஓம்பு\" என்றது உடையரோ? மெல்ல","முயங்கு, நின் முள் எயிறு உண்கும். எவன் கொலோ?","மாயப் பொதுவன் உரைத்த உரை எல்லாம்","வாய் ஆவது ஆயின், தலைப்பட்டாம்; பொய் ஆயின்","சாயல் இன் மார்பில், கமழ் தார் குழைத்த நின்","ஆய் இதழ் உண் கண் பசப்பத் தட மென் தோள்","சாயினும் ஏஎர் உடைத்து."],"p":"","r":"Stopped on her way, the girl asks who this unlettered herder in a garland mixed with water lotus is, whom her kin told her to avoid and not speak to; he says his kin told him never to let go of girls with shoulders like unplanted sugarcane whose eyes bring love-sickness; she says his kin taught him well to stop good women and sing of their eyes and shoulders, and hers are skilled at watching; he says her kin would have slighted them if, while she herds calves at an hour when no one passes, those who know right ways did not speak to her; she says she knows, let go; he refuses: her kin forbade her to speak before him, not to embrace him, so let her embrace him gently and he will drink from her sharp teeth; and she reflects that if all the cunning herder said comes true they are joined, and if it proves false, then even should her eyes pale and her shoulders waste from his fragrant chest, it has its beauty.","n":"The man turns her kin's prohibition into a loophole (they forbade speech, not embrace). Her last sentence accepts the risk of being deceived and counts even the pallor and wasting that would follow as beautiful. The speakers' turns are ours; lines 12 to 14 are hard to assign.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","punarchi","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 112","lb":"KL112","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",2,"maram","ornament",""],["பொதுவனை","பொதுவன்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["கரும்பு","கரும்பு",6,"maram","scene",""],["கன்று","கன்று",12,"maa","scene","calf"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:113":{"book":"kalittokai","num":113,"t":["நலம் மிக நந்திய நய வரு தட மென் தோள்,","அலமரல் அமர் உண் கண், அம் நல்லாய்! நீ உறீஇ,","உலமரல் உயவு நோய்க்கு உய்யும் ஆறு உரைத்துச் செல்.","பேர் ஏமுற்றார் போல, முன் நின்று, விலக்குவாய்","யார் - எல்லா! நின்னை அறிந்ததூ உம் இல் வழி?","தளிர் இயால்! என் அறிதல் வேண்டின், பகை அஞ்சாப்","புல் இனத்து ஆயர் மகனேன், மற்று யான்.","ஒக்கும் மன்;","புல் இனத்து ஆயனை நீ ஆயின், குடம் சுட்டு","நல் இனத்து ஆயர், எமர்.","எல்லா!","நின்னொடு சொல்லின், ஏதமோ இல்லை மன்;","ஏதம் அன்று எல்லை வருவான் விடு.","விடேன்,","உடம்பட்டு நீப்பார் கிளவி மடம் பட்டு.","மெல்லிய ஆதல் அறியினும், மெல்லியால்!","நின் மொழி கொண்டு, யானோ விடுவேன்? - மற்று என் மொழி கொண்டு","என் நெஞ்சம் ஏவல் செயின்?","'நெஞ்சு ஏவல் செய்யாது' என நின்றாய்க்கு, 'எஞ்சிய","காதல் கொள் காமம் கலக்குற' ஏதிலார்","பொய்ம் மொழி தேறுவது என்?","தெளிந்தேன், தெரி இழாய்! யான்.","பல் கால், யாம் கான் யாற்று அவிர் மணல் தண் பொழில்,","அல்கல் அகல் அறை, ஆயமொடு ஆடி,","முல்லை குருந்தொடு முச்சி வேய்ந்து, எல்லை","இரவு உற்றது; இன்னும் கழிப்பி; அரவு உற்று","உருமின் அதிரும் குரல் போல், பொரு முரண்","நல் ஏறு நாகு உடன் நின்றன,","பல் ஆன் இன நிரை நாம் உடன் செலற்கே."],"p":"","r":"A man asks the girl with soft shoulders and restless eyes to tell him, before she goes, how he may escape the love-sickness she has caused; she asks who he is to stop her like a madman when they have never met; he says he is a son of the sheep-herders who fear no foe; she answers that, if so, her kin are the good cattle-herders of the pot-mark, there is no harm in talking but someone may come, so let her go; he will not let go on the word of one who consents and then withdraws, for how can he obey her word when his heart does not obey his; she asks why trust the lies of strangers; he says he is sure of her, and reminds her that they have often played with the companions in the cool sand groves of the forest stream, crowned with mullai and kurunthu: night has come, and the bulls roaring like thunder that strikes the serpent stand with the heifers, so that the herd may go home with them.","n":"The two speak as members of rival herding groups (புல் இனத்து ஆயர், sheep and goats; குடம் சுட்டு நல் இனத்து ஆயர், cattle), and her objection is to his rank before it is to him. The closing image of the bulls standing with the heifers, ready to go home with the pair, is tagged as iraicchi below on a borderline deletion test.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kudi","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 113","lb":"KL113","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஆயர்","ஆயர்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",25,"maram","ornament",""],["குருந்தொடு","குருந்து",25,"maram","ornament",""],["அரவு","அரவு",26,"pira","simile","serpent"],["உருமின்","உரும்",27,"pira","simile","thunder"],["நல் ஏறு","ஏறு",28,"maa","iraicchi","bull"],["நாகு","நாகு",28,"maa","iraicchi","heifer"],["நிரை","நிரை",29,"maa","scene","herd"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","நல் ஏறு","not_confirmed","0/1","as the bulls keep with their mates, the man means the two of them to go home together"]],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:114":{"book":"kalittokai","num":114,"t":["வாரி, நெறிப்பட்டு, இரும் புறம் தாஅழ்ந்த","ஓரிப் புதல்வன் அழுதனன் என்பவோ...","புதுவ மலர் தைஇ, எமர் என் பெயரால்,","வதுவை அயர்வாரை கண்டு, 'மதி அறியா","ஏழையை' என்று அகல நக்கு, வந்தீயாய், நீ -","தோழி! - அவன் உழைச் சென்று","சென்று யான் அறிவேன்; கூறுக. மற்று இனி.","'சொல் அறியாப் பேதை - மடவை! - மற்று எல்லா!","நினக்கு ஒரூஉம்; மற்று என்று அகல் அகலும்; நீடு இன்று;","நினக்கு வருவதாக் காண்பாய். அனைத்து ஆகச்","சொல்லிய சொல்லும் வியம் கொளக் கூறு.","தரு மணல் தாழப் பெய்து, இல் பூவல் ஊட்டி,","எருமைப் பெடையோடு, எமர் ஈங்கு அயரும்","பெரு மணம் எல்லாம் தனித்தே ஒழிய -","வரி மணல் முன்துறைச் சிற்றில் புனைந்த","திரு நுதல் ஆயத்தார் தம்முள் புணர்ந்த","ஒரு மணம் தான் அறியும்; ஆயின் எனைத்தும்","தெருமரல் கைவிட்டு இருக்கோ... அலர்ந்த","விரி நீர் உடுக்கை உலகம் பெறினும்","அரு நெறி ஆயர் மகளிர்க்கு","இரு மணம் கூடுதல் இல் இயல்பு அன்றே?"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend, who laughed aloud when the kin began a wedding in her name with new flowers and called her a silly girl who knows nothing, to go to the herder and tell him, in words that will make him wonder, what she says: though the great wedding her kin prepare here, spreading sand low, washing the house with red earth and bringing a she-buffalo, be left void, she knows only the one wedding she made among her bright-browed companions who built play-houses on the striped sand of the ford; and even if they gained the whole sea-girt world, it is not the nature of herd girls of the hard way to join in two weddings.","n":"She dates her marriage to the secret union, so a wedding to another man would be a second marriage that chastity forbids: karpu here precedes the kin's rite. The poem has two gaps (lines 2 and 18) and lines 1 to 2 (the crying son with long hair) do not fit the rest; much of the reading is uncertain. The she-buffalo of line 13 is a field-land animal in a herders' poem.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["karpu","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 114","lb":"KL114","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["மலர்","மலர்",3,"maram","ornament",""],["மணல்","மணல்",12,"pira","scene","sand"],["பூவல்","பூவல்",12,"pira","scene","red earth"],["எருமைப்","எருமை",13,"maa","scene","buffalo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:115":{"book":"kalittokai","num":115,"t":["தோழி! நாம், காணாமை உண்ட கடும் கள்ளை, மெய் கூர","நாணாது சென்று நடுங்க உரைத்தாங்குக்","கரந்ததூஉம் கையொடு கோள் பட்டாம், கண்டாய்; நம்","புல் இனத்து ஆயர் மகன் சூடி வந்தது ஓர்","முல்லை ஒரு காழும் கண்ணியும், மெல்லியால்!","கூந்தலுள் பெய்து முடித்தேன் மன்; தோழி! யாய்","வெண்ணெய் உரைஇ விரித்த கதுப்போடே,","அன்னையும் அத்தனும் இல்லரா, யாய் நாண,","அன்னை முன் வீழ்ந்தன்று அப் பூ.","அதனை வினவலும் செய்யாள்; சினவலும் செய்யாள்;","நெருப்புக் கை தொட்டவர் போல விதிர்த்திட்டு,","நீங்கிப்புறங்கடை போயினாள். யானும், என்","சாந்து உளர் கூழை முடியா, நிலம் தாழ்ந்த","பூம் கரை நீலம் தழீஇத், தளர்பு ஒல்கிப்,","பாங்கரும் கானத்து ஒளித்தேன். - அதற்கு, எல்லா","ஈங்கு எவன் அஞ்சுவது?","அஞ்சல் - அவன் கண்ணி நீ புனைந்தாய் ஆயின், நமரும்","அவன் கண் அடை சூழ்ந்தார் நின்னை. அகல் கண்","வரைப்பில் மணல் தாழப் பெய்து, திரைப்பில்","வதுவையும் ஈங்கே அயர்ப; அதுவே யாம்","அல்கலும் சூழ்ந்த வினை!"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend they have been caught red-handed, like those who drink strong toddy in secret and then go and talk shamelessly till they shake: she had tied in her hair a strand of mullai and the chaplet the herder wore, and when her mother had rubbed butter in and spread out her hair, the flower fell before her; the mother neither asked nor scolded but shook her hand like one who had touched fire and went out by the back door, and she, hair unbound, clutching her blue-lily cloth and faltering, hid in the paangar woods; the friend answers that there is nothing to fear: if she wore his chaplet, their people too have resolved to give her to him, will spread sand in the wide yard and hold the wedding here, and that is what they have planned every night.","n":"The mother's silence (neither asking nor scolding) is the sharpest reproach in the poem and also its turning point: the flower makes the secret public, and the kin's decision follows. The kin's intention of line 18 (அடை சூழ்ந்தார்) is the same phrase as Kalittokai 107 line 33.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","naanam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 115","lb":"KL115","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கள்ளை","கள்",1,"unavu","simile","toddy"],["ஆயர்","ஆயர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",5,"maram","ornament",""],["வெண்ணெய்","வெண்ணெய்",7,"unavu","scene",""],["நெருப்புக்","நெருப்பு",11,"pira","simile","fire"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",14,"maram","ornament","blue lily"],["பாங்கரும்","பாங்கர்",15,"maram","scene",""],["மணல்","மணல்",19,"pira","scene","sand"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:116":{"book":"kalittokai","num":116,"t":["பாங்கு அரும் பாட்டம் கால் கன்றொடு செல்வேம் எம்","தாம்பின் ஒரு தலை பற்றினை, ஈங்கு எம்மை","முன்னை நின்றாங்கே விலக்கிய எல்லா! நீ","என்னையே முற்றாய் விடு.","விடேஎன்; தொடீஇய செல்வார்த் துமித்து, எதிர் மண்டும்","கடு வய நாகு போல் நோக்கித் தொழு வாயில்","நீங்கிச் சினவுவாய் மற்று.","நீ நீங்கு, கன்று சேர்ந்தார்கண் கத ஈற்று ஆ சென்றாங்கு","வன்கண்ணள் ஆய் வரல் ஓம்பு;","யாய் வருக ஒன்றோ, பிறர் வருக; மற்று நின்","கோ வரினும் இங்கே வருக; தளரேன் யான்,","நீ அருளி நல்க பெறின்.","நின்னை யான் சொல்லினவும் பேணாய், நினைஇ","கனை பெயல் ஏற்றின் தலை சாய்த்து, எனையதூஉம்","மாறு எதிர் கூறி, மயக்குப்படுகுவாய்! -","கலத்தொடு யாம் செல்வுழி நாடிப், புலத்தும்","வருவையால் - நாண் இலி! நீ."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells the man who caught one end of her tether rope and stopped her as she went with the calves to the field to let her go; he refuses: she glares like a fierce young cow that charges those who come to touch it, and will rage when she leaves the pen-gate; she bids him keep off and not come at her like a cow that has calved going at those near its calf; he says let her mother come, or others, or even her king, he will not falter if she grants him grace; and she calls him shameless, one who ignores what she said, bows his head like a bull in heavy rain, answers back to everything and is lost in confusion, and who will seek out where she goes with the milk-pots and come even to the field.","n":"Her last words (he will come even to the field where she takes the pots) tell him where to find her, so the scolding ends as an invitation. Both trade cattle images: the angry cow guarding her calf, the bull bowing under rain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 116","lb":"KL116","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["கன்றொடு","கன்று",1,"maa","scene","calf"],["நாகு","நாகு",6,"maa","simile","young cow"],["ஆ","ஆ",8,"maa","simile","cow"],["ஏற்றின்","ஏறு",14,"maa","simile","bull"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:117":{"book":"kalittokai","num":117,"t":["மாண உருக்கிய நல் பொன் மணி உறீஇ,","பேணித் துடைத்தன்ன மேனியாய்! கோங்கின்","முதிரா இள முகை ஒப்ப, எதிரிய","தொய்யில் பொறித்த வன முலையாய்! மற்று, நின்","கையது எவன்? மற்று உரை.","'கையதை - சேரிக் கிழவன் மகளேன் யான்; மற்று இஃது ஓர்","மாதர்ப் புலைத்தி விலை ஆகச் செய்தது ஓர்","போழில் புனைந்த வரிப் புட்டில்' - 'புட்டில் உள் என் உள?","காண்தக்காய்! என் காட்டிக் காண்.'","காண், இனி; தோட்டார் கதுப்பின் என் தோழி அவரொடு","காட்டுச் சார் கொய்த சிறு முல்லை, மற்று இவை.","முல்லை இவை ஆயின் - முற்றிய கூழையாய்!","எல்லிற்றுப் போழ்து ஆயின் - ஈதோளிக் கண்டேனால்;","'செல்' என்று நின்னை விடுவேன், யான்; மற்று எனக்கு","மெல்லியது, ஓராது அறிவு."],"p":"","r":"The man praises the girl, whose body is like refined gold set with gems and wiped clean and whose painted breasts are like the unopened buds of the kongu, and asks what she has in her hand; she says she is the daughter of the headman of the herders' quarter and it is a striped basket of palm strips that a pulaitti made for sale; he asks to be shown what is in it; she shows him the small mullai her friend picked with others by the forest; and he says that, if these are mullai and the hour is evening, now that he has seen her here he would tell her to go and let her go, but his understanding, not weighing it, is too weak.","n":"The mullai flowers, the flower of evening and of waiting, turn his small talk into a claim: the hour of meeting has come, and he cannot let her go. She names her father's rank before anything else.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","azhagu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 117","lb":"KL117","poet":"சோழன் நல்லுத்திரன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["பொன்","பொன்",1,"pira","simile","gold"],["மணி","மணி",1,"pira","simile","gem"],["கோங்கின்","கோங்கு",2,"maram","simile",""],["புலைத்தி","புலைத்தி",7,"seythi","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",11,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:7"},"kl:118":{"book":"kalittokai","num":118,"t":["வெல் புகழ் மன்னவன், விளங்கிய ஒழுக்கத்தால்,","நல் ஆற்றின் உயிர் காத்து, நடுக்கு அறத் தான் செய்த","தொல் வினைப் பயன் துய்ப்ப, துறக்கம் வேட்டு எழுந்தாற்போல் -","பல் கதிர் ஞாயிறு பகல் ஆற்றி மலை சேர,","ஆனாது கலுழ் கொண்ட உலகத்து, மற்று அவன்","ஏனையான் அளிப்பான் போல், இகல் இருள் மதி சீப்பக்,","குடை நிழல் ஆண்டாற்கும் ஆளிய வருவாற்கும்","இடை நின்ற காலம் போல், இறுத்தந்த மருள் மாலை!","மாலை நீ - தூ அறத் துறந்தாரை நினைத்தலின், கயம் பூத்த","போது போல் குவிந்த என் எழில் நலம் எள்ளுவாய்;","ஆய் சிறை வண்டு ஆர்ப்ப, சினைப் பூ போல் தளை விட்ட","காதலர்ப் புணர்ந்தவர் காரிகை கடிகல்லாய்.","மாலை நீ - தை எனக் கோவலர் தனிக் குழல் இசை கேட்டு","பையென்ற நெஞ்சத்தேம் பக்கம் பாராட்டுவாய்;","செவ்வழி யாழ் நரம்பு அன்ன கிளவியார் பாராட்டும்,","பொய் தீர்ந்த புணர்ச்சியுள் புது நலம் கடிகல்லாய்.","மாலை நீ - தகை மிக்க தாழ் சினைப் பதி சேர்ந்து புள் ஆர்ப்பப்","பகை மிக்க நெஞ்சத்தேம் புன்மை பாராட்டுவாய்;","தகை மிக்க புணர்ச்சியார் தாழ் கொடி நறு முல்லை","முகை முகம் திறந்தன்ன, முறுவலும் கடிகல்லாய்.","என ஆங்கு;","மாலையும் அலரும் நோனாது, எம் வயின்","நெஞ்சமும் எஞ்சும்மன் தில்ல - எஞ்சி,","உள்ளாது அமைந்தோர், உள்ளும்,","உள் இல் உள்ளம் உள் உள் உவந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The woman addresses the evening, which has come like the interval between a just king's going to heaven at the end of his reign and his successor's taking up the rule: it mocks her beauty, closed like a pond flower by thoughts of the man who left, fusses over hearts made weary by the herders' flute and the clamour of homing birds, yet cannot dim the beauty, sweet talk and opening smile of lovers who are together; unable to bear both the evening and the gossip, her heart wastes, while those who settled down without thinking of her rejoice.","n":"Each stanza sets what evening does to the parted against what it cannot do to the united (கடிகல்லாய்), so the hour is cruel only by her circumstance. The opening political simile makes evening a lawless interregnum. The closing lines (22 to 25) are compressed and line 23 was repaired; the reading of the last line, the empty heart of the forgetful man rejoicing, is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","alar","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 118","lb":"KL118","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["போது","போது",10,"maram","simile",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",11,"pira","simile",""],["சினைப் பூ","சினைப் பூ",11,"maram","simile",""],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",13,"seythi","scene",""],["குழல்","குழல்",13,"yaazh","scene",""],["யாழ்","யாழ்",15,"yaazh","simile",""],["புள்","புள்",17,"pul","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",19,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:119":{"book":"kalittokai","num":119,"t":["அகல் ஞாலம் விளக்கும் தன் பல் கதிர் வாய் ஆக","பகல் நுங்கியது போலப் படு சுடர் கல் சேர,","இகல் மிகு நேமியான் நிறம் போல இருள் இவர,","நிலவுக் காண்பது போல அணி மதி ஏர்தரக்,","கண் பாயல் பெற்ற போல் கணைக் கால மலர் கூம்பத்","தம் புகழ் கேட்டார் போல் தலை சாய்த்து மரம் துஞ்ச,","முறுவல் கொள்பவை போல முகை அவிழ்பு புதல் நந்தச்","சிறு வெதிர் குழல் போலச் சுரும்பு இமிர்ந்து இம்மெனப்,","பறவை தம் பார்ப்பு உள்ளக், கறவை தம் பதி வயின்","கன்று அமர் விருப்பொடு மன்று நிறை புகுதர,","மா வதி சேர, மாலை வாள் கொள","அந்தி அந்தணர் எதிர்கொள, அயர்ந்து","செந்தீச் செவ் அழல் தொடங்க - வந்ததை","வால் இழை மகளிர் உயிர் பொதி அவிழ்க்கும்","காலை ஆவது அறியார்,","மாலை என்மனார் மயங்கியோரே!"],"p":"","r":"As the sun sets as if it had swallowed the day, darkness rises the colour of the discus-bearer, the moon comes up, flowers close as if their eyes had found sleep, trees bow their heads, buds open like smiles, bees hum like bamboo flutes, birds think of their young, cows crowd home longing for their calves, beasts go to their lairs and brahmins light the evening fire; those who call this hour evening are deluded, not knowing it is the hour that unwraps the life women keep bound up.","n":"The whole catalogue is of homing and closing: birds, cows, beasts and the fire-priests all return to their own, which is the unspoken contrast with the absent man. The last word turns on காலை, which may mean the fixed hour (of ending) or the dawn; either way the poem denies that evening is merely evening.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 119","lb":"KL119","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மலர்","மலர்",5,"maram","simile",""],["மரம்","மரம்",6,"maram","simile",""],["முகை","முகை",7,"maram","simile",""],["புதல்","புதல்",7,"maram","scene",""],["வெதிர்","வெதிர்",8,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",8,"pira","simile",""],["பறவை","பறவை",9,"pul","scene",""],["கறவை","கறவை",9,"maa","scene",""],["கன்று","கன்று",10,"maa","scene",""],["மா","மா",11,"maa","scene","beast"],["அந்தணர்","அந்தணர்",12,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:120":{"book":"kalittokai","num":120,"t":["அருள் தீர்ந்த காட்சியான், அறன் நோக்கான், நயம் செய்யான்","வெருவுற உய்த்தவன் நெஞ்சம் போல், பைபய","இருள் தூர்பு, புலம்பு ஊரக், கனை சுடர் கல் சேர -","உரவுத் தகை மழுங்கித் தன் இடும்பையால் ஒருவனை","இரப்பவன் நெஞ்சம் போல் புல்லென்று, புறம் மாறிக்","கரப்பவன் நெஞ்சம் போல், மரம் எல்லாம் இலை கூம்பத் -","தோற்றம் சால் செக்கருள் பிறை நுதி எயிறு ஆக,","நால் திசையும் நடுக்குறூஉம் மடங்கல் காலைக்,","கூற்று நக்கது போலும், உட்குவரு கடு மாலை!","மாலை நீ - உள்ளம் கொண்டு அகன்றவர் துணை தாராப் பொழுதின் கண்","வெள்ள மான் நிறம் நோக்கி கணை தொடுக்கும் கொடியான் போல்,","அல்லற்பட்டு இருந்தாரை அயர்ப்பிய வந்தாயோ?","மாலை நீ - ஈரம் இல் காதலர் இகந்து, அருளா இடன் நோக்கிப்","போர் தொலைந்து இருந்தாரைப் பாடு எள்ளி நகுவார் போல்,","ஆர் அஞர் உற்றாரை அணங்கிய வந்தாயோ?","மாலை நீ - கந்து ஆதல் சான்றவர் களைதாராப் பொழுதின் கண்","வெந்தது ஓர் புண்ணின் கண் வேல் கொண்டு நுழைப்பான் போல்,","காய்ந்த நோய் உழப்பாரைக் கலக்கிய வந்தாயோ?","என ஆங்கு;","இடன் இன்று அலைத்தரும் இன்னா செய் மாலை","துனி கொள் துயர் தீர காதலர் துனை தர","மெல்லியான் பருவத்து மேல் நின்ற கடும் பகை","ஒல்லென நீக்கி, ஒருவாது காத்து ஓம்பும்","நல் இறை தோன்றக், கெட்டாங்கு -","இல்லாகின்றால் இருள் அகத்து ஒளித்தே."],"p":"","r":"Evening comes grim as Death grinning with the crescent for a tooth: darkness fills in like the heart of a pitiless man, the world dulls like the heart of one who must beg, the leaves close like the heart of one who hides from askers; the woman asks it whether it came, like a hunter aiming at a deer, like those who mock the beaten, like one who drives a spear into a burn, to torment those already suffering while their lover is away, and says this evening will vanish into darkness as a fierce enemy is driven off when a good king appears, once the lover hurries back.","n":"Every simile is of cruelty to the already defeated: the begging, the burnt, the routed. The ending turns the political figure round: the lover's return is a good king's arrival that drives off the enemy who oppressed a weak reign (மெல்லியான் பருவத்து).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 120","lb":"KL120","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மரம்","மரம்",6,"maram","simile",""],["மான்","வெள்ள மான்",11,"maa","simile","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:121":{"book":"kalittokai","num":121,"t":["ஒள் சுடர் கல் சேர, உலகு ஊரும் தகையது,","தெள் கடல் அழுவத்துத் திரை நீக்கா எழுதரூஉம்,","தண் கதிர் மதியத்து அணி நிலா நிறைத்தரப்,","புள் இனம் இரை மாந்திப் புகல் சேர, ஒலி ஆன்று,","வள் இதழ் கூம்பிய மணி மருள் இரும் கழி","பள்ளி புக்கது போலும் பரப்பு நீர்த் தண் சேர்ப்ப!","தாங்க அரும் காமத்தைத் தணந்து நீ புறம் மாறத்","தூங்கு நீர் இமிழ் திரை துணை ஆகி ஒலிக்குமே -","உறையொடு வைகிய போது போல், ஒய்யென,","நிறை ஆனாது இழிதரூஉம் நீர் நீந்து கண்ணாட்கு;","வாராய் நீ புறம் மாற, வருந்திய மேனியாட்கு,","ஆர் இருள் துணை ஆகி அசை வளி அலைக்குமே -","கமழ் தண் தாது உதிர்ந்து உக, ஊழ் உற்ற கோடல் வீ","இதழ் சோரும் குலை போல, இறை நீவு வளையாட்கு;","இன் துணை நீ நீப்ப, இரவின் உள் துணை ஆகித்,","தன் துணை பிரிந்து அயாஅம் தனி குருகு உசாவுமே -","ஒள் சுடர் ஞாயிற்று விளக்கத்தான் ஒளி சாம்பும்","நண்பகல் மதியம் போல், நலம் சாய்ந்த அணியாட்கு;","என ஆங்கு;","எறி திரை தந்திட, இழிந்த மீன் இன் துறை","மறி திரை வருந்தாமல் கொண்டாங்கு, நெறி தாழ்ந்து,","சாயினள் வருந்தியாள் இடும்பை","பாய் பரிக் கடு திண் தேர் களையினோ இடனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the cool shore, where at sunset the moon rises from the sea, birds go to roost and the dark backwater closes its flowers as if gone to bed, that since he turned away, the roaring waves are the only companion of the woman whose eyes swim with tears, the moving wind of the one whose body wastes, the lone heron pining for its mate of the one whose beauty fades like the moon at noon; as the returning wave takes back unharmed the fish the breaking wave left on the shore, his swift chariot could remove her pain.","n":"The three companions she is left with are the shore's own sounds and creatures, each a fellow sufferer; the heron that pines for its mate (line 16) almost carries an implied sense, but it is named outright as her companion, so it is scene, not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","iravu","melivu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 121","lb":"KL121","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கடல்","கடல்",2,"pira","scene",""],["புள் இனம்","புள்",4,"pul","scene",""],["கழி","கழி",5,"pira","simile",""],["திரை","திரை",8,"pira","scene",""],["போது","போது",9,"maram","simile",""],["வளி","வளி",12,"pira","scene",""],["கோடல்","கோடல்",13,"maram","simile",""],["குருகு","குருகு",16,"pul","scene",""],["மீன்","மீன்",20,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:122":{"book":"kalittokai","num":122,"t":["'கோதை ஆயமும் அன்னையும் அறிவுறப்,","போது எழில் உண் கண் புகழ் நலன் இழப்பக்,","காதல் செய்து அருளாது துறந்தார் மாட்டு, ஏது இன்றிச்,","சிறிய துனித்தனை; துன்னா செய்து அமர்ந்தனை;","பலவு நூறு அடுக்கினை; இனைபு ஏங்கி அழுதனை;","அலவலை உடையை' என்றி - தோழீ !","கேள் இனி;","மாண் எழில் மாதர் மகளிரோடு அமைந்து அவன்","காணும் பண்பு இலன் ஆதல் அறிவேன் மன்; அறியினும்,","பேணி அவன் சிறிது அளித்தக் கால், என்","நாண் இல் நெஞ்சம் நெகிழ்தலும் காண்பல்;","இருள் உறழ் இரும் கூந்தல் மகளிரோடு அமைந்து அவன்","தெருளும் பண்பு இலன் ஆதல் அறிவேன் மன்; அறியினும்,","அருளி அவன் சிறிது அளித்தக் கால், என்","மருளி நெஞ்சம் மகிழ்தலும் காண்பல்;","ஒள் இழை மாதர் மகளிரோடு அமைந்து அவன்","உள்ளும் பண்பு இலன் ஆதல் அறிவேன் மன்; அறியினும்,","புல்லி அவன் சிறிது அளித்தக் கால், என்","அல்லல் நெஞ்சம் அடங்கலும் காண்பல்;","அதனால்;","யாம நடுநாள் துயில் கொண்டு ஒளித்த","காம நோயின் கழீஇய நெஞ்சம் -","தான் அவர்பால் பட்டது ஆயின்,","நாம் உயிர் வாழ்தலோ நகை நனி உடைத்தே!"],"p":"","r":"Answering her friend, who says she sulked over small things without cause, wept and lost her beauty for a man who loves without grace, the woman says she knows he cannot see, understand or think of her while he keeps company with other women, yet when he tends her a little her shameless heart melts, rejoices and is calmed; so if her heart, washed by the love-sickness that hid at midnight, has gone over to him, her staying alive is a fine joke.","n":"The three stanzas move the heart from melting to rejoicing to being calmed, each with a harsher name (நாண் இல், மருளி, அல்லல்); the closing irony is aimed at herself. No shore thing is named, and the situation of other women is marutham's, so my thinai differs from the colophon's neythal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 122","lb":"KL122","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:123":{"book":"kalittokai","num":123,"t":["கரும் கோட்டு நறும் புன்னை மலர் சினை மிசை தொறும்","சுரும்பு ஆர்க்கும் குரலினோடு, இரும் தும்பி இயைபு ஊத,","ஒருங்கு உடன் இம்மென இமிர்தலின், பாடலோடு","அரும் பொருள் மரபின் மால் யாழ் கேளா கிடந்தான் போல்,","பெரும் கடல் துயில் கொள்ளும் வண்டு இமிர் நறு கானல் -","காணாமை இருள் பரப்பிக் கையற்ற கங்குலான்,","மாணா நோய் செய்தான்கண் சென்றாய்; மற்று அவனை நீ","காணவும் பெற்றாயோ? - காணாயோ? மட நெஞ்சே!","கொல் ஏற்றுச் சுறவு இனம் கடி கொண்ட மருள் மாலை,","அல்லல் நோய் செய்தான்கண் சென்றாய்; மற்று அவனை நீ","புல்லவும் பெற்றாயோ? - புல்லாயோ மட நெஞ்சே!","வெறி கொண்ட புள் இனம் வதி சேரும் பொழுதினான்,","செறி வளை நெகிழ்த்தான்கண் சென்றாய்; மற்று அவனை நீ","அறியவும் பெற்றாயோ? - அறியாயோ? மட நெஞ்சே!","என ஆங்கு;","எல்லையும் இரவும் துயில் துறந்து, பல் ஊழ்","அரும் படர் அவல நோய் செய்தான்கண் பெறல் நசைஇ,","இரும் கழி ஓதம் போல் தடுமாறி,","வருந்தினை அளிய என் மடம் கெழு நெஞ்சே!"],"p":"","r":"On the fragrant shore where bees hum in the punnai and the great sea sleeps as if listening to song and lute, the woman asks her simple heart, which went in the helpless night, at the bewildering evening when sharks keep watch, and when the birds roost, to the man who caused her pain, whether it got to see him, embrace him, know him, and pities it for tossing day and night like the backwater's tide in longing for him.","n":"The three questions (did you see him, embrace him, know him) climb in intimacy while the times of night move from darkness to evening to roosting, and each goes unanswered; the tide simile gives the heart's to-and-fro the shore's own rhythm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","iravu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 123","lb":"KL123","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene",""],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",2,"pira","scene",""],["தும்பி","தும்பி",2,"pira","scene",""],["யாழ்","யாழ்",4,"yaazh","simile",""],["கடல்","கடல்",5,"pira","simile",""],["சுறவு","சுறவு",9,"pira","scene",""],["புள் இனம்","புள்",12,"pul","scene",""],["ஓதம்","ஓதம்",18,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:124":{"book":"kalittokai","num":124,"t":["ஞாலம் மூன்று அடித் தாய முதல்வற்கு முது முறைப்","பால் அன்ன மேனியான் அணிபெறத் தைஇய","நீல நீர் உடை போலத், தகைபெற்ற வெண் திரை","வால் எக்கர் வாய் சூழும் வயங்கு நீர் தண் சேர்ப்ப!","ஊர் அலர் எடுத்து அரற்ற, உள்ளாய், நீ துறத்தலின்,","கூரும் தன் எவ்வ நோய் என்னையும் மறைத்தாள்மன் -","காரிகை பெற்ற தன் கவின் வாட கலுழ்பு, ஆங்கே","பீர் அலர் அணி கொண்ட பிறை நுதல் அல்லாக்கால்;","இணைபு இவ் ஊர் அலர் தூற்ற, எய்யாய், நீ துறத்தலின்,","புணை இல்லா எவ்வ நோய் என்னையும் மறைத்தாள் மன் -","துணையாருள் தகைபெற்ற தொல் நலம் இழந்து, இனி,","அணி வனப்பு இழந்த தன் அணை மென் தோள் அல்லாக்கால்;","இன்று இவ் ஊர் அலர் தூற்ற, எய்யாய், நீ துறத்தலின்,","நின்ற தன் எவ்வ நோய் என்னையும் மறைத்தாள் மன் -","வென்ற வேல் நுதி ஏய்க்கும் விறல் நலன் இழந்து, இனி,","நின்று நீர் உகக் கலுழும் நெடும் பெரும் கண் அல்லாக்கால்;","அதனால்;","பிரிவு இல்லாய் போல, நீ தெய்வத்தின் தெளித்தக் கால்,","அரிது என்னாள், துணிந்தவள் ஆய் நலம் பெயர்தரப்,","புரி உளைக் கலி மான் தேர் கடவுபு -","விரி தண் தார் வியல் மார்ப! - விரைக நின் செலவே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore, where the white waves ring the dune like the blue garment of Vishnu's milk-white elder brother, that since he left her while the town spread gossip, the woman hid her pain even from her friend, but could not hide her forehead yellowed with pallor, her wasted shoulders or her great eyes streaming; because she trusted the oath he swore by the god as one who would never part, she lost her beauty, so he should drive his horse-chariot and hasten his coming.","n":"The refrain என்னையும் மறைத்தாள் மன் (she hid it even from me) makes the friend's evidence the stronger: what the woman would not say, her forehead, shoulders and eyes said for her. The oath by the god (தெய்வத்தின் தெளித்த) turns his leaving into a breach of faith.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","pasalai","melivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 124","lb":"KL124","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["திரை","திரை",3,"pira","simile",""],["பீர்","பீர்",8,"maram","scene",""],["கலி மான்","மான்",20,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:125":{"book":"kalittokai","num":125,"t":["'கண்டவர் இல்', என உலகத்துள் உணராதார்,","தங்காது தகைவு இன்றித் தாம் செய்யும் வினைகளுள்,","நெஞ்சு அறிந்த கொடியவை மறைப்பினும், 'அறிபவர்","நெஞ்சத்துக் குறுகிய கரி இல்லை ஆகலின்',","வண் பரி நவின்ற வய மான் செல்வ!","நன்கு அதை அறியினும், நயன் இல்லா நாட்டத்தால்,","'அன்பு இலை' என வந்து கழறுவல்; ஐய! கேள்:","மகிழ் செய் தே மொழித் தொய்யில் சூழ் இள முலை","முகிழ் செய முள்கிய தொடர்பு, அவள் உண் கண்","அவிழ் பனி உறைப்பவும், நல்காது விடுவாய்!","இமிழ் திரை கொண்க! கொடியை காண் நீ;","இலங்கு ஏர் எல் வளை ஏர் தழை தைஇ,","நலம் செல நல்கிய தொடர்பு, அவள் சாஅய்ப்","புலந்து அழப், புல்லாது விடுவாய்!","இலங்கு நீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப! கொடியை காண் நீ;","இன் மணிச் சிலம்பின் சில் மொழி ஐம்பால்","பின்னொடு கெழீஇய தட அரவு அல்குல்","நுண் வரி, வாட, வாராது விடுவாய்!","தண்ணம் துறைவ! தகாஅய் காண் நீ ;","என ஆங்கு;","அனையள் என்று, அளிமதி, பெரும! நின் இன்று","இறை வரை நில்லா வளையள் இவட்கு, இனிப்","பிறை ஏர் சுடர் நுதல் பசலை","மறையச் செல்லும், நீ மணந்தனை விடினே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of strong horses that people who think 'no one saw' may hide the crooked deeds their own heart knows, but there is no witness closer than the heart, and though he knows this she comes to reproach him for having no love: he left untended the bond made when he drew patterns on her young breasts, the bond made when he dressed her in a leaf skirt, while she wept and wasted; let him pity her, for her bangles slip and the pallor will leave her crescent forehead if he marries her and does not leave.","n":"She opens with a general maxim on the heart as witness before turning to the charge, so the reproach is built as a moral argument he cannot answer. The three tokens of the bond (the drawn தொய்யில், the leaf skirt, the waist-band of fine lines) are kalavu's gifts, now used as evidence. The last line asks for marriage (மணந்தனை விடினே).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","pirivu","kalaviyal","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 125","lb":"KL125","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வய மான்","மான்",5,"maa","scene","horse"],["திரை","திரை",11,"pira","scene",""],["தழை","தழை",12,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:126":{"book":"kalittokai","num":126,"t":["பொன் மலை சுடர் சேரப், புலம்பிய இடன் நோக்கித்,","தன் மலைந்து உலகு ஏத்தத் தகை மதி ஏர்தரச்,","செக்கர் கொள் பொழுதினான் ஒலி நீவி, இன நாரை","முக்கோல் கொள் அந்தணர் முது மொழி நினைவார் போல்,","எக்கர் மேல் இறைகொள்ளும், இலங்கு நீர்த் தண் சேர்ப்ப!","அணிச் சிறை இனக் குருகு ஒலிக்கும்கால், நின் திண் தேர்","மணிக் குரல் என இவள் மதிக்கும்மன்: மதித்தாங்கே,","உள் ஆன்ற ஒலியவாய் இருப்பக் கண்டு, அவை கானல்","புள் என உணர்ந்து பின் புலம்பு கொண்டு இனையுமே;","நீர் நீவிக் கஞன்ற பூக் கமழும்கால், நின் மார்பின்","தார் நாற்றம் என இவள் மதிக்கும்மன்; மதித்தாங்கே,","அலர் பதத்து அசை வளி வந்து ஒல்கக், கழிப் பூத்த","மலர் என உணர்ந்து, பின் மம்மர் கொண்டு இனையுமே;","நீள் நகர் நிறை ஆற்றாள், நினையுநள் வதிந்தக் கால்,","தோள் மேலாய் என நின்னை மதிக்கும்மன்: மதித்தாங்கே,","நனவு என புல்லும்கால், காணாளாய்க், கண்டது","கனவு என உணர்ந்து, பின் கையற்றுக் கலங்குமே;","என ஆங்கு;","பல நினைந்து இனையும் பைதல் நெஞ்சின்,","அலமரல் நோயுள் உழக்கும் என் தோழி","மதி மருள் வாள் முகம் விளங்கப்,","புது நலம் ஏர்தரப், பூண்க, நின் தேரே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore, where at sunset the herons settle quietly on the dune like tridandi brahmins recalling the old sayings, that the woman takes the cry of the herons for the bell of his chariot, the scent of the backwater flowers for his garland, and in the long house, thinking of him, believes him on her shoulder and embraces him, then finds it was the birds, the flowers, a dream, and grieves; so let him yoke his chariot, that her face may shine again.","n":"The three mistakes rise from sound to scent to touch, and the last, the embrace of a dream taken for waking (நனவு என), is the cruellest. Her body is held by longing as the herons are held by the old sayings they mutter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kanavu","pirivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 126","lb":"KL126","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நாரை","நாரை",3,"pul","simile",""],["அந்தணர்","அந்தணர்",4,"seythi","simile",""],["குருகு","குருகு",6,"pul","scene",""],["பூ","பூ",10,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:127":{"book":"kalittokai","num":127,"t":["தெரி இணர் ஞாழலும், தேம் கமழ் புன்னையும்,","புரி அவிழ் பூவின கைதையும், செருந்தியும்,","வரி ஞிமிறு இமிர்ந்து ஆர்ப்ப, இரும் தும்பி இயைபு ஊதச் -","செரு மிகு நேமியான் தார் போலப் பெரும் கடல்","வரி மணல் வாய் சூழும் வயங்கு நீர்த் தண் சேர்ப்ப;","கொடும் கழி வளைஇய குன்று போல், வால் எக்கர்,","நடுங்கு நோய் தீர, நின் குறி வாய்த்தாள் என்பதோ -","கடும் பனி அறல் இகு கயல் ஏர் கண் பனி மல்க,","இடும்பையோடு இனைபு ஏங்க, இவளை நீ துறந்ததை!","குறி இன்றிப் பல்நாள், நின் கடும் திண் தேர் வருபதம் கண்டு,","எறி திரை இமிழ் கானல், எதிர்கொண்டாள் என்பதோ -","அறிவு அஞர் உழந்து ஏங்கி, ஆய் நலம் வறிது ஆகச்","செறி வளை தோள் ஊர, இவளை நீ துறந்ததை!","காண்வர இயன்ற இக் கவின் பெறு பனித் துறை,","யாமத்து வந்து, நின் குறி வாய்த்தாள் என்பதோ -","வேய் நலம் இழந்த தோள் விளங்கு இழை பொறை ஆற்றாள்,","வாள் நுதல் பசப்பு ஊர இவளை நீ துறந்ததை!","அதனால்;","இறை வளை நெகிழ்ந்த எவ்வ நோய் இவள் தீர,","'உரவுக் கதிர் தெறும்' என, ஓங்கு திரை விரைபு, தன்","கரை அமல் அடும்பு அளித்தாஅங்கு -","உரவு நீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப! - அருளினை அளிமே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the shore, where bees hum in the njaazhal, punnai, kaithai and cherunthi and the sea rings the sand like the garland of the discus-bearer, whether it was for keeping his trysts on the white dune, for coming out to meet his chariot many days without a sign, and for meeting him at midnight on this lovely ford that he left her weeping, her bangles slipping and her forehead pale; let him give grace to end her pain, as the high wave hurries to shelter the adumbu creeper on its bank, lest the strong sun scorch it.","n":"Each stanza sets her loyalty in the tryst (குறி) against his leaving, so the question 'was it for this?' turns her faithfulness into the charge against him. The closing image is stated with a marker and its tenor named (அருளினை), so it is an open comparison, not a hidden one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","pirivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 127","lb":"KL127","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஞாழலும்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene",""],["கைதை","கைதை",2,"maram","scene",""],["செருந்தி","செருந்தி",2,"maram","scene",""],["ஞிமிறு","ஞிமிறு",3,"pira","scene",""],["தும்பி","தும்பி",3,"pira","scene",""],["கயல்","கயல்",8,"pira","simile",""],["வேய்","வேய்",16,"maram","scene",""],["திரை","திரை",20,"pira","simile",""],["அடும்பு","அடும்பு",21,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:128":{"book":"kalittokai","num":128,"t":["'தோள் துறந்து, அருளாதவர் போல் நின்று,","வாடை தூக்க, வணங்கிய தாழை","ஆடு கோட்டு இருந்த அசை நடை நாரை,","நளி இரும் கங்குல், நம் துயர் அறியாது,","அளி இன்று, பிணி இன்று, விளியாது, நரலும்","கானல் அம் சேர்ப்பனைக் கண்டாய் போலப்","புதுவது கவினினை' என்றி ஆயின்,","நனவின் வாரா நயன் இலாளனைக்","கனவில் கண்டு, யான் செய்தது கேள், இனி:","'அலந்தாங்கு அமையலென்' என்றானைப் பற்றி, 'என்","நலம் தாராயோ?' எனத், தொடுப்பேன் போலவும்,","கலந்து ஆங்கே என் கவின் பெற முயங்கிப்","'புலம்பல் ஓம்பு' என, அளிப்பான் போலவும் -","'முலை இடைத் துயிலும் மறந்தீத்தோய்' என,","நிலை அழி நெஞ்சத்தேன் அழுவேன் போலவும்,","'வலை உறு மயிலின் வருந்தினை, பெரிது' எனத்","தலையுற முன் அடிப் பணிவான் போலவும் -","கோதை கோலா இறைஞ்சி நின்ற","ஊதை அம் சேர்ப்பனை, அலைப்பேன் போலவும்,","'யாது என் பிழைப்பு?' என நடுங்கி, ஆங்கே,","'பேதையை பெரிது' எனத் தெளிப்பான் போலவும்","ஆங்கு;","கனவினால் கண்டேன் - தோழி! - 'காண்தகக்","கனவின் வந்த கானலம் சேர்ப்பன்","நனவின் வருதலும் உண்டு' என,","அனை வரை நின்றது, என் அரும் பெறல் உயிரே."],"p":"","r":"When her friend says she shines anew as if she had seen the lord of the shore, where the heron on the screwpine bent by the north wind calls all night heedless of their pain, the woman tells her that she saw the graceless man in a dream: she held him and demanded her beauty back, he embraced her and told her not to grieve, she wept that he forgot sleeping on her breast, he bowed to her feet saying she suffered like a peacock in a net, she struck him and he trembled asking his fault; and her precious life stopped at the brink, hoping that the man who came in a dream may come awake too.","n":"The dream stages a full lovers' quarrel, demand, comfort, accusation, submission, blow and reconciliation, with every act prefixed by போலவும் (as if), so the poem keeps telling us none of it happened. The heron calling heedless in the night (line 5) is set against the man who comes only in dream.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","pirivu","uyir","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 128","lb":"KL128","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தாழை","தாழை",2,"maram","scene",""],["நாரை","நாரை",3,"pul","scene",""],["மயிலின்","மயில்",16,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:129":{"book":"kalittokai","num":129,"t":["தொல் ஊழி தடுமாறித் தொகல் வேண்டும் பருவத்தால்,","பல்வயின் உயிர் எல்லாம் படைத்தான்கண் பெயர்ப்பான் போல்,","எல் உறு தெறு கதிர் மடங்கித் தன் கதிர் மாய,","நல் அற நெறி நிறீஇ உலகு ஆண்ட அரசன் பின்,","அல்லது மலைந்திருந்து அற நெறி நிறுக்கல்லா","மெல்லியான் பருவம் போல், மயங்கு இருள் தலை வர:","எல்லைக்கு வரம்பு ஆய, இடும்பை கூர், மருள் மாலை -","பாய் திரைப் பாடு ஓவாப் பரப்பு நீர் பனிக் கடல்! -","'தூ அற துறந்தனன் துறைவன்' என்று, அவன் திறம்","நோய் தெற உழப்பார்கண் இமிழ்தியோ? எம் போலக்","காதல் செய்து அகன்றாரை உடையையோ? - நீ.","மன்று இரும் பெண்ணை மடல் சேர் அன்றில்! -","'நன்று அறை கொன்றனர், அவர்' எனக் கலங்கிய","என் துயர் அறிந்தனை நரறியோ? எம் போல","இன் துணைப் பிரிந்தாரை உடையையோ? - நீ.","பனி இருள் சூழ்தரப் பைதல் அம் சிறு குழல்! -","'இனி வரின், உயரும் மன் பழி' எனக் கலங்கிய","தனியவர் இடும்பை கண்டு இனைதியோ? எம் போல","இனிய செய்து அகன்றாரை உடையையோ? - நீ","என ஆங்கு;","அழிந்து, அயல் அறிந்த எவ்வம் மேற்படப்","பெரும் பேதுறுதல் களைமதி, பெரும!","வருந்திய செல்லல் தீர்த்த திறன் அறி ஒருவன்","மருந்து அறைகோடலின் கொடிதே, யாழ நின்","அருந்தியோர் நெஞ்சம் அழிந்து உக விடினே."],"p":"","r":"As the sun withdraws its rays like the creator gathering back all lives at the end of an age and the dark comes on like the weak reign that follows a king who ruled by the right way, the woman asks the roaring sea, the anril on the palmyra of the common and the little flute in the chill dark whether they too lament because someone loved them and left, and then asks the man to end the confusion of the one whose pain is now known to others, for leaving those who drank of him to break is crueller than a healer who cured a sickness smashing the medicine.","n":"The three questions read the night's sounds as laments like her own; the anril, the bird that cannot live apart from its mate, is the apt second witness. The closing image of the healer who breaks his own cure is a charge, not a plea: he began the healing and now withholds it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","alar","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 129","lb":"KL129","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கடல்","கடல்",8,"pira","scene",""],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",12,"maram","scene",""],["அன்றில்","அன்றில்",12,"pul","scene",""],["குழல்","குழல்",16,"yaazh","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:130":{"book":"kalittokai","num":130,"t":["'நயனும் வாய்மையும் நன்னர் நடுவும்","இவனின் தோன்றிய இவை' என இரங்கப்,","புரை தவ நாடிப் பொய் தபுத்து, இனிது ஆண்ட","அரைசனோடு உடன் மாய்ந்த நல் ஊழிச் செல்வம் போல்,","நிரை கதிர்க் கனலி பாடொடு பகல் செலக்","கல்லாது முதிர்ந்தவன் கண் இல்லா நெஞ்சம் போல்,","புல் இருள் பரத்தரூஉம் - புலம்பு கொள் மருள் மாலை.","இம் மாலை;","ஐயர் அவிர் அழல் எடுப்ப, அரோ, என்","கையறு நெஞ்சம் கனன்று தீ மடுக்கும்!","இம் மாலை;","இரும் கழி மா மலர் கூம்ப, அரோ, என்","அரும் படர் நெஞ்சம் அழிவொடு கூம்பும்!","இம் மாலை;","கோவலர் தீம் குழல் இனைய, அரோ என்","பூ எழில் உண் கண் புலம்பு கொண்டு இனையும்!","என ஆங்கு;","படுசுடர் மாலையொடு பைதல் நோய் உழப்பாளைக்,","குடி புறங்காத்து ஓம்பும் செம் கோலான் வியன் தானை","விடுவழி விடுவழிச் சென்றாங்கு, அவர்","தொடுவழித் தொடுவழி நீங்கின்றால் பசப்பே."],"p":"","r":"Evening comes desolate as the sun departs like the good age's wealth that died with a king who ruled without falsehood, and darkness spreads like the eyeless heart of a man grown old unlearned: as the priests lift the fire her helpless heart burns, as the backwater flowers close her heart closes in ruin, as the herders' flute plays her eyes grieve; yet the pallor of the woman suffering at sunset retreats wherever her lover touches her, as the vast army of a just king who guards his people goes wherever it is sent.","n":"Each of the three evening acts (the lit fire, the closing flowers, the flute) is matched by one of her own, so the poem is built on juxtaposition. Both political similes come from the same theory of kingship: the good king's passing leaves darkness, and his army's obedience is the image of the lover's healing touch.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","pasalai","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 130","lb":"KL130","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஐயர்","ஐயர்",9,"seythi","scene",""],["மலர்","மலர்",12,"maram","scene",""],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",15,"seythi","scene",""],["குழல்","குழல்",15,"yaazh","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:131":{"book":"kalittokai","num":131,"t":["பெரும் கடல் தெய்வம் நீர் நோக்கித் தெளித்து, என்","திருந்து இழை மென் தோள் மணந்தவன் செய்த","அரும் துயர் நீக்குவேன் போல்மன் - பொருந்துபு","பூக் கவின் கொண்ட புகழ் சால் எழில் உண் கண்,","நோக்கும்கால் நோக்கின் அணங்கு ஆக்கும், சாயலாய்! தாக்கி","இன மீன் இகல் மாற வென்ற சின மீன்","எறி சுறா வான் மருப்பு கோத்து, நெறி செய்த","நெய்தல் நெடு நார்ப் பிணித்து யாத்துக் கை உளர்வின்","யாழ் இசை கொண்ட இன வண்டு இமிர்ந்து ஆர்ப்பத்","தாழாது உறைக்கும் தட மலர்த் தண் தாழை","வீழ் ஊசல் தூங்க பெறின்.","மாழை, மட மான் பிணை இயல் வென்றாய்! நின் ஊசல்","கடைஇ யான் இகுப்ப, நீடு ஊங்காய் தட மென் தோள்","நீத்தான் திறங்கள் பகர்ந்து.","நாணின கொல் தோழி? நாணின கொல் தோழி?","இரவு எலாம் நல்தோழி நாணின - என்பவை","வாள் நிலா ஏய்க்கும் வயங்கு ஒளி எக்கர் மேல்,","ஆனாப் பரிய அலவன் அளை புகூஉம்...","கானல் கமழ் ஞாழல் வீ ஏய்ப்பத் தோழி! என்","மேனி சிதைத்தான் துறை.","மாரி வீழ் இரும் கூந்தல், மதைஇய நோக்கு எழில் உண்கண்","தாழ் நீர முத்தின் தகை ஏய்க்கும் முறுவலாய்!","தேயா நோய் செய்தான் திறம் கிளந்து நாம் பாடும்","சேய் உயர் ஊசல்சீர் நீ ஒன்று பாடித்தை.","பார்த்து உற்றன, தோழி! பார்த்து உற்றன, தோழி!","இரவு எலாம், நல்தோழி! பார்த்து உற்றன - என்பவை,","'தன் துணை இல்லாள் வருந்தினாள் கொல்?' என,","இன் துணை அன்றில் இரவின் அகவாவே -","அன்று, தான் ஈர்த்த கரும்பு அணி வாட, என்","மென்தோள் ஞெகிழ்த்தான் துறை.","கரை கவர் கொடும் கழிக் கண்கவர் புள் இனம்","திரை உறப் பொன்றிய புலவு மீன் அல்லதை,","இரை உயிர் செகுத்து உண்ணாத் துறைவனை யாம் பாடும்","அசை வரல் ஊசல்சீர் அழித்து ஒன்று பாடித்தை.","அருளின கொல் தோழி? அருளின கொல் தோழி?","இரவு எலாம், தோழி! அருளின - என்பவை,","கணம் கொள் இடு மணல் காவி வருந்தப்","பிணங்கு இரு மோட்ட திரை வந்து அளிக்கும் -","மணம் கமழ் ஐம்பாலார் ஊடலை ஆங்கே","வணங்கி உணர்ப்பான் துறை.","என, நாம்","பாட, மறை நின்று கேட்டனன், நீடிய","வால் நீர்க் கிடக்கை வயங்கு நீர்ச் சேர்ப்பனை","யான் என உணர்ந்து, நீ நனி மருளத்","தேன் இமிர் புன்னை பொருந்தித்,","தான் ஊக்கினன், அவ் ஊசலை வந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend pushes the woman on a swing hung from a screwpine, its frame made of shark's tusk tied with neythal fibre, and they take turns singing of the man who swore by the sea god and left: the shore where the crab hides in its hole at night, where the anril does not call at night lest the woman without her mate suffer, where the birds eat only fish killed by the waves and do not kill their prey, where the waves soothe the kaavi flower as he bows to appease sulking women; then the friend tells her that the man of the shore, who had been listening hidden, came and pushed the swing while she, thinking it was her friend, was bewildered.","n":"The swing songs praise his shore in order to blame him: its creatures are gentler than he is. The birds that eat only fish the waves have killed (lines 31 to 33) imply the same contrast as the anril but are given as scene. The end is a comic recognition: the man comes to the swing in the friend's place, so the lament ends in a meeting. Line 18 has a gap in the source.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalaviyal","pirivu","punarchi","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 131","lb":"KL131","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தெய்வம்","கடல் தெய்வம்",1,"deyvam","scene",""],["மீன்","மீன்",6,"pira","scene",""],["சுறா","சுறா",7,"pira","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",8,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",9,"pira","scene",""],["தாழை","தாழை",10,"maram","scene",""],["மான்","மான்",12,"maa","simile","deer"],["அலவன்","அலவன்",18,"pira","scene",""],["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",19,"maram","simile",""],["அன்றில்","அன்றில்",28,"pul","iraicchi",""],["கரும்பு","கரும்பு",29,"maram","ornament",""],["புள் இனம்","புள்",31,"pul","scene",""],["காவி","காவி",37,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",45,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","அன்றில்","confirmed","1/2","even the birds of his shore spare her at night, while the man who caused her pain stays away"]],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:132":{"book":"kalittokai","num":132,"t":["உரவு நீர்த் திரை பொர ஓங்கிய எக்கர் மேல்,","விரவுப் பல் உருவின வீழ் பெடை துணை ஆக,","இரை தேர்ந்து உண்டு, அசாவிடூஉம் புள் இனம் இறைகொள -","முரைசு மூன்று ஆள்பவர் முரணியோர் முரண் தப,","நிரை களிறு இடைபட, நெறி யாத்த இருக்கை போல்","சிதைவு இன்றிச் சென்றுழிச் சிறப்பு எய்தி, வினை வாய்த்துத்","துறைய கலம் வாய் சூழும் துணி கடல் தண் சேர்ப்ப!","புன்னைய நறும் பொழில் புணர்ந்தனை இருந்தக்கால்","'நல் நுதால் அஞ்சல் ஓம்பு' என்றதன் பயன் அன்றோ -","பாயின பசலையால், பகல் கொண்ட சுடர் போன்றாள்,","மாவின தளிர் போலும் மாண் நலம் இழந்ததை?","பல்மலர் நறும் பொழில் பழி இன்றிப் புணர்ந்தக்கால்","'சின் மொழி! தெளி' எனத் தேற்றிய சிறப்பு அன்றோ -","வாடுபு வனப்பு ஓடி வயக்கு உறா மணி போன்றாள்","நீடு இறை நெடு மென்தோள் நிரை வளை நெகிழ்ந்தந்தை?","அடும்பு இவர் அணி எக்கர் ஆடி நீ, மணந்தக்கால்","'கொடும் குழாய்! தெளி' எனக் கொண்டதன் கொளை அன்றோ -","பொறை ஆற்றா நுசுப்பினால், பூ வீந்த கொடி போன்றாள்","மறை பிறர் அறியாமை மாணா நோய் உழந்ததை?","என ஆங்கு -","வழிபட்ட தெய்வம்தான் வலி எனச் சார்ந்தார்கண்","கழியும் நோய் கைம்மிக அணங்கு ஆகியது போலப்,","பழி பரந்து அலர் தூற்ற, என் தோழி","அழி படர் அலைப்ப, அகறலோ கொடிதே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore, where birds rest with their mates on the dune and ships ring the harbour like the ordered camp of the three crowned kings, that her pallor, like a lamp in daylight, and her lost beauty like a mango's young leaves are the fruit of his 'do not fear' in the punnai grove; her slipping bangles, like a gem left unpolished, the gain of his 'trust me'; her hidden suffering, like a creeper shed of flowers, the yield of his promise on the dune; his going away while gossip spreads is as cruel as a god one worshipped as one's strength turning into an affliction.","n":"Each stanza names one of his reassurances (அஞ்சல் ஓம்பு, தெளி) and presents her loss as its 'fruit', 'gain' and 'yield', so the promise is turned into an account he must settle. The opening birds resting with their mates set off his leaving, but no second correspondence is stated, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalaviyal","pirivu","pasalai","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 132","lb":"KL132","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["திரை","திரை",1,"pira","scene",""],["பெடை","பெடை",2,"pul","scene",""],["புள் இனம்","புள்",3,"pul","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",5,"maa","simile",""],["கலம்","கலம்",7,"pira","simile",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",8,"maram","scene",""],["மாவின","மா",11,"maram","simile","mango"],["மணி","மணி",14,"pira","simile",""],["அடும்பு","அடும்பு",16,"maram","scene",""],["கொடி","கொடி",18,"maram","simile","creeper"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:133":{"book":"kalittokai","num":133,"t":["மா மலர் முண்டகம் தில்லையோடு ஒருங்கு உடன்","கானல் அணிந்த உயர் மணல் எக்கர் மேல்,","சீர் மிகு சிறப்பினோன் மர முதல் கை சேர்த்த","நீர் மலி கரகம் போல் பழம் தூங்கு முடத் தாழைப்","பூ மலர்ந்தவை போலப், புள் அல்கும் துறைவ! கேள்:","'ஆற்றுதல்' என்பது, ஒன்று அலந்தவர்க்கு உதவுதல்;","'போற்றுதல்' என்பது, புணர்ந்தாரை பிரியாமை;","'பண்பு' எனப்படுவது, பாடு அறிந்து ஒழுகுதல்;","'அன்பு' எனப்படுவது, தன் கிளை செறாஅமை;","'அறிவு' எனப்படுவது, பேதையார் சொல் நோன்றல்;","'செறிவு' எனப்படுவது, கூறியது மறாஅமை;","'நிறை' எனப்படுவது, மறை பிறர் அறியாமை;","'முறை' எனப்படுவது, கண்ணோடாது உயிர் வௌவல்;","'பொறை' எனப்படுவது, போற்றாரை பொறுத்தல்.","ஆங்கு அதை அறிந்தனிர் ஆயின், என் தோழி","நல் நுதல் நலன் உண்டு துறத்தல் - கொண்க! -","தீம் பால் உண்பவர் கொள் கலம் வரைதல்;","நின்தலை வருந்தியாள் துயரம்","சென்றனை களைமோ, பூண்க, நின் தேரே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore, where the screwpine's hanging fruit is like the ascetic's water-pot hung on a tree and birds rest on it like blossoms, what the virtues are: support is helping the distressed, care is not leaving the one you joined, nature is acting with regard for others, love is not being angry with your own, wisdom is bearing the words of fools, firmness is not going back on your word, restraint is keeping secrets from others, justice is taking life without favour, patience is bearing with those who do not cherish you; knowing these, to enjoy her beauty and leave is to deny the cup to one who drinks sweet milk, so he should go and remove her grief.","n":"The nine definitions sound general but each is aimed at him: போற்றுதல் is not leaving the one you joined, நிறை is the secrecy of kalavu that his leaving endangers, and முறை (taking life without favour) hints that he is as pitiless as a judge. The ascetic's pot in the opening simile prepares the moral register of the list.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","anbu","nirai","porai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 133","lb":"KL133","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முண்டகம்","முண்டகம்",1,"maram","scene",""],["தில்லை","தில்லை",1,"maram","scene",""],["தாழை","தாழை",4,"maram","simile",""],["புள்","புள்",5,"pul","simile",""],["பால்","பால்",17,"unavu","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:134":{"book":"kalittokai","num":134,"t":["மல்லரை மறம் சாய்த்த மலர் தண் தார் அகலத்தோன்","ஒல்லாதார் உடன்று ஓட, உருத்து, உடன் எறிதலின்,","கொல் யானை அணி நுதல் அழுத்திய ஆழி போல்,","கல் சேர்பு ஞாயிறு கதிர் வாங்கி மறைதலின்,","இரும் கடல் ஒலித்து ஆங்கே இரவுக் காண்பது போலப்,","பெரும் கடல் ஓத நீர் வீங்குபு கரை சேரப்,","போஒய வண்டினால் புல்லென்ற துறையவாய்ப்","பாயல் கொள்பவை போலக் கய மலர் வாய் கூம்ப,","ஒரு நிலையே நடுக்குற்று இவ் உலகு எலாம் அச்சுற,","இரு நிலம் பெயர்ப்பு அன்ன, எவ்வம் கூர் மருள் மாலை;","தவல் இல் நோய் செய்தவர்க் காணாமை நினைத்தலின்,","இகல் இடும் பனி தின, எவ்வத்துள் ஆழ்ந்து, ஆங்கே,","கவலை கொள் நெஞ்சினேன் கலுழ் தரக், கடல் நோக்கி,","அவலம் மெய்க் கொண்டது போலும் - அஃது எவன் கொலோ?","நடுங்கு நோய் செய்தவர் நல்காமை நினைத்தலின்,","கடும் பனி கைம்மிகக் கையாற்றுள் ஆழ்ந்து, ஆங்கே,","நடுங்கு நோய் உழந்த என் நலன் அழிய, மணல் நோக்கி,","இடும்பை நோய்க்கு இகுவன போலும் - அஃது எவன் கொலோ?","வையினர் நலன் உண்டார் வாராமை நினைத்தலின்,","கையறு நெஞ்சினேன் கலக்கத்துள் ஆழ்ந்து, ஆங்கே,","மையல் கொள் நெஞ்சொடு மயக்கத்தால், மரன் நோக்கி,","எவ்வத்தால் இயன்ற போல் இலை கூம்பல் எவன் கொலோ?","என ஆங்கு;","கரை காணாப் பௌவத்துக் கலம் சிதைந்து ஆழ்பவன்","திரை தரப் புணை பெற்றுத், தீது இன்றி உய்ந்தாங்கு","விரைவனர் காதலர் புகுதர,","நிரை தொடி துயரம் நீங்கின்றால் விரைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"At the bewildering evening, when the sun sinks like the discus Krishna drove into a war elephant's forehead, the sea swells as if to see the night and the pond flowers close as if for sleep, the woman, thinking of the man who has not come, asks why the sea seems to take on grief, the sand to sink under her pain and the trees to close their leaves as if made of sorrow; then her grief leaves her quickly as the lovers hasten home, as a man whose ship broke in a shoreless sea is saved by a raft the waves bring.","n":"The three questions (அஃது எவன் கொலோ) catch her reading her own grief into sea, sand and trees, and half doubting it. The final simile turns the sea from the image of her grief into the agent of rescue: the same waves bring the raft.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 134","lb":"KL134","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","simile",""],["கடல்","கடல்",5,"pira","simile",""],["வண்டினால்","வண்டு",7,"pira","scene",""],["மலர்","மலர்",8,"maram","simile",""],["மரன்","மரம்",21,"maram","simile",""],["கலம்","கலம்",24,"pira","simile",""],["புணை","புணை",25,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:8"},"kl:135":{"book":"kalittokai","num":135,"t":["துணை புணர்ந்து எழுதரும் தூ நிற வலம்புரி","இணை திரள் மருப்பு ஆக எறி வளி பாகனா -","அயில் திணி நெடும் கதவு அமைத்து, அடைத்து அணி கொண்ட","எயில் இடு களிறே போல் இடு மணல் நெடு கோட்டைப்","பயில் திரை, நடு நன்னாள், பாய்ந்து உறூஉம் துறைவ! கேள்:","கடி மலர்ப் புன்னைக் கீழ் காரிகை தோற்றாளைத்","தொடி நெகிழ்த்த தோளளாத் துறப்பாயால், மற்று நின்","குடிமைக் கண் பெரியது ஓர் குற்றமாய்க் கிடவாதோ?","ஆய் மலர்ப் புன்னைக் கீழ் அணி நலம் தோற்றாளை","நோய் மலி நிலையளாத் துறப்பாயால், மற்று நின்","வாய்மைக் கண் பெரியது ஓர் வஞ்சமாய்க் கிடவாதோ?","திகழ் மலர்ப் புன்னைக் கீழ் திரு நலம் தோற்றாளை","இகழ் மலர்க் கண்ணளாத் துறப்பாயால், மற்று நின்","புகழ்மைக் கண் பெரியது ஓர் புகர் ஆகி கிடவாதோ?","என ஆங்கு;","சொல்லக் கேட்டனை ஆயின், வல்லே,","அணி கிளர் நெடு வரை அலைக்கும் நின் அகலத்து,","மணி கிளர் ஆரம் தாரொடு துயல்வர","உயங்கினள் உயிர்க்கும் என் தோழிக்கு","இயங்கு ஒலி நெடும் திண் தேர் கடவுமதி விரைந்தே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore, where the wave, with paired conches for tusks and the wind for its mahout, charges the high dune at midnight like a war elephant at a fort gate, that if he abandons the girl who lost her beauty to him under the punnai, her bangles slipping, her body sick and her flower eyes scorned, it will lie as a great fault on his lineage, a deceit on his truthfulness and a blemish on his fame; if he has heard her, let him drive his tall chariot quickly to her, the gem chain swinging on his chest.","n":"The triple refrain sets three losses of hers (beauty, health, the bloom of her eyes) against three goods of his (குடிமை, வாய்மை, புகழ்மை), so her body becomes the ledger of his honour. The wave that rams the dune at midnight like an elephant at a fort may hint at the man's night visits, but only one correspondence maps, so it is left as a simile, not ullurai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","vaaimai","pugazh","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 135","lb":"KL135","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வலம்புரி","வலம்புரி",1,"pira","iraicchi","conch"],["களிறே","களிறு",4,"maa","simile","war elephant"],["புன்னைக்","புன்னை",6,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னைக்","புன்னை",9,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னைக்","புன்னை",12,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வலம்புரி","not_confirmed","0/1","even the conch of his own sea keeps to its partner, so a lord of this shore who leaves the woman he joined falls below the creatures of his shore"]],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:136":{"book":"kalittokai","num":136,"t":["இவர் திமில், எறி திரை ஈண்டி வந்து அலைத்தக்கால்,","உவறு நீர் உயர் எக்கர், அலவன் ஆடு அளை வரித்,","தவல் இல் தண் கழகத்துத் தவிராது வட்டிப்பக்,","கவறு உற்ற வடு ஏய்க்கும், காமரு பூங் கடல் சேர்ப்ப!","முத்து உறழ் மணல் எக்கர் அளித்தக்கால், முன் ஆயம்","பத்து உருவம் பெற்றவன் மனம் போல, நந்தியாள் -","அத் திறத்து நீ நீங்க, அணி வாடி, அவ் ஆயம்","வித்தத்தால் தோற்றான் போல், வெய் துயர் உழப்பவோ?","முடத் தாழை முடுக்கருள் அளித்தக்கால், வித்தாயம்","இடைத் தங்கக் கண்டவன் மனம் போல, நந்தியாள் -","கொடைத் தக்காய்! நீ ஆயின், நெறி அல்லாக் கதி ஓடி","உடைப் பொதி இழந்தான் போல் உறு துயர் உழப்பவோ?","நறு வீ தாழ் புன்னைக் கீழ் நயந்து நீ அளித்தக்கால்,","மறு வித்தம் இட்டவன் மனம் போல, நந்தியாள் -","அறிவித்து நீ நீங்கக் கருதியாய்க்கு, அப் பொருள்","சிறு வித்தம் இட்டான் போல், செறி துயர் உழப்பவோ?","ஆங்கு,","கொண்டு பலர் தூற்றும் கௌவை அஞ்சாய்","தீண்டற்கு அருளித் திறன் அறிந்து, எழீஇப்","பாண்டியம் செய்வான் பொருளினும்","ஈண்டுக, இவள் நலம்; ஏறுக, தேரே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the lovely sea, where crabs dance in burrows that the waves have scored like the marks dice leave on the gaming floor, that each time he was kind to the girl on the pearl-white dune, in the bent screwpine's thicket and under the punnai she swelled like a gambler whose throw comes up winning, and asks whether, now he means to leave, she must suffer like the one who lost by the stake or the traveller who strayed and lost his bundle; let him not fear the gossip, take her by the rightful way so that her beauty gathers greater than the wealth of the Pandya king, and mount the chariot.","n":"The whole poem is built on the dice game the opening scene supplies: the gaming words (பத்து, வித்தம், வித்தாயம், மறு வித்தம், சிறு வித்தம்) name throws and stakes whose exact values are not recoverable, but the pattern is clear, each meeting place a winning throw and each leaving a lost stake, so the friend turns his kindness into a gamble he must end by marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","alar","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 136","lb":"KL136","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["திமில்","திமில்",1,"seythi","scene","fishing boat"],["அலவன்","அலவன்",2,"pira","simile","crab"],["முத்து","முத்து",5,"pira","simile","pearl"],["தாழை","தாழை",9,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னைக்","புன்னை",13,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:137":{"book":"kalittokai","num":137,"t":["அரிதே, தோழி! நாண் நிறுப்பாம் என்று உணர்தல்;","பெரிதே காமம்; என் உயிர் தவச் சிறிதே;","பலவே யாமம்; பையுளும் உடைய;","சிலவே, நம்மோடு உசாவும் அன்றில்;","அழல் அவிர் வயங்கு இழை ஒலிப்ப, உலமந்து,","எழில் எஞ்சு மயிலின் நடுங்கிச், சேக்கையின்","அழல் ஆகின்று, அவர் நக்கதன் பயனே.","மெல்லிய நெஞ்சு பையுள் கூரத், தம்","சொல்லினான் எய்தமை அல்லது, அவர் நம்மை","வல்லவன் தைஇய வாக்கு அமை கடு விசை","வில்லினான் எய்தலோ இலர்மன்; ஆய் இழை!","வில்லினும் கடிது, அவர் சொல்லினுள் பிறந்த நோய்.","நகை முதல் ஆக, நட்பினுள் எழுந்த","தகைமையின் நலிதல் அல்லது, அவர் நம்மை","வகைமையின் எழுந்த தொல் முரண் முதல் ஆகப்,","பகைமையின் நலிதலோ இலர்மன்; ஆய் இழை!","பகைமையின் கடிது, அவர் தகைமையின் நலியும் நோய்.","'நீயலேன்' என்று என்னை அன்பினால் பிணித்துத், தம்","சாயலின் சுடுதல் அல்லது, அவர் நம்மைப்","பாய் இருள் அற நீக்கும் நோய் தபு நெடும் சுடர்த்","தீயினால் சுடுதலோ இலர்மன்; ஆய் இழை!","தீயினும் கடிது அவர் சாயலின் கனலும் நோய்.","ஆங்கு -","அன்னர் காதலர் ஆக, அவர் நமக்கு","இன் உயிர் போத்தரும் மருத்துவர் ஆயின்,","யாங்கு ஆவது கொல்? - தோழி! எனையதூஉம்.","தாங்குதல் வலித்தன்று ஆயின்,","நீங்க அரிது உற்றஅன்று, அவர் உறீஇய நோயே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that holding to modesty is hard, love is great and her life very small, the night watches are many and full of sorrow, and only the andril birds keep her company; trembling like a peacock losing its beauty, she finds her bed turned to fire, the fruit of his smile; he struck her only with words, not with a bow, yet the sickness born of his words is harsher than the bow; he hurt her only through the grace of friendship, not through old enmity, yet that is harsher than enmity; he burned her only with his gentleness, not with fire, yet that burns worse than fire; if such lovers are the physicians who could give back her life, what will become of her? unless she can hold firm, the sickness he gave will not leave.","n":"The three refrains each concede a milder weapon (word, friendship, gentleness) and then rank its wound above the harsher one (bow, enmity, fire), so the poem argues that love's injuries are worse because they come from kindness. The andril, the bird that will not part from its mate, keeps her company only by calling through the night; it carries a mood, not an act, and is left as scene. The last two lines turn on வலித்தன்று and can also be read as a resolve to endure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","naanam","iravu","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 137","lb":"KL137","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அன்றில்","அன்றில்",4,"pul","scene",""],["மயிலின்","மயில்",6,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:138":{"book":"kalittokai","num":138,"t":["எழில் மருப்பு எழில் வேழம் இகுதரு கடாத்தால்","தொழில் மாறித் தலை வைத்த தோட்டி கை நிமிர்ந்தாங்கு,","அறிவும், நம் அறிவு ஆய்ந்த அடக்கமும், நாணொடு,","வறிது ஆகப் பிறர் என்னை நகுபவும், நகுபு உடன்","மின் அவிர் நுடக்கமும் கனவும் போல், மெய் காட்டி -","என் நெஞ்சம் என்னோடு நில்லாமை நனி வௌவித்","தன் நலம் கரந்தாளைத் தலைப்படும் ஆறு எவன் கொலோ?","மணிப் பீலி சூட்டிய நூலொடு மற்றை","அணிப் பூளை, ஆவிரை, எருக்கொடு, பிணித்து யாத்து,","மல்லல் ஊர் மறுகின் கண் இவள் பாடும், இஃது ஒத்தன் -","எல்லீரும் கேட்டீமின் என்று.","படரும், பனை ஈன்ற மாவும் - சுடர் இழை","நல்கியாள் நல்கியவை;","பொறை என் வரைத்து அன்றிப் பூ நுதல் ஈத்த","நிறை அழி காம நோய் நீந்தி, அறை உற்ற","உப்பு இயல் பாவை உறை உற்றது போல,","உக்கு விடும் என் உயிர்.","பூளை, பொல மலர் ஆவிரை - வேய் வென்ற","தோளாள் எமக்கு ஈத்த பூ;","உரிது என் வரைத்து அன்றி, ஒள் இழை தந்த","பரிசு அழி பைதல் நோய் மூழ்கி, எரி பரந்த","நெய்யுள் மெழுகின் நிலையாது, பைபயத்","தேயும் - அளித்து - என் உயிர்.","இளையாரும், ஏதிலவரும் - உளைய, யான்","உற்றது உசாவும் துணை.","என்று யான் பாடக் கேட்டு,","அன்புறு கிளவியாள் அருளி வந்து அளித்தலின் -","துன்பத்தில் துணை ஆய மடல் இனி இவள் பெற","இன்பத்துள் இடம்படல் என்று இரங்கினள் - அன்புற்று","அடங்குஅரும் தோற்றத்து அரும் தவம் முயன்றோர் தம்","உடம்பு ஒழித்து உயர் உலகு இனிது பெற்றாங்கே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that as the goad straightens and fails in the mahout's hand when the elephant's must flows, so his knowledge, restraint and shame have gone and others laugh at him, for she who showed her form like the flicker of lightning or a dream has carried off his heart and hidden her beauty; tying peacock feathers, pulai, avirai and erukku to his horse, he sings in the street of the rich town: grief and the palmyra-born horse are what she gave, and his life will dissolve like a salt doll in rain; pulai and golden avirai are the flowers she gave, and his life will melt like wax in fire; only the young and strangers ask after him; hearing the song she came with loving words and grace, and she pitied the madal, his companion in suffering, which would have no place in his joy now that he had her, as those who strive in hard penance leave their bodies behind when they reach the high world.","n":"She gives two things in the refrains, grief together with the madal and the madal's flowers, so the instrument of his public shame is counted as her gift. The close is unexpected: she pities the madal, which served him in sorrow and must now be discarded, and the simile of ascetics leaving their bodies makes the palm-stem horse the body of his penance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamanoy","naanam","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 138","lb":"KL138","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","ur",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"],["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["வேழம்","வேழம்",1,"maa","simile","elephant"],["பீலி","பீலி",8,"pul","ornament","peacock feather"],["பூளை","பூளை",9,"maram","ornament",""],["ஆவிரை","ஆவிரை",9,"maram","ornament",""],["எருக்கொடு","எருக்கு",9,"maram","ornament",""],["பனை","பனை",12,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["பூளை","பூளை",18,"maram","gift_food",""],["ஆவிரை","ஆவிரை",18,"maram","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:139":{"book":"kalittokai","num":139,"t":["'சான்றவிர் வாழியோ! சான்றவிர்! என்றும்","பிறர் நோயும் தம் நோய் போல் போற்றி, அறன் அறிதல்","சான்றவர்க்கு எல்லாம் கடன் ஆனால், இவ் இருந்த","சான்றீர்! உமக்கு ஒன்று அறிவுறுப்பேன்: மான்ற","துளி இடை மின்னுப் போல் தோன்றி, ஒருத்தி,","ஒளியோடு உரு என்னைக் காட்டி, அளியள், என்","நெஞ்சு ஆறு கொண்டாள், அதன் கொண்டும் துஞ்சேன்;","அணி அலங்கு ஆவிரைப் பூவோடு எருக்கின்","பிணையல் அம் கண்ணி மிலைந்து, மணி ஆர்ப்ப,","ஓங்கு இரும் பெண்ணை மடல் ஊர்ந்து, என் எவ்வ நோய்","தாங்குதல் தேற்றா இடும்பைக்கு உயிர்ப்பு ஆக","வீங்கு இழை மாதர் திறத்து ஒன்று, நீங்காது,","பாடுவேன், பாய் மா நிறுத்து.","யாமத்தும் எல்லையும் எவ்வத் திரை அலைப்ப","'மா மேலேன்' என்று, மடல் புணையா நீந்துவேன் -","தே மொழி மாதர் உறாஅது உறீஇய","காமக் கடல் அகப்பட்டு.","உய்யா அரு நோய்க்கு உயவாகும் - மையல்","உறீஇயாள் ஈத்த இம் மா.","காணுநர் எள்ளக் கலங்கித், தலை வந்து, என்","ஆண் எழில் முற்றி உடைத்து உள் அழித்தரும் -","'மாண் இழை மாதராள் ஏஎர்' எனக் காமனது","ஆணையால் வந்த படை.","காமக் கடும் பகையின் தோன்றினேற்கு ஏமம் -","எழில் நுதல் ஈத்த இம் மா.","அகை எரி ஆனாது, என் ஆர் உயிர் எஞ்சும்","வகையினால், உள்ளம் சுடுதரும் மன்னோ -","முகை ஏர் இலங்கு எயிற்று இன் நகை மாதர்","தகையால் தலைக்கொண்ட நெஞ்சு!","அழல் மன்ற காம அரு நோய்; நிழல் மன்ற,","நேர் இழை ஈத்த இம் மா.","ஆங்கு அதை,","அறிந்தனிர் ஆயின், சான்றவிர்! தான் தவம்","ஒரீஇத் துறக்கத்தின் வழீஇ, ஆன்றோர்","உள் இடப்பட்ட அரசனைப் பெயர்த்து, அவர்","உயர்நிலை உலகம் உறீஇயாங்கு, என்","துயர் நிலை தீர்த்தல் நும் தலைக் கடனே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the worthies of the assembly that since it is the duty of the worthy to guard others' pain as their own and to know what is right, he will inform them: a woman appeared to him like lightning among rain drops, showed him her form and took his heart, so he cannot sleep; crowned with a garland of avirai and erukku, bells ringing, he rides the stem of the tall palmyra and sings of her; tossed night and day by waves of grief in the sea of love she caused without feeling it, he swims with the madal as his raft; the horse she gave is the cure for a sickness past cure, the shelter against the army that Kama sends to break his manly beauty, and the shade against love's fire; so, as the sages raised again to the high world the king who had fallen from heaven, it is their duty to end his distress.","n":"He frames the madal as a petition: the opening and closing lines put the duty on the worthies (சான்றீர்), and the three refrains call the horse a cure, a shelter and a shade, the very things the assembly should give. The king fallen from heaven and restored by sages is an epic story the poet assumes; the name is not given.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","saandror","oppuravu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 139","lb":"KL139","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","ur",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["ஆவிரைப்","ஆவிரை",8,"maram","ornament",""],["எருக்கின்","எருக்கு",8,"maram","ornament",""],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",10,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:140":{"book":"kalittokai","num":140,"t":["கண்டவிர் எல்லாம் கதுமென வந்து, ஆங்கே,","பண்டு அறியாதீர் போல நோக்குவீர்; கொண்டது","மா என்று உணர்மின்; மடல் அன்று; மற்று இவை","பூ அல்ல; பூளை, உழிந்ஞையோடு யாத்த","புன வரை இட்ட வயங்கு தார்ப் பீலி,","பிடி அமை நூலொடு பெய்ம் மணி கட்டி,","அடர் பொன் அவிர் ஏய்க்கும் ஆவிரம் கண்ணி,","நெடியோன் மகன் நயந்து தந்தாங்கு, அனைய","வடிய வடிந்த வனப்பின், என் நெஞ்சம்","இடிய இடைக் கொள்ளும் சாயல், ஒருத்திக்கு","அடியுறை காட்டிய செல்வேன்; மடியன்மின்,","அன்னேன் ஒருவனேன், யான்;","என்னானும், பாடு எனில் பாடவும் வல்லேன், சிறிது ஆங்கே","ஆடு எனில் ஆடலும் ஆற்றுகேன்; பாடுகோ -","என் உள் இடும்பை தணிக்கும் மருந்து ஆக,","நல் நுதல் ஈத்த இம் மா?","திங்கள் அரவு உறின், தீர்க்கலார் ஆயினும்,","தம் காதல் காட்டுவர் சான்றவர் - இன் சாயல்","ஒண் தொடி நோய் நோக்கில் பட்ட என் நெஞ்ச நோய்","கண்டும், கண்ணோடாது, இவ் ஊர்.","தாங்காச் சினத்தொடு காட்டி உயிர் செகுக்கும்","பாம்பும் அவைப் படில் உய்யும்ஆம் - பூம் கண்","வணர்ந்து ஒலி ஐம்பாலாள் செய்த இக் காமம்","உணர்ந்தும் உணராது இவ் ஊர்.","வெம் சுழிப் பட்ட மகற்குக் கரை நின்றார்","அஞ்சல் என்றாலும் உயிர்ப்பு உண்டுஆம் - அம் சீர்ச்","செறிந்து ஏர் முறுவலாள் செய்த இக் காமம்","அறிந்தும், அறியாது, இவ் ஊர்.","ஆங்க,","என் கண் இடும்பை அறீஇயினென்; நும் கண்","தெருளுற நோக்கித் தெரியும்கால், இன்ன","மருளுறு நோயொடு மம்மர் அகல,","இருளுறு கூந்தலாள் என்னை","அருளுறச் செயின், நுமக்கு அறனும்ஆர் அதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the people who rush up and stare as if they had never seen the like that what he rides is a horse, not a palm stem, and these are no mere flowers but a garland of pulai and ulinjai, peacock feathers from the hill field, bells on a thread and an avirai chaplet like beaten gold, given as if by the son of the Tall One; he goes to show his servitude to a woman of finished beauty whose gentleness breaks his heart; he can sing if told to sing and dance if told to dance, and offers to sing the horse she gave as a cure; the worthy show their love even when they cannot free the moon from the snake, a snake that kills in rage may be spared if caught by an assembly, a man in a whirlpool can breathe when those on the bank say 'fear not', but this town sees his sickness and knows his love and does not soften; if they look clearly and make her grant him grace, the virtue will be theirs.","n":"The three refrains each set a case where onlookers help though they cannot cure (the eclipse, the snake, the drowning boy) against the town that sees, knows and does nothing (கண்டும் கண்ணோடாது); the madal is an appeal to the town's கண்ணோட்டம, and the closing line makes granting it an act of அறம்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","kannottam","saandror","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 140","lb":"KL140","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","ur",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["பூளை","பூளை",4,"maram","ornament",""],["உழிந்ஞையோடு","உழிஞை",4,"maram","ornament",""],["பீலி","பீலி",5,"pul","ornament","peacock feather"],["ஆவிரம்","ஆவிரை",7,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:141":{"book":"kalittokai","num":141,"t":["அரிதினின் தோன்றிய யாக்கை புரிபு தாம்","வேட்டவை செய்து, ஆங்குக், காட்டி மற்று ஆங்கே,","அறம் பொருள் இன்பம் என்று அம் மூன்றின் ஒன்றன்","திறம் சேரார் செய்யும் தொழில்கள் அறைந்தன்று -","அணி நிலைப் பெண்ணை மடல் ஊர்ந்து, ஒருத்தி","அணி நலம் பாடி வரற்கு.","ஓர் ஒருகால் உள்வழியள் ஆகி, நிறை மதி","நீருள் நிழல்போல், கொளற்கு அரியள் - போருள்","அடல் மா மேல் ஆற்றுவேன், என்னை, மடல் மா மேல்","மன்றம் படர்வித்தவள் - வாழி, சான்றீர்!","பொய் தீர் உலகம் எடுத்த கொடி மிசை,","மை அறு மண்டிலம் வேட்டனள் - வையம்","புரவு ஊக்கும் உள்ளத்தேன் என்னை இரவு ஊக்கும்","இன்னா இடும்பை செய்தாள் - அம்ம, சான்றீர்!","கரந்தாங்கே இன்னா நோய் செய்யும், மற்று இஃதோ -","பரந்த சுணங்கின் பணைத் தோளாள் பண்பு?","இடி உமிழ் வானத்து, இரவு இருள் போழும்","கொடி மின்னுக் கொள்வேன் என்றன்னள் - வடி நாவின்","வல்லார் முன் சொல் வல்லேன் என்னைப் பிறர் முன்னர்க்","கல்லாமை காட்டியவள் - வாழி, சான்றீர்!","என்று ஆங்கே,","வருந்த மா ஊர்ந்து, மறுகின் கண் பாடத்","திருந்து இழைக்கு ஒத்த கிளவி கேட்டு, ஆங்கே","பொருந்தாதார் போர் வல் வழுதிக்கு அரும் திறை","போலக், கொடுத்தார் தமர்."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the worthies that the learned have declared riding the palm-stem horse and singing a woman's beauty to be among the acts of those who, born in a body hard to gain, reach none of the three ends of virtue, wealth and pleasure; yet she, close at times yet as hard to grasp as the full moon's image in water, made him, who rides the war horse in battle, go to the common on the madal horse; she wants the spotless disk on the banner of the truthful world, and made him, whose heart was set on protecting the earth, eager to beg; she seemed to want to seize the lightning that splits the night, and made him, who could speak before the sharpest tongues, show himself unlettered before others; as he rode in pain through the street and sang words fitting her, her kin gave her to him as enemies give hard tribute to Vazhuthi, mighty in war.","n":"Each refrain sets a reversal of rank: the war horse becomes the madal horse, the protector of the earth a beggar, the eloquent man a dunce; the opening concedes that the learned rank madal outside all three ends of life, so the plea is made against the code it cites. Lines 11 and 12 (the disk on the banner of the truthful world) are obscure, and the reading of that refrain is not settled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["madal","aanmai","iravu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 141","lb":"KL141","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","ur",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",5,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[["king","வழுதிக்கு",24]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:142":{"book":"kalittokai","num":142,"t":["புரிவு உண்ட புணர்ச்சிஉள் புல் ஆரா மாத்திரை,","அருகுவித்து ஒருவரை அகற்றலின், தெரிவார் கண்","செய நின்ற பண்ணின்உள் செவி சுவை கொள்ளாது,","நயம் நின்ற பொருள் கெடப் புரி அறு நரம்பினும்","பயன் இன்று மன்ற அம்ம, காமம் - இவள் மன்னும்,","ஒள் நுதல் ஆயத்தார் ஓராங்குத் திளைப்பினும்,","முள் நுனை தோன்றாமை முறுவல் கொண்டு, அடக்கித், தன்","கண்ணினும் முகத்தினும் நகுபவள்; பெண் இன்றி","யாவரும் தண் குரல் கேட்ப, நிரை வெண் பல்","மீ உயர் தோன்ற, நகாஅ, நக்காங்கே,","பூ உயிர்த்தன்ன புகழ் சால் எழில் உண் கண்","ஆய் இதழ் மல்க அழும்.","ஓஒ! அழிதகப் பாராதே, அல்லல் குறுகினம்;","காண்பாம் - கனம் குழை பண்பு.","என்று, எல்லீரும் என் செய்தீர்? என்னை நகுதிரோ?","நல்ல நகாஅலிர் மன் கொலோ - யான் உற்ற","அல்லல் உறீஇயான் மாய மலர் மார்பு","புல்லிப் புணரப் பெறின்.","'எல்லா! நீ உற்றது எவனோ மற்று? என்றீரேல், என் சிதை","செய்தான் இவன்' என, 'உற்றது இது' என,","எய்த உரைக்கும் உரன் அகத்து உண்டாயின்,","பைதல ஆகி பசக்குவ மன்னோ - என்","நெய்தல் மலர் அன்ன கண்?","கோடு வாய் கூடாப் பிறையைப், பிறிது ஒன்று","நாடுவேன், கண்டனென்; சிற்றில்உள் கண்டு, ஆங்கே,","ஆடையான் மூஉய் அகப்படுப்பேன்; சூடிய,","காணான், திரிதரும் கொல்லோ - மணி மிடற்று","மாண் மலர் கொன்றையவன்?","'தெள்ளியேம்' என்று உரைத்துத், தேராது, ஒரு நிலையே,","'வள்ளியை ஆக!' என நெஞ்சை வலி உறீஇ,","உள்ளி வருகுவர் கொல்லோ? வளைந்து யான்","எள்ளி இருக்குவேன்மன் கொலோ? நள்இருள்","மாந்தர் கடி கொண்ட கங்குல், கனவினால்,","தோன்றினன் ஆகத், தொடுத்தேன்மன், யான்; தன்னைப்","பையெனக் காண்கு விழிப்ப, யான் பற்றிய","கை உளே மாய்ந்தான், கரந்து.","கதிர் பகா ஞாயிறே! கல் சேர்தி ஆயின்,","அவரை நினைத்து, நிறுத்து என் கை நீட்டித்","தருகுவை ஆயின், தவிரும் - என் நெஞ்சத்து","உயிர் திரியா மாட்டிய தீ.","மை இல் சுடரே! மலை சேர்தி நீ ஆயின்,","பௌவ நீர்த் தோன்றிப் பகல் செய்யும் மாத்திரை,","கை விளக்கு ஆகக் கதிர் சில தாராய்! என்","தொய்யில் சிதைத்தானைத் தேர்கு.","சிதைத்தானைச் செய்வது எவன் கொலோ? எம்மை","நயந்து, நலம் சிதைத்தான்.","மன்றப் பனை மேல் மலை மாந் தளிரே! நீ","தொன்று இவ் உலகத்துக் கேட்டும் அறிதியோ?","மென் தோள் ஞெகிழ்த்தான் தகை அல்லால், யான் காணேன் -","நன்று தீது என்று பிற.","நோய் எரி ஆகச் சுடினும், சுழற்றி, என்","ஆய் இதழ் உள்ளே கரப்பன் - கரந்தாங்கே","நோய் உறு வெந் நீர்; தெளிப்பின், தலைக்கொண்டு","வேவது, அளித்து இவ் உலகு.","மெலியப் பொறுத்தேன் களைந்தீமின் - சான்றீர்! -","நலிதரும் காமமும் கௌவையும் என்று, இவ்","வலிதின் உயிர் காவாத் தூங்கி, ஆங்கு, என்னை","நலியும் விழுமம் இரண்டு.","எனப் பாடி,","இனைந்து நொந்து அழுதனள்; நினைந்து நீடு உயிர்த்தனள்;","எல்லையும் இரவும் கழிந்தன என்று எண்ணி, எல் இரா","நல்கிய கேள்வன் இவன் - மன்ற, மெல்ல","மணிஉள் பரந்த நீர் போலத் துணிவாம் -","கலம் சிதை இல்லத்துக் காழ் கொண்டு தேற்றக்","கலங்கிய நீர் போல் தெளிந்து நலம்பெற்றாள்,","நல் எழில் மார்பனைச் சார்ந்து!"],"p":"","r":"The poet says that love which, short of union, keeps one lover far is more fruitless than a lute whose string has snapped, and shows a girl who once smiled with hidden teeth among her friends now laughing aloud so that all hear and then weeping; she sings to the onlookers: would they laugh if she could embrace the deceiver's chest? if she had the strength to say 'he ruined me', would her neythal eyes have paled? she would catch the crescent moon and hide it in her little house, and would Shiva then wander looking for it? will he come back, or will she sit and scorn him? in a dream she seized him and he vanished from her hand as she woke; she asks the setting sun to put him in her outstretched hand, and to lend her a few rays as a lamp to search for him; she tells the mango shoot on the palmyra of the common that she sees nothing good or bad but his grace; her tears would burn the world if she let them fall; she begs the worthies to remove love and gossip, the two pains that hang her life; then the husband who had given her nights of grace came, and she grew clear and well again, like muddy water cleared with clearing-nut, leaning on his breast.","n":"Her speech moves from argument (would you laugh if I had him?) to fantasy (she would steal Shiva's crescent and he would wander for it) to appeals to the sun, a mango shoot and the worthies; the dream in which he vanishes from her hand on waking (lines 33 to 36) is the hinge. The two closing similes, water clearing in a gem and muddy water cleared by the nut, make her madness a clouding that his return settles.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","kanavu","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 142","lb":"KL142","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","ur",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["நரம்பினும்","நரம்பு",4,"yaazh","simile","lute string"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",23,"maram","simile","water lily"],["கொன்றையவன்","கொன்றையவன்",28,"deyvam","scene","Shiva"],["பனை","பனை",47,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["மாந்","மா",47,"maram","scene","mango"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:143":{"book":"kalittokai","num":143,"t":["\"அகல் ஆங்கண், இருள் நீங்க, அணி நிலாத் திகழ்ந்த பின்","பகல் ஆங்கண் பையென்ற மதியம் போல், நகல் இன்று","நல் நுதல் நீத்த திலகத்தள், 'மின்னி","மணி பொரு பசும் பொன் கொல்? மா ஈன்ற தளிரின் மேல்","கணிகாரம் கொட்கும் கொல்?' என்றாங்கு அணி செல,","மேனி மறைத்த பசலையள், ஆனாது","நெஞ்சம் வெறியா நினையா, நிலன் நோக்கா,","அஞ்சா, அழாஅ, அரற்றா, இஃது ஒத்தி","என் செய்தாள் கொல்?\" என்பீர் - கேட்டீமின்- பொன் செய்தேன்.","மறையின் தன் யாழ் கேட்ட மானை அருளாது,","அறை கொன்று, மற்று அதன் ஆர் உயிர் எஞ்ச,","பறை அறைந்தாங்கு, ஒருவன் நீத்தான் - அவனை","அறை நவ நாட்டில் நீர் கொண்டு தரின், யானும்","நிறை உடையேன் ஆகுவேன் மன்ற - மறையின் என்","மென் தோள் நெகிழ்த்தானை மேஎய், அவன் ஆங்கண்","சென்று, சேண் பட்டது என் நெஞ்சு.","'ஒன்றி முயங்கும்' என்று, என் பின் வருதிர்; மற்று ஆங்கே","'உயங்கினாள்' என்று, ஆங்கு உசாதிர்; 'மற்று அந்தோ","மயங்கினாள்!' என்று மருடிர்; கலங்கன்மின் -","இன் உயிர் அன்னார்க்கு எனைத்து ஒன்றும் தீது இன்மை","என் உயிர் காட்டாதோ மற்று?","'பழி தபு ஞாயிறே! பாடு அறியாதார் கண்","கழியக் கதழ்வை' எனக் கேட்டு, நின்னை","வழிபட்டு இரக்குவேன் வந்தேன் - என் நெஞ்சம்","அழியத் துறந்தானைச் சீறும்கால் என்னை","ஒழிய விடாதீமோ என்று.","அழிதக, மாஅம் தளிர் கொண்ட போழ்தினான், இவ் ஊரார்","தாஅம் தளிர் சூடித் தம் நலம் பாடுப;","ஆஅம் தளிர்க்கும் இடைச் சென்றார் மீள்தரின்,","யாஅம் தளிர்க்குவேம் மன்.","நெய்தல் நெறிக்கவும் வல்லன்; நெடு மென் தோள்","பெய் கரும்பு ஈர்க்கவும் வல்லன்; இள முலை மேல்","தொய்யில் எழுதவும் வல்லன்; தன் கையில்","சிலை வல்லான் போலும் செறிவினான்; நல்ல","பல வல்லன் - தோள் ஆள்பவன்.","நினையும் என் உள்ளம் போல், நெடும் கழி மலர் கூம்ப,","இனையும் என் நெஞ்சம் போல், இனம் காப்பார் குழல் தோன்றச்","சாய என் கிளவி போல், செவ்வழி யாழ் இசை நிற்ப,","போய என் ஒளியே போல், ஒரு நிலையே பகல் மாய,","காலன் போல் வந்த கலக்கத்தோடு என் தலை","மாலையும் வந்தன்று, இனி.","இருளொடு யான் ஈங்கு உழப்ப, என் இன்றிப் பட்டாய்,","அருள் இலை! வாழி! - சுடர்!","ஈண்டு நீர் ஞாலத்துள் எம் கேள்வர் இல் ஆயின்,","மாண்ட மனம் பெற்றார் மாசு இல் துறக்கத்து","வேண்டிய வேண்டியாங்கு எய்துதல் வாய் எனின்,","யாண்டும் உடையேன் இசை.","ஊர் அலர் தூற்றும்; இவ் உய்யா விழுமத்துப்","பீர் அலர் போல பெரியப் பசந்தன -","நீர் அலர் நீலம் என, அவர்க்கு, அஞ்ஞான்று,","பேர் அஞர் செய்த என் கண்.","தன் உயிர் போலத் தழீஇ, உலகத்து","மன் உயிர் காக்கும் இம் மன்னனும் என் கொலோ -","இன் உயிர் அன்னானைக் காட்டி, எனைத்து ஒன்றும்","என் உயிர் காவாதது?","என ஆங்கு,","மன்னிய நோயொடு மருள் கொண்ட மனத்தவள்,","பல் மலை இறந்தவன் பணிந்து வந்து அடி சேரத்,","தென்னவன் தெளித்த தேஎம் போல,","இன் நகை எய்தினள், இழந்த தன் நலனே!"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the onlookers, who ask what has happened to her, dulled like the moon by day, her tilakam gone, pallor covering her as if gold had been laid on her or kanikaram flowers whirled on mango shoots, that a man left her as openly as a drum proclaims, crueller than one who heard a deer drawn by his lute and killed it on the rock, and her heart has gone far after him; if they bring him she will regain her restraint; let them not fret, for her life shows him safe; she has come to beg the blameless sun, who scorches the unjust, not to leave her out when it punishes him; the town sings wearing mango shoots, and she would flower if he returned; he is skilled at braiding neythal, drawing cane on her shoulders and tholil on her breasts; evening has come like death, the backwater flowers closing like her thoughts, the herders' flute sounding like her grieving heart, the lute fading like her voice and the day vanishing like her lustre; the sun sets without her, gossip spreads, her eyes, once blue lilies to him, are pale as peerkku flowers; why does the king who guards all lives as his own not guard hers? then the man who had crossed many hills came humbly to her feet, and she regained her smile and her lost beauty like the land the Pandya made calm.","n":"The evening passage (lines 36 to 41) inverts the usual simile: the landscape is compared to her (the flowers close like her thought, the flute sounds like her heart), so the scene is read as the copy and her grief as the original. Her last appeal turns the puram ideal of the king who guards every life as his own against him, since he cannot guard hers. Lines 10 to 13 (the deer drawn by the lute and the land called அறை நவ நாடு) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","maalai","alar","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 143","lb":"KL143","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","ur",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மா","மா",4,"maram","simile","mango"],["கணிகாரம்","கணிகாரம்",5,"maram","simile",""],["மானை","மான்",10,"maa","scene","deer"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",10,"yaazh","scene",""],["பறை","பறை",12,"parai","simile",""],["தளிர்","மா தளிர்",27,"maram","ornament","mango shoot"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",31,"maram","ornament","water lily"],["கரும்பு","கரும்பு",32,"maram","ornament","sugarcane design"],["குழல்","குழல்",37,"yaazh","simile","herder's flute"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",38,"yaazh","simile",""],["பீர்","பீர்",49,"maram","simile",""],["நீலம்","நீலம்",50,"maram","simile","blue lily"]],"en":[["king","தென்னவன்",59]],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:144":{"book":"kalittokai","num":144,"t":["நல் நுதாஅல்! காண்டை; நினையா, நெடிது உயிரா,","என் உற்றாள் கொல்லோ? இஃது ஒத்தி - பல் மாண்","நகுதரும் - தன் நாணுக் கைவிட்டு, இகுதரும்","கண்ணீர் துடையாக், கவிழ்ந்து, நிலன் நோக்கி","அன்ன இடும்பை பல செய்து, தன்னை","வினவுவார்க்கு ஏதில சொல்லிக், கனவு போல்,","தெருளும் மருளும் மயங்கி வருபவள்","கூறுப கேளாமோ, சென்று?","'எல்லா! நீ என் அணங்கு உற்றனை? யார் நின் இது செய்தார்?","நின் உற்ற அல்லல் உரை', என என்னை","வினவுவீர்! தெற்றெனக் கேண்மின்; ஒருவன்,","'குரல் கூந்தால்! என் உற்ற எவ்வம் நினக்கு யான்","உரைப்பனைத் தங்கிற்று, என் இன் உயிர்' என்று","மருவு ஊட்டி, மாறியதன் கொண்டு, எனக்கு","மருவு உழிப் பட்டது, என் நெஞ்சு.","எங்கும் தெரிந்து அது கொள்வேன், அவன் உள் வழி.","பொங்கு இரு முந்நீர் அகம் எல்லாம் நோக்கினை","திங்கள்உள் தோன்றி இருந்த குறு முயால்! -","எம்கேள் இதன் அகத்து உள்வழிக் காட்டீமோ?","காட்டீயாய் ஆயின், கத நாய் கொளுவுவேன்;","வேட்டுவர் உள் வழிச் செப்புவேன்; ஆட்டி","மதியொடு பாம்பு மடுப்பேன்' மதி திரிந்த","என் அல்லல் தீராய் எனின்.","என்று ஆங்கே உள் நின்ற எவ்வம் உரைப்ப மதியொடு","வெள் மழை ஓடிப் புகுதி; சிறிது என்னைக்","கண்ணோடினாய் போறி, நீ.","நீடு இலைத் தாழைத் துவர் மணல் கானல்உள்","ஓடுவேன் ஓடி ஒளிப்பேன்! பொழில் தொறும்","நாடுவேன்; கள்வன் கரந்து இருக்கற்பாலன் கொல்?","ஆய் பூ அடும்பின் அலர் கொண்டு உதுக் காண், எம்","கோதை புனைந்த வழி.","உதுக் காண் - சாஅய் மலர் காட்டி, சால்பு இலான் யாம் ஆடும்","பாவை கொண்டு ஓடியுழி.","உதுக் காண் - தொய்யில் பொறித்த வழி.","உதுக் காண் - 'தையால்! தேறு' எனத் தேற்றி, அறன் இல்லான்","பைய முயங்கியுழி.","அளிய என் உள்ளத்து, உயவுத் தேர் ஊர்ந்து,","விளியா நோய் செய்து, இறந்த அன்புஇல் அவனைத்","தெளிய - விசும்பினும் ஞாலத்து அகத்தும்","வளியே! எதிர்போம் - பல கதிர் ஞாயிற்று","ஒளி உள் வழி எல்லாம் சென்று, முனிபு எம்மை","உண்மை நலன் உண்டு ஒளித்தானைக் காட்டீமோ;","காட்டாயேல், மண்ணகம் எல்லாம் ஒருங்கு சுடுவேன், என்","கண்ணீர் அழலால் தெளித்து.","பேணான் துறந்தானை நாடும் இடம் விடாய் ஆயின் -","பிறங்கு இரு முந்நீர்! - வெறு மணல் ஆகப்","புறம் காலின் போக இறைப்பேன், முயலின்","அறம் புணை ஆகலும் உண்டு.","துறந்தானை நாடித் தருகிற்பாய் ஆயின், நினக்கு ஒன்று","பாடுவேன், என் நோய் உரைத்து.","புல்லிய கேளிர் புணரும் பொழுது உணரேன் -","எல்லி ஆக, 'எல்லை' என்று ஆங்கே, பகல் முனிவேன்.","எல்லிய காலை இரா முனிவேன்; யான் உற்ற","அல்லல் களைவார் இலேன்.","ஓஒ! கடலே! தெற்றெனக் கண் உள்ளே தோன்ற இமை எடுத்துப்","'பற்றுவேன்' என்று, யான் விழிக்கும்கால் மற்றும் என்","நெஞ்சத்துஉள் ஓடி ஒளித்து, ஆங்கே, துஞ்சா நோய்","செய்யும், அறன் இல் அவன்.","ஓஒ! கடலே! ஊர் தலைக்கொண்டு கனலும் கடும் தீ உள்","நீர் பெய்த காலே சினம் தணியும்; மற்று இஃதோ","ஈரம் இல் கேள்வன் உறீஇய காமத் தீ","நீர் உள் புகினும் சுடும்.","ஓஒ! கடலே! 'எற்றம் இலாட்டி என் ஏமுற்றாள்?' என்று இந் நோய்","உற்று அறியாதாரோ நகுக! நயந்தாங்கே","இற்றா அறியின், முயங்கலேன், மற்று என்னை","அற்றத்து இட்டு ஆற்று அறுத்தான் மார்பு.","ஆங்கு,","கடலொடு புலம்புவோள் கலங்கு அஞர் தீரக்","கெடல் அரும் காதலர் துனை தரப், பிணி நீங்கி,","அறன் அறிந்து ஒழுகும் அங்கணாளனைத்","திறன் இலார் எடுத்து தீ மொழி எல்லாம்","நல் அவைஉள் படக் கெட்டாங்கு,","இல்லாகின்று அவள் ஆய் நுதல் பசப்பே."],"p":"","r":"Someone points out to a companion a girl who sighs, laughs, weeps without wiping her tears, stares at the ground and answers strangely, and they go to hear her; she says a man who swore his life hung on telling her his pain fed her love and changed, and she will search everywhere for him: she asks the hare in the moon, who sees the whole sea, to show where he is, or she will set fierce dogs on it, tell the hunters, and have the snake swallow it with the moon; when a cloud covers the moon she thinks it pities her; she will run through the screwpine groves and show where he made her garland of adumbu flowers, where he ran off with her doll, where he drew tholil on her, where he embraced her saying 'be consoled'; she asks the wind to find the one who ate her beauty and hid, or she will burn the earth with her tears, and threatens to bail the sea dry; she hates day and night alike; when she opens her eyes to catch him he runs and hides in her heart; love's fire burns even in water; had she known she would not have embraced him; then her lover came quickly and her pallor vanished like slander against a just man before a good assembly.","n":"Her threats to the moon, the wind and the sea are a child's threats (dogs for the hare, the snake for the moon, bailing the sea), and the places she points out are those of a childhood game (the doll he ran off with), so her madness returns her to the age of the first meeting. Lines 55 to 58 make the waking lover flee into her heart, the one place she cannot search.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kanavu","ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 144","lb":"KL144","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["vekuli","அலை"]],"k":[["முயால்","முயல்",18,"maa","scene","hare in the moon"],["நாய்","நாய்",20,"maa","scene","dog"],["வேட்டுவர்","வேட்டுவர்",21,"seythi","scene","hunters"],["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",22,"pira","scene","snake"],["தாழைத்","தாழை",27,"maram","scene",""],["அடும்பின்","அடும்பு",30,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:145":{"book":"kalittokai","num":145,"t":["'துனையுநர் விழைதக்க சிறப்பு போல், கண்டார்க்கு","நனவின்உள் உதவாது நள் இருள் வேறு ஆகும்","கனவின் நிலையின்றால், காமம்; ஒருத்தி","உயிர்க்கும்; உசாஅம்! உலம்வரும்; ஓவாள்,","கயல் புரை உண் கண் அரிப்ப அரி வாரப்,","பெயல் சேர் மதி போல, வாள் முகம் தோன்ற,","பல ஒலி கூந்தலாள் பண்பு எல்லாம் துய்த்துத்","துறந்தானை உள்ளி அழூஉம்; அவனை","மறந்தாள் போல் ஆலி நகூஉம்; மருளும்;","சிறந்த தன் நாணும் நலனும் நினையாது,","காமம் முனைஇயாள், அலந்தாள்' என்று, எனைக் காண,","நகான்மின்; கூறுவேன், மாக்காள்! மிகாஅது,","மகளிர் தோள் சேர்ந்த மாந்தர் துயர் கூர நீத்தலும்,","நீள் சுரம் போகியார் வல்லை வந்து அளித்தலும்,","ஊழ் செய்து, இரவும் பகலும் போல், வேறு ஆகி,","வீழ்வார் கண் தோன்றும் தடுமாற்றம் ஞாலத்துள்","வாழ்வார்கட்கு எல்லாம் வரும்;","தாழ்பு, துறந்து, தொடி நெகிழ்த்தான் போகிய கானம்","இறந்து எரி நையாமல், பாஅய் முழங்கி -","வறந்து என்னை செய்தியோ, வானம்? - சிறந்த என்","கண்ணீர்க் கடலால், கனை துளி வீசாயோ,","கொண்மூ குழீஇ முகந்து?","நுமக்கு எவன் போலுமோ? ஊரீர்! எமக்கும் எம்","கண் பாயல் கொண்டு, உள்ளாக் காதலவன் செய்த","பண்பு தர வந்த என் தொடர் நோய் வேது","கொள்வது போலும், கடும் பகல்? ஞாயிறே!","எல்லா கதிரும் பரப்பிப் பகலொடு","செல்லாது நின்றீயல் வேண்டுவல்; நீ செல்லின்,","புல்லென் மருள் மாலைப் போழ்து இன்று வந்து என்னைக்","கொல்லாது போதல் அரிதால்; அதனொடு யான்","செல்லாது நிற்றல் இலேன்","ஒல்லை எம் காதலர்க் கொண்டு, கடல் ஊர்ந்து, காலை நாள்,","போதரின் - காண்குவேன் மன்னோ - பனியொடு","மாலைப் பகை தாங்கி, யான்?","இனியன் என்று ஓம்படுப்பல், ஞாயிறு! இனி,","ஒள் வளை ஓடத் துறந்து; துயர் செய்த","கள்வன் பால் பட்டன்று, ஒளித்து என்னை, உள்ளி -","பெரும் கடல் புல்லெனக் கானல் புலம்ப,","இரும் கழி நெய்தல் இதழ் பொதிந்து தோன்ற,","விரிந்து இலங்கு வெண் நிலா வீசும் பொழுதினான்,","யான் வேண்டு ஒருவன், என் அல்லல் உறீஇயான்;","தான் வேண்டுபவரோடு துஞ்சும் கொல்? - துஞ்சாது","வானும் நிலனும் திசையும் துழாவும் என்","ஆனா படர் மிக்க நெஞ்சு.","ஊரவர்க்கு எல்லாம் பெரு நகை ஆகி, என்","ஆர் உயிர் எஞ்சும்மன்; அங்கு நீ சென்றீ -","நிலவு உமிழ் வான் திங்காள்! ஆய் தொடி கொட்ப,","அளி புறம் மாறி, அருளான் துறந்த அக்","காதலன் செய்த கலக்குறு நோய்க்கு ஏதிலார்","எல்லாரும் தேற்றர், மருந்து","வினைக் கொண்டு என் காம நோய் நீக்கிய ஊரீர்!","எனைத்தானும் எள்ளினும் எள்ளலன், கேள்வன்;","நினைப்பினும், கண் உள்ளே தோன்றும்; அனைத்தற்கே","ஏமராது, ஏமரா ஆறு.","கனை இருள் வானம்! - கடல் முகந்து, என் மேல்","உறையொடு நின்றீயல் வேண்டும்; ஒருங்கே -","நிறை வளை கொட்பித்தான் செய்த துயரால்","இறை இறை பொத்திற்றுத் தீ.","எனப் பாடி,","நோய் உடை நெஞ்சத்து எறியா, இனைபு ஏங்கி,","\"யாவிரும் எம் கேள்வன் காணீரோ?\" என்பவட்கு,","ஆர்வுற்ற பூசற்கு அறம் போல, எய்தந்தார்;","பாயல் கொண்டு உள்ளாதவரை வரக் கண்டு,","மாயவன் மார்பில் திருப் போல் அவள் சேர,","ஞாயிற்று முன்னர் இருள் போல மாய்ந்தது, என்","ஆய் இழை உற்ற துயர்."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the townspeople, who laugh that love is like a dream that gives nothing in waking and that she wanders, weeps for the man who enjoyed and left her and then laughs as if she had forgotten him, not to laugh: that men leave the women they embraced and those gone over long wastes come quickly back is a turn, like night and day, that comes to all who live; she asks the cloud why it roars dry over his forest instead of drawing rain from the sea of her tears; the fierce day seems to foment her sickness; she begs the sun not to set, for evening will kill her, and asks whether she could bear the enmity of evening and its dew if he came over the sea at dawn; when the sea dims, the shore grows lonely and the neythal closes under the moon, she asks whether the one she wants sleeps with the one he wants, while her heart searches sky and earth; she asks the moon to go to him; the townspeople cured her love with rites, but her husband would not scorn her however they mock; let the dark sky rain on her, for the fire has caught all over; then those who sought him brought him as virtue answers a just complaint, she joined him like Sri on Vishnu's chest, and her grief vanished like darkness before the sun.","n":"She answers the town's mockery with a general law (leaving and returning alternate like night and day for everyone), so her lament claims to be ordinary experience, not madness. The townspeople's rites to cure her (வினைக் கொண்டு, line 51) are the veri the akam heroine usually suffers at her mother's hands, here taken over by the town.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","alar","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 145","lb":"KL145","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","ur",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கயல்","கயல்",5,"pira","simile","carp"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",39,"maram","scene","water lily"],["மாயவன்","மாயவன்",64,"deyvam","simile","Vishnu"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:146":{"book":"kalittokai","num":146,"t":["உரை செல உயர்ந்து ஓங்கிச் சேர்ந்தாரை ஒரு நிலையே","வரை நில்லா விழுமம் உறீஇ நடுக்கு உரைத்து, தெறல் மாலை","அரைசினும் அன்பு இன்றாம், காமம்; புரை தீர","அன்ன மெல் சேக்கையுள் ஆராது அளித்தவன்","துன்னி அகலத் துறந்த அணியளாய்,","நாணும் நிறையும் உணர்கல்லாள், தோள் ஞெகிழ்பு,","பேர் அமர் உண்கண் நிறை மல்க, அந்நீர் தன்,","கூர் எயிறு ஆடி, குவி முலை மேல் வார்தர,","தேர் வழி நின்று தெருமரும், ஆய் இழை","கூறுப கேளாமோ, சென்று?","'எல் இழாய்! எற்றி வரைந்தானை, நாணும் மறந்தாள்' என்று,","உற்றனிர் போல, வினவுதிர்! மற்று இது","கேட்டீமின், எல்லீரும் வந்து:","வறம் தெற மாற்றிய வானமும் போலும்;","நிறைந்து என்னை மாய்ப்பது ஓர் வெள்ளமும் போலும் -","சிறந்தவன் தூ அற நீப்ப, பிறங்கி வந்து,","என் மேல் நிலைஇய நோய்.","'நக்கு நலனும் இழந்தாள், இவள்' என்னும்","தக்கவிர் போலும்! இழந்திலேன் மன்னோ -","மிக்க என் நாணும் நலனும் என் உள்ளமும்","அக்கால் அவன் உழை ஆங்கே ஒழிந்தன!","உக்காண் - இஃதோ உடம்பு உயிர்க்கு ஊற்று ஆகச்","செக்கர் அம் புள்ளித் திகிரி அலவனொடு, யான்","நக்கது, பல் மாண் நினைந்து.","கரை காணா நோயுள் அழுந்தாதவனைப்","புரை தவ கூறிக், கொடுமை நுவல்வீர்!","வரைபவன் என்னின் அகலான் - அவனைத்,","திரை தரும் முந்நீர் வளாஅகம் எல்லாம்,","நிரை கதிர் ஞாயிற்றை, நாடு என்றேன்; யானும்","உரை கேட்புஉழி எல்லாம் செல்வேன்; புரை தீர்ந்தான்","யாண்டு ஒளிப்பான் கொலோ மற்று?","மருள் கூர் பிணை போல் மயங்க, வெந்நோய் செய்யும்","மாலையும் வந்து, மயங்கி எரி நுதி","யாமம் தலை வந்தன்று ஆயின், அதற்கு என் நோய்","பாடுவேன், பல்லாருள் சென்று.","யான் உற்ற எவ்வம் உரைப்பின், பலர்த் துயிற்றும்","யாமம், நீ துஞ்சலை மன்.","எதிர்கொள்ளும் ஞாலம், துயில் ஆராது ஆங்கண்","முதிர்பு என் மேல் முற்றிய வெந்நோய் உரைப்பின்","கதிர்கள் மழுங்கி, மதியும் அதிர்வது போல்","ஓடிச் சுழல்வது மன்.","பேர் ஊர் மறுகில் பெரும் துயில் சான்றீரே!","நீரைச் செறுத்து, நிறைவுற ஓம்புமின்;","கார் தலைக்கொண்டு பொழியினும், தீர்வது","போலாது, என் மெய் கனலும் நோய்.","இருப்பினும் நெஞ்சம் கனலும்; செலினே,","வருத்துறும் யாக்கை, வருந்துதல் ஆற்றேன்;","அருப்பம் உடைத்து, என்னுள் எவ்வம் பொருத்திப்","பொறி செய் புனை பாவை போல வறிது உயங்கிச்","செல்வேன், விழுமம் உழந்து.","என ஆங்கு பாட, அருள் உற்று,","வறம் கூர் வானத்து வள் உறைக்கு அலமரும்","புள்ளிற்கு அது பொழிந்தாஅங்கு, மற்றுத் தன்","நல் எழில் மார்பன் முயங்கலின்","அல்லல் தீர்ந்தன்று ஆய் இழை பண்பே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that love, which raises those who join it high and then drops them into trouble without bound, is more pitiless than a king who rules by punishment, and shows a girl, left by the man who loved her on a swan-soft bed, standing on the chariot road, shame and restraint forgotten, tears running over her breasts; she tells those who ask whether she has lost her shame that the sickness he left on her is like a sky that ended a drought and like a flood that fills and drowns her; she has not lost her shame, beauty and heart, for they stayed with him; she laughed with the red-spotted crab, remembering; he who will marry her will not leave her; she told the sun to search the sea-girt world and will go wherever she hears of him; if evening and midnight come she will sing her pain among the crowd, and the night that sends all to sleep would not sleep if it heard her; she tells the sleeping worthies of the town to dam the water, for even the rains would not cool her; staying, her heart burns, going, her body suffers, so she will go like a jointed doll; hearing her, he came, and as rain pours on the bird that cries for drops in a dry sky, his embrace ended her distress.","n":"She denies the charge of shamelessness with a paradox: her shame, beauty and heart are not lost but left with him, so what the town sees is only the body without them. Her threat to the night (lines 36 to 41) is that her song would keep even the night awake and make the moon tremble.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","naanam","nalam","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 146","lb":"KL146","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","ur",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["அன்ன","அன்னம்",4,"pul","scene","swan"],["அலவனொடு","அலவன்",23,"pira","scene","crab"],["பிணை","பிணை",32,"maa","simile","doe"],["புள்ளிற்கு","புள்",53,"pul","simile","rain bird"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:147":{"book":"kalittokai","num":147,"t":["ஆறு அல்ல மொழி தோற்றி, அற வினை கலக்கிய,","தேறுகள் நறவு உண்டார் மயக்கம் போல், காமம்","வேறு ஒரு பாற்று ஆனது கொலோ? சீறு அடிச்","சிலம்பு ஆர்ப்ப இயலியாள் - இவள் மன்னோ, இனி மன்னும்","புலம்பு ஊரப் புல்லென்ற வனப்பினாள் - விலங்கு ஆக,","வேல் நுதி உற நோக்கி, வெயில் உற, உருகும் தன்","தோள் நலம் உண்டானைக் கெடுத்தாள் போல், தெருவில் பட்டு,","ஊண் யாதும் இலள் ஆகி, உயிரினும் சிறந்த தன்","நாண் யாதும் இலள் ஆகி, நகுதலும் நகூஉம்; ஆங்கே","பெண்மையும் இலள் ஆகி அழுதலும் அழூஉம்; தோழி! ஓர்","ஒள் நுதல் உற்றது உழைச் சென்று கேளாமோ?","இவர் யாவர்? ஏமுற்றார் கண்டீரே! ஓஒ!","அமையும் தவறிலீர்மன் கொலோ? - நகையின்","மிக்க தன் காமமும் ஒன்று என்ப; அம் மா","புது நலம் பூ வாடி அற்று, தாம் வீழ்வார்","மதி மருள நீத்தக்கடை.","என்னையே மூசிக், கதுமென நோக்கன்மின்; வந்து","கலைஇய கண், புருவம், தோள், நுசுப்பு, ஏஎர்","சில மழை போல் தாழ்ந்து இருண்ட கூந்தல், அவற்றை","விலை வளம் மாற அறியாது, ஒருவன்","வலை அகப்பட்டது - என் நெஞ்சு.","வாழிய, கேளிர்!","பலவும் சூள் தேற்றத் தெளித்தவன் என்னை","முலை இடை வாங்கி முயங்கினன்; நீத்த","கொலைவனைக் காணேன் கொல், யான்?","காணினும், என்னை அறிதிர்; கதிர் பற்றி,","ஆங்கு எதிர் நோக்குவன்- ஞாயிறே? - எம் கேள்வன்","யாங்கு உளன் ஆயினும் காட்டீமோ? காட்டாயேல்,","வானத்து எவன் செய்தி, நீ?","ஆர் இருள் நீக்கும் விசும்பின் மதி போல,","நீர் உள்ளும் தோன்றுதி, ஞாயிறே! அவ்வழித்","தேரை தினப்படல் ஓம்பு.","நல்கா ஒருவனை நாடி யான் கொள்வனை,","பல் கதிர் சாம்பிப் பகல் ஒழியப், பட்டீமோ -","செல் கதிர் ஞாயிறே! நீ","அறாஅல் இன்று அரி முன்கைக் கொட்கும்","பறாஅப் பருந்தின் கண் பற்றிப் புணர்ந்தான்","கறாஅ எருமைய காடு இறந்தான் கொல்லோ?","உறாஅத் தகை செய்து, இவ் ஊர் உள்ளான் கொல்லோ?","செறாஅது உளன் ஆயின், கொள்வேன்; அவனைப்","பெறாஅது யான் நோவேன்; அவனை என் காட்டிச்","சுறாஅக் கொடியான் கொடுமையை, நீயும்","உறாஅ அரைச! நின் ஓலைக் கண் கொண்டீ;","மறாஅ அரைச! நின் மாலையும் வந்தன்று;","அறாஅ தணிக, இந்நோய்.","தன் நெஞ்சு ஒருவற்கு இனைவித்தல் யாவர்க்கும்","அன்னவோ - காம! நின் அம்பு?","கையாறு செய்தானைக் காணின், கலுழ் கண்ணால்","பையென நோக்குவேன்; தாழ் தானை பற்றுவேன்;","ஐயம் கொண்டு, என்னை அறியான் விடுவானேல்,","'ஒய்' எனப் பூசல் இடுவேன்மன், யான் - அவனை","மெய் ஆகக் கள்வனோ என்று.","வினவன்மின் ஊரவிர்! என்னை, எஞ்ஞான்றும் -","மடாஅ நறவு உண்டார் போல, மருள","விடாஅது உயிரொடு கூடிற்று - என் உண்கண்","படாஅமை செய்தான் தொடர்பு.","கனவினான் காணிய, கண்படா ஆயின்,","நனவினான் ஞாயிறே! காட்டாய் நீ ஆயின்,","பனை ஈன்ற மா ஊர்ந்து அவன் வரக் காமன்","கணை இரப்பேன், கால் புல்லிக்கொண்டு.","என ஆங்கு,","கண் இனைபு, கலுழ்பு ஏங்கினள்;","தோள் ஞெகிழ்பு, வளை நெகிழ்ந்தனள்;","அன்னையோ! எல்லீரும் காண்மின்; மடவரல்","மெல் நடை பேடை துனை தரத் தன் சேர்ந்த","அன்ன வான் சேவல் புணர்ச்சி போல், ஒள் நுதல்","காதலன் மன்ற அவனை வரக் கண்டு, ஆங்கு","ஆழ் துயரம் எல்லாம் மறந்தனள், பேதை,","நகை ஒழிந்து, நாணு மெய் நிற்ப, இறைஞ்சி,","தகை ஆகத் தையலாள் சேர்ந்தாள் - நகை ஆக,","நல் எழில் மார்பன் அகத்து !"],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether love has become like the stupor of those who drink strong toddy, and shows a girl who once walked with ringing anklets now in the street as if she had lost the man who ate her beauty, without food, without the shame dearer than life, laughing and then weeping; she asks who these staring people are, says her heart was caught in the net of a man who did not know the worth of her eyes, brows, shoulders, waist and cloud-dark hair, and that he who calmed her with oaths embraced her and left, the killer; she asks the sun to show where he is or else what use is it in the sky, warns it of being devoured where it appears in water like the moon, and asks whether he has gone past the forest of unmilked buffaloes or stays in this town doing wrong; she asks Kama, king of the shark banner, to write down his cruelty, and asks whether his arrows torment all who love one man; if she sees him she will look softly, seize his cloth and cry 'thief!' if he pretends not to know her; his bond has joined her life like drink and made her eyes sleepless; if the sun will not show him she will clasp Kama's feet and beg his arrows so that he comes riding the palmyra horse; then, like the swan joining his mate as she hurries to him, she saw her lover come, forgot her grief and leaned shyly on his breast.","n":"The madal is turned around at the close: the woman does not ride it but begs Kama's arrows so that the man will ride it to her (lines 59, 60), keeping the convention that a woman does not mount the palm horse while making her the cause of his mounting. Lines 32 and 36 to 38 (the toad that eats the sun, the kite's eye, the forest of unmilked buffaloes) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","naanam","alar","madal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 147","lb":"KL147","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","ur",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["நறவு","நறவு",2,"unavu","simile","toddy"],["தேரை","தேரை",32,"pira","scene","toad"],["பருந்தின்","பருந்து",37,"pul","scene","kite"],["எருமைய","எருமை",38,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["சுறாஅக்","சுறா",42,"pira","ornament","shark banner"],["பனை","பனை",59,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["பேடை","பேடை",65,"pul","simile","swan hen"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",66,"pul","simile","swan cock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:148":{"book":"kalittokai","num":148,"t":["தொல் இயல் ஞாலத்துத் தொழில் ஆற்றி ஞாயிறு,","வல்லவன் கூறிய வினை தலை வைத்தான் போல்,","கல் அடைபு, கதிர் ஊன்றி, கண் பயம் கெடப் பெயர,","அல்லது கெடுப்பவன் அருள் கொண்ட முகம் போல,","மல்லல் நீர்த் திரை ஊர்பு, மால் இருள் மதி சீப்ப,","இல்லவர் ஒழுக்கம் போல் இரும் கழி மலர் கூம்ப,","செல்லும் என் உயிர் புறத்து இறுத்தந்த மருள் மாலை!","மாலை நீ -","இன்புற்றார்க்கு இறைச்சியாய் இயைவதோ செய்தாய்மன்;","அன்புற்றார் அழ, நீத்த அல்லலுள், கலங்கிய","துன்புற்றார்த் துயர் செய்தல் தக்கதோ, நினக்கு?","மாலை நீ -","கலந்தவர் காமத்தை கனற்றலோ செய்தாய்மன்;","நலம் கொண்டு நல்காதார் நனி நீத்த புலம்பின் கண்","அலந்தவர்க்கு அணங்கு ஆதல் தக்கதோ, நினக்கு?","மாலை நீ -","எம் கேள்வன் தருதல் உம் தருகல்லாய்; துணை அல்லை!","பிரிந்தவர்க்கு நோய் ஆகிப் புணர்ந்தவர்க்குப் புணை ஆகித்","திருந்தாத செயின் அல்லால் இல்லையோ, நினக்கு?","என ஆங்கு,","ஆய் இழை மடவரல் அவலம் அகல,","பாய் இருள் பரப்பினை பகல் களைந்தது போலப்","போய் அவர் மண் வௌவி வந்தனர் -","சேய் உறை காதலர் செய் வினை முடித்தே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the evening that it has come and camped outside her departing life, as the sun, its work in the old world done, goes to the hill like one who has carried out the task his master set, the moon combs the dark over the waves like the gracious face of one who destroys wrong, and the backwater flowers close like the conduct of the poor; she charges it that while it is a sweet suggestion to the happy and inflames the love of the united, it torments those left in tears and terrifies the deserted; it neither brings her husband nor keeps her company, a sickness to the parted and a raft to the joined; then, to end her grief, as day clears the spreading dark, her far-dwelling lover returned, his task done, having seized his enemies' land.","n":"Line 9 uses the word இறைச்சி itself: the evening is the suggestive setting (இறைச்சி) for the happy, so the poem names the device by which a time of day carries love's sense and then complains that the same hour means the opposite to the parted. The three similes of sunset (a servant done with work, a just ruler's face, the conduct of the poor) all come from the life of the town and the state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 148","lb":"KL148","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மலர்","கழி மலர்",6,"maram","simile","backwater flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:149":{"book":"kalittokai","num":149,"t":["நிரை திமில் களிறு ஆகத் திரை ஒலி பறை ஆகக்,","கரை சேர் புள் இனத்து அம் சிறை படை ஆக,","அரைசு கால் கிளர்ந்தன்ன உரவு நீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப! கேள்;","கற்பித்தான் நெஞ்சு அழுங்க பகர்ந்து உண்ணான், விச்சைக்கண்","தப்பித்தான் பொருளே போல் தமியவே தேயுமால்;","ஒற்கத்துள் உதவியார்க்கு உதவாதான், மற்று அவன்","எச்சத்துள் ஆயினும், அஃது எறியாது விடாதே காண்;","கேளிர்கள் நெஞ்சு அழுங்கக் கெழுவுற்ற செல்வங்கள்","தாள் இலான் குடியே போல் தமியவே தேயுமால்;","சூள் வாய்த்த மனத்தவன் வினை பொய்ப்பின், மற்று அவன்","வாள் வாய் நன்று ஆயினும், அஃது எறியாது விடாதே காண்;","ஆங்கு,","அனைத்து, இனி - பெரும! - அதன் நிலை, நினைத்துக் காண்;","சினைஇய வேந்தன் எயில் புறத்து இறுத்த","வினை வரு பருவரல் போல,","துனை வரு நெஞ்சமொடு வருந்தினள் பெரிதே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore, whose sea surges like a king on the march with rows of boats for elephants, the roar of waves for drums and the wings of the shore birds for his army, that her beauty wastes alone like the learning of a pupil who grieves his teacher and will not share, and like wealth hoarded while kin grieve in a family without effort; that failing those who helped one in want will strike the doer even in his descendants, and that a man who breaks the deed he swore will not be spared, however good his sword; let him think of her state, for she suffers with a racing heart like the distress of a town when an angry king camps outside its walls.","n":"The friend argues entirely in the language of puram and ethics (the ungrateful pupil, the hoarding family, the oath-breaker, the besieged town), so the case for marriage is put as a duty of gratitude and of keeping one's sworn word (சூள்), not as a plea of love. The sea pictured as a king on the march frames the man as the conqueror besieging her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","vaaimai","varaivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 149","lb":"KL149","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["திமில்","திமில்",1,"seythi","simile","fishing boat"],["பறை","பறை",1,"parai","simile",""],["புள்","புள்",2,"pul","simile","shore birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:9"},"kl:150":{"book":"kalittokai","num":150,"t":["அயம் திகழ் நறும் கொன்றை அலங்கல் அம் தெரியலான்","இயங்கு எயில் எயப் பிறந்த எரி போல, எவ் வாயும்,","கனை கதிர் தெறுதலின், கடுத்து எழுந்த காம்புத் தீ","மலை பரந்து தலைக் கொண்டு முழங்கிய முழங்கு அழல்","மயங்கு அதர் மறுகலின், மலை தலைக்கொண்டென,","விசும்பு உற நிவந்து அழலும், விலங்கு அரும் வெம் சுரம் -","இறந்து தாம் எண்ணிய எய்துதல் வேட்கையால்,","அறம் துறந்து - ஆய் இழாய்! ஆக்கத்தில் பிரிந்தவர்;","பிறங்கு நீர் சடைக் கரந்தான் அணி அன்ன நின் நிறம்","பசந்து, நீ இனையையாய், நீத்தலும் நீப்பவோ?","கரி காய்ந்த கவலைத்தாய்க், கல் காய்ந்த காட்டு அகம்,","'வெரு வந்த ஆறு' என்னார், விழுப் பொருட்கு அகன்றவர்;","உருவ ஏற்று ஊர்தியான் ஒள் அணி நக்கன்ன, நின்","உரு இழந்து இனையையாய், உள்ளலும் உள்ளுபவோ?","கொதித்து உராய்க் குன்று இவர்ந்து, கொடிக் கொண்ட கோடையால்,","'ஒதுக்கு அரிய நெறி' என்னார், ஒண் பொருட்கு அகன்றவர்;","புதுத் திங்கள் கண்ணியான் பொன் பூண் ஞான்று அன்ன, நின்","கதுப்பு உலறும் கவினையாய் காண்டலும் காண்பவோ?","ஆங்கு","அரும் பெறல் ஆதிரையான் அணிபெற மலர்ந்த","பெரும் தண் சண்பகம் போல, ஒருங்கு அவர்","பொய்யார் ஆகுதல் தெளிந்தனம் -","மை ஈர் ஓதி மட மொழியோயே!"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that those who left her for gain, giving up virtue, to cross the hot waste where a bamboo fire, kindled by the sun like the fire born when Shiva of the konrai garland shot the moving forts, spreads roaring over the hill, confuses the paths and leaps to the sky, will not abandon her while her colour, like the ornament of the god who hid the river in his hair, turns pale; those who left for great wealth, not calling the road fearful, will remember her as her form fades; those who left for bright wealth, not calling the path impassable, will see her as her hair dries; and so, like the great champak that bloomed in full to adorn the lord of the Ardra star, they are sure he will not prove false.","n":"Every simile comes from Shiva (the burning of the forts, the river in his hair, the bull-rider's ornament, the crescent chaplet, the Ardra star), so the friend's comfort is set in a frame of worship. She concedes that he left 'giving up virtue' (அறம் துறந்து) and still holds that he will not be false, separating his breach of virtue from his keeping of his word. The colophon files it under neythal, but nothing of the shore is in it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","vaaimai","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kalittokai 150","lb":"KL150","poet":"நல்லாந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",1,"maram","ornament",""],["காம்புத்","காம்பு",3,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["சண்பகம்","சண்பகம்",21,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"kl:9"},"ku:0":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":1,"t":["தாமரை புரையுங் காமர் சேவடிப்","பவழத் தன்ன மேனித் திகழொளிக்","குன்றி யேய்க்கும் உடுக்கைக் குன்றின்","நெஞ்சுபக எறிந்த அஞ்சுடர் நெடுவேற்","சேவலங் கொடியோன் காப்ப","ஏம வைகல் எய்தின்றால் உலகே."],"p":"","r":"The invocation: the world has come into safe days because the god of the cock banner guards it, he whose red feet are like the lotus, whose body is like coral, whose cloth is like the red kunri seed, and whose long bright spear split the mountain to its heart.","n":"Murugan is never named; he is drawn wholly in red (lotus feet, coral body, kunri-red cloth) and known by his spear and banner, and the poem's one claim is that the world's safety (ஏம வைகல்) follows from his guarding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 0","lb":"KU0","poet":"பெருந்தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["கொடியோன்","சேவற்கொடியோன்",5,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"],["சேவலங்","சேவல்",5,"pul","ornament","cock"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",1,"maram","simile",""],["குன்றி","குன்றி",3,"maram","simile","kunri seed"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:1":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":2,"t":["செங்களம் படக்கொன் றவுணர்த் தேய்த்த","செங்கோ லம்பிற் செங்கோட்டி யானைக்","கழல்தொடிச் சேஎய் குன்றம்","குருதிப் பூவின் குலைக்காந் தட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the hill of the Red One, who destroyed the demons till the field ran red, whose arrows are red-shafted, whose elephant is red-tusked and who wears the anklet and the bangle, is thick with kanthal in clusters the colour of blood.","n":"Everything is red, five times over (செங்களம், செங்கோல், செங்கோட்டு, சேஎய், குருதி), and the last red is the flower; the poem does not say why the friend speaks, and the old setting (she turns away the man's gift of kanthal, since her own hill is full of it) comes only from the tradition, not the text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 1","lb":"KU1","poet":"திப்புத் தோளார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சேஎய்","சேய்",3,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"],["யானைக்","யானை",2,"maa","scene",""],["குலைக்காந் தட்டே","காந்தள்",4,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:2":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":3,"t":["கொங்குதேர் வாழ்க்கை அஞ்சிறைத் தும்பி","காமம் செப்பாது கண்டது மொழிமோ","பயிலியது கெழீஇய நட்பின் மயிலியல்","செறியெயிற் றரிவை கூந்தலின்","நறியவும் உளவோ நீயறியும் பூவே."],"p":"","r":"The man asks the bee that lives by searching out pollen to tell him what it has seen and not what it wants: of all the flowers it knows, is there one more fragrant than the hair of this woman of close-set teeth and peacock grace, whose love has held fast through long closeness?","n":"He asks the bee for an eyewitness's report and forbids it to flatter (காமம் செப்பாது கண்டது மொழிமோ), which turns praise into a test of fact, and the answer is assumed in the asking; the fragrance is joined to a love already settled by long companionship (பயிலியது கெழீஇய நட்பின்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","azhagu","kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 2","lb":"KU2","poet":"இறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["தும்பி","தும்பி",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["மயிலியல்","மயில்",3,"pul","scene",""],["பூவே","பூ",5,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:3":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":4,"t":["நிலத்தினும் பெரிதே வானினும் உயர்ந்தன்று","நீரினும் ஆரள வின்றே சாரல்","கருங்கோற் குறிஞ்சிப் பூக்கொண்டு","பெருந்தேன் இழைக்கும் நாடனொடு நட்பே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that her love for the man of the hill country, where bees gather the flowers of the black-stemmed kurinji on the slope to build great honeycombs, is larger than the earth, higher than the sky and past measuring deeper than the sea.","n":"The three measures are of land, sky and water, the whole world, but the poem closes on a small slow labour: honey built from a shrub that flowers rarely; the vast love is set beside the patient making that produced it, and she calls it நட்பு, not காமம்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","anbu","kaamam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 3","lb":"KU3","poet":"தேவகுலத்தார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["குறிஞ்சிப்","குறிஞ்சி",3,"maram","scene",""],["பெருந்தேன்","தேன்",4,"pira","scene","honeycomb"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:4":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":5,"t":["நோம்என் நெஞ்சே நோம்என் நெஞ்சே","இமைதீய்ப் பன்ன கண்ணீர் தாங்கி","அமைதற் கமைந்தநங் காதலர்","அமைவிலர் ஆகுதல் நோம்என் நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The woman, holding back tears hot enough to scorch her eyelids, tells her heart three times that it aches, because the lover who was so fitting has now become unfitting.","n":"The refrain நோம்என் நெஞ்சே is said three times and the turn rests on one verb used twice: அமைதற்கு அமைந்த (made to be fitting) and அமைவிலர் (not fitting); the tears are held back (தாங்கி), not shed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 4","lb":"KU4","poet":"காமஞ்சேர் குளத்தார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகள் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","nenju",null,null,"தலைமகள் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:5":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":6,"t":["அதுகொல் தோழி காம நோயே","வதிகுரு குறங்கும் இன்னிழற் புன்னை","உடைதிரைத் திவலை அரும்புந் தீநீர்","மெல்லம் புலம்பன் பிரிந்தெனப்","பல்லிதழ் உண்கண் பாடொல் லாவே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether this is what love-sickness is: since the gentle lord of the shore has left, where herons sleep in the sweet shade of the punnai and the spray of breaking waves buds on it, her many-petalled darkened eyes cannot sleep.","n":"She finds out she is in love from a symptom (அதுகொல்... காம நோயே); the herons that sleep in his punnai shade are the opposite of her sleepless eyes, but they only sit in the scene: they do nothing a lover does, so they are not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kan","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 5","lb":"KU5","poet":"நரிவெரூ உத்தலையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குரு குறங்கும்","குருகு",2,"pul","scene","heron"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",2,"maram","scene",""],["திவலை","திவலை",3,"pira","scene","spray"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:6":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":7,"t":["நள்ளென் றன்றே யாமம் சொல்லவிந்","தினிதடங் கினரே மாக்கள் முனிவின்று","நனந்தலை உலகமும் துஞ்சும்","ஓர்யான் மன்ற துஞ்சா தேனே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says it is the dead of night; words have ceased, people have gone sweetly to rest, the whole wide world sleeps without care, and only she, surely, does not sleep.","n":"Three clauses of the world sleeping end on one clause of her waking, with ஓர்யான் (I alone) and the emphatic மன்ற; nothing in the scene names the lover at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 6","lb":"KU6","poet":"பதுமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:7":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":8,"t":["வில்லோன் காலன கழலே தொடியோள்","மெல்லடி மேலவும் சிலம்பே நல்லோர்","யார்கொல் அளியர் தாமே ஆரியர்","கயிறாடு பறையிற் கால்பொரக் கலங்கி","வாகை வெண்ணெற் றொலிக்கும்","வேய்பயில் அழுவம் முன்னி யோரே."],"p":"","r":"Onlookers on the road see a bowman with warrior's anklets on his legs and a bangled girl with anklets still on her soft feet, and ask who these good, pitiable people are, setting out into the bamboo wilds where the white vaakai pods rattle in the wind like the drum of Aryan rope-dancers.","n":"The onlookers read the pair from their anklets alone: his கழல், and her சிலம்பு still on, which tells them she is unwed and has gone with him; their only judgement is pity (அளியர்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 7","lb":"KU7","poet":"பெரும்பதுமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"கண்டோர் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["kandor","pira",null,"kalavu","கண்டோர் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வாகை","வாகை",5,"maram","simile",""],["பறையிற்","பறை",4,"parai","simile",""],["ஆரியர்","ஆரியர்",3,"seythi","simile","rope-dancers"],["வேய்பயில்","வேய்",6,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:8":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":9,"t":["கழனி மாஅத்து விளைந்துகு தீம்பழம்","பழன வாளை கதூஉ மூரன்","எம்மிற் பெருமொழி கூறித் தம்மிற்","கையும் காலும் தூக்கத் தூக்கும்","ஆடிப் பாவை போல","மேவன செய்யுந்தன் புதல்வன் தாய்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The other woman says that the man of the town where the vaalai fish of the pond snatch the sweet ripe fruit that falls from the mango in the field talks big about her in her house, but in his own home does whatever his son's mother wants, raising hand and foot as she raises hers, like an image in a mirror.","n":"The insult is aimed at the wife's power as much as at him: he is a mirror image, moving only as she moves; the fish that takes fruit falling into its mouth is the man who takes the pleasure that comes to him without effort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","penvazhi","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 8","lb":"KU8","poet":"ஆலங்குடி வங்கனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"காதற் பரத்தை கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","pira",null,"karpu","காதற் பரத்தை கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["மாஅத்து","மா",1,"maram","ullurai","mango"],["வாளை","வாளை",2,"pira","ullurai","fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","மாஅத்து வாளை","confirmed","1/1","the husband who takes the other woman's pleasure without effort and goes home to obey his wife"]],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:9":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":10,"t":["யாயா கியளே மாஅ யோளே","மடைமாண் செப்பில் தமிய வைகிய","பெய்யாப் பூவின் மெய்சா யினளே","பாசடை நிவந்த கணைக்கால் நெய்தல்","இனமீ னிருங்கழி யோத மல்குதொறும்","கயமூழ்கு மகளிர் கண்ணின் மானும்","தண்ணந் துறைவன் கொடுமை","நம்மு னாணிக் கரப்பா டும்மே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says the dark woman has become the mother of the house: her body has wilted like flowers never worn and left in a well-made box, and out of shame before them she hides the cruelty of the lord of the cool shore, where neythal lilies on tall stems rise above green leaves and look like the eyes of girls diving in the pond each time the tide fills the backwater with fish.","n":"The wilting has two halves: the flower was never worn (பெய்யா), so it was kept and not used, and that is the wife's condition; she does not complain, she conceals (கரப்பு), and the friend's speech exposes what she hides.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["karpu","naanam","parathai","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 9","lb":"KU9","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",4,"maram","simile",""],["இனமீ","மீன்",5,"pira","scene","fish"],["பூவின்","பூ",3,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:10":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":11,"t":["யாயா கியளே விழவுமுத லாட்டி","பயறுபோ லிணர பைந்தாது படீஇயர்","உழவர் வாங்கிய கமழ்பூ மென்சினைக்","காஞ்சி யூரன் கொடுமை","கரந்தன ளாகலின் நாணிய வருமே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says the wife, first lady of the festival, has become the mother of the house: because she hid the cruelty of the man of the town of kaanji trees, whose fragrant soft branches, with clusters like green gram, the ploughmen pull down to bathe in their fresh pollen, she comes ashamed.","n":"It opens with the same words as poem 9 (யாயாகியளே) on the same act of concealment; here the shame is the cost of hiding: she leads the festival and comes to it ashamed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["karpu","naanam","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 10","lb":"KU10","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காஞ்சி","காஞ்சி",4,"maram","ullurai",""],["உழவர்","உழவர்",3,"seythi","ullurai",""],["பயறுபோ","பயறு",2,"unavu","simile","green gram"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","காஞ்சி உழவர்","confirmed","1/1","the husband who lets other women draw him down and take their pleasure of him"]],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:11":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":12,"t":["கோடீர் இலங்குவளை ஞெகிழ நாடொறும்","பாடில கலிழும் கண்ணொடு புலம்பி","ஈங்கிவண் உறைதலும் உய்குவம் ஆங்கே","எழுவினி வாழியென் னெஞ்சே முனாஅது","குல்லைக் கண்ணி வடுகர் முனையது","வல்வேற் கட்டி நன்னாட் டும்பர்","மொழிபெயர் தேஎத்த ராயினும்","வழிபடல் சூழ்ந்திசின் அவருடை நாட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her heart that rather than stay here crying, her conch bangles slipping day by day, it should rise and go: though he is in a land of strange speech beyond the country of Katti of the strong spear, past the frontier of the Vadugar who wear kullai garlands, let them set out to follow him to his land.","n":"She does not ask him to return; she proposes to go herself, across a named frontier of foreign speech (மொழிபெயர் தேஎம்), and she frames staying as what cannot be survived.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","melivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 11","lb":"KU11","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","nenju",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["இலங்குவளை","வளை",1,"pira","ornament","conch bangle"],["குல்லைக்","குல்லை",5,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[["king","கட்டி",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:12":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":13,"t":["எறும்பி யளையிற் குறும்பல் சுனைய","உலைக்கல் அன்ன பாறை ஏறிக்","கொடுவில் எயினர் பகழி மாய்க்கும்","கவலைத் தென்பவர் தேர் சென்ற ஆறே","அதுமற் றவலங் கொள்ளாது","நொதுமற் கழறுமிவ் வழுங்க லூரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that the road his chariot took is said to be a forking track of tiny pools like ant-holes, where hunters with curved bows climb rocks hot as an anvil-stone to sharpen their arrows; yet this clamouring town, without grieving over that, scolds her like a stranger.","n":"Her fear is learned by hearsay (என்ப) and the town's blame is direct; she sets what she fears for him against what the town says of her, and finds the town has no pity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","pirivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 12","lb":"KU12","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["எறும்பி","எறும்பி",1,"pira","simile","ant"],["எயினர்","எயினர்",3,"seythi","scene","hunters"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:13":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":14,"t":["மாசறக் கழீஇய யானை போலப்","பெரும்பெயல் உழந்த இரும்பிணர்த் துறுகற்","பைதல் ஒருதலைச் சேக்கும் நாடன்","நோய்தந் தனனே தோழி","பசலை ஆர்ந்தநங் குவளையங் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the man of the country where a great dark rough boulder, beaten by heavy rain until it looks like an elephant washed clean, rests on one side of the slope, has given her this sickness, and pallor has filled her lily eyes.","n":"The boulder washed clean and at rest stands beside her eyes filled with pallor; the image belongs to his country, not to her, and the sense of பைதல் (sad, or cool and wet) decides whether the rock shares her grief.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kaamanoy","kan","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 13","lb":"KU13","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","simile",""],["துறுகற்","துறுகல்",2,"pira","scene","boulder"],["குவளையங்","குவளை",5,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:14":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":15,"t":["அமிழ்துபொதி செந்நா அஞ்ச வந்த","வார்ந்திலங்கு வையெயிற்றுச் சின்மொழி அரிவையைப்","பெறுகதில் அம்ம யானே பெற்றாங்","கறிகதில் அம்மவிவ் வூரே மறுகில்","நல்லோள் கணவன் இவனெனப்","பல்லோர் கூறயாம் நாணுகஞ் சிறிதே."],"p":"","r":"The man wishes he may win the girl of few words, whose long bright white teeth came so that her nectar-holding red tongue is shy behind them; and having won her, may this town know it, may many in the street say 'there goes the good woman's husband', and may he be a little ashamed.","n":"He wants the public naming (நல்லோள் கணவன் இவன்) and the small shame that comes with it (நாணுகஞ் சிறிதே); the tradition hears a madal threat in 'let the town know', but the poem's words are a wish for a public bond.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","alar","naanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 14","lb":"KU14","poet":"தொல்கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:15":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":16,"t":["பறைபடப் பணிலம் ஆர்ப்ப இறைகொள்பு","தொன்மூ தாலத்துப் பொதியிற் றோன்றிய","நாலூர்க் கோசர் நன்மொழி போல","வாயா கின்றே தோழி ஆய்கழற்","சேயிலை வெள்வேல் விடலையொடு","தொகுவளை முன்கை மடந்தை நட்பே."],"p":"","r":"The foster mother tells a friend that the love between the youth with fine anklets and red-leafed white spear and the girl with stacked bangles on her forearm has proved true, as true as the good word of the Kosar of Nalur spoken at the common hall under the ancient banyan, with drums sounding and conches blaring.","n":"The love's truth is measured by a public oath: the Kosar's word given in assembly with drums and conches; the foster mother vindicates the elopement by a standard of sworn faith, not of feeling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","natpu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 15","lb":"KU15","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செவிலி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","thozhi",null,"kalavu","செவிலி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பறைபடப்","பறை",1,"parai","simile",""],["பணிலம்","பணிலம்",1,"pira","simile","conch"],["தாலத்துப்","ஆலம்",2,"maram","simile","banyan"],["தொகுவளை","வளை",6,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[["town","நாலூர்க்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:16":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":17,"t":["உள்ளார் கொல்லோ தோழி கள்வர்","பொன்புனை பகழி செப்பங் கொண்மார்","உகிர்நுதி புரட்டும் ஓசை போலச்","செங்காற் பல்லி தன்றுணை பயிரும்","அங்காற் கள்ளியங் காடிறந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether they will not think of us, the men who crossed the forest of kalli, where the red-legged lizard calls to its mate with a sound like thieves flicking with their nail-tips the gold-bound arrows to test them straight.","n":"The simile brings robbers into the very sound of the forest, so the comfort is shadowed by danger; the question form (உள்ளார் கொல்லோ) expects the answer yes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 16","lb":"KU16","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["பல்லி","பல்லி",4,"pira","iraicchi","lizard"],["கள்ளியங்","கள்ளி",5,"maram","scene",""],["கள்வர்","கள்வர்",1,"seythi","simile","robbers"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பல்லி","confirmed","1/1","the man who passes and hears even the lizard call its mate will think of his own and turn home"]],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:17":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":18,"t":["மாவென மடலும் ஊர்ப பூவெனக்","குவிமுகிழ் எருக்கங் கண்ணியும் சூடுப","மறுகி னார்க்கவும் படுப","பிறிது மாகுப காமங்காழ் கொளினே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that when love hardens like heartwood, men ride the palm-stem calling it a horse, wear a garland of erukku buds calling it a flower, become a spectacle in the street, and become something else besides.","n":"He speaks in the third person plural, as a general rule of what love does, and ends vaguely (பிறிதும் ஆகுப), which is the threat: the next step is beyond naming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamam","naanam","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 17","lb":"KU17","poet":"பேரெயின் முறுவலார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["மடலும்","மடல்",1,"maram","scene","palm stem"],["எருக்கங்","எருக்கு",2,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:18":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":19,"t":["வேரல் வேலி வேர்க்கோட் பலவின்","சாரல் நாட செவ்வியை ஆகுமதி","யாரஃ தறிந்திசி னோரே சாரல்","சிறுகோட்டுப் பெரும்பழந் தூங்கி யாங்கிவள்","உயிர்தவச் சிறிது காமமோ பெரிதே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the slope where jackfruit, fenced with bamboo, bears even on its roots, to be timely; who knows how it stands? like a huge fruit hanging from a small stalk on the slope, her life is very small and her love very great.","n":"The same tree serves twice: fruit low on the root, within reach and fenced (the girl ready and guarded), then fruit too heavy for its stalk; the first image is a near miss for ullurai, since the poem itself draws the second as an open simile.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","uyir","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 18","lb":"KU18","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","மென்மை"]],"k":[["வேரல்","வேரல்",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பலவின்","பலா",1,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["பெரும்பழந்","பழம்",4,"maram","simile","jackfruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:19":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":20,"t":["எவ்வி இழந்த வறுமையாழ்ப் பாணர்","பூவில் வறுந்தலை போலப் புல்லென்","றினைமதி வாழியர் நெஞ்சே மனைமரத்","தெல்லுறு மௌவல் நாறும்","பல்லிருங் கூந்தல் யாரளோ நமக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart to grieve, dull and bare like the flowerless heads of the bards made poor by the loss of Evvi: who is she to us now, the woman of thick dark hair fragrant as the jasmine that blooms by day on the house-tree?","n":"The simile borrows a public mourning, bards who stopped wearing flowers when their patron died, for a private estrangement; and the question யாரளோ நமக்கே disowns her while the line before it lingers on her hair.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","oodal","kaiyarunilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 19","lb":"KU19","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பாணர்","பாணர்",1,"seythi","simile",""],["வறுமையாழ்ப்","யாழ்",1,"yaazh","simile",""],["பூவில்","பூ",2,"maram","absent",""],["மௌவல்","மௌவல்",4,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[["patron","எவ்வி",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:20":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":21,"t":["அருளும் அன்பும் நீக்கித் துணைதுறந்து","பொருள்வயிற் பிரிவோர் உரவோர் ஆயின்","உரவோர் உரவோர் ஆக","மடவம் ஆக மடந்தை நாமே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that if those who put aside grace and love and leave their companion to go after wealth are the strong and wise, then let them be the wise, and let us be the fools.","n":"The word உரவோர் is said three times until it turns to mockery; she concedes the name of wisdom to the leavers and claims folly (மடவம்) for the ones who stay, a refusal phrased as agreement.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","arul","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 20","lb":"KU20","poet":"கோப்பெருஞ்சோழன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:1"},"ku:21":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":22,"t":["வண்டுபடத் ததைந்த கொடியிணர் இடையிடுபு","பொன்செய் புனையிழை கட்டிய மகளிர்","கதுப்பிற் றோன்றும் புதுப்பூங் கொன்றை","கானங் காரெனக் கூறினும்","யானோ தேறேன் அவர் பொய்வழங் கலரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that even if the forest, where the new konrai flowers hang among their bee-thronged clusters like gold ornaments in women's hair, declares that the rains have come, she will not believe it, because he does not deal in lies.","n":"She turns the season's sign against itself: if he has not come, this cannot be the rains he named, for he keeps his word. The forest is made to 'say' (கூறினும) like a witness she refuses, so her faith in him outweighs the evidence of the land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","vaaimai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 21","lb":"KU21","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வண்டு","வண்டு",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",3,"maram","simile","konrai"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:22":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":23,"t":["நீர்வார் கண்ணை நீயிவண் ஒழிய","யாரோ பிரிகிற் பவரே சாரற்","சிலம்பணி கொண்ட வலஞ்சுரி மராஅத்து","வேனில் அஞ்சினை கமழும்","தேமூர் ஒண்ணுதல் நின்னொடுஞ் செலவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the weeping woman that no one would part from her and leave her here with streaming eyes: his journey will be made with her, she whose bright brow is fragrant as the spring branches of the whorled maraa that adorns the hill slope.","n":"'நின்னொடுஞ் செலவே' can mean that he will take her with him or that he cannot go at all without her; either way the friend answers the fear of parting by denying that it can happen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","udanpokku","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 22","lb":"KU22","poet":"சேரமானெந்தை","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மராஅத்து","மராம்",3,"maram","scene","kadamba"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:23":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":24,"t":["அகவன் மகளே அகவன் மகளே","மனவுக்கோப் பன்ன நன்னெடுங் கூந்தல்","அகவன் மகளே பாடுக பாட்டே","இன்னும் பாடுக பாட்டேஅவர்","நன்னெடுங் குன்றம் பாடிய பாட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The friend calls on the diviner woman, whose long fine hair is like a strung row of shell beads, to sing her song, and sing it again: the song she sang of his tall good hill.","n":"By asking for the song about his hill, the friend lets the rite itself disclose whom the girl pines for, without naming him before the household. The triple call அகவன் மகளே and the triple பாட்டே copy the chant of the rite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["agavanmagal","kalaviyal","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 23","lb":"KU23","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அகவன் மகளே","அகவன் மகள்",1,"seythi","scene","diviner woman"],["மனவுக்கோப்","மனவு",2,"pira","simile","shell beads"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:24":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":25,"t":["கருங்கால் வேம்பின் ஒண்பூ யாணர்","என்னை யின்றியுங் கழிவது கொல்லோ","ஆற்றயல் எழுந்த வெண்கோட் டதவத்","தெழுகுளிறு மிதித்த ஒருபழம் போலக்","குழையக் கொடியோர் நாவே","காதலர் அகலக் கல்லென் றவ்வே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks whether the new bounty of the dark-trunked neem's bright flowers will pass without her, and says that since her lover went away the tongues of the cruel have clamoured until she is crushed like a single fig fruit trodden by an elephant beside the river.","n":"The neem's flowering marks a season meant to be shared; the gossip, not the absence alone, is what crushes her, and one fallen fruit under a great beast measures how small she is against the town's tongues.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 24","lb":"KU24","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",1,"maram","scene","neem"],["வெண்கோட் டதவத்","அதவம்",3,"maram","simile","fig tree"],["குளிறு","களிறு",4,"maa","simile","elephant"],["பழம்","பழம்",4,"unavu","simile","fruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:25":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":26,"t":["யாரும் இல்லைத் தானே கள்வன்","தானது பொய்ப்பின் யானெவன் செய்கோ","தினைத்தாள் அன்ன சிறுபசுங் கால","ஒழுகுநீர் ஆரல் பார்க்கும்","குருகும் உண்டுதான் மணந்த ஞான்றே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that no one was there but the thief himself, and if he goes back on his word she can do nothing; only a heron was there, on thin green legs like millet stalks, watching the running water for aaral fish, on the day he took her.","n":"The one witness of the union is a heron whose eyes were on the fish, a witness that saw nothing; கள்வன் names the secret lover as a thief who left no proof.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","vaaimai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 25","lb":"KU25","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["தினைத்தாள்","தினைத் தாள்",3,"maram","simile","millet stalk"],["ஆரல்","ஆரல்",4,"pira","scene","eel fish"],["குருகும்","குருகு",5,"pul","simile","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:26":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":27,"t":["அரும்பற மலர்ந்த கருங்கால் வேங்கை","மேக்கெழு பெருஞ்சினை இருந்த தோகை","பூக்கொய் மகளிரிற் றோன்று நாடன்","தகாஅன் போலத் தான்றீது மொழியினும்","தன்கண் கண்டது பொய்க்குவ தன்றே","தேக்கொக் கருந்து முள்ளெயிற்றுத் துவர்வாய்","வரையாடு வன்பறழ்த் தந்தைக்","கடுவனும் அறியும்அக் கொடியோ னையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that though the man of the land where peacocks perched on the high branches of the fully bloomed dark-trunked vengai look like women plucking flowers should speak falsely, like one unworthy, what one has seen with one's own eyes does not lie; even the male monkey of the hill, sire of a sturdy young one, sharp-toothed and red-mouthed, eating sweet mango, knows that cruel man.","n":"Against the lover's possible denial the friend calls the hill's own creature as witness. Who 'தான்' and 'தன்கண்' refer to is not fixed (the lover, or a diviner who speaks wrongly of the girl's illness), so the occasion is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 26","lb":"KU26","poet":"கொல்லனழிசி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene","vengai"],["தோகை","தோகை",2,"pul","simile","peacock"],["தேக்கொக்","தேமா",6,"maram","gift_food","sweet mango"],["பறழ்த்","பறழ்",7,"maa","scene","young monkey"],["கடுவனும்","கடுவன்",8,"maa","scene","male monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:27":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":28,"t":["கன்று முண்ணாது கலத்தினும் படாது","நல்லான் தீம்பால் நிலத்துக் காஅங்","கெனக்கு மாகா தென்னைக்கு முதவாது","பசலை உணீஇயர் வேண்டும்","திதலை அல்குலென் மாமைக் கவினே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that her beauty, the dark glow of her mottled waist, of use neither to herself nor to her man, is left for pallor to eat, like a good cow's sweet milk that the calf does not drink and the pail does not catch, spilling on the ground.","n":"The simile makes beauty a yield owed to someone; unshared, it is not only lost but wasted, and உணீஇயர் gives the pallor an eater's appetite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","nalam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 27","lb":"KU27","poet":"வெள்ளிவீதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கன்று","கன்று",1,"maa","simile","calf"],["நல்லான்","ஆன்",2,"maa","simile","cow"],["தீம்பால்","பால்",2,"unavu","simile","milk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:28":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":29,"t":["மூட்டு வேன்கொல் தாக்கு வேன்கொல்","ஓரேன் யானுமோர் பெற்றி மேலிட்டு","ஆஅ ஒல்லெனக் கூவு வேன்கொல்","அலமரல் அசைவளி அலைப்பவென்","உயவுநோ யறியாது துஞ்சும் ஊர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks whether she should butt them, or strike them, or, not knowing what to do, find some pretext and cry out 'aah, help' to the town that sleeps on, unaware of the grief that torments her while the restless wind tosses her about.","n":"Her rage is at the sleeping town, not at the man: the whole community rests while she alone keeps watch with her pain, and the wind is the only thing awake with her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 28","lb":"KU28","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["vekuli","அலை"]],"k":[["அசைவளி","வளி",4,"pira","scene","wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:29":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":30,"t":["நல்லுரை யிகந்து புல்லுரை தாஅய்ப்","பெயல்நீர்க் கேற்ற பசுங்கலம் போல","உள்ளம் தாங்கா வெள்ளம் நீந்தி","அரிதவா உற்றனை நெஞ்சே நன்றும்","பெரிதால் அம்மநின் பூசல் உயர்கோட்டு","மகவுடை மந்தி போல","அகனுறத் தழீஇக் கேட்குநர்ப் பெறினே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart that, passing over good counsel and spreading worthless talk, it has plunged like a fresh unfired pot set out in the rain into a flood the mind cannot hold, and is in hard straits; its clamour would be very great indeed, if it found anyone to hold it close and listen, as a mother monkey on a high branch holds her young.","n":"The unfired pot dissolves in the very water it takes in: the heart is unmade by the desire it receives. The second simile is a wish that sharpens the loneliness: no one will hold and hear him as the monkey holds her child.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 29","lb":"KU29","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["மந்தி","மந்தி",6,"maa","simile","female monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:30":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":31,"t":["கேட்டிசின் வாழி தோழி அல்கற்","பொய்வ லாளன் மெய்யுறல் மரீஇய","வாய்த்தகைப் பொய்க்கனா மருட்ட ஏற்றெழுந்து","அமளி தைவந் தனனே குவளை","வண்டுபடு மலரிற் சாஅய்த்","தமியேன் மன்ற அளியேன் யானே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend to listen: last night a lying dream that seemed true, of the embrace of the man so skilled in lies, deceived her, and she rose and felt over the bed; now, wasted like a kuvalai flower that the bees have visited, she is alone and truly to be pitied.","n":"The man is called a master of lies and the dream a lie that looked true, so the dream repeats his falseness; waking is the moment of loss, her hand finding the empty bed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","pirivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 30","lb":"KU30","poet":"கச்சிப்பேட்டு நன்னாகையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குவளை","குவளை",4,"maram","simile","blue water lily"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",5,"pira","simile","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:31":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":32,"t":["மள்ளர் குழீஇய விழவி னானும்","மகளிர் தழீஇய துணங்கை யானும்","யாண்டுங் காணேன் மாண்தக் கோனை","யானுமோர் ஆடுகள மகளே என்கைக்","கோடீ ரிலங்குவளை நெகிழ்த்த","பீடுகெழு குரிசிலுமோர் ஆடுகள மகனே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that neither at the festival where warriors gather nor in the thunangai dance where women hold one another can she find the noble one anywhere; she too is a woman of the dancing ground, and the proud chief who loosened her bright conch bangles is a man of the dancing ground as well.","n":"The poet is Aathimanthiyaar, and the lost man of the dancing ground is the dancer whom other poems name; she searches the festival grounds because they are his own place, and the two parallel lines (ஆடுகள மகளே, ஆடுகள மகனே) claim an equal bond.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 31","lb":"KU31","poet":"ஆதிமந்தியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மள்ளர்","மள்ளர்",1,"seythi","scene","warriors"],["துணங்கை","துணங்கை",2,"yaazh","scene","dance"],["கோடீ","கோடு",5,"pira","ornament","conch"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:32":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":33,"t":["காலையும் பகலும் கையறு மாலையும்","ஊர்துஞ் சியாமமும் விடியலு மென்றிப்","பொழுதிடை தெரியிற் பொய்யே காமம்","மாவென மடலோடு மறுகில் தோன்றித்","தெற்றெனத் தூற்றலும் பழியே","வாழ்தலும் பழியே பிரிவுதலை வரினே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that if one can tell the hours apart, morning, day, helpless evening, the midnight when the town sleeps, and dawn, then love is a lie; to appear in the street on the palmyra-stem horse and proclaim it openly is shame, and to go on living is shame too, once parting comes.","n":"The two shames are made equal: riding the madal disgraces him, but living apart from her is no less a disgrace, so the threat is the lesser of two dishonours. Tholkappiyam files madal under perunthinai; here it is threatened, not yet done, and the poem stays in kurinji.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamam","pazhi","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 32","lb":"KU32","poet":"அள்ளூர் நன்முல்லையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாவென","மா",4,"maa","simile","horse"],["மடலோடு","மடல்",4,"maram","simile","palmyra stem"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:33":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":34,"t":["அன்னாய் இவனோர் இளமா ணாக்கன்","தன்னூர் மன்றத் தென்னன் கொல்லோ","இரந்தூ ணிரம்பா மேனியொடு","விருந்தின் ஊரும் பெருஞ்செம் மலனே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says to her companion that this young student, his body never filled by the food he begs, who comes into town as a guest, such a great noble fellow, must be someone of standing at the common ground of his own town.","n":"The praise is mockery: the go-between who comes begging as a guest is called a great man, so the heroine shuts him out with laughter instead of anger. Whose messenger he is, and whether அன்னாய் addresses the friend or the mother, the poem does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","oodal","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 33","lb":"KU33","poet":"படுமரத்து மோசிகீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["இரந்தூ","இரந்து ஊண்",3,"unavu","gift_food","begged food"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:34":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":35,"t":["ஒறுப்ப வோவலர் மறுப்பத் தேறலர்","தமியர் உறங்கும் கௌவை யின்றாய்","இனியது கேட்டின் புறுகவிவ் வூரே","முனாஅ தியானையங் குருகின் கானலம் பெருந்தோடு","அட்ட மள்ளர் ஆர்ப்பிசை வெரூஉம்","குட்டுவன் மரந்தை யன்னவெம்","குழைவிளங் காய்நுதற் கிழவனு மவனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says: let this town, which would not stop when we rebuked it nor believe when we denied, now that its gossip that we sleep alone is over, hear the sweet news and rejoice: the lord of our girl, whose bright brow with its shining ear-rings is lovely as Kuttuvan's Maranthai, where the great flock of herons in the seaside grove takes fright at the roar of the warriors, is that very man.","n":"The gossip ends by coming true: the man the town whispered about is now her lord (கிழவனும் அவனே), so the friend invites the town to the very news it guessed. The brow is measured by the beauty of a Chera port town.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","karpu","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 34","lb":"KU34","poet":"கொல்லிக் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","ur",null,null,"தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",4,"pul","simile","heron"],["மள்ளர்","மள்ளர்",5,"seythi","simile","warriors"]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவன்",6],["town","மரந்தை",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:35":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":36,"t":["நாணில மன்றவெங் கண்ணே நாணேர்பு","சினைப்பசும் பாம்பின் சூன்முதிர்ப் பன்ன","கனைத்த கரும்பின் கூம்புபொதி யவிழ","நுண்ணுறை யழிதுளி தலைஇய","தண்வரல் வாடையும் பிரிந்திசினோர்க் கழலே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says her eyes are shameless indeed; and the cold north wind, coming with fine drizzle that opens the folded bud of the dense sugarcane, swollen like the belly of a green snake heavy with young on a branch, is fire to those who have been parted.","n":"The cane's flower opening in the drizzle is a sign of fruitfulness that burns the parted woman; her shameless eyes weep openly and betray her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","naanam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 35","lb":"KU35","poet":"கழார்க்கீரனெயிற்றி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",2,"pira","simile","snake"],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",3,"maram","simile","sugarcane"],["வாடையும்","வாடை",5,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:36":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":37,"t":["துறுக லயலது மாணை மாக்கொடி","துஞ்சுகளி றிவரும் குன்ற நாடன்","நெஞ்சுகள னாக நீயலென் யானென","நற்றோள் மணந்த ஞான்றை மற்றவன்","தாவா வஞ்சின முரைத்தது","நோயோ தோழி நின்வயி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the man of the hill land, where the dark maanai creeper climbs an elephant asleep beside a boulder, swore an unfailing vow, 'with my heart as witness I will not leave you', on the day he took her shoulders; is the grief then yours, friend?","n":"The creeper that climbs the sleeping elephant as though it were the boulder beside it is a near miss for ullurai: one correspondence (a clinging thing on a body unaware of it) but no second pair, so it stays scene. His vow is called வஞ்சினம், the warrior's word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjinam","punarchi","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 36","lb":"KU36","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாணை","மாணை",1,"maram","scene","maanai creeper"],["துஞ்சுகளி","களிறு",2,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:37":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":38,"t":["நசைபெரி துடையர் நல்கலு நல்குவர்","பிடிபசி களைஇய பெருங்கை வேழம்","மென்சினை யாஅம் பொளிக்கும்","அன்பின தோழியவர் சென்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that he has great desire for her and will surely be gracious; the road he took is a loving one, where the big-trunked tusker strips the soft branches of the yaa tree to ease the hunger of his mate.","n":"The friend calls the road itself 'loving' (அன்பின) because of what is done on it: the hero cannot pass such care without being reminded of his own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 37","lb":"KU37","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பிடி","பிடி",2,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",2,"maa","iraicchi","tusker"],["யாஅம்","யா",3,"maram","scene","yaa tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வேழம்","confirmed","1/1","the man who sees such care on his road will remember his own wife and return to cherish her"]],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:38":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":39,"t":["கான மஞ்ஞை யறையீன் முட்டை","வெயிலாடு முசுவின் குருளை உருட்டும்","குன்ற நாடன் கேண்மை என்றும்","நன்றுமன் வாழி தோழி உண்கண்","நீரொ டொராங்குத் தணப்ப","உள்ளா தாற்றல் வல்லு வோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says, bitterly, that the friendship of the hill man, in whose land the young of the sun-basking langur roll the eggs the forest peahen laid on a rock, is good indeed for ever, friend, for those who can bear it without thinking of him while their kohl-lined eyes are spent with tears.","n":"The praise is irony: his bond is good only for one who can forget him. The langur's young rolling the peahen's eggs on bare rock is a near miss for ullurai (one correspondence, careless play with a fragile thing left exposed, but no second pair), so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","ninaivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 38","lb":"KU38","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",1,"pul","scene","peahen"],["முட்டை","முட்டை",1,"pira","scene","eggs"],["முசுவின்","முசு",2,"maa","scene","langur"],["குருளை","குருளை",2,"maa","scene","young"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:39":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":40,"t":["வெந்திறற் கடுவளி பொங்கர்ப் போந்தென","நெற்றுவிளை உழிஞ்சில் வற்றல் ஆர்க்கும்","மலையுடை அருஞ்சுரம் என்பநம்","முலையிடை முனிநர் சென்ற ஆறே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says they tell her that the road taken by him who tired of lying between her breasts is the hard mountain waste where, when the fierce strong wind sweeps through the grove, the dried ripe pods of the uzhinjil rattle.","n":"'என்ப', they say: she knows the road only by report, and the rattling pods are the sound she imagines on it. முனிநர், the one who tired of her, makes his leaving a rejection, not only a journey.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 39","lb":"KU39","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கடுவளி","வளி",1,"pira","scene","wind"],["உழிஞ்சில்","உழிஞ்சில்",2,"maram","scene","uzhinjil tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:40":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":41,"t":["யாயும் ஞாயும் யாரா கியரோ","எந்தையும் நுந்தையும் எம்முறைக் கேளிர்","யானும் நீயும் எவ்வழி யறிதும்","செம்புலப் பெயனீர் போல","அன்புடை நெஞ்சம் தாங்கலந் தனவே."],"p":"","r":"The man asks what his mother and hers were to each other, how his father and hers are kin, and how he and she ever came to know each other; yet like rain falling on red earth, their loving hearts have mingled into one.","n":"The three questions strip away every tie of kin and acquaintance before the simile, so the union owes nothing to family; rain on red soil takes its colour and cannot be separated out again. The poet is known only by this image (செம்புலப் பெயனீரார்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","punarchi","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 40","lb":"KU40","poet":"செம்புலப் பெயனீரார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பெயனீர்","பெயல் நீர்",4,"pira","simile","rain water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:2"},"ku:41":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":42,"t":["காதலர் உழைய ராகப் பெரிதுவந்து","சாறுகொ ளூரிற் புகல்வேன் மன்ற","அத்த நண்ணிய அங்குடிச் சீறூர்","மக்கள் போகிய அணிலாடு முன்றிற்","புலம்பில் போலப் புல்லென்று","அலப்பென் தோழியவர் அகன்ற ஞான்றே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that while her lover is near she delights like a town holding its festival, but since he went away she is desolate and dull, like an empty house in a little hamlet by the wasteland whose people have left and in whose yard squirrels play.","n":"Both similes are places, a crowded festival town and an emptied yard; the squirrels playing freely in the yard are the sign that no one is left, so her emptiness is measured by what now moves in it. The poet is named after line 4.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 41","lb":"KU41","poet":"அணிலாடு முன்றிலார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["அணிலாடு","அணில்",4,"maa","simile","squirrel"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:42":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":43,"t":["காமம் ஒழிவ தாயினும் யாமத்துக்","கருவி மாமழை வீழ்ந்தென அருவி","விடரகத் தியம்பு நாடவெம்","தொடர்புந் தேயுமோ நின்வயி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man of the hill land, where after the cloud-massed rain falls at midnight the waterfall goes on sounding in the clefts, whether, even if desire should end, our bond with him could ever wane.","n":"The friend grants that kaamam may end and asks only whether the bond will; தேயுமோ, 'will it wane', is the moon's verb. The image sits in the address to him, where the hill lord's own land answers her question before he can.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","natpu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 42","lb":"KU42","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,"தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாமழை","மா மழை",2,"pira","ullurai","great rain"],["அருவி","அருவி",2,"pira","ullurai","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","அருவி மாமழை","confirmed","1/1","desire and the bond that should outlast it"]],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:43":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":44,"t":["செல்வார் அல்லரென் றியானிகழ்ந் தனனே","ஒல்வாள் அல்லளென் றவரிகழ்ந் தனரே","ஆயிடை, இருபே ராண்மை செய்த பூசல்","நல்லராக் கதுவி யாங்கென்","அல்லல் நெஞ்சம் அலமலக் குறுமே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says she made light of it, thinking he would not go, and he made light of it, thinking she would not bear it; caught in the clash their two great prides made, her troubled heart writhes as if a cobra had seized it.","n":"Avvaiyar gives the same verb to both (இகழ்ந்தனனே, இகழ்ந்தனரே) and calls the silence of both ஆண்மை, a word of manly pride, used here of the woman too: each was sure of the other, neither spoke, and the parting happened between them. The poem does not say what the snake seizes; the moon in eclipse is one reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aanmai","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 43","lb":"KU43","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நல்லராக்","நல் அரா",4,"pira","simile","cobra"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:44":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":45,"t":["காலே பரிதப் பினவே கண்ணே","நோக்கி நோக்கி வாளிழந் தனவே","அகலிரு விசும்பின் மீனினும்","பலரே மன்றவிவ் வுலகத்துப் பிறரே."],"p":"","r":"The foster mother, following the girl who has gone off with her lover, says her legs are worn out and her eyes have lost their light from looking and looking, and that the others in this world, none of them her girl, are surely more than the stars of the wide dark sky.","n":"The poem never says whom she seeks; the speaker and the eyes that have lost their light from looking make it the search. பிறரே, 'others', is the last word: everyone she has looked at is someone else.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 44","lb":"KU44","poet":"வெள்ளிவீதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செவிலித்தாய் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,null,"செவிலித்தாய் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:45":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":46,"t":["காலை யெழுந்து கடுந்தேர் பண்ணி","வாலிழை மகளிர்த் தழீஇய சென்ற","மல்ல லூரன் எல்லினன் பெரிதென","மறுவருஞ் சிறுவன் தாயே","தெறுவ தம்மவித் திணைப்பிறத் தல்லே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the lord of the rich town rose at dawn, yoked his swift chariot and went to embrace women of bright jewels, and when he stays out long the mother of his little son grieves; how searing it is, she says, to be born into this station.","n":"திணை here is birth or station, not landscape: what burns is being born to a family whose wife must keep the house while the man goes to other women. The friend names the wife only as the mother of the little son, the title that binds her to stay. எல்லினன் is taken as 'he is late'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 45","lb":"KU45","poet":"ஆலங்குடி வங்கனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,"தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:46":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":47,"t":["ஆம்பற் பூவின் சாம்ப லன்ன","கூம்பிய சிறகர் மனையுறை குரீஇ","முன்றில் உணங்கல் மாந்தி மன்றத்து","எருவினுண் தாது குடைவன ஆடி","இல்லிறைப் பள்ளித்தம் பிள்ளையொடு வதியும்","புன்கண் மாலையும் புலம்பும்","இன்றுகொல் தோழியவர் சென்ற நாட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether, in the land where he has gone, there is no grieving, lonely evening like this one, when the house sparrows with folded wings the colour of water-lily ash, having eaten the grain drying in the yard and bathed in the fine dust of the dung in the common ground, settle with their young in the nest under the eaves.","n":"The colophon's thinai is marutham, the land of the village yard the sparrows live in; the situation, a man gone to another land and a woman alone at evening, is paalai's. The question is whether evening exists where he is: if it did, he would feel what she feels and come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 46","lb":"KU46","poet":"மாமலாடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஆம்பற்","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","simile","water-lily"],["குரீஇ","குரீஇ",2,"pul","iraicchi","house sparrow"],["உணங்கல்","உணங்கல்",3,"unavu","gift_food","drying grain"],["எருவினுண்","எரு",4,"pira","scene","dung dust"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","குரீஇ","confirmed","1/1","even the house sparrow is home with its young at nightfall, while he stays away in another land"]],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:47":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":48,"t":["கருங்கால் வேங்கை வீயுகு துறுகல்","இரும்புலிக் குருளையின் தோன்றுங் காட்டிடை","எல்லி வருநர் களவிற்கு","நல்லை யல்லை நெடுவெண் ணிலவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the long white moon that, in the forest where the boulder strewn with the falling blossoms of the black-trunked vengai looks like a great tiger's cub, it does no good to those who come at night for secret love.","n":"The moon does two harms at once: its light shows the lovers to watchers, and it makes the rock under the vengai look like a tiger, so the night path is both seen and fearful. The man is not addressed; he is meant to understand that the night meetings cannot go on. The poet is named after line 4.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 47","lb":"KU47","poet":"நெடுவெண்ணிலவினார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,"தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","simile","vengai tree"],["இரும்புலிக்","புலி",2,"maa","simile","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:48":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":49,"t":["தாதிற் செய்த தண்பனிப் பாவை","காலை வருந்துங் கையா றோம்பென","ஓரை யாயங் கூறக் கேட்டும்","இன்ன பண்பி னினைபெரி துழக்கும்","நன்னுதல் பசலை நீங்க வன்ன","நசையாகு பண்பின் ஒருசொல்","இசையாது கொல்லோ காதலர் தமக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether the lover will not send even one word of the kind she longs for, to clear the pallor from her fair forehead, though she suffers greatly in this way even after hearing her playmates at the oorai game tell her to beware the grief of the morning, when the cool dew-wet doll they made of pollen will wilt.","n":"The playmates' warning about the pollen doll that wilts by morning reads as said of her: her beauty is as perishable as the toy. இன்ன பண்பின், 'of such a nature', joins the two, but the construction of lines 1 to 4 is not certain. One kind word is all the friend asks of him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 48","lb":"KU48","poet":"பூங்கணுத்திரையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,"தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தாதிற்","தாது",1,"maram","scene","pollen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:49":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":50,"t":["அணிற்பல் அன்ன கொங்குமுதிர் முண்டகத்து","மணிக்கேழ் அன்ன மாநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப","இம்மை மாறி மறுமை யாயினும்","நீயா கியரென் கணவனை","யானா கியர்நின் னெஞ்சுநேர் பவளே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the lord of the shore of dark sapphire waters, where the water-thorn's blossoms with ripe pollen are like a squirrel's teeth, that even when this life turns into the next, may he be her husband and may she be the one close to his heart.","n":"The vow stretches the marriage past this life and asks for two things, not one: that he be her husband, and that she be the one his heart agrees with (நெஞ்சு நேர்பவள்). The second wish admits a doubt the first does not.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","karpu","vaazhkkaithunai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 49","lb":"KU49","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அணிற்பல்","அணில் பல்",1,"maa","simile","squirrel's teeth"],["முண்டகத்து","முண்டகம்",1,"maram","simile","water-thorn"],["மாநீர்ச்","மா நீர்",2,"pira","simile","sea water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:50":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":51,"t":["ஐயவி அன்ன சிறுவீ ஞாழல்","செவ்வி மருதின் செம்மலொடு தாஅய்த்","துறைஅணிந் தன்றவ ரூரே யிறையிறந்","திலங்குவளை ஞெகிழச் சாஅய்ப்","புலம்பணிந் தன்றவர் மணந்த தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says the ford of his town is adorned with small nyaazhal blossoms like mustard seeds strewn with the spent flowers of the fresh marutham, while the shoulders he embraced, wasted so that the bright bangles slip past the wrist, are adorned with loneliness.","n":"The same verb closes both halves: அணிந்தன்று, 'is adorned', for his ford and for her shoulders. His place wears flowers and hers wears loneliness; the parallel is the reproach. The colophon says marutham; the uri of lament, with the shore tree nyaazhal, points to neythal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 50","lb":"KU50","poet":"குன்றியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஐயவி","ஐயவி",1,"maram","simile","white mustard"],["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","simile","nyaazhal tree"],["மருதின்","மருது",2,"maram","scene","marutham tree"],["திலங்குவளை","வளை",4,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:51":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":52,"t":["கூன்முண் முண்டகக் கூர்ம்பனி மாமலர்","நூலறு முத்திற் காலொடு பாறித்","துறைதொறும் பரக்குந் தூமணற் சேர்ப்பனை","யானும் காதலென் யாயுநனி வெய்யள்","எந்தையுங் கொடீஇயர் வேண்டும்","அம்ப லூரும் அவனொடு மொழிமே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the lord of the shore of pure sand, where the cold dewy flowers of the bent-thorned water-thorn, blown by the wind like pearls from a broken string, spread over every ford, is loved by her too; their mother is very fond of him, their father wants to give the girl to him, and the gossiping town too speaks her name with his.","n":"Each one who might stand in the way is turned in his favour: the friend, the mother, the father and at last the town, whose அம்பல் usually threatens secret love and here pairs the girl with him. யானும் காதலென், 'I too love him', is the friend speaking as one with the girl.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 51","lb":"KU51","poet":"குன்றியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["முண்டகக்","முண்டகம்",1,"maram","simile","water-thorn"],["முத்திற்","முத்து",2,"pira","simile","pearl"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:52":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":53,"t":["ஆர்களிறு மிதித்த நீர்திகழ் சிலம்பிற்","சூர்நசைந் தனையையாய் நடுங்கல் கண்டே","நரந்த நாறுங் குவையிருங் கூந்தல்","நிரந்திலங்கு வெண்பல் மடந்தை","பரிந்தனென் அல்லனோ இறையிறை யானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl with thick dark hair smelling of naranda and rows of bright white teeth that when she saw her trembling, like one desired by the spirit of the water-bright slope trampled by the feeding elephant, she felt for her, little by little, did she not?","n":"சூர் நசைந்தனையை, 'like one the spirit has desired', is how a girl's lovesickness looks to those who do not know its cause; the friend says she saw it and understood. Whether பரிந்தனென் is pity for the girl or a plea made on her behalf is not settled by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kalaviyal","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 52","lb":"KU52","poet":"பனம்பாரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["ஆர்களிறு","களிறு",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["சூர்நசைந்","சூர்",2,"deyvam","simile","hill spirit"],["நரந்த","நரந்தம்",3,"maram","ornament","naranda fragrance"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:53":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":54,"t":["எம்மணங் கினவே மகிழ்ந முன்றில்","நனைமுதிர் புன்கின் பூத்தாழ் வெண்மணல்","வேலன் புனைந்த வெறியயர் களந்தொறும்","செந்நெல் வான்பொரி சிதறி யன்ன","எக்கர் நண்ணிய எம்மூர் வியன்துறை","நேரிறை முன்கை பற்றிச்","சூரர மகளிரோ டுற்ற சூளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the fields that what afflicts us is the oath he swore at the broad ford of our town, holding the girl's forearm with the hill-goddesses as witness, by the sand dune where the yard's punku blossoms lie on the white sand like the bright puffed red rice the velan scatters on every ground of his possession rite.","n":"அணங்கு is turned round: what afflicts them is not the goddess but the oath sworn before the goddesses, which will punish its breaker and, the friend fears, those he swore to. The velan's scattered rice brings the possession rite into the scene, as if the ground of the oath were already a ground of veri.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sool","anangu","veri","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 53","lb":"KU53","poet":"கோப்பெருஞ் சோழன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,"தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["புன்கின்","புன்கு",2,"maram","simile","punku tree"],["செந்நெல்","செந்நெல்",4,"unavu","simile","red paddy"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",3,"seythi","simile","velan, diviner"],["சூரர மகளிரோ","சூரர மகளிர்",7,"deyvam","scene","hill-goddesses"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:54":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":55,"t":["யானே யீண்டை யேனே யென்னலனே","ஏனல் காவலர் கவணொலி வெரீஇக்","கான யானை கைவிடு பசுங்கழை","மீனெறி தூண்டிலி னிவக்கும்","கானக நாடனொ டாண்டொழிந் தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says she is here, but her beauty has stayed there with the man of the forest land, where the green bamboo the forest elephant lets go, frightened by the sling-noise of the millet-field watchmen, springs up like a rod that has struck a fish.","n":"Line 1 splits her in two, I am here and my beauty is there, so nalam becomes a thing that can leave with him. The poet is named after the simile of line 4.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kalaviyal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 54","lb":"KU54","poet":"மீனெறிதூண்டிலார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",2,"maram","scene","millet"],["காவலர்","காவலர்",2,"seythi","scene","field watchmen"],["கவணொலி","கவண்",2,"seythi","scene","sling"],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","ullurai","elephant"],["பசுங்கழை","கழை",3,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["தூண்டிலி","தூண்டில்",4,"seythi","simile","fishing rod"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","யானை","confirmed","1/1","the man who lets her go for fear of the watch kept on her"]],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:55":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":56,"t":["மாக்கழி மணிப்பூக் கூம்பத் தூத்திரைப்","பொங்குபிதிர்த் துவலையொடு மங்குல் தைஇக்","கையற வந்த தைவரல் ஊதையொடு","இன்னா உறையுட் டாகும்","சின்னாட் டம்மவிச் சிறுநல் லூரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that as the sapphire flowers of the great backwater close, and the stroking cold wind comes bringing mist with the spray of the white waves to leave us helpless, this small good town has become, for these few days, a painful place to live.","n":"The closing flowers mark the evening without naming it. The town is called small and good (சிறுநல்லூர்) and painful in one breath: the place has not changed, only the season and his absence have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 55","lb":"KU55","poet":"நெய்தற் கார்க்கியர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,"தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மணிப்பூக்","மணிப்பூ",1,"maram","scene","blue flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:56":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":57,"t":["வேட்டச் செந்நாய் கிளைத்தூண் மிச்சில்","குளவி மொய்த்த அழுகற் சின்னீர்","வளையுடைக் கைய ளெம்மோ டுணீஇயர்","வருகதில் அம்ம தானே","அளியளோ அளியளென் னெஞ்சமர்ந் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The man on the waterless road says: let her of the bangled hands come and drink with me this little rotten water swarmed by wasps, left over when the hunting wild dogs dug and drank; she is to be pitied, pitied, she who dwells in my heart.","n":"The wish holds two readings: he would have her share even this water, so that love would make it sweet, or he pictures her drinking it and pities her for it; அளியளோ அளியள் keeps both. கிளைத்து, 'dug', can also mean 'with their kin', which would make the dogs a pack sharing the water as he would share it with her; that reading is too doubtful to tag.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 56","lb":"KU56","poet":"சிறைக்குடி ஆந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","மென்மை"]],"k":[["செந்நாய்","செந்நாய்",1,"maa","scene","wild dog"],["குளவி","குளவி",2,"pira","scene","wasp"],["சின்னீர்","சில் நீர்",2,"pira","gift_food","water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:57":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":58,"t":["பூவிடைப் படினும் யாண்டு கழிந்தன்ன","நீருறை மகன்றிற் புணர்ச்சி போலப்","பிரிவரி தாகிய தண்டாக் காமமொடு","உடனுயிர் போகுக தில்ல கடனறிந்","திருவேம் ஆகிய வுலகத்","தொருவே மாகிய புன்மை நாம் உயற்கே."],"p":"","r":"The woman wishes that, with a love that never slackens and cannot bear parting, like the union of the water-dwelling makanril pair for whom even a flower slipping between them seems a year gone by, their two lives might go out together, so that in this world where they live as two who know their duty they may escape the misery of one being left alone.","n":"கடனறிந்து இருவேம் ஆகிய, 'we two who live knowing our duty', places the wish inside married life: the fear is not of a journey but of one death before the other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","punarchi","pirivu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 57","lb":"KU57","poet":"சிறைக்குடி ஆந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பூவிடைப்","பூ",1,"maram","simile","flower"],["மகன்றிற்","மகன்றில்",2,"pul","simile","makanril water bird"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:58":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":59,"t":["இடிக்குங் கேளிர் நுங்குறை ஆக","நிறுக்க லாற்றினோ நன்றுமற் றில்ல","ஞாயிறு காயும் வெவ்வறை மருங்கிற்","கையில் ஊமன் கண்ணிற் காக்கும்","வெண்ணெய் உணங்கல் போலப்","பரந்தன் றிந்நோய் நோன்றுகொளற் கரிதே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the friends who rebuke him that it would be well indeed if they could hold back this sickness for his sake, but it has spread like butter melting on a sun-scorched rock, watched over by a handless mute with only his eyes, and it is hard to bear.","n":"The image stacks three helplessnesses: the keeper has no hands to gather the butter, no voice to call for help, and only his eyes to watch it melt. The rebukers are granted their good intent; the reply is that rebuke cannot reach what has already spread.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","idithal","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 58","lb":"KU58","poet":"வெள்ளி வீதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,null,"தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["வெண்ணெய்","வெண்ணெய்",5,"unavu","simile","butter"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:59":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":60,"t":["பதலைப் பாணிப் பரிசிலர் கோமான்","அதலைக் குன்றத் தகல்வாய்க் குண்டுசுனைக்","குவளையொடு பொதிந்த குளவி நாறுநின்","நறுநுதன் மறப்பரோ மற்றே முயலவும்","சுரம்பல விலங்கிய அரும்பொருள்","நிரம்பா ஆகலின் நீடலோ இன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether he could forget her fragrant forehead, which smells of wild jasmine bundled with blue water-lilies from the deep wide-mouthed spring of Athalai hill, ruled by the lord of the gift-seekers who beat the pathalai drum; and since rare wealth beyond many wastelands is never filled up however one strives, she says, he will not stay long.","n":"The comfort has two arguments: he cannot forget her, since the scent of a named hill's flowers is the memory he carries, and wealth is never complete, so there is no point in his staying to complete it. The second turns the reason for his journey against its length.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 59","lb":"KU59","poet":"மோசி கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பதலைப்","பதலை",1,"parai","scene","pathalai drum"],["குவளையொடு","குவளை",3,"maram","scene","blue water-lily"],["குளவி","குளவி",3,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"]],"en":[["hill","அதலைக்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:60":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":61,"t":["குறுந்தாட் கூதளி யாடிய நெடுவரைப்","பெருந்தேன் கண்ட இருங்கால் முடவன்","உட்கைச் சிறுகுடை கோலிக் கீழிருந்து","சுட்டுபு நக்கி யாங்குக் காதலர்","நல்கார் நயவா ராயினும்","பல்காற் காண்டலும் உள்ளத்துக் கினிதே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that, like a dark-legged cripple who sees a great honeycomb on the high cliff where short-stemmed koothali sways and, sitting below, cups his palm into a little bowl, points and licks it, even if her lover does not grace her or desire her, seeing him often is sweet to her heart.","n":"The cripple never tastes the honey: he points at it and licks his empty cupped hand, so the simile makes the sweetness of seeing him an imagined taste. நல்கார் நயவார் ஆயினும் concedes the worst before claiming the sweetness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","kaamam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 60","lb":"KU60","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["கூதளி","கூதளி",1,"maram","scene","koothali"],["பெருந்தேன்","தேன்",2,"unavu","simile","honeycomb"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:3"},"ku:61":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":62,"t":["தச்சன் செய்த சிறுமா வையம்","ஊர்ந்தின் புறாஅர் ஆயினுங் கையின்","ஈர்த்தின் புறூஉம் இளையோர் போல","உற்றின் புறேஎம் ஆயினும் நற்றேர்ப்","பொய்கை யூரன் கேண்மை","செய்தின் புற்றனெஞ் செறிந்தன வளையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that, like small boys who cannot ride the little cart a carpenter made yet delight in dragging it by hand, we take our delight in mere kinship with the man of the pond-rich town of good chariots, though we do not enjoy his embrace, and the bangles have grown tight again.","n":"The boys' toy cart is a pleasure taken without its proper use; the friend makes the household's dignity out of a love that has been reduced to a name, and the tightened bangles answer the gossip that she is wasting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","natpu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 61","lb":"KU61","poet":"தும்பிசேர் கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,"தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தச்சன்","தச்சன்",1,"seythi","simile","carpenter"],["பொய்கை","பொய்கை",5,"pira","scene","pond"],["வளையே","வளை",6,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:62":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":63,"t":["கோடல் எதிர்முகைப் பசுவீ முல்லை","நாறிதழ்க் குவளையொ டிடையிடுபு விரைஇ","ஐதுதொடை மாண்ட கோதை போல","நறிய நல்லோள் மேனி","முறியினும் வாய்வது முயங்கற்கும் இனிதே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that her body, fragrant as a finely made garland of upturned kodal buds, fresh mullai and sweet-petalled kuvalai woven in alternation, is softer than tender shoots and sweet to embrace.","n":"The garland mixes flowers of three lands, hill, woodland and water, so her one body gathers the scents a single landscape cannot give.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","azhagu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 62","lb":"KU62","poet":"சிறைக்குடி ஆந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கோடல்","கோடல்",1,"maram","simile","white kanthal"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",1,"maram","simile","jasmine"],["குவளை","குவளை",2,"maram","simile","blue water-lily"],["கோதை","கோதை",3,"maram","simile","garland"],["முறியினும்","முறி",5,"maram","simile","tender shoot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:63":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":64,"t":["ஈதலும் துய்த்தலும் இல்லோர்க் கில்லெனச்","செய்வினை கைம்மிக எண்ணுதி அவ்வினைக்கு","அம்மா அரிவையும் வருமோ","எம்மை உய்த்தியோ உரைத்திசின் நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The man asks his heart: you reckon that the work to be done must be pressed hard because there is neither giving nor enjoying for those who have nothing; but will the lovely young woman come along on that work, or will you send me off alone? Tell me.","n":"The heart's argument is the ethic of the giver, that wealth is for giving and enjoying; the man does not deny it but asks the one question it cannot answer, whether she can be taken along.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","inmai","eegai","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 63","lb":"KU63","poet":"உகாய்க்குடிகிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:64":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":65,"t":["பல்லா நெடுநெறிக் ககன்று வந்தெனப்","புன்றலை மன்றம் நோக்கி மாலை","மடக்கண் குழவி அலவந் தன்ன","நோயேம் ஆகுதல் அறிந்தும்","சேயர்தோழி சேய்நாட் டோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that like a dull-eyed calf that, when the many cows have gone far along the long path, looks at the bare common ground in the evening and cries, we are sick with longing; though he knows it, friend, the man of the far land stays far.","n":"The calf cries at the hour the herds should come home, so the simile carries mullai's own hour of waiting; and அறிந்தும், 'though he knows', puts the fault in his staying, not in his going.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 64","lb":"KU64","poet":"கருவூர்க் கதப்பிள்ளை","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பல்லா","பல் ஆ",1,"maa","simile","cows"],["குழவி","குழவி",3,"maa","simile","calf"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:65":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":66,"t":["வன்பரல் தெள்ளறல் பருகிய இரலைதன்","இன்புறு துணையொடு மறுவந் துகளத்","தான்வந் தன்றே தளிதரு தண்கார்","வாரா துறையுநர் வரனசைஇ","வருந்திநொந் துறைய இருந்திரோ எனவே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says the cool rain of dripping drops has come, and the stag that drank the clear water among the hard pebbles is back, frisking with its sweet mate, as if asking those who wait in longing for men who do not return, 'will you stay on, suffering and pained?'","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 65","lb":"KU65","poet":"கோவூர்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["இரலை","இரலை",1,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["தெள்ளறல்","அறல்",1,"pira","scene","clear water"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","இரலை","confirmed","1/1","even the stag keeps the season and comes back to its mate, yet the man who promised has not"]],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:66":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":67,"t":["மடவ மன்ற தடவுநிலைக் கொன்றை","கல்பிறங் கத்தஞ் சென்றோர் கூறிய","பருவம் வாரா அளவை நெரிதரக்","கொம்புசேர் கொடியிணர் ஊழ்த்த","வம்ப மாரியைக் காரென மதித்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says the konrai trees with their bent trunks are surely foolish: before the season named by the man who went over the stony path has come, they have taken the stray untimely rain for the rainy season and burst into clustered blooms on every branch.","n":"The friend denies the sign rather than the promise: by calling the trees fools she keeps his word intact, and so the comfort rests on her saying the season has not yet come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 66","lb":"KU66","poet":"கோவர்த்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","மடன்"]],"k":[["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",1,"maram","scene","laburnum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:67":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":68,"t":["உள்ளார் கொல்லோ தோழி கிள்ளை","வளைவாய்க் கொண்ட வேப்ப ஒண்பழம்","புதுநாண் நுழைப்பான் நுதிமாண் வள்ளுகிர்ப்","பொலங்கல ஒருகா சேய்க்கும்","நிலங்கரி கள்ளியங் காடிறந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether they do not think of us, the men who crossed the forest of kalli on scorched land, where the bright neem fruit held in the parrot's curved beak looks like a gold coin that a man with sharp fine nails threads on a new string.","n":"The one bright thing in the burnt land is a gold coin, so the image of the wealth he went for sits in the very scene of his road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 67","lb":"KU67","poet":"அள்ளூர் நன்முல்லையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கிள்ளை","கிள்ளை",1,"pul","simile","parrot"],["வேப்ப","வேம்பு",2,"maram","simile","neem"],["நுழைப்பான்","நுழைப்பான்",3,"seythi","simile","jeweller"],["கள்ளியங்","கள்ளி",5,"maram","scene","cactus spurge"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:68":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":69,"t":["பூழ்க்கா லன்ன செங்கால் உழுந்தின்","ஊழ்ப்படு முதுகாய் உழையினங் கவரும்","அரும்பனி அற்சிரந் தீர்க்கும்","மருந்துபிறி தில்லையவர் மணந்த மார்பே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says there is no other medicine for the hard-dewed early winter, when herds of deer eat the ripe old pods of the red-stemmed black gram whose stalks are like the quail's legs, than the chest of the man who embraced us.","n":"The cold is real and the cure is his body: the poem keeps the season's chill and the sickness of absence as one complaint, so 'medicine' needs no metaphor to cover both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 68","lb":"KU68","poet":"அள்ளூர் நன்முல்லையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பூழ்க்கா","பூழ்",1,"pul","simile","quail"],["உழுந்தின்","உழுந்து",1,"maram","scene","black gram"],["உழையினங்","உழை",2,"maa","scene","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:69":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":70,"t":["கருங்கண் தாக்கலை பெரும்பிறி துற்றெனக்","கைம்மை உய்யாக் காமர் மந்தி","கல்லா வன்பறழ் கிளைமுதற் சேர்த்தி","ஓங்குவரை அடுக்கத்துப் பாய்ந்துயிர் செகுக்கும்","சாரல் நாட நடுநாள்","வாரல் வாழியோ வருந்துதும் யாமே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the slopes, where a lovely female monkey, unable to bear widowhood after her black-eyed leaping mate has died, leaves her untaught young with her kin and leaps from the high ridge to end her life: do not come at midnight, may you live; we suffer.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kaimmai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 69","lb":"KU69","poet":"கடுந்தோட் கரவீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தாக்கலை","கலை",1,"maa","ullurai","male monkey"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",2,"maa","ullurai","female monkey"],["வன்பறழ்","பறழ்",3,"maa","scene","young monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","தாக்கலை மந்தி","confirmed","1/1","the man who risks death on the night path and the girl who would not outlive him"]],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:70":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":71,"t":["ஒடுங்கீர் ஓதி ஒண்ணுதற் குறுமகள்","நறுந்தண் ணீரள் ஆரணங் கினளே","இனையள் என்றவட் புனையள வறியேன்","சிலமெல் லியவே கிளவி","அணைமெல் லியள்யான் முயங்குங் காலே."],"p":"","r":"The man says the young girl of the gathered, glossy hair and bright brow is fragrant and cool and a great enchantress; I cannot measure her adornment to say she is such; her few words are soft, and she is soft as a pillow when I embrace her.","n":"'I cannot say she is such' is the lover's refusal to describe, and then he describes only what touch knows: coolness, fragrance, soft words, a soft body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","azhagu","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 70","lb":"KU70","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:71":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":72,"t":["மருந்தெனின் மருந்தே வைப்பெனின் வைப்பே","அரும்பிய சுணங்கின் அம்பகட் டிளமுலைப்","பெருந்தோள் நுணுகிய நுசுப்பிற்","கல்கெழு கானவர் நல்குறு மகளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says: if medicine, she is medicine; if treasure, she is treasure, the good young daughter of the forest folk of the rocky hills, with budding freckles, lovely broad young breasts, large shoulders and slender waist.","n":"வைப்பு is laid-up wealth: if the poem stands under paalai, as the colophon files it, the praise is an argument against leaving, since the treasure he would go for is already here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 71","lb":"KU71","poet":"கருவூர் ஓதஞானியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["கானவர்","கானவர்",4,"seythi","scene","forest hunters"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:72":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":73,"t":["பூவொத் தலமருந் தகைய ஏவொத்து","எல்லாரும் அறிய நோய்செய் தனவே","தேமொழித் திரண்ட மென்தோள் மாமலைப்","பரீஇ வித்திய ஏனற்","குரீஇ ஓப்புவாள் பெருமழைக் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The man says the large cool eyes of the girl with honeyed words and rounded soft shoulders, who scares the sparrows from the millet sown in the cleared land of the great hill, restless like flowers and like arrows, have caused him a sickness everyone can see.","n":"The girl's work is to drive off small birds with her cries, but it is her eyes that strike; and 'everyone knows' marks the moment his private wound becomes public talk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 72","lb":"KU72","poet":"மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பூவொத்","பூ",1,"maram","simile","flower"],["ஏனற்","ஏனல்",4,"maram","scene","millet"],["குரீஇ","குரீஇ",5,"pul","scene","sparrow"],["ஓப்புவாள்","ஓப்புதல்",5,"seythi","scene","field-guarding"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:73":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":74,"t":["மகிழ்நன் மார்பே வெய்யை யானீ","அழியல் வாழி தோழி நன்னன்","நறுமா கொன்று நாட்டிற் போக்கிய","ஒன்று மொழிக் கோசர் போல","வன்கட் சூழ்ச்சியும் வேண்டுமாற் சிறிதே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman: you long for your lover's chest; do not despair, may you live; like the Kosar of the single word, who cut down Nannan's fragrant mango tree and drove him from his land, a little hard-eyed scheming is needed.","n":"The scheme is not named: whether it is to withhold meetings so he must marry, or something else, the poem leaves open. மகிழ்நன் for the lover is a marutham word in a poem filed under kurinji.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","kaamam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 73","lb":"KU73","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நறுமா","மா",3,"maram","simile","mango"]],"en":[["king","நன்னன்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:74":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":75,"t":["விட்ட குதிரை விசைப்பி னன்ன","விசும்புதோய் பசுங்கழைக் குன்ற நாடன்","யாம்தற் படர்ந்தமை அறியான் தானும்","வேனில் ஆனேறுபோலச்","சாயினன் என்பநம் மாணலம் நயந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the man of the hill where green bamboo touching the sky springs up with the speed of a horse let loose, not knowing how we pined for him, has himself, they say, wasted like a bull in summer, longing for our fine beauty.","n":"The friend gives the heroine the comfort that the wasting is mutual; the summer bull, a beast of the pasture brought into the hill poem, makes his thinness a matter of season and hunger, the very terms of her own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 74","lb":"KU74","poet":"விட்டகுதிரையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["குதிரை","குதிரை",1,"maa","simile","horse"],["பசுங்கழைக்","கழை",2,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["ஆனேறு","ஆன் ஏறு",4,"maa","simile","bull"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:75":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":76,"t":["நீகண் டனையோ கண்டார்க் கேட்டனையோ","ஒன்று தெளிய நசையினம் மொழிமோ","வெண்கோட் டியானை சோணை படியும்","பொன்மலி பாடலி பெறீஇயர்","யார்வாய்க் கேட்டனை காதலர் வரவே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks: did you see it yourself, or hear it from someone who saw? Tell us one clear thing, for we long to know; may you win Pataliputra rich in gold, where white-tusked elephants bathe in the Son; from whose mouth did you hear that my lover is coming?","n":"The blessing, may you win Pataliputra, is the richest reward a Tamil poet could name, and it is offered before she knows if the news is true, so her hunger shows in the price she would pay.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 75","lb":"KU75","poet":"படுமரத்து மோசிகீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வெண்கோட் டியானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["river","சோணை",3],["town","பாடலி",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:76":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":77,"t":["காந்தள் வேலி ஓங்குமலை நல்நாட்டுச்","செல்ப என்பவோ கல்வரை மார்பர்","சிலம்பிற் சேம்பின் அலங்கல் வள்ளிலை","பெருங்களிற்றுச் செவியின் மானத் தைஇத்","தண்வரல் வாடை தூக்கும்","கடும்பனி அச்சிரம் நடுங்கஞர் உறவே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks whether they say he will go, the man with a chest like a rock face, of the good land of high hills fenced with kaanthal, in the hard-dewed early winter when the cold north wind sways the big leaves of the taro on the slopes like the ears of a great tusker, and sets us shivering in distress.","n":"The season is the argument: no one should leave in the cold of அச்சிரம், and the shiver she names is both the weather's and her own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 76","lb":"KU76","poet":"கிள்ளி மங்கலங்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",1,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["சேம்பின்","சேம்பு",3,"maram","simile","taro"],["பெருங்களிற்றுச்","களிறு",4,"maa","simile","tusker"],["வாடை","வாடை",5,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:77":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":78,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி யாவதும்","தவறெனின் தவறோ இலவே வெஞ்சுரத்து","உலந்த வம்பலர் உவலிடு பதுக்கை","நெடுநல் யானைக் கிடுநிழ லாகும்","அரிய கானஞ் சென்றோர்க்கு","எளிய வாகிய தடமென் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend there is no fault in him at all; if fault there is, it is that the broad soft shoulders became easy to leave for the man who went to the hard forest, where in the cruel waste the leaf-heaped cairns of dead travellers give shade to the tall elephant.","n":"She clears him and blames her own shoulders, but the desert of dead men's cairns he chose over them is a reproach she leaves standing; the elephant resting in the shade of graves is the land's indifference to death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nalam","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 77","lb":"KU77","poet":"மதுரை மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["வம்பலர்","வம்பலர்",3,"seythi","scene","travellers"],["உவலிடு","உவல்",3,"maram","scene","dry leaves"],["பதுக்கை","பதுக்கை",3,"pira","scene","stone cairn"],["யானைக்","யானை",4,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:78":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":79,"t":["பெருவரை மிசையது நெடுவெள் ளருவி","முதுவாய்க் கோடியர் முழவின் ததும்பிச்","சிலம்பின் இழிதரும் இலங்குமலை வெற்ப","நோதக் கன்றே காமம் யாவதும்","நன்றென உணரார் மாட்டும்","சென்றே நிற்கும் பெரும்பே தைமைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The companion tells the man of the shining hill, where the long white waterfall on the great peak resounds like the drum of the skilled old players as it pours down the slope: love is a painful thing; with great folly it goes and stands even by those who do not think it good.","n":"The companion does not blame the girl but love itself, which stands by those who do not value it; the drumming fall frames love as something that pours down unbidden.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pedhaimai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 78","lb":"KU78","poet":"நக்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பாங்கன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["paangan","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பாங்கன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெடுவெள் ளருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",2,"seythi","scene","dancers"],["முழவின்","முழவு",2,"parai","scene","drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:79":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":80,"t":["கான யானை தோனயந் துண்ட","பொரிதாள் ஓமை வளிபொரு நெடுஞ்சினை","அலங்கல் உலவை யேறி ஒய்யெனப்","புலம்புதரு குரல புறவுப்பெடை பயிரும்","அத்தம் நண்ணிய அங்குடிச் சீறூர்ச்","சேந்தனர் கொல்லோ தாமே யாந்தமக்கு","ஒல்லேம் என்ற தப்பற்குச்","சொல்லா தகறல் வல்லு வோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman wonders whether he has stopped in the pretty little hamlet near the desert where a female dove, perched on the swaying dead branch of the scorched-trunked omai whose bark the forest elephant ate with relish, calls with a cry that makes one lonely; he who could leave without a word because of our fault in saying we could not bear it.","n":"She blames herself, தப்பு, for refusing his going, and that refusal is why he went without telling her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 79","lb":"KU79","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["கான யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["ஓமை","ஓமை",2,"maram","scene","omai tree"],["உலவை","உலவை",3,"maram","scene","dead branch"],["புறவுப்பெடை","புறவு பெடை",4,"pul","iraicchi","female dove"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","புறவுப்பெடை","not_confirmed","0/1","the dove near the hamlet where he rests calls its partner as she, alone, calls for him"]],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:80":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":81,"t":["கூந்தல் ஆம்பல் முழுநெறி அடைச்சிப்","பெரும்புனல் வந்த இருந்துறை விரும்பி","யாமஃ தயர்கம் சேறும் தானஃது","அஞ்சுவ துடையள் ஆயின் வெம்போர்","நுகம்படக் கடக்கும் பல்வேல் எழினி","முனையான் பெருநிரை போலக்","கிளையொடுங் காக்கதன் கொழுநன் மார்பே."],"p":"","r":"The courtesan says: with whole stalks of water-lily fastened in our hair we will go to bathe at the broad ford where the great flood has come; if she fears that, let her and her kin guard her husband's chest, like the great herds at the front of Ezhini of many spears, who wins fierce wars till the yoke breaks.","n":"The taunt makes the husband a herd of cattle at a war front, property to be guarded against raiders, and herself the raider; the bathing in the flood with lilies is her open claim on him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 80","lb":"KU80","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தை கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["parathai","pira",null,"karpu","பரத்தை கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","ornament","water-lily"],["பெரும்புனல்","புனல்",2,"pira","scene","flood"],["பெருநிரை","நிரை",6,"maa","simile","cattle herd"]],"en":[["king","எழினி",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:4"},"ku:81":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":82,"t":["இவளே, நின்சொற் கொண்ட என்சொல் தேறிப்","பசுநனை ஞாழற் பல்சினை ஒருசிறைப்","புதுநலன் இழந்த புலம்புமார் உடையள்","உதுக்காண் தெய்ய உள்ளல் வேண்டும்","நிலவும் இருளும் போலப் புலவுத்திரைக்","கடலும் கானலுந் தோன்றும்","மடல்தாழ் பெண்ணையெம் சிறுநல் லூரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lover that the girl, who believed her word because it carried his, now sits alone in a corner under the many-branched njaazhal with its fresh buds, her new beauty gone; then she points: look over there and keep it in mind, our small good town of drooping palmyras, where the sea of smelling waves and the shore grove show side by side like moonlight and darkness.","n":"The friend admits her own part: the girl yielded because the friend's word stood surety for his (நின்சொற் கொண்ட என்சொல்). Pointing out the town and its landmarks is a way of telling him where to come openly, that is, to ask for her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 81","lb":"KU81","poet":"வடம வண்ணக்கண் பேரிசாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஞாழற்","ஞாழல்",2,"maram","scene",""],["பெண்ணையெம்","பெண்ணை",7,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:82":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":83,"t":["வாருறு வணர்கதுப் புளரிப் புறஞ்சேர்பு","அழாஅல் என்றுநம் அழுதகண் துடைப்பார்","யாரா குவர்கொல் தோழி சாரற்","பெரும்புனக் குறவன் சிறுதினை மறுகாற்","கொழுங்கொடி அவரை பூக்கும்","அரும்பனி அச்சிரம் வாரா தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend what sort of man he is, the one who used to stroke her long curled hair, hold her and wipe her weeping eyes saying 'do not cry', and who has not come back in the bitter-dew season, when the plump avarai creeper flowers on the second-sown millet of the hill farmer's wide field.","n":"The tender gestures she recalls are his own, so the question 'who will wipe my eyes?' is also an accusation: the comforter is the cause. The avarai flowering on the millet's second sowing dates the broken promise on the field itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 82","lb":"KU82","poet":"கடுவன் மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["குறவன்","குறவன்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["சிறுதினை","தினை",4,"unavu","scene",""],["அவரை","அவரை",5,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:83":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":84,"t":["அரும்பெறல் அமிழ்தம் ஆர்பதம் ஆகப்","பெரும்பெயர் உலகம் பெறீஇயரோ அன்னை","தம்மில் தமதுண் டன்ன சினைதொறும்","தீம்பழந் தூங்கும் பலவின்","ஓங்குமலை நாடனை வரும்என் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend blesses the mother: may she win the world of great name with rare ambrosia for her food, because she said that the lord of the high hill, where sweet jackfruit hang from every branch like one's own food eaten in one's own house, will come.","n":"The blessing is extravagant (the gods' food, the world above) for a single sentence, 'he will come'; the size of the gratitude shows how long the waiting has been. The simile of eating one's own food in one's own house anticipates the household the coming promises.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 83","lb":"KU83","poet":"வெண்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பலவின்","பலா",4,"maram","simile","jackfruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:84":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":85,"t":["பெயர்த்தனென் முயங்கயான் வியர்த்தனென் என்றனள்","இனியறிந் தேனது துனியா குதலே","கழல்தொடி ஆஅய் மழைதவழ் பொதியில்","வேங்கையும் காந்தளும் நாறி","ஆம்பல் மலரினும் தான்தண் ணியளே."],"p":"","r":"The foster mother recalls that when she turned the girl over to embrace her, the girl said 'I am sweating'; only now does she understand that it was distress; yet that girl, fragrant with the vengai and kaanthal of Aay's cloud-crept Pothiyil, was cooler to the touch than the water-lily.","n":"The last line undoes the first: the girl was cool by nature, so the 'sweat' was an excuse to slip the embrace of the woman who raised her, already turned toward another. The foster mother's memory of her daughter's fragrance is all hill-country flowers, the lover's land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ninaivu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 84","lb":"KU84","poet":"மோசிகீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செவிலி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வேங்கையும்","வேங்கை",4,"maram","scene",""],["காந்தளும்","காந்தள்",4,"maram","scene",""],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",5,"maram","simile","water-lily"]],"en":[["king","ஆஅய்",3],["hill","பொதியில்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:85":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":86,"t":["யாரினும் இனியன் பேரன் பினனே","உள்ளூர்க் குரீஇத் துள்ளுநடைச் சேவல்","சூன்முதிர் பேடைக் கீனி லிழைஇயர்","தேம்பொதிக் கொண்ட தீங்கழைக் கரும்பின்","நாறா வெண்பூக் கொழுதும்","யாண ரூரன் பாணன் வாயே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the lord of the rich town, where the hopping sparrow cock of the village plucks the scentless white flowers of the sweet-jointed sugarcane to line a nest for his hen near her laying, is sweeter than anyone and full of great love: in his bard's mouth.","n":"The praise collapses on its last word, வாயே: the great love exists in the bard's telling. The sparrow's flowers are scentless (நாறா); whether that is a further slight on the husband's gifts is doubtful and not tagged.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","thoothu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 85","lb":"KU85","poet":"வடம வண்ணக்கன் தாமோதரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["குரீஇத்","குரீஇ",2,"pul","iraicchi","sparrow"],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",4,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","குரீஇத்","confirmed","1/1","even a village sparrow provides for his mate in her time, while the husband's care for his wife exists only in the bard's words"]],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:86":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":87,"t":["சிறைபனி உடைந்த சேயரி மழைக்கண்","பொறையரு நோயொடு புலம்பலைக் கலங்கிப்","பிறருங் கேட்குநர் உளர்கொல் உறைசிறந்து","ஊதை தூற்றம் கூதிர் யாமத்து","ஆனுளம் புலம்புதொ றுளம்பும்","நாநவில் கொடுமணி நல்கூர் குரலே."],"p":"","r":"Her red-streaked cool eyes overflowing like a breached dam, sick with an unbearable illness and shaken by loneliness, the woman asks whether anyone else is listening, at midnight in the cold season when the north wind flings the heavy rain, to the thin, poor voice of the curved bell with its tongue that clangs each time the lonely cow stirs.","n":"'Does anyone else hear?' means 'is anyone else awake?': the only other wakeful thing is a cow whose loneliness (புலம்பு) rings out in the same word the poem uses for hers (புலம்பலை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 86","lb":"KU86","poet":"வெண்கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஊதை","ஊதை",4,"pira","scene","north wind"],["ஆனுளம்","ஆன்",5,"maa","scene","cow"],["கொடுமணி","மணி",6,"pira","scene","bell"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:87":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":88,"t":["மன்ற மராஅத்த பேஎமுதிர் கடவுள்","கொடியோர்த் தெறூஉம் என்ப யாவதும்","கொடியர் அல்லரெங் குன்றுகெழு நாடர்","பசைஇப் பசந்தன்று நுதலே","ஞெகிழ ஞெகிழ்ந்தன்று தடமென் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says people hold that the fearsome old god in the kadamba tree of the village common destroys the false; her hill lord is not false in the least; yet her forehead has paled with longing and her broad soft shoulders have grown slack as his care slackened.","n":"She clears him before the god so the god will not strike him for a broken oath; but the punishment the poem shows has landed on her own body, so the defence carries its own accusation. The doubled verbs (பசைஇப் பசந்தன்று, ஞெகிழ ஞெகிழ்ந்தன்று) tie each loss to his loosening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","anangu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 87","lb":"KU87","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மராஅத்த","மரா",1,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",1,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:88":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":89,"t":["ஒலிவெள் ளருவி ஓங்குமலை நாடன்","சிறுகண் பெருங்களிறு வயப்புலி தாக்கித்","தொல்முரண் சோருந் துன்னருஞ் சாரல்","நடுநாள் வருதலும் வரூஉம்","வடுநா ணலமே தோழி நாமே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the lord of the high hill of loud white waterfalls comes even at midnight across the hard slope where the small-eyed great tusker, struck by a strong tiger, loses its old fierceness; and they, for their part, will not shrink from the shame of scandal.","n":"The last line can be read as resolve (we will not let fear of talk keep him in danger: the love should be made known and the marriage pressed) or as a reckless 'we are past caring for blame'. The tusker that loses to the tiger shows what the path can do even to the strongest; it carries the danger, not an act of the lovers, so it is scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kalaviyal","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 88","lb":"KU88","poet":"மதுரைக் கதக்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["வெள் ளருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","scene",""],["பெருங்களிறு","களிறு",2,"maa","scene","elephant"],["வயப்புலி","புலி",2,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:89":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":90,"t":["பாவடி உரல பகுவாய் வள்ளை","ஏதின் மாக்கள் நுவறலும் நுவல்ப","அழிவ தெவன்கொலிப் பேதை யூர்க்கே","பெரும்பூண் பொறையன் பேஎமுதிர் கொல்லிக்","கருங்கண் தெய்வம் குடவரை யெழுதிய","நல்லியற் பாவை அன்னஇம்","மெல்லியற் குறுமகள் பாடினள் குறினே."],"p":"","r":"Strangers sing the open-mouthed pounding song at their broad-footed mortars and nobody minds; what harm can come to this foolish town if this soft young girl, like the lovely figure that the dark-eyed goddess drew on the western face of fearsome Kolli of Porayan of the great ornaments, sang as she pounded?","n":"The friend's point is the double standard: the same pounding song is harmless in strangers' mouths, but from this girl it is taken as a sign (presumably because she sang of the lover's hill). The town is called பேதை, the word usually given to an innocent girl.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","azhagu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 89","lb":"KU89","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வள்ளை","வள்ளை",1,"yaazh","scene","pounding song"],["தெய்வம்","தெய்வம்",5,"deyvam","simile",""]],"en":[["king","பொறையன்",4],["hill","கொல்லிக்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:90":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":91,"t":["எற்றோ வாழி தோழி முற்றுபு","கறிவளர் அடுக்கத் திரவின் முழங்கிய","மங்குல் மாமழை வீழ்ந்தெனப் பொங்குமயிர்க்","கலைதொட இழுக்கிய பூநாறு பலவுக்கனி","வரையிழி அருவி உண்துறைத் தரூஉம்","குன்ற நாடன் கேண்மை","மென்தோள் சாய்த்துஞ் சால்பீன் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The friend exclaims to the girl: what a thing it is! the friendship of the hill lord, from whose slopes where pepper vines climb, after the dark rain-cloud has thundered all night and fallen, the falls carry down to the drinking ford a fragrant jackfruit that a shaggy stag touched and loosened, has wasted your soft shoulders and yet has brought forth your constancy.","n":"A near miss for ullurai: the fruit that the stag loosens and the stream carries to a common ford invites a mapping (the girl won by the lover, then exposed to everyone), but the poem never anchors a second correspondence, so the scene stays scene. சால்பு is given a double edge: the friendship 'yielded' wasting and, with it, endurance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","melivu","saandraanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 90","lb":"KU90","poet":"மதுரை எழுத்தாளன் சேந்தன்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கறிவளர்","கறி",2,"maram","scene","pepper"],["கலைதொட","கலை",4,"maa","scene","stag"],["பலவுக்கனி","பலா",4,"maram","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",5,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:91":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":92,"t":["அரிற்பவர்ப் பிரம்பின் வரிப்புற விளைகனி","குண்டுநீ ரிலஞ்சிக் கெண்டை கதூஉம்","தண்டுறை ஊரன் பெண்டினை யாயிற்","பலவா குகநின் நெஞ்சிற் படரே","ஓவா தீயு மாரி வண்கைக்","கடும்பகட் டியானை நெடுந்தே ரஞ்சி","கொன்முனை இரவூர் போலச்","சிலவா குகநீ துஞ்சு நாளே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells herself that if she is to be the wife of the lord of the cool ford, where kendai fish in the deep pool snap at the ripe striped fruit of the tangled rattan, then let the cares in her heart be many and her nights of sleep few, like those of a town on the deadly frontier at night that fears Anji of the tall chariot and hard-driving elephants, whose hand gives like unceasing rain.","n":"A near miss for ullurai: fish snapping at whatever fruit drops into the pool can be read as the husband taking every woman who comes his way, but the poem never states his straying, so there is no anchor. The wife's curse on herself borrows its measure from war: her marriage will be a frontier town under raid.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","iravu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 91","lb":"KU91","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பிரம்பின்","பிரம்பு",1,"maram","scene","rattan"],["கெண்டை","கெண்டை",2,"pira","scene","carp"]],"en":[["king","நெடுந்தே ரஞ்சி",6]],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:92":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":93,"t":["ஞாயிறு பட்ட அகல்வாய் வானத்து","அளிய தாமே கொடுஞ்சிறைப் பறவை","இறையுறை வோங்கிய நெறியயல் மராஅத்த","பிள்ளை யுள்வாய்ச் செரீஇய","இரைகொண் டமையின் விரையுமாற் செலவே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says the curve-winged birds in the wide sky where the sun has set are to be pitied: carrying food to push into the mouths of their young in the tall kadamba beside the path where they roost, they hurry on their way.","n":"Her pity for the birds is pity for herself turned outward: their haste home at dusk with food for their young is what her own man does not show. The birds are the whole poem, so striking them leaves nothing; it fails the deletion test and is not tagged iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 92","lb":"KU92","poet":"தாமோதரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பறவை","பறவை",2,"pul","scene",""],["மராஅத்த","மரா",3,"maram","scene","kadamba"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:93":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":94,"t":["நன்னலந் தொலைய நலமிகச் சாஅய்","இன்னுயிர் கழியினும் உரைய லவர்நமக்கு","அன்னையும் அத்தனும் அல்லரோ தோழி","புலவியஃ தெவனோ அன்பிலங் கடையே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend not to speak against him even if her fine beauty fades, she wastes away and her sweet life leaves her: is he not mother and father to them? and what is sulking worth when love is gone?","n":"Two refusals in four lines: she will not let the friend blame him, because he is her whole family; and she will not sulk, because sulking presumes a love that would notice it. The second refusal is the bitterer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","anbu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 93","lb":"KU93","poet":"அள்ளூர் நன்முல்லையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:94":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":95,"t":["பெருந்தண் மாரிப் பேதைப் பித்திகத்து","அரும்பே முன்னும் மிகச்சிவந் தனவே","யானே மருள்வேன் தோழி பானாள்","இன்னுந் தமியர் கேட்பிற் பெயர்த்தும்","என்னா குவர்கொல் பிரிந்திசி னோரே","அருவி மாமலை தத்தக்","கருவி மாமழைச் சிலை தருங் குரலே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the buds of the foolish pitthikam, deceived by the great cold rains, have reddened even earlier than before, and she is bewildered: what will become of the man who left her, if he too hears alone at midnight the thunder of the massed storm clouds that shakes the great hill of waterfalls?","n":"The bud is 'foolish' for blooming to rains that are not the promised season, and she half admits she is like it. Her fear is turned toward him: she imagines him, not herself, alone under the thunder.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","mazhai","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 94","lb":"KU94","poet":"கதக்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["பித்திகத்து","பித்திகம்",1,"maram","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",6,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:95":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":96,"t":["மால்வரை இழிதருந் தூவெள் அருவி","கல்முகைத் ததும்பும் பன்மலர்ச் சாரல்","சிறுகுடிக் குறவன் பெருந்தோட் குறுமகள்","நீரோ ரன்ன சாயல்","தீயோ ரன்னவென் உரனவித் தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the grace of the broad-shouldered young daughter of the hill farmer of the small hamlet, on the flowered slope where the pure white falls from the great hill brim in the rock hollows, is like water, and it has put out his strength, which was like fire.","n":"The two similes are one action: water does not defeat fire by being stronger but by being what fire cannot survive. Softness (சாயல்) is set against will (உரன்), and the soft wins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","aanmai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 95","lb":"KU95","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","scene",""],["குறவன்","குறவன்",3,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:96":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":97,"t":["அருவி வேங்கைப் பெருமலை நாடற்கு","யானெவன் செய்கோ என்றி யானது","நகையென உணரேன் ஆயின்","என்னா குவைகொல் நன்னுதல் நீயே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says to her friend: you ask 'what can I do about the lord of the great hill of waterfalls and vengai trees?'; if I had not taken that as a joke, what would have become of you, fine-browed one?","n":"The friend's question tests her (shall I refuse him?); the woman answers that she chose to hear it as a joke, and that if she had taken it seriously the friend would have had to answer for the consequence. Who would suffer, and how, is left unsaid.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","natpu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 96","lb":"KU96","poet":"அள்ளூர் நன்முல்லையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","scene",""],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:97":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":98,"t":["யானே ஈண்டை யேனே யென்னலனே","ஆனா நோயொடு கான லஃதே","துறைவன் தம்மூ ரானே","மறையல ராகி மன்றத் தஃதே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says she is here; her beauty is there in the shore grove with an unending sickness; the lord of the shore is in his own town; and their secret, turned to open talk, is out in the common square.","n":"Four things in four places: herself, her beauty (left where they met), him, and the secret. The love has come apart into its parts, and the only part that has gone public is the talk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","nalam","pirivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 97","lb":"KU97","poet":"வெண்பூதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:98":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":99,"t":["இன்ன ளாயினள் நன்னுதல் என்றவர்த்","துன்னச் சென்று செப்புநர்ப் பெறினே","நன்றுமன் வாழி தோழிநம் படப்பை","நீர்வார் பைம்புதற் கலித்த","மாரிப் பீரத் தலர்சில கொண்டே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend it would be good if they could find someone who would go close to him, carrying a few flowers of the rainy-season peerkam that grows thick in the wet green bushes of their garden, and tell him 'the fine-browed one has become like this'.","n":"The message is the flower: the yellow peerkam, held up with 'she has become like this', shows her pallor without a word of complaint. That it is the rain-season flower also reminds him that the season of his return has come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","thoothu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 98","lb":"KU98","poet":"கோக்குள முற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பீரத்","பீரம்",5,"maram","scene","ridge gourd"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:99":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":100,"t":["உள்ளினென் அல்லனோ யானே உள்ளி","நினைத்தனென் அல்லனோ பெரிதே நினைத்து","மருண்டனென் அல்லனோ உலகத்துப் பண்பே","நீடிய மராஅத்த கோடுதோய் மலிர்நிறை","இறைத்துணச் சென்றற் றாஅங்கு","அனைப்பெருங் காமம் மீண்டுகடைக் கொளவே."],"p":"","r":"The man says: did I not remember? remembering, did I not think hard? thinking, was I not bewildered? but such is the way of the world, and my great desire held back to the end, like the flood that once touched the branches of the tall kadamba and has sunk until one must scoop it to drink.","n":"The three questions climb (remember, think, be bewildered) and each takes the last verb as its start. The flood simile can be read two ways: his desire was once as high as the treetops and shrank under duty, or it was a flood he held back to the last; the sense of மீண்டு கடைக்கொள is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 99","lb":"KU99","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மராஅத்த","மரா",4,"maram","simile","kadamba"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:100":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":101,"t":["அருவிப் பரப்பின் ஐவனம் வித்திப்","பருவிலைக் குளவியொடு பசுமரல் கட்கும்","காந்தள் வேலிச் சிறுகுடி பசிப்பிற்","கடுங்கண் வேழத்துக் கோடுநொடுத் துண்ணும்","வல்வில் ஓரி கொல்லிக் குடவரைப்","பாவையின் மடவந் தனளே","மணத்தற் கரிய பணைப்பெருந் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the girl of the kaanthal-fenced hamlet, where people sow mountain rice on the spread of the falls, weed out the broad-leafed kulavi and the green maral, and when hungry trade the tusks of fierce elephants for food, has the innocence of the figure on the western face of Kolli of Ori of the strong bow, and her broad shoulders, like bamboo, are hard to win.","n":"The girl comes from a hamlet that lives by elephant tusks, yet she is as unknowing as a painted figure; the hardness of her people's life and her innocence make her hard to win.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 100","lb":"KU100","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஐவனம்","ஐவனம்",1,"unavu","scene","hill rice"],["குளவியொடு","குளவி",2,"maram","scene",""],["பசுமரல்","மரல்",2,"maram","scene",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",3,"maram","scene",""],["வேழத்துக்","வேழம்",4,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["king","ஓரி",5],["hill","கொல்லிக்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:5"},"ku:101":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":102,"t":["விரிதிரைப் பெருங்கடல் வளைஇய உலகமும்","அரிதுபெறு சிறப்பிற் புத்தேள் நாடும்","இரண்டும் தூக்கிற் சீர்சா லாவே","பூப்போல் உண்கண் பொன்போல் மேனி","மாண்வரி அல்குற் குறுமகள்","தோள்மாறு படூஉம் வைகலோ டெமக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the sea-girt world and the hard-won land of the gods, weighed together, are not worth the day he lies arm in arm with the young girl whose kohl eyes are like flowers and whose body is like gold.","n":"தூக்கின் is the language of the balance: he does not say heaven is bad, only that both worlds on one pan do not lift a single day (வைகல்) of embrace on the other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 101","lb":"KU101","poet":"பரூஉ மோவாய்ப் பதுமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பூப்போல்","பூ",4,"maram","simile","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:102":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":103,"t":["உள்ளின் உள்ளம் வேமே உள்ளா","திருப்பினெம் அளவைத் தன்றே வருத்தி","வான்றோய் வற்றே காமம்","சான்றோர் அல்லர்யாம் மரீஇ யோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that if she thinks of him her heart burns, and not thinking is beyond her strength; this love has grown till it touches the sky, and the man she has clung to is not one of the noble.","n":"The bind is closed on both sides (உள்ளின் ... உள்ளாது இருப்பின்), and the last line turns from her pain to a judgment on him: சான்றோர் அல்லர், not noble, because he lets a love this large burn her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 102","lb":"KU102","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:103":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":104,"t":["கடும்புனல் தொகுத்த நடுங்கஞர் அள்ளல்","கவரிதழ் அன்ன தூவிச் செவ்வாய்","இரைதேர் நாரைக் கெவ்வ மாகத்","தூஉந் துவலைத் துயர்கூர் வாடையும்","வாரார் போல்வர்நங் காதலர்","வாழேன் போல்வல் தோழி யானே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that in the shivering mire gathered by the flood, where even the red-beaked egret hunting its food is pained by the spray the cold north wind drives, he seems not to be coming, and she seems not to be going to live.","n":"The two lines end alike (வாரார் போல்வர் / வாழேன் போல்வல்): his not coming and her not living are set as matching guesses. The egret, which only suffers and does no act of the lovers, sets the mood and is left as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 103","lb":"KU103","poet":"வாயிலான் தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கடும்புனல்","புனல்",1,"pira","scene","flood water"],["கவரிதழ்","இதழ்",2,"maram","simile","petal"],["நாரைக்","நாரை",3,"pul","scene","egret"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:104":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":105,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி காதலர்","நூலறு முத்தின் தண்சிதர் உறைப்பத்","தாளித் தண்பவர் நாளா மேயும்","பனிபடு நாளே பிரிந்தனர்","பிரியும் நாளும் பலவா குபவே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that her lover left in the dew season, when cold drops fall like pearls from a broken string and the cool thaali creeper is grazed at dawn, and the days of parting will be many.","n":"The season is the cruelty: he left not in summer but in the cold, when the pearl-like dew and early grazing mark each morning she counts alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 104","lb":"KU104","poet":"காவன் முல்லைப்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முத்தின்","முத்து",2,"pira","simile","pearl"],["தாளித்","தாளி",3,"maram","scene","creeper"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:105":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":106,"t":["புனவன் துடவைப் பொன்போற் சிறுதினைக்","கடியுண் கடவுட் கிட்ட செழுங்குரல்","அறியா துண்ட மஞ்ஞை ஆடுமகள்","வெறியுறு வனப்பின் வெய்துற்று நடுங்கும்","சூர்மலை நாடன் கேண்மை","நீர்மலி கண்ணொடு நினைப்பா கின்றே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that the bond with the man of the dread hill, where a peacock that unknowingly ate the rich millet ears set aside for the god trembles in the heat like a possessed dancing woman, has become something she remembers with streaming eyes.","n":"The peacock's fault is innocent (அறியாது உண்ட): it took what belonged to the god of the hill and now shakes like one seized. Her own wasting would be read at home as the god's seizure, and the hill is named சூர், the dreadful.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","ninaivu","anangu","veri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 105","lb":"KU105","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புனவன்","புனவன்",1,"seythi","scene","hill cultivator"],["சிறுதினைக்","தினை",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கடவுட்","கடவுள்",2,"deyvam","scene","god"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",3,"pul","ullurai","peacock"],["ஆடுமகள்","ஆடுமகள்",3,"seythi","simile","dancing woman"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","மஞ்ஞை","confirmed","1/1","the girl who took the hill man's love"]],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:106":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":107,"t":["புல்வீழ் இற்றிக் கல்லிவர் வெள்வேர்","வரையிழி அருவியின் தோன்றும் நாடன்","தீதில் நெஞ்சத்துக் கிளவி நம்வயின்","வந்தன்று வாழி தோழி நாமும்","நெய்பெய் தீயின் எதிர்கொண்டு","தான்மணந் தனையமென விடுகந் தூதே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that word has come from the man of the land where the white roots of the itti, climbing the rock, look like a waterfall coming down the slope, spoken from a heart without guile, and they should take it up as fire takes ghee and send back word that they are as good as wedded to him.","n":"தான் மணந்தனையம் claims the standing of a wife while love is still secret; the eager fire of ghee is hers, the friend is drawn into the reply (நாமும்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","varaivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 106","lb":"KU106","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["இற்றிக்","இற்றி",1,"maram","simile","fig tree"],["அருவியின்","அருவி",2,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["நெய்பெய்","நெய்",5,"unavu","simile","ghee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:107":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":108,"t":["குவியிணர்த் தோன்றி ஒண்பூ வன்ன","தொகுசெந் நெற்றிக் கணங்கொள் சேவல்","நள்ளிருள் யாமத் தில்லெலி பார்க்கும்","பிள்ளை வெருகிற் கல்கிரை யாகிக்","கடுநவைப் படீஇயரோ நீயே நெடுநீர்","யாணர்ஊரன் தன்னொடு வதிந்த","ஏம இன்துயில் எடுப்பி யோயே."],"p":"","r":"The wife curses the cock with its crest like a cluster of thondri flowers: may it fall prey to the young wildcat that watches for house rats at midnight and come to cruel harm, for it woke her from the safe sweet sleep she slept beside the lord of the rich, well-watered town.","n":"The curse is elaborate and patient (the cat that waits at midnight for rats), which measures how sweet the broken sleep was; ஏம இன் துயில், safe sweet sleep, is the sleep of a wife at home, and the cock's crow is the dawn that ends it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","karpu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 107","lb":"KU107","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தோன்றி","தோன்றி",1,"maram","simile","glory lily"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",2,"pul","scene","cock"],["தில்லெலி","இல் எலி",3,"maa","scene","house rat"],["வெருகிற்","வெருகு",4,"maa","scene","wildcat"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:108":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":109,"t":["மழைவிளை யாடுங் குன்றுசேர் சிறுகுடிக்","கறவை கன்றுவயிற் படரப் புறவிற்","பாசிலை முல்லை ஆசில் வான்பூச்","செவ்வான் செவ்வி கொண்டன்று","உய்யேன் போல்வல் தோழி யானே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that in the little village under the hill where rain clouds play, as the milch cows head back to their calves, the bright jasmine of the green-leaved scrub has taken on the beauty of the red sky, and she thinks she will not survive.","n":"The poem never says 'evening' or 'he has not come': the cows turning to their calves and the jasmine opening red in the sunset do it for her, and the last line is the whole of her speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 108","lb":"KU108","poet":"வாயிலான் தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கறவை","கறவை",2,"maa","iraicchi","milch cow"],["கன்றுவயிற்","கன்று",2,"maa","scene","calf"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",3,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கறவை","confirmed","2/2","even the cattle return to their young at nightfall, but her man has not come back to her"]],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:109":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":110,"t":["முட்கால் இறவின் முடங்குபுறப் பெருங்கிளை","புணரி இகுதிரை தரூஉந் துறைவன்","புணரிய இருந்த ஞான்றும்","இன்னது மன்னோ நன்னுதற் கவினே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says of the lord of the shore, where the waves bring in a great swarm of thorny-legged, bent-backed prawns, that even in the days when the two were together the beauty of the girl's fine forehead was not as it is now.","n":"மன்னோ marks something past and gone: her forehead was lovely then, and is pale now; said in the third person, it is a reproach meant for the man's hearing. The prawns that the waves bring are only the shore's plenty here; reading them as a hidden picture fails the two-pair test.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["melivu","azhagu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 109","lb":"KU109","poet":"நம்பி குட்டுவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["இறவின்","இறவு",1,"pira","scene","prawn"],["புணரி","புணரி",2,"pira","scene","sea"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:110":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":111,"t":["வாரா ராயினும் வரினும் அவர்நமக்கு","யாரா கியரோ தோழி நீர","நீலப் பைம்போ துளரிப் புதல","பீலி ஒண்பொறிக் கருவிளை யாட்டி","நுண்முள் ஈங்கைச் செவ்வரும் பூழ்த்த","வண்ணத் துய்ம்மலர் உதிரத் தண்ணென்று","இன்னா தெறிதரும் வாடையொடு","என்னா யினள்கொல் என்னா தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend what he is to them now, come or not, the man who never asked what had become of her in the cold cruel north wind that ruffles the blue water-lilies, sways the karuvilai spotted like peacock feathers and scatters the soft flowers of the thorny eengai.","n":"Her dismissal (யாராகியரோ, who is he to us) is the measure of her hurt; what she holds against him is not absence but the question he never asked (என்னாயினள்கொல்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaalam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 110","lb":"KU110","poet":"கிள்ளிமங்கலங்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["நீலப்","நீலம்",3,"maram","scene","blue water-lily"],["கருவிளை","கருவிளை",4,"maram","scene","karuvilai flower"],["ஈங்கைச்","ஈங்கை",5,"maram","scene","eengai bush"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:111":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":112,"t":["மென்தோள் நெகிழ்த்த செல்லல் வேலன்","வென்றி நெடுவேள் என்னும் அன்னையும்","அதுவென உணரும் ஆயின் ஆயிடைக்","கூழை இரும்பிடிக் கைகரந் தன்ன","கேழிருந் துறுகற் கெழுமலை நாடன்","வல்லே வருக தோழிநம்","இல்லோர் பெருநகை காணிய சிறிதே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that if the mother too takes the diviner's word that victorious Neduvel caused the sickness that has loosened the girl's soft arms, then let the man of the hill, where dark boulders look like a stub-tailed cow elephant with its trunk tucked in, come quickly, so they may see the household's great foolishness laughed at.","n":"The friend wants the man's arrival to expose the rite: the household will be the joke (பெருநகை) once its god-sickness turns out to be love. சிறிதே at the end keeps the mockery mild.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 111","lb":"KU111","poet":"தீன்மதி நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["வேலன்","வேலன்",1,"seythi","scene","diviner"],["நெடுவேள்","நெடுவேள்",2,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"],["இரும்பிடிக்","பிடி",4,"maa","simile","cow elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:112":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":113,"t":["கௌவை யஞ்சிற் காமம் எய்க்கும்","எள்ளற விடினே உள்ளது நாணே","பெருங்களிறு வாங்க முரிந்துநிலம் படாஅ","நாருடை ஒசியல் அற்றே","கண்டிசின் தோழியவர் உண்டஎன் நலனே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that if she fears the town's talk her love will waste, and if she lets restraint go only shame is left; she is like the fibrous branch a great elephant has bent, broken but not fallen to the ground; see what he has done to her beauty.","n":"The branch is held up by its own bark fibre (நாருடை): what keeps her from falling is the thread still joining her to him, which is also what the gossip would cut.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","alar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 112","lb":"KU112","poet":"ஆலத்தூர் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பெருங்களிறு","களிறு",3,"maa","simile","bull elephant"],["ஒசியல்","ஒசியல்",4,"maram","simile","bent branch"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:113":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":114,"t":["ஊர்க்கும் அணித்தே பொய்கை பொய்கைக்குச்","சேய்த்தும் அன்றே சிறுகான் யாறே","இரைதேர் வெண்குரு கல்ல தியாவதும்","துன்னல்போ கின்றாற் பொழிலே யாமெம்","கூழைக் கெருமண் கொணர்கஞ் சேறும்","ஆண்டும் வருகுவள் பெரும்பே தையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man the place: the pond is near the town, the little forest stream not far from the pond, and the grove there is visited by nothing but the white heron looking for food; she and the friend will go there to fetch rich clay for their hair, and the great simpleton will come there too.","n":"The directions are laid out as a chain (town, pond, stream, grove), and the heron is both the grove's only visitor and the sign that no one else will see. பெரும்பேதை, the great simpleton, is the friend's fond word for the girl who will come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 113","lb":"KU113","poet":"மாதீர்த்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பொய்கை","பொய்கை",1,"pira","scene","pond"],["யாறே","யாறு",2,"pira","scene","stream"],["வெண்குரு","குருகு",3,"pul","scene","heron"],["பொழிலே","பொழில்",4,"maram","scene","grove"],["கெருமண்","எருமண்",5,"pira","scene","hair-wash clay"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:114":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":115,"t":["நெய்தற் பரப்பிற் பாவை கிடப்பி","நின்குறி வந்தனென் இயல்தேர்க் கொண்க","செல்கம் செலவியங் கொண்மோ அல்கலும்","ஆரல் அருந்த வயிற்ற","நாரை மிதிக்கும் என்மகள் நுதலே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the well-made chariot that she has come to his trysting place leaving her doll lying in the neythal field, and asks leave to go, since every evening the egret, its belly full of aaral fish, tramples her little daughter's forehead.","n":"The 'daughter' is the doll she has left on the shore, a playful pretext to leave the lovers alone; the reading of என்மகள் as the doll is ours and the joke depends on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 114","lb":"KU114","poet":"பொன்னாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெய்தற்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","scene","neythal flower"],["ஆரல்","ஆரல்",4,"pira","scene","aaral fish"],["நாரை","நாரை",5,"pul","scene","egret"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:115":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":116,"t":["பெருநன் றாற்றிற் பேணாரும் உளரே","ஒருநன் றுடையள் ஆயினும் புரிமாண்டு","புலவி தீர அளிமதி இலைகவர்","பாடமை ஒழுகிய தண்ணறுஞ் சாரல்","மென்னடை மரையா துஞ்சும்","நன்மலை நாட நின்னல திலளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the good hill, where the soft-gaited wild cow sleeps on the cool fragrant slope, that even those who do great good may be slighted, but though she has only one good in her, he should be kind with a perfected love so that her sulking ends, for she has no one but him.","n":"The wild cow sleeping unafraid on his slope stands for the safety she should have in his grace; the opening maxim is a warning turned gently against him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","oodal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 115","lb":"KU115","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மரையா","மரை ஆ",5,"maa","iraicchi","wild cow"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மரையா","confirmed","1/1","as the creatures of his hill rest secure there, the girl who has no one but him should rest secure in his kindness"]],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:116":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":117,"t":["யானயந் துறைவோள் தேம்பாய் கூந்தல்","வளங்கெழு சோழர் உறந்தைப் பெருந்துறை","நுண்மணல் அறல்வார்ந் தன்ன","நன்னெறி யவ்வே நறுந்தண் ணியவே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the honey-dripping hair of the woman he loves and lives with is like the fine sand drawn into ripples at the great ford of Uranthai of the rich Cholas, well parted, fragrant and cool.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","punarchi","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 116","lb":"KU116","poet":"இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["நுண்மணல்","மணல்",3,"pira","simile","sand"]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",2],["town","உறந்தை",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:117":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":118,"t":["மாரி ஆம்ப லன்ன கொக்கின்","பார்வல் அஞ்சிய பருவரல் ஈர்ஞெண்டு","கண்டல் வேரளைச் செலீஇயர் அண்டர்","கயிறரி யெருத்திற் கதழுந் துறைவன்","வாரா தமையினும் அமைக","சிறியவும் உளவீண்டு விலைஞர்கை வளையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says of the lord of the shore, where a wet crab, frightened by the watch of the heron white as a rain-season lily, rushes to its hole among the mangrove roots like a herdsman's bull that has snapped its rope, that if he does not come, let it be: there are bangles here in the sellers' hands, small ones too.","n":"The jibe is that her bangles slip as she wastes, so she will need smaller ones, and these can be bought: the friend makes light of his absence to sting him. Read as a hidden picture (the crab fleeing the heron's watch as the girl hiding from the town's eyes) it has two pairs, but the poem names no watch or gossip to anchor them, so it fails condition 4.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 117","lb":"KU117","poet":"குன்றியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["ஆம்ப","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","simile","white water-lily"],["கொக்கின்","கொக்கு",1,"pul","simile","heron"],["ஈர்ஞெண்டு","ஞெண்டு",2,"pira","scene","crab"],["கண்டல்","கண்டல்",3,"maram","scene","mangrove"],["அண்டர்","அண்டர்",3,"seythi","simile","herdsmen"],["யெருத்திற்","எருது",4,"maa","simile","bull"],["வளையே","வளை",6,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:118":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":119,"t":["புள்ளும் மாவும் புலம்பொடு வதிய","நள்ளென வந்த நாரில் மாலைப்","பலர்புகு வாயில் அடைப்பக் கடவுநர்","வருவீர் உளீரோ எனவும்","வாரார் தோழிநங் காத லோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that in the loveless evening that has come down thick, when birds and beasts settle in loneliness and those shutting the much-used gate call out whether anyone else is coming, her lover does not come.","n":"The gatekeepers' call (வருவீர் உளீரோ) is the cruel detail: the house asks every evening who is still to come, and the one answer she waits for never comes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 118","lb":"KU118","poet":"நன்னாகையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புள்ளும்","புள்",1,"pul","scene","birds"],["மாவும்","மா",1,"maa","scene","beasts"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:119":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":120,"t":["சிறுவெள் ளரவின் அவ்வரிக் குருளை","கான யானை அணங்கி யாஅங்கு","இளையள் முளைவாள் எயிற்றள்","வளையுடைக் கையள்எம் அணங்கி யோளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that as the striped young of a small white snake can strike down a forest elephant, so this young girl with teeth like sprouts and bangles on her hands has struck him down.","n":"அணங்குதல் is what a god or demon does to a person; the scale of the simile (a hatchling snake, a wild elephant) is the hero's own wonder at being undone by someone so small and young.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kaamam","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 119","lb":"KU119","poet":"சத்திநாதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","சிறுமை"]],"k":[["ளரவின்","அரவு",1,"pira","simile","snake"],["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","simile","elephant"],["வளையுடைக்","வளை",4,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:120":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":121,"t":["இல்லோன் இன்பங் காமுற் றாஅங்கு","அரிதுவேட் டனையால் நெஞ்சே காதலி","நல்லள் ஆகுதல் அறிந்தாங்கு","அரியள் ஆகுதல் அறியா தோயே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart that it has longed for something hard to get, like a poor man longing for pleasure: it knew the beloved was good, but did not know she was hard to reach.","n":"The two knowings are set against each other (நல்லள் ஆகுதல் அறிந்து / அரியள் ஆகுதல் அறியாது): the heart learned her worth and missed its price, which is where the simile of the poor man's wish bites.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamam","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 120","lb":"KU120","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:6"},"ku:121":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":122,"t":["மெய்யே வாழி தோழி சாரல்","மைபட் டன்ன மாமுக முசுக்கலை","ஆற்றப் பாயாத் தப்பல் ஏற்ற","கோட்டொடு போகி யாங்கு நாடன்","தான்குறி வாயாத் தப்பற்குத்","தாம்பசந் தனஎன் தடமென் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The woman grants her friend that it is true: as the branch that took the miss of the black-faced langur, which leapt short on the slope, goes down with it, so for the hill man's failure to keep the tryst it is her broad soft shoulders that have turned pale.","n":"The poem is about where the cost of a fault falls: the langur misjudged the leap but the branch bears it, the man failed the meeting but her shoulders show it. The opening மெய்யே concedes something the friend has said, probably that he is not to blame, and the simile answers it without disputing it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","pasalai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 121","lb":"KU121","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முசுக்கலை","முசுக் கலை",2,"maa","simile","male langur"],["கோட்டொடு","கோடு",4,"maram","simile","branch"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:122":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":123,"t":["பைங்காற் கொக்கின் புன்புறத் தன்ன","குண்டுநீர் ஆம்பலும் கூம்பின இனியே","வந்தன்று வாழியோ மாலை","ஒருதான் அன்றே கங்குலும் உடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says the water-lilies of the deep water, grey as the dull back of the green-legged heron, have closed now; evening has come, and it has not come alone, for it brings the night with it.","n":"The closing lilies are the clock: their shut buds take the heron's colour as the light goes. The last line is the sting: evening, the hour of longing, is only the first of the hours she must get through without him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 122","lb":"KU122","poet":"ஓரம் போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கொக்கின்","கொக்கு",1,"pul","simile","heron"],["ஆம்பலும்","ஆம்பல்",2,"maram","scene","water-lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:123":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":124,"t":["இருள்திணிந் தன்ன ஈர்ந்தண் கொழுநிழல்","நிலவுக்குவித் தன்ன வெண்மணல் ஒருசிறைக்","கருங்கோட்டுப் புன்னைப் பூம்பொழில் புலம்ப","இன்னும் வாரார் வரூஉம்","பன்மீன் வேட்டத் தென்னையர் திமிலே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the flowering grove of black-trunked punnai, with shade cool and thick as packed darkness beside white sand heaped like gathered moonlight, lies lonely: he still has not come, and the boats of our kin who hunt many fish are coming back.","n":"Two similes make the grove a meeting place at once dark and bright. The line break sets வாரார் against வரூஉம்: the man does not come, the kinsmen do, so the time for meeting is closing. Said to the woman, perhaps for the man to hear, it presses him to come openly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 123","lb":"KU123","poet":"ஐயூர் முடவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புன்னைப்","புன்னை",3,"maram","scene",""],["பன்மீன்","மீன்",5,"pira","scene","fish"],["திமிலே","திமில்",5,"seythi","scene","fishing boat"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:124":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":125,"t":["உமணர் சேர்ந்து கழிந்து மருங்கி னகன்றலை","ஊர்பாழ்த் தன்ன ஓமையம் பெருங்காடு","இன்னா என்றி ராயின்","இனியவோ பெரும தமியோர்க்கு மனையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord that if he calls harsh the great forest of omai trees, desolate as a wide town left waste after the salt-traders camped and moved on, then is the house sweet to those left in it alone?","n":"She takes his own word (இன்னா) and turns it round: the abandoned camp of the simile is already a picture of the house he would leave. She does not plead; she makes the forest and the home equal in harm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ilvaazhkkai","innaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 124","lb":"KU124","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["உமணர்","உமணர்",1,"seythi","simile","salt-traders"],["ஓமையம்","ஓமை",2,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:125":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":126,"t":["இலங்குவளை நெகிழச் சாஅ யானே","உளெனே வாழி தோழி சாரல்","தழையணி அல்குல் மகளி ருள்ளும்","விழவுமேம் பட்டவென் நலனே பழவிறற்","பறைவலந் தப்பிய பைதல் நாரை","திரைதோய் வாங்குசினை யிருக்கும்","தண்ணந் துறைவனொடு கண்மா றின்றே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that though her bright bangles slip and she has wasted, she is still alive; but her beauty, which outshone at festivals even the leaf-skirted women of the slope, has turned away from her along with the lord of the cool harbour where a forlorn heron, its old wing-strength gone, sits on a bent branch the waves wash.","n":"The heron that can no longer fly and sits on a wave-washed branch is close to her own state, but she speaks as a girl of the slope about a man of the shore, so the condition that the heroine use only things she knows is not clear; it is left as scene (a near-miss ullurai). கண்மாறின்று, 'has turned its face away', makes her beauty a companion that has left with him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","melivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 125","lb":"KU125","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தழையணி","தழை",3,"maram","ornament","leaf-skirt"],["நாரை","நாரை",5,"pul","scene","heron"],["வாங்குசினை","சினை",6,"maram","scene","branch"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:126":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":127,"t":["இளமை பாரார் வளநசைஇச் சென்றோர்","இவணும் வாரார் எவண ரோவெனப்","பெயல்புறந் தந்த பூங்கொடி முல்லைத்","தொகுமுகை இலங்கெயி றாக","நகுமே தோழி நறுந்தண் காரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that those who went for wealth without regard for youth have not come back, and the fragrant cool rainy season, with the clustered buds of the flowering mullai the rain has fed for its bright teeth, laughs, as if asking where they are.","n":"The season's laugh is mockery: the buds are white teeth and the question எவணரோ is put in the season's mouth. The first line is the charge: they did not weigh youth, which passes, against wealth, which can wait.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","ilamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 126","lb":"KU126","poet":"ஒக்கூர் மாசாத்தியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["முல்லைத்","முல்லை",3,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:127":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":128,"t":["குருகுகொளக் குளித்த கெண்டை அயலது","உருகெழு தாமரை வான்முகை வெரூஉம்","கழனியம் படப்பைக் காஞ்சி யூர","ஒருநின் பாணன் பொய்ய னாக","உள்ள பாணர் எல்லாம்","கள்வர் போல்வர்நீ அகன்றிசி னோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the town of kaanji trees and field-plots, where the kendai fish that dived when a heron struck at it now fears the bright bud of the lotus beside it, that because his one bard proved a liar, every bard there is looks like a thief to the women he has left.","n":"The lotus bud is harmless, but white-tipped like a heron's beak; the fish once caught at shies from it. The friend never says the women are the fish, and the line நீ அகன்றிசினோர்க்கு lays the blame on his leaving, not on the bard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","vanjam","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 127","lb":"KU127","poet":"ஓரம் போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["குருகு","குருகு",1,"pul","ullurai","heron"],["கெண்டை","கெண்டை",1,"pira","ullurai","carp"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",2,"maram","ullurai",""],["காஞ்சி","காஞ்சி",3,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","குருகு கெண்டை தாமரை","confirmed","1/1","the wives the man left, made wary of every bard by one deceiver"]],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:128":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":129,"t":["குணகடல் திரையது பறைதபு நாரை","திண்டேர்ப் பொறையன் தொண்டி முன்றுறை","அயிரை ஆரிரைக் கணவந் தாங்குச்","சேயள் அரியோட் படர்தி","நோயை நெஞ்சே நோய்ப்பா லோயே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart that, like a heron on the waves of the eastern sea that has lost its wings and longs for the rich ayirai fish of the front harbour of Thondi of Poraiyan of the sturdy chariots, it pines for one who is far away and hard to win, and that suffering is its lot.","n":"The heron is on the east coast and Thondi is on the west, and the bird cannot fly: the simile measures the girl's distance by the width of the land and the lover's helplessness by the lost wings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamanoy","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 128","lb":"KU128","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நாரை","நாரை",1,"pul","simile","heron"],["அயிரை","அயிரை",3,"pira","simile","small fish"]],"en":[["king","பொறையன்",2],["town","தொண்டி",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:129":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":130,"t":["எலுவ சிறாஅர் ஏமுறு நண்ப","புலவர் தோழ கேளா யத்தை","மாக்கடல் நடுவண் எண்ணாட் பக்கத்துப்","பசுவெண் திங்கள் தோன்றி யாங்குக்","கதுப்பயல் விளங்கும் சிறுநுதல்","புதுக்கோள் யானையிற் பிணித்தற்றால் எம்மே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his comrade, the friend who gladdens boys and keeps company with poets, to listen: the small forehead gleaming beside her hair, like the fresh white moon of the eighth day rising out of the dark sea, has bound him like a newly captured elephant.","n":"The eighth-day moon is a half disc, the shape of a small brow, and its rising from black water is the brow against dark hair. The poet is the Chola Kopperunchozhan, and the image of capture is a king's: he who takes elephants is himself taken like one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","natpu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 129","lb":"KU129","poet":"கோப்பெருஞ்சோழன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["திங்கள்","திங்கள்",4,"pira","simile","moon"],["யானையிற்","யானை",6,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:130":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":131,"t":["நிலந்தொட்டுப் புகாஅர் வானம் ஏறார்","விலங்கிரு முந்நீர் காலிற் செல்லார்","நாட்டின் நாட்டின் ஊரின் ஊரின்","குடிமுறை குடிமுறை தேரிற்","கெடுநரும் உளரோநம் காதலோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says their lover will not burrow into the earth, climb the sky or walk across the sea; if they search country by country, town by town, household by household, can he be lost?","n":"The three denials close off every place but the peopled land, and the doubled words (நாட்டின் நாட்டின், ஊரின் ஊரின், குடிமுறை குடிமுறை) act out the search. The comfort lies in the certainty, not in any plan to go. Tradition makes the poet a woman who went in search of her husband; the colophon gives the words to the friend.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 130","lb":"KU130","poet":"வெள்ளி வீதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முந்நீர்","முந்நீர்",2,"pira","scene","sea"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:131":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":132,"t":["ஆடமை புரையும் வனப்பிற் பணைத்தோள்","பேரமர்க் கண்ணி யிருந்த ஊரே","நெடுஞ்சேண் ஆரிடை யதுவே நெஞ்சே","ஈரம் பட்ட செவ்விப் பைம்புனத்து","ஓரேர் உழவன் போலப்","பெருவிதுப் புற்றன்றால் நோகோ யானே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart that the town where she stays, she of broad shoulders lovely as swaying bamboo and large fighting eyes, lies very far over a hard road, yet the heart is as eager as a farmer with a single plough when his green field is wet and ready; and he grieves.","n":"The one-plough farmer has one chance while the soil holds the rain: the simile is about timing, the urgency of a moment that will pass. The poet's name, Orerruzhavanar, comes from this line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 131","lb":"KU131","poet":"ஒரேருழவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஆடமை","அமை",1,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["பைம்புனத்து","புனம்",4,"pira","simile","dry field"],["உழவன்","உழவன்",5,"seythi","simile","ploughman"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:132":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":133,"t":["கவவுக் கடுங்குரையள் காமர் வனப்பினள்","குவவுமென் முலையள் கொடிக்கூந் தலளே","யாங்குமறந் தமைகோ யானே ஞாங்கர்க்","கடுஞ்சுரை நல்லா னடுங்குதலைக் குழவி","தாய்காண் விருப்பி னன்ன","சாஅய்நோக் கினளே மாஅ யோளே."],"p":"","r":"The man asks how he can forget her, fierce in embrace, of lovely beauty, with rounded soft breasts and creeper-long hair, the dark girl who looked at him with a drooping gaze like the longing of the trembling-headed calf of a good full-uddered cow to see its mother.","n":"The list of her body ends on a look, and the look is likened not to a lover's but to a calf's need for its mother: helpless dependence is what binds him. The cow and calf are herders' things in a hill love poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 132","lb":"KU132","poet":"சிறைக்குடியாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நல்லா","நல் ஆன்",4,"maa","simile","cow"],["குழவி","குழவி",4,"maa","simile","calf"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:133":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":134,"t":["புனவன் துடவைப் பொன்போற் சிறுதினை","கிளிகுறைத் துண்ட கூழை யிருவி","பெரும்பெய லுண்மையி னிலையொலித் தாங்கென்","உரஞ் செத்தும் உளெனே தோழியென்","நலம்புதி துண்ட புலம்பி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that, like the stubble of the hill farmer's golden millet, cropped short by parrots, which puts out leaves again in heavy rain, she is still alive in the loneliness that followed his first taking of her beauty.","n":"The parrots that ate the millet and the man who 'newly ate' her beauty (நலம் புதிது உண்ட) share the verb உண், so the simile carries both the loss and the survival. உரஞ் செத்தும் may mean 'though my strength is dead' or 'thinking of his firmness'; the sense of line 4 turns on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nalam","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 133","lb":"KU133","poet":"உறையூர் முதுகண்ணன் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["புனவன்","புனவன்",1,"seythi","simile","hill farmer"],["சிறுதினை","தினை",1,"maram","simile","millet"],["கிளி","கிளி",2,"pul","simile","parrot"],["யிருவி","இருவி",2,"maram","simile","stubble"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:134":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":135,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நம்மொடு","பிரிவின் றாயின் நன்றுமற் றில்ல","குறும்பொறைத் தடைஇய நெடுந்தாள் வேங்கைப்","பூவுடை அலங்குசினை புலம்பத் தாக்கிக்","கல்பொரு திரங்கும் கதழ்வீழ் அருவி","நிலங்கொள் பாம்பின் இழிதரும்","விலங்குமலை நாடனொடு கலந்த நட்பே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the friendship joined with the man of the crossing hills, where the swift waterfall strikes the flowered swaying branch of the tall vengai on the small hill till it is bare, dashes roaring on the rocks and slides down like a snake taking the ground, would be good beyond anything if only it brought no parting.","n":"The waterfall strips the vengai of its flowers and then runs away down the hill; she names only the parting, and lets the scene say the rest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pirivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 134","lb":"KU134","poet":"கோவேங்கைப் பெருங்கதவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",3,"maram","ullurai",""],["அருவி","அருவி",5,"pira","ullurai","waterfall"],["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",6,"pira","simile","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","அருவி வேங்கைப்","confirmed","1/1","the man whose love has stripped the woman's bloom and who goes away"]],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:135":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":136,"t":["வினையே ஆடவர்க் குயிரே வாணுதல்","மனையுறை மகளிர்க் காடவர் உயிரென","நமக்குரைத் தோருந் தாமே","அழாஅல் தோழி அழுங்குவர் செலவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman not to cry: he himself told them that work is life to men and men are life to bright-browed women who keep the house, so he will put off his going.","n":"The hero spoke both halves of the maxim; the friend leans on the second to cancel the first. If he is her life as work is his, going for work would kill what he works for.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aalvinai","uyir","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 135","lb":"KU135","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங் கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:136":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":137,"t":["காமங் காமம் என்ப காமம்","அணங்கும் பிணியும் அன்றே நுணங்கிக்","கடுத்தலுந் தணிதலும் இன்றே யானை","குளகுமென் றாள்மதம் போலப்","பாணியும் உடைத்தது காணுநர்ப் பெறினே."],"p":"","r":"The man says people keep saying 'love, love', but love is neither a possessing spirit nor a disease; it does not grow fine and flare and subside; like the musth that comes on an elephant chewing soft fodder, it has its season, when it finds someone to look on.","n":"He answers a charge, probably his comrade's, that his love is an affliction: it is a native season of the body, latent like musth, which the sight of her brings out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anangu","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 136","lb":"KU136","poet":"மிளைப்பெருங் கந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","simile","elephant"],["குளகுமென்","குளகு",4,"maram","simile","fodder leaves"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:137":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":138,"t":["மெல்லியல் அரிவைநின் னல்லகம் புலம்ப","நிற்றுறந் தமைகுவெ னாயின் எற்றுறந்","திரவலர் வாரா வைகல்","பலவா குகயான் செலவுறு தகவே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his soft-natured wife that if he ever leaves her and stays away so that her good breast grows lonely, may he have many days when suppliants leave him and do not come: that is what his going would deserve.","n":"The curse he takes on is the worst a giver can suffer, to be passed over by those who ask, and men usually leave home to earn the wealth to give; so he stakes the reason for going against going.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","eegai","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 137","lb":"KU137","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங் கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:138":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":139,"t":["கொன்னூர் துஞ்சினும் யாந்துஞ் சலமே","எம்மி லயல தேழி லும்பர்","மயிலடி யிலைய மாக்குர னொச்சி","அணிமிகு மென்கொம் பூழ்த்த","மணிமருள் பூவின் பாடுநனி கேட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that though the town sleeps uselessly, they do not sleep, hearing clearly the sound of the sapphire-blue flowers dropping from the lovely soft branches of the dark-clustered nochchi with leaves like peacock's feet, beside their house on the height.","n":"They are awake enough to hear nochchi flowers fall, the faintest sound there is, and the sleeping town is set against the two who keep watch. Line 2 may name the hill Ezhil (அயலது ஏழில் உம்பர்), but the word division is unsure. Nothing in the poem is of the marutham fields; night, watching and the waiting for a man fit kurinji.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 138","lb":"KU138","poet":"கொல்லன் அழிசி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["னொச்சி","நொச்சி",3,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:139":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":140,"t":["மனையுறை கோழிக் குறுங்காற் பேடை","வேலி வெருகின மாலை யுற்றெனப்","புகுமிட னறியாது தொகுபுடன் குழீஇய","பைதற் பிள்ளைக் கிளைபயிர்ந் தாஅங்","கின்னா திசைக்கும் அம்பலொடு","வாரல் வாழிய ரையவெந் தெருவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man not to come to their street, for the gossip there sounds as harshly as the cry of a short-legged house hen who, when a wildcat comes to the fence at evening, cannot find where to go and calls her frightened chicks together.","n":"The simile makes the street an alarmed household and quietly casts the man as the cat at the fence. அம்பல் is the first murmur of talk about secret lovers, before it becomes அலர், so the situation is kalavu and the karu of house and fence are borrowed from the town.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 139","lb":"KU139","poet":"ஒக்கூர் மாசாத்தியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோழிக்","கோழி",1,"pul","simile","hen"],["வெருகின","வெருகு",2,"maa","simile","wildcat"],["பிள்ளைக்","பிள்ளை",4,"pul","simile","chicks"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:140":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":141,"t":["வேதின வெரிநின் ஓதிமுது போத்து","ஆறுசெல் மாக்கள் புட்கொளப் பொருந்தும்","சுரனே சென்றனர் காதலர் உரனழிந்து","ஈங்கியான் தாங்கிய எவ்வம்","யாங்கறிந் தன்றிவ் வழுங்க லூரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says her lover has gone through the waste where the old male chameleon with a saw-toothed back sits and travellers take it for an omen; how did this noisy town come to know the grief she has borne here with her strength broken?","n":"She has kept the grief to herself, so the town's knowing is a second injury: the gossip is about a pain she never voiced.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 140","lb":"KU140","poet":"அள்ளூர் நன்முல்லையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஓதி","ஓதி",1,"pira","scene","chameleon"],["மாக்கள்","மாக்கள்",2,"seythi","scene","travellers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:7"},"ku:141":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":142,"t":["வளைவாய்ச் சிறுகிளி விளைதினைக் கடீஇயர்","செல்கென் றோளே அன்னை எனநீ","சொல்லின் எவனோ தோழி கொல்லை","நெடுங்கை வன்மான் கடும்பகை யுழந்த","குறுங்கை யிரும்புலிக் கொலைவல் ஏற்றை","பைங்கட் செந்நாய் படுபதம் பார்க்கும்","ஆரிரு ணடுநாள் வருதி","சாரல் நாட வாரலோ எனவே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend what harm there would be in telling the lord of the slope that her mother has sent her to drive the curve-beaked parrots off the ripening millet, and in telling him not to come in the deep midnight, when a great tiger, bruised from a hard fight with an elephant, waits for its chance to kill a green-eyed red dog.","n":"The mother's errand is reported as news, but it tells him where she will be by day, so 'do not come at night' is half of an offer. The beasts are reasons, not a mood: even the tiger on that slope is wounded and lying in wait to kill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 141","lb":"KU141","poet":"மதுரைப் பெருங்கொல்லனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["சிறுகிளி","கிளி",1,"pul","scene",""],["விளைதினை","தினை",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["வன்மான்","மா",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["யிரும்புலி","புலி",5,"maa","scene","tiger"],["செந்நாய்","செந்நாய்",6,"maa","scene","dhole"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:142":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":143,"t":["சுனைப்பூக் குற்றுத் தொடலை தைஇப்","புனக்கிளி கடியும் பூங்கட் பேதை","தானறிந் தனளோ இலளோ பானாட்","பள்ளி யானையி னுயிர்த்தென்","உள்ளம் பின்னுந் தன்னுழை யதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The hero wonders whether the simple girl with flower-like eyes, who threads a garland of pool flowers and drives the parrots off the millet field, knows or does not know that at midnight he breathes heavily like an elephant in its resting place and that his heart is still there beside her.","n":"He has gone home but his heart has not, and he cannot tell whether she knows: the doubt of the first days of kalavu, before any word has passed between them. The sleeping elephant's breath gives the size of his longing in a scene of stillness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","nenju","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 142","lb":"KU142","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["சுனைப்பூ","சுனைப்பூ",1,"maram","ornament",""],["புனக்கிளி","கிளி",2,"pul","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:143":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":144,"t":["அழிய லாயிழை அன்பு பெரிதுடையன்","பழியும் அஞ்சும் பயமலை நாடன்","நில்லா மையே நிலையிற் றாகலின்","நல்லிசை வேட்ட நயனுடை நெஞ்சிற்","கடப்பாட் டாள னுடைப்பொருள் போலத்","தங்குதற் குரிய தன்றுநின்","அங்கலுழ் மேனிப் பாய பசப்பே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine not to despair: the lord of the fruitful hill loves her greatly and fears blame, and since only impermanence is permanent, the pallor spread over her lovely skin cannot stay, any more than wealth stays with a dutiful man of kind heart who seeks good fame.","n":"The maxim of impermanence, usually a warning, becomes comfort: what must pass includes her pallor. The simile is chosen with care, since the generous man's wealth leaves him because he gives it away: the pallor will leave by a good act, not by decay.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","nilaiyaamai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 143","lb":"KU143","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:144":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":145,"t":["கழிய காவி குற்றும் கடல","வெண்டலைப் புணரி யாடியும் நன்றே","பிரிவி லாய முரியதொன் றயர","இவ்வழிப் படுதலும் ஒல்லாள் அவ்வழிப்","பரல்பாழ் படுப்பச் சென்றனள் மாதோ","சென்மழை தவழும் சென்னி","விண்ணுயர் பிறங்கல் விலங்குமலை நாட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The foster mother says it was good when the girl picked water-lilies in the backwater and played in the white-crested waves of the sea; she would not even stay here to play as she should with her inseparable friends, and has gone that way, over desolate gravel, to a mountain country of sky-high ridges where moving clouds crawl over the peaks.","n":"She measures the going by landscapes: from the sea shore of the girl's play, across the stony waste, to the mountains beyond. பிரிவில் ஆயம், the troop that never parted from her, is the first thing she has parted from.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","pirivu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 144","lb":"KU144","poet":"மதுரை ஆசிரியர் கோடன் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செவிலித்தாய் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu","செவிலித்தாய் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["காவி","காவி",1,"maram","scene","water-lily"],["புணரி","புணரி",2,"pira","scene","waves"],["பரல்","பரல்",5,"pira","scene","gravel"],["சென்மழை","மழை",6,"pira","scene","cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:145":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":146,"t":["உறைபதி யன்றித் துறைகெழு சிறுகுடி","கானலஞ் சேர்ப்பன் கொடுமை ஏற்றி","ஆனாத் துயரமொடு வருந்திப் பானாள்","துஞ்சா துறைநரொ டுசாவாத்","துயிற்கண் மாக்களொடு நெட்டிரா வுடைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says this little harbour village is no place to live: charging the lord of the seaside grove with cruelty, she suffers in unending sorrow at midnight, and the long night here holds only people with sleeping eyes, who will not talk with those who lie awake.","n":"Who 'raises' the lord's cruelty is open, she or the village; either way the complaint lands on the sleepers, whose sleep is a refusal of company. Line 1's உறைபதி யன்றி may mean 'not our own town' rather than 'no place to dwell'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 145","lb":"KU145","poet":"கொல்லனழிசியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["கானலஞ்","கானல்",2,"maram","scene","seaside grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:146":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":147,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நம்மூர்ப்","பிரிந்தோர்ப் புணர்ப்போர் இருந்தனர் கொல்லோ","தண்டுடைக் கையர் வெண்டலைச் சிதவலர்","நன்றுநன் றென்னு மாக்களோ","டின்றுபெரி தென்னும் ஆங்கண தவையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the heroine whether the people who unite the parted have come to their town: men with staffs in their hands and white cloths on their heads, who say 'good, good' and, there in their assembly, 'today is a great day'.","n":"The elders are seen before their errand is named, by their staffs, white headgear and stock words of approval, so the friend lets the heroine read the signs of a marriage embassy herself and gives her joy as a question. வெண்டலை may be white heads of age rather than white cloth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","saandror","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 146","lb":"KU146","poet":"வெள்ளி வீதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:147":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":148,"t":["வேனிற் பாதிரிக் கூன்மல ரன்ன","மயிரேர் பொழுகிய வங்கலுழ் மாமை","நுண்பூண் மடந்தையைத் தந்தோய் போல","இன்றுயில் எடுப்புதி கனவே","எள்ளார் அம்ம துணைப்பிரிந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The hero tells the dream that, as if it had brought him the young woman of fine ornaments, whose lovely dark skin with its fine down is like the curved paathiri flower of summer, it wakes him from sweet sleep; those parted from their mates are not to be mocked.","n":"He blames the dream, not waking: its gift is a trick that ends in waking, so its kindness is a kind of mockery. The last line widens his case into a rule for all the parted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 147","lb":"KU147","poet":"கோப்பெருஞ் சோழன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,"தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["பாதிரி","பாதிரி",1,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:148":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":149,"t":["செல்வச் சிறாஅர் சீறடிப் பொலிந்த","தவளை வாய பொலஞ்செய் கிண்கிணிக்","காசி னன்ன போதீன் கொன்றை","குருந்தோ டலம்வரும் பெருந்தண் காலையும்","காரன் றென்றி யாயிற்","கனவோ மற்றிது வினவுவல் யானே."],"p":"","r":"Answering a friend who says the season has not come, the heroine asks: if this great cool time, when konrai buds like the gold coins of the frog-mouthed anklets on rich children's small feet bloom along with the kurundham, is not the rainy season, then is this a dream?","n":"The friend's usual argument, that the rains prove he will come, is turned round: the rains have come and he has not, so the friend must deny the season and the heroine must insist on it. Her question makes the comfort an admission that he broke his word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","kanavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 148","lb":"KU148","poet":"இளங் கீரந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தவளை","தவளை",2,"pira","simile","frog"],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",3,"maram","simile",""],["குருந்தோ","குருந்து",4,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:149":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":150,"t":["அளிதோ தானே நாணே நம்மொடு","நனிநீ டுழந்தன்று மன்னே இனியே","வான்பூங் கரும்பின் ஓங்குமணற் சிறுசிறை","தீம்புனல் நெரிதர வீய்ந்துக் காஅங்குத்","தாங்கு மளவைத் தாங்கிக்","காம நெரிதரக் கைந்நில் லாதே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says her modesty is to be pitied: it struggled long beside her, but now, like a small dam of heaped sand where white-flowered reeds grow, which gives way when the sweet flood presses, it has held as long as it could and, with desire pressing, no longer stays in her hand.","n":"Modesty is a companion that 'suffered with us' and is pitied, not a law she breaks. The dam is small and of sand and the flood is sweet: the image concedes that what breaks it is welcome.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kaamam","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 149","lb":"KU149","poet":"வெள்ளி வீதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",3,"maram","simile","reed"],["மணற்","மணல்",3,"pira","simile","sand"],["தீம்புனல்","புனல்",4,"pira","simile","flood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:150":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":151,"t":["சேணோன் மாட்டிய நறும்புகை ஞெகிழி","வான மீனின் வயின்வயின் இமைக்கும்","ஓங்குமலை நாடன் சாந்துபுல ரகலம்","உள்ளின் உண்ணோய் மல்கும்","புல்லின் மாய்வ தெவன்கொல் அன்னாய்"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that when she thinks of the sandal-dried chest of the lord of the high hill, where the fragrant smoking torches lit by the watchman on the platform flicker here and there like stars, the pain within her swells; when she embraces it, how is it that the pain vanishes?","n":"Thought and embrace act on one sickness in opposite ways, and she asks why as if it were a riddle; the asking itself reports that the embrace has happened.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","ninaivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 150","lb":"KU150","poet":"மாடலூர் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சேணோன்","சேணோன்",1,"seythi","scene","platform watchman"],["ஞெகிழி","ஞெகிழி",1,"pira","simile","torch"],["மீனின்","மீன்",2,"pira","simile","star"],["சாந்து","சாந்து",3,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:151":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":152,"t":["வங்காக் கடந்த செங்காற் பேடை","எழாஅலுற வீழ்ந்தெனக் கணவற் காணாது","குழலிசைக் குரல குறும்பல அகவும்","குன்றுகெழு சிறுநெறி அரிய என்னாது","மறப்பருங் காதலி யொழிய","இறப்ப லென்பதீண் டிளமைக்கு முடிவே."],"p":"","r":"The hero says that, not counting it hard to take the narrow path through the hills where the red-legged hen, her mate felled by a hawk as he rose, cannot find him and keeps crying short flute-sweet calls, to leave behind the beloved he cannot forget and go: that is the end of youth.","n":"'Going is the end of youth' is a verdict, not a regret: youth is the time to be with her, and the road can wait. வங்காக் கடந்த in line 1 is hard; it may name the vangaa bird whose hen she is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 151","lb":"KU151","poet":"தூங்கலோரியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வங்கா","வங்கா",1,"pul","scene",""],["பேடை","பேடை",1,"pul","iraicchi","hen bird"],["எழாஅலுற","எழால்",2,"pul","scene","hawk"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பேடை","confirmed","1/1","a woman left behind would call so for a man lost on such roads, so the hero who hears it will not leave his beloved"]],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:152":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":153,"t":["யாவதும் அறிகிலர் கழறு வோரே","தாயின் முட்டை போலவுட் கிடந்து","சாயின் அல்லது பிறிதெவ னுடைத்தே","யாமைப் பார்ப்பி னன்ன","காமங் காதலர் கையற விடினே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says those who reproach her know nothing at all: if her lover leaves it helpless, love, like the young of the tortoise, has nothing to do but lie within and waste away, as an egg does away from its mother.","n":"The image rests on the old belief that the tortoise leaves her eggs in the sand and the young live by her thought of them: love too lives only by the lover's thought, and withdrawn it can only shrink where it lies. தாயின் in line 2 (with or without the mother) is the crux.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ninaivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 152","lb":"KU152","poet":"கிளிமங்கலங்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முட்டை","முட்டை",2,"pira","simile","egg"],["யாமைப்","யாமை",4,"pira","simile","tortoise"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:153":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":154,"t":["குன்றக் கூகை குழறினும் முன்றிற்","பலவி னிருஞ்சினைக் கலைபாய்ந் துகளினும்","அஞ்சுமன் அளித்தெ னெஞ்ச மினியே","ஆரிருட் கங்குல் அவர்வயிற்","சாரல் நீளிடைச் செலவா னாதே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says her pitiable heart, which used to be afraid when the hill owl hooted or when the male monkey leapt on the dark branch of the jackfruit in the yard and shook it, now in the dense dark night never tires of going to him along the long way down the slope.","n":"The old fears are household ones, an owl, a monkey in the yard; the journey the heart now makes is the lover's own night road. She speaks of the heart as a separate creature whose character has changed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 153","lb":"KU153","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["கூகை","கூகை",1,"pul","scene","owl"],["பலவி","பலா",2,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["கலை","கலை",2,"maa","scene","male monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:154":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":155,"t":["யாங்கறிந் தனர்கொல் தோழி பாம்பின்","உரிநிமிர்ந் தன்ன உருப்பவி ரமையத்","திரைவேட் டெழுந்த சேவல் உள்ளிப்","பொறிமயிர் எருத்திற் குறுநடைப் பேடை","பொரிகாற் கள்ளி விரிகா யங்கவட்டுத்","தயங்க விருந்து புலம்பக் கூஉம்","அருஞ்சுர வைப்பிற் கானம்","பிரிந்துசே ணுறைதல் வல்லு வோரே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend how they learned it, those who can part and live far away in the forest of the hard waste, where in the hour when heat shimmers like a snake's stretched slough the short-stepping hen with spotted neck feathers, thinking of the cock that went to find food, sits on the forked branch of the parched kalli and calls out in loneliness.","n":"The cock has gone only to fetch food, the hero's own errand in small, and still the hen cries; 'how did they learn it' sets parting down as a skill learned against nature.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 154","lb":"KU154","poet":"மதுரைச் சீத்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",1,"pira","simile","snake"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",3,"pul","iraicchi","cock bird"],["பேடை","பேடை",4,"pul","iraicchi","hen bird"],["கள்ளி","கள்ளி",5,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பேடை","confirmed","1/1","even a bird whose mate is only gone to fetch food cries out, so the man who knows that road knows what his going does to her"]],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:155":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":156,"t":["முதைப்புனங் கொன்ற ஆர்கலி உழவர்","விதைக்குறு வட்டி போதொடு பொதுளப்","பொழுதோ தான்வந் தன்றே மெழுகான்","றூதுலைப் பெய்த பகுவாய்த் தெண்மணி","மரம்பயில் இறும்பி னார்ப்பச் சுரனிழிபு","மாலை நனிவிருந் தயர்மார்","தேர்வரும் என்னும் உரைவா ராதே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says the hour has come when the farmers who cleared the old field with a great din bring their seed baskets brimming with flowers, but no word comes that his chariot is on its way, its clear split-mouthed bells, cast from wax moulds and poured in the furnace, ringing through the thick-treed thicket as it comes down from the waste to arrive in the evening for a great feast with guests.","n":"The season and the hour are both here; what is missing is only the report, so she waits for news before the man. The chariot is drawn down to the casting of its bells, as if imagined over and over. விதைக்குறு வட்டி போதொடு பொதுள (line 2) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 155","lb":"KU155","poet":"உரோடகத்துக் காரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["உழவர்","உழவர்",1,"seythi","scene","farmers"],["போதொடு","போது",2,"maram","scene","flowers"],["இறும்பி","இறும்பு",5,"maram","scene","thicket"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:156":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":157,"t":["பார்ப்பன மகனே பார்ப்பன மகனே","செம்பூ முருக்கி னன்னார் களைந்து","தண்டொடு பிடித்த தாழ்கமண் டலத்துப்","படிவ உண்டிப் பார்ப்பன மகனே","எழுதாக் கற்பி னின்சொல் உள்ளும்","பிரிந்தோர்ப் புணர்க்கும் பண்பின்","மருந்தும் உண்டோ மயலோ விதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The hero calls to the Brahmin youth, who carries a water pot hung from a staff of red-flowered palasa stripped of its bark and eats the food of his vows: among the sweet words of your unwritten learning, is there a medicine with the power to unite those who are parted, or is this madness?","n":"எழுதாக் கற்பு, the learning not written, names the Veda by how it is kept, by mouth. மயலோ இதுவே leaves it open whether the madness is the question or the hero's own state, and so whether he mocks the Brahmin's lore or truly hopes in it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","kaamanoy","andhanar","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 156","lb":"KU156","poet":"பாண்டியன் ஏனாதி நெடுங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paarppaan",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முருக்கி","முருக்கு",2,"maram","scene","palasa"],["உண்டி","உண்டி",4,"unavu","scene","vowed food"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:157":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":158,"t":["குக்கூ வென்றது கோழி அதன்எதிர்","துட்கென் றன்றென் தூய நெஞ்சம்","தோடோய் காதலர்ப் பிரிக்கும்","வாள்போல் வைகறை வந்தன்றால் எனவே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that when the cock cried 'kukkoo' her pure heart started in fear, for dawn had come like a sword that cuts apart lovers whose shoulders touch.","n":"The cock is only the signal; the sword is the dawn itself. Her heart is pure and still it starts as at a blow, as if dawn's cut fell on her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","pirivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 157","lb":"KU157","poet":"அள்ளூர் நன்முல்லையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோழி","கோழி",1,"pul","scene","cock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:158":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":159,"t":["நெடுவரை மருங்கிற் பாம்புபட இடிக்கும்","கடுவிசை உருமின் கழறுகுரல் அளைஇக்","காலொடு வந்த கமஞ்சூல் மாமழை","ஆரளி யிலையோ நீயே பேரிசை","இமயமும் துளக்கும் பண்பினை","துணையிலர் அளியர் பெண்டிர் இஃதெவனோ."],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks the dark cloud heavy with rain, which came on the wind mingled with the roar of the swift thunder that strikes snakes dead on the slopes of the tall mountain, whether it has no pity: it has power to shake even the famed Himalaya, so what is this against women who are pitiable for want of a companion?","n":"The storm's might is granted and then turned into a reproach: a power that can shake the Himalaya has no need to try itself on a lone woman. The strong thing that leaves her unprotected stands for the absent man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","thunai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 158","lb":"KU158","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",1,"pira","scene","snake"],["உருமின்","உரும்",2,"pira","scene","thunder"],["மாமழை","மழை",3,"pira","scene","rain cloud"]],"en":[["hill","இமயமும்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:159":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":160,"t":["தழையணி அல்குல் தாங்கல் செல்லா","நுழைசிறு நுசுப்பிற் கெவ்வ மாக","அம்மெல் ஆக நிறைய வீங்கிக்","கொம்மை வரிமுலை செப்புடன் எதிரின","யாங்கா குவள்கொல் பூங்குழை என்னும்","அவல நெஞ்சமொ டுசாவாக்","கவலை மாக்கட்டிப் பேதை யூரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the girl's slender waist, too small to bear the leaf skirt on her hips, is in distress, for her soft chest has filled out and her round streaked breasts have risen to rival caskets, while this foolish town, full of worry, keeps saying with sorrowful hearts 'what will become of her, the girl with flower earrings?' and consults no one.","n":"Her coming of age is told as a burden on her waist and as worry in the town; both mean she will now be watched and kept at home, so said in the lover's hearing it presses him to marry. உசாவா in line 6 may mean the town will not consult her family, or will not talk to her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","varaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 159","lb":"KU159","poet":"வடம வண்ணக்கண் பேரிசாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தழையணி","தழை",1,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:160":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":161,"t":["நெருப்பி னன்ன செந்தலை யன்றில்","இறவி னன்ன கொடுவாய்ப் பேடையொடு","தடவின் ஓங்குசினைக் கட்சியிற் பிரிந்தோர்","கையற நரலு நள்ளென் யாமத்துப்","பெருந்தண் வாடையும் வாரார்","இஃதோ தோழிநங் காதலர் வரவே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that at hushed midnight, when the fire-red-headed anril cries with its prawn-beaked hen from their nest on the tall branch, making the parted helpless, and the great cold north wind blows as well, he does not come: so this is our lover's coming!","n":"The last line is bitter irony: the cries and the wind are all that came. The anril pair in one nest is the measure of her single bed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 160","lb":"KU160","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["யன்றில்","அன்றில்",1,"pul","iraicchi",""],["பேடை","பேடை",2,"pul","iraicchi","hen bird"],["இறவி","இறவு",2,"pira","simile","prawn"],["தடவின்","தடவு",3,"maram","scene","tree"],["வாடை","வாடை",5,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","யன்றில்","confirmed","1/1","even the birds keep their mates through the cold night, while her lover, who said he would come, stays away"]],"ch":"ku:8"},"ku:161":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":162,"t":["பொழுதும் எல்லின்று பெயலும் ஓவாது","கழுதுகண் பனிப்ப வீசும் அதன்றலைப்","புலிப்பல் தாலிப் புதல்வர்ப் புல்லி","அன்னா வென்னும் அன்னையு மன்னோ","என்மலைந் தனன்கொல் தானே தன்மலை","ஆரம் நாறு மார்பினன்","மாரி யானையின் வந்துநின் றனனே."],"p":"","r":"On a night when daylight has gone dark, the rain will not stop and a wind blows that would chill even a ghoul's eyes, while her mother clutches the boys with tiger-tooth pendants and cries out in alarm, the girl wonders what could have possessed the hill man, his chest smelling of sandal, to come and stand outside like an elephant in the rain.","n":"The danger is doubled: the storm outside and the wakeful mother inside. The elephant standing silent and soaked in the rain gives his bulk and his patience at once; the cry அன்னா is the household's alarm, which the girl must hear while knowing who is out there.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 161","lb":"KU161","poet":"நக்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["கழுதுகண்","கழுது",2,"pira","scene","ghoul"],["புலிப்பல்","புலிப் பல்",3,"maa","ornament","tiger tooth"],["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",6,"maram","ornament","sandal"],["யானையின்","யானை",7,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:162":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":163,"t":["கார்புறந் தந்த நீருடை வியன்புலத்துப்","பலர்புகு தரூஉம் புல்லென் மாலை","முல்லை வாழியோ முல்லை நீநின்","சிறுவெண் முகையின் முறுவல் கொண்டனை","நகுவை போலக் காட்டல்","தகுமோ மற்றிது தமியோர் மாட்டே."],"p":"","r":"In the wide watered land the rains have nursed, at the dull evening when everyone goes home, the man alone hails the jasmine and asks whether it is fair to put on a smile with its small white buds and seem to laugh at those who are by themselves.","n":"The buds are not called like a smile; they have taken the smile from someone (முறுவல் கொண்டனை), so the flower wears the absent woman's laugh. The evening when many come home is the mullai hour of return, which makes his being away the sharper.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 162","lb":"KU162","poet":"கருவூர்ப் பவுத்திரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",3,"maram","simile",""],["முகையின்","முகை",4,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:163":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":164,"t":["யாரணங் குற்றனை கடலே பூழியர்","சிறுதலை வெள்ளைத் தோடுபரந் தன்ன","மீனார் குருகின் கானலம் பெருந்துறை","வெள்வீத் தாழை திரையலை","நள்ளென் கங்குலுங் கேட்குநின் குரலே."],"p":"","r":"The girl asks the sea who has hurt it, since on the great shore of the grove, where fish-fed herons spread like a flock of the Puzhiyar's small-headed white sheep and the waves toss the white-flowered thazhai, she hears its voice even in the dead of night.","n":"The question is turned outward but answers itself: she hears the sea at midnight only because she is awake, so the sea's unrest is the mirror of her own, and யார் அணங்குற்றனை asks of the sea what others might ask of her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 163","lb":"KU163","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வெள்ளைத்","வெள்ளை",2,"maa","simile","sheep"],["மீனார்","மீன்",3,"pira","scene","fish"],["குருகின்","குருகு",3,"pul","simile",""],["தாழை","தாழை",4,"maram","scene",""],["திரை","திரை",4,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:164":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":165,"t":["கணைக்கோட்டு வாளை கமஞ்சூல் மடநாகு","துணர்த்தேக் கொக்கின் தீம்பழம் கதூஉம்","தொன்றுமுதிர் வேளிர் குன்றூர்க் குணாது","தண்பெரும் பவ்வம் அணங்குக தோழி","மனையோள் மடமையிற் புலக்கும்","அனையே மகிழ்நற்கியா மாயினம் எனினே."],"p":"","r":"The man's courtesan tells her friend: may the cool great sea east of the hill town of the ancient Velir, where the vaalai fish and its heavy-bellied young mate snatch the sweet fruit of the clustered mango, strike me, if I am the one the wife sulks over in her simplicity, if I am anything to her husband.","n":"An oath sworn by a named sea is a public statement: the courtesan disowns any claim on the man so that the wife's quarrel falls back on him. The fish pair feeding on fallen mango has been read as a hidden image of the man and his pregnant wife at ease, but it is the oath-place's description, with no second pair to anchor it, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 164","lb":"KU164","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"காதற்பரத்தை கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வாளை","வாளை",1,"pira","scene","vaalai fish"],["நாகு","நாகு",1,"pira","scene","young female fish"],["கொக்கின்","கொக்கு",2,"maram","scene","mango"],["பழம்","பழம்",2,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[["king","வேளிர்",3],["town","குன்றூர்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:165":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":166,"t":["மகிழ்ந்ததன் றலையும் நறவுண் டாங்கு","விழைந்ததன் றலையும் நீவெய் துற்றனை","அருங்கரை நின்ற உப்பொய் சகடம்","பெரும்பெய றலையவீந் தாங்கியவள்","இரும்பல் கூந்தல் இயலணி கண்டே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart that, like drinking toddy on top of being already merry, it has burned on top of its desire, and has given way like a salt cart standing on a steep bank that melts away in a heavy downpour, from seeing her thick dark hair and natural grace.","n":"Both images make the sight a second blow on an earlier one: toddy on top of joy, rain on top of salt. The salt cart is a coast and trade thing brought into a hill poem, and its cargo does not break, it dissolves, which is how he says his resolve went.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nenju","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 165","lb":"KU165","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நறவுண்","நறவு",1,"unavu","simile","toddy"],["உப்பொய் சகடம்","உப்பு ஒய் சகடம்",3,"seythi","simile","salt cart"],["பெரும்பெய","பெயல்",4,"pira","simile","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:166":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":167,"t":["தண்கடற் படுதிரை பெயர்த்தலின் வெண்பறை","நாரை நிரைபெயர்ந் தயிரை யாரும்","ஊரோ நன்றுமன் மரந்தை","ஒருதனி வைகிற் புலம்பா கின்றே."],"p":"","r":"Where the waves of the cool sea draw back and the white-winged storks move off in a line to feed on ayirai fish, the town of Maranthai is good indeed, says the friend, but for one who stays there all alone it turns to desolation.","n":"The storks move and feed in a row (நிரை), the company the speaker lacks; the praise of the town is granted only to be withdrawn by the last line. ஒருதனி doubles the word for alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ilvaazhkkai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 166","lb":"KU166","poet":"கூடலூர் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["படுதிரை","திரை",1,"pira","scene",""],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","iraicchi",""],["தயிரை","அயிரை",2,"pira","gift_food","ayirai fish"]],"en":[["town","மரந்தை",3]],"dv":[["iraicchi","நாரை","not_confirmed","0/1","even the storks of the shore feed in company, so a town, however good, is misery to the one left to live there alone"]],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:167":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":168,"t":["முளிதயிர் பிசைந்த காந்தண் மெல்விரல்","கழுவுறு கலிங்கங் கழாஅ துடீஇக்","குவளை யுண்கண் குய்ப்புகை கழுமத்","தான்றுழந் தட்ட தீம்புளிப் பாகர்","இனிதெனக் கணவ னுண்டலின்","நுண்ணிதின் மகிழ்ந்தன் றொண்ணுதல் முகனே."],"p":"","r":"The foster mother reports that the young wife, wiping her soft kanthal-like fingers that had kneaded thick curd on her unwashed garment and with her lily-like kohl eyes filled with seasoning smoke, stirred and cooked the sweet-sour stew herself, and when her husband ate it saying it was sweet, her bright-browed face glowed with a quiet joy.","n":"Every detail of the girl's discomfort (curd on her fingers, a dirty cloth, smoke in her painted eyes) is the price of the one word இனிது, and the joy is small and fine (நுண்ணிதின்). The foster mother, who knew her as a child, notices that she now cooks with her own hands (தான் துழந்து).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","ilvaazhkkai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 167","lb":"KU167","poet":"கூடலூர் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"செவிலித்தாய் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","nartaay",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["முளிதயிர்","தயிர்",1,"unavu","gift_food","curd"],["காந்தண்","காந்தள்",1,"maram","scene",""],["குவளை","குவளை",3,"maram","scene",""],["புளிப்பாகர்","புளிப் பாகர்",4,"unavu","gift_food","sour stew"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:168":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":169,"t":["மாரிப் பித்திகத்து நீர்வார் கொழுமுகை","இரும்பனம் பசுங்குடைப் பலவுடன் பொதிந்து","பெரும்பெயல் விடியல் விரித்துவிட் டன்ன","நறுந்தண் ணியளே நன்மா மேனி","புனற்புணை யன்ன சாயிறைப் பணைத்தோள்","மணத்தலுந் தணத்தலு மிலமே","பிரியின் வாழ்தல் அதனினும் இலமே."],"p":"","r":"The man says she is fragrant and cool, like dripping plump buds of the rain-season pithikam packed together in a fresh palmyra-leaf basket and opened out at dawn after heavy rain, her dark body lovely and her broad curved shoulders like a raft on a flood; for him there is no joining her and leaving her, and to live apart from her is even less possible.","n":"The raft simile is the poem's argument: shoulders that carry one across a flood are not left behind. மணத்தலும் தணத்தலும் இலமே says that with her there is no cycle of union and parting at all, so the journey he weighs is ruled out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 168","lb":"KU168","poet":"சிறைக்குடி யாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பித்திகத்து","பித்திகம்",1,"maram","simile",""],["இரும்பனம்","பனை",2,"maram","simile","palmyra"],["புனற்புணை","புணை",5,"pira","simile","raft"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:169":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":170,"t":["சுரஞ்செல் யானைக் கல்லுறு கோட்டிற்","றெற்றென இறீஇயரோ ஐய மற்றியாம்","நும்மொடு நக்க வால்வெள் ளெயிறே","பாணர், பசுமீன் சொரிந்த மண்டைபோல","எமக்கும் பெரும்புல வாகி","நும்மும் பெறேஎம் இறீஇயரெம் முயிரே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man: may the bright white teeth that laughed with you snap off at once like an elephant's tusk struck on a rock on the wasteland road; like the bowl in which bards poured fresh fish, we too reek to ourselves and cannot have you, so may our life break.","n":"புலவு (the stink of fish) carries புலவி (the sulk): she reeks to herself as the bards' fish bowl does. The curse falls on her own teeth, the part of her that once laughed with him, so the anger turns inward.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","uyir","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 169","lb":"KU169","poet":"வெள்ளிவீதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["vekuli","அலை"]],"k":[["யானைக்","யானை",1,"maa","simile",""],["பாணர்","பாணர்",4,"seythi","simile",""],["பசுமீன்","மீன்",4,"pira","simile","fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:170":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":171,"t":["பலரும் கூறுகவஃ தறியா தோரே","அருவி தந்த நாட்குர லெருவை","கயநா டியானை கவள மாந்தும்","மலைகெழு நாடன் கேண்மை","தலைபோ காமைநற் கறிந்தனென் யானே."],"p":"","r":"Let many say it, those who do not know, says the girl; I know well that the bond of the man of the hill country, where an elephant seeking a pool gulps a mouthful of the fresh-spiked eruvai grass the waterfall has brought down, will not come to an end.","n":"The elephant takes what the falls bring it without labour, which suits a love she counts as given; but the line that carries her claim, கேண்மை தலைபோகாமை, can be read either as a bond that will not break or one that will not reach its end in marriage, and the poem's confidence rests on that reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","alar","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 170","lb":"KU170","poet":"கருவூர்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",2,"pira","scene",""],["லெருவை","எருவை",2,"maram","scene","eruvai grass"],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:171":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":172,"t":["காணினி வாழி தோழி யாணர்க்","கடும்புன லடைகரை நெடுங்கயத் திட்ட","மீன்வலை மாப்பட் டாஅங்","கிதுமற் றெவனோ நொதுமலர் தலையே."],"p":"","r":"Look now, friend, says the girl: like a great beast caught in a fish net set in the long pond on the bank of the new fierce flood, what will come of the strangers' suit?","n":"A fish net cannot hold a large beast; it tears and the catch is lost. The strangers (நொதுமலர்) who have come asking for her are the net, and the girl who already belongs to another is what they cannot hold, so her question mocks their effort.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","karpu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 171","lb":"KU171","poet":"பூங்கணுத்திரையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["கடும்புன","புனல்",2,"pira","scene","flood"],["மீன்வலை","மீன் வலை",3,"seythi","simile","fish net"],["மாப்பட்","மா",3,"maa","simile","beast"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:172":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":173,"t":["தாஅ வலஞ்சிறை நொப்பறை வாவல்","பழுமரம் படரும் பையுள் மாலை","எமிய மாக ஈங்குத் துறந்தோர்","தமிய ராக இனியர் கொல்லோ","ஏழூர்ப் பொதுவினைக் கோரூர் யாத்த","உலைவாங்கு மிதிதோல் போலத்","தலைவரம் பறியாது வருந்துமென் னெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"In the aching evening when the bat with its soft flapping wings makes for the fruiting tree, the woman asks whether those who left her alone here are happy being alone themselves, while her heart toils without limit like the trodden bellows of a forge one village set up for the common work of seven.","n":"The bellows is worked by everyone and rests for no one, so the heart is pumped by every hour of the evening. Her question imagines him as lonely as she is and stops short of saying he is not.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 172","lb":"KU172","poet":"கச்சிப்பேட்டு நன்னாகையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வாவல்","வாவல்",1,"pul","iraicchi","bat"],["பழுமரம்","பழு மரம்",2,"maram","scene",""],["உலைவாங்கு மிதிதோல்","உலை மிதிதோல்",6,"seythi","simile","forge bellows"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வாவல்","confirmed","1/1","even the bat goes at dusk to where its food and rest are, yet the man stays away from her"]],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:173":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":174,"t":["பொன்னேர் ஆவிரைப் புதுமலர் மிடைந்த","பன்னூல் மாலைப் பனைபடு கலிமாப்","பூண்மணி கறங்க வேறி நாணட்","டழிபடர் உண்ணோய் வழிவழி சிறப்ப","இன்னள் செய்த திதுவென முன்னின்","றவள் பழி நுவலு மிவ்வூர்","ஆங்குணர்ந் தமையினீங் கேகுமா ருளெனே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that, mounting the palmyra horse hung with a many-stranded garland of fresh gold-like avirai flowers, its bells ringing, casting off shame as the pain within him grows ever worse, he is ready to go there, knowing that this town will stand before her and name her fault, saying 'such a woman did this'.","n":"The madal works through the town's tongue: his shame is to become her blame (அவள் பழி), which is what forces her kin. The garland of avirai is the madal rider's own sign; நாண் அட்டு says shame is killed, not merely set aside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","naanam","alar","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 173","lb":"KU173","poet":"மதுரைக் காஞ்சிப்புலவன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["ஆவிரைப்","ஆவிரை",1,"maram","ornament",""],["பனைபடு","பனை",2,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["கலிமாப்","கலி மா",2,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:174":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":175,"t":["பெயன்மழை துறந்த புலம்புறு கடத்துக்","கவைமுட் கள்ளிக் காய்விடு கடுநொடி","துதைமென் தூவித் துணைப்புற விரிக்கும்","அத்தம் அரிய என்னார் நத்துறந்து","பொருள்வயிற் பிரிவா ராயினிவ் வுலகத்துப்","பொருளே மன்ற பொருளே","அருளே மன்ற ஆருமில் லதுவே."],"p":"","r":"If he leaves us for wealth, not saying the roads are hard through the lonely wasteland the rain has forsaken, where the dried pod of the forked-thorn cactus bursts with a crack and scatters a pair of soft-feathered doves, then in this world wealth alone is wealth, and grace is something no one has.","n":"The doves are a pair (துணைப் புற) driven apart by a sound, a picture of what his going does, but they do nothing themselves, so they stay scene. The verdict is about the world, not only the man: பொருளே மன்ற பொருளே and அருளே மன்ற ஆருமில் லதுவே are built as a pair, wealth affirmed and grace orphaned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 174","lb":"KU174","poet":"வெண்பூதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கள்ளிக்","கள்ளி",2,"maram","scene",""],["துணைப்புற","புறா",3,"pul","scene","dove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:175":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":176,"t":["பருவத் தேனசைஇப் பல்பறைத் தொழுதி","உரவுத்திரை பொருத திணிமணல் அடைகரை","நனைந்த புன்னை மாச்சினை தொகூஉம்","மலர்ந்த பூவின் மாநீர்ச் சேர்ப்பற்","கிரங்கேன் தோழியீங் கென்கொ லென்று","பிறர்பிறர் அறியக் கூறல்","அமைந்தாங் கமைக அம்பலஃ தெவனே."],"p":"","r":"Of the lord of the wide waters, on whose wave-beaten shore of packed sand swarms of many-winged bees, wanting the season's honey, crowd the dark branches of the wet punnai, the girl tells her friend that she will not go about telling everyone 'I do not grieve; what is wrong here?'; let things be as they will, what is the gossip to her?","n":"The friend's advice to hide her pain is refused: the pretence that would quiet the town is what she will not perform. The construction of lines 5 and 6 is loose, and whether she refuses or grants the friend's request turns on how கூறல் is taken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kaamanoy","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 175","lb":"KU175","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தொழுதி","பறைத் தொழுதி",1,"pira","scene","bee swarm"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",3,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:176":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":177,"t":["ஒருநாள் வாரலன் இருநாள் வாரலன்","பன்னாள் வந்து பணிமொழி பயிற்றியென்","நன்னர் நெஞ்ச நெகிழ்த்த பின்றை","வரைமுதிர் தேனிற் போகி யோனே","ஆசா கெந்தை யாண்டுளன் கொல்லோ","வேறுபுல னன்னாட்டுப் பெய்த","ஏறுடை மழையிற் கலிழும்என் னெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says: he did not come for one day or two, but came for many days, spoke humble words and softened my good heart, and then went off like honey ripe on the high cliff; where is he now, he who was our support, while my heart runs muddy like thundering rain poured out on another land?","n":"The friend speaks of her own heart because the lover first won the go-between: his humble words were addressed to her, so his absence is her betrayal too. The rain falls on another country, wasted where it is not needed, and so her heart's flood reaches no one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 176","lb":"KU176","poet":"வருமுலையாரித்தியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தேனிற்","தேன்",4,"pira","simile","honey"],["ஏறுடை","ஏறு",7,"pira","simile","thunder"],["மழையிற்","மழை",7,"pira","simile","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:177":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":178,"t":["கடல்பா டவிந்து கானல் மயங்கித்","துறைநீர் இருங்கழி புல்லென் றன்றே","மன்றவம் பெண்ணை மடல்சேர் வாழ்க்கை","அன்றிலும் பையென நரலும் இன்றவர்","வருவர்கொல் வாழி தோழி நாந்தப்","புலப்பினும் பிரிவாங் கஞ்சித்","தணப்பருங் காமம் தண்டி யோரே."],"p":"","r":"The sea's roar has died down, the shore grove has dimmed, the dark backwater of the ford looks forlorn, and the anril that lives on the fronds of the palmyra in the common ground calls softly; will he come today, friend, the man who, afraid even of parting when we sulked, pressed on us a love hard to leave?","n":"The stilled sea, the dim grove and the hushed anril are all evening's quiet, and the friend sets against them the man's old fear of any parting. Line 5's reading differs between editions (நாந்தப், நாம் நகப், நாம் தம்), and whether the sulk was theirs or his changes the last two lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","maalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 177","lb":"KU177","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",3,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["அன்றிலும்","அன்றில்",4,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:178":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":179,"t":["அயிரை பரந்த அந்தண் பழனத்","தேந்தெழின் மலர தூம்புடைத் திரள்கால்","ஆம்பல் குறுநர் நீர்வேட் டாங்கிவள்","இடைமுலைக் கிடந்து நடுங்க லானீர்","தொழுதுகாண் பிறையிற் றோன்றி யாநுமக்","கரிய மாகிய காலைப்","பெரிய நோன்றனிர் நோகோ யானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man: like those who pluck the hollow-stemmed water lily in the cool field where ayirai fish spread and yet thirst for water, you once trembled lying between her breasts; now that we have become as hard to see as the crescent moon one worships, you have borne a great deal, and I grieve for it.","n":"The irony is in the reversal: he who shook at the gap between two breasts now endures a long absence with ease. The lily-pickers stand in water and thirst, which was his old love: desire even in possession. நோகோ யானே pities him only in form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 178","lb":"KU178","poet":"நெடும்பல்லியத்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["அயிரை","அயிரை",1,"pira","scene","ayirai fish"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",3,"maram","simile","water lily"],["குறுநர்","குறுநர்",3,"seythi","simile","pickers"],["பிறையிற்","பிறை",5,"pira","simile","crescent moon"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:179":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":180,"t":["கல்லென் கானத்துக் கடமா வாட்டி","எல்லும் எல்லின்று ஞமலியும் இளைத்தன","செல்லல் ஐஇய உதுவெம் மூரே","ஓங்குவரை யடுக்கத்துத் தீந்தேன் கிழித்த","குவையுடைப் பசுங்கழை தின்ற கயவாய்ப்","பேதை யானை சுவைத்த","கூழை மூங்கிற் குவட்டிடை யதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hunter: you have chased the wild beast through the noisy forest, the day has darkened and your dogs are worn out; do not go, sir; that is our village, there in the curve of the high hill among the stunted bamboo that a foolish soft-mouthed elephant chewed after eating green clustered bamboo that had torn the sweet honeycomb.","n":"The invitation to stay is given as plain hospitality, a tired man and tired dogs at nightfall, and the village is placed by a landmark only a local would use. The young elephant tasting honey-smeared bamboo has been read as a hidden image of the sweetness waiting there, but no situation in the poem anchors it, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","virundhu","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 179","lb":"KU179","poet":"குட்டுவன் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கடமா","கடமா",1,"maa","scene","gaur"],["ஞமலியும்","ஞமலி",2,"maa","scene","dog"],["தீந்தேன்","தேன்",4,"pira","scene","honeycomb"],["பசுங்கழை","கழை",5,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["யானை","யானை",6,"maa","scene",""],["மூங்கிற்","மூங்கில்",7,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:180":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":181,"t":["பழூஉப்பல் அன்ன பருவுகிர்ப் பாவடி","இருங்களிற் றினநிரை யேந்தல் வரின்மாய்ந்","தறைமடி கரும்பின் கண்ணிடை யன்ன","பைத லொருகழை நீடிய சுரனிறந்து","எய்தினர் கொல்லோ பொருளே யல்குல்","அவ்வரி வாடத் துறந்தோர்","வன்ப ராகத்தாஞ் சென்ற நாட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The friend wonders whether the man has reached his wealth, having crossed the waste where, when the herd of great broad-footed tuskers comes, with nails thick like a demon's teeth, the lone pining bamboo, like a sugarcane joint that has died and fallen on the rock, stands tall; and whether those who left, letting the lovely lines of her waist fade, have grown hard of heart in the land they went to.","n":"The lone bamboo is the waste's emblem of what survives alone, worn like a dead cane joint; the friend's two questions move from the road (did he arrive?) to his heart (has it hardened?), and the second is the one she fears.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 180","lb":"KU180","poet":"கச்சிப்பேட்டு நன்னாகையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["இருங்களிற் றினநிரை","களிறு",2,"maa","scene",""],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",3,"maram","simile","sugarcane"],["லொருகழை","கழை",4,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:9"},"ku:181":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":182,"t":["இதுமற் றெவனோ தோழி துனியிடை","இன்னர் என்னும் இன்னாக் கிளவி","இருமருப் பெருமை ஈன்றணிக் காரான்","உழவன் யாத்த குழவியி னகலாது","பாஅற் பைம்பயிர் ஆரு மூரன்","திருமனைப் பலகடம் பூண்ட","பெருமுது பெண்டிரேம் ஆகிய நமக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks her friend what good there is in the harsh word 'that is how she is', spoken in the quarrel, against women like them who have grown old as matrons carrying the many duties of the great house of the man from a town where a newly calved buffalo grazes the green crop near the calf the ploughman has tied, never moving away from it.","n":"She calls herself and her kind பெருமுது பெண்டிர், the old matrons who bear the house's many burdens, so the sting of the word is that it is said to a wife whose years of service should have earned better. The mother buffalo that will not leave its tied calf is read by some as the hero kept near home by his son while he grazes elsewhere; only one correspondence (staying near the child) can be made firm, so it fails the two-pair condition and stays scene. Who spoke the harsh word (the hero, or someone of the parathai's side) is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kadunjol","ilvaazhkkai","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 181","lb":"KU181","poet":"கிளிமங்கலங் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["காரான்","கார் ஆன்",3,"maa","scene","buffalo cow"],["உழவன்","உழவன்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["குழவியி","குழவி",4,"maa","scene","calf"],["பைம்பயிர்","பயிர்",5,"maram","scene","crop"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:182":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":183,"t":["விழுத்தலைப் பெண்ணை விளையன் மாமடல்","மணியணி பெருந்தார் மரபிற் பூட்டி","வெள்ளென் பணிந்துபிறர் எள்ளத் தோன்றி","ஒருநாண் மருங்கிற் பெருநா ணீக்கித்","தெருவின் இயலவும் தருவது கொல்லோ","கலிந்தவிர் அசைநடைப் பேதை","மெலிந்தில ணாம்விடற் கமைந்த தூதே."],"p":"","r":"The lover asks whether it will bring her to him if he ties a great garland hung with bells to the palm-stem horse cut from a fine-crowned palmyra, wears white bones, shows himself so that others mock, throws off his great shame for one day and goes down the street, since the simple girl of the swaying gait has not softened to the messenger they sent.","n":"The verb is a question (தருவது கொல்லோ): he weighs the madal as a last resort and counts its cost in the mockery of others before he has mounted it. The contrast ஒருநாள் / பெருநாண் sets one day's display against a lifetime's shame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["madal","naanam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 182","lb":"KU182","poet":"மடல் பாடிய மாதங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",1,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["மாமடல்","மடல்",1,"maram","scene","palm stem"],["மணியணி","மணி",2,"pira","ornament","bell"],["பெருந்தார்","தார்",2,"maram","ornament","garland"],["வெள்ளென்","வெள் என்பு",3,"pira","ornament","bones"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:183":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":184,"t":["சென்ற நாட்ட கொன்றையம் பசுவீ","நம்போற் பசக்குங் காலைத் தம்போற்","சிறுதலைப் பிணையிற் றீர்ந்த நெறிகோட்","டிரலை மானையுங் காண்பர்கொல் நமரே","புல்லென் காயாப் பூக்கெழு பெருஞ்சினை","மென்மயில் எருத்தில் தோன்றும்","கான வைப்பிற் புன்புலத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The wife wonders whether, in the land he went to, where the fresh konrai blossoms turn pale as she does, her man will see the stag with twisted horns parted like himself from its small-headed doe, there in the poor forest tract where the great flowering branch of the kaayaa shows like a peacock's neck.","n":"Two similes run in opposite directions: the flower is like her (நம்போல்), the stag is like him (தம்போல்). The season marks both partners, and her hope is that the sight of a parted pair will teach him what she feels. The stag is the subject of her question, so striking it removes the poem's sense; it is not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","paruvam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 183","lb":"KU183","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கொன்றையம்","கொன்றை",1,"maram","simile",""],["பிணையிற்","பிணை",3,"maa","simile","doe"],["டிரலை","இரலை",4,"maa","simile","stag"],["காயாப்","காயா",5,"maram","scene",""],["மயில்","மயில்",6,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:184":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":185,"t":["அறிகரி பொய்த்தல் ஆன்றோர்க் கில்லை","குறுக லோம்புமின் சிறுகுடிச் செலவே","இதற்கிது மாண்ட தென்னா ததற்பட்","டாண்டொழிந் தன்றே மாண்டகை நெஞ்சம்","மயிற்கண் அன்ன மாண்முடிப் பாவை","நுண்வலைப் பரதவர் மடமகள்","கண்வலைப் படுஉம் கான லானே."],"p":"","r":"The lover tells his companions that the wise do not bear false witness and warns them off the little fishing village: his noble heart, not weighing what is fit for what, fell there and is lost in the seaside grove where the young daughter of fishermen with fine nets, a doll with a fine knot of hair like a peacock's eye, catches men in the net of her eyes.","n":"The fishermen's fine net (நுண்வலை) and the daughter's net of eyes (கண்வலை) stand in successive lines: the girl practises her family's trade on men. The opening maxim is his reply to a companion's rebuke: he claims the evidence of his own lost heart as testimony the wise cannot doubt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nenju","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 184","lb":"KU184","poet":"ஆரிய வரசன் யாழ்ப்பிரமதத்தன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["மயிற்கண்","மயில் கண்",5,"pul","simile",""],["நுண்வலைப்","வலை",6,"pira","scene","net"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",6,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:185":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":186,"t":["நுதல்பசப் பிவர்ந்து திதலை வாடி","நெடுமென் பணைத்தோள் சாஅய்த் தொடி நெகிழ்ந்","தின்ன ளாகுத னும்மி னாகுமெனச்","சொல்லி னெவனாந் தோழி பல்வரிப்","பாம்புபை அவிழ்ந்தது போலக் கூம்பிக்","கொண்டலிற் றொலைந்த வொண்செங் காந்தள்","கன்மிசைக் கவியு நாடற்கென்","நன்மா மேனி யழிபடர் நிலையே."],"p":"","r":"The girl asks her friend what would come of telling the man of the land where the bright red kaanthal, closed like a striped snake that has let down its hood, ruined by the east wind, droops over the rock, that she has become like this through him: her brow pale, her spots faded, her long soft shoulders thinned, her bangles slipping, and her fine dark body in a state of ruinous grief.","n":"The kaanthal beaten down by the wind and hanging over the rock is the traditional figure of her own ruined beauty and, some say, of the man as hard as the rock it hangs on; only one firm pair (flower to her beauty) can be named, so it is not tagged ullurai. Her question is a refusal: she will not use her wasting as a reproach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","melivu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 185","lb":"KU185","poet":"மதுரை அறுவை வாணிகன் இளவேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பாம்புபை","பாம்பு",5,"pira","simile","snake"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",6,"maram","simile",""],["தொடி","தொடி",2,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:186":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":187,"t":["ஆர்கலி யேற்றொடு கார்தலை மணந்த","கொல்லைப் புனத்த முல்லை மென்கொடி","எயிறென முகையும் நாடற்குத்","துயிறுறந் தனவால் தோழியென் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that for the man of the land where the soft mullai creeper of the forest field, wedded by the rains with their loud thunder, has put out buds like teeth, her eyes have given up sleep.","n":"The rains 'wed' the land (மணந்த), and the creeper buds like a row of teeth, as if the season smiles at her: union and laughter in the land, sleeplessness in her eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 186","lb":"KU186","poet":"ஒக்கூர் மாசாத்தியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["யேற்றொடு","ஏறு",1,"pira","scene","thunder"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:187":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":188,"t":["செவ்வரைச் சேக்கை வருடை மான்மறி","சுரைபொழி தீம்பால் ஆர மாந்திப்","பெருவரை நீழ லுகளு நாடன்","கல்லினும் வலியன் தோழி","வலிய னென்னாது மெலியுமென் னெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that the man of the land where the kid of the mountain goat, bedded on red rock, drinks its fill of the sweet milk pouring from the udder and leaps in the shade of the great hill is harder than stone, yet her heart, never calling him hard, melts.","n":"Kapilar sets வலியன் against மெலியும்: the man is hard as stone, her heart grows soft, and the same root (வலி) is turned three ways in two lines. The kid nursed full and sheltered in the hill's shade is the care his own land gives its young and he withholds from her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 187","lb":"KU187","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","மென்மை"]],"k":[["வருடை","வருடை",1,"maa","scene","mountain goat"],["மான்மறி","மறி",1,"maa","iraicchi","kid"],["தீம்பால்","பால்",2,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கல்லினும்","கல்",4,"pira","simile","stone"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மான்மறி","not_confirmed","0/2","in his land even the young of beasts are nourished and sheltered, yet he leaves her uncared for"]],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:188":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":189,"t":["முகைமுற் றினவே முல்லை முல்லையொடு","தகைமுற் றினவே தண்கார் வியன்புனம்","வாலிழை நெகிழ்த்தோர் வாரார்","மாலை வந்தன்றென் மாணலங் குறித்தே."],"p":"","r":"The wife says the mullai buds have ripened, and with the mullai the wide field of the cool rains has come to its beauty; the man who loosened her bright bangles has not come, and evening has come, aiming at her fine beauty.","n":"The verb முற்றின is repeated for the buds and for the field's beauty, and then answered by வாரார் and வந்தன்று: the land has come to fullness on time, he has not come, and evening has come in his place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","paruvam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 188","lb":"KU188","poet":"மதுரை அளக்கர் ஞாழர் மகனார் மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",1,"maram","scene",""],["வாலிழை","இழை",3,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:189":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":190,"t":["இன்றே சென்று வருவது நாளைக்","குன்றிழி அருவியின் வெண்டேர் முடுக","இளம்பிறை யன்ன விளங்குசுடர் நேமி","விசும்புவீழ் கொள்ளியிற் பைம்பயிர் துமிப்பக்","காலியற் செலவின் மாலை எய்திச்","சின்னிரை வால்வளைக் குறுமகள்","பன்மா ணாக மணந்துவக் கும்மே."],"p":"","r":"The man says the errand is one of going today and coming back tomorrow: his white chariot will race like a waterfall coming down the hill, its bright wheel like the young crescent cutting the green crop like a firebrand falling from the sky, and travelling like the wind it will reach her by evening, and he will embrace the many-graced body of the young girl with few rows of white bangles and be glad.","n":"The addressee is not named; the chariot images suggest the charioteer. All the poem's speed belongs to the return: the parting is reduced to one day by the man, and the speed of the images measures his longing. Whether he speaks before setting out (a short parting, paalai) or on the road home (mullai) is open, as the colophon variants show.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 189","lb":"KU189","poet":"மதுரை ஈழத்துப் பூதன்தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["அருவியின்","அருவி",2,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["பைம்பயிர்","பயிர்",4,"maram","scene","crop"],["வால்வளைக்","வளை",6,"pira","ornament","conch bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:190":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":191,"t":["நெறியிருங் கதுப்பொடு பெருந்தோ ணீவிச்","செறிவளை நெகிழச் செய்பொருட் ககன்றோர்","அறிவர்கொல் வாழி தோழி பொறிவரி","வெஞ்சின அரவின் பைந்தலை துமிய","உரவுரும் உரறும் அரையிருள் நடுநாள்","நல்லே றியங்குதோ றியம்பும்","பல்லான் தொழுவத் தொருமணிக் குரலே."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks her friend whether the man who stroked her dark curled hair and great shoulders and left to make wealth, loosening her close bangles, knows of the single bell of the stall of many cows that rings each time the good bull moves, at the midnight of half-darkness when the mighty thunder roars to sever the green head of the angry spotted snake.","n":"That she hears the bull's one bell at every step is the proof that she lies awake; the thunder that kills the snake is the night's terror, the small bell its loneliness. His parting caress (நீவி) is remembered as the act that loosened her bangles.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pirivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 190","lb":"KU190","poet":"பூதம் புலவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அரவின்","அரவு",4,"pira","scene","snake"],["உரவுரும்","உரும்",5,"pira","scene","thunder"],["நல்லே றியங்கு","ஏறு",6,"maa","scene","bull"],["பல்லான்","ஆன்",7,"maa","scene","cows"],["தொருமணிக்","மணி",7,"pira","scene","bell"],["செறிவளை","வளை",2,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:191":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":192,"t":["உதுக்கா ணதுவே யிதுவென் மொழிகோ","நோன்சினை யிருந்த இருந்தோட்டுப் புள்ளினம்","தாம்புணர்ந் தமையிற் பிரிந்தோ ருள்ளத்","தீங்குரல் அகவக் கேட்டு நீங்கிய","ஏதி லாள ரிவண்வரிற் போதிற்","பொம்ம லோதியும் புனையல்","எம்முந் தொடாஅ லென்குவே மன்னே."],"p":"","r":"The wife says, look there, what shall I call this: a flock of dark-winged birds perched on a strong branch, having paired, call in sweet voices into the hearts of the parted; hearing them, she would have said to the stranger who left, should he come here, 'do not dress my thick hair with flowers, do not touch me', if only she could.","n":"மன்னே marks the resolve as one she knows she will not keep: she rehearses refusing his touch and his flowers, and the particle concedes she will yield. ஏதிலாளர், 'strangers', is her word for her own husband.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","oodal","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 191","lb":"KU191","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நோன்சினை","சினை",2,"maram","scene","branch"],["புள்ளினம்","புள்",2,"pul","iraicchi",""],["போதிற்","போது",5,"maram","ornament","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","புள்ளினம்","confirmed","1/1","even the birds keep with their mates in this season, while he has gone off like a stranger"]],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:192":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":193,"t":["ஈங்கே வருவர் இனையல் அவர்என","அழாஅற்கோ இனியே நோய்நொந் துறைவி","மின்னின் றூவி இருங்குயில் பொன்னின்","உரைதிகழ் கட்டளை கடுப்ப மாச்சினை","நறுந்தாது கொழுதும் பொழுதும்","வறுங்குரற் கூந்தல் தைவரு வேனே."],"p":"","r":"Told 'he will come right here, do not grieve', the wife asks whether she should not weep now, she who lives wasting with this pain, when the dark koel with glossy feathers, like a touchstone streaked bright with gold, tears at the fragrant pollen on the mango branch, and at this hour she strokes her hair empty of flowers.","n":"வறுங்குரல் கூந்தல், hair empty of flowers, says without saying it that she has not dressed her hair since he left; stroking it is all the touch it gets. The touchstone simile turns the koel into the assayer of the season: gold on black, the proof that spring has come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 192","lb":"KU192","poet":"கச்சிப்பேட்டு நன்னாகையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["இருங்குயில்","குயில்",3,"pul","simile",""],["மாச்சினை","மா",4,"maram","scene","mango"],["நறுந்தாது","தாது",5,"pira","scene","pollen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:193":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":194,"t":["மட்டம் பெய்த மணிக்கலத் தன்ன","இட்டுவாய்ச் சுனைய பகுவாய்த் தேரை","தட்டைப் பறையிற் கறங்கு நாடன்","தொல்லைத் திங்கள் நெடுவெண் ணிலவின்","மணந்தனன் மன்னெடுந் தோளே","இன்று முல்லை முகைநாறும்மே."],"p":"","r":"The girl says the man of the land where the open-mouthed frog in the narrow-mouthed pool, like a gem-bowl filled with toddy, croaks like a clapper drum, embraced her long shoulders in the long white moonlight of last month; today the mullai buds are fragrant.","n":"The poem's whole lament is in the two time-words: தொல்லைத் திங்கள், last month's moon, when he held her, and இன்று, today, when only the mullai is fragrant. The tattai is the clapper used to scare birds from the millet, so the frog's croak is a sound of the hill fields she remembers him by.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","paruvam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 193","lb":"KU193","poet":"அரிசில் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மட்டம்","மட்டு",1,"unavu","simile","toddy"],["சுனைய","சுனை",2,"pira","scene","pool"],["தேரை","தேரை",2,"pira","simile","frog"],["தட்டைப்","தட்டை",3,"parai","simile",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",6,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:194":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":195,"t":["என்னெனப் படுங்கொல் தோழி மின்னுவர","வானோர் பிரங்கும் ஒன்றோ அதனெதிர்","கான மஞ்ஞை கடிய ஏங்கும்","ஏதில கலந்த இரண்டற்கென்","பேதை நெஞ்சம் பெருமலக் குறுமே."],"p":"","r":"The girl asks her friend what this can be called: the sky roars with lightning flashing, that is one thing; against it the forest peacock cries out fiercely; for these two strangers that have joined together, her foolish heart is thrown into great turmoil.","n":"ஏதில, 'things that are nothing to me': the cloud and the peacock answer each other, a pair that has met while she has not, and her heart is shaken by a union that has no business with her. The peacock answers the thunder, so the bird is the subject of the poem, not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","nenju","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 194","lb":"KU194","poet":"கோவர்த்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",3,"pul","scene","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:195":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":196,"t":["சுடர்சினந் தணிந்து குன்றஞ் சேரப்","படர்சுமந் தெழுதரு பையுள் மாலை","யாண்டுளர் கொல்லோ வேண்டுவினை முடிநர்","இன்னா திரங்கும் என்னார் அன்னோ","தைவரல் அசைவளி மெய்பாய்ந் தூர்தரச்","செய்வுறு பாவை யன்னவென்","மெய்பிறி தாகுதல் அறியா தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks where they are now, the men gone to finish the work they wanted, as the sun, its anger cooled, reaches the hill in the sorrowful evening that rises bearing grief; alas, they do not say 'she suffers, she laments', not knowing that her body, like a crafted doll through which the caressing, swaying wind passes, has become something else.","n":"The wind is caressing (தைவரல்) where he is not, and her body has become an object it plays over. The plural verbs make her charge general: the men who go for their work do not say the words that would show they know.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 195","lb":"KU195","poet":"தேரதரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அசைவளி","வளி",5,"pira","scene","wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:196":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":197,"t":["வேம்பின் பைங்காயென் தோழி தரினே","தேம்பூங் கட்டி என்றனிர் இனியே","பாரி பறம்பிற் பனிச்சுனைத் தெண்ணீர்","தைஇத் திங்கள் தண்ணிய தரினும்","வெய்ய உவர்க்கும் என்றனிர்","ஐய அற்றால் அன்பின் பாலே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the husband that once, when her friend gave him the green fruit of the neem, he called it a lump of sweet jaggery, and now, when she gives him the cool clear water of the cold spring on Pari's Parambu in the month of Thai, he says it is hot and brackish; such, sir, is the way of love.","n":"The irony is in the last line: அற்றால் அன்பின் பாலே presents his fickleness as a law of love itself. Nothing she gives has changed; what he tastes has, and the friend's two examples make the bitter sweet and the sweetest bitter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","oodal","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 196","lb":"KU196","poet":"மிளைக் கந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",1,"maram","gift_food","neem"],["கட்டி","கட்டி",2,"unavu","scene","jaggery"],["தெண்ணீர்","நீர்",3,"pira","gift_food","water"]],"en":[["patron","பாரி",3],["hill","பறம்பிற்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:197":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":198,"t":["யாதுசெய் வாங்கொல் தோழி நோதக","நீரெதிர் கருவிய காரெதிர் கிளைமழை","ஊதையங் குளிரொடு பேதுற்று மயங்கிய","கூதிர் உருவிற் கூற்றம்","காதலர்ப் பிரிந்த எற்குறித்து வருமே."],"p":"","r":"The wife asks her friend what they can do: painfully, Death in the shape of the cold season, with clustered rain-clouds that have drawn up the sea's water and gathered, confused and wild with the cold north wind, is coming, aimed at her who is parted from her lover.","n":"Season and Death are fused in one phrase, கூதிர் உருவில் கூற்றம், Death wearing the cold season as its body, and it aims at her alone (எற் குறித்து), as evening aims at the wife's beauty in 188.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","pirivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 197","lb":"KU197","poet":"கச்சிப்பேட்டு நன்னாகையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["கிளைமழை","மழை",2,"pira","scene","rain"],["ஊதையங்","ஊதை",3,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:198":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":199,"t":["யாஅங் கொன்ற மரஞ்சுட் டியவிற்","கரும்புமருண் முதல பைந்தாட் செந்தினை","மடப்பிடித் தடக்கை யன்னபால் வார்பு","கரிக்குறட் டிறைஞ்சிய செறிகோட் பைங்குரற்","படுகிளி கடிகஞ் சேறும் அடுபோர்","எஃகுவிளங்கு தடக்கை மலையன் கானத்","தார நாறு மார்பினை","வாரற்க தில்ல வருகுவள் யாயே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man with a chest fragrant with sandal from the forest of Malaiyan, whose great hand shines with the spear of killing war, that they are going to drive off the parrots settling on the red millet sown where the yaa trees were felled and burned, millet with green stems and bases like sugarcane and dense green ears, dripping milk like the trunk of a young cow-elephant and bowed on their dark stalks; he should not come here, for her mother is coming.","n":"The refusal carries an invitation: telling him where they are going (to the millet field to scare parrots) while forbidding him the house names the place he may meet her. Malaiyan's war epithet is spent on a forest that perfumes the lover's chest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 198","lb":"KU198","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யாஅங்","யா",1,"maram","scene",""],["கரும்புமருண்","கரும்பு",2,"maram","simile",""],["செந்தினை","தினை",2,"maram","scene","millet"],["மடப்பிடித்","பிடி",3,"maa","simile","cow-elephant"],["படுகிளி","கிளி",5,"pul","scene","parrot"],["தார நாறு","ஆரம்",7,"maram","ornament","sandal"]],"en":[["patron","மலையன்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:199":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":200,"t":["பெறுவ தியையா தாயினும் உறுவதொன்","றுண்டுமன் வாழிய நெஞ்சே திண்டேர்க்","கைவள் ளோரி கானந் தீண்டி","எறிவளி கமழு நெறிபடு கூந்தல்","மையீ ரோதி மாஅ யோள்வயின்","இன்றை யன்ன நட்பி னிந்நோய்","இறுமுறை எனவொன் றின்றி","மறுமை யுலகத்து மன்னுதல் பெறுமே."],"p":"","r":"The lover tells his heart that even if it does not come to winning her, one thing is sure: this love-sickness for the dark woman with curled, oiled hair fragrant with the wind that touches the forest of Ori of the strong chariots and generous hand will, with a closeness like today's, have no end and will last on in the other world.","n":"He takes comfort not in winning her but in the endlessness of the pain: the illness (நோய்) itself is promised continuance in the next world, a consolation turned into a vow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","nenju","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 199","lb":"KU199","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எறிவளி","வளி",4,"pira","scene","wind"]],"en":[["patron","கைவள் ளோரி",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:200":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":201,"t":["பெய்த குன்றத்துப் பூநாறு தண்கலுழ்","மீமிசைச் தாஅய வீஇ சுமந்துவந்","திழிதரும் புனலும் வாரார் தோழி","மறந்தோர் மன்ற மறவா நாமே","கால மாரி மாலை மாமழை","இன்னிசை யுருமின முரலும்","முன்வரல் ஏமம் செய்தகன் றோரே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend that even the cool, flower-scented torrent that comes down from the hill where rain has fallen, carrying flowers strewn on the heights, has come, but he has not; surely he has forgotten, though she does not forget; the dark evening clouds of the season's rain rumble with their sweet-voiced thunder, the season before which he promised to come back when he left.","n":"The -உம் of புனலும் ('even the torrent') makes the flood a rival traveller: water from the hills keeps its time and arrives, he does not. மறந்தோர் மன்ற மறவா நாமே balances the two verbs of forgetting in five words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","ninaivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 200","lb":"KU200","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வீஇ","வீ",2,"maram","scene","flowers"],["புனலும்","புனல்",3,"pira","scene","torrent"],["யுருமின","உரும்",6,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:10"},"ku:201":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":202,"t":["அமிழ்த முண்கநம் அயலி லாட்டி","பால்கலப் பன்ன தேக்கொக் கருந்துபு","நீல மென்சிறை வள்ளுகிர்ப் பறவை","நெல்லி யம்புளி மாந்தி யயலது","முள்ளி லம்பணை மூங்கிற் றூங்கும்","கழைநிவந் தோங்கிய சோலை","மலைகெழு நாடனை வருமென் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine blesses the woman next door, may she eat ambrosia, because she brought word that the lord of the hill land will come, the land of tall bamboo groves where a soft blue-winged, sharp-clawed creature that has eaten honey-sweet mango like mixed milk gulps sour nelli and hangs from the thornless bamboo nearby.","n":"The blessing of food (அமிழ்தம் உண்க) repays the bearer of good news, as Kuruntokai 210 repays the crow; the creature that hangs from the bamboo is the bat. Its meal of the sweet and then the sour may be read as a hint of the hero's mixed dealings, but no second correspondence is anchored, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 201","lb":"KU201","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பால்","பால்",2,"unavu","simile","milk"],["தேக்கொக்","தேமா கொக்கு",2,"maram","scene","sweet mango"],["பறவை","பறவை",3,"pul","scene","bat"],["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",4,"maram","scene",""],["மூங்கிற்","மூங்கில்",5,"maram","scene",""],["சோலை","சோலை",6,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:202":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":203,"t":["நோமென் னெஞ்சே நோமென் னெஞ்சே","புன்புலத் தமன்ற சிறியிலை நெருஞ்சிக்","கட்கின் புதுமலர் முட்பயந் தாஅங்","கினிய செய்தநங் காதலர்","இன்னா செய்தல் நோமென் னெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her aching heart that, as the small-leaved nerunji thick on poor ground first gives a new flower sweet to the eye and then bears thorns, her lover who once did sweet things now does cruel ones.","n":"The refrain நோம் என் நெஞ்சே opens and closes the poem, so the simile sits inside the pain; the point of the nerunji is the order of its gifts, flower first and thorn after, which turns the comparison into a story of a love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","oodal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 202","lb":"KU202","poet":"அள்ளூர் நன்முல்லையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","nenju",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நெருஞ்சிக்","நெருஞ்சி",2,"maram","simile",""],["புதுமலர்","மலர்",3,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:203":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":204,"t":["மலையிடை யிட்ட நாட்டரு மல்லர்","மரந்தலை தோன்றா ஊரரு மல்லர்","கண்ணிற் காண நண்ணுவழி யிருந்தும்","கடவுள் நண்ணிய பாலோர் போல","ஒரீஇ ஒழுகும் என்னைக்குப்","பரியலென் மன்யான் பண்டொரு காலே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says her man is not from a land beyond the hills nor from a town too far to see its treetops; he lives within sight, yet keeps apart from her like those who have gone over to the god, and once she would not have grieved for him so.","n":"The two negations measure distance by what can be seen (hills between, treetops out of sight), so the pain is a nearness without contact; the simile of those who have reached the god makes his avoidance a kind of vow. The last line's மன் gives a past that is lost: before, she had no cause to pine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","pirivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 203","lb":"KU203","poet":"நெடும் பல்லியத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",4,"deyvam","simile",""],["மரந்தலை","மரம்",2,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:204":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":205,"t":["காமம் காமம் என்ப காமம்","அணங்கும் பிணியும் அன்றே நினைப்பின்","முதைச்சுவற் கலித்த முற்றா இளம்புல்","மூதா தைவந் தாங்கு","விருந்தே காமம் பெருந்தோ ளோயே."],"p":"","r":"The companion tells the broad-shouldered hero that people cry 'love, love', but love is neither a possessing spirit nor a disease: think of it, and it is like an old cow nibbling the young unripe grass that has sprung on old upland, a feast always new.","n":"The old cow can only lick at grass too short to graze, and gets pleasure without fullness; so the companion's 'feast' (விருந்து) is also a gentle deflation of the hero's suffering. அணங்கு and பிணி name exactly the two explanations, spirit and sickness, that akam poems give for a lover's state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 204","lb":"KU204","poet":"மிளைப்பெருங் கந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பாங்கன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["paangan","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இளம்புல்","புல்",3,"maram","simile",""],["மூதா","முது ஆ",4,"maa","simile","old cow"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:205":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":206,"t":["மின்னுச்செய் கருவிய பெயன்மழை தூங்க","விசும்பா டன்னம் பறைநிவந் தாங்குப்","பொலம்படைப் பொலிந்த வெண்டேர் ஏறிக்","கலங்குகடல் துவலை ஆழி நனைப்ப","இனிச்சென் றனனே இடுமணற் சேர்ப்பன்","யாங்கறிந் தன்றுகொல் தோழியென்","தேங்கமழ் திருநுதல் ஊர்தரும் பசப்பே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend how the pallor spreading over her fragrant forehead came to know it so soon, when the lord of the heaped-sand shore has only now left, mounting his white chariot bright with gold, rising like sky-going swans under lightning-laden rain clouds, its wheels wet with spray from the churning sea.","n":"The question gives the pallor a mind of its own (யாங்கு அறிந்தன்று), so the body knows the parting before she has taken it in; the white chariot rising like swans against dark rain cloud is the last thing she sees.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 205","lb":"KU205","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மழை","மழை",1,"pira","simile","rain cloud"],["டன்னம்","அன்னம்",2,"pul","simile","swan"],["கடல்","கடல்",4,"pira","scene",""],["மணற்","மணல்",5,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:206":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":207,"t":["அமிழ்தத் தன்ன அந்தீங் கிளவி","அன்ன இனியோள் குணனும் இன்ன","இன்னா அரும்படர் செய்யு மாயின்","உடனுறை வரிதே காமம்","குறுக லோம்புமின் அறிவுடை யீரே."],"p":"","r":"The hero says that if even the qualities of a woman so sweet, whose lovely words are like ambrosia, can bring such unbearable grief, love is hard to live with, and he warns the wise to keep from coming near it.","n":"The argument runs from the best case: not a cruel woman but a sweet one causes the pain, so love itself is the cause. The plural அறிவுடையீர் may be his companions, which makes the poem a reply to counsel like the companion's in Kuruntokai 204.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 206","lb":"KU206","poet":"ஐயூர் முடவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அமிழ்தத்","அமிழ்தம்",1,"unavu","simile","ambrosia"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:207":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":208,"t":["செப்பினஞ் செலினே செலவரி தாகுமென்","றத்த வோமை அங்கவட் டிருந்த","இனந்தீர் பருந்தின் புலம்புகொள் தெள்விளி","சுரஞ்செல் மாக்கட் குயவுத்துணை யாகும்","கல்வரை யயலது தொல்வழங்கு சிறுநெறி","நல்லடி பொறிப்பத் தாஅய்ச்","சென்றெனக் கேட்ட நம் ஆர்வலர் பலரே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says many who love her told her he went without telling her, thinking that if he spoke the going would become hard, along the old narrow path beside the rocky hill, printing his fine footsteps, where the clear lonely cry of a kite parted from its flock, perched in the fork of a dry omai, is the only companion in grief for those who cross the wasteland.","n":"She knows the road only by report, yet she pictures his footprints on it; the kite separated from its kind is a companion to the traveller, which may hint that he too is now alone, but the bird only cries, so it carries a mood and stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","idumbai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 207","lb":"KU207","poet":"உறையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வோமை","ஓமை",2,"maram","scene",""],["பருந்தின்","பருந்து",3,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:208":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":209,"t":["ஒன்றே னல்லேன் ஒன்றுவென் குன்றத்துப்","பொருகளிறு மிதித்த நெரிதாள் வேங்கை","குறவர் மகளிர் கூந்தற் பெய்ம்மார்","நின்றுகொய மலரும் நாடனொ","டொன்றேன் றோழி ஒன்றினானே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend she is not one who will refuse: she will be one with the lord of the land where the vengai, its trunk crushed by fighting elephants, blooms low enough for the hill women to pluck its flowers standing, for their hair; with anyone else she will not be one.","n":"The poem turns on ஒன்று, to be one, to agree, in five forms; the last line can be read as refusing another match the family proposes. The crushed vengai that blooms within reach can be taken as the heroine, hurt but still giving, but only one pair can be anchored, so it is a near miss and stays scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 208","lb":"KU208","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பொருகளிறு","களிறு",2,"maa","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",2,"maram","scene",""],["குறவர்","குறவர்",3,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:209":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":210,"t":["அறந்தலைப் பட்ட நெல்லியம் பசுங்காய்","மறப்புலிக் குருளை கோளிடங் கறங்கும்","இறப்பருங் குன்ற மிறந்த யாமே","குறுநடை பலவுள் ளலமே நெறிமுதற்","கடற்றிற் கலித்த முடச்சினை வெட்சி","தளையவிழ் பல்போது கமழும்","மையிருங் கூந்தன் மடந்தை நட்பே."],"p":"","r":"The hero, having crossed the hard hill where green nelli lies on the path and the tiger cub's kill-ground roars, says he does not count the many short steps (of the way), for he carries the love of the young woman whose dark thick hair smells of the many opening buds of the bent-branched vetchi growing in the forest at the path's edge.","n":"The sense of குறுநடை பல உள்ளலமே is unsettled: it may be that he does not think of the short stages of the road, or of her short-stepped walk; அறந்தலைப் பட்ட may mean the nelli has become a charity for travellers. The vetchi at the path's edge brings her scent into the wilderness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 209","lb":"KU209","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெல்லியம்","நெல்லி",1,"maram","scene",""],["மறப்புலிக்","புலி",2,"maa","scene",""],["வெட்சி","வெட்சி",5,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:210":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":211,"t":["திண்டேர் நள்ளி கானத் தண்டர்","பல்லாப் பயந்த நெய்யிற் றொண்டி","முழுதுடன் விளைந்த வெண்ணெல் வெஞ்சோ","றெழுகலத் தேந்தினுஞ் சிறிதென் றோழி","பெருந்தோ ணெகிழ்த்த செல்லற்கு","விருந்துவரக் கரைந்த காக்கையது பலியே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the returned hero that even if she offered in seven bowls hot white rice grown entire in Thondi with ghee from the many cows of the herdsmen of Nalli's forest, Nalli of the strong chariot, it would be little as the offering due to the crow that cawed to bring a guest and so ended the grief that wasted her friend's broad shoulders.","n":"The guest the crow called is the husband himself, now at the door, so the thanks to the bird is praise of his return said to his face. The meal is priced by the two most famous sources of plenty the poet knows, Nalli's herds and Thondi's rice; the poet's name, Kakkai Padiniyar, comes from this poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","pirivu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 210","lb":"KU210","poet":"காக்கை பாடினியார் நச்செள்ளையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["தண்டர்","அண்டர்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["பல்லாப்","ஆ",2,"maa","scene","cow"],["நெய்யிற்","நெய்",2,"unavu","gift_food",""],["வெண்ணெல்","வெண்ணெல்",3,"maram","gift_food",""],["வெஞ்சோ","சோறு",3,"unavu","gift_food",""],["காக்கை","காக்கை",6,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","நள்ளி",1],["town","றொண்டி",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:211":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":212,"t":["அஞ்சி லோதி யாய்வளை நெகிழ","நேர்ந்துநம் அருளார் நீத்தோர்க் கஞ்சல்","எஞ்சினம் வாழி தோழி யெஞ்சாத்","தீய்ந்த மராஅத் தோங்கல் வெஞ்சினை","வேனி லோரிணர் தேனோ டூதி","ஆராது பெயருந் தும்பி","நீரில் வைப்பிற் சுரனிறந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine that they have stopped fearing for the man who, unkind, agreed to leave her as her chosen bangles slipped, and crossed the waterless wasteland where a bee, sucking honey from the single summer cluster on the hot bough of a scorched kadamba, goes away unsatisfied.","n":"அஞ்சல் எஞ்சினம் is bitter: they have given up even fearing for him. The bee that leaves the one cluster on a burnt tree still hungry is what he has chosen over her full love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 211","lb":"KU211","poet":"காவன்முல்லைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மராஅத்","மராஅம்",4,"maram","scene",""],["லோரிணர்","இணர்",5,"maram","scene",""],["தேனோ","தேன்",5,"pira","scene",""],["தும்பி","தும்பி",6,"pira","iraicchi","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","தும்பி","confirmed","1/1","the man who left her full love has gone where nothing can satisfy him, so his leaving gains him nothing"]],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:212":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":213,"t":["கொண்கன் ஊர்ந்த கொடுஞ்சி நெடுந்தேர்","தெண்கட லடைகரைத் தெளிமணி யொலிப்பக்","காண வந்து நாணப் பெயரும்","அளிதோ தானே காமம்","விளிவது மன்ற நோகோ யானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says the long chariot with curved rail that the lord of the shore drives comes, its clear bells ringing on the shore of the clear sea, only to be seen and then goes back leaving shame; pitiable is such love, it will surely die, and she grieves.","n":"காண வந்து நாணப் பெயரும்: the visit gives sight and then shame, and nothing lasting; the friend's grief is a push toward marriage, since a love that only shows itself is one that will fade.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 212","lb":"KU212","poet":"நெய்தற் கார்க்கியன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தெண்கட","கடல்",2,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:213":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":214,"t":["நசைநன் குடையர் தோழி ஞெரேரெனக்","கவைத்தலை முதுகலை காலின் ஒற்றிப்","பசிப்பிணிக் கிறைஞ்சிய பரூஉம்பெருந் ததரல்","ஒழியின் உண்டு வழுவி னெஞ்சிற்","றெறித்துநடை மரபிற்றன் மறிக்குநிழ லாகி","நின்றுவெயில் கழிக்கு மென்பநம்","இன்றுயில் முனிநர் சென்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine that the man has deep love for them, since on the road taken by those who spurned their sweet sleep, they say, the old fork-horned stag pulls down with its foot the big dry leaves bent low and eats only if (its mate and young) leave any, and failing that stands as shade for its fawn and lets the sun pass.","n":"The words of lines 3 to 5 (ததரல், தெறித்து நடை மரபின்) are hard, and who leaves food for whom is not certain; the sense of a stag starving to shelter its young is clear. என்ப makes it hearsay of the road, which is how the friend can promise what he must have seen.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 213","lb":"KU213","poet":"கச்சிப்பேட்டுக் காஞ்சிக் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முதுகலை","கலை",2,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["ததரல்","ததரல்",3,"maram","scene","dry leaves"],["மறிக்கு","மறி",5,"maa","scene","fawn"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","முதுகலை","confirmed","1/1","the man who sees such care on his road will remember his own and come back to shelter her"]],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:214":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":215,"t":["மரங்கொல் கானவன் புனந்துளர்ந்து வித்திய","பிறங்குகுரல் இறடி காக்கும் புறந்தாழ்","அஞ்சி லோதி அசையியற் கொடிச்சி","திருந்திழை அல்குற்குப் பெருந்தழை உதவிச்","செயலை முழுமுதல் ஒழிய அயல","தரலை மாலை சூட்டி","ஏமுற் றன்றிவ் வழுங்க லூரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says this noisy town has been thrown into confusion since the hill girl of soft gait and lovely hair down her back, who guards the shining-eared millet the forest man sowed after clearing his plot, was given a great leaf skirt for her jewelled waist, stripping the asoka to its trunk, and crowned with a garland of the arali that grows nearby.","n":"The stripped asoka is the evidence: a gift that took a whole tree cannot be hidden, and the town reads it. ஏமுற்றன்று may also mean the town is deluded, taking the signs for a god's possession; the subject of the giving is not named, and the reading of the confusion is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 214","lb":"KU214","poet":"கூடலூர்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கானவன்","கானவன்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["இறடி","இறடி",2,"maram","scene","millet"],["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",3,"seythi","scene",""],["பெருந்தழை","தழை",4,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["செயலை","செயலை",5,"maram","absent","asoka"],["தரலை","அரலை",6,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:215":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":216,"t":["படரும் பைப்பயப் பெயருஞ் சுடரும்","என்றூழ் மாமலை மறையும் இன்றவர்","வருவர்கொல் வாழி தோழி நீரில்","வறுங்கயந் துழைஇய விலங்குமருப் பியானை","குறும்பொறை மருங்கின் அமர்துணை தழீஇக்","கொடுவரி இரும்புலி காக்கும்","நெடுவரை மருங்கிற் சுரனிறந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"As grief spreads and the sun slowly withdraws to hide behind the great hill of summer, the friend asks whether he will come today, he who crossed the wasteland by the tall mountain where the elephant with crossed tusks, having searched the dry pool, embraces his beloved mate beside the low rock while the great striped tiger lies in wait.","n":"The sunset and the question 'will he come today' make it an evening of waiting; the tiger that 'guards' the elephants is a watcher of prey, so the road is both a picture of faithful pairs and a danger to him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 215","lb":"KU215","poet":"மதுரை அளக்கர்ஞாழார் மகனார் மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"],["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["வறுங்கயந்","கயம்",4,"pira","absent",""],["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","iraicchi",""],["இரும்புலி","புலி",6,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","யானை","confirmed","1/1","even in drought and danger the beast keeps to its mate, so the man who sees it will turn back to her"]],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:216":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":217,"t":["அவரே, கேடில் விழுப்பொருள் தருமார் பாசிலை","வாடா வள்ளியங் காடிறந் தோரே","யானே, தோடார் எல்வளை ஞெகிழ ஏங்கிப்","பாடமை சேக்கையிற் படர்கூர்ந் திசினே","அன்னள் அளியள் என்னாது மாமழை","இன்னும் பெய்ய முழங்கி","மின்னுந் தோழியென் இன்னுயிர் குறித்தே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says he has crossed the forest of green-leaved, never-wilting valli to bring flawless wealth, while she, her rows of bright bangles slipping, has sighed and sunk in grief on the bed made for sleep; and the great cloud, without saying 'she is pitiable', still pours, thunders and flashes, friend, with her sweet life as its aim.","n":"அவரே and யானே set the two halves against each other, his errand and her bed; the rain, the season he named, comes as an attacker rather than a promise. The poem is spoken under the rains, so the waiting is mullai though the colophon files it under the journey.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 216","lb":"KU216","poet":"கச்சிப்பேட்டுக் காஞ்சிக் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வள்ளியங்","வள்ளி",2,"maram","scene",""],["மாமழை","மழை",5,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:217":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":218,"t":["தினைகிளி கடிகெனிற் பகலும் ஒல்லும்","இரவுநீ வருதலி னூறு மஞ்சுவல்","யாங்குச் செய்வாமெம் இடும்பை நோய்க்கென","ஆங்கியான் கூறிய அனைத்திற்குப் பிறிதுசெத்","தோங்குமலை நாடன் உயிர்த்தோன் மன்ற","ஐதே காமம் யானே","கழிமுதுக் குறைமையும் பழியுமென் றிசினே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says she told the hill lord that if they were sent to drive parrots from the millet they could meet by day, but she feared his coming by night, and asked what they could do for their trouble; he took all she said for something else and surely sighed; love is delicate, and she had spoken only of great prudence and of blame.","n":"She proposes the day tryst for safety and he hears a refusal; ஐதே காமம் admits how easily a word can wound it. The last line is hard: முதுக்குறைமை is mature prudence, and she may mean the girl's or her own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 217","lb":"KU217","poet":"தங்கால் முடக்கொல்லனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["தினை","தினை",1,"maram","scene",""],["கிளி","கிளி",1,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:218":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":219,"t":["விடர்முகை யடுக்கத்து விறல்கெழு சூலிக்குக்","கடனும் பூணாங் கைந்நூல் யாவாம்","புள்ளும் ஓராம் விரிச்சியு நில்லாம்","உள்ளலு முள்ளா மன்றே தோழி","உயிர்க்குயிர் அன்ன ராகலிற் றம்மின்","றிமைப்புவரை யமையா நம்வயின்","மறந்தாண் டமைதல் வல்லியோர் மாட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that she will make vows to the victorious goddess of the cleft hill caves, tie the sacred thread on her wrist, heed the birds and stand for chance words, and still think of him, friend, since he is like life to her life, he who once could not bear to be parted even for a blink and now can forget her and stay away.","n":"The list of rites is everything a woman could do to bring him back, and it closes on the one she cannot stop, thinking of him. The contrast is inside his own past: the man for whom a blink was too long a parting is the one who can now stay away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vazhipaadu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 218","lb":"KU218","poet":"கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["சூலிக்குக்","சூலி",1,"deyvam","scene",""],["புள்ளும்","புள்",3,"pul","scene","omen birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:219":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":220,"t":["பயப்பென் மேனி யதுவே நயப்பவர்","நாரில் நெஞ்சத் தாரிடை யதுவே","செறிவுஞ் சேணிகந் தன்றே யறிவே","ஆங்கட் செல்கம் எழுகென வீங்கே","வல்லா கூறியிருக்கு முள்ளிலைத்","தடவுநிலைத் தாழைச் சேர்ப்பர்க்","கிடமன் றோழியெந் நீரிரோ வெனினே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says her body is pale, her longing is with the unkind-hearted man, her restraint has gone far off, and her mind keeps saying 'rise, let us go to him', saying what cannot be done; but there will be no place for her, friend, with the lord of the shore of thorny-leaved broad-trunked screwpine, if he asks 'and who are you?'","n":"Each part of her is somewhere else (pallor on the body, longing with him, restraint far away, reason urging her out); the only thing that holds her is the fear of being met as a stranger. The phrasing of line 7 is restored from Swaminatha Aiyar's text and its sense is not settled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nirai","pasalai","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 219","lb":"KU219","poet":"வெள்ளூர்கிழார் மகனார் வெண்பூதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தாழைச்","தாழை",6,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:220":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":221,"t":["பழமழைக் கலித்த புதுப்புன வரகின்","இரலை மேய்ந்த குறைத்தலைப் பாவை","இருவிசேர் மருங்கிற் பூத்த முல்லை","வெருகுசிரித் தன்ன பசுவீ மென்பிணி","குறுமுகை அவிழ்ந்த நறுமலர்ப் புறவின்","வண்டுசூழ் மாலையும் வாரார்","கண்டிசிற் றோழி பொருட்பிரிந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend to see: in the woodland of fragrant flowers, where the stag has cropped the new varagu sprung from the old rain to stubble and the jasmine blooming by the straw has loosened its small buds like a wild cat smiling, even the evening ringed with bees has come and those who left for wealth have not.","n":"The varagu has been sown, grown and cropped since he left, so the scene is a clock of the seasons; the jasmine buds opening like a wild cat's bared teeth make the promised season's smile a sneer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 220","lb":"KU220","poet":"ஒக்கூர் மாசாத்தியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பழமழைக்","மழை",1,"pira","scene",""],["வரகின்","வரகு",1,"maram","scene",""],["இரலை","இரலை",2,"maa","scene","stag"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",3,"maram","simile",""],["வெருகு","வெருகு",4,"maa","simile","wild cat"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:11"},"ku:221":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":222,"t":["அவரோ வாரார் முல்லையும் பூத்தன","பறியுடைக் கையர் மறியினத் தொழியப்","பாலொடு வந்து கூழொடு பெயரும்","யாடுடை இடைமகன் சென்னிச்","சூடிய வெல்லாம் சிறுபசு முகையே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says he has not come though the mullai has bloomed: the goatherd who leaves his lambs, comes into the village with milk and goes back with gruel, wears nothing on his head but small green mullai buds.","n":"The season's proof is put on the humblest head: even the goatherd is crowned with the buds the man was to come back by. The goatherd also comes and goes back every day; a reader may hear a reproach in that, but nothing in the poem draws it, so the herdsman stays scene, not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 221","lb":"KU221","poet":"உறையூர் முதுகொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முல்லையும்","முல்லை",1,"maram","scene",""],["மறியினத்","மறி இனம்",2,"maa","scene","lambs"],["பாலொடு","பால்",3,"unavu","gift_food","milk"],["கூழொடு","கூழ்",3,"unavu","gift_food","gruel"],["யாடுடை","யாடு",4,"maa","scene","goats"],["இடைமகன்","இடைமகன்",4,"seythi","scene","goatherd"],["முகையே","முகை",5,"maram","ornament","bud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:222":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":223,"t":["தலைப்புணைக் கொளினே தலைப்புணைக் கொள்ளும்","கடைப்புணைக் கொளினே கடைப்புணைக் கொள்ளும்","புணைகை விட்டுப் புனலோ டொழுகின்","ஆண்டும் வருகுவள் போலு மாண்ட","மாரிப் பித்திகத்து நீர்வார் கொழுமுகைச்","செவ்வெரி நுறழும் கொழுங்கடை மழைக்கட்","டுளிதலைத் தலைஇய தளிரன் னோளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that in the water-play she takes the head of the raft when he does and the tail when he does, and if he lets the raft go and drifts with the stream she will seemingly come there too, she whose rain-cool eyes have corners red as the streaks of a wet jasmine bud and who is like a tender shoot drops have fallen on.","n":"The doubled lines (தலைப்புணை ... கடைப்புணை) move the way she moves, copying his every hold; the third conditional pushes the play to the point of risk, letting go into the current, and still she follows. The bud of the rainy-season jasmine is a mullai thing in a kurinji poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","anbu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 222","lb":"KU222","poet":"சிறைக்குடி யாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["புனலோ","புனல்",3,"pira","scene","stream"],["பித்திகத்து","பித்திகம்",5,"maram","simile","jasmine"],["தளிரன்","தளிர்",7,"maram","simile","shoot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:223":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":224,"t":["பேரூர் கொண்ட ஆர்கலி விழவில்","செல்வாம் செல்வாம் என்றி அன்றிவண்","நல்லோர் நல்ல பலவாற் றில்ல","தழலும் தட்டையும் முறியுந் தந்திவை","ஒத்தன நினக்கெனப் பொய்த்தன கூறி","அன்னை யோம்பிய ஆய்நலம்","என்னை கொண்டான்யாம் இன்னமா லினியே."],"p":"","r":"The woman reminds her friend that the friend kept saying 'let us go' to the festival that filled the big town, and that on that day, here, he gave her the bird-scaring rattles and tender leaves, said falsely that they suited her, and took the fine beauty her mother had guarded, so that she is now as she is.","n":"She turns the blame on the friend: while the friend wanted the town festival, the union happened 'here', in the field, through small gifts of the millet-guard's own tools. Line 3 (நல்லோர் நல்ல பலவாற் றில்ல) is hard to construe, so who 'the good ones' are is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","nalam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 223","lb":"KU223","poet":"மதுரைக் கடையத்தார் மகனார் வெண்ணாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["தழலும்","தழல்",4,"pira","gift_food","sling-rattle"],["தட்டையும்","தட்டை",4,"pira","gift_food","clapper"],["முறியுந்","முறி",4,"maram","gift_food","tender leaves"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:224":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":225,"t":["கவலை யாத்த அவல நீளிடைச்","சென்றோர் கொடுமை யெற்றித் துஞ்சா","நோயினு நோயா கின்றே கூவற்","குராலான் படுதுயர் இராவிற் கண்ட","உயர்திணை ஊமன் போலத்","துயர்பொறுக் கல்லேன் தோழி நோய்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that brooding on the cruelty of the man who went down the long road of forking paths makes her sleepless sickness worse than sickness, and that like a mute man who saw at night a cow suffering in a well she cannot bear the pain and cannot speak it.","n":"The simile has two sufferers: the cow in the well and the mute who sees it and can neither help nor call for help; she is the second, so the pain is doubled by being unspeakable. உயர்திணை, 'of the higher class', marks him as human, set against the animal he watches.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 224","lb":"KU224","poet":"கூவன் மைந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கூவற்","கூவல்",3,"pira","simile","well"],["குராலான்","குரால் ஆன்",4,"maa","simile","cow"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:225":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":226,"t":["கன்றுதன் பயமுலை மாந்த முன்றில்","தினைபிடி உண்ணும் பெருங்கல் நாட","கெட்டிடத் துவந்த உதவி கட்டில்","வீறுபெற்று மறந்த மன்னன் போல","நன்றிமறந் தமையா யாயின் மென்சீர்க்","கலிமயிற் கலாவத் தன்ன இவள்","ஒலிமென் கூந்தல் உரியவால் நினக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the great-rock country, where a she-elephant eats the millet in the yard while her calf drinks at her rich udder, that if he does not forget kindness, as a king forgets the help he received in ruin once he has gained the glory of the throne, then the girl's thick soft hair, like the spread tail of a proud peacock, is his.","n":"The gift of her hair is conditional: it is his only if he is not the ungrateful king, so the friend presses for marriage by naming the vice in advance. The she-elephant eating the household's millet while she suckles has been read as the lover taking freely what is not yet his, but the scene gives only one pair, so it stays scene (a near miss on ullurai condition 3).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 225","lb":"KU225","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கன்றுதன்","கன்று",1,"maa","scene","elephant calf"],["தினைபிடி","தினை",2,"unavu","scene","millet"],["தினைபிடி","பிடி",2,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["கலிமயிற்","மயில்",6,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:226":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":227,"t":["பூவொடு புரையுங் கண்ணும் வேயென","விறல்வனப் பெய்திய தோளும் பிறையென","மதிமயக் குறூஉ நுதலு நன்றும்","நல்லமன் வாழி தோழி அல்கலும்","தயங்குதிரை பொருத தாழை வெண்பூக்","குருகென மலரும் பெருந்துறை","விரிநீர்ச் சேர்ப்பனொடு நகாஅ வூங்கே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that her flower-like eyes, her shoulders lovely as bamboo and her forehead that could be mistaken for the crescent were truly fine before she laughed with the lord of the wide-water shore, where every night white screwpine buds struck by the waves open like herons.","n":"The praise of her beauty is all in the past tense (நல்லமன், 'were good indeed'): the catalogue of eyes, shoulders and brow is an inventory of what she has lost. The screwpine bud that looks like a heron is a thing that seems what it is not; some read the hero's look of constancy in it, but the poem gives no second pair, so it stays a simile.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","melivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 226","lb":"KU226","poet":"மதுரை எழுத்தாளனார் சேந்தம்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பூவொடு","பூ",1,"maram","simile","flower"],["வேயென","வேய்",1,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["தயங்குதிரை","திரை",5,"pira","scene","waves"],["தாழை","தாழை",5,"maram","simile","screwpine"],["குருகென","குருகு",6,"pul","simile","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:227":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":228,"t":["பூண்வனைந் தன்ன பொலஞ்சூட்டு நேமி","வாண்முகந் துமிப்ப வள்ளிதழ் குறைந்த","கூழை நெய்தலு முடைத்திவண்","தேரோன் போகிய கான லானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that here in the seaside grove the chariot-man drove away from, there are even stubby neythal flowers with their broad petals cut off, sliced by the bright face of the golden-rimmed wheel that looks like a wrought jewel.","n":"The poem says only that there is evidence: the lilies cut by a chariot wheel prove the man came to the grove. To whom the friend shows this sign, and why (to tell the heroine he came, or to let the household learn of the affair), the poem does not say, so the situation is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kurippu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 227","lb":"KU227","poet":"ஓதஞானியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெய்தலு","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","scene","water-lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:228":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":229,"t":["வீழ்தாழ் தாழை யூழுறு கொழுமுகை","குருகுளர் இறகின் விரிபுதோ டவிழும்","கானல் நண்ணிய சிறுகுடி முன்றில்","திரைவந்து பெயரும் என்பநத் துறந்து","நெடுஞ்சே ணாட்டார் ஆயினும்","நெஞ்சிற் கணியர் தண்கட னாட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that though the man of the cool sea left us and belongs to a far country, he is near to her heart, in the land where, they say, the waves come up to the yard of the little hamlet by the seaside grove and go back, and the ripe screwpine bud opens its petals like a heron preening its feathers.","n":"She knows his land only by report (என்ப, 'they say'), and what she chooses to repeat is the coming and going of the waves. The far country and the near heart are set side by side (நெடுஞ்சேண் ... நெஞ்சிற்கு அணியர) with no complaint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 228","lb":"KU228","poet":"செய்தி வள்ளுவர் பெருஞ்சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாழை","தாழை",1,"maram","simile","screwpine"],["குருகுளர்","குருகு",2,"pul","simile","heron"],["திரைவந்து","திரை",4,"pira","iraicchi","waves"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","திரைவந்து","confirmed","1/1","even the sea of his own country comes and returns, so his going is not the end of his coming"]],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:229":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":230,"t":["இவனிவ ளைம்பால் பற்றவும் இவளிவன்","புன்றலை யோரி வாங்குநள் பரியவும்","காதற் செவிலியர் தவிர்ப்பவுந் தவிரா","தேதில் சிறுசெரு வுறுப மன்னோ","நல்லைமன் றம்ம பாலே மெல்லியல்","துணைமலர்ப் பிணைய லன்னவிவர்","மணமகிழ் இயற்கை காட்டி யோயே."],"p":"","r":"Onlookers say that the two who once fought small senseless fights, he pulling her plaited hair and she pulling his thin tuft while their loving nurses tried and failed to stop them, have been shown the joy of marriage by fate, which has done well to join them like a garland of paired flowers.","n":"The childhood fights are the evidence of the match: those who could not be kept apart even to fight are now joined by பால், the portion fate deals. The poem has nothing of the paalai land; its paalai filing presumably follows the onlookers' frame of the couple seen on the road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","ilamai","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 229","lb":"KU229","poet":"மோதாசானார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"கண்டோர் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["kandor","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","இளமை"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["துணைமலர்ப்","துணை மலர்",6,"maram","simile","paired flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:230":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":231,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி கொண்கன்","தானது துணிகுவ னல்லன் யானென்","பேதை மையாற் பெருந்தகை கெழுமி","நோதகச் செய்ததொன் றுடையேன் கொல்லோ","வயச்சுறா வழங்குநீர் அத்தம்","சின்னாள் அன்ன வரவறி யானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the woman whether she herself, in her simplicity, did something to hurt that noble man when they met, for the lord of the shore is not one to resolve on such a thing, yet he no longer knows the way of coming, as he did in those few days, over the water path where fierce sharks move.","n":"The friend blames herself (யான் என் பேதைமையால்) rather than the man, a polite way of saying he has stopped coming without accusing him; the shark-haunted water reminds that his earlier visits braved danger, so their stopping cannot be fear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","pedhaimai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 230","lb":"KU230","poet":"அறிவுடை நம்பியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வயச்சுறா","சுறா",5,"pira","scene","shark"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:231":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":232,"t":["ஓரூர் வாழினும் சேரி வாரார்","சேரி வரினும் ஆர முயங்கார்","ஏதி லாளர் சுடலை போலக்","காணாக் கழிப மன்னே நாணட்டு","நல்லறி விழுந்த காமம்","வில்லுமிழ் கணியிற் சென்றுசேட் படவே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that though they live in one town he does not come to her street, and if he comes he does not embrace her fully, but passes by without looking, as one passes a stranger's burning-ground; and her desire, having killed shame and lost good sense, has gone far like an arrow loosed from the bow.","n":"The stranger's burning-ground is a place one passes with averted eyes, so the simile says he treats her as an unlucky sight. The closing turns from him to herself: her own desire has already gone, past shame and sense, and cannot be called back. Line 6 reads கணியிற், where the sense wants கணையின், 'arrow'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 231","lb":"KU231","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:232":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":233,"t":["உள்ளார் கொல்லோ தோழி உள்ளியும்","வாய்ப்புணர் வின்மையின் வாரார் கொல்லோ","மரற்புகா வருந்திய மாவெருத் திரலை","உரற்கா லியானை யொடித்துண் டெஞ்சிய","யாஅ வரிநிழல் துஞ்சும்","மாயிருஞ் சோலை மலையிறந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether they have not thought of us, or whether, thinking, they do not come for lack of a fitting chance, those who crossed the hill of dark groves where a dark-necked stag, weary for want of the marul to graze, sleeps in the striped shade of a yaa tree left after a mortar-legged elephant broke and ate it.","n":"The two questions give the absent man two excuses, forgetting or lack of chance, and the friend leaves both open. The stag that sleeps in the thin, striped shade left over by an elephant shows a land where even rest is a remnant; it carries a mood and does nothing a lover does, so it is scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 232","lb":"KU232","poet":"ஊண் பித்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மரற்புகா","மரல்",3,"maram","absent","bowstring hemp"],["திரலை","இரலை",3,"maa","scene","stag"],["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["யாஅ","யா",5,"maram","scene",""],["சோலை","சோலை",6,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:233":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":234,"t":["கவலை கெண்டிய அகல்வாய்ச் சிறுகுழி","கொன்றை யொள்வீ தாஅய்ச் செல்வர்","பொன்பெய் பேழை மூய்திறந் தன்ன","காரெதிர் புறவி னதுவே உயர்ந்தோர்க்கு","நீரொடு சொரிந்த மிச்சில் யாவர்க்கும்","வரைகோ ளறியாச் சொன்றி","நிரைகோற் குறுந்தொடி தந்தை யூரே!"],"p":"","r":"The man says that the town of the father of the girl with rows of small bangles is in the forest that has met the rains, where bright konrai flowers spread in small wide pits dug up by digging, like opened gold-filled caskets of the rich, and where the rice left after water-poured offerings to the great knows no limit to anyone.","n":"He praises her by her father's house: its leftovers are shared with everyone without limit, which makes her family the generous household the ethical books describe. The konrai fallen into pits like gold in caskets makes the rainy forest itself look rich. Whether he speaks returning or on the way to ask for her, the poem does not say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 233","lb":"KU233","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",2,"maram","simile",""],["சொன்றி","சொன்றி",6,"unavu","gift_food","cooked rice"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:234":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":235,"t":["சுடர்செல் வானஞ் சேப்பப் படர்கூர்ந்","தெல்லறு பொழுதின் முல்லை மலரும்","மாலை என்மனார் மயங்கி யோரே","குடுமிக் கோழி நெடுநக ரியம்பும்","பெரும்புலர் விடியலு மாலை","பகலும் மாலை துணையி லோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that those who call evening the hour when the sky reddens as the sun goes and the mullai blooms as the light fails are confused: for those without a partner, the great breaking dawn when the crested cock calls through the tall house is evening too, and so is the day.","n":"She uses the definition of evening (மாலை என்மனார், 'so they say') as a learned premise and refutes it from experience: the hour is not fixed by the sky but by the absence of a companion. It is a poem about the mutal itself, the time proper to mullai, overturned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 234","lb":"KU234","poet":"மிளைப்பெருங் கந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",2,"maram","scene",""],["கோழி","கோழி",4,"pul","scene","cock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:235":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":236,"t":["ஓம்புமதி வாழியோ வாடை பாம்பின்","தூங்குதோல் கடுக்குந் தூவெள் ளருவிக்","கல்லுயர் நண்ணி யதுவே நெல்லி","மரையின மாரு முன்றிற்","புல்வேய் குரம்பை நல்லோ ளூரே."],"p":"","r":"The man asks the north wind to guard it: near the high rock with its pure white waterfall hanging like a snake's cast skin, the town of his good woman is a grass-thatched hut in whose yard herds of marai deer feed on nelli.","n":"Her town is only a grass hut, but he names it as the one place he wants protected. The north wind, which brings the cold season, is asked to be gentle where she is: the season that makes her suffer is the one he appeals to. Everything he sees in her place is kurinji; the paalai is only in his being away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 235","lb":"KU235","poet":"மரயேண்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",1,"pira","simile","snake"],["ளருவிக்","அருவி",2,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",3,"maram","scene",""],["மரையின","மரை இனம்",4,"maa","scene","deer herd"],["குரம்பை","குரம்பை",5,"seythi","scene","hut"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:236":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":237,"t":["விட்டென விடுக்குநாள் வருக அதுநீ","நேர்ந்தனை யாயின் தந்தனை சென்மோ","குன்றத் தன்ன குவவுமணல் அடைகரை","நின்ற புன்னை நிலந்தோய் படுசினை","வம்ப நாரை சேக்கும்","தண்கடற் சேர்ப்பநீ உண்டவென் னலனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the cool sea, where a stray stork roosts on the low branch of a punnai standing on the shore of sand heaped like hills, to let the day of his leaving come if he has agreed to it, but first to give back the beauty he has consumed and then go.","n":"The demand is impossible on purpose: beauty that has been 'eaten' cannot be handed back, so the only way to settle the debt is not to leave. The friend speaks in the girl's person (என் நலன்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 236","lb":"KU236","poet":"நரிவெரூஉத் தலையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மணல்","மணல்",3,"pira","simile","sand"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",4,"maram","ullurai",""],["நாரை","நாரை",5,"pul","ullurai","stork"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","நாரை புன்னை","not_confirmed","0/1","the man who lodged for a while with the girl and may leave her"],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:237":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":238,"t":["அஞ்சுவ தறியா தமர்துணை தழீஇய","நெஞ்சுநப் பிரிந்தன் றாயினும் எஞ்சிய","கைபிணி நெகிழின்அஃ தெவனோ நன்றும்","சேய வம்ம இருவா மிடையே","மாக்கடல் திரையின் முழங்கி வலனேர்பு","கோட்புலி வழங்குஞ் சோலை","எனைத்தென் றெண்ணுகோ முயக்கிடை மலைவே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that though his heart, which knows no fear and has embraced its beloved, has left him for her, it matters little if the bond of his hands, all that is left, should loosen; for the space between the two of them is very great, and he cannot count what now stands in the way of their embrace: groves where the killing tiger roams to the right, roaring like the waves of the great sea.","n":"The heart has already crossed to her; only the body, held back by its grip, is here, and the obstacle between them is reckoned in forests and tigers. Lines 1 to 3 are compressed and line 2 is a repaired reading, so the exact turn of the argument (whose hands, what bond) is uncertain; மலைவு in line 7 may mean the hills between them or the obstruction itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 237","lb":"KU237","poet":"அள்ளூர் நன்முல்லையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["திரையின்","திரை",5,"pira","simile","waves"],["கோட்புலி","புலி",6,"maa","scene","tiger"],["சோலை","சோலை",6,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:238":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":239,"t":["பாசவ லிடித்த கருங்கா ழுலக்கை","ஆய்கதிர் நெல்லின் வரம்பணைத் துயிற்றி","ஒண்டொடி மகளிர் வண்ட லயரும்","தொண்டி யன்னவென் நலந்தந்து","கொண்டனை சென்மோ மகிழ்நநின் சூளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the fields to give back her beauty, which was like Tondi, where girls with bright bangles play at sand-houses after laying to sleep on the ridge of the ripe paddy the black-cored pestle that pounded green flattened rice, and then go, keeping his oath.","n":"The pestle 'put to sleep' on the field ridge and the girls at play describe a town so secure its work stops for games; that ease is what her beauty was. 'Go, keeping your oath' is bitter: the oath he swore is what he is breaking by going.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","sool","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 238","lb":"KU238","poet":"குன்றியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாசவ","அவல்",1,"unavu","scene","flattened rice"],["ழுலக்கை","உலக்கை",1,"seythi","scene","pestle"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",2,"unavu","scene","paddy"]],"en":[["town","தொண்டி",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:239":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":240,"t":["தொடிநெகிழ்ந் தனவே தோள்சா யினவே","விடுநாண் உண்டோ தோழி விடர்முகைச்","சிலம்புடன் கமழு மலங்குகுலைக் காந்தள்","நறுந்தா தூதுங் குறுஞ்சிறைத் தும்பி","பாம்புமிழ் மணியின் தோன்றும்","முந்தூழ் வேலிய மலைகிழ வோற்கே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that her bangles have slipped and her shoulders wasted, so is there any shame left to give up for the lord of the hill hedged with bamboo, where the short-winged bee sucking the fragrant pollen of the swaying kanthal that scents the hill at the cleft looks like a gem spat out by a snake?","n":"Her body has already published what modesty would hide, so there is no shame left to lose by speaking; the line prepares the move from secrecy to disclosure. The bee at the kanthal, a creature on a bloom shaped like a snake's hood, is a dangerous-seeming beauty; some read the lover in it, but no second pair is given, so it stays a simile.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","melivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 239","lb":"KU239","poet":"ஆசிரியர் பெருங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",3,"maram","simile",""],["தும்பி","தும்பி",4,"pira","simile","bee"],["பாம்புமிழ்","பாம்பு",5,"pira","simile","snake"],["முந்தூழ்","மூங்கில்",6,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:240":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":241,"t":["பனிப்புத லிவர்ந்த பைங்கொடி யவரைக்","கிளிவா யொப்பின் ஒளிவிடு பன்மலர்","வெருக்குப்பல் லுருவின் முல்லையொடு கஞலி","வாடை வந்ததன் றலையும் நோய்பொரக்","கண்டிசின் வாழி தோழி தெண்டிரைக்","கடலாழ் கலத்திற் றோன்றி","மாலை, மறையு மவர் மணிநெடுங் குன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend to see how, on top of the north wind's coming, when the many bright avarai flowers like parrots' beaks crowd on the bush with mullai buds like a wild cat's teeth, at evening his tall blue hill shows like a ship sinking into the sea and then vanishes, so that her sickness strikes.","n":"The one thing of his she can see is his hill, and evening takes even that: it does not vanish at once but sinks like a ship, a slow loss watched to the end. The sinking ship is a neythal thing in a mullai poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 240","lb":"KU240","poet":"கொல்லனழிசியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["யவரைக்","அவரை",1,"maram","scene","bean creeper"],["கிளிவா","கிளி",2,"pul","simile","parrot"],["வெருக்குப்பல்","வெருகு",3,"maa","simile","wild cat"],["முல்லையொடு","முல்லை",3,"maram","scene",""],["கலத்திற்","கலம்",6,"pira","simile","ship"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:12"},"ku:241":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":242,"t":["யாமெங் காமந் தாங்கவும் தாந்தம்","கெழுதகை மையி னழுதன தோழி","கன்றாற்றுப் படுத்த புன்றலைச் சிறாஅர்","மன்ற வேங்கை மலர்பத நோக்கி","ஏறா திட்ட ஏமப் பூசல்","விண்டோய் விடரகத் தியம்பும்","குன்ற நாடற் கண்டவெங் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that while she herself bears her love, her eyes, out of their own intimacy with him, have wept: the eyes that saw the man of the hill country, where the small-headed boys who have herded the calves home raise a guarding shout at the vengai blooming in the village common, without climbing it, and the shout echoes in the sky-touching clefts.","n":"She splits herself: she bears the desire in silence and the eyes break down. கெழுதகைமை is the closeness of old intimates, so the eyes weep out of their own bond with the man they saw, not on her orders. The boys' shout at the flowering vengai is the cry raised to bring its flowers down without climbing; the vengai's bloom also marks the millet harvest and the time for marriage, but the poem does not draw that out, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 241","lb":"KU241","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கன்றாற்றுப்","கன்று",3,"maa","scene","calves"],["சிறாஅர்","சிறார்",3,"seythi","scene","herd boys"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",4,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:242":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":243,"t":["கானங் கோழி கவர்குரற் சேவல்","ஒண்பொறி எருத்தின் தண்சிதர் உறைப்பப்","புதனீர் வாரும் பூநாறு புறவிற்","சீறூ ரோளே மடந்தை வேறூர்","வேந்துவிடு தொழிலொடு செலினும்","சேந்துவரல் அறியாது செம்மல் தேரே."],"p":"","r":"The foster mother says the girl lives in a small village in the flower-scented scrub, where the jungle cock with its calling voice, cool drops dripping from its bright-spotted neck, sweeps the water off the bushes; and even when the young lord goes to another town on work the king sends, his chariot does not know how to stay the night away.","n":"The proof of the marriage is negative: the chariot does not know how to stay away. The king's business, which elsewhere takes a husband away for whole seasons, cannot keep this one for a night. The wet cock of the rainy scrub sets the season of waiting that this household is spared, which is why the mullai uri is here only to be denied.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","ilvaazhkkai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 242","lb":"KU242","poet":"குழற்றத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"செவிலித்தாய் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கோழி","கானங்கோழி",1,"pul","scene","junglefowl"],["புதனீர்","புதல் நீர்",3,"maram","scene","bush"],["பூநாறு","பூ",3,"maram","scene","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:243":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":244,"t":["மானடி யன்ன கவட்டிலை அடும்பின்","தார்மணி யன்ன ஒண்பூக் கொழுதி","ஒண்தொடி மகளிர் வண்ட லயரும்","புள்ளிமிழ் பெருங்கடற் சேர்ப்பனை","உள்ளேன் தோழி படீஇயர்என் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says she will not think of the lord of the great sea shore where birds cry, where girls with bright bangles play at sand houses after plucking the bright flowers, like the bells on a garland, of the adumbu whose forked leaves are like a deer's hoofprint: let my eyes sleep, friend.","n":"The vow not to think is made so that the eyes may sleep, which concedes that thinking of him is what has kept them open. The girls at their sand houses are the play she has left behind, and she places them on his shore.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kan","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 243","lb":"KU243","poet":"நம்பி குட்டுவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மானடி","மான் அடி",1,"maa","simile","deer"],["அடும்பின்","அடும்பு",1,"maram","simile","beach creeper"],["ஒண்பூக்","பூ",2,"maram","scene","flowers"],["புள்ளிமிழ்","புள்",4,"pul","scene","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:244":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":245,"t":["பல்லோர் துஞ்சு நள்ளென் யாமத்","துரவுக்களிறு போல்வந் திரவுக்கதவு முயறல்","கேளே மல்லேங் கேட்டனெம் பெரும","ஓரி முருங்கப் பீலி சாய","நன்மயில் வலைப்பட் டாங்கியாம்","உயங்குதொறு முயங்கும் அறனில் யாயே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that they are not people who failed to hear him, in the dense midnight when everyone sleeps, trying the door at night like a strong elephant: they heard, lord, but like a fine peacock caught in a net, its crest ruffled and its plumes drooping, we lay held, because the unfair mother embraces us each time we stir.","n":"The mother's embrace, the sign of her love, is the trap: அறனில் யாய், the mother without fairness, holds the girl closest exactly when she would rise. The friend answers the man's force (the elephant at the door) with the girl's helplessness (the netted peacock, crest and plumes spoiled, as her beauty will be by the wait).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 244","lb":"KU244","poet":"கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["துரவுக்களிறு","உரவுக் களிறு",2,"maa","simile","elephant"],["நன்மயில்","மயில்",5,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:245":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":246,"t":["கடலங் கான லாய மாய்ந்தவென்","நலமிழந் ததனினு நனியின் னாதே","வாள்போல் வாய கொழுமடல் தாழை","மாலைவேல் நாட்டு வேலி யாகும்","மெல்லம் புலம்பன் கொடுமை","பல்லோர் அறியப் பரந்துவெளிப் படினே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that worse than the loss of her beauty, which her companions in the seaside grove admired, would be for the unkindness of the lord of the soft shore, where the screw-pine with lush, sword-edged spathes stands as a fence like spears planted in a row, to spread wide and come out for many to know.","n":"She ranks two losses and chooses the private one: her wasted beauty she can bear, the public knowledge of his unkindness she cannot, since it shames them both and closes the way to marriage. His shore is one where even the screw-pine stands armed as a fence, yet he guards nothing of hers; that is a single correspondence, so it is not tagged as ullurai. Whether மாலை in line 4 is a row (of spears) or evening, and how ஆய மாய்ந்த in line 1 divides, are not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","nalam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 245","lb":"KU245","poet":"மாலைமாறனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["தாழை","தாழை",3,"maram","simile","screw-pine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:246":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":247,"t":["பெருங்கடற் கரையது சிறுவெண் காக்கை","களிற்றுச்செவி யன்ன பாசடை மயக்கிப்","பனிக்கழி துழவும் பானாள் தனித்தோர்","தேர்வந்து பெயர்ந்த தென்ப வதற்கொண்","டோரு மலைக்கு மன்னை பிறரும்","பின்னுவிடு கதுப்பின் மின்னிழை மகளிர்","இளையரு மடவரும் உளரே","அலையாத் தாயரொடு நற்பா லோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that because people say a chariot came and turned back at midnight for one who was alone, when the small white crow of the great sea's shore, stirring the green leaves broad as an elephant's ear, searches the cold backwater, her mother torments her; yet there are other girls, young and simple, with braided hair and bright jewels, the lucky ones, whose mothers do not torment them.","n":"The rumour is true and the punishment falls on her alone, and she complains to fate: நற்பாலோர், those of good portion, are not better girls but girls with gentler mothers. The crow searching the cold backwater is awake at the hour the chariot came; it is witness and setting, and does nothing a person of the situation does, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","oozh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 246","lb":"KU246","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["காக்கை","காக்கை",1,"pul","scene","crow"],["களிற்றுச்செவி","களிறு செவி",2,"maa","simile","elephant"],["பாசடை","பாசடை",2,"maram","simile","green leaves"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:247":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":248,"t":["எழின்மிக வுடைய தீங்கணிப் படூஉம்","திறவோர் செய்வினை அறவ தாகும்","கிளையுடை மாந்தர்க்குப் புணையுமா மிவ்வென","ஆங்கறிந் திசினே தோழி வேங்கை","வீயா மென்சினை வீயுக யானை","ஆர்துயில் இயம்பு நாடன்","மார்புரித் தாகிய மறுவில் நட்பே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says she knew then that the flawless friendship from the chest of the man of the country, where the vengai sheds blossom from its soft unwithering branches and the elephant's deep sleep is heard, has great beauty, comes to sweet fruit, is righteous like the work of the able, and is a raft for people with kin.","n":"A secret love is praised in the language of public virtue, beauty, fruit, righteousness and a raft for the kin, so the friend presents it as an alliance fit for families. The elephant asleep under the flower-shedding vengai is the hill at rest; it does nothing a person of the situation does, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aram","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 247","lb":"KU247","poet":"சேந்தம் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",4,"maram","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:248":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":249,"t":["அதுவர லன்மையோ அரிதே அவன்மார்","புறுக வென்ற நாளே குறுகி","ஈங்கா கின்றே தோழி கானல்","ஆடரை புதையக் கோடை யிட்ட","அடும்பிவர் மணற்கோ டூர நெடும்பனைக்","குறிய வாகுந் துறைவனைப்","பெரிய கூறி யாயறிந் தனளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says the talk was sure to come: from the day he said 'embrace my chest' it has been drawing near and now it is here, friend; and the mother has learned of it, from those telling big things about the lord of the shore where the sand dune climbed by adumbu, which the west wind heaped up to bury the swaying trunks, makes the tall palmyras short.","n":"The landscape inverts measure: the wind's sand makes tall palms short, and the town's talk makes the affair big (பெரிய கூறி against குறிய ஆகும்). That is one correspondence of size, and it is not clear what the buried palm stands for, so it is recorded here and not tagged as ullurai. Lines 1 and 2 are compressed, and what அது refers to, the talk or his coming, is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 248","lb":"KU248","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["அடும்பிவர்","அடும்பு",5,"maram","scene","beach creeper"],["நெடும்பனைக்","பனை",5,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:249":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":250,"t":["இனமயில் அகவு மரம்பயில் கானத்து","நரைமுக ஊகம் பார்ப்பொடு பனிப்பப்","படுமழை பொழிந்த சாரலவர் நாட்டுக்","குன்ற நோக்கினென் தோழி","பண்டை யற்றோ கண்டிசின் நுதலே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that she looked toward the hills of his country, whose slopes the heavy rain has drenched, making the grey-faced langur shiver with its young, in the thick-treed forest where flocks of peacocks call: see her forehead, is it not as it was before?","n":"The cure is only a look at his hills from afar, and it works on her body at once: the forehead that had paled is as it was. The land she looks at is cold and wet, the langur and its young shivering in it; the pair does nothing a person of the situation does, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","ninaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 249","lb":"KU249","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["இனமயில்","மயில்",1,"pul","scene","peacocks"],["ஊகம்","ஊகம்",2,"maa","scene","langur"],["பார்ப்பொடு","பார்ப்பு",2,"maa","scene","young"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:250":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":251,"t":["பரலவல் படுநீர் மாந்தித் துணையோ","டிரலை நன்மா னெறிமுத லுகளும்","மாலை வாரா வளவைக் காலியற்","கடுமாக் கடவுமதி பாக நெடுநீர்ப்","பொருகயன் முரணிய உண்கண்","தெரிதீங் கிளவி தெருமர லுயவே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his charioteer to drive the wind-swift horses before evening comes, when the fine stag with its mate, having drunk the water in the pebbled hollow, frisks at the foot of the path: for the woman of painted eyes that rival the fighting carp of deep water, and of sweet, chosen words, is in bewildered distress.","n":"He races the evening because evening is her worst hour; the stag with its mate is already where he wants to be. The colophon's paalai is the journey; the poem itself is the return, which is mullai's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 250","lb":"KU250","poet":"நாமலார் மகனார் இளங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["டிரலை","இரலை",2,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["கடுமாக்","மா",4,"maa","scene","horses"],["பொருகயன்","கயல்",5,"pira","simile","carp"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","டிரலை","confirmed","1/1","the man who sees the pair together presses to reach his own wife before the evening she spends alone"]],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:251":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":252,"t":["மடவ வாழி மஞ்ஞை மாயினம்","கால மாரி பெய்தென அதனெதிர்","ஆலலு மாலின பிடவும் பூத்தன","காரன் றிகுளை தீர்கநின் படரே","கழிந்த மாரிக் கொழிந்த பழநீர்","புதுநீர் கொளீஇய வுகுத்தரும்","நொதுமல் வானத்து முழங்குகுரல் கேட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says the dark flocks of peacocks are foolish, bless them: thinking the season's rain had fallen, they danced to meet it, and the pidavam too has blossomed; but this is not the rainy season, friend, so let your grief end; they did it on hearing the thunder of a stray cloud that pours out the old water left from the rain that has passed so as to take in new.","n":"The friend overturns the evidence the heroine is reading: the peacocks and the pidavam are right that it rained and wrong that the season has come. The cloud is only emptying its old store, so the thunder is not the promised monsoon. The mutal is negated: the friend says outright that this is not kaar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 251","lb":"KU251","poet":"இடைக்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","மடன்"]],"k":[["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",1,"pul","iraicchi","peacocks"],["பிடவும்","பிடவம்",3,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மஞ்ஞை","not_confirmed","0/1","the heroine, grieving because she thinks the season of his promise has come and gone unkept, is answering the same false sign"]],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:252":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":253,"t":["நெடிய திரண்ட தோள்வளை ஞெகிழ்த்த","கொடிய னாகிய குன்றுகெழு நாடன்","வருவதோர் காலை யின்முகந் திரியாது","கடவுட் கற்பி னவனெதிர் பேணி","மடவை மன்ற நீயெனக் கடவுபு","துனியல் வாழி தோழி சான்றோர்","புகழு முன்னர் நாணுப","பழியாங் கொல்பவோ காணுங் காலே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend not to be angry for urging her, 'you are simple indeed', because when the crooked man of the hill country who loosened the armlets on her long rounded arms comes, she tends him with a chastity like a god's, her sweet face unchanged: the worthy feel shame even before praise, so how could they bring themselves to blame when they see him?","n":"She turns a lover's weakness into a rule of the noble: face to face is no place for reproach. She calls him கொடியன் and admits the fault in full before she explains why she will not speak it. The situation, a wife urged to sulk over the man's fault, is marutham's, though the man is of the hills.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","saandror","oodal","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 252","lb":"KU252","poet":"கிடங்கிற் குலபதி நக்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:253":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":254,"t":["கேளா ராகுவர் தோழி கேட்பின்","விழுமிது கழிவ தாயினு நெகிழ் நூற்","பூச்சே ரணையிற் பெருங்கவின் றொலைந்தநின்","நாட்டுயர் கெடப்பி னீடலர் மாதோ","ஒலிகழை நிவந்த ஓங்குமலைச் சாரற்","புலிபுகா வுறுத்த புலவுநாறு கல்லளை","ஆறுசென் மாக்கள் சேக்கும்","கோடுயர் பிறங்கன் மலையிறந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine that the men who crossed the high-peaked, crag-ringed hill, where on the slope of rustling tall bamboo travellers on the road sleep in a rock cave reeking of flesh where a tiger has been, are not men who will fail to hear; and if they hear of you lying on the flower bed of loosened threads, your great beauty lost, they will not tarry, even if a great thing is slipping from them.","n":"The comfort is doubled and conditional: they are not the kind who do not hear, and if they hear they will not delay. The road, where travellers sleep in a tiger's flesh-smelling den, shows both how far he is and that he has borne worse than coming home. புலிபுகா உறுத்த in line 6 is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 253","lb":"KU253","poet":"பூங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஒலிகழை","கழை",5,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["புலிபுகா","புலி",6,"maa","scene","tiger"],["மாக்கள்","மாக்கள்",7,"seythi","scene","travellers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:254":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":255,"t":["இலையி லஞ்சினை யினவண் டார்ப்ப","முலையேர் மென்முகை அவிழ்ந்த கோங்கின்","தலையலர் வந்தன வாரா தோழி","துயிலின் கங்குல் துயிலவர் மறந்தனர்","பயினறுங் கதுப்பிற் பாயலு முள்ளார்","செய்பொருள் தரனசைஇச் சென்றோர்","எய்தின ராலென வரூஉந் தூதே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the first blossoms of the kongu have come, its soft buds, lovely as breasts, opened on leafless branches loud with swarms of bees; but the message has not come saying he is here: the man who went in desire of bringing back wealth, who in the nights meant for sleep has forgotten sleep, and does not think of the bed of her hair fragrant with resin.","n":"The kongu, flowering on bare branches, keeps its time; the messenger is the sign that fails. She counts what he has forgotten in three things, sleep, her hair as his pillow and the season, all set against the wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 254","lb":"KU254","poet":"பார்காப்பானார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["யினவண்","வண்டு",1,"pira","scene","bees"],["கோங்கின்","கோங்கு",2,"maram","simile","kongu tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:255":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":256,"t":["பொத்தில் காழ அத்த யாஅத்துப்","பொரியரை முழுமுதல் உருவக் குத்தி","மறங்கெழு தடக்கையின் வாங்கி உயங்குநடைச்","சிறுகண் பெருநிரை உறுபசி தீர்க்கும்","தடமருப் பியானை கண்டனர் தோழி","தங்கட னிறீஇய ரெண்ணி யிடந்தொறும்","காமர் பொருட்பிணிப் போகிய","நாம்வெங் காதலர் சென்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine that on the road their beloved took, gone bound by the lovely desire for wealth and meaning to fulfil their duty, the men have seen, at place after place, the great-tusked elephant that gores the cracked-barked trunk of the solid-cored yaa tree on the path, pulls it down with its fierce broad trunk and so relieves the burning hunger of its small-eyed great herd that walks with weary steps.","n":"The elephant feeds not one mate but the whole herd, which matches the men who left to discharge their own obligation (தங்கடன்). The friend's comfort is that a man who watched that at every halt cannot forget whom he is bound to provide for.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 255","lb":"KU255","poet":"கடுகு பெருந்தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யாஅத்துப்","யா",1,"maram","scene","yaa tree"],["பெருநிரை","நிரை",4,"maa","scene","herd"],["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","iraicchi","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","யானை","confirmed","1/1","the men who saw it on the road will remember the household they went out to provide for, and come back to it"]],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:256":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":257,"t":["மணிவார்ந் தன்ன மாக்கொடி யறுகை","பிணிகான் மென்கொம்பு பிணையொடு மார்ந்த","மானே றுகளுங் கானம் பிற்பட","வினைநலம் படீஇ வருது மவ்வரைத்","தாங்கல் ஒல்லுமோ பூங்குழை யோயெனச்","சொல்லா முன்னர் நில்லா வாகி","நீர்விலங் கழுத லானா","தேர்விலங் கினவால் தெரிவை கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that before he could even say, 'Leaving behind the forest where the stag frisks after feeding with its doe on the soft tendrils of the dark arugu creeper, strung like gems, I will come back when the work is done; can you bear it till then, you with flower earrings?', her eyes would not stay, could not stop weeping their flood of tears, and stood across his chariot's way.","n":"The departure is defeated before it is announced: he has the sentence ready and her eyes answer it first. தேர் விலங்கின, the eyes stood across the chariot, makes tears a barrier on the road. The colophon names no poet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kan","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 256","lb":"KU256","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["யறுகை","அறுகை",1,"maram","simile","creeper"],["பிணையொடு","பிணை",2,"maa","scene","doe"],["மானே","மான் ஏறு",3,"maa","iraicchi","stag"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மானே","confirmed","1/1","the beasts keep to their mates, which is exactly what the man was proposing to give up"]],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:257":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":258,"t":["வேரு முதலும் கோடு மோராங்குத்","தொடுத்த போலத் தூங்குபு தொடரிக்","கீழ்தாழ் வன்ன வீழ்கோட் பலவின்","ஆர்கலி வெற்பன் வருதொறும் வரூஉம்","அகலினும் அகலா தாகி","இகலுந் தோழிநங் காமத்துப் பகையே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the enemy of our love comes every time the man of the roaring hill comes, the hill of jackfruit whose fruit hangs from root, trunk and branch alike as if strung together, low as the drooping thodari; and when he goes it does not go, but stays and fights.","n":"The enemy is not named; since it comes with him and stays after him it is most likely the town's talk or the watch kept on her. The jackfruit loaded on every part is the hill's plenty; a traditional reading takes fruit hanging from the very root as a love too heavy to stay hidden, but the poem draws no correspondence, so it is not tagged as ullurai. Line 2's தொடரி and what it compares are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 257","lb":"KU257","poet":"உறையூர்ச் சிறுகந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தொடரிக்","தொடரி",2,"maram","simile","thodari shrub"],["பலவின்","பலா",3,"maram","simile","jackfruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:258":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":259,"t":["வாரலெஞ் சேரி தாரனின் றாரே","அலரா கின்றாற் பெரும காவிரிப்","பலராடு பெருந்துறை மருதொடு பிணித்த","ஏந்துகோட் டியானைச் சேந்தன் தந்தை","அரியலம் புகவி னந்தோட்டு வேட்டை","நிரைய ஒள்வாள் இளைஞர் பெருமகன்","அழிசி ஆர்க்கா டன்ன விவள்","பழிதீர் மாணலந் தொலைதல் கண்டே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man not to come to their street and not to give his garland: talk has risen, lord, on seeing the flawless fine beauty of this girl wasting, she who is like Arkkadu of Azhisi, father of Chendan whose high-tusked elephant is tied to the marutham at the great Kaveri ford where many bathe, Azhisi, lord of young men with bright swords in rows who live on toddy and the hunt.","n":"Refusing the garland at the street refuses his re-entry. The girl is likened to a chieftain's rich town, so her beauty is a holding like Arkkadu, and its wasting is what the street has seen. Line 1's தாரனின் றாரே divides as தாரல் நின் தாரே; line 5 is compressed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","alar","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 258","lb":"KU258","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மருதொடு","மருது",3,"maram","scene","marutham tree"],["டியானைச்","யானை",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["அரியலம்","அரியல்",5,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"]],"en":[["river","காவிரிப்",2],["king","சேந்தன்",4],["king","அழிசி",7],["town","ஆர்க்கா",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:259":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":260,"t":["மழைசேர்ந் தெழுதரு மாரிக் குன்றத்","தருவி யார்ந்த தண்ணறுங் காந்தள்","முகையவிழந் தானா நாறு நறுநுதல்","பல்லிதழ் மழைக்கண் மாஅ யோயே","ஒல்வை யாயினுங் கொல்வை யாயினும்","நீயளந் தறிவைநின் புரைமை வாய்போற்","பொய்ம்மொழி கூறலஃ தெவனோ","நெஞ்ச நன்றே நின்வயி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the dark girl, whose fragrant forehead smells without end like the opened bud of the cool, fragrant kaanthal fed by the waterfalls of the rain-clouded hill, and whose rain-cool eyes are many-petalled, that whether she consents or kills, she herself has measured and knows her own worth: why tell lies that pass for truth? The heart is well disposed toward you.","n":"The friend corners the girl into the truth: she will not let her pretend nothing has happened. ஒல்வை யாயினுங் கொல்வை யாயினும், consent or kill, puts the man's life in the girl's hands, the vocabulary of a suit. Whose heart is good toward her in line 8, the friend's or the man's, is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","vaaimai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 259","lb":"KU259","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",2,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:260":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":261,"t":["குருகும் இருவிசும் பிவரும் புதலும்","வரிவண் டூத வாய்நெகிழ்ந் தனவே","சுரிவளைப் பொலிந்த தோளுஞ் செற்றும்","வருவர்கொல் வாழி தோழி பொருவார்","மண்ணெடுத் துண்ணும் அண்ணல் யானை","வண்தேர்த் தொண்டையர் வழையம லடுக்கத்துக்","கன்றி லோரா விலங்கிய","புன்றா ளோமைய சுரனிறந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether he will come: the herons climb the wide sky, the bushes have opened their mouths as striped bees hum, and the shoulders adorned with curled bangles are growing full; will they come back, friend, who crossed the wasteland of thin-stemmed omai trees lying across the way, near the vazhai-covered slopes of the Thondaiyar of generous chariots, whose lordly elephant takes the soil of its foes?","n":"The friend reads three signs at once, sky, bush and body: the shoulders that had wasted are filling out, taken as an omen. The return is still a question (வருவர்கொல்) and the road is hard, so the signs give hope, not news. Line 7, கன்றி லோரா விலங்கிய, does not construe with confidence, and செற்றும் in line 3 may mean the shoulders grow firm or are pressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 260","lb":"KU260","poet":"கல்லாடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குருகும்","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","herons"],["புதலும்","புதல்",1,"maram","scene","bushes"],["வரிவண்","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bees"],["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","scene","elephant"],["வழையம","வழை",6,"maram","scene","vazhai tree"],["ளோமைய","ஓமை",8,"maram","scene","omai tree"]],"en":[["king","தொண்டையர்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:13"},"ku:261":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":262,"t":["பழமழை பொழிந்தெனப் பதனழிந் துருகிய","சிதட்டுக்கா யெண்ணின் சில்பெயற் கடைநாள்","சேற்றுநிலை முனைஇய செங்கட் காரான்","நள்ளென் யாமத் தையெனக் கரையும்","அஞ்சுவரு பொழுதி னானு மென்கண்","துஞ்சா வாழி தோழி காவலர்","கணக்காய் வகையின் வருந்தியென்","நெஞ்சுபுண் ணுற்ற விழுமத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that at the end of the light rains, when the old downpour has rotted the sesame pods, even in the fearful midnight when the red-eyed buffalo, weary of standing in mud, lows, her eyes do not sleep, because her heart is wounded with a grief that keeps count like the watchmen.","n":"The buffalo is a field beast lowing in a hill poem's night; it does not stand for anyone, it only makes the hour audible. Lines 6 and 7 (காவலர் கணக்காய் வகையின்) seem to say she keeps the night's count as the watchmen do, so the town's guards and the lover's wakefulness are set side by side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kaamanoy","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 261","lb":"KU261","poet":"கழார்க் கீரனெயிற்றியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பழமழை","மழை",1,"pira","scene","rain"],["யெண்ணின்","எள்",2,"maram","scene","sesame"],["காரான்","கார் ஆன்",3,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["காவலர்","காவலர்",6,"seythi","scene","watchmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:262":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":263,"t":["ஊஉ ரலரெழச் சேரி கல்லென","ஆனா தலைக்கும் அறனி லன்னை","தானே இருக்க தன்மனை யானே","நெல்லி தின்ற முள்ளெயிறு தயங்க","உணலாய்ந் திசினா லவரொடு சேய்நாட்டு","விண்தொட நிவந்த விலங்குமலைக் கவாஅற்","கரும்புநடு பாத்தி யன்ன","பெருங்களிற் றடிவழி நிலைஇய நீரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says: let the town's gossip rise and the street clamour, and let the mother, who rails without justice, keep to her own house; the girl, her sharp teeth gleaming from the gooseberry she ate, has chosen to drink with him, in a far country on the slopes of a cross-lying hill that rises to touch the sky, the water left standing in a great tusker's footprint, like a bed dug for planting sugarcane.","n":"The gooseberry is the point: its sourness makes any water taste sweet, so the girl has already prepared her mouth for the worst water of the road. The friend sets the town's noise and the mother's anger against a choice already made.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 262","lb":"KU262","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",4,"maram","gift_food","gooseberry"],["கரும்புநடு","கரும்பு",7,"maram","simile","sugarcane"],["பெருங்களிற்","களிறு",8,"maa","scene","tusker"],["நீரே","நீர்",8,"pira","gift_food","water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:263":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":264,"t":["மறிக்குர லறுத்துத் தினைப்பிரப் பிரீஇச்","செல்லாற்றுக் கவலைப் பல்லியங் கறங்கத்","தோற்ற மல்லது நோய்க்குமருந் தாகா","வேற்றுப் பெருந் தெய்வம் பலவுடன் வாழ்த்திப்","பேஎய்க் கொளீஇயள் இவளெனப் படுதல்","நோதக் கன்றே தோழி மால்வரை","மழைவிளை யாடு நாடனைப்","பிழையே மாகிய நாமிதற் படவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says it is painful that, while the mother cuts the throat of a kid, scatters millet and, to the noise of many instruments at the forked path, praises many strange great gods who are only show and no cure for this sickness, the girl should be called possessed by a demon, when they have done no wrong to the lord of the high hill where clouds play.","n":"The friend's complaint is double: the gods praised are 'other' gods (வேற்று), not the cause, and the rite's verdict, that she is possessed, is a slander on a love she calls blameless (பிழையேம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","velan","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 263","lb":"KU263","poet":"பெருஞ்சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["மறிக்குர","மறி",1,"maa","gift_food","kid"],["தினைப்பிரப்","தினை",1,"maram","gift_food","millet"],["பல்லியங்","பல் இயம்",2,"parai","scene","instruments"],["தெய்வம்","தெய்வம்",4,"deyvam","scene","gods"],["பேஎய்க்","பேய்",5,"deyvam","scene","demon"],["மழைவிளை","மழை",7,"pira","scene","clouds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:264":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":265,"t":["கலிமழை கெழீஇய கான்யாற் றிகுகரை","ஒலிநெடும் பீலி துயில்வர இயலி","ஆடுமயி லகவும் நாடன் நம்மொடு","நயந்தனன் கொண்ட கேண்மை","பயந்தக் காலும் பயப்பொல் லாதே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that the friendship the lord of the land, where on the high bank of the forest river gathered with roaring rain the dancing peacock walks with its long rustling plumes at rest and calls, formed with her out of desire, even when it bore fruit, brought her nothing she can bear.","n":"The last line plays on பய: பயந்தக் காலும் (even when it yielded) against பயப்பு (yield, or the pallor of longing), so the bond's very fruit may be her pallor. The peacock only calls and walks; it carries the mood of the wet bank, not a sense of its own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kalaviyal","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 264","lb":"KU264","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கலிமழை","மழை",1,"pira","scene","rain"],["கான்யாற்","கான் யாறு",1,"pira","scene","forest river"],["பீலி","பீலி",2,"pul","scene","plumes"],["ஆடுமயி","மயில்",3,"pul","scene","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:265":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":266,"t":["காந்தளங் கொழுமுகை காவல் செல்லாது","வண்டுவாய் திறக்கும் பொழுதிற் பண்டும்","தாமறி செம்மைச் சான்றோர்க் கண்ட","கடனறி மாக்கள் போல இடன்விட்","டிதழ்தளை யவிழ்ந்த ஏகல் வெற்பன்","நன்னர் நெஞ்சத்தன் தோழி நின்னிலை","யான்றனக் குரைத்தனெ னாகத்","தானா ணினனிஃ தாகா வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the lord of the rock hill, where the plump kaanthal bud, its guard of no use, opens its mouth when the bee comes, as men who know their duty open to the worthy they have long known, and its petals loosen and come free, is of good heart: when she told him the girl's state he was ashamed that it should go on so.","n":"The simile is reversed: the flower is the tenor and human courtesy the vehicle, and then the whole picture is turned onto the man, whose shame works the way the bud opens, unforced, once the right one comes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","naanam","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 265","lb":"KU265","poet":"கருவூர்க்கதப் பிள்ளை","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காந்தளங்","காந்தள்",1,"maram","simile","glory lily"],["வண்டுவாய்","வண்டு",2,"pira","simile","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:266":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":267,"t":["நமக்கொன் றுரையா ராயினுந் தமக்கொன்","றின்னா இரவின் இன்றுணை யாகிய","படப்பை வேங்கைக்கு மறந்தனர் கொல்லோ","மறப்புரும் பணைத்தோள் மரீஇத்","துறத்தல் வல்லியோர் புள்வாய்த் தூதே."],"p":"","r":"The woman wonders whether those who could cling to her broad shoulders and then leave, though they told her nothing, have also forgotten the vengai in the garden that was their sweet companion through painful nights, and the message the birds' cries carry.","n":"She cannot accuse him outright, so she asks whether he has forgotten a tree: the vengai that sheltered their meetings is made a witness that he owes something to.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 266","lb":"KU266","poet":"நக்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வேங்கைக்கு","வேங்கை",3,"maram","scene","vengai"],["புள்வாய்த்","புள்",5,"pul","scene","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:267":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":268,"t":["இருங்கண் ஞாலத் தீண்டுபயப் பெருவளம்","ஒருங்குடன் இயைவ தாயினுங் கரும்பின்","காலெறி கடிகைக் கண்ணயின் றன்ன","வாலெயி றூறிய வசையில் தீநீர்க்","கோலமை குறுந்தொடிக் குறுமக ளொழிய","ஆள்வினை மருங்கிற் பிரியார் நாளும்","உறன்முறை மரபிற் கூற்றத்","தறனில் கோணற் கறிந்திசி னோரே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that even if all the great wealth of the wide world came together at once, those who know the lawless crookedness of Death, which takes in its due order every day, would not leave the young woman with small bangles, whose white teeth hold sweet faultless water like the taste of a joint of sugarcane cut at the node, to go after work.","n":"The argument from death, usually a reason to hurry after merit or wealth, is turned into a reason to stay: if life ends on Death's schedule, the hours with her are the only gain. The poet's name, காலெறி கடிகையார், is taken from line 3.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","nilaiyaamai","aalvinai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 267","lb":"KU267","poet":"காலெறி கடிகையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",2,"maram","simile","sugarcane"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:268":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":269,"t":["சேறிரோ எனச் செப்பலு மாற்றாம்","வருவி ரோஎன வினவலும் வினவாம்","யாங்குச் செய்வாங்கொல் தோழிபாம்பின்","பையுடை இருந்தலை துமிக்கும் ஏற்றொடு","நடுநாள் என்னார் வந்து","நெடுமென் பணைத்தோள் அடைந்திசி னோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says: we can neither say 'will you go?' nor ask 'will you come?'; what shall we do, friend, with him who, not minding that it was midnight, came with the thunder that splits the hooded heads of snakes and reached her long soft shoulders?","n":"Both courteous questions are closed to them: asking him to go is unkind, asking whether he will come again is shameless, so the friend's helplessness is a lesson in what the secret stage forbids saying.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kalavu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 268","lb":"KU268","poet":"கருவூர்ச் சேரமான் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",3,"pira","scene","snake"],["ஏற்றொடு","ஏறு",4,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:269":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":270,"t":["சேயாறு சென்று துனைபரி யசாவா","துசாவுநர்ப் பெறினே நன்றுமற் றில்ல","வயச்சுறா எறிந்த புண்தணிந் தெந்தையும்","நீனிறப் பெருங்கடல் புக்கனன் யாயும்","உப்பை மாறி வெண்ணெல் தரீஇய","உப்புவிளை கழனிச் சென்றனள் இதனால்","பனியிரும் பரப்பிற் சேர்ப்பற்","கினிவரி னெளியள் என்னும் தூதே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says it would be good to find someone who would go the long way without tiring and ask him, and this would be the message: father, his wound from the strong shark healed, has gone out on the blue sea, mother has gone to the salt fields to trade salt for white paddy, so if he comes now to the lord of the cold wide shore she is easy to meet.","n":"The message is built out of the household's working day: the father's healed wound and the barter of salt for paddy are why the house is empty, so the fisher family's labour becomes the girl's opportunity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kalavu","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 269","lb":"KU269","poet":"கல்லாடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வயச்சுறா","சுறா",3,"pira","scene","shark"],["தெந்தையும்","தந்தை",3,"seythi","scene","fisher father"],["பெருங்கடல்","கடல்",4,"pira","scene","sea"],["உப்பை","உப்பு",5,"pira","scene","salt"],["வெண்ணெல்","வெண்ணெல்",5,"unavu","gift_food","white paddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:270":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":271,"t":["தாழிருள் துமிய மின்னித் தண்ணென","வீழுறை யினிய சிதறி ஊழிற்","கடிப்பிகு முரசின் முழங்கி இடித்திடித்துப்","பெய்தினி வாழியோ பெருவான் யாமே","செய்வினை முடித்த செம்ம லுள்ளமோ","டிவளின் மேவின மாகிக் குவளைக்","குறுந்தாள் நாள்மலர் நாறும்","நறுமென் கூந்தல் மெல்லணை யேமே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the great sky to pour now and live long, flashing to cut the deep darkness, scattering cool sweet drops and roaring, thunder on thunder, like a drum beaten hard: with the full heart of one whose work is done he has joined her and lies on the soft pillow of her fragrant hair that smells of fresh short-stemmed blue lilies.","n":"The rain that in the waiting poems is the man's broken promise is here blessed: the same storm, once he is home, becomes a canopy for the union.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","mazhai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 270","lb":"KU270","poet":"பாண்டியன் பன்னாடுதந்தான்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["முரசின்","முரசு",3,"parai","simile","drum"],["குவளைக்","குவளை",6,"maram","ornament","blue lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:271":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":272,"t":["அருவி யன்ன பருவறை சிதறி","யாறுநிறை பகரு நாடனைத் தேறி","உற்றது மன்னு மொருநாள் மற்றது","தவப்பன் னாள்தோள் மயங்கி","வௌவும் பண்பின் நோயா கின்றே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that trusting the lord of the land, where the clouds scatter rain like waterfalls and the rivers run full, what happened was a single day, but it has become a disease that for very many days confuses her shoulders and seizes their beauty.","n":"The arithmetic carries the poem: ஒருநாள் against தவப்பன்னாள், one day of union against very many days of wasting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kalaviyal","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 271","lb":"KU271","poet":"அழிசி நச்சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["யாறுநிறை","யாறு",2,"pira","scene","river"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:272":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":273,"t":["தீண்டலும் இயைவது கொல்லோ மாண்ட","வில்லுடை வீளையர் கல்லிடு பெடுத்த","நனந்தலைக் கானத் தினந்தலைப் பிரிந்த","புன்கண் மடமா னேர்படத் தன்னையர்","சிலைமாண் கடுவிசைக் கலைநிறத் தழுத்திக்","குருதியொடு பறித்த செங்கோல் வாளி","மாறுகொண் டன்ன வுண்கண்","நாறிருங் கூந்தற் கொடிச்சி தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The man asks whether it will even be possible to touch the shoulders of the hill girl with dark fragrant hair, whose kohl-lined eyes are like the red-shafted arrow her brothers, when the sad young doe parted from its herd came in sight in the wide forest where whistling bowmen raised their din, drove with the bow's full force into the stag's side and drew out with its blood.","n":"The arrow that is her eye belongs to her brothers, so the look that wounds him is also a weapon of her kin. Read as a hidden comparison (the stag shot near the stray doe as the suitor, the brothers as her guardians) it would be a near miss: the eye is the stated tenor, and the second pair is not anchored, so it is not tagged ullurai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 272","lb":"KU272","poet":"ஒருசிறைப் பெரியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வீளையர்","வீளையர்",2,"seythi","scene","bowmen"],["மடமா","மான்",4,"maa","scene","doe"],["கலைநிறத்","கலை",5,"maa","simile","stag"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:273":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":274,"t":["அல்குறு பொழுதில் தாதுமுகை தயங்கப்","பெருங்காட் டுளரும் அசைவளி போலத்","தண்ணிய கமழும் ஒண்ணுத லோயே","நொந்தனை யாயிற் கண்டது மொழிவல்","பெருந்தேன் கண்படு வரையின் முதுமால்","பறியா தேறிய மடவோன் போல","ஏமாந் தன்றிவ் வுலகம்","நாமுளே மாகப் பிரியலன் தெளிமே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the bright-browed girl, cool and fragrant as the gentle evening wind that stirs the great forest so that pollen and buds tremble, that if she is hurt she will tell what she has seen: this world has been fooled like the simpleton who climbed the old cliff of the great honeycomb without a ladder; while we are here he will not leave, be sure of it.","n":"The friend laughs the rumour down: those who said he would go are like a man who climbs for honey with no way to reach it. முதுமால் பறியாது is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thelivu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 273","lb":"KU273","poet":"சிறைக்குடி யாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["தாதுமுகை","தாது முகை",1,"maram","simile","pollen and buds"],["அசைவளி","வளி",2,"pira","simile","wind"],["பெருந்தேன்","தேன்",5,"pira","simile","honeycomb"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:274":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":275,"t":["புறவுப் புறத்தன்ன புன்கா லுகாஅய்க்","காசினை யன்ன நளிகனி யுதிர","விடுகணை வில்லொடு பற்றிக் கோடிவர்பு","வருநர்ப் பார்க்கும் வன்கண் ஆடவர்","நீர்நசை வேட்கையி னார்மென்று தணியும்","இன்னாக் கானமும் இனிய பொன்னொடு","மணிமிடை யல்குல் மடந்தை","அணிமுலை யாக முயகினஞ் செலினே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that even the cruel forest, where the ukaay with thin stems like a dove's back drops its dense fruit like coins, where hard-eyed men climb its branches with bow and arrow to watch for travellers, and where the parched chew the fruit to ease their thirst, will be sweet if he goes holding the jewelled bosom of the girl whose girdle is set with gold and gems.","n":"Line 8 decides whether he means to take her with him: our text and tamilvu read 'embracing' (முயகினம் / முயங்கினம்), sangamtamil.com 'thinking of' (உள்கினம்), which would make it a lone journey sweetened by memory.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["udanpokku","pirivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 274","lb":"KU274","poet":"உருத்திரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["புறவுப்","புறவு",1,"pul","simile","dove"],["லுகாஅய்க்","உகாய்",1,"maram","scene","ukaay tree"],["நளிகனி","கனி",2,"maram","simile","fruit"],["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",4,"seythi","scene","highwaymen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:275":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":276,"t":["முல்லை யூர்ந்த கல்லுய ரேறிக்","கண்டனம் வருகஞ் சென்மோ தோழி","எல்லூர்ச் சேர்தரும் ஏறுடை யினத்துப்","புல்லார் நல்லான் பூண்மணி கொல்லோ","செய்வினை முடித்த செம்ம லுள்ளமொடு","வல்வில் இளையர் பக்கம் போற்ற","ஈர்மணற் காட்டாறு வரூஉம்","தேர்மணி கொல்லாண் டியம்பிய வுளவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says: let us climb the high rock overgrown with mullai and look, friend; is that the bell of a good grass-fed cow of the bull herd coming home to town at dusk, or are those the bells of the chariot of the man who, with the full heart of one whose work is done, comes down the wet-sand forest stream with young bowmen guarding his side?","n":"The two bells, the cow's and the chariot's, are the same sound at the same hour; the friend's question keeps hope and doubt together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 275","lb":"KU275","poet":"ஒக்கூர் மாசாத்தியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",1,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["ஏறுடை","ஏறு",3,"maa","scene","bull"],["நல்லான்","நல் ஆன்",4,"maa","scene","cow"],["இளையர்","இளையர்",6,"seythi","scene","young soldiers"],["காட்டாறு","காட்டாறு",7,"pira","scene","forest stream"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:276":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":277,"t":["பணைத்தோட் குறுமகள் பாவை தைஇயும்","பஞ்சாய்ப் பள்ளஞ் சூழ்ந்து மற்றிவள்","உருத்தெழு வனமுலை ஒளிபெற எழுதிய","தொய்யில் காப்போர் அறிதலும் அறியார்","முறையுடை யரசன் செங்கோல் அவையத்","தியான்றற் கடவின் யாங்கா வதுகொல்","பெரிதும் பேதை மன்ற","அளிதோ னேயிவ் வழுங்கல் ஊரே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the guards who watch the bright thoyyil painted on the rising breasts of the slim-shouldered girl, as she makes dolls and wanders round the reed pond, do not even know; if he were to take his case to the just king's court of the red sceptre what would become of her? She is truly very simple, and this clamouring town is to be pitied.","n":"He imagines suing a child at law: the king's court of justice is the only authority he can think of for a harm that has no culprit, and he drops the idea at once because she does not know what she has done.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sengol","pedhaimai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 276","lb":"KU276","poet":"கோழிக் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["பஞ்சாய்ப்","பஞ்சாய்",2,"maram","scene","reed"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:277":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":278,"t":["ஆசில் தெருவில் நாயில் வியன்கடைச்","செந்நெல் அமலை வெண்மை வெள்ளிழுது","ஓரிற் பிச்சை ஆர மாந்தி","அற்சிர வெய்ய வெப்பத் தண்ணீர்","சேமச் செப்பிற் பெறீஇயரோ நீயே","மின்னிடை நடுங்கும் கடைப்பெயல் வாடை","எக்கால் வருவ தென்றி","அக்கால் வருவரெங் காத லோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend blesses the mendicant who eats his fill of red rice and white butter begged at a single house, at a wide doorway with no dog in a clean street, that he may get hot water in a vessel in the cold season; he asks when the north wind of the last rains will come, the wind at which she of the lightning-slim waist trembles, and the friend answers that when it comes their beloved will come.","n":"The blessing is precise: a man who lives on alms in the cold season would want hot water most. Whether என்றி means 'you ask' or 'you said' decides whether the mendicant brought the promise or only the question.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 277","lb":"KU277","poet":"ஓரிற் பிச்சையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நாயில்","நாய்",1,"maa","absent","dog"],["செந்நெல்","செந்நெல்",2,"unavu","gift_food","red rice"],["வெள்ளிழுது","இழுது",2,"unavu","gift_food","butter"],["தண்ணீர்","தண்ணீர்",4,"pira","gift_food","hot water"],["வாடை","வாடை",6,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:278":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":279,"t":["உறுவளி உளரிய அந்தளிர் மாஅத்து","முறிகண் டன்ன மெல்லென் சீறடிச்","சிறுபசும் பாவையும் எம்மும் உள்ளார்","கொடியர் வாழி தோழி கடுவன்","ஊழுறு தீங்கனி உதிர்ப்பக் கீழிருந்","தேற்பன ஏற்பன உண்ணும்","பார்ப்புடை மந்திய மலையிறந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says they think neither of her small soft feet, like the tender shoots of the lovely mango stirred by a strong wind, nor of the small green doll; cruel are they, friend, who crossed the mountain where the male monkey shakes down the sweet ripe fruit and the mother monkey with her young sits below and eats all she can catch.","n":"The small green doll (சிறுபசும் பாவை) is the girl's plaything, but read with the monkey's young it may also be a child, which would make this a wife's poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 278","lb":"KU278","poet":"பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மாஅத்து","மா",1,"maram","simile","mango"],["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",4,"maa","iraicchi","male monkey"],["தீங்கனி","கனி",5,"maram","gift_food","fruit"],["மந்திய","மந்தி",7,"maa","iraicchi","female monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கடுவன்","confirmed","1/1","even the monkey on the mountain he crossed provides for its mate and young, while he who passed it does not think of her"]],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:279":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":280,"t":["திரிமருப் பெருமை யிருணிற மையான்","வரிமிடறு யாத்த பகுவாய்த் தெண்மணி","புலம்புகொள் யாமத் தியங்குதொ றிசைக்கும்","இதுபொழு தாகவும் வாரார் கொல்லோ","மழைகழூஉ மறந்த மாயிருந் துறுகல்","துகள்சூழ் யானையிற் பொலியத் தோன்றும்","இரும்பல் குன்றம் போகித்","திருந்திறைப் பணைத்தோள் உள்ளா தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks whether, even at this hour, when the clear bell tied to the striped neck of the dark ram with twisted horns rings at every step in the lonely night, he will not come, he who went past many great hills where a huge boulder the rain has forgotten to wash shines like an elephant covered in dust, and did not think of her well-jointed shoulders.","n":"Two places at one hour: her village, where the flock's bell keeps ringing at night, and his road, where the rain has failed; the season she was promised has come at home but not on the hills he crossed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","iravu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 279","lb":"KU279","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மையான்","மை யாடு",1,"maa","scene","ram"],["துறுகல்","துறுகல்",5,"pira","simile","boulder"],["யானையிற்","யானை",6,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:280":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":281,"t":["கேளிர் வாழியோ கேளிர் நாளுமென்","நெஞ்சுபிணிக் கொண்ட அஞ்சி லோதிப்","பெருந்தோட் குறுமகள் சிறுமெல் லாகம்","ஒருநாள் புணரப் புணரின்","அரைநாள் வாழ்க்கையும் வேண்டலென் யானே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his companions, long may they live, that if he could join for one day with the young woman of broad shoulders, small soft body and fine thin tresses who binds his heart every day, he would not want even half a day of life after.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 280","lb":"KU280","poet":"நக்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:14"},"ku:281":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":282,"t":["வெண்மணற் பொதுளிய பைங்காற் கருக்கின்","கொம்மைப் போந்தைக் குடுமி வெண்தோட்","டத்த வேம்பி னமலை வான்பூச்","சுரியா ருளைத்தலை பொலியச் சூடிக்","குன்றுதலை மணந்த கானம்","சென்றனர் கொல்லோ சேயிழை நமரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her bright-jewelled friend whether their man has gone into the forest where hills crowd together, his curly head bright with the white leaf from the crown of the palmyra that grows thick on white sand and with the pale flowers of the neem.","n":"She sees him in his going-out dress, palmyra leaf and neem flowers on his head, the kind of adornment worn by men setting out as warriors; the whole poem is one picture and one question, and the question (கொல்லோ) lets her hold off knowing. உளைத்தலை could also be the tufted head of a horse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 281","lb":"KU281","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வெண்மணற்","மணல்",1,"pira","scene","sand"],["போந்தைக்","போந்தை",2,"maram","ornament","palmyra"],["வேம்பி","வேம்பு",3,"maram","ornament","neem"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:282":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":283,"t":["செவ்விகொள் வரகின் செஞ்சுவற் கலித்த","கௌவை நாற்றின் காரிரு ளோரிலை","நவ்வி நாண்மறி கவ்விக் கடன்கழிக்கும்","காரெதிர் தண்புனங் காணிற் கைவளை","நீர்திகழ் சிலம்பின் ஓராங் கவிழ்ந்த","வெண்கூ தாளத் தந்தூம்பு புதுமலர்","ஆர்கழல் புகுவ போலச்","சோர்குவ வல்ல என்பர்கொல் நமரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether their man, seeing the cool clearing turning toward the rains, where the young fawn of the deer nibbles the dark leaf of the seedling sprung on the red ground of ripe millet, will not say that her bangles must be slipping off like the new hollow flowers of white koothalam dropping from their stems on the water-bright slope.","n":"The friend hopes the season itself will speak for the woman: the land he crosses shows him the rains, and she wants him to read her slipping bangles there. Line 7 (ஆர்கழல் புகுவ) is hard to construe; the falling-flower sense is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 282","lb":"KU282","poet":"நாகம் போத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வரகின்","வரகு",1,"maram","scene","millet"],["நவ்வி","நவ்வி",3,"maa","scene","deer"],["நாண்மறி","மறி",3,"maa","scene","fawn"],["கைவளை","வளை",4,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["சிலம்பின்","சிலம்பு",5,"pira","scene","hill slope"],["கூதாளத்","கூதாளம்",6,"maram","simile","koothalam"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:283":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":284,"t":["உள்ளது சிதைப்போர் உளரெனப் படாஅர்","இல்லோர் வாழ்க்கை இரவினும் இளிவெனச்","சொல்லிய வன்மை தெளியக் காட்டிச்","சென்றனர் வாழி தோழி யென்றும்","கூற்றத் தன்ன கொலைவேல் மறவர்","ஆற்றிருந் தல்கி வழங்குநர்ச் செகுத்த","படுமுடை பருந்துபார்த் திருக்கும்","நெடுமூ திடைய நீரில் ஆறே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that he left after showing her plainly the force of what he had said, that those who waste what they have are not counted as men and that the life of the poor is more shameful even than begging, and went by the waterless road through the long old waste where a hawk watches the reeking flesh of travellers killed by bandits whose spears kill like Death.","n":"She quotes his reasons almost as maxims and then sets them against the road: the argument that sent him is clear (தெளியக் காட்டி), and what waits on the road is a hawk over the bodies of men robbed of what they carried. The poem does not say whether she accepts the argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","inmai","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 283","lb":"KU283","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["மறவர்","மறவர்",5,"seythi","scene","bandits"],["பருந்துபார்த்","பருந்து",7,"pul","scene","hawk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:284":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":285,"t":["பொருத யானைப் புகர்முகங் கடுப்ப","மன்றத் துறுகல் மீமிசைப் பலவுடன்","ஒண்செங் காந்தள் அவிழும் நாடன்","அறவ னாயினும் அல்ல னாயினும்","நம்மே சுவரோ தம்மிலர் கொல்லோ","வரையிற் றாழ்ந்த வால்வெள் ளருவி","கொன்னிலைக் குரம்பையி னிழிதரும்","இன்னா திருந்தவிச் சிறுகுடி யோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that, whether the man of the land where many bright red kaanthal open together on the boulder of the village common like the spotted face of a fighting elephant proves good or not, the people of this small village, living wretchedly in their huts below the white waterfall that drops from the hill, will blame us, or care nothing for us.","n":"Line 5 (நம்மே சுவரோ தம்மிலர் கொல்லோ) is hard to divide, and the reading 'will they blame us' is a guess; what is clear is the friend weighing the lover's worth against the village's judgment, and finding the village careless either way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 284","lb":"KU284","poet":"மிளைவேள் தித்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானைப்","யானை",1,"maa","simile","elephant"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",3,"maram","scene","kaanthal"],["ளருவி","அருவி",6,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:285":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":286,"t":["வைகல் வைகல் வைகவும் வாரார்","எல்லா எல்லை எல்லையுந் தோன்றார்","யாண்டுளர் கொல்லோ தோழி ஈண்டிவர்","சொல்லிய பருவமோ இதுவே பல்லூழ்","புன்புறப் பெடையொடு பயிரி யின்புற","இமைக்கண் ஏதா கின்றோ ஞெமைத்தலை","ஊனசைஇ யொருபருந் திருக்கும்","வானுயர் பிறங்கல் மலையிறந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend where he can be, who crossed the sky-high mountains where a lone hawk sits on the top of the nyemai tree craving flesh: day after day passes and he does not come, every limit of time passes and he does not appear, though this is the season he named; is the bird calling again and again to its dull-backed mate for delight nothing to his eyes?","n":"The repeated words of lines 1 and 2 (வைகல் வைகல் வைகவும், எல்லா எல்லை எல்லையும்) make the days pile up in the sound. Lines 5 and 6 are hard; the bird and its mate are read as the season's sign the man ignores. The lone hawk waiting for flesh is a mood of the road, not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 285","lb":"KU285","poet":"பூதத் தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பெடையொடு","பெடை",5,"pul","iraicchi","hen-bird"],["ஞெமைத்தலை","ஞெமை",6,"maram","scene","nyemai tree"],["யொருபருந்","பருந்து",7,"pul","scene","hawk"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பெடையொடு","confirmed","1/1","at this season even birds call their mates, yet he does not come to her"]],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:286":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":287,"t":["உள்ளிக் காண்பென் போல்வன் முள்ளெயிற்","றமிழ்த மூறும்அஞ் செவ்வாய்க் கமழகில்","ஆர நாறும் அறல்போற் கூந்தல்","பேரமர் மழைக்கட் கொடிச்சி","மூரல் முறுவலொடு மதைஇய நோக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The man says he seems to see, as he thinks of it, the look of the hill-girl with wide rain-cool eyes, full of pride and soft smiling, her mouth red and lovely where nectar wells by her sharp teeth, her hair dark like rippled sand and fragrant with agil and sandal.","n":"The whole poem is one noun phrase hanging on நோக்கே, the last word: after the mouth, the hair and the eyes, what memory finally holds is a look, proud and smiling at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","azhagu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 286","lb":"KU286","poet":"எயிற்றியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கமழகில்","அகில்",2,"maram","ornament","agil"],["ஆர","ஆரம்",3,"maram","ornament","sandal"],["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",4,"seythi","scene","hill girl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:287":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":288,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி காதலர்","இன்னே கண்டுந் துறக்குவர் கொல்லோ","முந்நாற் றிங்க ணிறைபொறுத் தசைஇ","ஒதுங்கல் செல்லாப் பசும்புளி வேட்கைக்","கடுஞ்சூல் மகளிர் போலநீர் கொண்டு","விசும்பிவர் கல்லாது தாங்குபு புணரிச்","செழும்பல் குன்றம் நோக்கிப்","பெருங்கலி வான மேர்தரும் பொழுதே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks her friend whether their lover will forsake them even after seeing it: the time when the great roaring cloud, having drunk the sea, cannot climb the sky and holds itself back, gathering toward the many rich hills, like women heavy with a first child who, at the end of their months, cannot move and crave green tamarind.","n":"The cloud is pregnant with rain as women are with child, and the season it brings is the one he promised; the simile makes the rain season itself a time of fulfilment he would be failing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 287","lb":"KU287","poet":"கச்சிப்பேட்டு நன்னாகையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பசும்புளி","புளி",4,"maram","simile","tamarind"],["வான","வானம்",8,"pira","scene","cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:288":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":289,"t":["கறிவளர் அடுக்கத் தாங்கண் முறியருந்து","குரங்கொருங் கிருக்கும் பெருங்க னாடன்","இனிய னாகலி னினத்தி னியன்ற","இன்னா மையினு மினிதோ","இனிதெனப் படூஉம் புத்தே ணாடே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that because the lord of the great hill country, where monkeys sit together eating tender shoots on the slope where pepper grows, is dear to her, even the hurt that comes from kin is sweeter than the world of the gods that is called sweet.","n":"Whose kin (இனத்தின்) cause the hurt is not said: her own family's anger at the affair, or his people's coldness. The monkeys that sit together (ஒருங்கு) eating may hint at a troop that keeps its members, but the poem does not draw this, so they stay scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 288","lb":"KU288","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கறிவளர்","கறி",1,"maram","scene","pepper"],["முறியருந்து","முறி",1,"maram","gift_food","shoots"],["குரங்கொருங்","குரங்கு",2,"maa","scene","monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:289":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":290,"t":["வளர்பிறை போல வழிவழிப் பெருகி","இறைவளை நெகிழ்த்த எவ்வ நோயொடு","குழைபிசைந் தனையே மாகிச் சாஅய்","உழையர் அன்மையின் உழப்ப தன்றியும்","மழையுந் தோழி மான்றுபட் டன்றே","பட்ட மாரி படாஅக் கண்ணும்","அவர்திறத் திரங்கு நம்மினும்","நந்திறத் திரங்குமிவ் வழங்கல் ஊரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that, growing day by day like the waxing moon, the grief that loosened the bangles on her wrist has left her shrunk like a crushed sprout; besides the pain of his not being near, the rain has set in thick, and this clamouring town pities her more than she pities him out in the rain.","n":"The town here is not the hostile gossip of other poems but a chorus of pity, and its pity measures her own: she grieves for him in the rain, the town grieves for her. Line 6 (பட்ட மாரி படாஅக் கண்ணும்) is hard and the reading of it is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","melivu","alar","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 289","lb":"KU289","poet":"பெருங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["இறைவளை","வளை",2,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["குழைபிசைந்","குழை",3,"maram","simile","sprout"],["மழையுந்","மழை",5,"pira","scene","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:290":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":291,"t":["காமந் தாங்குமதி யென்போர் தாமஃ","தறியலர் கொல்லோ அனைமது கையர்கொல்","யாமெங் காதலர்க் காணே மாயிற்","செறிதுனி பெருகிய நெஞ்சமொடு பெருநீர்க்","கல்பொரு சிறுநுரை போல","மெல்ல மெல்ல இல்லா குதுமே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks whether those who tell her to bear her love do not know what it is, or are themselves that strong: if she does not see her lover, with a heart where clinging grief has swelled, she will waste away little by little like the small foam that breaks on a rock in deep water.","n":"The image gives no crash, only foam dissolving where it strikes; the doubled மெல்ல மெல்ல makes the dissolution slow. The poet is named from this simile (கல்பொரு சிறுநுரையார்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 290","lb":"KU290","poet":"கல்பொரு சிறுநுரையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["சிறுநுரை","நுரை",5,"pira","simile","foam"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:291":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":292,"t":["சுடுபுன மருங்கிற் கலித்த வேனற்","படுகிளி கடியுங் கொடிச்சிகைக் குளிரே","இசையின் இசையா இன்பா ணித்தே","கிளியவள் விளியென எழலொல் லாவே","அதுபுலந் தழுத கண்ணே சாரற்","குண்டுநீர்ப் பைஞ்சுனைப் பூத்த குவளை","வண்டுபயில் பல்லிதழ் கலைஇத்","தண்துளிக் கேற்ற மலர்போன் றனவே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that the clapper in the hand of the hill-girl driving parrots from the millet on the burnt clearing makes a call sweeter than music, so the parrots take it for one of their own and will not rise; and her eyes, which wept in vexation at that, were like the many-petalled kuvalai of the deep hill pool, ruffled by bees and taking the cool drops.","n":"The joke turns on her sweetness: she fails at her work because her voice is too lovely, and her vexed tears only add a second beauty. The man watches both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 291","lb":"KU291","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["வேனற்","ஏனல்",1,"maram","scene","millet"],["படுகிளி","கிளி",2,"pul","scene","parrot"],["கொடிச்சிகைக்","கொடிச்சி",2,"seythi","scene","hill girl"],["குவளை","குவளை",6,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["வண்டுபயில்","வண்டு",7,"pira","simile","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:292":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":293,"t":["மண்ணிய சென்ற ஒண்ணுத லரிவை","புனல்தரு பசுங்காய் தின்றதன் தப்பற்","கொன்பதிற் றொன்பது களிற்றொ டவணிறை","பொன்செய் பாவை கொடுப்பவுங் கொள்ளான்","பெண்கொலை புரிந்த நன்னன் போல","வரையா நிரையத்துச் செலீஇயரோ அன்னை","ஒருநாள் நகைமுக விருந்தினன் வந்தெனப்","பகைமுக ஊரின் துஞ்சலோ இலளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend curses her mother: as Nannan, when a bright-browed girl who had gone to bathe ate a green fruit the stream carried down, refused eighty-one elephants and a golden doll of her weight in amends and chose to kill a woman, so may her mother go to endless hell, who, since a smiling guest came one day, has not slept, as in a town that faces enemies.","n":"The curse is wildly out of proportion, and that is the point: one visit from a smiling guest has turned the house into a town under siege, and the friend answers with the worst story of cruelty she knows, a chief who would take no ransom for a girl's small fault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kolai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 292","lb":"KU292","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["vekuli","அலை"]],"k":[["பசுங்காய்","காய்",2,"maram","gift_food","green fruit"],["களிற்றொ","களிறு",3,"maa","gift_food","elephants"]],"en":[["king","நன்னன்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:293":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":294,"t":["கள்ளிற் கேளிர் ஆத்திரை யுள்ளூர்ப்","பாளை தந்த பஞ்சியங் குறுங்காய்","ஓங்கிரும் பெண்ணை நுங்கொடு பெயரும்","ஆதி யருமன் மூதூ ரன்ன","அயவெள் ளாம்பல் அம்பகை நெறித்தழை","தித்திக் குறங்கின் ஊழ்மா றலைப்ப","வருமே சேயிழை யந்திற்","கொழுநற் காணிய அளியேன் யானே."],"p":"","r":"The wife says she is to be pitied: the bright-jewelled one, lovely as the old town of Aathi Aruman where people go home with palmyra kernels from tall dark palms whose spathes give small fibrous fruit, comes there, her leaf-skirt of white lilies beating in turn on her spotted thighs, to see the husband.","n":"The rival is praised before she is resented: the wife grants her the beauty of a famous chief's old town and then ends on herself, அளியேன் யானே. Line 1 (கள்ளிற் கேளிர் ஆத்திரை) is obscure: ஆத்திரை may be a place or a person, and the poet's name is taken from it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 293","lb":"KU293","poet":"கள்ளில் ஆத்திரையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",3,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["நுங்கொடு","நுங்கு",3,"unavu","gift_food","palm kernel"],["ளாம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",5,"maram","ornament","white lily"],["நெறித்தழை","தழை",5,"maram","ornament","leaf-skirt"]],"en":[["patron","ஆதி யருமன்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:294":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":295,"t":["கடலுட னாடியும் கான லல்கியும்","தொடலை யாயமொடு தழுவணி யயர்ந்தும்","நொதுமலர் போலக் கதுமென வந்து","முயங்கினன் செலினே யலர்ந்தன்று மன்னே","தித்தி பரந்த பைத்தக லல்குல்","திருந்திழை துயல்வுக்கோட் டசைத்த பசுங்குழைத்","தழையினும் உழையிற் போகான்","தான்தந் தனன்யாய் காத்தோம் பல்லே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that after sea-bathing, resting in the shore grove and dancing in the garlanded troop, he came suddenly like a stranger, embraced her and went, and gossip spread; yet he never left her side, and himself gave the green-leaf skirt that swings on her spotted hips with its fresh shoots; now mother keeps her guarded.","n":"The gift of a leaf-skirt is a lover's pledge, and the friend sets it against his stranger-like coming and going: the bond is real, and now the mother's watch makes marriage the only road. The last line (காத்தோம் பல்லே) is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 294","lb":"KU294","poet":"அஞ்சிலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பசுங்குழைத்","குழை",6,"maram","ornament","shoots"],["தழையினும்","தழை",7,"maram","ornament","leaf-skirt"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:295":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":296,"t":["உடுத்துந் தொடுத்தும் பூண்டுஞ் செரீஇயும்","தழையணிப் பொலிந்த ஆயமொடு துவன்றி","விழவொடு வருதி நீயே யிஃதோ","ஓரான் வல்சிச் சீரில் வாழ்க்கை","பெருநலக் குறுமகள் வந்தென","இனிவிழ வாயிற் றென்னுமிவ் வூரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that he comes with the festival in a crowd of women bright with leaves worn as skirts, strung, put on and tucked in the hair, and that this town says the festival has truly begun now that the lovely young girl of a poor house that lives on a single cow has come.","n":"The town's praise of the new girl is reported by the friend as an accusation: 'the festival has come' now means 'your new woman has come', and the poverty of her house (one cow) sharpens the sting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 295","lb":"KU295","poet":"தூங்கலோரியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["தழையணிப்","தழை",2,"maram","ornament","leaves"],["ஓரான்","ஆன்",4,"maa","scene","cow"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:296":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":297,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி புன்னை","அலங்குசினை யிருந்த அஞ்சிறை நாரை","உறுகழிச் சிறுமீன் முனையிற் செறுவிற்","கள்நாறு நெய்தல் கதிரொடு நயக்கும்","தண்ணந் துறைவற் காணின் முன்னின்று","கடிய கழறல் ஓம்புமதி தொடியோள்","இன்ன ளாகத் துறத்தல்","நும்மின் தகுமோ என்றனை துணிந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend not to stand before the lord of the cool shore, where the stork sitting on the swaying punnai bough, tired of the small fish of the creek, longs for the toddy-scented neythal among the paddy of the field, and rebuke him harshly, as she had resolved, asking whether it suits him to forsake one who is in such a state.","n":"She forbids the rebuke while repeating it in full, so the reproach is spoken after all, through the friend's resolve. The stork leaving the creek's fish for the field is the hidden picture of his straying.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","karpu","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 296","lb":"KU296","poet":"பெரும்பாக்கனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene","punnai"],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","ullurai","stork"],["சிறுமீன்","மீன்",3,"pira","ullurai","fish"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",4,"maram","ullurai","water lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","நாரை","confirmed","2/2","the husband who tires of his wife and seeks women elsewhere"]],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:297":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":298,"t":["அவ்விளிம் புரீஇய கொடுஞ்சிலை மறவர்","வைவார் வாளி விறற்பகை பேணார்","மாறுநின் றிறந்த ஆறுசெல் வம்பலர்","உவலிடு பதுக்கை ஊரின் தோன்றும்","கல்லுயர் நனந்தலை நல்ல கூறிப்","புணர்ந்துடன் போதல் பொருளென","உணர்ந்தேன் மன்றவவர் உணரா வூங்கே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says she understood surely, before he did, that the right course was for the two to go off together, speaking good words of the wide rocky waste where the cairns heaped with leaves over travellers who stood against the bow-bearing bandits and died look like a village.","n":"The friend takes credit for the elopement plan, and lays its road out in its worst colours, a village of graves; the danger is not hidden from the girl but made part of the choice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 297","lb":"KU297","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம்பட்டினத்துக் காரிக்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["மறவர்","மறவர்",1,"seythi","scene","bandits"],["வம்பலர்","வம்பலர்",3,"seythi","scene","travellers"]],"en":[["herostone","பதுக்கை",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:298":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":299,"t":["சேரி சேர மெல்ல வந்துவந்","தரிது வாய்விட் டினிய கூறி","வைகல் தோறும் நிறம்பெயர்ந் துறையுமவன்","பைதல் நோக்கம் நினையாய் தோழி","இன்கடுங் கள்ளின் அகுதை தந்தை","வெண்கடைச் சிறுகோ லகவன் மகளிர்","மடப்பிடிப் பரிசில் மானப்","பிறிதொன்று குறித்ததவ னெடும்புற நிலையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the woman to think of the sorrowful look of the man who comes slowly again and again to their street, rarely opens his mouth to say sweet words and stays each day with his colour changed: his long standing at the back aims at something else, like the young she-elephants that Akuthai's father, of sweet strong toddy, gave as gifts to the singing women with small white-tipped sticks.","n":"The simile is the hard part: what the gift and the waiting share seems to be that each is an outward act aimed at something beyond itself, the chief's gift at the singers' praise, the man's patience at the girl. The friend thus casts the lover as a suppliant whose silence is a request.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kurippu","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 298","lb":"KU298","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கள்ளின்","கள்",5,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["மடப்பிடிப்","பிடி",7,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["லகவன்","அகவன் மகளிர்",6,"seythi","simile","singers"]],"en":[["patron","அகுதை",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:299":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":300,"t":["இதுமற் றெவனோ தோழி முதுநீர்ப்","புணரி திளைக்கும் புள்ளிமிழ் கானல்","இணரவிழ் புன்னை யெக்கர் நீழற்","புணர்குறி வாய்த்த ஞான்றைக் கொண்கற்","கண்டன மன்னெங் கண்ணே யவன்சொற்","கேட்டன மன்னெஞ் செவியே மற்றவன்","மணப்பின் மாணல மெய்தித்","தணப்பின் நெகிழ்பவெந் தடமென் றோளே."],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend what this is: in the shade of the clustered punnai on the dune of the shore grove where the old sea's waves play and birds cry, on the day their tryst came true, her eyes saw the lord of the shore and her ears heard his words; and now her wide soft shoulders take on great beauty when he joins her and waste when he leaves.","n":"Eyes and ears are named as the witnesses that did the harm, each with மன் (once, and no more); the body that now swells and wastes with his coming and going is the only record left.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","kuri","ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 299","lb":"KU299","poet":"வெண்மணிப்பூதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புள்ளிமிழ்","புள்",2,"pul","scene","birds"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",3,"maram","scene","punnai"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:300":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":301,"t":["குவளை நாறுங் குவையிருங் கூந்தல்","ஆம்பல் நாறும் தேம்பொதி துவர்வாய்க்","குண்டுநீர்த் தாமரைக் கொங்கின் அன்ன","நுண்பல் தித்தி மாஅ யோயே","நீயே, அஞ்ச லென்றவென் சொல்லஞ் சலையே","யானே, குறுங்கா லன்னங் குவவுமணற் சேக்கும்","கடல்சூழ் மண்டிலம் பெறினும்","விடல்சூ ழலனான் நின்னுடை நட்பே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells the dark girl with fine spots like the pollen of the deep-water lotus, with heaped dark hair smelling of kuvalai and a honeyed red mouth smelling of aambal, that she fears his very word 'do not fear'; but he would not think of leaving her friendship even if he gained the sea-ringed world where the short-legged swan rests on heaped sand.","n":"The setting of நீயே against யானே divides the poem between her fear and his oath. The swan resting on heaped sand is a shore image of rest, put inside the very world he says he would refuse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","anbu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 300","lb":"KU300","poet":"சிறைக்குடி ஆந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குவளை","குவளை",1,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",2,"maram","scene","white lily"],["தாமரைக்","தாமரை",3,"maram","simile","lotus"],["லன்னங்","அன்னம்",6,"pul","scene","swan"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:15"},"ku:301":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":302,"t":["முழவுமுத லரைய தடவுநிலைப் பெண்ணைக்","கொழுமட லிழைத்த சிறுகோற் குடம்பைக்","கருங்கா லன்றிற் காமர் கடுஞ்சூல்","வயவுப்பெடை யகவும் பானாட் கங்குல்","மன்றம் போழும் இனமணி நெடுந்தேர்","வாரா தாயினும் வருவது போலச்","செவிமுத லிசைக்கு மரவமொடு","துயில்துறந் தனவால் தோழியென் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that at midnight, when the pregnant female anril craving in its twig nest on the frond of the drum-trunked palmyra calls out, a sound rings in her ear as if the long chariot with its bells, cleaving the village common, were coming though it does not come, and her eyes have given up sleep.","n":"The bird calls from inside a nest it shares with its mate, at the same hour that she lies alone; the chariot exists only as a sound her own ear makes, so the poem places the whole tryst inside her listening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 301","lb":"KU301","poet":"குன்றியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["பெண்ணைக்","பெண்ணை",1,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["வயவுப்பெடை","அன்றில் பெடை",4,"pul","iraicchi","female anril"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வயவுப்பெடை","confirmed","1/1","the bird that has its mate beside it still calls for him, while the heroine, whose lover does not come, can only lie awake listening"]],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:302":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":303,"t":["உரைத்திசின் தோழியது புரைத்தோ அன்றே","அருந்துயர் உழத்தலும் ஆற்றாம் இதன்றலைப்","பெரும்பிறி தாகல் அதனினும் அஞ்சுதும்","அன்னோ இன்னும் நன்மலை நாடன்","பிரியா நண்பினர் இருவரும் என்னும்","அலரதற் கஞ்சினன் கொல்லோ பலருடன்","துஞ்சூர் யாமத் தானுமென்","நெஞ்சத் தல்லது வரவறி யானே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend whether it is not wrong: she can bear hard grief, but fears more that it may turn into something worse; alas, has the man of the good hill grown afraid of the gossip that says the two are inseparable, so that at midnight, when the town sleeps, he knows no way of coming except into her heart?","n":"பெரும்பிறிது, the greater other thing, is left unnamed; it may be death or the end of the bond, and the poem's fear lives in the gap.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","ninaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 302","lb":"KU302","poet":"மாங்குடிகிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:303":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":304,"t":["கழிதேர்ந் தசைஇய கருங்கால் வெண்குரு","கடைகரைத் தாழைக் குழீஇப் பெருங்கடல்","உடைதிரை ஒலியில் துஞ்சுந் துறைவ","தொன்னிலை நெகிழ்ந்த வளைய ளீங்குப்","பசந்தனள் மன்னென் தோழி யென்னொடும்","இன்னிணர்ப் புன்னையம் புகர்நிழற்","பொன்வரி அலவன் ஆட்டிய ஞான்றே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore, where the black-legged white heron that has searched the backwater for food gathers in the thaazhai at the edge of the shore and sleeps through the roar of the breaking waves, that her friend, her bangles slipped from their old place, has grown pale here ever since the day she played with her, chasing the gold-striped crab in the spotted shade of the sweet-clustered punnai.","n":"The heron that has fed and sleeps through the surf could be read as the man at ease while she wastes, but only one correspondence holds (his ease against her pain), so it fails condition 3 and stays scene; the crab game dates the pallor to the day of first meeting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","melivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 303","lb":"KU303","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["வெண்குரு","வெண்குருகு",1,"pul","scene","white heron"],["தாழைக்","தாழை",2,"maram","scene","screw pine"],["வளைய","வளை",4,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["புன்னையம்","புன்னை",6,"maram","scene",""],["அலவன்","அலவன்",7,"pira","scene","crab"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:304":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":305,"t":["கொல்வினைப் பொலிந்த கூர்வா யெறியுளி","முகம்பட மடுத்த முளிவெதிர் நோன்காழ்","தாங்கரு நீர்ச்சுரத் தெறிந்து வாங்குவிசைக்","கொடுந்திமிற் பரதவர் கோட்டுமீ னெறிய","நெடுங்கரை யிருந்த குறுங்கா லன்னத்து","வெண்டோ டிரியும் வீததை கானற்","கைதையந் தண்புனற் சேர்ப்பனொடு","செய்தனெ மன்றவோர் பகைதரு நட்பே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that with the lord of the shore of cool kaithai waters, where, as the fishermen of curved boats hurl the sharp-mouthed harpoon on its strong shaft of dry bamboo at the horned fish in the unbearable sea, the white flock of short-legged swans sitting on the long shore scatters through the flowered grove, they have surely made a friendship that brings enmity.","n":"The harpoon thrown at the shark scatters swans that were no part of the hunt; read as a hidden image it would make the lovers' bond scatter the innocent household, but no second pair can be fixed to the situation, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pagai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 304","lb":"KU304","poet":"கணக்காயர் தத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",4,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["கோட்டுமீ","கோட்டுமீன்",4,"pira","scene","shark"],["லன்னத்து","அன்னம்",5,"pul","scene","swan"],["கைதையந்","கைதை",7,"maram","scene","screw pine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:305":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":306,"t":["கண்தர வந்த காம ஒள்ளெரி","என்புற நலியினும் அவரொடு பேணிச்","சென்றுநாம் முயங்கற் கருங்காட் சியமே","வந்தஞர் களைதலை அவராற் றலரே","உய்த்தனர் விடாஅர் பிரித்திடை களையார்","குப்பைக் கோழித் தனிப்போர் போல","விளிவாங்கு விளியி னல்லது","களைவோர் இலையா முற்ற நோயே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says the bright fire of love the eyes brought torments her to the bone, yet she cannot go to him and embrace him, nor does he come to remove her pain; they neither let go nor part, and like the lone fight of the dunghill cocks, which ends only when one dies, this sickness that has reached its height has no one to stop it.","n":"The cocks fight on a rubbish heap with no one to part them; the image makes love-sickness a contest with no umpire, and 'ends only in dying' is said flatly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kan","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 305","lb":"KU305","poet":"குப்பைக்கோழியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["கோழித்","கோழி",6,"pul","simile","cock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:306":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":307,"t":["மெல்லிய இனிய மேவரு தகுந","இவைமொழி யாமெனச் சொல்லினு மவைநீ","மறத்தியோ வாழியென் னெஞ்சே பலவுடன்","காமர் மாஅத்துத் தாதமர் பூவின்","வண்டுவீழ் பயருங் கானல்","தண்கடற் சேர்ப்பனைக் கண்ட பின்னே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her heart whether, though it said 'these are the soft, sweet, welcome, fitting words we shall speak', it will forget them once it has seen the lord of the cool sea, of the shore grove where bees fall on the pollen-filled flowers of the lovely mango.","n":"The words may be the ones she means to say to him or the gentle words he once spoke; either way the heart's resolve is expected to fail at the sight of him. The bees dropping into the mango's pollen could figure the heart's fall, but they do no act of the situation, so they stay scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 306","lb":"KU306","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","nenju",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாஅத்துத்","மா",4,"maram","scene","mango"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",5,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:307":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":308,"t":["வளையுடைத் தனைய தாகிப் பலர்தொழச்","செவ்வாய் வானத் தையெனத் தோன்றி","இன்னம் பிறந்தன்று பிறையே அன்னோ","மறந்தனர் கொல்லோ தாமே களிறுதன்","உயங்குநடை மடப்பிடி வருத்த நோனாது","நிலையுயர் யாஅந் தொலையக் குத்தி","வெண்ணார் கொண்டு கைசுவைத் தண்ணாந்","தழுங்க னெஞ்சமொடு முழங்கும்","அத்த நீளிடை அழப்பிரிந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says the new crescent has already been born, shaped like a broken bangle, appearing slender in the red-mouthed sky as many worship it; alas, have they forgotten, the men who left her weeping for the long wasteland where a tusker, unable to bear the pain of his young female's weary gait, gores and fells a tall yaa tree, takes its white fibre, tastes it with his trunk and looks up trumpeting with a grieving heart?","n":"The crescent counts the months since he left; a broken bangle is also what wasting does to her own wrist.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 307","lb":"KU307","poet":"கடம்பனூர்ச் சாண்டிலியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பிறையே","பிறை",3,"pira","scene","crescent moon"],["களிறு","களிறு",4,"maa","iraicchi","tusker"],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",5,"maa","iraicchi","female elephant"],["யாஅந்","யா",6,"maram","scene",""],["வெண்ணார்","வெண்ணார்",7,"maram","gift_food","white fibre"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","களிறு","confirmed","1/1","even the elephant on that road cannot bear his mate's suffering, yet the man who crossed it has forgotten the one he left weeping"]],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:308":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":309,"t":["சோலை வாழைச் சுரிநுகும் பினைய","அணங்குடை அருந்தலை நீவலின் மதனழிந்து","மயங்குதுயர் உற்ற மையல் வேழம்","உயங்குயிர் மடப்பிடி யுலைபுறந் தைவர","ஆமிழி சிலம்பின் அரிதுகண் படுக்கும்","மாமலை நாடன் கேண்மை","காமந் தருவதோர் கைதாழ்ந் தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the bond of the man of the great hill, where an elephant, its fearsome head stroked by the curled bud of the grove banana, loses its might and in deluded grief sleeps with difficulty on the slope of falling waters while its tender, weary female strokes its back, has slackened in the hand that gave love.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are hard to construe (whether the banana bud grieves or strokes, and why the beast loses its might); the female soothing the stricken male could be an iraicchi of the heroine's care, but with the act and its subject unsure it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 308","lb":"KU308","poet":"பெருந்தோட் குறுஞ்சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வாழைச்","வாழை",1,"maram","scene","banana"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",3,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",4,"maa","scene","female elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:309":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":310,"t":["கைவினை மாக்கடம் செய்வினை முடிமார்","சுரும்புண மலர்ந்த வாசங் கீழ்ப்பட","நீடின வரம்பின் வாடிய விடினும்","கொடியரோ நிலம்பெயர்ந் துறைவே மென்னாது","பெயர்த்துங் கடிந்த செறுவிற் பூக்கும்","நின்னூர் நெய்த லனையேம் பெரும","நீயெமக், கின்னா தனபல செய்யினும்","நின்னின் றமைதல் வல்லா மாறே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord that, though the field workers finishing their weeding throw down the fragrant flowers that bees feed on and leave them to wither on the high ridges, the neythal of his town does not say 'they are cruel; we will move to another land', but blooms again in the very field it was weeded from; we are like that neythal, lord: though you do us many unkind things, we cannot do without you.","n":"The neythal is weeded out by the household's own hands and still returns to the same field, which makes the women's loyalty a matter of belonging to the soil rather than of being well treated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","karpu","porai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 309","lb":"KU309","poet":"உறையூர்ச் சல்லியன் குமாரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["கைவினை மாக்கடம்","கைவினை மாக்கள்",1,"seythi","scene","field workers"],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",2,"pira","scene","bee"],["நெய்த","நெய்தல்",6,"maram","simile","water lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:310":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":311,"t":["புள்ளும் புலம்பின பூவிங் கூம்பின","கானலும் புலம்புநனி யுடைத்தே வானமும்","நம்மே போலும் மம்மர்த் தாகி","எல்லைகழியப் புல்லென் றன்றே","இன்னும் உளெனே தோழி இந்நிலை","தண்ணிய கமழுஞாழல்","தண்ணந் துறைவர்க் குரைக்குநர்ப் பெறினே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says the birds have grown lonely, the flowers here have closed, the shore grove is full of loneliness, and the sky too, dazed like us, has turned dim past the day's end; friend, I may yet live in this state if I find someone to tell it to the lord of the cool ford where the cool njaazhal is fragrant.","n":"The evening's loneliness is shared by birds, flowers, grove and sky, and against all of it she sets one practical hope, a messenger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 310","lb":"KU310","poet":"பெருங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["புள்ளும்","புள்",1,"pul","scene","birds"],["பூவிங்","பூ",1,"maram","scene","flowers"],["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",6,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:311":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":312,"t":["அலர்யாங் கொழிவ தோழி பெருங்கடற்","புலவுநா றகன்றுறை வலவன் தாங்கவும்","நில்லாது கழிந்த கல்லென் கடுந்தேர்","யான்கண் டனனோ இலனோ பானாள்","ஓங்கல் வெண்மணல் தாழ்ந்த புன்னைத்","தாதுசேர் நிகர்மலர் கொய்யும்","ஆயம் எல்லாம் உடன்கண் டன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend how the gossip can end, when the swift, rattling chariot that would not stop though its driver on the broad fish-smelling ford held it back, whether she saw it or not, was seen by the whole troop of girls plucking the pollen-laden bright flowers of the punnai bent over the high white sand.","n":"'Whether I saw it or not' gives up her own witness: it no longer matters what she saw, only that everyone else did.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 311","lb":"KU311","poet":"சேந்தன் கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புன்னைத்","புன்னை",5,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:312":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":313,"t":["இரண்டறி கள்விநங் காத லோளே","முரண்கொள் துப்பிற் செவ்வேன் மலையன்","முள்ளூர்க் கான நாற வந்து","நள்ளென் கங்குல் நம்மோ ரன்னள்","கூந்தல் வேய்ந்த விரவுமலர் உதிர்த்துச்","சாந்துளர் நறுங்கதுப் பெண்ணெய் நீவி","அமரா முகத்த ளாகித்","தமரோ ரன்னள் வைகறை யானே."],"p":"","r":"The man says his beloved is a thief who knows two ways: in the thick of night she comes, filling with fragrance the forest of Mullur of Malaiyan of the red spear and hostile strength, as one of us; at dawn, having shaken out the mixed flowers she wore in her hair and smoothed her sandal-scented locks with oil, her face unmoved, she is as one of her own kin.","n":"The thief steals nothing but herself: the scent she carries at night and the oiled hair at dawn are her two disguises, and the man admires the skill that hides him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 312","lb":"KU312","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["விரவுமலர்","மலர்",5,"maram","ornament","flowers"],["சாந்து","சாந்து",6,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["பெண்ணெய்","எண்ணெய்",6,"pira","ornament","oil"]],"en":[["king","மலையன்",2],["hill","முள்ளூர்",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:313":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":314,"t":["பெருங்கடற் கரையது சிறுவெண் காக்கை","நீத்துநீர் இருங்கழி இரைதேர்ந் துண்டு","பூக்கமழ் பொதும்பிற் சேக்குந் துறைவனொடு","யாத்தேம் யாத்தன்று நட்பே","அவிழ்த்தற் கரிதது முடிந்தமைந் தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that with the lord of the shore, where the small white crow of the great sea's edge searches out and eats its prey in the dark backwater of flowing water and roosts in the flower-scented grove, they tied it, and the friendship is tied: it is knotted and settled, hard to untie.","n":"The bird's day ends in a fixed roost after its search; the bond, too, is spoken of as done, in the past tense, beyond anyone's untying.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 313","lb":"KU313","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["காக்கை","காக்கை",1,"pul","scene","sea crow"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:314":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":315,"t":["சேயுயர் விசும்பி னீருறு கமஞ்சூல்","தண்குரல் எழிலி ஒண்சுடர் இமைப்பப்","பெயல்தாழ் பிருளிய புலம்புகொள் மாலையும்","வாரார் வாழி தோழி வரூஉம்","இன்னுறழ் இளமுலை ஞெமுங்க","இன்னா வைப்பின் சுரனிறந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that even in this lonely evening, when the full-bellied cool-voiced cloud from the far high sky flashes bright lightning and the rain comes down darkening everything, he does not come, the man who crossed the wasteland of cruel places, who would come and press her young breasts in embrace.","n":"The rain cloud is a promise kept by the season and broken by the man; the line on her breasts recalls the return she was told to expect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","maalai","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 314","lb":"KU314","poet":"பேரிசாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:315":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":316,"t":["எழுதரு மதியங் கடற்கண் டாஅங்","கொழுகுவெள் ளருவி யோங்குமலை நாடன்","ஞாயி றனையன் தோழி","நெருஞ்சி யனையவென் பெரும்பணைத் தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that the man of the high hill, where the white waterfall pours like the rising moon seen over the sea, is like the sun, and her great bamboo-like shoulders are like the nerunji.","n":"The nerunji opens to the sun and follows it, so her shoulders flourish with his presence; but the same sun scorches the dry ground the nerunji grows on, and the pairing does not say which.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 315","lb":"KU315","poet":"மதுரை வேள்ஆதத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கடற்","கடல்",1,"pira","simile","sea"],["வெள் ளருவி","வெள்ளருவி",2,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["நெருஞ்சி","நெருஞ்சி",4,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:316":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":317,"t":["ஆய்வளை ஞெகிழவு மயர்வுமெய் நிறுப்பவும்","நோய்மலி வருத்தம் அன்னை யறியின்","உளெனோ வாழி தோழி விளியா","துரவுக்கடல் பொருத விரவுமண லடைகரை","ஓரை மகளி ரோராங் காட்ட","வாய்ந்த லவன் துன்புறு துனைபரி","ஓங்குவரல் விரிதிரை களையும்","துறைவன் சொல்லோ பிறவா யினவே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend whether she would live if her mother learned of this sickness-laden distress that loosens her chosen bangles and fills her body with faintness, now that the words of the lord of the shore, where the unceasing sea beats on the sand-heaped shore and the rising waves wipe out the fast-running crab the girls playing at orai point out, have turned to something else.","n":"The waves erasing the crab the girls were following could image his words wiped out, but lines 5 and 6 are unsure and only that one correspondence can be named, a near miss on condition 3.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","vaaimai","melivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 316","lb":"KU316","poet":"தும்பிசேர் கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["ஆய்வளை","வளை",1,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["லவன்","அலவன்",6,"pira","scene","crab"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:317":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":318,"t":["புரிமட மரையான் கருநரை நல்லேறு","தீம்புளி நெல்லி மாந்தி யயலது","தேம்பாய் மாமலர் நடுங்க வெய்துயிர்த்","தோங்குமலைப் பைஞ்சுனை பருகு நாடன்","நம்மைவிட் டமையுமோ மற்றே கைம்மிக","வடபுல வாடைக் கழிமழை","தென்புலம் படருந் தண்பனி நாளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether the man of the land where the fine dark-grey bull of the wild cattle, beloved of its young cow, eats sweet-sour nelli, sighs hot breath that makes the honey-dripping flowers beside it tremble, and drinks from the green spring of the high hill, will stay away from us in the cold dewy days when the north wind's rain, beyond measure, moves south.","n":"The bull's sour nelli followed by spring water (a taste that turns sweet after the sour) is traditionally read as the hard courtship turning sweet; the beast does nothing of the lovers' situation, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 317","lb":"KU317","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்டரதத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மரையான் கருநரை நல்லேறு","மரை ஏறு",1,"maa","scene","wild bull"],["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",2,"maram","gift_food","gooseberry"],["மாமலர்","மலர்",3,"maram","scene","flowers"],["பைஞ்சுனை","சுனை",4,"pira","scene","spring"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:318":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":319,"t":["எறிசுறாக் கலித்த இலங்குநீர்ப் பரப்பின்","நறுவீ ஞாழலொடு புன்னை தாஅய்","வெறியயர் களத்தினில் தோன்றுந் துறைவன்","குறியா னாயினும் குறிப்பினும் பிறிதொன்","றறியாற் குரைப்பலோ யானே யெய்த்தவிப்","பணையெழின் மென்றோ ளணைஇய வந்நாட்","பிழையா வஞ்சினஞ் செய்த","கள்வனும் கடவனும் புணைவனுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says of the lord of the shore, where fragrant njaazhal and punnai flowers strewn over the gleaming expanse of water alive with attacking sharks make it look like the ground of a frenzy rite, that though he gives no sign, shall she tell anything else to one who knows nothing else even in thought; on the day he embraced these wasted soft bamboo shoulders he swore an unfailing oath, and he himself is the thief, the one bound by it, and the raft that must carry her across.","n":"Thief, debtor and raft are three legal and practical names for one man: he took, he owes, and he alone can save; the word வஞ்சினம், elsewhere a warrior's oath, here binds a lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 318","lb":"KU318","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எறிசுறாக்","சுறா",1,"pira","scene","shark"],["ஞாழலொடு","ஞாழல்",2,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",2,"maram","scene",""],["வெறியயர்","வெறியாட்டு",3,"seythi","simile","frenzy rite"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:319":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":320,"t":["மானேறு மடப்பிணை தழீஇ மருள்கூர்ந்து","கான நண்ணிய புதன்மறைந் தொடுங்கவும்","கையுடை நன்மாப் பிடியொடு பொருந்தி","மையணி மருங்கின் மலையகஞ் சேரவும்","மாலைவந் தன்று மாரி மாமழை","பொன்னேர் மேனி நன்னலஞ் சிதைத்தோர்","இன்னும் வாரார் ஆயின்","என்னாந் தோழிநம் இன்னுயிர் நிலையே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that as the stag, clasping its young doe, shrinks in bewilderment into the bush near the forest, and the good elephant with its trunk joins its female and makes for the dark-sloped hill, the evening has come with the season's great dark rain; if those who ruined her gold-like body's beauty do not come even now, what will become of her sweet life?","n":"The elephant pair repeats the stag's act of sheltering with a mate as the rain-evening comes; the iraicchi is tagged on the stag, and the doubling makes the heroine the only creature left without her partner.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","maalai","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 319","lb":"KU319","poet":"தாயங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["மானேறு","மான் ஏறு",1,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["மடப்பிணை","பிணை",1,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["நன்மாப்","மா",3,"maa","scene","elephant"],["பிடியொடு","பிடி",3,"maa","scene","female elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மானேறு","confirmed","1/1","even the beasts seek their mates for shelter at the evening of the rains, while the man who should be with her has not come"]],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:320":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":321,"t":["பெருங்கடற் பரதவர் கொண்மீன் உணங்கல்","அருங்கழிக் கொண்ட இறவின் வாடலொடு","நிலவுநிற வெண்மணல் புலவப் பலவுடன்","எக்கர்தொறும் பரக்குந் துறைவனொ டொருநாள்","நக்கதோர் பழியு மிலமே போதவிழ்","பொன்னிணர் மரீஇய புள்ளிமிழ் பொங்கர்ப்","புன்னையஞ் சேரி யிவ்வூர்","கொன்னலர் தூற்றந்தன் கொடுமை யானே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that with the lord of the shore, where the fish the sea fishermen caught, drying with the prawns taken from the backwater, make the moon-white sand reek of flesh as they spread over every dune, they have not even the blame of having laughed together one day, yet this town of the punnai street, by the punnai grove of golden clusters where flowers open and birds call, spreads idle gossip out of its own cruelty.","n":"The drying catch is the town's own trade, and its smell spreads over sand that was clean; the heroine draws the moral without naming it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","pazhi","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 320","lb":"KU320","poet":"தும்பிசேர் கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["vekuli","அலை"]],"k":[["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",1,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["உணங்கல்","உணங்கல்",1,"unavu","ullurai","dried fish"],["இறவின்","இறா",2,"pira","ullurai","prawn"],["புள்ளிமிழ்","புள்",6,"pul","scene","birds"],["புன்னையஞ்","புன்னை",7,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","உணங்கல் இறவின்","confirmed","1/1","the town's gossip about the heroine and the lord of the shore"]],"ch":"ku:16"},"ku:321":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":322,"t":["மலைச்செஞ் சாந்தின் ஆர மார்பினன்","சுனைப்பூங் குவளைச் சுரும்பார் கண்ணியன்","நடுநாள் வந்து நம்மனைப் பெயரும்","மடவர லரிவைநின் மார்பமர் இன்றுணை","மன்ற மரையா இரிய ஏறட்டுச்","செங்கண் இரும்புலி குழுமும் அதனால்","மறைத்தற் காலையோ அன்றே","திறப்பல் வாழிவேண் டன்னைநம் கதவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the man who comes at midnight wearing red hill sandal on his chest and a bee-filled garland of spring kuvalai, and then goes back from their house, is her dear companion; now that on the common ground the red-eyed tiger roars after killing the bull of the marai deer, it is no longer a time for hiding, and she will open their door.","n":"The kill is made on the மன்றம், the village's own common ground, so the danger of the night path has come to the door; the friend's 'opening the door' turns secrecy into disclosure, the step toward marriage.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu","acham","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 321","lb":"KU321","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["சாந்தின்","சாந்து",1,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["குவளைச்","குவளை",2,"maram","ornament","blue water-lily"],["சுரும்பார்","சுரும்பு",2,"pira","scene","bee"],["மரையா","மரை ஆ",5,"maa","scene","marai deer"],["இரும்புலி","புலி",6,"maa","scene","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:322":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":323,"t":["அமர்க்க ணாமான் அஞ்செவிக் குழவி","கானவ ரெடுப்ப வெரீஇ யினந்தீர்ந்து","கான நண்ணிய சிறுகுடிப் பட்டென","இளைய ரோம்ப மரீஇயவ ணயந்து","மனையுறை வாழ்க்கை வல்லி யாங்கு","மருவின் இனியவு முளவோ","செல்வாந் தோழி யொல்வாங்கு நடந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that, as the soft-eared fawn of the gentle-eyed deer, frightened from its herd when forest folk took it up and brought to the small hamlet by the forest, came to love house life once the youths reared it, nothing is sweeter than what one grows used to; so let them go, walking as they can.","n":"The fawn was taken by force and learned to love the house; she applies it to her own leaving, so the image answers in advance the fear that she cannot live away from her mother's home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","pazhaimai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 322","lb":"KU322","poet":"ஐயூர் முடவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ணாமான்","ஆமான்",1,"maa","simile","wild cow-deer"],["குழவி","குழவி",1,"maa","simile","fawn"],["கானவ","கானவர்",2,"seythi","scene","forest folk"],["இளைய","இளையர்",4,"seythi","scene","youths"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:323":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":324,"t":["எல்லாம் எவனோ பதடி வைகல்","பாணர் படுமலை பண்ணிய எழாலின்","வானத் தெழுஞ்சுவர் நல்லிசை வீழப்","பெய்த புலத்துப் பூத்த முல்லைப்","பசுமுகைத் தாது நாறும் நறுநுதல்","அரிவை தோளிணைத் துஞ்சிக்","கழிந்த நாளிவண் வாழு நாளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says all other days are chaff; the only days he has lived are those spent sleeping on the two shoulders of the young woman whose fragrant forehead smells of the pollen of fresh mullai buds blooming in the rain-soaked field, when the thunder rises sweet in the sky like a bards' lute tuned to padumalai.","n":"பதடி வைகல், days of chaff, gives the poet his name; the thunder that in mullai marks the man's promised return here only sweetens the days he has already come back to.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 323","lb":"KU323","poet":"பதடி வைகலார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பாணர்","பாணர்",2,"seythi","simile","bards"],["எழாலின்","எழால்",2,"yaazh","simile","lute"],["முல்லைப்","முல்லை",4,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:324":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":325,"t":["கொடுங்கால் முதலைக் கோள்வ லேற்றை","வழிவழக் கறுக்குங் கானலம் பெருந்துறை","இனமீன் இருங்கழி நீந்தி நீநின்","நயனுடை மையின் வருதி யிவடன்","மடனுடை மையின் உயங்கும் யானது","கவைமக நஞ்சுண் டாஅங்","கஞ்சுவல் பெருமவென் னெஞ்சத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the great shore, where the crooked-legged male crocodile, strong at seizing, cuts off the paths, that he swims the dark backwater full of fish because of his love and comes, while she suffers because of her innocence, and the friend herself, like one whose twin children have both eaten poison, is afraid in her heart.","n":"The friend splits the situation in three: his love brings him, her innocence makes her suffer, and the fear for both is hers alone; the simile of the twins is what holds the two lovers in one fear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","acham","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 324","lb":"KU324","poet":"கவை மகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["முதலைக்","முதலை",1,"maa","scene","crocodile"],["இனமீன்","மீன்",3,"pira","scene","fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:325":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":326,"t":["சேறுஞ் சேறு மென்றலின் பண்டைத்தன்","மாயச் செலவாச் செத்து மருங்கற்று","மன்னிக் கழிகென் றேனே அன்னோ","ஆசா கெந்தை யாண்டுளன் கொல்லோ","கருங்கால் வெண்குருகு மேயும்","பெருங்குளம் ஆயிற்றென் இடைமுலை நிறைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that when he kept saying 'I am going, I am going' she took it for one of his old feigned departures and told him to go and stay away for good; alas, where is he now, her lord and support, when the space between her breasts has filled into a great pond where black-legged white herons feed.","n":"She blames herself, not him: the dismissal was hers, spoken in sulking because his departures had been false before; the hyperbole of the heron pond turns the cost of that one word into a landscape.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","oodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 325","lb":"KU325","poet":"நன்னாகையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வெண்குருகு","குருகு",5,"pul","scene","heron"],["பெருங்குளம்","குளம்",6,"pira","scene","pond"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:326":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":327,"t":["துணைத்த கோதைப் பணைப்பெருந் தோளினர்","கடலாடு மகளிர் கான லிழைத்த","சிறுமனைப் புணர்ந்த நட்பே தோழி","ஒருநாள் துறைவன் துறப்பின்","பன்னாள் வரூஉம் இன்னா மைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that the love that joined them at the little sand-houses that the broad-shouldered sea-bathing girls with doubled garlands built in the shore grove is such that, if the lord of the shore leaves it for one day, it brings many days of pain.","n":"The love began in a game of house-building on the sand, which makes its fragility literal; the arithmetic of one day against many is the heroine's argument that even a short absence is not small.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","natpu","innaa"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 326","lb":"KU326","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோதைப்","கோதை",1,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கடலாடு","கடல் ஆடு",2,"seythi","scene","sea-bathing"],["சிறுமனைப்","சிறுமனை",3,"pira","scene","sand-house"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:327":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":328,"t":["நல்கின் வாழும் நல்கூர்ந் தோர்வயின்","நயனில ராகுதல் நன்றென உணர்ந்த","குன்ற நாடன் தன்னினும் நன்றும்","நின்னிலை கொடிதால் தீய கலுழி","நம்மனை மடமகள் இன்ன மென்மைச்","சாயலள் அளியள் என்னாய்","வாழைதந் தனையாற் சிலம்புபுல் லெனவே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells the muddy hill torrent that it is crueller even than the hill lord, who has learnt that it is good to be unkind to the poor ones who live only if he is gracious, for without thinking how soft and pitiable the young girl of their house is, it has brought down a plantain and left the slope bare.","n":"The torrent comes down from his hill, so what it carries to her is a piece of his country; that is why it is crueller than he is: he only neglects her, while it reminds her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nayan","orutalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 327","lb":"KU327","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கலுழி","கலுழி",4,"pira","scene","muddy torrent"],["வாழைதந்","வாழை",7,"maram","scene","plantain"],["சிலம்பு","சிலம்பு",7,"pira","scene","hill slope"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:328":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":329,"t":["சிறுவீ ஞாழல் வேரளைப் பள்ளி","அலவன் சிறுமனை சிதையப் புணரி","குணில்வாய் முரசின் இயங்குந் துறைவன்","நல்கிய நாள்தவச் சிலவே அலரே","வில்கெழு தானை விச்சியர் பெருமகன்","வேந்தரொடு பொருத ஞான்றைப் பாணர்","புலிநோக் குறழ்நிலை கண்ட","கலிகெழு குறும்பூர் ஆர்ப்பினும் பெரிதே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says the lord of the shore, where the surf moving with the sound of a drum beaten with a stick ruins the crab's little house in its burrow among the roots of the small-flowered njazhal, was gracious for very few days, yet the gossip is louder than the uproar of Kurumbur on the day the Vicchiyar chief with his bow-armed host fought the kings and bards saw him stand with a tiger's glare.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","orutalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 328","lb":"KU328","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene","njazhal tree"],["அலவன்","அலவன்",2,"pira","ullurai","crab"],["புணரி","புணரி",2,"pira","ullurai","surf"],["முரசின்","முரசு",3,"parai","simile","drum"],["பாணர்","பாணர்",6,"seythi","scene","bards"],["புலிநோக்","புலி",7,"maa","simile","tiger"]],"en":[["king","விச்சியர் பெருமகன்",5],["town","குறும்பூர்",8]],"dv":[["ullurai","அலவன் புணரி","confirmed","1/1","the man whose brief love has wrecked the girl's good name"]],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:329":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":330,"t":["கான விருப்பை வேனல் வெண்பூ","வளிபொரு நெடுஞ்சினை உகுத்தலி னார்கழல்பு","களிறுவழங்கு சிறுநெறி புதையத் தாஅம்","பிறங்குமலை அருஞ்சுரம் இறந்தவர்ப் படர்ந்து","பயிலிருள் நடுநாள் துயிலரி தாகித்","தெண்ணீர் நிகர்மலர் புரையும்","நன்மலர் மழைக்கணிற் கெளியவாற் பனியே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that thinking of him who crossed the hard desert of towering hills, where white summer flowers of the forest iluppai, shed by long boughs the wind strikes, fall and cover the narrow path where elephants go, she cannot sleep in the thick dark of midnight, and the cold tears come easily to her fine rain-laden eyes like flowers in clear water.","n":"She sees in her mind the path he took, buried under fallen flowers, so the elephant path that should guide him is hidden; the sleepless night is spent on his road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","iravu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 329","lb":"KU329","poet":"ஓதலாந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["விருப்பை","இருப்பை",1,"maram","scene","iluppai"],["களிறு","களிறு",3,"maa","scene","elephant"],["நிகர்மலர்","மலர்",6,"maram","simile","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:330":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":331,"t":["நலத்தகைப் புலைத்தி பசைதோய்த் தெடுத்துத்","தலைப்புடைப் போக்கித் தண்கயத் திட்ட","நீரிற் பிரியாப் பரூஉத்திரி கடுக்கும்","பேரிலைப் பகன்றைப் பொதியவிழ் வான்பூ","இன்கடுங் கள்ளின் மணமில கமழும்","புன்கண் மாலையும் புலம்பும்","இன்றுகொல் தோழியவர் சென்ற நாட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend whether, in the land he went to, there is no sorrowful evening and its loneliness, when the large-leaved pakanrai opens from its bud a white flower like the thick wick a good washerwoman dipped in starch, lifted and beat and left in the cool pond, and spreads a smell that has nothing of sweet strong toddy.","n":"The question is an accusation: if evening reached his land he would feel what she feels and come home; the scentless flower, white and limp as a starched wick, is the evening of her own field.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 330","lb":"KU330","poet":"கழார்க் கீரனெயிற்றியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புலைத்தி","புலைத்தி",1,"seythi","simile","washerwoman"],["பகன்றைப்","பகன்றை",4,"maram","scene","pakanrai"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:331":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":332,"t":["நெடுங்கழை திரங்கிய நீரி லாரிடை","ஆறுசெல் வம்பலர் தொலைய மாறுநின்று","கொடுஞ்சிலை மறவர் கடறுகூட் டுண்ணும்","கடுங்கண் யானைக் கான நீந்தி","இறப்பர்கொல் வாழி தோழி நறுவடிப்","பைங்கால் மாஅத் தந்தளி ரன்ன","நன்மா மேனி பசப்ப","நம்மினுஞ் சிறந்த அரும்பொருள் தரற்கே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether he will cross the forest of fierce elephants, the waterless hard road where tall bamboo has withered and bowmen with curved bows stand against travellers and plunder the wilds, so that her fine dark body like the tender leaf of the fragrant green mango turns pale, to bring wealth that is hard to win and that he rates above them.","n":"நம்மினுஞ் சிறந்த, better than us, is the sting: the friend states his choice as a ranking in which the women come below the wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 331","lb":"KU331","poet":"வாடாப் பிரபந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["நெடுங்கழை","கழை",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["மறவர்","மறவர்",3,"seythi","scene","bowmen"],["யானைக்","யானை",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மாஅத்","மா",6,"maram","simile","mango"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:332":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":333,"t":["வந்த வாடைச் சில்பெயற் கடைநாள்","நோய்நீந் தரும்படர் தீரநீ நயந்து","கூறின் எவனோ தோழி நாறுயிர்","மடப்பிடி தழீஇத் தடக்கை யானை","குன்றச் சிறுகுடி யிழிதரு","மன்ற நண்ணிய மலைகிழ வோற்கே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the heroine what harm there would be if, on this last day of the light rains brought by the north wind, she spoke lovingly to the lord of the hill near the common ground where the great-trunked tusker, embracing his young she-elephant, comes down into the hill hamlet, so that the great anguish of swimming through this sickness may end.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivu","noy","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 332","lb":"KU332","poet":"மதுரை மருதங்கிழார் மகனார் இளம்போத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","iraicchi","elephant"],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",4,"maa","iraicchi","she-elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","யானை","confirmed","1/1","as the tusker brings his mate openly into the settlement, the man should take her openly into the village by marriage"]],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:333":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":334,"t":["குறும்படைப் பகழிக் கொடுவிற் கானவன்","புனமுண்டு கடிந்த பைங்கண் யானை","நறுந்தழை மகளிர் ஓப்புங் கிள்ளையொடு","குறும்பொறைக் கணவுங் குன்ற நாடன்","பணிக்குறை வருத்தம் வீடத்","துணியின் எவனோ தோழிநம் மறையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks what harm there would be in deciding to reveal their secret, so that the distress of the hill lord's pleading may end: he is lord of the land where the green-eyed elephant, driven off by the hunter with short-shafted arrows and crooked bow after eating the millet field, hangs about the low hillock with the parrots that girls in fragrant leaf-skirts chase away.","n":"கணவும் (line 4) is a rare verb; it is read here as hovering about. The parrots that are driven off with the elephant may also point to the girls' own guarding of the field, but that is not counted as a pair.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","karavu","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 333","lb":"KU333","poet":"உழுந்தினைம்புலவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கானவன்","கானவன்",1,"seythi","scene","hunter"],["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","ullurai","elephant"],["புனமுண்டு","புனம்",2,"pira","scene","millet field"],["நறுந்தழை","தழை",3,"maram","ornament","leaf-skirt"],["மகளிர்","மகளிர்",3,"seythi","scene","girls"],["கிள்ளையொடு","கிள்ளை",3,"pul","scene","parrot"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","யானை","confirmed","1/1","the man who has had the girl's love and is now kept from her"]],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:334":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":335,"t":["சிறுவெண் காக்கைச் செவ்வாய்ப் பெருந்தோ","டெறிதிரைத் திவலை யீர்ம்புற நனைப்பப்","பனிபுலந் துறையும் பல்பூங் கானல்","விரிநீர்ச் சேர்ப்பன் நீப்பி னொருநம்","இன்னுயி ரல்லது பிறிதொன்","றெவனோ தோழி நாமிழப் பதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine asks her friend what else they would lose, if the lord of the wide-watered shore left them, than their own one sweet life: he is lord of the many-flowered grove where the small white red-beaked sea crow, its wet back soaked by the spray of breaking waves, sits sulking at the cold.","n":"The bird sulking (புலந்து) at the cold looks like a lonely lover, but it does nothing that a person of the situation does; it carries a mood only, so it is scene, not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 334","lb":"KU334","poet":"இளம்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["காக்கைச்","காக்கை",1,"pul","scene","sea crow"],["திரைத்","திரை",2,"pira","scene","wave"],["கானல்","கானல்",3,"maram","scene","shore grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:335":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":336,"t":["நிரைவளை முன்கை நேரிழை மகளிர்","இருங்கல் வியலறைச் செந்தினை பரப்பிச்","சுனைபாய் சோர்விடை நோக்கிச் சினையிழிந்து","பைங்கண் மந்தி பார்ப்போடு கவரும்","வெற்பிடை நண்ணி யதுவே வார்கோல்","வல்விற் கானவர் தங்கைப்","பெருந்தோட் கொடிச்சி யிருந்த வூரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the village where the broad-shouldered hill girl, sister of hunters with long-shafted strong bows, lives lies close to the hill where bangled girls spread red millet on the wide dark rock and, when they slip off to bathe in the spring, the green-eyed female monkey climbs down the branches and carries it off with her young.","n":"Near miss for ullurai: the monkey watching for the moment the guards slip (சோர்விடை நோக்கி) could match a lover's stealth, and the bow-armed brothers the guarded girl, but neither poem nor colophon states the situation it would point to, so the scene stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 335","lb":"KU335","poet":"இருந்தையூர்க் கொற்றன் புலவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மகளிர்","மகளிர்",1,"seythi","scene","girls"],["செந்தினை","தினை",2,"unavu","scene","red millet"],["சுனைபாய்","சுனை",3,"pira","scene","spring"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",4,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["பார்ப்போடு","பார்ப்பு",4,"maa","scene","young"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",6,"seythi","scene","hunters"],["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",7,"seythi","scene","hill girl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:336":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":337,"t":["செறுவர்க் குவகை யாகத் தெறுவர","ஈங்கனம் வருபவோ தேம்பாய் துறைவ","சிறுநா வொண்மணி விளரி யார்ப்பக்","கடுமா நெடுந்தேர் நேமி போகிய","இருங்கழி நெய்தல் போல","வருந்தின ளளியணீ பிரிந்திசி னோளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the honeyed shore whether one should come like this, painfully, to the delight of enemies, for the girl he left suffers, pitiable, like the neythal of the dark backwater run over by the rim of his long chariot with swift horses while its small-tongued bright bells ring a mournful tune.","n":"The flower is crushed by his own chariot on its way, so the simile lays the harm at his door; 'the delight of enemies' is the gossip of those who are glad to see her fall.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","melivu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 336","lb":"KU336","poet":"குன்றியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["மணி","மணி",3,"pira","scene","bell"],["விளரி","விளரி",3,"yaazh","scene","mournful melody"],["கடுமா","மா",4,"maa","scene","horse"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",5,"maram","simile","water-lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:337":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":338,"t":["முலையே முகிழ்முகிழ்த் தனவே தலையே","கிளைஇய மென்குரல் கிழக்கு வீழ்ந் தனவே","செறிமுறை வெண்பலும் பறிமுறை நிரம்பின","சுணங்குஞ் சிலதோன் றினவே யணங்கென","யான்ற னறிவல் தானறி யலளே","யாங்கா குவள்கொ றானே","பெருமுது செல்வ ரொருமட மகளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says her breasts have budded, her soft gathered hair now hangs down, her close-set white teeth have come in again in order, and a few beauty spots have appeared; he knows she is a stricken power, but she does not know it: what will become of her, the one young daughter of very old rich parents?","n":"The body is catalogued as it changes from child to woman; the man's fear for her (யாங்கா குவள்) is also a fear for himself, since the only daughter of old, rich parents will be hard to win.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","ilamai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 337","lb":"KU337","poet":"பொதுக்கயத்துக் கீரந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kaikkilai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:338":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":339,"t":["திரிமருப் பிரலை யண்ணல் நல்லேறு","அரிமடப் பிணையோ டல்குநிழ லசைஇ","வீததை வியலரில் துஞ்சிப் பொழுதுசெலச்","செழும்பயறு கறிக்கும் புன்கண் மாலைப்","பின்பனிக் கடைநாள் தண்பனி அற்சிரம்","வந்தன்று பெருவிறல் தேரே பணைத்தோள்","விளங்குநக ரடங்கிய கற்பின்","நலங்கே ழரிவை புலம்பசா விடவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that at the sorrowful evening, when the fine stag with twisted horns, having rested in the shade with his gentle spotted doe and slept in the wide flower-strewn thicket, nibbles the rich green gram as the hour passes, in the cold dewy days at the end of the late dew season, the chariot of great victory has come, so that the loneliness and pallor of the lovely wife of restrained chastity in the bright house may leave.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","pasalai","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 338","lb":"KU338","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"karpu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பிரலை","இரலை",1,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["பிணையோ","பிணை",2,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["செழும்பயறு","பயறு",4,"unavu","scene","green gram"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பிரலை","confirmed","1/1","as the stag stays by his doe at the evening hour, the man has come back to stay with his wife"]],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:339":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":340,"t":["நறையகில் வயங்கிய நளிபுன நறும்புகை","உறையறு மையிற் போகிச் சாரற்","குறவர் பாக்கத் திழிதரு நாடன்","மயங்குமலர்க் கோதை நன்மார்பு முயங்கல்","இனிதுமன் வாழி தோழி மாயிதழ்க்","குவளை யுண்கண் கலுழப்","பசலை யாகா வூங்கலங் கடையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that to embrace the fine chest, with its garland of mixed flowers, of the lord of the land where the dense fragrant smoke of akil burned in the wide field drifts off like a cloud with no rain in it and comes down into the kuravar hamlet on the slope, is sweet indeed, but only until it brings tears to her dark kuvalai eyes and pallor.","n":"The smoke that looks like a rain-cloud and gives no rain may shadow a love that promises and brings no marriage; the poem does not draw the match, so it is left as a mood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","pasalai","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 339","lb":"KU339","poet":"பேயார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நறையகில்","அகில்",1,"maram","scene","agarwood"],["குறவர்","குறவர்",3,"seythi","scene","hill folk"],["கோதை","கோதை",4,"maram","ornament","garland"],["குவளை","குவளை",6,"maram","simile","blue water-lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:340":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":341,"t":["காமங் கடையிற் காதலர்ப் படர்ந்து","நாமவர்ப் புலம்பி னம்மோ டாகி","ஒருபாற் படுதல் செல்லா தாயிடை","அழுவ நின்ற அலர்வேய் கண்டல்","கழிபெயர் மருங்கி னொல்கி யோதம்","பெயர்தரப் பெயர்தந் தாங்கு","வருந்துந் தோழியவ ரிருந்தவென் நெஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The heroine says that when desire reaches its limit, her heart, longing for her lover yet pitying her and staying with her, cannot settle on either side; like the blossoming kandal standing in the deep water that bends toward where the backwater runs out and turns back when the tide returns, her heart, where he dwells, suffers.","n":"The kandal is rooted: it bends with the ebb and is pulled back by the flood but never leaves its place, which is the heart's state exactly, moving both ways and going nowhere.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 340","lb":"KU340","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கண்டல்","கண்டல்",4,"maram","simile","mangrove"],["யோதம்","ஓதம்",5,"pira","simile","tide"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:17"},"ku:341":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":342,"t":["பல்வீ படரிய பசுநனைக் குரவம்","பொரிப்பூம் புன்கொடு பொழிலணிக் கொளாஅச்","சினையினி தாகிய காலையுங் காதலர்","பேணா ராயினும் பெரியோர் நெஞ்சத்துக்","கண்ணிய ஆண்மை கடவ தன்றென","வலியா நெஞ்சம் வலிப்ப","வாழ்வேன் தோழியென் வன்க ணானே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that even in this season when the kuravam is spread with many blossoms and the punku with its puffed-rice flowers adorns the grove, though her lover does not cherish her, she will make her unwilling heart firm, telling it that the manly purpose great men set their hearts on is not to be faulted, and so live on by her own hardness.","n":"She answers her own grief with the man's own argument: the great are right to pursue their manly work (ஆண்மை), so she will not blame it, and she calls the strength that lets her survive her வன்கண், a word for cruelty or hardness, as if enduring were itself a fault. The colophon's neythal has nothing in the poem to support it: the flowers are of the spring, the season of paalai.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aanmai","nenju","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 341","lb":"KU341","poet":"மிளைகிழார் நல்வேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["குரவம்","குரவம்",1,"maram","scene","kuravam"],["புன்கொடு","புன்கு",2,"maram","scene","punku tree"],["பொழிலணிக்","பொழில்",2,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:342":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":343,"t":["கலைகை தொட்ட கமழ்சுளைப் பெரும்பழம்","காவல் மறந்த கானவன் ஞாங்கர்க்","கடியுடை மரந்தொறும் படுவலை மாட்டும்","குன்ற நாட தகுமோ பைஞ்சுனைக்","குவளைத் தண்தழை யிவளீண்டு வருந்த","நயந்தோர் புன்கண் தீர்க்கும்","பயந்தலைப் படாஅப் பண்பினை எனினே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man of the hill country, where the forest-dweller who forgot to guard the big fragrant-segmented jackfruit a male monkey has already handled now sets snares on every guarded tree, whether it is fitting that this girl suffers here over the cool kuvalai leaf-skirt he gave, if he is one whose nature never comes to the point of relieving the distress of those who love him.","n":"The leaf-skirt was a lover's gift; the friend makes it the cause of the girl's suffering, since the household has noticed it. The snares set after the fruit is touched read as the guard that comes too late, which is the household watch that now keeps the girl in.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalavu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 342","lb":"KU342","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம்பட்டினத்துக் கந்தரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கலைகை","கலை",1,"maa","ullurai","male monkey"],["பெரும்பழம்","பெரும் பழம்",1,"maram","ullurai","jackfruit"],["கானவன்","கானவன்",2,"seythi","ullurai","forest hunter"],["படுவலை","படு வலை",3,"seythi","scene","snare"],["பைஞ்சுனைக்","சுனை",4,"pira","scene","spring"],["குவளைத்","குவளை",5,"maram","ornament","blue lily"],["தண்தழை","தழை",5,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","கலைகை கானவன்","confirmed","2/2","the man who has already won the girl and the household that now guards her too late"]],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:343":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":344,"t":["நினையாய் வாழி தோழி நனைகவுள்","அண்ணல் யானை அணிமுகம் பாய்ந்தென","மிகுவலி இரும்புலிப் பகுவா யேற்றை","வெண்கொடு செம்மறுக் கொளீஇ விடர்முகைக்","கோடை யொற்றிய கருங்கால் வேங்கை","வாடுபூஞ் சினையிற் கிடக்கும்","உயர்வரை நாடனொடு பெயரும் ஆறே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the girl to think about the road that leaves with the man of the high hills, a road where a mighty tiger with gaping mouth that leapt at the adorned face of a tusker with musth-wet cheeks lies, its white teeth stained red, on the drooping flowered branch of a black-trunked vengai bent by the west wind at the mouth of a cleft.","n":"The tiger has fought and now rests, its prey not named as killed; the road is dangerous, but the danger lies spent on a flowering branch, and the friend may be telling the girl that with this man even such a road can be taken. Whether she urges the journey or warns against it turns on நினையாய், which can be 'consider it' or 'you do not consider'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","acham","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 343","lb":"KU343","poet":"ஈழத்துப் பூதன்றேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","scene","elephant"],["இரும்புலிப்","புலி",3,"maa","scene","tiger"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",5,"maram","scene","vengai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:344":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":345,"t":["நோற்றோர் மன்ற தோழி தண்ணெனத்","தூற்றுந் துவலைப் பனிக்கடுந் திங்கட்","புலம்பயி ரருந்த அண்ண லேற்றொடு","நிலந்தூங் கணல வீங்குமுலைச் செருத்தல்","பால்வார்பு குழவி யுள்ளி நிரையிறந்","தூர்வயிற் பெயரும் புன்கண் மாலை","அரும்பெறற் பொருட்பிணிப் போகிப்","பிரிந்துறை காதலர் வரக்காண் போரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that those who see their lovers come back, lovers who left in pursuit of rare wealth, must surely have done penance, in this cold harsh month of blown spray, in the sad evening when the cows with swollen udders, having grazed the field crops with the great bull, drip milk thinking of their calves, leave the herd and head for the village.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","paruvam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 344","lb":"KU344","poet":"குறுங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["துவலைப்","துவலை",2,"pira","scene","spray"],["புலம்பயி","புலம் பயிர்",3,"maram","scene","field crop"],["லேற்றொடு","ஏறு",3,"maa","scene","bull"],["செருத்தல்","செருத்தல்",4,"maa","iraicchi","milch cow"],["பால்வார்பு","பால்",5,"unavu","scene","milk"],["குழவி","குழவி",5,"maa","scene","calf"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","செருத்தல்","confirmed","1/1","even the cattle go home at evening to those who need them, but he stays away for wealth"]],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:345":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":346,"t":["இழையணிந் தியல்வருங் கொடுஞ்சி நெடுந்தேர்","வரைமருள் நெடுமணல் தவிர்த்துநின் றசைஇத்","தங்கினி ராயின் தவறோ தெய்ய","தழைதாழ் அல்குல் இவள்புலம் பகலத்","தாழை தைஇய தயங்குதிரைக் கொடுங்கழி","இழுமென ஒலிக்கும் ஆங்கண்","பெருநீர் வேலியெம் சிறுநல் லூரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man whether it would be a fault if he halted his tall chariot with its ornaments and curved seat on the long sand that looks like a hill and rested and stayed, so that the loneliness of this girl with the drooping leaf-skirt might leave her, at their small good town fenced by the great water, where the curving backwater with its shining waves, laced with thazhai, roars.","n":"The invitation is phrased as a question about blame (தவறோ), so it is the friend who takes the risk of asking; her loneliness (புலம்பு) is to end by his staying in the town, not by a night tryst.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","kalavu","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 345","lb":"KU345","poet":"அண்டர்மகன் குறுவழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தழைதாழ்","தழை",4,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["தாழை","தாழை",5,"maram","scene","screw pine"],["தயங்குதிரைக்","திரை",5,"pira","scene","wave"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:346":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":347,"t":["நாகுபிடி நயந்த முளைக்கோட் டிளங்களிறு","குன்றம் நண்ணிக் குறவர் ஆர்ப்ப","மன்றம் போழு நாடன் தோழி","சுனைப்பூங் குவளைத் தொடலை தந்தும்","தினைப்புன மருங்கிற் படுகிளி யோப்பியும்","காலை வந்து மாலைப் பொழுதில்","நல்லக நயந்துதான் உயங்கிச்","சொல்லவும் ஆகா தஃகி யோனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the man of the land where a young tusker with budding tusks, desiring a young she-elephant, comes near the hill and, while the hill people shout, cuts across the village common, came in the morning bringing a garland of spring kuvalai, chased the parrots by the millet field, and at evening, longing for her, wearied and shrank away unable even to speak.","n":"The bold elephant that cuts through the common in spite of the shouting is set against the man who, having come with gifts and served at the millet field, cannot get a word out: the friend's picture of his boldness outside and helplessness before her is a plea that the girl take pity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 346","lb":"KU346","poet":"வாயிலிளங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நாகுபிடி","பிடி",1,"maa","ullurai","young she-elephant"],["டிளங்களிறு","களிறு",1,"maa","ullurai","tusker"],["குறவர்","குறவர்",2,"seythi","ullurai","hill folk"],["குவளைத்","குவளை",4,"maram","ornament","blue lily"],["தினைப்புன","தினை",5,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கிளி","கிளி",5,"pul","scene","parrot"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","டிளங்களிறு குறவர்","confirmed","1/1","the young man who wants the girl and comes into the village despite its talk"]],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:347":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":348,"t":["மல்குசுனை உலர்ந்த நல்கூர் சுரமுதற்","குமரி வாகைக் கோலுடை நறுவீ","மடமாத் தோகைக் குடுமியிற் றோன்றும்","கான நீளிடைத் தானு நம்மொடு","ஒன்றுமணஞ் செய்தனள் இவளெனின்","நன்றே நெஞ்சம் நயந்தநின் துணிவே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart that if she has made one union with them on the long forest road at the edge of the wasteland where the full springs have dried, where the fragrant flowers of the young vaakai on their stalks look like the crest of a young dark peacock, then good is the resolve it desired.","n":"The road is dry, yet he sees in it a flower like a peacock's crest: with her beside him, the waste already looks adorned. ஒன்றுமணம், a union that makes them one, is the elopement read as a marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","nenju","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 347","lb":"KU347","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம்பட்டினத்துச் சேந்தங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மல்குசுனை","சுனை",1,"pira","absent","spring"],["வாகைக்","வாகை",2,"maram","simile","vaakai tree"],["தோகைக்","தோகை",3,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:348":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":349,"t":["தாமே செல்ப வாயிற் கானத்துப்","புலந்தேர் யானைக் கோட்டிடை யொழிந்த","சிறுவீ முல்லைக் கொம்பிற் றாஅய்","இதழழிந் தூறுங் கண்பனி மதரெழிற்","பூணக வனமுலை நனைத்தலும்","காணார் கொல்லோ மாணிழை நமரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the girl whether, if they go off by themselves, the man will not see her tears, which, like the small mullai flowers caught on the tusks of an elephant searching the land in the forest and scattered with crushed petals, flow and wet her lovely breasts with their ornaments.","n":"The simile takes the flowers from the elephant of the road he will travel, so the tears are pictured with the very road that causes them; the question 'will he not see' hopes that her weeping will stop his going.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kan","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 348","lb":"KU348","poet":"மாவளத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["யானைக்","யானை",2,"maa","simile","elephant"],["முல்லைக்","முல்லை",3,"maram","simile","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:349":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":350,"t":["அடும்பவிழ் அணிமலர் சிதைஇமீன் அருந்தும்","தடந்தாள் நாரை இருக்கும் எக்கர்த்","தண்ணந் துறைவற் றொடுத்து நந்நலம்","கொள்வாம் என்றி தோழி கொள்வாம்","இடுக்கண் அஞ்சி இரந்தோர் வேண்டிய","கொடுத்தவை தாவென் சொல்லினும்","இன்னா தோநம் இன்னுயிர் இழப்பே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend, who says they should take back their beauty from the lord of the cool shore where on the sand dune sits the big-legged stork that tears the lovely opened adumbu flowers and eats fish, that they could take it, but would losing their dear life be more painful than saying 'give back what was given' to those who, fearing hardship, begged and received.","n":"She grants the friend's claim and refuses it: a gift cannot be asked back, so her beauty, once given, is his even if she dies of it. The giver's code of the puram poets becomes the measure of a lover's dignity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","eegai","maanam","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 349","lb":"KU349","poet":"சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அடும்பவிழ்","அடும்பு",1,"maram","ullurai","adumbu creeper"],["மீன்","மீன்",1,"pira","ullurai","fish"],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","ullurai","stork"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","நாரை","confirmed","1/1","the man who spoiled her beauty for his pleasure and now sits at ease"]],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:350":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":351,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி முன்னின்று","பனிக்கடுங் குரையஞ் செல்லா தீமெனச்","சொல்லின மாயிற் செல்வர் கொல்லோ","ஆற்றய லிருந்த இருந்தோட் டஞ்சிறை","நெடுங்காற் கணந்துள் ஆளறி வுறீஇ","ஆறுசெல் வம்பலர் படைதலை பெயர்க்கும்","மலையுடைக் கான நீந்தி","நிலையாப் பொருட்பிணிப் பிரிந்திசி னோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says to the woman: had they stood before him and said 'we cannot bear the cold harsh season, do not go', would he have gone, the one who left in the grip of unstable wealth across the hilly forest where the long-legged lapwing with dark blades of wings sits by the path and, by sensing men, makes the travellers turn their weapons forward.","n":"The friend blames their own silence, not his going: had they spoken he would have stayed. நிலையாப் பொருள், wealth that does not stay, is the ethicists' phrase, here turned into the argument that he gave up what lasts for what does not. The bird's part in lines 5 and 6 is uncertain: it may warn travellers of robbers or warn robbers of travellers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","nilaiyaamai","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 350","lb":"KU350","poet":"ஆலத்தூர் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கணந்துள்","கணந்துள்",5,"pul","scene","lapwing"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:351":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":352,"t":["வளையோய் உவந்திசின் விரைவுறு கொடுந்தாள்","அளைவாழ் அலவன் கூருகிர் வரித்த","ஈர்மணல் மலிர்நெறி சிதைய இழுமென","உருமிசைப் புணரி உடைதரும் துறைவற்கு","உரிமை செப்பினர் நமரே விரியலர்ப்","புன்னை ஓங்கிய புலாலஞ் சேரி","இன்னகை ஆயத் தாரோடு","இன்னும் அற்றோஇவ் வழுங்க லூரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the bangled girl to rejoice: their people have declared the right to the lord of the shore where the thunder-voiced waves break with a roar, ruining the wet sand path the swift crooked-legged crab that lives in holes had striped with its sharp claws; and she asks whether this gossiping town, with the sweet-smiling girls of the fish-smelling quarter where blossoming punnai rise, is still the same.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 351","lb":"KU351","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["அலவன்","அலவன்",2,"pira","ullurai","crab"],["புணரி","புணரி",4,"pira","ullurai","breaking wave"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",6,"maram","scene","punnai"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","அலவன் புணரி","confirmed","1/1","the secret love and its traces, erased by the public declaration of marriage"]],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:352":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":353,"t":["நெடுநீ ராம்பல் அடைப்புறத் தன்ன","கொடுமென் சிறைய கூருகிர்ப் பறவை","அகலிலைப் பலவின் சாரல் முன்னிப்","பகலுறை முதுமரம் புலம்பப் போகும்","சிறுபுன் மாலை உண்மை","அறிவேன் தோழியவர்க் காணா ஊங்கே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that she learned the reality of the small dull evening, when the sharp-clawed creature with curved soft wings like the back of a long-stemmed water-lily leaf heads for the slope of broad-leaved jackfruit, leaving lonely the old tree where it rested by day, only after she stopped seeing him.","n":"The colophon files it under paalai; the poem has no road and no waste, only the evening hour and a woman who waits, so it is read here by its hour and its situation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 352","lb":"KU352","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ராம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","simile","water lily"],["பறவை","பறவை",2,"pul","iraicchi","bat"],["பலவின்","பலா",3,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["முதுமரம்","முது மரம்",4,"maram","scene","old tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பறவை","confirmed","1/2","as the bat abandons its day-tree at evening, so he left her, and she is the tree left lonely"],["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:353":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":354,"t":["ஆர்கலி வெற்பன் மார்புபுணை யாகக்","கோடுயர் நெடுவரைக் கவாஅற் பகலே","பாடின் அருவி ஆடுதல் இனிதே","நிரையிதழ் பொருந்தாக் கண்ணோ டிரவிற்","பஞ்சி வெண்திரிச் செஞ்சுடர் நல்லிற்","பின்னுவீழ் சிறுபுறந் தழீஇ","அன்னை முயங்கத் துயிலின் னாதே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that by day, bathing in the resounding waterfall on the slope of the tall-peaked hill with the noisy hill lord's chest as a raft is sweet, but at night, in the good house with the red flame of a white cotton wick, with eyes whose lined lids will not close, to sleep while mother embraces the small back where the braids fall is bitter.","n":"Day and night are set side by side: the daytime meeting is sweet, but the nights under the mother's guarding embrace are sleepless, so day meetings are no longer enough and he must marry her. The mother's embrace, a comfort, is felt as a guard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","iravu","punarchi","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 353","lb":"KU353","poet":"உறையூர் முதுகூற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",3,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["பஞ்சி","பஞ்சி",5,"maram","scene","cotton"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:354":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":355,"t":["நீர்நீ டாடிற் கண்ணுஞ் சிவக்கும்","ஆர்ந்தோர் வாயில் தேனும் புளிக்கும்","தணந்தனை யாயினெம் இல்லுய்த்துக் கொடுமோ","அந்தண் பொய்கை எந்தை எம்மூர்க்","கடும்பாம்பு வழங்குந் தெருவில்","நடுங்கஞர் எவ்வம் களைந்த எம்மே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that if one bathes too long even the eyes turn red and in the mouth of the sated even honey turns sour; if he has grown distant, let him take them back to their house, them whom he once freed from trembling fear and distress in the street where fierce snakes move in their father's town of the cool pond.","n":"Two maxims of satiety open the poem, and the friend lets them stand as the explanation of his coldness without accusing him; then she reminds him he was once the one who brought the girl out of fear, so returning her home would undo his own rescue.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","pirivu","pazhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 354","lb":"KU354","poet":"கயத்தூர்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தேனும்","தேன்",2,"unavu","scene","honey"],["பொய்கை","பொய்கை",4,"pira","scene","pond"],["கடும்பாம்பு","பாம்பு",5,"maa","scene","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:355":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":356,"t":["பெயல்கண் மறைத்தலின் விசும்புகா ணலையே","நீர்பரந் தொழுகலின் நிலங்கா ணலையே","எல்லை சேறலின் இருள்பெரிது பட்டன்று","பல்லோர் துஞ்சும் பானாட் கங்குல்","யாங்குவந் தனையோ ஓங்கல் வெற்ப","வேங்கை கமழுமெஞ் சிறுகுடி","யாங்கறிந் தனையோ நோகோ யானே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the tall hill how he came in the dead of a night when many sleep, when rain blinds the eyes so the sky cannot be seen, water spreads and flows so the ground cannot be seen, and day has gone so the dark is deep, and how he found their small hamlet where the vengai is fragrant; and she says she grieves.","n":"Three clauses each take away a way of seeing (sky, ground, light), so the question 'how did you come' is fear for him, and under it a hint that such nights should end in marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kuri","kalavu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 355","lb":"KU355","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",6,"maram","scene","vengai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:356":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":357,"t":["நிழலான் றவிந்த நீரில் ஆரிடைக்","கழலோன் காப்பக் கடுகுபு போகி","அறுசுனை மருங்கின் மறுகுபு வெந்த","வெவ்வெங் கலுழி தவ்வெனக் குடிக்கிய","யாங்கு வல்லுநள்கொல் தானே ஏந்திய","செம்பொற் புனைகலத் தம்பொரிக் கலந்த","பாலும் பலவென உண்ணாள்","கோலமை குறுந்தொடித் தளிரன் னோளே."],"p":"","r":"The foster mother wonders how she will manage, the girl like a tender shoot with small shapely bangles, who would not drink the milk mixed with fine puffed rice held up in a fashioned vessel of red gold, saying it was too much, now that, guarded by the anklet-wearing man, she hurries through the hard waterless way where the shade has died, and by a dried-up spring must gulp the scalding muddy water.","n":"The contrast is all in the mouth: milk in a gold bowl refused at home, scalding mud gulped on the road. The man appears only as the anklet-wearer who guards her, so the mother's grief is not against him but for the child's body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai","paasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 356","lb":"KU356","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செவிலி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["அறுசுனை","சுனை",3,"pira","absent","dried spring"],["கலுழி","கலுழி",4,"pira","gift_food","muddy water"],["தம்பொரிக்","பொரி",6,"unavu","gift_food","puffed rice"],["பாலும்","பால்",7,"unavu","gift_food","milk"],["தளிரன்","தளிர்",8,"maram","simile","tender shoot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:357":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":358,"t":["முனிபடர் உழந்த பாடில் உண்கண்","பனிகால் போழ்ந்து பணியெழில் ஞெகிழ்தோள்","மெல்லிய ஆகலின் மேவரத் திரண்டு","நல்ல என்னுஞ் சொல்லை மன்னிய","ஏனலஞ் சிறுதினை காக்குஞ் சேணோன்","ஞெகிழியிற் பெயர்ந்த நெடுநல் யானை","மின்படு சுடரொளி வெரூஉம்","வான்தோய் வெற்பன் மணவா ஊங்கே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that before the lord of the sky-touching hill, where the tall fine elephant that turned back from the firebrand of the watchman guarding the small millet fears even the gleam of lightning, came to unite with her, her shoulders were rounded and always earned the word 'good'; now her collyrium-lined eyes, sleepless and labouring in hateful grief, run with tears, and her shoulders, their beauty bowed, have grown thin and slip.","n":"The elephant scared once by the watchman's firebrand that now fears even lightning may hint at a man made timid by the household's watch, who no longer comes; but the poem draws no pair beyond one mood, so it stays scene. The syntax of lines 1 to 4 is hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nalam","melivu","varaivu","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 357","lb":"KU357","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஏனலஞ்","ஏனல்",5,"unavu","scene","millet"],["சேணோன்","சேணோன்",5,"seythi","scene","platform watchman"],["ஞெகிழியிற்","ஞெகிழி",6,"pira","scene","firebrand"],["யானை","யானை",6,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:358":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":359,"t":["வீங்கிழை நெகிழ விம்மி யீங்கே","எறிகண் பேதுற லாய்கோ டிட்டுச்","சுவர்வாய் பற்றுநின் படர்சே ணீங்க","வருவேம் என்ற பருவம் உதுக்காண்","தனியோர் இரங்கும் பனிகூர் மாலைப்","பல்லான் கோவலர் கண்ணிச்","சொல்லுப அன்ன முல்லைவெண் முகையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman, who sobs here with her heavy bangles slipping, bewildered, her darting eyes, scoring lines and clinging to the wall, that the season he promised, saying he would come so that her distant grief would end, is there to see: in the cold evening when the lonely grieve, the white buds of the mullai are like the cowherds' garlands, as if they spoke it.","n":"The scoring of lines on the wall is the counting of days, and the friend answers it with a sign outside the wall: the buds are the promise kept by the season, so he must be near. Line 7 was repaired from Swaminatha Aiyar's text and its syntax remains hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 358","lb":"KU358","poet":"கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பல்லான்","ஆன்",6,"maa","scene","cattle"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",6,"seythi","simile","cowherds"],["கண்ணிச்","கண்ணி",6,"maram","simile","garland"],["முல்லைவெண்","முல்லை",7,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:359":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":360,"t":["கண்டிசிற் பாண பண்புடைத் தம்ம","மாலை விரிந்த பசுவெண் ணிலவிற்","குறுங்கால் கட்டில் நறும்பூஞ் சேக்கைப்","பள்ளி யானையின் உயிர்த்தனன் நசைஇ","புதல்வற் றழீஇயினன் விறலவன்","புதல்வன் தாயவன் புறங்கவைஇ யினளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the bard to see how seemly it is: in the evening under the spreading fresh white moonlight, on the short-legged cot with its fragrant flower bed, the man, breathing like an elephant at rest, longingly embraced his son, and the son's mother embraced the victorious man's back.","n":"No one embraces the other directly: the father holds the son and the mother holds the father from behind, so the child closes the circle. Told to the bard, the go-between of the man's other loves, the scene also says his office is no longer needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","makkal","oodal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 359","lb":"KU359","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["யானையின்","யானை",4,"maa","simile","elephant"],["நறும்பூஞ்","பூ",3,"maram","scene","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:360":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":361,"t":["வெறியென உணர்ந்த வேல னோய்மருந்","தறியா னாகுதல் அன்னை காணிய","அரும்படர் எவ்வம் இன்றுநாம் உழப்பினும்","வாரற்க தில்ல தோழி சாரற்","பிடிக்கை அன்ன பெருங்குரல் ஏனல்","உண்கிளி கடியும் கொடிச்சிகைக் குளிரே","சிலம்பிற் சிலம்புஞ் சோலை","இலங்குமலை நாடன் இரவி னானே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that even if today they must bear the grievous pining because their mother sees that the velan, taking it for possession, does not know the cure for the sickness, may he not come at night, the lord of the shining hill with groves that echo on the slopes, where the rattle in the hill girl's hand scares off the parrots eating the big-eared millet like a she-elephant's trunk.","n":"She would rather suffer the mistaken rite at home than have him risk the night path: the veri rite misreads her sickness, and the one true cure she forbids for his sake.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","velan","iravu","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 360","lb":"KU360","poet":"மதுரை ஈழத்துப் பூதன்றேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பிடிக்கை","பிடி கை",5,"maa","simile","she-elephant trunk"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",5,"unavu","simile","millet"],["கிளி","கிளி",6,"pul","scene","parrot"],["கொடிச்சிகைக்","கொடிச்சி",6,"seythi","scene","hill girl"],["குளிரே","குளிர்",6,"pira","scene","rattle"],["சோலை","சோலை",7,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:18"},"ku:361":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":362,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி அன்னைக்","குயர்நிலை உலகமுஞ் சிறிதால் அவர்மலை","மாலைப் பெய்த மணங்கமழ் உந்தியொடு","காலை வந்த காந்தள் முழுமுதல்","மெல்லிலை குழைய முயங்கலும்","இல்லுய்த்து நடுதலுங் கடியா தோளே."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that her mother is worth more than the high world itself, for she did not forbid her to clasp, crushing its soft leaves, the whole kaanthal plant that the flood of the evening rain on his hill brought down in the morning, nor to carry it home and plant it.","n":"The mother's not forbidding is praised as above heaven because she does not know what the plant is: the girl embraces the hill of her lover in a flower the flood carried, and the mother's innocence is the girl's whole freedom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kalaviyal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 361","lb":"KU361","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",4,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["பெய்த","பெயல்",3,"pira","scene","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:362":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":363,"t":["முருகயர்ந் துவந்த முதுவாய் வேல","சினவ லோம்புமதி வினவுவ துடையேன்","பல்வே றுருவிற் சில்லவிழ் மடையொடு","சிறுமறி கொன்றிவள் நறுநுதல் நீவி","வணங்கினை கொடுத்தி யாயின் அணங்கிய","விண்தோய் மாமலைச் சிலம்பன்","ஒண்தார் அகலமும் உண்ணுமோ பலியே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the old-mouthed diviner who came rejoicing in Murugan's rite to hold back his anger and answer one question: if he kills a small kid, offers rice-balls of many colours, strokes the girl's fragrant brow and makes his worship, will the bright-garlanded chest of the man of the sky-touching hill who afflicted her also eat the offering?","n":"அணங்கிய names the man as the possessing power the rite blames on the god, so the question to the velan reveals the secret (அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்) while seeming only to mock the rite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 362","lb":"KU362","poet":"வேம்பற்றூர்க் கண்ணன் கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["முருகயர்ந்","முருகு",1,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"],["வேல","வேலன்",1,"seythi","scene","diviner"],["மடையொடு","மடை",3,"unavu","gift_food","offered rice"],["மறி","மறி",4,"maa","gift_food","kid"],["பலியே","பலி",7,"unavu","gift_food","offering"],["ஒண்தார்","தார்",7,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:363":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":364,"t":["கண்ணி மருப்பின் அண்ணல்நல் லேறு","செங்கோற் பதவின் வார்குரல் கறிக்கும்","மடக்கண் மரையா நோக்கிவெய் துற்றுப்","புல்லரை உகாஅய் வரிநிழல் வதியும்","இன்னா அருஞ்சுரம் இறத்தல்","இனிதோ பெரும இன்றுணைப் பிரிந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man whether it is sweet to cross the cruel hard waste, leaving his sweet companion, where the fine bull with garland-ringed horns chews the long-eared red-stemmed grass and, looking at his soft-eyed doe, suffers the heat and rests with her in the thin striped shade of the hollow-trunked ukaay.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 363","lb":"KU363","poet":"செல்லூர்க் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["லேறு","ஏறு",1,"maa","iraicchi","bull deer"],["மரையா","மரை ஆ",3,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["பதவின்","பதவு",2,"maram","gift_food","grass"],["உகாஅய்","உகாய்",4,"maram","scene","ukaay tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","லேறு","confirmed","2/2","even the beast of the waste keeps beside its mate in hardship, so the man should not leave his"]],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:364":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":365,"t":["அரிற்பவர்ப் பிரம்பின் வரிப்புற நீர்நாய்","வாளை நாளிரை பெறூஉம் ஊரன்","பொற்கோல் அவிர்தொடித் தற்கெழு தகுவி","எற்புறங் கூறும் என்ப தெற்றென","வணங்கிறைப் பணைத்தோள் எல்வளை மகளிர்","துணங்கை நாளும் வந்தன அவ்வரைக்","கண்பொர மற்றதன் கண்ணவர்","மணங்கொளற் கிவரும் மள்ளர் போரே."],"p":"","r":"The household concubine says that the proud wife of the man of the town where the otter among the tangled cane gets a vaalai fish for its daily food speaks behind her back, they say; well, the days of the thunangai dance of the bright-bangled women have come, and there, when eyes clash, comes the contest of young men eager to take brides.","n":"The otter that takes a vaalai every day is the traditional figure for the husband enjoying her without effort, but the poem names no situation it maps onto (the anchor is the wife's slander, not his pleasure), so it stays scene; the concubine answers the slander by a public challenge at the festival, not by denial.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 364","lb":"KU364","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"இற்பரத்தை கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","pira",null,"karpu","இற்பரத்தை கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பிரம்பின்","பிரம்பு",1,"maram","scene","rattan"],["நீர்நாய்","நீர்நாய்",1,"maa","scene","otter"],["வாளை","வாளை",2,"pira","scene","vaalai fish"],["அவிர்தொடித்","தொடி",3,"pira","ornament","armlet"],["எல்வளை","வளை",5,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["மள்ளர்","மள்ளர்",8,"seythi","scene","young warriors"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:365":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":366,"t":["கோடீர் இலங்குவளை நெகிழ நாளும்","பாடில கலிழ்ந்து பனியா னாவே","துன்னரும் நெடுவரைத் ததும்பிய அருவி","தண்ணென் முழவின் இமிழிசை காட்டும்","மருங்கிற் கொண்ட பலவிற்","பெருங்கல் நாடநீ நயந்தோள் கண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the land of great rocks, where the waterfall brimming on the tall peak no one can approach sounds like a cool drum beside the jackfruit trees, that the eyes of the girl he loves weep every day and never stop dripping while her shining conch bangles slip.","n":"The falls that sound like a drum in his own land hint at the wedding drum he has not yet sounded; that is a single match of mood, not two pairs, so it is left as a simile.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kaamanoy","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 365","lb":"KU365","poet":"மதுரை நல்வெள்ளியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["இலங்குவளை","வளை",1,"pira","ornament","conch bangle"],["அருவி","அருவி",3,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["முழவின்","முழவு",4,"parai","scene","drum"],["பலவிற்","பலா",5,"maram","scene","jackfruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:366":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":367,"t":["பால்வரைந் தமைத்த லல்ல தவர்வயிற்","சால்பளந் தறிதற் கியாஅம் யாரோ","வேறு யான் கூறவும் அமையாள் அதன்தலைப்","பைங்கண் மாச்சுனைப் பல்பிணி யவிழ்ந்த","வள்ளிதழ் நீலம் நோக்கி உள்ளகை","பழுத கண்ண ளாகிப்","பழூதன் றம்மவிவ் வாயிழை துணிவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that, beyond what destiny has fixed and arranged, who are we to measure the man's worth; when I spoke otherwise the girl would not accept it, and looking at the wide-petalled blue lily that has burst open in the green-eyed dark pool, her eyes ripened with tears: this jewelled girl's resolve is not false.","n":"பால் is the destiny that pairs lovers; the friend argues that the choice is not hers to judge, and the girl's tears at a flower like her own eyes are offered as proof. Lines 5 and 6 (உள்ளகை பழுத கண்ணள்) are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","karpu","thitpam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 366","lb":"KU366","poet":"பேரிசாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாச்சுனைப்","சுனை",4,"pira","scene","mountain pool"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",5,"maram","scene","blue lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:367":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":368,"t":["கொடியோர் நல்கா ராயினும் யாழநின்","தொடிவிளங் கிறைய தோள்கவின் பெறீஇயர்","உவக்காண் தோழி அவ்வந் திசினே","தொய்யல் மாமழை தொடங்கலின் அவர்நாட்டுப்","பூச லாயம் புகன்றிழி அருவியின்","மண்ணுறு மணியின் தோன்றும்","தண்ணறுந் துறுகல் ஓங்கிய மலையே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that even if the cruel man shows no grace, her bangled shoulders may regain their beauty: look there, now that the dense dark rain has begun, his hill has come into view in his land, its cool fragrant boulders shining like a washed gem in the falls down which the noisy troop of girls slides with delight.","n":"The sight of his hill is offered as a cure in itself: the friend does not promise he will come, only that seeing his land washed by the first rains will restore her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","mazhai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 367","lb":"KU367","poet":"மதுரை மருதனிள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாமழை","மழை",4,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["அருவியின்","அருவி",5,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["மணியின்","மணி",6,"pira","scene","gem"],["துறுகல்","துறுகல்",7,"pira","scene","boulder"],["தொடிவிளங்","தொடி",2,"pira","ornament","armlet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:368":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":369,"t":["மெல்லிய லோயே மெல்லிய லோயே","நன்னாண் நீத்த பழிதீர் மாமை","வன்பின் ஆற்றுதல் அல்லது செப்பிற்","சொல்ல கிற்றா மெல்லிய லோயே","சிறியரும் பெரியரும் வாழும் ஊர்க்கே","நாளிடைப் படாஅ நளிநீர் நீத்தத்","திண்கரைப் பெருமரம் போலத்","தீதில் நிலைமை முயங்குகம் பலவே."],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her gentle friend, three times over, that she can only bear with strength the shame of a dark beauty that has lost its good modesty, and cannot say it aloud; for the sake of the town where small and great live, let them embrace him often in a blameless state, like the great tree on the firm bank of a flood that never fails.","n":"The riverbank tree is the town's common shade, standing in a flood that comes every day: her quarrel would be a public loss, so she makes endurance a civic duty. Lines 2 and 3 are obscure, and the sense of நன்னாண் நீத்த is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","porai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 368","lb":"KU368","poet":"நக்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நளிநீர்","நீர்",6,"pira","simile","flood water"],["பெருமரம்","மரம்",7,"maram","simile","tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:369":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":370,"t":["அத்த வாகை அமலை வானெற்","றரியார் சிலம்பி னரிசி யார்ப்பக்","கோடை தூக்குங் கானம்","செல்வாந் தோழி நல்கினர் நமரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that their man has granted it: let us go through the forest of the waste, where the west wind swings the dense white pods of the vaakai and their seeds rattle like the pebbles of an anklet.","n":"The anklet's rattle in the dry pods is a wedding sound heard in the waste: the friend makes the dreaded road ring like the girl's own feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 369","lb":"KU369","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வாகை","வாகை",1,"maram","scene","vaakai tree"],["சிலம்பி","சிலம்பு",2,"pira","scene","anklet"],["கோடை","கோடை",3,"pira","scene","west wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:370":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":371,"t":["பொய்கை யாம்ப லணிநிறக் கொழுமுகை","வண்டுவாய் திறக்குந் தண்டுறை யூரனொடு","இருப்பி னிருமருங் கினமே கிடப்பின்","வில்லக விரலிற் பொருந்தியவன்","நல்லகஞ் சேரி னொருமருங் கினமே."],"p":"","r":"The courtesan says that with the man of the cool ford, where bees open the beautiful plump bud of the water-lily in the pond, when they sit they are two side by side, when they lie they fit together like fingers round a bow, and when he goes to his good home they are one, left on one side.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","punarchi","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 370","lb":"KU370","poet":"வில்லகவிரலினார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தை கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","pira",null,"karpu","பரத்தை கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["யாம்ப","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","ullurai","water-lily"],["வண்டுவாய்","வண்டு",2,"pira","ullurai","bee"],["பொய்கை","பொய்கை",1,"pira","scene","pond"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","வண்டுவாய் யாம்ப","confirmed","1/2","the man who opens a young woman's love and moves on"]],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:371":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":372,"t":["கைவளை நெகிழ்தலும் மெய்பசப் பூர்தலும்","மைபடு சிலம்பின் ஐவனம் வித்தி","அருவியின் விளைக்கும் நாடனொடு","மருவேன் தோழியது காமமோ பெரிதே."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that though she has not joined the man of the land where hill rice sown on the cloud-dark slope is ripened by the waterfall, her bangles slip and pallor spreads over her body: that love is great indeed.","n":"மருவேன் makes the paradox: the signs of a love affair without its embrace. The hill rice needs no irrigation, as her love needs no meeting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pasalai","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 371","lb":"KU371","poet":"உறையூர் முதுகூற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["ஐவனம்","ஐவனம்",2,"maram","ullurai","hill rice"],["அருவியின்","அருவி",3,"pira","ullurai","waterfall"],["கைவளை","வளை",1,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","ஐவனம் அருவியின்","not_confirmed","0/1","the love he sowed, which grows of itself without his tending"]],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:372":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":373,"t":["பனைத்தலைக், கருக்குடை நெடுமடல் குருத்தொடு மாயக்","கடுவளி தொகுத்த நெடுவெண் குப்பைக்","கணங்கொள் சிமைய வணங்குங் கானல்","ஆழிதலை வீசிய வயிர்ச்சேற் றருவிக்","கூழைபெய் எக்கர்க் குழீஇய பதுக்கை","புலர்பதங் கொள்ளா வளவை","அலரெழுந் தன்றிவ் வழங்க லூரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that in this town of passers-by gossip rose before the mound on the sand ridge could dry, a heap the sea threw up with flowing mud where the fierce wind piles long white dunes that bury the palmyras' crowns, their saw-edged fronds and shoots, in the shore grove where crested heaps bow.","n":"The wind piling sand over the palms looks like a figure of gossip burying the lovers, but the poem gives no pairs beyond the swiftness, so it is scene; the shore that keeps reshaping itself is the measure of how fast talk moves.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 372","lb":"KU372","poet":"விற்றூற்று மூதெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பனைத்தலைக்","பனை",1,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["கடுவளி","வளி",2,"pira","scene","wind"],["ஆழி","ஆழி",4,"pira","scene","sea"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:373":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":374,"t":["நிலம்புடை பெயரினு நீர்தீப் பிறழினும்","இலங்குதிரைப் பெருங்கடற் கெல்லை தோன்றினும்","வெவ்வாய்ப் பெண்டிர் கௌவை அஞ்சிக்","கேடெவன் உடைத்தோ தோழி நீடுமயிர்க்","கடும்பல் ஊகக் கறைவிரல் ஏற்றை","புடைத்தொடு புடைஇப் பூநாறு பலவுக்கனி","காந்தளஞ் சிறுகுடிக் கமழும்","ஓங்குமலை நாடனொ டமைந்தநந் தொடர்பே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks what harm can come, even if the earth shifts its place, water and fire change natures and the great sea of bright waves shows a shore, from fear of the hot-mouthed women's gossip to their bond with the man of the high hill, where the long-haired, sharp-toothed, black-fingered male langur beats the flower-scented jackfruit until it perfumes the kaanthal-fenced hamlet.","n":"The jackfruit's scent filling the hamlet after the langur beats it is a near miss for ullurai (the affair spreading through the village), but only one pair holds; the adynata of earth, fire, water and sea are the poem's own claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","alar","thitpam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 373","lb":"KU373","poet":"மதுரைக் கொல்லம் புல்லனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஊகக்","ஊகம்",5,"maa","scene","langur"],["பலவுக்கனி","பலா",6,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["காந்தளஞ்","காந்தள்",7,"maram","scene","glory lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:374":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":375,"t":["எந்தையும் யாயும் உணரக் காட்டி","ஒளித்த செய்தி வெளிப்படக் கிளந்தபின்","மலைகெழு வெற்பன் தலைவந் திரப்ப","நன்றுபுரி கொள்கையின் ஒன்றா கின்றே","முடங்கல் இறைய தூங்கணங் குரீஇ","நீடிரும் பெண்ணைத் தொடுத்த","கூடினும் மயங்கிய மைய லூரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that after she made father and mother understand and spoke out the hidden matter, the man of the hill came and asked for the girl, and in his good resolve it was all agreed; the town, deluded, is more tangled than the nests the curved-backed weaver birds hang from the tall dark palmyra.","n":"The weaver's nest is a marvel of order that looks like chaos; the town that misread the secret is more tangled still, now that the truth has ended in a proper asking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 374","lb":"KU374","poet":"உறையூர்ப் பல்காயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["குரீஇ","குருவி",5,"pul","scene","weaver bird"],["பெண்ணைத்","பெண்ணை",6,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:375":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":376,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி இன்றவர்","வாரா ராயினோ நன்றே சாரற்","சிறுதினை விளைந்த வியன்கண் இரும்புனத்","திரவரி வாரின் தொண்டகச் சிறுபறை","பானாள் யாமத்துங் கறங்கும்","யாமங் காவலர் அவியா மாறே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says it is good if he does not come today, for on the wide dark field of the slope where the small millet has ripened, if the striped one comes by night, the watchmen's small thondakam drum sounds even at midnight, since the night guards never sleep.","n":"Wishing him away is the friend's way of naming the dangers he braves; the drum guards the millet against the tiger and so guards the girl against him too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 375","lb":"KU375","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["சிறுதினை","தினை",3,"maram","scene","millet"],["திரவரி","வரி",4,"maa","scene","tiger"],["தொண்டகச்","தொண்டகம்",4,"parai","scene","hill drum"],["காவலர்","காவலர்",6,"seythi","scene","night guards"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:376":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":377,"t":["மன்னுயிர் அறியாத் துன்னரும் பொதியிற்","சூருடை அடுக்கத் தாரங் கடுப்ப","வேனி லானே தண்ணியள் பனியே","வாங்குகதிர் தொகுப்பக் கூம்பி ஐயென","அலங்குவெயிற் பொதிந்த தாமரை","உள்ளகத் தன்ன சிறுவெம் மையளே."],"p":"","r":"The man says that in summer she is cool like the sandal of the dread slopes of Pothiyil, hard to reach and unknown to living men, and in the cold season she is a little warm like the heart of a lotus that closed softly, holding the swaying sunlight, when the sun gathered its bent rays.","n":"She is a counter-climate: the lotus that shuts at sunset keeps the day's warmth inside, as she keeps warmth for the cold months.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","azhagu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 376","lb":"KU376","poet":"படுமாத்து மோசிகொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["சூருடை","சூர்",2,"deyvam","scene","dread deity"],["தாரங்","ஆரம்",2,"maram","simile","sandal"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",5,"maram","simile","lotus"]],"en":[["hill","பொதியிற்",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:377":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":378,"t":["மலர் ஏர் உண்கண் மாண் நலம் தொலைய,","வளையேர் மென்றோள் ஞெகிழ்ந்ததன் தலையும்","மாற்றா கின்றே தோழியாற் றலையே","அறிதற் கமையா நாடனொடு","செய்து கொண்டதோர் சிறுநன் னட்பே."],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that the beauty of her flower-like kohl-lined eyes is gone and, on top of that, her soft shoulders fair as bangles have grown thin, and there is no undoing the small good friendship she made with the man of a land beyond knowing.","n":"சிறுநன் னட்பு is bitter: a small, good friendship has cost her eyes and shoulders. Line 1 is supplied from another text and line 3 (தோழியாற் றலையே) is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","melivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 377","lb":"KU377","poet":"மோசி கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["மலர்","மலர்",1,"maram","scene","flower"],["வளையேர்","வளை",2,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:378":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":379,"t":["ஞாயிறு காயாது மரநிழற் பட்டு","மலைமுதற் சிறுநெறி மணன்மிகத் தாஅய்த்","தண்மழை தலைய வாகுக நந்நீத்துச்","சுடர்வாய் நெடுவேற் காளையொடு","மடமா அரிவை போகிய சுரனே."],"p":"","r":"The foster mother prays that the sun may not burn on the waste the young girl crossed, leaving us, with the youth of the bright-pointed long spear; may it be tree-shade, may the small path at the foot of the hill be thick with sand, and may cool clouds hang over it.","n":"Each wish undoes one property of paalai (sun, shadeless path, burning ground, cloudless sky), so the blessing is a description of the waste in the negative.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","anbu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 378","lb":"KU378","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செவிலி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"karpu","செவிலி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மரநிழற்","மரம்",1,"maram","scene","tree shade"],["மணன்","மணல்",2,"pira","scene","sand"],["தண்மழை","மழை",3,"pira","scene","cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:379":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":380,"t":["இன்றியாண் டையனோ தோழி குன்றத்துப்","பழங்குழி அகழ்ந்த கானவன் கிழங்கினொடு","கண்ணகன் தூமணி பெறூஉம் நாடன்","அறிவுகாழ்க் கொள்ளும் அளவைச் செறிதொடி","எம்மில் வருகுவை நீயெனப்","பொம்மல் ஓதி நீவி யோனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks where he is today, the man of the land where the hunter digging an old pit on the hill finds, with the tubers, a wide-faced pure gem: he who stroked your thick hair and said 'girl of close bangles, when your understanding ripens, you will come into our house.'","n":"The promise was conditional on her growing up (அறிவுகாழ்க் கொள்ளும் அளவை); the friend's 'where is he today' turns the image against him: he found the gem and has not taken it home.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 379","lb":"KU379","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கானவன்","கானவன்",2,"seythi","ullurai","hunter"],["கிழங்கினொடு","கிழங்கு",2,"unavu","ullurai","tuber"],["தூமணி","மணி",3,"pira","ullurai","gem"],["செறிதொடி","தொடி",4,"pira","ornament","bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","கானவன் தூமணி","confirmed","1/1","the man who sought a pleasure and found a wife"]],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:380":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":381,"t":["விசும்புகண் புதையப் பாஅய் வேந்தர்","வென்றெறி முரசின் நன்பல முழங்கிப்","பெயலா னாதே வானம் காதலர்","நனிசேய் நாட்டர் நம்முன் னலரே","யாங்குச்செய் வாங்கொல் தோழி யீங்கைய","வண்ணத் துய்ம்மலர் உதிர","முன்னர்த் தோன்றும் பனிக்கடு நாளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says the sky, spreading to blind the heavens and roaring many times like the drums kings beat in victory, does not stop raining; our lover is in a land very far off, not before us; how shall we bear it, friend, now that the soft-tufted eengai flowers fall and the cold harsh days stand before us?","n":"The season of his promised return has not only come but is passing into the cold; the cloud sounds like a king's victory drum, a reminder of what keeps men away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 380","lb":"KU380","poet":"கருவூர்க் கதப்பிள்ளை","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தோழி கூற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["முரசின்","முரசு",2,"parai","scene","war drum"],["வானம்","வானம்",3,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["யீங்கைய","ஈங்கை",5,"maram","scene","eengai"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:19"},"ku:381":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":382,"t":["தொல்கவின் தொலைந்து தோணலஞ் சாஅய்","அல்லல் நெஞ்சமோ டல்கலும் துஞ்சாது","பசலை யாகி விளிவது கொல்லோ","வெண்குருகு நரலுந் தண்கமழ் கானற்","பூமலி பொதும்பர் நாண்மலர் மயக்கி","விலங்குதிரை உடைதருந் துறைவனொ","டிலங்கெயிறு தோன்ற நக்கதன் பயனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether the girl, her old beauty gone, her shoulders wasted, her troubled heart sleepless even at night, will turn pale and die: that is all the fruit of once laughing, her bright teeth showing, with the lord of the shore, where white herons cry in the cool fragrant grove and the crosswise waves break, tangling the fresh flowers of the blossoming thicket.","n":"The whole cost is weighed against one laugh (நக்கதன் பயனே): a moment of shown teeth is set against lost beauty, sleeplessness and death. The waves that break and tangle the fresh flowers could be heard as the man who ruined her, but that is a single match of mood, not two pairs, so the shore stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kalaviyal","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 381","lb":"KU381","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வெண்குருகு","வெண் குருகு",4,"pul","scene","white heron"],["நாண்மலர்","நாள் மலர்",5,"maram","scene","fresh flowers"],["விலங்குதிரை","விலங்கு திரை",6,"pira","scene","crosswise waves"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:382":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":383,"t":["தண்துளிக் கேற்ற பைங்கொடி முல்லை","முகைதலைத் திறந்த நாற்றம் புதல்மிசை","பூவமல் தளவமொடு தேங்கமழ்பு கஞல","வம்புப் பெய்யுமால் மழையே வம்பன்று","காரிது பருவம் ஆயின்","வாரா ரோநம் காத லோரே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that the green mullai creeper, made for cool drops, has opened its buds, its scent rising over the bushes with the flower-laden jasmine, because the clouds pour untimely rain: if this were not untimely but the true rainy season, would our lover not have come?","n":"The comfort is an argument from the man's word to the season: since he promised to come with the rains and has not come, these cannot be the rains. The friend turns the proof of his failure into proof that the flowers are mistaken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 382","lb":"KU382","poet":"குறுங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",1,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["தளவமொடு","தளவம்",3,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["மழையே","மழை",4,"pira","scene","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:383":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":384,"t":["நீயுடம் படுதலின் யான்தர வந்து","குறிநின் றனனே குன்ற நாடன்","இன்றை யளவை சென்றைக் கென்றி","கையுங் காலும் ஓய்வன அழுங்கத்","தீயுறு தளிரின் நடுங்கி","யாவதும் இலையான் செயற்குரி யதுவே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that because she consented, the friend brought him and the man of the hills has come and stands at the meeting place; now she says today's meeting must wait for another day, and the friend, her hands and feet going slack with distress, trembles like a shoot touched by fire, with nothing she can do.","n":"The friend is caught between the two lovers: she arranged the meeting on the girl's word and must now face the man at the tryst. Line 3 (இன்றை யளவை சென்றைக் கென்றி) is hard to construe; the reading 'not today, another day' is a guess. The colophon's paalai does not fit a tryst on the hill.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 383","lb":"KU383","poet":"படுமரத்து மோசிகீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தளிரின்","தளிர்",5,"maram","simile","young shoot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:384":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":385,"t":["உழுந்துடைக் கழுந்திற் கரும்புடைப் பணைத்தோள்","நெடும்பல் கூந்தற் குறுந்தொடி மகளிர்","நலனுண்டு துறத்தி யாயின்","மிகநன் றம்ம மகிழ்நநின் சூளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that if he enjoys the beauty of the girls, with thick shoulders painted with sugarcane designs, long many-stranded hair and small bangles, and then abandons them, his oath is very fine indeed.","n":"மிகநன்று is praise turned to scorn: the oath he swore is 'very good' precisely because it keeps him free to take and leave. Line 1 (உழுந்துடைக் கழுந்திற்) is unclear: it may name a town of black-gram fields or compare the shoulders to a pounding pestle, so the girls' description is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 384","lb":"KU384","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["உழுந்துடைக்","உழுந்து",1,"unavu","scene","black gram"],["கரும்புடைப்","கரும்பு",1,"maram","ornament","sugarcane design"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:385":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":386,"t":["பலவிற் சேர்ந்த பழமார் இனக்கலை","சிலைவிற் கானவன் செந்தொடை வெரீஇச்","செருவுறு குதிரையிற் பொங்கிச் சாரல்","இருவெதிர் நீடமை தயங்கப் பாயும்","பெருவரை அடுக்கத்துக் கிழவோன் என்றும்","அன்றை யன்ன நட்பினன்","புதுவோர்த் தம்மவிவ் வழுங்க லூரே."],"p":"","r":"The girl says the lord of the great mountain slope, where a male monkey of the troop, full of fruit on the jackfruit tree, startled by the hunter's well-aimed bow, leaps like a war-horse so that the tall bamboo of the slope shakes, is always as friendly as on the first day; it is this town with its clamour that is new to her.","n":"She turns the blame from the man to the town: he has not changed, the gossip has. The startled monkey leaping so the bamboo shakes could be read as the lovers disturbed by the town's aim, but only the hunter's aim and the town's talk can be paired, and the poem names no harm, so the monkey stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 385","lb":"KU385","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பலவிற்","பலா",1,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["இனக்கலை","இனக் கலை",1,"maa","scene","male monkey"],["கானவன்","கானவன்",2,"seythi","scene","hunter"],["குதிரையிற்","குதிரை",3,"maa","simile","horse"],["இருவெதிர்","இரு வெதிர்",4,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:386":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":387,"t":["வெண்மணல் விரிந்த வீததை கானல்","தண்ணந் துறைவன் தணவா ஊங்கே","வாலிழை மகளிர் விழவணிக் கூட்டும்","மாலையோ அறிவேன் மன்னே மாலை","நிலம்பரந் தன்ன புன்கணொடு","புலம்புடைத் தாகுதல் அறியேன் யானே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that before the lord of the cool shore of spread white sand and crowded flowers left her, she knew evening only as the time that gathers bright-jewelled women for festival; she did not know that evening could be lonely, with a sorrow as wide as the earth.","n":"The same word மாலை closes line 4 and opens line 5, the festival evening turning into the lonely one at the line break. Her ignorance before is part of the complaint: the evening she knew was a public time of women together, the one she learns is private.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 386","lb":"KU386","poet":"வெள்ளிவீதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வெண்மணல்","வெண் மணல்",1,"pira","scene","white sand"],["வீததை","வீ",1,"maram","scene","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:387":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":388,"t":["எல்லை கழிய முல்லை மலரக்","கதிர்சினந் தணிந்த கையறு மாலையும்","இரவரம் பாக நீந்தின மாயின்","எவன்கொல் வாழி தோழி","கங்குல் வெள்ளம் கடலினும் பெரிதே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that even if, when the day's limit passes, the mullai blooms and the sun's rays cool their anger, they swim across the helpless evening and reach the night as its bank, what good is it: the flood of night is greater than the sea.","n":"The evening is a river crossed with the night as its far bank; reaching the bank only opens a second water, the night itself, larger than the sea. The poet was named for this line (கங்குல் வெள்ளத்தார்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 387","lb":"KU387","poet":"கங்குல் வெள்ளத்தார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",1,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:388":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":389,"t":["நீர்கால் யாத்த நிரையிதழ்க் குவளை","கோடை ஒற்றினும் வாடா தாகும்","கவணை அன்ன பூட்டுப்பொரு தசாஅ","உமணெருத் தொழுகைத் தோடுநிரைத் தன்ன","முளிசினை பிளக்கு முன்பின் மையின்","யானை கைமடித் துயவும்","கானமும் இனியவாம் நும்மொடு வரினே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that the row-petalled kuvalai bound to running water does not wither even if summer strikes it, and so the forest, where the dry branches stand like the long line of salt merchants' bullock carts straining at their yokes and the elephant, too weak to split them, folds its trunk and suffers, will be sweet if the girl comes with him.","n":"The lily is never said to be the girl; the friend sets it first and lets the forest follow, and the last line supplies the match. Line 3 (கவணை அன்ன பூட்டுப்பொரு தசாஅ) is hard to construe: the comparison of the yoke is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 388","lb":"KU388","poet":"ஔவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குவளை","குவளை",1,"maram","ullurai","water lily"],["உமணெருத்","உமண் எருது",4,"seythi","simile","salt merchants' bulls"],["முளிசினை","முளி சினை",5,"maram","scene","dry branches"],["யானை","யானை",6,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","குவளை","not_confirmed","0/1","the girl who will go with the man"]],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:389":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":390,"t":["நெய்கனி குறும்பூழ் காய மாக","ஆர்பதம் பெறுக தோழி அத்தை","பெருங்கல் நாடன் வரைந்தென அவனெதிர்","நன்றோ மகனே யென்றனென்","நன்றே போலும் என்றுரைத் தோனே."],"p":"","r":"The friend wishes, friend, that she may get a full meal with ghee-rich quail as its relish: when the man of the great hills came to ask for her in marriage, she said to him 'is it good, son?', and he answered 'it seems good'.","n":"Who is to be feasted, and who said 'is it good, son?', is unclear: என்றனென் is first person, so the friend may be reporting her own question to the suitor, and அத்தை may be a particle rather than a kinswoman. The relish of quail is the reward of good news.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 389","lb":"KU389","poet":"வேட்டகண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["நெய்கனி","நெய்",1,"unavu","gift_food","ghee"],["குறும்பூழ்","குறும்பூழ்",1,"pul","gift_food","quail"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:390":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":391,"t":["எல்லும் எல்லின்று பாடுங் கேளாய்","செல்லா தீமோ சிறுபிடி துணையே","வேற்றுமுனை வெம்மையிற் சாத்துவந் திறுத்தென","வளையணி நெடுவேல் ஏந்தி","மிளைவந்து பெயரும் தண்ணுமைக் குரலே."],"p":"","r":"Onlookers tell the man, mate of the young she-elephant, not to go on: the day has lost its light, and he should listen to the sound, for a caravan has halted because of the danger of the hostile frontier, and the drum of the guards, who come to the thorn hedge carrying long ringed spears and go back, is sounding.","n":"The onlookers name the man only through the girl, as the tusker of a young cow-elephant, so their warning is for her. The danger is told by sound alone: the caravan has stopped, and the guards' drum carries the frontier's threat after dark.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","iravu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 390","lb":"KU390","poet":"உறையூர் முதுகொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"கண்டோர் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["kandor","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["சிறுபிடி","சிறு பிடி",2,"maa","scene","young she-elephant"],["சாத்துவந்","சாத்து",3,"seythi","scene","caravan"],["தண்ணுமைக்","தண்ணுமை",5,"parai","scene","drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:391":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":392,"t":["உவரி யொருத்தல் உழாது மடியப்","புகரி புழுங்கிய புயனீங்கு புறவிற்","கடிதிடி உருமிற் பாம்புபை அவிய","இடியொடு மயங்கி இனிதுவீழ்ந் தன்றே","வீழ்ந்த மாமழை தழீஇப் பிரிந்தோர்","கையற வந்த பையுள் மாலைப்","பூஞ்சினை யிருந்த போழ்கண் மஞ்ஞை","தாஅம் நீர் நனந்தலை புலம்பக்","கூஉந் தோழி பெரும்பே தையவே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that where the bull of the salt ground lay idle without ploughing and the heat had steamed in the rainless scrub, the rain has fallen sweetly, mingled with thunder that strikes so fast the snake's hood shrinks; and in the sorrowful evening that came to leave the separated helpless, the peacock with eyed plumes, sitting on the flowering branch, calls across the wide wet land till it is desolate: they are very foolish.","n":"The peacocks rejoice at the rain that makes her helpless, and she calls them fools for it. The snake whose hood shrinks at the thunder sits beside her own shrinking, but it does nothing a person of the situation does, so it stays scene. Lines 1 and 2 (உவரி யொருத்தல், புகரி புழுங்கிய) are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","maalai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 391","lb":"KU391","poet":"பொன்மணியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["யொருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",1,"maa","scene","bull"],["பாம்புபை","பாம்பு",3,"pira","scene","snake"],["உருமிற்","உருமு",3,"pira","scene","thunder"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",7,"pul","scene","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:392":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":393,"t":["அம்ம வாழியோ அணிச்சிறைத் தும்பி","நன்மொழிக் கச்ச மில்லை யவர்நாட்","டண்ணல் நெடுவரைச் சேறி யாயிற்","கடமை மிடைந்த துடவையஞ் சிறுதினைத்","துளரெறி நுண்டுகட் களைஞர் தங்கை","தமரின் தீராள் என்மோ அரசர்","நிரைசெல னுண்டோல் போலப்","பிரசந் தூங்கு மலைகிழ வோற்கே."],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the bright-winged dragonfly, which need fear nothing for carrying good news, to go to the noble one's tall mountain and tell the lord of the hills, where honeycombs hang like the hide shields of kings' marching ranks, that the sister of the weeders who hoe the small millet field crowded with wild cattle is not let out by her kin.","n":"The message is a warning dressed as news: the girl is kept at home by her kin, so the man must come and marry. Calling her the weeders' sister names her as a hill girl of a working family, and the honeycombs like kings' shields make his hill a kingdom fit to send for her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","thoothu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 392","lb":"KU392","poet":"தும்பிசேர் கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தும்பி","தும்பி",1,"pira","scene","dragonfly"],["கடமை","கடமை",4,"maa","scene","wild cattle"],["சிறுதினைத்","சிறு தினை",4,"maram","scene","millet"],["களைஞர்","களைஞர்",5,"seythi","scene","weeders"],["பிரசந்","பிரசம்",8,"pira","simile","honeycomb"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:393":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":394,"t":["மயங்குமலர்க் கோதை குழைய மகிழ்நன்","முயங்கிய நாடவச் சிலவே அலரே","கூகைக் கோழி வாகைப் பறந்தலைப்","பசும்பூண் பாண்டியன் வினைவ லதிகன்","களிறொடு பட்ட ஞான்றை","ஒளிறுவாட் கொங்கர் ஆர்ப்பினும் பெரிதே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says the days on which the lover embraced, crushing a garland of mixed flowers, were few; but the gossip is louder than the shout of the Kongar with flashing swords on the day when Athikan, the skilled general of the Pandiyan with green ornaments, fell with his elephant on the battlefield of Vaakai where owls haunt.","n":"The measure of the gossip is a defeat: the Kongar's victory cry over the Pandiyan's fallen general. Whom the husband embraced is not said, so the friend may be scoffing at the scandal of his other women or at the cost of the few days he gave the wife. Athikan, the general, is named in line 4.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 393","lb":"KU393","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோதை","கோதை",1,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கூகைக்","கூகை",3,"pul","scene","owl"],["களிறொடு","களிறு",5,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["battle","வாகைப் பறந்தலை",3],["king","பாண்டியன்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:394":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":395,"t":["முழந்தாள் இரும்பிடிக் கயந்தலைக் குழவி","நறவுமலி பாக்கத்துக் குறமகள் ஈன்ற","குறியிறைப் புதல்வரொடு மறுவந் தோடி","முன் நாள் இனிய தாகிப் பின்னாள்","அவர்தினைப் புனம் மேய்ந் தாங்குப்","பகையா கின்றவர் நகைவிளை யாட்டே."],"p":"","r":"The friend says that their laughing play with the man has turned into an enemy, as the soft-headed calf of the big she-elephant with knee-high legs, running and frolicking with the children of the low-eaved huts whom the hill woman of the toddy-rich hamlet bore, was sweet in its early days and later grazed on their millet field.","n":"The calf does no wrong: it grows, and the same play that charmed the children ends in their field being eaten. So the affair has not changed its nature; it has only grown into a thing that costs the girl her standing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kalaviyal","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 394","lb":"KU394","poet":"குறியிரையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இரும்பிடிக்","இரும் பிடி",1,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["குழவி","குழவி",1,"maa","simile","calf"],["நறவுமலி","நறவு",2,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["குறமகள்","குறமகள்",2,"seythi","scene","hill woman"],["அவர்தினைப்","தினை",5,"maram","simile","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:395":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":396,"t":["நெஞ்சே நிறையொல் லாதே யவரே","அன்பின் மையின் அருள்பொருள் என்னார்","வன்கண் கொண்டு வலித்துவல் லுநரே","அரவுநுங்கு மதியினுக் கிவணோர் போலக்","களையார் ஆயினுங் கண்ணினிது படீஇயர்","அஞ்ச லென்மரும் இல்லை அந்தில்","அளிதோ தானே நாணே","ஆங்கவர் வதிவயின் நீங்கப் படினே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her heart that restraint is beyond her: he, having no love, does not count grace as worth anything and holds firm in his hardness; even if he will not free her as the people here free the moon the snake swallows, there is no one even to say 'fear not' so that her eyes may sleep; pitiable is her shame, if she must leave it to go where he lives.","n":"At an eclipse the people make a din to free the swallowed moon; she is swallowed and no one comes, not even to say a word of comfort. The last two lines are hard: they may mean that shame is pitiable if she leaves home for him, or that it is lost already.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nenju","naanam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 395","lb":"KU395","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","nenju",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அரவுநுங்கு","அரவு",4,"pira","simile","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:396":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":397,"t":["பாலும் உண்ணாள் பந்துடன் மேவாள்","விளையாடு ஆயமொடு அயர்வோ ளினியே","எளிதென உணர்ந்தனள் கொல்லோ முளிசினை","ஓமை குத்திய உயர்கோட் டொருத்தல்","வேனிற் குன்றத்து வெவ்வரைக் கவாஅன்","மழைமுழங்கு கடுங்குரல் ஓர்க்கும்","கழைதிரங் காரிடை அவனொடு செலவே."],"p":"","r":"The foster mother says the girl would not drink her milk or take up her ball, and was weary with her playmates; did she think it easy, then, the going with him through the land where bamboo withers, on the hot slopes of the summer hills, where the tall-tusked bull elephant that gored the dry-branched omai listens for the harsh voice of thundering clouds?","n":"The foster mother reads the signs too late: the refused milk and the dropped ball were the girl's mind already on the road. The elephant listening for thunder that does not come is the drought she now pictures the girl in.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 396","lb":"KU396","poet":"கமயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செவிலி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பாலும்","பால்",1,"unavu","gift_food","milk"],["ஓமை","ஓமை",4,"maram","scene","omai tree"],["டொருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",4,"maa","scene","bull elephant"],["மழைமுழங்கு","மழை",6,"pira","absent","rain cloud"],["கழைதிரங்","கழை",7,"maram","absent","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:397":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":398,"t":["நனைமுதிர் ஞாழற் தினைமருள் திரள்வீ","நெய்தல் மாமலர்ப் பெய்தல் போல","ஊதை தூற்றும் உரவுநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப","தாயுடன் றலைக்கும் காலையும் வாய்விட்","டன்னா வென்னுங் குழவி போல","இன்னா செயினும் இனிதுதலை யளிப்பினும்","நின்வரைப் பினளென் தோழி","தன்னுறு விழுமங் களைஞரோ இலளே."],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the shore of strong waters, where the cold wind scatters the millet-like clustered flowers of the full-budded gnaazhal onto the large neythal blossoms as though pouring them, that like a baby who cries 'mother' even while its angry mother beats it, her friend, whether he does her harm or shows her sweet kindness, is within his bounds and has no one else to remove her sorrow.","n":"The baby cries 'mother' to the one who is beating it, because there is no one else to cry to; so the girl's dependence is not a virtue to praise but a helplessness the man is asked to respect. The wind pouring one flower onto another is a gentle image set before the harsh one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","adaikkalam","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 397","lb":"KU397","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஞாழற்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene","gnaazhal tree"],["தினைமருள்","தினை",1,"maram","simile","millet"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","scene","water lily"],["ஊதை","ஊதை",3,"pira","scene","cold wind"],["குழவி","குழவி",5,"seythi","simile","baby"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:398":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":399,"t":["தேற்றா மன்றே தோழி தண்ணெனத்","தூற்றுந் துவலைத் துயர்கூர் காலைக்","கயலே ருண்கட் கனங்குழை மகளிர்","கைபுணை யாக நெய்பெய்து மாட்டிய","சுடர்துய ரெடுப்பும் புன்கண் மாலை","அரும்பெறற் காதலர் வந்தென விருந்தயர்பு","மெய்ம்மலி யுவகையி னெழுதரு","கண்கலி ழுகுபனி யரக்கு வோரே."],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that they cannot be consoled in the cold time when the spray scatters, in the sorrowful evening when women with carp-like kohl-lined eyes and heavy earrings, cupping their hands as a raft, lift the lamp-flame lit with ghee; those women, when their hard-won lovers come, prepare a feast and, with joy filling their bodies, wipe away the tears that rise.","n":"The lamp-lighting of the evening is seen from outside: other wives shelter the flame and later weep for joy at their men's return. Who the last lines describe is uncertain; they may instead be the friend's promise of a joy the woman refuses to believe in (தேற்றாம்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 398","lb":"KU398","poet":"பாலைபாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["துவலைத்","துவலை",2,"pira","scene","spray"],["கயலே","கயல்",3,"pira","simile","carp"],["நெய்பெய்து","நெய்",4,"unavu","scene","ghee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:399":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":400,"t":["ஊருண் கேணி யுண்டுறைக் தொக்க","பாசி யற்றே பசலை காதலர்","தொடுவுழித் தொடுவுழி நீங்கி","விடுவுழி விடுவுழிப் பரத்த லானே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says her pallor is like the moss at the drinking ghat of the village well: wherever her lover touches, it parts, and wherever he lets go, it spreads again.","n":"The moss parts when a pot or a hand breaks it and closes as soon as the hand is lifted: the pallor is not a slow wasting but a surface that re-forms the moment his touch stops. The doubled தொடுவுழித் தொடுவுழி and விடுவுழி விடுவுழி make the motion repeat in the line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 399","lb":"KU399","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கேணி","கேணி",1,"pira","simile","well"],["பாசி","பாசி",2,"maram","simile","moss"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:400":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":401,"t":["சேயாறு செல்வா மாயின் இடரின்று","களைகலம் காமம் பெருந்தோட் கென்று","நன்றுபுரிந் தெண்ணிய மனத்தை யாகி","முரம்புகண் உடைய வேகிக் கரம்பைப்","புதுவழிப் படுத்த மதியுடை வல்லோய்","இன்று தந்தனை தேரோ","நோயுழந் துறைவியை நல்க லானே."],"p":"","r":"The man tells his charioteer that, resolving with a mind set on good to go the far road and remove without hindrance the love-sickness of the broad-shouldered one, he who is wise and skilled broke across the gravel and led the chariot on a new way through the waste: today he has brought the chariot, and so given grace to her who lived in pain.","n":"The praise goes to the driver, not to the man himself: the charioteer took the shorter, harder way across the gravel because he too thought of the wife. The pain is hers (நோயுழந் துறைவியை), and the gift is the chariot's arrival.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","nanri","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 400","lb":"KU400","poet":"பேயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவன் கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"ku:20"},"ku:401":{"book":"kuruntokai","num":402,"t":["அடும்பி னாய்மலர் விரைஇ நெய்தல்","நெடுந்தொடை வேய்ந்த நீர்வார் கூந்தல்","ஓரை மகளி ரஞ்சியீர் ஞெண்டு","கடலிற் பரிக்குந் துறைவனொ டொருநாள்","நக்குவிளை யாடலுங் கடிந்தன்","றைதே கம்ம மெய்தோய் நட்பே."],"p":"","r":"The woman says that even laughing play, one day, with the lord of the shore, where the girls of the orai game, their wet hair crowned with long strands of neythal mixed with choice adumbu flowers, fear the wet crab that scuttles into the sea, has been forbidden: how slight is the friendship of touching bodies!","n":"The play on the beach with flower-crowned girls who shriek at crabs is the most innocent of meetings, and even that is now forbidden: the girl is kept at home. Line 6 (றைதே கம்ம) divides uncertainly; read as ஐதே, 'slight, fragile'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","natpu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kuruntokai 401","lb":"KU401","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி கூற்று","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["அடும்பி","அடும்பு",1,"maram","ornament","adumbu flower"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","ornament","water lily"],["ஞெண்டு","ஞெண்டு",3,"pira","scene","crab"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"ku:20"},"nt:0":{"book":"natrinai","num":1,"t":["மா நிலம் சேவடி ஆக தூநீர்","வளை நரல் பௌவம் உடுக்கை ஆக","விசும்பு மெய் ஆக திசை கை ஆக","பசுங் கதிர் மதியமொடு சுடர் கண் ஆக","இயன்ற எல்லாம் பயின்று அகத்து அடக்கிய","வேத முதல்வன் என்ப","தீது அற விளங்கிய திகிரியோனே"],"p":"","r":"The invocation: they say that the god of the discus, who shines free of all harm, is the first lord of the Veda, whose feet are the great earth, whose garment is the sea where conches sound, whose body is the sky, whose hands are the quarters, whose eyes are the moon of cool rays with the sun, and who holds within himself all that has come to be.","n":"The god is never named; he is known only by the discus (திகிரியோன்) and drawn as a body made of the cosmos, and the claim is put at one remove (என்ப, 'they say'), as if the poet reports the teaching of the Veda rather than a vision of his own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vedham","andam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 0","lb":"NT0","poet":"பெருந்தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["திகிரியோனே","திகிரியோன்",7,"deyvam","scene","Vishnu"],["வளை","வளை",2,"pira","scene","conch"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:1":{"book":"natrinai","num":2,"t":["நின்ற சொல்லர் நீடுதோறு இனியர்","என்றும் என் தோள் பிரிபு அறியலரே","தாமரைத் தண் தாது ஊதி மீமிசைச்","சாந்தில் தொடுத்த தீம் தேன் போல","புரைய மன்ற புரையோர் கேண்மை","நீர் இன்று அமையா உலகம் போலத்","தம் இன்று அமையா நம் நயந்தருளி","நறு நுதல் பசத்தல் அஞ்சிச்","சிறுமை உறுபவோ செய்பு அறியலரே"],"p":"","r":"The heroine answers the friend who told her he will leave: a man of steady word, sweeter the longer one knows him, he has never known how to part from her shoulders; the bond of the noble is high and pure like the sweet honey bees make from cool lotus pollen and store on a lofty sandal tree, and he, who graciously loves her because she cannot live without him as the world cannot live without water, fearing that her fragrant brow would pale, does not know how to do a mean thing.","n":"Her proof is his character, not his promise: she reasons from what kind of man he is (நின்ற சொல்லர், புரையோர் கேண்மை) to what he cannot do. The honey simile carries the reasoning: a sweetness gathered from the low lotus and stored at the top of the hill tree, a bond made in one land and kept high in another. The world-and-water clause is the formula of Kural 20, bent here to her dependence on him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pirivu","panpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 1","lb":"NT1","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பிரிவு உணர்த்திய தோழிக்குத் தலைவி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவு உணர்த்திய தோழிக்குத் தலைவி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாமரைத்","தாமரை",3,"maram","simile","lotus"],["சாந்தில்","சாந்து",4,"maram","simile","sandal tree"],["தேன்","தேன்",4,"unavu","simile","honey"],["நீர்","நீர்",6,"pira","simile","water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:2":{"book":"natrinai","num":3,"t":["அழுந்துபட வீழ்ந்த பெருந் தண் குன்றத்து","ஒலி வல் ஈந்தின் உலவைஅம் காட்டு","ஆறு செல் மாக்கள் சென்னி எறிந்த","செம் மறுத் தலைய நெய்த்தோர் வாய","வல்லியப் பெருந் தலைக் குருளை மாலை","மான் நோக்கு இண்டு இவர் ஈங்கைய சுரனே","வை எயிற்று ஐயள் மடந்தைமுன் உற்று","எல்லிடை நீங்கும் இளையோன் உள்ளம்","காலொடு பட்ட மாரி","மால் வரை மிளிர்க்கும் உருமினும் கொடிதே"],"p":"","r":"Onlookers on the wasteland road, where in the bare forest of rustling date palms below a great cool hill the big-headed tiger cub, its head stained red and its mouth bloody from the travellers it struck down, eyes the deer at evening among the climbing iṇṭu and īṅkai, say that the youth who goes ahead of his sharp-toothed lovely girl through that country has a heart crueler than the thunder that, with wind-driven rain, shakes the great mountains.","n":"The blame falls on the man alone, and its measure is a storm: beside the tiger cub's bloody evening the onlookers set a heart harder than mountain-shaking thunder, so the danger of the road and the youth's will are made the same kind of force. Line 1's அழுந்துபட and line 8's எல்லிடை (by night, or in the open light) leave the scene's time unsure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","acham","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 2","lb":"NT2","poet":"பெரும்பதுமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உடன் போகாநின்றாரை இடைச் சுரத்துக் கண்டார் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["kandor","kandor",null,"kalavu","இடைச் சுரத்துக் கண்டார் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["ஈந்தின்","ஈந்து",2,"maram","scene","date palm"],["வல்லியப்","வல்லியம்",5,"maa","scene","tiger"],["மான்","மான்",6,"maa","scene","deer"],["இண்டு","இண்டு",6,"maram","scene",""],["ஈங்கைய","ஈங்கை",6,"maram","scene",""],["மாரி","மாரி",9,"pira","simile","rain"],["உருமினும்","உரும்",10,"pira","simile","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:3":{"book":"natrinai","num":4,"t":["ஈன் பருந்து உயவும் வான் பொரு நெடுஞ் சினைப்","பொரி அரை வேம்பின் புள்ளி நீழல்","கட்டளை அன்ன இட்டு அரங்கு இழைத்து","கல்லாச் சிறாஅர் நெல்லி வட்டு ஆடும்","வில் ஏர் உழவர் வெம் முனைச் சீறூர்ச்","சுரன்முதல் வந்த உரன் மாய் மாலை","உள்ளினென் அல்லெனோ யானே உள்ளிய","வினை முடித்தன்ன இனியோள்","மனை மாண் சுடரொடு படர் பொழுது எனவே"],"p":"","r":"The man, whose heart again urges a journey for wealth, recalls an earlier one: at the strength-draining evening that met him at the edge of the wasteland, by a hot frontier hamlet of men whose plough is the bow, where under the dappled shade of a cracked-barked neem with sky-touching boughs, where the brooding kite cried, untaught children scratched a board like a gauge and played dice with gooseberries, did he not think of her, sweet as a finished task, at the hour she moves about the house with its fine lamp?","n":"He likens her to the very thing the journey seeks, a task completed (வினை முடித்தன்ன), so the wife at home already is the success he would go out to win. The children's gooseberry game on the hot frontier pictures a home life he lacked at the hour it most mattered: evening, when the lamp is lit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","maalai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 3","lb":"NT3","poet":"இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"முன் ஒரு காலத்துப் பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்த தலைமகன் பின்னும் பொருள் கடைக் கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"பொருள் கடைக் கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பருந்து","பருந்து",1,"pul","scene","kite"],["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",2,"maram","scene","neem"],["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",4,"maram","scene","gooseberry"],["வில் ஏர் உழவர்","உழவர்",5,"seythi","scene","bowmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:4":{"book":"natrinai","num":5,"t":["கானல் அம் சிறுகுடிக் கடல் மேம் பரதவர்","நீல் நிற புன்னைக் கொழு நிழல் அசைஇ","தண் பெரும் பரப்பின் ஒண் பதம் நோக்கி","அம் கண் அரில் வலை உணக்கும் துறைவனொடு","அலரே அன்னை அறியின் இவண் உறை வாழ்க்கை","அரிய ஆகும் நமக்கு எனக் கூறின்","கொண்டும் செல்வர்கொல் தோழி உமணர்","வெண் கல் உப்பின் கொள்ளை சாற்றி","கண நிரை கிளர்க்கும் நெடு நெறிச் சகடம்","மணல் மடுத்து உரறும் ஓசை கழனிக்","கருங் கால் வெண் குருகு வெரூஉம்","இருங் கழிச் சேர்ப்பின் தம் உறைவின் ஊர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"With the man standing unseen nearby, the friend asks the heroine: if we tell the lord of the shore, where the fishermen of the grove hamlet rest in the rich shade of the dark punnai and dry their nets watching for the bright hour on the cool wide sea, that if mother learns of the gossip our life here will become hard, will he take us away to his own town on the dark backwater coast, where the roar of the salt-sellers' long-road carts, sunk in sand as they cry the sale of white crystal salt and rouse their herds, frightens the black-legged white heron in the fields?","n":"The question is put so that he hears it, and its last image answers it: in his own town the clamour of the salt carts frightens even the heron of the fields, as the clamour of gossip frightens her here. The fishermen idling in the punnai shade while their nets dry is the leisure the lovers have had, now ending. Fear of the mother is entered as accham from இறை, the authority one lives under.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 4","lb":"NT4","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாக தோழி அலர் அச்சம் தோன்றச் சொல்லி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","அலர் அச்சம் தோன்றச் சொல்லி வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",1,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["புன்னைக்","புன்னை",2,"maram","scene",""],["வலை","வலை",4,"seythi","scene","net"],["உமணர்","உமணர்",7,"seythi","scene","salt traders"],["உப்பின்","உப்பு",8,"pira","scene","salt"],["சகடம்","சகடம்",9,"seythi","ullurai","cart"],["குருகு","குருகு",11,"pul","ullurai","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","சகடம் குருகு","confirmed","1/1","the town's gossip and the girl it frightens"]],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:5":{"book":"natrinai","num":6,"t":["நிலம் நீர் ஆர குன்றம் குழைப்ப","அகல் வாய்ப் பைஞ் சுனைப் பயிர் கால்யாப்ப","குறவர் கொன்ற குறைக் கொடி நறைப் பவர்","நறுங் காழ் ஆரம் சுற்றுவன அகைப்ப","பெரும் பெயல் பொழிந்த தொழில எழிலி","தெற்கு ஏர்பு இரங்கும் அற்சிரக் காலையும்","அரிதே காதலர்ப் பிரிதல் இன்று செல்","இளையர்த் தரூஉம் வாடையொடு","மயங்கு இதழ் மழைக் கண் பயந்த தூதே"],"p":"","r":"To the heroine, changed since she sensed the man's plan to leave, the friend says that even in this dewy season, when the clouds that have poured great rain thunder as they rise in the south, the land is soaked, the hills sprout, crops take root by the wide green pools and the fragrant creeper the hill-men cut sprouts again and twines round the sandal, it is hard for a lover to part; and the message her rain-heavy eyes have sent, with the north wind that brings back the young men setting out today, will hold him.","n":"The poem never says he will stay; it lets the season argue (rain, sprouting, the cut creeper twining the sandal again) and calls her tears a தூது, an envoy, as if weeping were already a message he cannot ignore. The cut creeper that sprouts and winds round the sandal can be read as parted lovers rejoined, but it does no act of a person here, so it stays scene. The sense of lines 7 to 9 is unsure, and line 8 is a repaired line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","thoothu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 5","lb":"NT5","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் செலவுக் குறிப்பு அறிந்து வேறுபட்ட தலைவிக்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"தலைவன் செலவுக் குறிப்பு அறிந்து வேறுபட்ட தலைவிக்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குறவர்","குறவர்",3,"seythi","scene","hill-men"],["நறைப் பவர்","நறை",3,"maram","scene","fragrant creeper"],["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",4,"maram","scene","sandal"],["எழிலி","எழிலி",5,"pira","scene","cloud"],["வாடையொடு","வாடை",8,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:6":{"book":"natrinai","num":7,"t":["நீர் வளர் ஆம்பற் தூம்புடைத்திரள் கால்","நார் உரித்தன்ன மதன் இல் மாமை","குவளை அன்ன ஏந்து எழில் மழைக் கண்","திதலை அல்குல் பெருந் தோள் குறுமகட்கு","எய்தச் சென்று செப்புநர்ப் பெறினே","இவர் யார் என்குவள் அல்லள் முனாஅது","அத்தக் குமிழின் கொடு மூக்கு விளை கனி","எறி மட மாற்கு வல்சி ஆகும்","வல் வில் ஓரி கானம் நாறி","இரும் பல் ஒலிவரும் கூந்தல்","பெரும் பேதுறுவள் யாம் வந்தனம் எனவே"],"p":"","r":"The man, kept from the night meeting, tells his own heart within the friend's hearing that if he could find someone to go to the young girl, with skin like the tender stalk of the water lily stripped of its fibre, eyes like kuvalai and full shoulders, and tell her, she would not ask 'who is he?' but, she whose dark thick hair smells of the forest of Ori of the strong bow, where the bent ripe fruit of the kumizh by the path falls as food for the young deer, would be wholly overcome at hearing 'he has come'.","n":"He does not doubt her love, only the means of reaching her: the speech is a wish for a messenger (செப்புநர்ப் பெறினே), and since it is said where the friend can hear, the wish is a request to her. Paranar gives the same pairing of Ori's forest and a girl's hair in Kuruntokai 199.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 6","lb":"NT6","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிப்பாற்பட்டு ஆற்றானாய தலைவன் தோழி கேட்ப தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju","thozhi","kalavu","இரவுக்குறிப்பாற்பட்டு ஆற்றானாய தலைவன் தோழி கேட்ப தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆம்பற்","ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","simile","water lily"],["குவளை","குவளை",3,"maram","simile",""],["குமிழின்","குமிழ்",7,"maram","scene",""],["மாற்கு","மான்",8,"maa","scene","deer"]],"en":[["patron","ஓரி",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:7":{"book":"natrinai","num":8,"t":["சூருடை நனந் தலைச் சுனை நீர் மல்க","பெரு வரை அடுக்கத்து அருவி ஆர்ப்ப","கல் அலைத்து இழிதரும் கடு வரற் கான் யாற்றுக்","கழை மாய் நீத்தம் காடு அலை ஆர்ப்ப","தழங்கு குரல் ஏறொடு முழங்கி வானம்","இன்னே பெய்ய மின்னுமால் தோழி","வெண்ணெல் அருந்திய வரி நுதல் யானை","தண் நறுஞ் சிலம்பில் துஞ்சும்","சிறியிலைச் சந்தின வாடு பெருங் காட்டே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine, whose man has gone after wealth without marrying her, that the sky, roaring with its thunderbolts, is flashing to pour at once on the great forest of small-leaved sandal on the cool fragrant slope where the stripe-browed elephant that ate white paddy sleeps, while the spring in the wide god-haunted upland brims, waterfalls roar on the great slopes and the forest torrent, tumbling stones and drowning the bamboo, makes the woods resound.","n":"The friend says nothing about him; she only announces the rains, and whether they comfort (the season he named for return has come) or alarm (the land he crosses is in flood) the poem leaves to the hearer. The fed elephant asleep on the cool slope is the one creature at rest in the storm; its ease, which she lacks, is a mood, not an act of the lovers, so it is scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 7","lb":"NT7","poet":"நல்வெள்ளியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பட்ட பின்றை வரையாது கிழவோன் நெட்டி இடைக் கழிந்து பொருள்வயிற் பிரிய ஆற்றாளாய தலைவிக்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","பட்ட பின்றை வரையாது கிழவோன் நெட்டி இடைக் கழிந்து பொருள்வயிற் பிரிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சூருடை","சூர்",1,"deyvam","scene","fearsome deity"],["அருவி","அருவி",2,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["கழை","கழை",4,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["வெண்ணெல்","வெண்ணெல்",7,"unavu","scene","white paddy"],["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","scene",""],["சந்தின","சந்து",9,"maram","scene","sandal"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:8":{"book":"natrinai","num":9,"t":["அல்கு படர் உழந்த அரி மதர் மழைக்கண்","பல் பூம் பகைத் தழை நுடங்கும் அல்குல்","திரு மணி புரையும் மேனி மடவோள்","யார் மகள்கொல் இவள் தந்தை வாழியர்","துயரம் உறீஇயினள் எம்மே அகல்வயல்","அரிவனர் அரிந்தும் தருவனர்ப் பெற்றும்","தண் சேறு தாஅய் மதனுடை நோன் தாள்","கண் போல் நெய்தல் போர்வில் பூக்கும்","திண் தேர்ப் பொறையன் தொண்டி","தன் திறம் பெறுக இவள் ஈன்ற தாயே"],"p":"","r":"At the end of their first meeting, seeing her again among her companions, the man asks whose daughter she is, this simple girl with proud, red-lined rain-eyes that have known lingering longing, a leaf skirt of many flowers swaying at her waist and a body like a fine gem, who has brought him such pain; long live her father, and may the mother who bore her have the fortune of Thondi of Poraiyan of the strong chariots, where in the wide fields, even as reapers cut and binders carry, the eye-like neythal, spreading its strong stems through the cool mud, blooms in the stacked sheaves.","n":"His first question is about her kin (யார் மகள்கொல்), and his first wish is a blessing on her father and mother, so desire already turns toward the family he will have to ask for her. The Thondi image is of fields so rich that flowers bloom in the cut stacks, a plenty that does not stop at harvest, and that is what he wishes on her mother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","azhagu","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 8","lb":"NT8","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இயற்கைப் புணர்ச்சி இறுதிக்கண் தலைமகளை ஆயத்தொடும் கண்ட தலைமகன் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","இயற்கைப் புணர்ச்சி இறுதிக்கண்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["தழை","தழை",2,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["அரிவனர்","அரிவனர்",6,"seythi","scene","reapers"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",8,"maram","simile","neythal flower"]],"en":[["king","பொறையன்",9],["town","தொண்டி",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:9":{"book":"natrinai","num":10,"t":["அழிவிலர் முயலும் ஆர்வ மாக்கள்","வழிபடு தெய்வம் கண் கண்டாஅங்கு","அலமரல் வருத்தம் தீர யாழ நின்","நல மென் பணைத் தோள் எய்தினம் ஆகலின்","பொரிப் பூம் புன்கின் அழற் தகை ஒண் முறி","சுணங்கு அணி வன முலை அணங்கு கொளத் திமிரி","நிழல் காண்தோறும் நெடிய வைகி","மணல் காண்தோறும் வண்டல் தைஇ","வருந்தாது ஏகுமதி வால் எயிற்றோயே","மா நனை கொழுதி மகிழ் குயில் ஆலும்","நறுந் தண் பொழில கானம்","குறும் பல் ஊர யாம் செல்லும் ஆறே"],"p":"","r":"Eloping with her, the man tells his white-toothed girl that, having won her soft bamboo-like shoulders so that his whirling distress is gone, as eager devotees who strive without fail see with their own eyes the god they worship, she may go without strain, rubbing the fire-bright young leaves of the flowering pungu on her lovely spotted breasts so that they hold a charm, lingering long at every shade and making sand dolls at every stretch of sand, for the road they take runs through forest of fragrant cool groves where the happy koel, pecking mango buds, calls, past many small villages.","n":"The wasteland of the colophon is drawn as a spring garden and the journey as play (shade, sand dolls, leaves to rub on her breasts), so the man answers in advance the fear every elopement poem raises. Likening himself to devotees who at last see their god puts the girl in the god's place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 9","lb":"NT9","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உடன்போகா நின்ற தலைமகன் தலைமகட்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","உடன்போகா நின்ற தலைமகன் தலைமகட்கு உரைத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["தெய்வம்","தெய்வம்",2,"deyvam","simile",""],["புன்கின்","புன்கு",5,"maram","ornament","pungu"],["குயில்","குயில்",10,"pul","scene","koel"],["மா நனை","மா நனை",10,"maram","scene","mango bud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:10":{"book":"natrinai","num":11,"t":["அண்ணாந்து ஏந்திய வன முலை தளரினும்","பொன் நேர் மேனி மணியின் தாழ்ந்த","நல் நெடுங் கூந்தல் நரையொடு முடிப்பினும்","நீத்தல் ஓம்புமதி பூக் கேழ் ஊர","இன் கடுங் கள்ளின் இழை அணி நெடுந் தேர்க்","கொற்றச் சோழர் கொங்கர்ப் பணீஇயர்","வெண் கோட்டு யானைப் போஒர் கிழவோன்","பழையன் வேல் வாய்த்தன்ன நின்","பிழையா நல் மொழி தேறிய இவட்கே"],"p":"","r":"Handing over the girl as she elopes, the friend tells the man of the flowering town: even when her lifted lovely breasts sag and her fine long hair, hanging below the gems on her gold-like body, is bound up with grey, take care never to leave her, who has trusted your unfailing good word as one trusts the spear of Pazhaiyan, lord of Por of the white-tusked elephants, which struck true so that the victorious Cholas, of tall ornamented chariots and sweet strong toddy, could humble the Kongar.","n":"The friend looks past the elopement to old age, naming the very changes (sagging breasts, grey hair) that might later be his excuse to leave, and binds him in advance. She holds him to his word with a war image: his promise must strike as surely as a famous spear, so his speech becomes a weapon he must answer for.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","vaaimai","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 10","lb":"NT10","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உடன்போக்கும் தோழி கையடுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","உடன்போக்கும் தோழி கையடுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கள்ளின்","கள்",5,"unavu","simile","toddy"],["யானைப்","யானை",7,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",6],["town","போஒர்",7],["king","பழையன்",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:11":{"book":"natrinai","num":12,"t":["பெய்யாது வைகிய கோதை போல","மெய் சாயினை அவர் செய் குறி பிழைப்ப","உள்ளி நொதுமலர் நேர்பு உரை தௌளிதின்","வாரார் என்னும் புலவி உட்கொளல்","ஒழிக மாள நின் நெஞ்சத்தானே","புணரி பொருத பூ மணல் அடைகரை","ஆழி மருங்கின் அலவன் ஓம்பி","வலவன் வள்பு ஆய்ந்து ஊர","நிலவு விரிந்தன்றால் கானலானே"],"p":"","r":"With the man standing unseen nearby, the friend tells the heroine, wilted in body like a garland left unworn since the signal he set failed her, to give up the resentful thought that he surely will not come: on the shore of flowered sand beaten by the waves the charioteer, sparing the crab beside the wheel's track, checks the reins as he drives, and the moonlight has spread over the seaside grove.","n":"The answer to her doubt is a sound and a sight, not a promise: the chariot is already on the shore, driven slowly so as not to crush the crabs, and the moon is up. The care that spares a crab is the care he brings to her, though the poem leaves that unsaid; the crab is acted on, not acting, so it is not iraicchi. Lines 3 and 4 are compressed and their exact construction is unsure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","oodal","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 11","lb":"NT11","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"காப்பு மிகுதிக்கண் இடையீடுபட்டு ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகட்கு தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","காப்பு மிகுதிக்கண் இடையீடுபட்டு ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகட்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோதை","கோதை",1,"maram","simile","garland"],["அலவன்","அலவன்",7,"pira","scene","crab"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:12":{"book":"natrinai","num":13,"t":["விளம்பழம் கமழும் கமஞ்சூற் குழிசிப்","பாசம் தின்ற தேய் கால் மத்தம்","நெய் தெரி இயக்கம் வெளில்முதல் முழங்கும்","வைகு புலர் விடியல் மெய் கரந்து தன் கால்","அரி அமை சிலம்பு கழீஇ பல் மாண்","வரி புனை பந்தொடு வைஇய செல்வோள்","இவை காண்தோறும் நோவர் மாதோ","அளியரோ அளியர் என் ஆயத்தோர் என","நும்மொடு வரவு தான் அயரவும்","தன் வரைத்து அன்றியும் கலுழ்ந்தன கண்ணே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that at first dawn, when the worn churning stick, its rope frayed, roars at the post as it stirs out the ghee in the full-bellied pot that smells of wood-apple, the girl, hiding herself, slipped the pebble-filled anklets from her feet and set aside her finely striped ball as she went, saying 'whenever they see these my companions will grieve; pitiable, pitiable they are', and even as she eagerly made ready to come with you, her eyes wept beyond her control.","n":"The report is meant to make him fear for her (the colophon's அஞ்சுவித்தது): her will is set but her eyes are not, and the things she leaves behind (anklets, the striped ball) are the things of girlhood her friends will find. Taking off the anklets, a rite of marriage, she does alone and in hiding while the house churns at dawn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","pirivu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 12","lb":"NT12","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி உடன்போக்கு அஞ்சுவித்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி உடன்போக்கு அஞ்சுவித்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["விளம்பழம்","விளம்பழம்",1,"maram","scene","wood-apple"],["மத்தம்","மத்தம்",2,"seythi","scene","churning stick"],["சிலம்பு","சிலம்பு",5,"pira","ornament","anklet"],["பந்தொடு","பந்து",6,"pira","scene","ball"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:13":{"book":"natrinai","num":14,"t":["எழாஅ ஆகலின் எழில் நலம் தொலைய","அழாஅ தீமோ நொதுமலர் தலையே","ஏனல் காவலர் மா வீழ்த்துப் பறித்த","பகழி அன்ன சேயரி மழைக் கண்","நல்ல பெருந் தோளோயே கொல்லன்","எறி பொன் பிதிரின் சிறு பல தாஅய்","வேங்கை வீ உகும் ஓங்கு மலைக் கட்சி","மயில் அறிபு அறியா மன்னோ","பயில் குரல் கவரும் பைம் புறக் கிளியே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, seeing the heroine changed the day after her first meeting and wanting her to hide it, says: you of the good great shoulders and red-lined rain-eyes like the arrows the millet-watchers pulled out after felling a beast, do not weep and spoil your beauty before strangers because the birds will not rise; the green-backed parrots that carry off the thick ears in the high hill glade, where vengai blossoms fall small and many like the sparks from gold a smith beats, know the peacock, it seems, and do not fear it.","n":"The friend hands the girl an excuse to give out (the parrots will not leave the millet, so she weeps) to hide the real cause, her new love. The arrow simile is pointed: eyes red-streaked like arrows drawn bloody from a beast, a hunter's image for a girl who has just been struck herself. Lines 1 and 8 can be construed more than one way, so the excuse is uncertain in its detail.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 13","lb":"NT13","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இயற்கைப் புணர்ச்சியின் பிற்றை ஞான்று தலைவியின் வேறுபாடு கண்ட தோழி தலைவி மறைத்தற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தலைவி மறைத்தற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",3,"unavu","scene","millet"],["மா","மா",3,"maa","simile","beast"],["கொல்லன்","கொல்லன்",5,"seythi","simile","smith"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",7,"maram","simile",""],["மயில்","மயில்",8,"pul","scene","peacock"],["கிளியே","கிளி",9,"pul","scene","parrot"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:14":{"book":"natrinai","num":15,"t":["தொல் கவின் தொலைய தோள் நலம் சாஅய","நல்கார் நீத்தனர்ஆயினும் நல்குவர்","நட்டனர் வாழி தோழி குட்டுவன்","அகப்பா அழிய நூறி செம்பியன்","பகல் தீ வேட்ட ஞாட்பினும் மிகப் பெரிது","அலர் எழச் சென்றனர் ஆயினும் மலர் கவிழ்ந்து","மா மடல் அவிழ்ந்த காந்தள்அம் சாரல்","இனம் சால் வயக் களிறு பாந்தட் பட்டென","துஞ்சாத் துயரத்து அஞ்சு பிடிப் பூசல்","நெடு வரை விடரகத்து இயம்பும்","கடு மான் புல்லிய காடு இறந்தோரே"],"p":"","r":"To the friend who blamed the man, the heroine speaks for him: though he left without grace, her old beauty gone and her shoulders wasted, he will be gracious, for he loved her; though he went with gossip rising louder than the battle in which Sembiyan shattered Kuttuvan's Akappa and lit a fire by day, he has crossed the forest where fierce beasts roam, where on the kanthal slope, its great petals open and flowers bowed, the cry of a she-elephant, sleepless and afraid because the strong bull of her herd was seized by a python, echoes through the long mountain clefts.","n":"She grants everything the friend says (the lost beauty, gossip loud as a war) and still affirms him; the comfort is her trust, not his return. The she-elephant crying for her mate on the road he crossed is a sound he too has heard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","alar","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 14","lb":"NT14","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"இயற்பழித்த தோழிக்குத் தலைவி இயற்பட மொழிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"இயற்பழித்த தோழிக்குத் தலைவி இயற்பட மொழிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",7,"maram","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",8,"maa","scene","bull elephant"],["பாந்தட்","பாந்தள்",8,"pira","scene","python"],["பிடிப்","பிடி",9,"maa","iraicchi","she-elephant"],["மான்","மான்",11,"maa","scene","fierce beast"]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவன்",3],["town","அகப்பா",4],["king","செம்பியன்",4]],"dv":[["iraicchi","பிடிப்","confirmed","1/1","the man who crossed that land has heard a mate grieving for its lost partner, and will remember the one he left"]],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:15":{"book":"natrinai","num":16,"t":["முழங்கு திரை கொழீஇய மூரி எக்கர்","நுணங்கு துகில் நுடக்கம் போல கணம் கொள","ஊதை தூற்றும் உரவுநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப","பூவின் அன்ன நலம் புதிது உண்டு","நீ புணர்ந்தனையேம் அன்மையின் யாமே","நேர்புடை நெஞ்சம் தாங்கத் தாங்கி","மாசு இல் கற்பின் மடவோள் குழவி","பேஎய் வாங்கக் கைவிட்டாங்கு","சேணும் எம்மொடு வந்த","நாணும் விட்டேம் அலர்க இவ் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"Pressing the man to marry, the friend tells the lord of the shore of strong waters, where the north wind blows the huge dune the roaring waves heaped up into drifts like the ripple of fine cloth, that since we are not women you joined only to taste a flower-like beauty while it was new, we have held up our pledged heart as best we could, and, like a chaste young mother who lets her child go when a demon seizes it, have let go even the modesty that came with us from far back: let this town gossip.","n":"The simile is the poem's force: modesty is given up as a faultless mother gives up her child to a demon, not by choice but to a power she cannot resist, so 'let the town talk' is defeat turned into a threat. The waves heap the dune and the wind scatters it; a reader may see his love and the talk that disperses it, but the poem anchors no such pairing, so the dune is scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","alar","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 15","lb":"NT15","poet":"அறிவுடைநம்பி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரைவு நீட்டித்தவழி தோழி தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு நீட்டித்தவழி தோழி தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லி வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஊதை","ஊதை",3,"pira","scene","cold wind"],["பூவின்","பூ",4,"maram","simile","flower"],["பேஎய்","பேய்",8,"deyvam","simile","demon"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:16":{"book":"natrinai","num":17,"t":["புணரின் புணராது பொருளே பொருள்வயிற்","பிரியின் புணராது புணர்வே ஆயிடைச்","செல்லினும் செல்லாய் ஆயினும் நல்லதற்கு","உரியை வாழி என் நெஞ்சே பொருளே","வாடாப் பூவின் பொய்கை நாப்பண்","ஓடு மீன் வழியின் கெடுவ யானே","விழுநீர் வியலகம் தூணி ஆக","எழு மாண் அளக்கும் விழு நெதி பெறினும்","கனங்குழைக்கு அமர்த்த சேயரி மழைக் கண்","அமர்ந்து இனிது நோக்கமொடு செகுத்தனென்","எனைய ஆகுக வாழிய பொருளே"],"p":"","r":"The man, his heart set on a journey for wealth, gives it up: stay with her and wealth will not come, go after wealth and union will not stay; between these, my heart, whether you go or not you have a claim to the good; but wealth perishes like the trail of a fish darting through a pond of unwithering flowers, and even if I won a treasure measured seven times over with the wide wet world as the grain measure, I would have killed the sweet gentle look of her red-lined rain-eyes that suit her heavy earrings; let wealth be whatever it may.","n":"The poem is a dilemma argued out, and the choice turns on what each side loses: wealth leaves no trace, like a fish's path in water, while going would destroy a look no measure of treasure could buy back. The world used as a grain measure makes the refusal absolute.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pirivu","nenju","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 16","lb":"NT16","poet":"சிறைக்குடி ஆந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் கடைக்கூட்டிய நெஞ்சினை நெருங்கித் தலைவன் செலவு அழுங்கியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"பொருள் கடைக்கூட்டிய நெஞ்சினை நெருங்கித் தலைவன் செலவு அழுங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பூவின்","பூ",5,"maram","scene","flower"],["மீன்","மீன்",6,"pira","simile","fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:17":{"book":"natrinai","num":18,"t":["நாள் மழை தலைஇய நல் நெடுங்குன்றத்து","மால் கடல் திரையின் இழிதரும் அருவி","அகல் இருங் கானத்து அல்கு அணி நோக்கி","தாங்கவும் தகைவரை நில்லா நீர் சுழல்பு","ஏந்து எழில் மழைக் கண் கலுழ்தலின் அன்னை","எவன் செய்தனையோ நின் இலங்கு எயிறு உண்கு என","மெல்லிய இனிய கூறலின் வல் விரைந்து","உயிரினும் சிறந்த நாணும் நனி மறந்து","உரைத்தல் உய்ந்தனனே தோழி சாரல்","காந்தள் ஊதிய மணி நிறத் தும்பி","தீம் தொடை நரம்பின் இமிரும்","வான் தோய் வெற்பன் மார்பு அணங்கு எனவே"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend, in words meant for others too, that when, gazing at the beauty lingering in the wide dark forest of the tall hill where the day's rain fell and the falls come down like the waves of the great sea, her lifted lovely rain-eyes wept, the water swirling and not stopping though she tried to hold it, her mother asked 'what have you done? let me kiss your shining teeth', and at such soft sweet words she nearly forgot the modesty dearer than life and almost said that what afflicted her was the chest of the man of the sky-touching hill, where the sapphire-coloured bee that sucked the kanthal on the slope hums like a sweet strung lute; she escaped saying it.","n":"Kindness, not threat, nearly breaks her secret: the mother's tenderness is the danger. The bee that sucked the kanthal and now hums sweetly may be read as the man who enjoyed her and goes on content, but the poem gives only that one match, so it is a near miss for ullurai and stays scene. 'Modesty dearer than life' is the ranking the Tholkappiyam itself states.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 17","lb":"NT17","poet":"நொச்சிநியமங்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"முன்னிலைப்புறமொழியாகத் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச்சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","முன்னிலைப்புறமொழியாகத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",2,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",10,"maram","scene",""],["தும்பி","தும்பி",10,"pira","simile","bee"],["நரம்பின்","நரம்பு",11,"yaazh","simile","lute string"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:18":{"book":"natrinai","num":19,"t":["பருவரல் நெஞ்சமொடு பல் படர் அகல","வருவர் வாழி தோழி மூவன்","முழு வலி முள் எயிறு அழுத்திய கதவின்","கானல்அம் தொண்டிப் பொருநன் வென் வேல்","தெறல் அருந் தானைப் பொறையன் பாசறை","நெஞ்சம் நடுக்குறூஉம் துஞ்சா மறவர்","திரை தபு கடலின் இனிது கண் படுப்ப","கடாஅம் கழீஇய கதன் அடங்கு யானைத்","தடாஅ நிலை ஒரு கோட்டன்ன","ஒன்று இலங்கு அருவிய குன்று இறந்தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the heroine, unable to bear the parting, that he will come and the many sorrows of her troubled heart will leave: he crossed the hill whose single shining waterfall is like the lone tusk of a war elephant whose fury is spent after its must was washed off, in the war camp of Poraiyan of the victorious spear and unconquerable army, lord of Thondi of the seaside grove whose gate has Moovan's thorn-sharp teeth pressed into it, where the sleepless warriors who make hearts tremble sleep sweetly like a sea whose waves have stilled.","n":"The whole picture is of rest after violence (an elephant calmed when its must is washed, warriors who never sleep sleeping like a stilled sea), so the friend wraps her promise of return in a war that is over. Moovan's teeth set in Thondi's gate are a war trophy remembered in a love poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 18","lb":"NT18","poet":"பொய்கையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைவியைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைவியைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானைத்","யானை",8,"maa","simile",""],["அருவிய","அருவி",10,"pira","simile","waterfall"]],"en":[["king","மூவன்",2],["town","தொண்டி",4],["king","பொறையன்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:19":{"book":"natrinai","num":20,"t":["இறவுப் புறத்து அன்ன பிணர் படு தடவு முதல்","சுறவுக் கோட்டன்ன முள் இலைத் தாழை","பெருங் களிற்று மருப்பின் அன்ன அரும்பு முதிர்பு","நல் மான் உழையின் வேறுபடத் தோன்றி","விழவுக் களம் கமழும் உரவு நீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப","இன மணி நெடுந் தேர் பாகன் இயக்க","செலீஇய சேறி ஆயின் இவளே","வருவை ஆகிய சில் நாள்","வாழாளாதல் நற்கு அறிந்தனை சென்மே"],"p":"","r":"To the man who has met her and is leaving, the friend says: lord of the shore of strong waters, where the thazhai with leaves thorny like a shark's horn, its rough bent trunk like a prawn's back and its buds ripening like a great tusker's tusks, stands out like a fine deer apart from the herd and fills the festival ground with scent, if you are going, your charioteer driving the tall chariot of clustered bells, then go, knowing well that she will not live through the few days until you come back.","n":"The leave is granted only in form (செலீஇய சேறி ஆயின் ... சென்மே): 'go' brackets a sentence of death, which turns the farewell into a demand for marriage. The thazhai whose scent fills the festival ground is read by some as a love that can no longer stay hidden; the poem anchors no such pairing, so it is scene. Line 4's comparison is unsure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 19","lb":"NT19","poet":"நக்கண்ணையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"புணர்ந்து நீங்கிய தலைவனைத் தோழி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","புணர்ந்து நீங்கிய தலைவனைத் தோழி வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இறவுப்","இறவு",1,"pira","simile","prawn"],["சுறவுக்","சுறவு",2,"pira","simile","shark"],["தாழை","தாழை",2,"maram","scene","screwpine"],["களிற்று","களிறு",3,"maa","simile","tusker"],["மான்","மான்",4,"maa","simile","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:20":{"book":"natrinai","num":21,"t":["ஐய குறுமகட் கண்டிகும் வைகி","மகிழ்நன் மார்பில் துஞ்சி அவிழ் இணர்த்","தேம் பாய் மராஅம் கமழும் கூந்தல்","துளங்குஇயல் அசைவர கலிங்கம் துயல்வர","செறிதொடி தெளிர்ப்ப வீசி மறுகில்","பூப் போல் உண்கண் பெயர்ப்ப நோக்கி","சென்றனள் வாழிய மடந்தை நுண் பல்","சுணங்கு அணிவுற்ற விளங்கு பூணள்","மார்புறு முயக்கிடை ஞெமிர்ந்த சோர் குழை","பழம் பிணி வைகிய தோள் இணைக்","குழைந்த கோதை கொடி முயங்கலளே"],"p":"","r":"To the husband back from another woman who says he knows no one, the heroine says: sir, I saw the young girl; having passed the night asleep on the lord's chest, her hair fragrant with honey-dripping kadamba blossom, her gait swaying, her cloth fluttering, her tight bangles jingling as she swung her arms, she went off down the street turning her flower-like kohl eyes to look about, long may the girl live, wearing bright jewels over her many fine spots, her drooping earring crushed in the chest-to-chest embrace, her garland withered on shoulders where an old longing has settled, she who clung like a creeper.","n":"She never accuses; she describes, and the description is the evidence (the crushed earring, the withered garland, a night on 'the lord's chest'), closed by a blessing (வாழிய மடந்தை) that is pure irony. The colophon's second reading gives the speech to the friend, but the address ஐய, 'sir', fits the husband better.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 20","lb":"NT20","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தையிற்பிரிந்து வந்த தலைமகன் யாரையும் அறியேன் என்றாற்குத் தலைவி சொல்லியது வாயிலாகப் புக்க தோழிதலைவிக்குச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","பரத்தையிற்பிரிந்து வந்த தலைமகன் யாரையும் அறியேன் என்றாற்குத் தலைவி சொல்லியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","வாயிலாகப் புக்க தோழிதலைவிக்குச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["மராஅம்","மராம்",3,"maram","ornament","kadamba"],["கோதை","கோதை",11,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:1"},"nt:21":{"book":"natrinai","num":22,"t":["விரைப் பரி வருந்திய வீங்கு செலல் இளையர்","அரைச் செறி கச்சை யாப்பு அழித்து அசைஇ","வேண்டு அமர் நடையர் மென்மெல வருக","தீண்டா வை முள் தீண்டி நாம் செலற்கு","ஏமதி வலவ தேரே உதுக் காண்","உருக்குறு நறு நெய் பால் விதிர்த்தன்ன","அரிக் குரல் மிடற்ற அம் நுண் பல் பொறிக்","காமரு தகைய கான வாரணம்","பெயல் நீர் போகிய வியல் நெடும் புறவில்","புலரா ஈர் மணல் மலிரக் கெண்டி","நாள் இரை கவர மாட்டி தன்","பேடை நோக்கிய பெருந்தகு நிலையே"],"p":"","r":"Returning with his work done, the man tells his charioteer to let the weary attendants loosen their tight girdles and come on slowly at their own pace, and to use the goad he has never had to use and drive on, and bids him look at the forest cock, its throat speckled like milk sprinkled on melted ghee, which on the wide scrubland the rains have left scratches up the wet sand for its morning food and stands looking to its hen.","n":"The man is easy on his tired men while hurrying himself, and he gives the poem's last words, 'noble bearing' (பெருந்தகு நிலை), to the bird, not to himself: his longing for his wife is shown only through what he points the charioteer to.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","ilvaazhkkai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 21","lb":"NT21","poet":"மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி மீள்வான் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றி மீள்வான்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இளையர்","இளையர்",1,"seythi","scene","attendants"],["வாரணம்","கான வாரணம்",8,"pul","iraicchi","jungle fowl"],["பேடை","பேடை",12,"pul","iraicchi","hen"],["நெய்","நெய்",6,"unavu","simile",""],["பால்","பால்",6,"unavu","simile",""],["மணல்","மணல்",10,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வாரணம்","confirmed","1/1","the man's own errand of provision is done and, like the fowl, he turns to the wife he provides for"]],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:22":{"book":"natrinai","num":23,"t":["கொடிச்சி காக்கும் அடுக்கற் பைந்தினை","முந்து விளை பெருங் குரல் கொண்ட மந்தி","கல்லாக் கடுவனொடு நல் வரை ஏறி","அங்கை நிறைய ஞெமிடிக் கொண்டு தன்","திரை அணற் கொடுங் கவுள் நிறைய முக்கி","வான் பெயல் நனைந்த புறத்த நோன்பியர்","கை ஊண் இருக்கையின் தோன்றும் நாடன்","வந்தனன் வாழி தோழி உலகம்","கயம் கண் அற்ற பைது அறு காலை","பீளொடு திரங்கிய நெல்லிற்கு","நள்ளென் யாமத்து மழை பொழிந்தாங்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the man has come, the lord of the land where a female monkey, taking the first-ripened heavy heads of the millet a hill girl guards, climbs the rock with her untaught mate and, crushing them in her palms and stuffing her cheeks, looks like rain-soaked ascetics eating from their hands; his coming is like rain pouring at midnight on paddy shrivelled in its sheath in a dry season when the ponds have lost their water.","n":"The rain is timed to the last moment: it falls on paddy already shrivelled, at midnight when no one looks for it. The monkey who steals the guarded millet's first heads with her mate is a near miss for ullurai (only one pair, the guarded crop to the guarded girl, maps cleanly), so it is scene and simile here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 22","lb":"NT22","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரைவு மலிந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு மலிந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",1,"seythi","scene","hill girl"],["பைந்தினை","தினை",1,"unavu","scene",""],["மந்தி","மந்தி",2,"maa","simile","female monkey"],["கடுவனொடு","கடுவன்",3,"maa","scene","male monkey"],["நோன்பியர்","நோன்பியர்",6,"seythi","simile","ascetics"],["நெல்லிற்கு","நெல்",10,"unavu","simile",""],["மழை","மழை",11,"pira","simile","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:23":{"book":"natrinai","num":24,"t":["தொடி பழி மறைத்தலின் தோள் உய்ந்தனவே","வடிக் கொள் கூழை ஆயமோடு ஆடலின்","இடிப்பு மெய்யது ஒன்று உடைத்தே கடிக் கொள","அன்னை காக்கும் தொல் நலம் சிதைய","காண்தொறும் கலுழ்தல் அன்றியும் ஈண்டு நீர்","முத்துப் படு பரப்பின் கொற்கை முன்துறைச்","சிறு பாசடைய செப்பு ஊர் நெய்தல்","தெண் நீர் மலரின் தொலைந்த","கண்ணே காமம் கரப்பு அரியவ்வே"],"p":"","r":"Pressing the man to marry, the friend says the girl's shoulders escaped blame because her bangles hid their thinness, and her loosened hair has the excuse of play with her companions, but her eyes, which besides weeping each time they see the old beauty her mother watches over wasting away, have faded like the small-leaved neythal flower in the clear water of Korkai's pearl-bearing harbour, cannot hide love.","n":"The friend sorts the signs into those an excuse can cover (bangles, hair) and the one that cannot (the eyes); line 3 (இடிப்பு மெய்யது ஒன்று உடைத்தே) is hard, and the hair's excuse is my reading of it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 23","lb":"NT23","poet":"கணக்காயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி துயர் ஆற்றாமை உணர்ந்த தோழி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தொடி","தொடி",1,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["முத்துப்","முத்து",6,"pira","scene","pearl"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",7,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["town","கொற்கை",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:24":{"book":"natrinai","num":25,"t":["பார் பக வீழ்ந்த வேருடை விழுக் கோட்டு","உடும்பு அடைந்தன்ன நெடும் பொரி விளவின்","ஆட்டு ஒழி பந்தின் கோட்டு மூக்கு இறுபு","கம்பலத்தன்ன பைம் பயிர்த் தாஅம்","வெள்ளில் வல்சி வேற்று நாட்டு ஆர் இடைச்","சேறும் நாம் எனச் சொல்ல சேயிழை","நன்று எனப் புரிந்தோய் நன்று செய்தனையே","செயல்படு மனத்தர் செய்பொருட்கு","அகல்வர் ஆடவர் அது அதன் பண்பே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend, who said 'good' when the man said they would go to the hard tract of a strange land, where wood-apple trees, their trunks rough as if monitor lizards clung to them, drop fruit like balls left after play, their stalks snapped, onto green creepers spread like a blanket, and wood-apple is the only food, that she did well: men whose minds are set on action leave for the wealth to be made, and that is their nature.","n":"The colophon says she speaks with joy, and the maxim is given as approval; set beside the many parting poems in which the woman refuses, the praise may also be read as bitter, and the poem does not settle which.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 24","lb":"NT24","poet":"கணக்காயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருட்பிரிவுக்கு உடன்பட்ட தோழியைத் தலைவி உவந்து கூறியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பொருட்பிரிவுக்கு உடன்பட்ட"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["உடும்பு","உடும்பு",2,"maa","simile","monitor lizard"],["விளவின்","விளவு",2,"maram","scene","wood-apple"],["பயிர்த்","பயிர்",4,"maram","scene","creeper"],["வெள்ளில்","வெள்ளில்",5,"unavu","gift_food","wood-apple fruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:25":{"book":"natrinai","num":26,"t":["அவ் வளை வெரிநின் அரக்கு ஈர்த்தன்ன","செவ் வரி இதழ சேண் நாறு பிடவின்","நறுந் தாது ஆடிய தும்பி பசுங் கேழ்ப்","பொன் உரை கல்லின் நல் நிறம் பெறூஉம்","வள மலை நாடன் நெருநல் நம்மொடு","கிளை மலி சிறு தினைக் கிளி கடிந்து அசைஇ","சொல்லிடம் பெறாஅன் பெயர்ந்தனன் பெயர்ந்தது","அல்லல் அன்று அது காதல் அம் தோழி","தாது உண் வேட்கையின் போது தெரிந்து ஊதா","வண்டு ஓரன்ன அவன் தண்டாக் காட்சி","கண்டும் கழல் தொடி வலித்த என்","பண்பு இல் செய்தி நினைப்பு ஆகின்றே"],"p":"","r":"Pleading the man's case, the friend tells the girl that the lord of the rich hill, where a bee dusted with the fragrant pollen of the pidavu, whose red-veined petals look like lac drawn on the back of a conch bangle, takes on the colour of a touchstone rubbed with gold, chased the parrots from the millet with them yesterday and, finding no chance to speak, went away; his going is not sorrow but love, and having seen his unwearied gaze, like a bee that, craving pollen, does not pick among the buds, she now thinks of her own graceless act of pressing back her slipping bangle.","n":"The friend speaks as if the man's silent plea and her own refusal (the bangle pressed back) were hers, which lets her press his case without naming the girl's feelings; whether the 'graceless act' is the friend's or the girl's is not certain from the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kaamam","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 25","lb":"NT25","poet":"பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகளைத் தோழி குறை நயப்புக் கூறியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","குறை நயப்புக் கூறியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அரக்கு","அரக்கு",1,"pira","simile","lac"],["பிடவின்","பிடவு",2,"maram","simile",""],["தும்பி","தும்பி",3,"pira","simile","bee"],["தினைக்","தினை",6,"unavu","scene",""],["கிளி","கிளி",6,"pul","scene",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",10,"pira","simile","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:26":{"book":"natrinai","num":27,"t":["நோகோ யானே நெகிழ்ந்தன வளையே","செவ்வி சேர்ந்த புள்ளி வெள் அரை","விண்டுப் புரையும் புணர் நிலை நெடுங் கூட்டுப்","பிண்ட நெல்லின் தாய் மனை ஒழிய","சுடர் முழுது எறிப்பத் திரங்கிச் செழுங் காய்","முட முதிர் பலவின் அத்தம் நும்மொடு","கெடு துணை ஆகிய தவறோ வை எயிற்று","பொன் பொதிந்தன்ன சுணங்கின்","இருஞ் சூழ் ஓதி பெருந் தோளாட்கே"],"p":"","r":"To keep the man from going, the friend asks: shall I grieve? her bangles have slipped; would it be a wrong if she, of the sharp white teeth, the gold-like spots, the full dark hair and broad shoulders, left her mother's house, with its tall granary of paddy, white-based and spotted, like a hill, to be your companion, even one who perishes, on the dry tract where under the full sun the jackfruit shrivels on the bent old tree?","n":"The friend stops the journey by offering the girl as a fellow traveller, which the man must refuse; the rich granary of the mother's house is set against the shrivelled fruit of the road. Line 7 (கெடு துணை ஆகிய தவறோ) can also mean 'is it her fault that she has become a lost companion', and I cannot fix which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","udanpokku","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 26","lb":"NT26","poet":"சாத்தந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவி பிரிவு உணர்ந்து வேறுபட்டமை சொல்லி தோழி செலவு அழுங்குவித்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","செலவு அழுங்குவித்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வளையே","வளை",1,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",4,"unavu","scene",""],["பலவின்","பலா",6,"maram","scene","jackfruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:27":{"book":"natrinai","num":28,"t":["நீயும் யானும் நெருநல் பூவின்","நுண் தாது உறைக்கும் வண்டினம் ஓப்பி","ஒழி திரை வரித்த வெண் மணல் அடைகரைக்","கழி சூழ் கானல் ஆடியது அன்றி","கரந்து நாம் செய்தது ஒன்று இல்லை உண்டு எனின்","பரந்து பிறர் அறிந்தன்றும் இலரே நன்றும்","எவன் குறித்தனள் கொல் அன்னை கயந்தோறு","இற ஆர் இனக் குருகு ஒலிப்ப சுறவம்","கழி சேர் மருங்கின் கணைக் கால் நீடி","கண் போல் பூத்தமை கண்டு நுண் பல","சிறு பாசடைய நெய்தல்","குறுமோ சென்று எனக் கூறா தோளே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening nearby, the friend tells the girl that yesterday they only played in the grove by the backwater, on the white sand the waves had lined, chasing off the bees that sprinkle fine pollen, and did nothing in secret, or if they did, no one else knew; so what did mother mean when she said, 'do not go and pluck the small-leaved neythal that, on long stems by the backwaters where the herons cry in flocks and sharks come, has bloomed like eyes'?","n":"The mother's ban on picking flowers is the girl's confinement told sideways, so that the man overhearing learns the meetings are over; the reversed simile (flowers like eyes) puts watching eyes on every stem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 27","lb":"NT27","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பூவின்","பூ",1,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bees"],["குருகு","குருகு",8,"pul","scene","heron"],["சுறவம்","சுறா",8,"pira","scene","shark"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",11,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:28":{"book":"natrinai","num":29,"t":["என் கைக் கொண்டு தன் கண்ஒற்றியும்","தன் கைக் கொண்டு என் நல் நுதல் நீவியும்","அன்னை போல இனிய கூறியும்","கள்வர் போலக் கொடியன் மாதோ","மணி என இழிதரும் அருவி பொன் என","வேங்கை தாய ஓங்கு மலை அடுக்கத்து","ஆடு கழை நிவந்த பைங் கண் மூங்கில்","ஓடு மழை கிழிக்கும் சென்னி","கோடு உயர் பிறங்கல் மலைகிழவோனே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that the lord of the high-ridged hill, where falls drop like gems, the vengai spreads like gold over the slopes and swaying green bamboo rises to tear the running clouds, took her hand and pressed it to his eyes, stroked her brow with his own hand and spoke sweetly like a mother, yet he is cruel as thieves.","n":"The two similes are set against each other in successive lines: like a mother, like thieves; the man's tender acts are the evidence for his cruelty, since they are what make his absence hurt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kalaviyal","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 28","lb":"NT28","poet":"முதுகூற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவின்கண் ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைவிக்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது - குறை நயப்பும் ஆம்","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","பிரிவின்கண் ஆற்றாளாகிய"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","குறை நயப்பும் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",5,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",6,"maram","simile","vengai tree"],["மூங்கில்","மூங்கில்",7,"maram","scene",""],["மழை","மழை",8,"pira","scene","cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:29":{"book":"natrinai","num":30,"t":["நின்ற வேனில் உலந்த காந்தள்","அழல் அவிர் நீள் இடை நிழலிடம் பெறாஅது","ஈன்று கான் மடிந்த பிணவுப் பசி கூர்ந்தென","மான்ற மாலை வழங்குநர்ச் செகீஇய","புலி பார்த்து உறையும் புல் அதர்ச் சிறு நெறி","யாங்கு வல்லுநள்கொல்தானே யான் தன்","வனைந்து ஏந்து இள முலை நோவகொல் என","நினைந்து கைந்நெகிழ்ந்த அனைத்தற்குத் தான் தன்","பேர் அமர் மழைக் கண் ஈரிய கலுழ","வெய்ய உயிர்க்கும் சாயல்","மை ஈர் ஓதி பெரு மடத்தகையே"],"p":"","r":"The mother whose daughter has gone with her lover asks how the girl can manage the narrow grassy track where, in the lingering summer, the kanthal has withered, the long blazing stretch gives no shade, and a tiger, its mate starving after giving birth, waits in the dim evening to kill travellers, when at home, if her mother merely loosened her embrace for fear of hurting her young breasts, she would weep from her large rain-dark eyes and sigh hotly, that gentle girl of dark hair and great innocence.","n":"The tiger hunting for its starving mate is a male providing for its mate, but striking its lines leaves the mother's question with no road to ask about, so it fails the deletion test and stays scene; the poem's weight is the contrast between a loosened hug and the tiger's track.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 29","lb":"NT29","poet":"பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகள் போக்கிய தாய் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu","மகள் போக்கிய தாய்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",1,"maram","absent",""],["பிணவுப்","பிணவு",3,"maa","scene","tigress"],["புலி","புலி",5,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:30":{"book":"natrinai","num":31,"t":["கண்டனென் மகிழ்ந கண்டு எவன்செய்கோ","பாணன் கையது பண்புடைச் சீறியாழ்","யாணர் வண்டின் இம்மென இமிரும்","ஏர்தரு தெருவின் எதிர்ச்சி நோக்கி நின்","மார்பு தலைக்கொண்ட மாணிழை மகளிர்","கவல் ஏமுற்ற வெய்து வீழ் அரிப் பனி","கால் ஏமுற்ற பைதரு காலை","கடல்மரம் கவிழ்ந்தெனக் கலங்கி உடன் வீழ்பு","பலர் கொள் பலகை போல","வாங்கவாங்க நின்று ஊங்கு அஞர் நிலையே"],"p":"","r":"To the husband who says he knows no other woman, the friend says she has seen it, and what can she do: on the fine street where his bard's little lute hums like bees, the decked women who had laid claim to his chest stood watching for him to pass, and like the many who, when the ship capsizes in a gale, cling together to one plank with hot tears falling, they pull him this way and that in endless distress.","n":"The shipwreck makes the man a plank, one object many lives cling to, so the friend's mockery deflates both his denial and his importance; the bard's lute, which announces his coming, is what gives him away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 30","lb":"NT30","poet":"கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தையிற் பிரிந்து வந்த தலைவன் யாரையும் அறியேன் என்றார்க்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","யாரையும் அறியேன் என்றார்க்குத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பாணன்","பாணன்",2,"seythi","scene","bard"],["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",2,"yaazh","scene","small lute"],["வண்டின்","வண்டு",3,"pira","simile","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:31":{"book":"natrinai","num":32,"t":["மா இரும் பரப்பு அகம் துணிய நோக்கி","சேயிறா எறிந்த சிறு வெண் காக்கை","பாய் இரும் பனிக் கழி துழைஇ பைங் கால்","தான் வீழ் பெடைக்குப் பயிரிடூஉ சுரக்கும்","சிறு வீ ஞாழல் துறையுமார் இனிதே","பெரும் புலம்பு உற்ற நெஞ்சமொடு பல நினைந்து","யானும் இனையேன் ஆயின் ஆனாது","வேறு பல் நாட்டில் கால் தர வந்த","பல உறு பண்ணியம் இழிதரு நிலவு மணல்","நெடுஞ் சினை புன்னை கடுஞ் சூல் வெண் குருகு","உலவுத் திரை ஓதம் வெரூஉம்","உரவு நீர்ச் சேர்ப்பனொடு மணவா ஊங்கே"],"p":"","r":"With the man standing nearby, the girl answers her friend's reassurance: the shore of small-flowered njaazhal, where a little white sea crow, having struck a red prawn after scanning the wide dark water, searches the cold backwater and calls to the mate it loves, would be sweet to her too, were she not grieving, thinking many things with a lonely heart, until she is married to the lord of the strong-watered shore, where goods brought by the wind from many lands are unloaded on the moonlit sand and a heavy-bellied white heron on the long-branched punnai fears the roaming tide.","n":"The pregnant heron that fears the tide is close to the girl's own fear, but only that one pair maps, so it is scene. Line 5 recurs as Natrinai 311 line 7.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 31","lb":"NT31","poet":"நக்கிரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாக தலைவி வன்புறை எதிர் அழிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","வன்புறை எதிர் அழிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["சேயிறா","சேயிறா",2,"pira","scene","red prawn"],["காக்கை","காக்கை",2,"pul","iraicchi","sea crow"],["பெடைக்குப்","பெடை",4,"pul","iraicchi","mate"],["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",5,"maram","scene",""],["பண்ணியம்","பண்ணியம்",9,"pira","scene","goods"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",10,"maram","scene",""],["குருகு","குருகு",10,"pul","scene","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","காக்கை","confirmed","1/1","even the shore bird provides for and calls its mate, while the man has not yet married the girl"]],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:32":{"book":"natrinai","num":33,"t":["மாயோன் அன்ன மால் வரைக் கவாஅன்","வாலியோன் அன்ன வயங்கு வெள் அருவி","அம் மலை கிழவோன் நம் நயந்து என்றும்","வருந்தினன் என்பது ஓர் வாய்ச் சொல் தேறாய்","நீயும் கண்டு நுமரொடும் எண்ணி","அறிவு அறிந்து அளவல் வேண்டும் மறுத்தரற்கு","அரிய வாழி தோழி பெரியோர்","நாடி நட்பின் அல்லது","நட்டு நாடார் தம் ஒட்டியோர் திறத்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the lord of the hill, whose great slope is dark like Mayon and whose falls are bright white like Valiyon, desires them and has long suffered, yet she will not trust that true word: let her see him herself, weigh it with her people, learn his mind and judge, since once accepted it is hard to undo; the great take a friend only after inquiry, and do not inquire after they have befriended.","n":"The closing maxim belongs to friendship (நட்பு), and the friend uses it in both directions at once: inquire, because once he is accepted there is no going back; the dark god and the white god give the hill its colours before any argument begins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","natpu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 32","lb":"NT32","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவிக்குக் குறை நயப்புக் கூறியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","குறை நயப்புக் கூறியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மாயோன்","மாயோன்",1,"deyvam","simile","Mayon"],["வாலியோன்","வாலியோன்",2,"deyvam","simile","Valiyon"],["அருவி","அருவி",2,"pira","simile","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:33":{"book":"natrinai","num":34,"t":["படு சுடர் அடைந்த பகு வாய் நெடு வரை","முரம்பு சேர் சிறுகுடி பரந்த மாலை","புலம்பு கூட்டுண்ணும் புல்லென் மன்றத்து","கல்லுடை படுவில் கலுழி தந்து","நிறை பெயல் அறியாக் குன்றத்து ஊண் அல்லில்","துவர் செய் ஆடை செந் தொடை மறவர்","அதர் பார்த்து அல்கும் அஞ்சுவரு நெறியிடை","இறப்ப எண்ணுவர் அவர் எனின் மறுத்தல்","வல்லுவம்கொல்லோ மெல்லியல் நாம் என","விம்முறு கிளவியள் என் முகம் நோக்கி","நல் அக வன முலைக் கரை சேர்பு","மல்கு புனல் பரந்த மலர் ஏர் கண்ணே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that the girl, sobbing, looked into her face and said 'if he means to cross the fearful path where, when the setting sun reaches the cleft hills and evening spreads over the hamlet on the gravel, by the dreary common where loneliness feeds, in hills that have never known full rain, where muddy water is drawn from stony pits, bowmen in dyed cloth with arrows on the string lie in wait for travellers, could we, soft as we are, stop him?', and her flower-like eyes brimmed and overflowed onto her breasts.","n":"The friend reports the girl's words rather than pleading herself, so the girl's question (could we stop him?) reaches him as a fact already decided: she has not asked him to stay, only wept.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kan","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 33","lb":"NT33","poet":"இளவேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட தலைமகளது குறிப்பு அறிந்த தோழி தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,"குறிப்பு அறிந்த தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கலுழி","கலுழி",4,"pira","scene","muddy water"],["மறவர்","மறவர்",6,"seythi","scene","bowmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:34":{"book":"natrinai","num":35,"t":["கடவுட் கற்சுனை அடை இறந்து அவிழ்ந்த","பறியாக் குவளை மலரொடு காந்தள்","குருதி ஒண் பூ உரு கெழக் கட்டி","பெரு வரை அடுக்கம் பொற்பச் சூர்மகள்","அருவி இன் இயத்து ஆடும் நாடன்","மார்பு தர வந்த படர் மலி அரு நோய்","நின் அணங்கு அன்மை அறிந்தும் அண்ணாந்து","கார் நறுங் கடம்பின் கண்ணி சூடி","வேலன் வேண்ட வெறி மனை வந்தோய்","கடவுள் ஆயினும் ஆக","மடவை மன்ற வாழிய முருகே"],"p":"","r":"Speaking as if to the god, the friend tells Murugan that the heavy lovesickness was given by the chest of the lord of the land where a nymph, wearing a garland of the unplucked blue lilies of the god's rock spring and blood-bright kanthal, dances to the sweet music of the falls on the great slope; knowing it is not his seizure, he still came, wearing a kadampu garland, to the veri house at the velan's call: god he may be, but he is surely a fool.","n":"The friend's speech to the god is meant for the household, which has called the velan; by telling Murugan he knows the sickness is not his, she tells them where it came from (the hill lord's chest) without naming the man to them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","velan","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 34","lb":"NT34","poet":"பிரமசாரி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தெய்வத்திற்கு உரைப்பாளாய் வெறி விலக்கியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu","வெறி விலக்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["குவளை","குவளை",2,"maram","ornament","blue lily"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",2,"maram","ornament",""],["சூர்மகள்","சூர்மகள்",4,"deyvam","scene","hill nymph"],["அருவி","அருவி",5,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["கடம்பின்","கடம்பு",8,"maram","ornament",""],["வேலன்","வேலன்",9,"seythi","scene","diviner"],["முருகே","முருகு",11,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:35":{"book":"natrinai","num":36,"t":["பொங்கு திரை பொருத வார் மணல் அடை கரைப்","புன் கால் நாவல் பொதிப் புற இருங் கனி","கிளை செத்து மொய்த்த தும்பி பழம் செத்துப்","பல் கால் அலவன் கொண்ட கோட்கு அசாந்து","கொள்ளா நரம்பின் இமிரும் பூசல்","இரை தேர் நாரை எய்தி விடுக்கும்","துறை கெழு மரந்தை அன்ன இவள் நலம்","பண்டும் இற்றே கண்டிசின்தெய்ய","உழையின் போகாது அளிப்பினும் சிறிய","ஞெகிழ்ந்த கவின் நலம்கொல்லோ மகிழ்ந்தோர்","கட்களி செருக்கத்து அன்ன","காமம்கொல் இவள் கண் பசந்ததுவே"],"p":"","r":"The day after the wedding, when the groom thanks her for keeping the girl well, the friend says that the girl's beauty, like that of Maranthai with its harbour, where on the long wave-beaten sandbank a bee takes the dark fruit of the naval for its kin and swarms on it, a crab takes the bee for fruit and seizes it, and the bee's buzz, like an untuned string, draws the prey-seeking heron, which lets them go, was always so, as he can see; but though he has not left her side, has her beauty slackened a little, or is it love, like the elation of toddy-drinkers, that has paled her eyes?","n":"The chain of mistakes on the shore (bee taking fruit for kin, crab taking bee for fruit, heron letting all go) is a traditional site for hidden meaning, but no two of its parts map firmly onto the wedding situation, so it stays scene; the friend turns the groom's thanks back on him by giving the pallor to love rather than to her care.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","nalam","pasalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 35","lb":"NT35","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"மணமகனைப் பிற்றை ஞான்று புக்க தோழி நன்கு ஆற்றுவித்தாய் என்ற தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","நன்கு ஆற்றுவித்தாய் என்ற தலைமகற்குச்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நாவல்","நாவல்",2,"maram","scene",""],["தும்பி","தும்பி",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["அலவன்","அலவன்",4,"pira","scene","crab"],["நாரை","நாரை",6,"pul","scene","heron"]],"en":[["town","மரந்தை",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:36":{"book":"natrinai","num":37,"t":["குறுங் கை இரும் புலிக் கோள் வல் ஏற்றை","பூ நுதல் இரும் பிடி புலம்ப தாக்கி","தாழ் நீர் நனந் தலை பெருங் களிறு அடூஉம்","கல்லக வெற்பன் சொல்லின் தேறி","யாம் எம் நலன் இழந்தனமே யாமத்து","அலர் வாய்ப் பெண்டிர் அம்பலொடு ஒன்றி","புரை இல் தீ மொழி பயிற்றிய உரை எடுத்து","ஆனா கௌவைத்து ஆக","தான் என் இழந்தது இவ் அழுங்கல் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening at the night tryst, the friend says that, trusting the word of the hill lord, in whose land a short-legged tiger, strong to kill, strikes down a great tusker in a deep pool so that his flower-browed mate is left to grieve, they have lost their beauty, while the noisy town, joining the gossiping women's whispers at midnight and taking up their wicked talk, has made an endless scandal of it: what has the town itself lost?","n":"The tiger could instead be the man, whose word has cost them their beauty; either way the scene of the killing and the lone mate is not joined to the speech by any marker.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 36","lb":"NT36","poet":"சீத்தலை சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிச் சிறைப் புறமாகத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறிச் சிறைப் புறமாகத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["புலிக்","புலி",1,"maa","ullurai",""],["பிடி","பிடி",2,"maa","ullurai","cow elephant"],["களிறு","களிறு",3,"maa","ullurai","tusker"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","புலிக் பிடி களிறு","confirmed","1/1","the lovers parted by the town's slander"]],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:37":{"book":"natrinai","num":38,"t":["பிணங்கு அரில் வாடிய பழ விறல் நனந் தலை","உணங்கு ஊண் ஆயத்து ஓர் ஆன் தெள் மணி","பைபய இசைக்கும் அத்தம் வை எயிற்று","இவளொடும் செலினோ நன்றே குவளை","நீர் சூழ் மா மலர் அன்ன கண் அழ","கலை ஒழி பிணையின் கலங்கி மாறி","அன்பிலிர் அகறிர் ஆயின் என் பரம்","ஆகுவது அன்று இவள் அவலம் நாகத்து","அணங்குடை அருந் தலை உடலி வலன் ஏர்பு","ஆர் கலி நல் ஏறு திரிதரும்","கார் செய் மாலை வரூஉம் போழ்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man, who is leaving with the marriage put off, that it would be good if he took the sharp-toothed girl with him across the old wide wilds, where the tangled thickets have withered and a single cow's clear bell tinkles softly in a herd grazing on dry fodder; but if he leaves without love, so that her eyes like great water-girt blue lilies weep and she is distraught like a doe deserted by its stag, her grief will be more than the friend can bear when the rain-making evening comes, with the roaring thunder that strikes the dread heads of serpents as it wanders the sky.","n":"The friend's threat is the rainy evening of mullai, the season of waiting, carried into a poem of the dry road: the danger she names is not the wilds but the evening at home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","varaivu","udanpokku","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 37","lb":"NT37","poet":"பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"வரைவிடை வைத்துப் பிரிவின்கண் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவிடை வைத்துப் பிரிவின்கண்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஆன்","ஆன்",2,"maa","scene","cow"],["மணி","மணி",2,"pira","scene","bell"],["குவளை","குவளை",4,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["கலை","கலை",6,"maa","simile","stag"],["பிணையின்","பிணை",6,"maa","simile","doe"],["நாகத்து","நாகம்",8,"pira","scene","serpent"],["ஏறு","ஏறு",10,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:38":{"book":"natrinai","num":39,"t":["வேட்டம் பொய்யாது வலை வளம் சிறப்ப","பாட்டம் பொய்யாது பரதவர் பகர","இரும் பனந் தீம் பிழி உண்போர் மகிழும்","ஆர் கலி யாணர்த்து ஆயினும் தேர் கெழு","மெல்லம் புலம்பன் பிரியின் புல்லென","புலம்பு ஆகின்றே தோழி கலங்கு நீர்","கழி சூழ் படப்பை காண்டவாயில்","ஒலி கா ஓலை முள் மிடை வேலி","பெண்ணை இவரும் ஆங்கண்","வெள் மணல் படப்பை எம் அழுங்கல் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"The girl answers her friend's reassurance: though their noisy town, Kaandavaayil, with its gardens ringed by the backwater, its fences of thorny palm-leaf over which the palmyra climbs and its white sands, is full of bustle and plenty, the catch never failing, the nets rich, the rains never failing, the fishermen bartering and drinkers merry on sweet palm toddy, it turns desolate if the lord of the shore with his chariot leaves her.","n":"The prosperity is listed only to be cancelled: the whole loud town weighs less than one man's going, and the closing 'our noisy town' (அழுங்கல் ஊர்) is the same noise heard as loneliness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 38","lb":"NT38","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி வன்புறை எதிர் அழிந்து சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"வன்புறை எதிர் அழிந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வலை","வலை",1,"seythi","scene","net"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",2,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["பனந் தீம் பிழி","பனம் பிழி",3,"unavu","gift_food","palm toddy"],["ஓலை","ஓலை",8,"maram","scene","palm leaf"],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",9,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[["town","காண்டவாயில்",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:39":{"book":"natrinai","num":40,"t":["சொல்லின் சொல் எதிர் கொள்ளாய் யாழ நின்","திரு முகம் இறைஞ்சி நாணுதி கதுமென","காமம் கைம்மிகின் தாங்குதல் எளிதோ","கொடுங் கேழ் இரும் புறம் நடுங்கக் குத்தி","புலி விளையாடிய புலவு நாறு வேழத்தின்","தலை மருப்பு ஏய்ப்ப கடை மணி சிவந்த நின்","கண்ணே கதவ அல்ல நண்ணார்","அரண் தலை மதிலர் ஆகவும் முரசு கொண்டு","ஓம்பு அரண் கடந்த அடு போர் செழியன்","பெரும் பெயர் கூடல் அன்ன நின்","கரும்புடைத் தோளும் உடைய வால் அணங்கே"],"p":"","r":"At their second meeting the man tells the girl that she does not answer his words and bows her lovely face in shame, yet when love overflows is it easy to hold back? her eyes, red at the corners like the tusk tips of an elephant reeking of flesh after a tiger has gored its dark back and toyed with it, are not angry, but her shoulders painted with sugarcane designs, like the great-named Koodal of Cheliyan who took the drums and crossed the guarded walls of foes, overpower him, bright goddess.","n":"Both similes are of violence (the gored elephant's bloody tusk, the conqueror's city), turned on a shy girl who says nothing: the man reads his own defeat in her body, and calls her அணங்கு, a power that strikes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam","kan","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 39","lb":"NT39","poet":"மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரண்டாம் கூட்டத்து எதிர்ச்சியில் தலைவன் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","இரண்டாம் கூட்டத்து எதிர்ச்சியில்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["புலி","புலி",5,"maa","simile",""],["வேழத்தின்","வேழம்",5,"maa","simile","elephant"],["கரும்புடைத்","கரும்பு",11,"maram","ornament","sugarcane design"]],"en":[["king","செழியன்",9],["town","கூடல்",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:40":{"book":"natrinai","num":41,"t":["நெடு நா ஒள் மணி கடி மனை இரட்ட","குரை இலைப் போகிய விரவு மணற் பந்தர்","பெரும் பாண் காவல் பூண்டென ஒரு சார்","திருந்து இழை மகளிர் விரிச்சி நிற்ப","வெறி உற விரிந்த அறுவை மெல் அணைப்","புனிறு நாறு செவிலியொடு புதல்வன் துஞ்ச","ஐயவி அணிந்த நெய்யாட்டு ஈரணிப்","பசு நெய் கூர்ந்த மென்மை யாக்கைச்","சீர் கெழு மடந்தை ஈர் இமை பொருந்த","நள்ளென் கங்குல் கள்வன் போல","அகல் துறை ஊரனும் வந்தனன்","சிறந்தோன் பெயரன் பிறந்த மாறே"],"p":"","r":"The other woman says, within hearing of the wife's attendants, that at the guarded house where the long-tongued bell rings, the sand-strewn pandal is roofed with leaves, great bards keep watch and decked women stand on one side seeking omens, while the boy sleeps on a soft bed under spread cloth beside a nurse smelling of new birth and the noble mother, bathed in ghee with white mustard, soft-bodied, has shut both eyes, the lord of the wide-ford town too came at midnight like a thief, because a son was born bearing his grandfather's name.","n":"The 'too' (ஊரனும்) sets the husband among the other night visitors to a guarded house; the courtesan describes the birth-house rites in close detail, so her mockery shows she knows the household from outside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","makkal","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 40","lb":"NT40","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைமகட்குச் பாங்காயினார் கேட்பப் பரத்தை சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","pira","pira","karpu","பாங்காயினார் கேட்பப்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["மணி","மணி",1,"pira","scene","bell"],["மணற்","மணல்",2,"pira","scene",""],["பாண்","பாண்",3,"seythi","scene","bards"],["ஐயவி","ஐயவி",7,"maram","scene","white mustard"],["நெய்","நெய்",8,"unavu","scene","ghee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:2"},"nt:41":{"book":"natrinai","num":42,"t":["பைங் கண் யானை பரூஉ தாள் உதைத்த","வெண் புறக் களரி விடு நீறு ஆடி","சுரன் முதல் வருந்திய வருத்தம் பைபய","பாஅர் மலி சிறு கூவலின் தணியும்","நெடுஞ் சேண் சென்று வருந்துவர் மாதோ","எல்லி வந்த நல் இசை விருந்திற்கு","கிளர் இழை அரிவை நெய் துழந்து அட்ட","விளர் ஊன் அம் புகை எறிந்த நெற்றி","சிறு நுண் பல் வியர் பொறித்த","குறு நடைக் கூட்டம் வேண்டுவோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend steadies the wife by the way of the world: men go far and suffer in the waste, easing their weariness only at a small gravel-choked well after an elephant's kick has raised the salt dust, because they want the life in which a young wife, her forehead pricked with sweat from the smoke of ghee-stirred meat, cooks for a guest of good name who arrives at night.","n":"The friend's comfort is not that he will soon return but that his suffering has a purpose she can picture: the household's hospitality. The close-up of sweat on the wife's forehead puts the reward of his journey in her own kitchen, so the absence is reframed as work done for her house.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","virundhu","ilvaazhkkai","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 41","lb":"NT41","poet":"இளந்தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்டு ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகளைத் தோழி உலகியல் கூறி வற்புறுத்தியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","உலகியல் கூறி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கூவலின்","கூவல்",4,"pira","scene","small well"],["நெய்","நெய்",7,"unavu","gift_food","ghee"],["ஊன்","ஊன்",8,"unavu","gift_food","meat"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:42":{"book":"natrinai","num":43,"t":["மறத்தற்கு அரிது ஆல் பாக பல் நாள்","அறத்தொடு வருந்திய அல்கு தொழில் கொளீஇய","பழ மழை பொழிந்த புது நீர் அவல","நா நவில் பல் கிளை கறங்க மாண் வினை","மணி ஒலி கேளாள் வாணுதல் அதனால்","ஏகுமின் என்ற இளையர் வல்லே","இல் புக்கு அறியுநர் ஆக மெல்லென","மண்ணாக் கூந்தல் மாசு அற கழீஇ","சில் போது கொண்டு பல் குரல் அழுத்திய","அந்நிலை புகுதலின் மெய் வருத்துறாஅ","அவிழ் பூ முடியினள் கவைஇய","மட மா அரிவை மகிழ்ந்து அயர் நிலையே"],"p":"","r":"Returning from his task, the man tells his charioteer he cannot forget how, because the croaking of the frogs in the rain-filled hollows would drown the chariot's bells, the servants ran ahead to tell her, and she quickly washed her unwashed hair, pressed a few flowers into it and, without fuss, embraced him in her joy.","n":"Sending the servants ahead is a courtesy to her: she is spared being found unkempt. The poem's whole picture is the few minutes between the warning and the embrace, a domestic haste the hero treasures more than the welcome itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","punarchi","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 42","lb":"NT42","poet":"கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி மீள்வான் தேர்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றி மீள்வான் தேர்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மழை","மழை",3,"pira","scene","rain"],["கிளை","கிளை",4,"pira","scene","frogs (the croaking kin)"],["போது","போது",9,"maram","ornament","flower bud"],["பூ","பூ",11,"maram","ornament","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:43":{"book":"natrinai","num":44,"t":["துகில் விரித்தன்ன வெயில் அவிர் உருப்பின்","என்றூழ் நீடிய குன்றத்துக் கவாஅன்","ஓய்ப்பசி செந்நாய் உயங்கு மரை தொலைச்சி","ஆர்ந்தன ஒழிந்த மிச்சில் சேய் நாட்டு","அருஞ் சுரம் செல்வோர்க்கு வல்சி ஆகும்","வெம்மை ஆர் இடை இறத்தல் நுமக்கே","மெய் மலி உவகை ஆகின்று இவட்கே","அஞ்சல் என்ற இறை கைவிட்டென","பைங் கண் யானை வேந்து புறத்து இறுத்தலின்","களையுநர்க் காணாது கலங்கிய உடை மதில்","ஓர் எயில் மன்னன் போல","அழிவு வந்தன்றால் ஒழிதல் கேட்டே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that crossing the waste, where in heat that shimmers like spread cloth a starving red dog kills a weary wild cow and its leftovers feed travellers, is a joy to him, but for her, hearing of his going, ruin has come, like the king of a single fort whose overlord, after saying 'fear not', abandoned him when an elephant-led army laid siege and his broken wall found no defender.","n":"The simile turns the hero's earlier promise into the overlord's 'fear not' (அஞ்சல் என்ற இறை): she is the vassal fort he pledged to protect, and his departure is the abandonment. The only food on the road is a predator's leftovers, the measure of what he trades her for.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","arasu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 43","lb":"NT43","poet":"எயினந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட தோழி தலைவனைச் செலவு அழுங்குவித்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,"தோழி தலைவனைச் செலவு அழுங்குவித்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["செந்நாய்","செந்நாய்",3,"maa","scene","red dog"],["மரை","மரை",3,"maa","scene","wild cow"],["யானை","யானை",9,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:44":{"book":"natrinai","num":45,"t":["பொரு இல் ஆயமோடு அருவி ஆடி","நீர் அலைச் சிவந்த பேர் அமர் மழைக் கண்","குறியா நோக்கமொடு முறுவல் நல்கி","மனை வயின் பெயர்ந்த காலை நினைஇய","நினக்கோ அறியுநள் நெஞ்சே புனத்த","நீடு இலை விளை தினை கொடுங் கால் நிமிரக்","கொழுங் குரல் கோடல் கண்ணி செழும் பல","பல் கிளைக் குறவர் அல்கு அயர் முன்றில்","குடக் காய் ஆசினிப் படப்பை நீடிய","பல் மர உயர் சினை மின்மினி விளக்கத்து","செல் மழை இயக்கம் காணும்","நல் மலை நாடன் காதல் மகளே"],"p":"","r":"Kept from her after her confinement, the man at the meeting place asks his heart whether the daughter of the hill lord, who bathed in the falls with her companions until her large eyes reddened, gave him an unaimed glance and a smile and went home, can know what it longs for, in the land where kuravar wearing kodal garlands rest in their yards after the millet harvest and by the light of fireflies in the jackfruit groves watch the clouds pass.","n":"குறியா நோக்கம், a look not aimed at him, is all he has to go on, and the question to his heart is whether that look meant anything. The closing scene of people at ease in their yards watching clouds by firefly light is the leisure he no longer has.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","nenju","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 44","lb":"NT44","poet":"பெருங் கௌசிகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இச்செறிப்பின் பிற்றை ஞான்று தலைமகன் குறியிடத்து வந்து சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","தலைமகன் குறியிடத்து வந்து சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["தினை","தினை",6,"maram","scene","millet"],["கோடல்","கோடல்",7,"maram","ornament","glory lily"],["குறவர்","குறவர்",8,"seythi","scene","hill people"],["ஆசினிப்","ஆசினி",9,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["மின்மினி","மின்மினி",10,"pira","scene","firefly"],["மழை","மழை",11,"pira","scene","cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:45":{"book":"natrinai","num":46,"t":["இவளே கானல் நண்ணிய காமர் சிறுகுடி","நீல் நிறப் பெருங் கடல் கலங்க உள்புக்கு","மீன் எறி பரதவர் மகளே நீயே","நெடுங் கொடி நுடங்கும் நியம மூதூர்க்","கடுந் தேர்ச் செல்வன் காதல் மகனே","நிணச் சுறா அறுத்த உணக்கல் வேண்டி","இனப் புள் ஒப்பும் எமக்கு நலன் எவனோ","புலவு நாறுதும் செல நின்றீமோ","பெரு நீர் விளையுள் எம் சிறு நல் வாழ்க்கை","நும்மொடு புரைவதோ அன்றே","எம்மனோரில் செம்மலும் உடைத்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend turns the suitor away: she is the daughter of fishermen of the shore hamlet who go out into the blue sea to spear fish, and he the dear son of a rich man of the old market town of long flags and swift chariots; we who drive birds off the shark meat we dry smell of fish, so move on, for our small life from the sea is no match for yours, though our people too have their men of worth.","n":"The rebuff is pride dressed as humility: she lists their fish smell and small life only to end on செம்மலும் உடைத்தே, 'we too have our nobles'. The test is whether the rich man's son will persist across the difference of station.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","kalaviyal","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 45","lb":"NT45","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"குறை வேண்டிய தலைவனைத் தோழி சேட்படுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","குறை வேண்டிய தலைவனைத் தோழி சேட்படுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மீன்","மீன்",3,"pira","scene","fish"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",3,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["சுறா","சுறா",6,"pira","scene","shark"],["புள்","புள்",7,"pul","scene","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:46":{"book":"natrinai","num":47,"t":["வைகல் தோறும் இன்பமும் இளமையும்","எய் கணை நிழலின் கழியும் இவ் உலகத்து","காணீர் என்றலோ அரிதே அது நனி","பேணீர் ஆகுவிர் ஐய என் தோழி","பூண் அணி ஆகம் புலம்ப பாணர்","அயிர்ப்புக் கொண்டன்ன கொன்றை அம் தீம் கனி","பறை அறை கடிப்பின் அறை அறையாத் துயல்வர","வெவ் வளி வழங்கும் வேய் பயில் அழுவத்து","எவ்வம் மிகூஉம் அருஞ் சுரம் இறந்து","நல் வாய் அல்லா வாழ்க்கை","மன்னாப் பொருட் பிணிப் பிரிதும் யாம் எனவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man who has announced his going that pleasure and youth pass day by day like the shadow of a shot arrow, which he cannot claim not to see, yet he will not cherish them: he says he will leave her jewelled breast lonely, crossing the hard waste where the hot wind blows through the bamboo and the kondrai pods swing like drumsticks beating, held by the desire for wealth that does not last.","n":"Her argument uses the moralist's premise, that youth and pleasure are fleeting, to reach the lover's conclusion: what passes must be enjoyed now, and it is the wealth (மன்னாப் பொருள்) that is the impermanent thing. The image of the bards (line 6) is obscure; the drumstick image beside it is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","ilamai","pirivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 46","lb":"NT46","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவு உணர்த்திய தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,"பிரிவு உணர்த்திய தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாணர்","பாணர்",5,"seythi","simile","bards"],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",6,"maram","simile","kondrai"],["பறை","பறை",7,"parai","simile","drum"],["வளி","வளி",8,"pira","scene","wind"],["வேய்","வேய்",8,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:47":{"book":"natrinai","num":48,"t":["பெருங் களிறு உழுவை அட்டென இரும் பிடி","உயங்கு பிணி வருத்தமொடு இயங்கல் செல்லாது","நெய்தல் பாசடை புரையும் அம் செவிப்","பைதல் அம் குழவி தழீஇ ஒய்யென","அரும் புண் உறுநரின் வருந்தி வைகும்","கானக நாடற்கு இது என யான் அது","கூறின் எவனோ தோழி வேறு உணர்ந்து","அணங்கு அறி கழங்கின் கோட்டம் காட்டி","வெறி என உணர்ந்த உள்ளமொடு மறி அறுத்து","அன்னை அயரும் முருகு நின்","பொன் நேர் பசலைக்கு உதவா மாறே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend asks the girl what harm there would be in telling the lord of the forest land, where a cow elephant whose great tusker a tiger has killed cannot move for grief and suffers like one gravely wounded, holding her calf with ears like green neythal leaves, that the mother, misreading the sign of the molucca beans as possession, kills a kid for the Murugu rite that cannot help her gold-like pallor.","n":"The bereaved elephant shows what the friend fears the girl will become if he does not come to marry her, and the rite shows why no one at home can cure her. Telling him 'this' (இது) is the whole message, and he is standing where he can hear it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","pasalai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 47","lb":"NT47","poet":"நல்வெள்ளியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி தலைமகட்கு உரைப்பாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி தலைமகட்கு உரைப்பாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",1,"maa","ullurai","tusker"],["உழுவை","உழுவை",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["பிடி","பிடி",1,"maa","ullurai","cow elephant"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","simile","water lily"],["குழவி","குழவி",4,"maa","scene","calf"],["கழங்கின்","கழங்கு",8,"pira","scene","molucca beans"],["மறி","மறி",9,"maa","scene","kid (goat)"],["முருகு","முருகு",10,"deyvam","scene","Murugu"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","களிறு பிடி","not_confirmed","0/1","the girl, left to waste if her lover is lost to her"],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:48":{"book":"natrinai","num":49,"t":["அன்றை அனைய ஆகி இன்று உம் எம்","கண் உள போலச் சுழலும் மாதோ","புல் இதழ்க் கோங்கின் மெல் இதழ்க் குடைப் பூ","வைகுறு மீனின் நினையத் தோன்றி","புறவு அணி கொண்ட பூ நாறு கடத்திடை","கிடின் என இடிக்கும் கோல் தொடி மறவர்","வடி நவில் அம்பின் வினையர் அஞ்சாது","அமர் இடை உறுதர நீக்கி நீர்","எமர் இடை உறுதர ஒளித்த காடே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man who has announced his parting that even today, as on that day, the forest swirls before their eyes: the soft umbrella blossoms of the kongu shining like the stars at dawn in the flower-scented waste, the bangled bowmen with whetted arrows who came fearlessly at them, whom he drove off in the fight, and the forest where he hid them when their own kin came close.","n":"The memory is of the elopement, when he fought robbers and hid her from her pursuing kin. Recalling it now, the friend sets his old courage for her against his present wish to leave her; the reproach is never spoken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ninaivu","pirivu","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 48","lb":"NT48","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவு உணர்த்திய தலைவற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","பிரிவு உணர்த்திய தலைவற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கோங்கின்","கோங்கு",3,"maram","scene","kongu"],["மீனின்","மீன்",4,"pira","simile","star"],["மறவர்","மறவர்",6,"seythi","scene","bowmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:49":{"book":"natrinai","num":50,"t":["படு திரை கொழீஇய பால் நிற எக்கர்த்","தொடியோர் மடிந்தெனத் துறை புலம்பின்றே","முடி வலை முகந்த முடங்கு இறா பரவைப்","படு புள் ஓப்பலின் பகல் மாய்ந்தன்றே","கோட்டுமீன் எறிந்த உவகையர் வேட்டம் மடிந்து","எமரும் அல்கினர் ஏமார்ந்தனம் என","சென்று நாம் அறியின் எவனோ தோழி","மன்றப் புன்னை மா சினை நறு வீ","முன்றில் தாழையொடு கமழும்","தெண் கடற் சேர்ப்பன் வாழ் சிறு நல் ஊர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the bangled women have gone in from the milk-white dune and the shore is empty, the day has ended with driving birds off the curled prawns spread to dry, and their own men, happy with the sharks they speared, have stopped fishing and gone to rest; so what if they went now, feeling safe, to the small good town of the lord of the clear sea, where the punnai flowers of the common ground mingle their scent with the screwpine of the yard?","n":"Every clause of the first half closes something down (the women, the shore, the day, the men), and only then does the friend propose they go to him. The stillness of their own people is the permission for the night meeting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 49","lb":"NT49","poet":"நெய்தல் தத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகளை இரவுக்குறி நயப்பித்தது சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி ஆற்றாமை வியந்தூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி தலைமகளை இரவுக்குறி நயப்பித்தது"],["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி ஆற்றாமை வியந்தூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இறா","இறா",3,"pira","scene","prawn"],["புள்","புள்",4,"pul","scene","birds"],["கோட்டுமீன்","கோட்டு மீன்",5,"pira","scene","shark"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",8,"maram","scene","punnai"],["தாழையொடு","தாழை",9,"maram","scene","screwpine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:50":{"book":"natrinai","num":51,"t":["அறியாமையின் அன்னை அஞ்சி","குழையன் கோதையன் குறும் பைத் தொடியன்","விழவு அயர் துணங்கை தழூஉகம் செல்ல","நெடு நிமிர் தெருவில் கை புகு கொடு மிடை","நொதுமலாளன் கதுமெனத் தாக்கலின்","கேட்போர் உளர்கொல் இல்லைகொல் போற்று என","யாணது பசலை என்றனன் அதன் எதிர்","நாண் இலை எலுவ என்று வந்திசினே","செறுநரும் விழையும் செம்மலோன் என","நறு நுதல் அரிவை போற்றேன்","சிறுமை பெருமையின் காணாது துணிந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the bard how his master, decked in earrings, garland and a small green bangle, jostled her as if a stranger in the narrow press of the long street as she went to join the festival dance, warning her that someone might hear and remarking on pallor, how she answered 'you have no shame, fellow' and came away, and how she had failed to guard the girl because she trusted him, unseeing, as a man even his enemies admire, taking his smallness for greatness.","n":"The bard comes as go-between, and the friend answers with an incident that shows the master's true size. The last line is her own confession: the fault was trusting without looking.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","therithal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 50","lb":"NT50","poet":"மருதம் பாடிய இளங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி பாணர்க்கு வாயில் மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","paanan",null,"karpu","தோழி பாணர்க்கு வாயில் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோதையன்","கோதை",2,"maram","ornament","garland"],["துணங்கை","துணங்கை",3,"yaazh","scene","festival dance"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:51":{"book":"natrinai","num":52,"t":["யாங்குச் செய்வாம்கொல் தோழி ஓங்கு கழைக்","காம்புடை விடர் அகம் சிலம்ப பாம்பு உடன்று","ஓங்கு வரை மிளிர ஆட்டி வீங்கு செலல்","கடுங் குரல் ஏறொடு கனை துளி தலைஇப்","பெயல் ஆனாதே வானம் பெயலொடு","மின்னு நிமிர்ந்தன்ன வேலன் வந்தென","பின்னு விடு முச்சி அளிப்பு ஆனாதே","பெருந் தண் குளவி குழைத்த பா அடி","இருஞ் சேறு ஆடிய நுதல கொல்களிறு","பேதை ஆசினி ஒசித்த","வீ ததர் வேங்கைய மலை கிழவோற்கே"],"p":"","r":"The girl asks her friend what they can do: the rain will not stop, with loud thunder that makes the bamboo clefts ring and angers the snakes and shakes the high hills, and with the rain the velan has come like stretched lightning, and the caressing of her loosened hair goes on, all for the lord of the hill where a killer tusker, its feet soaked in crushed wild jasmine and its forehead caked with mud, has broken the innocent young jackfruit tree among vengai trees strewn with flowers.","n":"Her fear has two objects: the storm on the path he must take by night, and the rite that will expose her. பேதை ஆசினி, 'the innocent jackfruit', uses a word for a young girl, which marks the broken tree as her own case.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veri","velan","acham","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 51","lb":"NT51","poet":"பேராலவாயர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"ஆற்றது ஏதம் அஞ்சி வேறுபட்டாள் வெறியாடலுற்ற இடத்து சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","வெறியாடலுற்ற இடத்து சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["கழைக்","கழை",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",2,"pira","scene","snake"],["ஏறொடு","ஏறு",4,"pira","scene","thunder"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",6,"seythi","scene","diviner"],["குளவி","குளவி",8,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"],["கொல்களிறு","கொல் களிறு",9,"maa","ullurai","killer tusker"],["ஆசினி","ஆசினி",10,"maram","ullurai","jackfruit"],["வேங்கைய","வேங்கை",11,"maram","ullurai","vengai"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","கொல்களிறு ஆசினி வேங்கைய","confirmed","1/1","the hill lord and the girl his secret love has wasted"]],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:52":{"book":"natrinai","num":53,"t":["மாக் கொடி அதிரற் பூவொடு பாதிரித்","தூத் தகட்டு எதிர் மலர் வேய்ந்த கூந்தல்","மணம் கமழ் நாற்றம் மரீஇ யாம் இவள்","சுணங்கு அணி ஆகம் அடைய முயங்கி","வீங்கு உவர்க் கவவின் நீங்கல் செல்லேம்","நீயே ஆள்வினை சிறப்ப எண்ணி நாளும்","பிரிந்து உறை வாழ்க்கை புரிந்து அமையலையே","அன்பு இலை வாழி என் நெஞ்சே வெம் போர்","மழவர் பெரு மகன் மா வள் ஓரி","கை வளம் இயைவது ஆயினும்","ஐது ஏகு அம்ம இயைந்து செய் பொருளே"],"p":"","r":"The man answers his heart: he will not leave the embrace of his wife, whose hair is crowned with athiral and fresh pathiri blossoms, but the heart, intent on effort, wants a life of living apart and is never content; it is loveless, for even if wealth came equal to the giving of Ori, the generous lord of the Mazhavar fierce in war, wealth made by toil would be a slight thing.","n":"The hero measures the most he could gain by a patron's giving and finds it small against one embrace. வாழி, 'long may you live', said to the loveless heart, is an ironic blessing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","nenju","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 52","lb":"NT52","poet":"பாலத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் செலவு அழுங்கியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","தலைமகன் செலவு அழுங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["அதிரற்","அதிரல்",1,"maram","ornament","athiral"],["பாதிரித்","பாதிரி",1,"maram","ornament","pathiri"]],"en":[["patron","ஓரி",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:53":{"book":"natrinai","num":54,"t":["யான் அஃது அஞ்சினென் கரப்பவும் தான் அஃது","அறிந்தனள்கொல்லோ அருளினள்கொல்லோ","எவன்கொல் தோழி அன்னை கண்ணியது","வான் உற நிவந்த பெரு மலைக் கவாஅன்","ஆர் கலி வானம் தலைஇ நடு நாள்","கனை பெயல் பொழிந்தென கானக் கல் யாற்று","முளி இலை கழித்தன முகிழ் இணரொடு வரும்","விருந்தின் தீம் நீர் மருந்தும் ஆகும்","தண்ணென உண்டு கண்ணின் நோக்கி","முனியாது ஆடப் பெறின் இவள்","பனியும் தீர்குவள் செல்க என்றோளே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend tells the girl she hid it out of fear but wonders whether the mother knew or simply took pity, for the mother said: the sweet new water of the forest's stony stream, which after midnight rain on the sky-high hill slope comes down with dead leaves and budding clusters, is medicine too; if she drinks it cool, looks on it and bathes in it gladly, her shivering will pass; go.","n":"The mother prescribes water from the lover's own hill. The friend's question (does she know, or is she being kind?) is meant for the man: if the mother knows, the secret is over and he must marry; if she pities, time is short. பனி is both the chill of fever and the trembling of love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","marundhu","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 53","lb":"NT53","poet":"நல்வேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரைவு நீட்டிப்ப தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","வரைவு நீட்டிப்ப தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["யாற்று","யாறு",6,"pira","scene","stream"],["இலை","இலை",7,"maram","scene","leaf"],["இணரொடு","இணர்",7,"maram","scene","flower cluster"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:54":{"book":"natrinai","num":55,"t":["வளை நீர் மேய்ந்து கிளை முதல்செலீஇ","வாப் பறை விரும்பினைஆயினும் தூச் சிறை","இரும் புலா அருந்தும் நின் கிளையொடு சிறிது இருந்து","கருங் கால் வெண் குருகு எனவ கேண்மதி","பெரும் புலம்பின்றே சிறு புன் மாலை","அது நீ அறியின் அன்புமார் உடையை","நொதுமல் நெஞ்சம் கொள்ளாது என் குறை","இற்றாங்கு உணர உரைமதி தழையோர்","கொய்குழை அரும்பிய குமரி ஞாழல்","தெண் திரை மணிப் புறம் தைவரும்","கண்டல் வேலி நும் துறை கிழவோற்கே"],"p":"","r":"The woman begs the white heron with black legs, which after feeding in the curving water longs to fly home to its kin, to stay a little with its fish-eating flock and listen: the small dull evening is deeply lonely; if it knows that, it has love too, so let it tell her plight as it is, not with a stranger's heart, to the lord of its shore fenced with kandal, where the young njazhal whose shoots the leaf-skirted girls pluck is caressed by clear waves.","n":"She calls the shore 'your shore' (நும் துறை), giving the bird a claim on the man that she does not yet have. The heron going home at evening is the very thing she lacks, which is why she asks it to wait.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 54","lb":"NT54","poet":"சேந்தங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"காமம் மிக்க கழிபடர்கிளவி","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,"காமம் மிக்க கழிபடர்கிளவி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["புலா","புலா",3,"unavu","gift_food","fish flesh"],["குருகு","குருகு",4,"pul","scene","heron"],["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",9,"maram","scene","njazhal"],["கண்டல்","கண்டல்",11,"maram","scene","kandal"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:55":{"book":"natrinai","num":56,"t":["ஓங்கு மலை நாட ஒழிக நின் வாய்மை","காம்பு தலைமணந்த கல் அதர்ச் சிறு நெறி","உறு பகை பேணாது இரவின் வந்து இவள்","பொறி கிளர் ஆகம் புல்ல தோள் சேர்பு","அறுகாற் பறவை அளவு இல மொய்த்தலின்","கண் கோள் ஆக நோக்கி பண்டும்","இனையையோ என வினவினள் யாயே","அதன் எதிர் சொல்லாளாகி அல்லாந்து","என் முகம் நோக்கியோளே அன்னாய்","யாங்கு உணர்ந்து உய்குவள்கொல் என மடுத்த","சாந்த ஞெகிழி காட்டி","ஈங்கு ஆயினவால் என்றிசின் யானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hill lord to keep his truthfulness: he came by night down the stony path among the bamboo, careless of danger, and embraced the girl, and when countless bees swarmed on her from his sandal, the mother looked hard and asked 'were you like this before?'; the girl, unable to answer, looked at the friend's face, and the friend, wondering how she would escape, pointed to a burning sandalwood firebrand and said 'that is what did it'.","n":"ஒழிக நின் வாய்மை is sarcasm: his honest love has forced the women into a lie. The friend's quick wit saves them once, which is her warning that the night visits cannot go on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","iravu","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 55","lb":"NT55","poet":"பெருவழுதி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரைவிடை மெலிவு ஆற்றுவிக்கும் தோழி தலைவற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவிடை மெலிவு ஆற்றுவிக்கும் தோழி தலைவற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["காம்பு","காம்பு",2,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பறவை","அறுகால் பறவை",5,"pira","scene","bee"],["சாந்த","சாந்தம்",11,"maram","scene","sandalwood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:56":{"book":"natrinai","num":57,"t":["குறு நிலைக் குரவின் சிறு நனை நறு வீ","வண்டு தரு நாற்றம் வளி கலந்து ஈய","கண் களி பெறூஉம் கவின் பெறு காலை","எல் வளை ஞெகிழ்த்தோர்க்கு அல்லல் உறீஇச்","சென்ற நெஞ்சம் செய்வினைக்கு அசாவா","ஒருங்கு வரல் நசையொடு வருந்தும்கொல்லோ","அருளான் ஆதலின் அழிந்து இவண் வந்து","தொல் நலன் இழந்த என் பொன் நிறம் நோக்கி","ஏதிலாட்டி இவள் எனப்","போயின்று கொல்லோ நோய் தலைமணந்த"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether, in this lovely season when the wind carries the scent bees bring from the small kuravam buds, her heart, which went after the man who loosened her bangles, is suffering with him, unwearied by his task, in the hope of coming back together with him, or whether, since he is not kind, it came back, saw her golden colour lost its old beauty, and went away saying 'she is a stranger', joined to her sickness.","n":"Her two guesses are both about the heart's loyalty, not the man's: either it stays faithful to him, or it disowns her now that her beauty is gone. The poem ends on the word தலைமணந்த, as if broken off.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pasalai","varaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 56","lb":"NT56","poet":"பெருவழுதி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"வரைவிடை மெலிவு ஆற்றுவிக்கும் தோழிக்குத் தலைவி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தோழிக்குத் தலைவி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குரவின்","குரவு",1,"maram","scene","kuravam"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bee"],["வளி","வளி",2,"pira","scene","wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:57":{"book":"natrinai","num":58,"t":["தடங்கோட்டு ஆமான் மடங்கல் மா நிரைக்","குன்ற வேங்கைக் கன்றொடு வதிந்தென","துஞ்சு பதம் பெற்ற துய்த் தலை மந்தி","கல்லென் சுற்றம் கை கவியாக் குறுகி","வீங்கு சுரை ஞெமுங்க வாங்கி தீம் பால்","கல்லா வன் பறழ்க் கைந் நிறை பிழியும்","மா மலை நாட மருட்கை உடைத்தே","செங் கோல் கொடுங் குரல் சிறு தினை வியன் புனம்","கொய் பதம் குறுகும்காலை எம்","மை ஈர் ஓதி மாண் நலம் தொலைவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the great hill that it is bewildering how on his slopes, when the herd of big-horned wild cows rests with their calves by the hill vengai, a tufted female monkey that has caught them asleep creeps up with her noisy troop, cupping her hands, squeezes the swollen udder and wrings the sweet milk into the hands of her untaught young; but when the time to pluck the red-stalked millet comes near, the girl's oiled dark hair will lose its beauty.","n":"பதம, the right moment, links the two halves: the monkey takes its moment while the herd sleeps (துஞ்சு பதம்), and the girl's moment is closing as the millet reaches its plucking time (கொய் பதம்). The friend's message is that the stolen meetings end with the harvest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 57","lb":"NT57","poet":"பொதும்பில் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["ஆமான்","ஆ மான்",1,"maa","ullurai","wild cow"],["வேங்கைக்","வேங்கை",2,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",3,"maa","ullurai","female monkey"],["பறழ்க்","பறழ்",6,"maa","scene","young monkey"],["தினை","தினை",8,"maram","scene","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","ஆமான் மந்தி","confirmed","1/1","the secret love taken from the kin's keeping while they are off guard"]],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:58":{"book":"natrinai","num":59,"t":["பெரு முது செல்வர் பொன்னுடைப்புதல்வர்","சிறு தோட் கோத்த செவ் அரிப்பறையின்","கண்ணகத்து எழுதிய குரீஇப் போல","கோல் கொண்டு அலைப்பப் படீஇயர்மாதோ","வீரை வேண்மான் வெளியன் தித்தன்","முரசு முதல் கொளீஇய மாலை விளக்கின்","வெண் கோடு இயம்ப நுண் பனி அரும்ப","கையற வந்த பொழுதொடு மெய் சோர்ந்து","அவல நெஞ்சினம் பெயர உயர் திரை","நீடு நீர்ப் பனித் துறைச் சேர்ப்பன்","ஓடு தேர் நுண் நுகம் நுழைந்த மாவே"],"p":"","r":"As the man leaves after the daytime meeting, the friend curses his horse: may it be beaten with a stick like the sparrow painted on the face of the red-striped toy drum hung on the small shoulder of a rich old family's gold-decked son, the horse yoked to the running chariot of the lord of the cold shore, which took him away at the hour when, with the evening lamp lit at the first drum of Veliyan Thithan of Veerai, the white conch sounding and fine dew forming, we turned back drooping and sad.","n":"The curse is comic in scale, a toy drum and a painted bird, which keeps the anger from touching the man himself. The evening is fixed by a chieftain's court ritual, the lamp and drum of Thithan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kalaviyal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 58","lb":"NT58","poet":"முதுகூற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறி வந்து நீங்கும் தலைமகன் போக்கு நோக்கி தோழி மாவின்மேல்வைத்துச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"kalavu","பகற்குறி வந்து நீங்கும் தலைமகன் போக்கு நோக்கி தோழி மாவின்மேல்வைத்துச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பறையின்","பறை",2,"parai","simile","toy drum"],["குரீஇப்","குரீஇ",3,"pul","simile","sparrow"],["முரசு","முரசு",6,"parai","scene","drum"],["கோடு","கோடு",7,"pira","scene","conch"],["மாவே","மா",11,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["patron","வெளியன் தித்தன்",5],["town","வீரை",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:59":{"book":"natrinai","num":60,"t":["உடும்பு கொலீஇ வரி நுணல் அகழ்ந்து","நெடுங் கோட்டுப் புற்றத்து ஈயல் கெண்டி","எல்லு முயல் எறிந்த வேட்டுவன் சுவல","பல் வேறு பண்டத் தொடை மறந்து இல்லத்து","இரு மடைக் கள்ளின் இன் களி செருக்கும்","வன் புலக் காட்டு நாட்டதுவேஅன்பு கலந்து","நம்வயின் புரிந்த கொள்கையொடு நெஞ்சத்து","உள்ளினள் உறைவோள் ஊரே முல்லை","நுண் முகை அவிழ்ந்த புறவின்","பொறை தலை மணந்தன்று உயவுமார் இனியே"],"p":"","r":"Returning from his task, the man tells his charioteer that her town, she who lives holding him in her heart with the loving resolve she gave him, is in the hard forest land where the hunter who killed a monitor lizard, dug out a striped frog, cut termites from a tall anthill and struck down a hare, comes home with his load and, forgetting his many wares, revels in sweet toddy; and now that the mullai's fine buds have opened in the woods and the rocks are covered, it is painful too.","n":"The hunter's evening is the homecoming the hero is driving toward: work over, the catch home, everything else forgotten in ease. The last line turns: the season of return has come, and its beauty hurts while he is still on the road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","ninaivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 59","lb":"NT59","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைமுற்றி மீள்வான்தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினைமுற்றி மீள்வான்தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["உடும்பு","உடும்பு",1,"maa","scene","monitor lizard"],["நுணல்","நுணல்",1,"pira","scene","frog"],["ஈயல்","ஈயல்",2,"pira","scene","winged termites"],["முயல்","முயல்",3,"maa","scene","hare"],["வேட்டுவன்","வேட்டுவன்",3,"seythi","iraicchi","hunter"],["கள்ளின்","கள்",5,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",8,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வேட்டுவன்","not_confirmed","0/1","the hero, his task done, is going home to the rest and the wife that wait for him"],["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:60":{"book":"natrinai","num":61,"t":["மலை கண்டன்ன நிலை புணர்நிவப்பின்","பெரு நெற் பல் கூட்டு எருமை உழவ","கண்படை பெறாஅது தண் புலர் விடியல்","கருங் கண் வராஅல் பெருந் தடி மிளிர்வையொடு","புகர்வை அரிசிப் பொம்மற் பெருஞ் சோறு","கவர் படு கையை கழும மாந்தி","நீர் உறு செறுவின் நாறு முடி அழுத்த நின்","நடுநரொடு சேறிஆயின் அவண்","சாயும் நெய்தலும் ஓம்புமதி எம்மில்","மா இருங் கூந்தல் மடந்தை","ஆய் வளை கூட்டும் அணியுமார் அவையே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend tells the ploughman of the buffaloes, whose paddy stacks stand like a mountain, who goes out sleepless at cool dawn after gorging on a heap of rice with thick slices of black-eyed varal fish, that if he goes with his planters to press the seedlings into the flooded field, he should spare the sedge and the water lilies there, for they are what the dark-haired girl of our house threads into her bangles and wears.","n":"The request to spare the field plants is a message about the girl: she is kept at home now and will not be in the fields, and the man who used to meet her there is told so through a ploughman.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 60","lb":"NT60","poet":"தூங்கலோரியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாக உழவர்க்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தோழி செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாக உழவர்க்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தோழி செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நெற்","நெல்",2,"unavu","scene","paddy"],["எருமை","எருமை",2,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["உழவ","உழவன்",2,"seythi","scene","ploughman"],["வராஅல்","வரால்",4,"pira","gift_food","varal fish"],["அரிசிப்","அரிசி",5,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["நாறு","நாறு",7,"maram","scene","seedlings"],["சாயும்","சாய்",9,"maram","ornament","sedge"],["நெய்தலும்","நெய்தல்",9,"maram","ornament","water lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:3"},"nt:61":{"book":"natrinai","num":62,"t":["கேளாய் எல்ல தோழி அல்கல்","வேணவா நலிய வெய்ய உயிரா","ஏ மான் பிணையின் வருந்தினெனாக","துயர் மருங்கு அறிந்தனள் போல அன்னை","துஞ்சாயோ என் குறுமகள் என்றலின்","சொல் வெளிப்படாமை மெல்ல என் நெஞ்சில்","படு மழை பொழிந்த பாறை மருங்கில்","சிரல் வாய் உற்ற தளவின் பரல் அவல்","கான் கெழு நாடற் படர்ந்தோர்க்குக்","கண்ணும் படுமோ என்றிசின் யானே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker tells her friend that last night, tormented by longing and breathing hot, she suffered like a doe struck by an arrow; her mother, as if she guessed the cause, asked 'Don't you sleep, my little one?', and she, keeping the words from coming out, said softly in her heart: can eyes sleep for those who long for the man of the forest land, where by the rain-washed rock the stony hollow holds jasmine buds like a kingfisher's beak?","n":"The answer is never spoken: the reply to the mother is made inside the heart (சொல் வெளிப்படாமை), so the poem shows secrecy holding even at the moment the mother comes closest to knowing. The colophon makes the friend the speaker, voicing this as the heroine's night so that the man, who has come and stands near, hears what his delay costs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kaamanoy","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 61","lb":"NT61","poet":"சிறுமோலிகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் வரவு உணர்ந்து தலைவிக்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய்த் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைவன் வரவு உணர்ந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மான்","மான்",3,"maa","scene",""],["மழை","மழை",7,"pira","scene","rain"],["சிரல்","சிரல்",8,"pul","scene","kingfisher"],["தளவின்","தளவம்",8,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:62":{"book":"natrinai","num":63,"t":["வேர் பிணி வெதிரத்துக் கால் பொரு நரல் இசை","கந்து பிணி யானை அயர் உயிர்த்தன்ன","என்றூழ் நீடிய வேய் பயில் அழுவத்து","குன்று ஊர் மதியம் நோக்கி நின்று நினைந்து","உள்ளினென் அல்லெனோ யானே முள் எயிற்று","திலகம் தைஇய தேம் கமழ் திரு நுதல்","எமதும் உண்டு ஓர் மதிநாட் திங்கள்","உரறு குரல் வெவ் வளி எடுப்ப நிழல் தப","உலவை ஆகிய மரத்த","கல் பிறங்கு உயர் மலை உம்பரஃது எனவே"],"p":"","r":"Pressed again toward wealth, the man reminds his heart how on the earlier journey, in the long-summer bamboo waste where wind-rubbed bamboos groan like a tethered elephant's weary sigh, he stood looking at the moon climbing the hill and thought: we too have a moon of our own, her sweet-smelling, tilakam-marked forehead, beyond that stony mountain where hot roaring wind has stripped the shade and left the trees dry.","n":"The refusal to go is never stated: the poem offers only a memory, and the memory is enough. The moon he saw on the road is demoted to a second moon; the first is at home, so the journey is pictured as going away from the light.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","ninaivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 62","lb":"NT62","poet":"இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"முன் ஒரு காலத்துப்பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்து வந்த தலைவன் பின்னும் பொருள்வலிக்கப்பட்ட நெஞ்சிற்குச் செலவு அழுங்குவித்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","நெஞ்சிற்குச் செலவு அழுங்குவித்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வெதிரத்துக்","வெதிரம்",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","simile",""],["வேய்","வேய்",3,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["மரத்த","மரம்",9,"maram","absent",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:63":{"book":"natrinai","num":64,"t":["உரவுக் கடல் உழந்த பெரு வலைப்பரதவர்","மிகு மீன் உணக்கிய புது மணல் ஆங்கண்","கல்லென் சேரிப் புலவற் புன்னை","விழவு நாறு விளங்கு இணர் அவிழ்ந்து உடன் கமழும்","அழுங்கல் ஊரோ அறன் இன்று அதனால்","அறன் இல் அன்னை அருங் கடிப் படுப்ப","பசலை ஆகி விளிவதுகொல்லோ","புள் உற ஒசிந்த பூ மயங்கு அள்ளல்","கழிச் சுரம் நிவக்கும் இருஞ் சிறை இவுளி","திரை தரு புணரியின் கழூஉம்","மலி திரைச் சேர்ப்பனொடு அமைந்த நம் தொடர்பே"],"p":"","r":"With the man standing by, the friend says the clamorous shore town, where fishermen dry their catch on fresh sand and the punnai blooms with a festival fragrance over the stench of fish, has no justice; so the unjust mother has put the girl under strict guard, and their bond with the lord of the wave-washed shore, whose dark-winged horses rise over the flower-choked backwater mud and are washed by the sea, may waste into pallor and die.","n":"The word அறன் is used twice in two lines, of the town and of the mother: gossip and guard are the same injustice seen from outside and inside the house. The bond, not the girl, is what turns pale (பசலை), a bold shift of the lovesick sign onto the relation itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["irserippu","alar","pasalai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 63","lb":"NT63","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"அலர் அச்சத்தால்தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகச் செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["மீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","scene","fish"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",3,"maram","scene",""],["புள்","புள்",8,"pul","scene",""],["பூ","பூ",8,"maram","scene",""],["இவுளி","இவுளி",9,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:64":{"book":"natrinai","num":65,"t":["என்னர்ஆயினும் இனி நினைவு ஒழிக","அன்னவாக இனையல் தோழி யாம்","இன்னமாக நத் துறந்தோர் நட்பு எவன்","மரல் நார் உடுக்கை மலை உறை குறவர்","அறியாது அறுத்த சிறியிலைச் சாந்தம்","வறனுற்று ஆர முருக்கி பையென","மரம் வறிதாகச் சோர்ந்து உக்காங்கு என்","அறிவும் உள்ளமும் அவர் வயின் சென்றென","வறிதால் இகுளை என் யாக்கை இனி அவர்","வரினும் நோய் மருந்து அல்லர் வாராது","அவணர் ஆகுக காதலர் இவண் நம்","காமம் படர் அட வருந்திய","நோய் மலி வருத்தம் காணன்மார் எமரே"],"p":"","r":"When her friend wants to send him word, the woman says: stop thinking of him, whatever he is; what is the friendship of one who left us like this? As the small-leaved sandal tree that the hill Kuravar, clad in maral fibre, cut without knowing dries in the drought and slowly withers to an empty trunk, so my mind and heart have gone to him and my body is empty; even if he comes he is no medicine for this sickness, so let him stay there, and let my kin not see the suffering of love that crushes me.","n":"The sandal tree is cut 'without knowing' (அறியாது), so the image blames carelessness, not cruelty; that is why she can refuse him without hating him. Her last wish is not for him but that her kin should not see: secrecy outlives hope.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy","marundhu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 64","lb":"NT64","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பிரிவிடைத் தலைவியது அருமை கண்டு தூதுவிடக் கருதிய தோழிக்குத் தலைவி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தூதுவிடக் கருதிய தோழிக்குத் தலைவி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மரல்","மரல்",4,"maram","scene",""],["குறவர்","குறவர்",4,"seythi","simile",""],["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",5,"maram","simile","sandalwood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:65":{"book":"natrinai","num":66,"t":["அமுதம் உண்க நம் அயல் இலாட்டி","கிடங்கில் அன்ன இட்டுக் கரைக் கான் யாற்றுக்","கலங்கும் பாசி நீர் அலைக் கலாவ","ஒளிறு வெள் அருவி ஒண் துறை மடுத்து","புலியொடு பொருத புண் கூர் யானை","நற் கோடு நயந்த அன்பு இல் கானவர்","விற் சுழிப்பட்ட நாமப் பூசல்","உருமிடைக் கடி இடி கரையும்","பெரு மலை நாடனை வரூஉம் என்றோளே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, delighted, says: may our neighbour woman eat ambrosia, for she said 'he will come', the man of the great mountain land where, at the bright ford of a white waterfall and a forest river banked like a moat with swirling moss, an elephant wounded in a fight with a tiger is beset by pitiless hunters who covet its tusk, and their fearsome clamour sounds through the thunder.","n":"The neighbour's chance words are taken as an omen (the colophon's விரிச்சி): the friend blesses an unknowing speaker for a sentence not meant for them. The violent hill scene that follows is the land's ordinary life, offered as the man's address, not as a warning.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virichi","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 65","lb":"NT65","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"விரிச்சி பெற்றுப்புகன்ற தோழி தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","விரிச்சி பெற்றுப்புகன்ற தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பாசி","பாசி",3,"maram","scene","moss"],["அருவி","அருவி",4,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["புலியொடு","புலி",5,"maa","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","scene",""],["கானவர்","கானவர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["உருமிடைக்","உருமு",8,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:66":{"book":"natrinai","num":67,"t":["மிளகு பெய்தனைய சுவைய புன் காய்","உலறு தலை உகாஅய்ச் சிதர் சிதர்ந்து உண்ட","புலம்பு கொள் நெடுஞ் சினை ஏறி நினைந்து தன்","பொறி கிளர் எருத்தம் வெறி பட மறுகி","புன் புறா உயவும் வெந் துகள் இயவின்","நயந்த காதலற் புணர்ந்தனள் ஆயினும்","சிவந்து ஒளி மழுங்கி அமர்த்தனகொல்லோ","கோதை மயங்கினும் குறுந் தொடி நெகிழினும்","காழ் பெயல் அல்குல் காசு முறை திரியினும்","மாண் நலம் கையறக் கலுழும் என்","மாயக் குறுமகள் மலர் ஏர் கண்ணே"],"p":"","r":"The mother, at home after her daughter has left with her lover, wonders whether on the hot dusty track, where a dull dove, having pecked the pepper-sharp small fruits of the dry-topped ukaay, climbs a lonely long branch and, ruffling its spotted neck, moans, her deceitful little girl's flower-like eyes, which used to weep away their beauty if her garland was disarranged, her small bangle loosened or her coin girdle shifted, are now red, dimmed and troubled, even though she is with the man she loves.","n":"The mother does not doubt the man; she grants that the girl has joined him (புணர்ந்தனள் ஆயினும்). Her worry is built from memory of tiny vanities, so the measure of the road's hardship is a childhood in which a loosened bangle was a disaster. மாயக் குறுமகள், 'deceitful little girl', is tender, not reproach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["udanpokku","kan","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 66","lb":"NT66","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மனை மருட்சி இனிசந்த நாகனார்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu","மனை மருட்சி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மிளகு","மிளகு",1,"unavu","simile","pepper"],["உகாஅய்ச்","உகாய்",2,"maram","scene",""],["புறா","புறா",5,"pul","scene","dove"],["கோதை","கோதை",8,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:67":{"book":"natrinai","num":68,"t":["சேய் விசும்பு இவர்ந்த செழுங் கதிர் மண்டிலம்","மால் வரை மறைய துறை புலம்பின்றே","இறவு அருந்தி எழுந்த கருங் கால் வெண் குருகு","வெண் கோட்டு அருஞ் சிறைத் தாஅய் கரைய","கருங் கோட்டுப் புன்னை இறைகொண்டனவே","கணைக் கால் மா மலர் கரப்ப மல்கு கழித்","துணைச் சுறா வழங்கலும் வழங்கும் ஆயிடை","எல் இமிழ் பனிக் கடல் மல்கு சுடர்க் கொளீஇ","எமரும் வேட்டம் புக்கனர் அதனால்","தங்கின் எவனோதெய்ய பொங்கு பிசிர்","முழவு இசைப் புணரி எழுதரும்","உடை கடற் படப்பை எம் உறைவின் ஊர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"As the man who came for the day meeting turns to go, the friend says the red sun has crossed the far sky and hidden behind the great hill, the ford is lonely, the black-legged white herons that fed on prawns have settled on the black-branched punnai, paired sharks prowl the backwater hiding the big flowers, and our men, lighting lamps on the cold dark sea, have gone fishing: so what harm if you stayed in our seaside town where the spray-flinging waves rise with the sound of drums?","n":"The invitation to stay one night is the pressure to marry: a man who sleeps in her town must become its son-in-law. The detail that 'our men' are out on the sea makes the offer bolder than it sounds, and the prowling sharks quietly tell him the shore path is no place for a night journey.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kuri","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 67","lb":"NT67","poet":"பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறி வந்து நீங்கும் தலைமகனைத் தோழி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இறவு","இறவு",3,"pira","gift_food","prawn"],["குருகு","குருகு",3,"pul","iraicchi","heron"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",5,"maram","scene",""],["மா மலர்","மலர்",6,"maram","scene",""],["சுறா","சுறா",7,"pira","scene","shark"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","குருகு","confirmed","1/1","even the birds come home to roost at dusk, so the man too should stay and make this town his home, that is, marry"]],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:68":{"book":"natrinai","num":69,"t":["விளையாடு ஆயமொடு ஓரை ஆடாது","இளையோர் இல்லிடத்து இற்செறிந்திருத்தல்","அறனும் அன்றே ஆக்கமும் தேய்ம் என","குறு நுரை சுமந்து நறு மலர் உந்தி","பொங்கி வரு புது நீர் நெஞ்சு உண ஆடுகம்","வல்லிதின் வணங்கிச் சொல்லுநர்ப் பெறினே","செல்க என விடுநள்மன்கொல்லோ எல் உமிழ்ந்து","உரவு உரும் உரறும் அரை இருள் நடு நாள்","கொடி நுடங்கு இலங்கின மின்னி","ஆடு மழை இறுத்தன்று அவர் கோடு உயர் குன்றே"],"p":"","r":"With the man outside, the friend tells the girl: if we found someone to bow and say skilfully that for young girls to sit shut in the house, not playing orai with their companions, is neither right nor good for the household, and that they should bathe to their heart's content in the swelling new flood that carries small foam and pushes fragrant flowers, would mother let us go? Meanwhile at midnight, in half-dark, the mighty thunder roars spitting light, lightning flashes like waving banners, and the drifting cloud has settled on his high-peaked hill.","n":"The friend's speech is a request for an intercessor, and the man listening is the obvious one: the person who can 'bow and speak' to the mother is a suitor asking for her. The storm on his hill at midnight closes the night path as firmly as the mother closes the door.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["irserippu","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 68","lb":"NT68","poet":"பிரான் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத்தோழி தலைவிக்கு உரைப்பாளாய்ச் செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஓரை","ஓரை",1,"seythi","scene","girls' game"],["நறு மலர்","மலர்",4,"maram","scene",""],["புது நீர்","புது நீர்",5,"pira","scene","new flood"],["உரும்","உரும்",8,"pira","scene","thunder"],["மழை","மழை",10,"pira","scene","cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:69":{"book":"natrinai","num":70,"t":["பல் கதிர் மண்டிலம் பகல் செய்து ஆற்றி","சேய் உயர் பெரு வரைச் சென்று அவண் மறைய","பறவை பார்ப்புவயின் அடைய புறவில்","மா எருத்து இரலை மடப் பிணை தழுவ","முல்லை முகை வாய் திறப்ப பல் வயின்","தோன்றி தோன்றுபு புதல் விளக்கு உறாஅ","மதர்வை நல் ஆன் மாசு இல் தெண் மணி","கொடுங் கோல் கோவலர் குழலோடு ஒன்றி","ஐது வந்து இசைக்கும் அருள் இல் மாலை","ஆள்வினைக்கு அகன்றோர் சென்ற நாட்டும்","இனையவாகித் தோன்றின்","வினை வலித்து அமைதல் ஆற்றலர்மன்னே"],"p":"","r":"The wife who cannot bear his absence on work says: the many-rayed sun has done its day and set behind the far high hill, birds go home to their young, in the scrub the dark-necked stag embraces his gentle doe, jasmine buds open their mouths, thondri lights up the bushes, and the clear bells of the proud cows blend with the flutes of the cowherds with crooked staves in this pitiless evening; if evening came like this in the land where he went for his work, he could not hold to his resolve.","n":"She does not accuse him; she supposes that his land has no such evening, since if it had he would be home. The excuse is also a reproach, and it keeps her faith intact.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 69","lb":"NT69","poet":"சேகம்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினைவயிற் பிரிதல்ஆற்றாளாய தலைவி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","வினைவயிற் பிரிதல்ஆற்றாளாய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பறவை","பறவை",3,"pul","scene",""],["இரலை","இரலை",4,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",5,"maram","scene",""],["தோன்றி","தோன்றி",6,"maram","scene",""],["ஆன்","ஆன்",7,"maa","scene","cow"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",8,"seythi","scene",""],["குழலோடு","குழல்",8,"yaazh","scene","flute"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","இரலை","confirmed","2/2","even the stag is with his mate at nightfall, which the absent husband is not"]],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:70":{"book":"natrinai","num":71,"t":["சிறு வெள்ளாங்குருகே சிறு வெள்ளாங்குருகே","துறை போகு அறுவைத் தூ மடி அன்ன","நிறம் கிளர் தூவிச் சிறு வெள்ளாங்குருகே","எம் ஊர் வந்து எம் உண்துறைத் துழைஇ","சினைக் கெளிற்று ஆர்கையை அவர் ஊர்ப் பெயர்தி","அனைய அன்பினையோ பெரு மறவியையோ","ஆங்கண் தீம் புனல் ஈங்கண் பரக்கும்","கழனி நல் ஊர் மகிழ்நர்க்கு என்","இழை நெகிழ் பருவரல் செப்பாதோயே"],"p":"","r":"The woman calls to the little white heron, whose bright feathers are like the pure folds of cloth washed at the ford: you come to our town, search our drinking ford, eat the roe-filled keliru fish and go back to his town; is it your love or your great forgetfulness that you never tell the man of that good field town, whose sweet water spreads here, of my grief and my loosening bangles?","n":"The heron already travels the route a messenger would take and eats at her ford, so its silence is a kind of ingratitude; the line about water flowing from his town to hers says the towns are joined while the man is not.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thoothu","melivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 70","lb":"NT70","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"காமம் மிக்க கழிபடர்கிளவி வெள்ளி வீதியார்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu","கழிபடர்கிளவி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["குருகே","குருகு",1,"pul","scene","heron"],["கெளிற்று","கெளிறு",5,"pira","gift_food","catfish"],["புனல்","புனல்",7,"pira","scene","fresh water"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:71":{"book":"natrinai","num":72,"t":["மன்னாப் பொருட் பிணி முன்னி இன்னதை","வளை அணி முன்கை நின் இகுளைக்கு உணர்த்து எனப்","பல் மாண் இரத்திர்ஆயின் சென்ம் என","விடுநள் ஆதலும் உரியள் விடினே","கண்ணும் நுதலும் நீவி முன் நின்று","பிரிதல் வல்லிரோ ஐய செல்வர்","வகை அமர் நல் இல் அக இறை உறையும்","வண்ணப் புறவின் செங் காற் சேவல்","வீழ் துணைப் பயிரும் கையறு முரல் குரல்","நும் இலள் புலம்பக் கேட்டொறும்","பொம்மல் ஓதி பெரு விதுப்புறவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man bent on the sickness for wealth that does not last: if you beg her friend in many ways to tell this to the bangled girl, she may let you go, saying 'go'; but if she does, can you stand before her, stroke her eyes and forehead and leave, sir, while the red-legged male of the coloured dove living under the inner eaves of a rich house calls its beloved mate in a helpless moaning voice, and each time she hears it alone without you, the woman of thick hair will tremble?","n":"The friend concedes the woman's consent in advance, then removes its value: the leave-taking itself, the hand on her eyes, is what he cannot survive. மன்னாப் பொருள் calls wealth impermanent at the poem's first word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 71","lb":"NT71","poet":"வண்ணப்புறக் கந்தரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவனைத் தோழி செலவு அழுங்குவித்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","செலவு அழுங்குவித்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["சேவல்","சேவல்",8,"pul","iraicchi","male dove"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","சேவல்","confirmed","1/1","a bird that stays at home calls its mate, while the man means to leave his; she will hear in it the call he will not make"]],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:72":{"book":"natrinai","num":73,"t":["பேணுப பேணார் பெரியோர் என்பது","நாணு தக்கன்று அது காணுங்காலை","உயிர் ஓரன்ன செயிர் தீர் நட்பின்","நினக்கு யான் மறைத்தல் யாவது மிகப் பெரிது","அழிதக்கன்றால் தானே கொண்கன்","யான் யாய் அஞ்சுவல் எனினும் தான் எற்","பிரிதல் சூழான்மன்னே இனியே","கானல் ஆயம் அறியினும் ஆனாது","அலர் வந்தன்றுகொல் என்னும் அதனால்","புலர்வதுகொல் அவன் நட்பு எனா","அஞ்சுவல் தோழி என் நெஞ்சத்தானே"],"p":"","r":"With the man outside, the friend tells the girl: it is shameful that the great do not cherish what they should; in a flawless friendship like one life between us, hiding anything from you would be a great wrong; once, when I said I feared mother, the lord of the shore would never think of leaving; now, even though our shore companions may know, he keeps asking 'has gossip come?', so I fear in my heart, friend, that his friendship is drying up.","n":"The man's new question about gossip is read as a sign of cooling: caution once came from the girl's side and now comes from his. The friend speaks it within the women's own friendship (உயிர் ஓரன்ன) so the listener feels judged by two.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","natpu","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 72","lb":"NT72","poet":"இளம்போதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைவிக்கு உரைப்பாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைவிக்கு உரைப்பாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:73":{"book":"natrinai","num":74,"t":["வேனில் முருக்கின் விளை துணர் அன்ன","மாணா விரல வல் வாய்ப் பேஎய்","மல்லல் மூதூர் மலர்ப் பலி உணீஇய","மன்றம் போழும் புன்கண் மாலை","தம்மொடும் அஞ்சும் நம் இவண் ஒழியச்","செல்ப என்ப தாமே செவ் அரி","மயிர் நிரைத்தன்ன வார் கோல் வாங்கு கதிர்ச்","செந்நெல்அம் செறுவின் அன்னம் துஞ்சும்","பூக் கெழு படப்பைச் சாய்க்காட்டு அன்ன என்","நுதற் கவின் அழிக்கும் பசலையும்","அயலோர் தூற்றும் அம்பலும் அளித்தே"],"p":"","r":"The woman says that they say he will leave us here, we who are afraid even with him, in the cruel evening when the strong-mouthed ghoul with ungainly fingers like the ripe clusters of the summer murukku splits the public square to eat the flower offerings of the rich old town; pitiable then are the pallor that ruins my forehead, once lovely as Saykkadu with its flowered gardens where the swan sleeps in red-rice fields whose long bent ears are like rows of red hair, and the gossip the neighbours spread.","n":"The fear is set at home, not on his road: it is the town's evening, with its ghoul in the square, that she will face alone. Her past beauty is measured by a rich field town, so what the pallor destroys is a kind of wealth, the very thing he leaves to seek.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pasalai","alar","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 73","lb":"NT73","poet":"மூலங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவுக் குறிப்பு அறிந்து வேறுபட்ட தலைவி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"செலவுக் குறிப்பு அறிந்து வேறுபட்ட"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முருக்கின்","முருக்கு",1,"maram","simile","flame of the forest"],["பேஎய்","பேய்",2,"pira","scene","ghoul"],["செந்நெல்அம்","செந்நெல்",8,"maram","simile","red rice"],["அன்னம்","அன்னம்",8,"pul","simile","swan"]],"en":[["town","சாய்க்காட்டு",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:74":{"book":"natrinai","num":75,"t":["வடிக் கதிர் திரித்த வல் ஞாண்பெரு வலை","இடிக் குரற் புணரிப் பௌவத்து இடுமார்","நிறையப் பெய்த அம்பி காழோர்","சிறை அருங் களிற்றின் பரதவர் ஒய்யும்","சிறு வீ ஞாழற் பெருங் கடற் சேர்ப்பனை","ஏதிலாளனும் என்ப போது அவிழ்","புது மணற் கானல் புன்னை நுண் தாது","கொண்டல் அசை வளி தூக்குதொறும் குருகின்","வெண் புறம் மொசிய வார்க்கும் தெண் கடல்","கண்டல் வேலிய ஊர் அவன்","பெண்டு என அறிந்தன்று பெயர்த்தலோ அரிதே"],"p":"","r":"Refusing the bard who comes for the man, the woman says they call even the lord of the great shore of small-flowered nyazhal, where fishermen push the boat loaded with a great net of strong twisted cord into the thunder-voiced sea as elephant-keepers with goads drive an elephant hard to hold, a stranger now; yet the town knows me as the woman of him whose town has kandal hedges by the clear sea, where each gust of the east wind shakes the fine pollen of the punnai in the fresh-sand grove until it heaps on the white backs of the herons, and that is hard to undo.","n":"She refuses entry while admitting she cannot undo the bond: the town's knowledge makes her his whether he behaves or not. The punnai's pollen settling on the herons' backs is the image of a mark that the wind keeps adding and nothing washes off.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","alar","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 74","lb":"NT74","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவி பாணற்கு வாயில்மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu","பாணற்கு வாயில்மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["களிற்றின்","களிறு",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["ஞாழற்","ஞாழல்",5,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",7,"maram","ullurai",""],["குருகின்","குருகு",8,"pul","ullurai","heron"],["கண்டல்","கண்டல்",10,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","புன்னை குருகின்","confirmed","1/1","the woman whom the town now knows as the man's"]],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:75":{"book":"natrinai","num":76,"t":["நயன் இன்மையின் பயன் இது என்னாது","பூம் பொறிப் பொலிந்த அழல் உமிழ் அகன் பை","பாம்பு உயிர் அணங்கியாங்கும் ஈங்கு இது","தகாஅது வாழியோ குறுமகள் நகாஅது","உரைமதி உடையும் என் உள்ளம் சாரல்","கொடு விற் கானவன் கோட்டுமா தொலைச்சிப்","பச்சூன் பெய்த பகழி போல","சேயரி பரந்த மா இதழ் மழைக் கண்","உறாஅ நோக்கம் உற்ற என்","பைதல் நெஞ்சம் உய்யுமாறே"],"p":"","r":"Turned away, the man says to the girl, so that her friend hears: not thinking 'this is the fruit of unkindness', as one might torment the life of a snake with a fire-spitting broad hood of bright spots, this is not fitting here; long live, little one: speak without laughing, for my heart is breaking; tell me how my aching heart can live, struck by the glance that will not look of your large rain-cool eyes streaked with red, like the arrow wet with raw flesh that the hill hunter with curved bow shot to kill a tusked beast on the slope.","n":"உறாஅ நோக்கம், the look that does not touch, is the weapon: indifference wounds like a hunter's arrow. Asking her to speak 'without laughing' shows he fears her mockery more than her refusal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","kalaviyal","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 75","lb":"NT75","poet":"மாமூலனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சேட்படுக்கப்பட்டு ஆற்றானாகிய தலைமகன் தோழி கேட்பச்சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi","thozhi","kalavu","சேட்படுக்கப்பட்டு ஆற்றானாகிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",3,"pira","simile","cobra"],["கானவன்","கானவன்",6,"seythi","simile","hunter"],["கோட்டுமா","கோட்டுமா",6,"maa","simile","tusked beast"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:76":{"book":"natrinai","num":77,"t":["வருமழை கரந்த வால் நிற விசும்பின்","நுண் துளி மாறிய உலவை அம் காட்டு","ஆல நீழல் அசைவு நீக்கி","அஞ்சுவழி அஞ்சாது அசைவழி அசைஇ","வருந்தாது ஏகுமதி வால் இழைக் குறுமகள்","இம்மென் பேர் அலர் நும் ஊர்ப் புன்னை","வீ மலர் உதிர்ந்த தேன் நாறு புலவின்","கானல் வார் மணல் மரீஇ","கல் உறச் சிவந்த நின் மெல் அடி உயற்கே"],"p":"","r":"Eloping with her, the man tells the girl on the way: in this forest of dry trees under a white sky that has hidden the coming rain and stopped even fine drizzle, rest from weariness in the banyan's shade; unafraid where there is cause for fear, resting where it is right to rest, go on without strain, girl of bright jewels, so that your soft feet, used to the long sand of the shore grove of your town, where the great gossip hums and fallen punnai flowers smell of honey and fish, and now reddened against the stones, may recover.","n":"Her old town is remembered for its gossip and its soft sand in one breath: what she has left is both what hurt her and what was gentle to her feet. He sets the pace by her feet, not by the road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["udanpokku","anbu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 76","lb":"NT76","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"புணர்ந்து உடன்போகாநின்ற தலைவன் இடைச் சுரத்துத் தலைவிக்கு உரைத்தது அம்மூவனார்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","உடன்போகாநின்ற தலைவன் இடைச் சுரத்துத் தலைவிக்கு உரைத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆல","ஆல்",3,"maram","scene","banyan"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",6,"maram","scene",""],["கானல்","கானல்",8,"pira","scene","shore grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:77":{"book":"natrinai","num":78,"t":["மலையன் மா ஊர்ந்து போகி புலையன்","பெருந் துடி கறங்கப் பிற புலம் புக்கு அவர்","அருங் குறும்பு எருக்கி அயா உயிர்த்தாஅங்கு","உய்த்தன்றுமன்னே நெஞ்சே செவ் வேர்ச்","சினைதொறும் தூங்கும் பயம் கெழு பலவின்","சுளையுடை முன்றில் மனையோள் கங்குல்","ஒலி வெள் அருவி ஒலியின் துஞ்சும்","ஊறலஞ் சேரிச் சீறூர் வல்லோன்","வாள் அரம் பொருத கோள் நேர் எல் வளை","அகன் தொடி செறித்த முன்கை ஒள் நுதல்","திதலை அல்குல் குறுமகள்","குவளை உண்கண் மகிழ் மட நோக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The man who keeps following her tells his heart: as Malaiyan rode his horse into enemy land with the Pulaiyan's great tudi drum sounding, broke their hard strongholds and then breathed out his weariness, so once, heart, the glad innocent look of her kuvalai-dark kohl eyes brought me relief: the bright-browed girl with spotted hips, whose forearm is filled with broad bangles that the skilled smith of the small town of Ooral filed with a saw, where in the yard full of jackfruit hanging on every branch, the housewife sleeps at night to the roar of the white waterfall.","n":"மன்னே marks the relief as past: the poem is spoken now that it is gone. The king's sigh comes after the raid, not during it, so the comparison is to exhausted ease after victory, not to conquest; the woman of the yard who sleeps by the waterfall's noise is the calm he no longer has.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","nenju","ninaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 77","lb":"NT77","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பின்னின்ற தலைவன் நெஞ்சிற்கு உரைத்தது கபிலர்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","பின்னின்ற தலைவன் நெஞ்சிற்கு உரைத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மா","மா",1,"maa","simile","horse"],["புலையன்","புலையன்",1,"seythi","simile",""],["துடி","துடி",2,"parai","simile",""],["பலவின்","பலா",5,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["அருவி","அருவி",7,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["வல்லோன்","வல்லோன்",8,"seythi","scene","bangle smith"],["குவளை","குவளை",12,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["king","மலையன்",1]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:78":{"book":"natrinai","num":79,"t":["கோட் சுறா வழங்கும் வாள் கேழ்இருங் கழி","மணி ஏர் நெய்தல் மா மலர் நிறைய","பொன் நேர் நுண் தாது புன்னை தூஉம்","வீழ் தாழ் தாழைப் பூக் கமழ் கானல்","படர் வந்து நலியும் சுடர் செல் மாலை","நோய் மலி பருவரல் நாம் இவண் உய்கம்","கேட்டிசின் வாழி தோழி தெண் கழி","வள் வாய் ஆழி உள் வாய் தோயினும்","புள்ளு நிமிர்ந்தன்ன பொலம் படைக் கலி மா","வலவன் கோல் உற அறியா","உரவு நீர்ச் சேர்ப்பன் தேர்மணிக் குரலே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl: in the shore grove fragrant with the hanging-rooted thazhai, where in the dark backwater gleaming like a sword and roamed by killing sharks the punnai sprinkles fine gold-like pollen to fill the great gem-blue neythal flowers, we shall now escape the grief that torments us at the evening hour when the sun goes; listen, friend: the bell of the chariot of the lord of the mighty sea, whose horses with golden trappings leap like rising birds so that the charioteer never has to touch them with the whip, even when the sharp wheel-rims sink into the clear backwater.","n":"The chariot is heard, not seen: the bell's sound alone is news of marriage. The horses that need no whip carry the point that he now comes of his own eagerness, not pressed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivu","maalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 78","lb":"NT78","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரைவு மலிந்தது கீரங்கீரனார்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு மலிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["சுறா","சுறா",1,"pira","scene","shark"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","scene","blue water-lily"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",3,"maram","scene",""],["தாழைப்","தாழை",4,"maram","scene",""],["புள்ளு","புள்",9,"pul","simile",""],["மா","மா",9,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:79":{"book":"natrinai","num":80,"t":["சிறை நாள் ஈங்கை உறை நனி திரள்வீ","கூரை நல் மனைக் குறுந் தொடி மகளிர்","மணல் ஆடு கழங்கின் அறை மிசைத் தாஅம்","ஏர் தரலுற்ற இயக்கு அருங் கவலைப்","பிரிந்தோர் வந்து நப்புணரப் புணர்ந்தோர்","பிரிதல் சூழ்தலின் அரியதும் உண்டோ","என்று நாம் கூறிக் காமம் செப்புதும்","செப்பாது விடினே உயிரொடும் வந்தன்று","அம்ம வாழி தோழி","யாதனின் தவிர்க்குவம் காதலர் செலவே"],"p":"","r":"Sensing he will leave, the woman tells her friend: shall we tell him our love, saying 'is anything harder than this, that those who parted come back and join us and then, having joined, plan to part again, by the forked path hard to cross where clustered eengai flowers fall on the rock like the molucca seeds that bangled girls of good houses play with on sand?' If we don't tell him, it has come for life itself; so, friend, by what can we stop the lover's going?","n":"The argument she rehearses is a small piece of logic about cycles: union after parting should make parting harder, not easier. The last line asks for a means, not a reason: she already knows words may not be enough.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 79","lb":"NT79","poet":"கண்ணகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவு உணர்ந்து வேறுபட்ட தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவு உணர்ந்து வேறுபட்ட"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஈங்கை","ஈங்கை",1,"maram","scene",""],["கழங்கின்","கழங்கு",3,"pira","scene","molucca seeds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:80":{"book":"natrinai","num":81,"t":["மன்ற எருமை மலர் தலைக் காரான்","இன் தீம் பாற்பயம் கொண்மார் கன்று விட்டு","ஊர்க் குறுமாக்கள் மேற்கொண்டு கழியும்","பெரும் புலர் விடியலின் விரும்பிப் போத்தந்து","தழையும் தாரும் தந்தனன் இவன் என","இழை அணி ஆயமொடு தகு நாண் தடைஇ","தைஇத் திங்கள் தண் கயம் படியும்","பெருந் தோட் குறுமகள் அல்லது","மருந்து பிறிது இல்லை யான் உற்ற நோய்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"Kept away, the man tells his heart so the friend can hear: in the dawn when the town's small boys, wanting the sweet milk of the common's dark buffalo with flowering head, loose its calf and ride it off, she came eager, and with her jewelled companions, restrained by fitting shyness and saying 'this one brought leaf skirts and garlands', she bathes in the cool pond in the month of Thai; there is no medicine but that broad-shouldered girl for the sickness I have.","n":"His gifts (தழையும் தாரும்) are named in her voice, so he knows she has noticed him; the friend's refusal is answered by her own acknowledgement. The Thai bath is a maidens' rite, which makes her not yet anyone's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","marundhu","naanam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 80","lb":"NT80","poet":"பூதன்தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"சேட்படுக்கப்பட்டு ஆற்றானாய தலைவன் தோழி கேட்ப தன் நெஞ்சிற்கு உரைத்தது","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju","thozhi","kalavu","சேட்படுக்கப்பட்டு ஆற்றானாய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["கன்று","கன்று",2,"maa","scene",""],["தழையும்","தழை",5,"maram","gift_food","leaf skirt"],["தாரும்","தார்",5,"maram","gift_food","garland"],["கயம்","கயம்",7,"pira","scene","pond"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:4"},"nt:81":{"book":"natrinai","num":82,"t":["இரு நிலம் குறையக் கொட்டிப்பரிந்தின்று","ஆதி போகிய அசைவு இல் நோன் தாள்","மன்னர் மதிக்கும் மாண் வினைப் புரவி","கொய்ம் மயிர் எருத்தில் பெய்ம் மணி ஆர்ப்ப","பூண்கதில் பாக நின் தேரே பூண் தாழ்","ஆக வன முலைக் கரைவலம் தெறிப்ப","அழுதனள் உறையும் அம் மா அரிவை","விருந்து அயர் விருப்பொடு வருந்தினள் அசைஇய","முறுவல் இன் நகை காண்கம்","உறு பகை தணித்தனன் உரவு வாள் வேந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Back from the king's war, the man tells his charioteer to yoke the chariot whose swift horses, prized by kings, have bells ringing on their clipped-mane necks: the strong-sworded king has quelled the enemy, and they will see the sweet smile of the lovely young wife who has stayed weeping, tears dropping on her breasts, worn out by her longing to welcome guests.","n":"Her grief is named as a thwarted wish to host guests (விருந்து அயர் விருப்பு), a duty a wife cannot keep alone, so his return restores the household's work as well as the marriage. The king's victory is held back to the last line, as the thing that releases him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","pirivu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 81","lb":"NT81","poet":"அகம்பன்மாலாதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றிய தலைவன்தேர்ப்பாகற்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றிய தலைவன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["புரவி","புரவி",3,"maa","scene","horse"],["மணி","மணி",4,"pira","ornament","bells"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:82":{"book":"natrinai","num":83,"t":["நோயும் நெகிழ்ச்சியும் வீடச்சிறந்த","வேய் வனப்புற்ற தோளை நீயே","என் உயவு அறிதியோ நல் நடைக் கொடிச்சி","முருகு புணர்ந்து இயன்ற வள்ளி போல நின்","உருவு கண் எறிப்ப நோக்கல் ஆற்றலெனே","போகிய நாகப் போக்கு அருங் கவலை","சிறு கட் பன்றிப் பெருஞ் சின ஒருத்தல்","சேறு ஆடு இரும் புறம் நீறொடு சிவண","வெள் வசிப் படீஇயர் மொய்த்த வள்பு அழீஇ","கோள் நாய் கொண்ட கொள்ளைக்","கானவர் பெயர்க்கும் சிறுகுடியானே"],"p":"","r":"The man asks the hill girl of graceful gait, whose bamboo-lovely shoulders could end his sickness and weakness, whether she knows his anguish: he cannot bear to look at her form, which strikes the eyes like Valli joined with Murugan, she of the small village where hunters carry home the spoil their dogs took when they broke the leash and swarmed an angry boar, its dark mud-bathed back mingled with dust, at the forked path where snakes go.","n":"Line 1 credits her shoulders with ending the very sickness she causes. The closing scene of dogs swarming a proud boar while the hunters take the spoil can be read as the man brought down by her, but no second correspondence is anchored in the poem, so the boar stays scene (a near miss for ullurai). Line 7 recurs as Natrinai 386 line 1.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","azhagu","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 82","lb":"NT82","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழியிற்புணர்ச்சிக்கண் தன்னிலைக் கொளீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழியிற்புணர்ச்சிக்கண்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["வேய்","வேய்",2,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",3,"seythi","scene","hill girl"],["முருகு","முருகு",4,"deyvam","simile","Murugan"],["வள்ளி","வள்ளி",4,"deyvam","simile","Valli"],["நாகப்","நாகம்",6,"pira","scene","snake"],["பன்றிப்","பன்றி",7,"maa","scene","boar"],["நாய்","நாய்",10,"maa","scene","hunting dog"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",11,"seythi","scene","hunters"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:83":{"book":"natrinai","num":84,"t":["எம் ஊர் வாயில் உண்துறைத் தடைஇய","கடவுள் முது மரத்து உடன் உறை பழகிய","தேயா வளை வாய் தெண் கண் கூர் உகிர்","வாய்ப் பறை அசாஅம் வலி முந்து கூகை","மை ஊன் தெரிந்த நெய் வெண் புழுக்கல்","எலி வான் சூட்டொடு மலியப் பேணுதும்","எஞ்சாக் கொள்கை எம் காதலர் வரல் நசைஇத்","துஞ்சாது அலமரு பொழுதின்","அஞ்சு வரக் கடுங் குரல் பயிற்றாதீமே"],"p":"","r":"The friend promises the owl that has long lived in the old god-haunted tree at the drinking ghat by their town's gate, sharp of claw and curved of beak, a rich feast of white rice cooked in ghee with dark meat and roasted white rat, if only it will not keep sounding its harsh, fearful cry in the hour when they lie awake and restless, longing for the coming of their lover of unfailing resolve.","n":"The plea to the owl is meant for the man waiting outside: it tells him they are awake and waiting, and that the meeting depends on the house not being roused. Rice, ghee and meat are the stuff of offerings at a god's tree, so the friend is bribing an omen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 83","lb":"NT83","poet":"பெருந்தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி வந்த தலைவன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாக தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறி வந்த தலைவன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",2,"deyvam","scene","god of the tree"],["முது மரத்து","முது மரம்",2,"maram","scene","old tree"],["கூகை","கூகை",4,"pul","scene","owl"],["ஊன்","ஊன்",5,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["நெய்","நெய்",5,"unavu","gift_food","ghee"],["புழுக்கல்","புழுக்கல்",5,"unavu","gift_food","boiled rice"],["எலி","எலி",6,"maa","gift_food","rat"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:84":{"book":"natrinai","num":85,"t":["கண்ணும் தோளும் தண் நறுங்கதுப்பும்","திதலை அல்குலும் பல பாராட்டி","நெருநலும் இவணர் மன்னே இன்றே","பெரு நீர் ஒப்பின் பேஎய் வெண் தேர்","மரன் இல் நீள் இடை மான் நசையுறூஉம்","சுடுமண் தசும்பின் மத்தம் தின்ற","பிறவா வெண்ணெய் உருப்பு இடந்தன்ன","உவர் எழு களரி ஓமை அம் காட்டு","வெயில் வீற்றிருந்த வெம்பு அலை அருஞ் சுரம்","ஏகுவர் என்ப தாமே தம்வயின்","இரந்தோர் மாற்றல் ஆற்றா","இல்லின் வாழ்க்கை வல்லாதோரே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that only yesterday he was here, praising her eyes, her shoulders, her cool fragrant hair and her speckled waist, and today they say he will go by the hard road where the sun sits enthroned, through omai forest on salt waste cracked like unformed butter split by heat, where the white ghost-chariot of the mirage, like a great water, lures the deer across the long treeless stretch: he is one who cannot live a home life that fails to relieve those who come to beg.","n":"The last lines give his reason without blame: a household that cannot relieve suppliants is no life for him, so the ideal of giving is what takes him from her. ஏகுவர் என்ப (they say he will go) shows she has the news from others, not from him, and the colophon's 'in separation' anticipates a parting not yet made.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","eegai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 84","lb":"NT84","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாய தலைவி தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மான்","மான்",5,"maa","scene","deer"],["மத்தம்","மத்தம்",6,"unavu","simile","churned curd"],["வெண்ணெய்","வெண்ணெய்",7,"unavu","simile","butter"],["ஓமை","ஓமை",8,"maram","scene","omai tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:85":{"book":"natrinai","num":86,"t":["ஆய் மலர் மழைக் கண் தெண் பனி உறைப்பவும்","வேய் மருள் பணைத் தோள் விறல் இழை நெகிழவும்","அம்பல் மூதூர் அரவம் ஆயினும்","குறு வரி இரும் புலி அஞ்சிக் குறு நடைக்","கன்றுடை வேழம் நின்று காத்து அல்கும்","ஆர் இருள் கடுகிய அஞ்சு வரு சிறு நெறி","வாரற்கதில்ல தோழி சாரல்","கானவன் எய்த முளவு மான் கொழுங் குறை","தேம் கமழ் கதுப்பின் கொடிச்சி கிழங்கொடு","காந்தள்அம் சிறுகுடிப் பகுக்கும்","ஓங்கு மலை நாடன் நின் நசையினானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that though her fine flower eyes drip tears, her bangles slip from her bamboo-like shoulders and the gossiping old town is loud, she wishes the lord of the high mountain would not come for love of her by the narrow, fearful path black with night, where an elephant, afraid of the small-striped tiger, stands guarding its short-stepping calf: his is the land where the hill girl with honey-scented hair shares out, with tubers, in their kaanthal hamlet, the fat cut of the porcupine her hunter shot.","n":"வாரற்கதில்ல (may he not come) is a wish against the woman's own longing, made from fear for him. The closing picture of the hunter's kill shared out in the hamlet sets the safe daylight order of a married hill household against the night path.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 85","lb":"NT85","poet":"நல்விளக்கனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவன் வரவு உணர்ந்த தோழி தலைவிக்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தலைவன் வரவு உணர்ந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["மலர்","மலர்",1,"maram","simile","flower"],["வேய்","வேய்",2,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["புலி","புலி",4,"maa","scene","tiger"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",5,"maa","iraicchi","elephant"],["கானவன்","கானவன்",8,"seythi","scene","hunter"],["முளவு மான்","முளவு மான்",8,"maa","gift_food","porcupine"],["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",9,"seythi","scene","hill girl"],["கிழங்கொடு","கிழங்கு",9,"unavu","gift_food","tubers"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",10,"maram","scene","kaanthal"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வேழம்","not_confirmed","0/1","as the elephant keeps its young from the path's danger, the women's love for the man makes them wish him kept from that path at night, so love here means holding back, not coming"]],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:86":{"book":"natrinai","num":87,"t":["அறவர் வாழி தோழி மறவர்","வேல் என விரிந்த கதுப்பின் தோல","பாண்டில் ஒப்பின் பகன்றை மலரும்","கடும் பனி அற்சிரம் நடுங்க காண்தகக்","கை வல் வினைவன் தையுபு சொரிந்த","சுரிதக உருவின ஆகிப் பெரிய","கோங்கம் குவி முகை அவிழ ஈங்கை","நல் தளிர் நயவர நுடங்கும்","முற்றா வேனில் முன்னி வந்தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend blesses the men as righteous: through the harsh dew of the late cold season, when the pakandrai blooms like leather cups and the great closed buds of the koongam open curled like a skilled craftsman's stitched work, they came back ahead of the early summer, when the eengai's good shoots sway, before the time they had named.","n":"அறவர் (they are righteous) is praise for a kept promise, not for piety: keeping the season they named is what makes the man good. The flowers of the late dew and the not-yet-ripe summer fix that he beat his own deadline.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","vaaimai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 86","lb":"NT86","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"குறித்த பருவத்தின்வினைமுடித்து வந்தமை கேட்ட தோழி தலைவிக்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","வினைமுடித்து வந்தமை"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மறவர்","மறவர்",1,"seythi","simile","warriors"],["பகன்றை","பகன்றை",3,"maram","scene","pakandrai"],["வினைவன்","வினைவன்",5,"seythi","simile","craftsman"],["கோங்கம்","கோங்கம்",7,"maram","scene","koongam"],["ஈங்கை","ஈங்கை",7,"maram","scene","eengai"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:87":{"book":"natrinai","num":88,"t":["உள் ஊர் மா அத்த முள் எயிற்று வாவல்","ஓங்கல்அம் சினைத் தூங்கு துயில் பொழுதின்","வெல் போர்ச் சோழர் அழிசிஅம் பெருங் காட்டு","நெல்லி அம் புளிச் சுவைக் கனவியாஅங்கு","அது கழிந்தன்றே தோழி அவர் நாட்டுப்","பனி அரும்பு உடைந்த பெருந் தாட் புன்னை","துறை மேய் இப்பி ஈர்ம் புறத்து உறைக்கும்","சிறுகுடிப் பரதவர் மகிழ்ச்சியும்","பெருந் தண் கானலும் நினைந்த அப் பகலே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the day she spent thinking of the joys of the fisherfolk of his small village, where the dew-broken buds of the great punnai drip on the wet backs of oysters grazing at the shore, and of his great cool grove, has passed like the dream that a sharp-toothed bat, hanging asleep on a high mango branch in the town, dreams of the sour taste of gooseberries in Azhisi's great forest in the land of the war-winning Cholas.","n":"The bat on the town's mango dreams of fruit in a far forest it is not in, as she dwells on a shore she has not reached: the simile makes her day of longing as unreal and as hungry as the bat's dream. The colophon says she saw a dream; the poem gives the dream to the bat and calls her own state remembering (நினைந்த).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kanavu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 87","lb":"NT87","poet":"நக்கண்ணையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரைவிடை வைத்துப்பிரிய ஆற்றாளாய தலைவி கனாக் கண்டு தோழிக்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வரைவிடை வைத்துப்பிரிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மா அத்த","மா",1,"maram","scene","mango"],["வாவல்","வாவல்",1,"maa","simile","bat"],["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",4,"maram","simile","gooseberry"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",6,"maram","scene","punnai"],["இப்பி","இப்பி",7,"pira","scene","oyster"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",8,"seythi","scene","fisherfolk"]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",3],["king","அழிசி",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:88":{"book":"natrinai","num":89,"t":["யாம் செய் தொல் வினைக்கு எவன்பேதுற்றனை","வருந்தல் வாழி தோழி யாம் சென்று","உரைத்தனம் வருகம் எழுமதி புணர்திரைக்","கடல் விளை அமுதம் பெயற்கு ஏற்றாஅங்கு","உருகி உகுதல் அஞ்சுவல் உதுக்காண்","தம்மோன் கொடுமை நம் வயின் எற்றி","நயம் பெரிது உடைமையின் தாங்கல் செல்லாது","கண்ணீர் அருவியாக","அழுமே தோழி அவர் பழம் முதிர் குன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman not to be stricken as though by old deeds of their own, not to grieve, but to rise so they can go, speak to him and come back; she fears the woman will melt away like the sea-grown salt that has taken the rain, and, look, his hill heavy with ripe fruit, taking his cruelty to them to heart and too kind to bear it, will weep with waterfalls for tears.","n":"The friend refuses fate (யாம் செய் தொல் வினை) as an explanation and proposes to act, and says it for the ears of the man outside: his own hill is shown weeping at his cruelty, so the land shames its lord. The salt of the shore, called the sea's nectar, is a neythal thing used in a hill poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 88","lb":"NT88","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத்தோழி தலைவிக்கு உரைப்பாளாய்ச்சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகத்தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அமுதம்","அமுதம்",4,"pira","simile","salt"],["அருவியாக","அருவி",8,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["பழம்","பழம்",9,"unavu","scene","fruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:89":{"book":"natrinai","num":90,"t":["கொண்டல் ஆற்றி விண்தலைச்செறீஇயர்","திரைப் பிதிர் கடுப்ப முகடு உகந்து ஏறி","நிரைத்து நிறை கொண்ட கமஞ் சூல் மா மழை","அழி துளி கழிப்பிய வழி பெயற் கடை நாள்","இரும் பனிப் பருவத்த மயிர்க் காய் உழுந்தின்","அகல் இலை அகல வீசி அகலாது","அல்கலும் அலைக்கும் நல்கா வாடை","பரும யானை அயா உயிர்த்தாஅங்கு","இன்னும் வருமே தோழி வாரா","வன்கணாளரோடு இயைந்த","புன்கண் மாலையும் புலம்பும் முந்துறுத்தே"],"p":"","r":"As the great full-bellied clouds that rose in ranks like the spray of waves have spent their last torrents and the season of heavy dew comes, the ungracious north wind, which every night tosses wide the broad leaves of the hairy-podded black gram, sighing like a caparisoned elephant, will come again, friend, with the sorrowful evening and loneliness before it, joined with the hard-hearted ones who do not come.","n":"The wind and the evening are made allies of the absent men (வன்கணாளரோடு இயைந்த), so the season does his cruelty for him. The colophon gives the poem to the friend, who has heard he is coming back with wealth, but the address தோழி and 'the ones who do not come' read more naturally as the woman's own complaint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 89","lb":"NT89","poet":"இளம் புல்லூர்க் காவிதி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பொருள் முற்றி மறுத்தந்தான் எனக் கேட்ட தோழி தலைவிக்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பொருள் முற்றி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கொண்டல்","கொண்டல்",1,"pira","scene","east wind"],["மழை","மழை",3,"pira","scene","cloud"],["உழுந்தின்","உழுந்து",5,"maram","scene","black gram"],["வாடை","வாடை",7,"pira","scene","north wind"],["யானை","யானை",8,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:90":{"book":"natrinai","num":91,"t":["ஆடு இயல் விழவின் அழுங்கல் மூதூர்","உடையோர் பன்மையின் பெருங் கை தூவா","வறன் இல் புலைத்தி எல்லித் தோய்த்த","புகாப் புகர் கொண்ட புன் பூங் கலிங்கமொடு","வாடா மாலை துயல்வர ஓடி","பெருங் கயிறு நாலும் இரும் பனம் பிணையல்","பூங் கண் ஆயம் ஊக்க ஊங்காள்","அழுதனள் பெயரும் அம் சில் ஓதி","நல்கூர் பெண்டின் சில் வளைக் குறுமகள்","ஊசல் உறு தொழில் பூசல் கூட்டா","நயன் இல் மாக்களொடு கெழீஇ","பயன் இன்று அம்ம இவ் வேந்துடை அவையே"],"p":"","r":"In the old town loud with its dancing festival, the young daughter of a poor woman, with few bangles and lovely sparse hair, dressed in a flowered cloth that the tireless washerwoman, who never rests her hands for her many clients, had washed by night, ran up with an unfading garland swinging, but would not ride the great palmyra-rope swing though her bright-eyed companions urged her, and went away weeping; this king's assembly, keeping company with graceless men who would not end the quarrel over the swing, is of no use.","n":"The poem says only that a poor girl lost her turn at the festival swing and that the court did nothing; said within the panan's hearing, it seems to set the wife, pushed aside in her own town while those who should judge look on, beside the girl. As ullurai it fails condition 1: the vehicle is a person and a court, not a native thing, so the hidden comparison is left to this note. The night-washed cloth and the unfading garland may mark her finery as borrowed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inmai","avai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 90","lb":"NT90","poet":"அஞ்சில் அஞ்சியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகளுக்கு உரைப்பாளாய் பாணனை நெருங்கி வாயில்மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","paanan","karpu","வாயில்மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["புலைத்தி","புலைத்தி",3,"seythi","scene","washerwoman"],["கலிங்கமொடு","கலிங்கம்",4,"pira","ornament","cloth"],["மாலை","மாலை",5,"maram","ornament","garland"],["பனம்","பனை",6,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["வளை","வளை",9,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["ஊசல்","ஊசல்",10,"pira","scene","swing"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:91":{"book":"natrinai","num":92,"t":["நீ உணர்ந்தனையே தோழி வீ உகப்","புன்னை பூத்த இன் நிழல் உயர் கரைப்","பாடு இமிழ் பனிக் கடல் துழைஇ பெடையோடு","உடங்கு இரை தேரும் தடந் தாள் நாரை","ஐய சிறு கண் செங் கடைச் சிறு மீன்","மேக்கு உயர் சினையின் மீமிசைக் குடம்பை","தாய்ப் பயிர் பிள்ளை வாய்ப் படச் சொரியும்","கானல் அம் படப்பை ஆனா வண் மகிழ்ப்","பெரு நல் ஈகை நம் சிறு குடிப் பொலிய","புள் உயிர்க் கொட்பின் வள் உயிர் மணித் தார்க்","கடு மாப் பூண்ட நெடுந் தேர்","நெடு நீர்ச் சேர்ப்பன் பகல் இவண் வரவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman she must have sensed it: so that their small village may shine with a great, unstinting joy of giving, the long-shored lord's tall chariot, yoked to swift horses whose bells cry out like whirling birds, comes here by day, to the shore garden where the large-legged heron, searching the roaring cold sea with its mate under the high bank of flower-shedding punnai, drops red-edged little fish into the mouths of its calling young in the nest atop the branch.","n":"Coming by day (பகல் இவண் வரவே) means coming openly, to ask for her in marriage; the giving that will make the village shine is the wedding's bounty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","eegai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 91","lb":"NT91","poet":"பிசிராந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகட்கு வரைவு மலிந்து உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு மலிந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",2,"maram","scene","punnai"],["நாரை","நாரை",4,"pul","iraicchi","heron"],["மீன்","மீன்",5,"pira","gift_food","fish"],["மணித் தார்","மணித் தார்",10,"pira","ornament","bell harness"],["கடு மாப்","மா",11,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","நாரை","confirmed","1/1","the man who now comes openly will keep a house as the heron does, seeking with his wife and feeding their own"]],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:92":{"book":"natrinai","num":93,"t":["உள்ளார்கொல்லோ தோழி துணையொடு","வேனில் ஓதி பாடு நடை வழலை","வரி மரல் நுகும்பின் வாடி அவண","வறன் பொருந்து குன்றத்து உச்சி கவாஅன்","வேட்டச் சீறூர் அகன் கண் கேணிப்","பய நிரைக்கு எடுத்த மணி நீர்ப் பத்தர்","புன் தலை மடப் பிடி கன்றோடு ஆர","வில் கடிந்து ஊட்டின பெயரும்","கொல் களிற்று ஒருத்தல் சுரன் இறந்தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether they will not think of us, the men who crossed the waste where a summer chameleon with its mate and a slow-gaited snake shrivel among the striped maral, and where, on the summit of a drought-held hill by the wide well of a hunters' hamlet, a killing tusker, warding off the bowmen, lets its young she-elephant and her calf drink from the gem-clear trough drawn for the cattle, and then goes its way.","n":"The question expects yes: what the man saw on the road, a male winning water for its mate and calf and then leaving, is what he himself is doing, so the friend makes the road remind him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 92","lb":"NT92","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட கிழத்திக்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை வேறுபட்ட"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஓதி","ஓதி",2,"pira","scene","chameleon"],["வழலை","வழலை",2,"pira","scene","snake"],["மரல்","மரல்",3,"maram","scene","maral"],["நிரைக்கு","நிரை",6,"maa","scene","cattle"],["பிடி","பிடி",7,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["களிற்று ஒருத்தல்","களிறு",9,"maa","iraicchi","tusker"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","களிற்று ஒருத்தல்","confirmed","1/1","the man who crossed that land, seeing a male provide for its own and move on as he did, will think of the wife he left"]],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:93":{"book":"natrinai","num":94,"t":["பிரசம் தூங்க பெரும் பழம் துணர","வரை வெள் அருவி மாலையின் இழிதர","கூலம் எல்லாம் புலம்புஉக நாளும்","மல்லற்று அம்ம இம் மலை கெழு வெற்பு எனப்","பிரிந்தோர் இரங்கும் பெருங் கல் நாட","செல்கம் எழுமோ சிறக்க நின் ஊழி","மருங்கு மறைத்த திருந்து இழைப் பணைத் தோள்","நல்கூர் நுசுப்பின் மெல் இயல் குறுமகள்","பூண் தாழ் ஆகம் நாண் அட வருந்திய","பழங்கண் மாமையும் உடைய தழங்கு குரல்","மயிர்க் கண் முரசினோரும் முன்","உயிர்க் குறியெதிர்ப்பை பெறல் அருங்குரைத்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the great rock land, where those who leave lament that this hill-crowned mountain, its honeycombs hanging, its great fruits clustered, its white falls coming down like garlands and all its grains spilling, is rich every day, to rise and come, long life to him: the soft young girl, slender-waisted, with jewels on bamboo shoulders that hide her sides, has the old pallor of one worn by shame, and her life, given like a pledge, is hard to win even for kings whose hairy-eyed drums thunder.","n":"குறியெதிர்ப்பை is a loan repaid in kind: her life is given to him as such a loan, one even kings could not obtain, so his delay wastes what kings could not buy. The opening praise of a land so rich that leavers weep hints that he too should not leave her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","naanam","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 93","lb":"NT93","poet":"மலையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பிரசம்","பிரசம்",1,"pira","scene","honeycomb"],["பழம்","பழம்",1,"unavu","scene","fruit"],["அருவி","அருவி",2,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["கூலம்","கூலம்",3,"unavu","scene","grain"],["முரசினோரும்","முரசு",11,"parai","scene","drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:94":{"book":"natrinai","num":95,"t":["நோய் அலைக் கலங்கிய மதன் அழி பொழுதில்","காமம் செப்பல் ஆண்மகற்கு அமையும்","யானே பெண்மை தட்ப நுண்ணிதின் தாங்கி","கை வல் கம்மியன் கவின் பெறக் கழாஅ","மண்ணாப் பசு முத்து ஏய்ப்ப குவி இணர்ப்","புன்னை அரும்பிய புலவு நீர்ச் சேர்ப்பன்","என்ன மகன்கொல் தோழி தன்வயின்","ஆர்வம் உடையர் ஆகி","மார்பு அணங்குறுநரை அறியாதோனே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that when love-sickness tosses one and desire breaks composure, speaking out one's longing suits a man, while she must hold it in finely as womanhood restrains her; what kind of man is the lord of the fish-smelling shore, where punnai buds cluster like fresh pearls a skilled craftsman has not yet washed to beauty, that he does not know those who long for him and are tormented by his chest?","n":"Said for the man outside to hear: she states the rule that only a man may speak his love and turns it into a reproach, since he, who may speak, does not. The pearls still unwashed, lovely but not yet brought out, are the shore's own figure for a love kept unspoken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kaamam","madal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 94","lb":"NT94","poet":"இளந்திரையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகன்சிறைப்புறமாக தலைவி தோழிக்கு உரைப்பாளாய்ச்சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன்சிறைப்புறமாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["கம்மியன்","கம்மியன்",4,"seythi","simile","craftsman"],["முத்து","முத்து",5,"pira","simile","pearl"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",6,"maram","simile","punnai"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:95":{"book":"natrinai","num":96,"t":["கழை பாடு இரங்க பல் இயம் கறங்க","ஆடு மகள் நடந்த கொடும் புரி நோன் கயிற்று","அதவத் தீம் கனி அன்ன செம் முகத்","துய்த் தலை மந்தி வன் பறழ் தூங்க","கழைக் கண் இரும் பொறை ஏறி விசைத்து எழுந்து","குறக் குறுமாக்கள் தாளம் கொட்டும் அக்","குன்றகத்ததுவே குழு மிளைச் சீறூர்","சீறூரோளே நாறு மயிர்க் கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி கையகத்ததுவே பிறர்","விடுத்தற்கு ஆகாது பிணித்த என் நெஞ்சே"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his companion where his heart is: on the hill where, while the bamboo pole hums and many instruments sound, the strong cub of a fluffy-headed monkey, red-faced like a sweet fig, swings on the strong twisted rope a dancing girl walked, then climbs the great rock by the bamboo and leaps up while the hill children clap time, there is a small hamlet ringed with thickets; in that hamlet is the hill girl of fragrant hair; and in her hand is his heart, bound so that no one else can free it.","n":"The poem narrows like a chain, each phrase's last word starting the next (சீறூர், சீறூரோளே; கொடிச்சி, கொடிச்சி), from hill to hamlet to girl to her hand. The monkey cub playing on the dancer's rope while children clap is the place's ease, offered to the companion as directions.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kalaviyal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 95","lb":"NT95","poet":"கோட்டம்பலவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் பாங்கற்கு இவ்விடத்து இத்தன்மைத்து என உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu","இவ்விடத்து இத்தன்மைத்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கழை","கழை",1,"maram","scene","bamboo pole"],["இயம்","இயம்",1,"parai","scene","instruments"],["ஆடு மகள்","ஆடு மகள்",2,"seythi","scene","rope dancer"],["அதவத்","அதவம்",3,"maram","simile","fig"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",4,"maa","scene","she-monkey"],["பறழ்","பறழ்",4,"maa","scene","monkey cub"],["குறக் குறுமாக்கள்","குறவர்",6,"seythi","scene","hill children"],["தாளம்","தாளம்",6,"yaazh","scene","clapped time"],["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",8,"seythi","scene","hill girl"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:96":{"book":"natrinai","num":97,"t":["இதுவே நறு வீ ஞாழல் மா மலர்தாஅய்","புன்னை ததைந்த வெண் மணல் ஒரு சிறை","புதுவது புணர்ந்த பொழிலே உதுவே","பொம்மற் படு திரை நம்மோடு ஆடி","புறம் தாழ்பு இருளிய பிறங்குகுரல் ஐம்பால்","துவரினர் அருளிய துறையே அதுவே","கொடுங் கழி நிவந்த நெடுங் கால் நெய்தல்","அம் பகை நெறித் தழை அணி பெறத் தைஇ","தமியர் சென்ற கானல் என்று ஆங்கு","உள்ளுதோறு உள்ளுதோறு உருகி","பைஇப் பையப் பசந்தனை பசப்பே"],"p":"","r":"The friend points out to the woman the grove on one side of the white sand, crowded with punnai and strewn with the gnaazhal's fragrant flowers, where she newly joined him; the shore where the swelling waves played with them and he kindly dried the dark five-braided hair that hung down her back; and the shore garden where she went alone wearing a leaf dress woven of the long-stalked neythal of the curving backwater: thinking of them again and again she melts, and slowly the pallor has spread over her.","n":"இதுவே, உதுவே, அதுவே point to this place, that one and the one further off, so the friend walks the overhearing man back through his own meeting places; ending on the pallor, which she addresses directly, she tells him the memories have become a sickness only marriage will cure. Line 8 recurs as Natrinai 123 line 7.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pasalai","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 96","lb":"NT96","poet":"கோக்குளமுற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத்தோழி தலைவிக்கு உரைப்பாளாய் வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene","gnaazhal"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",2,"maram","scene","punnai"],["மணல்","மணல்",2,"pira","scene","sand"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",7,"maram","ornament","neythal flower"],["தழை","தழை",8,"maram","ornament","leaf dress"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:97":{"book":"natrinai","num":98,"t":["அழுந்து படு விழுப் புண் வழும்பு வாய்புலரா","எவ்வ நெஞ்சத்து எஃகு எறிந்தாங்கு","பிரிவில புலம்பி நுவலும் குயிலினும்","தேறு நீர் கெழீஇய யாறு நனி கொடிதே","அதனினும் கொடியள் தானே மதனின்","துய்த் தலை இதழ பைங் குருக்கத்தியொடு","பித்திகை விரவு மலர் கொள்ளீரோ என","வண்டு சூழ் வட்டியள் திரிதரும்","தண்டலை உழவர் தனி மட மகளே"],"p":"","r":"The woman says that crueler than the koel, which cries out lonely though it is not parted, as cruel as a spear thrust into an aching heart whose deep wound's pus has not dried, is the river of clear water, and crueler still is the lone young daughter of the grove's farmers who wanders with a bee-circled basket calling, 'Won't you take mixed flowers of pitthikai and fresh kurukkatthi with fluffy-tipped petals?'","n":"Each thing is crueler for being more ordinary: a bird's cry, then the river, then a farm girl selling flowers that a wife with her husband away will not wear, so the climax is a human voice selling the season. The koel is cruel because it laments without having been parted (பிரிவில புலம்பி). The colophon's mullai rests on the waiting; the season the poem names is spring.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 97","lb":"NT97","poet":"மாறன் வழுதி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பருவம் கண்டு ஆற்றாளாய தலைவி தோழிக்கு உரைத்தது","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பருவம் கண்டு ஆற்றாளாய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குயிலினும்","குயில்",3,"pul","scene","koel"],["யாறு","யாறு",4,"pira","scene","river"],["குருக்கத்தியொடு","குருக்கத்தி",6,"maram","scene","kurukkatthi"],["பித்திகை","பித்திகை",7,"maram","scene","pitthikai"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",8,"pira","scene","bee"],["உழவர்","உழவர்",9,"seythi","scene","farmers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:98":{"book":"natrinai","num":99,"t":["எய்ம் முள் அன்ன பரூஉ மயிர்எருத்தின்","செய்ய்ம்ம் மேவல் சிறு கட் பன்றி","ஓங்கு மலை வியன் புனம் படீஇயர் வீங்கு பொறி","நூழை நுழையும் பொழுதில் தாழாது","பாங்கர்ப் பக்கத்துப் பல்லி பட்டென","மெல்லமெல்லப் பிறக்கே பெயர்ந்து தன்","கல் அளைப் பள்ளி வதியும் நாடன்","எந்தை ஓம்பும் கடியுடை வியல் நகர்த்","துஞ்சாக் காவலர் இகழ் பதம் நோக்கி","இரவின் வரூஉம் அதனினும் கொடிதே","வைகலும் பொருந்தல் ஒல்லாக்","கண்ணொடு வாரா என் நார் இல் நெஞ்சே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the land where a small-eyed boar with coarse neck bristles like porcupine quills, coming to wallow in the wide millet field of the high mountain, is about to pass the gap where a heavy trap is set when a lizard chirps beside it, and at once backs away, slowly, slowly, to rest in its rock-cave bed: crueler even than his coming by night, watching for a lapse of the sleepless guards of her father's well-guarded wide house, is her own feckless heart, which will not keep company with eyes that cannot close any night.","n":"The boar heeds a lizard's chirp and turns back from the trap; the man ignores the guards and keeps coming. 'My heart' may be the friend speaking in the girl's person: her heart goes out to him at night while her eyes stay awake, so the danger is now inside the house as well.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","varaivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 98","lb":"NT98","poet":"உக்கிரப் பெருவழுதி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி வந்து ஒழுகும்தலைவனைத் தோழி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["எய்ம்","எய்",1,"maa","simile","porcupine"],["பன்றி","பன்றி",2,"maa","ullurai","boar"],["பொறி","பொறி",3,"pira","scene","trap"],["பல்லி","பல்லி",5,"pira","scene","lizard"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","பன்றி","confirmed","1/1","the man who comes to the guarded house by night"]],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:99":{"book":"natrinai","num":100,"t":["நீர் அற வறந்த நிரம்பா நீள்இடை","துகில் விரித்தன்ன வெயில் அவிர் உருப்பின்","அஞ்சுவரப் பனிக்கும் வெஞ் சுரம் இறந்தோர்","தாம் வரத் தெளித்த பருவம் காண்வர","இதுவோ என்றிசின் மடந்தை மதி இன்று","மறந்து கடல் முகந்த கமஞ் சூல் மா மழை","பொறுத்தல்செல்லாது இறுத்த வண் பெயல்","கார் என்று அயர்ந்த உள்ளமொடு தேர்வு இல","பிடவமும் கொன்றையும் கோடலும்","மடவ ஆகலின் மலர்ந்தன பலவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend answers the young woman who asked whether this is the season the men promised when they crossed the hot waste, its long way waterless, its heat shimmering like spread cloth: the great full-bellied cloud, mindless and forgetting itself, scooped up the sea and, unable to bear the load, let fall a heavy rain, and the pidavam, the konrai and the kodal, taking it for the rains with minds that cannot tell, have bloomed in numbers, because they are foolish.","n":"The friend does not deny that it rains; she denies that the rain means anything, by making both the cloud (மதி இன்று, மறந்து) and the flowers (மடவ) mistaken. Laughing at the flowers' folly lets the woman laugh at her own fear. The flowers mistake a sign as she did, so they are the poem's subject, not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","vaaimai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 99","lb":"NT99","poet":"இளந்திரையனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பருவம் கண்டு ஆற்றாளாய தலைவியைத் தோழி பருவம் அன்று என்று வற்புறுத்தியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"பருவம் அன்று என்று வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","மடன்"]],"k":[["மழை","மழை",6,"pira","scene","cloud"],["பிடவமும்","பிடவம்",9,"maram","scene","pidavam"],["கொன்றையும்","கொன்றை",9,"maram","scene","konrai"],["கோடலும்","கோடல்",9,"maram","scene","kodal"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:100":{"book":"natrinai","num":101,"t":["உள்ளுதொறும் நகுவேன் தோழி வள்உகிர்","மாரிக் கொக்கின் கூரல் அன்ன","குண்டு நீர் ஆம்பல் தண் துறை ஊரன்","தேம் கமழ் ஐம்பால் பற்றி என் வயின்","வான் கோல் எல் வளை வெளவிய பூசல்","சினவிய முகத்து சினவாது சென்று நின்","மனையோட்கு உரைப்பல் என்றலின் முனை ஊர்ப்","பல் ஆ நெடு நிரை வில்லின் ஒய்யும்","தேர் வண் மலையன் முந்தை பேர் இசைப்","புலம் புரி வயிரியர் நலம் புரி முழவின்","மண் ஆர் கண்ணின் அதிரும்","நன்னர் ஆளன் நடுங்கு அஞர் நிலையே"],"p":"","r":"The other woman says she laughs whenever she thinks of it: when the lord of the town of cool fords, where aambal stands in deep water like the plumage of the sharp-clawed heron of the rains, seized her honey-scented braids and snatched her bright bangles, and at the uproar she said, with an angry face but no anger, that she would go and tell his wife, the good man's trembling terror throbbed like the clay-smeared face of the drum the musicians play lovingly in the land of Malaiyan, giver of chariots, of great old fame, whose bow drives off the long cattle herds of enemy towns.","n":"She mocks him as நன்னர் ஆளன் (the good man) and makes sure the wife's people overhear: the man who grabs her in the street shakes at the thought of his wife, so the other woman claims the upper hand. A great raider's festival drum is borrowed to measure a philanderer's fright.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 100","lb":"NT100","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தை தலைவிக்குப்பாங்காயினார் கேட்ப விறலிக்கு உடம்படச்சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","virali","pira","karpu","விறலிக்கு உடம்படச்சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["கொக்கின்","கொக்கு",2,"pul","simile","heron"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",3,"maram","simile","water lily"],["வளை","வளை",5,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["பல் ஆ","ஆ",8,"maa","scene","cattle"],["வயிரியர்","வயிரியர்",10,"seythi","simile","musicians"],["முழவின்","முழவு",10,"parai","simile","drum"]],"en":[["king","மலையன்",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:5"},"nt:101":{"book":"natrinai","num":102,"t":["முற்றா மஞ்சட் பசும் புறம் கடுப்பச்","சுற்றிய பிணர சூழ் கழி இறவின்","கணம் கொள் குப்பை உணங்கு திறன் நோக்கி","புன்னை அம் கொழு நிழல் முன் உய்த்துப் பரப்பும்","துறை நணி இருந்த பாக்கம் உம் உறை நனி","இனிதுமன் அளிதோ தானே துனி தீர்ந்து","அகன்ற அல்குல் ஐது அமை நுசுப்பின்","மீன் எறி பரதவர் மட மகள்","மான் அமர் நோக்கம் காணா ஊங்கே"],"p":"","r":"The man, within the friend's hearing, says the shore hamlet where curled prawns are heaped to dry in the punnai's shade was a sweet place to live, until he saw the deer-like look of the fisherman's young daughter.","n":"The whole poem is a before and after: the busy, pleasant work of the shore is described only to be lost, and the loss is dated to one sight of her eyes; spoken where the friend can hear, it is his plea for her help.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 101","lb":"NT101","poet":"வெள்ளியந்தின்னனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பின்னின்ற தலைமகன் தோழி கேட்பச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira","thozhi","kalavu","பின்னின்ற தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மஞ்சட்","மஞ்சள்",1,"maram","simile","turmeric"],["இறவின்","இறவு",2,"pira","scene","prawn"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",4,"maram","scene",""],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",8,"seythi","scene",""],["மான்","மான்",9,"maa","simile","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:102":{"book":"natrinai","num":103,"t":["கொடுங் குரற் குறைத்த செவ் வாய்ப்பைங் கிளி","அஞ்சல் ஓம்பி ஆர் பதம் கொண்டு","நின் குறை முடித்த பின்றை என் குறை","செய்தல்வேண்டுமால் கை தொழுது இரப்பல்","பல் கோட் பலவின் சாரல் அவர் நாட்டு","நின் கிளை மருங்கின் சேறிஆயின்","அம் மலை கிழவோற்கு உரைமதி இம் மலைக்","கானக் குறவர் மட மகள்","ஏனல் காவல் ஆயினள் எனவே"],"p":"","r":"The woman begs the red-beaked parrot, with joined hands, to eat its fill of the millet without fear and then, when it goes to its kin on his jackfruit slopes, to tell the lord of that hill that the young daughter of this hill's kuravar has been set to guard the millet.","n":"She strikes a bargain with the very bird she is posted to drive away: it may eat the crop if it carries her message, and the message itself (she is at the field by day) is the invitation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thoothu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 102","lb":"NT102","poet":"செம்பியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"காமம் மிக்க கழிபடர்கிளவி","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu","காமம் மிக்க கழிபடர்கிளவி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கிளி","கிளி",1,"pul","scene","parrot"],["பலவின்","பலா",5,"maram","scene",""],["குறவர்","குறவர்",8,"seythi","scene",""],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",9,"unavu","scene","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:103":{"book":"natrinai","num":104,"t":["ஒன்று தெரிந்து உரைத்திசின் நெஞ்சே புன் கால்","சிறியிலை வேம்பின் பெரிய கொன்று","கடாஅம் செருக்கிய கடுஞ் சின முன்பின்","களிறு நின்று இறந்த நீர் அல் ஈரத்து","பால் அவி தோல் முலை அகடு நிலம் சேர்த்திப்","பசி அட முடங்கிய பைங் கட் செந்நாய்","மாயா வேட்டம் போகிய கணவன்","பொய்யா மரபின் பிணவு நினைந்து இரங்கும்","விருந்தின் வெங் காட்டு வருந்துதும் யாமே","ஆள்வினைக்கு அகல்வாம் எனினும்","மீள்வாம் எனினும் நீ துணிந்ததுவே"],"p":"","r":"Midway on the road for wealth, the man tells his heart to decide one thing: in this strange hot forest, where a hungry red she-dog with dried teats lies curled on ground wet with no water and her mate, gone on his unfailing hunt, grieves thinking of her, we suffer; whether we go on for our work or turn back, it is your choice.","n":"The wild dogs are a couple split by hunger exactly as he and his wife are split by the hunt for wealth; the male who has gone to hunt yet grieves for his mate is the man himself, so the heart's choice is already answered by the scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 103","lb":"NT103","poet":"மருதன் இள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற்பிரிந்த தலைவன் இடைச்சுரத்து ஆற்றாதாகிய நெஞ்சினைக்கழறியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பொருள்வயிற்பிரிந்த தலைவன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",2,"maram","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",4,"maa","scene","tusker"],["செந்நாய்","செந்நாய்",6,"maa","iraicchi","wild dog"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","செந்நாய்","not_confirmed","0/1","the man who has left on his own hunt for wealth is bound to grieve for his wife in the same way, so the heart should turn back"],["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:104":{"book":"natrinai","num":105,"t":["பூம் பொறி உழுவைப் பேழ் வாய்ஏற்றை","தேம் கமழ் சிலம்பின் களிற்றொடு பொரினே","துறுகல் மீமிசை உறுகண் அஞ்சாக்","குறக் குறுமாக்கள் புகற்சியின் எறிந்த","தொண்டகச் சிறு பறைப் பாணி அயலது","பைந் தாள் செந்தினைப் படு கிளி ஓப்பும்","ஆர் கலி வெற்பன் மார்பு நயந்து உறையும்","யானே அன்றியும் உளர்கொல் பானாள்","பாம்புடை விடர ஓங்கு மலை மிளிர","உருமு சிவந்து எறியும் பொழுதொடு பெரு நீர்","போக்கு அற விலங்கிய சாரல்","நோக்கு அருஞ் சிறு நெறி நினையுமோரே"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks whether anyone but she, who lives loving the chest of the lord of the loud hill where hill boys beat drums at a tiger fighting a tusker and others scare parrots off the millet, ever thinks of the narrow, hard-to-see path he takes at midnight, when thunder strikes the snake-holed hill and the flood blocks the way.","n":"The hill's children turn the tiger-elephant fight into a game for their drums, fearless; she is the one person on that hill who is afraid, and the fear is for him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 104","lb":"NT104","poet":"பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைவி ஆறுபார்த்து உற்ற அச்சத்தால் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","ஆறுபார்த்து உற்ற அச்சத்தால்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["உழுவைப்","உழுவை",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["களிற்றொடு","களிறு",2,"maa","scene","tusker"],["குறக்","குறவர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["தொண்டகச்","தொண்டகப் பறை",5,"parai","scene",""],["செந்தினைப்","தினை",6,"unavu","scene",""],["கிளி","கிளி",6,"pul","scene",""],["பாம்புடை","பாம்பு",9,"pira","scene",""],["உருமு","உருமு",10,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:105":{"book":"natrinai","num":106,"t":["முளி கொடி வலந்த முள் அரை இலவத்து","ஒளிர் சினை அதிர வீசி விளிபட","வெவ் வளி வழங்கும் வேய் பயில் மருங்கில்","கடு நடை யானை கன்றொடு வருந்த","நெடு நீர் அற்ற நிழல் இல் ஆங்கண்","அருஞ் சுரக் கவலைய என்னாய் நெடுஞ் சேண்","பட்டனை வாழிய நெஞ்சே குட்டுவன்","குட வரைச் சுனைய மா இதழ்க் குவளை","வண்டு படு வான் போது கமழும்","அம் சில் ஓதி அரும் படர் உறவே"],"p":"","r":"The man scolds his heart, which wants to turn back midway: it never weighed the forked tracks of this shadeless, waterless waste where hot wind shakes the thorny silk-cotton and an elephant suffers with its calf, and now it has come far only to pine for her soft hair, fragrant as the bee-visited kuvalai of the springs on Kuttuvan's western hill.","n":"The rebuke is double-edged: the heart is blamed for not foreseeing the road, but its real fault is that it already turns home to her, and the one rich image in the waste is her hair.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 105","lb":"NT105","poet":"முடத்திருமாறன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"இடைச் சுரத்து மீளலுற்ற நெஞ்சினைத் தலைமகன் கழறியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"மீளலுற்ற நெஞ்சினைத் தலைமகன் கழறியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["இலவத்து","இலவம்",1,"maram","scene",""],["வேய்","வேய்",3,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene",""],["குவளை","குவளை",8,"maram","ornament",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",9,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவன்",7],["hill","குட வரை",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:106":{"book":"natrinai","num":107,"t":["அறிதலும் அறிதியோ பாக பெருங்கடல்","எறி திரை கொழீஇய எக்கர் வெறி கொள","ஆடு வரி அலவன் ஓடுவயின் ஆற்றாது","அசைஇ உள் ஒழிந்த வசை தீர் குறுமகட்கு","உயவினென் சென்று யான் உள் நோய் உரைப்ப","மறுமொழி பெயர்த்தல் ஆற்றாள் நறு மலர்","ஞாழல் அம் சினைத் தாழ்இணர் கொழுதி","முறி திமிர்ந்து உதிர்த்த கையள்","அறிவு அஞர் உறுவி ஆய் மட நிலையே"],"p":"","r":"Turning back as the season brings a past moment to mind, the man asks his charioteer whether he knows how, on the wave-heaped dune where the striped crab ran and she, unable to keep up, stayed behind, he went and told her his inner pain, and she, unable to answer, pulled down the gnaazhal's hanging cluster and crushed its sprouts till they fell from her hand, lost in her shy distress.","n":"Her answer to his first confession is a gesture, not a word: her hand tearing at the flowering branch says what her mouth cannot, and it is this silence, not any speech, that calls him home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","naanam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 106","lb":"NT106","poet":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பருவ வரவின்கண் பண்டு நிகழ்ந்ததோர் குறிப்பு உணர்ந்த தலைவன் அதனைக் கண்டு தாங்ககில்லானாய் மீள்கின்றான் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச்சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,"தேர்ப்பாகற்குச்சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அலவன்","அலவன்",3,"pira","scene","crab"],["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",7,"maram","scene",""],["முறி","முறி",8,"maram","scene","tender leaf"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:107":{"book":"natrinai","num":108,"t":["உள்ளுதொறும் நகுவேன் தோழி வள்உகிர்ப்","பிடி பிளந்திட்ட நார் இல் வெண் கோட்டுக்","கொடிறு போல் காய வால் இணர்ப் பாலை","செல் வளி தூக்கலின் இலை தீர் நெற்றம்","கல் இழி அருவியின் ஒல்லென ஒலிக்கும்","புல் இலை ஓமைய புலி வழங்கு அத்தம்","சென்ற காதலர்வழி வழிப்பட்ட","நெஞ்சே நல்வினைப்பாற்றே ஈண்டு ஒழிந்து","ஆனாக் கௌவை மலைந்த","யானே தோழி நோய்ப்பாலேனே"],"p":"","r":"The wasting woman tells her friend that she laughs whenever she thinks of it: her heart, which went with her lover along the tiger-haunted track of omai trees where dry paalai pods rattle in the wind like a waterfall, has the good share, while she, left here wearing endless gossip, has the share of pain.","n":"She divides herself in two and envies the half that left; the laughter is at her own lot, and the pod simile (like the pincer-shaped stalk a she-elephant splits) turns on rare words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 107","lb":"NT107","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைவி தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைவி தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"],["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["பிடி","பிடி",2,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["பாலை","பாலை",3,"maram","scene","paalai tree"],["அருவியின்","அருவி",5,"pira","simile",""],["ஓமைய","ஓமை",6,"maram","scene",""],["புலி","புலி",6,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:108":{"book":"natrinai","num":109,"t":["மலை அயற் கலித்த மை ஆர் ஏனல்","துணையின் தீர்ந்த கடுங்கண் யானை","அணையக் கண்ட அம் குடிக் குறவர்","கணையர் கிணையர் கை புனை கவணர்","விளியர் புறக்குடி ஆர்க்கும் நாட","பழகிய பகையும் பிரிவு இன்னாதே","முகை ஏர் இலங்கு எயிற்று இன் நகை மடந்தை","சுடர் புரை திரு நுதல் பசப்ப","தொடர்பு யாங்கு விட்டனை நோகோ யானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the land where hill folk, with arrows, drums and slings, shout from the village edge at a fierce elephant parted from its mate as it nears the millet: even parting from a familiar enemy hurts; how could he let go the bond while this girl with bud-like teeth and flame-bright brow turns pale; she grieves for it.","n":"The proverb at the turn (பழகிய பகையும் பிரிவு இன்னாதே) shames him: parting from a known foe is hard, and he parts lightly from a lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivu","pasalai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 108","lb":"NT108","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரையாது நெடுங் காலம்வந்து ஒழுகலாற்றாளாய தோழி தலைமகளது ஆற்றாமை கூறி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",1,"unavu","scene","millet"],["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","ullurai",""],["குறவர்","குறவர்",3,"seythi","ullurai",""],["கிணையர்","கிணை",4,"parai","scene",""],["முகை","முகை",7,"maram","simile","bud"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","யானை குறவர்","confirmed","1/2","the lover who keeps coming unmarried and the watchful village"]],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:109":{"book":"natrinai","num":110,"t":["ஒன்றுதும் என்ற தொன்று படு நட்பின்","காதலர் அகன்றென கலங்கிப் பேதுற்று","அன்னவோ இந் நன்னுதல் நிலை என","வினவல் ஆனாப் புனையிழை கேள் இனி","உரைக்கல் ஆகா எவ்வம் இம்மென","இரைக்கும் வாடை இருள் கூர் பொழுதில்","துளியுடைத் தொழுவின் துணிதல் அற்றத்து","உச்சிக் கட்டிய கூழை ஆவின்","நிலை என ஒருவேன் ஆகி","உலமர கழியும் இப் பகல் மடி பொழுதே"],"p":"","r":"The woman answers her friend, who keeps asking whether this is her state now that the lover of old friendship, who promised they would be one, has gone: listen; her sorrow cannot be told; as the evening passes with the north wind humming in the dark, she stands alone and turning, like a cow tied by the head in a dripping shed.","n":"The simile is exact about helplessness: the tied cow in the cold shed cannot move to shelter or to its calf, as she can neither go to him nor stop waiting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 109","lb":"NT109","poet":"மீளிப் பெரும்பதுமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகளது நிலைகண்ட தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தொழுவின்","தொழு",7,"pira","simile","cattle shed"],["ஆவின்","ஆ",8,"maa","simile","cow"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:110":{"book":"natrinai","num":111,"t":["பிரசம் கலந்த வெண் சுவைத் தீம்பால்","விரி கதிர்ப் பொற்கலத்து ஒரு கை ஏந்தி","புடைப்பின் சுற்றும் பூந் தலைச் சிறு கோல்","உண் என்று ஓக்குபு பிழைப்ப தெண் நீர்","முத்து அரிப் பொற்சிலம்பு ஒலிப்பத் தத்துற்று","அரி நரைக் கூந்தற் செம் முது செவிலியர்","பரி மெலிந்து ஒழிய பந்தர் ஓடி","ஏவல் மறுக்கும் சிறு விளையாட்டி","அறிவும் ஒழுக்கமும் யாண்டு உணர்ந்தனள்கொல்","கொண்ட கொழுநன் குடி வறன் உற்றென","கொடுத்த தந்தை கொழுஞ் சோறு உள்ளாள்","ஒழுகு நீர் நுணங்கு அறல் போல","பொழுது மறுத்து உண்ணும் சிறு மதுகையளே"],"p":"","r":"Wondering at a daughter's household, the speaker recalls the little girl who ran, gold anklets ringing, under the pandal to refuse the honeyed milk her old nurses chased her with, and asks where she learned this sense and conduct: now that her husband's family has fallen poor, she does not think of the rich rice her father sends, and eats only at alternate meals, small and strong-willed.","n":"The poem rests on the gap between the two pictures, the spoilt child who would not eat and the wife who will not eat more than her husband's house can give; the closing simile of the fine ripples in flowing water is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karpu","inmai","ilvaazhkkai","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 110","lb":"NT110","poet":"போதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மனைமருட்சி மகள்நிலை உரைத்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","nartaay",null,"karpu","மகள்நிலை உரைத்ததூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["பிரசம்","பிரசம்",1,"unavu","gift_food","honey"],["தீம்பால்","பால்",1,"unavu","gift_food","milk"],["சோறு","சோறு",11,"unavu","gift_food",""],["அறல்","அறல்",12,"pira","simile","fine sand ripples"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:111":{"book":"natrinai","num":112,"t":["அத்த இருப்பைப் பூவின் அன்ன","துய்த் தலை இறவொடு தொகை மீன் பெறீஇயர்","வரி வலைப் பரதவர் கரு வினைச் சிறாஅர்","மரல் மேற்கொண்டு மான் கணம் தகைமார்","வெந் திறல் இளையவர் வேட்டு எழுந்தாங்கு","திமில் மேற்கொண்டு திரைச் சுரம் நீந்தி","வாள் வாய்ச் சுறவொடு வய மீன் கெண்டி","நிணம் பெய் தோணியர் இகு மணல் இழிதரும்","பெருங் கழிப் பாக்கம் கல்லென","வருமே தோழி கொண்கன் தேரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, glad at a good omen, tells the woman that to the great backwater hamlet, loud as the fisher boys come down the sloping sand with boats full of fat after crossing the wave-desert on their catamarans, like young hunters setting out for deer, to catch swordfish and prawns with heads like iruppai flowers, the lord's chariot is coming.","n":"The fleet's noisy homecoming is the sound that announces the chariot; the fishermen's sea is drawn as a paalai waste (திரைச் சுரம்) and their fishing as a deer hunt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 111","lb":"NT111","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"விரிச்சி பெற்றுப்புகன்ற தோழி தலைவிக்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"விரிச்சி பெற்றுப்புகன்ற தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["இருப்பைப்","இருப்பை",1,"maram","simile",""],["இறவொடு","இறவு",2,"pira","scene","prawn"],["மீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","scene",""],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["மான்","மான்",4,"maa","simile","deer"],["சுறவொடு","சுறவு",7,"pira","scene","shark"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:112":{"book":"natrinai","num":113,"t":["விருந்து எவன்செய்கோ தோழி சாரல்","அரும்பு அற மலர்ந்த கருங் கால் வேங்கைச்","சுரும்பு இமிர் அடுக்கம் புலம்பக் களிறு அட்டு","உரும்பு இல் உள்ளத்து அரிமா வழங்கும்","பெருங் கல் நாடன் வரவு அறிந்து விரும்பி","மாக் கடல் முகந்து மணி நிறத்து அருவித்","தாழ் நீர் நனந் தலை அழுந்து படப் பாஅய்","மலை இமைப்பது போல் மின்னி","சிலை வல் ஏற்றொடு செறிந்த இம் மழைக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks what feast she can give this rain-cloud that, knowing the coming of the lord of the great-rock land where vengai has bloomed and a fearless lion kills an elephant, has gladly scooped the dark sea, poured down the sapphire falls and flashes like the mountain blinking, packed with strong thunder.","n":"She turns the rain, which the waiting woman dreads as a sign of broken time, into a guest who has come ahead of him, and the joke of feasting a cloud is her way of making the heroine smile.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","virundhu","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 112","lb":"NT112","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பருவ வரவின்கண்ஆற்றாளாய தலைவியைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வேங்கைச்","வேங்கை",2,"maram","scene",""],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["களிறு","களிறு",3,"maa","scene",""],["அரிமா","அரிமா",4,"maa","scene","lion"],["ஏற்றொடு","ஏறு",9,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:113":{"book":"natrinai","num":114,"t":["உழை அணந்து உண்ட இறை வாங்கு உயர்சினைப்","புல் அரை இரத்திப் பொதிப் புறப் பசுங் காய்","கல் சேர் சிறு நெறி மல்கத் தாஅம்","பெருங் காடு இறந்தும் எய்த வந்தனவால்","அருஞ் செயல் பொருட் பிணி முன்னி யாமே","சேறும் மடந்தை என்றலின் தான் தன்","நெய்தல் உண்கண் பைதல் கூர","பின் இருங் கூந்தலின் மறையினள் பெரிது அழிந்து","உதியன் மண்டிய ஒலி தலை ஞாட்பின்","இம்மென் பெருங் களத்து இயவர் ஊதும்","ஆம்பல்அம் குழலின் ஏங்கி","கலங்கு அஞர் உறுவோள் புலம்பு கொள் நோக்கே"],"p":"","r":"Unable to go on in the wilds, the man says that though he has crossed the great forest where green iratthi pods, nibbled by deer, lie strewn on the stony path, her look has come with him: when he said he would go after hard-won wealth, she hid her neythal eyes in her long hair, broken, and sobbed like the ambal pipe the musicians blow on the hushed field after Udhiyan's loud battle.","n":"The sob is likened not to a lament but to the pipe played on a battlefield after the fighting, so her one moment of grief carries the hush of a field of the dead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 113","lb":"NT113","poet":"இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"இடைச் சுரத்து ஆற்றானாய தலைவன் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"இடைச் சுரத்து ஆற்றானாய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["உழை","உழை",1,"maa","scene","deer"],["இரத்திப்","இரத்தி",2,"maram","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",7,"maram","simile",""],["இயவர்","இயவர்",10,"seythi","simile",""],["குழலின்","குழல்",11,"yaazh","simile",""]],"en":[["king","உதியன்",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:114":{"book":"natrinai","num":115,"t":["வெண் கோடு கொண்டு வியல் அறை வைப்பவும்","பச்சூன் கெண்டி வள் உகிர் முணக்கவும்","மறுகுதொறு புலாவும் சிறுகுடி அரவம்","வைகிக் கேட்டுப் பையாந்திசினே","அளிதோ தானே தோழி அல்கல்","வந்தோன்மன்ற குன்ற நாடன்","துளி பெயல் பொறித்த புள்ளித் தொல் கரை","பொரு திரை நிவப்பின் வரும் யாறு அஞ்சுவல்","ஈர்ங் குரல் உருமின் ஆர் கலி நல் ஏறு","பாம்பு கவின் அழிக்கும் ஓங்கு வரை பொத்தி","மையல் மடப் பிடி இனைய","கை ஊன்றுபு இழிதரு களிறு எறிந்தன்றே"],"p":"","r":"With the man nearby, the friend tells the woman that, awake all night, she heard the hamlet's noise as white tusks were laid on the rock and raw meat cut, and was troubled: he surely came last night, the hill lord, and she fears the river that rises in waves against its rain-pitted banks, and the thunder that struck the tusker coming down the high hill, leaving the young she-elephant grieving.","n":"The hamlet's feast on a killed elephant and the thunder that kills another frame the night path as a place where tuskers die; she says it to the heroine but means him to hear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["acham","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 114","lb":"NT114","poet":"தொல்கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"ஆறு பார்த்து உற்ற அச்சத்தால் தோழி தலைவிக்கு உரைப்பாளாய் சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","ஆறு பார்த்து உற்ற அச்சத்தால்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["கோடு","கோடு",1,"maa","scene","tusk"],["பச்சூன்","ஊன்",2,"unavu","gift_food",""],["யாறு","யாறு",8,"pira","scene",""],["உருமின்","உருமு",9,"pira","scene","thunder"],["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",10,"pira","scene",""],["பிடி","பிடி",11,"maa","iraicchi","she-elephant"],["களிறு","களிறு",12,"maa","iraicchi","tusker"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பிடி","not_confirmed","0/1","the heroine would be left the same way if the man keeps taking the night path, so he should stop coming by night"],["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:115":{"book":"natrinai","num":116,"t":["மலர்ந்த பொய்கைப் பூக் குற்று அழுங்க","அயர்ந்த ஆயம் கண் இனிது படீஇயர்","அன்னையும் சிறிது தணிந்து உயிரினள் இன் நீர்த்","தடங் கடல் வாயில் உண்டு சில் நீர் என","மயில் அடி இலைய மாக் குரல் நொச்சி","மனை நடு மௌவலொடு ஊழ் முகை அவிழ","கார் எதிர்ந்தன்றால் காலை காதலர்","தவச் சேய் நாட்டர்ஆயினும் மிகப் பேர்","அன்பினர் வாழி தோழி நன் புகழ்","உலப்பு இன்று பெறினும் தவிரலர்","கேட்டிசின் அல்லெனோ விசும்பின் தகவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that the rains have come this morning, the peacock-foot leaved nocchi and the jasmine in the yard opening their buds, so that her tired playmates may sleep and even mother breathes easier; though her lover is in a far country he has very great love, and would not stay away even for endless fame: has she not heard what the sky's keeping of time shows?","n":"The proof is the sky itself: the rains have kept their season, and the friend asks her to read his promise by it; the last words set love above the one thing puram men are praised for seeking, fame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","anbu","pugazh","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 115","lb":"NT115","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகளைத் தோழி பருவம் காட்டி வற்புறுத்தியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவம் காட்டி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பொய்கைப்","பொய்கை",1,"pira","scene",""],["மயில்","மயில்",5,"pul","scene",""],["நொச்சி","நொச்சி",5,"maram","scene",""],["மௌவலொடு","மௌவல்",6,"maram","scene","jasmine"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:116":{"book":"natrinai","num":117,"t":["தீமை கண்டோர் திறத்தும்பெரியோர்","தாம் அறிந்து உணர்க என்பமாதோ","வழுவப் பிண்டம் நாப்பண் ஏமுற்று","இரு வெதிர் ஈன்ற வேல் தலைக் கொழு முளை","சூல் முதிர் மடப் பிடி நாள் மேயல் ஆரும்","மலை கெழு நாடன் கேண்மை பலவின்","மாச் சினை துறந்த கோள் முதிர் பெரும் பழம்","விடர் அளை வீழ்ந்து உக்காஅங்கு தொடர்பு அறச்","சேணும் சென்று உக்கன்றே அறியாது","ஏ கல் அடுக்கத்து இருள் முகை இருந்த","குறிஞ்சி நல் ஊர்ப் பெண்டிர்","இன்னும் ஓவார் என் திறத்து அலரே"],"p":"","r":"Refusing her friend's comfort, the woman says the great say one should understand even those who wrong us; the friendship of the hill lord, where a pregnant young she-elephant grazes on bamboo shoots, has broken off and gone far, like a ripe jackfruit that leaves its branch and bursts in a rock cleft, yet the women of the kurinji town, not knowing that, still keep up their gossip about her.","n":"The bitterness is that the gossip outlives the love it was about: the bond has burst like the fallen fruit, but the town's talk goes on; the opening maxim is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","natpu","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 116","lb":"NT116","poet":"கந்தரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரைவு நீட்டிப்ப ஆற்றாளாய தலைவி தோழிக்கு வன்புறை எதிரழிந்து சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வன்புறை எதிரழிந்து சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["வெதிர்","வெதிர்",4,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பிடி","பிடி",5,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["பலவின்","பலா",6,"maram","simile",""],["குறிஞ்சி","குறிஞ்சி",11,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:117":{"book":"natrinai","num":118,"t":["பெருங் கடல் முழங்க கானல் மலர","இருங் கழி ஓதம் இல் இறந்து மலிர","வள் இதழ் நெய்தல் கூம்ப புள் உடன்","கமழ் பூம் பொதும்பர்க் கட்சி சேர","செல் சுடர் மழுங்கச் சிவந்து வாங்கு மண்டிலம்","கல் சேர்பு நண்ணிப் படர் அடைபு நடுங்க","புலம்பொடு வந்த புன்கண் மாலை","அன்னர் உன்னார் கழியின் பல் நாள்","வாழலென் வாழி தோழி என்கண்","பிணி பிறிதாகக் கூறுவர்","பழி பிறிதாகல் பண்புமார் அன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Refusing comfort, the woman tells her friend that in the sorrowful evening, when the sea roars, the tide overflows the houses, the neythal closes, birds go together to their nests and the reddened sun sinks to the hill, he does not think of her, and she will not live many days; people call her sickness something else, and it is not right that the blame should fall elsewhere either.","n":"The closing lines turn from grief to a sharper point: if her love-sickness is taken for something else, the man escapes the blame, and she will not let him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 117","lb":"NT117","poet":"குன்றியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரைவு நீட ஆற்றாளாய தலைவி வன்புறை எதிரழிந்து சொல்லியது சிறைப்புறமும்ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வன்புறை எதிரழிந்து சொல்லியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமும்ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஓதம்","ஓதம்",2,"pira","scene","tide"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","scene",""],["புள்","புள்",3,"pul","iraicchi",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","புள்","not_confirmed","0/1","even the birds come home together at dusk, while he does not come or think of her"]],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:118":{"book":"natrinai","num":119,"t":["அடைகரை மாஅத்து அலங்கு சினை பொலியத்","தளிர் கவின் எய்திய தண் நறும் பொதும்பில்","சேவலொடு கெழீஇய செங் கண் இருங் குயில்","புகன்று எதிர் ஆலும் பூ மலி காலையும்","அகன்றோர்மன்ற நம் மறந்திசினோர் என","இணர் உறுபு உடைவதன்தலையும் புணர்வினை","ஓவ மாக்கள் ஒள் அரக்கு ஊட்டிய","துகிலிகை அன்ன துய்த் தலைப் பாதிரி","வால் இதழ் அலரி வண்டு பட ஏந்தி","புது மலர் தெருவுதொறு நுவலும்","நொதுமலாட்டிக்கு நோம் என் நெஞ்சே"],"p":"","r":"The woman says that even in the flowering season, when the red-eyed koel with its mate calls back eagerly from the mango boughs on the bank, she thinks he who left has surely forgotten them; and beyond that, her heart aches at the stranger woman who goes street by street crying new pathiri flowers, fluffy-tipped like a painter's brush dipped in lac.","n":"The flower-seller is a stranger to her grief and that is the wound: the season is being sold cheerfully in every street, and she must hear it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 118","lb":"NT118","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பருவம் கண்டு ஆற்றாளாய தலைவி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பருவம் கண்டு ஆற்றாளாய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மாஅத்து","மா",1,"maram","scene","mango"],["குயில்","குயில்",3,"pul","iraicchi","koel"],["ஓவ","ஓவம்",7,"seythi","simile","painters"],["பாதிரி","பாதிரி",8,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",9,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","குயில்","confirmed","1/1","in the season when even the koel is with its mate, the man who left has failed to come, so she reads it as his forgetting"]],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:119":{"book":"natrinai","num":120,"t":["தினை உண் கேழல் இரிய புனவன்","சிறு பொறி மாட்டிய பெருங் கல் அடாஅர்","ஒண் கேழ் வயப் புலி படூஉம் நாடன்","ஆர் தர வந்தனன் ஆயினும் படப்பை","இன் முசுப் பெருங் கலை நன் மேயல் ஆரும்","பல் மலர்க் கான் யாற்று உம்பர் கருங் கலை","கடும்பு ஆட்டு வருடையொடு தாவன உகளும்","பெரு வரை நீழல் வருகுவன் குளவியொடு","கூதளம் ததைந்த கண்ணியன் யாவதும்","முயங்கல் பெறுகுவன் அல்லன்","புலவி கொளீஇயர் தன் மலையினும் பெரிதே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend says that the lord of the land where a hill farmer's stone trap, set for the millet-eating boar, catches a bright strong tiger, though he came to have his fill, will come to the shade of the great hill beyond the flowered forest river, where langurs and stags leap with wild goats, wearing kulavi and kuuthalam, and will not get a single embrace; let his sulking be greater than his mountain.","n":"She tells him that the girl is now shut in the house: he will come to the usual place in his hill garland and find no one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 119","lb":"NT119","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத்தோழி செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தினை","தினை",1,"unavu","scene",""],["கேழல்","கேழல்",1,"maa","scene","boar"],["புனவன்","புனவன்",1,"seythi","ullurai",""],["புலி","புலி",3,"maa","ullurai","tiger"],["முசுப்","முசு",5,"maa","scene","langur"],["கலை","கலை",6,"maa","scene","stag"],["வருடையொடு","வருடை",7,"maa","scene","wild goat"],["யாற்று","யாறு",6,"pira","scene",""],["குளவியொடு","குளவி",8,"maram","ornament",""],["கூதளம்","கூதளம்",9,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","புலி புனவன்","confirmed","1/1","the lover and the girl's family who have shut her in"]],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:120":{"book":"natrinai","num":121,"t":["தட மருப்பு எருமை மட நடைக் குழவி","தூண் தொறும் யாத்த காண்தகு நல் இல்","கொடுங் குழை பெய்த செழுஞ் செய் பேதை","சிறு தாழ் செறித்த மெல் விரல் சேப்ப","வாளை ஈர்ந் தடி வல்லிதின் வகைஇ","புகை உண்டு அமர்த்த கண்ணள் தகை பெறப்","பிறை நுதல் பொறித்த சிறு நுண் பல் வியர்","அம் துகில் தலையில் துடையினள் நப் புலந்து","அட்டிலோளே அம் மா அரிவை","எமக்கே வருகதில் விருந்தே சிவப்பாள் அன்று","சிறு முள் எயிறு தோன்ற","முறுவல் கொண்ட முகம் காண்கம்மே"],"p":"","r":"Having come in with a guest as his way in, the man says that in the fine house where buffalo calves are tied to every pillar, his young wife, her soft fingers reddening as she cuts wet slices of vaalai fish, eyes smarting with smoke, wiping the sweat from her crescent brow with her garment's edge and sulking at him, is in the kitchen; let guests come to them, for she will not be angry then, and he will see her face smile with small sharp teeth showing.","n":"The guest is his weapon in the quarrel: hospitality forbids her a sour face, so the man who caused the sulk can count on her smile.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","oodal","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 120","lb":"NT120","poet":"மாங்குடி கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"விருந்து வாயிலாகப்புக்க தலைவன் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","விருந்து வாயிலாகப்புக்க"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","scene",""],["வாளை","வாளை",5,"pira","gift_food","vaalai fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:6"},"nt:121":{"book":"natrinai","num":122,"t":["விதையர் கொன்ற முதையல் பூழி","இடு முறை நிரப்பிய ஈர் இலை வரகின்","கவைக் கதிர் கறித்த காமர் மடப் பிணை","அரலை அம் காட்டு இரலையொடு வதியும்","புறவிற்று அம்ம நீ நயந்தோள் ஊரே","எல்லி விட்டன்று வேந்து எனச் சொல்லுபு","பரியல் வாழ்க நின் கண்ணி காண் வர","விரி உளைப் பொலிந்த வீங்கு செலல் கலி மா","வண் பரி தயங்க எழீஇ தண் பெயற்","கான் யாற்று இகுமணற் கரை பிறக்கு ஒழிய","எல் விருந்து அயரும் மனைவி","மெல் இறைப் பணைத் தோள் துயில் அமர்வோயே"],"p":"","r":"The charioteer tells the hero, his work done and the king's leave given, not to fret: the town of the woman he loves lies in forest land where a young doe that nibbled the forked ears of millet rests beside her stag, and the plumed horses will cross the rain-cooled stream so that he may sleep tonight on the soft shoulders of the wife who is making ready the evening feast for guests.","n":"The king's release is given in one clause and the rest of the poem is road and home: duty ends abruptly, and the charioteer measures the distance left by things the hero can see, the doe at rest, the stream bank falling behind, so that the wife's evening feast for guests becomes the poem's goal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","ilvaazhkkai","virundhu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 121","lb":"NT121","poet":"ஒரு சிறைப்பெரியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி மறுத்தரும் தலைமகற்குத் தேர்ப்பாகன் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["pira","thalaivan",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றி மறுத்தரும்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["விதையர்","விதையர்",1,"seythi","scene","sowers"],["வரகின்","வரகு",2,"unavu","scene",""],["பிணை","பிணை",3,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["அரலை","அரலை",4,"maram","scene",""],["இரலையொடு","இரலை",4,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["மா","மா",8,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","இரலையொடு","confirmed","1/1","the pair at rest after feeding promises the hero the same rest beside his wife tonight"]],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:122":{"book":"natrinai","num":123,"t":["இருங் கல் அடுக்கத்து என்னையர் உழுத","கருங் கால் செந்தினை கடியுமுண்டென","கல்லக வரைப்பில் கான் கெழு சிறுகுடி","மெல் அவல் மருங்கின் மௌவலும் அரும்பின","நரை உரும் உரறும் நாம நள் இருள்","வரையக நாடன் வரூஉம் என்பது","உண்டுகொல் அன்றுகொல் யாதுகொல் மற்று என","நின்று மதி வல் உள்ளமொடு மறைந்தவை ஆடி","அன்னையும் அமரா முகத்தினள் நின்னொடு","நீயே சூழ்தல் வேண்டும்","பூ வேய் கண்ணி அது பொருந்துமாறே"],"p":"","r":"With the hero listening outside, the friend tells the girl that the red millet their brothers ploughed on the slope will soon be guarded, the jasmine by the hamlet has budded, the mother has turned a frowning face on her secret doings and asks whether the hill man really comes through the thunder-filled midnight, so the girl must herself decide how the flower garland can be made to fit, that is, how the man can be brought to marry.","n":"Every detail is a clock for the man outside: the millet to be guarded, the jasmine in bud and the mother's question all say the meetings are nearly over, and the closing riddle of the garland leaves him to supply the word marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","iravu","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 122","lb":"NT122","poet":"செங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத்தோழி தலைவிக்கு உரைப்பாளாய்த் தலைவன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகத்தோழி தலைவிக்கு உரைப்பாளாய்த் தலைவன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["செந்தினை","செந்தினை",2,"unavu","scene",""],["மௌவலும்","மௌவல்",4,"maram","scene",""],["உரும்","உரும்",5,"pira","scene","thunder"],["கண்ணி","கண்ணி",11,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:123":{"book":"natrinai","num":124,"t":["உரையாய் வாழி தோழி இருங் கழி","இரை ஆர் குருகின் நிரை பறைத் தொழுதி","வாங்கு மடற் குடம்பை தூங்கு இருள் துவன்றும்","பெண்ணை ஓங்கிய வெண் மணற் படப்பை","கானல் ஆயமொடு காலைக் குற்ற","கள் கமழ் அலர தண் நறுங் காவி","அம் பகை நெறித் தழை அணிபெறத் தைஇ","வரி புனை சிற்றில் பரி சிறந்து ஓடி","புலவுத் திரை உதைத்த கொடுந் தாட் கண்டல்","சேர்ப்பு ஏர் ஈர் அளை அலவன் பார்க்கும்","சிறு விளையாடலும் அழுங்கி","நினைக்குறு பெருந் துயரம் ஆகிய நோயே"],"p":"","r":"With the hero outside, the friend asks the girl to speak: where flocks of fed herons crowd at dusk into nests among the curved fronds of the tall palmyra on the white sand, even their old games of the morning, weaving leaf skirts of fragrant cool lilies with the grove companions, running to the little striped play house, watching the crab at its wet hole under the kandal, have stopped, and the memory has become a great affliction.","n":"The list of games is the real message to the listener: the girl who was a child of the grove in the morning is now unable to play, so the man who caused it must act; the only creatures with a settled home at nightfall are the herons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kalaviyal","noy","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 123","lb":"NT123","poet":"காஞ்சிப் புலவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவன்சிறைப்புறத்தானாக தோழி தலைவிக்கு உரைப்பாளாய்ச்சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைவன்சிறைப்புறத்தானாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",2,"pul","iraicchi",""],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",4,"maram","scene",""],["காவி","காவி",6,"maram","scene",""],["தழை","தழை",7,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["கண்டல்","கண்டல்",9,"maram","scene",""],["அலவன்","அலவன்",10,"pira","scene","crab"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","குருகின்","not_confirmed","0/1","even the birds of the backwater return to a shared home at night, while the man still leaves the girl without one"]],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:124":{"book":"natrinai","num":125,"t":["ஒன்று இல் காலை அன்றில் போலப்","புலம்பு கொண்டு உறையும் புன்கண் வாழ்க்கை","யானும் ஆற்றேன் அதுதானும் வந்தன்று","நீங்கல் வாழியர் ஐய ஈங்கை","முகை வீ அதிரல் மோட்டு மணல் எக்கர்","நவ்வி நோன் குளம்பு அழுந்தென வெள்ளி","உருக்குறு கொள்கலம் கடுப்ப விருப்புறத்","தெண் நீர்க் குமிழி இழிதரும்","தண்ணீர் ததைஇ நின்ற பொழுதே"],"p":"","r":"Told that the hero means to leave, the friend begs him not to go: she cannot bear a life of grief like the anril bird alone without its mate, and the season has come when the eengai buds, the athiral flowers on the sand dune, the deer's hooves sink in it, and bubbles slide over the cool pooled water like a vessel of molten silver.","n":"The friend says யானும் ஆற்றேன், I myself cannot bear it, speaking in the first person for the woman; the season is offered as proof that a departure now would break the rule that men return before the rains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 124","lb":"NT124","poet":"மோசி கண்ணத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட தோழி தலைவற்கு உரைத்தது","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,"பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அன்றில்","அன்றில்",1,"pul","simile",""],["ஈங்கை","ஈங்கை",4,"maram","scene",""],["அதிரல்","அதிரல்",5,"maram","scene",""],["நவ்வி","நவ்வி",6,"maa","scene","deer"],["தண்ணீர்","தண்ணீர்",9,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:125":{"book":"natrinai","num":126,"t":["இரை தேர் எண்கின் பகு வாய் ஏற்றை","கொடு வரிப் புற்றம் வாய்ப்ப வாங்கி","நல் அரா நடுங்க உரறி கொல்லன்","ஊது உலைக் குருகின் உள் உயிர்த்து அகழும்","நடு நாள் வருதல் அஞ்சுதும் யாம் என","வரைந்து வரல் இரக்குவம் ஆயின் நம் மலை","நல் நாள் வதுவை கூடி நீடு இன்று","நம்மொடு செல்வர்மன் தோழி மெல்ல","வேங்கைக் கண்ணியர் எருது எறி களமர்","நிலம் கண்டன்ன அகன் கண் பாசறை","மென் தினை நெடும் போர் புரிமார்","துஞ்சு களிறு எடுப்பும் தம் பெருங் கல் நாட்டே"],"p":"","r":"The friend comforts the girl as the marriage is delayed: if we tell him we fear his coming at midnight, when the hungry male bear tears at the striped anthill, roaring so the snake trembles and breathing like a smith's bellows as it digs, and beg him to come to marry, then after a wedding on a good day on our hill he will not delay but take us to his own great hill country, where men in vengai garlands driving oxen on a floor as wide as open land wake the sleeping elephant as they stack the millet.","n":"The bear at the anthill is the danger of his night road, and a reader may hear in it the man's own hungry coming (seeking at midnight, frightening those within); only one pair holds clearly, so it is left as scene, a near miss for ullurai. The poem's comfort lies in the contrast of nights: the fearful midnight here, the noisy harvest that wakes an elephant in his country.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","iravu","acham","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 125","lb":"NT125","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரைவு நீட்டிப்ப ஆற்றாளாய தலைவியைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு நீட்டிப்ப"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["எண்கின்","எண்கு",1,"maa","scene","bear"],["புற்றம்","புற்று",2,"pira","scene",""],["அரா","அரா",3,"pira","scene","snake"],["கொல்லன்","கொல்லன்",3,"seythi","simile",""],["வேங்கைக்","வேங்கை",9,"maram","ornament",""],["எருது","எருது",9,"maa","scene",""],["களமர்","களமர்",9,"seythi","scene",""],["தினை","தினை",11,"unavu","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",12,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:126":{"book":"natrinai","num":127,"t":["பைங் காய் நல் இடம் ஒரீஇய செங்காய்க்","கருங் களி ஈந்தின் வெண் புறக் களரி","இடு நீறு ஆடிய கடு நடை ஒருத்தல்","ஆள் பெறல் நசைஇ நாள் சுரம் விலங்கி","துனைதரும் வம்பலர்க் காணாது அச் சினம்","பனைக் கான்று ஆறும் பாழ் நாட்டு அத்தம்","இறந்து செய் பொருளும் இன்பம் தரும் எனின்","இளமையின் சிறந்த வளமையும் இல்லை","இளமை கழிந்த பின்றை வளமை","காமம் தருதலும் இன்றே அதனால்","நில்லாப் பொருட் பிணிச் சேறி","வல்லே நெஞ்சம் வாய்க்க நின் வினையே"],"p":"","r":"The hero rebukes the heart that is set on wealth: across the ruined land where a dust-bathed bull elephant, hungry to find a man, blocks the path and, seeing no travellers, cools its rage on a palmyra, wealth won by crossing may give pleasure, but no wealth surpasses youth, and once youth has passed wealth cannot bring love, so go alone, heart, with your sickness for wealth that does not last, and may your business prosper.","n":"The argument turns on a reversal of the usual order: wealth is called நில்லாப் பொருள், the thing that does not stay, and youth the true வளமை; the last line's blessing on the heart's business is ironic, since the man himself stays.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","selvam","nilaiyaamai","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 126","lb":"NT126","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் வலித்த நெஞ்சினைத் தலைவன் நெருங்கிச் செலவு அழுங்கியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"செலவு அழுங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஈந்தின்","ஈந்து",2,"maram","scene",""],["ஒருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",3,"maa","scene","bull elephant"],["வம்பலர்க்","வம்பலர்",5,"seythi","scene","travellers"],["பனைக்","பனை",6,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:127":{"book":"natrinai","num":128,"t":["இருங் கழி துழைஇய ஈர்ம் புற நாரை","இற எறி திவலையின் பனிக்கும் பாக்கத்து","உவன் வரின் எவனோ பாண பேதை","கொழு மீன் ஆர்கைச் செழு நகர் நிறைந்த","கல்லாக் கதவர் தன் ஐயர் ஆகவும்","வண்டல் ஆயமொடு பண்டு தான் ஆடிய","ஈனாப் பாவை தலையிட்டு ஓரும்","மெல்லம் புலம்பன் அன்றியும்","செல்வாம் என்னும் கானலானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend turns away the bard: in this hamlet where the wet-backed stork that searched the backwater shivers in the flung spray, what if your man comes? The simple girl, whose brothers are the rough unlettered men who fill the rich fish-eating town, still takes up the doll she played with in the sand long ago and ponders it, and says 'let us go' only to the grove, not to the lord of the soft shore.","n":"The refusal is made by calling the girl a child: the doll and the sand games say she is not yet a woman to be courted, and her fierce brothers say the courtship is dangerous. Refusing the bard's entry is usually marutham's oodal; here it is set wholly in shore things.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kalaviyal","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 127","lb":"NT127","poet":"சீத்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பாணற்குத் தோழி வாயில் மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","paanan",null,null,"வாயில் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நாரை","நாரை",1,"pul","scene",""],["மீன்","மீன்",4,"unavu","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:128":{"book":"natrinai","num":129,"t":["பகல் எரி சுடரின் மேனி சாயவும்","பாம்பு ஊர் மதியின் நுதல் ஒளி கரப்பவும்","எனக்கு நீ உரையாயாயினை நினக்கு யான்","உயிர் பகுத்தன்ன மாண்பினேன் ஆகலின்","அது கண்டிசினால் யானே என்று நனி","அழுதல் ஆன்றிசின் ஆயிழை ஒலி குரல்","ஏனல் காவலினிடை உற்று ஒருவன்","கண்ணியன் கழலன் தாரன் தண்ணெனச்","சிறு புறம் கவையினனாக அதற்கொண்டு","அஃதே நினைந்த நெஞ்சமொடு","இஃது ஆகின்று யான் உற்ற நோயே","குறை நேர்ந்த தோழி தலைவி குறை நயப்பக் கூறியது"],"p":"","r":"The friend says the girl's body has paled like a lamp burning by day and her forehead's light is hidden like the moon the snake swallows, yet she has not told her who is like half of her own life, so let her stop weeping; and the girl answers that while she guarded the millet a man with a garland, an anklet and a chain came and coolly clasped her small back, and since then, with a heart full of that, this is the sickness she has.","n":"Two similes of light put out by day and by eclipse make the friend's case before the girl speaks; the confession is all objects (garland, anklet, chain) and one touch, the clasp of the small back, which is the whole of the event.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","pasalai","natpu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 128","lb":"NT128","poet":"நற்சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழிக்குத் தலைவி அறத்தொடு நின்றதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","அறத்தொடு நின்றதூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",2,"pira","simile",""],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",7,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கண்ணியன்","கண்ணி",8,"maram","ornament","garland"],["தாரன்","தார்",8,"maram","ornament","chest garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:129":{"book":"natrinai","num":130,"t":["பெரு நகை கேளாய் தோழி காதலர்","ஒரு நாள் கழியினும் உயிர் வேறுபடூஉம்","பொம்மல் ஓதி நம் இவண் ஒழியச்","செல்ப என்ப தாமே சென்று","தம் வினை முற்றி வரூஉம் வரை நம் மனை","வாழ்தும் என்ப நாமே அதன்தலை","கேழ் கிளர் உத்தி அரவுத் தலை பனிப்ப","படு மழை உருமின் உரற்று குரல்","நடு நாள் யாமத்தும் தமியம் கேட்டே"],"p":"","r":"Hear a great joke, friend: the lover whose life changed if a single day passed without us says he will go, leaving us here with our thick hair, and says that we will live on in our house until he finishes his work and returns, and that while we hear alone, at midnight, the roar of thunder from the pouring clouds that makes the bright-hooded snake tremble.","n":"The joke is the clash of his two sayings: the man who could not survive one day apart now proposes that we survive his whole absence; the closing midnight thunder is what 'we will live' will actually mean.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","iravu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 129","lb":"NT129","poet":"ஒளவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட தோழி தலைமகளை முகம் புக்கது","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"முகம் புக்கது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"],["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["அரவுத்","அரவு",7,"pira","scene","snake"],["உருமின்","உரும்",8,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:130":{"book":"natrinai","num":131,"t":["வடு இன்று நிறைந்த மான் தேர்த்தெண் கண்","மடிவாய்த் தண்ணுமை நடுவண் ஆர்ப்ப","கோலின் எறிந்து காலைத் தோன்றிய","செந் நீர்ப் பொது வினைச் செம்மல் மூதூர்த்","தமது செய் வாழ்க்கையின் இனியது உண்டோ","எனை விருப்புடையர் ஆயினும் நினைவிலர்","நேர்ந்த நெஞ்சும் நெகிழ்ந்த தோளும்","வாடிய வரியும் நோக்கி நீடாது","எவன் செய்தனள் இப் பேர் அஞர் உறுவி என்று","ஒரு நாள் கூறின்றுமிலரே விரிநீர்","வையக வரையளவு இறந்த","எவ்வ நோய் பிறிது உயவுத் துணை இன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Wasting in separation and unmoved by comfort, the woman says that for men of the old town of the chief, where the drum sounds at the centre and the morning's common work begins, nothing is sweeter than the life of their own work; however much they love, they do not think of us, and not once did he look at her yielding heart, loosened shoulders and faded lines and ask what this suffering one will do, so her pain, past the bounds of the sea-girt world, has no companion.","n":"The complaint is general before it is personal: men find their own work sweetest; then it narrows to the one sentence he never said. The opening lines of chariot, drum and public work are hard to construe and the sense of 'red-water common work' is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vinai","melivu","thunai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 130","lb":"NT130","poet":"நெய்தல் தத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைவி வன்புறை எதிரழிந்து சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"வன்புறை எதிரழிந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மான்","மான்",1,"maa","scene","horse"],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",2,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:131":{"book":"natrinai","num":132,"t":["ஆடிய தொழிலும் அல்கிய பொழிலும்","உள்ளல் ஆகா உயவு நெஞ்சமொடு","ஊடலும் உடையமோ உயர் மணற் சேர்ப்ப","திரை முதிர் அரைய தடந் தாள் தாழைச்","சுறவு மருப்பு அன்ன முட் தோடு ஒசிய","இறவு ஆர் இனக் குருகு இறைகொள இருக்கும்","நறவு மகிழ் இருக்கை நல் தேர்ப் பெரியன்","கள் கமழ் பொறையாறு அன்ன என்","நல் தோள் நெகிழ மறத்தல் நுமக்கே"],"p":"","r":"When the newly married hero praises the friend for keeping the girl from wasting, she answers: lord of the high sand shore, could we, whose sad hearts cannot even recall the games we played and the groves we rested in, have sulked at all? It was yours to forget, so that my shoulders, fair as Poraiyaru, fragrant with toddy, the town of Periyan of the fine chariot where the herons settle on the screwpine's shark-tooth leaves, grew loose.","n":"She refuses the praise by refusing the credit: there was no sulking because there was not even strength to remember, and the wasting she prevented was his doing. She says என் நல் தோள், my shoulders, speaking as the girl.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","pirivu","melivu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 131","lb":"NT131","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"மணமனையில் பிற்றை ஞான்று புக்க தோழியைத் தலைவன் வேறுபடாமை ஆற்றுவித்தாய் பெரியை காண் என்றாற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","மணமனையில் பிற்றை ஞான்று புக்க"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாழைச்","தாழை",4,"maram","scene",""],["சுறவு","சுறவு",5,"pira","simile","shark"],["இறவு","இறவு",6,"pira","scene","prawn"],["குருகு","குருகு",6,"pul","scene",""],["நறவு","நறவு",7,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"]],"en":[["patron","பெரியன்",7],["town","பொறையாறு",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:132":{"book":"natrinai","num":133,"t":["பேர் ஊர் துஞ்சும் யாரும் இல்லை","திருந்து வாய்ச் சுறவம் நீர் கான்று ஒய்யெனப்","பெருந் தெரு உதிர்தரு பெயலுறு தண் வளி","போர் அமை கதவப் புரை தொறும் தூவ","கூர் எயிற்று எகினம் நடுங்கும் நல் நகர்ப்","பயில்படை நிவந்த பல் பூஞ் சேக்கை","அயலும் மாண் சிறையதுவே அதன்தலை","காப்புடை வாயில் போற்று ஓ என்னும்","யாமம் கொள்பவர் நெடு நா ஒண் மணி","ஒன்று எறி பாணியின் இரட்டும்","இன்றுகொல் அளியேன் பொன்றும் நாளே"],"p":"","r":"The great town sleeps and no one is awake to help; the rain-laden cold wind, spilling from the shark-mouthed spouts onto the broad street, sprays through every chink of the strong doors so that the sharp-toothed dog trembles; the high flowered bed in the fine mansion has guards beside it, and above all the watchmen crying 'guard the gate' strike the long-tongued bell in double beats; is today, wretched as I am, the day I die?","n":"The whole town is asleep but the house is not: wind, dog, guards and the watchmen's bell are a ring of wakefulness around one bed, so the sleeping town gives no help. The shark-mouthed spouts are carved work, not a creature of the shore, so the poem has no shore things.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","noy","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 132","lb":"NT132","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"காப்பு மிகுதிக்கண்ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைவிக்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","காப்பு மிகுதிக்கண்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"],["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["எகினம்","எகினம்",5,"maa","scene","dog"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:133":{"book":"natrinai","num":134,"t":["தோளே தொடி கொட்பு ஆனா கண்ணே","வாள் ஈர் வடியின் வடிவு இழந்தனவே","நுதலும் பசலை பாயின்று திதலைச்","சில் பொறி அணிந்த பல் காழ் அல்குல்","மணி ஏர் ஐம்பால் மாயோட்கு என்று","வெவ் வாய்ப் பெண்டிர் கவ்வை தூற்ற","நாம் உறு துயரம் செய்யலர் என்னும்","காமுறு தோழி காதல்அம் கிளவி","இரும்பு செய் கொல்லன் வெவ் உலைத் தெளித்த","தோய் மடற் சில் நீர் போல","நோய் மலி நெஞ்சிற்கு ஏமம் ஆம் சிறிதே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her comforting friend that her bangles keep slipping, her eyes have lost their sword-cut shape, pallor has spread over her forehead, and cruel-mouthed women spread talk about the dark girl with spotted skin and gem-bright hair; the loving friend's dear word that he will not cause us suffering is, to her sick heart, a small comfort, like the few drops a smith sprinkles from a dipped frond onto his hot furnace.","n":"The simile measures the comfort exactly: a smith's sprinkle does not cool the furnace, it only checks it for a moment, and the closing சிறிதே, a little, is the poem's last word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","alar","melivu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 133","lb":"NT133","poet":"நற்றமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரைவிடை வைத்துப்பிரிவு ஆற்றாளாய தலைவி வற்புறுத்தும் தோழிக்குச்சொல்லியது","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"வரைவிடை வைத்துப்பிரிவு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["கொல்லன்","கொல்லன்",9,"seythi","simile",""],["மடற்","மடல்",10,"maram","simile","frond"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:134":{"book":"natrinai","num":135,"t":["இனிதின் இனிது தலைப்படும் என்பது","இதுகொல் வாழி தோழி காதலர்","வரு குறி செய்த வரையகச் சிறு தினைச்","செவ் வாய்ப் பாசினம் கடீஇயர் கொடிச்சி","அவ் வாய்த் தட்டையொடு அவணை ஆக என","ஏயள்மன் யாயும் நுந்தை வாழியர்","அம் மா மேனி நிரை தொடிக் குறுமகள்","செல்லாயோ நின் முள் எயிறு உண்கு என","மெல்லிய இனிய கூறலின் யான் அஃது","ஒல்லேன் போல உரையாடுவலே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl, who feared she would be shut indoors, that this must be what is meant by sweet things coming upon sweet: the mother herself told her, 'mountain girl, go with your rattle to drive the red-beaked parrots from the millet on the hill where the lover set the sign of meeting', and the father, long may he live, said softly, 'won't you go, little one of bangles, let me kiss your sharp teeth', while she answered as if unwilling.","n":"The comedy is that the parents send the girl to the very field where the lover waits, and the friend can only pretend reluctance; the 'sweet upon sweet' proverb is both the joy of the errand and the relief that the confinement feared has not come.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 134","lb":"NT134","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இற்செறிப்பார் என ஆற்றாளாய தலைவியை அ·து இலர் என்பது பட தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","இற்செறிப்பார் என"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"],["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["தினைச்","தினை",3,"unavu","scene",""],["பாசினம்","பாசினம்",4,"pul","scene","parrots"],["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",4,"seythi","scene","hill girl"],["தட்டையொடு","தட்டை",5,"seythi","scene","rattle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:135":{"book":"natrinai","num":136,"t":["தூங்கல் ஓலை ஓங்கு மடற் பெண்ணை","மா அரை புதைத்த மணல் மலி முன்றில்","வரையாத் தாரம் வரு விருந்து அயரும்","தண் குடி வாழ்நர் அம் குடிச் சீறூர்","இனிது மன்றம்ம தானே பனி படு","பல் சுரம் உழந்த நல்கூர் பரிய","முழங்கு திரைப் புது மணல் அழுந்தக் கொட்கும்","வால் உளைப் பொலிந்த புரவித்","தேரோர் நம்மொடு நகாஅ ஊங்கே"],"p":"","r":"With the hero outside, the friend says their little seaside village, of sand-filled yards where palmyras with hanging leaves rise and the people of cool homes entertain arriving guests with gifts unstinted, was sweet indeed before the men of the chariot, whose white-plumed horses, worn from many dewy tracts, wheel and sink in the new sand of the roaring waves, began laughing with us.","n":"The praise of a village that welcomes every guest is a pointed reproach: the man came as a guest and laughed with them but has not entered as a bridegroom, and since his visits the sweet village has become a place of talk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","virundhu","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 135","lb":"NT135","poet":"கதப்பிள்ளையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரைவு நீட்டிப்ப அலர்ஆம் எனக் கவன்ற தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகச்சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","வரைவு நீட்டிப்ப"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",1,"maram","scene",""],["தாரம்","தாரம்",3,"pira","gift_food","gifts"],["புரவித்","புரவி",8,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:136":{"book":"natrinai","num":137,"t":["திருந்து கோல் எல் வளை வேண்டி யான் அழவும்","அரும் பிணி உறுநர்க்கு வேட்டது கொடாஅது","மருந்து ஆய்ந்து கொடுத்த அறவோன் போல","என்னை வாழிய பலவே பன்னிய","மலை கெழு நாடனொடு நம்மிடைச் சிறிய","தலைப்பிரிவு உண்மை அறிவான் போல","நீப்ப நீங்காது வரின் வரை அமைந்து","தோள் பழி மறைக்கும் உதவிப்","போக்கு இல் பொலந் தொடி செறீஇயோனே"],"p":"","r":"With the hero outside, the girl tells her friend that when she wept for well-made bright bangles, her father, long may he live, as though he knew of the brief separation between them and the hill lord, did as a righteous physician does who, refusing the gravely sick what they crave, chooses a medicine and gives it: he fitted on her golden bangles that cannot slip off, a help that hides the blame her shoulders would tell.","n":"She speaks for the man outside: the bangles that slip would announce her wasting, and the father's gold bangle, which cannot slip, hides it; the cure he chose treats the sign, not the sickness, which only the man can cure. The phrase நீப்ப நீங்காது வரின் வரை அமைந்து is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","melivu","kalaviyal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 136","lb":"NT136","poet":"நற்றங் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத்தலைவி தோழிக்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகத்தலைவி தோழிக்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வளை","வளை",1,"pira","ornament","conch bangle"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:137":{"book":"natrinai","num":138,"t":["தண்ணிய கமழும் தாழ் இருங் கூந்தல்","தட மென் பணைத் தோள் மட நல்லோள்வயின்","பிரியச் சூழ்ந்தனை ஆயின் அரியது ஒன்று","எய்தினை வாழிய நெஞ்சே செவ் வரை","அருவி ஆன்ற நீர் இல் நீள் இடை","கயந் தலை மடப் பிடி உயங்கு பசி களைஇயர்","பெருங் களிறு தொலைத்த முடத் தாள் ஓமை","அருஞ் சுரம் செல்வோர்க்கு அல்குநிழல் ஆகும்","குன்ற வைப்பின் கானம்","சென்று சேண் அகறல் வல்லிய நீயே"],"p":"","r":"The hero tells his heart that if it plans to leave the young woman of cool fragrant hanging hair and broad soft shoulders, it has achieved a rare thing indeed, being strong enough to go far through the hill-set forest of the long waterless stretch where the falls of the red rocks have ceased, where the crooked-trunked omai that a great tusker broke to ease the wasting hunger of his young cow-elephant becomes resting shade for those crossing the hard track.","n":"The praise of the heart is ironic: the 'rare thing' it has achieved is hardness, and the road it would travel shows a beast that breaks a tree to feed its mate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 137","lb":"NT137","poet":"பெருங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைவன் செலவு அழுங்கியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"செலவு அழுங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",5,"pira","absent","waterfall"],["பிடி","பிடி",6,"maa","iraicchi","cow-elephant"],["களிறு","களிறு",7,"maa","iraicchi","tusker"],["ஓமை","ஓமை",7,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","களிறு","confirmed","1/1","even the elephant in the waterless land feeds and stays by its mate, so the man who leaves his wife is harder than the beast"]],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:138":{"book":"natrinai","num":139,"t":["உவர் விளை உப்பின் குன்று போல்குப்பை","மலை உய்த்துப் பகரும் நிலையா வாழ்க்கை","கணம் கொள் உமணர் உயங்குவயின் ஒழித்த","பண் அழி பழம் பார் வெண் குருகு ஈனும்","தண்ணம் துறைவன் முன் நாள் நம்மொடு","பாசடைக் கலித்த கணைக் கால் நெய்தல்","பூவுடன் நெறிதரு தொடலை தைஇ","கண் அறிவுடைமை அல்லது நுண் வினை","இழை அணி அல்குல் விழவு ஆடு மகளிர்","முழங்கு திரை இன் சீர் தூங்கும்","அழுங்கல் மூதூர் அறிந்தன்றோ இன்றே"],"p":"","r":"With the hero outside, the friend tells the girl troubled by talk: the lord of the cool ford, where the white heron lays its eggs on the broken old cart floor left by bands of salt merchants who carry heaps of salt like hills up to the mountains to sell and abandon what fails them, once wove with us a garland of thick-stemmed neythal flowers; it was known only to our eyes then, but does the noisy old town, where festive women in fine girdles sway to the sweet beat of the waves, not know it today?","n":"The heron nesting in the cart the wandering merchants abandoned could be read as the girl left by a passing man, but no second pair holds, so it stays scene. The contrast is between kinds of knowing: once only eyes knew, now the whole festive town does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 138","lb":"NT138","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"அலர் ஆயிற்று என ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகட்குத் தலைவன் சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","அலர் ஆயிற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["உப்பின்","உப்பு",1,"pira","simile",""],["உமணர்","உமணர்",3,"seythi","scene","salt merchants"],["குருகு","குருகு",4,"pul","scene","heron"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",6,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:139":{"book":"natrinai","num":140,"t":["உலகிற்கு ஆணியாகப் பலர் தொழ","பல வயின் நிலைஇய குன்றின் கோடுதோறு","ஏயினை உரைஇயரோ பெருங் கலி எழிலி","படுமலை நின்ற நல் யாழ் வடி நரம்பு","எழீஇயன்ன உறையினை முழவின்","மண் ஆர் கண்ணின் இம்மென இமிரும்","வணர்ந்து ஒலி கூந்தல் மாஅயோளொடு","புணர்ந்து இனிது நுகர்ந்த சாரல் நல் ஊர்","விரவு மலர் உதிர வீசி","இரவுப் பெயல் பொழிந்த உதவியோயே"],"p":"","r":"Back from his work and lying in the bedchamber, the hero blesses the great roaring cloud: may it spread over every peak of the hills set in many places as the world's linchpin and worshipped by many, for with drops like the plucked fine string of a lute tuned to the padumalai mode, humming like the clay-smeared face of the drum, it scattered mixed flowers and poured night rain on the slope town where he lay embraced with the dark woman of thick curled hair and enjoyed her sweetly.","n":"A blessing is a thanksgiving: the cloud is praised as a helper (உதவியோயே) for a private night, and the poem's music images (lute string, drum) make the rain an accompaniment to the reunion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","punarchi","nanri","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 139","lb":"NT139","poet":"பெருங்கௌசிகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைவன் வினைமுற்றி வந்து பள்ளி இடத்தானாக பெய்த மழையை வாழ்த்தியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","பெய்த மழையை வாழ்த்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["எழிலி","எழிலி",3,"pira","scene","cloud"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",4,"yaazh","simile",""],["முழவின்","முழவு",5,"parai","simile",""],["மலர்","மலர்",9,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:140":{"book":"natrinai","num":141,"t":["கொண்டல் மா மழை குடக்கு ஏர்பு குழைத்த","சிறு கோல் இணர பெருந் தண் சாந்தம்","வகை சேர் ஐம்பால் தகை பெற வாரி","புலர்விடத்து உதிர்த்த துகள் படு கூழைப்","பெருங் கண் ஆயம் உவப்ப தந்தை","நெடுந் தேர் வழங்கும் நிலவு மணல் முற்றத்து","பந்தொடு பெயரும் பரிவிலாட்டி","அருளினும் அருளாள் ஆயினும் பெரிது அழிந்து","பின்னிலை முனியல்மா நெஞ்சே என்னதூஉம்","அருந் துயர் அவலம் தீர்க்கும்","மருந்து பிறிது இல்லை யான் உற்ற நோய்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"Refused, the hero tells his heart not to weary of following: the merciless girl who combs into five parts the sandal paste of the cool tree fed by the eastern rain clouds, sheds its dust when it dries on her cropped hair, and goes with her ball across the moonlit sand yard where her father's tall chariot runs, to her big-eyed companions' delight; whether she is kind or not, there is no other medicine to end the hard grief of the sickness he has.","n":"The girl he cannot win is shown entirely at play, combing, scattering dust, chasing a ball, and the word பரிவிலாட்டி, the woman without pity, is fitted to a child who does not yet know she has been asked.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","marundhu","nenju","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 140","lb":"NT140","poet":"பூதங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"குறை மறுக்கப்பட்ட தலைவன் தன் நெஞ்சினை நெருங்கியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","குறை மறுக்கப்பட்ட"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["மழை","மழை",1,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",2,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:7"},"nt:141":{"book":"natrinai","num":142,"t":["இருஞ் சேறு ஆடிய கொடுங் கவுள் கய வாய்","மாரி யானையின் மருங்குல் தீண்டி","பொரி அரை ஞெமிர்ந்த புழற் காய்க் கொன்றை","நீடிய சடையோடு ஆடா மேனிக்","குன்று உறை தவசியர் போல பல உடன்","என்றூழ் நீள் இடைப் பொற்பத் தோன்றும்","அருஞ் சுரம் எளியமன் நினக்கே பருந்து பட","பாண்டிலொடு பொருத பல் பிணர்த் தடக் கை","ஏந்து கோட்டு யானை இசை வெங் கிள்ளி","வம்பு அணி உயர் கொடி அம்பர் சூழ்ந்த","அரிசில் அம் தண் அறல் அன்ன இவள்","விரி ஒலி கூந்தல் விட்டு அமைகலனே"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart, bent on going for wealth, that the hard dry tract where long-podded konrai trees, rubbed by rain-season elephants muddy from wallowing, stand together in the heat like unbathed hill ascetics with long matted locks, would be easy for it; but he will not leave her spreading, thick hair, like the cool dark ripples of the Arisil that runs round Ambar of the tall flags, town of Killi, famous for his tusked elephants that fought chariots while kites wheeled above.","n":"மன் in line 7 makes the ease counterfactual: the road would be easy, if he could go. The ascetic simile turns the konrai, a rain-season tree of the mullai, into figures of renunciation standing in the waste, which is exactly what he declines to become.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 141","lb":"NT141","poet":"சல்லியங்குமரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் கடைக்கூட்டிய நெஞ்சிற்குத் தலைவன் சொல்லிச் செலவு அழுங்கியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"செலவு அழுங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானையின்","யானை",2,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",3,"maram","simile",""],["யானை","யானை",9,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["king","கிள்ளி",9],["town","அம்பர்",10],["river","அரிசில்",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:142":{"book":"natrinai","num":143,"t":["வான் இகுபு சொரிந்த வயங்கு பெயற்கடை நாள்","பாணி கொண்ட பல் கால் மெல் உறி","ஞெலி கோல் கலப் பை அதளொடு சுருக்கி","பறிப் புறத்து இட்ட பால் நொடை இடையன்","நுண் பல் துவலை ஒரு திறம் நனைப்ப","தண்டு கால் வைத்த ஒடுங்கு நிலை மடி விளி","சிறு தலைத் தொழுதி ஏமார்த்து அல்கும்","புறவினதுவே பொய்யா யாணர்","அல்லில் ஆயினும் விருந்து வரின் உவக்கும்","முல்லை சான்ற கற்பின்","மெல் இயற் குறுமகள் உறைவின் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"Returning with his task done, the man tells his charioteer that her town lies in the woodland of unfailing plenty, where at the end of the rains a milk-trading herdsman, his rope-sling, fire-drill and pot bundled with a hide on his back, stands on one leg propped on his staff in the drizzle, whistling through folded lips to settle his small-headed flock; there lives the gentle young woman whose chastity befits the mullai, who rejoices when a guest comes even at night.","n":"The herdsman holding still in the rain to keep his flock together is the picture of patient keeping that the last three lines give to the wife; and the praise he chooses is her welcome to guests at night, a householder's virtue, not her beauty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","virundhu","ilvaazhkkai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 142","lb":"NT142","poet":"இடைக்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி மீளும்தலைமகன் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றி மீளும்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["இடையன்","இடையன்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["தொழுதி","தொழுதி",7,"maa","scene","flock of sheep"],["ஞெலி கோல்","ஞெலி கோல்",3,"pira","scene","fire-drill"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",10,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:143":{"book":"natrinai","num":144,"t":["ஐதே கம்ம யானே ஒய்யென","தரு மணல் ஞெமிரிய திரு நகர் முற்றத்து","ஓரை ஆயமும் நொச்சியும் காண்தொறும்","நீர் வார் கண்ணேன் கலுழும் என்னினும்","கிள்ளையும் கிளை எனக் கூஉம் இளையோள்","வழு இலள் அம்ம தானே குழீஇ","அம்பல் மூதூர் அலர் வாய்ப் பெண்டிர்","இன்னா இன் உரை கேட்ட சில் நாள்","அறியேன் போல உயிரேன்","நறிய நாறும் நின் கதுப்பு என்றேனே"],"p":"","r":"The mother, faint, says that every time she sees the swept sand of the fine courtyard, the girl's playmates and the nochchi, her eyes run with tears; yet the girl who called even her parrot kin is without fault: in the few days when she heard the cruel sweet talk of the gossiping women gathered in the old town, she lived on as if she did not know, and said only, 'your hair smells fragrant'.","n":"The last line is a confession: the scent of her daughter's hair was the sign of a lover, and the mother, already hearing the gossip, let it pass with a remark. She clears the girl (வழு இலள்) and keeps the fault for herself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal","alar","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 143","lb":"NT143","poet":"கண்ணகாரன் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மனை மருட்சி","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,null,"மனை மருட்சி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நொச்சியும்","நொச்சி",3,"maram","scene",""],["கிள்ளையும்","கிள்ளை",5,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:144":{"book":"natrinai","num":145,"t":["பெருங் களிறு உழுவை தாக்கலின் இரும் பிடி","கருவி மா மழையின் அரவம் அஞ்சுபு","போது ஏர் உண் கண் கலுழவும் ஏதில்","பேதை நெஞ்சம் கவலை கவற்ற","ஈங்கு ஆகின்றால் தோழி பகுவாய்ப்","பிணவுப் புலி வழங்கும் அணங்கு அருங் கவலை","அவிர் அறல் ஒழுகும் விரை செலல் கான் யாற்றுக்","கரை அருங் குட்டம் தமியர் நீந்தி","விரவு மலர் பொறித்த தோளர்","இரவின் வருதல் அறியாதேற்கே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening nearby, the woman tells her friend that as the dark she-elephant, when a tiger attacks her great bull, shrinks from the uproar as from the roar of massed rain-clouds, so her own flower-like eyes stream and her foolish heart frets, now that she knows he comes by night, flowers pressed on his shoulders, alone across the hard forked paths where the gaping tigress prowls and the deep pools of the fast forest stream are hard to cross.","n":"Lines 1 to 2 can also be read as the beginning of the scene she fears rather than a matched picture; either way the she-elephant's fear for her mate stands next to the woman's fear for hers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 144","lb":"NT144","poet":"கச்சிப்பேட்டுப் பெருந்தச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"ஆற்றது ஏதத்திற்குக்கவன்று சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைவி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","ஆற்றது ஏதத்திற்குக்கவன்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",1,"maa","iraicchi","bull elephant"],["பிடி","பிடி",1,"maa","iraicchi","she-elephant"],["உழுவை","உழுவை",1,"maa","scene",""],["மழையின்","மழை",2,"pira","simile","rain-cloud"],["புலி","புலி",6,"maa","scene",""],["கான் யாற்றுக்","கான் யாறு",7,"pira","scene",""],["மலர்","மலர்",9,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பிடி","confirmed","1/2","the woman's fear for the man crossing tiger country by night is the fear even a beast feels for its mate, so it is love, not weakness, and he should end it"],["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:145":{"book":"natrinai","num":146,"t":["இருங் கழி பொருத ஈர வெண் மணல்","மாக் கொடி அடும்பின் மா இதழ் அலரி","கூந்தல் மகளிர் கோதைக் கூட்டும்","காமர் கொண்கன் நாம் வெங் கேண்மை","ஐது ஏய்ந்தில்லா ஊங்கும் நம்மொடு","புணர்ந்தனன் போல உணரக் கூறி","தான் யாங்கு என்னும் அறன் இல் அன்னை","யான் எழில் அறிதலும் உரியள் நீயும் நம்","பராரைப் புன்னைச் சேரி மெல்ல","நள்ளென் கங்குலும் வருமரோ","அம்ம வாழி தோழி அவர் தேர் மணிக் குரலே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend tells the woman that their unjust mother, even before their bond with the handsome shore lord, where on the wet white sand by the dark backwater women add the large purple blossoms of the adumbu creeper to their garlands, had grown at all close, spoke as if she had already lain with him and asked where she had been; she has a right to read the change in their looks, and now even in the dead of night the bells of his chariot come softly to their punnai-lined street.","n":"The midnight chariot bells are told to the man who drives the chariot: the house now hears what the mother only suspected, and the only way out of the suspicion is marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","varaivu","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 145","lb":"NT145","poet":"நம்பி குட்டுவன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி வந்து தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாக தோழி வரைவுகடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","வரைவுகடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கழி","கழி",1,"pira","scene","backwater"],["அடும்பின்","அடும்பு",2,"maram","ornament",""],["புன்னைச்","புன்னை",9,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:146":{"book":"natrinai","num":147,"t":["வில்லாப் பூவின் கண்ணி சூடி","நல் ஏமுறுவல் என பல் ஊர் திரிதரு","நெடு மாப் பெண்ணை மடல் மானோயே","கடன் அறி மன்னர் குடை நிழற் போலப்","பெருந் தண்ணென்ற மர நிழல் சிறிது இழிந்து","இருந்தனை சென்மோ வழங்குக சுடர் என","அருளிக் கூடும் ஆர்வ மாக்கள்","நல்லேம் என்னும் கிளவி வல்லோன்","எழுதி அன்ன காண் தகு வனப்பின்","ஐயள் மாயோள் அணங்கிய","மையல் நெஞ்சம் என் மொழிக் கொளினே"],"p":"","r":"Speaking so the friend will hear, the man who keeps following addresses a rider of the tall palmyra-stem horse, crowned with flowers no one would sell and roaming town after town as one well smitten: if he will take his word, let him come down a little into a tree's shade as cool as the umbrella of a king who knows his duty and rest before he goes on while the sun moves, for kind people will gather round him; such is the deluded heart that the dark girl, lovely as if painted by a master, has struck.","n":"வில்லாப் பூ, the flower no one sells, is the erukku of the madal garland, named by what it is not. The colophon calls the poem kurinji; by its matter, the madal, it is the perunthinai of the Tholkappiyam.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamanoy","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 146","lb":"NT146","poet":"கந்தரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பின்னின்ற தலைவன்முன்னிலைப் புறமொழியாக தோழி கேட்பச்சொல்லியது","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira","thozhi","kalavu","பின்னின்ற தலைவன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பூவின்","பூ",1,"maram","ornament",""],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",3,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["மர நிழல்","மரம்",5,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:147":{"book":"natrinai","num":148,"t":["யாங்கு ஆகுவமோ அணி நுதற் குறுமகள்","தேம் படு சாரற் சிறு தினைப் பெருங் குரல்","செவ் வாய்ப் பைங் கிளி கவர நீ மற்று","எவ் வாய்ச் சென்றனை அவண் எனக் கூறி","அன்னை ஆனாள் கழற முன் நின்று","அருவி ஆர்க்கும் பெரு வரை நாடனை","அறியலும் அறியேன் காண்டலும் இலனே","வெதிர் புனை தட்டையேன் மலர் பூக் கொய்து","சுனை பாய்ந்து ஆடிற்றும் இலன் என நினைவிலை","பொய்யல் அந்தோ வாய்த்தனை அது கேட்டு","தலை இறைஞ்சினளே அன்னை","செலவு ஒழிந்தனையால் அளியை நீ புனத்தே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend tells the girl of the bright forehead that they are done for: when their mother scolded her, asking where she had gone while the red-beaked green parrots carried off the heavy millet heads on the honeyed slope, she stood before her and said she neither knew nor had ever seen the lord of the great waterfall hills, nor plucked flowers with her bamboo clapper, nor dived and bathed in the pool; she did not think, and the lie told the truth; hearing it the mother bowed her head, and the girl's going to the field is over, poor thing.","n":"The girl's denial lists, unasked, exactly what the lovers did, so the mother learns everything from it; told in the man's hearing, the comic scene is a warning that the field trysts are ended and only marriage remains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","pedhaimai","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 147","lb":"NT147","poet":"கொள்ளம்பக்கனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத்தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகத்தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["தினைப்","தினை",2,"maram","scene",""],["கிளி","கிளி",3,"pul","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",6,"pira","scene",""],["வெதிர்","வெதிர்",8,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["சுனை","சுனை",9,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:148":{"book":"natrinai","num":149,"t":["வண்ணம் நோக்கியும் மென் மொழி கூறியும்","நீ அவண் வருதல் ஆற்றாய் எனத் தாம்","தொடங்கி ஆள்வினைப் பிரிந்தோர் இன்றே","நெடுங் கயம் புரிந்த நீர் இல் நீள் இடை","செங் கால் மராஅத்து அம் புடைப் பொருந்தி","வாங்கு சிலை மறவர் வீங்கு நிலை அஞ்சாது","கல் அளைச் செறிந்த வள் உகிர்ப் பிணவின்","இன் புனிற்று இடும்பை தீர சினம் சிறந்து","செங் கண் இரும் புலிக் கோள் வல் ஏற்றை","உயர் மருப்பு ஒருத்தல் புகர் முகம் பாயும்","அருஞ் சுரம் இறப்ப என்ப","வருந்தேன் தோழி வாய்க்க அவர் செலவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend reassures the woman: he who, looking at her colour and speaking softly, said she could not bear to come there, set out today on his work; they say he is crossing the hard tract along the long waterless stretch where the deep ponds have dried, where in the shade of the red-stemmed kadamba, fearless of the bowmen with their bent bows, a red-eyed tiger, fierce at the kill, springs on the spotted face of a high-tusked bull elephant to end the pangs of his sharp-clawed mate, newly delivered in her rock cave; 'I will not grieve, friend; may his journey prosper.'","n":"The last line can be heard in the woman's own voice, answering the comfort; the colophon gives the whole poem to the friend, who would then be modelling the answer she wants the woman to give.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aalvinai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 148","lb":"NT148","poet":"கள்ளம்பாளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவுணர்ந்து வேறுபட்ட தலைவியைத் தோழி வற்புறீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"வற்புறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மராஅத்து","மரா",5,"maram","scene",""],["மறவர்","மறவர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["பிணவின்","பிணவு",7,"maa","iraicchi","tigress"],["ஏற்றை","ஏற்றை",9,"maa","iraicchi","male tiger"],["ஒருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",10,"maa","scene","bull elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","ஏற்றை","confirmed","1/1","the man crossing that land is doing the same for the woman he left, so his going is care for her and she may bear it"]],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:149":{"book":"natrinai","num":150,"t":["சிலரும் பலரும் கடைக்கண் நோக்கி","மூக்கின் உச்சிச் சுட்டு விரல் சேர்த்தி","மறுகில் பெண்டிர் அம்பல் தூற்ற","சிறு கோல் வலந்தனள் அன்னை அலைப்ப","அலந்தனென் வாழி தோழி கானல்","புது மலர் தீண்டிய பூ நாறு குரூஉச் சுவல்","கடு மான் பரிய கதழ் பரி கடைஇ","நடு நாள் வரூஉம் இயல் தேர்க் கொண்கனொடு","செலவு அயர்ந்திசினால் யானே","அலர் சுமந்து ஒழிக இவ் அழுங்கல் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that the street women, some and many, glance sidelong and touch forefinger to nose-tip spreading the gossip, and their mother beats her with a little cane; worn out, she has made ready to go with the shore lord whose chariot comes at midnight driven by swift horses whose coloured manes smell of the fresh flowers of the seaside grove they brushed; let this clamouring town carry its gossip and be done.","n":"The friend says 'I have made ready to go' (யானே) for the woman, speaking as one with her. The finger on the nose-tip is the town's gesture of scandal, the cane the house's answer, and the elopement is the answer to both. The colophon files it under neythal by its shore things; its situation is the elopement of paalai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","alar","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 149","lb":"NT149","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி தலைவியை உடன்போக்கு வலித்தது சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","உடன்போக்கு வலித்தது"],["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["கானல்","கானல்",5,"pira","scene","seaside grove"],["மலர்","மலர்",6,"maram","scene",""],["மான்","மா",7,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:150":{"book":"natrinai","num":151,"t":["நகை நன்கு உடையன் பாண நும் பெருமகன்","மிளை வலி சிதையக் களிறு பல பரப்பி","அரண் பல கடந்த முரண் கொள் தானை","வழுதி வாழிய பல எனத் தொழுது ஈண்டு","மன் எயில் உடையோர் போல அஃது யாம்","என்னதும் பரியலோ இலம் எனத் தண் நடைக்","கலி மா கடைஇ வந்து எம் சேரித்","தாரும் கண்ணியும் காட்டி ஒருமைய","நெஞ்சம் கொண்டமை விடுமோ அஞ்ச","கண்ணுடைச் சிறு கோல் பற்றிக்","கதம் பெரிது உடையள் யாய் அழுங்கலோ இலளே"],"p":"","r":"The courtesan tells the bard that his lord is a fine joke: like the holders of strong forts who bow and cry 'long live Vazhuthi', whose warring army scattered elephants to break the guard-forest and took many forts, he bowed here, then said he cares nothing at all for it; he drove his prancing horse into her street, showed his garland and chaplet and took her whole heart; will he let it go now? Her mother, cane in hand, is furious and will not relent.","n":"The Pandya's conquests are borrowed as a measure of submission: the lord is mocked as a vassal who paid homage and then denied it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","kaamam","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 150","lb":"NT150","poet":"கடுவன் இளமள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைநின்று ஒழுகப்படா நின்ற பரத்தை தலைவனை நெருங்கிப் பாணற்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","paanan",null,"karpu","பாணற்கு உரைத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",2,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மா","மா",7,"maa","scene","horse"],["தாரும்","தார்",8,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[["king","வழுதி",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:151":{"book":"natrinai","num":152,"t":["நல் நுதல் பசப்பினும் பெருந் தோள் நெகிழினும்","கொல் முரண் இரும் புலி அரும் புழைத் தாக்கிச்","செம் மறுக் கொண்ட வெண் கோட்டு யானை","கல் மிசை அருவியின் கழூஉஞ் சாரல்","வாரற்கதில்ல தோழி கடுவன்","முறி ஆர் பெருங் கிளை அறிதல் அஞ்சி","கறி வளர் அடுக்கத்து களவினில் புணர்ந்த","செம் முக மந்தி செய்குறி கருங் கால்","பொன் இணர் வேங்கைப் பூஞ் சினைச் செலீஇயர்","குண்டு நீர் நெடுஞ் சுனை நோக்கிக் கவிழ்ந்து தன்","புன் தலைப் பாறு மயிர் திருத்தும்","குன்ற நாடன் இரவினானே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend says that even if the girl's fair forehead pales and her broad shoulders grow thin, may the hill lord not come by night, from the slope where a white-tusked elephant, its tusk reddened after goring a fierce tiger at a narrow pass, washes it in the waterfall on the rock, and where a red-faced she-monkey who mated in secret on the pepper-vine slope, afraid that the male's great leaf-eating troop will find out, bends over the deep pool and smooths her tousled hair before climbing the flowering branch of the gold-clustered vengai.","n":"The elephant washing the tiger's blood from its tusk is the danger of the night path, told as scene; the monkey is the lovers' secret.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu","pasalai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 151","lb":"NT151","poet":"இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறிச்சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறிச்சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["புலி","புலி",2,"maa","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","scene","elephant"],["அருவியின்","அருவி",4,"pira","scene",""],["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",5,"maa","ullurai","male monkey"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",8,"maa","ullurai","she-monkey"],["கறி","கறி",7,"maram","scene","pepper vine"],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",9,"maram","scene",""],["சுனை","சுனை",10,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","மந்தி கடுவன்","confirmed","1/1","the girl and the hill lord meeting secretly by night"]],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:152":{"book":"natrinai","num":153,"t":["மடலே காமம் தந்தது அலரே","மிடை பூ எருக்கின் அலர் தந்தன்றே","இலங்கு கதிர் மழுங்கி எல் விசும்பு படர","புலம்பு தந்தன்றே புகன்று செய் மண்டிலம்","எல்லாம் தந்ததன்தலையும் பையென","வடந்தை துவலை தூவ குடம்பைப்","பெடை புணர் அன்றில் உயங்கு குரல் அளைஇ","கங்குலும் கையறவு தந்தன்று","யாங்கு ஆகுவென்கொல் அளியென் யானே"],"p":"","r":"The man set on the palmyra-stem horse says, so the friend will hear, that love gave him the madal and the blossoms of the close-strung erukku gave him gossip; the sun, fond in its work, gave him loneliness as its bright rays dimmed and it crossed the evening sky; and on top of all that, as the north wind gently scatters spray, night too gave him helplessness, mixed with the weary call of the andril joined with its mate in the nest; what will become of him, pitiable as he is.","n":"அலர் is both the blossom of the erukku and the town's gossip: the flower he wears is the talk he earns. Each line gives one hour its own gift of pain. The andril joined with its mate only calls; it does nothing a lover does, so it is scene, not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","alar","maalai","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 152","lb":"NT152","poet":"ஆலம்பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"மடல் வலித்த தலைவன்முன்னிலைப் புறமொழியாக தோழி கேட்பச்சொல்லியது","thd":1,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira","thozhi","kalavu","மடல் வலித்த தலைவன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["எருக்கின்","எருக்கு",2,"maram","ornament",""],["வடந்தை","வடந்தை",6,"pira","scene","north wind"],["அன்றில்","அன்றில்",7,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:153":{"book":"natrinai","num":154,"t":["குண கடல் முகந்து குடக்கு ஏர்பு இருளி","மண் திணி ஞாலம் விளங்க கம்மியர்","செம்பு சொரி பானையின் மின்னி எவ் வாயும்","தன் தொழில் வாய்த்த இன் குரல் எழிலி","தென்புல மருங்கில் சென்று அற்றாங்கு","நெஞ்சம் அவர்வயின் சென்றென ஈண்டு ஒழிந்து","உண்டல் அளித்து என் உடம்பே விறல் போர்","வெஞ் சின வேந்தன் பகை அலைக் கலங்கி","வாழ்வோர் போகிய பேர் ஊர்ப்","பாழ் காத்திருந்த தனி மகன் போன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The woman, wasting in separation, says that as the sweet-voiced cloud that scooped up the eastern sea, rose westward darkening, flashed like a smith's pot pouring copper so that the earth shone, and its work done everywhere went off to the south and was spent, so her heart has gone to him and stayed there; her body, left here, lives on by eating, like the lone man who stayed to guard the ruins of a great town whose people fled when the furious king of mighty wars struck it.","n":"The poet is named Thanimakanaar, the lone man, from line 10. The body is not dead but a caretaker of an emptied place: it eats so that something remains to guard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","melivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 153","lb":"NT153","poet":"தனிமகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைவி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கம்மியர்","கம்மியர்",2,"seythi","simile","smiths"],["எழிலி","எழிலி",4,"pira","simile","cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:154":{"book":"natrinai","num":155,"t":["கானமும் கம்மென்றன்றே வானமும்","வரை கிழிப்பன்ன மை இருள் பரப்பி","பல் குரல் எழிலி பாடு ஓவாதே","மஞ்சு தவழ் இறும்பில் களிறு வலம் படுத்த","வெஞ் சின உழுவைப் பேழ் வாய் ஏற்றை","அஞ்சுதக உரறும் ஓசை கேளாது","துஞ்சுதியோ இல தூவிலாட்டி","பேர் அஞர் பொருத புகர் படு நெஞ்சம்","நீர் அடு நெருப்பின் தணிய இன்று அவர்","வாரார் ஆயினோ நன்றே சாரல்","விலங்கு மலை ஆர் ஆறு உள்ளுதொறும்","நிலம் பரந்து ஒழுகும் என் நிறை இல் நெஞ்சே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the woman asks her friend whether she can sleep, not hearing it: the forest has fallen silent, the sky spreads a pitch dark as if to split the hills, the many-voiced cloud thunders on without pause, and in the cloud-wrapped thicket a fierce gaping-mouthed tiger that has felled an elephant roars fearfully; her heart, scarred by great distress, would cool like fire quenched with water if he did not come tonight, for each time she thinks of the hard path through the hills that bar the slope, her unrestrained heart runs out over the ground.","n":"She would rather he did not come than come through such a night, which tells the listening man that the night meetings must end in marriage. தூவிலாட்டி in line 7 is a rare form of address.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","acham","nirai","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 154","lb":"NT154","poet":"நல்லாவூர் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறித் தலைவன்சிறைப்புறமாக வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["எழிலி","எழிலி",3,"pira","scene","rain-cloud"],["களிறு","களிறு",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["உழுவைப்","உழுவை",5,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:155":{"book":"natrinai","num":156,"t":["ஒள் இழை மகளிரொடு ஓரையும் ஆடாய்","வள் இதழ் நெய்தற் தொடலையும் புனையாய்","விரி பூங் கானல் ஒரு சிறை நின்றோய்","யாரையோ நிற் தொழுதனெம் வினவுதும்","கண்டோர் தண்டா நலத்தை தெண் திரைப்","பெருங் கடல் பரப்பின் அமர்ந்து உறை அணங்கோ","இருங் கழி மருங்கு நிலைபெற்றனையோ","சொல் இனி மடந்தை என்றனென் அதன் எதிர்","முள் எயிற்று முறுவல் திறந்தன","பல் இதழ் உண்கணும் பரந்தவால் பனியே"],"p":"","r":"The man tells how, meeting her a second time, he said to her: you do not play the orai game with your bright-jewelled companions, you do not wear a garland of broad-petalled neythal, you stand apart in the flowering seaside grove; who are you? we bow to you and ask: with a beauty no one tires of seeing, are you a goddess who lives in the wide sea of clear waves, or have you settled on the bank of the dark backwater? speak now, girl; and in answer her sharp-toothed smile opened, and tears spread over her many-petalled kohl eyes.","n":"She answers his question only with a smile and tears, which no goddess would give: the reply settles that she is a woman. The colophon files it under neythal by its shore; the situation, a meeting of lovers, is the punarthal of kurinji.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kalaviyal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 155","lb":"NT155","poet":"பராயனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"இரண்டாம் கூட்டத்துத்தலைவியை எதிர்ப்பட்டுத் தலைவன் சொல்லியது உணர்ப்பு வயின் வாரா ஊடற்கண் தலைவன் சொற்றதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","இரண்டாம் கூட்டத்துத்தலைவியை எதிர்ப்பட்டுத் தலைவன் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu","உணர்ப்பு வயின் வாரா ஊடற்கண் தலைவன் சொற்றதூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["நெய்தற்","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","absent",""],["கானல்","கானல்",3,"pira","scene","seaside grove"],["கடல்","கடல்",6,"pira","scene",""],["அணங்கோ","அணங்கு",6,"deyvam","scene","sea goddess"],["கழி","கழி",7,"pira","scene","backwater"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:156":{"book":"natrinai","num":157,"t":["நீயே அடி அறிந்து ஒதுங்கா ஆர் இருள் வந்து எம்","கடியுடை வியல் நகர்க் காவல் நீவியும்","பேர் அன்பினையே பெருங் கல் நாட","யாமே நின்னும் நின் மலையும் பாடி பல் நாள்","சிறு தினை காக்குவம் சேறும் அதனால்","பகல் வந்தீமோ பல் படர் அகல","எருவை நீடிய பெரு வரைச் சிறுகுடி","அரியல் ஆர்ந்தவர் ஆயினும் பெரியர்","பாடு இமிழ் விடர் முகை முழங்க","ஆடு மழை இறுத்தது எம் கோடு உயர் குன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the great rocky hills that his coming through darkness where feet cannot find the way, slipping past the guard of their wide house, shows his great love; but they, singing of him and his hill, will guard the small millet for many days and will go there, so let him come by day and end their many cares; for the people of their hamlet on the great hill of tall reeds, though they have drunk their toddy, are formidable, and the swaying rain has settled on their high-peaked hill, roaring in the rock clefts.","n":"She refuses the night only by offering the day: the millet field the girls guard is the place she names for the meeting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 156","lb":"NT156","poet":"கண்ணங் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தினை","தினை",5,"maram","scene",""],["எருவை","எருவை",7,"maram","scene","tall reed"],["அரியல்","அரியல்",8,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["மழை","மழை",10,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:157":{"book":"natrinai","num":158,"t":["இருங் கண் ஞாலத்து ஈண்டு தொழில் உதவிப்","பெரும் பெயல் பொழிந்த வழி நாள் அமையத்து","பல் பொறி அரவின் செல் புறம் கடுப்ப","யாற்று அறல் நுணங்கிய நாட் பத வேனில்","இணர் துதை மாஅத்த புணர் குயில் விளித்தொறும்","நம்வயின் நினையும் நெஞ்சமொடு கைம்மிகக்","கேட்டொறும் கலுழுமால் பெரிதே காட்ட","குறும் பொறை அயல நெடுந் தாள் வேங்கை","அம் பூந் தாது உக்கன்ன","நுண் பல் தித்தி மாஅயோளே"],"p":"","r":"Away for wealth, the man tells his heart, which has sensed the season, that now, after the great rains that served the world's labour, in early summer when the river sand is rippled fine like the back of a spotted moving snake, each time the paired koel calls from the cluster-laden mango, the dark girl with fine scattered freckles, like the pollen shed by the tall vengai beside the low rocks of the forest, will think of them and weep greatly as she hears it.","n":"The paired koel only calls: its sound carries the season's pain but it does nothing a lover does, so it is scene, not iraicchi. He measures her grief by his own heart, which has already felt the season.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 157","lb":"NT157","poet":"இளவேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்த தலைவன் பருவம் உணர்ந்த நெஞ்சிற்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"பருவம் உணர்ந்த நெஞ்சிற்கு உரைத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அரவின்","அரவு",3,"maa","simile","snake"],["மாஅத்த","மா",5,"maram","scene","mango"],["குயில்","குயில்",5,"pul","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",8,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:158":{"book":"natrinai","num":159,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நம்வயின்","யானோ காணேன் அதுதான் கரந்தே","கல் அதர் மன்னும் கால் கொல்லும்மே","கனை இருள் மன்னும் கண் கொல்லும்மே","விடர் முகைச் செறிந்த வெஞ் சின இரும் புலி","புகர் முக வேழம் புலம்பத் தாக்கி","குருதி பருகிய கொழுங் கவுட் கய வாய்","வேங்கை முதலொடு துடைக்கும்","ஓங்கு மலை நாடன் வரூஉம் ஆறே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the woman tells her friend that she has not seen it, for it lies hidden, the path the lord of the high hills comes by: there the stony track must surely break the feet, the thick darkness must surely put out the eyes, and a fierce tiger from a cleft in the rocks, having struck a spotted-faced elephant till it cries out, wipes its fat-cheeked, blood-drinking mouth against the trunk of a vengai.","n":"Her fear is all imagination: she has never seen the path (யானோ காணேன்), and the tiger wiping its mouth on the vengai is what her mind sets on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 158","lb":"NT158","poet":"வெள்ளைக்குடி நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"ஆறு பார்த்து உற்ற அச்சத்தால் சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","ஆறு பார்த்து உற்ற அச்சத்தால் சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["புலி","புலி",5,"maa","scene",""],["வேழம்","வேழம்",6,"maa","scene","elephant"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",8,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:159":{"book":"natrinai","num":160,"t":["மணி துணிந்தன்ன மா இரும் பரப்பின்","உரவுத் திரை கொழீஇய பூ மலி பெருந் துறை","நிலவுக் குவித்தன்ன மோட்டு மணல் இடிகரை","கோடு துணர்ந்தன்ன குருகு ஒழுக்கு எண்ணி","எல்லை கழிப்பினம்ஆயின் மெல்ல","வளி சீத்து வரித்த புன்னை முன்றில்","கொழு மீன் ஆர்கைச் செழு நகர்ச் செலீஇய","எழு எனின் அவளும் ஒல்லாள் யாமும்","ஒழி என அல்லம் ஆயினம் யாமத்து","உடைதிரை ஒலியின் துஞ்சும் மலி கடற்","சில் குடிப் பாக்கம் கல்லென","அல்குவதாக நீ அமர்ந்த தேரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that on the great flower-strewn shore heaped up by the strong waves of the dark wide sea, gleaming like cut sapphire, on the steep sand dune piled like moonlight, they pass the day counting the lines of herons like clustered conches; then if they say 'rise, let us go to the rich house with its punnai yard swept and patterned by the wind, where fat fish are eaten', she will not agree, and they cannot tell her 'stay'; so let the chariot he loves lodge tonight in the little fishing hamlet that sleeps to the sound of breaking waves, so that it wakes in uproar.","n":"The request that his chariot stay the night is a demand for publicity: a hamlet woken by it will know, and what the town knows must end in marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","alar","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 159","lb":"NT159","poet":"கண்ணம் புல்லனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைவியின் ஆற்றாமையும் உலகியலும் கூறி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["திரை","திரை",2,"pira","scene",""],["கோடு","கோடு",4,"pira","simile","conch"],["குருகு","குருகு",4,"pul","simile","heron"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",6,"maram","scene",""],["மீன்","மீன்",7,"unavu","gift_food","fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:160":{"book":"natrinai","num":161,"t":["நயனும் நண்பும் நாணு நன்கு உடைமையும்","பயனும் பண்பும் பாடு அறிந்து ஒழுகலும்","நும்மினும் அறிகுவென்மன்னே கம்மென","எதிர்த்த தித்தி ஏர் இள வன முலை","விதிர்த்து விட்டன்ன அந் நுண் சுணங்கின்","ஐம் பால் வகுத்த கூந்தல் செம் பொறி","திரு நுதல் பொலிந்த தேம் பாய் ஓதி","முது நீர் இலஞ்சிப் பூத்த குவளை","எதிர் மலர்ப் பிணையல் அன்ன இவள்","அரி மதர் மழைக் கண் காணா ஊங்கே"],"p":"","r":"The man answers his friend's rebuke: good sense, friendship, shame, decency, usefulness, character and conduct that knows what is fitting, he once knew them better than his friend does, before he saw the wide, cool, intoxicating eyes of this girl, like a garland of facing kuvalai blossoms bloomed in an old pond, with her young breasts flecked with freckles, the fine pale spots as if sprinkled, her hair dressed in five parts and her fragrant locks over a fair forehead marked with a red dot.","n":"He does not deny the virtues his friend preaches; he claims to know them better, so the rebuke teaches him nothing. The list of virtues runs two lines; the list of her features runs seven.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idithal","naanam","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 160","lb":"NT160","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"கழற்று எதிர்மறை","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu","கழற்று எதிர்மறை"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["இலஞ்சிப்","இலஞ்சி",8,"pira","scene","pond"],["குவளை","குவளை",8,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:8"},"nt:161":{"book":"natrinai","num":162,"t":["இறையும் அருந் தொழில் முடித்தென பொறைய","கண் போல் நீலம் சுனைதொறும் மலர","வீ ததர் வேங்கைய வியல் நெடும் புறவின்","இம்மென் பறவை ஈண்டு கிளை இரிய","நெடுந் தெரு அன்ன நேர் கொள் நெடு வழி","இளையர் ஏகுவனர் பரிப்ப வளை எனக்","காந்தள் வள் இதழ் கவிகுளம்பு அறுப்ப","தோள் வலி யாப்ப ஈண்டு நம் வரவினைப்","புள் அறிவுறீஇயினகொல்லோ தெள்ளிதின்","காதல் கெழுமிய நலத்தள் ஏதில்","புதல்வற் காட்டிப் பொய்க்கும்","திதலை அல்குல் தேம் மொழியாட்கே"],"p":"","r":"Going home with the king's hard work finished, the man tells his charioteer of the long straight road through the forest, where blue lilies open like eyes in every hill pool and the vengai sheds its flowers, and wonders whether the omen birds have already told of his coming to her, the honey-voiced woman who soothes their son with a pretence by pointing out strangers to him.","n":"The hooves cut kaanthal petals that look like bangles (வளை என), and the next words wish her shoulders firm again (தோள் வலி யாப்ப): the road home is already undoing the slack bangles of her waiting. Pointing out strangers to the boy is read here as her passing them off as his father; the phrase is compressed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","makkal","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 161","lb":"NT161","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றிப் பெயரும்தலைவன் தேர்ப்பாகன் கேட்ப சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றிப் பெயரும்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["நீலம்","நீலம்",2,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["வேங்கைய","வேங்கை",3,"maram","scene",""],["பறவை","பறவை",4,"pul","scene",""],["இளையர்","இளையர்",6,"seythi","scene","attendants"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",7,"maram","simile",""],["குளம்பு","குளம்பு",7,"maa","scene","horse hoof"],["புள்","புள்",9,"pul","scene","omen bird"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:162":{"book":"natrinai","num":163,"t":["மனை உறை புறவின் செங் காற் பேடைக்","காமர் துணையொடு சேவல் சேர","புலம்பின்று எழுதரு புன்கண் மாலைத்","தனியே இருத்தல் ஆற்றேன் என்று நின்","பனி வார் உண்கண் பைதல கலுழ","நும்மொடு வருவல் என்றி எம்மொடு","பெரும் பெயர்த் தந்தை நீடு புகழ் நெடு நகர்","யாயொடு நனி மிக மடவை முனாஅது","வேனில் இற்றித் தோயா நெடு வீழ்","வழி நார் ஊசலின் கோடை தூக்குதொறும்","துஞ்சு பிடி வருடும் அத்தம்","வல்லை ஆகுதல் ஒல்லுமோ நினக்கே"],"p":"","r":"To the girl who weeps that she cannot bear to be alone at the painful evening when even the red-legged dove of the house joins its mate, and says she will come with him, the man answers that she, so delicate beside her mother in her famous father's great house, could hardly be strong enough for the waste where in summer the long hanging roots of the fig swing like a rope cradle in the west wind and stroke a sleeping she-elephant.","n":"The refusal is tender: the one gentle thing he finds in the waste, a hanging root rocking a sleeping elephant like a cradle, recalls the nursed childhood he names in line 8. The doves of lines 1 and 2 come close to iraicchi (a pair joined at dusk), but the girl draws the sense herself (தனியே இருத்தல் ஆற்றேன்), so they stay scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","pirivu","maalai","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 162","lb":"NT162","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உடன் போதுவல் என்ற தலைவிக்குத் தலைவன் சொற்றது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","உடன் போதுவல் என்ற தலைவிக்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["புறவின்","புறா",1,"pul","scene","dove"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",2,"pul","scene","male dove"],["இற்றித்","இற்றி",9,"maram","simile",""],["பிடி","பிடி",11,"maa","scene","she-elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:163":{"book":"natrinai","num":164,"t":["உயிர்த்தனவாகுக அளிய நாளும்","அயிர்த் துகள் முகந்த ஆனா ஊதையொடு","எல்லியும் இரவும் என்னாது கல்லெனக்","கறங்கு இசை இன மணி கைபுணர்ந்து ஒலிப்ப","நிலவுத் தவழ் மணற் கோடு ஏறிச் செலவர","இன்று என் நெஞ்சம் போல தொன்று நனி","வருந்துமன் அளிய தாமே பெருங் கடல்","நீல் நிறப் புன்னைத் தமி ஒண் கைதை","வானம் மூழ்கிய வயங்கு ஒளி நெடுஞ் சுடர்க்","கதிர் காய்ந்து எழுந்து அகம் கனலி ஞாயிற்று","வைகுறு வனப்பின் தோன்றும்","கைதைஅம் கானல் துறைவன் மாவே"],"p":"","r":"Glad that the marriage is settled, she says: may they breathe again at last, the pitiable blue-dark punnai and the lone bright kaithai of the shore grove, which suffered long, as her own heart did, while day and night, in the sand-laden wind, the shore lord's horses, bells ringing, climbed the moonlit dunes, horses that appear with the beauty of the sun rising at dawn.","n":"The pity goes to the trees: the punnai and kaithai trampled by his night visits carry the cost of the secret love, and the marriage frees them as it frees her heart (என் நெஞ்சம் போல). Whether அளிய (the pitiable ones) is the trees or the horses is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalavu","nenju","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 163","lb":"NT163","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரைவு மலிந்து சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு மலிந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஊதையொடு","ஊதை",2,"pira","scene","cold wind"],["மணி","மணி",4,"pira","ornament","bell"],["புன்னைத்","புன்னை",8,"maram","simile",""],["கைதை","கைதை",8,"maram","simile",""],["மாவே","மா",12,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:164":{"book":"natrinai","num":165,"t":["உறை துறந்திருந்த புறவில் தனாது","செங் கதிர்ச் செல்வன் தெறுதலின் மண் பக","உலகு மிக வருந்தி உயாவுறுகாலைச்","சென்றனர் ஆயினும் நன்று செய்தனர் எனச்","சொல்லின் தெளிப்பவும் தெளிதல் செல்லாய்","செங்கோல் வாளிக் கொடு வில் ஆடவர்","வம்ப மாக்கள் உயிர்த் திறம் பெயர்த்தென","வெங் கடற்று அடை முதல் படு முடை தழீஇ","உறு பசிக் குறு நரி குறுகல் செல்லாது","மாறு புறக்கொடுக்கும் அத்தம்","ஊறு இலராகுதல் உள்ளாமாறே"],"p":"","r":"The friend reminds the girl that when he left in the season the rain had deserted and the sun cracked the earth, she told her he had done well though he went, yet the girl would not be convinced, unable to think he could come back unharmed through the waste where bowmen with red-shafted arrows take travellers' lives and even the starving jackal turns back from the rotting dead; now he has come back with his wealth.","n":"The jackal that will not go near the dead (குறுகல் செல்லாது) measures the road: too dreadful even for the scavenger, so the girl's disbelief was fair, and the friend's news answers fear rather than reproach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","thelivu","acham","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 164","lb":"NT164","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் முடித்து வந்தான் என்பது வாயில்கள்வாய்க் கேட்ட தோழி தலைவிக்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பொருள் முடித்து வந்தான்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",6,"seythi","scene","bowmen"],["குறு நரி","நரி",9,"maa","scene","jackal"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:165":{"book":"natrinai","num":166,"t":["அமர்க் கண் ஆமான் அரு நிறம் முள்காது","பணைத்த பகழிப் போக்கு நினைந்து கானவன்","அணங்கொடு நின்றது மலை வான் கொள்க எனக்","கடவுள் ஓங்கு வரை பேண்மார் வேட்டு எழுந்து","கிளையொடு மகிழும் குன்ற நாடன்","அடைதரும்தோறும் அருமை தனக்கு உரைப்ப","நப் புணர்வு இல்லா நயன் இலோர் நட்பு","அன்ன ஆகுக என்னான்","ஒல்காது ஒழி மிகப் பல்கின தூதே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the hill man, from a land where a hunter whose arrow missed a fierce-eyed wild cow decides the mountain held a god and goes up with his kin to worship the peak, keeps telling her how hard it is to meet, yet never says the word that would end it, while marriage messages from other suitors keep multiplying.","n":"The hunter puts his failed shot down to the hill's divinity and goes to worship rather than shoot again; set beside a lover who only complains of difficulty, it may hint at a man who blames obstacles instead of acting, but only one point maps, so it stays scene. Lines 7 and 8 are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalavu","thoothu","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 165","lb":"NT165","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"நொதுமலர் வரையும் பருவத்து தோழி தலைவிக்கு அறத்தொடு நிலை பயப்பச் சொல்லியது வரைவு மலிந்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","அறத்தொடு நிலை பயப்பச் சொல்லியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு மலிந்ததூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆமான்","ஆமான்",1,"maa","scene","wild cow"],["கானவன்","கானவன்",2,"seythi","scene","hunter"],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",4,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:166":{"book":"natrinai","num":167,"t":["பொன்னும் மணியும் போலும் யாழ நின்","நன்னர் மேனியும் நாறு இருங் கதுப்பும்","போதும் பணையும் போலும் யாழ நின்","மாதர் உண்கணும் வனப்பின் தோளும்","இவை காண்தோறும் அகம் மலிந்து யானும்","அறம் நிலைபெற்றோர் அனையேன் அதன்தலை","பொலந்தொடிப் புதல்வனும் பொய்தல் கற்றனன்","வினையும் வேறு புலத்து இலெனே நினையின்","யாதனின் பிரிகோ மடந்தை","காதல் தானும் கடலினும் பெரிதே"],"p":"","r":"To his wife, changed by signs that he means to leave, the man says her body and fragrant dark hair are like gold and sapphire, her eyes and shoulders like flowers and bamboo; whenever he sees them his heart fills and he is like those settled in virtue; their son with gold bracelets has learnt to play, and he has no work in another land, so why should he part from her, when his love is greater than the sea?","n":"He answers her fear with an inventory of the home: her beauty, his settled virtue, the son's first games, no work abroad. Saying that delight at home makes him like those established in aram (அறம் நிலைபெற்றோர் அனையேன்) treats the household as the seat of virtue, and the very fullness of the denial is the kind of excess a wife reads as a sign.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ilvaazhkkai","makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 166","lb":"NT166","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவுக் குறிப்பினால் வேறுபட்ட கிழத்திக்குத் தலைவன் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu","செலவுக் குறிப்பினால் வேறுபட்ட கிழத்திக்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["பொன்னும்","பொன்",1,"pira","simile","gold"],["மணியும்","மணி",1,"pira","simile","sapphire"],["போதும்","போது",3,"maram","simile","flower"],["பணையும்","பணை",3,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["கடலினும்","கடல்",10,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:167":{"book":"natrinai","num":168,"t":["கருங் கோட்டுப் புன்னைக் குடக்கு வாங்கு பெருஞ் சினை","விருந்தின் வெண் குருகு ஆர்ப்பின் ஆஅய்","வண் மகிழ் நாளவைப் பரிசில் பெற்ற","பண் அமை நெடுந் தேர்ப் பாணியின் ஒலிக்கும்","தண்ணம் துறைவன் தூதொடும் வந்த","பயன் தெரி பனுவற் பை தீர் பாண","நின் வாய்ப் பணி மொழி களையா பல் மாண்","புது வீ ஞாழலொடு புன்னை தாஅம்","மணம் கமழ் கானல் மாண் நலம் இழந்த","இறை ஏர் எல் வளைக் குறுமகள்","பிறை ஏர் திரு நுதல் பாஅய பசப்பே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the bard who came with the shore lord's message, a knower of profitable songs and free of care, that his humble words will not remove the pallor spread over the crescent-bright forehead of the bangled girl, who lost her beauty in the fragrant grove where punnai and fresh ngaazhal flowers fall; on that shore the white heron, a guest on the punnai's westward-bending branch, cries with the sound of the tuned chariots bards received at Aay's generous feast-day assembly.","n":"Each compliment to the bard is barbed: he knows songs that bring gain, he is free of grief, and his master's shore rings with the chariots bards win from Aay. This bard's errand earns nothing here; the only thing spread on his master's shore is the pallor (பாஅய பசப்பே) the master left.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","oodal","thoothu","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 167","lb":"NT167","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி பாணற்கு வாயில் மறுத்தது தூதொடு வந்த பாணற்குச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","paanan",null,"karpu","பாணற்கு வாயில் மறுத்தது"],["thozhi","paanan",null,"karpu","தூதொடு வந்த பாணற்குச் சொல்லியதூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["புன்னைக்","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene",""],["குருகு","குருகு",2,"pul","simile","heron"],["ஞாழலொடு","ஞாழல்",8,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","ஆஅய்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:168":{"book":"natrinai","num":169,"t":["சுரும்பு உண விரிந்த கருங் கால் வேங்கைப்","பெருஞ் சினைத் தொடுத்த கொழுங் கண் இறாஅல்","புள்ளுற்றுக் கசிந்த தீம் தேன் கல் அளைக்","குறக் குறுமாக்கள் உண்ட மிச்சிலைப்","புன் தலை மந்தி வன் பறழ் நக்கும்","நன் மலை நாட பண்பு எனப் படுமோ","நின் நயந்து உறைவி இன் உயிர் உள்ளாய்","அணங்குடை அரவின் ஆர் இருள் நடு நாள்","மை படு சிறு நெறி எஃகு துணை ஆக","ஆரம் கமழும் மார்பினை","சாரற் சிறுகுடி ஈங்கு நீ வரலே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the good hill, where honey that birds have pierced from a comb on the vengai branch drips into a rock hollow and the young of the dull-headed female monkey lick what the hill children left, whether it can be called good conduct to come at midnight, in the thick dark of the dread snake, down the narrow black path with only his spear for company, his chest fragrant with sandal, to their small village on the slope, not thinking of the dear life of the girl who lives loving him.","n":"The scene descends in shares: bees open the flowers, birds pierce the comb, the hill children drink, the monkey's young lick the leftovers. A reader may sense a love enjoyed by stealth and in pieces, but nothing in the poem anchors a second correspondence, so it stays scene. The reproach is for her life (இன் உயிர்), not his: his danger is her danger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kalavu","kuri","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 168","lb":"NT168","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி இரவுக்குறி மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene",""],["இறாஅல்","இறால்",2,"unavu","scene","honeycomb"],["தீம் தேன்","தேன்",3,"unavu","gift_food",""],["புள்ளுற்றுக்","புள்",3,"pul","scene",""],["குறக்","குறவர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["மந்தி","மந்தி",5,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["அரவின்","அரவு",8,"pira","scene","snake"],["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",10,"maram","ornament","sandal"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:169":{"book":"natrinai","num":170,"t":["முன்னியது முடித்தனம் ஆயின் நன்னுதல்","வருவம் என்னும் பருவரல் தீர","படும்கொல் வாழி நெடுஞ் சுவர்ப் பல்லி","பரற் தலை போகிய சிரற் தலைக் கள்ளி","மீமிசைக் கலித்த வீ நறு முல்லை","ஆடு தலைத் துருவின் தோடு தலைப்பெயர்க்கும்","வன் கை இடையன் எல்லிப் பரீஇ","வெண் போழ் தைஇய அலங்கல்அம் தொடலை","மறுகுடன் கமழும் மாலை","சிறுகுடிப் பாக்கத்து எம் பெரு நகரானே"],"p":"","r":"On his way back with the task done, the man tells his heart: if we have finished what we set out to do, will you chirp, lizard of the long wall, to end the worry of the fair-browed one who waits saying he will come, in our great house in the small village, where at evening the strong-handed herdsman, turning back the leading sheep, plucks the jasmine that has climbed over the kalli on the gravel and weaves it with white palm strips into a garland whose scent fills the street?","n":"He does not wait for her omen: he speaks to the lizard as if he could make it chirp, so the sign she listens for at home becomes a message he would send. The herdsman's jasmine garland scenting the street at dusk is the mullai evening at its gentlest, the hour she is likeliest to grieve.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","nenju","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 169","lb":"NT169","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி மறுத்தராநின்றான் நெஞ்சிற்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றி மறுத்தராநின்றான்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பல்லி","பல்லி",3,"pira","scene","lizard"],["கள்ளி","கள்ளி",4,"maram","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",5,"maram","scene",""],["துருவின்","துரு",6,"maa","scene","sheep"],["இடையன்","இடையன்",7,"seythi","scene","herdsman"],["தொடலை","தொடலை",8,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:170":{"book":"natrinai","num":171,"t":["மடக் கண் தகரக் கூந்தல் பணைத் தோள்","வார்ந்த வால் எயிற்று சேர்ந்து செறி குறங்கின்","பிணையல் அம் தழை தைஇ துணையிலள்","விழவுக் களம் பொலிய வந்து நின்றனளே","எழுமினோ எழுமின் எம் கொழுநற் காக்கம்","ஆரியர் துவன்றிய பேர் இசை முள்ளூர்","பலர் உடன் கழித்த ஒள் வாள் மலையனது","ஒரு வேற்கு ஓடியாங்கு நம்","பன்மையது எவனோ இவள் வன்மை தலைப்படினே"],"p":"","r":"Turning away the dancer sent for the husband, the friend describes a girl with innocent eyes, tagaram-scented hair, bamboo-smooth arms, white teeth and close thighs, wearing a leaf skirt, who has come alone and stands making the festival ground shine; rise, rise, she cries, to guard our husband, and yet, as the many Aryans thronging famous Mullur fled before the single spear of Malaiyan of the bright sword, what use are our numbers if this one woman's strength prevails?","n":"The wives speak as an army and the rival as a lone champion, and the borrowed battle runs against them: in the story the many fled and the one won. The friend turns the household's numbers into a joke at its own expense, and the joke is her refusal: the dancer's errand is pointless when the husband is already won. The colophon gives the speech to the friend, though எம் கொழுநன் sounds like the wives' own voice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","vali"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 170","lb":"NT170","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி விறலிக்கு வாயில் மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","virali",null,"karpu","விறலிக்கு வாயில் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["தகரக்","தகரம்",1,"maram","ornament","tagaram scent"],["அம் தழை","தழை",3,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"]],"en":[["hill","முள்ளூர்",6],["king","மலையனது",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:171":{"book":"natrinai","num":172,"t":["நீர் நசைக்கு ஊக்கிய உயவல் யானை","வேனிற் குன்றத்து வெவ் வரைக் கவாஅன்","நிலம் செல செல்லாக் கயந் தலைக் குழவி","சேரி அம் பெண்டிர் நெஞ்சத்து எறிய","ஊர் ஆன்கன்றொடு புகுதும் நாடன்","பன் மலை அருஞ் சுரம் இறப்பின் நம் விட்டு","யாங்கு வல்லுந மற்றே ஞாங்க","வினைப் பூண் தெண் மணி வீழ்ந்தன நிகர்ப்பக்","கழுது கால்கொள்ளும் பொழுது கொள் பானாள்","ஆர்வ நெஞ்சமொடு அளைஇ","மார்பு உறப் படுத்தல் மரீஇய கண்ணே"],"p":"","r":"Told that the man will leave, the friend says to the girl: he comes from a land where the elephant, straining for water on the scorched summer slope, leaves behind its soft-headed calf that cannot keep pace, and the calf, to the pity of the village women, walks into the town with the cows' calves; if he crosses the many-hilled hard waste and leaves us, how will her eyes bear it, eyes used to lying on his chest with eager heart at midnight, the hour the ghosts walk?","n":"The calf left behind by a herd gone for water is a near miss for iraicchi: one carrier, and its plight is hers, but it does nothing a person of the situation does; it is the one abandoned, and the poem never draws the parallel. A land where even a strayed calf is taken in sharpens the question of who will take her in. Lines 7 to 9 (ஞாங்க ... நிகர்ப்ப) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kan","iravu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 171","lb":"NT171","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட தோழி தலைமகட்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","scene",""],["குழவி","குழவி",3,"maa","scene","elephant calf"],["ஆன்கன்றொடு","ஆன் கன்று",5,"maa","scene","calf of cows"],["மணி","மணி",8,"pira","simile","bell"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:172":{"book":"natrinai","num":173,"t":["விளையாடு ஆயமொடு வெண் மணல் அழுத்தி","மறந்தனம் துறந்த காழ் முளை அகைய","நெய் பெய் தீம் பால் பெய்து இனிது வளர்ப்ப","நும்மினும் சிறந்தது நுவ்வை ஆகும் என்று","அன்னை கூறினள் புன்னையது நலனே","அம்ம நாணுதும் நும்மொடு நகையே","விருந்தின் பாணர் விளர் இசை கடுப்ப","வலம்புரி வான் கோடு நரலும் இலங்கு நீர்த்","துறை கெழு கொண்க நீ நல்கின்","இறைபடு நீழல் பிறவுமார் உளவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the shore lord who has come for a daytime meeting that as girls at play they pressed a seed into the white sand and forgot it; when it sprouted they raised it with sweet milk and ghee, and their mother said this punnai is your elder sister, nobler than you; so they are ashamed to laugh with him beneath it, and if he means to grant them grace there are other shades, on his shore where the white right-spiralled conch sounds like the pale music of guest bards.","n":"The mother's word makes the tree kin, and kin may not witness a secret meeting: the friend's shame is a polite fiction that turns the tryst away and points him to the one proper shade, marriage, which நீ நல்கின் says without saying it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kuri","varaivu","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 172","lb":"NT172","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறி வந்த தலைமகனைத் தோழி வரைவு கடாயது குறிபெயர்த்தீடும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"],["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","குறிபெயர்த்தீடும்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காழ்","காழ்",2,"maram","scene","seed"],["தீம் பால்","பால்",3,"unavu","gift_food",""],["புன்னையது","புன்னை",5,"maram","scene",""],["பாணர்","பாணர்",7,"seythi","simile",""],["வலம்புரி","வலம்புரி",8,"pira","simile","conch"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:173":{"book":"natrinai","num":174,"t":["சுனைப் பூக் குற்றும் தொடலை தைஇயும்","மலைச் செங் காந்தட் கண்ணி தந்தும்","தன் வழிப் படூஉம் நம் நயந்தருளி","வெறி என உணர்ந்த அரிய அன்னையை","கண்ணினும் கனவினும் காட்டி இந் நோய்","என்னினும் வாராது மணியின் தோன்றும்","அம் மலை கிழவோன் செய்தனன் இது எனின்","படு வண்டு ஆர்க்கும் பைந் தார் மார்பின்","நெடு வேட்கு ஏதம் உடைத்தோ","தொடியோய் கூறுமதி வினவுவல் யானே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening outside, the friend asks the girl: the mother who loves them took for the god's seizure what followed his gifts of spring flowers, garlands and red kaanthal chaplets of the hill, and has it shown in waking and in dreams; if she is told that the lord of that gem-bright mountain caused this sickness, which comes no other way, would that wrong tall Vel of the bee-loud green garland? Tell me, bangled one, I ask.","n":"The question is a move, not a scruple: asking whether naming the man would offend Murugan lets the overhearing lover know that disclosure (அறத்தொடு நிலை) is near and that the veri rite is being held for the wrong cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","velan","kalavu","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 173","lb":"NT173","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தலைவிக்கு உரைப்பாளாய் சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது வெறி அச்சுறீஇத் தோழி அறத்தொடு நிலை பயப்பித்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வெறி அச்சுறீஇத் தோழி அறத்தொடு நிலை பயப்பித்ததூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["சுனைப் பூ","சுனைப் பூ",1,"maram","ornament",""],["தொடலை","தொடலை",1,"maram","ornament","garland"],["காந்தட்","காந்தள்",2,"maram","ornament",""],["மணியின்","மணி",6,"pira","simile","gem"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",8,"pira","scene",""],["நெடு வேட்கு","வேள்",9,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:174":{"book":"natrinai","num":175,"t":["கற்றை ஈந்தின் முற்றுக் குலை அன்ன","ஆள் இல் அத்தத் தாள் அம் போந்தைக்","கோளுடை நெடுஞ் சினை ஆண் குரல் விளிப்பின்","புலி எதிர் வழங்கும் வளி வழங்கு ஆர் இடைச்","சென்ற காதலர் வந்து இனிது முயங்கி","பிரியாது ஒரு வழி உறையினும் பெரிது அழிந்து","உயங்கினை மடந்தை என்றி தோழி","அற்றும் ஆகும் அஃது அறியாதோர்க்கே","வீழாக் கொள்கை வீழ்ந்த கொண்டி","மல்லல் மார்பு மடுத்தனன்","புல்லு மற்று எவனோ அன்பு இலங்கடையே"],"p":"","r":"To the friend who says that though her lover is back from the windy waste, where the lone palmyra bears fruit like ripe date clusters and the tiger walks when the male call sounds from its branch, and has embraced her sweetly and stays by her without parting, she is still wasted, the girl answers that it may look so to those who do not know: he has given his rich chest to a woman who takes without loving, and what is an embrace worth where there is no love?","n":"The friend measures the return by presence (பிரியாது ஒரு வழி உறையினும); the girl measures it by love and finds it gone. The paalai waste of the opening is only the road he came back by; the grief is a marutham grief, another woman at his chest. வீழாக் கொள்கை வீழ்ந்த கொண்டி is compressed and its sense is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","anbu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 174","lb":"NT174","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி வந்து எய்திய காலத்து ஆற்றாளாய தலைவியைத் தோழி வற்புறீஇ நின்றாட்கு அவள் சொல்லியது","thd":1,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","வற்புறீஇ நின்றாட்கு அவள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["ஈந்தின்","ஈந்து",1,"maram","simile","date palm"],["போந்தைக்","போந்தை",2,"maram","simile","palmyra"],["புலி","புலி",4,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:175":{"book":"natrinai","num":176,"t":["நெடுங் கடல் அலைத்த கொடுந் திமிற் பரதவர்","கொழு மீன் கொள்ளை அழி மணல் குவைஇ","மீன் நெய் அட்டிக் கிளிஞ்சில் பொத்திய","சிறு தீ விளக்கில் துஞ்சும் நறு மலர்ப்","புன்னை ஓங்கிய துறைவனொடு அன்னை","தான் அறிந்தன்றோ இலளே பானாள்","சேரிஅம் பெண்டிர் சிறு சொல் நம்பி","சுடுவான் போல நோக்கும்","அடு பால் அன்ன என் பசலை மெய்யே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend says that the mother does not even know the lord of the shore, where fishermen who tossed on the long sea in curved boats heap their fat catch on the sand and sleep by small lamps of shells filled with fish oil under the fragrant punnai; yet at midnight, trusting the petty talk of the hamlet women, she looks as if to burn at my body, pale as boiled milk.","n":"The mother has never seen the man and judges by gossip alone, so her burning look falls on the only evidence she can see, the pallor. The fishermen's small shell lamps, fires that let them sleep, stand against a mother's look that lets the girl have no rest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","pasalai","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 175","lb":"NT175","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",1,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["கொழு மீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","scene",""],["மீன் நெய்","மீன் நெய்",3,"pira","scene","fish oil"],["கிளிஞ்சில்","கிளிஞ்சில்",3,"pira","scene","shell"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",5,"maram","scene",""],["அடு பால்","பால்",9,"unavu","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:176":{"book":"natrinai","num":177,"t":["எம் நயந்து உறைவி ஆயின் யாம் நயந்து","நல்கினம் விட்டது என் நலத்தோன் அவ் வயின்","சால்பின் அளித்தல் அறியாது அவட்கு அவள்","காதலள் என்னுமோ உரைத்திசின் தோழி","நிரைத்த யானை முகத்து வரி கடுப்பப்","போது பொதி உடைந்த ஒண் செங் காந்தள்","வாழை அம் சிலம்பின் வம்பு படக் குவைஇ","யாழ் ஓர்த்தன்ன இன் குரல் இன வண்டு","அருவி முழவின் பாடொடு ஒராங்கு","மென்மெல இசைக்கும் சாரல்","குன்ற வேலித் தம் உறைவின் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"The other woman, speaking to a dancer so that those close to the wife's friend may hear, asks whether the wife can really claim to love the man when she does not know how to keep him with grace, since she herself loved him and freely let him go; and she points to their home, a hill-fenced village on the plantain slope where red kaanthal bursts from the bud like the stripes on elephants' faces and bees hum like a lute to the drum of the falling stream.","n":"The boast carries a jab: the rival says she let him go out of generosity, so his being at home is her gift, not the wife's hold. The soft hill-slope ending, bees and waterfall in one music, makes the wife's home sound idyllic only to say it could not keep him. Lines 1 to 4 are compressed and the sense is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","nayan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 176","lb":"NT176","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பரத்தை தலைவியின் பாங்கிக்குப் பாங்காயினார் கேட்ப விறலிக்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":1,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["parathai","virali","pira","karpu","பாங்காயினார் கேட்ப விறலிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","simile",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",6,"maram","simile",""],["வாழை","வாழை",7,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",8,"pira","simile",""],["அருவி","அருவி",9,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["முழவின்","முழவு",9,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:177":{"book":"natrinai","num":178,"t":["பரந்து படு கூர் எரி கானம் நைப்ப","மரம் தீயுற்ற மகிழ் தலைஅம் காட்டு","ஒதுக்கு அரும் வெஞ் சுரம் இறந்தனர் மற்றவர்","குறிப்பின் கண்டிசின் யானே நெறிப் பட","வேலும் இலங்கு இலை துடைப்ப பலகையும்","பீலி சூட்டி மணி அணிபவ்வே","பண்டினும் நனி பல அளிப்ப இனியே","வந்தன்று போலும் தோழி நொந்து நொந்து","எழுது எழில் உண்கண் பாவை","அழிதரு வெள்ளம் நீந்தும் நாளே"],"p":"","r":"Reading his signs, the girl tells her friend that he has already crossed in mind the hot, hard waste where spreading fire wears down the forest and the trees burn; she has seen it in his ways: his spear is wiped bright along the blade, his shield crowned with peacock feathers and hung with bells, and he is far more tender than before; now, friend, the day seems to have come when the pupils of her painted eyes, aching again and again, will swim in a rising flood.","n":"She reads two signs together, the war gear made ready and the excess of tenderness (பண்டினும் நனி பல அளிப்ப); either alone would mean little. Her tears become a flood the little figure in her eye must swim, grief turned into a drowning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","pirivu","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 177","lb":"NT177","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவுக் குறிப்பு அறிந்த தலைமகள் தோழிக்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"செலவுக் குறிப்பு அறிந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மரம்","மரம்",2,"maram","absent",""],["பீலி","பீலி",6,"pul","ornament","peacock feather"],["மணி","மணி",6,"pira","ornament","bell"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:178":{"book":"natrinai","num":179,"t":["ஆடு அமை ஆக்கம் ஐது பிசைந்தன்ன","தோடு அமை தூவித் தடந் தாள் நாரை","நலன் உணப்பட்ட நல்கூர் பேடை","கழி பெயர் மருங்கில் சிறு மீன் உண்ணாது","கைதை அம் படு சினைப் புலம்பொடு வதியும்","தண்ணம் துறைவன் தேரே கண்ணின்","காணவும் இயைந்தன்று மன்னே நாணி","நள்ளென் யாமத்தும் கண் படை பெறேஎன்","புள் ஒலி மணி செத்து ஓர்ப்ப","விளிந்தன்றுமாது அவர்த் தெளிந்த என் நெஞ்சே"],"p":"","r":"With the man standing by, the friend says that the chariot of the lord of the cool shore, where the poor female heron, whose beauty her big-legged mate with crest feathers like finely kneaded soft pith has enjoyed, will not eat the small fish at the backwater's edge but sits in loneliness on the drooping kaithai branch, could once be seen with the eyes; now, ashamed, I get no sleep even in the dead of night, I take every bird cry for his bells and listen, and my heart that trusted him has perished.","n":"The heron hen carries the whole situation unspoken: enjoyed and left (நலன் உணப்பட்ட), she will not eat, and she sits alone, as the girl, now kept at home (செறிப்பு), wastes and cannot sleep. The friend speaks the last lines in the girl's first person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","nenju","kalavu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 178","lb":"NT178","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["அமை","அமை",1,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","ullurai","heron"],["பேடை","பேடை",3,"pul","ullurai","female heron"],["சிறு மீன்","மீன்",4,"pira","scene",""],["கைதை","கைதை",5,"maram","scene",""],["புள்","புள்",9,"pul","scene",""],["மணி","மணி",9,"pira","simile","bell"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","பேடை நாரை","not_confirmed","0/1","the girl and the shore lord who enjoyed her love and now stays away"],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:179":{"book":"natrinai","num":180,"t":["இல் எழு வயலை ஈற்று ஆ தின்றென","பந்து நிலத்து எறிந்து பாவை நீக்கி","அவ் வயிறு அலைத்த என் செய் வினைக் குறுமகள்","மான் அமர்ப்பன்ன மையல் நோக்கமொடு","யானும் தாயும் மடுப்ப தேனொடு","தீம் பால் உண்ணாள் வீங்குவனள் விம்மி","நெருநலும் அனையள்மன்னே இன்றே","மை அணற் காளை பொய் புகலாக","அருஞ் சுரம் இறந்தனள் என்ப தன்","முருந்து ஏர் வெண் பல் முகிழ் நகை திறந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The foster mother recalls that when the calving cow ate the vayalai creeper growing by the house, her girl threw her ball to the ground, cast away her doll and beat her own belly, and with a bewildered look like a doe's would not drink the honey and sweet milk she and the mother pressed on her but swelled with sobs; she was like that only yesterday, and today they say she has crossed the hard waste trusting the false word of a dark-bearded youth, opening her budding smile of white teeth like peacock-quill tips.","n":"The poem ends on a smile: the child who sobbed over a creeper and refused milk from two mothers goes off smiling, and the bewilderment at home (மனை மருட்சி) is at that change. The speaker's யானும் தாயும் makes her the foster mother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 179","lb":"NT179","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மனை மருட்சி","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","pira",null,null,"மனை மருட்சி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வயலை","வயலை",1,"maram","scene",""],["ஈற்று ஆ","ஆ",1,"maa","scene","cow"],["மான்","மான்",4,"maa","simile","deer"],["தேனொடு","தேன்",5,"unavu","gift_food",""],["தீம் பால்","பால்",6,"unavu","gift_food",""],["முருந்து","முருந்து",10,"pira","simile","peacock quill"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:180":{"book":"natrinai","num":181,"t":["பழனப் பாகல் முயிறு மூசு குடம்பை","கழனி நாரை உரைத்தலின் செந்நெல்","விரவு வெள்ளரிசியின் தாஅம் ஊரன்","பலர்ப் பெறல் நசைஇ நம் இல் வாரலனே","மாயோள் நலத்தை நம்பி விடல் ஒல்லாளே","அன்னியும் பெரியன் அவனினும் விழுமிய","இரு பெரு வேந்தர் பொரு களத்து ஒழித்த","புன்னை விழுமம் போல","என்னொடு கழியும் இவ் இருவரது இகலே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, who had let the husband in, seeing the wife's jealousy says: the man of the town where the field heron rubs against the ant-swarmed nest on the bitter gourd and the ants spill like white rice mixed with red paddy, wanting many women, does not come to our house, and she, trusting her dark beauty, will not let him go; like the ruin of the punnai felled on the battlefield of two great kings, Anni great and the other greater, this quarrel of the two of them will end with me.","n":"The friend casts herself as the punnai, the tree destroyed because two powers fought over it, and the go-between who let the husband in fears she will pay for the couple's quarrel. The heron breaking the ants' nest is a near miss for ullurai (a man breaking a household), but only one pair maps. Who trusts the dark beauty in line 5 is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","igal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 180","lb":"NT180","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைமகற்கு வாயில் நேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகளிடத்துப் பொறாமை கண்டு சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"karpu","பொறாமை கண்டு சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["பாகல்","பாகல்",1,"maram","scene","bitter gourd"],["முயிறு","முயிறு",1,"pira","simile","red ant"],["நாரை","நாரை",2,"pul","scene","heron"],["செந்நெல்","நெல்",2,"unavu","simile",""],["வெள்ளரிசியின்","அரிசி",3,"unavu","simile",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",8,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["king","அன்னியும்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:9"},"nt:181":{"book":"natrinai","num":182,"t":["உள் இறைக் குரீஇக் கார் அணற் சேவல்","பிற புலத் துணையோடு உறை புலத்து அல்கி","வந்ததன் செவ்வி நோக்கி பேடை","நெறி கிளர் ஈங்கைப் பூவின் அன்ன","சிறு பல் பிள்ளையொடு குடம்பை கடிதலின்","துவலையின் நனைந்த புறத்தது அயலது","கூரல் இருக்கை அருளி நெடிது நினைந்து","ஈர நெஞ்சின் தன் வயின் விளிப்ப","கையற வந்த மையல் மாலை","இரீஇய ஆகலின் இன் ஒலி இழந்த","தார் அணி புரவி தண் பயிர் துமிப்ப","வந்தன்று பெருவிறல் தேரே","உய்ந்தன்றாகும் இவள் ஆய் நுதற் கவினே"],"p":"","r":"The friend watches the evening: a sparrow cock that had lodged with companions in other fields comes back drenched, and the hen, guarding her small chicks bright as iingai flowers, first bars him from the nest, then, seeing his wet back, gives him a place beside her and calls him in with a softened heart; because that helpless, bewildering evening had come, the chariot came, its garlanded horses cutting the cool crops, and the beauty of this girl's brow is saved.","n":"Line 2 lets the cock's night away be read as a stay with another mate, which would make the hen's relenting a lesson in forgiveness; the friend does not press it, and the poem only needs the evening return. The horses 'cutting the cool crops' shows the chariot taking the shortest way, through the fields.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","nalam","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 181","lb":"NT181","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றிப் புகுந்தது கண்ட தோழி மகிழ்ந்து உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றிப் புகுந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["சேவல்","குரீஇச் சேவல்",1,"pul","iraicchi","cock sparrow"],["பேடை","பேடை",3,"pul","iraicchi","hen sparrow"],["ஈங்கைப்","ஈங்கை",4,"maram","simile",""],["பிள்ளையொடு","பிள்ளை",5,"pul","scene",""],["குடம்பை","குடம்பை",5,"pira","scene","nest"],["புரவி","புரவி",11,"maa","scene","horse"],["பயிர்","பயிர்",11,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பேடை","not_confirmed","0/1","the wife who waited will take back the man who comes home at the rainy evening, and the house will close round him"],["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:182":{"book":"natrinai","num":183,"t":["நிலவும் மறைந்தன்று இருளும் பட்டன்று","ஓவத்து அன்ன இடனுடை வரைப்பின்","பாவை அன்ன நிற் புறங்காக்கும்","சிறந்த செல்வத்து அன்னையும் துஞ்சினள்","கெடுத்துப்படு நன் கலம் எடுத்துக் கொண்டாங்கு","நன் மார்பு அடைய முயங்கி மென்மெல","கண்டனம் வருகம் சென்மோ தோழி","கீழும் மேலும் காப்போர் நீத்த","வறுந் தலைப் பெருங் களிறு போல","தமியன் வந்தோன் பனியலை நீயே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening outside, the friend tells the girl the moon has set and darkness has come, and the mother of great wealth who guards her, doll-like, in the wide house that looks like a painting, sleeps; let us go softly, see him, and embrace his good chest close as one takes up a fine jewel that was lost, for he has come alone like a great tusker left by the keepers above and below, out in the cold.","n":"The proposal is addressed to the girl but aimed at the listener: the riskiness of the night, the sleeping guardian and his lonely state as a keeperless elephant all tell him that secret visits cannot go on, which is how the friend presses for marriage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 182","lb":"NT182","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரைவு நீட்டிப்ப தலைமகள் ஆற்றாமை அறிந்த தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லி வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",9,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:183":{"book":"natrinai","num":184,"t":["தம் நாட்டு விளைந்த வெண்ணெல் தந்து","பிற நாட்டு உப்பின் கொள்ளை சாற்றி","நெடு நெறி ஒழுகை நிலவு மணல் நீந்தி","அவண் உறை முனிந்த ஒக்கலொடு புலம் பெயர்ந்து","உமணர் போகலும் இன்னாதாகும்","மடவை மன்ற கொண்க வயின்தோறு","இன்னாது அலைக்கும் ஊதையொடு ஓரும்","நும் இல் புலம்பின் மாலையும் உடைத்தே","இன மீன் ஆர்ந்த வெண் குருகு மிதித்த","வறு நீர் நெய்தல் போல","வாழாள் ஆதல் சூழாதோயே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the shore lord, who is leaving with the marriage put off, that even the salt merchants' going is painful, who bring white paddy from their own country, cry the price of salt from another, drive long lines of carts across moonlit sand and move with their kin; he is foolish, for evenings with the cruel wind now hold the loneliness of his absence, and he does not consider that she will not live, like a neythal in shallow water trampled by a white heron that has fed on shoals of fish.","n":"The salt merchants are strangers who leave with their whole household and still sadden the shore; he leaves without taking anyone, which is the friend's point about marriage. The heron has fed full and the flower is trampled in passing: the harm is careless, not cruel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","varaivu","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 183","lb":"NT183","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரைவிடை வைத்துப் பிரியும் தலைவற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவிடை வைத்துப் பிரியும் தலைவற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வெண்ணெல்","வெண்ணெல்",1,"unavu","scene",""],["உப்பின்","உப்பு",2,"pira","scene",""],["உமணர்","உமணர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["ஊதையொடு","ஊதை",7,"pira","scene","cold wind"],["மீன்","மீன்",9,"pira","scene","fish"],["குருகு","குருகு",9,"pul","simile","heron"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",10,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:184":{"book":"natrinai","num":185,"t":["ஒரு மகள் உடையேன் மன்னே அவளும்","செரு மிகு மொய்ம்பின் கூர்வேற் காளையொடு","பெரு மலை அருஞ் சுரம் நெருநல் சென்றனள்","இனியே தாங்கு நின் அவலம் என்றிர் அது மற்று","யாங்ஙனம் ஒல்லுமோ அறிவுடையீரே","உள்ளின் உள்ளம் வேமே உண்கண்","மணி வாழ் பாவை நடை கற்றன்ன என்","அணி இயற் குறுமகள் ஆடிய","மணி ஏர் நொச்சியும் தெற்றியும் கண்டே"],"p":"","r":"The mother says she had one daughter, and she too went yesterday with a sharp-speared youth across the hard waste of the great hills; you tell me to bear my grief, wise ones, but how can that be, when my heart burns at the very thought, seeing the bright nochchi and the raised terrace where my lovely girl played, she who was like the figure in the eye's pupil learning to walk.","n":"'One daughter' is the whole argument against consolation: there is no other child to turn to. The pupil image is doubled, the dearest thing in the eye and a tiny figure only just learning to walk, so the girl is remembered as a toddler at the moment she has gone off as a bride.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ninaivu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 184","lb":"NT184","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மனை மருட்சி","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,null,"மனை மருட்சி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நொச்சியும்","நொச்சி",9,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:185":{"book":"natrinai","num":186,"t":["ஆனா நோயோடு அழி படர்க் கலங்கி","காமம் கைம்மிக கையறு துயரம்","காணவும் நல்காய் ஆயின் பாணர்","பரிசில் பெற்ற விரி உளை நல் மான்","கவி குளம்பு பொருத கல் மிசைச் சிறு நெறி","இரவலர் மெலியாது ஏறும் பொறையன்","உரை சால் உயர் வரைக் கொல்லிக் குடவயின்","அகல் இலைக் காந்தள் அலங்கு குலைப் பாய்ந்து","பறவை இழைத்த பல் கண் இறாஅல்","தேனுடை நெடு வரை தெய்வம் எழுதிய","வினை மாண் பாவை அன்னோள்","கொலை சூழ்ந்தனளால் நோகோ யானே"],"p":"","r":"The man says that if you will not even look on the helpless pain of passion grown past bearing, then she, like the finely wrought figure a god painted on the western face of the famous high Kolli of Porayan, where the bards' gift horses with worn hooves climb the small rock path and suppliants go up without tiring, where honeycombs hang among kaanthal clusters, has plotted my death; shall I grieve?","n":"நல்காய், 'you do not grant', suits the second alternative better, the friend who keeps him off. The girl is a figure painted by a god: a made, divine beauty, so the plot to kill is hers and not hers; and the long praise of Porayan's open-handed hill sets her refusal against a lord who turns no suppliant away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","azhagu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 185","lb":"NT185","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பாங்கற்குத் தலைவன் சொல்லியது சேட்படுக்கும் தோழிக்குத் தலைவன் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu","பாங்கற்குத் தலைவன் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu","சேட்படுக்கும் தோழிக்குத் தலைவன் சொல்லியதூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["மான்","மான்",4,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",8,"maram","scene",""],["பறவை","பறவை",9,"pira","scene","bee"],["இறாஅல்","இறால்",9,"pira","scene","honeycomb"],["தெய்வம்","தெய்வம்",10,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[["king","பொறையன்",6],["hill","கொல்லிக்",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:186":{"book":"natrinai","num":187,"t":["கல் ஊற்று ஈண்டல கயன் அற வாங்கி","இரும் பிணர்த் தடக் கை நீட்டி நீர் நொண்டு","பெருங் கை யானை பிடி எதிர் ஓடும்","கானம் வெம்பிய வறம் கூர் கடத்திடை","வேனில் ஓதி நிறம் பெயர் முது போத்து","பாண் யாழ் கடைய வாங்கி பாங்கர்","நெடு நிலை யாஅம் ஏறும் தொழில","பிறர்க்கு என முயலும் பேர் அருள் நெஞ்சமொடு","காமர் பொருட் பிணி போகிய","நாம் வெங் காதலர் சென்ற ஆறே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her wasting friend that the road their beloved took runs through a scorched, drought-struck forest where the great elephant, the rock springs not filling, stretches its rough trunk, scoops up water and runs to his mate, and the old lizard, its colour changed by summer, climbs the tall yaa bent like the end of a bard's lute; he went with a great-hearted wish to strive for others, held by the lovely bond of wealth.","n":"The roles are reversed: the wife defends the absent husband to the friend, and justifies the journey by its purpose, wealth sought to be given to others, not by its gain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 186","lb":"NT186","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தோழிக்குத் தலைவி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தோழிக்குத் தலைவி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",3,"maa","iraicchi","elephant"],["பிடி","பிடி",3,"maa","iraicchi","cow elephant"],["ஓதி","ஓதி",5,"pira","scene","lizard"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",6,"yaazh","scene",""],["யாஅம்","யா",7,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","யானை","confirmed","1/1","even on that road a male's first care is his mate, so the man crossing it holds his wife in mind and works for her"]],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:187":{"book":"natrinai","num":188,"t":["நெய்தல் கூம்ப நிழல் குணக்கு ஒழுக","கல் சேர் மண்டிலம் சிவந்து நிலம் தணிய","பல் பூங் கானலும் அல்கின்றன்றே","இன மணி ஒலிப்ப பொழுது படப் பூட்டி","மெய்ம் மலி காமத்து யாம் தொழுது ஒழிய","தேரும் செல் புறம் மறையும் ஊரொடு","யாங்கு ஆவதுகொல் தானே தேம் பட","ஊது வண்டு இமிரும் கோதை மார்பின்","மின் இவர் கொடும் பூண் கொண்கனொடு","இன் நகை மேவி நாம் ஆடிய பொழிலே"],"p":"","r":"The girl, to herself, watches the evening close in, the neythal folding, shadows running east, the sun reddening at the hill and the many-flowered shore grove going quiet, as his chariot is yoked with ringing bells and its back vanishes while she stands worshipping in full desire; she asks what will become, with the town, of the grove where they played and laughed with the shore lord of the bee-humming garland and bright curved ornaments.","n":"The worry is about the place, not the man: 'with the town' (ஊரொடு) fears that the grove of their meetings will become the town's talk. Every sign of the hour is also a sign of closing, the flower, the shadows, the grove, and then the chariot's back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","maalai","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 187","lb":"NT187","poet":"ஒளவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகன் பகற்குறி வந்து மீள்வானது செலவு நோக்கி தலைமகள் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லுவாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","nenju",null,"kalavu","பகற்குறி வந்து மீள்வானது செலவு நோக்கி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",1,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",8,"pira","scene","bee"],["கோதை","கோதை",8,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:188":{"book":"natrinai","num":189,"t":["படு நீர்ச் சிலம்பில் கலித்த வாழைக்","கொடு மடல் ஈன்ற கூர் வாய்க் குவி முகை","ஒள் இழை மகளிர் இலங்கு வளைத் தொடூஉம்","மெல் விரல் மோசை போல காந்தள்","வள் இதழ் தோயும் வான் தோய் வெற்ப","நன்றி விளைவும் தீதொடு வரும் என","அன்று நற்கு அறிந்தனள் ஆயின் குன்றத்துத்","தேம் முதிர் சிலம்பில் தடைஇய","வேய் மருள் பணைத் தோள் அழியலள்மன்னே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the sky-touching hill, where on a wet slope the banana's curved sheath has put out a sharp closed bud that touches the broad kaanthal petal as women's soft fingers touch their bright bangles, that had the girl known well then that even good fortune comes with harm, her bamboo-like shoulders would not now be wasting.","n":"The banana bud touching the kaanthal is a near miss for ullurai (the man's approach to the girl), but it gives one pair only, so it stays scene. The maxim of line 6, good bringing harm with it, turns their happy daytime meetings into the cause of her wasting, which is why the friend refuses them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kuri","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 188","lb":"NT188","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பகற்குறி மறுத்து வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகற்குறி மறுத்து வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வாழைக்","வாழை",1,"maram","scene",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",4,"maram","scene",""],["வேய்","வேய்",9,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:189":{"book":"natrinai","num":190,"t":["தம் அலது இல்லா நம் நயந்து அருளி","இன்னும் வாரார் ஆயினும் சென்னியர்","தெறல் அருங் கடவுள் முன்னர் சீறியாழ்","நரம்பு இசைத்தன்ன இன் குரற் குருகின்","கங்கை வங்கம் போகுவர்கொல்லோ","எவ் வினை செய்வர்கொல் தாமே வெவ் வினைக்","கொலை வல் வேட்டுவன் வலை பரிந்து போகிய","கானப் புறவின் சேவல் வாய் நூல்","சிலம்பி அம் சினை வெரூஉம்","அலங்கல் உலவை அம் காடு இறந்தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend says that though he, who has no one but us and loves us, has not yet come, will he really go off in the ships of the Ganges before the hard-to-anger god of the Chenniyar, where the heron's sweet voice sounds like a plucked string of the small lute; what can they be doing, who crossed the lovely forest of withered branches where the wood-dove cock that broke free of the cruel hunter's net now fears even the spider's thread on the bough?","n":"The dove that escaped the net and now shies at a cobweb is not iraicchi under the rule (it fears, it does not do what a lover does or should do); it gives the road its mood of fright after danger. Lines 2 to 5 are best taken as a reassuring question: he will not go as far as the Ganges.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 189","lb":"NT189","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",3,"deyvam","scene",""],["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",3,"yaazh","simile",""],["குருகின்","குருகு",4,"pul","simile","heron"],["வேட்டுவன்","வேட்டுவன்",7,"seythi","scene",""],["புறவின்","புறவு",8,"pul","scene","wood dove"],["சிலம்பி","சிலம்பி",9,"pira","scene","spider"],["உலவை","உலவை",10,"maram","absent",""]],"en":[["king","சென்னியர்",2],["river","கங்கை",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:190":{"book":"natrinai","num":191,"t":["நோ இனி வாழிய நெஞ்சே மேவார்","ஆர் அரண் கடந்த மாரி வண் மகிழ்த்","திதலை எஃகின் சேந்தன் தந்தை","தேம் கமழ் விரி தார் இயல் தேர் அழிசி","வண்டு மூசு நெய்தல் நெல்லிடை மலரும்","அரியல் அம் கழனி ஆர்க்காடு அன்ன","காமர் பணைத் தோள் நலம் வீறு எய்திய","வலை மான் மழைக் கண் குறுமகள்","சில் மொழித் துவர் வாய் நகைக்கு மகிழ்ந்தோயே"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart to suffer now, the heart that delighted in the few words and coral-mouthed smile of the young girl with eyes cool and frightened as a netted deer's, whose lovely shoulders took a beauty like Arkkadu of Azhisi, father of Chendan of the spotted spear, giver lavish as rain in his joy, lord of the fine chariot and fragrant garland, where neythal blooms among the paddy in fields of toddy.","n":"The heart is blamed for its pleasure: 'suffer now' because it once rejoiced. The fields of Arkkadu are marutham, so a kurinji love is measured by a lowland town; the colophon's three alternatives fit because the poem names no occasion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","nalam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 190","lb":"NT190","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பின்னின்ற தலைமகன் ஆற்றானாகி நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது அல்லகுறிப்பட்டு மீள்வான் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம் இடைச் சுரத்துச் சென்று தலைமகள் நலம் உள்ளி மீளலுற்ற நெஞ்சினைக் கழறியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","பின்னின்ற தலைமகன் ஆற்றானாகி நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","அல்லகுறிப்பட்டு மீள்வான் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியதூஉம்"],["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","இடைச் சுரத்துச் சென்று தலைமகள் நலம் உள்ளி மீளலுற்ற நெஞ்சினைக் கழறியதூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வண்டு","வண்டு",5,"pira","scene","bee"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",5,"maram","scene",""],["நெல்லிடை","நெல்",5,"maram","scene",""],["மான்","மான்",8,"maa","scene","deer"]],"en":[["king","சேந்தன்",3],["king","அழிசி",4],["town","ஆர்க்காடு",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:191":{"book":"natrinai","num":192,"t":["சிறு வீ ஞாழல் தேன் தோய் ஒள் இணர்","நேர் இழை மகளிர் வார் மணல் இழைத்த","வண்டற் பாவை வன முலை முற்றத்து","ஒண் பொறிச் சுணங்கின் ஐது படத் தாஅம்","கண்டல் வேலிக் காமர் சிறுகுடி","எல்லி வந்தன்றோ தேர் எனச் சொல்லி","அலர் எழுந்தன்று இவ் ஊரே பலருளும்","என் நோக்கினளே அன்னை நாளை","மணிப் பூ முண்டகம் கொய்யேன் ஆயின்","அணிக் கவின் உண்மையோ அரிதே மணிக் கழி","நறும் பூங் கானல் வந்து அவர்","வறுந் தேர் போதல் அதனினும் அரிதே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend says that in their lovely hamlet fenced with kandal, where the honeyed bright clusters of the small-flowered njaazhal fall lightly on the spotted breasts of the sand dolls the girls shape on the long sand, the town has raised talk, saying a chariot came at night, and among them all mother looked at me; if I do not pick the blue mundakam tomorrow there will be nothing to adorn her, and harder still will be his chariot coming to the fragrant shore grove and going away empty.","n":"The confinement is announced without naming it: if the girls cannot go out for flowers tomorrow, the man's chariot will find the grove empty. The flowers dropping on the sand doll's breasts recall the girls' play that is about to end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 191","lb":"NT191","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறமாக செறிப்பு அறிவுறுப்பான் வேண்டிச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறுப்பான் வேண்டிச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",1,"maram","scene",""],["கண்டல்","கண்டல்",5,"maram","scene",""],["முண்டகம்","முண்டகம்",9,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:192":{"book":"natrinai","num":193,"t":["குருதி வேட்கை உரு கெழு வய மான்","வலி மிகு முன்பின் மழ களிறு பார்க்கும்","மரம் பயில் சோலை மலிய பூழியர்","உருவத் துருவின் நாள் மேயல் ஆரும்","மாரி எண்கின் மலைச் சுர நீள் இடை","நீ நயந்து வருதல் எவன் எனப் பல புலந்து","அழுதனை உறையும் அம் மா அரிவை","பயம் கெழு பலவின் கொல்லிக் குட வரைப்","பூதம் புணர்த்த புதிது இயல் பாவை","விரி கதிர் இள வெயில் தோன்றி அன்ன நின்","ஆய் நலம் உள்ளி வரின் எமக்கு","ஏமம் ஆகும் மலைமுதல் ஆறே"],"p":"","r":"The man tells the girl, who wept and complained at length asking why he comes in love by the long mountain path where the blood-thirsty tiger watches the young elephant and rain-dark bears graze daily like the Puzhiyar's flocks in the dense grove, that when he comes thinking of her fine beauty, like the new figure the spirits set on the western hill of Kolli rich in jackfruit, shining like young sunlight of spreading rays, the mountain path itself becomes his safety.","n":"Her fear lists the dangers, his answer does not deny them: the thought of her is a charm (ஏமம்) that makes the road safe. The comparison to a figure set up by spirits gives her beauty the protective power of a shrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","nalam","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 192","lb":"NT192","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி மறுக்கப்பட்டு ஆற்றானாய தலைமகன் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி மறுக்கப்பட்டு ஆற்றானாய தலைமகன் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["மான்","வய மான்",1,"maa","scene","tiger"],["களிறு","களிறு",2,"maa","scene","elephant"],["துருவின்","துரு",4,"maa","scene","sheep"],["எண்கின்","எண்கு",5,"maa","scene","bear"],["பலவின்","பலா",8,"maram","scene",""],["பூதம்","பூதம்",9,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[["hill","கொல்லிக்",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:193":{"book":"natrinai","num":194,"t":["அட்டரக்கு உருவின் வட்டு முகை ஈங்கைத்","துய்த் தலைப் புது மலர்த் துளி தலைக் கலாவ","நிறை நீர்ப் புனிற்றுப் புலம் துழைஇ ஆனாய்","இரும் புறம் தழூஉம் பெருந் தண் வாடை","நினக்குத் தீது அறிந்தன்றோ இலமே","பணைத் தோள் எல் வளை ஞெகிழ்த்த எம் காதலர்","அருஞ் செயல் பொருட் பிணிப் பிரிந்தனராக","யாரும் இல் ஒரு சிறை இருந்து","பேர் அஞர் உறுவியை வருத்தாதீமே"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks the great cold north wind, which roams the new-wet flooded fields, mixes drops into the soft-tipped new flowers of the iingai with round buds the colour of molten lac, and endlessly hugs her dark back, whether she has done it any harm: her lover, who loosened the bright bangles of her shoulders, has left for hard-won wealth, so let it not torment one who sits alone in a corner in great sorrow.","n":"The wind 'embraces her back' (இரும் புறம் தழூஉம்): the only embrace she has is the cold one, and she reproaches it as if it were a person doing a wrong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 193","lb":"NT193","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகள் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["ஈங்கைத்","ஈங்கை",1,"maram","scene",""],["வாடை","வாடை",4,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:194":{"book":"natrinai","num":195,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி கைம்மாறு","யாது செய்வாங்கொல் நாமே கய வாய்க்","கன்றுடை மருங்கின் பிடி புணர்ந்து இயலும்","வலன் உயர் மருப்பின் நிலம் ஈர்த் தடக் கை","அண்ணல் யானைக்கு அன்றியும் கல் மிசைத்","தனி நிலை இதணம் புலம்பப் போகி","மந்தியும் அறியா மரம் பயில் ஒரு சிறை","குன்ற வெற்பனொடு நாம் விளையாட","இரும்பு கவர்கொண்ட ஏனற்","பெருங் குரல் கொள்ளாச் சிறு பசுங் கிளிக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, with the hill lord listening, asks what return they can make, not only to the lordly elephant with high tusks and trailing trunk that moves with his mate beside their soft-mouthed calf, but to the small green parrot that can no longer take the great ears of millet the sickle has seized, since thanks to them they played with the hill lord in a hidden corner of dense trees that even the monkey does not know, leaving the lone guard platform on the rock deserted.","n":"The thanks are ironic and the news is plain: the millet is reaped, so the girls' guarding and their meetings in the field are over. Why the elephant is thanked is not said; it may be the danger that kept others off the field.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","nanri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 194","lb":"NT194","poet":"மதுரை மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகச் செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகச் செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["பிடி","பிடி",3,"maa","iraicchi","cow elephant"],["யானைக்கு","யானை",5,"maa","iraicchi","elephant"],["இதணம்","இதணம்",6,"seythi","scene","guard platform"],["மந்தியும்","மந்தி",7,"maa","scene","monkey"],["ஏனற்","ஏனல்",9,"maram","scene","millet"],["கிளிக்கே","கிளி",10,"pul","scene","parrot"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","யானைக்கு","not_confirmed","0/1","the hill lord should keep the girl as the tusker keeps his mate and young, in a household, now that the field meetings have ended"]],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:195":{"book":"natrinai","num":196,"t":["அருளாயாகலோ கொடிதே இருங் கழிக்","குருளை நீர்நாய் கொழு மீன் மாந்தி","தில்லைஅம் பொதும்பில் பள்ளி கொள்ளும்","மெல்லம் புலம்ப யான் கண்டிசினே","கல்லென் புள்ளின் கானல்அம் தொண்டி","நெல் அரி தொழுவர் கூர் வாள் உற்றென","பல் இதழ் தயங்கிய கூம்பா நெய்தல்","நீர் அலைத் தோற்றம் போல","ஈரிய கலுழும் நீ நயந்தோள் கண்ணே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the gentle shore, where an otter cub gorges on fat fish of the dark backwater and sleeps in the thillai thicket, that it is cruel not to show grace: she has seen that the eyes of the girl he loves weep wet, like the many-petalled unclosing neythal of Thondi, shore of clamouring birds, tossing in water when the reapers' sharp sickles strike it.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kan","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 195","lb":"NT195","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"களவின்கண் நெடுங்காலம் வந்தொழுக ஆற்றாளாயின தோழி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நீர்நாய்","நீர்நாய்",2,"maa","ullurai","otter"],["மீன்","மீன்",2,"pira","ullurai","fish"],["தில்லை","தில்லை",3,"maram","scene",""],["புள்ளின்","புள்",5,"pul","scene",""],["நெல்","நெல்",6,"maram","scene",""],["தொழுவர்","தொழுவர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",7,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["town","தொண்டி",5]],"dv":[["ullurai","நீர்நாய் மீன்","not_confirmed","0/1","the lover who enjoys the girl and rests at ease"],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:196":{"book":"natrinai","num":197,"t":["பளிங்கு செறிந்தன்ன பல் கதிர் இடைஇடை","பால் முகந்தன்ன பசு வெண் நிலவின்","மால்பு இடர் அறியா நிறையுறு மதியம்","சால்பும் செம்மையும் உடையை ஆதலின்","நிற் கரந்து உறையும் உலகம் இன்மையின்","எற் கரந்து உறைவோர் உள்வழி காட்டாய்","நற் கவின் இழந்த என் தோள் போல் சாஅய்","சிறுகுபு சிறுகுபு செரீஇ","அறி கரி பொய்த்தலின் ஆகுமோ அதுவே"],"p":"","r":"The woman, alone with the full moon, tells it that since it has nobility and rectitude, a moon of many crystal rays and fresh white light as if scooped from milk, which no ladder reaches, and since no world can hide from it, it should show where they live who hide from her; or else, shrinking little by little like her shoulders that lost their beauty, it will waste, as a witness that knows and lies must.","n":"She argues like a litigant: the moon sees everything, so it is a witness, and a witness who knows and keeps silent is a liar; its monthly waning is then the punishment she wishes on it, the same wasting her own shoulders suffer.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 196","lb":"NT196","poet":"வெள்ளைக்குடி நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"நெட்டிடை கழிந்து பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்த காலத்து ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகள் திங்கள் மேலிட்டுத் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu","திங்கள் மேலிட்டுத் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:197":{"book":"natrinai","num":198,"t":["தோளே தொடி நெகிழ்ந்தனவே நுதலே","பீர் இவர் மலரின் பசப்பு ஊர்ந்தன்றே","கண்ணும் தண் பனி வைகின அன்னோ","தெளிந்தனம் மன்ற தேயர் என் உயிர் என","ஆழல் வாழி தோழி நீ நின்","தாழ்ந்து ஒலி கதுப்பின் வீழ்ந்த காலொடு","வண்டு படு புது மலர் உண்துறைத் தரீஇய","பெரு மட மகளிர் முன்கைச் சிறு கோல்","பொலந் தொடி போல மின்னி கணங் கொள்","இன் இசை முரசின் இரங்கி மன்னர்","எயில் ஊர் பல் தோல் போலச்","செல் மழை தவழும் அவர் நல் மலை நாட்டே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl, whose bangles slip, whose brow is overrun with pallor like peerkku flowers and whose eyes hold cool tears, not to sink saying her life will surely wane: in his good hill country the moving clouds now crawl, flashing like the gold bangles on the small forearms of young women who bring bee-visited new flowers to the bathing ghat, thundering like a troop of sweet-voiced drums, massed like the many shields of kings scaling forts.","n":"The colophon is paalai, but the poem's comfort is the rainy season on his hills, the time a man returns; the three similes move from women's bangles to drums to shields, as if the clouds were an army coming home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","pasalai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 197","lb":"NT197","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"வரைவு நீட ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகளைத் தோழி ஆற்றுவித்தது","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு நீட ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகளைத் தோழி ஆற்றுவித்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பீர்","பீர்",2,"maram","simile",""],["மலர்","மலர்",7,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",7,"pira","scene","bee"],["முரசின்","முரசு",10,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:198":{"book":"natrinai","num":199,"t":["சேயின் வரூஉம் மதவலி யா உயர்ந்து","ஓமை நீடிய கான் இடை அத்தம்","முன்நாள் உம்பர்க் கழிந்த என் மகள்","கண்பட நீர் ஆழ்ந்தன்றே தந்தை","தன் ஊர் இடவயின் தொழுவேன் நுண் பல்","கோடு ஏந்து அல்குல் அரும்பிய திதலை","வார்ந்து இலங்கு வால் எயிற்று பொலிந்த தாஅர்","சில் வளை பல் கூந்தலளே அவளே","மை அணல் எருத்தின் முன்பின் தடக் கை","வல் வில் அம்பின் எய்யா வண் மகிழ்த்","தந்தைதன் ஊர் இதுவே","ஈன்றேன் யானே பொலிக நும் பெயரே"],"p":"","r":"The woman who followed the girl into the waste tells those she meets on the forest path of tall yaa and omai that her daughter passed this way yesterday, and her eyes are sunk in tears; she describes the girl, with spotted waist, fine bright teeth, few bangles and thick hair, learns that this is the town of the youth's father, a strong bowman whose arrows do not miss and who gives freely in his joy, and blesses them: I am the one who bore her; may your name prosper.","n":"The search ends in a blessing rather than a demand: finding herself at the house of the youth's father, the woman gives the girl up and wishes the family well.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 198","lb":"NT198","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பின் சென்ற செவிலி இடைச் சுரத்துக் கண்டார்க்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","kandor",null,null,"இடைச் சுரத்துக் கண்டார்க்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["யா","யா",1,"maram","scene",""],["ஓமை","ஓமை",2,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:199":{"book":"natrinai","num":200,"t":["ஓங்கு மணல் உடுத்த நெடு மாப் பெண்ணை","வீங்கு மடல் குடம்பைப் பைதல் வெண் குருகு","நள்ளென் யாமத்து உயவுதோறு உருகி","அள்ளல் அன்ன என் உள்ளமொடு உள் உடைந்து","உளெனே வாழி தோழி வளை நீர்க்","கடுஞ் சுறா எறிந்த கொடுந் திமிற் பரதவர்","வாங்கு விசைத் தூண்டில் ஊங்குஊங்கு ஆகி","வளி பொரக் கற்றை தாஅய் நளி சுடர்","நீல் நிற விசும்பின் மீனொடு புரைய","பைபய இமைக்கும் துறைவன்","மெய் தோய் முயக்கம் காணா ஊங்கே"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that each time the grieving white heron in its nest in the swollen frond of the tall palmyra on the high dune cries in the dense midnight, she melts, her heart breaking inside like a mire; she lives on only until she meets the body-touching embrace of the shore lord, where the lamps of fishers in curved boats that hunt fierce sharks, strung out on their bent lines and scattered by the wind, twinkle slowly like stars in the blue sky.","n":"She answers the friend's comfort by refusing it: she 'lives' (உளெனே) only provisionally. The heron only cries, so it carries mood, not iraicchi; the twinkling lamps of the fishers are the lights of his shore seen from hers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nenju","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 199","lb":"NT199","poet":"பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வன்புறை எதிரழிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வன்புறை எதிரழிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",1,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["குருகு","குருகு",2,"pul","scene","heron"],["சுறா","சுறா",6,"pira","scene","shark"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",6,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:200":{"book":"natrinai","num":201,"t":["கண்ணி கட்டிய கதிர அன்ன","ஒண் குரல் நொச்சித் தெரியல் சூடி","யாறு கிடந்தன்ன அகல் நெடுந் தெருவில்","சாறு என நுவலும் முது வாய்க் குயவ","ஈதும் ஆங்கண் நுவன்றிசின் மாதோ","ஆம்பல் அமன்ற தீம் பெரும் பழனத்துப்","பொய்கை ஊர்க்குப் போவோய்ஆகி","கை கவர் நரம்பின் பனுவற் பாணன்","செய்த அல்லல் பல்குவ வை எயிற்று","ஐது அகல் அல்குல் மகளிர் இவன்","பொய் பொதி கொடுஞ் சொல் ஓம்புமின் எனவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, while the bard who came as go-between listens, calls the old-tongued potter who, wearing a nochchi garland of bright clusters like a chaplet of grain ears, cries the festival through the long wide street that lies like a river, and asks him to cry this too in the pond town of sweet fields thick with water-lily: the troubles this bard of the hand-plucked lute has caused are many, so let the women with fine teeth and broad hips guard themselves from his lie-wrapped crooked words.","n":"The insult is public by design: the town crier is asked to proclaim the bard a liar, so the refusal reaches the husband through the whole street rather than through his messenger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 200","lb":"NT200","poet":"கூடலூர்ப் பல் கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகளது குறிப்பு அறிந்து வாயிலாகப் புக்க பாணன் கேட்ப குயவனைக் கூவி இங்ஙனம் சொல்லாயோ என்று குயவற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira","paanan","karpu","குயவனைக் கூவி இங்ஙனம் சொல்லாயோ என்று குயவற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["நொச்சித்","நொச்சி",2,"maram","ornament",""],["குயவ","குயவன்",4,"seythi","scene","potter"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",6,"maram","scene",""],["நரம்பின்","நரம்பு",8,"yaazh","scene",""],["பாணன்","பாணன்",8,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:10"},"nt:201":{"book":"natrinai","num":202,"t":["மலை உறை குறவன் காதல் மட மகள்","பெறல் அருங்குரையள் அருங் கடிக் காப்பினள்","சொல் எதிர் கொள்ளாள் இளையள் அனையோள்","உள்ளல் கூடாது என்றோய் மற்றும்","செவ் வேர்ப் பலவின் பயம் கெழு கொல்லித்","தெய்வம் காக்கும் தீது தீர் நெடுங் கோட்டு","அவ் வெள் அருவிக் குட வரையகத்து","கால் பொருது இடிப்பினும் கதழ் உறை கடுகினும்","உரும் உடன்று எறியினும் ஊறு பல தோன்றினும்","பெரு நிலம் கிளரினும் திரு நல உருவின்","மாயா இயற்கைப் பாவையின்","போதல் ஒல்லாள் என் நெஞ்சத்தானே"],"p":"","r":"The man answers the companion who told him the hill hunter's young daughter is too closely guarded, too young and too unwilling to be thought of: like the image on Kolli's western hill that the god guards, which no wind, lashing rain, thunder or heaving earth can move, she will not leave his heart.","n":"The companion's four objections (hard to win, guarded, deaf to words, young) are met by five assaults on the image (wind, rain, thunder, many harms, the earth rising), so the reply outnumbers the rebuke; and the guarded girl is answered by a guarded image, the god's keeping set against her father's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","nenju","ninaivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 201","lb":"NT201","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"கழறிய பாங்கற்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu","கழறிய பாங்கற்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குறவன்","குறவன்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["பலவின்","பலா",5,"maram","scene",""],["தெய்வம்","தெய்வம்",6,"deyvam","scene",""],["அருவிக்","அருவி",7,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[["hill","கொல்லித்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:202":{"book":"natrinai","num":203,"t":["புலி பொரச் சிவந்த புலால் அம் செங் கோட்டு","ஒலி பல் முத்தம் ஆர்ப்ப வலி சிறந்து","வன் சுவல் பராரை முருக்கி கன்றொடு","மடப் பிடி தழீஇய தடக் கை வேழம்","தேன் செய் பெருங் கிளை இரிய வேங்கைப்","பொன் புரை கவளம் புறந்தருபு ஊட்டும்","மா மலை விடரகம் கவைஇ காண்வர","கண்டிசின் வாழியோ குறுமகள் நுந்தை","அறுமீன் பயந்த அறம் செய் திங்கள்","செல் சுடர் நெடுங் கொடி போல","பல் பூங் கோங்கம் அணிந்த காடே"],"p":"","r":"Eloping with the girl, the man bids her look at the great hill's rifts, where a tusker whose tusk reddened fighting a tiger breaks a thick tree and feeds his cow and calf golden mouthfuls of vengai, and at the forest decked with kongam flowers like the long rows of lamps lit in the holy month of the six stars.","n":"The festival of the Karthigai month is the girl's home festival, so the hero dresses the strange forest in the lights she has just left; நுந்தை in line 8 (your father) is hard to place, and may tie the lamps to her father's town.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","punarchi","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 202","lb":"NT202","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங் கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உடன் போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","உடன் போகாநின்ற"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["புலி","புலி",1,"maa","scene",""],["முத்தம்","முத்து",2,"pira","scene",""],["வேழம்","வேழம்",4,"maa","iraicchi",""],["பிடி","பிடி",4,"maa","scene","cow elephant"],["தேன்","தேன்",5,"pira","scene","bee swarm"],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",5,"maram","gift_food","kino tree"],["கோங்கம்","கோங்கு",11,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வேழம்","confirmed","1/1","the man leading her away from her kin will feed and shelter her on the road as the tusker does his mate"]],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:203":{"book":"natrinai","num":204,"t":["முழங்கு திரை கொழீஇய மூரி எக்கர்","தடந் தாட் தாழை முள்ளுடை நெடுந் தோட்டு","அக மடல் பொதுளிய முகை முதிர் வான் பூங்","கோடு வார்ந்தன்ன வெண் பூத் தாழை","எறி திரை உதைத்தலின் பொங்கித் தாது சோர்பு","சிறுகுடிப் பாக்கத்து மறுகு புலா மறுக்கும்","மணம் கமழ் கானல் இயைந்த நம் கேண்மை","ஒரு நாள் பிரியினும் உய்வு அரிது என்னாது","கதழ் பரி நெடுந் தேர் வரவு ஆண்டு அழுங்கச்","செய்த தன் தப்பல் அன்றியும்","உயவுப் புணர்ந்தன்று இவ் அழுங்கல் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend says that in the fragrant shore grove, where screwpine flowers like drawn-out conch burst their pollen under the waves and wipe the fish smell from the hamlet's street, their bond was formed, and he, not thinking a day apart unbearable, has let his swift chariot stop coming, a fault of his own, while this noisy town has fallen into distress.","n":"The screwpine's scent that overcomes the street's fish smell is a near miss for ullurai (the love whose fragrance escapes into the town as talk): only one pair is clear, so it stays scene; the closing 'this clamorous town' turns the gossip itself into the sufferer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 203","lb":"NT203","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாக தோழி சொல்லி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["திரை","திரை",1,"pira","scene",""],["தாழை","தாழை",2,"maram","scene",""],["தாழை","தாழை",4,"maram","simile",""],["புலா","புலவு",6,"pira","scene","fish smell"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:204":{"book":"natrinai","num":205,"t":["தளிர் சேர் தண் தழை தைஇ நுந்தை","குளிர் வாய் வியன் புனத்து எல் பட வருகோ","குறுஞ் சுனைக் குவளை அடைச்சி நாம் புணரிய","நறுந் தண் சாரல் ஆடுகம் வருகோ","இன் சொல் மேவலைப்பட்ட என் நெஞ்சு உணக்","கூறு இனி மடந்தை நின் கூர் எயிறு உண்கு என","யான் தன் மொழிதலின் மொழி எதிர் வந்து","தான் செய் குறி நிலை இனிய கூறி","ஏறு பிரி மடப் பிணை கடுப்ப வேறுபட்டு","உறு கழை நிவப்பின் சிறுகுடிப் பெயரும்","கொடிச்சி செல்புறம் நோக்கி","விடுத்த நெஞ்சம் விடல் ஒல்லாதே"],"p":"","r":"The man who has followed the girl tells his heart, with the friend listening, how he asked whether he should come at dusk to her father's millet field wearing a leaf skirt or bathe with her on the slope of their meeting, begging a word to slake his heart; she answered by naming sweetly the place of tryst, and went off changed, like a young doe parted from its stag, to the bamboo-high village, and his heart, sent after her, will not let her go.","n":"He offers to come in a leaf skirt (தழை), the girl's own dress, so the lover proposes to pass unseen as one of the millet guards; and the heart he 'sent' with her is the subject of the last line, as if the heart, not he, had refused to return.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 204","lb":"NT204","poet":"மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பின்னின்ற தலைமகன் ஆற்றானாய் தோழி கேட்பத் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju","thozhi","kalavu","பின்னின்ற தலைமகன் ஆற்றானாய்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தழை","தழை",1,"maram","ornament",""],["குவளை","குவளை",3,"maram","ornament",""],["பிணை","பிணை",9,"maa","simile","doe"],["ஏறு","ஏறு",9,"maa","simile","stag"],["கழை","கழை",10,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:205":{"book":"natrinai","num":206,"t":["அருவி ஆர்க்கும் பெரு வரை அடுக்கத்து","ஆளி நன் மான் வேட்டு எழு கோள் உகிர்ப்","பூம் பொறி உழுவை தொலைச்சிய வைந் நுதி","ஏந்து வெண் கோட்டு வயக் களிறு இழுக்கும்","துன் அருங் கானம் என்னாய் நீயே","குவளை உண்கண் இவள் ஈண்டு ஒழிய","ஆள்வினைக்கு அகறிஆயின் இன்றொடு","போயின்றுகொல்லோ தானே படப்பைக்","கொடு முள் ஈங்கை நெடு மா அம் தளிர்","நீர் மலி கதழ் பெயல் தலைஇய","ஆய் நிறம் புரையும் இவள் மாமைக் கவினே"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart, which counts as nothing the forest by the waterfall slopes where the aali drags off the white-tusked bull elephant that killed a striped tiger, that if he leaves for wealth with her here, her dark beauty, like the fine colour of tender mango and thorny eengai shoots in the garden after heavy rain, will be gone from this very day.","n":"The forest's chain of killing (tiger by elephant, elephant by aali) measures danger to the traveller; the beauty at home is measured by tender rain-washed shoots, so the poem sets the strongest beast against the most perishable leaf, and the leaf wins: he stays.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aalvinai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 205","lb":"NT205","poet":"இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லி செலவு அழுங்கியது தோழி செலவு அழுங்கச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","செலவு அழுங்கியது"],["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தோழி செலவு அழுங்கச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","scene",""],["ஆளி","ஆளி",2,"maa","scene",""],["உழுவை","உழுவை",3,"maa","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",4,"maa","scene",""],["குவளை","குவளை",6,"maram","simile",""],["ஈங்கை","ஈங்கை",9,"maram","simile",""],["மா","மா",9,"maram","simile","mango"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:206":{"book":"natrinai","num":207,"t":["துய்த் தலைப் புனிற்றுக் குரல் பால் வார்பு இறைஞ்சி","தோடு அலைக் கொண்டன ஏனல் என்று","துறு கல் மீமிசைக் குறுவன குழீஇ","செவ் வாய்ப் பாசினம் கவரும் என்று அவ் வாய்த்","தட்டையும் புடைத்தனை கவணையும் தொடுக்க என","எந்தை வந்து உரைத்தனனாக அன்னையும்","நல் நாள் வேங்கையும் மலர்கமா இனி என","என் முகம் நோக்கினள் எவன்கொல் தோழி","செல்வாள் என்றுகொல் செறிப்பல் என்றுகொல்","கல் கெழு நாடன் கேண்மை","அறிந்தனள்கொல் அஃது அறிகலென் யானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, with the man listening, says that father came saying the milky millet ears are bending and red-beaked parrots gather on the rocks, so beat the clapper and string the sling; and mother, saying 'now may the vengai of the good day flower', looked at her face: does she mean to let them go out or to shut them in, and has she learned of their bond with the man of the stony land?","n":"The vengai's flowering marks the season of weddings, so the mother's wish is ambiguous on purpose: a wedding with a stranger or a suspicion of the lover; the friend's 'I do not know' is the pressure on the listening man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","kurippu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 206","lb":"NT206","poet":"ஐயூர் முடவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறத்தானாகத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",2,"maram","scene",""],["பாசினம்","பாசினம்",4,"pul","scene","parrot flock"],["தட்டையும்","தட்டை",5,"seythi","scene",""],["கவணையும்","கவண்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["வேங்கையும்","வேங்கை",7,"maram","scene","kino tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:207":{"book":"natrinai","num":208,"t":["கண்டல் வேலிக் கழி சூழ் படப்பை","முண்டகம் வேய்ந்த குறியிறைக் குரம்பைக்","கொழு மீன் கொள்பவர் பாக்கம் கல்லென","நெடுந் தேர் பண்ணி வரல் ஆனாதே","குன்றத்து அன்ன குவவு மணல் நீந்தி","வந்தனர் பெயர்வர்கொல் தாமே அல்கல்","இளையரும் முதியரும் கிளையுடன் குழீஇ","கோட் சுறா எறிந்தென சுருங்கிய நரம்பின்","முடி முதிர் பரதவர் மட மொழிக் குறுமகள்","வலையும் தூண்டிலும் பற்றி பெருங் கால்","திரை எழு பௌவம் முன்னிய","கொலை வெஞ் சிறாஅர் பாற்பட்டனளே"],"p":"","r":"Telling the foster mother the truth when strangers seek the girl, the friend says a tall chariot keeps coming noisily to the fishers' hamlet of thatched huts among the backwaters, crossing dunes like hills, and asks whether its rider will turn back; yet in a night gathering of young and old, the girl of the old shark-scarred fishermen has been given to the murderous young men who go to sea with net and line.","n":"The chariot is the lover's and marks a man not of the fishers; the friend sets it against the kin's choice of sea-going youths, so the revelation is made by contrast, without naming him; who the 'fierce youths' are (the strangers' party or the girl's own kin) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 207","lb":"NT207","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"நொதுமலர் வரைவுழி தோழி செவிலிக்கு அறத்தொடு நின்றது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu","செவிலிக்கு அறத்தொடு நின்றது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கண்டல்","கண்டல்",1,"maram","scene",""],["முண்டகம்","முண்டகம்",2,"maram","scene",""],["மீன்","மீன்",3,"pira","scene",""],["சுறா","சுறா",8,"pira","scene",""],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",9,"seythi","scene",""],["வலையும்","வலை",10,"seythi","scene",""],["தூண்டிலும்","தூண்டில்",10,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:208":{"book":"natrinai","num":209,"t":["விறல் சால் விளங்கு இழை நெகிழ விம்மி","அறல் போல் தெள் மணி இடை முலை நனைப்ப","விளிவு இல கலுழும் கண்ணொடு பெரிது அழிந்து","எவன் இனைபு வாடுதி சுடர் நுதற் குறுமகள்","செல்வார் அல்லர் நம் காதலர் செலினும்","நோன்மார் அல்லர் நோயே மற்று அவர்","கொன்னும் நம்புங் குரையர் தாமே","சிறந்த அன்பினர் சாயலும் உரியர்","பிரிந்த நம்மினும் இரங்கி அரும் பொருள்","முடியாதுஆயினும் வருவர் அதன்தலை","இன் துணைப் பிரிந்தோர் நாடித்","தருவது போலும் இப் பெரு மழைக் குரலே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the weeping girl why she wilts, her bangles loosening and tears clear as stream water wetting her breasts: their lover will not go, and if he goes he cannot bear the pain; he deserves trust, loves deeply and grieves more than they, and will come back even with his wealth unwon; besides, this great rain's voice sounds as if it were seeking out those parted from sweet partners to bring them home.","n":"The friend reasons in steps (he won't go; if he goes he'll suffer; if he suffers he'll return), then gives the clinching proof to the rain, which she makes a fetcher of the parted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","anbu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 208","lb":"NT208","poet":"நொச்சி நியமங் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவுற்றாரது குறிப்பு அறிந்து ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகள் உரைப்ப தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","செலவுற்றாரது குறிப்பு அறிந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:209":{"book":"natrinai","num":210,"t":["மலை இடம்படுத்துக் கோட்டிய கொல்லைத்","தளி பதம் பெற்ற கான் உழு குறவர்","சில வித்து அகல இட்டென பல விளைந்து","இறங்குகுரல் பிறங்கிய ஏனல் உள்ளாள்","மழலை அம் குறுமகள் மிழலைஅம் தீம் குரல்","கிளியும் தாம் அறிபவ்வே எனக்கே","படும்கால் பையுள் தீரும் படாஅது","தவிரும்காலைஆயின் என்","உயிரோடு எல்லாம் உடன் வாங்கும்மே"],"p":"","r":"Refused, the man tells his heart that the girl in the millet field, where hill farmers sowed a few seeds widely on a cleared slope after rain and reaped plenty, has a sweet lisping voice that even the parrots know; when it reaches him his sorrow ends, and if it stays away it will take his life and everything with it.","n":"The few seeds that yield a heavy crop sit beside the friend's small favour he begs for (the colophon says the friend hears and grants the suit); one pair only, so a near miss for ullurai, left as scene. The parrots 'know' her voice because they hear her scare them all day.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kaamanoy","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 209","lb":"NT209","poet":"நொச்சி நியமங்கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"குறை மறுக்கப்பட்டுப் பின்னின்ற தலைமகன் ஆற்றானாய் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லுவானாய்ச் சொல்லியது தோழி கேட்டுக் குறை முடிப்பது பயன்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju","thozhi","kalavu","குறை மறுக்கப்பட்டுப் பின்னின்ற"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குறவர்","குறவர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",4,"maram","scene",""],["கிளியும்","கிளி",6,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:210":{"book":"natrinai","num":211,"t":["அரிகால் மாறிய அம் கண் அகல் வயல்","மறு கால் உழுத ஈரச் செறுவின்","வித்தொடு சென்ற வட்டி பற்பல","மீனொடு பெயரும் யாணர் ஊர","நெடிய மொழிதலும் கடிய ஊர்தலும்","செல்வம் அன்று தன் செய் வினைப் பயனே","சான்றோர் செல்வம் என்பது சேர்ந்தோர்","புன்கண் அஞ்சும் பண்பின்","மென் கட் செல்வம் செல்வம் என்பதுவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the rich town, where the seed basket carried to a re-ploughed wet field comes home full of fish, that grand talk and harsh riding are not wealth but the fruit of one's deeds; what the worthy call wealth is the gentle-eyed wealth of a nature that fears the distress of those who depend on one.","n":"The friend grants him entry while lecturing him, and the lecture is a definition of wealth aimed at a rich man who has hurt his dependant wife. The basket that went out with seed and returns with fish (more than was sent) is a near miss for ullurai: one pair only.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kannottam","saandror","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 210","lb":"NT210","poet":"மிளைகிழான் நல்வேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகனை நெருங்கிச் சொல்லுவாளாய் வாயில் நேர்ந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","வாயில் நேர்ந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வித்தொடு","வித்து",3,"seythi","scene",""],["வட்டி","வட்டி",3,"seythi","scene",""],["மீனொடு","மீன்",4,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:211":{"book":"natrinai","num":212,"t":["யார்க்கு நொந்து உரைக்கோ யானே ஊர் கடல்","ஓதம் சென்ற உப்புடைச் செறுவில்","கொடுங் கழி மருங்கின் இரை வேட்டு எழுந்த","கருங் கால் குருகின் கோள் உய்ந்து போகிய","முடங்கு புற இறவின் மோவாய் ஏற்றை","எறி திரை தொகுத்த எக்கர் நெடுங் கோட்டுத்","துறு கடற் தலைய தோடு பொதி தாழை","வண்டு படு வான் போது வெரூஉம்","துறை கெழு கொண்கன் துறந்தனன் எனவே"],"p":"","r":"Alone and overheard, the friend asks to whom she can tell her pain: the lord of the shore, where a bent-backed male prawn that escaped a black-legged heron hunting in the salt pans takes fright at the white screwpine bud on the dune, has abandoned the girl, so they say.","n":"The prawn fears a harmless flower that looks like the heron, as the girl, who has come safely through the lover's absences, now dreads a report (எனவே, so they say) as if it were the desertion itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","varaivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 211","lb":"NT211","poet":"கோட்டியூர் நல்லந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரைவு நீட ஒருதலை ஆற்றாளாம் என்ற தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகத் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira","thalaivan","kalavu","வரைவு நீட"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["குருகின்","குருகு",4,"pul","scene",""],["இறவின்","இறவு",5,"pira","ullurai","prawn"],["தாழை","தாழை",7,"maram","ullurai",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",8,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","இறவின் தாழை","confirmed","1/1","the girl, afraid of the rumour that the man has left her"]],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:212":{"book":"natrinai","num":213,"t":["பார்வை வேட்டுவன் படு வலை வெரீஇ","நெடுங் கால் கணந்துள்அம் புலம்பு கொள் தெள் விளி","சுரம் செல் கோடியர் கதுமென இசைக்கும்","நரம்பொடு கொள்ளும் அத்தத்து ஆங்கண்","கடுங் குரற் பம்பைக் கத நாய் வடுகர்","நெடும் பெருங் குன்றம் நீந்தி நம் வயின்","வந்தனர் வாழி தோழி கையதை","செம் பொன் கழல்தொடி நோக்கி மா மகன்","கவவுக் கொள் இன் குரல் கேட்டொறும்","அவவுக் கொள் மனத்தேம் ஆகிய நமக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that across the wasteland, where the clear lonely cry of the long-legged kanandhul, afraid of a decoy hunter's net, is caught by passing bards on their strings, and across the tall hills of the Vadugar with their harsh drums and fierce dogs, he has come back to them, who grew anxious each time they heard their little son's sweet voice asking to be held as he looked at the red gold bracelet on his hand.","n":"The bards tune their strings to a frightened bird's cry, so even the road's terror becomes music; the household's anxiety is told through the child, whose request to be held stands for the absent father.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","makkal","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 212","lb":"NT212","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் முடித்துத் தலைமகனோடு வந்த வாயில்கள்வாய் வரவு கேட்ட தோழி தலை மகட்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பொருள் முடித்துத் தலைமகனோடு வந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வேட்டுவன்","வேட்டுவன்",1,"seythi","scene",""],["வலை","வலை",1,"seythi","scene",""],["கணந்துள்அம்","கணந்துள்",2,"pul","scene",""],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["நரம்பொடு","நரம்பு",4,"yaazh","scene",""],["பம்பைக்","பம்பை",5,"parai","scene",""],["நாய்","நாய்",5,"maa","scene",""],["கழல்தொடி","தொடி",8,"pira","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:213":{"book":"natrinai","num":214,"t":["அருவி ஆர்க்கும் பெரு வரை நண்ணி","கன்று கால்யாத்த மன்றப் பலவின்","வேர்க் கொண்டு தூங்கும் கொழுஞ் சுளைப் பெரும் பழம்","குழவிச் சேதா மாந்தி அயலது","வேய் பயில் இறும்பின் ஆம் அறல் பருகும்","பெருங் கல் வேலிச் சிறுகுடி யாது என","சொல்லவும் சொல்லீர் ஆயின் கல்லென","கருவி மா மழை வீழ்ந்தென எழுந்த","செங் கேழ் ஆடிய செழுங் குரற் சிறு தினைக்","கொய் புனம் காவலும் நுமதோ","கோடு ஏந்து அல்குல் நீள் தோளீரே"],"p":"","r":"Seeking the friend's help, the man asks the long-armed girls which is the small village fenced by great rocks near the roaring falls, where a young red cow eats the ripe jackfruit hanging by the roots of the common's tree where calves are tied and drinks clear water in the bamboo thicket; if they will not say even that, is the guarding of this red-eared millet, swaying after the cloud's rain, theirs too?","n":"The question is a pretext: asking the way and who owns the field is how the lover opens talk, and the second question presses them to admit they are the guards he can meet there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 213","lb":"NT213","poet":"கச்சிப்பேட்டுப் பெருந்தச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"மதி உடன்படுக்கும் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thozhi",null,"kalavu","மதி உடன்படுக்கும் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","scene",""],["பலவின்","பலா",2,"maram","scene",""],["சேதா","சேதா",4,"maa","scene","red cow"],["வேய்","வேய்",5,"maram","scene",""],["தினைக்","தினை",9,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:214":{"book":"natrinai","num":215,"t":["இசையும் இன்பமும் ஈதலும் மூன்றும்","அசையுநர் இருந்தோர்க்கு அரும் புணர்வு ஈன்ம் என","வினைவயின் பிரிந்த வேறுபடு கொள்கை","அரும்பு அவிழ் அலரிச் சுரும்பு உண் பல் போது","அணிய வருதும் நின் மணி இருங் கதுப்பு என","எஞ்சா வஞ்சினம் நெஞ்சு உணக் கூறி","மை சூழ் வெற்பின் மலை பல இறந்து","செய் பொருட்கு அகன்ற செயிர் தீர் காதலர்","கேளார்கொல்லோ தோழி தோள","இலங்கு வளை நெகிழ்த்த கலங்கு அஞர் எள்ளி","நகுவது போல மின்னி","ஆர்ப்பது போலும் இக் கார்ப் பெயற் குரலே"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether her lover, who left for wealth saying fame, pleasure and giving do not come to those who sit idle, and who swore he would return to deck her hair with alari flowers, will not hear this rain cloud's voice, flashing as if to mock the grief that loosened her bangles and roaring as if to shout at it.","n":"His reason is a maxim in three terms, quoted back to her, and the season he promised arrives without him: the thunder mocks not his absence but her trust in the maxim and the oath.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aalvinai","paruvam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 214","lb":"NT214","poet":"கருவூர்க் கோசனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உலகியலால் பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட தலைமகன் குறித்த பருவம் கண்டு தலைமகள் சொல்லியது","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","குறித்த பருவம் கண்டு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அலரிச்","அலரி",4,"maram","ornament",""],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",4,"pira","scene",""],["வெற்பின்","வெற்பு",7,"pira","scene","hill"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:215":{"book":"natrinai","num":216,"t":["குண கடல் இவர்ந்து குரூஉக் கதிர் பரப்பி","பகல் கெழு செல்வன் குடமலை மறைய","புலம்பு வந்து இறுத்த புன்கண் மாலை","இலங்கு வளை மகளிர் வியல் நகர் அயர","மீன் நிணம் தொகுத்த ஊன் நெய் ஒண் சுடர்","நீல் நிறப் பரப்பில் தயங்கு திரை உதைப்ப","கரை சேர்பு இருந்த கல்லென் பாக்கத்து","இன்று நீ இவணை ஆகி எம்மொடு","தங்கின் எவனோதெய்ய செங்கால்","கொடு முடி அவ் வலை பரியப் போகிய","கோட் சுறாக் குறித்த முன்பொடு","வேட்டம் வாயாது எமர் வாரலரே"],"p":"","r":"As the sun that climbed from the eastern sea sinks behind the western hill and the lonely evening comes, with bangled women lighting fish-fat lamps in the wide houses of the noisy hamlet by the shore, the friend asks the man what harm there is in staying with them tonight, since their kin, out after a shark that tore through their net, have not come back from a failed hunt.","n":"The invitation is also a refusal: staying the night openly in the hamlet is what only a husband could do, so the friend's 'stay' presses for marriage, as the colophon's second reading says.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","varaivu","kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 215","lb":"NT215","poet":"மதுரைச் சுள்ளம் போதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற் குறி வந்து மீள்வானை அவள் ஆற்றும் தன்மையள் அல்லள் நீயிர் இங்குத் தங்கற் பாலீர் எமரும் இன்னது ஒரு தவற்றினர் எனத் தோழி தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது இரவுக் குறி மறுத்து வரைவு கடாயதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகற் குறி வந்து மீள்வானை"],["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக் குறி மறுத்து வரைவு கடாயதூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மீன்","மீன்",5,"pira","scene",""],["சுறாக்","சுறா",11,"pira","scene",""],["வலை","வலை",10,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:216":{"book":"natrinai","num":217,"t":["துனி தீர் கூட்டமொடு துன்னார் ஆயினும்","இனிதே காணுநர்க் காண்புழி வாழ்தல்","கண்ணுறு விழுமம் கை போல் உதவி","நம் உறு துயரம் களையார்ஆயினும்","இன்னாதுஅன்றே அவர் இல் ஊரே","எரி மருள் வேங்கைக் கடவுள் காக்கும்","குருகு ஆர் கழனியின் இதணத்து ஆங்கண்","ஏதிலாளன் கவலை கவற்ற","ஒரு முலை அறுத்த திருமாவுண்ணிக்","கேட்டோர் அனையராயினும்","வேட்டோர் அல்லது பிறர் இன்னாரே"],"p":"","r":"The other woman, speaking to the bard so the wife's people hear, says that even if her lovers do not come to end her sulking and do not help in her sorrow as the hand helps a hurt eye, the town where they live is not hateful, since one lives where one sees those who look kindly; but others than one's own beloved, even if they are like those who heard of Thirumaavunni, who cut off a breast at the field near the fire-red vengai the god guards, are unbearable.","n":"Thirumaavunni, who tore off a breast when a stranger's trouble wrung her, is a story the poet's audience knew and we only partly know; lines 6 to 11 are hard, and the sense that 'those who heard her story' stand for pitying outsiders is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","natpu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 216","lb":"NT216","poet":"மதுரை மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைமகட்குப் பாங்காயினார் கேட்பத் தலைமகன் தலைநின்று ஒழுகப் படாநின்ற பரத்தை பாணற்கு ஆயினும் விறலிக்குஆயினும் சொல்லுவாளாய் நெருங்கிச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","paanan","pira","karpu","பரத்தை பாணற்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கைக்","வேங்கை",6,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",6,"deyvam","scene",""],["குருகு","குருகு",7,"pul","scene",""],["இதணத்து","இதணம்",7,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:217":{"book":"natrinai","num":218,"t":["இசை பட வாழ்பவர் செல்வம் போலக்","காண் தொறும் பொலியும் கதழ் வாய் வேழம்","இருங் கேழ் வயப் புலி வெரீஇ அயலது","கருங் கால் வேங்கை ஊறுபட மறலி","பெருஞ் சினம் தணியும் குன்றநாடன்","நனி பெரிது இனியனாயினும் துனி படர்ந்து","ஊடல் உறுவேன் தோழி நீடு","புலம்பு சேண் அகல நீக்கி","புலவி உணர்த்தல் வன்மையானே"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells her friend that though the man of the hill country, where a fierce elephant, more splendid each time it is seen like the wealth of those who live in fame, takes fright at a tiger and vents its rage on a black-stemmed vengai until its anger cools, is very sweet to her, she will brood and sulk, for making him feel her sulk is the strong way to drive off her long loneliness.","n":"The elephant that fears the tiger and spends its anger on a tree is a near miss for ullurai (the man who takes out on the wife what he dares not face elsewhere): the second pair is not anchored, so it stays scene. Line 9 can also mean that making him understand is hard, which reverses her confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 217","lb":"NT217","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகள் வாயில் மறுத்தது","thd":1,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைமகள் வாயில் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேழம்","வேழம்",2,"maa","simile",""],["புலி","புலி",3,"maa","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",4,"maram","scene","kino tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:218":{"book":"natrinai","num":219,"t":["ஞாயிறு ஞான்று கதிர் மழுங்கின்றே","எல்லியும் பூ வீ கொடியின் புலம்பு அடைந்தன்றே","வாவலும் வயின்தொறும் பறக்கும் சேவலும்","நகை வாய்க் கொளீஇ நகுதொறும் விளிக்கும்","ஆயாக் காதலொடு அதர்ப் படத் தெளித்தோர்","கூறிய பருவம் கழிந்தன்று பாரிய","பராரை வேம்பின் படு சினை இருந்த","குராஅற் கூகையும் இராஅ இசைக்கும்","ஆனா நோய் அட வருந்தி இன்னும்","தமியேன் கேட்குவென் கொல்லோ","பரியரைப் பெண்ணை அன்றிற் குரலே"],"p":"","r":"Answering the friend's reassurance, the girl says the sun has set with dimmed rays, the night grows lonely like a creeper whose flowers have dropped, bats fly to their haunts, the cock calls each time his mate laughs, the season named by the man who swore love on the path has passed, the owl in the forked neem hoots through the night; worn by unending pain, must she still hear alone the andril's cry from the thick palmyra?","n":"Every creature at nightfall either goes home or calls its mate, and the last, the andril, is the bird of inseparable pairs, so the catalogue ends on what she lacks; lines 3 and 4 (the laughing cock) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","varaivu","kaamanoy","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 218","lb":"NT218","poet":"கிடங்கில் காவிதிக் கீரங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரைவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகள் வன்புறை எதிர்மொழிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வன்புறை எதிர்மொழிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கொடியின்","கொடி",2,"maram","simile",""],["வாவலும்","வாவல்",3,"pul","scene","bat"],["சேவலும்","சேவல்",3,"pul","scene",""],["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",7,"maram","scene",""],["கூகையும்","கூகை",8,"pul","scene",""],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",11,"maram","scene",""],["அன்றிற்","அன்றில்",11,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:219":{"book":"natrinai","num":220,"t":["கண்ணும் தோளும் தண் நறுங் கதுப்பும்","பழ நலம் இழந்து பசலை பாய","இன் உயிர் பெரும்பிறிது ஆயினும் என்னதூஉம்","புலவேன் வாழி தோழி சிறு கால்","அலவனொடு பெயரும் புலவுத் திரை நளி கடல்","பெரு மீன் கொள்ளும் சிறுகுடிப் பரதவர்","கங்குல் மாட்டிய கனை கதிர் ஒண் சுடர்","முதிரா ஞாயிற்று எதிர் ஒளி கடுக்கும்","கானல்அம் பெருந் துறைச் சேர்ப்பன்","தானே யானே புணர்ந்தமாறே"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend that though her eyes, shoulders and cool fragrant hair lose their old beauty and pallor spreads, and though her life should pass into death, she will not be angry at all, because the lord of the wide shore, where fishermen light flames at night that look like the light of a sun not yet risen, and she joined of their own accord.","n":"தானே யானே: he himself and I myself, with no go-between; the union was their own choice, so there is no one to blame, not even him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kalaviyal","punarchi","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 219","lb":"NT219","poet":"தாயங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரைவிடை வைத்துப் பிரிய ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வரைவிடை வைத்துப் பிரிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அலவனொடு","அலவன்",5,"pira","scene","crab"],["மீன்","மீன்",6,"pira","scene",""],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["சுடர்","சுடர்",7,"seythi","simile","lamp"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:220":{"book":"natrinai","num":221,"t":["சிறு மணி தொடர்ந்து பெருங் கச்சு நிறீஇ","குறு முகிழ் எருக்கங் கண்ணி சூடி","உண்ணா நல் மாப் பண்ணி எம்முடன்","மறுகுடன் திரிதரும் சிறு குறுமாக்கள்","பெரிதும் சான்றோர்மன்ற விசிபிணி","முழவுக் கண் புலரா விழவுடை ஆங்கண்","ஊரேம் என்னும் இப் பேர் ஏமுறுநர்","தாமே ஒப்புரவு அறியின் தேமொழிக்","கயல் ஏர் உண்கண் குறுமகட்கு","அயலோர் ஆகல் என்று எம்மொடு படலே"],"p":"","r":"The little boys who tie small bells to a band, wear erukku-bud garlands, make a horse that does not eat and roam the street with him are truly wise; but the deluded people of this festive town of ever-sounding drums, who call themselves the town, would join him too if they knew fellow-feeling, telling him not to be a stranger to the sweet-voiced girl with eyes like kayal fish.","n":"The boys' game is the madal itself, played as a toy, so the lover's last resort has become a street spectacle children copy; calling the children 'great worthies' (சான்றோர்) mocks the town's elders by contrast.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","oppuravu","alar","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 220","lb":"NT220","poet":"குண்டுகட்பாலியாதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"perunthinai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"குறை நேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகளை முகம் புக்கது பின்னின்ற தலைமகன் தோழி கேட்பத் தலைமகளை ஓம்படுத்ததூஉம் ஆம்.தான் ஆற்றானாய்ச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","குறை நேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகளை முகம் புக்கது"],["thalaivan","thalaivi","thozhi","kalavu","தலைமகளை ஓம்படுத்ததூஉம் ஆம்"],["thalaivan","pira",null,"kalavu","ஆற்றானாய்ச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["மணி","மணி",1,"pira","ornament","bell"],["எருக்கங்","எருக்கு",2,"maram","ornament",""],["மாப்","மா",3,"maa","scene","horse"],["முழவுக்","முழவு",6,"parai","scene",""],["கயல்","கயல்",9,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:11"},"nt:221":{"book":"natrinai","num":222,"t":["மணி கண்டன்ன மா நிறக் கருவிளை","ஒண் பூந் தோன்றியொடு தண் புதல் அணிய","பொன் தொடர்ந்தன்ன தகைய நன் மலர்க்","கொன்றை ஒள் இணர் கோடுதொறும் தூங்க","வம்பு விரித்தன்ன செம் புலப் புறவில்","நீர் அணிப் பெரு வழி நீள் இடைப் போழ","செல்க பாக நின் செய்வினை நெடுந் தேர்","விருந்து விருப்புறூஉம் பெருந் தோட் குறுமகள்","மின் ஒளிர் அவிர் இழை நல் நகர் விளங்க","நடை நாட் செய்த நவிலாச் சீறடிப்","பூங் கட் புதல்வன் உறங்குவயின் ஒல்கி","வந்தீக எந்தை என்னும்","அம் தீம் கிளவி கேட்கம் நாமே"],"p":"","r":"His task done, the man tells his charioteer to drive the long chariot through the red-soil scrub, where karuvilai blue as a gem and bright thondri deck the bushes and konrai clusters hang like gold chains from every bough, so that he may hear the sweet words of his little son, newly walking on untried feet, who leaves his bed and cries 'come, father', in the shining house of his wife who loves to receive guests.","n":"The reward he drives toward is not first the wife but the household she keeps, hospitable and now with a child who has just learned to call him; the flowering season tells him he is already late.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","makkal","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 221","lb":"NT221","poet":"இடைக்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி மறுத்தராநின்ற தலைமகன் பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றி மறுத்தராநின்ற"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கருவிளை","கருவிளை",1,"maram","simile","clitoria"],["தோன்றியொடு","தோன்றி",2,"maram","scene",""],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",4,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:222":{"book":"natrinai","num":223,"t":["கருங் கால் வேங்கைச் செவ் வீவாங்கு சினை","வடுக் கொளப் பிணித்த விடுபுரி முரற்சிக்","கை புனை சிறு நெறி வாங்கி பையென","விசும்பு ஆடு ஆய் மயில் கடுப்ப யான் இன்று","பசுங் காழ் அல்குல் பற்றுவனன் ஊக்கிச்","செலவுடன் விடுகோ தோழி பலவுடன்","வாழை ஓங்கிய வழை அமை சிலம்பில்","துஞ்சு பிடி மருங்கின் மஞ்சு பட காணாது","பெருங் களிறு பிளிறும் சோலை அவர்","சேண் நெடுங் குன்றம் காணிய நீயே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, knowing the man stands near, asks the girl whether she should push her today on the swing hung from the bent red-flowered vengai branch, its twisted rope scarring the bark, so that she, holding her girdle of green beads, may rise like a peacock playing in the sky and see his far tall hill, where on the slope of plantain, wazhai and bamboo the great tusker, unable to see his mate asleep beside him when the mist comes down, trumpets.","n":"'Today' is the edge: the girl is about to be kept indoors, so this is the last swing and the last sight of his hill, and the man listening is told so without being addressed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 222","lb":"NT222","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகன் வரவு உணர்ந்து சிறைப்புறமாகச் செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","scene",""],["மயில்","மயில்",4,"pul","simile","peacock"],["வாழை","வாழை",7,"maram","scene",""],["வழை","வழை",7,"maram","scene",""],["அமை","அமை",7,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பிடி","பிடி",8,"maa","iraicchi","she-elephant"],["களிறு","களிறு",9,"maa","iraicchi","tusker"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","களிறு","confirmed","1/1","once the girl is shut in the house she will be near yet out of his sight, and the man will cry out like the tusker unless he marries her"]],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:223":{"book":"natrinai","num":224,"t":["இவள்தன் காமம் பெருமையின் காலை என்னாள் நின்","அன்பு பெரிது உடைமையின் அளித்தல் வேண்டி","பகலும் வருதி பல் பூங் கானல்","இன்னீர்ஆகலோ இனிதால் எனின் இவள்","அலரின் அருங் கடிப் படுகுவள் அதனால்","எல்லி வம்மோ மெல்லம் புலம்ப","சுறவினம் கலித்த நிறை இரும் பரப்பின்","துறையினும் துஞ்சாக் கண்ணர்","பெண்டிரும் உடைத்து இவ் அம்பல் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man of the shore that because the girl's desire is great she forgets it is daytime, and because his love is great he comes even by day to the flower-filled grove; that is sweet, but gossip will have her strictly guarded, so let him come at night instead, except that this gossiping town has women whose eyes never sleep even at the ford of the wide sea where shark shoals teem.","n":"The offer of a night tryst is withdrawn in the same breath: neither day nor night is safe, so the only road left is marriage, which is what the friend wants him to hear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","varaivu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 223","lb":"NT223","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறி வந்து மீள்வானைத் தோழி இரவுக்குறி நேர்வாள் போன்று அதுவும் மறுத்து வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி நேர்வாள் போன்று அதுவும் மறுத்து வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சுறவினம்","சுறா",7,"pira","scene","shark"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:224":{"book":"natrinai","num":225,"t":["அன்பினர் மன்னும் பெரியர் அதன்தலை","பின்பனி அமையம் வரும் என முன்பனிக்","கொழுந்து முந்துறீஇக் குரவு அரும்பினவே","புணர்ந்தீர் புணர்மினோ என்ன இணர்மிசைச்","செங் கண் இருங் குயில் எதிர் குரல் பயிற்றும்","இன்ப வேனிலும் வந்தன்று நம்வயின்","பிரியலம் என்று தெளித்தோர் தேஎத்து","இனி எவன் மொழிகோ யானே கயன் அறக்","கண் அழிந்து உலறிய பல் மர நெடு நெறி","வில் மூசு கவலை விலங்கிய","வெம் முனை அருஞ் சுரம் முன்னியோர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"Told by her friend that he is leaving, the woman says: they are loving, and great besides; yet the kuravu has budded ahead of the late dew, the red-eyed koel calls from the clusters as if saying 'you who are joined, stay joined', and sweet spring has come to us; what can I say now to those who assured me 'we will not part' and have set their minds on the hot hard waste of dried pools and withered trees where bowmen swarm at the forks?","n":"Her praise of him (loving, great) is what makes argument impossible: she cannot accuse, only set his promise against a season that is itself pleading for union.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 224","lb":"NT224","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழியால் பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட தலைமகள் பெயர்த்தும் சொல் கடாவப்பட்டு அறிவிலாதேம் என்னை சொல்லியும் பிரியார் ஆகாரோ என்று சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தோழியால் பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட தலைமகள்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குரவு","குரவு",3,"maram","scene",""],["குயில்","குயில்",5,"pul","scene",""],["பல் மர","மரம்",9,"maram","absent",""],["வில்","வில்",10,"seythi","scene","bowmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:225":{"book":"natrinai","num":226,"t":["முருகு உறழ் முன்பொடு கடுஞ் சினம் செருக்கிப்","பொருத யானை வெண் கோடு கடுப்ப","வாழை ஈன்ற வை ஏந்து கொழு முகை","மெல் இயல் மகளிர் ஓதி அன்ன","பூவொடு துயல் வரும் மால் வரை நாடனை","இரந்தோர் உளர்கொல் தோழி திருந்து இழைத்","தொய்யில் வன முலை வரி வனப்பு இழப்பப்","பயந்து எழு பருவரல் தீர","நயந்தோர்க்கு உதவா நார் இல் மார்பே"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether anyone has had to beg the lord of the great mountain, where the plantain's swollen bud, like the white tusk of an elephant that fought in fury with Murugan's strength, sways with its flower like the loose hair of soft women, for the chest without kindness that will not help those who love it, so as to cure the pain that has risen and ruined the drawn lines of her breasts.","n":"The question is bitter: a help that has to be begged for is no help, so she refuses the friend's comfort by asking whether any lover should need to plead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","arul","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 225","lb":"NT225","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வன்புறை எதிர் அழிந்தது பரத்தை தலைமகட்குப் பாங்காயின வாயில் கேட்பச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"வன்புறை எதிர் அழிந்தது"],["parathai","pira","pira","karpu","பரத்தை தலைமகட்குப் பாங்காயின வாயில் கேட்பச் சொல்லியதூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முருகு","முருகன்",1,"deyvam","simile",""],["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","simile",""],["வாழை","வாழை",3,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:226":{"book":"natrinai","num":227,"t":["மரம் சா மருந்தும் கொள்ளார் மாந்தர்","உரம் சாச் செய்யார் உயர்தவம் வளம் கெடப்","பொன்னும் கொள்ளார் மன்னர் நன்னுதல்","நாம் தம் உண்மையின் உளமே அதனால்","தாம் செய்பொருள் அளவு அறியார் தாம் கசிந்து","என்றூழ் நிறுப்ப நீள் இடை ஒழிய","சென்றோர்மன்ற நம் காதலர் என்றும்","இன்ன நிலைமைத்து என்ப","என்னோரும் அறிப இவ் உலகத்தானே"],"p":"","r":"Answering comfort, the woman says that people do not take a medicine that kills the tree, the great do not do a penance that kills their strength, and kings do not take gold that ruins the land's wealth; yet since our life hangs on his, our lover, not knowing the measure of the wealth he goes to make, has gone over the long scorched way and left us, and the whole world knows it is always so.","n":"Each maxim is about taking from a source without destroying it; she makes herself the source he is draining, so the proverbs of the wise become the charge against him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","alavu","uyir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 226","lb":"NT226","poet":"கணி புன்குன்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகள் வன்புறை எதிர்மொழிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகள் வன்புறை எதிர்மொழிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மரம்","மரம்",1,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:227":{"book":"natrinai","num":228,"t":["அறிந்தோர் அறன் இலர் என்றலின் சிறந்த","இன் உயிர் கழியினும் நனி இன்னாதே","புன்னைஅம் கானல் புணர் குறி வாய்த்த","பின் ஈர் ஓதி என் தோழிக்கு அன்னோ","படு மணி யானைப் பசும்பூட் சோழர்","கொடி நுடங்கு மறுகின் ஆர்க்காட்டு ஆங்கண்","கள்ளுடைத் தடவில் புள் ஒலித்து ஓவாத்","தேர் வழங்கு தெருவின் அன்ன","கௌவை ஆகின்றது ஐய நின் அருளே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that worse than losing precious life is to have the wise say one lacks virtue, and yet, alas, his grace to her friend of long thick hair, who met him at the tryst in the punnai grove, has become talk as loud as the streets of the Cholas' Arkkadu, with its belled elephants and banners, where birds cry without stop by the toddy vats and chariots ply.","n":"'Your grace' is the sting: what he gave as kindness has turned into the town's noise, and only marriage turns it back into virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","varaivu","aram","pazhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 227","lb":"NT227","poet":"தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரையாது நெடுங்காலம் வந்து ஒழுக தோழி தலைமகனை வரைவு முடுகச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு முடுகச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",3,"maram","scene",""],["யானைப்","யானை",5,"maa","simile",""],["கள்ளுடைத்","கள்",7,"unavu","simile","toddy"],["புள்","புள்",7,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",5],["town","ஆர்க்காட்டு",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:228":{"book":"natrinai","num":229,"t":["என் எனப்படுமோ தோழி மின்னு வசிபு","அதிர் குரல் எழிலி முதிர் கடன் தீர","கண் தூர்பு விரிந்த கனை இருள் நடு நாள்","பண்பு இல் ஆர் இடை வரூஉம் நம் திறத்து","அருளான்கொல்லோ தானே கானவன்","சிறு புறம் கடுக்கும் பெருங் கை வேழம்","வெறி கொள் சாபத்து எறி கணை வெரீஇ","அழுந்துபட விடரகத்து இயம்பும்","எழுந்து வீழ் அருவிய மலை கிழவோனே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, speaking to the girl so that the man nearby hears, asks what it can mean that on a midnight of thick darkness, when lightning splits the thundering clouds and they pour out their full load, the lord of the mountain of leaping falls, where the great tusker, startled by the arrow shot from the hunter's fierce bow, slides into a crevice and its cry echoes, comes by the cruel hard path: is it that he has no pity for us?","n":"Her fear is for him, not of him: his coming through the night's dangers is called pitiless because it puts the girl through terror, which is the friend's way of urging marriage. The elephant falling into the cleft may be read as the danger awaiting the man, but no second correspondence is stated, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kalaviyal","acham","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 228","lb":"NT228","poet":"முடத்திருமாறனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய் தலைமகன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய் தலைமகன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["எழிலி","எழிலி",2,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["கானவன்","கானவன்",5,"seythi","scene","hunter"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",6,"maa","scene",""],["சாபத்து","சாபம்",7,"seythi","scene","bow"],["அருவிய","அருவி",9,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:229":{"book":"natrinai","num":230,"t":["சேறும் சேறும் என்றலின் பல புலந்து","சென்மின் என்றல் யான் அஞ்சுவலே","செல்லாதீம் எனச் செப்பின் பல்லோர்","நிறத்து எறி புன் சொலின்திறத்து அஞ்சுவலே","அதனால் சென்மின் சென்று வினை முடிமின் சென்றாங்கு","அவண் நீடாதல் ஓம்புமின் யாமத்து","இழை அணி ஆகம் வடுக் கொள முயங்கி","உழையீராகவும் பனிப்போள் தமியே","குழைவான் கண்ணிடத்து ஈண்டித் தண்ணென","ஆடிய இள மழைப் பின்றை","வாடையும் கண்டிரோ வந்து நின்றதுவே"],"p":"","r":"Told by the man that he is leaving, the friend says: since you keep saying 'we go, we go', I fear to sulk and say 'go', and if I say 'do not go' I fear the petty words of many that wound; so go, go and finish the work, but once there do not linger, for she trembles alone at midnight even when you are near after an embrace that marks her jewelled breast; and have you seen that the north wind has already come and stands here, after the young rains that played coolly where the clouds gathered?","n":"Her consent is made of two fears, and its last line undoes it: the cold wind that has already come is the season in which no man should be away, so the permission ends as a plea to stay, which is why the colophon gives the alternative reading that she held him back.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 229","lb":"NT229","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகனால் பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட தோழி தலைமகளை ஆற்றுவித்துச் செல்ல உடன்பட்டது செலவு அழுங்குவித்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","செல்ல உடன்பட்டது"],["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","செலவு அழுங்குவித்ததூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மழைப்","மழை",10,"pira","scene","rain"],["வாடையும்","வாடை",11,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:230":{"book":"natrinai","num":231,"t":["முயப் பிடிச் செவியின் அன்ன பாசடை","கயக் கணக் கொக்கின் அன்ன கூம்பு முகை","கணைக் கால் ஆம்பல் அமிழ்து நாறு தண் போது","குணக்குத் தோன்று வெள்ளியின் இருள் கெட விரியும்","கயற்கணம் கலித்த பொய்கை ஊர","முனிவு இல் பரத்தையை எற் துறந்து அருளாய்","நனி புலம்பு அலைத்த எல்லை நீங்க","புதுவறம்கூர்ந்த செறுவில் தண்ணென","மலி புனல் பரத்தந்தாஅங்கு","இனிதே தெய்ய நின் காணுங்காலே"],"p":"","r":"Refusing the man entry, the friend says in the wife's voice: lord of the town with ponds full of carp, where the cool water-lily, its green leaves like a young she-elephant's ears and its closed buds like a flock of herons, opens smelling of nectar as the morning star rises in the east and the dark goes, you left me for a tireless courtesan and showed no grace; how sweet it is to see you, like a full flood spreading coolly over a field gripped by fresh drought, ending the loneliness that tormented me.","n":"The flood simile can be heard straight or as irony: so rarely does he come that his sight is like rain on a parched field, which is itself the reproach that keeps the door shut.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 230","lb":"NT230","poet":"ஆலங்குடி வங்கனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி வாயில் மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","வாயில் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பிடிச்","பிடி",1,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["கொக்கின்","கொக்கு",2,"pul","simile","heron"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",3,"maram","scene",""],["கயற்கணம்","கயல்",5,"pira","scene","carp"],["புனல்","புனல்",9,"pira","simile","flood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:231":{"book":"natrinai","num":232,"t":["மை அற விளங்கிய மணி நிற விசும்பில்","கைதொழும் மரபின் எழு மீன் போல","பெருங் கடற் பரப்பின் இரும் புறம் தோய","சிறு வெண் காக்கை பலவுடன் ஆடும்","துறை புலம்பு உடைத்தே தோழி பண்டும்","உள் ஊர்க் குரீஇக் கரு உடைத்தன்ன","பெரும் போது அவிழ்ந்த கருந் தாட் புன்னைக்","கானல்அம் கொண்கன் தந்த","காதல் நம்மொடு நீங்காமாறே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, with the man within hearing, tells the girl that the shore where many small white crows play, dipping their dark backs in the wide sea like the seven stars worshipped by custom in the spotless sapphire sky, is now desolate, though before it was such that the love given by the lord of the grove of black-trunked punnai, whose big blossoms open like sparrows' eggs broken in the inner town, never left us.","n":"The playful crows in company make the shore's emptiness sharper; the friend recalls the old love only to say that the place where it lived is now lonely, so the man must marry to restore it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 231","lb":"NT231","poet":"இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி சொல்லி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["காக்கை","காக்கை",4,"pul","simile","crow"],["குரீஇக்","குருவி",6,"pul","simile","sparrow"],["புன்னைக்","புன்னை",7,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:232":{"book":"natrinai","num":233,"t":["சிறு கண் யானைப் பெருங் கை ஈர் இனம்","குளவித் தண் கயம் குழையத் தீண்டி","சோலை வாழை முணைஇ அயலது","வேரல் வேலிச் சிறுகுடி அலற","செங் காற் பலவின் தீம் பழம் மிசையும்","மா மலை நாட காமம் நல்கென","வேண்டுதும் வாழிய எந்தை வேங்கை","வீ உக வரிந்த முன்றில்","கல் கெழு பாக்கத்து அல்கினை செலினே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man who comes by day: lord of the great mountain, where a herd of small-eyed, great-trunked elephants muddies the cool pool of wild jasmine, tires of the grove's plantains and eats the sweet fruit of the red-stemmed jackfruit beside the bamboo-fenced village while the village cries out, we beg you to grant her love; long may you live, our lord, if you stay the night and go from our stony hamlet, whose courtyard is strewn with fallen vengai blossom.","n":"The elephants that leave the grove for the village's own trees and set it crying are what his daytime visits look like from the house: the request to come by night and lodge in the hamlet is a step toward being received there openly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","iravu","alar","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 232","lb":"NT232","poet":"முதுவெங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பகல் வருவானை இரவு வா எனத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகல் வருவானை இரவு வா"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானைப்","யானை",1,"maa","ullurai",""],["குளவித்","குளவி",2,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"],["வாழை","வாழை",3,"maram","scene",""],["வேரல்","வேரல்",4,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பலவின்","பலா",5,"maram","scene",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",7,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","யானைப்","confirmed","1/1","the man who comes by day close to the girl's house"]],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:233":{"book":"natrinai","num":234,"t":["கல்லாக் கடுவன் நடுங்க முள் எயிற்று","மட மா மந்தி மாணா வன் பறழ்","கோடு உயர் அடுக்கத்து ஆடு மழை ஒளிக்கும்","பெருங் கல் நாடனை அருளினை ஆயின்","இனி என கொள்ளலைமன்னே கொன் ஒன்று","கூறுவென் வாழி தோழி முன்னுற","நாருடை நெஞ்சத்து ஈரம் பொத்தி","ஆன்றோர் செல் நெறி வழாஅச்","சான்றோன் ஆதல் நன்கு அறிந்தனை தெளிமே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, so that the man nearby hears, tells the girl who suffers from the long unmarried visits: if you have given your grace to the lord of the great rocky land, where on the high slope the sturdy young of the female monkey, whose untaught male trembles, hides in the drifting cloud, why will you not take my word? I say one plain thing: be sure that he is a worthy man who keeps the tenderness of a kind heart and does not stray from the path the wise have walked.","n":"Praise is pressure: by calling him one who walks the path of the wise in his hearing, the friend makes delay a breach of that path. The trembling monkey and the hidden young may hint at the anxious family, but only one correspondence is clear, so no ullurai is tagged.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 233","lb":"NT233","poet":"அஞ்சில் ஆந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரையாது நெடுங்காலம் வந்து ஒழுக இவள் ஆற்றாள் என்பது உணர்ந்து சிறைப் புறமாகத் தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","இவள் ஆற்றாள் என்பது உணர்ந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",1,"maa","scene","male monkey"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",2,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["பறழ்","பறழ்",2,"maa","scene","young"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:235":{"book":"natrinai","num":235,"t":["உரவுத் திரை பொருத பிணர் படு தடவு முதல்","அரவு வாள் வாய முள் இலைத் தாழை","பொன் நேர் தாதின் புன்னையொடு கமழும்","பல் பூங் கானல் பகற்குறி வந்து நம்","மெய் கவின் சிதையப் பெயர்ந்தனனாயினும்","குன்றின் தோன்றும் குவவு மணல் ஏறி","கண்டனம் வருகம் சென்மோ தோழி","தண் தார் அகலம் வண்டு இமிர்பு ஊத","படு மணிக் கலி மாக் கடைஇ","நெடு நீர்ச் சேர்ப்பன் வரூஉம் ஆறே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that though the lord of the long waters came to the day tryst in the shore grove where the saw-edged, thorny-leaved thaazhai on the wave-beaten rough trunk and the gold-pollened punnai give off their scent together, and left so that her beauty wasted, they should now climb the heaped dune that rises like a hill and watch the road by which he will come, bees humming over the cool garland on his chest, driving his proud horses with ringing bells.","n":"The grievance and the joy are in one sentence: the wasting he caused is admitted, then set aside for the sight of him arriving with the full show of a bridegroom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","melivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 235","lb":"NT235","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரைவு நீட ஆற்றாளாங் காலத்துத் தோழி வரைவு மலிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு மலிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["தாழை","தாழை",2,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னையொடு","புன்னை",3,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",8,"pira","scene","bee"],["மாக்","மா",9,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:236":{"book":"natrinai","num":236,"t":["நோயும் கைம்மிகப் பெரிதே மெய்யும்","தீ உமிழ் தெறலின் வெய்தாகின்றே","ஒய்யெனச் சிறிது ஆங்கு உயிரியர் பையென","முன்றில் கொளினே நந்துவள் பெரிது என","நிரைய நெஞ்சத்து அன்னைக்கு உய்த்து ஆண்டு","உரை இனி வாழி தோழி புரை இல்","நுண் நேர் எல் வளை நெகிழ்த்தோன் குன்றத்து","அண்ணல் நெடு வரை ஆடி தண்ணென","வியல் அறை மூழ்கிய வளி என்","பயலை ஆகம் தீண்டிய சிறிதே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend, with the man standing by: my sickness is past bearing and my body burns as if spitting fire; so go and tell my mother of the hellish heart that if I am let out into the courtyard to breathe a little I will recover much, for a little while ago the wind that played on the tall peak of the hill of him who loosened my fine bright bangles, and plunged into its wide rock pools, touched my pale body and cooled it.","n":"Only she and the overhearing man know why the courtyard would heal her: the wind comes from his hill, so her cure is to be nearer him, which is a plea to him to come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pasalai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 236","lb":"NT236","poet":"நம்பி குட்டுவன்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறமாக வற்புறுக்கும் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","வற்புறுக்கும் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","பிணி"]],"k":[["வளை","வளை",7,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["வளி","வளி",9,"pira","scene","wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:237":{"book":"natrinai","num":237,"t":["நனி மிகப் பசந்து தோளும் சாஅய்","பனி மலி கண்ணும் பண்டு போலா","இன் உயிர் அன்ன பிரிவு அருங் காதலர்","நீத்து நீடினர் என்னும் புலவி","உட்கொண்டு ஊடின்றும் இலையோ மடந்தை","உவக்காண் தோன்றுவ ஓங்கி வியப்புடை","இரவலர் வரூஉம் அளவை அண்டிரன்","புரவு எதிர்ந்து தொகுத்த யானை போல","உலகம் உவப்ப ஓது அரும்","வேறு பல் உருவின் ஏர்தரும் மழையே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the girl, her shoulders pale and shrunken and her eyes full of tears as never before, whether she has no sulk at all against the lover dear as life who has left and stays away; look there, she says, the rain clouds of many shapes beyond telling are rising high and wonderful so that the world rejoices, like the elephants Andiran gathered when he undertook to keep the suppliants who come to him.","n":"The friend turns from grief to a sign: the clouds rise like a patron's gift laid up for those who ask, so the season itself promises that what is owed will come, and the girl's sulk can wait for him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","oodal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 237","lb":"NT237","poet":"காரிக்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி உரை மாறுபட்டது","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தோழி உரை மாறுபட்டது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",8,"maa","simile",""],["மழையே","மழை",10,"pira","simile","rain cloud"]],"en":[["patron","அண்டிரன்",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:238":{"book":"natrinai","num":238,"t":["வறம் கொல வீந்த கானத்து குறும் பூங்","கோதை மகளிர் குழூஉ நிரை கடுப்ப","வண்டு வாய் திறப்ப விண்ட பிடவம்","மாலை அந்தி மால் அதர் நண்ணிய","பருவம் செய்த கருவி மா மழை","அவர் நிலை அறியுமோ ஈங்கு என வருதல்","சான்றோர்ப் புரைவதோ அன்றே மான்று உடன்","உர உரும் உரறும் நீரின் பரந்த","பாம்பு பை மழுங்கல் அன்றியும் மாண்ட","கனியா நெஞ்சத்தானும்","இனிய அல்ல நின் இடி நவில் குரலே"],"p":"","r":"Seeing the season, the woman reproaches the great cloud: in the forest the drought had killed, the pidavam has opened as bees part it, like rows of women wearing short flower garlands, and you, cloud that brings the season along the dim paths of evening, come here as if asking whether I know where he is; that is not how the noble behave; and besides dulling the hoods of snakes in the water spread by the roaring thunder, even to a heart that has not softened your thundering voice is not sweet.","n":"She judges the cloud by the conduct of the noble, who would not torment a woman alone; the season that should mean his return arrives as an insult because he has not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","pirivu","saandror"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 238","lb":"NT238","poet":"கந்தரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைமகள் பருவம் கண்டு அழிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu","பருவம் கண்டு அழிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பிடவம்","பிடவம்",3,"maram","simile",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",9,"pira","scene","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:239":{"book":"natrinai","num":239,"t":["ஞான்ற ஞாயிறு குடமலை மறைய","மான்ற மாலை மகிழ்ந்த பரதவர்","இனிது பெறு பெரு மீன் எளிதினின் மாறி","அலவன் ஆடிய புலவு மணல் முன்றில்","காமர் சிறுகுடிச் செல்நெறி வழியின்","ஆய் மணி பொதி அவிழ்ந்தாங்கு நெய்தல்","புல் இதழ் பொதிந்த பூத் தப மிதிக்கும்","மல்லல் இருங் கழி மலி நீர்ச் சேர்ப்பற்கு","அமைந்து தொழில் கேட்டன்றோ இலமே முன்கை","வார் கோல் எல் வளை உடைய வாங்கி","முயங்கு எனக் கலுழ்ந்த இவ் ஊர்","எற்று ஆவதுகொல் யாம் மற்றொன்று செயினே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, with the man within hearing, says: as the setting sun hides behind the western hill in the dimming evening, the glad fishermen easily trade the great fish they caught and, on the way to their lovely hamlet with its fish-smelling sandy yard where crabs play, trample the neythal flowers whose dull petals unwrap like a bundle of fine gems; we have not even agreed to hear of the shore lord's suit, and yet this town has stirred up talk as if he had pulled my friend's bright bangles till they broke, saying 'embrace'; what would it make of it if we did something more?","n":"The fishermen treading down the flowers on their careless way home have been read as the man's carelessness of her, but the pairs are not secure; the poem's point is that gossip already runs ahead of any deed, so he must act to make it true by marrying.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 239","lb":"NT239","poet":"குன்றியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",2,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["மீன்","மீன்",3,"pira","scene","fish"],["அலவன்","அலவன்",4,"pira","scene","crab"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",6,"maram","simile","water lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:240":{"book":"natrinai","num":240,"t":["ஐதே கம்ம இவ் உலகு படைத்தோனே","வை ஏர் வால் எயிற்று ஒள் நுதற் குறுமகள்","கை கவர் முயக்கம் மெய் உறத் திருகி","ஏங்கு உயிர்ப்பட்ட வீங்கு முலை ஆகம்","துயில் இடைப்படூஉம் தன்மையதுஆயினும்","வெயில் வெய்துற்ற பரல் அவல் ஒதுக்கில்","கணிச்சியில் குழித்த கூவல் நண்ணி","ஆன் வழிப் படுநர் தோண்டிய பத்தல்","யானை இன நிரை வெளவும்","கானம் திண்ணிய மலை போன்றிசினே"],"p":"","r":"Pushed by his heart toward wealth, the man says: how strange is the one who made this world! though the embrace of the young girl with fine sharp white teeth and bright brow, her arms clasping, body pressed and twisting, her swelling breast heaving, is such that sleep can come between us, still the forest, where at the well dug with picks in a pebbled hollow sheltered from the burning sun the elephant herd seizes the trough the herders dug for the cattle, stands before me like a firm mountain.","n":"He knows even the closest embrace has gaps, and that is the excuse the heart uses; but the road, where even the herders' water is taken by elephants, is the obstacle he cannot argue away. The colophon also gives the lines to the heroine wasting in separation.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","selvam","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 240","lb":"NT240","poet":"நப்பாலத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகள் சொல்லியது நெஞ்சினால் பொருள் வலிக்கப்பட்டு ஆற்றானாய தலைமகன் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","நெஞ்சினால் பொருள் வலிக்கப்பட்டு ஆற்றானாய தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கணிச்சியில்","கணிச்சி",7,"seythi","scene","pick"],["கூவல்","கூவல்",7,"pira","scene","well"],["ஆன்","ஆ",8,"maa","scene","cattle"],["யானை","யானை",9,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:12"},"nt:241":{"book":"natrinai","num":241,"t":["உள்ளார்கொல்லோ தோழி கொடுஞ் சிறைப்","புள் அடி பொறித்த வரியுடைத் தலைய","நீர் அழி மருங்கின் ஈர் அயிர் தோன்ற","வளரா வாடை உளர்பு நனி தீண்டலின்","வேழ வெண் பூ விரிவன பலவுடன்","வேந்து வீசு கவரியின் பூம் புதல் அணிய","மழை கழி விசும்பின் மாறி ஞாயிறு","விழித்து இமைப்பது போல் விளங்குபு மறைய","எல்லை போகிய பொழுதின் எல் உற","பனிக்கால் கொண்ட பையுள் யாமத்து","பல் இதழ் உண்கண் கலுழ","நில்லாப் பொருட்பிணிப் பிரிந்திசினோரே"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether they do not think of her, the men who left her for the restless sickness of wealth, now that the water has drawn back to show wet fine sand printed with the feet of curve-winged birds, the northern wind stirs the reed's white flowers till they deck the bush like the whisk a king waves, and the sun, in a sky cleared of rain, opens and shuts like a blinking eye and is gone, so that in the dewy midnight her kohl-lined eyes run with tears.","n":"Everything she names is a sign of the cold season settling in: the receded water, the north wind, the reed in flower, a sun that only blinks between clouds. The question is not whether he remembers her in general but whether this season, which she reads hour by hour from dusk to midnight, reaches him where he is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 241","lb":"NT241","poet":"மதுரைப் பெருமருதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகள் வன்புறை எதிர் அழிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","வன்புறை எதிர் அழிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புள்","புள்",2,"pul","scene","bird"],["அயிர்","அயிர்",3,"pira","scene","fine sand"],["வேழ","வேழம்",5,"maram","simile","reed"],["கவரியின்","கவரி",6,"pira","simile","yak-tail whisk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:242":{"book":"natrinai","num":242,"t":["இலை இல பிடவம் ஈர் மலர் அரும்ப","புதல் இவர் தளவம் பூங் கொடி அவிழ","பொன் எனக் கொன்றை மலர மணி எனப்","பல் மலர் காயாங் குறுஞ் சினை கஞல","கார் தொடங்கின்றே காலை வல் விரைந்து","செல்க பாக நின் தேரே உவக்காண்","கழிப் பெயர் களரில் போகிய மட மான்","விழிக் கட் பேதையொடு இனன் இரிந்து ஓட","காமர் நெஞ்சமொடு அகலா","தேடூஉ நின்ற இரலை ஏறே"],"p":"","r":"Returning with his work done, the man sees the rains begin (the leafless pidavam budding, the thalavam vine opening on the bush, konrai flowering like gold and kaayaa crowding its twigs with gem-blue blossom) and tells his charioteer to drive fast this morning, pointing to the stag on the salt flat who, when the herd fled with his young doe, stands with a longing heart, not leaving, searching for her.","n":"The poet's name, Vizhikkat Pethai Perungannanar, is taken from line 8, the wide-eyed doe. The stag is the one creature in the poem that does not move: everything else opens, blooms or flees, and he stands searching, which is the man's state put outside him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","paruvam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 242","lb":"NT242","poet":"விழிக்கட்பேதைப் பெருங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி மறுத்தராநின்ற தலைமகன் கார் கண்டு பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றி மறுத்தராநின்ற"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பிடவம்","பிடவம்",1,"maram","scene",""],["தளவம்","தளவம்",2,"maram","scene",""],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",3,"maram","simile",""],["காயாங்","காயா",4,"maram","simile",""],["மான்","மான்",7,"maa","scene","doe"],["இரலை","இரலை",10,"maa","iraicchi","stag"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","இரலை","confirmed","1/1","the man too will not rest until he has found his wife again, so the chariot must hurry"]],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:243":{"book":"natrinai","num":243,"t":["தேம் படு சிலம்பில் தெள் அறல் தழீஇய","துறுகல் அயல தூ மணல் அடைகரை","அலங்கு சினை பொதுளிய நறு வடி மாஅத்துப்","பொதும்புதோறு அல்கும் பூங் கண் இருங் குயில்","கவறு பெயர்த்தன்ன நில்லா வாழ்க்கை இட்டு","அகறல் ஓம்புமின் அறிவுடையீர் என","கையறத் துறப்போர்க் கழறுவ போல","மெய் உற இருந்து மேவர நுவல","இன்னாது ஆகிய காலை பொருள்வயிற்","பிரியல் ஆடவர்க்கு இயல்பு எனின்","அரிது மன்றம்ம அறத்தினும் பொருளே"],"p":"","r":"The woman says that the dark koel, staying in every grove of the fragrant mango on the bank of the clear stream below the honeyed slope, sits body to body with its mate and calls lovingly, as if rebuking those who leave helplessly, 'wise ones, do not go away and leave a life that does not stay, like dice turned over'; and if parting for wealth even in such a painful season is the nature of men, then wealth, surely, is dearer than virtue.","n":"The last line turns the moral order upside down on purpose: by men's conduct wealth is rated above virtue. The koel's call is given words by the speaker herself, so the rebuke she cannot speak to him she puts in a bird's mouth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","aram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 243","lb":"NT243","poet":"காமக்கணிப் பசலையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகள் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அறல்","அறல்",1,"pira","scene","stream sand"],["துறுகல்","துறுகல்",2,"pira","scene","boulder"],["மாஅத்துப்","மா",3,"maram","scene","mango"],["குயில்","குயில்",4,"pul","simile",""],["கவறு","கவறு",5,"pira","simile","dice"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:244":{"book":"natrinai","num":244,"t":["விழுந்த மாரிப் பெருந் தண் சாரல்","கூதிர்க் கூதளத்து அலரி நாறும்","மாதர் வண்டின் நயவரும் தீம் குரல்","மணம் நாறு சிலம்பின் அசுணம் ஓர்க்கும்","உயர் மலை நாடற்கு உரைத்தல் ஒன்றோ","துயர் மருங்கு அறியா அன்னைக்கு இந் நோய்","தணியுமாறு இது என உரைத்தல் ஒன்றோ","செய்யாய் ஆதலின் கொடியை தோழி","மணி கெழு நெடு வரை அணி பெற நிவந்த","செயலை அம் தளிர் அன்ன என்","மதன் இல் மா மெய்ப் பசலையும் கண்டே"],"p":"","r":"The girl tells her friend she is cruel: she could tell the lord of the high hill, where on the cool rain-soaked slope the koothalam flower of the cold season smells and the asunam listens to the sweet humming of bees, or she could tell the mother, who does not know where the grief comes from, that this is how the sickness will abate; she does neither, though she has seen the pallor on the girl's body, like the tender shoots of the asoka on the tall gem-bright hill.","n":"She sets out the friend's two possible acts as a choice (ஒன்றோ ... ஒன்றோ) and blames her for taking neither, which is the moment of அறத்தொடுநிலை from the girl's side. The asunam, an animal that hangs on music, listening to bees, is scene; read as a hint that the girl too is held by sweet sound it is a single match of mood, not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 244","lb":"NT244","poet":"கூற்றங்குமரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடுநிலை வலித்த தோழியைத் தலைவி முகம் புக்கது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","அறத்தொடுநிலை வலித்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கூதளத்து","கூதளம்",2,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டின்","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["அசுணம்","அசுணம்",4,"maa","scene","asunam"],["செயலை","செயலை",10,"maram","simile","asoka"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:245":{"book":"natrinai","num":245,"t":["நகையாகின்றே தோழி தகைய","அணி மலர் முண்டகத்து ஆய் பூங்கோதை","மணி மருள் ஐம்பால் வண்டு படத் தைஇ","துணி நீர்ப் பௌவம் துணையோடு ஆடி","ஒழுகு நுண் நுசுப்பின் அகன்ற அல்குல்","தெளிதீம் கிளவி யாரையோ என்","அரிது புணர் இன் உயிர் வவ்விய நீ என","பூண் மலி நெடுந் தேர்ப் புரவி தாங்கி","தான் நம் அணங்குதல் அறியான் நம்மின்","தான் அணங்குற்றமை கூறி கானல்","சுரும்பு இமிர் சுடர் நுதல் நோக்கி","பெருங் கடற் சேர்ப்பன் தொழுது நின்றதுவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, who has agreed to help him, tells the girl it makes her laugh: when they had woven a garland of mundakam flowers into her gem-dark hair and played with their companions in the clear sea, the shore lord reined in the horses of his tall chariot and, asking 'who are you, slender-waisted, sweet-spoken, who have seized my life?', told them how he had been struck, never knowing that he was striking them, and stood with joined hands gazing at her bright brow.","n":"The joke is the reversal: the man reports himself as the victim of an அணங்கு, while the girls are the ones afflicted, so each side thinks itself the one struck. The friend makes the girl laugh in order to open her to the man's suit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","anangu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 245","lb":"NT245","poet":"அல்லங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"குறை நேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகளை முகம் புக்கது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","குறை நேர்ந்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["முண்டகத்து","முண்டகம்",2,"maram","ornament",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["புரவி","புரவி",8,"maa","scene","horse"],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",11,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:246":{"book":"natrinai","num":246,"t":["இடூஉ ஊங்கண் இனிய படூஉம்","நெடுஞ் சுவர்ப் பல்லியும் பாங்கில் தேற்றும்","மனை மா நொச்சி மீமிசை மாச் சினை","வினை மாண் இருங் குயில் பயிற்றலும் பயிற்றும்","உரம் புரி உள்ளமொடு சுரம் பல நீந்தி","செய்பொருட்கு அகன்றனராயினும் பொய்யலர்","வருவர் வாழி தோழி புறவின்","பொன் வீக் கொன்றையொடு பிடவுத் தளை அவிழ","இன் இசை வானம் இரங்கும் அவர்","வருதும் என்ற பருவமோ இதுவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the wasting woman that he will come and does not lie, though he crossed many wastes with a firm heart for wealth to be made: the lizard on the long wall chirps sweetly on the lucky side, the dark koel on the top branch of the house's nochchi calls again and again, and in the scrub the golden konrai is out with the pidavu buds opening and the sky rumbling with sweet sound; is this not the season he named when he said 'we will come'?","n":"The reassurance moves from the house outward: two household omens first (the lizard, the koel on the nochchi by the door), then the land, then the sky, and it closes as a question, so the friend's certainty is offered for the woman to confirm. The colophon's thinai is paalai, by the man's road; what the poem shows is the waiting at home in the rains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 246","lb":"NT246","poet":"காப்பியஞ் சேந்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறீஇயது","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பல்லியும்","பல்லி",2,"pira","scene","lizard"],["நொச்சி","நொச்சி",3,"maram","scene",""],["குயில்","குயில்",4,"pul","scene",""],["கொன்றையொடு","கொன்றை",8,"maram","scene",""],["பிடவுத்","பிடவு",8,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:247":{"book":"natrinai","num":247,"t":["தொன்று படு துப்பொடு முரண் மிகச் சினைஇக்","கொன்ற யானைச் செங் கோடு கழாஅ","அழி துளி பொழிந்த இன் குரல் எழிலி","எஃகுறு பஞ்சிற்று ஆகி வைகறைக்","கோடு உயர் நெடு வரை ஆடும் நாட நீ","நல்காய்ஆயினும் நயன் இல செய்யினும்","நின் வழிப்படூஉம் என் தோழி நல் நுதல்","விருந்து இறைகூடிய பசலைக்கு","மருந்து பிறிது இன்மை நன்கு அறிந்தனை சென்மே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hill lord, in whose land the sweet-voiced cloud, having poured heavy drops that wash the red tusks of an elephant killed in a fierce old enmity, turns to carded cotton and plays over the tall peaks at dawn, that even if he does not grant grace, even if he does unkind things, her fair-browed friend will follow his way; and that he knows well there is no medicine but him for the pallor that has come to her like a guest.","n":"The pallor is a விருந்து, a guest who has settled in the house; a guest is fed and kept, so the figure says the girl's sickness is now at home with her. The killed elephant whose tusks the rain washes is scene; a reading of it as the danger he runs, or as a wrong the rain cleans away, has only one point of contact.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","pasalai","marundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 247","lb":"NT247","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"நீட்டியாமை வரை எனத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","நீட்டியாமை வரை"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானைச்","யானை",2,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:248":{"book":"natrinai","num":248,"t":["சிறு வீ முல்லைத் தேம் கமழ் பசு வீ","பொறி வரி நல் மான் புகர் முகம் கடுப்ப","தண் புதல் அணிபெற மலர வண் பெயல்","கார் வரு பருவம் என்றனர்மன் இனி","பேர் அஞர் உள்ளம் நடுங்கல் காணியர்","அன்பு இன்மையின் பண்பு இல பயிற்றும்","பொய் இடி அதிர் குரல் வாய் செத்து ஆலும்","இன மயில் மடக் கணம் போல","நினை மருள்வேனோ வாழியர் மழையே"],"p":"","r":"Addressing the cloud, the friend says that he said the season would be when the small honey-scented mullai flowers bloom to adorn the cool bush like the spotted face of the striped deer and the rich rain comes; now, lacking love, the cloud practises unkindness with the roaring voice of false thunder to see the suffering heart tremble; but will she be fooled like the flock of young peacocks that take it for true and dance? Long live, cloud!","n":"She grants that the signs look right and refuses them: the thunder is பொய், and she sets herself apart from the peafowl who believe it. To deny the season is to keep the man's promise unbroken, since if this is not the season he is not late.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 248","lb":"NT248","poet":"காசிபன் கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பருவம் கண்டு ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகளைத் தோழி மழை மேல் வைத்துப் பருவம் மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"karpu","மழை மேல் வைத்துப் பருவம் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முல்லைத்","முல்லை",1,"maram","simile",""],["மான்","மான்",2,"maa","simile","deer"],["மயில்","மயில்",8,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:249":{"book":"natrinai","num":249,"t":["இரும்பின் அன்ன கருங் கோட்டுப் புன்னை","நீலத்து அன்ன பாசிலை அகம்தொறும்","வெள்ளி அன்ன விளங்கு இணர் நாப்பண்","பொன்னின் அன்ன நறுந் தாது உதிர","புலிப் பொறிக் கொண்ட பூ நாறு குரூஉச் சுவல்","வரி வண்டு ஊதலின் புலி செத்து வெரீஇ","பரியுடை வயங்கு தாள் பந்தின் தாவத்","தாங்கவும் தகை வரை நில்லா ஆங்கண்","மல்லல்அம் சேரி கல்லெனத் தோன்றி","அம்பல் மூதூர் அலர் எழ","சென்றது அன்றோ கொண்கன் தேரே"],"p":"","r":"The shore lord's chariot came clattering into the rich street and went, setting gossip rising in the old whispering town: there, under the punnai with its trunk like iron, leaves like sapphire, clusters like silver and pollen like gold falling between, the striped bees hummed on the horse's flower-scented neck, spotted like a tiger with the pollen, and the horse, taking itself for set upon by a tiger, leapt like a ball and would not stand however it was reined.","n":"The chain of causes is comic and exact: pollen spots the horse, bees come to the pollen, the horse takes the spots and the humming for a tiger, bolts, and the noise in the street announces what the lovers kept secret. The poem ends on the chariot going, so the gossip outlasts the visit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","varaivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 249","lb":"NT249","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரைவிடை மெலிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu","வரைவிடை மெலிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene",""],["இரும்பின்","இரும்பு",1,"pira","simile","iron"],["நீலத்து","நீலம்",2,"pira","simile","sapphire"],["வெள்ளி","வெள்ளி",3,"pira","simile","silver"],["பொன்னின்","பொன்",4,"pira","simile","gold"],["புலிப்","புலி",5,"maa","simile","tiger"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene","bee"],["பந்தின்","பந்து",7,"pira","simile","ball"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:250":{"book":"natrinai","num":250,"t":["நகுகம் வாராய் பாண பகுவாய்","அரி பெய் கிண்கிணி ஆர்ப்ப தெருவில்","தேர் நடைபயிற்றும் தேமொழிப் புதல்வன்","பூ நாறு செவ் வாய் சிதைத்த சாந்தமொடு","காமர் நெஞ்சம் துரப்ப யாம் தன்","முயங்கல் விருப்பொடு குறுகினேமாக","பிறை வனப்பு உற்ற மாசு அறு திரு நுதல்","நாறு இருங் கதுப்பின் எம் காதலி வேறு உணர்ந்து","வெரூஉம் மான் பிணையின் ஒரீஇ","யாரையோ என்று இகந்து நின்றதுவே"],"p":"","r":"The man, come home with his son, tells the bard to come and laugh with him: when his sweet-lisping son, anklets ringing, was practising walking his little chariot in the street, his flower-scented red mouth smeared with sandal paste, the man, his heart urging him, came close wanting to hug him, and his beloved, with her crescent-lovely brow and fragrant dark hair, taking it otherwise, shied away like a startled doe and stood apart, saying 'who are you?'","n":"The joke is against himself: the wife greets her own husband with the question a stranger gets. He used the son as his way back into the house, and she reads even his embrace of the child as coming from elsewhere; the 'laugh' covers his failure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","makkal","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 250","lb":"NT250","poet":"மதுரை ஓலைக் கடையத்தார் நல்வெள்ளையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"புதல்வனொடு புக்க தலைமகன் ஆற்றானாய்ப் பாணற்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paanan",null,"karpu","புதல்வனொடு புக்க"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["கிண்கிணி","கிண்கிணி",2,"pira","ornament","anklet bells"],["சாந்தமொடு","சாந்தம்",4,"pira","ornament","sandal paste"],["மான்","மான்",9,"maa","simile","doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:251":{"book":"natrinai","num":251,"t":["நெடு நீர் அருவிய கடும் பாட்டு ஆங்கண்","பிணி முதல் அரைய பெருங் கல் வாழைக்","கொழு முதல் ஆய் கனி மந்தி கவரும்","நல் மலை நாடனை நயவா யாம் அவன்","நனி பேர் அன்பின் நின் குரல் ஓப்பி","நின் புறங்காத்தலும் காண்போய் நீ என்","தளிர் ஏர் மேனித் தொல் கவின் அழிய","பலி பெறு கடவுட் பேணி கலி சிறந்து","நுடங்கு நிலைப் பறவை உடங்கு பீள் கவரும்","தோடு இடம் கோடாய் கிளர்ந்து","நீடினை விளைமோ வாழிய தினையே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening outside, the speaker turns to the millet: in the land of the good hill where the falls roar and a female monkey snatches the fine fruit from the thick stem of the great wild plantain, we, loving its lord, have guarded you with great love and driven the birds off your ears, you have seen it; now that the old beauty of my shoot-like body is fading and the household, worshipping the god of offerings, makes a festival, do not bend your ears for the swaying birds to snatch, but rise and ripen late, long live, millet!","n":"The plea to the millet is the news the man is meant to hear: when the crop is in, the girl's days in the field end and she is shut in the house. Whether the festival for the god is a harvest rite or the mother's remedy for the girl's changed looks, the text does not settle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","pasalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 251","lb":"NT251","poet":"மதுரைப் பெருமருதிள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகச் செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira","thalaivan","kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவிய","அருவி",1,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["வாழைக்","வாழை",2,"maram","scene","plantain"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",3,"maa","iraicchi","female monkey"],["கடவுட்","கடவுள்",8,"deyvam","scene",""],["பறவை","பறவை",9,"pul","scene",""],["தினையே","தினை",11,"unavu","scene","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மந்தி","not_confirmed","0/1","the man should take the girl now, while she is still in the field, before the harvest shuts her away"]],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:252":{"book":"natrinai","num":252,"t":["உலவை ஓமை ஒல்கு நிலை ஒடுங்கி","சிள்வீடு கறங்கும் சேய் நாட்டு அத்தம்","திறம் புரி கொள்கையொடு இறந்து செயின அல்லது","அரும் பொருட் கூட்டம் இருந்தோர்க்கு இல் என","வலியா நெஞ்சம் வலிப்ப சூழ்ந்த","வினை இடை விலங்கல போலும் புனை சுவர்ப்","பாவை அன்ன பழிதீர் காட்சி","ஐது ஏய்ந்து அகன்ற அல்குல் மை கூர்ந்து","மலர் பிணைத்தன்ன மா இதழ் மழைக் கண்","முயல் வேட்டு எழுந்த முடுகு விசைக் கத நாய்","நல் நாப் புரையும் சீறடி","பொம்மல் ஓதி புனைஇழை குணனே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the anxious woman that his heart, once unresolved, resolved on the far country's track where the dead oomai stands bent and cicadas shrill, holding that rare wealth does not gather for those who stay unless they go with a firm will and act; but the work so planned seems to be blocked by her qualities: her blameless look like a doll on a painted wall, her wide hips, her dark rain-eyes like flowers strung in pairs, her small feet like the good tongue of the fierce dog leaping to hunt a hare, and her full hair and fine jewels.","n":"The man's maxim is given its full weight before it is checked, and what checks it is not counter-argument but a list of her features: the friend comforts her by telling her what her own beauty does to his resolve. The dog's tongue simile, red and soft and narrow, is the oddest and most exact of the set.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","muyarchi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 252","lb":"NT252","poet":"அம்மெய்யன் நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரியும் எனக் கவன்ற தலைமகட்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பொருள்வயிற் பிரியும் எனக் கவன்ற"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஓமை","ஓமை",1,"maram","scene",""],["சிள்வீடு","சிள்வீடு",2,"pira","scene","cicada"],["பாவை","பாவை",7,"pira","simile","painted figure"],["முயல்","முயல்",10,"maa","simile","hare"],["நாய்","நாய்",10,"maa","simile","dog"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:253":{"book":"natrinai","num":253,"t":["புள்ளுப் பதி சேரினும் புணர்ந்தோர்க் காணினும்","பள்ளி யானையின் வெய்ய உயிரினை","கழிபட வருந்திய எவ்வமொடு பெரிது அழிந்து","எனவ கேளாய் நினையினை நீ நனி","உள்ளினும் பனிக்கும் ஒள் இழைக் குறுமகள்","பேர் இசை உருமொடு மாரி முற்றிய","பல் குடைக் கள்ளின் வண் மகிழ்ப் பாரி","பலவு உறு குன்றம் போல","பெருங் கவின் எய்திய அருங் காப்பினளே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that when birds return to their roosts or he sees couples together he sighs hot breaths like a stalled elephant, wasting with pain, and does not hear her, only thinks; the bright-jewelled girl, whom one trembles even to think of, has come to great beauty and is now hard to reach, guarded like the jackfruit-laden hill of Paari, generous with many pots of toddy, where thunder of great sound and rain have closed round.","n":"Paari's hill is the measure of both beauty and inaccessibility: rich and besieged, it could not be taken by force. The friend's point is that the time for stealing visits is over.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 253","lb":"NT253","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"செறிப்பு அறிவிறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புள்ளுப்","புள்",1,"pul","scene",""],["யானையின்","யானை",2,"maa","simile","elephant"],["கள்ளின்","கள்",7,"unavu","simile","toddy"],["பலவு","பலா",8,"maram","simile","jackfruit"]],"en":[["patron","பாரி",7]],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:254":{"book":"natrinai","num":254,"t":["வண்டல் தைஇயும் வரு திரை உதைத்தும்","குன்று ஓங்கு வெண் மணற் கொடி அடும்பு கொய்தும்","துனி இல் நல்மொழி இனிய கூறியும்","சொல் எதிர் பெறாஅய் உயங்கி மெல்லச்","செலீஇய செல்லும் ஒலி இரும் பரப்ப","உமணர் தந்த உப்பு நொடை நெல்லின்","அயினி மா இன்று அருந்த நீலக்","கணம் நாறு பெருந் தொடை புரளும் மார்பின்","துணை இலை தமியை சேக்குவை அல்லை","நேர் கண் சிறு தடி நீரின் மாற்றி","வானம் வேண்டா உழவின் எம்","கானல்அம் சிறு குடிச் சேந்தனை செலின"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the wide roaring sea, who built sand houses with them, kicked the incoming waves, plucked the adumbu creeper on the high white dune and spoke sweet words without sulking, but got no answer and is going away slowly, wearied, that if he stays in their small hamlet by the shore, whose farming (changing the water with small straight staves) needs no rain, and eats today the rice bartered for the salt the umanar brought, he will not sleep alone with no companion, his chest where the large blue-lily garland rolls.","n":"The colophon says the friend says what she has made up (படைத்து மொழிந்தது): the invitation is a test or a feint, and the reader cannot tell whether the companion she promises is the girl. The salt pan, farming that needs no sky, is a picture of the shore people's self-sufficiency set against the man's need.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","virundhu","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 254","lb":"NT254","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி படைத்து மொழிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","படைத்து மொழிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["திரை","திரை",1,"pira","scene","wave"],["அடும்பு","அடும்பு",2,"maram","scene",""],["உமணர்","உமணர்",6,"seythi","scene","salt traders"],["உப்பு","உப்பு",6,"pira","gift_food","salt"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",6,"unavu","gift_food",""],["நீலக்","நீலம்",7,"maram","ornament","blue lily"],["உழவின்","உழவு",11,"seythi","scene","salt farming"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:255":{"book":"natrinai","num":255,"t":["கழுது கால் கிளர ஊர் மடிந்தன்றே","உரு கெழு மரபின் குறிஞ்சி பாடி","கடியுடை வியல் நகர்க் கானவர் துஞ்சார்","வயக் களிறு பொருத வாள் வரி உழுவை","கல் முகைச் சிலம்பில் குழுமும் அன்னோ","மென் தோள் நெகிழ்ந்து நாம் வருந்தினும் இன்று அவர்","வாரார்ஆயினோ நன்றுமன்தில்ல","உயர் வரை அடுக்கத்து ஒளிறுபு மின்னிப்","பெயல் கால்மயங்கிய பொழுது கழி பானாள்","திருமணி அரவுத் தேர்ந்து உழல","உருமுச் சிவந்து எறியும் ஓங்கு வரை ஆறே"],"p":"","r":"The girl says the ghouls stir and the town has gone to sleep, the hill folk of the guarded house stay awake singing the awesome kurinji, and the sword-striped tiger that fought a strong tusker roars on the slope of rock caves; alas, though our soft shoulders loosen and we suffer, it would be good if he did not come tonight, by the path on the high hill where lightning flashes on the ridges, rain and wind mingle past midnight, the snake wanders searching for its gem and the thunder, red, strikes.","n":"She wishes away the meeting she longs for, setting her own wasting (மென் தோள் நெகிழ்ந்து) against his danger and choosing his safety; the path itself is the poem's subject, as the colophon's ஆறு பார்த்து says.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 255","lb":"NT255","poet":"ஆலம்பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"ஆறு பார்த்து உற்றது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","ஆறு பார்த்து உற்றது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["கழுது","கழுது",1,"pira","scene","ghoul"],["குறிஞ்சி","குறிஞ்சி",2,"yaazh","scene","kurinji tune"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",3,"seythi","scene","hill folk"],["களிறு","களிறு",4,"maa","scene","tusker"],["உழுவை","உழுவை",4,"maa","scene","tiger"],["அரவுத்","அரவு",10,"pira","scene","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:256":{"book":"natrinai","num":256,"t":["நீயே பாடல் சான்ற பழி தபு சீறடி","அல்கு பெரு நலத்து அமர்த்த கண்ணை","காடே நிழல் கவின் இழந்த அழல் கவர் மரத்த","புலம்பு வீற்றிருந்து நலம் சிதைந்தனவே","இந் நிலை தவிர்ந்தனம் செலவே வைந் நுதிக்","களவுடன் கமழ பிடவுத் தளை அவிழ","கார் பெயல் செய்த காமர் காலை","மடப் பிணை தழீஇய மா எருத்து இரலை","காழ் கொள் வேலத்து ஆழ் சினை பயந்த","கண் கவர் வரி நிழல் வதியும்","தண் படு கானமும் தவிர்ந்தனம் செலவே"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his wife, who grieved thinking he had left for wealth: you have small blameless feet worth singing, great beauty and warring eyes, while the forest's trees, seized by fire, have lost the beauty of their shade, and loneliness sits enthroned there; so we have given up going; and even the cool forest of the rains, when the kalaa blooms with the pidavu and the dark-necked stag, holding his young doe, rests in the striped shade of the thorny vela tree, we have given that up too.","n":"He renounces both seasons of the road, the burnt summer forest and the pleasant forest of the rains, and the doubled refrain (தவிர்ந்தனம் செலவே) is the promise. The stag who holds his doe in the shade is what he chooses to be.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ilvaazhkkai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 256","lb":"NT256","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்தான் என்று ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகளைத் தலைமகன் ஆற்றியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தலைமகளைத் தலைமகன் ஆற்றியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மரத்த","மரம்",3,"maram","absent",""],["களவுடன்","களா",6,"maram","scene",""],["பிடவுத்","பிடவு",6,"maram","scene",""],["பிணை","பிணை",8,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["இரலை","இரலை",8,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["வேலத்து","வேலம்",9,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","இரலை","confirmed","1/1","the man will stay and keep his wife as the stag keeps his doe, even in the season that invites travel"]],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:257":{"book":"natrinai","num":257,"t":["விளிவு இல் அரவமொடு தளி சிறந்து உரைஇ","மழை எழுந்து இறுத்த நளிர் தூங்கு சிலம்பின்","கழை அமல்பு நீடிய வான் உயர் நெடுங் கோட்டு","இலங்கு வெள் அருவி வியன் மலைக் கவாஅன்","அரும்பு வாய் அவிழ்ந்த கருங் கால் வேங்கைப்","பொன் மருள் நறு வீ கல்மிசைத் தாஅம்","நல் மலை நாட நயந்தனை அருளாய்","இயங்குநர் மடிந்த அயம் திகழ் சிறு நெறிக்","கடு மா வழங்குதல் அறிந்தும்","நடு நாள் வருதி நோகோ யானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the good hill, where cloud has risen with endless roar and pouring drops and settled on the cold dripping slope, bamboo grows thick to the sky on the tall peak, white falls shine on the side of the wide hill and the dark-trunked vengai's gold-like fragrant flowers spread on the rock, that he loves but is not gracious: knowing that fierce beasts roam the narrow path by the pools where no traveller goes, he comes at midnight, and she grieves for it.","n":"'You love, yet you are not gracious' splits the man's two duties: desire brings him at midnight, but grace would spare her the fear, which only marriage can do. The scene is all beauty; the danger is stated in two lines without description.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","iravu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 257","lb":"NT257","poet":"வண்ணக்கன் சோருமருங்குமரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகனது ஏதம் சொல்லி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","ஏதம் சொல்லி வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["கழை","கழை",3,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["அருவி","அருவி",4,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",5,"maram","simile","vengai tree"],["மா","மா",9,"maa","scene","beast"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:258":{"book":"natrinai","num":258,"t":["பல் பூங் கானல் பகற்குறி மரீஇ","செல்வல் கொண்க செறித்தனள் யாயே","கதிர் கால் வெம்பக் கல்காய் ஞாயிற்றுத்","திருவுடை வியல் நகர் வரு விருந்து அயர்மார்","பொற்றொடி மகளிர் புறங்கடை உகுத்த","கொக்கு உகிர் நிமிரல் மாந்தி எல் பட","அகல் அங்காடி அசை நிழல் குவித்த","பச்சிறாக் கவர்ந்த பசுங் கட் காக்கை","தூங்கல் வங்கத்துக் கூம்பில் சேக்கும்","மருங்கூர்ப் பட்டினத்து அன்ன இவள்","நெருங்கு ஏர் எல்வளை ஓடுவ கண்டே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the shore lord that she is going, she who frequented the day trysts in the flowered shore grove: the mother has shut the girl in, seeing her close-fitting bright bangles slipping, the girl who is like the port of Marungur, where the gold-bangled women of the rich wide houses spill at the back door the rice, like heron's claws, cooked for arriving guests, and the fresh-eyed crow, having eaten it while the sun burns the rocks, at dusk snatches the fresh prawns heaped in the shade of the market and roosts on the mast of a rocking ship.","n":"The comparison runs on the port's fullness, a whole day of plenty from the guest's rice to the roost on the mast, and the girl is like that abundance; the crow that eats freely all day is set against the girl now shut in. Read as the man feeding at will, the crow has one point of contact, not two, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 258","lb":"NT258","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொக்கு","கொக்கு",6,"pul","simile","heron"],["நிமிரல்","நிமிரல்",6,"unavu","gift_food","cooked rice"],["பச்சிறாக்","இறா",8,"pira","gift_food","prawn"],["காக்கை","காக்கை",8,"pul","scene","crow"],["வங்கத்துக்","வங்கம்",9,"seythi","scene","ship"]],"en":[["town","மருங்கூர்ப்",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:259":{"book":"natrinai","num":259,"t":["யாங்குச் செய்வாம்கொல் தோழி பொன் வீ","வேங்கை ஓங்கிய தேம் கமழ் சாரல்","பெருங் கல் நாடனொடு இரும் புனத்து அல்கி","செவ் வாய்ப் பைங் கிளி ஓப்பி அவ் வாய்ப்","பெரு வரை அடுக்கத்து அருவி ஆடி","சாரல் ஆரம் வண்டு பட நீவி","பெரிது அமர்ந்து இயைந்த கேண்மை சிறு நனி","அரிய போலக் காண்பேன் விரி திரைக்","கடல் பெயர்ந்தனைய ஆகி","புலர் பதம் கொண்டன ஏனற் குரலே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the girl what they will do: the friendship they made in great delight with the lord of the great-rock land, on the honey-scented slope where the gold-flowered vengai stands tall (staying in the wide millet field, chasing the red-beaked green parrots, bathing in the falls of the great hill's side, smoothing on the slope's sandal where bees settle), she now sees becoming very hard to keep, for the millet heads have taken their dry ripeness, like the spreading waves of the sea drawing back.","n":"The list of shared pleasures is all of the field season, so the ripened millet ends the whole catalogue at once; the sea simile in a hill poem measures the field's change from green waves to dry stalks.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 259","lb":"NT259","poet":"கொற்றங் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகளைச் செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",2,"maram","scene",""],["கிளி","கிளி",4,"pul","scene",""],["அருவி","அருவி",5,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",6,"maram","ornament","sandal"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene","bee"],["ஏனற்","ஏனல்",10,"unavu","simile","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:260":{"book":"natrinai","num":260,"t":["கழுநீர் மேய்ந்த கருந் தாள் எருமை","பழனத் தாமரைப் பனிமலர் முணைஇ","தண்டு சேர் மள்ளரின் இயலி அயலது","குன்று சேர் வெண் மணல் துஞ்சும் ஊர","வெய்யை போல முயங்குதி முனை எழத்","தெவ்வர்த் தேய்த்த செவ் வேல் வயவன்","மலி புனல் வாயில் இருப்பை அன்ன என்","ஒலி பல் கூந்தல் நலம் பெறப் புனைந்த","முகை அவிழ் கோதை வாட்டிய","பகைவன்மன் யான் மறந்து அமைகலனே"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the lord of the town, where the dark-legged buffalo, having grazed on the red lilies and tired of the cold lotus of the field, walks like warriors with staves and sleeps on the hill-high white sand nearby, that he embraces her as if he desired her; but he who withered the bud-opened garland once adorned to beautify her thick hair, beautiful as Iruppai at the flood-fed gate of the red-speared warrior who wore down his foes, was her enemy, and she cannot forget it and be at peace.","n":"'My enemy' (பகைவன்மன்) is said with the regretful மன்: he was, once, the man who spoiled her garland in love; now he is the man who spoils it by neglect, and the old embrace makes the new one a counterfeit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 260","lb":"NT260","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"ஊடல் மறுத்த தலைமகள் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","ஊடல் மறுத்த"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கழுநீர்","கழுநீர்",1,"maram","ullurai","red lily"],["எருமை","எருமை",1,"maa","ullurai","buffalo"],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",2,"maram","ullurai","lotus"],["மள்ளரின்","மள்ளர்",3,"seythi","simile","warriors"],["மணல்","மணல்",4,"pira","ullurai","sand"],["கோதை","கோதை",9,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[["town","இருப்பை",7]],"dv":[["ullurai","எருமை","confirmed","1/1","the husband and the women he goes to"]],"ch":"nt:13"},"nt:261":{"book":"natrinai","num":261,"t":["அருளிலர்வாழி தோழி மின்னு வசிபு","இருள் தூங்கு விசும்பின் அதிரும் ஏறொடு","வெஞ் சுடர் கரந்த கமஞ் சூல் வானம்","நெடும் பல் குன்றத்துக் குறும் பல மறுகி","தா இல் பெரும் பெயல் தலைஇய யாமத்து","களிறு அகப்படுத்த பெருஞ் சின மாசுணம்","வெளிறு இல் காழ் மரம் பிணித்து நனி மிளிர்க்கும்","சாந்தம் போகிய தேம் கமழ் விடர் முகை","எருவை நறும் பூ நீடிய","பெரு வரைச் சிறு நெறி வருதலானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, knowing the man stands within hearing, tells the girl he has no pity: he comes at midnight in the heavy rain, when lightning splits the dark sky and thunder roars over the many tall hills, by the narrow path of the great rock where a raging python that has caught an elephant coils round a hard-cored tree and wrenches it, past caves fragrant with sandal and tall eruvai flowers.","n":"'He has no grace' is said of a man who risks his life to come: the complaint is the friend's way of telling him, overhearing, that the night tryst must end and the only safe road is marriage. The python crushing the elephant gives the hearer the path's danger in one picture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu","varaivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 261","lb":"NT261","poet":"சேந்தன் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி இரவுக்குறி விலக்கி வரைவு கடாயது தலைமகள் இயற்பட மொழிந்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","இரவுக்குறி விலக்கி வரைவு கடாயது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைமகள் இயற்பட மொழிந்ததூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",6,"maa","scene",""],["மாசுணம்","மாசுணம்",6,"pira","scene","python"],["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",8,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["எருவை","எருவை",9,"maram","scene","reed flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:262":{"book":"natrinai","num":262,"t":["தண் புனக் கருவிளைக் கண் போல் மா மலர்","ஆடு மயிற் பீலியின் வாடையொடு துயல்வர","உறை மயக்குற்ற ஊர் துஞ்சு யாமத்து","நடுங்கு பிணி நலிய நல் எழில் சாஅய்","துனி கூர் மனத்தள் முனி படர் உழக்கும்","பணைத் தோள் அரும்பிய சுணங்கின் கணைக் கால்","குவளை நாறும் கூந்தல் தேமொழி","இவளின் தீர்ந்தும் ஆள்வினை வலிப்ப","பிரிவல் நெஞ்சு என்னும்ஆயின்","அரிது மன்றம்ம இன்மையது இளிவே"],"p":"","r":"The hero tells his heart that she is already wasting: in the midnight of the sleeping town, when the north wind sways the great karuvilai flowers like the feathers of a dancing peacock, a trembling sickness thins her beauty; yet even if the heart, set on effort, says it will leave this girl with flower-scented hair and honeyed speech, the shame of having nothing is hard to bear.","n":"The poem ends on the other side of the scale: the last line, about the shame of poverty, is the argument for going, and the colophon reads the whole as his checking his departure. He imagines her suffering in a future night as if it were already present.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","inmai","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 262","lb":"NT262","poet":"பெருந்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகள் ஆற்றாக் குறிப்பு அறிந்து பிரிவிடை விலக்கியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை விலக்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["கருவிளைக்","கருவிளை",1,"maram","simile","butterfly-pea"],["மயிற்","மயில்",2,"pul","simile",""],["குவளை","குவளை",7,"maram","scene","blue water-lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:263":{"book":"natrinai","num":263,"t":["பிறை வனப்பு இழந்த நுதலும் யாழ நின்","இறை வரை நில்லா வளையும் மறையாது","ஊர் அலர் தூற்றும் கௌவையும் நாண் விட்டு","உரை அவற்கு உரையாம்ஆயினும் இரை வேட்டு","கடுஞ் சூல் வயவொடு கானல் எய்தாது","கழனி ஒழிந்த கொடு வாய்ப் பேடைக்கு","முட முதிர் நாரை கடல் மீன் ஒய்யும்","மெல்லம் புலம்பற் கண்டு நிலைசெல்லாக்","கரப்பவும் கரப்பவும் கைம்மிக்கு","உரைத்த தோழி உண்கண் நீரே"],"p":"","r":"Overheard by the man, the friend tells the girl that though they never speak of it to him, out of shame, the forehead that has lost its crescent beauty, the bangles that slip from her wrist and the gossip of the town, her kohled tears, hidden again and again, have overflowed and told it all at the sight of the gentle lonely shore where an old lame stork, hunting, carries sea fish to his hen who, heavy with young, stays back in the field.","n":"The tears 'speak' though the women do not: the friend makes the girl's body the messenger, so that the man hears a demand without being addressed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","alar","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 263","lb":"NT263","poet":"இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி தலைமகனை வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகனை வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நாரை","நாரை",7,"pul","iraicchi","stork"],["பேடைக்கு","பேடை",6,"pul","iraicchi","hen"],["மீன்","மீன்",7,"pira","scene",""],["கழனி","கழனி",6,"pira","scene","paddy field"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","நாரை","confirmed","2/2","even a crippled old bird provides for the mate who carries his young; the man, whole and young, has not even married the girl he has made to suffer"]],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:264":{"book":"natrinai","num":264,"t":["பாம்பு அளைச் செறிய முழங்கி வலன் ஏர்பு","வான் தளி பொழிந்த காண்பு இன் காலை","அணி கிளர் கலாவம் ஐது விரித்து இயலும்","மணி புரை எருத்தின் மஞ்ஞை போல நின்","வீ பெய் கூந்தல் வீசு வளி உளர","ஏகுதி மடந்தை எல்லின்று பொழுதே","வேய் பயில் இறும்பில் கோவலர் யாத்த","ஆ பூண் தெண் மணி இயம்பும்","ஈகாண் தோன்றும் எம் சிறு நல் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"Leading the girl away with him, the man urges her on: the rain has poured with thunder that drives snakes into their holes, the morning is lovely, and she walks with the wind stirring her flower-strewn hair like a peacock with a sapphire neck spreading its bright tail; the day is ending, so go on, girl, for listen, the clear bells on the cows the cowherds have tied in the bamboo thicket are ringing, and there is our small good town.","n":"The road of elopement, which paalai poems fill with heat and danger, is here a rain-washed mullai evening, and the end of it is not a wilderness but 'our town' with its cow-bells: the man reassures her by showing her the home she is coming to.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","mazhai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 264","lb":"NT264","poet":"ஆவூர்க் காவிதிகள் சாதேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உடன் போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தலைமகளை வற்புறீஇயது உடன்போய் மறுத்தரா நின்றான் ஊர்காட்டி வற்புறீஇயதும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","உடன் போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தலைமகளை வற்புறீஇயது"],["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,null,"ஊர்காட்டி வற்புறீஇயதும்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",1,"pira","scene","snake"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",4,"pul","simile","peacock"],["வீ","வீ",5,"maram","ornament",""],["வேய்","வேய்",7,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",7,"seythi","scene","cowherds"],["ஆ","ஆ",8,"maa","scene","cows"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:265":{"book":"natrinai","num":265,"t":["இறுகு புனம் மேய்ந்த அறு கோட்டு முற்றல்","அள்ளல் ஆடிய புள்ளி வரிக் கலை","வீளை அம்பின் வில்லோர் பெருமகன்","பூந் தோள் யாப்பின் மிஞிலி காக்கும்","பாரத்து அன்ன ஆர மார்பின்","சிறு கோற் சென்னி ஆரேற்றன்ன","மாரி வண் மகிழ் ஓரி கொல்லிக்","கலி மயில் கலாவத்து அன்ன இவள்","ஒலி மென் கூந்தல் நம் வயினானே"],"p":"","r":"The man who has followed the girl tells his heart that her thick soft hair, precious as Paaram that Minjili, chief of the bowmen whose whistling arrows bring down the spotted stag, guards, as the town of the Chola with the small sceptre, and like the plumage of the joyous peacocks on Kolli, the hill of Ori whose giving is like the rain, is now ours.","n":"Each comparison is a guarded or famous possession of a chief, so the girl's hair is praised as a prize won from the strongholds of the hill; the poem has no main verb as we have it, and the sense of the Chenni line is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","punarchi","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 265","lb":"NT265","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பின்னின்ற தலைமகன் நெஞ்சிற்கு உரைத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","பின்னின்ற தலைமகன் நெஞ்சிற்கு உரைத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கலை","கலை",2,"maa","scene","stag"],["வில்லோர்","வில்லோர்",3,"seythi","scene","bowmen"],["மயில்","மயில்",8,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[["king","மிஞிலி",4],["town","பாரத்து",5],["king","சென்னி",6],["king","ஓரி",7],["hill","கொல்லிக்",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:266":{"book":"natrinai","num":266,"t":["கொல்லைக் கோவலர் குறும்புனம் சேர்ந்த","குறுங் காற் குரவின் குவி இணர் வான் பூ","ஆடுடை இடைமகன் சூடப் பூக்கும்","அகலுள் ஆங்கண் சீறூரேமே","அதுவே சாலும் காமம் அன்றியும்","எம் விட்டு அகறிர்ஆயின் கொன் ஒன்று","கூறுவல் வாழியர் ஐய வேறுபட்டு","இரீஇய காலை இரியின்","பெரிய அல்லவோ பெரியவர் நிலையே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that they are folk of a small village on the wide land where, by the cowherds' little field, the short-stemmed kuravam blooms its clustered white flowers for the goatherd to wear, and that is enough for them; but besides love, if he leaves them she will say one thing: if a man flees once she has been made to live apart from her own people, is that not a great thing to say of the great?","n":"The village's contentment with wild kuravam flowers is the friend's claim that they ask for no wealth, so the only reason to leave is his own; the last line praises 'the great' in a way that shames him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","karpu","perumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 266","lb":"NT266","poet":"கச்சிப்பேட்டு இளந்தச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைமகனைச் செலவுடன்பட்டது கடிநகர் வரைப்பில் கண்டு மகிழ்ந்த தலைமகற்குத் தோழி நும்மாலே ஆயிற்று என்று சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தலைமகனைச் செலவுடன்பட்டது"],["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","நும்மாலே ஆயிற்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",1,"seythi","scene","cowherds"],["குரவின்","குரவம்",2,"maram","ornament",""],["ஆடுடை","ஆடு",3,"maa","scene","goats"],["இடைமகன்","இடைமகன்",3,"seythi","scene","herdsman"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:267":{"book":"natrinai","num":267,"t":["நொச்சி மா அரும்பு அன்ன கண்ண","எக்கர் ஞெண்டின் இருங் கிளைத் தொழுதி","இலங்கு எயிற்று ஏஎர் இன் நகை மகளிர்","உணங்கு தினை துழவும் கை போல் ஞாழல்","மணம் கமழ் நறு வீ வரிக்கும் துறைவன்","தன்னொடு புணர்த்த இன் அமர் கானல்","தனியே வருதல் நனி புலம்பு உடைத்து என","வாரேன்மன் யான் வந்தனென் தெய்ய","சிறு நா ஒண் மணித் தெள் இசை கடுப்ப","இன மீன் ஆர்கை ஈண்டு புள் ஒலிக்குரல்","இவை மகன் என்னா அளவை","வய மான் தோன்றல் வந்து நின்றனனே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, speaking where the man can hear, says that she would not have come to the sweet grove where the lord of the shore, whose crowds of crabs with eyes like nochchi buds scratch at fragrant njaazhal flowers as women with shining teeth stir drying millet with their hands, first met the girl, since coming there alone is so lonely; yet she came, and before she could say that the cries of the birds swarming over the shoals of fish, like the clear sound of small-tongued bells, were no man, he appeared there, strong as a lion.","n":"The friend pretends the grove is haunted only by memory and birds, then lets the man's sudden arrival end the poem: the meeting place is still in use, and with the guard tightened, that is the reason to marry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","kuri","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 267","lb":"NT267","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி காப்புக் கைமிக்குக் காமம் பெருகிய காலத்துச் சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது வரைவு கடாயதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","காப்புக் கைமிக்குக் காமம் பெருகிய காலத்துச் சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","வரைவு கடாயதூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["நொச்சி","நொச்சி",1,"maram","simile",""],["ஞெண்டின்","ஞெண்டு",2,"pira","simile","crab"],["தினை","தினை",4,"unavu","simile","millet"],["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",4,"maram","scene",""],["மீன்","மீன்",10,"pira","scene",""],["புள்","புள்",10,"pul","simile",""],["மான்","மான்",12,"maa","simile","lion"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:268":{"book":"natrinai","num":268,"t":["சூருடை நனந் தலைச் சுனை நீர் மல்க","மால் பெயல் தலைஇய மன் நெடுங் குன்றத்து","கருங் காற் குறிஞ்சி மதன் இல் வான் பூ","ஓவுக் கண்டன்ன இல்வரை இழைத்த","நாறு கொள் பிரசம் ஊறு நாடற்குக்","காதல் செய்தவும் காதலன்மை","யாதனிற்கொல்லோ தோழி வினவுகம்","பெய்ம் மணல் முற்றம் கடி கொண்டு","மெய்ம் மலி கழங்கின் வேலற் தந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Of the man of the land where, in the tall hills, heavy rain fills the springs of the haunted wide places and the honey of the black-stemmed kurinji's pure white flowers oozes in hives built at the rock's edge as if painted, the speaker asks why the love he made has become unlove: let us ask, friend, by guarding the sand-strewn courtyard and bringing the velan with his molucca beans.","n":"The veri rite is meant to name the god who has seized the girl; turning it to the question 'why has his love failed?' mocks the rite and points, past the velan, to the man as the real cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","velan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 268","lb":"NT268","poet":"வெறி பாடிய காமக்கண்ணியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது தலைமகன் வந்தொழுகவும் வேறுபாடு கண்டாள் அவன் வருவானாகவும் நீ வேறுபட்டாய் வெறி எடுத்துக் கொள்ளும் வகையான் என்றதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வெறி எடுத்துக் கொள்ளும் வகையான்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["சுனை","சுனை",1,"pira","scene","spring"],["குறிஞ்சி","குறிஞ்சி",3,"maram","scene",""],["பிரசம்","பிரசம்",5,"pira","simile","honey"],["கழங்கின்","கழங்கு",9,"pira","scene","divining beans"],["வேலற்","வேலன்",9,"seythi","scene","priest of Murugan"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:269":{"book":"natrinai","num":269,"t":["குரும்பை மணிப் பூண் பெருஞ் செங் கிண்கிணிப்","பால் ஆர் துவர் வாய்ப் பைம் பூட் புதல்வன்","மாலைக் கட்டில் மார்பு ஊர்பு இழிய","அவ் எயிறு ஒழுகிய அவ் வாய் மாண் நகைச்","செயிர் தீர் கொள்கை நம் உயிர் வெங் காதலி","திருமுகத்து அலமரும் கண் இணைந்து அல்கலும்","பெரும வள்ளியின் பிணிக்கும் என்னார்","சிறு பல் குன்றம் இறப்போர்","அறிவார் யார் அவர் முன்னியவ்வே"],"p":"","r":"Refusing the man entry, the friend reminds him of his son with bead-jewels and a big red anklet, his milky coral mouth, who climbs down his chest on the evening bed, and of the wife dear as their own life, with lovely teeth and a fine smile and blameless ways, whose eyes rove over his face, who binds him every day like the valli creeper; men who cross the many small hills do not think of that, and who knows what they intend?","n":"The friend never says 'do not go': she sets the bed, the child and the wife's gaze before him and ends on a question about 'those who cross the hills', talking of him in the third person as if he were already gone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","makkal","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 269","lb":"NT269","poet":"எயினந்தை மகன் இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி வாயில் மறுத்தது செலவு அழுங்குவித்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தோழி வாயில் மறுத்தது"],["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","செலவு அழுங்குவித்ததூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வள்ளியின்","வள்ளி",7,"maram","simile","creeper"],["கிண்கிணிப்","கிண்கிணி",1,"pira","ornament","anklet bells"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:270":{"book":"natrinai","num":270,"t":["தடந் தாள் தாழைக் குடம்பை நோனாத்","தண்டலை கமழும் வண்டு படு நாற்றத்து","இருள் புரை கூந்தல் பொங்கு துகள் ஆடி","உருள் பொறி போல எம் முனை வருதல்","அணித் தகை அல்லது பிணித்தல் தேற்றாப்","பெருந் தோட் செல்வத்து இவளினும் எல்லா","எற் பெரிது அளித்தனை நீயே பொற்புடை","விரி உளைப் பொலிந்த பரியுடை நன் மான்","வேந்தர் ஓட்டிய ஏந்து வேல் நன்னன்","கூந்தல் முரற்சியின் கொடிதே","மறப்பல் மாதோ நின் விறல் தகைமையே"],"p":"","r":"Letting the husband in, the friend tells him with irony that he has been far kinder to her than to the girl of broad shoulders who knows only how to adorn herself and not how to bind him, while the other woman with dark hair fragrant of the screwpine comes rolling to their side like a toy wheel; his cruelty is worse than the cruelty Nannan, the spear-lifting king of fine maned horses who routed kings, did to a woman's hair, and she will never forget his valour.","n":"The praise of his valour and the comparison with Nannan's notorious cruelty turn the friend's consent into a rebuke: she lets him in by naming what he has done.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","kodungol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 270","lb":"NT270","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி வாயில் நேர்கின்றாள் தலைமகனை நெருங்கிச் சொல்லி வாயில் எதிர்கொண்டது உடனிலைக் கிளவி வகையால்","thd":1,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தோழி வாயில் நேர்கின்றாள் தலைமகனை நெருங்கிச் சொல்லி வாயில் எதிர்கொண்டது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["தாழைக்","தாழை",1,"maram","scene","screwpine"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",2,"pira","scene","bees"],["மான்","மான்",8,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["king","நன்னன்",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:271":{"book":"natrinai","num":271,"t":["இரும் புனிற்று எருமைப் பெருஞ் செவிக் குழவி","பைந் தாது எருவின் வைகு துயில் மடியும்","செழுந் தண் மனையோடு எம் இவண் ஒழிய","செல் பெருங் காளை பொய்ம் மருண்டு சேய் நாட்டுச்","சுவைக் காய் நெல்லிப் போக்கு அரும் பொங்கர்","வீழ் கடைத் திரள் காய் ஒருங்குடன் தின்று","வீ சுனைச் சிறு நீர் குடியினள் கழிந்த","குவளை உண்கண் என் மகள் ஓரன்ன","செய் போழ் வெட்டிய பொய்தல் ஆயம்","மாலை விரி நிலவில் பெயர்பு புறங்காண்டற்கு","மா இருந் தாழி கவிப்ப","தா இன்று கழிக எற் கொள்ளாக் கூற்றே"],"p":"","r":"A mother, bewildered in her house, says her daughter with kohled lily eyes has left the rich cool house, where the big-eared calf of the dark buffalo sleeps on fresh dung, to go with a bold youth, deceived by his lies, to a far land, eating with him the fallen gooseberries of the impassable grove and drinking the little water of flower-strewn springs; while her playmates, girls of her age who cut palm-leaf toys, go home in the evening moonlight, she asks that the great dark urn cover her and curses Death for passing her by.","n":"The calf asleep by its mother at home stands against the daughter who has gone, and the playmates returning at evening measure her absence; the mother's grief turns into a wish to be buried.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["udanpokku","magatpaal","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 271","lb":"NT271","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மனை மருண்டு சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,null,"மனை மருண்டு சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["எருமைப்","எருமை",1,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["குழவி","குழவி",1,"maa","scene","calf"],["நெல்லிப்","நெல்லி",5,"maram","gift_food","gooseberry"],["சுனைச்","சுனை",7,"pira","gift_food",""],["குவளை","குவளை",8,"maram","scene","blue water-lily"],["தாழி","தாழி",11,"pira","scene","burial urn"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:272":{"book":"natrinai","num":272,"t":["கடல்அம் காக்கைச் செவ் வாய்ச் சேவல்","படிவ மகளிர் கொடி கொய்து அழித்த","பொம்மல் அடும்பின் வெண் மணல் ஒரு சிறை","கடுஞ் சூல் வதிந்த காமர் பேடைக்கு","இருஞ் சேற்று அயிரை தேரிய தெண் கழிப்","பூஉடைக் குட்டம் துழவும் துறைவன்","நல்காமையின் நசை பழுதாக","பெருங் கையற்ற என் சிறுமை பலர் வாய்","அம்பல் மூதூர் அலர்ந்து","நோய் ஆகின்று அது நோயினும் பெரிதே"],"p":"","r":"The girl, worn by his long visits without marriage, says that the lord of the shore, where the red-beaked sea crow searches the flowered pool of the clear backwater for ayirai fish of the dark mud to feed his lovely hen resting heavy with young on a strip of white sand among the adumbu that ascetic women have stripped, has not been kind; her hope has come to nothing, her helplessness is in many mouths in the old town of gossip, and that has become a sickness greater than the sickness.","n":"'Greater than the sickness' ranks the pains: love-sickness she can bear, the town's talk she cannot, which is the reason the friend wants the man to hear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","varaivu","noy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 272","lb":"NT272","poet":"முக்கல் ஆசான் நல்வெள்ளையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரையாது நெடுங்காலம் வந்தொழுக ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகள் சொல்லியது தோழி தலைமகளுக்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய் தலைமகன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வரையாது நெடுங்காலம் வந்தொழுக ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியதூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["காக்கைச்","கடல் காக்கை",1,"pul","iraicchi","sea crow"],["பேடைக்கு","பேடை",4,"pul","iraicchi","hen"],["படிவ மகளிர்","படிவ மகளிர்",2,"seythi","scene","women under vow"],["அடும்பின்","அடும்பு",3,"maram","scene","beach creeper"],["அயிரை","அயிரை",5,"pira","scene","small fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","காக்கைச்","confirmed","1/1","a crow provides for the mate it has made pregnant, while the man of the same shore leaves the girl he has taken neither married nor cared for"]],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:273":{"book":"natrinai","num":273,"t":["இஃது எவன்கொல்லோ தோழி மெய் பரந்து","எவ்வம் கூர்ந்த ஏமுறு துயரம்","வெம்மையின் தான் வருத்துறீஇ நம் வயின்","அறியாது அயர்ந்த அன்னைக்கு வெறி என","வேலன் உரைக்கும் என்ப ஆகலின்","வண்ணம் மிகுந்த அண்ணல் யானை","நீர் கொள் நெடுஞ் சுனை அமைந்து வார்ந்து உறைந்து என்","கண் போல் நீலம் தண் கமழ் சிறக்கும்","குன்ற நாடனை உள்ளுதொறும்","நெஞ்சு நடுக்குறூஉம் அவன் பண்பு தரு படரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, knowing the man has come, tells the girl, so that he may hear, that the mother, not knowing about them and troubled by the fearful suffering spreading through her body, will hear the velan call it the god's seizure; and her heart trembles whenever she thinks of the man of the hill country, where a lordly elephant drinks at a deep spring and the cool blue lily like her eyes grows fragrant.","n":"Announcing the veri within his hearing is the friend's lever: if the rite is held the secret will come out, and only marriage forestalls it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","velan","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 273","lb":"NT273","poet":"மதுரை இளம்பாலாசிரியன் சேந்தன் கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகனது வரவு உணர்ந்து தலைமகட்கு உரைப்பாளாய் நின் வேறுபாடு தாய்க்குப் புலனாக அவள் வேலனைக் கூவி வெறி அயரும் என்பது படச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","வேலனைக் கூவி வெறி அயரும்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",6,"maa","scene",""],["சுனை","சுனை",7,"pira","scene","spring"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",8,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",5,"seythi","scene","priest of Murugan"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:274":{"book":"natrinai","num":274,"t":["நெடு வான் மின்னி குறுந் துளி தலைஇ","படு மழை பொழிந்த பகுவாய்க் குன்றத்து","உழை படு மான் பிணை தீண்டலின் இழை மகள்","பொன் செய் காசின் ஒண் பழம் தாஅம்","குமிழ் தலைமயங்கிய குறும் பல் அத்தம்","எம்மொடு வருதியோ பொம்மல் ஓதி எனக்","கூறின்றும் உடையரோ மற்றே வேறுபட்டு","இரும் புலி வழங்கும் சோலை","பெருங் கல் வைப்பின் சுரன் இறந்தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that the man who once asked her to come with him through the hills where rain has poured from the lightning of the tall sky, where a doe brushing past scatters the bright kumizh fruits like gold coins on the many short paths, has now changed and crossed the wasteland of great rocks where the tiger roams in the groves: does he still have such words to say?","n":"The rains that should bring him home only remind her of the invitation he made in the same season; the friend's question sets his old words against his present absence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 274","lb":"NT274","poet":"காவன் முல்லைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி பருவம் மாறுபட்டது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","தோழி பருவம் மாறுபட்டது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மான்","மான்",3,"maa","scene","deer"],["குமிழ்","குமிழ்",5,"maram","simile",""],["புலி","புலி",8,"maa","scene","tiger"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:275":{"book":"natrinai","num":275,"t":["செந்நெல் அரிநர் கூர் வாட் புண்ணுறக்","காணார் முதலொடு போந்தென பூவே","படையொடும் கதிரொடும் மயங்கிய படுக்கைத்","தன்னுறு விழுமம் அறியா மென்மெல","தெறு கதிர் இன் துயில் பசு வாய் திறக்கும்","பேதை நெய்தற் பெரு நீர்ச் சேர்ப்பதற்கு","யான் நினைந்து இரங்கேனாக நோய் இகந்து","அறனிலாளன் புகழ எற்","பெறினும் வல்லேன்மன் தோழி யானே"],"p":"","r":"Overheard by the man, the girl answers her friend's comfort: to the lord of the great-water shore, where a simple neythal flower, cut with its stalk by red-paddy reapers with sharp sickles who did not see it, lies among the sheaves and, not knowing its own ruin, slowly opens its fresh mouth to the burning sun as if waking from sweet sleep, she would not remember or grieve; even if that graceless man praised her she could bear it, she says, but in vain.","n":"The -மன் of வல்லேன்மன் makes the boast hollow: she knows she cannot hold out, and the flower opening its face to the sun that will kill it is her own case.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","ninaivu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 275","lb":"NT275","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகனது வரவுணர்ந்து வற்புறுப்ப வன்புறை எதிர்மொழிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","வன்புறை எதிர்மொழிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["செந்நெல்","செந்நெல்",1,"unavu","scene","red paddy"],["அரிநர்","அரிநர்",1,"seythi","scene","reapers"],["நெய்தற்","நெய்தல்",6,"maram","ullurai","white water-lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","நெய்தற்","confirmed","1/1","the girl, ruined by the man and still turning to him"]],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:276":{"book":"natrinai","num":276,"t":["கோடு துவையா கோள் வாய் நாயொடு","காடு தேர்ந்து அசைஇய வய மான் வேட்டு","வயவர் மகளிர் என்றிஆயின்","குறவர் மகளிரேம் குன்று கெழு கொடிச்சியேம்","சேணோன் இழைத்த நெடுங் காற் கழுதில்","கான மஞ்ஞை கட்சி சேக்கும்","கல் அகத்தது எம் ஊரே செல்லாது","சேந்தனை சென்மதி நீயே பெரு மலை","வாங்கு அமைப் பழுனிய நறவு உண்டு","வேங்கை முன்றில் குரவையும் கண்டே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man who leaves after a daytime meeting that if he takes them for daughters of hunters who hunt the strong beast that rests after searching the forest with fierce dogs, they are only daughters of the hill folk, girls of the hill; their village is among the rocks, where the forest peacock nests on the high-legged platform the watchman built; so he should not go, but stay and go later, drinking the toddy matured in the curved bamboo of the great hill and watching the kuravai dance in the courtyard under the vengai.","n":"Under 'the ways of the world' the colophon names, the friend offers the hill's hospitality, toddy and dance, to a man who comes only by day and leaves: an invitation to be received openly in the village, which is to say, to marry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","virundhu","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 276","lb":"NT276","poet":"தொல் கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பகற்குறி வந்து பெயரும் தலைமகனை உலகியல் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகற்குறி வந்து பெயரும் தலைமகனை உலகியல் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["நாயொடு","நாய்",1,"maa","scene","hunting dogs"],["மான்","மான்",2,"maa","scene","beast"],["குறவர்","குறவர்",4,"seythi","scene","hill folk"],["சேணோன்","சேணோன்",5,"seythi","scene","field watchman"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",6,"pul","scene","peacock"],["அமைப்","அமை",9,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["நறவு","நறவு",9,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",10,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["குரவையும்","குரவை",10,"yaazh","scene","round dance"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:277":{"book":"natrinai","num":277,"t":["கொடியை வாழி தும்பி இந் நோய்","படுகதில் அம்ம யான் நினக்கு உரைத்தென","மெய்யே கருமை அன்றியும் செவ்வன்","அறிவும் கரிதோ அறனிலோய் நினக்கே","மனை உறக் காக்கும் மாண் பெருங் கிடக்கை","நுண் முள் வேலித் தாதொடு பொதுளிய","தாறு படு பீரம் ஊதி வேறுபட","நாற்றம் இன்மையின் பசலை ஊதாய்","சிறு குறும் பறவைக்கு ஓடி விரைவுடன்","நெஞ்சு நெகிழ் செய்ததன் பயனோ அன்பு இலர்","வெம் மலை அருஞ் சுரம் இறந்தோர்க்கு","என் நிலை உரையாய் சென்று அவண் வரவே"],"p":"","r":"The girl, whose lover went far for wealth without marrying her, tells the bee it is cruel and wishes her sickness on it: besides its black body, is its mind black too? It sucks the pollen-rich peeram flowers on the fine-thorned hedge of the well-guarded house but will not touch her pallor, the same yellow, because it has no scent; she has cared for it, and it brings her nothing; let it go to the loveless men who crossed the hard hot hills, tell them her state, and come back.","n":"The yellow peeram and the yellow pallor are set side by side: the bee can tell them apart only by smell, and so cannot be made to carry the one that matters.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","thoothu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 277","lb":"NT277","poet":"தும்பி சேர் கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பட்ட பின்றை வரையாது கிழவோன் நெட்டிடைக் கழிந்து பொருள்வயிற் பிரியஆற்றளாகிய தலைமகள் தும்பிக்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu","தலைமகள் தும்பிக்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தும்பி","தும்பி",1,"pira","scene","bee"],["பீரம்","பீரம்",7,"maram","scene","ridge-gourd flower"],["பறவைக்கு","பறவை",9,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:278":{"book":"natrinai","num":278,"t":["படு காழ் நாறிய பராஅரைப் புன்னை","அடு மரல் மொக்குளின் அரும்பு வாய் அவிழ","பொன்னின் அன்ன தாது படு பல் மலர்","சூடுநர் தொடுத்த மிச்சில் கோடுதொறும்","நெய் கனி பசுங் காய் தூங்கும் துறைவனை","இனி அறிந்திசினே கொண்கன் ஆகுதல்","கழிச் சேறு ஆடிய கணைக் கால் அத்திரி","குளம்பினும் சேயிறா ஒடுங்கின","கோதையும் எல்லாம் ஊதை வெண் மணலே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl that she now knows the lord of the shore, where the broad-trunked punnai's buds open like bubbles, its gold-like pollen-laden flowers are what garland-makers left over, and green ghee-rich fruit hangs from every branch, is to be her husband: the red prawns have shrunk away even under the hooves of the thick-legged mules that splashed in the backwater mud, and the garlands are all white sand in the north wind.","n":"The proof is in the mud: tracks of many mules on the shore mean a large party has come, the kind that comes to ask for a bride.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 278","lb":"NT278","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகட்கு வரைவு மலிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி தலைமகட்கு வரைவு மலிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene",""],["மரல்","மரல்",2,"maram","simile","bowstring hemp"],["அத்திரி","அத்திரி",7,"maa","scene","mule"],["சேயிறா","சேயிறா",8,"pira","scene","red prawn"],["கோதையும்","கோதை",9,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:279":{"book":"natrinai","num":279,"t":["வேம்பின் ஒண் பழம் முணைஇ இருப்பைத்","தேம் பால் செற்ற தீம் பழம் நசைஇ","வைகு பனி உழந்த வாவல் சினைதொறும்","நெய் தோய் திரியின் தண் சிதர் உறைப்ப","நாட் சுரம் உழந்த வாள் கேழ் ஏற்றையொடு","பொருத யானைப் புட் தாள் ஏய்ப்ப","பசிப் பிடி உதைத்த ஓமைச் செவ் வரை","வெயில் காய் அமையத்து இமைக்கும் அத்தத்து","அதர் உழந்து அசையினகொல்லோ ததர்வாய்ச்","சிலம்பு கழீஇய செல்வம்","பிறருழைக் கழிந்த என் ஆயிழை அடியே"],"p":"","r":"The mother whose daughter has gone off with her lover asks whether the feet of her jewelled girl, whose anklets were taken off and who has gone to another's house, are weary struggling on the path through a wasteland that glitters in the sun's heat, where bats, tired of neem fruit and craving the sweet milky iluppai, drip cold drops from every branch like ghee from a wick, and a hungry she-elephant has kicked the red bark of the omai tree.","n":"The removal of the anklets (சிலம்பு கழீஇய) was the rite before marriage; the mother names it as the one wealth of a girl who has gone to marry elsewhere, so the poem grieves both the road and the wedding she did not see.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","magatpaal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 279","lb":"NT279","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"மகட் போக்கிய தாய் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,null,"மகட் போக்கிய தாய் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",1,"maram","scene","neem"],["இருப்பைத்","இருப்பை",1,"maram","scene","iluppai"],["வாவல்","வாவல்",3,"pul","simile","bat"],["ஏற்றையொடு","ஏற்றை",5,"maa","scene","male tiger"],["யானைப்","யானை",6,"maa","simile",""],["பிடி","பிடி",7,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["ஓமைச்","ஓமை",7,"maram","scene",""],["சிலம்பு","சிலம்பு",10,"pira","ornament","anklet"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:280":{"book":"natrinai","num":280,"t":["கொக்கினுக்கு ஒழிந்த தீம் பழம் கொக்கின்","கூம்பு நிலை அன்ன முகைய ஆம்பல்","தூங்கு நீர்க் குட்டத்து துடுமென வீழும்","தண் துறை ஊரன் தண்டாப் பரத்தமை","புலவாய் என்றி தோழி புலவேன்","பழன யாமைப் பாசடைப் புறத்து","கழனி காவலர் சுரி நந்து உடைக்கும்","தொன்று முதிர் வேளிர் குன்றூர் அன்ன என்","நல் மனை நனி விருந்து அயரும்","கைதூவு இன்மையின் எய்தாமாறே"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the friend who asks her not to sulk at the man of the cool-ford town, where the sweet fruit the stork missed falls with a plop into the deep pool among water-lily buds shaped like a stork's hunched stance, for his endless philandering, that she will not sulk: her good house, like Kunrur of the ancient Velir where field guards break curly snails on the green-mossed back of the field turtle, is so busy feasting guests that she has no leisure for it.","n":"The refusal is a sulk in itself: 'I have no time to sulk' says that she has the house, the guests and the standing, and he has nothing she needs. The fruit missed by the stork that drops among stork-shaped buds hints at favours falling to the women who look like the wife, but it does not give two clear pairs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 280","lb":"NT280","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"வாயில் வேண்டிச் சென்ற தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் மறுத்து மொழிந்தது தலைமகனை ஏற்றுக்கொண்டு வழிபட்டாளைப் புகழ்ந்து புக்க தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","வாயில் வேண்டிச் சென்ற தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் மறுத்து மொழிந்தது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தலைமகனை ஏற்றுக்கொண்டு வழிபட்டாளைப் புகழ்ந்து புக்க தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொக்கினுக்கு","கொக்கு",1,"pul","scene","stork"],["பழம்","பழம்",1,"unavu","scene",""],["கொக்கின்","கொக்கு",1,"pul","simile","stork"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",2,"maram","simile","water-lily"],["யாமைப்","யாமை",6,"pira","scene","turtle"],["காவலர்","காவலர்",7,"seythi","scene","field guards"],["நந்து","நந்து",7,"pira","scene","snail"]],"en":[["king","வேளிர்",8],["town","குன்றூர்",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:14"},"nt:281":{"book":"natrinai","num":281,"t":["மாசு இல் மரத்த பலி உண் காக்கை","வளி பொரு நெடுஞ் சினை தளியொடு தூங்கி","வெல் போர்ச் சோழர் கழாஅர்க் கொள்ளும்","நல் வகை மிகு பலிக் கொடையோடு உகுக்கும்","அடங்காச் சொன்றி அம் பல் யாணர்","விடக்குடைப் பெருஞ் சோறு உள்ளுவன இருப்ப","மழை அமைந்து உற்ற மால் இருள் நடு நாள்","தாம் நம் உழையராகவும் நாம் நம்","பனிக் கடுமையின் நனி பெரிது அழுங்கி","துஞ்சாம் ஆகலும் அறிவோர்","அன்பிலர் தோழி நம் காதலோரே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that their lover has no love: on this rain-soaked midnight the crows that live on offerings hang dripping on the wind-beaten branch, thinking of the great meat-and-rice offerings the Cholas take at Kazhar, while he, who knows that even with him beside us we lie sleepless and wretched in the cold, has left us to it.","n":"The crows endure the wet night on the memory of a feast; the woman endures it on the memory of the man. Line 8 (தாம் நம் உழையராகவும்) can also be read of the crows (though they are near us) rather than of the lover, which changes who is beside her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","iravu","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 281","lb":"NT281","poet":"கழார்க் கீரன் எயிற்றியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"வன்பொறை எதிர் அழிந்த தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது ஆற்றாள் எனக் கவன்ற தோழி தலைமகட்கு உரைத்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","வன்பொறை எதிர் அழிந்த தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","ஆற்றாள் எனக் கவன்ற தோழி தலைமகட்கு உரைத்ததூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மரத்த","மரம்",1,"maram","scene",""],["காக்கை","காக்கை",1,"pul","iraicchi","crow"],["சொன்றி","சொன்றி",5,"unavu","scene","boiled rice"],["விடக்குடைப்","விடக்கு",6,"unavu","scene","meat"],["மழை","மழை",7,"pira","scene","rain"]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",3],["town","கழாஅர்",3]],"dv":[["iraicchi","காக்கை","not_confirmed","0/1","she too lies awake through the cold night living on the thought of what she longs for, and he who knows it is not with her"]],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:282":{"book":"natrinai","num":282,"t":["தோடு அமை செறிப்பின் இலங்கு வளை ஞெகிழ","கோடு ஏந்து அல்குல் அவ் வரி வாட","நல் நுதல் சாய படர் மலி அரு நோய்","காதலன் தந்தமை அறியாது உணர்த்த","அணங்குறு கழங்கின் முது வாய் வேலன்","கிளவியின் தணியின் நன்றுமன் சாரல்","அகில் சுடு கானவன் உவல் சுடு கமழ் புகை","ஆடு மழை மங்குலின் மறைக்கும்","நாடு கெழு வெற்பனொடு அமைந்த நம் தொடர்பே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend says the girl's bangles slip, the lines of her waist fade and her forehead pales with a sickness her lover gave, which the old diviner with his possession-beans will explain without knowing; it would be fine indeed if his words could ease their bond with the man of the hill where the smoke of agil and brush a hunter burns hides the slope like drifting cloud.","n":"The closing image is apt to the friend's purpose: a fragrant smoke that hides the hillside as cloud does, as their love has been hidden, though its scent now spreads; it has one match, not the two that ullurai needs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","velan","varaivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 282","lb":"NT282","poet":"நல்லூர்ச் சிறு மேதாவியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத் தோழி செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கழங்கின்","கழங்கு",5,"pira","scene","molucca beans"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",5,"seythi","scene","diviner"],["அகில்","அகில்",7,"maram","scene",""],["கானவன்","கானவன்",7,"seythi","scene","hunter"],["உவல்","உவல்",7,"maram","scene","dry brush"],["புகை","புகை",7,"pira","scene",""],["மழை","மழை",8,"pira","scene","cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:283":{"book":"natrinai","num":283,"t":["ஒள் நுதல் மகளிர் ஓங்கு கழிக் குற்ற","கண் நேர் ஒப்பின கமழ் நறு நெய்தல்","அகல் வரிச் சிறு மனை அணியும் துறைவ","வல்லோர் ஆய்ந்த தொல் கவின் தொலைய","இன்னை ஆகுதல் தகுமோ ஓங்கு திரை","முந்நீர் மீமிசைப் பலர் தொழத் தோன்றி","ஏமுற விளங்கிய சுடரினும்","வாய்மை சான்ற நின் சொல் நயந்தோர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the shore, where bright-browed girls pick fragrant neythal flowers like eyes from the backwater to deck their little striped sand-houses, whether it is right that the girl's long-admired beauty should waste away so, for those who trusted his word, truer than the sun that rises over the sea and is worshipped by many.","n":"The praise is the reproach: by setting his word above the sun that all trust, the friend makes any delay in marrying a breach of something public.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","vaaimai","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 283","lb":"NT283","poet":"மதுரை மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறி வந்த தலைமகனைத் தோழி வரைவு கடாயது. கடிநகர் புக்க தோழி பிற்றை ஞான்று வேறுபடாது ஆற்றினாய் என்று சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகற்குறி வந்த தலைமகனைத் தோழி வரைவு கடாயது"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","கடிநகர் புக்க தோழி பிற்றை ஞான்று வேறுபடாது ஆற்றினாய் என்று சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கழிக்","கழி",1,"pira","scene","backwater"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","simile",""],["மனை","சிறு மனை",3,"pira","scene","sand house"],["திரை","திரை",5,"pira","scene",""],["சுடரினும்","சுடர்",7,"deyvam","simile","sun"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:284":{"book":"natrinai","num":284,"t":["புறம் தாழ்பு இருண்ட கூந்தல் போதின்","நிறம் பெறும் ஈர் இதழ்ப் பொலிந்த உண்கண்","உள்ளம் பிணிக்கொண்டோள்வயின் நெஞ்சம்","செல்லல் தீர்கம் செல்வாம் என்னும்","செய்வினை முடியாது எவ்வம் செய்தல்","எய்யாமையோடு இளிவு தலைத்தரும் என","உறுதி தூக்காத் தூங்கி அறிவே","சிறிது நனி விரையல் என்னும் ஆயிடை","ஒளிறு ஏந்து மருப்பின் களிறு மாறு பற்றிய","தேய்புரிப் பழங் கயிறு போல","வீவதுகொல் என் வருந்திய உடம்பே"],"p":"","r":"Away on work not yet done, the man says his heart urges him to go to the woman of the dark hanging hair and flower-coloured kohl eyes and end its pain, while his reason, weighing the outcome, says that leaving a task unfinished brings ignorance and disgrace and bids him not hurry at all; caught between them, he asks whether his aching body will perish like an old frayed rope that two tuskers with gleaming tusks have seized at either end.","n":"The heart and the reason are both given speech (என்னும், twice), so the poem is a debate in which the body is the only loser; the poet's name is taken from the rope.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","arivu","pirivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 284","lb":"NT284","poet":"தேய்புரிப் பழங்கயிற்றினார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் முடியாநின்ற தலைமகன் ஆற்றானாகிச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பொருள் முடியாநின்ற தலைமகன் ஆற்றானாகிச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["போதின்","போது",1,"maram","simile","bud"],["களிறு","களிறு",9,"maa","simile","tusker"],["கயிறு","பழங் கயிறு",10,"pira","simile","rope"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:285":{"book":"natrinai","num":285,"t":["அரவு இரை தேரும் ஆர் இருள் நடு நாள்","இரவின் வருதல் அன்றியும் உரவுக் கணை","வன் கைக் கானவன் வெஞ் சிலை வணக்கி","உளமிசைத் தவிர்த்த முளவுமான் ஏற்றையொடு","மனைவாய் ஞமலி ஒருங்கு புடை ஆட","வேட்டு வலம் படுத்த உவகையன் காட்ட","நடு காற் குரம்பைத் தன் குடிவயிற் பெயரும்","குன்ற நாடன் கேண்மை நமக்கே","நன்றால் வாழி தோழி என்றும்","அயலோர் அம்பலின் அகலான்","பகலின் வரூஉம் எறி புனத்தானே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend tells the girl that the friendship of the hill lord, where a hunter who has shot a porcupine with his bow goes home to his stilted hut delighted, his house dog romping beside the kill, is good for them indeed: not only does he come in the dead of night when the snake hunts its prey, but, never kept away by the neighbours' talk, he now comes by day to the cleared field as well.","n":"The praise is ironic: coming by night and by day has made the talk loud, and the friend's real word is that the affair can no longer stay hidden. The hunter carrying his catch home is the act the man has not yet done.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","iravu","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 285","lb":"NT285","poet":"மதுரைக் கொல்லன் வெண்ணாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய் தலைமகன் கேட்ப அம்பலும் அலரும் ஆயிற்று என்று சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","அம்பலும் அலரும் ஆயிற்று என்று சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["அரவு","அரவு",1,"pira","scene","snake"],["கானவன்","கானவன்",3,"seythi","iraicchi","hunter"],["முளவுமான்","முளவுமான்",4,"maa","scene","porcupine"],["ஞமலி","ஞமலி",5,"maa","scene","dog"],["குரம்பை","குரம்பை",7,"pira","scene","hut"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கானவன்","confirmed","1/2","the man should likewise take what he has won home openly, instead of coming to her by night and by day"]],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:286":{"book":"natrinai","num":286,"t":["ஊசல் ஒண் குழை உடை வாய்த்தன்ன","அத்தக் குமிழின் ஆய் இதழ் அலரி","கல் அறை வரிக்கும் புல்லென் குன்றம்","சென்றோர்மன்ற செலீஇயர் என் உயிர் என","புனை இழை நெகிழ விம்மி நொந்து நொந்து","இனைதல் ஆன்றிசின் ஆயிழை நினையின்","நட்டோர் ஆக்கம் வேண்டியும் ஒட்டிய","நின் தோள் அணி பெற வரற்கும்","அன்றோ தோழி அவர் சென்ற திறமே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the grieving girl, who sobs again and again with her jewels slipping, 'he has surely gone over the bare hills where the kumizh flower, like a bright swinging earring in a slit lobe, streaks the rock floor; let my life go', to stop lamenting: if she thinks about it, he went to win the advancement of his friends and to come back so that her shoulders regain their beauty.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","oppuravu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 286","lb":"NT286","poet":"துறைக்குறுமாவிற் பாலங் கொற்றனார","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["குழை","குழை",1,"pira","simile","earring"],["குமிழின்","குமிழ்",2,"maram","simile",""],["அறை","கல் அறை",3,"pira","scene","rock floor"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:287":{"book":"natrinai","num":287,"t":["விசும்பு உறழ் புரிசை வெம்ப முற்றி","பைங் கண் யானை வேந்து புறத்து இறுத்த","நல் எயிலுடையோர் உடையம் என்னும்","பெருந் தகை மறவன் போல கொடுங் கழிப்","பாசடை நெய்தற் பனி நீர்ச் சேர்ப்பன்","நாம முதலை நடுங்கு பகை அஞ்சான்","காமம் பெருமையின் வந்த ஞான்றை","அருகாது ஆகி அவன்கண் நெஞ்சம்","நள்ளென் கங்குல் புள் ஒலி கேட்டொறும்","தேர் மணித் தெள் இசைகொல் என","ஊர் மடி கங்குலும் துயில் மறந்ததுவே"],"p":"","r":"The girl, unable to bear the tightened watch, says that since the day the lord of the cold shore of neythal-leaved backwaters came, driven by great love and unafraid of the dread crocodile, like a noble warrior who says 'we hold the good fort' while a king with green-eyed elephants besieges its sky-high wall, her heart has not left him, and whenever in the dead of night she hears a bird's cry she takes it for the clear chime of his chariot bell and forgets sleep while the whole town sleeps.","n":"The siege simile is grammatically the man's (fearless before the crocodile), but the colophon's watch (காப்பு மிகுதி) lets it fall also on her: a heart besieged by her household that still says 'we hold it'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu","iravu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 287","lb":"NT287","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"காப்பு மிகுதிக்கண் ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகள் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,"kalavu","காப்பு மிகுதிக்கண் ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",2,"maa","simile","elephant"],["நெய்தற்","நெய்தல்",5,"maram","scene",""],["முதலை","முதலை",6,"maa","scene","crocodile"],["புள்","புள்",9,"pul","scene","bird"],["தேர்","தேர்",10,"pira","scene","chariot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:288":{"book":"natrinai","num":288,"t":["அருவி ஆர்க்கும் அணங்குடை நெடுங் கோட்டு","ஞாங்கர் இள வெயில் உணீஇய ஓங்கு சினைப்","பீலி மஞ்ஞை பெடையோடு ஆலும்","குன்ற நாடன் பிரிவின் சென்று","நல் நுதல் பரந்த பசலை கண்டு அன்னை","செம் முது பெண்டிரொடு நெல் முன் நிறீஇ","கட்டின் கேட்கும்ஆயின் வெற்பில்","ஏனற் செந் தினைப் பால் ஆர் கொழுங் குரற்","சிறு கிளி கடிகம் சென்றும் இந்","நெடு வேள் அணங்கிற்று என்னும்கொல் அதுவே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend tells the girl that, seeing the pallor spread on her forehead since the lord of the hill went away, the hill where a plumed peacock dances with its hen on a high branch of the waterfall-loud, god-haunted peak to take the young sun, their mother has set paddy before the red old women to consult the diviner; and if she asks, will the diviner not say that the girl went to the hill to scare small parrots from the red millet and that great Vel has seized her?","n":"The diviner's verdict names the true place (the millet field) and the wrong cause (the god), so the friend's fear is also a hint to the listener that the truth is half out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veri","velan","pasalai","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 288","lb":"NT288","poet":"குளம்பனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகட்கு உரைப்பாளாய் வெறி அறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","வெறி அறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",3,"pul","iraicchi","peacock"],["பெடையோடு","பெடை",3,"pul","iraicchi","peahen"],["நெல்","நெல்",6,"unavu","scene",""],["ஏனற்","ஏனல்",8,"maram","scene","millet field"],["தினைப்","செந் தினை",8,"unavu","scene","millet"],["கிளி","கிளி",9,"pul","scene","parrot"],["வேள்","நெடு வேள்",10,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மஞ்ஞை","confirmed","1/1","the creatures of his own hill keep company with their mates while he has gone away and left her to pale"]],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:289":{"book":"natrinai","num":289,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி காதலர்","நிலம் புடைபெயர்வதாயினும் கூறிய","சொல் புடைபெயர்தலோ இலரே வானம்","நளி கடல் முகந்து செறிதக இருளி","கனை பெயல் பொழிந்து கடுங் குரல் பயிற்றி","கார் செய்து என் உழையதுவே ஆயிடை","கொல்லைக் கோவலர் எல்லி மாட்டிய","பெரு மர ஒடியல் போல","அருள் இலேன் அம்ம அளியேன் யானே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that her lover would not shift from a word he had spoken even if the earth shifted from its place, yet the sky has scooped up the sea, darkened thickly, poured heavy rain and sounded its harsh voice again and again, and has made the rainy season here beside her; meanwhile, like the great log the herdsmen of the scrub set burning at night, she is unpitied and wretched.","n":"She does not doubt his word; the season he named has simply arrived without him, so the burning log, lit and left by others to burn down alone, carries the whole of her complaint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","vaaimai","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 289","lb":"NT289","poet":"மருங்கூர்ப் பட்டினத்துச் சேந்தன் குமரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பிரிவிடைப் பருவம் கண்டு சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடைப் பருவம் கண்டு சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கடல்","கடல்",4,"pira","scene","sea"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",7,"seythi","simile","herdsmen"],["ஒடியல்","பெரு மர ஒடியல்",8,"maram","simile","log"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:290":{"book":"natrinai","num":290,"t":["வயல் வெள் ஆம்பல் சூடு தரு புதுப் பூக்","கன்றுடைப் புனிற்றா தின்ற மிச்சில்","ஓய்நடை முது பகடு ஆரும் ஊரன்","தொடர்பு நீ வெஃகினை ஆயின் என் சொல்","கொள்ளல்மாதோ முள் எயிற்றோயே","நீயே பெரு நலத்தையே அவனே","நெடு நீர்ப் பொய்கை நடு நாள் எய்தி","தண் கமழ் புது மலர் ஊதும்","வண்டு என மொழிப மகன் என்னாரே"],"p":"","r":"Aiming her words at the wife through the dancing woman, the courtesan says to her: if you want the friendship of the lord of the town where an old slow-footed bull grazes on the leftovers of the fresh white field lilies that a cow with a new calf has eaten, do not take my word for it, sharp-toothed one: you are a great beauty, but he, they say, is a bee that comes at midnight to the deep pond and sucks the cool fragrant new flowers; they do not call him a man.","n":"The courtesan's mockery cuts three ways: at the dancing woman as the new favourite, at the wife as the cow whose leftovers he grazes, and at the man as a bee, not a man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 290","lb":"NT290","poet":"மதுரை மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தை விறலிமேல் வைத்துத் தலைமகளை நெருங்கிச் சொல்லியது பரத்தையிற்பிரிய வாயிலாய்ப் புக்க பாணன் கேட்ப தோழி சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","virali","thalaivi","karpu","பரத்தை விறலிமேல் வைத்துத் தலைமகளை நெருங்கிச் சொல்லியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi","paanan","karpu","வாயிலாய்ப் புக்க பாணன் கேட்ப தோழி சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["ஆம்பல்","வெள் ஆம்பல்",1,"maram","ullurai","white water lily"],["புனிற்றா","புனிற்று ஆ",2,"maa","ullurai","cow with new calf"],["பகடு","முது பகடு",3,"maa","ullurai","bull"],["பொய்கை","பொய்கை",7,"pira","scene",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",9,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","புனிற்றா பகடு","confirmed","1/1","the husband, who after his wife has borne a son grazes on what other flowers remain"]],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:291":{"book":"natrinai","num":291,"t":["நீர் பெயர்ந்து மாறிய செறி சேற்று அள்ளல்","நெய்த் தலைக் கொழு மீன் அருந்த இனக் குருகு","குப்பை வெண் மணல் ஏறி அரைசர்","ஒண் படைத் தொகுதியின் இலங்கித் தோன்றும்","தண் பெரும் பௌவ நீர்த் துறைவற்கு நீயும்","கண்டாங்கு உரையாய் கொண்மோ பாண","மா இரு முள்ளூர் மன்னன் மா ஊர்ந்து","எல்லித் தரீஇய இன நிரைப்","பல் ஆன் கிழவரின் அழிந்த இவள் நலனே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, knowing the wife's mind, tells the bard who came as the man's go-between to tell the lord of the cool sea shore exactly what he has seen: where the water has drawn back from the thick mire, a flock of herons that came to eat fat oily-headed fish climbs the heaped white sand and shines like the bright array of kings' armies; and her beauty is ruined like the owners of the many cows the king of great dark Mullur drove off at nightfall, riding his horse.","n":"The herons that feast where the water has left mirror a man who has taken his pleasure and gone; the closing simile takes the side of the herd-owners, not of the raiding king the puram poets praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","thoothu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 291","lb":"NT291","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வாயிலாகப் புக்க பாணற்குத் தோழி தலைமகளது குறிப்பு அறிந்து நெருங்கிச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","paanan",null,"karpu","வாயிலாகப் புக்க பாணற்குத் தோழி தலைமகளது குறிப்பு அறிந்து நெருங்கிச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["மீன்","கொழு மீன்",2,"pira","ullurai","fish"],["குருகு","இனக் குருகு",2,"pul","ullurai","heron"],["மணல்","வெண் மணல்",3,"pira","scene",""],["மா","மா",7,"maa","simile","horse"],["ஆன்","ஆன்",9,"maa","simile","cows"]],"en":[["town","முள்ளூர்",7],["king","மன்னன்",7]],"dv":[["ullurai","குருகு மீன்","confirmed","1/2","the man who took his pleasure of her and went"]],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:292":{"book":"natrinai","num":292,"t":["நெடுந் தண் ஆரத்து அலங்கு சினை வலந்த","பசுங் கேழ் இலைய நறுங் கொடித் தமாலம்","தீம் தேன் கொள்பவர் வாங்குபு பரியும்","யாணர் வைப்பின் கானம் என்னாய்","களிறு பொரக் கரைந்த கயவாய்க் குண்டு கரை","ஒளிறு வான் பளிங்கொடு செம் பொன் மின்னும்","கருங் கற் கான்யாற்று அருஞ் சுழி வழங்கும்","கராஅம் பேணாய் இரவரின்","வாழேன் ஐய மை கூர் பனியே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man that he does not think of the forest where honey-gatherers pull down and break the fragrant green-leaved tamaala creeper wound round the swaying branch of tall cool sandal, nor heed the crocodile that moves in the hard whirlpools of the black-rock forest stream whose deep bank, broken where elephants fought, glitters with crystal and red gold; if he comes by night in this thick dark dew, she will not live.","n":"Traditionally the creeper torn from the sandal by honey-takers is the girl harmed by the man's pleasure; the poem gives no second correspondence, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","iravu","acham","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 292","lb":"NT292","poet":"நல்வேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["ஆரத்து","ஆரம்",1,"maram","scene","sandal"],["தமாலம்","தமாலம்",2,"maram","scene",""],["தேன்","தேன்",3,"unavu","scene","honey"],["களிறு","களிறு",5,"maa","scene","tusker"],["பளிங்கொடு","பளிங்கு",6,"pira","scene","crystal"],["கராஅம்","கராம்",8,"maa","scene","crocodile"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:293":{"book":"natrinai","num":293,"t":["மணிக் குரல் நொச்சித் தெரியல் சூடி","பலிக் கள் ஆர் கைப் பார் முது குயவன்","இடு பலி நுவலும் அகன்தலை மன்றத்து","விழவுத் தலைக்கொண்ட பழ விறல் மூதூர்ப்","பூங் கண் ஆயம் காண்தொறும் எம்போல்","பெரு விதுப்புறுகமாதோ எம் இற்","பொம்மல் ஓதியைத் தன் மொழிக் கொளீஇ","கொண்டு உடன் போக வலித்த","வன்கண் காளையை ஈன்ற தாயே"],"p":"","r":"The mother, bewildered in her house, wishes that the mother who bore the cruel young man, who won her thick-haired daughter with his words and resolved to take her away, may, whenever she sees the bright-eyed band of girls in the wide common of the ancient town of old renown, where the festival has begun and the old potter in his nochchi garland, toddy of the offering in hand, cries out the offerings, tremble with as great an anguish as hers.","n":"The curse falls not on the young man but on his mother, the one person whose loss would match her own; the festival crowd of girls is where the absent daughter would have been.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ilamai","kaiyarunilai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 293","lb":"NT293","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தாய் மனை மருண்டு சொல்லியது அவரிடத்தாரைக் கண்டு சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu","தாய் மனை மருண்டு சொல்லியது"],["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu","அவரிடத்தாரைக் கண்டு சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["vekuli","குடிகோள்"]],"k":[["நொச்சித்","நொச்சி",1,"maram","ornament",""],["கள்","பலிக் கள்",2,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["குயவன்","குயவன்",2,"seythi","scene","potter"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:294":{"book":"natrinai","num":294,"t":["தீயும் வளியும் விசும்பு பயந்தாங்கு","நோயும் இன்பமும் ஆகின்றுமாதோ","மாயம் அன்று தோழி வேய் பயின்று","எருவை நீடிய பெரு வரைஅகம்தொறும்","தொன்று உறை துப்பொடு முரண் மிகச் சினைஇக்","கொன்ற யானைக் கோடு கண்டன்ன","செம் புடைக் கொழு முகை அவிழ்ந்த காந்தள்","சிலம்புடன் கமழும் சாரல்","இலங்கு மலை நாடன் மலர்ந்த மார்பே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, entering the wedding house and seeing the bride's beauty, says it is no illusion: just as the sky bore both fire and wind, so the open chest of the lord of the shining hill, whose slopes are fragrant with kaanthal whose red-sided buds have opened like the tusk of an elephant killed by an old enemy in the bamboo-thick rock clefts, has become both the sickness and the joy.","n":"The cosmological simile (the sky as the source of both fire and wind) makes the cure and the sickness one origin, now that the marriage has turned the one into the other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","inbam","varaivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 294","lb":"NT294","poet":"புதுக்கயத்து வண்ணக்கன் கம்பூர் கிழான்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"மணமனையுள் புக்க தோழி தலைமகளது கவின் கண்டு சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","மணமனையுள் புக்க தோழி தலைமகளது கவின் கண்டு சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வேய்","வேய்",3,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["எருவை","எருவை",4,"maram","scene","reed"],["யானைக்","யானை",6,"maa","simile","elephant"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",7,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:295":{"book":"natrinai","num":295,"t":["முரிந்த சிலம்பின் நெரிந்த வள்ளியின்","புறன் அழிந்து ஒலிவரும் தாழ் இருங் கூந்தல்","ஆயமும் அழுங்கின்று யாயும் அஃது அறிந்தனள்","அருங் கடி அயர்ந்தனள் காப்பே எந்தை","வேறு பல் நாட்டுக் கால் தர வந்த","பல வினை நாவாய் தோன்றும் பெருந் துறை","கலி மடைக் கள்ளின் சாடி அன்ன எம்","இள நலம் இற்கடை ஒழியச்","சேறும் வாழியோ முதிர்கம் யாமே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, with the man able to hear, says that the girl's long dark hair, spoiled at the back like valli creeper crushed on a broken slope, has set her playmates grieving and her mother knowing, and the mother has laid a hard watch on her; so, at our father's great port where ships of many tasks come wind-driven from many lands, our young beauty, like the toddy jar at a noisy feast, will be left behind the house door while we go on and grow old.","n":"The toddy jar is set out for a crowd and emptied by others; the friend's image warns that beauty kept indoors under guard is spent with no one to enjoy it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","ilamai","nalam","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 295","lb":"NT295","poet":"ஒளவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது சிறைப்புறமும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது"],["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமும் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சிலம்பின்","சிலம்பு",1,"pira","simile","hill slope"],["வள்ளியின்","வள்ளி",1,"maram","simile","valli creeper"],["நாவாய்","நாவாய்",6,"pira","scene","ship"],["கள்ளின்","கள்",7,"unavu","simile","toddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:296":{"book":"natrinai","num":296,"t":["என் ஆவதுகொல் தோழி மன்னர்","வினை வல் யானைப் புகர் முகத்து அணிந்த","பொன் செய் ஓடைப் புனை நலம் கடுப்ப","புழற் காய்க் கொன்றைக் கோடு அணி கொடி இணர்","ஏ கல் மீமிசை மேதக மலரும்","பிரிந்தோர் இரங்கும் அரும் பெறல் காலையும்","வினையே நினைந்த உள்ளமொடு துனைஇச்","செல்ப என்ப காதலர்","ஒழிதும் என்ப நாம் வருந்து படர் உழந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Told by her friend that he is leaving, the woman asks what will become of her: even in the rare season when the parted grieve, when the kondrai's tube-podded hanging clusters bloom grandly on the lone rock like the gold forehead-plate on the spotted face of a king's war elephant, they say her lover will hurry off with a heart set only on his work, and they say she will stay behind, suffering in grief.","n":"The repeated என்ப (they say) twice keeps the news at second hand, so her question is also a refusal to take it as settled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","vinai","paruvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 296","lb":"NT296","poet":"குதிரைத் தறியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழியால் பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட தலைமகள் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","தோழியால் பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["யானைப்","யானை",2,"maa","simile","elephant"],["ஓடைப்","ஓடை",3,"pira","simile","forehead plate"],["கொன்றைக்","கொன்றை",4,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:297":{"book":"natrinai","num":297,"t":["பொன் செய் வள்ளத்துப் பால் கிழக்கு இருப்ப","நின் ஒளி எறியச் சேவடி ஒதுங்காய்","பல் மாண் சேக்கைப் பகை கொள நினைஇ","மகிழா நோக்கம் மகிழ்ந்தனை போன்றனை","எவன்கொல் என்று நினைக்கலும் நினைத்திலை","நின்னுள் தோன்றும் குறிப்பு நனி பெரிதே","சிதர் நனை முணைஇய சிதர் கால் வாரணம்","முதிர் கறி யாப்பின் துஞ்சும் நாடன்","மெல்ல வந்து நல் அகம் பெற்றமை","மையல் உறுகுவள் அன்னை","ஐயம் இன்றிக் கடுங் கவவினளே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend tells the girl that she leaves her milk in its gold bowl, does not stir her red feet, quarrels with her fine bed, wears a joyless look as if joyful, and never asks herself why: the sign showing in her is very plain, and the mother, seeing that the lord of the land where the spotted-legged jungle fowl, sated on fallen buds, sleeps in the ripe pepper vines, came softly and won her heart, will be troubled, and has already held her close without a doubt.","n":"The friend's list of symptoms is aimed at the hidden listener as much as at the girl; the last lines are hard and can also be read as the mother's suspicion rather than her embrace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kalaviyal","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 297","lb":"NT297","poet":"மதுரை அளக்கர் ஞாழார் மகனார் மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகட்கு உரைப்பாளாய் தலைமகன் கேட்பச் சொல்லி யது தோழி தலைமகளை அறத்தொடுநிலை வலிப்பித்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகட்கு உரைப்பாளாய் தலைமகன் கேட்பச்"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தோழி தலைமகளை அறத்தொடுநிலை வலிப்பித்ததூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பால்","பால்",1,"unavu","scene","milk"],["நனை","நனை",7,"maram","scene","buds"],["வாரணம்","வாரணம்",7,"pul","scene","jungle fowl"],["கறி","கறி",8,"maram","scene","pepper"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:298":{"book":"natrinai","num":298,"t":["வம்ப மாக்கள் வரு திறம் நோக்கி","செங் கணை தொடுத்த செயிர் நோக்கு ஆடவர்","மடி வாய்த் தண்ணுமைத் தழங்கு குரல் கேட்ட","எருவைச் சேவல் கிளைவயிற் பெயரும்","அருஞ் சுரக் கவலை அஞ்சுவரு நனந்தலைப்","பெரும் பல் குன்றம் உள்ளியும் மற்று இவள்","கரும்புடைப் பணைத் தோள் நோக்கியும் ஒரு திறம்","பற்றாய் வாழி எம் நெஞ்சே நல் தார்ப்","பொற்றேர்ச் செழியன் கூடல் ஆங்கண்","ஒருமை செப்பிய அருமை வான் முகை","இரும் போது கமழும் கூந்தல்","பெரு மலை தழீஇயும் நோக்கு இயையுமோமற்றே"],"p":"","r":"Having been urged by the friend to go for wealth and then come back to the woman, the man tells his heart that it holds to neither side: it thinks of the many great hills of the fearful forked wasteland, where fierce-eyed men with red arrows fitted watch for travellers and the male vulture, hearing the slack-mouthed drum, goes back to its flock, and it looks at her bamboo-smooth shoulders painted with sugarcane; can it both embrace the great mountains and keep its gaze on her hair, fragrant with dark flowers like the rare buds of Koodal of Chezhiyan of the golden chariot?","n":"The man stays: the colophon calls it செலவு அழுங்கியது. The closing lines are hard; the reading of Koodal's flower as the measure of her hair is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 298","lb":"NT298","poet":"விற்றூற்று வண்ணக்கன் தத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழியால் பொருள் வலிப்பித்துத் தலைமகளை எய்தி ஆற்றாதாய நெஞ்சினை நெருங் கிச் சொல்லி தலைமகன் செலவு அழுங்கியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","ஆற்றாதாய நெஞ்சினை"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",2,"seythi","scene","robbers"],["தண்ணுமைத்","தண்ணுமை",3,"parai","scene",""],["எருவைச்","எருவை",4,"pul","scene","vulture"],["கரும்புடைப்","கரும்பு",7,"maram","ornament","sugarcane pattern"],["போது","இரும் போது",11,"maram","ornament","flowers"]],"en":[["king","செழியன்",9],["town","கூடல்",9]],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:299":{"book":"natrinai","num":299,"t":["உரு கெழு யானை உடை கோடு அன்ன","ததர் பிணி அவிழ்ந்த தாழை வான் பூ","தயங்கு இருங் கோடை தூக்கலின் நுண் தாது","வயங்கு இழை மகளிர் வண்டல் தாஅம்","காமர் சிறுகுடி புலம்பினும் அவர்காண்","நாம் இலம் ஆகுதல் அறிதும் மன்னோ","வில் எறி பஞ்சி போல மல்கு திரை","வளி பொரு வயங்கு பிசிர் பொங்கும்","நளி கடற் சேர்ப்பனொடு நகாஅ ஊங்கே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend says that even if the lovely little village, where the strong west wind shakes the great white thazhai flower, loosened from its tight bonds like the broken tusk of a fearsome elephant, and spreads its fine pollen on the sand-play of the jewelled girls, should grieve, they had known that without him they would be nothing, before ever they laughed with the lord of the shore of the dense sea where full waves rise in spray, struck by wind, like cotton beaten by a bow.","n":"The syntax of lines 5 to 9 is loose; the friend seems to say the girl went into the affair knowing its cost, which makes the man's delay the only thing left to answer for.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","alar","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 299","lb":"NT299","poet":"வடம வண்ணக்கன் பேரிசாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தோழி தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",1,"maa","simile","elephant"],["தாழை","தாழை",2,"maram","simile",""],["கோடை","கோடை",3,"pira","scene","west wind"],["பஞ்சி","பஞ்சி",7,"pira","simile","cotton"],["திரை","திரை",7,"pira","simile","waves"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:300":{"book":"natrinai","num":300,"t":["சுடர்த் தொடிக் கோமகள் சினந்தென அதன் எதிர்","மடத் தகை ஆயம் கைதொழுதாஅங்கு","உறு கால் ஒற்ற ஒல்கி ஆம்பல்","தாமரைக்கு இறைஞ்சும் தண் துறை ஊரன்","சிறு வளை விலை எனப் பெருந் தேர் பண்ணி எம்","முன் கடை நிறீஇச் சென்றிசினோனே","நீயும் தேரொடு வந்து பேர்தல் செல்லாது","நெய் வார்ந்தன்ன துய் அடங்கு நரம்பின்","இரும் பாண் ஒக்கல் தலைவன் பெரும் புண்","ஏஎர் தழும்பன் ஊணூர் ஆங்கண்","பிச்சை சூழ் பெருங் களிறு போல எம்","அட்டில் ஓலை தொட்டனை நின்மே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the town of the cool ford, where the water lily, swaying under a strong wind, bows to the lotus as the tender attendants bow with joined hands to an angry princess, that a man has decked his great chariot as the price of the girl's little bangles, stood it at our front gate and gone; and you too come with your chariot and will not leave, touching our kitchen's palm-leaf screen like the great begging elephant at Unur of Thazhumban of the great scar, chief of the bards' kin whose soft-fibred strings shine as if dripping ghee.","n":"The colophon's note says rival strangers have already offered marriage; the friend uses that chariot at the gate to shame the lover, who comes for scraps like a begging elephant instead of asking for her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","oodal","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 300","lb":"NT300","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"வாயில் மறுத்தது வரைவு கடாயதூஉம் ஆம் மாற்றோர் நொதுமலாளர் வரைவின் மேலிட்டு மருதத்துக் களவு","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வாயில் மறுத்தது"],["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயதூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",3,"maram","simile",""],["தாமரைக்கு","தாமரை",4,"maram","simile",""],["தேர்","பெருந் தேர்",5,"pira","scene","chariot"],["நெய்","நெய்",8,"unavu","simile","ghee"],["நரம்பின்","நரம்பு",8,"yaazh","simile","lute strings"],["களிறு","பெருங் களிறு",11,"maa","simile","elephant"]],"en":[["patron","தழும்பன்",10],["town","ஊணூர்",10]],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:15"},"nt:301":{"book":"natrinai","num":301,"t":["நீள் மலைக் கலித்த பெருங் கோற் குறிஞ்சி","நாள்மலர் புரையும் மேனி பெருஞ் சுனை","மலர் பிணைத்தன்ன மா இதழ் மழைக் கண்","மயில் ஓரன்ன சாயல் செந் தார்க்","கிளி ஓரன்ன கிளவி பணைத் தோள்","பாவை அன்ன வனப்பினள் இவள் என","காமர் நெஞ்சமொடு பல பாராட்டி","யாய் மறப்பு அறியா மடந்தை","தேம் மறப்பு அறியாக் கமழ் கூந்தலளே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, to herself, recalls how the mother praises the girl item by item, a body like the fresh kurinji bloom of the long hill, rain-dark eyes like paired flowers of the spring, a peacock's grace, a parrot's speech, a doll's beauty, and sees that the girl the mother never once forgets is the one now kept from her lover.","n":"The catalogue of beauty is the mother's, not the lover's, and that is the point: the same loving attention that praises every limb is the watch that never lapses, so the friend hears in the praise the reason the man is kept away.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 301","lb":"NT301","poet":"பாண்டியன் மாறன் வழுதி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சேட்படுத்து பிரிவின்கண் அன்பின் இயற்கையில் தகுவகையதோர் ஆற்றாமையினான் என்று தோழி தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nenju",null,"kalavu","தோழி தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குறிஞ்சி","குறிஞ்சி",1,"maram","simile",""],["சுனை","சுனை",2,"pira","scene",""],["மலர்","மலர்",3,"maram","simile",""],["மயில்","மயில்",4,"pul","simile",""],["கிளி","கிளி",5,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:302":{"book":"natrinai","num":302,"t":["இழை அணி மகளிரின் விழைதகப் பூத்த","நீடு சுரி இணர சுடர் வீக் கொன்றைக்","காடு கவின் பூத்தஆயினும் நன்றும்","வரு மழைக்கு எதிரிய மணி நிற இரும் புதல்","நரை நிறம் படுத்த நல் இணர்த் தெறுழ் வீ","தாஅம் தேரலர்கொல்லோ சேய் நாட்டு","களிறு உதைத்து ஆடிய கவிழ் கண் இடு நீறு","வெளிறு இல் காழ வேலம் நீடிய","பழங்கண் முது நெறி மறைக்கும்","வழங்கு அருங் கானம் இறந்திசினோரே"],"p":"","r":"The woman, seeing the season pass, says that though the forest has flowered with konrai clusters as lovely as jewelled women, surely the man who crossed the far waste, where elephant-kicked dust hides the old sad track among hard-cored velam trees, will notice the therul flowers strewn on the dark bushes that face the coming rain.","n":"She does not doubt his love but his eyes: the rain's flowers are everywhere at home, and her hope is that somewhere on his road the same sign is falling where he must see it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 302","lb":"NT302","poet":"மதுரை மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பருவம் கழிந்தது கண்டு தலைமகள் சொல்லியது","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பருவம் கழிந்தது கண்டு தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கொன்றைக்","கொன்றை",2,"maram","scene",""],["புதல்","புதல்",4,"maram","scene",""],["தெறுழ்","தெறுழ்",5,"maram","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",7,"maa","scene",""],["வேலம்","வேலம்",8,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:303":{"book":"natrinai","num":303,"t":["ஒலி அவிந்து அடங்கி யாமம்","நள்ளென","கலி கெழு பாக்கம் துயில் மடிந்தன்றே","தொன்று உறை கடவுள் சேர்ந்த பராரை","மன்றப் பெண்ணை வாங்கு மடற் குடம்பைத்","துணை புணர் அன்றில் உயவுக் குரல் கேட்டொறும்","துஞ்சாக் கண்ணள் துயர் அடச் சாஅய்","நம்வயின் வருந்தும் நன்னுதல் என்பது","உண்டுகொல் வாழி தோழி தெண் கடல்","வன் கைப் பரதவர் இட்ட செங் கோல்","கொடு முடி அவ் வலை பரியப் போக்கி","கடு முரண் எறி சுறா வழங்கும்","நெடுநீர்ச் சேர்ப்பன்தன் நெஞ்சத்தானே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that at deep midnight, when the bustling hamlet has fallen silent, every cry of the anril joined with its mate in the palmyra nest of the common ground keeps her sleepless and wasting, and she asks whether the lord of the shore, where the shark tears the fishermen's nets and roams the deep water, holds in his heart the thought that she suffers for him.","n":"The paired anril is no comfort but a reproach, a creature that does not part from its mate crying beside her; the shark that tears the net and swims free may hint at a man who slips every bond, but no second correspondence is drawn, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kaamanoy","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 303","lb":"NT303","poet":"மதுரை ஆருலவியநாட்டு ஆலம்பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வேட்கை தாங்ககில்லாளாய்த் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது சிறைப்புறத்தான் என்பது மலிந்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வேட்கை தாங்ககில்லாளாய்த் தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறத்தான் என்பது மலிந்ததூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",4,"deyvam","scene",""],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",5,"maram","scene",""],["அன்றில்","அன்றில்",6,"pul","scene",""],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",10,"seythi","scene",""],["சுறா","சுறா",12,"pira","scene","shark"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:304":{"book":"natrinai","num":304,"t":["வாரல் மென் தினைப் புலர்வுக் குரல் மாந்தி","சாரல் வரைய கிளைஉடன் குழீஇ","வளி எறி வயிரின் கிளி விளி பயிற்றும்","நளி இருஞ் சிலம்பின் நல் மலை நாடன்","புணரின் புணருமார் எழிலே பிரியின்","மணி மிடை பொன்னின் மாமை சாய என்","அணி நலம் சிதைக்குமார் பசலை அதனால்","அசுணம் கொல்பவர் கை போல் நன்றும்","இன்பமும் துன்பமும் உடைத்தே","தண் கமழ் நறுந் தார் விறலோன் மார்பே"],"p":"","r":"Answering her friend's reassurance, the woman says that the hill lord's cool fragrant-garlanded chest is like the hands of those who kill the asunam, full of pleasure and of pain: when he joins her, her beauty joins her, and when he parts, pallor fades her gem-and-gold complexion and wrecks her looks.","n":"The asunam is the creature that is lured by music and then killed, so the simile says that his chest gives joy and pain from the same hand, not by turns from different causes; the parrots calling like a wind-blown horn set the scene of the hill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 304","lb":"NT304","poet":"மாறோக்கத்து நப்பசலையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரையாது நெடுங்காலம் வந்தொழுக ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகள் வன்புறை எதிர் மொழிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வன்புறை எதிர் மொழிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தினைப்","தினை",1,"unavu","scene",""],["கிளி","கிளி",3,"pul","scene",""],["அசுணம்","அசுணம்",8,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:305":{"book":"natrinai","num":305,"t":["வரி அணி பந்தும் வாடிய வயலையும்","மயில் அடி அன்ன மாக் குரல் நொச்சியும்","கடியுடை வியல் நகர் காண் வரத் தோன்ற","தமியே கண்ட தண்டலையும் தெறுவர","நோய் ஆகின்றே மகளை நின் தோழி","எரி சினம் தணிந்த இலை இல் அம் சினை","வரிப் புறப் புறவின் புலம்பு கொள் தெள் விளி","உருப்பு அவிர் அமையத்து அமர்ப்பனள் நோக்கி","இலங்கு இலை வெள் வேல் விடலையை","விலங்கு மலை ஆர் இடை நலியும்கொல் எனவே"],"p":"","r":"The mother tells the friend that the striped ball, the withered vayalai creeper, the nochchi with clusters like a peacock's foot and the garden her daughter used to see alone have become a burning pain to her; and she wonders whether the girl, hearing a striped dove's lonely call on a leafless branch in the shimmering heat, fears that the hill pass may harm the young man with the white spear.","n":"The mother's grief turns at line 6 into the daughter's fear: she pictures the girl on the road not afraid for herself but for the man she went with.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ninaivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 305","lb":"NT305","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"நற்றாய் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது மனை மருட்சியும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["nartaay","thozhi",null,"kalavu","நற்றாய் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது"],["nartaay","pira",null,"kalavu","மனை மருட்சியும் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["வயலையும்","வயலை",1,"maram","absent",""],["மயில்","மயில்",2,"pul","simile",""],["நொச்சியும்","நொச்சி",2,"maram","scene",""],["புறவின்","புறவு",7,"pul","scene","dove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:306":{"book":"natrinai","num":306,"t":["தந்தை வித்திய மென் தினை பைபயச்","சிறு கிளி கடிதல் பிறக்கு யாவணதோ","குளிர் படு கையள் கொடிச்சி செல்க என","நல்ல இனிய கூறி மெல்லக்","கொயல் தொடங்கினரே கானவர் கொடுங் குரல்","சூற் பொறை இறுத்த கோல் தலை இருவி","விழவு ஒழி வியன் களம் கடுப்பத் தெறுவர","பைதல் ஒரு நிலை காண வைகல்","யாங்கு வருவதுகொல்லோ தீம் சொல்","செறி தோட்டு எல் வளைக் குறுமகள்","சிறு புனத்து அல்கிய பெரும் புற நிலையே"],"p":"","r":"Within hearing of the man, the friend says the millet the father sowed is being reaped, the hunters sending the cold-handed hill girl home with kind words, and the stubble stands like a festival ground after the feast; how will he come, day after day, to see the sad bare field where the sweet-voiced girl once stayed?","n":"The friend says nothing of marriage; the empty field does the work, since its emptiness means she is now kept at home and he can meet her only by asking for her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 306","lb":"NT306","poet":"உரோடோகத்துக் கந்தரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"புனம் மடிவு உரைத்துச் செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது சிறைப்புறமும் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","புனம் மடிவு உரைத்துச் செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது"],["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமும் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தினை","தினை",1,"unavu","scene",""],["கிளி","கிளி",2,"pul","scene",""],["கானவர்","கானவர்",5,"seythi","scene",""],["இருவி","இருவி",6,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:307":{"book":"natrinai","num":307,"t":["கவர் பரி நெடுந் தேர் மணியும் இசைக்கும்","பெயர் பட இயங்கிய இளையரும் ஒலிப்பர்","கடல் ஆடு வியல் இடைப் பேர் அணிப் பொலிந்த","திதலை அல்குல் நலம் பாராட்டிய","வருமே தோழி வார் மணற் சேர்ப்பன்","இறை பட வாங்கிய முழவுமுதற் புன்னை","மா அரை மறைகம் வம்மதி பானாள்","பூ விரி கானல் புணர் குறி வந்து நம்","மெல் இணர் நறும் பொழில் காணா","அல்லல் அரும் படர் காண்கம் நாம் சிறிதே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman, anxious that the tryst is delayed, that the lord of the long sands is coming, his chariot bells ringing and his young men calling; let them hide behind the dark trunk of the drum-rooted punnai and, when he comes at midnight to the flowered grove, watch a little of the pain he suffers at not finding them.","n":"The comfort is a game: by turning the waiting round, so that he is the one who searches and grieves, the friend gives the woman back the upper hand the delay had taken from her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 307","lb":"NT307","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"குறி நீட ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","குறி நீட ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",6,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:308":{"book":"natrinai","num":308,"t":["செல விரைவுற்ற அரவம் போற்றி","மலர் ஏர் உண்கண் பனி வர ஆயிழை","யாம் தற் கரையவும் நாணினள் வருவோள்","வேண்டாமையின் மென்மெல வந்து","வினவலும் தகைத்தலும் செல்லாள் ஆகி","வெறி கமழ் துறு முடி தயங்க நல் வினைப்","பொறி அழி பாவையின் கலங்கி நெடிது நினைந்து","ஆகம் அடைதந்தோளே அது கண்டு","ஈர் மண் செய்கை நீர் படு பசுங் கலம்","பெரு மழைப் பெயற்கு ஏற்றாங்கு எம்","பொருள் மலி நெஞ்சம் புணர்ந்து உவந்தன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The man, urged by his heart to go for wealth, tells it that when she noticed the bustle of departure she came slowly, tears in her flower-like eyes, unable to ask or to stop him, troubled like a doll whose mechanism has broken, and pressed herself to his chest; and at that his wealth-filled heart melted into hers like a fresh pot of wet clay set out in the heavy rain, and he will not go.","n":"She wins without a word: the poem stresses that she could neither question nor hold him, and the silence is what undoes his resolve; the unfired pot in the rain says his purpose dissolved, not that it was overruled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 308","lb":"NT308","poet":"எயினந்தை மகன் இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"நெஞ்சினால் பொருள் வலிக்கப்பட்ட தலைமகன் தலைமகளை எய்தி ஆற்றானாய் நெஞ்சினைச் சொல்லிச் செலவு அழுங்கியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","நெஞ்சினால் பொருள் வலிக்கப்பட்ட தலைமகன் தலைமகளை எய்தி ஆற்றானாய் நெஞ்சினைச் சொல்லிச் செலவு அழுங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கலம்","கலம்",9,"seythi","simile","clay pot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:309":{"book":"natrinai","num":309,"t":["நெகிழ்ந்த தோளும் வாடிய வரியும்","தளிர் வனப்பு இழந்த என் நிறனும் நோக்கி","யான் செய்தன்று இவள் துயர் என அன்பின்","ஆழல் வாழி தோழி வாழைக்","கொழு மடல் அகல் இலைத் தளி தலைக் கலாவும்","பெரு மலை நாடன் கேண்மை நமக்கே","விழுமமாக அறியுநர் இன்று என","கூறுவைமன்னோ நீயே","தேறுவன்மன் யான் அவருடை நட்பே"],"p":"","r":"The woman consoles her friend, who sees her slack arms, faded lines and lost leaf-bright colour and grieves that she caused it: do not sink in grief; you may say that no one knows the hill lord's friendship has become our trouble, but I trust his friendship.","n":"The roles are reversed from the usual: the friend has broken down and the heroine does the comforting, and her comfort is a single act of trust set against all the visible evidence of her own wasting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","varaivu","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 309","lb":"NT309","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரைவு நீட ஆற்றாள் எனக் கவன்று தான் ஆற்றாளாகிய தோழியைத் தலைமகள் ஆற்றுவித்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தான் ஆற்றாளாகிய தோழியைத் தலைமகள் ஆற்றுவித்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வாழைக்","வாழை",4,"maram","scene",""],["தளி","தளி",5,"pira","scene","raindrop"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:310":{"book":"natrinai","num":310,"t":["விளக்கின் அன்ன சுடர் விடு தாமரை","களிற்றுச் செவி அன்ன பாசடை தயங்க","உண்துறை மகளிர் இரிய குண்டு நீர்","வாளை பிறழும் ஊரற்கு நாளை","மகட் கொடை எதிர்ந்த மடம் கெழு பெண்டே","தொலைந்த நாவின் உலைந்த குறு மொழி","உடன்பட்டு ஓராத் தாயரொடு ஒழிபுடன்","சொல்லலைகொல்லோ நீயே வல்லை","களிறு பெறு வல்சிப் பாணன் கையதை","வள் உயிர்த் தண்ணுமை போல","உள் யாதும் இல்லது ஓர் போர்வைஅம் சொல்லே","வாயிலாகப் புக்க விறலியைத் தோழி சொல்லியது"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the singing-woman who has come as the man's go-between that the town's lord, where lotuses burn like lamps among leaves like elephant ears and the vaalai leaps in the deep water so the bathing women flee, has agreed to a bride tomorrow, and that her clever words are a cover with nothing inside, like the loud drum in the hand of a bard who is fed on gifts of elephants.","n":"Lines 5 to 8 are hard to construe (who has agreed to give a daughter, and who are the mothers with failing speech), so the exact charge is uncertain; the leaping fish that scatters the bathing women may glance at the man's disorder in the households, but only one correspondence can be drawn, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","sol","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 310","lb":"NT310","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"விறலியை எதிர்ப்பட்ட பரத்தை சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","virali",null,"karpu",null],["parathai","virali",null,"karpu","விறலியை எதிர்ப்பட்ட பரத்தை சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",1,"maram","simile",""],["களிற்றுச்","களிறு",2,"maa","simile",""],["வாளை","வாளை",4,"pira","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",9,"maa","gift_food",""],["பாணன்","பாணன்",9,"seythi","scene",""],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",10,"parai","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:311":{"book":"natrinai","num":311,"t":["பெயினே விடு மான் உளையின் வெறுப்பத் தோன்றி","இருங் கதிர் நெல்லின் யாணரஃதே","வறப்பின் மா நீர் முண்டகம் தாஅய்ச் சேறு புலர்ந்து","இருங் கழிச் செறுவின் வெள் உப்பு விளையும்","அழியா மரபின் நம் மூதூர் நன்றே","கொழு மீன் சுடு புகை மறுகினுள் மயங்கி","சிறு வீ ஞாழல் துறையுமார் இனிதே","ஒன்றே தோழி நம் கானலது பழியே","கருங் கோட்டுப் புன்னை மலர்த் தாது அருந்தி","இருங் களிப் பிரசம் ஊத அவர்","நெடுந் தேர் இன் ஒலி கேட்டலோ அரிதே"],"p":"","r":"Consoling the woman who suffers from gossip, the friend praises their old town, rich with paddy when it rains and with white salt from the backwater pans when it is dry, and its ford sweet with the smoke of roasting fish and njazhal flowers; its grove has one fault only, that the bees drunk on punnai pollen hum so loudly that his chariot's sweet sound is hard to hear.","n":"The friend never names the gossip; she moves the blame onto the grove, where the drunken bees' din drowns his chariot, and the hearer is left to match the bees with the talking town.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 311","lb":"NT311","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"அலர் கூறப்பட்டு ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகளைத் தோழி ஆற்றுவித்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","அலர் கூறப்பட்டு ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகளைத் தோழி ஆற்றுவித்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மான்","மான்",1,"maa","simile","horse"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",2,"unavu","scene",""],["முண்டகம்","முண்டகம்",3,"maram","scene",""],["உப்பு","உப்பு",4,"pira","scene",""],["மீன்","மீன்",6,"pira","gift_food",""],["ஞாழல்","ஞாழல்",7,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",9,"maram","scene",""],["பிரசம்","பிரசம்",10,"pira","ullurai","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","பிரசம்","not_confirmed","0/1","the gossiping town and the man's kept-off visits"]],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:312":{"book":"natrinai","num":312,"t":["நோகோ யானே நோம் என் நெஞ்சே","பனிப் புதல் ஈங்கை அம் குழை வருட","சிறை குவிந்திருந்த பைதல் வெண் குருகு","பார்வை வேட்டுவன் காழ் களைந்தருள","மாரி நின்ற மையல் அற்சிரம்","யாம் தன் உழையம் ஆகவும் தானே","எதிர்த்த தித்தி முற்றா முலையள்","கோடைத் திங்களும் பனிப்போள்","வாடைப் பெரும் பனிக்கு என்னள்கொல் எனவே"],"p":"","r":"The man, set on wealth, tells his heart that he grieves and it grieves too: in the misty cold when the rains have stopped, when the shivering white heron sits with folded wings and even the decoy hunter takes up his stakes in pity, she who shivers in the summer months even with him beside her, what will become of her in the north wind's great cold?","n":"The reason to go is never stated, only the reason not to; the hunter who lays off in pity of a cold bird is the one figure in the scene who does what the man is being asked to do.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 312","lb":"NT312","poet":"கழார்க் கீரன் எயிற்றியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள் வலித்த தலைமகன் நெஞ்சினை நெருங்கிச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பொருள் வலித்த தலைமகன் நெஞ்சினை நெருங்கிச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["ஈங்கை","ஈங்கை",2,"maram","scene",""],["குருகு","குருகு",3,"pul","scene","heron"],["வேட்டுவன்","வேட்டுவன்",4,"seythi","iraicchi",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வேட்டுவன்","not_confirmed","0/1","even a hunter spares a creature shivering in the cold, so the man should not leave his shivering wife in it"],["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:313":{"book":"natrinai","num":313,"t":["கருங் கால் வேங்கை நாள் உறு புதுப் பூ","பொன் செய் கம்மியன் கைவினை கடுப்ப","தகை வனப்புற்ற கண்ணழி கட்டழித்து","ஒலி பல் கூந்தல் அணி பெறப் புனைஇ","காண்டற் காதல் கைம்மிக கடீஇயாற்கு","யாங்கு ஆகுவம்கொல் தோழி காந்தள்","கமழ் குலை அவிழ்ந்த நயவருஞ் சாரல்","கூதள நறும் பொழில் புலம்ப ஊர்வயின்","மீள்குவம் போலத் தோன்றும் தோடு புலர்ந்து","அருவியின் ஒலித்தல் ஆனா","கொய்பதம் கொள்ளும் நாம் கூஉம் தினையே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend asks the woman what will become of them, now that he who, longing to see her, dressed her thick hair with fresh vengai blossom like a goldsmith's work must find the koothalam grove on the kaanthal slope deserted; for the millet they guard with shouts, its leaves dried, has reached the time for reaping, and they will go back to town.","n":"The vengai flowers he set in her hair are the memory of the kalavu, and the reaping is its end; the friend speaks as one who fears the separation, so that the listening man hears the date of it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 313","lb":"NT313","poet":"தங்கால் பொற்கொல்லன் வெண்ணாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லுவாளாய் புனம் அழிவு உரைத்துசெறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","புனம் அழிவு உரைத்துசெறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","ornament",""],["கம்மியன்","கம்மியன்",2,"seythi","simile",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",6,"maram","scene",""],["கூதள","கூதளம்",8,"maram","scene",""],["அருவியின்","அருவி",10,"pira","simile",""],["தினையே","தினை",11,"unavu","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:314":{"book":"natrinai","num":314,"t":["முதிர்ந்தோர் இளமை அழிந்தும் எய்தார்","வாழ் நாள் வகை அளவு அறிஞரும் இல்லை","மாரிப் பித்திகத்து ஈர் இதழ் அலரி","நறுங் காழ் ஆரமொடு மிடைந்த மார்பில்","குறும் பொறிக் கொண்ட கொம்மை அம் புகர்ப்பின்","கருங் கண் வெம் முலை ஞெமுங்கப் புல்லிக்","கழிவதாக கங்குல் என்று","தாம் மொழி வன்மையின் பொய்த்தனர் வாழிய","நொடி விடுவன்ன காய் விடு கள்ளி","அலங்கல்அம் பாவை ஏறி புலம்பு கொள்","புன் புறா வீழ் பெடைப் பயிரும்","என்றூழ் நீளிடைச் சென்றிசினோரே"],"p":"","r":"The wasting woman says that the old never get back their lost youth and no one knows the measure of a life, yet the men who held her close, saying 'let the night pass so', lied by the strength of their words and went, may they live, into the long hot track where a dull lonely dove climbs the swaying kalli, whose pods crack like snapping fingers, and calls to its fallen mate.","n":"The opening maxim is her argument against his journey: youth does not return and life's length is unknown, so the time he spends earning is spent out of the only time love has; her blessing on the liars is bitter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ilamai","nilaiyaamai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 314","lb":"NT314","poet":"முப்பேர் நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகள் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகள் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பித்திகத்து","பித்திகம்",3,"maram","ornament",""],["ஆரமொடு","ஆரம்",4,"maram","ornament","sandal"],["கள்ளி","கள்ளி",9,"maram","simile",""],["புறா","புறா",11,"pul","iraicchi",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","புறா","confirmed","1/1","even a dove in the hot waste calls for its mate, while the man who went that way has left his own"]],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:315":{"book":"natrinai","num":315,"t":["ஈண்டு பெருந் தெய்வத்து யாண்டு பல கழிந்தென","பார்த் துறைப் புணரி அலைத்தலின் புடை கொண்டு","மூத்து வினை போகிய முரி வாய் அம்பி","நல் எருது நடை வளம் வைத்தென உழவர்","புல்லுடைக் காவில் தொழில் விட்டாங்கு","நறு விரை நன் புகை கொடாஅர் சிறு வீ","ஞாழலொடு கெழீஇய புன்னை அம் கொழு நிழல்","முழவு முதற் பிணிக்கும் துறைவ நன்றும்","விழுமிதின் கொண்ட கேண்மை நொவ்விதின்","தவறும் நன்கு அறியாய்ஆயின் எம் போல்","ஞெகிழ் தோள் கலுழ்ந்த கண்ணர்","மலர் தீய்ந்தனையர் நின் நயந்தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The courtesan, hurt, tells the lord of the ford, where an old boat with a broken prow, worn by years of beating surf, its work done, lies tied to the drum-like root of the punnai in the njazhal's shade and no longer gets its fragrant smoke, as ploughmen leave a spent ox in a grassy grove, that if he does not see the fault of so lightly dropping a bond so fully taken, all who love him will end like her, arms slack, eyes weeping, like burnt flowers.","n":"Lines 1, 4 and 5 are hard to construe, so the reading of the worship (the boat once given incense, now not) and of the ox is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","melivu","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 315","lb":"NT315","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகனைப் பரத்தை நொந்து சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["parathai","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தலைமகனைப் பரத்தை நொந்து சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இளிவு"]],"k":[["தெய்வத்து","தெய்வம்",1,"deyvam","scene",""],["அம்பி","அம்பி",3,"pira","ullurai","boat"],["எருது","எருது",4,"maa","simile",""],["உழவர்","உழவர்",4,"seythi","simile",""],["ஞாழலொடு","ஞாழல்",7,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",7,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","அம்பி","confirmed","1/1","the courtesan, used and laid aside by the man"]],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:316":{"book":"natrinai","num":316,"t":["மடவது அம்ம மணி நிற எழிலி","மலரின் மௌவல் நலம் வரக் காட்டி","கயல் ஏர் உண்கண் கனங்குழை இவை நின்","எயிறு ஏர் பொழுதின் ஏய்தருவேம் என","கண் அகன் விசும்பின் மதி என உணர்ந்த நின்","நல் நுதல் நீவிச் சென்றோர் தம் நசை","வாய்த்து வரல் வாரா அளவை அத்தக்","கல் மிசை அடுக்கம் புதையக் கால் வீழ்த்து","தளி தரு தண் கார் தலைஇ","விளி இசைத்தன்றால் வியல் இடத்தானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the wasting woman how foolish the sapphire cloud is: he left, stroking her brow, which he thought the moon of the wide sky, saying he would come when the mouval flowers show a beauty like her teeth, and before his wish is won and he returns, the cool rain cloud has poured down, hiding the rock ridges, and thundered across the wide sky.","n":"Calling the cloud foolish declares the rain false: it has come before its time, so his promise is not yet broken; the comfort works by blaming the season rather than the man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 316","lb":"NT316","poet":"இடைக்காடனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","மடன்"]],"k":[["மௌவல்","மௌவல்",2,"maram","simile",""],["கயல்","கயல்",3,"pira","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:317":{"book":"natrinai","num":317,"t":["நீடு இருஞ் சிலம்பின் பிடியொடு புணர்ந்த","பூம் பொறி ஒருத்தல் ஏந்து கை கடுப்ப","தோடு தலை வாங்கிய நீடு குரற் பைந் தினை","பவளச் செவ் வாய்ப் பைங் கிளி கவரும்","உயர் வரை நாட நீ நயந்தோள் கேண்மை","அன்னை அறிகுவள்ஆயின் பனி கலந்து","என் ஆகுவகொல்தானே எந்தை","ஓங்கு வரைச் சாரல் தீம் சுனை ஆடி","ஆயமொடு குற்ற குவளை","மா இதழ் மா மலர் புரைஇய கண்ணே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the high hills, where the long millet ear curves like the raised trunk of a spotted tusker mated with his cow and the coral-beaked parrot carries it off, what will become of the girl's eyes, like the dark kuvalai she picked with her companions after bathing in the spring of her father's slope, if her mother learns of their love.","n":"The parrot stealing the father's ripe millet suggests the man taking what the family guards, but only that one correspondence is drawn, so it stays scene; the threat is in the eyes, which will weep.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","kan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 317","lb":"NT317","poet":"மதுரைப் பூவண்ட நாகன் வேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகனை வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி தலைமகனை வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பிடியொடு","பிடி",1,"maa","simile",""],["ஒருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",2,"maa","simile",""],["தினை","தினை",3,"unavu","scene",""],["கிளி","கிளி",4,"pul","scene",""],["சுனை","சுனை",8,"pira","scene",""],["குவளை","குவளை",9,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:318":{"book":"natrinai","num":318,"t":["நினைத்தலும் நினைதிரோ ஐய அன்று நாம்","பணைத் தாள் ஓமைப் படு சினை பயந்த","பொருந்தாப் புகர் நிழல் இருந்தனெமாக","நடுக்கம் செய்யாது நண்ணுவழித் தோன்றி","ஒடித்து மிசைக் கொண்ட ஓங்கு மருப்பு யானை","பொறி படு தடக்கை சுருக்கி பிறிது ஓர்","ஆறு இடையிட்ட அளவைக்கு வேறு உணர்ந்து","என்றூழ் விடர் அகம் சிலம்ப","புன் தலை மடப் பிடி புலம்பிய குரலே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, told he is leaving, asks the man whether he even remembers the day they sat in the dappled shade of the omai, when a tall-tusked elephant came near without frightening them, broke branches and ate, then curled its trunk and went off by another path, and at once the young cow-elephant, sensing it, cried out alone till the hot rock clefts rang.","n":"The friend tells only what the elephants did and leaves him to match it: he is the tusker who turns off by another path, and the cow's cry echoing in the rocks is what his leaving will bring.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 318","lb":"NT318","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங் கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட தலைமகனைத் தோழி சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,"பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்ட தலைமகனைத் தோழி சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஓமைப்","ஓமை",2,"maram","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",5,"maa","ullurai",""],["பிடி","பிடி",9,"maa","ullurai",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","யானை பிடி","not_confirmed","0/1","the man about to leave and the woman left behind"],["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:319":{"book":"natrinai","num":319,"t":["ஓதமும் ஒலி ஓவின்றே ஊதையும்","தாது உளர் கானல் தவ்வென்றன்றே","மணல் மலி மூதூர் அகல் நெடுந் தெருவில்","கூகைச் சேவல் குராலோடு ஏறி","ஆர் இருஞ் சதுக்கத்து அஞ்சுவரக் குழறும்","அணங்கு கால் கிளரும் மயங்கு இருள் நடு நாள்","பாவை அன்ன பலர் ஆய் வனப்பின்","தட மென் பணைத் தோள் மடம் மிகு குறுமகள்","சுணங்கு அணி வன முலை முயங்கல் உள்ளி","மீன் கண் துஞ்சும் பொழுதும்","யான் கண் துஞ்சேன் யாதுகொல் நிலையே"],"p":"","r":"The man, kept away by the close watch, says to himself that the tide has stopped roaring and the wind is still over the pollen-strewn grove, the male owl and its mate hoot fearfully from the crossroads of the sandy old town, and at midnight, when spirits walk in the thick dark and even the fish's eyes sleep, he lies awake longing to embrace the girl of doll-like beauty; what will become of him?","n":"Everything in the night is stilled or asleep, even the fish that never closes its eyes, so his wakefulness is set against the whole world; the owl pair and the walking spirits make the hour that a meeting would need, and that the watch denies him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kalaviyal","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 319","lb":"NT319","poet":"வினைத்தொழில் சோகீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"காப்பு மிகுதிக்கண் ஆற்றானாகிய தலைமகன் தலைமகளை நினைந்து தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","தலைமகளை நினைந்து தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["கூகைச்","கூகை",4,"pul","scene","owl"],["குராலோடு","குரால்",4,"pul","scene","female owl"],["அணங்கு","அணங்கு",6,"deyvam","scene",""],["மீன்","மீன்",10,"pira","scene","fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:320":{"book":"natrinai","num":320,"t":["விழவும் மூழ்த்தன்று முழவும் தூங்கின்று","எவன் குறித்தனள்கொல் என்றி ஆயின்","தழை அணிந்து அலமரும் அல்குல் தெருவின்","இளையோள் இறந்த அனைத்தற்கு பழ விறல்","ஓரிக் கொன்ற ஒரு பெருந் தெருவில்","காரி புக்க நேரார் புலம்போல்","கல்லென்றன்றால் ஊரே அதற்கொண்டு","காவல் செறிய மாட்டி ஆய்தொடி","எழில் மா மேனி மகளிர்","விழுமாந்தனர் தம் கொழுநரைக் காத்தே"],"p":"","r":"The courtesan, within hearing of those on her side, says the festival is over and the drum is hung up, and if you ask what she meant: for no more than a young girl in a leaf skirt walking down the street, the town broke into uproar like the land of his enemies when Kaari entered the great street where old Ori was killed, and the fair-bangled wives shut the watch tight and grew anxious, guarding their husbands.","n":"She mocks the wives by measuring their panic against a town sacked in war: one girl walking past is enough to make the household behave like a captured city.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","alar","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 320","lb":"NT320","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தை தனக்குப் பாங்காயினார் கேட்ப நெருங்கிச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","pira",null,"karpu","பரத்தை தனக்குப் பாங்காயினார் கேட்ப நெருங்கிச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["முழவும்","முழவு",1,"parai","scene",""],["தழை","தழை",3,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"]],"en":[["king","ஓரி",5],["king","காரி",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:16"},"nt:321":{"book":"natrinai","num":321,"t":["செந் நிலப் புறவின் புன் மயிர்ப் புருவை","பாடு இன் தெள் மணித் தோடு தலைப்பெயர","கான முல்லைக் கய வாய் அலரி","பார்ப்பன மகளிர் சாரற் புறத்து அணிய","கல் சுடர் சேரும் கதிர் மாய் மாலை","புல்லென் வறு மனை நோக்கி மெல்ல","வருந்தும்கொல்லோ திருந்துஇழை அரிவை","வல்லைக் கடவுமதி தேரே சென்றிக","குருந்து அவிழ் குறும்பொறை பயிற்ற","பெருங் கலி மூதூர் மரம் தோன்றும்மே"],"p":"","r":"His task done, the man tells his charioteer that at this evening, when the short-fleeced sheep of the red grassland turn home to the clear sweet sound of their bells, brahmin women wear the wide-mouthed wild jasmine at the edge of the slope and the sun's rays sink into the rock, his finely-jewelled wife may be grieving quietly as she looks at the dull empty house; so drive fast, for as the low kurundhu-flowered hillocks go by, the trees of the old noisy town come into view.","n":"The flock going home with its bells is the evening's picture of return, and it makes the house he imagines, empty and dull, the sharper: he reads his own lateness in the scene before he names her grief, and the urging of the chariot follows from it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","maalai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 321","lb":"NT321","poet":"மதுரை அளக்கர் ஞாழார் மகனார் மள்ளனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி மீள்வான் தேர்ப்பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["pira","pira",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றி மீள்வான்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புருவை","புருவை",1,"maa","scene","sheep"],["முல்லைக்","முல்லை",3,"maram","ornament","wild jasmine"],["பார்ப்பன","பார்ப்பனர்",4,"seythi","scene",""],["குருந்து","குருந்து",9,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:322":{"book":"natrinai","num":322,"t":["ஆங்கனம் தணிகுவதுஆயின் யாங்கும்","இதனின் கொடியது பிறிது ஒன்று இல்லை","வாய்கொல் வாழி தோழி வேய் உயர்ந்து","எறிந்து செறித்தன்ன பிணங்கு அரில் விடர் முகை","ஊன் தின் பிணவின் உயங்கு பசி களைஇயர்","ஆள் இயங்கு அரும் புழை ஒற்றி வாள் வரிக்","கடுங் கண் வயப் புலி ஒடுங்கும் நாடன்","தண் கமழ் வியல் மார்பு உரிதினின் பெறாது","நல் நுதல் பசந்த படர் மலி அரு நோய்","அணங்கு என உணரக் கூறி வேலன்","இன் இயம் கறங்கப் பாடி","பல் மலர் சிதறிப் பரவுறு பலிக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker asks her friend, so the man nearby may hear, whether it is true that if the sickness were eased that way nothing anywhere would be crueller: for the grievous longing that paled the girl's fine brow, because she cannot rightly have the cool fragrant chest of the man of the land where a fierce sword-striped tiger crouches at the narrow track men use to ease the hunger of its flesh-eating mate in a thicket-choked cleft, the velan has declared to be a spirit's work, singing to sweet instruments and scattering many flowers as an offering of praise.","n":"The friend's scorn falls on the velan's diagnosis: the cause is a man's chest, and a rite to a spirit cannot reach it. The tiger that waits at the men's track to feed its mate keeps the danger of the night path in view; read as the man who should provide for his mate it is a near miss for iraicchi, since the act carries no single sense the closing lines confirm. How lines 1 and 2 bear on the rest is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veri","velan","pasalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 322","lb":"NT322","poet":"மதுரைப் பாலாசிரியன் சேந்தன் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது தலைமகள் பாங்கிக்கு உரைத்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைமகள் பாங்கிக்கு உரைத்ததூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வேய்","வேய்",3,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பிணவின்","பிணவு",5,"maa","scene","tigress"],["புலி","புலி",7,"maa","scene",""],["வேலன்","வேலன்",10,"seythi","scene",""],["இயம்","இயம்",11,"parai","scene",""],["மலர்","மலர்",12,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:323":{"book":"natrinai","num":323,"t":["ஓங்கித் தோன்றும் தீம் கள் பெண்ணை","நடுவணதுவேதெய்ய மடவரல்","ஆயமும் யானும் அறியாது அவணம்","ஆய நட்பின் மாண் நலம் ஒழிந்து நின்","கிளைமை கொண்ட வளை ஆர் முன்கை","நல்லோள் தந்தை சிறுகுடிப் பாக்கம்","புலி வரிபு எக்கர்ப் புன்னை உதிர்த்த","மலி தாது ஊதும் தேனோடு ஒன்றி","வண்டு இமிர் இன் இசை கறங்க திண் தேர்த்","தெரி மணி கேட்டலும் அரிதே","வரும் ஆறு ஈது அவண் மறவாதீமே"],"p":"","r":"Agreeing to a night meeting, the friend tells the man that the tall sweet-toddy palm stands in the very middle of the small shore hamlet of the father of the bangled girl who, unknown to her companions and to the friend, set aside their fine friendship and took kinship with him; there, where bees humming with honeybees over the heavy pollen the punnai sheds on the tiger-striped dune make sweet music, the bells of his strong chariot will hardly be heard: this is the way to come, and he must not forget it.","n":"The friend gives a route by landmarks, and her real point is practical: the hum of the punnai grove will cover the chariot bells, so he can come unheard. Lines 3 to 5 (the companions and I not knowing, the friendship set aside) are hard to construe and the sense of புலி வரிபு is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 323","lb":"NT323","poet":"வடம வண்ணக்கன் பேரிசாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி இரவுக்குறி நேர்ந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி நேர்ந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",1,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["கள்","கள்",1,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",7,"maram","scene",""],["தேனோடு","தேன்",8,"pira","scene","honeybee"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",9,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:324":{"book":"natrinai","num":324,"t":["அந்தோ தானே அளியள் தாயே","நொந்து அழி அவலமொடு என் ஆகுவள்கொல்","பொன் போல் மேனித் தன் மகள் நயந்தோள்","கோடு முற்று யானை காடுடன் நிறைதர","நெய் பட்டன்ன நோன் காழ் எஃகின்","செல்வத் தந்தை இடனுடை வரைப்பின்","ஆடு பந்து உருட்டுநள் போல ஓடி","அம் சில் ஓதி இவள் உறும்","பஞ்சி மெல் அடி நடைபயிற்றும்மே"],"p":"","r":"Alas, pitiable is the mother: crushed by grief, what will become of her who loved her gold-bodied daughter, now that this girl of lovely sparse locks, who in her rich father's wide house of oil-sheened strong spears ran about as if chasing a ball at play, walks on her soft cotton-like feet through a forest full of full-tusked elephants?","n":"The pity goes not to the girl, who still runs as at play, but to the mother at home, so the elopement is weighed by what it costs the house left behind. The colophon's thinai is குறிஞ்சி, but nothing of the poem's situation belongs to the hill tryst.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","anbu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 324","lb":"NT324","poet":"கயமனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் பாங்கற்குச் சொல்லியது இடைச் சுரத்துக் கண்டோர் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":1,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","paangan",null,"kalavu","தலைமகன் பாங்கற்குச் சொல்லியது"],["kandor","pira",null,"kalavu","இடைச் சுரத்துக் கண்டோர் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["பஞ்சி","பஞ்சி",9,"maram","simile","cotton"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:325":{"book":"natrinai","num":325,"t":["கவிதலை எண்கின் பரூஉ மயிர் ஏற்றை","இரை தேர் வேட்கையின் இரவில் போகி","நீடு செயல் சிதலைத் தோடு புனைந்து எடுத்த","அர வாழ் புற்றம் ஒழிய ஒய்யென","முர வாய் வள் உகிர் இடப்ப வாங்கும்","ஊக்கு அருங் கவலை நீந்தி மற்று இவள்","பூப்போல் உண்கண் புது நலம் சிதைய","வீங்கு நீர் வாரக் கண்டும்","தகுமோ பெரும தவிர்க நும் செலவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man whether it is fitting for him to cross the hard forked paths where a bent-headed thick-haired male bear, eager for food, goes out at night and with rough mouth and strong claws tears open the snake-haunted termite mound that long-working termites built up in layers, even after seeing tears swell from her flower-like kohl eyes and spoil their fresh beauty; lord, give up your going.","n":"The bear wrecks in a moment what the termites built over a long time, which is what the friend says his going will do to the girl's new beauty; she never draws the match herself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nalam","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 325","lb":"NT325","poet":"மதுரைக் காருலவியங் கூத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி செலவு அழுங்குவித்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,null,"செலவு அழுங்குவித்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["எண்கின்","எண்கு",1,"maa","ullurai","bear"],["சிதலைத்","சிதலை",3,"pira","scene","termites"],["அர","அரவு",4,"pira","scene","snake"],["புற்றம்","புற்று",4,"pira","ullurai","termite mound"],["பூப்போல்","பூ",7,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","எண்கின் புற்றம்","not_confirmed","0/1","the man going after wealth and the fresh beauty his going will ruin"]],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:326":{"book":"natrinai","num":326,"t":["கொழுஞ் சுளைப் பலவின் பயம் கெழு கவாஅன்","செழுங் கோள் வாங்கிய மாச் சினைக் கொக்கினம்","மீன் குடை நாற்றம் தாங்கல்செல்லாது","துய்த் தலை மந்தி தும்மும் நாட","நினக்கும் உரைத்தல் நாணுவல் இவட்கே","நுண் கொடிப் பீரத்து ஊழ் உறு பூ எனப்","பசலை ஊரும் அன்னோ பல் நாள்","அரி அமர் வனப்பின் எம் கானம் நண்ண","வண்டு எனும் உணராவாகி","மலர் என மரீஇ வரூஉம் இவள் கண்ணே"],"p":"","r":"Pressing the man to marry, the friend tells the lord of the land where, on the fruitful jackfruit slope, a fluff-headed female monkey sneezes, unable to bear the smell of the fish the heron flock bring onto the dark fruit-laden branch, that she is ashamed to say it even to him: for many days pallor has spread over the girl like the ripe flower of the fine-stemmed peerkku, alas, while even the bees, knowing no better, take her eyes for flowers and come to them when she enters their lovely forest.","n":"The pallor and the eyes the bees mistake for flowers are set side by side: her beauty is fading and still draws the bees, so the time to marry her is now. The herons' fish smell on the jackfruit slope, which the monkey cannot bear, may suggest gossip reaching the hill, but no second correspondence is anchored in the poem, so it stays scene (a near miss for ullurai).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","pasalai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 326","lb":"NT326","poet":"மதுரை மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகனை வரைவுகடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவுகடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பலவின்","பலா",1,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["கொக்கினம்","கொக்கு",2,"pul","scene","heron"],["மீன்","மீன்",3,"pira","scene",""],["மந்தி","மந்தி",4,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["பீரத்து","பீரம்",6,"maram","simile",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",9,"pira","scene",""],["மலர்","மலர்",10,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:327":{"book":"natrinai","num":327,"t":["நாடல் சான்றோர் நம்புதல் பழி எனின்","பாடு இல கலுழும் கண்ணொடு சாஅய்ச்","சாதலும் இனிதே காதல்அம் தோழி","அந் நிலை அல்லஆயினும் சான்றோர்","கடன் நிலை குன்றலும் இலர் என்று உடன் அமர்ந்து","உலகம் கூறுவது உண்டு என நிலைஇய","தாயம் ஆகலும் உரித்தே போது அவிழ்","புன்னை ஓங்கிய கானற்","தண்ணம் துறைவன் சாயல் மார்பே"],"p":"","r":"Answering her friend's reassurance, the girl says that if trusting the sought-after noble ones is a fault, then wasting away with eyes that weep without rest, and even dying, is sweet, dear friend; and if it is not so, since the noble never fall short of what they owe, it is right that the soft chest of the lord of the cool shore, where punnai with opening buds stands over the grove, should become her settled inheritance, which the world gathered together will acknowledge.","n":"She puts it as all or nothing: either her trust was a fault and death is sweet, or he is truly one of the noble and his chest is her lawful portion. The word தாயம், inheritance, gives the marriage she wants the standing of a property claim acknowledged in public.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","saandror","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 327","lb":"NT327","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரையாது நெடுங்காலம் வந்தொழுக ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகள் வன்புறை எதிர் அழிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வன்புறை எதிர் அழிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",8,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:328":{"book":"natrinai","num":328,"t":["கிழங்கு கீழ் வீழ்ந்து தேன் மேல் தூங்கி","சிற்சில வித்திப் பற்பல விளைந்து","தினை கிளி கடியும் பெருங் கல் நாடன்","பிறப்பு ஓரன்மை அறிந்தனம் அதனால்","அது இனி வாழி தோழி ஒரு நாள்","சிறு பல் கருவித்து ஆகி வலன் ஏர்பு","பெரும் பெயல் தலைக புனனே இனியே","எண் பிழி நெய்யொடு வெண் கிழி வேண்டாது","சாந்து தலைக்கொண்ட ஓங்கு பெருஞ் சாரல்","விலங்கு மலை அடுக்கத்தானும்","கலம் பெறு விறலி ஆடும் இவ் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"Comforting the girl in the wait before marriage, the friend says they have come to know that the lord of the great hill land, where tubers go down and honey hangs above and a little sown yields much as people drive parrots from the millet, is not of a birth apart from theirs; so may the clouds one day gather and rise to the right and pour great rain and flood; and now, in this town on the high sandal-covered slope among cross-lying ranges, the virali who receives ornaments dances, asking for no sesame oil or white cloth.","n":"The hill where little sown yields much reads as a promise for the union, and the dancing virali points to the wedding festival the friend says is near; but the sense of பிறப்பு ஓரன்மை (line 4) and why she wishes for the flood are hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kudi","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 328","lb":"NT328","poet":"தொல் கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி வரைவிடை ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகளை வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வரைவிடை ஆற்றாளாகிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கிழங்கு","கிழங்கு",1,"unavu","scene",""],["தேன்","தேன்",1,"unavu","scene","honey"],["தினை","தினை",3,"unavu","scene",""],["கிளி","கிளி",3,"pul","scene",""],["சாந்து","சாந்து",9,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["விறலி","விறலி",11,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:329":{"book":"natrinai","num":329,"t":["வரையா நயவினர் நிரையம் பேணார்","கொன்று ஆற்றுத் துறந்த மாக்களின் அடு பிணன்","இடு முடை மருங்கில் தொடும் இடம் பெறாஅது","புனிற்று நிரை கதித்த பொறிய முது பாறு","இறகு புடைத்து இற்ற பறைப் புன் தூவி","செங் கணைச் செறித்த வன்கண் ஆடவர்","ஆடு கொள் நெஞ்சமோடு அதர் பார்த்து அல்கும்","அத்தம் இறந்தனர் ஆயினும் நத் துறந்து","அல்கலர் வாழி தோழி உதுக் காண்","இரு விசும்பு அதிர மின்னி","கருவி மா மழை கடல் முகந்தனவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend comforts the wasting wife: though he has crossed the wasteland where greedy men who care nothing for hell kill travellers and leave them, where an old spotted vulture, finding no room by the reeking corpses, flaps out the broken down of its wings, and hard-eyed men whose red arrows are fletched with it lie watching the track with their hearts set on plunder, he will not stay away forsaking her; look there, the dark clouds have drawn up the sea and flash, shaking the vast sky.","n":"The friend sets the road's horrors and the season's sign side by side, so that the rain clouds, the time he named for his return, answer the fear the road raises.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 329","lb":"NT329","poet":"மதுரை மருதங்கிழார் மகனார் சொகுத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகளைத் தோழி பருவம் காட்டி வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,null,"பருவம் காட்டி வற்புறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பாறு","பாறு",4,"pul","scene","vulture"],["ஆடவர்","ஆடவர்",6,"seythi","scene",""],["மழை","மழை",11,"pira","scene","rain cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:330":{"book":"natrinai","num":330,"t":["தட மருப்பு எருமைப் பிணர்ச் சுவல் இரும் போத்து","மட நடை நாரைப் பல் இனம் இரிய","நெடு நீர்த் தண் கயம் துடுமெனப் பாய்ந்து","நாட் தொழில் வருத்தம் வீட சேண் சினை","இருள் புனை மருதின் இன் நிழல் வதியும்","யாணர் ஊர நின் மாண் இழை மகளிரை","எம் மனைத் தந்து நீ தழீஇயினும் அவர்தம்","புன் மனத்து உண்மையோ அரிதே அவரும்","பைந் தொடி மகளிரொடு சிறுவர்ப் பயந்து","நன்றி சான்ற கற்பொடு","எம் பாடு ஆதல் அதனினும் அரிதே"],"p":"","r":"Refusing the man entry, the friend tells the lord of the rich town, where a wide-horned rough-necked dark male buffalo plunges with a splash into the deep cool pond, scattering the many flocks of soft-stepping herons, and rests from the day's labour in the sweet shade of the marutham tree whose far branches are clothed in darkness, that even if he brings his finely-jewelled women into their house and embraces them, truth in their mean hearts is rare, and rarer still that they should bear sons as the green-bangled wives do and stand on the household's side in good and grateful chastity.","n":"The friend grants the worst, that he brings the other women home, and argues past it: what she denies them is the household's virtues, truth, children and karpu, so the refusal is a judgement on them more than a plea to him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 330","lb":"NT330","poet":"ஆலங்குடி வங்கனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகனை வாயில் மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","வாயில் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["எருமைப்","எருமை",1,"maa","ullurai","buffalo"],["நாரைப்","நாரை",2,"pul","ullurai","heron"],["மருதின்","மருது",5,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","எருமைப் நாரைப்","confirmed","1/1","the man, his other women and the household he disturbs"]],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:331":{"book":"natrinai","num":331,"t":["உவர் விளை உப்பின் உழாஅ உழவர்","ஒழுகை உமணர் வரு பதம் நோக்கி","கானல் இட்ட காவற் குப்பை","புலவு மீன் உணங்கல் படு புள் ஓப்பி","மட நோக்கு ஆயமொடு உடன் ஊர்பு ஏறி","எந்தை திமில் இது நுந்தை திமில் என","வளை நீர் வேட்டம் போகிய கிளைஞர்","திண் திமில் எண்ணும் தண் கடற் சேர்ப்ப","இனிதேதெய்ய எம் முனிவு இல் நல் ஊர்","இனி வரின் தவறும் இல்லை எனையதூஉம்","பிறர் பிறர் அறிதல் யாவது","தமர் தமர் அறியாச் சேரியும் உடைத்தே"],"p":"","r":"Agreeing to a night meeting, the friend tells the lord of the cool sea, where salt-makers who never plough keep guarded heaps of brine-grown salt in the grove for the coming of the salt carters, and girls scaring birds from the drying fish climb up with their soft-eyed companions and count the strong boats of the kinsmen gone fishing, 'that is my father's boat, that is yours', that their faultless good town is sweet: if he comes now there is no wrong in it, for how would anyone come to know, when the town has streets where kin do not know kin?","n":"The girls naming each boat by its owner show a town where everyone is known by family; the friend's last line turns it round, saying the town is so crowded that kin do not know kin, so a night visitor will pass unknown.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 331","lb":"NT331","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி இரவுக்குறி நேர்ந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி நேர்ந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["உப்பின்","உப்பு",1,"pira","scene",""],["உழவர்","உழவர்",1,"seythi","scene","salt-makers"],["உமணர்","உமணர்",2,"seythi","scene",""],["மீன்","மீன்",4,"pira","scene",""],["புள்","புள்",4,"pul","scene",""],["திமில்","திமில்",6,"pira","scene","boat"],["கிளைஞர்","கிளைஞர்",7,"seythi","scene","fishermen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:332":{"book":"natrinai","num":332,"t":["இகுளை தோழி இஃது என் எனப்படுமோ","குவளை குறுநர் நீர் வேட்டாங்கு","நாளும்நாள் உடன் கவவவும் தோளே","தொல் நிலை வழீஇய நின் தொடி எனப் பல் மாண்","உரைத்தல் ஆன்றிசின் நீயே விடர் முகை","ஈன் பிணவு ஒடுக்கிய இருங் கேழ் வயப் புலி","இரை நசைஇப் பரிக்கும் மலைமுதல் சிறு நெறி","தலைநாள் அன்ன பேணலன் பல நாள்","ஆர் இருள் வருதல் காண்பேற்கு","யாங்கு ஆகும்மே இலங்கு இழை செறிப்பே"],"p":"","r":"To her friend, who keeps telling her in many ways that her bangles have slipped from their old place, the girl says to stop and asks what this can be called: like people who gather water-lilies and still thirst for water, her shoulders waste though he embraces her day after day; and how can her shining bangles stay tight when she sees him come through thick darkness night after night, no longer heeding as on the first day, by the small path at the mountain foot where a dark strong tiger that has sheltered its newly delivered mate in a rock cleft prowls for prey?","n":"The wasting is not from his absence but from fear for him: she has him every day and still pines, because every meeting means the tiger's path at night. பேணலன் may mean that he no longer heeds the danger or that he cares for her less than on the first day; the reading takes the first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","kalaviyal","acham","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 332","lb":"NT332","poet":"குன்றூர்கிழார் மகன் கண்ணத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகளைத் தோழி களவுக் காலத்து வற்புறுப்பதலைவி கூறியது வன்புறை எதிர்மறுத்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","களவுக் காலத்து"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வன்புறை எதிர்மறுத்ததூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["குவளை","குவளை",2,"maram","simile","water-lily"],["பிணவு","பிணவு",6,"maa","scene","tigress"],["புலி","புலி",6,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:333":{"book":"natrinai","num":333,"t":["மழை தொழில் உலந்து மா விசும்பு உகந்தென","கழை கவின் அழிந்த கல் அதர்ச் சிறு நெறிப்","பரல் அவல் ஊறல் சிறு நீர் மருங்கின்","பூ நுதல் யானையொடு புலி பொருது உண்ணும்","சுரன் இறந்து அரிய என்னார் உரன் அழிந்து","உள் மலி நெஞ்சமொடு வண்மை வேண்டி","அரும் பொருட்கு அகன்ற காதலர் முயக்கு எதிர்ந்து","திருந்திழைப் பணைத் தோள் பெறுநர் போலும்","நீங்குகமாதோ நின் அவலம் ஓங்குமிசை","உயர் புகழ் நல் இல் ஒண் சுவர்ப் பொருந்தி","நயவரு குரல பல்லி","நள்ளென் யாமத்து உள்ளுதொறும் படுமே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the grieving wife to let her sorrow go: her lover, who went far for rare wealth wanting the means to give, and who does not call hard the wasteland where, after the clouds have ended their work and the bamboo has lost its beauty along the stony path, a tiger fights the spot-browed elephant to drink at the little seep in a pebbled hollow, now seems, his resolve worn down and his heart full, ready to come back to her embrace and have her fine-jewelled bamboo shoulders; for the lizard of pleasing voice, clinging to the bright wall of their high famous house, chirps at deep midnight each time she thinks of him.","n":"The comfort rests on a household omen, not on news: the lizard answering each of her thoughts is taken to show that the absent man is turning home. The motive the friend gives him is வண்மை, wealth sought so that he can give.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","eegai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 333","lb":"NT333","poet":"கள்ளிக் குடிப் பூதம் புல்லனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிவின்கண் ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பொருள்வயிற் பிரிவின்கண்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கழை","கழை",2,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["யானையொடு","யானை",4,"maa","scene",""],["புலி","புலி",4,"maa","scene",""],["பல்லி","பல்லி",11,"pira","scene","house lizard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:334":{"book":"natrinai","num":334,"t":["கரு விரல் மந்திச் செம் முகப் பெருங் கிளை","பெரு வரை அடுக்கத்து அருவி ஆடி","ஓங்கு கழை ஊசல் தூங்கி வேங்கை","வெற்பு அணி நறு வீ கற்சுனை உறைப்ப","கலையொடு திளைக்கும் வரைஅக நாடன்","மாரி நின்ற ஆர் இருள் நடு நாள்","அருவி அடுக்கத்து ஒரு வேல் ஏந்தி","மின்னு வசி விளக்கத்து வருமெனின்","என்னோ தோழி நம் இன் உயிர் நிலையே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the girl what would become of their dear life if the lord of the hill land, where a big red-faced troop of black-fingered female monkeys bathe in the falls of the high range, swing on tall bamboo and frolic with their male while the fragrant vengai blossoms that adorn the hill drop into the rock pool, should come at midnight in the dense dark of lingering rain, bearing a single spear along the slope of falls by the light of the splitting lightning.","n":"The colophon says the friend opens the matter of a night meeting; she does it by voicing her fear of it, so that the girl's consent and the man's danger are raised in one breath.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kuri","kalaviyal","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 334","lb":"NT334","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி இரவுக்குறி முகம் புக்கது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி முகம் புக்கது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மந்திச்","மந்தி",1,"maa","iraicchi","female monkey"],["கழை","கழை",3,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",3,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["கலையொடு","கலை",5,"maa","iraicchi","male monkey"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","கலையொடு","confirmed","1/1","the lovers of this hill should have their joy together openly, as the monkeys do, not by a lone night journey through rain and lightning"]],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:335":{"book":"natrinai","num":335,"t":["திங்களும் திகழ் வான் ஏர்தரும் இமிழ் நீர்ப்","பொங்கு திரைப் புணரியும் பாடு ஓவாதே","ஒலி சிறந்து ஓதமும் பெயரும் மலி புனற்","பல் பூங் கானல் முள் இலைத் தாழை","சோறு சொரி குடையின் கூம்பு முகை அவிழ","வளி பரந்து ஊட்டும் விளிவு இல் நாற்றமொடு","மை இரும் பனைமிசைப் பைதல உயவும்","அன்றிலும் என்புற நரலும் அன்றி","விரல் கவர்ந்து உழந்த கவர்வின் நல் யாழ்","யாமம் உய்யாமை நின்றன்று","காமம் பெரிதே களைஞரோ இலரே"],"p":"","r":"Overcome by longing, the girl says the moon rises in the shining sky, the surging waves of the roaring sea never stop their sound, the tide comes loud and goes, the wind spreads the unending fragrance of the thorn-leaved thaazhai in the flowered grove as its closed bud opens like an umbrella spilling rice, the anril on the dark palm calls in distress so that it pierces to the bone, and besides, a good lute plucked by grasping fingers keeps on so that the night cannot be survived: love is great, and there is no one to clear it away.","n":"Every sound and scent of the shore night presses on her at once, and she names no man: the poem is the night itself closing in, ending in the bare statement that love is great.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 335","lb":"NT335","poet":"வெள்ளிவீதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"காமம் மிக்க கழிபடர்கிளவி மிதூர்ந்து தலைமகள் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","pira",null,null,"கழிபடர்கிளவி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தாழை","தாழை",4,"maram","scene","screwpine"],["சோறு","சோறு",5,"unavu","simile",""],["பனைமிசைப்","பனை",7,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["அன்றிலும்","அன்றில்",8,"pul","scene",""],["யாழ்","யாழ்",9,"yaazh","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:336":{"book":"natrinai","num":336,"t":["பிணர்ச் சுவற் பன்றி தோல்முலைப் பிணவொடு","கணைக் கால் ஏனல் கைம்மிகக் கவர்தலின்","கல் அதர் அரும் புழை அல்கி கானவன்","வில்லின் தந்த வெண் கோட்டு ஏற்றை","புனை இருங் கதுப்பின் மனையோள் கெண்டி","குடி முறை பகுக்கும் நெடு மலை நாட","உரவுச் சின வேழம் உறு புலி பார்க்கும்","இரவின் அஞ்சாய் அஞ்சுவல் அரவின்","ஈர் அளைப் புற்றம் கார் என முற்றி","இரை தேர் எண்கினம் அகழும்","வரை சேர் சிறு நெறி வாராதீமே"],"p":"","r":"Fearing the path, the speaker tells the lord of the tall mountain, where a hunter lying in wait at the hard narrow stony pass brings down with his bow the white-tusked boar that raided the thick-stalked millet with its slack-teated sow, and his wife of dressed dark hair cuts it up and shares it among the households in turn: you do not fear the night when the fierce elephant watches for the tiger, but I do; do not come by the small path at the mountain foot where a pack of bears hunting food dig into the snake's wet-holed mound.","n":"The opening is a raid ending in a fair share: the boar that took the millet is killed and divided by custom. A reader may see the secret lover and the town's sharing of talk in it, but the poem anchors no such match, so it is left as scene (a near miss for ullurai).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kuri","acham","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 336","lb":"NT336","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"ஆறு பார்த்துற்றுச்சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","ஆறு பார்த்துற்றுச்சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["பன்றி","பன்றி",1,"maa","scene","boar"],["பிணவொடு","பிணவு",1,"maa","scene","sow"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",2,"maram","scene","millet"],["கானவன்","கானவன்",3,"seythi","scene",""],["ஏற்றை","ஏற்றை",4,"maa","gift_food","boar"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",7,"maa","scene",""],["புலி","புலி",7,"maa","scene",""],["அரவின்","அரவு",8,"pira","scene","snake"],["புற்றம்","புற்று",9,"pira","scene","termite mound"],["எண்கினம்","எண்கு",10,"maa","scene","bear"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:337":{"book":"natrinai","num":337,"t":["உலகம் படைத்த காலை தலைவ","மறந்தனர்கொல்லோ சிறந்திசினோரே","முதிரா வேனில் எதிரிய அதிரல்","பராரைப் பாதிரிக் குறு மயிர் மா மலர்","நறு மோரோடமொடு உடன் எறிந்து அடைச்சிய","செப்பு இடந்தன்ன நாற்றம் தொக்கு உடன்","அணி நிறம் கொண்ட மணி மருள் ஐம் பால்","தாழ் நறுங் கதுப்பில் பையென முள்கும்","அரும் பெறல் பெரும் பயம் கொள்ளாது","பிரிந்து உறை மரபின பொருள் படைத்தோரே"],"p":"","r":"Seeing that the man means to leave for wealth, the friend asks whether the great ones forgot, when the world was made: those who have made wealth have the custom of living apart, not taking the rare great gift of gently burrowing into her long fragrant five-plaited hair, coloured like sapphire, where the scents of early-summer athiral, the soft-haired dark flowers of thick-based paathiri and fragrant morodam gather as from an opened casket.","n":"The complaint aims past the man at the order of the world: she blames whoever fixed custom for a rule under which wealth is bought with the very pleasure it should serve.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 337","lb":"NT337","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகன் பொருள்வயிற் பிரிதலுற்றானது குறிப்பறிந்து விலக்கியது தோழி உலகியல் கூறிப் பிரிவு உணர்த்தியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","பொருள்வயிற் பிரிதலுற்றானது குறிப்பறிந்து விலக்கியது"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","உலகியல் கூறிப் பிரிவு உணர்த்தியதூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அதிரல்","அதிரல்",3,"maram","ornament",""],["பாதிரிக்","பாதிரி",4,"maram","ornament",""],["மோரோடமொடு","மோரோடம்",5,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:338":{"book":"natrinai","num":338,"t":["கடுங் கதிர் ஞாயிறு மலை மறைந்தன்றே","அடும்பு கொடி துமிய ஆழி போழ்ந்து அவர்","நெடுந் தேர் இன் ஒலி இரவும் தோன்றா","இறப்ப எவ்வம் நலியும் நின் நிலை","நிறுத்தல் வேண்டும் என்றி நிலைப்ப","யாங்ஙனம் விடுமோ மற்றே மால் கொள","வியல் இரும் பரப்பின் இரை எழுந்து அருந்துபு","புலவு நாறு சிறுகுடி மன்றத்து ஓங்கிய","ஆடு அரைப் பெண்ணைத் தோடு மடல் ஏறி","கொடு வாய்ப் பேடைக் குடம்பைச் சேரிய","உயிர் செலக் கடைஇப் புணர் துணைப்","பயிர்தல் ஆனா பைதல்அம் குருகே"],"p":"","r":"Answering her friend, who says she must hold firm against the grief that torments her, the wife asks how she can, when the harsh-rayed sun has gone behind the hill, the sweet sound of his tall chariot whose wheel cuts the adumbu creepers is not heard even by night, and, as dusk blurs everything, the pitiable heron that rose to feed on the wide dark sea flies as if its life would leave it to reach its curved-beaked mate's nest on the frond of the swaying palm in the commons of the fish-smelling hamlet, never ceasing to call its partner.","n":"The heron's homecoming is the reproach she cannot say outright: the bird keeps calling to its mate as it hurries back, and the chariot that should come at the same hour is silent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","nirai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 338","lb":"NT338","poet":"மதுரை ஆருலவியநாட்டு ஆலம்பேரிசாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"ஒருவழித் தணந்த காலை ஆற்றாத தலைமகள் வன்புறை எதிர்மொழிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"வன்புறை எதிர்மொழிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["அடும்பு","அடும்பு",2,"maram","scene",""],["பெண்ணைத்","பெண்ணை",9,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["பேடைக்","பேடை",10,"pul","iraicchi","female heron"],["குருகே","குருகு",12,"pul","iraicchi","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","குருகே","confirmed","1/1","even the heron hurries home at nightfall to the mate it calls, while her man, gone only a short way, does not come"]],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:339":{"book":"natrinai","num":339,"t":["தோலாக் காதலர் துறந்து நம் அருளார்","அலர்வது அன்றுகொல் இது என்று நன்றும்","புலரா நெஞ்சமொடு புதுவ கூறி","இருவேம் நீந்தும் பருவரல் வெள்ளம்","அறிந்தனள்போலும் அன்னை சிறந்த","சீர் கெழு வியல் நகர் வருவனள் முயங்கி","நீர் அலைக் கலைஇய ஈர் இதழ்த் தொடையல்","ஒள் நுதல் பெதும்பை நல் நலம் பெறீஇ","மின் நேர் ஓதி இவளொடு நாளை","பல் மலர் கஞலிய வெறி கமழ் வேலித்","தெண் நீர் மணிச் சுனை ஆடின்","என்னோ மகளிர்தம் பண்பு என்றோளே"],"p":"","r":"So that the man standing by may hear, the friend tells the girl that their mother seems to know the flood of distress the two of them swim, saying strange things with unsettled hearts and fearing 'will this not turn to gossip?' now that the unfailing lover has left them and shows no grace: the mother came into the fine great house, embraced her and said, 'tomorrow, so that this lightning-haired, bright-browed young girl in her wet petalled garland may get back her good looks, let her bathe in the clear gem-water spring in the fragrant hedged place crowded with flowers; what is this way of girls?'","n":"The mother's plan of a bath to restore the girl's looks shows she has seen the change; whether she suspects love or illness is left open, and the friend reports it within the man's hearing so he knows time is short. Who embraces whom in line 6, and the tone of the mother's last question, are not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 339","lb":"NT339","poet":"சீத்தலைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைவன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைவன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தொடையல்","தொடையல்",7,"maram","ornament","garland"],["மலர்","மலர்",10,"maram","scene",""],["சுனை","சுனை",11,"pira","scene","spring"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:340":{"book":"natrinai","num":340,"t":["புல்லேன் மகிழ்ந புலத்தலும் இல்லேன்","கல்லா யானைக் கடுந் தேர்ச் செழியன்","படை மாண் பெருங் குள மடை நீர் விட்டென","கால் அணைந்து எதிரிய கணைக் கோட்டு வாளை","அள்ளல்அம் கழனி உள்வாய் ஓடி","பகடு சேறு உதைத்த புள்ளி வெண் புறத்து","செஞ் சால் உழவர் கோல் புடை மதரி","பைங் காற் செறுவின் அணைமுதல் பிறழும்","வாணன் சிறுகுடி அன்ன என்","கோள் நேர் எல் வளை நெகிழ்த்த நும்மே"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man, back from other women, that she will not embrace him nor even sulk: he loosened her bright well-fitting bangles, precious as Vaanan's Sirukudi, where, when Chezhiyan of the unschooled elephants and swift chariots lets out the sluice water of his great tank, the vaalai fish with arrow-like spines meets the inflow, runs into the mud fields and, bold, flips at the base of the ridges of the green-stalked fields, struck by the ploughmen's sticks on its spotted white back where the bullocks kicked up mud.","n":"Refusing both embrace and sulking is the coldest answer akam allows, since sulking would still be a lovers' game. The whole fertile town is spent on the bangles he has loosened, so what he has taken from her is measured by a king's wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 340","lb":"NT340","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தையிற் மறுத்தந்த தலைமகனைத் தலைமகள் நொந்து சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தலைமகனைத் தலைமகள் நொந்து சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானைக்","யானை",2,"maa","scene",""],["வாளை","வாளை",4,"pira","scene","vaalai fish"],["பகடு","பகடு",6,"maa","scene","bullock"],["உழவர்","உழவர்",7,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[["king","செழியன்",2],["patron","வாணன்",9],["town","சிறுகுடி",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:17"},"nt:341":{"book":"natrinai","num":341,"t":["வங்கா வரிப் பறைச் சிறு பாடு முணையின்","செம் பொறி அரக்கின் வட்டு நா வடிக்கும்","விளையாடு இன் நகை அழுங்கா பால் மடுத்து","அலையா உலவை ஓச்சி சில கிளையாக்","குன்றக் குறவனொடு குறு நொடி பயிற்றும்","துணை நன்கு உடையள் மடந்தை யாமே","வெம் பகை அரு முனைத் தண் பெயல் பொழிந்தென","நீர் இரங்கு அரை நாள் மயங்கி கூதிரொடு","வேறு புல வாடை அலைப்ப","துணை இலேம் தமியேம் பாசறையேமே"],"p":"","r":"Alone in the war camp on a hostile frontier, where cool rain has poured, water drips through half the night and the north wind of a strange land buffets him in the cold season, the man pictures the girl at home well companioned: tired of the vangaa bird's small song she plays with a red-spotted lac piece, drinks her milk without losing her laughter, waves a harmless twig and trades little riddles with the hill man and her kin, while he is without companion, alone, in the camp.","n":"The poem turns on the double துணை: she has companions in plenty (துணை நன்கு உடையள்), he has none (துணை இலேம்). Rather than imagine her pining, he envies her company, which makes his own loneliness the subject. Lines 1 to 4 describe her games in rare words (வட்டு நா வடிக்கும், அலையா உலவை), and their exact sense is not sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 341","lb":"NT341","poet":"மதுரை மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வினைவயிற் பிரிந்து ஆற்றானாகிய தலைமகன் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,null,"வினைவயிற் பிரிந்து ஆற்றானாகிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வங்கா","வங்கா",1,"pul","scene","vangaa bird"],["அரக்கின்","அரக்கு",2,"pira","ornament","lac"],["பால்","பால்",3,"unavu","gift_food","milk"],["உலவை","உலவை",4,"maram","scene","twig"],["குறவனொடு","குறவன்",5,"seythi","scene","hill man"],["பெயல்","பெயல்",7,"pira","scene","rain"],["வாடை","வாடை",9,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:342":{"book":"natrinai","num":342,"t":["மா என மதித்து மடல் ஊர்ந்து ஆங்கு","மதில் என மதித்து வெண் தேர் ஏறி","என் வாய் நின் மொழி மாட்டேன் நின் வயின்","சேரி சேரா வருவோர்க்கு என்றும்","அருளல் வேண்டும் அன்பு உடையோய் என","கண் இனிதாகக் கோட்டியும் தேரலள்","யானே எல்வளை யாத்த கானல்","வண்டு உண் நறு வீ நுண்ணிதின் வரித்த","சென்னிச் சேவடி சேர்த்தின்","என் எனப் படுமோ என்றலும் உண்டே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, who agreed to carry the man's plea, says to herself that she took his words into her mouth as one takes a palmyra stem for a horse and rides the madal, or a mirage for a wall and climbs it: she told the girl that one who has love must always be gracious to those who come to her from another street, put it sweetly, and still the girl would not take it; now she even thinks of laying at the girl's red feet his head, striped with the bee-sipped flowers of the shore grove, and wonders what would come of that.","n":"The madal, the man's last threat, is here the friend's own image for her errand: she has ridden a palm stem as if it were a horse. Taking the mirage (வெண் தேர்) for a wall is a second figure of the same self-deception. The last lines may mean she will bring him to fall at the girl's feet; the syntax of lines 7 to 10 is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["madal","thoothu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 342","lb":"NT342","poet":"மோசி கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"குறை நேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகளை முகம்புக்க தன் சொல் கேளாது விடலின் இறப்ப ஆற்றான் ஆயினான் என உணர்ந்து ஆற்றாளாய்த் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது தலைமகனுக்குக் குறை நேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகளை முகம் புக்கலளாய் ஆற்றாது தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nenju",null,"kalavu","குறை நேர்ந்த தோழி"],["thozhi","nenju",null,"kalavu","முகம் புக்கலளாய்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மா","மா",1,"maa","simile","horse"],["மடல்","மடல்",1,"maram","simile","palmyra stem"],["தேர்","வெண் தேர்",2,"pira","simile","mirage"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",8,"pira","scene",""],["வீ","வீ",8,"maram","ornament","flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:343":{"book":"natrinai","num":343,"t":["முல்லை தாய கல் அதர்ச் சிறு நெறி","அடையாது இருந்த அம் குடிச் சீறூர்த்","தாது எரு மறுகின் ஆ புறம் தீண்டும்","நெடு வீழ் இட்ட கடவுள் ஆலத்து","உகு பலி அருந்திய தொகு விரற் காக்கை","புன்கண் அந்திக் கிளைவயின் செறிய","படையொடு வந்த பையுள் மாலை","இல்லைகொல் வாழி தோழி நத்துறந்து","அரும் பொருட் கூட்டம் வேண்டிப்","பிரிந்து உறை காதலர் சென்ற நாட்டே"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend whether there is no such evening in the land where her lover went, leaving them to seek hard-won wealth: here, in the small village off the stony mullai-covered path, where cows rub their backs on the long-rooted banyan of the god in the dung-dusted street, the crow that ate the spilt offerings goes to crowd in with its kin in the sad dusk, and the grieving evening comes as if with an army.","n":"The question is whether evening exists where he is: if it did, he could not stay. The colophon files it under paalai for the parting, but nothing in the poem is of the road; the village, cows, mullai and evening are all mullai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 343","lb":"NT343","poet":"கருவூர்க் கதப்பிள்ளைச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"தலைமகள் பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,null,"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாய்ச்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["முல்லை","முல்லை",1,"maram","scene",""],["ஆ","ஆ",3,"maa","scene","cow"],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",4,"deyvam","scene",""],["ஆலத்து","ஆலம்",4,"maram","scene","banyan"],["பலி","பலி",5,"unavu","gift_food","offering"],["காக்கை","காக்கை",5,"pul","iraicchi","crow"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","காக்கை","confirmed","1/1","even the crow goes home to its own at evening, while the man stays away in the far land for wealth"]],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:344":{"book":"natrinai","num":344,"t":["அணி வரை மருங்கின் ஐது வளர்ந்திட்ட","மணி ஏர் தோட்ட மை ஆர் ஏனல்","இரும் பிடித் தடக் கையின் தடைஇய பெரும் புனம்","காவல் கண்ணினம்ஆயின் ஆயிழை","நம் நிலை இடை தெரிந்து உணரான் தன் மலை","ஆரம் நீவிய அணி கிளர் ஆகம்","சாரல் நீள் இடைச் சால வண்டு ஆர்ப்ப","செல்வன் செல்லும்கொல் தானே உயர் வரைப்","பெருங் கல் விடரகம் சிலம்ப இரும் புலி","களிறு தொலைத்து உரறும் கடி இடி மழை செத்து","செந் தினை உணங்கல் தொகுக்கும்","இன் கல் யாணர்த் தம் உறைவின் ஊர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening unseen, the friend tells the girl that if they are set to guard the great field of dark millet on the hill slope, its heads bent like a she-elephant's trunk, he will not learn how things stand with them; will he simply go home, bees humming along the long slope at his sandal-smeared chest, to his town of rich rocks, where people mistake the roar of a tiger that has killed an elephant in the echoing cave for thunder and gather up the red millet drying in the yard?","n":"Meant for the man's ears: once the millet is ripe the field-watch, and with it their meetings, will end. The closing scene, where a tiger's roar is taken for thunder and the drying millet is hurried in, is a near miss for ullurai (an alarm misread and a harvest gathered in might answer to gossip and the girl kept indoors), but neither pair is anchored in the poem, so it is scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 344","lb":"NT344","poet":"மதுரை அறுவை வாணிகன் இளவேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகத் தலைமகன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","சிறைப்புறமாகத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",2,"unavu","scene","millet"],["பிடித்","பிடி",3,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",6,"maram","ornament","sandal"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",7,"pira","scene",""],["புலி","புலி",9,"maa","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",10,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மழை","மழை",10,"pira","scene","thunder-cloud"],["தினை","தினை",11,"unavu","scene","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:345":{"book":"natrinai","num":345,"t":["கானற் கண்டல் கழன்று உகு பைங் காய்","நீல் நிற இருங் கழி உட்பட வீழ்ந்தென","உறு கால் தூக்க தூங்கி ஆம்பல்","சிறு வெண் காக்கை ஆவித்தன்ன","வெளிய விரியும் துறைவ என்றும்","அளிய பெரிய கேண்மை நும் போல்","சால்பு எதிர்கொண்ட செம்மையோரும்","தேறா நெஞ்சம் கையறுபு வாட","நீடின்று விரும்பார் ஆயின்","வாழ்தல் மற்று எவனோ தேய்கமா தெளிவே"],"p":"","r":"To the lord of the shore, where the green fruit of the kandal drops into the dark blue backwater and the water lily, swung by the strong wind, opens white like a small white gull yawning, the friend says: if even upright men like you, who have taken on nobility and whose friendship is always kind and great, delay and do not want her, so that her unassured heart withers helplessly, what is the use of living; let assurance perish.","n":"The curse falls on தெளிவு itself, the assurance he gave: if even the noble do not keep it, let trust perish. The falling fruit that sets the lily swinging open is a near miss for ullurai, with only one pair (his act, her opening), so it is scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 345","lb":"NT345","poet":"நம்பி குட்டுவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தௌ¢விடை விலங்கியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","விலங்கியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கண்டல்","கண்டல்",1,"maram","scene","mangrove"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",3,"maram","simile","water lily"],["காக்கை","காக்கை",4,"pul","simile","gull"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:346":{"book":"natrinai","num":346,"t":["குண கடல் முகந்து குடக்கு ஏர்பு இருளி","தண் கார் தலைஇய நிலம் தணி காலை","அரசு பகை நுவலும் அரு முனை இயவின்","அழிந்த வேலி அம் குடிச் சீறூர்","ஆள் இல் மன்றத்து அல்கு வளி ஆட்ட","தாள் வலி ஆகிய வன்கண் இருக்கை","இன்று நக்கனைமன் போலா என்றும்","நிறையுறு மதியின் இலங்கும் பொறையன்","பெருந் தண் கொல்லிச் சிறு பசுங் குளவிக்","கடி பதம் கமழும் கூந்தல்","மட மா அரிவை தட மென் தோளே"],"p":"","r":"The man, on the hard frontier road where kings speak of war, in a small village with its fence destroyed and its common empty and swept by lingering wind, in the season when cool rain clouds drawn from the eastern sea have darkened the west and cooled the land, tells his heart that it seemed to laugh today at the thought of the broad soft shoulders of his young wife, whose hair smells of the small green kulavi of the great cool Kolli of Porayan, who shines like the full moon.","n":"The rains of line 2 are mullai's season and the kulavi of Kolli a kurinji flower, so the road poem is mixed. Lines 6 and 7 (தாள் வலி ஆகிய வன்கண் இருக்கை / இன்று நக்கனைமன் போலா) are hard: the heart may have laughed once, or seem to laugh now, at the memory.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 346","lb":"NT346","poet":"எயினந்தை மகன் இளங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்த தலைமகன் ஆற்றானாய்த் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["குளவிக்","குளவி",9,"maram","ornament","wild jasmine"]],"en":[["king","பொறையன்",8],["hill","கொல்லி",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:347":{"book":"natrinai","num":347,"t":["முழங்கு கடல் முகந்த கமஞ் சூல் மா மழை","மாதிர நனந் தலை புதையப் பாஅய்","ஓங்கு வரை மிளிர ஆட்டி பாம்பு எறிபு","வான் புகு தலைய குன்றம் முற்றி","அழி துளி தலைஇய பொழுதில் புலையன்","பேழ் வாய்த் தண்ணுமை இடம் தொட்டன்ன","அருவி இழிதரும் பெரு வரை நாடன்","நீர் அன நிலையன் பேர் அன்பினன் எனப்","பல் மாண் கூறும் பரிசிலர் நெடுமொழி","வேனில் தேரையின் அளிய","காண வீடுமோ தோழி என் நலனே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend that the bards who praise in many ways the lord of the great hill, where in the rain the great cloud, heavy with the roaring sea, hides the directions, shakes the high peaks, strikes the snakes and pours, and the falls come down sounding like a drum struck on its left face by the drummer, calling him as steady as water and full of love, make grand speeches as pitiable as frogs in summer; will her beauty come back for the sight of such praise?","n":"The praise she scorns is the bards' (பரிசிலர்), the professional voices of puram; she turns their நெடுமொழி, the word for a hero's vaunt, into the croaking of frogs in a season with no rain, so the court's praise is weighed against a man who will not marry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","varaivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 347","lb":"NT347","poet":"பெருங்குன்றூர் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரையாது நெடுங்காலம் வந்தொழுக ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுக்க மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வற்புறுக்க மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மழை","மழை",1,"pira","scene","cloud"],["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",3,"pira","scene","snake"],["புலையன்","புலையன்",5,"seythi","simile","drummer"],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",6,"parai","simile",""],["அருவி","அருவி",7,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["தேரையின்","தேரை",10,"pira","simile","frog"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:348":{"book":"natrinai","num":348,"t":["நிலவே நீல் நிற விசும்பில் பல் கதிர் பரப்பி","பால் மலி கடலின் பரந்து பட்டன்றே","ஊரே ஒலி வரும் சும்மையொடு மலிபு தொகுபு ஈண்டி","கலி கெழு மறுகின் விழவு அயரும்மே","கானே பூ மலர் கஞலிய பொழில் அகம்தோறும்","தாம் அமர் துணையொடு வண்டு இமிரும்மே","யானே புனை இழை ஞெகிழ்த்த புலம்பு கொள் அவலமொடு","கனை இருங் கங்குலும் கண்படை இலெனே","அதனால் என்னொடு பொரும்கொல் இவ் உலகம்","உலகமொடு பொரும்கொல் என் அவலம் உறு நெஞ்சே"],"p":"","r":"The woman says the moon has spread its many rays across the blue sky like a sea full of milk, the town in its noisy crowds celebrates a festival in its busy streets, in every flower-thick grove the bees hum with the mates they love, and she alone, her jewels slipping, in lonely grief, has no sleep through the dense dark night; so is this world fighting her, or is her grieving heart fighting the world?","n":"Three ‑ஏ clauses (நிலவே, ஊரே, கானே) set out the full world before the fourth, யானே, sets her apart; the closing question will not settle whether she or the world is at fault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","iravu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 348","lb":"NT348","poet":"வெள்ளி வீதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வேட்கை பெருகத் தாங்கலளாய் ஆற்றாமை மீதூர்கின்றாள் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","nenju",null,null,"வேட்கை பெருகத் தாங்கலளாய்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["பூ","பூ",5,"maram","scene",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",6,"pira","iraicchi","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வண்டு","confirmed","1/1","even the bees have their partners beside them tonight, while she lies alone without hers"]],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:349":{"book":"natrinai","num":349,"t":["கடுந் தேர் ஏறியும் காலின் சென்றும்","கொடுங் கழி மருங்கின் அடும்பு மலர் கொய்தும்","கைதை தூக்கியும் நெய்தல் குற்றும்","புணர்ந்தாம் போல உணர்ந்த நெஞ்சமொடு","வைகலும் இனையம் ஆகவும் செய் தார்ப்","பசும் பூண் வேந்தர் அழிந்த பாசறை","ஒளிறு வேல் அழுவத்துக் களிறு படப் பொருத","பெரும் புண்ணுறுநர்க்குப் பேஎய் போல","பின்னிலை முனியா நம்வயின்","என் என நினையும்கொல் பரதவர் மகளே"],"p":"","r":"The man says to himself, the friend listening, that riding his swift chariot or going on foot, plucking adumbu flowers by the curving backwater, lifting the screw-pine and picking the neythal, his heart feeling as if they had already met, he is distressed day after day; and he asks what the fisher girl can think of him, who never tires of hanging back behind her like the ghoul that hovers by men gravely wounded in the camp's spear-bright battlefield, where crowned kings fell and elephants were killed.","n":"He gathers her shore's flowers as if gathering her, then sees himself as she must: a battlefield ghoul waiting by the wounded. The simile brings a whole puram scene into a love poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 349","lb":"NT349","poet":"மிளை கிழான் நல்வேட்டனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தலைமகன் தோழி கேட்பத் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju","thozhi","kalavu","தோழி கேட்பத் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["அடும்பு","அடும்பு",2,"maram","scene",""],["கைதை","கைதை",3,"maram","scene","screw-pine"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","scene",""],["களிறு","களிறு",7,"maa","simile","elephant"],["பேஎய்","பேய்",8,"pira","simile","ghoul"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",10,"seythi","scene","fishers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:350":{"book":"natrinai","num":350,"t":["வெண்ணெல் அரிநர் தண்ணுமை வெரீஇ","பழனப் பல் புள் இரிய கழனி","வாங்கு சினை மருதத் தூங்குதுணர் உதிரும்","தேர் வண் விராஅன் இருப்பை அன்ன என்","தொல் கவின் தொலையினும் தொலைக சார","விடேஎன் விடுக்குவென்ஆயின் கடைஇக்","கவவுக் கை தாங்கும் மதுகைய குவவு முலை","சாடிய சாந்தினை வாடிய கோதையை","ஆசு இல் கலம் தழீஇயற்று","வாரல் வாழிய கவைஇ நின்றோளே"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells the man that even if her old beauty, like Iruppai of Viraan of the generous chariots, where the reapers' drum in the white paddy scares the many birds of the field into flight and the hanging clusters of the bent marutham drop, should perish, let it; she will not let him near, for to embrace him, his sandal crushed by the round breasts of another and his garland withered, would be like clasping a vessel that gives no hold; do not come, and long life to the woman who held you.","n":"She stakes her beauty, which is a prosperous town, on the refusal: let it wane rather than let him in. The crushed sandal and withered garland are the evidence she reads off his body. ஆசு இல் கலம் is obscure: a vessel without a grip, or without a flaw, which would reverse the sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 350","lb":"NT350","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"தலைமகள் ஊடல் மறுத்தாள் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","ஊடல் மறுத்தாள்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வெண்ணெல்","வெண்ணெல்",1,"unavu","scene","white paddy"],["அரிநர்","அரிநர்",1,"seythi","scene","reapers"],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",1,"parai","scene",""],["புள்","புள்",2,"pul","scene",""],["மருதத்","மருதம்",3,"maram","scene",""],["சாந்தினை","சாந்து",8,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["கோதையை","கோதை",8,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[["patron","விராஅன்",4],["town","இருப்பை",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:351":{"book":"natrinai","num":351,"t":["இளமை தீர்ந்தனள் இவள் என வள மனை","அருங்கடிப் படுத்தனை ஆயினும் சிறந்து இவள்","பசந்தனள் என்பது உணராய் பல் நாள்","எவ்வ நெஞ்சமொடு தெய்வம் பேணி","வருந்தல் வாழி வேண்டு அன்னை கருந் தாள்","வேங்கைஅம் கவட்டிடைச் சாந்தின் செய்த","களிற்றுத் துப்பு அஞ்சாப் புலி அதள் இதணத்து","சிறு தினை வியன் புனம் காப்பின்","பெறுகுவள்மன்னோ என் தோழி தன் நலனே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that, saying the girl had outgrown childhood, she shut her in strict guard in the rich house, yet does not see that the girl has only grown paler; let her not toil for many days worshipping the gods with a troubled heart: if the girl guarded the wide field of small millet from the platform of tiger hide, fearless of the elephant's might, built of sandalwood in the fork of the black-trunked vengai, she would surely get her beauty back.","n":"The cure she prescribes is the cause: the millet field is where the girl meets the man. Her words tell the mother as much as can be told without confessing the affair.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kalaviyal","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 351","lb":"NT351","poet":"மதுரைக் கண்ணத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி அருகு அடுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","nartaay",null,"kalavu","அருகு அடுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தெய்வம்","தெய்வம்",4,"deyvam","scene",""],["வேங்கைஅம்","வேங்கை",6,"maram","scene",""],["சாந்தின்","சாந்து",6,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["களிற்றுத்","களிறு",7,"maa","scene","elephant"],["புலி","புலி",7,"maa","scene","tiger hide"],["தினை","தினை",8,"unavu","scene","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:352":{"book":"natrinai","num":352,"t":["இலை மாண் பகழிச் சிலை மாண் இரீஇய","அன்பு இல் ஆடவர் அலைத்தலின் பலருடன்","வம்பலர் தொலைந்த அஞ்சுவரு கவலை","அழல் போல் செவிய சேவல் ஆட்டி","நிழலொடு கதிக்கும் நிணம் புரி முது நரி","பச்சூன் கொள்ளை மாந்தி வெய்துற்று","தேர் திகழ் வறும் புலம் துழைஇ நீர் நயந்து","பதுக்கை நீழல் ஒதுக்கு இடம் பெறாஅ","அருஞ் சுரக் கவலை வருதலின் வருந்திய","நமக்கும் அரிய ஆயின அமைத் தோள்","மாண்புடைக் குறுமகள் நீங்கி","யாங்கு வந்தனள்கொல் அளியள் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Halfway through the waste, the man says to himself that on this fearful forked road, where loveless men with bows and leaf-bladed arrows have killed many travellers, the old fat-loving jackal, driving off the cock bird with fire-red ears, runs with its shadow, gorges on raw flesh, grows hot, searches the bare mirage-bright ground for water and finds no shelter even in the shade of the cairns; the road has been hard even for him, so how has she come, the fine young girl of bamboo shoulders, pitiable as she is?","n":"The closing question may mean she has come to him in thought across a road too hard for him, which is the likeliest reading, or that she followed him; the poem does not settle it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 352","lb":"NT352","poet":"மதுரைப் பள்ளிமருதங்கிழார் மகனார் சொகுத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்த தலைமகன் இடைச் சுரத்துக்கண் ஆற்றானாய்த் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"இடைச் சுரத்துக்கண் ஆற்றானாய்த் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["சேவல்","சேவல்",4,"pul","scene","cock bird"],["நரி","நரி",5,"maa","scene","jackal"],["பச்சூன்","பசு ஊன்",6,"unavu","gift_food","raw flesh"],["பதுக்கை","பதுக்கை",8,"pira","scene","cairn"],["அமைத்","அமை",10,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:353":{"book":"natrinai","num":353,"t":["ஆள் இல் பெண்டிர் தாளின் செய்த","நுணங்கு நுண் பனுவல் போல கணம் கொள","ஆடு மழை தவழும் கோடு உயர் நெடு வரை","முட முதிர் பலவின் குடம் மருள் பெரும் பழம்","கல் கெழு குறவர் காதல் மடமகள்","கரு விரல் மந்திக்கு வரு விருந்து அயரும்","வான் தோய் வெற்ப சான்றோய்அல்லை எம்","காமம் கனிவதுஆயினும் யாமத்து","இரும் புலி தொலைத்த பெருங் கை யானை","வெஞ் சின உருமின் உரறும்","அஞ்சுவரு சிறு நெறி வருதலானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the sky-touching hill, where clouds drift in flocks over the high peaks like the fine thread that husbandless women spin by hand, and the hill people's dear young daughter feasts the black-fingered female monkey on the pot-sized fruit of an old bent jackfruit tree as on an arriving guest, that he is not noble, even if their love ripens, because he comes at midnight on the fearful narrow path where the great elephant that killed a tiger roars like fierce thunder.","n":"சான்றோய் அல்லை is a hard word for a lover: his nobility is measured by whether he spares her the fear of his night journey.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kalaviyal","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 353","lb":"NT353","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி ஆற்றது அருமை அஞ்சி தான் ஆற்றாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","ஆற்றது அருமை அஞ்சி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["மழை","மழை",3,"pira","simile","cloud"],["பலவின்","பலா",4,"maram","scene","jackfruit tree"],["பழம்","பழம்",4,"unavu","gift_food",""],["குறவர்","குறவர்",5,"seythi","scene","hill people"],["மடமகள்","மடமகள்",5,"seythi","iraicchi","young daughter"],["மந்திக்கு","மந்தி",6,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["புலி","புலி",9,"maa","scene",""],["யானை","யானை",9,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","மடமகள்","not_confirmed","0/1","in this country a guest who comes openly is fed and made welcome, so the man should come as a guest by marriage, not by the fearful night path"]],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:354":{"book":"natrinai","num":354,"t":["தான் அது பொறுத்தல் யாவது கானல்","ஆடு அரை ஒழித்த நீடு இரும் பெண்ணை","வீழ் காவோலைச் சூழ் சிறை யாத்த","கானல் நண்ணிய வார் மணல் முன்றில்","எல்லி அன்ன இருள் நிறப் புன்னை","நல் அரை முழுமுதல் அவ் வயின் தொடுத்த","தூங்கல் அம்பித் தூவல் அம் சேர்ப்பின்","கடு வெயில் கொதித்த கல் விளை உப்பு","நெடு நெறி ஒழுகை நிரை செலப் பார்ப்போர்","அளம் போகு ஆகுலம் கடுப்ப","கௌவை ஆகின்றது ஐய நின் நட்பே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the man how the girl is to bear it, and tells him that at the spray-wet shore of the grove, with its yard of long sand near the grove fenced with fallen fronds of the tall dark palmyra and a small boat tied to the trunk of the punnai dark as night, his friendship has become gossip as loud as the din of the salt men watching their carts of sun-boiled salt move in rows down the long road.","n":"The long description is of the girl's own yard, now a place he cannot reach; the salt carts are the noise of trade, and his secret love has become as public as a market.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","varaivu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 354","lb":"NT354","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழியால் செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது மனைவயின் தோழியைத் தலைமகன் புகழ்ந்தாற்கு மறுத்துச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇ வரைவு கடாயது"],["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தலைமகன் புகழ்ந்தாற்கு மறுத்துச்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",2,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["காவோலைச்","காவோலை",3,"maram","scene","dry frond"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",5,"maram","scene",""],["அம்பித்","அம்பி",7,"pira","scene","boat"],["உப்பு","உப்பு",8,"pira","scene","salt"],["ஒழுகை","ஒழுகை",9,"pira","scene","cart train"],["பார்ப்போர்","பார்ப்போர்",9,"seythi","simile","salt traders"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:355":{"book":"natrinai","num":355,"t":["புதல்வன் ஈன்ற பூங் கண் மடந்தை","முலை வாய் உறுக்கும் கை போல் காந்தட்","குலைவாய் தோயும் கொழு மடல் வாழை","அம் மடல் பட்ட அருவித் தீம் நீர்","செம் முக மந்தி ஆரும் நாட","முந்தை இருந்து நட்டோர் கொடுப்பின்","நஞ்சும் உண்பர் நனி நாகரிகர்","அம் சில் ஓதி என் தோழி தோட் துயில்","நெஞ்சின் இன்புறாய்ஆயினும் அது நீ","என் கண் ஓடி அளிமதி","நின் கண் அல்லது பிறிது யாதும் இலளே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the land, where the kaandal cluster touches the plump banana leaf as a flower-eyed young mother who has borne a son presses her breast to his mouth, and the red-faced monkey drinks the sweet falls-water caught on the leaf, that the truly refined will drink even poison if friends of long standing offer it; even if he finds no joy at heart in sleeping on her friend's shoulders, let him be kind for her sake, for the girl has nothing but him.","n":"She asks him to act by கண் ஓடி, kindly regard, with the proverb of the poison as her proof: a refined man takes even what he does not want from a friend. The kaandal that guides the falls-water to the monkey like a mother's hand is a near miss for ullurai (the hand that offers maps to the friend who offers the girl, but no second pair holds), so it is a simile and scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","varaivu","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 355","lb":"NT355","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி அருகு அடுத்தது தோழி தலைமகளது ஆற்றாமை கண்டு வரைவு கடாயதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி அருகு அடுத்தது"],["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயதூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["காந்தட்","காந்தள்",2,"maram","simile",""],["வாழை","வாழை",3,"maram","scene","banana"],["அருவித்","அருவி",4,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",5,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["நஞ்சும்","நஞ்சு",7,"pira","gift_food","poison"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:356":{"book":"natrinai","num":356,"t":["நிலம் தாழ் மருங்கின் தெண் கடல் மேய்ந்த","விலங்கு மென் தூவிச் செங் கால் அன்னம்","பொன் படு நெடுங் கோட்டு இமயத்து உச்சி","வான் அரமகளிர்க்கு மேவல் ஆகும்","வளராப் பார்ப்பிற்கு அல்கு இரை ஒய்யும்","அசைவு இல் நோன் பறை போல செல வர","வருந்தினை வாழி என் உள்ளம் ஒரு நாள்","காதலி உழையளாக","குணக்குத் தோன்று வெள்ளியின் எமக்குமார் வருமே"],"p":"","r":"Refused the girl in marriage, the man tells his heart that it has toiled going and coming like the tireless strong wings of the red-legged swan with soft crossing feathers, which grazes in the clear sea where the land lies low and carries the gathered food to its unfledged chicks on the peak of the tall gold-bearing Himalaya, haunt of the celestial women; one day, with his beloved beside him, joy will come to them too, like the morning star rising in the east.","n":"The swan's flight spans the whole land, from the southern sea to the Himalaya, and it flies to feed young that cannot yet fly: the heart's errands are for a future household. The morning star is an omen of the good day.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 356","lb":"NT356","poet":"பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரைவு மறுக்கப்பட்டு ஆற்றானாகிய தலைமகன் தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","வரைவு மறுக்கப்பட்டு ஆற்றானாகிய"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["அன்னம்","அன்னம்",2,"pul","simile","swan"],["பார்ப்பிற்கு","பார்ப்பு",5,"pul","scene","chicks"]],"en":[["hill","இமயத்து",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:357":{"book":"natrinai","num":357,"t":["நின் குறிப்பு எவனோ தோழி என் குறிப்பு","என்னொடு நிலையாதுஆயினும் என்றும்","நெஞ்சு வடுப்படுத்துக் கெட அறியாதே","சேண் உறத் தோன்றும் குன்றத்துக் கவாஅன்","பெயல் உழந்து உலறிய மணிப் பொறிக் குடுமிப்","பீலி மஞ்ஞை ஆலும் சோலை","அம் கண் அறைய அகல் வாய்ப் பைஞ் சுனை","உண்கண் ஒப்பின் நீலம் அடைச்சி","நீர் அலைக் கலைஇய கண்ணிச்","சாரல் நாடனொடு ஆடிய நாளே"],"p":"","r":"The woman asks her friend what she means to do, and says that her own mind, though it does not stay with her, never fades but has scarred her heart: the day she played with the man of the slopes, wearing a garland of blue lilies like her kohl eyes, gathered from the wide green pool among the rocks of the grove on the far-seen hillside where the peacock with gem-spotted crest dances after drying from the rain, the garland loosened by the tossing water.","n":"The peacock that dances once it has dried after the rain is close to her own case, but it only shows a mood and does nothing a person of the situation does, so it is scene. The poet is a woman, Kurumakal Kuriyeyini.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 357","lb":"NT357","poet":"குறமகள் குறியெயினி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தலைமகன் வரைவு நீடிய இடத்து ஆற்றுவல் என்பது படச் சொல்லியது மனை மருண்டு வேறுபாடாயினாய் என்ற தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","ஆற்றுவல் என்பது படச் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","மனை மருண்டு வேறுபாடாயினாய் என்ற தோழிக்குத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",6,"pul","scene","peacock"],["சுனை","சுனை",7,"pira","scene","pool"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",8,"maram","ornament","blue lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:358":{"book":"natrinai","num":358,"t":["பெருந் தோள் நெகிழ அவ் வரி வாட","சிறு மெல் ஆகம் பெரும் பசப்பு ஊர","இன்னேம் ஆக எற் கண்டு நாணி","நின்னொடு தெளித்தனர் ஆயினும் என்னதூஉம்","அணங்கல் ஓம்புமதி வாழிய நீ என","கணம் கெழு கடவுட்கு உயர் பலி தூஉய்","பரவினம் வருகம் சென்மோ தோழி","பெருஞ் சேயிறவின் துய்த் தலை முடங்கல்","சிறு வெண் காக்கை நாள் இரை பெறூஉம்","பசும் பூண் வழுதி மருங்கை அன்ன என்","அரும் பெறல் ஆய் கவின் தொலைய","பிரிந்து ஆண்டு உறைதல் வல்லியோரே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells her friend: since he, ashamed to see her so, her broad shoulders slack, her fine lines fading and pallor spreading over her small soft chest, swore assurances to the friend, let them go scatter high offerings to the god who dwells with its host and pray, long life to you, never harm him in any way, though he is the one hard enough to stay away there while her rare beauty, like Marungai of Valuthi of the green jewels, where the small white gull gets its daily food of the curled-headed big red shrimp, fades.","n":"The god punishes broken oaths, so the woman, the one wronged, prays for the oath-breaker's safety. The prayer reassures the friend while it names his fault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 358","lb":"NT358","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பட்டபின்றை வரையாது பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்த காலத்து தோழி இவள் ஆற்றா ளாயினாள் இவளை இழந்தேன் எனக் கவன்றாள் வற்புறுத்தது அக்காலத்து ஆற்றாளாய் நின்ற தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","இவளை இழந்தேன் எனக் கவன்றாள் வற்புறுத்தது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கடவுட்கு","கடவுள்",6,"deyvam","scene",""],["பலி","பலி",6,"unavu","gift_food","offering"],["சேயிறவின்","சேயிறா",8,"pira","scene","red shrimp"],["காக்கை","காக்கை",9,"pul","scene","gull"]],"en":[["king","வழுதி",10],["town","மருங்கை",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:359":{"book":"natrinai","num":359,"t":["சிலம்பின் மேய்ந்த சிறு கோட்டுச் சேதா","அலங்கு குலைக் காந்தள் தீண்டி தாது உக","கன்று தாய் மருளும் குன்ற நாடன்","உடுக்கும் தழை தந்தனனே யாம் அஃது","உடுப்பின் யாய் அஞ்சுதுமே கொடுப்பின்","கேளுடைக் கேடு அஞ்சுதுமே ஆயிடை","வாடலகொல்லோ தாமே அவன் மலைப்","போருடை வருடையும் பாயா","சூருடை அடுக்கத்த கொயற்கு அருந் தழையே"],"p":"","r":"The friend says the lord of the hill land, where a small-horned red cow grazing the slope brushes the swaying kaandal and is so covered in pollen that her calf does not know her, has given them a leaf skirt to wear; if they wear it they fear the mother, if they give it back they fear hurting the kinsman; meanwhile will those leaves wither, so hard to pluck from his hill's god-haunted slopes where even the fighting mountain goats do not leap?","n":"The gift is precious because of where it grew: leaves from slopes even the goats will not climb. The friend's dilemma, between the mother and the man, is set out in two balanced clauses (உடுப்பின் ... கொடுப்பின்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 359","lb":"NT359","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தழையேற்றுக் கொண்டு நின்று தலைமகன் குறிப்பின் ஓடியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","தழையேற்றுக் கொண்டு நின்று"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["சேதா","சேதா",1,"maa","ullurai","red cow"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",2,"maram","ullurai",""],["கன்று","கன்று",3,"maa","ullurai","calf"],["தழை","தழை",4,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["வருடையும்","வருடை",8,"maa","scene","mountain goat"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","சேதா காந்தள் கன்று","confirmed","1/1","the girl marked by the man's gift, and her mother"]],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:360":{"book":"natrinai","num":360,"t":["முழவு முகம் புலர்ந்து முறையின் ஆடிய","விழவு ஒழி களத்த பாவை போல","நெருநைப் புணர்ந்தோர் புது நலம் வெளவி","இன்று தரு மகளிர் மென் தோள் பெறீஇயர்","சென்றீ பெரும சிறக்க நின் பரத்தை","பல்லோர் பழித்தல் நாணி வல்லே","காழின் குத்திக் கசிந்தவர் அலைப்ப","கைஇடை வைத்தது மெய்யிடைத் திமிரும்","முனியுடைக் கவளம் போல நனி பெரிது","உற்ற நின் விழுமம் உவப்பென்","மற்றும் கூடும் மனை மடி துயிலே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man to go and win the soft shoulders of the women brought today, having taken the fresh beauty of those he joined yesterday and left them like the doll in the festival ground after the festival, when the drums' faces have dried and the turns of dancing are done; may his courtesans flourish; she is glad at the great distress he has come to, which he wastes like the morsel that an elephant in a sulk, goaded with spikes by its keepers, smears over its body instead of eating; and the lazy sleep of the house will come again.","n":"The festival doll is the courtesans' fate, not the wife's: he uses each woman for a day. Lines 6 to 11 are hard; whose shame (பல்லோர் பழித்தல் நாணி) and whose sleep is meant is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 360","lb":"NT360","poet":"ஓரம்போகியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தையிற் பிரிந்த தலைமகனைத் தோழி தலைமகள் குறிப்பறிந்து வாயில் மறுத்தது தலைமகள் ஊடிச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","வாயில் மறுத்தது"],["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu","தலைமகள் ஊடிச் சொல்லியதூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["முழவு","முழவு",1,"parai","simile",""],["பாவை","பாவை",2,"pira","simile","doll"],["கவளம்","கவளம்",9,"unavu","simile","elephant's morsel"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:18"},"nt:361":{"book":"natrinai","num":361,"t":["சிறு வீ முல்லைப் பெரிது கமழ் அலரி","தானும் சூடினன் இளைஞரும் மலைந்தனர்","விசும்பு கடப்பன்ன பொலம் படைக் கலி மா","படு மழை பொழிந்த தண் நறும் புறவில்","நெடு நா ஒண் மணி பாடு சிறந்து இசைப்ப","மாலை மான்ற மணம் மலி வியல் நகர்த்","தந்தன நெடுந்தகை தேரே என்றும்","அரும் படர் அகல நீக்கி","விருந்து அயர் விருப்பினள் திருந்துஇழையோளே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, answering the go-betweens, says the tall man's chariot has come home at evening to the fragrant house: he and his young men wear the small-flowered mullai, his gold-harnessed horses run as if crossing the sky, his long-tongued bells ring through the rain-cooled scrub, and the woman of fine jewels, her long ache put away, is now eager to feast guests.","n":"The proof of the return is a garland, not a sight of the man: the friend tells the go-betweens that even his young men wear the mullai, so the waiting is over and their errand is not needed; the wife's wish to feast guests is the sign that the house is whole again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","karpu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 361","lb":"NT361","poet":"மதுரைப் பேராலவாயர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வாயில்களோடு தோழி உறழ்ந்து சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira",null,"karpu","வாயில்களோடு தோழி உறழ்ந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["முல்லைப்","முல்லை",1,"maram","ornament",""],["மா","மா",3,"maa","scene","horse"],["மணி","மணி",5,"pira","scene","bell"],["நகர்த்","நகர்",6,"pira","scene","house"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:362":{"book":"natrinai","num":362,"t":["வினை அமை பாவையின் இயலி நுந்தை","மனை வரை இறந்து வந்தனை ஆயின்","தலை நாட்கு எதிரிய தண் பத எழிலி","அணி மிகு கானத்து அகன் புறம் பரந்த","கடுஞ் செம்மூதாய் கண்டும் கொண்டும்","நீ விளையாடுக சிறிதே யானே","மழ களிறு உரிஞ்சிய பராரை வேங்கை","மணல் இடு மருங்கின் இரும் புறம் பொருந்தி","அமர் வரின் அஞ்சேன் பெயர்க்குவென்","நுமர் வரின் மறைகுவென் மாஅயோளே"],"p":"","r":"The eloping man tells the girl who moved like a crafted doll and crossed the bounds of her father's house that she may play a while, watching and gathering the red velvet mites spread over the forest that the first cool rains have adorned, while he leans on the broad trunk of a vengai rubbed by a young elephant: if a fight comes he will not fear but drive it off, and if her own people come he will hide.","n":"His courage has a precise limit: strangers he will fight, her kin he will hide from, because a blow against her people would end the marriage the flight is meant to make; the road is offered to her as a playground of red mites, not a waste.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","anjaamai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 362","lb":"NT362","poet":"மதுரை மருதன் இள நாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உடன்போகாநின்ற தலைமகன் தலைமகட்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","உடன்போகாநின்ற தலைமகன்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["எழிலி","எழிலி",3,"pira","scene","cloud"],["செம்மூதாய்","செம்மூதாய்",5,"pira","scene","velvet mite"],["களிறு","களிறு",7,"maa","scene","young elephant"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",7,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["மணல்","மணல்",8,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:363":{"book":"natrinai","num":363,"t":["கண்டல் வேலிக் கழி சூழ் படப்பைத்","தெண் கடல் நாட்டுச் செல்வென் யான் என","வியம் கொண்டு ஏகினைஆயின் எனையதூஉம்","உறு வினைக்கு அசாவா உலைவு இல் கம்மியன்","பொறி அறு பிணைக் கூட்டும் துறை மணல் கொண்டு","வம்மோ தோழி மலி நீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப","பைந் தழை சிதைய கோதை வாட","நன்னர் மாலை நெருநை நின்னொடு","சில விளங்கு எல் வளை ஞெகிழ","அலவன் ஆட்டுவோள் சிலம்பு ஞெமிர்ந்து எனவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the full-watered shore that if he takes leave saying he will go to his clear-sea country of backwater fields hedged with kandal, he should come back bringing the sand of the ford with which the tireless smith fuses broken links, for yesterday at fine evening, with him, her green leaf-skirt was torn, her garland faded, a few bright bangles slipped, and the anklet of the girl who made the crabs dance was crushed.","n":"The torn skirt, faded garland, loosened bangles and crushed anklet are the evidence of the day's meeting that the house will read; the friend's demand for the smith's fusing sand is a half-joking order to undo the damage before anyone sees it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 363","lb":"NT363","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பகற்குறி வந்து நீங்கும் தலைமகனைத் தோழி தலைமகளை என்னை ஆற்றுவிக்குமென்று ஆகாதோ எம்பெருமான் கவலாது செல்வது யான் ஆற்றுவிக்குமிடத்துக் கவன்றால் நீ ஆற்றுவி எனச் சொல்லியது கையுறை நேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகட்குக் கையுறை உரைத்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகற்குறி வந்து நீங்கும் தலைமகனை"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","கையுறை நேர்ந்த தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கண்டல்","கண்டல்",1,"maram","scene",""],["கம்மியன்","கம்மியன்",4,"seythi","scene","smith"],["மணல்","மணல்",5,"pira","scene","sand"],["தழை","தழை",7,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["கோதை","கோதை",7,"maram","ornament","garland"],["அலவன்","அலவன்",10,"pira","scene","crab"],["சிலம்பு","சிலம்பு",10,"pira","ornament","anklet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:364":{"book":"natrinai","num":364,"t":["சொல்லிய பருவம் கழிந்தன்று எல்லையும்","மயங்கு இருள் நடு நாள் மங்குலோடு ஒன்றி","ஆர் கலி வானம் நீர் பொதிந்து இயங்க","பனியின் வாடையொடு முனிவு வந்து இறுப்ப","இன்ன சில் நாள் கழியின் பல் நாள்","வாழலென் வாழி தோழி ஊழின்","உரும் இசை அறியாச் சிறு செந் நாவின்","ஈர் மணி இன் குரல் ஊர் நணி இயம்ப","பல் ஆ தந்த கல்லாக் கோவலர்","கொன்றைஅம் தீம் குழல் மன்றுதோறு இயம்ப","உயிர் செலத் துனைதரும் மாலை","செயிர் தீர் மாரியொடு ஒருங்கு தலைவரினே"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that the season he named is past, and at midnight the roaring sky moves heavy with water in the mist and the cold north wind brings misery; if a few more such days pass she will not live many more, when in turn the sweet bells with small red tongues that never knew thunder ring near the town, and the unlettered herdsmen who brought home the many cows play sweet kondrai flutes in every common, in the evening that hurries life away, if it comes together with the faultless rain.","n":"The thunder and the midnight storm she can bear; it is the softest sounds, bells too small to know thunder and the flutes of unlearned herdsmen, that she says will take her life, because they mark the evening hour of homecoming in which he does not come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 364","lb":"NT364","poet":"கிடங்கில் காவிதிப் பெருங் கொற்றனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"தலைமகள் பிரிவிடை மெலிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பிரிவிடை மெலிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["வாடையொடு","வாடை",4,"pira","scene","north wind"],["மணி","மணி",8,"pira","scene","cattle bell"],["ஆ","ஆ",9,"maa","scene","cows"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",9,"seythi","scene","herdsmen"],["கொன்றைஅம்","கொன்றை",10,"maram","scene",""],["குழல்","குழல்",10,"yaazh","scene","flute"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:365":{"book":"natrinai","num":365,"t":["அருங் கடி அன்னை காவல் நீவி","பெருங் கடை இறந்து மன்றம் போகி","பகலே பலரும் காண வாய் விட்டு","அகல் வயற் படப்பை அவன் ஊர் வினவி","சென்மோ வாழி தோழி பல் நாள்","கருவி வானம் பெய்யாதுஆயினும்","அருவி ஆர்க்கும் அயம் திகழ் சிலம்பின்","வான் தோய் மா மலைக் கிழவனை","சான்றோய் அல்லை என்றனம் வரற்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, with the hero standing unseen nearby, tells the heroine: let us slip mother's strict watch, pass the great door, go into the common and, in daylight before everyone, ask aloud the way to his town of wide fields, and tell the lord of the sky-touching hill, whose slopes roar with falls even when the clouds have not rained for many days, 'you are not a man of worth', and come back.","n":"The hill gives water when the sky gives none, and the reproach lies in the gap: its lord does not give even what is due. The threat is to make the secret public in daylight, the one step a woman of the house is not supposed to take; a near miss for ullurai, since only one pair (the hill's unfailing falls, his failing presence) can be named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","saandraanmai","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 365","lb":"NT365","poet":"கிள்ளிமங்கலம் கிழார் மகனார் சேர கோவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாக தலைமகட்கு உரைப்பாளாய் இயற்பழித்துஇன்னது செய்தும் என்பாளாய்ச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","இயற்பழித்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வானம்","வானம்",6,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["அருவி","அருவி",7,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:366":{"book":"natrinai","num":366,"t":["அரவுக் கிளர்ந்தன்ன விரவுறு பல் காழ்","வீடுறு நுண் துகில் ஊடு வந்து இமைக்கும்","திருந்துஇழை அல்குல் பெருந் தோட் குறுமகள்","மணி ஏர் ஐம்பால் மாசு அறக் கழீஇ","கூதிர் முல்லைக் குறுங் கால் அலரி","மாதர் வண்டொடு சுரும்பு பட முடித்த","இரும் பல் மெல் அணை ஒழிய கரும்பின்","வேல் போல் வெண் முகை விரியத் தீண்டி","முதுக் குறைக் குரீஇ முயன்று செய் குடம்பை","மூங்கில்அம் கழைத் தூங்க ஒற்றும்","வட புல வாடைக்குப் பிரிவோர்","மடவர் வாழி இவ் உலகத்தானே"],"p":"","r":"The man tells his heart, set on leaving for wealth, that those who part from the girl with great shoulders, whose fine cloth lets her many-stranded girdle gleam through like a rising snake, whose gem-dark hair is washed clean and tied with short-stemmed cold-season mullai visited by bees, and leave her soft bed in the north wind that opens the spear-white buds of the sugarcane and shakes the nest the wise sparrow toiled to hang from the bamboo, are fools, and long live this world.","n":"The argument is made by season rather than by road: the cold wind that would lash the traveller is the same wind that rocks a sparrow's hard-built nest, so the poem ends on the general maxim the colophon calls உலகியல், that leavers are fools.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 366","lb":"NT366","poet":"மதுரை ஈழத்துப் பூதன் தேவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உலகியல் கூறிப் பொருள்வயிற் பிரிய வலித்த நெஞ்சிற்குத் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"karpu","பொருள்வயிற் பிரிய வலித்த நெஞ்சிற்கு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["முல்லைக்","முல்லை",5,"maram","ornament",""],["வண்டொடு","வண்டு",6,"pira","scene","bee"],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",7,"maram","scene","sugarcane"],["குரீஇ","குரீஇ",9,"pul","iraicchi","sparrow"],["மூங்கில்அம்","மூங்கில்",10,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["வாடைக்குப்","வாடை",11,"pira","scene","north wind"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","குரீஇ","not_confirmed","0/1","even a small bird holds to the home it built when the cold wind blows, so a man who leaves his wife's bed in that wind is a fool"]],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:367":{"book":"natrinai","num":367,"t":["கொடுங் கண் காக்கைக் கூர் வாய்ப் பேடை","நடுங்கு சிறைப் பிள்ளை தழீஇ கிளை பயிர்ந்து","கருங் கண் கருனைச் செந்நெல் வெண் சோறு","சூருடைப் பலியொடு கவரிய குறுங் கால்","கூழுடை நல் மனைக் குழுவின இருக்கும்","மூதில் அருமன் பேர் இசைச் சிறுகுடி","மெல் இயல் அரிவை நின் பல் இருங் கதுப்பின்","குவளையொடு தொடுத்த நறு வீ முல்லைத்","தளை அவிழ் அலரித் தண் நறுங் கோதை","இளையரும் சூடி வந்தனர் நமரும்","விரி உளை நன் மாக் கடைஇ","பரியாது வருவர் இப் பனி படு நாளே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the soft girl: in the far-famed little village of Aruman's old house, where the sharp-beaked hen of the crooked-eyed crow, gathering her trembling-winged chick and calling her kin, snatches the white rice of red paddy with dark fried meat along with the offering to the fierce god and sits in flocks at the house rich in food, the young men have come wearing a cool garland of open mullai bound with blue lily, like the one in your thick dark hair, and our man too, driving fine horses with spread manes, will come without fail this dewy day.","n":"The crow hen gathering her chick and kin to the house's food is read traditionally as the omen of a coming guest; here it does not pass the act test cleanly (her feeding is not something the wife does or fails to do) and is left as scene, a near miss for iraicchi. The proof is again the retinue's garland.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","virundhu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 367","lb":"NT367","poet":"நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வரவு மலிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","வரவு மலிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["காக்கைக்","காக்கை",1,"pul","scene","crow"],["செந்நெல்","செந்நெல்",3,"unavu","gift_food",""],["சோறு","சோறு",3,"unavu","gift_food",""],["பலியொடு","பலி",4,"unavu","gift_food","offering"],["குவளையொடு","குவளை",8,"maram","ornament",""],["முல்லைத்","முல்லை",8,"maram","ornament",""],["மாக்","மா",11,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["patron","அருமன்",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:368":{"book":"natrinai","num":368,"t":["பெரும் புனம் கவரும் சிறு கிளி ஓப்பி","கருங் கால் வேங்கை ஊசல் தூங்கி","கோடு ஏந்து அல்குல் தழை அணிந்து நும்மொடு","ஆடினம் வருதலின் இனியதும் உண்டோ","நெறி படு கூழைக் கார் முதிர்பு இருந்த","வெறி கமழ் கொண்ட நாற்றமும் சிறிய","பசலை பாய்தரு நுதலும் நோக்கி","வறிது உகு நெஞ்சினள் பிறிது ஒன்று சுட்டி","வெய்ய உயிர்த்தனள் யாயே","ஐய அஞ்சினம் அளியம் யாமே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hero: what is sweeter than driving small parrots from the great field, swinging on the black-stemmed vengai and playing with you in leaf-skirts and coming home? But mother, seeing the wild fragrance in her coiled dark hair and the pallor spreading on her small forehead, her heart sinking for no clear reason, hinting at something else, sighed hot breaths; sir, we are afraid, we are to be pitied.","n":"The mother never accuses; she reads two signs, the strange scent in the hair and the pallor, says something else, and sighs, and that silence is what tells the friend the girl will be kept indoors. The joy of the first four lines exists to be lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","pasalai","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 368","lb":"NT368","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகற்குச் செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"],["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["புனம்","புனம்",1,"pira","scene","millet field"],["கிளி","கிளி",1,"pul","scene","parrot"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",2,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["தழை","தழை",3,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:369":{"book":"natrinai","num":369,"t":["சுடர் சினம் தணிந்து குன்றம் சேர","நிறை பறைக் குருகினம் விசும்பு உகந்து ஒழுக","எல்லை பைபயக் கழிப்பி முல்லை","அரும்பு வாய் அவிழும் பெரும் புன் மாலை","இன்றும் வருவது ஆயின் நன்றும்","அறியேன் வாழி தோழி அறியேன்","ஞெமை ஓங்கு உயர் வரை இமையத்து உச்சி","வாஅன் இழிதரும் வயங்கு வெள் அருவிக்","கங்கைஅம் பேர் யாற்றுக் கரை இறந்து இழிதரும்","சிறை அடு கடும் புனல் அன்ன என்","நிறை அடு காமம் நீந்துமாறே"],"p":"","r":"The heroine tells her friend that as the sun's anger cools and it reaches the hill, flocks of herons with full wings rise and stream across the sky, the day slowly passes and the mullai buds open their mouths in the great dim evening; if this comes again today she does not know, does not know, how she will swim across her love, which breaks her restraint as the fierce flood of the Ganga, falling from the shining white falls of the sky on the Himalaya's peak where ñemai trees rise, overruns its banks and breaks the dams.","n":"The simile is built as a pun: the flood breaks dams (சிறை அடு) and her love breaks restraint (நிறை அடு), so the far-off Ganga falling from the Himalaya measures a feeling at the evening shore. She does not ask how to stop the flood but how to swim it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nirai","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 369","lb":"NT369","poet":"மதுரை ஓலைக் கடையத்தார் நல்வெள்ளையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"பட்ட பின்றை வரையாது பொருள்வயிற் பிரிந்து ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகள் வன்புறை எதிர் அழிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வன்புறை எதிர் அழிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["குருகினம்","குருகு",2,"pul","scene","heron"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",3,"maram","scene",""],["ஞெமை","ஞெமை",7,"maram","scene",""],["அருவிக்","அருவி",8,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[["hill","இமையத்து",7],["river","கங்கைஅம்",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:370":{"book":"natrinai","num":370,"t":["வாராய் பாண நகுகம் நேரிழை","கடும்புடைக் கடுஞ் சூல் நம் குடிக்கு உதவி","நெய்யோடு இமைக்கும் ஐயவித் திரள் காழ்","விளங்கு நகர் விளங்கக் கிடந்தோட் குறுகி","புதல்வன் ஈன்றெனப் பெயர் பெயர்த்து அவ் வரித்","திதலை அல்குல் முது பெண்டு ஆகி","துஞ்சுதியோ மெல் அம் சில் ஓதி என","பல் மாண் அகட்டில் குவளை ஒற்றி","உள்ளினென் உறையும் எற் கண்டு மெல்ல","முகை நாண் முறுவல் தோற்றி","தகை மலர் உண்கண் கை புதைத்ததுவே"],"p":"","r":"The man, whose wife's sulking has gone on too long, says: come, bard, let us laugh; when she who had helped our clan with her heavy pregnancy among her kin lay in the bright house shining with mustard seed glistening with ghee, and, having borne a son, changed her name and became, with her lined waist, an 'old woman', I came near saying 'are you asleep, soft one of thin locks?', laid a blue lily on her lovely belly and stood thinking, and she saw me, slowly showed a smile shy as a bud, and covered her flower-like painted eyes with her hand.","n":"The joke he invites the bard to share is his wife's new title, 'old woman', given her by the birth of a son, and her shyness at being found by him in the lying-in room; in a marutham poem of oodal the tenderest scene is told as his evidence that she is still the bashful girl he married.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","makkal","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 370","lb":"NT370","poet":"உறையூர்க் கதுவாய்ச் சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"ஊடல் நீட ஆற்றானாய் நின்றான் பாணர்க்குச் சொல்லியது முன் நிகழ்ந்ததனைப் பாணர்க்குச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","paanan",null,"karpu","ஊடல் நீட ஆற்றானாய்"],["thalaivan","paanan",null,"karpu","முன் நிகழ்ந்ததனைப் பாணர்க்குச்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","மடன்"]],"k":[["நெய்யோடு","நெய்",3,"unavu","scene",""],["ஐயவித்","ஐயவி",3,"maram","scene","white mustard"],["குவளை","குவளை",8,"maram","ornament","blue lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:371":{"book":"natrinai","num":371,"t":["காயாங் குன்றத்துக் கொன்றை போல","மா மலை விடர் அகம் விளங்க மின்னி","மாயோள் இருந்த தேஎம் நோக்கி","வியல் இரு விசும்பு அகம் புதையப் பாஅய்","பெயல் தொடங்கினவே பெய்யா வானம்","நிழல் திகழ் சுடர்த் தொடி ஞெகிழ ஏங்கி","அழல் தொடங்கினளே ஆயிழை அதன் எதிர்","குழல் தொடங்கினரே கோவலர்","தழங்கு குரல் உருமின் கங்குலானே"],"p":"","r":"The man returning with his work done tells his charioteer: like kondrai on a hill of kaayaa, lightning flashes so that the clefts of the great mountain shine, and the cloud that had not rained, looking toward the land where his dark girl lives, has spread to cover the wide sky and begun to rain; she, her bright bangles slipping, sighs and has begun to burn; and against that the herdsmen have begun their flutes, in the night of roaring thunder.","n":"Three verbs of beginning (தொடங்கினவே, தொடங்கினளே, தொடங்கினரே) run in parallel: the rain begins, she begins to burn, the herdsmen begin to play; he imagines her evening at the very hour it starts, which is why the chariot must hurry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 371","lb":"NT371","poet":"ஒளவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி மறுத்தராநின்றான் பாகற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","pira",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றி மறுத்தராநின்றான்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["காயாங்","காயா",1,"maram","simile",""],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",1,"maram","simile",""],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",8,"seythi","scene","herdsmen"],["குழல்","குழல்",8,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["உருமின்","உரும்",9,"pira","scene","thunder"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:372":{"book":"natrinai","num":372,"t":["அழிதக்கன்றே தோழி கழி சேர்பு","கானற் பெண்ணைத் தேனுடை அழி பழம்","வள் இதழ் நெய்தல் வருந்த மூக்கு இறுபு","அள்ளல் இருஞ் சேற்று ஆழப் பட்டென","கிளைக் குருகு இரியும் துறைவன் வளைக் கோட்டு","அன்ன வெண் மணற்று அகவயின் வேட்ட","அண்ணல் உள்ளமொடு அமர்ந்து இனிது நோக்கி","அன்னை தந்த அலங்கல் வான் கோடு","உலைந்தாங்கு நோதல் அஞ்சி அடைந்ததற்கு","இனையல் என்னும் என்ப மனை இருந்து","இருங் கழி துழவும் பனித் தலைப் பரதவர்","திண் திமில் விளக்கம் எண்ணும்","கண்டல் வேலிக் கழி நல் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"The heroine, knowing she is to be kept indoors and speaking for the hero to overhear, tells her friend it is ruinous: on the backwater the honey-full overripe fruit of the grove's palm falls, crushing the broad-petalled neythal, its tip breaking, and sinks deep in the dark mire so the flock of herons flees, at the shore of the lord who, on the white sand like the curve of a conch, looked at her sweetly with a willing heart; and they say, fearing she would grieve as when the swaying white thing mother gave was broken, her people tell her not to grieve over what has come, in the good town hedged with kandal where cold-headed fishermen at home count the lights of the sturdy boats that search the dark backwater.","n":"A near miss for ullurai: the ripe palm fruit that crushes the neythal and scares off the herons invites the reading of a love that has fallen on her and set the town talking, but only one pair can be named with confidence and the poem does not anchor it. Lines 8 to 10 are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","acham","alar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 372","lb":"NT372","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"மேல் இற்செறிப்பான் அறிந்து ஆற்றாளாகி நின்ற தலைமகள் ஆற்ற வேண்டி உலகியல் மேல் வைத்துச் சிறைப்புறமாகச் செறியார் எனச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","இற்செறிப்பான் அறிந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["பெண்ணைத்","பெண்ணை",2,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["பழம்","பழம்",2,"maram","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",3,"maram","scene",""],["குருகு","குருகு",5,"pul","scene","heron"],["வளைக்","வளை",5,"pira","simile","conch"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",11,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["திமில்","திமில்",12,"pira","scene","boat"],["கண்டல்","கண்டல்",13,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:373":{"book":"natrinai","num":373,"t":["முன்றிற் பலவின் படு சுளை மரீஇ","புன் தலை மந்தி தூர்ப்ப தந்தை","மை படு மால் வரை பாடினள் கொடிச்சி","ஐவன வெண்ணெல் குறூஉம் நாடனொடு","சூருடைச் சிலம்பின் அருவி ஆடி","கார் அரும்பு அவிழ்ந்த கணி வாய் வேங்கைப்","பா அமை இதணம் ஏறி பாசினம்","வணர் குரற் சிறு தினை கடிய","புணர்வதுகொல்லோ நாளையும் நமக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether it will be ours tomorrow too, the joy of the girl of the hills who sang of her father's dark cloud-capped mountain as she pounded white hill-paddy while the grey-headed monkey scattered the fallen jackfruit segments in the yard, bathing with the man of the land in the falls of the god-haunted slope, and climbing the built platform on the vengai whose rain-opened buds tell the season, to drive the green flock from the curved ears of the small millet.","n":"The whole poem is one long relative clause hanging on the final question, so the day's meeting is listed in full as something that may not happen again; the warning of confinement is never stated, only implied by நாளையும் 'tomorrow too'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 373","lb":"NT373","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பலவின்","பலா",1,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",2,"maa","scene","monkey"],["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",3,"seythi","scene","hill girl"],["ஐவன","ஐவனம்",4,"unavu","scene","hill paddy"],["அருவி","அருவி",5,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",6,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["பாசினம்","பாசினம்",7,"pul","scene","parrot flock"],["தினை","தினை",8,"unavu","scene","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:374":{"book":"natrinai","num":374,"t":["முரம்பு தலை மணந்த நிரம்பா இயவின்","ஓங்கித் தோன்றும் உமண் பொலி சிறுகுடிக்","களரிப் புளியின் காய் பசி பெயர்ப்ப","உச்சிக் கொண்ட ஓங்கு குடை வம்பலீர்","முற்றையும் உடையமோ மற்றே பிற்றை","வீழ் மா மணிய புனை நெடுங் கூந்தல்","நீர் வார் புள்ளி ஆகம் நனைப்ப","விருந்து அயர் விருப்பினள் வருந்தும்","திருந்துஇழை அரிவைத் தேமொழி நிலையே"],"p":"","r":"The man returning with his work done asks the wayfarers with tall umbrellas, who quell their hunger with the fruit of the salt-land tamarind on the unfinished road of hard gravel, by the little village crowded with salt merchants, whether he still has what he had before, and then how it is with the sweet-spoken girl of fine jewels who, eager to feast guests, suffers as the water from her long hair dressed with dark gems wets her freckled breast.","n":"Line 5 is compressed: he seems to ask whether the life he had before still waits for him, and only then to ask after her; the formula 'eager to feast guests' (also natrinai 361) shows she keeps the house ready while she weeps.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","virundhu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 374","lb":"NT374","poet":"வன் பரணர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி மீள்வான் இடைச் சுரத்துக் கண்டார்க்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","kandor",null,"karpu","வினை முற்றி மீள்வான்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["உமண்","உமண்",2,"seythi","scene","salt merchants"],["புளியின்","புளி",3,"maram","gift_food","tamarind"],["குடை","குடை",4,"pira","scene","umbrella"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:375":{"book":"natrinai","num":375,"t":["நீடு சினைப் புன்னை நறுந் தாது உதிர","கோடு புனை குருகின் தோடு தலைப் பெயரும்","பல் பூங் கானல் மல்கு நீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப","அன்பு இலை ஆதலின் தன் புலன் நயந்த","என்னும் நாணும் நன்னுதல் உவப்ப","வருவைஆயினோ நன்றே பெருங் கடல்","இரவுத் தலை மண்டிலம் பெயர்ந்தென உரவுத் திரை","எறிவன போல வரூஉம்","உயர் மணல் படப்பை எம் உறைவின் ஊரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the lord of the full-watered shore of many-flowered groves, where the long-branched punnai drops fragrant pollen and the flocks of crested herons move off, that since he lacks love it would be good if he came in a way that gladdened the girl of fine brow, who desired him with her senses, and me and her shame together, to our town of high sand gardens, where at the head of night, when the moon has moved, the strong waves come as if they were hurling themselves.","n":"'Come so that her shame too rejoices' means come openly, as a suitor: a coming that pleases shame is a wedding, not a tryst. The reading of lines 4 and 5 is uncertain in its syntax.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","naanam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 375","lb":"NT375","poet":"பொதும்பில் கிழார் மகன் வெண்கண்ணி","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரையாது நெடுங்காலம் வந்தொழுக தலைமகளது நிலை உணர்ந்த தோழி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புன்னை","புன்னை",1,"maram","scene",""],["குருகின்","குருகு",2,"pul","scene","heron"],["திரை","திரை",7,"pira","scene","wave"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:376":{"book":"natrinai","num":376,"t":["முறஞ்செவி யானைத் தடக் கையின் தடைஇ","இறைஞ்சிய குரல பைந் தாட் செந் தினை","வரையோன் வண்மை போல பல உடன்","கிளையோடு உண்ணும் வளைவாய்ப் பாசினம்","குல்லை குளவி கூதளம் குவளை","இல்லமொடு மிடைந்த ஈர்ந் தண் கண்ணியன்","சுற்று அமை வில்லன் செயலைத் தோன்றும்","நல் தார் மார்பன் காண்குறின் சிறிய","நன்கு அவற்கு அறிய உரைமின் பிற்றை","அணங்கும் அணங்கும் போலும் அணங்கி","வறும் புனம் காவல் விடாமை","அறிந்தனிர்அல்லிரோ அறன் இல் யாயே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, with the hero standing unseen, tells the curve-beaked green parrots who eat together with their kin, as freely as the hill lord gives, the red millet with green stalks whose heads droop like the bent trunk of a winnow-eared elephant: if you see the man with the good garland on his chest, a cool wet chaplet of kullai, kulavi, koothalam and blue lily woven with illam, a bow bound round, appearing by the asoka, tell him plainly so he knows that our mother without justice, afflicting like an affliction, will not stop guarding the empty field.","n":"The parrots share their millet as the hill lord shares his wealth, which flatters the overhearing hero and hints at what he should do; the harvested field still guarded by the mother means the meeting place is closed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","varaivu","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 376","lb":"NT376","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி கிளிமேல் வைத்துச் சிறைப்புறமாகச் செறிப்பு அறிவுறீஇயது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","pira","thalaivan","kalavu","கிளிமேல் வைத்துச் சிறைப்புறமாக"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"]],"k":[["யானைத்","யானை",1,"maa","simile","elephant"],["செந் தினை","செந்தினை",2,"unavu","scene","red millet"],["பாசினம்","பாசினம்",4,"pul","simile","parrot flock"],["குல்லை","குல்லை",5,"maram","ornament",""],["குளவி","குளவி",5,"maram","ornament",""],["கூதளம்","கூதளம்",5,"maram","ornament",""],["குவளை","குவளை",5,"maram","ornament",""],["இல்லமொடு","இல்லம்",6,"maram","ornament",""],["செயலைத்","செயலை",7,"maram","scene","asoka"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:377":{"book":"natrinai","num":377,"t":["மடல் மா ஊர்ந்து மாலை சூடி","கண் அகன் வைப்பின் நாடும் ஊரும்","ஒள் நுதல் அரிவை நலம் பாராட்டி","பண்ணல் மேவலமாகி அரிது உற்று","அது பிணி ஆக விளியலம்கொல்லோ","அகல் இரு விசும்பின் அரவுக் குறைபடுத்த","பசுங் கதிர் மதியத்து அகல் நிலாப் போல","அளகம் சேர்ந்த திருநுதல்","கழறுபு மெலிக்கும் நோய் ஆகின்றே"],"p":"","r":"The man, turned away, says within the friend's hearing: shall we, riding the palm-stem horse and wearing a garland, praising the beauty of the bright-browed girl through lands and towns of wide spaces, intent on doing it and suffering hard, not die of it as of a sickness? Her lovely forehead fringed with curls, like the wide light of the green-rayed moon that the snake has bitten into in the vast dark sky, chides and wastes me and has become my disease.","n":"Riding the madal would put him in perunthinai; here it is still a threat spoken for the friend's ears, so the poem stays in kalavu. The eclipse simile is exact: the dark curls bite into the bright forehead as the snake bites the moon.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamanoy","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 377","lb":"NT377","poet":"மடல் பாடிய மாதங்கீரனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"சேட்படுக்கப்பட்டு ஆற்றானாகிய தலைமகன் தோழி கேட்ப தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju","thozhi","kalavu","சேட்படுக்கப்பட்டு"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["மடல்","மடல்",1,"maram","scene","palm stem"],["மாலை","மாலை",1,"maram","ornament","garland"],["அரவுக்","அரவு",6,"pira","simile","snake"],["மதியத்து","மதியம்",7,"pira","simile","moon"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:378":{"book":"natrinai","num":378,"t":["யாமமும் நெடிய கழியும் காமமும்","கண்படல் ஈயாது பெருகும் தெண் கடல்","முழங்கு திரை முழவின் பாணியின் பைபய","பழம் புண் உறுநரின் பரவையின் ஆலும்","ஆங்கு அவை நலியவும் நீங்கி யாங்கும்","இரவு இறந்து எல்லை தோன்றலது அலர் வாய்","அயல் இற் பெண்டிர் பசலை பாட","ஈங்கு ஆகின்றால் தோழி ஓங்கு மணல்","வரி ஆர் சிறு மனை சிதைஇ வந்து","பரிவுதரத் தொட்ட பணிமொழி நம்பி","பாடு இமிழ் பனி நீர்ச் சேர்ப்பனொடு","நாடாது இயைந்த நண்பினது அளவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, with the hero standing unseen, tells the heroine: the night watch is long, the backwater is long, and love grows and lets the eyes not close, as the clear sea's roaring waves, slow as a drum's beat, toss like men with an old wound; though these afflict us, nowhere does day come after the night, and the neighbours' wives sing of her pallor with gossiping mouths; this, friend, is the measure of the bond formed without inquiry with the lord of the cold shore loud with sound, trusting the humble words he spoke, touching us to comfort us, after he came and ruined our little striped sand-house.","n":"Three long things open the poem, the watch, the backwater and love, and the ending names its cause in a word of the ethics books: a friendship made without inquiry (நாடாது). The ruined sand-house is the girls' play, so the courtship began as a boy's prank on their game.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","natpu","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 378","lb":"NT378","poet":"வடம வண்ணக்கன் பேரி சாத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது தலைமகன் ஒருவழித் தணந்த பின்னை வன்புறை எதிர்மொழிந்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தோழி சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","வன்புறை எதிர்மொழிந்ததூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["திரை","திரை",3,"pira","scene","wave"],["முழவின்","முழவு",3,"parai","simile","drum"],["மனை","மனை",9,"pira","scene","sand house"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:379":{"book":"natrinai","num":379,"t":["புன் தலை மந்தி கல்லா வன் பறழ்","குன்று உழை நண்ணிய முன்றில் போகாது","எரி அகைந்தன்ன வீ ததை இணர","வேங்கைஅம் படு சினைப் பொருந்தி கைய","தேம் பெய் தீம் பால் வெளவலின் கொடிச்சி","எழுது எழில் சிதைய அழுத கண்ணே","தேர் வண் சோழர் குடந்தைவாயில்","மாரி அம் கிடங்கின் ஈரிய மலர்ந்த","பெயல் உறு நீலம் போன்றன விரலே","பாஅய் அவ் வயிறு அலைத்தலின் ஆனாது","ஆடு மழை தவழும் கோடு உயர் பொதியில்","ஓங்கு இருஞ் சிலம்பில் பூத்த","காந்தள்அம் கொழு முகை போன்றன சிவந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the hero that when the strong untaught young of the grey-headed monkey, not leaving the yard by the hill, clung to the low branch of the vengai with flower clusters like leaping flame and snatched the honey-sweet milk from her hand, the hill girl wept till the painted beauty of her eyes was spoiled, and they became like the blue lilies that bloom wet in the rain-filled moat at the gate of Kudanthai of the Cholas rich in chariots, and her fingers, beating her lovely belly without stopping, reddened like the plump buds of kanthal flowering on the dark slopes of high-peaked Pothiyil where clouds drift.","n":"A child's grief over stolen milk is drawn with two royal similes, a Chola moat and the Pothiyil hill, so the friend's report of the girl's simplicity is also a portrait of her beauty; told to the hero, it says she is too young to be left waiting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","ilamai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 379","lb":"NT379","poet":"குடவாயிற் கீரத்தனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகற்குத் தலைமகளை மடமை கூறியது காப்புக் கைம்மிக்க காலத்துத் தலைமகள் தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தலைமகளை மடமை கூறியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","காப்புக் கைம்மிக்க காலத்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["மந்தி","மந்தி",1,"maa","scene","monkey"],["பறழ்","பறழ்",1,"maa","scene","young"],["வேங்கைஅம்","வேங்கை",4,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["பால்","பால்",5,"unavu","scene","milk"],["கொடிச்சி","கொடிச்சி",5,"seythi","scene","hill girl"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",9,"maram","simile","blue lily"],["காந்தள்அம்","காந்தள்",13,"maram","simile",""]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",7],["town","குடந்தைவாயில்",7],["hill","பொதியில்",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:380":{"book":"natrinai","num":380,"t":["நெய்யும் குய்யும் ஆடி மெய்யொடு","மாசு பட்டன்றே கலிங்கமும் தோளும்","திதலை மென் முலைத் தீம் பால் பிலிற்ற","புதல்வற் புல்லிப் புனிறு நாறும்மே","வால் இழை மகளிர் சேரித் தோன்றும்","தேரோற்கு ஒத்தனெம் அல்லேம் அதனால்","பொன் புரை நரம்பின் இன் குரல் சீறியாழ்","எழாஅல் வல்லை ஆயினும் தொழாஅல்","கொண்டு செல் பாண நின் தண் துறை ஊரனை","பாடு மனைப் பாடல் கூடாது நீடு நிலைப்","புரவியும் பூண் நிலை முனிகுவ","விரகு இல மொழியல் யாம் வேட்டது இல் வழியே"],"p":"","r":"The friend refuses the bard: our clothes and shoulders are soiled with ghee and the smoke of seasoning, sweet milk oozes from our soft spotted breasts, and holding our son we smell of new birth; we are no match for the chariot-man who shows himself in the street of brightly jewelled women; so, bard, though you can raise your small lute of sweet voice with strings like gold, do not bow, take your lord of the cool ford and go; even the horses that stand long tire of the harness while he is kept from the singing houses; do not speak artless words where we have no wish.","n":"The refusal is made with the body of new motherhood itself: the ghee, the smoke, the milk and the smell of birth are offered as the reason she cannot compete with the jewelled women, which is both humility and accusation. Lines 10 and 11 are compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 380","lb":"NT380","poet":"கூடலூர்ப் பல்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பாணற்குத் தோழி வாயில் மறுத்தது","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","paanan",null,"karpu","பாணற்குத் தோழி வாயில் மறுத்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["நெய்யும்","நெய்",1,"unavu","scene",""],["பால்","பால்",3,"unavu","scene","milk"],["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",7,"yaazh","scene","small lute"],["புரவியும்","புரவி",11,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:19"},"nt:381":{"book":"natrinai","num":381,"t":["அருந் துயர் உழத்தலின் உண்மை சான்ம் எனப்","பெரும்பிறிது இன்மையின் இலேனும் அல்லேன்","கரை பொருது இழிதரும் கான் யாற்று இகுகரை","வேர் கிளர் மராஅத்து அம் தளிர் போல","நடுங்கல் ஆனா நெஞ்சமொடு இடும்பை","யாங்கனம் தாங்குவென் மற்றே ஓங்கு செலல்","கடும் பகட்டு யானை நெடு மான் அஞ்சி","ஈர நெஞ்சமோடு இசை சேண் விளங்க","தேர் வீசு இருக்கை போல","மாரி இரீஇ மான்றன்றால் மழையே"],"p":"","r":"In the season of rain the woman says she is neither dead nor truly alive; her heart trembles without rest like the fresh shoots of a kadamba whose roots lie bared on the eroding bank of a forest stream, and she cannot bear the pain, for the rain has settled in and darkened the sky as steadily as Neduman Anji, kind of heart and famous far, keeps giving away chariots from his seat.","n":"Avvaiyar turns her own patron's unstinting gifts into the image of a rain that will not stop: the plenty that delights a bard is, for a woman waiting through the season, a torment that keeps on coming.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","paruvam","idumbai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 381","lb":"NT381","poet":"ஒளவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாகிய தலைமகள் பருவ வரவின்கண் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","பருவ வரவின்கண் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மராஅத்து","மராஅம்",4,"maram","simile","kadamba"],["யானை","யானை",7,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["patron","நெடு மான் அஞ்சி",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:382":{"book":"natrinai","num":382,"t":["கானல் மாலைக் கழி நீர் மல்க","நீல் நிற நெய்தல் நிரை இதழ் பொருந்த","ஆனாது அலைக்கும் கடலே மீன் அருந்தி","புள்ளினம் குடம்பை உடன் சேர்பு உள்ளார்","துறந்தோர் தேஎத்து இருந்து நனி வருந்தி","ஆர் உயிர் அழிவதுஆயினும் நேரிழை","கரத்தல் வேண்டுமால் மற்றே பரப்பு நீர்த்","தண்ணம் துறைவன் நாண","நண்ணார் தூற்றும் பழிதான் உண்டே"],"p":"","r":"At evening in the shore grove, as the backwater swells, the blue neythal close their petals and the sea beats without end, and the birds, fed on fish, go back together to their nests, the woman tells her friend that though she suffers here while he stays away without a thought of her, she must hide it even if her life goes, because the slander his enemies would spread would shame the lord of the cool shore.","n":"Her reason for silence is not her own modesty but his name: concealment becomes a service to the absent man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","pazhi","maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 382","lb":"NT382","poet":"நிகண்டன் கலைக்கோட்டுத் தண்டனார","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"ஒருவழித் தணந்த காலத்துப் பொழுதுபட ஆற்றாளாகி நின்ற தலைமகளைத் தோழி ஆற்றுவிக்கல்லாள் ஆயினாட்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","ஒருவழித் தணந்த காலத்துப் பொழுதுபட"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",2,"maram","scene",""],["மீன்","மீன்",3,"pira","scene","fish"],["புள்ளினம்","புள் இனம்",4,"pul","iraicchi","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","புள்ளினம்","confirmed","1/1","even the birds come home together at nightfall, while he stays away without a thought of her"]],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:383":{"book":"natrinai","num":383,"t":["கல் அயற் கலித்த கருங் கால் வேங்கை","அலங்கல்அம் தொடலை அன்ன குருளை","வயப் புனிற்று இரும் பிணப் பசித்தென வயப் புலி","புகர் முகம் சிதையத் தாக்கி களிறு அட்டு","உரும் இசை உரறும் உட்குவரு நடு நாள்","அருளினை போலினும் அருளாய் அன்றே","கனை இருள் புதைத்த அஞ்சுவரும் இயவில்","பாம்பு உடன்று இரிக்கும் உருமோடு","ஓங்கு வரை நாட நீ வருதலானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, having seen the path, tells the man of the high hill that he seems gracious but is not, coming at the dreadful midnight along a road buried in thick darkness, where thunder routs the snakes and a tiger, its striped cubs like a garland of vengai flowers and its mate hungry after birth, smashes a tusker's spotted face, kills it and roars like thunder.","n":"The tiger hunting for a mate that has just given birth is the very danger the man walks into; the reproach is a way of saying his love endangers both of them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","acham","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 383","lb":"NT383","poet":"கோளியூர்கிழார் மகனார் செழியனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி ஆறு பார்த்துற்றுச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","ஆறு பார்த்துற்றுச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",1,"maram","simile","kino tree"],["குருளை","குருளை",2,"maa","simile","cubs"],["புலி","புலி",3,"maa","scene","tiger"],["களிறு","களிறு",4,"maa","scene","tusker"],["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",8,"pira","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:384":{"book":"natrinai","num":384,"t":["பைம் புறப் புறவின் செங் காற் சேவல்","களரி ஓங்கிய கவை முடக் கள்ளி","முளரி அம் குடம்பை ஈன்று இளைப்பட்ட","உயவு நடைப் பேடை உணீஇய மன்னர்","முனை கவர் முது பாழ் உகு நெற் பெறூஉம்","அரண் இல் சேய் நாட்டு அதர் இடை மலர்ந்த","நல் நாள் வேங்கைப் பொன் மருள் புதுப் பூப்","பரந்தன நடக்க யாம் கண்டனம் மாதோ","காண் இனி வாழி என் நெஞ்சே நாண் விட்டு","அருந் துயர் உழந்த காலை","மருந்து எனப்படூஉம் மடவோளையே"],"p":"","r":"Eloping with the girl, the man tells his heart to look: on the path through a far land without forts, where the red-legged dove gathers spilt paddy from an old village laid waste by kings to feed his mate, slow and spent after laying in a brushwood nest on the forked kalli of the salt waste, the new gold-like vengai flowers have spread for her to walk on, she, the innocent one, who was the cure when he suffered after casting off shame.","n":"The cure he once needed is now walking beside him, and the waste itself seems to lay down flowers for her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","marundhu","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 384","lb":"NT384","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"உடன் போகாநின்றான் மலிந்து தன் நெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,"kalavu","உடன் போகாநின்றான் மலிந்து"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["சேவல்","சேவல்",1,"pul","iraicchi","male dove"],["கள்ளி","கள்ளி",2,"maram","scene",""],["பேடை","பேடை",4,"pul","iraicchi","female dove"],["நெற்","நெல்",5,"unavu","gift_food","paddy"],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",7,"maram","scene","kino tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","சேவல்","confirmed","1/1","the man taking her across the waste means to provide for her as the dove provides for his mate"]],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:385":{"book":"natrinai","num":385,"t":["எல்லை சென்றபின் மலரும் கூம்பின","புலவு நீர் அடைகரை யாமைப் பார்ப்போடு","அலவனும் அளைவயிற் செறிந்தன கொடுங் கழி","இரை நசை வருத்தம் வீட மரமிசைப்","புள்ளும் பிள்ளையொடு வதிந்தன அதனால்","பொழுதன்றுஆதலின் தமியை வருதி","எழுது எழில் மழைக்க ...",".."],"p":"","r":"The speaker, probably the friend, tells the man that the day is over, the flowers have closed, the tortoise with its young and the crab have crowded into their holes on the reeking shore of the curving backwater, and the birds, their labour of seeking food done, rest in the trees with their chicks, so it is no hour for him to come alone; the rest of the poem is lost.","n":"Every creature named is at home with its young; the unmarried man alone is abroad, and the friend's list is already a demand that he give her a home.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["iravu","kalaviyal","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 385","lb":"NT385","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மலரும்","மலர்",1,"maram","scene",""],["யாமைப்","யாமை",2,"pira","scene","tortoise"],["அலவனும்","அலவன்",3,"pira","scene","crab"],["புள்ளும்","புள்",5,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:386":{"book":"natrinai","num":386,"t":["சிறு கட் பன்றிப் பெருஞ் சின ஒருத்தல்","துறுகட் கண்ணிக் கானவர் உழுத","குலவுக் குரல் ஏனல் மாந்தி ஞாங்கர்","விடர் அளைப் பள்ளி வேங்கை அஞ்சாது","கழை வளர் சாரல் துஞ்சும் நாடன்","அணங்குடை அருஞ் சூள் தருகுவென் என நீ","நும்மோர் அன்னோர் துன்னார் இவை என","தெரிந்து அது வியந்தனென் தோழி பணிந்து நம்","கல் கெழு சிறுகுடிப் பொலிய","வதுவை என்று அவர் வந்த ஞான்றே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman that when the man of the land where a small-eyed, great-tempered boar gorges on the hunters' millet and then sleeps on the bamboo slope unafraid of the tiger in its rock-cleft lair offered to swear a dread oath by the god, she marvelled, knowing that men of his kind do not stoop to such things; yet he came, humbly, saying 'wedding', so that their small stony village shone.","n":"The boar that eats the guarded millet and sleeps unafraid near the tiger could be read as the lover who took the girl under her kin's eyes, but no part of the poem maps it to a second correspondence, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","sool","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 386","lb":"NT386","poet":"தங்கால் ஆத்திரேயன் செங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பரத்தையின் மறுத்தந்த தலைமகற்கு வாயில் நேர்ந்த தோழி தலைமகளை முகம்புகுவல் என முற்பட்டாள் தலைமகள் மாட்டு நின்ற பொறாமை நீங்காமை அறிந்து பிறிது ஒன்றன்மேல் வைத்து பாவியேன் இன்று பேதைமை செய்தேன் எம்பெருமாட்டி குறிப்பு உணர்ந்து வழிபடுவேனாவேன்மன்னோ எனச்சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பரத்தையின் மறுத்தந்த தலைமகற்கு வாயில் நேர்ந்த தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["பன்றிப்","பன்றி",1,"maa","scene","boar"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",2,"seythi","scene","hunters"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",3,"unavu","scene","millet"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",4,"maa","scene","tiger"],["கழை","கழை",5,"maram","scene","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:387":{"book":"natrinai","num":387,"t":["நெறி இருங் கதுப்பும் நீண்ட தோளும்","அம்ம நாளும் தொல் நலம் சிதைய","ஒல்லாச் செந் தொடை ஒரீஇய கண்ணிக்","கல்லா மழவர் வில்லிடை விலங்கிய","துன் அருங் கவலை அருஞ் சுரம் இறந்தோர்","வருவர் வாழி தோழி செரு இறந்து","ஆலங்கானத்து அஞ்சுவர இறுத்த","வேல் கெழு தானைச் செழியன் பாசறை","உறை கழி வாளின் மின்னி உதுக்காண்","நெடும் பெருங் குன்றம் முற்றி","கடும் பெயல் பொழியும் கலி கெழு வானே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the woman, whose curled hair and long arms lose their old beauty day by day, that the man who crossed the hard waste of forked tracks barred by the bows of untaught Mazhavar will come: look, the clamorous cloud has ringed the tall hill and pours heavy rain, flashing like swords drawn from their sheaths in the camp of Chezhiyan, whose spear-bearing army won the war and camped in terror at Alankanam.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","pirivu","melivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 387","lb":"NT387","poet":"பொதும்பில் கிழார் மகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை மெலிந்த தலைமகளைத் தோழி பருவம் காட்டி வற்புறீஇயது","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பருவம் காட்டி வற்புறீஇயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மழவர்","மழவர்",4,"seythi","scene","Mazhavar bowmen"]],"en":[["king","செழியன்",8],["battle","ஆலங்கானத்து",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:388":{"book":"natrinai","num":388,"t":["அம்ம வாழி தோழி நன்னுதற்கு","யாங்கு ஆகின்றுகொல் பசப்பே நோன் புரிக்","கயிறு கடை யாத்த கடு நடை எறி உளித்","திண் திமில் பரதவர் ஒண் சுடர்க் கொளீஇ","நடு நாள் வேட்டம் போகி வைகறைக்","கடல் மீன் தந்து கானற் குவைஇ","ஓங்கு இரும் புன்னை வரி நிழல் இருந்து","தேம் கமழ் தேறல் கிளையொடு மாந்தி","பெரிய மகிழும் துறைவன் எம்","சிறிய நெஞ்சத்து அகல்வு அறியானே"],"p":"","r":"Asked how pallor could come to her fine brow, the woman says the lord of the cool shore never leaves her small heart, the shore where fishermen in sturdy boats, with harpoons on strong ropes, light bright torches, hunt at midnight, bring fish at dawn, heap it in the grove and, in the striped shade of the tall punnai, drink honey-fragrant toddy with their kin and rejoice greatly.","n":"The fishermen's great rejoicing is set against her 'small heart': his shore is all feasting, and the only place he stays is inside her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pasalai","varaivu","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 388","lb":"NT388","poet":"மதுரை மருதங்கிழார் மகனார் பெருங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"வரைவு நீட ஆற்றாளாகிய தோழிக்குத் தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறமாகச் சொல்லியது மனையுள் வேறுபடாது ஆற்றினாய் என்றாற்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi","thalaivan","kalavu","வரைவு நீட ஆற்றாளாகிய தோழிக்குத்"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","மனையுள் வேறுபடாது ஆற்றினாய்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",4,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["மீன்","மீன்",6,"pira","scene","fish"],["புன்னை","புன்னை",7,"maram","scene",""],["தேறல்","தேறல்",8,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:389":{"book":"natrinai","num":389,"t":["வேங்கையும் புலி ஈன்றன அருவியும்","தேம் படு நெடு வரை மணியின் மானும்","அன்னையும் அமர்ந்து நோக்கினளே என்னையும்","களிற்று முகம் திறந்த கல்லா விழுத் தொடை","ஏவல் இளையரொடு மா வழிப்பட்டென","சிறு கிளி முரணிய பெருங் குரல் ஏனல்","காவல் நீ என்றோளே சேவலொடு","சிலம்பின் போகிய சிதர் கால் வாரணம்","முதைச் சுவல் கிளைத்த பூழி மிகப் பல","நன் பொன் இமைக்கும் நாடனொடு","அன்புறு காமம் அமைக நம் தொடர்பே"],"p":"","r":"With the man listening, the friend tells the girl that the vengai has put out its tiger-striped blossoms and the falls on the honeyed hill shine like gems, their mother has looked on them kindly, and their father, going off after game with his young servants whose arrows split elephants' faces, has told her to guard the heavy-eared millet the small parrots raid; so let their bond be a loving passion with the man of the land where the dust the jungle fowl and its mate scratch up on the old upland glitters with gold.","n":"The father's command to guard the millet is, without his knowing it, the opening of the daytime meeting place; the friend says it aloud so that the man will know the chance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 389","lb":"NT389","poet":"காவிரிப் பூம்பட்டினத்துச் செங்கண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"பகற்குறி வந்து ஒழுகாநின்ற காலத்துத் தலைமகன் கேட்பச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","பகற்குறி வந்து ஒழுகாநின்ற காலத்துத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வேங்கையும்","வேங்கை",1,"maram","simile","kino tree"],["புலி","புலி",1,"maa","simile","tiger"],["அருவியும்","அருவி",1,"pira","scene",""],["களிற்று","களிறு",4,"maa","scene","tusker"],["இளையரொடு","இளையர்",5,"seythi","scene","servants"],["கிளி","கிளி",6,"pul","scene",""],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",6,"unavu","scene","millet"],["சேவலொடு","சேவல்",7,"pul","scene","cock"],["வாரணம்","வாரணம்",8,"pul","scene","jungle fowl"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:390":{"book":"natrinai","num":390,"t":["வாளை வாளின் பிறழ நாளும்","பொய்கை நீர்நாய் வைகுதுயில் ஏற்கும்","கை வண் கிள்ளி வெண்ணி சூழ்ந்த","வயல் வெள் ஆம்பல் உருவ நெறித் தழை","ஐது அகல் அல்குல் அணி பெறத் தைஇ","விழவின் செலீஇயர் வேண்டும்மன்னோ","யாணர் ஊரன் காணுநன்ஆயின்","வரையாமைஓ அரிதே வரையின்","வரைபோல் யானை வாய்மொழி முடியன்","வரை வேய் புரையும் நல் தோள்","அளிய தோழி தொலையுந பலவே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says she would like to go to the festival wearing a leaf skirt of white water lilies from the fields around Venni of generous Killi, where vaalai fish flash like swords and the otter of the pond takes its morning sleep; if the lord of the rich town sees her he can hardly fail to take her, and if he does, the fine arms of many women, like the bamboo of the hill of Mudiyan of true word and hill-like elephants, will be pitiable and waste.","n":"The boast is made to be overheard: the speaker parades her power over the man to those who carry word to the house.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 390","lb":"NT390","poet":"ஒளவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பாங்கு ஆயின வாயில் கேட்ப நெருங்கிச் சொல்லியது தலைமகள் தோழிக்கு உரைப் பாளாய் வாயிலாகப் புக்கார் கேட்ப சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thozhi","pira","karpu","பாங்கு ஆயின வாயில் கேட்ப"],["thalaivi","thozhi","pira","karpu","வாயிலாகப் புக்கார் கேட்ப"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["வாளை","வாளை",1,"pira","simile","vaalai fish"],["நீர்நாய்","நீர்நாய்",2,"maa","scene","otter"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",4,"maram","ornament","water lily"],["தழை","தழை",4,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["யானை","யானை",9,"maa","scene","elephant"],["வேய்","வேய்",10,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[["king","கிள்ளி",3],["town","வெண்ணி",3],["patron","முடியன்",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:391":{"book":"natrinai","num":391,"t":["ஆழல் மடந்தை அழுங்குவர் செலவே","புலிப் பொறி அன்ன புள்ளி அம் பொதும்பின்","பனிப் பவர் மேய்ந்த மா இரு மருப்பின்","மலர் தலைக் காரான் அகற்றிய தண் நடை","ஒண் தொடி மகளிர் இழை அணிக் கூட்டும்","பொன் படு கொண்கான நன்னன் நல் நாட்டு","ஏழிற்குன்றம் பெறினும் பொருள்வயின்","யாரோ பிரிகிற்பவரே குவளை","நீர் வார் நிகர் மலர் அன்ன நின்","பேர் அமர் மழைக் கண் தெண் பனி கொளவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the girl not to weep, for he will give up the journey: who would leave for wealth, even to win the Ezhil hill in Nannan's good gold-bearing Konkanam, where bright-bangled women gather gold from the cool track the dark-horned buffalo opened after grazing the cold creepers of a grove dappled like a tiger's spots, and make her great rain-dark eyes, like a water-dripping kuvalai, fill with tears?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 391","lb":"NT391","poet":"பாலை பாடிய பெருங்கடுங்கோ","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்டு ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகளைத் தோழி வற்புறுத்தியது வரைவு உணர்த்தியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"karpu","பிரிவு உணர்த்தப்பட்டு ஆற்றாளாய"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு உணர்த்தியதூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புலிப்","புலி",2,"maa","simile","tiger"],["பவர்","பவர்",3,"maram","scene","creeper"],["காரான்","கார் ஆன்",4,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["குவளை","குவளை",8,"maram","simile","blue water lily"]],"en":[["patron","நன்னன்",6],["hill","ஏழிற்குன்றம்",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:392":{"book":"natrinai","num":392,"t":["கடுஞ் சுறா எறிந்த கொடுந் தாட் தந்தை","புள் இமிழ் பெருங் கடல் கொள்ளான் சென்றென","மனை அழுது ஒழிந்த புன் தலைச் சிறாஅர்","துணையதின் முயன்ற தீம் கண் நுங்கின்","பணை கொள் வெம் முலை பாடு பெற்று உவக்கும்","பெண்ணை வேலி உழை கண் சீறூர்","நல் மனை அறியின் நன்றுமன்தில்ல","செம்மல் நெஞ்சமொடு தாம் வந்து பெயர்ந்த","கானலொடு அழியுநர் போலாம் பானாள்","முனி படர் களையினும் களைப","நனி பேர் அன்பினர் காதலோரே"],"p":"","r":"The friend wishes the lovers knew their good house in the small town fenced with palmyra, where the shock-haired boys who cried at home when their shark-spearing father went off to the bird-loud sea without taking them are cheered by the warm swelling flesh of sweet palm fruit; those who came proudly and turned back seem to suffer with the shore grove, but they, of very great love, may yet clear the lonely sorrow of the midnight.","n":"The boys consoled with palm fruit while their father is at sea are a small model of the girl, comforted with promises while her lover is away.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","varaivu","anbu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 392","lb":"NT392","poet":"மதுரை மருதன் இளநாகனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"இரவுக்குறி முகம்புக்கது வரைவு நீட ஆற்றாளாய தலைமகளைத் தோழி வரைவு உணர்த்தி வற்புறுத்தியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி முகம்புக்கது"],["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு உணர்த்தி வற்புறுத்தியதூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["சுறா","சுறா",1,"pira","scene","shark"],["தந்தை","தந்தை",1,"seythi","scene","fisherman"],["புள்","புள்",2,"pul","scene",""],["நுங்கின்","நுங்கு",4,"unavu","gift_food","palm fruit"],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",6,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[],"dv":[["ullurai","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:393":{"book":"natrinai","num":393,"t":["நெடுங் கழை நிவந்த நிழல் படு சிலம்பின்","கடுஞ் சூல் வயப்பிடி கன்று ஈன்று உயங்க","பால் ஆர் பசும் புனிறு தீரிய களி சிறந்து","வாலா வேழம் வணர் குரல் கவர்தலின்","கானவன் எறிந்த கடுஞ் செலல் ஞெகிழி","வேய் பயில் அடுக்கம் சுடர மின்னி","நிலை கிளர் மீனின் தோன்றும் நாடன்","இரவின் வரூஉம் இடும்பை நாம் உய","வரைய வந்த வாய்மைக்கு ஏற்ப","நமர் கொடை நேர்ந்தனர்ஆயின் அவருடன்","நேர்வர்கொல் வாழி தோழி நம் காதலர்","புதுவர் ஆகிய வரவும் நின்","வதுவை நாண் ஒடுக்கமும் காணுங்காலே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks whether, now that their people have agreed to give the girl to the man who came truly to marry and they are spared the dangers of his night visits, the family will accept him when they see their lover come as a new man and her bridal shyness, he of the land where a young tusker, wild with joy, seizes the curved millet to restore his mate weak from calving, and the firebrand the hunter hurls at him lights the bamboo slope like a shooting star.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","naanam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 393","lb":"NT393","poet":"கோவூர் கிழார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"வரைவு மலிந்தது","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi",null,"kalavu","வரைவு மலிந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கழை","கழை",1,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["பிடி","பிடி",2,"maa","iraicchi","she-elephant"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",4,"maa","iraicchi","tusker"],["குரல்","குரல்",4,"unavu","gift_food","millet ear"],["கானவன்","கானவன்",5,"seythi","scene","hunter"],["ஞெகிழி","ஞெகிழி",5,"pira","simile","firebrand"],["மீனின்","மீன்",7,"pira","simile","star"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","வேழம்","confirmed","1/1","the man who braved the night roads will now provide for her as a husband"]],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:394":{"book":"natrinai","num":394,"t":["மரந்தலை மணந்த நனந் தலைக் கானத்து","அலந்தலை ஞெமையத்து இருந்த குடிஞை","பொன் செய் கொல்லனின் இனிய தெளிர்ப்ப","பெய்ம் மணி ஆர்க்கும் இழை கிளர் நெடுந் தேர்","வன் பரல் முரம்பின் நேமி அதிர","சென்றிசின் வாழியோ பனிக் கடு நாளே","இடைச் சுரத்து எழிலி உறைத்தென மார்பின்","குறும் பொறிக் கொண்ட சாந்தமொடு","நறுந் தண்ணியன்கொல் நோகோ யானே"],"p":"","r":"Onlookers on the waste road tell the returning man that he went, bless him, in the harsh cold season, his tall jewelled chariot ringing with bells and its wheels rattling over the hard gravel, through a forest crowded with trees where an owl on a withered nyemai calls as sweetly as a goldsmith tapping, and that since the cloud has poured midway, the sandal paste on his chest must be cool and fragrant; they grieve for her.","n":"Strangers on the road can only imagine the welcome: the rain that cools him is the sign she has been waiting through.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 394","lb":"NT394","poet":"ஒளவையார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"வினை முற்றி மறுத்தராநின்ற தலைமகனை இடைச் சுரத்துக் கண்டார் சொல்லியது வன்சொல்லால் குறை நயப்பித்த தோழி தந்து அளித்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[["kandor","thalaivan",null,"karpu","இடைச் சுரத்துக் கண்டார் சொல்லியது"],["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வன்சொல்லால் குறை நயப்பித்த தோழி"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஞெமையத்து","ஞெமை",2,"maram","scene",""],["குடிஞை","குடிஞை",2,"pul","simile","owl"],["சாந்தமொடு","சாந்தம்",8,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:395":{"book":"natrinai","num":395,"t":["யாரை எலுவ யாரே நீ எமக்கு","யாரையும் அல்லை நொதுமலாளனை","அனைத்தால் கொண்க நம்மிடையே நினைப்பின்","கடும் பகட்டு யானை நெடுந் தேர்க் குட்டுவன்","வேந்து அடு மயக்கத்து முரசு அதிர்ந்தன்ன","ஓங்கற் புணரி பாய்ந்து ஆடு மகளிர்","அணிந்திடு பல் பூ மரீஇ ஆர்ந்த","ஆ புலம் புகுதரு பேர் இசை மாலைக்","கடல் கெழு மாந்தை அன்ன எம்","வேட்டனை அல்லையால் நலம் தந்து சென்மே"],"p":"","r":"The friend asks the lord of the shore who he is to them, a mere stranger, and whether that is how things stand between them: he did not desire their girl, who is like Maanthai on the sea, loud at evening when cows come home after feeding on the flowers dropped by girls who dive in waves that roar like the drums in Kuttuvan's king-slaying battle; so let him give back her beauty and go.","n":"'Give back her beauty and go' is a legal formula of restitution: the friend treats the man's courtship as a taking that must be repaid by marriage or undone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","nalam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 395","lb":"NT395","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"நலம் தொலைந்தது எனத் தலைவனைத் தோழி கூறி வரைவு கடாயது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","நலம் தொலைந்தது எனத் தலைவனைத் தோழி கூறி வரைவு கடாயது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முரசு","முரசு",5,"parai","simile","war drum"],["பூ","பூ",7,"maram","scene",""],["ஆ","ஆ",8,"maa","scene","cows"]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவன்",4],["town","மாந்தை",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:396":{"book":"natrinai","num":396,"t":["பெய்து போகு எழிலி வைகு மலை சேர","தேன் தூங்கு உயர் வரை அருவி ஆர்ப்ப","வேங்கை தந்த வெற்பு அணி நல் நாள்","பொன்னின் அன்ன பூஞ் சினை துழைஇ","கமழ் தாது ஆடிய கவின் பெறு தோகை","பாசறை மீமிசைக் கணம் கொள்பு ஞாயிற்று","உறு கதிர் இள வெயில் உண்ணும் நாடன்","நின் மார்பு அணங்கிய செல்லல் அரு நோய்","யார்க்கு நொந்து உரைக்கோ யானே பல் நாள்","காமர் நனி சொல் சொல்லி","ஏமம் என்று அருளாய் நீ மயங்கினையே"],"p":"","r":"To the man of the hill where the cloud that has rained rests on the peak, the falls roar on the honey-hung heights, and on the good day when the vengai decks the mountain a lovely peacock, having nosed through its gold-like flowering boughs and bathed in its pollen, drinks the young sunlight with its flock on the rock, the friend says she has no one to tell the grievous sickness his chest has caused; for many days he spoke lovely words and called himself her protection, and now he has lost his way without grace.","n":"The peacock dusted with vengai pollen basking in the morning sun is the content the girl had expected; it carries a mood and no act, so it stays scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","arul","varaivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 396","lb":"NT396","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"தோழி தலைமகனை வரைவு கடாயது வரைவு உணர்த்தப்பட்டு ஆற்றாளாய்ச் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம் இரவுக்குறி மறுத்ததூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","தோழி தலைமகனை வரைவு கடாயது"],["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","வரைவு உணர்த்தப்பட்டு ஆற்றாளாய்ச்"],["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","இரவுக்குறி மறுத்ததூஉம்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தேன்","தேன்",2,"pira","scene","honeycomb"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",3,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["தோகை","தோகை",5,"pul","scene","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:397":{"book":"natrinai","num":397,"t":["தோளும் அழியும் நாளும் சென்றென","நீள் இடை அத்தம் நோக்கி வாள் அற்றுக்","கண்ணும் காட்சி தௌவின என் நீத்து","அறிவும் மயங்கி பிறிது ஆகின்றே","நோயும் பெருகும் மாலையும் வந்தன்று","யாங்கு ஆகுவென்கொல் யானே ஈங்கோ","சாதல் அஞ்சேன் அஞ்சுவல் சாவின்","பிறப்புப் பிறிது ஆகுவதுஆயின்","மறக்குவேன்கொல் என் காதலன் எனவே"],"p":"","r":"The woman tells the friend who comforts her that her arms waste as the days go by, her eyes, gazing at the long waste road, have lost their light, her mind has left her and become something else, the sickness grows and evening has come; she does not fear dying, but fears that if in death she is born into another life she may forget her lover.","n":"The fear is precise: not death, not the next birth as such, but the forgetting a new birth would bring.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","ninaivu","pirivu","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 397","lb":"NT397","poet":"அம்மூவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"பிரிவிடை ஆற்றாளாகி நின்ற தலைமகளை வற்புறாநின்ற தோழிக்கு ஆற்றுவல் என்பது படச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"karpu","ஆற்றுவல் என்பது படச் சொல்லியது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:398":{"book":"natrinai","num":398,"t":["உரு கெழு தெய்வமும் கரந்து உறையின்றே","விரி கதிர் ஞாயிறும் குடக்கு வாங்கும்மே","நீர் அலைக் கலைஇய கூழை வடியாச்","சாஅய் அவ் வயிறு அலைப்ப உடன் இயைந்து","ஓரை மகளிரும் ஊர் எய்தினரே","பல் மலர் நறும் பொழில் பழிச்சி யாம் முன்","சென்மோ சேயிழை என்றனம் அதன் எதிர்","சொல்லாள் மெல்லியல் சிலவே நல் அகத்து","யாணர் இள முலை நனைய","மாண் எழில் மலர்க் கண் தெண் பனி கொளவே"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the man leaving the day tryst that the fearsome god has withdrawn, the sun of spreading rays bends west, and the girls of the oorai game, hair wet from the waves and bellies heaving, have gone home; when they praised the fragrant grove and said to the girl 'let us go ahead', she answered only a few words, and her young breasts were wet as her lovely flower-like eyes filled with tears.","n":"Everything else has gone home in order; the friend reports the girl's silence and tears so that the man will be the one to console her, and by marrying her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 398","lb":"NT398","poet":"உலோச்சனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"neythal","cth":"நெய்தல்","ctu":"முன்னுற உணர்ந்து பகற்குறி வந்து மீளும் தலைமகனை நீ தான் இவளது தன்மையை ஆற்றுவி எனச் சொல்லியது","thd":0,"uri":"irangal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivan",null,"kalavu","பகற்குறி வந்து மீளும் தலைமகனை"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["தெய்வமும்","தெய்வம்",1,"deyvam","scene",""],["மலர்","மலர்",6,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:399":{"book":"natrinai","num":399,"t":["அருவி ஆர்க்கும் பெரு வரை அடுக்கத்து","குருதி ஒப்பின் கமழ் பூங் காந்தள்","வரி அணி சிறகின் வண்டு உண மலரும்","வாழை அம் சிலம்பில் கேழல் கெண்டிய","நிலவரை நிவந்த பல உறு திரு மணி","ஒளி திகழ் விளக்கத்து ஈன்ற மடப் பிடி","களிறு புறங்காப்ப கன்றொடு வதியும்","மா மலை நாடன் நயந்தனன் வரூஉம்","பெருமை உடையள் என்பது","தருமோ தோழி நின் திரு நுதல் கவினே"],"p":"","r":"The friend, with the man listening, tells the girl that her bright brow's beauty will show she has the worth for which the lord of the great hill comes desiring her, the hill where by roaring falls the blood-red kaanthal opens for striped-winged bees, and on the plantain slope a young she-elephant that has calved rests with her calf in the gleam of gems a boar dug up, while the tusker guards them from behind.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","kalaviyal","nalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 399","lb":"NT399","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"நெடுங்காலம் வந்து ஒழுக ஆற்றாமை வேறுபட நின்ற தலைமகளைத் தோழி எம்பெருமான் இதற்காய நல்லது புரியும் என்று தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகச் சொல்லியது இதற்காய நல்லது புரியும் பெருமான் திறம் வேண்டும் என்றாட்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியதூஉம் ஆம்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","thalaivi","thalaivan","kalavu","தலைமகன் சிறைப்புறத்தானாகச் சொல்லியது"],["thalaivi","thozhi",null,"kalavu","பெருமான் திறம் வேண்டும் என்றாட்குத்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",1,"pira","scene",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",2,"maram","simile",""],["வண்டு","வண்டு",3,"pira","scene","bee"],["வாழை","வாழை",4,"maram","scene",""],["கேழல்","கேழல்",4,"maa","scene","boar"],["மணி","மணி",5,"pira","scene","gem"],["பிடி","பிடி",6,"maa","iraicchi","she-elephant"],["களிறு","களிறு",7,"maa","iraicchi","tusker"],["கன்றொடு","கன்று",7,"maa","scene","calf"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","களிறு","confirmed","1/1","the man will come to wed her and guard her and their household as the tusker guards his own"]],"ch":"nt:20"},"nt:400":{"book":"natrinai","num":400,"t":["வாழை மென் தோடு வார்புஉறுபு ஊக்கும்","நெல் விளை கழனி நேர் கண் செறுவின்","அரிவனர் இட்ட சூட்டு அயல் பெரிய","இருஞ் சுவல் வாளை பிறழும் ஊர","நினின்று அமைகுவென்ஆயின் இவண் நின்று","இன்னா நோக்கமொடு எவன் பிழைப்பு உண்டோ","மறம் கெழு சோழர் உறந்தை அவையத்து","அறம் கெட அறியாதாங்கு சிறந்த","கேண்மையொடு அளைஇ நீயே","கெடு அறியாய் என் நெஞ்சத்தானே"],"p":"","r":"The courtesan tells the lord of the town, where by paddy fields with plantain leaves bending low the great dark-backed vaalai leaps beside the sheaves the reapers have laid, that if she could do without him she would stay here and gain something by sulking; but bound with a rare friendship, he knows no failing in her heart, just as justice never fails in the Uranthai court of the warlike Cholas.","n":"Her praise is also a renunciation of oodal: she cannot even sulk at him, because his place in her heart is as fixed as an institution.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","natpu","oodal","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Natrinai 400","lb":"NT400","poet":"ஆலங்குடி வங்கனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"மருதம்","ctu":"பரத்தை தலைவனைப் புகழ்ந்தது முன்பு நின்று யாதோ புகழ்ந்தவாறு எனின் நின் இன்று அமையாம் என்று சொன்னமையான் என்பது நற்றிணை முற்றும்","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thalaivan",null,"karpu","பரத்தை தலைவனைப் புகழ்ந்தது"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வாழை","வாழை",1,"maram","scene",""],["நெல்","நெல்",2,"unavu","scene","paddy"],["அரிவனர்","அரிவனர்",3,"seythi","scene","reapers"],["வாளை","வாளை",4,"pira","scene","vaalai fish"]],"en":[["king","சோழர்",7],["town","உறந்தை",7]],"dv":[],"ch":"nt:20"}},"links":[{"a":"ai:4","b":"k:1311","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 4: the girls wish that the lover's chest never become a common pond (பழனம்). Kural 1311: the wife refuses her husband's chest because all women feast on it in common with their eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The fear the Ainkurunuru girls voice before marriage, that his chest may become common property, is the very charge the Kural's wife brings in a sulk after it, so the two show the marutham husband's straying anticipated as a danger and then turned into a reason to refuse him."},{"a":"ai:9","b":"k:1143","type":"tension","note":"Ainkurunuru 9: the girls wish that their bond with the lover not become town gossip (அம்பல்). Kural 1143: the man welcomes the gossip the whole town knows, since it feels like having her already.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same talk of the town that the women pray to escape is a pleasure the man savours, so read together the two show that in secret love gossip threatened the woman's standing while it flattered the man's hopes.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:8","b":"k:545","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 8: the mother wishes that the king rule justly and there be no theft (and in poem 10, that the rains come). Kural 545: rain and harvest gather in the land of a king who rules by the sceptre's nature.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The mother's prayers for just rule and for timely rain stand as separate wishes in the decade, while the Kural makes the second follow from the first, so a household's prayer list shows the belief that the Kural later states as a law of kingship."},{"a":"ai:8","b":"ku:53","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 8: the girls wish that the lover's oath sworn here may prove true. Kuruntokai 53: the friend tells the man of the fields that what afflicts them is the oath he swore at the broad ford of their town, holding the girl's forearm.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The oath the Ainkurunuru girls hope will come true is, in the Kuruntokai, already the cause of their suffering, so the pair shows the kalavu vow of the field country both as the girl's only security and as the wound when it lags."},{"a":"ai:5","b":"a:160","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 5: the girls wish that the lover's chariot stand at their front gate. Akananuru 160: the friend tells the girl that his chariot, which used to come by night, has now come by day, loudly, for the whole village to see.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Akananuru scene shows what the Ainkurunuru wish means: a chariot that comes by day to the gate, seen by the village, is a suitor's public claim, so the single wish for 'the chariot at our gate' is a request for marriage.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:6","b":"ai:7","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 6: may he marry her and our father give her. Ainkurunuru 7: may he take her and go to his own town.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read in order, the decade's wishes walk through the rite step by step, the father's giving (6) and then the bride's leaving for the husband's town (7), so the series records the sequence of a Sangam marriage that no single poem states.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:11","b":"k:1236","type":"qualifies","note":"In both the wife's shoulders waste and she will not call him cruel; in Ainkurunuru 11 she says he is good while her own shoulders say he is not, in Kural 1236 she is pained that others call him cruel because her shoulders and bangles slacken.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam poem sets the accusation inside her own body, against her own words, while Valluvar moves it to other people reading her body, so the couplet's defence of him against outsiders presupposes the older poem's private split between what she says and what her shoulders show."},{"a":"ai:12","b":"ai:11","type":"develops","note":"The two poems share the refrain துறைகேழ் ஊரன் கொடுமை ... யாமே ... என் தடமென் தோளே; in 11 she calls him good and the shoulders contradict her, in 12 she lets him keep his cruelty and lets the shoulders lose.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read in order, the shared refrain moves the wife from denial, where her body overrules her words, to surrender, where she grants him the cruelty and her body the defeat, so the decade builds a progression out of near-identical lines."},{"a":"ai:15","b":"a:186","type":"tension","note":"Ainkurunuru 15 has the wife say that the man whose town's reed is a float to the bathing women is no husband of hers; in Akananuru 186 the courtesan says their bond was only as far as bathing together on a float of reed (வேழத்துப் புணைதுணை யாகப் புனல்ஆடு கேண்மை).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same reed float is the wife's proof that he belongs to other women and the courtesan's plea that the affair was mere water-play, so the pair shows the marutham reed raft as a contested sign whose meaning depends on which woman speaks."},{"a":"ai:14","b":"n:389","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 14 praises the chest of the ford-town lord for its softness that gives cool sleep; Naladiyar 389's wife of the field-town lord looks at the same chest smeared with sandal by other women's breasts.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The marutham husband's chest is the wife's place of rest in the Sangam poem and the evidence of his other women in the Naladiyar, so the later moralist turns the older image of comfort into the record of its betrayal."},{"a":"ai:18","b":"k:1154","type":"develops","note":"In both the wife recalls that he promised not to leave (பிரியலென், அஞ்சல்) and then left; Ainkurunuru 18 ends on her weeping eyes and the question, Kural 1154 asks whether those who believed his assuring words are at fault.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam wife quotes his broken promise as an accusation, while Valluvar's wife turns it into a question of blame for having believed, so the couplet adds to the older scene a moral reckoning about trust that the poem leaves unasked."},{"a":"ai:20","b":"k:1157","type":"qualifies","note":"In both the wife's bangles slip from her wrist because of the man; in Ainkurunuru 20 they loosen as she thinks of him, in Kural 1157 they broadcast that he has left.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam poem treats the slipping bangle as the private effect of memory, while Valluvar makes the same bangle a gossip that announces his desertion to everyone, so the couplet turns an inner sign into public scandal."},{"a":"ai:21","b":"k:1154","type":"qualifies","note":"Both turn on assurance (தெளிப்பவும், தெளித்தசொல்): in Ainkurunuru 21 the friend sets the man's assurances against the girl's paling eyes; in Kural 1154 the woman asks whether those who trusted the assuring word are at fault if he leaves.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural asks who bears the blame once a trusted promise fails, while the Ainkurunuru shows the body already failing while the promise is still being spoken, so the couplet's moral question comes after a doubt that the older poem places in the girl's eyes before any leaving."},{"a":"ai:24","b":"k:1183","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 24 says he takes the beauty (நலங்கொண்டு) of the women he embraced and abandons them; Kural 1183 says he took her grace and modesty and gave sickness and pallor in exchange.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural turns the friend's accusation of plain taking into an exchange in which pallor is the payment, so the same loss that the Sangam friend calls the act of a kin-destroying creature becomes, in the couplet, the woman's own bitter bookkeeping."},{"a":"ai:24","b":"sp:10","type":"develops","note":"Both use the belief that the crab is killed by its own young: Ainkurunuru 24 names the crab born at its mother's death (தாய்சாப் பிறக்கும்), Sirupanchamoolam 10 lists its own brood as death to the crab (ஞெண்டிற்குத் தன்பார்ப்பு).","grade":"sound","better":"same_image","te":"The pairing shows a piece of field lore surviving from the akam landscape into the didactic list: the Sangam friend uses it to charge a man with destroying his own, while the later moralist uses it to say a thing's own offspring or speech is its death, so the same belief turned from accusation of another into warning to oneself.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:25","b":"k:1311","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the man's chest belongs to many women: Ainkurunuru 25's friend says the ooran's chest (மார்புபலர்க்கு) brings grief to many; Kural 1311's wife refuses his chest (மார்பு) because all women feast on it with their eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend counts the cost to the many women his chest has harmed, while the Kural wife refuses that chest because it is shared even by looks, so held together they show the same charge of a common chest used first as report to the mother and then as the wife's own ground for refusal."},{"a":"ai:28","b":"k:1234","type":"develops","note":"Both mark the loss in the shoulders and the slipping bangle: Ainkurunuru 28 has the bright bangle loosening as the soft shoulders pale (ஒண்டொடி நெகிழ, மென் தோள் பசப்பது); Kural 1234 has the bangle slipping from shoulders that have lost their old beauty.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Ainkurunuru friend uses the paled shoulders to tell the mother that a man, not the ford goddess, is the cause, while the Kural keeps only the signs, so the couplet's portrait of wasting shoulders was in the older poem a piece of evidence in a disclosure.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:30","b":"a:176","type":"same_image","note":"Both open on the neem-bud-eyed crab of the marutham field and its earthen hole (வேப்புநனை யன்ன நெடுங்கள் களவன், வேப்புநனை அன்ன நெடுங்கண் நீர்ஞெண்டு, மண்அளை): in Ainkurunuru 30 it fills its hole with paddy blossom while the girl loses her beauty; in Akananuru 176 it flees the heron into its hole in a poem refusing the husband at the door.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The neem-bud simile and the crab's earthen hole are a shared marutham formula, and the two poems make it mean opposite things by the act they give it, hoarding in the four-line poem and fearful hiding in the long one, so in this stock image the sense lies in what the crab does, not in how it looks."},{"a":"ai:31","b":"ku:53","type":"qualifies","note":"Both end on the same words, உற்ற சூளே, an oath sworn at the broad ford of 'our town'; in Kuruntokai 53 the friend says it was sworn holding the girl's forearm with the hill goddesses as witness and now afflicts them, in Ainkurunuru 31 the other woman says it was sworn before the bathing companions and asks whether he will call it no debt.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The witness decides what a broken ford-oath costs: sworn before goddesses it turns into a danger that falls on the women, sworn before human bathers it shrinks to a debt the man can simply disown, so the courtesan's oath is the weaker for lacking a god."},{"a":"ai:31","b":"a:166","type":"tension","note":"In Ainkurunuru 31 the other woman holds the lord to an oath he swore her at the bathing ford; in Akananuru 166 the same kind of lord swears to his wife by the god of Velur that he never bathed with the other woman, and the courtesan asks who then held the float with her yesterday.","grade":"forced","better":"develops","te":"Held together, the two show the marutham lord's oaths cancelling each other: the bathing he swore by to the courtesan is the bathing he forswears to his wife, so one of the two oaths must be false and each woman is left holding an oath the other's makes worthless."},{"a":"ai:32","b":"a:76","type":"develops","note":"Both put the courtesan's report of the wives in the same formula: அழுப என்ப அவன் பெண்டிர் (they say his women weep) in Ainkurunuru 32, கழறுப என்ப அவன் பெண்டிர் (they say his women rail) in Akananuru 76.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The one formula frames the wives two ways, as melting in tears like wax or as clamouring like a bard's drum, and the courtesan's answer scales with it, from a mocking count of days in the short poem to an open vow to hold him by force in the long one."},{"a":"ai:36","b":"k:1207","type":"tension","note":"Ainkurunuru 36 says that if he forgets them, they too could forget and be content, were it not that her eyes go pale; Kural 1207 has the woman say she does not know forgetting, and even remembering burns.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Sangam speaker claims forgetting is in her power and lays the failure on her paling eyes, while the Kural woman denies she can forget at all, so the older poem places love's hold in the body against the will and the couplet moves it into the mind itself."},{"a":"ai:37","b":"k:1232","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 37 has நயந்தோர் உண்கண் பசந்துபனி மல்க, the eyes of those who loved him pale and brimming; Kural 1232 has நயந்தவர் ... பசந்து பனிவாரும் கண், pale eyes streaming that seem to tell of the beloved's unkindness.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Valluvar keeps the Ainkurunuru's very words for the pale, wet eyes but changes their office: in the Sangam poem they are evidence the wronged woman cites against his lying oath, in the couplet they become speakers that publish his unkindness to others, so the image moves from accusation to involuntary gossip."},{"a":"ai:41","b":"a:146","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 41 says the lord turns gold-pale the bodies of those who trusted his word; Akananuru 146 has the wife foresee that the next woman who believes the deceiver's word (வாய்மொழி நம்பி) will weep and go pale as she did.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two show that pallor from a trusted word was a fixed charge against the marutham husband: the Ainkurunuru states it as a habit of his town in four lines, and the Akananuru turns the same charge into a forecast for the rival, so the wronged wife becomes the proof of what awaits the other woman."},{"a":"ai:42","b":"k:1311","type":"develops","note":"In both the wife refuses the straying husband's chest: in Ainkurunuru 42 the friend reports it to him as if it were joy-drunk folly; in Kural 1311 the wife herself refuses it because all women feast on it with their eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The refusal of the chest was a recognised act of the sulking wife before Valluvar; the Sangam friend presents it with mock surprise to shame the man, while the Kural gives the wife her own reason, so the couplet supplies the motive that the older poem leaves to irony."},{"a":"ai:43","b":"a:56","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 43 calls the husband's bard a bigger liar than the husband, full of oaths; Akananuru 56 shows that bard on the street hurrying to bring new brides to the lord, scared into the wife's own house by a cow, and laughed at.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two give the bard's double role in marutham: the Ainkurunuru names the charge (he lies and swears for his master) and the Akananuru shows why the wife can mock him openly, since attacking the go-between lets her shame the husband without naming him."},{"a":"ai:44","b":"s:176","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 44 says the tortoise's young grow by looking at the mother's face; Siddhar 176 says the sea tortoise's eggs take form as the mother thinks of them, and asks when the poet will so reach God.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same belief, that the tortoise nurtures without touch, serves the Sangam friend to argue that a wife needs only her husband's presence and the later siddhar to describe grace given by attention alone, so a piece of animal lore carried a model of care at a distance from household love into devotion."},{"a":"ai:49","b":"a:126","type":"qualifies","note":"In Ainkurunuru 49 the bard-woman pours a few fish and gets much paddy; in Akananuru 126 the bard's daughter in Evvi's Kaviri land will not even take old red paddy for her brothers' vaalai fish and is given pearls.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The barter of the bard's women was a stock picture of the bard family getting more than it gives; the Akananuru uses it as praise of a patron's wealth, while the Ainkurunuru turns the same profit into an accusation, since the bard who gets much for little also gets much for his lies."},{"a":"ai:50","b":"k:1303","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1303 says that not embracing the one who sulks is like adding pain to those already in distress; Ainkurunuru 50 tells the husband who gives no refuge that the household and they suffer, and his wife weeps too.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the cost of withheld grace for one sulking woman, while the Ainkurunuru friend counts it across the whole household, its wealth and its people, so the older poem makes the husband's refusal a failure of the house and not only of love."},{"a":"ai:53","b":"ku:53","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 53 names what afflicts them as the oath he swore at their town's ford, holding her forearm before the water goddesses; Ainkurunuru 53 has the friend ask why the ford should be blamed for their sickness when the cause is his oath.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they show that the ford was both where the oath was sworn and where the household looked for the spirit that seized the girl, so the friend's correction turns the family's diagnosis of possession into a charge of perjury against the man."},{"a":"ai:55","b":"k:1183","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1183 the woman says he took her grace and modesty and gave her sickness and pallor in exchange; in Ainkurunuru 55 the friend tells the man that because he desired her beauty and left her, her brow has paled for many to see.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural keeps the exchange of beauty for pallor as the woman's private complaint, while the Ainkurunuru friend turns the same exchange into public evidence held up to the man, so the pallor that is a lament in the couplet becomes an accusation in the town.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:58","b":"nt:350","type":"qualifies","note":"Both measure a woman's beauty by Iruppai of the generous Viraan: in Natrinai 350 the wife says she would let that old beauty perish rather than accept the straying man; in Ainkurunuru 58 the friend says he seems possessed by it yet keeps house for others.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same rich town names the wife's beauty both as what the straying husband pretends to be captured by and as what the wife herself is ready to lose rather than take him back, so the marutham quarrel is fought over who holds that beauty as a claim."},{"a":"ai:59","b":"k:1102","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1102 says the woman is herself the cure for the sickness she causes the man; in Ainkurunuru 59 the friend says she was once the cure for his love-sick heart but can be no cure for the girl now.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the beloved the only remedy for the man's love-sickness, but the Sangam friend shows a third person who served as that remedy by bringing them together, and who has none for the woman once he strays, so the cure flows only toward the man."},{"a":"ai:60","b":"a:162","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 162 the man tells his heart that he stood in a midnight storm at a corner of her father's tall house until the strict guards slackened; in Ainkurunuru 60 the friend tells the man that he comes to the tall house while all sleep and asks whether he does not fear her father's spear.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The danger at the father's tall house that the lover recounts to himself as proof of his devotion is the very thing the friend names to him as a reason to stop coming by night, so the same risk is a boast in his mouth and a demand for marriage in hers."},{"a":"ai:61","b":"ku:8","type":"same_image","note":"Both open with the ripe sweet mango that falls of itself into the water below; in Kuruntokai 8 the courtesan has the vaalai fish snatch it, in Ainkurunuru 61 it drops into the deep pond of Kazhar, the town the wife uses to measure his new women.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the formula shows the falling fruit as the man who drops to whoever waits below, used by the courtesan to boast she caught him and by the wife to say he keeps falling into new marriages."},{"a":"ai:61","b":"a:226","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 226 sets the husband's scandalous bathing with another woman at Matti's Kazhar ford; Ainkurunuru 61 makes Matti's Kazhar the standard for the women he courts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"For the marutham wife the patron's rich ford had become shorthand for the husband's other women, so a chief's town entered akam as a place of the hero's pleasures, not only of the patron's fame."},{"a":"ai:63","b":"k:1311","type":"develops","note":"Both are the wife in oodal refusing the husband's chest; Ainkurunuru 63 because other women have lain on it, Kural 1311 because all women consume him with their eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet raises the bar of the older refusal: where the Sangam wife needs the touch of other women to refuse him, Valluvar's wife refuses for their looks alone, so the Kural turns a grievance about deeds into a jealousy of glances."},{"a":"ai:63","b":"ku:364","type":"same_image","note":"The same line, the otter getting a vaalai fish as its daily food, addresses the husband in both; in Kuruntokai 364 it is spoken by the household concubine answering the wife's slander, in Ainkurunuru 63 by the wife refusing him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Because rival women use the identical otter line, the image belongs to the husband, a daily hunter in his own pond, and each woman fits it to her side of the quarrel."},{"a":"ai:64","b":"a:266","type":"develops","note":"Both have the wife charging the husband with bathing in the new flood with other women; Ainkurunuru 64 rests on the many who saw it, Akananuru 266 says the talk is loud as a feast and then that this is no grief to her compared with the oath he swore before Murugan.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The long poem moves the offence the short one names: in Ainkurunuru 64 the wound is that many saw, while Akananuru 266 dismisses the gossip and fixes on his broken oath, so publicity was the first charge but perjury the deeper one."},{"a":"ai:64","b":"ku:80","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 80 has the other woman announce she will bathe with lilies in her hair at the flooded ford and dare the wife to guard her husband; Ainkurunuru 64 has the wife report that many saw him bathing there with his beloved.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Read together, the public bathing that the wife cites as proof of his fault is the very display the rival intends as her victory, so the same act counts as scandal in one voice and as triumph in the other."},{"a":"ai:65","b":"nt:380","type":"develops","note":"Both refuse the husband by pointing to the body of a nursing mother: Ainkurunuru 65 says her son-bearing body would spoil his chest; in Natrinai 380 the friend lists the ghee, smoke, milk and birth-smell that make them no match for the man of the courtesans' street.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Natrinai 380 itemises the household marks that Ainkurunuru 65 leaves as one sarcastic phrase, showing that 'it would spoil your chest' means the labour of the home set against the adorned women of the street."},{"a":"ai:66","b":"a:16","type":"develops","note":"In Ainkurunuru 66 the wife asks who the woman is who stole his homecoming to his toddling son; in Akananuru 16 the wife tells how a young courtesan found that son alone in the chariot street and hugged him as her own.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The son is the wife's claim on the husband, and the pair shows the rival reaching for it from both ends: diverting the father on his way to the child, and taking the child into her own arms."},{"a":"ai:67","b":"ku:8","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 8 has the courtesan boast that the husband talks big at her house but dances like a mirror puppet to his son's mother; Ainkurunuru 67 has the wife call the new woman naive for matching herself against her, since the women who have drawn him off outnumber bees. On one reading each woman claims the other holds no power over him.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Each side belittles the other through the man's nature, the courtesan by making him the wife's puppet and the wife by making him a bee of countless flowers, so neither woman's speech can be taken as the truth of the household."},{"a":"ai:68","b":"nt:300","type":"qualifies","note":"Natrinai 300 has the water-lily bend to the lotus in the wind as attendants bow to an angry princess; Ainkurunuru 68 has the lily open at dawn like the lotus, while the wife complains that the other woman will not hold herself in.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows that lily and lotus were a shared code of rank in marutham: in Natrinai the lesser flower bows and order holds, in Ainkurunuru it apes the greater flower, which is exactly the other woman's offence."},{"a":"ai:69","b":"ai:125","type":"same_image","note":"The line 'she stood crying till her kohl eyes went red' closes both; in 69 the river flood sweeps away the rival's sand-play at the bathing ford, in 125, a neythal poem, the clear wave snatches her doll.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The mockery of the rival as a crying child was a stock retort carried across landscapes by changing only the water and the toy, river flood and sand-play for marutham, sea wave and doll for neythal."},{"a":"ai:70","b":"a:26","type":"develops","note":"Both set the wife who has borne a son against the women he prefers: in Ainkurunuru 70 his women are clean and fragrant while she is like a ghoul; in Akananuru 26 she turns to the boy saying 'you suit the good women; I suit this rich one' after the husband shrank from her milk.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 26 shows the scene behind the four-line retort, the husband's flinch from the nursing body, so the Ainkurunuru 'ghoul' reads as the wife's bitter echo of his own recoil."},{"a":"ai:71","b":"a:266","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 71: the wife says people report he bathed yesterday with his beloved (நெருநை ... ஆடினை என்ப), and the gossip can no more be hidden than sunlight. Akananuru 266: the wife opens with the same report (ஆடினை என்ப மகிழ்ந, நெருநை), compares the uproar to a victory feast, then says the gossip is no grief to her beside his broken oath.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same reported charge, that he bathed with her yesterday, is the whole case in the Ainkurunuru but only the preface in the Akananuru, whose wife sets the gossip aside for the oath he broke, so the pair shows the bathing formula as a fixed opening of marutham complaint that a longer poem could push past to a deeper wrong."},{"a":"ai:74","b":"pn:243","type":"same_image","note":"Ainkurunuru 74: a young woman climbs the marutham tree on the bank and leaps into the water, her hair spreading like a descending peacock. Purananuru 243: an old man recalls how in youth he climbed the low marutham branch at the ford and leapt into the deep pool to the wonder of those on the bank.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The leap from the riverside marutham was a common game of youth in the field towns, and the two books use it oppositely: the akam poet freezes it as the height of a woman's beauty seen by a lover, while the puram poet, leaning on his staff, makes the same leap the measure of what age has taken."},{"a":"ai:77","b":"ku:80","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 77: the other woman asks the husband to bathe with her so that gossip rises in the town, and not to go home. Kuruntokai 80: the courtesan says she will go to bathe at the flooded ford, and if the wife is afraid, let her guard her husband's chest with her kin like Ezhini's herds.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kuruntokai courtesan aims her dare at the wife, while the Ainkurunuru rival speaks to the man and names the gossip itself as her goal, so together they show the flood-bath as a public move in the rivalry, staged to be talked about and to pull him from his house, not a pleasure she hides."},{"a":"ai:78","b":"a:312","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 78: a woman asks the man to bathe in the dam-breaking flood, likened to Killi's wall-breaking elephant, and take her shoulders as his raft. Akananuru 312: the friend says that if the hill lord means to marry, they will bathe in the flood with his chest as their raft, and the lightning is likened to Vazhuthi's spear.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The raft of the lover's body runs in both directions: in the kurinji poem the girls take the man's chest as their float as the reward of a promised marriage, while in the marutham poem a woman offers her shoulders as his float to draw him away, and both dress the flood in a king's weapon, so the same intimate game and the same borrowed war image serve the making of a marriage in one land and its straining in the other."},{"a":"ai:80","b":"n:387","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 80: the wife asks why his eyes are so red after he bathed in the first-rain flood as the shoulder-companion of fine women. Naladiyar 387: the wife complains that the man who embraced other women comes to embrace her too without bathing.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"In the Naladiyar the husband's fault is that he comes to his wife unwashed from other women, while in the Ainkurunuru the bath itself is the scene of his straying and its red eyes the evidence, so the pair shows bathing in the field country as both the cleansing a wife expects after his absence and the occasion of the absence."},{"a":"ai:80","b":"kl:98","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 80: the wife asks why his eyes are red after bathing in the first-rain flood with fine women. Kalittokai 98: the husband says he was delayed bathing in the new flood of the Vaigai, and the wife turns the flood, point by point, into the woman he bathed with.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kalittokai shows the flood-bath as the husband's ready alibi and the wife unpicking it at length, while the Ainkurunuru wife grants the bath in a word and reads his red eyes as the trace of the women in it, so the pair shows the new flood as the standard excuse of the marutham husband and its marks on his body as the wife's standard proof.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:80","b":"k:1313","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 80: the wife, saying she is not sulking, reads his reddened eyes after the flood-bath as a sign of the women he bathed with. Kural 1313: the husband says that even if he wears a wreath of branch flowers, she grows angry, saying he wore it to show another woman.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both turn the husband's body into evidence in a love-quarrel, but the Kural's husband protests that the sign is innocent and the suspicion hers, while the Ainkurunuru sign comes from a bath with women he did take, so together they show that the oodal's reading of marks on the man can be either jealous fancy or plain proof, and the couplet's comedy depends on the scene being the first."},{"a":"ai:81","b":"nt:100","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 81: the other woman says his wife would grieve if she heard him say he desired her. Natrinai 100: the other woman laughs at how the man trembled when she said she would go and tell his wife.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In both the other woman's hold on the man is the wife's knowledge: the Ainkurunuru voices it as mock pity for the wife, the Natrinai as a threat that sets him shaking, so together they show that the courtesan's power in marutham rests on what the wife may hear."},{"a":"ai:84","b":"k:1311","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 84: the other woman calls his chest a Thai bathing pond that many plunge into. Kural 1311: the wife refuses his chest because all women feast on it with their eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural wife's complaint that his chest is common property is made, in the Ainkurunuru, by one of the women who share it, who boasts of it rather than complains, so the same image of the common chest serves as reproach from the house and as triumph from outside it."},{"a":"ai:84","b":"ai:4","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 4: the girls wish that the lover's chest never become a common field-pond (பழனம்). Ainkurunuru 84: the other woman says his chest is a pond where many bathe.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The decade of wishes prays against exactly the outcome this decade reports, his chest as a pond for all, so the anthology's own order sets the bride's fear and the courtesan's taunt in one image eight decades apart."},{"a":"ai:87","b":"a:186","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 87: the other woman says the wife sulks at everyone, so why at her. Akananuru 186: the courtesan says the wife quarrels with her (எம்மொடு புலக்கும்), but she is not the enemy; the husband who leaves women pale is.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The four-line protest that the wife's sulk is aimless becomes in the Akananuru a full defence that redirects the blame to the husband, so the pair shows the courtesan's answer to the wife's anger as a set speech with two steps: she is not singled out, and the fault lives in the wife's own house."},{"a":"ai:89","b":"n:390","type":"tension","note":"Ainkurunuru 89: the other woman tells the bard the wife is greatly graced, only because she wants the name of wife. Naladiyar 390: the wife tells the bard not to lie that he will be kind; she is the dry end of the cane and the others its sweet middle. On a plain reading each woman says the other has his favour.","grade":"forced","better":"develops","te":"Each woman tells the same messenger that the other is the favoured one, so the bard meets the identical complaint at both doors and the triangle has no one who admits to being loved; the Naladiyar files that voice under the chaste wife, the Ainkurunuru gives it to the rival."},{"a":"ai:90","b":"ku:8","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 90: the other woman says the husband has the bee's nature, and his son's mother wrongly sulks with her. Kuruntokai 8: the other woman says the man boasts in her house but at home moves like a mirror image to whatever his son's mother wants.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Both courtesans name the wife only as his son's mother, but one says he roves like a bee and the other that he obeys his wife like a puppet, so together they show the other woman claiming no hold on him at all: he belongs to every flower and, at home, to the mother of his son.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:92","b":"ku:181","type":"develops","note":"Both set a newly calved buffalo cow beside her calf in the marutham town: in Ainkurunuru 92 the suitor names it as he promises to come for the girl, in Kuruntokai 181 the wife names the same cow, grazing near the calf the ploughman tied, as she speaks of matrons worn by the great house's duties.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The suitor's emblem of the home he offers becomes, in the wife's mouth, the emblem of the home that has used her up, so the one buffalo image measures how courtship's promise of motherhood reads after years of karpu."},{"a":"ai:93","b":"ku:2","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 2 has the man ask the bee whether any flower it knows is more fragrant than the girl's hair; Ainkurunuru 93 shows bees, already fed full in many groves, coming to hum at her flowered crown.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What Kuruntokai 2 leaves as a question to the bee, Ainkurunuru 93 answers from the bee's own conduct, since a bee sated on every grove that still seeks her hair has given the verdict the lover asked for."},{"a":"ai:94","b":"nt:260","type":"qualifies","note":"Both liken the marutham bull buffalo to field warriors (மள்ளர் அன்ன, மள்ளரின்): in Ainkurunuru 94 it rests with its mate in the shaded pool of the girl's father's fields, in Natrinai 260 it leaves the lotus it is tired of and sleeps alone on the sand, in the wife's refusal of the lord.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same warrior-like bull stands for the husband to come when it stays beside its mate and for the husband who strays when it wanders off sated, so the image's sense in marutham is fixed by whether the beast keeps to its partner, not by its strength."},{"a":"ai:95","b":"a:46","type":"develops","note":"Both open on a buffalo that breaks its tether (கயிறுபரிந்து; நோன் தளை பரிந்து) and grazes the town's crops; Akananuru 46 anchors the image in a second marriage over sixteen lines, Ainkurunuru 95 only in 'he gave us a hard sickness even by day'.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"That a four-line poem can rest the whole charge on the rope-breaking buffalo, with no wedding named, shows the image was already a fixed sign of the straying lord, which the longer Akananuru poem spells out rather than invents."},{"a":"ai:98","b":"k:57","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 98 has the friend tell the lover that the girl's parents guard her more strictly than she guards herself; Kural 57 asks what use guarding a woman by confinement is, since the guard of her own steadfastness is the best.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's rule ranks a woman's own guard above confinement, but the Sangam friend relies on the confinement exactly because in courtship the girl's own guard has already given way, so the household's watch the couplet finds useless is what turns a lover into a husband."},{"a":"ai:99","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"Both say the woman is the cure of the love sickness: Kural 1102 says every other illness has a different cure but hers is cured by herself; Ainkurunuru 99 says the broad-shouldered girl has become the medicine for his sickness.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet's paradox that the cause is the cure is put by the Sangam poem as something that has already happened (ஆகிய), placing the claim after the first meeting, which is where the Kural sets it too, under the joy of union."},{"a":"ai:99","b":"nt:80","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 80 has the man, kept away, say there is no medicine but the broad-shouldered girl for his sickness; Ainkurunuru 99 has him say the broad-shouldered girl has become the medicine for it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The one formula of the girl as the only medicine marks the two ends of courtship, a remedy denied while the friend keeps him off in Natrinai 80 and a remedy received in Ainkurunuru 99, so the tense of the line tells where in the affair the man stands."},{"a":"ai:101","b":"k:1102","type":"qualifies","note":"Both make the cause of love-sickness its cure; in Kural 1102 the man says the girl is the medicine for the sickness she gives, in Ainkurunuru 101 the friend tells the mother the lord's chariot is the medicine for the sickness the girl's eyes caught.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Valluvar states the paradox as the lover's private wonder, while the Sangam friend uses the same paradox as evidence before the mother, so the idea that only the beloved can cure love began as an argument for marriage before it was a maxim of delight."},{"a":"ai:101","b":"a:22","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 22 the girl laughs inwardly that the velan's rite was wasted when her lover, the true cure, came by night; in Ainkurunuru 101 the friend tells the mother outright that the coming chariot is the cure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two show the same diagnosis moving from the girl's secret mockery of the mother's remedy to the friend's open correction of it, which is the step the disclosure to the mother exists to take."},{"a":"ai:102","b":"nt:145","type":"develops","note":"In Natrinai 145 the friend tells the girl that the unjust mother suspects them and still the chariot bell comes softly down their street at midnight; in Ainkurunuru 102 the friend tells the mother herself that the chariot bell rings sweetly to end the girl's grief.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The chariot bell is the sound of stealth to be hidden from a suspicious mother in one poem and the sound announced to that mother as relief in the other, so the pair shows the disclosure changing the meaning of the lover's signal from danger to remedy."},{"a":"ai:105","b":"a:156","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 156 the mother, not knowing the cause, weeps and offers toddy and a goat to the god of the ford while the girl's gem-dark skin turns gold; in Ainkurunuru 105 the friend tells the mother that the girl's brow has gone more golden than gold since the lord came.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The golden pallor that sends the mother to the god in the Akananuru is given its human cause in the Ainkurunuru, so reading them together shows that the friend's disclosure is the answer to the mother's misdirected rite."},{"a":"ai:107","b":"nt:61","type":"develops","note":"In Natrinai 61 the mother asks the sleepless girl 'don't you sleep, my little one?' and the girl keeps the reason in her heart; in Ainkurunuru 107 the friend tells the mother that the girl does not sleep whenever she hears the waves.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The question the girl will not answer in the Natrinai is answered for her by the friend in the Ainkurunuru, so the pair shows the friend speaking the very secret the girl's modesty withholds from the mother."},{"a":"ai:108","b":"ai:109","type":"develops","note":"Both open on எந்தோள் துறந்த and ask எவன்கொல்: 108 sets the abandoned shoulders against the shoulders he desired, 109 against the many days of his grace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read as a pair, the near-identical poems move the friend's charge from the body he wanted to the time he spent, so the decade builds the case against the lord in two steps, desire then history, before the mother."},{"a":"ai:110","b":"k:1130","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1130 the woman says he lives in her heart while this town (இவ் வூர்) says he is loveless and far; in Ainkurunuru 110 the friend says we call him ours while this town (இவ்வூர்) says otherwise, and asks fate to make it so.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both set the lovers' word against the town's, but Valluvar's woman rests in her inner certainty while the Sangam friend turns to fate to make the bond public, so the couplet's private comfort appears in the older poem as a demand for marriage.","gr":3},{"a":"ai:109","b":"k:1209","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1209 the woman's life fails as she dwells on the lack of grace of one who said they were not apart; in Ainkurunuru 109 the friend asks what the many days of his grace were worth now that he has left.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Valluvar turns the memory of past grace into a slow inward death, while the Sangam friend turns it into a reproach spoken to the mother, so the same memory serves as private lament in the couplet and as public evidence in the decade."},{"a":"ai:111","b":"ku:344","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 111 says she cannot live parted because she cannot do hard penance (அருந்தவம் முயறல் ஆற்றாதேமே); Kuruntokai 344 says those who see their lovers return must have done penance (நோற்றோர் மன்ற).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In one poem penance is the endurance a woman would need to survive separation, in the other the merit that earns the lover's return, so Sangam akam uses tapas both as a power to bear absence and as the price of its ending."},{"a":"ai:111","b":"k:261","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 261 defines தவம் as bearing the pain that comes and harming no life; Ainkurunuru 111's woman says she is no performer of hard penance and so could not live apart from him.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The heroine's disclaimer assumes exactly the Kural's definition of penance as the bearing of suffering, so the pair shows that the endurance of separation was already counted as an ascetic feat, and that the lover's refusal of it is a refusal of tapas."},{"a":"ai:112","b":"k:1257","type":"agrees","note":"Both are the woman's own words: Kural 1257 says she knows nothing called modesty when the beloved does what love wants; Ainkurunuru 112 tells her modest heart they have forgotten, as they wait to see him come.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's general claim that shame vanishes before love is shown in the poem as a single moment, the heroine speaking to the heart that owns modesty just as she gives it up to watch him arrive."},{"a":"ai:113","b":"k:1147","type":"develops","note":"Both pair the town's gossip with the mother's word: Kural 1147 says gossip is manure and mother's words water to this sickness; in Ainkurunuru 113 the town names her the lord's woman, mother calls her, and she says it is only a fever (வெம்மை).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural calls the love a sickness that gossip and the mother feed, and the poem shows the girl turning that very likeness into her alibi, naming the fever and hiding its cause, so the couplet's metaphor began as the lover's cover story.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:116","b":"k:1225","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1225 asks what good she did to morning and what harm to evening; Ainkurunuru 116 has evening come while they weep, having driven before it a morning like itself.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural keeps a kind morning against a hostile evening, while the older poem, on one reading, lets the evening swallow the morning too, so the couplet's neat split of the day is a later tidying of a grief that had no safe hour.","gr":4},{"a":"ai:117","b":"k:1235","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1235 has the wasted shoulders declare the cruelty of the cruel man (கொடியார் கொடுமை); in Ainkurunuru 117 the woman says it is cruel (கொடிதே) that beauty comes to this in those who do not forget him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend in the Kural lays the cruelty on the man, while the heroine in the poem lays it on the condition of loving him, so the same lost beauty is a charge against him from outside and a lament without an accused from within."},{"a":"ai:118","b":"k:1284","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1284: she went to sulk, but her heart forgot it and went to unite; Ainkurunuru 118: she went meaning to rage at the unjust man and hold him back, but thought again, relented and came away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both women set out angry and lose the anger on the way, but the Kural's anger turns into union while the Sangam heroine's turns into retreat, so a failed quarrel need not end in an embrace: it can end in pity and silence."},{"a":"ai:120","b":"k:1265","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1265 wishes to see the கொண்கன் with full eyes so that the pallor of her soft shoulders (மென்தோள்) may leave; Ainkurunuru 120 reports that her soft shoulders (மெல் தோளே) have become beautiful because the lord has come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states as a hope that the sight of the shore lord will cure the shoulders, and the Ainkurunuru records the cure as done, so the couplet's optative has an older poem in which the wish was already fulfilled."},{"a":"ai:121","b":"nt:20","type":"develops","note":"Both answer a husband who denies the other woman with the same verb, கண்டிகும் (we saw): Natrinai 20's heroine describes a woman leaving his bed with crushed garland and loosened hair, Ainkurunuru 121's friend a girl leaping into the sea with her garland soaked.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The one retort to a denial can be built two opposite ways, by showing the rival as a spent lover whose body carries his marks or as a child at play, so the 'we saw her' formula's sting lies in how the witness chooses to portray the woman, not in the seeing."},{"a":"ai:122","b":"nt:30","type":"qualifies","note":"In Natrinai 30 the friend tells the denying husband கண்டனென் ... கண்டு எவன்செய்கோ, I saw, and what can I do having seen; in Ainkurunuru 122 the friend's having seen becomes a mocking portrait of a girl asking a heron for her jewel.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Natrinai 30 admits that the friend's witness gives her no power over the husband, and the Ainkurunuru decade shows what she does with that powerless witness instead: she turns it into ridicule, the one weapon left to a go-between who cannot compel."},{"a":"ai:124","b":"pn:243","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 243's old man remembers garlanding a doll of packed sand and leaping into deep water as the untaught youth he has lost; Ainkurunuru 124 shows a girl with a sand doll punishing the sea that took it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same shore games of sand dolls and leaps into water are, in the puram elegy, the innocence age mourns, and in the akam barb, the proof that a man has taken up with a child, so the scene of youth carries nostalgia or contempt according to who recalls it and for what end."},{"a":"ai:127","b":"nt:170","type":"tension","note":"Both have the wife's side describe the rival by her dress: in Natrinai 170 the girl in a leaf skirt who comes alone to the festival is a danger the wives must rise against, while in Ainkurunuru 127 the garland across budding breasts marks her as a child of no account.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"On a defensible reading the two friends make incompatible claims about what a young rival's youth means, a strength that may overwhelm the household or a weakness that makes her laughable, showing that the wife's party had no settled verdict on the young courtesan and chose the one their moment needed."},{"a":"ai:131","b":"k:1147","type":"tension","note":"In Ainkurunuru 131 the woman says the lord's friendship is good only while no gossip rises in the town; in Kural 1147 the woman says the town's gossip is the manure on which her love-sickness grows.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Valluvar's woman turns the gossip into nourishment for her love, while the Sangam woman, speaking to the man's bard, treats the same gossip as the limit the bond cannot outlive, so the couplet's paradox rewrites a fear the older poem left plain."},{"a":"ai:132","b":"ai:131","type":"develops","note":"131 makes the friendship good only so long as gossip does not rise; 132 says the town has now made gossip of the lord's very grace.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence the decade's first two poems pass from the condition to its breach: the gossip that 131 named as the bond's limit has arisen in 132, and it has risen not from his neglect but from his kindness."},{"a":"ai:133","b":"k:1234","type":"develops","note":"Both have the woman's bangled shoulders lose their beauty when her partner leaves (Kural: துணைநீங்கித் தொல்கவின் வாடிய தோள்; Ainkurunuru: பிரிந்தெனப் புல்லென்றன என் புரிவளைத் தோளே).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet describes the wasted shoulders as a fact, while the poem puts them as a helpless question to the absent man's own bard, so the same bodily sign becomes in the older poem a complaint lodged with his side of the household.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:134","b":"k:1265","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1265 the woman wishes to see her husband (கொண்கனை) and says the pallor of her shoulders will then leave; in Ainkurunuru 134 she tells the bard her dark-gold beauty has come back with the chariot of the shore lord (கொண்க).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Valluvar states as a wish that only the sight of the husband will cure the pallor; the Sangam poem shows the cure already happened, the beauty arriving in his chariot, so the couplet's claim that news is not enough rests on the older scene of the bodily return."},{"a":"ai:137","b":"ai:134","type":"tension","note":"134 says the woman's beauty came back by itself with the shore lord; 137 asks whether the women who loved the lord ever get back their former beauty, implying they do not.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Within one decade the same speaker holds both that beauty returns with the lord and that those who loved him never regain it, so the decade shows the restored beauty of 134 as one turn of a cycle that 137 suspects will not last."},{"a":"ai:136","b":"nt:167","type":"qualifies","note":"Both refuse the bard who comes with the shore lord's words: Natrinai 167 says his humble words will not remove the pallor of the bangled girl, Ainkurunuru 136 calls him shameless for pouring out words for the lord who made her bangles slip.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Natrinai friend grants the bard's skill and denies only its effect, while the Ainkurunuru woman denies him decency itself, so set together they show the refusal of the go-between ranging from courtesy to contempt within the same neythal situation."},{"a":"ai:138","b":"ai:136","type":"develops","note":"136 (நாணிலை மன்ற பாண) blames the bard for spilling words for the lord; 138 (பண்பிலை மன்ற பாண) blames him for harsh words in the town and for not bringing the lord.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The paired rebukes show the bard condemned from both sides, for speaking on the lord's behalf and for failing to deliver the lord, so what the woman rejects is his offering words where only the man's presence would do."},{"a":"ai:139","b":"a:146","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 146 the wife, refusing the bard, says the woman who trusts the deceiving philanderer's word (மாயப் பரத்தன் வாய்மொழி) will lose her beauty; in Ainkurunuru 139 she tells the lord that his bard ruins good women's beauty more than he does.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The longer poem lays the ruin of beauty on the husband's own lying word, the short one shifts it to the bard who carries that word, so the pair shows the go-between counted as an agent of the harm and not merely its messenger.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:140","b":"nt:291","type":"develops","note":"In both the bard who came for the shore lord is told to report what he has seen of the woman: Natrinai 291 கண்டாங்கு உரையாய், Ainkurunuru 140 காண்மதி பாண நீ உரைத்தற்கு உரியை.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Ainkurunuru 140 names the message only as the state of her slipped bangles, while Natrinai 291 fills the same eyewitness report with a puram image of cattle raided at night, so the pair shows the three-line convention of the bard as witness expanded by borrowing from war poetry."},{"a":"ai:140","b":"k:1157","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1157 the woman says her slipping bangles spread the news that the shore lord (துறைவன்) has left her; in Ainkurunuru 140 she asks the bard to tell the lord of the ford (துறைகெழு கொண்கன்) the state of her bangles since he left.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Valluvar makes the bangles themselves the gossips who publish his departure, while the Sangam woman needs the bard to carry that sign to the man, so the couplet's bangles that speak for themselves compress a scene in which the body's testimony still had to be delivered."},{"a":"ai:142","b":"k:1207","type":"tension","note":"Ainkurunuru 142's heroine resolves not to think of the shore lord so that her eyes may sleep; in Kural 1207 the woman says she does not know how to forget and asks what would become of her if she did.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Sangam heroine treats not-thinking as a cure she can choose for her sleeplessness, while Valluvar's woman declares forgetting impossible and fatal, so the couplet denies exactly the resolve the older poem lets her voice."},{"a":"ai:142","b":"ku:243","type":"develops","note":"Both close on the same line, உள்ளேன் தோழி படீஇயர் என் கண்ணே, after a bird-loud shore; Kuruntokai 243 fills the shore with girls playing at sand houses, Ainkurunuru 142 with birds settling to roost.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The refusal to think of him was a stock closing formula, and each poet earns it with a different shore picture, the girls' play recalling her past in one and the roosting birds setting her sleeplessness against their rest in the other."},{"a":"ai:144","b":"ai:146","type":"qualifies","note":"The two poems end on the same words, என் மாமைக் கவினே, for the same shore lord: in 144 the beauty has turned pale, in 146 it is surely sweet to him.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Read together, the decade measures the whole state of the love by one phrase about her complexion, which can stand for neglect or for delight with nothing changed but the verb."},{"a":"ai:145","b":"k:1188","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1188 people say only that she has grown pale and never that he has left; in Ainkurunuru 145 the friend says the shore lord has now taken her pallor away.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend names the man as the one who can remove the pallor, which makes him its cause, so the Sangam poem supplies the very attribution that the couplet's woman complains no one makes."},{"a":"ai:147","b":"nt:359","type":"develops","note":"In Natrinai 359 the hill lord's gift of a leaf skirt is a danger, since wearing it will alarm the mother; in Ainkurunuru 147 the shore lord gives his country as the price of the leaf skirt.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two poems show the leaf skirt moving from a secret courting gift that cannot be worn openly to the token for which a bride price is paid, so the same object marks both ends of secret love."},{"a":"ai:149","b":"k:1151","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are spoken to the man about to leave, by the friend on one reading of the Kural: Ainkurunuru 149 asks him not to become able to go after causing her affliction, Kural 1151 tells him to promise a quick return only to those who will survive it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam friend still appeals to the man's character, asking him not to be the kind who can leave, whereas the couplet has given up persuasion and answers his promise with the threat of her death.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:150","b":"k:1152","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 150's heroine calls the shore lord bitter in union (புணர்வின் இன்னான்) and hard to unite with; in Kural 1152 the woman says union now holds pain for fear of parting.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Valluvar gives the reason the Sangam poem leaves implicit, that union turns bitter because parting is foreseen, while the poem's pun of the ever-embracing wave (புணரி) shows what that bitterness is measured against."},{"a":"ai:151","b":"k:1246","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are the woman speaking of her heart against the man in oodal: the couplet says the heart's anger is false because it yields at the sight of him; Ainkurunuru 151 grants that the heart has already melted and refuses all the same.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet treats a heart that yields as proof the quarrel was never real, while the poem keeps the refusal standing after the heart has gone over, so the Sangam woman's oodal is a decision of will against her own feeling rather than a mood that the man's presence dissolves."},{"a":"ai:154","b":"ku:34","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 154's woman can only call the town a liar and ask what she can do; in Kuruntokai 34 the friend says the town would not believe their denials (மறுப்பத் தேறலர்) and is silenced only by the sweet news that the lord will marry her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show that in the neythal love story gossip cannot be ended by calling it false, since the town does not believe denials, and the only cure the poets allow is marriage, which turns the lying town into one that rejoices.","gr":4},{"a":"ai:159","b":"ku:349","type":"tension","note":"Ainkurunuru 159's friend demands that the lord give back her friend's beauty and go, insisting she is not begging; Kuruntokai 349's heroine refuses her friend's proposal to take back their beauty, saying that asking for it back is like demanding the return of what was given to those who begged, and worse than losing life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"On a defensible reading the two make incompatible claims about the demand 'give back her beauty', a just reclaiming of what was taken or a shameful asking-back of a gift, which shows the formula belonged to the friend's voice and that the heroine could reject it as beneath her.","gr":3},{"a":"ai:159","b":"nt:395","type":"develops","note":"Both are Ammuvanar's and end on the same demand, தந்தனை சென்மோ ... நலனே and நலம் தந்து சென்மே; Natrinai 395's colophon says the friend uses it to press the lover to marry.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same poet's colophoned use of the formula supplies the occasion that this decade's colophons leave out, so 159's demand for the beauty back can be read as a push to marriage (வரைவு கடாயது) rather than a farewell."},{"a":"ai:163","b":"k:1157","type":"develops","note":"Same situation and speaker: the woman of the shore says the lord of the ford (துறைவன்) has left her and her bangles slip from her forearm; the Kural keeps the Ainkurunuru's words துறைவன், துற, முன்கை, இறை and வளை.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet lifts a neythal formula whole, but gives the bangles a new office: in the Ainkurunuru they imitate his desertion, in the Kural they broadcast it like gossip, so the later poet turned the body from mirror of the man into witness to the town."},{"a":"ai:167","b":"k:805","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural tells friends to read a painful act of an old friend as folly or deep intimacy; the heroine of 167 calls the man who promised grace and gave none an old companion, தொல் கேள்.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The heroine applies the ethics book's rule of old friendship inside love, and the poem shows its cost: the same rule that keeps a long bond alive lets a broken promise go unanswered, so the wronged side carries the whole duty of forbearance."},{"a":"ai:168","b":"a:48","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 48 the foster mother asks why her daughter will not drink milk (பாலும் உண்ணாள்) and has paled; in 168 the friend tells the man that the girl will drink milk again if he shows grace.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Refusing milk is a fixed sign of secret love that the friend can turn two ways: toward the household, as the symptom she explains in disclosing the affair, and toward the lover, as the cure only he can give, so one sign drives both halves of the push toward marriage."},{"a":"ai:170","b":"k:1190","type":"tension","note":"On the plain reading the claims are incompatible: in 170 the woman holds up her pallid eyes as evidence against the friend's word that he is good, while in Kural 1190 she would rather be called pallid than have the friend blame his unkindness.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Read together, the two show the heroine taking the side opposite to whoever speaks to her, so her pallor is not a fixed verdict on the man but an argument she turns against his praise or for his defence as the friend's stance requires."},{"a":"ai:169","b":"k:1181","type":"develops","note":"In both the woman has accepted the man's part with her mind, in Kural 1181 by consenting to his absence, in 169 by knowing the truth of his heart, and still the pallor spreads on her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes her consent the reason she cannot complain of her pallor, and the Ainkurunuru adds that even trust in his heart does not stop it, so pallor in this tradition is a bodily fact beyond the will rather than a charge against the man."},{"a":"ai:180","b":"ku:128","type":"qualifies","note":"Both set a wing-worn heron at the ford of Thondi: Kuruntokai 128's பறைதபு நாரை on the eastern sea longs in vain for the ayirai fish of Thondi's harbour, as the man's heart longs for a distant girl; Ainkurunuru 180's பறைதபு முதுகுருகு sits fed on the rich fish the netters bring.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The flightless heron of Thondi is hopeless longing when it must reach the fish itself and secure dependence when the fishermen bring the fish to it, so the friend's version answers the lover's lament: the helpless are fed only when someone provides, which is what she asks the man to do by marrying."},{"a":"ai:178","b":"a:290","type":"qualifies","note":"Both liken the girl to Kuttuvan's Thondi: in Ainkurunuru 178 the courting man calls her like Thondi of Kuttuvan of the just sceptre, while in Akananuru 290 the heroine says her eyes, once like the neythal of Kuttuvan's Thondi harbour, have lost that likeness and turned pale while he delays marriage.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Thondi likeness is the lover's praise in courtship and becomes the heroine's own measure of loss when marriage is delayed, so the beauty the man praises to win her is the very thing his delay spends, and the friend of 179 and 180 is right to press him."},{"a":"ai:173","b":"k:1081","type":"develops","note":"Both have the man name the girl's power with the word அணங்கு: in Kural 1081 he cannot tell whether she is a goddess, a peahen or a woman; in Ainkurunuru 173 her fragrant hair is the அணங்கு and those it seizes lose their sleep.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural leaves the smitten man's question open, and the Sangam poem answers it not by settling what she is but by the symptom, sleepless nights and a cutting pain, so that in the tradition the afflicting goddess is known by what she does to the one who sees her."},{"a":"ai:172","b":"k:1136","type":"develops","note":"Both have the man in secret love say the girl keeps his eyes from sleep at night: Ainkurunuru 172 likens his wakefulness to the never-resting waves of Thondi; Kural 1136 has him, sleepless at midnight, think of riding the palmyra horse.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Ainkurunuru 172 stops at the sleepless night and gives it the image of the restless sea, while the Kural shows where that night leads, to the threat of the madal, so the pair marks sleeplessness as the stage of love just before shame is given up."},{"a":"ai:181","b":"k:1150","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1150 says the town raises the very talk we want and the lover will grant (நல்குவர்) if he wishes; Ainkurunuru 181 says if the shore lord grants (நல்கின்), this clamouring town will be sweet to live in.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the town's clamour as a lever that can push the lover to grant marriage, and the Ainkurunuru poem shows the far side of that lever: once he grants, the same noise becomes the festive kuravai of a town she can live in happily, so gossip and celebration are one sound judged before and after his word."},{"a":"ai:182","b":"a:272","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 272 the mother, seeing the garlanded lover at night, takes him for Murugu and worships; in Ainkurunuru 182 the friend points to a garlanded chest and says he is no god of power.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two poems show the misreading the friend's disclosure has to undo: the household's habit of turning a garlanded young man into a god, which in Akananuru 272 leads to worship and in Ainkurunuru 182 is corrected by naming the garland as a shore man's hand-woven wreath."},{"a":"ai:182","b":"nt:155","type":"qualifies","note":"In Natrinai 155 the hero on the neythal shore asks the girl whether she is a goddess of the sea; in Ainkurunuru 182 the friend says the one who struck the girl at the great ford is no god.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The belief that a beloved seen at the shore must be a divine power runs both ways, the man thinking her a sea goddess and the household thinking her stricken by a god, and in both the cure of the error is the same: the striker turns out to be a human lover who must be named.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:183","b":"k:1225","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1225's woman asks what good she has done the morning and what wrong the evening, so morning is the friendly hour; Ainkurunuru 183 ends that even if morning comes there is no one to clear her pain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's separated woman still has a kind hour in the morning, while the Ainkurunuru heroine denies even that, so the pair shows two degrees of the same grief: one that rises and falls with the day, and one that the day's turn no longer touches."},{"a":"ai:183","b":"k:1227","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1227 says the sickness buds in the morning, ripens through the day and blooms at evening; in Ainkurunuru 183 evening arrives early in the harsh day while the neythal of the backwater closes.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural's flower metaphor for evening pain gains a real flower in the Sangam poem, and the two move in opposite directions: as the shore's neythal closes at evening, the sickness opens, so the landscape's hour of rest is the lover's hour of full bloom of pain.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:184","b":"ku:3","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 3's woman says her love for the hill man is larger than the earth, higher than the sky and deeper than the sea; Ainkurunuru 184's says the friendship with the man whose town the sea adorns is greater than the sea.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Each heroine measures love against her own lover's world: the hill woman needs the whole cosmos, while the shore woman takes the one thing that crowns his town and sets it below their bond, so the boast of measureless love takes its scale from the land of the man loved."},{"a":"ai:185","b":"a:27","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 185's man is taken by the Korkai girl's teeth bright as pearls; in Akananuru 27 the friend says the girl's red mouth with teeth like Korkai pearls may not hold the man who means to leave for wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pearl-toothed mouth of Korkai is what draws the man at first sight in Ainkurunuru 185 and what the friend in Akananuru 27 fears is not enough to keep him, so the same harbour formula marks both the start of the beauty's power and its limit against the call of wealth."},{"a":"ai:186","b":"a:20","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 20 the friend says mother, on the word of spiteful women, has confined the girl because a chariot circles the great ford day and night; in Ainkurunuru 186 the friend quotes mother herself saying a chariot comes often to this ford, so do not go.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the long poem the confinement rests on the town's report, in the short one on the mother's own sight of the chariot, so the friend's warning to the lover can come in two strengths, and the second leaves him no excuse that it is only gossip."},{"a":"ai:188","b":"nt:23","type":"tension","note":"Ainkurunuru 188's man compares his love's eyes to the neythal blooming at dawn at Korkai's great ford; in Natrinai 23 the friend says the girl's eyes have faded like the small-leaved neythal of Korkai's harbour.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same Korkai neythal serves for the eyes in bloom when the lover praises them and for the eyes faded when the friend presses for marriage, so the flower's daily opening and closing lets one image carry both the courtship's first wonder and its later cost."},{"a":"ai:189","b":"ai:190","type":"tension","note":"In 189 the lord of the gentle shore has come and her eyes have become good; in 190 the same மெல்லம் புலம்பன் is the one who made her many-petalled eyes weep.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Placed side by side in the decade with the same name for the man, the two poems make the heroine's eyes a gauge wholly in his hands, healed by his coming and wet by his absence, and the neythal image follows them, dusted with gold in one and cut down in the other.","gr":3},{"a":"ai:190","b":"ai:170","type":"qualifies","note":"In 170 a small white gull tears the neythal of the backwater, and the friend is asked why the eyes of one with such a good man grow pale; in 190 reapers cut down the neythal flower while harvesting white paddy, and the man has made her eyes weep.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both hide the spoiled eyes in a destroyed neythal, but the destroyers differ: the gull tears it to feed, the reapers fell it only because it stands among the grain they want, so the decade offers two kinds of blame, appetite and indifference, for the same harm."},{"a":"ai:190","b":"k:1171","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1171's woman asks why her eyes weep when it was they who showed him to her; Ainkurunuru 190's woman says the lord of the shore surely is the one who made her eyes weep.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"For the same tears the Kural's woman blames her own eyes for having looked, while the Ainkurunuru woman blames the man with an emphatic மன்ற, so the pair shows the two directions in which the abandoned lover can turn the fault, inward on her own seeing or outward on him."},{"a":"ai:191","b":"k:1081","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural man cannot tell whether the woman is a goddess, a peahen or a woman; the Ainkurunuru man knows she is a shore girl and says she is harder to win than the mountain nymphs (வரையர மகளிரின் அரியள்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The likeness to a goddess can carry two different fears: in the couplet it is bewilderment at what she is, in the shore poem it is despair of reaching her, so the divine comparison in akam measures distance as often as beauty.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:192","b":"k:1277","type":"develops","note":"Both have the woman of the shore say her bangles registered the lord's leaving (தணந்தமை ... உணர்ந்த வளை; பிரிந்தென நெகிழ்ந்தன); Ainkurunuru 192 adds that they have grown tight again (வீங்கின).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural treats the bangle only as an early witness of separation, while the Ainkurunuru poem shows it registering his return as well, so the convention is a two-way gauge of the man's presence and not merely a sign of wasting."},{"a":"ai:194","b":"nt:282","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 282's friend, the hero within hearing, describes the slipping bangles, faded brow and the old diviner misreading the illness; Ainkurunuru 194's friend tells the hero directly that the mother has suspected a wrong cause (கொன் ஒன்று கடுத்தனள்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside Natrinai's full scene of pebbles and diviner, the Ainkurunuru friend's two words show the misread illness was familiar enough to be invoked in a half-line, and that in both its use is the same: to press the man to marry before a wrong cure exposes the girl."},{"a":"ai:195","b":"k:1135","type":"develops","note":"Both men say the bangled girl has given them suffering, with the verb of a gift (துயரம் நல்கி; தந்தாள் ... துயர்); in the Kural the gift is the palmyra horse and the evening's pain, in Ainkurunuru 195 a grief that will not pass and lost sleep.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the stages of the same plea to the friend: the Ainkurunuru man stops at sleeplessness, and the couplet shows where his complaint goes next if no one answers it, to the threat of riding the palmyra horse."},{"a":"ai:198","b":"a:380","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 380 the shore lord leaves his chariot far off and asks the girls the name of their town; in Ainkurunuru 198 the tall-shouldered man stands asking about the small ford where girls play.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The question about a ford or a town is the fixed first move of the shore courtship: Ainkurunuru 198 catches it as it is asked, and Akananuru 380 shows the friend later reading the same pretext as the start of a suit that ends in his shame and her consent."},{"a":"ai:199","b":"k:1157","type":"qualifies","note":"In the Kural the slipping bangles spread the news (தூற்றா) that the lord of the shore has left; in Ainkurunuru 199 the woman names him by the bangles he loosened and asks to climb the high dune to gaze at his land.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's woman fears her bangles will publish the loss to the town, while the Ainkurunuru woman makes the loss her name for him and climbs where all can see her looking, so the same token can be suffered as exposure or claimed in the open.","gr":3},{"a":"ai:201","b":"nt:359","type":"develops","note":"In Natrinai 359 the friend says the hill lord gave them a leaf skirt (உடுக்கும் தழை) that they dare not wear for fear of the mother nor return for fear of hurting him; in Ainkurunuru 201 the leaves have become their skirts (எமக்கும் தழையாயின) and the friend asks the mother what tree on his slope they come from.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The leaf skirt that in Natrinai is a gift too dangerous to wear before the mother becomes in the Ainkurunuru the very thing the friend shows her, so the pair traces one courtship token from the secret it endangers to the disclosure it carries."},{"a":"ai:203","b":"nt:53","type":"qualifies","note":"In Natrinai 53 the mother herself says the new stream water coming down from the high hill is medicine and sends the girl to drink and bathe in it; in Ainkurunuru 203 the friend tells the mother that the muddy water of his land is sweeter than their honeyed milk.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The mother who prescribes the water of the far hill without knowing whose hill it is, and the friend who tells her that the water of his land is sweeter than home's milk, together show that in kurinji the lover's water stands for the lover, so the disclosure turns the mother's innocent remedy into a confession."},{"a":"ai:203","b":"ku:356","type":"tension","note":"In Kuruntokai 356 the foster mother grieves that the girl who refused milk mixed with puffed rice must now gulp scalding muddy water (கலுழி) by a dried spring; in Ainkurunuru 203 the girl's side says the muddy water (கலிழி) of his land is sweeter than honeyed milk.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two poems make opposite claims about the same exchange of home's milk for the forest's muddy water, a pitiable loss to the foster mother and a gain to the girl's side, which shows that the pathos of the elopement poems is the mother's view and not the girl's."},{"a":"ai:206","b":"a:272","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 272 the mother sees the night visitor wet from the hill, garlanded with koothalam, and takes him for Murugu, scattering millet in worship; in Ainkurunuru 206 the friend shows the mother a man wet with spray and dew and likens him to a watchman of the rainy hill.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the mother left to herself reads the drenched visitor from the hill as a god, the friend who discloses the love names him as a man like their own field watchmen, so the disclosure works by bringing the stranger down from the sacred to the familiar."},{"a":"ai:209","b":"a:378","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 378 the deserted girl says she lives only because the peak of his hill, crowned with a waterfall, is in sight (வான்தோய் சிமையம் தோன்றலானே); in Ainkurunuru 209 she says she cannot forget him because his cloud-crowned hill never stops appearing (தோன்றல் அனாது).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same visible peak is what keeps the girl alive in the Akananuru and what defeats any demand that she forget in the Ainkurunuru, so together they show the hill working as a bond that neither absence nor counsel can loosen."},{"a":"ai:210","b":"ku:367","type":"develops","note":"In Kuruntokai 367 the friend tells the girl that her shoulders may regain their beauty now that his hill with its cool boulders (துறுகல்) shows after the rain; in Ainkurunuru 210 the friend tells the mother the girl's sickness may abate if she climbs their boulder (துறுகல்) and looks at his hill.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sight of his hill, offered privately as comfort to the girl in the Kuruntokai, becomes in the Ainkurunuru a cure the friend asks the mother to allow, so the pair shows a consolation turned into an open request that names its source."},{"a":"ai:208","b":"ai:210","type":"develops","note":"In 208 the girl's eyes fill with tears whenever his mountain is hidden; in 210 the friend says her sickness may abate if she stands looking at his hill.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together the two poems make the girl's state a function of the hill's visibility, tears when it is hidden and healing when it is seen, which is the argument the friend needs before the mother for letting the girl climb and look.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:202","b":"ai:101","type":"qualifies","note":"Both open on the same refrain to the mother and point to the lover's mount: in the neythal decade the shore lord's chariot is the medicine for the girl's sickness, in the kurinji decade the hill lord's horses have tufted heads like the town's Brahmin boy.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The two disclosure decades present the lover's mount to the mother as evidence in opposite tones, the neythal friend solemnly as a cure and the kurinji friend comically through a homely likeness, so the same act of disclosure could be made by gravity or by disarming laughter."},{"a":"ai:212","b":"k:1154","type":"tension","note":"Ainkurunuru 212: the girl asks why they should be parted from a man of virtue (அறவன்). Kural 1154: the woman asks whether those who trusted the assurances of the one who said 'do not fear' are at fault if he leaves.","grade":"forced","better":"qualifies","te":"The girl rests her whole security on the man's being virtuous, while the Kural imagines that very assurer leaving and moves the blame onto him, so the pair shows that in secret love the only guarantee was his character and the Kural tests it to the point of failure.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:213","b":"k:1263","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 213: the girl says she can live only if he comes. Kural 1263: the wife says she still lives on the longing for her husband's return from war.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"For the wife the longing for return is already enough to keep her alive, while the unmarried girl says life itself waits on his coming, so the pair grades the same dependence: hope sustains in marriage, only presence sustains in secret love."},{"a":"ai:215","b":"k:1228","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 215: at evening the bee hums sweeter than the musicians' flute, and those who leave then will do worse. Kural 1228: the herdsman's flute is the burning evening's messenger and a killing weapon.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the evening flute itself the weapon, while the Ainkurunuru keeps the music sweet and turns its sweetness into the measure of the leaver's cruelty, so together they show evening music wounding the parted woman not by harshness but by sweetness heard alone."},{"a":"ai:217","b":"k:1187","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 217: the friend asks why the girl pales even though the man keeps coming. Kural 1187: the woman says pallor came on her thickly the moment she turned a little in his embrace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend's puzzle, pallor despite his visits, finds its answer in the Kural's hyperbole that the least gap in union breeds pallor, so the pair shows that secret visits, each ending in parting, feed the pallor rather than cure it."},{"a":"ai:218","b":"a:141","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 218: the girl's eyes throb and her bangles tighten, and she asks if he will come. Akananuru 141: answering the anxious friend, the wife says her dreams are sweet and the house birds give good signs, so may he come for the festival.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Akananuru wife reads hope in dreams and household birds while the Ainkurunuru girl reads it in her own eyes and bangles, so the pair shows the omen-reading of akam reaching from the house to the body, and always voiced by the one who waits, against the friend's fear."},{"a":"ai:218","b":"k:1203","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 218: throbbing eyes and tightening bangles promise his coming. Kural 1203: a sneeze that starts and dies away makes the woman fear he began to think of her and stopped.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both women read the absent man in involuntary signs of their own bodies, but the girl's signs come to completion and promise him while the Kural's sneeze aborts and suggests his forgetting, so the pair shows the same bodily sign-language turned to hope or doubt."},{"a":"ai:219","b":"k:1188","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 219: the friend asks why the girl's forehead pales since the hill man went away. Kural 1188: the woman complains that people say she has grown pale, and no one says that he left her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend's question names the man's leaving together with the pallor, which is exactly the acknowledgement the Kural's woman says the world withholds, so the pair shows the friend as the one voice that ties the girl's pallor to the man who caused it.","gr":3},{"a":"ai:221","b":"k:1156","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1156 says that a man who can speak of leaving leaves no hope; Ainkurunuru 221 shows the heroine's beauty fading at the quoted word செல்வல், before any journey begins.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet judges the announcement as a moral sign of hardness, while the poem shows it working on the body at once, so together they make the spoken intention, not the absence, the first wound of parting in Tamil love poetry."},{"a":"ai:224","b":"a:38","type":"qualifies","note":"In Ainkurunuru 224 the heroine says bathing in the falls with them has become hard for him; in Akananuru 38 the friend imagines him finding the pool clear because the girls no longer leap in and bathe there.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same lost bathing in the hill pool is blamed on his growing distance by the heroine and traced by the friend to the girls' own absence, so the end of shared play reads as neglect from inside the house and as confinement from the friend's wider view.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:225","b":"k:1188","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1188 says people call her pale but no one says he left her; Ainkurunuru 225 asks whether the lover himself does not perceive the paling of her brow.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together they show the pallor seen by the whole town yet unread by the one man it concerns, so the symptom that exposes her to gossip fails at the only task she wants of it, reaching him."},{"a":"ai:227","b":"nt:358","type":"qualifies","note":"Both heroines face a lover who swore assurances to the friend and stays apart while her beauty fades; Natrinai 358's heroine goes to pray that the oath's god not harm him, Ainkurunuru 227's asks the friend whether she has released him from it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"An oath sworn before the friend binds the lover through a god's punishment, so the heroine is caught between wanting it held and fearing what holding it would do to him, and the two poems show the two halves of that bind."},{"a":"ai:228","b":"k:1155","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1155 asks the friend to prevent the departure of the man fit for her, since union is hard once he goes; Ainkurunuru 228 asks what becomes of their life if he leaves without having pleaded for favour.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The poem supplies the mechanism the couplet leaves out: in secret love the man's suit must pass through the friend, so the heroine's plea that the friend stop his leaving is a plea that she accept his pleading."},{"a":"ai:229","b":"k:1265","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1265 says the pallor of her shoulders will leave once she has seen her husband her eyes' fill; in Ainkurunuru 229 the friend sees the brow's golden beauty returned and asks whether he came in the night.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the lover's presence the cure for pallor, and the poem turns that rule into a means of detection, so the cure that the heroine longs for is also what betrays her secret meeting to the friend."},{"a":"ai:230","b":"nt:386","type":"develops","note":"In Natrinai 386 the friend recalls the hill-lord coming humbly with the word வதுவை so that the village shone; in Ainkurunuru 230 the friend announces the wedding for the hill-lord who once guarded millet with them and wasted the heroine's beauty.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both friends mark the wedding as the end of kalavu, but one remembers the suitor's humility and the other his damage, so the go-between's announcement of marriage could be told as the lover's submission or as payment of what he owed."},{"a":"ai:234","b":"k:1213","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1213's woman says she lives because she sees in dreams the man who gives no grace awake; in Ainkurunuru 234 the friend tells the lover that the girl sees his chest in dreams and not awake, and that is why she pales.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same split between dream and waking presence is the woman's own comfort in the Kural and the friend's evidence of harm in the Sangam poem, so the couplet's consolation is shown to be what an onlooker reads as wasting, and the dream becomes a lever for marriage rather than a way to live."},{"a":"ai:235","b":"ai:236","type":"develops","note":"Both close on எழுகமோ, shall we rise; 235 sets it against holding him and saying 'we will not part', 236 gives the destination, நும்மூர்ச் செல்கம், we will go to your town.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read alone, 235's 'shall we rise' could mean only leaving the tryst; the next poem fixes it as setting out for his town and names what forced the choice, the mother's knowing and the gossip, so the decade moves from weighing elopement to proposing it."},{"a":"ai:236","b":"a:107","type":"develops","note":"In both the friend tells the lover that the girl will go with him: in Akananuru 107 because she cannot bear his repeated departures, in Ainkurunuru 236 because the mother knows, gossip has spread and the house is desolate.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend's offer of elopement is made from two opposite pressures, the grief of his going in the Akananuru and exposure at home in the Ainkurunuru, so udanpokku appears in the anthologies as the answer both to separation and to discovery."},{"a":"ai:236","b":"a:35","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 236's friend says the north wind leaves the good house's tall mansion desolate (நன்மனை நெடுநகர் புலம்புகொள) as a reason to leave; Akananuru 35's mother says the girl left the high-walled good house desolate (நன்னகர் புலம்ப) by going.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same desolation of the great house is the cause of the girl's going in the friend's plea and its effect in the mother's lament, so the two speakers each blame the empty house on the other side and the elopement is seen whole only from both."},{"a":"ai:239","b":"a:78","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are a friend's reproach to the hill lord for his absences in courtship, with a tusker's embrace in the same words (இரும்பிணர் ... தழு): in Akananuru 78 he clasps his trembling pregnant mate in his rough trunk, in Ainkurunuru 239 he clasps a rough boulder, taking it for his mate.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The one image of a tusker's embrace serves the friend two ways, as the care the man owes the girl when the mate is there and as the blind longing of a beast whose mate is gone, so the reproach can shame the lover either by what the beast does for its mate or by what it suffers without her."},{"a":"ai:240","b":"ku:2","type":"develops","note":"In Kuruntokai 2 the man asks the pollen-seeking bee whether any flower is more fragrant than his woman's hair; in Ainkurunuru 240 the friend says bee swarms crowd his chest because it smells of the fragrant girl's hair.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The scent of her hair that the lover praises in private to the bee is the very thing that betrays the secret union to the friend, so the bees that witness his boast in one poem become the witnesses against him in the other.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:241","b":"nt:34","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 241 doubts that the velan can know the girl's bond with the hill man; Natrinai 34 tells Murugu himself that he came to the veri house knowing the sickness was not his seizure (நின் அணங்கு அன்மை அறிந்தும்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Ainkurunuru blames the rite's error on the diviner's ignorance, while Natrinai charges the god, who knows the truth, with folly for coming anyway, so the pair shows the friend's protest against veri aimed either at the man who performs it or at the deity who accepts it."},{"a":"ai:242","b":"a:98","type":"develops","note":"In both the mother acts out of not knowing (அறியாமையின் / அறிந்தனள் அல்லள்) and calls the rite; Akananuru 98 fears that if the rite fails the secret will become gossip, Ainkurunuru 242 says it is cruel to leave the mother in her grief.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 98 counts the cost of the rite to the girl, the secret turning into gossip, and Ainkurunuru 242 its cost to the mother, a grief spent on a wrong cause, so the case for disclosure in the veri situation rests on two separate harms."},{"a":"ai:243","b":"ku:263","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 263 protests to the girl that the mother's offerings to strange gods are no cure and brand her possessed; Ainkurunuru 243 says to the mother herself that she will believe the ignorant diviner.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same protest against the rite is spoken between the girls in Kuruntokai and turned on the mother in Ainkurunuru, so the pair shows the friend's objection to veri moving from private complaint to the first step of telling the household."},{"a":"ai:244","b":"a:98","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 98 the velan sings the god's great name (செல்வன் பெரும்பெயர் ஏத்தி) at the veri ground; in Ainkurunuru 244 the girl says the veri is useless unless the velan sings the hill of her man.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 98 shows what the diviner's song praises in the rite, the god's name, and Ainkurunuru 244 asks for the same song turned to the man's hill, so the girl imagines the only working cure as the rite itself with her lover in the god's place."},{"a":"ai:245","b":"ai:247","type":"develops","note":"Both turn on the same line, முருகென மொழியும் ஆயின்: 245 asks whether naming Murugu is a kindness to the girl's afflicter, 247 whether Murugu is the hill man's name.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The decade gives the one conditional two opposite ironies: in 245 the wrong name shields the man by hiding him, in 247 it names him by accident, so the misdiagnosis is shown as both the secret's cover and its leak."},{"a":"ai:246","b":"nt:322","type":"develops","note":"Both set the hill tiger with its mate beside the velan's rite: in Natrinai 322 the tiger lurks to ease its hungry female, in Ainkurunuru 246 it ends its distress by joining her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Holding the two together shows the kurinji tiger used as the counter-image to the veri rite: what relieves a creature of the hill, by hunting for its mate or by joining her, is the partner and never the offering."},{"a":"ai:248","b":"nt:282","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 282 says it would be fine indeed (நன்றுமன்) if the old diviner's word could calm the bond; Ainkurunuru 248 says fine indeed (நன்றால் அம்ம) will be her beauty if his beads find the cause.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same mock approval of the diviner is aimed in Natrinai at his cure and in Ainkurunuru at his diagnosis, so the friend's ironic நன்று is shown as a set turn of the veri situation that can be pointed at either half of the rite."},{"a":"ai:249","b":"ai:241","type":"develops","note":"241 asks whether the velan can know the hill man's bond; 249 blesses the velan because he does not know the hill man.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The decade's first poem raises the diviner's ignorance as a doubt and its ninth turns it into a blessing, so across the sequence the friend comes to see his ignorance as the secret's safety and not only as the rite's failure."},{"a":"ai:250","b":"a:388","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 388 would ask the velan whether his god goes hunting with blood-smeared arrows; Ainkurunuru 250 tells the beads that Vel, lord of the valli forest, is not the afflicter.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Akananuru separates god from lover by asking about the lover's hunt, Ainkurunuru by naming the god's own peacock and valli, so together they show the girls answering the rite by setting the two lords side by side in each other's emblems.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:256","b":"k:1081","type":"develops","note":"Both have the man, on seeing the girl, take her for an அணங்கு (Kural: அணங்குகொல்; Ainkurunuru: ஆர் அணங்கினளே).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Valluvar's man hesitates between goddess, peahen and woman, while the Sangam man has settled the question and pins it on her youth (இளையள் ஆயினும்), so the couplet's doubt is a refinement of an older certainty that a hill girl can be a striking power.","gr":3},{"a":"ai:255","b":"ai:256","type":"develops","note":"255 says the girl's grace resembles the mountain goddesses (வரையர மகளிர்ப் புரையும்); 256 says she herself is a great அணங்கு.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Two consecutive poems on the same opening line move from likeness to identity, so the decade itself shows a comparison with the hill goddesses hardening into the claim that the girl is one.","gr":3},{"a":"ai:257","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"In both the friend (on Parimelazhagar's reading of the Kural) speaks to the man who is about to leave, to hold him back.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet holds the man back with a threat that she will not live, while the Sangam friend holds him back with the girl's worth, a daughter begged from the god, so the pair shows two arguments for the same refusal: her death against her value."},{"a":"ai:259","b":"ai:257","type":"develops","note":"In 257 the father worshipped the god and begged for the daughter; in 259 the daughter herself worships the clan god of the hill.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together the two poems pass the rite from father to daughter: the god who gave her in answer to the father's begging now receives her own offering, made when she has a love of her own to pray for."},{"a":"ai:258","b":"a:178","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 178's friend rejoices that marriage is settled (வரைவு மலிந்து); Ainkurunuru 258's friend says the household has agreed to give her if he weds, yet her grief has not ceased.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Against the friend's joy in Akananuru 178, Ainkurunuru 258 shows that the household's consent alone ends nothing for the girl, so the akam sequence separates the family's yes from the man's coming as two steps.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:260","b":"a:28","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 28 warns that if the girl does not keep scaring parrots from the millet, mother will set others in her place and his chest will be hard to reach (உறற்கு அரிது); Ainkurunuru 260 says the millet has ripened and the girl is hard to win (பெறற்கரிது).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 28 spells out the custom that the Ainkurunuru poem only implies: the girl's freedom lasts exactly as long as the field needs guarding, so 'the millet has ripened' in four lines carries the whole threat of her being kept at home."},{"a":"ai:254","b":"ai:258","type":"develops","note":"258 says the household will give the girl if the lord of the hill weds her; 254 says the wealthy ones have taken her to their hill country.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read against 258's conditional promise, 254 is its fulfilment, so within one decade the hill man's daughter passes from being offered to being carried off to the man's country."},{"a":"ai:261","b":"nt:386","type":"qualifies","note":"In Natrinai 386 the great-tempered boar that gorges on the hunters' millet and sleeps unafraid of the tiger frames a man who offers the dread oath and comes to wed; in Ainkurunuru 261 the boar that has grazed the millet and sleeps on the rock slope frames a man the heroine suspects of fearing her father.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The fearless sleeping boar was a stock frame whose point turns on the man set beside it, matching the bold suitor in Natrinai and shaming the timid one in Ainkurunuru, so the same image can carry praise or an unspoken reproach.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:263","b":"k:1265","type":"develops","note":"Both are spoken by the woman about the pallor that the man's presence will remove: Kural 1265 wishes to see him and says the pallor will then leave; Ainkurunuru 263 says he has come and her beauty has come back.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam poem gives as an accomplished fact, in two verbs set side by side, what the couplet can only promise, showing the restored beauty as the very event of his arrival rather than its later result."},{"a":"ai:264","b":"ai:266","type":"develops","note":"The two poems close on nearly the same words to the hill lord about the eyes of the woman he loves: in 264 they have yielded pallor, in 266 tears (பனி), after the friend owns their great modesty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read in order, the decade escalates the friend's case for marriage from pallor to weeping by changing one word, while the boar beside it changes from mating with its sow to fighting a tiger."},{"a":"ai:265","b":"a:248","type":"develops","note":"Both are Kapilar's: in Akananuru 248 a boar holding a narrow pass to guard its young is spared by a hunter who sees in it his own chief holding the path; in Ainkurunuru 265 a boar rears the young of a sow the tiger killed, beside a man who left his wife and son.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Kapilar's boar guarding its young is a model of protection that even the hunter honours as heroic, which makes the husband of Ainkurunuru 265 fail a standard that both the beast and the hunter keep."},{"a":"ai:265","b":"ai:268","type":"same_image","note":"Both set a boar with a striped young that has lost its mother beside a man who has left: in 265 the boar rears it after a tiger killed the sow, in 268 it sleeps with it on the peak.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Taken together the pair fixes the reading of the motherless young in 268 by the tiger-killed sow named in 265, and shows the decade using one creature's care twice, once against a husband and once against a lover."},{"a":"ai:267","b":"nt:386","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 267 opens with the same boar formula as Natrinai 386 (சிறுகண் பன்றிப் பெருஞ்சின ஒருத்தல்); Natrinai's boar sleeps unafraid after raiding the millet, Ainkurunuru's drives off the bowmen to seize the hill rice, beside a man whose words are not to be trusted.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"One formula line for the raiding boar served poets for opposite men, the suitor who swears and weds and the one who flatters without pledge, so the boar's daring is admired or suspected according to what the man goes on to do."},{"a":"ai:269","b":"ai:270","type":"develops","note":"Both open on the earth a boar has ploughed: in 269 a wild reed rises there like a crop and the friend fears the lord will stay away; in 270 the hunters reap the first crop from it and the lord comes of himself.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The decade ends by answering the friend's fear on the same boar-tilled ground, where the field that bore only a wild reed now yields a harvest and his return, so the image tracks whether the love will bear fruit."},{"a":"ai:270","b":"pn:168","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 168 praises Kotran's hill, where hill people sow millet unploughed in the earth a boar has turned and feast on the first new grain; in Ainkurunuru 270 the hunters reap the first crop from the boar-ploughed slope and leave the hill bare.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same unploughed tillage and first harvest is plenty and hospitality in the praise of a patron but, for lovers who met at the millet watch, the end of their season and a bare hill, so akam reads the hill's farming calendar as a calendar of meeting."},{"a":"ai:273","b":"nt:168","type":"qualifies","note":"Natrinai 168 and Ainkurunuru 273 share a formula: the young of the dull-headed female monkey feeding on the good hill and the girl called நின் நயந்து உறைவி; in Natrinai 168 the friend uses it to forbid his coming at midnight, in Ainkurunuru 273 to forbid his going away.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same formula serves to stop the lover coming and to stop him leaving, which shows the double bind of secret love: the girl's life is staked both on the danger of his night visits and on his absence, and only marriage ends both."},{"a":"ai:274","b":"ku:69","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kuruntokai 69 a widowed female monkey leaps from the ridge to her death and the friend begs the lover not to come at midnight; in Ainkurunuru 274 the male monkey carries his mate up the slope from the roaring tiger while the girl's lover has gone away.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the monkey couple names both fears of secret love: in Kuruntokai the girl would not survive losing him to the night path, in Ainkurunuru she is left by a man less faithful than the beast that shields its mate, so the danger lies as much in his leaving as in his coming."},{"a":"ai:278","b":"k:1183","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1183 has the woman say he took her grace and modesty and gave her sickness and pallor in exchange; Ainkurunuru 278 says the lord gave a sickness never forgotten and took the beauty onlookers never tired of.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Valluvar keeps only the exchange as the woman's own complaint, while the Sangam poem sets it in an image of a bamboo springing back like a rod that has landed a fish, which shows the taking and the man's unharmed recoil that the couplet's bare bargain leaves out."},{"a":"ai:279","b":"nt:334","type":"qualifies","note":"In Natrinai 334 the monkeys frolic with their male in the falls and the friend fears for the girl's life if the lover comes through the stormy night; in Ainkurunuru 279 the female monkey frolics with her mate and the friend warns that his coming will set the village gossiping.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Under the same image of monkeys at play with their mates, the friend has two separate reasons to keep the lover away, the night path's danger in Natrinai and the village tongue in Ainkurunuru, so the pair shows that the creatures' open play stands for exactly the freedom secret love lacks on both counts."},{"a":"ai:280","b":"ai:103","type":"develops","note":"In Ainkurunuru 103 the friend tells the mother that the shore lord has become fit for the girl and she for him; in Ainkurunuru 280 the friend reminds the hill lord that on hearing he would marry she told her mother.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The neythal decade stages the friend's disclosure to the mother, and Kapilar's poem shows its price: she spoke only on the strength of the man's word, so her standing at home now hangs on his keeping it.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:281","b":"nt:134","type":"develops","note":"Both count the girl's posting to the millet field as luck for the secret love: in Natrinai 134 the friend tells the girl that the mother herself sent her with the clapper to the field near the meeting place; in Ainkurunuru 281 the man blesses the parrots whose raids set her to guard.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two poems show that the field watch which makes the day trysts possible is set up by agents who do not know what they serve, the mother by her order and the parrots by their raids, so the kalavu depends on the household's own ignorance."},{"a":"ai:282","b":"ku:141","type":"develops","note":"Both refuse the night visit on the hill path: Kuruntokai 141's girl would tell him she has been sent to drive parrots from the millet and that he should not come in the deep midnight when the tiger waits; Ainkurunuru 282's friend tells him not to come in the dark where tuskers roam.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Ainkurunuru 282 gives only the refusal, while Kuruntokai 141 adds the alternative the refusal implies, the millet field by day, so the pair shows that the night ban in kurinji is a redirection of the tryst to the guarded field, not a denial of it."},{"a":"ai:283","b":"ku:25","type":"qualifies","note":"Both turn on the man's possible lie: in Kuruntokai 25 the girl says that if he denies his word she can do nothing, since only a heron watched; in Ainkurunuru 283 the friend says that even if he makes great promises and leaves, many women are caught in his net of lies.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The girl of Kuruntokai 25 treats his lie as her private helplessness with no witness, while the friend of Ainkurunuru 283 recasts it as his habit, a net that has caught many, so the friend's voice turns the heroine's fear into a public charge that can be used to press him."},{"a":"ai:284","b":"nt:306","type":"qualifies","note":"Both look at the reaped millet field: in Natrinai 306 the friend, in the man's hearing, says the girl has been sent home and asks how he will come day after day to the bare stubble; in Ainkurunuru 284 the parrots keep coming to the stubble, unable to part from it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Natrinai 306 assumes that the harvest ends the man's reason to come, but Ainkurunuru 284 shows creatures whose love keeps them at the empty field anyway, so the harvest cannot end such a love; it only leaves marriage as the way it can go on."},{"a":"ai:285","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Both have the friend (for Parimelazhagar in the Kural) speak to the man about to part: Kural 1151 refuses his promise of a quick return, saying tell it to those who will be alive; Ainkurunuru 285 asks how he can part and leave her here with her bangles slipping.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the friend's refusal as a bare threat to the girl's life, while the Sangam poem sets the same refusal in the girl's daily field life and names the visible sign, the slipping bangle, so the Kural's abstract 'those who will be alive' is shown as wasting already begun."},{"a":"ai:286","b":"k:1184","type":"agrees","note":"Both have the girl hold to the man's goodness while her pallor spreads: in Kural 1184 she thinks of him and speaks only of his merits yet pallor comes; in Ainkurunuru 286 she says he seems to have loved her without blemish yet takes her beauty.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"In both the girl refuses to blame the man and lays the loss elsewhere, on the pallor as a cheat in the Kural and on his love itself in the Sangam poem, so the pair shows a fixed rule of her speech: the waste of her beauty is never his fault in her own mouth.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:286","b":"ku:133","type":"qualifies","note":"Both use the reaped millet stubble of the hill farmer: in Kuruntokai 133 the stubble cropped by parrots puts out leaves again in the rain, as the girl lives on after his first taking of her beauty; in Ainkurunuru 286 the stubble bears pods for his ever-yielding land while her own glow is taken.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same stubble is a figure of the girl's own survival in Kuruntokai 133 but of the man's endless plenty in Ainkurunuru 286, set against her fading, so the image of renewal cuts both ways: what revives on his hills need not revive in her.","gr":4},{"a":"ai:288","b":"nt:194","type":"qualifies","note":"Both ask what return can be made to the small parrot of the millet field: in Natrinai 194 the friend asks it of the parrot that did not take the millet ears while they played with the hill lord; in Ainkurunuru 288 the man asks it of the parrots that eat the millet while the girl guards it.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The parrot is thanked in one poem for eating the crop and in the other for sparing it, opposite acts earning the same gratitude, which shows that what the lovers prize is not the bird's deed but whatever keeps the field watch going and hides what they do there."},{"a":"ai:288","b":"k:101","type":"develops","note":"Kural 101 says that help given unasked cannot be repaid by earth and heaven together; in Ainkurunuru 288 the man, knowing the good of the parrots' help, asks his heart what he can do in return.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's unrepayable help is a deliberate kindness, while the parrots helped without knowing or meaning it, so the lover's echo of the gratitude maxim stretches it to an unintended benefit and makes the debt even less payable, since the helpers cannot know they are owed."},{"a":"ai:289","b":"ku:291","type":"develops","note":"Both have the parrots take the girl's sound for their own call: in Kuruntokai 291 the man says the parrots take her clapper's sweet call for one of their own and will not rise, so she weeps; in Ainkurunuru 289 the friend says the parrots, taking her voice for a parrot's, come to the millet, so the elders may stop her guarding.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kuruntokai 291 makes the image a lover's praise and a small vexation, while Ainkurunuru 289 follows it to its consequence in the household, the loss of the guard duty and so of the trysts, which turns the man's compliment into the friend's argument for marriage."},{"a":"ai:289","b":"a:28","type":"qualifies","note":"Both warn that the girl may lose the guard of the millet: in Akananuru 28 the friend, with the man nearby, warns that if she does not rise and scare the parrots her mother will send others; in Ainkurunuru 289 the friend says the elders may stop her guarding because her own voice draws the parrots.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The threat to the field meetings comes in Akananuru 28 from the girl's neglect, which she could mend, and in Ainkurunuru 289 from her very voice, which she cannot, so the second friend can leave the man only one remedy, to marry her."},{"a":"ai:290","b":"k:1103","type":"develops","note":"Both have the man in love rank a small favour of the beloved above the highest good: Kural 1103 asks whether the world of the lotus-eyed god is sweeter than sleep in her soft arms; Ainkurunuru 290 says parrots merely looked at and shooed by the hill girl are more fortunate than a just king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural sets union against heaven, while the Sangam poem sets far less, a glance and a shooing given to birds, against a righteous kingdom, so the pair shows the measure working at both ends: even the least share of her notice outweighs the highest worldly good."},{"a":"ai:298","b":"k:1125","type":"qualifies","note":"Both give the man the same words for constancy: Kural 1125 உள்ளுவன் ... மறப்பறியேன், answering the friend who asks whether he remembers; Ainkurunuru 298 உள்ளுபு மறந்தறியேமே, said of a girl who shows him no grace.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural's joke that he cannot remember what he never forgets answers a doubt about absence, while the Ainkurunuru sets the same claim against her coldness, so together they show the man's never-forgetting was the stock answer to both doubts the friend tests him with: will he stay away, and will he give up if she is unkind."},{"a":"ai:299","b":"k:1114","type":"develops","note":"Both have the man say the blue water-lily (குவளை) cannot match the girl's eyes: Ainkurunuru 299 says the lily of her own hill spring can hardly open as her eyes do, and Kural 1114 that the lily, if it could see, would bow its head ashamed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Ainkurunuru states the defeat of the lily as a plain fact of the girl's own hill, and the Kural gives the lily sight and a modest bowed head, so the pair shows the Kural's conceit growing out of the akam habit of measuring the hill girl against her land's flowers, with the lily now made to act out her own modesty."},{"a":"ai:295","b":"k:1248","type":"qualifies","note":"Both have the woman speak of her heart that has gone after the man: in Ainkurunuru 295 she wonders whether the heart that went with the hill man will come back or settle there; in Kural 1248 she calls her heart foolish for following the one who left, pining that he will not show grace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural judges the following heart a fool because the man has gone and withholds his grace, while the Ainkurunuru girl, with no doubt of him, only wonders whether her heart will return, so the pair shows that the same act of the heart going after the man is not folly in itself and is judged by how the man answers it."},{"a":"ai:296","b":"a:92","type":"develops","note":"Both have the friend bid the hill lord not come at midnight for fear of beasts: Ainkurunuru 296 in four lines, fearing a fierce beast; Akananuru 92 with a tiger striking an elephant, and then the offer that tomorrow she and the girl will be guarding the millet, where he may meet her by day (பகற்குறி, the colophon says, to press marriage).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 92 spells out the day tryst at the millet field, which shows that the opening of Ainkurunuru 296, the millet the hill girl guards, is not idle scenery but the same unspoken offer, so the short poem's refusal of the night already points to where and when he may come."},{"a":"ai:293","b":"nt:28","type":"qualifies","note":"Both turn on the girl's hand laid on the man's eyes: in Natrinai 28 the girl recalls how in the secret love he took her hand and pressed it to his eyes, then left her like a thief; in Ainkurunuru 293 the wife covers his eyes from behind in play and he says no one else lives in his heart.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"In Natrinai 28 the hand on his eyes was his gesture of a promise that his leaving made false, while in Ainkurunuru 293 it is her game in the married bed that draws from him the vow of no other, so the pair shows the same touch losing its doubt once the promise of secret love has been kept in marriage."},{"a":"ai:302","b":"k:1151","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1151 refuses any promise of quick return and asks only whether he is leaving; Ainkurunuru 302 grants that his work for wealth has its own right and argues only that the woman has an equal one.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the friend's dissuasion in two registers, the couplet's flat ultimatum of death and the poem's balanced concession of rights, so the Kural's line is an extreme the Sangam friend reaches only after first admitting the claim of wealth."},{"a":"ai:305","b":"a:91","type":"qualifies","note":"Both use the elephant pair shrinking together in hunger on the scorched hill; in Akananuru 91 the friend uses it after he has gone to comfort the woman, in Ainkurunuru 305 before he goes to shame the man out of going.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same image of the starving pair that will not part is turned on the man as reproach before the journey and on the woman as reassurance after it, so in the convention one karu image carries opposite arguments depending on whether the departure is still preventable."},{"a":"ai:309","b":"k:64","type":"develops","note":"Kural 64 makes the gruel stirred by children's small hands sweeter than amrita; Ainkurunuru 309 asks whether wealth gained across the waste is sweeter than an unborn son's smile.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's household ranking of children above every delicacy becomes, in the friend's mouth, a direct argument against the wealth journey, showing that the Kural's measure of sweetness was already a tool for weighing a husband's absence against his family."},{"a":"ai:310","b":"a:271","type":"develops","note":"In both the friend tells the man who has announced his journey that the woman's bangles will slip; Akananuru 271 wishes his work be capable (வல்வதாக) and asks if there is medicine for her, Ainkurunuru 310 asks whether he is capable (வல்லுவை) of parting.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both friends turn வல், the capacity a man needs for his work, into the hardness that parting demands, so the pair shows the dissuasion convention attacking the hero's prized strength itself rather than his reasons for going."},{"a":"ai:311","b":"a:69","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 311 has the woman ask whether the man who crossed the forest will stay long (நீடுவர் கொல்); in Akananuru 69 the friend, by its colophon, comforts the woman who fears his delay with the answer that he will not stay long (நீடலர்) though he crossed the Mauryan cut.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The four-line poem gives only the woman's question and the long poem only the friend's answer, so together they show that the paalai exchange turned on a single verb of delay, asked in one mouth as a fear and returned in the other as a promise."},{"a":"ai:312","b":"k:1210","type":"qualifies","note":"Both blessings are said by the woman to a thing of nature that governs her sight of the man who has left: the Kural blesses the moon on condition that it not set (படாஅதி வாழி மதி); Ainkurunuru 312 blesses the hill that has already hidden him (மறைத்த குன்றே).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's blessing is a bargain for sight, while the Sangam woman blesses the hill after it has hidden him, so her wish can only be for what the hill will do to him on the way, which shows the blessing turning from her own need to his safety."},{"a":"ai:317","b":"k:1248","type":"qualifies","note":"In both the woman's heart has followed the man who left (பிரிந்தவர் பின்செல்வாய்; இறந்தோர் தேஎத்துச் சென்ற நெஞ்சம்); the Kural calls it a fool, Ainkurunuru 317 asks the friend why it is so slow to come back.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Kural judges the heart's going as folly, but the Ainkurunuru woman takes the going for granted and worries about the heart's delay, so the same flight of the heart becomes, in the older poem, a second absence that mirrors the man's.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:318","b":"k:1234","type":"develops","note":"Both state the same sign of separation on the same body part: the broad shoulders (பணைத்தோள்; பணைநீங்கி) waste and the bangles slip once the partner is gone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the slipping bangle as a plain effect of the partner's absence, while the Ainkurunuru woman names the departed men as those who killed her longing, so the poem shows the bodily sign being used as an accusation, which the couplet's general statement leaves out."},{"a":"ai:319","b":"k:1207","type":"develops","note":"Both turn on the woman's own inability to forget (மறப்பறியேன்; மறவா நம்மே); the Kural asks what would become of her if she forgot, Ainkurunuru 319 asks whether he has forgotten her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural keeps the question inside her (remembering burns, forgetting would kill), while the Sangam poem turns the same constancy outward into a charge against him, so read together they show the two directions the claim 'I do not forget' could be pointed."},{"a":"ai:320","b":"a:185","type":"same_image","note":"In both a woman left behind speaks of a man who crossed the hills where the silk-cotton blooms: Akananuru 185 compares the leafless ilavam in flower to the lamps of a great festival (பெருவிழா விளக்கம் போல), Ainkurunuru 320 compares its falling flowers to embers struck by thunder (உருமுப்படு கனலின்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same flowering tree on the paalai road is a festival of lamps in one woman's mouth and a thunderbolt's embers in the other's, so the silk-cotton was not a fixed sign of danger but a vehicle that swung with the charge each woman laid against her man, his iron will in one and the endless sickness he gave in the other."},{"a":"ai:318","b":"a:185","type":"develops","note":"Both are the woman's complaint that ends on almost the same closing line (கோடுயர் பிறங்கல் மலை இறந் தோரே; நிலையுயர் பிறங்கல் மலைஇறந் தோரே) after naming her wasted broad shoulders (பணைத்தோள்) and slipping bangles.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The five-line decade poem is the bare frame of the thirteen-line Akananuru poem, the body's signs followed by the closing formula of the men who crossed the high hills, so the pair shows how a long paalai poem was built by filling the road between those two fixed ends with a scene."},{"a":"ai:322","b":"k:1104","type":"tension","note":"The Kural's man says the fire she gives burns when he moves away from her (நீங்கின் தெறூஉம்); Ainkurunuru 322's man, far from her on the summer road, says the thought of her makes the burning path cool (தண்ணிய).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together they give opposite laws for distance: in the couplet absence from her is the source of heat, while in the desert poem absence is already given and her remembered presence is the only coolness, so whether she burns or cools depends on whether the man is measuring his desire or the land."},{"a":"ai:328","b":"ai:322","type":"qualifies","note":"In 322 thinking of her turns a hot road cool; in 328 a road cooled by rain and flowers is hot because he has left her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two poems of one decade run the same switch in both directions, so the poet shows that paalai heat is set by her presence or absence in the man's mind and can override real rain as easily as real sun."},{"a":"ai:330","b":"a:5","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 5's colophon has the man give up the journey (செலவழுங்கியது) at the sight of her tearful face before he sets out; in Ainkurunuru 330 he has already crossed the scorching forest (வந்தனம் ஆயினும்) when he calls off the journey (ஒழிகஇனிச் செலவே) thinking of her weeping eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that giving up the journey is not tied to a moment: the tears seen at the doorstep in Akananuru 5 and the tears only imagined halfway in Ainkurunuru 330 do the same work, so in this situation her grief, present or pictured, is what overrules the errand, not the danger of the road."},{"a":"ai:329","b":"a:19","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 19 the man reproaches a heart that neither stayed with her nor crosses the hills with him; in Ainkurunuru 329 he says the heart was taken by her and wonders whether it will come back with him.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Read together they show two accounts of the traveller's divided heart: the Akananuru man blames it for wavering between her and the road, while the Ainkurunuru man concedes it never left her, so the reproach of Akananuru 19 becomes in the short poem a plain admission that he travels heartless.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:324","b":"a:39","type":"qualifies","note":"Both men, on the burning road, see the girl in sleep; Ainkurunuru 324 says he will surely see her (காண்குவென் மன்ற), while Akananuru 39 calls the dream false (வாய்அல் கனவின்) because it left his hand empty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The short poem gives only the traveller's confidence in the dream, and the long one tells how it ends on waking, so together they show the road-dream as both his comfort and his torment, and explain why the returned man of Akananuru 39 can answer her charge of forgetting with it.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:323","b":"a:197","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 197 the friend comforts the woman with the image of a tusker embracing his pregnant mate on the man's road; in Ainkurunuru 323 the man himself crosses a road where a red dog hunts for his craving pregnant mate.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend's reassurance rests on an assumption that the man will see such creatures and remember, and Ainkurunuru 323 shows it from his side: he does see the male caring for its mate and finds the girl's nature has come with him, so the comfort offered at home is confirmed by the road."},{"a":"ai:321","b":"ku:285","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kuruntokai 285 the waiting woman asks where he can be, who crossed sky-high hills where a lone kite sits on the nyemai top; in Ainkurunuru 321 the man on such hills hears the hen kite call from the omai and says her nature never leaves him.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same bare tree and kite are imagined by the woman and actually met by the man, and the pair answers her question from the other end of the road: where she fears he has forgotten her, the same scene brings her back to him."},{"a":"ai:325","b":"k:1201","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's man says love is sweeter than toddy because merely thinking of it brings unfailing joy; in Ainkurunuru 325 the man says the scorching waste does not harm him because he has her nature as companion.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet states that remembered love never fails to give joy, and the Ainkurunuru puts that claim to a hard test, the peopleless summer waste, where the memory works as a travelling companion (துணை), so the maxim gains a situation in which its truth is a matter of survival.","gr":3},{"a":"ai:334","b":"k:1248","type":"qualifies","note":"Both have the woman's foolish heart following the man who has left (Ainkurunuru: பேதை நெஞ்சம் பின்செல; Kural: பின்செல்வாய் பேதைஎன் நெஞ்சு).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"With the same words the couplet blames her own heart for going after an unkind man, while the Sangam wife lets the heart go and blames the man as harder than stone, so the following heart was an image that could lay the fault on either lover."},{"a":"ai:337","b":"a:51","type":"tension","note":"Akananuru 51's man tells his heart that wealth gained by parting is not worth leaving his wife's embrace; Ainkurunuru 337's wife says that for her man wealth is sweeter than the embrace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two poems weigh the same two goods, wealth and the embrace, and reach opposite verdicts according to who speaks: the man at home can still choose the embrace, while the wife whose man has gone can only infer that he chose wealth, so the akam tradition hands each verdict to the one who lives with it."},{"a":"ai:336","b":"a:1","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 1's woman asks whether he has forgotten his word that they would never part (பிரியலம் என்ற சொல்) now that he has gone for gold; Ainkurunuru 336 says he embraced her as if he would never part (பிரியலர் போலப் புணர்ந்தோர்) and went for wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 1 rests the betrayal on a spoken vow, while the Ainkurunuru wife rests it on the embrace alone, so together they show that in paalai the embrace itself was read as a promise that the pursuit of wealth broke, with or without words."},{"a":"ai:339","b":"k:1222","type":"develops","note":"In both the wife, parted from her man, speaks of the evening: the Kural asks the evening whether its partner is as hard as hers; Ainkurunuru 339 asks whether evening exists in the land he went to.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives evening a hard partner of its own, and the Sangam wife supposes his land has no evening at all, so the pair shows the same assumption from two sides: evening is the hour that should force lovers together, and only an absent or hard-hearted partner escapes it."},{"a":"ai:332","b":"ai:333","type":"develops","note":"In 332 the beasts fail to tell him that parting is cruel; in 333 the birds fail to tell him that they themselves live paired.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read in order, the decade moves from the argument the beasts should have made by precept (parting is cruel) to the one the birds should have made by their own example (we live with our mates), so the wife tries both ways of restraining a man and puts each in a creature's mouth.","gr":3},{"a":"ai:341","b":"ai:350","type":"develops","note":"Every poem of the decade says the time has come (தான் வந்தன்றே); 350 says it is the time of which he assured them with sweet words (தேம்படு கிளவி ... தெளிக்கும் பொழுதே).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read from 350 back to 341, the unnamed 'it' of the refrain turns out to be his promised date, so each spring sign in the decade is a clause of his oath being checked off, not a description of the season."},{"a":"ai:341","b":"a:355","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 355 says spring has come of itself (செவ்விவேனில் தானே வந்தன்று) to a woman whose man is about to leave; the Ainkurunuru refrain says the same with தான் வந்தன்றே after he has gone.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same formula serves first as the woman's argument against going, the season lovers spend in cool groves, and then as the proof that he went anyway, so 'it came of itself' carries the reproach that the season keeps its time unasked while the man does not."},{"a":"ai:345","b":"a:355","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 355 says in spring lovers stay in the cool grove of dropping pollen and forget their rich homes (தண்பொழில் அல்கிக் காதலர் செழுமனை மறக்கும்); Ainkurunuru 345 says our fragrant cool grove (நம் மணங்கமழ் தண்பொழில்) is in bloom.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 355 supplies the custom that the four-line poem leaves out: the flowering grove is where lovers spend spring together, so 'our grove blooms' is an empty place set for two, not a pleasant view."},{"a":"ai:346","b":"nt:224","type":"qualifies","note":"In Natrinai 224 the red-eyed dark koel (செங் கண் இருங் குயில்) calls as if saying 'you who are joined, stay joined' before the man leaves; in Ainkurunuru 346 the same koel (செங்கண இருங்குயில்) cries after he has gone and not returned.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"One bird in one spring is a plea against parting while the man is still at home and an announcement of his failure once he is away, so the koel's call has no fixed sense of its own and takes its meaning from the stage of the parting.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:350","b":"nt:224","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 224 sets 'sweet spring has come' (இன்ப வேனிலும் வந்தன்று) beside 'those who assured us we will not part' (பிரியலம் என்று தெளித்தோர்); Ainkurunuru 350 ends with the time of which he assured them (தெளிக்கும் பொழுதே).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Natrinai uses தெளித்தல் for the man's sworn assurance in the very poem where spring arrives, which supports reading Ainkurunuru 350's தெளிக்கும் as his promise, and shows that spring's coming and the lover's oath were a fixed pair in paalai poetry."},{"a":"ai:350","b":"a:317","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 317 the man's quoted promise names the spring signs, the koel in the mango and the kongu and kuravam dropping flowers, and he arrives on time; in Ainkurunuru 350 the time of his assurance comes and he does not.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 317 shows that the catalogue of spring flowers and birds was itself the wording of the man's promise, so the Ainkurunuru decade's list of the same signs is the promise read back to an absent man, and 317's ending is the one the decade is denied."},{"a":"ai:342","b":"kn:4","type":"qualifies","note":"Kar Narpathu 4's friend calls the season of humming bees and a forest in bloom the medicine for the heroine's pallor (பசலை மருந்து); in Ainkurunuru 342 the bees revel on the flowering nunavam and he has not come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend can call the flowering season a cure only because it guarantees his return; Ainkurunuru 342 shows the same flowering turning into the sickness once the season arrives without him, so the convention's comfort is only as good as the man's timekeeping.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:350","b":"k:1269","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1269 says one day is like seven for those who pine with their eyes on the day of return (வருநாள்வைத்து ஏங்குபவர்க்கு); Ainkurunuru 350 is spoken on that day, the time he named, and he has not come.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet measures the waiting before the fixed day as the long part, while the poem shows the fixed day arriving empty, so the Kural's pain has a limit that the Ainkurunuru woman has already passed, when waiting no longer has a date to count toward."},{"a":"ai:355","b":"a:337","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 355: he turned back from the wealth journey without blessing it; Akananuru 337: the heart once said 'let wealth stay there' (அவணதாகப் பொருள்) and came back alone, and now presses for a second journey.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 337 supplies the sequel the Ainkurunuru decade leaves out: the return for love that 355 presents as final is, a season later, reopened by the same heart, so the turning back is one move in a recurring quarrel between wealth and the wife.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:357","b":"a:97","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 97 the kuravam blooms (குரவுமலர்ந்து) in a spring when the man has not come and the woman cannot stop her tears; in Ainkurunuru 357 the kuravam's flowering is what makes the man give up his journey.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two poems show the spring flowers acting on both lovers at once: the same blossoming that tortures the waiting woman in Akananuru 97 is, in 357, what turns the man back, so the season's sign has power over him and fails only when he ignores it."},{"a":"ai:354","b":"nt:43","type":"qualifies","note":"In Natrinai 43 a starving red dog kills a weary wild cow on the road the man is about to take; in Ainkurunuru 354 the red dog, with its mate, lets the doe with her fawn go on the road he has taken home.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same predator of the waste kills on the outbound road and holds back on the homeward one, so the paalai poets set the beast's act to the direction of the journey: cruelty when the man leaves, restraint when he comes back to his wife."},{"a":"ai:352","b":"a:53","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the formula விழுத்தொடை மறவர் வில் இட ... எழுத்துடை நடுகல்; in Akananuru 53 it is the stone on the outbound road the woman says he chose over her, in Ainkurunuru 352 a simile for an elephant on the road he did not call hard on his way back.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The one formula of the lettered hero-stone serves opposite arguments: in Akananuru 53 the woman reads the deadly road as proof he loves wealth more than her, while 352 makes the same road proof of love because he crossed it home without counting its danger."},{"a":"ai:358","b":"k:1170","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1170 the woman says her eyes would not swim in a flood of tears if they could go to him as her heart does; in Ainkurunuru 358 the eyes, which have become a flood however often they are wiped, will not let him stay away long.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the flood of tears a sign of the eyes' helplessness, bound to the house while the heart travels, whereas the Sangam poem turns that same flood into a power that pulls him back, so the tears are complaint in one and pledge of return in the other."},{"a":"ai:360","b":"a:9","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 360: the long burnt road was easy because he drove his chariot thinking of her; Akananuru 9: the returning man's heart outran even his sleepless chariot and has already reached her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together the two returns show desire working on the journey in two stages: in 360 it shrinks the road the chariot must cover, and in Akananuru 9 it leaves the chariot behind altogether, so the heart arrives before the man does."},{"a":"ai:361","b":"a:99","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 99 the man leading the eloped girl through the waste likens the opening kongu buds to women's jewelled breasts and points to paathiri petals on the path; in Ainkurunuru 361 the girl wears a paathiri garland by the summer stream and her breasts and shoulders strike him harder than her eyes.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 99 makes the waste's flowers stand for her body to coax her onward, while 361 has her wearing those same flowers and her body outdoing them, so the eloping man's praise moves from comparing the land to her to letting her eclipse it."},{"a":"ai:361","b":"k:1085","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1085 finds death, the eye and the doe all in the young woman's look; Ainkurunuru 361 grants the eyes are fierce, then ranks her breasts fiercer and her shoulders fiercer still.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's chapter on the first sight puts all the deadliness in the look, and the Ainkurunuru poet answers that the eyes are only the first and mildest weapon, which shows the old akam poets could treat the same stricken lover as half joking where the couplet keeps him grave."},{"a":"ai:362","b":"nt:144","type":"qualifies","note":"In Natrinai 144 the girl, with the man overhearing, says her heart frets now that she knows he comes alone by night over the forked paths (கவலை) where the tigress prowls; in Ainkurunuru 362 the same fear of the forked night path (கவலை) and its elephant is put to the man's face.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The overheard complaint of Natrinai 144 and the open question of 362 make the same plea, stop coming by night, in two registers, and holding them together shows that Ainkurunuru's direct-address decade turns the indirect device of the overhearer into plain speech without changing the situation."},{"a":"ai:363","b":"k:1081","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1081: on first sight the man cannot tell whether she is a goddess (அணங்கு), a peahen or a woman, and his heart reels; Ainkurunuru 363: the man tells the hunters' sister that what she calls freckles (சுணங்கு) his stricken heart calls a goddess (அணங்கு).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet leaves the goddess as one of three guesses about the whole woman, 363 pins the striking power on one small mark she herself thinks a blemish, so the pair shows the anangu is not in her but in the gap between how she sees herself and how he is struck."},{"a":"ai:366","b":"a:48","type":"develops","note":"Both open with the friend's formula to the mother, அன்னாய் வாழி வேண்டு அன்னை, answering her question why the girl has grown pale (பசந்தனள்); in Akananuru 48 the friend tells the whole story of the hunter who came asking after a tiger, in 366 she answers only that the kongu has flowered.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The shared formula shows these are two answers to one stock question: Akananuru 48 discloses the love by a full story and leaves the wise to infer the rest, while 366 compresses the same disclosure into one seasonal sign, so the reader learns how far the friend's hint could be shortened and still be understood."},{"a":"ai:366","b":"a:317","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 317 the man had promised to come back in the spring when the kongu and kuravam flower and the koel calls, and his arrival ends a pallor the mother had noticed; in Ainkurunuru 366 the friend tells the mother the girl's pallor is because the kongu has flowered.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Read through 317, the friend's excuse in 366 is no excuse at all: the kongu's flowering is the season a man promises to return by, so pointing the mother to the tree points her to an absent lover, and the pair shows a seasonal sign doing the work of a confession."},{"a":"ai:367","b":"a:48","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 48 the friend tells the foster mother of a youth with a flower garland and a chariot who stopped by the vengai slope and gazed at the girl; in Ainkurunuru 367 the friend tells the mother of the man who came near with his chariot wearing kongu and vengai flowers, and says the girl's life is joined to his name.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same disclosure scene, chariot, flowered youth and vengai included, ends in Akananuru 48 with an inference left to the mother and in 367 with a flat verdict that the girl is already bound to him, which shows the friend's revelation could be pitched as a hint or as a fait accompli."},{"a":"ai:368","b":"k:1151","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1151 the friend tells the departing man to promise a quick return only to those who will still be alive; in Ainkurunuru 368 the friend invites him to stay and enjoy the cool summer with them if the girl is not to be ruined.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both hold the girl's life against the journey, but the couplet does it by refusing his comfort and 368 by tempting him with the season's pleasure, so together they show the two strategies of the friend who would stop a departure, the bitter and the sweet, around the same threat."},{"a":"ai:369","b":"k:1146","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1146 the woman says she saw him on one day only and the gossip has spread like the snake seizing the moon; in Ainkurunuru 369 the friend tells the man that his tryst of yesterday (நெருநல்) is already louder in the town's mouth than the seasonal koel.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both measure a single meeting against a public noise, the eclipse in the couplet and the koel's cry in 369, and holding them together shows the alar topic's fixed shape: the smaller and more recent the secret, the larger the natural event the gossip is said to outdo.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:371","b":"ku:378","type":"develops","note":"Both wish the wasteland the girl has crossed with her lover cool with rain or cloud, in the same frame (...ஆகுக ... போகிய சுரனே); Kuruntokai's speaker is the foster mother, Ainkurunuru's the mother.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kuruntokai's wish asks only shade, sand and cloud for a frightened road, while Ainkurunuru adds that the girl judged the going to be the path of right, so the same blessing formula could also carry the household's grudging moral consent to the elopement."},{"a":"ai:376","b":"ai:371","type":"tension","note":"371 says the girl went having judged this the path of right (அறநெறி); 376 calls the fate that joined and sent her without right (அறனில் பால்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One decade by one poet lets the mother both concede that her daughter chose rightly and curse the joining as wrong, so the dispute over whether elopement is அறம் is staged inside a single lament rather than between mother and girl."},{"a":"ai:373","b":"nt:293","type":"develops","note":"Both mothers wish on the mother who bore the youth the same grief they feel (Natrinai: எம்போல் பெரு விதுப்புறுக; Ainkurunuru: நினைத்தொறும் கலிழும் இடும்பை எய்துக).","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Natrinai ties the wished grief to the sight of the girl's playmates at a festival, Ainkurunuru to every thought of the road, so the curse on the youth's mother was a set move whose point is to make the other mother feel exactly the speaker's own loss."},{"a":"ai:373","b":"a:195","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 195's mother says the youth's mother will joyfully paint her walls for the couple's coming; Ainkurunuru 373's mother wishes that same woman grief.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two poems show the bereaved mother's view of the other house in its two moods, envy of the joy it awaits and a curse that it should share her grief, so the youth's mother is a fixed counter-figure of this lament."},{"a":"ai:375","b":"nt:143","type":"develops","note":"Both mothers weep each time they see what the girl left (காண்தொறும்): Ainkurunuru the doll and the parrot she reared, Natrinai the courtyard, the playmates and the nochchi.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Ainkurunuru stops at the doubled grief of looking, while Natrinai turns from the same sight to declare the girl faultless, so the scene of the left belongings could lead either to bare lament or to the mother's acquittal of her daughter."},{"a":"ai:377","b":"a:369","type":"qualifies","note":"Ainkurunuru 377 says the girl left her ball, doll and pebbles to us; Akananuru 369 says the parrot will not drink and the doll gets no offering since she went.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"In Akananuru the left things mourn with the household, while in Ainkurunuru they are a bequest the girl hands back with her childhood, so the same toys can be made grieving witnesses or the plain sign that childhood is over."},{"a":"ai:378","b":"a:63","type":"develops","note":"Both use the turn 'I do not grieve that she went; I grieve for...' (நோவேன் ... நோவல்): Akananuru 63 for the girl's night in a robbers' hamlet, Ainkurunuru 378 for the friend left behind.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The 'not this but that' turn of the mother's lament could redirect grief either to the girl's hardships on the road or, as in Ainkurunuru, to the companion at home, so the lament counts the friend's loss as a second wound of the elopement.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:379","b":"a:369","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 369's girl refused the rich wedding her kin were preparing (தமர்மணன் அயரவும் ஒல்லாள்); Ainkurunuru 379's mother asks whether going with him is sweeter than a good wedding among her companions.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Akananuru states the refusal of the home wedding as a grievance, and Ainkurunuru asks the question that refusal implies, whether the road was sweeter to the girl, so the mother's lament moves from blaming the choice to trying to imagine the girl's own preference."},{"a":"ai:380","b":"a:263","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 263's mother says that had she known she would gladly have married the girl to him in her rich house; Ainkurunuru 380's mother says she was only mother in name and the playmates gave her away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together the two show the mother grieving a lost office as much as a lost daughter: the giving of the bride that 263 wishes she had performed, 380 says was done behind her back by the girl's companions."},{"a":"ai:374","b":"a:315","type":"tension","note":"Akananuru 315's mother saw the girl's hair curl and breasts swell and blames herself for not guarding her; Ainkurunuru 374's mother says the girl's hair was not yet long enough to be knotted.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"On one reading the two mothers read the same hair in opposite ways, as a sign of womanhood missed in Akananuru and as proof of childhood in Ainkurunuru, so the lament can either confess overlooked signs or deny that there were any."},{"a":"ai:372","b":"a:383","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 372 asks whether the girl thinks even of her mother (என்னும் உள்ளினள் கொல்லோ); Akananuru 383 says the girl never thought of the mother who raised her; gossip rises in both.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"What Ainkurunuru leaves as an anxious question Akananuru settles as a charge, so the pair shows the mother's doubt about being remembered hardening, in the longer poem, into reproach."},{"a":"ai:381","b":"ku:7","type":"develops","note":"Both have onlookers ask of the couple on the road யார்கொல் அளியர் தாமே; Kuruntokai leaves the question open over the rattling waste, Ainkurunuru answers it with the magandril pair.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same onlookers' formula could end in fear for the pair (Kuruntokai's dangerous bamboo waste) or in a verdict that they are to be pitied only because they cannot be parted, so அளியர் held both pity and admiration."},{"a":"ai:382","b":"a:321","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 321's foster mother wonders whether the girl will rest with her companion in a small hamlet (சீறூர்) or walk on into the night; Ainkurunuru 382 is told from the hamlet where they rested at nightfall (எல்லிடை அசைந்த ... சீறூர்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"What the foster mother can only imagine is answered from the hamlet itself, where strange women who have daughters feel her pain, so the grief of an elopement was pictured as reaching every house with a girl along the road."},{"a":"ai:383","b":"nt:362","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 362's eloping man tells the girl to play gathering velvet mites while he leans on a vengai on guard; Ainkurunuru 383 shows from outside the youth holding the maraam branch while she picks flowers for her doll.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Natrinai gives the man's own promise of guardianship and Ainkurunuru the onlooker's view of his delight in her child's play, so together they fix the eloping hero as an indulgent guardian of a girl still at play rather than a seducer."},{"a":"ai:384","b":"nt:12","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 12's girl, leaving, pities the companions who will grieve at her ball and anklets; Ainkurunuru 384's girl, on the road, sends those companions word through brahmins that she went so her beauty might flourish.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The companions are the girl's first thought at both ends of the flight, pitied as she leaves and answered once she is away, and her message turns the pity into a justification that her going completes her mother's care."},{"a":"ai:385","b":"ai:376","type":"tension","note":"376's mother curses the fate that joined and sent the girl as without right (அறனில் பால்); 385's girl calls her mother without right (அறனில் யாய்) for keeping her at home.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In two adjoining decades by one poet each side of the elopement denies aram to the other, the mother blaming the fate that joined the lovers and the daughter blaming the mother's guard, so the dispute over whether the going was right is staged from both ends."},{"a":"ai:386","b":"a:35","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 35's girl leaves the sky-touching walled house desolate (வான்தோய் இஞ்சி நன்னகர் புலம்ப); in Ainkurunuru 386 the mother is bewildered in the tall-walled good house (நெடுஞ்சுவர் நல்லில் மருண்ட).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The high wall that in Akananuru stands empty after the girl has slipped it becomes in Ainkurunuru the place where the mother is lost, so the house built to guard a daughter is shown to hold only the one left behind."},{"a":"ai:387","b":"kl:9","type":"develops","note":"In both a searching woman asks brahmins on the waste road (அந்தணீர், அந்தணிர்) and they answer that they saw the girl (கண்டனம், கண்டனெம்); Kalittokai adds the sandal, pearl and lute argument and says the way never leaves aram.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The scene that Ainkurunuru leaves as a bare eyewitness report of the lover's tenderness was later expanded by Kalittokai into an argument that a daughter belongs to the man she follows, so the brahmins' reply grew from testimony into doctrine.","gr":3},{"a":"ai:388","b":"kl:9","type":"qualifies","note":"Kalittokai's brahmins tell the searching mother not to grieve for the girl who followed the man she chose; Ainkurunuru 388's travellers tell the searcher to rest out the noon heat and go on over the little hill to see the couple's road.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Wayside people meet the pursuer with two different answers, reasons to stop grieving in Kalittokai and directions to go on in Ainkurunuru, so the pursuit was not always treated as something to be talked out of."},{"a":"ai:389","b":"a:17","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 17's foster mother asks whether her girl's small anklet-filled feet (சிலம்புஆர் சீறுடி) can walk the waterless waste; Ainkurunuru 389's searcher asks whether her anklet feet (அம்சிலம் படியே) kept pace with the youth who walked slowly.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The anklet foot is the searching woman's measure in both, but Akananuru weighs it against the road's hardship while Ainkurunuru, told the youth went slowly, weighs it against him, so the same worry could move from the danger of the way to the fitness of the match."},{"a":"ai:390","b":"nt:198","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 198 gives the foster mother's own words to those she meets in the waste, describing her daughter and blessing them; Ainkurunuru 390 gives the onlookers' reply to a woman who folds her hands to every good person and asks again and again.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Natrinai lets the searcher speak and Ainkurunuru shows how she looked to those she asked, wandering and worshipping strangers, so the two halves of the wayside exchange make up one recognised scene in which the onlookers' certainty answers her supplication."},{"a":"ai:391","b":"ku:210","type":"develops","note":"Both reward the crow whose cawing brings a guest (விருந்துவரக் கரைந்த காக்கை; வரக்கரைந் தீமே): Kuruntokai's friend after the man has come, Ainkurunuru's speaker before the girl has.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kuruntokai pays the crow afterwards and calls even seven bowls too little, while Ainkurunuru promises meat in a gold bowl in advance, so together they show the crow offering working as a bargain struck with the omen on either side of the homecoming."},{"a":"ai:393","b":"ai:376","type":"develops","note":"376 is the mother's curse on the fate without right (அறனில் பால்) that sent the girl; 393 describes her from outside as one who quarrelled with right and blamed it (அறம்புலந்து பழிக்கும்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The curse the mother speaks in her own voice in decade 38 is named in decade 40 as a quarrel with அறம் that the daughter's return is meant to heal, so the poet frames the lament as a dispute with the moral order that the homecoming settles."},{"a":"ai:394","b":"ai:376","type":"qualifies","note":"376 calls the joining that sent the girl away a fate without right; 394 says the loveless right has after all shown grace (அன்பில் அறனும் அருளிற்று).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same poet lets the verdict on அறம் turn with events, loveless when the girl left and gracious when she returns, so in these decades the moral order is judged by its outcome for the household rather than held fixed."},{"a":"ai:395","b":"nt:264","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are the man's words to the girl at the end of the road as his own place comes into view (எம் சிறு நல் ஊரே; நம் மலைகெழு நாட்டே); Natrinai urges her on (ஏகுதி) because the day is ending, Ainkurunuru tells her to go slowly (மென்மெல ஏகுமதி) because the wasteland is behind them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same command to walk on means hurry in Natrinai and go slowly in Ainkurunuru, so the man's care at the journey's end is shaped by what threatens, the coming night or the danger just passed."},{"a":"ai:396","b":"a:257","type":"develops","note":"In both the man decks the eloping girl's hair with forest flowers, maraa in Akananuru 257 on the summer road, vengai in Ainkurunuru 396 at the edge of his town.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru's adorning happens on the march and admires how she walks, while Ainkurunuru's is a pause that lets her rest before she enters his town as a guest, so the flowers in her hair move from a token of the road to a preparation for her reception."},{"a":"ai:397","b":"nt:12","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 12's girl, leaving by stealth, pities her companions (அளியரோ அளியர் என் ஆயத்தோர்) who will grieve at the ball and anklets she left; Ainkurunuru 397's speaker sends word ahead to her companions (என் ஆயத்தோர்க்கே) that she is coming.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The companions whose grief the girl foresaw as she slipped away are the first she sends a message to on the way back, so the playmates frame the elopement at both ends, its first cost and its first news."},{"a":"ai:398","b":"ai:373","type":"develops","note":"373 wishes on the youth's mother the grief of weeping at every thought (நினைத்தொறும் கலிழும் இடும்பை); 398's speaker says that she herself weeps so at every thought (நினைத்தொறும் கலிழும் என்னினும்) but the town is lonelier still.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the curse in 373 turns out to be the speaker's own condition, wished on the other mother, and in 398 the same grief is no longer used against anyone but set beside the town's loss while she blesses the road."},{"a":"ai:399","b":"a:195","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 195's mother asks the diviner whether the youth will bring the girl first to her house or to his own (எம்மனை முந்துறத் தருமோ? தன்மனை உய்க்குமோ?); Ainkurunuru 399 proposes the answer: the anklet-removal in his house, the wedding in ours.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The question Akananuru leaves to the diviner Ainkurunuru settles by dividing the rites between the two houses, which shows that which house received the couple was a matter of standing that families negotiated, not only a mother's anxiety.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:399","b":"a:369","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 369's foster mother fears the girl had her anklets removed without honour in a poor stranger's hut (சிலம்புடன் கழீஇ); Ainkurunuru 399 concedes the anklet-removal to the youth's house but claims the wedding for the girl's own.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The anklet-removal that Akananuru dreads as a rite done without the family is in Ainkurunuru granted to the other house on condition of a proper wedding at home, so the girl's people could accept the lost rite once the honour of the wedding was kept."},{"a":"ai:410","b":"k:66","type":"develops","note":"Both say the lute is outdone by one's child: Kural 66 that only those who have not heard their children's prattle call the lute sweet; Ainkurunuru 410 that the bard's lute did not match the hour when the son rode on his father's chest.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The Kural's maxim has a scene behind it in which the lute is actually playing in the courtyard while the child plays, so the comparison was first a judgment between two pleasures heard together, and the child's worth lay in the whole family hour, not in speech alone.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:403","b":"pn:188","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 188 says a rich man's days are fruitless without toddling children (குறுகுறு நடந்து); Ainkurunuru 403 shows a toddler (தளர்நடை) pulling a toy chariot and named after his grandfather.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Purananuru gives as general counsel that toddling children are what make a life worth having, and Ainkurunuru adds, through the grandfather's name the boy bears, that the toddler is valued also as the next link of a famous line."},{"a":"ai:405","b":"a:184","type":"develops","note":"Both call the wife who bore a son the lamp of the house in a mullai poem: Akananuru 184 குடிக்குவிளக்கு ஆகிய புதல்வற் பயந்த, Ainkurunuru 405 மனைக்குவிளக்காயினள் ... புதல்வன் தாயே.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the lamp of the house was a fixed title of the mullai wife won by bearing a son: Akananuru grounds it in godlike chastity and fame, while Ainkurunuru turns the title back into a seen flame, the red light of a bowl lamp."},{"a":"ai:404","b":"a:26","type":"develops","note":"The same gesture, the husband holding his wife's small back (சிறுபுறம் கவையினன்) while she attends to their son: in Akananuru 26 after he had held off for fear of spilling her milk, in Ainkurunuru 404 while she nurses.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 26 shows the back embrace as the husband's answer to a wife who has turned to the child after his coolness, so when the foster mother in 404 calls the same gesture good she approves the father's inclusion of himself in the mother and son, the very move that ended a sulk elsewhere.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:410","b":"ai:402","type":"qualifies","note":"402 measures the couple's bed by the sweetness of bards plucking strings; 410 says the bard's lute could not match the joy once the son rode on his father's chest.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"Within one decade the bard's music first serves as the measure of the couple's sweetness and then falls short of the family hour, so the poet ranks the joy of the child above the joy of the couple's union."},{"a":"ai:409","b":"ai:401","type":"qualifies","note":"Both repeat இனிது மன்றஅவர் கிடக்கை; 401 says this world and heaven cannot easily obtain it, 409 says it is worth the whole of this world.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same refrain carries two prices, above both worlds and equal to this one, so the foster mother's hyperbole is a varied formula of the decade rather than a fixed claim about the household's worth."},{"a":"ai:415","b":"ku:126","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai's waiting woman says the men went for wealth without regard for youth (இளமை பாரார்); Ainkurunuru's returned hero says youth is sweet if spent together (இனிதுடன் கழிக்கின் இளமை).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The charge the abandoned woman makes in the rains is answered in the same season by the man come home, who adopts her measure of youth as his own, so the two poems stage the mullai argument over youth and wealth from both sides of the parting."},{"a":"ai:415","b":"n:16","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar says the passing joy of youth is not found among the wise; the Ainkurunuru hero says youth spent together is sweet and the union is sweet now.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both accept that youth passes, but the Naladiyar draws from that fact a duty to withhold delight, while the Sangam hero draws from it a reason to spend youth in shared pleasure, so the same premise yields opposite counsel in the old love poetry and the later ascetic quatrains."},{"a":"ai:419","b":"ku:65","type":"same_image","note":"In Kuruntokai the stag frisking with its sweet mate in the cool rain seems to taunt the waiting woman; in Ainkurunuru the hero shows his wife the beasts joined in delight, like us.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The paired beasts of the rainy scrub are one image read two ways: before the return they reproach the absent man through the woman's grief, after it they mirror the couple's reunion, so the image takes its sense from whether the speaker is still waiting."},{"a":"ai:419","b":"k:1122","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's man says the bond between him and the girl is like that of body and life; the Ainkurunuru hero speaks of their faultless bond as lives mingled into one (உயிர்கலந்து ஒன்றிய).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the life-bond as a lover's private claim, while the Ainkurunuru hero, past a parting, finds it acted out by the scrub's mating beasts and makes the couple the measure of them, so the general likeness gains a witness in the season of return."},{"a":"ai:414","b":"a:134","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 134 the returning hero hushes his chariot so the stag and doe in union are not parted; in Ainkurunuru 414, now home, he bids his wife look at birds and beasts frisking in pairs.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"On the road the hero shields the beasts' union as a sign of the one he is hurrying to, and once home he shows the paired creatures to his wife as a shared sight, so the mullai beasts move from his private token to the couple's common pleasure."},{"a":"ai:420","b":"nt:242","type":"develops","note":"Both use the same pair of similes for the rain flowers: konrai like gold (பொன் என), kaayaa like sapphire (மணி என); Natrinai's returning hero urges his charioteer on, Ainkurunuru's promises to bring his wife to see the scrub.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same jewelled konrai and kaayaa that make the returning man in Natrinai hurry home become, once he is home, a sight he promises to return to with his wife, so the formula's force changes from summons to leisure with the man's place in the journey."},{"a":"ai:417","b":"nt:139","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai's hero, back from his work, blesses the cloud whose night rain fell on the town where he lay embraced with his wife; Ainkurunuru's says the rains mingled with the scrub and she embraced him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Natrinai turns the coincidence of rain and embrace into thanks owed to the cloud, while Ainkurunuru sets the two side by side without comment, so together they show the returning hero crediting the season with the reunion it once threatened."},{"a":"ai:418","b":"k:1108","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's man says an embrace no breeze can part is sweet to two who love; the Ainkurunuru hero asks if his wife, who held him close against her breasts (மாண்முலை அடைய முயங்கியோயே), is a woman of the sky.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the close embrace as a pleasure common to all lovers, but the Ainkurunuru hero, after absence, finds the same embrace so beyond the human that he takes his own wife for a celestial, so the general sweetness is shown as wonder when it is regained."},{"a":"ai:424","b":"a:66","type":"develops","note":"In 424 the friend warns the man that his son will cry for the breast if he goes; in Akananuru 66 the crying son actually stops the man's chariot on the way to another wedding.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend's threat in Ainkurunuru 424 is the plan of which Akananuru 66 shows the working: the son as the one claim a departing husband cannot argue with was a recognised move, used against a journey for the king's work and against a journey to another woman alike."},{"a":"ai:424","b":"nt:166","type":"develops","note":"In Natrinai 166 the husband himself tells his wife that their son has learnt to play and asks why he should part from her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Natrinai gives the husband's own voice answering the friend's argument of Ainkurunuru 424, so the son as a reason to stay worked from both sides of the speech situation: urged on the man by the friend, and taken up by him unprompted.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:426","b":"a:214","type":"tension","note":"In 426 the man has given up the king's hard task to stay with his wife; in Akananuru 214 the man in the camp says the war is their own duty (அமரும் நம்வயினதுவே) while imagining her lonely rainy evening.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Held together, the poems show that the mullai husband's bond to the king's war was not fixed: one binds himself to it despite her loneliness, the other renounces it for her brow, so akam staged a real choice between royal service and the wife."},{"a":"ai:428","b":"a:84","type":"qualifies","note":"In 428 the rains, with the rainbow, halt the chariots and end the king's task; in Akananuru 84 the rains and the rainbow come but the angry king refuses tribute and moves his army on.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The convention that the rains end a campaign, which 428 takes for granted, is broken in Akananuru 84 by one king's anger, so the rainbow is a promise of return that only the king can keep or cancel."},{"a":"ai:429","b":"ai:426","type":"qualifies","note":"In 426 the man has given up the king's war; in 429 he says he would go to it if the woman puts away her pallor.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Within one decade the poet sets a flat refusal of royal service beside a conditional consent to it whose condition, her pallor gone, is undone by the going itself, so 429 reads as a refusal phrased as a promise."},{"a":"ai:421","b":"a:5","type":"develops","note":"Both men give up a planned journey because of the woman; in 421 her shoulders 'block' the going, in Akananuru 5 her silent grieving and the son at her breast.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Ainkurunuru keeps only the outcome and one bodily sign of what Akananuru 5 unfolds as a scene of silent pleading, so the short form shows that the persuasion could be left wholly implied once the convention of the abandoned journey was known."},{"a":"ai:425","b":"a:254","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 254 the waiting wife watches the house dove fly with its mate while the man drives home; in 425 the man on the road hears the forest dove croon for its mate.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Together the poems place the paired dove at both ends of the return journey, in the wife's courtyard and on the husband's road, so the same creature serves as the sign by which each partner reads the other's longing."},{"a":"ai:423","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Both dissuade the man from leaving, in the friend's voice on Parimelazhagar's reading of the couplet: the Kural argues from her survival, 423 from the rain already falling and her filled eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two show that the dissuasion of a departure had two set arguments, the season and her life, and the Kural keeps only the harsher second, while the Ainkurunuru friend leans on the order of the seasons that a man who leaves in the rains breaks."},{"a":"ai:431","b":"kn:4","type":"develops","note":"Both set peacocks in their finery and the konrai-lovely forest of the rains before a waiting woman; Kar Narpathu's friend names the season the cure (மருந்து) for her pallor.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kar Narpathu spells out that the season's beauty is a promise of return and so a cure, while Ainkurunuru only calls his road good for having it, so the decade's bare praise of the road is the same reassurance left unspoken."},{"a":"ai:432","b":"ku:21","type":"qualifies","note":"Both see the konrai as gold (Kuruntokai: பொன்செய் புனையிழை; Ainkurunuru: சுடுபொன் அன்ன கொன்றை); Kuruntokai's heroine refuses the forest's word that the rains have come, since he does not lie.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The gold konrai that Kuruntokai's woman must deny, because admitting the season means admitting a broken promise, is here worn by men on his road and praised, so the same flower could be held off as false witness or welcomed as the season's adornment of his way."},{"a":"ai:433","b":"ku:66","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai's friend calls the konrai foolish for taking a stray shower for the kaar rains (வம்ப மாரியைக் காரென); Ainkurunuru calls the forest on his road one that has met the kaar rain (கார்ப்பெயற்கு எதிரிய).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Holding the two together shows that whether the forest's flowering marks the true kaar was itself what the waiting woman and her friend argued over, Kuruntokai explaining the signs away while Ainkurunuru accepts them."},{"a":"ai:434","b":"a:34","type":"develops","note":"Both put a doe with her frisking fawn on the rainy mullai road (Ainkurunuru: மறியுடை மான்பிணை; Akananuru: மறிஆடு மருங்கின் மடப்பிணை); in Akananuru the stag feeds and guards her and the returning man hurries his chariot home.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The image the waiting woman sets on his road without comment is, in Akananuru, the very sight that turns the man homeward, so the doe and fawn worked as a shared sign that the rains call the traveller back to wife and child."},{"a":"ai:440","b":"nt:242","type":"develops","note":"Both list the flowers of the first rains (Ainkurunuru: தோன்றி, தளவம்; Natrinai: பிடவம், தளவம், கொன்றை, காயா); in Natrinai the returning man sees them and tells his charioteer the kaar has begun.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the woman praises as the beauty of his road Natrinai shows from the other end, the man reading those same flowers as his deadline and driving hard, so her praise rests on a hope the hero's poem shows being kept."},{"a":"ai:441","b":"a:304","type":"develops","note":"Both men in the war camp turn on the wife's ignorance of their pain: Akananuru 304 says she does not know it (நந்நோய் தன்வயின் அறியாள்), Ainkurunuru 441 wishes she knew it (அவள் அறியினோ நன்றே).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 304 spells out what the wife who does not know will think, that the man is without right or grace, so the bare wish of 441 is revealed as the soldier's answer to an imagined accusation of neglect."},{"a":"ai:442","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"Both set the home feast against the king's war: the couplet says let the king fight and win and let us feast at evening; 442 says if the king's war subsides she will receive guests in plenty.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem names whose feast the couplet's விருந்து is, the wife as hostess of guests and mother of a son, so the couplet's private wish is shown to be the restoration of a whole household that the king's war holds in suspense."},{"a":"ai:444","b":"a:164","type":"develops","note":"Both end on the same conditional of the camp: the king's elephants with tusk-rings split on enemy forts, and 'if the fierce king releases us' (வினைவிடப் பெறினே / முன்னுதல் பெறினே).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 164 sets the fort-blunted tusks after a whole season scene and her regained beauty, while 444 keeps only the tusks and the sight of her, which shows the worn elephant is the paasarai situation's own sign of a war too long, not a seasonal ornament."},{"a":"ai:448","b":"a:24","type":"same_image","note":"The same deep-voiced drum (தழங்குகுரல் முரசம்) sounds in both camps; in Akananuru 24 it keeps the sword-bearing army awake at night, in 448 the man meets sleeplessness thinking of her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the drum that keeps the soldiers sleepless for the king's war is the same drum under which the man lies sleepless for his wife, so the camp's wakefulness is shown to be both duty and longing at once."},{"a":"ai:449","b":"kn:20","type":"develops","note":"449 has the chariot and horses ready on condition the king finishes his work (வேந்துவினை முடினே); Kar Narpathu 20 reports the king's work done (வேந்தன் வினையு முடிந்தன) and the road grown pleasant.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair gives the two moments of the mullai return, the chariot readied in the camp while the king's work hangs, and the fulfilment when the work is done and the rain-cloud itself seems an army going home."},{"a":"ai:451","b":"a:214","type":"tension","note":"In Akananuru 214 the man in the rain-soaked camp says the war is ours (அமரும் நம்வயினதுவே) while the king does not sleep; in Ainkurunuru 451 the wife says holding out in camp is the king's work (வேந்தனது தொழிலே), not her husband's.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"On the same rainy-season campaign the soldier claims the war as his own duty and the wife assigns it wholly to the king, so the mullai situation is a dispute over where a man's obligation ends, and each side rests on a reading of whose war it is.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:453","b":"a:364","type":"qualifies","note":"Both open with frogs in every hollow (அவல்தோறு, அவல்தொறும்) and the formula கார்தொடங் கின்றே/கின்றால் காலை; in Akananuru 364 the woman concludes that he in the king's camp does not think of us, in Ainkurunuru 453 that his chariot has set out toward us.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same signs of the first rains, in nearly the same words, lead one wife to despair and another to expect the chariot, so the season's onset in mullai poetry is not itself a message: its meaning is decided by what the woman believes of the man.","gr":3},{"a":"ai:455","b":"k:1234","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1234: with the partner gone, the shoulders lose their old beauty (தொல்கவின்) and the bangles slip; Ainkurunuru 455: the shoulders, their bangles loosened, have lost their old beauty (தொன்னலம்) just as the rains begin that stop the kings' war.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet states the wasting of the shoulders as the general mark of separation; the Ainkurunuru poem times it to the rains that end the campaign, so the slipping bangle becomes evidence that he is overdue, not only that he is away.","gr":3},{"a":"ai:456","b":"a:24","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 24: in the last days of the cold rains, when the pakanrai opens, the man in the king's camp pictures his wife crossing the cold nights alone; Ainkurunuru 456: in the dewy cold season of pakanrai, the wife asks whether the men who promised to stay do not think of her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Her question in 456, does he not think of us, is answered in Akananuru 24, where in the same pakanrai season he does think of her from the camp, so the pair shows that remembering runs both ways while neither lover can know it."},{"a":"ai:458","b":"k:1231","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1231: thinking of him who went far, her eyes are ashamed before fragrant flowers; Ainkurunuru 458: the rain-beaten kondrai flowers have come to resemble her eyes, which lost their beauty when he left.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The couplet's eyes lose a contest with flowers they once outshone; the Ainkurunuru poem reverses the direction, bringing the flowers down to the state of the eyes, so the wasted eyes become the measure the season is read by."},{"a":"ai:459","b":"k:1265","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1265: once she has seen her husband to her eyes' fill the pallor of her soft shoulders will leave; Ainkurunuru 459: she asks whether she can embrace his chest so the pallor of her shoulders goes, while he is away with the king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both name the husband's presence as the cure for pallor, but the couplet makes the cure sight while the Ainkurunuru wife makes it embrace and doubts it is possible at all, because the king's war, not the man, decides when he comes."},{"a":"ai:460","b":"ai:451","type":"develops","note":"In 451 the wife argues that endurance in the camp is the king's work, not her husband's; in 460 she admits the king does not weary of the camp and no message comes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read in order, the decade moves from argument to helplessness: the claim in 451 that the king alone should bear the camp turns in 460 into the recognition that the king's appetite for the camp is exactly what holds her husband there."},{"a":"ai:462","b":"nt:99","type":"develops","note":"In both, the friend tells the waiting woman that the konrai bloomed foolishly, taking an unseasonal shower for the rains (Natrinai: கார் என்று அயர்ந்த ... மடவ; Ainkurunuru: காரென மயங்கிய பேதையம் கொன்றை).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Natrinai answers a question about the promised date by blaming both the mindless cloud and the flowers, while Ainkurunuru answers tears by blaming only the konrai and then vouching for the man, so the 'foolish flower' was a stock rebuttal the friend could aim at the calendar or at grief."},{"a":"ai:462","b":"ku:21","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai's heroine herself refuses to believe the forest's konrai that says the rains have come, because he does not lie; Ainkurunuru's friend calls the same konrai deceived.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Read together, the two show that discrediting the konrai's sign was the shared way to keep the man's promise unbroken, whether the heroine did it out of trust in his word or the friend did it to stop her weeping."},{"a":"ai:461","b":"a:364","type":"tension","note":"Akananuru 364's heroine says the rains have begun and her lover in the fierce king's great camp, set on victory, does not think of them (நம்மும் உள்ளார்); Ainkurunuru 461's friend says the men of the victorious king's camp will not rest content having left her.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same facts, the rains begun and the man held in a victorious king's camp, support despair in the heroine's mouth and assurance in the friend's, so the mullai situation was fixed and only the speaker decided its meaning.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:468","b":"a:364","type":"same_image","note":"Both open on striped frogs croaking and close the scene with the formula கார்தொடங் கின்றே காலை.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The identical formula leads in Akananuru to an evening the heroine cannot swim through and in Ainkurunuru to a chariot arriving today, showing that the first-rain formula was a neutral hinge onto which either the dread of evening or the promise of return could be fastened."},{"a":"ai:467","b":"a:164","type":"tension","note":"In Akananuru 164 the hero in camp says his wife could regain her beauty this season if only the fierce king would release him; in Ainkurunuru 467 the friend says the king is hurrying more than they are.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The hero's own speech shows the king as what holds him back, while the friend at home claims the king is more eager than the wives, so the friend's comfort is revealed as persuasion built on a report the camp itself contradicts."},{"a":"ai:466","b":"kn:20","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu 20's hero says the king's work is finished and the roads are pleasant; Ainkurunuru 466's friend says the king has released him after the great work and she is sure of his coming.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hero announces the release as a fact on the road, while the friend can only infer it at home and stake her certainty on it, so the release from the king's work was the one event both ends of the mullai separation waited on."},{"a":"ai:469","b":"kn:12","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu 12's friend takes the dark clouds rising on the right (ஏரும் வலம்) every day as a sign beyond doubt of his coming; Ainkurunuru 469's cloud rises on the right (வலன்ஏர்ப்பு) to bring him.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Kar Narpathu treats the rightward rising cloud as evidence to be read, Ainkurunuru as an escort that brings the man and an occasion to go out and watch, so the omen of the right side could serve the friend both as proof and as a reason to act."},{"a":"ai:464","b":"kn:9","type":"same_image","note":"Both say the rain-season flowers have opened like eyes (கண்ணெனக் கருவிளை மலர; கண்மலர்போற் பூத்தன) as the friend's season sign.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In Kar Narpathu the eye-like flowers speak and announce the man's return, in Ainkurunuru they only mark a season he cannot forget, so the same flower simile let the friend present the hedge either as a messenger or as a calendar."},{"a":"ai:471","b":"ai:477","type":"qualifies","note":"471's heroine resents that the bard keeps coming (இன்னும் வருதி); in 477 the man tells the bard to stay a little as her comfort (உயவுத்துணை).","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Read together, the bard's persistence that the wife takes for indifference turns out to be the man's own order, so the decade shows one act judged from both ends of the separation."},{"a":"ai:480","b":"ai:475","type":"develops","note":"In 475 the heroine says the bard grieves for her more than his master does; in 480 the bard himself renounces his master over her tears.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sympathy the wife reads in the bard's face becomes, by the decade's last poem, a spoken break with the patron, so the decade moves the go-between from the man's party to the woman's."},{"a":"ai:472","b":"nt:86","type":"qualifies","note":"Natrinai 86 blesses the men as அறவர் for coming back before the season they named; Ainkurunuru 472 says the season has come and its makers feel no shame at their false word.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The named season works as a vow with a moral verdict either way: kept early it earns the title of righteous men, broken it is charged as shamelessness, so the return date was judged as truthfulness rather than as mere timing."},{"a":"ai:476","b":"a:14","type":"develops","note":"Both wives put to the bard a question about the evening of the herding land in the rains (Akananuru: மாலையும் உள்ளார் ஆயின் ... பாண; Ainkurunuru: மாலையும் உடைத்தோ ... பாண).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Ainkurunuru gives only the wife's accusing question and her name for him, loveless bard, while Akananuru tells it from the bard's side, who could not answer, played and prayed and went toward the man, so the pair shows the question was one the bard had no words for."},{"a":"ai:476","b":"k:1222","type":"qualifies","note":"Both have the waiting woman put a question to or about evening: the Kural asks evening whether its partner is as hard as hers; Ainkurunuru asks whether his land has this loveless evening at all.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural explains the man's calm by a hard heart that evening shares, the Ainkurunuru by a land the evening may not reach, so the two give the waiting woman two answers to why he does not suffer the hour she suffers."},{"a":"ai:477","b":"a:314","type":"develops","note":"In 477 the man tells the bard to stay with his grieving wife (துனி மலி துயரமொடு) and promises his chariot; in Akananuru 314 the wife has asked the bard what will become of her, and the man arrives to end her துனிகொள் பருவரல்.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The man's instruction and promise in Ainkurunuru and the friend's welcome in Akananuru are two ends of one convention: the bard is posted with the wife to field her question, and the chariot's coming is what answers it."},{"a":"ai:479","b":"a:244","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 244 the bard's report of the wife's words makes the man yoke the horses at once, his work being done; in 479 the man, still kept away by the lands between, asks to hear her words again because they are sweet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same report of the wife's words could be a spur that ends the campaign or a comfort savoured while the man stays on, so the bard's message did different work depending on whether the man's task was finished."},{"a":"ai:475","b":"pn:144","type":"develops","note":"In Purananuru 144 bards see a woman weeping over Pekan's neglect and rebuke him; in 475 the heroine says the bard wept over her wasting more than his master.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Puram shows the bard's pity for the neglected woman turned into a public rebuke of his patron, and akam shows the woman herself noticing that pity and setting it against her lord's coldness, so the bard's tears were a recognised charge against the man in both genres."},{"a":"ai:480","b":"pn:145","type":"qualifies","note":"In Purananuru 145 Paranar asks as his only gift that Pekan ride to the grieving woman tonight; in 480 the bard, unheard, declares that he is no longer the man's bard.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Puram's bard spends his claim on the patron to win the woman's cause, while the Ainkurunuru bard, his plea having failed, gives up the claim itself, so the pair shows the patron bond as the bard's leverage and its renunciation as his last resort."},{"a":"ai:481","b":"nt:21","type":"develops","note":"Both returning men order the goad (Ainkurunuru: முள் இட்டு ஊர்மதி; Natrinai: தீண்டா வை முள் தீண்டி), and Natrinai's order opens with the same half-line ஏமதி வலவ தேரே that Ainkurunuru 485 uses.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Natrinai adds that this goad has never before touched these horses, which shows what Ainkurunuru's bare order assumes: the goad's sting was the convention for an urgency greater than any the charioteer had known."},{"a":"ai:482","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"Both set the king's war against the home feast in the man's mouth: the Kural lets the king win and the man feast at evening with his wife; Ainkurunuru says one more day with the victorious king would be an age.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the two duties as a calm pair of wishes, while the Sangam poem shows the strain inside that balance, since the king's victory is honoured in a line and then a single day of further service is weighed as an aeon."},{"a":"ai:482","b":"nt:81","type":"qualifies","note":"Natrinai 81's man pictures his wife eager to prepare a feast for his coming (விருந்து அயர் விருப்பொடு); Ainkurunuru 482's man calls himself the great feast she will have (பெருவிருந்து).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Read together they show the word virundhu turning in the return poems from the meal the wife prepares to the husband himself as the guest she receives, so his homecoming is framed as the household's rite of hospitality.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:483","b":"ai:200","type":"develops","note":"483's man drives so the woman may get back her beauty (தன்னலம் பெறவே); in 200 the friend sees the bangles tighten and the brow grow lovely now that the lord of the golden chariot has come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same anthology gives the restoring of beauty first as the returning man's purpose and then as a fact the friend watches on the woman's body, so nalam regained is the agreed proof, in mullai and neythal alike, that a separation is over."},{"a":"ai:484","b":"a:144","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 144's man imagines his wife saying he does not even remember her hair bare of mullai (கதுப்பும் உள்ளார்); Ainkurunuru 484's man says himself that they have stayed long, forgetting her (நீடினம் நேரிழை மறந்தே).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The charge of forgetting that the longer poem puts in the waiting wife's mouth, the short poem has the husband confess unprompted, so the return poems treat the man's own guilt over the delay as the counterpart of her reproach and the hurried drive as his answer to it."},{"a":"ai:485","b":"nt:242","type":"develops","note":"In both the returning man drives through the rain-season scrub where deer run startled; Natrinai 242 follows one stag that will not leave but searches for its scattered doe.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Ainkurunuru leaves the startled beasts as the land the chariot breaks through, while Natrinai singles out one stag seeking its mate, so the pair shows how the same stock scene becomes a mirror of the man's search for his wife only when one creature is given a lover's act."},{"a":"ai:487","b":"ku:400","type":"develops","note":"Both praise the charioteer's wit with the same epithet (Ainkurunuru: மதியுடை வலவ; Kuruntokai: மதியுடை வல்லோய்): Ainkurunuru before the drive, Kuruntokai after it, for cutting a new road and bringing the chariot home today.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Ainkurunuru asks for the charioteer's skill and Kuruntokai thanks him for having used it, so the two show the charioteer as more than a driver: the returning man credits him with the judgement that ends the wife's suffering."},{"a":"ai:488","b":"a:74","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 74 the friend comforts the heroine that the man, seeing the rain-washed scrub, will think of her and say 'drive, charioteer of the strong chariot'; Ainkurunuru 488 is a returning man saying just that as the rains begin.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 74 shows the waiting wife doubting the comfort that he will remember her at the rains, and Ainkurunuru 488 gives the very order the friend predicted, so the decade supplies the man's side of a promise that the akam comforting formula only foretells."},{"a":"ai:489","b":"a:204","type":"develops","note":"Both bid the charioteer drive fast at the evening of the rains, when swarms of bees hum at the fragrant mullai; Akananuru 204 frames it with the Pandya's victory camp and ends on the woman's shoulders.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Ainkurunuru gives the bee-and-mullai evening with only the girl's sorrowing heart for a goal, Akananuru wraps the same scene in a Pandya campaign, so the pair shows the king's war as an optional frame around a fixed core of season, hour and hurried drive."},{"a":"ai:492","b":"kn:9","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu's friend sees flowers bloom like the woman's eyes and hears them announce his return; in 492 the man sees peacock, mullai and deer as likenesses of her and comes home.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same likening of the season's things to the woman works from both ends of the separation: for her the flowers that resemble her eyes are messengers of his return, while for him the creatures that resemble her are what sent him home, so the comparison is the bond that carries the promise across."},{"a":"ai:495","b":"a:4","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 4's friend says the flowering land turns its back on loneliness (புலம்புபுறக் கொடுப்ப) as his chariot comes into view; 495's man says the scrub's loneliness has ended (புலம்பு தீர்ந்து) because he came.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"What the friend in Akananuru offers as a sign that he is about to arrive, the man in 495 claims as his own doing, so the land's loneliness was read as the woman's own, ending at the same moment hers does.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:497","b":"pn:51","type":"same_image","note":"Both have winged termites pouring from a red anthill (Purananuru: செம்புற்று ஈயல்; Ainkurunuru: நெடுஞ்செம் புற்றம் ஈயல்); Purananuru makes them the one-day life of kings who defied Vazhuthi.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The swarm of the first rains meant short-lived ruin in a war poem and food that ends the beasts' hunger in a love poem, and 497's war-loving lord stands between the two, come home from the kind of war in which other men were the termites."},{"a":"ai:498","b":"a:164","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 164 the man in the war camp says she would regain her old beauty in this season if only the king released him from his work (வேந்தன் வினைவிடப் பெறினே); 498 reports that the king left off his war (வேந்துதொழில் விட்டென) and her shoulders, bangles and eyes recovered.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two poems give the conditional and its fulfilment of one plot, and show that in these mullai returns the woman's beauty hung on the king's campaign, not on the husband's will alone."},{"a":"ai:498","b":"k:1265","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's woman says the pallor of her soft shoulders will leave only once she has seen him to her eyes' fill; 498 says her shoulders have regained their beauty because he came.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states as the woman's own condition that only his presence cures the pallor, and 498 shows that condition met and observed from outside, item by item on her body, so the couplet's claim is confirmed as the accepted course of the return.","gr":2},{"a":"ai:496","b":"k:1263","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural's woman says he went away with his resolve as companion (உள்ளம் துணையாக) and she lives only by longing for his return; 496's friend says he has come back to be the companion of her shoulders (தோள் துணையாக).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The word துணை marks the turn of the plot: the reproach that he chose his resolve as companion over her is answered on his return, when the companionship is given back to her as embrace, so the Kural's complaint is a stage the akam story expects to close."},{"a":"ai:499","b":"ai:357","type":"qualifies","note":"In 357 the man sees the season-dressed forest himself and gives up the journey for wealth; in 499 he gives up his hard work because she would grieve if she saw the rain-dressed forest.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Ainkurunuru gives two reasons for the same turning back, the forest's beauty moving the man himself in the paalai decade and the forest's power to hurt her moving him in the mullai decade, so the season is at once his longing and his sense of her danger."},{"a":"a:0","b":"pn:1","type":"same_image","note":"Both invocations, ascribed to Perundevanar, list Shiva by the same marks: the konrai garland and chaplet, the bull, the dark throat, the crescent, the matted hair. Akananuru 0 says Umai is joined to him (சேர்ந்தோள் உமையே); Purananuru 1 says the woman's form is one part of him that he can hide within himself.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Placed side by side, the two openings show each anthology choosing the god's form that suits its matter: the love anthology gives him a consort beside him, the outer anthology a body that holds the woman within it and shelters all lives."},{"a":"a:1","b":"a:61","type":"develops","note":"Both are Mamoolanar's paalai poems that use Avi's Pothini: in 1 the woman fears the man has forgotten a vow fixed like Pothini's lac-set stone; in 61 the friend tells her he cannot stay away, forgetting her breast, as fine as Pothini.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One poet hands the same town to both sides of the separation, so Pothini is the woman's measure of the promise in one poem and the friend's measure of the man's longing in the other: the fear and its answer are built from one image."},{"a":"a:1","b":"k:1154","type":"qualifies","note":"Both speak in the woman's voice after a promise not to part is broken by his leaving: the Kural asks if the believer is at fault; Akananuru 1 asks whether he has forgotten (மறந்தனர் கொல்லோ).","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural turns the broken promise into a question of blame, while the Sangam heroine keeps it a question of memory; the poem shows a woman who will not yet say he broke his word, which the couplet's moral question has already assumed.","gr":4},{"a":"a:2","b":"a:12","type":"develops","note":"Both are Kapilar's poems in which the friend presses the hill lord to marry: in 2 she still allows that he may come at night past the watchmen, under the vengai and the moon; in 12 she refuses night meetings outright and says the girl will not live if he comes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence they show the friend's pressure tightening from an ironic permission to a flat refusal, so the varaivu demand is a process with stages, not a single plea."},{"a":"a:3","b":"a:19","type":"same_image","note":"Both men speak to the heart that stands behind them (பின்நின்று): in 3 it drives him from behind toward wealth (பின்நின்று துரக்கும்), in 19 it plans to stay behind with her (பின்நின்று ஒழியச் சூழ்ந்தனை).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The heart at the man's back is a fixed paalai figure that can push either way, toward the road or toward home, so in these poems the heart is not one side of the debate but the place where the debate happens.","gr":4},{"a":"a:4","b":"pn:395","type":"same_image","note":"Both place a spot by the formula 'east of Uranthai' (உறந்தைக் குணாது): in Akananuru 4 the tall hill whose kanthal she smells of, in Purananuru 395 Pidavur, the patron Sathan's town.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same coordinates serve a patron's address and a woman's fragrance, which shows that akam's landscape was drawn on the real map of Chola country that puram poets used for their patrons.","gr":2},{"a":"a:5","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Both answer a man's announced departure for wealth: in Kural 1151 she says 'if you are not going, tell me; tell your quick return to those who will be alive'; in Akananuru 5 she says nothing, and he gives up the journey.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the refusal its sharpest words, and the Sangam poem shows the same refusal working without any: from the man's side, it is the unspoken sign and the withering garland on the child that stop him."},{"a":"a:6","b":"a:226","type":"same_image","note":"Both Paranar poems throw at the husband the same scandal, bathing with his beloved in the Kaveri flood (நீ வெய்யோளொடு), and both set it in Chola names: Thithan's Uranthai and Mathi's Kazhar. In 6 the wife speaks; in 226 the friend refuses him entry.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The repeated phrase and place-names show one marutham scandal reused by its poet from two speakers, so the bath in the Kaveri was a known situation that a hearer recognised from a few words."},{"a":"a:6","b":"pn:352","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the formula of Thithan the great giver (மாவண் தித்தன்) and his paddy-rich Uranthai: Purananuru 352 likens the fine gifts offered for a girl, and refused, to Uranthai; Akananuru 6 sets the husband's riverside affair at Uranthai.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A single praise formula for a Chola chief served the puram poet as the measure of a refused bride-price and the akam poet as the stage of a husband's betrayal, which shows how much shared fame the two kinds of poem drew on."},{"a":"a:7","b":"a:17","type":"develops","note":"Both are Kayamanar's poems of the foster mother after the girl's elopement: in 17 she sits at home picturing the girl's small feet on the waste; in 7 she has followed into the forest and is asking a doe and a hill girl for news.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they give the foster mother's grief two stages, imagination at home and pursuit on the road, and show that the poet built the udanpokku mother as a figure who moves through the story.","gr":3},{"a":"a:9","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"Both speak in the voice of the man returning from work to an evening at home: Kural 1268 wishes the king's war won so that he may feast with his wife at evening; Akananuru 9's work is done and his heart has gone ahead to her at the evening when calves come home.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the evening the man looks forward to, and the Sangam poem fills it from the woman's side, her prayer at the lizard's chirp and the game of covered eyes, with his heart arriving before his chariot."},{"a":"a:10","b":"pn:48","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the Chera port Thondi famous for its shore and its fragrance: Purananuru 48's bard calls it his own town, smelling of neythal, to direct another bard to its king; Akananuru 10's friend likens the girl's beauty to rich Thondi.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend values a girl in the terms a bard used for a king's town, so a woman's worth in akam could be stated in the public currency of puram praise, as a reason for open marriage rather than secret meeting."},{"a":"a:13","b":"pn:205","type":"same_image","note":"Both praise the lord of Kodai (கோடைப் பொருநன் / கோடைப் பொருந) for giving elephants: Purananuru 205 says the bard's troupe never leaves him without elephants and chariots; Akananuru 13 says he gives to suppliants without limit, pausing only to tame a pit-caught elephant.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend in Akananuru 13 takes the very generosity that Purananuru 205 praises and makes it a ceiling: even wealth that large is not worth a winter apart, so puram praise becomes an akam argument against seeking wealth."},{"a":"a:15","b":"a:17","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are mothers after an elopement: the mother in 15 ends by wishing hospitality on the man's town and acknowledging her daughter's resolve; the foster mother in 17 cannot get past the girl's small, tired feet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set together, the two mothers show that the udanpokku lament could close in blessing or in fear, and that the birth mother is the one given the larger acceptance of the daughter's choice."},{"a":"a:18","b":"a:12","type":"develops","note":"Both are Kapilar's friend speaking to the hill lord about his night visits: in 18 she asks him to come by day instead, to the kanthal grove; in 12, by its colophon after day meetings have been stopped, she refuses the night as well and demands marriage.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two poems show the friend's options closing one by one, night refused for day and then day lost to confinement, so the only path left is marriage, the end both poems are built toward.","gr":4},{"a":"a:20","b":"k:1147","type":"qualifies","note":"Both join the town's gossip (கவ்வை, கௌவை) and the mother's response: Kural 1147 says gossip is manure and the mother's words water to the woman's sickness; in Akananuru 20 the mother acts on the gossip and puts the girl under guard.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural reads gossip and the mother as feeding love from within, while the Sangam poem shows their outward effect, confinement, and uses it to press the hidden lover to marry, so the couplet's paradox has a practical role in the akam plot."},{"a":"a:21","b":"a:33","type":"develops","note":"Both are a man mid-road rebuking his heart for wanting to turn back; 21 holds the heart to its own choice and names the aim as making the house glad (மனைநக), 33 concedes never leaving would have been better and names the penalty as others' laughter (பிறர்நகு).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the mid-road speech as a set argument with two stakes, the household's joy if he finishes and public ridicule if he quits, both expressed through laughter."},{"a":"a:33","b":"k:467","type":"develops","note":"Kural 467 says to think before resolving and calls rethinking after resolve a lapse; in Akananuru 33 the man tells his heart it did not think then, and that to halt the work now would make him a thing others laugh at.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem shows the Kural's rule against second thoughts applied by a lover to his own heart, and adds what the maxim omits: the resolved deed costs a wife left behind, and the man accepts that cost rather than the shame of turning back."},{"a":"a:23","b":"a:24","type":"parallel_domain","note":"23 is the wife at the end of the rains, seeing the promised season come without him; 24 is a man in the king's camp at the end of the cold rains, picturing his wife alone through the nights.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set side by side in the anthology's order, the same season is seen from the empty house and from the sleepless camp, so each poem supplies the half of the separation the other cannot see."},{"a":"a:24","b":"pp:5:45-66","type":"same_image","note":"Both describe the king's war camp at midnight with the long-tongued bright bell (நெடுநா ஒண்மணி) and the roaring drum; Mullaippattu's king lies awake thinking of the enemy while his queen waits, and Akananuru 24's soldier speaks of his wife alone at home.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared bell formula shows the midnight camp was a stock scene, and the two poems use it from different ranks: the idyll gives it to the king, the short poem to a man in his army whose wife waits like the idyll's queen."},{"a":"a:24","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1268 has a man serving in the king's war wish the king victory so that he may feast at home with his wife by evening; Akananuru 24 is such a man in the king's camp at the end of the rains, measuring the camp's sleepless night against his wife's lonely one.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet states the soldier-husband's wish as a sentence; the poem gives the night that produces it, the blunted tusks, the drums and the cold rain, so the Kural's two clauses read as the end of a long vigil.","gr":3},{"a":"a:25","b":"a:36","type":"same_image","note":"In 25 the falls on the hill sound like the sweet music of Thithiyan, lord of Pothiyil, victor in battle; in 36 Thithiyan is one of the seven kings crushed by Chezhiyan at Alankanam.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One chief serves in adjacent akam poems as a figure of glory and as one of the defeated, which shows the poets drew on a chief's fame and his fall alike as material for similes."},{"a":"a:36","b":"pn:76","type":"same_image","note":"Both tell Nedunchezhiyan's one-man victory over seven kings at Alankanam in the same phrase, எழுவர் நல்வலம் அடங்க; Purananuru 76 praises it as unheard of, Akananuru 36 uses its victory shout to measure the noise of gossip about a husband's new wife.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The identical phrase in a praise poem and in a wife's complaint shows the battle had a fixed formula that akam poets lifted whole, so a private quarrel could borrow the loudest public event the audience knew."},{"a":"a:35","b":"pn:264","type":"same_image","note":"Both show hero-stones by a stony cairn (பதுக்கை) crowned with peacock feathers (பீலி சூட்டி) for men who died over cattle; Purananuru 264 is the lament for one such fallen man, Akananuru 35 sees such stones only as worship on the road a runaway daughter took.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poem tells who lies under the stone and who will miss him; the akam poem shows how the same stones looked to a mother who saw only their danger, so a hero's memorial became a mark of the perils of the wild road."},{"a":"a:35","b":"pn:126","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 126 praises Malaiyaman Kari as lord of the Pennai country and giver to bards; Akananuru 35 likens a daughter's hair to the fine sand-ripples of the Pennai at Kari's ford.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"A patron's river, praised in puram for the gifts it brings bards, became in akam a stock likeness of a girl's beauty, so a chief's fame lent its landscape to love poetry."},{"a":"a:22","b":"pp:1:227-249","type":"qualifies","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai lists the velan's frenzy-ground (வேலன் தைஇய வெறியயர் களனும்) among Murugan's abodes, with red millet and blood scattered and the god called down (முருகாற்றுப் படுத்த), where seekers gain what they ask; Akananuru 22 describes the same rite and the heroine laughs that it was wasted on a stranger priest, since her lover was the cure.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The devotional idyll and the love poem share the rite's very words, but the akam poem limits its power: the god may come, yet the velan's diagnosis of a girl's sickness is wrong, so the rite's authority stops where secret love begins."},{"a":"a:26","b":"k:1323","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1323 asks whether any heaven equals sulking with one joined to you as water to earth; in Akananuru 26 the wife cannot sulk because her husband melted like ploughed red earth taking the rain.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The couplet makes the earth-and-water bond the reason sulking is sweet, while the poem uses the same image to end the sulk, which shows the likeness can either sustain lovers' quarrel or dissolve it, depending on who is melting.","gr":3},{"a":"a:29","b":"a:39","type":"agrees","note":"In both a man back from the road answers his wife's charge that he forgot her: 29 says his body was held by shame to the work while his heart stayed with her, 39 offers a dream on the road in which he comforted her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two poets give the same answer in two forms, a division of body from heart and a dream, which shows that the reply to 'did you think of us' had to prove an inner presence the separation itself denied."},{"a":"a:42","b":"k:1192","type":"same_image","note":"Both liken the beloved's coming to rain on those who live by it: the Kural states it as a rule of lovers' grace, Akananuru 42 makes it a drought broken at dawn over a whole countryside.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's two-line rain simile is the compressed form of a scene the Sangam poet builds at length, so the couplet's image carries the whole drought-and-tank picture behind it.","gr":3},{"a":"a:43","b":"a:53","type":"tension","note":"In 43 the man calls leaving a companion for wealth folly (மடமை) and pities those who do it; in 53 the heroine says her man left because his heart could not refuse the needy, a virtue she still reads as love of wealth over her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the paalai debate from both sides: the same departure is folly to one man and an unrefusable duty of giving to another, and the woman left behind refuses to call either grace.","gr":4},{"a":"a:44","b":"k:1268","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1268 says let the king fight and win, and let us feast at home in the evening; Akananuru 44 opens with the king's work done and the tribute paid, and bids the chariot race home to sleep on her breast.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Kural's couplet is the akam homecoming formula of the returning warrior reduced to its two clauses, the king's victory first and the household's evening second.","gr":4},{"a":"a:44","b":"a:186","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 44 records Pazhaiyan's fall in a war of many chiefs; Akananuru 186 uses Pazhaiyan's town Por on the Kaveri as a likeness for a bangle that will not be broken.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same chief appears in one akam poem as a fallen man and in another as the owner of an unbreakable town, so akam poets drew on a chief's fame both before and after his fall.","gr":3},{"a":"a:45","b":"a:145","type":"same_image","note":"Both use Anni's cutting of Thithiyan's punnai at Kurukkai: 45 measures the gossip by the bards' clamour that day, 145 wishes on the foster mother's hand the fate of that felled tree.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One puram event served akam poets in two opposite ways, as a measure of noise and as a curse of destruction, which shows these battles were a shared stock of reference the audience was expected to know.","gr":4},{"a":"a:46","b":"k:1311","type":"agrees","note":"Both have the wife's side refuse the husband who has been with other women: the Kural's woman will not touch the chest other women's eyes have fed on, the friend of 46 refuses him the door and says 'go, who stops you'.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural compresses into one refusal what the Sangam poem makes a situation with a hidden buffalo image, a report of a new marriage and a mocking release, so the couplet is the end point of a whole marutham scene.","gr":7},{"a":"a:46","b":"a:186","type":"same_image","note":"Both liken a woman's bangle to a rich king's town (Chezhiyan's Allur in 46, Pazhaiyan's Por in 186), one from the wife's side and one from the courtesan's.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The town-like bangle is a shared formula that both rivals in the marutham triangle could use, so the formula marks a woman's claim on the man, not which side she is on.","gr":2},{"a":"a:48","b":"a:52","type":"same_image","note":"In both a cry of 'tiger' on the vengai slope brings men running: in 48 the girls' cry brings the young hunter who becomes the lover, in 52 a hill girl's cry for flowers brings the hill men with bows.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show the vengai-plucking cry as a known custom of the hill, and 48 turns that public alarm into the private occasion of a first meeting that 52's heroine now wants kept secret.","gr":4},{"a":"a:51","b":"k:1155","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1155 says if he leaves, union becomes hard (நீங்கின் அரிதால் புணர்வு); in 51 the man tells his heart that wealth is joined only by parting, and chooses to stay.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same word புணர்வு carries both sides of one exchange: the Kural's woman counts what parting costs union, and the Sangam man counts what union costs wealth.","gr":5},{"a":"a:52","b":"k:1138","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1138 says love breaks through secrecy into the public square whatever the lover's restraint; in 52 the heroine, her pallor already visible, still forbids her friend to name it even at the cost of her life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's rule that love cannot be hidden is qualified by the Sangam heroine's will: the body discloses it, but the naming is still hers to refuse.","gr":4},{"a":"a:53","b":"k:223","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 223 counts not saying 'I have nothing' and giving as the mark of the well-born; in 53 that very inability to refuse the needy is what drives the man away from the heroine.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's praise of giving has a cost the Kural does not count: in akam the generous man's virtue is the cause of the woman's abandonment.","gr":3},{"a":"a:54","b":"pn:173","type":"same_image","note":"Pannan of Sirukudi is praised in Purananuru 173 as the healer of hunger whose house feeds crowds; in Akananuru 54 his village's gooseberry gives the simile and he is called one who lives not for himself but for others.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"An akam poet could fold a puram patron's reputation into one epithet inside a love poem, which shows how well-known Pannan's generosity was.","gr":4},{"a":"a:54","b":"pn:182","type":"agrees","note":"Purananuru 182 says the world stands because there are those who strive not for themselves but for others (தமக்கென முயலா ... பிறர்க்கென முயலுநர்); Akananuru 54 calls Pannan தனக்கென வாழாப் பிறர்க்கு உரியாளன்.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The kaanchi poem's general ideal and the akam poem's praise of one patron use the same contrast of self and others, so the maxim was already a phrase of praise for real patrons.","gr":4},{"a":"a:55","b":"pn:65","type":"same_image","note":"Both tell of Cheralathan, ashamed of his back wound from the fight with Karikalan, sitting facing north to die; Purananuru 65 laments the king, Akananuru 55 turns to the noble men who died with him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam poem keeps a detail the puram lament leaves out, the companions who followed the king into death, and uses it to measure a mother's wish that her life would follow her daughter.","gr":5},{"a":"a:55","b":"a:246","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the battle at Venni: in 246 the uproar of Karikalan's victory measures the town's gossip, in 55 the defeated Chera's death measures a mother's grief.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One battle gave akam poets images from both sides, the victor's clamour for gossip and the loser's death for grief, so a puram event's meaning in akam depends on whose side the image is taken from.","gr":2},{"a":"a:57","b":"a:149","type":"same_image","note":"Both name Chezhiyan's attack on Musiri, the Chera port: 149 has him seize an image there, 57 compares the wife's suffering to the wounded in that clamorous siege.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two separate paalai poems witness the same Pandya assault on Musiri, which shows a single war entering the akam repertoire as a ready image of suffering and plunder.","gr":3},{"a":"a:61","b":"pn:93","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 93 describes the rite of cutting with a sword the body of a king who died of sickness, so that he goes where battle-slain warriors go; Akananuru 61 opens with the saying behind that rite (blessed are those who die at others' hands, not seized by Death) as the reason a man leaves home.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the warrior's creed about a good death working at two scales: as a royal funeral rite in puram, and in akam as a proverb that sends an ordinary man away from his wife to strive.","gr":5},{"a":"a:61","b":"a:209","type":"same_image","note":"Both are the friend's comfort in paalai: in 61 even winning Pulli's Venkatam will not keep him, in 209 even going beyond Pulli's Venkatam he will not forget; both close by likening the heroine's beauty to a famous place (Pothini, the Kolli image).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Pulli's Venkatam served the paalai poets as the fixed northern limit of a man's absence, so different poets could promise his return by measuring it against the same frontier and the same kind of famous-place likeness."},{"a":"a:62","b":"a:209","type":"same_image","note":"Paranar in 62 and Kalladanar in 209 both liken the heroine to the image on Kolli that a god made: one in the lover's own memory after a failed tryst, the other in the friend's comfort during his absence.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kolli image was a shared likeness for a beauty that cannot be harmed or taken, used by a kurinji lover and a paalai friend alike, so the legend of the hill carried a fixed sense of untouchable loveliness."},{"a":"a:64","b":"a:34","type":"develops","note":"Both are the returning man's words to his charioteer in mullai, and both set a pair of beasts going together (the stag and doe in 34, the bull and heifer in 64) beside his haste; 64 adds the cows' evening bells, which the wife hears alone.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The pairing shows the iraicchi tied to the speech situation rather than to one animal, and 64 develops it by making the herd's homecoming the very sound that hurts the woman, so the same image both promises his return and measures her waiting.","gr":2},{"a":"a:65","b":"pn:2","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 2 praises Udhiyan Cheralathan for giving the great feast without limit; Akananuru 65 likens the girls' joy at eloping to that of the bards who went singing to Udhiyan Cheral.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The king's puram fame as a feeder of bards had become a proverbial measure of gladness, so an akam friend could express release from a watchful mother by the bards' delight at his court."},{"a":"a:66","b":"k:62","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 62 says evil will not touch for seven births those who have children free of blame; Akananuru 66 quotes a saying of the same kind (blameless sons bring fame here and a stainless next world) and finds it true when a toddler stops his father from marrying another wife.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows a maxim about a child's merit protecting the parent being tested in a household quarrel, where its proof is not the next world but a husband held at home, which shifts the rule from the father's destiny to the mother's marriage."},{"a":"a:67","b":"a:131","type":"same_image","note":"Lines 10 and 11 of 67 repeat lines 11 and 12 of 131 word for word (the hero-stones inscribed with names, crowned with peacock feathers, like a battle front with planted spears); in 67 the woman knows the road by report, in 131 the man refuses to take her along it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The repeated couplet shows the hero-stone road as a fixed formula shared by poets, and the two poems turn it to opposite ends: hearsay that terrifies the woman left at home, and the man's reason for not taking her."},{"a":"a:69","b":"pn:175","type":"same_image","note":"Both use nearly the same words for the Moriyar, whose chariots under sky-touching umbrellas cut a path through the rock with their wheels: in 69 that rock marks how far the man has gone, in Purananuru 175 the cutting's circle is a likeness for a patron's constancy.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The northern invaders' cut through the hills was a shared memory that akam used as a mark of distance and puram as an image of something fixed and open to all, showing one historical feature serving both kinds of poem.","gr":2},{"a":"a:69","b":"k:228","type":"develops","note":"Kural 228 asks whether hoarders do not know the joy of giving; Akananuru 69 says the man left for wealth wanting exactly that joy (ஈதல் இன்பம் வெஃகி), and the friend draws from it that he will not delay.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows the Kural's joy of giving as the stated motive of the akam journey for wealth, so the separation the paalai poems lament is grounded in a virtue, and the friend can use that virtue as a promise of his return."},{"a":"a:70","b":"pn:378","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 70 likens the town's silence after the wedding to the banyan Rama hushed at Kodi for his secret counsel; Purananuru 378 likens a bard's kin, putting on jewels the wrong way, to the monkeys who found Sita's jewels on the ground.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"These are two early Tamil uses of the Rama story, both of minor episodes and both as similes, one akam and one puram, which shows the story was familiar enough to be drawn on for a passing likeness rather than told."},{"a":"a:75","b":"k:247","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 247 sets wealth and grace side by side as what this world and the next require; the man in Akananuru 75 quotes the saying that effort for wealth, not grace, is a man's task and refuses it, since nothing in this world can give back what parting wastes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the Kural's balance of wealth and grace becomes a choice in a marriage, and the akam man qualifies the Kural's grant of this world to wealth by naming one thing in this world that wealth cannot restore."},{"a":"a:76","b":"a:45","type":"develops","note":"In 45 the wife likens herself to Aathi Manthi, distraught after losing her husband; in 76 the courtesan tells the same legend and casts herself as the Kaveri that held the dancer, leaving the wife as Aathi Manthi.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same legend serves the wife's lament and the courtesan's boast, which shows the akam poets reading a story from either side of a quarrel, and that the river's part could be claimed with pride."},{"a":"a:77","b":"pn:172","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 172 praises Pittan, lord of the hard country, for his spear that wins hard battles and his great giving; Akananuru 77 names Pittan, Chera's warrior with unending toddy, and likens the woman's tearful eyes to the spear he raised against hostile kings.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows a chief's puram praise (his spear and his gifts) reused in akam, where his famous spear becomes the measure of the pain her eyes will cause, so the poet trusts listeners to know the patron's weapon.","gr":2},{"a":"a:78","b":"pn:109","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 109 is Kapilar's boast during the siege of Paari's Parambu that the three kings cannot take it by force; Akananuru 78 recalls Paari with Kapilar beside him, holding out for years and driving away the kings with elephants.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam poem treats as settled fame what the puram poem speaks in the middle of the siege, so Paari's resistance with Kapilar had become a reference point another poet could use to praise a girl's fragrance.","gr":7},{"a":"a:83","b":"a:61","type":"same_image","note":"Both name Pulli's Venkatam: in 61 the friend says the man would not stay even for Venkatam with its festivals; in 83 the man says her virtues followed him past Venkatam regardless of distance.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Pulli's Venkatam served akam poets as the fixed far edge of the wealth journey, and they turned it both ways: a prize too small to hold the man, and a distance too short to stop the woman's pull.","gr":7},{"a":"a:85","b":"a:83","type":"same_image","note":"Venkatam is Thiraiyan's hill of the victorious spear in 85 and lies in the land of Pulli, chief of the unlettered young men, in 83.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same frontier hill is credited to two different lords by poets of one anthology, so Venkatam in akam is a border claimed by more than one chief, not a fixed realm."},{"a":"a:84","b":"a:44","type":"qualifies","note":"In 44 the enemies pay their tribute and the man drives home; in 84 the king refuses the tribute offered and the man stays in the camp through the promised rains.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The mullai homecoming hangs on one puram act, the king's acceptance of tribute: taken, it frees the soldier, refused in anger, it holds him, so the woman's waiting is timed by a royal decision she never sees."},{"a":"a:89","b":"a:99","type":"tension","note":"The foster mother in 89 sees the elopement road as a waste of marauders, slaughtered guards and failed rains; the lover in 99 calls the same kind of forest good and lovely, a temple town strewn with flowers.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The anthology gives the elopement road two incompatible descriptions from the two people with a stake in it, so the paalai landscape is a speaker's argument, made terrible by the one who loses the girl and beautiful by the one who takes her."},{"a":"a:91","b":"pn:343","type":"same_image","note":"Kuttuvan's land is the measure of wealth in both: in Purananuru 343 a father refuses wealth like Kuttuvan's Musiri as bride-price; in Akananuru 91 the man would not stay for Kuttuvan's Kudanadu, forgetting his wife.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kuttuvan's riches stood in both books as the outer limit of wealth, and both poems use it to say a woman outweighs it: in puram the father refuses it for his daughter, in akam the husband refuses it for his wife."},{"a":"a:93","b":"pn:39","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 39 praises the Chola assembly at Uraiyur where justice stands unfailing; Akananuru 93 compares the man's hard-won treasure to the virtue-filled assembly of the Cholas at Uraiyur.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The justice of Uraiyur's assembly, a praise theme in puram, was famous enough to serve an akam poet as a simile for something precious, so the court's moral fame had become a byword for worth itself."},{"a":"a:93","b":"k:524","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 524 says the fruit of gaining wealth is to be surrounded by kin; the man in 93 gives exactly this reason (ending the kin's ruin, feeding relatives, making strangers kin) for the journey that took him from his wife.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's purpose for wealth is the very reason the paalai hero gives for leaving, so the akam poem shows what the maxim costs in separation, and puts the return to the wife, not the kin, at the end."},{"a":"a:95","b":"k:1147","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1147 the woman says the town's gossip manures her love-sickness and her mother's words water it; in Akananuru 95 the mother answers the gossiping women in her daughter's defence, and that trust is what the girl cannot face.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's couplet assumes a scolding mother whose words feed the love; the Sangam scene shows the harder case, where the mother's kindness against the town's gossip is what leaves the girl helpless, so the same two pressures can work through trust as well as blame."},{"a":"a:98","b":"pp:1:227-249","type":"tension","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai lists the velan's veriyattu ground (வேலன் தைஇய வெறியயர் களனும்) among the true places where Murugan comes to those who seek him; in Akananuru 98 the same rite, with kadambu, palm leaf and the god's great name praised, is a misreading of love-sickness by deluded women.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The same veriyattu rite is a real dwelling of the god in the devotional idyll and a comic error in the love poem, so the Sangam akam convention could treat Murugan's possession as a cover story without denying the god, who here might even cure her."},{"a":"a:101","b":"k:210","type":"tension","note":"Kural 210 says that ruin is rare for one who does not stray into evil; the heroine of 101 quotes the same rule as an old proverb (நன்றுசெய் மருங்கில் தீதுஇல்) and asks whether it has turned false, since she did nothing hateful and is still left. On her reading the rule has failed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows that the Kural's assurance was already an old proverb in the Sangam age, and that akam poetry was where it was tested: a blameless woman's abandonment is the counter-case the maxim does not answer.","gr":2},{"a":"a:105","b":"pn:158","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 158 names Ezhini among the seven dead givers, with his horse and sharp spear; Akananuru 105 names Ezhini of many spears with his horse, whose bowmen drive off cattle on the daughter's road. That both mean the same chief is likely but not certain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The chief keeps the same epithets of horse and spear in both books, but the bard remembers him as a lost giver while the mother sees only his raiders on her daughter's road, so a patron's fame looked different from the path his men made dangerous."},{"a":"a:106","b":"a:116","type":"same_image","note":"Both marutham poems bring in Chezhiyan's wars: in 106 the courtesan wishes the wife would beat her own belly like the drum his bard beats at each victory; in 116 the friend says the gossip about the hero's river-play is louder than the shout at Koodal when Chezhiyan routed two kings.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set side by side they show the Pandya's victories serving both parties to one marutham quarrel, the courtesan's curse and the wife's friend's reproach, so royal war was the common measure of household noise and anger whoever spoke."},{"a":"a:109","b":"k:551","type":"develops","note":"Kural 551 judges the king who harasses his people and does wrong to be crueller than murderers; Akananuru 109 sets the murderers' road, with cairns over men shot though they carried nothing, in hills ruled by a king without aram (அறன்இல் வேந்தன்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural ranks the bad king against killers in the abstract, while the akam poet already draws the lawless realm and the murderous road as one landscape, so a lover's journey becomes the evidence of what a king without aram costs those who travel his land."},{"a":"a:112","b":"k:657","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 657 says the worthy prefer utter poverty to wealth gained under blame; the friend in Akananuru 112 says the worthy, however deep in love, do not want a pleasure that comes with blame (சான்றோர் பழியொடு வரூஉம் இன்பம் வெஃகார்), and so the hero should marry.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same rule of the worthy, refusing any gain that brings blame, governs wealth in the Kural and love in the Akananuru, and the friend turns it from a counsel of going without into an argument for marriage, which removes the blame without removing the pleasure.","gr":4},{"a":"a:114","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"Same situation: a man held by the king's war who longs to be home at evening. Kural 1268 says let the king fight and win, and we will hold the evening feast with the household; in Akananuru 114 the war is done and the hero urges his charioteer to reach his wife before the evening's grief.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's two clauses, victory and then the evening at home, become in the poem a race against dusk, and the poem adds what the couplet leaves out: evening is the hour when the waiting wife suffers most, so his haste is to end her sorrow as much as to begin his feast."},{"a":"a:115","b":"pn:233","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 233 is a bard's cry at the dawn news of Evvi's wounds, wishing it false; Akananuru 115 recalls the battle where Evvi fell and, on our reading, the bards breaking off and laying down the curved necks of their harps in worship.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The puram poem gives the bards' words at Evvi's fall and the akam poem their gesture, so together they show that his death was mourned with a known bardic rite that a love poet could cite as an image for seed pods on a road.","gr":4},{"a":"a:116","b":"pn:76","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 76 praises Chezhiyan, who alone defeated the seven who joined against him; Akananuru 116 recalls Chezhiyan at Koodal routing two great kings who rose together. The battles may not be the same.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both poets make the Pandya's fame the defeat of an alliance, seven kings in the puram praise and two great kings in Paranar's simile, so one king against a coalition was the shape his fame took, whichever battle is meant.","gr":2},{"a":"a:117","b":"a:105","type":"develops","note":"Both are paalai laments over a daughter who eloped: in 105 the mother recalls the girl refusing milk from her nurses and caring nothing for the house's wealth; in 117 the foster mother recalls the girl refusing to let her wash her hair, which the lover now dresses.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two laments divide the girl's upbringing between the women who raised her, the mother grieving through food and wealth and the foster mother through the hair she tended, and each measures the loss by a care the lover now takes over."},{"a":"a:118","b":"a:112","type":"develops","note":"In 118 the friend closes both trysts, fearing gossip by day and the tiger by night; in 112 she meets him at night, says he did wrong to come through the tiger's grove, and gives the moral ground for marriage.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Taken together they show the friend's case for marriage in two steps: 118 makes every meeting impossible without naming marriage, and 112 names it and supplies the reason a worthy man must accept, that the worthy want no pleasure that brings blame."},{"a":"a:125","b":"pn:66","type":"qualifies","note":"Both turn on Karikalan's victories: Purananuru 66 praises the king but sets above him the defeated king at Venni who, ashamed of a wound in the back, starved himself to death; Akananuru 125 likens the fleeing north wind to the nine kings who abandoned their umbrellas on the Vaakai field.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the warrior code from both sides: the loser who dies of shame earns more praise than the victor, while the losers who run are proverbial for disgrace, so an akam poet could call a wind cowardly by naming Karikalan's battle."},{"a":"a:126","b":"a:145","type":"same_image","note":"Both tell of Anni and Thithiyan's punnai tree: 145 has Anni felling the old punnai at the Kurukkai field, 126 has Anni, deaf to Evvi's counsel, coveting the punnai and dying in the fight.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two poems give two halves of one story, the felling of the tree in 145 and the rejected counsel and death in 126, and each poet used only the half his speaker needed: a curse on the man who took a daughter, a warning to a stubborn heart.","gr":2},{"a":"a:127","b":"a:347","type":"same_image","note":"Both name Cheralathan who drove off the sea-foe and cut the kadamba: in 127 his buried tribute is the measure of wealth the lover would refuse, in 347 the drum made after that victory is the measure of the town's loud gossip.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One king's campaign gave akam poets two different scales, its plunder for riches and its victory drum for noise, which shows how a puram deed was broken into separate stock comparisons."},{"a":"a:130","b":"k:1084","type":"develops","note":"Both are the man's words after first seeing the girl, about her eyes: the Kural says her look eats the life of those who see it; the Akananuru man tells the friends who rebuke him that they would not rebuke him if they had seen her look.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the power of the look as a general truth, while the Akananuru puts it into a defence before the man's companions, showing that the couplet's claim first served as the hero's answer to rebuke (கழற்றெதிர்மறை)."},{"a":"a:131","b":"pn:264","type":"same_image","note":"Both describe the hero-stone of a man killed recovering cattle, crowned with peacock feather and inscribed with his name (பீலி சூட்டி, பெயர் பொறித்து); 264 mourns the dead man, 131 lines the road with such stones.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Purananuru 264 shows one stone as a single loss to the bards who depended on the man, and Akananuru 131 shows rows of them as the reason a lover will not travel, so the same memorial carried grief in puram and deterrence in akam.","gr":3},{"a":"a:134","b":"a:4","type":"develops","note":"In both a returning man in the rains spares mating creatures: in 4 he has tied his chariot bells so the bees paired in the flowers are not startled, in 134 he tells the charioteer to drive gently lest the stag and doe in union hear the chariot.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Placed side by side the two poems show a fixed mullai motif, the returning husband's gentleness toward paired creatures, that a poet could vary from bees to deer and from the man's own act to his order to the charioteer."},{"a":"a:135","b":"a:45","type":"same_image","note":"Both have the waiting girl say her pallor is like the peerkku flower (பீர்) and her bewilderment like Aathi Manthi's (பேதுற்று), alongside a siege simile for her state.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The repetition of three linked images in two poems by different poets shows that pallor, Aathi Manthi and a besieged town formed a ready set for the abandoned girl's lament.","gr":3},{"a":"a:135","b":"a:365","type":"same_image","note":"Kazhuvul's Kamur appears in both: in 135 it is the town besieged by fourteen Velir, the image of her shaken heart; in 365 it is the place whose fragrant vengai the girl's hair recalls to the travelling man.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same chieftain's town served one poet as a picture of ruin and another as a picture of fragrance, so akam poets drew on a place's whole fame, its war and its groves, and not on a single fixed meaning."},{"a":"a:139","b":"a:4","type":"qualifies","note":"Both describe the rains that mark the promised return: in 4 the friend points to the rains to assure the girl he is coming; in 139 the rains are already over and he has not come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence the friend's argument from the season in 4 meets its test in 139, where the same signs, unanswered, turn from a promise into proof of delay."},{"a":"a:140","b":"a:130","type":"develops","note":"Both are neythal poems in which the man answers his companion about a girl's eyes; in 130 she stands on Korkai's pearl-strewn harbour, in 140 she is a salt-merchant's daughter crying her wares in the street.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same thurai is given to a girl of the rich harbour and to a salt-seller's daughter, and in 140 the man even compares himself to her father's ox, so the kalavu situation was open to heroines of trading families and drew its images from their work."},{"a":"a:148","b":"a:208","type":"same_image","note":"Both are Paranar's and both tell of Aay Eyinan falling to Minjili: in 208 the birds gather to shade the fallen man from the sun; in 148 the owl, ashamed, does not go out by day to see him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the legend had a second bird in it: the night owl that could not join the daylight shading, and the poet uses that one bird's shame as a reason for lovers to meet by night.","gr":2},{"a":"a:142","b":"a:181","type":"qualifies","note":"In 142 the man Minjili killed at Nannan's Paazhi is Athikan, 'a protection to the birds' (புள்ளிற்கு ஏமம்); in 181 it is Aay Eyinan who falls to Minjili and a flock of birds hides the sun over him.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The epithet in 142 is explained by the scene in 181, but the name differs, so the bird-shade legend either belonged to one hero known by two names or moved between heroes of the Paazhi wars.","gr":2},{"a":"a:145","b":"a:45","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the felling of the old punnai at Kurukkai in the war of Anni and Thithiyan: in 45 the gossip is louder than the bards' cheers that day; in 145 the foster mother curses her own hand to be cut down like that tree.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One puram event served the akam poets as a measure of two different things, public noise in one poem and deserved ruin in the other, so the war's fame had become a store of ready comparisons.","gr":4},{"a":"a:143","b":"pn:170","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 170 praises Pittan as soft to those who come in hope (நசைவர்க்கு மென்மை) and iron to enemies; Akananuru 143 calls him the giver of fine vessels to those who live by hope (நசையின் வாழ்நர்க்கு) and sets the woman's tears in a pool on his hill.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The akam poet takes from Pittan's fame the same formula of giving to the hopeful that the praise poem uses, which shows a patron's epithet passing whole between the two kinds of poetry."},{"a":"a:149","b":"pn:343","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In Purananuru 343 a father refuses suitors even if they bow and offer wealth (விழுப்பொருள்) like Muziri's, where ships bring gold for pepper; in Akananuru 149 the man refuses precious wealth (விழுப்பொருள்) even if easily won, beside Muziri where Yavana ships bring gold and take pepper.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Muziri's sea trade was the stock measure of the wealth a woman outweighs, used by the war poem for a father keeping his daughter and by the love poem for a husband keeping his wife.","gr":4},{"a":"a:152","b":"pn:134","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 152 names Aay as the one who gives to all who come, whether able or not (வல்லினும் வல்லார் ஆயினும்); Purananuru 134 says Aay is no trader in virtue who gives in this life for a return in the next.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they make Aay's giving unconditional on both sides, asking nothing of the receiver's skill and nothing of the next world, which neither poem says alone."},{"a":"a:151","b":"k:1047","type":"agrees","note":"The man's maxim quoted in 151 is that the poor cannot support their dependants or laugh among their kin; Kural 1047 says poverty makes a man a stranger even to the mother who bore him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states as a rule the claim the akam hero uses to justify leaving, and the poem shows what the rule costs when it is acted on: the wife says it leaves no room for grace.","gr":2},{"a":"a:155","b":"k:760","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 760 says that for those who make bright wealth the other two (virtue and pleasure) come easily together; in Akananuru 155 the man says two things come from wealth, a life free of wrong and a wealth that never begs, and leaves.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The hero's version of the Kural's claim drops pleasure from the pair, and the poem makes that omission visible: the household pleasure is exactly what his errand for wealth suspends."},{"a":"a:143","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Both answer the man's announced departure: in Kural 1151 the reply (the friend's or the woman's) is a sharp refusal, tell your quick return to those who will survive; in Akananuru 143 the friend only reports the woman's tears at his 'small word', and the colophon says he stayed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same thurai had two answers, the Kural's barbed argument and the Sangam friend's report of tears, and the poem shows the second one succeeding, which the couplet's general statement cannot show."},{"a":"a:149","b":"a:155","type":"tension","note":"In 149 the man says even easily won wealth is not worth her tears and stays; in 155 the man holds that virtue and independence both come only from wealth, and goes. On the plain reading they are incompatible verdicts on whether wealth justifies the parting.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The anthology sets the paalai argument on both sides in the men's own voices, so the case for going and the case for staying are each given their strongest form."},{"a":"a:153","b":"a:145","type":"develops","note":"Both are a foster mother's lament after the girl has eloped across the desert; 153 blames the girl's trust in a hard man and fears for her soft feet, 145 turns the blame on the speaker's own hand that once beat her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they give the two directions of the mother's grief in this thurai, outward to the girl's heedlessness and inward to her own harshness, both ending on the girl's tender body set against the road."},{"a":"a:154","b":"a:34","type":"same_image","note":"Both are the homecoming man's words to his charioteer, and both put a stag with twisted horns (திரிமருப்பு இரலை, இருதிரி மருப்பின் இரலை) by clear water (தெள்அறல்) caring for or resting with his mate, with the word ஏமுற.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Shared phrases across two poets show the stag with his mate is a fixed formula of this thurai, and in 34 ஏமுற is the man's own heart's ease while in 154 it is the beasts', so the formula passes the same rest from beast to man."},{"a":"a:161","b":"k:1157","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1157 has the woman say her slipping bangles already announce that the lord of the shore has left; in Akananuru 161 the friend tells the man the girl's tears show she has already understood his plan to go.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem turns the couplet's body-as-herald into an argument: the friend uses the tears that announce his going as the reason he should not go, so the sign the Kural records becomes a plea."},{"a":"a:161","b":"a:179","type":"develops","note":"Both are the friend speaking to a man about to leave for the waste; 161 argues from the girl's tears, which already know, 179 from the dangers of the road and the girl's face.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show two tactics in the same office of dissuasion: one presents the departure as already a wound, the other as a risk set against a beauty left alone."},{"a":"a:162","b":"a:168","type":"qualifies","note":"In 162 the man tells his heart of the midnight storm and the guarded house he braved to meet her; in 168 the friend tells such a man that the night path, with its tiger and darkness, must be given up.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The danger the lover counts as proof of his love is the very thing the friend uses to end the night meetings and press for marriage, so the same night risk carries opposite verdicts by speaker."},{"a":"a:163","b":"a:170","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both women, left alone, send a non-human messenger to the absent man: in 163 the cold north wind, in 170 the crab of the backwater.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The messenger is drawn from the poem's own season and land, the wind of the rains' end in one and the shore crab in the other, so even the convention of the go-between is fitted to its landscape."},{"a":"a:164","b":"a:174","type":"develops","note":"Both are a man in the king's war camp telling his heart about his wife in the rains; in 164 he waits for the king to release him, in 174 he has turned away from further battle and she does not yet know he is coming.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they mark two moments of the same camp poem: the season of return arriving while duty holds him, and the moment he is free but her grief still runs ahead of the news."},{"a":"a:166","b":"a:176","type":"qualifies","note":"In 166 the other woman mocks the husband's false oath so the wife's people hear; in 176 the wife's friend describes the other woman's public rage and refuses him the door.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Heard from both sides of the quarrel, the affair is fought through what the town hears: each woman's party uses the other's public display as its weapon against the husband."},{"a":"a:167","b":"a:169","type":"develops","note":"In 167 the man, before leaving, pictures the ruined village where he would sleep and gives up the journey; in 169 a man who has already crossed many wastes pictures his wife grieving at evening.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the road imagined from both ends of the decision: before setting out it is a desolation that keeps him home, after setting out it is the distance that makes her grief visible to him."},{"a":"a:168","b":"pn:2","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 168 compares the roar of the waterfalls to the kitchen of Udhiyan, bound to the duty of giving; Purananuru 2 praises the Chera king who gave great food without limit to the armies of the war.","grade":"forced","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The akam poet can borrow the noise of Udhiyan's kitchen as a simile because his feeding was proverbial in puram praise, so a king's fame for food had become a stock measure of uproar.","gr":4},{"a":"a:172","b":"a:178","type":"develops","note":"In 172 the friend reproaches the hill-man for lovelessness to press him to marry; in 178 marriage is settled and the friend blesses the union, quoting his own words of devotion.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence the friend's two speeches show the pressure for marriage and its answer, and her blessing in 178 quotes the very love she doubted in 172."},{"a":"a:173","b":"k:247","type":"develops","note":"Kural 247 states that this world is not for those without wealth; in Akananuru 173 the man says that dharma and helping kin in ruin are not for those who sit at ease, and leaves to earn.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem shows the Kural's premise about wealth working inside a marriage: it becomes the husband's reason for leaving and the friend's ground for comfort, and it shows the cost the maxim omits."},{"a":"a:173","b":"pn:151","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 173 places the man's road on the gold-bearing slopes of Nannan of the swift chariot; Purananuru 151 names a patron as kin of Nannan of the golden chariot, whose hill was shut against poets.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both books know Nannan by his chariot and his hill, but the puram poem remembers his line for a door shut against singers while the akam poem uses his range only for its gold and its danger, so the same name carries blame in one genre and mere geography in the other.","gr":2},{"a":"a:173","b":"a:177","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are the friend assuring the woman that the absent man will return; in 173 he will return because of his principle and his promise, in 177 because he will remember her body.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Side by side they show two grounds the friend can give for hope, his word and his desire, so the same office of comfort can rest on duty or on longing.","gr":2},{"a":"a:175","b":"pn:23","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 175 compares the lightning to the spear Chezhiyan raised after he won the battle at Alangaanam (ஆலங் கானத்து அமர்கடந்து); Purananuru 23 praises Nedunchezhiyan with the same phrase.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The identical phrase shows the victory at Alangaanam was already a formula, so an akam poet could use a celebrated battle to light up a love poem's rain clouds."},{"a":"a:175","b":"k:1154","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1154 asks whether those who trusted the assuring word are at fault when the one who said 'do not fear' leaves; in Akananuru 175 the woman recalls his vows, the hand on her wrist and the promised season, which has now come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem supplies the concrete pledge the couplet only names (a vow, a grasped wrist, a named season) and fixes the moment of testing at the arrival of the promised rains."},{"a":"a:177","b":"pn:173","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 177 places a fertile mango grove on the Kaveri's north bank in the land of Pannan of the warrior anklets; Purananuru 173 praises Pannan of Sirukudi as the healer of hunger whose house rings with feeding.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"If this is the same Pannan, akam uses the puram patron's land as a byword for fertile beauty, so a patron's renown for feeding becomes a measure of a woman's beauty.","gr":2},{"a":"a:179","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1151, which Parimelazhagar gives to the friend, refuses to hear of a quick return; in Akananuru 179 the friend asks the departing man whether he can bear to live away while she is left alone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet compresses the friend's dissuasion into a threat of death, and the poem shows the fuller form of the same speech, arguing from the dangers of the road and the woman's beauty."},{"a":"a:179","b":"pn:151","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 179 line 1 and Purananuru 151 line 2 are the same line (விண்தோய் சிமைய விறல்வரைக் கவாஅன்); in puram it is the slope of a patron's hill, in akam the slope of the dangerous waste.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The repeated line shows the poets shared formulas across akam and puram, and that the setting decides whether the same sky-touching slope means a patron's wealth or a lover's danger.","gr":2},{"a":"a:181","b":"a:208","type":"same_image","note":"Both Paranar poems recall Aay Eyinan falling to Minjili with birds over the field: in 208 the birds shade the dead hero from the sun with their wings, in 181 a great flock dances thick in the sky and hides the sun's heat as he falls.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read with 208, the bird flock in 181 is the known sign of Eyinan's death, the birds' honour to a fallen giver, so Paranar can use that public story as a mere measure of how thickly the Kaveri heaps its sand."},{"a":"a:196","b":"a:262","type":"develops","note":"262 tells the story of Anni Minjili, who refused food and white clothes after the Kosar blinded her father and was satisfied only when Thithiyan killed them; 196 names her in one line as the measure of the rival's gait.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"262 shows Anni Minjili's revenge as the lover's own surge of joy, while 196 turns the same woman into the wife's bitter picture of a triumphant rival, so one puram story served akam poets for opposite feelings."},{"a":"a:186","b":"a:326","type":"same_image","note":"Both Paranar marutham poems use Pazhaiyan, the Chola's warrior, lord of Por on the Kaveri: in 186 his town is the image of bangles never broken, in 326 his thrown spear is the image of a girl's eyes that never miss.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show Paranar drawing on one patron's fame for women's power in the marutham quarrel, the fort for what holds and the spear for what strikes, so Pazhaiyan's name carried a known strength the audience could read at once."},{"a":"a:198","b":"k:1081","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1081 is the man's first sight: goddess, peahen or woman, his heart reels; in 198 he has already held her and still decides she is not a woman but the dread maiden of a mountain spring, having likened her coming to a peahen in cloud.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet places the doubt at first sight and the commentators settle it in favour of a woman, but the poem keeps the same three terms after the union and settles them the other way, so the awe of the anangu was not only a first impression."},{"a":"a:182","b":"a:148","type":"qualifies","note":"Same occasion reversed: in 182 Kapilar's friend tells the man who comes at night to come by day, for fear of the storm; in 148 Paranar's friend tells the man who comes by day to come at evening, for the girl's suffering through the long day.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set side by side the two colophons show that the friend's ruling on when to meet is no fixed rule of kalavu but a reply to the present danger, and each poem names its own reason, the storm-struck night in one and the unbearable day in the other."},{"a":"a:195","b":"a:189","type":"develops","note":"Both are Kayamanar's poems of the mothers left after an elopement: in 189 the foster mother wanders the house and says the town has lost its treasure; in 195 the birth mother claims her years of care and asks the diviner which house the pair will reach first.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together the two mothers' griefs differ in kind: the foster mother mourns a public loss, while the birth mother, conceding she has no claim on the youth, turns grief into a practical question about the return, which shows the elopement already moving toward an accepted marriage.","gr":5},{"a":"a:193","b":"a:199","type":"develops","note":"In both the man refuses a journey his heart has resolved on: in 193 he grants the road is easy and names his wife's shoulders as the one thing he cannot leave; in 199 he lets the heart go alone and refuses even a king's restored wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Taken together the two show the refusal hardening from a weighing of costs into a full split from his own heart, and 199 adds a puram measure of the wealth refused that 193 never names."},{"a":"a:183","b":"a:194","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both are the woman telling her friend that the rainy season he promised has come without him; the colophon files 183 under paalai and 194 under mullai.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The pair shows the colophon thinai following different cues for one situation: 183 is labelled by the road he crossed and 194 by the fields at home, though both speak from the waiting house in the rains.","gr":4},{"a":"a:201","b":"a:213","type":"develops","note":"Both are a friend comforting a woman whose man has crossed the desert, and both argue that no gain could keep him: 201 says not even the Chola rice country, 213 not even the world of the worthy with its nectar.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Side by side they show the friend's reassurance as a scale that can be pushed from an earthly kingdom to heaven, always measuring his love by what he would refuse rather than by any promise he made.","gr":4},{"a":"a:204","b":"a:117","type":"same_image","note":"204 says the wife's hair smells of the groves of Vaanan's Sirukudi; 117 compares the eloping girl's braided hair to the ripples of the stream north of Vaanan's Sirukudi.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two poets use the same chief's village to praise a woman's hair, one by its scent and one by its waves, so Vaanan's Sirukudi had become a standing measure of hair rather than a single poet's image."},{"a":"a:205","b":"k:1154","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1154 asks whether those who trusted the man who said 'fear not' are at fault if he leaves; in 205 the woman names his oath 'we will not part' a lie and yet wishes his journey success.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet treats the broken promise as a question of blame, while the poem shows the same woman blaming and blessing him at once, so the trust the Kural judges was not withdrawn when it was betrayed."},{"a":"a:208","b":"a:181","type":"same_image","note":"Both are Paranar's and both tell of Aay Eyinan falling to Minjili: 181 has a flock of birds cover the sun over the dead man; 208 has Nannan refuse to look on the field where the birds shade him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show the bird-shade legend was told with a witness who would not look, so the poet could use the same event for wonder in a journey poem and for a ruler's shame in a love poem."},{"a":"a:209","b":"a:36","type":"same_image","note":"36 (Nakkirar) and 209 (Kalladanar) both measure gossip against the victory roar at Alankanam, where the Pandya Chezhiyan beat seven kings in a day.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two poets reached for the same Pandya victory to size two different scandals, a husband's new marriage and a girl's secret love, which shows the battle's roar had become a set unit of public noise in akam."},{"a":"a:209","b":"pn:158","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 158 lists Ori who ruled Kolli and Malaiyan who rode the horse Kaari side by side among the seven dead givers; Akananuru 209 says Kaari of Mullur killed Ori and gave Kolli to the Cheras.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The bards' roll of the seven great givers sets a killer and his victim together as equals in generosity, and only the akam simile keeps the memory that one of them ended the other."},{"a":"a:211","b":"a:226","type":"same_image","note":"226 names Mathi, lord of the fishermen, at the Kazhar ford where the Kaveri flooded; 211 names Mathi's stony ford and the gate of Venmani where Ezhini's teeth were set, and measures the gossip by the roar of its water.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the akam poets drew their measures of public noise from the same Mathi's river country, one by a flood and the other by a war trophy, so a chief's ford could carry both his water and his fame."},{"a":"a:215","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Both answer a man who announces his departure: Kural 1151 says tell your quick return to those who will be alive then; 215 says wishing you a safe journey is for those who can bear to stay.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem shows how the couplet's refusal works in practice: the friend withholds the ritual farewell and fills the road with killers and vultures, so the threat of death the Kural only implies is made visible.","gr":2},{"a":"a:216","b":"pn:396","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 396 praises Ezhini Aathan of Vaattaaru as strength to the weak and kin to the kinless, in a land where the Kosar drink toddy; Akananuru 216 recalls the spear of the lord of Chelli, a Kosar town, pressed into Aathan Ezhini's chest.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"If the two name the same chief, the patron the bard praised as a protector was remembered in akam only for his death wound, which a courtesan uses as a measure of how much she can hurt her rivals.","gr":2},{"a":"a:218","b":"a:202","type":"develops","note":"202 counts the dangers of the lover's night path (thunder, snake, dark) to press for marriage; 218 counts the same dangers (tiger, night clefts) and moves the meeting to daytime instead.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read in order they show the friend's two moves against the night meeting: first making the danger the women's suffering, then offering daylight, which trades his peril for the risk of the mother's knowing."},{"a":"a:219","b":"a:207","type":"same_image","note":"Both mothers recall feeding the girl milk at home, 219 coaxing her with 'your father's share too', 207 holding out milk with honey she refused, and both set it against the water of the desert road.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two poets build the eloping daughter's mother around the same contrast of home milk and road water, so the feeding bowl was the stock emblem of the care the daughter left behind."},{"a":"a:220","b":"a:90","type":"same_image","note":"Both are Maruthan Ila Naganar's neythal poems naming Chellur: in 90 it is the town of the mighty god by the roaring sea, in 220 the place of the sacrifice of the axe-bearing tall one with its guarded pillar.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same poet ties Chellur's sanctity once to its god and once to a famous sacrifice, so for him the town was a sacred place whose guarded objects could stand for a girl kept from her lover.","gr":2},{"a":"a:222","b":"a:236","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the Kazhar legend of Aathi Manthi and Attan Atthi; 222 names Maruthi, who restored the lover and entered the sea, 236 has Manthi searching town after town.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One legend served two speakers: the friend holds up Maruthi's fatal deed as a fame to win, the wife holds up Manthi's lament as her own shame, so the story was a shared stock the poets bent to opposite purposes.","gr":5},{"a":"a:226","b":"a:6","type":"same_image","note":"Both marutham poems reproach the husband for bathing in the Kaveri flood with the woman he desires (நீ வெய் யோளொடு), and both place it at Matthi's Kazhar near Thithan's Uranthai.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The shared phrase and places show that the bathing scandal at Kazhar was a set situation of marutham, which each poet then sharpened with a different puram comparison, a rout in 226 and a lost youth in 6.","gr":3},{"a":"a:226","b":"k:1146","type":"agrees","note":"Both measure gossip against a public spectacle: the Kural against the serpent swallowing the moon, the Akananuru against the shout at Katti's flight.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural keeps the old akam habit of sizing a private scandal by a famous public event, but trades the historical rout for a cosmic eclipse, losing the political sting while keeping the scale.","gr":3},{"a":"a:227","b":"pn:348","type":"same_image","note":"Thazhumban's Unur stands as the measure of wealth in both, here as the region beyond which the lover's noisy departure is likened to Marungur's market, there as the town a girl's beauty is compared to.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A chieftain's town famous in puram praise was reused by akam poets as a ready scale of plenty, so a patron's fame circulated as a stock of comparison across the two kinds of poem.","gr":3},{"a":"a:227","b":"k:1189","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the absent man's wellbeing above the woman's pallor: the friend's blessing நோய்இலராக, the Kural's 'let my body pale, if he is well'.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural compresses into the woman's own mouth a blessing that the Akananuru gives to the friend, showing how the anthology's go-between voice was absorbed into the single speaker of the later couplets.","gr":3},{"a":"a:229","b":"k:1151","type":"agrees","note":"Both refuse the promise of a quick return: the girl quotes his 'we will come right away' against the spring, the Kural says tell your quick return to those who will be alive.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the same refusal of comfort in two registers, the Kural as a sharp retort before parting and the Akananuru as proof gathered afterwards from the season's signs.","gr":5},{"a":"a:230","b":"k:1093","type":"qualifies","note":"In both the girl looks and bows her head; the Kural takes it as water poured into the bond, while here the bowed head with checked tears leaves the hero in pain.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural fixes the bowed head as a sign of consent, but the Akananuru shows the same gesture as unreadable to the man at the moment it happens, so the maxim is a reading made after the fact.","gr":5},{"a":"a:231","b":"k:1263","type":"agrees","note":"Both give the man's reason for parting as the pursuit of glory or strength (நல்லிசை, உரன்), which takes his resolve as companion instead of her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend's argument that glory is not for those who stay home is the very reason the Kural's woman lives on in longing, so the same heroic value comforts in one poem and wounds in the other.","gr":2},{"a":"a:233","b":"pn:2","type":"same_image","note":"Uthiyan Cheral's great feast (பெருஞ்சோறு) is praised in Purananuru 2 as a king's gift; here the crowd at that feast becomes a simile for hills crowding on the desert track.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"An akam poet could assume his hearers knew a king's famous feast well enough to turn its crowd of eaters into a picture of a landscape, which tells us how far puram fame was common knowledge.","gr":5},{"a":"a:234","b":"k:1268","type":"agrees","note":"Both have the returning man wish to finish the work and reach home for the evening; the Kural's evening feast at home is the poem's dusk arrival when the wife loosens her braid.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states as a wish what the mullai poem stages as a drive at dusk, showing that the evening return was the fixed goal of the man's speech in both traditions.","gr":3},{"a":"a:235","b":"k:1223","type":"agrees","note":"In both the evening comes with cold and brings the woman's sorrow to its height.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural names the evening's chill and the swelling grief in two lines, while the Akananuru supplies the season's things, the north wind, the rain and the flowers, that make the evening cold, so the couplet presupposes the landscape.","gr":2},{"a":"a:237","b":"a:231","type":"develops","note":"Both are the friend's assurance that he will return; 231 grants that glory took him away, 237 says no rich town, not even Uranthai, will keep him from her.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Taken together the two comforts answer the two fears of the waiting woman, that his honour will keep him away and that pleasure will, and show the friend's arguments as a set repertoire.","gr":3},{"a":"a:238","b":"a:152","type":"same_image","note":"Both liken the woman's fragrance to the kaanthal flowers of Nalli's hill slopes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Nalli's hill and its kaanthal were a fixed image for a woman's scent, so a patron's country lent its fame to the beloved's body in more than one poet's hands.","gr":8},{"a":"a:239","b":"k:759","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural commands making wealth as the sharpest weapon; the hero leaving for wealth that is hard to earn counts what the going costs the girl he leaves.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's bare imperative to make wealth is weighed here against a girl's quoted plea not to stay long, showing that the duty the maxim states without condition was, in akam, always paid for by someone left behind.","gr":8},{"a":"a:243","b":"n:110","type":"qualifies","note":"Both name former deeds as the cause of suffering: the woman's தொல்வினைப் பயன், the Naladiyar's fixed mark that cannot shrink or grow.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Naladiyar draws from fixed former deeds the lesson that grief is useless, but the Akananuru woman names the same cause through streaming eyes and says she cannot bear it, so the explanation was available to akam speakers long before it was offered as a cure."},{"a":"a:245","b":"a:265","type":"qualifies","note":"Both weigh wealth against the wife: in 265 she says he held wealth weightier than her; in 245 he imagines her saying wealth is dearer than life and refuses to go.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same scale of wealth against the wife is read from both ends, and in 245 it is the wife's readiness to accept the man's reason that makes him unable to act on it, a turn the complaint of 265 cannot show."},{"a":"a:246","b":"pn:66","type":"same_image","note":"Karikal's victory at Venni: Purananuru 66 praises the defeated king who starved himself facing north in shame at his back wound; 246 measures gossip against the victory shout at Azhundur.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poet honours the loser of Venni above the victor while the akam poet borrows only the victor's roar, which shows how differently one battle served praise of honour and a scale for noise."},{"a":"a:246","b":"a:55","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the battle of Venni: 55 as Cheralathan's starving to death after his wound, 246 as Karikal's triumph.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Within one anthology the same battle supplies a mother's grief with the image of a death beyond comfort and a friend's scorn with the image of a deafening cheer, so akam poets picked a side of a public event to suit the speaker's mood.","gr":2},{"a":"a:246","b":"a:226","type":"same_image","note":"Both are the friend refusing the husband after he bathed in the river with his woman, and both end 'the gossip is greater than the shout' (ஆர்ப்பினும் பெரிதே) at a historical event.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The repeated frame shows that measuring marutham gossip by a public shout was a fixed form, and the poets made their point by which shout they chose: a Chola victory in 246, a rival's flight in 226."},{"a":"a:251","b":"pn:175","type":"same_image","note":"Both name the rock-cut road the Moriyar's chariot wheels made: in the Purananuru the sun's disc standing over that pass is the image of the patron's daily charity; here the pass is the far road the lover crossed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A northern dynasty's road through the southern hills was common knowledge to both kinds of poem, so the same landmark could stand for a patron's open-handedness and for the distance that keeps a lover away."},{"a":"a:251","b":"a:69","type":"develops","note":"Both are the friend's comfort that he will return although he has passed the Moriyar's rock-cut road; 69 says he will not stay long, 251 that not even Nanda's wealth would keep him.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Put side by side the two comforts show that the Moriyar road had become the stock boundary of the far journey, and that 251 sharpens the assurance by setting against it the greatest wealth the poets knew."},{"a":"a:251","b":"a:265","type":"tension","note":"265's woman asks whether it is the Nandas' treasure hidden under the Ganges that he values more than her; 251's friend says he would not stay even if he got Nanda's wealth.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Nandas' treasure is the same weight on the scale in both poems, but the woman fears it outweighs her and the friend swears it does not, so the comfort and the complaint are exact answers to each other."},{"a":"a:253","b":"a:177","type":"develops","note":"Both are the friend's comfort that the man, gone past the Ayiri river, will come back because he will remember the woman's body (her thoyyil marks in 177, her face and look in 253).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the friend's argument from memory of the body as a set move, and 253 adds what 177 lacks, the woman's own fears of mother and town, which the remembered body is offered to outweigh."},{"a":"a:253","b":"a:246","type":"same_image","note":"Both measure gossip by a public shout over a royal victory, 253 at the Pandyan's Kudal after the Kongar were driven out, 246 at the Chola's Azhundur after Venni.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In paalai as in marutham the gossip-and-shout frame fits each speaker's land and dynasty, so the formula traveled across thinais while the king named changed with the poet."},{"a":"a:255","b":"pp:4:346-362","type":"same_image","note":"Both show a lighthouse lamp on a tall tower that calls ships to the shore at night: the idyll as a sight of the port on the guide's road, the poem as the pilot's sign of landfall.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The idyll records the harbour light as a fact of the port; the akam poet turns the same light into the sure sign of a voyage's end and sets it against the woman's lack of any messenger, so a civic fixture became an emblem of return."},{"a":"a:256","b":"a:246","type":"develops","note":"Both refuse the husband and measure the gossip by a public shout; 246 names a battle, 256 names an assembly's punishment of a man who falsely denied an affair.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The later poem bends the frame into a threat: the shout it chooses was raised against a man who swore 'I do not know her', exactly the denial the husband is making, so the comparison judges as well as measures."},{"a":"a:258","b":"a:142","type":"same_image","note":"Both are the man speaking to his heart about a night meeting and both name Nannan's fortress Paazhi.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Paazhi stands for a well-guarded prize in both of the man's speeches to his heart, rejoicing in 142 and despairing in 258, which shows that a chieftain's fortress had become the akam measure of a girl's inaccessibility."},{"a":"a:259","b":"a:55","type":"qualifies","note":"259's friend tells the girl to bear her mother's grief and go; in 55 the mother of an eloped girl says she cannot sleep and wishes her life had left with her daughter.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend names the mother's grief only as a weight the girl must carry away with her, while 55 shows from inside that the grief is a wish to die, so the anthology measures the cost the friend's counsel asks the girl to accept."},{"a":"a:260","b":"pp:8:215-233","type":"same_image","note":"Both list the signs of dusk, with the anril calling or settling in the palmyra's inner frond (பெண்ணை அகமடல்) among them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kapilar's idyll uses the dusk catalogue as the moment the man promises marriage, while 260 uses the same signs to open a night of dread, so the formula of nightfall was shared and its turn at the end set its meaning.","gr":2},{"a":"a:257","b":"a:259","type":"develops","note":"259 is the friend urging the girl to go now the season is right; 257 is the man on the road praising her walk through a summer waste.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read in order the two poems show the elopement from its promised green season to the real summer road, and the man's praise of her walk answers the friend's request that she go with a steady mind."},{"a":"a:262","b":"a:196","type":"same_image","note":"Both are Paranar's and both use Anni Minili, whose father the Kosar blinded and whose vow Thithiyan fulfilled: in 196 the wife names the courtesan as one who carries herself like Minili; in 262 the lover's joy is like Minili's when her anger was released.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One poet draws on either half of the same local revenge story, the proud wrath in 196 and the relief after vengeance in 262, so the story was known well enough to be cited by a single name for a single mood."},{"a":"a:262","b":"pn:142","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 142 (Paranar) praises Pekan as giving like rain that falls everywhere without choosing; Akananuru 262 (Paranar) gives Pekan's hill as கொண்டல் மாமலை, the rain-cloud hill whose drizzle wets the bushes, and lends its scent to the girl's voice.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same poet's figure for Pekan's indiscriminate giving, rain, is carried into akam as the smell of his rain-soaked hill, showing a patron's praise-image reused as a lover's sensory measure."},{"a":"a:266","b":"pn:233","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 233 is a lament that wishes false the dawn report of Evvi's many wounds; Akananuru 266 measures the gossip about the husband by the roar of the feast at Uraththur after Evvi's victory.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Evvi's name travels with loud public report in both books, a victory feast in akam and a death rumour in puram, so the akam poet uses his fame precisely as a gauge of how far news spreads."},{"a":"a:269","b":"pn:264","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 264 sets up a stone for a man who recovered cattle, crowned with red flowers tied with marral fibre and peacock feather and inscribed with his name; Akananuru 269 shows such stones on the road, names inscribed, washed, smeared with turmeric and crowned with red karanthai for men who held off the Mazhavar to recover cattle.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poem gives the stone on the day of grief, when the bards still come not knowing; the akam poem shows the same rite kept up later on a travelled road, so together they show the hero-stone moving from mourning to a standing roadside cult."},{"a":"a:270","b":"a:44","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 270 names Kazhumalam as the town of Kuttuvan, the Chera, as a figure of beauty; Akananuru 44 tells how the Chola Senni took Kazhumalam after capturing Kanaiyan, and uses Senni's Azhumbil as its simile.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same town appears as a Chera possession in one akam simile and as a Chola conquest in another, so the similes preserve a contested place under rival owners and are evidence for its history, not only ornament."},{"a":"a:280","b":"pn:343","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 343, a maganmaruttal poem, has the father refuse his daughter even if suitors bow and give நலஞ்சால் விழுப்பொருள்; Akananuru 280 has the lover say she cannot be had even for நலம்சால் விழுப்பொருள் given by the shipload, and resolve to serve her fisherman father instead.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The near-identical phrase shows the akam suitor speaking inside the puram convention of the father who refuses wealth, and gives the convention's other side: where puram suitors bring siege, the akam lover answers the refusal with servitude."},{"a":"a:276","b":"k:1007","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Kural 1007 likens the wealth of a man who gives nothing to the needy to a beautiful woman growing old alone; the courtesan in Akananuru 276 likens her own beauty, if she fails to win the man, to the wealth of one who hoarded and never gave to those who asked.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two verses run the same comparison in opposite directions, beauty as vehicle for hoarded wealth in the Kural and hoarded wealth as vehicle for beauty in the poem, which shows the pairing was a shared idea in which each term could explain the other.","gr":3},{"a":"a:273","b":"k:1147","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1147 has the woman say her love-sickness grows with the town's gossip as manure and her mother's words as water; in Akananuru 273 she says the sickness sprouted between her breasts, grew a trunk, spread branches of gossip and budded with scandal into a tree shading the world.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural's two-line plant metaphor, gossip feeding the disease, is the seed of Avvaiyar's full tree, which adds the stages of growth and makes the scandal not only food for the love but its flowering.","gr":4},{"a":"a:267","b":"k:1236","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1236 the woman tells her friend that with bangles and shoulders slackening what hurts is hearing him called cruel; in Akananuru 267, when the friend says the oath-breaker has changed, the wife replies that he is not at fault and her shoulders, which let the bangles slip, are.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Same speaker, addressee and occasion: the Kural states the defence of the absent man as a feeling, and the poem shows its rhetorical move, turning the blame on her own body, so the couplet's restraint is seen as an argument with the friend."},{"a":"a:271","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1151 (the friend's words for Parimelazhagar) tells the departing man to promise his quick return to those who will still be alive; in Akananuru 271, the friend tells the departing man to prosper, but asks whether there is any medicine for the girl in the land where he will stay.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both turn the farewell into a threat of her death, but the poem argues it through the road, whose nelli fruit revives dying travellers, so the couplet's bare warning is given a reason: the waste has remedies for strangers and none for her."},{"a":"a:263","b":"a:275","type":"develops","note":"Both are a mother after her daughter's elopement: in 263 she says she would gladly have married the girl to the youth had she known; in 275 she recalls the girl's play and lisping speech and points onlookers to the sand house the girl left.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they give the mother's two answers to the loss, regret that she was not asked and grief over the childhood left behind, so the elopement is a wound to her authority as well as to her love."},{"a":"a:261","b":"a:275","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 261 the man, back home, recalls the girl on the elopement road too shy to walk ahead of him, while a tiger roared and bowmen's drums sounded; in 275 the mother imagines her daughter on the bear-haunted hot road and is left with her sand house.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same journey is seen from both ends: for the mother the road is only danger and loss, while the man remembers it as the girl's shyness and a slow camp in the wild, so the anthology lets each speaker hold the half the other cannot know."},{"a":"a:281","b":"a:69","type":"same_image","note":"Both set the man's road at the rock the Moriyar's wheels cut and arm him with a bow wound with a peacock's shed feather; in 69 the friend says he will not delay, in 281 the woman says he went without fear of breaking his word.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The same stock road and the same feathered bow serve one poet as a promise of return and another as proof of desertion, so the image fixed the distance while the speaker decided what the distance meant."},{"a":"a:281","b":"pn:175","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 175 likens a patron's lasting charity to the rock-cut pass of the Moriyar's chariot wheels, a public way open to all; Akananuru 281 sends the lover over that same pass.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In praise poetry the Moriyar's cut road stands for something permanent and shared, while the akam poet uses it as the point past which a man is lost to one woman, so one piece of remembered history carried opposite values in the two kinds of poetry."},{"a":"a:281","b":"k:1180","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the beaten drum (அறைபறை) for love exposed to the town while the man is away; in the Kural it is her own eyes, in 281 it is the gossip he left behind.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural takes the stock drum of gossip and turns it on the woman's own eyes, so the pairing shows the couplet moving the blame for exposure from the town, as the Sangam poem has it, to her own body."},{"a":"a:286","b":"k:467","type":"develops","note":"Kural 467 says to deliberate and then resolve, never resolve and then deliberate; the friend in 286 says the great weigh virtue, wealth and their own worth before carrying out what they intend.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem shows the Kural's rule of deliberate action used as a lever in courtship: if the great think before they promise, a lover who swore cannot later plead haste, so the maxim becomes a way to bind a promise."},{"a":"a:289","b":"a:61","type":"same_image","note":"In 61 the friend tells the woman not to grieve gazing at the wall scored with the days; in 289 the man on the road imagines her looking at that wall.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The wall marked with the days of absence was known to both sides of the parting: the man can picture the very ritual the friend tries to stop, so his guilt in 289 rests on a convention the woman's side also names."},{"a":"a:290","b":"pn:48","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 48 says Thondi smells of the neythal flowers blooming in its great backwater; Akananuru 290 says the woman's eyes were once like the great cool neythal of Kuttuvan's Thondi harbour.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Thondi's neythal was the town's own emblem in praise of its king, so when the akam poet says her eyes have lost that likeness he is measuring her pallor against a flower the audience knew as Thondi's pride."},{"a":"a:295","b":"a:61","type":"develops","note":"Both are Mamoolanar's: in 61 the man would not stay even for Pulli's festive Venkadam; in 295 he has gone past Pulli's hill into the Vadugar country where the language changes.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Read together, the poet's Pulli's hill is the last familiar landmark: one poem stops at it as the measure of a great prize, the other goes beyond it to a land of strange speech, giving his geography of absence an inner and an outer edge."},{"a":"a:296","b":"a:116","type":"same_image","note":"Both are marutham poems in which the friend refuses the husband and compares the gossip about him to noise at Chezhiyan's Koodal; in 116 it is louder than the shout of the battlefield where he routed two kings, in 296 it is like the city's everyday clamour.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam poets measured scandal by the noise of the Pandya capital in war and in peace alike, so Koodal's fame worked as a ready scale for how far gossip had spread."},{"a":"a:297","b":"pn:264","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 264 tells of a hero-stone newly set up with his name engraved and crowned with a peacock feather; Akananuru 297 shows such a stone grown old, its inscribed name passed unread by weary travellers.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poem records the moment a name is carved to outlast death, and the akam poem shows the same kind of stone generations on with no one reading it, so the pair qualifies the promise of the engraved name."},{"a":"a:301","b":"a:309","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the wandering kodiyar troupe: in 301 their leaving empties a town's commons, and that emptiness is the heroine's evening; in 309 their going to Vaanavan is the model for the heroine's own journey after her man.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same performers figure both sides of parting, the place left desolate and the traveller who will not count the forest long, so akam poets used the bards' life as a ready picture of both staying and going."},{"a":"a:303","b":"pn:108","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 303 calls Paari the giver of hill-like elephants to any suppliant and makes his Parambu the place sparrows always return to at evening; Purananuru 108 says Paari never answers a suppliant with 'I will not come'.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The akam poet borrows the certainty of Paari's giving, famous from the puram poems, to make the sparrows' return seem certain, so that the heroine's disappointment is measured against a patron who never failed anyone."},{"a":"a:304","b":"a:314","type":"same_image","note":"Both open in the first rains with a stag, a doe and her fawn together; in 304 the hero sees them from the war camp and imagines his wife's reproach, in 314 the friend sees them as the hero comes home in time.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One scene of the deer family carries reproach while he is away and fulfilment when he returns, which shows that what a karu thing implies is fixed by the uri of the poem, not by the thing itself."},{"a":"a:307","b":"pn:52","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture a public hall (பொதியில்) fallen to ruin, its pillar abandoned by its god; in Purananuru 52 it is the fate of lands the Pandya conquers, in Akananuru 307 a dove clings to it with its mate.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A ruined hall abandoned by its god was a stock picture of a conquered land, and the akam poet turns it round: even in that ruin a pair of birds keeps faith, which makes the hero's leaving harsher.","gr":4},{"a":"a:312","b":"pn:52","type":"same_image","note":"The same poet, Maruthan Ilanagan, praises Vazhuthi's war in Purananuru 52 and in Akananuru 312 likens lightning to the spear Vazhuthi raises against fleeing enemies.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"One poet's war praise for a Pandya becomes, in his love poem, the measure of a storm's power, showing puram fame travelling into akam as a simile that its first hearers would recognise.","gr":2},{"a":"a:313","b":"pn:3","type":"same_image","note":"Both describe the waterless road where bowmen shoot travellers and birds feed on the dead; in Purananuru 3 suppliants brave it for the Pandya's gifts, in Akananuru 313 the husband crosses it for wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same death road is crossed for the same motive, gain, by the suppliant and by the husband; puram counts the crossing as praise of the patron, akam as the measure of what the wife fears.","gr":2},{"a":"a:316","b":"k:1302","type":"develops","note":"Both are the friend telling the wife not to prolong her sulking: Kural 1302 says sulking is salt and too much spoils it; Akananuru 316 shows women who sulked too long left alone, poor and nursing hungry sons.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"What the Kural calls spoiling by too much salt the Sangam friend spells out as the loss of fortune and household, so the couplet's measured image stands for a social and economic ruin, not only a lost pleasure.","gr":2},{"a":"a:318","b":"a:308","type":"develops","note":"Both are the friend turning back a man who comes at night by a beast-haunted hill path: in 308 she offers day meetings instead, in 318 she bans his visits and offers marriage.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read in order, the two refusals show the stages of the friend's pressure: first moving the meeting to day, still within secret love, then closing the meetings so that marriage is the only road left."},{"a":"a:319","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Both are spoken to a man who announces his departure: Kural 1151 says tell me if you are not going, and keep your promise of a quick return for those who will still be alive; Akananuru 319's friend says go and get your wealth, since the girl cannot even say she will stay behind.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam poem shows why the friend speaks in this situation at all: the girl is too young and loving to answer him herself, which supports reading the couplet's 'tell me' as the friend speaking for her."},{"a":"a:326","b":"a:44","type":"same_image","note":"326 likens the young woman's unerring eyes to the spear raised by Pazhaiyan, lord of Por and the Cholas' champion; 44 recalls the battle in which Pazhaiyan fell (பழையன் பட்டென) and Chenni avenged him.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Akam poets drew on both halves of one champion's story, his unfailing aim for a woman's dangerous eyes and his death for the scale of a war that keeps a husband from home, so the hero's fame served love poetry whole.","gr":3},{"a":"a:336","b":"a:186","type":"develops","note":"Both are marutham speeches by a courtesan meant for the hearing of others, and both stake her bangles on Chola martial formulas: 186 says the lord never broke her bangles, using Pazhaiyan's 'rain of arrows, cloud-dark shields' (மாரி அம்பின் மழைத்தோல்); 336 applies the same phrase to the Cholas and curses her bangles to break like the Aryans at Vallam.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the courtesan's boast as a set form: her bangles stand for her hold on the man, and she guarantees them with the same Chola war formula, as a warrior swears by his king."},{"a":"a:338","b":"a:209","type":"develops","note":"338 wishes love to last like the Porai's Kolli; 209 tells how Kari killed Ori and gave Kolli to the Cheras, who set a goddess image there.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read with 209, the hero's figure of permanence is a hill that changed lords by a killing, so what the poet makes lasting is the hill itself, not any house that held it.","gr":3},{"a":"a:338","b":"pn:158","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 158 names Kolli as the hill Ori ruled, among seven givers now dead; Akananuru 338 calls Kolli the Porai's and makes it the measure of a love that must not perish.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The puram poem remembers Kolli by a dead patron and the akam poem by its new Chera master, so the hill a lover swears by already carries the record of a fallen lord."},{"a":"a:322","b":"a:25","type":"same_image","note":"Both name Thithiyan as lord of Pothiyil: 25 has his drums echoed in the waterfalls of the hills the absent man crossed; 322 makes his guarded, honey-hung peak the measure of how hard the girl is to reach.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One chief's hill serves two opposite uses, the distance a husband has gone and the closeness a lover cannot reach, which shows akam poets taking a puram place for its fit to the speaker's situation, not for its praise.","gr":2},{"a":"a:331","b":"a:322","type":"same_image","note":"Both poems in this chapter name Thithiyan: 322 his war elephants and his guarded Pothiyil; 331 his daily court, his gifts to bards and the sword he drew against the Velir.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same patron lends the frustrated lover his inaccessible hill and the abandoned wife his sword, so each speaker takes from a king's fame the face that matches her or his own trouble."},{"a":"a:340","b":"a:85","type":"same_image","note":"85 names Thiraiyan's Venkatam hill on the road the husband crossed; 340 names Thiraiyan's flowery seaside town Pavaththiri as the measure of the girl's beauty.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same chief furnishes a mountain for a paalai poem and a shore town for a neythal one, so a patron's domain was divided among the landscapes as the thinai required."},{"a":"a:334","b":"a:34","type":"develops","note":"Same thurai: a man, his task done, urges his charioteer home. 34 drives by the stag that feeds and guards its doe; 334 by the breaking rains, hail and peacocks, and by the tribute won with the war drum.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show the homecoming poem built in two ways: by a creature whose care mirrors the husband's intent (34), or by the season itself whose arrival is the pledge kept (334)."},{"a":"a:328","b":"a:332","type":"same_image","note":"Both are night-tryst poems the lover overhears, and both close on the same formula, a tusker and his mate asleep on the plantain slope (வாழையஞ் சிலம்பிற் றுஞ்சும் / வாழையம் சிலம்பில் துஞ்சும்): in 328 he strokes a mate who has just calved, in 332 he lies down with her after killing a tiger.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"A shared formula takes a different act each time to suit the speech: the calving mate presses toward married life in a poem of fear, the tiger-killing protector confirms the lover in a poem of praise.","gr":5},{"a":"a:327","b":"a:337","type":"develops","note":"Both are men arguing with hearts set on wealth, and both make the desert's killers the argument: in 327 the archers pity no victim and grieve only when they miss; in 337 the Mazhavar kill a starving messenger in case he has gold and leave scorning his rags.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Side by side they show the road weighing a man's life only by what he might carry, which is the very reckoning the heart is making when it trades her company for wealth.","gr":4},{"a":"a:339","b":"k:1124","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1124: she is living to my life when with me and dying when she leaves; Akananuru 339 ends with love like the life's living and parting like dying, spoken by a husband as he sets out.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural says it in courtship as praise; the poem puts the same claim in the mouth of a man choosing to leave, so the saying becomes the cost he knowingly pays, burning at both ends of the firebrand."},{"a":"a:339","b":"k:1122","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 1122 calls the bond with the girl that of body and life (உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை ... நட்பு); 339 has the same word and image, friendship like life joined to the body (யாக்கைக்கு உயிர்இயைந் தன்ன நட்பின்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared image and even the shared word நட்பு show the Kural's couplet as a distilled form of an akam figure that the poem still spends a scene of departure to earn."},{"a":"a:335","b":"k:247","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 247 grants that this world is not for those without wealth; Akananuru 335 opens by conceding that even the most compassionate cannot give without wealth, then gives up the journey for wealth anyway.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem accepts the Kural's premise about wealth in so many words yet refuses its practical pull, showing that the premise alone did not settle whether a husband should leave home to earn."},{"a":"a:327","b":"k:629","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 629: one who does not crave the pleasure within pleasure does not feel the pain within pain; Akananuru 327 tells the heart that joy and grief come by turns like day and night, so it need not go seeking hardship for wealth.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same doctrine of joy and grief in turn, which the Kural uses to teach calm under misfortune, becomes in the lover's mouth an argument for staying home rather than chasing gain."},{"a":"a:324","b":"k:81","type":"develops","note":"Kural 81: all of keeping house is for receiving guests; in Akananuru 324 the attendants announce the husband's return by saying his wife will now have guests to feed too.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem shows the Kural's rule from the inside of an absence: hospitality belonged to the couple, so a wife alone could not host, and the husband's return is announced as the return of guests."},{"a":"a:344","b":"a:34","type":"develops","note":"Both are mullai poems in which the hero, his work done, tells the charioteer to drive fast through the rain-fed forest to his waiting wife.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 34 grounds the hurry in an animal pair (the stag feeding and guarding his doe) while 344 grounds it in the clock, before evening falls, so the homecoming formula could be driven either by an emblem of care or by the promised hour."},{"a":"a:354","b":"a:344","type":"develops","note":"Both are mullai homecomings after the task: in 344 the hero urges his charioteer; in 354 the attendants urge the hero, naming the victory and the cowherds' evening.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the same return from both ends of command: the hero who orders speed in 344 is himself ordered by his men in 354, and both make the wife's pallor the thing his arrival cures."},{"a":"a:347","b":"a:127","type":"same_image","note":"Both poems by Mamoolanar use Cheralathan, who drove back the sea and cut the kadambu: 127 his tribute treasure, 347 his drum.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One poet drew two different measures from a single Chera victory, its plunder as the scale of the wealth the husband will not stay for and its drum as the scale of the gossip's noise, so akam borrowed puram events for value and for loudness alike."},{"a":"a:345","b":"a:349","type":"same_image","note":"Both paalai poems pass through the country of Ezhil hill: 345 recalls it as the hill sung by a poet given horses, 349 as Nannan's land where bards are given wealth unasked.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show that Ezhil hill was known through its patron's gifts to poets, so the absent husband's road ran through a landscape whose fame was generosity, sharpening the contrast with the man who left home to get wealth.","gr":2},{"a":"a:351","b":"k:1261","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's woman has worn her fingers marking the days since he left; 351 names the marks drawn on the wall that she counts each day.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet speaks only from the woman's side, while 351 puts the same tally marks in the returning man's mind, so the counting of days on the wall was a known practice that both partners could picture, not a private secret of the one who waits."},{"a":"a:353","b":"n:11","type":"tension","note":"Both start from the coming of age: the Naladiyar's wise renounce early because grey hair will come; 353's hero refuses to leave his wife because the days pass like a dream and age is coming.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same premise, that youth is short, yields opposite counsels, renunciation in the Naladiyar and staying in married love in 353, so impermanence was a shared starting point that the akam poets turned toward the household and the didactic poets away from it."},{"a":"a:353","b":"a:351","type":"develops","note":"Both are spoken by the hero to his heart about the journey for wealth: 351 as he returns with it, 353 when the heart at home urges a second journey.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Placed in sequence the two show the pull of wealth outlasting its success: the heart that raced home in 351 is restless again in 353, and only the argument from age and the wife's virtue holds it back.","gr":2},{"a":"a:354","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"Both are the man at the king's war: the couplet wishes the king victory so that he may join his wife for the evening feast; in 354 the king has won and raised his flag, and the attendants urge the hero home before her pallor deepens.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's wish has two conditions, the king's victory and the evening at home, and 354 shows them met in order (the flag raised, the herds coming in at dusk), so the Kural's line compresses a whole mullai homecoming whose hinge is that the hero's private return waits on the public war."},{"a":"a:352","b":"pn:92","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 92 is Avvai's song to Anji calling her words a child's babble he graciously hears; 352 measures the friend's sweetness against a famous bard's new tune for Anji's praise.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The praise songs for Anji, which Avvai offers modestly as babble, had become in akam a byword for sweetness, showing that puram performances were known to the love poets' audience as celebrated pieces of music.","gr":8},{"a":"a:360","b":"a:350","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are neythal poems in which the friend answers a lover's daytime visit at dusk: 350 asks him to stay and points to the town, 360 refuses the day meeting and sets a secret night one.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"At the same hour the friend has two opposite tactics, drawing the lover openly toward the village in 350 and hiding him by night in 360, so the dusk scene of neythal could press either toward marriage or toward deeper secrecy."},{"a":"a:355","b":"a:341","type":"qualifies","note":"Both open on the spring signs of new leaves and the koel calling in the mango and set them against the man's absence.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same signs bring helpless lament in 341 ('there is no way through') and a planned reproach in 355 (we will go and quarrel with him), so the season's cue fixed the occasion but not the heroine's response."},{"a":"a:361","b":"k:1108","type":"develops","note":"Both make the man say that the sweetest thing is an embrace that lets nothing come between: in the Kural not even a breeze, in the poem not even a thread, at which she sulks.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet states the delight as settled, while the poem quotes it as the argument the man lost to his heart's desire for wealth, so the Kural's claim is shown being tested and overruled inside the paalai situation."},{"a":"a:372","b":"a:258","type":"same_image","note":"Both are the hero's words to his heart under the same occasion (அல்ல குறி), both liken the girl to the treasure the old Velir guarded at Pazhi, and both turn on எனினும் அஃது ஒல்லாய்.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The repeated opening under one thurai shows a fixed formula for the spoiled night meeting, the Velir's hidden gold at Pazhi standing for the guarded girl, which 258 then ends in the heart's ruin and 372 in a run of similes for its swinging.","gr":3},{"a":"a:375","b":"a:372","type":"same_image","note":"Pazhi is the Velir's god-guarded treasure city in 372 and the copper-walled fort Ilamperunchenni broke in 375.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One fort served akam poets for both halves of its story, as the byword for what cannot be reached and as the scene of a famous breach, so a puram place-name carried opposite meanings depending on which part of its history the speaker needed."},{"a":"a:365","b":"a:135","type":"same_image","note":"Kazhuvul's Kaamur is a place of blessed vengai fragrance in 365 and the town thrown into turmoil by the fourteen Velir in 135.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same chieftain's town gives one poet the image of calm sweetness for the wife's hair and another the image of a besieged, shaken heart, showing that a puram name entered akam with its whole history, peace and siege alike, available for comparison.","gr":5},{"a":"a:366","b":"pn:24","type":"same_image","note":"Evvi is the unsparing giver (ஓம்பா ஈகை மாவேள் எவ்வி) in the Purananuru poem and the lord who gives toddy to the fishermen to end a brawl in 366.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Purananuru names Evvi's giving as a title, while the akam simile shows it as a scene of keeping peace between farmers and salt-makers and makes his shade a measure of a woman's comfort, so a patron's fame for protection became an akam image of shelter.","gr":3},{"a":"a:373","b":"pn:338","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the figure of the ஓர்எயில் மன்னன, the chieftain of a single fort set against crowned kings: in 338 he will not give his daughter to them, in 373 he lies sleepless under their siege.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The kaanchi poem on a daughter refused to kings shows where the akam poet's image came from: the lone fort's siege was a known situation of puram, and the akam poet borrowed only its sleepless watch to picture the wife.","gr":2},{"a":"a:376","b":"a:396","type":"same_image","note":"Both are a courtesan's words to the man, both liken his loss to the Kaveri seizing and hiding Atthi, and both end on the demand to give back her beauty and go (நலம்தந்து சென்மே).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two poems sharing speaker, legend and closing line show that the Atthi story and the 'return my beauty' demand formed a stock courtesan's reproach, which each poet then fitted to a different king's town as the measure of her lost beauty."},{"a":"a:376","b":"pn:66","type":"same_image","note":"Karikal is the victor of Venni in the Purananuru poem and the onlooker at Atthi's flood-dance at Kazhar in 376.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The conqueror praised in puram appears in akam only as a royal spectator who dates and places a love legend, so akam poets used a war king's fame as a frame for a story of loss rather than of victory.","gr":2},{"a":"a:377","b":"k:231","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says to give and live with renown (இசைபட) as the only gain; the poem names those who give with renown (இசைபடப் பெய்தல்) as the men who must sleep in ruined halls on the road to wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem shows what the Kural's rule costs a married man: renown from giving needs wealth, and wealth needs the wasteland and separation, so the maxim's single profit is bought with the akam hero's loneliness."},{"a":"a:379","b":"k:332","type":"develops","note":"Both say great wealth does not last: the Kural likens its coming and going to a crowd at a dance hall, the poem likens its passing to a night's dream.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem turns the Kural's general warning about wealth into a reason for a husband to stay home, using impermanence not to urge giving but to set the wife's embrace above a second journey for gain."},{"a":"a:369","b":"a:15","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are a mother's words after the daughter's elopement; in 15 the mother prays that the town the girl reaches be rich and welcoming to strangers, in 369 the foster mother fears she has married in a poor one-cow hut.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Set side by side, the two laments show that imagining the daughter's new home was a set part of the eloped-daughter situation, and that the poets could make it either a blessing or a wound to family pride."},{"a":"a:387","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Both are the plea of the one left behind against a departure: the couplet says tell your quick return to those still alive; 387 asks him to say he will return, and then shows a road where great men turn back at a lizard's cry.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet only doubts the traveller's promise; the poem shows why it is empty, since on that road a return depends on omens and beasts, not on the man's word.","gr":3},{"a":"a:390","b":"k:1084","type":"develops","note":"Both are the man's account of a first sight, and both set a simple girl (பேதை) against eyes that make war (அமர்த்தன / அமர்த்தனள்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's paradox of war-making eyes in an artless girl gets a cause in the poem: she glares because a stranger blocks her road and jokes about her body, so the 'deadly' look is her self-possession, and it ends in a smile.","gr":2},{"a":"a:396","b":"k:1183","type":"qualifies","note":"Both speak of a lover who took a woman's beauty in exchange: the couplet's woman says he took grace and modesty and gave sickness and pallor; the courtesan of 396 demands her beauty back before he goes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the claim that a lover owes back the beauty he took was not the wife's alone: the courtesan states it as a debt to be repaid, which the couplet's wife can only lament."},{"a":"a:396","b":"a:376","type":"same_image","note":"Both are Paranar's courtesan poems, both end with the demand to give back her beauty and then go (நலம்தந்து சென்மே), and both use the Kaviri carrying off Aththi.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In 376 the river's seizing of Aththi is left without its tenor; 396 names it (I fear your wife will take you as the Kaviri hid Aththi), so the legend served Paranar as a fixed figure for the wife's lawful claim over the courtesan."},{"a":"a:396","b":"a:208","type":"develops","note":"208 tells how Aay Eyinan fell to Minjili at Paazhi and the birds shaded him; 396 says he died because he had promised 'fear not' to those Nannan drove from Punnadu.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"396 supplies the cause behind 208's famous death: Eyinan died keeping a promise of refuge, which is why a courtesan could cite him against a lover who broke an oath made before a god."},{"a":"a:386","b":"a:44","type":"same_image","note":"Kanaiyan appears in 44 as the chief captured by the Chola after Pazhaiyan fell, and in 386 as the patron shamed when Paanan broke his Aariya wrestler.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The akam poets used Kanaiyan only in defeat, once in capture and once in public shame, so his name had become a byword for humiliation before an onlooking crowd, which is exactly what the wife of 386 feels.","gr":3},{"a":"a:389","b":"pn:2","type":"same_image","note":"Puram 2 praises the Chera as வான வரம்பன், whose realm runs from the sea of sunrise to the sea of sunset; in 389 the husband goes beyond Vaana varamban's good land into tiger country.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The title that in puram means a king bounded only by the sky becomes in akam the edge of the safe world: the lover's journey is measured as going past a realm that claims to have no beyond."},{"a":"a:393","b":"a:311","type":"same_image","note":"Both are Mamoolanar's comfort poems: the man has gone beyond Pulli's good land (புல்லி நன்னாட்டு உம்பர்), where herders feed travellers, and he will not delay (நீடலர்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show a fixed comforting formula in which the herders' hospitality on Pulli's road is the reason the friend gives for the man's safety, so the same road scene doubles as evidence that he will come back."},{"a":"a:391","b":"a:393","type":"develops","note":"In 391 the wife sees the tuft he dressed with athiral and mullai and remembers; in 393 the friend says he cannot stay away, having forgotten the sleep beside her hair dressed with blue lily and summer athiral.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The athiral in her hair is the shared token of the separation: for the wife it is proof of loss, while the friend turns the same token into proof that he must return."},{"a":"a:388","b":"a:382","type":"develops","note":"Both are hill poems spoken at the night meeting with the lover listening, and both turn on the household's possession rite for Murugan.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The rite works two ways in the kurinji poems: in 382 its threat is news to hurry the lover, while in 388 the girl plans to use the diviner's own diagnosis to name the hunter, so the rite becomes the channel through which secret love is disclosed."},{"a":"a:394","b":"a:384","type":"develops","note":"394 invites the lover to eat milk rice in a mullai village home 'as your wife prepares it'; 384 shows the returned husband bringing a guest into such a home, where the wife receives him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend's invitation in 394 pictures, as a reason to marry, the very scene 384 shows as marriage achieved: a mullai home where the wife serves guests.","gr":2},{"a":"a:397","b":"a:385","type":"develops","note":"Both foster mothers set the girl's flight against a festival at home: in 385 a festival is prepared and the girl says nothing; in 397 the youth will not take her in marriage at the house where the drum never dries.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the elopement grieved as a refused home wedding, blamed in one poem on the girl's silence and in the other on the youth's haste.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:1","b":"a:0","type":"same_image","note":"Both anthology invocations show Shiva by his marks with the goddess beside him: Akananuru 0 sets Umai at his side on the bull; Kalittokai 1 has her keep the beat, measure and rhythm while he dances.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Each love anthology gives the god a partner suited to its form: the Akananuru, a book of spoken poems, seats her beside him, while the sung Kalittokai makes her the one who keeps time for his dance, so the consort's role follows the anthology's metre.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:1","b":"t:2733","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 1 names three of Shiva's dances (kodukotti, pandarangam, kapalam); Tirumanthiram 2733 lists kodukotti and pandarangam among the dances whose king he is and places them in the nadis and at Tillai.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The dance names the Tirumanthiram files into a yogic doctrine were already in the oldest love anthology as stage dances with a partner keeping time, so the later mystic list inherited a performance repertoire, not a private vision.","gr":4},{"a":"kl:2","b":"pn:122","type":"qualifies","note":"Kalittokai 2 calls the wife's chastity bright as the northern star (வடமீன் போல்) and sets her shoulders above wealth for suppliants; Purananuru 122 says Kari gives his land to brahmins and his tribute to bards and keeps as his own only the shoulders of his wife chaste as the northern star (வடமீன் புரையுங் கற்பின்).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The puram poem shows the patron's answer to the dilemma the akam friend poses: a giver can give everything away and still keep the wife's shoulders as his only possession, so generosity and staying home need not be traded off as the man in Kalittokai 2 supposes.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:2","b":"a:53","type":"tension","note":"In both the man leaves because he cannot refuse those who ask; in Akananuru 53 the wife says this means he loves wealth more than her and rejects the friend's calling it grace; in Kalittokai 2 the friend herself argues that such leaving is not love and wins.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same honourable motive is judged in opposite directions by the same two persons in different poems: the friend excuses it in one and refutes it in the other, so the giving motive was a debated case in akam, not a settled virtue.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:4","b":"k:1152","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1152 says that union, which once gave delight even at a glance, now carries the pain of fearing separation; Kalittokai 4 shows the woman reading his coming departure in caresses that exceed the usual.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem supplies the mechanism the couplet leaves out: the fear spoils union because his tenderness itself has become the sign of leaving, so the more he gives the surer she is he will go.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:5","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1151: if you are not leaving, tell me; tell your quick return to those who will still be alive. Kalittokai 5 ends: whatever day you go, on that day her life will go.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both refuse the comfort of a promised return by tying her death to the moment of departure, and the Kalittokai shows the couplet's sharp line as the close of a full argument, after images of the emptied festival ground and the lotus out of water have prepared it.","gr":4},{"a":"kl:6","b":"kl:13","type":"develops","note":"In Kalittokai 13 the man lists what the waste would do to her if she came; in 6 the woman answers such a list by asking for hardship as her companion.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the danger catalogue is a convention both lovers know how to answer: in 13 the friend discounts it as teasing, in 6 the woman turns it into the reason to go together, so the list deters no one.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:8","b":"k:81","type":"develops","note":"Kural 81 says the whole of living at home and guarding one's goods is for feeding guests; Kalittokai 8 ends that living in one's own house, protected by the king and tending guests, is itself wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend turns the Kural's account of what wealth is for into an argument against the wealth journey: if a household already feeds its guests, it already has what wealth exists to buy.","gr":5},{"a":"kl:8","b":"k:333","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 333 says wealth does not stay, so do at once what does; Kalittokai 8 says wealth is less stable than a lute, Fortune or a cruel king, so do not go after it.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"From the same premise the two draw different conclusions: the Kural says to spend unstable wealth on lasting good, the love poem says not to leave love to seek it, so impermanence argued for staying home as well as for giving.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:9","b":"a:7","type":"same_image","note":"Both have a mother figure following an eloped girl and asking those on the road whether they saw her: in Akananuru 7 the foster mother asks a doe and a hill girl; in Kalittokai 9 the mother asks brahmin ascetics, who answer.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Akananuru leaves the question unanswered on the empty road, while the Kalittokai brings in renouncers to answer it with a doctrine, so the later anthology turns the mother's lament into a debate the elopement wins.","gr":3},{"a":"kl:9","b":"a:263","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 263 the mother says that had she known her daughter's mind she would have married her to the youth; in Kalittokai 9 the ascetics tell the mother that following the chosen man is the way of virtue.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the Akananuru mother reaches only as regret, the Kalittokai states as a principle from outside the family: the girl's choice is rightful, so both books grant the eloping daughter the moral case against her parents.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:10","b":"k:557","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 557 likens the king's lack of grace to the world's lack of rain; Kalittokai 10 likens the drought-dried waste to a world under a crooked-sceptred king whose men rob and kill.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The comparison runs both ways: the ethics book explains tyranny by drought and the love poet explains drought by tyranny, so the bond between bad rule and failed rain was common enough to be used as vehicle or as tenor.","gr":5},{"a":"kl:11","b":"a:59","type":"tension","note":"Both use an elephant who gives food to his mate on the road the man took; in Kalittokai 11 the woman concludes that such a forest will bring him back; in Akananuru 59 the friend sets the same care against the men who do not remember (உள்ளார்).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"One image of animal care is made to argue both for and against the absent man's faithfulness, so the force of the iraicchi depends on the speaker's purpose, not on the creature.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:11","b":"a:34","type":"develops","note":"In Kalittokai 11 the woman infers that the man who saw a stag shade its doe will come home; in Akananuru 34 the man, having seen a stag feed and guard its doe, urges his charioteer home.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The woman's inference is borne out from the man's side in the other anthology: the creature's care on the road does turn the traveller homeward, so the iraicchi is a shared convention both lovers trust.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:12","b":"n:20","type":"tension","note":"Both say death comes unseen and no one knows the last day; the Naladiyar concludes that one must lay up provisions of virtue in time, Kalittokai 12 that one must not let the days of embrace pass for wealth.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same reminder of death is used to opposite ends: the Jain moralist spends the uncertain time on merit, the love poet on love, so the memento mori did not belong to renunciation alone.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:14","b":"k:247","type":"tension","note":"Kural 247 states that this world is not for those without wealth; Kalittokai 14 puts the like saying (காதலார் எவன் செய்ப பொருள் இல்லாதார்க்கு) in the mouths of strangers and rejects it.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"A maxim the Kural states as fact is quoted in the love poem as hostile outsiders' talk, so the wealth maxim was public wisdom that akam speakers could set against love and refuse.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:14","b":"k:113","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 113 says to give up at once gain made by leaving fairness, even if it brings good; Kalittokai 14 says wealth made by leaving the straight way is an enemy in this life and the next.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural allows that unfair gain may bring good and asks for sacrifice anyway, while the love poem denies it brings good in either world, so the woman's argument is stronger than the ethics book's rule.","gr":3},{"a":"kl:15","b":"n:17","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 17 says youth falls like fruit, so do not desire a woman for her eyes; Kalittokai 15 says wealth can be made any day but youth cannot be brought back, so do not leave her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"From the same fact that youth passes, the moralist counsels turning from desire and the love poet counsels holding fast to it, so the two books read the brevity of youth as a reason for opposite lives.","gr":6},{"a":"kl:16","b":"k:55","type":"develops","note":"Kural 55 says the wife who worships her husband commands rain; Kalittokai 16 calls the woman the chaste one who gives rain to the world in drought and says virtue will not let her pale.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The power the Kural states as a marvel works in the love poem as an argument: a woman who can bring rain cannot be left to waste, so the belief served to bind husbands as well as to praise wives.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:17","b":"a:5","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 5 the man says he gave up the journey because of his wife's silent, tearful sign; in Kalittokai 17 the friend says her own words, like medicine, made him give it up.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The same outcome is credited to whoever speaks the poem, the husband to the wife's mute grief and the friend to her own argument, so the given-up journey is a shared ending each voice claims.","gr":3},{"a":"kl:18","b":"a:51","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 51 the man tells his heart that wealth won by parting is not worth having and he will stay and hold her; in Kalittokai 18 the woman tells him the same and adds that even sharing one cloth, the united live the only true life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The case against the wealth journey is made from both sides, and the woman's version goes further by accepting poverty outright, which the man's version never has to face.","gr":3},{"a":"kl:20","b":"kl:13","type":"tension","note":"In 13 the man tells the woman that if she came the stones would blacken her lotus soles, the beasts' cries would startle her and the hot wind would wither her; in 20 the woman takes the same objections (her feet, no water, no shade, the heat) and denies that any of them weighs against going with him.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Held together the two poems stage both sides of one dispute: the man argues from her body's frailty, the woman from her life's shortness and the deer pair that cross together, so the paalaikkali treats the wife's right to share the road as a question it argues, not a given."},{"a":"kl:20","b":"a:133","type":"tension","note":"In Akananuru 133 the man once asked the woman whether she could come with him, then crossed alone the forest where the twisted-horned stag keeps his young doe; in Kalittokai 20 the woman asks to go and the man refuses, saying the forest is too hot, while she points to the stag and doe that go together.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The two poems give opposite men, one who invites idly and one who forbids, while the deer pair on the road answers both: whoever speaks, the creatures of the waste show the journey can be shared, so the stag and doe work as the paalai poets' standing proof against the lone journey."},{"a":"kl:21","b":"k:619","type":"tension","note":"The friend argues that wealth moves from one to another by former deeds (பழ வினை), so leaving to earn it is pointless; Kural 619 holds that even when fate denies, effort pays the wage of the body's labour.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend uses the doctrine of former deeds to keep the man home, while the Kural grants that doctrine only to deny that it excuses idleness, so the same belief about fate served the akam poets as an argument against striving and the moralist as one to be overruled."},{"a":"kl:21","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"In both the friend (for one Kural commentator) tells the man who announces his going that the woman will not survive him: 'tell your quick return to those who will be alive' and 'she will not live a blink without you'.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's bare refusal of comfort becomes in the kali an argued case: the friend first sets his two promises against each other and shows that wealth cannot be earned, and only then says the girl will not live, so the Kural's line reads as the last move of a dispute the long poem spells out."},{"a":"kl:22","b":"n:45","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 45 scorns men who praise teeth as jasmine buds, having seen fallen teeth in the burning ground; in Kalittokai 22 the woman asks the man who praised her teeth like jasmine buds whether he praised them falling out.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both deflate the same compliment with the same decay, but to opposite ends: the ascetic uses the fallen teeth to free a man from desire, the wife uses them to bind him to her for life, so the image of the body's ruin cut both for and against staying with a woman.","gr":3},{"a":"kl:22","b":"kl:14","type":"develops","note":"In 14 the woman says his past praise of her shoulders, eyes, teeth, brow, hair and breasts has turned to estrangement; in 22 she asks whether he has praised the same teeth, hair and breasts as they will be when old.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The two poems run the same catalogue of praise through time: 14 looks back at praise already withdrawn, 22 looks ahead to the aged body, so together they make the lover's blazon a promise that covers the whole life of the body, which leaving for wealth would break.","gr":3},{"a":"kl:24","b":"k:1155","type":"develops","note":"In both the woman tells her friend, before the man leaves, that she cannot survive his going: Kural 1155 asks her to stop it since union will be impossible once he goes, and Kalittokai 24 says her life has already gone with him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The kali adds how she knows and why she yields: she learned of the journey from his sleep-talk and gives up her life to save his name from blame, so the couplet's plea to prevent the departure is shown coming from a woman who has already lost the chance to plead."},{"a":"kl:25","b":"k:557","type":"develops","note":"Kural 557: as the lack of rain is to the earth, so the king's lack of grace (அளி) is to those who live; Kalittokai 25: as is this world when the sky withholds rain, so is her beauty when his grace (அளி) is withdrawn.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The kali poet takes the moralist's image of rain and royal grace and applies it to a husband, so the man stands to his wife as a king to his realm and his leaving becomes a failure of rule, not only of love."},{"a":"kl:25","b":"k:820","type":"develops","note":"Kural 820 warns against the bond of those who are friendly at home and slander in the assembly; Kalittokai 25 compares her eyes, which shine while he is near and weep when he goes, to the bond of mean men who praise to the face and slander behind.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend turns the moralist's type of false friend into a figure for the woman's own body, whose eyes, bangles and brow publish her state once he leaves, so the danger the Kural sees in other people becomes, in love, the betrayal of the self by its own signs."},{"a":"kl:26","b":"pn:22","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 22 praises the Chera on campaign abroad, under whose white umbrella's shade the unarmed sleep safe while he does not sleep; Kalittokai 26 has the woman say her man guards unfailingly those who come weary to his shade, praised by the world, while he lives in a stranger's land.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam woman quotes the very virtue the puram poet praises, shelter given abroad to all who seek it, and asks why it does not reach her, so the pairing shows the cost of the heroic ideal falling on the household the hero leaves."},{"a":"kl:27","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 216 likens the wealth of the generous man to a fruit tree ripening in the town; Kalittokai 27 likens the riverbank trees flourishing in spring to the wealth of a man who gives without stint and lives by virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The comparison runs one way in the Kural (wealth like a tree) and the other in the kali (trees like wealth), showing that the moral image had become common enough for a love poet to turn it round and use the giver's wealth to describe the season."},{"a":"kl:27","b":"k:639","type":"develops","note":"Kural 639 says seventy crore enemies are better than a minister at one's side who plots harm; Kalittokai 27 has spring encamp like an enemy army in the land of an unworthy king whose foolish minister let it in.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the danger of a bad counsellor as a maxim, while the kali shows its outcome, the enemy camped inside the land, and uses that sight for spring's assault on a lonely woman, which shows the statecraft maxim already worked as a picture poets could borrow."},{"a":"kl:34","b":"k:555","type":"develops","note":"Kural 555: the tears of those who cannot bear their affliction wear away the wealth of the king who causes it; Kalittokai 34: her eyes stream like the subjects under a king whose justice has failed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read with the Kural, the woman's tears carry the threat the moralist gives to the subjects' tears, that they wear down the ruler who causes them, so her weeping is an accusation against the absent husband as well as a sign of grief."},{"a":"kl:34","b":"k:788","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 788: friendship clears distress at once, as the hand of a man whose garment slips; Kalittokai 34: the lover has come on the promised day as a hand clears what troubles the eye.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both use the hand that moves to the body without thought as the figure of help that needs no asking; the Kural gives it to friendship, the kali to a husband returning on the counted day, so the reflex image measures the ideal of timely help in both public and private bonds."},{"a":"kl:37","b":"k:1257","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1257: the woman says she knows nothing of shame when her beloved does what she longs for. Kalittokai 37: the girl says that, since he could not speak and she might not, she resolved (துணிதந்து) to do one shameless deed (நாண் இன்மை செய்தேன்), staging a fall into his arms.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the loss of shame something that happens to a woman when the lover acts, while Kapilar's girl chooses the shameless act herself because the man will not, and then praises him for the restraint that sends her home; held together they show that akam allows the heroine to set aside naanam as a tactic when the rules of speech leave both lovers mute."},{"a":"kl:38","b":"kl:10","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the simile of a poor man's youth (பொருள் இல்லான் இளமை, 38 line 15; the waste's trees in 10): poem 10 measures a withered landscape by it, poem 38 the girl's face drooping after a night of fear.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The paalai poet and Kapilar draw on one stock of moral similes, but Kapilar moves it from the land to the woman's body and pairs it with its opposite (the gracious man's wealth), so the girl's face becomes a landscape that his coming can drought or rain on, which is the kali form's way of turning a moral maxim into a lover's argument for marriage.","gr":4},{"a":"kl:39","b":"k:55","type":"develops","note":"Kural 55: the wife who rises worshipping her husband, not a god, commands rain. Kalittokai 39: the girl, embraced by the man who saved her from the flood, could bring rain at need (line 6), and because hill women rise worshipping their husbands (கேள்வர்த் தொழுது எழலால்) the hunters' arrows do not miss.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's claim appears in the friend's disclosure as a belief the hill folk already hold, extended from rain to the whole hill economy and staked on a single premarital embrace, so the maxim on the wife's power was a shared folk conviction that the friend could use to argue the girl was already a faithful wife."},{"a":"kl:41","b":"k:957","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 957: a fault in the well-born stands out like the spot on the moon. Kalittokai 41: if a falsehood appeared in the hill lord's truth it would be like fire appearing in the moon (திங்களுள் தீத் தோன்றியற்று).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural uses the moon to say a noble man's fault cannot be hidden; the girls use it to say such a fault is impossible, so the same figure that warns the great serves in akam as a pledge of trust sung for the lover to overhear, binding him to live up to it."},{"a":"kl:42","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"Kural 428: not to fear what should be feared is folly, and to fear it is the work of the wise. Kalittokai 42 line 26: the friend checks her own reproach, saying he is not a lawless man who fears nothing that should be feared (அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சா அறன் இலி அல்லன்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's prudence about danger is turned by the friend into a moral standard for a lover, where what should be feared is breaking a girl's heart, and failing it makes a man அறன் இலி; the pair shows the maxim already working as a test of character in love, not only in counsel."},{"a":"kl:43","b":"k:230","type":"develops","note":"Kural 230: nothing is more bitter than dying, yet even that is sweet when giving is out of reach. Kalittokai 43 lines 26-27: the lover is praised as one who, if he could not fill the want of one who told him of poverty, would give up his body (தன் மெய் துறப்பான்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's ranking of giving above life is sung by the girls as a fact about one man to cure a girl's wasting, so the ethical maxim served in akam as the highest praise a lover could receive, and the praise itself works the cure before he comes."},{"a":"kl:44","b":"k:980","type":"develops","note":"Kural 980: greatness hides another's weakness, smallness tells only faults. Kalittokai 44: the girl hid the man's ungraciousness from her friend, the street and her companions, ashamed that others would blame him, and the friend calls this a nature rare even in a mother.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's mark of greatness is shown in the one wronged, a girl hiding her lover's fault, and the friend's report of it becomes a reproach, since the man learns that the greater one in the affair is she; the pair shows the maxim working as leverage in a love quarrel."},{"a":"kl:45","b":"k:1112","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1112: the man scolds his heart for reeling at flowers, thinking her eyes resemble what many can see. Kalittokai 45: the friend asks whether he ruined the girl's eyes because they resembled the flowers of his hill's springs (மலர் ஏய்க்கும்). On one reading the claims are incompatible: the Kural's lover refuses the likeness as beneath her, Kalittokai's friend holds him to it as the motive of harm.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The flower comparison that the Kural's lover rejects to honour the girl is used by the friend against the man as an accusation, so the same convention of beauty can be denied as praise or pressed as a charge, depending on whether the lover or the friend wields it."},{"a":"kl:46","b":"k:1192","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1192: the grace a lover shows the one who loves is like rain to those who live by it. Kalittokai 46: the girl longed for his grace like the bird that sings up to the sky for raindrops (துளி நசை ... அளி நசை), yet it was a stormy midnight (கனை பெயல் நடுநாள்) that kept him from the tryst.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural's image of grace as rain is set in Kalittokai against real rain, which is what stopped the meeting, so the girl craves one rain while the other keeps it from her; the pairing shows the kali poet testing a stock equivalence against the weather of the kurinji night."},{"a":"kl:47","b":"k:1133","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1133: the man says he once had shame and sound manliness and now has the palmyra horse. Kalittokai 47: the girl says a man of great strength and restraint has put off his manliness (தன் ஆண்தகை விட்டு) to tell her he cannot live without her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the lover's own account of losing his manliness, the threat behind the madal, while Kalittokai gives the girl's hearing of it, weighing whether to believe it and what consenting would cost her name, so the pair shows both sides of the same pressure in secret love."},{"a":"kl:48","b":"ku:3","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 3: the girl says her bond with the hill lord is larger than the earth, higher than the sky, deeper than the sea. Kalittokai 48: the friend tells the hill lord that the girl's grief, weighed, is greater than the hill whose bounds none knows (மலையினும் பெரிதே).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same kurinji measure by immensity that the girl uses for her love the friend uses for its cost, so the pair shows that the size of the bond and the size of the grief at his absence are one quantity, and the friend can turn the heroine's boast into a charge against the man."},{"a":"kl:49","b":"a:308","type":"develops","note":"Both are the friend telling the hill lord not to come by night but by day (இரவின் வாரல் ... பகலும் பெறுவை; இரவின் வருதல் எவனோ? பகல்வரின்), and both open on a tusker after a fight with a tiger, wounded in Akananuru 308, dreaming and ashamed in Kalittokai 49.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 308 turns the refusal into a full plan of the day tryst, while Kalittokai 49 builds a triple refrain that calls his love sweet and his coming bitter, so the pair shows the same convention used once as an invitation and once to separate the lover from his risk, with the tiger-fight's aftermath serving each as the danger of the night path.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:51","b":"k:1095","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1095: the man says she does not look at him directly but smiles with one eye narrowed. Kalittokai 51: the girl says the young man looked at her from the corner of his eye as if to kill and smiled (கடைக் கண்ணால் ... நகை கூட்டம் செய்தான்).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural reads the sidelong smile as the girl's sign of consent, seen by the man; Kalittokai gives the same sign to the man, seen by the girl after she has lied for him, so the gesture of kurippu is not tied to one sex and seals a secret both have now chosen."},{"a":"kl:54","b":"pn:165","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 165 opens with those who wished to last in the unlasting world (மன்னா உலகத்து மன்னுதல் குறித்தோர்) and set up their fame; Kalittokai 54 ends with the girl wanting it said she acted from love, which in the unlasting world (மன்னா உலகத்து) is what lasts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam heroine takes over the puram formula for lasting fame and applies it to a woman's choice of lover, so the Sangam ideal of a name that outlives one could be earned by love as well as by giving."},{"a":"kl:55","b":"k:1117","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1117 asks whether a woman's face has spots as the moon does; Kalittokai 55 has the man make the same correction six times over (not the moon, for it has no stain; not the crescent, for it does not wane), reported by the girl.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's single corrected simile is shown in the Kalittokai as a wooing technique that the girl sees through, so the man's praise-by-correction is not only admiration but a calculated approach she names as such."},{"a":"kl:56","b":"k:1081","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1081 the man, first seeing her, cannot tell if she is a goddess, a peahen or a woman; in Kalittokai 56 he asks if she is a craftsman's doll, a woman assembled from all women's best parts, or Death in a form it chose.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both stage the first sight as a failure to classify the woman, but the Kalittokai adds Death and a girl too young to know her effect, so the Kural's bewilderment turns into a complaint that her beauty is a public danger no one has announced."},{"a":"kl:58","b":"ku:32","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 32 says appearing in the street on the palm-stem horse is blame (பழியே) for the man; Kalittokai 58 has the man say riding it will fix the blame on the girl (நீ படு பழியே).","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"On a defensible reading the two disagree about whom the madal shames, the rider or the woman, which shows the threat worked as leverage precisely because the disgrace could be made to fall on her household."},{"a":"kl:61","b":"k:230","type":"develops","note":"Kural 230 says death is sweet when giving is out of reach; in Kalittokai 61 the friend says the girl, who would find it better to die than live unable to give to suppliants (ஈதல் இரந்தார்க்கு ஒன்று ஆற்றாது வாழ்தலின் சாதலும் கூடுமாம்), would give him whatever he lacks.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend's offer turns the Kural's maxim into a trap for the household: once the lover presents himself as a suppliant for grace rather than wealth, the giving ethic obliges the girl to yield.","gr":4},{"a":"kl:61","b":"k:1133","type":"agrees","note":"In Kural 1133 the man says he once had shame and manliness and now has the palm-stem horse; in Kalittokai 61 the friend says the tusker-like warrior who threatens the mocked madal is truly without shame (நாண் இலன்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the man's own confession that madal means the loss of shame and manliness; the Kalittokai gives the woman's side seeing that loss, and shows it is what persuades her that he will not stop."},{"a":"kl:62","b":"pn:12","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 12 asks the Pandya Kudumi whether it is dharma to take others' land so that it is painful to them (இன்னா ஆகப் பிறர் மண் கொண்டு) and do sweet things for his admirers; in Kalittokai 62 the girl asks whether forcing on others what pains them because it is sweet to oneself (தமக்கு இனிது என்று, வலிதின் பிறர்க்கு இன்னா செய்வது) is good.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same sweet-for-oneself, painful-for-others test is put to a conquering king and to a forceful lover, and in both the one questioned claims it as virtue (அறன்), so the Sangam poets judged seizure in war and in love by one moral rule."},{"a":"kl:62","b":"k:316","type":"tension","note":"Kural 316 says one must not do to another what one knows to be painful; in Kalittokai 62 the girl states that rule and the man answers that sweetness lies in the one who needs, and that seizing moon-faced women is virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kalittokai lets the lover contest the Kural's rule with an argument from pleasure rather than ignore it, showing that the love poets knew the non-harm maxim and staged its failure under desire.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:60","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1102 says the jewelled woman is herself the cure for the sickness she causes; in Kalittokai 60 the man tells the friend his only medicine is to see the girl's face, and there is no other.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the paradox as the man's private discovery after union; in the Kalittokai it is relayed by the friend as a plea before union, so the same conceit serves as an argument that obliges the girl to grant the cure."},{"a":"kl:58","b":"kl:61","type":"develops","note":"In Kalittokai 58 the man threatens madal to the girl herself; in 61 the friend reports the same threat, laughs at it as shameless, and still concludes they must act.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show where the madal threat lands: the girl too young to understand it in 58 cannot be moved by it, while the friend in 61 is, so the threat is effectively addressed to the household's guardians.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:67","b":"k:1284","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 67's three refrains say 'I would say I will sulk' (புலப்பேன் யான் என்பேன்மன்) and the heart says it will join him; Kural 1284 has the woman go to sulk and her heart go to unite (ஊடற்கண் சென்றேன்மன் ... கூடற்கண் சென்றது).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's couplet is one turn of a song the Kalittokai performs three times with three different marks on the man's body, and the Kalittokai names the heart's act outright as treason (அறைபோகு), showing that the couplet's quiet split between woman and heart was heard as betrayal from within."},{"a":"kl:77","b":"k:1254","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1254 says she used to call herself restrained (நிறையுடையேன் என்பேன்மன்) but desire breaks out in public; Kalittokai 77 asks whether restraint (நிறை) is easy for one who lives with an enemy, her own heart.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural blames desire in general for breaking restraint, while the Kalittokai wife places the failure in a precise moment, the sight of the unfaithful husband at her door, so the same loss of நிறை turns from a lover's confession into a wronged wife's helplessness against her own grievance."},{"a":"kl:76","b":"k:1143","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1143 welcomes the gossip the whole town knows, because it gives as if already got what is not yet got; Kalittokai 76 ends that if the town has done what their kin would do at the wedding, nothing is left to do.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both turn alar from shame into a kind of marriage, and together they show the two uses of the same claim: the Kural lover savours the gossip as a foretaste, while the Kalittokai friend uses it as an argument that the wedding is already public and must now follow."},{"a":"kl:71","b":"k:1192","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1192 likens a lover's grace to the rain that sustains those who live by it; Kalittokai 71 likens the husband's once-a-year visit to a drizzle that cannot satisfy paddy withering for want of rain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's rain stands for grace given in its season, and the Kalittokai shows the same image under neglect: a token visit is not rain but drizzle, so the wife measures grace by its sufficiency, a test the Kural's simile assumes but does not state."},{"a":"kl:74","b":"k:580","type":"tension","note":"Kural 580 praises those who, wanting a refinement worth loving, see poison poured and drink it; Kalittokai 74 calls the women who trust the husband's words though they see his graceless ways deluded, like those who eat poison knowing it kills.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Knowingly swallowing poison is a mark of courtesy in the Kural and a mark of delusion in the Kalittokai, so the same act is judged by whom it is done for: forbearance toward a friend is refinement, forbearance toward a faithless lover is folly."},{"a":"kl:68","b":"k:1215","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1215 says what was seen awake was sweet then, and the dream too is sweet while it is seen; Kalittokai 68 says the husband's embrace is like wealth won in a dream, different on waking.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Kural's lover levels waking and dream, both sweet while they last, but the Kalittokai wife uses the dream to name a possession that vanishes, so the dream simile can console a parted lover or indict an unfaithful husband depending on whether the absence is chosen."},{"a":"kl:81","b":"nt:250","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 250: the man came near his son practising walking with his little chariot in the street (தேர் நடைபயிற்றும்) and his wife shied away saying 'who are you?'; Kalittokai 81: the son practises walking with his chariot in the yard (கால் வல் தேர் கையின் இயக்க, நடை பயிற்றா) while husband and wife argue over him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Natrinai gives the husband's side in one recoil, while the Kalittokai stages the whole quarrel around the same toddling child, so together they show the son with his toy chariot as the standing occasion for the husband's return in marutham, and the wife's refusal as a scene both anthologies expected."},{"a":"kl:82","b":"a:16","type":"tension","note":"In Akananuru 16 the wife finds a courtesan clasping their son in the chariot street and gently asks if she is his mother too; in Kalittokai 82 the son's visits to the father's women, who adorn and kiss him, make the wife call for a stick.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same encounter of the son with his father's women draws grace from one wife and fury from another, showing that marutham poets used it to test the wife's karpu: the Akananuru wife proves her dignity by kindness, the Kalittokai wife her love by jealousy."},{"a":"kl:75","b":"ku:359","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 359: the friend shows the bard the man embracing his son on the bed and the wife embracing his back; Kalittokai 75: when she quarrels, he hugs the son and feigns sleep (புதல்வன் புல்லிப் பொய் துயில் துஞ்சும்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the Kuruntokai's picture of a seemly household and the Kalittokai's account of the husband's tactic show that the son held in sleep was both the sign of reconciliation and the means of forcing it, and the wife in 75 knows it and yields anyway."},{"a":"kl:79","b":"a:66","type":"tension","note":"In Akananuru 66 the husband, on his way to a new wedding, lifts the crying son to his chest and gives up the wedding; in Kalittokai 79 the wife forbids him to take up the son, since the boy would wash off the other women's marks, and sends him back to them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The husband's embrace of the son is the turning point of return in the Akananuru but is refused as a ruse in the Kalittokai, so the same gesture can end the husband's straying or be read by the wife as one more performance."},{"a":"kl:69","b":"a:86","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 86 recalls a wedding of matrons pouring water with petals and paddy and the bride curled in her new cloth (கோடிக் கலிங்கத்து ஒடுங்கினள்); Kalittokai 69 compares the swan pair to a brahmin circling the fire with his shy bride shrunk in her cloth (கலிங்கத்துள் ஒடுங்கிய).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The shy bride in her new cloth is a shared formula, but the rite around her differs: the Akananuru wedding has matrons, water and paddy and no fire, while the Kalittokai sets her beside a Veda-reciting priest circling the fire, so the two anthologies record two wedding rites in Tamil country."},{"a":"kl:80","b":"pn:92","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 92 says a son's prattle keeps no tune and makes no sense, yet moves fathers to grace; in Kalittokai 80 the mother hears the son's sweet 'attatta' but the father is away with other women.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The puram poet's comparison assumes a father present to be moved by the prattle, and the Kalittokai shows the house where he is not, so the grace the prattle should draw from the father falls to the mother and is mixed with pain.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:84","b":"a:16","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 16 the wife meets a courtesan cuddling her son in the chariot street and asks 'are you his mother too?'; in Kalittokai 84 the husband's women, the boy's 'mothers' (தாயர்), stop him in the street and load him with gifts, and the wife reads the shark ring as a message.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two poems show that the son in the street was a fixed channel between wife and courtesan: the Akananuru wife disarms the rival by granting her a mother's place, while the Kalittokai wife finds that the same motherly gesture is used to send her a sign, so the courtesy of Akananuru 16 had a hostile counterpart."},{"a":"kl:86","b":"pn:312","type":"tension","note":"Purananuru 312 assigns bearing the son to the mother and making him worthy (சான்றோன் ஆக்குதல்) to the father; in Kalittokai 86 the mother herself teaches the son which of his father's qualities to copy and which to refuse.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Read together, they show the puram division of duties failing inside the house: when the father is the bad example, the mother takes over his task of forming the son, sorting his public virtues from his private fault, which the puram poem's neat allocation never anticipates."},{"a":"kl:86","b":"k:70","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 70 says a son repays his father by making people ask what penance the father did; Kalittokai 86 has the mother wish the son to resemble the father in valour, justice and giving but not in leaving women.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural assumes the father is the standard the son's goodness reflects back on, while the akam mother makes the son's goodness a correction of the father, so the same likeness that honours a father in the Kural reproaches him in the Kalittokai."},{"a":"kl:87","b":"k:1259","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1259 the woman went to sulk and embraced him when she saw her heart joining him; in Kalittokai 87 the wife tells her heart that sulking at a shameless man is pointless and gives up her complaint.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both wives abandon the sulk through the heart, but for opposite reasons: in the Kural the heart's desire overrides her, in the Kalittokai the heart concludes that a man without shame cannot be moved by sulking, so ending an oodal can be resignation as well as longing."},{"a":"kl:88","b":"k:1313","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1313 the man complains that even if he wears a garland she accuses him of wearing it for another woman; in Kalittokai 88 the wife points to his faded garland, the pollen of other women's hair and the nail marks on him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural presents the wife's suspicion of a garland as groundless jealousy from the man's side, while the Kalittokai wife reads the same evidence correctly, so the couplet's comic complaint rests on an akam scene in which the garland really was a sign."},{"a":"kl:89","b":"k:1325","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1325 has a faultless man delight in being kept from his beloved's arms; in Kalittokai 89 the husband says he has done no wrong (தீதோ இலேன்) and the wife calls the claim deceit and expects him to fall at her feet confessing.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Kural builds the pleasure of oodal on the man's real innocence, while the Kalittokai shows the same plea of innocence as the stock defence of a straying husband, so the couplet's playful exclusion presupposes a faultlessness that akam wives treat as the thing in dispute."},{"a":"kl:90","b":"k:1260","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1260 asks whether those whose hearts melt like fat in fire can say 'we will join and then sulk'; Kalittokai 90 ends with the wife asking whether women whose hearts cannot hold restraint (நிறை ஆற்றா நெஞ்சு) can say they will not bear his wrongs.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kalittokai gives the couplet's rhetorical question a full case: a detailed charge of a night visit, his dream defence and his plea, so that the wife's collapse comes after she has proved him guilty, showing that the melting heart of the Kural overrides evidence and not only pride."},{"a":"kl:91","b":"k:1288","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1288 the woman says his chest is to her as liquor is to drinkers, though it does shameful harm; Kalittokai 91 ends with the wife saying her heart melts when she sees him whatever wrong he does, so he need not try to please her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the compulsion as the woman's confession, while the Kalittokai draws its consequence for the man: because she always yields, he has no motive to behave, so the wife's helplessness becomes part of the reason the straying continues.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:93","b":"pr:5:1-21","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 5 opens by hailing the spear that cut down Sur; in Kalittokai 93 one of the husband's 'goddesses' sang of the red-speared slayer of Sur with him on the hill through the rains.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the Murugan hymn in two settings: in the Paripadal it is temple praise, while the Kalittokai places the same theme in a lovers' pastime on the hill in the rainy season, so devotional song at Murugan's hill was also an occasion for courtship that the wife can use as evidence."},{"a":"kl:98","b":"a:296","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 296 the friend refuses the man who bathed in the great Vaigai with a dark girl and comes home with his chest still wet; in Kalittokai 98 the man claims he only bathed in the Vaigai's new flood and the wife turns the flood itself into the courtesan.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Akananuru records water play in the Vaigai as the plain occasion of an affair and its gossip, while the Kalittokai husband offers that same occasion as an innocent excuse and the wife answers by making the river a figure for the woman, so a known scene of straying becomes the material for the wife's metaphor."},{"a":"kl:99","b":"pn:35","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 35 tells Killivalavan that his tall umbrella is not for shade from the sun but to shelter suffering subjects, and that rain comes in the land of the just sceptre; Kalittokai 99 asks the husband, addressed as king, whether his wife fell outside the shade of the parasol he calls virtue's shade and the working of his sceptre.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend in the Kalittokai uses the same counsel a puram poet gave a king, that the parasol exists to shade those who suffer, and turns it on the household, showing that the akam poets could hold a husband to the standard of kingship and find him failing his nearest subject."},{"a":"kl:99","b":"k:559","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 559 says that if the ruler bends justice the sky withholds rain; Kalittokai 99 praises the king for rains that come by his rightness and then asks whether his wife alone lies outside that rightness.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural ties rain to the king's public justice only, while the Kalittokai grants the rain and still finds injustice at home, so the friend shows that the doctrine's measure of a ruler can be met in full while a wife is wronged."},{"a":"kl:100","b":"pn:55","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 55 wishes the Pandya the heat of the sun, the coolness of the moon and the giving of the rain; Kalittokai 100 praises the husband, as king, with the sun's harshness to foes, the moon's coolness to those under his parasol and the rain's giving, then says each praise is ruined by his wife's tears, bangles and pallor.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The akam friend takes the stock triad of royal praise found in the Purananuru and makes each term hostage to a woman's body, showing that the same encomium could be turned into a reproach by asking whether its subject meets it at home."},{"a":"kl:101","b":"pn:342","type":"qualifies","note":"Kalittokai 101 says the herd girl's shoulders go to the one who seizes a bull that does not fear even a rutting tusker (கடாஅக் களிற்றினும் கண்ணஞ்சா ஏற்றை); Purananuru 342 says the chieftain's daughter's beauty is for fighters alone (பொருநர்க்கு அல்லது பிறர்க்கு ஆகாதே), her brothers threshing the battlefield with elephants for oxen.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both give the girl only to proven courage, but the chieftain's family proves it in a war that uses elephants as threshing oxen, while the herders stage the proof on their own working animal inside a fenced pen, so the mullai contest is the puram bride-test turned from a siege that ruins the town into a bounded festival."},{"a":"kl:103","b":"pn:343","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 343: the father refuses even wealth like Kuttuvan's Musiri humbly offered, and suitors' armies crowd the town; Kalittokai 103: the herd girls say they want no price (விலை வேண்டார்), only a man who will fall between the killer bull's horns as between their breasts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they show who sets the bride's terms: in the puram town the father refuses wealth and the girl becomes the cause of a war, while in the herders' song the girls voice the refusal themselves and name the one price they accept, so the mullai poem gives the woman the choice the puram poem leaves to her father and the kings."},{"a":"kl:104","b":"pn:2","type":"same_image","note":"Kalittokai 104 likens the pen strewn with gored men to the field where the five archers killed the hundred (நூற்றுவர் மடங்க... ஐவர் அட்ட பொரு களம்); Purananuru 2 praises the Chera who gave food without limit while the hundred fought the five and fell on the field.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Bharata war that Purananuru 2 treats as a dated occasion for one king's generosity is already, in Kalittokai, a stock picture of any heap of the fallen, used even for a village bull-catch, so the epic had passed from a remembered event into the common image store of the poets."},{"a":"kl:104","b":"k:1150","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1150: the friend says the town raises the very gossip they want, and the lover will grant marriage if asked; Kalittokai 104: the friend tells the girl that her kin have gladly agreed to give her to the herder who leapt on the black bull, trampling on the heads of the town that raised the gossip (அலர் எடுத்த ஊராரை உச்சி மிதித்து).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet hopes that the town's talk will move the lover to marry; the herders' poem shows a second way gossip ends, through the kin's public consent once the bull is taken, so in the mullai village it is the girl's family the gossip moves, and the wedding then silences the town."},{"a":"kl:105","b":"k:1147","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1147: the sickness of love grows long with the town's gossip for manure and the mother's words for water; Kalittokai 105: the girl asks what joy equals this, that her mother beat her with a stick and the town made gossip linking her to the herder (யாய் என்னைக் கண் உடைக் கோலள் அலைத்ததற்கு... அலர் செய்துவிட்டது இவ் ஊர்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same two forces, the town's talk and the mother's harshness, feed a growing pain in the couplet but are welcomed as joy by the herd girl, because once the bond is public her marriage follows from the bull-catch; whether exposure hurts depends on whether an open road to marriage lies ahead."},{"a":"kl:106","b":"pn:278","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 278: the old mother, told her son fled, searches the field and rejoices more than at his birth to find him cut to pieces; Kalittokai 106: the herd girls sing that they will bind the bull-gored wounds against their breasts and that the tamer's blood on their curd-spotted shoulders is an ornament.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akam takes over the puram pride in wounds taken facing the foe and moves it from the mother to the beloved: the wound that proves in Purananuru 278 that a son died well proves in Kalittokai 106 that a man is fit to be embraced, so the herd girls' desire is built on the warrior ethic."},{"a":"kl:107","b":"kl:115","type":"develops","note":"In Kalittokai 107 the bull tosses the herder's mullai chaplet into the girl's hair by chance; in 115 she herself tied his chaplet in her hair and it fell before her mother; in both the kin then resolve to give her to him (அடை சூழ்ந்தார்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same outcome from accident in one poem and from the girl's own act in the other shows that among the herders the man's flowers in a girl's hair bind her however they got there: the kin read the first as a god's sign and the second as a fact to be ratified, and both end in betrothal."},{"a":"kl:108","b":"k:1083","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1083: the man says he now knows Death, which has a woman's grace and big eyes that make war (பேரமர்க் கட்டு); Kalittokai 108: the man calls the girl the warrior who took up arms against his life, and mocks her trust in her mullai-bud teeth and battle eyes (பேர் அமர் உண் கண்ணும்), while she answers back and at last sets the tryst.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's lone confession of being slain by her eyes appears in the herders' poem as a line of banter that the girl hears, throws back and answers with a meeting place, which shows the conceit of the killing eye at work as courtship speech addressed to the woman, not only as the man's inner wonder."},{"a":"kl:109","b":"k:1081","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1081: on first seeing her the man cannot tell whether she is a goddess (அணங்கு), a peahen or a woman; Kalittokai 109: the man watching the buttermilk girl reports that the town's wives, judging her fit to be an anangu to everyone (யார்க்கும் அணங்கு ஆதல் சான்றாள்), keep their husbands in and shut their doors when she comes.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Beside the couplet, the herders' poem shows that the word அணங்கு named a real danger, a beauty that strikes whoever sees it: what is one man's private bewilderment in the Kural becomes in Kalittokai a judgment the whole town shares and acts on by guarding its men."},{"a":"kl:111","b":"a:180","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 180: while the girls play at sand houses, a man drives up, hangs a kuvalai wreath down her braid unasked, gazes and leaves, and the town starts talking; Kalittokai 111: a herder offers to build the girl's play-house, tie a garland in her hair and paint her breasts, and she refuses each, saying a girl who takes a stranger's flowers is a great fool (ஏதிலார் தந்த பூக் கொள்வாய்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The herd girl's refusal states the rule the shore poem leaves unsaid, that a stranger's flowers in a girl's hair are a claim on her; that rule is why the silent wreath of Akananuru 180 sets the town talking, and why the herd girl answers the same offer by asking her friend to tell her parents.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:114","b":"a:86","type":"tension","note":"Akananuru 86 presents the rite (sand spread under the pandal, தருமணல் ஞெமிரி; the matrons' blessing of chastity; the kin giving the bride, தமர்தர) as the start of the marriage; in Kalittokai 114 the girl, seeing her kin prepare a wedding with sand spread low (தரு மணல் தாழப் பெய்து), says she knows only the one union she made among her play-house companions and that two weddings are against the nature of herd girls. On this reading the rite that makes a marriage in 86 makes none in 114.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two show that akam could place the binding moment of marriage either in the kin's sand-strewn rite or before it in the secret union, so the same ceremony that founds a wife's chastity in Akananuru 86 would, given to another man, be a forbidden second marriage for the herd girl."},{"a":"kl:118","b":"k:1229","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1229 says that when evening spreads the whole town is bewildered and suffers; Kalittokai 118's refrain says evening torments those parted but cannot dim (கடிகல்லாய்) the beauty, sweet speech and smile of lovers who are together.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read beside the poem, the couplet's 'the whole town suffers' shows itself as the lonely woman's projection, since the kali poem insists evening harms only the parted, so the grief belongs to separation and not to the hour."},{"a":"kl:119","b":"k:1221","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1221 tells the hour it is not evening but the time that eats the lives of the wedded; Kalittokai 119, after a long catalogue of dusk, says those who call it evening are deluded, not knowing it is the hour that unwraps women's lives.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet compresses into one address the argument the kali poem builds through fifteen lines of homing birds, closing flowers and lit fires, so the Kural's 'not evening but a killing hour' is a summary of an established neythal rebuttal rather than a fresh conceit.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:119","b":"ku:234","type":"develops","note":"Both close on the same half-line, மாலை என்மனார் மயங்கியோரே; Kuruntokai 234 says that for the lonely even dawn and day are evening, Kalittokai 119 says that evening is really the hour that undoes women's lives.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared closing formula was a set rebuttal that could be turned two ways: the short poem stretches evening over the whole day, the long one narrows it to a killing hour, so 'those who call it evening are deluded' was a commonplace the poets argued with, not one poet's phrase."},{"a":"kl:120","b":"k:1224","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1224: in the lover's absence evening comes like enemies to a killing field; Kalittokai 120 makes evening a hunter aiming at a deer, a mocker of the beaten, a man pushing a spear into a burn, and Death grinning with the crescent as its tooth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem unfolds the couplet's single image of evening as executioner into a series of cruelties each aimed at someone already wounded, which shows that the Kural's 'like enemies' means an attack on the defenceless, not just a hostile arrival."},{"a":"kl:120","b":"kl:129","type":"develops","note":"Both use the reign of a weak ruler (மெல்லியான் பருவம்) for nightfall: in 129 the confused dark comes like the weak reign after a just king; in 120 the cruel evening vanishes as the enemy of a weak reign is driven off when a good king appears, the lover's return.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same poet places one political figure at both ends of the night, darkness arriving as misrule after a just king and lifting when a rightful king returns, so in these poems the absent lover stands in the place of the king and his coming restores order, not only pleasure.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:122","b":"k:1246","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1246 tells the heart its anger is false, since it cannot stay angry on seeing the man who ends quarrels by union; Kalittokai 122 has the woman, chided for sulking, say she knows he keeps other women, yet when he tends her a little her shameless heart melts and is calmed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The kali poem supplies the setting the couplet leaves out, a friend mocking her sulks as causeless, so the Kural's confession that the heart's anger is false turns out to answer that reproach: she knows his fault fully, and her heart still gives way."},{"a":"kl:123","b":"k:1248","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1248 calls the heart a fool for following the man who left while lamenting his lack of grace; Kalittokai 123 asks the simple heart (மட நெஞ்சே) that went to him at night whether it saw, embraced or knew him, and pities it for tossing like the backwater tide.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem shows what the couplet's 'you go after him' looks like as an event, the heart's nightly journey met by three questions it cannot answer, so the Kural's rebuke of the foolish heart is the end of a scene in which the heart keeps coming back empty."},{"a":"kl:124","b":"k:1235","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1235: the friend says the shoulders, bangles gone and old beauty faded, declare the cruel man's cruelty; Kalittokai 124: the friend tells the man the woman hid her pain even from her, but her pale forehead, wasted shoulders and streaming eyes could not hide it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the body as a witness that overrides the woman's own silence: the poem adds that she tried to keep the pain even from her closest friend, so what the couplet calls the shoulders 'declaring' is a betrayal of her will to conceal, which the friend then turns against the man."},{"a":"kl:125","b":"k:293","type":"develops","note":"Kural 293: do not lie about what your own heart knows, for after the lie your heart burns you; Kalittokai 125 opens with the maxim that those who think no one saw may hide what their heart knows, but no witness is closer than the heart, and then charges the lover.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam friend turns the ethical maxim into a weapon of courtship: the Kural's inner witness, a general law of truthfulness, becomes the ground on which she charges a lover with knowing his own neglect, so the doctrine of conscience was already available to love poetry as rhetoric."},{"a":"kl:126","b":"k:1218","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1218: when I sleep he is on my shoulder, when I wake he runs into my heart; Kalittokai 126: the friend says the woman, thinking of him, believes him on her shoulder (தோள் மேலாய்), embraces him as if awake, finds nothing and knows it was a dream.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem tells the couplet's moment from outside and counts its cost: what the Kural's woman offers as a defence of her lover (he never leaves me) the friend reports as the last of three daily delusions ending in grief, so the shoulder-dream is comfort in the first person and harm in the third."},{"a":"kl:127","b":"k:1157","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1157: the bangles slipping past her wrist announce that the lord of the shore has left; Kalittokai 127: the friend tells the lord of the shore that her bangles slip (இறை வளை நெகிழ்ந்த) and asks him to give grace as the wave shelters the adumbu creeper.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet has the bangles accuse the man to the woman herself; the poem has the friend carry the same evidence to the man's face and pair it with a remedy, so the slipping bangle moves from private proof of desertion to an argument for his return.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:128","b":"k:1213","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1213: my life lasts because I see in dreams the man who gives no grace awake; Kalittokai 128: the woman tells of a dream in which she quarrelled with the man and he bowed at her feet, and says her life held at the brink hoping he would come awake too.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem shows the mechanism the couplet only asserts: the dream keeps her alive by letting her act out the quarrel and his submission and by feeding the hope of a real coming, so the Kural's 'my life lasts' rests on both satisfaction and expectation."},{"a":"kl:128","b":"ku:30","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 30 calls the dream of the lover's embrace a lying dream (பொய்க்கனா) that deceived her and left her wasted; Kalittokai 128 says the dream in which he came kept her life from going, in hope he may come awake. On a plain reading one treats the dream as deceit, the other as ground for hope.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The same dream of the lover is judged a cruel lie in one poem and the thing that keeps the woman alive in the other, so akam poets held no single view of dreams and used the dream's truth or falseness to mark whether hope survived."},{"a":"kl:129","b":"k:1222","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1222 asks the bewildered evening whether its partner is as hard as hers; Kalittokai 129 asks the sea, the anril on the palmyra and the little flute in turn whether, like her, they have someone who loved and left them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem shows the couplet's question as one move in a round of addresses to sea, bird and music, so the Kural's single address to evening reads as a compressed survival of a sung form in which each sound of nightfall is asked whether it too was abandoned."},{"a":"kl:130","b":"k:1227","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1227: the sickness buds at morning and blooms at evening; Kalittokai 130: as the great backwater flowers close at evening, her heart closes in ruin (அழிவொடு கூம்பும்).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The two invert one flower image: the couplet makes the evening sickness bloom, the poem makes the heart shut with the evening flowers, so love's pain at dusk could be figured as opening or as closing, and the Kural's paradox depends on reversing the shore's ordinary evening closure."},{"a":"kl:132","b":"k:1154","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1154: if the man who said 'do not fear' leaves, is the fault in those who trusted his word; Kalittokai 132: the friend asks the man whether the woman's pallor and lost beauty are not the fruit of his 'do not fear' (அஞ்சல் ஓம்பு) and 'trust me' (தெளி) at their secret meetings.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's question of who bears the fault is answered in the poem by the friend, who reads each reassurance as the direct cause of a named loss, so the Kural's moral question has a speech situation behind it in which the promise is handed back to the man as a debt."},{"a":"kl:133","b":"k:153","type":"qualifies","note":"Kalittokai 133 defines அறிவு as bearing the words of fools (பேதையார் சொல் நோன்றல்) and பொறை as bearing with those who do not cherish you; Kural 153 calls bearing with fools the strength of strengths.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The same act is filed under different virtues: the kali friend calls forbearance toward fools wisdom and keeps patience for those who neglect you, while the Kural calls it strength, so this forbearance had no fixed place yet in the lists of virtues, standing under wisdom in one and strength in the other."},{"a":"kl:133","b":"k:541","type":"agrees","note":"Kalittokai 133: justice (முறை) is taking life without favour (கண்ணோடாது உயிர் வௌவல்); Kural 541: justice is to examine, show favour to no one (கண்ணோடாது) and act after weighing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both define justice with the same word கண்ணோடாது, but the love poem gives its harshest form, taking life, in a list meant to shame a lover, so the definition of முறை as unbending punishment was common coin a friend could quote, and the Kural's procedural version tempers a stock definition centred on the death sentence.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:134","b":"k:1164","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1164: the sea of love is there but there is no raft of safety to cross it; Kalittokai 134: her grief ends as a man whose ship broke in a shoreless sea is saved by a raft the waves bring, the lovers hastening home. One says no raft exists, the other that the sea itself brings one.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The two use the same sea and raft with opposite outcomes, which shows that in love the raft is not something the sufferer can find for herself: the couplet's woman has none, and in the poem it arrives only with the beloved's return."},{"a":"kl:135","b":"k:957","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 135's friend tells the shore lord that abandoning the girl would lie as a great fault on his lineage (குடிமைக் கண் பெரியது ஓர் குற்றம்); Kural 957 says a fault in the well-born stands out high like the spot on the moon.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's rule that high birth makes a fault more visible is what gives the friend's threat its force: she aims at his lineage rather than his love because a lord's desertion, unlike a commoner's, would be seen by all, so the maxim is already a working argument in akam persuasion."},{"a":"kl:136","b":"k:932","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 136 likens the girl's joy at each meeting to a gambler's winning throw and her grief at his leaving to the loss of a stake; Kural 932 says the gambler gains one and loses a hundred.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Read with the couplet's arithmetic of the dice, the friend's simile becomes a warning: a secret love that runs on wins and losses must end in ruin on balance, which is why she ends by telling him to take the girl in marriage and stop the game."},{"a":"kl:137","b":"k:1159","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 137 says his gentleness burns her worse than fire (தீயினும் கடிது); Kural 1159 says fire burns only when touched, while love's sickness burns when the lover is away.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The couplet states as a single paradox what the kali poem reaches through three graded steps (word above bow, kindness above enmity, gentleness above fire), so the Kural's compressed contrast between fire and love was the end point of an argued rhetoric the longer form still displays."},{"a":"kl:138","b":"k:1135","type":"agrees","note":"Kalittokai 138's madal rider sings that grief and the palmyra horse are what she gave (படரும் பனை ஈன்ற மாவும் நல்கியவை); in Kural 1135 the man says the girl gave him the madal and evening's anguish.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both men list the madal among the woman's gifts, so the convention made the beloved the author of the lover's public shame; the kali poem adds the outcome the couplet leaves open, her coming in grace, which shows the gift was meant to be returned."},{"a":"kl:139","b":"k:1131","type":"agrees","note":"Kalittokai 139 calls the horse she gave a shelter (ஏமம்) against the army of love; Kural 1131 says that for those who suffer in love there is no safeguard (ஏமம்) and no strength but the madal.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both use the same word ஏமம் for an act of humiliation, and the kali poem shows how the safeguard works: it is spoken to the worthies whose duty is to treat another's pain as their own, so the madal protects by putting the assembly under obligation."},{"a":"kl:139","b":"k:1134","type":"qualifies","note":"Kalittokai 139's rider swims the sea of love with the madal as his raft (மடல் புணையா நீந்துவேன்); Kural 1134 says the fierce flood of love carries off the raft of shame and manliness.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Where the couplet's flood sweeps away the raft of shame, the kali lover takes the shameful act itself as his raft, so the two together show the madal as what is left when shame has failed: a raft made of the loss of one.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:140","b":"k:576","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 140's rider complains that the town sees his sickness and does not soften (கண்டும் கண்ணோடாது இவ் ஊர்); Kural 576 says those who have eyes and do not turn them kindly are like trees fixed in the soil.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The madal rider appeals to the civic virtue the Kural names கண்ணோட்டம், so the madal is shown to work through the town's duty of kindly regard, and the town that will not soften is, by the Kural's measure, as insensible as a tree."},{"a":"kl:141","b":"k:1133","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 141's rider says she made him, who rides the war horse in battle, ride the madal to the common; in Kural 1133 the man says he once had shame and manliness and now has the madal.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's before-and-after inventory is spelled out in the kali poem as three lost ranks (warrior, protector, orator), which shows that the manliness the Kural says is lost is a public standing, not only a private virtue.","gr":2},{"a":"kl:142","b":"k:1138","type":"agrees","note":"Kalittokai 142's girl, once laughing with hidden teeth, now laughs aloud and weeps so all hear and sings her love to the worthies; Kural 1138 says love does not stop to weigh restraint but breaks secrecy and comes into the open.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet states as a law what the kali poem stages as a scene with an audience and a song, and shows the price: once love breaks into the open, the woman faces two pains, love and gossip (காமமும் கௌவையும்), where the couplet names only the first."},{"a":"kl:143","b":"pn:186","type":"qualifies","note":"Kalittokai 143's woman asks why the king who guards the world's lives as his own does not guard hers; Purananuru 186 says the world lives by its king and the king must know he is its life.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The deserted woman takes the puram doctrine that the king is the people's life and finds its edge: a king can guard every life from war and want but not from a lover's leaving, so the akam poem marks a limit of royal protection that the puram counsel does not state.","gr":4},{"a":"kl:144","b":"k:1218","type":"agrees","note":"Kalittokai 144's girl says that when she opens her eyes to seize him he runs and hides in her heart; Kural 1218 says that when she sleeps he is on her shoulder and when she wakes he quickly moves into her heart.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same fancy of the lover fleeing into the heart on waking appears in both, but the kali poem frames it as a madwoman's complaint to the sea, so what the couplet offers as consolation (he never leaves her) is also a sign of her derangement."},{"a":"kl:145","b":"k:1225","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 145's woman begs the sun not to set and speaks of bearing evening's enmity (மாலைப் பகை); Kural 1225 asks what good she did to morning and what enmity (பகை) to evening.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's riddle about why evening is hostile is answered in the kali poem by an action: she asks the sun to stay and hopes the man will come at dawn over the sea, so the hours are enemies or friends according to when the parted can expect his return."},{"a":"kl:146","b":"k:1168","type":"agrees","note":"Kalittokai 146's woman says that if she told her pain to the night that puts many to sleep (பலர்த் துயிற்றும் யாமம்), it would not sleep; Kural 1168 says the night that puts all beings to sleep has no companion but her.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Both make the night, the one who puts all to sleep, the only wakeful companion of the deserted woman; the kali poem goes further and threatens to keep the night itself awake with her song, turning the couplet's pity for the night into her power over it."},{"a":"kl:147","b":"k:1137","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1137 says nothing is worthier than womanhood that suffers a sea of desire and does not ride the madal; Kalittokai 147's woman, maddened in the street, begs Kama's arrows so that the man will come riding the palmyra horse.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The kali woman keeps the rule the couplet praises, she does not mount the madal, but she gets around it by wishing the madal on him, so the restriction on women survives even in the most extreme akam scene and shapes the form her desire takes."},{"a":"kl:148","b":"k:1226","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 148 calls evening a sickness to the parted and a raft to the joined; in Kural 1226 the woman did not know evening brings sickness while her husband was still with her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet records the discovery and the kali poem the double nature behind it: the same hour is a raft and a suggestive setting (இறைச்சி) for lovers together and a sickness for the parted, which is why she learned its cruelty only after he left."},{"a":"kl:149","b":"k:110","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 149's friend warns that failing those who helped in want will strike the doer even in his descendants; Kural 110 says one who kills a good done to him has no escape.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend applies the Kural's gravest moral rule to a lover's delay, so the woman who gave herself in secret is counted as a benefactor, and his failure to marry her becomes ingratitude, the one wrong with no escape."},{"a":"kl:149","b":"pn:34","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 34 quotes dharma that there is expiation for many crimes but none for those who kill a benefit; Kalittokai 149's friend says ingratitude to helpers will not spare a man, however good his sword.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The rule the puram poet uses to praise a king's gifts to bards is turned in the akam poem against a lover, which shows the ethic of gratitude ran across both halves of Sangam poetry and could bind a man's private love as tightly as a patron's duty."},{"a":"kl:150","b":"kl:16","type":"tension","note":"In Kalittokai 16 virtue itself runs to block the man's venture for wealth; in Kalittokai 150 the friend says he left 'giving up virtue' (அறம் துறந்து) and still assures the woman that he will not prove false.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"In 16 virtue is the force that keeps him home, while in 150 the friend grants that he broke virtue by going and yet trusts his word, so the two poems split the man's duty into staying (அறம்) and keeping faith (not being பொய்), which can come apart."},{"a":"ku:0","b":"p:323","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 0 knows Murugan by his lotus-red feet, the spear that split the mountain and the cock banner, and says his guarding keeps the world safe; Thiruppugazh 323 names the same spear that pierced Krauncha, the cock banner and the golden anklet feet, and asks for release from desire.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same three marks of Murugan pass from a guarantee of the whole world's safety in the oldest invocation to one devotee's plea for rescue from his own lust, so the iconography stayed fixed while the god's protection moved from the world to the self."},{"a":"ku:2","b":"k:1111","type":"develops","note":"In both the man praises the woman he has just joined by addressing a creature of the flowers: Kuruntokai 2 asks the bee whether any flower is more fragrant than her hair; Kural 1111 tells the anicham it is soft but she is softer.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Valluvar keeps the Sangam device of praise through an apostrophe to nature but changes the sense judged, from smell to touch, and drops the witness: the Kuruntokai man asks the bee for evidence, the Kural man simply declares the verdict."},{"a":"ku:5","b":"k:1179","type":"qualifies","note":"Both have the woman in separation with eyes that cannot sleep; Kuruntokai 5 uses the sleeplessness to ask whether this is love-sickness, Kural 1179 says the eyes do not sleep when he is away nor when he comes.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Sangam woman learns from sleepless eyes that she is in love, while the Kural woman, past that discovery, finds the sleeplessness survives even his return, so the couplet assumes and goes beyond the moment the older poem records."},{"a":"ku:6","b":"k:1168","type":"qualifies","note":"Both set the sleeping world against the one woman awake in separation; Kuruntokai 6 leaves her alone awake under the dead night, Kural 1168 makes the night itself her companion, pitied for having no one else.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural turns the bare Sangam contrast of a sleeping world and one waking woman into a conceit in which the night shares her vigil, so the couplet adds a companion, even a mocked one, to a solitude the older poem leaves complete."},{"a":"ku:7","b":"a:321","type":"same_image","note":"In Kuruntokai 7 onlookers see anklets still on the eloping girl's soft feet; in Akananuru 321 the foster mother says the girl took off her pebble-filled anklets for fear her mother would hear, before crossing the bamboo hills.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show the anklet as the sign of an unwed girl read from two sides: at home its sound must be silenced for her to escape, while on the road strangers recognise from it, still on her feet, that the pair are lovers in flight rather than a married couple."},{"a":"ku:8","b":"k:906","type":"tension","note":"Kural 906 says men who fear their wife's shoulders are worthless even if they live like gods; in Kuruntokai 8 the other woman mocks the man for doing his son's mother's bidding like a mirror image, and on the akam reading this obedience is the household holding him, which the poem does not condemn.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Read together they show that the conduct the Kural brands as a man's vice appears in akam only as the other woman's spiteful jibe, so the ethical charge of wife-obedience and the love poem's victory of the wife are the same act seen from opposite sides."},{"a":"ku:9","b":"ku:10","type":"develops","note":"Both open with யாயாகியளே and both have the friend tell how the wife hides her husband's cruelty (கொடுமை) out of shame; in 9 she wilts like an unworn flower, in 10 she comes as leader of the festival yet ashamed.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"Placed together the two poets show the wife's concealment as a set karpu situation with two costs: it wastes her body in private and shames her in public, and in each case it is the friend, not the wife, who breaks the silence.","gr":3},{"a":"ku:11","b":"k:1248","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1248 has the woman call her heart a fool for running after the man who left; in Kuruntokai 11 the woman herself bids her heart rise and follow him even past the land of the Vadugar, since staying here cannot be survived.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The heart that the Kural woman scolds for following is the one the Sangam woman sends out on purpose, so the same impulse is folly in the Kural's chapter and the only means of survival in Maamulanar's poem."},{"a":"ku:13","b":"k:1183","type":"develops","note":"In both the woman says her lover gave her sickness and pallor: Kuruntokai 13 says plainly he gave the sickness and pallor fills her eyes; Kural 1183 says he took her grace and modesty and gave sickness and pallor in exchange.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural turns Kapilar's single complaint of a gift into an exchange with two sides, naming what was taken (சாயல், நாண்) as well as what was given, so the pair show the 'he gave me pallor' formula growing into an account of loss."},{"a":"ku:15","b":"a:113","type":"same_image","note":"Kuruntokai 15 measures the lovers' fidelity by the good word of the Kosar of Nalur given under the old banyan; Akananuru 113 calls the Kosar many-speared guards who never swerve from friendship even in bad times.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 113 supplies the reputation the Kuruntokai simile depends on: the Kosar were known for keeping faith in adversity, so the foster mother vindicates the eloped girl's bond by the standard of a war band's unbreakable loyalty, not by its feeling."},{"a":"ku:17","b":"k:1133","type":"develops","note":"Both are the man speaking of the madal: Kuruntokai 17 lists as a general rule what men do when love hardens (ride the palm horse, wear erukku, become a street spectacle); Kural 1133 says he once had shame and manliness and now has the madal.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kuruntokai poem supplies the public ritual (the erukku garland, the mocking street) that the Kural compresses into a single inventory of loss, and the Kural adds what the older poem leaves unstated: that taking up the madal costs a man his shame and manliness."},{"a":"ku:18","b":"k:1163","type":"same_image","note":"Kuruntokai 18 says her love hangs on her small life like a great jackfruit on a small stalk; Kural 1163 says love and shame hang from the two ends of a carrying pole that is her life.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both picture love as a weight hanging from life, but the Kural adds shame as a counterweight, so the Sangam image of a stalk about to snap under one load becomes in the couplet a pole balanced between two loads that will snap either way."},{"a":"ku:19","b":"pn:233","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 233 refuses to believe the dawn news that Evvi, leader of the bards' families, lies with spear wounds in his chest; Kuruntokai 19 compares the man's grieving heart to the flowerless heads of the bards impoverished by Evvi's loss.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The puram lament gives the moment of the news, and Paranar's simile gives its aftermath, bards going bare-headed without flowers, so the pair show a patron's death becoming a recognised mourning custom that akam could borrow to measure a lover's estrangement."},{"a":"ku:20","b":"a:43","type":"tension","note":"In Akananuru 43 the man who stays calls those who leave their companion for wealth fools (மடமையோர்); in Kuruntokai 20 the woman left behind grants the leavers the name of the wise (உரவோர்) and claims folly (மடவம்) for those who stay.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The paalai debate is fought over the same pair of words, wise and foolish: the staying man pins folly on the leavers, while the abandoned woman lets them keep wisdom and takes folly as the side of love, so who names whom foolish depends on who was left."},{"a":"ku:20","b":"pn:216","type":"develops","note":"Both are by Kopperunchozhan: in Purananuru 216 he says his unseen friend Pisirandhaiyar calls himself 'the foolish Chola's'; in Kuruntokai 20 his heroine says let the leavers be wise and let us be fools.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same king, in puram and in akam, gives the name of folly to loyalty that prudence would abandon, his friend's in the one and the waiting woman's in the other, so the akam poem's irony has a model in the king's own creed of friendship."},{"a":"ku:21","b":"kn:4","type":"tension","note":"Kar Narpathu 4: the friend points to the bloomed konrai that has made the forest lovely as proof that the season of his return has come. Kuruntokai 21: the woman says that even if the konrai forest declares the rains, she will not believe it, since he does not lie.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same konrai sign is proof of his return in the friend's mouth and a sign the woman refuses in her own, so the season argument of the mullai poems could be turned around: faith in his word can deny the season rather than read it."},{"a":"ku:26","b":"ku:25","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 25: the only witness of the secret union was a heron watching for fish. Kuruntokai 26: against the lover's possible denial, the friend says even the male monkey of the hill knows that cruel man.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two poems show the lack of a human witness to kalavu as a standing fear, answered by pointing to the land's own creatures: the woman doubts that the heron saw anything, while the friend insists that the monkey knows."},{"a":"ku:27","b":"k:1189","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1189: the woman says let her body turn pale as it will, if the man who persuaded her is well. Kuruntokai 27: her beauty, of use neither to her nor to him, is left for pallor to eat, like milk spilt on the ground.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural lets the woman accept pallor as a price paid for his welfare, while the Kuruntokai poet counts it pure waste, a yield that reaches no one, so the same pallor in absence is read as sacrifice in one and as loss with no gain in the other.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:28","b":"k:1168","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1168: the night, which puts all living beings to sleep, is to be pitied, for it has no companion but her. Kuruntokai 28: she wants to strike, butt or cry out at the town that sleeps unaware of her pain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both set one sleepless woman against a sleeping world, but the Kural turns the loneliness into pity for the night, while the Sangam poem turns it into rage at the town, which shows that the same isolation could be voiced as tender complaint or as a wish to wake everyone."},{"a":"ku:28","b":"k:1229","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1229: when evening spreads, the whole town is bewildered and suffers (on the reading that the town shares her pain). Kuruntokai 28: the town sleeps, not knowing her grief.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On that reading the Kural imagines the town grieving with the lovesick woman, while Kuruntokai 28 has the town sleep through her grief, so whether the community shares a private sorrow is answered both ways in the love poetry."},{"a":"ku:29","b":"k:660","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 660: making wealth by deceit and feeling safe is like keeping water in an unfired clay pot. Kuruntokai 29: the heart, passing over good counsel, is like a fresh unfired pot set out in the rain, plunged into a flood it cannot hold.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In both the unfired pot is ruined by what it is made to hold, so the image locates the fault in the vessel rather than the water: wealth got by fraud undoes its keeper as desire taken in against counsel undoes the heart."},{"a":"ku:30","b":"k:1216","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1216: if there were no waking, the lover in the dream would not leave. Kuruntokai 30: a lying dream of his embrace deceived her, and she rose and felt over the bed, alone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural blames waking in the abstract, while the Kuruntokai poem shows the very moment, the hand on the empty bed, so the couplet's complaint gains a scene and the scene gains a name for its cause."},{"a":"ku:30","b":"k:1218","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1218: in sleep he is on her shoulder, and when she wakes he quickly moves into her heart. Kuruntokai 30: on waking from the dream she finds only the empty bed and calls herself alone and pitiable.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural consoles the dreamer that waking only moves the lover inward, while the Kuruntokai woman finds no such refuge and is left alone, so the two disagree on whether the waking after a love dream is a loss or a passage into the heart."},{"a":"ku:31","b":"a:236","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 236 likens the heroine to Aathimanthi, who wept from land to land and town to town asking 'have you not seen Aattan Atthi?', the dancer the flood took. Kuruntokai 31, by Aathimanthiyaar, has a woman of the dancing ground search festival and dance for the chief who is a man of the dancing ground.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read with Paranar's simile, the unnamed 'man of the dancing ground' in Kuruntokai 31 is the lost dancer of the legend, and the poem gives in the first person the search the Akananuru turns into a stock comparison for a woman maddened by loss."},{"a":"ku:31","b":"a:222","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 222 tells how Aathimanthi, after the Kaveri seized Aattan Atthi, searched in bewilderment until Maruthi showed her the lover and entered the sea. Kuruntokai 31 is the woman's own voice at the stage where she finds him nowhere.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together the two poems order the story: Kuruntokai 31 stands in the middle of the search, 'nowhere do I see him', and the Akananuru supplies the end that the short poem never reaches, so the legend outlived the poet's own lament."},{"a":"ku:32","b":"k:1131","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1131: for those who suffer in love there is no safeguard and no strength but the palmyra horse. Kuruntokai 32: to ride the madal in the street is shame, and to live apart is shame too.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The same lover's threat is a safeguard in the Kural and a disgrace in the Kuruntokai, which only weighs it as the lesser of two shames, so the madal was argued both as the lover's last strength and as an equal dishonour to living on without her."},{"a":"ku:32","b":"k:1227","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1227: love-sickness buds in the morning, ripens through the day and opens in the evening. Kuruntokai 32: if one can tell morning, day, evening, midnight and dawn apart, love is a lie.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives love a daily clock with evening as its worst hour, while the Kuruntokai lover says true love cannot tell the hours apart, so the two set the convention of the evening's pain against the claim that real longing knows no time."},{"a":"ku:34","b":"a:376","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 376: the courtesan asks the lord to give back her beauty, which was like Kuttuvan's Maranthai. Kuruntokai 34: the friend calls the wife's bright brow lovely as Kuttuvan's Maranthai.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same Chera port is the standard of beauty for a wedded girl and for a courtesan, so the akam poets used Maranthai's fame as a neutral measure of a woman's nalam whatever her standing."},{"a":"ku:36","b":"pn:71","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 71: Boothappandiyan vows that if he does not rout the allied kings he may be parted from his wide-eyed wife. Kuruntokai 36: the lover swore a vanjinam that he would not leave her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Separation from the beloved is the penalty the king stakes on his battle vow and the very thing the lover's vow forswears, so the akam vow borrows the puram form and the friend's trust in it rests on the warrior's honour that a vanjinam is never broken."},{"a":"ku:37","b":"a:59","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 59: the friend tells the wasting heroine that on the road the man took a tusker bends a tall yaa tree to feed its young mate, and the poem ends on 'the road he went'. Kuruntokai 37 gives the same tusker easing its mate's hunger on the yaa and the same close, 'the road he went'.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The long poem spends its lines on her lost beauty, a Mal simile and his forgetting before reaching the tusker and the road, so Kuruntokai 37 shows the consolation reduced to its working parts: a promise of his love, the creature's care as proof, and the road."},{"a":"ku:37","b":"kl:11","type":"develops","note":"Kalittokai 11: the woman says the man himself told her that in the burning forest the tusker lets his mate drink the little muddy water first, the dove fans its tired mate and the stag shades the doe, so he will return. Kuruntokai 37: the friend gives one tusker stripping the yaa for his mate.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The later Kalittokai multiplies the single tusker into three caring males and puts the inference in the heroine's own mouth, which shows the comforting iraicchi of porul 5.35 growing from the friend's one proof into a catalogue the heroine argues from."},{"a":"ku:39","b":"a:45","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 45: the lover crossed a summer forest where the dried pods of the uzhunjil rattle like the drums of a dancing ground. Kuruntokai 39: they say his road is the hard waste where the wind makes the dried uzhinjil pods rattle.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The rattling uzhinjil pods are a fixed sign by which a waiting woman pictures the man's road; the Akananuru makes the sound a dance drum, while Kuruntokai 39 keeps it as hearsay ('they say'), showing that the woman's knowledge of the waste is report, not sight."},{"a":"ku:40","b":"k:1122","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1122: the man says the bond between him and the girl is that of body and life. Kuruntokai 40: the man says their mothers, fathers and they themselves were strangers, yet their loving hearts have mingled like rain in red earth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both men declare an inseparable bond, but the Kuruntokai first denies any tie of kin, so the union it praises is made between strangers and inseparable only after mingling, which the Kural's body-and-life image, a pair joined from birth, does not show."},{"a":"ku:42","b":"n:125","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 125 says the bond (தொடர்பு) with small men wanes (தேயுமே) of itself like the moon while friendship with the great grows; Kuruntokai 42 asks the hill lord whether our bond (எம் தொடர்பும்) will wane (தேயுமோ) even if desire ends.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same two words, bond and waning, show the friend putting the lover to the Naladiyar's test of character: a bond that wanes once its first cause, desire, is spent marks the small man, so her question demands that he prove himself one of the great."},{"a":"ku:43","b":"k:1151","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1151 has the woman (or her friend) speak at once: if you are not leaving, tell me; your quick return, tell those who will still be alive. Kuruntokai 43 says neither spoke: she thought he would not go, he thought she would not bear it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet is the sentence whose absence Avvaiyar's poem mourns: set beside it, the poem shows a parting that happened because pride on both sides kept that sentence unsaid, so the Kural's bluntness reads as the cure for the silence that undid the Kuruntokai pair."},{"a":"ku:44","b":"a:7","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 7 the foster mother, following her eloped daughter into the waste, cannot catch up and questions a hill girl she meets; in Kuruntokai 44 her legs are worn out, her eyes dimmed from looking, and the others she sees are more than the stars.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they give the search its course: the long poem is its middle, where the nurse still questions each stranger in hope, and the four-line poem is its end, where every face has proved to be someone else's and the looking itself has cost her sight."},{"a":"ku:46","b":"k:1222","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1222: the woman tells the evening it looks sad-eyed (புன்கண்ணை) and asks whether its partner is as hard-eyed as hers. Kuruntokai 46 asks whether the sad evening (புன்கண் மாலை) is absent in the land where he has gone.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both women make the evening's sorrow a test of the absent man: the couplet asks whether the evening's partner is as hard as hers, the poem asks whether he has any evening at all, and its sparrows settling with their young supply what the couplet leaves out, that evening is when every creature with a home returns to it."},{"a":"ku:47","b":"k:1119","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1119: the man tells the moon that if it would resemble her face it must not appear for many to see. Kuruntokai 47: the friend tells the long white moon it does no good to those who come at night for secret love.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both speakers wish the moon would not shine for others, for opposite reasons: the Kural's lover wants her face kept to himself, the Kuruntokai friend wants the meeting kept from watchers, so the moon's openness, which the couplet turns into a compliment, is in the older poem the danger that ends secret love."},{"a":"ku:49","b":"k:1315","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1315: the man says they will not part in this birth, and her eyes fill with tears. Kuruntokai 49: the woman asks that even when this life turns into the next he be her husband.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's joke rests on a wish it never states, that the bond reach past this birth; Kuruntokai 49 is that wish in the woman's own mouth, so the pair shows why 'in this birth' wounds her: she had asked for the next one too."},{"a":"ku:50","b":"k:1234","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1234: with her partner gone, her shoulders have lost their old beauty and fullness and her bright bangles slip. Kuruntokai 50: the shoulders he embraced have wasted so that the bright bangles slip past the wrist, adorned with loneliness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet names the wasted shoulder and slipping bangle as the sign of separation; the poem shows what the sign was used to say, setting it against his town's ford adorned with flowers, so the body's loss becomes an accusation of a place that still flourishes.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:51","b":"k:1143","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1143: the man asks whether the gossip the town knows is not welcome, since it is like having what he has not yet obtained. Kuruntokai 51: the friend says the gossiping town too speaks of the girl with him, and her mother and father favour him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet values gossip as a foretaste of a union not yet had; the poem shows the next step, when talk that pairs the lovers joins the parents' consent, so the gossip the Kural's lover enjoys in fancy becomes one of the voices that brings the marriage about."},{"a":"ku:53","b":"pn:216","type":"qualifies","note":"Kopperunchozhan, the poet of Kuruntokai 53, is the king of Purananuru 216, who tells the doubters that his unseen friend Pisirandhaiyar wants no lie that would spoil his fame and will come to share his death. In Kuruntokai 53 his friend-speaker holds a lover to an oath sworn before the hill-goddesses, which he has broken.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together, the king's two voices weigh faith two ways: in puram a friend never met keeps faith with no oath at all, while in his akam poem a lover breaks faith despite an oath sworn before goddesses, so the poet who staked his death on unsworn friendship knew how little a sworn word could hold in love.","gr":4},{"a":"ku:58","b":"k:447","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 447: who can ruin those who keep companions that rebuke them? Kuruntokai 58: the man tells his rebuking companions (இடிக்குங் கேளிர்) that it would be well if they could hold back his sickness, but it has spread past bearing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the rebuking companion a ruler's best protection; the lover grants the rebuke its good intent and shows where it stops, since love has spread like butter on hot rock before any word could check it, so the ethic of correction meets its limit at kaamam."},{"a":"ku:60","b":"k:1199","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1199: though the one she longs for gives no grace (நல்கார்), even word of him is sweet to her ears. Kuruntokai 60: even if her lover does not grace her (நல்கார்) or desire her, seeing him often is sweet to her heart.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both women claim sweetness from a lover who gives none, with the same verb; Paranar's simile of the cripple licking his empty palm beneath the honeycomb shows what the couplet's maxim hides, that this sweetness is tasted only in imagination, a pleasure that is also the measure of what is withheld."},{"a":"ku:63","b":"pn:189","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 189 concludes that the use of wealth is giving, since king and hunter alike eat one measure; Kuruntokai 63's heart argues the same, that there is no giving and enjoying for the penniless, to drive the man to work.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram maxim that wealth exists to be given becomes, in the akam poem, the very reason a man leaves his wife, so the pair shows the giver's ethic carrying a private cost the praise poem never counts.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:63","b":"k:247","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 247 takes as plain fact that this world is not for those without wealth; the heart in Kuruntokai 63 states that premise, and the man answers it only by asking whether she can come along.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set beside the couplet, the poem shows the Kural's blunt premise as a voice inside a lover's head that he can neither refute nor obey, so the maxim's truth is granted and its price is made visible in the separation."},{"a":"ku:65","b":"a:34","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 34 the returning man sees a stag feeding and guarding its doe and hurries home; in Kuruntokai 65 the waiting woman sees the stag back with its mate in the rains while her man does not come.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same sign of the stag with its mate is read from both ends of the separation: for the man it is a spur to return, for the woman it is evidence against him, so the iraicchi's force depends on who is watching."},{"a":"ku:66","b":"kn:4","type":"tension","note":"Kar Narpathu 4's friend points to the bloomed konrai as the season and the cure of pallor; Kuruntokai 66's friend calls the bloomed konrai foolish, fooled by stray rain, and denies that the season has come.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The friend's comfort can use the konrai either way, as proof the promised season is here or as a false sign to be dismissed, which shows that the consolation lies in the friend's argument and not in the flower itself.","gr":3},{"a":"ku:69","b":"pn:246","type":"develops","note":"Perunkoppendu in Purananuru 246 refuses a widow's life and chooses the pyre; the female monkey in Kuruntokai 69, unable to bear widowhood (கைம்மை உய்யா), leaves her young with kin and leaps to her death.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The akam friend borrows the puram queen's refusal of widowhood and gives it to a creature of the hill, making the girl's not outliving her lover a law of nature for the night-visitor to weigh, not a royal vow.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:71","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1102: other diseases have other cures, but this woman is the cure of the sickness she gives; Kuruntokai 71: if medicine, she is medicine; if treasure, she is treasure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The man's claim that she is his medicine is the same in both, but the poem adds treasure (வைப்பு), so under its paalai colophon the cure-paradox of the couplet becomes an argument against leaving to seek wealth."},{"a":"ku:71","b":"ku:63","type":"develops","note":"Both are the man's speech under paalai about leaving for wealth: 63 asks his heart whether she can come along on the work; 71 calls her both medicine and laid-up treasure.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence, the two give the man's full answer to the case for earning: 63 asks how wealth helps if it takes him from her, and 71 says she is already the treasure, so the journey has no object."},{"a":"ku:72","b":"k:1084","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1084: the eyes of this artless girl devour the life of those who see them; Kuruntokai 72: the eyes of the girl scaring sparrows from the millet have, like arrows, made him sick for all to see.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem places the couplet's paradox, the simple girl with killing eyes, in her actual work of chasing small birds off the hill millet, and adds what the couplet lacks: the wound is public, known to everyone."},{"a":"ku:73","b":"a:113","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 113 praises the Kosar who never swerve from friendship even in bad times, with unswerving plans (திரிபில் சூழ்ச்சி); Kuruntokai 73 holds up the Kosar of the single word, who cut down Nannan's mango, as the model of hard-eyed scheming (வன்கண் சூழ்ச்சி).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kosar's fame was for a kept word carried out by planning, and akam poets drew on it two ways, as loyalty in one poem and as ruthless resolve in the other, so the same people supplied both the virtue and the hard edge of a sworn word."},{"a":"ku:75","b":"a:265","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 265: the woman asks whether the wealth her man went for is as great as the Nandas' treasure hidden at Pataliputra; Kuruntokai 75: the woman wishes the bearer of news of her lover's return to win Pataliputra rich in gold.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Pataliputra's gold was the akam measure of the greatest wealth, and the two women price it against love from opposite sides: one doubts it outweighs her, the other would give it for word of his coming."},{"a":"ku:77","b":"k:1236","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1236: with bangles and shoulders slackening, what hurts the woman is hearing him called cruel; Kuruntokai 77: there is no fault in him, or if any, it is that her soft shoulders proved easy to leave.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Both women defend the absent man with their wasted shoulders as the evidence, but the poem goes further than the couplet by moving the blame onto those shoulders, while its desert of dead travellers' cairns lets the reproach stand unspoken."},{"a":"ku:80","b":"pn:230","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 230: while Ezhini lived, the herds rested in the forest unguarded and safe; Kuruntokai 80: the courtesan tells the wife to guard her husband like the great herds at Ezhini's war front.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The courtesan's taunt rests on Ezhini's puram fame as the protector of herds, so she casts herself as the cattle-raider and the husband as plunder that only a war-chief's watch could keep, using the heroic guard to mock a household one."},{"a":"ku:83","b":"k:1107","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the saying 'eating one's own in one's own house': Kural 1107 for the joy of the beloved's embrace, Kuruntokai 83 for jackfruit hanging sweet on the lover's own hill as the mother announces he will come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the phrase was a common measure of untroubled, owned pleasure; the Kural applies it to union itself, while the Kuruntokai friend lays it on the lover's land at the moment marriage is promised, so the image belongs to the settled household rather than to secret love."},{"a":"ku:86","b":"k:1168","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1168 the woman says the night that lulls every creature to sleep has only her for company; in Kuruntokai 86 she asks at midnight whether anyone else hears the cow's bell.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural asserts that she alone is awake; the Kuruntokai poem tests the claim with a question and finds one other wakeful thing, a lonely cow whose bell rings with her, so the solitude the Kural states is shown as a search for a companion."},{"a":"ku:87","b":"k:1188","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1188: people say she has paled, no one says he left her. Kuruntokai 87: the god destroys the false, my lover is not false, yet my brow has paled.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they show why the blame lands on her body: in the Kuruntokai the woman herself clears him before the avenging god, so the only visible sign of the wrong is her pallor, which is exactly the one-sided verdict the Kural's woman complains of."},{"a":"ku:89","b":"ku:100","type":"same_image","note":"Both compare a hill girl to the figure carved on Kolli's western face; 89 calls Kolli Porayan's, 100 calls it Ori's.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same carved figure serves two poets as the measure of a girl's innocence while the hill is credited to different lords, the Chera Porayan and the archer Ori, so the simile was fixed to the place and outlived the change of its master.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:91","b":"pn:97","type":"develops","note":"Avvaiyar's Purananuru 97 warns Anji's enemies that his worn swords, spears and elephants prove resistance is hopeless; her Kuruntokai 91 makes the night terror of a frontier town fearing Anji the measure of a wife's sleepless marriage.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same poet supplies both sides of Anji's war: the puram poem speaks for the conqueror's threat, the akam poem borrows the sleeplessness of the threatened town, showing that the fear puram praise produces was available to akam as an image of domestic misery."},{"a":"ku:92","b":"k:1222","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1222 asks the pale evening whether its partner is as hard-hearted as hers; Kuruntokai 92 at sunset watches birds hurry home with food for their young.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural guesses that the evening suffers from a cruel partner like hers; the Kuruntokai's dusk answers that every creature in it is hurrying home to its own, so the hard-heartedness is her man's alone and not the hour's."},{"a":"ku:93","b":"k:1308","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1308: what use is suffering where there is no lover who knows you suffer; Kuruntokai 93: what is sulking worth when love is gone; do not speak against him, he is mother and father to us.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the reasoning that sulking needs a lover who will notice; the Kuruntokai wife draws the conclusion in her own case and adds the reason she cannot quarrel, her total dependence on him, so the maxim is shown ending in silence rather than in oodal."},{"a":"ku:95","b":"k:1088","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1088: my might, feared in battle, broke before her bright brow; Kuruntokai 95: her grace like water quenched my strength like fire.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural reports the man's defeat as a wonder of scale, so great a strength and so small a brow; the Kuruntokai explains it by kind, water putting out fire, so his strength was not outmatched but met by its natural undoing."},{"a":"ku:97","b":"k:1144","type":"tension","note":"On the Kural reading where gossip feeds love and keeps it from drooping, Kuruntokai 97 disagrees: the secret turned to talk in the square is one of four things scattered apart, with her beauty lost and the man in his own town.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The man in the Kural can welcome gossip as fuel for love, while the woman in the Kuruntokai counts it among her losses, so the pair shows that rumour's value in the akam world depends on which lover bears it."},{"a":"ku:101","b":"k:1103","type":"develops","note":"Both have the man set the gods' world against lying in the beloved's arms: the Kural asks whether Vishnu's world is sweeter, Kuruntokai 101 puts earth and heaven together on one pan of a balance against a single day of embrace.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's open question about heaven has an older Sangam answer: the poet had already weighed both worlds together against one day and found them light, so the couplet's question reads as an allusion to that verdict rather than a doubt."},{"a":"ku:102","b":"k:1207","type":"develops","note":"Both put the woman in the same bind, nearly in the same words (உள்ளின் உள்ளம் வேமே; உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்): remembering burns her and not remembering is impossible.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural keeps Avvaiyar's bind and drops her last line, the charge that the man is not noble, so set side by side they show the couplet turning a complaint against him into a pure description of her state."},{"a":"ku:107","b":"a:122","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 122 the house cock and the owl that eats house rats are among the night's watchers that keep the secret lover away; in Kuruntokai 107 the wife curses the cock for ending her sleep beside her husband and wishes it prey to the wildcat that hunts house rats.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Held together they show that marriage did not free the lovers' night from its old enemy: the cock that guarded the house against the secret lover still cuts short the wedded night, and the wife's curse sends it to the very rat-hunters of the courtship poem.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:108","b":"k:1227","type":"develops","note":"Both make the woman's pain open with the evening: the Kural's sickness buds at dawn and blooms at evening, and in Kuruntokai 108 the jasmine opens red in the sunset as the cows go home and she says she will not survive.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's flower metaphor for the sickness rests on a real mullai sight that Kuruntokai 108 shows, the jasmine opening at red dusk as the herds return, so the couplet's conceit is that scene folded into the body of the woman who watches it."},{"a":"ku:111","b":"a:22","type":"develops","note":"In Kuruntokai 111 the friend wishes the hill man would come and expose the household that believes the diviner's word about Murugan; in Akananuru 22 the girl, embraced by her lover on the very night of the rite, laughs (நக்கனென்) at the offering wasted on a stranger priest.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The laugh the friend in Kuruntokai 111 hopes for is the laugh the girl of Akananuru 22 actually has, so together they show the veriyattu rite was framed in akam as a comic error waiting for the lover's arrival to undo it."},{"a":"ku:112","b":"k:1163","type":"develops","note":"Both hang the woman between love and shame: the Kural has the two hung from a carrying pole that is her life, Kuruntokai 112 names the town's talk as the fear and pictures her as a branch an elephant has snapped that still hangs by its fibre.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kuruntokai 112 gives the Kural's evenly balanced pole a cause and a breaking point: the weight on shame's side is the town's gossip, and the balance is already a break held only by a strand, which the couplet's still image does not show."},{"a":"ku:117","b":"k:1157","type":"qualifies","note":"Both tie the slipping bangles to the absence of the lord of the shore (துறைவன்): in the Kural they announce his leaving to the town; in Kuruntokai 117 the friend says let him stay away, smaller bangles can be bought from the sellers.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same sign of wasting is grief in the woman's mouth and a weapon in the friend's: Kuruntokai 117 shows the slipping bangle could be turned into mockery meant to shame the man, which the Kural's lament never does."},{"a":"ku:118","b":"k:1222","type":"qualifies","note":"In the Kural the woman pities the evening as a fellow sufferer and asks if its mate is as hard as hers; in Kuruntokai 118 the evening is loveless (நாரில்) and the birds and beasts that settle in loneliness are company she gets no comfort from.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show two answers to the same hour: the Kural's woman finds a companion in the grieving evening, while the Sangam woman finds the whole world, beasts and gatekeepers included, going home around her, so the evening isolates rather than consoles."},{"a":"ku:119","b":"k:1088","type":"develops","note":"Both have the man say that his strength has been broken by a young girl: the Kural by her bright forehead alone, though foes in battle feared him; Kuruntokai 119 by the likeness of a hatchling snake felling a wild elephant.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kuruntokai 119 supplies the image behind the Kural's paradox of the mighty man undone by a forehead: the elephant brought down by a hatchling snake, a picture of poison not strength, which explains why the couplet's warrior has no defence."},{"a":"ku:124","b":"kl:6","type":"develops","note":"Both by the poet of the paalai (Perunkadungo): the man calls the waste harsh (இன்னா, Kuruntokai 124; கடுமைய காடு, Kalittokai 6) and the woman's side answers him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same poet gives the same plea two answers: in the short poem the friend argues the house alone is as harsh as the forest, a reason for him to stay, while the long poem has the woman draw the next step, that hardship shared on the road is her only joy, so the argument from equal harm leads straight to the demand to go with him."},{"a":"ku:126","b":"kn:14","type":"tension","note":"Both say the fragrant cool rain-season laughs with mullai buds for bright teeth (முல்லை ... இலங்கெயிறு ... நறுந்தண் கார் ... நகும்); in Kar Narpathu 14 the friend reads the laugh as proof he will come quickly, in Kuruntokai 126 the woman hears it mocking her: where are they?","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The later poet took the older line almost word for word and reversed what it means: the same laughing season is a sign of his return to the friend who comforts and a jeer at the deserted woman, which shows that the image itself carries no fixed mood and the speaker's place in the situation decides it."},{"a":"ku:128","b":"a:60","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the same half-line, திண்டேர்ப் பொறையன் தொண்டி: Akananuru 60 makes the girl's bangle as fine as Poraiyan's Thondi; Kuruntokai 128 makes the girl the ayirai fish of Thondi's harbour, longed for by a wingless heron on the eastern sea.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Poraiyan's Thondi served the love poets as a stock measure of the desirable, but they could aim it two ways: as a measure of the girl's worth that the man must win by marriage, or, set against the eastern sea, as a measure of how far out of reach she is."},{"a":"ku:129","b":"pn:216","type":"develops","note":"Both are by Kopperunchozhan: in Kuruntokai 129 he tells his comrade, 'friend of poets', that a girl's brow has bound him like a new-caught elephant; in Purananuru 216, fasting to death, he tells doubters his poet-friend, whom he has never seen, will surely come.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together, the king's two voices turn on the same bond of male friendship: in the love poem the comrade is the one he confides his captivity to, in the death poem the friend's coming is the proof of his worth, so the paangan of akam convention is, for this poet, a role he filled with a real friendship of poets."},{"a":"ku:135","b":"a:75","type":"develops","note":"Both quote a saying about men's work: 'work is life to men' (வினையே ஆடவர்க்கு உயிரே, Kuruntokai 135) and 'effort, not grace, is men's wealth' (அருள் அன்று ஆக ஆள்வினை ஆடவர் பொருள், Akananuru 75); in both the man does not leave.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The maxim that a man's life or wealth is his work was common enough to be quoted, yet in both poems it is quoted only to be overruled: the Akananuru hero weighs it against beauty that cannot be given back, and the Kuruntokai friend completes it with its other half, that men are women's life, so the love poets used the work ethic as the premise they argue against."},{"a":"ku:135","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1151 (the friend or the woman to the man): if you are not leaving, tell me; tell your quick return to those who will be alive then. Kuruntokai 135: the friend tells the woman he himself said men are women's life, so he will hold back.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet states as a threat what the Kuruntokai friend leaves the man to infer from his own words: if men are the women's life, then his going kills her and any promise of a quick return is addressed to the dead, which is why the friend can be sure he will stay."},{"a":"ku:136","b":"k:1227","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1227 (the woman) calls love a sickness that buds in the morning, opens through the day and blooms at evening; Kuruntokai 136 (the man) says love is neither a spirit nor a disease and does not swell and subside, but has its season like an elephant's musth when it finds one to look on.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two agree that love keeps time but disagree on what kind of thing it is: the Kural's woman makes it a disease with a daily clock inside her, the Kuruntokai's man denies it is a sickness at all and makes it a latent season set off from outside by sight, so whether love is illness depends on whether one is waiting or seeing."},{"a":"ku:137","b":"a:279","type":"tension","note":"In Akananuru 279 the man leaves because men who stay at their wife's breast cannot bear to see friends in want and kin in distress; in Kuruntokai 137 the man swears that if he ever leaves his wife, may suppliants stop coming to him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same value, being the one the needy come to, drives the man out of the house in the Akananuru and binds him to it in the Kuruntokai, where losing the suppliants is the curse for leaving, so the puram duty to give could be argued on either side of the akam choice to go or stay."},{"a":"ku:140","b":"k:1180","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 140: the woman asks how the gossiping town came to know the grief she bore in silence; Kural 1180: the woman says it is not hard for the town to learn the secret of those whose eyes beat it out like a drum.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural answers the Kuruntokai woman's question: she kept silent, but her eyes told the town, so the gossip she resents is her own grief made public by her body, not betrayal by anyone else.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:141","b":"a:18","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 18 has the same thurai, the night-comer told to come by day: the friend tells the hill lord the night ford is deadly and he may meet the girl by day in the kaanthal grove. Kuruntokai 141 gives the refusal to the heroine, who only reports her mother's errand to the millet field.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together they show the one message in two voices: the friend may name the day meeting outright, while the heroine can offer it only by passing on her mother's errand, so the day tryst comes through indirection exactly where the speaker's freedom is narrowest."},{"a":"ku:143","b":"n:2","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 2: wealth stays level with no one but turns like a cart wheel, so share it the day it comes. Kuruntokai 143 uses the wealth that does not stay with a generous man as the image of pallor that will pass.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar argues from wealth's instability to giving, and the love poem had already fused the two into one image, the giver whose wealth leaves him, and turned that ethical commonplace into a promise that sorrow too is the kind of thing that leaves."},{"a":"ku:143","b":"k:331","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 331 calls it the lowest folly to take what does not last for lasting. Kuruntokai 143's friend says impermanence alone is permanent, and draws hope from it.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Kural uses impermanence to rebuke attachment, but the friend of the love poem applies the same truth to grief, showing that the doctrine was a two-edged tool: what cannot last includes the pallor of longing, so it consoles as readily as it warns."},{"a":"ku:145","b":"k:1168","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1168: the night that puts all living things to sleep is pitiable, having no companion but her. Kuruntokai 145: the long night here has only sleepers who will not talk with the sleepless.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both women stay awake while everyone sleeps, but the Kural turns her loneliness into a mock pity for the night, while the Kuruntokai blames the sleeping village; together they show that the wakeful heroine's complaint can fall on the night or on the people, and that the couplet's wit grows from the poem's social grievance."},{"a":"ku:146","b":"ku:156","type":"develops","note":"146: the friend asks whether 'those who unite the parted' (பிரிந்தோர்ப் புணர்ப்போர்) have come, staffs in hand. 156: the hero asks the Brahmin with his staff whether his learning holds a medicine 'that unites the parted' (பிரிந்தோர்ப் புணர்க்கும்).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same phrase names the go-between's office from both sides of kalavu: in the friend's mouth the uniters arrive as joyful elders of a marriage embassy, while the hero doubts that a Brahmin's book can unite anyone, so the phrase shows that union was expected from social go-betweens, not from learning."},{"a":"ku:147","b":"k:1216","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1216: if there were no waking, the lover met in the dream would not leave. Kuruntokai 147: the hero says the dream, as if it had brought her, rouses him from sweet sleep.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's woman blames waking and spares the dream; the Kuruntokai's man blames the dream itself for ending his sleep, so set together they show that the dream meeting was felt as both gift and cheat, and that the blame shifts with which side of it the lover dwells on."},{"a":"ku:148","b":"kn:4","type":"qualifies","note":"Kar Narpathu 4: the friend shows the bloomed konrai and the dancing peacocks and calls the season medicine for the pallor. Kuruntokai 148: the friend denies that the konrai and kurundham mean the rains, and the heroine asks if she is dreaming.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend's proof from the season only works before the season is spent; once the konrai has bloomed and he has not come, the same flower proves his breach, so the comforter must deny the season and the heroine turns the argument against her."},{"a":"ku:149","b":"k:1134","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 1134: the fierce flood of desire carries off the raft of shame and manliness (the man speaks). Kuruntokai 149: her modesty is a small sand dam that holds as long as it can and gives way when the sweet flood presses (the woman speaks).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both picture desire as a flood against shame, but for the man shame is a raft that is swept away with him on it, and for the woman it is a dam that holds the water back, so the shared image is divided by gender: his shame rides the flood, hers resists it."},{"a":"ku:149","b":"k:1251","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1251: the axe of desire breaks the door of restraint bolted by shame (the woman speaks). Kuruntokai 149: her modesty struggled long, held as long as it could and now gives way.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the collapse of restraint as a single blow of an axe, while the Kuruntokai gives it duration and fellowship, a modesty that 'suffered with us' long before giving way, which shows the couplet compressing a process the older poem tells as a struggle."},{"a":"ku:150","b":"k:1104","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1104: the man asks where she got this fire that burns when he moves away and cools when he draws near. Kuruntokai 150: the woman says the pain swells when she thinks of his chest and vanishes when she embraces it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's man finds the paradoxical fire in the woman, but the Kuruntokai heroine finds the same sickness in herself and sets it off by memory, not distance, so the pair shows that the burning-and-cooling belongs to both lovers and that thinking of the absent one is itself the burning."},{"a":"ku:151","b":"ku:154","type":"develops","note":"151: on the hill path a hen whose mate fell to a hawk keeps calling, and the hero decides leaving her would be the end of youth. 154: in the waste a hen whose cock has gone to find food calls in loneliness, and the heroine asks how men learn to part.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same carrier, a hen calling for an absent mate on the road, works from both sides of the separation: in the hero's mouth it is the reason he stays, in the heroine's it is the reproach to a man who went anyway, so one native image can hold both the decision and its blame."},{"a":"ku:151","b":"a:337","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 337: the hero reminds his heart, which presses for another journey, that once it found parting no joy, said 'let wealth stay where it is' and came back alone through the robbers' waste. Kuruntokai 151: the hero refuses the hill path, saying going would end his youth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kuruntokai 151 gives the moment of refusal, and Akananuru 337 shows that such a refusal does not settle anything: the heart that once turned back is urging the road again, so the decision not to leave is a choice that must be remade, not a resolution."},{"a":"ku:153","b":"ku:141","type":"develops","note":"141: the heroine asks the friend to tell the lover not to come in the deep night, when the tiger lies in wait on the slope. 153: her heart, once afraid of an owl or a monkey in the yard, now goes along the slope to him in the dense dark night.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The dangers of the night slope are the argument she uses to keep him away, yet her own heart travels that road without fear, so the pairing shows the night-danger plea as care for him rather than prudence she lives by.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:153","b":"k:1249","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1249: the woman asks her heart where it goes seeking, since the lover lives within it. Kuruntokai 153: her heart, once timid, now goes along the night slope to him without tiring.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural mocks the heart's going out as needless because he is already within, while the Kuruntokai heroine marvels at the same going as courage, so the pairing shows the couplet's wit resting on an older picture of the heart as a traveller on the lover's road."},{"a":"ku:156","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 156: the lovesick hero asks the Brahmin whether his unwritten learning holds a medicine to unite the parted. Kural 1102: the man says other illnesses have other cures, but this woman is herself the cure for her sickness.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet reads as the answer to the Kuruntokai hero's question: no learned remedy exists, because only the woman herself cures the sickness she causes, so the pair turns the hero's doubt of the Brahmin's lore into the Kural's general rule of love's medicine."},{"a":"ku:155","b":"kn:10","type":"qualifies","note":"Kar Narpathu 10: as thunder roars and the bull bellows back, the swift chariot comes with the sound of a forest stream. Kuruntokai 155: the evening of the season has come, but not the word that his chariot with its ringing bells is on its way.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The venba lets the sound of the chariot arrive with the season, but the Kuruntokai keeps season and chariot apart and has the heroine wait first for the report of it, which shows the chariot-sound of mullai as a promise the older poem could leave unkept."},{"a":"ku:158","b":"kn:17","type":"same_image","note":"Kar Narpathu 17: on the high rock-ledged hill the thunder crushes snakes and the sky, thick with dark, shows the season; the friend reads it as his return. Kuruntokai 158: the heroine asks the cloud, whose thunder strikes snakes dead on the tall slope, whether it has no pity on lone women.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same storm with its snake-killing thunder is a sign of comfort when the friend reads it as the promised season and a terror when the lone heroine faces it at night, so the image carries whatever the speaker's situation gives it rather than a fixed meaning."},{"a":"ku:160","b":"a:305","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 305: on cold north-wind midnights a single anril on the palm cries in grief, and the heroine asks how she can live while he is away for wealth. Kuruntokai 160: at hushed midnight in the cold north wind the anril cries with its hen from their nest, and he does not come.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The anril's midnight cry in the north wind is shared, but the Akananuru bird is alone and mirrors her, while the Kuruntokai bird is with its mate and shows what she lacks; together they show that the anril, bird of the unparted, can serve as either likeness or contrast to the waiting woman."},{"a":"ku:144","b":"a:17","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 17: the foster mother remembers how the girl, tired after ball and jacks, would lie in her arms, and wonders how her small feet will manage the waste. Kuruntokai 144: the foster mother remembers the girl's play in the backwater and the waves and sees her gone over the gravel to the far mountains.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both foster mothers measure the elopement by the girl's play, but the Akananuru measures it on the body (tired feet, sweat), while the Kuruntokai measures it on the map, from sea shore through waste to mountain, so the pair shows the sevili's lament working through both the body and the landscapes the girl crosses."},{"a":"ku:165","b":"k:1090","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1090 says liquor delights only one who drinks it while love delights at a look; in Kuruntokai 165 the man, having only seen her hair and grace, says the sight came on his desire like toddy on top of joy.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem accepts the Kural's claim that love works by sight alone but denies the contrast with liquor: for the man who has looked, seeing acts exactly like a second drink, so the couplet's rule holds while its comparison is turned around."},{"a":"ku:173","b":"k:1133","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1133 the man says he once had shame and manliness and now has the palmyra horse; in Kuruntokai 173 he mounts it with shame killed (நாணட்டு) and says the town will name her fault.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural counts only what the madal costs the rider, his shame; the poem names what it is for, turning his public shame into her blame on the town's tongue, which is the pressure that makes the threat work on her kin."},{"a":"ku:163","b":"k:1222","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1222 the woman asks the pale evening whether its partner is as hard as hers; in Kuruntokai 163 she asks the sea who has hurt it, since she hears it crying at midnight.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both women treat a natural thing as a fellow sufferer, but the poem shows how the projection is built: she knows the sea grieves because she herself is awake to hear it, so the sympathy is evidence of her sleeplessness rather than a fancy."},{"a":"ku:174","b":"k:247","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 247 says this world is not for those without wealth and the other world not for those without grace; Kuruntokai 174 says that if he leaves for wealth, in this world wealth alone is wealth and grace has no one.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The heroine grants the Kural's premise about this world in its own words (இவ்வுலகத்து) and drops its consolation: with no other world in view, the couplet's balance of wealth and grace collapses into a world where grace has no owner."},{"a":"ku:174","b":"a:75","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 75 the man quotes the saying that effort, not grace, is a man's wealth and declines to follow it; in Kuruntokai 174 the woman, whose man did follow it, concludes that wealth alone is wealth and grace belongs to no one.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the wealth-over-grace maxim as a live argument of the paalai poems argued from both ends: the man can refuse it out of love, and the woman, when he does not, turns it from a rule of conduct into a bitter verdict on the whole world."},{"a":"ku:175","b":"k:1148","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1148 the woman says hoping to quench love with gossip is like quenching fire with ghee; in Kuruntokai 175 she refuses to tell everyone she is not grieving and asks what the gossip matters.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's woman answers the town with an argument that talk only feeds love, while the poem's woman refuses the remedy the friend proposes, public denial, so the pair separates two defences against gossip: rebutting its power and declining to perform the concealment that would silence it."},{"a":"ku:167","b":"n:363","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 363 calls a wife who does not enter the kitchen at dawn a disease and one who does not serve what she cooked a demon; Kuruntokai 167 shows a young wife, smoke in her kohl eyes, cooking and serving with delight at her husband's praise. On the Naladiyar's reading the act is a duty enforced by threat; on the poem's it is the wife's own joy.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The same kitchen duty that the ethics book defines by the horror of its failure is shown in the Sangam poem as freely chosen pleasure, so the pair shows the domestic norm seen from the husband's fear and from the wife's satisfaction."},{"a":"ku:172","b":"ku:180","type":"develops","note":"Both poems are by Kachchippettu Nannakaiyar and both ask about the man who left (துறந்தோர்): in 172 the woman at evening asks whether he is happy being alone; in 180 the friend asks whether he has grown hard in the land he went to.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the poet's two questions mark the turn of a long absence: the first still imagines him as lonely as she is, the second fears his heart has changed, so the doubt moves from his comfort to his constancy."},{"a":"ku:182","b":"k:1132","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 182: the lover asks whether throwing off his great shame (பெருநாண் நீக்கி) and riding the palm horse through the street will win her after the messenger failed; Kural 1132: body and life will mount the palm horse once shame is put aside (நாணினை நீக்கி).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the madal threat as a general law of the unbearable, while the Kuruntokai shows its occasion (a failed go-between) and counts its price in other men's mockery, so the Kural's 'putting shame aside' is a public cost the hero weighs, not a private loss."},{"a":"ku:184","b":"k:1084","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 184: the hero tells his companions his heart is caught in the net of the fisher girl's eyes; Kural 1084: the man says the artless girl's eyes have a look that eats the life of those who see them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both men describe a first sight as the girl's eyes acting on them, but the Kuruntokai draws the weapon from her own people's trade (the fishermen's fine net), showing that the Kural's abstract killing gaze was, in the older poetry, given a landscape and a craft."},{"a":"ku:185","b":"k:1236","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 185: the girl asks what use it is to tell him she has wasted because of him; Kural 1236: with bangles and shoulders slackening, what hurts her is hearing him called cruel.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both heroines decline to blame the man for their wasting body, but for opposite reasons: the Kural's woman defends him, the Kuruntokai's expects no answer from a man as unmoved as the rock under the beaten kaanthal, so the same refusal can be loyalty or despair."},{"a":"ku:187","b":"k:1291","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 187: he is harder than stone, yet her heart, never calling him hard, melts; Kural 1291: she asks her heart why it is not hers when his heart stays with him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kapilar gives the image and the Kural the argument of one complaint: the man's heart keeps to itself like stone while hers goes over to him, and together they show the heroine's quarrel is with her own heart's softness more than with his hardness."},{"a":"ku:188","b":"k:1221","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 188: evening has come, aiming at her beauty, when the mullai has budded and he has not come; Kural 1221: she tells evening it is not evening but a time that eats the lives of wives.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kuruntokai shows why the evening attacks: it arrives with the season he promised to return by, so its hostility is the broken promise made visible, which the Kural's address to the evening leaves unstated."},{"a":"ku:190","b":"k:1168","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 190: at midnight in the storm she hears the cattle stall's one bell ring with every step of the bull; Kural 1168: the night that puts all beings to sleep has her alone for company.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's claim that she alone keeps the night company is proved in the Kuruntokai by what she hears: only a wakeful listener counts the bull's every step by its bell, so the single sound measures the sleepless solitude the couplet asserts."},{"a":"ku:192","b":"a:97","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 192 and Akananuru 97: the friend says do not grieve (இனையல், ஆழேல்), and the heroine answers that she cannot stop weeping when the koel calls in the flowering mango.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same reply to the same comfort shows the spring koel in the mango was a fixed answer in the heroine's mouth: the long Akananuru spends its lines on the dangers of his road, while the Kuruntokai replaces them with the gesture of stroking flowerless hair, so the short form puts the proof in her body rather than in the landscape.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:196","b":"pn:176","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 196: the husband who once called neem sweet now calls the cool spring water of Pari's Parambu hot and brackish; Purananuru 176: the poet regrets the days lost like people who scorned the clear spring of Parambu because it was in their own town.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Read with the Purananuru, the friend's choice of Parambu's water carries its proverbial lesson: the sweetest thing is scorned when it is near, so she diagnoses the husband's turning as contempt bred by having the wife at home."},{"a":"ku:199","b":"k:1315","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 199: the lover tells his heart his love-sickness will last on into the other world; Kural 1315: he told her they would not part in this birth, and her eyes filled with tears.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's joke works only because Paranar's belief was the convention: love was expected to outlast this birth, so a lover who promised only 'this birth' had set a limit the older poem's lover would not accept."},{"a":"ku:200","b":"k:1207","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 200: surely he has forgotten, but we do not forget; Kural 1207: what would become of her if she forgot, when she knows no forgetting and remembering burns.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural keeps forgetting inside the woman, as a danger she cannot afford, while Avvaiyar turns it outward as a charge against the man, proven by the rains that came on time, so the same refusal to forget can be self-torment or an accusation.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:183","b":"a:34","type":"same_image","note":"Kuruntokai 183: the wife wonders whether he will see the stag parted from its doe; Akananuru 34: the hero, seeing a stag feed and guard its doe, bids his chariot hurry home.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the stag and doe of the scrub working from both ends of a separation: the waiting wife hopes he will see a parted pair and feel her loss, while the returning hero is moved by a pair together, so the same creatures carry absence for her and return for him."},{"a":"ku:202","b":"k:1120","type":"same_image","note":"Kuruntokai 202 sets the nerunji's flower, sweet to the eye, before its thorn as the lover's kindness before his cruelty; Kural 1120 uses only the nerunji fruit, the thorn, to say how soft things feel to her feet.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both poets know the nerunji as a plant that pleases first and pricks after; the couplet takes only the prick to praise a woman's delicacy, while the Sangam poem uses the order of flower and thorn to tell the history of a love, so the same plant can measure a body or a story."},{"a":"ku:204","b":"ku:206","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 204: the companion says love is neither a spirit nor a disease but a feast always new; 206: the hero says love brings unbearable grief and warns the wise to keep away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two poems are the two sides of the companion's rebuke and the hero's answer: the friend outside love calls it a light pleasure, and the man inside it calls it a pain the wise should flee, so the anthology lets each speaker's place decide what love is."},{"a":"ku:206","b":"n:41","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 206 tells the wise to keep from love because the woman is too sweet, her words like ambrosia; Naladiyar 41 tells men to turn from desire because the admired body is a frail bag the crows would tear.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same counsel, keep away from love, rests on opposite judgments of the beloved: the Sangam lover flees because she is ambrosia and the pain is love's own, the Jain moralist because she is carrion under skin, so the later ethic keeps the advice and reverses its reason.","gr":3},{"a":"ku:205","b":"k:1185","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1185: look there, my lover is going; look here, the pallor is spreading on my body. Kuruntokai 205 asks how the pallor on her forehead knew so soon, when the shore lord has only now driven away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's two pointing words make his going and her paling one instant; the Kuruntokai poem has the same instant but turns it into a question to the pallor itself, and supplies the scene the couplet leaves out, the white chariot rising like swans and the sea spray on its wheels."},{"a":"ku:207","b":"k:1151","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1151: if you are not leaving, tell me; tell your quick return to those who will still be alive. Kuruntokai 207: he left without telling her, thinking that if he spoke, going would become hard, and she heard of it from others.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's demand to be told assumes the parting is spoken; the Kuruntokai man knows the power of that speech so well that he avoids it, so the heroine is denied even the chance to answer him that the Kural's speaker uses."},{"a":"ku:210","b":"pn:150","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 150: Nalli, unnamed, roasts fat in his forest for a starving poet and gives him his own chain. Kuruntokai 210: the friend prices the meal owed to the crow that called a guest in the ghee of the herds of Nalli's forest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Holding the two together shows why Nalli's forest is the measure in the love poem: he was remembered as the host who fed a stranger in the woods unasked, so the friend, repaying a bird for bringing a guest, reaches for the most famous hospitality she knows and still calls it too little."},{"a":"ku:213","b":"a:59","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 213: the friend says he has deep love, since on his road the stag goes hungry and stands as shade for its young. Akananuru 59: the friend says on his road the bull elephant bends a tree so its mate can eat, yet those men do not remember her.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same device, a beast caring for its own on the road the man took, is used for opposite ends: in Kuruntokai the friend makes it proof that he will come back, in the Akananuru she makes it the reproach that he does not, so the image does not carry its comfort in itself but takes it from the speaker's verdict."},{"a":"ku:213","b":"a:34","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 34: the returning man sees a stag feed its doe and guard her sleep and bids his charioteer hurry home. Kuruntokai 213: the friend says that on his road, they say, the stag starves to shelter its young, so he has deep love.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The friend's comfort in Kuruntokai is a prediction that the man will see the beasts' care and be moved; Akananuru 34 shows that very sight working on the man himself, so the two poems give the two ends of one iraicchi, the promise at home and its fulfilment on the road."},{"a":"ku:216","b":"kn:1","type":"tension","note":"Kar Narpathu 1: the friend says the man who promised to come when the rain hung low can hardly fail to come now that the clouds roar and pour. Kuruntokai 216: the heroine says the great cloud pours, thunders and flashes with no pity, aimed at her life.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The rain of the promised season is the same sign in both, but the friend reads it as his oath falling due and the heroine as the deadline passing on her; set side by side, the poems show the rainy season carries both senses at once, and the speaker decides which is heard."},{"a":"ku:216","b":"kn:3","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu 3: the friend, showing her own distress, says the wind, hail and thundering sky act as if all aimed at one lone woman. Kuruntokai 216: the heroine says the thundering cloud flashes with her life as its aim.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The complaint that the storm is aimed at her is the heroine's own in the older poem, and in the later venba the friend speaks it for her about 'one woman', so the charge against the rain had become a formula that could be handed from the sufferer to her companion.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:218","b":"k:1209","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1209: her life is ending as she thinks of the unkindness of one who said they were not separate. Kuruntokai 218: he, like life to her life, who could not bear a parting even a blink long, can now forget her and stay away, and she will still think of him.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The couplet names the wound as the broken claim of oneness; the Kuruntokai poem gives the claim its measure, not a blink apart, and adds what the woman does with the wound, vows, omens and thought, so the poem shows the couplet's dying as a life kept busy with ritual."},{"a":"ku:219","b":"k:1255","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1255: the dignity of not going after one who has turned away is unknown to those sick with love. Kuruntokai 219: her restraint has gone far and her mind urges her to go to him, but she holds back for fear he will ask 'who are you?'","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet says the lovesick cannot know the pride of not following; the Kuruntokai heroine, lovesick, still stays, held back not by pride but by fear of being met as a stranger, so the poem shows a restraint that survives the loss of honour and the couplet's claim is too absolute."},{"a":"ku:220","b":"k:1227","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1227: this sickness buds in the morning, swells through the day and blossoms in the evening. Kuruntokai 220: in the evening the jasmine opens its small buds like a wild cat smiling, and the man who left for wealth has not come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes a flower's day the shape of love-sickness; the Kuruntokai poem shows the real flower, the mullai opening its buds at dusk in the season he named, at the moment her grief opens, so the metaphor rests on the evening flowering of the jasmine land."},{"a":"ku:220","b":"kn:14","type":"tension","note":"Kar Narpathu 14: the friend says they are sure he will come quickly, since the rain cloud laughs softly as the mullai shows its bright teeth. Kuruntokai 220: the jasmine buds open like a wild cat smiling, the evening is here, and he has not come.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Both poems read the opening jasmine as a smile with teeth, but in the friend's venba it is the season laughing in welcome and in the heroine's Kuruntokai poem it is a wild cat's grin, so the one image turns from promise to mockery once the day of return has passed."},{"a":"ku:221","b":"a:274","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 274 the returning man says her town lies in the rainy scrub where a lone herdsman stands in the drizzle with his lambs; in Kuruntokai 221 the waiting woman sees the goatherd with his lambs crowned with mullai buds and says the man has not come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The herdsman of the mullai scrub is the season's witness from both sides: on the man's road he marks the way home, at the woman's door he wears the proof that the promised season has come without the man, so one figure serves as sign of return or of broken promise."},{"a":"ku:222","b":"k:1122","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1122: the man says the bond between him and the girl is that of body and life; in Kuruntokai 222 the man says she takes the head of the raft when he does, the tail when he does, and would follow him into the current.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet names the inseparability as a metaphor, and the poem shows how a lover knows it: she mirrors his every movement in play and would follow him into danger, so the body-and-life bond is proved by action before it is stated."},{"a":"ku:225","b":"pn:34","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 34 states that there is no expiation for killing a benefit and binds the poet to praise the king who fed the bards; Kuruntokai 225 makes a king who forgets, once enthroned, the help he had in ruin the type of ingratitude the lover must not imitate.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poem casts the king as the benefactor to whom gratitude is owed, while the akam friend can cite the king who forgets his helpers as a familiar figure, so the same norm of gratitude was also turned against kings, and borrowed to bind a lover to marriage."},{"a":"ku:225","b":"k:110","type":"develops","note":"Kural 110: there is escape for every other killing of good, none for one who kills a benefit; in Kuruntokai 225 the friend promises the girl's hair to the man only if he does not forget kindness as an enthroned king does.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's absolute rule becomes a condition of marriage in the poem: the friend uses ingratitude, the one unpardonable fault, to define in advance what deserting the girl would be."},{"a":"ku:228","b":"k:1249","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1249: the woman asks her heart whom it goes to seek when the lover is inside it; Kuruntokai 228: though he left for a far country, he is near to her heart.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same indwelling is used two ways: the couplet makes it a reason to scold the restless heart, while the poem makes it a consolation that cancels the distance, so the lover in the heart can either rebuke longing or quiet it."},{"a":"ku:229","b":"ku:40","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 40 marvels that two whose mothers, fathers and selves were strangers have hearts mixed like rain in red earth; Kuruntokai 229 marvels that two who fought as children under their nurses' eyes have been joined in marriage, and credits fate (பால்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The anthology explains a match both between strangers and between childhood neighbours, and in each case puts the cause outside the families: loving hearts mixing on their own in 40, fate's apportioning in 229, so union by kin arrangement is the case neither poem celebrates."},{"a":"ku:231","b":"k:1247","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1247: the woman tells her heart to give up either desire or shame, since she cannot bear both; Kuruntokai 231: neglected by the man in her own town, she says her desire, having killed shame and lost good sense, has gone far like a loosed arrow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet presents a choice still open between desire and shame, while the poem shows it already made and past recall, desire having killed shame on its own, so the Kural's dilemma is the moment before the poem's confession."},{"a":"ku:233","b":"k:85","type":"develops","note":"Kural 85 asks whether the field of one who feeds guests and eats the leftover (மிச்சில்) needs sowing at all; Kuruntokai 233 praises her father's town where the leftover (மிச்சில்) of rice poured out for the great is shared with all without limit.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The love poet measures a bride's family by the very household ethic the Kural states, even with its word for the leftover, so the hospitality rule was already a mark of a good marriage before it was a maxim about prosperity."},{"a":"ku:234","b":"k:1227","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1227: the sickness buds in the morning, opens through the day and blooms at evening; Kuruntokai 234: for those without a partner the dawn and the day are evening too.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet keeps evening as the climax of a daily growth, while the poem refuses any such gradation and makes every hour evening, so on one reading the parted woman has no easier morning at all, which the Kural's flower image allows.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:236","b":"k:1183","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1183: the woman says he took her grace and modesty and gave sickness and pallor in exchange; Kuruntokai 236: the friend tells the man that if he means to leave he must first give back the beauty he has consumed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet records the exchange as done, and the poem turns the same commercial idea into a demand for repayment that cannot be met, which makes it an argument that he must stay."},{"a":"ku:237","b":"k:1108","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1108: an embrace so close not even the wind can part it is sweet to two who desire each other; Kuruntokai 237: the man far away says he cannot count what now stands between their embrace, groves where tigers roar.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the measure of separation is the same embrace the couplet praises: where once not even a breeze passed between them, now forests and tigers do, so the poem's distance is felt as a gap inside the embrace."},{"a":"ku:238","b":"a:60","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 60 the friend warns the hero not to crush the bangle of a girl fine as Porayan's Tondi; in Kuruntokai 238 the friend tells the lord of the fields to give back a beauty that was like Tondi and go.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both friends price the girl's beauty by the Chera port of Tondi, one before harm, as a warning, and one after, as a demand for restitution, so the town's fame served as the standard of a beauty that can be damaged and must be repaid."},{"a":"ku:239","b":"k:1234","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1234: with her partner gone, her shoulders lose their old beauty and her bangles slip; Kuruntokai 239: her bangles have slipped and her shoulders wasted, so is there shame left to give up?","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet lists the slipping bangles as a sign of grief, while the poem draws from the same sign a conclusion: since the body has already told, modesty has nothing left to hide, so the wasting licenses the woman to speak out.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:240","b":"ku:234","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 234 says that for those without a partner every hour is evening; Kuruntokai 240 shows one evening's work, as the north wind blows and his hill sinks from view like a ship.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show why evening is the worst hour: in 234 it is a state that swallows all hours, in 240 it is the moment the last visible trace of him, his hill, is taken away, so the evening's pain is the loss of sight of him.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:241","b":"k:1171","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1171: the woman asks why her eyes weep, when it was they who showed him to her and brought on the sickness; Kuruntokai 241: she bears her desire, while her eyes, which saw the hill man, weep out of their own intimacy.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet blames the eyes as the cause that now complains, while the poem excuses them as loyal intimates of the man, so the same weeping eyes can be charged as culprits or honoured as the part of her that keeps faith with what it saw."},{"a":"ku:243","b":"k:1207","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1207: she does not know forgetting, and even remembering burns; Kuruntokai 243: she resolves not to think of the shore lord so that her eyes may sleep.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The poem tries the remedy the couplet says is impossible, a vow not to remember, and the very need to vow it confirms the couplet: remembering keeps her awake and forgetting cannot be chosen, only resolved."},{"a":"ku:245","b":"k:1188","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1188: people say she has grown pale, and no one says he left her; Kuruntokai 245: worse than her lost beauty would be his unkindness becoming known to many.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On the plain reading the two women want opposite things from the town: the couplet's woman resents that the blame falls on her body and not on him, the poem's woman dreads exactly that blame reaching him, so the lover can either demand that the fault be named or shield it at her own cost."},{"a":"ku:247","b":"k:791","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 791: nothing is as ruinous as befriending without inquiry, for once formed a friendship cannot be left; Kuruntokai 247: the friend says she knew then that the man's friendship is flawless, fruitful, righteous and a raft for kin.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's test of friendship is meant to come before the bond, but in secret love the bond comes first, so the friend's knowing it to be flawless arrives after the fact and has to argue, in the Kural's own terms of fruit and kin, that the inquiry would have passed."},{"a":"ku:250","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1268: let the king fight and win, and let me join my home and feast the evening; Kuruntokai 250: the man tells his charioteer to drive the swift horses before evening comes, for her distress.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet names evening as the feast the returning man looks to; the poem shows why it is a deadline as well as a reward: evening is the hour the stag is with its mate and the hour she suffers most alone, so he must beat it.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:250","b":"ku:65","type":"same_image","note":"Kuruntokai 65: the stag that drank the clear water among the hard pebbles frisks with its sweet mate, as if mocking the woman who waits; Kuruntokai 250: the stag that drank the water of the pebbled hollow frisks with its mate as the man hurries home.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The anthology uses one sight from both ends of the separation: to the waiting woman the frisking pair is a taunt, to the returning man it is a spur, so the same iraicchi accuses the absence on one side and hastens its end on the other."},{"a":"ku:251","b":"ku:66","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 66: the friend calls the konrai foolish for taking a stray rain for the season and flowering; Kuruntokai 251: the friend calls the peacocks foolish for dancing to a stray cloud's thunder, and explains the cloud is only emptying its old water.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the friend's consolation as an argument about evidence, not a denial of rain: in 66 the flowers misread the rain, in 251 the cloud's thunder is given a cause of its own, so the friend separates rain from season, the very distinction the man's promise rests on."},{"a":"ku:252","b":"k:1286","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1286: when she sees him she sees no fault, when she does not see him she sees only faults; Kuruntokai 252: urged by her friend to reproach him, she says the worthy cannot speak blame to a face.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet presents her silence before him as a blindness of love; the poem's woman sees the fault plainly and calls him crooked, yet keeps silent by a rule of the noble, so the same silence can be read as love's blindness or as chosen restraint."},{"a":"ku:255","b":"a:59","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 59: on the road for wealth an elephant bends a yaa tree so its young mate can eat, yet the man forgets her; Kuruntokai 255: on that road the elephant strips the yaa tree to feed its whole hungry herd, and the men went to fulfil their duty.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same road-sign is turned two ways by the friend: feeding one mate in Akananuru 59 shames the man who forgets his wife, while feeding the whole herd in Kuruntokai 255 matches the man's duty to his household, so the friend can use the elephant to reproach the journey or to justify it.","gr":4},{"a":"ku:256","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1151: if you are not leaving tell me; tell your quick return to those who will still be alive; Kuruntokai 256: before he could say he would return when the work was done, her weeping eyes blocked his chariot.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the woman's spoken refusal of the promise of a quick return; the poem shows that refusal made without words, before the promise is spoken, and records that it worked: the chariot stopped."},{"a":"ku:260","b":"k:1234","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1234: with her partner gone her shoulders have lost their fullness and the bangles slip; Kuruntokai 260: the bangled shoulders are filling out again, read with heron and bush as a sign he will return.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the shoulders a record of his absence; the poem reads them forward as an omen of his return, so the body that registers separation is also consulted to foretell its end."},{"a":"ku:263","b":"a:292","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 292 the girl dreads the stranger velan's rite and the kid's killing because she would be ashamed; in Kuruntokai 263 the friend protests that the same rite praises strange gods that cure nothing and brands the girl as possessed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two poems show the veri rite wounding the secret love from both sides: to the girl it threatens exposure, and to the friend it threatens a false name, possession, for a love she insists is blameless."},{"a":"ku:265","b":"k:804","type":"develops","note":"Kural 804: the wise welcome the unasked acts of old friends; Kuruntokai 265 likens the kaanthal bud opening to the bee to duty-knowing men opening to the worthy they have long known, then shows the man ashamed and ready to act once told.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's ethic of intimacy is used in the poem as a simile for nature and then as a forecast of the lover's conduct, so the old-friendship rule becomes the friend's evidence that he will marry without being pressed."},{"a":"ku:267","b":"k:335","type":"tension","note":"Kural 335 says that, because death stops the tongue unannounced, good deeds must be rushed to; the man of Kuruntokai 267 argues from Death's crooked, daily taking that those who know it do not leave their women to go after work and wealth.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The same awareness of death leads to opposite conduct: the Kural spends the short life on merit done in haste, the lover on staying with her and refusing the journey, so impermanence by itself does not decide between duty and love."},{"a":"ku:270","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1268: let the king fight and win, and let me join my wife and feast the evening; Kuruntokai 270: his work done, the man lies on her hair and bids the rain pour.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's wish is the poem's fulfilled state: the evening feast he pictured while the king's work held him is given its weather and its pillow, and the rain he dreaded on campaign becomes a blessing once he is home."},{"a":"ku:270","b":"ku:275","type":"develops","note":"Both poems use the same line, செய்வினை முடித்த செம்மல் உள்ளம், for the man whose task is done: in 270 he says it of himself, already with her in the rain; in 275 the friend imagines it of him as she listens for a bell at dusk.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The formula is the hinge of the mullai return seen from both sides of the door: the friend's hopeful projection in 275 is exactly what the man claims in 270, so the waiting and the arrival are framed by one satisfied heart."},{"a":"ku:271","b":"ku:280","type":"tension","note":"In 280 the man would give up even half a day of life after a single day's union; in 271 the woman says one day's union has become a disease lasting very many days.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same single day of union is priced in opposite currencies: the man offers the rest of his life for it, and the woman, who has had it, pays in many days of wasting, so the two speakers measure the secret love by different costs."},{"a":"ku:271","b":"k:1269","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1269: for those who wait for the day of return, one day passes like seven; Kuruntokai 271: one day of union has turned into very many days of sickness.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The couplet stretches the waiting days, the poem multiplies the one day of joy into a long illness, so the woman's suffering is measured not only by slow time but by a debt that a single meeting keeps collecting.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:272","b":"k:1085","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1085 asks whether her look is Death, an eye or a doe; Kuruntokai 272 likens her eyes to the bloody arrow her brothers drew from a stag near a doe parted from its herd.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem stages the couplet's three-way question as one hunt: doe, killing shaft and eye stand together, and because the shaft belongs to her brothers, the deadliness of her look is also the threat of her kin to the suitor."},{"a":"ku:274","b":"ku:262","type":"develops","note":"In 262 the friend says the girl has chosen to drink elephant-footprint water with him in a far country; in 274 the man says the cruel forest will be sweet if he goes holding her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The claim that the beloved's company sweetens the desert is made for the girl by the friend and by the man for himself, so the elopement rests on a premise both lovers hold, voiced by different speakers."},{"a":"ku:276","b":"k:541","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 541 defines justice as investigating without favour and acting after judgement toward everyone; in Kuruntokai 276 the lover thinks of taking his case to the just king's court and at once asks what would become of her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The lover's question shows where the Kural's impartial justice stops: a harm done without intent by an innocent girl is real but cannot be judged, so the just court is imagined only to be abandoned."},{"a":"ku:280","b":"k:1124","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1124: she is to him as life when present and as death when she goes; Kuruntokai 280: for one day's union he would not want half a day of life after.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet equates her presence with life, the poem goes further and trades the rest of life for one day of her, so the man's praise of love moves from equation to sacrifice."},{"a":"ku:283","b":"k:1064","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 283 reports the man's maxim that the life of those who have nothing is more shameful even than begging, and he leaves for wealth; Kural 1064 says the worth that will not beg even when nothing is left is too great for the world to hold. On the reading that 283 ranks poverty below begging, the two rank the same states in opposite order.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the destitute man who refuses to beg the height of dignity, while the akam hero's maxim makes destitution itself the deepest shame, so the poems show that the urge to go for wealth rested on a ranking of poverty and begging the Kural explicitly reverses."},{"a":"ku:288","b":"k:1103","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1103: the man asks whether the lotus-eyed one's world is sweeter than sleep on the beloved's soft arms; Kuruntokai 288: the woman asks whether the world of the gods is sweeter even than the hurt that comes from kin because of her lover.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet weighs heaven against the joy of love, the poem weighs it against love's pain, so the woman's claim is the stronger: not only the embrace but even the suffering it brings outweighs the gods' world."},{"a":"ku:299","b":"k:1233","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1233: the shoulders that swelled on the day of union now seem to announce the parting; Kuruntokai 299: the woman's shoulders gain beauty when he joins her and waste when he leaves, after the tryst on the shore.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the swelling and the wasting as public evidence that others read, while the poem gives them as the woman's own bewildered discovery, tied to the day her eyes saw him and her ears heard him, so the same bodily sign is both the town's proof and her private record."},{"a":"ku:289","b":"k:1130","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1130: he lives joyfully in her heart, while the town says he is unloving and lives apart; Kuruntokai 289: she grieves for him out in the rain, and the clamouring town grieves for her more.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In both the woman's thought goes out to the absent man while the town's goes to her plight, but the town's verdict differs, blame in the Kural and pity in the poem, so the town's talk in akam is not fixed as hostile gossip and can be her chorus.","gr":3},{"a":"ku:290","b":"a:45","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 45, told to bear up, the woman answers with the dangers of his road, the gossip and her own coming madness like Aathi Manthi's; Kuruntokai 290 answers the same counsel ('bear your love') by saying she will waste away like foam on a rock.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two poems answer the same counsel to endure in opposite registers, one with public ruin and a famous madness, the other with a silent dissolving, which shows that the heroine's refusal of endurance could be voiced as loud breakdown or as quiet vanishing."},{"a":"ku:292","b":"ku:73","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 73 (Paranar) tells of the Kosar who cut down Nannan's fragrant mango and drove him from his land; Kuruntokai 292 (Paranar) tells of Nannan killing a girl who ate a green fruit the stream carried down, refusing eighty-one elephants and her weight in gold.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, Paranar's two poems hold both halves of one tale, the cruelty over a fruit and the tree's later felling, and each friend uses a different half: one as a model of hard scheming, the other as the type of cruelty to curse a watchful mother with.","gr":3},{"a":"ku:298","b":"pn:233","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 233 names Akuthai as the strong one whose elephants never ran short for those who came for gifts; Kuruntokai 298 recalls the young she-elephants Akuthai's father gave to singing women and likens the lover's long waiting to that gift.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The puram lament knows the house of Akuthai only as givers of elephants, and the akam friend turns that known generosity into a figure for a lover's patience, so a patron's fame for gifts served akam as a ready image of an act that waits on a return."},{"a":"ku:300","b":"ku:288","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kuruntokai 300 the man says he would not leave her even for the sea-ringed world; in Kuruntokai 288 the woman says even the hurt love brings is sweeter than the world of the gods.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The man measures his love against earthly rule and the woman against heaven, so the two speakers of kalavu each renounce the highest good of their own sphere, kingship for him, the gods' world for her."},{"a":"ku:301","b":"k:1179","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1179 says her eyes do not sleep when he fails to come nor when he comes; in Kuruntokai 301 he does not come, and the bell of a chariot that is not there rings in her ear at midnight while her eyes give up sleep.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem shows how the couplet's 'when he does not come' still keeps the eyes open: the ear makes his chariot out of the night, so absence is lived as an arrival that never quite happens."},{"a":"ku:302","b":"k:1205","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1205 rebukes the man for barring her from his heart while he keeps coming into hers; in Kuruntokai 302 the man likewise comes only into her heart at midnight, and she asks whether fear of gossip keeps him away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they give two verdicts on the same phantom visit: the couplet calls it shameless trespass, the poem excuses it as fear of the town, so whether his absence is a fault depends on the reason the heroine allows him."},{"a":"ku:302","b":"k:1150","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1150 says the town is raising the very gossip they want, since it will bring the lover round; in Kuruntokai 302 the gossip that calls the pair inseparable is what she fears has kept him away. On this reading the same talk helps in one and harms in the other.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The pair shows alar cutting both ways in secret love: the talk the Kural's speaker welcomes as a lever toward marriage can, as the poem fears, frighten a lover into staying away altogether.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:305","b":"k:1171","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1171 says the eyes showed him and so she came to see an unending sickness; Kuruntokai 305 opens with the bright fire of love that the eyes brought and follows it to a sickness no one can stop.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet blames the eyes and stops at 'unending'; the poem carries the same sickness to its end in the dunghill cocks' fight that only death stops, so 'unending' is shown to mean ending only in death.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:307","b":"a:91","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 91 the friend points to the elephant that shrinks together with its hungry mate on the scorched road as proof that the man still loves; in Kuruntokai 307 the heroine points to a tusker that fells a tree to feed its weary mate as proof that he has forgotten.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same iraicchi of the tusker's care on the wasteland serves the friend as comfort and the heroine as reproach, so the device's sense is fixed by who is speaking and what she wants, not by the beast."},{"a":"ku:309","b":"k:1288","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1288 tells the thief that his chest is to her as toddy is to drinkers, though it does them shameful harm; Kuruntokai 309 says the women are like the neythal that blooms again in the field it was weeded from, though he does them many unkind things.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both say she cannot leave him though he harms her, but the Kural calls the bond an addiction while the poem calls it rootedness in his own field, so the pair shows the same dependence judged as weakness and as loyalty."},{"a":"ku:310","b":"k:1222","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1222 asks the dim evening whether its partner is as hard-hearted as hers; Kuruntokai 310 sees the sky dazed like herself at day's end and then says she may yet live if someone will carry word to him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet stops at fellow-feeling with the evening; the poem turns that fellow-feeling into a need for a messenger, so the evening lament is shown as the point where the heroine starts to seek a go-between."},{"a":"ku:311","b":"ku:212","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 212 has the friend say the shore lord's chariot comes, bells ringing, only to be seen and goes back leaving shame; in Kuruntokai 311 the chariot that would not stop was seen by the whole troop of flower-gathering girls, and the heroine asks how the gossip can end.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the chariot that the friend in 212 calls mere display is in 311 the very evidence the gossip rests on, so the lover's conspicuous visits are both his courtship and the cause of the alar."},{"a":"ku:312","b":"pn:123","type":"same_image","note":"Kapilar praises Malaiyan in Purananuru 123 as giving more chariots than the raindrops on the heights of Mullur; in Kuruntokai 312, also Kapilar's, the girl comes at midnight filling Malaiyan's Mullur forest with her fragrance.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same poet uses his patron's hill both as the measure of a king's countless gifts and as the secret ground of a tryst, which shows a court poet setting his love scenes inside the land he praised in puram."},{"a":"ku:312","b":"k:1271","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1271 has the man tell the girl that though she hides it, her eyes go beyond her control and speak; in Kuruntokai 312 the man marvels that by dawn she is wholly one of her kin again, face unmoved. On the poem's reading her concealment holds.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pair sets two views of the girl's secret side by side: the Kural's man reads it in her eyes, Kapilar's man admires a disguise he cannot see through, so concealment in kalavu is both a failure and an art."},{"a":"ku:304","b":"ku:313","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 313 says the friendship with the lord of the shore is knotted and settled, hard to untie; Kuruntokai 304 says the friendship made with the lord of the shore has brought enmity.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"Held together they show why 304's grief is so sharp: the bond that 313 praises as impossible to untie is the same bond that, once it brings hostility, cannot be undone either."},{"a":"ku:313","b":"k:791","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 791 warns that nothing ruins like befriending without inquiry, since for the faithful there is no leaving once the friendship is made; Kuruntokai 313 says the friendship with the shore lord is tied and settled, hard to untie.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The heroine states as her settled joy the very fact the Kural gives as the reason for caution, so the pair shows that love's bond is the friendship the ethics book warns about, entered without the inquiry it prescribes."},{"a":"ku:314","b":"kn:12","type":"tension","note":"Kar Narpathu 12 has the friend say that his coming is certain, shown beyond doubt by the dark clouds that rise every day; in Kuruntokai 314 the full rain cloud flashes lightning at evening and the heroine says he still does not come. On this reading the sign the friend calls certain has failed.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Set beside the Kar Narpathu's confident friend, the poem shows the other side of the season-sign: the cloud that is offered as proof of return becomes, when he does not come, the measure of his broken word.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:315","b":"ku:202","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 202 compares the lover to the nerunji, which first gives a sweet flower and then thorns; Kuruntokai 315 makes the man the sun and her shoulders the nerunji that turns to it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"One weed of poor ground carries two opposite lessons in the same anthology, its turning to the sun as her dependence and its later thorns as his change, so the heroine's image in 315 already holds the pain that 202 states."},{"a":"ku:318","b":"k:1288","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1288 addresses the lover as thief whose chest is like toddy that harms; Kuruntokai 318 calls the man thief too, but also the one bound by his oath and the raft who must carry her across.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's 'thief' names only the sweet harm, while the poem turns the same title into a claim: whoever stole is bound to restore, so the word becomes the ground of her demand for marriage."},{"a":"ku:319","b":"k:1221","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1221 says the hour is not evening but a spear that eats the life of the wedded; in Kuruntokai 319 the rain-evening comes as the stag and the elephant take shelter with their mates, and she asks what will become of her life if he does not come.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem shows why the evening kills: it is the hour when every creature goes to its mate, so the Kural's bare claim that evening eats life gets its cause in the paired beasts the heroine watches."},{"a":"ku:320","b":"k:1146","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1146 says she saw him on one day only, yet the talk has spread like the serpent seizing the moon; Kuruntokai 320 says they have not even the blame of laughing together one day, yet the town spreads gossip.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural concedes one meeting behind the talk while the poem denies even that, so the pair shows the gossip of kalavu needing ever less fact, until in 320 it rests on nothing but the town's cruelty."},{"a":"ku:323","b":"k:1103","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1103 asks whether heaven is sweeter than sleeping on the beloved's soft arms; in Kuruntokai 323 the man calls every other day chaff and counts as lived only the days spent sleeping on her shoulders.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet ranks the embrace above heaven's pleasure; the poem turns the same ranking on time itself, so the pair shows the claim growing from a comparison of joys into a measure of which days of a life count as lived."},{"a":"ku:327","b":"k:1192","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1192 says a lover's grace to those who love is like rain to those who live by it; Kuruntokai 327's man has learned to withhold grace from those who live only if he is gracious (நல்கின் வாழும்), and water reaches her as a flood from his hill.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the couplet's rain of grace is shown in reverse: the one who lives by it is denied it, and the water that does come down from the lover's hill is muddy and destroys, so dependence on grace is seen from the side of its failure."},{"a":"ku:328","b":"pn:151","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 151 refuses the young Vicchi lord the poet's embrace because of a door shut against singers; Kuruntokai 328 measures gossip by the uproar of Kurumbur when bards saw the Vicchiyar chief stand like a tiger against the kings.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Vicchi house is remembered by singers in two ways at once, its war-cry loud enough to be an akam poet's yardstick for noise and its door shut against bards in puram, so the pair shows that a chief's fame for valour could circulate apart from, and despite, his name for giving."},{"a":"ku:329","b":"k:1167","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1167 the woman swims love's flood without seeing a shore and at midnight is the only one awake; in Kuruntokai 329 she cannot sleep in midnight's thick dark, thinking of the man on the desert road.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the midnight wakefulness as a bare state; the poem gives it a content, the road he took buried under fallen iluppai flowers, so the pair shows the sleepless mind of separation travelling the absent man's road.","gr":3},{"a":"ku:330","b":"k:1222","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1222 the woman asks the sad-eyed evening whether its partner is as hard as hers; in Kuruntokai 330 she asks whether the sorrowful evening (புன்கண் மாலை) and its loneliness exist at all in the land he went to.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both make the evening testify against the absent man, but in opposite ways: the couplet makes it a fellow sufferer, the poem imagines a land without it, which would explain why he stays away, so the pair shows the evening serving as companion and as accusation."},{"a":"ku:331","b":"ku:63","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kuruntokai 63 the man's heart argues that work for wealth must be pressed because the penniless can neither give nor enjoy; in 331 the friend says he goes for wealth he rates above them (நம்மினுஞ் சிறந்த).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same journey for wealth is a duty in the man's reckoning and a ranking of the women below gold in the friend's, so the pair shows that the paalai departure is argued from both houses and that neither side's account mentions the other's."},{"a":"ku:334","b":"k:1155","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1155 the woman tells her friend to prevent the departure of the one fit for her, since once he goes union is hard; in Kuruntokai 334 she tells her friend that if he leaves, what they lose is only their one life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both speak to the friend before a parting, but the couplet asks for help to stop it while the poem asks for none and names the price, her life, so the pair shows the same plea in two moods, one still bargaining and one already resigned."},{"a":"ku:337","b":"k:1081","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1081 the man cannot tell whether she is an anangu, a peahen or a woman; in Kuruntokai 337 he is sure she is an anangu (அணங்கென) and adds that she herself does not know it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's bewilderment is about what she is; the poem moves it to the gap between her power and her ignorance of it, the one-sided love of kaikkilai, so the pair shows the anangu figure turning from a lover's confusion into an observation about a girl not yet aware of herself."},{"a":"ku:338","b":"a:34","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 34 the returning man sees a stag feed and guard its doe and hurries his chariot home; in Kuruntokai 338 the friend sets a stag resting with its doe at evening beside the news that the victorious chariot has come.","grade":"sound","better":"same_image","te":"The stag with its doe is the spur to return in the man's mouth and the sign that the return has happened in the friend's, so the pair shows one iraicchi working at both ends of the journey, as motive and as fulfilment."},{"a":"ku:338","b":"ku:65","type":"tension","note":"In Kuruntokai 65 the stag back with its mate in the rains mocks the waiting woman because her man has not come; in 338 the same pairing at evening comes with the chariot that ends her pallor. On the waiting woman's reading in 65 the sign is false; in 338 it is kept.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image","te":"The same image of the stag beside its mate is read as a reproach when the man fails the season and as a promise kept when he arrives, so the pair shows that the iraicchi carries no fixed sense apart from whether the man returns."},{"a":"ku:340","b":"k:1249","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1249 the woman asks her heart whom it goes seeking, since the lover is within it; in Kuruntokai 340 her heart, where he dwells, can settle on neither side and sways like a kandal with ebb and tide.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet treats the heart's going out to him as an absurdity to point out; the poem's rooted kandal, bent out by the ebb and back by the flood, makes the same divided heart a rhythm she cannot stop, so the paradox becomes a condition rather than an error."},{"a":"ku:325","b":"ku:176","type":"qualifies","note":"The line ஆசா கெந்தை யாண்டுளன் கொல்லோ closes the friend's memory of how he won the girl in 176 and stands in 325 after the heroine admits she herself told him to go.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same cry for the lost support carries blame of the man in the friend's poem and self-reproach in the heroine's, so the pair shows a formula line taking its charge from who speaks it and what precedes it."},{"a":"ku:344","b":"a:54","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 54 the returning man sees the milch herd hurrying home at dusk to the calves with the cowherds behind and wants to reach his wife and son; in Kuruntokai 344 the waiting woman sees the cows with dripping udders leave the herd for the village at dusk and says only the fortunate see their lovers return.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same dusk image of cows hurrying home to their calves is the man's cue to return when he speaks and the woman's reproach when she speaks, so the herd's homing is a fixed sign of the duty to come home that each side reads against the other."},{"a":"ku:345","b":"a:110","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 110 a stranger asks the bathing girls if he may eat with them and stay in their noisy fishing hamlet, and they turn him away; in Kuruntokai 345 the friend herself asks the man whether staying with his chariot at their small town by the great water would be a fault.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The night's stay in the fishing hamlet is the neythal courtship's measure of trust: asked for by the stranger at the start and refused, it is offered by the friend once the love is known, so who proposes the stay marks how far the affair has gone.","gr":3},{"a":"ku:349","b":"ku:236","type":"tension","note":"In Kuruntokai 236 the friend tells the lord of the shore to give back the beauty he consumed before he leaves; in Kuruntokai 349 the woman answers such a proposal by saying that demanding back what was given is worse than losing one's life.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The demand to return her beauty, which the friend can make as a just claim, the woman herself refuses because it treats her love as a loan, so the same claim is fair in a friend's mouth and shameful in the giver's, by the code that a gift is not taken back."},{"a":"ku:350","b":"k:333","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 333 says wealth does not stay, so whoever gets it should at once do what lasts; in Kuruntokai 350 the friend calls the wealth for which the man left நிலையாப் பொருள், wealth that does not stay, and regrets they did not stop him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The ethicist's premise that wealth does not stay becomes in akam an argument against the journey itself: the Kural spends unstable wealth on lasting good, while the friend counts the lasting thing given up as the woman's company, so the same premise judges the pursuit of wealth from the household's side."},{"a":"ku:351","b":"k:1150","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1150 the friend tells the woman the town raises the very gossip they want, since the lover will grant marriage when asked; in Kuruntokai 351 the friend tells the girl to rejoice because their kin have declared the marriage, and asks whether the gossiping town is still the same.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet hopes the town's gossip will force the marriage, and the poem shows the end of that hope: once the kin declare the marriage, the talk that pressed for it has nothing left to say, so the gossip served its purpose and the friend can mock it.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:352","b":"k:1226","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1226: the woman did not know evening brings sickness while her husband had not left; Kuruntokai 352: the woman says she knew the reality of the small dull evening only when she could not see him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet states the late discovery of evening's pain as a bare fact, and the poem gives the sign through which she learned it, the creature that leaves its day-tree lonely at dusk, so the knowledge the Kural reports is shown arising from a scene in which the hour itself repeats the parting."},{"a":"ku:356","b":"a:105","type":"develops","note":"Both foster mothers ask யாங்கு வல்லுநள்கொல் தானே of a girl who refused milk held up in a fine vessel at home: Akananuru 105 follows the question with the raiders' road of Ezhini's bowmen, Kuruntokai 356 with the scalding muddy water she must gulp.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The shared formula of the refused milk and 'how will she manage' is a stock frame of the foster mother's lament, and the two poems show its slot filled differently: the long poem measures the girl's leaving by the road's dangers, the short one by what she must now drink, so the same frame can carry either fear or tenderness.","gr":4},{"a":"ku:358","b":"k:1261","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1261: the woman's fingers are worn from marking the days since he left; Kuruntokai 358: the friend speaks to the woman who scores lines and clings to the wall, and points to the mullai buds as the season he promised.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the counting of days only as the woman's wasting, while the poem lets the friend answer it with a sign outside the wall, the season's buds, so the count the Kural leaves open is closed by the promised season and becomes ground for comfort."},{"a":"ku:359","b":"a:26","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 26 the wife turns to their son and the husband, saying he too loves the child, embraces her back and weeps; in Kuruntokai 359 the friend tells the bard that the man embraced his son and the son's mother embraced the man's back.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In both reconciliations the son carries the embrace between husband and wife, and with the roles reversed (in one the husband clasps the wife's back, in the other the wife the husband's), so the akam poets made the child the agreed route back into a marriage after estrangement."},{"a":"ku:360","b":"a:22","type":"tension","note":"In Akananuru 22 the girl laughs because during the velan's midnight rite her lover slipped past the guards and his embrace cured her; in Kuruntokai 360 the girl, while the velan misreads her sickness, prays that the lover not come at night.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The two girls in the same veri situation take opposite views of the lover's night visit, one as the cure that mocks the rite and the other as a danger worse than the rite's misdiagnosis, so the veri scene could be turned either to triumph or to selfless restraint."},{"a":"ku:362","b":"a:98","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 98 the girl tells her friend that the mother does not know her ache is eased only on the hill lord's chest, while the velan's rite goes on; in Kuruntokai 362 the friend puts that same claim to the velan as a question: will his chest eat the offering?","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the step from private knowledge to disclosure: what the girl can only say to her friend in the long poem, the friend turns into a riddle spoken at the rite itself, so the mockery of the diviner is the means by which the secret reaches the mother."},{"a":"ku:363","b":"k:1151","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1151's friend refuses comfort with an ultimatum about who will still be alive when he returns; Kuruntokai 363's friend asks whether crossing the waste is sweet when even the bull there stays by its doe.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same dissuasion of a man about to leave is made by threat in the couplet and by the example of the road's own creatures in the poem, so the pair shows the friend has two arguments, death of the one left and the shame of being less faithful than a beast.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:364","b":"a:76","type":"develops","note":"Both are courtesans answering, within hearing of the wife's side, the wife's talk against them: in Akananuru 76 she vows to hold him as the Kaveri took Atti; in Kuruntokai 364 she points to the coming thunangai festival where eyes clash.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two poems show the concubine's convention of answering slander not with denial but with a public contest announced in advance, a river's seizure or a festival's rivalry, so the household quarrel is moved out into the town's open spaces."},{"a":"ku:366","b":"ku:229","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kuruntokai 229 onlookers see destiny (பால்) at work in two who fought as children and are now joined; in 366 the friend invokes the same destiny before any outcome to refuse to judge the man.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Destiny serves after the fact as the onlookers' explanation and before the fact as the friend's argument for consent, so the pair shows the akam idea of பால் doing persuasive work, not only describing what happened.","gr":2},{"a":"ku:369","b":"ku:378","type":"tension","note":"In 369 the friend calls the paalai road pleasant, its pods rattling like anklets, and urges the girl to go; in 378 the foster mother, after the girl has gone, can only pray that the same road's sun and bare paths be cooled.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The one elopement road is a promise to the girl's accomplice and a danger to the woman who raised her, so the pair shows the akam books dividing a single event between voices who cannot both be right about the land."},{"a":"ku:373","b":"pn:3","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 3 tells the Pandya that even if the earth shifts his word must not; Kuruntokai 373's friend says that even if the earth shifts and fire and water change places, the lovers' bond suffers no harm from gossip.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The akam friend borrows the puram formula for a king's unbreakable word and gives it to a secret love, so the pair shows that the lovers' bond was claimed in the language of royal oath, and that a pledge, not a feeling, is what kalavu asks to be trusted."},{"a":"ku:375","b":"a:122","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 122 the girl lists the town, mother, guards, dogs, moon and owl that keep watch, and complains that when all sleep he does not come; in Kuruntokai 375 the friend says it is better he does not come, since the guards beat their drum all night against the tiger.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same sleepless watch is used to reproach the lover for missing his chance and to wish him away from it, so the pair shows that the catalogue of obstacles is addressed to the man in both directions, and that its real end is to make the night visit impossible and marriage necessary."},{"a":"ku:376","b":"k:1104","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1104's man asks where she found a fire that burns when he moves away and cools when he draws near; Kuruntokai 376's man says she is cool in summer and warm in the cold season.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both men praise her as a reversal of temperature, but the couplet makes it depend on his distance and the poem on the season, so together they show the convention of the beloved as a climate of her own, which the Kural turns inward into the lover's desire."},{"a":"ku:378","b":"a:283","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 283 the friend, arranging the elopement, wishes rain to fall on the forest and cool it so the road is sweet; in Kuruntokai 378 the foster mother, after the girl has gone, prays the sun not burn and cool clouds hang over the waste.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same wish for a cooled desert passes from the accomplice who sends the girl to the woman who has lost her, so the pair shows the foster mother's grief taking the form of blessing, joining in the elopement she did not choose."},{"a":"ku:380","b":"a:84","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 84 the man in the king's war camp sees the drum-voiced rain-clouds and thinks of his wife in her small village; in Kuruntokai 380 the waiting women hear the cloud roar like the drums kings beat in victory while the lover stays far away.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the simile in the women's mouth names the cause the man's poem shows from inside, the king's campaign, so the rains are the same drum to both and the season of war and the season of waiting are one."},{"a":"ku:381","b":"ku:401","type":"develops","note":"In 381 the friend says the girl's wasting and pallor are the only fruit of having laughed with the shore lord; in 401 the girl says that even laughing play with the shore lord has been forbidden.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together the two neythal poems show laughter with the shore lord as the whole of kalavu at its smallest unit: one laugh costs the girl her beauty in 381, and in 401 the household guards against exactly that, so the forbidding is the town's answer to the danger the friend names."},{"a":"ku:382","b":"ku:66","type":"develops","note":"In 66 the friend calls the konrai foolish for taking the untimely rain for the season; in 382 the friend says the rain itself is untimely, since if it were the season the man would have come.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The friend's comfort moves from blaming the tree to denying the season: in 66 nature is fooled by false rain, in 382 the man's promise is made the test of what the rain is, so his word outranks the calendar of flowers.","gr":3},{"a":"ku:386","b":"k:1226","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1226: the woman did not know evening brings sickness while her husband had not yet left; in 386 the woman knew evening only as the festival hour of jewelled women and did not know its sorrow wide as the earth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem fills in what the couplet's woman did know of evening before: the public festival hour of the women's gathering, so the Kural's 'I did not know' is the reversal of a known, shared evening into a private sorrow."},{"a":"ku:387","b":"k:1167","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1167: the woman swims the fierce flood of desire and sees no shore, and at midnight only she is awake; in 387 she swims across the evening to reach the night as its bank, and finds the night's flood larger than the sea.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's shoreless flood gets its hours in the poem: the evening is the first water and the night a second, larger one, so the shore the Kural's woman cannot see is the bank of evening that only opens onto the night."},{"a":"ku:393","b":"a:253","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 253 gossip is louder than the cheering at Kudal when the green-jewelled Pandiyan drove out the Kongar; in Kuruntokai 393 gossip is louder than the Kongar's shout when the green-jewelled Pandiyan's general Athikan fell at Vaakai.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Pandiyan's wars with the Kongar gave akam poets their yardstick for gossip from both outcomes, his victory cheer at Kudal and the enemy's cry over his fallen general, so the measure was the loudest noise of a known war whichever side made it."},{"a":"ku:396","b":"a:48","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 48 the foster mother asks why the girl will not drink milk and has paled, and the friend tells her of the young hunter; in Kuruntokai 396 the foster mother, after the girl has gone with her man, recalls that she would not drink her milk or take up her ball.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same symptom, refused milk, stands at two points of one story: in Akananuru 48 it is a question the friend answers in time to open the way to marriage, in Kuruntokai 396 a clue the foster mother reads only after the elopement, so the akam poets used it as the sign that decides whether disclosure or udanpokku follows."},{"a":"ku:399","b":"k:1187","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1187: she lay in his embrace and turned aside, and at once pallor was on her thick enough to scoop up; in 399 pallor is moss on the village well that parts where he touches and spreads where he lets go.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The couplet's instant hyperbole is given a mechanism by the poem: pallor is a surface like well-moss that closes the moment the hand lifts, so the Kural's 'at that very moment' is not exaggeration but what anyone at a village well has seen.","gr":3},{"a":"ku:395","b":"k:1146","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1146: gossip about one day's meeting has spread like the serpent seizing the moon; in 395 the woman says no one frees her as the people here free the moon the snake swallows, nor even tells her not to fear.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The eclipse served both as a spectacle everyone sees and as a rite in which everyone rushes to help: the Kural takes the first sense for gossip, the poem the second for rescue, and the woman's complaint is that her eclipse draws the town's eyes but none of its help."},{"a":"ku:400","b":"a:34","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 34 the man, his work done, bids the charioteer drive fast so that his wife may regain her beauty; in Kuruntokai 400 the man thanks the charioteer who cut a new way across the gravel and brought the chariot home to the wife in pain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two poems are the command and the thanks of one homeward drive: Akananuru 34 asks for speed, Kuruntokai 400 credits the charioteer with the resolve and the shortcut, so the driver is shown sharing the husband's care for the wife and earns praise for it."},{"a":"ku:390","b":"ku:7","type":"develops","note":"In 7 onlookers on the road ask who the pitiable bowman and his anklet-wearing girl are, setting out into the wilds; in 390 onlookers tell the man, mate of the young she-elephant, to stop for the night because of the frontier guards' drum.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The onlookers of udanpokku move from pity to protection: in 7 they only wonder at the couple, in 390 they name the danger of the road at nightfall and halt them, so the kandor are not only witnesses of the elopement but its guardians on the way."},{"a":"nt:0","b":"t:2774","type":"same_image","note":"Natrinai 0 builds the discus-bearer's body from the cosmos: earth his feet, sea his garment, sky his body, the quarters his hands, moon and sun his eyes; Tirumanthiram 2774 gives Shiva the same map, space his body, the eight directions his hands, the three lights his three eyes, as he dances.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The body made of sky, quarters and heavenly lights is a frame older than either sect: the Sangam invocation fills it for the discus-bearer as a fact the Veda reports, and Tirumular fills it for Shiva and sets it dancing with a third eye of fire, so the image passed between gods while its parts stayed fixed."},{"a":"nt:1","b":"k:783","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 1's heroine calls her man sweeter the longer one knows him (நீடுதோறு இனியர்) and the bond of the noble lofty; Kural 783 says the companionship of people of character delights the more it is practised, as a book does the more it is studied.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The heroine uses the Kural's rule about friendship with the noble as the premise of a love argument, reasoning from his character that he cannot leave her, so a maxim about good company served in akam as evidence against a report of parting."},{"a":"nt:1","b":"k:20","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the formula of the world that cannot do without water (நீர் இன்று அமையா உலகம், நீர்இன்று அமையாது உலகு): Kural 20 as the first step of an argument that all conduct depends on rain, Natrinai 1 as the measure of how she cannot live without him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same clause is a step in Valluvar's proof about rain and a simile of a woman's dependence in Kapilar, which shows it was a common saying both drew on, apt to the akam poet for a lover's need as it was to the moralist for the world's."},{"a":"nt:2","b":"nt:9","type":"tension","note":"Natrinai 2's onlookers see the elopement road as a wasteland where a tiger cub with blood on its mouth eyes deer at evening, and call the youth's heart crueler than thunder; in Natrinai 9 the eloping man describes the road as cool groves of koels and mango buds where she may linger in the shade and make sand dolls.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two poems give the same udanpokku road two natures by speaker, a tiger's evening for the bystanders and a spring garden for the lover, so the danger of paalai is a judgment the voices make and not only a fact of the land."},{"a":"nt:2","b":"ku:7","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are Perumpathumanar's onlookers on an eloping pair: in Kuruntokai 7 they ask who these good, pitiable people are, the bowman and the girl with anklets still on, heading into the bamboo wilds; in Natrinai 2 they say the youth's heart is crueler than mountain-shaking thunder.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same poet lets the onlookers pity both runaways in one poem and in the other lay the whole blame on the man who leads her into the tiger's country, so the kandor voice was a place to judge the hero, not only to witness the pair."},{"a":"nt:3","b":"nt:16","type":"develops","note":"Both are a man answering a heart that urges a journey for wealth: in Natrinai 3 he recalls thinking of her at lamp-lighting time on the edge of the wasteland on an earlier journey; in Natrinai 16 he sets out the dilemma that wealth and union cannot both be had and gives up the journey.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same refusal is reached once by memory and once by argument: Natrinai 3 calls her sweet as a finished task, putting the journey's goal already at home, and Natrinai 16 states the reason as a rule that wealth leaves no trace, so the decision is held both as felt experience and as reasoned principle."},{"a":"nt:4","b":"k:1143","type":"tension","note":"Both concern the town's gossip about a secret love: Kural 1143's man asks whether gossip the whole town knows is not good for him, since it is like having what he has not yet got; Natrinai 4's friend fears that gossip reaching the mother will end the meetings and asks whether the man will take them to his own town.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The gossip that the Kural's man welcomes as a foretaste of union is, for the women of Natrinai 4, the danger that ends their meetings, which they can only turn into pressure to marry, so the worth of alar in the secret stage depends on who bears its cost."},{"a":"nt:6","b":"pn:152","type":"qualifies","note":"Both name வல்வில் ஓரி: Purananuru 152 shows the hunter whose single arrow kills an elephant, a tiger, a stag, a boar and an iguana, and who feeds the bard venison; in Natrinai 6 the girl's hair smells of Ori's forest, where the ripe fruit of the kumizh falls as food for young deer.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The love poet takes from the hunter-patron's fame only his forest's scent and plenty, a place where the very deer his bow kills feed unharmed, so akam borrowed a puram name for the bounty of a land and left its violence behind."},{"a":"nt:8","b":"k:1081","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are the man at first sight of the girl: Kural 1081 asks whether she is a goddess, a peahen or a woman, his heart reeling; Natrinai 8 asks whose daughter she is, blesses her father and wishes on her mother the fortune of Thondi.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Valluvar's man asks what she is and the Natrinai man whose she is, his first words going to her parents, so the Sangam poem shows the couplet's stunned wonder already turning toward the kin and the marriage that will need them.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:8","b":"a:290","type":"same_image","note":"Natrinai 8 places in Poraiyan's Thondi neythal flowers that look like eyes; in Akananuru 290 the heroine says her eyes, once like the cool neythal of Kuttuvan's Thondi harbour, have lost that beauty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Thondi neythal and a woman's eyes were a fixed pair that ran both ways, the flower measured by eyes in a man's first praise and the eyes by the flower in a woman's grief at separation, so one token marked beauty found and beauty lost at two stages of love.","gr":3},{"a":"nt:12","b":"ku:7","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 7's onlookers see anklets still on the eloping girl's feet; in Natrinai 12 the friend tells how the girl, hiding herself at dawn, slipped off her pebble-filled anklets and left her striped ball before going.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show the anklet as the mark of an unmarried girl, read by strangers on the road in one poem and taken off by the girl herself in secret in the other, so the eloping girl performs alone at dawn the change of state that a wedding would have made in public."},{"a":"nt:15","b":"k:1254","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are about restraint that can no longer keep love hidden: Kural 1254's woman says she used to think herself restrained but her love now breaks out in public; Natrinai 15's friend tells the man they have let go even their long modesty and the town may gossip.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural records the loss of restraint as an inner confession, while Natrinai 15 makes the same loss a threat spoken to the man, likened to a mother forced to give her child to a demon, so the collapse of naanam also served as a lever to force marriage."},{"a":"nt:16","b":"k:332","type":"qualifies","note":"Both hold that wealth does not stay: Kural 332 says great wealth comes like a crowd gathering at a dance and goes as it disperses; Natrinai 16 says wealth vanishes like the trail of a fish in a pond.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Kural uses wealth's impermanence to counsel detachment, while the Natrinai man uses it to choose his wife's gaze over a journey, so the same doctrine served an argument for staying at home in love as well as one for letting go."},{"a":"nt:17","b":"k:1017","type":"qualifies","note":"Both rank modesty above life: Kural 1017 says those ruled by shame give up life for it but never shame for life; Natrinai 17's girl says she almost forgot the modesty dearer than life when her mother spoke softly.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the ranking as a firm rule, and the Natrinai girl who holds it nearly breaks it not under threat but under a mother's tenderness, so the poem shows the rule's weak point to be a kindness the maxim does not foresee."},{"a":"nt:19","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Both answer a lover's departure by saying she will not live until he returns: Kural 1151 says to tell his quick return to those who will still be alive; Natrinai 19's friend says go, knowing well she will not live the few days till you come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's bitter refusal of comfort appears in Natrinai 19 inside a friend's demand for marriage in the secret stage, so the claim that she will die before he returns worked in kalavu as pressure to marry and not only as grief.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:20","b":"k:1311","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are a wife's sulk at the husband's straying, fixed on his chest: Kural 1311 refuses his chest because all women feast on it with their eyes; Natrinai 20 describes the girl who slept on the lord's chest and walked off at dawn with crushed earring and withered garland.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural states the wife's charge in general terms, and Natrinai 20 shows the single scene behind such a charge, the rival's disordered body read as proof and blessed in irony, so the couplet's reproach rests on a convention of reading the other woman's appearance as evidence."},{"a":"nt:21","b":"a:34","type":"same_image","note":"Both are by Maruthan Ilanaganar on the same occasion (the man, his work done, speaks to his charioteer): in Akananuru 34 a stag feeds and guards his doe, in Natrinai 21 a forest cock scratches out its morning food and looks to its hen, and both close the image on the same word, பெருந்தகை / பெருந்தகு.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Holding the two together shows one poet keeping a fixed frame for this thurai, a male creature's noble care for its mate seen from the speeding chariot, and changing only the creature, so the iraicchi of the homecoming was a reusable device rather than a chance sight."},{"a":"nt:23","b":"k:1271","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 23's friend says the girl's thinness is hidden by her bangles and her loose hair excused by play, but her eyes cannot hide love; Kural 1271 has the man tell her that though she hides it, her kohl-lined eyes go beyond her control and have something to say.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Natrinai supplies the test behind Valluvar's claim by sorting the signs of love into those an excuse can cover and the eyes, which none can, so the couplet's 'though you hide' turns out to name a practice of concealment the older poem lays out part by part.","gr":4},{"a":"nt:24","b":"ku:135","type":"tension","note":"Both rest on the maxim that work is what men are: in Kuruntokai 135 the friend adds that men are the life of the women at home and concludes that he will put off going; in Natrinai 24 the woman adds nothing and praises her friend for consenting to his going. On the plain reading the two conclusions are incompatible.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The pair shows that the saying about men and work was common ground each speaker could turn either way, toward consenting to the parting or toward stopping it, so the maxim settled nothing and each poem's argument lies in what it adds to it."},{"a":"nt:25","b":"a:242","type":"develops","note":"Both are by Peri Saathanar and both name the man as the one who chased parrots from the millet with them (கிளி கடிந்து ... நம்மொடு; கிள்ளை நம்மொடு கடிந்தோன்): in Natrinai 25 he leaves without finding a chance to speak, and in Akananuru 242 the mother, not knowing this same man came in love, calls the velan.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the poet's one formula marks two stages of a single courtship, the silent visitor at the millet field whose plea the friend carries and the unseen cause of the sickness the mother misreads, so the parrot-chasing stranger is what separates the friend's knowledge from the mother's ignorance.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:32","b":"k:791","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 32's friend ends with the saying that the great take a friend after inquiry and do not inquire after befriending, and that once accepted it is hard to undo; Kural 791 says nothing brings ruin like befriending without inquiry, since there is no leaving once the bond is formed.","grade":"sound","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural's rule and its reason, inquire first because a formed bond cannot be left, already serve in the Natrinai as a proverb a friend applies to a girl choosing a lover, so the ethics of friendship framed the irrevocability of a love match before Valluvar set them apart in his book on polity."},{"a":"nt:34","b":"a:242","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 242 the friend calls the velan ignorant (அறியா வேலன்) for finding Murugan's seizure in the girl; in Natrinai 34 the friend addresses Murugan himself, who knows the sickness is not his yet comes to the veri at the velan's call, and calls the god a fool.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the friend's scorn for the veri rite moving up from the priest to the god: where one poem blames only the diviner's ignorance, the other makes the god complicit, since he knows the truth and comes anyway."},{"a":"nt:35","b":"a:376","type":"qualifies","note":"Both liken a woman's beauty to Kuttuvan's harbour town Maranthai: in Akananuru 376 the deserted courtesan asks the man to give back her Maranthai-like beauty before he goes; in Natrinai 35 the friend, the day after the wedding, asks whether the wife's Maranthai-like beauty has slackened though he has not left her side.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same city-simile measures a beauty the man must answer for in both, but the pair shows it waning for opposite reasons, the courtesan's because he left and the new wife's while he stays, so the Natrinai places the loss in love itself rather than in absence."},{"a":"nt:39","b":"pn:72","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 72 has Neduncheliyan vow to rout the kings who mocked him and seize them together with their drums (முரசமொடு); Natrinai 39 names Cheliyan as the one who took the drums and crossed the foes' guarded walls (முரசு கொண்டு ஓம்பு அரண் கடந்த) and likens the girl's shoulders to his Koodal. The Natrinai does not say which Cheliyan; the drum-taking matches.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows a king's battle vow hardened into a stock epithet inside a love simile and turned on the lover: the city of the king who took walled forts becomes the measure of shoulders that overpower the man, so puram conquest lends akam its language of love's assault."},{"a":"nt:41","b":"k:81","type":"develops","note":"Kural 81 says the whole of keeping a household is for caring for guests; Natrinai 41's friend says men suffer far off in the waste because they want the household where the wife cooks for a night guest.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's rule becomes, in the friend's mouth, a reason for the separation itself: if the house exists for the guest, then the husband's absence earning for it is the rule at work, so the Kural's praise of hospitality is shown to carry the cost the wife bears in paalai."},{"a":"nt:43","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1151, in Parimelazhagar's reading the friend's words: if you are not leaving tell me, your quick return tell those who will be alive. Natrinai 43: the friend tells the departing man his going is joy to him and ruin to her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both friends answer an announced departure by saying she will not survive it, but the poem adds the grounds the couplet leaves out: the man once said 'fear not', so his leaving is a broken pledge of protection, which explains why the couplet treats talk of a quick return as worthless."},{"a":"nt:44","b":"k:1094","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1094: when I look she looks down, when I do not she looks and softly smiles. Natrinai 44: after bathing she gave him an unaimed look and a smile and went home, and now, confined, he asks his heart whether she knows.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet treats the averted look and smile as a sign the man can read with confidence; the poem shows the same sign after the girl has been shut in, when there is no second meeting to confirm it, and the reading collapses into doubt, so the Kural's reading of hints depends on continued access to her."},{"a":"nt:46","b":"n:16","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 16: the fleeting joy of youth, like a sacrificial lamb nibbling the garland leaves at the slaughter ground, is not found among the wise. Natrinai 46: pleasure and youth pass like an arrow's shadow, so the man should not leave her for wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two start from the same premise, the speed with which youth passes, and draw opposite duties from it: the Naladiyar says renounce its pleasure, the Sangam friend says spend it in love and despise wealth instead, which shows that impermanence was an argument both renouncers and lovers used, and settles nothing by itself."},{"a":"nt:46","b":"k:333","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 333: wealth does not stay, so when you get it do at once what stays. Natrinai 46 calls the wealth the man leaves for மன்னாப் பொருள், wealth that does not last.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both call wealth impermanent, but the Kural still tells a man to get it and convert it into lasting good, while the friend uses its impermanence to argue against going after it at all, which shows the couplet's maxim leaving room for the very journey the akam poem condemns."},{"a":"nt:47","b":"a:242","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 242: with the man listening, the friend tells the girl the mother has called the velan, and the man should come and hold her before the goat is killed. Natrinai 47: with the man listening, the friend asks whether she may tell him that the mother's Murugu rite cannot help the girl's pallor.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In the same overheard situation the Akananuru friend names the cure outright while the Natrinai friend only asks whether she may speak and lets a bereaved elephant show the loss to come, so the pair shows how much directness the overheard speech allowed: the remedy stated, or the danger shown and the remedy left for him to supply."},{"a":"nt:50","b":"k:508","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 508: one who trusts an outsider without examining him brings trouble without end. Natrinai 50: the friend failed to guard the girl because she trusted the man without seeing, taking his smallness for greatness.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The couplet is a rule for a king choosing men; the friend applies the same failure to herself in the household and names its cause, a public reputation (even his enemies admire him) taken in place of examination, so the pair shows the akam go-between bound by the same duty of testing the Kural lays on the king."},{"a":"nt:51","b":"a:292","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 292: the girl, afraid of the veri rite, blames the hill lord, where a sling stone flung at an elephant scatters vengai blossom and lodges in a jackfruit. Natrinai 51: the girl, facing the veri rite, names the hill lord where a killer tusker broke the young jackfruit among vengai with strewn flowers.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Two poets give the girl facing the velan the same hill emblem, a blow among vengai blossom that strikes a jackfruit, which shows it was a stock figure for the harm the unmarried lover does and supports reading the broken young jackfruit of Natrinai 51 as a hidden comparison rather than scenery."},{"a":"nt:52","b":"pn:152","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 152: Ori, met as a hunter in the wilds, gives a strange troupe meat, toddy, gold and gems unasked. Natrinai 52: even wealth equal to the giving of Ori, lord of the Mazhavar, would be slight against his wife's embrace.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poem shows Ori's giving as so lavish it reached strangers in the wilderness, and the akam poem takes exactly that giving as the upper limit of what a man could hope to gain and still sets it below love, so the pair shows a patron's fame serving akam poets as a fixed measure of wealth."},{"a":"nt:52","b":"k:616","type":"tension","note":"Kural 616: effort produces prosperity and its lack ushers in poverty. Natrinai 52: the heart set on ஆள்வினை is loveless, and wealth made by toil is a slight thing.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Kural makes effort the source of fortune and the duty of a man; the husband of Natrinai 52 calls the heart that obeys that duty loveless and declares its fruit slight, so read together they show that the ethics of ஆள்வினை and the akam of the home make incompatible claims on the same man."},{"a":"nt:53","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1102: medicine for a disease is something other than it, but for the sickness this woman gives, she herself is the cure. Natrinai 53: the mother prescribes the stream water from the lover's hill as medicine for the girl's shivering.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet states as a paradox that the cause of love-sickness is its only cure; the poem shows the mother unknowingly obeying it, sending the girl to water that comes from the lover's own hill, and the friend reports it so that the man listening learns he is the medicine and must come to marry."},{"a":"nt:54","b":"k:1226","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1226: she did not know evening brings sickness while he who wed her had not left. Natrinai 54: the small dull evening is deeply lonely, she tells a heron going home to its kin, and begs it to carry her plight to him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet says evening hurts only when he is gone; the poem shows why, through the heron that at evening flies home to its flock, so the hour that sends every creature to its kin is the hour that marks her lack, and her plea that the bird delay its homing for her is the couplet's sickness acted out."},{"a":"nt:55","b":"pn:108","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 108: in Pari's Parambu the firebrand a hill woman lights is sandalwood, its smoke reaching the vengai. Natrinai 55: the friend explains the bees on the girl by pointing to a burning sandalwood firebrand.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poem records that hill households burned sandal as ordinary firewood, which is what makes the friend's lie in Natrinai 55 credible to the mother: the girl could smell of sandal from the hearth, so the trick depends on a fact of hill life the puram poet praises as the land's wealth.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:56","b":"k:1293","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1293: heart, do you follow him as you please because the ruined have no friends? Natrinai 56: has my heart stayed with him to return together, or has it seen my lost beauty and left me as a stranger?","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives only the bitter answer, that the heart deserts her as fair-weather friends desert the ruined; the poem holds that answer beside a kinder one, that the heart is loyally waiting with him, so the Kural's accusation is shown to be one horn of a doubt the woman cannot settle.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:57","b":"a:2","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 2: a monkey drunk on fallen jackfruit wine sleeps content; the friend says if beasts find unplanned joy so easily, the hill lord's planned joy should not be hard. Natrinai 57: a monkey steals milk from a sleeping wild cow, and the friend ends on the coming harvest that will shut the girl in.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both friends press the hill lord to marry with a monkey of his own slopes, but Akananuru 2 draws the moral aloud while Natrinai 57 leaves the monkey's seized moment unexplained beside the girl's closing one, so the pair shows the same urging done once by open comparison and once by hidden comparison."},{"a":"nt:59","b":"a:34","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 34: the returning man bids his charioteer hurry past a stag that feeds and guards its doe. Natrinai 59: the returning man tells his charioteer of the land where a hunter comes home with his catch and revels at ease.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both returning husbands read their own homecoming in a figure of the road, and the figures differ: the stag shows the care he will give, the hunter the ease he will take, so the pair shows the mullai homecoming imagined both as tending and as rest."},{"a":"nt:64","b":"nt:80","type":"tension","note":"In Natrinai 80 the man says there is no medicine but the girl for his sickness; in Natrinai 64 the deserted woman says that even if he comes he is no medicine for her sickness.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Read together, the two poems show the anthology using one figure, the beloved as the only cure, at both ends of secret love: the pursuing man asserts it and the abandoned woman revokes it, so the claim holds only while hope does."},{"a":"nt:80","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"Both have the man say that the woman herself is the only cure for the sickness she causes; the Kural states it as a paradox of medicine, Natrinai 80 says it to his own heart so that the friend who keeps him off will hear.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's general paradox becomes in the older poem a lever in a negotiation: by declaring the girl his only medicine where her guardian can overhear, the rejected man turns a private conceit into a claim on the friend's pity."},{"a":"nt:79","b":"k:1155","type":"develops","note":"In both the woman tells her friend that the man's departure must be stopped because union after he goes will be hard; Natrinai 79 rehearses the words she might say and asks by what means to stop him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the friend a reason to act, while the Natrinai woman supplies the argument she would use, that rejoining after parting makes another parting harder, and then doubts any words will work; the poem shows the couplet's advice as a thing she has already tried in her head."},{"a":"nt:71","b":"k:1151","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are the friend's speech to a man about to leave: Kural 1151 says tell me only if you are not going, and tell your quick return to those who will still be alive; Natrinai 71 grants the woman may let him go and asks whether he can stroke her eyes and leave.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet argues from the woman's death, the Natrinai friend from the man's own tenderness at the moment of leave-taking, so the two show the dissuasion speech had two separate levers, her survival and his ability to bear the parting."},{"a":"nt:71","b":"k:333","type":"qualifies","note":"Natrinai 71 opens by calling wealth impermanent (மன்னாப் பொருள்) to keep the man at home; Kural 333 says wealth does not stay, so whoever gets it should do at once what lasts.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same premise, that wealth does not last, yields opposite advice: the Kural tells a man to seek and spend it on what endures, the friend uses it to argue he should not seek it at all, which shows nilaiyaamai served the akam poets as a rhetorical weapon before it was a doctrine."},{"a":"nt:72","b":"k:1150","type":"tension","note":"Both are the friend speaking to the woman while the man overhears, about the town's gossip; in Kural 1150 the gossip is welcome because it will push him to marry, in Natrinai 72 his anxiety about gossip is read as a sign his love is drying up.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"In the same speech situation the friend can treat gossip as an ally or as a threat depending on how the man reacts to it, so the pair shows that alar works on the lover's resolve, not on the woman's name.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:75","b":"k:1099","type":"qualifies","note":"Both concern the woman's indifferent glance in early love: Kural 1099 says looking at each other like strangers is found only in lovers; in Natrinai 75 the rejected man says her glance that does not look has wounded him like a hunter's arrow.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The look the Kural's observer reads as proof of love is, to the man who receives it, a fatal wound, so the pair shows the same sign read from outside as a disguise and from inside as a refusal."},{"a":"nt:69","b":"k:1228","type":"develops","note":"Both have the wife alone at evening hearing the cowherds' flutes; Kural 1228 calls the flute the messenger of evening and a killing weapon, Natrinai 69 sets the flutes and cow bells among the evening's signs and says that if such an evening came where he is, he could not stay at his work.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the evening's music a weapon only against her, while the Natrinai wife imagines it falling on him too and so excuses his absence by supposing his land has no such evenings, turning the same lament into a defence of his love."},{"a":"nt:68","b":"k:57","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 57 asks what use guarding women by confinement is, since their own steadfastness is the best guard; in Natrinai 68 the friend says shutting young girls in the house is neither right nor good for the household.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both reject confinement, but the Kural does so for the wife because her virtue makes it needless, while the Natrinai girls reject it for the unmarried daughter because it blocks a love headed for marriage, so the same verdict rests on opposite views of what the woman's will is for."},{"a":"nt:77","b":"pn:158","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 158 lists Malaiyan who rode Kaari and won great battles among the seven givers; in Natrinai 77 Kapilar likens the relief the girl's look once gave the lover to Malaiyan riding his horse into enemy land, breaking forts and breathing out his weariness.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poem keeps Malaiyan as a rider and giver in a public roll, while the akam poem takes the same rider at the private moment after the raid, his sigh of relief, so akam used the king's fame for the feeling of a finished deed rather than for the deed."},{"a":"nt:81","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1268 wishes the king victory so that the man may join his wife and keep an evening feast for guests; Natrinai 81 is that moment reached: the king has quelled the enemy and the man hurries home to a wife whose grief was her longing to host guests.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together they show that the soldier's picture of home was a feast for guests, voiced as a wish before victory in the Kural and as a race home after it in the Natrinai, which adds that the wife's sorrow was her inability to host alone, so hospitality is a couple's act that war suspends.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:82","b":"k:1082","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1082 the man is struck by her answering look as by a goddess with an army; in Natrinai 82 the man says he cannot bear to look at her form, which strikes his eyes like Valli joined with Murugan.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural puts the wound in her returned glance, the Natrinai in his own looking at her beauty, and only the Natrinai can name the goddess as the hill's own Valli, so the divine attack of the Kural's abstract couplet is, in the older poem, a local deity of the land where the love happens."},{"a":"nt:83","b":"ku:153","type":"same_image","note":"In Kuruntokai 153 the girl's heart, which once took fright when the hill owl hooted, now goes out through the dark night toward him; in Natrinai 83 the friend begs the owl at the town ghat not to sound its fearful cry while they wait awake for him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The owl's cry is the standard measure of the night tryst's fear, and the pair shows it split between two roles: love has carried the girl's heart past that fear, while the friend, who manages the meeting, still has to buy the owl's silence with offerings."},{"a":"nt:84","b":"k:81","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 81 says that all of keeping a home and guarding its goods is for caring for guests and doing good; Natrinai 84 says the man leaves his wife for the waste because he cannot live a home life that fails to relieve those who come begging.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Natrinai shows the cost of the Kural's rule: if a household exists to give, a household without wealth fails its purpose, so the very ideal that defines home life is what sends the husband away from home and leaves the wife to grieve."},{"a":"nt:86","b":"ku:21","type":"develops","note":"In Kuruntokai 21 the woman refuses to believe the konrai's sign of the rains because he does not lie; in Natrinai 86 the friend calls the men righteous because they came back before the season they had named.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kuruntokai woman's faith that he keeps his word is a wager, and the Natrinai shows the wager won, with the kept promise named as அறவர், virtue, so truthfulness about the season is what the akam poets count as a husband's righteousness."},{"a":"nt:86","b":"nt:99","type":"qualifies","note":"In Natrinai 86 the friend reads the flowers of the late dew and early summer as true signs that the man came before his season; in Natrinai 99 she calls the rainy-season flowers foolish, blooming for a rain that is not the season, because he has not come.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show that the friend's reading of the season's signs follows the man's conduct, not the signs themselves: when he keeps time the flowers are witnesses, when he does not they are fools, so the season in akam is an argument made for the woman's sake."},{"a":"nt:87","b":"ku:201","type":"same_image","note":"In Kuruntokai 201 a bat that has eaten sweet mango gulps sour nelli and hangs from the bamboo, in a poem of good news that the hill lord will come; in Natrinai 87 a bat hanging asleep on a mango only dreams of the sour nelli of a far forest, and her day of longing passes like that dream.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same bat, mango and sour nelli carry fulfilment or its absence according to whether the bat eats or only dreams, matching the good news of Kuruntokai 201 and the empty day of Natrinai 87, so the poets varied one fixed image by a single verb to change its sense."},{"a":"nt:88","b":"k:620","type":"develops","note":"Kural 620 says those who toil on without flagging see fate's back; in Natrinai 88 the friend tells the girl not to be stricken as though by their own old deeds but to rise, go and speak to the man.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states for the man of affairs that effort overturns fate, and the Natrinai shows the same refusal of fate in a girl's friend inside a secret love, where the effort is speech to the man, so the rule had a domestic use, and the friend's action is an answer to the household's fatalism."},{"a":"nt:89","b":"k:1222","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1222 pities the sad-eyed (புன்கண்ணை) evening and asks whether its partner is as hard-eyed (வன்கண்ணது) as hers; Natrinai 89 calls the evening sad (புன்கண் மாலை) but makes it the ally of the hard-hearted (வன்கணாளர்) who do not come. On this reading the evening is a fellow victim in one and an accomplice in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same two words, புன்கண் and வன்கண், are distributed differently: the Kural gives the evening its own hard partner and so makes it a sister in grief, while the Natrinai joins the evening to her own hard man, so the woman's view of the hour turns on whether she sees it as abandoned or as sent."},{"a":"nt:92","b":"ku:16","type":"same_image","note":"Both open with உள்ளார் கொல்லோ தோழி, the friend asking whether the men who crossed the waste will not think of them; Kuruntokai 16 answers with a lizard calling to its mate, Natrinai 92 with a tusker winning water for its mate and calf.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The formula comes with its answer, a male creature caring for its mate on the road the man has taken, and the pair shows that answer sharpened: a lizard's call only reminds, while a tusker that provides and then moves on mirrors the man who left to provide, so the friend's comfort lies in the likeness."},{"a":"nt:94","b":"k:1137","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1137 praises womanhood that suffers a sea of desire and does not mount the palmyra horse; in Natrinai 94 the woman herself says that speaking out desire suits a man while womanhood makes her hold it in, and asks what kind of man he is not to know.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's praise of the woman's silence becomes in the Natrinai a grievance spoken by the woman under that rule, aimed at the man who is free to speak and does not, so the restraint the couplet admires is shown from inside as a constraint she resents."},{"a":"nt:96","b":"k:1185","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1185 is built on the pointing words உவக்காண், இவக்காண்: look there, he goes; look here, the pallor spreads. Natrinai 96 points with இதுவே, உதுவே, அதுவே to the places where she met him, and ends on the pallor.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both make pallor out of pointing, but the Kural points across the distance of a departure seen as it happens, while the Natrinai friend walks back through the places of meeting before an overhearing man, so pallor comes in one from the sight of leaving and in the other from memory of place, used to press for marriage."},{"a":"nt:99","b":"ku:66","type":"develops","note":"In Kuruntokai 66 the friend says the konrai is foolish to bloom for a stray rain before the promised season; in Natrinai 99 the friend, answering the woman's own question, says the cloud itself forgot itself and the pidavam, konrai and kodal bloomed because they are foolish.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Natrinai takes the Kuruntokai's single foolish tree and widens the folly to the sky and to three flowers, and places it as the answer to the woman's doubt, so the consolation grows from a remark about one tree into a whole season declared mistaken."},{"a":"nt:100","b":"pn:123","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 123 makes chariot-giving Malaiyan's mark, chariots given sober outnumbering the raindrops on Mullur; Natrinai 100 names him only as தேர் வண் மலையன் and borrows his musicians' drum to measure a frightened husband's trembling.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam poet can name Malaiyan by the epithet the puram poems established and rely on his fame being known, and turns it to mockery: the drum of a great giver's festivals throbs for a man afraid of his wife, so puram glory serves akam as a scale for comic smallness."},{"a":"nt:101","b":"k:1085","type":"develops","note":"Both are the man struck by the girl's look and both find a doe in it: Kural 1085 asks whether her look is Death, an eye or a doe; Natrinai 101 says the shore hamlet was sweet to live in before he saw her deer-like look.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet says what the look contains, the doe beside Death; the Natrinai man shows what Death in it did, measuring the wound as the loss of his home's sweetness from the day he saw her."},{"a":"nt:102","b":"ku:141","type":"develops","note":"In both the heroine sends the hill lord word that she has been set to guard the millet against parrots: in Kuruntokai 141 through the friend, with a warning not to come at midnight, in Natrinai 102 through a parrot itself.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The millet-guard message was a fixed kalavu signal meaning she can be met by day, and Natrinai turns the crop's enemy into its carrier, so the bird she is posted to chase becomes the go-between she bribes with the crop."},{"a":"nt:103","b":"a:91","type":"develops","note":"Both put a drought-starved animal couple on the man's road for wealth: in Akananuru 91 the friend cites the elephant shrinking beside his hungry mate to assure the wife of his love; in Natrinai 103 the man himself sees the male wild dog grieving for his starving mate.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The image the friend offers the wife as reassurance is the one the man meets as self-reproach on the road, so the pair shows the same iraicchi working from both ends of the separation, comfort at home and conscience in the waste."},{"a":"nt:105","b":"a:91","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 91 says the man would not stay away even if he gained Kuttuvan's rich Kudanadu; in Natrinai 105 the man's heart, midway on the road, turns back to her hair, fragrant as the kuvalai of Kuttuvan's western hill.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"What the friend in Akananuru 91 asserts from outside, that Kuttuvan's whole land would not hold him, Natrinai 105 shows from inside his heart, where Kuttuvan's country survives only as a measure of her fragrance."},{"a":"nt:107","b":"k:1248","type":"tension","note":"Both have the woman's heart following the man who has left; Kural 1248 calls the heart a fool for going after him, Natrinai 107 says the heart that went has the good share and she who stayed has the pain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same split between a heart that follows and a self left behind is judged in opposite ways: Valluvar's woman despises the following heart, the Natrinai woman envies it, because she is the one left to bear the town's gossip."},{"a":"nt:109","b":"k:1228","type":"develops","note":"Both are the separated woman at evening in a pastoral scene: in Kural 1228 the herdsman's flute is the burning evening's messenger and a killing weapon; in Natrinai 109 she stands alone like a cow tied by the head in a dripping shed.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pastoral evening gives the Kural woman a sound that attacks her and the Natrinai woman a posture she shares, so the mullai hour wounds in one from outside and in the other makes her one of its own tethered creatures."},{"a":"nt:110","b":"k:51","type":"develops","note":"Kural 51 defines the wife as one who lives within the means of the man who took her; Natrinai 110 shows a spoilt girl who once refused honeyed milk now eating only at alternate meals in her husband's poor house, refusing her rich father's food.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem shows what the couplet's rule costs and how strange it looks to her own family, who ask where she learned it, so living within his means appears as a change of person rather than a skill."},{"a":"nt:113","b":"pn:2","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 2 praises Udhiyan Cheralathan for the great food he gave at the war of the hundred and the five; Natrinai 113 likens the heroine's sob to the ambal pipe the musicians blow on the hushed field after Udhiyan's loud battle.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Where the praise poem remembers Udhiyan's war for its feeding of the armies, the love poet remembers its aftermath, the silent field and the mourning pipe, so akam took from the same king's fame the grief that puram praise leaves out."},{"a":"nt:113","b":"a:233","type":"develops","note":"Both paalai poems use Udhiyan Cheral's great occasions as similes: Akananuru 233 compares the crowded hills of the man's road to the ghouls gathered at Udhiyan's great feast; Natrinai 113 compares the left wife's sob to the pipe blown after Udhiyan's battle.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together they show akam poets dividing one king's famous scenes between the two halves of separation, his feast for the harsh road the man crosses and his battlefield's lament for the woman he leaves."},{"a":"nt:114","b":"a:182","type":"develops","note":"Both have the friend fearing the man's night path, where storms strike down snakes on the hill; Akananuru 182 tells him directly to come by day, Natrinai 114 speaks to the heroine within his hearing and adds the thunder-killed tusker and his grieving mate.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the two routes of the same warning: the direct plea in Akananuru 182, and in Natrinai 114 the overheard speech that lets the friend show him, through the she-elephant, what his death would leave behind without telling him what to do."},{"a":"nt:115","b":"pn:182","type":"tension","note":"Purananuru 182 says the world stands because there are men who would give their lives for fame; Natrinai 115 says the absent lover, of great love, would not stay away even for endless fame.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"On one defensible reading the two rank the same goods in opposite order, puram putting fame above life and akam putting love above fame, so the friend's comfort works only by overturning the heroic scale the puram poem praises."},{"a":"nt:115","b":"a:91","type":"develops","note":"Both are the friend comforting the wife with the same formula, that the man has very great love (பெரும்பேர் அன்பினர், மிகப் பேர் அன்பினர்) and would not stay even if he gained something: Kuttuvan's land in Akananuru 91, endless fame in Natrinai 115.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The comfort is a fixed frame whose slot each poet fills with the highest prize of the puram world, land in one and fame in the other, so the formula is a measure of love against the goods of war."},{"a":"nt:116","b":"k:1149","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are the deserted woman facing the town's gossip: in Kural 1149 she asks how she can be ashamed of gossip when the man who told her not to fear has left her; in Natrinai 116 the bond has burst like a fallen jackfruit yet the women still keep up their talk of her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the Kural his leaving cancels the shame of the gossip, while in Natrinai the gossip outlives the bond it was about, so the pair shows two answers to the same abandonment: indifference to talk or bearing talk about a love that is already gone."},{"a":"nt:117","b":"k:1227","type":"develops","note":"Both are the woman's love-sickness at evening in separation: Kural 1227 says the sickness buds at morning, grows through the day and blooms at evening; Natrinai 117 sets her evening on the shore, where the neythal closes and birds go home together while he does not think of her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set beside the shore's evening, where the neythal shuts, the couplet's sickness that blooms at evening reads as the one flower that opens when the others close, and the poem supplies the scene the couplet compresses into a single growth.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:120","b":"k:90","type":"develops","note":"Kural 90 says a guest withers when the host's face changes; in Natrinai 120 the man brings a guest into the house because his sulking wife will not show an angry face to a guest and he will see her smile.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The rule of the welcoming face that the Kural lays on the host becomes, in the poem, the husband's lever against the wife's sulk, so the guest is both the object of hospitality and the akam go-between (வாயில்) that ends an oodal."},{"a":"nt:121","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1268 has the man wish that the king fight and win so that he may join his wife and hold the evening feast; in Natrinai 121 the charioteer reports that the king has let them go and drives him home to the wife preparing the evening feast for guests.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet is a wish spoken while the war still holds him, the Natrinai poem the same wish granted and on the road, so together they show the full arc of the mullai return, and that the king's release and the evening guest-feast were the fixed terms of it."},{"a":"nt:121","b":"a:34","type":"same_image","note":"In both a man returning from work passes a deer pair: in Akananuru 34 he himself sees the stag feed and guard his doe and urges the charioteer on; in Natrinai 121 the charioteer points to the doe resting with her stag to calm him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same deer pair works as a spur when the hero reads it and as a sedative when the charioteer offers it, so the mullai image carries a promise of home whose force depends on who in the chariot speaks."},{"a":"nt:124","b":"k:1155","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1155 the woman asks her friend, if she would guard her, to stop the man's departure; in Natrinai 124 the friend does exactly that, telling the man not to go and pointing to the season.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives only the request, while the Natrinai shows the friend carrying it out, adding the rainy-season argument and the anril image, so the couplet's appeal is revealed as the opening move of a known scene in which the friend speaks for the woman to the man."},{"a":"nt:126","b":"n:16","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 16 says the fleeting pleasures of youth, like a sacrificial lamb nibbling its garland, are not found among the wise; Natrinai 126 says no wealth surpasses youth and that love must be taken in youth because wealth cannot buy it later.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both start from the shortness of youth and draw opposite duties from it: the Naladiyar to refuse youth's pleasure, the Natrinai hero to refuse wealth for its sake, so the same premise of impermanence served the akam poets as an argument for love and the didactic poets as one against it.","gr":3},{"a":"nt:126","b":"nt:137","type":"develops","note":"Both are poems in which the hero stops his heart from leaving; 126 argues that youth is worth more than wealth, 137 sets before the heart the tusker who breaks a tree to feed his mate in the drought.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same thurai (செலவு அழுங்குதல்) is won once by reasoning and once by an animal example from the very road to be travelled, so the anthology shows two ways a hero could argue himself out of leaving: a calculation of value, or the shame of being harder than a beast."},{"a":"nt:129","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1151 says: if you are not leaving, tell me; tell your quick return to those who will still be alive; Natrinai 129 mocks as a great joke the man who says he will go and that we will live on at home until he returns.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both turn the man's promise of return into a question of whether she will survive to see it, but the Natrinai supplies what the couplet leaves out, the midnight thunder she must hear alone, so the Kural's bitter one-liner has behind it a scene in which the waiting itself is the threat."},{"a":"nt:132","b":"k:1168","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1168 has the sleepless woman say the night, which puts all creatures to sleep, has only her for company; Natrinai 132 begins with the great town asleep but fills the house with the cold wind, a trembling dog, guards by the bed and watchmen ringing the bell.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's solitude of the one waker is qualified by a poem in which others do wake, but only to keep her in, so the sleepless night of the guarded girl is not loneliness but confinement."},{"a":"nt:133","b":"k:1236","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1236 the woman, bangles slipping, is hurt when her friend calls the man cruel; in Natrinai 133, with the same slipping bangles, the friend says he will not cause them suffering, and the word gives the woman a little relief.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together they show the friend's two possible speeches and the woman's response to each, blame wounds her and defence of him comforts her, yet the Natrinai measures that comfort as a smith's sprinkle on a furnace, small and passing."},{"a":"nt:136","b":"k:1157","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1157 says the bangles slipping past the wrist spread the news that the lord of the shore has left; in Natrinai 136 the father fits the girl with golden bangles that cannot slip, which hide the blame of her shoulders.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"If, as the Kural says, slipping bangles are the body's gossip, the Natrinai shows that gossip could be silenced by a tight bangle, so the sign of separation was something a household could conceal, though only the lover could cure what it signified."},{"a":"nt:138","b":"k:1146","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1146 says she saw him on only one day yet the gossip spread like the eclipse; Natrinai 138 says that their meeting at the ford was once known only to their eyes and asks whether the whole festive town does not know it today.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural compresses into an eclipse the leap from one private sight to public talk that the Natrinai spreads over a remembered garland and a dancing town, and the Natrinai's overhearing hero shows the complaint was spoken to push him toward marriage."},{"a":"nt:139","b":"k:211","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 211 asks what the world can give back to the rain, whose obligation seeks no return; in Natrinai 139 the hero blesses the rain cloud as his helper for the night rain that fell while he lay with his wife.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's rain serves the world and gets nothing back, while the Natrinai hero answers its private help with a blessing, so the poem shows the one return a person could make to the rain and turns the rain's public good into a favour done to two people."},{"a":"nt:140","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1102 the man says every disease has a cure other than itself, but this woman is herself the cure for the sickness she causes; in Natrinai 140 the refused man tells his heart there is no other medicine for the sickness he has.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the paradox from the side of a man already united with her, the Natrinai from the side of a man refused, so the same claim that she alone is the cure is a joke in fulfilment and a sentence of endless pursuit in refusal."},{"a":"nt:134","b":"nt:122","type":"qualifies","note":"In 122 the friend warns that the millet will be guarded and the mother frowns on the girl's secret doings; in 134 the mother herself sends the girl to drive parrots from the millet on the hill where the lover set his sign.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Millet-guarding is both the cover and the clock of the secret love: the same parental order that sends the girl to the field makes the meeting possible, and its ending, which 122 foresees, closes it, so the hill harvest sets the term within which the man must marry."},{"a":"nt:137","b":"a:91","type":"same_image","note":"In Akananuru 91 the friend comforts the waiting woman with the elephant who in the drought shrinks together with his hungry mate; in Natrinai 137 the hero shames his own heart with the tusker who breaks an omai tree to feed his young mate.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The drought elephant that keeps faith with its mate is used by the friend to assure the woman that the man still loves her and by the man to accuse himself of leaving, so the image's single sense, even beasts do not desert their mates, could be turned to comfort or to reproach."},{"a":"nt:141","b":"a:117","type":"same_image","note":"Both compare a woman's hair to the bright ripples (அறல்) of a named river: the Arisil by Killi's Ambar in Natrinai 141, the forest stream north of Vaanan's Sirukudi in Akananuru 117.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The one simile marks the two ends of the girl's story: in Natrinai 141 the river-dark hair is what keeps the lover from leaving her, and in Akananuru 117 it is the mother's last picture of the daughter who left with him, dressed now by his hands."},{"a":"nt:142","b":"k:84","type":"develops","note":"Kural 84: the goddess of fortune dwells gladly in the house of one who tends good guests with a glad face. Natrinai 142 sets the wife who rejoices at a guest even at night in a woodland of unfailing plenty (பொய்யா யாணர்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem gives the couplet's householder a face and a hour: the glad host is the waiting wife, her welcome extends even to guests who arrive after dark, and the plenty the Kural promises is already the landscape the returning husband drives through."},{"a":"nt:143","b":"a:165","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 165: onlookers see the mother after the elopement rest under the nochchi the girl touched and embrace her doll. Natrinai 143: the mother herself weeps at the playmates and the nochchi and confesses she noticed the scent of the girl's hair and said nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two give the mother's grief from outside and from inside: what onlookers see as pure loss, her own words show to include guilt, since she read the signs in the girl's hair, heard the gossip, and chose silence."},{"a":"nt:143","b":"a:117","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 117 the foster mother asks whether the hair she once combed and the girl would not let her wash now falls loose, dressed with her lover's flowers; in Natrinai 143 the mother recalls telling the girl her hair smelled fragrant.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both mothers read the daughter's love in her hair, one before the flight and one after: the fragrance the mother let pass in Natrinai 143 is the lover's flowers that Akananuru 117 imagines on the road, so the hair is the household's evidence of a bond it could see but not stop."},{"a":"nt:146","b":"ku:17","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 17: when love hardens, men ride the palm stem as a horse, wear erukku buds as flowers and are jeered in the street. Natrinai 146 addresses such a rider in his erukku chaplet and promises that kind people will gather and offer him shade like a just king's umbrella.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The street that mocks the madal rider in Kuruntokai 17 is also the street that pities him in Natrinai 146; set side by side they show that the ride works by public feeling, ridicule and compassion together, which is what presses the girl's people to yield."},{"a":"nt:147","b":"a:28","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 28 the friend warns that if the girl will not scare the parrots her mother will send others to guard the millet and the lover will be out of reach. In Natrinai 147 the parrots have taken the millet, the mother questions her, her denial gives her away, and her going to the field is over.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two friends' speeches are the before and after of one plot: the warning of Akananuru 28 comes true in Natrinai 147, and the second poem shows how, through the girl's own guilty denial rather than through the parrots."},{"a":"nt:148","b":"k:1151","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1151: if you are not going, tell me; tell your quick return to those who will be alive then. Natrinai 148 ends, after the man has gone for his work, with 'I will not grieve, friend; may his journey prosper'.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"On the same departure for work the two voices take incompatible stands, the couplet refusing all comfort as if the going were a death, the poem blessing the journey; the iraicchi of the tiger hunting for its mate shows how the friend's comfort turns the one stance into the other."},{"a":"nt:149","b":"k:1147","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1147: the town's gossip is the manure and the mother's words the water of this sickness. In Natrinai 149 the street women spread the gossip, the mother beats the girl with a cane, and she resolves to leave with the shore lord.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet says that gossip and the mother's scolding feed the love they mean to kill; the poem shows what that fed love finally does, turning the same two pressures, the street's finger at the nose and the mother's cane, into the reason for elopement.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:152","b":"ku:32","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 32: if one can tell morning, day, helpless evening, midnight and dawn apart, love is false. Natrinai 152's lover tells the hours exactly: the sun as it dims gave him loneliness, and the night with the andril's call gave him helplessness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"On one reading the claims are incompatible: Kuruntokai 32 makes losing track of the hours the proof of true love, while Natrinai 152 gives a lover whose love is real precisely because each hour is known by its own fresh pain, so knowing the clock is no sign of coolness."},{"a":"nt:152","b":"k:1133","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1133: he once had shame and manliness, and now has the palmyra horse that lovers ride. Natrinai 152: love gave him the madal, the erukku gave him gossip, the sun gave loneliness, night gave helplessness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet records one exchange, virtues lost for a madal; the poem turns it into a chain of gifts in which each thing hands on the next harm, so the madal is not the end of the loss but the first link of a day-long sequence.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:153","b":"k:338","type":"develops","note":"Kural 338: the bond of body and life is like a bird that flies off leaving its nest alone. Natrinai 153: her heart has gone after him and her body, left behind, lives on by eating, like the lone man guarding a ruined town.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem shows the couplet's departure without the death: the heart flies off as the life does in the Kural, yet the body stays, sustained only to keep watch over its own emptiness, so separation in love is a death the body is made to outlive."},{"a":"nt:155","b":"k:1081","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1081: on first seeing her the man cannot tell whether she is a goddess, a peahen or a woman. Natrinai 155: at their second meeting he asks her aloud whether she is the goddess of the sea or of the backwater, and she answers with a smile and tears.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet leaves the man's doubt as a question in his head; the poem puts the same question to the girl and lets her answer it, since a smile and tears are a woman's reply, not a goddess's, so the doubt is settled by her response."},{"a":"nt:156","b":"a:118","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 118 the friend fears gossip if the hero comes by day and fears the tiger still more if he comes alone at midnight, and says the girl has stopped the millet songs. In Natrinai 156 the friend refuses the night tryst and asks him to come by day, since they will guard the millet for many days.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two friends weigh the same choice of day or night and decide it by the millet: while the girls still guard the field, as in Natrinai 156, the day offers a safe meeting place, and once the guarding has stopped, as in Akananuru 118, day and night are both closed and only marriage is left."},{"a":"nt:159","b":"ku:14","type":"develops","note":"In Kuruntokai 14 the man wishes that once he wins the girl the town would know it and many in the street would call him the good woman's husband. In Natrinai 159 the friend asks him to let his chariot lodge in the fishing hamlet so that it wakes in uproar.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both treat the town's knowing as the way to marriage, but from opposite sides: the man in Kuruntokai 14 wishes for it after winning her, while the friend in Natrinai 159 demands it now as the means of winning her, using the hamlet's noise as pressure."},{"a":"nt:160","b":"ku:58","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 58: the man tells his rebuking friends it would be well if they could hold back his sickness, but it has spread like butter melting on a hot rock. Natrinai 160: he tells his rebuker that he knew good sense, shame and conduct better than him, before he saw her eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"These are two answers to the same rebuke: one grants that the friends' aim is good and says no one can carry it out, the other says the friend's lesson is one he already knew best, so together they show the rebuke fails not for lack of knowledge but because sight of her has overridden it."},{"a":"nt:160","b":"k:1133","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1133: he once had shame and manliness, and now has the madal. Natrinai 160: shame, decency and good conduct he knew better than his friend, before he saw her.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The couplet says the virtues were possessed and are now gone; the poem says they are still known, better than the rebuker knows them, and simply no longer govern him, so love takes away their rule, not the knowledge of them."},{"a":"nt:161","b":"a:54","type":"same_image","note":"Both returning men, speaking to the charioteer, picture the wife with the same words, the freckle-waisted woman who deceives their son (புதல்வற் பொய்க்கும், திதலை அல்குல்); Akananuru 54 fills it with her promising the moon milk for a gold tali, Natrinai 161 with her pointing out strangers to the boy.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared phrase shows that soothing the son with a fiction was a fixed mullai picture of the home a returning man imagines, and the two fillings show where the poet's freedom lay: in choosing the fiction, a coaxed moon or a stranger passed off as the father."},{"a":"nt:161","b":"nt:169","type":"develops","note":"Both men are going home with the task done; in 161 he wonders whether the omen birds have already told her, in 169 he speaks to the house lizard and asks it to chirp for her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the returning husband imagining the omen from her side and then trying to work it from his own, so the signs she watches for become signs he wishes he could send."},{"a":"nt:166","b":"k:49","type":"develops","note":"Kural 49 says that what is called virtue is household life; in Natrinai 166 a husband says that seeing his wife he is like those settled in virtue, and gives that and his son's first play as reasons he will not leave.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The poem shows the Kural's claim used as an argument against the paalai journey: a husband offers the household's settled virtue as his answer to the pull of wealth abroad, which makes the maxim a reason to stay."},{"a":"nt:166","b":"a:66","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 66 the sight of his small son stops a husband on his way to a second wedding; in Natrinai 166 the husband himself names his son's first games as one reason he will not leave.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the son is the akam poets' anchor of the husband to the house, named by the man himself in 166 and working against his intent in 66, so in both the wife's fear is answered by the child rather than by her own claim on him."},{"a":"nt:167","b":"pn:127","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 127 praises Aay, whose bards led away his elephants and whose palace stood bare from giving; Natrinai 167 makes the rattle of the chariots bards won at Aay's feast the sound of a heron's cry on the shore of the man whose bard the friend turns away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows an akam friend borrowing Aay's puram fame as the measure of a generous patron, so the bard who in puram goes home rich is in akam the go-between sent home empty: one profession seen from the giver's hall and from the wronged wife's door."},{"a":"nt:168","b":"a:72","type":"tension","note":"In Akananuru 72 the girl says the man who came through the midnight rain with only a spear for company (எஃகு துணையாக) is not cruel and the fault is hers; in Natrinai 168 the friend asks the man who comes at midnight with his spear for company (எஃகு துணை ஆக) whether that can be called good conduct. The same act is blameless in one and a failure of conduct in the other.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same lone midnight journey is innocent to the girl and faulty to the friend, which shows the night tryst was judged by who spoke: the girl takes the blame on herself, while the friend's charge is her lever toward marriage."},{"a":"nt:169","b":"a:351","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 351 the returning man pictures the lizard chirping at her ear as she worships the omen birds; in Natrinai 169 he speaks to the lizard on the long wall and asks whether it will chirp to end her worry.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the house lizard as an oracle both ends of the separation rely on: she listens for it at home, and he, on the road, begs it to speak for him."},{"a":"nt:170","b":"pn:123","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 123 praises Malaiyan for chariots given more freely than the raindrops on the peak of Mullur; Natrinai 170 recalls Malaiyan's single spear routing the many Aryans at Mullur, as the image of wives helpless before one rival.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together they show a chieftain's fame had two faces, giving and lone valour, and the akam poet chose the war face because the household's problem was one woman beating many: akam borrowed from puram exactly the facet its situation needed."},{"a":"nt:173","b":"a:388","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 388 the girl would ask the mother's velan whether his god goes hunting on a deer's track with a bloody arrow; in Natrinai 173 the friend asks whether saying that the hill lord caused the sickness would wrong Murugan.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both turn the veri rite's diagnosis against itself by slipping the real cause, the hill man, into a question about the god, which shows that disclosure (அறத்தொடு நிலை) had a recognised form: a mock-pious question put within the lover's hearing."},{"a":"nt:174","b":"k:1311","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1311 has the woman refuse her man's chest because all women feast on him with their eyes; in Natrinai 174 the wife, told she should be well now that he is back and never leaves her side, says he gave his chest to another woman and an embrace is worth nothing without love.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural grounds the refusal in shared possession; the poem grounds it in absent love even when he is wholly present, so the pair separates two grievances the oodal chapters run together: that he is shared, and that he is there without love.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:175","b":"k:1147","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1147 says love's sickness grows with the town's gossip as manure and the mother's words as water; Natrinai 175 shows a mother who has never met the man, believing the hamlet women's petty talk, glaring at the girl's pale body at midnight.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The poem supplies the order the couplet only implies: the gossip reaches the mother first and her watch follows from it, so she acts on hearsay about a man she does not know, which is why her words feed the sickness instead of curing it."},{"a":"nt:177","b":"k:1276","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1276: the woman tells her friend that his great comfort and eager lovemaking hold the hardship to come and the thought of his lack of love; in Natrinai 177 the girl tells her friend she reads his going in his being more tender than before, along with his spear wiped and his shield decked.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet names one sign, excess tenderness; the poem shows it read together with the readied weapons, so the Kural's inference is the abstract of a scene in which caress and war gear together give the man away."},{"a":"nt:179","b":"a:17","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 17 the foster mother remembers her girl tiring at a little ball play and lying in her arms, and asks whether those feet can walk the waste; in Natrinai 179 the nurse recalls the girl sobbing and refusing milk over a creeper the cow ate, and hears she has crossed the waste smiling.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both measure the elopement by remembered childish frailty, but 179 ends on the girl's smile, so the pair shows the nurse's lament with two endings: fear for a frail child in 17, and in 179 bewilderment that a child who wept at a small loss left without grief."},{"a":"nt:180","b":"a:145","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the punnai felled in the war of Anni and Thithiyan as a simile: in Akananuru 145 the foster mother wishes on her own hand the ruin of that tree; in Natrinai 180 the friend fears she will be ruined like it in the quarrel of husband and wife.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the felled guardian tree was a stock figure of ruin, and 180 draws from it what 145 does not need, the tree's innocence: it is the bystander destroyed because two powers fought over it, which is the go-between's place in a marriage quarrel."},{"a":"nt:185","b":"nt:192","type":"tension","note":"Both men measure the girl by a divine figure on Kolli: in 185 the figure-like girl has plotted his death; in 192 thinking of her figure-like beauty makes the beast-haunted path safe.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Read together, the Kolli image proves double-edged: the same god-made beauty is a killing power when the girl or her friend refuses him and a protecting charm when she fears for him, so its force depends on which way her favour runs."},{"a":"nt:185","b":"ku:89","type":"same_image","note":"The figure painted by a god on the western face of Porayan's Kolli measures the girl in both; in the Kuruntokai the friend uses it, in Natrinai 185 the lover.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend uses the Kolli figure to plead that so innocent a girl can bring no harm to the town, while the lover uses the same figure to say she is killing him; the shared image shows the figure stood for a beauty both harmless in itself and dangerous to the one who sees it."},{"a":"nt:190","b":"ku:258","type":"same_image","note":"Both compare the girl's beauty to Arkkadu of Azhisi, father of Chendan: Natrinai 190 at its height, which the lover's heart rejoiced in; Kuruntokai 258 as it wastes under gossip.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two poems use Azhisi's rich town as a fixed standard of the girl's beauty and show both ends of it, the lover's delight and the friend's report of its ruin, so that one patron's town served the poets as a measure of beauty gained and lost."},{"a":"nt:195","b":"a:290","type":"same_image","note":"The neythal of Thondi stands for the girl's eyes in both: in 195 the flower cut by reapers tossing in water, in Akananuru 290 the flower whose sapphire beauty has turned to gold.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that Thondi's neythal was a set measure for the beloved's eyes, and that two poets spoiled it in two ways: by tears, the cut flower tossing in water, and by pallor, the blue turned to gold."},{"a":"nt:196","b":"k:1210","type":"develops","note":"In both the separated woman speaks to the moon about the man who is gone: the Kural asks it not to set so she may see him; Natrinai 196 asks it to show where he hides.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural compresses into one blessing and plea a scene the Natrinai poem argues at length: the moon sees all worlds, so its silence is a witness's lie and its waning the punishment, which shows how much reasoning lies behind the Kural's single request."},{"a":"nt:184","b":"a:117","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 117 the foster mother says the girl did not think of the nochchi or her playmates when she left; in Natrinai 184 the mother cannot bear to see the nochchi and the terrace where the girl played.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The pair shows the same household shrub on both sides of the elopement: forgotten by the daughter who has gone, and unbearable to the mother who stays, so the left-behind play things measure how unequal the parting is.","gr":4},{"a":"nt:186","b":"k:212","type":"develops","note":"Kural 212 says wealth earned by effort exists to do good to the worthy; in Natrinai 186 the wife says her husband crossed the waste for wealth with a heart that strives for others.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The wife in the poem defends a painful separation with the Kural's ethic of earning in order to give, so the household rule appears here as the reason that made the paalai journey honourable in her eyes.","gr":3},{"a":"nt:194","b":"k:211","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 211: duty seeks no return, and the world makes none to the rain. Natrinai 194: the friend asks what return (கைம்மாறு) they can make to the elephant and the parrot.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The friend applies the Kural's word for repayment to beasts who helped without meaning to, the parrot that raided the millet and so kept the girls in the field, so her mock gratitude turns the rule of unrepayable benefit into a teasing way of announcing that the meetings are over."},{"a":"nt:200","b":"pn:228","type":"qualifies","note":"Both poems call on the potter of the town: Purananuru 228 to make the burial urn for a dead Chola king; Natrinai 200 as the crier who proclaims festivals, asked to proclaim the bard a liar.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Taken together, the potter appears as the town's public servant for both its funerals and its festivals, and the friend's commission parodies the solemn address to him by making the crier's voice carry a domestic quarrel."},{"a":"nt:201","b":"a:209","type":"develops","note":"Both use the same formula line (செவ் வேர்ப் பலவின் பயம் கெழு கொல்லி) and the god-made image on Kolli that does not decay: in Natrinai 201 the man says the girl stays in his heart like that image under storm and earthquake; in Akananuru 209 the friend says the absent man cannot forget her beauty, which is like that image.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kolli image lends its one property, that it does not perish, to the lover's memory in one poem and to the woman's beauty in the other, and both times it answers a doubter (the rebuking companion, the weeping girl), so the image worked as a stock guarantee that a bond will last.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:202","b":"a:17","type":"same_image","note":"Both are elopement poems that see the flowering trees of the wasteland as the lamps of a town festival: kongam like Karthigai lamps in Natrinai 202, silk-cotton like a festive town's oil lamps in Akananuru 17.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The eloping man offers the festival-lit forest to the girl as something homely and beautiful, while the foster mother left behind sees the same lights on a waterless road her girl's little feet must cross, so one image of the paalai road comforts or terrifies according to who speaks."},{"a":"nt:206","b":"a:302","type":"develops","note":"In both the friend, with the man listening, reads the mother's look at harvest time: in Akananuru 302 the mother keeps the girl home and stares at her body; in Natrinai 206 the father sends her to guard the millet while the mother wishes the wedding vengai would flower and looks at her face.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together the two show the ripe millet as the moment when the parents read their daughter either as a field guard or as a bride, and the friend in both turns the mother's silent look into the deadline that should make the listening man ask for marriage."},{"a":"nt:208","b":"nt:214","type":"tension","note":"In Natrinai 208 the friend says the great rain's voice seems to seek out parted lovers and bring them home; in Natrinai 214 the woman hears the rain's voice as flashing and roaring in mockery of her grief, because he has not come.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The monsoon thunder is the same sign of the promised season in both, but the friend before the parting reads it as a power that fetches the man and the wife after the deadline reads it as a jeer, so the rain's meaning in paalai depends on whether the promise is still ahead or already broken.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:210","b":"nt:214","type":"tension","note":"Natrinai 210 defines the wealth of the worthy as gentleness that fears the distress of those who depend on one; in Natrinai 214 the man leaves his wife, whose bangles slip from grief, to win wealth because fame, pleasure and giving need it.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"By 210's definition the departing man of 214 is pursuing the wrong wealth, since the distress of a dependant is exactly what true wealth fears to cause, so the anthology holds the akam parting for wealth and a moral critique of it side by side."},{"a":"nt:210","b":"k:241","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 210 says grand speech and harsh riding are not wealth, only the fruit of deeds, and the wealth of the worthy is a gentle-eyed nature; Kural 241 says the wealth of grace is the wealth of wealths, since the base also hold goods.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend aims the Kural's general ranking of wealths at a particular rich husband who has hurt his wife, and narrows grace to fear of the dependant's distress, so the maxim is shown at work as a rebuke inside a marriage."},{"a":"nt:212","b":"k:66","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 66 says only those who have not heard their own children's prattle call the flute and lute sweet; in Natrinai 212 the bards tune their strings on the road while at home the child's sweet voice asking to be held made the mother anxious each time.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The child's voice that the Kural ranks above music becomes, while the father is away, a reminder of his absence, so the couplet's household joy assumes the father is present to hear it."},{"a":"nt:214","b":"k:613","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 613 says the pride of generosity rests on effort; the man of Natrinai 214 leaves saying fame, pleasure and giving do not come to those who sit idle.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The akam poem shows the Kural's rule as a departing husband's stock reason, quoted by the wife he leaves and then mocked by the thunder, so the maxim that giving needs effort was already common speech, and the poem counts what it costs the one left behind."},{"a":"nt:216","b":"k:788","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 788 says friendship clears distress at once, like the hand of one whose garment slips; the other woman of Natrinai 216 says her lovers do not help her sorrow as the hand helps a hurt eye, yet the town they live in is still sweet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The parathai admits her lovers fail the Kural's test of friendship, the hand's unthinking help, and still finds their mere presence in town enough, so the pair shows love surviving a failure that by the Kural's measure would end a friendship."},{"a":"nt:217","b":"k:1260","type":"tension","note":"In Kural 1260 the woman says those whose hearts melt like fat in fire cannot say they will join and then hold out in a sulk; in Natrinai 217 the wife says that though the man is very sweet she will brood and sulk, since making him feel it drives off her loneliness.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Kural woman doubts that a loving heart can keep a sulk, while the Natrinai wife resolves to keep one precisely because she loves him and wants the long loneliness to end, so the two disagree on whether the sulk is weakness overcome or a strategy of love."},{"a":"nt:218","b":"k:1222","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1222 the woman asks the bewildered evening whether its partner is as hard-hearted as hers; in Natrinai 218 the girl, past the promised season, lists the evening's creatures, the bats going home, the cock calling his mate, the owl, and asks if she must hear the andril alone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural woman makes evening a fellow sufferer, while the Natrinai girl finds every creature of the evening paired or going home, so the same projection onto nightfall can give companionship or prove isolation, and the unmarried girl gets only the second."},{"a":"nt:219","b":"k:1188","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1188 the woman says people speak of her pallor but none says he left her; in Natrinai 219 the girl says she will not blame him though pallor spreads and she dies, since he and she joined of their own accord.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural woman complains that blame falls on her skin and not on him, while the Natrinai girl takes the pallor as her own because the union was her own choice, so the pair shows how a self-chosen secret union changes who can be blamed for the suffering."},{"a":"nt:220","b":"ku:17","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 17 says men whose love hardens ride a palm stem as a horse, wear erukku buds as a garland and become a spectacle in the street; in Natrinai 220 small boys with erukku garlands and a horse that does not eat roam the street with the man.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Kuruntokai 17 states the madal as a lover's desperate public act, and Natrinai 220 shows children copying its very costume as a street game, so the madal was familiar enough to be play, and the lover uses the children's ease against the adults' censure."},{"a":"nt:220","b":"k:214","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 214 says one who knows what is fitting (ஒப்புரவு) lives and others are counted among the dead; in Natrinai 220 the lover says the townspeople, if they knew ஒப்புரவு, would side with him and tell him not to be a stranger to the girl.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The lover appeals to the Kural's civic duty of fellow-feeling to claim the town's support for a love match it is condemning, so the pair shows ஒப்புரவு invoked against the town's own idea of propriety."},{"a":"nt:221","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1268: let the king fight and win, and let me join my home and keep the evening feast. Natrinai 221: the work done, the man bids the charioteer hurry to the wife who loves to host guests and the son who now calls him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the soldier's wish to trade the king's war for the evening feast at home in two lines; the Natrinai scene fills that home with a hospitable wife and a child just learning to walk and call him father, so the 'feast' of the couplet is shown to be a whole household's welcome, not only the wife's."},{"a":"nt:221","b":"a:374","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 374: campaign over, the man bids the chariot go through the morning rains so the slender wife may receive him as a guest. Natrinai 221: the man bids the charioteer go so he may hear his small son's first call to him.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both homecoming speeches to the charioteer turn on விருந்து, but the Akananuru makes the returning husband the guest his wife receives, while the Natrinai makes the wife the one who loves receiving guests and adds the son, so the convention could place the hero either as guest or as master of a hospitable house.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:224","b":"k:1154","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1154: if he who said 'fear not' leaves, is there any fault in those who trusted his assuring word? Natrinai 224: what can I say now to those who assured me 'we will not part' and set off for the waste?","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural poses as a moral question who is at fault when an assurance is broken; the Natrinai woman, using the same verb of assuring (தெளித்த), cannot even ask it aloud and lets the budding kuravu and the koel's 'stay joined' do the accusing, so the broken word is judged by the season instead of by her."},{"a":"nt:226","b":"pn:184","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 184: a king who takes his due like an elephant fed balls of paddy keeps the land rich, but one who grabs is like an elephant loosed in the field. Natrinai 226: kings do not take gold that ruins the land's wealth, yet her lover does not know the measure of the wealth he seeks.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The counsel that a king must take tribute without ruining its source had become a proverb a wife could quote, and Natrinai 226 turns it on the household: a husband who leaves to make wealth regardless of her life is the elephant loose in the field."},{"a":"nt:227","b":"ku:258","type":"same_image","note":"Kuruntokai 258: the friend says talk has risen on seeing the beauty, like Azhisi's Arkkadu, of this girl wasting. Natrinai 227: the friend says the man's grace has become talk as loud as the streets of the Cholas' Arkkadu.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In two speeches of the same kind, a friend telling the man that gossip has risen, the one town Arkkadu stands once for the girl's endangered beauty and once for the noise of the gossip itself, so a famous town was a measure akam poets could set against either side of the alar."},{"a":"nt:229","b":"k:1151","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1151: if you are not going, tell me; tell your quick return to those who will be alive then. Natrinai 229: so go, finish the work, but do not linger, for the north wind has come.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"Faced with the same announcement of departure, the Kural's speaker refuses any promise of a quick return as useless to the dying, while the Natrinai friend bargains for exactly that promise and binds it to a season already arrived, so two stances to a leaving husband stand side by side: refusal by death, and consent with a deadline."},{"a":"nt:237","b":"pn:133","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 133: go and see Ay of the chariots, whose giving is like the rain. Natrinai 237: the rain clouds rise like the elephants Andiran gathered for suppliants.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poet likens Ay's giving to rain, and the akam poet turns it round and likens the rain clouds to Ay's gift elephants, so the patron's bounty and the monsoon were interchangeable images and the friend can offer the season as a promise as sure as a great giver's word."},{"a":"nt:237","b":"kn:12","type":"same_image","note":"Kar Narpathu 12: his coming is certain; every day the dark clouds, like elephants anointed with oil, rise on the right. Natrinai 237: look, the clouds rise like the elephants Andiran gathered.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both friends point a pining woman to clouds that rise like elephants as proof of the season's return, but Kar Narpathu's anointed elephants and the rising on the right make the cloud an omen, while Natrinai's patron's elephants make it a gift laid up for those who wait, so the same image could reassure by augury or by the ethic of giving."},{"a":"nt:238","b":"ku:158","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 158: the woman asks the storm cloud, whose thunder strikes snakes on the slopes and can shake the Himalaya, whether it has no pity for women without a companion. Natrinai 238: the woman tells the season's cloud that its coming is not like the noble, and its thunder that dulls the snakes' hoods is not sweet even to a hard heart.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both women rebuke the thundering cloud of the season, but the Kuruntokai shames it by its might, while the Natrinai holds it to the conduct of the noble (சான்றோர்), so the reproach to the absent man is voiced in one through power misused and in the other through manners breached."},{"a":"nt:240","b":"k:1108","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1108: sweet to two who desire each other is an embrace that not even a breeze can part. Natrinai 240: even the closest embrace is such that sleep comes between, yet the forest road stands like a mountain.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the gapless embrace the ideal of union; the Natrinai lover admits that even such an embrace is parted by sleep, and that admission is what lets his heart argue for leaving, so the ideal of perfect closeness becomes, in the man's mouth, a reason that union is already imperfect."},{"a":"nt:241","b":"ku:16","type":"develops","note":"Both open with the same words, உள்ளார் கொல்லோ தோழி: in Kuruntokai 16 the woman pictures the kalli waste he crossed, a lizard calling its mate with a sound like thieves testing arrows; in Natrinai 241 she pictures her own surroundings, the reed flowering in the north wind and the dewy midnight.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the formula question can be answered from either end of the separation, the dangers of his road or the season at her door, so 'do they not think of us' is a frame into which the poet sets whichever world the woman imagines, and the choice of world is the poem's real statement."},{"a":"nt:243","b":"k:754","type":"tension","note":"Kural 754 says wealth rightly gained yields virtue and pleasure, placing wealth below and in service of aram; Natrinai 243's woman says that if parting for wealth in such a season is men's nature, wealth must be dearer than aram itself. On the plain reading men's conduct, as she sees it, contradicts the Kural's ordering.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the Kural's ordering of virtue over wealth being turned into an accusation from inside the household: the woman accepts the scale but finds that men's going inverts it, so the ethical rule became a weapon in the akam speaker's mouth."},{"a":"nt:245","b":"nt:250","type":"develops","note":"The same question, யாரையோ, is asked in both: in 245 the shore lord, struck at first sight, asks the girl 'who are you?'; in 250 the wife, estranged, asks her husband 'who are you?' as he comes to embrace their son.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Holding the two together traces the question across the whole course of akam love, from the wonder of a stranger at first meeting to a wife's pretended non-recognition in sulking, so the same words that began the bond are later used to deny it."},{"a":"nt:246","b":"kn:12","type":"develops","note":"In Natrinai 246 the friend reassures the woman with a lizard chirping on the lucky side, a koel on the house's nochchi and the season's flowers; in Kar Narpathu 12 she calls his coming certain because the dark clouds rise every day on the right.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that the friend's reassurance in the rains rested on reading omens, the right side being lucky in both, and that the rainy season itself was read as one more omen of return, which is why Natrinai 246 can end its certainty as a question."},{"a":"nt:247","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1102 has the man say that this jewelled girl is herself the medicine for the sickness she causes; Natrinai 247's friend tells the man that for the girl's pallor there is no medicine other than him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the cure that is also the cause belongs to both lovers, and the Natrinai shows the claim used as pressure: what the man says of her in delight, the friend turns back on him as an argument that only marriage can treat the girl's sickness."},{"a":"nt:248","b":"ku:66","type":"develops","note":"In Kuruntokai 66 the friend calls the konrai foolish for taking untimely rain for the season the man named; in Natrinai 248 she calls the thunder false and refuses to be fooled like the young peafowl who dance at it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows a fixed move of the friend's, denying the season by calling some creature of the land deceived, and in Natrinai 248 the move turns inward, since she speaks of herself as the one who will not be fooled, so the device is an argument about belief, not about weather."},{"a":"nt:248","b":"kn:1","type":"tension","note":"In Kar Narpathu 1 the friend says that with the sky roaring and pouring, the man who said 'we will come' can hardly fail to come now; in Natrinai 248 the friend says the same roar is false thunder and not the promised season. On the plain reading the two friends make incompatible claims about what the rain shows.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Set side by side, the same evidence serves the friend either way: when the season must mean his return she affirms it, and when his absence would make him a liar she denies it, so the season's sign was something the friend argued about rather than read off."},{"a":"nt:250","b":"a:16","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 16 the wife finds a courtesan secretly embracing her son in the chariot street; in Natrinai 250 the husband goes to embrace the son in the street and the wife, taking it otherwise, shies away.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the small son in the street is where the marriage's suspicions are read from both sides, another woman betraying herself by loving him and the husband being refused because of what his embrace carries, so the child who should join the parents becomes the evidence between them."},{"a":"nt:252","b":"k:616","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 616 says effort brings prosperity and its absence brings want; Natrinai 252 states the same as the man's resolve (no rare wealth for those who stay) and then shows the planned work blocked by the woman's beauty.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The pair shows the maxim of effort accepted in full by the lover and yet not acted on, so the Natrinai marks the limit of the Kural's rule: it decides nothing while something dearer than prosperity stands in the way.","gr":3},{"a":"nt:253","b":"pn:109","type":"develops","note":"Both are by Kapilar: in Purananuru 109 the kings besieging Paari's Parambu, with its jackfruit and its springs, cannot take it by force, but bards who come singing will be given it; in Natrinai 253 the girl, grown beautiful and hard to reach, is like Paari's jackfruit hill closed in by rain.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read with the puram poem, the akam simile carries its argument: Parambu yields not to siege but to those who come asking, so the friend's comparison tells the man that the girl will not be had by stealth but only by asking for her in marriage."},{"a":"nt:253","b":"pn:337","type":"same_image","note":"In Purananuru 337 a girl courted by kings is hard to see, like the cold spring of Paari's Parambu, shut in the house while her brothers fight the suitors; in Natrinai 253 the girl, guarded like Paari's hill, is hard to reach for her secret lover.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows Parambu as the standard image of the guarded marriageable girl in both akam and puram, and sets two kinds of suitor against the same closed house: kings who would take her by war and a lover who must now stop coming by night."},{"a":"nt:255","b":"a:72","type":"develops","note":"Both are the girl's fear for the man on the hill path at midnight in rain: Akananuru 72 lists the path's dangers (a tiger that killed an elephant, a snake's gem, crocodiles) and ends by blaming herself; Natrinai 255 lists ghouls, a tiger that fought a tusker, the snake searching for its gem and thunder, and ends by wishing he would not come.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the same catalogue of night dangers leads to two different conclusions, self-blame for drawing him out and the wish that he stay away, so the poets used the fixed list as a premise and placed the girl's character in what she concludes from it."},{"a":"nt:255","b":"ku:375","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 375 says it is good if he does not come today because the millet has ripened and the night watchmen beat their drum at midnight; Natrinai 255 says the same, வாரார்ஆயினோ நன்றுமன், because of ghouls, a tiger, a snake and thunder on his path.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two give the same wish two different reasons, discovery by the watchmen and death on the path, so the wish that he not come is a set response whose grounds range from the secret to his life, and Natrinai 255 shows the graver one."},{"a":"nt:256","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1151 says: if you are not going, tell me; tell your quick return to those who will be alive then. Natrinai 256 is the man telling his grieving wife that he has given up going, through the burnt forest and even through the cool forest of the rains.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the Natrinai supplies the one answer the Kural's speaker would accept, a plain 'I am not going', and shows that to be believed it had to renounce the pleasant season as well as the harsh one, since a man who stays only while the road is burnt will go when it rains."},{"a":"nt:256","b":"nt:242","type":"develops","note":"In both the stag (இரலை) with his young doe is shown to the reader in the rains: in 242 he stands searching for her when the herd scatters, and the returning man hurries; in 256 he rests holding her in the shade, and the man gives up going.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Holding the two together shows the iraicchi's sense fixed while the man's act changes: the stag keeping to his doe stands for the man's duty to his wife both when he hurries home and when he declines to leave."},{"a":"nt:257","b":"nt:255","type":"develops","note":"In 255 the girl fears the midnight path for him and wishes he would not come; in 257 the friend tells the man himself that she knows fierce beasts roam the path and he still comes at midnight, and presses marriage.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows one danger serving two speakers: the girl's private fear in 255 becomes in the friend's mouth in 257 a lever on the man, so the convention of the dangerous path is both a feeling and an argument for marriage."},{"a":"nt:259","b":"nt:251","type":"develops","note":"In 251 the speaker begs the millet to ripen late so the girl's days in the field may last; in 259 the millet heads have ripened like the sea drawing back, and the friend asks what they will do.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence, the two poems are the before and after of one threat, making the millet's ripening the clock of the secret love: the harvest that ends the field season also ends the meetings unless the man marries."},{"a":"nt:260","b":"a:46","type":"develops","note":"Both use the marutham buffalo as ullurai for the straying husband: in Akananuru 46 it breaks its tether at night, scatters the fish and grazes the lotus; in Natrinai 260 it has grazed the red lilies, tired of the lotus, and sleeps on the sand away from the field.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the one creature carries different stages of the husband's straying, the breaking out in Akananuru 46 and the surfeit and sleeping elsewhere in Natrinai 260, so the buffalo ullurai was a fixed vehicle whose actions the poets chose to fit the moment of the quarrel."},{"a":"nt:262","b":"k:1044","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1044 says poverty brings even the well-born to degrading words; Natrinai 262's man, seeing his wife already wasting, ends on 'hard indeed is the shame of having nothing' (இன்மையது இளிவே).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Natrinai shows what Valluvar's shame of poverty weighs against in a man's life: it is the one argument strong enough to set beside a wife's midnight suffering, so the couplet's social fear turns out to be the engine of the paalai parting."},{"a":"nt:262","b":"a:199","type":"tension","note":"Both are the man arguing with his heart over leaving his wife for wealth; in Akananuru 199 he refuses even riches like the land Narmudi Cheral won back, in Natrinai 262 he ends on the unbearable shame of poverty, which on the plain reading tips him toward going.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together they show that the debate with the heart had no fixed outcome in the tradition: the same scene of the wife's shoulders could close either way, and what decides each poem is the argument the poet places last."},{"a":"nt:263","b":"nt:272","type":"same_image","note":"Both are neythal poems about a man who will not marry: in 263 an old lame stork brings sea fish to his pregnant hen in the field, in 272 a red-beaked sea crow searches the backwater for fish for his hen heavy with young on the sand.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Two poets put the same act, a male shore bird feeding its pregnant mate, beside the same complaint, so the provider bird was a conventional reproach that let the women shame an unmarried lover without naming his fault."},{"a":"nt:265","b":"a:152","type":"same_image","note":"Both are Paranar's, both the man speaking to his heart after meeting the girl: in Akananuru 152 her shoulders are like the plumage of the joyous peacock on the Ezhil hill of Nannan, lord of Paaram; in Natrinai 265 her hair is like the plumage of the joyous peacock (கலி மயில் கலாவத்து) on Ori's Kolli, and Paaram, guarded by Minjili, is another of her measures.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows Paranar keeping one formula, a chief's hill peacock, for the girl and changing only the chief, and Akananuru 152 supplies the Paaram-Nannan link that makes sense of the elliptic 265: her beauty is ranked among the guarded possessions of the hill chiefs."},{"a":"nt:265","b":"a:208","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 208, also Paranar's man speaking to his heart after union, tells of Minjili's war at Paazhi and ends on her hair fragrant as the flowers of Ori's Kolli; Natrinai 265 names Minjili guarding Paaram and Ori's Kolli in its run of comparisons for her hair.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The longer poem tells the war story behind the names that the short one only lists, so Natrinai 265's Minjili is not a decorative name but the victor of a battle Paranar's audience knew, and the girl is measured against fame won in war."},{"a":"nt:269","b":"k:1151","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are, on Parimelazhagar's reading of the Kural, the friend trying to stop the man's departure: Kural 1151 tells him to promise a quick return to those who will still be alive; Natrinai 269 sets before him his son climbing down his chest and his wife's eyes on his face.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows two opposite tactics for one thurai, the Kural's friend making the woman's death the stake and the Natrinai's friend making the man's own pleasures at home the stake, so the couplet's threat is one option among the friend's arguments, not the only one."},{"a":"nt:270","b":"ku:292","type":"same_image","note":"Both are Paranar's and both use Nannan's cruelty to a woman as a measure: Kuruntokai 292 curses the mother to go to hell like Nannan who killed a girl for eating a fruit; Natrinai 270 tells the husband his cruelty is worse than Nannan's to a woman's hair.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Holding them together shows that Paranar made one king's notorious offence against a woman into a standing scale for cruelty in love, turned in one poem on a guarding mother and in the other on a straying husband.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:271","b":"a:165","type":"same_image","note":"Both are about a mother whose daughter has eloped: Akananuru 165, seen by onlookers, has a calf drinking at the buffalo in the old town, the playmates mourning and the mother crying 'let my life end'; Natrinai 271, in the mother's own voice, has a buffalo calf asleep on the dung of the house, the playmates going home at evening and her curse on Death for not taking her.","grade":"wrong","better":null,"te":"The two poems share an inventory for the house a daughter has left (the buffalo calf beside its mother, the girl's playmates, the mother's wish to die), which shows these were the fixed signs of that grief and lets the calf safe beside its dam be read as the daughter who did not stay."},{"a":"nt:272","b":"a:170","type":"same_image","note":"Both are neythal poems of a girl neglected by the lord of the shore with the sea crow and its 'lovely hen' (காமர் பேடை, காமர் பெடை): in Akananuru 170 the small sea crow rests with its mate while she is alone at midnight, in Natrinai 272 the crow fishes for its pregnant mate while the man is not kind.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the shore crow with its mate measuring the man's neglect in two ways, by companionship in the one and by provision in the other, so the bird's pairing was a flexible yardstick for whatever the girl lacks."},{"a":"nt:273","b":"a:242","type":"develops","note":"Both have the friend speak to the girl while the man overhears about the mother who, not knowing the cause, will bring the velan to hold a veri; in Akananuru 242 the friend says they should go to him before the goat is offered, in Natrinai 273 the poem ends on her trembling heart.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the announced veri as a set lever on the overhearing man, the false diagnosis that will expose the secret, and Akananuru 242 states the remedy, his embrace before the sacrifice, that Natrinai 273 leaves him to supply."},{"a":"nt:274","b":"ku:66","type":"qualifies","note":"Both carry the thurai of the friend disputing the season of his return: in Kuruntokai 66 the konrai are foolish to take the untimely rain for his season; in Natrinai 274 the rain has come and she asks whether the man who invited the girl along the rain-washed hills has those words now.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows two ways to deny the season: Kuruntokai 66 blames the trees and so keeps faith in his promise, while Natrinai 274 sets his own old words against the rain and so doubts him, so the same thurai could comfort or accuse."},{"a":"nt:277","b":"ku:2","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kuruntokai 2 the man asks the bee, knower of flowers, whether any is more fragrant than her hair; in Natrinai 277 the woman curses the bee for sucking the yellow peeram but not her yellow pallor, because it has no scent, and asks it to carry word to her man.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Akam's bee is a judge of scent, and the pair shows the limit of that office: it can testify to a beloved's beauty in union but cannot be made to carry the scentless pallor of separation, which is why the girl's appeal to it fails."},{"a":"nt:277","b":"a:170","type":"develops","note":"Both have the girl, with no human messenger, beg a creature to go to her man: in Akananuru 170 the crab, since grove, backwater and punnai will not speak; in Natrinai 277 the bee, after she scolds it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the creature-messenger as the last resort of a girl who cannot speak openly, and Natrinai 277 adds the grievance that the creature has its own pleasures to prefer, so the appeal is also a complaint that even the messenger neglects her."},{"a":"nt:280","b":"k:81","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 81 says the whole of keeping a house is for tending guests; in Natrinai 280 the wife, urged not to sulk at her philandering husband, says she cannot even sulk because her house, like the Velir's Kunrur, is never free of feasting guests.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The Natrinai shows the Kural's household duty turned into a weapon in love: hospitality is a reason no one can fault, so the wife uses it to refuse both the sulk and the husband, and the virtue becomes the frame of her pride."},{"a":"nt:284","b":"k:612","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 612 warns against letting a task die half done; in Natrinai 284 the man's own reason says the same, that leaving work unfinished brings ignorance and disgrace, while his heart urges him home.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the Kural's maxim on unfinished work already living inside an akam man as the voice of his reason, and the Natrinai adds what the maxim leaves out: obeying it costs a body torn like a rope between two tuskers."},{"a":"nt:284","b":"k:1163","type":"same_image","note":"Natrinai 284 has the man's body as an old rope pulled at both ends by two tuskers, heart against reason; Kural 1163 has the woman's life as a carrying pole with love and shame hung at its two ends, in a body that cannot bear them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Set side by side, the two show one figure, a load-bearing cord or pole strained between two claims, serving both the man's conflict of love against duty and the woman's of love against shame, so the tradition pictured a lover's divided mind as a physical strain whatever the second claim was."},{"a":"nt:291","b":"pn:123","type":"qualifies","note":"Kapilar in Purananuru 123 praises Malaiyan of Mullur for chariots given while sober, more than the raindrops on Mullur's heights; Kapilar in Natrinai 291 measures a girl's ruined beauty by the herd-owners whose cattle the king of Mullur drove off at nightfall on his horse.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the same poet's two Mullur poems show the giving king's other face: the wealth praised in puram as countless gifts came partly from night raids on herds, and akam borrows the raid from the side of those who lost everything."},{"a":"nt:293","b":"pn:291","type":"same_image","note":"The bereaved mother in Natrinai 293 wishes that the young man's mother may suffer great anguish like hers (எம்போல் பெரு விதுப்புறுகமாதோ); the mourner of Purananuru 291 wishes the same on the king (என்போற் பெருவிதுப்பு உறுக) whose garland sent the man to his death.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared curse formula shows that akam treated a daughter's elopement as a bereavement in the full puram sense: the mother speaks in the words a mourner uses over a warrior's body, aimed at the one who caused the loss."},{"a":"nt:300","b":"pn:348","type":"develops","note":"Paranar in Purananuru 348 compares a girl to rich Unur of truthful Thazhumban and pictures suitors' chariots in every shade and elephants tied at every place for her; Paranar in Natrinai 300, with strangers' marriage offers in the colophon, has a chariot set at the gate as her bride-price and likens the lover to Unur's begging elephant at the kitchen.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Holding the two Paranar poems together shows him using Thazhumban's Unur for the same occasion, a girl besieged by suitors with chariots and elephants: in puram the siege threatens the town, in akam the friend turns it into a goad to make the hidden lover ask for her before others do."},{"a":"nt:288","b":"a:242","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 242 the mother calls the diviner not knowing that the man who chased parrots from the millet with them came to the girl in love; in Natrinai 288 the friend fears the diviner will say the girl went to scare small parrots from the millet and Vel seized her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Natrinai 288 puts in the diviner's own verdict the very scene Akananuru 242 says the mother does not know, so the pair shows the veri's error lying not in the facts but in the agent: it finds the right place, the millet field, and names the god instead of the man."},{"a":"nt:289","b":"k:1154","type":"tension","note":"Natrinai 289's woman says her lover would not shift from his word even if the earth shifted, though the promised rains have come without him; Kural 1154's woman asks whether any fault lies with those who believed the assuring words of one who then left.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"On the plain reading the two women answer the same broken promise in incompatible ways, one keeping her faith in his word intact and blaming only her unpitied state, the other turning the promise into a charge against him, which marks out the range a waiting woman's speech could take."},{"a":"nt:296","b":"k:1156","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1156: if he is hard enough to speak of leaving, the hope of his grace is vain; Natrinai 296: told he will go, the woman asks what will become of her, since he leaves with a heart set on work even in the season when the kondrai blooms and the parted grieve.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kural 1156 finds the man's hardness in the announcement itself, and Natrinai 296 adds a second measure the couplet does not give, the timing: to leave in the very season made for grieving apart shows a heart given wholly to work."},{"a":"nt:294","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1102: the jewelled woman is herself the cure of the sickness she causes; Natrinai 294: at the wedding, the friend says the man's chest has become both the sickness and the joy, as the sky bore both fire and wind.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet states the paradox as the lover's private discovery in secret union, while Natrinai 294 has the friend confirm it at the wedding, so the pair shows the same claim moving from a lover's wonder to a public fact once marriage makes the cause of the sickness its lawful cure."},{"a":"nt:298","b":"nt:284","type":"develops","note":"In Natrinai 284 the man already away hears heart and reason argue and fears his body will snap like a rope; in Natrinai 298 the man about to go tells his heart it cannot hold both the hills and her shoulders, and he stays.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read as a pair, the two poems show the same divided man before and after the journey begins: before it, the heart can still win and the journey is dropped, but once the work is under way reason rules and only the body is left to break."},{"a":"nt:303","b":"k:1168","type":"develops","note":"Both set a sleepless woman against a night in which everyone else sleeps: Kural 1168 says the night that lulls all creatures has only her for company; in Natrinai 303 the hamlet has fallen silent and only the paired anril cries.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's lonely vigil turns out, in the Natrinai scene, to have one other waker, a bird that has its mate, so her sleeplessness is measured against a pairing she lacks, and her question is whether he lies awake too."},{"a":"nt:304","b":"k:1187","type":"develops","note":"Both make pallor the immediate price of the lover's absence: Kural 1187 says it came the moment she turned in his embrace; Natrinai 304 says beauty joins her when he joins and pallor wrecks it when he parts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet records the speed of the pallor; the Natrinai woman draws the moral that the same chest is joy and harm at once, like the hand that plays to the asunam and then kills it."},{"a":"nt:306","b":"nt:313","type":"develops","note":"Both are the friend's warning, heard by the man, that the millet field is ending and the girl will be confined: in 313 the crop has just reached reaping time; in 306 the reaping is done and the field is bare stubble.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they show the harvest working as a clock for the secret love, with the friend's message moving from 'soon' to 'now' as the millet goes from ripe ear to stubble."},{"a":"nt:306","b":"a:38","type":"same_image","note":"Both describe the reaped millet field as a broken stalk-head stubble in nearly the same words (பொறை இறுத்த கோல் தலை இருவி; குலவுப்பொறை யிறுத்த கோல்தலை மருவி), and both picture the man finding it empty and sad (பைதல்; பைதலன்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared formula shows that the reaped field was a fixed sign of the closed meeting-place: Natrinai's friend uses it as a threat to make him ask for the girl, while Akananuru's friend imagines him already standing there grieving."},{"a":"nt:308","b":"k:1151","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are the moment the man announces departure: in Kural 1151 she answers with a sharp demand, tell me if you are not going; in Natrinai 308 she can neither ask nor stop him and only presses against his chest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the woman a defiant sentence to stop the journey, while Natrinai shows wordless grief doing what the sentence could not, since it is her silence that melts his resolve."},{"a":"nt:314","b":"n:11","type":"tension","note":"Both start from the same premise, that youth passes and does not return; Naladiyar 11 concludes that the wise renounce desire from childhood; Natrinai 314 uses it to condemn the man who left an embrace for wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same commonplace of fleeting youth served opposite ends: for the akam woman it is a reason to spend youth in love now, for the didactic poet a reason to give love up, so the ascetic use of the maxim takes over an argument love poetry had already made."},{"a":"nt:314","b":"n:19","type":"qualifies","note":"Both argue from an unknown lifespan against putting something off: Naladiyar 19 says do not defer good deeds because the wind may drop the unripe fruit; Natrinai 314 says no one knows the measure of a life, yet he left her for later gain.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The pairing shows that 'do not defer, life is short' was a shared argument whose object changed with the genre, virtue in the Naladiyar and love in the Natrinai, where wealth-seeking is the deferral blamed.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:316","b":"ku:66","type":"develops","note":"Both are the friend's comfort that the rain is false: in Kuruntokai 66 the konrai is foolish for taking stray rain for the season 'before the season comes' (பருவம் வாரா அளவை); in Natrinai 316 the cloud itself is foolish for pouring 'before he comes' (வரல் வாரா அளவை).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Natrinai moves the foolishness from the flowers that believe the rain to the cloud that sends it, so the consolation could discredit the season at either end, the sign or its source."},{"a":"nt:319","b":"nt:303","type":"develops","note":"Both are the same midnight on the guarded shore: in 303 the woman lies awake while the hamlet sleeps and asks whether he knows; in 319 the man, kept away by the watch, lies awake while even the fish sleep.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Set side by side, 319 answers the question in 303: he is awake too, and the anthology places the same sleepless midnight on both sides of the guard that separates them."},{"a":"nt:320","b":"pn:158","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 158 lists Ori of Kolli and Malaiyan who rode Kaari side by side among the seven great givers; Natrinai 320 remembers the street where Ori was killed and Kaari entered as a byword for a stricken town.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The bards' canon of seven generous patrons hides that two of them were enemies, one killing the other, and the love poem shows that killing had become a proverb for a town in panic.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:327","b":"k:990","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 327's girl stakes her life on the belief that the noble do not fail in what they owe; Kural 990 says that if the worthy declined in worth the earth could not bear its load.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the failure of the worthy a cosmic impossibility, while the Natrinai girl turns the same belief into a private wager on one man, so the pair shows what the maxim costs when a single life rests on someone being counted among the worthy."},{"a":"nt:335","b":"k:1167","type":"develops","note":"Both have a woman awake at midnight with a love too great to get past: the Kural's flood with no shore and only herself awake, Natrinai 335's great love with no one to clear it while moon, surf, wind, anril and lute keep the night going.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet's 'only I am here at midnight' is filled in by the older poem with a crowd of sounds and scents that will not stop, showing that the woman's solitude is not silence but a shore night that keeps working on her."},{"a":"nt:340","b":"a:204","type":"same_image","note":"Vaanan's Sirukudi, its fields and groves, is the vehicle for the wife in both: her hair smells like its groves in Akananuru 204, her bangles are as precious as the town in Natrinai 340.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two show one patron's fertile town used as the standard of a wife's worth from both sides of a marriage: the faithful husband hurries home to a beauty like Sirukudi, and the wronged wife names the same town to measure what her husband has taken."},{"a":"nt:336","b":"nt:325","type":"same_image","note":"In both a bear digs into a snake-haunted termite mound on the path: in 325 on the wasteland road the man would take for wealth, in 336 on the hill path he takes to the night meeting.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same bear tearing open the snake's mound marks the danger of the road away from her and of the road to her, so in the anthology the friend's fear attaches to the man's going whichever way he goes."},{"a":"nt:337","b":"nt:52","type":"qualifies","note":"Natrinai 52 has the man refuse the 'life of living apart' (பிரிந்து உறை வாழ்க்கை) for his wife's athiral-and-paathiri scented hair; Natrinai 337 has the friend say that wealth-makers by custom live apart (பிரிந்து உறை மரபின) and forgo that same scented hair.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The shared phrase and the same flowers show the choice put in fixed terms, and the pair tempers the friend's complaint: what she calls a custom set when the world was made, the man of Natrinai 52 does turn down, so it is a choice and not a law."},{"a":"nt:324","b":"nt:66","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 66 is the mother at home picturing her eloped daughter on the hot track; Natrinai 324 is the man or onlookers on the track picturing the mother at home.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Each poem imagines the other end of the elopement: the mother sees only her daughter's eyes red with suffering, while those beside the girl see her running as at play and give their pity to the mother, so each grief misjudges what the other side sees."},{"a":"nt:322","b":"nt:34","type":"develops","note":"In Natrinai 34 the friend tells Murugan he knows the sickness is not his affliction; in Natrinai 322 she tells the girl, within the man's hearing, that the velan has called it a spirit's work.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together the two show the friend undoing the velan's diagnosis before two different audiences, the god himself and the hidden lover, and only the second can end the rite, by marrying the girl."},{"a":"nt:341","b":"a:24","type":"qualifies","note":"Both are cold-season war-camp poems that end on the same turn, யாமே ... பாசறையேமே: in Akananuru 24 the man pictures his wife swimming alone through the long cold nights (தமியள் நீந்தி); in Natrinai 341 he pictures her well companioned at play and calls himself the one without company (துணை இலேம் தமியேம்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"With the frame and the closing words the same, the pair shows that the camp poem could place the loneliness on either side, so whether the wife pines or the soldier envies her company was the poet's choice and not something the convention fixed."},{"a":"nt:343","b":"k:1222","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1222's woman asks the bewildered evening whether its partner is as hard-hearted as hers; Natrinai 343's woman asks whether there is no such evening in the land where her lover went.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both turn the woman's grief into a question about where evening reaches, but the Natrinai grounds it in a village dusk of cows, offerings and a crow going home to its kin, so the couplet's bare question gains the scene that makes the absent man's freedom from evening seem unnatural."},{"a":"nt:346","b":"pn:158","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 158 names Ori as the one who ruled Kolli (கொல்லி ஆண்ட வல்வில் ஓரியும்) among the seven givers now dead; Natrinai 346 measures the wife's fragrant hair by the kulavi of the great cool Kolli of Porayan.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Set beside the elegy that names Ori as Kolli's dead lord, the love poem's passing epithet shows the hill in the Chera Porayan's hands, so the pair records that Kolli changed masters after Ori, which neither poem states."},{"a":"nt:348","b":"k:1168","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1168's woman says the night that puts every creature to sleep has only her as companion; Natrinai 348's woman lies sleepless while moon, festival town and paired bees all rejoice, and asks whether the world fights her or she the world.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the sleepless woman the night's one companion in a sleeping world, while the Natrinai wakes the whole world in celebration around her, so the same sleeplessness becomes either a bond with the dark or a quarrel with everything else."},{"a":"nt:349","b":"pn:281","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 281 has women guard a wounded warrior from ghouls with neem, music, mustard seed and incense; Natrinai 349's man likens himself, ever behind the fisher girl, to the ghoul that hovers by men gravely wounded on the battlefield.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read with the warding rite, the lover's simile casts him as the very presence a household drives from its wounded with neem and smoke, so his self-portrait as tireless follower carries his knowledge that he is the unwanted one at her door."},{"a":"nt:351","b":"nt:68","type":"develops","note":"In Natrinai 68 the friend wishes someone would bow and tell the mother that shutting young girls in the house is neither right nor good for it; in 351 she tells the mother herself that the confinement has made the girl paler, that worshipping gods will not help, and that guarding the millet field would restore her.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Together they show the friend's case against the girl's confinement in two stages: a wished-for argument from what is proper, then her own direct plea that adds the evidence of pallor the mother has misread and a cure that quietly returns the girl to her lover's field.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:354","b":"k:1150","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kural 1150 the friend tells the girl, the man overhearing, that the town is raising the very gossip they want; in Natrinai 354 the friend tells the man to his face that his friendship has become gossip as loud as the salt traders' din.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows the friend aiming the same town gossip toward marriage from two sides: told to the girl in his hearing it is the talk they hoped for, told to the man directly it is a public noise that shames his friendship.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:355","b":"k:580","type":"develops","note":"Kural 580 says that those who want a refinement worth loving will drink poison they see poured; Natrinai 355's friend says the very refined (நனி நாகரிகர்) drink even poison if old friends give it, and asks the man to be kind to the girl by கண் ஓடி.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Valluvar files the poison saying under கண்ணோட்டம; the Natrinai quotes it and then asks for exactly that kindly regard, so the pairing shows the courtesy ideal of the chapter on statecraft working in love poetry as the friend's lever to bind a man to a girl who has no one else."},{"a":"nt:356","b":"pn:67","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 67 asks the swan, flying from the ford of Kumari to the northern mountain, to stop at the Chola king's palace with the poet's name; Natrinai 356 likens the man's toiling heart to the swan that grazes the southern sea and carries food to its chicks on the Himalaya.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both send the swan between the southern sea and the northern mountain, but puram makes it a messenger to a king while akam makes its tireless feeding flight the lover's own heart, so the long-flight swan served as a go-between in one genre and as the self in the other."},{"a":"nt:357","b":"k:1207","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1207's woman says she does not know forgetting and that even remembering burns; Natrinai 357's woman says the remembered day of play with the man has scarred her heart and will not fade, and on that she says she can bear the delay.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In both, memory cannot be shed, but the couplet makes it a burn and a danger, while the Natrinai makes one remembered day the scar on which the woman rests her claim to endure, so the same unfading memory is wound in one and support in the other."},{"a":"nt:358","b":"ku:87","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 87's woman meets the belief that the old god of the common destroys the false by saying her lord is not false at all, though her brow pales; Natrinai 358's woman grants that he swore and stays away and goes to make offerings so that the god will not harm him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the belief that the oath-god punishes the perjured lover pressing on the woman in two moves, first denial that he is false and then propitiation on the oath-breaker's behalf, so the one he wronged becomes his protector before the god."},{"a":"nt:359","b":"ku:214","type":"develops","note":"In Kuruntokai 214 the hill girl has been given a great leaf skirt and garland and the whole town is thrown into uproar; in Natrinai 359 the friend, holding the man's gift of leaves, fears the mother if they wear it and hurting him if they return it.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the leaf skirt shows itself as a public sign of courtship: the Natrinai gives the friend's dilemma before it is worn, and the Kuruntokai gives what follows the wearing, the town's uproar, which is just what the friend feared."},{"a":"nt:360","b":"k:1311","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1311's woman refuses the philanderer's chest because all women feast on him with their eyes; in Natrinai 360 the friend, or on the colophon's second reading the wife in oodal, refuses him at the door and pictures yesterday's women left like a doll after the festival.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both refuse the straying husband, but the couplet objects that he is shared, while the Natrinai shows how he uses and discards each courtesan, so the wife's scorn reaches past her own wrong to the rivals he abandons."},{"a":"nt:361","b":"nt:374","type":"develops","note":"Both poems close on the same formula for the waiting wife, விருந்து அயர் விருப்பினள் with திருந்துஇழை: in 361 the friend reports it as the wife's state once the chariot is home; in 374 the returning husband imagines it on the road, joined to வருந்தும், her suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same line serves as the end of waiting in one poem and as the husband's picture of waiting in the other, so the wish to feast guests was a fixed sign of the wife who keeps the house ready, and whether it is joy or grief depends only on whether the chariot has arrived."},{"a":"nt:362","b":"s:922","type":"same_image","note":"The eloping man in Natrinai 362 vows that if a fight comes he will not fear and will drive it off, but if her kin come he will hide (நுமர் வரின் மறைகுவென்); Pattinathar's antadhi verse 922 gives the hero the same two conditions, hill men he will make food for kites, but if they are her kin he will hide (உமராயின் மறைகுவனே).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Pattinathar kept not only the eloping lover's boast but its exact limit, courage against strangers and concealment before her family, which shows the devotional kovai inherited the Sangam udanpokku speech as a fixed thurai with its own logic, since striking her kin would undo the marriage the flight is meant to make."},{"a":"nt:364","b":"k:1228","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1228 has the waiting woman say the herdsman's flute is the messenger of the burning evening and a weapon that kills; Natrinai 364's heroine says the herdsmen's kondrai flutes and the cattle bells at evening will take her life if they come with the rain after the named season has passed.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's flute that kills is given its setting by the Natrinai poem, cows driven home, bells that never knew thunder and the season he named already gone, which shows why a sweet pastoral sound became deadly: it marks the homecoming hour, and the season promise turns it into proof that he has not come."},{"a":"nt:371","b":"nt:364","type":"develops","note":"In 371 the returning hero tells his charioteer that the rain has begun toward where she lives, that she has begun to burn and the herdsmen have begun their flutes; in 364 the heroine herself says the herdsmen's flutes in the rainy evening will take her life.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two poems show the man's guess on the road matching what the woman says at home almost sound for sound, so the herdsmen's evening flute was a shared code in mullai by which each partner knew the other's hour, and the hero's haste is grounded in knowing exactly how that evening falls on her.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:365","b":"k:1137","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1137 holds that nothing is nobler than a woman who suffers a sea of desire yet does not ride the madal, that is, does not make her love public; in Natrinai 365 the friend proposes that they slip the mother's watch, go to the common in daylight before everyone and ask the way to his town to rebuke him.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The couplet makes the woman's silence a virtue, but the Natrinai friend uses the threat of breaking it, a daylight public walk being the woman's form of the madal, as a lever on the overhearing hero, so the restraint the Kural praises was also a weapon that worked only while it might be dropped.","gr":2},{"a":"nt:366","b":"a:167","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 167 the man tells his heart, which is pressing him to leave for wealth, that after a sweet night with his wife in the tall house he would lie sleepless in a ruined village hall, and gives up the journey; in Natrinai 366 he tells his heart that whoever leaves her washed, flower-tied hair and soft bed in the north wind that shakes the sparrow's nest is a fool.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The two give the same refusal two arguments: Akananuru 167 sets the home against the terror of the road, while Natrinai 366 never reaches the road and argues from the season alone, a cold wind that even a bird's home must withstand, so the stay-at-home decision could rest either on fear or on a general law of the world (உலகியல்).","gr":4},{"a":"nt:367","b":"nt:361","type":"develops","note":"In 367 the friend tells the heroine that the young men have come wearing a garland of mullai and blue lily and that her man will surely come today; in 361 the friend reports that he himself and his young men are wearing mullai as his chariot reaches the house.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence the two poems show that the mullai garland was the badge of the returning party, worn first by the retinue as a herald and then by the master on arrival, so the friend could promise the return from the young men's flowers before any chariot was seen."},{"a":"nt:369","b":"k:1251","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1251 has the woman say love's axe breaks the door of restraint (நிறை) barred with shame; Natrinai 369's heroine, left unmarried while he seeks wealth, says she cannot swim across a love that breaks her restraint (நிறை அடு காமம்) like the Ganga in flood breaking dams.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both name the same loss, restraint broken by love, but the Kural's image is a single blow to a closed door while the Natrinai's is a flood from the Himalaya that keeps coming each evening, so the poem adds what the couplet lacks: that the breaking recurs at a fixed hour and the woman's question is endurance, how to swim, not resistance."},{"a":"nt:370","b":"nt:380","type":"tension","note":"In 370 the husband recalls his wife in the lying-in room, smeared with ghee and mustard, newly called 'old woman' after bearing a son, and finds her shy smile the sweetest thing; in 380 the friend says the wife's ghee-soiled clothes, milk-wet breasts and smell of new birth make her no match for the jewelled women he visits.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same marks of motherhood are the husband's proof of tenderness in one poem and the wife's side's proof of his desertion in the other, which shows that the marutham quarrel is fought over what a mother's body means, and that the husband's memory in 370 is an answer to exactly the charge 380 makes."},{"a":"nt:374","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1268 has the man, held by the king's war, wish that the king win so that he may join his wife and hold the evening feast for guests; Natrinai 374's man, returning from his work, asks travellers about the wife who longs to feast guests yet weeps.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the feast for guests as the man's own goal after duty, while the Natrinai poem puts the same wish in the wife and shows it unfulfilled in her tears, so the household's guest-feast was the shared picture of reunion that each partner ascribed to the other.","gr":3},{"a":"nt:376","b":"a:38","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 38 the friend imagines the hill man, garlanded with vengai and blue lily and carrying a bow, coming to the swing by the asoka and the harvested millet field and finding them deserted; in Natrinai 376 she tells the parrots to tell the man with the garland and bow who appears by the asoka that the mother still guards even the empty field.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together they show the harvested millet field as the clock of the kalavu plot: once the crop is cut the daytime meeting place is gone, and the friend's two messages, where our village is in Akananuru 38 and the guard that stays on the empty field in Natrinai 376, both push the man to come openly as a suitor."},{"a":"nt:377","b":"k:1133","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1133 the turned-away man says he once had shame and manliness and now has the madal that lovers ride; in Natrinai 377 the turned-away man, speaking for the friend to hear, asks whether riding the madal and praising the girl through lands and towns will not kill him like a disease.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states what the madal costs the man, his shame, while the Natrinai shows what it would do, carry her beauty's praise through every town, so the threat works on the friend because his loss of shame would become the girl's public exposure."},{"a":"nt:378","b":"k:791","type":"develops","note":"Kural 791 says nothing is as ruinous as making a friend without inquiry, since the devoted cannot leave once the bond is made; Natrinai 378 ends with the friend (or the heroine) calling her sleepless nights and the neighbours' gossip the measure of the bond made without inquiry (நாடாது இயைந்த நண்பு) with the lord of the shore.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam poem applies the ethics book's rule for choosing friends to a love made at a girl's sand-house, and its suffering shows the second half of the couplet at work, that she cannot leave the bond once made, so the maxim on friendship was available as a verdict on unwise love."},{"a":"nt:381","b":"pn:101","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 101: Avvai tells her heart that Athiyaman's gift, late or soon, is as sure as the food an elephant holds between its tusks. Natrinai 381 (also Avvaiyar): the rain has settled in like Neduman Anji's seat from which he keeps giving chariots.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same poet who in puram counts on Anji's giving as something that never fails makes that unfailing giving the image of a rain a waiting woman cannot bear, so the patron's plenty is a blessing to the bard and a torment to the lover depending on who receives it."},{"a":"nt:382","b":"k:1161","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1161: she would hide this pain, but it wells up like spring water when drawn. Natrinai 382: at evening she must hide her grief even if her life goes.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet gives the woman's wish to hide her pain without a reason and says it fails; the Natrinai woman supplies the reason, that the slander of his enemies would shame him, so concealment in separation is shown to be a duty to his name and not only her modesty."},{"a":"nt:384","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1102: other diseases have other cures, but the sickness this jewelled woman causes she alone cures. Natrinai 384: eloping, the man calls her the one who was the medicine when he suffered after casting off shame.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's paradox that the woman is the cure for the sickness she causes becomes in Natrinai 384 the eloping man's reason for taking her across the waste: he now carries his medicine with him, so the couplet's moment of union is extended into the whole journey of udanpokku."},{"a":"nt:387","b":"a:175","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 175: the clouds flash more often than the spear Chezhiyan raised after his victory at Alankanam; the time he set for return has come. Natrinai 387: the cloud flashes like swords drawn in Chezhiyan's camp at Alankanam; he will come.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In both poems the lightning of the promised rains is measured by the weapons of Chezhiyan's victory at Alankanam, spoken once by the waiting woman and once by her friend, so the battle had become a stock sign of the season of return and could serve either as complaint (the time is here) or as comfort (he will come)."},{"a":"nt:391","b":"a:91","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 91: even if he won Kuttuvan's Kudanadu, he would not stay there forgetting her beauty. Natrinai 391: who would leave for wealth even to win Nannan's Ezhil hill, and make her eyes weep?","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend's comfort had a fixed shape, a famous chief's rich land weighed against the woman's beauty or tears, and the pair shows its two stages: in the Akananuru the man has already gone and will not linger, in the Natrinai the journey is only announced and will not be made at all."},{"a":"nt:395","b":"a:376","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 376: the courtesan tells the lord to give back her beauty, which was like Kuttuvan's Maranthai, and then go. Natrinai 395: the friend tells the shore lord to give back the girl's beauty, which is like Maanthai on the sea, and go.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same formula, beauty likened to a Chera town and demanded back before he leaves, is spoken by a discarded courtesan in the Akananuru and by the friend of an unmarried girl here, so the restitution formula belonged to the speech of any woman wronged by the man, and in the friend's mouth it becomes a demand for marriage."},{"a":"nt:397","b":"ku:49","type":"qualifies","note":"Kuruntokai 49 (Ammuvanar): even when this life changes to the next, may you be my husband and I the one close to your heart. Natrinai 397 (Ammuvanar): I do not fear death, but I fear that in another birth I may forget my lover.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same poet gives the hope and the fear of the same belief: in Kuruntokai 49 rebirth is a chance to be joined again, in Natrinai 397 it is the risk of forgetting, so for these women the next birth mattered only as it kept or lost the present love."},{"a":"nt:400","b":"pn:39","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 39: because justice stands firm and unfailing in the Chola court at Uranthai, justice is not this king's own fame. Natrinai 400: like justice that never fails in the Uranthai court of the warlike Cholas, he knows no failing in her heart.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poet says Uranthai's justice is too settled to count as any one king's merit, and the courtesan borrows exactly that settledness for her lover's place in her heart, so an inherited institution, not a person's virtue, was the akam poet's measure of a love that cannot change."}]});
